    ipbes:hasDescription """
The Nature Futures Framework (being developed by the IPBES task force on scenarios and models) provides a foundation for envisaging positive futures for sustainable use of wild species, since it places human-nature relations at its core, and reflects the multiple ways in which both people and nature can benefit from the use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qkk6vefz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lundquist, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Lundquist, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19361,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NJN9IQQU"],"itemData":{"id":19361,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Visions for nature and nature’s contributions to people for the 21 st century","author":[{"family":"Lundquist","given":"C.J."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"H.M."},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"R."},{"family":"Belder","given":"E."},{"family":"Carvalho Ribeiro","given":"S."},{"family":"Davies","given":"K."},{"family":"Lindgren-Streicher","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Importantly, these visions are not mutually exclusive, but rather they offer a plurality of approaches for how sustainable use of wild species can be realized (Figure 5.8).

Figure 5.8. A plurality of visions for sustainable use of wild species, based on the nature futures framework. Adapted from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8AJHmBrN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. M. Pereira, 2020)","plainCitation":"(L. M. Pereira, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/532LHIG3"],"itemData":{"id":19100,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"People and Nature","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"1172–1195","title":"Developing multiscale and integrative nature–people scenarios using the Nature Futures Framework","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Pereira","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Davies","given":"K.K."},{"family":"Belder","given":"E."},{"family":"Ferrier","given":"S."},{"family":"Karlsson-Vinkhuyzen","given":"S."},{"family":"Kim","given":"H."},{"family":"Kuiper","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Okayasu","given":"S."},{"family":"Palomo","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"H.M."},{"family":"Peterson","given":"G."},{"family":"Sathyapalan","given":"J."},{"family":"Schoolenberg","given":"M."},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"R."},{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"S.Carvalho"},{"family":"Greenaway","given":"A."},{"family":"Hauck","given":"J."},{"family":"King","given":"N."},{"family":"Lazarova","given":"T."},{"family":"Ravera","given":"F."},{"family":"Chettri","given":"N."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W.L."},{"family":"Hendriks","given":"R.J.J."},{"family":"Kolomytsev","given":"G."},{"family":"Leadley","given":"P."},{"family":"Metzger","given":"J.-P."},{"family":"Ninan","given":"K.N."},{"family":"Pichs","given":"R."},{"family":"Popp","given":"A."},{"family":"Rondinini","given":"C."},{"family":"Rosa","given":"I."},{"family":"Vuuren","given":"D."},{"family":"Lundquist","given":"C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, under license CC BY-NC-ND 4.0.
The Nature Futures Framework is a heuristic tool developed by the IPBES Task Force on Scenarios and Models that can help to explore and define positive relationships of humans with nature in order to create desirable nature scenariosITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mGlcuS2C","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. M. Pereira, 2020)","plainCitation":"(L. M. Pereira, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/532LHIG3"],"itemData":{"id":19100,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"People and Nature","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"1172–1195","title":"Developing multiscale and integrative nature–people scenarios using the Nature Futures Framework","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Pereira","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Davies","given":"K.K."},{"family":"Belder","given":"E."},{"family":"Ferrier","given":"S."},{"family":"Karlsson-Vinkhuyzen","given":"S."},{"family":"Kim","given":"H."},{"family":"Kuiper","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Okayasu","given":"S."},{"family":"Palomo","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"H.M."},{"family":"Peterson","given":"G."},{"family":"Sathyapalan","given":"J."},{"family":"Schoolenberg","given":"M."},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"R."},{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"S.Carvalho"},{"family":"Greenaway","given":"A."},{"family":"Hauck","given":"J."},{"family":"King","given":"N."},{"family":"Lazarova","given":"T."},{"family":"Ravera","given":"F."},{"family":"Chettri","given":"N."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W.L."},{"family":"Hendriks","given":"R.J.J."},{"family":"Kolomytsev","given":"G."},{"family":"Leadley","given":"P."},{"family":"Metzger","given":"J.-P."},{"family":"Ninan","given":"K.N."},{"family":"Pichs","given":"R."},{"family":"Popp","given":"A."},{"family":"Rondinini","given":"C."},{"family":"Rosa","given":"I."},{"family":"Vuuren","given":"D."},{"family":"Lundquist","given":"C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the context of the sustainable use of wild species, the framework could be applied across different scales to target and achieve positive futures. When designing interventions to enhance sustainable use of wild species within the nature futures framework, a plurality of values needs to be included. Importantly, there is a need to “build on common interests between conservationists and [wild species users] wherever these occur, but also engage in honest discussion about genuine conflicts of interest where these exist and work towards negotiated solutions” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tedGC3Jn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Newing & Perram, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Newing & Perram, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19379,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y6DZSBSG"],"itemData":{"id":19379,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605319000917","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"595–596","title":"What do you know about conservation and human rights?","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Newing","given":"H."},{"family":"Perram","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Box 5.8 presents an example of a conceptual application of the Nature Futures Framework to wild species use in a fisheries management context under the three most distinct nature perspectives identified by IPBES - the points of the triangle: nature for nature (intrinsic values of nature), nature for society (nature’s benefits to people) and nature as culture (relational values with nature). Box 5.8 shows an example of sustainable use in the Amazon as envisioned by the Nature Futures Framework.
      
Box 5.8. Nature future framework in fisheries management for the sustainable use of marine resources.
This example (Figure 5.9) represents a simple conceptual illustration of the potential application of the Nature Futures Framework to develop desirable future scenarios for both people (fishing activities) and nature (exploited wild species in marine ecosystems) under three different values perspectives ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZRXhb9vD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u8220{}IPBES Task Force on Scenarios and Models, 2021,\\\\uc0\\\\u8221{} n.d.)","plainCitation":"(“IPBES Task Force on Scenarios and Models, 2021,” n.d.)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JSCE3YHC"],"itemData":{"id":19331,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Report of the IPBES task force on scenarios and models on its workshop on modelling Nature Futures scenarios under the 2030 IPBES rolling work programme","language":"en","title":"IPBES Task force on scenarios and models, 2021","URL":"https://ipbes.net/system/files/2021-09/Ipbes-tf-scn-wsp-2021-1-6-workshop%20report%20on%20modelling%20Nature%20Futures_20210901.pdf"}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This example aims to build different narratives related to fisheries management, focusing primarily on reference points. Here, these narratives have shared outcomes referred to as “common features” that are essential assumptions for achieving any of the positive visions embodied in the nature futures framework (e.g., application of the precautionary approach). The common features as shared elements aim to ensure a reference baseline for sustainable use. The specific features distinguish these narratives from one another. In this example, the differences between narratives were highlighted through three categories: (i) restriction strategies in mixed fisheries (output control in multispecies fisheries), (ii) management scale, and (iii) indicators of interest. These categories are not exhaustive and could be enriched to better describe different exploitation scenarios for marine species under the nature future framework. 
Figure 5.9. Potential application of the Nature Futures Framework in fisheries management. Source: Halouani et al (in prep).
     
Box 5.9 Nature Futures Framework scenarios for the sustainable use of forest resources in the Brazilian Amazon 
The Nature Futures Framework promotes participatory and inclusive approaches to developing scenarios with stakeholders by co-creating narratives and modelling frameworks and co-identifying or developing indicators to inform decision making ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yqkcziB7","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(L. M. Pereira, 2020; Kim, 2021)","plainCitation":"(L. M. Pereira, 2020; Kim, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/532LHIG3"],"itemData":{"id":19100,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"People and Nature","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"1172–1195","title":"Developing multiscale and integrative nature–people scenarios using the Nature Futures Framework","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Pereira","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Davies","given":"K.K."},{"family":"Belder","given":"E."},{"family":"Ferrier","given":"S."},{"family":"Karlsson-Vinkhuyzen","given":"S."},{"family":"Kim","given":"H."},{"family":"Kuiper","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Okayasu","given":"S."},{"family":"Palomo","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"H.M."},{"family":"Peterson","given":"G."},{"family":"Sathyapalan","given":"J."},{"family":"Schoolenberg","given":"M."},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"R."},{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"S.Carvalho"},{"family":"Greenaway","given":"A."},{"family":"Hauck","given":"J."},{"family":"King","given":"N."},{"family":"Lazarova","given":"T."},{"family":"Ravera","given":"F."},{"family":"Chettri","given":"N."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W.L."},{"family":"Hendriks","given":"R.J.J."},{"family":"Kolomytsev","given":"G."},{"family":"Leadley","given":"P."},{"family":"Metzger","given":"J.-P."},{"family":"Ninan","given":"K.N."},{"family":"Pichs","given":"R."},{"family":"Popp","given":"A."},{"family":"Rondinini","given":"C."},{"family":"Rosa","given":"I."},{"family":"Vuuren","given":"D."},{"family":"Lundquist","given":"C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":20133,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EANEG4CK"],"itemData":{"id":20133,"type":"report","abstract":"The Nature Futures Framework (NFF) is a heuristic tool for co-creating positive futures for nature and people. It seeks to open up a diversity of futures through mainly three value perspectives on nature – Nature for Nature, Nature for Society, Nature as Culture. In this paper, we describe how the NFF can be applied in modelling to support policy. First, it describes key building blocks of the NFF in developing qualitative and quantitative scenarios: i) multiple value perspectives on nature and the frontier representing their improvements, ii) incorporating mutually reinforcing and key feedbacks of social-ecological systems, iii) indicators describing the evolution of social-ecological systems. We then present three approaches to modelling Nature Futures scenarios in review, screening and design phases of policy processes. This paper seeks to facilitate the integration of relational values of nature in models and to strengthen modelled linkages across biodiversity, nature’s contributions to people and quality of life.","genre":"preprint","note":"DOI: 10.31235/osf.io/93sqp","publisher":"SocArXiv","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Towards a better future for biodiversity and people: modelling Nature Futures","title-short":"Towards a better future for biodiversity and people","URL":"https://osf.io/93sqp","author":[{"family":"Kim","given":"HyeJin"},{"family":"Peterson","given":"Garry"},{"family":"Cheung","given":"William"},{"family":"Ferrier","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Arneth","given":"Almut"},{"family":"Kuiper","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Okayasu","given":"Sana"},{"family":"Pereira","given":"Laura M."},{"family":"Acosta","given":"Lilibeth A."},{"family":"kramer","given":"rebecca","non-dropping-particle":"chaplin-"},{"family":"Belder","given":"Eefje","dropping-particle":"den"},{"family":"Eddy","given":"Tyler"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Justin"},{"family":"Karlsson-Vinkhuysen","given":"Sylvia"},{"family":"Kok","given":"Marcel"},{"family":"Leadley","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Leclère","given":"David"},{"family":"Lundquist","given":"Carolyn J."},{"family":"Rondinini","given":"Carlo"},{"family":"Scholes","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Schoolenberg","given":"Machteld"},{"family":"Shin","given":"Yunne-Jai"},{"family":"Stehfest","given":"Elke"},{"family":"Stephenson","given":"Fabrice"},{"family":"Visconti","given":"Piero"},{"family":"Vuuren","given":"Detlef P.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Wabnitz","given":"Colette C."},{"family":"Alava","given":"Juan José"},{"family":"Cuadros-Casanova","given":"Ivon"},{"family":"Davies","given":"Kathryn K."},{"family":"Gasalla","given":"Maria A."},{"family":"Halouani","given":"Ghassen"},{"family":"Harfoot","given":"Michael B. J."},{"family":"Hashimoto","given":"Shizuka"},{"family":"Hickler","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Hirsch","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Kolomytsev","given":"Grigory"},{"family":"Miller","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Ohashi","given":"Haruka"},{"family":"Palomo","given":"Maria Gabriela"},{"family":"Popp","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Remme","given":"Roy Paco"},{"family":"Saito","given":"Osamu"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"Rashid"},{"family":"Willcock","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Pereira","given":"Henrique"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",7,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. By doing this, the goal is for the Nature Futures Framework to facilitate and enable transformative change by helping people to reflect on different decision options from diverse value perspectives. 
Using the Nature Futures Framework and the framework on Nature’s Contributions to People ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aIRRpRJi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Díaz, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5725,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VIK3WAQX"],"itemData":{"id":5725,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The purpose of this research was to investigate the conceptions and misconceptions of young children (ages 3 - 8) related to science concepts, skills, and phenomena. These conceptions and misconceptions were investigated within the framework of the Pennsylvania Early Learning Standards for Pre-Kindergarten and the Pennsylvania Standards for Kindergarten as developed and published by the Pennsylvania Department of Education in 2005. In addition, the National Science Education Standards also served as a foundation for our research. Findings reveal the most common conceptions related to matter, magnetism, density, and air. Extrapolations from this research can be used by pre-service teachers, in-service teachers, and teacher educators to implement experiences in the classroom that assist in developing and refining young children's understandings of scientific concepts, skills, and phenomena.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aap8826","issue":"6373","note":"PMID: 29348221","page":"270–272","title":"Assessing nature's contributions to people","volume":"359","author":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Pascual","given":"U."},{"literal":"Stenseke M."},{"family":"Martín-López","given":"B."},{"family":"Watson","given":"R. T."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z. Hill R."},{"family":"Chai","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Baste","given":"I. A."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"S."},{"family":"Church","given":"A."},{"family":"Lonsdale","given":"M."},{"family":"Larigauderie","given":"A."},{"family":"Leadley","given":"P. W."},{"family":"Oudenhoven","given":"A. P. E.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Plaat","given":"F.","non-dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Schröter","given":"M."},{"family":"Lavorel","given":"S."},{"family":"Aumeeruddy-Thomas","given":"Y."},{"family":"Bukvareva","given":"E."},{"family":"Davies","given":"K."},{"family":"Demissew","given":"S."},{"family":"Erpul","given":"G."},{"family":"Failler","given":"P."},{"family":"Guerra","given":"C. A."},{"family":"Hewitt","given":"C. L."},{"family":"Keune","given":"H."},{"family":"Lindley","given":"S."},{"family":"Shirayama","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"b5lUYVY8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Siqueira-Gay, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Siqueira-Gay, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1667,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YFUBRG42"],"itemData":{"id":1667,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Mining is a significant driver of deforestation. Not only do mines clear native forests for mineral extraction, they also often establish new infrastructure, which indirectly facilitates new access to land and further clearing. Forest loss and fragmentation have serious effects on biodiversity, yet rarely are these cumulative impacts of mining studied at the regional scale. Here, we examine potential impacts of mining in a biodiverse region of the Brazilian Amazon. The National Reserve of Copper and Associates (“Renca”) is currently off limits to mining activities but was recently threatened with a move to permit mineral exploration. We analyzed historic forest loss and fragmentation within two mining regions neighboring Renca. We also investigated historic deforestation trajectories within Renca's protected areas, to determine how well conserved these forests are against current threats. We found that mining, and other infrastructure associated with mines (i.e. roads), caused significant forest loss and fragmentation within neighboring mining sectors, and that Renca's protected areas are not currently immune to forest loss. Permitting new mines within and surrounding Renca will place additional pressure on its biodiversity. If mineral development is to proceed, huge regulatory changes will be required to effectively manage negative impacts on forests and biodiversity. Environmental Impact Assessments for new mining projects must assess and mitigate the cumulative region-wide effects on forests, while existing protected areas must be strengthened to ensure they are not directly or indirectly compromised by mining activities.","container-title":"Resources Policy","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.resourpol.2020.101662","ISSN":"0301-4207","page":"101662","title":"Exploring potential impacts of mining on forest loss and fragmentation within a biodiverse region of Brazil's northeastern Amazon","volume":"67","author":[{"family":"Siqueira-Gay","given":"Juliana"},{"family":"Sonter","given":"Laura J."},{"family":"Sánchez","given":"Luis E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} identified trajectories leading to positive futures in the Brazilian Amazon of Pará State, including indigenous peoples and local communities’ perspectives. They created three positive scenarios addressing negative anthropogenic drivers:
1. Land management to tackle illegal deforestation (Pará minus)
2. Changes in consumption behavior (Pará Consumo)
3. Combining (i) and (ii)
The Pará minus scenario includes policies that address rural land occupation, agriculture and pasture expansion, unofficial road building and forest degradation with co-management, and decentralized environmental governance with user coordinated actions for sustainable management of natural resources. The Pará Consumo scenario includes policies to reduce excessive meat consumption and clearing of forest areas for soy plantation for feeding animal through environmental education to modify consumption behavior ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"opXyszL5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Siqueira-Gay, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Siqueira-Gay, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1667,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YFUBRG42"],"itemData":{"id":1667,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Mining is a significant driver of deforestation. Not only do mines clear native forests for mineral extraction, they also often establish new infrastructure, which indirectly facilitates new access to land and further clearing. Forest loss and fragmentation have serious effects on biodiversity, yet rarely are these cumulative impacts of mining studied at the regional scale. Here, we examine potential impacts of mining in a biodiverse region of the Brazilian Amazon. The National Reserve of Copper and Associates (“Renca”) is currently off limits to mining activities but was recently threatened with a move to permit mineral exploration. We analyzed historic forest loss and fragmentation within two mining regions neighboring Renca. We also investigated historic deforestation trajectories within Renca's protected areas, to determine how well conserved these forests are against current threats. We found that mining, and other infrastructure associated with mines (i.e. roads), caused significant forest loss and fragmentation within neighboring mining sectors, and that Renca's protected areas are not currently immune to forest loss. Permitting new mines within and surrounding Renca will place additional pressure on its biodiversity. If mineral development is to proceed, huge regulatory changes will be required to effectively manage negative impacts on forests and biodiversity. Environmental Impact Assessments for new mining projects must assess and mitigate the cumulative region-wide effects on forests, while existing protected areas must be strengthened to ensure they are not directly or indirectly compromised by mining activities.","container-title":"Resources Policy","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.resourpol.2020.101662","ISSN":"0301-4207","page":"101662","title":"Exploring potential impacts of mining on forest loss and fragmentation within a biodiverse region of Brazil's northeastern Amazon","volume":"67","author":[{"family":"Siqueira-Gay","given":"Juliana"},{"family":"Sonter","given":"Laura J."},{"family":"Sánchez","given":"Luis E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The core actions for the implementation of policies in these scenarios are listed in Table 5.8. 

Table 5.11 Core actions for policy implementation in two sustainable forest scenarios named Pará minus and Pára Consumo (source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tYnck6ZC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Siqueira-Gay, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Siqueira-Gay, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1667,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YFUBRG42"],"itemData":{"id":1667,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Mining is a significant driver of deforestation. Not only do mines clear native forests for mineral extraction, they also often establish new infrastructure, which indirectly facilitates new access to land and further clearing. Forest loss and fragmentation have serious effects on biodiversity, yet rarely are these cumulative impacts of mining studied at the regional scale. Here, we examine potential impacts of mining in a biodiverse region of the Brazilian Amazon. The National Reserve of Copper and Associates (“Renca”) is currently off limits to mining activities but was recently threatened with a move to permit mineral exploration. We analyzed historic forest loss and fragmentation within two mining regions neighboring Renca. We also investigated historic deforestation trajectories within Renca's protected areas, to determine how well conserved these forests are against current threats. We found that mining, and other infrastructure associated with mines (i.e. roads), caused significant forest loss and fragmentation within neighboring mining sectors, and that Renca's protected areas are not currently immune to forest loss. Permitting new mines within and surrounding Renca will place additional pressure on its biodiversity. If mineral development is to proceed, huge regulatory changes will be required to effectively manage negative impacts on forests and biodiversity. Environmental Impact Assessments for new mining projects must assess and mitigate the cumulative region-wide effects on forests, while existing protected areas must be strengthened to ensure they are not directly or indirectly compromised by mining activities.","container-title":"Resources Policy","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.resourpol.2020.101662","ISSN":"0301-4207","page":"101662","title":"Exploring potential impacts of mining on forest loss and fragmentation within a biodiverse region of Brazil's northeastern Amazon","volume":"67","author":[{"family":"Siqueira-Gay","given":"Juliana"},{"family":"Sonter","given":"Laura J."},{"family":"Sánchez","given":"Luis E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
Pará minus scenario: Land management to tackle illegal deforestation
Pará Consumo scenario: Changes in consumption behavior
i. Enforce forest decentralization efforts to allow small governance units to take decisions about their resources in a sustainable way
ii. Provide technological tools and training to communities to facilitate sustainable development and monitoring efforts
iii. Enforce the protection of indigenous territories and protected areas by creating an inheritance tax scheme and fines for illegally clearing forest areas
iv. Perform a land reform to distribute underused or abandoned land to individuals or organizations committed to sustainability and conservation efforts or return the land to indigenous or traditional communities
v. Regulate for mandatory sustainable use of undesignated public lands, and prohibit (with fines applicable) clearing of pristine forest areas
vi. Create new and strengthen existing alliances to make forest monitoring and controlling efforts more effective, while facilitating social learning processes in local communities
i. Create an educational program to promote awareness on nature’s contributions to people provision, the value of forest conservation, and damage caused by cattle ranching. This program would be integrated into the educational system by restructuring the curriculum
ii. Promote alternative options for protein consumption instead of beef
iii. Create a tax incentive for large companies that join the beef moratorium (an agreement to not buy meat from newly deforested areas) or that support the educational program of awareness on nature’s contributions to people provision (action i)

      The three positive scenarios formulated for the Pará State in ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZDpfsIVW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Siqueira-Gay, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Siqueira-Gay, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1667,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YFUBRG42"],"itemData":{"id":1667,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Mining is a significant driver of deforestation. Not only do mines clear native forests for mineral extraction, they also often establish new infrastructure, which indirectly facilitates new access to land and further clearing. Forest loss and fragmentation have serious effects on biodiversity, yet rarely are these cumulative impacts of mining studied at the regional scale. Here, we examine potential impacts of mining in a biodiverse region of the Brazilian Amazon. The National Reserve of Copper and Associates (“Renca”) is currently off limits to mining activities but was recently threatened with a move to permit mineral exploration. We analyzed historic forest loss and fragmentation within two mining regions neighboring Renca. We also investigated historic deforestation trajectories within Renca's protected areas, to determine how well conserved these forests are against current threats. We found that mining, and other infrastructure associated with mines (i.e. roads), caused significant forest loss and fragmentation within neighboring mining sectors, and that Renca's protected areas are not currently immune to forest loss. Permitting new mines within and surrounding Renca will place additional pressure on its biodiversity. If mineral development is to proceed, huge regulatory changes will be required to effectively manage negative impacts on forests and biodiversity. Environmental Impact Assessments for new mining projects must assess and mitigate the cumulative region-wide effects on forests, while existing protected areas must be strengthened to ensure they are not directly or indirectly compromised by mining activities.","container-title":"Resources Policy","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.resourpol.2020.101662","ISSN":"0301-4207","page":"101662","title":"Exploring potential impacts of mining on forest loss and fragmentation within a biodiverse region of Brazil's northeastern Amazon","volume":"67","author":[{"family":"Siqueira-Gay","given":"Juliana"},{"family":"Sonter","given":"Laura J."},{"family":"Sánchez","given":"Luis E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} anticipate different positive outcomes. In the Pará minus scenario, land reform and regulation strengthen conservation values, social learning promotes collaboration between stakeholders and integrates their knowledge, and economic development doesn’t depend on the extractive use of natural resources while traditional extractive activities continue in a sustainable manner. In the Pará Consumo scenario, the food market motivates local production and consumption, reducing carbon emissions from transportation of goods and creating nature-based recreational opportunities for people. There is active urban farming and recycling to reduce waste, and values transformation through social welfare and innovation. In the scenario that combines the two, sustainable economic development is envisaged with green solutions and enhanced social empowerment through social learning and education. Overall, the policies implemented in these scenarios make positive steps towards sustainable land use and land change, consider and help to mitigate climate change, and sustain natural resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"85eIBUNa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Siqueira-Gay, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Siqueira-Gay, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1667,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YFUBRG42"],"itemData":{"id":1667,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Mining is a significant driver of deforestation. Not only do mines clear native forests for mineral extraction, they also often establish new infrastructure, which indirectly facilitates new access to land and further clearing. Forest loss and fragmentation have serious effects on biodiversity, yet rarely are these cumulative impacts of mining studied at the regional scale. Here, we examine potential impacts of mining in a biodiverse region of the Brazilian Amazon. The National Reserve of Copper and Associates (“Renca”) is currently off limits to mining activities but was recently threatened with a move to permit mineral exploration. We analyzed historic forest loss and fragmentation within two mining regions neighboring Renca. We also investigated historic deforestation trajectories within Renca's protected areas, to determine how well conserved these forests are against current threats. We found that mining, and other infrastructure associated with mines (i.e. roads), caused significant forest loss and fragmentation within neighboring mining sectors, and that Renca's protected areas are not currently immune to forest loss. Permitting new mines within and surrounding Renca will place additional pressure on its biodiversity. If mineral development is to proceed, huge regulatory changes will be required to effectively manage negative impacts on forests and biodiversity. Environmental Impact Assessments for new mining projects must assess and mitigate the cumulative region-wide effects on forests, while existing protected areas must be strengthened to ensure they are not directly or indirectly compromised by mining activities.","container-title":"Resources Policy","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.resourpol.2020.101662","ISSN":"0301-4207","page":"101662","title":"Exploring potential impacts of mining on forest loss and fragmentation within a biodiverse region of Brazil's northeastern Amazon","volume":"67","author":[{"family":"Siqueira-Gay","given":"Juliana"},{"family":"Sonter","given":"Laura J."},{"family":"Sánchez","given":"Luis E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      As illustrated in Pará State’s scenarios, scientific and local knowledge, models, and indicators generate diverse and complementary evidence for evaluating the roles and impacts of different policy and management options in conserving nature and providing benefits to people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4QHNnxzw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kim, 2021; Tengo, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Kim, 2021; Tengo, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20133,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EANEG4CK"],"itemData":{"id":20133,"type":"report","abstract":"The Nature Futures Framework (NFF) is a heuristic tool for co-creating positive futures for nature and people. It seeks to open up a diversity of futures through mainly three value perspectives on nature – Nature for Nature, Nature for Society, Nature as Culture. In this paper, we describe how the NFF can be applied in modelling to support policy. First, it describes key building blocks of the NFF in developing qualitative and quantitative scenarios: i) multiple value perspectives on nature and the frontier representing their improvements, ii) incorporating mutually reinforcing and key feedbacks of social-ecological systems, iii) indicators describing the evolution of social-ecological systems. We then present three approaches to modelling Nature Futures scenarios in review, screening and design phases of policy processes. This paper seeks to facilitate the integration of relational values of nature in models and to strengthen modelled linkages across biodiversity, nature’s contributions to people and quality of life.","genre":"preprint","note":"DOI: 10.31235/osf.io/93sqp","publisher":"SocArXiv","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Towards a better future for biodiversity and people: modelling Nature Futures","title-short":"Towards a better future for biodiversity and people","URL":"https://osf.io/93sqp","author":[{"family":"Kim","given":"HyeJin"},{"family":"Peterson","given":"Garry"},{"family":"Cheung","given":"William"},{"family":"Ferrier","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Arneth","given":"Almut"},{"family":"Kuiper","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Okayasu","given":"Sana"},{"family":"Pereira","given":"Laura M."},{"family":"Acosta","given":"Lilibeth A."},{"family":"kramer","given":"rebecca","non-dropping-particle":"chaplin-"},{"family":"Belder","given":"Eefje","dropping-particle":"den"},{"family":"Eddy","given":"Tyler"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Justin"},{"family":"Karlsson-Vinkhuysen","given":"Sylvia"},{"family":"Kok","given":"Marcel"},{"family":"Leadley","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Leclère","given":"David"},{"family":"Lundquist","given":"Carolyn J."},{"family":"Rondinini","given":"Carlo"},{"family":"Scholes","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Schoolenberg","given":"Machteld"},{"family":"Shin","given":"Yunne-Jai"},{"family":"Stehfest","given":"Elke"},{"family":"Stephenson","given":"Fabrice"},{"family":"Visconti","given":"Piero"},{"family":"Vuuren","given":"Detlef P.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Wabnitz","given":"Colette C."},{"family":"Alava","given":"Juan José"},{"family":"Cuadros-Casanova","given":"Ivon"},{"family":"Davies","given":"Kathryn K."},{"family":"Gasalla","given":"Maria A."},{"family":"Halouani","given":"Ghassen"},{"family":"Harfoot","given":"Michael B. J."},{"family":"Hashimoto","given":"Shizuka"},{"family":"Hickler","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Hirsch","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Kolomytsev","given":"Grigory"},{"family":"Miller","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Ohashi","given":"Haruka"},{"family":"Palomo","given":"Maria Gabriela"},{"family":"Popp","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Remme","given":"Roy Paco"},{"family":"Saito","given":"Osamu"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"Rashid"},{"family":"Willcock","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Pereira","given":"Henrique"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",7,22]]}}},{"id":19431,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R9PCWDQT"],"itemData":{"id":19431,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"J. Rural Stud","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"461–74","title":"Connecting Diverse Knowledge Systems for Enhanced Ecosystem Governance: The Multiple Evidence Base Approach:13.Tovar","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Tengo","given":"M."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E.S."},{"family":"Elmqvist","given":"T."},{"family":"Malmer","given":"P."},{"family":"Spierenburg","given":"M."},{"family":"L.G.","given":"Martin"},{"family":"L.","given":"Cruz"},{"family":"M.A.G.","given":"Mutersbaugh"},{"literal":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The illustrative scenario and modeling framework for Pará State (Figure 5.10) could be developed for and applied to other places or systems to explore the consequences of nature- and people-positive visions in informing future policy and management decisions in a more solution and action-oriented way. By bringing diverse value perspectives on nature (i.e., intrinsic, instrumental and relational values) into scenario development, the Nature Futures Framework can help stakeholders recognize the multiple benefits of conserving nature and its ecological processes, while preserving and creating space for culture. In this sense, the Nature Futures Framework becomes a heuristic and an entry point for visioning and assessing radical yet plausible pathways towards living in harmony with nature. 
  Figure 5.10. An illustrative Nature Futures Framework in the Brazilian Amazon of Pará State for assessing the potential consequences of different policies on nature and people. Source: Adapted from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EaTT75PP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Siqueira-Gay, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Siqueira-Gay, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1667,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YFUBRG42"],"itemData":{"id":1667,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Mining is a significant driver of deforestation. Not only do mines clear native forests for mineral extraction, they also often establish new infrastructure, which indirectly facilitates new access to land and further clearing. Forest loss and fragmentation have serious effects on biodiversity, yet rarely are these cumulative impacts of mining studied at the regional scale. Here, we examine potential impacts of mining in a biodiverse region of the Brazilian Amazon. The National Reserve of Copper and Associates (“Renca”) is currently off limits to mining activities but was recently threatened with a move to permit mineral exploration. We analyzed historic forest loss and fragmentation within two mining regions neighboring Renca. We also investigated historic deforestation trajectories within Renca's protected areas, to determine how well conserved these forests are against current threats. We found that mining, and other infrastructure associated with mines (i.e. roads), caused significant forest loss and fragmentation within neighboring mining sectors, and that Renca's protected areas are not currently immune to forest loss. Permitting new mines within and surrounding Renca will place additional pressure on its biodiversity. If mineral development is to proceed, huge regulatory changes will be required to effectively manage negative impacts on forests and biodiversity. Environmental Impact Assessments for new mining projects must assess and mitigate the cumulative region-wide effects on forests, while existing protected areas must be strengthened to ensure they are not directly or indirectly compromised by mining activities.","container-title":"Resources Policy","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.resourpol.2020.101662","ISSN":"0301-4207","page":"101662","title":"Exploring potential impacts of mining on forest loss and fragmentation within a biodiverse region of Brazil's northeastern Amazon","volume":"67","author":[{"family":"Siqueira-Gay","given":"Juliana"},{"family":"Sonter","given":"Laura J."},{"family":"Sánchez","given":"Luis E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. © 2020 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved. License number 5293081246924.
