	The experts coded the 100 selected case studies, to allow for a systematic analysis to compare individual cases across policy instrument, practice, and geography (Table 6.2), allowing them to postulate that certain attributes can be taken as criteria for assessing causality, e.g., (1) strength of association between the policy instruments application and sustainable use outcomes; (2) consistency of association in various conditions across ecosystems, practices and local contexts; (3) plausibility of causal explanations; (4) coherence with paradigms and knowledge of each practice; and (5) temporality, where presence of attributes preceded success ) (see data management report et https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4663236)ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"afl8qi0c0o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"\\\\uldash{(Hicks, 2022)}","plainCitation":"(Hicks, 2022)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"RflDKERw/fFBtmZtu","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PK46XH4J"],"itemData":{"id":27987,"type":"report","abstract":"This data management report explains the process for conducting the systematic review on policy effectiveness for the sustainable use of wild species, as part of Chapter 6. Policy options for governing sustainable use of wild species, in the the thematic assessment of the sustainable use of wild species of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services.","language":"en","note":"version: draft\\nDOI: 10.5281/ZENODO.4663236","publisher":"Zenodo","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"IPBES Sustainable Use of Wild Species Assessment - Chapter 6 - Data management report for the systematic review on policy effectiveness for the sustainable use of wild species","URL":"https://zenodo.org/record/4663236","author":[{"family":"Hicks","given":"Christina"},{"family":"Park","given":"Mi Sun"},{"family":"Avila-Foucat","given":"Véronique Sophie"},{"family":"Dhyani","given":"Shalini"},{"family":"Islas","given":"Ca