There have been very few international assessments targeting the sustainable use of wild species and those past reports focused only on a few taxa and/or case studies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aDCYHiiy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Prescott-Allen & Prescott-Allen, 1996)","plainCitation":"(Prescott-Allen & Prescott-Allen, 1996)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30209,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8UBUSNEK"],"itemData":{"id":30209,"type":"book","call-number":"QH705 .A77 1996","collection-number":"no. 12","collection-title":"Occasional paper of the IUCN Species Survival Commission","event-place":"Gland","ISBN":"978-2-8317-0287-2","number-of-pages":"135","publisher":"IUCN","publisher-place":"Gland","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Assessing the sustainability of uses of wild species: case studies and initial assessment procedure","title-short":"Assessing the sustainability of uses of wild species","author":[{"family":"Prescott-Allen","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Prescott-Allen","given":"Christine"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nonetheless, previous biodiversity and sectoral assessments offer valuable insights into uses of wild species, their contributions to human wellbeing, and factors contributing to long-term prospects for their sustainable use. The sustainable use assessment builds on global assessments produced by IPBES, United Nations scientific organizations, and other global  organizations. It also includes one national assessment dedicated to non-timber forest products, since the practice of gathering (1.3.3) was not thoroughly reflected in the other global assessments. These assessments address human uses of wild species and factors contributing to their sustainability (or lack thereof) as a primary or secondary focus. 
      Collectively, the assessments document widespread global dependence on wild species for food, medicine, and other purposes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IK7HI0fH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2018c, 2018b, 2019, 2020b, 2020a; HLPE, 2017; IPBES, 2018a; Vira, 2015; WHO & CBD, 2015)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2018c, 2018b, 2019, 2020b, 2020a; HLPE, 2017; IPBES, 2018a; Vira, 2015; WHO & CBD, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NIZN4X56","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M2WPUUSR"],"itemData":{"id":7733,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development\\n(2030 Agenda for short) offers a vision of a\\nfairer, more peaceful world in which no one is\\nleft behind. The 2030 Agenda also sets aims for\\nthe contribution and conduct of fisheries and\\naquaculture towards food security and nutrition,\\nand the sector’s use of natural resources, in a\\nway that ensures sustainable development in\\neconomic, social and environmental terms,\\nwithin the context of the FAO Code of Conduct\\nfor Responsible Fisheries (FAO, 1995). A major\\nchallenge to implementation of the 2030 Agenda\\nis the sustainability divide between developed\\nand developing countries which has partially\\nresulted from increased economic\\ninterdependencies, coupled with limited\\nmanagement and governance capacity in\\ndeveloping countries. To eliminate this disparity\\nwhile making progress towards the target for\\nrestoration of overfished stocks set by the 2030\\nAgenda, the global community needs to support\\ndeveloping nations to achieve their full fisheries\\nand aquaculture potential. \\n\\nReport is rich in data.","event-place":"Rome","language":"English","note":"978-92-5-130562-1","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture 2018 - Meeting the sustainable development goals","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/I9540EN/i9540en.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEAU4TNP"],"itemData":{"id":35107,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"eng","note":"978-92-5-130561-4","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"The State of the world's forests 2018 - Forests pathways to sustainable development","editor":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AFH3MZXV"],"itemData":{"id":35102,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","page":"572","publisher":"Commission on Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of the world's biodiversity for food and agriculture","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/CA3129EN/CA3129EN.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Bélanger","given":"J."},{"family":"Pilling","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":"BOVAcHko/MTNp8Tsl","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A6FP6AF5"],"itemData":{"id":123716,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132974-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1195470916","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Global Forest Resources Assessment (FRA) 2020: main report","title-short":"GLOBAL FOREST RESOURCES ASSESSMENT (FRA) 2020","URL":"https://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8753en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30174,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HC35DGNH"],"itemData":{"id":30174,"type":"report","abstract":"This report is an evidence-based, comprehensive analysis of the diverse, direct and indirect, contributions of forests and trees to FSN. Chapter 1 examines the linkages between forests\\nand FSN and proposes, for the purpose of this report, a conceptual framework and a forest typology grounded on management criteria. Chapter 2 provides an in-depth analysis of the channels through which forests and trees contribute to FSN. Chapter 3 reviews the state of the world’s forests and identifies challenges and opportunities for forestry in relation to FSN. Chapter 4 is solution-oriented and discusses how to optimize the contributions of forests and trees to FSN in a sustainable manner.","collection-title":"HLPE Report Series","event-place":"Rome","genre":"Report of the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition","language":"English","note":"I739EN/1/06.17","number":"HLPE Report 11","page":"136","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Zotero","title":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition:  A report by the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition of the Committee on World Food Security","title-short":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/a-i7395e.pdf","author":[{"family":"HLPE","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",6]]}}},{"id":35018,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NHSBQV7E","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EY6CU4ER"],"itemData":{"id":35018,"type":"book","abstract":"Reproduction This publication may be reproduced in whole or in part and in any form for educational or non-profit services without special permission from the copyright holder, provided acknowledgement of the source is made. The IPBES secretariat would appreciate receiving a copy of any publication that uses this publication as a source. No use of this publication may be made for resale or any other commercial purpose whatsoever without prior permission in writing from the IPBES secretariat. Applications for such permission, with a statement of the purpose and extent of the reproduction, should be addressed to the IPBES secretariat. The use of information from this publication concerning proprietary products for publicity or advertising is not permitted. Traceable accounts The chapter references enclosed in curly brackets (e.g. {2.3.1, 2.3.1.2, 2.3.1.3}) are traceable accounts and refer to sections of the chapters of the IPBES Regional Assessment Report on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services for Africa. A traceable account is a description within the corresponding texts of these chapters, reflecting the evaluation of the type, amount, quality, and consistency of evidence and the degree of agreement for that particular statement or key finding.","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-00-3","number-of-pages":"49","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the regional assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services for Africa of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"http://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3236178","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"literal":"E. Archer"},{"literal":"L. E. Dziba"},{"literal":"K. J. Mulongoy"},{"literal":"M. A. Maoela"},{"literal":"M. Walters"},{"literal":"R. Biggs"},{"literal":"M-C. Cormier-Salem"},{"literal":"F. DeClerck"},{"literal":"M. C. Diaw"},{"literal":"A. E. Dunham"},{"literal":"P. Failler"},{"literal":"C. Gordon"},{"literal":"K. A. Harhash"},{"literal":"R. Kasisi"},{"literal":"F. Kizito"},{"literal":"W. D. Nyingi"},{"literal":"N. Oguge"},{"literal":"B. Osman-Elasha"},{"literal":"L. C. Stringer"},{"literal":"L. Tito de Morais"},{"literal":"A. Assogbadjo"},{"literal":"B. N. Egoh"},{"literal":"M. W. Halmy"},{"literal":"K. Heubach"},{"literal":"A. Mensah"},{"literal":"L. Pereira"},{"literal":"N. Sitas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4CNIVICV"],"itemData":{"id":35087,"type":"report","abstract":"As population estimates for 2050 reach over 9 billion, issues of food security and nutrition have been dominating academic and policy debates, especially in relation to the global development agenda beyond 2015. A total of 805 million people are undernourished worldwide and malnutrition affects nearly every country on the planet. There is considerable evidence that forests and tree-based systems can play an important role in complementing agricultural production in providing better and more nutritionally-balanced diets; wood fueld for cooking; greater control over food consumption choices, particularly during lean seasons and periods of vulnerability (especially for marginalised groups); and deliver a broad set of ecosystem services which enhance and support crop production. While precise figures are difficult to come by, it has been estimated that approximately 1.2-1.5 biooion people (just under 20 percent of the global population) are forest dependent. These estimates include about 60 million indigenous people who are almost wholly dependent on forests. The loss and degradation of forests exacerbate the problem of food insecurity both directly and indirectly. However, the complex, overlapping and interconnecting processes which link tree products and services to food security and nutrition are currently not adequately represented in forestry, agriculture, food or nutrition-related strategies at global and national levels, though their importance is often well known at more local scales by consumers, forest producers and farmers.    While the evidence base for the role of forests and tree-based systems for food security and nutrition is growing, there remain many gaps in our understanding of this relationship and its potential contribution to reducing global hunger and malnutrition. There is a need to explore the forest-food nexus in much more detail, particularly in relation to the integrated management of multi-functional landscapes, ad the multi-scalar and cross-sectoral governance approaches that are required for the equitable deliver of these benefits. This report documents evidence of the relationships between forests and tree-based systems and food security and nutrition from different agro-ecological zones in all continents. The systems included inour analysis include management of forests to optimise yields of wild foods and fodder.","collection-title":"IUFRO World Series","event-place":"Vienna","genre":"IUFRO World Series","language":"English","note":"978-3-902762-40-5","number":"Volume 33","page":"172","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","publisher-place":"Vienna","title":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition. A global assessment report","title-short":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition","URL":"https://www.iufro.org/science/gfep/forests-and-food-security-panel/report/","author":[{"family":"Vira","given":"Bhaskar"},{"family":"Wildburger","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Mansourian","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":1402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MRNUQFSG"],"itemData":{"id":1402,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Brussels/Montreal, 4 June 2015. Healthy communities rely on well-functioning ecosystems. They provide clean air, fresh water, medicines and food security. They also limit disease and stabilize the climate. But biodiversity loss is happening at unprecedented rates, impacting human health worldwide, according to a new state of knowledge review of the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) and World Health Organization (WHO). The report, Connecting Global Priorities: Biodiversity and Human Health, launched today in Brussels at Green Week 2015, Europe's biggest annual conference on environmental policy, focuses on the complex and multi-faceted connections between biodiversity and human health, and how the loss of biodiversity and corresponding ecosystem services may negatively influence health. One of the first integrative reviews of its kind, the report brings together knowledge from several scientific disciplines, including public health, conservation, agriculture, epidemiology and development. 'This state of knowledge review is a significant contribution to our understanding of the complex relationships between biodiversity and health,' said Braulio Ferreira de Souza Dias, Executive Secretary of the Convention on Biological Diversity and Assistant Secretary-General of the United Nations. 'Increasing our knowledge enables us to develop effective solutions capable of strengthening ecosystem resilience and mitigating the forces that impede their ability to deliver life-supporting services.' All aspects of human well-being depend on ecosystem goods and services, which in turn depend on biodiversity. Biodiversity loss can destabilize ecosystems, promote outbreaks of infectious disease, and undermine development progress, nutrition, security and protection from natural disasters,' said Dr Maria Neira, WHO Director, Department of Public Health, Environmental and Social Determinants of Health. 'Protecting public health from these risks lies outside of the traditional roles of the Health sector. We are ready to work with other sectors to bring about change. ' Access to sufficient quantity, quality and diversity of foods, clean air, freshwater, medicines and healthcare are not only central to maintaining healthy populations, they are foundational pillars of sustainable development. Meeting these needs while facing the persistent challenges of biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, emerging disease pandemics and shifting disease burdens is not an insurmountable feat, but it does require concerted action, based on robust evidence and coordinated cross-sector solutions. This comprehensive report brings this knowledge to the forefront, demonstrating that biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation and ill health often share common threats and pointing toward innovative, mutually-supportive and cross-sectoral solutions. This contribution is especially timely as governments finalize agreement on the post-2015 development agenda and Sustainable Development Goals and prepare to reach new global commitments to tackle climate change by the end of 2015. Among the shared threats identified throughout the report, land-use change is identified as an important driver of biodiversity loss with concurrent implications for many of our most pressing public health challenges. For example, land use change through deforestation is a leading driver of disease emergence in humans and is believed to have contributed to the recent Ebola outbreak in West Africa. While the resulting development of our freshwater and terrestrial resources is also associated with some health benefits, these are unevenly distributed, often to the detriment of our most vulnerable populations and carry unintended consequences. For example, under unsustainable conditions, industrial agricultural practices in many parts of the world may also exacerbate biodiversity loss, pest and disease outbreaks, micronutrient deficiencies, antibiotic resistance and the impacts of climate change on the health. These outcomes are not inevitable. They can be averted through concerted global efforts, by connecting our vast scientific and local knowledge, and developing coherent, cross-sectoral policy priorities toward a healthier, more equitable, sustainable future. Contributions from over 100 scientists and practitioners working in global public health and biodiversity conservation policy were included in the report, including experts from Bioversity International, COHAB Initiative, DIVERSITAS, EcoHealth Alliance, Harvard School of Public Health, the Oswaldo Cruz Foundation, IUCN, United Nations University, and the Wildlife Conservation Society.\\"--Press release","ISBN":"978-92-4-150853-7","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 910881539","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Connecting global priorities: biodiversity and human health: a state of knowledge review.","title-short":"Connecting global priorities","URL":"http://apps.who.int/iris/bitstream/10665/174012/1/9789241508537_eng.pdf?ua=1","author":[{"literal":"WHO"},{"literal":"CBD"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Contributions of use of wild species to good quality of life include direct provisioning, sources of income, and energy, the latter especially for cooking and sanitizing water ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"E2RfJamc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2018c, 2018b, 2020b, 2020a; HLPE, 2017; IPBES, 2016, 2018a, 2018b; Vira, 2015)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2018c, 2018b, 2020b, 2020a; HLPE, 2017; IPBES, 2016, 2018a, 2018b; Vira, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NIZN4X56","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M2WPUUSR"],"itemData":{"id":7733,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development\\n(2030 Agenda for short) offers a vision of a\\nfairer, more peaceful world in which no one is\\nleft behind. The 2030 Agenda also sets aims for\\nthe contribution and conduct of fisheries and\\naquaculture towards food security and nutrition,\\nand the sector’s use of natural resources, in a\\nway that ensures sustainable development in\\neconomic, social and environmental terms,\\nwithin the context of the FAO Code of Conduct\\nfor Responsible Fisheries (FAO, 1995). A major\\nchallenge to implementation of the 2030 Agenda\\nis the sustainability divide between developed\\nand developing countries which has partially\\nresulted from increased economic\\ninterdependencies, coupled with limited\\nmanagement and governance capacity in\\ndeveloping countries. To eliminate this disparity\\nwhile making progress towards the target for\\nrestoration of overfished stocks set by the 2030\\nAgenda, the global community needs to support\\ndeveloping nations to achieve their full fisheries\\nand aquaculture potential. \\n\\nReport is rich in data.","event-place":"Rome","language":"English","note":"978-92-5-130562-1","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture 2018 - Meeting the sustainable development goals","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/I9540EN/i9540en.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEAU4TNP"],"itemData":{"id":35107,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"eng","note":"978-92-5-130561-4","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"The State of the world's forests 2018 - Forests pathways to sustainable development","editor":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":"BOVAcHko/MTNp8Tsl","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A6FP6AF5"],"itemData":{"id":123716,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132974-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1195470916","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Global Forest Resources Assessment (FRA) 2020: main report","title-short":"GLOBAL FOREST RESOURCES ASSESSMENT (FRA) 2020","URL":"https://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8753en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30174,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HC35DGNH"],"itemData":{"id":30174,"type":"report","abstract":"This report is an evidence-based, comprehensive analysis of the diverse, direct and indirect, contributions of forests and trees to FSN. Chapter 1 examines the linkages between forests\\nand FSN and proposes, for the purpose of this report, a conceptual framework and a forest typology grounded on management criteria. Chapter 2 provides an in-depth analysis of the channels through which forests and trees contribute to FSN. Chapter 3 reviews the state of the world’s forests and identifies challenges and opportunities for forestry in relation to FSN. Chapter 4 is solution-oriented and discusses how to optimize the contributions of forests and trees to FSN in a sustainable manner.","collection-title":"HLPE Report Series","event-place":"Rome","genre":"Report of the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition","language":"English","note":"I739EN/1/06.17","number":"HLPE Report 11","page":"136","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Zotero","title":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition:  A report by the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition of the Committee on World Food Security","title-short":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/a-i7395e.pdf","author":[{"family":"HLPE","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",6]]}}},{"id":35028,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YG7LF893","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AGYQARR9"],"itemData":{"id":35028,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-92-807-3568-0","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","title":"Summary for policymakers of the assessment report of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services on pollinators, pollination and food production.","URL":"https://www.ipbes.net/sites/default/files/spm_deliverable_3a_pollination_20170222.pdf","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"Potts","given":"S. G."},{"family":"Imperatriz-Fonseca","given":"V. L."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H. T."},{"family":"Biesmeijer","given":"J. C."},{"family":"Breeze","given":"T. D."},{"family":"Dicks","given":"L. V."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Hill","given":"R."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Vanbergen","given":"A. J."},{"family":"Aizen","given":"M. A."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"S. A."},{"family":"Eardley","given":"C."},{"family":"Freitas","given":"B. M."},{"family":"Gallai","given":"N."},{"family":"Kevan","given":"P. G."},{"family":"Kovács-Hostyánszki","given":"A."},{"family":"Kwapong","given":"P. K."},{"family":"Li","given":"J."},{"family":"Li","given":"X."},{"family":"Martins","given":"D. J."},{"family":"Nates-Parra","given":"G."},{"family":"J. S. Pettis","given":"R. Rader"},{"family":"Viana","given":"B. F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":35018,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NHSBQV7E","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EY6CU4ER"],"itemData":{"id":35018,"type":"book","abstract":"Reproduction This publication may be reproduced in whole or in part and in any form for educational or non-profit services without special permission from the copyright holder, provided acknowledgement of the source is made. The IPBES secretariat would appreciate receiving a copy of any publication that uses this publication as a source. No use of this publication may be made for resale or any other commercial purpose whatsoever without prior permission in writing from the IPBES secretariat. Applications for such permission, with a statement of the purpose and extent of the reproduction, should be addressed to the IPBES secretariat. The use of information from this publication concerning proprietary products for publicity or advertising is not permitted. Traceable accounts The chapter references enclosed in curly brackets (e.g. {2.3.1, 2.3.1.2, 2.3.1.3}) are traceable accounts and refer to sections of the chapters of the IPBES Regional Assessment Report on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services for Africa. A traceable account is a description within the corresponding texts of these chapters, reflecting the evaluation of the type, amount, quality, and consistency of evidence and the degree of agreement for that particular statement or key finding.","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-00-3","number-of-pages":"49","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the regional assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services for Africa of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"http://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3236178","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"literal":"E. Archer"},{"literal":"L. E. Dziba"},{"literal":"K. J. Mulongoy"},{"literal":"M. A. Maoela"},{"literal":"M. Walters"},{"literal":"R. Biggs"},{"literal":"M-C. Cormier-Salem"},{"literal":"F. DeClerck"},{"literal":"M. C. Diaw"},{"literal":"A. E. Dunham"},{"literal":"P. Failler"},{"literal":"C. Gordon"},{"literal":"K. A. Harhash"},{"literal":"R. Kasisi"},{"literal":"F. Kizito"},{"literal":"W. D. Nyingi"},{"literal":"N. Oguge"},{"literal":"B. Osman-Elasha"},{"literal":"L. C. Stringer"},{"literal":"L. Tito de Morais"},{"literal":"A. Assogbadjo"},{"literal":"B. N. Egoh"},{"literal":"M. W. Halmy"},{"literal":"K. Heubach"},{"literal":"A. Mensah"},{"literal":"L. Pereira"},{"literal":"N. Sitas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35013,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XW6KN3MC","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GWN68BXX"],"itemData":{"id":35013,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-02-7","number-of-pages":"41","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the regional assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services for Asia and the Pacific of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3237373","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"literal":"M. Karki"},{"literal":"S. Senaratna Sellamuttu"},{"literal":"S. Okayasu"},{"literal":"W. Suzuki"},{"literal":"L. A. Acosta"},{"literal":"Y. Alhafedh"},{"literal":"J. A. Anticamara"},{"literal":"A. G. Ausseil"},{"literal":"K. Davies"},{"literal":"A. Gasparatos"},{"literal":"H. Gundimeda"},{"literal":"I. Faridah-Hanum"},{"literal":"R. Kohsaka"},{"literal":"R. Kumar"},{"literal":"S. Managi"},{"literal":"N. Wu"},{"literal":"A. Rajvanshi"},{"literal":"G. S. Rawat"},{"literal":"P. Riordan"},{"literal":"S. Sharma"},{"literal":"A. Virk"},{"literal":"C. Wang"},{"literal":"T. Yahara"},{"literal":"Y. C. Youn"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4CNIVICV"],"itemData":{"id":35087,"type":"report","abstract":"As population estimates for 2050 reach over 9 billion, issues of food security and nutrition have been dominating academic and policy debates, especially in relation to the global development agenda beyond 2015. A total of 805 million people are undernourished worldwide and malnutrition affects nearly every country on the planet. There is considerable evidence that forests and tree-based systems can play an important role in complementing agricultural production in providing better and more nutritionally-balanced diets; wood fueld for cooking; greater control over food consumption choices, particularly during lean seasons and periods of vulnerability (especially for marginalised groups); and deliver a broad set of ecosystem services which enhance and support crop production. While precise figures are difficult to come by, it has been estimated that approximately 1.2-1.5 biooion people (just under 20 percent of the global population) are forest dependent. These estimates include about 60 million indigenous people who are almost wholly dependent on forests. The loss and degradation of forests exacerbate the problem of food insecurity both directly and indirectly. However, the complex, overlapping and interconnecting processes which link tree products and services to food security and nutrition are currently not adequately represented in forestry, agriculture, food or nutrition-related strategies at global and national levels, though their importance is often well known at more local scales by consumers, forest producers and farmers.    While the evidence base for the role of forests and tree-based systems for food security and nutrition is growing, there remain many gaps in our understanding of this relationship and its potential contribution to reducing global hunger and malnutrition. There is a need to explore the forest-food nexus in much more detail, particularly in relation to the integrated management of multi-functional landscapes, ad the multi-scalar and cross-sectoral governance approaches that are required for the equitable deliver of these benefits. This report documents evidence of the relationships between forests and tree-based systems and food security and nutrition from different agro-ecological zones in all continents. The systems included inour analysis include management of forests to optimise yields of wild foods and fodder.","collection-title":"IUFRO World Series","event-place":"Vienna","genre":"IUFRO World Series","language":"English","note":"978-3-902762-40-5","number":"Volume 33","page":"172","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","publisher-place":"Vienna","title":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition. A global assessment report","title-short":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition","URL":"https://www.iufro.org/science/gfep/forests-and-food-security-panel/report/","author":[{"family":"Vira","given":"Bhaskar"},{"family":"Wildburger","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Mansourian","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For many indigenous peoples and local communities, the use of wild species also are essential to identity and culture ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jl6dD3n7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018; IPBES, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018; IPBES, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1448,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SERE3KJN"],"itemData":{"id":1448,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Nontimber forest products (NTFPs) are fundamental to the functioning of healthy forests and play vital roles in the cultures and economies of the people of the United States. However, these plants and fungi used for food, medicine, and other purposes have not been fully incorporated into management, policy, and resource valuation. This report is a forest-sectorwide assessment of the state of the knowledge regarding NTFPs science and management information for U.S. forests and rangelands (and hereafter referred to as the NTFP assessment). The NTFP assessment serves as a baseline science synthesis and provides information for managing nontimber forest resources in the United States. In addition, this NTFP assessment provides information for national-level reporting on natural capital and the ecosystem services NTFPs provide. The report also provides technical input to the 2017 National Climate Assessment (NCA) under development by the U.S. Global Change Research Program (USGCRP).","DOI":"10.2737/SRS-GTR-232","issue":"June","page":"267","title":"Assessment of Nontimber Forest Products in the United States Under Changing Conditions. A report for the United States Department of Agriculture. General Technical Report SRS-232.","URL":"https://doi.org/10.2737/SRS-GTR-232","author":[{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla. R."},{"family":"Patel-Weynard","given":"Toral."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35028,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YG7LF893","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AGYQARR9"],"itemData":{"id":35028,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-92-807-3568-0","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","title":"Summary for policymakers of the assessment report of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services on pollinators, pollination and food production.","URL":"https://www.ipbes.net/sites/default/files/spm_deliverable_3a_pollination_20170222.pdf","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"Potts","given":"S. G."},{"family":"Imperatriz-Fonseca","given":"V. L."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H. T."},{"family":"Biesmeijer","given":"J. C."},{"family":"Breeze","given":"T. D."},{"family":"Dicks","given":"L. V."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Hill","given":"R."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Vanbergen","given":"A. J."},{"family":"Aizen","given":"M. A."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"S. A."},{"family":"Eardley","given":"C."},{"family":"Freitas","given":"B. M."},{"family":"Gallai","given":"N."},{"family":"Kevan","given":"P. G."},{"family":"Kovács-Hostyánszki","given":"A."},{"family":"Kwapong","given":"P. K."},{"family":"Li","given":"J."},{"family":"Li","given":"X."},{"family":"Martins","given":"D. J."},{"family":"Nates-Parra","given":"G."},{"family":"J. S. Pettis","given":"R. Rader"},{"family":"Viana","given":"B. F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These contributions of use of wild species to human wellbeing are continuous and quotidian, as well as episodic and exceptional. 
      The International Union of Forest Research Organizations estimates that nearly 20 % of the global population directly depend on forests, tree-based systems, and the wild species in them ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pap7QIXh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vira, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Vira, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4CNIVICV"],"itemData":{"id":35087,"type":"report","abstract":"As population estimates for 2050 reach over 9 billion, issues of food security and nutrition have been dominating academic and policy debates, especially in relation to the global development agenda beyond 2015. A total of 805 million people are undernourished worldwide and malnutrition affects nearly every country on the planet. There is considerable evidence that forests and tree-based systems can play an important role in complementing agricultural production in providing better and more nutritionally-balanced diets; wood fueld for cooking; greater control over food consumption choices, particularly during lean seasons and periods of vulnerability (especially for marginalised groups); and deliver a broad set of ecosystem services which enhance and support crop production. While precise figures are difficult to come by, it has been estimated that approximately 1.2-1.5 biooion people (just under 20 percent of the global population) are forest dependent. These estimates include about 60 million indigenous people who are almost wholly dependent on forests. The loss and degradation of forests exacerbate the problem of food insecurity both directly and indirectly. However, the complex, overlapping and interconnecting processes which link tree products and services to food security and nutrition are currently not adequately represented in forestry, agriculture, food or nutrition-related strategies at global and national levels, though their importance is often well known at more local scales by consumers, forest producers and farmers.    While the evidence base for the role of forests and tree-based systems for food security and nutrition is growing, there remain many gaps in our understanding of this relationship and its potential contribution to reducing global hunger and malnutrition. There is a need to explore the forest-food nexus in much more detail, particularly in relation to the integrated management of multi-functional landscapes, ad the multi-scalar and cross-sectoral governance approaches that are required for the equitable deliver of these benefits. This report documents evidence of the relationships between forests and tree-based systems and food security and nutrition from different agro-ecological zones in all continents. The systems included inour analysis include management of forests to optimise yields of wild foods and fodder.","collection-title":"IUFRO World Series","event-place":"Vienna","genre":"IUFRO World Series","language":"English","note":"978-3-902762-40-5","number":"Volume 33","page":"172","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","publisher-place":"Vienna","title":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition. A global assessment report","title-short":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition","URL":"https://www.iufro.org/science/gfep/forests-and-food-security-panel/report/","author":[{"family":"Vira","given":"Bhaskar"},{"family":"Wildburger","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Mansourian","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, while the FAO considers that fisheries and aquaculture support the livelihoods of 12 % of people around the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sCY6silj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2018c, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2018c, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NIZN4X56","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M2WPUUSR"],"itemData":{"id":7733,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development\\n(2030 Agenda for short) offers a vision of a\\nfairer, more peaceful world in which no one is\\nleft behind. The 2030 Agenda also sets aims for\\nthe contribution and conduct of fisheries and\\naquaculture towards food security and nutrition,\\nand the sector’s use of natural resources, in a\\nway that ensures sustainable development in\\neconomic, social and environmental terms,\\nwithin the context of the FAO Code of Conduct\\nfor Responsible Fisheries (FAO, 1995). A major\\nchallenge to implementation of the 2030 Agenda\\nis the sustainability divide between developed\\nand developing countries which has partially\\nresulted from increased economic\\ninterdependencies, coupled with limited\\nmanagement and governance capacity in\\ndeveloping countries. To eliminate this disparity\\nwhile making progress towards the target for\\nrestoration of overfished stocks set by the 2030\\nAgenda, the global community needs to support\\ndeveloping nations to achieve their full fisheries\\nand aquaculture potential. \\n\\nReport is rich in data.","event-place":"Rome","language":"English","note":"978-92-5-130562-1","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture 2018 - Meeting the sustainable development goals","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/I9540EN/i9540en.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition to serving as primary sources of subsistence resources, income, and identity, the use of wild species also serve as safety nets enabling survival during seasonal shortfalls and crises provoked by anthropogenic (e.g., armed conflicts) and non-anthropogenic (e.g., slow and rapid onset natural disasters) forces (see Chapter 4; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QQgVFOsc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2015, 2018b, 2019, 2020a; HLPE, 2017; Vira, 2015; WHO & CBD, 2015)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2015, 2018b, 2019, 2020a; HLPE, 2017; Vira, 2015; WHO & CBD, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34531,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JQD8NIXN"],"itemData":{"id":34531,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-108704-6","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 911397186","number-of-pages":"34","publisher":"FAO","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Voluntary guidelines for securing sustainable small-scale fisheries in the context of food security and poverty eradication.","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/a-i4356en.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":35107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEAU4TNP"],"itemData":{"id":35107,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"eng","note":"978-92-5-130561-4","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"The State of the world's forests 2018 - Forests pathways to sustainable development","editor":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AFH3MZXV"],"itemData":{"id":35102,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","page":"572","publisher":"Commission on Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of the world's biodiversity for food and agriculture","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/CA3129EN/CA3129EN.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Bélanger","given":"J."},{"family":"Pilling","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":"BOVAcHko/MTNp8Tsl","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A6FP6AF5"],"itemData":{"id":123716,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132974-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1195470916","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Global Forest Resources Assessment (FRA) 2020: main report","title-short":"GLOBAL FOREST RESOURCES ASSESSMENT (FRA) 2020","URL":"https://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8753en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30174,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HC35DGNH"],"itemData":{"id":30174,"type":"report","abstract":"This report is an evidence-based, comprehensive analysis of the diverse, direct and indirect, contributions of forests and trees to FSN. Chapter 1 examines the linkages between forests\\nand FSN and proposes, for the purpose of this report, a conceptual framework and a forest typology grounded on management criteria. Chapter 2 provides an in-depth analysis of the channels through which forests and trees contribute to FSN. Chapter 3 reviews the state of the world’s forests and identifies challenges and opportunities for forestry in relation to FSN. Chapter 4 is solution-oriented and discusses how to optimize the contributions of forests and trees to FSN in a sustainable manner.","collection-title":"HLPE Report Series","event-place":"Rome","genre":"Report of the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition","language":"English","note":"I739EN/1/06.17","number":"HLPE Report 11","page":"136","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Zotero","title":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition:  A report by the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition of the Committee on World Food Security","title-short":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/a-i7395e.pdf","author":[{"family":"HLPE","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",6]]}}},{"id":35087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4CNIVICV"],"itemData":{"id":35087,"type":"report","abstract":"As population estimates for 2050 reach over 9 billion, issues of food security and nutrition have been dominating academic and policy debates, especially in relation to the global development agenda beyond 2015. A total of 805 million people are undernourished worldwide and malnutrition affects nearly every country on the planet. There is considerable evidence that forests and tree-based systems can play an important role in complementing agricultural production in providing better and more nutritionally-balanced diets; wood fueld for cooking; greater control over food consumption choices, particularly during lean seasons and periods of vulnerability (especially for marginalised groups); and deliver a broad set of ecosystem services which enhance and support crop production. While precise figures are difficult to come by, it has been estimated that approximately 1.2-1.5 biooion people (just under 20 percent of the global population) are forest dependent. These estimates include about 60 million indigenous people who are almost wholly dependent on forests. The loss and degradation of forests exacerbate the problem of food insecurity both directly and indirectly. However, the complex, overlapping and interconnecting processes which link tree products and services to food security and nutrition are currently not adequately represented in forestry, agriculture, food or nutrition-related strategies at global and national levels, though their importance is often well known at more local scales by consumers, forest producers and farmers.    While the evidence base for the role of forests and tree-based systems for food security and nutrition is growing, there remain many gaps in our understanding of this relationship and its potential contribution to reducing global hunger and malnutrition. There is a need to explore the forest-food nexus in much more detail, particularly in relation to the integrated management of multi-functional landscapes, ad the multi-scalar and cross-sectoral governance approaches that are required for the equitable deliver of these benefits. This report documents evidence of the relationships between forests and tree-based systems and food security and nutrition from different agro-ecological zones in all continents. The systems included inour analysis include management of forests to optimise yields of wild foods and fodder.","collection-title":"IUFRO World Series","event-place":"Vienna","genre":"IUFRO World Series","language":"English","note":"978-3-902762-40-5","number":"Volume 33","page":"172","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","publisher-place":"Vienna","title":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition. A global assessment report","title-short":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition","URL":"https://www.iufro.org/science/gfep/forests-and-food-security-panel/report/","author":[{"family":"Vira","given":"Bhaskar"},{"family":"Wildburger","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Mansourian","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":1402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MRNUQFSG"],"itemData":{"id":1402,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Brussels/Montreal, 4 June 2015. Healthy communities rely on well-functioning ecosystems. They provide clean air, fresh water, medicines and food security. They also limit disease and stabilize the climate. But biodiversity loss is happening at unprecedented rates, impacting human health worldwide, according to a new state of knowledge review of the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) and World Health Organization (WHO). The report, Connecting Global Priorities: Biodiversity and Human Health, launched today in Brussels at Green Week 2015, Europe's biggest annual conference on environmental policy, focuses on the complex and multi-faceted connections between biodiversity and human health, and how the loss of biodiversity and corresponding ecosystem services may negatively influence health. One of the first integrative reviews of its kind, the report brings together knowledge from several scientific disciplines, including public health, conservation, agriculture, epidemiology and development. 'This state of knowledge review is a significant contribution to our understanding of the complex relationships between biodiversity and health,' said Braulio Ferreira de Souza Dias, Executive Secretary of the Convention on Biological Diversity and Assistant Secretary-General of the United Nations. 'Increasing our knowledge enables us to develop effective solutions capable of strengthening ecosystem resilience and mitigating the forces that impede their ability to deliver life-supporting services.' All aspects of human well-being depend on ecosystem goods and services, which in turn depend on biodiversity. Biodiversity loss can destabilize ecosystems, promote outbreaks of infectious disease, and undermine development progress, nutrition, security and protection from natural disasters,' said Dr Maria Neira, WHO Director, Department of Public Health, Environmental and Social Determinants of Health. 'Protecting public health from these risks lies outside of the traditional roles of the Health sector. We are ready to work with other sectors to bring about change. ' Access to sufficient quantity, quality and diversity of foods, clean air, freshwater, medicines and healthcare are not only central to maintaining healthy populations, they are foundational pillars of sustainable development. Meeting these needs while facing the persistent challenges of biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, emerging disease pandemics and shifting disease burdens is not an insurmountable feat, but it does require concerted action, based on robust evidence and coordinated cross-sector solutions. This comprehensive report brings this knowledge to the forefront, demonstrating that biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation and ill health often share common threats and pointing toward innovative, mutually-supportive and cross-sectoral solutions. This contribution is especially timely as governments finalize agreement on the post-2015 development agenda and Sustainable Development Goals and prepare to reach new global commitments to tackle climate change by the end of 2015. Among the shared threats identified throughout the report, land-use change is identified as an important driver of biodiversity loss with concurrent implications for many of our most pressing public health challenges. For example, land use change through deforestation is a leading driver of disease emergence in humans and is believed to have contributed to the recent Ebola outbreak in West Africa. While the resulting development of our freshwater and terrestrial resources is also associated with some health benefits, these are unevenly distributed, often to the detriment of our most vulnerable populations and carry unintended consequences. For example, under unsustainable conditions, industrial agricultural practices in many parts of the world may also exacerbate biodiversity loss, pest and disease outbreaks, micronutrient deficiencies, antibiotic resistance and the impacts of climate change on the health. These outcomes are not inevitable. They can be averted through concerted global efforts, by connecting our vast scientific and local knowledge, and developing coherent, cross-sectoral policy priorities toward a healthier, more equitable, sustainable future. Contributions from over 100 scientists and practitioners working in global public health and biodiversity conservation policy were included in the report, including experts from Bioversity International, COHAB Initiative, DIVERSITAS, EcoHealth Alliance, Harvard School of Public Health, the Oswaldo Cruz Foundation, IUCN, United Nations University, and the Wildlife Conservation Society.\\"--Press release","ISBN":"978-92-4-150853-7","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 910881539","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Connecting global priorities: biodiversity and human health: a state of knowledge review.","title-short":"Connecting global priorities","URL":"http://apps.who.int/iris/bitstream/10665/174012/1/9789241508537_eng.pdf?ua=1","author":[{"literal":"WHO"},{"literal":"CBD"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The use of wild species contributes also to the global economy. In the European Union alone, international legal trade of wild species is estimated at 100 billion euros worth importations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ez6UKh3L","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(TRAFFIC, 2020)","plainCitation":"(TRAFFIC, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35012,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E7WVBGMW"],"itemData":{"id":35012,"type":"article-journal","title":"Legal wildlife trade","URL":"https://www.traffic.org/about-us/legal-wildlife-trade/","author":[{"literal":"TRAFFIC"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.   
      These various contexts mean there are intra- and interannual variations in the frequency and intensity of the use of wild species throughout and across the years, and these variations may impact the sustainability of the use. Whether quotidian or exceptional, nearly all assessments note that human uses of wild species are particularly important to vulnerable individuals and groups including women, marginalized peoples, indigenous peoples, and forest-dependent communities (note that these may be overlapping rather than mutually exclusive categories;ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nOYpvvaf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2015, 2018b, 2019, 2020a; HLPE, 2017; Vira, 2015; WHO & CBD, 2015)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2015, 2018b, 2019, 2020a; HLPE, 2017; Vira, 2015; WHO & CBD, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34531,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JQD8NIXN"],"itemData":{"id":34531,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-108704-6","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 911397186","number-of-pages":"34","publisher":"FAO","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Voluntary guidelines for securing sustainable small-scale fisheries in the context of food security and poverty eradication.","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/a-i4356en.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":35107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEAU4TNP"],"itemData":{"id":35107,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"eng","note":"978-92-5-130561-4","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"The State of the world's forests 2018 - Forests pathways to sustainable development","editor":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AFH3MZXV"],"itemData":{"id":35102,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","page":"572","publisher":"Commission on Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of the world's biodiversity for food and agriculture","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/CA3129EN/CA3129EN.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Bélanger","given":"J."},{"family":"Pilling","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":"BOVAcHko/MTNp8Tsl","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A6FP6AF5"],"itemData":{"id":123716,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132974-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1195470916","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Global Forest Resources Assessment (FRA) 2020: main report","title-short":"GLOBAL FOREST RESOURCES ASSESSMENT (FRA) 2020","URL":"https://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8753en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30174,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HC35DGNH"],"itemData":{"id":30174,"type":"report","abstract":"This report is an evidence-based, comprehensive analysis of the diverse, direct and indirect, contributions of forests and trees to FSN. Chapter 1 examines the linkages between forests\\nand FSN and proposes, for the purpose of this report, a conceptual framework and a forest typology grounded on management criteria. Chapter 2 provides an in-depth analysis of the channels through which forests and trees contribute to FSN. Chapter 3 reviews the state of the world’s forests and identifies challenges and opportunities for forestry in relation to FSN. Chapter 4 is solution-oriented and discusses how to optimize the contributions of forests and trees to FSN in a sustainable manner.","collection-title":"HLPE Report Series","event-place":"Rome","genre":"Report of the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition","language":"English","note":"I739EN/1/06.17","number":"HLPE Report 11","page":"136","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Zotero","title":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition:  A report by the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition of the Committee on World Food Security","title-short":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/a-i7395e.pdf","author":[{"family":"HLPE","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",6]]}}},{"id":35087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4CNIVICV"],"itemData":{"id":35087,"type":"report","abstract":"As population estimates for 2050 reach over 9 billion, issues of food security and nutrition have been dominating academic and policy debates, especially in relation to the global development agenda beyond 2015. A total of 805 million people are undernourished worldwide and malnutrition affects nearly every country on the planet. There is considerable evidence that forests and tree-based systems can play an important role in complementing agricultural production in providing better and more nutritionally-balanced diets; wood fueld for cooking; greater control over food consumption choices, particularly during lean seasons and periods of vulnerability (especially for marginalised groups); and deliver a broad set of ecosystem services which enhance and support crop production. While precise figures are difficult to come by, it has been estimated that approximately 1.2-1.5 biooion people (just under 20 percent of the global population) are forest dependent. These estimates include about 60 million indigenous people who are almost wholly dependent on forests. The loss and degradation of forests exacerbate the problem of food insecurity both directly and indirectly. However, the complex, overlapping and interconnecting processes which link tree products and services to food security and nutrition are currently not adequately represented in forestry, agriculture, food or nutrition-related strategies at global and national levels, though their importance is often well known at more local scales by consumers, forest producers and farmers.    While the evidence base for the role of forests and tree-based systems for food security and nutrition is growing, there remain many gaps in our understanding of this relationship and its potential contribution to reducing global hunger and malnutrition. There is a need to explore the forest-food nexus in much more detail, particularly in relation to the integrated management of multi-functional landscapes, ad the multi-scalar and cross-sectoral governance approaches that are required for the equitable deliver of these benefits. This report documents evidence of the relationships between forests and tree-based systems and food security and nutrition from different agro-ecological zones in all continents. The systems included inour analysis include management of forests to optimise yields of wild foods and fodder.","collection-title":"IUFRO World Series","event-place":"Vienna","genre":"IUFRO World Series","language":"English","note":"978-3-902762-40-5","number":"Volume 33","page":"172","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","publisher-place":"Vienna","title":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition. A global assessment report","title-short":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition","URL":"https://www.iufro.org/science/gfep/forests-and-food-security-panel/report/","author":[{"family":"Vira","given":"Bhaskar"},{"family":"Wildburger","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Mansourian","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":1402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MRNUQFSG"],"itemData":{"id":1402,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Brussels/Montreal, 4 June 2015. Healthy communities rely on well-functioning ecosystems. They provide clean air, fresh water, medicines and food security. They also limit disease and stabilize the climate. But biodiversity loss is happening at unprecedented rates, impacting human health worldwide, according to a new state of knowledge review of the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) and World Health Organization (WHO). The report, Connecting Global Priorities: Biodiversity and Human Health, launched today in Brussels at Green Week 2015, Europe's biggest annual conference on environmental policy, focuses on the complex and multi-faceted connections between biodiversity and human health, and how the loss of biodiversity and corresponding ecosystem services may negatively influence health. One of the first integrative reviews of its kind, the report brings together knowledge from several scientific disciplines, including public health, conservation, agriculture, epidemiology and development. 'This state of knowledge review is a significant contribution to our understanding of the complex relationships between biodiversity and health,' said Braulio Ferreira de Souza Dias, Executive Secretary of the Convention on Biological Diversity and Assistant Secretary-General of the United Nations. 'Increasing our knowledge enables us to develop effective solutions capable of strengthening ecosystem resilience and mitigating the forces that impede their ability to deliver life-supporting services.' All aspects of human well-being depend on ecosystem goods and services, which in turn depend on biodiversity. Biodiversity loss can destabilize ecosystems, promote outbreaks of infectious disease, and undermine development progress, nutrition, security and protection from natural disasters,' said Dr Maria Neira, WHO Director, Department of Public Health, Environmental and Social Determinants of Health. 'Protecting public health from these risks lies outside of the traditional roles of the Health sector. We are ready to work with other sectors to bring about change. ' Access to sufficient quantity, quality and diversity of foods, clean air, freshwater, medicines and healthcare are not only central to maintaining healthy populations, they are foundational pillars of sustainable development. Meeting these needs while facing the persistent challenges of biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, emerging disease pandemics and shifting disease burdens is not an insurmountable feat, but it does require concerted action, based on robust evidence and coordinated cross-sector solutions. This comprehensive report brings this knowledge to the forefront, demonstrating that biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation and ill health often share common threats and pointing toward innovative, mutually-supportive and cross-sectoral solutions. This contribution is especially timely as governments finalize agreement on the post-2015 development agenda and Sustainable Development Goals and prepare to reach new global commitments to tackle climate change by the end of 2015. Among the shared threats identified throughout the report, land-use change is identified as an important driver of biodiversity loss with concurrent implications for many of our most pressing public health challenges. For example, land use change through deforestation is a leading driver of disease emergence in humans and is believed to have contributed to the recent Ebola outbreak in West Africa. While the resulting development of our freshwater and terrestrial resources is also associated with some health benefits, these are unevenly distributed, often to the detriment of our most vulnerable populations and carry unintended consequences. For example, under unsustainable conditions, industrial agricultural practices in many parts of the world may also exacerbate biodiversity loss, pest and disease outbreaks, micronutrient deficiencies, antibiotic resistance and the impacts of climate change on the health. These outcomes are not inevitable. They can be averted through concerted global efforts, by connecting our vast scientific and local knowledge, and developing coherent, cross-sectoral policy priorities toward a healthier, more equitable, sustainable future. Contributions from over 100 scientists and practitioners working in global public health and biodiversity conservation policy were included in the report, including experts from Bioversity International, COHAB Initiative, DIVERSITAS, EcoHealth Alliance, Harvard School of Public Health, the Oswaldo Cruz Foundation, IUCN, United Nations University, and the Wildlife Conservation Society.\\"--Press release","ISBN":"978-92-4-150853-7","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 910881539","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Connecting global priorities: biodiversity and human health: a state of knowledge review.","title-short":"Connecting global priorities","URL":"http://apps.who.int/iris/bitstream/10665/174012/1/9789241508537_eng.pdf?ua=1","author":[{"literal":"WHO"},{"literal":"CBD"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Multiple assessments document the particularly important contributions of use of wild species to food security and nutrition ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"B9fPluvD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2015, 2019; HLPE, 2017; Vira, 2015; WHO & CBD, 2015)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2015, 2019; HLPE, 2017; Vira, 2015; WHO & CBD, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34531,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JQD8NIXN"],"itemData":{"id":34531,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-108704-6","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 911397186","number-of-pages":"34","publisher":"FAO","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Voluntary guidelines for securing sustainable small-scale fisheries in the context of food security and poverty eradication.","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/a-i4356en.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":35102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AFH3MZXV"],"itemData":{"id":35102,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","page":"572","publisher":"Commission on Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of the world's biodiversity for food and agriculture","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/CA3129EN/CA3129EN.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Bélanger","given":"J."},{"family":"Pilling","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":30174,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HC35DGNH"],"itemData":{"id":30174,"type":"report","abstract":"This report is an evidence-based, comprehensive analysis of the diverse, direct and indirect, contributions of forests and trees to FSN. Chapter 1 examines the linkages between forests\\nand FSN and proposes, for the purpose of this report, a conceptual framework and a forest typology grounded on management criteria. Chapter 2 provides an in-depth analysis of the channels through which forests and trees contribute to FSN. Chapter 3 reviews the state of the world’s forests and identifies challenges and opportunities for forestry in relation to FSN. Chapter 4 is solution-oriented and discusses how to optimize the contributions of forests and trees to FSN in a sustainable manner.","collection-title":"HLPE Report Series","event-place":"Rome","genre":"Report of the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition","language":"English","note":"I739EN/1/06.17","number":"HLPE Report 11","page":"136","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Zotero","title":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition:  A report by the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition of the Committee on World Food Security","title-short":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/a-i7395e.pdf","author":[{"family":"HLPE","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",6]]}}},{"id":35087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4CNIVICV"],"itemData":{"id":35087,"type":"report","abstract":"As population estimates for 2050 reach over 9 billion, issues of food security and nutrition have been dominating academic and policy debates, especially in relation to the global development agenda beyond 2015. A total of 805 million people are undernourished worldwide and malnutrition affects nearly every country on the planet. There is considerable evidence that forests and tree-based systems can play an important role in complementing agricultural production in providing better and more nutritionally-balanced diets; wood fueld for cooking; greater control over food consumption choices, particularly during lean seasons and periods of vulnerability (especially for marginalised groups); and deliver a broad set of ecosystem services which enhance and support crop production. While precise figures are difficult to come by, it has been estimated that approximately 1.2-1.5 biooion people (just under 20 percent of the global population) are forest dependent. These estimates include about 60 million indigenous people who are almost wholly dependent on forests. The loss and degradation of forests exacerbate the problem of food insecurity both directly and indirectly. However, the complex, overlapping and interconnecting processes which link tree products and services to food security and nutrition are currently not adequately represented in forestry, agriculture, food or nutrition-related strategies at global and national levels, though their importance is often well known at more local scales by consumers, forest producers and farmers.    While the evidence base for the role of forests and tree-based systems for food security and nutrition is growing, there remain many gaps in our understanding of this relationship and its potential contribution to reducing global hunger and malnutrition. There is a need to explore the forest-food nexus in much more detail, particularly in relation to the integrated management of multi-functional landscapes, ad the multi-scalar and cross-sectoral governance approaches that are required for the equitable deliver of these benefits. This report documents evidence of the relationships between forests and tree-based systems and food security and nutrition from different agro-ecological zones in all continents. The systems included inour analysis include management of forests to optimise yields of wild foods and fodder.","collection-title":"IUFRO World Series","event-place":"Vienna","genre":"IUFRO World Series","language":"English","note":"978-3-902762-40-5","number":"Volume 33","page":"172","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","publisher-place":"Vienna","title":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition. A global assessment report","title-short":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition","URL":"https://www.iufro.org/science/gfep/forests-and-food-security-panel/report/","author":[{"family":"Vira","given":"Bhaskar"},{"family":"Wildburger","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Mansourian","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":1402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MRNUQFSG"],"itemData":{"id":1402,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Brussels/Montreal, 4 June 2015. Healthy communities rely on well-functioning ecosystems. They provide clean air, fresh water, medicines and food security. They also limit disease and stabilize the climate. But biodiversity loss is happening at unprecedented rates, impacting human health worldwide, according to a new state of knowledge review of the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) and World Health Organization (WHO). The report, Connecting Global Priorities: Biodiversity and Human Health, launched today in Brussels at Green Week 2015, Europe's biggest annual conference on environmental policy, focuses on the complex and multi-faceted connections between biodiversity and human health, and how the loss of biodiversity and corresponding ecosystem services may negatively influence health. One of the first integrative reviews of its kind, the report brings together knowledge from several scientific disciplines, including public health, conservation, agriculture, epidemiology and development. 'This state of knowledge review is a significant contribution to our understanding of the complex relationships between biodiversity and health,' said Braulio Ferreira de Souza Dias, Executive Secretary of the Convention on Biological Diversity and Assistant Secretary-General of the United Nations. 'Increasing our knowledge enables us to develop effective solutions capable of strengthening ecosystem resilience and mitigating the forces that impede their ability to deliver life-supporting services.' All aspects of human well-being depend on ecosystem goods and services, which in turn depend on biodiversity. Biodiversity loss can destabilize ecosystems, promote outbreaks of infectious disease, and undermine development progress, nutrition, security and protection from natural disasters,' said Dr Maria Neira, WHO Director, Department of Public Health, Environmental and Social Determinants of Health. 'Protecting public health from these risks lies outside of the traditional roles of the Health sector. We are ready to work with other sectors to bring about change. ' Access to sufficient quantity, quality and diversity of foods, clean air, freshwater, medicines and healthcare are not only central to maintaining healthy populations, they are foundational pillars of sustainable development. Meeting these needs while facing the persistent challenges of biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, emerging disease pandemics and shifting disease burdens is not an insurmountable feat, but it does require concerted action, based on robust evidence and coordinated cross-sector solutions. This comprehensive report brings this knowledge to the forefront, demonstrating that biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation and ill health often share common threats and pointing toward innovative, mutually-supportive and cross-sectoral solutions. This contribution is especially timely as governments finalize agreement on the post-2015 development agenda and Sustainable Development Goals and prepare to reach new global commitments to tackle climate change by the end of 2015. Among the shared threats identified throughout the report, land-use change is identified as an important driver of biodiversity loss with concurrent implications for many of our most pressing public health challenges. For example, land use change through deforestation is a leading driver of disease emergence in humans and is believed to have contributed to the recent Ebola outbreak in West Africa. While the resulting development of our freshwater and terrestrial resources is also associated with some health benefits, these are unevenly distributed, often to the detriment of our most vulnerable populations and carry unintended consequences. For example, under unsustainable conditions, industrial agricultural practices in many parts of the world may also exacerbate biodiversity loss, pest and disease outbreaks, micronutrient deficiencies, antibiotic resistance and the impacts of climate change on the health. These outcomes are not inevitable. They can be averted through concerted global efforts, by connecting our vast scientific and local knowledge, and developing coherent, cross-sectoral policy priorities toward a healthier, more equitable, sustainable future. Contributions from over 100 scientists and practitioners working in global public health and biodiversity conservation policy were included in the report, including experts from Bioversity International, COHAB Initiative, DIVERSITAS, EcoHealth Alliance, Harvard School of Public Health, the Oswaldo Cruz Foundation, IUCN, United Nations University, and the Wildlife Conservation Society.\\"--Press release","ISBN":"978-92-4-150853-7","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 910881539","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Connecting global priorities: biodiversity and human health: a state of knowledge review.","title-short":"Connecting global priorities","URL":"http://apps.who.int/iris/bitstream/10665/174012/1/9789241508537_eng.pdf?ua=1","author":[{"literal":"WHO"},{"literal":"CBD"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Consumption of wild animals, fungi, and plants contributes directly to human health and wellbeing through dietary quality and diversity, in rural and urban areas, in developed and developing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ipoNRPuD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(WHO & CBD, 2015)","plainCitation":"(WHO & CBD, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MRNUQFSG"],"itemData":{"id":1402,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Brussels/Montreal, 4 June 2015. Healthy communities rely on well-functioning ecosystems. They provide clean air, fresh water, medicines and food security. They also limit disease and stabilize the climate. But biodiversity loss is happening at unprecedented rates, impacting human health worldwide, according to a new state of knowledge review of the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) and World Health Organization (WHO). The report, Connecting Global Priorities: Biodiversity and Human Health, launched today in Brussels at Green Week 2015, Europe's biggest annual conference on environmental policy, focuses on the complex and multi-faceted connections between biodiversity and human health, and how the loss of biodiversity and corresponding ecosystem services may negatively influence health. One of the first integrative reviews of its kind, the report brings together knowledge from several scientific disciplines, including public health, conservation, agriculture, epidemiology and development. 'This state of knowledge review is a significant contribution to our understanding of the complex relationships between biodiversity and health,' said Braulio Ferreira de Souza Dias, Executive Secretary of the Convention on Biological Diversity and Assistant Secretary-General of the United Nations. 'Increasing our knowledge enables us to develop effective solutions capable of strengthening ecosystem resilience and mitigating the forces that impede their ability to deliver life-supporting services.' All aspects of human well-being depend on ecosystem goods and services, which in turn depend on biodiversity. Biodiversity loss can destabilize ecosystems, promote outbreaks of infectious disease, and undermine development progress, nutrition, security and protection from natural disasters,' said Dr Maria Neira, WHO Director, Department of Public Health, Environmental and Social Determinants of Health. 'Protecting public health from these risks lies outside of the traditional roles of the Health sector. We are ready to work with other sectors to bring about change. ' Access to sufficient quantity, quality and diversity of foods, clean air, freshwater, medicines and healthcare are not only central to maintaining healthy populations, they are foundational pillars of sustainable development. Meeting these needs while facing the persistent challenges of biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, emerging disease pandemics and shifting disease burdens is not an insurmountable feat, but it does require concerted action, based on robust evidence and coordinated cross-sector solutions. This comprehensive report brings this knowledge to the forefront, demonstrating that biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation and ill health often share common threats and pointing toward innovative, mutually-supportive and cross-sectoral solutions. This contribution is especially timely as governments finalize agreement on the post-2015 development agenda and Sustainable Development Goals and prepare to reach new global commitments to tackle climate change by the end of 2015. Among the shared threats identified throughout the report, land-use change is identified as an important driver of biodiversity loss with concurrent implications for many of our most pressing public health challenges. For example, land use change through deforestation is a leading driver of disease emergence in humans and is believed to have contributed to the recent Ebola outbreak in West Africa. While the resulting development of our freshwater and terrestrial resources is also associated with some health benefits, these are unevenly distributed, often to the detriment of our most vulnerable populations and carry unintended consequences. For example, under unsustainable conditions, industrial agricultural practices in many parts of the world may also exacerbate biodiversity loss, pest and disease outbreaks, micronutrient deficiencies, antibiotic resistance and the impacts of climate change on the health. These outcomes are not inevitable. They can be averted through concerted global efforts, by connecting our vast scientific and local knowledge, and developing coherent, cross-sectoral policy priorities toward a healthier, more equitable, sustainable future. Contributions from over 100 scientists and practitioners working in global public health and biodiversity conservation policy were included in the report, including experts from Bioversity International, COHAB Initiative, DIVERSITAS, EcoHealth Alliance, Harvard School of Public Health, the Oswaldo Cruz Foundation, IUCN, United Nations University, and the Wildlife Conservation Society.\\"--Press release","ISBN":"978-92-4-150853-7","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 910881539","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Connecting global priorities: biodiversity and human health: a state of knowledge review.","title-short":"Connecting global priorities","URL":"http://apps.who.int/iris/bitstream/10665/174012/1/9789241508537_eng.pdf?ua=1","author":[{"literal":"WHO"},{"literal":"CBD"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Reports to FAO from 91 countries with a fair geographical representation  showed in 2017 that over 2,800 distinct species are used for human food across the world, the largest share of wild species used for food coming from aquatic and forest production systems, with capture fisheries being likely the largest use of wild food by volume, for human consumption or feeding aquaculture species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fXXkX5wr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2019, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2019, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AFH3MZXV"],"itemData":{"id":35102,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","page":"572","publisher":"Commission on Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of the world's biodiversity for food and agriculture","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/CA3129EN/CA3129EN.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Bélanger","given":"J."},{"family":"Pilling","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A majority of wild fungi, insects, plants and, to a lesser extent, mammals and birds are obtained from forested ecosystems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NCnVBqm8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2018b, 2019)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2018b, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEAU4TNP"],"itemData":{"id":35107,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"eng","note":"978-92-5-130561-4","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"The State of the world's forests 2018 - Forests pathways to sustainable development","editor":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AFH3MZXV"],"itemData":{"id":35102,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","page":"572","publisher":"Commission on Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of the world's biodiversity for food and agriculture","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/CA3129EN/CA3129EN.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Bélanger","given":"J."},{"family":"Pilling","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Consumption of wild food also enables greater dietary choice (i.e., food sovereignty) and is often used as a complement to a limited set of cultivated crops ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YV7mKQgj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2019; WHO & CBD, 2015)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2019; WHO & CBD, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AFH3MZXV"],"itemData":{"id":35102,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","page":"572","publisher":"Commission on Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of the world's biodiversity for food and agriculture","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/CA3129EN/CA3129EN.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Bélanger","given":"J."},{"family":"Pilling","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":1402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MRNUQFSG"],"itemData":{"id":1402,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Brussels/Montreal, 4 June 2015. Healthy communities rely on well-functioning ecosystems. They provide clean air, fresh water, medicines and food security. They also limit disease and stabilize the climate. But biodiversity loss is happening at unprecedented rates, impacting human health worldwide, according to a new state of knowledge review of the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) and World Health Organization (WHO). The report, Connecting Global Priorities: Biodiversity and Human Health, launched today in Brussels at Green Week 2015, Europe's biggest annual conference on environmental policy, focuses on the complex and multi-faceted connections between biodiversity and human health, and how the loss of biodiversity and corresponding ecosystem services may negatively influence health. One of the first integrative reviews of its kind, the report brings together knowledge from several scientific disciplines, including public health, conservation, agriculture, epidemiology and development. 'This state of knowledge review is a significant contribution to our understanding of the complex relationships between biodiversity and health,' said Braulio Ferreira de Souza Dias, Executive Secretary of the Convention on Biological Diversity and Assistant Secretary-General of the United Nations. 'Increasing our knowledge enables us to develop effective solutions capable of strengthening ecosystem resilience and mitigating the forces that impede their ability to deliver life-supporting services.' All aspects of human well-being depend on ecosystem goods and services, which in turn depend on biodiversity. Biodiversity loss can destabilize ecosystems, promote outbreaks of infectious disease, and undermine development progress, nutrition, security and protection from natural disasters,' said Dr Maria Neira, WHO Director, Department of Public Health, Environmental and Social Determinants of Health. 'Protecting public health from these risks lies outside of the traditional roles of the Health sector. We are ready to work with other sectors to bring about change. ' Access to sufficient quantity, quality and diversity of foods, clean air, freshwater, medicines and healthcare are not only central to maintaining healthy populations, they are foundational pillars of sustainable development. Meeting these needs while facing the persistent challenges of biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, emerging disease pandemics and shifting disease burdens is not an insurmountable feat, but it does require concerted action, based on robust evidence and coordinated cross-sector solutions. This comprehensive report brings this knowledge to the forefront, demonstrating that biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation and ill health often share common threats and pointing toward innovative, mutually-supportive and cross-sectoral solutions. This contribution is especially timely as governments finalize agreement on the post-2015 development agenda and Sustainable Development Goals and prepare to reach new global commitments to tackle climate change by the end of 2015. Among the shared threats identified throughout the report, land-use change is identified as an important driver of biodiversity loss with concurrent implications for many of our most pressing public health challenges. For example, land use change through deforestation is a leading driver of disease emergence in humans and is believed to have contributed to the recent Ebola outbreak in West Africa. While the resulting development of our freshwater and terrestrial resources is also associated with some health benefits, these are unevenly distributed, often to the detriment of our most vulnerable populations and carry unintended consequences. For example, under unsustainable conditions, industrial agricultural practices in many parts of the world may also exacerbate biodiversity loss, pest and disease outbreaks, micronutrient deficiencies, antibiotic resistance and the impacts of climate change on the health. These outcomes are not inevitable. They can be averted through concerted global efforts, by connecting our vast scientific and local knowledge, and developing coherent, cross-sectoral policy priorities toward a healthier, more equitable, sustainable future. Contributions from over 100 scientists and practitioners working in global public health and biodiversity conservation policy were included in the report, including experts from Bioversity International, COHAB Initiative, DIVERSITAS, EcoHealth Alliance, Harvard School of Public Health, the Oswaldo Cruz Foundation, IUCN, United Nations University, and the Wildlife Conservation Society.\\"--Press release","ISBN":"978-92-4-150853-7","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 910881539","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Connecting global priorities: biodiversity and human health: a state of knowledge review.","title-short":"Connecting global priorities","URL":"http://apps.who.int/iris/bitstream/10665/174012/1/9789241508537_eng.pdf?ua=1","author":[{"literal":"WHO"},{"literal":"CBD"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It has demonstrated benefits in terms of reducing metabolic diseases such as obesity and diabetes, which are epidemic in some parts of the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aVvHypCz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vira, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Vira, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4CNIVICV"],"itemData":{"id":35087,"type":"report","abstract":"As population estimates for 2050 reach over 9 billion, issues of food security and nutrition have been dominating academic and policy debates, especially in relation to the global development agenda beyond 2015. A total of 805 million people are undernourished worldwide and malnutrition affects nearly every country on the planet. There is considerable evidence that forests and tree-based systems can play an important role in complementing agricultural production in providing better and more nutritionally-balanced diets; wood fueld for cooking; greater control over food consumption choices, particularly during lean seasons and periods of vulnerability (especially for marginalised groups); and deliver a broad set of ecosystem services which enhance and support crop production. While precise figures are difficult to come by, it has been estimated that approximately 1.2-1.5 biooion people (just under 20 percent of the global population) are forest dependent. These estimates include about 60 million indigenous people who are almost wholly dependent on forests. The loss and degradation of forests exacerbate the problem of food insecurity both directly and indirectly. However, the complex, overlapping and interconnecting processes which link tree products and services to food security and nutrition are currently not adequately represented in forestry, agriculture, food or nutrition-related strategies at global and national levels, though their importance is often well known at more local scales by consumers, forest producers and farmers.    While the evidence base for the role of forests and tree-based systems for food security and nutrition is growing, there remain many gaps in our understanding of this relationship and its potential contribution to reducing global hunger and malnutrition. There is a need to explore the forest-food nexus in much more detail, particularly in relation to the integrated management of multi-functional landscapes, ad the multi-scalar and cross-sectoral governance approaches that are required for the equitable deliver of these benefits. This report documents evidence of the relationships between forests and tree-based systems and food security and nutrition from different agro-ecological zones in all continents. The systems included inour analysis include management of forests to optimise yields of wild foods and fodder.","collection-title":"IUFRO World Series","event-place":"Vienna","genre":"IUFRO World Series","language":"English","note":"978-3-902762-40-5","number":"Volume 33","page":"172","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","publisher-place":"Vienna","title":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition. A global assessment report","title-short":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition","URL":"https://www.iufro.org/science/gfep/forests-and-food-security-panel/report/","author":[{"family":"Vira","given":"Bhaskar"},{"family":"Wildburger","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Mansourian","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Madagascar, it was found that risk of aenemia in children from poor households was lowered by nearly 30% when the household consumed wild meat, even in small quantities (Golden 2011 inITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kjAcUTCd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(WHO & CBD, 2015)","plainCitation":"(WHO & CBD, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MRNUQFSG"],"itemData":{"id":1402,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Brussels/Montreal, 4 June 2015. Healthy communities rely on well-functioning ecosystems. They provide clean air, fresh water, medicines and food security. They also limit disease and stabilize the climate. But biodiversity loss is happening at unprecedented rates, impacting human health worldwide, according to a new state of knowledge review of the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) and World Health Organization (WHO). The report, Connecting Global Priorities: Biodiversity and Human Health, launched today in Brussels at Green Week 2015, Europe's biggest annual conference on environmental policy, focuses on the complex and multi-faceted connections between biodiversity and human health, and how the loss of biodiversity and corresponding ecosystem services may negatively influence health. One of the first integrative reviews of its kind, the report brings together knowledge from several scientific disciplines, including public health, conservation, agriculture, epidemiology and development. 'This state of knowledge review is a significant contribution to our understanding of the complex relationships between biodiversity and health,' said Braulio Ferreira de Souza Dias, Executive Secretary of the Convention on Biological Diversity and Assistant Secretary-General of the United Nations. 'Increasing our knowledge enables us to develop effective solutions capable of strengthening ecosystem resilience and mitigating the forces that impede their ability to deliver life-supporting services.' All aspects of human well-being depend on ecosystem goods and services, which in turn depend on biodiversity. Biodiversity loss can destabilize ecosystems, promote outbreaks of infectious disease, and undermine development progress, nutrition, security and protection from natural disasters,' said Dr Maria Neira, WHO Director, Department of Public Health, Environmental and Social Determinants of Health. 'Protecting public health from these risks lies outside of the traditional roles of the Health sector. We are ready to work with other sectors to bring about change. ' Access to sufficient quantity, quality and diversity of foods, clean air, freshwater, medicines and healthcare are not only central to maintaining healthy populations, they are foundational pillars of sustainable development. Meeting these needs while facing the persistent challenges of biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, emerging disease pandemics and shifting disease burdens is not an insurmountable feat, but it does require concerted action, based on robust evidence and coordinated cross-sector solutions. This comprehensive report brings this knowledge to the forefront, demonstrating that biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation and ill health often share common threats and pointing toward innovative, mutually-supportive and cross-sectoral solutions. This contribution is especially timely as governments finalize agreement on the post-2015 development agenda and Sustainable Development Goals and prepare to reach new global commitments to tackle climate change by the end of 2015. Among the shared threats identified throughout the report, land-use change is identified as an important driver of biodiversity loss with concurrent implications for many of our most pressing public health challenges. For example, land use change through deforestation is a leading driver of disease emergence in humans and is believed to have contributed to the recent Ebola outbreak in West Africa. While the resulting development of our freshwater and terrestrial resources is also associated with some health benefits, these are unevenly distributed, often to the detriment of our most vulnerable populations and carry unintended consequences. For example, under unsustainable conditions, industrial agricultural practices in many parts of the world may also exacerbate biodiversity loss, pest and disease outbreaks, micronutrient deficiencies, antibiotic resistance and the impacts of climate change on the health. These outcomes are not inevitable. They can be averted through concerted global efforts, by connecting our vast scientific and local knowledge, and developing coherent, cross-sectoral policy priorities toward a healthier, more equitable, sustainable future. Contributions from over 100 scientists and practitioners working in global public health and biodiversity conservation policy were included in the report, including experts from Bioversity International, COHAB Initiative, DIVERSITAS, EcoHealth Alliance, Harvard School of Public Health, the Oswaldo Cruz Foundation, IUCN, United Nations University, and the Wildlife Conservation Society.\\"--Press release","ISBN":"978-92-4-150853-7","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 910881539","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Connecting global priorities: biodiversity and human health: a state of knowledge review.","title-short":"Connecting global priorities","URL":"http://apps.who.int/iris/bitstream/10665/174012/1/9789241508537_eng.pdf?ua=1","author":[{"literal":"WHO"},{"literal":"CBD"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). Some assessments note that wild species are an important or primary source of protein ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LWO4JtWC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2018a)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2018a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35018,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NHSBQV7E","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EY6CU4ER"],"itemData":{"id":35018,"type":"book","abstract":"Reproduction This publication may be reproduced in whole or in part and in any form for educational or non-profit services without special permission from the copyright holder, provided acknowledgement of the source is made. The IPBES secretariat would appreciate receiving a copy of any publication that uses this publication as a source. No use of this publication may be made for resale or any other commercial purpose whatsoever without prior permission in writing from the IPBES secretariat. Applications for such permission, with a statement of the purpose and extent of the reproduction, should be addressed to the IPBES secretariat. The use of information from this publication concerning proprietary products for publicity or advertising is not permitted. Traceable accounts The chapter references enclosed in curly brackets (e.g. {2.3.1, 2.3.1.2, 2.3.1.3}) are traceable accounts and refer to sections of the chapters of the IPBES Regional Assessment Report on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services for Africa. A traceable account is a description within the corresponding texts of these chapters, reflecting the evaluation of the type, amount, quality, and consistency of evidence and the degree of agreement for that particular statement or key finding.","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-00-3","number-of-pages":"49","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the regional assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services for Africa of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"http://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3236178","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"literal":"E. Archer"},{"literal":"L. E. Dziba"},{"literal":"K. J. Mulongoy"},{"literal":"M. A. Maoela"},{"literal":"M. Walters"},{"literal":"R. Biggs"},{"literal":"M-C. Cormier-Salem"},{"literal":"F. DeClerck"},{"literal":"M. C. Diaw"},{"literal":"A. E. Dunham"},{"literal":"P. Failler"},{"literal":"C. Gordon"},{"literal":"K. A. Harhash"},{"literal":"R. Kasisi"},{"literal":"F. Kizito"},{"literal":"W. D. Nyingi"},{"literal":"N. Oguge"},{"literal":"B. Osman-Elasha"},{"literal":"L. C. Stringer"},{"literal":"L. Tito de Morais"},{"literal":"A. Assogbadjo"},{"literal":"B. N. Egoh"},{"literal":"M. W. Halmy"},{"literal":"K. Heubach"},{"literal":"A. Mensah"},{"literal":"L. Pereira"},{"literal":"N. Sitas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and allow people to get specific micronutrients. Examples show that, in many instances, wild species contain a much higher level of micronutrients, vitamins and proteins than similar domesticated crops or livestock ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"i62qDn5a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2019; WHO & CBD, 2015)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2019; WHO & CBD, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AFH3MZXV"],"itemData":{"id":35102,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","page":"572","publisher":"Commission on Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of the world's biodiversity for food and agriculture","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/CA3129EN/CA3129EN.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Bélanger","given":"J."},{"family":"Pilling","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":1402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MRNUQFSG"],"itemData":{"id":1402,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Brussels/Montreal, 4 June 2015. Healthy communities rely on well-functioning ecosystems. They provide clean air, fresh water, medicines and food security. They also limit disease and stabilize the climate. But biodiversity loss is happening at unprecedented rates, impacting human health worldwide, according to a new state of knowledge review of the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) and World Health Organization (WHO). The report, Connecting Global Priorities: Biodiversity and Human Health, launched today in Brussels at Green Week 2015, Europe's biggest annual conference on environmental policy, focuses on the complex and multi-faceted connections between biodiversity and human health, and how the loss of biodiversity and corresponding ecosystem services may negatively influence health. One of the first integrative reviews of its kind, the report brings together knowledge from several scientific disciplines, including public health, conservation, agriculture, epidemiology and development. 'This state of knowledge review is a significant contribution to our understanding of the complex relationships between biodiversity and health,' said Braulio Ferreira de Souza Dias, Executive Secretary of the Convention on Biological Diversity and Assistant Secretary-General of the United Nations. 'Increasing our knowledge enables us to develop effective solutions capable of strengthening ecosystem resilience and mitigating the forces that impede their ability to deliver life-supporting services.' All aspects of human well-being depend on ecosystem goods and services, which in turn depend on biodiversity. Biodiversity loss can destabilize ecosystems, promote outbreaks of infectious disease, and undermine development progress, nutrition, security and protection from natural disasters,' said Dr Maria Neira, WHO Director, Department of Public Health, Environmental and Social Determinants of Health. 'Protecting public health from these risks lies outside of the traditional roles of the Health sector. We are ready to work with other sectors to bring about change. ' Access to sufficient quantity, quality and diversity of foods, clean air, freshwater, medicines and healthcare are not only central to maintaining healthy populations, they are foundational pillars of sustainable development. Meeting these needs while facing the persistent challenges of biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, emerging disease pandemics and shifting disease burdens is not an insurmountable feat, but it does require concerted action, based on robust evidence and coordinated cross-sector solutions. This comprehensive report brings this knowledge to the forefront, demonstrating that biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation and ill health often share common threats and pointing toward innovative, mutually-supportive and cross-sectoral solutions. This contribution is especially timely as governments finalize agreement on the post-2015 development agenda and Sustainable Development Goals and prepare to reach new global commitments to tackle climate change by the end of 2015. Among the shared threats identified throughout the report, land-use change is identified as an important driver of biodiversity loss with concurrent implications for many of our most pressing public health challenges. For example, land use change through deforestation is a leading driver of disease emergence in humans and is believed to have contributed to the recent Ebola outbreak in West Africa. While the resulting development of our freshwater and terrestrial resources is also associated with some health benefits, these are unevenly distributed, often to the detriment of our most vulnerable populations and carry unintended consequences. For example, under unsustainable conditions, industrial agricultural practices in many parts of the world may also exacerbate biodiversity loss, pest and disease outbreaks, micronutrient deficiencies, antibiotic resistance and the impacts of climate change on the health. These outcomes are not inevitable. They can be averted through concerted global efforts, by connecting our vast scientific and local knowledge, and developing coherent, cross-sectoral policy priorities toward a healthier, more equitable, sustainable future. Contributions from over 100 scientists and practitioners working in global public health and biodiversity conservation policy were included in the report, including experts from Bioversity International, COHAB Initiative, DIVERSITAS, EcoHealth Alliance, Harvard School of Public Health, the Oswaldo Cruz Foundation, IUCN, United Nations University, and the Wildlife Conservation Society.\\"--Press release","ISBN":"978-92-4-150853-7","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 910881539","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Connecting global priorities: biodiversity and human health: a state of knowledge review.","title-short":"Connecting global priorities","URL":"http://apps.who.int/iris/bitstream/10665/174012/1/9789241508537_eng.pdf?ua=1","author":[{"literal":"WHO"},{"literal":"CBD"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Beyond addressing primary needs, wild species may also be preferred to domestic species even when those latest are available through markets. Preference for wild species comes from traditional food preferences (e.g., for people moving from rural to urban areas) or “from a revival of interest in wild foods”, mainly because they are associated with cultural, recreational or nutritional nature’s contributions to people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"46x6DD0Z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2019)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AFH3MZXV"],"itemData":{"id":35102,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","page":"572","publisher":"Commission on Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of the world's biodiversity for food and agriculture","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/CA3129EN/CA3129EN.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Bélanger","given":"J."},{"family":"Pilling","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Use of wild species may include risks for human health, through the transmission of diseases. Hunting, butchering, transportation and storage of live wild animals or their meat in unsafe conditions presents particularly risk of zoonotic diseases ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UI9lsNRw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2020; WHO & CBD, 2015)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2020; WHO & CBD, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":66,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMSKDRYR"],"itemData":{"id":66,"type":"report","abstract":"The IPBES Bureau and Multidisciplinary Expert Panel (MEP) authorized a workshop on biodiversity and pandemics that was held virtually on 27-31 July 2020 in accordance with the provisions on “Platform workshops” in support of Plenary-approved activities, set out in section 6.1 of the procedures for the preparation of Platform deliverables (IPBES-3/3, annexe I). This workshop report and any recommendations or conclusions contained therein have not been reviewed, endorsed or approved by the IPBES Plenary. The workshop report is considered supporting material available to authors in the preparation of ongoing or future IPBES assessments. While undergoing a scientific peer-review, this material has not been subjected to formal IPBES review processes. <strong>Workshop report:</strong> “<em>IPBES Pandemics Workshop Report 20201029 v1.1.pdf</em>”<br> This workshop report is released in a non-laid out format. It will undergo minor editing before being released in a laid-out format. <strong>Executive summary</strong>: “<em>IPBES Pandemics Workshop Executive Summary 20201028 v1.0.pdf</em>” To get the latest version of the report visit https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4147317 For inquiries, please contact secretariat@ipbes.net https://ipbes.net/events/ipbes-workshop-biodiversity-and-pandemics","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","language":"en","license":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International, Open Access","note":"version: 1.3\\nDOI: 10.5281/ZENODO.4147317","publisher":"IPBES secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"Workshop Report on Biodiversity and Pandemics of the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES)","URL":"https://zenodo.org/record/4147317","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."},{"family":"Neves","given":"C.","non-dropping-particle":"das"},{"family":"Amuasi","given":"J."},{"family":"Hayman","given":"D."},{"family":"Kuiken","given":"T."},{"family":"Roche","given":"B."},{"family":"Zambrana-Torrelio","given":"C."},{"family":"Buss","given":"P."},{"family":"Dundarova","given":"H."},{"family":"Feferholtz","given":"Y."},{"family":"Foldvari","given":"G."},{"family":"Igbinosa","given":"E."},{"family":"Junglen","given":"S."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Q."},{"family":"Suzan","given":"G."},{"family":"Uhart","given":"M."},{"family":"Wannous","given":"C."},{"family":"Woolaston","given":"K."},{"family":"Mosig Reidl","given":"P."},{"family":"O'Brien","given":"K."},{"family":"Pascual","given":"U."},{"family":"Stoett","given":"P."},{"family":"Li","given":"H."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H. T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]}}},{"id":1402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MRNUQFSG