* wild species populations (e.g., a disease affecting the wild species, or climate change leading to a shift in wild species range ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CUUDq1RL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018; FAO, 2019, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018; FAO, 2019, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1448,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SERE3KJN"],"itemData":{"id":1448,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Nontimber forest products (NTFPs) are fundamental to the functioning of healthy forests and play vital roles in the cultures and economies of the people of the United States. However, these plants and fungi used for food, medicine, and other purposes have not been fully incorporated into management, policy, and resource valuation. This report is a forest-sectorwide assessment of the state of the knowledge regarding NTFPs science and management information for U.S. forests and rangelands (and hereafter referred to as the NTFP assessment). The NTFP assessment serves as a baseline science synthesis and provides information for managing nontimber forest resources in the United States. In addition, this NTFP assessment provides information for national-level reporting on natural capital and the ecosystem services NTFPs provide. The report also provides technical input to the 2017 National Climate Assessment (NCA) under development by the U.S. Global Change Research Program (USGCRP).","DOI":"10.2737/SRS-GTR-232","issue":"June","page":"267","title":"Assessment of Nontimber Forest Products in the United States Under Changing Conditions. A report for the United States Department of Agriculture. General Technical Report SRS-232.","URL":"https://doi.org/10.2737/SRS-GTR-232","author":[{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla. R."},{"family":"Patel-Weynard","given":"Toral."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AFH3MZXV"],"itemData":{"id":35102,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","page":"572","publisher":"Commission on Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of the world's biodiversity for food and agriculture","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/CA3129EN/CA3129EN.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Bélanger","given":"J."},{"family":"Pilling","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; 
* the practices (e.g., technological change leading to more intensive or extensive harvesting, industrialization of a wild species use, commercial or demographic drivers leading to increased demand for a wild species, often entailing expansion from local to regional or global markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"P3eARSpT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2019, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2019, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AFH3MZXV"],"itemData":{"id":35102,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","page":"572","publisher":"Commission on Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of the world's biodiversity for food and agriculture","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/CA3129EN/CA3129EN.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Bélanger","given":"J."},{"family":"Pilling","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; 
* the uses (e.g., new applications found for wild species, or shift from subsistence to commercial uses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vKkD7veL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018; FAO, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018; FAO, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1448,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SERE3KJN"],"itemData":{"id":1448,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Nontimber forest products (NTFPs) are fundamental to the functioning of healthy forests and play vital roles in the cultures and economies of the people of the United States. However, these plants and fungi used for food, medicine, and other purposes have not been fully incorporated into management, policy, and resource valuation. This report is a forest-sectorwide assessment of the state of the knowledge regarding NTFPs science and management information for U.S. forests and rangelands (and hereafter referred to as the NTFP assessment). The NTFP assessment serves as a baseline science synthesis and provides information for managing nontimber forest resources in the United States. In addition, this NTFP assessment provides information for national-level reporting on natural capital and the ecosystem services NTFPs provide. The report also provides technical input to the 2017 National Climate Assessment (NCA) under development by the U.S. Global Change Research Program (USGCRP).","DOI":"10.2737/SRS-GTR-232","issue":"June","page":"267","title":"Assessment of Nontimber Forest Products in the United States Under Changing Conditions. A report for the United States Department of Agriculture. General Technical Report SRS-232.","URL":"https://doi.org/10.2737/SRS-GTR-232","author":[{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla. R."},{"family":"Patel-Weynard","given":"Toral."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AFH3MZXV"],"itemData":{"id":35102,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","page":"572","publisher":"Commission on Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of the world's biodiversity for food and agriculture","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/CA3129EN/CA3129EN.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Bélanger","given":"J."},{"family":"Pilling","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
       For extractive practices, all those drivers may lead to an increased share of the biomass being removed from nature, thus disturbing the whole social-ecological system equilibrium. However, some assessments also note that much biodiversity is underpinned by the long-term results of indigenous and local knowledge and indigenous peoples’ and local communities’ customary practices associated with uses of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jyDX92m2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018; FAO, 2018b, 2020a; Forest Peoples Program, 2020; IPBES, 2016, 2019d)","plainCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018; FAO, 2018b, 2020a; Forest Peoples Program, 2020; IPBES, 2016, 2019d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1448,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SERE3KJN"],"itemData":{"id":1448,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Nontimber forest products (NTFPs) are fundamental to the functioning of healthy forests and play vital roles in the cultures and economies of the people of the United States. However, these plants and fungi used for food, medicine, and other purposes have not been fully incorporated into management, policy, and resource valuation. This report is a forest-sectorwide assessment of the state of the knowledge regarding NTFPs science and management information for U.S. forests and rangelands (and hereafter referred to as the NTFP assessment). The NTFP assessment serves as a baseline science synthesis and provides information for managing nontimber forest resources in the United States. In addition, this NTFP assessment provides information for national-level reporting on natural capital and the ecosystem services NTFPs provide. The report also provides technical input to the 2017 National Climate Assessment (NCA) under development by the U.S. Global Change Research Program (USGCRP).","DOI":"10.2737/SRS-GTR-232","issue":"June","page":"267","title":"Assessment of Nontimber Forest Products in the United States Under Changing Conditions. A report for the United States Department of Agriculture. General Technical Report SRS-232.","URL":"https://doi.org/10.2737/SRS-GTR-232","author":[{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla. R."},{"family":"Patel-Weynard","given":"Toral."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEAU4TNP"],"itemData":{"id":35107,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"eng","note":"978-92-5-130561-4","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"The State of the world's forests 2018 - Forests pathways to sustainable development","editor":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":"BOVAcHko/MTNp8Tsl","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A6FP6AF5"],"itemData":{"id":123716,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132974-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1195470916","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Global Forest Resources Assessment (FRA) 2020: main report","title-short":"GLOBAL FOREST RESOURCES ASSESSMENT (FRA) 2020","URL":"https://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8753en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30471,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6ZY9V68D"],"itemData":{"id":30471,"type":"report","event-place":"Moreton-in-Marsh, England","publisher":"Forest Peoples Programme","publisher-place":"Moreton-in-Marsh, England","title":"Local Biodiversity Outlooks 2: The contributions of indigenous peoples and local communities to the implementation of the Strategic Plan for Biodiversity 2011–2020 and to renewing nature and cultures. A complement to the fifth edition of Global Biodiversity Outlook.","URL":"www.localbiodiversityoutlooks.net","author":[{"family":"Forest Peoples Program","given":""},{"family":"International Indigenous Forum on Biodiversity","given":""},{"family":"Indigenous Women’s Biodiversity Network","given":""},{"family":"Centres of Distinction on Indigenous and Local Knowledge","given":""},{"family":"CBD","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":35028,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YG7LF893","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AGYQARR9"],"itemData":{"id":35028,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-92-807-3568-0","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","title":"Summary for policymakers of the assessment report of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services on pollinators, pollination and food production.","URL":"https://www.ipbes.net/sites/default/files/spm_deliverable_3a_pollination_20170222.pdf","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"Potts","given":"S. G."},{"family":"Imperatriz-Fonseca","given":"V. L."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H. T."},{"family":"Biesmeijer","given":"J. C."},{"family":"Breeze","given":"T. D."},{"family":"Dicks","given":"L. V."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Hill","given":"R."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Vanbergen","given":"A. J."},{"family":"Aizen","given":"M. A."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"S. A."},{"family":"Eardley","given":"C."},{"family":"Freitas","given":"B. M."},{"family":"Gallai","given":"N."},{"family":"Kevan","given":"P. G."},{"family":"Kovács-Hostyánszki","given":"A."},{"family":"Kwapong","given":"P. K."},{"family":"Li","given":"J."},{"family":"Li","given":"X."},{"family":"Martins","given":"D. J."},{"family":"Nates-Parra","given":"G."},{"family":"J. S. Pettis","given":"R. Rader"},{"family":"Viana","given":"B. F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Further, erosion of indigenous and local knowledge, in concert with indigenous peoples and local communities’ loss of access and tenure rights to traditional territories and resources appears to be a driver of unsustainable uses of wild species and biodiversity loss ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eBBCkDwT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018; FAO, 2018b, 2020a; HLPE, 2017; IPBES, 2016, 2019d)","plainCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018; FAO, 2018b, 2020a; HLPE, 2017; IPBES, 2016, 2019d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1448,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SERE3KJN"],"itemData":{"id":1448,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Nontimber forest products (NTFPs) are fundamental to the functioning of healthy forests and play vital roles in the cultures and economies of the people of the United States. However, these plants and fungi used for food, medicine, and other purposes have not been fully incorporated into management, policy, and resource valuation. This report is a forest-sectorwide assessment of the state of the knowledge regarding NTFPs science and management information for U.S. forests and rangelands (and hereafter referred to as the NTFP assessment). The NTFP assessment serves as a baseline science synthesis and provides information for managing nontimber forest resources in the United States. In addition, this NTFP assessment provides information for national-level reporting on natural capital and the ecosystem services NTFPs provide. The report also provides technical input to the 2017 National Climate Assessment (NCA) under development by the U.S. Global Change Research Program (USGCRP).","DOI":"10.2737/SRS-GTR-232","issue":"June","page":"267","title":"Assessment of Nontimber Forest Products in the United States Under Changing Conditions. A report for the United States Department of Agriculture. General Technical Report SRS-232.","URL":"https://doi.org/10.2737/SRS-GTR-232","author":[{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla. R."},{"family":"Patel-Weynard","given":"Toral."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEAU4TNP"],"itemData":{"id":35107,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"eng","note":"978-92-5-130561-4","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"The State of the world's forests 2018 - Forests pathways to sustainable development","editor":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":"BOVAcHko/MTNp8Tsl","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A6FP6AF5"],"itemData":{"id":123716,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132974-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1195470916","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Global Forest Resources Assessment (FRA) 2020: main report","title-short":"GLOBAL FOREST RESOURCES ASSESSMENT (FRA) 2020","URL":"https://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8753en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30174,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HC35DGNH"],"itemData":{"id":30174,"type":"report","abstract":"This report is an evidence-based, comprehensive analysis of the diverse, direct and indirect, contributions of forests and trees to FSN. Chapter 1 examines the linkages between forests\\nand FSN and proposes, for the purpose of this report, a conceptual framework and a forest typology grounded on management criteria. Chapter 2 provides an in-depth analysis of the channels through which forests and trees contribute to FSN. Chapter 3 reviews the state of the world’s forests and identifies challenges and opportunities for forestry in relation to FSN. Chapter 4 is solution-oriented and discusses how to optimize the contributions of forests and trees to FSN in a sustainable manner.","collection-title":"HLPE Report Series","event-place":"Rome","genre":"Report of the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition","language":"English","note":"I739EN/1/06.17","number":"HLPE Report 11","page":"136","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Zotero","title":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition:  A report by the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition of the Committee on World Food Security","title-short":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/a-i7395e.pdf","author":[{"family":"HLPE","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",6]]}}},{"id":35028,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YG7LF893","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AGYQARR9"],"itemData":{"id":35028,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-92-807-3568-0","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","title":"Summary for policymakers of the assessment report of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services on pollinators, pollination and food production.","URL":"https://www.ipbes.net/sites/default/files/spm_deliverable_3a_pollination_20170222.pdf","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"Potts","given":"S. G."},{"family":"Imperatriz-Fonseca","given":"V. L."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H. T."},{"family":"Biesmeijer","given":"J. C."},{"family":"Breeze","given":"T. D."},{"family":"Dicks","given":"L. V."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Hill","given":"R."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Vanbergen","given":"A. J."},{"family":"Aizen","given":"M. A."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"S. A."},{"family":"Eardley","given":"C."},{"family":"Freitas","given":"B. M."},{"family":"Gallai","given":"N."},{"family":"Kevan","given":"P. G."},{"family":"Kovács-Hostyánszki","given":"A."},{"family":"Kwapong","given":"P. K."},{"family":"Li","given":"J."},{"family":"Li","given":"X."},{"family":"Martins","given":"D. J."},{"family":"Nates-Parra","given":"G."},{"family":"J. S. Pettis","given":"R. Rader"},{"family":"Viana","given":"B. F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. All assessments in this review identify governance as an important factor in support of sustainable uses of wild species (see section 1.1.2 and 1.3 for a more detailed discussion on governance as a driver of the sustainable use of wild species). Several document positive ecological and social outcomes from decentralized, multi-sectoral approaches in which decision making and management authority do not rest solely at the level of the nation state ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Tg2P9lj5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Forest Peoples Program, 2020; IPBES, 2016, 2018a, 2018b; Vira, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Forest Peoples Program, 2020; IPBES, 2016, 2018a, 2018b; Vira, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30471,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6ZY9V68D"],"itemData":{"id":30471,"type":"report","event-place":"Moreton-in-Marsh, England","publisher":"Forest Peoples Programme","publisher-place":"Moreton-in-Marsh, England","title":"Local Biodiversity Outlooks 2: The contributions of indigenous peoples and local communities to the implementation of the Strategic Plan for Biodiversity 2011–2020 and to renewing nature and cultures. A complement to the fifth edition of Global Biodiversity Outlook.","URL":"www.localbiodiversityoutlooks.net","author":[{"family":"Forest Peoples Program","given":""},{"family":"International Indigenous Forum on Biodiversity","given":""},{"family":"Indigenous Women’s Biodiversity Network","given":""},{"family":"Centres of Distinction on Indigenous and Local Knowledge","given":""},{"family":"CBD","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":35028,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YG7LF893","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AGYQARR9"],"itemData":{"id":35028,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-92-807-3568-0","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","title":"Summary for policymakers of the assessment report of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services on pollinators, pollination and food production.","URL":"https://www.ipbes.net/sites/default/files/spm_deliverable_3a_pollination_20170222.pdf","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"Potts","given":"S. G."},{"family":"Imperatriz-Fonseca","given":"V. L."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H. T."},{"family":"Biesmeijer","given":"J. C."},{"family":"Breeze","given":"T. D."},{"family":"Dicks","given":"L. V."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Hill","given":"R."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Vanbergen","given":"A. J."},{"family":"Aizen","given":"M. A."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"S. A."},{"family":"Eardley","given":"C."},{"family":"Freitas","given":"B. M."},{"family":"Gallai","given":"N."},{"family":"Kevan","given":"P. G."},{"family":"Kovács-Hostyánszki","given":"A."},{"family":"Kwapong","given":"P. K."},{"family":"Li","given":"J."},{"family":"Li","given":"X."},{"family":"Martins","given":"D. J."},{"family":"Nates-Parra","given":"G."},{"family":"J. S. Pettis","given":"R. Rader"},{"family":"Viana","given":"B. F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":35018,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NHSBQV7E","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EY6CU4ER"],"itemData":{"id":35018,"type":"book","abstract":"Reproduction This publication may be reproduced in whole or in part and in any form for educational or non-profit services without special permission from the copyright holder, provided acknowledgement of the source is made. The IPBES secretariat would appreciate receiving a copy of any publication that uses this publication as a source. No use of this publication may be made for resale or any other commercial purpose whatsoever without prior permission in writing from the IPBES secretariat. Applications for such permission, with a statement of the purpose and extent of the reproduction, should be addressed to the IPBES secretariat. The use of information from this publication concerning proprietary products for publicity or advertising is not permitted. Traceable accounts The chapter references enclosed in curly brackets (e.g. {2.3.1, 2.3.1.2, 2.3.1.3}) are traceable accounts and refer to sections of the chapters of the IPBES Regional Assessment Report on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services for Africa. A traceable account is a description within the corresponding texts of these chapters, reflecting the evaluation of the type, amount, quality, and consistency of evidence and the degree of agreement for that particular statement or key finding.","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-00-3","number-of-pages":"49","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the regional assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services for Africa of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"http://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3236178","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"literal":"E. Archer"},{"literal":"L. E. Dziba"},{"literal":"K. J. Mulongoy"},{"literal":"M. A. Maoela"},{"literal":"M. Walters"},{"literal":"R. Biggs"},{"literal":"M-C. Cormier-Salem"},{"literal":"F. DeClerck"},{"literal":"M. C. Diaw"},{"literal":"A. E. Dunham"},{"literal":"P. Failler"},{"literal":"C. Gordon"},{"literal":"K. A. Harhash"},{"literal":"R. Kasisi"},{"literal":"F. Kizito"},{"literal":"W. D. Nyingi"},{"literal":"N. Oguge"},{"literal":"B. Osman-Elasha"},{"literal":"L. C. Stringer"},{"literal":"L. Tito de Morais"},{"literal":"A. Assogbadjo"},{"literal":"B. N. Egoh"},{"literal":"M. W. Halmy"},{"literal":"K. Heubach"},{"literal":"A. Mensah"},{"literal":"L. Pereira"},{"literal":"N. Sitas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35013,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XW6KN3MC","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GWN68BXX"],"itemData":{"id":35013,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-02-7","number-of-pages":"41","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the regional assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services for Asia and the Pacific of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3237373","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"literal":"M. Karki"},{"literal":"S. Senaratna Sellamuttu"},{"literal":"S. Okayasu"},{"literal":"W. Suzuki"},{"literal":"L. A. Acosta"},{"literal":"Y. Alhafedh"},{"literal":"J. A. Anticamara"},{"literal":"A. G. Ausseil"},{"literal":"K. Davies"},{"literal":"A. Gasparatos"},{"literal":"H. Gundimeda"},{"literal":"I. Faridah-Hanum"},{"literal":"R. Kohsaka"},{"literal":"R. Kumar"},{"literal":"S. Managi"},{"literal":"N. Wu"},{"literal":"A. Rajvanshi"},{"literal":"G. S. Rawat"},{"literal":"P. Riordan"},{"literal":"S. Sharma"},{"literal":"A. Virk"},{"literal":"C. Wang"},{"literal":"T. Yahara"},{"literal":"Y. C. Youn"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4CNIVICV"],"itemData":{"id":35087,"type":"report","abstract":"As population estimates for 2050 reach over 9 billion, issues of food security and nutrition have been dominating academic and policy debates, especially in relation to the global development agenda beyond 2015. A total of 805 million people are undernourished worldwide and malnutrition affects nearly every country on the planet. There is considerable evidence that forests and tree-based systems can play an important role in complementing agricultural production in providing better and more nutritionally-balanced diets; wood fueld for cooking; greater control over food consumption choices, particularly during lean seasons and periods of vulnerability (especially for marginalised groups); and deliver a broad set of ecosystem services which enhance and support crop production. While precise figures are difficult to come by, it has been estimated that approximately 1.2-1.5 biooion people (just under 20 percent of the global population) are forest dependent. These estimates include about 60 million indigenous people who are almost wholly dependent on forests. The loss and degradation of forests exacerbate the problem of food insecurity both directly and indirectly. However, the complex, overlapping and interconnecting processes which link tree products and services to food security and nutrition are currently not adequately represented in forestry, agriculture, food or nutrition-related strategies at global and national levels, though their importance is often well known at more local scales by consumers, forest producers and farmers.    While the evidence base for the role of forests and tree-based systems for food security and nutrition is growing, there remain many gaps in our understanding of this relationship and its potential contribution to reducing global hunger and malnutrition. There is a need to explore the forest-food nexus in much more detail, particularly in relation to the integrated management of multi-functional landscapes, ad the multi-scalar and cross-sectoral governance approaches that are required for the equitable deliver of these benefits. This report documents evidence of the relationships between forests and tree-based systems and food security and nutrition from different agro-ecological zones in all continents. The systems included inour analysis include management of forests to optimise yields of wild foods and fodder.","collection-title":"IUFRO World Series","event-place":"Vienna","genre":"IUFRO World Series","language":"English","note":"978-3-902762-40-5","number":"Volume 33","page":"172","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","publisher-place":"Vienna","title":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition. A global assessment report","title-short":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition","URL":"https://www.iufro.org/science/gfep/forests-and-food-security-panel/report/","author":[{"family":"Vira","given":"Bhaskar"},{"family":"Wildburger","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Mansourian","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Rather, they recommend approaches that bring together the public and private sectors, non-governmental organizations, and local communities and integrate multi-sectoral actors from resource management, health, education, and others ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KuFXgchB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vira, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Vira, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4CNIVICV"],"itemData":{"id":35087,"type":"report","abstract":"As population estimates for 2050 reach over 9 billion, issues of food security and nutrition have been dominating academic and policy debates, especially in relation to the global development agenda beyond 2015. A total of 805 million people are undernourished worldwide and malnutrition affects nearly every country on the planet. There is considerable evidence that forests and tree-based systems can play an important role in complementing agricultural production in providing better and more nutritionally-balanced diets; wood fueld for cooking; greater control over food consumption choices, particularly during lean seasons and periods of vulnerability (especially for marginalised groups); and deliver a broad set of ecosystem services which enhance and support crop production. While precise figures are difficult to come by, it has been estimated that approximately 1.2-1.5 biooion people (just under 20 percent of the global population) are forest dependent. These estimates include about 60 million indigenous people who are almost wholly dependent on forests. The loss and degradation of forests exacerbate the problem of food insecurity both directly and indirectly. However, the complex, overlapping and interconnecting processes which link tree products and services to food security and nutrition are currently not adequately represented in forestry, agriculture, food or nutrition-related strategies at global and national levels, though their importance is often well known at more local scales by consumers, forest producers and farmers.    While the evidence base for the role of forests and tree-based systems for food security and nutrition is growing, there remain many gaps in our understanding of this relationship and its potential contribution to reducing global hunger and malnutrition. There is a need to explore the forest-food nexus in much more detail, particularly in relation to the integrated management of multi-functional landscapes, ad the multi-scalar and cross-sectoral governance approaches that are required for the equitable deliver of these benefits. This report documents evidence of the relationships between forests and tree-based systems and food security and nutrition from different agro-ecological zones in all continents. The systems included inour analysis include management of forests to optimise yields of wild foods and fodder.","collection-title":"IUFRO World Series","event-place":"Vienna","genre":"IUFRO World Series","language":"English","note":"978-3-902762-40-5","number":"Volume 33","page":"172","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","publisher-place":"Vienna","title":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition. A global assessment report","title-short":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition","URL":"https://www.iufro.org/science/gfep/forests-and-food-security-panel/report/","author":[{"family":"Vira","given":"Bhaskar"},{"family":"Wildburger","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Mansourian","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Acknowledgement of and support for indigenous and local knowledge, tenure, and access to resources, including genetic resources, is identified as a key step toward sustainable use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ilMmjNtk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Forest Peoples Program, 2020; HLPE, 2017; IPBES, 2016, 2018e; Vira, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Forest Peoples Program, 2020; HLPE, 2017; IPBES, 2016, 2018e; Vira, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30471,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6ZY9V68D"],"itemData":{"id":30471,"type":"report","event-place":"Moreton-in-Marsh, England","publisher":"Forest Peoples Programme","publisher-place":"Moreton-in-Marsh, England","title":"Local Biodiversity Outlooks 2: The contributions of indigenous peoples and local communities to the implementation of the Strategic Plan for Biodiversity 2011–2020 and to renewing nature and cultures. A complement to the fifth edition of Global Biodiversity Outlook.","URL":"www.localbiodiversityoutlooks.net","author":[{"family":"Forest Peoples Program","given":""},{"family":"International Indigenous Forum on Biodiversity","given":""},{"family":"Indigenous Women’s Biodiversity Network","given":""},{"family":"Centres of Distinction on Indigenous and Local Knowledge","given":""},{"family":"CBD","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30174,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HC35DGNH"],"itemData":{"id":30174,"type":"report","abstract":"This report is an evidence-based, comprehensive analysis of the diverse, direct and indirect, contributions of forests and trees to FSN. Chapter 1 examines the linkages between forests\\nand FSN and proposes, for the purpose of this report, a conceptual framework and a forest typology grounded on management criteria. Chapter 2 provides an in-depth analysis of the channels through which forests and trees contribute to FSN. Chapter 3 reviews the state of the world’s forests and identifies challenges and opportunities for forestry in relation to FSN. Chapter 4 is solution-oriented and discusses how to optimize the contributions of forests and trees to FSN in a sustainable manner.","collection-title":"HLPE Report Series","event-place":"Rome","genre":"Report of the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition","languag