. F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":86,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KXQGTCY8"],"itemData":{"id":86,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-09-6","number-of-pages":"744","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"The IPBES assessment report on Land Degradation and Restoration","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3237392","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"Montanarella","given":"L"},{"family":"Scholes","given":"R"},{"family":"Brainich","given":"A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4CNIVICV"],"itemData":{"id":35087,"type":"report","abstract":"As population estimates for 2050 reach over 9 billion, issues of food security and nutrition have been dominating academic and policy debates, especially in relation to the global development agenda beyond 2015. A total of 805 million people are undernourished worldwide and malnutrition affects nearly every country on the planet. There is considerable evidence that forests and tree-based systems can play an important role in complementing agricultural production in providing better and more nutritionally-balanced diets; wood fueld for cooking; greater control over food consumption choices, particularly during lean seasons and periods of vulnerability (especially for marginalised groups); and deliver a broad set of ecosystem services which enhance and support crop production. While precise figures are difficult to come by, it has been estimated that approximately 1.2-1.5 biooion people (just under 20 percent of the global population) are forest dependent. These estimates include about 60 million indigenous people who are almost wholly dependent on forests. The loss and degradation of forests exacerbate the problem of food insecurity both directly and indirectly. However, the complex, overlapping and interconnecting processes which link tree products and services to food security and nutrition are currently not adequately represented in forestry, agriculture, food or nutrition-related strategies at global and national levels, though their importance is often well known at more local scales by consumers, forest producers and farmers.    While the evidence base for the role of forests and tree-based systems for food security and nutrition is growing, there remain many gaps in our understanding of this relationship and its potential contribution to reducing global hunger and malnutrition. There is a need to explore the forest-food nexus in much more detail, particularly in relation to the integrated management of multi-functional landscapes, ad the multi-scalar and cross-sectoral governance approaches that are required for the equitable deliver of these benefits. This report documents evidence of the relationships between forests and tree-based systems and food security and nutrition from different agro-ecological zones in all continents. The systems included inour analysis include management of forests to optimise yields of wild foods and fodder.","collection-title":"IUFRO World Series","event-place":"Vienna","genre":"IUFRO World Series","language":"English","note":"978-3-902762-40-5","number":"Volume 33","page":"172","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","publisher-place":"Vienna","title":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition. A global assessment report","title-short":"Forests, trees and landscapes for food security and nutrition","URL":"https://www.iufro.org/science/gfep/forests-and-food-security-panel/report/","author":[{"family":"Vira","given":"Bhaskar"},{"family":"Wildburger","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Mansourian","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Likewise, the importance of a rights-based approach with particular importance given to complying with international human rights laws and standards in all governance instruments and processes is noted as fundamental to governing the use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GFBukIwa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Forest Peoples Program, 2020; HLPE, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Forest Peoples Program, 2020; HLPE, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30471,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6ZY9V68D"],"itemData":{"id":30471,"type":"report","event-place":"Moreton-in-Marsh, England","publisher":"Forest Peoples Programme","publisher-place":"Moreton-in-Marsh, England","title":"Local Biodiversity Outlooks 2: The contributions of indigenous peoples and local communities to the implementation of the Strategic Plan for Biodiversity 2011–2020 and to renewing nature and cultures. A complement to the fifth edition of Global Biodiversity Outlook.","URL":"www.localbiodiversityoutlooks.net","author":[{"family":"Forest Peoples Program","given":""},{"family":"International Indigenous Forum on Biodiversity","given":""},{"family":"Indigenous Women’s Biodiversity Network","given":""},{"family":"Centres of Distinction on Indigenous and Local Knowledge","given":""},{"family":"CBD","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30174,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HC35DGNH"],"itemData":{"id":30174,"type":"report","abstract":"This report is an evidence-based, comprehensive analysis of the diverse, direct and indirect, contributions of forests and trees to FSN. Chapter 1 examines the linkages between forests\\nand FSN and proposes, for the purpose of this report, a conceptual framework and a forest typology grounded on management criteria. Chapter 2 provides an in-depth analysis of the channels through which forests and trees contribute to FSN. Chapter 3 reviews the state of the world’s forests and identifies challenges and opportunities for forestry in relation to FSN. Chapter 4 is solution-oriented and discusses how to optimize the contributions of forests and trees to FSN in a sustainable manner.","collection-title":"HLPE Report Series","event-place":"Rome","genre":"Report of the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition","language":"English","note":"I739EN/1/06.17","number":"HLPE Report 11","page":"136","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Zotero","title":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition:  A report by the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition of the Committee on World Food Security","title-short":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/a-i7395e.pdf","author":[{"family":"HLPE","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      No assessment on non-extractive practices was identified, but some activities associated with nature-based tourism have been the focus of global reports ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eNyRucur","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(INTOSAI WGEA, 2013; Tapper, 2006)","plainCitation":"(INTOSAI WGEA, 2013; Tapper, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32782,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQDLDNH8"],"itemData":{"id":32782,"type":"report","page":"44","title":"Impact of Tourism on Wildlife Conservation","URL":"http://iced.cag.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2014/02/2013_wgea_Wild-Life_view.pdf","author":[{"family":"INTOSAI WGEA","given":""}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Karis","given":"Alar"},{"family":"Utouh","given":"Ludovick"},{"family":"Liphafa","given":"Lucy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":30175,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V8AECTQY"],"itemData":{"id":30175,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","note":"ISBN  3 – 937429 – 10 – 7","page":"68","publisher":"UNEP/CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife Watching and Tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","author":[{"family":"Tapper","given":"Richard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      A study conducted in the early 2000s found that 20% to 40% of all international tourists included some form of wildlife watching in their trip. Tourism focusing on wild species sustains the whole tourism sector in some countries, and provides income and employment to people such as guides and drivers, as well as employees in the hotel and food services industries. Wildlife watching tourism “ranked as one of the top three export sectors for more than three-quarters of all developing countries in 2000, and was the principle export in a third of these countries” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DRNzuGAS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tapper, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Tapper, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30175,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V8AECTQY"],"itemData":{"id":30175,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","note":"ISBN  3 – 937429 – 10 – 7","page":"68","publisher":"UNEP/CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife Watching and Tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","author":[{"family":"Tapper","given":"Richard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Globally, “wildlife tourism” was estimated to represent 9% of gross domestic product in 2011 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gnjs1LNr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(INTOSAI WGEA, 2013)","plainCitation":"(INTOSAI WGEA, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32782,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQDLDNH8"],"itemData":{"id":32782,"type":"report","page":"44","title":"Impact of Tourism on Wildlife Conservation","URL":"http://iced.cag.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2014/02/2013_wgea_Wild-Life_view.pdf","author":[{"family":"INTOSAI WGEA","given":""}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Karis","given":"Alar"},{"family":"Utouh","given":"Ludovick"},{"family":"Liphafa","given":"Lucy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Because protected areas are often wildlife watching destinations it also can support biodiversity conservation. It is therefore in the interest of national or local governments, but also of the tourism businesses, to support conservation of those areasITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Xt1aNuVR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(INTOSAI WGEA, 2013; Tapper, 2006)","plainCitation":"(INTOSAI WGEA, 2013; Tapper, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32782,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQDLDNH8"],"itemData":{"id":32782,"type":"report","page":"44","title":"Impact of Tourism on Wildlife Conservation","URL":"http://iced.cag.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2014/02/2013_wgea_Wild-Life_view.pdf","author":[{"family":"INTOSAI WGEA","given":""}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Karis","given":"Alar"},{"family":"Utouh","given":"Ludovick"},{"family":"Liphafa","given":"Lucy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":30175,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V8AECTQY"],"itemData":{"id":30175,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","note":"ISBN  3 – 937429 – 10 – 7","page":"68","publisher":"UNEP/CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife Watching and Tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","author":[{"family":"Tapper","given":"Richard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. . 
      In the early 2000s, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"u4PQjwV4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tapper, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Tapper, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30175,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V8AECTQY"],"itemData":{"id":30175,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","note":"ISBN  3 – 937429 – 10 – 7","page":"68","publisher":"UNEP/CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife Watching and Tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","author":[{"family":"Tapper","given":"Richard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} noted that little was known about the actual income generated by wildlife watching or the impacts of tourism activities on the targeted species. Auditing of the activities around wildlife watching, to assess their ecological and social impacts, were still scarce ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FvVWjGem","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(INTOSAI WGEA, 2013)","plainCitation":"(INTOSAI WGEA, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32782,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQDLDNH8"],"itemData":{"id":32782,"type":"report","page":"44","title":"Impact of Tourism on Wildlife Conservation","URL":"http://iced.cag.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2014/02/2013_wgea_Wild-Life_view.pdf","author":[{"family":"INTOSAI WGEA","given":""}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Karis","given":"Alar"},{"family":"Utouh","given":"Ludovick"},{"family":"Liphafa","given":"Lucy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another report by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PqcBhwZk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tonazzini, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Tonazzini, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1410,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LFBTSJJU"],"itemData":{"id":1410,"type":"report","event-place":"Barcelona, Spain","page":"145","publisher":"eco-union","publisher-place":"Barcelona, Spain","title":"Blue Tourism. Towards a sustainable coastal and maritime tourism in world marine regions.","author":[{"family":"Tonazzini","given":"Debora"},{"family":"Fosse","given":"Jérémie"},{"family":"Morales","given":"Eloïse"},{"family":"González","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Klarwein","given":"Salvador"},{"family":"Moukaddem","given":"Karine"},{"family":"Louveau","given":"Oriane"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, focusing on coastal and marine tourism concluded, as did Tapper, that to avoid negative impacts, tourist density should be constrained. When nature-based tourism is over developed, it can have negative impacts on the wild species through increased disturbance, and on the local communities who risk being excluded from the benefits with the arrival of bigger, external players investing in tourism activities or through the commodification of people’s culture. The emphasis of the economy on wild species may also contribute to an increase in local conflicts between people and wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TvGy5Y7P","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(INTOSAI WGEA, 2013)","plainCitation":"(INTOSAI WGEA, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32782,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQDLDNH8"],"itemData":{"id":32782,"type":"report","page":"44","title":"Impact of Tourism on Wildlife Conservation","URL":"http://iced.cag.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2014/02/2013_wgea_Wild-Life_view.pdf","author":[{"family":"INTOSAI WGEA","given":""}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Karis","given":"Alar"},{"family":"Utouh","given":"Ludovick"},{"family":"Liphafa","given":"Lucy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Further, if it becomes a main or single source of income, households and communities are vulnerable to the variations in tourist flows within and across the years. 
      The picture of the relationship between human uses of wild species and biodiversity that emerges from this review of previous assessments is far from simple. Opportunities for and outcomes of human uses of wild species are mediated by complex, interacting factors including environmental, economic, social, and cultural contexts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BWsbiKFT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(HLPE, 2017)","plainCitation":"(HLPE, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30174,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HC35DGNH"],"itemData":{"id":30174,"type":"report","abstract":"This report is an evidence-based, comprehensive analysis of the diverse, direct and indirect, contributions of forests and trees to FSN. Chapter 1 examines the linkages between forests\\nand FSN and proposes, for the purpose of this report, a conceptual framework and a forest typology grounded on management criteria. Chapter 2 provides an in-depth analysis of the channels through which forests and trees contribute to FSN. Chapter 3 reviews the state of the world’s forests and identifies challenges and opportunities for forestry in relation to FSN. Chapter 4 is solution-oriented and discusses how to optimize the contributions of forests and trees to FSN in a sustainable manner.","collection-title":"HLPE Report Series","event-place":"Rome","genre":"Report of the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition","language":"English","note":"I739EN/1/06.17","number":"HLPE Report 11","page":"136","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Zotero","title":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition:  A report by the High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition of the Committee on World Food Security","title-short":"Sustainable forestry for food security and nutrition","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/a-i7395e.pdf","author":[{"family":"HLPE","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are clearly documented instances in which the relationship between biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people through the use of wild species is reciprocal; nature’s contributions to people depend on biodiversity and much biodiversity is maintained through indigenous and local knowledge and practices related to the use of wild species. Equally, there are well-documented cases in which the use of wild species result in biodiversity losses. There are both rich sources of information on these processes and significant gaps in data and synthesis of existing knowledge from diverse sources. This impedes understanding of these processes and of the pathways to greater social and ecological sustainability of the use of wild species. 
Contributions of the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species  
The IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species is the first international endeavor to be fully comprehensive of all practices and uses associated with the sustainable uses of wild species. While many previous assessments focus on drivers of unsustainable uses of wild species and their consequences for biodiversity, the mandate of the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species is to identify solutions that enhance their sustainable uses. To do so, this assessment uses a groundbreaking conceptualization that enables analysis of any type of use of any species (see the organizing structure in section 1.3.4) and supports analyses across scales and comparison of successes and failures of interventions across practices. The framing of the analysis within the sustainability issue is also a novel aspect of this assessment. Building on assessments that have come before, the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species approaches the task through systematic review of the scientific literature and and addressing and working with indigenous and local knowledge to fill gaps in knowledge and identify pathways to enhanced sustainability of the use of wild species that contribute to conservation and development. 
      Regarding fishing and logging, the sustainable use assessment draws from the work of the FAO and especially the State of the World's Forests and the State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture, which focus on the resources (respectively, timber and non-timber forest products, and fish and shellfish,), in order to present the various uses associated with these species and where, how and why those uses are sustainable. Regarding terrestrial animal harvesting, gathering and non-extractive uses, the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species similarly draws the bigger picture related to those practices, while also collating for the first time global data and evidence on those species' uses. This assessment also gives a clear picture of where gaps are in data and synthesis of existing knowledge around the sustainable use of wild species (see supplementary material S1.1). As those gaps are filled, understanding of pathways to greater social-ecological sustainability of the use of wild species will improve.

