    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/SUA22> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """
Enhancing the sustainable use of wild species could provide benefits to both people and nature, but transformative change is needed if these benefits are to be realized. Given the vast diversity of life on earth, and the range of contexts and values that shape human uses of wild species, a pluralistic approach will be required, which recognizes and celebrates diversity in the relationships between people and wild species (also see Section 5.8). As the previous section has indicated, the Nature Futures Framework may also be a useful tool to help to envision these transformations and highlight leverage points and pathways. In this section, we explore some approaches towards transformative change as applied to scenarios of sustainable use.
5.9.1  Transformative change, scenarios, and sustainable use
Transformative change through “deliberative transformations” (i.e., those caused by intentional interventions) very often involves a move towards collaborative adaptive management – which is precipitated by crisis or turmoil ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cR3CsZ3q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gelcich, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Gelcich, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2013,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EVV9MAHV"],"itemData":{"id":2013,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1012021107","issue":"39","page":"16794-16799","title":"Navigating transformations in governance of Chilean marine coastal resources","volume":"107","author":[{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"T.P."},{"family":"Olsson","given":"P."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"Defeo","given":"O."},{"family":"Fernández","given":"M."},{"family":"Foale","given":"S."},{"family":"Gunderson","given":"L.H."},{"family":"Rodríguez-Sickert","given":"C."},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"M."},{"family":"Steneck","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Castilla","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Actors such as policy makers, donor agencies, non-governmental organizations, private corporations, and scientists who intervene in the right times and places can play a catalytic role when acting in appropriate ways at the right place and at the right time ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a408dn6ptr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Olsson, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Olsson, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20662,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9EYRSBEA"],"itemData":{"id":20662,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-003-0101-7","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Adaptive Comanagement for Building Resilience in Social?Ecological Systems","title-short":"Adaptive Comanagement for Building Resilience in Social?","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-003-0101-7","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Olsson","given":"Per"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Regulation has been a predominant approach to controlling wild species use. Regulations can take multiple forms, from strict spatial and species-specific prohibitions to rules for how and where species can be used (e.g. gear restrictions in fisheries, protected areas) and in what quantities (e.g. quotas). Some form of regulation is often necessary to support the sustainable use of wild species; however, it is not usually sufficient for positive transformative outcomes. Firstly, in order to be effective, regulations require appropriate compliance management, such as through monitoring and enforcement. Secondly, excessive and indiscriminate regulation can undermine incentives for sustainable use and lead to polarized narratives and an over-focus on illegality ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iiphFvbu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Challender, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Challender, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":3900,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YTYZQRIJ"],"itemData":{"id":3900,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"International trade in wildlife is a key threat to biodiversity conservation. CITES, the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, seeks to ensure international wildlife trade is sustainable, relying on trade bans and controls. However, there has been little comprehensive review of its effectiveness and here we review approaches taken to regulate wildlife trade in CITES. Although assessing its effectiveness is problematic, we assert that CITES boasts few measurable conservation successes. We attribute this to: non-compliance, an over reliance on regulation, lack of knowledge and monitoring of listed species, ignorance of market forces, and influence among CITES actors. To more effectively manage trade we argue that interventions should go beyond regulation and should be multi-faceted, reflecting the complexity of wildlife trade. To inform these interventions we assert an intensive research effort is needed around six key areas: (1) factors undermining wildlife trade governance at the national level, (2) determining sustainable harvest rates for, and adaptive management of CITES species, (3) gaining the buy-in of local communities in implementing CITES, (4) supply and demand based market interventions, (5) means of quantifying illicit trade, and (6) political processes and influence within CITES.","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/J.GECCO.2014.11.010","ISSN":"2351-9894","page":"129–148","title":"Towards informed and multi-faceted wildlife trade interventions","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Challender","given":"Daniel W.S."},{"family":"Harrop","given":"Stuart R."},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"Douglas C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This may drive ‘vicious cycles’ that constrain pathways to transformative change (Figure 5.11). Yet if appropriately and anticipatorily governed via a mix of regulatory and economic instruments which are aligned with a plurality of values and visions, wild species can be sustainable used (noting that ‘use’ can be consumptive and non-consumptive, as per the Nature Futures Framework, Figure 5.9). It can simultaneously support the pursuit of the Sustainable Development Goals (Box 5.10) and international conservation goals such as the Convention on Biological Diversity post-2020 framework ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"k59zQpfz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u8216{}t Sas-Rolfes, 2019)","plainCitation":"(‘t Sas-Rolfes, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19119,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2XMEHQUI"],"itemData":{"id":19119,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Illegal wildlife trade (IWT) has increased in profile in recent years as a global policy issue, largely because of its association with declines in prominent internationally trafficked species. In this review, we explore the scale of IWT, associated threats to biodiversity, and appropriate responses to these threats. We discuss the historical development of IWT research and highlight the uncertainties that plague the evidence base, emphasizing the need for more systematic approaches to addressing evidence gaps in a way that minimizes the risk of unethical or counterproductive outcomes for wildlife and people. We highlight the need for evaluating interventions in order to learn, and the importance of sharing datasets and lessons learned. A more collaborative approach to linking IWT research, practice, and policy would better align public policy discourse and action with research evidence. This in turn would enable more effective policy making that contributes to reducing the threat to biodiversity that IWT represents.","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033253","ISSN":"1543-5938, 1545-2050","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Environ. Resour.","language":"en","page":"201-228","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Illegal Wildlife Trade: Scale, Processes, and Governance","title-short":"Illegal Wildlife Trade","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"‘t Sas-Rolfes","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Challender","given":"Daniel W.S."},{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Diogo"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Importantly, there is a need to understand trade-offs between the costs and benefits of different types of wild species use, how interventions might enhance or exacerbate them, and for whom (Box 5.10). A plurality of values should be considered when understanding these costs and benefits (e.g. economic, social, ethical), as per the Nature Futures Framework (and also see Section 5.8). In particular, the value systems of people who will be most affected by interventions need to be foremost in the design of these interventions.
      An appropriate mix of interventions, predicated on a good understanding of such costs and benefits, could promote a transition from vicious cycles of unsustainable use to virtuous cycles of sustainable use (Figure 5.11a). In order to be effective, these interventions need to target both micro-level changes to transform individual human actions, and macro-level changes, which can transform social structures and norms ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cJ8vubFD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Naito, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Naito, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19374,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVHB6E74"],"itemData":{"id":19374,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"SocArxiv. Paper","DOI":"10.31235/osf.io/5zmxd","language":"pt","title":"An integrative framework for transformative social change: A case in global wild species trade","author":[{"family":"Naito","given":"R."},{"family":"Zhao","given":"J."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K.M.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, regulations can act as structural interventions which recraft the choice environment; while behavioral interventions - such as enforcement of regulations, positive economic incentives or promotion of good-will values - can address socio-psychological barriers and act as enablers which promote pro-environmental social change (Figure 5.11b).

a)

b)

Figure 5.11. a) The vicious cycle of unsustainable use and the virtuous cycle of sustainable use, with illustrations of how leverage points can cause shifts between them. These leverage points need to be applied in concert to obtain transformative change; one alone is unlikely to shift the system effectively. b) an integrative framework for pro-environmental social change. Source: Adapted from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0LE9kZsH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Naito, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Naito, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19374,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVHB6E74"],"itemData":{"id":19374,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"SocArxiv. Paper","DOI":"10.31235/osf.io/5zmxd","language":"pt","title":"An integrative framework for transformative social change: A case in global wild species trade","author":[{"family":"Naito","given":"R."},{"family":"Zhao","given":"J."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K.M.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Copyright © 2022, Springer Japan KK, part of Springer Nature, under license CC BY-NC-ND 4.0.

Box 5.10. Wild species use and sustainable development.
Enhancing the sustainable use of wild species requires a holistic understanding of how different use regimes can contribute to society. Moreover, by focusing on an outcome goal such as ‘sustainable development’, heterogeneous pathways to this goal can be devised. The figure below shows illustrative examples of how interventions under differing value systems aligned with the Nature Futures Framework can alter progress towards the Sustainable Development Goals relative to a business-as-usual framework.  

Figure 5.12: Positive (green) and negative (red) contributions of trade in horseshoe bats (top row) and waterfowl (bottom row) to the Sustainable Development Goals under a ‘business-as-usual’ scenario (left column) and under the effects of specified trade interventions (right column), specifically a ‘Nature for Nature’ approach (top right) and a ‘Nature for Society’ approach (bottom right). Source: Adapted from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gIWTVSTE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Booth, Arias,, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Booth, Arias,, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19128,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WV4T2QRV"],"itemData":{"id":19128,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The COVID-19 pandemic has caused huge loss of life, and immense social and economic harm. Wildlife trade has become central to discourse on COVID-19, zoonotic pandemics, and related policy responses, which must focus on “saving lives, protecting livelihoods, and safeguarding nature.” Proposed policy responses have included extreme measures such as banning all use and trade of wildlife, or blanket measures for entire Classes. However, different trades pose varying degrees of risk for zoonotic pandemics, while some trades also play critical roles in delivering other key aspects of sustainable development, particularly related to poverty and hunger alleviation, decent work, responsible consumption and production, and life on land and below water. Here we describe how wildlife trade contributes to the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) in diverse ways, with synergies and trade-offs within and between the SDGs. In doing so, we show that prohibitions could result in severe trade-offs against some SDGs, with limited benefits for public health via pandemic prevention. This complexity necessitates context-specific policies, with multi-sector decision-making that goes beyond simple top-down solutions. We encourage decision-makers to adopt a risk-based approach to wildlife trade policy post-COVID-19, with policies formulated via participatory, evidence-based approaches, which explicitly acknowledge uncertainty, complexity, and conflicting values across different components of the SDGs. This should help to ensure that future use and trade of wildlife is safe, environmentally sustainable and socially just.","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.3389/fevo.2021.639216","ISSN":"2296-701X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Ecol. Evol.","page":"639216","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"“Saving Lives, Protecting Livelihoods, and Safeguarding Nature”: Risk-Based Wildlife Trade Policy for Sustainable Development Outcomes Post-COVID-19","title-short":"“Saving Lives, Protecting Livelihoods, and Safeguarding Nature”","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Booth","given":"Hollie"},{"family":"Arias","given":"Melissa"},{"family":"Brittain","given":"Stephanie"},{"family":"Challender","given":"Daniel W. S."},{"family":"Khanyari","given":"Munib"},{"family":"Kuiper","given":"Timothy"},{"family":"Li","given":"Yuhan"},{"family":"Olmedo","given":"Alegria"},{"family":"Oyanedel","given":"Rodrigo"},{"family":"Pienkowski","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC-BY 4.0.
      A transformative shift to a virtuous cycle may be feasible under almost all of the IPBES archetype scenarios and positive visions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fErwEdB1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lundquist, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Lundquist, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19361,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NJN9IQQU"],"itemData":{"id":19361,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Visions for nature and nature’s contributions to people for the 21 st century","author":[{"family":"Lundquist","given":"C.J."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"H.M."},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"R."},{"family":"Belder","given":"E."},{"family":"Carvalho Ribeiro","given":"S."},{"family":"Davies","given":"K."},{"family":"Lindgren-Streicher","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, provided certain enabling conditions and leverage points are in place.
      Transformative processes may start with technological innovation which, if combined with social transformation, can signal a fundamental transition in a new direction. Enabling conditions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a241hk8h5cq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. Pereira, 2015)","plainCitation":"(L. Pereira, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20663,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/79HFKVZJ"],"itemData":{"id":20663,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","DOI":"10.3390/ijerph120606027","ISSN":"1660-4601","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"IJERPH","language":"en","page":"6027-6044","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Organising a Safe Space for Navigating Social-Ecological Transformations to Sustainability","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Pereira","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Karpouzoglou","given":"Timothy"},{"family":"Doshi","given":"Samir"},{"family":"Frantzeskaki","given":"Niki"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",5,28]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} for transformations to sustainability include emancipation and empowerment, knowledge co-production, iterative learning, and a political environment that encourages and nurtures innovations. Building blocks are intermediate conditions for transition. In small-scale fisheries, for example, five building blocks (local leadership; secure funding; support from local government; cooperation and awareness) were identified in a Vietnamese lagoon fishery ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2uxXYCi4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Andrachuk, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Andrachuk, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20135,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HA9K5NG5"],"itemData":{"id":20135,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-10006-230226","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art26","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Building blocks for social-ecological transformations: identifying and building on governance successes for small-scale fisheries","title-short":"Building blocks for social-ecological transformations","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Andrachuk","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Armitage","given":"Derek"},{"family":"Hoang","given":"Ha Dung"},{"family":"Le","given":"Nam Van"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; Figure 5.13). 

Figure 5.13. Building blocks for social-ecological transformation in the Cau Hai Lagoon. Dotted blocks suggest supporting conditions for transformation; the nonlinear arrangements of blocks along the pathway is a reminder that building blocks will not be the same for all fishing associations. Source: Adapted from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bwzx9WV0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Andrachuk, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Andrachuk, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20135,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HA9K5NG5"],"itemData":{"id":20135,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-10006-230226","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art26","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Building blocks for social-ecological transformations: identifying and building on governance successes for small-scale fisheries","title-short":"Building blocks for social-ecological transformations","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Andrachuk","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Armitage","given":"Derek"},{"family":"Hoang","given":"Ha Dung"},{"family":"Le","given":"Nam Van"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY-NC-ND 4.0.
      To drive transformative change at scale, it will be necessary to set a united outcome-based vision for nature and people, which will provide an over-arching ‘direction of travel’ for other leverage points. These leverage points include: political prioritization (including coordinated policy at the international, national and local levels), aligned incentives and participatory processes (including transparent decision-making), which enable social change at micro- and macro-levels, supported by positively-framed approaches to adaptation and technological advances (Box 5.11, Figure 5.14).
      
Box 5.11: Leverage points for transformation to sustainability
Drawing on the findings of the IPBES Global Assessment of Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dA25yPRr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chan, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Chan, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19279,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HSF94QXS"],"itemData":{"id":19279,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"People and Nature","language":"en","page":"10 1002 3 10124","title":"Levers and leverage points for pathways to sustainability","volume":"2","editor":[{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai"},{"family":"Boyd","given":"David"},{"family":"Gould","given":"Rachelle"},{"family":"Jetzkowitz","given":"Jens"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Jianguo"},{"family":"Muraca","given":"Barbara"},{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Olmsted","given":"Paige"},{"family":"Satterfield","given":"Terre"},{"family":"Selomane","given":"Odirilwe"},{"family":"Singh","given":"Gerald"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"Rashid"},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H.T."},{"family":"Boedhihartono","given":"Agni"},{"family":"Agard","given":"John"},{"family":"Aguiar","given":"Ana Paula"},{"family":"Armenteras","given":"Dolors"},{"family":"Balint","given":"Lenke"},{"family":"Barrington?Leigh","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Brondízio","given":"Eduardo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} highlight eight leverage points for transformation to sustainability, which may equally apply to sustainable use (Figure 5.14). These leverage points can be shifted, using five interrelated “levers”. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DdbiQTtH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chan, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Chan, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19279,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HSF94QXS"],"itemData":{"id":19279,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"People and Nature","language":"en","page":"10 1002 3 10124","title":"Levers and leverage points for pathways to sustainability","volume":"2","editor":[{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai"},{"family":"Boyd","given":"David"},{"family":"Gould","given":"Rachelle"},{"family":"Jetzkowitz","given":"Jens"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Jianguo"},{"family":"Muraca","given":"Barbara"},{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Olmsted","given":"Paige"},{"family":"Satterfield","given":"Terre"},{"family":"Selomane","given":"Odirilwe"},{"family":"Singh","given":"Gerald"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"Rashid"},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H.T."},{"family":"Boedhihartono","given":"Agni"},{"family":"Agard","given":"John"},{"family":"Aguiar","given":"Ana Paula"},{"family":"Armenteras","given":"Dolors"},{"family":"Balint","given":"Lenke"},{"family":"Barrington?Leigh","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Brondízio","given":"Eduardo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} make the point that these elements are “non-substitutable”, and, when used together, may lead to “synergistic benefits”.

Figure 5.14. Implementation of interventions (levers) targeting key leverage points to enable transformative change towards greater sustainability. A range of actors (such as intergovernmental organizations, governments, non?governmental organizations, citizen and community groups, indigenous peoples and local communities, donor agencies, science and educational organizations and the private sector), can apply the levers at multiple leverage points. Source: Adapted from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WJ1X6ula","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chan, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Chan, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19279,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HSF94QXS"],"itemData":{"id":19279,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"People and Nature","language":"en","page":"10 1002 3 10124","title":"Levers and leverage points for pathways to sustainability","volume":"2","editor":[{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai"},{"family":"Boyd","given":"David"},{"family":"Gould","given":"Rachelle"},{"family":"Jetzkowitz","given":"Jens"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Jianguo"},{"family":"Muraca","given":"Barbara"},{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Olmsted","given":"Paige"},{"family":"Satterfield","given":"Terre"},{"family":"Selomane","given":"Odirilwe"},{"family":"Singh","given":"Gerald"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"Rashid"},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H.T."},{"family":"Boedhihartono","given":"Agni"},{"family":"Agard","given":"John"},{"family":"Aguiar","given":"Ana Paula"},{"family":"Armenteras","given":"Dolors"},{"family":"Balint","given":"Lenke"},{"family":"Barrington?Leigh","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Brondízio","given":"Eduardo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, under license CC-BY 4.0.
5.9.2 Setting an outcome-based vision for nature and people
A key first step in enhancing sustainable use of wild species is to set a united and aspirational outcome goal for nature and people, which focuses on a desired end state ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CSfmk2aR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bull, 2020; Maron, 2021, p. 202)","plainCitation":"(Bull, 2020; Maron, 2021, p. 202)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19101,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZXHEVUL"],"itemData":{"id":19101,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.1038/s41559-019-1022-z","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"4–7","title":"Net positive outcomes for nature","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Bull","given":"J.W."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."},{"family":"Addison","given":"P.F.E."},{"family":"Arlidge","given":"W.N.S."},{"family":"Baker","given":"J."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"T.M."},{"family":"Burgass","given":"M.J."},{"family":"Hinsley","given":"A."},{"family":"Maron","given":"M."},{"family":"Robinson","given":"J.G."},{"family":"Sekhran","given":"N."},{"family":"Sinclair","given":"S.P."},{"family":"Stuart","given":"S.N."},{"family":"Ermgassen","given":"S.O.S.E."},{"family":"Watson","given":"J.E.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":19102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P5F2RUTS"],"itemData":{"id":19102,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/CONL.12816","language":"en","title":"Setting robust biodiversity goals","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1111/CONL.12816","volume":"e12816","author":[{"family":"Maron","given":"M."},{"family":"Juffe-Bignoli","given":"D."},{"family":"Krueger","given":"L."},{"family":"Kiesecker","given":"J."},{"family":"Kümpel","given":"N.F."},{"family":"Kate","given":"K.ten"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."},{"family":"Arlidge","given":"W.N.S."},{"family":"Booth","given":"H."},{"family":"Bull","given":"J.W."},{"family":"Starkey","given":"M."},{"family":"Ekstrom","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Strassburg","given":"B."},{"family":"Verburg","given":"P.H."},{"family":"Watson","given":"J.E.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}},"locator":"202"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is in contrast to process- or performance-based goals, which outline specific approaches or standards for achieving the end state (i.e., outcome goals focus on the ends, while process- or performance-based goals typically focus on the means). 
In this case, the desired goal(s) may be, for example, sustainable use of wild species helping to “create a better and more sustainable future for all” and/or to “save lives, protect livelihoods and safeguard nature” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jhL1SV7y","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Booth, Arias,, 2021; IPBES, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Booth, Arias,, 2021; IPBES, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19128,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WV4T2QRV"],"itemData":{"id":19128,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The COVID-19 pandemic has caused huge loss of life, and immense social and economic harm. Wildlife trade has become central to discourse on COVID-19, zoonotic pandemics, and related policy responses, which must focus on “saving lives, protecting livelihoods, and safeguarding nature.” Proposed policy responses have included extreme measures such as banning all use and trade of wildlife, or blanket measures for entire Classes. However, different trades pose varying degrees of risk for zoonotic pandemics, while some trades also play critical roles in delivering other key aspects of sustainable development, particularly related to poverty and hunger alleviation, decent work, responsible consumption and production, and life on land and below water. Here we describe how wildlife trade contributes to the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) in diverse ways, with synergies and trade-offs within and between the SDGs. In doing so, we show that prohibitions could result in severe trade-offs against some SDGs, with limited benefits for public health via pandemic prevention. This complexity necessitates context-specific policies, with multi-sector decision-making that goes beyond simple top-down solutions. We encourage decision-makers to adopt a risk-based approach to wildlife trade policy post-COVID-19, with policies formulated via participatory, evidence-based approaches, which explicitly acknowledge uncertainty, complexity, and conflicting values across different components of the SDGs. This should help to ensure that future use and trade of wildlife is safe, environmentally sustainable and socially just.","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.3389/fevo.2021.639216","ISSN":"2296-701X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Ecol. Evol.","page":"639216","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"“Saving Lives, Protecting Livelihoods, and Safeguarding Nature”: Risk-Based Wildlife Trade Policy for Sustainable Development Outcomes Post-COVID-19","title-short":"“Saving Lives, Protecting Livelihoods, and Safeguarding Nature”","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Booth","given":"Hollie"},{"family":"Arias","given":"Melissa"},{"family":"Brittain","given":"Stephanie"},{"family":"Challender","given":"Daniel W. S."},{"family":"Khanyari","given":"Munib"},{"family":"Kuiper","given":"Timothy"},{"family":"Li","given":"Yuhan"},{"family":"Olmedo","given":"Alegria"},{"family":"Oyanedel","given":"Rodrigo"},{"family":"Pienkowski","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]}}},{"id":1487,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GKD27JDM"],"itemData":{"id":1487,"type":"report","title":"COVID-19 Stimulus Measures Must Save Lives, Protect Livelihoods, and Safeguard Nature to Reduce the Risk of Future Pandemics","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, the Convention on Biological Diversity's 2050 vision of “living in harmony with nature”, underpinned by a target of nature recovery, could provide a broad aspirational outcome goal within which to embed diverse strategies for enhancing the sustainable use of wild species. Importantly, these outcome goals allow for a plurality of values (as per the Nature Futures Framework), which can consider the multi-dimensional well-being of all living things, both humans and non-human. 
      Such outcome-based goals can provide a common vision which diverse stakeholders at multiple levels of society can work towards, whilst allowing for a plurality of approaches to get there. This means specific interventions can be designed to suit different species and contexts, allowing room for different values (i.e., as per the Nature Futures Framework, nature for nature, nature for society and nature as culture), and integrating multiple international priorities under different multilateral environmental agreements (Box 5.10, Box 5.11). It also limits potential perverse ecological outcomes, cost inefficiencies and social losses that can come from setting ‘one-size-fits-all’ process-based or activity-based goals (e.g., for protected area coverage; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gcFB9cC9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Banks-Leite, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Banks-Leite, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19266,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4LSKX2JD"],"itemData":{"id":19266,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology Letters","DOI":"10.1111/ele.13668","language":"en","page":"1–2","title":"The suggestion that landscapes should contain 40% of forest cover lacks evidence and is problematic","author":[{"family":"Banks-Leite","given":"C."},{"family":"Larrosa","given":"C."},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L.R."},{"family":"Tambosi","given":"L.R."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      However, ambitious outcome goals alone are not enough to drive transformative change. There is a need to “mainstream” nature, by translating high-level goals into meaningful and inclusive actions at multiple scales throughout society (Box 5.11). Coordination between multilateral conventions, and between different arms of government, business and civil society, is required; this brings us to the "enabling" leverage points. 

5.9.3 Political prioritization: embedding nature within high-level political targets
Enhancing sustainable use of wild species requires making managing human-nature interactions a top political priority, with decision-makers committed to inclusive, equitable, and evidence-based policies, with nature mainstreamed across all government sectors. Policy windows (“the emergence of junctures or openings for concerted action”, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NK8rB2KP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Armitage, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Armitage, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":10271,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L8GRFD3Q"],"itemData":{"id":10271,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2011.02.011","ISSN":"0308597X","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"703-711","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Early-stage transformation of coastal marine governance in Vietnam?","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Armitage","given":"Derek"},{"family":"Marschke","given":"Melissa"},{"family":"Tuyen","given":"Truong","non-dropping-particle":"van"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} are crucial to overcoming political inertia, particularly in the early stages of transformation, and may open new possibilities for incentive systems, and new ways of allocating access rights.  
      In light of the COVID-19 pandemic, there have been calls for a ‘green recovery’. Nature is also increasingly acknowledged as an economic priority, as reflected in the 2020 World Economic Risk Report and the Dasgupta review ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"e1KDNbAK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dasgupta, 2021b)","plainCitation":"(Dasgupta, 2021b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":10154,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/584LMVXU"],"itemData":{"id":10154,"type":"book","edition":"Updated: 18 February 2021","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-911680-29-1","language":"en","number-of-pages":"610","publisher":"HM Treasury","publisher-place":"London","source":"K10plus ISBN","title":"The economics of biodiversity: the Dasgupta review: full report","title-short":"The economics of biodiversity","author":[{"family":"Dasgupta","given":"Partha"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This high-level prioritization and mainstreaming of nature into economic decisions could help to pave the way for enhancing sustainable use of wild species in the future. As reflected in the seven IPBES visions, to ensure a full societal shift towards sustainable use, this needs to be supported by shifts in high-level political targets, from growth-oriented goals which over-represent economic welfare (i.e., gross domestic product) relative to goals based on holistic social welfare and long-term sustainability. Changing this focus could help facilitate moves towards de-growth, ecological optimization and/or circular economy paradigms, which ensure that economic activities do not over-exploit wild species. 
5.9.4 Aligned incentives: ensuring people are no worse off, via appropriate instrument mixes
Enhancing sustainable use of wild species will require behavior change and innovation across all sectors of society. Broadly speaking, there are two main types of instruments which can facilitate this transformational change; regulatory and market-based interventions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wh6zH5iP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Young & Gunningham, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Young & Gunningham, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19103,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ULFC9CIM"],"itemData":{"id":19103,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"OECD International Conference an Biodiversity Incentive Measures","language":"en","page":"141–165","title":"Mixing instruments and institutional arrangements for optimal biodiversity conservation","URL":"http://hdl.handle.net/102.100.100/223210?index=1","author":[{"family":"Young","given":"M.D."},{"family":"Gunningham","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Regulations which consider both the bio-economics of the sustainable use of different species and ecosystems, and the socio-economic costs and benefits of their use are needed. Under the Nature Futures Framework, in situations in which "nature for nature" is a dominant paradigm, extractive use of wild species can be prohibited while allowing for well-regulated non- extractive use such as photographic tourism. In "nature for society" situations, regulations such as standards and quotas can help to ensure that use is compatible with the survival of wild species. For such regulations to act as effective safeguards for sustainability, they should also be associated with robust monitoring and adaptive management, as well as strong institutions which can insulate against poor governance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dHCDwRxX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Young & Gunningham, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Young & Gunningham, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19103,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ULFC9CIM"],"itemData":{"id":19103,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"OECD International Conference an Biodiversity Incentive Measures","language":"en","page":"141–165","title":"Mixing instruments and institutional arrangements for optimal biodiversity conservation","URL":"http://hdl.handle.net/102.100.100/223210?index=1","author":[{"family":"Young","given":"M.D."},{"family":"Gunningham","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Regulations should also be supported by complementary rights- and incentive-based instruments for aligning socio-economic and sustainable use objectives, especially where indigenous peoples and local communities may be impacted. For example, in "nature for society" situations, where commercial use of wild species can be compatible with their survival in the wild, and with the economic welfare of society, mechanisms need to be put in place to ensure appropriate distribution of these economic benefits to people who are living in association with wild species, or who can act as stewards of wild populations and/or their habitats. An example is the commercial hunting of bighorn sheep in Mexico, where local people provide access and guiding services to hunters, and income from hunting permits supports habitat management and payments to communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Nw4Rr8Vk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C.I.T.E.S. & Livelihoods, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(C.I.T.E.S. & Livelihoods, 2019b)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19283,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RXKLVNJT"],"itemData":{"id":19283,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Saltwater Crocodile harvest and trade in Australia","author":[{"literal":"C.I.T.E.S."},{"literal":"Livelihoods"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another is the harvesting of saltwater crocodile eggs in the Northern Territory of Australia, where indigenous communities have use rights to benefit directly from egg harvesting, while outsiders can harvest eggs for an access fee ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5RxqCH0Z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C.I.T.E.S. & Livelihoods, 2019)","plainCitation":"(C.I.T.E.S. & Livelihoods, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19282,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YDV8H9DP"],"itemData":{"id":19282,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Community-based trophy hunting of Bighorn Sheep in Mexico Introduction:Retrieved from","URL":"https://cites.org/sites/default/files/eng/prog/Livelihoods/case_studies/6Mexico_bighornsheep_long_Aug2.pdf","author":[{"literal":"C.I.T.E.S."},{"literal":"Livelihoods"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Appropriate interventions to enhance sustainability may include supporting local communities to achieve secure tenure of their resources, and promoting social justice and equity, such as implementation of Conservation Basic Income schemes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JuoR09qH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fletcher & B\\\\uc0\\\\u252{}scher, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Fletcher & Büscher, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19307,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MW8H5X7I"],"itemData":{"id":19307,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2020.108520","language":"fr","page":"108520","title":"Conservation basic income: A non-market mechanism to support convivial conservation","volume":"244","author":[{"family":"Fletcher","given":"R."},{"family":"Büscher","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In "nature for nature" situations, it may be necessary to directly reward or compensate people for protecting wild species and their habitats. Examples include shark reef in Fiji, where fisher communities are directly paid for protecting a no-take zone ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hMr2yuDx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(W.C.S., 2020)","plainCitation":"(W.C.S., 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19447,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N68T54QQ"],"itemData":{"id":19447,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Suva, Fiji","title":"Marine Conservation Agreements Guidance for the Touwrism Industry in Fiji","author":[{"literal":"W.C.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and performance-based payments to protect endangered Ibis in Cambodia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1eBLNjsO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Clements, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Clements, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19285,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TAFTDTQU"],"itemData":{"id":19285,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2009.11.010","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1283–1291","title":"Payments for biodiversity conservation in the context of weak institutions: Comparison of three programs from Cambodia","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Clements","given":"T."},{"family":"John","given":"A."},{"family":"Nielsen","given":"K."},{"family":"An","given":"D."},{"family":"Tan","given":"S."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, it may be necessary to develop negative incentives for unsustainable damage to wild species, for example through systems of ‘green’ or ‘blue’ taxes which are levied against corporations that exploit wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7vWyCVBC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zhou & Segerson, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Zhou & Segerson, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19450,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7E3UDTAL"],"itemData":{"id":19450,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su4061329","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1329–1353","title":"Are Green Taxes a Good Way to Help Solve State Budget Deficits?","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Zhou","given":"R."},{"family":"Segerson","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In general, it will be important to set social outcome goals alongside outcome goals for nature, such as ensuring people have higher wellbeing as a result of conservation interventions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XQGbRgAF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Griffiths, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Griffiths, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19315,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LHZ4B9LR"],"itemData":{"id":19315,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13184","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"76–87","title":"No net loss for people and biodiversity","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Griffiths","given":"V.F."},{"family":"Bull","given":"J.W."},{"family":"Baker","given":"J."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
5.9.5 Intrinsic motivations: driving behavioral tipping points though social norms
Intrinsic motivations, such as social norms, can interact with regulatory and market-based approaches, and drive large-scale behavioral change across systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BUtvShWB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nyborg, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Nyborg, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":17183,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6TXY2GX3"],"itemData":{"id":17183,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aaf8317.","issue":"6308","language":"fr","page":"42–43","title":"Social Norms as Solutions","volume":"354","author":[{"family":"Nyborg","given":"Karine"},{"family":"Anderies","given":"John M."},{"family":"Dannenberg","given":"Astrid"},{"family":"Lindahl","given":"Therese"},{"family":"Schill","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Maja Schlüter","given":"W.Neil Adger"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, leveraging social change through social marketing techniques could create positive outcomes for wild species, by both discouraging illegal and unsustainable use of wildlife (e.g. products directly derived from protected or endangered wildlife, and products that indirectly drive loss of nature such as industrial domestic animal production ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kSBHrxu7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chaves, 2018; Doughty, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Chaves, 2018; Doughty, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5521,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JS7F7SHF"],"itemData":{"id":5521,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Millions of people across the tropics rely on wildlife for food and income. However, overhunting to satisfy this demand is causing the decline of many species; an issue known as the wild meat crisis. We applied a before-after control-intervention design to assess the effects of social marketing (an information campaign and community engagement) with and without an economic incentive (discount coupons for chicken) on wild meat consumption. Coupons increased chicken consumption, as expected, but did not reduce wild meat consumption. In contrast, social marketing without the price incentive reduced wild meat consumption by ?62%. This study demonstrates how social marketing and price incentives may be effective at reducing demand for meat and other wildlife products.","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12391","ISSN":"1755-263X","issue":"2","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/conl.12391","page":"e12391","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Changing Wild Meat Consumption: An Experiment in the Central Amazon, Brazil","title-short":"Changing Wild Meat Consumption","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Chaves","given":"Willandia A."},{"family":"Valle","given":"Denis R."},{"family":"Monroe","given":"Martha C."},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"David S."},{"family":"Sieving","given":"Kathryn E."},{"family":"Sadowsky","given":"Brooke"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":19302,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LP82SKWW"],"itemData":{"id":19302,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Science and Practice","DOI":"10.1111/csp2.272","issue":"10","language":"en","page":"1–14","title":"Strategic advertising of online news articles as an intervention to influence wild species product consumers","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Doughty","given":"H."},{"family":"Wright","given":"J."},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"D."},{"family":"Lee","given":"J.S.H."},{"family":"Oliver","given":"K."},{"family":"Milner?Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and promoting behavioral shifts towards more environmentally-friendly diets (e.g. more plant-based diets and sustainably sourced animal products, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TcRbj3QT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nyborg, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Nyborg, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":17183,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6TXY2GX3"],"itemData":{"id":17183,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aaf8317.","issue":"6308","language":"fr","page":"42–43","title":"Social Norms as Solutions","volume":"354","author":[{"family":"Nyborg","given":"Karine"},{"family":"Anderies","given":"John M."},{"family":"Dannenberg","given":"Astrid"},{"family":"Lindahl","given":"Therese"},{"family":"Schill","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Maja Schlüter","given":"W.Neil Adger"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Novel approaches to producing social change include deploying social media and mobile technology, for example through targeted advertisements or inducing peer pressure via online social networks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"18mOLJcO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Doughty, 2020; Mani, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Doughty, 2020; Mani, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19302,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LP82SKWW"],"itemData":{"id":19302,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Science and Practice","DOI":"10.1111/csp2.272","issue":"10","language":"en","page":"1–14","title":"Strategic advertising of online news articles as an intervention to influence wild species product consumers","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Doughty","given":"H."},{"family":"Wright","given":"J."},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"D."},{"family":"Lee","given":"J.S.H."},{"family":"Oliver","given":"K."},{"family":"Milner?Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":19363,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7J3PRFJ2"],"itemData":{"id":19363,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep01735","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1735","title":"Inducing Peer Pressure to Promote Cooperation","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Mani","given":"A."},{"family":"Rahwan","given":"I."},{"family":"Pentland","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; or through improved supply chain traceability and sharing of knowledge and data on the impacts of different actors on wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"I9KxpbPi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u214{}sterblom, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Österblom, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4020,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/US7KQSPS"],"itemData":{"id":4020,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The ocean represents a fundamental source of micronutrients and protein for a growing world population. Seafood is a highly traded and sought after commodity on international markets, and is critically dependent on healthy marine ecosystems. A global trend of wild stocks being overfished and in decline, as well as multiple sustainability challenges associated with a rapid growth of aquaculture, represent key concerns in relation to the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals. Existing efforts aimed to improve the sustainability of seafood production have generated important progress, primarily at the local and national levels, but have yet to effectively address the global challenges associated with the ocean. This study highlights the importance of transnational corporations in enabling transformative change, and thereby contributes to advancing the limited understanding of large-scale private actors within the sustainability science literature. We describe how we engaged with large seafood producers to coproduce a global science–business initiative for ocean stewardship. We suggest that this initiative is improving the prospects for transformative change by providing novel links between science and business, between wild-capture fisheries and aquaculture, and across geographical space. We argue that scientists can play an important role in facilitating change by connecting knowledge to action among global actors, while recognizing risks associated with such engagement. The methods developed through this case study contribute to identifying key competences in sustainability science and hold promises for other sectors as well.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1704453114","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"34","journalAbbreviation":"PNAS","language":"en","note":"PMID: 28784792","page":"9038-9043","source":"www.pnas.org","title":"Emergence of a global science–business initiative for ocean stewardship","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Österblom","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Jouffray","given":"Jean-Baptiste"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Rockström","given":"Johan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Consumer awareness and social change can also drive corporate social responsibility for sustainable use of wild species, or work in synergy with corporate activism. For example, a global science-business initiative for ocean stewardship has been created to enhance sustainable use of wild fish stocks, by using data and transparency to drive corporate and consumer change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7UtwXTB2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u214{}sterblom, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Österblom, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4020,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/US7KQSPS"],"itemData":{"id":4020,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The ocean represents a fundamental source of micronutrients and protein for a growing world population. Seafood is a highly traded and sought after commodity on international markets, and is critically dependent on healthy marine ecosystems. A global trend of wild stocks being overfished and in decline, as well as multiple sustainability challenges associated with a rapid growth of aquaculture, represent key concerns in relation to the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals. Existing efforts aimed to improve the sustainability of seafood production have generated important progress, primarily at the local and national levels, but have yet to effectively address the global challenges associated with the ocean. This study highlights the importance of transnational corporations in enabling transformative change, and thereby contributes to advancing the limited understanding of large-scale private actors within the sustainability science literature. We describe how we engaged with large seafood producers to coproduce a global science–business initiative for ocean stewardship. We suggest that this initiative is improving the prospects for transformative change by providing novel links between science and business, between wild-capture fisheries and aquaculture, and across geographical space. We argue that scientists can play an important role in facilitating change by connecting knowledge to action among global actors, while recognizing risks associated with such engagement. The methods developed through this case study contribute to identifying key competences in sustainability science and hold promises for other sectors as well.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1704453114","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"34","journalAbbreviation":"PNAS","language":"en","note":"PMID: 28784792","page":"9038-9043","source":"www.pnas.org","title":"Emergence of a global science–business initiative for ocean stewardship","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Österblom","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Jouffray","given":"Jean-Baptiste"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Rockström","given":"Johan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
5.9.6 Transparent, participatory processes and adaptive management
Policy interventions and socio-economic instruments need to be co-designed with affected people, and in particular need to consider social justice and equity, both in terms of process and outcomes. This requires participatory processes and transparency with respect to value-based judgements ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fObyGkGX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(DeFries & Nagendra, 2017; Kenter, 2011)","plainCitation":"(DeFries & Nagendra, 2017; Kenter, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4902,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WLKQ2ILQ"],"itemData":{"id":4902,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ecosystems are self-regulating systems that provide societies with food, water, timber, and other resources. As demands for resources increase, management decisions are replacing self-regulating properties. Counter to previous technical approaches that applied simple formulas to estimate sustainable yields of single species, current research recognizes the inherent complexity of ecosystems and the inability to foresee all consequences of interventions across different spatial, temporal, and administrative scales. Ecosystem management is thus more realistically seen as a “wicked problem” that has no clear-cut solution. Approaches for addressing such problems include multisector decision-making, institutions that enable management to span across administrative boundaries, adaptive management, markets that incorporate natural capital, and collaborative processes to engage diverse stakeholders and address inequalities. Ecosystem management must avoid two traps: falsely assuming a tame solution and inaction from overwhelming complexity. An incremental approach can help to avoid these traps.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aal1950","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"6335","page":"265–270","title":"Ecosystem management as a wicked problem","volume":"356","author":[{"family":"DeFries","given":"Ruth"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":19343,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y5XSATHH"],"itemData":{"id":19343,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2011.01.001","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"505–521","title":"The importance of deliberation in valuing ecosystem services in developing countries-Evidence from the Solomon Islands","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Kenter","given":"J.O."},{"family":"Hyde","given":"T."},{"family":"Christie","given":"M."},{"family":"Fazey","given":"I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which in turn can improve the social legitimacy of interventions promoting the sustainability of wild use, and their effectiveness in driving sustainability through behavior change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VTb7SogG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bonwitt, 2018; Levi, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Bonwitt, 2018; Levi, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19271,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LAJ3CHCS"],"itemData":{"id":19271,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Social Science and Medicine","DOI":"10.1016/j.socscimed.2017.12.028","language":"en","page":"166–173","title":"Unintended consequences of the ‘bushmeat ban’ in West Africa during the 2013–2016 Ebola virus disease epidemic","volume":"200","author":[{"family":"Bonwitt","given":"J."},{"family":"Dawson","given":"M."},{"family":"Kandeh","given":"M."},{"family":"Ansumana","given":"R."},{"family":"Sahr","given":"F."},{"family":"Brown","given":"H."},{"family":"Kelly","given":"A.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":19358,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9SAPSEC9"],"itemData":{"id":19358,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"American Behavioral Scientist","DOI":"10.1177/0002764209338797","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"354–375","title":"Conceptualizing Legitimacy, Measuring Legitimating Beliefs","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Levi","given":"M."},{"family":"Sacks","given":"A."},{"family":"Tyler","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In cases where the values of different stakeholders diverge, techniques such as describing and sharing mental models can help to improve common understanding about complex issues ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"H3X190kO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Biggs, 2011)","plainCitation":"(D. Biggs, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19270,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5BLYKRVK"],"itemData":{"id":19270,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/j.1755-263X.2011.00170.x","issue":"3","language":"fr","page":"169–183","title":"The implementation crisis in conservation planning: Could “mental models” help?","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"D."},{"family":"Abel","given":"N."},{"family":"Knight","given":"A.T."},{"family":"Leitch","given":"A."},{"family":"Langston","given":"A."},{"family":"Ban","given":"N.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, while positive message framing can promote inclusive action ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8rwLefIU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jacobson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Jacobson, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19336,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A5KIR6DQ"],"itemData":{"id":19336,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Applied Environmental Education and Communication","DOI":"10.1080/1533015X.2018.1456380","issue":"3","language":"fr","page":"252–265","title":"Love or Loss: Effective message framing to promote environmental conservation","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Jacobson","given":"S.K."},{"family":"Morales","given":"N.A."},{"family":"Chen","given":"B."},{"family":"Soodeen","given":"R."},{"family":"Moulton","given":"M.P."},{"family":"Jain","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      All interventions to enhance sustainable use of wild species will also require adaptive management. For complex, dynamic issues like wild species use, there is rarely one static universal solution. This requires that the impacts of interventions are assessed, with room for ‘optimistic’ and ‘fail safe’ adaptation, that provides room for learning from failures, and allows challenges to contribute to institutional knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"O9wAUjSp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Catalano, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Catalano, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19277,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N965ZF76"],"itemData":{"id":19277,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2019.108223","issue":"September","language":"fr","page":"108223","title":"Learning from published project failures in conservation","volume":"238","author":[{"family":"Catalano","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Lyons-White","given":"J."},{"family":"Mills","given":"M.M."},{"family":"Knight","given":"A.T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Horizon scanning may also be a useful component of an adaptive approach to transformative change in dynamic systems, which can be used to inform scenario-building and policy formulation (Box 5.11). 
Box 5.11. A horizon scan of the illegal wild species trade
To help inform proactive policy responses in the face of uncertainty, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hH0dzbgL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Esmail, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Esmail, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19303,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LHYYV7C7"],"itemData":{"id":19303,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12715","language":"fr","page":"1–10","title":"Emerging illegal wild species trade issues: A global horizon scan","author":[{"family":"Esmail","given":"N."},{"family":"Wintle","given":"B.C."},{"family":"Sas-Rolfes","given":"M."},{"family":"Athanas","given":"A."},{"family":"Beale","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Bending","given":"Z."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} conducted a horizon scan of significant emerging issues for the illegal wildlife trade. This covers the “hunting” and “gathering” practices discussed above, with a focus on international trade. Building upon existing iterative horizon scanning methods, they used an open and participatory approach to evaluate and rank issues from a diverse range of sources. The top 20 issues fell under three overarching themes: (i) Geographic (political, demographic and socio-economic) shifts and influences; (ii) Scientific and technological innovation, and (iii) Changing trends in demand and information (see Figure 5.15). 

Figure 5.15. The top 20 emerging IWT issues, illustrating linkages between them. Numbering represents the rank order of the issues (referred to in the text as Horizon scan issues 1-20). Those outlined in black are cross-thematic issues. Source: Adapted from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rcdwXlSE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Esmail, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Esmail, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19303,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LHYYV7C7"],"itemData":{"id":19303,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12715","language":"fr","page":"1–10","title":"Emerging illegal wild species trade issues: A global horizon scan","author":[{"family":"Esmail","given":"N."},{"family":"Wintle","given":"B.C."},{"family":"Sas-Rolfes","given":"M."},{"family":"Athanas","given":"A."},{"family":"Beale","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Bending","given":"Z."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, under license CC-BY 4.0.
Issues under the first theme include changing geopolitical processes and the rising global influence of East Asia. Political, demographic and economic changes could facilitate greater access to wild species and stimulate growing demand for wild species products, but also opportunities for sustainable use. For example, the political and cultural revival of Traditional Chinese Medicine, the increasing role of China in developing countries, and the rapid expansion of new international trade routes, particularly in the context of the Belt and Road Initiative, could bring both new threats and new opportunities for sustainable wild species trade.
	Issues under the second theme fell into two broad categories; biotechnology and information technology. For example, genetic technological advancements could enable rapid, cost-effective assessments and traceability of product identity and source at the species and individual levels. This could lead to better enforcement of regulations, and potentially promote sustainable sourcing. Cross-thematic issues which touch on the vicious-virtuous cycle of Figure 5.14 included that, in the modern age of networked communication, misinformation (from market participants, intergovernmental bodies, non-governmental organizations, policymakers and/or the media) can rapidly influence policy and practice. This can be difficult to correct and can undermine conservation efforts by skewing policy responses and potentially misdirecting scarce resources. Horizon scans are meant to be repeated at regular intervals or when circumstances have changed. A post-COVID-19 scan would pick up some of the same issues (potentially intensified) as well as bringing in new ones.

