There is an increasing tendency today to shift the focus away from sustainable use of wild species; whereas the emphasis is to view biodiversity conservation and sustainable use through the lens of ecosystem functioning and its capacity to produce ecosystem goods and services ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ag2i3cmij","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Heywood, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Heywood, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31600,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HSVJUMVU"],"itemData":{"id":31600,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Despite the massive efforts that have been made to conserve plant diversity across the world during the past few decades, it is becoming increasingly evident that our current strategies are not suf?ciently effective to prevent the continuing decline in biodiversity. As a recent report by the CBD indicates, current progress and commitments are insuf?cient to achieve the Aichi Biodiversity Targets by 2020. Threatened species lists continue to grow while the world's governments fail to meet biodiversity conservation goals. Clearly, we are failing in our attempts to conserve biodiversity on a suf?cient scale. The reasons for this situation are complex, including scienti?c, technical, sociological, economic and political factors. The conservation community is divided about how to respond. Some believe that saving all existing biodiversity is still an achievable goal. On the other hand, there are those who believe that we need to accept that biodiversity will inevitably continue to be lost, despite all our conservation actions and that we must focus on what to save, why and where. It has also been suggested that we need a new approach to conservation in the face of the challenges posed by the Anthropocene biosphere which we now inhabit. Whatever view one holds on the above issues, it is clear that we need to review the effectiveness of our current conservation strategies, identify the limiting factors that are preventing the Aichi goals being met and at the same time take whatever steps are necessary to make our conservation protocols more explicit, operational and ef?cient so as to achieve the maximum conservation effect. This paper addresses the key issues that underlie our failure to meet agreed targets and discusses the necessary changes to our conservation approaches. While we can justi?ably be proud of our many achievements and successes in plant conservation in the past 30 years, which have helped slow the rate of loss, unless we devise a more coherent, consistent and integrated global strategy in which both the effectiveness and limitations of our current policies, action plans and procedures are recognized, and re?ect this in national strategies, and then embark on a much bolder and ambitious set of actions, progress will be limited and plant diversity will continue to decline.","container-title":"Plant Diversity","DOI":"10.1016/j.pld.2017.10.004","ISSN":"24682659","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Plant Diversity","language":"en","page":"314-330","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Plant conservation in the Anthropocene – Challenges and future prospects","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2468265917300847","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Heywood","given":"Vernon H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Therefore, it is very challenging to compile knowledge gaps on sustainable use of wild species as there is lack of consistency among worldwide databases to quantify the harvesting and use of wild species by people in different countries across the world. This happens because different countries and organizations have different accounting methodologies, making the merging of different datasets a huge challenge. Major knowledge gaps in the sustainable use of wild species are summarized here.

(i) Across all practices, and especially in global fishing, existing data and reporting do not differentiate adequately between wild and non-wild species. Explained most explicitly in sections 3.2 (global overview), 3.3.1 (fishing), global indicators and data reported by the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations and other agencies do not separate out wild and aquaculture, wild and farmed, wild and plantation, or wild and domesticated species when calculating global or regional off-takes. This makes it almost impossible to accurately assess and report on status and trends in sustainable use of wild species. There is vast legacy of available data on species taxonomy, conservation or economic value related to trade and markets rather than specifically on use as defined in the assessment. In addition, most of the datasets available lack detailed information on practices and uses of utilized and non-utilized species that challenges to make comparative account of population trends.

(ii) Knowledge gap in status of taxonomic groups and their uses at different levels and scales. Information is available on the conservation status of vertebrates, particularly with regard to mammals and birds, to a lesser extent with amphibians and fish including demersal fish; however  knowledge on conservation status and use is severely lacking for invertebrates (insects), fungi and microbial species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a21p7ag0lif","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. L. Coleman, 2019; Naranjo\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Ortiz & Gabald\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}n, 2019; Willis, 2018)","plainCitation":"(J. L. Coleman, 2019; Naranjo?Ortiz & Gabaldón, 2019; Willis, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32312,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TQ9E659T"],"itemData":{"id":32312,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Southeast (SE) Asia holds high regional biodiversity and endemism levels but is also one of the world's most threatened regions. Local, regional and global threats could have severe consequences for the future survival of many species and the provision of ecosystem services.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2019.03.028","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"211-220","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Top 100 research questions for biodiversity conservation in Southeast Asia","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320718315751","volume":"234","author":[{"family":"Coleman","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Ascher","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Bickford","given":"D."},{"family":"Buchori","given":"D."},{"family":"Cabanban","given":"A."},{"family":"Chisholm","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Chong","given":"K.Y."},{"family":"Christie","given":"P."},{"family":"Clements","given":"G.R."},{"family":"Cruz","given":"T.E.E.","non-dropping-particle":"dela"},{"family":"Dressler","given":"W."},{"family":"Edwards","given":"D.P."},{"family":"Francis","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Friess","given":"D.A."},{"family":"Giam","given":"X."},{"family":"Gibson","given":"L."},{"family":"Huang","given":"D."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Jaafar","given":"Z."},{"family":"Jain","given":"A."},{"family":"Koh","given":"L.P."},{"family":"Kudavidanage","given":"E.P."},{"family":"Lee","given":"B.P.Y.-H."},{"family":"Lee","given":"J."},{"family":"Lee","given":"T.M."},{"family":"Leggett","given":"M."},{"family":"Leimona","given":"B."},{"family":"Linkie","given":"M."},{"family":"Luskin","given":"M."},{"family":"Lynam","given":"A."},{"family":"Meijaard","given":"E."},{"family":"Nijman","given":"V."},{"family":"Olsson","given":"A."},{"family":"Page","given":"S."},{"family":"Parolin","given":"P."},{"family":"Peh","given":"K.S.-H."},{"family":"Posa","given":"M.R."},{"family":"Prescott","given":"G.W."},{"family":"Rahman","given":"S.A."},{"family":"Ramchunder","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Rao","given":"M."},{"family":"Reed","given":"J."},{"family":"Richards","given":"D.R."},{"family":"Slade","given":"E.M."},{"family":"Steinmetz","given":"R."},{"family":"Tan","given":"P.Y."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"D."},{"family":"Todd","given":"P.A."},{"family":"Vo","given":"S.T."},{"family":"Webb","given":"E.L."},{"family":"Ziegler","given":"A.D."},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L.R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6]]}}},{"id":31604,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SMD24923"],"itemData":{"id":31604,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fungi are a highly diverse group of heterotrophic eukaryotes characterized by the absence of phagotrophy and the presence of a chitinous cell wall. While unicellular fungi are far from rare, part of the evolutionary success of the group resides in their ability to grow inde?nitely as a cylindrical multinucleated cell (hypha). Armed with these morphological traits and with an extremely high metabolical diversity, fungi have conquered numerous ecological niches and have shaped a whole world of interactions with other living organisms. Herein we survey the main evolutionary and ecological processes that have guided fungal diversity. We will ?rst review the ecology and evolution of the zoosporic lineages and the process of terrestrialization, as one of the major evolutionary transitions in this kingdom. Several plausible scenarios have been proposed for fungal terrestralization and we here propose a new scenario, which considers icy environments as a transitory niche between water and emerged land. We then focus on exploring the main ecological relationships of Fungi with other organisms (other fungi, protozoans, animals and plants), as well as the origin of adaptations to certain specialized ecological niches within the group (lichens, black fungi and yeasts). Throughout this review we use an evolutionary and comparative-genomics perspective to understand fungal ecological diversity. Finally, we highlight the importance of genome-enabled inferences to envision plausible narratives and scenarios for important transitions.","container-title":"Biological Reviews","DOI":"10.1111/brv.12510","ISSN":"1464-7931, 1469-185X","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Biol Rev","language":"en","page":"1443-1476","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fungal evolution: major ecological adaptations and evolutionary transitions","title-short":"Fungal evolution","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/brv.12510","volume":"94","author":[{"family":"Naranjo?Ortiz","given":"Miguel A."},{"family":"Gabaldón","given":"Toni"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",8]]}}},{"id":30357,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MX282NHI"],"itemData":{"id":30357,"type":"book","event-place":"Richmond, UK","publisher":"Kew Publishing","publisher-place":"Richmond, UK","title":"State of the world's fungi 2018. Report.","author":[{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."}],"editor":[{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and in some taxa, especially invertebrates and fungi, there are still thousands of species yet to be described and being named. The knowledge gap also includes widely used and internationally traded species, for example porcini mushrooms (Boletus spp.). 
      Marine species are especially susceptible to exploitation. However, the status of half of the world’s fisheries, largely from Southeast Asia, is not scientifically assessed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1c44pk66f3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Costello, 2012)","plainCitation":"(C. Costello, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31424,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/55D9HWJC"],"itemData":{"id":31424,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1223389","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6106","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"517-520","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Status and Solutions for the World's Unassessed Fisheries","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1223389","volume":"338","author":[{"family":"Costello","given":"C."},{"family":"Ovando","given":"D."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"R."},{"family":"Gaines","given":"S. D."},{"family":"Deschenes","given":"O."},{"family":"Lester","given":"S. E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",10,26]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. We know less about inland fisheries than marine fisheries. Marine mammals are especially susceptible to exploitation due to low reproductive rates and the many other threats they face, including noise pollution and climate change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1pdrpo2aq2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Perrin, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Perrin, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31599,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SQ6YILRK"],"itemData":{"id":31599,"type":"book","edition":"2. ed","event-place":"Burlington,MA","ISBN":"978-0-12-373553-9","number-of-pages":"1316","publisher":"Academic Press","publisher-place":"Burlington,MA","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Encyclopedia of marine mammals","editor":[{"family":"Perrin","given":"William F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      With regards to insects, fungi and microbes, insufficient taxonomic information makes it difficult to assess the sustainability of their use, and more generally knowledge on their roles in the supply of nature’s contributions to people is limited ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1u4ftkiu3u","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kassas, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Kassas, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31605,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5MCLKBDT"],"itemData":{"id":31605,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Convention on Biological Diversity set conservation of biodiversity on the world agenda. Gaps in knowledge need to be addressed for actions to be effective and sustainable. Gaps include: species diversity, micro-organisms and their ecological roles, ecological and geographical status of species, human capacity to assess and forecast bio-ecological degradation. Requirements for global inventories call for worldwide collaboration. Criteria for setting priorities need to be formulated and agreed.","language":"en","page":"8","source":"Zotero","title":"Biodiversity: gaps in knowledge","author":[{"family":"Kassas","given":"M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, it is believed that more than 90% of species remain unknown to science out of 148,000 species of fungi that have been scientifically identified ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"avrkjcmac8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Antonelli, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Antonelli, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30901,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G9883U5U"],"itemData":{"id":30901,"type":"report","note":"DOI: 10.34885/172","publisher":"Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"State of the World's Plants and Fungi 2020","URL":"https://kew.iro.bl.uk/work/23394899-864d-4ef0-b864-857efea500c5","author":[{"family":"Antonelli","