iven":"B."},{"family":"Dröge","given":"G."},{"family":"Dulloo","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Fang","given":"R."},{"family":"Farlow","given":"A."},{"family":"Farrar","given":"K."},{"family":"Fay","given":"M.F."},{"family":"Felix","given":"J."},{"family":"Forest","given":"F."},{"family":"Forrest","given":"L.L."},{"family":"Fulcher","given":"T."},{"family":"Gafforov","given":"Y."},{"family":"Gardiner","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Gâteblé","given":"G."},{"family":"Gaya","given":"E."},{"family":"Geslin","given":"B."},{"family":"Gonçalves","given":"S.C."},{"family":"Gore","given":"C.J.N."},{"family":"Govaerts","given":"R."},{"family":"Gowda","given":"B."},{"family":"Grace","given":"O.M."},{"family":"Grall","given":"A."},{"family":"Haelewaters","given":"D."},{"family":"Halley","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Hamilton","given":"M.A."},{"family":"Hazra","given":"A."},{"family":"Heller","given":"T."},{"family":"Hollingsworth","given":"P.M."},{"family":"Holstein","given":"N."},{"family":"Howes","given":"M.-J.R."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"M."},{"family":"Hunter","given":"D."},{"family":"Hutchinson","given":"N."},{"family":"Hyde","given":"K."},{"family":"Iganci","given":"J."},{"family":"Jones","given":"M."},{"family":"Kelly","given":"L.J."},{"family":"Kirk","given":"P."},{"family":"Koch","given":"H."},{"family":"Grisai-Greilhuber","given":"I."},{"family":"Lall","given":"N."},{"family":"Langat","given":"M.K."},{"family":"Leaman","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Leão","given":"T.C."},{"family":"Lee","given":"M.A."},{"family":"Leitch","given":"I.J."},{"family":"Leon","given":"C."},{"family":"Lettice","given":"E."},{"family":"Lewis","given":"G.P."},{"family":"Li","given":"L."},{"family":"Lindon","given":"H."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Liu","given":"U."},{"family":"Llewellyn","given":"T."},{"family":"Looney","given":"B."},{"family":"Lovett","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Luczaj","given":"L."},{"family":"Lulekal","given":"E."},{"family":"Maggassouba","given":"S."},{"family":"Malécot","given":"V."},{"family":"Martin","given":"C."},{"family":"Masera","given":"O.R."},{"family":"Mattana","given":"E."},{"family":"Maxted","given":"N."},{"family":"Mba","given":"C."},{"family":"McGinn","given":"K.J."},{"family":"Metheringham","given":"C."},{"family":"Miles","given":"S."},{"family":"Miller","given":"J."},{"family":"Milliken","given":"W."},{"family":"Moat","given":"J."},{"family":"Moore","given":"P.G.P."},{"family":"Morim","given":"M.P."},{"family":"Mueller","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Muminjanov","given":"H."},{"family":"Negrão","given":"R."},{"family":"Nic Lughadha","given":"E."},{"family":"Nicholson","given":"N."},{"family":"Niskanen","given":"T."},{"family":"Nono Womdim","given":"R."},{"family":"Noorani","given":"A."},{"family":"Obreza","given":"M."},{"family":"O'Donnell","given":"K."},{"family":"O'Hanlon","given":"R."},{"family":"Onana","given":"J.-M."},{"family":"Ondo","given":"I."},{"family":"Padulosi","given":"S."},{"family":"Paton","given":"A."},{"family":"Pearce","given":"T."},{"family":"Pérez Escobar","given":"O.A."},{"family":"Pieroni","given":"A."},{"family":"Pironon","given":"S."},{"family":"Prescott","given":"T.A.K."},{"family":"Qi","given":"Y.D."},{"family":"Qin","given":"H."},{"family":"Quave","given":"C.L."},{"family":"Rajaovelona","given":"L."},{"family":"Razanajatovo","given":"H."},{"family":"Reich","given":"P.B."},{"family":"Rianawati","given":"E."},{"family":"Rich","given":"T.C.G."},{"family":"Richards","given":"S.L."},{"family":"Rivers","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Ross","given":"A."},{"family":"Rumsey","given":"F."},{"family":"Ryan","given":"M."},{"family":"Ryan","given":"P."},{"family":"Sagala","given":"S."},{"family":"Sanchez","given":"M.D."},{"family":"Sharrock","given":"S."},{"family":"Shrestha","given":"K.K."},{"family":"Sim","given":"J."},{"family":"Sirakaya","given":"A."},{"family":"Sjöman","given":"H."},{"family":"Smidt","given":"E.C."},{"family":"Smith","given":"D."},{"family":"Smith","given":"P."},{"family":"Smith","given":"S.R."},{"family":"Sofo","given":"A."},{"family":"Spence","given":"N."},{"family":"Stanworth","given":"A."},{"family":"Stara","given":"K."},{"family":"Stevenson","given":"P.C."},{"family":"Stroh","given":"P."},{"family":"Suz","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Tambam","given":"B.B."},{"family":"Tatsis","given":"E.C."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"I."},{"family":"Thiers","given":"B."},{"family":"Thormann","given":"I."},{"family":"Vaglica","given":"V."},{"family":"Vásquez-Londoño","given":"C."},{"family":"Victor","given":"J."},{"family":"Viruel","given":"J."},{"family":"Walker","given":"B.E."},{"family":"Walker","given":"K."},{"family":"Walsh","given":"A."},{"family":"Way","given":"M."},{"family":"Wilbraham","given":"J."},{"family":"Wilkin","given":"P."},{"family":"Wilkinson","given":"T."},{"family":"Williams","given":"C."},{"family":"Winterton","given":"D."},{"family":"Wong","given":"K.M."},{"family":"Woodfield-Pascoe","given":"N."},{"family":"Woodman","given":"J."},{"family":"Wyatt","given":"L."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"R."},{"family":"Zhang","given":"B.G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Sustainability of wild algae, fungi and plants harvesting is challenged by many factors and comprises interlinked dimensions such as socio-cultural, economic and political ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a18pv59nuhm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. K. Ghimire, 2008)","plainCitation":"(S. K. Ghimire, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31609,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9KMWDXFV"],"itemData":{"id":31609,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Medicinal plants (MPs) have a high share in non-timber forest products (NTFPs) collected from the Himalaya. These are the major sources of medication for a wide range of ailments for the rural people. Besides their importance in health care, MPs have high socio-cultural, symbolic and economic values, providing income and employment to millions of people living in the region. Unfortunately, in recent decades, the use of many plant-based NTFPs, including MPs, has gone from subsistence collection to large-scale commercial extraction, increasing the probability of overexploitation. Knowledge of the sustainability of the use of such plant resources is thus urgently needed. Sustainability of the use and management of plant resources can be assured with an understanding of the biological/ecological, economic, socio-cultural and political aspects of resource base; understanding of the complex interactions between many of these factors; and with careful planning and management grounded in ecoiogical principles. Management also requires understanding local perceptions, knowledge and decision making systems relating to the resources. Unfortunately, these aspects of research are greatly lacking from the Himalaya. There is a general lack of precise scientific knowledge regarding both the biological and socio-cultural aspects of the resource base and potential for sustainable harvest. This paper reviews the biological and sociocultural aspects of rese?.rch on Himalayan MPs! evaluates current issues and gaps on research on MPs, and outlines the approaches of ecological research in achieving harvesting sustainability of wild MP resources.","language":"en","page":"19","source":"Zotero","title":"Medicinal plants in the Nepal Himalaya: Current issues, sustainable harvesting, knowledge gaps and research priorities","author":[{"family":"Ghimire","given":"Suresh K"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, the sustainable management of medicinal trees requires knowledge on how different species respond to different harvesting techniques ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2fdf1t0csj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Delvaux, Sinsin, Darchambeau, & Van Damme, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Delvaux, Sinsin, Darchambeau, & Van Damme, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31606,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NWQUY2S7"],"itemData":{"id":31606,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2664.2009.01639.x","ISSN":"00218901, 13652664","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"703-712","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recovery from bark harvesting of 12 medicinal tree species in Benin, West Africa","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1365-2664.2009.01639.x","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Delvaux","given":"Claire"},{"family":"Sinsin","given":"Brice"},{"family":"Darchambeau","given":"François"},{"family":"Van Damme","given":"Patrick"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As discussed in section 3.3.3.3.3 invertebrates provide an important source of nutrition in some areas, but data are missing on the sustainability or unsustainability of the gathering of edible insects. Overexploitation probably only concerns some species, but insects and fungi (sections 3.3.2.1; 3.3.3.2.3) on the whole are vulnerable due to the destruction of their habitats, to pesticides and other pollution, and to climate change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2c06c74bq3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arnold van Huis, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Arnold van Huis, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31171,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HAFIMCUM"],"itemData":{"id":31171,"type":"book","collection-number":"171","collection-title":"FAO forestry paper","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-107595-1","number-of-pages":"187","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"Edible insects: future prospects for food and feed security","URL":"https://www.fao.org/3/i3253e/i3253e.pdf","volume":"171","author":[{"family":"Huis","given":"Arnold","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Itterbeeck","given":"Joost Van"},{"family":"Klunder","given":"Harmke"},{"family":"Mertens","given":"Esther"},{"family":"Halloran","given":"Afton"},{"family":"Muir","given":"Giulia"},{"family":"Vantomme","given":"Paul"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} 
      Another limitation of indicators of sustainable use is related to spatial scales. Not all populations, taxa, systems and regions are equally or adequately represented in the scientific literature, meaning that while it is possible to assess the available knowledge, it is not actually possible to assess the sustainability of use. At the global level, there is a lack of pertinent data for many species of whales and seals, and the polar bear (Ursus maritimus) in the Arctic ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1eurp61akb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tierney, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Tierney, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30364,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HZASKJT7"],"itemData":{"id":30364,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            The unsustainable use of wild animals and plants is thought to be a significant driver of biodiversity loss in many regions of the world. The international community has therefore called for action to ensure the sustainable use of living resources and safeguard them for future generations. Indicators that can track changes in populations of species used by humans are essential tools for measuring progress towards these ideals and informing management decisions. Here we present two indicators that could be used to track changes in populations of utilized vertebrate species and levels of harvest sustainability. Preliminary results based on sample data both at the global level and for the Arctic show that utilized species are faring better than other species overall. This could be a consequence of better management of these populations, as indicated by more sustainable harvest levels in recent decades. Limitations of the indicators are still apparent; in particular, there is a lack of data on harvested populations of some vertebrate classes and from certain regions. Focusing monitoring efforts on broadening the scope of data collected and identifying interactions with other potential drivers of decline will strengthen these indicators as policy tools and improve their potential to be incorporated into future sets of indicators to track progress towards global biodiversity targets.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605313000653","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Oryx","language":"en","page":"420-429","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Use it or lose it: measuring trends in wild species subject to substantial use","title-short":"Use it or lose it","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0030605313000653/type/journal_article","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Tierney","given":"Megan"},{"family":"Almond","given":"Rosamunde"},{"family":"Stanwell-Smith","given":"Damon"},{"family":"McRae","given":"Louise"},{"family":"Zöckler","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Collen","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Walpole","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Hutton","given":"Jon"},{"family":"Bie","given":"Steven","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and for many small-scale fisheries in tropical developing countries, such as in Africa, Asia and South America (see small-scale fisheries section). There is a lack of data on how many species in each vertebrate class are used and how much is harvested. For example, data on harvested Arctic species are biased towards marine mammal and marine fish populations, and this could mask declines in some seabird colonies that are over-harvested ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aokqcnesfo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tierney, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Tierney, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30364,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HZASKJT7"],"itemData":{"id":30364,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            The unsustainable use of wild animals and plants is thought to be a significant driver of biodiversity loss in many regions of the world. The international community has therefore called for action to ensure the sustainable use of living resources and safeguard them for future generations. Indicators that can track changes in populations of species used by humans are essential tools for measuring progress towards these ideals and informing management decisions. Here we present two indicators that could be used to track changes in populations of utilized vertebrate species and levels of harvest sustainability. Preliminary results based on sample data both at the global level and for the Arctic show that utilized species are faring better than other species overall. This could be a consequence of better management of these populations, as indicated by more sustainable harvest levels in recent decades. Limitations of the indicators are still apparent; in particular, there is a lack of data on harvested populations of some vertebrate classes and from certain regions. Focusing monitoring efforts on broadening the scope of data collected and identifying interactions with other potential drivers of decline will strengthen these indicators as policy tools and improve their potential to be incorporated into future sets of indicators to track progress towards global biodiversity targets.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605313000653","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Oryx","language":"en","page":"420-429","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Use it or lose it: measuring trends in wild species subject to substantial use","title-short":"Use it or lose it","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0030605313000653/type/journal_article","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Tierney","given":"Megan"},{"family":"Almond","given":"Rosamunde"},{"family":"Stanwell-Smith","given":"Damon"},{"family":"McRae","given":"Louise"},{"family":"Zöckler","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Collen","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Walpole","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Hutton","given":"Jon"},{"family":"Bie","given":"Steven","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Relatedly, many of the conservation models, protocols, procedures, monitoring and assessments are based on experience of animals, notably mammals and birds, and do not necessarily apply to plants, invertebrates or fungi ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"alvo15ptuo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Heywood, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Heywood, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31600,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HSVJUMVU"],"itemData":{"id":31600,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Despite the massive efforts that have been made to conserve plant diversity across the world during the past few decades, it is becoming increasingly evident that our current strategies are not suf?ciently effective to prevent the continuing decline in biodiversity. As a recent report by the CBD indicates, current progress and commitments are insuf?cient to achieve the Aichi Biodiversity Targets by 2020. Threatened species lists continue to grow while the world's governments fail to meet biodiversity conservation goals. Clearly, we are failing in our attempts to conserve biodiversity on a suf?cient scale. The reasons for this situation are complex, including scienti?c, technical, sociological, economic and political factors. The conservation community is divided about how to respond. Some believe that saving all existing biodiversity is still an achievable goal. On the other hand, there are those who believe that we need to accept that biodiversity will inevitably continue to be lost, despite all our conservation actions and that we must focus on what to save, why and where. It has also been suggested that we need a new approach to conservation in the face of the challenges posed by the Anthropocene biosphere which we now inhabit. Whatever view one holds on the above issues, it is clear that we need to review the effectiveness of our current conservation strategies, identify the limiting factors that are preventing the Aichi goals being met and at the same time take whatever steps are necessary to make our conservation protocols more explicit, operational and ef?cient so as to achieve the maximum conservation effect. This paper addresses the key issues that underlie our failure to meet agreed targets and discusses the necessary changes to our conservation approaches. While we can justi?ably be proud of our many achievements and successes in plant conservation in the past 30 years, which have helped slow the rate of loss, unless we devise a more coherent, consistent and integrated global strategy in which both the effectiveness and limitations of our current policies, action plans and procedures are recognized, and re?ect this in national strategies, and then embark on a much bolder and ambitious set of actions, progress will be limited and plant diversity will continue to decline.","container-title":"Plant Diversity","DOI":"10.1016/j.pld.2017.10.004","ISSN":"24682659","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Plant Diversity","language":"en","page":"314-330","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Plant conservation in the Anthropocene – Challenges and future prospects","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2468265917300847","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Heywood","given":"Vernon H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
(iii) Life histories and stocks of marine fish species not well understood. In most fisheries, there exist large gaps in understanding of life histories for many marine fish species, information on total cumulative anthropogenic levels of fishery removals from an individual population, knowledge of the conservation status of individual populations, and deficits in monitoring, including in data collection protocols, observer coverage rates, and sufficient time-series to detect the response in absolute population abundance of long-lived species to this anthropogenic mortality source ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aLG7GUSM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Gilman, 2014, 2020; Lewison, 2004; Musick, 1999)","plainCitation":"(E. Gilman, 2014, 2020; Lewison, 2004; Musick, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R7EZ9ZUS","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IVW97XZW"],"itemData":{"id":31589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12021","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"327-351","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Performance of regional fisheries management organizations: ecosystem-based governance of bycatch and discards","title-short":"Performance of regional fisheries management organizations","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/faf.12021","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Gilman","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Passfield","given":"Kelvin"},{"family":"Nakamura","given":"Katrina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",6]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31590,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PCFC58VI"],"itemData":{"id":31590,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/s41598-020-71021-x","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"14017","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Benchmarking global fisheries discards","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-020-71021-x","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Gilman","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Perez Roda","given":"A."},{"family":"Huntington","given":"T."},{"family":"Kennelly","given":"S. J."},{"family":"Suuronen","given":"P."},{"family":"Chaloupka","given":"M."},{"family":"Medley","given":"P. A. H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31588,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GRQJITCS"],"itemData":{"id":31588,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2004.09.004","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"598-604","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Understanding impacts of fisheries bycatch on marine megafauna","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534704002642","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Lewison","given":"R"},{"family":"Crowder","given":"L"},{"family":"Read","given":"A"},{"family":"Freeman","given":"S"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",11]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":8635,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PMJQPPXD"],"itemData":{"id":8635,"type":"book","call-number":"QL121 .L53 1999","collection-number":"23","collection-title":"American Fisheries Society symposium","event-place":"Bethesda, Md","ISBN":"978-1-888569-15-5","number-of-pages":"265","publisher":"American Fisheries Society","publisher-place":"Bethesda, Md","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Life in the slow lane: ecology and conservation of long-lived marine animals","title-short":"Life in the slow lane","editor":[{"family":"Musick","given":"John A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Status of fish stocks of both large- and small-scale fishing is little understood for those countries and regions where fishing management intensity is low. Further, there is data of status and trends individual fish stocks for IPBES regions such as Europe (e.g., https://www.eumofa.eu/) and North America, whereas data for other IPBES regions are missing.

(iv) Knowledge gap in direct and collateral sources of fishing mortality on associated and dependent species. While there is increasing understanding of the status of stocks of principal market species of marine capture fisheries, albeit still incomplete especially in low-income countries, there remains a very large gap in knowledge of the effects of direct and collateral sources of fishing mortality on associated and dependent species including fecund species. For example, rare-event bycatch of species such as toothed whales and some pelagic sharks are unmonitored in most fisheries, there is a lack of knowledge of which populations are captured in individual fisheries, and as a result of these data quality constraints, extremely limited understanding of the sustainability of the ‘use’ of these wild species. For instance, 47 of 68 fisheries that catch marine resources managed by regional fisheries management organizations have no observer coverage ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mp6Aq4xh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Gilman, 2014)","plainCitation":"(E. Gilman, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R7EZ9ZUS","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IVW97XZW"],"itemData":{"id":31589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12021","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"327-351","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Performance of regional fisheries management organizations: ecosystem-based governance of bycatch and discards","title-short":"Performance of regional fisheries management organizations","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/faf.12021","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Gilman","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Passfield","given":"Kelvin"},{"family":"Nakamura","given":"Katrina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",6]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} for the vast majority of the ca. 4.6 million fishing vessels globally, information on non-retained catch is non-existent, and information on retained catch only is available in some cases. While a target stock of a relatively productive species may be determined to be sustainable when assessed against various standards, the sustainability of the fishery and when assessed against impacts on incidentally captured species is very often unknown. Stock assessments which do not incorporate recreational fishing do not provide accurate assessments of global uptake and fish mortality.

(v) Data gaps on sustainable use of wild species and their monitoring regarding small-scale fisheries, inland fisheries, marine and freshwater fisheries, and reef fisheries. One of the major challenges or data gaps to properly assess sustainable use of wild species, especially regarding small-scale fisheries and inland fisheries in tropical developing countries consists in the lack of long temporal series of data on resource use. Most of the small-scale fisheries worldwide show a chronic lack of monitoring data on time series of landings, fishing effort, biology of exploited species, among other relevant fisheries indicators ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kfWdlfVo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Welcomme, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Welcomme, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31578,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M8FMGU2T"],"itemData":{"id":31578,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","ISSN":"1095-9289","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","page":"1751-1756","title":"An overview of global catch statistics for inland fish","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Welcomme","given":"Robin L"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, there is no reliable information on value or number and diversity of sustainability of marine and freshwater ornamental fishery, and many species of reef fishes lack biological and ecological information. This indicates that conservation status of almost half of the species is still unknown ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1felmn4r4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(SOTWP, 2016)","plainCitation":"(SOTWP, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30348,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QD6CQVU9"],"itemData":{"id":30348,"type":"speech","abstract":"KEW,  State of the World's Plants","event-title":"https://stateoftheworldsplants.org/2016/(acessed 2020)","title":"KEW,  State of the World's Plants","author":[{"literal":"SOTWP"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      This lack of data precludes a proper assessment of the sustainability of most small-scale fisheries and inland fisheries. Furthermore, those indicators based on stock dynamics or population parameters, which have been widely applied in industrial fisheries, may not be suitable to complex, multispecies small-scale fisheries, or data needed to calculate these indicators cannot be gathered on a cost-effective and timely manner to inform policy intervention in many small-scale fisheries and inland fisheries. However, these limitations have been successfully addressed, in the context of small-scale fisheries, by studies adopting a scientific approach to record and analyze local or indigenous knowledge held by small-scale fishers on resource use over broad temporal scales (see section 3.3.1.3.2).
      
(vi) Research gap in gathering. Estimates on the number wild plant species that are used across different regions are unclear, despite documentation from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a21vmb7gr86","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(SOTWP, 2020)","plainCitation":"(SOTWP, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30349,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W7HU2HP9"],"itemData":{"id":30349,"type":"speech","abstract":"KEW, State of the World's Plants","title":"KEW, State of the World's Plants","URL":"Kew Science | Kew","author":[{"family":"SOTWP","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"abqpsm6gpf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FOC, 2020)","plainCitation":"(FOC, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30353,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7DIQAQCF"],"itemData":{"id":30353,"type":"speech","title":"The Flora of China (FOC)","URL":"http://www.iplant.cn/frps/jingji/2?page=2","author":[{"literal":"FOC"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, there are limited information on wild species used as food, and these come mainly from ethnological or ecological inventories. As a global phenomenon, urban gathering that promotes positive cultural, ecological, economic and health outcomes research has received little scholarly attention and due emphasis has not been equally given in all regions of the globe. For example, 70% of the studies are from the Americas, Europe and Central Asia, 20% are from Africa, and the remaining are from Asia and the Pacific based on literature search retrieved for this assessment. Recently, an emerging gap has been in high demand of collection of recently described new species or rare species when their type localities were published in particular by specialized collectors. For this reason, an increasing number of scientists warn against publishing type localities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aaptto7bpm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lindenmayer & Scheele, 2017b)","plainCitation":"(Lindenmayer & Scheele, 2017b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31580,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SLL9DKQ9"],"itemData":{"id":31580,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Limiting open-access information on rare and endangered species will help to protect them\\n          , \\n            \\n              Biologists have long valued publishing detailed information on rare and endangered species. Until relatively recently, much of this information was accessible only through accessing specialized scientific journals in university libraries. However, much of these data have been transferred online with the advent of digital platforms and a rapid push to open-access publication. Information is increasingly also available online in public reports and wildlife atlases, and research published behind paywalls can often be found in the public domain. Increased data and information accessibility has many benefits, such as helping to improve repeatability in scientific studies and enhancing collaboration (\\n              1\\n              ,\\n              2\\n              ). However, such readily accessible information also creates major problems in the context of conserving endangered species.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aan1362","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6340","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"800-801","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Do not publish","URL":"https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aan1362","volume":"356","author":[{"family":"Lindenmayer","given":"David"},{"family":"Scheele","given":"Ben"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",5,26]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; and the sustainability of this form of consumer-driven use is unclear. 

(vii) No data for global sale of cut flowers from wild and cultivated conditions. Cut flower or foliage of Bromeliads, or ornamental plants like Aloe and Orchids share global market and these plant species are either gathered from cultivated or wild sources. But no data was available at the time of this assessment on the share of global market sales from wild vs cultivated plants.  

(viii) Gaps in ex-situ conservation of wild plant species. Botanic gardens gather live plant species from wild for conservation purpose, however, those botanical collections have focused mainly in the temperate parts of the world. For example, the PlantSearch database hosted by Botanic Gardens Conservation International indicates that 107,340 accepted species grow in botanic garden collections, representing 31% of vascular plant species. However, 93% of these species are held in temperate parts of the world. As a result, a temperate species has a 60% chance of being cultivated within the botanic garden network, whereas a tropical species has only a 25% chance. Similarly, the diversity of crop wild relatives is poorly represented in gene banks. For example, there are over 78,000 accessions representing about 688 species of crop wild relatives in gene banks, and over 70% of taxa are recommended as high priority for gathering so as to improve their representation in gene banks. However, gaps in gathering occur in the Mediterranean and the near East, Western and Southern Europe, Southeast and East Asia, and South America (Figure 3.45).  

(viii) Identification gaps in taxonomic groups of terrestrial animal harvesting. Some groups of terrestrial animals harvested mainly for trade lack proper identification. For example, more than 50% of all traded individuals of reptiles had no species-specific identification, and this makes implementation of species-based regulations ineffective. Further, scientific studies suggest that consumption of Didelphis marsupialis, a species of undeniable cultural significance for local communities in Latin America, but carrying a reservoir of parasites that cause severe diseases, should be the subject of further study.   
 
(ix) Insufficient information on recreation from green hunting. Green hunting that takes place with the help of tranquilizer dart guns is cheaper and less harmful compared to traditional hunting (section 3.3.3.4.2. However, green hunting is as of yet not a significant recreational activity. There exists insufficient information on the status, trends and/or impact of the activity with regards to its potential impact on sustainable use of terrestrial animal harvesting from wild. 

(x) Gap of trade of exotic pet animal species under the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora list. Many wild animal species have been unsustainably traded to supply the international pet markets for natural breeding purpose, including rare and endemic species that are most threatened. Even with existing international regulations, the majority of species in exotic pet trade are not protected under the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, therefore, leaving international trade mostly unregulated and unmonitored of threatened species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2kgpjvgn4q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Janssen & Shepherd, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Janssen & Shepherd, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31029,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WNNTFEXS"],"itemData":{"id":31029,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000514194200002","container-title":"Journal of Asia-Pacific Biodiversity","DOI":"10.1016/j.japb.2018.09.003","issue":"4","page":"476-481","title":"Challenges in documenting trade in non CITES-listed species: A case study on crocodile skinks (Tribolonotus spp.)","URL":"://WOS:000514194200002","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Janssen","given":"J."},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"C. R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (section 3.3.3).

(xii) Inadequate available information on wild species informal and formal trade, and consumption. Wild species are traded in informal and formal markets. Much of this trade goes unrecorded and is difficult to monitor. A complex and nuanced temporal association between the illegal and legal wild species trades exist ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ag2kj600k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tittensor, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Tittensor, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31584,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XEA5AS6W"],"itemData":{"id":31584,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The international legal trade in wildlife can provide economic and other benefits, but when unsustainable can be a driver of population declines. This impact is magnified by the additional burden of illegal trade, yet how it covaries with legal trade remains little explored. We combined law-enforcement time-series of seizures of wildlife goods imported into the United States (US) and the European Union (EU) with data on reported legal trade to evaluate the evidence for any relationships. Our analysis examined 28 US and 20 EU products derived from CITES-listed species with high volume and frequency of both reported trade and seizures. On average, seizures added 28% and 9% to US and EU reported legal trade levels respectively, and in several cases exceeded legal imports. We detected a significant but weak overall positive relationship between seizure volumes and reported trade into the US over time, but not into the EU. These results highlight the importance of maintaining long-term records of border seizures and enforcement effort, and accounting for illegal trade where possible in non-detriment findings. Our findings suggest a complex and nuanced temporal association between the illegal and legal wildlife trades.","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12724","ISSN":"1755-263X, 1755-263X","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"CONSERVATION LETTERS","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Evaluating the relationships between the legal and illegal international wildlife trades","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/conl.12724","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Tittensor","given":"Derek P."},{"family":"Harfoot","given":"Michael"},{"family":"McLardy","given":"Claire"},{"family":"Britten","given":"Gregory L."},{"family":"Kecse?Nagy","given":"Katalin"},{"family":"Landry","given":"Bryan"},{"family":"Outhwaite","given":"Willow"},{"family":"Price","given":"Becky"},{"family":"Sinovas","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Blanc","given":"Julian"},{"family":"Burgess","given":"Neil D."},{"family":"Malsch","given":"Kelly"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The gap is so great that in many cases the phrase, “we do not know what we do not know” applies. Cases where it is known that data are lacking include tropical fish for the aquarium trade, freshwater turtles and tortoises for terraria, recreational fishing (including catch and release) and spearfishing, amphibians, and reptiles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aencq7fkbh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. R. N. Alves, Rosa,, 2013; Leandro Castello, McGrath, & Beck, 2011b; C. Costello, 2012; Schlaepfer, Hoover, & Dodd, 2005b)","plainCitation":"(R. R. N. Alves, Rosa,, 2013; Leandro Castello, McGrath, & Beck, 2011b; C. Costello, 2012; Schlaepfer, Hoover, & Dodd, 2005b)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31097,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BWZDUXDM"],"itemData":{"id":31097,"type":"chapter","abstract":"The use of animals, plants, and fungi as a major source of local medicine for people’s well-being in rural areas has been documented in different parts of the world, and has also been recorded in urban environments. In urban settings, the trade of wildlife-based medicinal products is concentrated in local and traditional markets, where various species of medicinal plants and animals are commercialized. The commercialization and use of biological resources as remedies encompass cultural, social, and economic aspects and bear important implications for conservation, especially in relation to the most heavily exploited species. This chapter reviews the literature on commercialization of medicinal animals in local markets, focusing on urban zootherapy and on the social actors involved in these practices. In doing so, we hope to highlight the importance of further research on the topic and to explore ways through which conservation and management initiatives can improve dialog with resource users and traders.","container-title":"Animals in Traditional Folk Medicine","event-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","ISBN":"978-3-642-29025-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-642-29026-8_3","page":"25-42","publisher":"Springer Berlin Heidelberg","publisher-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Medicine from the Wild: An Overview of the Use and Trade of Animal Products in Traditional Medicines","title-short":"Medicine from the Wild","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-642-29026-8_3","editor":[{"family":"Alves","given":"Rômulo Romeu Nóbrega"},{"family":"Rosa","given":"Ierecê Lucena"}],"author":[{"family":"Alves","given":"Rômulo Romeu Nóbrega"},{"family":"Rosa","given":"Ierecê Lucena"},{"family":"Albuquerque","given":"Ulysses Paulino"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":34945,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8Z5MJ63Q"],"itemData":{"id":34945,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Resource assessments of small-scale ?sheries in the Amazon are rare, making it dif?cult to improve ?sheries management. This study assessed the sustainability of small-scale ?shery resources in the Lower Amazon region using data from over 20,000 interviews collected in three ?oodplain ?shing communities between 1992 and 2007. The data were analyzed with respect to theoretical maxima of catches and historical trends of (i) catch per unit effort (CPUE), (ii) species composition of the catch, and (iii) mean body length of the most-caught species. The most important results were: First, observed catches have been about 54–58% of theoretical maxima in all communities. Second, CPUE has remained stable over time in all communities. Third, there has been no substitution of ?sh species in the catch. Fourth, mean body lengths of ?ve of the nine most-caught species were below length-at-?rst-reproduction, and the body lengths of some have increased overtime while that of others have remained stable or decreased. Overall, ?shery resources in the region appear to be moderately exploited, with some key species showing typical signs of overexploitation. This would indicate that the principal threat to the sustainability of ?shery resources is excessive concentration of ?shing effort on a few target species combined with unsustainable ?shing practices. Management and research recommendations are proposed.","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2011.05.002","ISSN":"01657836","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Research","language":"en","page":"356-364","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Resource sustainability in small-scale fisheries in the Lower Amazon floodplains","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0165783611001913","volume":"110","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"McGrath","given":"David G."},{"family":"Beck","given":"Pieter S.A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",7]]}}},{"id":31424,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/55D9HWJC"],"itemData":{"id":31424,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1223389","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6106","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"517-520","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Status and Solutions for the World's Unassessed Fisheries","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1223389","volume":"338","author":[{"family":"Costello","given":"C."},{"family":"Ovando","given":"D."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"R."},{"family":"Gaines","given":"S. D."},{"family":"Deschenes","given":"O."},{"family":"Lester","given":"S. E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",10,26]]}}},{"id":31579,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SCFZCTV6"],"itemData":{"id":31579,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Amphibians and reptiles are taken from the wild and sold commercially as food, pets, and traditional medicines. The overcollecting of some species highlights the need to assess the trade and ensure that it is not contributing to declines in wild populations. Unlike most countries, the United States tracks the imports and exports of all amphibians and reptiles. Records from 1998 to 2002 reveal a US trade of several million wild-caught amphibians and reptiles each year, although many shipments are not recorded at the species level. The magnitude and content of the global commercial trade carries even greater unknowns. The absence of accurate trade and biological information for most species makes it difficult to establish whether current take levels are sustainable. The void of information also implies that population declines due to overcollecting could be going undetected. Policy changes to acquire baseline biological information and ensure a sustainable trade are urgently needed.","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1641/0006-3568(2005)055[0256:CIETIO]2.0.CO;2","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"BioScience","language":"en","page":"256","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Challenges in Evaluating the Impact of the Trade in Amphibians and Reptiles on Wild Populations","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/bioscience/article/55/3/256-264/249719","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Schlaepfer","given":"Martin A."},{"family":"Hoover","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Dodd","given":"C. Kenneth"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, insects, especially butterflies and beetles, are harvested and traded all over the world, but there are few data about this exploitation and trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2d4p2hoclc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alan L. Yen, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Alan L. Yen, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30881,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RHVCQBU7"],"itemData":{"id":30881,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"With an increasing human population and environmental degradation, the world faces a major problem in providing adequate animal based proteins. Many traditional societies have used or still use insects as a protein source, while westernized societies are reluctant to use insects, despite being the major consumers of animal proteins. We now need to consider insects as a source of food for humans in a manner that acknowledges both the role of entomophagy in indigenous societies and the need for westernized societies to reduce the size of their environmental footprint with regard to food production. The situation on continents such as Africa, Asia, and Central and South America has some parallels to Australia in that there are two forces in operation: the sustainable traditional use of edible insects and the “westernization” of these societies leading to a movement away from entomophagy. However, the potential to reach a compromise is greater in these continents because entomophagy is already accepted. The major challenges will be establishing sustainable production systems that include food safety and security as well as environmental protection. Whether this will happen or not will depend upon: (i) a major change in attitude in westernized societies towards entomophagy; (ii) pressure to conserve remaining habitats in a sustainable manner; (iii) economic impetus to develop food production systems that include insects; and (iv) an acknowledgement that achieving adequate nutrition on a global basis will involve different diets in much of the developed world.","container-title":"Entomological Research","DOI":"10.1111/j.1748-5967.2009.00239.x","ISSN":"17382297, 17485967","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"289-298","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Edible insects: Traditional knowledge or western phobia?","title-short":"Edible insects","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1748-5967.2009.00239.x","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Yen","given":"Alan L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Further, the consumer-driven harvest of live specimens may have benefits for local peoples’ economically, however, sustainability of use is unclear. 
      Wild meat harvest and trade are often excluded from official statistics ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a6g6g7chg9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pangau-Adam, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Pangau-Adam, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30899,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NM3LY8VL"],"itemData":{"id":30899,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"It is well known that wild game provides a significant proportion of the dietary protein of the indigenous people of the eastern half of New Guinea (PNG), but almost nothing is known of its importance in the western half (the Indonesian province of Papua or Irian Jaya). We quantified hunting effort, harvest rates and wild meat consumption and sale in the Jayapura region of north-east Papua through interviews with 147 hunters from 21 villages and meal surveys in 93 households. Ten species of mammals, seven species of birds and at least two species of reptiles were harvested in our study area, but the introduced wild pig and rusa deer were the major target species. Hunting in our study area has shifted from a purely subsistence activity towards a more commercial form at least partly due to the emergence of markets created by Indonesian transmigrants. Although the hunting of non-indigenous and certain native species might be sustainable, the maintenance of populations of large threatened species will require sensitive management.","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-012-9492-5","ISSN":"1572-9915","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Hum Ecol","language":"en","page":"611-621","source":"Springer Link","title":"Wildmeat or Bushmeat? Subsistence Hunting and Commercial Harvesting in Papua (West New Guinea), Indonesia","title-short":"Wildmeat or Bushmeat?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10745-012-9492-5","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Pangau-Adam","given":"Margaretha"},{"family":"Noske","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Muehlenberg","given":"Michael"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",8,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Overall, there is much less information available on wild meat harvest in the Asian tropics, especially outside Borneo  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a12p7985noe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Swamy & Pinedo-Vasquez, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Swamy & Pinedo-Vasquez, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31602,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9I42YN9I"],"itemData":{"id":31602,"type":"article-journal","language":"en","page":"32","source":"Zotero","title":"Bushmeat harvest in tropical forests","author":[{"family":"Swamy","given":"Varun"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A conspicuous knowledge gap concerning the causes of lion mortality has been identified, and this requires knowledge of both the existing population size and its dynamics over time and space (fecundity and mortality) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2d0qc8laj6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(David W. Macdonald, 2017)","plainCitation":"(David W. Macdonald, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31096,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/USYEP3YC"],"itemData":{"id":31096,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"What does trophy hunting (selective hunting for recreation) contribute to wild lion conservation? Macdonald (Report on Lion Conservation with Particular Respect to the Issue of Trophy Hunting. WildCRU, Oxford, UK, 2016) summarises what we know. We identify unknowns, gaps in the knowledge that inhibit conservation planning, including: the causes of lion mortality, the amount of land used for lion trophy hunting, the extent to which trophy hunting depends on lions for financial viability, and the vulnerability of areas used for hunting to conversion to land not used for wildlife, if trophy hunting ceased. The cost of reversing biodiversity loss exceeds income from tourism, including hunting. New financial models are needed, particularly in view of the expanding human population in Africa.","container-title":"Mammal Review","DOI":"10.1111/mam.12096","ISSN":"03051838","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Mam Rev","language":"en","page":"247-253","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Lions, trophy hunting and beyond: knowledge gaps and why they matter","title-short":"Lions, trophy hunting and beyond","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/mam.12096","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."},{"family":"Loveridge","given":"Andrew J."},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Paul J."},{"family":"Jacobsen","given":"Kim S."},{"family":"Du Preez","given":"Byron"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition, where markets in such species are monitored, often it is not clear whether sources are wild or domesticated. 
      Existing data are available mainly for timber species traded in the global market ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a96um8vps1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2018a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2018a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31586,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YNF6233U"],"itemData":{"id":31586,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","collection-title":"State of the world's forests","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"en","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Forests pathways to sustainable development","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but timber from illegal logging activities used within producing countries as well as across the transboundary are not available ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ac0mkb2b62","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. P. Chaudhary, 2016)","plainCitation":"(R. P. Chaudhary, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31610,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9MC5TAJV"],"itemData":{"id":31610,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological and Environmental hazards, risks, and disasters","note":"publisher: Elsevier Amsterdam","page":"335–372","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Deforestation in Nepal: Causes, consequences and responses","title-short":"Deforestation in Nepal","author":[{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"R. P."},{"family":"Uprety","given":"Y."},{"family":"Rimal","given":"S. K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  

(xii) Knowledge gap in logging. Timbers are supplied to the markets; however, it is unclear to estimate which come from legal or illegal sources as well as differentiate timber from wild vs plantation sources. 

(xiii) Knowledge gap in non-extractive practice and uses. Assessment of knowledge gap in non-extractive practice and use is challenging as the non-extractive use of nature often does not include species described at a species level, but frequently they appear as part of a functional group (e.g., trees in urban green spaces) or in terms of multifunctional landscapes (e.g., worship of sacred groves). Further research is especially needed to clarify the benefits of living in nature and focus on ecosystem elements. For example, in commercial wildlife watching, an increasing number of wild species such as megafauna and ‘charismatic’ wild species are integrated into tourism operations. Megafauna are well studied taxa of animals, whereas there is a lack of research on the impacts of tourism on the lesser fauna, e.g., ground-dwelling mammals, small reptiles, insects, etc. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aml177u14l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. D. Wolf, 2019)","plainCitation":"(I. D. Wolf, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31325,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZH5VK5XU"],"itemData":{"id":31325,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environments","DOI":"10.3390/environments6090104","issue":"9","note":"publisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"104","title":"Nature Conservation and Nature-Based Tourism: A Paradox?","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Wolf","given":"Isabelle D"},{"family":"Croft","given":"David B"},{"family":"Green","given":"Ronda J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The literature on the non-extractive use of wild species for medicine and hygiene shows many positive benefits on human individuals, but there is an absence of research on the effects of wild species on human community health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a14main1osg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nesbitt, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Nesbitt, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31462,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M5VGC7AJ"],"itemData":{"id":31462,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2017.05.005","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"103-111","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The social and economic value of cultural ecosystem services provided by urban forests in North America: A review and suggestions for future research","title-short":"The social and economic value of cultural ecosystem services provided by urban forests in North America","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1618866717300456","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Nesbitt","given":"Lorien"},{"family":"Hotte","given":"Ngaio"},{"family":"Barron","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Cowan","given":"Judith"},{"family":"Sheppard","given":"Stephen R.J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There is almost no information on the global or regional trends in the non-extractive use of wild species for human health. No research has looked at the sustained, long-term effects of nature-based therapies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2n2rjnlkrj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rajoo, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Rajoo, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31461,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3NKRR7PR"],"itemData":{"id":31461,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2020.126744","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126744","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The physiological and psychosocial effects of forest therapy: A systematic review","title-short":"The physiological and psychosocial effects of forest therapy","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1618866720304039","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Rajoo","given":"Keeren Sundara"},{"family":"Karam","given":"Daljit Singh"},{"family":"Abdullah","given":"Mohd Zaki"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
 
(xiv) Gaps in inter-practice trade-offs and synergy. It is well known that different practices interact themselves and are connected with each other; however, the knowledge gap involves the lack of inter-practice trade-offs and synergies, such as between and among fishing, gathering, terrestrial animal harvesting, logging, and non-extractive practices across global, regional, national and local policy and program.  

(xv) Lack of critical linkages between nature’s contributions to people and quality of life and benefit gaps. There has been broad uptake of the critical linkages between nature’s contributions to people and quality of life. However, knowledge gap exists on the status of species and nature’s contribution to people linked to specific ecosystem functions, and interrelationships between gender equality, nature and nature’s contribution to people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1i2fpf3lpp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Therefore, enhanced attention is needed to develop specific variables and indicators to understand the multiple intricate ways in which peoples’ well-being / quality of life and nature’s contributions to people influence each other in a two-way feedback-oriented process ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2fior4rq8h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chaplin-Kramer, 2019; S. D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az, Demissew, Joly, Lonsdale, & Larigauderie, 2015; IPBES, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Chaplin-Kramer, 2019; S. Díaz, Demissew, Joly, Lonsdale, & Larigauderie, 2015; IPBES, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31608,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KPWRBEYK"],"itemData":{"id":31608,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The magnitude and pace of global change demand rapid assessment of nature and its contributions to people. We present a fine-scale global modeling of current status and future scenarios for several contributions: water quality regulation, coastal risk reduction, and crop pollination. We find that where people’s needs for nature are now greatest, nature’s ability to meet those needs is declining. Up to 5 billion people face higher water pollution and insufficient pollination for nutrition under future scenarios of land use and climate change, particularly in Africa and South Asia. Hundreds of millions of people face heightened coastal risk across Africa, Eurasia, and the Americas. Continued loss of nature poses severe threats, yet these can be reduced 3- to 10-fold under a sustainable development scenario.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aaw3372","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6462","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"255-258","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Global modeling of nature’s contributions to people","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.aaw3372","volume":"366","author":[{"family":"Chaplin-Kramer","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Sharp","given":"Richard P."},{"family":"Weil","given":"Charlotte"},{"family":"Bennett","given":"Elena M."},{"family":"Pascual","given":"Unai"},{"family":"Arkema","given":"Katie K."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"Kate A."},{"family":"Bryant","given":"Benjamin P."},{"family":"Guerry","given":"Anne D."},{"family":"Haddad","given":"Nick M."},{"family":"Hamann","given":"Maike"},{"family":"Hamel","given":"Perrine"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Justin A."},{"family":"Mandle","given":"Lisa"},{"family":"Pereira","given":"Henrique M."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Ruckelshaus","given":"Mary"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"M. Rebecca"},{"family":"Silver","given":"Jessica M."},{"family":"Vogl","given":"Adrian L."},{"family":"Daily","given":"Gretchen C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,11]]}}},{"id":15,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U4MZ3EBT"],"itemData":{"id":15,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"After a long incubation period, the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES) is now underway. Underpinning all its activities is the IPBES Conceptual Framework (CF), a simplified model of the interactions between nature and people. Drawing on the legacy of previous large-scale environmental assessments, the CF goes further in explicitly embracing different disciplines and knowledge systems (including indigenous and local knowledge) in the co-construction of assessments of the state of the world's biodiversity and the benefits it provides to humans. The CF can be thought of as a kind of \\"Rosetta Stone\\" that highlights commonalities between diverse value sets and seeks to facilitate crossdisciplinary and crosscultural understanding. We argue that the CF will contribute to the increasing trend towards interdisciplinarity in understanding and managing the environment. Rather than displacing disciplinary science, however, we believe that the CF will provide new contexts of discovery and policy applications for it.","container-title":"PLoS biology","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.1002040","ISSN":"15457885","issue":"1","note":"PMID: 25585296","page":"e1002040","title":"A Rosetta Stone for nature's benefits to people","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002040","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Demissew","given":"Sebsebe"},{"family":"Joly","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Lonsdale","given":"W. Mark"},{"family":"Larigauderie","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is also important to ascertain that such a connection draws on integration of indigenous and local knowledge and their effective participation pays judicious attention to scientific knowledge and strengthens linkage between nature and nature’s contribution to people (ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a12nn4mh4ae","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az, 2015)","plainCitation":"(S. Díaz, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":15,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U4MZ3EBT"],"itemData":{"id":15,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"After a long incubation period, the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES) is now underway. Underpinning all its activities is the IPBES Conceptual Framework (CF), a simplified model of the interactions between nature and people. Drawing on the legacy of previous large-scale environmental assessments, the CF goes further in explicitly embracing different disciplines and knowledge systems (including indigenous and local knowledge) in the co-construction of assessments of the state of the world's biodiversity and the benefits it provides to humans. The CF can be thought of as a kind of \\"Rosetta Stone\\" that highlights commonalities between diverse value sets and seeks to facilitate crossdisciplinary and crosscultural understanding. We argue that the CF will contribute to the increasing trend towards interdisciplinarity in understanding and managing the environment. Rather than displacing disciplinary science, however, we believe that the CF will provide new contexts of discovery and policy applications for it.","container-title":"PLoS biology","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.1002040","ISSN":"15457885","issue":"1","note":"PMID: 25585296","page":"e1002040","title":"A Rosetta Stone for nature's benefits to people","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002040","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Demissew","given":"Sebsebe"},{"family":"Joly","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Lonsdale","given":"W. Mark"},{"family":"Larigauderie","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	There are important methodological limitations to many of the studies exploring nature-based therapy or the presence of wild species on human health. Furthermore, the majority of the studies are correlative and the involvement of medical professionals is encouraged, as well as an increased diversity of study participants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ff2o5s2b1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rajoo, 2020; Sandifer, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Rajoo, 2020; Sandifer, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31461,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3NKRR7PR"],"itemData":{"id":31461,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2020.126744","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126744","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The physiological and psychosocial effects of forest therapy: A systematic review","title-short":"The physiological and psychosocial effects of forest therapy","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1618866720304039","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Rajoo","given":"Keeren Sundara"},{"family":"Karam","given":"Daljit Singh"},{"family":"Abdullah","given":"Mohd Zaki"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}},{"id":31455,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F2ZASMCA"],"itemData":{"id":31455,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2014.12.007","ISSN":"22120416","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystem Services","language":"en","page":"1-15","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Exploring connections among nature, biodiversity, ecosystem services, and human health and well-being: Opportunities to enhance health and biodiversity conservation","title-short":"Exploring connections among nature, biodiversity, ecosystem services, and human health and well-being","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2212041614001648","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Sandifer","given":"Paul A."},{"family":"Sutton-Grier","given":"Ariana E."},{"family":"Ward","given":"Bethney P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The causal mechanisms that underlie the benefits people receive from health-based use of wild species is underexplored ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1lg5ieti0c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sandifer, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Sandifer, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31455,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F2ZASMCA"],"itemData":{"id":31455,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2014.12.007","ISSN":"22120416","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystem Services","language":"en","page":"1-15","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Exploring connections among nature, biodiversity, ecosystem services, and human health and well-being: Opportunities to enhance health and biodiversity conservation","title-short":"Exploring connections among nature, biodiversity, ecosystem services, and human health and well-being","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2212041614001648","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Sandifer","given":"Paul A."},{"family":"Sutton-Grier","given":"Ariana E."},{"family":"Ward","given":"Bethney P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Currently, there is limited evidence for environmental microbial exposure boosting human immune system response and no causal evidence for the phytoncides hypothesis was identified.
      Another important aspect to consider is that there is a poor understanding of how biodiversity affects people’s well-being and health through cultural pathways, and how that is being affected by changes in the status and trends in sustainable use. A better understanding by linking biodiversity change with human culture values, well-being, and health might be profoundly important for biodiversity conservation and public health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aabqg1nqoc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. E. Clark, 2014)","plainCitation":"(N. E. Clark, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31591,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DJ9YYVFK"],"itemData":{"id":31591,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2014.01.009","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"198-204","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Biodiversity, cultural pathways, and human health: a framework","title-short":"Biodiversity, cultural pathways, and human health","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534714000238","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Clark","given":"Natalie E."},{"family":"Lovell","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Wheeler","given":"Benedict W."},{"family":"Higgins","given":"Sahran L."},{"family":"Depledge","given":"Michael H."},{"family":"Norris","given":"Ken"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is believed that diversity of positive values is important for countering negative values and support conservation action when needed.
      
(xvi) Inadequate economic valuation of wild species. A considerable body of valuation studies focuses on the economics of nature’s contributions to people at the global scale (e.g., carbon stocks and flows) delivered to people outside the countries where natural ecosystems and wild species occur ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a16mf3ijgv2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the societal values of the gathering and use of wild species in local markets have not been properly addressed. Wild species are an integral component of ecosystems, and the value they provide in terms of services should be a standard part of ecosystem assessments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a25ahl8i3co","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Puri, Yadav, & Joshi, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Puri, Yadav, & Joshi, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31598,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LVQWMNZP"],"itemData":{"id":31598,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This article focuses on the functional role of Asian elephant in maintaining forest ecosystem and biodiversity. Elephant performs different activities in forest which greatly influence ecosystem processes, plant communities and trophic structure of the community. Elephants have cascading effects in altering the vegetation regeneration and providing habitat, water and nutrients to other animals. Besides, elephants also have affirmative effects in controlling illegal poaching, promoting ecotourism and act as an indicator species to know about natural calamities. The information compiled in the present note is based on the literature available and field experience gained in the Rajaji National Park, India. Since elephants play a crucial role in ecological trajectories of ecological communities, developing a rigorous framework to evaluate the qualitative influence made by the elephants in ecosystem would be of paramount importance.","container-title":"Asian Journal of Environment & Ecology","DOI":"10.9734/ajee/2019/v9i230091","ISSN":"2456-690X","journalAbbreviation":"AJEE","language":"en","page":"1-8","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Functional Role of Elephants in Maintaining Forest Ecosystem and Biodiversity: Lessons from Northwestern Elephant Range in India","title-short":"Functional Role of Elephants in Maintaining Forest Ecosystem and Biodiversity","URL":"http://www.journalajee.com/index.php/AJEE/article/view/30091","author":[{"family":"Puri","given":"Kanchan"},{"family":"Yadav","given":"Vishant"},{"family":"Joshi","given":"Ritesh"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",5,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Though, it needs to be recognized that there is no distinct division between wild (unmanaged) biodiversity and human managed biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ai8m1vjrgr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. A. Tisdell, 2015)","plainCitation":"(C. A. Tisdell, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31582,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QLIFI7DG"],"itemData":{"id":31582,"type":"chapter","abstract":"Whereas the previous part of this book focused primarily on human managed biodiversity and human developed ecosystems, this part deals mainly with the economics of the conservation and loss of wild biodiversity and natural ecosystems. The discussion in this chapter extends that in Chapters 2 and 3, by paying particular attention to the basic economics of the loss of wild biodiversity, including natural ecosystems. However, it needs to be recognized that there is no distinct division between wild (unmanaged) biodiversity and human managed biodiversity because the extent to which human beings influence biodiversity forms a continuous spectrum.\\n\\nNevertheless, because of bounded rationality, it is not practical to analyse all possibilities on the multidimensional spectrum of biodiversity. It is only practical to analyse some of the ‘ideal’ or selected types in this spectrum. By doing so, we can obtain an improved appreciation of the whole spectrum and can develop our selection of economic techniques to assess these different situations. In cases where biodiversity is highly controlled by human beings, private goods (the consumption of which is rivalrous and excludable) are mainly produced. By contrast, in cases where wild (unmanaged) biodiversity is a prominent feature of an ecosystem, the commodities (services) supplied usually contain a large public good or quasi-public good component, even though a private good component may also be present. For example, products, such as wild fish, involving rivalrous consumption may be extracted from unmanaged or slightly managed ecosystems.","container-title":"Sustaining Biodiversity and Ecosystem Functions","ISBN":"978-1-78536-268-2","page":"245-274","publisher":"Edward Elgar Publishing","title":"The conservation and loss of wild biodiversity and natural ecosystems: basic economic issues","author":[{"family":"Tisdell","given":"C A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A comprehensive assessment of the contributions (current or potential) of wild species in protected areas (terrestrial and marine), such as watching of animals, recreational tourism, recreational fishing, trophy hunting, among others to promote social and economic sustainability, besides ecological sustainability is lacking. Further, several species of frogs in Africa, including endemic species (Conraua sp, Trichobatrachus sp. and Astylosternus sp.) are mainly harvested from wild used for local consumption and local trading; however, assessments of the value chains are poor, especially Central Africa regions (See section 3.3.3.3.3).

(xvii) Knowledge gap on global scale of sustainable use of wild species among indigenous people and local communities. The importance of wild species that contribute to livelihood strategies, in particular for indigenous people and local communities is well recognized. However, little information exist in the available global indicator sets to comprehensively quantify the spatial and temporal scales of sustainable use of wild species occurring specifically in indigenous people and local communities across the globe; and the United Nations are aware of this gap.

(xviii) Knowledge gap on quality assurance, safety and efficacy to assess traditional medicine. Wild species have been used in traditional medicinal practice for millennia. To control quality and to ensure safety and efficacy in production of traditional medicines is difficult. The World Health Organization has produced a series of technical documents in this field, including publications on good agricultural and collection practices and good manufacturing practices, along with other technical support, to assist with standardization and creation of high-quality products ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2l5b5rb4sp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(WHO, 2013)","plainCitation":"(WHO, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34663,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FF8XUCL9"],"itemData":{"id":34663,"type":"book","call-number":"R733 .W4882 2013","event-place":"Geneva","ISBN":"978-92-4-150609-0","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn877906161","number-of-pages":"76","publisher":"World Health Organization","publisher-place":"Geneva","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"WHO traditional medicine strategy. 2014-2023","editor":[{"family":"WHO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The World Health Organization urges member states to cooperate with each other and to share knowledge while working to strengthen communication between conventional and traditional practitioners. Evaluation of quality, safety and efficacy based on research is needed to improve approaches to assessment of traditional medicines, a situation made difficult to remedy in light of historically inadequate public and private funding to address this growing concern ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1id2cc2e1d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(WHO, 2013)","plainCitation":"(WHO, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34663,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FF8XUCL9"],"itemData":{"id":34663,"type":"book","call-number":"R733 .W4882 2013","event-place":"Geneva","ISBN":"978-92-4-150609-0","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn877906161","number-of-pages":"76","publisher":"World Health Organization","publisher-place":"Geneva","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"WHO traditional medicine strategy. 2014-2023","editor":[{"family":"WHO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

(xix) Insufficient bridging of indigenous and science-based knowledge. The incorporation of multiple types of knowledge (e.g., science, indigenous knowledge, traditional ecological knowledge) is especially critical for the sustainable use of wild species, which can strengthen the evidence-base for policy advice, decision making, and environmental management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1lobbm9snh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Hill, 2017)","plainCitation":"(R. Hill, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31611,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P4IL2W5T"],"itemData":{"id":31611,"type":"article-journal","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2016.12.005","page":"17–25","title":"ScienceDirect Weaving knowledge systems in IPBES , CBD and beyond — lessons learned for sustainability","author":[{"family":"Hill","given":"Rosemary"},{"family":"Malmer","given":"Pernilla"},{"family":"Tengo","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Raymond","given":"Christopher M"},{"family":"Spierenburg","given":"Marja"},{"family":"Danielsen","given":"Finn"},{"family":"Elmqvist","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While the benefits of incorporating multiple types of knowledge in environmental research and management are many, successfully doing so has remained a challenge. In response there have been a number of recent reviews that have sought to better understand the bridging of indigenous, local, and science-based knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2688iv1m1e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barron, 2015; Berkes, 2010; Berkes & Berkes, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Barron, 2015; Berkes, 2010; Berkes & Berkes, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31706,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8YGJF47E"],"itemData":{"id":31706,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Progress in Physical Geography","issue":"5","page":"640–660","title":"Names matter: Interdisciplinary research on taxonomy and nomenclature for ecosystem management","URL":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/0309133315589706","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Barron","given":"Elizabeth S"},{"family":"Sthultz","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Pringle","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":31607,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NYLJUE5T"],"itemData":{"id":31607,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"How can the governance of environment and resources be devolved in a way that incorporates effective user participation and feedback learning? Approaches that use the idea of adaptive management or learning-by-doing, combined with co-management, are particularly promising. Using an interdisciplinary literature covering many types of resources, and a conceptual model with three phases (communicative action, self-organization and collective action), the paper identi?es some of the major processes leading to adaptive co-management. These include deliberation, visioning, building social capital, trust and institutions, capacity-building through networks and partnerships, and action-re?ection-action loops for social learning. Such adaptive co-management is not simply a theoretical possibility but something that has been documented in a number of forestry, ?sheries, wildlife, protected area, and wetland cases from both developed and developing countries. However, the experience with the decentralization reforms of the 1990s is largely negative for a number of reasons. Effective devolution takes time, requiring a shift in focus from a static concept of management to a dynamic concept of governance shaped by interactions, feedback learning and adaptation over time. Sharing of governance responsibilities and an ability to learn from experience are among the emerging trends in environmental management.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S037689291000072X","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Conserv.","language":"en","page":"489-500","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Devolution of environment and resources governance: trends and future","title-short":"Devolution of environment and resources governance","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S037689291000072X/type/journal_article","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",12]]}}},{"id":31601,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UER4R9PN"],"itemData":{"id":31601,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Some indigenous knowledge is said to be holistic in the way it deals with the environment. Given the dif?culties of Western science with complex environmental problems, any insights from the holism of indigenous knowledge are of major interest. Based on examples from Inuit and other northern peoples, it appears that indigenous knowledge approaches complex systems by using simple prescriptions consistent with fuzzy logic. Speci?cally, indigenous knowledge pursues holism through the continued reading of the environment, collection of large amounts of information, and the construction of collective mental models that can adjust to new information. Such an approach serves the assessment of a large number of variables qualitatively, as opposed to focusing on a small number of variables quantitatively.","container-title":"Futures","DOI":"10.1016/j.futures.2008.07.003","ISSN":"00163287","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Futures","language":"en","page":"6-12","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ecological complexity, fuzzy logic, and holism in indigenous knowledge","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0016328708001092","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Mina Kislalioglu"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Yet there continues to be a need for methods, models, and approaches for integrative work ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1c6oa4tb5e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barron, 2020; R. Hill, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Barron, 2020; R. Hill, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31623,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M8PV6QS4"],"itemData":{"id":31623,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Local Environment","DOI":"10.1080/13549839.2020.1768518","ISSN":"1354-9839, 1469-6711","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Local Environment","language":"en","page":"447-462","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"From place to emplacement: the scalar politics of sustainability","title-short":"From place to emplacement","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13549839.2020.1768518","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Barron","given":"Elizabeth S."},{"family":"Hartman","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Hagemann","given":"Frederik"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,2]]}}},{"id":31611,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P4IL2W5T"],"itemData":{"id":31611,"type":"article-journal","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2016.12.005","page":"17–25","title":"ScienceDirect Weaving knowledge systems in IPBES , CBD and beyond — lessons learned for sustainability","author":[{"family":"Hill","given":"Rosemary"},{"family":"Malmer","given":"Pernilla"},{"family":"Tengo","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Raymond","given":"Christopher M"},{"family":"Spierenburg","given":"Marja"},{"family":"Danielsen","given":"Finn"},{"family":"Elmqvist","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This approach seeks to examine the extent, range, and nature of the published literature (i.e., peer-reviewed and grey) that integrates and/or includes indigenous, local, and science-based knowledge in sustainable use of wild species research, monitoring, or management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a6n37um4eu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Provencher, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Provencher, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1597,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PJ86XPL3","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IMTW4KLR"],"itemData":{"id":1597,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Background:? The incorporation of multiple types of knowledge (e.g., science, Indigenous knowledge, traditional ecological knowledge) is an important undertaking, which can strengthen the evidence-base for policy advice, decision making, and environmental management. While the benefits of incorporating multiple types of knowledge in environmental research and management are many, successfully doing so has remained a challenge. In response there has been a number of recent reviews that have sought to better understand the what and how, when it comes to bridging Indigenous and science-based knowledge. Yet there continues to be a need for methods, models, and approaches for integrative work. This systematic map seeks to examine the extent, range, and nature of the published literature (i.e., peer-reviewed and grey) that integrates and/or includes Indigenous and science-based knowledge in coastal-marine research, monitoring, or management in Canada. Results from this study can be used to inform new and ongoing research and monitoring efforts and highlight evidence gaps. Methods:? The systematic map will aim to capture all available studies relevant to the question found in the peerreviewed and grey literature. Accordingly, the search will leverage four databases focused on peer reviewed publications, carefully selected specialist websites, and two web-based search engines. Reference sections of relevant review articles will also be cross-checked to identify articles that were not found using the search strategy. All searches will be conducted in English. Search results will be reviewed in two stages: (1) title and abstract; and (2) full text. All screening decisions will be included in the database. The systematic map will employ a narrative synthesis approach that will include the use of descriptive statistics, tables (including SM database), and figures (including map with the studies geospatially referenced). In addition, an online version of the map and queryable database will be developed similar to other knowledge mobilization tools.","container-title":"Environmental Evidence","DOI":"10.1186/s13750-019-0159-1","ISSN":"2047-2382","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Environ Evid","language":"en","page":"15","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Bridging Indigenous and science-based knowledge in coastal-marine research, monitoring, and management in Canada: a systematic map protocol","title-short":"Bridging Indigenous and science-based knowledge in coastal-marine research, monitoring, and management in Canada","URL":"https://environmentalevidencejournal.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13750-019-0159-1","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Provencher","given":"Jennifer F."},{"family":"Henri","given":"Dominique A."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Jessica J."},{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."},{"family":"Alexander","given":"M."},{"family":"Provencher","given":"F. Provencher"},{"family":"Dominique","given":"A. Henri"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      There is no solid mechanism developed for knowledge transfer from indigenous communities to scientific communities and vice versa, and as discussed in section 3.3.2, in many cases attempts to do so have led to issues of intellectual property and biopiracy ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a29qap2osq0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barron, 2015; Berkes & Berkes, 2009; S. Devkota, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Barron, 2015; Berkes & Berkes, 2009; S. Devkota, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31706,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8YGJF47E"],"itemData":{"id":31706,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Progress in Physical Geography","issue":"5","page":"640–660","title":"Names matter: Interdisciplinary research on taxonomy and nomenclature for ecosystem management","URL":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/0309133315589706","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Barron","given":"Elizabeth S"},{"family":"Sthultz","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Pringle","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":31601,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UER4R9PN"],"itemData":{"id":31601,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Some indigenous knowledge is said to be holistic in the way it deals with the environment. Given the dif?culties of Western science with complex environmental problems, any insights from the holism of indigenous knowledge are of major interest. Based on examples from Inuit and other northern peoples, it appears that indigenous knowledge approaches complex systems by using simple prescriptions consistent with fuzzy logic. Speci?cally, indigenous knowledge pursues holism through the continued reading of the environment, collection of large amounts of information, and the construction of collective mental models that can adjust to new information. Such an approach serves the assessment of a large number of variables qualitatively, as opposed to focusing on a small number of variables quantitatively.","container-title":"Futures","DOI":"10.1016/j.futures.2008.07.003","ISSN":"00163287","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Futures","language":"en","page":"6-12","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ecological complexity, fuzzy logic, and holism in indigenous knowledge","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0016328708001092","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Mina Kislalioglu"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",2]]}}},{"id":32889,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BT48RECB"],"itemData":{"id":32889,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Cordyceps sinensis (Berk.) Sacc., a well-known and valued traditional medicine, is also called winter worm summer grass. The product deserves high potential to generate income opportunities, enhance rural income and raise the national revenue. The study of C. sinensis was carried out in Dolpa district, Western Nepal to document ethnomycological uses and local practices. Data were collected using open ended questionnaires administered to collectors/users (n = 74), traders (n = 25) and local healers or amchis (n = 3). Indigenous peoples are utilizing this Himalayan treasure for the treatment of different diseases like diarrhea, headache, cough, rheumatism, liver disease, and also as an aphrodisiac and tonic. Internationally it is regarded as Himalayan Viagra. The present study therefore aims at highlighting the ethnomycological uses of C. sinensis resource of the Trans-Himalayan region in their niche.","container-title":"Our Nature","DOI":"10.3126/on.v4i1.502","ISSN":"2091-2781, 1991-2951","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Our Nature","language":"en","page":"48-52","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Yarsagumba [<i>Cordyceps sinensis</i> (Berk.) Sacc.]; Traditional Utilization in Dolpa District, Western Nepal","URL":"https://www.nepjol.info/index.php/ON/article/view/502","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Devkota","given":"S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wild species are being used as a rich source of medicine because they produce a host of bioactive molecules, most of which probably evolved as chemical defenses against predation or infection. Wild plant species are chosen for pharmaceutical studies through different methods, one of the methods include ethnobotanical approach, i.e., indigenous uses of plant species based on indigenous and local knowledge that can offer strong clues to the biological activities of those plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2e78a7d0dt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Cox & Balick, 1994)","plainCitation":"(P. Cox & Balick, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31587,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GBJNSZBN"],"itemData":{"id":31587,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific American","issue":"6","page":"82-87","title":"The Ethnobotanical Approach to Drug Discovery","volume":"270","author":[{"family":"Cox","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Balick","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are well-established drugs that were developed after scientists began to analyze the chemical constituents of plants used by indigenous peoples and local communities for medicinal or other biological effects.
      
