Criteria and indicators translate concepts and ideas about sustainability into factors that can then be measured and monitored ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GGlVurLl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Linser, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Linser, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34995,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TVKBWDMV","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8AR3JJVI"],"itemData":{"id":34995,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The use of criteria and indicators (C & I) for data collection, monitoring, assessing and reporting on sustainable forest management (SFM) has been growing since the Earth Summit in 1992, supported by eleven intergovernmental, regional and international forest-related C & I processes. The initial effort led to varying levels of implementation across countries. Several processes never went much beyond the adoption of a first set of C & I while others have made substantial progress. In recent years, interest in C & I for SFM has again increased. In light of the Sustainable Development Goals and emerging global challenges the contribution of C & I to monitor, assess and report on forest conditions and trends is increasingly important. We compare and analyse the structure, activities and progress of the intergovernmental C & I processes. The work is based on document analysis and questionnaires sent to the secretariats of the processes and C & I experts. We found many similarities but also major differences in the structure and content of the C & I sets. The results provide a context for discussing and understanding why some of the C & I processes are successful in their work while others have stalled. Finally, we propose the required ingredients for success for the future activities of the forest-related intergovernmental C & I processes.","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f9090515","ISSN":"19994907","issue":"9","page":"1–23","title":"25 years of criteria and indicators for sustainable forest management: Why some intergovernmental C & I processes flourished while others faded","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Linser","given":"Stefanie"},{"family":"Wolfslehner","given":"Bernhard"},{"family":"Asmar","given":"Fady"},{"family":"Bridge","given":"Simon R.J."},{"family":"Gritten","given":"David"},{"family":"Guadalupe","given":"Vicente"},{"family":"Jafari","given":"Mostafa"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Laclau","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Robertson","given":"Guy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Therefore, the types of indicators used can reflect conceptualizations of sustainable use and of the relative importance placed on different aspects or elements of sustainable use. As ideas, understanding, and societal risk tolerances change about the elements of sustainable use, criteria and indicators are continually updated. For example, criteria and indicators in sustainable forest use standards have changed as perceptions of forests change, with more emphasis on economic and social values in recent versions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3zSa5HZ0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Linser, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Linser, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34995,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TVKBWDMV","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8AR3JJVI"],"itemData":{"id":34995,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The use of criteria and indicators (C & I) for data collection, monitoring, assessing and reporting on sustainable forest management (SFM) has been growing since the Earth Summit in 1992, supported by eleven intergovernmental, regional and international forest-related C & I processes. The initial effort led to varying levels of implementation across countries. Several processes never went much beyond the adoption of a first set of C & I while others have made substantial progress. In recent years, interest in C & I for SFM has again increased. In light of the Sustainable Development Goals and emerging global challenges the contribution of C & I to monitor, assess and report on forest conditions and trends is increasingly important. We compare and analyse the structure, activities and progress of the intergovernmental C & I processes. The work is based on document analysis and questionnaires sent to the secretariats of the processes and C & I experts. We found many similarities but also major differences in the structure and content of the C & I sets. The results provide a context for discussing and understanding why some of the C & I processes are successful in their work while others have stalled. Finally, we propose the required ingredients for success for the future activities of the forest-related intergovernmental C & I processes.","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f9090515","ISSN":"19994907","issue":"9","page":"1–23","title":"25 years of criteria and indicators for sustainable forest management: Why some intergovernmental C & I processes flourished while others faded","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Linser","given":"Stefanie"},{"family":"Wolfslehner","given":"Bernhard"},{"family":"Asmar","given":"Fady"},{"family":"Bridge","given":"Simon R.J."},{"family":"Gritten","given":"David"},{"family":"Guadalupe","given":"Vicente"},{"family":"Jafari","given":"Mostafa"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Laclau","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Robertson","given":"Guy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Because indicator sets may influence the development of policies on sustainable use of wild resources, differences in the conception, measuring and monitoring of indicators may translate into differences in policies with potentially different outcomes for nature and people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LVRe9reT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Linser, 2018; Sterling, Filardi,, 2017; Sterling, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Linser, 2018; Sterling, Filardi,, 2017; Sterling, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34995,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TVKBWDMV","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8AR3JJVI"],"itemData":{"id":34995,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The use of criteria and indicators (C & I) for data collection, monitoring, assessing and reporting on sustainable forest management (SFM) has been growing since the Earth Summit in 1992, supported by eleven intergovernmental, regional and international forest-related C & I processes. The initial effort led to varying levels of implementation across countries. Several processes never went much beyond the adoption of a first set of C & I while others have made substantial progress. In recent years, interest in C & I for SFM has again increased. In light of the Sustainable Development Goals and emerging global challenges the contribution of C & I to monitor, assess and report on forest conditions and trends is increasingly important. We compare and analyse the structure, activities and progress of the intergovernmental C & I processes. The work is based on document analysis and questionnaires sent to the secretariats of the processes and C & I experts. We found many similarities but also major differences in the structure and content of the C & I sets. The results provide a context for discussing and understanding why some of the C & I processes are successful in their work while others have stalled. Finally, we propose the required ingredients for success for the future activities of the forest-related intergovernmental C & I processes.","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f9090515","ISSN":"19994907","issue":"9","page":"1–23","title":"25 years of criteria and indicators for sustainable forest management: Why some intergovernmental C & I processes flourished while others faded","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Linser","given":"Stefanie"},{"family":"Wolfslehner","given":"Bernhard"},{"family":"Asmar","given":"Fady"},{"family":"Bridge","given":"Simon R.J."},{"family":"Gritten","given":"David"},{"family":"Guadalupe","given":"Vicente"},{"family":"Jafari","given":"Mostafa"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Laclau","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Robertson","given":"Guy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":30472,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IKRSUBB3"],"itemData":{"id":30472,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Achieving sustainable development globally requires multilevel and interdisciplinary efforts and perspectives. Global goals shape priorities and actions at multiple scales, creating cascading impacts realized at the local level through the direction of financial resources and implementation of programs intended to achieve progress towards these metrics. We explore ways to localize global goals to best support human well-being and environmental health by systematically comparing the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) with regionally-derived well-being dimensions that encompass components of social–ecological resilience across the Pacific Islands. Our research shows that, in the context of the Pacific, there are overlaps but also significant gaps between regional conceptions of well-being and the globally-derived SDGs. Some dimensions, related to human health and access to infrastructure and finances, are well represented in the SDGs. Other dimensions of high importance when localizing perspectives of well-being, such as those regarding connections between and across people and place and Indigenous and local knowledge, are not. Furthermore, internationally generated indicators may result in trade-offs and measurement challenges in local contexts. Creating space for place-based values in global sustainability planning aligns with international calls for transformational changes needed to achieve global goals. We identify challenges in applying SDG indicators at the local level and provide lessons learned to foster equitable and holistic approaches and outcomes for sustainability.","container-title":"Sustainability Science","DOI":"10.1007/s11625-020-00822-w","ISSN":"1862-4065, 1862-4057","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Sustain Sci","language":"en","page":"1129-1147","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Creating a space for place and multidimensional well-being: lessons learned from localizing the SDGs","title-short":"Creating a space for place and multidimensional well-being","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11625-020-00822-w","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor J."},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua?ala"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Mandle","given":"Lisa"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Aini","given":"John"},{"family":"Albert","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Caselle","given":"Jennifer E."},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Samantha H."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Darling","given":"Emily S."},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."},{"family":"Mawyer","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Mejia","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Morishige","given":"Kanoe?ulalani"},{"family":"Nainoca","given":"Winifereti"},{"family":"Parks","given":"John"},{"family":"Tanguay","given":"Jamie"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Vave","given":"Ron"},{"family":"Wase","given":"Veronica"},{"family":"Wongbusarakum","given":"Supin"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7]]}}},{"id":30422,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3FWUINXT"],"itemData":{"id":30422,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"https://doi-org.ezproxy.uvm.edu/10.1038/s41559-017-0349-6","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Ecol Evol","page":"1798–1806","title":"Biocultural approaches to well-being and sustainability indicators across scales","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor J."},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Toomey","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Newell","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Albert","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Alvira","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Bergamini","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Blair","given":"Mary"},{"family":"Boseto","given":"David"},{"family":"Burrows","given":"Kate"},{"family":"Bynum","given":"Nora"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Casselle","given":"Jennifer E."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Cullman","given":"Georgina"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Eyzaguirre","given":"Pablo B."},{"family":"Gray","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Herrera","given":"James"},{"family":"Kenilorea","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Kinney","given":"Kealohanuiopuna"},{"family":"Kurashima","given":"Natalie"},{"family":"Macey","given":"Suzanne"},{"family":"Malone","given":"Cynthia"},{"family":"Mauli","given":"Senoveva"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"},{"family":"McMillen","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua'ala"},{"family":"Pikacha","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Porzecanski","given":"Ana L."},{"family":"Robert","given":"Pascale","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Salpeteur","given":"Matthieu"},{"family":"Siricolo","given":"Myknee"},{"family":"Stege","given":"Mark H."},{"family":"Stege","given":"Kristina"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Vave","given":"Ron"},{"family":"Wali","given":"Alaka"},{"family":"West","given":"Paige"},{"family":"Winter","given":"Kawika B."},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The use of criteria and indicators can be expressed in policies for sustainable use in multiple ways, including as reporting tools for description and diagnosis; as a means of providing a framework for policy making or to identify enabling conditions, including financial and technical resources, to implement management; as a reference framework for the development of policies; and as assessment tools for evaluating the effectiveness of programs and measures ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hH39pu9b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Linser, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Linser, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34995,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TVKBWDMV","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8AR3JJVI"],"itemData":{"id":34995,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The use of criteria and indicators (C & I) for data collection, monitoring, assessing and reporting on sustainable forest management (SFM) has been growing since the Earth Summit in 1992, supported by eleven intergovernmental, regional and international forest-related C & I processes. The initial effort led to varying levels of implementation across countries. Several processes never went much beyond the adoption of a first set of C & I while others have made substantial progress. In recent years, interest in C & I for SFM has again increased. In light of the Sustainable Development Goals and emerging global challenges the contribution of C & I to monitor, assess and report on forest conditions and trends is increasingly important. We compare and analyse the structure, activities and progress of the intergovernmental C & I processes. The work is based on document analysis and questionnaires sent to the secretariats of the processes and C & I experts. We found many similarities but also major differences in the structure and content of the C & I sets. The results provide a context for discussing and understanding why some of the C & I processes are successful in their work while others have stalled. Finally, we propose the required ingredients for success for the future activities of the forest-related intergovernmental C & I processes.","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f9090515","ISSN":"19994907","issue":"9","page":"1–23","title":"25 years of criteria and indicators for sustainable forest management: Why some intergovernmental C & I processes flourished while others faded","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Linser","given":"Stefanie"},{"family":"Wolfslehner","given":"Bernhard"},{"family":"Asmar","given":"Fady"},{"family":"Bridge","given":"Simon R.J."},{"family":"Gritten","given":"David"},{"family":"Guadalupe","given":"Vicente"},{"family":"Jafari","given":"Mostafa"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Laclau","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Robertson","given":"Guy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
     This section examines how sustainable use is measured and monitored, with a focus on indicators used across practices and scales, from global to local. First, a review of indicator choice is presented. Then, given the relevance of the Sustainable Development Goals commitments to the future dialogue on policy and progress for sustainable use of wild species, this is followed by an evaluation of the relevance of each indicator to the sustainable use of wild species. To identify how conceptualizations of sustainable use of wild species are reflected in approaches to measure and monitor use, a review of global indicator sets and indicators in indigenous peoples and local communities are presented. Finally, a crosswalk of the academic literature, global principles and policies, and indigenous peoples and local communities’ conceptualizations was carried out with the indicators, to identify which ideas about sustainable use are captured in commonly used metrics of sustainable use and which are poorly represented. 
     
2.3.1 Indicator choice and interpretation for assessing sustainable use of wild species
2.3.1.1 Context and literature review of criteria used in indicator selection
Indicators are important to contemporary governance processes. They can serve functions as diverse but vital as expert assessments of status and trends of components of the natural world, their uses, and well-being of people; informing decision-making processes with regard to needs for actions and effectiveness of measures or programs in place, and facilitating communication among experts, decision-makers, stakeholders, rights-holders, civil society and media ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pdN4y2mu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lakhani, 2005; Lyytim\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}ki, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Lakhani, 2005; Lyytimäki, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30418,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DJTQTS58"],"itemData":{"id":30418,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Sustainable Development and World Ecology","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"385–393","title":"The use, non-use and misuse of indicators in ustainability assessment and communication","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Lyytimäki","given":"J."},{"family":"Tapio","given":"P."},{"family":"Varho","given":"V."},{"family":"Söderman","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":30589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EFRB9UZE"],"itemData":{"id":30589,"type":"book","event-place":"London","publisher":"London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine","publisher-place":"London","title":"Compendium of clinical and health indicators user guide","author":[{"family":"Lakhani","given":"A."},{"family":"Olearnik","given":"H."},{"family":"Eayres","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. All of these functions can be important to sustainable use of wild species, individually or in combination. 
      Even for single aspects of biodiversity or human well-being, a single indicator rarely serves all of these functions robustly, so use of suites of multiple indicators is common, with different members of the suite having different strengths and vulnerabilities. Correspondingly, reviews have found thousands of indicators have been proposed, and the number has more than doubled between reviews by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TqBNMX25","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gudmundsson, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Gudmundsson, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30595,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LW8S9T5D"],"itemData":{"id":30595,"type":"chapter","collection-number":"R282","collection-title":"Les collections de l'INRETS","container-title":"Indicators of environmental sustainability in transport: an interdisciplinary approch to methods","event-place":"Bron","ISBN":"978-2-85782-684-2","language":"en","page":"426","publisher":"INRETS","publisher-place":"Bron","title":"Criteria and methods for indicator assessment and validation","URL":"https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-00492823/fr/","author":[{"family":"Gudmundsson","given":"H."},{"family":"Tennøy","given":"A."},{"family":"Joumard","given":"R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"})ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KKH8XCkJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gudmundsson, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Gudmundsson, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30595,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LW8S9T5D"],"itemData":{"id":30595,"type":"chapter","collection-number":"R282","collection-title":"Les collections de l'INRETS","container-title":"Indicators of environmental sustainability in transport: an interdisciplinary approch to methods","event-place":"Bron","ISBN":"978-2-85782-684-2","language":"en","page":"426","publisher":"INRETS","publisher-place":"Bron","title":"Criteria and methods for indicator assessment and validation","URL":"https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-00492823/fr/","author":[{"family":"Gudmundsson","given":"H."},{"family":"Tennøy","given":"A."},{"family":"Joumard","given":"R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ew6qrkoU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pires, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Pires, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30594,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L3AIAJM4"],"itemData":{"id":30594,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1007/s10668-019-00508-z","ISSN":"1387-585X, 1573-2975","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Environ Dev Sustain","language":"en","page":"6671-6689","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesizing and standardizing criteria for the evaluation of sustainability indicators in the water sector","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10668-019-00508-z","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Pires","given":"A."},{"family":"Morato","given":"J."},{"family":"Peixoto","given":"H."},{"family":"Bradley","given":"S."},{"family":"Muller","given":"A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). This has resulted in a proliferation of not only indicators, but even criteria and processes for selecting appropriate suites of indicators. 
      The recent review of ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0CJaqxFD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pires, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Pires, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30594,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L3AIAJM4"],"itemData":{"id":30594,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1007/s10668-019-00508-z","ISSN":"1387-585X, 1573-2975","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Environ Dev Sustain","language":"en","page":"6671-6689","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesizing and standardizing criteria for the evaluation of sustainability indicators in the water sector","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10668-019-00508-z","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Pires","given":"A."},{"family":"Morato","given":"J."},{"family":"Peixoto","given":"H."},{"family":"Bradley","given":"S."},{"family":"Muller","given":"A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found that approximately 350 criteria have been advocated in various expert applications, and even after overlaps and redundancies among criteria were taken into account and removed, 60 different criteria for selecting indicators were identified. This demonstrates that choices are necessary in selecting even the criteria and standards for choosing indicators. Using more selection criteria may increase the quality of the assessment by allowing multiple perspectives on sustainability to be accommodated ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XBmZoRm3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Niemeijer & Groot, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Niemeijer & Groot, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30417,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ABN68BUI"],"itemData":{"id":30417,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoli","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"14–25","title":"A conceptual framework for selecting environmental indicator sets","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Niemeijer","given":"D."},{"family":"Groot","given":"R.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, as the number of selection criteria increases the complexity and cost of even choosing the indicators, let alone using them in an assessment, also increases. 
      The findings of the ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ni1YCDRN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pires, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Pires, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30594,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L3AIAJM4"],"itemData":{"id":30594,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1007/s10668-019-00508-z","ISSN":"1387-585X, 1573-2975","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Environ Dev Sustain","language":"en","page":"6671-6689","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesizing and standardizing criteria for the evaluation of sustainability indicators in the water sector","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10668-019-00508-z","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Pires","given":"A."},{"family":"Morato","given":"J."},{"family":"Peixoto","given":"H."},{"family":"Bradley","given":"S."},{"family":"Muller","given":"A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} review, and earlier ones approaching the problem of indicator selection for sustainable practices from various perspectives (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"evbW0zm8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cloquell-Ballester, 2006; James, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Cloquell-Ballester, 2006; James, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30603,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GC6BP7VU"],"itemData":{"id":30603,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Impact Assessment Review","DOI":"10.1016/j.eiar.2005.06.002.","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"79–105","title":"Indicators validation for the improvement of environmental and social impact quantitative assessment","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Cloquell-Ballester","given":"V.A."},{"family":"Monterde-Díaz","given":"R."},{"family":"Santamarina-Siurana","given":"M.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":30612,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DY8WUTIB"],"itemData":{"id":30612,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s0026","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"703–719","title":"A methodology for evaluating and ranking water quantity indicators in support of ecosystem-based management","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"James","given":"C.A."},{"family":"Kershner","given":"J."},{"family":"O’Neill","given":"S."},{"family":"Levin","given":"P.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} are important for the the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species, where indicators have several roles (see Chapter 1). Interpretation throughout the assessment of both findings from its own summaries of information and findings taken from publications and other sources often are in the form of indicator values and trends, and the indicators need to interpreted with appropriate caution and confidence. 
      Reviews considered typically were consistent with the approach of ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RsJFUd7u","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pires, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Pires, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30594,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L3AIAJM4"],"itemData":{"id":30594,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1007/s10668-019-00508-z","ISSN":"1387-585X, 1573-2975","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Environ Dev Sustain","language":"en","page":"6671-6689","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesizing and standardizing criteria for the evaluation of sustainability indicators in the water sector","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10668-019-00508-z","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Pires","given":"A."},{"family":"Morato","given":"J."},{"family":"Peixoto","given":"H."},{"family":"Bradley","given":"S."},{"family":"Muller","given":"A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, even if they used different terminology in presenting their findings. In fact, as ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"W8vjo6HA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pires, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Pires, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30594,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L3AIAJM4"],"itemData":{"id":30594,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1007/s10668-019-00508-z","ISSN":"1387-585X, 1573-2975","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Environ Dev Sustain","language":"en","page":"6671-6689","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesizing and standardizing criteria for the evaluation of sustainability indicators in the water sector","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10668-019-00508-z","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Pires","given":"A."},{"family":"Morato","given":"J."},{"family":"Peixoto","given":"H."},{"family":"Bradley","given":"S."},{"family":"Muller","given":"A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} note, there is no consensus among experts on the terms to be used for specific properties of indicators or their criteria, so substantial inference is needed to identify similarities of concepts presented in different words. There is also no consensus on the best processes for selecting suites of indicators, among options as diverse as modeling, expert opinion, participatory processes with users and stakeholders, empirical validation with reference data sets, and efforts at mathematical optimization of indicator coverage. Moreover, indicator selection processes can be conducted as top-down or bottom-up exercises, and in highly structured ways, such as formal Delphi methods for consultation, or very informally, seeking broad buy-in of experts, stakeholders, rights-holders, and decision-makers to a final suite of indicators, even though no single perspective may have confidence in all members of the set.  
