2.3.2.3 Status of wildlife watching indicators
Analysis of global sustainability indicators in the context of wildlife watching is challenging due to absence of a global or even regional governance authorities focused on this practice. At the same time, there is proliferation of small and micro level measures, focused on specific species, practice and/or geographic area, aimed to increase sustainability of wildlife watching. These are first and foremost, wildlife watching focused codes of conduct, ecolabelling and certification, briefly discussed below.
      Although codes of conduct in wildlife watching are too numerous for a comprehensive overview, some common patterns can nevertheless be identified. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KEQRVegE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Reis, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Reis, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30610,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MFWSSIF5"],"itemData":{"id":30610,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Advances in Management and Innovation Proceedings, Cardiff School of Management","language":"en","page":"5–7,","title":"United we stand-an approach to consolidating marine wildlife tourism codes of conduct in West Wales","author":[{"family":"Reis","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} identified 22 codes of conduct pertaining to marine wildlife tourism management, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hJOJHCRu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fennell & Yazdanpanah, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Fennell & Yazdanpanah, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30608,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CCVAVRYH"],"itemData":{"id":30608,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of Tourism Research","language":"en","page":"103023","title":"Tourism and wildlife photography codes of ethics: Developing a clearer picture","volume":"85","author":[{"family":"Fennell","given":"D.A."},{"family":"Yazdanpanah","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} identified 32 codes for wildlife photography, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vHv7gYRu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garrod & Fennell, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Garrod & Fennell, 2004)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34357,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/39ACYLH6"],"itemData":{"id":34357,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of Tourism Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.annals.2003.12.003","ISSN":"01607383","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Annals of Tourism Research","language":"en","page":"334-352","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An analysis of whalewatching codes of conduct","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0160738303001385","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Garrod","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Fennell","given":"David A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} talk about 58 codes for whale watching tourism, and ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fMfz2h0a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u214{}qvist, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Öqvist, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30421,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HZ4FTLHW"],"itemData":{"id":30421,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism in Marine Environments","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–15","title":"Seal watching: An investigation of codes of conduct","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Öqvist","given":"E.L."},{"family":"Granquist","given":"S.M."},{"family":"Burns","given":"G.L."},{"family":"Angerbjörn","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} mention 35 seal watching codes. It is emphasized, however, that codes of conduct at least need to be accompanied with educational and training activities to have any tangible impact ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"74SESwgu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Lima & Green, 2017; Garrod & Fennell, 2004; Reis, 2020)","plainCitation":"(de Lima & Green, 2017; Garrod & Fennell, 2004; Reis, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31437,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2TK4XTS4"],"itemData":{"id":31437,"type":"book","collection-title":"Geoheritage, Geoparks and Geotourism book series","note":"publisher: Springer","publisher":"Springer","title":"Wildlife Tourism, Environmental Learning and Ethical Encounters","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-55574-4","editor":[{"family":"Lima","given":"Ismar Borges","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Green","given":"Ronda J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":34357,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/39ACYLH6"],"itemData":{"id":34357,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of Tourism Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.annals.2003.12.003","ISSN":"01607383","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Annals of Tourism Research","language":"en","page":"334-352","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An analysis of whalewatching codes of conduct","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0160738303001385","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Garrod","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Fennell","given":"David A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",4]]}}},{"id":30610,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MFWSSIF5"],"itemData":{"id":30610,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Advances in Management and Innovation Proceedings, Cardiff School of Management","language":"en","page":"5–7,","title":"United we stand-an approach to consolidating marine wildlife tourism codes of conduct in West Wales","author":[{"family":"Reis","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition, even if voluntary, clear links between codes of conduct and wild species legislation and monitoring organizations need to be established, otherwise “the recommendations will sit on a shelf, along with all the others” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RznEJmoa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Reis, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Reis, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30610,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MFWSSIF5"],"itemData":{"id":30610,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Advances in Management and Innovation Proceedings, Cardiff School of Management","language":"en","page":"5–7,","title":"United we stand-an approach to consolidating marine wildlife tourism codes of conduct in West Wales","author":[{"family":"Reis","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Overall, codes of conduct still continue to multiply without any consolidation on regional or global levels, despite calls for internationally recognized codes of conduct and regulatory bodies have been visible in the research literature for decades ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"l6X5KQ1b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Buckley & Pegas, 2013; Fennell & Yazdanpanah, 2020; Garrod & Fennell, 2004; \\\\uc0\\\\u214{}qvist, 2018; Reis, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Buckley & Pegas, 2013; Fennell & Yazdanpanah, 2020; Garrod & Fennell, 2004; Öqvist, 2018; Reis, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30607,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ABGD2X8C"],"itemData":{"id":30607,"type":"chapter","container-title":"A Handbook of Tourism and the Environment","event-place":"London","language":"en","page":"521–530","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London","title":"Tourism and CSR","author":[{"family":"Buckley","given":"R."},{"family":"Pegas","given":"F."}],"editor":[{"family":"Holden","given":"A."},{"family":"Fennell","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":30608,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CCVAVRYH"],"itemData":{"id":30608,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of Tourism Research","language":"en","page":"103023","title":"Tourism and wildlife photography codes of ethics: Developing a clearer picture","volume":"85","author":[{"family":"Fennell","given":"D.A."},{"family":"Yazdanpanah","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":34357,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/39ACYLH6"],"itemData":{"id":34357,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of Tourism Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.annals.2003.12.003","ISSN":"01607383","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Annals of Tourism Research","language":"en","page":"334-352","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An analysis of whalewatching codes of conduct","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0160738303001385","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Garrod","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Fennell","given":"David A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",4]]}}},{"id":30421,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HZ4FTLHW"],"itemData":{"id":30421,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism in Marine Environments","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–15","title":"Seal watching: An investigation of codes of conduct","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Öqvist","given":"E.L."},{"family":"Granquist","given":"S.M."},{"family":"Burns","given":"G.L."},{"family":"Angerbjörn","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":30610,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MFWSSIF5"],"itemData":{"id":30610,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Advances in Management and Innovation Proceedings, Cardiff School of Management","language":"en","page":"5–7,","title":"United we stand-an approach to consolidating marine wildlife tourism codes of conduct in West Wales","author":[{"family":"Reis","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
     Patterns observable with codes of conduct in wildlife watching are also present in ecolabelling and certification. First, there are similarly proliferation of ecolabels and certification schemes with relatively low efficiency and international recognition. According to Ecolabel Index there are currently more than 400 ecolabels globally (www.ecolabelindex.com) of which at least 50 focus on tourism ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IRJRy1oh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dziuba, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Dziuba, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30602,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PIXX488P"],"itemData":{"id":30602,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Comparative Economic","issue":",19(2)","language":"en","page":"112–128","title":"Sustainable development of tourism – EU ecolabel standards illustrated using the example of Poland","author":[{"family":"Dziuba","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and their number keeps growing. In fact, only within the first decade of the 21st century 70 new ecolabels were launched within tourism market ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jNCeCH8W","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bu\\\\uc0\\\\u269{}ar, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Bu?ar, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30606,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5JNUD594"],"itemData":{"id":30606,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism: An International Interdisciplinary Journal","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"365–374","title":"Ecolabelling in tourism: The disconnect between theory and practice","volume":"67","author":[{"family":"Bu?ar","given":"K."},{"family":"Van Rheenen","given":"D."},{"family":"Hendija","given":"Z."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although the exact number is unclear, this situation has been referred to as an “ecolabel jungle”, given the high numbers, diversity and lack of quality regulation of these labels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Mbaa5vh7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bu\\\\uc0\\\\u269{}ar, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Bu?ar, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30606,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5JNUD594"],"itemData":{"id":30606,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism: An International Interdisciplinary Journal","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"365–374","title":"Ecolabelling in tourism: The disconnect between theory and practice","volume":"67","author":[{"family":"Bu?ar","given":"K."},{"family":"Van Rheenen","given":"D."},{"family":"Hendija","given":"Z."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
     A major challenge hindering efficiency of ecolabelling and certification schemes in tourism in general and wildlife watching in particular remains the nature of overwhelming majority of these businesses. As mentioned above, wildlife watching tourism firms are usually not small and medium entrepreneurs but rather micro entrepreneurs. Micro-enterprises often lack resources, knowledge, skills and willingness to engage in formalized sustainability schemes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DNNLqPi2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Margaryan & Stensland, 2017; Tippett, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Margaryan & Stensland, 2017; Tippett, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34212,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JKTAUVMK"],"itemData":{"id":34212,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Cleaner Production","DOI":"10.1016/j.jclepro.2017.06.060","ISSN":"09596526","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Cleaner Production","language":"en","page":"559-567","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Sustainable by nature? The case of (non)adoption of eco-certification among the nature-based tourism companies in Scandinavia","title-short":"Sustainable by nature?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0959652617312301","volume":"162","author":[{"family":"Margaryan","given":"Lusine"},{"family":"Stensland","given":"Stian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",9]]}}},{"id":30419,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4LEKT47C"],"itemData":{"id":30419,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Intterreg report","language":"en","title":"Ecolabelling for tourism enterprises: What, why and how","URL":"https://ntnuopen.ntnu.no/ntnu-xmlui/handle/11250/2684344","author":[{"family":"Tippett","given":"A.W."},{"family":"Yttredal","given":"E.R."},{"family":"Strand","given":"Ø."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This however, does not mean that these businesses are not interested in managing their impacts and pursuing sustainability goals. Quite often the contrary is the case, as many nature-based tourism entrepreneurs are so-called lifestyle entrepreneurs, for whom achieving certain lifestyle goals is prioritized over economic goals and business growth ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"B0bPzdzo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jenkins, 2004; Margaryan, 2020; Margaryan & Stensland, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Jenkins, 2004; Margaryan, 2020; Margaryan & Stensland, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30600,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XATN2R2M"],"itemData":{"id":30600,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of General Management","DOI":"10.1177/030630700402900403","ISSN":"0306-3070, 1759-6106","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of General Management","language":"en","page":"37-57","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A Critique of Conventional CSR Theory: An SME Perspective","title-short":"A Critique of Conventional CSR Theory","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/030630700402900403","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Jenkins","given":"Heledd"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",6]]}}},{"id":30599,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T36PUCSZ"],"itemData":{"id":30599,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Degrowth and Tourism","edition":"1st Edition","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-0-429-32059-0","page":"13","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London","title":"Lifestyle entrepreneurs as agents of degrowth. The case of nature-based tourism businesses in Scandinavia","URL":"https://www.taylorfrancis.com/chapters/edit/10.4324/9780429320590-4/lifestyle-entrepreneurs-agents-degrowth-lusine-margaryan-peter-fredman-stian-stensland","author":[{"family":"Margaryan","given":"L."},{"family":"Fredman","given":"P."},{"family":"Stensland","given":"S."}],"editor":[{"family":"Hall","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Lundmark","given":"L."},{"family":"Jasmine","given":"J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":34212,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JKTAUVMK"],"itemData":{"id":34212,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Cleaner Production","DOI":"10.1016/j.jclepro.2017.06.060","ISSN":"09596526","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Cleaner Production","language":"en","page":"559-567","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Sustainable by nature? The case of (non)adoption of eco-certification among the nature-based tourism companies in Scandinavia","title-short":"Sustainable by nature?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0959652617312301","volume":"162","author":[{"family":"Margaryan","given":"Lusine"},{"family":"Stensland","given":"Stian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Motivations such as “contributing to sustainability”, “educating people about nature” or “using local natural resources” are ranking very high among the motivations to run nature-based tourism business in Sweden and Norway, although adoption of ecolabels remains very low. There is a strong perception that small and micro firms do not need to formalize or legitimize their sustainability efforts, and that ecolabelling and certification schemes favor big players and are a redundant bureaucratic effort overall ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dv6sezO0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Margaryan & Stensland, 2017; Tippett, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Margaryan & Stensland, 2017; Tippett, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34212,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JKTAUVMK"],"itemData":{"id":34212,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Cleaner Production","DOI":"10.1016/j.jclepro.2017.06.060","ISSN":"09596526","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Cleaner Production","language":"en","page":"559-567","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Sustainable by nature? The case of (non)adoption of eco-certification among the nature-based tourism companies in Scandinavia","title-short":"Sustainable by nature?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0959652617312301","volume":"162","author":[{"family":"Margaryan","given":"Lusine"},{"family":"Stensland","given":"Stian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",9]]}}},{"id":30419,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4LEKT47C"],"itemData":{"id":30419,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Intterreg report","language":"en","title":"Ecolabelling for tourism enterprises: What, why and how","URL":"https://ntnuopen.ntnu.no/ntnu-xmlui/handle/11250/2684344","author":[{"family":"Tippett","given":"A.W."},{"family":"Yttredal","given":"E.R."},{"family":"Strand","given":"Ø."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
     Further, similarly to the codes of conduct, the majority of tourist ecolabels focus on local tourism impacts of businesses, leaving the surrounding impacts out of scope, e.g., transportation of tourists, products and other resources to and from the destination. In this context, greenwashing remains a major issue, when an ecolabel is used purely for marketing purposes, without transforming business practices towards sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uWmcUloI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Buckley & Pegas, 2013; Tippett, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Buckley & Pegas, 2013; Tippett, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30607,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ABGD2X8C"],"itemData":{"id":30607,"type":"chapter","container-title":"A Handbook of Tourism and the Environment","event-place":"London","language":"en","page":"521–530","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London","title":"Tourism and CSR","author":[{"family":"Buckley","given":"R."},{"family":"Pegas","given":"F."}],"editor":[{"family":"Holden","given":"A."},{"family":"Fennell","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":30419,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4LEKT47C"],"itemData":{"id":30419,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Intterreg report","language":"en","title":"Ecolabelling for tourism enterprises: What, why and how","URL":"https://ntnuopen.ntnu.no/ntnu-xmlui/handle/11250/2684344","author":[{"family":"Tippett","given":"A.W."},{"family":"Yttredal","given":"E.R."},{"family":"Strand","given":"Ø."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"v9IUAAaw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Buckley, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Buckley, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30598,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FMEFT3NI"],"itemData":{"id":30598,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.tourman.2013.01.004","ISSN":"02615177","journalAbbreviation":"Tourism Management","language":"en","page":"203-209","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Social-benefit certification as a game","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0261517713000083","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Buckley","given":"Ralf"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} claims that ecocertification schemes can be largely understood as a political game between business and civic interests, because contrary to economic logic, the “market” of ecolabels has become neither more mature nor more solidified around the most successful and high-quality labels over time. Consequently, he argues that ecolabels in tourism have not become more useful to consumers, businesses and regulating authorities, although they have currently more relevance and transparency than they had three decades ago ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"syVtKi8u","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Buckley, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Buckley, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30598,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FMEFT3NI"],"itemData":{"id":30598,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.tourman.2013.01.004","ISSN":"02615177","journalAbbreviation":"Tourism Management","language":"en","page":"203-209","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Social-benefit certification as a game","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0261517713000083","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Buckley","given":"Ralf"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
     The quantity of codes of conduct, ecolabels and certification schemes in tourism continues to increase, although the same cannot be said about their quality and efficiency. The theory and practice of ecolabelling and certification in tourism have not yet converged ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VUyjIqGJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bu\\\\uc0\\\\u269{}ar, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Bu?ar, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30606,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5JNUD594"],"itemData":{"id":30606,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism: An International Interdisciplinary Journal","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"365–374","title":"Ecolabelling in tourism: The disconnect between theory and practice","volume":"67","author":[{"family":"Bu?ar","given":"K."},{"family":"Van Rheenen","given":"D."},{"family":"Hendija","given":"Z."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Calls for two-tiered approaches, i.e., combining abstract and general principles with factors specific to certain species and geographical contexts (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aIXqp8bl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fennell & Yazdanpanah, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Fennell & Yazdanpanah, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30608,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CCVAVRYH"],"itemData":{"id":30608,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of Tourism Research","language":"en","page":"103023","title":"Tourism and wildlife photography codes of ethics: Developing a clearer picture","volume":"85","author":[{"family":"Fennell","given":"D.A."},{"family":"Yazdanpanah","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} as well as strengthening global wild species governance in general ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7SwASdxm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Decker, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Decker, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1317,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4R75QVGM"],"itemData":{"id":1317,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Wildlife Society Bulletin","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"821–826","title":"Wildlife governance in the 21st century—Will sustainable use endure?","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Decker","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Organ","given":"J.F."},{"family":"Forstchen","given":"A.B."},{"family":"Jacobson","given":"C.A."},{"family":"Siemer","given":"W.F."},{"family":"Smith","given":"C.A."},{"family":"Schiavone","given":"M.V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} have begun to appear in the literature. 
2.3.3 Indicators of sustainable use of wild species among indigenous peoples and local communities 

The above sections focus on indicators of sustainable use of wild species used at global and regional scales. There is also a diversity of indicators of sustainable use of wild species used at national and local scales. Indigenous peoples and local communities in particular have long used indicators to effectively measure and monitor the status of wild species (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RrCZjRes","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2017; Lyver, 2017b; Parlee, 2005a; E. J. Sterling, Filardi,, 2017; E. J. Sterling, 2020; K.-L. Thompson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2017; Lyver, 2017b; Parlee, 2005a; E. J. Sterling, Filardi,, 2017; E. J. Sterling, 2020; K.-L. Thompson, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30604,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TMW3XSC3"],"itemData":{"id":30604,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Routledge","title":"Sacred ecology","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":34994,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X88VIJET"],"itemData":{"id":34994,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The underlying ethos of ‘nature’s benefits’ contributing to human wellbeing provides a common platform for understanding the function and value of biodiversity for stakeholders. Diverse societal worldviews however create differences in the way cultures relate to and understand the environment. The objective of this study was to identify community-based indicators and metrics used by M?ori in New Zealand to monitor forest health and community wellbeing. Eighty semi-directed interviews were conducted with 55 forest users within the Tuawhenua tribal group to identify forest health indicators and associated gradient of metrics to assess each indicator. Indicators were grouped within nine culturally-relevant themes: (1) food procurement (mahinga kai), (2) natural productivity (hua o te whenua), (3) nature of water (?hua o te wai), (4) nature of the land (?hua o te whenua), (5) nature of the forest (?hua o te ngahere), (6) perpetual occupation of land and place (ahikaaroa), (7) spiritual dimension (taha wairua), (8) physical health (taha kikokiko), and (9) mental health (taha hinengaro). Within these themes, indicators and associated metrics were aligned within two monitoring approaches: field survey and interview-based. Community members (n = 35 individuals) were asked to prioritise field survey indicators using a seven point Likert Scale of importance. A second survey was also conducted with Tuawhenua elders (n = 43 individuals) to determine changes in the frequency of forest use by the community. A decline in the proportion of the community venturing into the forest over the last 60 years for activities such as hunting, fishing, camping, and collecting plant resources was reported. This decline in regular forest use suggests a field survey approach would be an effective method for applying community-based indicators and to gain an understanding of forest health. Forest indicators that are evaluated over a longer timeframe (months, seasons or even years), or those indicators aligned with community wellbeing, would be better evaluated using an interview-based approach. The alignment of some community-based indicators with scientific-based measures would enrich and deepen knowledge about the state of biodiversity, broaden the relevance of monitoring and reporting within indigenous communities, and help to mitigate issues of ‘shifting baselines’.","call-number":"Esub43","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-016-1142-6","ISSN":"0960-3115, 1572-9710","issue":"13","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"3183-3212","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An indigenous community-based monitoring system for assessing forest health in New Zealand","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10531-016-1142-6","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Lyver","given":"P. O’. B."},{"family":"Timoti","given":"P."},{"family":"Jones","given":"C. J."},{"family":"Richardson","given":"S. J."},{"family":"Tahi","given":"B. L."},{"family":"Greenhalgh","given":"S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}},{"id":34990,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8232FSVJ"],"itemData":{"id":34990,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"26–37","title":"Using Traditional Knowledge to Adapt to Ecological Change: Denésôliné Monitoring of Caribou Movements","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"B."},{"family":"Manseau","given":"M."},{"family":"Dene","given":"Å.K.É."},{"family":"Nation","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":30472,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IKRSUBB3"],"itemData":{"id":30472,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Achieving sustainable development globally requires multilevel and interdisciplinary efforts and perspectives. Global goals shape priorities and actions at multiple scales, creating cascading impacts realized at the local level through the direction of financial resources and implementation of programs intended to achieve progress towards these metrics. We explore ways to localize global goals to best support human well-being and environmental health by systematically comparing the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) with regionally-derived well-being dimensions that encompass components of social–ecological resilience across the Pacific Islands. Our research shows that, in the context of the Pacific, there are overlaps but also significant gaps between regional conceptions of well-being and the globally-derived SDGs. Some dimensions, related to human health and access to infrastructure and finances, are well represented in the SDGs. Other dimensions of high importance when localizing perspectives of well-being, such as those regarding connections between and across people and place and Indigenous and local knowledge, are not. Furthermore, internationally generated indicators may result in trade-offs and measurement challenges in local contexts. Creating space for place-based values in global sustainability planning aligns with international calls for transformational changes needed to achieve global goals. We identify challenges in applying SDG indicators at the local level and provide lessons learned to foster equitable and holistic approaches and outcomes for sustainability.","container-title":"Sustainability Science","DOI":"10.1007/s11625-020-00822-w","ISSN":"1862-4065, 1862-4057","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Sustain Sci","language":"en","page":"1129-1147","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Creating a space for place and multidimensional well-being: lessons learned from localizing the SDGs","title-short":"Creating a space for place and multidimensional well-being","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11625-020-00822-w","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor J."},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua?ala"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Mandle","given":"Lisa"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Aini","given":"John"},{"family":"Albert","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Caselle","given":"Jennifer E."},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Samantha H."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Darling","given":"Emily S."},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."},{"family":"Mawyer","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Mejia","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Morishige","given":"Kanoe?ulalani"},{"family":"Nainoca","given":"Winifereti"},{"family":"Parks","given":"John"},{"family":"Tanguay","given":"Jamie"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Vave","given":"Ron"},{"family":"Wase","given":"Veronica"},{"family":"Wongbusarakum","given":"Supin"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7]]}}},{"id":30422,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3FWUINXT"],"itemData":{"id":30422,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"https://doi-org.ezproxy.uvm.edu/10.1038/s41559-017-0349-6","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Ecol Evol","page":"1798–1806","title":"Biocultural approaches to well-being and sustainability indicators across scales","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor J."},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Toomey","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Newell","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Albert","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Alvira","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Bergamini","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Blair","given":"Mary"},{"family":"Boseto","given":"David"},{"family":"Burrows","given":"Kate"},{"family":"Bynum","given":"Nora"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Casselle","given":"Jennifer E."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Cullman","given":"Georgina"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Eyzaguirre","given":"Pablo B."},{"family":"Gray","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Herrera","given":"James"},{"family":"Kenilorea","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Kinney","given":"Kealohanuiopuna"},{"family":"Kurashima","given":"Natalie"},{"family":"Macey","given":"Suzanne"},{"family":"Malone","given":"Cynthia"},{"family":"Mauli","given":"Senoveva"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"},{"family":"McMillen","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua'ala"},{"family":"Pikacha","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Porzecanski","given":"Ana L."},{"family":"Robert","given":"Pascale","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Salpeteur","given":"Matthieu"},{"family":"Siricolo","given":"Myknee"},{"family":"Stege","given":"Mark H."},{"family":"Stege","given":"Kristina"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Vave","given":"Ron"},{"family":"Wali","given":"Alaka"},{"family":"West","given":"Paige"},{"family":"Winter","given":"Kawika B."},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":30592,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B8GZIVKX"],"itemData":{"id":30592,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Researchers and government agencies are increasingly embracing Indigenous knowledge to inform ecological monitoring. However, there are few detailed accounts of designing monitoring methods based in Indigenous knowledge to meet Indigenous objectives. This research details the design of a program initiated by the Gitga’at First Nation to document the knowledge and observations of their harvesters as a contemporary monitoring initiative. We, Gitga’at and academic researchers, first conducted informal interviews with knowledge holders to gauge interest and to establish community objectives. We then convened community meetings and workshops to design methods to document harvesters’ knowledge and observations. We tested and revised these methods (a post-harvest season interview guide, and a logbook to be completed by harvesters) over the course of two harvest seasons. Semi-structured interviews were more successful than the logbooks in meeting multiple community monitoring objectives. However, we were encouraged by younger participants’ suggestions to develop a digital app based on the logbook to encourage future participation. Our work can serve as a guide to other Indigenous peoples and collaborators who wish to leverage the knowledge of their land and (or) sea users, and the methods we develop are available to adapt to other cultural, social-ecological, and political contexts.","container-title":"FACETS","DOI":"10.1139/facets-2019-0006","ISSN":"2371-1671","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"FACETS","language":"en","page":"293-314","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"“We monitor by living here”: community-driven actualization of a social-ecological monitoring program based in the knowledge of Indigenous harvesters","title-short":"“We monitor by living here”","URL":"http://www.facetsjournal.com/doi/10.1139/facets-2019-0006","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Thompson","given":"Kim-Ly"},{"family":"Reece","given":"Nikkita"},{"family":"Robinson","given":"Nicole"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Havana-Jae"},{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C."},{"family":"Picard","given":"Chris R."}],"editor":[{"family":"Klenk","given":"Nicole L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Consistent with the broad commonalities across many indigenous peoples and local communities’ worldviews (see sections 2.2.4 and 2.2.8), the sustained use and health of wild-species and their habitats is often conceptualized as fundamentally interconnected to community well-being and cultural continuity. Monitoring, which is often carried out through the act of harvesting and harvest-related activities (e.g., “monitoring through harvesting”), includes interlinked indicators that capture social, ecological, and social-ecological (linkages and feedbacks among social and ecological components) aspects of sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HKYvxtKe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2017; Lyver, 2017; Parlee, 2005a; Sterling, Filardi,, 2017; Sterling, 2020; K.-L. Thompson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2017; Lyver, 2017; Parlee, 2005a; Sterling, Filardi,, 2017; Sterling, 2020; K.-L. Thompson, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30604,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TMW3XSC3"],"itemData":{"id":30604,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Routledge","title":"Sacred ecology","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":34994,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X88VIJET"],"itemData":{"id":34994,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The underlying ethos of ‘nature’s benefits’ contributing to human wellbeing provides a common platform for understanding the function and value of biodiversity for stakeholders. Diverse societal worldviews however create differences in the way cultures relate to and understand the environment. The objective of this study was to identify community-based indicators and metrics used by M?ori in New Zealand to monitor forest health and community wellbeing. Eighty semi-directed interviews were conducted with 55 forest users within the Tuawhenua tribal group to identify forest health indicators and associated gradient of metrics to assess each indicator. Indicators were grouped within nine culturally-relevant themes: (1) food procurement (mahinga kai), (2) natural productivity (hua o te whenua), (3) nature of water (?hua o te wai), (4) nature of the land (?hua o te whenua), (5) nature of the forest (?hua o te ngahere), (6) perpetual occupation of land and place (ahikaaroa), (7) spiritual dimension (taha wairua), (8) physical health (taha kikokiko), and (9) mental health (taha hinengaro). Within these themes, indicators and associated metrics were aligned within two monitoring approaches: field survey and interview-based. Community members (n = 35 individuals) were asked to prioritise field survey indicators using a seven point Likert Scale of importance. A second survey was also conducted with Tuawhenua elders (n = 43 individuals) to determine changes in the frequency of forest use by the community. A decline in the proportion of the community venturing into the forest over the last 60 years for activities such as hunting, fishing, camping, and collecting plant resources was reported. This decline in regular forest use suggests a field survey approach would be an effective method for applying community-based indicators and to gain an understanding of forest health. Forest indicators that are evaluated over a longer timeframe (months, seasons or even years), or those indicators aligned with community wellbeing, would be better evaluated using an interview-based approach. The alignment of some community-based indicators with scientific-based measures would enrich and deepen knowledge about the state of biodiversity, broaden the relevance of monitoring and reporting within indigenous communities, and help to mitigate issues of ‘shifting baselines’.","call-number":"Esub43","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-016-1142-6","ISSN":"0960-3115, 1572-9710","issue":"13","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"3183-3212","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An indigenous community-based monitoring system for assessing forest health in New Zealand","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10531-016-1142-6","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Lyver","given":"P. O’. B."},{"family":"Timoti","given":"P."},{"family":"Jones","given":"C. J."},{"family":"Richardson","given":"S. J."},{"family":"Tahi","given":"B. L."},{"family":"Greenhalgh","given":"S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}},{"id":34990,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8232FSVJ"],"itemData":{"id":34990,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"26–37","title":"Using Traditional Knowledge to Adapt to Ecological Change: Denésôliné Monitoring of Caribou Movements","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"B."},{"family":"Manseau","given":"M."},{"family":"Dene","given":"Å.K.É."},{"family":"Nation","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":30422,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3FWUINXT"],"itemData":{"id":30422,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"https://doi-org.ezproxy.uvm.edu/10.1038/s41559-017-0349-6","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Ecol Evol","page":"1798–1806","title":"Biocultural approaches to well-being and sustainability indicators across scales","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor J."},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Toomey","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Newell","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Albert","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Alvira","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Bergamini","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Blair","given":"Mary"},{"family":"Boseto","given":"David"},{"family":"Burrows","given":"Kate"},{"family":"Bynum","given":"Nora"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Casselle","given":"Jennifer E."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Cullman","given":"Georgina"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Eyzaguirre","given":"Pablo B."},{"family":"Gray","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Herrera","given":"James"},{"family":"Kenilorea","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Kinney","given":"Kealohanuiopuna"},{"family":"Kurashima","given":"Natalie"},{"family":"Macey","given":"Suzanne"},{"family":"Malone","given":"Cynthia"},{"family":"Mauli","given":"Senoveva"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"},{"family":"McMillen","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua'ala"},{"family":"Pikacha","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Porzecanski","given":"Ana L."},{"family":"Robert","given":"Pascale","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Salpeteur","given":"Matthieu"},{"family":"Siricolo","given":"Myknee"},{"family":"Stege","given":"Mark H."},{"family":"Stege","given":"Kristina"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Vave","given":"Ron"},{"family":"Wali","given":"Alaka"},{"family":"West","given":"Paige"},{"family":"Winter","given":"Kawika B."},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":30472,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IKRSUBB3"],"itemData":{"id":30472,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Achieving sustainable development globally requires multilevel and interdisciplinary efforts and perspectives. Global goals shape priorities and actions at multiple scales, creating cascading impacts realized at the local level through the direction of financial resources and implementation of programs intended to achieve progress towards these metrics. We explore ways to localize global goals to best support human well-being and environmental health by systematically comparing the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) with regionally-derived well-being dimensions that encompass components of social–ecological resilience across the Pacific Islands. Our research shows that, in the context of the Pacific, there are overlaps but also significant gaps between regional conceptions of well-being and the globally-derived SDGs. Some dimensions, related to human health and access to infrastructure and finances, are well represented in the SDGs. Other dimensions of high importance when localizing perspectives of well-being, such as those regarding connections between and across people and place and Indigenous and local knowledge, are not. Furthermore, internationally generated indicators may result in trade-offs and measurement challenges in local contexts. Creating space for place-based values in global sustainability planning aligns with international calls for transformational changes needed to achieve global goals. We identify challenges in applying SDG indicators at the local level and provide lessons learned to foster equitable and holistic approaches and outcomes for sustainability.","container-title":"Sustainability Science","DOI":"10.1007/s11625-020-00822-w","ISSN":"1862-4065, 1862-4057","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Sustain Sci","language":"en","page":"1129-1147","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Creating a space for place and multidimensional well-being: lessons learned from localizing the SDGs","title-short":"Creating a space for place and multidimensional well-being","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11625-020-00822-w","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor J."},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua?ala"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Mandle","given":"Lisa"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Aini","given":"John"},{"family":"Albert","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Caselle","given":"Jennifer E."},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Samantha H."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Darling","given":"Emily S."},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."},{"family":"Mawyer","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Mejia","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Morishige","given":"Kanoe?ulalani"},{"family":"Nainoca","given":"Winifereti"},{"family":"Parks","given":"John"},{"family":"Tanguay","given":"Jamie"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Vave","given":"Ron"},{"family":"Wase","given":"Veronica"},{"family":"Wongbusarakum","given":"Supin"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7]]}}},{"id":30592,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B8GZIVKX"],"itemData":{"id":30592,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Researchers and government agencies are increasingly embracing Indigenous knowledge to inform ecological monitoring. However, there are few detailed accounts of designing monitoring methods based in Indigenous knowledge to meet Indigenous objectives. This research details the design of a program initiated by the Gitga’at First Nation to document the knowledge and observations of their harvesters as a contemporary monitoring initiative. We, Gitga’at and academic researchers, first conducted informal interviews with knowledge holders to gauge interest and to establish community objectives. We then convened community meetings and workshops to design methods to document harvesters’ knowledge and observations. We tested and revised these methods (a post-harvest season interview guide, and a logbook to be completed by harvesters) over the course of two harvest seasons. Semi-structured interviews were more successful than the logbooks in meeting multiple community monitoring objectives. However, we were encouraged by younger participants’ suggestions to develop a digital app based on the logbook to encourage future participation. Our work can serve as a guide to other Indigenous peoples and collaborators who wish to leverage the knowledge of their land and (or) sea users, and the methods we develop are available to adapt to other cultural, social-ecological, and political contexts.","container-title":"FACETS","DOI":"10.1139/facets-2019-0006","ISSN":"2371-1671","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"FACETS","language":"en","page":"293-314","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"“We monitor by living here”: community-driven actualization of a social-ecological monitoring program based in the knowledge of Indigenous harvesters","title-short":"“We monitor by living here”","URL":"http://www.facetsjournal.com/doi/10.1139/facets-2019-0006","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Thompson","given":"Kim-Ly"},{"family":"Reece","given":"Nikkita"},{"family":"Robinson","given":"Nicole"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Havana-Jae"},{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C."},{"family":"Picard","given":"Chris R."}],"editor":[{"family":"Klenk","given":"Nicole L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indicators in indigenous peoples and local communities may also take many forms, from evaluations of the quantity and quality of species, habitats and interactions, to those embedded in stories, songs, ceremonies, oral histories and what ex situ actors might view as “art” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"w1rnZclC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sterling, Filardi,, 2017; Sterling, Ticktin,, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Sterling, Filardi,, 2017; Sterling, Ticktin,, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30591,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZGUTDLIZ"],"itemData":{"id":30591,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and Society","DOI":"10.3167/ares.2017.080104","ISSN":"2150-6779, 2150-6787","issue":"1","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Culturally Grounded Indicators of Resilience in Social-Ecological Systems","URL":"http://berghahnjournals.com/view/journals/environment-and-society/8/1/ares080104.xml","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"E.J."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Kipa Kepa Morgan","given":"T?"},{"family":"Cullman","given":"Georgina"},{"family":"Alvira","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Andrade","given":"Pelika"},{"family":"Bergamini","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Burrows","given":"Kate"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Eyzaguirre","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Giardina","given":"Christian"},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy"},{"family":"Kinney","given":"Kealohanuiopuna"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Mejia","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Morishige","given":"Kanoe"},{"family":"Newell","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Noori","given":"Lihla"},{"family":"Parks","given":"John"},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua‘ala"},{"family":"Ravikumar","given":"Ashwin"},{"family":"Tanguay","given":"Jamie"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Stege","given":"Tina"},{"family":"Stege","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Wali","given":"Alaka"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,1]]}}},{"id":30422,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3FWUINXT"],"itemData":{"id":30422,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"https://doi-org.ezproxy.uvm.edu/10.1038/s41559-017-0349-6","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Ecol Evol","page":"1798–1806","title":"Biocultural approaches to well-being and sustainability indicators across scales","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor J."},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Toomey","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Newell","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Albert","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Alvira","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Bergamini","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Blair","given":"Mary"},{"family":"Boseto","given":"David"},{"family":"Burrows","given":"Kate"},{"family":"Bynum","given":"Nora"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Casselle","given":"Jennifer E."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Cullman","given":"Georgina"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Eyzaguirre","given":"Pablo B."},{"family":"Gray","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Herrera","given":"James"},{"family":"Kenilorea","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Kinney","given":"Kealohanuiopuna"},{"family":"Kurashima","given":"Natalie"},{"family":"Macey","given":"Suzanne"},{"family":"Malone","given":"Cynthia"},{"family":"Mauli","given":"Senoveva"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"},{"family":"McMillen","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua'ala"},{"family":"Pikacha","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Porzecanski","given":"Ana L."},{"family":"Robert","given":"Pascale","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Salpeteur","given":"Matthieu"},{"family":"Siricolo","given":"Myknee"},{"family":"Stege","given":"Mark H."},{"family":"Stege","given":"Kristina"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Vave","given":"Ron"},{"family":"Wali","given":"Alaka"},{"family":"West","given":"Paige"},{"family":"Winter","given":"Kawika B."},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Box 2.6 provides an example of monitoring and indicators for wild species used by the Gitga’at, on the northwest coast of North America. 

Box 2.6. “We monitor by living here”: social-ecological approaches to monitoring and indicators by Gitga’at resource users

The Gitga’at are a Tsimshian (Ts’msyen) tribal group whose people have occupied and stewarded their lands and waters on the northwest coast of North America since time immemorial. As has been the case for millennia, hereditary leaders continue oversee the stewardship, allocation and management of resources based on an intimate knowledge of their territories, adaawx (oral history), and ayaalx (Tsimshian law). Gitga’at territorial management activities now also draws on the methods and technology offered by science (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"70zeHHm1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Keen, 2017; Ritts, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Keen, 2017; Ritts, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30611,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ESHT6WHQ"],"itemData":{"id":30611,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Ecology Progress Series","DOI":"10.3354/meps12012","language":"en","page":"211–233","title":"Whale Wave’: Shifting Strategies Structure the Complex Use of Critical Fjord Habitat by Humpbacks","volume":"567","author":[{"family":"Keen","given":"E.M."},{"family":"Wray","given":"J."},{"family":"Meuter","given":"H."},{"family":"Thompson","given":"K.-L."},{"family":"Barlow","given":"J.P."},{"family":"Picard","given":"C.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":30415,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PIYBSBCK"],"itemData":{"id":30415,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2016.04.002","language":"en","page":"25–38","title":"Collaborative research praxis to establish baseline ecoacoustics conditions in Gitga’at Territory","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Ritts","given":"M."},{"family":"Gage","given":"S.H."},{"family":"Picard","given":"C.R."},{"family":"Dundas","given":"E."},{"family":"Dundas","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, with advice and technical administration provided by the Gitga’at Oceans and Lands Department, including the Gitga’at Guardians ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"t14lMKkO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gitga\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}at First Nation, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Gitga’at First Nation, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30601,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4ISK5395"],"itemData":{"id":30601,"type":"book","edition":"Working Draft December 1, 2011","title":"Gitga'at Marine Use Plan","URL":"http://gitgaatnation.ca/wp-content/uploads/2018/01/MarineUsePlan.pdf","author":[{"family":"Gitga'at First Nation","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Despite colonial policies of cultural assimilation and land dispossession, many Gitga’at cultural identity persists and continues to be underpinned by the harvest, consumption, trading, and celebrating of traditional foods on a daily-basis ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pwXySdnk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fediuk & Reid, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Fediuk & Reid, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30414,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VEMV9IWU"],"itemData":{"id":30414,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Gitga’at Nutrition Survey: Consumption Rate Study Summary Report","author":[{"family":"Fediuk","given":"K."},{"family":"Reid","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

In 2016, the Gitga’at Oceans and Lands Department invited university researchers to assist in designing and piloting a monitoring program that would focus documenting the observations of Gitga’at harvesters and knowledge holders ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wKhwRQzx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K.-L. Thompson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(K.-L. Thompson, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30592,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B8GZIVKX"],"itemData":{"id":30592,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Researchers and government agencies are increasingly embracing Indigenous knowledge to inform ecological monitoring. However, there are few detailed accounts of designing monitoring methods based in Indigenous knowledge to meet Indigenous objectives. This research details the design of a program initiated by the Gitga’at First Nation to document the knowledge and observations of their harvesters as a contemporary monitoring initiative. We, Gitga’at and academic researchers, first conducted informal interviews with knowledge holders to gauge interest and to establish community objectives. We then convened community meetings and workshops to design methods to document harvesters’ knowledge and observations. We tested and revised these methods (a post-harvest season interview guide, and a logbook to be completed by harvesters) over the course of two harvest seasons. Semi-structured interviews were more successful than the logbooks in meeting multiple community monitoring objectives. However, we were encouraged by younger participants’ suggestions to develop a digital app based on the logbook to encourage future participation. Our work can serve as a guide to other Indigenous peoples and collaborators who wish to leverage the knowledge of their land and (or) sea users, and the methods we develop are available to adapt to other cultural, social-ecological, and political contexts.","container-title":"FACETS","DOI":"10.1139/facets-2019-0006","ISSN":"2371-1671","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"FACETS","language":"en","page":"293-314","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"“We monitor by living here”: community-driven actualization of a social-ecological monitoring program based in the knowledge of Indigenous harvesters","title-short":"“We monitor by living here”","URL":"http://www.facetsjournal.com/doi/10.1139/facets-2019-0006","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Thompson","given":"Kim-Ly"},{"family":"Reece","given":"Nikkita"},{"family":"Robinson","given":"Nicole"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Havana-Jae"},{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C."},{"family":"Picard","given":"Chris R."}],"editor":[{"family":"Klenk","given":"Nicole L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The monitoring objectives of the program (now known as “We monitor by living here”) were established by harvesters and knowledge holders, and include: tracking changes in Gitga’at territory, including traditional food species, to inform stewardship decisions and adaptation measures; encouraging youth to learn about traditional foods and how the territory is changing; strengthening the case for Gitga’at rights and title; and informing health and wellness programming. 

Over the course of two pilot data collection seasons a monitoring framework was co-developed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RpktyzqM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. L. Thompson, Lantz,, 2020)","plainCitation":"(K. L. Thompson, Lantz,, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30420,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WEWYV6CX"],"itemData":{"id":30420,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-11503-250210","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"1–27","title":"A review of indigenous knowledge and participation in environmental monitoring","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Thompson","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Lantz","given":"T.C."},{"family":"Ban","given":"N.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The framework includes the elements and indicators that Gitga’at people monitor through the harvesting and harvest related activities including processing, preserving, cooking and sharing. It made explicit the numerous interlinked social, ecological, and social-ecological elements that are monitored by Gitga’at land- and sea-users including the quality and abundance of food and medicine species, habitat quality, harvest intensity, sharing and trading institutions, accessibility of resources, weather patterns, cultural continuity, and abnormal occurrences in the territory (Table 2.9, Figure 2.8). It is important to note that the distinction between social and ecological elements of the monitoring framework was not made by Gitga’at participants, as occurrences in the spiritual and social-political world and the natural world are understood as inseparable. 

Table 2.9. Non-comprehensive list of concepts and indicators that Gitga’at people described monitoring during harvesting activities (from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rPoItHIw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. L. Thompson, Hill,, 2020)","plainCitation":"(K. L. Thompson, Hill,, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30597,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RPGERWKM"],"itemData":{"id":30597,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"People and Nature","DOI":"10.1002/pan3.10135","language":"en","page":"1–15","title":"Indigenous food harvesting as social–ecological monitoring: A case study with the Gitga’at First Nation","volume":"June","author":[{"family":"Thompson","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Hill","given":"C."},{"family":"Ojeda","given":"J."},{"family":"Ban","given":"N.C."},{"family":"Picard","given":"C.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

