      The example of Gitga’at monitoring through land- and sea-based practices is similar to reports from other indigenous communities. For example, M?ori communities monitor forest health and community well-being using indicators that include prevalence of certain species, sounds associated with the forest, intensity of weather, and the strength of people’s connection to the forest ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"svYQwRgV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lyver, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Lyver, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34994,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X88VIJET"],"itemData":{"id":34994,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The underlying ethos of ‘nature’s benefits’ contributing to human wellbeing provides a common platform for understanding the function and value of biodiversity for stakeholders. Diverse societal worldviews however create differences in the way cultures relate to and understand the environment. The objective of this study was to identify community-based indicators and metrics used by M?ori in New Zealand to monitor forest health and community wellbeing. Eighty semi-directed interviews were conducted with 55 forest users within the Tuawhenua tribal group to identify forest health indicators and associated gradient of metrics to assess each indicator. Indicators were grouped within nine culturally-relevant themes: (1) food procurement (mahinga kai), (2) natural productivity (hua o te whenua), (3) nature of water (?hua o te wai), (4) nature of the land (?hua o te whenua), (5) nature of the forest (?hua o te ngahere), (6) perpetual occupation of land and place (ahikaaroa), (7) spiritual dimension (taha wairua), (8) physical health (taha kikokiko), and (9) mental health (taha hinengaro). Within these themes, indicators and associated metrics were aligned within two monitoring approaches: field survey and interview-based. Community members (n = 35 individuals) were asked to prioritise field survey indicators using a seven point Likert Scale of importance. A second survey was also conducted with Tuawhenua elders (n = 43 individuals) to determine changes in the frequency of forest use by the community. A decline in the proportion of the community venturing into the forest over the last 60 years for activities such as hunting, fishing, camping, and collecting plant resources was reported. This decline in regular forest use suggests a field survey approach would be an effective method for applying community-based indicators and to gain an understanding of forest health. Forest indicators that are evaluated over a longer timeframe (months, seasons or even years), or those indicators aligned with community wellbeing, would be better evaluated using an interview-based approach. The alignment of some community-based indicators with scientific-based measures would enrich and deepen knowledge about the state of biodiversity, broaden the relevance of monitoring and reporting within indigenous communities, and help to mitigate issues of ‘shifting baselines’.","call-number":"Esub43","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-016-1142-6","ISSN":"0960-3115, 1572-9710","issue":"13","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"3183-3212","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An indigenous community-based monitoring system for assessing forest health in New Zealand","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10531-016-1142-6","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Lyver","given":"P. O’. B."},{"family":"Timoti","given":"P."},{"family":"Jones","given":"C. J."},{"family":"Richardson","given":"S. J."},{"family":"Tahi","given":"B. L."},{"family":"Greenhalgh","given":"S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. ?Denésô?iné hunters monitor barren ground caribou migrations using physical indicators such as body condition and population size as well as spiritual indicators to explain variability in migration patterns ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"93ncrbPH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Parlee, 2005b)","plainCitation":"(Parlee, 2005b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":29326,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R2N822FE"],"itemData":{"id":29326,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic","issue":"1","page":"26–37","title":"Using Traditional Knowledge to Adapt to Ecological Change: Denésôliné Monitoring of Caribou Movements","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"},{"family":"Manseau","given":"Micheline"},{"family":"Dene","given":"Åutsÿl K É"},{"family":"Nation","given":"First"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	 A recent global review of participation of indigenous peoples and their knowledge in environmental monitoring highlights that in collaborative monitoring efforts, the degree of power and participation of indigenous peoples and local communities influences which monitoring indicators are used ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0oZtn5We","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. L. Thompson, Hill,, 2020)","plainCitation":"(K. L. Thompson, Hill,, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30597,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RPGERWKM"],"itemData":{"id":30597,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"People and Nature","DOI":"10.1002/pan3.10135","language":"en","page":"1–15","title":"Indigenous food harvesting as social–ecological monitoring: A case study with the Gitga’at First Nation","volume":"June","author":[{"family":"Thompson","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Hill","given":"C."},{"family":"Ojeda","given":"J."},{"family":"Ban","given":"N.C."},{"family":"Picard","given":"C.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Initiatives with strong indigenous leadership throughout all phases of monitoring, including initiating and setting monitoring objectives, designing methods and indicators, and ultimately making management decisions, were most likely to monitor a diversity of indicators, including social-ecological, social, and ecological indicators within the same initiative. For example, Inuit people monitoring environmental change paid attention to ecological indicators, such as the body condition of caribou, social-ecological indicators such as hunting success, and social indicators such as the prevalence of knowledge about seasonal cycles in their communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rAoMOUzv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34999,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QYNCQSJT"],"itemData":{"id":34999,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1080/08920750600970487","ISSN":"0892-0753","issue":"1","note":"ISBN: 0892-0753","page":"143–162","title":"Collaborative integrated management in Canada's north: The role of local and traditional knowledge and community-based monitoring","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Mina Kislalioglu"},{"family":"Fast","given":"Helen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Collaborative initiatives with indigenous participation were most likely to monitor a combination of ecological and social ecological indicators. For example, Kaxinawá people in collaboration with non-profit organizations monitored wild species in their territory using ecological indicators, such as the mean body mass and abundance of preferred harvest species, as well as social-ecological indicators such as the catch-per-unit effort of harvest species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9jxQSWAe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Constantino, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Constantino, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34997,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K7WM8WDH"],"itemData":{"id":34997,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2008.08.008","language":"en","page":"2718–2729","title":"Indigenous Collaborative Research for Wildlife Management in Amazonia: The Case of the Kaxinawa","volume":"141","author":[{"family":"Constantino","given":"P."},{"family":"Fortini","given":"L."},{"family":"Kaxinawa","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Meanwhile, initiatives with less strong indigenous involvement in phases of design and management were most likely to focus solely on monitoring ecological indicators. Indeed, monitoring initiatives driven by external agencies tended to focus primarily on ecological indicators, while those led by indigenous peoples tended to include a more holistic suite of indicators including social (i.e., human processes such as spirituality, language), ecological (i.e., biological, physical, or chemical), and social-ecological indicators (i.e., interactions between humans and the natural world such as hunting activities) (Figure 2.9). 


Figure 2.9. Percent of monitoring initiatives displayed according to the type of indicators used. Initiatives are classified according to the degree of participation of indigenous people: the blue dashed line indicates externally driven initiatives with indigenous people as data collectors; orange dotted line indicates collaborative initiatives with indigenous people playing some role in design and execution; grey solid line indicates collaborative initiatives with indigenous people playing a strong role in design, execution and management; and the yellow dashed line indicates autonomous monitoring by indigenous people with some external support (modified from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XeokM0bM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. L. Thompson, Lantz,, 2020)","plainCitation":"(K. L. Thompson, Lantz,, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30420,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WEWYV6CX"],"itemData":{"id":30420,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-11503-250210","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"1–27","title":"A review of indigenous knowledge and participation in environmental monitoring","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Thompson","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Lantz","given":"T.C."},{"family":"Ban","given":"N.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, under CC BY-NC 4.0 License: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/).

2.3.4 Summary of global and local indicators of sustainable use of wild species 

How sustainable use of wild species is measured and monitored is shaped by the ways in which it is conceptualized. At the global level, as conceptualizations of sustainable use have changed over time (see section 2.2.2, 2.2.3), indicators for sustainability have also shifted, for example from a fairly narrow focus on ecological aspects towards inclusion of social, especially economic and governance aspects ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cIRX8kGE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Linser, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Linser, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34995,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TVKBWDMV","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8AR3JJVI"],"itemData":{"id":34995,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The use of criteria and indicators (C & I) for data collection, monitoring, assessing and reporting on sustainable forest management (SFM) has been growing since the Earth Summit in 1992, supported by eleven intergovernmental, regional and international forest-related C & I processes. The initial effort led to varying levels of implementation across countries. Several processes never went much beyond the adoption of a first set of C & I while others have made substantial progress. In recent years, interest in C & I for SFM has again increased. In light of the Sustainable Development Goals and emerging global challenges the contribution of C & I to monitor, assess and report on forest conditions and trends is increasingly important. We compare and analyse the structure, activities and progress of the intergovernmental C & I processes. The work is based on document analysis and questionnaires sent to the secretariats of the processes and C & I experts. We found many similarities but also major differences in the structure and content of the C & I sets. The results provide a context for discussing and understanding why some of the C & I processes are successful in their work while others have stalled. Finally, we propose the required ingredients for success for the future activities of the forest-related intergovernmental C & I processes.","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f9090515","ISSN":"19994907","issue":"9","page":"1–23","title":"25 years of criteria and indicators for sustainable forest management: Why some intergovernmental C & I processes flourished while others faded","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Linser","given":"Stefanie"},{"family":"Wolfslehner","given":"Bernhard"},{"family":"Asmar","given":"Fady"},{"family":"Bridge","given":"Simon R.J."},{"family":"Gritten","given":"David"},{"family":"Guadalupe","given":"Vicente"},{"family":"Jafari","given":"Mostafa"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Laclau","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Robertson","given":"Guy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; see section 2.3.2). Today, global indicator sets clearly capture many of ecological, economic and social components of sustainable use that are broadly agreed upon in the global conservation arena (see section 2.2.6). They also overlap with some of the indicators used in indigenous peoples and local communities, for example ecological indicators of abundance and distributions in harvested species (see section 2.3.3).  
      Nonetheless, there are also some widely agreed upon aspects of sustainable use that are poorly represented in global indicator sets. In particular, in the academic literature today, there is widespread agreement that the harvest of wild species is best understood as a social-ecological system, where sustainable use requires understanding and maintaining linkages and feedbacks among and between social and ecological elements (see section 2.2.3). There is also growing recognition of the importance of socio-cultural dimensions of sustainability, including relational values (see section 2.2.3). Similarly, in indigenous peoples and local communities, the sustained use and health of wild-species and their habitats is often conceptualized as fundamentally interconnected to community well-being and cultural continuity (see sections 2.2.4, 2.2.8, 2.3.3). However, indicators that capture these concepts of sustainable use, i.e., social-ecological indicators and socio-cultural indicators, including those that capture relational values, are sparse in global indicator sets (see section 2.3.2). Similarly, despite their representation in principles of sustainable use of wild species (see section 2.2.6.), indicators that relate to indigenous peoples and local communities’ community rights and access, and to monitoring that involves both indigenous and local knowledge and scientific knowledge are poorly represented.
      The underrepresentation of these kinds of indicators can have multiple consequences for the sustainable use of wild species. First, regardless of the scale in which they are applied, missing key elements of sustainable use can increase the potential for misdiagnosis and poor design of interventions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WgdOU6F7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sterling, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Sterling, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30472,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IKRSUBB3"],"itemData":{"id":30472,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Achieving sustainable development globally requires multilevel and interdisciplinary efforts and perspectives. Global goals shape priorities and actions at multiple scales, creating cascading impacts realized at the local level through the direction of financial resources and implementation of programs intended to achieve progress towards these metrics. We explore ways to localize global goals to best support human well-being and environmental health by systematically comparing the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) with regionally-derived well-being dimensions that encompass components of social–ecological resilience across the Pacific Islands. Our research shows that, in the context of the Pacific, there are overlaps but also significant gaps between regional conceptions of well-being and the globally-derived SDGs. Some dimensions, related to human health and access to infrastructure and finances, are well represented in the SDGs. Other dimensions of high importance when localizing perspectives of well-being, such as those regarding connections between and across people and place and Indigenous and local knowledge, are not. Furthermore, internationally generated indicators may result in trade-offs and measurement challenges in local contexts. Creating space for place-based values in global sustainability planning aligns with international calls for transformational changes needed to achieve global goals. We identify challenges in applying SDG indicators at the local level and provide lessons learned to foster equitable and holistic approaches and outcomes for sustainability.","container-title":"Sustainability Science","DOI":"10.1007/s11625-020-00822-w","ISSN":"1862-4065, 1862-4057","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Sustain Sci","language":"en","page":"1129-1147","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Creating a space for place and multidimensional well-being: lessons learned from localizing the SDGs","title-short":"Creating a space for place and multidimensional well-being","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11625-020-00822-w","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor J."},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua?ala"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Mandle","given":"Lisa"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Aini","given":"John"},{"family":"Albert","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Caselle","given":"Jennifer E."},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Samantha H."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Darling","given":"Emily S."},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."},{"family":"Mawyer","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Mejia","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Morishige","given":"Kanoe?ulalani"},{"family":"Nainoca","given":"Winifereti"},{"family":"Parks","given":"John"},{"family":"Tanguay","given":"Jamie"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Vave","given":"Ron"},{"family":"Wase","given":"Veronica"},{"family":"Wongbusarakum","given":"Supin"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, indicator sets that lack social-ecological linkages may miss important connections and feedback loops that are critical to ensuring sustainable use. This potential for misdiagnosis and subsequent poor design of interventions is aggravated by the strong tendency for indicators of all aspects of sustainability to be more sensitive than specific, calling attention to the need to address a shortcoming in performance without guidance on what practices are actually responsible for the shortcomings 
       Second, if indicators fail to measure aspects of sustainable use perceived locally to be critical, they will hold little meaning locally, may fail to inspire appropriate action, and in addition, have the potential to cause both environmental and social harm ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8U9Yi4Mc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sterling, 2020; Sterling, Ticktin,, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Sterling, 2020; Sterling, Ticktin,, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30472,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IKRSUBB3"],"itemData":{"id":30472,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Achieving sustainable development globally requires multilevel and interdisciplinary efforts and perspectives. Global goals shape priorities and actions at multiple scales, creating cascading impacts realized at the local level through the direction of financial resources and implementation of programs intended to achieve progress towards these metrics. We explore ways to localize global goals to best support human well-being and environmental health by systematically comparing the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) with regionally-derived well-being dimensions that encompass components of social–ecological resilience across the Pacific Islands. Our research shows that, in the context of the Pacific, there are overlaps but also significant gaps between regional conceptions of well-being and the globally-derived SDGs. Some dimensions, related to human health and access to infrastructure and finances, are well represented in the SDGs. Other dimensions of high importance when localizing perspectives of well-being, such as those regarding connections between and across people and place and Indigenous and local knowledge, are not. Furthermore, internationally generated indicators may result in trade-offs and measurement challenges in local contexts. Creating space for place-based values in global sustainability planning aligns with international calls for transformational changes needed to achieve global goals. We identify challenges in applying SDG indicators at the local level and provide lessons learned to foster equitable and holistic approaches and outcomes for sustainability.","container-title":"Sustainability Science","DOI":"10.1007/s11625-020-00822-w","ISSN":"1862-4065, 1862-4057","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Sustain Sci","language":"en","page":"1129-1147","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Creating a space for place and multidimensional well-being: lessons learned from localizing the SDGs","title-short":"Creating a space for place and multidimensional well-being","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11625-020-00822-w","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor J."},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua?ala"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Mandle","given":"Lisa"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Aini","given":"John"},{"family":"Albert","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Caselle","given":"Jennifer E."},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Samantha H."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Darling","given":"Emily S."},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."},{"family":"Mawyer","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Mejia","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Morishige","given":"Kanoe?ulalani"},{"family":"Nainoca","given":"Winifereti"},{"family":"Parks","given":"John"},{"family":"Tanguay","given":"Jamie"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Vave","given":"Ron"},{"family":"Wase","given":"Veronica"},{"family":"Wongbusarakum","given":"Supin"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7]]}}},{"id":30591,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZGUTDLIZ"],"itemData":{"id":30591,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and Society","DOI":"10.3167/ares.2017.080104","ISSN":"2150-6779, 2150-6787","issue":"1","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Culturally Grounded Indicators of Resilience in Social-Ecological Systems","URL":"http://berghahnjournals.com/view/journals/environment-and-society/8/1/ares080104.xml","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"E.J."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Kipa Kepa Morgan","given":"T?"},{"family":"Cullman","given":"Georgina"},{"family":"Alvira","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Andrade","given":"Pelika"},{"family":"Bergamini","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Burrows","given":"Kate"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Eyzaguirre","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Giardina","given":"Christian"},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy"},{"family":"Kinney","given":"Kealohanuiopuna"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Mejia","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Morishige","given":"Kanoe"},{"family":"Newell","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Noori","given":"Lihla"},{"family":"Parks","given":"John"},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua‘ala"},{"family":"Ravikumar","given":"Ashwin"},{"family":"Tanguay","given":"Jamie"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Stege","given":"Tina"},{"family":"Stege","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Wali","given":"Alaka"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ultimately the impacts of global goals and indicators are felt at the local level through the direction of financial resources and implementation of programs intended to achieve progress towards these metrics ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Vd9OtfrS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sterling, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Sterling, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30472,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IKRSUBB3"],"itemData":{"id":30472,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Achieving sustainable development globally requires multilevel and interdisciplinary efforts and perspectives. Global goals shape priorities and actions at multiple scales, creating cascading impacts realized at the local level through the direction of financial resources and implementation of programs intended to achieve progress towards these metrics. We explore ways to localize global goals to best support human well-being and environmental health by systematically comparing the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) with regionally-derived well-being dimensions that encompass components of social–ecological resilience across the Pacific Islands. Our research shows that, in the context of the Pacific, there are overlaps but also significant gaps between regional conceptions of well-being and the globally-derived SDGs. Some dimensions, related to human health and access to infrastructure and finances, are well represented in the SDGs. Other dimensions of high importance when localizing perspectives of well-being, such as those regarding connections between and across people and place and Indigenous and local knowledge, are not. Furthermore, internationally generated indicators may result in trade-offs and measurement challenges in local contexts. Creating space for place-based values in global sustainability planning aligns with international calls for transformational changes needed to achieve global goals. We identify challenges in applying SDG indicators at the local level and provide lessons learned to foster equitable and holistic approaches and outcomes for sustainability.","container-title":"Sustainability Science","DOI":"10.1007/s11625-020-00822-w","ISSN":"1862-4065, 1862-4057","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Sustain Sci","language":"en","page":"1129-1147","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Creating a space for place and multidimensional well-being: lessons learned from localizing the SDGs","title-short":"Creating a space for place and multidimensional well-being","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11625-020-00822-w","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor J."},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua?ala"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Mandle","given":"Lisa"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Aini","given":"John"},{"family":"Albert","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Caselle","given":"Jennifer E."},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Samantha H."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Darling","given":"Emily S."},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."},{"family":"Mawyer","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Mejia","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Morishige","given":"Kanoe?ulalani"},{"family":"Nainoca","given":"Winifereti"},{"family":"Parks","given":"John"},{"family":"Tanguay","given":"Jamie"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Vave","given":"Ron"},{"family":"Wase","given":"Veronica"},{"family":"Wongbusarakum","given":"Supin"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Designing global indicators is complex (see section 2.3.1) and designing those that capture social-ecological linkages and socio-cultural components poses even more challenges since global processes rely on indicators that are easy to quantify, compare, aggregate and communicate across scales. Nonetheless, there are examples of existing global indicators that encompass these aspects of sustainable use (see section 2.3.2.2), for example, numerous social-ecological indicators in the fishing indicator sets reviewed, and socio-cultural indicators, including relational indicators, in one of the forestry indicator sets reviewed. These and other indicators could be appropriately adapted to other practices and/or contexts.
      Moreover, increased and improved collaboration with indigenous peoples and local communities represents a critical opportunity for better measuring and monitoring of sustainable use at both local and global scales (Figure 2.9). Indicators of sustainable use that have long-been used in indigenous peoples and local communities to monitor the linkages among social and ecological elements, and that link to community wellbeing and cultural continuity, can inform the development of appropriate global indicators. Conversely, collaborations with indigenous peoples and local community knowledge holders and knowledge experts can lead to the co-creation of local indicators that can help localize global, regional or national indicators to local realities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7qNpcmuH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dacks, 2019; Sterling, 2020; Sterling, Filardi,, 2017; Sterling, Ticktin,, 2017; K. L. Thompson, Hill,, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dacks, 2019; Sterling, 2020; Sterling, Filardi,, 2017; Sterling, Ticktin,, 2017; K. L. Thompson, Hill,, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30590,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TFYPV3VG"],"itemData":{"id":30590,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Science and Practice","DOI":"10.1111/csp2.38","ISSN":"2578-4854, 2578-4854","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Conservat Sci and Prac","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Developing biocultural indicators for resource management","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/csp2.38","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Mawyer","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Fabre","given":"Pauline"},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Mejia","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua‘ala"},{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor"},{"family":"Wongbusarakum","given":"Supin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6]]}}},{"id":30472,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IKRSUBB3"],"itemData":{"id":30472,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Achieving sustainable development globally requires multilevel and interdisciplinary efforts and perspectives. Global goals shape priorities and actions at multiple scales, creating cascading impacts realized at the local level through the direction of financial resources and implementation of programs intended to achieve progress towards these metrics. We explore ways to localize global goals to best support human well-being and environmental health by systematically comparing the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) with regionally-derived well-being dimensions that encompass components of social–ecological resilience across the Pacific Islands. Our research shows that, in the context of the Pacific, there are overlaps but also significant gaps between regional conceptions of well-being and the globally-derived SDGs. Some dimensions, related to human health and access to infrastructure and finances, are well represented in the SDGs. Other dimensions of high importance when localizing perspectives of well-being, such as those regarding connections between and across people and place and Indigenous and local knowledge, are not. Furthermore, internationally generated indicators may result in trade-offs and measurement challenges in local contexts. Creating space for place-based values in global sustainability planning aligns with international calls for transformational changes needed to achieve global goals. We identify challenges in applying SDG indicators at the local level and provide lessons learned to foster equitable and holistic approaches and outcomes for sustainability.","container-title":"Sustainability Science","DOI":"10.1007/s11625-020-00822-w","ISSN":"1862-4065, 1862-4057","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Sustain Sci","language":"en","page":"1129-1147","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Creating a space for place and multidimensional well-being: lessons learned from localizing the SDGs","title-short":"Creating a space for place and multidimensional well-being","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11625-020-00822-w","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor J."},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua?ala"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Mandle","given":"Lisa"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Aini","given":"John"},{"family":"Albert","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Caselle","given":"Jennifer E."},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Samantha H."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Darling","given":"Emily S."},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."},{"family":"Mawyer","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Mejia","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Morishige","given":"Kanoe?ulalani"},{"family":"Nainoca","given":"Winifereti"},{"family":"Parks","given":"John"},{"family":"Tanguay","given":"Jamie"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Vave","given":"Ron"},{"family":"Wase","given":"Veronica"},{"family":"Wongbusarakum","given":"Supin"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7]]}}},{"id":30422,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3FWUINXT"],"itemData":{"id":30422,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"https://doi-org.ezproxy.uvm.edu/10.1038/s41559-017-0349-6","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Ecol Evol","page":"1798–1806","title":"Biocultural approaches to well-being and sustainability indicators across scales","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor J."},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Toomey","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Newell","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Albert","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Alvira","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Bergamini","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Blair","given":"Mary"},{"family":"Boseto","given":"David"},{"family":"Burrows","given":"Kate"},{"family":"Bynum","given":"Nora"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Casselle","given":"Jennifer E."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Cullman","given":"Georgina"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Eyzaguirre","given":"Pablo B."},{"family":"Gray","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Herrera","given":"James"},{"family":"Kenilorea","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Kinney","given":"Kealohanuiopuna"},{"family":"Kurashima","given":"Natalie"},{"family":"Macey","given":"Suzanne"},{"family":"Malone","given":"Cynthia"},{"family":"Mauli","given":"Senoveva"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"},{"family":"McMillen","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua'ala"},{"family":"Pikacha","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Porzecanski","given":"Ana L."},{"family":"Robert","given":"Pascale","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Salpeteur","given":"Matthieu"},{"family":"Siricolo","given":"Myknee"},{"family":"Stege","given":"Mark H."},{"family":"Stege","given":"Kristina"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Vave","given":"Ron"},{"family":"Wali","given":"Alaka"},{"family":"West","given":"Paige"},{"family":"Winter","given":"Kawika B."},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":30591,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZGUTDLIZ"],"itemData":{"id":30591,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and Society","DOI":"10.3167/ares.2017.080104","ISSN":"2150-6779, 2150-6787","issue":"1","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Culturally Grounded Indicators of Resilience in Social-Ecological Systems","URL":"http://berghahnjournals.com/view/journals/environment-and-society/8/1/ares080104.xml","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Sterling","given":"E.J."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Kipa Kepa Morgan","given":"T?"},{"family":"Cullman","given":"Georgina"},{"family":"Alvira","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Andrade","given":"Pelika"},{"family":"Bergamini","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Betley","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Burrows","given":"Kate"},{"family":"Caillon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Dacks","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Eyzaguirre","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Filardi","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Gazit","given":"Nadav"},{"family":"Giardina","given":"Christian"},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy"},{"family":"Kinney","given":"Kealohanuiopuna"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Mejia","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Morishige","given":"Kanoe"},{"family":"Newell","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Noori","given":"Lihla"},{"family":"Parks","given":"John"},{"family":"Pascua","given":"Pua‘ala"},{"family":"Ravikumar","given":"Ashwin"},{"family":"Tanguay","given":"Jamie"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Stege","given":"Tina"},{"family":"Stege","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Wali","given":"Alaka"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,1]]}}},{"id":30597,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RPGERWKM"],"itemData":{"id":30597,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"People and Nature","DOI":"10.1002/pan3.10135","language":"en","page":"1–15","title":"Indigenous food harvesting as social–ecological monitoring: A case study with the Gitga’at First Nation","volume":"June","author":[{"family":"Thompson","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Hill","given":"C."},{"family":"Ojeda","given":"J."},{"family":"Ban","given":"N.C."},{"family":"Picard","given":"C.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Tracking Change project conducted with communities across the Mackenzie, Mekong, and Amazon River basins, as well as community-based observation networks across coastal Arctic communities, are demonstrating this potential by building local monitoring indicators, and networking knowledge gained ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"S2kgj8Vq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Michell, 2018; Parlee & Mahoney, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Michell, 2018; Parlee & Mahoney, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34953,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SAR9EKU"],"itemData":{"id":34953,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Green Theory and Praxis Journal","DOI":"10.1016/S0963-8687(12)00009-1","issue":"1","page":"3–17","title":"Tu ëhena - \\"Water is Life\\": Tracking Changes on the Land, Land, and River Systems in the Northern Saskatchewan Athabasca Region from the Perspectives of Denesuline Peoples","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Michell","given":"Hermann"},{"family":"Tsannie","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Adam","given":"Allan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":34957,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D2EUWHCB"],"itemData":{"id":34957,"type":"report","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1097/HCO.0000000000000406","title":"Tracking Change: Local and Traditional Knowlegde in Watershed Governance","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"B."},{"family":"Mahoney","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These collaborations are effective when colonial governments recognize the authority of indigenous peoples as managers of their territories and when power is shared between indigenous experts and outside scientists. 
      In sum, while there are some broad commonalities, conceptualizations of sustainable use of wild species are highly dynamic and variable across practices, and economic, cultural and social contexts. Ultimately, the diversity of ways in which sustainability is conceptualized means that there is no “one size fits all” approach to appropriately and effectively measure and monitor sustainable use.

""" ;
    ipbes:identifier "2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "How is sustainable use of wild species measured and monitored?" .

