    ipbes:hasDescription """
With the increase of the use of scenarios and the number of publications reporting on them, the number of definitions of what a ‘scenario’ is has also increased. Some scholars treat scenarios as being tightly connected to models, and therefore use both terms inseparably. In this chapter, we follow the definition of scenarios as provided in the IPBES methodological assessment report on scenarios and models: “Scenarios are representations of possible futures for one or more components of a system, particularly, in this assessment, for drivers of change in nature and nature’s benefits, including alternative policy or management options” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UnWAIzSw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ferrier, n.d.)","plainCitation":"(Ferrier, n.d.)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19027,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A6ZW8IXH"],"itemData":{"id":19027,"type":"chapter","container-title":"2016 Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","language":"en","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"The methodological assessment report on scenarios and models of biodiversity and ecosystem services","author":[{"family":"Ferrier","given":"S."},{"family":"Ninan","given":"K.N."},{"family":"Leadley","given":"P."},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"R."},{"family":"Acosta","given":"L.A."},{"family":"Akçakaya","given":"H.R."},{"family":"Brotons","given":"L."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W.L."},{"family":"Christensen","given":"V."},{"family":"Harhash","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Kabubo-Mariara","given":"J."},{"family":"Lundquist","given":"C."},{"family":"Obersteiner","given":"M."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"H.M."},{"family":"Peterson","given":"G."},{"family":"Pichs-Madruga","given":"R."},{"family":"Ravindranath","given":"N."},{"family":"Rondinini","given":"C."},{"family":"Wintle","given":"B.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is important to highlight the last part of this definition, as it points at a crucial distinction that most scholars acknowledge (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Gsm9Erwe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}rjeson, H\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}jer, Dreborg, Ekvall, & Finnveden, 2006; Kok, van Vliet, B\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}rlund, Dubel, & Sendzimir, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Börjeson, Höjer, Dreborg, Ekvall, & Finnveden, 2006; Kok, van Vliet, Bärlund, Dubel, & Sendzimir, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19273,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R95UTYXZ"],"itemData":{"id":19273,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Futures","language":"fr","page":"723–739","title":"Scenario types and techniques — towards a user's guide","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Börjeson","given":"L."},{"family":"Höjer","given":"M."},{"family":"Dreborg","given":"K.-H."},{"family":"Ekvall","given":"T."},{"family":"Finnveden","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":19522,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ICECZI9Q"],"itemData":{"id":19522,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Technological Forecasting and Social Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.techfore.2011.01.004","ISSN":"00401625","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Technological Forecasting and Social Change","language":"en","page":"835-851","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Combining participative backcasting and exploratory scenario development: Experiences from the SCENES project","title-short":"Combining participative backcasting and exploratory scenario development","volume":"78","author":[{"family":"Kok","given":"Kasper"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Mathijs","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Bärlund","given":"Ilona"},{"family":"Dubel","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Sendzimir","given":"Jan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, namely between exploratory scenarios and target-seeking scenarios. This fundamental division is also highlighted in several IPBES assessments, notably the Methodological Assessment Report on Scenarios and Models, where four types of scenarios are discerned, three of which are of importance here: (i) “exploratory scenarios”, which represent different plausible futures, often based on storylines; (ii) “target-seeking scenarios”, also known as “normative scenarios”, which represent an agreed-upon future target and scenarios that provide alternative pathways for reaching this target; and (iii) “policy-screening scenarios”, also known as “ex-ante scenarios”, which represent various policy options under consideration ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"owmd1KPW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ferrier, n.d.)","plainCitation":"(Ferrier, n.d.)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19027,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A6ZW8IXH"],"itemData":{"id":19027,"type":"chapter","container-title":"2016 Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","language":"en","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"The methodological assessment report on scenarios and models of biodiversity and ecosystem services","author":[{"family":"Ferrier","given":"S."},{"family":"Ninan","given":"K.N."},{"family":"Leadley","given":"P."},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"R."},{"family":"Acosta","given":"L.A."},{"family":"Akçakaya","given":"H.R."},{"family":"Brotons","given":"L."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W.L."},{"family":"Christensen","given":"V."},{"family":"Harhash","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Kabubo-Mariara","given":"J."},{"family":"Lundquist","given":"C."},{"family":"Obersteiner","given":"M."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"H.M."},{"family":"Peterson","given":"G."},{"family":"Pichs-Madruga","given":"R."},{"family":"Ravindranath","given":"N."},{"family":"Rondinini","given":"C."},{"family":"Wintle","given":"B.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
Figure 5.2. Scenarios in the spectrum of complexity and uncertainty. Source: Adapted from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rYYyWfU7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zurek & Henrichs, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Zurek & Henrichs, 2007)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19451,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WZ3593S5"],"itemData":{"id":19451,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Technological Forecasting and Social Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.techfore.2006.11.005","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"1282–1295","title":"Linking scenarios across geographical scales in international environmental assessments","volume":"74","author":[{"family":"Zurek","given":"M.B."},{"family":"Henrichs","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} © 2006 Elsevier Inc., license number 5154260505736. 
      Scenarios are distinguished from other approaches to future assessment, such as forecasting and risk assessment, by being specifically intended for situations in which the factors shaping the future are highly uncertain and largely uncontrollable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lJQ5JIMr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biggs, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Biggs, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2752,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NSX6QE44"],"itemData":{"id":2752,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The rich biodiversity of southern Africa has to date been relatively unimpacted by the activities of modern society, but to what degree will this situation persist into the 21st century? We use a leading global environmental assessment model (IMAGE) to explore future land use and climate change in southern Africa under the scenarios developed by the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment. We assess the impacts on terrestrial biodiversity using the Biodiversity Intactness Index, which gives the average change in population size relative to the pre-modern state, across all terrestrial species of plants and vertebrates. Over the coming century, we project absolute declines in the average population sizes of these taxa that are two to three times greater than the reductions that have occurred since circa 1700. Our results highlight the immense challenges faced by efforts to reduce rates of biodiversity loss in southern Africa, even under relatively optimistic scenarios. These results stress the urgent need for better aligning biodiversity conservation and development priorities in the region. Furthermore, we suggest that context-sensitive conservation targets that account for the development imperatives in different parts of the region are needed.","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2008.02.001","ISSN":"0959-3780","issue":"2","page":"296–309","title":"Scenarios of biodiversity loss in southern Africa in the 21st century","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"Reinette"},{"family":"Simons","given":"Henk"},{"family":"Bakkenes","given":"Michel"},{"family":"Scholes","given":"Robert J"},{"family":"Eickhout","given":"Bas"},{"family":"Vuuren","given":"Detlef","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"Rob"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; see Figure 5.2). Scenarios thus serve to structure the uncertainty of future developments of complex systems, and to provide a palette of plausible futures and possible actions. 
      In this chapter, we used scenarios both to explore what could happen (exploratory scenarios) and to present strategies and actions for what should happen (target-seeking and policy-screening scenarios). Analyses of the scenarios literature based on the different projections and plurality of visions were conducted to evaluate what drives sustainable use in general. In section 5.2.1, we elaborate on the most important aspects of scenarios and how to understand them in this chapter.
5.2.1 Types of scenarios included 
5.2.1.1 Terminology
The term ‘scenario’ is used by many different communities across scientific domains, scales, as well as in policy and practice. The scenario literature is, therefore, vast, rapidly increasing, and in partial disagreement on what a scenario is, what it can be used for, what methods are most appropriate, and what results it generates. In general, the term ‘scenario’ is most often used by those that set out to develop exploratory scenarios and translate those into model projections. It is reasonable to assume that papers included in this assessment will largely belong to the category of model-based explorations. More importantly, target seeking scenarios are often not referred to as scenarios. Particularly at local scales, normative scenarios are mostly referred to as ‘pathways’. When it concerns policy screening scenarios, a range of other terms is often used to describe them, including strategies, plans, policies, options, or actions. It is also reasonable to assume that the papers included in this assessment might not have picked up on all the target-seeking scenarios. A number of keywords were added to the search string to ensure that the database was not limited by terminological differences.
5.2.1.2 Exploratory scenarios
Exploratory scenarios ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SUNAmeYs","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Van Notten, 2003; Van der Heijden, 2005; Avin & Goodspeed, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Van Notten, 2003; Van der Heijden, 2005; Avin & Goodspeed, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19440,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DWRIT6ZZ"],"itemData":{"id":19440,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Futures","issue":"5","language":"pt","page":"423–443","title":"An updated scenario typology","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Van Notten","given":"P.W.F."},{"family":"Rotmans","given":"J."},{"family":"Asselt","given":"M.B.A."},{"family":"Rothman","given":"D.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":19544,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JMBKUU2F"],"itemData":{"id":19544,"type":"book","call-number":"HD30.3 .V36 2005","edition":"2nd ed","event-place":"Chichester, West Sussex ; Hoboken, N.J","ISBN":"978-0-470-02368-6","number-of-pages":"356","publisher":"John Wiley & Sons","publisher-place":"Chichester, West Sussex ; Hoboken, N.J","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Scenarios: the art of strategic conversation","title-short":"Scenarios","author":[{"family":"Van der Heijden","given":"Kees"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":19265,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PG6WY3M3"],"itemData":{"id":19265,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of the American Planning Association","language":"en","page":"403–416","title":"Using Exploratory Scenarios in Planning Practice","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Avin","given":"U."},{"family":"Goodspeed","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} examine plausible futures, based on potential trajectories of drivers, either indirect (e.g., socio-political, economic and technological factors) or direct (e.g., habitat conversion and climate change). Exploratory scenarios can illuminate the discourse on specific problems, by illustrating various potential futures starting from the current point in time. Despite the relatively short history of developing exploratory scenarios – that started with the publication of the Global Scenario Group scenarios ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GvDEazxk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gallopin, 1997; P. Raskin, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Gallopin, 1997; P. Raskin, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19309,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZK3VAIBP"],"itemData":{"id":19309,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PoleStar Series Report","issue":"7","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Stockholm Environment Institute, Stockholm","title":"Branch Points: Global scenarios and human choice. A Resource Paper of the Global Scenario Group","author":[{"family":"Gallopin","given":"G."},{"family":"Hammond","given":"A."},{"family":"Raskin","given":"P."},{"family":"Swart","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}},{"id":19404,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/34RRINHX"],"itemData":{"id":19404,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"SEI PoleStar Series Report","issue":"10","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Boston\\npublisher: Stockholm Environment Institute","title":"Great Transition: The promise and lure of the times ahead. A report of the Global Scenario Group","author":[{"family":"Raskin","given":"P."},{"family":"Tariq","given":"B."},{"family":"Gallopin","given":"G."},{"family":"Gutman","given":"P."},{"family":"Hammond","given":"A."},{"family":"Kates","given":"R."},{"family":"Swart","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} – an enormous number of scenarios have been developed across the full range of scales from local to global (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pj8wglfZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. V. L. Hunt, 2012)","plainCitation":"(D. V. L. Hunt, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2671,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KAMQ2IMW"],"itemData":{"id":2671,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Future scenarios provide challenging, plausible and relevant stories about how the future could unfold. Urban Futures (UF) research has identified a substantial set (\\\\textgreater450) of seemingly disparate scenarios published over the period 1997–2011 and within this research, a sub-set of \\\\textgreater160 scenarios has been identified (and categorized) based on their narratives according to the structure first proposed by the Global Scenario Group (GSG) in 1997; three world types (Business as Usual, Barbarization, and Great Transitions) and six scenarios, two for each world type (Policy Reform—PR, Market Forces—MF, Breakdown—B, Fortress World—FW, Eco-Communalism—EC and New Sustainability Paradigm—NSP). It is suggested that four of these scenario archetypes (MF, PR, NSP and FW) are sufficiently distinct to facilitate active stakeholder engagement in futures thinking. Moreover they are accompanied by a well-established, internally consistent set of narratives that provide a deeper understanding of the key fundamental drivers (e.g., STEEP—Social, Technological, Economic, Environmental and Political) that could bring about realistic world changes through a push or a pull effect. This is testament to the original concept of the GSG scenarios and their development and refinement over a 16 year period.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su4040740","issue":"4","title":"Scenario Archetypes: Converging Rather than Diverging Themes","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Hunt","given":"Dexter V L"},{"family":"Lombardi","given":"D Rachel"},{"family":"Atkinson","given":"Stuart"},{"family":"Barber","given":"Austin R G"},{"family":"Barnes","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Boyko","given":"Christopher T"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Julie"},{"family":"Bryson","given":"John"},{"family":"Butler","given":"David"},{"family":"Caputo","given":"Silvio"},{"family":"Caserio","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Coles","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Cooper","given":"Rachel F D"},{"family":"Farmani","given":"Raziyeh"},{"family":"Gaterell","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Hale","given":"James"},{"family":"Hales","given":"Chantal"},{"family":"Hewitt","given":"C Nicholas"},{"family":"Jankovic","given":"Lubo"},{"family":"Jefferson","given":"I"},{"family":"Leach","given":"J"},{"family":"MacKenzie","given":"A Rob"},{"family":"Memon","given":"Fayyaz Ali"},{"family":"Sadler","given":"Jon P"},{"family":"Weingaertner","given":"Carina"},{"family":"Whyatt","given":"J Duncan"},{"family":"Rogers","given":"Christopher D F"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aojPooR8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Amer, 2013; Priess & Hauck, 2014; Rothman, 2008; Rounsevell & Metzger, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Amer, 2013; Priess & Hauck, 2014; Rothman, 2008; Rounsevell & Metzger, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5053,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZZSFUVSQ"],"itemData":{"id":5053,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper reviews the scenario planning literature looking for answers for the following questions: How do qualitative and quantitative scenario methods differ and what are the advantages and disadvantages? What methods exist for quantitative scenario planning? Particularly quantitative scenario methods often lead to a large number of so-called \\"raw\\" scenarios that need to be further refined, discussed, and verbally described. How do scenario planners select raw scenarios for further exploration and how many should they choose? How is the problem of validation addressed in scenario studies?. © 2012 Elsevier Ltd.","container-title":"Futures","DOI":"10.1016/j.futures.2012.10.003","ISSN":"00163287","page":"23–40","title":"A review of scenario planning","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Amer","given":"Muhammad"},{"family":"Daim","given":"Tugrul U."},{"family":"Jetter","given":"Antonie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":19402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z8DNP9XE"],"itemData":{"id":19402,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology & Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-06168-190112.","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"12","title":"Integrative scenario development","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Priess","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Hauck","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":19413,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TL76PV77"],"itemData":{"id":19413,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Environmental futures: the practice of environmental scenario analysis. Developments in integrated environmental assessment","event-place":"Elsevier, Amsterdam","language":"en","page":"37–65","publisher-place":"Elsevier, Amsterdam","title":"A survey of environmental scenarios","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Rothman","given":"D.S."}],"editor":[{"family":"Alcamo","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":19414,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8EM65G67"],"itemData":{"id":19414,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"WIREs Clim Change","DOI":"10.1002/wcc.63.","language":"it","page":"606–619","title":"Developing qualitative scenario storylines for environmental change assessment","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Rounsevell","given":"M.D.A."},{"family":"Metzger","given":"M.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Influential global scenarios include those of the Global Environment Outlook 3 and 4 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"u89Qc5ko","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(United Nations Environment Programme & Earthscan, 2002; United Natio