ly":"Fritz","given":"S."},{"family":"Skalsky","given":"R."},{"family":"Aoki","given":"K."},{"family":"De Cara","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}), natural vegetation (LPJ; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LHtqt0gt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Smith, Prentice, & Sykes, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Smith, Prentice, & Sykes, 2001)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19426,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JV3TW9JE"],"itemData":{"id":19426,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology & Biogeography","language":"en","page":"621–637","title":"Representation of vegetation dynamics in the modelling of terrestrial ecosystems: comparing two contrasting approaches within European climate space","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Smith","given":"B."},{"family":"Prentice","given":"I.C."},{"family":"Sykes","given":"M.T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and biodiversity (GLOBIO; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kvM6IFN4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alkemade, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Alkemade, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19261,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NYXX6VD2"],"itemData":{"id":19261,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1007/s10021-009-9229-5","language":"en","page":"374–390","title":"GLOBIO3: A framework to investigate options for reducing global terrestrial biodiversity loss","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Alkemade","given":"R."},{"family":"Van Oorschot","given":"M."},{"family":"Miles","given":"L."},{"family":"Nellemann","given":"C."},{"family":"Bakkenes","given":"M."},{"family":"Ten Brink","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In summary, there are a large and growing number of initiatives that have developed qualitative stories and/or quantitative models to explore what could happen to a range of environmental issues, including biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people. 
5.2.1.3 Exploratory scenario archetypes
 “Scenario archetypes” describe different general patterns of future developments and can be useful in summarizing and harmonizing the overwhelming amount of information in individual sets of scenarios. The scenario archetype approach has been recognized by IPBES ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"C5ThKHpo","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2015; Ferrier, 2016)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2015; Ferrier, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19565,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2CVXGGY8"],"itemData":{"id":19565,"type":"document","title":"Intergovernmental Platform for Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services IPBES/4/8: Scoping report for a global assessment on biodiversity and ecosystem services (deliverable 2 (c)). Secretariat of the IPBES, Bonn, Germany.","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":19027,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A6ZW8IXH"],"itemData":{"id":19027,"type":"chapter","container-title":"2016 Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","language":"en","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"The methodological assessment report on scenarios and models of biodiversity and ecosystem services","author":[{"family":"Ferrier","given":"S."},{"family":"Ninan","given":"K.N."},{"family":"Leadley","given":"P."},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"R."},{"family":"Acosta","given":"L.A."},{"family":"Akçakaya","given":"H.R."},{"family":"Brotons","given":"L."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W.L."},{"family":"Christensen","given":"V."},{"family":"Harhash","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Kabubo-Mariara","given":"J."},{"family":"Lundquist","given":"C."},{"family":"Obersteiner","given":"M."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"H.M."},{"family":"Peterson","given":"G."},{"family":"Pichs-Madruga","given":"R."},{"family":"Ravindranath","given":"N."},{"family":"Rondinini","given":"C."},{"family":"Wintle","given":"B.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} as a way to help to synthesize findings from scenarios for the global and throughout the four IPBES regional assessments. A set of six global scenario archetypes was used, based on scenario families described by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zGxVz7xK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Van Vuuren, Kok, Girod, Lucas, & Vries, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Van Vuuren, Kok, Girod, Lucas, & Vries, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19443,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q42V5S4A"],"itemData":{"id":19443,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental","language":"pt","title":"Scenarios in global environmental assessments: key characteristics and lessons for future use","author":[{"family":"Van Vuuren","given":"D.P."},{"family":"Kok","given":"M.T.J."},{"family":"Girod","given":"B."},{"family":"Lucas","given":"P.L."},{"family":"Vries","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the regional assessments, these six archetypes were also used, although in some cases with slight modifications. In the IPBES Regional Assessment on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services for Europe and Central Asia, for example, “reformed markets” was omitted as a separate archetype, and another sixth archetype was added (“inequality”). 
      In this chapter, we use a set of four main archetypes, most of which are further subdivided into 2-3 subtypes (Box 5.1). This set does not completely match any earlier proposed set, but it does include all archetypes used in the global and regional assessments. The main reason for deviation from previous sets is the fact that they will not be used in their own right, but in combination with target-seeking scenarios. This set was seen as best to facilitate combination with normative scenarios, while maintaining a similar selection.
Box 5.1 describes the 8 types of scenarios archetypes considered.
1. Market forces. Global developments steered by economic growth result in a strong dominance of international markets with a decreasing degree of regulation. Environmental problems are only dealt with when solutions are of economic interest. This archetype includes two recurrent variants:
   1a. A less extreme variant includes business-as-usual and reference type of scenarios, as well as those scenarios typified as strongly market-driven. All assume current trends to continue without strongly nonlinear changes. Typical examples: SSP2 (see ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8sYtmzJ2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Neill, 2017)","plainCitation":"(O’Neill, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19388,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GHUN3UKV"],"itemData":{"id":19388,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2015.01.004","language":"en","page":"169–180","title":"The roads ahead: Narratives for shared socioeconomic pathways describing world futures in the 21st century","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"O’Neill","given":"B.C."},{"family":"Kriegler","given":"E."},{"family":"Ebi","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Kemp-Benedict","given":"E."},{"family":"Riahi","given":"K."},{"family":"Rothman","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Solecki","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Markets First (GEO3; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"x1u3HflE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations Environment Programme & Earthscan, 2002)","plainCitation":"(United Nations Environment Programme & Earthscan, 2002)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NQ9H59ME"],"itemData":{"id":19548,"type":"book","event-place":"London","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1101262717","publisher":"Earthscan [on behalf of] UNEP.","publisher-place":"London","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Global environment outlook 3 3","author":[{"literal":"United Nations Environment Programme"},{"literal":"Earthscan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}).
   1b. A more extreme variant of market-led environmental management with highly equal and healthy societies. In terms of biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people, this archetype can range from devastating (environmental destruction) to positive (economically viable nature-based solutions). Typical example: SSP5 (ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JXLWFVMK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Neill, 2017)","plainCitation":"(O’Neill, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19388,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GHUN3UKV"],"itemData":{"id":19388,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2015.01.004","language":"en","page":"169–180","title":"The roads ahead: Narratives for shared socioeconomic pathways describing world futures in the 21st century","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"O’Neill","given":"B.C."},{"family":"Kriegler","given":"E."},{"family":"Ebi","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Kemp-Benedict","given":"E."},{"family":"Riahi","given":"K."},{"family":"Rothman","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Solecki","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}).
2. New sustainability paradigm. A world with an increasingly proactive attitude of policymakers and the public at large towards environmental issues and a high level of regulation. All variants of this archetype are beneficial for biodiversity, either through behavioral change, top-down “green” policies, or through green technology development. In all cases, this is reinforced by a proactive attitude to dealing with environmental problems. Three main variants can be discerned:
   2a. Technological solutions with strong technological development in all sectors, including for example engineered ecosystems to deliver ecosystem services. Typical example: TechnoGarden ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1ATw9kdP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cork, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Cork, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HI8XX6HJ"],"itemData":{"id":19288,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"11","title":"Synthesis of the storylines","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Cork","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Peterson","given":"G.D."},{"family":"Bennett","given":"E.M."},{"family":"Petschel-Held","given":"G."},{"family":"Zurek","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
   2b. Global sustainable development with strong, mostly top-down, governance structures that are effective in realizing a more sustainable world. Typical example: Policy First (GEO3; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JF1SlptV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations Environment Programme & Earthscan, 2002)","plainCitation":"(United Nations Environment Programme & Earthscan, 2002)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NQ9H59ME"],"itemData":{"id":19548,"type":"book","event-place":"London","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1101262717","publisher":"Earthscan [on behalf of] UNEP.","publisher-place":"London","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Global environment outlook 3 3","author":[{"literal":"United Nations Environment Programme"},{"literal":"Earthscan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}).
   2c. Regional sustainability with fundamental change being initiated by a broadly supported, and bottom-up enforced paradigm shift, often accompanied by a dematerialization process and a ‘back to nature’ attitude. Typical example: Rural Revival (OpenNESS) or B2 (Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change Special Report on Emissions Scenarios)
3. Fortress world. A regionalized world based on economic developments. The market mechanism fails, leading to a growing gap between rich and poor. In turn, this results in increasing problems with crime, violence and terrorism, which eventuates in strong trade and other barriers. Two variants exist:
   3a. Regional economic growth. A less extreme variant where, despite strong barriers, the quality of life for most is secured and most problems are dealt with adequately. Typical example: Order from Strength (MA; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tHLnKKw4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cork, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Cork, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HI8XX6HJ"],"itemData":{"id":19288,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"11","title":"Synthesis of the storylines","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Cork","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Peterson","given":"G.D."},{"family":"Bennett","given":"E.M."},{"family":"Petschel-Held","given":"G."},{"family":"Zurek","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
   3b. Breakdown. A more extreme variant, where organized crime and terrorism eventually lead to institutional disintegration and economic collapse. This variant is rarely elaborated in the literature. Typical example: Breakdown ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cVbzRcc5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gallopin, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Gallopin, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19309,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZK3VAIBP"],"itemData":{"id":19309,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PoleStar Series Report","issue":"7","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Stockholm Environment Institute, Stockholm","title":"Branch Points: Global scenarios and human choice. A Resource Paper of the Global Scenario Group","author":[{"family":"Gallopin","given":"G."},{"family":"Hammond","given":"A."},{"family":"Raskin","given":"P."},{"family":"Swart","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
The effects on the environment and biodiversity are mixed. Overall, there is a tendency towards increased security, which can either be positive (protect biodiversity) or negative (intensify agricultural production). Particularly in low-income countries, deforestation and loss of natural areas is a risk.
4. Inequality. A world of growing inequalities, both within and between countries. The increasingly powerful elite takes environmental responsibility, while the large lower class is poor but kept satisfied. The effects on the environment differ greatly, depending on location and type of issue. Importantly, the global “green” elite actively combats globally important issues, such as climate change, which has a positive impact on biodiversity. Although increasing inequalities have negative consequences for economic and social development, biodiversity and ecosystems by and large benefit. Typical example: SSP4 (ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DEvKtecP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Neill, 2017)","plainCitation":"(O’Neill, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19388,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GHUN3UKV"],"itemData":{"id":19388,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2015.01.004","language":"en","page":"169–180","title":"The roads ahead: Narratives for shared socioeconomic pathways describing world futures in the 21st century","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"O’Neill","given":"B.C."},{"family":"Kriegler","given":"E."},{"family":"Ebi","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Kemp-Benedict","given":"E."},{"family":"Riahi","given":"K."},{"family":"Rothman","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Solecki","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}).
Of particular relevance to the focus of this assessment, the exploratory archetypes encompass important obstacles and limitations for sustainable use, such as follows: 
1. Market Forces: There is a lack of interest in the environment. Sustainable development is not a focus and many wild species that have less direct economic revenue might not be protected sufficiently. 
2a. Technological solutions: There is a very strong emphasis on technological end-of-pipe solutions. Not all wild species can be used sustainably this way, and technological solutions are likely to have a limited scope. 
2b. Global sustainability: Top-down enforcement of laws and regulations might be ineffective for many locally specific contexts.
2c. Regional sustainability: Bottom-up solutions will hamper those aspects that need global coordination, such as climate change mitigation; pandemics; or ecological corridors. 
3/4. Fortress worlds and inequality: In these archetypes, social and human problems will worsen, including increased poverty and inequality. This is likely to strongly inhibit sustainable development, because of a lack of financial support, lack of public and political interests, and/or lack of general importance.
In this chapter, the archetypes will be used in an overarching way, but our analysis is not confined to these archetypes. As these scenario archetypes are constructed to categorize exploratory scenarios, the set as shown above cannot be directly adopted, but needs to be linked and combined with target-seeking scenarios. The procedure used to do this will be explained in section 5.2.1.6.
5.2.1.4 Intervention scenarios: target-seeking and policy scenarios
‘Intervention scenarios’ evaluate alternative policy or management options, by developing either ‘target-seeking’ or ‘policy-screening’ scenarios. In policy-screening scenarios, a policy, or set of policies, is applied and an assessment of how the policy modifies the future is carried out. Target-seeking scenarios (also known as ‘normative scenarios’) are a valuable tool for examining the viability and effectiveness of alternative pathways to the desired outcome. They start with the definition of a clear objective or a set of objectives that can either be specified in terms of achievable targets or as an objective function to be optimized. What both have in common is the search for effective policies or actions to reach a commonly agreed (normative) target. In contrast to the exploratory scenarios, intervention scenarios are much less developed at global level, and as a result, there is a much larger diversity. This is due to the disconnectedness of communities of practices, but also the more diverse set of locally or regionally contextualized issues that need to be addressed. As a result, it is much more difficult to provide a concise overview or attempt to categorize that overview into a limited number of archetypical descriptions. To illustrate this diversity, we refer to the different IPBES Regional Assessments, all of which include a section on pathways and other normative scenarios.
5.2.1.5 Pathway archetypes
To illustrate an attempt to categorize archetypes at the regional level, the IPBES Regional Assessment for Europe and Central Asia recognizes four “clusters of internally consistent pathways” based on ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uvyRTShC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Luederitz, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Luederitz, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19569,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ETXQQDRB"],"itemData":{"id":19569,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Cleaner Production","DOI":"10.1016/j.jclepro.2016.09.005","ISSN":"09596526","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Cleaner Production","language":"en","page":"61-76","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Learning through evaluation – A tentative evaluative scheme for sustainability transition experiments","volume":"169","author":[{"family":"Luederitz","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Schäpke","given":"Niko"},{"family":"Wiek","given":"Arnim"},{"family":"Lang","given":"Daniel J."},{"family":"Bergmann","given":"Matthias"},{"family":"Bos","given":"Joannette J."},{"family":"Burch","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Davies","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Evans","given":"James"},{"family":"König","given":"Ariane"},{"family":"Farrelly","given":"Megan A."},{"family":"Forrest","given":"Nigel"},{"family":"Frantzeskaki","given":"Niki"},{"family":"Gibson","given":"Robert B."},{"family":"Kay","given":"Braden"},{"family":"Loorbach","given":"Derk"},{"family":"McCormick","given":"Kes"},{"family":"Parodi","given":"Oliver"},{"family":"Rauschmayer","given":"Felix"},{"family":"Schneidewind","given":"Uwe"},{"family":"Stauffacher","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Stelzer","given":"Franziska"},{"family":"Trencher","given":"Gregory"},{"family":"Venjakob","given":"Johannes"},{"family":"Vergragt","given":"Philip J."},{"family":"Wehrden","given":"Henrik","non-dropping-particle":"von"},{"family":"Westley","given":"Frances R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}: 
- The “green economy” pathway addresses transitions toward decreased environmental degradation and resource depletion through green growth supported by policy instruments that stimulate specific economic activities.
- The “low carbon transformation” pathway encompasses all pathways focusing primarily on mitigating climate change and adapting to climate change impacts, locally and globally. 
- The “ecotopian solutions” pathway addresses transitions towards increased socio-ecological integrity. It does this by challenging current belief systems, lifestyles and living spaces with bottom-up, politically alternative initiatives of self-organization at the community or neighborhood level to work towards local-scale, self-sufficiency. 
- The “transition movements” pathway also focuses on fundamental individual and social changes, but in contrast to ecotopian solutions, transition movements aim to scale-up to whole system transformation. 
5.2.1.6 Integrated scenarios and pathways
Exploratory scenarios and target-seeking pathways and intervention scenarios provide a palette of plausible futures and possible policies, actions, and other management options. Often, they are used together in what is referred to as “scenario planning”. Exploratory scenarios sketch future possibilities and are used as multiple baselines against which the effectiveness of policies and pathways can be tested. This approach yields ‘robust’ or ‘no-regret’ policies that would work in all plausible different future outlooks.
      Here, we take a different approach by combining exploratory and normative scenarios in one set of integrated archetypes. The starting point is the exploratory scenario archetypes that are combined with the pathway archetypes by indicating whether or not a specific pathway of interventions is compatible with the archetype (Table 5.1). There are strong matches between the market forces and green economy archetypes as well as between regional sustainability and ecotopian solutions. The low carbon society could be combined with many archetypes but is less relevant for our context of assessing the sustainability of use of wild species, while the Transition archetype combines top-down and bottom-up elements from almost all archetypes and would thus partly work in all archetypes.
