      This set of plausible changes and possible intervention archetypes will be the starting point for the elaboration of the scenarios in this chapter.
5.2.2  Methodological considerations for scenario development
This chapter assesses the scenarios and interventions that have been proposed in the literature, thus focusing on the resulting future outlooks and measures, and much less on the process and methods that were used to develop the scenarios. There are, however, a number of methodological aspects that are strongly tied to the outcome of this assessment. The methods employed can also facilitate processes of change that can be part of the solution. Scenarios can be co-created with stakeholders and this participatory process offers the possibility to aim for, among others, social learning, conflict management, or understanding of multiple perspectives. As such, scenarios can be a platform for public participation, and the process of deliberation and negotiation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zcouuZqv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Patel, 2007; Reed, 2013; Rounsevell & Metzger, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Patel, 2007; Reed, 2013; Rounsevell & Metzger, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19393,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9DESZUZ7"],"itemData":{"id":19393,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land Use Policy","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"546–561","title":"Participatory planning in land use analysis. An insight into the experiences and opportunities created by stakeholder involvement in scenario construction in the Northern Mediterranean","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Patel","given":"M."},{"family":"Kok","given":"K."},{"family":"Rothman","given":"D.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":19407,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F22QXEGJ"],"itemData":{"id":19407,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"uplands Journal of Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"345–362,","title":"Participatory scenario development for environmental management: A methodological framework illustrated with experience from the UK","volume":"128","author":[{"family":"Reed","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Kenter","given":"J."},{"family":"Bonn","given":"A."},{"family":"Broad","given":"K."},{"family":"Burt","given":"T.P."},{"family":"Fazey","given":"I.R."},{"family":"Fraser","given":"E.D.G."},{"family":"Hubacek","given":"K."},{"family":"Nainggolan","given":"D."},{"family":"Quinn","given":"C.H."},{"family":"L.C.","given":"F.Ravera"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":19414,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8EM65G67"],"itemData":{"id":19414,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"WIREs Clim Change","DOI":"10.1002/wcc.63.","language":"it","page":"606–619","title":"Developing qualitative scenario storylines for environmental change assessment","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Rounsevell","given":"M.D.A."},{"family":"Metzger","given":"M.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This transdisciplinary process of stakeholder engagement resonates well with the regional sustainability/ecotopian pathway archetype and is often seen as essential for its implementation. Box 5.2 elaborates on an example of (multi-scale) participatory scenario development. 
      Many scenario-development methods advocate the development of multi-scale scenarios. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Zy4LwFwm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. T. J. Kok, 2016)","plainCitation":"(M. T. J. Kok, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19348,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A3HIWDPY"],"itemData":{"id":19348,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability Science","DOI":"10.1007/s11625-016-0354-8","language":"fr","page":"177–181","title":"Biodiversity and ecosystem services require IPBES to take novel approach to scenarios","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Kok","given":"M.T.J."},{"family":"Kok","given":"K."},{"family":"Peterson","given":"G.D."},{"family":"Hill","given":"R."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Carpenter","given":"SR"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} advise the IPBES on their overall strategy for incorporating multiple scales in their assessments. In an important paper, based on experiences within the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7z7mprJV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zurek & Henrichs, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Zurek & Henrichs, 2007)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19451,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WZ3593S5"],"itemData":{"id":19451,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Technological Forecasting and Social Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.techfore.2006.11.005","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"1282–1295","title":"Linking scenarios across geographical scales in international environmental assessments","volume":"74","author":[{"family":"Zurek","given":"M.B."},{"family":"Henrichs","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} provide an overview of the degree to which scenarios can be linked across scales. The process of multi-scale scenario development can either be predominantly top-down (e.g., Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, see ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"umN15pqo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Biggs, 2007; K. Kok, Biggs, & Zurek, 2007)","plainCitation":"(R. Biggs, 2007; K. Kok, Biggs, & Zurek, 2007)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19269,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N3TV8XJF"],"itemData":{"id":19269,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecol Soc","issue":"1","language":"en","title":"Linking futures across scales: a dialog on multiscale scenarios","URL":"URL:","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"R."},{"family":"Raudsepp-Hearne","given":"C."},{"family":"Atkinson-Palombo","given":"C."},{"family":"Bohensky","given":"E."},{"family":"Boyd","given":"E."},{"family":"Cundill","given":"G."},{"family":"Fox","given":"H."},{"family":"Ingram","given":"S."},{"family":"Kok","given":"K."},{"family":"Spehar","given":"S."},{"family":"TengöM","given":"Timmer"},{"family":"D","given":"Zurek"},{"literal":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":19349,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QWISEJ93"],"itemData":{"id":19349,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"8","title":"Methods for Developing Multiscale Participatory Scenarios: Insights from Southern Africa and Europe","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Kok","given":"K."},{"family":"Biggs","given":"R."},{"family":"Zurek","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or predominantly bottom-up (e.g. seeds of a good Anthropocene; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CJsHWOE5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bennett, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Bennett, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19586,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X7C33X7H"],"itemData":{"id":19586,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","DOI":"10.1002/fee.1309","ISSN":"1540-9295, 1540-9309","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Front Ecol Environ","language":"en","page":"441-448","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Bright spots: seeds of a good Anthropocene","title-short":"Bright spots","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"Elena M"},{"family":"Solan","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Biggs","given":"Reinette"},{"family":"McPhearson","given":"Timon"},{"family":"Norström","given":"Albert V"},{"family":"Olsson","given":"Per"},{"family":"Pereira","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Peterson","given":"Garry D"},{"family":"Raudsepp?Hearne","given":"Ciara"},{"family":"Biermann","given":"Frank"},{"family":"Carpenter","given":"Stephen R"},{"family":"Ellis","given":"Erle C"},{"family":"Hichert","given":"Tanja"},{"family":"Galaz","given":"Victor"},{"family":"Lahsen","given":"Myanna"},{"family":"Milkoreit","given":"Manjana"},{"family":"Martin López","given":"Berta"},{"family":"Nicholas","given":"Kimberly A"},{"family":"Preiser","given":"Rika"},{"family":"Vince","given":"Gaia"},{"family":"Vervoort","given":"Joost M"},{"family":"Xu","given":"Jianchu"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Top-down scenarios can easily be classified as they are linked to higher-level, often global, scenarios. Local scenarios developed through bottom-up processes can benefit from alignment with scenario archetypes to facilitate comparison and synthesis. The fact that many scenarios are either bottom-up or stand-alone studies, was an important justification for the consideration of scenario archetypes in this chapter.
      One essential feature of scenarios is their ability to integrate. This can be across scale, sectors, actor groups, or topics. Particularly when combining narratives and models, scenarios are an excellent tool to deal with the complexity of the entire social-ecological system under study.  The level of integration increases further when exploratory and target-seeking scenarios are combined. Not all scenario studies make use of the potential for integration – many modelling studies use a single, sectoral, model and scenarios to provide model input – but here we combine scenario archetypes with other approaches to sketch a more complete picture of potential futures. 

Box 5.2 Co-creation and participatory processes in scenarios of sustainable use.
Scenarios and scenario planning have a long history, initiated by the Rand cooperation in the 1950s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xvfTrxXd","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Kahn & Wiener, 1967; Bradfield, Wright, Burt, Cairns, & Heijden, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Kahn & Wiener, 1967; Bradfield, Wright, Burt, Cairns, & Heijden, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19339,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GQ6UPCMJ"],"itemData":{"id":19339,"type":"book","event-place":"NewYork","language":"en","publisher":"Macmillan","publisher-place":"NewYork","title":"The Year 2000: A Framework for Speculation on the Next Thirty-three Years","author":[{"family":"Kahn","given":"H."},{"family":"Wiener","given":"A.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1967"]]}}},{"id":19274,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8DKT4HJC"],"itemData":{"id":19274,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Futures","language":"fr","page":"795–812","title":"The origins and evolution of scenario techniques in long range business planning","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Bradfield","given":"R."},{"family":"Wright","given":"G."},{"family":"Burt","given":"G."},{"family":"Cairns","given":"G.V."},{"family":"Heijden","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and extensively used by oil companies, such as Royal Dutch Shell ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nTgPXDVr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wack, 1985)","plainCitation":"(Wack, 1985)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2660,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HKQALG4Y"],"itemData":{"id":2660,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Harvard Business Review","issue":"6","page":"139–150","title":"Scenarios: Shooting the Rapids","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Wack","given":"P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1985"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/sch