attedCitation":"(Gallopin, 1997; P. Raskin, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Gallopin, 1997; P. Raskin, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19309,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZK3VAIBP"],"itemData":{"id":19309,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PoleStar Series Report","issue":"7","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Stockholm Environment Institute, Stockholm","title":"Branch Points: Global scenarios and human choice. A Resource Paper of the Global Scenario Group","author":[{"family":"Gallopin","given":"G."},{"family":"Hammond","given":"A."},{"family":"Raskin","given":"P."},{"family":"Swart","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}},{"id":19404,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/34RRINHX"],"itemData":{"id":19404,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"SEI PoleStar Series Report","issue":"10","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Boston\\npublisher: Stockholm Environment Institute","title":"Great Transition: The promise and lure of the times ahead. A report of the Global Scenario Group","author":[{"family":"Raskin","given":"P."},{"family":"Tariq","given":"B."},{"family":"Gallopin","given":"G."},{"family":"Gutman","given":"P."},{"family":"Hammond","given":"A."},{"family":"Kates","given":"R."},{"family":"Swart","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qYNHhJMZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nakicenovic, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Nakicenovic, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19555,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CY34C7K7"],"itemData":{"id":19555,"type":"article-journal","title":"Special report on emissions scenarios","author":[{"family":"Nakicenovic","given":"Nebojsa"},{"family":"Alcamo","given":"Joseph"},{"family":"Davis","given":"Gerald"},{"family":"Vries","given":"B","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Fenhann","given":"Joergen"},{"family":"Gaffin","given":"Stuart"},{"family":"Gregory","given":"Kenneth"},{"family":"Grubler","given":"Arnulf"},{"family":"Jung","given":"Tae Yong"},{"family":"Kram","given":"Tom"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} were quickly followed by the Global Environment Outlook ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GFpEtoak","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(United Nations Environment Programme & Earthscan, 2002; United Nations Environment Programme, 2007; UNEP, 2012, p. 2012)","plainCitation":"(United Nations Environment Programme & Earthscan, 2002; United Nations Environment Programme, 2007; UNEP, 2012, p. 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NQ9H59ME"],"itemData":{"id":19548,"type":"book","event-place":"London","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1101262717","publisher":"Earthscan [on behalf of] UNEP.","publisher-place":"London","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Global environment outlook 3 3","author":[{"literal":"United Nations Environment Programme"},{"literal":"Earthscan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":19549,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZIDVR6BQ"],"itemData":{"id":19549,"type":"book","call-number":"GE170 .G55518 2007","event-place":"Nairobi, Kenya : London","ISBN":"978-92-807-2836-1","note":"OCLC: ocn175284923","number-of-pages":"540","publisher":"United Nations Environment Programme ; Stationery Office [distributor]","publisher-place":"Nairobi, Kenya : London","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Global environment outlook: environment for development, GEO 4","title-short":"Global environment outlook","editor":[{"family":"United Nations Environment Programme","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":2972,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PGKDSC76"],"itemData":{"id":2972,"type":"report","note":"DOI: 10.2307/2807995","number":"9789280731774","page":"40-40","title":"Global Environment Outlook 5. Environment for the future we want","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/2807995?origin=crossref","author":[{"literal":"UNEP"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}},"locator":"2012"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment (2005) and others. These early practitioners paved the way by showing the power of scenario assessments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bcN4mxVP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. D. Raskin, 2005)","plainCitation":"(P. D. Raskin, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2661,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KNUF2HY9"],"itemData":{"id":2661,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The long-range outlook for the world's ecosystems depends on the course taken by global development in the coming decades. Current global trends and ecological dynamics are consistent with very different outcomes, defined by alternative assumptions about the technological, economic, demographic, geopolitical, and social aspects of development and the ways in which institutions, personal and public values, and natural systems may be expected to respond to historically novel stressors. Recent advances in scenario analysis have addressed the dual methodological challenge of exploring these uncertainties in an organized way and determining what would be needed to make the transition to sustainability. This paper reviews global scenario research, setting current efforts in a historical context. It focuses on seven recent studies that are comprehensive, regionally disaggregated, and narratively rich—and thus of greatest relevance to the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment (MA). It summarizes their social visions and the level of quantitative detail used in these exercises. Taken together, this suite of global scenario studies provides a useful platform for the MA by offering insight into the complex factors that drive ecosystem change, estimating the magnitude of regional pressures on ecosystems, sounding the alert on critical uncertainties that could undermine sustainable development, and understanding the importance of institutions and values. But these studies are only a point of departure. The integration of changing ecosystem conditions into global development scenarios, as both effects and causes, is at the cutting edge of scenario analysis. The paper concludes by identifying directions for this research program and suggesting ways that the MA can contribute to this effort.","container-title":"Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1007/s10021-004-0074-2","ISSN":"1435-0629","issue":"2","page":"133–142","title":"Global Scenarios: Background Review for the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Raskin","given":"Paul D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which also contributed to a rapid expansion of national and local scenario studies, for example through the sub-global assessments of the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZAbW1ccZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lebel, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Lebel, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19355,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GJC94MKP"],"itemData":{"id":19355,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Ecosystems and Human Well-being (Volume 4): Multiscale assessments. Findings of the sub-global assessments working group of the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment","event-place":"Washington","language":"en","page":"229–259","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington","title":"Sub-global scenarios","author":[{"family":"Lebel","given":"L."},{"family":"Thongbai","given":"P."},{"family":"Kok","given":"K."}],"editor":[{"family":"Capistrano","given":"D."},{"family":"Samper","given":"C.K."},{"family":"Lee","given":"M.J."},{"family":"Rauseppe-Hearne","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      With this increase in use came an equally swift increase in the number of different methods employed to develop scenarios. An important dichotomy was the choice between qualitative and quantitative approaches. Recognizing their complementarity, Alcamo ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"n6PD20Cs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alcamo, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Alcamo, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19259,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JFKGZZL6"],"itemData":{"id":19259,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Environmental futures: the practice of environmental scenario analysis, Developments in Integrated Environmental Assessment","event-place":"Amsterdam","language":"en","page":"123–150","publisher":"Elsevier","publisher-place":"Amsterdam","title":"The SAS approach: combining qualitative and quantitative knowledge in environmental scenarios","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Alcamo","given":"J."}],"editor":[{"family":"Alcamo","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} described an approach to develop scenarios by combining and integrating qualitative stories and quantitative models. This Story-And-Simulation approach shows how stakeholders can be involved in a participatory process of storyline development, and how stories can be translated into model inputs and outputs that can be discussed with the stakeholders in an iterative procedure. A wide range of participatory methods have since been developed and used to engage stakeholders in the process of scenario development. In a landmark paper, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7xO7kvXN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Reed, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Reed, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19407,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F22QXEGJ"],"itemData":{"id":19407,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"uplands Journal of Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"345–362,","title":"Participatory scenario development for environmental management: A methodological framework illustrated with experience from the UK","volume":"128","author":[{"family":"Reed","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Kenter","given":"J."},{"family":"Bonn","given":"A."},{"family":"Broad","given":"K."},{"family":"Burt","given":"T.P."},{"family":"Fazey","given":"I.R."},{"family":"Fraser","given":"E.D.G."},{"family":"Hubacek","given":"K."},{"family":"Nainggolan","given":"D."},{"family":"Quinn","given":"C.H."},{"family":"L.C.","given":"F.Ravera"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} provide an overview of methods that have been employed, and present a methodological framework including all steps from defining the context and aims of the process to the actual co-production methods. First described by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eDeUSfHn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schwartz, 1991)","plainCitation":"(Schwartz, 1991)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19418,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J56NJKD9"],"itemData":{"id":19418,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: New York","title":"The Art of the Long View: Planning for the Future in an Uncertain World Doubleday","author":[{"family":"Schwartz","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the ‘intuitive logic’ or what has become known as the “2 x 2 matrix approach”, is a way to develop a set of four scenarios with stakeholders that has now been mainstreamed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"imTwVCuc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ramirez & Wilkinson, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Ramirez & Wilkinson, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19403,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WC3JVYEB"],"itemData":{"id":19403,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Technological Forecasting & Social Change","language":"pt","page":"254–264","title":"Rethinking the 2 × 2 scenario method: Grid or frames?","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Ramirez","given":"R."},{"family":"Wilkinson","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Likewise, target-seeking scenarios have been closely linked to co-production techniques ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"or4YnXu7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2016)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1072,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4G8W6EBN"],"itemData":{"id":1072,"type":"book","edition":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services, Bonn, Germany.","ISBN":"978-92-807-3569-7","number-of-pages":"348","title":"The methodological assessment report on scenarios and models of biodiversity and ecosystem services. S. Ferrier, K. N. Ninan, P. Leadley, R. Alkemade, L. A. Acosta, H. R. Akçakaya, L. Brotons, W. W. L. Cheung, V. Christensen, K. A. Harhash, J. Kabubo-Mariara, C. Lundquist, M. Obersteiner, H. M. Pereira, G. Peterson, R. Pichs-Madruga, N. Ravindranath, C. Rondinini and B. A. Wintle.","URL":"https://www.ipbes.net/sites/default/files/downloads/pdf/2016.methodological_assessment_report_scenarios_models.pdf","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the 1980s, the term ‘backcasting’ was coined ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ffQrdybB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. B. Robinson, 1982)","plainCitation":"(J. B. Robinson, 1982)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19409,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CZWXTIV8"],"itemData":{"id":19409,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy Policy","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"337–344","title":"Energy Backcasting: A Proposed Method of Policy Analysis","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"J.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1982"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, followed later  by descriptions of participatory backcasting methods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DyyvsUgh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Robinson, 2003)","plainCitation":"(J. Robinson, 2003)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19408,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HFZK8JGL"],"itemData":{"id":19408,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Futures","language":"pt","page":"839–856","title":"Future subjunctive: backcasting as social learning","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which is still being successfully applied ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Olk4Kqp2","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Vergragt & Quist, 2011; De Bruin, Kok, & Hoogstra-Klein, n.d.)","plainCitation":"(Vergragt & Quist, 2011; De Bruin, Kok, & Hoogstra-Klein, n.d.)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19445,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CLMAEBSZ"],"itemData":{"id":19445,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Technological Forecasting and Social Change","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"747–755","title":"Backcasting for sustainability: Introduction to the special issue","volume":"78","author":[{"family":"Vergragt","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Quist","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":19298,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JDTBPFJG"],"itemData":{"id":19298,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"269–282","title":"Exploring the potential of combining participative backcasting and exploratory scenarios for a robust (policy) strategy: Insights from a workshop for the Dutch forest sector","volume":"85","author":[{"family":"De Bruin","given":"J."},{"family":"Kok","given":"K."},{"family":"Hoogstra-Klein","given":"M.Submitted"}]}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other approaches that engage stakeholders in the process of developing target-seeking scenarios include transition management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ldO3cEMD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Loorbach & Rotmans, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Loorbach & Rotmans, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R7EXM7S8"],"itemData":{"id":19359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Futures","issue":"3","language":"fr","page":"237–246","title":"The practice of transition management: Examples and lessons from four distinct cases","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Loorbach","given":"D."},{"family":"Rotmans","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, visioning ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2GWXeHs2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(van der Helm, 2009)","plainCitation":"(van der Helm, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19603,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XDZYE99Y"],"itemData":{"id":19603,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Futures","DOI":"10.1016/j.futures.2008.07.036","ISSN":"00163287","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Futures","language":"en","page":"96-104","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The vision phenomenon: Towards a theoretical underpinning of visions of the future and the process of envisioning","title-short":"The vision phenomenon","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Helm","given":"Ruud","non-dropping-particle":"van der"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, or strategic niche management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aU6bFv5J","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schot & Geels, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Schot & Geels, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19416,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7ZWG2LNV"],"itemData":{"id":19416,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Technology Analysis & Strategic Management","DOI":"10.1080/09537320802292651.","language":"en","page":"537–554,","title":"Strategic niche management and sustainable innovation journeys: theory, findings, research agenda, and policy","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Schot","given":"J."},{"family":"Geels","given":"F.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Overall, a wide range of tools for scenario development exist, many of which can either be participatory or can be innovatively combined with a participatory component to answer different questions about the future. However, a gap persists in integrating these quantitative and qualitative methods at the global level ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EojN0tvJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pereira, Ternes, Nunes, & Giglio, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Pereira, Ternes, Nunes, & Giglio, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7210,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LPA4H43A"],"itemData":{"id":7210,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2020.108940","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"108940","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Overexploitation and behavioral changes of the largest South Atlantic parrotfish (Scarus trispinosus): Evidence from fishers' knowledge","title-short":"Overexploitation and behavioral changes of the largest South Atlantic parrotfish (Scarus trispinosus)","volume":"254","author":[{"family":"Pereira","given":"Pedro Henrique Cipresso"},{"family":"Ternes","given":"Maria Laura F."},{"family":"Nunes","given":"José Anchieta C.C."},{"family":"Giglio","given":"Vinicius J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Participatory scenarios have been applied to natural resource management and climate change mitigation as powerful, multi-scale processes. Some examples include integrated scenarios for: multi-scale stakeholder engagement ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pnGwkRYd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gramberger, Zellmer, Kok, & Metzger, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Gramberger, Zellmer, Kok, & Metzger, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19314,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7LEEU6Y2"],"itemData":{"id":19314,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Climatic Change","language":"en","page":"201–214","title":"Stakeholder Integrated Research (STIR): A new approach tested it in climate change adaptation research","volume":"128","author":[{"family":"Gramberger","given":"M."},{"family":"Zellmer","given":"K."},{"family":"Kok","given":"K."},{"family":"Metzger","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; qualitative stories ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aAG3Rrtp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pedde, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Pedde, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19394,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7DF8XSWM"],"itemData":{"id":19394,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-11241-240430.","issue":"4","language":"en","title":"Archetyping shared socioeconomic pathways across scales: an application to central Asia and European case studies","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-11241-240430.","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Pedde","given":"S."},{"family":"Kok","given":"K."},{"family":"Hölscher","given":"K."},{"family":"Oberlack","given":"C."},{"family":"Harrison","given":"P.A."},{"family":"Leemans","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; sectoral and integrated models (IAP; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2S7qCClQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harrison, Dunford, & Holman, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Harrison, Dunford, & Holman, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19323,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y8V63I9D"],"itemData":{"id":19323,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10113-018-1352-4","language":"en","page":"695–709","title":"Differences between low-end and high-end climate change impacts in Europe across multiple sectors","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Harrison","given":"P.A."},{"family":"Dunford","given":"R.W."},{"family":"Holman","given":"I.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; and exploratory ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rBknOSxs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. Kok, Pedde, Gramberger, Harrison, & Holman, 2019)","plainCitation":"(K. Kok, Pedde, Gramberger, Harrison, & Holman, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19351,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6D5QREUP"],"itemData":{"id":19351,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10113-018-1400-0.","language":"en","page":"643–654","title":"New European socio-economic scenarios for climate change research: operationalising concepts to extend the shared socio-economic pathways","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Kok","given":"K."},{"family":"Pedde","given":"S."},{"family":"Gramberger","given":"M."},{"family":"Harrison","given":"P.A."},{"family":"Holman","given":"I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and target-seeking scenarios ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tM3Z33g9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Frantzeskaki, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Frantzeskaki, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19308,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6LN5JTXT"],"itemData":{"id":19308,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10113-019-01475-x.","language":"en","page":"777–789","title":"Transition pathways to sustainability in greater than 2 °C climate futures of Europe","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Frantzeskaki","given":"N."},{"family":"Hölscher","given":"K."},{"family":"Holman","given":"I.P.Pedde"},{"family":"S.","given":"Jaeger"},{"family":"J.","given":"Kok"},{"family":"Harrison","given":"K."},{"literal":"P.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Figure 5.3 shows the sequence of events, the types of scenarios and type of stakeholder engagement that were undertaken in European Union-funded projects CLIMSAVE and IMPRESSIONS. Figure 5.4 exemplifies different products that were developed in a series of stakeholder workshops, preceded by interviews and interlaced with online questionnaires and email exchanges, where exploratory scenarios were developed and combined with pathways to identify sets of (robust) transformative solutions across scale. The effort convincingly demonstrates how using participatory methods in a co-creation process will not only yield qualitative products such as stories or cartoons but can also be used to determine model input and output. These products in turn are fundamental to discussions on target-seeking pathways and finding the most promising solutions, both for a single case study but in a multi-scale design, providing insights in other places or at another scale. 
      Within the biodiversity and broader sustainability scenario space, participatory scenario processes have been widely used at the local level ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7TP4sRsh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Oteros-Rozas, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Oteros-Rozas, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19391,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6WIWURIM"],"itemData":{"id":19391,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecol Soc","DOI":"10.5751/ES-07985-200432","language":"en","title":"Participatory scenario planning in place-based social-ecological research: Insights and experiences from 23 case studies","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Oteros-Rozas","given":"E."},{"family":"Martín-López","given":"B."},{"family":"Daw","given":"T.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An attempt to collect these social-ecological scenarios into a database is now underway (https://www.biospherefutures.net/). One of the biggest benefits of participatory processes in co-creating futures with stakeholders is the ability to engage the imagination; something that has largely been lacking in global-level scenario processes, especially those used in assessments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a11chc0358","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. M. Pereira, 2020)","plainCitation":"(L. M. Pereira, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/532LHIG3"],"itemData":{"id":19100,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"People and Nature","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"1172–1195","title":"Developing multiscale and integrative nature–people scenarios using the Nature Futures Framework","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Pereira","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Davies","given":"K.K."},{"family":"Belder","given":"E."},{"family":"Ferrier","given":"S."},{"family":"Karlsson-Vinkhuyzen","given":"S."},{"family":"Kim","given":"H."},{"family":"Kuiper","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Okayasu","given":"S."},{"family":"Palomo","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"H.M."},{"family":"Peterson","given":"G."},{"family":"Sathyapalan","given":"J."},{"family":"Schoolenberg","given":"M."},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"R."},{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"S.Carvalho"},{"family":"Greenaway","given":"A."},{"family":"Hauck","given":"J."},{"family":"King","given":"N."},{"family":"Lazarova","given":"T."},{"family":"Ravera","given":"F."},{"family":"Chettri","given":"N."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W.L."},{"family":"Hendriks","given":"R.J.J."},{"family":"Kolomytsev","given":"G."},{"family":"Leadley","given":"P."},{"family":"Metzger","given":"J.-P."},{"family":"Ninan","given":"K.N."},{"family":"Pichs","given":"R."},{"family":"Popp","given":"A."},{"family":"Rondinini","given":"C."},{"family":"Rosa","given":"I."},{"family":"Vuuren","given":"D."},{"family":"Lundquist","given":"C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Following the methodological assessment of scenarios ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qMXkWeQm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2016)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1072,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4G8W6EBN"],"itemData":{"id":1072,"type":"book","edition":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services, Bonn, Germany.","ISBN":"978-92-807-3569-7","number-of-pages":"348","title":"The methodological assessment report on scenarios and models of biodiversity and ecosystem services. S. Ferrier, K. N. Ninan, P. Leadley, R. Alkemade, L. A. Acosta, H. R. Akçakaya, L. Brotons, W. W. L. Cheung, V. Christensen, K. A. Harhash, J. Kabubo-Mariara, C. Lundquist, M. Obersteiner, H. M. Pereira, G. Peterson, R. Pichs-Madruga, N. Ravindranath, C. Rondinini and B. A. Wintle.","URL":"https://www.ipbes.net/sites/default/files/downloads/pdf/2016.methodological_assessment_report_scenarios_models.pdf","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the IPBES former expert group on scenarios and models undertook to stimulate the development of new global scenarios that put nature at the center of the story ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bTFSSTnC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rosa, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Rosa, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2713,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BPKIDHC7"],"itemData":{"id":2713,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Targets for human development are increasingly connected with targets for nature, however, existing scenarios do not explicitly address this relationship. Here, we outline a strategy to generate scenarios centred on our relationship with nature to inform decision-making at multiple scales.","container-title":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1038/s41559-017-0273-9","ISSN":"2397-334X","issue":"10","page":"1416–1419","title":"Multiscale scenarios for nature futures","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Rosa","given":"Isabel M D"},{"family":"Pereira","given":"Henrique M"},{"family":"Ferrier","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Acosta","given":"Lilibeth A"},{"family":"Akcakaya","given":"H Resit"},{"family":"Belder","given":"Eefje","non-dropping-particle":"den"},{"family":"Fazel","given":"Asghar M"},{"family":"Fujimori","given":"Shinichiro"},{"family":"Harfoot","given":"Mike"},{"family":"Harhash","given":"Khaled A"},{"family":"Harrison","given":"Paula A"},{"family":"Hauck","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Hendriks","given":"Rob J J"},{"family":"Hernández","given":"Gladys"},{"family":"Jetz","given":"Walter"},{"family":"Karlsson-Vinkhuyzen","given":"Sylvia I"},{"family":"Kim","given":"HyeJin"},{"family":"King","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Kok","given":"Marcel T J"},{"family":"Kolomytsev","given":"Grygoriy O"},{"family":"Lazarova","given":"Tanya"},{"family":"Leadley","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Lundquist","given":"Carolyn J"},{"family":"García Márquez","given":"Jaime"},{"family":"Meyer","given":"Carsten"},{"family":"Navarro","given":"Laetitia M"},{"family":"Nesshöver","given":"Carsten"},{"family":"Ngo","given":"Hien T"},{"family":"Ninan","given":"Karachepone N"},{"family":"Palomo","given":"Maria G"},{"family":"Pereira","given":"Laura M"},{"family":"Peterson","given":"Garry D"},{"family":"Pichs","given":"Ramon"},{"family":"Popp","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Purvis","given":"Andy"},{"family":"Ravera","given":"Federica"},{"family":"Rondinini","given":"Carlo"},{"family":"Sathyapalan","given":"Jyothis"},{"family":"Schipper","given":"Aafke M"},{"family":"Seppelt","given":"Ralf"},{"family":"Settele","given":"Josef"},{"family":"Sitas","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Vuuren","given":"Detlef","non-dropping-particle":"van"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The culmination of this process, following a participatory visioning process, has been the development of the Nature Futures Framework ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yzrg1LUv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. M. Pereira, 2020)","plainCitation":"(L. M. Pereira, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/532LHIG3"],"itemData":{"id":19100,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"People and Nature","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"1172–1195","title":"Developing multiscale and integrative nature–people scenarios using the Nature Futures Framework","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Pereira","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Davies","given":"K.K."},{"family":"Belder","given":"E."},{"family":"Ferrier","given":"S."},{"family":"Karlsson-Vinkhuyzen","given":"S."},{"family":"Kim","given":"H."},{"family":"Kuiper","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Okayasu","given":"S."},{"family":"Palomo","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"H.M."},{"family":"Peterson","given":"G."},{"family":"Sathyapalan","given":"J."},{"family":"Schoolenberg","given":"M."},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"R."},{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"S.Carvalho"},{"family":"Greenaway","given":"A."},{"family":"Hauck","given":"J."},{"family":"King","given":"N."},{"family":"Lazarova","given":"T."},{"family":"Ravera","given":"F."},{"family":"Chettri","given":"N."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W.L."},{"family":"Hendriks","given":"R.J.J."},{"family":"Kolomytsev","given":"G."},{"family":"Leadley","given":"P."},{"family":"Metzger","given":"J.-P."},{"family":"Ninan","given":"K.N."},{"family":"Pichs","given":"R."},{"family":"Popp","given":"A."},{"family":"Rondinini","given":"C."},{"family":"Rosa","given":"I."},{"family":"Vuuren","given":"D."},{"family":"Lundquist","given":"C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; Fig. 5.6) that has the participation of diverse stakeholders at its core.   Figure 5.3. Scenario development and type of stakeholder engagement undertaken in European Union-funded projects CLIMSAVE and IMPRESSIONS. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AWHI1TTM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tabara, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Tabara, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19427,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7VTGAXV3"],"itemData":{"id":19427,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su10010161","language":"en","page":"161","title":"Exploring Institutional Transformations to Address High-End Climate Change in Iberia","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Tabara","given":"J.D."},{"family":"Cots","given":"F."},{"family":"Pedde","given":"S."},{"family":"Hölscher","given":"K."},{"family":"Kok","given":"K."},{"family":"Lovanova","given":"A."},{"family":"Capela Lourenço","given":"T."},{"family":"Frantzeskaki","given":"N."},{"family":"Etherington","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY-NC-ND 4.0.Figure 5.4. Sequence of events, the types of scenarios and type of stakeholder engagement in EU-funded projects CLIMSAVE and IMPRESSIONS. Source: CLIMSAVE (http://www.climsave.eu), CLIMSAVE IAP (http://www.impressions-project.eu); illustration by © Talitha Dijkhuizen. License CC BY. 

5.2.3 How scenarios might be used in decision making under uncertain conditions
Summarizing the previous sections, scenarios are an excellent tool to use when the system under study is complex and its future changes therefore uncertain. Scenarios come in many shapes and forms and can address fundamentally different questions that directly or indirectly speak to decision makers. “What should happen?” is perhaps more often posed by decision makers, and target-seeking or other intervention scenarios can help answering it. “What could happen?” seems to bear less direct relevance to decision-makers but is often an essential first step to map out the ‘uncertainty space’, providing insights into changes beyond the control of the decision maker, which will influence the solutions required. Scenarios can thus help to facilitate the process of identifying actions that need to be taken, given an uncertain future outlook that is continuously changing. A large diversity of concepts, methods, and tools can assist this process. It is not a matter of wondering if scenarios are a good tool to use, but a matter of how scenarios might be best used in decision making to help identify the actions that can be taken to move towards more sustainable use of wild species. 

""" ;
