    ipbes:hasDescription """

Use of wild species varies across space and over time. While use of wild species is often addressed based on local case studies, a global overview on status and trends of wild species use is lacking. In order to provide this global overview, a search was conducted across different global organization websites to select available datasets and global estimates on wild species use (3.2.1) (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Indicators for sustainable use of wild species were selected based on criteria in the scoping document and Chapter 1; this process is outlined in section 3.2.2. From a high diversity of indicators available (see chapter 2), this section focuses on (i) indicators selected by IPBES experts in the context of the global and regional assessments, (ii) Sustainable Development Goals indicators by the United Nations and (iii) the Aichi Biodiversity Target indicators by the Convention on Biological Diversity, particularly focusing on indicators within the theme “sustainable use” and “species”. In addition to searching data and indicators across different institution websites, a literature search was completed in “Google” and “Science Direct” using the following keywords: “wild species” AND “use” AND “indicators” OR “indices” OR “indexes” (accessed in June 2020). Data sources and indicators suggested by reviewers during internal and external reviews were also reviewed and considered. Following the section indicators which focuses on spatial scales and distribution, we include a special section on the importance of the temporal scale in relation to use of wild species (3.2.3). Finally, this we explore the relative importance of different contexts in which wild species are used both for subsistence and trade (3.2.4).

3.2.1. Datasets available and global estimates of wild species used

Estimates on the number of wild species used by humans across the different regions of the globe are scarce and scattered amongst different datasets and organizations. The review of datasets presented below show that there is an uneven distribution of data available across the world documenting the number of wild species and their direct uses by humans. Most of the global datasets reviewed predominantly register and document use of wild species in the Northern Hemisphere (Figures 3.1 to 3.3).  However, evidence suggests that the greater part of global biodiversity occurs in the tropical and subtropical regions, and in many of these countries local communities depend heavily on direct use of natural resources.   
      Uses are dynamic and change over time. Traditional knowledge and skills as well as science and technology continue to develop novel techniques and adapt to changing uses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2b3gi0j81","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kersey, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Kersey, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30362,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GPR5J2CG"],"itemData":{"id":30362,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLANTS, PEOPLE, PLANET","DOI":"10.1002/ppp3.10136","ISSN":"2572-2611, 2572-2611","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Plants People Planet","language":"en","page":"409-420","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Selecting for useful properties of plants and fungi – Novel approaches, opportunities, and challenges","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ppp3.10136","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Kersey","given":"Paul J."},{"family":"Collemare","given":"Jerome"},{"family":"Cockel","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Das","given":"Dibakar"},{"family":"Dulloo","given":"Ehsan M."},{"family":"Kelly","given":"Laura J."},{"family":"Lettice","given":"Eoin"},{"family":"Malécot","given":"Valery"},{"family":"Maxted","given":"Nigel"},{"family":"Metheringham","given":"Carey"},{"family":"Thormann","given":"Imke"},{"family":"Leitch","given":"Ilia J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The evolving relationships between wild species use and associated knowledge/skills, together with the development of science and technology, drives the creation of novel economies surrounding to and associated with the use of wild species.  Unfortunately, the review shows that although traditional and scientific knowledge often highlight that one wild species can have many uses (e.g., food, raw material, cultural expression, etc.), and provide a range of nature’s contributions to people (NCP) benefits, the datasets reviewed generally focus on a single use category for a single species.  
       Table 3.1 summarizes key estimates in order to provide an overview of the total number of wild species and their uses across different taxa and practices of use. The practices are further analyzed in the following sections (3.3.1 to 3.3.5). 
Table 3.1 Number of species and their uses by practice. The table shows estimates from different sources. Only estimates corroborated by two or more sources are included. Sources: Flora of China (FOC), The Plant List (TPL), World Flora Online (World Flora Online), State of the World's Plants 2016 (SOTWP-2016), State of the World's Plants and Fungi 2020 (SOTWPF-2020), State of the World’s Fungi 2018 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"arml1t3tlv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Willis, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Willis, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30357,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MX282NHI"],"itemData":{"id":30357,"type":"book","event-place":"Richmond, UK","publisher":"Kew Publishing","publisher-place":"Richmond, UK","title":"State of the world's fungi 2018. Report.","author":[{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."}],"editor":[{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO), Butchart, (2008), Global Tree Assessment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1st7jciima","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Global Tree Assessment, 2020, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Global Tree Assessment, 2020, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26778,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N9G2NZK5","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AJ86J3M5"],"itemData":{"id":26778,"type":"report","title":"State of the World’s Trees Report","URL":"https://www.bgci.org/wp/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/FINAL-GTAReportMedRes-1.pdf","author":[{"family":"Global Tree Assessment","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":30340,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QXEN772N"],"itemData":{"id":30340,"type":"report","title":"State of the World’s Trees Report","URL":"https://www.globaltreeassessment.org/progress/%20Rivers,%202020)","author":[{"literal":"Global Tree Assessment"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qVmpdoNC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Balmford, 2015; WTTC, 2019a)","plainCitation":"(Balmford, 2015; WTTC, 2019a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQW7BDCB"],"itemData":{"id":30359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS Biol","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.1002074","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e1002074","title":"Walk on the wild side: estimating the global magnitude of visits to protected areas","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Green","given":"Jonathan MH"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Beresford","given":"James"},{"family":"Huang","given":"Charles"},{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Walpole","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Manica","given":"Andrea"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":482,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K9XD2NW5"],"itemData":{"id":482,"type":"report","publisher":"World Travel & Tourism Council","title":"The economic impact of global wildlife tourism: Travel & tourism as an economic tool for the protection of wildlife","URL":"https://wttc.org/Portals/0/Documents/Reports/2019/Sustainable%20Growth-Economic%20Impact%20of%20Global%20Wildlife%20Tourism-Aug%202019.pdf?ver=2021-02-25-182802-167","author":[{"family":"WTTC","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
