-Approximately 5,600 terrestrial bird, mammal, amphibian, and squamate reptile species are used and traded globally ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"V7AtlGXc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Scheffers, Oliveira, Lamb, & Edwards, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Scheffers, Oliveira, Lamb, & Edwards, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30343,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5HSGZWNZ"],"itemData":{"id":30343,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aav5327","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6461","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"71-76","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Global wildlife trade across the tree of life","URL":"https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aav5327","volume":"366","author":[{"family":"Scheffers","given":"Brett R."},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"Brunno F."},{"family":"Lamb","given":"Ieuan"},{"family":"Edwards","given":"David P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}
-Approximately 2,000 species of invertebrates, amphibians, fish, reptiles, birds and mammals are used as wild meat across the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a28a329rpr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Coad, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Coad, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":327,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/97LTS7HN"],"itemData":{"id":327,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","ISBN":"978-602-387-083-7","note":"DOI: 10.17528/cifor/007046","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","title":"Towards a sustainable, participatory and inclusive wild meat sector","URL":"https://www.cifor.org/online-library/browse/view-publication/publication/7046.html","author":[{"family":"Coad","given":"L"},{"family":"Fa","given":"JE"},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"K"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"N","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Santamaria","given":"C"},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"D"},{"family":"El Bizri","given":"HR"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"DJ"},{"family":"Cawthorn","given":"DM"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}
-4,561 birds are used for food or as pets  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1k862f21k9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Butchart, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Butchart, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32360,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LFV76C7F"],"itemData":{"id":32360,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            After habitat loss and degradation, the leading threats to biodiversity are over-exploitation and invasive alien species. For birds, newly synthesised data using the standard classification schemes for utilisation and threat types for the IUCN Red List allow novel analyses on the importance of these threats and permit the calculation of Red List Indices (RLIs) to show trends in the status of birds driven by these factors. At least 45.7% of extant bird species (4,561 species) are used by humans, principally for pets (37.0%) and for hunting for food (14.2%), but other uses include sport hunting, ornamentation and traditional medicine. Much of this use drives trade at an international scale, involving at least 3,337 species (33.9%, substantially higher than previous estimates), mostly for the pet trade. RLIs show that although successful control and management of use and trade have led to some species improving in status, this has been outweighed by the number of species deteriorating in status owing to unsustainable exploitation. Overall, the RLI showing trends in extinction risk driven by issues related to use shows a negative slope: human use of birds is currently unsustainable. Similarly, and of relevance to the Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species (CITES), the RLI for internationally traded bird species showing trends in extinction risk driven by issues related to international trade is also declining: international trade remains a threat to the world's birds. Invasive species impact at least one third of the world's threatened bird species (398 species, 32.6%), with mammals being the most important (impacting 81.1%), particularly through predation by carnivores and rodents. The RLI illustrating impacts of invasive species shows that they are driving a deterioration in the status of the world's birds. RLIs for the impacts of use and invasive species will be important indicators to help track progress towards the target of significantly reducing biodiversity loss by 2010.","container-title":"Bird Conservation International","DOI":"10.1017/S095927090800035X","ISSN":"0959-2709, 1474-0001","issue":"S1","journalAbbreviation":"Bird Conservation International","language":"en","page":"S245-S262","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Red List Indices to measure the sustainability of species use and impacts of invasive alien species","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S095927090800035X/type/journal_article","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}
-Over 300 mammal species are used for hunting (ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2813vghqvc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Alves, 2012; Chapman & Peres, 2001; Dirzo, 2014; IUCN, 2016; W. J. Ripple, 2014; William J. Ripple, 2016)","plainCitation":"(R. Alves, 2012; Chapman & Peres, 2001; Dirzo, 2014; IUCN, 2016; W. J. Ripple, 2014; William J. Ripple, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32719,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/56855GSE"],"itemData":{"id":32719,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Faunal resources have played a wide range of roles in human life from the earliest days of recorded history. In addition to their utilitarian importance, animals have been recognized in religion, art, music and literature and other different cultural manifestations of mankind. Animals uses and the means by which they are exploited by humans, as well as the cultural aspects which conduct interactions between people and animals, are implicated in a lower or higher pressure on populations of exploited species, reflected in either their sustainable use or even lead to their extinction. The variety of interactions between humans and animals is the subject matter of ethnozoology - the branch of ethnobiology that investigates the knowledge human societies have accumulated concerning animals, as well as their significance to those people and their uses. Ethnozoological studies can be a valuable asset to increase our understanding of the cultural, economic, social, and traditional roles of played by animals. In this context, they have a central role in conservation and management. This work provides a review on the main forms of interactions between humans and the fauna along history, and their ecological implications, and discusses the role of the ethnozoology in animal conservation.","container-title":"Ethnobiology and Conservation","DOI":"10.15451/ec2012-8-1.2-1-69","journalAbbreviation":"Ethnobiology and Conservation","page":"1-69","source":"ResearchGate","title":"Relationship between fauna and people and the role of ethnozoology in animal conservation","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Alves","given":"Rômulo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",8,20]]}}},{"id":30371,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JMHL3DTU"],"itemData":{"id":30371,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Evolutionary Anthropology: Issues, News, and Reviews","DOI":"10.1002/1520-6505(2001)10:1<16::AID-EVAN1010>3.0.CO;2-O","ISSN":"1520-6505","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2001 John Wiley & Sons, Inc.","page":"16-33","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Primate conservation in the new millennium: The role of scientists","title-short":"Primate conservation in the new millennium","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/1520-6505%282001%2910%3A1%3C16%3A%3AAID-EVAN1010%3E3.0.CO%3B2-O","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Chapman","given":"Colin A."},{"family":"Peres","given":"Carlos A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":33046,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8GHEY4CS"],"itemData":{"id":33046,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1251817","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6195","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"401-406","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Defaunation in the Anthropocene","URL":"http://www.sciencemag.org/cgi/doi/10.1126/science.1251817","volume":"345","author":[{"family":"Dirzo","given":"R."},{"family":"Young","given":"H. S."},{"family":"Galetti","given":"M."},{"family":"Ceballos","given":"G."},{"family":"Isaac","given":"N. J. B."},{"family":"Collen","given":"B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",7,25]]}}},{"id":30369,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NP7U3IRZ"],"itemData":{"id":30369,"type":"report","abstract":"Trophy hunting is hunting of animals with specific desired characteristics (such as large antlers), and overlaps with widely practiced hunting for meat. ...","publisher":"IUCN","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Informing decisions on trophy hunting: A briefing paper for European Union decision-makers regarding potential plans for restriction of imports of hunting trophies","title-short":"Informing decisions on trophy hunting","URL":"https://www.iucn.org/sites/dev/files/iucn_sept_briefing_paper_-_informingdecisionstrophyhunting.pdf","author":[{"literal":"IUCN"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33015,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4QZYP2I7"],"itemData":{"id":33015,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1241484","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6167","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"1241484-1241484","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Status and Ecological Effects of the World's Largest Carnivores","URL":"http://www.sciencemag.org/cgi/doi/10.1126/science.1241484","volume":"343","author":[{"family":"Ripple","given":"W. J."},{"family":"Estes","given":"J. A."},{"family":"Beschta","given":"R. L."},{"family":"Wilmers","given":"C. C."},{"family":"Ritchie","given":"E. G."},{"family":"Hebblewhite","given":"M."},{"family":"Berger","given":"J."},{"family":"Elmhagen","given":"B."},{"family":"Letnic","given":"M."},{"family":"Nelson","given":"M. P."},{"family":"Schmitz","given":"O. J."},{"family":"Smith","given":"D. W."},{"family":"Wallach","given":"A. D."},{"family":"Wirsing","given":"A. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",1,10]]}}},{"id":32514,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/99SRFGYK"],"itemData":{"id":32514,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Terrestrial mammals are experiencing a massive collapse in their population sizes and geographical ranges around the world, but many of the drivers, patterns and consequences of this decline remain poorly understood. Here we provide an analysis showing that bushmeat hunting for mostly food and medicinal products is driving a global crisis whereby 301 terrestrial mammal species are threatened with extinction. Nearly all of these threatened species occur in developing countries where major coexisting threats include deforestation, agricultural expansion, human encroachment and competition with livestock. The unrelenting decline of mammals suggests many vital ecological and socio-economic services that these species provide will be lost, potentially changing ecosystems irrevocably. We discuss options and current obstacles to achieving effective conservation, alongside consequences of failure to stem such anthropogenic mammalian extirpation. We propose a multi-pronged conservation strategy to help save threatened mammals from immediate extinction and avoid a collapse of food security for hundreds of millions of people.","container-title":"Royal Society Open Science","DOI":"10.1098/rsos.160498","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Royal Society Open Science","note":"publisher: Royal Society","page":"160498","source":"royalsocietypublishing.org (Atypon)","title":"Bushmeat hunting and extinction risk to the world's mammals","URL":"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/full/10.1098/rsos.160498","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Ripple","given":"William J."},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"Katharine"},{"family":"Betts","given":"Matthew G."},{"family":"Chapron","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Dirzo","given":"Rodolfo"},{"family":"Galetti","given":"Mauro"},{"family":"Levi","given":"Taal"},{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter A."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."},{"family":"Machovina","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Newsome","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Peres","given":"Carlos A."},{"family":"Wallach","given":"Arian D."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Young","given":"Hillary"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}),
