tability of Local Resource Management Practices Based on Supernatural Enforcement Mechanisms in the Local Social-cultural Context","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/26269203","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Sasaoka","given":"Masatoshi"},{"family":"Laumonier","given":"Yves"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",12]]}}},{"id":855,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5ZBRSF3D"],"itemData":{"id":855,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Indigenous Peoples’ food systems & well-being: interventions & policies for healthy communities","event-place":"Rome, Italy and Montreal, Quebec","page":"23-38","publisher-place":"Rome, Italy and Montreal, Quebec","title":"Global Environmental Challenges to the integrity of Indigenous Peoples’ Food Systems","author":[{"family":"Turner","given":"Nancy"},{"family":"Plotkin","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Kuhnlein","given":"Harriet V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and other requirements for human wellbeing is receiving increasing global attention.
      There is consistent and substantial evidence that use of wild species has in many cases occurred at unsustainable levels and exceeded the populations’ capacities to recover ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cc1HsCu3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019d; Vignieri, 2014)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019d; Vignieri, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":1449,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HFGZ6ZT4"],"itemData":{"id":1449,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"[Special section on vanishing fauna][1] During the Pleistocene epoch, only tens of thousands of years ago, our planet supported large, spectacular animals. Mammoths, terror birds, giant tortoises, and saber-toothed cats, as well as many less familiar species such as giant ground sloths (some of which reached 7 meters in height) and glyptodonts (which resembled car-sized armadillos), roamed freely. Since then, however, the number and diversity of animal species on Earth have consistently and steadily declined. Today we are left with a relatively depauperate fauna, and we continue to lose animal species to extinction rapidly. Although some debate persists, most of the evidence suggests that humans were responsible for extinction of this Pleistocene fauna, and we continue to drive animal extinctions today through the destruction of wild lands, consumption of animals as a resource or a luxury, and persecution of species we see as threats or competitors. ![Figure][2]\\\\textless/img\\\\textgreater Animals are rapidly disappearing from forests in Borneo and across the world. PHOTO: CHRISTIAN ZIEGLER Such global loss of animal species, or defaunation, is increasingly recognized as a problem akin to deforestation in terms of scale and impact. Though for emotional or aesthetic reasons we may lament the loss of large charismatic species, such as tigers, rhinos, and pandas, we now know that loss of animals, from the largest elephant to the smallest beetle, will also fundamentally alter the form and function of the ecosystems upon which we all depend (see Dirzo , p. [401][3]). Identifying the drivers of these extinctions is straightforward, but stemming the loss is a daunting challenge. Animal species continue to decline in, and disappear from, even large, long-protected reserves, due both to direct impacts, such as poaching, and indirect ecological feedbacks, such as habitat fragmentation. Though hunting and poaching might seem obvious candidates for targeted policy and management interventions, there are complex social issues underlying these activities that will require coordinated and cooperative actions by nations (see Brashares , p. [376][4]). ![Figure][2]\\\\textless/img\\\\textgreater Twilight for animals Large numbers of animal species face extinction in Southeast Asia, the Amazon, and the Andes, as shown in this map of mammals, amphibians, and birds. Animals also face high rates of extinction in Europe and North America, where fewer species are found overall. MAP: FÉLIX PHARAND-DESCHÊNES (VISUALIZATION), CLINTON JENKINS (DATA PROCESSING), IUCN/BIRDLIFE INTERNATIONAL (DATA) While stemming this loss remains a challenging goal, attempts to reverse the extinction trend are increasing. Such “refaunation” efforts involve a variety of approaches, including breeding animals in captivity, with the hope of reintroducing them to the wild, and assisting recolonization of areas where species have become locally extinct (see Seddon , p. [406][5]). Active reversal of animal extinctions is proving just as challenging as preventing extinctions in the first place, but a few success stories provide some hope. Many note and mourn the loss of animals but have not recognized that the impacts of this loss go beyond an aesthetic and emotional need to maintain animals as a part of nature. Current research reveals startling rates of animal declines and extinctions and confirms the importance of these species to ecosystems (see Stokstad, p. [396][6]). Further, and more broadly, it suggests that if we are unable to end or reverse the rate of their loss, it will mean more for our own future than a broken heart or an empty forest. [1]: http://www.sciencemag.org/site/special/vanishing/index.xhtml [2]: pending:yes [3]: /lookup/volpage/345/401?iss=6195 [4]: /lookup/volpage/345/376?iss=6195 [5]: /lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1251818 [6]: /lookup/doi/10.1126/science.345.6195.396","container-title":"Science (New York, N.Y.)","DOI":"10.1126/science.345.6195.392","ISSN":"1095-9203","issue":"6195","note":"PMID: 25061199","page":"392–5","title":"Vanishing fauna. Introduction.","URL":"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/25061199","volume":"345","author":[{"family":"Vignieri","given":"Sacha"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Declines in a wide range of taxa (both plants and animals), due to non-sustainable use of wild species, have been recorded across marine ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ekdT5eXD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020b; Lam & Sadovy de Mitcheson, 2011; Pacoureau, 2021; Worm, 2009)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020b; Lam & Sadovy de Mitcheson, 2011; Pacoureau, 2021; Worm, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":1422,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UAY6LMNN"],"itemData":{"id":1422,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-2979.2010.00383.x","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"51-74","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The sharks of South East Asia - unknown, unmonitored and unmanaged: Sharks of South East Asia","title-short":"The sharks of South East Asia - unknown, unmonitored and unmanaged","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1467-2979.2010.00383.x","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Lam","given":"Vivian Y Y"},{"family":"Sadovy de Mitcheson","given":"Yvonne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",3]]}}},{"id":31564,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KZDH9PEF"],"itemData":{"id":31564,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-020-03173-9","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7843","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"567-571","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Half a century of global decline in oceanic sharks and rays","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-03173-9","volume":"589","author":[{"family":"Pacoureau","given":"Nathan"},{"family":"Rigby","given":"Cassandra L."},{"family":"Kyne","given":"Peter M."},{"family":"Sherley","given":"Richard B."},{"family":"Winker","given":"Henning"},{"family":"Carlson","given":"John K."},{"family":"Fordham","given":"Sonja V."},{"family":"Barreto","given":"Rodrigo"},{"family":"Fernando","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Francis","given":"Malcolm P."},{"family":"Jabado","given":"Rima W."},{"family":"Herman","given":"Katelyn B."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Kwang-Ming"},{"family":"Marshall","given":"Andrea D."},{"family":"Pollom","given":"Riley A."},{"family":"Romanov","given":"Evgeny V."},{"family":"Simpfendorfer","given":"Colin A."},{"family":"Yin","given":"Jamie S."},{"family":"Kindsvater","given":"Holly K."},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,28]]}}},{"id":326,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CEWYIKU4"],"itemData":{"id":326,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"After a long history of overexploitation, increasing efforts to restore marine ecosystems and rebuild fisheries are under way. Here, we analyze current trends from a fisheries and conservation perspective. In 5 of 10 well-studied ecosystems, the average exploitation rate has recently declined and is now at or below the rate predicted to achieve maximum sustainable yield for seven systems. Yet 63% of assessed fish stocks worldwide still require rebuilding, and even lower exploitation rates are needed to reverse the collapse of vulnerable species. Combined fisheries and conservation objectives can be achieved by merging diverse management actions, including catch restrictions, gear modification, and closed areas, depending on local context. Impacts of international fleets and the lack of alternatives to fishing complicate prospects for rebuilding fisheries in many poorer regions, highlighting the need for a global perspective on rebuilding marine resources.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1173146","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5940","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"578-585","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Rebuilding Global Fisheries","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1173146","volume":"325","author":[{"family":"Worm","given":"Boris"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Baum","given":"Julia K."},{"family":"Branch","given":"Trevor A."},{"family":"Collie","given":"Jeremy S."},{"family":"Costello","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Fogarty","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Fulton","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Hutchings","given":"Jeffrey A."},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Olaf P."},{"family":"Lotze","given":"Heike K."},{"family":"Mace","given":"Pamela M."},{"family":"McClanahan","given":"Tim R."},{"family":"Minto","given":"Cóilín"},{"family":"Palumbi","given":"Stephen R."},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Ricard","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Rosenberg","given":"Andrew A."},{"family":"Watson","given":"Reg"},{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",7,31]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, inland waters ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NUr1I16k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Allan, 2005; FAO, 2020b; Fluet-Chouinard, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Allan, 2005; FAO, 2020b; Fluet-Chouinard, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31099,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WXDUK6AI"],"itemData":{"id":31099,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Inland waters have received only slight consideration in recent discussions of the global fisheries crisis, even though inland fisheries provide much-needed protein, jobs, and income, especially in poor rural communities of developing countries. Systematic overfishing of fresh waters is largely unrecognized because of weak reporting and because fishery declines take place within a complex of other pressures. Moreover, the ecosystem consequences of changes to the species, size, and trophic composition of fish assemblages are poorly understood. These complexities underlie the paradox that overexploitation of a fishery may not be marked by declines in total yield, even when individual species and long-term sustainability are highly threatened. Indeed, one of the symptoms of intense fishing in inland waters is the collapse of particular stocks even as overall fish production rises—a biodiversity crisis more than a fisheries crisis.","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1641/0006-3568(2005)055[1041:OOIW]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0006-3568","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"BioScience","page":"1041-1051","source":"Silverchair","title":"Overfishing of Inland Waters","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1641/0006-3568(2005)055[1041:OOIW]2.0.CO;2","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Allan","given":"J. David"},{"family":"Abell","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Hogan","given":"Zeb"},{"family":"Revenga","given":"Carmen"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Brad W."},{"family":"Welcomme","given":"Robin L."},{"family":"Winemiller","given":"Kirk"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",12,1]]}}},{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":1444,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H2VSGMMM"],"itemData":{"id":1444,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Consumption of wild-caught freshwater fish is concentrated in low-income countries, where it makes a critical contribution to food security and livelihoods. Underestimation of inland harvests in official statistics has long been suspected due to unmonitored subsistence fisheries. To overcome the lack of data from extensive small-scale harvests, we used household consumption surveys to estimate freshwater fish catches in 42 low- and middle-income countries between 1997 and 2014. After accounting for trade and aquaculture, these countries collectively consumed 3.6 MT (CI, 1.5–5.8) more wild-caught freshwater fish than officially reported, reflecting a net underreporting of 64.8% (CI, 27.1–103.9%). Individual countries were more likely to underestimate (n = 31) than overestimate (n = 11) catches, despite conservative assumptions in our calculations. Extrapolating our findings suggests that the global inland catch reported as 10.3 MT in 2008 was more likely 16.6 MT (CI, 2.3–30.9), which accords with recent independent predictions for rivers and lakes. In human terms, these hidden harvests are equivalent to the total animal protein consumption of 36.9 (CI, 30.8–43.4) million people, including many who rely upon wild fish to achieve even minimal protein intake. The widespread underreporting uncovered by household consumption surveys indicates that inland fisheries contribute far more to global food security than has been recognized previously. Our findings also amplify concerns about the sustainability of intensive fishery exploitation as degradation of rivers, lakes, and wetlands continues apace.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1721097115","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"29","journalAbbreviation":"PNAS","language":"en","license":"© 2018 . http://www.pnas.org/site/aboutpnas/licenses.xhtmlPublished under the PNAS license.","note":"PMID: 29915069","page":"7623-7628","source":"www.pnas.org","title":"Global hidden harvest of freshwater fish revealed by household surveys","URL":"https://www.pnas.org/content/115/29/7623","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Fluet-Chouinard","given":"Etienne"},{"family":"Funge-Smith","given":"Simon"},{"family":"McIntyre","given":"Peter B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and terrestrial ecosystems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UnBBRiTh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Coad, 2019; Fa, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Coad, 2019; Fa, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":327,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/97LTS7HN"],"itemData":{"id":327,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","ISBN":"978-602-387-083-7","note":"DOI: 10.17528/cifor/007046","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","title":"Towards a sustainable, participatory and inclusive wild meat sector","URL":"https://www.cifor.org/online-library/browse/view-publication/publication/7046.html","author":[{"family":"Coad","given":"L"},{"family":"Fa","given":"JE"},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"K"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"N","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Santamaria","given":"C"},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"D"},{"family":"El Bizri","given":"HR"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"DJ"},{"family":"Cawthorn","given":"DM"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":1435,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CXF8R89Z"],"itemData":{"id":1435,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wild meat harvests in African moist forests are presumed to exceed production, even in the case of traditional societies still using rudimentary hunting methods. Though some approximations do exist of the volume of bushmeat harvested in some Central African moist forest areas, estimates based on extensive and simultaneous sampling, within a large geographical region, are not available. Here, we present the results of the ?rst reported study of this kind. During a period of 5 month, we counted bushmeat carcasses deposited in 89 urban and rural markets in a 35,000 km2 area between the Cross River in Nigeria and the Sanaga River in Cameroon. We used these data to calculate annual bushmeat volume traded by site, species and overall in the study area.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2005.11.031","ISSN":"00063207","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"497-510","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Getting to grips with the magnitude of exploitation: Bushmeat in the Cross–Sanaga rivers region, Nigeria and Cameroon","title-short":"Getting to grips with the magnitude of exploitation","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320705005045","volume":"129","author":[{"family":"Fa","given":"John E."},{"family":"Seymour","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Dupain","given":"Jef"},{"family":"Amin","given":"Rajan"},{"family":"Albrechtsen","given":"Lise"},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",5]]}}},{"id":35107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEAU4TNP"],"itemData":{"id":35107,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"eng","note":"978-92-5-130561-4","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"The State of the world's forests 2018 - Forests pathways to sustainable development","editor":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Direct exploitation by humans has been identified as the most serious driver of biodiversity loss in marine ecosystems and as the second most important driver in terrestrial and freshwater ecosystems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HVMMQwu8","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Coad, 2019; IPBES, 2018b; Vignieri, 2014; Fa, 2006; Allan, 2005; Fluet-Chouinard, 2018; FAO, 2018a, 2018b)","plainCitation":"(Coad, 2019; IPBES, 2018b; Vignieri, 2014; Fa, 2006; Allan, 2005; Fluet-Chouinard, 2018; FAO, 2018a, 2018b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":327,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/97LTS7HN"],"itemData":{"id":327,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","ISBN":"978-602-387-083-7","note":"DOI: 10.17528/cifor/007046","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","title":"Towards a sustainable, participatory and inclusive wild meat sector","URL":"https://www.cifor.org/online-library/browse/view-publication/publication/7046.html","author":[{"family":"Coad","given":"L"},{"family":"Fa","given":"JE"},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"K"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"N","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Santamaria","given":"C"},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"D"},{"family":"El Bizri","given":"HR"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"DJ"},{"family":"Cawthorn","given":"DM"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":35013,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XW6KN3MC","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GWN68BXX"],"itemData":{"id":35013,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-02-7","number-of-pages":"41","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the regional assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services for Asia and the Pacific of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3237373","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"literal":"M. Karki"},{"literal":"S. Senaratna Sellamuttu"},{"literal":"S. Okayasu"},{"literal":"W. Suzuki"},{"literal":"L. A. Acosta"},{"literal":"Y. Alhafedh"},{"literal":"J. A. Anticamara"},{"literal":"A. G. Ausseil"},{"literal":"K. Davies"},{"literal":"A. Gasparatos"},{"literal":"H. Gundimeda"},{"literal":"I. Faridah-Hanum"},{"literal":"R. Kohsaka"},{"literal":"R. Kumar"},{"literal":"S. Managi"},{"literal":"N. Wu"},{"literal":"A. Rajvanshi"},{"literal":"G. S. Rawat"},{"literal":"P. Riordan"},{"literal":"S. Sharma"},{"literal":"A. Virk"},{"literal":"C. Wang"},{"literal":"T. Yahara"},{"literal":"Y. C. Youn"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":1449,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HFGZ6ZT4"],"itemData":{"id":1449,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"[Special section on vanishing fauna][1] During the Pleistocene epoch, only tens of thousands of years ago, our planet supported large, spectacular animals. Mammoths, terror birds, giant tortoises, and saber-toothed cats, as well as many less familiar species such as giant ground sloths (some of which reached 7 meters in height) and glyptodonts (which resembled car-sized armadillos), roamed freely. Since then, however, the number and diversity of animal species on Earth have consistently and steadily declined. Today we are left with a relatively depauperate fauna, and we continue to lose animal species to extinction rapidly. Although some debate persists, most of the evidence suggests that humans were responsible for extinction of this Pleistocene fauna, and we continue to drive animal extinctions today through the destruction of wild lands, consumption of animals as a resource or a luxury, and persecution of species we see as threats or competitors. ![Figure][2]\\\\textless/img\\\\textgreater Animals are rapidly disappearing from forests in Borneo and across the world. PHOTO: CHRISTIAN ZIEGLER Such global loss of animal species, or defaunation, is increasingly recognized as a problem akin to deforestation in terms of scale and impact. Though for emotional or aesthetic reasons we may lament the loss of large charismatic species, such as tigers, rhinos, and pandas, we now know that loss of animals, from the largest elephant to the smallest beetle, will also fundamentally alter the form and function of the ecosystems upon which we all depend (see Dirzo , p. [401][3]). Identifying the drivers of these extinctions is straightforward, but stemming the loss is a daunting challenge. Animal species continue to decline in, and disappear from, even large, long-protected reserves, due both to direct impacts, such as poaching, and indirect ecological feedbacks, such as habitat fragmentation. Though hunting and poaching might seem obvious candidates for targeted policy and management interventions, there are complex social issues underlying these activities that will require coordinated and cooperative actions by nations (see Brashares , p. [376][4]). ![Figure][2]\\\\textless/img\\\\textgreater Twilight for animals Large numbers of animal species face extinction in Southeast Asia, the Amazon, and the Andes, as shown in this map of mammals, amphibians, and birds. Animals also face high rates of extinction in Europe and North America, where fewer species are found overall. MAP: FÉLIX PHARAND-DESCHÊNES (VISUALIZATION), CLINTON JENKINS (DATA PROCESSING), IUCN/BIRDLIFE INTERNATIONAL (DATA) While stemming this loss remains a challenging goal, attempts to reverse the extinction trend are increasing. Such “refaunation” efforts involve a variety of approaches, including breeding animals in captivity, with the hope of reintroducing them to the wild, and assisting recolonization of areas where species have become locally extinct (see Seddon , p. [406][5]). Active reversal of animal extinctions is proving just as challenging as preventing extinctions in the first place, but a few success stories provide some hope. Many note and mourn the loss of animals but have not recognized that the impacts of this loss go beyond an aesthetic and emotional need to maintain animals as a part of nature. Current research reveals startling rates of animal declines and extinctions and confirms the importance of these species to ecosystems (see Stokstad, p. [396][6]). Further, and more broadly, it suggests that if we are unable to end or reverse the rate of their loss, it will mean more for our own future than a broken heart or an empty forest. [1]: http://www.sciencemag.org/site/special/vanishing/index.xhtml [2]: pending:yes [3]: /lookup/volpage/345/401?iss=6195 [4]: /lookup/volpage/345/376?iss=6195 [5]: /lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1251818 [6]: /lookup/doi/10.1126/science.345.6195.396","container-title":"Science (New York, N.Y.)","DOI":"10.1126/science.345.6195.392","ISSN":"1095-9203","issue":"6195","note":"PMID: 25061199","page":"392–5","title":"Vanishing fauna. Introduction.","URL":"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/25061199","volume":"345","author":[{"family":"Vignieri","given":"Sacha"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",7]]}}},{"id":1435,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CXF8R89Z"],"itemData":{"id":1435,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wild meat harvests in African moist forests are presumed to exceed production, even in the case of traditional societies still using rudimentary hunting methods. Though some approximations do exist of the volume of bushmeat harvested in some Central African moist forest areas, estimates based on extensive and simultaneous sampling, within a large geographical region, are not available. Here, we present the results of the ?rst reported study of this kind. During a period of 5 month, we counted bushmeat carcasses deposited in 89 urban and rural markets in a 35,000 km2 area between the Cross River in Nigeria and the Sanaga River in Cameroon. We used these data to calculate annual bushmeat volume traded by site, species and overall in the study area.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2005.11.031","ISSN":"00063207","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"497-510","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Getting to grips with the magnitude of exploitation: Bushmeat in the Cross–Sanaga rivers region, Nigeria and Cameroon","title-short":"Getting to grips with the magnitude of exploitation","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320705005045","volume":"129","author":[{"family":"Fa","given":"John E."},{"family":"Seymour","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Dupain","given":"Jef"},{"family":"Amin","given":"Rajan"},{"family":"Albrechtsen","given":"Lise"},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",5]]}}},{"id":31099,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WXDUK6AI"],"itemData":{"id":31099,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Inland waters have received only slight consideration in recent discussions of the global fisheries crisis, even though inland fisheries provide much-needed protein, jobs, and income, especially in poor rural communities of developing countries. Systematic overfishing of fresh waters is largely unrecognized because of weak reporting and because fishery declines take place within a complex of other pressures. Moreover, the ecosystem consequences of changes to the species, size, and trophic composition of fish assemblages are poorly understood. These complexities underlie the paradox that overexploitation of a fishery may not be marked by declines in total yield, even when individual species and long-term sustainability are highly threatened. Indeed, one of the symptoms of intense fishing in inland waters is the collapse of particular stocks even as overall fish production rises—a biodiversity crisis more than a fisheries crisis.","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1641/0006-3568(2005)055[1041:OOIW]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0006-3568","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"BioScience","page":"1041-1051","source":"Silverchair","title":"Overfishing of Inland Waters","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1641/0006-3568(2005)055[1041:OOIW]2.0.CO;2","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Allan","given":"J. David"},{"family":"Abell","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Hogan","given":"Zeb"},{"family":"Revenga","given":"Carmen"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Brad W."},{"family":"Welcomme","given":"Robin L."},{"family":"Winemiller","given":"Kirk"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",12,1]]}}},{"id":1444,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H2VSGMMM"],"itemData":{"id":1444,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Consumption of wild-caught freshwater fish is concentrated in low-income countries, where it makes a critical contribution to food security and livelihoods. Underestimation of inland harvests in official statistics has long been suspected due to unmonitored subsistence fisheries. To overcome the lack of data from extensive small-scale harvests, we used household consumption surveys to estimate freshwater fish catches in 42 low- and middle-income countries between 1997 and 2014. After accounting for trade and aquaculture, these countries collectively consumed 3.6 MT (CI, 1.5–5.8) more wild-caught freshwater fish than officially reported, reflecting a net underreporting of 64.8% (CI, 27.1–103.9%). Individual countries were more likely to underestimate (n = 31) than overestimate (n = 11) catches, despite conservative assumptions in our calculations. Extrapolating our findings suggests that the global inland catch reported as 10.3 MT in 2008 was more likely 16.6 MT (CI, 2.3–30.9), which accords with recent independent predictions for rivers and lakes. In human terms, these hidden harvests are equivalent to the total animal protein consumption of 36.9 (CI, 30.8–43.4) million people, including many who rely upon wild fish to achieve even minimal protein intake. The widespread underreporting uncovered by household consumption surveys indicates that inland fisheries contribute far more to global food security than has been recognized previously. Our findings also amplify concerns about the sustainability of intensive fishery exploitation as degradation of rivers, lakes, and wetlands continues apace.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1721097115","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"29","journalAbbreviation":"PNAS","language":"en","license":"© 2018 . http://www.pnas.org/site/aboutpnas/licenses.xhtmlPublished under the PNAS license.","note":"PMID: 29915069","page":"7623-7628","source":"www.pnas.org","title":"Global hidden harvest of freshwater fish revealed by household surveys","URL":"https://www.pnas.org/content/115/29/7623","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Fluet-Chouinard","given":"Etienne"},{"family":"Funge-Smith","given":"Simon"},{"family":"McIntyre","given":"Peter B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7,17]]}}},{"id":48,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L9N7XCWM"],"itemData":{"id":48,"type":"article-journal","title":"FAO Term Portal. Retrieved November 28, 2018, from http://www.fao.org/faoterm/en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEAU4TNP"],"itemData":{"id":35107,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"eng","note":"978-92-5-130561-4","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"The State of the world's forests 2018 - Forests pathways to sustainable development","editor":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The extent of decline has clearly shown that many current policies are inadequate and/or their implementation is ineffective. This has increased the urgency to identify ongoing policies that work and scale them up, or find alternative approaches when ongoing policies have been unsuccessful ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"S6EuKkhj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019d)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The decline in biodiversity also affects human communities who directly or indirectly depend on the use of wild species. Regular use of wild foods is an important component of global food and nutrition security. Although more data are needed to establish a complete picture of the contributions of wild foods to people around the world, some nations report that between one third and 100% of their national or subnational populations regularly use wild foods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CstMVLoG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2019)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AFH3MZXV"],"itemData":{"id":35102,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","page":"572","publisher":"Commission on Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of the world's biodiversity for food and agriculture","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/CA3129EN/CA3129EN.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Bélanger","given":"J."},{"family":"Pilling","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wild marine and freshwater species (mostly fish, crustaceans and mollusks) provide 54% of the world's seafood production which accounted, in 2017, for 17% of the global population’s intake of animal proteins and 7% of all proteins consumed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fbZ1V79j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wild meat from terrestrial animals remains also a major source of protein for rural and urban populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vYizcKqJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Coad, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Coad, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":327,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/97LTS7HN"],"itemData":{"id":327,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","ISBN":"978-602-387-083-7","note":"DOI: 10.17528/cifor/007046","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","title":"Towards a sustainable, participatory and inclusive wild meat sector","URL":"https://www.cifor.org/online-library/browse/view-publication/publication/7046.html","author":[{"family":"Coad","given":"L"},{"family":"Fa","given":"JE"},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"K"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"N","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Santamaria","given":"C"},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"D"},{"family":"El Bizri","given":"HR"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"DJ"},{"family":"Cawthorn","given":"DM"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A wide range of wild-harvested plants, fungi and lichens are used and traded, including 30,000 plant species for medicinal or aromatic uses, an estimated 60-90% of which are gathered in the wild ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4zZZqW7d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2018b, 2018c; Jenkins, 2018)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2018b, 2018c; Jenkins, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEAU4TNP"],"itemData":{"id":35107,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"eng","note":"978-92-5-130561-4","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"The State of the world's forests 2018 - Forests pathways to sustainable development","editor":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":7733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NIZN4X56","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M2WPUUSR"],"itemData":{"id":7733,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development\\n(2030 Agenda for short) offers a vision of a\\nfairer, more peaceful world in which no one is\\nleft behind. The 2030 Agenda also sets aims for\\nthe contribution and conduct of fisheries and\\naquaculture towards food security and nutrition,\\nand the sector’s use of natural resources, in a\\nway that ensures sustainable development in\\neconomic, social and environmental terms,\\nwithin the context of the FAO Code of Conduct\\nfor Responsible Fisheries (FAO, 1995). A major\\nchallenge to implementation of the 2030 Agenda\\nis the sustainability divide between developed\\nand developing countries which has partially\\nresulted from increased economic\\ninterdependencies, coupled with limited\\nmanagement and governance capacity in\\ndeveloping countries. To eliminate this disparity\\nwhile making progress towards the target for\\nrestoration of overfished stocks set by the 2030\\nAgenda, the global community needs to support\\ndeveloping nations to achieve their full fisheries\\nand aquaculture potential. \\n\\nReport is rich in data.","event-place":"Rome","language":"English","note":"978-92-5-130562-1","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture 2018 - Meeting the sustainable development goals","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/I9540EN/i9540en.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":1433,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/URMHTYZ6"],"itemData":{"id":1433,"type":"report","collection-title":"Traffic Report","event-place":"Cambridge, UK","language":"English","page":"43","publisher":"TRAFFIC International","publisher-place":"Cambridge, UK","title":"Wild at Home. Exploring the global harvest, trade and use of wild plant ingredients","author":[{"family":"Jenkins","given":"M"},{"family":"Timoshyna","given":"Anastasiya"},{"family":"Cornthwaite","given":"Marcus"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wild forests (or natural forests) provide a significant proportion of wood for material and construction, energy (fuelwood or charcoal) and food to people, particularly in developing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VYTLPEHH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2018b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2018b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEAU4TNP"],"itemData":{"id":35107,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"eng","note":"978-92-5-130561-4","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"The State of the world's forests 2018 - Forests pathways to sustainable development","editor":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An estimated 5% to 8% of current global crop production  depends on wild animal pollination, which corresponds to an annual market value of US$235 billion to US$577 billion ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QlUKJhWF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2016)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35028,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YG7LF893","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AGYQARR9"],"itemData":{"id":35028,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-92-807-3568-0","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","title":"Summary for policymakers of the assessment report of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services on pollinators, pollination and food production.","URL":"https://www.ipbes.net/sites/default/files/spm_deliverable_3a_pollination_20170222.pdf","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"Potts","given":"S. G."},{"family":"Imperatriz-Fonseca","given":"V. L."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H. T."},{"family":"Biesmeijer","given":"J. C."},{"family":"Breeze","given":"T. D."},{"family":"Dicks","given":"L. V."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Hill","given":"R."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Vanbergen","given":"A. J."},{"family":"Aizen","given":"M. A."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"S. A."},{"family":"Eardley","given":"C."},{"family":"Freitas","given":"B. M."},{"family":"Gallai","given":"N."},{"family":"Kevan","given":"P. G."},{"family":"Kovács-Hostyánszki","given":"A."},{"family":"Kwapong","given":"P. K."},{"family":"Li","given":"J."},{"family":"Li","given":"X."},{"family":"Martins","given":"D. J."},{"family":"Nates-Parra","given":"G."},{"family":"J. S. Pettis","given":"R. Rader"},{"family":"Viana","given":"B. F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"N3ADeihp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(SOFIA, 2018)","plainCitation":"(SOFIA, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"BOVAcHko/1HiAeZq5","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/QUA8WLEB"],"itemData":{"id":5,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","collection-title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130562-1","language":"en","number-of-pages":"210","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"The State of the Worlds Fisheries and Aquaculture","editor":[{"family":"SOFIA","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}Vulnerable and marginalised people are often most reliant on wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xZwvItev","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(SOFO, 2018)","plainCitation":"(SOFO, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"BOVAcHko/n9QQExya","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/DZA95468"],"itemData":{"id":4,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","collection-title":"State of the world's forests","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"en","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"FAO","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"State of the World's Forests","editor":[{"family":"SOFO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}and are most likely to benefit from more sustainable forms of use of wild species that can be used as pathways to more secure livelihoods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"P6bQyvKP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(SOFO, 2018)","plainCitation":"(SOFO, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"BOVAcHko/n9QQExya","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/DZA95468"],"itemData":{"id":4,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","collection-title":"State of the world's forests","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"en","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"FAO","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"State of the World's Forests","editor":[{"family":"SOFO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Biodiversity decline nevertheless also affects the economies of developed countries, especially those depending on the use of wild species, such as fisheries, logging industry, medicinal plant industry or tourism (see Chapters 3 and 4). Further, the use of wild species provides non material contributions through enriching people's physical and psychological experiences, including their religious and ceremonial lives ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Bo7sbSLK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Anthony & Bellinger, 2007; Russell, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Anthony & Bellinger, 2007; Russell, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1423,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S2TFP4A6"],"itemData":{"id":1423,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"South African Journal of Science","page":"148-154","title":"Importance value of landscapes, flora and fauna to Tsongo communities in the rural areas of Limpopo province, South Africa","volume":"103","author":[{"family":"Anthony","given":"Brandon P."},{"family":"Bellinger","given":"Edward G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",4]]}}},{"id":32723,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EF73QVSJ"],"itemData":{"id":32723,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-012312-110838","note":"publisher: Annual Reviews","page":"473–502","title":"Humans and nature: how knowing and experiencing nature affect well-being","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Russell","given":"Roly"},{"family":"Guerry","given":"Anne D"},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Gould","given":"Rachelle K"},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai MA"},{"family":"Klain","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Levine","given":"Jordan"},{"family":"Tam","given":"Jordan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Determining and enhancing the sustainability of uses of wild species is thus a critical issue for conserving biodiversity and contributing to human wellbeing.
      Although overexploitation of wild species is well documented, there are many examples of success in maintaining or restoring populations for long-term use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"drz6Lx8N","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cromsigt Joris P. G. M., 2018; Lichtenstein & Vil\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}, 2003; Mahoney & Geist, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Cromsigt Joris P. G. M., 2018; Lichtenstein & Vilá, 2003; Mahoney & Geist, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HI9TPQKW","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQXZW48B"],"itemData":{"id":31,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The loss of megafauna at the terminal Pleistocene has been linked to a wide range of Earth-system-level changes, such as altered greenhouse gas budgets, fire regimes and biome-level vegetation changes. Given these influences and feedbacks, might part of the solution for mitigating anthropogenic climate change lie in the restoration of extant megafauna to ecosystems? Here, we explore the potential role of trophic rewilding on Earth's climate system. We first provide a novel synthesis of the various ways that megafauna interact with the major drivers of anthropogenic climate change, including greenhouse gas storage and emission, aerosols and albedo. We then explore the role of rewilding as a mitigation tool at two scales: (i) current and near-future opportunities for national or regional climate change mitigation portfolios, and (ii) more radical opportunities at the global scale. Finally, we identify major knowledge gaps that complicate the complete characterization of rewilding as a climate change mitigation strategy. Our perspective is urgent since we are losing the Earth's last remaining megafauna, and with it a potential option to address climate change.This article is part of the theme issue ‘Trophic rewilding: consequences for ecosystems under global change’.","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences","DOI":"10.1098/rstb.2017.0440","issue":"1761","journalAbbreviation":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences","page":"20170440","source":"royalsocietypublishing.org (Atypon)","title":"Trophic rewilding as a climate change mitigation strategy?","URL":"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/abs/10.1098/rstb.2017.0440","volume":"373","author":[{"literal":"Cromsigt Joris P. G. M."},{"literal":"te Beest Mariska"},{"literal":"Kerley Graham I. H."},{"literal":"Landman Marietjie"},{"literal":"le Roux Elizabeth"},{"literal":"Smith Felisa A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12,5]]}}},{"id":24,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RE4E67DM"],"itemData":{"id":24,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mountain Research and Development","DOI":"10.1659/0276-4741(2003)023[0197:VUBACA]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0276-4741, 1994-7151","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Mountain Research and Development","language":"en","page":"198-201","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Vicuna Use by Andean Communities: An Overview","title-short":"Vicuna Use by Andean Communities","URL":"http://www.bioone.org/doi/abs/10.1659/0276-4741%282003%29023%5B0197%3AVUBACA%5D2.0.CO%3B2","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Vilá","given":"Bibiana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",5]]}}},{"id":26,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PG2CTVSH","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HNUMASNM"],"itemData":{"id":26,"type":"book","number-of-pages":"176","publisher":"Johns Hopkins University Press Books","title":"The North American Model of Wildlife Conservation","URL":"https://jhupbooks.press.jhu.edu/title/north-american-model-wildlife-conservation","author":[{"family":"Mahoney","given":"P."},{"family":"Geist","given":"V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Finding ways to prevent the ongoing loss of species and mitigate the concomitant impacts on human wellbeing remains a major challenge. Human societies have grappled for millennia with profound questions relating to the harvest of wild species to meet their needs, the balance required for those same species to thrive, and the distribution of benefits derived from wild species. The knowledge, customary institutions and practices of many indigenous peoples and local communities ensure sustainable use of the species and environments on which they rely ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LHhBEzew","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2018; Comberti, 2015; Minnis & Elisens, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2018; Comberti, 2015; Minnis & Elisens, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":846,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UPCMNE8P"],"itemData":{"id":846,"type":"book","edition":"Fourth","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-1-138-07149-0","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Sacred Ecology","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":30207,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZFFG62F5"],"itemData":{"id":30207,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2015.07.007","page":"247-262","title":"Ecosystem services or services to ecosystems?","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Comberti","given":"C."},{"family":"Thornton","given":"T.F."},{"family":"Wyllie de Echeverria","given":"V."},{"family":"Patterson","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":1401,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NQKGJRZ8"],"itemData":{"id":1401,"type":"book","event-place":"Norman","ISBN":"0-8061-3232-9","publisher":"University of Oklahoma Press","publisher-place":"Norman","title":"Biodiversity and Native America","author":[{"family":"Minnis","given":"Paul E."},{"family":"Elisens","given":"Wayne J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, local and global changes in the environment, consumption patterns and the rapid growth in human population have greatly altered the context in which wild species are used and managed. Complex social-ecological dynamics operating from local to global scales underlie the use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nGd21ubK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brashares & Gaynor, 2017; Ostrom, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Brashares & Gaynor, 2017; Ostrom, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35007,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UMZ4CPCH"],"itemData":{"id":35007,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife harvest and depletion compromise socioecological stability\\nWildlife harvest and depletion compromise socioecological stability","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aan0499","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6334","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2017, American Association for the Advancement of Science","note":"publisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science\\nsection: Perspectives\\nPMID: 28408560","page":"136-137","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"Eating ecosystems","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/356/6334/136","volume":"356","author":[{"family":"Brashares","given":"Justin S."},{"family":"Gaynor","given":"Kaitlyn M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",4,14]]}}},{"id":36,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5F6IBU5V","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2NACCRB4"],"itemData":{"id":36,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1172133","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5939","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"419-422","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A General Framework for Analyzing Sustainability of Social-Ecological Systems","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1172133","volume":"325","author":[{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",7,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and influence the likely outcomes of policies and practices aimed to maintain and restore them. 
      There have been numerous national, bilateral and multilateral initiatives to find policy and management solutions favoring the sustainable use  of wild species. National policies and regulations relating to the use of wild species date back more than 2000 years in China ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"D2IPlE3w","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sch\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}fer, 2018; Yi-Ming, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Schäfer, 2018; Yi-Ming, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U9D8QYFM"],"itemData":{"id":9,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Animals through Chinese History","edition":"1","ISBN":"978-1-108-55157-1","note":"DOI: 10.1017/9781108551571.002","page":"1-19","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Knowing Animals in China’s History: An Introduction","title-short":"Knowing Animals in China’s History","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/9781108551571%23CT-bp-1/type/book_part","author":[{"family":"Schäfer","given":"Dagmar"},{"family":"Siebert","given":"Martina"},{"family":"Sterckx","given":"Roel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12,13]]}}},{"id":12,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3HY5Y9AV"],"itemData":{"id":12,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1023/A:1008905430813","ISSN":"09603115","issue":"7","page":"901-918","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Illegal Wildlife trade in the Himalaya Region of China","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1023/A:1008905430813","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Yi-Ming","given":"Li"},{"family":"Zenxiang","given":"Gao"},{"family":"Xinhai","given":"Li"},{"family":"Sung","given":"Wang"},{"family":"Niemelä","given":"Jari"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and at least to the 13th century in Europe and Great Britain ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yCMysgb9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bazeley, 1921; R. C. Hoffmann, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Bazeley, 1921; R. C. Hoffmann, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35010,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H6BY3HCE"],"itemData":{"id":35010,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The first difficulty which confronts the student of the English forest is to be found in the ambiguity of the terms which are used to describe it. In its proper sense the word “forest” denotes either the whole area in which the king's game is protected by a special law, or a separate administrative district within it. But there are other and looser uses to which the term is put in the records. In the first place, it is frequently applied to mere woods in a forest area. Thus the wood of Islip, near Oxford, known by the distinctive name of “Cauda Aliz” or “La Quealiz,” appears at one time as a part of the forest of Brill, at another as a separate forest coupled with it. Brill, in its turn, was merely a part of the forest of Bernwood, which was itself a member of the “Forest between the bridges of Oxford and Stamford”.","container-title":"Transactions of the Royal Historical Society","DOI":"10.2307/3678331","ISSN":"0080-4401, 1474-0648","journalAbbreviation":"Transactions of the Royal Historical Society","language":"en","page":"140-159","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Extent of the English Forest in the Thirteenth Century","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0080440100015826/type/journal_article","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Bazeley","given":"Margaret Ley"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1921",12]]}}},{"id":35004,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M6XPH6WK"],"itemData":{"id":35004,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Humans have exploited European aquatic resources since at least the Palaeolithic, but during the Middle Ages rising human populations and demand initiated great changes in many ?sheries. To help understand the past and present of the Wadden Sea, this paper sets the main developments of medieval ?sheries in the context of changing larger European social and aquatic environments ca. 500–1500 A.D. Anthropogenic in?uences on ?sh populations and aquatic habitats interacted with natural environmental variations. Both nutritional and cultural needs shaped human consumption of aquatic organisms. Many ?sheries met the demand for food by economic reorientation from subsistence to artisanal and then even fully commercial purposes. Exploitation slowly shifted from limited or deteriorating local inland and inshore ?sh populations to frontier, commonly marine, and increasingly pelagic resources. Some inland regions developed aquaculture to enhance local supplies of fresh ?sh. General processes are illustrated by case studies of selected indicator species from freshwater and marine habitats generally pertinent to the region surrounding the Wadden Sea. Anadromous salmon (Salmo salar) and sturgeon (Acipenser sturio) were negatively a?ected by over?shing and by unintentional human alteration of critical habitat. Habitat preferences of catadromous eel (Anguilla anguilla) and exotic carp (Cyprinus carpio) let these species gain from medieval human activities. In the case of herring (Clupea harengus), Europe’s largest early commercial marine ?shery, technological innovations which raised production and consumption played o? against long-term consequences of intensely exploiting sensitive natural systems.","container-title":"Helgoland Marine Research","DOI":"10.1007/s10152-004-0203-5","ISSN":"1438-387X, 1438-3888","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Helgol Mar Res","language":"en","page":"22-30","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A brief history of aquatic resource use in medieval Europe","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10152-004-0203-5","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Richard C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Over the past century, many agreements for the use of wild species have been developed and refined ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ruOPl7CG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gulbrandsen & Humphreys, 2006; McDermott, 2007; Rice, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Gulbrandsen & Humphreys, 2006; McDermott, 2007; Rice, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J5TVB56K"],"itemData":{"id":11,"type":"report","event-place":"Norway","language":"English","number":"4/2006","page":"67","publisher":"Fridtjof Nansen Institute","publisher-place":"Norway","title":"International Initiatives to address tropical timber logging and trade","author":[{"family":"Gulbrandsen","given":"L.H."},{"family":"Humphreys","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":10,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6BLFY2SI"],"itemData":{"id":10,"type":"report","language":"English","page":"131","publisher":"Department of Economic and Social Affairs- United Nations Forum on Forests Secretariat","title":"International Forest Policy- the instruments, agreements and processes that shape it","author":[{"family":"McDermott","given":"C.L."},{"family":"O'Carroll","given":"A."},{"family":"Wood","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":27,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZUVIWC4U"],"itemData":{"id":27,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            Rice, J. 2014. Evolution of international commitments for fisheries sustainability. – ICES Journal of Marine Science, 71: 157–165. The basic standards for the sustainability of fisheries were set by international policy in the UN Fish Stocks Agreement (FSA). However, each year since the FSA was ratified, the United Nations General Assembly has negotiated and agreed to resolutions on Ocean Law of the Sea and on Sustainable Fisheries. This paper reviews chronologically how the interpretation of “sustainability” has evolved in those resolutions, as well as been addressed in the decadal world summits on sustainable development. Although the basic biological benchmarks for sustainability have not been altered by these resolutions, commitments for the standards to be met by all ecosystem components impacted by fishing have become increasingly strong. The annual resolutions have increasingly stressed that environmental sustainability is critically important, but is not more important than social well-being aspects of sustainability, with fisheries having a vital role in sustainable development in many parts of the world. In addition, agreements on biodiversity conservation made largely in Oceans and Law of the Sea resolutions are increasingly influencing the nature and pace of evolution of how “sustainability” is interpreted in fisheries.","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fst078","ISSN":"1095-9289, 1054-3139","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"157-165","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Evolution of international commitments for fisheries sustainability","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/icesjms/article/71/2/157/777867","volume":"71","author":[{"family":"Rice","given":"Jake"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These include specific agreements on whales, fishing, logging, and hunting that have been set at national levels or through specific international management bodies, such as the International Whaling Commission, regional fisheries management organisations, and the International Tropical Timber Organization, which all aim to ensure more sustainable harvest of wild species. They also include more general instruments, such as the Addis Ababa Principles for sustainable use adopted under the Convention on Biological Diversity. Various global targets for sustainable use have been developed, such as the Global Strategy for Plant Conservation, the Aichi Biodiversity Targets of the Strategic Plan for Biodiversity 2011-2020 and the Sustainable Development Goals adopted by the United Nations. However, these are non-binding policy instruments and the Global Strategy for Plant Conservation and Aichi Biodiversity Targets failed to achieve targets related to sustainable use  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0UQaFigX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019d)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It will remain a challenge to achieve the targets under the Sustainable Development Goals by 2030 and it is therefore important to understand how sustainable use of wild species  contributes to the Sustainable Development Goals and what can be done to achieve sustainable outcomes. 
      Sustainable use of wild species features prominently in several international assessments, such as those done by the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations (FAO) on fisheries and forestry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"t2Sr9dQB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2018b, 2018c, 2020b, 2020a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2018b, 2018c, 2020b, 2020a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEAU4TNP"],"itemData":{"id":35107,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130561-4","language":"eng","note":"978-92-5-130561-4","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"The State of the world's forests 2018 - Forests pathways to sustainable development","editor":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":7733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NIZN4X56","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M2WPUUSR"],"itemData":{"id":7733,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development\\n(2030 Agenda for short) offers a vision of a\\nfairer, more peaceful world in which no one is\\nleft behind. The 2030 Agenda also sets aims for\\nthe contribution and conduct of fisheries and\\naquaculture towards food security and nutrition,\\nand the sector’s use of natural resources, in a\\nway that ensures sustainable development in\\neconomic, social and environmental terms,\\nwithin the context of the FAO Code of Conduct\\nfor Responsible Fisheries (FAO, 1995). A major\\nchallenge to implementation of the 2030 Agenda\\nis the sustainability divide between developed\\nand developing countries which has partially\\nresulted from increased economic\\ninterdependencies, coupled with limited\\nmanagement and governance capacity in\\ndeveloping countries. To eliminate this disparity\\nwhile making progress towards the target for\\nrestoration of overfished stocks set by the 2030\\nAgenda, the global community needs to support\\ndeveloping nations to achieve their full fisheries\\nand aquaculture potential. \\n\\nReport is rich in data.","event-place":"Rome","language":"English","note":"978-92-5-130562-1","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture 2018 - Meeting the sustainable development goals","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/I9540EN/i9540en.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":"BOVAcHko/MTNp8Tsl","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A6FP6AF5"],"itemData":{"id":123716,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132974-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1195470916","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Global Forest Resources Assessment (FRA) 2020: main report","title-short":"GLOBAL FOREST RESOURCES ASSESSMENT (FRA) 2020","URL":"https://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8753en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the Center for International Forestry Research on terrestrial animal wildlife ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FrzMtQpn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Coad, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Coad, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":327,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/97LTS7HN"],"itemData":{"id":327,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","ISBN":"978-602-387-083-7","note":"DOI: 10.17528/cifor/007046","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","title":"Towards a sustainable, participatory and inclusive wild meat sector","URL":"https://www.cifor.org/online-library/browse/view-publication/publication/7046.html","author":[{"family":"Coad","given":"L"},{"family":"Fa","given":"JE"},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"K"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"N","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Santamaria","given":"C"},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"D"},{"family":"El Bizri","given":"HR"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"DJ"},{"family":"Cawthorn","given":"DM"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and IPBES on biodiversity at global ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MDX8t4FB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019d)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or regional levels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dV6XV9h8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2018d, 2018a, 2018b, 2018c)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2018d, 2018a, 2018b, 2018c)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":91,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HLBA5ANA","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HY7NC7ZH"],"itemData":{"id":91,"type":"book","abstract":"Reproduction This publication may be reproduced in whole or in part and in any form for educational or non-profit services without special permission from the copyright holder, provided acknowledgement of the source is made. The IPBES secretariat would appreciate receiving a copy of any publication that uses this publication as a source. No use of this publication may be made for resale or any other commercial purpose whatsoever without prior permission in writing from the IPBES secretariat. Applications for such permission, with a statement of the purpose and extent of the reproduction, should be addressed to the IPBES secretariat. The use of information from this publication concerning proprietary products for publicity or advertising is not permitted. Traceable accounts The chapter references enclosed in curly brackets (e.g. {2.3.1, 2.3.1.2, 2.3.1.3}) are traceable accounts and refer to sections of the chapters of the IPBES Regional Assessment Report on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services for the Americas. A traceable account is a description within the corresponding texts of these chapters, reflecting the evaluation of the type, amount, quality, and consistency of evidence and the degree of agreement for that particular statement or key finding.","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-01-0","number-of-pages":"41","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the regional assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services for the Americas of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3236252","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"literal":"J. Rice"},{"literal":"C.S. Seixas"},{"literal":"M.E. Zaccagnini"},{"literal":"M. Bedoya-Gaitán"},{"literal":"N. Valderrama"},{"literal":"C.B. Anderson"},{"literal":"M.T.K. Arroyo"},{"literal":"M. Bustamante"},{"literal":"J. Cavender-Bares"},{"literal":"A. Diaz-de-Leon"},{"literal":"S. Fennessy"},{"literal":"J. R. García Márquez"},{"literal":"K. Garcia"},{"literal":"E.H. Helmer"},{"literal":"B. Herrera"},{"literal":"B. Klatt"},{"literal":"J.P. Ometo"},{"literal":"V. Rodríguez Osuna"},{"literal":"F.R. Scarano"},{"literal":"S. Schill"},{"literal":"J. S. Farinaci"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35018,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NHSBQV7E","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EY6CU4ER"],"itemData":{"id":35018,"type":"book","abstract":"Reproduction This publication may be reproduced in whole or in part and in any form for educational or non-profit services without special permission from the copyright holder, provided acknowledgement of the source is made. The IPBES secretariat would appreciate receiving a copy of any publication that uses this publication as a source. No use of this publication may be made for resale or any other commercial purpose whatsoever without prior permission in writing from the IPBES secretariat. Applications for such permission, with a statement of the purpose and extent of the reproduction, should be addressed to the IPBES secretariat. The use of information from this publication concerning proprietary products for publicity or advertising is not permitted. Traceable accounts The chapter references enclosed in curly brackets (e.g. {2.3.1, 2.3.1.2, 2.3.1.3}) are traceable accounts and refer to sections of the chapters of the IPBES Regional Assessment Report on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services for Africa. A traceable account is a description within the corresponding texts of these chapters, reflecting the evaluation of the type, amount, quality, and consistency of evidence and the degree of agreement for that particular statement or key finding.","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-00-3","number-of-pages":"49","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the regional assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services for Africa of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"http://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3236178","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"literal":"E. Archer"},{"literal":"L. E. Dziba"},{"literal":"K. J. Mulongoy"},{"literal":"M. A. Maoela"},{"literal":"M. Walters"},{"literal":"R. Biggs"},{"literal":"M-C. Cormier-Salem"},{"literal":"F. DeClerck"},{"literal":"M. C. Diaw"},{"literal":"A. E. Dunham"},{"literal":"P. Failler"},{"literal":"C. Gordon"},{"literal":"K. A. Harhash"},{"literal":"R. Kasisi"},{"literal":"F. Kizito"},{"literal":"W. D. Nyingi"},{"literal":"N. Oguge"},{"literal":"B. Osman-Elasha"},{"literal":"L. C. Stringer"},{"literal":"L. Tito de Morais"},{"literal":"A. Assogbadjo"},{"literal":"B. N. Egoh"},{"literal":"M. W. Halmy"},{"literal":"K. Heubach"},{"literal":"A. Mensah"},{"literal":"L. Pereira"},{"literal":"N. Sitas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":35013,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XW6KN3MC","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GWN68BXX"],"itemData":{"id":35013,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-02-7","number-of-pages":"41","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the regional assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services for Asia and the Pacific of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3237373","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"literal":"M. Karki"},{"literal":"S. Senaratna Sellamuttu"},{"literal":"S. Okayasu"},{"literal":"W. Suzuki"},{"literal":"L. A. Acosta"},{"literal":"Y. Alhafedh"},{"literal":"J. A. Anticamara"},{"literal":"A. G. Ausseil"},{"literal":"K. Davies"},{"literal":"A. Gasparatos"},{"literal":"H. Gundimeda"},{"literal":"I. Faridah-Hanum"},{"literal":"R. Kohsaka"},{"literal":"R. Kumar"},{"literal":"S. Managi"},{"literal":"N. Wu"},{"literal":"A. Rajvanshi"},{"literal":"G. S. Rawat"},{"literal":"P. Riordan"},{"literal":"S. Sharma"},{"literal":"A. Virk"},{"literal":"C. Wang"},{"literal":"T. Yahara"},{"literal":"Y. C. Youn"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":96,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4F3RSRRX","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VN8H2QX5"],"itemData":{"id":96,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-03-4","number-of-pages":"48","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the regional assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services for Europe and Central Asia of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3237428","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"M. Fischer, M. Rounsevell, A. Torre-Marin Rando, A. Mader, A. Church, M. Elbakidze, V. Elias, T. Hahn, P.A. Harrison, J. Hauck, B. Martín-López, I. Ring, C. Sandström, I. Sousa Pinto, P. Visconti","given":"N.E. Zimmermann"},{"family":"Christie","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These assessments provide a strong background on the status and trends of wild species and problems relating to use of wild species (see section 1.5). Nevertheless, the status of many species remains unassessed because of a lack of monitoring, technical limitations, unreported illegal practices and trade, lack of appropriate experts or simply lack of recognition of existing practices and sources of knowledge (e.g., indigenous and local knowledge, see Chapters 3 and 4). Governance systems in place are also often undocumented, particularly those of indigenous peoples and local communities. Further, the nature and extent of human uses, harvesting rates and traditional management practices remain poorly documented, especially for uses that are not subject to trade. Despite accepted general rules, such as those identified  by the precautionary approach ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qk3dcXn1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garcia, 1996)","plainCitation":"(Garcia, 1996)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IC7JK6RD"],"itemData":{"id":34879,"type":"chapter","collection-number":"350","collection-title":"FAO Fisheries Technical Paper","container-title":"Precautionary approach to fisheries. Part 2: scientific papers. Prepared for the Technical Consultation on the Precautionary Approach to Capture Fisheries (Including Species Introductions)","edition":"FAO","event-place":"Rome, Italy","language":"eng","publisher-place":"Rome, Italy","title":"The precautionary approach to fisheries and its implications for fishery research, technology and management: and updated review","URL":"https://www.fao.org/3/W1238E/W1238E01.htm#ch1","volume":"2","contributor":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"author":[{"family":"Garcia","given":"Serge M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the uncertainty or the lack of consensus regarding the scientific advice (e.g., about the way to model population dynamics under use) or the successes or failures in relation to sustainable use of wild species has resulted in often divergent views on which policies to support and sometimes led to heated controversies, such as on whaling, fisheries or trophy hunting (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9F91KlmP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fromentin, 2014; Mkono, 2019; Peace, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Fromentin, 2014; Mkono, 2019; Peace, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34496,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6VFSD4FF"],"itemData":{"id":34496,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2014.01.018","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"8-14","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The spectre of uncertainty in management of exploited fish stocks: The illustrative case of Atlantic bluefin tuna","title-short":"The spectre of uncertainty in management of exploited fish stocks","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X1400030X","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Fromentin","given":"Jean-Marc"},{"family":"Bonhommeau","given":"Sylvain"},{"family":"Arrizabalaga","given":"Haritz"},{"family":"Kell","given":"Laurence T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",7]]}}},{"id":31043,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TDVJ5M64"],"itemData":{"id":31043,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Tourism","DOI":"10.1080/09669582.2019.1604719","ISSN":"0966-9582, 1747-7646","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Sustainable Tourism","language":"en","page":"689-704","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Neo-colonialism and greed: Africans’ views on trophy hunting in social media","title-short":"Neo-colonialism and greed","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/09669582.2019.1604719","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Mkono","given":"Mucha"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",5,4]]}}},{"id":1432,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RXEMRXG8"],"itemData":{"id":1432,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Anthropology Today","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-8322.2010.00734.x","ISSN":"0268540X, 14678322","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"5-9","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The whaling war: Conflicting cultural perspectives","title-short":"The whaling war","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1467-8322.2010.00734.x","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Peace","given":"Adrian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This could lead to policy inertia or persisting with policies that are ineffective or, worse, that provide perverse incentives resulting in further loss of biodiversity and human wellbeing. It is also crucial to clearly understand and define people’s needs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"odzwPo8k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Singh, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Singh, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1439,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5WBLZAW2"],"itemData":{"id":1439,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.2100205118","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"e2100205118","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Will understanding the ocean lead to “the ocean we want”?","title-short":"Opinion","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.2100205118","volume":"118","author":[{"family":"Singh","given":"Gerald G."},{"family":"Harden-Davies","given":"Harriet"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"Andrés M."},{"family":"Swartz","given":"Wilf"},{"family":"Crosman","given":"Katherine M."},{"family":"Ota","given":"Yoshitaka"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}) and to assess with them potential solutions to optimize the social acceptability of various policy instruments put in place to regulate the use of wild species (see also Chapters 4 and 6).
   The IPBES thematic assessment of the sustainable use of wild species (hereafter “the sustainable use assessment”) is a comprehensive and ambitious intergovernmental effort that aims to build on previous assessments and address the challenges faced by policymakers. According to the scoping report adopted by the IPBES Plenary at its fifth session, and set out in Annex IV to decision IPBES-5/1, the objective of this thematic assessment is: “to consider various approaches to the enhancement of the sustainability of the use of wild species and to strengthen related practices, measures, capacities and tools for their conservation through such use”. In other words, the aim of this assessment is not to evaluate the status of wild species worldwide, nor to exhaustively document the impacts of human uses on wild populations or the various biotic and abiotic components of the ecosystems that they inhabit, as this has already been done by the IPBES Global Assessment of Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"u0vXHKcQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019d)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or by IUCN ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Kj6aD1jB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2020)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":883,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CBPTIH6I"],"itemData":{"id":883,"type":"webpage","abstract":"Established in 1964, the IUCN Red List of Threatened Species has evolved to become the world’s most comprehensive information source on the global conservation status of animal, fungi and plant species.","container-title":"IUCN Red List of Threatened Species","title":"The IUCN Red List Data","URL":"https://www.iucnredlist.org/en","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Instead, the core of this assessment is to evaluate sustainability through the lens of different practices and uses by:
* reflecting on how sustainable use is conceptualized by different groups (stakeholders, scientists, indigenous people, local communities);
* assessing the status and trends in use of wild species and its consequence for nature and nature’s contributions to people;
* understanding and assessing the main drivers of change;
* examining scenarios for the future;
* assessing the effectiveness of policies, governance systems and institutions for managing the sustainable use of wild species;
* identifying the main gaps in knowledge and data as well as the main challenges and opportunities associated with the sustainable use of wild species;
* addressing uncertainties regarding the outcomes of policies and actions; and 
* providing an objective assessment of disputed facts and statistics. 

   1.1.1 Diverse uses of wild species by people and practices associated with them
All uses of wild species are imbedded in social-ecological systems, defined as complex adaptive systems that include social (human) and ecological (biophysical) subsystems in a two-way feedback relationship ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nCPgvN15","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2011; Chapin, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2011; Chapin, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30189,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AB942MBX"],"itemData":{"id":30189,"type":"chapter","container-title":"World Fisheries","event-place":"Oxford, UK","ISBN":"978-1-4443-9224-1","note":"DOI: 10.1002/9781444392241.ch2","page":"9-28","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","publisher-place":"Oxford, UK","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Restoring Unity: The Concept of Marine Social-Ecological Systems","title-short":"Restoring Unity","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/9781444392241.ch2","editor":[{"family":"Ommer","given":"Rosemary E."},{"family":"Perry","given":"R. Ian"},{"family":"Cochrane","given":"Kevern"},{"family":"Cury","given":"Philippe"}],"author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",3,17]]}}},{"id":34789,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3Q49BQQW"],"itemData":{"id":34789,"type":"book","call-number":"QH75 .P7528 2009","edition":"1st ed","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-0-387-73032-5","note":"OCLC: ocn422587121","number-of-pages":"401","publisher":"Springer","publisher-place":"New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Principles of ecosystem stewardship: resilience-based natural resource management in a changing world","title-short":"Principles of ecosystem stewardship","editor":[{"family":"Chapin","given":"F. Stuart"},{"family":"Kofinas","given":"Gary P."},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Because the social and ecological subsystems function as a coupled, interdependent and co-evolutionary system ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hxqrB9sO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes & Folke, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Berkes & Folke, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34574,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/44YM8BPL"],"itemData":{"id":34574,"type":"book","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-0-521-78562-4","number-of-pages":"459","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Linking social and ecological systems : management practices and social mechanisms for building resilience","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/de/academic/subjects/life-sciences/ecology-and-conservation/linking-social-and-ecological-systems-management-practices-and-social-mechanisms-building-resilience?format=PB","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret."},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, social-ecological systems constitute natural units of analysis of the sustainability of wild species use. An analytical framework proposed by Ostrom ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4X92ZMYG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ostrom, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Ostrom, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":36,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5F6IBU5V","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2NACCRB4"],"itemData":{"id":36,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1172133","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5939","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"419-422","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A General Framework for Analyzing Sustainability of Social-Ecological Systems","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1172133","volume":"325","author":[{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",7,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} distinguishes four interacting subsystems which comprise the resource units (e.g., fish), the resource systems (e.g., coastal fishery), the users (fishers) and the governance systems (organizations and rules that govern fishing on that coast). These components interact to produce outcomes at the system level, which are also influenced by external drivers (see Figure 1.1). For the purpose of this assessment, drivers are recognized as all the factors that directly or indirectly influence the use of wild species, such as the environmental, economic or cultural drivers (see Chapter 4).  
      A first step for undertaking an assessment of the sustainable use of wild species is to recognize the diverse ways in which people use wild species. This diversity results from the intersection of several dimensions (Figure 1.1), primarily: (i) the wild species used by people, which encompass all main taxa, including algae, animals, fungi and plants, from freshwater, marine and terrestrial habitats; (ii) the goods and services derived from those species, referred to in this assessment as "uses", including food, energy, materials, medicine or recreation (see section 1.3.4); (iii) the means or practices used to obtain benefits from wild species, including extractive and non-extractive practices; (iv) the destination and distribution of benefits derived from wild species, ranging from personal/family use or consumption to products sold in local informal markets, to those traded in the international market as global commodities; and (v) the statutory, customary and informal institutions, access regimes and rules that regulate uses and practices. The intensity of use of wild species and the scale of the operations involved vary enormously and are the main factors in evaluating sustainability and suitable forms of regulation. Furthermore, the components are affected by various drivers and the way they are managed can determine whether the outcomes of such complex social-ecological systems are consistent with sustainable use. 
      

Figure 1.1. A diagrammatic representation of the interactions between the end use of wild species, the practices associated with use, and the populations that are subjected to use within the context of the drivers and responses that may influence sustainability. CC-BY.
      The use of wild species involves various practices associated with their harvest and/or other kinds of interactions with them. For the purpose of the assessment, these practices have been classified into five categories, which are generally associated with the taxa utilized, i.e., fishing, gathering, terrestrial animal harvesting, logging and non-extractive practices. While most practices used to obtain benefits from wild species clearly fit within one of these five categories, there are some that are not so obviously classified. To avoid ambiguity, a working definition of the five practices for use across all chapters is developed in Section 1.3.4. Within each practice, uses may be typified according to several axes of diversity as described above, which characterize the users, the wild species and ecosystems used, their interaction and the context. 
      The wide diversity of uses within each practice needs to be considered in order to provide a balanced and representative assessment of the sustainable use of wild species, as well as a suitable analysis of possible pathways to further sustainability. For example, a discussion of policy measures that may be effective at achieving sustainable fishing needs to consider the full spectrum of tools and institutions, but also the different types of fishing categories and operations, as well as the local context. What may work for large, industrial fisheries from regions with strong management capacity and resources tends not to be suitable for the management of small-scale artisanal fisheries, especially in resource and capacity-limited situations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"r87r0G9U","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020b; Hilborn, 2020)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020b; Hilborn, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":34306,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C29NHAMV"],"itemData":{"id":34306,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Marine fish stocks are an important part of the world food system and are particularly important for many of the poorest people of the world. Most existing analyses suggest overfishing is increasing, and there is widespread concern that fish stocks are decreasing throughout most of the world. We assembled trends in abundance and harvest rate of stocks that are scientifically assessed, constituting half of the reported global marine fish catch. For these stocks, on average, abundance is increasing and is at proposed target levels. Compared with regions that are intensively managed, regions with less-developed fisheries management have, on average, 3-fold greater harvest rates and half the abundance as assessed stocks. Available evidence suggests that the regions without assessments of abundance have little fisheries management, and stocks are in poor shape. Increased application of area-appropriate fisheries science recommendations and management tools are still needed for sustaining fisheries in places where they are lacking.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1909726116","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"2218-2224","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effective fisheries management instrumental in improving fish stock status","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1909726116","volume":"117","author":[{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo Oscar"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Christopher M."},{"family":"Baum","given":"Julia K."},{"family":"Branch","given":"Trevor A."},{"family":"Costello","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Moor","given":"Carryn L.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Faraj","given":"Abdelmalek"},{"family":"Hively","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Olaf P."},{"family":"Kurota","given":"Hiroyuki"},{"family":"Little","given":"L. Richard"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"McClanahan","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Melnychuk","given":"Michael C."},{"family":"Minto","given":"Cóilín"},{"family":"Osio","given":"Giacomo Chato"},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Pons","given":"Maite"},{"family":"Segurado","given":"Susana"},{"family":"Szuwalski","given":"Cody S."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"Jono R."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Yimin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1,28]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This lack of unique solutions and the dependency of most suitable forms of governance and tools on the attributes of the social-ecological system involved and the operational scale and intensity of wild species use is common to all the practices. 
      The operational scales of extractive practices cover the full spectrum, ranging from manual gathering of a few grams of biomass to highly technological and capital-intensive harvesting methods that remove tons. While the total quantity extracted relative to the total population biomass or abundance (i.e., the harvest rate) has a direct implication on the impact and sustainability of the extraction, the operational scale of the extractive activity per se has implications on the need for regulations and the types of regulatory schemes that may be most effective to achieve sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"09R0wNsY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kurien & Willmann, 2009; Parma, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Kurien & Willmann, 2009; Parma, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30182,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/94FGDH7S"],"itemData":{"id":30182,"type":"chapter","container-title":"A Fishery Manager's Guidebook","event-place":"Oxford, UK","ISBN":"978-1-4443-1631-5","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1002/9781444316315.ch15","page":"404-424","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","publisher-place":"Oxford, UK","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Special Considerations for Small-Scale Fisheries Management in Developing Countries","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/9781444316315.ch15","editor":[{"family":"Cochrane","given":"Kevern L."},{"family":"Garcia","given":"Serge M."}],"author":[{"family":"Kurien","given":"John"},{"family":"Willmann","given":"Rolf"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",5,22]]}}},{"id":34969,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MVVRMNQX"],"itemData":{"id":34969,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bulletin of Marine Science","issue":"78","page":"411-427","title":"The good, the bad, and the ugly: learning from experience to achieve sustainable fisheries","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Parma","given":"A. M."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"R."},{"family":"Orensanz","given":"J.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Depending on the intensity of use, the population impact of a small-scale artisanal extraction is not necessarily lower than that of an industrial operation; however, the suitable means for keeping harvest rates within sustainable levels will likely be very different (see Table 6.5 in Chapter 6, section 6.4.4.5). Similarly, non-extractive uses include a wide range of operational scales which have different regulatory implications, from the individual experiences of watching wild species for recreation and inspiration to the high-end tourist industry built around whale watching, diving or game watching. Especially at the large-scale end of the spectrum, non-extractive recreational uses supporting the tourist industry can have negative impacts on wild species and their ecosystems if not properly conducted; hence the need for guidelines and regulations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GxLteSMW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Parsons & Brown, 2017; Tapper, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Parsons & Brown, 2017; Tapper, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30183,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IBENJ9QP"],"itemData":{"id":30183,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Whale-watching research encompasses a wide variety of disciplines and fields of study, from monitoring the biological impacts of whale-watching activities on cetaceans and assessments of the effectiveness of whale-watching management and regulations, to the sociological and economic\\n aspects of whale watching. This article is the latest in a series of annual digests, which describes the variety and findings of whale-watching studies published June 2015 to May 2016.","container-title":"Tourism in Marine Environments","DOI":"10.3727/154427317X694728","ISSN":"1544-273X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Tourism in Marine Environments","language":"en","page":"125-137","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recent Advances in Whale-Watching Research: 2015–2016","title-short":"Recent Advances in Whale-Watching Research","URL":"https://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.3727/154427317X694728","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Parsons","given":"E. C. M."},{"family":"Brown","given":"Danielle M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",5,15]]}}},{"id":30175,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V8AECTQY"],"itemData":{"id":30175,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","note":"ISBN  3 – 937429 – 10 – 7","page":"68","publisher":"UNEP/CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife Watching and Tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","author":[{"family":"Tapper","given":"Richard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5zDnp2Nz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Leslie, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Leslie, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1453,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YABIYX4Z"],"itemData":{"id":1453,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Environmental governance is more effective when the scales of ecological processes are well matched with the human institutions charged with managing human–environment interactions. The social-ecological systems (SESs) framework provides guidance on how to assess the social and ecological dimensions that contribute to sustainable resource use and management, but rarely if ever has been operationalized for multiple localities in a spatially explicit, quantitative manner. Here, we use the case of small-scale fisheries in Baja California Sur, Mexico, to identify distinct SES regions and test key aspects of coupled SESs theory. Regions that exhibit greater potential for social-ecological sustainability in one dimension do not necessarily exhibit it in others, highlighting the importance of integrative, coupled system analyses when implementing spatial planning and other ecosystem-based strategies.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1414640112","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"19","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"5979-5984","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Operationalizing the social-ecological systems framework to assess sustainability","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1414640112","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Leslie","given":"Heather M."},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Nenadovic","given":"Mateja"},{"family":"Sievanen","given":"Leila"},{"family":"Cavanaugh","given":"Kyle C."},{"family":"Cota-Nieto","given":"Juan José"},{"family":"Erisman","given":"Brad E."},{"family":"Finkbeiner","given":"Elena"},{"family":"Hinojosa-Arango","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Moreno-Báez","given":"Marcia"},{"family":"Nagavarapu","given":"Sriniketh"},{"family":"Reddy","given":"Sheila M. W."},{"family":"Sánchez-Rodríguez","given":"Alexandra"},{"family":"Siegel","given":"Katherine"},{"family":"Ulibarria-Valenzuela","given":"José Juan"},{"family":"Weaver","given":"Amy Hudson"},{"family":"Aburto-Oropeza","given":"Octavio"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",5,12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}The sustainable use of wild species can be viewed as one possible outcome of the complex social-ecological interactions involved, where the system is managed to maintain and/or enhance nature’s contributions to people, derived from the use of wild species, while ensuring that the productivity of the species (or population) used and the integrity and functioning of their ecosystems are preserved to meet current and future human needs. Outcomes may be highly heterogeneous across the different dimensions of the social-ecological system, and specific management interventions may be focused on strengthening sustainability of the resource system (e.g., reduce harvest rate to improve population status), the users (e.g., build users capacity) or the governance system (improve institutional arrangements) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aCckem7B","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Leslie, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Leslie, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1453,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YABIYX4Z"],"itemData":{"id":1453,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Environmental governance is more effective when the scales of ecological processes are well matched with the human institutions charged with managing human–environment interactions. The social-ecological systems (SESs) framework provides guidance on how to assess the social and ecological dimensions that contribute to sustainable resource use and management, but rarely if ever has been operationalized for multiple localities in a spatially explicit, quantitative manner. Here, we use the case of small-scale fisheries in Baja California Sur, Mexico, to identify distinct SES regions and test key aspects of coupled SESs theory. Regions that exhibit greater potential for social-ecological sustainability in one dimension do not necessarily exhibit it in others, highlighting the importance of integrative, coupled system analyses when implementing spatial planning and other ecosystem-based strategies.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1414640112","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"19","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"5979-5984","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Operationalizing the social-ecological systems framework to assess sustainability","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1414640112","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Leslie","given":"Heather M."},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Nenadovic","given":"Mateja"},{"family":"Sievanen","given":"Leila"},{"family":"Cavanaugh","given":"Kyle C."},{"family":"Cota-Nieto","given":"Juan José"},{"family":"Erisman","given":"Brad E."},{"family":"Finkbeiner","given":"Elena"},{"family":"Hinojosa-Arango","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Moreno-Báez","given":"Marcia"},{"family":"Nagavarapu","given":"Sriniketh"},{"family":"Reddy","given":"Sheila M. W."},{"family":"Sánchez-Rodríguez","given":"Alexandra"},{"family":"Siegel","given":"Katherine"},{"family":"Ulibarria-Valenzuela","given":"José Juan"},{"family":"Weaver","given":"Amy Hudson"},{"family":"Aburto-Oropeza","given":"Octavio"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",5,12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Furthermore, these complex interactive systems can be managed to produce wider ecosystem outcomes linked to sustainable use, beyond sustaining the wild species used and the direct benefits derived from them, such as to incentivise habitat and biodiversity conservation  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xmz1ssuR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Abensperg-Traun, 2009; Allen & Edwards, 1995)","plainCitation":"(Abensperg-Traun, 2009; Allen & Edwards, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34640,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WZBYGGSA"],"itemData":{"id":34640,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2008.12.034","ISSN":"00063207","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"948-963","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"CITES, sustainable use of wild species and incentive-driven conservation in developing countries, with an emphasis on southern Africa","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320709000615","volume":"142","author":[{"family":"Abensperg-Traun","given":"Max"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",5]]}}},{"id":34639,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QKF99EKX"],"itemData":{"id":34639,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Few issues in the conservation community rival the intesity of the debate over sustainable use of wild species. At on extreme, people advacate that sustainable use ensures conservation of the resource. Others view it as a guise to exploit wild species. Somewhere between these position, scientists point out that if impossible to guarantee sustainability given the complexity of human and biological factors that must be balanced. All these points ofview are Represented among the membership of the IUCN","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605300020950","ISSN":"13653008","note":"PMID: 313","title":"The Sustainable-Use Debate: Observations from IUCN","author":[{"family":"Allen","given":"Catherine M."},{"family":"Edwards","given":"Stephen R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or to improve human wellbeing and achieve sustainable development goals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nZSnkdAE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mahapatra & Mitchell, 1997; Pullanikkatil & Shackleton, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Mahapatra & Mitchell, 1997; Pullanikkatil & Shackleton, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":18,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3VCC6HMA"],"itemData":{"id":18,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/S0378-1127(97)00001-7","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"1-3","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"15-29","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Sustainable development of non-timber forest products: implication for forest management in India","title-short":"Sustainable development of non-timber forest products","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112797000017","volume":"94","author":[{"family":"Mahapatra","given":"Ajay"},{"family":"Mitchell","given":"C.Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997",6]]}}},{"id":6,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D2JBAK2R"],"itemData":{"id":6,"type":"book","collection-title":"Sustainable development goals series$dno poverty","event-place":"Cham, Switzerland","ISBN":"978-3-319-75580-9","language":"eng","number-of-pages":"160","publisher":"Springer","publisher-place":"Cham, Switzerland","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Poverty reduction through non-timber forest products: personal stories","title-short":"Poverty reduction through non-timber forest products","editor":[{"family":"Pullanikkatil","given":"Deepa"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

1.1.2 Placing the assessment within the IPBES conceptual framework and nature’s contributions to people  
The IPBES conceptual framework provides a simplified model of the interactions between people and the natural world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HOa6ScP4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az, Demissew, Carabias,, 2015; D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az, Demissew, Joly,, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Díaz, Demissew, Carabias,, 2015; Díaz, Demissew, Joly,, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35075,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PKF4C362"],"itemData":{"id":35075,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The first public product of the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES) is its Conceptual Framework. This conceptual and analytical tool, presented here in detail, will underpin all IPBES functions and provide structure and comparability to the syntheses that IPBES will produce at different spatial scales, on different themes, and in different regions. Salient innovative aspects of the IPBES Conceptual Framework are its transparent and participatory construction process and its explicit consideration of diverse scientific disciplines, stakeholders, and knowledge systems, including indigenous and local knowledge. Because the focus on co-construction of integrative knowledge is shared by an increasing number of initiatives worldwide, this framework should be useful beyond IPBES, for the wider research and knowledge-policy communities working on the links between nature and people, such as natural, social and engineering scientists, policy-makers at different levels, and decision-makers in different sectors of society.","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2014.11.002","ISSN":"18773435","note":"PMID: 25585296","page":"1–16","title":"The IPBES Conceptual Framework - connecting nature and people","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Demissew","given":"Sebsebe"},{"family":"Carabias","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Joly","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Lonsdale","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Ash","given":"Neville"},{"family":"Larigauderie","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Adhikari","given":"Jay Ram"},{"family":"Arico","given":"Salvatore"},{"family":"Bartuska","given":"Ann"},{"family":"Baste","given":"Ivar Andreas"},{"family":"Bilgin","given":"Adem"},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai M A"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Viviana Elsa"},{"family":"Duraiappah","given":"Anantha"},{"family":"Fischer","given":"Markus"},{"family":"Hill","given":"Rosemary"},{"family":"Koetz","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Leadley","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Lyver","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Georgina M."},{"family":"Martin-Lopez","given":"Berta"},{"family":"Okumura","given":"Michiko"},{"family":"Pacheco","given":"Diego"},{"family":"Pascual","given":"Unai"},{"family":"Reyers","given":"Belinda"},{"family":"Roth","given":"Eva"},{"family":"Saito","given":"Osamu"},{"family":"Scholes","given":"Robert John"},{"family":"Sharma","given":"Nalini"},{"family":"Tallis","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Thaman","given":"Randolph"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Yahara","given":"Tetsukazu"},{"family":"Hamid","given":"Zakri Abdul"},{"family":"Akosim","given":"Callistus"},{"family":"Al-Hafedh","given":"Yousef"},{"family":"Allahverdiyev","given":"Rashad"},{"family":"Amankwah","given":"Edward"},{"family":"Asah","given":"T. Stanley"},{"family":"Asfaw","given":"Zemede"},{"family":"Bartus","given":"Gabor"},{"family":"Brooks","given":"Anathea L."},{"family":"Caillaux","given":"Jorge"},{"family":"Dalle","given":"Gemedo"},{"family":"Darnaedi","given":"Dedy"},{"family":"Driver","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Erpul","given":"Gunay"},{"family":"Escobar-Eyzaguirre","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Failler","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Fouda","given":"Ali Moustafa Mokhtar"},{"family":"Fu","given":"Bojie"},{"family":"Gundimeda","given":"Haripriya"},{"family":"Hashimoto","given":"Shizuka"},{"family":"Homer","given":"Floyd"},{"family":"Lavorel","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Mala","given":"William Armand"},{"family":"Mandivenyi","given":"Wadzanayi"},{"family":"Matczak","given":"Piotr"},{"family":"Mbizvo","given":"Carmel"},{"family":"Mehrdadi","given":"Mehrasa"},{"family":"Metzger","given":"Jean Paul"},{"family":"Mikissa","given":"Jean Bruno"},{"family":"Moller","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Mooney","given":"Harold A."},{"family":"Mumby","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"},{"family":"Nesshover","given":"Carsten"},{"family":"Oteng-Yeboah","given":"Alfred Apau"},{"family":"Rubis","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Schultz","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Peggy"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"Rashid"},{"family":"Takeuchi","given":"Kazuhiko"},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Spencer"},{"family":"Verma","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Yeo-Chang","given":"Youn"},{"family":"Zlatanova","given":"Diana"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":15,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UMYETH7B","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U4MZ3EBT"],"itemData":{"id":15,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"After a long incubation period, the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES) is now underway. Underpinning all its activities is the IPBES Conceptual Framework (CF), a simplified model of the interactions between nature and people. Drawing on the legacy of previous large-scale environmental assessments, the CF goes further in explicitly embracing different disciplines and knowledge systems (including indigenous and local knowledge) in the co-construction of assessments of the state of the world's biodiversity and the benefits it provides to humans. The CF can be thought of as a kind of \\"Rosetta Stone\\" that highlights commonalities between diverse value sets and seeks to facilitate crossdisciplinary and crosscultural understanding. We argue that the CF will contribute to the increasing trend towards interdisciplinarity in understanding and managing the environment. Rather than displacing disciplinary science, however, we believe that the CF will provide new contexts of discovery and policy applications for it.","container-title":"PLoS biology","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.1002040","ISSN":"15457885","issue":"1","note":"PMID: 25585296","page":"e1002040","title":"A Rosetta Stone for nature's benefits to people","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002040","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Demissew","given":"Sebsebe"},{"family":"Joly","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Lonsdale","given":"W. Mark"},{"family":"Larigauderie","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which serves as a tool for assessing issues relevant to nature’s contributions to people and good quality of life. Since its approval by the IPBES Plenary in 2013, the conceptual framework underpins all IPBES deliberations and provides a consistent structure and terminology to IPBES products across spatial scales, themes, and regions. The conceptual framework includes six primary interlinked elements that operate across different spatial and temporal scales: 
* nature; 
* nature’s contributions to people; 
* anthropogenic assets; 
* institutions and governance systems and other indirect drivers of change; 
* direct drivers of change; and 
* good quality of life. 
      These elements have been conceived as broad, inclusive categories intended to be meaningful to a wide range of stakeholders while being flexible and amenable to interpretation as the conceptual framework is applied to particular topics of interest to the members and stakeholders. Sustainable use of wild species is a case in point. Previous IPBES assessments have quite reasonably treated “use” as one driver of change in natural systems, among many. In contrast, use of wild species is the central focus of this assessment. The flexibility of the IPBES conceptual framework enables its application to the assessment of the sustainable use of wild species. In the section that follows, this assessment shows how sustainable use can be mapped onto the conceptual framework (Figure 1.2.), highlighting some concepts that are particularly germane to this assessment. 

Figure 1.2. The sustainable use of wild species in the IPBES conceptual framework. The boxes and arrows of the central panel, delimited in grey, denote the elements of nature and society that are at the main focus of IPBES. In each box, the headlines in black are inclusive categories for stakeholders involved in IPBES and embrace the categories of science (in green) and equivalent categories according to other knowledge systems (in blue) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oWYEafox","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Diaz, Demissew, Carabias,, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Diaz, Demissew, Carabias,, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35075,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PKF4C362"],"itemData":{"id":35075,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The first public product of the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES) is its Conceptual Framework. This conceptual and analytical tool, presented here in detail, will underpin all IPBES functions and provide structure and comparability to the syntheses that IPBES will produce at different spatial scales, on different themes, and in different regions. Salient innovative aspects of the IPBES Conceptual Framework are its transparent and participatory construction process and its explicit consideration of diverse scientific disciplines, stakeholders, and knowledge systems, including indigenous and local knowledge. Because the focus on co-construction of integrative knowledge is shared by an increasing number of initiatives worldwide, this framework should be useful beyond IPBES, for the wider research and knowledge-policy communities working on the links between nature and people, such as natural, social and engineering scientists, policy-makers at different levels, and decision-makers in different sectors of society.","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2014.11.002","ISSN":"18773435","note":"PMID: 25585296","page":"1–16","title":"The IPBES Conceptual Framework - connecting nature and people","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Demissew","given":"Sebsebe"},{"family":"Carabias","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Joly","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Lonsdale","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Ash","given":"Neville"},{"family":"Larigauderie","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Adhikari","given":"Jay Ram"},{"family":"Arico","given":"Salvatore"},{"family":"Bartuska","given":"Ann"},{"family":"Baste","given":"Ivar Andreas"},{"family":"Bilgin","given":"Adem"},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai M A"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Viviana Elsa"},{"family":"Duraiappah","given":"Anantha"},{"family":"Fischer","given":"Markus"},{"family":"Hill","given":"Rosemary"},{"family":"Koetz","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Leadley","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Lyver","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Georgina M."},{"family":"Martin-Lopez","given":"Berta"},{"family":"Okumura","given":"Michiko"},{"family":"Pacheco","given":"Diego"},{"family":"Pascual","given":"Unai"},{"family":"Reyers","given":"Belinda"},{"family":"Roth","given":"Eva"},{"family":"Saito","given":"Osamu"},{"family":"Scholes","given":"Robert John"},{"family":"Sharma","given":"Nalini"},{"family":"Tallis","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Thaman","given":"Randolph"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Yahara","given":"Tetsukazu"},{"family":"Hamid","given":"Zakri Abdul"},{"family":"Akosim","given":"Callistus"},{"family":"Al-Hafedh","given":"Yousef"},{"family":"Allahverdiyev","given":"Rashad"},{"family":"Amankwah","given":"Edward"},{"family":"Asah","given":"T. Stanley"},{"family":"Asfaw","given":"Zemede"},{"family":"Bartus","given":"Gabor"},{"family":"Brooks","given":"Anathea L."},{"family":"Caillaux","given":"Jorge"},{"family":"Dalle","given":"Gemedo"},{"family":"Darnaedi","given":"Dedy"},{"family":"Driver","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Erpul","given":"Gunay"},{"family":"Escobar-Eyzaguirre","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Failler","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Fouda","given":"Ali Moustafa Mokhtar"},{"family":"Fu","given":"Bojie"},{"family":"Gundimeda","given":"Haripriya"},{"family":"Hashimoto","given":"Shizuka"},{"family":"Homer","given":"Floyd"},{"family":"Lavorel","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Mala","given":"William Armand"},{"family":"Mandivenyi","given":"Wadzanayi"},{"family":"Matczak","given":"Piotr"},{"family":"Mbizvo","given":"Carmel"},{"family":"Mehrdadi","given":"Mehrasa"},{"family":"Metzger","given":"Jean Paul"},{"family":"Mikissa","given":"Jean Bruno"},{"family":"Moller","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Mooney","given":"Harold A."},{"family":"Mumby","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"},{"family":"Nesshover","given":"Carsten"},{"family":"Oteng-Yeboah","given":"Alfred Apau"},{"family":"Rubis","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Schultz","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Peggy"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"Rashid"},{"family":"Takeuchi","given":"Kazuhiko"},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Spencer"},{"family":"Verma","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Yeo-Chang","given":"Youn"},{"family":"Zlatanova","given":"Diana"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This assessment foregrounds wild species uses as anthropogenic drivers and notes that many indigenous and local knowledge systems view such uses through a lens of people’s contributions to nature. Modified  from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EH3B7yo4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az, Demissew, Joly,, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Díaz, Demissew, Joly,, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":15,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UMYETH7B","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U4MZ3EBT"],"itemData":{"id":15,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"After a long incubation period, the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES) is now underway. Underpinning all its activities is the IPBES Conceptual Framework (CF), a simplified model of the interactions between nature and people. Drawing on the legacy of previous large-scale environmental assessments, the CF goes further in explicitly embracing different disciplines and knowledge systems (including indigenous and local knowledge) in the co-construction of assessments of the state of the world's biodiversity and the benefits it provides to humans. The CF can be thought of as a kind of \\"Rosetta Stone\\" that highlights commonalities between diverse value sets and seeks to facilitate crossdisciplinary and crosscultural understanding. We argue that the CF will contribute to the increasing trend towards interdisciplinarity in understanding and managing the environment. Rather than displacing disciplinary science, however, we believe that the CF will provide new contexts of discovery and policy applications for it.","container-title":"PLoS biology","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.1002040","ISSN":"15457885","issue":"1","note":"PMID: 25585296","page":"e1002040","title":"A Rosetta Stone for nature's benefits to people","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002040","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Demissew","given":"Sebsebe"},{"family":"Joly","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Lonsdale","given":"W. Mark"},{"family":"Larigauderie","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. CC-BY 4.0.

      For the core concepts in the framework, definitions here draw on those from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RyNme0Vf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az, Demissew, Carabias,, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Díaz, Demissew, Carabias,, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35075,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PKF4C362"],"itemData":{"id":35075,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The first public product of the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES) is its Conceptual Framework. This conceptual and analytical tool, presented here in detail, will underpin all IPBES functions and provide structure and comparability to the syntheses that IPBES will produce at different spatial scales, on different themes, and in different regions. Salient innovative aspects of the IPBES Conceptual Framework are its transparent and participatory construction process and its explicit consideration of diverse scientific disciplines, stakeholders, and knowledge systems, including indigenous and local knowledge. Because the focus on co-construction of integrative knowledge is shared by an increasing number of initiatives worldwide, this framework should be useful beyond IPBES, for the wider research and knowledge-policy communities working on the links between nature and people, such as natural, social and engineering scientists, policy-makers at different levels, and decision-makers in different sectors of society.","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2014.11.002","ISSN":"18773435","note":"PMID: 25585296","page":"1–16","title":"The IPBES Conceptual Framework - connecting nature and people","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Demissew","given":"Sebsebe"},{"family":"Carabias","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Joly","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Lonsdale","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Ash","given":"Neville"},{"family":"Larigauderie","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Adhikari","given":"Jay Ram"},{"family":"Arico","given":"Salvatore"},{"family":"Bartuska","given":"Ann"},{"family":"Baste","given":"Ivar Andreas"},{"family":"Bilgin","given":"Adem"},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai M A"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Viviana Elsa"},{"family":"Duraiappah","given":"Anantha"},{"family":"Fischer","given":"Markus"},{"family":"Hill","given":"Rosemary"},{"family":"Koetz","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Leadley","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Lyver","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Georgina M."},{"family":"Martin-Lopez","given":"Berta"},{"family":"Okumura","given":"Michiko"},{"family":"Pacheco","given":"Diego"},{"family":"Pascual","given":"Unai"},{"family":"Reyers","given":"Belinda"},{"family":"Roth","given":"Eva"},{"family":"Saito","given":"Osamu"},{"family":"Scholes","given":"Robert John"},{"family":"Sharma","given":"Nalini"},{"family":"Tallis","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Thaman","given":"Randolph"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Yahara","given":"Tetsukazu"},{"family":"Hamid","given":"Zakri Abdul"},{"family":"Akosim","given":"Callistus"},{"family":"Al-Hafedh","given":"Yousef"},{"family":"Allahverdiyev","given":"Rashad"},{"family":"Amankwah","given":"Edward"},{"family":"Asah","given":"T. Stanley"},{"family":"Asfaw","given":"Zemede"},{"family":"Bartus","given":"Gabor"},{"family":"Brooks","given":"Anathea L."},{"family":"Caillaux","given":"Jorge"},{"family":"Dalle","given":"Gemedo"},{"family":"Darnaedi","given":"Dedy"},{"family":"Driver","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Erpul","given":"Gunay"},{"family":"Escobar-Eyzaguirre","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Failler","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Fouda","given":"Ali Moustafa Mokhtar"},{"family":"Fu","given":"Bojie"},{"family":"Gundimeda","given":"Haripriya"},{"family":"Hashimoto","given":"Shizuka"},{"family":"Homer","given":"Floyd"},{"family":"Lavorel","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Mala","given":"William Armand"},{"family":"Mandivenyi","given":"Wadzanayi"},{"family":"Matczak","given":"Piotr"},{"family":"Mbizvo","given":"Carmel"},{"family":"Mehrdadi","given":"Mehrasa"},{"family":"Metzger","given":"Jean Paul"},{"family":"Mikissa","given":"Jean Bruno"},{"family":"Moller","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Mooney","given":"Harold A."},{"family":"Mumby","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"},{"family":"Nesshover","given":"Carsten"},{"family":"Oteng-Yeboah","given":"Alfred Apau"},{"family":"Rubis","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Schultz","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Peggy"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"Rashid"},{"family":"Takeuchi","given":"Kazuhiko"},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Spencer"},{"family":"Verma","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Yeo-Chang","given":"Youn"},{"family":"Zlatanova","given":"Diana"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Elements of special importance to sustainable use of wild species are put forward, with the benefit of insights that emerged from a participatory process intended, among other things, to ensure that understandings of the conceptual framework elements would be meaningful and relevant to all stakeholders. 
      The current assessment engaged indigenous peoples and local communities following the IPBES approach to recognizing and working with indigenous and local knowledge systems and seeks to include their perspective and insights for the sustainable use of wild species (see section 1.4). Through this process, participants suggested the phrase “people's contributions to nature” would better capture the multiple ways humans engage with, maintain, and, to varying degrees, produce nature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gjMLhhkm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b, 2019c)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b, 2019c)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":76,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEBMMIP2"],"itemData":{"id":76,"type":"report","event-place":"Paris, France","language":"English","page":"53","publisher":"UNESCO","publisher-place":"Paris, France","title":"Report of the first ILK dialogue workshop for the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species, held in Paris, France, on 6-7 May 2019.","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Daguitan, Florence","given":""},{"family":"Eduardo da Silva","given":"Franklin Paulo"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Viviana"},{"family":"Hernández Márquez","given":"Guadalupe Yesenia"},{"family":"Kaptoyo","given":"Edna"},{"family":"Kozanayi","given":"Witness"},{"family":"Nuri Sherpa","given":"Lakpa"},{"family":"Pictou","given":"Sherry"},{"family":"Shadrin","given":"Vyacheslav"},{"family":"Shulbaeva","given":"Polina"},{"family":"Springer","given":"Hannah Kihalani"},{"family":"Stryamets","given":"Nataliya"},{"family":"Tima","given":"Tilia Tao"},{"family":"Thevenin","given":"Thierry"},{"family":"Trakansuphakon","given":"Prasert"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":73,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TASKTWP4"],"itemData":{"id":73,"type":"report","event-place":"Montreal, Canada","language":"English","page":"40","publisher":"UNESCO","publisher-place":"Montreal, Canada","title":"Report of the ILK dialogue workshop for the first order draft of the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species, held in Montreal, Canada, on 8-9 October 2019.","URL":"https://ipbes.net/sites/default/files/inline-files/IPBES_SusUse_2ndILKDialogue_Report_final_forWeb_0.pdf","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Dicker","given":"Jason"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Viviana"},{"family":"Hernández Márquez","given":"Guadalupe Yesenia"},{"family":"Jacobs","given":"Lynn"},{"family":"Kozanayi","given":"Witness"},{"family":"Mulenkei","given":"Lucy"},{"family":"Pictou","given":"Sherry"},{"family":"Nuri Sherpa","given":"Lakpa"},{"family":"Shulbaeva","given":"Polina"},{"family":"Springer","given":"Hannah Kihalani"},{"family":"Stryamets","given":"Nataliya"},{"family":"Trakansuphakon","given":"Prasert"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In proposing the language of people’s contributions to nature, participants in indigenous and local knowledge dialogues explicitly insisted on the importance of conceptual symmetry between nature’s contributions to people and an expanded understanding of what is taken into account as direct and indirect anthropogenic drivers. In particular, their worldviews and experience suggested a need to re-examine the notion of human impacts as acting upon a separate pre-existing nature (see 2.2.4). Workshop participants were concerned that a language of “drivers” alone could not easily include concepts, such as caring for, nurturing, or stewarding nature, as well as broader concepts such as responsibility and reciprocity relative to the complex interactions between the natural world and human societies. The following definitions, as well as inclusive text added to the direct drivers box in Figure 1.2 endeavor to honor this input while following the definitions, approved by the IPBES Plenary, for the main elements of the IPBES conceptual framework ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"u42ix0pP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019d)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The following text reproduces partially or in full these definitions and provides additional insights compatible with the IPBES commitment to understand elements of the framework in an inclusive manner in the context of the current assessment.
* Nature encompasses the nonhuman world, with particular emphasis on living organisms, their diversity, their interactions among themselves but also with their abiotic environment. The current assessment is particularly attentive to the abundance, distribution, and traits of wild species that are used by people as well as the entanglements of nature and society that those uses imply. The inclusivity of this element is manifest in the multiple ways nature can be understood and engaged. Within the framing of the natural sciences, for example, nature includes all dimensions of biodiversity, from the genes to the ecosystems, including species and communities and their associated ecological, evolutionary and biogeochemical processes. Within the framework of ecological economics, it includes disciplinarily specific categories such as biotic natural resources, natural capital and natural assets. In the context of other social sciences and humanities disciplines, as well as interdisciplinary environmental sciences, nature is referred to with categories such as natural heritage, living environment, or the nonhuman. Finally, within the framing of a wide range of knowledge systems grounded outside science, nature includes categories such as Mother Earth (shared by many indigenous peoples and local communities around the world), Pachamama (South American Andes), se¯nluo´-wa`nxia`ng and tien-ti (East Asia), Country (Australia), fonua/vanua/whenua/ples (South Pacific Islands), Iwigara (northern Mexico), Ixofijmogen (southern Argentina and Chile), among many others ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CF7wfFDv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az, Demissew, Joly,, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Díaz, Demissew, Joly,, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":15,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UMYETH7B","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U4MZ3EBT"],"itemData":{"id":15,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"After a long incubation period, the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES) is now underway. Underpinning all its activities is the IPBES Conceptual Framework (CF), a simplified model of the interactions between nature and people. Drawing on the legacy of previous large-scale environmental assessments, the CF goes further in explicitly embracing different disciplines and knowledge systems (including indigenous and local knowledge) in the co-construction of assessments of the state of the world's biodiversity and the benefits it provides to humans. The CF can be thought of as a kind of \\"Rosetta Stone\\" that highlights commonalities between diverse value sets and seeks to facilitate crossdisciplinary and crosscultural understanding. We argue that the CF will contribute to the increasing trend towards interdisciplinarity in understanding and managing the environment. Rather than displacing disciplinary science, however, we believe that the CF will provide new contexts of discovery and policy applications for it.","container-title":"PLoS biology","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.1002040","ISSN":"15457885","issue":"1","note":"PMID: 25585296","page":"e1002040","title":"A Rosetta Stone for nature's benefits to people","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002040","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Demissew","given":"Sebsebe"},{"family":"Joly","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Lonsdale","given":"W. Mark"},{"family":"Larigauderie","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Across different knowledge systems, both scientific and non-scientific, the degree to which humans are considered part of nature varies considerably. 
* Nature’s contributions to people are all the contributions of nature to the quality of life of humans, as individuals, societies or humanity as a whole that emerge from the interactions of nature with anthropogenic assets, institutions, and governance. In particular, the use of wild species makes possible a wide range of direct and tangible contributions to people that include food, energy, medicine, materials, physical and psychological experiences, learning and inspiration, and identity. In some knowledge systems such contributions may be understood as, for example, “ecosystem goods and services,” while in others they may better be seen as “nature’s gifts.” Furthermore, nature’s contributions to people have replaced nature’s benefits to people (found in earlier versions of the conceptual framework) to not only accommodate the multiple ways nature’s contributions to people are understood and enacted, but also to better reflect the fact that contributions may have detrimental or beneficial consequences for people’s quality of life. 
* Good quality of life is the achievement of a fulfilled human life. Visions, concepts and indicators of good quality of life are highly diverse, both in cultural roots and in geographical application. Approaches applied internationally can be based solely on the economic aspects of social systems (e.g., gross domestic product per capita), be more inclusive in the social factors considered (e.g., human development index, inclusive wealth) or holistic framings (e.g., living in harmony, gross national happiness index). Other more culturally specific and place-based approaches include Sumak Kawsay/Buen vivir (Central Andes), teko porã (Paraguay), vida plena (Amazonian basin), or shizen kyosei shakai (Japan) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VZCLBgpJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az, Demissew, Joly,, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Díaz, Demissew, Joly,, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":15,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UMYETH7B","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U4MZ3EBT"],"itemData":{"id":15,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"After a long incubation period, the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES) is now underway. Underpinning all its activities is the IPBES Conceptual Framework (CF), a simplified model of the interactions between nature and people. Drawing on the legacy of previous large-scale environmental assessments, the CF goes further in explicitly embracing different disciplines and knowledge systems (including indigenous and local knowledge) in the co-construction of assessments of the state of the world's biodiversity and the benefits it provides to humans. The CF can be thought of as a kind of \\"Rosetta Stone\\" that highlights commonalities between diverse value sets and seeks to facilitate crossdisciplinary and crosscultural understanding. We argue that the CF will contribute to the increasing trend towards interdisciplinarity in understanding and managing the environment. Rather than displacing disciplinary science, however, we believe that the CF will provide new contexts of discovery and policy applications for it.","container-title":"PLoS biology","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.1002040","ISSN":"15457885","issue":"1","note":"PMID: 25585296","page":"e1002040","title":"A Rosetta Stone for nature's benefits to people","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002040","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Demissew","given":"Sebsebe"},{"family":"Joly","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Lonsdale","given":"W. Mark"},{"family":"Larigauderie","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Within the context of a wide range of good quality of life conceptualizations, wild species are vitally important as sources of nutrients, energy, medicine and other materials; a current and potential source of livelihoods; and a key contributor to rich and meaningful cultural and spiritual lives. A society’s quality of life and the vision of what constitutes a good quality of life strongly influence all other elements in the conceptual framework. For the purposes of this assessment, people’s quality of life is regarded not just as an outcome of nature’s contributions to people but also as influencing direct anthropogenic drivers that impact and shape nature. 
* ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ss3kyoKe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b, 2019c)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b, 2019c)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":76,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEBMMIP2"],"itemData":{"id":76,"type":"report","event-place":"Paris, France","language":"English","page":"53","publisher":"UNESCO","publisher-place":"Paris, France","title":"Report of the first ILK dialogue workshop for the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species, held in Paris, France, on 6-7 May 2019.","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Daguitan, Florence","given":""},{"family":"Eduardo da Silva","given":"Franklin Paulo"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Viviana"},{"family":"Hernández Márquez","given":"Guadalupe Yesenia"},{"family":"Kaptoyo","given":"Edna"},{"family":"Kozanayi","given":"Witness"},{"family":"Nuri Sherpa","given":"Lakpa"},{"family":"Pictou","given":"Sherry"},{"family":"Shadrin","given":"Vyacheslav"},{"family":"Shulbaeva","given":"Polina"},{"family":"Springer","given":"Hannah Kihalani"},{"family":"Stryamets","given":"Nataliya"},{"family":"Tima","given":"Tilia Tao"},{"family":"Thevenin","given":"Thierry"},{"family":"Trakansuphakon","given":"Prasert"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":73,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TASKTWP4"],"itemData":{"id":73,"type":"report","event-place":"Montreal, Canada","language":"English","page":"40","publisher":"UNESCO","publisher-place":"Montreal, Canada","title":"Report of the ILK dialogue workshop for the first order draft of the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species, held in Montreal, Canada, on 8-9 October 2019.","URL":"https://ipbes.net/sites/default/files/inline-files/IPBES_SusUse_2ndILKDialogue_Report_final_forWeb_0.pdf","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Dicker","given":"Jason"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Viviana"},{"family":"Hernández Márquez","given":"Guadalupe Yesenia"},{"family":"Jacobs","given":"Lynn"},{"family":"Kozanayi","given":"Witness"},{"family":"Mulenkei","given":"Lucy"},{"family":"Pictou","given":"Sherry"},{"family":"Nuri Sherpa","given":"Lakpa"},{"family":"Shulbaeva","given":"Polina"},{"family":"Springer","given":"Hannah Kihalani"},{"family":"Stryamets","given":"Nataliya"},{"family":"Trakansuphakon","given":"Prasert"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}Direct drivers refer to natural and anthropogenic factors that act directly on nature. Natural drivers are abiotic forces that have generally been regarded as beyond human control, such as disturbances caused by earthquakes or extreme climate events, although the concept of the anthropocene calls attention to the increasing degree to which human actions may influence these systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"niWRnNGb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Steffen, 2011, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Steffen, 2011, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1454,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/86NH52AD"],"itemData":{"id":1454,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"AMBIO","DOI":"10.1007/s13280-011-0185-x","ISSN":"0044-7447, 1654-7209","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"AMBIO","language":"en","page":"739-761","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Anthropocene: From Global Change to Planetary Stewardship","title-short":"The Anthropocene","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s13280-011-0185-x","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Steffen","given":"Will"},{"family":"Persson","given":"Åsa"},{"family":"Deutsch","given":"Lisa"},{"family":"Zalasiewicz","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Richardson","given":"Katherine"},{"family":"Crumley","given":"Carole"},{"family":"Crutzen","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Gordon","given":"Line"},{"family":"Molina","given":"Mario"},{"family":"Ramanathan","given":"Veerabhadran"},{"family":"Rockström","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"Marten"},{"family":"Schellnhuber","given":"Hans Joachim"},{"family":"Svedin","given":"Uno"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",11]]}}},{"id":1452,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ICCWGA9G"],"itemData":{"id":1452,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"We explore the risk that self-reinforcing feedbacks could push the Earth System toward a planetary threshold that, if crossed, could prevent stabilization of the climate at intermediate temperature rises and cause continued warming on a “Hothouse Earth” pathway even as human emissions are reduced. Crossing the threshold would lead to a much higher global average temperature than any interglacial in the past 1.2 million years and to sea levels significantly higher than at any time in the Holocene. We examine the evidence that such a threshold might exist and where it might be. If the threshold is crossed, the resulting trajectory would likely cause serious disruptions to ecosystems, society, and economies. Collective human action is required to steer the Earth System away from a potential threshold and stabilize it in a habitable interglacial-like state. Such action entails stewardship of the entire Earth System—biosphere, climate, and societies—and could include decarbonization of the global economy, enhancement of biosphere carbon sinks, behavioral changes, technological innovations, new governance arrangements, and transformed social values.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1810141115","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"33","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"8252-8259","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trajectories of the Earth System in the Anthropocene","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1810141115","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Steffen","given":"Will"},{"family":"Rockström","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Richardson","given":"Katherine"},{"family":"Lenton","given":"Timothy M."},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Liverman","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Summerhayes","given":"Colin P."},{"family":"Barnosky","given":"Anthony D."},{"family":"Cornell","given":"Sarah E."},{"family":"Crucifix","given":"Michel"},{"family":"Donges","given":"Jonathan F."},{"family":"Fetzer","given":"Ingo"},{"family":"Lade","given":"Steven J."},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"Marten"},{"family":"Winkelmann","given":"Ricarda"},{"family":"Schellnhuber","given":"Hans Joachim"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",8,14]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In contrast, direct anthropogenic drivers comprise factors that are under human control. This assessment focuses on the practices related to the use of wild species (i.e., fishing, gathering, terrestrial animal harvesting, logging, and non-extractive practices) as direct drivers (see Chapter 3). In scientific knowledge systems, direct anthropogenic drivers often are conceived as external factors that change or transform a pre-existing nature. In other knowledge systems, including much indigenous and local knowledge, humans are considered part of nature. Indeed, nature itself is co-produced through interactions between humans and nonhumans, with both playing an active role. Ideally, but far from always, this process of co-production is characterized by mutual caring and responsibility. However, just as nature’s contributions to people may be beneficial or adverse, people’s contributions to nature can include positive and negative outcomes. For example, the age and sex classes targeted by some hunters may disrupt breeding success or increase population productivity. This perspective explicitly contains conceptual room for a range of actual and potential human “contributions” to nature and can facilitate identification of and support for existing sustainable uses of wild species.
* Institutions and governance systems and other indirect drivers include human actions and decisions that indirectly affect nature by altering and influencing human practices relating to the use of wild species. Along with good quality of life, they influence and mediate direct anthropogenic drivers that affect nature both positively and negatively. Indirect drivers include economic, demographic, institutional, technological and cultural processes (see Chapters 2, 4 and 5). Special attention is given, among indirect drivers, to the role of institutions and governance systems, including formal and customary systems of access to land and property rights as well as those emerging from indigenous and local knowledge systems (see Chapter 6). Indirect drivers include, for example, socially shared rules, legislative arrangements, international regimes such as agreements for the protection of endangered species, and economic policies. It is important to note that this assessment does not deal separately with direct and indirect drivers, which are dealt with collectively in Chapter 4. The reason for this approach is that sustainable use is an outcome of a system that includes nature, and the drivers acting on it, as well as the institutions and governance systems that act as drivers of uses and practices (as depicted in Figure 1.1). Sustainable use is therefore affected by multiple interacting drivers comprising both direct and indirect drivers considered by IPBES the conceptual framework. Nevertheless, the assessment followed the IPBES model by trying to identify the key underlying drivers which may provide entry points and pathways to improve sustainable use. 
* Anthropogenic assets refer to knowledge (including indigenous and local knowledge and technical or scientific knowledge), technology (both physical objects and procedures), work, financial assets, and built infrastructure. They are closely tied to institutions, governance and other indirect drivers as indicated by the line linking them in the conceptual framework figure (Figure 1.2), which suggests that accumulated assets and systems of decision making work together to influence and shape interactions between nature and human society. While such interactions clearly emerge as nature’s contributions to people, they also are integral to those practices and uses that directly produce nature. In this case, anthropogenic assets may contribute as much to people’s contribution to nature as they do to nature’s contributions to people.
""" ;
