\"reclaimed\\"; (2) drawing on lessons from fisheries to distinguish between wild and farmed products, and associated activities; (3) moving beyond \\n              product \\n              towards \\n              activity \\n              classifications to capture gathering that may not be accounted \\n for under crops or forest products because it takes place across landscapes and outside of these sectoral boundaries.","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554820828671553","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"101-119","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Into the wild: disentangling non-wood terms and definitions for improved forest statistics","title-short":"Into the wild","URL":"https://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/146554820828671553","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Muir","given":"G.F."},{"family":"Sorrenti","given":"S."},{"family":"Vantomme","given":"P."},{"family":"Vidale","given":"E."},{"family":"Masiero","given":"M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In this worldview, the social and ecological dimensions of the use of wild species are inseparable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AA6cFDFk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nadasdy, 2007; Polfus, 2016; Robinson & Raven, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Nadasdy, 2007; Polfus, 2016; Robinson & Raven, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33681,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VT2R5J5N"],"itemData":{"id":33681,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"American Ethnologist","DOI":"10.1525/ae.2007.34.1.25","ISSN":"00940496","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"25-43","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The gift in the animal: The ontology of hunting and human-animal sociality","title-short":"The gift in the animal","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1525/ae.2007.34.1.25","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Nadasdy","given":"Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",2]]}}},{"id":1436,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SZNGDJ2D"],"itemData":{"id":1436,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-08284-210218","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art18","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"?eghágots'enet? (learning together): the importance of indigenous perspectives in the identification of biological variation","title-short":"&#321;egha&#769;gots'enete&#808; (learning together)","URL":"http://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol21/iss2/art18/","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Polfus","given":"Jean L."},{"family":"Manseau","given":"Micheline"},{"family":"Simmons","given":"Deborah"},{"family":"Neyelle","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Bayha","given":"Walter"},{"family":"Andrew","given":"Frederick"},{"family":"Andrew","given":"Leon"},{"family":"Klütsch","given":"Cornelya F. C."},{"family":"Rice","given":"Keren"},{"family":"Wilson","given":"Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":1404,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G44UJY54"],"itemData":{"id":1404,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trade For Development News","note":"EIF, WTO, Geneva","title":"Ethical Trade in Natural Products based on Traditional Knowledge","URL":"https://trade4devnews.enhancedif.org/en/news/ethical-trade-natural-products-based-traditional-knowledge","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"D. F."},{"family":"Raven","given":"M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. To be sustainable, the use of wild species should ensure the wellbeing of both humans and other species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"j3KtwSCb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sangha, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Sangha, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30198,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/36SK8F9V"],"itemData":{"id":30198,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2015.06.008","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"197-206","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ecosystems and indigenous well-being: An integrated framework","title-short":"Ecosystems and indigenous well-being","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2351989415000700","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Sangha","given":"Kamaljit K."},{"family":"Le Brocque","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Costanza","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Cadet-James","given":"Yvonne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Seen through this lens, to choose between human wellbeing and that of wild species is both unethical and untenable. 
      In defining sustainable use, the following aspects, which are discussed thoroughly in Chapter 2, are critical to this assessment:
* Sustainable use is an outcome of social-ecological systems in which nature’s contributions to people derived from the harvest or non-extractive use of wild species is managed to meet current human needs while maintaining or co-creating natural systems that promote the continued survival of the species being used and the ongoing provision of nature’s contributions to people. It is not, and cannot be, a static endpoint because the target species, the ecosystems that support them, and the social systems within which uses occur are dynamic and change over time as well as in space. Consequently, the sustainability of use is an ongoing adaptive process, which may be depicted as follows: (i) assess status and trends in wild species under use, (ii) identify drivers of (un)sustainability, (iii), adapt uses and management (which may include, if necessary, stopping use for a period of time), (iv) re-assess after a given time interval and re-adapt use and management if needed. 
* Sustainable use has a narrower and more specific meaning than sustainable development or sustainable management and these should not be used as synonyms. Sustainable development has a clear economic basis and can be seen as a quest to reconcile economic growth with social and ecological challenges and problems (often conceptualized through the three-pillars, social-economic-environmental, diagram, see ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"svfDrpqO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Purvis, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Purvis, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31701,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IRWPBZRX"],"itemData":{"id":31701,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability Science","DOI":"10.1007/s11625-018-0627-5","ISSN":"1862-4065, 1862-4057","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Sustain Sci","language":"en","page":"681-695","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Three pillars of sustainability: in search of conceptual origins","title-short":"Three pillars of sustainability","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11625-018-0627-5","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Purvis","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Mao","given":"Yong"},{"family":"Robinson","given":"Darren"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). Sustainable management also encompasses a broader array of actions related to the reduction of environmental and social and economic impacts associated with any activity, not only use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gSlNkTrO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cooney, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Cooney, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30664,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GIQ6WPGX"],"itemData":{"id":30664,"type":"report","event-place":"White Oak Plantation, Florida","page":"76","publisher":"IUCN Species Survival Commission's Sustainable Use Specialist Group","publisher-place":"White Oak Plantation, Florida","title":"Sustainable Use: Concepts, Ambiguities, Challenges","URL":"https://www.iucn.org/sites/dev/files/whiteoakmtgfinalbackgroundjuly07.pdf","author":[{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
* Sustainable use emerges from social-ecological systems that meet human needs without compromising ecosystem health. Sustainable use is thus not limited to anthropocentric considerations (i.e., the sustainability of the use for the benefit of people) or to ecological/environmental considerations (i.e., the conservation of the target resource from an ecosystem perspective), but it encompasses both social and ecological considerations as well as the multiple aspects of their interactions.
* Human views on sustainable use are diverse, resulting from local, indigenous and scientific knowledge that should be all appreciated as relevant experiments in sustainable use from which people can learn.
* For practical purposes, especially for an assessment of this nature, the definition of sustainable use or what is assessed as being sustainable, needs to be precise enough that assessors can determine whether or not use is sustainable.
* Sustainability should therefore be clearly defined through a set of specific targets or indicators in the social and ecological domains. Ideally, this set of indicators should be developed jointly by all the actors of the social-ecological system.
* Animal welfare and animal protection are important considerations receiving increasing social, ethical and legal consideration worldwide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sYL4lQsO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Independent  Group  of  Scientists  appointed  by  the  Secretary-General, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Independent  Group  of  Scientists  appointed  by  the  Secretary-General, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1409,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XTUTRR2N"],"itemData":{"id":1409,"type":"report","event-place":"New York","page":"252","publisher":"United Nations","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Global  Sustainable  Development Report 2019: The Future is Now – Science for Achieving Sustainable Development","URL":"https://sustainabledevelopment.un.org/content/documents/24797GSDR_report_2019.pdf","author":[{"family":"Independent  Group  of  Scientists  appointed  by  the  Secretary-General","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Animal welfare is increasingly incorporated into the concepts of sustainable use of wild species, but it was not identified in the scoping report for the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species  (Annex IV to decision IPBES-5/1). Therefore, animal welfare was not addressed in this assessment, but this issue would deserve a dedicated assessment. 

Figure 1.3.  Different conceptions  of  the sustainable use of wild species, illustrating the increasing degree of complexity associated with them as well as the availability of methods and indicators to evaluate sustainable use. Most international organizations consider only the "single species" dimension when evaluating sustainable use and managing human uses of wild species, others have introduced the "multi-species" dimension, while some seek to use an ecosystem approach (for example, taking into account the impacts on the functioning of ecosystems and habitats). The economic and social dimensions are much more rarely taken into account by international organizations, while the indigenous and local knowledge approach is often more integrative and considers the socio-economic and ecological dimensions as inseparable. The availability of indicators and methods by dimensions is approximated by the color level and is inversely proportional to the complexity or the number of dimensions, illustrating the more comprehensive development of indicators at the species level and the general lack of indicators for higher level ecological dimensions or social dimensions. CC-BY.

1.3.2 Unpacking the definition of wild species 
The scoping report for the sustainable use assessment (Annex IV to decision IPBES-5/1) identified the need for a general definition of wild species that could be applied in different assessments and across conventions. Despite the widespread reference to specimens from the the wild, or wild species, in both the academic literature and policy documents (see Chapter 2), there is no universally accepted definition and the majority of papers do not refer to a clear definition for wild species.
      The definition of wild species adopted for the sustainable use assessment (1.3) clarifies two aspects that are relevant to this assessment. First, the definition focuses attention on wild populations and not on the species as an entirety. This is because populations of the same species can span a continuum from those that do not experience any human intervention to being fully domesticated. The naming of domesticated taxa in relation to their wild progenitors, linked to debates about whether domesticated forms should be recognized as distinct species, has been extensively discussed in terms of both the International Code of Zoological Nomenclature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FPv7LkNq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gentry, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Gentry, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":13,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2CUSSW4V"],"itemData":{"id":13,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Archaeological Science","DOI":"10.1016/j.jas.2003.10.006","ISSN":"03054403","issue":"5","note":"section: 645","page":"645-651","title":"The naming of wild animal species and their domestic derivatives","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Gentry","given":"Anthea"},{"family":"Clutton-Brock","given":"Juliet"},{"family":"Groves","given":"Colin P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and the International Code of Nomenclature for Algae, Fungi and Plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"o5JxYTQw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Spooner, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Spooner, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/27WVNBME"],"itemData":{"id":5,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Horticultural Reviews","page":"1–60","title":"Plant Nomenclature and Taxonomy An Horticultural and Agronomic Perspective","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Spooner","given":"David"},{"family":"Hetterscheid","given":"Wilbert","suffix":"L.A."},{"family":"Berg","given":"Ronald","non-dropping-particle":"van den","suffix":"G."},{"family":"Brandenburg","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As noted by Gentry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Zm0crHEB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gentry, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Gentry, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":13,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2CUSSW4V"],"itemData":{"id":13,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Archaeological Science","DOI":"10.1016/j.jas.2003.10.006","ISSN":"03054403","issue":"5","note":"section: 645","page":"645-651","title":"The naming of wild animal species and their domestic derivatives","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Gentry","given":"Anthea"},{"family":"Clutton-Brock","given":"Juliet"},{"family":"Groves","given":"Colin P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} for animals, some domesticated forms have scientific names which are distinct from those applied to their wild ancestors, but the majority of wild progenitor species and their domestic derivatives are considered to be the same species and this is also true for plants, algae and fungi. Some examples include Coffea arabica for wild and domesticated coffee ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZkeWgLPt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wiersum, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Wiersum, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35002,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4Z6YZ85G"],"itemData":{"id":35002,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","DOI":"10.1080/14728028.2008.9752614","ISSN":"1472-8028 2164-3075","issue":"1","note":"section: 9","page":"9-21","title":"Certification of Wild Coffee in Ethiopia: Experiences and Challenges","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Wiersum","given":"K. F."},{"family":"Gole","given":"T. W."},{"family":"Gatzweiler","given":"F."},{"family":"Volkmann","given":"J."},{"family":"Bognetteau","given":"E."},{"family":"Wirtu","given":"Olani"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Salmo salar for wild and cultured Atlantic Salmon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"S0AGdPi0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Teletchea & Fontaine, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Teletchea & Fontaine, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30172,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ELTR5PMC"],"itemData":{"id":30172,"type":"article-journal","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12006","page":"181–195","title":"Levels of domestication in fi sh : implications for the sustainable future of aquaculture","author":[{"family":"Teletchea","given":"Fabrice"},{"family":"Fontaine","given":"Pascal"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"},  Agaricus bisporus (button mushrooms) and Struthio camelus for wild and domesticated forms of ostrich ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5gmHq76Q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Spinu, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Spinu, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30188,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M38PVZF2"],"itemData":{"id":30188,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"British Poultry Science","DOI":"10.1080/00071669986981","ISSN":"0007-1668, 1466-1799","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"British Poultry Science","language":"en","page":"613-618","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Haematological and immunological variables in a domesticated and wild subspecies of ostrich (Struthio camelus)","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/00071669986981","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Spinu","given":"M."},{"family":"Spinu","given":"O."},{"family":"Degen","given":"A.A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For precision, the assessment should refer consistently to wild populations of particular species but this is often not practical and any reference to wild species should be read as an abbreviation for wild populations of a species.
      The second clarification relates to the term ‘wild’ which, although commonly used, is open to different interpretations and needs to be more precisely defined in this assessment. A common starting point is to distinguish wild forms from domesticated ones. For example, the IUCN defines wildlife as living things that are neither human nor domesticated ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wEbc6dbx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2021a)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2021a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30187,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D2XQEQUB"],"itemData":{"id":30187,"type":"document","language":"EN","title":"IUCN Glossary of Definitions","URL":"https://www.iucn.org/resources/publications/publishing-iucn","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",5,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Convention on Biological Diversity does not define wild but, instead, defines domesticated to mean species in which the evolutionary process has been influenced to meet human needs. By implication, wild populations are those that don’t fit the definition of domesticated. 
      It is well established in both scientific and indigenous and local knowledge systems that a binary separation between ‘wild’ and ‘domesticated’ populations does not exist. Scientific analyses of use systems for plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3CGpOMKE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Muir, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Muir, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1431,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/26MRGHXT"],"itemData":{"id":1431,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As scientists strive to make nature's value visible, a large portion of forests and wild biodiversity known as non-wood forest products (NWFPs) continues to remain largely invisible and unaccounted for. At the core of the problem is wide disaccord over what is a NWFP (and correlate \\n terms), a debate which has been running in circles for nearly three decades. This paper reviews existing terms and definitions, with the aim of improving forest statistics and the visibility of NWFPs. The paper starts by (1) clarifying boundaries between agricultural and forest products, so \\n forest products currently under agriculture can be \\"reclaimed\\"; (2) drawing on lessons from fisheries to distinguish between wild and farmed products, and associated activities; (3) moving beyond \\n              product \\n              towards \\n              activity \\n              classifications to capture gathering that may not be accounted \\n for under crops or forest products because it takes place across landscapes and outside of these sectoral boundaries.","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554820828671553","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"101-119","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Into the wild: disentangling non-wood terms and definitions for improved forest statistics","title-short":"Into the wild","URL":"https://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/146554820828671553","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Muir","given":"G.F."},{"family":"Sorrenti","given":"S."},{"family":"Vantomme","given":"P."},{"family":"Vidale","given":"E."},{"family":"Masiero","given":"M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, terrestrial animals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LmO9Qnrz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Child, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Child, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RI3HJDKI"],"itemData":{"id":7,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13299","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"1106-1119","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A framework to measure the wildness of managed large vertebrate populations","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/cobi.13299","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Child","given":"Matthew F."},{"family":"Selier","given":"S. A. Jeanetta"},{"family":"Radloff","given":"Frans G. T."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"W. Andrew"},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Nel","given":"Lizanne"},{"family":"Power","given":"R. John"},{"family":"Birss","given":"Coral"},{"family":"Okes","given":"Nicola C."},{"family":"Peel","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Mallon","given":"David"},{"family":"Davies?Mostert","given":"Harriet"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and aquatic organisms ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ES0MSLmU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bell, 2006; Hilborn & Hilborn, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Bell, 2006; Hilborn & Hilborn, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30203,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3JVSVII7"],"itemData":{"id":30203,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2006.03.008","ISSN":"01657836","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Research","language":"en","page":"1-8","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Restocking and stock enhancement of coastal fisheries: Potential, problems and progress","title-short":"Restocking and stock enhancement of coastal fisheries","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0165783606001147","volume":"80","author":[{"family":"Bell","given":"Johann D."},{"family":"Bartley","given":"Devin M."},{"family":"Lorenzen","given":"Kai"},{"family":"Loneragan","given":"Neil R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",8]]}}},{"id":30325,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UF5G3H9S"],"itemData":{"id":30325,"type":"book","ISBN":"0-19-257612-7","publisher":"Oxford University Press","title":"Ocean Recovery: A sustainable future for global fisheries?","author":[{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ulrike"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} recognize  intermediate stages between wild and domesticated. At one extreme, wild populations are identified as those that retain the capacity to evolve autonomously under conditions where genetic diversity enables natural selection to produce adaptation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RSelLZQf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mallon & Stanley Price, 2013; Redford, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Mallon & Stanley Price, 2013; Redford, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":22,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SJ8MGI2A"],"itemData":{"id":22,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S003060531300121X","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Oryx","language":"en","page":"467-468","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The fall of the wild","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S003060531300121X/type/journal_article","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Mallon","given":"David P."},{"family":"Stanley Price","given":"Mark R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",10]]}}},{"id":33,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/48MQPEG7","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CT8PSFQD"],"itemData":{"id":33,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The conservation of species is one of the foundations of conservation biology. Successful species conservation has often been defined as simply the avoidance of extinction. We argue that this focus, although important, amounts to practicing conservation at the “emergency room door,” and will never be a sufficient approach to conserving species. Instead, we elaborate a positive definition of species conservation on the basis of six attributes and propose a categorization of different states of species conservation using the extent of human management and the degree to which each of the attributes is conserved. These states can be used to develop a taxonomy of species “recovery” that acknowledges there are multiple stable points defined by ecological and social factors. With this approach, we hope to contribute to a new, optimistic conservation biology that is not based on underambitious goals and that seeks to create the conditions under which Earth's biological systems can thrive.","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1525/bio.2011.61.1.9","ISSN":"00063568","note":"PMID: 57643525","title":"What Does It Mean to Successfully Conserve a (Vertebrate) Species?","author":[{"family":"Redford","given":"Kent H."},{"family":"Amato","given":"George"},{"family":"Baillie","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Beldomenico","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Bennett","given":"Elizabeth L."},{"family":"Clum","given":"Nancy"},{"family":"Cook","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Fonseca","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Hedges","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Launay","given":"Frederic"},{"family":"Lieberman","given":"Susan"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Georgina M."},{"family":"Murayama","given":"Akira"},{"family":"Putnam","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Robinson","given":"John G."},{"family":"Rosenbaum","given":"Howard"},{"family":"Sanderson","given":"Eric W."},{"family":"Stuart","given":"Simon N."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Thorbjarnarson","given":"John"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Along the continuum towards domestication, populations may be managed by people, to a greater or lesser extent, to increase accessibility and productivity or to promote the forms with traits most sought after by the people concerned. In this transition, the population may increasingly depend on human intervention to survive one or more life history stage, till finally at the other extreme the population is selectively bred over multiple generations to serve human needs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FAFbCI8d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vigne, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Vigne, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5EVDDXIB"],"itemData":{"id":8,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Comptes Rendus Biologies","DOI":"10.1016/j.crvi.2010.12.009","ISSN":"16310691","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Comptes Rendus Biologies","language":"en","page":"171-181","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The origins of animal domestication and husbandry: A major change in the history of humanity and the biosphere","title-short":"The origins of animal domestication and husbandry","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1631069110002982","volume":"334","author":[{"family":"Vigne","given":"Jean-Denis"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Typically, the path to domestication is characterized by intensification of the relationship between forms derived from wild progenitors and human societies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"b9o7KrJy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vigne, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Vigne, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5EVDDXIB"],"itemData":{"id":8,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Comptes Rendus Biologies","DOI":"10.1016/j.crvi.2010.12.009","ISSN":"16310691","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Comptes Rendus Biologies","language":"en","page":"171-181","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The origins of animal domestication and husbandry: A major change in the history of humanity and the biosphere","title-short":"The origins of animal domestication and husbandry","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1631069110002982","volume":"334","author":[{"family":"Vigne","given":"Jean-Denis"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, is accompanied by changes in phenotype and has been defined as a distinctive coevolutionary, mutualistic relationship between domesticator and domesticate ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Eo6bIYtB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zeder, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Zeder, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1428,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IAR8PK98"],"itemData":{"id":1428,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The domestication of plants and animals is a key transition in human history, and its profound and continuing impacts are the focus of a broad range of transdisciplinary research spanning the physical, biological, and social sciences. Three central aspects of domestication that cut across and unify this diverse array of research perspectives are addressed here. Domestication is defined as a distinctive coevolutionary, mutualistic relationship between domesticator and domesticate and distinguished from related but ultimately different processes of resource management and agriculture. The relative utility of genetic, phenotypic, plastic, and contextual markers of evolving domesticatory relationships is discussed. Causal factors are considered, and two leading explanatory frameworks for initial domestication of plants and animals, one grounded in optimal foraging theory and the other in niche-construction theory, are compared.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1501711112","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"3191-3198","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Core questions in domestication research","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1501711112","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Zeder","given":"Melinda A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",3,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      A commonly accepted definition for wild animals, accepted by the Collaborative Partnership on Sustainable Wildlife Management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7PA1U18o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUFRO, 2018)","plainCitation":"(IUFRO, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1447,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J83JG63P"],"itemData":{"id":1447,"type":"document","publisher":"IUFRO","title":"Glossary of wildlife management terms and definitions","URL":"https://www.iufro.org/fileadmin/material/science/spps/silvavoc/wildlife-glossary.pdf","author":[{"family":"IUFRO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, applies to animals that have a phenotype unaffected by human selection and can live independently of direct human supervision or control. There may be a continuum from truly wild, where animal populations are self-sustaining and exist without human intervention, through various levels of confinement and intervention (such as feeding), to domestication. Similarly, wild products from plants have been described as those derived from untended wild resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Gyu5Pt7N","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Muir, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Muir, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1431,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/26MRGHXT"],"itemData":{"id":1431,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As scientists strive to make nature's value visible, a large portion of forests and wild biodiversity known as non-wood forest products (NWFPs) continues to remain largely invisible and unaccounted for. At the core of the problem is wide disaccord over what is a NWFP (and correlate \\n terms), a debate which has been running in circles for nearly three decades. This paper reviews existing terms and definitions, with the aim of improving forest statistics and the visibility of NWFPs. The paper starts by (1) clarifying boundaries between agricultural and forest products, so \\n forest products currently under agriculture can be \\"reclaimed\\"; (2) drawing on lessons from fisheries to distinguish between wild and farmed products, and associated activities; (3) moving beyond \\n              product \\n              towards \\n              activity \\n              classifications to capture gathering that may not be accounted \\n for under crops or forest products because it takes place across landscapes and outside of these sectoral boundaries.","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554820828671553","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"101-119","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Into the wild: disentangling non-wood terms and definitions for improved forest statistics","title-short":"Into the wild","URL":"https://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/146554820828671553","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Muir","given":"G.F."},{"family":"Sorrenti","given":"S."},{"family":"Vantomme","given":"P."},{"family":"Vidale","given":"E."},{"family":"Masiero","given":"M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Domestication is not just a state of being, but a process that results in genetic adaptation to the extent that animals will breed readily in captivity, their owner has some control over reproduction, and it results in detectable differences between the domestic populations and their wild progenitors. 
      The scientific literature and indigenous and local knowledge literature are also quite clear that there is no single dimensional point that can be used to distinguish wild from not wild ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8NuYV4V2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Child, 2019; Cruz-Garcia, 2017; Redford, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Child, 2019; Cruz-Garcia, 2017; Redford, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RI3HJDKI"],"itemData":{"id":7,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13299","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"1106-1119","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A framework to measure the wildness of managed large vertebrate populations","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/cobi.13299","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Child","given":"Matthew F."},{"family":"Selier","given":"S. A. Jeanetta"},{"family":"Radloff","given":"Frans G. T."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"W. Andrew"},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Nel","given":"Lizanne"},{"family":"Power","given":"R. John"},{"family":"Birss","given":"Coral"},{"family":"Okes","given":"Nicola C."},{"family":"Peel","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Mallon","given":"David"},{"family":"Davies?Mostert","given":"Harriet"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}},{"id":1445,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WTD3GLUV"],"itemData":{"id":1445,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.3389/fevo.2017.00127","page":"1-17","title":"Management and motivations to manage 'wild' food plants. A case study in a Mestizo village in the Amazon deforestation frontier","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Cruz-Garcia","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/48MQPEG7","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CT8PSFQD"],"itemData":{"id":33,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The conservation of species is one of the foundations of conservation biology. Successful species conservation has often been defined as simply the avoidance of extinction. We argue that this focus, although important, amounts to practicing conservation at the “emergency room door,” and will never be a sufficient approach to conserving species. Instead, we elaborate a positive definition of species conservation on the basis of six attributes and propose a categorization of different states of species conservation using the extent of human management and the degree to which each of the attributes is conserved. These states can be used to develop a taxonomy of species “recovery” that acknowledges there are multiple stable points defined by ecological and social factors. With this approach, we hope to contribute to a new, optimistic conservation biology that is not based on underambitious goals and that seeks to create the conditions under which Earth's biological systems can thrive.","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1525/bio.2011.61.1.9","ISSN":"00063568","note":"PMID: 57643525","title":"What Does It Mean to Successfully Conserve a (Vertebrate) Species?","author":[{"family":"Redford","given":"Kent H."},{"family":"Amato","given":"George"},{"family":"Baillie","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Beldomenico","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Bennett","given":"Elizabeth L."},{"family":"Clum","given":"Nancy"},{"family":"Cook","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Fonseca","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Hedges","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Launay","given":"Frederic"},{"family":"Lieberman","given":"Susan"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Georgina M."},{"family":"Murayama","given":"Akira"},{"family":"Putnam","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Robinson","given":"John G."},{"family":"Rosenbaum","given":"Howard"},{"family":"Sanderson","given":"Eric W."},{"family":"Stuart","given":"Simon N."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Thorbjarnarson","given":"John"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Biologists have proposed various ways to categorise wild populations which may vary depending on the purpose of the categorization. These include a five-node system for evaluating self-sustaining sub-populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"U9BYtO1l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Redford, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Redford, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/48MQPEG7","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CT8PSFQD"],"itemData":{"id":33,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The conservation of species is one of the foundations of conservation biology. Successful species conservation has often been defined as simply the avoidance of extinction. We argue that this focus, although important, amounts to practicing conservation at the “emergency room door,” and will never be a sufficient approach to conserving species. Instead, we elaborate a positive definition of species conservation on the basis of six attributes and propose a categorization of different states of species conservation using the extent of human management and the degree to which each of the attributes is conserved. These states can be used to develop a taxonomy of species “recovery” that acknowledges there are multiple stable points defined by ecological and social factors. With this approach, we hope to contribute to a new, optimistic conservation biology that is not based on underambitious goals and that seeks to create the conditions under which Earth's biological systems can thrive.","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1525/bio.2011.61.1.9","ISSN":"00063568","note":"PMID: 57643525","title":"What Does It Mean to Successfully Conserve a (Vertebrate) Species?","author":[{"family":"Redford","given":"Kent H."},{"family":"Amato","given":"George"},{"family":"Baillie","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Beldomenico","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Bennett","given":"Elizabeth L."},{"family":"Clum","given":"Nancy"},{"family":"Cook","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Fonseca","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Hedges","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Launay","given":"Frederic"},{"family":"Lieberman","given":"Susan"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Georgina M."},{"family":"Murayama","given":"Akira"},{"family":"Putnam","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Robinson","given":"John G."},{"family":"Rosenbaum","given":"Howard"},{"family":"Sanderson","given":"Eric W."},{"family":"Stuart","given":"Simon N."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Thorbjarnarson","given":"John"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} focusing on reintroducing captive animals into the wild; a timebound system, also focusing on reintroductions, where populations that can survive longer than ten years in the absence of human intervention would be wild ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KRVigVpo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hayward, 2015; IUCN Standards and Petitions Committee, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Hayward, 2015; IUCN Standards and Petitions Committee, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E6B342ZU"],"itemData":{"id":32,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The IUCN Red List is the most widely used tool to measure extinction risk and report biodiversity trends. Accurate and standardised conservation status assessments for the IUCN Red List are limited by a lack of adequate information; and need consistent and unbiased interpretation of that information. Variable interpretation stems from a lack of quantified thresholds in certain areas of the Red List guidelines. Thus, even in situations with sufficient information to make a Red List assessment, inconsistency can occur when experts, especially from different regions, interpret the guidelines differently, thereby undermining the goals and credibility of the process. In such an information vacuum, assessors make assumptions depending on their level of Red List experience (subconscious bias) and their personal values or agendas (conscious bias). We highlight two major issues where such bias influences assessments: relating to fenced subpopulations that require intensive management; and defining benchmark geographic distributions and thus the inclusion/exclusion of introduced subpopulations. We suggest assessor bias can be reduced by refining the Red List guidelines to include quantified thresholds for when to include fenced/intensively managed subpopulations or subpopulations outside the benchmark distribution; publishing case studies of difficult assessments to enhance cohesion between Specialist Groups; developing an online accreditation course on applying Red List criteria as a prerequisite for assessors; and ensuring that assessments of species subject to trade and utilisation are represented by all dissenting views (for example, both utilitarian and preservationist) and reviewed by relevant Specialist Groups. We believe these interventions would ensure consistent, reliable assessments of threatened species between regions and across assessors with divergent views, and will thus improve comparisons between taxa and counteract the use of Red List assessments as a tool","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.3389/fevo.2015.00087","ISSN":"2296-701X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Ecol. Evol.","language":"English","source":"Frontiers","title":"Ambiguity in guideline definitions introduces assessor bias and influences consistency in IUCN Red List status assessments","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fevo.2015.00087/full","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Hayward","given":"Matt W."},{"family":"Child","given":"Matthew F."},{"family":"Kerley","given":"Graham I. H."},{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter A."},{"family":"Somers","given":"Michael John"},{"family":"Burns","given":"Bruce"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":31576,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RK7PK87I"],"itemData":{"id":31576,"type":"document","publisher":"IUCN Standards and Petitions Committee","title":"Guidelines for Using the IUCN Red List Categories and Criteria. Version 14","URL":"http://www.iucnredlist.org/documents/RedListGuidelines.pdf","author":[{"family":"IUCN Standards and Petitions Committee","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; a composite score based on the existence of certain management interventions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HjNZN1sl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Child, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Child, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RI3HJDKI"],"itemData":{"id":7,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13299","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"1106-1119","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A framework to measure the wildness of managed large vertebrate populations","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/cobi.13299","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Child","given":"Matthew F."},{"family":"Selier","given":"S. A. Jeanetta"},{"family":"Radloff","given":"Frans G. T."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"W. Andrew"},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Nel","given":"Lizanne"},{"family":"Power","given":"R. John"},{"family":"Birss","given":"Coral"},{"family":"Okes","given":"Nicola C."},{"family":"Peel","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Mallon","given":"David"},{"family":"Davies?Mostert","given":"Harriet"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; and a five-stages process from wild to domesticated for fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"drHWLMxh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Teletchea & Fontaine, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Teletchea & Fontaine, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30172,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ELTR5PMC"],"itemData":{"id":30172,"type":"article-journal","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12006","page":"181–195","title":"Levels of domestication in fi sh : implications for the sustainable future of aquaculture","author":[{"family":"Teletchea","given":"Fabrice"},{"family":"Fontaine","given":"Pascal"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). Indigenous peoples and local communities have developed their own classification systems based on different criteria to define wildness. For example, some communities in the Amazon categorize ‘wild’ plants into six different groups based on how they are used and managed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rhc2xkt8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cruz-Garcia, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Cruz-Garcia, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1445,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WTD3GLUV"],"itemData":{"id":1445,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.3389/fevo.2017.00127","page":"1-17","title":"Management and motivations to manage 'wild' food plants. A case study in a Mestizo village in the Amazon deforestation frontier","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Cruz-Garcia","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and indigenous peoples and local communities in Peru distinguish between domesticated and two types of wild animals on the basis of their interactions with humans and domesticated animals.
      The relationship between wild, introduced, feral, domesticated and captive can be mapped using three axes linked to human interventions (Figure 1.4). These are (i) the extent of  human intervention in reproduction and survival; (ii) multigenerational selection for traits by people to suit human needs; and (iii) the extent to which populations are dispersed through human intervention. In interpreting the scope of this assessment, the emphasis is on those populations that are situated on the lower end of these three axes, i.e., those that still occur in their natural range, have not undergone multigenerational selection for traits and can survive without human intervention. Nevertheless, some of the intermediate conditions along the continuum from wild to domesticated can be important for any assessment of sustainable use and are included in the assessment when appropriate (see Box 1.1). The terminology for the intermediate states between wild and domesticated varies between taxa (e.g., animals and plants), practices (e.g., fishing or gathering) and purpose (e.g., conservation versus use) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JQG9dqmp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bell, 2006; Child, 2019; Lorenzen, 2000; Muir, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Bell, 2006; Child, 2019; Lorenzen, 2000; Muir, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30203,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3JVSVII7"],"itemData":{"id":30203,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2006.03.008","ISSN":"01657836","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Research","language":"en","page":"1-8","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Restocking and stock enhancement of coastal fisheries: Potential, problems and progress","title-short":"Restocking and stock enhancement of coastal fisheries","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0165783606001147","volume":"80","author":[{"family":"Bell","given":"Johann D."},{"family":"Bartley","given":"Devin M."},{"family":"Lorenzen","given":"Kai"},{"family":"Loneragan","given":"Neil R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",8]]}}},{"id":7,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RI3HJDKI"],"itemData":{"id":7,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13299","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"1106-1119","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A framework to measure the wildness of managed large vertebrate populations","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/cobi.13299","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Child","given":"Matthew F."},{"family":"Selier","given":"S. A. Jeanetta"},{"family":"Radloff","given":"Frans G. T."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"W. Andrew"},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Nel","given":"Lizanne"},{"family":"Power","given":"R. John"},{"family":"Birss","given":"Coral"},{"family":"Okes","given":"Nicola C."},{"family":"Peel","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Mallon","given":"David"},{"family":"Davies?Mostert","given":"Harriet"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}},{"id":30202,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CYCJ99JM"],"itemData":{"id":30202,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Aquaculture in the Third Millennium. Technical Proceedings of the Conference on Aquaculture in the Third Millennium, Bangkok, Thailand.","event-place":"Ban","page":"221-237","publisher-place":"Ban","title":"Strategic Review of Enhancements and Culture-based Fisheries","author":[{"family":"Lorenzen","given":"Kai"},{"family":"Amarasinghe","given":"U.S."},{"family":"Bartley","given":"D. M."},{"family":"Bell","given":"J.D."},{"family":"Bilio","given":"M."},{"family":"Silva","given":"S.S.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Garaway","given":"C.J."},{"family":"Hartmann","given":"W.D."},{"family":"Kapetsky","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Laleye","given":"P."},{"family":"Moreau","given":"J."},{"family":"Sugunan","given":"V. V."},{"family":"Swar","given":"D.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":1431,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/26MRGHXT"],"itemData":{"id":1431,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As scientists strive to make nature's value visible, a large portion of forests and wild biodiversity known as non-wood forest products (NWFPs) continues to remain largely invisible and unaccounted for. At the core of the problem is wide disaccord over what is a NWFP (and correlate \\n terms), a debate which has been running in circles for nearly three decades. This paper reviews existing terms and definitions, with the aim of improving forest statistics and the visibility of NWFPs. The paper starts by (1) clarifying boundaries between agricultural and forest products, so \\n forest products currently under agriculture can be \\"reclaimed\\"; (2) drawing on lessons from fisheries to distinguish between wild and farmed products, and associated activities; (3) moving beyond \\n              product \\n              towards \\n              activity \\n              classifications to capture gathering that may not be accounted \\n for under crops or forest products because it takes place across landscapes and outside of these sectoral boundaries.","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554820828671553","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"101-119","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Into the wild: disentangling non-wood terms and definitions for improved forest statistics","title-short":"Into the wild","URL":"https://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/146554820828671553","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Muir","given":"G.F."},{"family":"Sorrenti","given":"S."},{"family":"Vantomme","given":"P."},{"family":"Vidale","given":"E."},{"family":"Masiero","given":"M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For the purposes of this assessment, the typology of wild, wild managed, farmed and domesticated provides a sufficient framework for analysis (see Table 1.1).
      
Figure 1.4. A diagramatic presentation of the multiple dimensions that may be used to define the continuum between ‘wild’ and and domesticated. The bubbles represent various examples of states that can exist between the extremes of wild populations occurring freely within their original habitat and those that are fully domesticated. This is not a complete list of possible states. CC-BY.

