. Moller"},{"family":"Malhi","given":"Y."},{"family":"Miranda","given":"I. P. de Andrade"},{"family":"Phillips","given":"J. F."},{"family":"Prieto","given":"A."},{"family":"Rudas","given":"A."},{"family":"Ruschel","given":"A. R."},{"family":"Silva","given":"N."},{"family":"Hildebrand","given":"P.","non-dropping-particle":"von"},{"family":"Vos","given":"V. A."},{"family":"Zent","given":"E. L."},{"family":"Zent","given":"S."},{"family":"Cintra","given":"B. B. L."},{"family":"Nascimento","given":"M. T."},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"A. A."},{"family":"Ramirez-Angulo","given":"H."},{"family":"Ramos","given":"J. F."},{"family":"Rivas","given":"G."},{"family":"Schongart","given":"J."},{"family":"Sierra","given":"R."},{"family":"Tirado","given":"M."},{"family":"Heijden","given":"G.","non-dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Torre","given":"E. V."},{"family":"Wang","given":"O."},{"family":"Young","given":"K. R."},{"family":"Baider","given":"C."},{"family":"Cano","given":"A."},{"family":"Farfan-Rios","given":"W."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"C."},{"family":"Hoffman","given":"B."},{"family":"Mendoza","given":"C."},{"family":"Mesones","given":"I."},{"family":"Torres-Lezama","given":"A."},{"family":"Medina","given":"M. N. U."},{"family":"Andel","given":"T. R.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Villarroel","given":"D."},{"family":"Zagt","given":"R."},{"family":"Alexiades","given":"M. N."},{"family":"Balslev","given":"H."},{"family":"Garcia-Cabrera","given":"K."},{"family":"Gonzales","given":"T."},{"family":"Hernandez","given":"L."},{"family":"Huamantupa-Chuquimaco","given":"I."},{"family":"Manzatto","given":"A. G."},{"family":"Milliken","given":"W."},{"family":"Cuenca","given":"W. P."},{"family":"Pansini","given":"S."},{"family":"Pauletto","given":"D."},{"family":"Arevalo","given":"F. R."},{"family":"Reis","given":"N. F. Costa"},{"family":"Sampaio","given":"A. F."},{"family":"Giraldo","given":"L. E. Urrego"},{"family":"Sandoval","given":"E. H. Valderrama"},{"family":"Gamarra","given":"L. Valenzuela"},{"family":"Vela","given":"C. I. A."},{"family":"Steege","given":"H.","non-dropping-particle":"ter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",3,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Likewise, the species composition in these areas reflects varying intensities of management by indigenous peoples and local communities. For example, Peacock & Turner ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c0djVSm9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Peacock & Turner, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Peacock & Turner, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":21,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8TBWUWNT"],"itemData":{"id":21,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Biodiversity and native America","event-place":"Norman (Okla.)","ISBN":"978-0-8061-3345-4","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 470893627","page":"133-79","publisher":"University of Oklahoma Press","publisher-place":"Norman (Okla.)","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"\\"Just Like A Garden\\": Traditional Resource Management and Biodiversity Conservation on the Interior Plateau of British Columbia","editor":[{"family":"Minnis","given":"Paul E"},{"family":"Elisens","given":"Wayne J"}],"author":[{"family":"Peacock","given":"Sandra","suffix":"L."},{"family":"Turner","given":"Nancy J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Anderson ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8T2syS53","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. Anderson, 2013)","plainCitation":"(K. Anderson, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":125,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RD3MEI22"],"itemData":{"id":125,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-0-520-28043-4","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 937063134","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Tending the wild: Native American knowledge and the management of California's natural resources","title-short":"Tending the wild","author":[{"family":"Anderson","given":"Kat"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} note that indigenous peoples of northwestern North America managed the land on which they lived at scales from the individual plant (e.g., through practices such as pruning and weeding) to the landscape ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GEI6R2OA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Deur & Turner, 2005; N. J. Turner, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Deur & Turner, 2005; N. J. Turner, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30212,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9UWP3K9V"],"itemData":{"id":30212,"type":"book","event-place":"Seattle","ISBN":"0-295-98565-8","number-of-pages":"404","publisher":"University of Washington press","publisher-place":"Seattle","title":"Keeping It Living: Traditions of Plant Use and Cultivation on the Northwest Coast of North America","author":[{"family":"Deur","given":"Douglas"},{"family":"Turner","given":"Nancy J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":30211,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/946RZ45C"],"itemData":{"id":30211,"type":"book","collection-number":"74","collection-title":"McGill-Queen's Indigenous and Northern Studies","event-place":"Montreal","ISBN":"978-0-7735-4380-5","number-of-pages":"1056","publisher":"McGill-Queens University Press","publisher-place":"Montreal","title":"Ancient Pathways, Ancestral Knowledge","author":[{"family":"Turner","given":"Nancy J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The area managed by indigenous peoples coincides with approximately 40% of terrestrial conserved areas, including those with high biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6yYPwKJr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garnett, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Garnett, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30330,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/387NZ3WP"],"itemData":{"id":30330,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Sustainability","DOI":"10.1038/s41893-018-0100-6","ISSN":"2398-9629","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Sustain","language":"en","page":"369-374","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A spatial overview of the global importance of Indigenous lands for conservation","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41893-018-0100-6","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Garnett","given":"Stephen T."},{"family":"Burgess","given":"Neil D."},{"family":"Fa","given":"John E."},{"family":"Fernández-Llamazares","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Zsolt"},{"family":"Robinson","given":"Cathy J."},{"family":"Watson","given":"James E. M."},{"family":"Zander","given":"Kerstin K."},{"family":"Austin","given":"Beau"},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"Eduardo S."},{"family":"Collier","given":"Neil French"},{"family":"Duncan","given":"Tom"},{"family":"Ellis","given":"Erle"},{"family":"Geyle","given":"Hayley"},{"family":"Jackson","given":"Micha V."},{"family":"Jonas","given":"Harry"},{"family":"Malmer","given":"Pernilla"},{"family":"McGowan","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Sivongxay","given":"Amphone"},{"family":"Leiper","given":"Ian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In many cases, loss of indigenous and local knowledge and practices results in reduced species population size and distribution (see, for example, the case of camas bulbs Camassia spp., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rgMFSrtS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. J. Turner & Turner, 2008)","plainCitation":"(N. J. Turner & Turner, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WJ52FNBK"],"itemData":{"id":19,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Knowledge and practices of indigenous peoples relating to local plants used for food, medicine, materials, and other purposes are threatened in many parts of the world. The reasons for declining knowledge and use of traditional resources are complex and multifaceted. We review a series of case examples of culturally valued food plants in British Columbia and identify a suite of interacting social and environmental factors that have resulted in decreased use of and dwindling cultural knowledge about these plants over the past 150 years. Reasons for this loss include compounding influences of changing knowledge systems owing to religious conversion and residential schools, loss of indigenous languages, loss of time and opportunity for traditional practices owing to participation in the wage economy, increasing urbanization of indigenous populations, loss of access to traditional resources, restriction of management practices for sustaining these resources, and most recently, forces of globalization and industrialization. Efforts to renew and restore traditional practices and relationships with plants and environments must recognize the cumulative effects of these factors and find ways to retain and reinforce the knowledge and practices still held by individuals and communities, to reverse some of the negative influences on cultural retention, and to develop new, relevant, and effective ways to revitalize languages, cultures, and ethnobotanical knowledge within contemporary contexts.","container-title":"Botany","DOI":"10.1139/B07-020","ISSN":"1916-2790, 1916-2804","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Botany","language":"en","page":"103-115","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"“Where our women used to get the food”: cumulative effects and loss of ethnobotanical knowledge and practice; case study from coastal British Columbia","title-short":"“Where our women used to get the food”","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/B07-020","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Turner","given":"Nancy J."},{"family":"Turner","given":"Katherine L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). This perspective suggests that the defining characteristics of ‘wild’ cannot be represented as a simple dichotomy between management and no management by humans and that excluding all human management from the concept of wild species is problematic ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"393Z1BF7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lepofsky & Caldwell, 2013; Mathews & Turner, 2017; Mueller-Dombois, 2007; Mueller-Dombois & Wirawan, 2005; N. Turner, 2013; Zeder, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Lepofsky & Caldwell, 2013; Mathews & Turner, 2017; Mueller-Dombois, 2007; Mueller-Dombois & Wirawan, 2005; N. Turner, 2013; Zeder, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1438,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3K3JYMGQ"],"itemData":{"id":1438,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Processes","DOI":"10.1186/2192-1709-2-12","ISSN":"2192-1709","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Process","language":"en","page":"12","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Indigenous marine resource management on the Northwest Coast of North America","URL":"https://ecologicalprocesses.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/2192-1709-2-12","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Lepofsky","given":"Dana"},{"family":"Caldwell","given":"Megan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",12]]}}},{"id":1408,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GZ7MFW7Q"],"itemData":{"id":1408,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Conservation for the Anthropocene Ocean","ISBN":"978-0-12-805375-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1016/B978-0-12-805375-1.00009-X","page":"169-206","publisher":"Elsevier","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ocean Cultures: Northwest Coast Ecosystems and Indigenous Management Systems","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/B978012805375100009X","author":[{"family":"Mathews","given":"Darcy L."},{"family":"Turner","given":"Nancy J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":1407,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NQ94WPPW"],"itemData":{"id":1407,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bishop  Museum  Bulletin  in  Cultural  and Environmental Studies","page":"23-33","title":"The Hawaiian Ahupua‘a Land Use System: Its Biological Resource Zones and the Challenge for Silvicultural Restoration","URL":"http://hbs.bishopmuseum.org/pubs-online/strm/bces3r.pdf#page=32","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Mueller-Dombois","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":1406,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JCEXZ95W"],"itemData":{"id":1406,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pacific Science","issue":"2","page":"293-314","title":"The Kahana Valley Ahupua‘a, a PABITRA Study Site on O‘ahu, Hawaiian Islands","URL":"https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstream/10125/24180/1/PacSci_293-314.pdf","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Mueller-Dombois","given":"D."},{"family":"Wirawan","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":855,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5ZBRSF3D"],"itemData":{"id":855,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Indigenous Peoples’ food systems & well-being: interventions & policies for healthy communities","event-place":"Rome, Italy and Montreal, Quebec","page":"23-38","publisher-place":"Rome, Italy and Montreal, Quebec","title":"Global Environmental Challenges to the integrity of Indigenous Peoples’ Food Systems","author":[{"family":"Turner","given":"Nancy"},{"family":"Plotkin","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Kuhnlein","given":"Harriet V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":1428,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IAR8PK98"],"itemData":{"id":1428,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The domestication of plants and animals is a key transition in human history, and its profound and continuing impacts are the focus of a broad range of transdisciplinary research spanning the physical, biological, and social sciences. Three central aspects of domestication that cut across and unify this diverse array of research perspectives are addressed here. Domestication is defined as a distinctive coevolutionary, mutualistic relationship between domesticator and domesticate and distinguished from related but ultimately different processes of resource management and agriculture. The relative utility of genetic, phenotypic, plastic, and contextual markers of evolving domesticatory relationships is discussed. Causal factors are considered, and two leading explanatory frameworks for initial domestication of plants and animals, one grounded in optimal foraging theory and the other in niche-construction theory, are compared.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1501711112","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"3191-3198","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Core questions in domestication research","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1501711112","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Zeder","given":"Melinda A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",3,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Rather, wild is located on a continuum somewhere between instances in which humans have no current or remembered historical contact with a location and the species in it and intensive interventions with landscapes and the species in them ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rj2Llt4Z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ford, 1985)","plainCitation":"(Ford, 1985)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":29,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TXTTFPUV"],"itemData":{"id":29,"type":"book","call-number":"GN2 E98.A3 .M5 no. 75","collection-number":"no. 75","collection-title":"Anthropological papers / Museum of Anthropology, University of Michigan","event-place":"Ann Arbor, Mich","ISBN":"978-0-915703-01-2","number-of-pages":"411","publisher":"Museum of Anthropology, University of Michigan","publisher-place":"Ann Arbor, Mich","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Prehistoric food production in North America","editor":[{"family":"Ford","given":"Richard I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1985"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Indigenous peoples and local communities’ world views and concepts of nature offer an additional perspective on definitions of wild in relationship to animals, fungi and plants. Indigenous peoples and local communities’ definitions of wild species arise from worldviews that emphasize material and spiritual relationships between humans and other beings ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"m80SDI8i","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019c)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019c)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":73,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TASKTWP4"],"itemData":{"id":73,"type":"report","event-place":"Montreal, Canada","language":"English","page":"40","publisher":"UNESCO","publisher-place":"Montreal, Canada","title":"Report of the ILK dialogue workshop for the first order draft of the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species, held in Montreal, Canada, on 8-9 October 2019.","URL":"https://ipbes.net/sites/default/files/inline-files/IPBES_SusUse_2ndILKDialogue_Report_final_forWeb_0.pdf","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Dicker","given":"Jason"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Viviana"},{"family":"Hernández Márquez","given":"Guadalupe Yesenia"},{"family":"Jacobs","given":"Lynn"},{"family":"Kozanayi","given":"Witness"},{"family":"Mulenkei","given":"Lucy"},{"family":"Pictou","given":"Sherry"},{"family":"Nuri Sherpa","given":"Lakpa"},{"family":"Shulbaeva","given":"Polina"},{"family":"Springer","given":"Hannah Kihalani"},{"family":"Stryamets","given":"Nataliya"},{"family":"Trakansuphakon","given":"Prasert"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While the defining characteristics of wild vary between cultures (where the concept does exist), commonalities and differences between them offer insights into how indigenous peoples and local communities’ definitions of wild species are intertwined with notions of sustainable use (see section 1.4.1). Some cultures do not identify a sharp boundary between wild and domesticated species. However, many indigenous peoples and local communities recognize distinct types of animals and plants based on the level of care they need from humans to reproduce and thrive. This distinction may be further expressed in terms of the realm or power with which a species is associated or the degree of freedom it can exercise. For example, the Mixtec peoples of Mexico conceptualize wild as something that grows by itself, without need for external help. In Russia and other eastern European countries, wild species often are described as gifts of the forest while Native Hawaiian, many Andean, and other cultures regard wild species as belonging to or associated with gods. Thus, relationships between humans and these species also entail relationships with these other entities, with all the significance and consequences that this may imply ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QCLy2tZg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019c, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019c, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":73,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TASKTWP4"],"itemData":{"id":73,"type":"report","event-place":"Montreal, Canada","language":"English","page":"40","publisher":"UNESCO","publisher-place":"Montreal, Canada","title":"Report of the ILK dialogue workshop for the first order draft of the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species, held in Montreal, Canada, on 8-9 October 2019.","URL":"https://ipbes.net/sites/default/files/inline-files/IPBES_SusUse_2ndILKDialogue_Report_final_forWeb_0.pdf","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Dicker","given":"Jason"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Viviana"},{"family":"Hernández Márquez","given":"Guadalupe Yesenia"},{"family":"Jacobs","given":"Lynn"},{"family":"Kozanayi","given":"Witness"},{"family":"Mulenkei","given":"Lucy"},{"family":"Pictou","given":"Sherry"},{"family":"Nuri Sherpa","given":"Lakpa"},{"family":"Shulbaeva","given":"Polina"},{"family":"Springer","given":"Hannah Kihalani"},{"family":"Stryamets","given":"Nataliya"},{"family":"Trakansuphakon","given":"Prasert"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":76,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEBMMIP2"],"itemData":{"id":76,"type":"report","event-place":"Paris, France","language":"English","page":"53","publisher":"UNESCO","publisher-place":"Paris, France","title":"Report of the first ILK dialogue workshop for the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species, held in Paris, France, on 6-7 May 2019.","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Daguitan, Florence","given":""},{"family":"Eduardo da Silva","given":"Franklin Paulo"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Viviana"},{"family":"Hernández Márquez","given":"Guadalupe Yesenia"},{"family":"Kaptoyo","given":"Edna"},{"family":"Kozanayi","given":"Witness"},{"family":"Nuri Sherpa","given":"Lakpa"},{"family":"Pictou","given":"Sherry"},{"family":"Shadrin","given":"Vyacheslav"},{"family":"Shulbaeva","given":"Polina"},{"family":"Springer","given":"Hannah Kihalani"},{"family":"Stryamets","given":"Nataliya"},{"family":"Tima","given":"Tilia Tao"},{"family":"Thevenin","given":"Thierry"},{"family":"Trakansuphakon","given":"Prasert"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These concepts are examined in greater detail in section 2.2.4. 
      Philosophers have identified three constituents of wildness (or wild), namely locational, dispositional and constitutional ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"n9cj1fkw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Palmer, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Palmer, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9DMEEFCY"],"itemData":{"id":34,"type":"chapter","abstract":"This article considers whether a morally relevant distinction can be drawn between wild and domesticated animals. The term “wildness” can be used in several different ways, only one of which (constitutive wildness, meaning an animal that has not been domesticated by being bred in particular ways) is generally paired and contrasted with “domesticated.” Domesticated animals are normally deliberately bred and confined. One of the article's arguments concerns human initiatives that establish relations with animals and thereby change what is owed to these animals. The main relations of interest in ethics are the vulnerability and dependence in animals that are created when humans establish certain relations with them on farms, in zoos, in laboratories, and the like. Domestication is a pervasive way in which humans make animals vulnerable, and thereby duties of animal care and protection arise in a persistent way. Keywords:","container-title":"The Oxford Handbook of Animal Ethics","ISBN":"978-0-19-994013-4","note":"DOI: 10.1093/oxfordhb/9780195371963.013.0026","title":"The Moral Relevance of the Distinction Between Domesticated and Wild Animals","author":[{"family":"Palmer","given":"Clare"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These constituents provide a qualitative framework for assessing wildness. Locational wildness refers to existence in an area free of human management; dispositional wildness relates to the state and behaviour of the organism; whereas constitutional wildness refers to an organism that has not been domesticated. 
Box 1.1. Case study on challenges with operationalizing the concept of wild species
In South Africa, a dramatic surge in the conversion of land use from livestock to wild species ranches resulted in an increase of wild species numbers from 0.5 million head of game in the 1960s to 16-20 million in 2014 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jzGFl5pZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carruthers, 2008; Taylor, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Carruthers, 2008; Taylor, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UWFSQRW8"],"itemData":{"id":2,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Transactions of the Royal Society of South Africa","issue":"2","page":"160–181","title":"“Wilding the farm or farming the wild”? The evolution of scientific game ranching in South Africa from the 1960s to the present","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Carruthers","given":"Jane"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":20,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IPF93A3M"],"itemData":{"id":20,"type":"report","event-place":"Johannesburg, South Africa","page":"139","publisher":"The Endangered Wildlife Trust","publisher-place":"Johannesburg, South Africa","title":"An assessment of the economic, social and conservation value of the wildlife ranching industry and its potential to support the green economy in South Africa","URL":"https://www.sagreenfund.org.za/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/EWT-RESEARCH-REPORT.pdf","author":[{"family":"Taylor","given":"W. A."},{"family":"Lindsey","given":"P. A."},{"family":"Davies-Mostert","given":"H. T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As a result, Southern Africa is the only region on the African continent where mammal populations, on aggregate, are not in decline ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uStHMega","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Craigie, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Craigie, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JRGCXGCW"],"itemData":{"id":30,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2010.06.007","ISSN":"00063207","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"2221-2228","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Large mammal population declines in Africa’s protected areas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320710002739","volume":"143","author":[{"family":"Craigie","given":"Ian D."},{"family":"Baillie","given":"Jonathan E.M."},{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Carbone","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Collen","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Green","given":"Rhys E."},{"family":"Hutton","given":"Jon M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and private landowners have played a major role in reversing the trend. This rewilding has been viewed as a successful model for sustainable use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Q8eKDnM4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cromsigt Joris P. G. M., 2018)","plainCitation":"(Cromsigt Joris P. G. M., 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HI9TPQKW","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQXZW48B"],"itemData":{"id":31,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The loss of megafauna at the terminal Pleistocene has been linked to a wide range of Earth-system-level changes, such as altered greenhouse gas budgets, fire regimes and biome-level vegetation changes. Given these influences and feedbacks, might part of the solution for mitigating anthropogenic climate change lie in the restoration of extant megafauna to ecosystems? Here, we explore the potential role of trophic rewilding on Earth's climate system. We first provide a novel synthesis of the various ways that megafauna interact with the major drivers of anthropogenic climate change, including greenhouse gas storage and emission, aerosols and albedo. We then explore the role of rewilding as a mitigation tool at two scales: (i) current and near-future opportunities for national or regional climate change mitigation portfolios, and (ii) more radical opportunities at the global scale. Finally, we identify major knowledge gaps that complicate the complete characterization of rewilding as a climate change mitigation strategy. Our perspective is urgent since we are losing the Earth's last remaining megafauna, and with it a potential option to address climate change.This article is part of the theme issue ‘Trophic rewilding: consequences for ecosystems under global change’.","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences","DOI":"10.1098/rstb.2017.0440","issue":"1761","journalAbbreviation":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences","page":"20170440","source":"royalsocietypublishing.org (Atypon)","title":"Trophic rewilding as a climate change mitigation strategy?","URL":"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/abs/10.1098/rstb.2017.0440","volume":"373","author":[{"literal":"Cromsigt Joris P. G. M."},{"literal":"te Beest Mariska"},{"literal":"Kerley Graham I. H."},{"literal":"Landman Marietjie"},{"literal":"le Roux Elizabeth"},{"literal":"Smith Felisa A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12,5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but the process has also highlighted the need to avoid a shifting baseline for what is meant by “wildness” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kL5dpF5N","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Child, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Child, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QMXBU63G"],"itemData":{"id":4,"type":"article-journal","note":"publisher: South African National Biodiversity Institute and Endangered Wildlife Trust, South Africa","title":"The 2016 Red List of Mammals of South Africa, Swaziland and Lesotho.","URL":"https://www.ewt.org.za/resources/resources-mammal-red-list/","editor":[{"family":"Child","given":"Matthew F."},{"family":"Roxburgh","given":"Lizanne"},{"family":"Do Linh San","given":"Emmanuel"},{"family":"Raimondo","given":"Domitilla"},{"family":"Davies-Mostert","given":"Harriet T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
Management interventions on wild species ranches may include artificially increasing the abundance of a population through supplementary resources, veterinary care or controlling which individuals breed with each other to enhance an economically valuable trait. This negates natural selection and may hinder adaptation to changing environments. Thus, population abundance does not necessarily equate to an ecologically functioning population if those animals are disconnected from the surrounding landscape and cut off from evolutionary trajectories. Metrics based on numbers alone may mask a gradual shifting baseline in what it means to successfully conserve wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"48Gr7W4G","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Redford, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Redford, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/48MQPEG7","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CT8PSFQD"],"itemData":{"id":33,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The conservation of species is one of the foundations of conservation biology. Successful species conservation has often been defined as simply the avoidance of extinction. We argue that this focus, although important, amounts to practicing conservation at the “emergency room door,” and will never be a sufficient approach to conserving species. Instead, we elaborate a positive definition of species conservation on the basis of six attributes and propose a categorization of different states of species conservation using the extent of human management and the degree to which each of the attributes is conserved. These states can be used to develop a taxonomy of species “recovery” that acknowledges there are multiple stable points defined by ecological and social factors. With this approach, we hope to contribute to a new, optimistic conservation biology that is not based on underambitious goals and that seeks to create the conditions under which Earth's biological systems can thrive.","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1525/bio.2011.61.1.9","ISSN":"00063568","note":"PMID: 57643525","title":"What Does It Mean to Successfully Conserve a (Vertebrate) Species?","author":[{"family":"Redford","given":"Kent H."},{"family":"Amato","given":"George"},{"family":"Baillie","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Beldomenico","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Bennett","given":"Elizabeth L."},{"family":"Clum","given":"Nancy"},{"family":"Cook","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Fonseca","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Hedges","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Launay","given":"Frederic"},{"family":"Lieberman","given":"Susan"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Georgina M."},{"family":"Murayama","given":"Akira"},{"family":"Putnam","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Robinson","given":"John G."},{"family":"Rosenbaum","given":"Howard"},{"family":"Sanderson","given":"Eric W."},{"family":"Stuart","given":"Simon N."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Thorbjarnarson","given":"John"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
Child ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ywVypabS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Child, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Child, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RI3HJDKI"],"itemData":{"id":7,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13299","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"1106-1119","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A framework to measure the wildness of managed large vertebrate populations","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/cobi.13299","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Child","given":"Matthew F."},{"family":"Selier","given":"S. A. Jeanetta"},{"family":"Radloff","given":"Frans G. T."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"W. Andrew"},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Nel","given":"Lizanne"},{"family":"Power","given":"R. John"},{"family":"Birss","given":"Coral"},{"family":"Okes","given":"Nicola C."},{"family":"Peel","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Mallon","given":"David"},{"family":"Davies?Mostert","given":"Harriet"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} produced a framework to quantify the wildness of a managed population by mapping management thresholds between wildness ‘nodes’, using attributes such as available space, food and water provision, breeding freedom, and disease resistance, which have been demonstrated to influence the ecological and evolutionary attributes of a species. They found that population abundance did not correlate with wildness but that the area available to a population was a strong predictor of wildness whereas the frequency of supplementary feeding, density of artificial water-points and the degree of veterinary care provided were predictors of shifts away from wildness.  
One of the key policy challenges is how to unlock economic opportunities associated with wild species without creating perverse incentives that may affect the wildness of managed populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wsD8DdmE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Turpie & Letley, 2018; Wanger, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Turpie & Letley, 2018; Wanger, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":3,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BG49PYRG"],"itemData":{"id":3,"type":"report","event-place":"Pretoria, South Africa","page":"102","publisher":"United Nations Development Programme and Department of Environmental Affairs","publisher-place":"Pretoria, South Africa","title":"An initial investigation into the potential feasibility and design of a market-based certification scheme for the wildlife sector of South Africa","author":[{"family":"Turpie","given":"Jane"},{"family":"Letley","given":"Gwyneth"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11]]}}},{"id":35003,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7PJ5I2VC"],"itemData":{"id":35003,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1038/s41559-017-0387-0","ISSN":"2397-334X","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Ecol Evol","language":"en","page":"1791-1793","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trophy hunting certification","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41559-017-0387-0","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Wanger","given":"Thomas C."},{"family":"Traill","given":"Lochran W."},{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"},{"family":"Rhodes","given":"Jonathan R."},{"family":"Tscharntke","given":"Teja"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

1.3.3 Impacts of nature conceptualizations on the uses of wild species 
The traditional “western” view of human-nature relationship tends to separate nature (what exists by itself) from culture (what has been produced by humans). This human-nature dualism is deeply rooted in people’s perception of the functioning of the biosphere as well as in their common language. It is pervasive and may be found in most national and international agreements and policies related to the conservation of biodiversity or the regulation of use of wild species. IPBES does not make exception to this approach. The IPBES conceptual framework presented above (see section 1.2) indeed defines nature as the entity that “encompasses the nonhuman world, with particular emphasis on living organisms, their diversity, their interactions among themselves but also with their abiotic environment”. This influence can be subtle, such as in the definition of natural environment given by the Convention for Biological Diversity: “the natural environment comprises all living and non-living things that occur naturally on Earth. In its purest sense, it is thus an environment that is not the result of human activity or intervention” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vl5mCH6U","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CBD, 2008)","plainCitation":"(CBD, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30197,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A686HTL8"],"itemData":{"id":30197,"type":"document","title":"Biodiversity Glossary","URL":"https://www.cbd.int/cepa/toolkit/2008/doc/CBD-Toolkit-Glossaries.pdf","author":[{"family":"CBD","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Traces of this human-nature dualism can already be found in the Antiquity, notably among the Greek philosophers of the 7th and 6th centuries BC ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cYMILsIk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bouchard, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Bouchard, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30196,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VEHQXPG3"],"itemData":{"id":30196,"type":"thesis","publisher":"Université du Québec à Rimouski","title":"Protection de la nature et diversité ontologique chez Philippe Descola : Quelques enjeux et une possibilité de dépassement avec le contextualisme de Mark Hunyadi","URL":"https://semaphore.uqar.ca/id/eprint/1828/1/Olivier_Bouchard_juillet2020.pdf","author":[{"family":"Bouchard","given":"Olivier"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and in the Bible as well, especially the Genesis (chapter 1, 27-30):  
(27) “God created mankind in his own image; in the image of God he created them; male and female he created them.” 
(28) “God blessed them and said to them, “Be fruitful and increase in number; fill the Earth and subdue it. Rule over the fish in the sea and the birds in the sky and over every living creature that moves on the ground.” 
(29) “I give you every seed-bearing plant on the face of the whole Earth and every tree that has fruit with seed in it. They will be yours for food.”  
(30) “And to all the beasts of the Earth and all the birds in the sky and all the creatures that move along the ground—everything that has the breath of life in it—I give every green plant for food.” And it was so. 
      In Europe and the Mediterranean area, this human-nature dualism continued to develop during the Middle Ages with Christianity, according to which all creation proceeds from God, including human who is the center of this creation and has the divine mission to control it (see Genesis citations above). God has thus bestowed strong power on humans and it is God's intention that humanity multiplies itself, spreads out over the Earth, makes its dominion over the creation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1fMWdLKo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Glacken, 1976)","plainCitation":"(Glacken, 1976)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30145,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VWGWZ6R6"],"itemData":{"id":30145,"type":"book","event-place":"Berkley, California","number-of-pages":"763","publisher":"University of California Press","publisher-place":"Berkley, California","title":"Traces on the Rhodian shore","author":[{"family":"Glacken","given":"C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1976"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. If the humanity is clearly distinct from and positioned over the other living organisms in a hierarchy of creation, various passages from the old and new testaments also attest to the love and pleasure of human for nature, which appears to be a manifestation of God’s work ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jloaT0ty","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Glacken, 1976)","plainCitation":"(Glacken, 1976)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30145,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VWGWZ6R6"],"itemData":{"id":30145,"type":"book","event-place":"Berkley, California","number-of-pages":"763","publisher":"University of California Press","publisher-place":"Berkley, California","title":"Traces on the Rhodian shore","author":[{"family":"Glacken","given":"C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1976"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      This dichotomy between human and nature has deeply fed the western thought over two millennia, through the arts, philosophy and science. In ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lr1Ym0lW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Descartes, 1637)","plainCitation":"(Descartes, 1637)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30192,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6G8B2FU5"],"itemData":{"id":30192,"type":"book","event-place":"Paris","number-of-pages":"192","publisher":"Flammarion","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Discours de la méthode","author":[{"family":"Descartes","given":"René"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1637"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Descartes in the Discours de la Méthode went a step further in this conceptualization of human-nature relationship through his famous quote: “to know the laws which govern nature to become masters and owners of nature”. For Descartes, this expressed a dream of liberation of human through technique from the grip of magical explanations of nature. Descartes wanted to demystify nature, understand its laws, so that humans can have control over their environment and no longer simply endure it. In this conceptualization, nature is, however, seen as an object that humans can or must take advantage of. Descartes philosophy was later seen as the manifesto of human excess, of an anthropocentric conceptualization based on the sciences and techniques, which will be disputed by several philosophers of the 20th century, among which ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MsdNUPtb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Plumwood, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Plumwood, 2002)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30191,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AE94QZT3"],"itemData":{"id":30191,"type":"book","event-place":"London","number-of-pages":"304","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London","title":"Environmental Culture: The Ecological Crisis of Reason","author":[{"family":"Plumwood","given":"Val"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      This mechanistic view of nature where the role of human is to discover the underlying laws of nature has pervaded the development of modern science since the 17th century and greatly contributed to its extraordinary success in e.g., astronomy, biology, chemistry, medicine or physics. Following a series of epistemological debates in Germany and North America at the end of the 19th century, the human-nature dualism finally led to the distinction of two broad scientific categories: the natural sciences and the cultural sciences, or simply the humanities, which still strongly structures the scientific fields of today ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ySbCEHTw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bouchard, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Bouchard, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30196,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VEHQXPG3"],"itemData":{"id":30196,"type":"thesis","publisher":"Université du Québec à Rimouski","title":"Protection de la nature et diversité ontologique chez Philippe Descola : Quelques enjeux et une possibilité de dépassement avec le contextualisme de Mark Hunyadi","URL":"https://semaphore.uqar.ca/id/eprint/1828/1/Olivier_Bouchard_juillet2020.pdf","author":[{"family":"Bouchard","given":"Olivier"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Darwin's ground-breaking publication on the origins of species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xh81PRe2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Darwin, 1859)","plainCitation":"(Darwin, 1859)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30195,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C47VUTJL"],"itemData":{"id":30195,"type":"book","event-place":"London","publisher":"John Murray","publisher-place":"London","title":"On the origin of species by means of natural selection, or the preservation of favoured races in the struggle for life","author":[{"family":"Darwin","given":"Charles"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1859"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} was the first scientific study (with Wallace’s study published at the same time) challenging the divine creation of human and so, the foundation of this separation between human and nature. This publication is indeed considered to be the founding text of the scientific theory of evolution, according to which all current living species, including human, have evolved from extinct ancestral species, by means of natural selection. The theory of evolution has been refined considerably since Darwin. It has in particular been enhanced by the contributions of genetics and it is today the unifying paradigm in life sciences ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dMgvEaj5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Richerson, 2021; Scheiner & Mindell, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Richerson, 2021; Scheiner & Mindell, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30177,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ITDUX3AE"],"itemData":{"id":30177,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"150 years of\\n              The Descent of Man\\n            \\n            \\n              Charles Darwin's\\n              The Descent of Man\\n              was published in 1871. Ever since, it has been the foundation stone of human evolutionary studies. Richerson\\n             \\n              reviewed how modern studies of human biological and cultural evolution reflect the ideas in Darwin's work. They emphasize how cooperation, social learning, and cumulative culture in the ancestors of modern humans were key to our evolution and were enhanced during the environmental upheavals of the Pleistocene. The evolutionary perspective has come to permeate not just human biology but also the social sciences, vindicating Darwin's insights.\\n            \\n            \\n              Science\\n              , aba3776, this issue p.\\n              eaba3776\\n            \\n          , \\n            A Review suggests that evolving cumulative culture and expanding cooperation in unstable Pleistocene environments drove human origins.\\n          , \\n            \\n              BACKGROUND\\n              \\n                Charles Darwin’s\\n                The Descent of Man\\n                , published on 24 February 1871, laid the grounds for scientific studies into human origins and evolution. We look at the advances in our understanding of these processes through the lenses of modern speciation theory. Applying this theory to specific cases requires one to identify and understand the nature of (i) the ancestor and various preexisting adaptations and traits that it possessed that allowed or simplified the speciation process, (ii) evolutionary forces responsible for major differences between the emergent species and its close relatives, and (iii) the most salient adaptations characteristic of the new species and its evolutionary history (such as genetic, morphological, behavioral, spatial, and temporal).\\n              \\n            \\n            \\n              ADVANCES\\n              Modern research shows that we share many developmental, physiological, morphological, cognitive, and psychological characteristics as well as about 96% of our DNA with the anthropoid apes. We now know that since our last common ancestor with the other apes 6 million to 8 million years ago, human evolution followed the path common for other species with diversification into closely related species and some subsequent hybridization between them. Since Darwin, a long series of unbridgeable gaps have been proposed between humans and other animals. They focused on tool-making, cultural learning and imitation, empathy, prosociality and cooperation, planning and foresight, episodic memory, metacognition, and theory of mind. However, new insights from neurobiology, genetics, primatology, and behavioral biology only reinforce Darwin’s view that most differences between humans and higher animals are “of degree and not of kind.” What makes us different is that our ancestors evolved greatly enhanced abilities for (and reliance on) cooperation, social learning, and cumulative culture—traits emphasized already by Darwin. Cooperation allowed for environmental risk buffering, cost reduction, and the access to new resources and benefits through the “economy of scale.” Learning and cumulative culture allowed for the accumulation and rapid spread of beneficial innovations between individuals and groups. The enhanced abilities to learn from and cooperate with others became a universal tool, removing the need to evolve specific biological organs for specific environmental challenges. These human traits likely evolved as a response to increasing high-frequency climate changes on the millennial and submillennial scales during the Pleistocene. Once the abilities for cumulative culture and extended cooperation were in place, a suite of subsequent evolutionary changes became possible and likely unavoidable. In particular, human social systems evolved to support mothers through the recruitment of males and nonreproductive females. The most distinctive feature of our species, language, appeared arguably driven by selection for simplifying cooperation. Reliance on social learning and conformity led to the emergence of new factors constraining and driving human behavior, such as morality, social norms, and social institutions. These forces often act against the immediate biological or material interests of individuals, promoting instead the interests of the society as a whole or of its powerful segments. Continuous engagement in cooperation has led to the evolution of strong coalitionary psychology, which can bring us together whenever we perceive that our identity group faces outside threats. Coalitionary psychology also has an undesirable byproduct: often negative or even hostile reaction to others who differ from us in their looks, behaviors, beliefs, caste, or class.\\n            \\n            \\n              OUTLOOK\\n              \\n                Our society faces challenges, including climate change; various types of inequality; economic crises; political, cultural, and religious conflicts; and pandemics. Similar challenges have repeatedly arisen and were dealt with in the past with varying success. What makes the current situation different is not only the scale of societal threats but also that modern science can provide guidance on how to respond to them. Adequately answering these challenges requires understanding humans’ social behavior and the roles of cooperation, social learning, and culture for human decision-making. Evolutionary perspective is already helping to synthesize the contributions of social sciences, including anthropology, psychology, economics, political science, and history. The impact of\\n                Descent\\n                on the social sciences and on the development and implementation of different policies by practitioners and policymakers to improve our society will only grow.\\n              \\n              \\n                \\n                  Depictions of organic evolution versus cultural evolution.\\n                  \\n                    (Left) Organic evolution and (right) cultural evolution, as depicted in Alfred L. Kroeber’s 1923 textbook\\n                    Anthropology: Cultural Patterns and Processes\\n                    . Biological inheritance is rigid from parents to offspring in eukaryotes, and species mostly do not exchange genes. Culture is potentially acquired from anyone in a person’s social network, and ideas spread rather readily from culture to culture.\\n                  \\n                \\n                \\n                \\n                  IMAGE: N. CARY/\\n                  SCIENCE\\n                \\n              \\n            \\n          , \\n            \\n              Charles Darwin’s\\n              The Descent of Man\\n              , published 150 years ago, laid the grounds for scientific studies into human origins and evolution. Three of his insights have been reinforced by modern science. The first is that we share many characteristics (genetic, developmental, physiological, morphological, cognitive, and psychological) with our closest relatives, the anthropoid apes. The second is that humans have a talent for high-level cooperation reinforced by morality and social norms. The third is that we have greatly expanded the social learning capacity that we see already in other primates. Darwin’s emphasis on the role of culture deserves special attention because during an increasingly unstable Pleistocene environment, cultural accumulation allowed changes in life history; increased cognition; and the appearance of language, social norms, and institutions.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aba3776","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6544","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"eaba3776","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Modern theories of human evolution foreshadowed by Darwin’s Descent of Man","URL":"https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aba3776","volume":"372","author":[{"family":"Richerson","given":"Peter J."},{"family":"Gavrilets","given":"Sergey"},{"family":"Waal","given":"Frans B. M.","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",5,21]]}}},{"id":35109,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A4TBPY8C"],"itemData":{"id":35109,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Evolutionary theory has come a long way since the days of Charles Darwin. Today, evolutionary biologists and influenced by such concepts as plate tectonics, chromosomes and DNA as the material of inheritance, epigenetics, and much else that wasn't known to Darwin and his contemporaries. The goal of this book is to explore and develop a modern theoretical framework for the field. To that end, the book is divided into two sections. The first examines topics that cross multiple areas of evolution theory, and the second section examines the strengths, weaknesses, and gaps in those areas.\\"--","call-number":"QH366.2 .T525 2020","event-place":"Chicago","ISBN":"978-0-226-67102-4","number-of-pages":"442","publisher":"The University of Chicago Press","publisher-place":"Chicago","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The theory of evolution: principles, concepts, and assumptions","title-short":"The theory of evolution","editor":[{"family":"Scheiner","given":"Samuel M."},{"family":"Mindell","given":"David P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} for a recent review on the scientific theory of evolution).  
      The theory of evolution fully changed the Judeo-Christian view of human-nature relationship in the western culture, as, from an evolutionary perspective, human belongs to the long chain of living organisms on Earth and is thus part of it. This new paradigm has also fed other scientific fields, such as ecology, which defines the ecosystem as the largest functional unit that includes both living organisms and the abiotic environment, each influencing the properties of the other, and the two being necessary to maintain life as it exists on Earth ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gIlYbiSx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Odum, 1953)","plainCitation":"(Odum, 1953)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34551,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GATXT5CM"],"itemData":{"id":34551,"type":"book","event-place":"Philadelphia","number-of-pages":"383","publisher":"W. B. Saunders Company","publisher-place":"Philadelphia","title":"Fundamentals of ecology","author":[{"family":"Odum","given":"Eugene P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1953"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There is a general consensus within the scientific community to consider that human is part of ecosystems, which is moreover reflected World Charter for Nature adopted by the United Nations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fbPJG3cf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UN General Assembly, 1982)","plainCitation":"(UN General Assembly, 1982)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35001,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MK25SARS"],"itemData":{"id":35001,"type":"document","language":"Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian, Spanish","publisher":"United Nations","title":"UN World Charter for Nature","URL":"https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/39295?ln=en","author":[{"family":"UN General Assembly","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1982"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} which declares that “mankind is part of nature and life depends on the uninterrupted functioning of natural systems which ensure the supply of energy and nutrients” and that “civilization is rooted in nature, which has shaped human culture and influenced all artistic and scientific achievement”. 
      As demonstrated in this assessment and other works (e.g., through the “One Health” approach, see ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mF94XvoI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2020)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":66,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMSKDRYR"],"itemData":{"id":66,"type":"report","abstract":"The IPBES Bureau and Multidisciplinary Expert Panel (MEP) authorized a workshop on biodiversity and pandemics that was held virtually on 27-31 July 2020 in accordance with the provisions on “Platform workshops” in support of Plenary-approved activities, set out in section 6.1 of the procedures for the preparation of Platform deliverables (IPBES-3/3, annexe I). This workshop report and any recommendations or conclusions contained therein have not been reviewed, endorsed or approved by the IPBES Plenary. The workshop report is considered supporting material available to authors in the preparation of ongoing or future IPBES assessments. While undergoing a scientific peer-review, this material has not been subjected to formal IPBES review processes. <strong>Workshop report:</strong> “<em>IPBES Pandemics Workshop Report 20201029 v1.1.pdf</em>”<br> This workshop report is released in a non-laid out format. It will undergo minor editing before being released in a laid-out format. <strong>Executive summary</strong>: “<em>IPBES Pandemics Workshop Executive Summary 20201028 v1.0.pdf</em>” To get the latest version of the report visit https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4147317 For inquiries, please contact secretariat@ipbes.net https://ipbes.net/events/ipbes-workshop-biodiversity-and-pandemics","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","language":"en","license":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International, Open Access","note":"version: 1.3\\nDOI: 10.5281/ZENODO.4147317","publisher":"IPBES secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"Workshop Report on Biodiversity and Pandemics of the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES)","URL":"https://zenodo.org/record/4147317","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."},{"family":"Neves","given":"C.","non-dropping-particle":"das"},{"family":"Amuasi","given":"J."},{"family":"Hayman","given":"D."},{"family":"Kuiken","given":"T."},{"family":"Roche","given":"B."},{"family":"Zambrana-Torrelio","given":"C."},{"family":"Buss","given":"P."},{"family":"Dundarova","given":"H."},{"family":"Feferholtz","given":"Y."},{"family":"Foldvari","given":"G."},{"family":"Igbinosa","given":"E."},{"family":"Junglen","given":"S."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Q."},{"family":"Suzan","given":"G."},{"family":"Uhart","given":"M."},{"family":"Wannous","given":"C."},{"family":"Woolaston","given":"K."},{"family":"Mosig Reidl","given":"P."},{"family":"O'Brien","given":"K."},{"family":"Pascual","given":"U."},{"family":"Stoett","given":"P."},{"family":"Li","given":"H."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H. T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, humans are indeed intimately connected to their environment and to the other living micro- and macro-organisms on which they rely for their food, their health, their shelter and the energy necessary for their activities. Humans are also strongly impacted by various processes linked to the functioning of ecosystems, and more generally of the whole planet, such as droughts, floods, storms, fires, desertification or zoonotic diseases. While most of these phenomena are intrinsic to the functioning of ecosystems, they have become more intense consequently to the degradation of the integrity and functioning of most terrestrial and aquatic ecosystems due to human activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tRLt9yBT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Crutzen & Stoermer, 2000; IPBES, 2019d)","plainCitation":"(Crutzen & Stoermer, 2000; IPBES, 2019d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34523,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5XJKKAFP"],"itemData":{"id":34523,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Newsletter","page":"17-17","title":"The “Anthropocene”","URL":"http://www.igbp.net/download/18.316f18321323470177580001401/1376383088452/NL41.pdf","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Crutzen","given":"Paul J"},{"family":"Stoermer","given":"Eugene F"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      This conceptualization which assumes that human is part of nature remains however still marginal in the western culture, but it is echoing the much older concept of "Mother Nature" or “Mother Earth” of other human civilizations for which nature and human are indivisible, such as in many indigenous cultures (see section 1.4) or within Taoism ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4WSBo9pl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ma, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Ma, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30194,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TVMVG3R3"],"itemData":{"id":30194,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"National Science Review","DOI":"10.1093/nsr/nwaa265","ISSN":"2095-5138, 2053-714X","issue":"7","language":"en","page":"nwaa265","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Unity of Nature and Man: a new vision and conceptual framework for the Post-2020 Global Biodiversity Framework","title-short":"Unity of Nature and Man","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/nsr/article/doi/10.1093/nsr/nwaa265/5936570","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Ma","given":"Tianxiao"},{"family":"Hu","given":"Yisi"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Meng"},{"family":"Yu","given":"Lijun"},{"family":"Wei","given":"Fuwen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",7,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is also echoing American philosophers from the 19th and early 20th centuries, such as Emerson, Thoreau and especially Leopold. In his famous book A Sand County Almanac ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7A3qRU55","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Leopold, 1949)","plainCitation":"(Leopold, 1949)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30650,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XZIFUAU7"],"itemData":{"id":30650,"type":"book","edition":"First","event-place":"New York","number-of-pages":"212","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"New York","title":"A Sand County Almanac And Sketches Here and There","author":[{"family":"Leopold","given":"Aldo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1949"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Leopold poses the basis of an ecological ethics, which can be summed up as an ethics of the earth which should not prohibit the exploitation of its resources, but which affirms the right of the living organisms of the earth to continue to exist in a natural state. The human thus passes from the role of conqueror of nature, inherited from the Judeo-Christian theology, to that of member and citizen of the earth community. This conception leads to respect for other members and the community as such and to a lower anthropocentrism, without sanctuarizing nature which is constantly evolving. The human-nature relationship therefore implies a continuous coordination of the interests of the different members of the earth community.  
      On the contrary, the human-nature dualism has fostered the illusion that humanity would exist apart from the rest of nature, to such an extent that humans’ use of nature ad libitum ultimately led to major environmental crises ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"h0bz52rt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ma, 2021; Plumwood, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Ma, 2021; Plumwood, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30194,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TVMVG3R3"],"itemData":{"id":30194,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"National Science Review","DOI":"10.1093/nsr/nwaa265","ISSN":"2095-5138, 2053-714X","issue":"7","language":"en","page":"nwaa265","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Unity of Nature and Man: a new vision and conceptual framework for the Post-2020 Global Biodiversity Framework","title-short":"Unity of Nature and Man","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/nsr/article/doi/10.1093/nsr/nwaa265/5936570","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Ma","given":"Tianxiao"},{"family":"Hu","given":"Yisi"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Meng"},{"family":"Yu","given":"Lijun"},{"family":"Wei","given":"Fuwen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",7,24]]}}},{"id":30191,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AE94QZT3"],"itemData":{"id":30191,"type":"book","event-place":"London","number-of-pages":"304","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London","title":"Environmental Culture: The Ecological Crisis of Reason","author":[{"family":"Plumwood","given":"Val"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An observation that is shared by the scientific community, who has pointed out the direct responsibility of human activity in climate change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5B9Qn2P7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPCC, 2019b, 2019a)","plainCitation":"(IPCC, 2019b, 2019a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32308,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TCQLBKC7"],"itemData":{"id":32308,"type":"chapter","container-title":"IPCC Special Report on the Ocean and Cryosphere in a Changing Climate","event-place":"In press","page":"35","publisher-place":"In press","title":"Summary for Policymakers","author":[{"family":"IPCC","given":""}],"editor":[{"family":"Pörtner","given":"H.-O."},{"family":"Roberts","given":"D.C."},{"family":"Masson-Delmotte","given":"V."},{"family":"Zhai","given":"P."},{"family":"Tignor","given":"M."},{"family":"Poloczanska","given":"E."},{"family":"Mintenbeck","given":"K."},{"family":"Alegría","given":"A."},{"family":"Nicolai","given":"M."},{"family":"Okem","given":"A."},{"family":"Petzold","given":"J."},{"family":"Rama","given":"B."},{"family":"Weyer","given":"N. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":33815,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XM9G23XG"],"itemData":{"id":33815,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Climate  Change  and Land:  an  IPCC  special  report  on  climate  change,  desertification,   land  degradation,  sustainable  land  management,  food  security, and  greenhouse  gas  fluxes  in  terrestrial ecosystems","event-place":"In press","page":"36","publisher-place":"In press","title":"Summary  for  Policymakers","author":[{"family":"IPCC","given":""}],"editor":[{"family":"Shukla","given":"P. R."},{"family":"Skea","given":"J."},{"family":"Calvo Buendia","given":"E."},{"family":"Masson-Delmotte","given":"V."},{"family":"Pörtner","given":"H.-O."},{"family":"Roberts","given":"D.C."},{"family":"Zhai","given":"P."},{"family":"Slade","given":"R."},{"family":"Connors","given":"S."},{"family":"Diemen","given":"R.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Ferrat","given":"M."},{"family":"Haughey","given":"E."},{"family":"Luz","given":"S."},{"family":"Neogi","given":"S."},{"family":"Pathak","given":"M."},{"family":"Petzold","given":"J."},{"family":"Portugal Pereira","given":"J."},{"family":"Vyas","given":"P."},{"family":"Huntley","given":"E."},{"family":"Kissick","given":"K."},{"family":"Belkacemi","given":"M."},{"family":"Malley","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, biodiversity decline ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IYnkRlea","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019d)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and the modification of the main planetary natural processes, which opened a new geological era, the Anthropocene ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lgO7dNgn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zalasiewicz, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Zalasiewicz, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35077,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YHDAKCXD"],"itemData":{"id":35077,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"The Anthropocene, a term launched into public debate by Nobel Prize winner Paul Crutzen, has been used informally to describe the time period during which human actions have had a drastic effect on the Earth and its ecosystems.\\"--","call-number":"GF75 .A644 2018","event-place":"Cambridge","ISBN":"978-1-108-47523-5","number-of-pages":"361","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The anthropocene as a geological time unit: a guide to the scientific evidence and current debate","title-short":"The anthropocene as a geological time unit","editor":[{"family":"Zalasiewicz","given":"J. A."},{"family":"Waters","given":"C. N."},{"family":"Williams","given":"M."},{"family":"Summerhayes","given":"C. P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The human-nature dualism also engendered the notion of wilderness (or pristine nature). This notion is mostly a western cultural construction, notably due to Europeans and North Americans during the 18th and 19th centuries. “Wilderness” idealizes nature as a sublime, primitive and virgin natural space where the human has no place, except as a contemplative traveller (see section 1.3.2, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cTHBvljT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cronon, 1996)","plainCitation":"(Cronon, 1996)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":14,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TF4DFF6H"],"itemData":{"id":14,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"William Cronon;  The Trouble with Wilderness; Or, Getting Back to the Wrong Nature, Environmental History, Volume 1, Issue 1, 1 January 1996, Pages 7–28, https:","container-title":"Environmental History","DOI":"10.2307/3985059","ISSN":"1084-5453","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Enviro Hist Durh N C","language":"en","page":"7-28","source":"academic.oup.com","title":"The Trouble with Wilderness; or, Getting Back to the Wrong Nature","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/envhis/article/1/1/7/525047","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Cronon","given":"William"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Such cultural construction of wilderness is problematic in general and particularly for the purpose of this assessment because (i) it ignores past and current scientific evidence according to which nearly three quarters of terrestrial nature (including landscapes and biodiversity) has been shaped over several millennia by diverse histories of human habitation and use by indigenous peoples and local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tp2RTADp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ellis, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Ellis, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30171,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TTWVRJGV"],"itemData":{"id":30171,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Archaeological and paleoecological evidence shows that by 10,000 BCE, all human societies employed varying degrees of ecologically transformative land use practices, including burning, hunting, species propagation, domestication, cultivation, and others that have left long-term legacies across the terrestrial biosphere. Yet, a lingering paradigm among natural scientists, conservationists, and policymakers is that human transformation of terrestrial nature is mostly recent and inherently destructive. Here, we use the most up-to-date, spatially explicit global reconstruction of historical human populations and land use to show that this paradigm is likely wrong. Even 12,000 y ago, nearly three quarters of Earth?s land was inhabited and therefore shaped by human societies, including more than 95% of temperate and 90% of tropical woodlands. Lands now characterized as ?natural,? ?intact,? and ?wild? generally exhibit long histories of use, as do protected areas and Indigenous lands, and current global patterns of vertebrate species richness and key biodiversity areas are more strongly associated with past patterns of land use than with present ones in regional landscapes now characterized as natural. The current biodiversity crisis can seldom be explained by the loss of uninhabited wildlands, resulting instead from the appropriation, colonization, and intensifying use of the biodiverse cultural landscapes long shaped and sustained by prior societies. Recognizing this deep cultural connection with biodiversity will therefore be essential to resolve the crisis.","archive_location":"WOS:000648438500015","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.2023483118","ISSN":"0027-8424","issue":"17","language":"English","title":"People have shaped most of terrestrial nature for at least 12,000 years","volume":"118","author":[{"family":"Ellis","given":"EC"},{"family":"Gauthier","given":"N"},{"family":"Goldewijk","given":"KK"},{"family":"Bird","given":"RB"},{"family":"Boivin","given":"N"},{"family":"Diaz","given":"S"},{"family":"Fuller","given":"DQ"},{"family":"Gill","given":"JL"},{"family":"Kaplan","given":"JO"},{"family":"Kingston","given":"N"},{"family":"Locke","given":"H"},{"family":"McMichael","given":"CNH"},{"family":"Ranco","given":"D"},{"family":"Rick","given":"TC"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"MR"},{"family":"Stephens","given":"L"},{"family":"Svenning","given":"JC"},{"family":"Watson","given":"JEM"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, (ii) it tends to sanctify the notion of pristine landscapes in conservation practices and policies, which in turn denies agency, access rights and knowledge of indigenous peoples and local communities in the maintenance of their territories and traditional uses of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qXV5isBb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fletcher, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Fletcher, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30186,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C6HG5UGT"],"itemData":{"id":30186,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The environmental crises currently gripping the Earth have been codified in a new proposed geological epoch: the Anthropocene. This epoch, according to the Anthropocene Working Group, began in the mid-20th century and reflects the “great acceleration” that began with industrialization in Europe [J. Zalasiewicz,\\n              Anthropocene\\n              19, 55–60 (2017)]. Ironically, European ideals of protecting a pristine “wilderness,” free from the damaging role of humans, is still often heralded as the antidote to this human-induced crisis [J. E. M. Watson,\\n              Nature\\n              , 563, 27–30 (2018)]. Despite decades of critical engagement by Indigenous and non-Indigenous observers, large international nongovernmental organizations, philanthropists, global institutions, and nation-states continue to uphold the notion of pristine landscapes as wilderness in conservation ideals and practices. In doing so, dominant global conservation policy and public perceptions still fail to recognize that Indigenous and local peoples have long valued, used, and shaped “high-value” biodiverse landscapes. Moreover, the exclusion of people from many of these places under the guise of wilderness protection has degraded their ecological condition and is hastening the demise of a number of highly valued systems. Rather than denying Indigenous and local peoples’ agency, access rights, and knowledge in conserving their territories, we draw upon a series of case studies to argue that wilderness is an inappropriate and dehumanizing construct, and that Indigenous and community conservation areas must be legally recognized and supported to enable socially just, empowering, and sustainable conservation across scale.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.2022218118","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"40","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"e2022218118","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Indigenous knowledge and the shackles of wilderness","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.2022218118","volume":"118","author":[{"family":"Fletcher","given":"Michael-Shawn"},{"family":"Hamilton","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Dressler","given":"Wolfram"},{"family":"Palmer","given":"Lisa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",10,5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and (iii) it does not allow the possibility to human to find an ethical and sustainable place in nature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DG4dwKAj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cronon, 1996)","plainCitation":"(Cronon, 1996)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":14,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TF4DFF6H"],"itemData":{"id":14,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"William Cronon;  The Trouble with Wilderness; Or, Getting Back to the Wrong Nature, Environmental History, Volume 1, Issue 1, 1 January 1996, Pages 7–28, https:","container-title":"Environmental History","DOI":"10.2307/3985059","ISSN":"1084-5453","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Enviro Hist Durh N C","language":"en","page":"7-28","source":"academic.oup.com","title":"The Trouble with Wilderness; or, Getting Back to the Wrong Nature","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/envhis/article/1/1/7/525047","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Cronon","given":"William"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Considering humans as part of nature (the earth community of Leopold) would modify the relationship of humans with other living organisms and the terrestrial abiotic environment and would probably make this relationship more respectful and more sustainable, as demonstrated by indigenous peoples and local communities' traditional practices and uses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eg1VCCiY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barthel, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Barthel, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30193,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XWWM8UM7"],"itemData":{"id":30193,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Food security for a growing world population is high on the list of grand sustainability challenges, as is reducing the pace of biodiversity loss in landscapes of food production. Here we shed new insights on areas that harbor place specific social memories related to food security and stewardship of biodiversity. We call them Bio-cultural refugia. Our goals are to illuminate how bio-cultural refugia store, revive and transmit memory of agricultural biodiversity and ecosystem services, and how such social memories are carried forward between people and across cohorts. We discuss the functions of such refugia for addressing the twin goals of food security and biodiversity conservation in landscapes of food production. The methodological approach is first of its kind in combining the discourses on food security, social memory and biodiversity management. We find that the rich biodiversity of many regionally distinct cultural landscapes has been maintained through a mosaic of management practices that have co-evolved in relation to local environmental fluctuations, and that such practices are carried forward by both biophysical and social features in bio-cultural refugia including; genotypes, artifacts, written accounts, as well as embodied rituals, art, oral traditions and self-organized systems of rules. Combined these structure a diverse portfolio of practices that result in genetic reservoirs — source areas — for the wide array of species, which in interplay produce vital ecosystem services, needed for future food security related to environmental uncertainties, volatile financial markets and large scale conflicts. In Europe, processes related to the large-scale industrialization of agriculture threaten such bio-cultural refugia. The paper highlights that the dual goals to reduce pressures from modern agriculture on biodiversity, while maintaining food security, entails more extensive collaboration with farmers oriented toward ecologically sound practices.","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.17615/P389-W730","issue":"5","license":"In Copyright","note":"publisher: The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill University Libraries","page":"1142-1152","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"Bio-cultural refugia—Safeguarding diversity of practices for food security and biodiversity","URL":"https://cdr.lib.unc.edu/concern/articles/q524jx579","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Barthel","given":"Stephan"},{"family":"Svedin","given":"Uno"},{"family":"Crumley","given":"Carole"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indigenous peoples and local communities indeed offer a diversity of alternative worldviews, human-nature relationships, cosmologies and philosophies, which, in general, include a lack of division between nature and culture and respectful relations of kinship and reciprocity between humans and non-humans ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pVp63NKB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brond\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}zio, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Brondízio, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32706,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KFYELQZ8"],"itemData":{"id":32706,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","note":"publisher: Annual Reviews","page":"481–509","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Locally Based, Regionally Manifested, and Globally Relevant: Indigenous and Local Knowledge, Values, and Practices for Nature","title-short":"Locally Based, Regionally Manifested, and Globally Relevant","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Brondízio","given":"Eduardo S."},{"family":"Aumeeruddy-Thomas","given":"Yildiz"},{"family":"Bates","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Carino","given":"Joji"},{"family":"Fernández-Llamazares","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Ferrari","given":"Maurizio Farhan"},{"family":"Galvin","given":"Kathleen"},{"family":"Reyes-García","given":"Victoria"},{"family":"McElwee","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"Molnar","given":"Zsolt"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Such considerations have heavy implications for this assessment, as sustainable use of wild species would imply a transformative change in people’s conceptualization of nature, shifting from the traditional, and still deeply rooted, western human-nature dualism to a more systemic view where the human is a member and citizen of nature, among others.  
      
1.3.4 Organizing structure
As set out in Section 1.1.2, the core factors to consider in the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species are the components of nature that are affected (wild species), the practices associated with the use of wild species, and the end uses, i.e., nature’s contributions to people. These take place within a given culture, economy, governance system, and technological development. To ensure consistency across the assessment, and to facilitate the distillation of key findings, a common organizing structure has been used throughout and is summarized in Figure 1.5. Categories within each system for the use of wild species are presented in diagram form below with narrative explanations in the subsequent paragraphs. They are not presented as a hierarchy but rather as interacting elements of the social-ecological systems of the use of wild species. 
      It is worth noting that many categories are not mutually exclusive, a point essential to understanding wild species, their uses, and benefits to people. For example, many species occur in different ecosystems over the course of their lifecycle. Wild anadromous fish, such as salmonids are a case in point as their lifecycle includes time spent in both freshwater and marine environments. Similarly, a single species may be the support of multiple uses. For example, wild rice (Zizania palustris L.) is both a food and a ceremonial resource while the white gum tree or gum Arabic (Acacia senegal) is widely used as a food additive and in the pharmaceutical industry for medical puposes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VPoamcQU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Catarino, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Catarino, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30713,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8LNRCBGI"],"itemData":{"id":30713,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.6736","ISSN":"2167-8359","page":"e6736","title":"Conservation and sustainable use of the medicinal Leguminosae plants from Angola.","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Catarino","given":"Silvia"},{"family":"Duarte","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Costa","given":"Esperança"},{"family":"Carrero","given":"Paula"},{"family":"Romeiras","given":"Maria M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, multiple activities, or practices, may be employed to make use of a single species for a single purpose. In several African countries, the large terrestrial animals are subject to multiple  activities on the same territory, such as non-extractive practices (wildlife watching) and trophy hunting (terrestrial animal harvesting).

Figure 1.5. Common organizing structure of the sustainable use assessment. CC-BY.
The wild species panel
Taxa. For practical purposes, species may be assessed in terms of higher taxonomic levels. The sustainable use assessment used the following higher level categories of taxa: algae, fungi, plants, trees, terrestrial animals and aquatic animals because there are differences in the status of use and practices of these groups as well as in the policy options for managing their uses (Figure 1.6).
Biomes, ecoregions and ecosystems. Each species of a given taxa take place in a given ecosystem or ecoregion of a given biome. To avoid a level of detail that will obscure identification of key points for policy makers, some information may be analysed according to ecological biomes (terrestrial, marine and freshwater). These have been defined at a fairly high level using the typology adopted by the IPBES Global Assessment of Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services, which is more aggregated than most biological typologies. Elements that are relevant to this assessment are: 
* Freshwater biomes are classified as: (i) wetlands and (ii) inland waters. 
* Marine biomes are classified as: (i) coastal systems, (ii) shelf systems and (iii) open and deep seas.
* Terrestrial biomes are classified as:  (i) grasslands-steppes-savannas, (ii) forests (iii) deserts and (iv) mountains. Where appropriate, ‘anthropogenic biomes’ are also included, namely urban areas and cultivated areas.
The practices panel
For the purposes of this assessment, use of wild species involves both the practices associated with harvest or other direct interactions with wild species, as well as the end purpose for which the species is used (e.g., for food or medicine). Although the sustainable use of wild species has been discussed in academic and policy documents for more than 40 years, there is no single or accepted set of definitions for the practices associated with the use of wild species. Here the practices are defined to show how they have been applied in the assessment.
      The scoping report directs us to consider consumptive and non-consumptive activities, or practices (Annex IV to decision IPBES-5/1, 2018). These terms have been in use for a long time referring mostly to the use of wild animals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c3OZVvny","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 1995)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":28897,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BD3KX62A","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LT3AI4JH"],"itemData":{"id":28897,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","publisher":"Food and Agriculture ORganization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, they can be confusing due to various possible interpretations of consumption, particularly when one is looking for a term that can be used for both plants and animals. Following the FAO, this assessment uses the alternative terms of extractive (consumptive) versus non-extractive (non-consumptive) practices. Extractive practices involve temporary or permanent removal of organisms, part of them or materials derived from them, and may result in mortality of the individual to be used (e.g., hunting or whole plant harvest), but does not necessarily do so (e.g., limited collection of plant propagules or wild honey, or shearing and releasing of vicuña). Non-extractive use does not directly entail removal of materials from wild populations (e.g., wildlife photography) but may result in inadvertent mortality as a result of, for example, disease transmission at artificial feeders.  
      For clarity, the sustainable use assessment then mapped different types and modalities of practices against the main taxonomic groups (Figure 1.6). It started first by categorising the nature of different practices based on whether they were extractive or not, then on additional characteristics of the practices such as whether it involved the entire organism or only a part or product of the organism. This mapping exercise showed that certain terms in common use were typically associated with a set of practices mostly aimed at one higher taxonomic group, e.g., fishing incorporates several practices relating to the extractive use of aquatic animals. In order to align most closely with common understanding of terms, the sustainable use assessment used the terms of fishing, gathering, and logging to cover the practices set out in Figure 1.6. The one diversion from this common approach was for hunting. Whereas fishing is understood as almost any practice relating to the harvest of aquatic animals, and can even be applied to harvest of animals in captivity, hunting generally applies to only a subset of these activities for terrestrial animals. As a result, terrestrial animal harvesting has been adopted as the generic term for the extractive practice of terrestrial animals (see below for a full definition).

