5.4.1 Introduction (including practices considered)
In this section, scenario material from the literature search, supplemented with expert knowledge (see data management report at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6451922), is assessed by resource use practice, based on social, technological, economic, environmental, political (STEEP) categories ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OWzIqYaI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Reed, Everard, Reed, & Kenter, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Reed, Everard, Reed, & Kenter, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19079,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SCBWP929"],"itemData":{"id":19079,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2016.08.001","issue":"B","language":"en","page":"230–240","title":"The ripple effect: Institutionalising pro-environmental values to shift societal norms and behaviours","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Reed","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Everard","given":"M."},{"family":"Reed","given":"M."},{"family":"Kenter","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}  including cultural, here though referred as STEEP+C. Crucially, it is important to recognize that for some practices (e.g., gathering), scenarios and projections are extremely limited and/or unavailable in the literature. In such circumstances, rather than leaving the sections empty, instead ‘scenario-based drivers’ of sustainable use are explored; that is, studies of factors that could affect sustainable use when integrated into scenarios. These sections are then identified as knowledge gaps. 
5.4.2 Fishing
5.4.2.1 Introduction
Fisheries contribute to food security with fish being a major source of animal protein for about 1 billion people worldwide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qSRjpri1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F.A.O., 2020)","plainCitation":"(F.A.O., 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19304,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4V4PXIGH"],"itemData":{"id":19304,"type":"document","language":"it","note":"publisher-place: Rome\\nDOI: 10.4060/ca8843en","title":"Addressing inequality in times of COVID-19","URL":"https://doi.org/10.4060/ca8843en","author":[{"literal":"F.A.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Annual marine fisheries production has been relatively stagnant over the past 3 decades. In 2018, global catch totaled 84 million tons with about 73% of catches destined for human consumption and the remaining 27% for fishmeal and fish-oil. Fisheries production is expected to stay at high levels, reaching about 96 million tonnes in 2030 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mSoV9LGR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5385,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":5385,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and almost 100 by 2050 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a263k6mi1j8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations Nutrition, 2021)","plainCitation":"(United Nations Nutrition, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":23280,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LXE4254L"],"itemData":{"id":23280,"type":"document","title":"The role of aquatic foods in sustainable healthy diets. Roma: UN Nutrition Secretariat, 2021.","author":[{"family":"United Nations Nutrition","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  The proportion of fish production destined for human consumption is projected to continue to grow, reaching 89 percent by 2030 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MX1e9Xnp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5385,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":5385,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and 92% in 2050 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kDBJxsng","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations Nutrition, 2021)","plainCitation":"(United Nations Nutrition, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":23280,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LXE4254L"],"itemData":{"id":23280,"type":"document","title":"The role of aquatic foods in sustainable healthy diets. Roma: UN Nutrition Secretariat, 2021.","author":[{"family":"United Nations Nutrition","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Future patterns of demand for marine biological resources will be shaped by social, environmental and economic factors including stagnating capture fishery production, a growing population, increasing wealth ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TXpmo6xa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garcia & Grainger, 2005; Garcia & Rosenberg, 2010; Guillen, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Garcia & Grainger, 2005; Garcia & Rosenberg, 2010; Guillen, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":3098,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XXII8E56"],"itemData":{"id":3098,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences","DOI":"10.1098/rstb.2004.1580","ISSN":"0962-8436, 1471-2970","issue":"1453","language":"en","page":"21-46","source":"Crossref","title":"Gloom and doom? The future of marine capture fisheries","title-short":"Gloom and doom?","volume":"360","editor":[{"family":"Beddington","given":"J. R."},{"family":"Kirkwood","given":"G. P."}],"author":[{"family":"Garcia","given":"Serge M."},{"family":"Grainger","given":"Richard J. R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",1,29]]}}},{"id":20142,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MK7JWF2R"],"itemData":{"id":20142,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"World population is expected to grow from the present 6.8 billion people to about 9 billion by 2050. The growing need for nutritious and healthy food will increase the demand for fisheries products from marine sources, whose productivity is already highly stressed by excessive fishing pressure, growing organic pollution, toxic contamination, coastal degradation and climate change. Looking towards 2050, the question is how fisheries governance, and the national and international policy and legal frameworks within which it is nested, will ensure a sustainable harvest, maintain biodiversity and ecosystem functions, and adapt to climate change. This paper looks at global fisheries production, the state of resources, contribution to food security and governance. It describes the main changes affecting the sector, including geographical expansion, fishing capacity-building, natural variability, environmental degradation and climate change. It identifies drivers and future challenges, while suggesting how new science, policies and interventions could best address those challenges.","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences","DOI":"10.1098/rstb.2010.0171","ISSN":"0962-8436, 1471-2970","issue":"1554","journalAbbreviation":"Phil. Trans. R. Soc. B","language":"en","page":"2869-2880","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Food security and marine capture fisheries: characteristics, trends, drivers and future perspectives","title-short":"Food security and marine capture fisheries","volume":"365","author":[{"family":"Garcia","given":"Serge M."},{"family":"Rosenberg","given":"Andrew A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",9,27]]}}},{"id":20140,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3CGNBHBP"],"itemData":{"id":20140,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2019.02.045","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"19-28","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Aquaculture subsidies in the European Union: Evolution, impact and future potential for growth","title-short":"Aquaculture subsidies in the European Union","volume":"104","author":[{"family":"Guillen","given":"Jordi"},{"family":"Asche","given":"Frank"},{"family":"Carvalho","given":"Natacha"},{"family":"Fernández Polanco","given":"José M."},{"family":"Llorente","given":"Ignacio"},{"family":"Nielsen","given":"Rasmus"},{"family":"Nielsen","given":"Max"},{"family":"Villasante","given":"Sebastian"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, increasing aquaculture production and competition with wild capture fisheries for natural resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UeeRLxi1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kristofersson & Anderson, 2006; Tacon & Metian, 2008, 2009, 2015, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Kristofersson & Anderson, 2006; Tacon & Metian, 2008, 2009, 2015, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20144,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GUU2I7P5"],"itemData":{"id":20144,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2005.11.004","ISSN":"0308597X","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"721-725","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Is there a relationship between fisheries and farming? Interdependence of fisheries, animal production and aquaculture","title-short":"Is there a relationship between fisheries and farming?","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Kristofersson","given":"Dadi"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"James L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",11]]}}},{"id":4145,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2NTQX6BN"],"itemData":{"id":4145,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The ?n?sh and crustacean aquaculture sector is still highly dependent upon marine capture ?sheries for sourcing key dietary nutrient inputs, including ?sh meal and ?sh oil. This dependency is particularly strong within compound aquafeeds for farmed carnivorous ?n?sh species and marine shrimp.","container-title":"Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1016/j.aquaculture.2008.08.015","ISSN":"00448486","issue":"1-4","language":"en","page":"146-158","source":"Crossref","title":"Global overview on the use of fish meal and fish oil in industrially compounded aquafeeds: Trends and future prospects","title-short":"Global overview on the use of fish meal and fish oil in industrially compounded aquafeeds","volume":"285","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G.J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",12]]}}},{"id":4148,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TDJJGRG7"],"itemData":{"id":4148,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1080/10641260802677074","ISSN":"1064-1262, 1547-6553","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"305-317","source":"Crossref","title":"Fishing for Aquaculture: Non-Food Use of Small Pelagic Forage Fish—A Global Perspective","title-short":"Fishing for Aquaculture","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",6,17]]}}},{"id":4146,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LGDYCGI4"],"itemData":{"id":4146,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The rise of aquaculture has attracted a great deal of attention and this has increased since the sector is now providing more ?sh and crustaceans than capture ?sheries. This global prominence has been partly facilitated by the availability and onfarm provision of feed inputs within the major aquaculture producing countries. More than 70% of the total global aquaculture production is dependent upon the supply of external feed inputs. For the aquaculture sector to maintain its current growth rate, the supply of nutrient and feed inputs will have to grow at a similar rate, while aquatic ingredients production remains static and other sectors compete for same feed resources. This paper attempts to make a global analysis of aquaculture growth, its role in global food production, and to update the estimates of compound feed dependent ?sh and crustacean species.","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science & Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1080/23308249.2014.987209","ISSN":"2330-8249, 2330-8257","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1-10","source":"Crossref","title":"Feed Matters: Satisfying the Feed Demand of Aquaculture","title-short":"Feed Matters","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1,2]]}}},{"id":4146,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LGDYCGI4"],"itemData":{"id":4146,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The rise of aquaculture has attracted a great deal of attention and this has increased since the sector is now providing more ?sh and crustaceans than capture ?sheries. This global prominence has been partly facilitated by the availability and onfarm provision of feed inputs within the major aquaculture producing countries. More than 70% of the total global aquaculture production is dependent upon the supply of external feed inputs. For the aquaculture sector to maintain its current growth rate, the supply of nutrient and feed inputs will have to grow at a similar rate, while aquatic ingredients production remains static and other sectors compete for same feed resources. This paper attempts to make a global analysis of aquaculture growth, its role in global food production, and to update the estimates of compound feed dependent ?sh and crustacean species.","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science & Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1080/23308249.2014.987209","ISSN":"2330-8249, 2330-8257","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1-10","source":"Crossref","title":"Feed Matters: Satisfying the Feed Demand of Aquaculture","title-short":"Feed Matters","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, dietary preferences, and the impacts of climate change on existing and novel fisheries. 
      As well as providing a source of calories and protein, aquatic species provide many nutritional benefits to the human population. An assessment of the nutritional value of aquatic animal food-sources in comparison to terrestrial has shown that the top seven categories of aquatic food, including pelagic fishes, some shellfish, and salmonids are more nutritious than beef, lamb, goat, chicken or pork when averaging across the seven nutrients assessed (omega-3 long-chain polyunsaturated fatty acids, vitamins A and B12, calcium, iodine, iron and zinc) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tl3Qpecr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Golden, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Golden, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19104,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5GZ94WQQ"],"itemData":{"id":19104,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-021-03917-1","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7880","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"315-320","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Aquatic foods to nourish nations","volume":"598","author":[{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Koehn","given":"J. Zachary"},{"family":"Shepon","given":"Alon"},{"family":"Passarelli","given":"Simone"},{"family":"Free","given":"Christopher M."},{"family":"Viana","given":"Daniel F."},{"family":"Matthey","given":"Holger"},{"family":"Eurich","given":"Jacob G."},{"family":"Gephart","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Fluet-Chouinard","given":"Etienne"},{"family":"Nyboer","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Lynch","given":"Abigail J."},{"family":"Kjellevold","given":"Marian"},{"family":"Bromage","given":"Sabri"},{"family":"Charlebois","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Barange","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Vannuccini","given":"Stefania"},{"family":"Cao","given":"Ling"},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin M."},{"family":"Rimm","given":"Eric B."},{"family":"Danaei","given":"Goodarz"},{"family":"DeSisto","given":"Camille"},{"family":"Kelahan","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Fiorella","given":"Kathryn J."},{"family":"Little","given":"David C."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Fanzo","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"Shakuntala H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",10,14]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition to their contributions to global and local food security and nutrition, fisheries are also economically and culturally important, and provide other social opportunities such as for recreation, and also contribute to cultural/traditional heritage. 
      Nevertheless, the development of modern fishing practices driven by advances in technology and growing demand, particularly with the advent of industrialized fishing practices, has led to the depletion of numerous individual fish stocks and a decline of the genetic diversity of harvested fish populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ulenoSX8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pinsky & Palumbi, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Pinsky & Palumbi, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19080,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NXRHUY6R"],"itemData":{"id":19080,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Molecular Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/mec.12509","language":"en","page":"29–39","title":"Meta-analysis reveals lower genetic diversity in overfished populations","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Pinsky","given":"M.L."},{"family":"Palumbi","given":"S.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  According to the most recent Food and Agriculture Organization Report of the State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ag7bdl5eee","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5385,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":5385,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, in 2017 just under 66% of assessed fisheries remain within biologically sustainable levels, meaning that about 34% of fish stocks are overexploited. 
      Annual marine fisheries production has been relatively stagnant over the past 3 decades. In 2018, global catch totaled 84 million tons with about 74% of catches destined for human consumption and the remaining 27% for fishmeal and fish-oil. 
      In the following sections, models and scenarios for the future of fisheries are explored, to examine what insight can be gained around the challenges and solutions that lie ahead. Projections of the future of fisheries at multiple scales are examined from social, technological, economic, environmental, political and cultural perspectives.
5.4.2.2 Social
Small-scale fisheries are prevalent in tropical and developing countries where dependence on fish for food and livelihoods is high. Fish consumption can address micronutrient (for example, vitamin A, calcium and iron) deficiencies and improve human health by providing the dominant source of the omega-3 long-chain polyunsaturated fatty acids ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1kuDCS0b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Golden, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Golden, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19104,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5GZ94WQQ"],"itemData":{"id":19104,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-021-03917-1","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7880","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"315-320","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Aquatic foods to nourish nations","volume":"598","author":[{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Koehn","given":"J. Zachary"},{"family":"Shepon","given":"Alon"},{"family":"Passarelli","given":"Simone"},{"family":"Free","given":"Christopher M."},{"family":"Viana","given":"Daniel F."},{"family":"Matthey","given":"Holger"},{"family":"Eurich","given":"Jacob G."},{"family":"Gephart","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Fluet-Chouinard","given":"Etienne"},{"family":"Nyboer","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Lynch","given":"Abigail J."},{"family":"Kjellevold","given":"Marian"},{"family":"Bromage","given":"Sabri"},{"family":"Charlebois","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Barange","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Vannuccini","given":"Stefania"},{"family":"Cao","given":"Ling"},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin M."},{"family":"Rimm","given":"Eric B."},{"family":"Danaei","given":"Goodarz"},{"family":"DeSisto","given":"Camille"},{"family":"Kelahan","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Fiorella","given":"Kathryn J."},{"family":"Little","given":"David C."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Fanzo","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"Shakuntala H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",10,14]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some countries with inadequate nutrient intakes, fish catches exceed dietary requirements for populations within 100km of the coast, emphasizing the local and regional nutritional and income benefits from fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ormkWL38","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hicks, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Hicks, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":15438,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E3GIZU2D"],"itemData":{"id":15438,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-019-1592-6","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7776","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"95-98","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Harnessing global fisheries to tackle micronutrient deficiencies","volume":"574","author":[{"family":"Hicks","given":"Christina C."},{"family":"Cohen","given":"Philippa J."},{"family":"Graham","given":"Nicholas A. J."},{"family":"Nash","given":"Kirsty L."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"D’Lima","given":"Coralie"},{"family":"Mills","given":"David J."},{"family":"Roscher","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"Shakuntala H."},{"family":"Thorne-Lyman","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"MacNeil","given":"M. Aaron"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Climate change is broadly expected to reduce fish catch potential in many regions (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bgZigT2j","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Lotze, Milewski, Fast, Kay, & Worm, 2019; Lam, n.d., p. 2020; Tittensor, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Lotze, Milewski, Fast, Kay, & Worm, 2019; Lam, n.d., p. 2020; Tittensor, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4665,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NNNVNWGC"],"itemData":{"id":4665,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanica Marina","DOI":"10.1515/bot-2019-0027","ISSN":"0006-8055","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Bot Mar","language":"English","page":"395-409","title":"Ecosystem-based management of seaweed harvesting","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Lotze","given":"H. K."},{"family":"Milewski","given":"I."},{"family":"Fast","given":"J."},{"family":"Kay","given":"L."},{"family":"Worm","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}},{"id":19021,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N2QE9LQL"],"itemData":{"id":19021,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"2020 Nature Reviews Earth & Environment","language":"en","page":"440–454","title":"Rashid Sumaila Climate change, tropical fisheries and prospects for sustainable development","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Lam","given":"Vicky W.Y."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Bell","given":"Johann D."},{"family":"Blythe","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Cheung","given":"William W.L."},{"family":"Frölicher","given":"Thomas L."},{"family":"Gasalla","given":"Maria A."},{"literal":"U."}]},"locator":"2020"},{"id":19106,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BMPUDWKE"],"itemData":{"id":19106,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            Projections of climate change impacts on marine ecosystems have revealed long-term declines in global marine animal biomass and unevenly distributed impacts on fisheries. Here we apply an enhanced suite of global marine ecosystem models from the Fisheries and Marine Ecosystem Model Intercomparison Project (Fish-MIP), forced by new-generation Earth system model outputs from Phase 6 of the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project (CMIP6), to provide insights into how projected climate change will affect future ocean ecosystems. Compared with the previous generation CMIP5-forced Fish-MIP ensemble, the new ensemble ecosystem simulations show a greater decline in mean global ocean animal biomass under both strong-mitigation and high-emissions scenarios due to elevated warming, despite greater uncertainty in net primary production in the high-emissions scenario. Regional shifts in the direction of biomass changes highlight the continued and urgent need to reduce uncertainty in the projected responses of marine ecosystems to climate change to help support adaptation planning.","container-title":"Nature Climate Change","DOI":"10.1038/s41558-021-01173-9","ISSN":"1758-678X, 1758-6798","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Nat. Clim. Chang.","language":"en","page":"973-981","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Next-generation ensemble projections reveal higher climate risks for marine ecosystems","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Tittensor","given":"Derek P."},{"family":"Novaglio","given":"Camilla"},{"family":"Harrison","given":"Cheryl S."},{"family":"Heneghan","given":"Ryan F."},{"family":"Barrier","given":"Nicolas"},{"family":"Bianchi","given":"Daniele"},{"family":"Bopp","given":"Laurent"},{"family":"Bryndum-Buchholz","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Britten","given":"Gregory L."},{"family":"Büchner","given":"Matthias"},{"family":"Cheung","given":"William W. L."},{"family":"Christensen","given":"Villy"},{"family":"Coll","given":"Marta"},{"family":"Dunne","given":"John P."},{"family":"Eddy","given":"Tyler D."},{"family":"Everett","given":"Jason D."},{"family":"Fernandes-Salvador","given":"Jose A."},{"family":"Fulton","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Galbraith","given":"Eric D."},{"family":"Gascuel","given":"Didier"},{"family":"Guiet","given":"Jerome"},{"family":"John","given":"Jasmin G."},{"family":"Link","given":"Jason S."},{"family":"Lotze","given":"Heike K."},{"family":"Maury","given":"Olivier"},{"family":"Ortega-Cisneros","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Palacios-Abrantes","given":"Juliano"},{"family":"Petrik","given":"Colleen M."},{"family":"Pontavice","given":"Hubert","non-dropping-particle":"du"},{"family":"Rault","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Richardson","given":"Anthony J."},{"family":"Shannon","given":"Lynne"},{"family":"Shin","given":"Yunne-Jai"},{"family":"Steenbeek","given":"Jeroen"},{"family":"Stock","given":"Charles A."},{"family":"Blanchard","given":"Julia L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, disrupting food security and livelihoods. For instance, domestic demand for fish in the Solomon Islands is expected to exceed supply from domestic capture fisheries and aquaculture if no climate adaptation action is taken ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"b25iXTvG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5385,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":5385,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Expected decreases in global crop production after 2050 due to warmer temperatures will exacerbate food insecurity, and impose additional pressure on small-scale fisheries to fill the food gap, with some countries facing a ‘double-jeopardy’ of simultaneous impacts on both marine and terrestrial production ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2Yqoz2T7","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Rosenzweig, 2014; Blanchard, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Rosenzweig, 2014; Blanchard, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19081,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J5XMIACF"],"itemData":{"id":19081,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PNAS","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1222463110","language":"it","page":"3268–3273","title":"Assessing agricultural risks of climate change in the 21st century in a global gridded crop model intercomparison","volume":"111","author":[{"family":"Rosenzweig","given":"C."},{"family":"Elliott","given":"J."},{"family":"Deryng","given":"D."},{"family":"Ruane","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Müller","given":"C."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Boote","given":"K.J."},{"family":"Folberth","given":"C."},{"family":"Glotter","given":"M."},{"family":"Khabarov","given":"N."},{"family":"Neumann","given":"K."},{"family":"Piontek","given":"F."},{"family":"Pugh","given":"T.A.M."},{"family":"Schmid","given":"E."},{"family":"Stehfest","given":"E."},{"family":"Yang","given":"H."},{"family":"Jones","given":"J.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":18786,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FNXPNGIX"],"itemData":{"id":18786,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Linked sustainability challenges and trade-offs among fisheries, aquaculture and agriculture 2017 Nature Ecology and Evolution","author":[{"family":"Blanchard","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Watson","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Fulton","given":"E.A."},{"family":"Cottrell","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Nash","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Bryndum-Buchholz","given":"A."},{"family":"Buchner","given":"M."},{"family":"Carozza","given":"D.A."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W.L."},{"family":"Elliott","given":"J."},{"family":"Davidson","given":"L.N.K."},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"N.K."},{"family":"Dunne","given":"J.P."},{"family":"Eddy","given":"T.D."},{"family":"Galbraith","given":"E."},{"family":"Lotze","given":"H.K."},{"family":"Maury","given":"O."},{"family":"Muller","given":"C."},{"family":"Tittensor","given":"D.P."},{"family":"Jenning","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Demographic pressures such as high population growth both globally and in individual regions, conflicts in sea use, and land use practices that degrade marine habitats may further aggravate climate impacts and amplify fisheries overexploitation, biodiversity loss and environmental degradation. Given the diversified nature of small-scale fisheries, fishers may be able to shift to exploit less climate-impacted fish species, but this is contingent on fishers’ knowledge, gear and spatial use practices, and the status of alternate fish stocks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HVQLV62I","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bell, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Bell, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19082,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z75HBMJT"],"itemData":{"id":19082,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.05.019","language":"en","page":"303–314","title":"Adaptations to maintain the contributions of small-scale fisheries to food security in the Pacific Islands","volume":"88","author":[{"family":"Bell","given":"J.D."},{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"A."},{"family":"Hanich","given":"Q."},{"family":"Johnson","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Lehodey","given":"P."},{"family":"Moore","given":"B.R."},{"family":"Pratchett","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Reygondeau","given":"G."},{"family":"Senina","given":"I."},{"family":"Virdin","given":"J."},{"family":"Wabnitz","given":"C.C.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Projected demographic and social trends may also affect recreational fisheries. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ObpziH4k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arlinghaus, Tillner, & Bork, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Arlinghaus, Tillner, & Bork, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":10040,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W2Q6QY4P"],"itemData":{"id":10040,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/fme.12075","ISSN":"0969997X","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Manag Ecol","language":"en","page":"45-55","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Explaining participation rates in recreational fishing across industrialised countries","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"R."},{"family":"Tillner","given":"R."},{"family":"Bork","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} argued that urbanization reduces individuals’ exposure to traditional rural recreational activities like fishing, which may lead to reduced participation rates. However, human population growth also could maintain or even increase absolute levels of recreational fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2k67892k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. L. Hunt, 2017)","plainCitation":"(T. L. Hunt, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20145,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KGXYWGJB"],"itemData":{"id":20145,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2016.09.003","ISSN":"01657836","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Research","language":"en","page":"468-477","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Assessing the cost-effectiveness of a fish stocking program in a culture-based recreational fishery","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Hunt","given":"Taylor L."},{"family":"Scarborough","given":"Helen"},{"family":"Giri","given":"Khageswor"},{"family":"Douglas","given":"John W."},{"family":"Jones","given":"Paul"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      Future socio-economic conditions will also influence the fleet behavior of large-scale fisheries. The application of a scenario planning approach for the Indian Ocean tuna purse seine fishery identified some critical aspects of fleet dynamics to take into account for future management interventions, such as a switch in fishing practices, a reallocation of effort in space, or an exit from the fishery ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aZs9azHy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Davies, Mees, & E.J., 2015)","plainCitation":"(Davies, Mees, & E.J., 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19010,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BRTJJM8E"],"itemData":{"id":19010,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Milner-Gullan Second-guessing uncertainty: Scenario planning for management of the Indian Ocean tuna purse seine fishery","author":[{"family":"Davies","given":"Tim K."},{"family":"Mees","given":"Chris C."},{"literal":"E.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      On the other hand, the emergence of social responsibility principles that adhere to a human rights-based approach to management in recent policy discourse could steer fisheries development along a fairer path. Such a path would enable vulnerable groups such as small-scale fishers and indigenous communities to continue accessing their resource base and the significant benefits that fisheries provide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6P47jW1i","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Teh, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Teh, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19083,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5AJ6ZLVL"],"itemData":{"id":19083,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0210241","language":"en","page":"0210241","title":"The role of human rights in implementing socially responsible seafood","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Teh","given":"L.C.L."},{"family":"Caddell","given":"R."},{"family":"Allison","given":"E.H."},{"family":"Finkbeiner","given":"E.M."},{"family":"Kittinger","given":"J.N."},{"family":"Nakamura","given":"K."},{"family":"Ota","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Projections of both fish production and per capita consumption by 2050 under 3 different scenarios are indicated in Table 5.3. It is notable that production from aquaculture will substantially surpass capture fisheries in all scenarios. This might attract more interest in aquaculture than in fishing with implications in policy and management shifts, e.g. a diminishing importance of fisheries management with reduced investment which would severely affect sustainable use.
Table 5.3. Projection of production and per capita consumption of fish under 3 different scenarios. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XDXWEeEQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"\\\\uldash{(United Nations Nutrition, 2021)}","plainCitation":"(United Nations Nutrition, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":23280,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LXE4254L"],"itemData":{"id":23280,"type":"document","title":"The role of aquatic foods in sustainable healthy diets. Roma: UN Nutrition Secretariat, 2021.","author":[{"family":"United Nations Nutrition","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

      It is pertinent to note that projections indicate that increasing fish yield reduces land and water use by up to half, and optimizing gears reduces capture fishery emissions by more than half for some species groups, which highlights opportunities to improve environmental performance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FM5rwBHk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gephart, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Gephart, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19108,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9FAQ9IV"],"itemData":{"id":19108,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-021-03889-2","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7876","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"360-365","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Environmental performance of blue foods","volume":"597","author":[{"family":"Gephart","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Henriksson","given":"Patrik J. G."},{"family":"Parker","given":"Robert W. R."},{"family":"Shepon","given":"Alon"},{"family":"Gorospe","given":"Kelvin D."},{"family":"Bergman","given":"Kristina"},{"family":"Eshel","given":"Gidon"},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."},{"family":"Hornborg","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Jonell","given":"Malin"},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"},{"family":"Mifflin","given":"Kathleen"},{"family":"Newton","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Tyedmers","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Wenbo"},{"family":"Ziegler","given":"Friederike"},{"family":"Troell","given":"Max"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,16]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.With regard to demand and supply scenarios, projections showed that edible food from the sea could increase by 21-44 million tons by 2050, a 36–74% increase compared to current yields ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mKulRkQo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Costello, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Costello, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19291,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4K83HCRT"],"itemData":{"id":19291,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-020-2616-y","language":"en","page":"95–100","title":"The future of food from the sea","volume":"588","author":[{"family":"Costello","given":"C."},{"family":"Cao","given":"L."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Cisneros-Mata","given":"M.Á."},{"family":"Free","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Froehlich","given":"H.E."},{"family":"Golden","given":"C.D."},{"family":"Ishimura","given":"G."},{"family":"Maier","given":"J."},{"family":"Macadam-Somer","given":"I."},{"family":"Mangin","given":"T."},{"family":"Melnychuk","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Miyahara","given":"M."},{"family":"Moor","given":"C.L."},{"family":"Naylor","given":"R."},{"family":"Nøstbakken","given":"L."},{"family":"Ojea","given":"E."},{"family":"O’Reilly","given":"E."},{"family":"Parma","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Plantinga","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"S.H."},{"family":"Lubchenco","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The social benefits of small-scale fisheries (SSF) are broader than economic value alone. SSF are important for food and nutrition security, and globalization can force trade-offs between economic gains from distant markets and a reduction in nutritional benefits to local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"O6lmQ3ox","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Short, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Short, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19110,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L4ZARGMQ"],"itemData":{"id":19110,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Food","DOI":"10.1038/s43016-021-00363-0","ISSN":"2662-1355","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Food","language":"en","page":"733-741","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Harnessing the diversity of small-scale actors is key to the future of aquatic food systems","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Short","given":"Rebecca E."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Little","given":"David C."},{"family":"Micheli","given":"Fiorenza"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Belton","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Brugere","given":"Cecile"},{"family":"Bush","given":"Simon R."},{"family":"Cao","given":"Ling"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Cohen","given":"Philippa J."},{"family":"Defeo","given":"Omar"},{"family":"Edwards","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Ferguson","given":"Caroline E."},{"family":"Franz","given":"Nicole"},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."},{"family":"Hazen","given":"Lucie"},{"family":"Hicks","given":"Christina"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Derek"},{"family":"Kaminski","given":"Alexander M."},{"family":"Mangubhai","given":"Sangeeta"},{"family":"Naylor","given":"Rosamond L."},{"family":"Reantaso","given":"Melba"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U. Rashid"},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"Shakuntala H."},{"family":"Tigchelaar","given":"Michelle"},{"family":"Wabnitz","given":"Colette C. C."},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Wenbo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Maintaining and expanding the diversity and flexibility of SSF and addressing possible unintended consequences will be crucial. Characteristics such as gender (but also class, education, identity, etc) strongly affect the experiences of different SSF participants (including women in post-harvest and trading), and this should be recognized in terms of future projections and scenarios, particularly for consequences for local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jyxahbwy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Short, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Short, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19110,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L4ZARGMQ"],"itemData":{"id":19110,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Food","DOI":"10.1038/s43016-021-00363-0","ISSN":"2662-1355","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Food","language":"en","page":"733-741","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Harnessing the diversity of small-scale actors is key to the future of aquatic food systems","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Short","given":"Rebecca E."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Little","given":"David C."},{"family":"Micheli","given":"Fiorenza"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Belton","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Brugere","given":"Cecile"},{"family":"Bush","given":"Simon R."},{"family":"Cao","given":"Ling"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Cohen","given":"Philippa J."},{"family":"Defeo","given":"Omar"},{"family":"Edwards","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Ferguson","given":"Caroline E."},{"family":"Franz","given":"Nicole"},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."},{"family":"Hazen","given":"Lucie"},{"family":"Hicks","given":"Christina"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Derek"},{"family":"Kaminski","given":"Alexander M."},{"family":"Mangubhai","given":"Sangeeta"},{"family":"Naylor","given":"Rosamond L."},{"family":"Reantaso","given":"Melba"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U. Rashid"},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"Shakuntala H."},{"family":"Tigchelaar","given":"Michelle"},{"family":"Wabnitz","given":"Colette C. C."},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Wenbo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
5.4.2.3 Technological
Technological advances have been identified as a key aspect affecting the economic viability of fisheries, and need to be incorporated into scenarios and storylines ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KCW4MZAo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Maury, Campling, Arrizabalaga, Aumont, Bopp, Merino,, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Maury, Campling, Arrizabalaga, Aumont, Bopp, Merino,, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20146,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6ADN3K8G"],"itemData":{"id":20146,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2017.06.007","ISSN":"09593780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"203-216","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"From shared socio-economic pathways (SSPs) to oceanic system pathways (OSPs): Building policy-relevant scenarios for global oceanic ecosystems and fisheries","title-short":"From shared socio-economic pathways (SSPs) to oceanic system pathways (OSPs)","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Maury","given":"O."},{"family":"Campling","given":"L."},{"family":"Arrizabalaga","given":"H."},{"family":"Aumont","given":"O."},{"family":"Bopp","given":"L."},{"family":"Merino","given":"G."},{"family":"Squires","given":"D."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W."},{"family":"Goujon","given":"M."},{"family":"Guivarch","given":"C."},{"family":"Lefort","given":"S."},{"family":"Marsac","given":"F."},{"family":"Monteagudo","given":"P."},{"family":"Murtugudde","given":"R."},{"family":"Österblom","given":"H."},{"family":"Pulvenis","given":"J.F."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Y."},{"family":"Ruijven","given":"B.J.","non-dropping-particle":"van"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Broadly speaking, this includes technologies that lead to an increased ability to find fish and reduce bycatch or catch of undersized fish, improvements in gear design and processing capacity, and so forth. ‘Technological creep’ has been identified as increasing catchability by around 2-4% per year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5L8ZRmV4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Palomares & Pauly, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Palomares & Pauly, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1333,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EFGHQL7E"],"itemData":{"id":1333,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Palomares, M. L. D., and D. Pauly. 2019. On the creeping increase of vessels’ fishing power. Ecology and Society 24(3):31. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-11136-240331","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-11136-240331","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"publisher: The Resilience Alliance","source":"www.ecologyandsociety.org","title":"On the creeping increase of vessels’ fishing power","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol24/iss3/art31/","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Palomares","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",9,16]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} , a trend which is likely to continue. However, future scenarios of the global tuna supply chain suggest a limitation of technical efficiency as a potential countermeasure to reduce the negative effects of increasing demand ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"08X1Snta","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mullon, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Mullon, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19373,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5XRZEP7B"],"itemData":{"id":19373,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Deep Sea Research Part II: Topical Studies in Oceanography","DOI":"10.1016/j.dsr2.2016.08.004","language":"en","page":"251–267","title":"Exploring future scenarios for the global supply chain of tuna","volume":"140","author":[{"family":"Mullon","given":"C."},{"family":"Guillotreau","given":"P."},{"family":"Galbraith","given":"E.D."},{"family":"Fortilus","given":"J."},{"family":"Chaboud","given":"C."},{"family":"Bopp","given":"L."},{"family":"Aumont","given":"O."},{"family":"Kaplan","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Technological advances to reduce environmental impacts may also play a role that should be captured in scenarios and projections. Regarding climate change, reducing fuel use represents the primary stressor improvement opportunity. In this sense, projections  show that increasing stock biomass could reduce fuel use per tonne of fish landed, where a 13% catch increase with 56% of the effort corresponds to a 50% reduction in greenhouse gas emissions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"50VhpD33","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gephart, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Gephart, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19108,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9FAQ9IV"],"itemData":{"id":19108,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-021-03889-2","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7876","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"360-365","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Environmental performance of blue foods","volume":"597","author":[{"family":"Gephart","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Henriksson","given":"Patrik J. G."},{"family":"Parker","given":"Robert W. R."},{"family":"Shepon","given":"Alon"},{"family":"Gorospe","given":"Kelvin D."},{"family":"Bergman","given":"Kristina"},{"family":"Eshel","given":"Gidon"},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."},{"family":"Hornborg","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Jonell","given":"Malin"},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"},{"family":"Mifflin","given":"Kathleen"},{"family":"Newton","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Tyedmers","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Wenbo"},{"family":"Ziegler","given":"Friederike"},{"family":"Troell","given":"Max"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,16]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Alternatively, prioritizing low-fuel gears within each fishery could reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 4–61%, depending on the species. In some cases, this could create co-benefits for biodiversity impacts. Another important strategy is to transition fishing fleets to low-emission technologies  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Fg14NTXl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gephart, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Gephart, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19108,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9FAQ9IV"],"itemData":{"id":19108,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-021-03889-2","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7876","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"360-365","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Environmental performance of blue foods","volume":"597","author":[{"family":"Gephart","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Henriksson","given":"Patrik J. G."},{"family":"Parker","given":"Robert W. R."},{"family":"Shepon","given":"Alon"},{"family":"Gorospe","given":"Kelvin D."},{"family":"Bergman","given":"Kristina"},{"family":"Eshel","given":"Gidon"},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."},{"family":"Hornborg","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Jonell","given":"Malin"},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"},{"family":"Mifflin","given":"Kathleen"},{"family":"Newton","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Tyedmers","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Wenbo"},{"family":"Ziegler","given":"Friederike"},{"family":"Troell","given":"Max"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,16]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
5.4.2.4 Economic
Fish is among the most traded food commodities, with about 38% of global fish production entering international trade in 2018 (an export value of US$164 billion), an annual growth rate of 8 percent in nominal terms from 1976 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZJ9GaBf2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5385,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":5385,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. International trade expansion has been facilitated by globalization and rapid improvements in logistics (i.e., transportation, post-harvest handling, processing, preservation, packaging and storage). In addition, population and economic growth drive higher demand for seafood. The average global per capita consumption of marine fish (including shellfish) was approximately 8 kg per annum in 2016, and seafood demand is expected to rise in line with projected growth in national economies and spending power. However, the relationship between per capita fish consumption and gross domestic product per capita is significantly weaker for fish than for terrestrial meat ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mV1jUVpw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Naylor, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Naylor, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19377,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4HZZ4VUG"],"itemData":{"id":19377,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nat Commun","DOI":"10.1038/s41467-021-25516-4","language":"fr","page":"5413","title":"Blue food demand across geographic and temporal scales","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Naylor","given":"R.L."},{"family":"Kishore","given":"A."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U.R."},{"family":"Issifu","given":"I."},{"family":"Hunter","given":"B.P."},{"family":"Belton","given":"B."},{"family":"Bush","given":"S.R."},{"family":"Cao","given":"L."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Gephart","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Golden","given":"C.D."},{"family":"Jonell","given":"M."},{"family":"Koehn","given":"J.Z."},{"family":"Little","given":"D.C."},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"S.H."},{"family":"Tigchelaar","given":"M."},{"family":"Crona","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The interlinkages between social and economic scenarios are considerable. According to ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MhK1zVjG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Naylor, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Naylor, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19377,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4HZZ4VUG"],"itemData":{"id":19377,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nat Commun","DOI":"10.1038/s41467-021-25516-4","language":"fr","page":"5413","title":"Blue food demand across geographic and temporal scales","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Naylor","given":"R.L."},{"family":"Kishore","given":"A."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U.R."},{"family":"Issifu","given":"I."},{"family":"Hunter","given":"B.P."},{"family":"Belton","given":"B."},{"family":"Bush","given":"S.R."},{"family":"Cao","given":"L."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Gephart","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Golden","given":"C.D."},{"family":"Jonell","given":"M."},{"family":"Koehn","given":"J.Z."},{"family":"Little","given":"D.C."},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"S.H."},{"family":"Tigchelaar","given":"M."},{"family":"Crona","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, global fish demand is projected to almost double by mid-century, and will increase in all regions of the world. Asia will continue to lead in freshwater fish consumption and is projected to have the highest demand for fish overall in 2050, with China remaining the world’s largest fish consumer and demand in India greatly increasing. While the individual species consumed by different nations is likely to remain variable, increasing fish consumption is likely to benefit diets in terms of micronutrients ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aeq0OqKB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Golden, 2021; Naylor, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Golden, 2021; Naylor, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19104,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5GZ94WQQ"],"itemData":{"id":19104,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-021-03917-1","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7880","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"315-320","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Aquatic foods to nourish nations","volume":"598","author":[{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Koehn","given":"J. Zachary"},{"family":"Shepon","given":"Alon"},{"family":"Passarelli","given":"Simone"},{"family":"Free","given":"Christopher M."},{"family":"Viana","given":"Daniel F."},{"family":"Matthey","given":"Holger"},{"family":"Eurich","given":"Jacob G."},{"family":"Gephart","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Fluet-Chouinard","given":"Etienne"},{"family":"Nyboer","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Lynch","given":"Abigail J."},{"family":"Kjellevold","given":"Marian"},{"family":"Bromage","given":"Sabri"},{"family":"Charlebois","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Barange","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Vannuccini","given":"Stefania"},{"family":"Cao","given":"Ling"},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin M."},{"family":"Rimm","given":"Eric B."},{"family":"Danaei","given":"Goodarz"},{"family":"DeSisto","given":"Camille"},{"family":"Kelahan","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Fiorella","given":"Kathryn J."},{"family":"Little","given":"David C."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Fanzo","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"Shakuntala H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",10,14]]}}},{"id":19377,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4HZZ4VUG"],"itemData":{"id":19377,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nat Commun","DOI":"10.1038/s41467-021-25516-4","language":"fr","page":"5413","title":"Blue food demand across geographic and temporal scales","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Naylor","given":"R.L."},{"family":"Kishore","given":"A."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U.R."},{"family":"Issifu","given":"I."},{"family":"Hunter","given":"B.P."},{"family":"Belton","given":"B."},{"family":"Bush","given":"S.R."},{"family":"Cao","given":"L."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Gephart","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Golden","given":"C.D."},{"family":"Jonell","given":"M."},{"family":"Koehn","given":"J.Z."},{"family":"Little","given":"D.C."},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"S.H."},{"family":"Tigchelaar","given":"M."},{"family":"Crona","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Estimates show China, Europe, North America and South America consuming a diverse set of species in 2050, including crustaceans, demersal fish, and cephalopods, while Ghana and Peru will continue to dominate the consumption of small pelagic fish.
      Projections of future food systems to 2030 suggest that high levels of growth in aquatic animal-source food production may decrease food prices by up at a quarter, resulting in increased consumption and potentially thus causing reductions in both consumptions of red and processed meats and micronutrient deficiencies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LlSZ5Hpe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Golden, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Golden, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19104,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5GZ94WQQ"],"itemData":{"id":19104,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-021-03917-1","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7880","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"315-320","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Aquatic foods to nourish nations","volume":"598","author":[{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Koehn","given":"J. Zachary"},{"family":"Shepon","given":"Alon"},{"family":"Passarelli","given":"Simone"},{"family":"Free","given":"Christopher M."},{"family":"Viana","given":"Daniel F."},{"family":"Matthey","given":"Holger"},{"family":"Eurich","given":"Jacob G."},{"family":"Gephart","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Fluet-Chouinard","given":"Etienne"},{"family":"Nyboer","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Lynch","given":"Abigail J."},{"family":"Kjellevold","given":"Marian"},{"family":"Bromage","given":"Sabri"},{"family":"Charlebois","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Barange","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Vannuccini","given":"Stefania"},{"family":"Cao","given":"Ling"},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin M."},{"family":"Rimm","given":"Eric B."},{"family":"Danaei","given":"Goodarz"},{"family":"DeSisto","given":"Camille"},{"family":"Kelahan","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Fiorella","given":"Kathryn J."},{"family":"Little","given":"David C."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Fanzo","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"Shakuntala H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",10,14]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The operating cost of global fisheries was approximately US$73 billion in the mid-2000s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2Bb7tFxp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V.W.Y. Lam, Sumaila, Dyck, Pauly, & Watson, 2011)","plainCitation":"(V.W.Y. Lam, Sumaila, Dyck, Pauly, & Watson, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4IRRNIVU"],"itemData":{"id":19087,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsr121","language":"en","page":"1996–2004","title":"Construction and first applications of a global cost of fishing database","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Lam","given":"V.W.Y."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U.R."},{"family":"Dyck","given":"A."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."},{"family":"Watson","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fishing costs and cost structures vary widely by type of fishery and country. For example, global estimates of operating and total cost associated with catching a ton of fish using nets typical of small-scale coastal fisheries averaged US$180 and US$241 respectively (2005 values), while costs of off-shore fishing for tuna using longlines were US$2,604 and US$2,903 respectively ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4ZMxN0Ka","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V.W.Y. Lam, Sumaila, Dyck, Pauly, & Watson, 2011)","plainCitation":"(V.W.Y. Lam, Sumaila, Dyck, Pauly, & Watson, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4IRRNIVU"],"itemData":{"id":19087,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsr121","language":"en","page":"1996–2004","title":"Construction and first applications of a global cost of fishing database","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Lam","given":"V.W.Y."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U.R."},{"family":"Dyck","given":"A."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."},{"family":"Watson","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. On the other hand, the financial subsidies given to industrial fleets - even to those causing overfishing - are key elements for future scenarios and are very much aligned with the need for a reduction of overcapacity. Indeed, the analysis of global marine fisheries subsidies revealed that almost 90% of capacity enhancing subsidies (US$22.2 billion) are provided to large scale industrialized fisheries, which impair the viability of small-scale fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JMhsedUJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schuhbauer, Chuenpagdee, Cheung, Greer, & Sumaila, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Schuhbauer, Chuenpagdee, Cheung, Greer, & Sumaila, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19088,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RI3KZBS7"],"itemData":{"id":19088,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.05.013","language":"en","page":"114–121","title":"How subsidies affect the economic viability of small-scale fisheries","volume":"82","author":[{"family":"Schuhbauer","given":"A."},{"family":"Chuenpagdee","given":"R."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W.L."},{"family":"Greer","given":"K."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition to impacts on the level of fishing, harmful subsidies result in increased greenhouse gas emissions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oMO75P7l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Machado, Halmenschlager, Abdallah, Teixeira, & Sumaila, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Machado, Halmenschlager, Abdallah, Teixeira, & Sumaila, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19089,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IDDB9UAS"],"itemData":{"id":19089,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2021.107057","language":"en","page":"107057","title":"The relation between fishing subsidies and CO2 emissions in the fisheries sector","volume":"185","author":[{"family":"Machado","given":"F.L.V."},{"family":"Halmenschlager","given":"V."},{"family":"Abdallah","given":"P.R."},{"family":"Teixeira","given":"G.da S."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} .
      Another ongoing economic consideration is the blue economy initiatives that are making their mark on national and international agendas. In the context of fisheries, blue growth policies lean towards a rights-based approach to fisheries management, which aim to achieve economic efficiency in resource exploitation by defining exclusive ownership or access to fisheries resources. However, this conversion of public goods to private goods can potentially lead to inequalities in how stakeholders access and share ocean benefits. Furthermore, climate change may exacerbate disparities between fishing sectors. Simulations of Australian and New Zealand fisheries using the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change Special Report on Emissions A2 emissions scenario and moderate global economic growth revealed a relative increase in the value of large-scale commercial fisheries by 90% but decreases in small-scale and recreational fisheries of between 30% and 50% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GJSCjRi0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fulton, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Fulton, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19090,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XAETJ58G"],"itemData":{"id":19090,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsr032","language":"en","page":"1329–1342","title":"Interesting times: winners, losers, and system shifts under climate change around Australia","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Fulton","given":"E.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Small-scale fishers often engage in alternative economic activities to supplement their income, but these too may become threatened by climate change, thereby limiting small-scale fishers’ livelihood options and perpetuating pressure on fisheries. 
      Allocating a relatively small amount of time to fishing can make a notable contribution to livelihoods with modest investment and minimal exposure to risks. However fishing strategies such as damming channels, applying destructive fishing methods, or using fine mesh nets could threaten future fish stocks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a146fir1uls","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bailey & Sumaila, 2015; Short, 2019; Sugden, & Punch, 2011; Sumaila, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Bailey & Sumaila, 2015; Short, 2019; Sugden, & Punch, 2011; Sumaila, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":23627,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KW4M37VY"],"itemData":{"id":23627,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Ecology Progress Series","DOI":"10.3354/meps11352","ISSN":"0171-8630, 1616-1599","journalAbbreviation":"Mar. Ecol. Prog. Ser.","language":"en","page":"195-211","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Destructive fishing and fisheries enforcement in eastern Indonesia","volume":"530","author":[{"family":"Bailey","given":"M"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"Ur"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",6,18]]}}},{"id":19091,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TWZYINVL"],"itemData":{"id":19091,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Achieving Net Benefits: A Road Map for Cross-sectoral Policy Development in Response to the Unintended Use of Mosquito Nets as Fishing Gear","URL":"https://osf.io/preprints/socarxiv/2g7vb/","author":[{"family":"Short","given":"R.E."},{"family":"Addison","given":"P."},{"family":"Hill","given":"N."},{"family":"Arlidge","given":"W."},{"family":"Berthe","given":"S."},{"literal":"Castello"},{"family":"Tickell","given":"S."},{"family":"Coulthard","given":"S."},{"family":"Lorenz","given":"L."},{"family":"Sibanda","given":"M."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":23629,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3G6IPVVT"],"itemData":{"id":23629,"type":"document","title":"Highland aquatic resources conservation and sustainable development: overview report on livelihoods and aquatic resource use in upland India, Vietnam and China. Vietnam and China Stirling: University of Stirling.\\"","author":[{"family":"Sugden,","given":"F."},{"family":"Punch","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":210,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2VIDK9QJ"],"itemData":{"id":210,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.abm1680","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6567","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"544-544","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"WTO must ban harmful fisheries subsidies","volume":"374","author":[{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U. Rashid"},{"family":"Skerritt","given":"Daniel J."},{"family":"Schuhbauer","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Villasante","given":"Sebastian"},{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"Andrés M."},{"family":"Sinan","given":"Hussain"},{"family":"Burnside","given":"Duncan"},{"family":"Abdallah","given":"Patrízia Raggi"},{"family":"Abe","given":"Keita"},{"family":"Addo","given":"Kwasi A."},{"family":"Adelsheim","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Adewumi","given":"Ibukun J."},{"family":"Adeyemo","given":"Olanike K."},{"family":"Adger","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Adotey","given":"Joshua"},{"family":"Advani","given":"Sahir"},{"family":"Afrin","given":"Zahidah"},{"family":"Aheto","given":"Denis"},{"family":"Akintola","given":"Shehu L."},{"family":"Akpalu","given":"Wisdom"},{"family":"Alam","given":"Lubna"},{"family":"Alava","given":"Juan José"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Amon","given":"Diva J."},{"family":"Anderies","given":"John M."},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Christopher M."},{"family":"Andrews","given":"Evan"},{"family":"Angelini","given":"Ronaldo"},{"family":"Anna","given":"Zuzy"},{"family":"Antweiler","given":"Werner"},{"family":"Arizi","given":"Evans K."},{"family":"Armitage","given":"Derek"},{"family":"Arthur"