ley","given":"Frances"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Stella"},{"family":"Wisz","given":"Mary S."},{"family":"Worm","given":"Boris"},{"family":"Xiao","given":"Lan"},{"family":"Yagi","given":"Nobuyuki"},{"family":"Yamazaki","given":"Satoshi"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Hong"},{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",10,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, while the global efforts to reduce illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing in the high-seas will require investments in surveillance and international coordination. These economic factors will also shape future scenarios.
5.4.2.5 Environmental
Climate is a key factor in biophysical, chemical and ecological changes that regulate the distribution of fish species, their abundance, physical condition, and their use of habitat. In a future with high greenhouse gas emissions (RCP8.5), marine species in the Atlantic and Pacific oceans are projected to generally shift poleward following the coastline, with many species shifting more than 1,000km ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"j8SJUoaO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Morley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Morley, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19114,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/88VAKSZE"],"itemData":{"id":19114,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0196127","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e0196127","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Projecting shifts in thermal habitat for 686 species on the North American continental shelf","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Morley","given":"James W."},{"family":"Selden","given":"Rebecca L."},{"family":"Latour","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Frölicher","given":"Thomas L."},{"family":"Seagraves","given":"Richard J."},{"family":"Pinsky","given":"Malin L."}],"editor":[{"family":"MacKenzie","given":"Brian R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",5,16]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fisheries management will have to be anticipatory rather than responsive to predicted climate impacts on marine ecosystems, in order to ensure that use remains sustainable as ecosystems change. Indeed, climate change impacts might affect exploitation reference points and the associated level of catch ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FX3nbsM7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Travers-Trolet, Bourdaud, Genu, Velez, & Vermard, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Travers-Trolet, Bourdaud, Genu, Velez, & Vermard, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19432,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GPRSDXNK"],"itemData":{"id":19432,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Front. Mar. Sci","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2020.568232","language":"en","page":"568232","title":"The Risky Decrease of Fishing Reference Points Under Climate Change","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Travers-Trolet","given":"M."},{"family":"Bourdaud","given":"P."},{"family":"Genu","given":"M."},{"family":"Velez","given":"L."},{"family":"Vermard","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Yet uncertainties over adaptation and evolutionary processes in marine organisms and the temporal scale at which they occur, the influence of climate change on life history traits, impacts of extreme events (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a7kciaart9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"\\\\uldash{(Babcock, 2019)}","plainCitation":"(Babcock, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20152,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YT6AU7U7"],"itemData":{"id":20152,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2019.00411","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","page":"411","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Severe Continental-Scale Impacts of Climate Change Are Happening Now: Extreme Climate Events Impact Marine Habitat Forming Communities Along 45% of Australia’s Coast","title-short":"Severe Continental-Scale Impacts of Climate Change Are Happening Now","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Babcock","given":"Russell C."},{"family":"Bustamante","given":"Rodrigo H."},{"family":"Fulton","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Fulton","given":"Derek J."},{"family":"Haywood","given":"Michael D. E."},{"family":"Hobday","given":"Alistair James"},{"family":"Kenyon","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Matear","given":"Richard James"},{"family":"Plagányi","given":"Eva E."},{"family":"Richardson","given":"Anthony J."},{"family":"Vanderklift","given":"Mathew A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and morphological constraints that limit certain species’ response to environmental change, may reduce the effectiveness of climate mitigating measures. For example, even in the absence of fishing, climate change has been projected to decrease marine animal biomass (which underlies wild capture fisheries) by around 5% for every one degree of warming ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"o9J3D92E","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lotze, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Lotze, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4665,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NNNVNWGC"],"itemData":{"id":4665,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanica Marina","DOI":"10.1515/bot-2019-0027","ISSN":"0006-8055","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Bot Mar","language":"English","page":"395-409","title":"Ecosystem-based management of seaweed harvesting","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Lotze","given":"H. K."},{"family":"Milewski","given":"I."},{"family":"Fast","given":"J."},{"family":"Kay","given":"L."},{"family":"Worm","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and historical modeling supports an impact of warming on stock biomass, though the impacts on individual species vary ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QuIrGPsC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Free, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Free, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":16369,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YBILDXFV"],"itemData":{"id":16369,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"6430","language":"en","page":"979–983","title":"Impacts of historical warming on marine fisheries production","volume":"363","author":[{"family":"Free","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Thorson","given":"J.T."},{"family":"Pinsky","given":"M.L."},{"family":"Oken","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Wiedenmann","given":"J."},{"family":"Jensen","given":"O.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, effective management, including transboundary management, can help to offset these impacts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ah3b6hbqfc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gaines, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Gaines, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20154,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/34KSJD8D"],"itemData":{"id":20154,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Future effects of climate change on ocean fisheries could be more than offset by management reforms for current fisheries.\\n          , \\n            The world’s oceans supply food and livelihood to billions of people, yet species’ shifting geographic ranges and changes in productivity arising from climate change are expected to profoundly affect these benefits. We ask how improvements in fishery management can offset the negative consequences of climate change; we find that the answer hinges on the current status of stocks. The poor current status of many stocks combined with potentially maladaptive responses to range shifts could reduce future global fisheries yields and profits even more severely than previous estimates have suggested. However, reforming fisheries in ways that jointly fix current inefficiencies, adapt to fisheries productivity changes, and proactively create effective transboundary institutions could lead to a future with higher profits and yields compared to what is produced today.","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.aao1378","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Sci. Adv.","language":"en","page":"eaao1378","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Improved fisheries management could offset many negative effects of climate change","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Gaines","given":"Steven D."},{"family":"Costello","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Owashi","given":"Brandon"},{"family":"Mangin","given":"Tracey"},{"family":"Bone","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Molinos","given":"Jorge García"},{"family":"Burden","given":"Merrick"},{"family":"Dennis","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."},{"family":"Kappel","given":"Carrie V."},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin M."},{"family":"Ovando","given":"Daniel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",8,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, emphasizing the crucial importance of governance structures ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Wfx7heEe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Free, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Free, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":16369,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YBILDXFV"],"itemData":{"id":16369,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"6430","language":"en","page":"979–983","title":"Impacts of historical warming on marine fisheries production","volume":"363","author":[{"family":"Free","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Thorson","given":"J.T."},{"family":"Pinsky","given":"M.L."},{"family":"Oken","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Wiedenmann","given":"J."},{"family":"Jensen","given":"O.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Despite these projections many uncertainties abound. Surprises may also emerge as the future veers into environmental conditions that have not been previously experienced. For example, climatic changes may increase some species’ susceptibility to disease and has the potential to cause unforeseen collapse in fisheries. Climate impacts on fisheries will be felt unevenly, with the tropics predicted to bear the brunt of losses in fish catch potential and fisheries revenues. On the other hand, climate change may open up the potential for new Arctic marine fisheries through increased access to fish stocks and increased catch potential ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1c4eui757c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Burgass, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Burgass, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20156,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U7MRF8D7"],"itemData":{"id":20156,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10113-018-1395-6","ISSN":"1436-3798, 1436-378X","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Reg Environ Change","language":"en","page":"293-308","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A pan-Arctic assessment of the status of marine social-ecological systems","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Burgass","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E. J."},{"family":"Stewart Lowndes","given":"Julia S."},{"family":"O’Hara","given":"Casey"},{"family":"Afflerbach","given":"Jamie C."},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      To give a specific example of an important commercial taxon, most of the 14 distinct species of tuna from 4 main genera (Auxis, Euthynnus, Katsuwonus and Thunnus) are commercially harvested. Tuna have high economic value, representing about 9% of the internationally traded fish and fishery products in terms of value in 2018 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cQMn4qhS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5385,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":5385,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Climate change will affect the phenology, physiology, biology and ecology of tuna and the ecosystems within which they exist, and the impacts will vary across species, life history stage and population/region. The outcomes of climate change on tuna will have knock-on effects on the distribution, composition and timing of tuna catches worldwide. 
      Rising water temperature impacts tuna survival by affecting habitat suitability for tuna species at different life stages. By 2100, climate change projections suggest that Western Central Pacific water temperatures will be too warm for T. obesus to spawn, while temperatures will be optimal in subtropical latitudes and the Eastern Tropical Pacific ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zZRkjbZD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lehodey, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Lehodey, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19093,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SS4HCQHG"],"itemData":{"id":19093,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Progress in Oceanography, CLimate Impacts on Oceanic TOp Predators (CLIOTOP","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1016/j.pocean.2010.04.021","page":"302–315","title":"Preliminary forecasts of Pacific bigeye tuna population trends under the A2 IPCC scenario","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pocean.2010.04.021","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Lehodey","given":"P."},{"family":"Senina","given":"I."},{"family":"Sibert","given":"J."},{"family":"Bopp","given":"L."},{"family":"Calmettes","given":"B."},{"family":"Hampton","given":"J."},{"family":"Murtugudde","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Rising temperatures will lead to an expansion of favorable habitat for adult skipjack tuna (Katsuwonus pelamis) throughout the tropics; however more recent estimates indicate a deterioration of tropical habitats and an improvement of habitat at higher latitudes. Nonetheless, there is agreement that rising temperatures will drive moderate increases in skipjack tuna catch and biomass until 2050. On the other hand, under current fishing pressure the population of albacore tuna (Thunnus alalonga) in the South Pacific is predicted to keep declining until 2035 when they may begin to stabilize. By 2080, new spawning grounds are predicted to emerge in the North Tasman Sea, helping reverse some of the decline ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rDgSfBZa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lehodey, Senina, Nicol, & Hampton, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Lehodey, Senina, Nicol, & Hampton, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19094,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NZBVB5EP"],"itemData":{"id":19094,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Impacts of climate on marine top predators","DOI":"10.1016/j.dsr2.2014.10.028","language":"en","page":"246–259","title":"Modelling the impact of climate change on South Pacific albacore tuna. Deep Sea Research Part II: Topical Studies in Oceanography","volume":"113","author":[{"family":"Lehodey","given":"P."},{"family":"Senina","given":"I."},{"family":"Nicol","given":"S."},{"family":"Hampton","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Small pelagic fish are extremely abundant and support large capture fisheries for human consumption, aquaculture feed, and fish oil. Between 2005 and 2014, 16.2 million tons of small pelagic fishes were caught each year, representing 20% of the global catch of all fish species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1ON0EkMz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2018)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2631,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EXBGI93X"],"itemData":{"id":2631,"type":"book","collection-title":"FAO yearbook","event-place":"Rome","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"Fishery and Aquaculture Statistics 2016","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Small pelagic fishes exhibit natural, multidecadal fluctuations in abundance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a197b8iiev8","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"\\\\uldash{(Soutar & Isaacs, 1969; Soutar, 1974; Baumgartner, T.R., Soutar, A. and Ferreira-Bartrina, V., 1992; McClatchie, 2017)}","plainCitation":"(Soutar & Isaacs, 1969; Soutar, 1974; Baumgartner, T.R., Soutar, A. and Ferreira-Bartrina, V., 1992; McClatchie, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20158,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2GZ39HC9"],"itemData":{"id":20158,"type":"article-newspaper","title":"History of fish populations inferred from fish scales in anaerobic sediments off California. Calif. Coop. Oceanic Fish. Invest. Rep. 13:63-70.","author":[{"family":"Soutar","given":"A"},{"family":"Isaacs","given":"J. D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1969"]]}}},{"id":20159,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4SYDNPW8"],"itemData":{"id":20159,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fishery Bulletin","page":"257–273","title":"Abundance of pelagic fish during the 19th and 20th centuries as recorded in anaerobic sediment off the Californias","author":[{"family":"Soutar","given":"Andrew"},{"literal":"John"},{"family":"Isaacs","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1974"]]}}},{"id":20160,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C2UBSYD8"],"itemData":{"id":20160,"type":"article-newspaper","title":"Reconstruction of the History of Pacific Sardine and Northern Anchovy Populations over the Last Two Millennia from Sediments of the Santa Barbara Basin, California. CalCOFI Reports, 33, 24-40.","author":[{"family":"Baumgartner, T.R., Soutar, A. and Ferreira-Bartrina, V.","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}},{"id":20161,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/72ETRVNZ"],"itemData":{"id":20161,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geophysical Research Letters","DOI":"10.1002/2016GL071751","ISSN":"0094-8276, 1944-8007","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Geophys. Res. Lett.","language":"en","page":"1877-1885","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Collapse and recovery of forage fish populations prior to commercial exploitation","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"McClatchie","given":"S."},{"family":"Hendy","given":"I. L."},{"family":"Thompson","given":"A. R."},{"family":"Watson","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2,28]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} that have led to rapid and dramatic fluctuations in industrial and small-scale fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1pq294n30f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chavez, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Chavez, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20163,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TNYTLW4U"],"itemData":{"id":20163,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In the Pacific Ocean, air and ocean temperatures, atmospheric carbon dioxide, landings of anchovies and sardines, and the productivity of coastal and open ocean ecosystems have varied over periods of about 50 years. In the mid-1970s, the Pacific changed from a cool “anchovy regime” to a warm “sardine regime.” A shift back to an anchovy regime occurred in the middle to late 1990s. These large-scale, naturally occurring variations must be taken into account when considering human-induced climate change and the management of ocean living resources.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1075880","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5604","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"217-221","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"From Anchovies to Sardines and Back: Multidecadal Change in the Pacific Ocean","title-short":"From Anchovies to Sardines and Back","volume":"299","author":[{"family":"Chavez","given":"Francisco P."},{"family":"Ryan","given":"John"},{"family":"Lluch-Cota","given":"Salvador E."},{"family":"Ñiquen C.","given":"Miguel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",1,10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Due to the observed occurrence of these fluctuations prior to large-scale exploitation, these changes in small pelagic fish biomass are usually attributed to variations in oceanic climate rather than overexploitation. Small pelagic fishes typically respond to warming water temperatures by undergoing poleward distribution shifts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a116inqlvvd","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Beare, 2004; Hsieh, 2008; Nye, 2009; Kanamori, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Beare, 2004; Hsieh, 2008; Nye, 2009; Kanamori, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20164,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B9II6NCF"],"itemData":{"id":20164,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1529-8817.2003.00790.x","ISSN":"13541013","issue":"7","language":"en","page":"1209-1213","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An increase in the abundance of anchovies and sardines in the north-western North Sea since 1995: ABUNDANCE OF ANCHOVIES AND SARDINES IN NORTH SEA","title-short":"An increase in the abundance of anchovies and sardines in the north-western North Sea since 1995","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Beare","given":"Doug"},{"family":"Burns","given":"Finlay"},{"family":"Jones","given":"Emma"},{"family":"Peach","given":"Kevin"},{"family":"Portilla","given":"Enrique"},{"family":"Greig","given":"Tony"},{"family":"McKenzie","given":"Eddie"},{"family":"Reid","given":"Dave"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",7]]}}},{"id":20165,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RKRN5PVC"],"itemData":{"id":20165,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The American Naturalist","DOI":"10.1086/524202","ISSN":"0003-0147, 1537-5323","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"The American Naturalist","language":"en","page":"71-80","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Extending Nonlinear Analysis to Short Ecological Time Series","volume":"171","author":[{"family":"Hsieh","given":"Chih?hao"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Christian"},{"family":"Sugihara","given":"George"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",1]]}}},{"id":20166,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YHZJA3X9"],"itemData":{"id":20166,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Ecology Progress Series","DOI":"10.3354/meps08220","ISSN":"0171-8630, 1616-1599","journalAbbreviation":"Mar. Ecol. Prog. Ser.","language":"en","page":"111-129","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Changing spatial distribution of fish stocks in relation to climate and population size on the Northeast United States continental shelf","volume":"393","author":[{"family":"Nye","given":"Ja"},{"family":"Link","given":"Js"},{"family":"Hare","given":"Ja"},{"family":"Overholtz","given":"Wj"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",10,30]]}}},{"id":20168,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8KN6UWKN"],"itemData":{"id":20168,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Ecology Progress Series","page":"155–166","title":"Climate change shifts the spawning ground northward and extends the spawning period of chub mackerel in the western North Pacific","volume":"624","author":[{"family":"Kanamori","given":"Yuki"},{"family":"Takasuka","given":"Akinori"},{"family":"Nishijima","given":"Shota"},{"family":"Okamura","given":"Hiroshi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Projections of the spatial distribution of seven of the most harvested small pelagic fish species in Europe suggested that environmental suitability for most of these species may strongly decrease and local extinction are expected under the “Business as usual” (RCP8.5) climate change scenario ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jlHBkBal","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schickele, n.d., p. 2021)","plainCitation":"(Schickele, n.d., p. 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19024,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JW5PJWUG"],"itemData":{"id":19024,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"European small pelagic fish distribution under global change scenarios. 2020 Fish and Fisheries","author":[{"family":"Schickele","given":"A."},{"family":"Goberville","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Leroy","given":"Boris"},{"family":"Beaugrand","given":"Gregory"},{"family":"Hattab","given":"T."},{"family":"Francour","given":"Patrice"},{"family":"Raybaud","given":"Virginie"}]},"locator":"2021"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition to spatio-temporal distribution shifts, changes in the productivity of upwelling ecosystems and plankton species composition under global warming ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a148iepi04j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Marinov, 2010; Rykaczewski, 2015; Rykaczewski & Dunne, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Marinov, 2010; Rykaczewski, 2015; Rykaczewski & Dunne, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20171,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N6PBTKWZ"],"itemData":{"id":20171,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract. The response of ocean phytoplankton community structure to climate change depends, among other factors, upon species competition for nutrients and light, as well as the increase in surface ocean temperature. We propose an analytical framework linking changes in nutrients, temperature and light with changes in phytoplankton growth rates, and we assess our theoretical considerations against model projections (1980–2100) from a global Earth System model. Our proposed \\"critical nutrient hypothesis\\" stipulates the existence of a critical nutrient threshold below (above) which a nutrient change will affect small phytoplankton biomass more (less) than diatom biomass, i.e. the phytoplankton with lower half-saturation coefficient K are influenced more strongly in low nutrient environments. This nutrient threshold broadly corresponds to 45° S and 45° N, poleward of which high vertical mixing and inefficient biology maintain higher surface nutrient concentrations and equatorward of which reduced vertical mixing and more efficient biology maintain lower surface nutrients. In the 45° S–45° N low nutrient region, decreases in limiting nutrients – associated with increased stratification under climate change – are predicted analytically to decrease more strongly the specific growth of small phytoplankton than the growth of diatoms. In high latitudes, the impact of nutrient decrease on phytoplankton biomass is more significant for diatoms than small phytoplankton, and contributes to diatom declines in the northern marginal sea ice and subpolar biomes. In the context of our model, climate driven increases in surface temperature and changes in light are predicted to have a stronger impact on small phytoplankton than on diatom biomass in all ocean domains. Our analytical predictions explain reasonably well the shifts in community structure under a modeled climate-warming scenario. Climate driven changes in nutrients, temperature and light have regionally varying and sometimes counterbalancing impacts on phytoplankton biomass and structure, with nutrients and temperature dominant in the 45° S–45° N band and light-temperature effects dominant in the marginal sea-ice and subpolar regions. As predicted, decreases in nutrients inside the 45° S–45° N \\"critical nutrient\\" band result in diatom biomass decreasing more than small phytoplankton biomass. Further stratification from global warming could result in geographical shifts in the \\"critical nutrient\\" threshold and additional changes in ecology.","container-title":"Biogeosciences","DOI":"10.5194/bg-7-3941-2010","ISSN":"1726-4189","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Biogeosciences","language":"en","page":"3941-3959","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Response of ocean phytoplankton community structure to climate change over the 21st century: partitioning the effects of nutrients, temperature and light","title-short":"Response of ocean phytoplankton community structure to climate change over the 21st century","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Marinov","given":"I."},{"family":"Doney","given":"S. C."},{"family":"Lima","given":"I. D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",12,2]]}}},{"id":20173,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WPG8GKN6"],"itemData":{"id":20173,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geophysical Research Letters","DOI":"10.1002/2015GL064694","ISSN":"00948276","issue":"15","journalAbbreviation":"Geophys. Res. Lett.","language":"en","page":"6424-6431","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Poleward displacement of coastal upwelling-favorable winds in the ocean's eastern boundary currents through the 21st century: UPWELLING RESPONSES TO CLIMATE CHANGE","title-short":"Poleward displacement of coastal upwelling-favorable winds in the ocean's eastern boundary currents through the 21st century","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Rykaczewski","given":"Ryan R."},{"family":"Dunne","given":"John P."},{"family":"Sydeman","given":"William J."},{"family":"García-Reyes","given":"Marisol"},{"family":"Black","given":"Bryan A."},{"family":"Bograd","given":"Steven J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",8,16]]}}},{"id":20169,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J22CCAYD"],"itemData":{"id":20169,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geophysical Research Letters","DOI":"10.1029/2010GL045019","ISSN":"00948276","issue":"21","journalAbbreviation":"Geophys. Res. Lett.","language":"en","page":"n/a-n/a","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Enhanced nutrient supply to the California Current Ecosystem with global warming and increased stratification in an earth system model: FUTURE NUTRIENT SUPPLY TO THE CCE","title-short":"Enhanced nutrient supply to the California Current Ecosystem with global warming and increased stratification in an earth system model","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Rykaczewski","given":"Ryan R."},{"family":"Dunne","given":"John P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} are likely to impact fisheries for small pelagic species. Under a high emissions climate change scenario habitat suitability for sardines in the Gulf of California is projected to decline by as much as 95% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ano7vk5jm6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Petat\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}n\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Ram\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}rez, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Petatán?Ramírez, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20175,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GXL75HRY"],"itemData":{"id":20175,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.14496","ISSN":"0022-1112, 1095-8649","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"1177-1186","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Habitat suitability of <span style=\\"font-variant:small-caps;\\"> <i>Rhincodon typus</i> </span> in three localities of the Gulf of California: Environmental drivers of seasonal aggregations","title-short":"Habitat suitability of <span style=\\"font-variant","volume":"97","author":[{"family":"Petatán?Ramírez","given":"David"},{"family":"Whitehead","given":"Darren A."},{"family":"Guerrero?Izquierdo","given":"Triana"},{"family":"Ojeda?Ruiz","given":"Miguel A."},{"family":"Becerril?García","given":"Edgar E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Further, ocean acidification has been associated with reduced survival of small pelagic fish eggs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1pdrtsi9d4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shen, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Shen, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20176,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YS3ZAEIT"],"itemData":{"id":20176,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Ecology Progress Series","DOI":"10.3354/meps11791","ISSN":"0171-8630, 1616-1599","journalAbbreviation":"Mar. Ecol. Prog. Ser.","language":"en","page":"173-183","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Otolith size and the vestibulo-ocular reflex of larvae of white seabass Atractoscion nobilis at high pCO2","volume":"553","author":[{"family":"Shen","given":"Sg"},{"family":"Chen","given":"F"},{"family":"Schoppik","given":"De"},{"family":"Checkley","given":"Dm"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",7,14]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, while habitat compression can potentially alter the fishes’ mortality rate by increasing spatial overlap between small pelagic species and their predators ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1mmp926cgq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Netburn & Anthony Koslow, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Netburn & Anthony Koslow, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20184,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AUVAKGPV"],"itemData":{"id":20184,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Deep Sea Research Part I: Oceanographic Research Papers","DOI":"10.1016/j.dsr.2015.06.006","ISSN":"09670637","journalAbbreviation":"Deep Sea Research Part I: Oceanographic Research Papers","language":"en","page":"149-158","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Dissolved oxygen as a constraint on daytime deep scattering layer depth in the southern California current ecosystem","volume":"104","author":[{"family":"Netburn","given":"Amanda N."},{"family":"Anthony Koslow","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the Eastern Mediterranean Sea, future scenarios of marine resources showed that alien species invasions may have substantial impacts on fisheries and ecosystems in addition to sea warming ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2zPyaoXH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Corrales, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Corrales, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19095,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z2VCJPQN"],"itemData":{"id":19095,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sci Rep","DOI":"10.1038/s41598-018-32666-x","language":"en","page":"14284","title":"Future scenarios of marine resources and ecosystem conditions in the Eastern Mediterranean under the impacts of fishing, alien species and sea warming","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Corrales","given":"X."},{"family":"Coll","given":"M."},{"family":"Ofir","given":"E."},{"family":"Heymans","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Steenbeek","given":"J."},{"family":"Goren","given":"M."},{"family":"Edelist","given":"D."},{"family":"Gal","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Human use of small pelagic fishes is expected to increase in the future due to both greater demand for aquaculture feed and for fish-based protein due to population growth and projected declines in agricultural productivity under climate change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a48n0fc5og","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Checkley, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Checkley, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20179,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TIN3UPSZ"],"itemData":{"id":20179,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Anchovy and sardine populated productive ocean regions over hundreds of thousands of years under a naturally varying climate, and are now subject to climate change of equal or greater magnitude occurring over decades to centuries. We hypothesize that anchovy and sardine populations are limited in size by the supply of nitrogen from outside their habitats originating from upwelling, mixing, and rivers. Projections of the responses of anchovy and sardine to climate change rely on a range of model types and consideration of the effects of climate on lower trophic levels, the effects of fishing on higher trophic levels, and the traits of these two types of fish. Distribution, phenology, nutrient supply, plankton composition and production, habitat compression, fishing, and acclimation and adaptation may be affected by ocean warming, acidification, deoxygenation, and altered hydrology. Observations of populations and evaluation of model skill are essential to resolve the effects of climate change on these fish.","container-title":"Annual Review of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-marine-122414-033819","ISSN":"1941-1405, 1941-0611","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Mar. Sci.","language":"en","page":"469-493","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Climate, Anchovy, and Sardine","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Checkley","given":"David M."},{"family":"Asch","given":"Rebecca G."},{"family":"Rykaczewski","given":"Ryan R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, improved ecological forecasts that anticipate climate-related fluctuations in fish abundance may aid sustainable exploitation of small pelagic fishes in the future ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1mi3fcu8kk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kaplan, 2017; Tommasi, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Kaplan, 2017; Tommasi, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20181,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QN45AFDZ"],"itemData":{"id":20181,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Modelling","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolmodel.2017.05.007","ISSN":"03043800","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Modelling","language":"en","page":"1-24","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Modeling food web effects of low sardine and anchovy abundance in the California Current","volume":"359","author":[{"family":"Kaplan","given":"Isaac C."},{"family":"Koehn","given":"Laura E."},{"family":"Hodgson","given":"Emma E."},{"family":"Marshall","given":"Kristin N."},{"family":"Essington","given":"Timothy E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",9]]}}},{"id":20182,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2ZFWXNWZ"],"itemData":{"id":20182,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2017.00201","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","page":"201","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Multi-Annual Climate Predictions for Fisheries: An Assessment of Skill of Sea Surface Temperature Forecasts for Large Marine Ecosystems","title-short":"Multi-Annual Climate Predictions for Fisheries","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Tommasi","given":"Desiree"},{"family":"Stock","given":"Charles A."},{"family":"Alexander","given":"Michael A."},{"family":"Yang","given":"Xiaosong"},{"family":"Rosati","given":"Anthony"},{"family":"Vecchi","given":"Gabriel A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",6,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Additionally, trends such as the shift away from fish protein to seaweed in aquaculture could improve sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cEPpTqGS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Emblemsv\\\\uc0\\\\u229{}g, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Emblemsvåg, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19116,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DV9BJ55X"],"itemData":{"id":19116,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            Soy protein concentrate (SPC) is a key ingredient in fish feed and most of it originates from Brazil. However, the Brazilian soy industry has reportedly resulted in significant environmental problems including deforestation. Consequently, new sources for protein are investigated and protein extracted from farmed seaweed is considered an alternative. Therefore, we investigate how seaweed protein product (SPP) can compete against SPC as a protein ingredient for fish feed. The study uses the positioning matrix, cost analyses involving the power law, and uncertainty analysis using Monte Carlo simulations, and key research challenges are identified. The initial finding is that, with the emerging seaweed industry, the cost of producing SPP is too high to be competitive for fish feed applications. To overcome this challenge, two solutions are investigated. First, substantial investments in cultivation and processing infrastructure are needed to accomplish scale, and a break-even scale of 65,000 tonnes is suggested. The second but more promising avenue, preferably in combination with the former, is the extraction of seaweed protein and high-value seaweed components. With mannitol and laminaran as co-products to the SPP, there is a 25–30% probability of a positive bottom line. Researches on extraction processes are therefore a necessity to maximize the extraction of value-added ingredients. Over time, it is expected that the competitive position of SPP will improve due to the upscaling of the volume of production as well as better biorefinery processes.","container-title":"Journal of Applied Phycology","DOI":"10.1007/s10811-020-02234-w","ISSN":"0921-8971, 1573-5176","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Phycol","language":"en","page":"4159-4169","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Strategic considerations for establishing a large-scale seaweed industry based on fish feed application: a Norwegian case study","title-short":"Strategic considerations for establishing a large-scale seaweed industry based on fish feed application","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Emblemsvåg","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Kvadsheim","given":"Nina Pereira"},{"family":"Halfdanarson","given":"Jon"},{"family":"Koesling","given":"Matthias"},{"family":"Nystrand","given":"Bjørn Tore"},{"family":"Sunde","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Rebours","given":"Céline"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Finally, coral reef fish are important for livelihoods and food security in many tropical countries. Climate change impacts on coral reef fishes are varied and difficult to predict, and are influenced by species’ sensitivity to increased temperatures and rising CO2 levels as well as their capacity to adapt to environmental change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"j64rurz6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pratchett, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Pratchett, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19096,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BSH23VWP"],"itemData":{"id":19096,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Rev Fish Biol Fisheries","DOI":"10.1007/s11160-016-9455-9","language":"en","page":"297–316","title":"Effects of climate change on coral grouper (Plectropomus spp.) and possible adaptation options","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Pratchett","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Cameron","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Donelson","given":"J."},{"family":"Evans","given":"L."},{"family":"Frisch","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Hobday","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Hoey","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Marshall","given":"N.A."},{"family":"Messmer","given":"V."},{"family":"Munday","given":"P.L."},{"family":"Pears","given":"R."},{"family":"Pecl","given":"G."},{"family":"Reynolds","given":"A."},{"family":"Scott","given":"M."},{"family":"Tobin","given":"A."},{"family":"Tobin","given":"R."},{"family":"Welch","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Williamson","given":"D.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Research on climate change effects on coral reef fishes has been limited to relatively few species. Recent studies on the sensitivity of commercially valuable coral grouper (P. leopardus) to climate change indicates that sustained increases in ocean temperature will negatively affect the performance and fitness of coral groupers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cZj2xJQY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pratchett, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Pratchett, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19096,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BSH23VWP"],"itemData":{"id":19096,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Rev Fish Biol Fisheries","DOI":"10.1007/s11160-016-9455-9","language":"en","page":"297–316","title":"Effects of climate change on coral grouper (Plectropomus spp.) and possible adaptation options","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Pratchett","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Cameron","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Donelson","given":"J."},{"family":"Evans","given":"L."},{"family":"Frisch","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Hobday","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Hoey","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Marshall","given":"N.A."},{"family":"Messmer","given":"V."},{"family":"Munday","given":"P.L."},{"family":"Pears","given":"R."},{"family":"Pecl","given":"G."},{"family":"Reynolds","given":"A."},{"family":"Scott","given":"M."},{"family":"Tobin","given":"A."},{"family":"Tobin","given":"R."},{"family":"Welch","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Williamson","given":"D.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This will potentially decrease catchability and availability of the fish, and ultimately lead to a drop in coral grouper catches in the tropics, where much of the world’s Plectropomus fisheries occur. Ecosystem models of Caribbean coral reefs show that deoxygenation from warming temperature and rising CO2 levels will lead to a drop in fish biomass and produce negative economic consequences, with the sharpest biomass declines likely to be in some commercially important species such as sharks, snappers, lobsters, shrimps and bivalves ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JGBIvTlv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alva-Basurto & Arias-Gonz\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}lez, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Alva-Basurto & Arias-González, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19166,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YPZDZKBH"],"itemData":{"id":19166,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Modelling","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolmodel.2014.06.014","ISSN":"03043800","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Modelling","language":"en","page":"1-14","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Modelling the effects of climate change on a Caribbean coral reef food web","volume":"289","author":[{"family":"Alva-Basurto","given":"Jorge Christian"},{"family":"Arias-González","given":"Jesús Ernesto"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
5.4.2.6 Political 
Effective fisheries management, combined with broader marine spatial planning efforts and a wider recognition of sustainable small-scale fisheries to food security, will together play a key role in the sustainability of wild capture fisheries into the future. Managing all fisheries to maximize long-term food production would result in 2050 in an increase of 16% of total harvest ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eQ12rSEF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Costello, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Costello, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19291,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4K83HCRT"],"itemData":{"id":19291,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-020-2616-y","language":"en","page":"95–100","title":"The future of food from the sea","volume":"588","author":[{"family":"Costello","given":"C."},{"family":"Cao","given":"L."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Cisneros-Mata","given":"M.Á."},{"family":"Free","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Froehlich","given":"H.E."},{"family":"Golden","given":"C.D."},{"family":"Ishimura","given":"G."},{"family":"Maier","given":"J."},{"family":"Macadam-Somer","given":"I."},{"family":"Mangin","given":"T."},{"family":"Melnychuk","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Miyahara","given":"M."},{"family":"Moor","given":"C.L."},{"family":"Naylor","given":"R."},{"family":"Nøstbakken","given":"L."},{"family":"Ojea","given":"E."},{"family":"O’Reilly","given":"E."},{"family":"Parma","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Plantinga","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"S.H."},{"family":"Lubchenco","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, requiring governance at local, national and inter-regional levels to ensure equity and sustainability. 
      Fisheries face constant change in their social, economic and governance spheres, and drivers in these systems may interact with, amplify, or overshadow climate impacts on fish stocks. It should be noted that data scarcity undermines fishery management, particularly for tropical fisheries, increasing their vulnerabilities.
      Climate-driven impacts on ocean biomass are also likely to widen the socioeconomic equity gaps between nations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2imk4ebj3e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Boyce, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Boyce, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20185,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/USCPWBA7"],"itemData":{"id":20185,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              Future climate impacts and their consequences are increasingly being explored using multi-model ensembles that average across individual model projections. Here we develop a statistical framework that integrates projections from coupled ecosystem and earth-system models to evaluate significance and uncertainty in marine animal biomass changes over the 21\\n              st\\n              century in relation to socioeconomic indicators at national to global scales. Significant biomass changes are projected in 40%–57% of the global ocean, with 68%–84% of these areas exhibiting declining trends under low and high emission scenarios, respectively. Given unabated emissions, maritime nations with poor socioeconomic statuses such as low nutrition, wealth, and ocean health will experience the greatest projected losses. These findings suggest that climate-driven biomass changes will widen existing equity gaps and disproportionally affect populations that contributed least to global CO\\n              2\\n              emissions. However, our analysis also suggests that such deleterious outcomes are largely preventable by achieving negative emissions (RCP 2.6).","container-title":"Nature Communications","DOI":"10.1038/s41467-020-15708-9","ISSN":"2041-1723","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Commun","language":"en","page":"2235","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Future ocean biomass losses may widen socioeconomic equity gaps","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Boyce","given":"Daniel G."},{"family":"Lotze","given":"Heike K."},{"family":"Tittensor","given":"Derek P."},{"family":"Carozza","given":"David A."},{"family":"Worm","given":"Boris"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and interact with impacts on agricultural food sources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HUtYXAhe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Blanchard, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Blanchard, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":18786,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FNXPNGIX"],"itemData":{"id":18786,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Linked sustainability challenges and trade-offs among fisheries, aquaculture and agriculture 2017 Nature Ecology and Evolution","author":[{"family":"Blanchard","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Watson","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Fulton","given":"E.A."},{"family":"Cottrell","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Nash","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Bryndum-Buchholz","given":"A."},{"family":"Buchner","given":"M."},{"family":"Carozza","given":"D.A."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W.L."},{"family":"Elliott","given":"J."},{"family":"Davidson","given":"L.N.K."},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"N.K."},{"family":"Dunne","given":"J.P."},{"family":"Eddy","given":"T.D."},{"family":"Galbraith","given":"E."},{"family":"Lotze","given":"H.K."},{"family":"Maury","given":"O."},{"family":"Muller","given":"C."},{"family":"Tittensor","given":"D.P."},{"family":"Jenning","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These interactions compound the uncertainty associated with predicting how climate impacts will actually play out, particularly at the household and community level.
      Enhanced fisheries management may be able to reduce the negative effects of climate change, or at least reduce the pace at which multiple climate drivers act upon the ocean and buy time for marine socio-ecological systems to adapt ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2photc6eab","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gaines, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Gaines, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20154,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/34KSJD8D"],"itemData":{"id":20154,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Future effects of climate change on ocean fisheries could be more than offset by management reforms for current fisheries.\\n          , \\n            The world’s oceans supply food and livelihood to billions of people, yet species’ shifting geographic ranges and changes in productivity arising from climate change are expected to profoundly affect these benefits. We ask how improvements in fishery management can offset the negative consequences of climate change; we find that the answer hinges on the current status of stocks. The poor current status of many stocks combined with potentially maladaptive responses to range shifts could reduce future global fisheries yields and profits even more severely than previous estimates have suggested. However, reforming fisheries in ways that jointly fix current inefficiencies, adapt to fisheries productivity changes, and proactively create effective transboundary institutions could lead to a future with higher profits and yields compared to what is produced today.","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.aao1378","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Sci. Adv.","language":"en","page":"eaao1378","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Improved fisheries management could offset many negative effects of climate change","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Gaines","given":"Steven D."},{"family":"Costello","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Owashi","given":"Brandon"},{"family":"Mangin","given":"Tracey"},{"family":"Bone","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Molinos","given":"Jorge García"},{"family":"Burden","given":"Merrick"},{"family":"Dennis","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."},{"family":"Kappel","given":"Carrie V."},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin M."},{"family":"Ovando","given":"Daniel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",8,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A better understanding of the relationship between people, their communities and the environment will be required to enhance adaptation planning for communities that are most dependent on climate-impacted fisheries. Transboundary management will also become crucial; geopolitical issues may arise from the redistribution of resources in and out of countries’ jurisdictional areas. Sustainable management of fisheries that straddle the high seas may be able to mitigate climate impacts on fisheries within countries’ exclusive economic zones, but the extent to which this generates benefits to society and biodiversity varies depending on the type of ocean governance that prevails. At the extreme, a full closure of the high seas to fishing would increase the resilience of many commercially important fish stocks to both climate change and fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1nknb7qo4i","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cheung, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Cheung, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20147,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P46488P7"],"itemData":{"id":20147,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Marine benefits of the Paris Agreement\\n            \\n              Keeping recent global agreements to limit temperature increases to 1.5° to 2°C above preindustrial levels will have benefits across terrestrial ecosystems. But what about marine ecosystems? Cheung\\n             \\n              modeled the influence of temperature increases on two key measures of fishery sustainability, catch and species turnover (see the Perspective by Fulton). Limiting temperature increases to 1.5°C substantially improved catch potential and decreased turnover of harvested species. These results provide further support for meeting this important goal.\\n            \\n            \\n              Science\\n              , this issue p.\\n              1591\\n              ; see also p.\\n              1530\\n            \\n          , \\n            Meeting the goal of limiting climate warming should improve fisheries across the globe.\\n          , \\n            Translating the Paris Agreement to limit global warming to 1.5°C above preindustrial level into impact-related targets facilitates communication of the benefits of mitigating climate change to policy-makers and stakeholders. Developing ecologically relevant impact-related targets for marine ecosystem services, such as fisheries, is an important step. Here, we use maximum catch potential and species turnover as climate-risk indicators for fisheries. We project that potential catches will decrease by more than 3 million metric tons per degree Celsius of warming. Species turnover is more than halved when warming is lowered from 3.5° to 1.5°C above the preindustrial level. Regionally, changes in maximum catch potential and species turnover vary across ecosystems, with the biggest risk reduction in the Indo-Pacific and Arctic regions when the Paris Agreement target is achieved.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aag2331","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6319","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"1591-1594","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Large benefits to marine fisheries of meeting the 1.5°C global warming target","volume":"354","author":[{"family":"Cheung","given":"William W. L."},{"family":"Reygondeau","given":"Gabriel"},{"family":"Frölicher","given":"Thomas L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12,23]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Scenarios of global governance, management, and economy (the ‘oceanic system pathways’), including geopolitics and corporate influence, have been developed for oceanic fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pG6Ly6uh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Maury, n.d., p. 2017)","plainCitation":"(Maury, n.d., p. 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19011,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z5QNNEDX"],"itemData":{"id":19011,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Change","title":"From shared socio-economic pathways (SSPs) to oceanic system pathways (OSPs): building policy-relevant scenarios for global oceanic ecosystems and fisheries. 2017 Glob. Environ","author":[{"family":"Maury","given":"O."},{"family":"Campling","given":"L."},{"family":"Arrizabalaga","given":"H."},{"family":"Aumont","given":"O."},{"family":"Bopp","given":"L."},{"family":"Merino","given":"G."}]},"locator":"2017"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, broadly mapping on to the shared socio-economic pathways. However, they have not yet been fully applied in making explicit projections around the sustainability of stocks into the future. A nationalistic outlook where fisheries are propped up by subsidies would cause fishing profits to fall in all countries, as would scenarios characterized by a fossil fueled lifestyle, while ecological productivity would be negatively affected in both scenarios. More stark inequalities may emerge under worsening climate change. High seas fisheries could become increasingly economically viable for high-income countries under high carbon emissions (RCP 8.5) and a rapid economic development model, but the increased fishing intensity could potentially deprive middle and low-income countries of fishing opportunities. With multiple interactions taking place across spatial and temporal scales, outcomes will vary depending on whether they are viewed from ecological, economic, or social perspectives. Furthermore, an exploratory scenario approach based on socio-economic and political trends suggests that overfishing and climate change could increase the likelihood of fishery conflicts in the mid-century ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"atd17no5qi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Spijkers, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Spijkers, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20187,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YGNC82NI"],"itemData":{"id":20187,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"One Earth","DOI":"10.1016/j.oneear.2021.02.004","ISSN":"25903322","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"One Earth","language":"en","page":"386-396","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Exploring the future of fishery conflict through narrative scenarios","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Spijkers","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Merrie","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Wabnitz","given":"Colette C.C."},{"family":"Osborne","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Mobjörk","given":"Malin"},{"family":"Bodin","given":"Örjan"},{"family":"Selig","given":"Elizabeth R."},{"family":"Le Billon","given":"Philippe"},{"family":"Hendrix","given":"Cullen S."},{"family":"Singh","given":"Gerald G."},{"family":"Keys","given":"Patrick W."},{"family":"Morrison","given":"Tiffany H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Marine protected areas for biodiversity conservation, can also provide benefits for food provisioning (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1bdise8j7t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"\\\\uldash{(Gill, 2019)}","plainCitation":"(Gill, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20189,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JXQHGSMT"],"itemData":{"id":20189,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","note":"publisher: Annual Reviews","page":"347–372","title":"Social synergies, tradeoffs, and equity in marine conservation impacts","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Gill","given":"David A"},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Samantha H"},{"family":"Glew","given":"Louise"},{"family":"Aigner","given":"Ernest"},{"family":"Bennett","given":"Nathan J"},{"family":"Mascia","given":"Michael B"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but should recognize that social equity and the systematic assessment of their local impacts are critical to success, as is the case for fisheries more generally ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ePmUWAGp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cochrane, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Cochrane, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1783,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RQGI57LD"],"itemData":{"id":1783,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/faf.12521","ISSN":"1467-2960","issue":"n/a","note":"type: Journal Article","title":"Reconciling sustainability, economic efficiency and equity in marine fisheries: Has there been progress in the last 20 years?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1111/faf.12521","volume":"n/a","author":[{"family":"Cochrane","given":"Kevern L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Expanding the global marine protected areas network to cover 28% of the ocean could increase food provisioning by 5.9 million tonnes, as well as providing carbon sequestration benefits in addition to their biodiversity conservation benefits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2egk8ha6le","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sala, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Sala, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20190,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/92R5UY2C"],"itemData":{"id":20190,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-021-03371-z","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7854","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"397-402","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Protecting the global ocean for biodiversity, food and climate","volume":"592","author":[{"family":"Sala","given":"Enric"},{"family":"Mayorga","given":"Juan"},{"family":"Bradley","given":"Darcy"},{"family":"Cabral","given":"Reniel B."},{"family":"Atwood","given":"Trisha B."},{"family":"Auber","given":"Arnaud"},{"family":"Cheung","given":"William"},{"family":"Costello","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Ferretti","given":"Francesco"},{"family":"Friedlander","given":"Alan M."},{"family":"Gaines","given":"Steven D."},{"family":"Garilao","given":"Cristina"},{"family":"Goodell","given":"Whitney"},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."},{"family":"Hinson","given":"Audra"},{"family":"Kaschner","given":"Kristin"},{"family":"Kesner-Reyes","given":"Kathleen"},{"family":"Leprieur","given":"Fabien"},{"family":"McGowan","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Morgan","given":"Lance E."},{"family":"Mouillot","given":"David"},{"family":"Palacios-Abrantes","given":"Juliano"},{"family":"Possingham","given":"Hugh P."},{"family":"Rechberger","given":"Kristin D."},{"family":"Worm","given":"Boris"},{"family":"Lubchenco","given":"Jane"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As with fisheries, it is also important to build climate resilience into marine protected areas and to recognize the challenges that climate change poses to their effectiveness ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"andfj0m4d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tittensor, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Tittensor, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20192,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JC3AXHWL"],"itemData":{"id":20192,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"We evaluate the extent of climate change adaptation in the global protected seascape, and identify ways to further advance it.\\n          , \\n            The impacts of climate change and the socioecological challenges they present are ubiquitous and increasingly severe. Practical efforts to operationalize climate-responsive design and management in the global network of marine protected areas (MPAs) are required to ensure long-term effectiveness for safeguarding marine biodiversity and ecosystem services. Here, we review progress in integrating climate change adaptation into MPA design and management and provide eight recommendations to expedite this process. Climate-smart management objectives should become the default for all protected areas, and made into an explicit international policy target. Furthermore, incentives to use more dynamic management tools would increase the climate change responsiveness of the MPA network as a whole. Given ongoing negotiations on international conservation targets, now is the ideal time to proactively reform management of the global seascape for the dynamic climate-biodiversity reality.","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.aay9969","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Sci. Adv.","language":"en","page":"eaay9969","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Integrating climate adaptation and biodiversity conservation in the global ocean","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Tittensor","given":"Derek P."},{"family":"Beger","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Boerder","given":"Kristina"},{"family":"Boyce","given":"Daniel G."},{"family":"Cavanagh","given":"Rachel D."},{"family":"Cosandey-Godin","given":"Aurelie"},{"family":"Crespo","given":"Guillermo Ortuño"},{"family":"Dunn","given":"Daniel C."},{"family":"Ghiffary","given":"Wildan"},{"family":"Grant","given":"Susie M."},{"family":"Hannah","given":"Lee"},{"family":"Halpin","given":"Patrick N."},{"family":"Harfoot","given":"Mike"},{"family":"Heaslip","given":"Susan G."},{"family":"Jeffery","given":"Nicholas W."},{"family":"Kingston","given":"Naomi"},{"family":"Lotze","given":"Heike K."},{"family":"McGowan","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"McLeod","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"McOwen","given":"Chris J."},{"family":"O’Leary","given":"Bethan C."},{"family":"Schiller","given":"Laurenne"},{"family":"Stanley","given":"Ryan R. E."},{"family":"Westhead","given":"Maxine"},{"family":"Wilson","given":"Kristen L."},{"family":"Worm","given":"Boris"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition to marine protected areas, “human-used areas” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qqX4Kjo8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hilborn & Sinclair, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Hilborn & Sinclair, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19327,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8P8XQ4ZJ"],"itemData":{"id":19327,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12830","language":"en","title":"Biodiversity protection in the 21st century needs intact habitat and protection from overexploitation whether inside or outside parks","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Hilborn","given":"R."},{"family":"Sinclair","given":"A.R.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and other spatial management such as Other Effective Area-based Conservation Measures which can define fishery closures or areas with fishery restrictions, can also contribute to the sustainable use (e.g. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"21AYOZb4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Petza, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Petza, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19118,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MHZG9MM5"],"itemData":{"id":19118,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2019.01.005","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"28-39","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An operational framework to assess the value of fisheries restricted areas for marine conservation","volume":"102","author":[{"family":"Petza","given":"Dimitra"},{"family":"Chalkias","given":"Christos"},{"family":"Koukourouvli","given":"Nikoleta"},{"family":"Coll","given":"Marta"},{"family":"Vassilopoulou","given":"Vassiliki"},{"family":"Karachle","given":"Paraskevi K."},{"family":"Markantonatou","given":"Vasiliki"},{"family":"Tsikliras","given":"Athanasios C."},{"family":"Katsanevakis","given":"Stelios"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The focus for the future must be on learning how to merge enhanced human food security with the long-term persistence of biota needed for the stability of ecosystems.
      Overall, improving fisheries management and effective harvest control rules imply a reduction of overfishing in addition to the rebuilding of depleted stocks. Management actions show cumulative benefits and a broad suite of management measures at local, national and international levels appears to be key to sustaining fish populations and food production ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7hFCqYfY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Melnychuk, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Melnychuk, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":10256,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GPRZVEWL"],"itemData":{"id":10256,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Sustainability","DOI":"10.1038/s41893-020-00668-1","ISSN":"2398-9629","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Sustain","language":"en","page":"440-449","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Identifying management actions that promote sustainable fisheries","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Melnychuk","given":"Michael C."},{"family":"Kurota","given":"Hiroyuki"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Pamela M."},{"family":"Pons","given":"Maite"},{"family":"Minto","given":"Cóilín"},{"family":"Osio","given":"Giacomo Chato"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Olaf P."},{"family":"Moor","given":"Carryn L.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Richard Little","given":"L."},{"family":"Hively","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Ashbrook","given":"Charmane E."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Nicole"},{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo O."},{"family":"Branch","given":"Trevor A."},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Christopher M."},{"family":"Szuwalski","given":"Cody S."},{"family":"Baum","given":"Julia K."},{"family":"McClanahan","given":"Tim R."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Yimin"},{"family":"Ligas","given":"Alessandro"},{"family":"Bensbai","given":"Jilali"},{"family":"Thompson","given":"Grant G."},{"family":"DeVore","given":"John"},{"family":"Magnusson","given":"Arni"},{"family":"Bogstad","given":"Bjarte"},{"family":"Wort","given":"Edward"},{"family":"Rice","given":"Jake"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Among the most effective actions are rebuilding plans that rapidly lower fishing pressure towards target levels, enabling overfished populations to recover ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WXQgA13j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Melnychuk, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Melnychuk, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":10256,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GPRZVEWL"],"itemData":{"id":10256,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Sustainability","DOI":"10.1038/s41893-020-00668-1","ISSN":"2398-9629","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Sustain","language":"en","page":"440-449","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Identifying management actions that promote sustainable fisheries","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Melnychuk","given":"Michael C."},{"family":"Kurota","given":"Hiroyuki"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Pamela M."},{"family":"Pons","given":"Maite"},{"family":"Minto","given":"Cóilín"},{"family":"Osio","given":"Giacomo Chato"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Olaf P."},{"family":"Moor","given":"Carryn L.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Richard Little","given":"L."},{"family":"Hively","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Ashbrook","given":"Charmane E."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Nicole"},{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo O."},{"family":"Branch","given":"Trevor A."},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Christopher M."},{"family":"Szuwalski","given":"Cody S."},{"family":"Baum","given":"Julia K."},{"family":"McClanahan","given":"Tim R."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Yimin"},{"family":"Ligas","given":"Alessandro"},{"family":"Bensbai","given":"Jilali"},{"family":"Thompson","given":"Grant G."},{"family":"DeVore","given":"John"},{"family":"Magnusson","given":"Arni"},{"family":"Bogstad","given":"Bjarte"},{"family":"Wort","given":"Edward"},{"family":"Rice","given":"Jake"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Additionally, the ratification of international fishing agreements, and harvest control rules specifying how catch limits should vary with population biomass help to reduce overfishing and rebuild biomass. Notably, the cumulative benefits of management actions lead to stock status improvements and predicted long-term catch increases ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WDfpfvWM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Melnychuk, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Melnychuk, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":10256,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GPRZVEWL"],"itemData":{"id":10256,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Sustainability","DOI":"10.1038/s41893-020-00668-1","ISSN":"2398-9629","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Sustain","language":"en","page":"440-449","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Identifying management actions that promote sustainable fisheries","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Melnychuk","given":"Michael C."},{"family":"Kurota","given":"Hiroyuki"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Pamela M."},{"family":"Pons","given":"Maite"},{"family":"Minto","given":"Cóilín"},{"family":"Osio","given":"Giacomo Chato"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Olaf P."},{"family":"Moor","given":"Carryn L.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Richard Little","given":"L."},{"family":"Hively","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Ashbrook","given":"Charmane E."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Nicole"},{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo O."},{"family":"Branch","given":"Trevor A."},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Christopher M."},{"family":"Szuwalski","given":"Cody S."},{"family":"Baum","given":"Julia K."},{"family":"McClanahan","given":"Tim R."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Yimin"},{"family":"Ligas","given":"Alessandro"},{"family":"Bensbai","given":"Jilali"},{"family":"Thompson","given":"Grant G."},{"family":"DeVore","given":"John"},{"family":"Magnusson","given":"Arni"},{"family":"Bogstad","given":"Bjarte"},{"family":"Wort","given":"Edward"},{"family":"Rice","given":"Jake"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Regarding policies for securing sustainable small-scale fisheries, investments in alternative livelihoods have been insufficient and deeper structural changes, such as changes to property rights that explicitly recognize securing sustainable small-scale fisheries and their unique needs are required. Policies that recognize, rather than undermine, traditional and indigenous rights and access rights, but that may also support more inclusive relationships with state and/or corporate actors may be an important element ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"h4q8QuYQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rebecca E. Short, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Rebecca E. Short, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19110,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L4ZARGMQ"],"itemData":{"id":19110,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Food","DOI":"10.1038/s43016-021-00363-0","ISSN":"2662-1355","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Food","language":"en","page":"733-741","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Harnessing the diversity of small-scale actors is key to the future of aquatic food systems","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Short","given":"Rebecca E."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Little","given":"David C."},{"family":"Micheli","given":"Fiorenza"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Belton","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Brugere","given":"Cecile"},{"family":"Bush","given":"Simon R."},{"family":"Cao","given":"Ling"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Cohen","given":"Philippa J."},{"family":"Defeo","given":"Omar"},{"family":"Edwards","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Ferguson","given":"Caroline E."},{"family":"Franz","given":"Nicole"},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."},{"family":"Hazen","given":"Lucie"},{"family":"Hicks","given":"Christina"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Derek"},{"family":"Kaminski","given":"Alexander M."},{"family":"Mangubhai","given":"Sangeeta"},{"family":"Naylor","given":"Rosamond L."},{"family":"Reantaso","given":"Melba"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U. Rashid"},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"Shakuntala H."},{"family":"Tigchelaar","given":"Michelle"},{"family":"Wabnitz","given":"Colette C. C."},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Wenbo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
5.4.2.7 Cultural
Localized subsistence and indigenous fisheries tend to fall outside the scale at which global climate assessments are conducted, yet subsistence fishing in many developing countries takes place in intertidal areas including shallow reef flats and mangroves that are threatened by climate change. A large proportion of subsistence catch is made up of bivalves, gastropods and other invertebrates; calcifying species that are expected to be negatively impacted by ocean acidification. The predicted loss for subsistence fishing is likely to reduce overall household well-being, including health and socio-cultural aspects. However, at a local scale, invertebrates in the Pacific islands are expected to experience only moderate decline from climate change, thus subsistence gleaning may become even more important in the face of reduced coral reef fish catch. On a global scale, many of the world’s poorest countries are also the most heavily reliant on fish for protein, thus future climate-driven impacts are likely to result in additional socio-economic hardship in countries that are more reliant on fisheries but have limited capacity to adapt  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15fftoijd5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nash, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Nash, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20194,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BX5RPP6Q"],"itemData":{"id":20194,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"One Earth","DOI":"10.1016/j.oneear.2020.01.008","ISSN":"25903322","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"One Earth","language":"en","page":"161-173","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"To Achieve a Sustainable Blue Future, Progress Assessments Must Include Interdependencies between the Sustainable Development Goals","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Nash","given":"Kirsty L."},{"family":"Blythe","given":"Jessica L."},{"family":"Cvitanovic","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Fulton","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."},{"family":"Addison","given":"Prue F.E."},{"family":"Pecl","given":"Gretta T."},{"family":"Watson","given":"Reg A."},{"family":"Blanchard","given":"Julia L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      In North America, climate impacts on indigenous fisheries are expected to be variable. In western Canada, warmer-water species such as Pacific sardines are projected to increase and provide an opportunity to develop or expand new commercial harvests. However, declines are expected in commercial herring and salmon stocks that contribute significantly to First Nations’ fisheries revenues, as well as in species important for food and ceremonial purposes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"keirserl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Weatherdon, Ota, Jones, Close, & Cheung, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Weatherdon, Ota, Jones, Close, & Cheung, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19099,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VB6Q2PJ4"],"itemData":{"id":19099,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0145285","language":"en","page":"0145285","title":"Projected Scenarios for Coastal First Nations’ Fisheries Catch Potential under Climate Change: Management Challenges and Opportunities","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Weatherdon","given":"L.V."},{"family":"Ota","given":"Y."},{"family":"Jones","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Close","given":"D.A."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. From a nutritional perspective, health may be negatively impacted as nutrient intake derived from traditional seafood consumption declines, and this nutrient deficit is not easily substituted by other food sources. In order to meet future challenges related to food security, livelihoods, cultural integrity and equity provided by small-scale fisheries, it becomes important to support the diversity of small-scale actors through appropriate policies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ThmdK18D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rebecca E. Short, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Rebecca E. Short, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19110,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L4ZARGMQ"],"itemData":{"id":19110,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Food","DOI":"10.1038/s43016-021-00363-0","ISSN":"2662-1355","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Food","language":"en","page":"733-741","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Harnessing the diversity of small-scale actors is key to the future of aquatic food systems","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Short","given":"Rebecca E."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Little","given":"David C."},{"family":"Micheli","given":"Fiorenza"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Belton","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Brugere","given":"Cecile"},{"family":"Bush","given":"Simon R."},{"family":"Cao","given":"Ling"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Cohen","given":"Philippa J."},{"family":"Defeo","given":"Omar"},{"family":"Edwards","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Ferguson","given":"Caroline E."},{"family":"Franz","given":"Nicole"},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."},{"family":"Hazen","given":"Lucie"},{"family":"Hicks","given":"Christina"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Derek"},{"family":"Kaminski","given":"Alexander M."},{"family":"Mangubhai","given":"Sangeeta"},{"family":"Naylor","given":"Rosamond L."},{"family":"Reantaso","given":"Melba"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U. Rashid"},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"Shakuntala H."},{"family":"Tigchelaar","given":"Michelle"},{"family":"Wabnitz","given":"Colette C. C."},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Wenbo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. and the inclusion of cultural asset preservation and benefits in future scenarios.
5.4.2.8  Summary of plausible futures for fisheries
Fisheries provide significant socio-economic benefits by contributing to local and global food security and employment. Characterizing the plausible futures of fisheries is key to assessing fish provision, demand and consumption in the next decades under differing projections of population, income growth and climate change. 
      Global catches are projected to stay at high levels with fluctuations due to the El Nino phenomenon in South America ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JtoYgYa2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5385,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":5385,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A continued trend of industrial exploitation rates (business as usual) may likely increase the number of overfished stocks which should be reversed by improving harvest control rules, technological advances on surveillance, and recovery plans in fisheries management. Projections indicate that an increase of fish yields should also reduce land and water use by a half. 
      Climate change is recognized as a major threat, which will affect multiple aspects of marine ecosystems (e.g., species distribution, biological invasions, species life history traits, etc.) and impact aquatic food systems from production to consumption worldwide. The effects of climate change on fisheries production systems are already visible in some regions of the world and are projected to have higher impacts on the food security of fisheries-dependent communities, which could put more pressure on small-scale and subsistence fisheries. Additional pressures due to demographic growth and conflicts in sea use may aggravate climate impacts and lead these communities to adapt their fishing behavior and affect socio-cultural aspects of their practices. Climate-driven impacts on species ranges and changes in fisheries productivity are expected to profoundly affect the benefits that wild capture fisheries provide to the human population, including aspects such as food provision, nutrients, social benefits, and livelihoods. 
      Therefore, in order to mitigate changes on current production systems, governance, fishing practices and economic factors should be evaluated to help transition operations and build climate resilience. This is even more important as global fish demand is projected to almost double by mid-century. In order to meet challenges related to food security, livelihoods, cultural integrity and equity, it is important to support the diversity of small-scale fisheries. Projections of bio-economic impacts on wild-capture fisheries show they are likely to be substantial for many regions of the world (in particular the Global South), both for small- and large-scale fisheries. Furthermore, future socio-economic and political trends show that overfishing and climate change could increase the likelihood of fishery conflicts by the mid-century.
      Overall, the consequences of climate impacts on fisheries will reverberate into different sectors of society worldwide, with those dependent on fishing for food, livelihoods and cultural purposes most severely affected. Climate change will interact in complex ways with other drivers of change in fishing practices and intensity, at multiple scales and across jurisdictions. Hence the need for effective coastal and high seas fisheries management into the future, to ensure the sustainability of wild capture fisheries and the resilience of ?sh stock.
      Preventing overfishing and developing management strategies that are robust to environmentally-driven changes in productivity are essential to maintain and rebuild the capacity for global wild-capture fisheries to supply food. Harvest control rules and marine protected areas are among the management approaches that may provide benefits, help to prevent overfishing, and rebuild depleted populations. Yet the poor current status of many stocks combined with potentially maladaptive responses to range shifts could reduce future global fisheries yields and profits. However, reforming fisheries in ways that jointly fix current inefficiencies, adapt to fisheries productivity changes, and proactively create effective transboundary institutions to provide continuity in management practices are key elements for sustaining wild capture species and food production. 

5.4.3  Gathering
5.4.3.1 Introduction
Globally, hundreds-to-thousands of species of algae, fungi and plants are gathered for food, medicine, construction and other uses. Gathering occurs in ecosystems from boreal forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yXqYeiEd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Uprety, Asselin, Bergeron, Doyon, & Boucher, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Uprety, Asselin, Bergeron, Doyon, & Boucher, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4468,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9DTX9R8"],"itemData":{"id":4468,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Traditional knowledge has become a topic of considerable interest within the research and development environment. The contribution of traditional knowledge to conservation and management is increasingly recognized, and implementation endeavours are underway in several countries. The current scale of ecosystem degradation underscores the need for restoration interventions. It is increasingly recognized that successful ecological restoration depends on effective coordination of science and traditional ecological knowledge. This paper synthesizes the literature to evaluate the present and potential contribution of traditional knowledge to ecological restoration. Despite a growing number of articles published on traditional knowledge, only a few have addressed its contributions to ecological restoration per se. The main contributions of traditional knowledge to ecological restoration are in construction of reference ecosystems, particularly when historical information is not available; species selection for restoration plantations; site selection for restoration; knowledge about historical land management practices; management of invasive species; and post-restoration monitoring. Traditional knowledge and science are complementary and should be used in conjunction in ecological restoration projects. Incorporation of traditional knowledge can contribute to build a strong partnership for the successful implementation of restoration projects and increase their social acceptability, economical feasibility, and ecological viability.","call-number":"Sub180 or Esub20k","container-title":"Écoscience","DOI":"10.2980/19-3-3530","ISSN":"1195-6860","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Écoscience","page":"225–237","title":"Contribution of traditional knowledge to ecological restoration: Practices and applications","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Uprety","given":"Yadav"},{"family":"Asselin","given":"Hugo"},{"family":"Bergeron","given":"Yves"},{"family":"Doyon","given":"Frédérik"},{"family":"Boucher","given":"Jean-François"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} to semi-arid savannahs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ht2spN5r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schumann, Wittig, Thiombiano, Becker, & Hahn, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Schumann, Wittig, Thiombiano, Becker, & Hahn, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19222,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4VPW6LA4"],"itemData":{"id":19222,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"2035–2044","title":"Impact of land-use type and bark-and leaf-harvesting on population structure and fruit production of the baobab tree (Adansonia digitata L.) in a semi-arid savanna, West Africa","volume":"260","author":[{"family":"Schumann","given":"K."},{"family":"Wittig","given":"R."},{"family":"Thiombiano","given":"A."},{"family":"Becker","given":"U."},{"family":"Hahn","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and from high altitude environments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vjVAhFo2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pradhan & Badola, 2015; Rana, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Pradhan & Badola, 2015; Rana, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19218,"uris