erties":{"formattedCitation":"(Delgado-Lemus, 2014; Uprety, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Delgado-Lemus, 2014; Uprety, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19182,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R9RREZT5"],"itemData":{"id":19182,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–12","title":"Distribution, abundance and traditional management of Agave potatorumin the Tehuacán Valley, Mexico: Bases for sustainable use of non-timber forest products","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Delgado-Lemus","given":"A."},{"family":"Casas","given":"A."},{"family":"Téllez","given":"O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":4468,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9DTX9R8"],"itemData":{"id":4468,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Traditional knowledge has become a topic of considerable interest within the research and development environment. The contribution of traditional knowledge to conservation and management is increasingly recognized, and implementation endeavours are underway in several countries. The current scale of ecosystem degradation underscores the need for restoration interventions. It is increasingly recognized that successful ecological restoration depends on effective coordination of science and traditional ecological knowledge. This paper synthesizes the literature to evaluate the present and potential contribution of traditional knowledge to ecological restoration. Despite a growing number of articles published on traditional knowledge, only a few have addressed its contributions to ecological restoration per se. The main contributions of traditional knowledge to ecological restoration are in construction of reference ecosystems, particularly when historical information is not available; species selection for restoration plantations; site selection for restoration; knowledge about historical land management practices; management of invasive species; and post-restoration monitoring. Traditional knowledge and science are complementary and should be used in conjunction in ecological restoration projects. Incorporation of traditional knowledge can contribute to build a strong partnership for the successful implementation of restoration projects and increase their social acceptability, economical feasibility, and ecological viability.","call-number":"Sub180 or Esub20k","container-title":"Écoscience","DOI":"10.2980/19-3-3530","ISSN":"1195-6860","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Écoscience","page":"225–237","title":"Contribution of traditional knowledge to ecological restoration: Practices and applications","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Uprety","given":"Yadav"},{"family":"Asselin","given":"Hugo"},{"family":"Bergeron","given":"Yves"},{"family":"Doyon","given":"Frédérik"},{"family":"Boucher","given":"Jean-François"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} but a lack of baseline data, as well as the highly dispersed, low entry cost nature of this practice makes it challenging to determine trends in the numbers of people who gather and the volumes of all algae, fungi and plants gathered at a global scale. However, although incomplete, data are available for some gathered materials that are commercially traded and a number of studies detail the social, cultural and economic significance of gathering and uses of gathered materials in all regions of the world.
      Over exploitation has been identified as an important driver of global plant extinctions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FoXhc0jj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kor, Homewood, Dawson, & Diazgranados, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Kor, Homewood, Dawson, & Diazgranados, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19205,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TEUB24GR"],"itemData":{"id":19205,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ambio","issue":"9","language":"en","page":"1681–1697","title":"Sustainability of wild plant use in the Andean Community of South America","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Kor","given":"L."},{"family":"Homewood","given":"K."},{"family":"Dawson","given":"T.P."},{"family":"Diazgranados","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, a systematic review of 101 ecological studies addressing population-level consequences of gathering ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Nxardqyk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stanley, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Stanley, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5172,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S5QGTRKX"],"itemData":{"id":5172,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ethnobiology and Conservation","ISSN":"2238-4782","journalAbbreviation":"Ethnobio Conserv","language":"en","source":"www.ethnobioconservation.com","title":"Is Non-Timber Forest Product Harvest Sustainable in the Less Developed World? A Systematic Review of the Recent Economic and Ecological Literature","title-short":"Is Non-Timber Forest Product Harvest Sustainable in the Less Developed World?","URL":"https://www.ethnobioconservation.com/index.php/ebc/article/view/19","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Stanley","given":"Denise"},{"family":"Voeks","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Short","given":"Leaa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",10,11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found that in almost two-thirds (63.3%) of the cases examined, rates of extraction were or likely were sustainable while less than one-fifth (17.8%) were unsustainable. Few scenarios explicitly address the sustainability of gathering (but see ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1dBiwHEL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bond\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Bondé, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19174,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6KJFBZI6"],"itemData":{"id":19174,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biota Neotropica","language":"en","title":"Scenario-modelling for the sustainable management of non-timber forest products in tropical ecosystems","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Bondé","given":"L."},{"family":"Assis","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Benavides-Gordillo","given":"S."},{"family":"Canales-Gomez","given":"E."},{"family":"Fajardo","given":"J."},{"family":"Marrón-Becerra","given":"A."},{"family":"Ament","given":"J.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, although scenarios that project possible futures for forests and other ecosystems in which wild algae, fungi and plants occur are relevant, as are broader climate change scenarios. In contrast, several modeling methods are commonly applied to predict the future social and ecological sustainability of gathering. Many models designed to assess ecological sustainability focus on changes in habitat extent and distribution under climate change alone or in combination with other drivers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5H0xUOeU","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Ardestani & Ghahfarrokhi, 2021; Asase & Peterson, 2019; Chitale, Silwal, & Matin, 2018; Groner, 2021; Jansen, Anten, Bongers, Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}nez-Ramos, & Zuidema, 2018; Munt, Mu\\\\uc0\\\