re stewardship increases the population growth rate of an understorey herb","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Hart?Fredeluces","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."},{"family":"Lake","given":"F.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}},"locator":"-"},{"id":19197,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IP2W3ASX"],"itemData":{"id":19197,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Population ecology","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"463–476","title":"Demography of Oenocarpus bataua and implications for sustainable harvest of its fruit in western Amazon","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Isaza","given":"C."},{"family":"Martorell","given":"C."},{"family":"Cevallos","given":"D."},{"family":"Galeano","given":"G."},{"family":"Valencia","given":"R."},{"family":"Balslev","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Projections of economic sustainability generally emphasize the financial returns to gatherers and/or the state ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XOWd9lGz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Saha & Sundriyal, 2012; Stanley, 2012; Van Andel, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Saha & Sundriyal, 2012; Stanley, 2012; Van Andel, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19221,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GIF6BGV7"],"itemData":{"id":19221,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"28–40","title":"Utilization of non-timber forest products in humid tropics: Implications for management and livelihood","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Saha","given":"D."},{"family":"Sundriyal","given":"R.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":5172,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S5QGTRKX"],"itemData":{"id":5172,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ethnobiology and Conservation","ISSN":"2238-4782","journalAbbreviation":"Ethnobio Conserv","language":"en","source":"www.ethnobioconservation.com","title":"Is Non-Timber Forest Product Harvest Sustainable in the Less Developed World? A Systematic Review of the Recent Economic and Ecological Literature","title-short":"Is Non-Timber Forest Product Harvest Sustainable in the Less Developed World?","URL":"https://www.ethnobioconservation.com/index.php/ebc/article/view/19","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Stanley","given":"Denise"},{"family":"Voeks","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Short","given":"Leaa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",10,11]]}}},{"id":19228,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BNCKP2PF"],"itemData":{"id":19228,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"173–181","title":"Prioritizing West African medicinal plants for conservation and sustainable extraction studies based on market surveys and species distribution models","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Van Andel","given":"T.R."},{"family":"Croft","given":"S."},{"family":"Van Loon","given":"E.E."},{"family":"Quiroz","given":"D."},{"family":"Towns","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Raes","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, with the contributions of subsistence uses to local livelihoods rarely incorporated into models.
 
5.4.3.2 Social
Millions of people worldwide participate in gathering algae, fungi and plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WENaAIxc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gaoue, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Gaoue, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19186,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VNCBBEVY"],"itemData":{"id":19186,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"844–852","title":"Human impacts on leaf economics in heterogeneous landscapes: the effect of harvesting non?timber forest products from African mahogany across habitats and climates","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Gaoue","given":"O.G."},{"family":"Sack","given":"L."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. One systematic literature review estimates that 80% of people living in developing countries rely on wild algae, fungi and plants as the main source to meet their nutrition and health needs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6r9FTb5n","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Mello, Gulinck, Van den Broeck, & Parra, 2020)","plainCitation":"(de Mello, Gulinck, Van den Broeck, & Parra, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7469,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TV355YM2"],"itemData":{"id":7469,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2020.102109","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"102109","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Social-ecological sustainability of non-timber forest products: A review and theoretical considerations for future research","title-short":"Social-ecological sustainability of non-timber forest products","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Mello","given":"Natália Girão Rodrigues","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Gulinck","given":"Hubert"},{"family":"Van den Broeck","given":"Pieter"},{"family":"Parra","given":"Constanza"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Gathering of algae, fungi and plants make important contributions to food security ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Q5Yaj774","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Campbell, 2021; P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Moreno, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Campbell, 2021; Pérez?Moreno, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19175,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NW6JQYX2"],"itemData":{"id":19175,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Sustainable Food Systems","language":"en","page":"150","title":"Wild food harvest, food security and biodiversity conservation in Jamaica: As case study of the Millbank farming region","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Campbell","given":"D."},{"family":"Moulton","given":"A."},{"family":"Barker","given":"D."},{"family":"Malcolm","given":"T."},{"family":"Scott","given":"L."},{"family":"Spence","given":"A."},{"family":"Wallace","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":20100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E35V4YCV"],"itemData":{"id":20100,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLANTS, PEOPLE, PLANET","DOI":"10.1002/ppp3.10199","ISSN":"2572-2611, 2572-2611","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Plants People Planet","language":"en","page":"471-490","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Edible mycorrhizal fungi of the world: What is their role in forest sustainability, food security, biocultural conservation and climate change?","title-short":"Edible mycorrhizal fungi of the world","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Pérez?Moreno","given":"Jesús"},{"family":"Guerin?Laguette","given":"Alexis"},{"family":"Rinaldi","given":"Andrea C."},{"family":"Yu","given":"Fuqiang"},{"family":"Verbeken","given":"Annemieke"},{"family":"Hernández?Santiago","given":"Faustino"},{"family":"Martínez?Reyes","given":"Magdalena"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but knowledge regarding their nutritional contributions is limited ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2hpqhdj96q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vinceti, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Vinceti, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19230,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZBKKKCHW"],"itemData":{"id":19230,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"4797–4824","title":"The contribution of forests and trees to sustainable diets","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Vinceti","given":"B."},{"family":"Termote","given":"C."},{"family":"Ickowitz","given":"A."},{"family":"Powell","given":"B."},{"family":"Kehlenbeck","given":"K."},{"family":"Hunter","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} . In some places, however, participation in gathering and reliance on gathered materials may be declining in response to urbanization and increased access to infrastructure and services ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZSyq81hN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gray, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Gray, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19189,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4DPZCQ6M"],"itemData":{"id":19189,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological conservation","language":"en","page":"270–277","title":"Declining use of wild resources by indigenous peoples of the Ecuadorian Amazon","volume":"182","author":[{"family":"Gray","given":"C.L."},{"family":"Bozigar","given":"M."},{"family":"Bilsborrow","given":"R.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, few scenarios and modeling studies explicitly include non-economic social factors in their parameters and this remains a knowledge gap regarding sustainable gathering.
 
5.4.3.3 Technological
Typically, the tools used in gathering are manual. Thus, the methods or techniques used to gather wild algae, fungi and plants and the knowledge underlying those methods constitute the most significant technological aspects of gathering. Key dimensions of gathering techniques include the places and times in which harvesting does or does not occur, the individual specimens and part or parts thereof to be harvested and volumes of material to be taken. Harvest impact studies and models make it clear that the sustainability of harvesting techniques are species and context specific. While there are cases in which empirical data and models indicate that the techniques in use have or could reduce populations of the gathered species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YBhHmzg3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a, 2016; Hern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Barrios, 2015; Isaza, 2016)","plainCitation":"(García, 2016; Hernández?Barrios, 2015; Isaza, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19470,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R9MAF4BG"],"itemData":{"id":19470,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biotropica","issue":"5","language":"es","page":"598–607","title":"Demography and sustainable management of two fiber?producing Astrocaryum palms in Colombia","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"García","given":"N."},{"family":"Zuidema","given":"P.A."},{"family":"Galeano","given":"G."},{"family":"Bernal","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":19194,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U6K7E248"],"itemData":{"id":19194,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"389–401","title":"Sustainable harvesting of non?timber forest products based on ecological and economic criteria","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Hernández?Barrios","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Anten","given":"N.P."},{"family":"Martínez?Ramos","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":19197,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IP2W3ASX"],"itemData":{"id":19197,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Population ecology","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"463–476","title":"Demography of Oenocarpus bataua and implications for sustainable harvest of its fruit in western Amazon","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Isaza","given":"C."},{"family":"Martorell","given":"C."},{"family":"Cevallos","given":"D."},{"family":"Galeano","given":"G."},{"family":"Valencia","given":"R."},{"family":"Balslev","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and may present a risk of localized extinctions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Jp4Kjc5X","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(De Angeli, 2021)","plainCitation":"(De Angeli, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19178,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QGQ4VKLV"],"itemData":{"id":19178,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Modelling","language":"en","page":"109328","title":"Modeling the impact of wild harvest on plant–disperser mutualisms: Plant and disperser co-harvest model","volume":"439","author":[{"family":"De Angeli","given":"K."},{"family":"Abbasi","given":"E."},{"family":"Gan","given":"A."},{"family":"Ingram","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Giam","given":"X."},{"family":"Chang","given":"C.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} there are also cases in which the outcomes of gathering can be neutral at the population level or may even enhance the vital rates (i.e., growth, reproductive success and survival) of individual plants and/or populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9GxFtul4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hart\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Fredeluces, 2021, p.; Kurttila, Pukkala, & Miina, 2018; Varghese, Ticktin, Mandle, & Nath, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Hart?Fredeluces, 2021, p.; Kurttila, Pukkala, & Miina, 2018; Varghese, Ticktin, Mandle, & Nath, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19191,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CJCBTM79"],"itemData":{"id":19191,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ecology","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"1133–1147","title":"Simulated Indigenous fire stewardship increases the population growth rate of an understorey herb","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Hart?Fredeluces","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."},{"family":"Lake","given":"F.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}},"locator":"-"},{"id":1380,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BREIDW6I"],"itemData":{"id":1380,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.3390/f9070417","issue":"7","note":"publisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"417","title":"Synergies and trade-offs in the production of NWFPs predicted in boreal forests","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Kurttila","given":"Mikko"},{"family":"Pukkala","given":"Timo"},{"family":"Miina","given":"Jari"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":17777,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVLWWHTI"],"itemData":{"id":17777,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS One","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"0119634","title":"Assessing the effects of multiple stressors on the recruitment of fruit harvested trees in a tropical dry forest, Western Ghats, India","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Varghese","given":"A."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."},{"family":"Mandle","given":"L."},{"family":"Nath","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Modeling corroborates empirical findings that gathering techniques tailored to the biology, ecology and life stage of the target species are more likely to be sustainable. For many species, size and age class play an important role in whether gathering is sustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ek8R7T6M","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Groner, 2021; Isaza, Bernal, Galeano, & Martorell, 2017; Jansen, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Groner, 2021; Isaza, Bernal, Galeano, & Martorell, 2017; Jansen, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19190,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U72QZCK2"],"itemData":{"id":19190,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","language":"en","title":"Climate change, land cover change, and overharvesting threaten a widely used medicinal plant in South Africa","volume":"e2545","author":[{"family":"Groner","given":"V.P."},{"family":"Nicholas","given":"O."},{"family":"Mabhaudhi","given":"T."},{"family":"Slotow","given":"R."},{"family":"Akçakaya","given":"H.R."},{"family":"Mace","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Pearson","given":"R.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":19198,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3GYHRUAV"],"itemData":{"id":19198,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biotropica","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"653–664","title":"Demography of Euterpe precatoria and Mauritia flexuosa in the Amazon: application of integral projection models for their harvest","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Isaza","given":"C."},{"family":"Bernal","given":"R."},{"family":"Galeano","given":"G."},{"family":"Martorell","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":7638,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U684F2U9"],"itemData":{"id":7638,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/1365-2664.13100","ISSN":"00218901","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Ecol","language":"en","page":"1682-1691","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Towards smarter harvesting from natural palm populations by sparing the individuals that contribute most to population growth or productivity","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Jansen","given":"Merel"},{"family":"Anten","given":"Niels P. R."},{"family":"Bongers","given":"Frans"},{"family":"Martínez-Ramos","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Zuidema","given":"Pieter A."}],"editor":[{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"Lucas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, models of methods used in the harvest of Natal Lily (Clivia miniata (Lindl.) Verschaff) in South Africa found that gathering individuals from all life stages would have a more negative effect on the overall population than would the harvest of only juvenile plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6o7NbGWR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Groner, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Groner, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19190,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U72QZCK2"],"itemData":{"id":19190,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","language":"en","title":"Climate change, land cover change, and overharvesting threaten a widely used medicinal plant in South Africa","volume":"e2545","author":[{"family":"Groner","given":"V.P."},{"family":"Nicholas","given":"O."},{"family":"Mabhaudhi","given":"T."},{"family":"Slotow","given":"R."},{"family":"Akçakaya","given":"H.R."},{"family":"Mace","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Pearson","given":"R.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Likewise, the sustainability of gathering frequently is contingent upon the habitat in which it takes place ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ckOoVn64","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Klimas, Cropper, Kainer, & Wadt, n.d.; Isaza, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Klimas, Cropper, Kainer, & Wadt, n.d.; Isaza, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":18480,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G3R2343N"],"itemData":{"id":18480,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Lu'cia Helena MultiModel Projections for Evaluating Sustainable Timber and Seed Harvest of Carapa guianensis 2018 FOREST SCIENCE","author":[{"family":"Klimas","given":"Christie M."},{"family":"Cropper","given":"Wendell P.","suffix":"Jr."},{"family":"Kainer","given":"Karen A."},{"family":"Wadt","given":"Oliveira"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":19197,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IP2W3ASX"],"itemData":{"id":19197,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Population ecology","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"463–476","title":"Demography of Oenocarpus bataua and implications for sustainable harvest of its fruit in western Amazon","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Isaza","given":"C."},{"family":"Martorell","given":"C."},{"family":"Cevallos","given":"D."},{"family":"Galeano","given":"G."},{"family":"Valencia","given":"R."},{"family":"Balslev","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, modeled effects of the compatibility of gathering seeds from the medicinal tree Carapas guianensis Aubl. and logging it for timber in western Amazonia indicate that there is no sustainable harvest level for seeds and full-tree harvest in upland forests, while in occasionally flooded forest lands populations could sustain gathering of 10% of seeds and logging of all trees over 50 centimeters in diameter. Landscape ecology also exerts a strong influence over the sustainability of gathering practices. One example is the interaction of gathering leaves from the western North American species beargrass (Xerophyllum tenax (Pursh) Nutt.). Scenarios examining this interaction forecast that in a business-as-usual future, in which there is a greater than 50% chance of high-intensity fire, and a future in which all fire is excluded, beargrass populations would be significantly lower than in a future characterized by cultural burning of the landscape by indigenous peoples ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zrnmtGkC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hart\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Fredeluces, 2021, p.)","plainCitation":"(Hart?Fredeluces, 2021, p.)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19191,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CJCBTM79"],"itemData":{"id":19191,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ecology","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"1133–1147","title":"Simulated Indigenous fire stewardship increases the population growth rate of an understorey herb","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Hart?Fredeluces","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."},{"family":"Lake","given":"F.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}},"locator":"-"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

5.4.3.4 Economic
Gathering provides essential livelihood resources to millions of people worldwide on an ongoing basis ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MVvKK5LL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gaoue, 2011; Jansen, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Gaoue, 2011; Jansen, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19186,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VNCBBEVY"],"itemData":{"id":19186,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"844–852","title":"Human impacts on leaf economics in heterogeneous landscapes: the effect of harvesting non?timber forest products from African mahogany across habitats and climates","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Gaoue","given":"O.G."},{"family":"Sack","given":"L."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":7638,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U684F2U9"],"itemData":{"id":7638,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/1365-2664.13100","ISSN":"00218901","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Ecol","language":"en","page":"1682-1691","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Towards smarter harvesting from natural palm populations by sparing the individuals that contribute most to population growth or productivity","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Jansen","given":"Merel"},{"family":"Anten","given":"Niels P. R."},{"family":"Bongers","given":"Frans"},{"family":"Martínez-Ramos","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Zuidema","given":"Pieter A."}],"editor":[{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"Lucas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} through subsistence consumption of gathered materials ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FFpxZ4qu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(De Angeli, 2021; P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Moreno, 2021; Saha & Sundriyal, 2012; Stanley, 2012)","plainCitation":"(De Angeli, 2021; Pérez?Moreno, 2021; Saha & Sundriyal, 2012; Stanley, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19178,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QGQ4VKLV"],"itemData":{"id":19178,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Modelling","language":"en","page":"109328","title":"Modeling the impact of wild harvest on plant–disperser mutualisms: Plant and disperser co-harvest model","volume":"439","author":[{"family":"De Angeli","given":"K."},{"family":"Abbasi","given":"E."},{"family":"Gan","given":"A."},{"family":"Ingram","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Giam","given":"X."},{"family":"Chang","given":"C.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":20100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E35V4YCV"],"itemData":{"id":20100,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLANTS, PEOPLE, PLANET","DOI":"10.1002/ppp3.10199","ISSN":"2572-2611, 2572-2611","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Plants People Planet","language":"en","page":"471-490","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Edible mycorrhizal fungi of the world: What is their role in forest sustainability, food security, biocultural conservation and climate change?","title-short":"Edible mycorrhizal fungi of the world","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Pérez?Moreno","given":"Jesús"},{"family":"Guerin?Laguette","given":"Alexis"},{"family":"Rinaldi","given":"Andrea C."},{"family":"Yu","given":"Fuqiang"},{"family":"Verbeken","given":"Annemieke"},{"family":"Hernández?Santiago","given":"Faustino"},{"family":"Martínez?Reyes","given":"Magdalena"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",9]]}}},{"id":19221,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GIF6BGV7"],"itemData":{"id":19221,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"28–40","title":"Utilization of non-timber forest products in humid tropics: Implications for management and livelihood","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Saha","given":"D."},{"family":"Sundriyal","given":"R.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":5172,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S5QGTRKX"],"itemData":{"id":5172,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ethnobiology and Conservation","ISSN":"2238-4782","journalAbbreviation":"Ethnobio Conserv","language":"en","source":"www.ethnobioconservation.com","title":"Is Non-Timber Forest Product Harvest Sustainable in the Less Developed World? A Systematic Review of the Recent Economic and Ecological Literature","title-short":"Is Non-Timber Forest Product Harvest Sustainable in the Less Developed World?","URL":"https://www.ethnobioconservation.com/index.php/ebc/article/view/19","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Stanley","given":"Denise"},{"family":"Voeks","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Short","given":"Leaa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",10,11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and income derived from trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UiIrBOp8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mumcu Kucuker & Baskent, 2015; Van Andel, 2015; Walsh & Douglas, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Mumcu Kucuker & Baskent, 2015; Van Andel, 2015; Walsh & Douglas, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19213,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DWF98U85"],"itemData":{"id":19213,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mycorrhiza","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–11","title":"Spatial prediction of Lactarius deliciosus and Lactarius salmonicolor mushroom distribution with logistic regression models in the K?z?lcasu Planning Unit, Turkey","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Mumcu Kucuker","given":"D."},{"family":"Baskent","given":"E.Z."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":19228,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BNCKP2PF"],"itemData":{"id":19228,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"173–181","title":"Prioritizing West African medicinal plants for conservation and sustainable extraction studies based on market surveys and species distribution models","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Van Andel","given":"T.R."},{"family":"Croft","given":"S."},{"family":"Van Loon","given":"E.E."},{"family":"Quiroz","given":"D."},{"family":"Towns","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Raes","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":2525,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BYDDK24W"],"itemData":{"id":2525,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Improvement in Aboriginal people’s livelihoods and economic opportunities has been a major aim of increased research and development on bush foods over the past decade. But worldwide the development of trade in non-timber forest products from natural populations has raised questions about the ecological sustainability of harvest. Trade-offs and tensions between commercialisation and cultural values have also been found. We investigated the sustainability of the small-scale commercial harvest and trade in native plant products sourced from central Australian rangelands (including Solanum centrale J.M. Black, Acacia Mill. spp.). We used semi-structured interviews with traders and Aboriginal harvesters, participant observation of trading and harvesting trips, and analysis of species and trader records. An expert Aboriginal reference group guided the project. We found no evidence of either taxa being vulnerable to over-harvest. S. centrale production is enhanced by harvesting when it co-occurs with patch-burning. Extreme fluctuations in productivity of both taxa, due to inter-annual rainfall variability, have a much greater impact on supply than harvest effects. Landscape-scale degradation (including cattle grazing and wildfire) affected ecological sustainability according to participants. By contrast, we found that sustainability of bush food trade is more strongly impacted by social and economic factors. The relationship-based links between harvesters and traders are critical to monetary trade. Harvesters and traders identified access to productive lands and narrow economic margins between costs and returns as issues for the future sustainability of harvest and trade. Harvesters and the reference group emphasised that sustaining bush harvest relies on future generations having necessary knowledge and skills; these are extremely vulnerable to loss. Aboriginal people derive multiple livelihood benefits from harvest and trade. Aboriginal custodians and harvester groups involved in recent trade are more likely to benefit from research and development investment to inter-generational knowledge and skill transfer than from investments in plant breeding and commercial horticultural development. In an inductive comparison, our study found there to be strong alignment between key findings about the strategies used by harvesters and traders in bush produce and the ‘desert system’..","container-title":"The Rangeland Journal","DOI":"10.1071/RJ11028","ISSN":"1834-7541","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Rangel. J.","language":"en","note":"publisher: CSIRO PUBLISHING","page":"395-416","source":"www.publish.csiro.au","title":"No bush foods without people: the essential human dimension to the sustainability of trade in native plant products from desert Australia","title-short":"No bush foods without people","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Walsh","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Douglas","given":"Josie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition, gathering and gathered materials are important safety nets in times of environmental and economic shocks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DBJ9Cm6b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Mello, 2020)","plainCitation":"(de Mello, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7469,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TV355YM2"],"itemData":{"id":7469,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2020.102109","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"102109","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Social-ecological sustainability of non-timber forest products: A review and theoretical considerations for future research","title-short":"Social-ecological sustainability of non-timber forest products","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Mello","given":"Natália Girão Rodrigues","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Gulinck","given":"Hubert"},{"family":"Van den Broeck","given":"Pieter"},{"family":"Parra","given":"Constanza"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Both subsistence uses of wild algae, fungi and plants and trade in them have particular importance for low-income and marginalized peoples ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7OJ7faO3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Moreno, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Pérez?Moreno, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E35V4YCV"],"itemData":{"id":20100,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLANTS, PEOPLE, PLANET","DOI":"10.1002/ppp3.10199","ISSN":"2572-2611, 2572-2611","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Plants People Planet","language":"en","page":"471-490","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Edible mycorrhizal fungi of the world: What is their role in forest sustainability, food security, biocultural conservation and climate change?","title-short":"Edible mycorrhizal fungi of the world","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Pérez?Moreno","given":"Jesús"},{"family":"Guerin?Laguette","given":"Alexis"},{"family":"Rinaldi","given":"Andrea C."},{"family":"Yu","given":"Fuqiang"},{"family":"Verbeken","given":"Annemieke"},{"family":"Hernández?Santiago","given":"Faustino"},{"family":"Martínez?Reyes","given":"Magdalena"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, although gathered materials are used by households across the economic spectrum. Subsistence use of and trade in gathered materials is fundamental to the lives and livelihoods of indigenous peoples ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ceTj8NtD","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Isaza, 2016, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Isaza, 2016, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19197,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IP2W3ASX"],"itemData":{"id":19197,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Population ecology","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"463–476","title":"Demography of Oenocarpus bataua and implications for sustainable harvest of its fruit in western Amazon","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Isaza","given":"C."},{"family":"Martorell","given":"C."},{"family":"Cevallos","given":"D."},{"family":"Galeano","given":"G."},{"family":"Valencia","given":"R."},{"family":"Balslev","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":19198,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3GYHRUAV"],"itemData":{"id":19198,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biotropica","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"653–664","title":"Demography of Euterpe precatoria and Mauritia flexuosa in the Amazon: application of integral projection models for their harvest","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Isaza","given":"C."},{"family":"Bernal","given":"R."},{"family":"Galeano","given":"G."},{"family":"Martorell","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Be3kvXhS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Papageorgiou, Bebeli, Panitsa, & Schunko, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Papageorgiou, Bebeli, Panitsa, & Schunko, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1340,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZH3L9RVH"],"itemData":{"id":1340,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13002-020-00390-4","issue":"1","note":"publisher: BioMed Central","page":"1–23","title":"Local knowledge about sustainable harvesting and availability of wild medicinal plant species in Lemnos island, Greece","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Papageorgiou","given":"Dimitrios"},{"family":"Bebeli","given":"Penelope J"},{"family":"Panitsa","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Schunko","given":"Christoph"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and a source of empowerment for women ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"US4uJUyV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Moreno, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Pérez?Moreno, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E35V4YCV"],"itemData":{"id":20100,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLANTS, PEOPLE, PLANET","DOI":"10.1002/ppp3.10199","ISSN":"2572-2611, 2572-2611","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Plants People Planet","language":"en","page":"471-490","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Edible mycorrhizal fungi of the world: What is their role in forest sustainability, food security, biocultural conservation and climate change?","title-short":"Edible mycorrhizal fungi of the world","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Pérez?Moreno","given":"Jesús"},{"family":"Guerin?Laguette","given":"Alexis"},{"family":"Rinaldi","given":"Andrea C."},{"family":"Yu","given":"Fuqiang"},{"family":"Verbeken","given":"Annemieke"},{"family":"Hernández?Santiago","given":"Faustino"},{"family":"Martínez?Reyes","given":"Magdalena"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      A subset of the thousands of species gathered worldwide are commercially traded, with fewer still entering large-scale commodity markets. Most modeling and scenario development focuses on commercially traded species. In the case of wild algae, fungi and plants that enter large-scale commercial markets, gatherers can face the dilemma of maximizing harvest for short-term income, eventually reducing populations of the target species below commercially viable levels, or gathering lower volumes of material to sustain species populations and ensure income through time ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"loUzupl6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Barrios, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Hernández?Barrios, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19194,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U6K7E248"],"itemData":{"id":19194,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"389–401","title":"Sustainable harvesting of non?timber forest products based on ecological and economic criteria","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Hernández?Barrios","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Anten","given":"N.P."},{"family":"Martínez?Ramos","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This dilemma may be particularly acute where the price per unit of raw gathered material is low and gatherers must increase harvest volumes to meet their economic needs or goals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oIM8DuJ7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Mello, 2020)","plainCitation":"(de Mello, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7469,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TV355YM2"],"itemData":{"id":7469,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2020.102109","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"102109","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Social-ecological sustainability of non-timber forest products: A review and theoretical considerations for future research","title-short":"Social-ecological sustainability of non-timber forest products","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Mello","given":"Natália Girão Rodrigues","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Gulinck","given":"Hubert"},{"family":"Van den Broeck","given":"Pieter"},{"family":"Parra","given":"Constanza"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Further, a review of 87 cases of hunting and gathering in developing countries found that, together with high species resilience, low gross domestic product per capita and high poverty ratios were strong predictors of unsustainable outcomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HRFHtv4k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Leao, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Leao, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6191,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WK8GDKM8"],"itemData":{"id":6191,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological economics","page":"14–21","title":"Economic and biological conditions influence the sustainability of Harvest of wild animals and plants in developing countries","volume":"140","author":[{"family":"Leao","given":"Tarciso CC"},{"family":"Lobo","given":"Diele"},{"family":"Scotson","given":"Lorraine"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Over the long term unsustainable gathering adversely affects the livelihoods and wellbeing of local peoples ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4wueL8ng","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vallejo, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Vallejo, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6037,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZDHJHPU3"],"itemData":{"id":6037,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Palm heart is an important non-timber forest product obtained from various palm species in tropical forests. We studied the effect of four decades of palm heart extraction from the clonal palm Euterpe oleracea at the southern Paci?c coast of Colombia. We monitored populations that had been subject to a range of harvest intensities and used measured vital rates (survival, growth, sexual and clonal reproduction) to construct population matrix models. We then used these models to simulate several harvest scenarios and to project the population dynamics for the next 50 years. Our projections suggest that the currently implemented intensive harvest regimes – which involve up to four harvests per year – result in dramatic demographic changes, primarily affecting seedlings and adults. In addition, current harvest regimes affect the future supply of palm heart, which is projected to drop sharply during the ?rst years following harvest and fails to recover unless a number of stems are spared. Our simulations indicate that the most sustainable scenarios involve annual harvest between 50% and 75% of all harvestable stems, without any removal of small shoots from the clumps. Implementation of this regime must be accompanied by other management practices, including planning harvestable areas, marking the stems to be cut during subsequent harvests, assigning harvesters to speci?c areas, and leaving harvest residues as mulch around clumps. The degradation of populations of E. oleracea directly affects livelihoods of local people, by reducing cash income from palm heart sales and by reducing availability of palm fruits, a locally important food resource.","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2014.01.028","ISSN":"03781127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"274-284","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The fate of populations of Euterpe oleracea harvested for palm heart in Colombia","volume":"318","author":[{"family":"Vallejo","given":"Martha Isabel"},{"family":"Galeano","given":"Gloria"},{"family":"Bernal","given":"Rodrigo"},{"family":"Zuidema","given":"Pieter A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Modeling studies suggest a number of strategies to enhance the sustainability of gathering, with a focus on commercially traded species. Agroforestry may increase production to meet demand and decrease pressure on wild populations, while fair trade schemes may help to ensure equitable sharing of benefits with gatherers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Sy4ueUv2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bond\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, 2020; P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez-Negr\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}n, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Bondé, 2020; Pérez-Negrón, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19174,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6KJFBZI6"],"itemData":{"id":19174,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biota Neotropica","language":"en","title":"Scenario-modelling for the sustainable management of non-timber forest products in tropical ecosystems","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Bondé","given":"L."},{"family":"Assis","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Benavides-Gordillo","given":"S."},{"family":"Canales-Gomez","given":"E."},{"family":"Fajardo","given":"J."},{"family":"Marrón-Becerra","given":"A."},{"family":"Ament","given":"J.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":19217,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4F3DPD2A"],"itemData":{"id":19217,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–16","title":"Use of columnar cacti in the Tehuacán Valley, Mexico: perspectives for sustainable management of non-timber forest products","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Pérez-Negrón","given":"E."},{"family":"Dávila","given":"P."},{"family":"Casas","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some models indicate that multiple use forest management can increase economic returns for forest owners and identify the optimal mix of logging and gathering under current and future conditions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CntE718W","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kurttila, 2018; Miina, Kurttila, Calama, de-Miguel, & Pukkala, 2020; Mumcu Kucuker & Baskent, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Kurttila, 2018; Miina, Kurttila, Calama, de-Miguel, & Pukkala, 2020; Mumcu Kucuker & Baskent, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1380,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BREIDW6I"],"itemData":{"id":1380,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.3390/f9070417","issue":"7","note":"publisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"417","title":"Synergies and trade-offs in the production of NWFPs predicted in boreal forests","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Kurttila","given":"Mikko"},{"family":"Pukkala","given":"Timo"},{"family":"Miina","given":"Jari"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":1346,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WPEQ998A"],"itemData":{"id":1346,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Forestry Reports","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s40725-020-00130-7","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1–14","title":"Modelling Non-timber Forest Products for Forest Management Planning in Europe","author":[{"family":"Miina","given":"Jari"},{"family":"Kurttila","given":"Mikko"},{"family":"Calama","given":"Rafael"},{"family":"Miguel","given":"Sergio","non-dropping-particle":"de-"},{"family":"Pukkala","given":"Timo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":19213,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DWF98U85"],"itemData":{"id":19213,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mycorrhiza","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–11","title":"Spatial prediction of Lactarius deliciosus and Lactarius salmonicolor mushroom distribution with logistic regression models in the K?z?lcasu Planning Unit, Turkey","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Mumcu Kucuker","given":"D."},{"family":"Baskent","given":"E.Z."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Managing forests for both logging and gathering may enhance populations of gathered species. Where income from commercially traded wild fungi or plants becomes a priority for forest owners and forest managers, there is likely to be an increased emphasis on controlling access to such species.
 
5.4.3.5 Environmental
Scenarios and models, as well as empirical data, indicate that the future sustainability of gathering will be a function of interacting gathering techniques, environmental conditions and anthropogenic and biophysical drivers, including climate change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nd7Iojfz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(del Castillo, 2013; Hart\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Fredeluces, 2021; Mandle, 2015)","plainCitation":"(del Castillo, 2013; Hart?Fredeluces, 2021; Mandle, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20096,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2TXWK8BD"],"itemData":{"id":20096,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.1002/ece3.765","ISSN":"2045-7758, 2045-7758","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Evol","language":"en","page":"3935-3946","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Possible combined effects of climate change, deforestation, and harvesting on the epiphyte <i>Catopsis compacta</i> : a multidisciplinary approach","title-short":"Possible combined effects of climate change, deforestation, and harvesting on the epiphyte <i>Catopsis compacta</i>","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Castillo","given":"Rafael F.","non-dropping-particle":"del"},{"family":"Trujillo?Argueta","given":"Sonia"},{"family":"Rivera?García","given":"Raul"},{"family":"Gómez?Ocampo","given":"Zaneli"},{"family":"Mondragón?Chaparro","given":"Demetria"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",10]]}}},{"id":19191,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CJCBTM79"],"itemData":{"id":19191,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ecology","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"1133–1147","title":"Simulated Indigenous fire stewardship increases the population growth rate of an understorey herb","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Hart?Fredeluces","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."},{"family":"Lake","given":"F.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":19210,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KQF62M6Z"],"itemData":{"id":19210,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ecology","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"1032–1043","title":"Resilience of palm populations to disturbance is determined by interactive effects of fire, herbivory and harvest","volume":"103","author":[{"family":"Mandle","given":"L."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."},{"family":"Zuidema","given":"P.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These factors may interact in additive, synergistic or antagonistic ways ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IdOfDLJR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Groner, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Groner, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19190,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U72QZCK2"],"itemData":{"id":19190,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","language":"en","title":"Climate change, land cover change, and overharvesting threaten a widely used medicinal plant in South Africa","volume":"e2545","author":[{"family":"Groner","given":"V.P."},{"family":"Nicholas","given":"O."},{"family":"Mabhaudhi","given":"T."},{"family":"Slotow","given":"R."},{"family":"Akçakaya","given":"H.R."},{"family":"Mace","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Pearson","given":"R.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, producing species-, habitat- and site-specific outcomes for the sustainability of gathering. As a result, gathering regimes that are sustainable for one species may not be for another. Similarly, gathering techniques that are sustainable for a species in one location may not be so in a place where environmental conditions and drivers are significantly different.
      As previously noted, species biology (e.g., growth rate, reproductive strategy and, sometimes, population density), plays an important role in their response to gathering ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Q0BksCZF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(De Angeli, 2021; Isaza, 2016; Papageorgiou, 2020; Walsh & Douglas, 2011; Yadav, 2021)","plainCitation":"(De Angeli, 2021; Isaza, 2016; Papageorgiou, 2020; Walsh & Douglas, 2011; Yadav, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19178,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QGQ4VKLV"],"itemData":{"id":19178,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Modelling","language":"en","page":"109328","title":"Modeling the impact of wild harvest on plant–disperser mutualisms: Plant and disperser co-harvest model","volume":"439","author":[{"family":"De Angeli","given":"K."},{"family":"Abbasi","given":"E."},{"family":"Gan","given":"A."},{"family":"Ingram","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Giam","given":"X."},{"family":"Chang","given":"C.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":19197,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IP2W3ASX"],"itemData":{"id":19197,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Population ecology","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"463–476","title":"Demography of Oenocarpus bataua and implications for sustainable harvest of its fruit in western Amazon","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Isaza","given":"C."},{"family":"Martorell","given":"C."},{"family":"Cevallos","given":"D."},{"family":"Galeano","given":"G."},{"family":"Valencia","given":"R."},{"family":"Balslev","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":1340,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZH3L9RVH"],"itemData":{"id":1340,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13002-020-00390-4","issue":"1","note":"publisher: BioMed Central","page":"1–23","title":"Local knowledge about sustainable harvesting and availability of wild medicinal plant species in Lemnos island, Greece","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Papageorgiou","given":"Dimitrios"},{"family":"Bebeli","given":"Penelope J"},{"family":"Panitsa","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Schunko","given":"Christoph"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":2525,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BYDDK24W"],"itemData":{"id":2525,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Improvement in Aboriginal people’s livelihoods and economic opportunities has been a major aim of increased research and development on bush foods over the past decade. But worldwide the development of trade in non-timber forest products from natural populations has raised questions about the ecological sustainability of harvest. Trade-offs and tensions between commercialisation and cultural values have also been found. We investigated the sustainability of the small-scale commercial harvest and trade in native plant products sourced from central Australian rangelands (including Solanum centrale J.M. Black, Acacia Mill. spp.). We used semi-structured interviews with traders and Aboriginal harvesters, participant observation of trading and harvesting trips, and analysis of species and trader records. An expert Aboriginal reference group guided the project. We found no evidence of either taxa being vulnerable to over-harvest. S. centrale production is enhanced by harvesting when it co-occurs with patch-burning. Extreme fluctuations in productivity of both taxa, due to inter-annual rainfall variability, have a much greater impact on supply than harvest effects. Landscape-scale degradation (including cattle grazing and wildfire) affected ecological sustainability according to participants. By contrast, we found that sustainability of bush food trade is more strongly impacted by social and economic factors. The relationship-based links between harvesters and traders are critical to monetary trade. Harvesters and traders identified access to productive lands and narrow economic margins between costs and returns as issues for the future sustainability of harvest and trade. Harvesters and the reference group emphasised that sustaining bush harvest relies on future generations having necessary knowledge and skills; these are extremely vulnerable to loss. Aboriginal people derive multiple livelihood benefits from harvest and trade. Aboriginal custodians and harvester groups involved in recent trade are more likely to benefit from research and development investment to inter-generational knowledge and skill transfer than from investments in plant breeding and commercial horticultural development. In an inductive comparison, our study found there to be strong alignment between key findings about the strategies used by harvesters and traders in bush produce and the ‘desert system’..","container-title":"The Rangeland Journal","DOI":"10.1071/RJ11028","ISSN":"1834-7541","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Rangel. J.","language":"en","note":"publisher: CSIRO PUBLISHING","page":"395-416","source":"www.publish.csiro.au","title":"No bush foods without people: the essential human dimension to the sustainability of trade in native plant products from desert Australia","title-short":"No bush foods without people","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Walsh","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Douglas","given":"Josie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12,20]]}}},{"id":19232,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NHQG28BI"],"itemData":{"id":19232,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Modeling Earth Systems and Environment","language":"en","page":"1–20","title":"Predicting impact of climate change on geographical distribution of major NTFP species in the Central India Region","author":[{"family":"Yadav","given":"S."},{"family":"Bhattacharya","given":"P."},{"family":"Areendran","given":"G."},{"family":"Sahana","given":"M."},{"family":"Raj","given":"K."},{"family":"Sajjad","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/cita