topsis compacta</i>","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Castillo","given":"Rafael F.","non-dropping-particle":"del"},{"family":"Trujillo?Argueta","given":"Sonia"},{"family":"Rivera?García","given":"Raul"},{"family":"Gómez?Ocampo","given":"Zaneli"},{"family":"Mondragón?Chaparro","given":"Demetria"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some land cover changes and landscape management systems have been identified as enhancing the sustainability of gathering of particular species. For example, fungal biodiversity in Mediterranean scrublands is increased by carefully timed treatments including controlled burning and clearance of vegetation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VmFy3Qml","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez-Rodr\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}guez, de-Miguel, Pukkala, Oria-de-Rueda, & Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}n-Pinto, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Hernández-Rodríguez, de-Miguel, Pukkala, Oria-de-Rueda, & Martín-Pinto, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19195,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PQ7UQHZE"],"itemData":{"id":19195,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agricultural and Forest Meteorology","language":"en","page":"173–182","title":"Climate-sensitive models for mushroom yields and diversity in Cistus ladanifer scrublands","volume":"213","author":[{"family":"Hernández-Rodríguez","given":"M."},{"family":"Miguel","given":"S.","non-dropping-particle":"de-"},{"family":"Pukkala","given":"T."},{"family":"Oria-de-Rueda","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Martín-Pinto","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, while forest fragmentation has increased populations of the epiphytic bromeliad Catopsis compacta Mez. in México by opening up the canopy and increasing the area of forest perimeter ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CTu023zO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(del Castillo, 2013)","plainCitation":"(del Castillo, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20096,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2TXWK8BD"],"itemData":{"id":20096,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.1002/ece3.765","ISSN":"2045-7758, 2045-7758","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Evol","language":"en","page":"3935-3946","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Possible combined effects of climate change, deforestation, and harvesting on the epiphyte <i>Catopsis compacta</i> : a multidisciplinary approach","title-short":"Possible combined effects of climate change, deforestation, and harvesting on the epiphyte <i>Catopsis compacta</i>","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Castillo","given":"Rafael F.","non-dropping-particle":"del"},{"family":"Trujillo?Argueta","given":"Sonia"},{"family":"Rivera?García","given":"Raul"},{"family":"Gómez?Ocampo","given":"Zaneli"},{"family":"Mondragón?Chaparro","given":"Demetria"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Similar patterns hold true for macrofungi gathered for food, medicine and other purposes. Mycorrhizal fungi associated with boreal pine forests are less adapted to high-intensity wildfire than are those in Mediterranean pine forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uh2lpLDI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Franco-Manch\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}n, Salo, Oria-de-Rueda, Bonet, & Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}n-Pinto, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Franco-Manchón, Salo, Oria-de-Rueda, Bonet, & Martín-Pinto, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19185,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RHQ5F3LK"],"itemData":{"id":19185,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"309","title":"Are wildfires a threat to fungi in European pinus forests? A case study of boreal and mediterranean forests","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Franco-Manchón","given":"I."},{"family":"Salo","given":"K."},{"family":"Oria-de-Rueda","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Bonet","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Martín-Pinto","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some cases, silvicultural prescriptions can increase fruiting by edible wild fungi, although the degree of this effect depends on the extent of thinning of the forest canopy and site hydrology and temperature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fqGWEx7l","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Miina, 2020; Herrero, 2019; Kurttila, 2018; de-Miguel, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Miina, 2020; Herrero, 2019; Kurttila, 2018; de-Miguel, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1346,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WPEQ998A"],"itemData":{"id":1346,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Forestry Reports","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s40725-020-00130-7","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1–14","title":"Modelling Non-timber Forest Products for Forest Management Planning in Europe","author":[{"family":"Miina","given":"Jari"},{"family":"Kurttila","given":"Mikko"},{"family":"Calama","given":"Rafael"},{"family":"Miguel","given":"Sergio","non-dropping-particle":"de-"},{"family":"Pukkala","given":"Timo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":19192,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I2HV79RL"],"itemData":{"id":19192,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"206","title":"Predicting mushroom productivity from long-term field-data series in Mediterranean Pinus pinaster Ait. forests in the context of climate change","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Herrero","given":"C."},{"family":"Berraondo","given":"I."},{"family":"Bravo","given":"F."},{"family":"Pando","given":"V."},{"family":"Ordóñez","given":"C."},{"family":"Olaizola","given":"J."},{"family":"Rueda","given":"J.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":1380,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BREIDW6I"],"itemData":{"id":1380,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.3390/f9070417","issue":"7","note":"publisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"417","title":"Synergies and trade-offs in the production of NWFPs predicted in boreal forests","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Kurttila","given":"Mikko"},{"family":"Pukkala","given":"Timo"},{"family":"Miina","given":"Jari"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":1679,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CUWXKULS"],"itemData":{"id":1679,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The aim of this study was to predict the effect of forest management intensity on mushroom productivity at the landscape level by means of a model-based scenario analysis. The study area was Catalonia region, north-eastern Iberian Peninsula. Mushroom yield models were developed for the most common pine-dominated forest ecosystems. The models accounted for the effect of site and stand structure on mushroom occurrence and yield. The mushroom yield models and individual-tree growth models were used in continuous cover forestry simulation and optimization to assess the impact of alternative regional forest management intensity scenarios on landscape-level mushroom productivity. The baseline scenario was defined as the estimated current forest harvesting intensity in Catalonia (i.e., 25% of annual forest growth). The time frame was 30yr. The current average productivity of valuable mushrooms is 14kgha?1yr?1 (4600tonnesyr?1). Under the current forest management intensity, a loss of 220tonnesyr?1 (5%) in mushroom production at the regional level can be expected. In the absence of forest management, the loss in productivity can attain almost 500tonnesyr?1 (11%). With forest harvesting intensity similar to the average in Europe, an increase of 100tonnesyr?1 (2%) in mushroom production could be expected. If forest harvesting was equal to 100% of annual forest growth, an increase of 262tonnesyr?1 (6%) could be expected. Mushroom productivity increases with increasing forest management intensity. Low forest management intensities may result in a progressive reduction in mushroom yield. Intermediate management intensities would maintain the current mushroom productivity. Sustained yield harvesting policy would contribute to increasing mushroom productivity in continuous cover forestry.","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.foreco.2014.07.014","ISSN":"0378-1127","page":"218-227","title":"Impact of forest management intensity on landscape-level mushroom productivity: A regional model-based scenario analysis","volume":"330","author":[{"family":"Miguel","given":"Sergio","non-dropping-particle":"de-"},{"family":"Bonet","given":"José Antonio"},{"family":"Pukkala","given":"Timo"},{"family":"Aragón","given":"Juan Martínez","dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Climate change will affect most of the variables that will determine the sustainability of gathering in the future. Many studies have modeled the probable occurrence of suitable habitats for individual species or taxa of gathered species under climate change scenarios ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VJxJgwLC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Heubes, 2012; Miina, 2020; Rana, 2020; Sinasson, Shackleton, Teka, & Sinsin, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Heubes, 2012; Miina, 2020; Rana, 2020; Sinasson, Shackleton, Teka, & Sinsin, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19196,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ALEADIQH"],"itemData":{"id":19196,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic botany","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"383–397","title":"Impact of future climate and land use change on non-timber forest product provision in Benin, West Africa: linking niche-based modeling with ecosystem service values","volume":"66","author":[{"family":"Heubes","given":"J."},{"family":"Heubach","given":"K."},{"family":"Schmidt","given":"M."},{"family":"Wittig","given":"R."},{"family":"Zizka","given":"G."},{"family":"Nuppenau","given":"E.A."},{"family":"Hahn","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":1346,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WPEQ998A"],"itemData":{"id":1346,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Forestry Reports","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s40725-020-00130-7","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1–14","title":"Modelling Non-timber Forest Products for Forest Management Planning in Europe","author":[{"family":"Miina","given":"Jari"},{"family":"Kurttila","given":"Mikko"},{"family":"Calama","given":"Rafael"},{"family":"Miguel","given":"Sergio","non-dropping-particle":"de-"},{"family":"Pukkala","given":"Timo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":19219,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7PWCZF9W"],"itemData":{"id":19219,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","language":"en","page":"106435","title":"Climate-change threats to distribution, habitats, sustainability and conservation of highly traded medicinal and aromatic plants in Nepal","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Rana","given":"S.K."},{"family":"Rana","given":"H.K."},{"family":"Ranjitkar","given":"S."},{"family":"Ghimire","given":"S.K."},{"family":"Gurmachhan","given":"C.M."},{"family":"O'Neill","given":"A.R."},{"family":"Sun","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":19223,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NMMZLSFX"],"itemData":{"id":19223,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"01527","title":"Ecological patterns and effectiveness of protected areas in the preservation of Mimusops species’ habitats under climate change","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Sinasson","given":"G.K."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Teka","given":"O."},{"family":"Sinsin","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, as well as the effects of potential changes in precipitation and/or temperature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lqS7J9YB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ardestani & Ghahfarrokhi, 2021; Kumar, 2021; Yadav, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Ardestani & Ghahfarrokhi, 2021; Kumar, 2021; Yadav, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19169,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LC9XWDVT"],"itemData":{"id":19169,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"01488","title":"Ensemble species distribution modeling of Salvia hydrangea under future climate change scenarios in Central Zagros Mountains, Iran","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Ardestani","given":"E.G."},{"family":"Ghahfarrokhi","given":"Z.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":19206,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VXCIJZ7H"],"itemData":{"id":19206,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Research on Medicinal and Aromatic Plants","language":"en","page":"100309","title":"Predicting the current and future suitable habitat distribution of the medicinal tree Oroxylum indicum (L.) Kurz in India","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Kumar","given":"D."},{"family":"Rawat","given":"S."},{"family":"Joshi","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":19232,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NHQG28BI"],"itemData":{"id":19232,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Modeling Earth Systems and Environment","language":"en","page":"1–20","title":"Predicting impact of climate change on geographical distribution of major NTFP species in the Central India Region","author":[{"family":"Yadav","given":"S."},{"family":"Bhattacharya","given":"P."},{"family":"Areendran","given":"G."},{"family":"Sahana","given":"M."},{"family":"Raj","given":"K."},{"family":"Sajjad","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Results suggest that some species will benefit from expanded distribution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6lxJ2vDv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chitale, 2018; Yadav, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Chitale, 2018; Yadav, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19177,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M9FLNZ44"],"itemData":{"id":19177,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nepal. Resources","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"66","title":"Assessing the impacts of climate change on distribution of major non-timber forest plants in Chitwan Annapurna Landscape","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Chitale","given":"V."},{"family":"Silwal","given":"R."},{"family":"Matin","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":19232,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NHQG28BI"],"itemData":{"id":19232,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Modeling Earth Systems and Environment","language":"en","page":"1–20","title":"Predicting impact of climate change on geographical distribution of major NTFP species in the Central India Region","author":[{"family":"Yadav","given":"S."},{"family":"Bhattacharya","given":"P."},{"family":"Areendran","given":"G."},{"family":"Sahana","given":"M."},{"family":"Raj","given":"K."},{"family":"Sajjad","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the distribution of other species can be expected to decrease ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NXnfNmLO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ardestani & Ghahfarrokhi, 2021; Chitale, 2018; Uprety, 2012; Yadav, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Ardestani & Ghahfarrokhi, 2021; Chitale, 2018; Uprety, 2012; Yadav, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19169,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LC9XWDVT"],"itemData":{"id":19169,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"01488","title":"Ensemble species distribution modeling of Salvia hydrangea under future climate change scenarios in Central Zagros Mountains, Iran","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Ardestani","given":"E.G."},{"family":"Ghahfarrokhi","given":"Z.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":19177,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M9FLNZ44"],"itemData":{"id":19177,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nepal. Resources","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"66","title":"Assessing the impacts of climate change on distribution of major non-timber forest plants in Chitwan Annapurna Landscape","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Chitale","given":"V."},{"family":"Silwal","given":"R."},{"family":"Matin","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":4468,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9DTX9R8"],"itemData":{"id":4468,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Traditional knowledge has become a topic of considerable interest within the research and development environment. The contribution of traditional knowledge to conservation and management is increasingly recognized, and implementation endeavours are underway in several countries. The current scale of ecosystem degradation underscores the need for restoration interventions. It is increasingly recognized that successful ecological restoration depends on effective coordination of science and traditional ecological knowledge. This paper synthesizes the literature to evaluate the present and potential contribution of traditional knowledge to ecological restoration. Despite a growing number of articles published on traditional knowledge, only a few have addressed its contributions to ecological restoration per se. The main contributions of traditional knowledge to ecological restoration are in construction of reference ecosystems, particularly when historical information is not available; species selection for restoration plantations; site selection for restoration; knowledge about historical land management practices; management of invasive species; and post-restoration monitoring. Traditional knowledge and science are complementary and should be used in conjunction in ecological restoration projects. Incorporation of traditional knowledge can contribute to build a strong partnership for the successful implementation of restoration projects and increase their social acceptability, economical feasibility, and ecological viability.","call-number":"Sub180 or Esub20k","container-title":"Écoscience","DOI":"10.2980/19-3-3530","ISSN":"1195-6860","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Écoscience","page":"225–237","title":"Contribution of traditional knowledge to ecological restoration: Practices and applications","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Uprety","given":"Yadav"},{"family":"Asselin","given":"Hugo"},{"family":"Bergeron","given":"Yves"},{"family":"Doyon","given":"Frédérik"},{"family":"Boucher","given":"Jean-François"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",9]]}}},{"id":19232,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NHQG28BI"],"itemData":{"id":19232,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Modeling Earth Systems and Environment","language":"en","page":"1–20","title":"Predicting impact of climate change on geographical distribution of major NTFP species in the Central India Region","author":[{"family":"Yadav","given":"S."},{"family":"Bhattacharya","given":"P."},{"family":"Areendran","given":"G."},{"family":"Sahana","given":"M."},{"family":"Raj","given":"K."},{"family":"Sajjad","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, while some will remain largely stable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XkUsIbjs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Asase & Peterson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Asase & Peterson, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19170,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XPC6GRZ4"],"itemData":{"id":19170,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Research on Medicinal and Aromatic Plants","language":"en","page":"100206","title":"Predicted impacts of global climate change on the geographic distribution of an invaluable African medicinal plant resource, Alstonia boonei De Wild","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Asase","given":"A."},{"family":"Peterson","given":"A.T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A shift in range to higher latitudes and altitudes is expected for some species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6XhJGkbt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ardestani & Ghahfarrokhi, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Ardestani & Ghahfarrokhi, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19169,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LC9XWDVT"],"itemData":{"id":19169,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"01488","title":"Ensemble species distribution modeling of Salvia hydrangea under future climate change scenarios in Central Zagros Mountains, Iran","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Ardestani","given":"E.G."},{"family":"Ghahfarrokhi","given":"Z.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Climate change may also affect the use of fire as a landscape management tool, as well as the frequency and severity of wildfires, which are expected to increase, with attendant effects on populations of gathered species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XqRN8IFv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Franco-Manch\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}n, 2019; Hart\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Fredeluces, 2021; Sinasson, 2021; Varghese, 2015; Walsh & Douglas, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Franco-Manchón, 2019; Hart?Fredeluces, 2021; Sinasson, 2021; Varghese, 2015; Walsh & Douglas, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19185,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RHQ5F3LK"],"itemData":{"id":19185,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"309","title":"Are wildfires a threat to fungi in European pinus forests? A case study of boreal and mediterranean forests","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Franco-Manchón","given":"I."},{"family":"Salo","given":"K."},{"family":"Oria-de-Rueda","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Bonet","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Martín-Pinto","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":19191,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CJCBTM79"],"itemData":{"id":19191,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ecology","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"1133–1147","title":"Simulated Indigenous fire stewardship increases the population growth rate of an understorey herb","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Hart?Fredeluces","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."},{"family":"Lake","given":"F.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":19223,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NMMZLSFX"],"itemData":{"id":19223,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"01527","title":"Ecological patterns and effectiveness of protected areas in the preservation of Mimusops species’ habitats under climate change","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Sinasson","given":"G.K."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Teka","given":"O."},{"family":"Sinsin","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":17777,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVLWWHTI"],"itemData":{"id":17777,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS One","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"0119634","title":"Assessing the effects of multiple stressors on the recruitment of fruit harvested trees in a tropical dry forest, Western Ghats, India","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Varghese","given":"A."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."},{"family":"Mandle","given":"L."},{"family":"Nath","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":2525,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BYDDK24W"],"itemData":{"id":2525,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Improvement in Aboriginal people’s livelihoods and economic opportunities has been a major aim of increased research and development on bush foods over the past decade. But worldwide the development of trade in non-timber forest products from natural populations has raised questions about the ecological sustainability of harvest. Trade-offs and tensions between commercialisation and cultural values have also been found. We investigated the sustainability of the small-scale commercial harvest and trade in native plant products sourced from central Australian rangelands (including Solanum centrale J.M. Black, Acacia Mill. spp.). We used semi-structured interviews with traders and Aboriginal harvesters, participant observation of trading and harvesting trips, and analysis of species and trader records. An expert Aboriginal reference group guided the project. We found no evidence of either taxa being vulnerable to over-harvest. S. centrale production is enhanced by harvesting when it co-occurs with patch-burning. Extreme fluctuations in productivity of both taxa, due to inter-annual rainfall variability, have a much greater impact on supply than harvest effects. Landscape-scale degradation (including cattle grazing and wildfire) affected ecological sustainability according to participants. By contrast, we found that sustainability of bush food trade is more strongly impacted by social and economic factors. The relationship-based links between harvesters and traders are critical to monetary trade. Harvesters and traders identified access to productive lands and narrow economic margins between costs and returns as issues for the future sustainability of harvest and trade. Harvesters and the reference group emphasised that sustaining bush harvest relies on future generations having necessary knowledge and skills; these are extremely vulnerable to loss. Aboriginal people derive multiple livelihood benefits from harvest and trade. Aboriginal custodians and harvester groups involved in recent trade are more likely to benefit from research and development investment to inter-generational knowledge and skill transfer than from investments in plant breeding and commercial horticultural development. In an inductive comparison, our study found there to be strong alignment between key findings about the strategies used by harvesters and traders in bush produce and the ‘desert system’..","container-title":"The Rangeland Journal","DOI":"10.1071/RJ11028","ISSN":"1834-7541","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Rangel. J.","language":"en","note":"publisher: CSIRO PUBLISHING","page":"395-416","source":"www.publish.csiro.au","title":"No bush foods without people: the essential human dimension to the sustainability of trade in native plant products from desert Australia","title-short":"No bush foods without people","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Walsh","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Douglas","given":"Josie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, different modeling approaches may produce divergent results about the impacts of fire on specific species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aps090o30a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Klimas, 2017)","plainCitation":"(C. Klimas, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":20196,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GNBMIY5P"],"itemData":{"id":20196,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Science","DOI":"10.5849/FS-2017-001","ISSN":"0015-749X","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Science","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Multi-Model Projections for Evaluating Sustainable Timber and Seed Harvest of <i>Carapa guianensis</i>","URL":"http://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.5849/FS-2017-001","author":[{"family":"Klimas","given":"Christie"},{"family":"Cropper, Jr.","given":"Wendell"},{"family":"Kainer","given":"Karen"},{"family":"Oliveira Wadt","given":"Lúcia","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Models again suggest that outcomes of fire are a function of interactions with other factors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WZqFWdtE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mandle, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Mandle, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19210,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KQF62M6Z"],"itemData":{"id":19210,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ecology","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"1032–1043","title":"Resilience of palm populations to disturbance is determined by interactive effects of fire, herbivory and harvest","volume":"103","author":[{"family":"Mandle","given":"L."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."},{"family":"Zuidema","given":"P.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
 
5.4.3.6 Political
Although we did not identify models or scenarios that explicitly address the outcomes of policies and governance for the sustainability of gathering, many have clear policy implications. Models and scenarios help identify needs and opportunities for policy to support the social and ecological sustainability of gathering. The likelihood of shifting ranges for gathered species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CSgbX7Zk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ardestani & Ghahfarrokhi, 2021; Sinasson, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Ardestani & Ghahfarrokhi, 2021; Sinasson, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19169,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LC9XWDVT"],"itemData":{"id":19169,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"01488","title":"Ensemble species distribution modeling of Salvia hydrangea under future climate change scenarios in Central Zagros Mountains, Iran","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Ardestani","given":"E.G."},{"family":"Ghahfarrokhi","given":"Z.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":19223,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NMMZLSFX"],"itemData":{"id":19223,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"01527","title":"Ecological patterns and effectiveness of protected areas in the preservation of Mimusops species’ habitats under climate change","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Sinasson","given":"G.K."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Teka","given":"O."},{"family":"Sinsin","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} makes it clear that existing governance regimes such as protected areas may no longer encompass important populations. Similarly, some species may migrate outside the territories of indigenous peoples and local communities, depriving them of important livelihood and cultural resources. Policies that support the contributions of gathering to food security and community wellbeing will benefit both people and conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AWzLQNM7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Campbell, 2021; Kor, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Campbell, 2021; Kor, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19175,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NW6JQYX2"],"itemData":{"id":19175,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Sustainable Food Systems","language":"en","page":"150","title":"Wild food harvest, food security and biodiversity conservation in Jamaica: As case study of the Millbank farming region","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Campbell","given":"D."},{"family":"Moulton","given":"A."},{"family":"Barker","given":"D."},{"family":"Malcolm","given":"T."},{"family":"Scott","given":"L."},{"family":"Spence","given":"A."},{"family":"Wallace","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":19205,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TEUB24GR"],"itemData":{"id":19205,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ambio","issue":"9","language":"en","page":"1681–1697","title":"Sustainability of wild plant use in the Andean Community of South America","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Kor","given":"L."},{"family":"Homewood","given":"K."},{"family":"Dawson","given":"T.P."},{"family":"Diazgranados","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The results of several models also highlight current and likely future mismatches between regulations and other measures necessary to assure sustainable gathering ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RZtkMggE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Mello, 2020; Hern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Barrios, 2015)","plainCitation":"(de Mello, 2020; Hernández?Barrios, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7469,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TV355YM2"],"itemData":{"id":7469,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2020.102109","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"102109","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Social-ecological sustainability of non-timber forest products: A review and theoretical considerations for future research","title-short":"Social-ecological sustainability of non-timber forest products","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Mello","given":"Natália Girão Rodrigues","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Gulinck","given":"Hubert"},{"family":"Van den Broeck","given":"Pieter"},{"family":"Parra","given":"Constanza"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}},{"id":19194,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U6K7E248"],"itemData":{"id":19194,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"389–401","title":"Sustainable harvesting of non?timber forest products based on ecological and economic criteria","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Hernández?Barrios","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Anten","given":"N.P."},{"family":"Martínez?Ramos","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, while assisting in the identification of locations where harvest regulations and monitoring can be especially effective ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"U8jTVE5N","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Franco-Maass, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Franco-Maass, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19184,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AEYY6TNZ"],"itemData":{"id":19184,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"239–250","title":"A local knowledge-based approach to predict anthropic harvesting pressure zones of wild edible mushrooms as a tool for forest conservation in Central Mexico","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Franco-Maass","given":"S."},{"family":"Burrola-Aguilar","given":"C."},{"family":"Arana-Gabriel","given":"Y."},{"family":"García-Almaraz","given":"L.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, as well as species that will benefit from strengthened legal and institutional frameworks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aR5oXrGI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garcia-Barreda, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Garcia-Barreda, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6826,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4GX7DAUC"],"itemData":{"id":6826,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-017-0973-6","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"535-544","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Black Truffle Harvesting in Spanish Forests: Trends, Current Policies and Practices, and Implications on its Sustainability","title-short":"Black Truffle Harvesting in Spanish Forests","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Garcia-Barreda","given":"Sergi"},{"family":"Forcadell","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Sánchez","given":"Sergio"},{"family":"Martín-Santafé","given":"María"},{"family":"Marco","given":"Pedro"},{"family":"Camarero","given":"J. Julio"},{"family":"Reyna","given":"Santiago"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and flexible management policies and plans tailored to species and context ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EzSarqgL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Delgado-Lemus, 2014; Franco-Maass, 2016; Garcia-Barreda, 2018; Kor, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Delgado-Lemus, 2014; Franco-Maass, 2016; Garcia-Barreda, 2018; Kor, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19182,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R9RREZT5"],"i