A variety of policy options have been introduced to control, or support, the sustainable use of wild species (see preceding section 6.4). Evaluating the effectiveness of these policy options is an important stage in the policy cycle ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1nj3c8r7j6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Giorgi, 2017; E. Young & Quinn, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Giorgi, 2017; E. Young & Quinn, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34013,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MHEHXE8Z"],"itemData":{"id":34013,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"CECAN Report. Available at: https://www. cecan. ac. uk/resources","source":"Google Scholar","title":"How to improve the evaluation of complex systems to better inform policymaking learning from evaluating Defra’s reward & recognition fund","author":[{"family":"Giorgi","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":34014,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7GP9FBSH"],"itemData":{"id":34014,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Open Society Institute, Budapest","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Writing effective public policy papers","author":[{"family":"Young","given":"Eóin"},{"family":"Quinn","given":"Lisa"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Effectiveness refers to whether a policy works as intended and meets the purpose for which it was designed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a10khlugjip","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sadler, 1996)","plainCitation":"(Sadler, 1996)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34390,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E5GH4SFY"],"itemData":{"id":34390,"type":"book","call-number":"TD194.6 .S24 1996","event-place":"Ottawa","ISBN":"978-0-662-24702-9","number-of-pages":"10","publisher":"Canadian Environmental Assessment Agency","publisher-place":"Ottawa","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Environmental assessment in a changing world: evaluating practice to improve performance","title-short":"Environmental assessment in a changing world","author":[{"family":"Sadler","given":"Barry"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Canadian Environmental Assessment Agency","given":""},{"family":"International Association for Impact Assessment","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Policy effectiveness can be assessed in terms of objectives, outcomes and impacts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a19kfm1a2a6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Broc, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Broc, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34401,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/97P2M85I"],"itemData":{"id":34401,"type":"document","title":"Evaluation into Practice – Lessons learnt from 23 Evaluations of Energy Efficiency Policies","URL":"https://epatee.eu/case-studies","author":[{"family":"Broc","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Oikonomou","given":"V."},{"family":"Dragovic","given":"M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This section synthesizes the evidence on policy effectiveness for the sustainable use of wild species, evaluates the causal effects of specific policy combinations or programs as well as the conditions under which this effect arises. This chapter focuses on disentangling the impacts and effects resulting from different interventions in diverse contexts. Establishing when and why particular policy approaches are most likely to succeed is key to enabling the sustainable use of wild species. Because effectiveness is context specific, variables through time, practice, place, and culture, are considered. The concept of “enabling conditions” is used, which centers on conditions that facilitate approaches to address social and ecological challenges. They can be defined as factors that increase the likelihood of an intended change in the governance approach, strategy, or management regime. The presence of enabling conditions can facilitate the emergence of a particular environmental policy. In contrast constraining conditions are, factors that create barriers to effective management and policy implementation. These may comprise the absence of key enabling conditions or arise independently ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2uhonphsr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Huber-Stearns, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Huber-Stearns, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34382,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/USA5QU86"],"itemData":{"id":34382,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"1","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Social-ecological enabling conditions for payments for ecosystem services","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Huber-Stearns","given":"Heidi R."},{"family":"Bennett","given":"Drew E."},{"family":"Posner","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Richards","given":"Ryan C."},{"family":"Fair","given":"Jenn Hoyle"},{"family":"Cousins","given":"Stella JM"},{"family":"Romulo","given":"Chelsie L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Assessing policy effectiveness contributes to the experts' understanding of what worked as planned and provides input to the redesign or improvement of policies at the stage of policy evaluation. It contributes to narrowing the knowledge gap and supporting evidence-based decision making ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2k4ib2ubif","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Artelle, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Artelle, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34395,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SYKB8IH7"],"itemData":{"id":34395,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.aao0167","issue":"3","note":"publisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science","page":"eaao0167","source":"science.org (Atypon)","title":"Hallmarks of science missing from North American wildlife management","URL":"https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.aao0167","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Artelle","given":"Kyle A."},{"family":"Reynolds","given":"John D."},{"family":"Treves","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Walsh","given":"Jessica C."},{"family":"Paquet","given":"Paul C."},{"family":"Darimont","given":"Chris T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
       Taking advantage of the multi-sector approach Chapter 6’s authors adopt in this chapter the analyses of effectiveness is based on a combination of: the general review; the mixed methods systematic review (based on selected case studies, see data management report at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4663236)ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"osfURxVZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"\\\\uldash{(Hicks, 2022)}","plainCitation":"(Hicks, 2022)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"RflDKERw/fFBtmZtu","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PK46XH4J"],"itemData":{"id":27987,"type":"report","abstract":"This data management report explains the process for conducting the systematic review on policy effectiveness for the sustainable use of wild species, as part of Chapter 6. Policy options for governing sustainable use of wild species, in the the thematic assessment of the sustainable use of wild species of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services.","language":"en","note":"version: draft\\nDOI: 10.5281/ZENODO.4663236","publisher":"Zenodo","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"IPBES Sustainable Use of Wild Species Assessment - Chapter 6 - Data management report for the systematic review on policy effectiveness for the sustainable use of wild species","URL":"https://zenodo.org/record/4663236","author":[{"family":"Hicks","given":"Christina"},{"family":"Park","given":"Mi Sun"},{"family":"Avila-Foucat","given":"Véronique Sophie"},{"family":"Dhyani","given":"Shalini"},{"family":"Islas","given":"Camila Alvez"},{"family":"Kolding","given":"Jeppe"},{"family":"Reidl","given":"Paola Mosig"},{"family":"Raab","given":"Kristina"},{"family":"Shkaruba","given":"Anton"},{"family":"Skandrani","given":"Zina"},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2022"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; and boxes selected to be illustrative of key points (see methodological approach section 6.2); that in combination draw on the best available evidence providing guidance to policymakers that can and should be updated over time. 
       Many studies made an explicit effort to identify and describe the enabling or constraining conditions for the effectiveness of policy instruments (one example being ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"afn50c58od","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McCay, 2014)","plainCitation":"(McCay, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34281,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q7KBTGPF"],"itemData":{"id":34281,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ten ?shery cooperatives of the Paci?c coast of Mexico were studied to examine reasons for successful community-based management of the ?shery commons. The cooperatives hold exclusive rights to ‘concession’ territories for major ?sheries and are linked by geographic adjacency and through a federation. The case study underscores the role of factors such as smallness of scale; the productivity, visibility and legibility of the resources and ?sheries involved; clarity of social and territorial boundaries; adjacency and linkages among territorial units; a strong sense of community. The cooperatives also made considerable investments in attaining high levels of knowledge, leadership, transparent and democratic decision-making, and “vigilance,” or enforcement of the rules and the running of the organization. The study also shows the workings of windows of opportunity and experience with environmental change in the development of strong and adaptive capacities for co-management between local organizations and government agencies. Although particular histories and larger legal, political, and cultural contexts matter, the Mexican case supports arguments for greater community-level engagement in “catch share” and territorial management throughout the Paci?c.","container-title":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"11","source":"Zotero","title":"Cooperatives, concessions, and co-management on the Pacific coast of Mexico","author":[{"family":"McCay","given":"Bonnie J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, in many cases, and as anecdotally described by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2d3up7ghcd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jentoft, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Jentoft, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":398,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MKMIY36A"],"itemData":{"id":398,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Fisheries Co-management: The Way Forward","event-place":"Penang, Malaysia: Worldfish Center","event-title":"Proceedings of the International Workshop on Fisheries Co-management.","publisher-place":"Penang, Malaysia: Worldfish Center","title":"Fisheries Co-management Research and the Case Study Method","author":[{"family":"Jentoft","given":"Svein"}],"editor":[{"family":"Viswanathan","given":"Kuperan"},{"family":"Mahfuzuddin","given":"Ahmed"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, sometimes it takes an outside perspective to understand the inside ongoing of a particular situation, or to identify key factors that seemed obvious before, because a culturally embedded institution seemed to be a given. For example, in countries where legal compliance is high, compliance is not usually highlighted as an enabling condition. Information access, technical infrastructure and capabilities may be other such factors that are not emphasized due to their prevalence. Therefore, in drawing information from across case studies on what conditions exist where policies are more effective, and conversely what conditions exist where policies are less effective, we are able identify patterns that enable or constrain policy effectiveness. 
       Most policies have the potential to be successful, however they also have the potential to fail and even exacerbate existing social and ecological conditions. The success of a given policy depends on the context and history in which it is applied. We identified a number of enabling and constraining conditions associated with the application of different policy instruments across practices. Enabling and constraining conditions were related to the values, norms, and principles (see section 6.5.1, 6.6.1, 6.6.2, 6.6.3), the rules and institutions (see section 6.5.2, 6.5.3), and the power asymmetries (See section 6.5.4) of the governing system and system to be governed (sensu the interactive governance framework Figure 6.1). These resulted in seven overarching constraining and seven associated enabling conditions (Figure 6.4). When rights are not recognized, power relations are unbalanced, and policies ignore social dimensions whether of the local context social, or policy objectives the effectiveness of policies are constrained. However, in contrast governing systems that are inclusive and participatory, recognize and respect diverse forms of knowledge, and ensure benefits (and costs) are shared equally can result in more effective sustainable use policies. Similarly, when policy approaches overlook the historical context, focus on a limited number of commonly used instruments, rules inadvertently criminalize the most marginalized, and policies are not aligned across scale nor do they facilitate interactions the effectiveness of policies are constrained. In contrast, when governance institutions are robust, sustainable use policies are supported by broader policies, instruments are tailored to the local context, and rules and instruments are adaptive and processes democratic, sustainable use policies are more effective (Figure 6.4). 
       
       
Figure 6.4 Conditions and characteristics that enable (green) or constrain (red) sustainable use policies. CC-BY
       
6.5.1 Governance characteristics that enable sustainable use

6.5.1.1 Inclusive & participatory process

Policy tends to be more effective when developed through inclusive and participatory processes that involve representative leaders, transparent institutions, and community-based approaches. Legitimate participatory processes that involve a more equal balance of power, tend to support more effective policies because they draw on diverse perspectives and forms of knowledge, support collaboration, and increase buy-in. This in turn leads to better self-regulation, particularly for high value species.
	The participatory model is built on the premise that local involvement is essential to achieve social justice and the long-term protection of the natural environment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a6emgbblmj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Meola, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Meola, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":355,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QRE94CDT"],"itemData":{"id":355,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Though women's empowerment remains a central topic in feminist scholarship and a growing focus within international development, leadership has not received much attention, particularly in the context of natural resource management. Interventions tend to focus on participation rather than leadership as a means of empowerment. The introduction of a participatory conservation program to the Mamirauá Sustainable Development Reserve in Amazonas, Brazil1 has had a striking impact on gender relations, increasing not only women's participation in organized groups but also female leadership in social, economic, and political spheres. This paper examines how rural women succeed in overcoming structural obstacles to leadership. Through field observation, focus groups, and semi-structured interviews, I investigate the opportunities for leadership, the environmental, and social factors that condition women's ability to assume leadership roles, and the assistance they do or do not receive in overcoming barriers. The paper concludes with a discussion of how the social structures that shape women's lives vary between leader and non-leader categories and how conservation programing might be altered to increase both women's participation and leadership. 1. Names of individuals and villages have been changed to protect the identity of research participants. Names of regional urban centers and Mamirauá Sectors are real.","container-title":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","DOI":"10.1080/14728028.2013.798947","ISSN":"1472-8028","issue":"2","page":"106–123","title":"Navigating gender structure: women's leadership in a Brazilian participatory conservation project","title-short":"Navigating gender structure","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/14728028.2013.798947","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Meola","given":"Catherine A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Full and inclusive consultations encourage exchange and understanding, and provide awareness and opportunities for indigenous peoples and local communities to share local and scientific knowledge among relevant actors. Legitimate and effective engagement can also help build trust between actors, which is key for the long-term success of sustainable use initiatives. Multi-tiered systems of regulation that incorporate statutory and customary/local rules of use distribute responsibilities among the actors best able to meet them while participatory development of regulations motivates more effective enforcement at a local level. Evidence from the gathering case studies found that local communities are more flexible in adapting their management approaches for gathering wild species including high value species irrespective of the legal setting of the area, highlighting potential for adaptive management. Participation is achieved when all actors are included, and differences that exist across genders, identities, and abilities are taken into consideration.
	Indeed, within terrestrial animal harvesting case studies, effective policy implementation was directly related to multi-actor involvement from the beginning of the policy process (e.g., Box 6.4). The successful terrestrial animal harvesting case studies established institutions including government structure, which monitored the regulation and application of norms and rules for conservation and sustainable use. These coincided with a mode of co-governing, through which all actors involved participated in the decision-making process and other activities. There was a similar emphasis in the fisheries case studies, on exchange, dialogue, and conflict resolution. Co-management was found to be more effective where transparency, fairness, leadership, and conflict resolution mechanisms were present.
 
Box 6.4 Inclusion of actors across multiple scales enables sustainability: Morelet’s crocodile (Crocodylus moreletii) skins in Mexico

The Morelet’s crocodile (Crocodylus moreletii) is distributed in Mexico, Belize and Guatemala. In Mexico it is found on the slope of the Gulf of Mexico and the Yucatan Peninsula ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"av9pkg4o7i","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Platt, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Platt, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":397,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KXBK4AC6"],"itemData":{"id":397,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Crocodile Specialist Group: Darwin.","container-title":"Crocodiles: Status Survey and Conservation Action Plan. Third Edition","language":"en","page":"5","source":"Zotero","title":"Morelet’s Crocodile Crocodylus moreletii","author":[{"family":"Platt","given":"Steven G"},{"family":"Sigler","given":"Luis"},{"family":"Rainwater","given":"Thomas R"}],"editor":[{"family":"Manolis","given":"S.C."},{"family":"Stevenson","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Morelet’s crocodile have historically been overharvested. In the 1970s, because of overhunting and unregulated skin trade, Morelet's crocodile populations were threatened with extinction. This led Mexico to prohibit the commercial harvest of wild individuals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1uu8k2k4qs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(SEMARNAP, 1999)","plainCitation":"(SEMARNAP, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":396,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M5K7SFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":396,"type":"report","language":"Spanish","number":"INE/SEMARNAP","page":"107","title":"Proyecto para la Conservación y Aprovechamiento Sustentable de los Crocodylia en México (COMACROM)","URL":"https://sistemamid.com/panel/uploads/biblioteca/1/59/74/76/85/3474.pdf","author":[{"family":"SEMARNAP","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
       Consequently, Mexico launched a pilot project to support the conservation of these crocodiles based on scientific evidence, monitoring, and regulated trade. In 2017, the “Cocodrilos Chacchoben” project was sustainably and legally harvesting wild C. moreletii eggs, that were later sold to grow in optimal conditions to reach commercial sizes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"arqi50lail","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mosig, Anta\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}o,, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Mosig, Antaño,, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":394,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SXEE3UWA"],"itemData":{"id":394,"type":"speech","genre":"Poster presented at 23 Meeting of SBSSTA (CBD). Canadá, 25-29 November 2019","title":"Promoting the Conservation of Mortelet’s crocodile (Crocodylus moreletii) by sustainably managing it through community-based ranching activities in Quintana Roo, Mexico","URL":"https://www.biodiversidad.gob.mx/media/1/planeta/cites/images/postercocodriloSBSTTA.jpg","author":[{"family":"Mosig","given":"P."},{"family":"Antaño","given":"L."},{"family":"Guerrero","given":"S."},{"family":"Cruz","given":"A."},{"family":"Torres","given":"L.A."},{"family":"Coral","given":"R."},{"family":"Martínez","given":"I"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A key factor for the success of the pilot project was the involvement of and collaboration with multiple actors at various scales of governance, from international to local communities.
		The Morelet’s crocodile monitoring program Mexico-Belize-Guatemala ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"anckl1g6pi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}nchez-Herrera, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Sánchez-Herrera, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34297,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6TTGEJAE"],"itemData":{"id":34297,"type":"book","event-place":"S.l.","ISBN":"978-607-7607-53-3","language":"es","note":"OCLC: 1111225791","publisher":"s.n.","publisher-place":"S.l.","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Programa de monitoreo del Cocodrilo de pantano (Crocodylus moreletii) México, Belice y Guatemala","URL":"https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/title/116577","author":[{"family":"Sánchez-Herrera","given":"Oscar"},{"family":"López-Segurajáuregui","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Ortíz de la Huerta","given":"Alejandra"},{"family":"Benítez-Díaz","given":"Hesiquio"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2018",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, was implemented in Mexico for five breeding seasons (2011-2015). Over this period of time, an increase of the average estimated number of wild Morelet’s crocodile was observed: from 44,890 in 2011, to 104,815 in 2015; with a general average for the 5 years of about 74,000 individuals, with an encounter rate of 3.2 ± 1.4 (ind/km), along a total of 22,833 km of potential habitat. In over 70% of the Morelet’s crocodile monitoring program sites, a general upward population trend was observed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aqmbti44rm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rivera-T\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}llez, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Rivera-Téllez, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":395,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LGVNKQYZ"],"itemData":{"id":395,"type":"report","collection-title":"Comisión Nacional para el Conocimiento y Uso de la Biodiversidad","event-place":"México","page":"35","publisher-place":"México","title":"Informe del Programa de Monitoreo del Cocodrilo de Pantano en México, temporadas 2014 a 2015 y análisis de tendencias del 2011 al 2015","URL":"https://www.biodiversidad.gob.mx/media/1/planeta/cites/files/Informe%202011-2015-v7.pdf","author":[{"family":"Rivera-Téllez","given":"E."},{"family":"López Segurajáuregui","given":"G."},{"family":"Antaño Díaz","given":"L.A."},{"family":"Benítez Díaz","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The information gathered through the national implementation of the Morelet’s crocodile monitoring program on status and trends, indicates that wild populations of Morelet’s crocodile are in good condition and that there is a potential to develop sustainable productive projects for the benefit of indigenous people and local communities, of the species and its habitat.
	In 2017, a pilot project was developed to establish an integrated production system of high-quality C. moreletii skins, based on the conservation of the species and its habitat, as well as on a sustainable, legal, and traceable scheme, with fair and equitable distribution of benefits throughout the productive chain, particularly for indigenous peoples and local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dpsgDh2y","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mosig, Anta\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}o,, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Mosig, Antaño,, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":394,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SXEE3UWA"],"itemData":{"id":394,"type":"speech","genre":"Poster presented at 23 Meeting of SBSSTA (CBD). Canadá, 25-29 November 2019","title":"Promoting the Conservation of Mortelet’s crocodile (Crocodylus moreletii) by sustainably managing it through community-based ranching activities in Quintana Roo, Mexico","URL":"https://www.biodiversidad.gob.mx/media/1/planeta/cites/images/postercocodriloSBSTTA.jpg","author":[{"family":"Mosig","given":"P."},{"family":"Antaño","given":"L."},{"family":"Guerrero","given":"S."},{"family":"Cruz","given":"A."},{"family":"Torres","given":"L.A."},{"family":"Coral","given":"R."},{"family":"Martínez","given":"I"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Activities carried out by the community members that are part of the pilot project, are guided by the ranching protocol for Morelet’s crocodile ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a4v20o9p0e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barrios & Cremieux, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Barrios & Cremieux, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34298,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5IJAC5LC"],"itemData":{"id":34298,"type":"book","event-place":"Mexico","publisher":"Comisión Nacional para el Conocimiento y Uso de la Biodiversidad (CONABIO)","publisher-place":"Mexico","title":"Protocolo de rancheo para cocodrilo de pantano (Crocodylus moreletii) en México.","author":[{"family":"Barrios","given":"Gabriel"},{"family":"Cremieux","given":"Juan Carlos"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The ranching scheme consists in harvesting eggs from the wild and taking them to incubators managed by the local communities, which rises the offspring survival rate from 1% in the wild, to up to 90% in captivity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GnELnno8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barrios & Cremieux, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Barrios & Cremieux, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34298,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5IJAC5LC"],"itemData":{"id":34298,"type":"book","event-place":"Mexico","publisher":"Comisión Nacional para el Conocimiento y Uso de la Biodiversidad (CONABIO)","publisher-place":"Mexico","title":"Protocolo de rancheo para cocodrilo de pantano (Crocodylus moreletii) en México.","author":[{"family":"Barrios","given":"Gabriel"},{"family":"Cremieux","given":"Juan Carlos"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Through its implementation, standardized information on nests is obtained, which is the basis for defining sustainable egg harvest quotas and non-detriment findings on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora framework. It also provides detailed guidelines for nest extraction, egg transportation and the incubation processes.
	After over 40 years of a national prohibition to harvest crocodiles, in 2017, “Cocodrilos Chacchoben”, was the first Management Unit for Wildlife Conservation (UMA) to sustainably and legally harvest wild C. moreletii eggs. The hatchlings were later sold to the farm “Cocodrilia” where they grow in optimal conditions until they reach commercial sizes for the production of high-quality skins for the international leather market ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2YEaev6X","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mosig, Anta\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}o,, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Mosig, Antaño,, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":394,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SXEE3UWA"],"itemData":{"id":394,"type":"speech","genre":"Poster presented at 23 Meeting of SBSSTA (CBD). Canadá, 25-29 November 2019","title":"Promoting the Conservation of Mortelet’s crocodile (Crocodylus moreletii) by sustainably managing it through community-based ranching activities in Quintana Roo, Mexico","URL":"https://www.biodiversidad.gob.mx/media/1/planeta/cites/images/postercocodriloSBSTTA.jpg","author":[{"family":"Mosig","given":"P."},{"family":"Antaño","given":"L."},{"family":"Guerrero","given":"S."},{"family":"Cruz","given":"A."},{"family":"Torres","given":"L.A."},{"family":"Coral","given":"R."},{"family":"Martínez","given":"I"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A key factor for the success of the Pilot Project has been multistakeholder -multisectorial collaboration; involvement of federal and state governments of both environmental and agricultural sectors, Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora authorities, indigenous peoples and local communities, the private sector, the crocodile specialist group of the International Union for Conservation of Nature, national experts part of the group of crocodilians specialists in Mexico and civil society.
	Through the implementation of the pilot project, the community of Chacchoben, has benefited from the sustainable ranching of Morelet’s crocodile eggs. Knowledge about the species and population trends continues to be generated through the Morelet’s crocodile monitoring pProgram. As a result, the crocodiles that used to be considered a threat, are now valued and they represent an additional income. This income incentivized the community to conserve 4,658 ha of habitat that is also home to about 560 other species of fauna and flora ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lNl4Vouz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mosig, Anta\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}o,, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Mosig, Antaño,, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":394,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SXEE3UWA"],"itemData":{"id":394,"type":"speech","genre":"Poster presented at 23 Meeting of SBSSTA (CBD). Canadá, 25-29 November 2019","title":"Promoting the Conservation of Mortelet’s crocodile (Crocodylus moreletii) by sustainably managing it through community-based ranching activities in Quintana Roo, Mexico","URL":"https://www.biodiversidad.gob.mx/media/1/planeta/cites/images/postercocodriloSBSTTA.jpg","author":[{"family":"Mosig","given":"P."},{"family":"Antaño","given":"L."},{"family":"Guerrero","given":"S."},{"family":"Cruz","given":"A."},{"family":"Torres","given":"L.A."},{"family":"Coral","given":"R."},{"family":"Martínez","given":"I"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.


Photos: Morelet’s crocodile (Crocodylus moreletii) © Iván Montes de oca Cacheux / CONABIO CC-BY and © Mariana del Carmen Gonzalez Ramón CC-BY

	Similarly, within gathering case studies reviewed, whenever legal and regulatory policies involved local communities along with local non-governmental organizations as facilitators in the process it resulted in an overall positive impact on ecological, economic and social development. Involvement of local communities in legal and regulatory policies along with intermediaries as facilitators emerged as an enabling condition to ensure sustainable gathering and overall positive impact on the socio-economic development of the community. 
	Empowering more women in local resource decision-making can lead to better resource governance and conservation outcomes. This is in part because women typically use natural resources differently than men, but are often left out of decision making on how local resources are managed, creating gaps in resource management decision making and policy. For example, there is strong and clear evidence from India and Nepal, of how including women in forest management groups results in better resource governance and conservation outcomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aisgppgnmg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Leisher, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Leisher, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34271,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8CC85F46"],"itemData":{"id":34271,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Women often use natural resources differently than men yet frequently have minimal influence on how local resources are managed. An emerging hypothesis is that empowering more women in local resource decision-making may lead to better resource governance and conservation. Here we focus on the forestry and fisheries sectors to answer the question: What is the evidence that the gender composition of forest and fisheries management groups affects resource governance and conservation outcomes? We present a systematic map detailing the geographic and thematic extent of the evidence base and assessing the quality of the evidence, as per a published a priori protocol.","container-title":"Environmental Evidence","DOI":"10.1186/s13750-016-0057-8","ISSN":"2047-2382","issue":"1","page":"6","title":"Does the gender composition of forest and fishery management groups affect resource governance and conservation outcomes? A systematic map","title-short":"Does the gender composition of forest and fishery management groups affect resource governance and conservation outcomes?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13750-016-0057-8","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Leisher","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Temsah","given":"Gheda"},{"family":"Booker","given":"Francesca"},{"family":"Day","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Samberg","given":"Leah"},{"family":"Prosnitz","given":"Debra"},{"family":"Agarwal","given":"Bina"},{"family":"Matthews","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Russell","given":"Diane"},{"family":"Sunderland","given":"Terry"},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In 2006, India had 106,482 registered as “joint forest management groups”. Joint forest management guidelines, issued in 2000, recommended that village forest committees should consist of 50% women members, with at least 33% women on the executive committee-the principal decision-making body. Similarly, Nepal had 17,685 forest user groups in 2011, with approximately 800 women-only groups; government guidelines for community forestry recommended that women comprise 50% of a forest user group’s executive committee. In both countries, groups with a higher proportion of women in their executive committee showed significantly greater improvements in forest condition than groups with a lower proportion of women ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7bHfgOdT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Leisher, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Leisher, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34271,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8CC85F46"],"itemData":{"id":34271,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Women often use natural resources differently than men yet frequently have minimal influence on how local resources are managed. An emerging hypothesis is that empowering more women in local resource decision-making may lead to better resource governance and conservation. Here we focus on the forestry and fisheries sectors to answer the question: What is the evidence that the gender composition of forest and fisheries management groups affects resource governance and conservation outcomes? We present a systematic map detailing the geographic and thematic extent of the evidence base and assessing the quality of the evidence, as per a published a priori protocol.","container-title":"Environmental Evidence","DOI":"10.1186/s13750-016-0057-8","ISSN":"2047-2382","issue":"1","page":"6","title":"Does the gender composition of forest and fishery management groups affect resource governance and conservation outcomes? A systematic map","title-short":"Does the gender composition of forest and fishery management groups affect resource governance and conservation outcomes?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13750-016-0057-8","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Leisher","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Temsah","given":"Gheda"},{"family":"Booker","given":"Francesca"},{"family":"Day","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Samberg","given":"Leah"},{"family":"Prosnitz","given":"Debra"},{"family":"Agarwal","given":"Bina"},{"family":"Matthews","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Russell","given":"Diane"},{"family":"Sunderland","given":"Terry"},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These benefits were likely in part due to greater degrees of cooperation among women, and women using their knowledge of plant species and methods of product extraction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aak5arkcob","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Agarwal, 2009; Leisher, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Agarwal, 2009; Leisher, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":371,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5425FRFV"],"itemData":{"id":371,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Would enhancing women's presence in community institutions of forest governance improve resource conservation and regeneration? This paper focuses on this little addressed question. Based on the author's primary data on communities managing their local forests in parts of India and Nepal, it statistically assesses whether the gender composition of a local forest management group affects forest conservation outcomes, after controlling for other characteristics of the management group, aspects of institutional functioning, forest and population characteristics, and related factors. It is found that groups with a high proportion of women in their executive committee (EC)—the principal decision-making body—show significantly greater improvements in forest condition in both regions. Moreover, groups with all-women ECs in the Nepal sample have better forest regeneration and canopy growth than other groups, despite receiving much smaller and more degraded forests. Older EC members, especially older women, also make a particular difference, as does employing a guard. The beneficial impact of women's presence on conservation outcomes is attributable especially to women's contributions to improved forest protection and rule compliance. More opportunity for women to use their knowledge of plant species and methods of product extraction, as well as greater cooperation among women, are also likely contributory factors.","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2009.04.025","ISSN":"0921-8009","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"2785–2799","title":"Gender and forest conservation: The impact of women's participation in community forest governance","title-short":"Gender and forest conservation","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0921800909001852","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Agarwal","given":"Bina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]],"season":"Setembro"}}},{"id":34271,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8CC85F46"],"itemData":{"id":34271,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Women often use natural resources differently than men yet frequently have minimal influence on how local resources are managed. An emerging hypothesis is that empowering more women in local resource decision-making may lead to better resource governance and conservation. Here we focus on the forestry and fisheries sectors to answer the question: What is the evidence that the gender composition of forest and fisheries management groups affects resource governance and conservation outcomes? We present a systematic map detailing the geographic and thematic extent of the evidence base and assessing the quality of the evidence, as per a published a priori protocol.","container-title":"Environmental Evidence","DOI":"10.1186/s13750-016-0057-8","ISSN":"2047-2382","issue":"1","page":"6","title":"Does the gender composition of forest and fishery management groups affect resource governance and conservation outcomes? A systematic map","title-short":"Does the gender composition of forest and fishery management groups affect resource governance and conservation outcomes?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13750-016-0057-8","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Leisher","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Temsah","given":"Gheda"},{"family":"Booker","given":"Francesca"},{"family":"Day","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Samberg","given":"Leah"},{"family":"Prosnitz","given":"Debra"},{"family":"Agarwal","given":"Bina"},{"family":"Matthews","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Russell","given":"Diane"},{"family":"Sunderland","given":"Terry"},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, women had a significantly positive effect on cooperation in forest management in Paraguay. Women have also demonstrated the ability to mitigate elite capture of benefits during the process of decentralization, which could be particularly relevant during initial stages of the logging project. Therefore, a deliberate attempt to integrate women into project planning (and beyond) would likely benefit both the process and longer-term outcomes of extractive reserve logging ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"avb32rguul","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cooper & Kainer, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Cooper & Kainer, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34270,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IEMNZG9M"],"itemData":{"id":34270,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Cooper, N. A., and K. A. Kainer. 2018. To log or not to log: local perceptions of timber management and its implications for well-being within a sustainable-use protected area. Ecology and Society 23(2):4. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-09995-230204","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-09995-230204","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"2","language":"en","license":"© 2018 by the author(s)","title":"To log or not to log: local perceptions of timber management and its implications for well-being within a sustainable-use protected area","title-short":"To log or not to log","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol23/iss2/art4/","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Cooper","given":"Natalie"},{"family":"Kainer","given":"Karen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]],"season":"Abril"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	In contrast, where women remain excluded from governance and decision-making processes, sustainable use can be hampered ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"av92r3dr3q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rohe, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Rohe, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34272,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GKBSYGFQ"],"itemData":{"id":34272,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Women play an important role within small-scale fishing communities in the South Pacific, contributing to food security and income. Yet, decisions on the management of coastal fisheries are mostly taken by male community leaders. Given that women and men interact with marine spaces differently, there is a need to further analyze women’s and men’s differentiated roles and participation in marine resource use and governance. This study does so by drawing on qualitative data from a case study in Solomon Islands. In the fishing community studied here, women had crucial and differentiated effects on social, economic, and ecological sustainability. Our study reveals that women provided significant social and economic benefits to their families and the broader community. At the same time, we find that some women were inclined towards breaking local marine management rules (i.e., potentially lowering positive ecological effects of the conservation efforts) because (1) women had been little involved in the decision-making with regard to local marine management; (2) women had partly lost trust in the local male leadership due to a perceived misuse of money; and (3) women were more constrained in their fishing activities because a marine closure was located where mainly women used to fish. Our study highlights the importance of paying attention to women’s needs and actions in the governance of the fishery—including both the positive as well as potentially negative consequences thereof. Furthermore, our study shows that, besides gender, other socio-cultural variables (i.e., religious denomination and place of birth) shaped a person’s role and interactions in the fishery. It thus adds weight to intersectional approaches to gender.","container-title":"Maritime Studies","DOI":"10.1007/s40152-018-0106-8","ISSN":"2212-9790","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"155–162","title":"A gender lens on women’s harvesting activities and interactions with local marine governance in a South Pacific fishing community","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s40152-018-0106-8","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Rohe","given":"Janne"},{"family":"Schlüter","given":"Achim"},{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]],"season":"Outubro"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, although women play an important role in small scale fishing communities in Solomons, such as contributing to food security, decisions on how the resource is managed are mostly taken by men. This has impacted social and ecological sustainability leaving women more inclined to break local management rules because: i) they were not involved in decision-making; ii) they had lost trust in the local male leadership, who they perceived had misused the money, and; iii) the management rules constrained the women’s activities most as the marine closure was located where the women used to fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XK8UQS38","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rohe, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Rohe, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34272,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GKBSYGFQ"],"itemData":{"id":34272,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Women play an important role within small-scale fishing communities in the South Pacific, contributing to food security and income. Yet, decisions on the management of coastal fisheries are mostly taken by male community leaders. Given that women and men interact with marine spaces differently, there is a need to further analyze women’s and men’s differentiated roles and participation in marine resource use and governance. This study does so by drawing on qualitative data from a case study in Solomon Islands. In the fishing community studied here, women had crucial and differentiated effects on social, economic, and ecological sustainability. Our study reveals that women provided significant social and economic benefits to their families and the broader community. At the same time, we find that some women were inclined towards breaking local marine management rules (i.e., potentially lowering positive ecological effects of the conservation efforts) because (1) women had been little involved in the decision-making with regard to local marine management; (2) women had partly lost trust in the local male leadership due to a perceived misuse of money; and (3) women were more constrained in their fishing activities because a marine closure was located where mainly women used to fish. Our study highlights the importance of paying attention to women’s needs and actions in the governance of the fishery—including both the positive as well as potentially negative consequences thereof. Furthermore, our study shows that, besides gender, other socio-cultural variables (i.e., religious denomination and place of birth) shaped a person’s role and interactions in the fishery. It thus adds weight to intersectional approaches to gender.","container-title":"Maritime Studies","DOI":"10.1007/s40152-018-0106-8","ISSN":"2212-9790","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"155–162","title":"A gender lens on women’s harvesting activities and interactions with local marine governance in a South Pacific fishing community","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s40152-018-0106-8","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Rohe","given":"Janne"},{"family":"Schlüter","given":"Achim"},{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]],"season":"Outubro"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Similarly, some of the least successful gathering case studies did not specify the process followed to consult or include indigenous peoples and local communities or other actors in the development and/or implementation of projects, and as a result inclusive and transparent participation was not achieved. Full participation of all actors is crucial to guarantee that all points of view are taken into consideration and that the project is culturally appropriate and not imposed by an external actor.
       Across practices, several successful cases studies involved co-governing or co-management through participation and collaboration by multiple actors. The interaction between different actors has been highlighted as a priority for transparency and participation to build governance. For example, in Costa Rica, three national institutions – the ministry of environment and energy, the institute of marine fisheries and the association for rural economic development – retain responsibilities for various aspects of the project ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a22h4meus1g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. Campbell, 1998)","plainCitation":"(L. Campbell, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34295,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MW68ZTQP"],"itemData":{"id":34295,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"'Sustainable use' of wildlife resources and 'community based conservation' are two themes recurrent in contemporary statements of wildlife conservation policy, and their use is in response to a perceived 'deep conservation crisis' which has in part arisen from exclusionary and restrictive conservation practices. The extent to which the legal harvest of marine turtle eggs in Ostional, Costa Rica, is an example of sustainable use and community based conservation is evaluated in this paper. Field research using in-depth interviewing and a household questionnaire was undertaken in Ostional during 1994 and 1995, to investigate local perceptions of the egg harvesting project, both positive and negative. Socio-economic benefits from, and legal and administrative structures supporting, the project were found to be fundamental to community support for a limited egg harvest and allowed for community participation in, and control of, resource use. Participation and control were key to local support for conservation of nesting marine turtles and their eggs. Attempts to use wildlife sustainably must be considered on a case by case basis, to account for the biological nature of the wildlife resource and environment in question and for local socio-economic, political and historical conditions. Nevertheless, some of the lessons learned from the attempt to implement sustainable use and community based conservation in Ostional may be more widely generalized, and may help inform other efforts to reconcile wildlife conservation objectives with local development needs.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892998000393","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Conservation","page":"305-319","source":"ResearchGate","title":"Use them or lose them? Conservation and the consumptive use of marine turtle eggs at Ostional, Costa Rica","title-short":"Use them or lose them?","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Campbell","given":"Lisa"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Ostional integrated development association (community institution), managed by an elected “junta directive” (board of directors), is responsible for the day-to-day activities.
	In contrast, none of the five least successful terrestrial animal harvesting case studies applied community-based management which seems to be key for successful implementation of the policy instruments, although two of them considered traditional resource rights and traditional ecological knowledge. At the same time, the least successful terrestrial animal harvesting cases tended to exclude indigenous peoples and local communities in managing the resource and within the harvesting practices, either by the imposition of legislation that prohibited harvesting, or by an increase in resource management by the private sector.

	The involvement, participation and empowerment of traditional populations and local communities is a particularly relevant governance approach to promote sustainability of small-scale fisheries worldwide, through a wide variety of collaborative arrangements between governments and users, in a spectrum ranging from instructive to informative ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1be4hjbsni","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sen & Raakjaer Nielsen, 1996)","plainCitation":"(Sen & Raakjaer Nielsen, 1996)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34265,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WTMDHWPE"],"itemData":{"id":34265,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/0308-597X(96)00028-0","ISSN":"0308597X","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"405-418","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fisheries co-management: a comparative analysis","title-short":"Fisheries co-management","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/0308597X96000280","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Sen","given":"Sevaly"},{"family":"Raakjaer Nielsen","given":"Jesper"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (see Figure 6.3). Such bottom-up management approaches that include indigenous and local knowledge have been promising, especially in a context of lack of resources to monitor fisheries and enforce regulations by the central government ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a23hocsg60u","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Begossi, 2008, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Begossi, 2008, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":389,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ED6ZW8BC"],"itemData":{"id":389,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1007/s10668-008-9150-7","issue":"5","page":"591-603","title":"Local knowledge and training towards management","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-50649116171&doi=10.1007%2fs10668-008-9150-7&partnerID=40&md5=6934550048fea0cd8ffc30d4ea55c980","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Begossi","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":388,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3H7TG8YZ"],"itemData":{"id":388,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1007/s10668-013-9471-z","issue":"1","page":"5-34","title":"Ecological, cultural, and economic approaches to managing artisanal fisheries","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84891633373&doi=10.1007%2fs10668-013-9471-z&partnerID=40&md5=f8336900019620ef7ac80c6edeced0a6","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Begossi","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A brief overview of management and co-management systems directed to small-scale fisheries worldwide is provided here. Due to the spectrum of local arrangements (see Figure 6.3), which are rarely identified, the success and specific results are highly context dependent and not easy to generalize. However, most co-management arrangements are still more inclined towards government based, than to fully empowered and self-managed approaches.
	Most of the more sustainable inland fisheries in South America include the involvement of local fishers in co-management schemes aiming to maintain the harvest of valuable fishing resources, such as the pirarucu (Arapaima gigas) (Box 6.5) and freshwater turtles, in spatially defined boundaries, such as floodplain lakes in the Brazilian Amazon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a19695vp1e5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Campos-Silva & Peres, 2016, 2016; Castello, 2009; Petersen, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Campos-Silva & Peres, 2016, 2016; Castello, 2009; Petersen, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11559,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PZ8SCRFZ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVPJDIXP"],"itemData":{"id":11559,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep34745","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"34745","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community-based management induces rapid recovery of a high-value tropical freshwater fishery","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/srep34745","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Campos-Silva","given":"João Vitor"},{"family":"Peres","given":"Carlos A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}},{"id":11559,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PZ8SCRFZ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVPJDIXP"],"itemData":{"id":11559,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep34745","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"34745","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community-based management induces rapid recovery of a high-value tropical freshwater fishery","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/srep34745","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Campos-Silva","given":"João Vitor"},{"family":"Peres","given":"Carlos A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}},{"id":34927,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CYJQYLNZ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TQF9WJ9U"],"itemData":{"id":34927,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-008-9220-5","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"197-209","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Lessons from Integrating Fishers of Arapaima in Small-Scale Fisheries Management at the Mamirauá Reserve, Amazon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-008-9220-5","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Viana","given":"João P."},{"family":"Watkins","given":"Graham"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Luzadis","given":"Valerie A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",2]]}}},{"id":34287,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TYRINMEW"],"itemData":{"id":34287,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.12968","ISSN":"00221112","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"241-248","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recovery of <i>Arapaima</i> sp. populations by community-based management in floodplains of the Purus River, Amazon: recovery of <i>arapaima</i> sp. populations","title-short":"Recovery of <i>Arapaima</i> sp. populations by community-based management in floodplains of the Purus River, Amazon","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/jfb.12968","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Petersen","given":"T. A."},{"family":"Brum","given":"S. M"},{"family":"Rossoni","given":"F."},{"family":"Silveira","given":"G. F. V."},{"family":"Castello","given":"L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some of these co-management systems have shown additional ecological benefits on top of increases in targeted species in the Brazilian Amazonian rivers, including increases in fish, aquatic mammals, birds and even invertebrate species not targeted by the fishery ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1dmaile1c3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Campos-Silva, 2017; Silvano, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Campos-Silva, 2017; Silvano, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26387,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KG7K2HVI"],"itemData":{"id":26387,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Perspectives in Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/J.PECON.2017.08.004","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"266–270","title":"Community-based population recovery of overexploited Amazonian wildlife","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Campos-Silva","given":"J.V."},{"family":"Peres","given":"C.A."},{"family":"Antunes","given":"A.P."},{"family":"Valsecchi","given":"J."},{"family":"Pezzuti","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":34286,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S643RFEE"],"itemData":{"id":34286,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology of Freshwater Fish","DOI":"10.1111/j.1600-0633.2008.00333.x","ISSN":"09066691, 16000633","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"156-166","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effects of fisheries management on fish communities in the floodplain lakes of a Brazilian Amazonian Reserve","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1600-0633.2008.00333.x","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Silvano","given":"R. A. M."},{"family":"Ramires","given":"M."},{"family":"Zuanon","given":"J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and in overall fish diversity in both managed and nearby unmanaged lakes after a period of 15 years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"af0erqbnvb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Medeiros-Leal, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Medeiros-Leal, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34301,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/76UP77TM"],"itemData":{"id":34301,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Co-management is increasingly recognized as an effective model for managing fisheries, but little information exists on whether co-management can produce effects in species other than the target species. Fishery co-management in the tropics, where fish diversity is high and fish catches tend to be multispecies, is prone to produce assemblage-wide effects via alterations in the food web and changes in the overall capture of non-target species. Here, we assessed the effects of co-management for the species\\n              Arapaima\\n              sp. in relation to the structure and composition of the overall fish assemblage in floodplain lakes of the central Amazon Basin. These floodplain lakes are managed under a system of zoning of fishing activities. We used data from surveys of six floodplain lakes, including two lakes of each of three categories (lakes where fishing is prohibited, limited-access lakes, and open fishing lakes). The surveys were carried out before and after implementation of co-management, through gillnet fishing. The study area was the lower Solimões River, in the Amazon Basin, Brazil. Statistical models showed significant changes in the composition and structure of the fish assemblages after the implementation of the co-management, regardless of the zoning category. Through regulation of gear use and fishing practices, co-management allowed the colonization of species that had not been present before, which lead to higher richness and consequently increased fish sizes, abundance and biomass. Species of sedentary habits, migrants of short and medium distances, with commercial importance benefited the most from co-management. In the results presented in temporal scale, it was possible to observe a potential spillover effect being provided by the lakes where fishing is prohibited (no-take zones) and those of limited access that benefited those open to fishing. Thus, co-management had positive effects in the structure and composition of fish assemblages in all lakes, regardless of zoning category.","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.3389/fevo.2021.604170","ISSN":"2296-701X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Ecol. Evol.","page":"604170","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Single-Species Co-management Improves Fish Assemblage Structure and Composition in a Tropical River","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fevo.2021.604170/full","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Medeiros-Leal","given":"Wendell M."},{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Freitas","given":"Carlos E. C."},{"family":"Siqueira-Souza","given":"Flávia K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.	These co-management systems consist of formal agreements among local fishers' organizations, fishing companies, local and State governments, and non-government organizations. These agreements recognize the rights of local fishers to exclusive access to fishing grounds, and tenure rights over those fishing resources. A local participatory management board establishes local rules of access and use, recorded in annual management plans. The boards can also apply graduated sanctions when these rules are not met. Local fishers protect, monitor and assess fish stocks, and annual fishing quotas are granted by authorities based on these assessments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ddl88h726","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Castello, 2009; Petersen, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Castello, 2009; Petersen, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34927,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CYJQYLNZ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TQF9WJ9U"],"itemData":{"id":34927,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-008-9220-5","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"197-209","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Lessons from Integrating Fishers of Arapaima in Small-Scale Fisheries Management at the Mamirauá Reserve, Amazon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-008-9220-5","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Viana","given":"João P."},{"family":"Watkins","given":"Graham"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Luzadis","given":"Valerie A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",2]]}}},{"id":34287,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TYRINMEW"],"itemData":{"id":34287,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.12968","ISSN":"00221112","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"241-248","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recovery of <i>Arapaima</i> sp. populations by community-based management in floodplains of the Purus River, Amazon: recovery of <i>arapaima</i> sp. populations","title-short":"Recovery of <i>Arapaima</i> sp. populations by community-based management in floodplains of the Purus River, Amazon","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/jfb.12968","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Petersen","given":"T. A."},{"family":"Brum","given":"S. M"},{"family":"Rossoni","given":"F."},{"family":"Silveira","given":"G. F. V."},{"family":"Castello","given":"L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These Amazonian co-management systems originated from demands of local communities to reduce conflicts and restrict access of large-scale commercial fishers to harvest large lakes in the Brazilian Amazon, especially in the Middle Solimões and Lower Amazon River regions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a18ie72avgo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Castello, 2009; de Castro & McGrath, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Castello, 2009; de Castro & McGrath, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34927,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CYJQYLNZ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TQF9WJ9U"],"itemData":{"id":34927,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-008-9220-5","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"197-209","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Lessons from Integrating Fishers of Arapaima in Small-Scale Fisheries Management at the Mamirauá Reserve, Amazon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-008-9220-5","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Viana","given":"João P."},{"family":"Watkins","given":"Graham"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Luzadis","given":"Valerie A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",2]]}}},{"id":34300,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ICRDIFXD"],"itemData":{"id":34300,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Local management systems are generally regarded as traditional systems developed over generations. However, in the Brazilian Amazon, as in many other regions, community or collective management is a response to more recent changes in the exploitation of local fisheries and other common-pool resources. Greeted with optimism initially as a potentially effective way of reconciling social and conservation objectives in rural development, experience with community management over the last decade has shown that achieving this potential can be elusive. This paper examines the process of forming community agreements for the floodplain lake fisheries of the Lower Amazon through the analysis of 77 written fishing documents produced from 1981 to 1997. We examine approaches to resource management revealed in the accords, the institutional arrangements for implementing them, and evaluate the performance of these accords over this 15-year period. The paper focuses on the strengths and limitations of this institution in its social and ecological characteristics, institutional robustness, and evolving relations with parallel changes in formal government management policy for regional fisheries.","container-title":"Human Organization","ISSN":"00187259, 19383525","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Society for Applied Anthropology","page":"123–133","title":"Moving Toward Sustainability in the Local Management of Floodplain Lake Fisheries in the Brazilian Amazon","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/44127340","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Castro","given":"Fabio","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"McGrath","given":"David G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Although widespread throughout the major rivers in the Brazilian Amazon, there are only a few studies on the efficacy of these fishing accords ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2q0ov1nrjp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Almeida, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Almeida, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34115,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DJJ86FQK"],"itemData":{"id":34115,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2400.2008.00647.x","ISSN":"0969997X, 13652400","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"61-67","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishing agreements in the lower Amazon: for gain and restraint","title-short":"Fishing agreements in the lower Amazon","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1365-2400.2008.00647.x","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Almeida","given":"O. T."},{"family":"Lorenzen","given":"K."},{"family":"McGRATH","given":"D. G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Moreover, the ability of these co-management systems to improve individual fish catches can be more related to informal local organization and capacity to enforce rules of each community, than to a formal recognition by the Brazilian government ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a645e0i6lp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. F. M. Lopes, 2019)","plainCitation":"(P. F. M. Lopes, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34278,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EVFTKYRU"],"itemData":{"id":34278,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Viability and Sustainability of Small-Scale Fisheries in Latin America and The Caribbean","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-319-76077-3","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-76078-0_10","page":"219-241","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Challenge of Managing Amazonian Small-Scale Fisheries in Brazil","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-319-76078-0_10","volume":"19","editor":[{"family":"Salas","given":"Silvia"},{"family":"Barragán-Paladines","given":"María José"},{"family":"Chuenpagdee","given":"Ratana"}],"author":[{"family":"Lopes","given":"Priscila F. M."},{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Begossi","given":"Alpina"},{"family":"Isaac","given":"Victória J."},{"family":"Almeida","given":"Morgana"},{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato A. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",4,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another co-management system in the Brazilian Amazonian rivers is that of the extractive reserves, which have shown promising evidences of increased abundance of fished resources, when compared to non-managed sites ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2edoqe3i5m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hallwass, 2019, 2020; Keppeler, 2017; Silvano, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Hallwass, 2019, 2020; Keppeler, 2017; Silvano, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":382,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2T4NL3H7"],"itemData":{"id":382,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The local ecological knowledge (LEK) of ?shermen has been applied to reconstruct temporal patterns of resource abundance and to assess resource exploitation, especially in tropical ecosystems. Protected areas (PAs) are the main conservation measure in the Amazon, but their ef?cacy to improve ?sheries is poorly known. The main goal of this study was to apply ?shermen’ LEK to evaluate temporal changes in composition and abundance of exploited ?sh species among three different categories of PAs in the Tapajo s River, Brazilian Amazon. We interviewed 203 ?shers about their highest ?sh catches, and the main ?sh caught at two time periods: the beginning of their ?shing careers and currently. Fishers’ LEK indicated decreases in citations of larger-bodied ?shes and in reported catches over time, evidencing a gradient of conservation according to PA’s time of establishment and management rules. We noticed higher changes (reduced catches and smaller ?sh exploited) in the Environmental PA, established 10 years ago and lacking management plan and buffer zone. In National Forest, which was created 40 years ago with more restrictive management rules, ?sh composition and catches included larger commercial ?sh. The Extractive Reserve, which was created 15 years ago and lacks speci?c ?shing rules, may be in an intermediate stage, as large ?sh and highest catches decreased over time, but medium and large ?sh are still exploited. Fishers’ LEK can be an invaluable but neglected source of data on temporal patterns of ?shing pressure and conservation needs, especially in tropical megadiverse, poorly known and threatened rivers.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/acv.12504","ISSN":"1367-9430, 1469-1795","journalAbbreviation":"Anim Conserv","language":"en","page":"acv.12504","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers’ knowledge indicates temporal changes in composition and abundance of fishing resources in Amazon protected areas","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/acv.12504","author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"G."},{"family":"Schiavetti","given":"A."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"R. A. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,11]]}}},{"id":34116,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NLRK9NNB"],"itemData":{"id":34116,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries in the Brazilian Amazon","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-030-49145-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-030-49146-8_3","page":"23-39","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Small-scale Fisheries, Livelihoods, and Food Security of Riverine People","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-030-49146-8_3","editor":[{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato A.M."}],"author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Silva","given":"Luís Henrique Tomazoni","non-dropping-particle":"da"},{"family":"Nagl","given":"Paula"},{"family":"Clauzet","given":"Mariana"},{"family":"Begossi","given":"Alpina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":391,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F8E9M956"],"itemData":{"id":391,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"ORYX","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605316000247","issue":"2","page":"268-279","title":"Influence of protected areas on fish assemblages and fisheries in a large tropical river","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84976553035&doi=10.1017%2fS0030605316000247&partnerID=40&md5=0ea9128733605cda73567a7b54828141","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Keppeler","given":"F.W."},{"family":"Hallwass","given":"G."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"R.A.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":366,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VNKH3NDI"],"itemData":{"id":366,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1007/s10021-013-9722-8","ISSN":"1432-9840, 1435-0629","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystems","language":"en","page":"271-285","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Co-management and Spatial Features Contribute to Secure Fish Abundance and Fishing Yields in Tropical Floodplain Lakes","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10021-013-9722-8","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato A. M."},{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Lopes","given":"Priscila F."},{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"Ariane R."},{"family":"Lima","given":"Renata P."},{"family":"Hasenack","given":"Heinrich"},{"family":"Juras","given":"Anastácio A."},{"family":"Begossi","given":"Alpina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, although some high valued fish species may had decreased in abundance. This decrease, and lack of temporal data, make it difficult to confirm the ecological sustainability in these extractive reserves ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"asvheuirk8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hallwass, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Hallwass, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34116,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NLRK9NNB"],"itemData":{"id":34116,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries in the Brazilian Amazon","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-030-49145-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-030-49146-8_3","page":"23-39","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Small-scale Fisheries, Livelihoods, and Food Security of Riverine People","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-030-49146-8_3","editor":[{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato A.M."}],"author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Silva","given":"Luís Henrique Tomazoni","non-dropping-particle":"da"},{"family":"Nagl","given":"Paula"},{"family":"Clauzet","given":"Mariana"},{"family":"Begossi","given":"Alpina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Box 6.5 Participatory co-management enables sustainable use: Pirarucu in the Amazon

The Pirarucu fishery is an example of a successful participatory, and adaptive co-management initiative, where indigenous and local, and scientific knowledge are used together to guide management and monitoring. The initial alliance formed between fishers and government agencies, established a clearly articulated division of powers, benefits were equitably distributed across the community, and capacity building was a clear feature, together ensuring successful social and ecological benefits. 
       Pirarucu (Arapaima gigas) is among the largest freshwater fishes in the Amazon, reaching 3 meters in length and almost 200 kilograms ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ao7224h12t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Queiroz, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Queiroz, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34221,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2N8H5K2G"],"itemData":{"id":34221,"type":"thesis","abstract":"The pirarucu, Arapaima gigas (Osteoglossidae, Teleostei), an important natural resource in the Amazonian flooded forest, has been exploited for centuries and is now threatened by overfishing. The aim of this thesis, therefore, is to investigate the biology and ecology of this species so that sustainable management programmes can be put in place and the conservation of this fish ensured. Most of the research was carried out at Mamiraua Reserve, a protected area of Varzea forest in the Central Brazilian Amazon. Additional data were also recorded at the aquarium  of the Amazonian Museum of Natural History, at Manaus, Brazil. A study of the main environmental aspects varzea aquatic systems, showed that the lakes at Mamiraua Reserve could be grouped into two main categories based on their limnological morphological features. The investigation of the fisheries management by local fishermen revealed that the use of traditional fishing technology was responsible for the maintenance of local stocks. However, recent technological changes had increased CPUE to alarming level. The analysis of pirarucu feeding habits demonstrated that pirarucus are specialised carnivores, living mainly on fishes. Other invertebrate species are also important in their diet. High degrees of food selectivity and strong seasonal changes in diet proved to be the most important factor affecting the seasonal growth of pirarucus. Pirarucu growth was examined by analysing the growth marks deposited on scales twice a year, at times of high ingestion of food. L was circa 2.5.m and k was about 0.18. No significant differences in growth patterns between the sexes were found. Ten cohorts or annual age classes were identified. These ranged from 0.80m to 2.25m in total length. Very high annual growth rates during the first years are replaced by moderate rates after the age of first sexual maturation. Maturity is reached when pirarucus are around 5 years of age, or about 1.65m in total length. Fecundity increases with age of females, and so does oocyte diameter. However, fecundity is divided between 4 to 6 clutches of gametes that can be used throughout the breeding season. Each clutch is spawned separately, in small batches with less than 500 eggs in average. This strategy probably protects pirarucu against the unpredictability of the environment and avoids reproductive failures - total loss of fertilised eggs - due to catastrophic events. By spawning eggs in separate batches pairs may breed again if the first attempt was not successful. Males perform parental care, protecting the nest and guarding the offspring for about 6 months. The typical red coloration of pirarucus is particularly evident in males after sexual maturation. Almost 70% of male body surface may be covered in bright red pigmentation, brighter than among females. It is proposed that red coloration signals the size and/or age of pirarucus, and is used in the context of sexual selection. Male-male competition and female choice of males are likely to be operating in this species. The information in this thesis leads to several recommendations for the conservation of pirarucus. Criteria for selection of lakes in a rotational system of fisheries management, and age-specific impact of fishing and natural mortality rates on the stock are major topics discussed. The aim is the maximisation of breeding stock and local natural recruitment. Pirarucu farming and large scale conservation actions are also considered. Suggestions for future research on the biology of conservation of pirarucus are made.","genre":"Thesis","language":"en","license":"Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivs 3.0 Unported","note":"Accepted: 2012-06-20T09:59:48Z","publisher":"University of St Andrews","source":"research-repository.st-andrews.ac.uk","title":"Natural history and conservation of pirarucu, Àrapaima gigas', at the Amazonian Várzea : red giants in muddy waters","title-short":"Natural history and conservation of pirarucu, Àrapaima gigas', at the Amazonian Várzea","URL":"https://research-repository.st-andrews.ac.uk/handle/10023/2818","author":[{"family":"Queiroz","given":"Helder Lima","dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, playing an important part in the Amazonian economy and culture since the 16th century ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ds97jatoc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de MENEZES, 1951; Queiroz & Sardinha, 1999; Verissimo, 1895; Viana, 2007)","plainCitation":"(de MENEZES, 1951; Queiroz & Sardinha, 1999; Verissimo, 1895; Viana, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34223,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R6SJTXLR"],"itemData":{"id":34223,"type":"book","collection-title":"SIA. Estudos Técnicos","publisher":"SIA","title":"Notas biológicas e econômicas sobre o pirarucu Arapaima gigas (Cuvier) (Actinopterygii, Arapaimidae).","URL":"https://books.google.fr/books?id=m7t-HAAACAAJ","author":[{"family":"MENEZES","given":"R.S.","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1951"]]}}},{"id":34220,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FP9TVRVG"],"itemData":{"id":34220,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Brasília: SCM; CNPq/MCT, 208p.","container-title":"Estratégias para manejo dos recursos pesqueiros em Mamirauá.","title":"A preservação e o uso sustentado dos pirarucus (Arapaima gigas, Osteoglossidae) em Mamirauá","author":[{"family":"Queiroz","given":"Helder L."},{"family":"Sardinha","given":"Arluce D."}],"editor":[{"family":"Queiroz","given":"Helder"},{"family":"Crampton","given":"W.G.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":34219,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N2U5LPP7"],"itemData":{"id":34219,"type":"book","title":"A Pesca na Amazônia. Monographias Brasileiras III. Livraria Clássica Alves & Cia. 206p. Rio de Janeiro.","author":[{"family":"Verissimo","given":"José"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1895"]]}}},{"id":34225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JN2TWCHX"],"itemData":{"id":34225,"type":"chapter","page":"239-261","title":"Manejo Comunitário do Pirarucu Arapaima gigas na Reserva de Desenvolvimento Sustentável Mamirauá Amazonas, Brasil","author":[{"family":"Viana","given":"João"},{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Damasceno","given":"José"},{"family":"Amaral","given":"Ellen"},{"family":"Estupinan","given":"Guillermo"},{"family":"Arantes","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Batista","given":"Gelson"},{"family":"Garcez","given":"Danielle"},{"family":"Barbosa","given":"Saíde"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As one of the main impacts of the introduction of modern technologies in the second half of the 20th Century, the increase of the fishing pressure and offtake led to the overfishing of pirarucu stocks in most parts of the Amazon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2dhu64drki","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Isaac & Barthem, 1995; Queiroz & Sardinha, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Isaac & Barthem, 1995; Queiroz & Sardinha, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34228,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TZPXEFMA"],"itemData":{"id":34228,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper presents a review ofali available data on the ecology and economy offisheries in the Brazilian Amazon basin. A description of fisheries, stock assessment oftarget species, policies and problems concerning fish exploitation are discussed. Recommendations for management are given.","language":"pt","page":"23","source":"Zotero","title":"OS RECURSOS PESQUEIROS   DA AMAZÔNIA BRASILEIRA","author":[{"family":"Isaac","given":"Victoria J"},{"family":"Barthem","given":"Ronaldo B"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}},{"id":34220,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FP9TVRVG"],"itemData":{"id":34220,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Brasília: SCM; CNPq/MCT, 208p.","container-title":"Estratégias para manejo dos recursos pesqueiros em Mamirauá.","title":"A preservação e o uso sustentado dos pirarucus (Arapaima gigas, Osteoglossidae) em Mamirauá","author":[{"family":"Queiroz","given":"Helder L."},{"family":"Sardinha","given":"Arluce D."}],"editor":[{"family":"Queiroz","given":"Helder"},{"family":"Crampton","given":"W.G.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Official protective measures were first introduced in the 1980s by government agencies, but had little or no effect due to the lack of enforcement capacity of local authorities. Almost forty years after those attempts to protect the species, stocks are now recovered and increasing, due to a more effective protective measure, the community-based management of this fishery, introduced in the 1990s.  
        One of the main success cases of community-based initiatives of sustainable use of natural resources and biodiversity conservation in the Brazilian Amazon was first implemented by the small riverine communities of Mamirauá reserve, by the Japurá river, in the Amazonas state. The traditional ecological knowledge of the local populations was adopted in the management system, especially in the annual stock assessment technique in place ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2b8bt6rfcp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Arantes, 2007; Castello, 2004; K. Lopes & Queiroz, 2009)","plainCitation":"(C. Arantes, 2007; Castello, 2004; K. Lopes & Queiroz, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34310,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EYHECDK6"],"itemData":{"id":34310,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pan-American Journal of Aquatic Sciences","journalAbbreviation":"Pan-American Journal of Aquatic Sciences","title":"Variações entre contagens de Arapaima gigas (Schinz) (Osteoglossomorpha, Osteoglossidae) feitas por pescadores individualmente em Mamirauá, Brasil","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Arantes","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"CASTELLO","given":"LEANDRO"},{"family":"Garcez","given":"Danielle"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",1,1]]}}},{"id":34299,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3RR3X8QS"],"itemData":{"id":34299,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The giant and obligate air-breathing ?sh pirarucu Arapaima gigas (also known as arapaima) is a species endemic to the Amazon Basin that is increasingly managed by artisanal ?shers because of government failure to control the ?shery. In this study the ability of experienced artisanal ?shers to count the number of pirarucu at the moment of aerial breathing was assessed. Counts were strongly correlated (r ? 0.98) with mark–recapture abundance estimates calculated for the same populations. The potential for trained ?shers to train other ?shers to count pirarucu without slow and expensive mark–recapture work was also assessed and con?rmed. Not only are the counts a cost-effective method for assessing pirarucu populations, they also allow ?sher participation in decision making and contribute to effective management. The increasing number of community-based management schemes can now be matched with the training of ?shers to count pirarucu.","container-title":"North American Journal of Fisheries Management","DOI":"10.1577/M02-024.1","ISSN":"0275-5947, 1548-8675","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"North American Journal of Fisheries Management","language":"en","page":"379-389","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A Method to Count Pirarucu <i>Arapaima gigas</i> : Fishers, Assessment, and Management","title-short":"A Method to Count Pirarucu <i>Arapaima gigas</i>","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1577/M02-024.1","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",5]]}}},{"id":34224,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AWXNVME2"],"itemData":{"id":34224,"type":"article-journal","title":"UMA REVISÃO DAS FASES DE DESENVOLVIMENTO GONADAL DE PIRARUCUS Arapaima gigas (Schinz, 1822)","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Lopes","given":"Kelven"},{"family":"Queiroz","given":"Helder"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",9,10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. To provide technical support to this fisheries management, scientific research was conducted on the biology of the species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"au3nfja3ds","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. C. Arantes, 2010, 2013; Araripe, 2013; Castello, 2008a, 2008b; Castello, Stewart,, 2011; Coutinho, 2010; Queiroz, 2000)","plainCitation":"(C. C. Arantes, 2010, 2013; Araripe, 2013; Castello, 2008a, 2008b; Castello, Stewart,, 2011; Coutinho, 2010; Queiroz, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34114,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9JVHYR3"],"itemData":{"id":34114,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology of Freshwater Fish","DOI":"10.1111/j.1600-0633.2010.00431.x","ISSN":"09066691","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"455-465","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Population density, growth and reproduction of arapaima in an Amazonian river-floodplain: Density, growth and reproduction of arapaima","title-short":"Population density, growth and reproduction of arapaima in an Amazonian river-floodplain","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1600-0633.2010.00431.x","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Arantes","given":"C. C."},{"family":"Castello","given":"L."},{"family":"Stewart","given":"D. J."},{"family":"Cetra","given":"M."},{"family":"Queiroz","given":"H. L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",9]]}}},{"id":34309,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X2SPG3UJ"],"itemData":{"id":34309,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Biology of Fishes","DOI":"10.1007/s10641-011-9917-9","ISSN":"0378-1909, 1573-5133","issue":"10-11","journalAbbreviation":"Environ Biol Fish","language":"en","page":"1257-1267","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Environmental influences on the distribution of arapaima in Amazon floodplains","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10641-011-9917-9","volume":"96","author":[{"family":"Arantes","given":"Caroline C."},{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Cetra","given":"Mauricio"},{"family":"Schilling","given":"Ana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",11]]}}},{"id":34113,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U9WGUT8M"],"itemData":{"id":34113,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0054470","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e54470","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Dispersal Capacity and Genetic Structure of Arapaima gigas on Different Geographic Scales Using Microsatellite Markers","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0054470","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Araripe","given":"Juliana"},{"family":"Rêgo","given":"Péricles Sena","dropping-particle":"do"},{"family":"Queiroz","given":"Helder"},{"family":"Sampaio","given":"Iracilda"},{"family":"Schneider","given":"Horacio"}],"editor":[{"family":"Laudet","given":"Vincent"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",1,23]]}}},{"id":34938,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZTI2FLST"],"itemData":{"id":34938,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology of Freshwater Fish","DOI":"10.1111/j.1600-0633.2007.00255.x","ISSN":"0906-6691, 1600-0633","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Freshwater Fish","language":"en","page":"38-46","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Lateral migration of Arapaima gigas in floodplains of the Amazon","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1600-0633.2007.00255.x","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",3]]}}},{"id":34934,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HKRR2ZTX"],"itemData":{"id":34934,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1095-8649.2007.01778.x","ISSN":"0022-1112, 1095-8649","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biology","language":"en","page":"1520-1528","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Nesting habitat of Arapaima gigas (Schinz) in Amazonian floodplains","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1095-8649.2007.01778.x","volume":"72","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",4]]}}},{"id":34940,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KF66ZGHD"],"itemData":{"id":34940,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1007/s11160-010-9197-z","ISSN":"0960-3166, 1573-5184","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Rev Fish Biol Fisheries","language":"en","page":"623-640","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Modeling population dynamics and conservation of arapaima in the Amazon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11160-010-9197-z","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"L."},{"family":"Stewart","given":"D. J."},{"family":"Arantes","given":"C. C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",9]]}}},{"id":34218,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7G3U6NBQ"],"itemData":{"id":34218,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Pirarucu (Arapaima gigas) has been of the most important natural fishing resources of the Amazon region. Due to its economic importance, and the necessity to preserve the species hand, field research concerning the habits and behavior of the pirarucu has been increasing for the last 20 years. The aim of this paper is to present a mathematical model for the pirarucu population dynamics considering the species peculiarities, particularly the male parental care over the offspring. The solution of the dynamical systems indicates three possible equilibrium points for the population. The first corresponds to extinction; the third corresponds to a stable population close to the environmental carrying capacity. The second corresponds to an unstable equilibrium located between extinction and full use of the carrying capacity. It is shown that lack of males’ parental care closes the gap between the point corresponding to the unstable equilibrium and the point of stable non-trivial equilibrium. If guarding failure reaches a critical point the two points coincide and the population tends irreversibly to extinction. If some event tends to destabilize the population equilibrium, as for instance inadequate parental care, the model responds in such a way as to restore the trajectory towards the stable equilibrium point avoiding the route to extinction. The parameters introduced to solve the system of equations are partially derived from limited but reliable field data collected at the Mamirauá Sustainable Development Reserve (MSDR) in the Brazilian Amazonian Region.\\n          , \\n            Pirarucu (Arapaima gigas) tem sido um dos mais importantes recursos pesqueiros naturais da Amazônia. Devido à sua importância econômica, por um lado, e a necessidade de preservar a espécie, por outro lado, o domínio da investigação relativa a hábitos e comportamento do pirarucu tem sido crescente nos últimos 20 anos. O objetivo deste trabalho é apresentar um modelo matemático para a dinâmica populacional do pirarucu considerando as peculiaridades da espécie, particularmente o cuidado parental do macho sobre seus descendentes. A solução dos sistemas dinâmicos indicaram três possíveis pontos de equilíbrio para a população. O primeiro corresponde à extinção; o terceiro corresponde a uma população estável próxima da capacidade suporte do ambiente. O segundo corresponde a um equilíbrio instável localizado entre a extinção e a capacidade suporte do ambiente. Foi mostrado que a falta do cuidado parental de machos aproxima o ponto que corresponde ao equilíbrio instável e o ponto de equilíbrio estável não trivial. Se a falta de cuidado dos machos alcança um ponto crítico, os dois pontos coincidem e a população tende irreversivelmente à extinção. Os parâmetros introduzidos para resolver o sistema de equações foram derivados parcialmente de limitados, mas confiáveis dados de campo coletados na Reserva de Desenvolvimento Sustentável Mamirauá (RDSM) na Amazônia brasileira.","container-title":"Acta Amazonica","DOI":"10.1590/S0044-59672010000200012","ISSN":"0044-5967","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Acta Amaz.","page":"333-345","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Population Dynamics Modeling of Arapaima gigas","URL":"http://www.scielo.br/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0044-59672010000200012&lng=en&tlng=en","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Coutinho","given":"Eliane dos Santos de Souza"},{"family":"Bevilacqua","given":"Luiz"},{"family":"Queiroz","given":"Helder Lima","dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":34221,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2N8H5K2G"],"itemData":{"id":34221,"type":"thesis","abstract":"The pirarucu, Arapaima gigas (Osteoglossidae, Teleostei), an important natural resource in the Amazonian flooded forest, has been exploited for centuries and is now threatened by overfishing. The aim of this thesis, therefore, is to investigate the biology and ecology of this species so that sustainable management programmes can be put in place and the conservation of this fish ensured. Most of the research was carried out at Mamiraua Reserve, a protected area of Varzea forest in the Central Brazilian Amazon. Additional data were also recorded at the aquarium  of the Amazonian Museum of Natural History, at Manaus, Brazil. A study of the main environmental aspects varzea aquatic systems, showed that the lakes at Mamiraua Reserve could be grouped into two main categories based on their limnological morphological features. The investigation of the fisheries management by local fishermen revealed that the use of traditional fishing technology was responsible for the maintenance of local stocks. However, recent technological changes had increased CPUE to alarming level. The analysis of pirarucu feeding habits demonstrated that pirarucus are specialised carnivores, living mainly on fishes. Other invertebrate species are also important in their diet. High degrees of food selectivity and strong seasonal changes in diet proved to be the most important factor affecting the seasonal growth of pirarucus. Pirarucu growth was examined by analysing the growth marks deposited on scales twice a year, at times of high ingestion of food. L was circa 2.5.m and k was about 0.18. No significant differences in growth patterns between the sexes were found. Ten cohorts or annual age classes were identified. These ranged from 0.80m to 2.25m in total length. Very high annual growth rates during the first years are replaced by moderate rates after the age of first sexual maturation. Maturity is reached when pirarucus are around 5 years of age, or about 1.65m in total length. Fecundity increases with age of females, and so does oocyte diameter. However, fecundity is divided between 4 to 6 clutches of gametes that can be used throughout the breeding season. Each clutch is spawned separately, in small batches with less than 500 eggs in average. This strategy probably protects pirarucu against the unpredictability of the environment and avoids reproductive failures - total loss of fertilised eggs - due to catastrophic events. By spawning eggs in separate batches pairs may breed again if the first attempt was not successful. Males perform parental care, protecting the nest and guarding the offspring for about 6 months. The typical red coloration of pirarucus is particularly evident in males after sexual maturation. Almost 70% of male body surface may be covered in bright red pigmentation, brighter than among females. It is proposed that red coloration signals the size and/or age of pirarucus, and is used in the context of sexual selection. Male-male competition and female choice of males are likely to be operating in this species. The information in this thesis leads to several recommendations for the conservation of pirarucus. Criteria for selection of lakes in a rotational system of fisheries management, and age-specific impact of fishing and natural mortality rates on the stock are major topics discussed. The aim is the maximisation of breeding stock and local natural recruitment. Pirarucu farming and large scale conservation actions are also considered. Suggestions for future research on the biology of conservation of pirarucus are made.","genre":"Thesis","language":"en","license":"Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivs 3.0 Unported","note":"Accepted: 2012-06-20T09:59:48Z","publisher":"University of St Andrews","source":"research-repository.st-andrews.ac.uk","title":"Natural history and conservation of pirarucu, Àrapaima gigas', at the Amazonian Várzea : red giants in muddy waters","title-short":"Natural history and conservation of pirarucu, Àrapaima gigas', at the Amazonian Várzea","URL":"https://research-repository.st-andrews.ac.uk/handle/10023/2818","author":[{"family":"Queiroz","given":"Helder Lima","dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, on the main aspects of its fishery ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1u2122slo0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Andrade, 2011; Queiroz & Sardinha, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Andrade, 2011; Queiroz & Sardinha, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34312,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G5KMN7KY"],"itemData":{"id":34312,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Uakari","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.31420/UAKARI.V7I1.87","issue":"1","page":"29-40","title":"RECOUNT PIRARUCU: A METHOD FOR ASSESSING THE QUALITY OF PIRAUCU COUNTINGS","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Andrade","given":"Lorena Candice de Araújo"},{"family":"Amara","given":"Ellen Silvia Ramos"},{"family":"Silva","given":"Nataluzo Balbino","non-dropping-particle":"da"},{"family":"Queiroz","given":"Helder Lima","dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":34220,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FP9TVRVG"],"itemData":{"id":34220,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Brasília: SCM; CNPq/MCT, 208p.","container-title":"Estratégias para manejo dos recursos pesqueiros em Mamirauá.","title":"A preservação e o uso sustentado dos pirarucus (Arapaima gigas, Osteoglossidae) em Mamirauá","author":[{"family":"Queiroz","given":"Helder L."},{"family":"Sardinha","given":"Arluce D."}],"editor":[{"family":"Queiroz","given":"Helder"},{"family":"Crampton","given":"W.G.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and also, on the social and economic aspects of the fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1jdgurco11","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. S. R. Amaral, 2008; Lima & Peralta, 2017; Peralta, 2010)","plainCitation":"(E. S. R. Amaral, 2008; Lima & Peralta, 2017; Peralta, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34311,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S89ZGTN6"],"itemData":{"id":34311,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The community management of pirarucu fish (Arapaima gigas) is carried out by riverine communities of the Mamirauá and Amanã Reserves since 1999. Since the beginning of management, the population has showed positive signs of recovery of stocks. The commercialization of production, on the other hand, has been one of the main bottlenecks of the process. Thus, this study aimed to identify, from technical and local viewpoints, the main problems faced in the commercialization of managed pirarucu, besides supporting strategies to mitigate these problems. The methods employed were documental research, discussions with communities in meetings, as well as technical assistance during the 2004 and 2005 fish harvests. It was observed that the main challenges were low prices, difficulty to reach favorable markets, as well as a low level of community involvement in the negotiations and sales processes. After new commercial strategies developed by technicians and communities, there was a decrease of problems in the area, development of new market strategies, and more community participation in the process. This study, thus suggests that the best way to deal with the challenges of the market, and consequently, with conservation of the species is to promote mechanisms to facilitate community participation in the commercial process.","container-title":"Scientific Magazine UAKARI","DOI":"10.31420/uakari.v3i2.27","ISSN":"1981-4518, 1981-450X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Uakari","page":"7-17","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A COMUNIDADE E O MERCADO: OS DESAFIOS NA COMERCIALIZAÇÃO DE PIRARUCU MANEJADO DAS RESERVAS MAMIRAUÁ E AMANÃ, AMAZONAS - BRASIL.","title-short":"A COMUNIDADE E O MERCADO","URL":"http://uakari.mamiraua.org.br/UAKARI/article/view/27","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Amaral","given":"Ellen Sílvia Ramos"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9,28]]}}},{"id":34216,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/74XKG392"],"itemData":{"id":34216,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Latin American Studies","page":"799 - 827","title":"Developing Sustainability in the Brazilian Amazon: Twenty Years of History in the Mamirauá and Amanã Reserves","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Lima","given":"Deborah de Magalhães"},{"family":"Peralta","given":"Nelissa"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":34227,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/378KZLUK"],"itemData":{"id":34227,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Protected areas of sustainable use have been created as part of a strategy toward sustainable development. The common use of its resources is their main directive. According to rational choice theory, however, collective use of resources would generate a situation where social actors operating rationally toward their self interest would eventually deteriorate their pool of resources (Hardin, 1968; Olson, 1965). Taking as a starting point Hardin´s classic article, this paper presents the theoretical debate about collective use of resources, situating the contributions of sociological theory. Among main contributors, Ostrom (1986, 1990) identified a set of key variables correlated positively with resource management: the resource type, nature of groups that depend on natural common resources, institutional regime regulating use, the relations between the group and external forces such as markets and the state. Hardin´s model may be applied to situations where groups are large and do not communicate, each one acting independently, and cost of organization or institutional change is high. In situations of interdependence, however, group members may develop norms and patterns of reciprocity, social capital necessary in order to solving dilemmas imposed by collective use of resources.","container-title":"Uakari","issue":"1","language":"pt","page":"61-72","source":"Zotero","title":"A contribuição da Teoria da Escolha Racional para o debate sobre o uso comum dos recursos naturais.","URL":"https://www.mamiraua.org.br/documentos/d69c9dda46643887cbd05bb4ae0e3ae9.pdf","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Peralta","given":"Nelissa"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The first proposal for a formal management system of pirarucu was approved in an alliance with local fishermen of Mamirauá Reserve in 1998, and implemented since 1999 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a3pepq4ung","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Castello, 2009; Figueiredo, 2013; Viana, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Castello, 2009; Figueiredo, 2013; Viana, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34927,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CYJQYLNZ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TQF9WJ9U"],"itemData":{"id":34927,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-008-9220-5","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"197-209","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Lessons from Integrating Fishers of Arapaima in Small-Scale Fisheries Management at the Mamirauá Reserve, Amazon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-008-9220-5","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Viana","given":"João P."},{"family":"Watkins","given":"Graham"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Luzadis","given":"Valerie A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",2]]}}},{"id":34217,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S94US59G"],"itemData":{"id":34217,"type":"book","collection-title":"Tefé: IDSM,","ISBN":"978-85-88758-29-2","title":"Biologia, Conservação e Manejo Participativo de Pirarucus na Pan-Amazônia.","URL":"https://www.academia.edu/11596195/Biologia_Conservação_e_Manejo_de_Pirarucus_na_Amazônia","author":[{"family":"Figueiredo","given":"Ellen S.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":34225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JN2TWCHX"],"itemData":{"id":34225,"type":"chapter","page":"239-261","title":"Manejo Comunitário do Pirarucu Arapaima gigas na Reserva de Desenvolvimento Sustentável Mamirauá Amazonas, Brasil","author":[{"family":"Viana","given":"João"},{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Damasceno","given":"José"},{"family":"Amaral","given":"Ellen"},{"family":"Estupinan","given":"Guillermo"},{"family":"Arantes","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Batista","given":"Gelson"},{"family":"Garcez","given":"Danielle"},{"family":"Barbosa","given":"Saíde"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Members from the local communities agreed to negotiate, approve and adopt sustainability measures, adjust in fishing gear, establishing annual extraction quotas, monitoring and controlling the offtake, adopt a minimum size for the catch and the interruption of the activity during a banning period, at the reproductive period of the species. Management measures also included other participatory action, such as training courses and meetings to establish rules of access and use of fishing grounds. 
        Between 1999 and 2002 the experience at the Mamirauá Reserve was consolidated and underwent a first phase of expansion, replicated for a larger number of communities and their management associations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"acqmrt24fi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Viana, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Viana, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JN2TWCHX"],"itemData":{"id":34225,"type":"chapter","page":"239-261","title":"Manejo Comunitário do Pirarucu Arapaima gigas na Reserva de Desenvolvimento Sustentável Mamirauá Amazonas, Brasil","author":[{"family":"Viana","given":"João"},{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Damasceno","given":"José"},{"family":"Amaral","given":"Ellen"},{"family":"Estupinan","given":"Guillermo"},{"family":"Arantes","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Batista","given":"Gelson"},{"family":"Garcez","given":"Danielle"},{"family":"Barbosa","given":"Saíde"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This co-management was based on the division of powers and responsibilities among different institutions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a11ek5eedav","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Amaral, 2013; Peralta & Lima, 2012; J. B. F. Silva, 2013)","plainCitation":"(E. Amaral, 2013; Peralta & Lima, 2012; J. B. F. Silva, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34230,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R9WIYD7J"],"itemData":{"id":34230,"type":"book","call-number":"MLCM 2018/40926 (S)","collection-number":"1","collection-title":"Série Protocolos de manejo dos recursos naturais","event-place":"Tefé","ISBN":"978-85-88758-15-5","number-of-pages":"76","publisher":"Instituto de Desenvolvimento Sustentável Mamirauá","publisher-place":"Tefé","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Manejo de pirarucus (Arapaima gigas) em lagos de várzea de uso exclusivo de pescadores urbanos","editor":[{"family":"Amaral","given":"Ellen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":34222,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LGSXCJ3D"],"itemData":{"id":34222,"type":"chapter","container-title":"REUNIÃO BRASILEIRA DE ANTROPOLOGIA (28: 2012: São Paulo – São Paulo). Desafios antropológicos contemporâneos. São Paulo. p.1-39.","title":"Conhecimentos Científicos e Saberes tradicionais: Sinergia ou Tradução?","author":[{"family":"Peralta","given":"Nelissa"},{"family":"Lima","given":"Deborah"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",7,1]]}}},{"id":34226,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I7YUWDTV"],"itemData":{"id":34226,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Tefé: IDSM,","container-title":"Biologia, Conservação e Manejo Participativo de Pirarucus na Pan-Amazônia.","ISBN":"978-85-88758-29-2","title":"O papel da Secretaria de Estado do Meio Ambiente e Desenvolvimento Sustentável do Amazonas (SDS) no apoio ao manejo participativo de pirarucu (Arapaima gigas) nas Unidades de Conservação Estaduais","URL":"https://www.academia.edu/11596195/Biologia_Conservação_e_Manejo_de_Pirarucus_na_Amazônia","container-author":[{"family":"Figueiredo","given":"Ellen S.A."}],"author":[{"family":"Silva","given":"João Bosco F."},{"family":"Oliveira Jr.","given":"Flavio R.P."},{"family":"Batista","given":"Gelson S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The governance system built was based on local management committees that are able to set and enforce rules, conduct and oversee the activity and equitably distribute the benefits generated, ensuring resilience and growth. Several stakeholders are involved in the participatory governance of the pirarucu fisheries. Fishermen provided their traditional knowledge and are responsible not only for protecting of the fishing grounds, the local lakes. Fishermen are also responsible for the organization and distribution of fishing groups in the managed lakes, they perform the annual assessment of the stocks, the capture of pirarucus, they are responsible for marketing and trade, and of the local monitoring of all activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a6pttmf8nt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Viana, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Viana, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JN2TWCHX"],"itemData":{"id":34225,"type":"chapter","page":"239-261","title":"Manejo Comunitário do Pirarucu Arapaima gigas na Reserva de Desenvolvimento Sustentável Mamirauá Amazonas, Brasil","author":[{"family":"Viana","given":"João"},{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Damasceno","given":"José"},{"family":"Amaral","given":"Ellen"},{"family":"Estupinan","given":"Guillermo"},{"family":"Arantes","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Batista","given":"Gelson"},{"family":"Garcez","given":"Danielle"},{"family":"Barbosa","given":"Saíde"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. After the fishing season, they are responsible for the equitable sharing of benefits. The technicians from the supporting institutions are responsible for building capacity among local fisher’s associations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"au12lip40q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Castello, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Castello, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34927,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CYJQYLNZ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TQF9WJ9U"],"itemData":{"id":34927,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-008-9220-5","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"197-209","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Lessons from Integrating Fishers of Arapaima in Small-Scale Fisheries Management at the Mamirauá Reserve, Amazon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-008-9220-5","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Viana","given":"João P."},{"family":"Watkins","given":"Graham"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Luzadis","given":"Valerie A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. They also provide guidance to the fishing groups and supervise their actions regarding the compliance to the guidelines established in the annual management plan. Technicians are also responsible for monitoring the sustainability of the management system based on a group of environmental, social and economic indicators. The representatives of the government are responsible for licensing, for the oversight and for the annual evaluation the management. Local institutions maintain research programs that also act as monitoring programs, providing the follow up, assessing the sustainability of pirarucu fisheries, and a continuous evaluation of the ecological, socioeconomic and socio-political impacts of the community-based management and the participatory governance system in place. The results of these on-going surveys and evaluations allow the enhancement of the technical guidelines supporting the activity, in a truly adaptive management approach ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15hgl3ml3r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gon\\\\uc0\\\\u231{}alves, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Gonçalves, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34229,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8IIDAUR4"],"itemData":{"id":34229,"type":"book","collection-title":"Tefé: IDSM,","ISBN":"978-85-88758-29-2","title":"O Gigante Amazônico: Manejo Sustentável de Pirarucu.","URL":"https://www.academia.edu/11596195/Biologia_Conservação_e_Manejo_de_Pirarucus_na_Amazônia","author":[{"family":"Gonçalves","given":"Ana Claudia T."},{"family":"Cunha","given":"Jao B.C."},{"family":"Batista","given":"Jonas S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
        After more than two decades, pirarucu fisheries management proved that conservation of the species can be reconciled with its sustainable use, generating social, ecological and economic results. Management projects conducted in the Amazon are largely successful. In those places where all criteria are met, the stocks of the species increased annually by almost 25%. Income generation by fishermen and women involved more than doubled, and an additional 4 million United States dollars were generated and distributed to local fisher’s associations. These very positive results attracted fishermen from urban areas around the protected areas, and also from other small communities. Federal Brazilian government formally recognized all those participatory fisheries management initiatives ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"avnui275rk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Figueiredo, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Figueiredo, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34217,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S94US59G"],"itemData":{"id":34217,"type":"book","collection-title":"Tefé: IDSM,","ISBN":"978-85-88758-29-2","title":"Biologia, Conservação e Manejo Participativo de Pirarucus na Pan-Amazônia.","URL":"https://www.academia.edu/11596195/Biologia_Conservação_e_Manejo_de_Pirarucus_na_Amazônia","author":[{"family":"Figueiredo","given":"Ellen S.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and during these processes, local fisher’s associations had visibility and social recognition, increased their participation in public policy discussions, and had some access to their social rights. There was an important increase in gender equity. In 2017, 38% of members of pirarucu community-based fishery management were women, but in first years, 1998-2002, they were less than 5% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15vi6v5j7h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gon\\\\uc0\\\\u231{}alves, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Gonçalves, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34229,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8IIDAUR4"],"itemData":{"id":34229,"type":"book","collection-title":"Tefé: IDSM,","ISBN":"978-85-88758-29-2","title":"O Gigante Amazônico: Manejo Sustentável de Pirarucu.","URL":"https://www.academia.edu/11596195/Biologia_Conservação_e_Manejo_de_Pirarucus_na_Amazônia","author":[{"family":"Gonçalves","given":"Ana Claudia T."},{"family":"Cunha","given":"Jao B.C."},{"family":"Batista","given":"Jonas S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As a consequence of all these positive outcomes, a second expansion and replication took place after 2010 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1s6vm4v7co","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Campos-Silva, 2017; Campos-Silva & Peres, 2016; Castello, McGrath,, 2011; Figueiredo, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Campos-Silva, 2017; Campos-Silva & Peres, 2016; Castello, McGrath,, 2011; Figueiredo, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26387,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KG7K2HVI"],"itemData":{"id":26387,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Perspectives in Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/J.PECON.2017.08.004","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"266–270","title":"Community-based population recovery of overexploited Amazonian wildlife","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Campos-Silva","given":"J.V."},{"family":"Peres","given":"C.A."},{"family":"Antunes","given":"A.P."},{"family":"Valsecchi","given":"J."},{"family":"Pezzuti","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":11559,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PZ8SCRFZ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVPJDIXP"],"itemData":{"id":11559,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep34745","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"34745","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community-based management induces rapid recovery of a high-value tropical freshwater fishery","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/srep34745","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Campos-Silva","given":"João Vitor"},{"family":"Peres","given":"Carlos A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}},{"id":34945,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JK27E44D","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8Z5MJ63Q"],"itemData":{"id":34945,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Resource assessments of small-scale ?sheries in the Amazon are rare, making it dif?cult to improve ?sheries management. This study assessed the sustainability of small-scale ?shery resources in the Lower Amazon region using data from over 20,000 interviews collected in three ?oodplain ?shing communities between 1992 and 2007. The data were analyzed with respect to theoretical maxima of catches and historical trends of (i) catch per unit effort (CPUE), (ii) species composition of the catch, and (iii) mean body length of the most-caught species. The most important results were: First, observed catches have been about 54–58% of theoretical maxima in all communities. Second, CPUE has remained stable over time in all communities. Third, there has been no substitution of ?sh species in the catch. Fourth, mean body lengths of ?ve of the nine most-caught species were below length-at-?rst-reproduction, and the body lengths of some have increased overtime while that of others have remained stable or decreased. Overall, ?shery resources in the region appear to be moderately exploited, with some key species showing typical signs of overexploitation. This would indicate that the principal threat to the sustainability of ?shery resources is excessive concentration of ?shing effort on a few target species combined with unsustainable ?shing practices. Management and research recommendations are proposed.","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2011.05.002","ISSN":"01657836","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Research","language":"en","page":"356-364","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Resource sustainability in small-scale fisheries in the Lower Amazon floodplains","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0165783611001913","volume":"110","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"McGrath","given":"David G."},{"family":"Beck","given":"Pieter S.A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",7]]}}},{"id":34217,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S94US59G"],"itemData":{"id":34217,"type":"book","collection-title":"Tefé: IDSM,","ISBN":"978-85-88758-29-2","title":"Biologia, Conservação e Manejo Participativo de Pirarucus na Pan-Amazônia.","URL":"https://www.academia.edu/11596195/Biologia_Conservação_e_Manejo_de_Pirarucus_na_Amazônia","author":[{"family":"Figueiredo","given":"Ellen S.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nowadays the community-based management of pirarucu is performed at hundreds of small local communities spread the Brazilian Amazon, and in some other Amazonian countries, both inside and outside protected areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"REaM1kkM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gon\\\\uc0\\\\u231{}alves, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Gonçalves, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34229,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8IIDAUR4"],"itemData":{"id":34229,"type":"book","collection-title":"Tefé: IDSM,","ISBN":"978-85-88758-29-2","title":"O Gigante Amazônico: Manejo Sustentável de Pirarucu.","URL":"https://www.academia.edu/11596195/Biologia_Conservação_e_Manejo_de_Pirarucus_na_Amazônia","author":[{"family":"Gonçalves","given":"Ana Claudia T."},{"family":"Cunha","given":"Jao B.C."},{"family":"Batista","given":"Jonas S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

	Coastal fisheries have also been improved by co-management or commons-based management systems, through established territorial rights to local fishers, who exploit highly valuable and resident resources. For example, commons-based tenure systems that recognize rights to fishers to exclusively exploit coastal areas by following well defined management rules have contributed to recover or maintain catches of valuable marine invertebrates, such as shellfish or lobsters, in Mexico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2o65v4o71u","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u193{}lvarez, 2018; De la Cruz-Gonz\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}lez, 2018; Salas, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Álvarez, 2018; De la Cruz-González, 2018; Salas, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZKJFMIDT"],"itemData":{"id":359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2018.08.029","page":"203-214","title":"Environmental history of Mexican North Pacific fishing communities","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85052975797&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2018.08.029&partnerID=40&md5=7b1889198ba3e82c414b1cbb823188d6","volume":"165","author":[{"family":"Álvarez","given":"P."},{"family":"Espejel","given":"I."},{"family":"Bocco","given":"G."},{"family":"Cariño","given":"M."},{"family":"Seingier","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":358,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NAZ4F7DS"],"itemData":{"id":358,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2018.01.008","page":"96-102","title":"Self-empowerment and successful co-management in an artisanal fishing community: Santa Cruz de Miramar, Mexico","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85041435659&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2018.01.008&partnerID=40&md5=2e93d9232595fcefceb19189c999a30c","volume":"154","author":[{"family":"De la Cruz-González","given":"F.J."},{"family":"Patiño-Valencia","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Luna-Raya","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"A.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":34923,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LKWAR2SL"],"itemData":{"id":34923,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"December","DOI":"10.13140/2.1.2031.9048","language":"es","title":"Capítulo I. Aproximación a los conocimientos tradicionales relativos a la biodiversidad","URL":"https://doi.org/10.13140/2.1.2031.9048","author":[{"family":"Salas","given":"B."},{"family":"Garnatje","given":"T."},{"family":"National","given":"S."},{"family":"Latorre","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyes-garc","given":"V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Chile ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1mtdgnnrbc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Defeo, 2016; Gelcich, 2010, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Defeo, 2016; Gelcich, 2010, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MVWWMNWF"],"itemData":{"id":34288,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Co-management (Co-M), de?ned as the sharing of management tasks and responsibilities between governments and local users, is emerging as a powerful institutional arrangement to redress ?sheries paradigm failures, yet long-term assessments of its performance are lacking. A comparative analysis of ?ve smallscale Latin American shell?sheries was conducted to identify factors suggesting success and failure. In Chile, Uruguay and Mexico Co-M produced positive effects, including stabilization of landings at low levels, increase in abundance, CPUE, unit prices and revenues per unit of effort, and reduced interannual variability in several ?shery indicators, particularly in landings. Co-M was successful because it was mainly bottom-up implemented and accompanied by-catch shares (spatial property rights and community quotas). By contrast, Co-M implementation was unable to prevent the collapse of the Galapagos sea cucumber ?shery, as re?ected by a decrease in abundance and CPUE. Negative effects were also observed in the Galapagos spiny lobster ?shery during Co-M implementation. However, recovery was observed in recent years, re?ected in a stabilization of ?shing effort and the highest CPUE and economic revenues observed since the beginning of the Co-M implementation phase. The combined effects of market forces, climate variability and a moratorium on ?shing effort were critical in ?shery recovery. We conclude that Co-M is not a blueprint that can be applied to all shell?sheries to enhance their governability. These social–ecological systems need to be managed by jointly addressing problems related to the resources, their marine environment and the people targeting them, accounting for their socioeconomic and cultural contexts.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12101","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"176-192","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Co-management in Latin American small-scale shellfisheries: assessment from long-term case studies","title-short":"Co-management in Latin American small-scale shellfisheries","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/faf.12101","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Defeo","given":"Omar"},{"family":"Castrejón","given":"Mauricio"},{"family":"Pérez-Castañeda","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Castilla","given":"Juan C"},{"family":"Gutiérrez","given":"Nicolás L"},{"family":"Essington","given":"Timothy E"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",3]]}}},{"id":363,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EVV9MAHV"],"itemData":{"id":363,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1012021107","issue":"39","page":"16794-16799","title":"Navigating transformations in governance of Chilean marine coastal resources","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-78049276265&doi=10.1073%2fpnas.1012021107&partnerID=40&md5=0a50cd77256a5b88f2095e7c9f9f915c","volume":"107","author":[{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"T.P."},{"family":"Olsson","given":"P."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"Defeo","given":"O."},{"family":"Fernández","given":"M."},{"family":"Foale","given":"S."},{"family":"Gunderson","given":"L.H."},{"family":"Rodríguez-Sickert","given":"C."},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"M."},{"family":"Steneck","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Castilla","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":364,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KY3M28JI"],"itemData":{"id":364,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Bulletin of Marine Science","DOI":"10.5343/bms.2015.1082","issue":"1","page":"53-67","title":"Fishers' perceptions on the Chilean coastal TURF system after two decades: Problems, benefits, and emerging needs","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85009956088&doi=10.5343%2fbms.2015.1082&partnerID=40&md5=282619c33be9c7354daa0444ec5ce5c2","volume":"93","author":[{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Cinner","given":"J."},{"family":"Donlan","given":"C.J."},{"family":"Tapia-Lewin","given":"S."},{"family":"Godoy","given":"N."},{"family":"Castilla","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Uruguay ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1md1cabjq1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Defeo, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Defeo, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MVWWMNWF"],"itemData":{"id":34288,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Co-management (Co-M), de?ned as the sharing of management tasks and responsibilities between governments and local users, is emerging as a powerful institutional arrangement to redress ?sheries paradigm failures, yet long-term assessments of its performance are lacking. A comparative analysis of ?ve smallscale Latin American shell?sheries was conducted to identify factors suggesting success and failure. In Chile, Uruguay and Mexico Co-M produced positive effects, including stabilization of landings at low levels, increase in abundance, CPUE, unit prices and revenues per unit of effort, and reduced interannual variability in several ?shery indicators, particularly in landings. Co-M was successful because it was mainly bottom-up implemented and accompanied by-catch shares (spatial property rights and community quotas). By contrast, Co-M implementation was unable to prevent the collapse of the Galapagos sea cucumber ?shery, as re?ected by a decrease in abundance and CPUE. Negative effects were also observed in the Galapagos spiny lobster ?shery during Co-M implementation. However, recovery was observed in recent years, re?ected in a stabilization of ?shing effort and the highest CPUE and economic revenues observed since the beginning of the Co-M implementation phase. The combined effects of market forces, climate variability and a moratorium on ?shing effort were critical in ?shery recovery. We conclude that Co-M is not a blueprint that can be applied to all shell?sheries to enhance their governability. These social–ecological systems need to be managed by jointly addressing problems related to the resources, their marine environment and the people targeting them, accounting for their socioeconomic and cultural contexts.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12101","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"176-192","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Co-management in Latin American small-scale shellfisheries: assessment from long-term case 