2.2.1 Overview of approach
“Sustainable use” can mean very different things to different people, agencies, and institutions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"30ofVaZE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cooney, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Cooney, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30664,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GIQ6WPGX"],"itemData":{"id":30664,"type":"report","event-place":"White Oak Plantation, Florida","page":"76","publisher":"IUCN Species Survival Commission's Sustainable Use Specialist Group","publisher-place":"White Oak Plantation, Florida","title":"Sustainable Use: Concepts, Ambiguities, Challenges","URL":"https://www.iucn.org/sites/dev/files/whiteoakmtgfinalbackgroundjuly07.pdf","author":[{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ideas about sustainable use have also varied greatly over time. The scientific (natural and social) and economic/policy literature on the concept of sustainability and sustainable use reviewed in sections 2.2.2 ad 2.2.3 is dominated by publications from the perspectives of countries from the Global North, particularly prior to the 21st century. With many of the foundational policy documents drafted and negotiated in the late 20th century these perspectives on sustainable use are prominent in the language of international agreements and other policy documents. However, concepts of sustainable relations of humans and nature are found in all cultures, and not solely cultures rooted in the western, largely Judeo-Christian world. By the United Nations Conference on Sustainable Development in 1992, the voices of indigenous peoples and local communities were increasingly prominent, with recognition that their cultural practices and traditional livelihoods have been tied closely to nature, often including values and approaches that are inherently oriented to sustainable uses of nature. This knowledge of indigenous peoples and local communities is recognized by IPBES and increasingly by the international policy world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Cql76bd4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hill, 2020; Thaman, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Hill, 2020; Thaman, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33315,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J2FVG23E"],"itemData":{"id":33315,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2019.12.006","ISSN":"18773435","journalAbbreviation":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","language":"en","page":"8-20","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Working with Indigenous, local and scientific knowledge in assessments of nature and nature’s linkages with people","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1877343519301447","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Hill","given":"Rosemary"},{"family":"Adem","given":"Çi?dem"},{"family":"Alangui","given":"Wilfred V"},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Zsolt"},{"family":"Aumeeruddy-Thomas","given":"Yildiz"},{"family":"Bridgewater","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Tengö","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Thaman","given":"Randy"},{"family":"Adou Yao","given":"Constant Y"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Carino","given":"Joji"},{"family":"Carneiro da Cunha","given":"Manuela"},{"family":"Diaw","given":"Mariteuw C"},{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Viviana E"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Judy"},{"family":"Hardison","given":"Preston"},{"family":"Ichikawa","given":"Kaoru"},{"family":"Kariuki","given":"Peris"},{"family":"Karki","given":"Madhav"},{"family":"Lyver","given":"Phil OB"},{"family":"Malmer","given":"Pernilla"},{"family":"Masardule","given":"Onel"},{"family":"Oteng Yeboah","given":"Alfred A"},{"family":"Pacheco","given":"Diego"},{"family":"Pataridze","given":"Tamar"},{"family":"Perez","given":"Edgar"},{"family":"Roué","given":"Michèle-Marie"},{"family":"Roba","given":"Hassan"},{"family":"Rubis","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Saito","given":"Osamu"},{"family":"Xue","given":"Dayuan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",4]]}}},{"id":30662,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C2NJAUIB"],"itemData":{"id":30662,"type":"report","event-place":"Paris, UNESCO","genre":"IPBES Expert Meeting Report","page":"49","publisher":"UNESCO/UNU","publisher-place":"Paris, UNESCO","title":"The Contribution of Indigenous and Local Knowledge Systems to IPBES: Building Synergies with Science","URL":"https://www.besnet.world/sites/default/files//mediafile/The%20Contribution%20of%20local%20and%20indiogenous%20knowledge%20to%20Ipbes.pdf","author":[{"family":"Thaman","given":"R."},{"family":"Lyver","given":"P."},{"family":"Mpande","given":"R."},{"family":"Perez","given":"E."},{"family":"Cariño","given":"J."},{"family":"Takeuchi","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Section 2.2.4 introduces some of the diversity of conceptualizations and perspectives of indigenous peoples and local communities on the notion of “sustainable use”, together these overviews of evolving perspectives provides a foundation to discuss what differing worldviews, values and resultant conceptualizations may mean for policies and practices on the sustainable use of wild species.
     
2.2.2 Historical development of the concept of “sustainable use” in the global conservation arena
Ideas and conceptualizations of sustainability have a long and complex history. In this section, the historical background of academic, largely western, conceptualizations of sustainability is presented, focusing mainly on aspects related to the sustainable use of wild species. Following this, the narrower and shorter history of the explicit use of the concept ‘sustainable use of wild species’ is narrated. The historical account presented in this section is based on a literature review of 179 sources. The data management report for this review is available at: 10.5281/zenodo.6472995. 
2.2.2.1. Historical background of western conceptualizations of sustainability

The word ‘sustainability’ did not emerge in the English language until the early 1970s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"R1bDwYxM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. A. Simpson, 1989)","plainCitation":"(J. A. Simpson, 1989)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30638,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7NCSRFLZ"],"itemData":{"id":30638,"type":"book","call-number":"PE1625 .O87 1991","edition":"2nd ed","event-place":"Oxford : Oxford ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-19-861186-8","number-of-pages":"20","publisher":"Clarendon Press ; Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"Oxford : Oxford ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The Oxford English dictionary","editor":[{"family":"Simpson","given":"J. A."},{"family":"Weiner","given":"E. S. C."},{"family":"Oxford University Press","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1989"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but the German equivalent, Nachhaltigkeit, was coined in the mid-eighteenth century ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bMgBbd4O","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Warde, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Warde, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30660,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YJKSYCX7"],"itemData":{"id":30660,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This essay attempts something a little peculiar: a study of the genesis of a concept within discourses which did not, in fact, use the word. This is at least true of “sustainability” in English. The emergence of the German equivalent,\\n              Nachhaltigkeit\\n              , which might also be expressed by the idea of “lasting-ness”, is, however, usually dated to the use of the word\\n              nachhalthende\\n              by Hanns Carl von Carlowitz in his\\n              Sylvicultura oeconomica\\n              of 1713, the first great forestry manual of the eighteenth century. In fact, the term can be found in the 1650s.","container-title":"Modern Intellectual History","DOI":"10.1017/S1479244311000096","ISSN":"1479-2443, 1479-2451","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Mod. Intell. Hist.","language":"en","page":"153-170","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The invention of sustainability","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S1479244311000096/type/journal_article","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Warde","given":"Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the historical background of ideas and conceptualizations of sustainability extends beyond explicit use of the term. The survival and well-being of people has always depended on a sustained output of food and other material derived from natural resources. Considerations of sustained yield from the natural environment have existed at least since the agrarian revolution. However, the historical background of the conceptualization of sustainability reflects a societal issue and discourse that comprises more than local concerns over needs and benefits.
      Sustainability in this context emerged in early modern Europe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HDbLjTnv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Warde, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Warde, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FYD2NDU5"],"itemData":{"id":30666,"type":"book","edition":"1","ISBN":"978-1-316-58476-7","note":"DOI: 10.1017/9781316584767","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Invention of Sustainability: Nature and Destiny, c.1500–1870","title-short":"The Invention of Sustainability","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/9781316584767/type/book","author":[{"family":"Warde","given":"Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Japan ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ROgFNwpk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Caradonna, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Caradonna, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30661,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I435AWQ2"],"itemData":{"id":30661,"type":"book","call-number":"GE195 .C379 2014","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-0-19-937240-9","number-of-pages":"331","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Sustainability: a history","title-short":"Sustainability","author":[{"family":"Caradonna","given":"Jeremy L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The conceptual development was to some degree global , as aspects of it related to the European exploration and colonialism of the period ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"T8K8OOE8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grove, 1995)","plainCitation":"(Grove, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30663,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E86ZNN4V"],"itemData":{"id":30663,"type":"book","call-number":"GE195 .G76 1995","collection-title":"Studies in environment and history","event-place":"Cambridge ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-521-40385-6","number-of-pages":"540","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Green imperialism: colonial expansion, tropical island Edens, and the origins of environmentalism, 1600-1860","title-short":"Green imperialism","author":[{"family":"Grove","given":"Richard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The discourse on sustainability involved many factors related to the political, economic and environmental management of emerging nation-States and their increasingly proactive governance from the sixteenth century ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Fyr3thBR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Warde, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Warde, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FYD2NDU5"],"itemData":{"id":30666,"type":"book","edition":"1","ISBN":"978-1-316-58476-7","note":"DOI: 10.1017/9781316584767","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Invention of Sustainability: Nature and Destiny, c.1500–1870","title-short":"The Invention of Sustainability","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/9781316584767/type/book","author":[{"family":"Warde","given":"Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As the state came to rely on revenue from the exploitation of natural resources to compete internationally in commerce, war and religion, the natural world increasingly became a political issue and object of the governance by nation states. The earliest discourses about state-governed sustained yield centered around the supply of grain and timber products ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EPCBlZtr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grober & Cunningham, 2012; Scott, 1998; Warde, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Grober & Cunningham, 2012; Scott, 1998; Warde, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30656,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JMRBC9GT"],"itemData":{"id":30656,"type":"book","call-number":"GE196 .G7613 2012","event-place":"Totnes, UK","ISBN":"978-0-85784-045-5","language":"eng","note":"OCLC: ocn794815276","number-of-pages":"221","publisher":"Green Books","publisher-place":"Totnes, UK","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Sustainability: a cultural history","title-short":"Sustainability","author":[{"family":"Grober","given":"Ulrich"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Ray"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":30668,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B48JA3PU"],"itemData":{"id":30668,"type":"book","collection-title":"Yale agrarian studies","edition":"Nachdr.","event-place":"New Haven, Conn.","ISBN":"978-0-300-07815-2","language":"eng","note":"OCLC: 553929986","number-of-pages":"445","publisher":"Yale Univ. Press","publisher-place":"New Haven, Conn.","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Seeing like a state: how certain schemes to improve the human condition have failed","title-short":"Seeing like a state","author":[{"family":"Scott","given":"James C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":30666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FYD2NDU5"],"itemData":{"id":30666,"type":"book","edition":"1","ISBN":"978-1-316-58476-7","note":"DOI: 10.1017/9781316584767","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Invention of Sustainability: Nature and Destiny, c.1500–1870","title-short":"The Invention of Sustainability","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/9781316584767/type/book","author":[{"family":"Warde","given":"Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The development of scientific, knowledge-producing networks in early modern Europe also played a central role in emerging discourse on sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Lcd3nOAR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Warde, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Warde, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FYD2NDU5"],"itemData":{"id":30666,"type":"book","edition":"1","ISBN":"978-1-316-58476-7","note":"DOI: 10.1017/9781316584767","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Invention of Sustainability: Nature and Destiny, c.1500–1870","title-short":"The Invention of Sustainability","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/9781316584767/type/book","author":[{"family":"Warde","given":"Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many of the active network participants optimistically saw this knowledge generation as part of a larger project to improve states’ and privileged individuals’ wealth by increasing output of natural resources. By the end of the eighteenth century, development of methodologies for survey, measurement and control provided a quantifiable framework that enabled assessment of the degree to which natural resource output was sustained or not. This was particularly well developed within the emerging field of forestry. Technologies that made nature ‘legible’ to States in a quantified manner, were decisive in framing a particular conceptualization in the developing discourse on sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3qVoQza3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Scott, 1998; Warde, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Scott, 1998; Warde, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30668,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B48JA3PU"],"itemData":{"id":30668,"type":"book","collection-title":"Yale agrarian studies","edition":"Nachdr.","event-place":"New Haven, Conn.","ISBN":"978-0-300-07815-2","language":"eng","note":"OCLC: 553929986","number-of-pages":"445","publisher":"Yale Univ. Press","publisher-place":"New Haven, Conn.","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Seeing like a state: how certain schemes to improve the human condition have failed","title-short":"Seeing like a state","author":[{"family":"Scott","given":"James C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":30666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FYD2NDU5"],"itemData":{"id":30666,"type":"book","edition":"1","ISBN":"978-1-316-58476-7","note":"DOI: 10.1017/9781316584767","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Invention of Sustainability: Nature and Destiny, c.1500–1870","title-short":"The Invention of Sustainability","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/9781316584767/type/book","author":[{"family":"Warde","given":"Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). During the industrial revolution, the limits of natural resources and degradation of environments became gradually clearer, due both to improved knowledge generation and highly visible environmental destruction. As a result, some of the optimism and beliefs in unlimited progress and growth diminished. The optimism was, replaced by a growing concern with sustainability and the realization that development and progress could potentially be unsustainable, and that individuals, the state and the environment might suffer from it ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7auOCS6r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dresner, 2008; Warde, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Dresner, 2008; Warde, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30659,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A2YZ44NQ"],"itemData":{"id":30659,"type":"book","call-number":"HD75.6 .D735 2008","edition":"2nd ed","event-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","ISBN":"978-1-84407-496-9","note":"OCLC: ocn227000213","number-of-pages":"205","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The principles of sustainability","author":[{"family":"Dresner","given":"Simon"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":30666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FYD2NDU5"],"itemData":{"id":30666,"type":"book","edition":"1","ISBN":"978-1-316-58476-7","note":"DOI: 10.1017/9781316584767","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Invention of Sustainability: Nature and Destiny, c.1500–1870","title-short":"The Invention of Sustainability","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/9781316584767/type/book","author":[{"family":"Warde","given":"Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Timber was a valuable natural resource to the emerging nation states of Europe due to its military and industrial uses. As the industrial revolution and growing populations required increasing amounts of wood products, timber scarcity became a problem and issue for governance in many localities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ja2SuWYm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Caradonna, 2014; Warde, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Caradonna, 2014; Warde, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30661,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I435AWQ2"],"itemData":{"id":30661,"type":"book","call-number":"GE195 .C379 2014","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-0-19-937240-9","number-of-pages":"331","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Sustainability: a history","title-short":"Sustainability","author":[{"family":"Caradonna","given":"Jeremy L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":30666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FYD2NDU5"],"itemData":{"id":30666,"type":"book","edition":"1","ISBN":"978-1-316-58476-7","note":"DOI: 10.1017/9781316584767","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Invention of Sustainability: Nature and Destiny, c.1500–1870","title-short":"The Invention of Sustainability","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/9781316584767/type/book","author":[{"family":"Warde","given":"Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another factor that might have advanced discourses of sustainability related to timber was the long time horizon compared to other wild species in use; meeting timber demands required planning and governance that spanned human generations. It was in this context that Hans Carl von Carlowitz wrote Sylvicultura oeconomica, often viewed as the work that established forestry as a science and management field, and the first to explicitly address sustainable use of a wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ODYzNVOs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(von Carlowitz, 1713)","plainCitation":"(von Carlowitz, 1713)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30144,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ICB2ITN7"],"itemData":{"id":30144,"type":"book","event-place":"Braun, Leipzig","publisher":"Anweisung zur wilden Baum-Zucht","publisher-place":"Braun, Leipzig","title":"Sylvicultura oeconomica","author":[{"family":"Carlowitz","given":"H.C.","non-dropping-particle":"von"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1713"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Von Carlowitz saw the growing scarcity of wood as a threat to further progress of western civilization, and argued that if replanted and cultivated properly forests could produce a significantly higher timber yield that could be sustained over time ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VNx61QDr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Warde, 2018; Wiersum, 1995; Worster, 1993)","plainCitation":"(Warde, 2018; Wiersum, 1995; Worster, 1993)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FYD2NDU5"],"itemData":{"id":30666,"type":"book","edition":"1","ISBN":"978-1-316-58476-7","note":"DOI: 10.1017/9781316584767","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Invention of Sustainability: Nature and Destiny, c.1500–1870","title-short":"The Invention of Sustainability","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/9781316584767/type/book","author":[{"family":"Warde","given":"Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,30]]}}},{"id":30476,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MYLGN2SU"],"itemData":{"id":30476,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Since the end of the 1980s the concept of sustainable development has gained general acceptance, but much uncertainty still exists on how to operationalize this concept. In forestry the concept of sustainability has been an accepted principle since the 18th century. The experiences with its application in forestry may contribute towards obtaining a better insight into the implications and operational significance of the concept of sustainability. This article describes the history of sustainability in forestry, including the various social values on which its interpretation has been based. The original principle of sustained yield has gradually been broadened to a more inclusive principle of sustainable forest management. The dynamics in social valuation of forest resources resulted in various attempts at practical operationalization of the principle. Notwithstanding 200 years of efforts to operationalize the concept of sustainability, its exact application in forestry remains troublesome. Three lessons are drawn: (1) the need to recognize the different nature of ecological limits and social dynamics, (2) the role of dynamic social values with respect to forest resources, and (3) the significance of operational experiences in trying to attain sustainability within a concrete context. © 1995 Springer-Verlag New York Inc.","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/BF02471975","ISSN":"0364152X","issue":"3","page":"321–329","title":"200 years of sustainability in forestry: Lessons from history","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Wiersum","given":"K. Freerk"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}},{"id":30655,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UUZV7P94"],"itemData":{"id":30655,"type":"book","call-number":"GF503 .W68 1993","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-0-19-507624-0","number-of-pages":"255","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The wealth of nature: environmental history and the ecological imagination","title-short":"The wealth of nature","author":[{"family":"Worster","given":"Donald"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. John Evelyn, Jean-Baptiste Colbert, Jean-Jacques Rousseau and Thomas Malthus also contributed to the further development of the discourse on sustainable forestry, and sustainability more generally ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qj3j7M12","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Caradonna, 2014; Dale, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Caradonna, 2014; Dale, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30661,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I435AWQ2"],"itemData":{"id":30661,"type":"book","call-number":"GE195 .C379 2014","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-0-19-937240-9","number-of-pages":"331","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Sustainability: a history","title-short":"Sustainability","author":[{"family":"Caradonna","given":"Jeremy L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":30658,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MIJQERPK"],"itemData":{"id":30658,"type":"chapter","abstract":"\\"The Routledge Handbook of the History of Sustainability is a far-reaching survey of the deep and contemporary history of sustainability. This innovative resource will help to define the history of sustainability as an identifiable field. It provides a unique resource for advanced undergraduates, graduate students, and scholars, and delivers essential context for understanding the current state and future path of the sustainability movement. The history of sustainability is an increasingly important domain within the discipline of history, which draws on an interdisciplinary set of fields, ranging from energy studies, transportation, and urbanism to environmental history, economics, and philosophy. Key sections in this handbook cover the historiography of sustainability, resilience and collapse in historical societies, the deep roots of sustainability (seventeenth century to nineteenth century), the recent history of sustainability (twentieth century to present), and core issues and key debates in sustainability. This handbook is an invaluable research and teaching tool for those interested in the history and development of sustainability and an essential resource for the many sustainability studies programs that now exist in the world's universities.\\"--Provided by publisher.","container-title":"Routledge handbook of the history of sustainability","ISBN":"978-0-367-85584-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1120972110","publisher":"Routledge","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Sustaining what? Scarcity, growth, and the natural order in the discourse on sustainability","author":[{"family":"Dale","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Most western nations established forestry institutions to manage their forests in line with this ideology in the 18th and 19th century.
      The pursuit of increased and sustained yield from natural resources that emerged with forestry in the 18th and 19th centuries had repercussions for the understanding and management of other wild species understood to be natural resources. Declining populations of wild terrestrial animals became a concern in the same period, both in Europe and, in particular, in areas under the influence of European colonization ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"r7pxpjuy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barrow, 2009; Worster, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Barrow, 2009; Worster, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30657,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X74Q3IYY"],"itemData":{"id":30657,"type":"book","call-number":"QL84.2 .B377 2009","event-place":"Chicago ; London","ISBN":"978-0-226-03814-8","number-of-pages":"497","publisher":"The University of Chicago Press","publisher-place":"Chicago ; London","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Nature's ghosts: confronting extinction from the age of Jefferson to the age of ecology","title-short":"Nature's ghosts","author":[{"family":"Barrow","given":"Mark V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":30654,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ANYE4PIJ"],"itemData":{"id":30654,"type":"book","call-number":"QH540.8 .W67 1994","collection-title":"Studies in environment and history","edition":"2nd ed","event-place":"Cambridge ; New York, NY, USA","ISBN":"978-0-521-45273-1","number-of-pages":"505","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge ; New York, NY, USA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Nature's economy: a history of ecological ideas","title-short":"Nature's economy","author":[{"family":"Worster","given":"Donald"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In North America, dramatic declines in once numerous species were clearly documented. During the 19th century, in particular, game animals came to be understood as natural resources in a utilitarian, resource conservation perspective inspired by agronomy and forestry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rswFRJMg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dunlap, 1988; Scott, 1998; Worster, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Dunlap, 1988; Scott, 1998; Worster, 1994)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30653,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VD5F2DF8"],"itemData":{"id":30653,"type":"book","event-place":"Princeton, N.J","ISBN":"978-0-691-04750-8","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 728663414","publisher":"Princeton University Press","publisher-place":"Princeton, N.J","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Saving America's wildlife","author":[{"family":"Dunlap","given":"Thomas R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1988"]]}}},{"id":30668,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B48JA3PU"],"itemData":{"id":30668,"type":"book","collection-title":"Yale agrarian studies","edition":"Nachdr.","event-place":"New Haven, Conn.","ISBN":"978-0-300-07815-2","language":"eng","note":"OCLC: 553929986","number-of-pages":"445","publisher":"Yale Univ. Press","publisher-place":"New Haven, Conn.","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Seeing like a state: how certain schemes to improve the human condition have failed","title-short":"Seeing like a state","author":[{"family":"Scott","given":"James C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":30654,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ANYE4PIJ"],"itemData":{"id":30654,"type":"book","call-number":"QH540.8 .W67 1994","collection-title":"Studies in environment and history","edition":"2nd ed","event-place":"Cambridge ; New York, NY, USA","ISBN":"978-0-521-45273-1","number-of-pages":"505","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge ; New York, NY, USA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Nature's economy: a history of ecological ideas","title-short":"Nature's economy","author":[{"family":"Worster","given":"Donald"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Correspondingly, game management institutions were established in many western nations and tasked with securing a maximized and sustained yield of game animals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Gz1pzd6E","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stokland, 2015; Worster, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Stokland, 2015; Worster, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30667,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B7RL3XLN"],"itemData":{"id":30667,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of the History of Biology","DOI":"10.1007/s10739-014-9393-0","ISSN":"0022-5010, 1573-0387","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Hist Biol","language":"en","page":"1-36","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Field Studies in Absentia: Counting and Monitoring from a Distance as Technologies of Government in Norwegian Wolf Management (1960s–2010s)","title-short":"Field Studies in Absentia","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10739-014-9393-0","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Stokland","given":"Håkon B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",2]]}}},{"id":30654,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ANYE4PIJ"],"itemData":{"id":30654,"type":"book","call-number":"QH540.8 .W67 1994","collection-title":"Studies in environment and history","edition":"2nd ed","event-place":"Cambridge ; New York, NY, USA","ISBN":"978-0-521-45273-1","number-of-pages":"505","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge ; New York, NY, USA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Nature's economy: a history of ecological ideas","title-short":"Nature's economy","author":[{"family":"Worster","given":"Donald"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The eradication of game predators was widely thought to be a prerequisite for fulfilling this task, and became central to ‘sustainability’ of game management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YG3N1Rmg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Coleman, 2004; M. J. Robinson, 2005; Stokland, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Coleman, 2004; M. J. Robinson, 2005; Stokland, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30379,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/76SUZH53"],"itemData":{"id":30379,"type":"book","abstract":"In this history of wolves in America - and of the humans who have hated and then loved them - Jon Coleman investigates a fraught relationship between two species and uncovers striking similarities, deadly differences, and, all too frequently, tragic misunderstanding.","ISBN":"978-0-300-11972-5","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 70671569","publisher":"Yale University Press","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Vicious: wolves and men in America","title-short":"Vicious","author":[{"family":"Coleman","given":"Jon T"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":30648,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/26JECT22"],"itemData":{"id":30648,"type":"book","call-number":"SF810.7.W65 R63 2005","event-place":"Boulder, Colo","ISBN":"978-0-87081-818-9","number-of-pages":"473","publisher":"University Press of Colorado","publisher-place":"Boulder, Colo","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Predatory bureaucracy: the extermination of wolves and the transformation of the West","title-short":"Predatory bureaucracy","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"Michael J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":30665,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JLC84XJ6"],"itemData":{"id":30665,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and History","DOI":"10.3197/096734016X14574329314326","ISSN":"09673407, 17527023","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"environ hist camb","language":"en","page":"191-227","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"How Many Wolves Does it Take to Protect the Population? Minimum Viable Population Size as a Technology of Government in Endangered Species Management (Norway, 1970s-2000s)","title-short":"How Many Wolves Does it Take to Protect the Population?","URL":"http://openurl.ingenta.com/content/xref?genre=article&issn=0967-3407&volume=22&issue=2&spage=191","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Stokland","given":"Håkon B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",5,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In the late 19th century, a more ecologically-based and romanticist conservation ideology emerged with growing environmental movements. This ideology was more inclined towards preservation and ecological limitations, and developed in dialogue and tension with the utilitarian conservation ideology ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5oHGS7Lk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Robinson, 2004; Worster, 1994)","plainCitation":"(J. Robinson, 2004; Worster, 1994)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30652,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AUFFDASW"],"itemData":{"id":30652,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2003.10.017","ISSN":"09218009","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"369-384","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Squaring the circle? Some thoughts on the idea of sustainable development","title-short":"Squaring the circle?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800904000175","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"John"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",4]]}}},{"id":30654,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ANYE4PIJ"],"itemData":{"id":30654,"type":"book","call-number":"QH540.8 .W67 1994","collection-title":"Studies in environment and history","edition":"2nd ed","event-place":"Cambridge ; New York, NY, USA","ISBN":"978-0-521-45273-1","number-of-pages":"505","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge ; New York, NY, USA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Nature's economy: a history of ecological ideas","title-short":"Nature's economy","author":[{"family":"Worster","given":"Donald"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These ideological developments are exemplified by the conservation ethos and practices of Americans John Muir and Gifford Pinchot, respectively ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AE7ZVVhg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. W. Johnson & Greenberg, 2018)","plainCitation":"(E. W. Johnson & Greenberg, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30651,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M953EJCP"],"itemData":{"id":30651,"type":"chapter","abstract":"\\"The Routledge Handbook of the History of Sustainability is a far-reaching survey of the deep and contemporary history of sustainability. This innovative resource will help to define the history of sustainability as an identifiable field. It provides a unique resource for advanced undergraduates, graduate students, and scholars, and delivers essential context for understanding the current state and future path of the sustainability movement. The history of sustainability is an increasingly important domain within the discipline of history, which draws on an interdisciplinary set of fields, ranging from energy studies, transportation, and urbanism to environmental history, economics, and philosophy. Key sections in this handbook cover the historiography of sustainability, resilience and collapse in historical societies, the deep roots of sustainability (seventeenth century to nineteenth century), the recent history of sustainability (twentieth century to present), and core issues and key debates in sustainability. This handbook is an invaluable research and teaching tool for those interested in the history and development of sustainability and an essential resource for the many sustainability studies programs that now exist in the world's universities.\\"--Provided by publisher.","container-title":"Routledge handbook of the history of sustainability","ISBN":"978-0-367-85584-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1120972110","publisher":"Routledge","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"The US environmental movement of the 1960s and 1970s: building frameworks of sustainability","author":[{"family":"Johnson","given":"E. W."},{"family":"Greenberg","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and the more utilitarian conservation ideology of Aldo Leopold (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BdVTZruA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Leopold, 1933, 1949)","plainCitation":"(Leopold, 1933, 1949)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31986,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PAWE3ZJG"],"itemData":{"id":31986,"type":"book","event-place":"New York; London","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 560822","publisher":"C. Scribner's Sons","publisher-place":"New York; London","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Game management","author":[{"family":"Leopold","given":"Aldo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1933"]]}}},{"id":30650,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XZIFUAU7"],"itemData":{"id":30650,"type":"book","edition":"First","event-place":"New York","number-of-pages":"212","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"New York","title":"A Sand County Almanac And Sketches Here and There","author":[{"family":"Leopold","given":"Aldo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1949"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Environmental movements became prominent in the “age of ecology” (1960s and 1970s) playing a central role in this formative phase of the sustainability concept ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"frHNaE0E","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. W. Johnson & Greenberg, 2018; Worster, 1994)","plainCitation":"(E. W. Johnson & Greenberg, 2018; Worster, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30651,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M953EJCP"],"itemData":{"id":30651,"type":"chapter","abstract":"\\"The Routledge Handbook of the History of Sustainability is a far-reaching survey of the deep and contemporary history of sustainability. This innovative resource will help to define the history of sustainability as an identifiable field. It provides a unique resource for advanced undergraduates, graduate students, and scholars, and delivers essential context for understanding the current state and future path of the sustainability movement. The history of sustainability is an increasingly important domain within the discipline of history, which draws on an interdisciplinary set of fields, ranging from energy studies, transportation, and urbanism to environmental history, economics, and philosophy. Key sections in this handbook cover the historiography of sustainability, resilience and collapse in historical societies, the deep roots of sustainability (seventeenth century to nineteenth century), the recent history of sustainability (twentieth century to present), and core issues and key debates in sustainability. This handbook is an invaluable research and teaching tool for those interested in the history and development of sustainability and an essential resource for the many sustainability studies programs that now exist in the world's universities.\\"--Provided by publisher.","container-title":"Routledge handbook of the history of sustainability","ISBN":"978-0-367-85584-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1120972110","publisher":"Routledge","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"The US environmental movement of the 1960s and 1970s: building frameworks of sustainability","author":[{"family":"Johnson","given":"E. W."},{"family":"Greenberg","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":30654,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ANYE4PIJ"],"itemData":{"id":30654,"type":"book","call-number":"QH540.8 .W67 1994","collection-title":"Studies in environment and history","edition":"2nd ed","event-place":"Cambridge ; New York, NY, USA","ISBN":"978-0-521-45273-1","number-of-pages":"505","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge ; New York, NY, USA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Nature's economy: a history of ecological ideas","title-short":"Nature's economy","author":[{"family":"Worster","given":"Donald"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The issues of pollution and pesticides, as well as ecological limits to growth, received increased attention after publications such as Rachel Carson’s Silent Spring ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"saxrco6g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carson, 1962)","plainCitation":"(Carson, 1962)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30647,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YVNUC2GB"],"itemData":{"id":30647,"type":"book","edition":"Houghton Mifflin","event-place":"Boston, MA","publisher-place":"Boston, MA","title":"Silent Spring","author":[{"family":"Carson","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1962"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and the Club of Rome’s Limits to Growth ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"40QdUzl3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Meadows, 1972)","plainCitation":"(Meadows, 1972)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33785,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SP5GYQJR"],"itemData":{"id":33785,"type":"book","call-number":"HC59 .L54","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-0-87663-165-2","number-of-pages":"205","publisher":"Universe Books","publisher-place":"New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The Limits to growth: a report for the Club of Rome's project on the predicament of mankind","title-short":"The Limits to growth","editor":[{"family":"Meadows","given":"Donella H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1972"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Drawing public attention to environmental concerns, and emphasizing the science of ecology and a greater sensitivity to the ways in which human socio-economic and biophysical systems interact, the environmental movements prepared the ground for ecological issues to become prominent on governmental, business, and international institutions’ agendas. A crucial step in this development was the linking of human well-being and economic development to ecological systems – familiar now as a central tenet of sustainability – in issues such as pesticides, water pollution, and smog ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LxSZZbFf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. W. Johnson & Greenberg, 2018; Worster, 1994)","plainCitation":"(E. W. Johnson & Greenberg, 2018; Worster, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30651,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M953EJCP"],"itemData":{"id":30651,"type":"chapter","abstract":"\\"The Routledge Handbook of the History of Sustainability is a far-reaching survey of the deep and contemporary history of sustainability. This innovative resource will help to define the history of sustainability as an identifiable field. It provides a unique resource for advanced undergraduates, graduate students, and scholars, and delivers essential context for understanding the current state and future path of the sustainability movement. The history of sustainability is an increasingly important domain within the discipline of history, which draws on an interdisciplinary set of fields, ranging from energy studies, transportation, and urbanism to environmental history, economics, and philosophy. Key sections in this handbook cover the historiography of sustainability, resilience and collapse in historical societies, the deep roots of sustainability (seventeenth century to nineteenth century), the recent history of sustainability (twentieth century to present), and core issues and key debates in sustainability. This handbook is an invaluable research and teaching tool for those interested in the history and development of sustainability and an essential resource for the many sustainability studies programs that now exist in the world's universities.\\"--Provided by publisher.","container-title":"Routledge handbook of the history of sustainability","ISBN":"978-0-367-85584-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1120972110","publisher":"Routledge","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"The US environmental movement of the 1960s and 1970s: building frameworks of sustainability","author":[{"family":"Johnson","given":"E. W."},{"family":"Greenberg","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":30654,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ANYE4PIJ"],"itemData":{"id":30654,"type":"book","call-number":"QH540.8 .W67 1994","collection-title":"Studies in environment and history","edition":"2nd ed","event-place":"Cambridge ; New York, NY, USA","ISBN":"978-0-521-45273-1","number-of-pages":"505","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge ; New York, NY, USA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Nature's economy: a history of ecological ideas","title-short":"Nature's economy","author":[{"family":"Worster","given":"Donald"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In the 1980s, sustainability became an identifiable and publicly discussed concept, growing out of the work of ecologists, economists, systems theorists, energy specialists, environmentalists, biologists and other scientists, and diplomats or appointees within the Organization of Economic Cooperation and Development and the United Nations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"300CC72z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Caradonna, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Caradonna, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30646,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y7CUZRAG"],"itemData":{"id":30646,"type":"chapter","abstract":"\\"The Routledge Handbook of the History of Sustainability is a far-reaching survey of the deep and contemporary history of sustainability. This innovative resource will help to define the history of sustainability as an identifiable field. It provides a unique resource for advanced undergraduates, graduate students, and scholars, and delivers essential context for understanding the current state and future path of the sustainability movement. The history of sustainability is an increasingly important domain within the discipline of history, which draws on an interdisciplinary set of fields, ranging from energy studies, transportation, and urbanism to environmental history, economics, and philosophy. Key sections in this handbook cover the historiography of sustainability, resilience and collapse in historical societies, the deep roots of sustainability (seventeenth century to nineteenth century), the recent history of sustainability (twentieth century to present), and core issues and key debates in sustainability. This handbook is an invaluable research and teaching tool for those interested in the history and development of sustainability and an essential resource for the many sustainability studies programs that now exist in the world's universities.\\"--Provided by publisher.","container-title":"Routledge handbook of the history of sustainability","ISBN":"978-0-367-85584-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1120972110","publisher":"Routledge","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Sustainability: a new historiography","author":[{"family":"Caradonna","given":"Jeremy L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The political context of the 1980s, in which free-market economic logics rose to dominant influence, posed a major challenge to ideas about the limits of growth and ecological concerns. The concept of sustainability, which focused on self-interested movement towards production and development processes with both ecological and economic benefits, found its place on the international stage in this decade through the merger of environment and development concerns ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fbynAGgd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. W. Johnson & Greenberg, 2018)","plainCitation":"(E. W. Johnson & Greenberg, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30651,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M953EJCP"],"itemData":{"id":30651,"type":"chapter","abstract":"\\"The Routledge Handbook of the History of Sustainability is a far-reaching survey of the deep and contemporary history of sustainability. This innovative resource will help to define the history of sustainability as an identifiable field. It provides a unique resource for advanced undergraduates, graduate students, and scholars, and delivers essential context for understanding the current state and future path of the sustainability movement. The history of sustainability is an increasingly important domain within the discipline of history, which draws on an interdisciplinary set of fields, ranging from energy studies, transportation, and urbanism to environmental history, economics, and philosophy. Key sections in this handbook cover the historiography of sustainability, resilience and collapse in historical societies, the deep roots of sustainability (seventeenth century to nineteenth century), the recent history of sustainability (twentieth century to present), and core issues and key debates in sustainability. This handbook is an invaluable research and teaching tool for those interested in the history and development of sustainability and an essential resource for the many sustainability studies programs that now exist in the world's universities.\\"--Provided by publisher.","container-title":"Routledge handbook of the history of sustainability","ISBN":"978-0-367-85584-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1120972110","publisher":"Routledge","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"The US environmental movement of the 1960s and 1970s: building frameworks of sustainability","author":[{"family":"Johnson","given":"E. W."},{"family":"Greenberg","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The United Nations adopted the concept of sustainable development in the 1980s and sponsored a series of conferences and committees notably the 1972 Stockholm conference, the 1980 report World Conservation Strategy, the 1982 World Charter for Nature, and the World Commission on Environment and Development that produced the report Our Common Future (ibid). The latter popularized the notion that sustainability is about meeting current needs without jeopardizing the ability of future generations to satisfy their own needs.
      Through initiatives such as the 1992 Rio Earth Summit, the 2005 Millennium Development Goals, and the 2015 Sustainable Development Goals, sustainability has become a mainstream concern. Now a standard feature of public and political discourse, most major institutions in the industrialized world have either a department or office of sustainability, and almost any business of a certain size has identified Sustainable Development Goals to which it contributes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"m4qVpWkD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Caradonna, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Caradonna, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30646,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y7CUZRAG"],"itemData":{"id":30646,"type":"chapter","abstract":"\\"The Routledge Handbook of the History of Sustainability is a far-reaching survey of the deep and contemporary history of sustainability. This innovative resource will help to define the history of sustainability as an identifiable field. It provides a unique resource for advanced undergraduates, graduate students, and scholars, and delivers essential context for understanding the current state and future path of the sustainability movement. The history of sustainability is an increasingly important domain within the discipline of history, which draws on an interdisciplinary set of fields, ranging from energy studies, transportation, and urbanism to environmental history, economics, and philosophy. Key sections in this handbook cover the historiography of sustainability, resilience and collapse in historical societies, the deep roots of sustainability (seventeenth century to nineteenth century), the recent history of sustainability (twentieth century to present), and core issues and key debates in sustainability. This handbook is an invaluable research and teaching tool for those interested in the history and development of sustainability and an essential resource for the many sustainability studies programs that now exist in the world's universities.\\"--Provided by publisher.","container-title":"Routledge handbook of the history of sustainability","ISBN":"978-0-367-85584-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1120972110","publisher":"Routledge","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Sustainability: a new historiography","author":[{"family":"Caradonna","given":"Jeremy L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The sustainability concept is seen by many as a critical reappraisal of the values of industrialism and growth-based capitalism, but has also received much criticism. A common critique of the concept, and particularly of the ‘sustainable development’ variant with its explicit focus on development, is that it represents little more than business-as-usual economic development that does not value the idea of living within biophysical limits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3O5fiwH5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Caradonna, 2018; Purvis, 2019; J. Robinson, 2004; Worster, 1993)","plainCitation":"(Caradonna, 2018; Purvis, 2019; J. Robinson, 2004; Worster, 1993)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30646,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y7CUZRAG"],"itemData":{"id":30646,"type":"chapter","abstract":"\\"The Routledge Handbook of the History of Sustainability is a far-reaching survey of the deep and contemporary history of sustainability. This innovative resource will help to define the history of sustainability as an identifiable field. It provides a unique resource for advanced undergraduates, graduate students, and scholars, and delivers essential context for understanding the current state and future path of the sustainability movement. The history of sustainability is an increasingly important domain within the discipline of history, which draws on an interdisciplinary set of fields, ranging from energy studies, transportation, and urbanism to environmental history, economics, and philosophy. Key sections in this handbook cover the historiography of sustainability, resilience and collapse in historical societies, the deep roots of sustainability (seventeenth century to nineteenth century), the recent history of sustainability (twentieth century to present), and core issues and key debates in sustainability. This handbook is an invaluable research and teaching tool for those interested in the history and development of sustainability and an essential resource for the many sustainability studies programs that now exist in the world's universities.\\"--Provided by publisher.","container-title":"Routledge handbook of the history of sustainability","ISBN":"978-0-367-85584-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1120972110","publisher":"Routledge","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Sustainability: a new historiography","author":[{"family":"Caradonna","given":"Jeremy L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":31701,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IRWPBZRX"],"itemData":{"id":31701,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability Science","DOI":"10.1007/s11625-018-0627-5","ISSN":"1862-4065, 1862-4057","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Sustain Sci","language":"en","page":"681-695","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Three pillars of sustainability: in search of conceptual origins","title-short":"Three pillars of sustainability","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11625-018-0627-5","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Purvis","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Mao","given":"Yong"},{"family":"Robinson","given":"Darren"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",5]]}}},{"id":30652,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AUFFDASW"],"itemData":{"id":30652,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2003.10.017","ISSN":"09218009","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"369-384","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Squaring the circle? Some thoughts on the idea of sustainable development","title-short":"Squaring the circle?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800904000175","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"John"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",4]]}}},{"id":30655,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UUZV7P94"],"itemData":{"id":30655,"type":"book","call-number":"GF503 .W68 1993","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-0-19-507624-0","number-of-pages":"255","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The wealth of nature: environmental history and the ecological imagination","title-short":"The wealth of nature","author":[{"family":"Worster","given":"Donald"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The tensions and critiques that have at times riddled the sustainability concept have a historical context. The sustainability concept has roots in ideologies of both economic growth and ecological limitations, intertwined in discourses on the maximization of natural resources use, the progress of nation states, environmental preservation, pollution and human health, ecological science, international collaboration and more, and has developed across multiple and shifting governance contexts and academic disciplines. As a consequence, sustainability has been conceptualized in multiple and shifting ways by different actors over time, including different understandings of the concept that stand in internal tension ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1Bs6Qkl4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Borowy, 2018; Caradonna, 2014; Purvis, 2019; J. Robinson, 2004; Warde, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Borowy, 2018; Caradonna, 2014; Purvis, 2019; J. Robinson, 2004; Warde, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30649,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZPFBG3X2"],"itemData":{"id":30649,"type":"chapter","abstract":"\\"The Routledge Handbook of the History of Sustainability is a far-reaching survey of the deep and contemporary history of sustainability. This innovative resource will help to define the history of sustainability as an identifiable field. It provides a unique resource for advanced undergraduates, graduate students, and scholars, and delivers essential context for understanding the current state and future path of the sustainability movement. The history of sustainability is an increasingly important domain within the discipline of history, which draws on an interdisciplinary set of fields, ranging from energy studies, transportation, and urbanism to environmental history, economics, and philosophy. Key sections in this handbook cover the historiography of sustainability, resilience and collapse in historical societies, the deep roots of sustainability (seventeenth century to nineteenth century), the recent history of sustainability (twentieth century to present), and core issues and key debates in sustainability. This handbook is an invaluable research and teaching tool for those interested in the history and development of sustainability and an essential resource for the many sustainability studies programs that now exist in the world's universities.\\"--Provided by publisher.","container-title":"Routledge handbook of the history of sustainability","ISBN":"978-0-367-85584-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1120972110","publisher":"Routledge","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Sustainable Development and the United Nations","author":[{"family":"Borowy","given":"I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":30661,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I435AWQ2"],"itemData":{"id":30661,"type":"book","call-number":"GE195 .C379 2014","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-0-19-937240-9","number-of-pages":"331","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Sustainability: a history","title-short":"Sustainability","author":[{"family":"Caradonna","given":"Jeremy L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":31701,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IRWPBZRX"],"itemData":{"id":31701,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability Science","DOI":"10.1007/s11625-018-0627-5","ISSN":"1862-4065, 1862-4057","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Sustain Sci","language":"en","page":"681-695","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Three pillars of sustainability: in search of conceptual origins","title-short":"Three pillars of sustainability","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11625-018-0627-5","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Purvis","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Mao","given":"Yong"},{"family":"Robinson","given":"Darren"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",5]]}}},{"id":30652,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AUFFDASW"],"itemData":{"id":30652,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2003.10.017","ISSN":"09218009","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"369-384","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Squaring the circle? Some thoughts on the idea of sustainable development","title-short":"Squaring the circle?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800904000175","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"John"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",4]]}}},{"id":30666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FYD2NDU5"],"itemData":{"id":30666,"type":"book","edition":"1","ISBN":"978-1-316-58476-7","note":"DOI: 10.1017/9781316584767","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Invention of Sustainability: Nature and Destiny, c.1500–1870","title-short":"The Invention of Sustainability","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/9781316584767/type/book","author":[{"family":"Warde","given":"Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As such, there has never been consensus on what constitutes sustainability. However, the objective of avoiding environmental degradation that would lead to a worsening of human conditions in the future has to a large degree been a common denominator of the different conceptualizations. There has been less agreement on how this can be achieved, and whether, or to which degree, it can involve economic growth.
2.2.2.2 The conceptualization of sustainable use of wild species in international agreements

The Stockholm Declaration from the United Nations Conference on the Human Environment in 1972 contains no mention of the terms “sustainable”, “sustainability”, or “sustainable use” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aZQzucOc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cooney, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Cooney, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30664,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GIQ6WPGX"],"itemData":{"id":30664,"type":"report","event-place":"White Oak Plantation, Florida","page":"76","publisher":"IUCN Species Survival Commission's Sustainable Use Specialist Group","publisher-place":"White Oak Plantation, Florida","title":"Sustainable Use: Concepts, Ambiguities, Challenges","URL":"https://www.iucn.org/sites/dev/files/whiteoakmtgfinalbackgroundjuly07.pdf","author":[{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, it states that natural resources, including fauna, flora and natural ecosystems, should be safeguarded for the benefit of present and future generations (Principle 2), and that the capacity of the earth to produce vital renewable resources should be maintained (Principle 3). Likewise, the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora and the Ramsar Convention on wetlands, which both came into force in 1975, as well as the Convention on the Conservation of Migratory Species of Wild Animals, which came into force in 1983, were related to sustainable use (of wild species in international trade, conservation and “wise use” of wetlands, and conservation and management of migratory species, respectively) without expressing it explicitly in terms of sustainability at the time. The United Nations Convention on Law of the Sea (1982) does refer explicitly to “sustainable yield” in both articles 61 and 119, in the context of status of harvested fish stocks, but does not extend the concept explicitly to more general biodiversity properties of the ocean. As is described further down, however, definitions in these conventions were developed in the following decades in parallel with the general development of the conceptualizations of sustainability and sustainable use.
      The 1980 World Conservation Strategy, co-authored by the International Union for the Conservation of Nature, the United Nations Environment Program (UNEP) and the World Wildlife Fund, provided an early conceptualization of sustainable use as part of an overall conservation strategy. It recognized the essential role of use of nature and living natural resources in meeting the needs of all humans, and highlighted the importance of ‘sustainable use’ of living natural resources for conservation success. Similarly, the World Charter for Nature, that was adopted by the United Nations in 1982 and proclaimed five “principles of conservation”, included the following conceptualization of sustainable use: “Ecosystems and organisms, as well as the land, marine and atmospheric resources that are utilized by man, shall be managed to achieve and maintain optimum sustainable productivity, but not in such a way as to endanger the integrity of those other ecosystems or species with which they coexist.”
      In 1987 the World Commission on Environment and Development (commonly referred to as the “Brundtland Commission”) established the concept of sustainable development as a central vision and objective in international environmental policy, in Our Common Future ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7CKmLzwr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Commission on Environment and Development, 1987)","plainCitation":"(World Commission on Environment and Development, 1987)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30634,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BIVH3JF7"],"itemData":{"id":30634,"type":"book","call-number":"HD75.6 .O97 1987","collection-title":"Oxford paperbacks","event-place":"Oxford ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-19-282080-8","number-of-pages":"383","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"Oxford ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Our common future","editor":[{"family":"World Commission on Environment and Development","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1987"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It had wide influence on the further understanding of sustainability in general, and on biodiversity conservation specifically. The sustainable use of wild species was mentioned explicitly in the report, but not thoroughly conceptualized. However, the report firmly established a specific conceptualization of biodiversity conservation related to sustainable use; first, it highlighted the importance of biodiversity for sustainable development, and second, it advocated the need to move beyond the “historical approach of establishing national parks that are somehow isolated from the greater society” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AYoMR49u","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Commission on Environment and Development, 1987)","plainCitation":"(World Commission on Environment and Development, 1987)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30634,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BIVH3JF7"],"itemData":{"id":30634,"type":"book","call-number":"HD75.6 .O97 1987","collection-title":"Oxford paperbacks","event-place":"Oxford ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-19-282080-8","number-of-pages":"383","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"Oxford ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Our common future","editor":[{"family":"World Commission on Environment and Development","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1987"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}: Part II, 6, V, 39), and address how development patterns affect biodiversity. Thus, the report emphasized the interdependency between biodiversity conservation and sustainable development. 
      In parallel with the development of the report from the Brundtland Commission, the Ramsar Convention’s definition of wise use of wetlands was updated in 1987, as “their sustainable utilization for the benefit of mankind in a way compatible with the maintenance of the natural properties of the ecosystem” (Ramsar Recommendation 3.3). The new definition reflected a similar understanding of the interactions between biodiversity conservation and use as the former report. Further, the 1980 World Conservation Strategy was updated in 1991, reiterating the importance of sustainable use of living natural resources for their conservation. 
      The sustainable use concept and its operationalization was given increasing attention within the International Union for the Conservation of Nature in the 1990s. A specific endorsement of the role of sustainable use in conservation strategies was made by the International Union for the Conservation of Nature General Assembly in Perth in 1990. Specifically, the International Union for the Conservation of Nature here endorsed the idea that under appropriate circumstances, use of living resources could itself contribute to their conservation. However, the specific meaning of sustainable use proved challenging to operationalize into recommendations at the time, because of the complexity of the issue and the balancing of environmental, social and economic aspects of sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rYCoGTpJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cooney, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Cooney, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30664,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GIQ6WPGX"],"itemData":{"id":30664,"type":"report","event-place":"White Oak Plantation, Florida","page":"76","publisher":"IUCN Species Survival Commission's Sustainable Use Specialist Group","publisher-place":"White Oak Plantation, Florida","title":"Sustainable Use: Concepts, Ambiguities, Challenges","URL":"https://www.iucn.org/sites/dev/files/whiteoakmtgfinalbackgroundjuly07.pdf","author":[{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The International Union for the Conservation of Nature sought to resolve these issues by the 1995 Sustainable use initiative and the formation of the sustainable use specialist group, as well as later efforts to identify the factors that influence the sustainability of use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MjOHcKSH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zaccagnini, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Zaccagnini, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30637,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MB4N2JQX"],"itemData":{"id":30637,"type":"report","publisher":"IUCN","title":"Analytic Framework for Assessing Factors that Influence Sustainability of Uses of Wild Living Natural Resources","URL":"http://iucn.org/themes/ssc/susg/","author":[{"family":"Zaccagnini","given":"M. E."},{"family":"Cloquell","given":"S."},{"family":"Fernandez","given":"E."},{"family":"Gonzalez","given":"C."},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"G."},{"family":"Novaro","given":"A."},{"family":"Panigati","given":"J. L."},{"family":"Rabinovich","given":"J."},{"family":"Tomasini","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The Rio Declaration adopted at the United Nations Conference on Environment and Development in 1992 further developed the concept of sustainable development from the Brundtland Commission’s report, and included reference to “sustainable production and consumption”, but did not make specific reference to sustainable use of wild species. However, the Convention on Biological Diversity was also an outcome of this conference, and sustainable use of biodiversity was granted a central position in it. Specifically, it constituted one of the three objectives of the Convention on Biological Diversity, which are the conservation of biological diversity (Article 1), the sustainable use of its components (Article 2), and the fair and equitable sharing of benefits from the use of genetic resources (Article 3). It was defined as follows: “Sustainable use means the use of components of biological diversity in a way and at a rate that does not lead to the long-term decline of biological diversity, thereby maintaining its potential to meet the needs and aspirations of present and future generations” (Article 2).
      In 1994 the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora adopted at its 13th Conference of the Parties Resolution 8.3: Recognition of the benefits of trade in wildlife. This constituted a recognition of some of the basic tenets of sustainable use, recognizing potential benefits of commercial trade to the conservation of species and/or ecosystems, and the potential of incentives for sustainable use of wild animals and plants to avoid conversion of wild landscapes to alternative land uses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2ZaPk1mu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cooney, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Cooney, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30664,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GIQ6WPGX"],"itemData":{"id":30664,"type":"report","event-place":"White Oak Plantation, Florida","page":"76","publisher":"IUCN Species Survival Commission's Sustainable Use Specialist Group","publisher-place":"White Oak Plantation, Florida","title":"Sustainable Use: Concepts, Ambiguities, Challenges","URL":"https://www.iucn.org/sites/dev/files/whiteoakmtgfinalbackgroundjuly07.pdf","author":[{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The resolution has been understood as a compromise, following intense debates over the position that the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora should adopt in relation to sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vuTjs7EK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Favre, 1993; Garrison, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Favre, 1993; Garrison, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30636,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PGDSCY2F"],"itemData":{"id":30636,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Natural Resources Journal","issue":"4","page":"875","title":"Debate Within the CITES Community: What Direction for the Future","URL":"ttps://digitalrepository.unm.edu/nrj/vol33/iss4/2","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Favre","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}},{"id":30635,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AE2EZCWS"],"itemData":{"id":30635,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pace Environmental Law Review","issue":"1","page":"301","title":"The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and the Debate over Sustainable Use","URL":"http://digitalcommons.pace.edu/pelr/vol12/iss1/12","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Garrison","given":"J. L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In 1995 the Convention on Biological Diversity adopted the ecosystem approach as the “primary framework” of action to be taken under the convention (Decision II/8). The ecosystem approach was defined as “a strategy for the integrated management of land, water and living resources that promotes conservation and sustainable use in an equitable way” (Decision V/6). The approach directed attention to the structure, processes, functions and interactions within an ecosystem, rather than exclusively on specific elements such as single species or populations. This meant that the sustainable use of biodiversity is also considered from an ecosystem perspective, rather than understood narrowly as the maintenance of single species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2ncmlE7I","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cooney, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Cooney, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30664,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GIQ6WPGX"],"itemData":{"id":30664,"type":"report","event-place":"White Oak Plantation, Florida","page":"76","publisher":"IUCN Species Survival Commission's Sustainable Use Specialist Group","publisher-place":"White Oak Plantation, Florida","title":"Sustainable Use: Concepts, Ambiguities, Challenges","URL":"https://www.iucn.org/sites/dev/files/whiteoakmtgfinalbackgroundjuly07.pdf","author":[{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      At the second World Conservation Congress in 2000, the International Union for the Conservation of Nature adopted a Policy Statement on Sustainable Use of Wild Living Resources, as well as recommendation 2.92 on indigenous peoples, sustainable use of natural resources, and international trade. In 2001, the International Union for the Conservation of Nature presented the White Oak Principles of Sustainable Use, a short document establishing a definition, seven axioms and eight principles for sustainable use. The following definition was adopted for sustainable use: “Sustainable use, both extractive and non-extractive, is a dynamic process toward which one strives in order to maintain biodiversity and enhance ecological and socio-economic services, recognizing that the greater the equity and degree of participation in governance, the greater the likelihood of achieving these objectives for present and future generations”. Thus, the conceptualization of sustainable use within the International Union for the Conservation of Nature was progressing towards the inclusion of social and economic aspects, emphasizing equity and participation in governance.
      The Addis Ababa Principles and Guidelines for the Sustainable Use of Biodiversity (see supplementary materials S2.1) were adopted in 2004, at the 7th Conference of the Parties to the Convention on Biological Diversity (Decision VII/12). They comprise a set of 14 “practical principles”, each with associated operational guidelines. The conceptualization of sustainable use incorporated in these principles and guidelines indicate a progression towards inclusion of social and economic aspects similar to that within the International Union for the Conservation of Nature, and include topics such as supportive legislative and policy arrangements, empowerment of local resource users, removal of perverse incentives, adaptive management, and avoidance of impacts on nature’s contributions to people.
     A similar conceptualization of sustainable use was incorporated in the Aichi Biodiversity Targets, adopted in 2010 as part of the Convention on Biological Diversity's Strategic Plan for Biodiversity 2011-2020. The targets addressed five strategic goals identified in the strategy, of which Strategic Goal B: Reduce the direct pressures on biodiversity and promote sustainable use, was directly related to the sustainable use of wild species. Aichi Biodiversity Target 6 addressed sustainable use and management of marine biodiversity (fish, invertebrate stocks and aquatic plants) in order to avoiding overfishing and other negative impacts on biodiversity. Target 3 addressed the removal of negative incentives, and development and application of positive incentives for conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity, while Target 18 addressed the integration in legislation and relevant international obligations of traditional knowledge, innovations and practices of indigenous and local communities relevant for ecosystem services and the conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity.
2.2.3 Current academic conceptualization of sustainable use by practice
2.2.3.1 Introduction 
Although the history of expert research publications on the sustainable use of natured dates back a couple of centuries (section 2.2.2), publications with new interpretations of what constitutes sustainable use continue in all fields. Reviewing these evolving or new aspects of how sustainable use is conceptualized within each practice, and examining the commonalities and differences of these developments among the practices, is a crucial part of this chapter. It largely delineates the academic context within which the evaluations in the following chapters will be conducted, with implications for application of the conceptualizations as well.  
      This literature review summarizes widely agreed upon ideas of sustainable use up until 2010, and then reviews the post 2010 literature to identify new and emerging ideas. The review faced several challenges, related to the scope and size of the review; and the different publication rates among practices and between ecological and social aspects of sustainable use within practices. Those challenges are described, along with the review methodology that was designed to overcome them, in the data management report available at 10.5281/zenodo.6472995. As per IPBES protocol, the systematic reviews were focused on English language journals. Although these reviews include papers by authors from across the globe, trends reported here may not be representative across all regions and disciplines.
      A challenge not discussed explicitly in the data management report was the many scales and value systems within which sustainable use may be conceptualized. Historically the research community has not been strongly focused on research on small-scale uses of nature. Nevertheless, it was important to this literature review (and this assessment) to capture developments in those areas. The academic literature has an intrinsic over-representation of reports from scientific types of knowledge, so thinking from other knowledge systems is under-represented. To deal with potential differences of coverage of various scales, the screening of “hits” was directed to be vigilant for papers with a focus on small-scale uses of nature, to ensure they would be well-represented in the papers evaluated in this review. Interpreting the findings of this literature review should be done with an awareness of these potential shortcomings in the academic literature, and should be complemented by information on the indigenous peoples and local communities’ conceptualizations of sustainable use (section 2.2.4). The academic literature reviews for the five practices follow in sections 2.2.3.2-2.2.3.6. The outcomes of the literature review for each practice are presented separately, with the main findings summarized. Then a final subsection (2.2.3.7) highlights emergent patterns and messages that cut across all practices, as well as implications of any major differences that are present in the current academic literature on each practice.  
2.2.3.2 Conceptualizations of sustainable fishing in the academic literature 
The literature on sustainable fishing is particularly large. Consequently, even a high level review of literature prior to 2010 has a relatively large number of influential references. Moreover, there is a policy benchmark in 2010 with Aichi Biodiversity Target 6, that gives a foundation presenting what the parties to the Convention on Biological Diversity agreed as comprising sustainable use of fish stocks and the ecosystems in which they are found. Similarly, the very large number of post-2010 publications also influence the approach to both screening papers down to a feasible number to review, and allows the findings to be presented in a tabular as well as narrative format. 
2.2.3.2.1 Conceptualization of fishing in academic and technical literature up until 2010
In fishing a parameterized conceptualization of sustainable use began as early as the 1950s, when benchmarks of sustainable or “optimal” use of the target species were identified ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IAqdjFJB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Beverton & Holt, 1957; Ricker, 1955)","plainCitation":"(Beverton & Holt, 1957; Ricker, 1955)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30405,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L6BDZWBV"],"itemData":{"id":30405,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fishery Investigations Series","language":"en","page":"533","title":"On the dynamics of exploited fish populations","volume":"II, 19","author":[{"family":"Beverton","given":"R.J.H."},{"family":"Holt","given":"S.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1957"]]}}},{"id":30623,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/98G5FU28"],"itemData":{"id":30623,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bulletin of the Fisheries Research Board of Canada","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Ottawa, Canada","page":"382","title":"Computation and Interpretation of Biological Statistics of Fish Populations","volume":"191","author":[{"family":"Ricker","given":"W.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1955"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The biologically defined benchmarks such as Bmsy (the biomass producing maximum sustainable yield) were quickly adapted to reflect that economic aspects of fishing, such as cost per unit of fishing effort, were part of sustainability, with the benchmark of Bmey (biomass producing the maximum economic yield, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9oNJwhwN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. W. Clark, 1973; C. w Clark & Munro, 1975; Roedel, 1975)","plainCitation":"(C. W. Clark, 1973; C. w Clark & Munro, 1975; Roedel, 1975)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QZNXVZP5"],"itemData":{"id":30402,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","language":"en","page":"630–634","title":"The Economics of Overexploitation","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Clark","given":"C.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1973"]]}}},{"id":30386,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JEVKI8TE"],"itemData":{"id":30386,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal Of Environmental Economics And Management","language":"en","page":"92–106","title":"Economics of fishing and modern capital theory - simplified approach","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Clark","given":"C. W."},{"family":"Munro","given":"G. R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1975"]]}}},{"id":30622,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6H25Q69G"],"itemData":{"id":30622,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Special Publication of the American Fisheries Society","language":"en","title":"Optimum sustainable yield as a concept in fishery management","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Roedel","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1975"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As the importance of precaution in uses of natural resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nwKqkBqf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garcia, 1994; L. J. Richards & Maguire, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Garcia, 1994; L. J. Richards & Maguire, 1998)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34887,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q9KUFMHC"],"itemData":{"id":34887,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"99–125","title":"The Precautionary Principle - Its Implications in Capture Fisheries Management Ocean &","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Garcia","given":"S.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}},{"id":30383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QC4NGC3U"],"itemData":{"id":30383,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Canadian Journal Of Fisheries And Aquatic Sciences","language":"en","page":"1545–1552","title":"Recent international agreements and the precautionary approach: new directions for fisheries management science","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Richards","given":"L. J."},{"family":"Maguire","given":"J. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} gained traction, many papers subsequently challenged details of these benchmarks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kfZugkRu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Butterworth & Punt, 2003; Grafton, 2007; Mace, 1994; Schnute & Richards, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Butterworth & Punt, 2003; Grafton, 2007; Mace, 1994; Schnute & Richards, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30404,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6WYPLD93"],"itemData":{"id":30404,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Responsible fisheries in the marine ecosystem","event-place":"Rome","language":"en","page":"311–319","publisher":"CABI Press FAO","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The role of harvest control laws, risk and uncertainty and the precautionary approach in ecosystem-based management","author":[{"family":"Butterworth","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Punt","given":"A.E."}],"editor":[{"family":"Sinclair","given":"M.F."},{"family":"Valdemarsson","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":30411,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6U3NS2JL"],"itemData":{"id":30411,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","language":"en","page":"1601–1607","title":"Economics of overexploitation revisited","volume":"318","author":[{"family":"Grafton","given":"R.Q."},{"family":"Kompas","given":"T."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":30630,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M65KEJKL"],"itemData":{"id":30630,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Canadian Journal Of Fisheries And Aquatic Sciences","language":"en","page":"110–122","title":"Relationships Between Common Biological Reference Points Used As Thresholds And Targets Of Fisheries Management Strategies","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Mace","given":"P.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}},{"id":30627,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JLDFN4GM"],"itemData":{"id":30627,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Canadian Journal Of Fisheries And Aquatic Sciences","language":"en","page":"515–528","title":"Analytical models for fishery reference points","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Schnute","given":"J.T."},{"family":"Richards","given":"L.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the conceptualization of sustainable use in fishing never abandoned the properties of both keeping biomass at or above a level producing a high yield (taking into account the productivity of a stock), and ensuring that macro-economically the costs of harvesting would be less than the revenues from the yield ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7TM5JApo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Apkalu, 2009; Harris, 2002; Holt, 2009; Martinet, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Apkalu, 2009; Harris, 2002; Holt, 2009; Martinet, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30387,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SDU3ACCJ"],"itemData":{"id":30387,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolecon.2009.05.014","issue":"10","language":"en","note":"page: 2729–273\\nvolume: 68","page":"2729-2733","title":"Economics of biodiversity and sustainable fisheries management Ecological Economics","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Apkalu","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":30410,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YHLE4BCN"],"itemData":{"id":30410,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"South African Journal Of Marine Science-Suid-Afrikaanse Tydskrif Vir Seewetenskap","language":"en","page":"24","title":"Recommendations for the management of subsistence fisheries in South Africa","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Harris","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Branch","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Clark","given":"B.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":30409,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7AJ2QDP9"],"itemData":{"id":30409,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","language":"en","page":"3–10","title":"Sunken Billions - But how many?","volume":"97","author":[{"family":"Holt","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":30382,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TIQ9AB2U"],"itemData":{"id":30382,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"411–422","title":"Defining viable recovery paths toward sustainable fisheries","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Martinet","given":"V."},{"family":"Thebaud","given":"O."},{"family":"Doyen","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The latter resulted in early criticisms of subsidies as promoting unsustainable levels of fishing capacity; a criticism addressed with Aichi Biodiversity Target 3.
      By the 1980s, fisheries management was challenged to include the ways that fishing impacted the food webs and habitats in which it occurred ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1pePVKRo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. P. Andersen & Ursin, 1977)","plainCitation":"(K. P. Andersen & Ursin, 1977)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":127,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D47QZADK"],"itemData":{"id":127,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Medd. Dan. Fiski- Havunders","language":"en","page":"219–435","title":"A multispecies extension to the Beverton and Holt theory of fishing, with accounts of phosphorous circulation and primary production","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Andersen","given":"K.P."},{"family":"Ursin","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1977"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This prompted development of analytical tools and models to assess the degree to which fishing on lower trophic levels might deplete the food supply of higher predators ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4272UI4p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gislason & Rice, 1998; Hollowed, 2000; Pope, 1991; Pope, 2006; Sissenwine & Daan, 1991; Yodzis, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Gislason & Rice, 1998; Hollowed, 2000; Pope, 1991; Pope, 2006; Sissenwine & Daan, 1991; Yodzis, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30613,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2JSCEJBP"],"itemData":{"id":30613,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal Of Marine Science","language":"en","page":"362–370","title":"Modelling the response of size and diversity spectra of fish assemblages to changes in exploitation ICES","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Gislason","given":"H."},{"family":"Rice","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":30388,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3TZZPPZR"],"itemData":{"id":30388,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"707–719","title":"Are multispecies models an improvement on single-species models for measuring fishing impacts on marine ecosystems?","volume":"57","author":[{"family":"Hollowed","given":"A. B."},{"family":"Bax","given":"N."},{"family":"Beamish","given":"R."},{"family":"Collie","given":"J."},{"family":"Fogarty","given":"M."},{"family":"Livingston","given":"P."},{"family":"Pope","given":"J."},{"family":"Rice","given":"J. C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":30625,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H2H9TGNK"],"itemData":{"id":30625,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Marine Science Symposia","language":"en","page":"22–33","title":"The ICES Multispecies Assessment Working Group: evolution, insights, and future problems","volume":"193","author":[{"family":"Pope","given":"J.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}},{"id":30621,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/985T7WAN"],"itemData":{"id":30621,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal Of Marine Science","language":"en","page":"1029–1044","title":"Modelling an exploited marine fish community with 15 parameters - results from a simple size-based model/","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Pope","given":"J.G."},{"family":"Rice","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Daan","given":"N."},{"family":"Gislason","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":30626,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HH3QNWBZ"],"itemData":{"id":30626,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Marine Science Symposia","language":"en","page":"6–14","title":"An overview of multispecies models relevant to management of living resources","volume":"193","author":[{"family":"Sissenwine","given":"M.P."},{"family":"Daan","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}},{"id":30643,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FSQYLURP"],"itemData":{"id":30643,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","language":"en","page":"51–58","title":"Predator-prey theory and management of multispecies fisheries","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Yodzis","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. or result in trophic cascades if populations of higher predators were depleted, allowing lower trophic levels to increase unchecked ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DxKJWiPo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Baum & Worm, 2009; Fogarty & Murawski, 1998; Gjosaeter, 1995; Sala, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Baum & Worm, 2009; Fogarty & Murawski, 1998; Gjosaeter, 1995; Sala, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30400,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DDCVXYQZ"],"itemData":{"id":30400,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal Of Animal Ecology","language":"en","page":"699–714","title":"Cascading top-down effects of changing oceanic predator abundances","volume":"78","author":[{"family":"Baum","given":"J.K."},{"family":"Worm","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":30632,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/34UBQ8BJ"],"itemData":{"id":30632,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"page: 6– 22","title":"Large-scale disturbance and the structure of marine system: Fishery impacts on Georges Bank Ecological Applications ? 8: Supplement","author":[{"family":"Fogarty","given":"M.J."},{"family":"Murawski","given":"S.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":30412,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7VERBDP2"],"itemData":{"id":30412,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic","language":"en","page":"267–278","title":"Pelagic Fish And The Ecological Impact Of The Modern Fishing Industry In The Barents Sea","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Gjosaeter","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}},{"id":30618,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7DDJD4J4"],"itemData":{"id":30618,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"page: 425–439\\nvolume: 82","title":"Fishing, trophic cascades, and the structure of algal assemblages: evaluation of an old but untested paradigm Oikos","author":[{"family":"Sala","given":"E."},{"family":"Boudouresque","given":"C.F."},{"family":"Harmelin-Vivien","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The conceptualization of sustainable fishing correspondingly expanded to require consideration of both types of outcomes, and any other large or expanding alterations of trophic relationships ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"blvlpgMs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fowler, 1999; Larkin, 1996)","plainCitation":"(Fowler, 1999; Larkin, 1996)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30633,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JTDXH3UF"],"itemData":{"id":30633,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal Of Marine Science","language":"en","page":"927–932","title":"Management of multi-species fisheries: from overfishing to sustainability ICES","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Fowler","given":"C.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":30631,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SQQBWIGA"],"itemData":{"id":30631,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews In Fish Biology And Fisheries","language":"en","page":"139–164","title":"Concepts and issues in marine ecosystem management","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Larkin","given":"P.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Bycatches that depleted non-target species were also identified as a potential unsustainable consequence of fishing, and limiting bycatches to levels that did not deplete the populations of non-targeted species also became a standard for sustainable fishing by the 1990s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qicvsvjI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alverson, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Alverson, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30617,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FUAISKX3"],"itemData":{"id":30617,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Technical Paper","issue":"339","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Rome\\npublisher: FAO","title":"A global assessment of fisheries bycatch and discards.FAO","author":[{"family":"Alverson","given":"D.L."},{"family":"Freeberg","given":"M.H."},{"family":"Murawaski","given":"S.A."},{"family":"Pope","given":"J.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There was particular emphasis on minimizing, if not avoiding completely, the bycatches of marine mammals, seabirds, and other marine taxa with long life expectancies and low productivity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XqGNiU29","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dillingham & Fletcher, 2008; Niel & Lebreton, 2005; S. Zhou, 2008; Zydelis, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Dillingham & Fletcher, 2008; Niel & Lebreton, 2005; S. Zhou, 2008; Zydelis, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30380,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IFJZ6V24"],"itemData":{"id":30380,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"page: 1783–1792\\nvolume: 141","title":"Estimating the ability of birds to sustain additional human-caused mortalities using a simple decision rule and allometric relationships Biological Conservation","author":[{"family":"Dillingham","given":"P.W."},{"family":"Fletcher","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":30629,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/54EVWFJ6"],"itemData":{"id":30629,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biology","language":"en","page":"826–835","title":"Using demographic invariants to detect overharvested bird populations from incomplete data Conservation","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Niel","given":"C."},{"family":"Lebreton","given":"J.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":30641,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DQTSTM5L"],"itemData":{"id":30641,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish And Fisheries","language":"en","page":"308–315","title":"Fishery by-catch and discards: a positive perspective from ecosystem-based fishery management","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Zhou","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":30615,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FM8KET4Z"],"itemData":{"id":30615,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"608–616","title":"Conservation of Marine Megafauna through Minimization of Fisheries Bycatch","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Zydelis","given":"R."},{"family":"Wallace","given":"B.P."},{"family":"Gilman","given":"E.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In parallel, the impacts of fishing, particularly with mobile bottom-contacting gears, on seafloor habitats and benthic species received substantial attention in the literature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zK6qIhSU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lindeboom & Groot, 1998; Rijnsdorp, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Lindeboom & Groot, 1998; Rijnsdorp, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30628,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KBJPGZ7N"],"itemData":{"id":30628,"type":"report","event-place":"Texel, Netherlands, Netherlands","genre":"NIOZ- Report 1998-1/RIVO-DLO Report C003/98.","language":"en","page":"404","publisher":"Den Burg","publisher-place":"Texel, Netherlands, Netherlands","title":"IMPACT-II: The effects of different types of fisheries on the North Sea and Irish Sea benthic ecosystems","author":[{"family":"Lindeboom","given":"H.J."},{"family":"Groot","given":"S.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":30619,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/47GI6PFT"],"itemData":{"id":30619,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","language":"en","page":"403–419","title":"Micro-scale distribution of beam trawl effort in the southern North Sea between 1993 and 1996 in relation to the trawling frequency of the sea bed and the impact on benthic organisms","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"A.D."},{"family":"Buys","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Storbeck","given":"F."},{"family":"Visser","given":"E.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Expert groups of the International Council for the Exploration of the Sea and other regional centres consolidated the burgeoning literature and developed standards and guidance for keeping such impacts within sustainable bounds ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"A1jHlvxa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 1999; S. Zhou & Griffiths, 2008)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 1999; S. Zhou & Griffiths, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31430,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NS7U6M69"],"itemData":{"id":31430,"type":"book","call-number":"QL82 .I58 1999","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-104332-5","note":"OCLC: ocm43326999","number-of-pages":"26","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"International plan of action for reducing incidental catch of seabirds in longline fisheries.","title-short":"International plan of action for reducing incidental catch of seabirds in longline fisheries","editor":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":30616,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YZPVKWE2"],"itemData":{"id":30616,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","language":"en","page":"56–68","title":"Sustainability Assessment for Fishing Effects (SAFE): A new quantitative ecological risk assessment method and its application to elasmobranch bycatch in an Australian trawl fishery","volume":"91","author":[{"family":"Zhou","given":"S."},{"family":"Griffiths","given":"S.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Debate continued about whether the standards and benchmarks were set in the correct levels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Y4p9yZzn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Frid, 1999; Furness, 2002; Kaiser, 2000; J. C. Rice & Legac\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, 2007; S. Turner, 1999; J. L. Young, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Frid, 1999; Furness, 2002; Kaiser, 2000; J. C. Rice & Legacé, 2007; S. Turner, 1999; J. L. Young, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30407,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V9UHQXUT"],"itemData":{"id":30407,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Ecology Progress Series","language":"en","page":"13–20","title":"Long term changes in the benthos of a heavily fished ground off the NE coast of England","volume":"188","author":[{"family":"Frid","given":"C.J.L."},{"family":"Clark","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Hall","given":"J.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":30406,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3758YQ35"],"itemData":{"id":30406,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","language":"en","page":"261–269","title":"Management implications of interactions between fisheries and sandeel-dependent seabirds and seals in the North Sea","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Furness","given":"R.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":30381,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FKVSVZMT"],"itemData":{"id":30381,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Animal Ecology","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1046/j.1365-2656.2000.00412.x","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"494–503","title":"Chronic fishing disturbance has changed shelf sea benthic community structure","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Kaiser","given":"M. J."},{"family":"Ramsay","given":"K."},{"family":"Richardson","given":"C.A."},{"family":"Spence","given":""},{"family":"Brand","given":"A. R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":30620,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q32JTXKD"],"itemData":{"id":30620,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","language":"en","page":"718–722","title":"When control rules collide: a comparison of fisheries management reference points and IUCN criteria for assessing risk of extinction","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Rice","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Legacé","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":30645,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LESCP4Q6"],"itemData":{"id":30645,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Management Ecology","language":"en","page":"401–420","title":"Fishing impacts and the degradation or loss of habitat structure","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Turner","given":"S."},{"family":"Thrush","given":"S.F."},{"family":"Hewitt","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Cummings","given":"V.J."},{"family":"Funnell","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":30642,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3MH47NF6"],"itemData":{"id":30642,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish And Fisheries","language":"en","page":"262–283","title":"Integrating physiology and life history to improve fisheries management and conservation","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Young","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Bornik","given":"Z.B."},{"family":"Marcotte","given":"M.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, there was no dispute within the expert literature that, as with trophic impacts of fishing, bycatches and habitat impacts had to be taken into account in evaluating the sustainability of fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DUn4OIY8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2009; Garcia & Cochrane, 2005)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2009; Garcia & Cochrane, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34893,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KV53XLMT"],"itemData":{"id":34893,"type":"book","call-number":"639.206 8","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-006258-7","publisher":"FAO","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"BnF ISBN","title":"International guidelines for the management of deep-sea fisheries in the high seas","editor":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":34885,"