      Nearly 20% of all papers reviewed looked directly at the social context of sustainability of fishing. This is a marked increase from 2010. Interactions of the social context of fisheries with environmental and economic considerations and the fishery itself all received attention, in contexts such as the role of socially well-adapted fishing in perpetuating particular ecosystem configurations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yqBPO1aX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Caswell, 2020; Tregidgo, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Caswell, 2020; Tregidgo, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30693,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/23JMQG4A"],"itemData":{"id":30693,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","language":"en","page":"774 –796","title":"Something old, something new: Historical perspectives provide lessons for blue growth agendas","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Caswell","given":"B.A."},{"family":"Klein","given":"E.S."},{"family":"Alleway","given":"H.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30585,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DPVZIH9N"],"itemData":{"id":30585,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1614499114","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"32","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"8655-8659","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Rainforest metropolis casts 1,000-km defaunation shadow","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1614499114","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Tregidgo","given":"Daniel J."},{"family":"Barlow","given":"Jos"},{"family":"Pompeu","given":"Paulo S."},{"family":"Almeida Rocha","given":"Mayana","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Parry","given":"Luke"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8,8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and the social status of fisher harvesters and traders in communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Bwi9Q0eD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. L. Cochrane, 2011; Pihlajamaki, 2020; Twist, 2016)","plainCitation":"(K. L. Cochrane, 2011; Pihlajamaki, 2020; Twist, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30697,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FQK7MJJY"],"itemData":{"id":30697,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","language":"en","page":"275–288","title":"Primary fisheries management: a minimum requirement for provision of sustainable human benefits in small-scale fisheries","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Cochrane","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Andrew","given":"N.L."},{"family":"Parma","given":"A.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":30708,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y8S4NL2Q"],"itemData":{"id":30708,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","language":"en","page":"797–812","title":"Catching the future: Applying Bayesian belief networks to exploratory scenario storylines to assess long-term changes in Baltic herring (Clupea harengus membras, Clupeidae) and salmon (Salmo salar, Salmonidae) fisheries","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Pihlajamaki","given":"M."},{"family":"Helle","given":"I."},{"family":"Haapasaari","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30532,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KQ6TPWCK"],"itemData":{"id":30532,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","language":"en","page":"384–393","title":"Distribution of the New Zealand scallop (Pecten novaezealandiae) within and surrounding a customary fisheries area","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Twist","given":"B.A."},{"family":"Hepburn","given":"C.D."},{"family":"Rayment","given":"W.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      However, the importance of small-scale fisheries’ contribution to community identity, livelihoods and overall wellbeing received the most focused attention (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"v58aDbBG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Asche, 2018; Cinner, 2016, 2019; Galland, 2017; Voyer, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Asche, 2018; Cinner, 2016, 2019; Galland, 2017; Voyer, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30690,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5VZ8DGNC"],"itemData":{"id":30690,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","event-place":"USA","language":"en","page":"11221–11225","publisher-place":"USA","title":"Three pillars of sustainability in fisheries","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Asche","given":"F."},{"family":"Garlock","given":"T.M."},{"family":"Anderson","given":"J.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":30692,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L3CFCZXB"],"itemData":{"id":30692,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","language":"en","page":"416–419","title":"Bright spots among the world's coral reefs","volume":"535","author":[{"family":"Cinner","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Huchery","given":"C."},{"family":"MacNeil","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":30696,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDHWL6AS"],"itemData":{"id":30696,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences – USA","language":"en","page":"26474–26483","title":"Sixteen years of social and ecological dynamics reveal challenges and opportunities for adaptive management in sustaining the commons","volume":"116","author":[{"family":"Cinner","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Lau","given":"J.D."},{"family":"Bauman","given":"A.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":30704,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UFW7KBWU"],"itemData":{"id":30704,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","language":"en","page":"558–558","title":"Fishing responsibly and sustainably","volume":"357","author":[{"family":"Galland","given":"G.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":30530,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KE37KCXQ"],"itemData":{"id":30530,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","language":"en","page":"1134–1149","title":"Using a well-being approach to develop a framework for an integrated socio-economic evaluation of professional fishing","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Voyer","given":"M."},{"family":"Barclay","given":"K."},{"family":"McIlgorm","given":"A."},{"family":"Mazur","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These ideas were being discussed in the decades before the 20-teens ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RkaPSUIV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2015)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30673,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9QM368CL"],"itemData":{"id":30673,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome, Italy","ISBN":"978-92-5-108704-6","language":"en","number-of-pages":"35","publisher-place":"Rome, Italy","title":"Voluntary Guidelines for Securing Sustainable Small-Scale Fisheries in the Context of Food Security and Poverty Eradication","URL":"https://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/I4356EN","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} but in the 20-teens they have taken a central place in discussing sustainability of fishing. Correspondingly, fully 20% of the papers presenting new or adapted ideas of the social aspects of sustainable fishing deal directly with governance and or the use of alternative knowledge systems in sustainability of small-scale fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"x6coeg8m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Al-Humaidhi, 2013; Groeneveld, 2011; Maravelias, 2018; McClenachan, 2014; O. R. Young, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Al-Humaidhi, 2013; Groeneveld, 2011; Maravelias, 2018; McClenachan, 2014; O. R. Young, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30688,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F8IE8FL5"],"itemData":{"id":30688,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","language":"en","page":"13–23","title":"The local management of migratory stocks: Implications for sustainable fisheries management","volume":"141","author":[{"family":"Al-Humaidhi","given":"A.W."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Young","given":"T.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":30702,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3R48RGPM"],"itemData":{"id":30702,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","language":"en","page":"919–928","title":"Quantifying fishers' and citizens' support for Dutch flatfish management policy","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Groeneveld","given":"R.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33028,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6B8SX8WW"],"itemData":{"id":33028,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In the Mediterranean Sea, the dominant type of fisheries is small-scale. Coastal communities remain dependent on fisheries for their income, some of them with limited potential for economic diversification. The top-down micro-management regime has proven ineffective to secure ecological and social sustainability as it lacks flexibility and adaptation to local and regional conditions. This paper...","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","ISSN":"1054-3139","issue":"6","language":"English","page":"2097-2106","source":"www.cabdirect.org","title":"Participatory management in a high value small-scale fishery in the Mediterranean Sea.","URL":"https://www.cabdirect.org/cabdirect/abstract/20203083988","volume":"75","author":[{"family":"Maravelias","given":"C. D."},{"family":"Vasilakopoulos","given":"P."},{"family":"Kalogirou","given":"S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":30683,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GQUSH28Z"],"itemData":{"id":30683,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fishres.2014.03.016","language":"en","page":"62–69","title":"Do community supported fisheries (CSFs) improve sustainability?","volume":"157","author":[{"family":"McClenachan","given":"L."},{"family":"Neal","given":"B.P."},{"family":"Al-Abdulrazzak","given":"D."},{"family":"Witkin","given":"T."},{"family":"Fisher","given":"K."},{"family":"Kittinger","given":"J. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":30710,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7FE7X9HE"],"itemData":{"id":30710,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","language":"en","page":"9065–9073","title":"Moving beyond panaceas in fisheries governance","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Young","given":"O.R."},{"family":"Webster","given":"D.G."},{"family":"Cox","given":"M.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Primarily concerns in governance issues are how increasingly concentrated wealth and power can result in a small number of voices and perspectives having a disproportionate influence over governance processes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7ITL7TCu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cinner, 2016; Hilborn, 2020; Nielsen, 2018; Osterblom, 2017; Schultz, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Cinner, 2016; Hilborn, 2020; Nielsen, 2018; Osterblom, 2017; Schultz, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30692,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L3CFCZXB"],"itemData":{"id":30692,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","language":"en","page":"416–419","title":"Bright spots among the world's coral reefs","volume":"535","author":[{"family":"Cinner","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Huchery","given":"C."},{"family":"MacNeil","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":34306,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C29NHAMV"],"itemData":{"id":34306,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Marine fish stocks are an important part of the world food system and are particularly important for many of the poorest people of the world. Most existing analyses suggest overfishing is increasing, and there is widespread concern that fish stocks are decreasing throughout most of the world. We assembled trends in abundance and harvest rate of stocks that are scientifically assessed, constituting half of the reported global marine fish catch. For these stocks, on average, abundance is increasing and is at proposed target levels. Compared with regions that are intensively managed, regions with less-developed fisheries management have, on average, 3-fold greater harvest rates and half the abundance as assessed stocks. Available evidence suggests that the regions without assessments of abundance have little fisheries management, and stocks are in poor shape. Increased application of area-appropriate fisheries science recommendations and management tools are still needed for sustaining fisheries in places where they are lacking.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1909726116","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"2218-2224","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effective fisheries management instrumental in improving fish stock status","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1909726116","volume":"117","author":[{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo Oscar"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Christopher M."},{"family":"Baum","given":"Julia K."},{"family":"Branch","given":"Trevor A."},{"family":"Costello","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Moor","given":"Carryn L.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Faraj","given":"Abdelmalek"},{"family":"Hively","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Olaf P."},{"family":"Kurota","given":"Hiroyuki"},{"family":"Little","given":"L. Richard"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"McClanahan","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Melnychuk","given":"Michael C."},{"family":"Minto","given":"Cóilín"},{"family":"Osio","given":"Giacomo Chato"},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Pons","given":"Maite"},{"family":"Segurado","given":"Susana"},{"family":"Szuwalski","given":"Cody S."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"Jono R."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Yimin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1,28]]}}},{"id":30520,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6Y7KY4UV"],"itemData":{"id":30520,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","language":"en","page":"363–376","title":"A framework for results-based management in fisheries","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Nielsen","given":"K.N."},{"family":"Aschan","given":"M.M."},{"family":"Agnarsson","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":30527,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JENGRYR2"],"itemData":{"id":30527,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","language":"en","page":"9038–9043","title":"Emergence of a global science-business initiative for ocean stewardship","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Osterblom","given":"H."},{"family":"Jouffray","given":"J.B."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"Rockstrom","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":30684,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KGT2SSJ2"],"itemData":{"id":30684,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National academy of sciences – USA","language":"en","page":"7369–7374","title":"Adaptive governance, ecosystem management, and natural capital","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Schultz","given":"L."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"Osterblom","given":"H."},{"family":"Olsson","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Prior to 2010, governance was conceptualized as an external factor that influences the sustainability of fishing in various ways whereas governance is now understood as an inherent aspect of sustainable fishing. Inclusiveness, equity and small-scale self-governance are widely argued to be essential to sustainability, which is a major development in the 20-teens. However, taking climate change directly into account in the prosecution and management of fisheries is still infrequent, with only 14 explicit mentions. It ranks well behind governance as an expanding concept in the conceptualization of sustainable fishing. Experts still primarily seem to consider climate change an external factor that needs to be taken into account in prosecuting and managing fisheries sustainably.
      The identification and formal use of harvest control rules and quantitative or semi-quantitative benchmarks for the exploited stocks, species taken as bycatch, and impacts on seabed habitats has gained significant momentum through the 2010s, appearing in nearly 10% of all papers reviewed. The use of multiple knowledge systems is also being called for although not as a feature of how sustainability of fishing is conceptualized but rather, as a superior approach to evaluate any and all aspects of sustainability of uses. 
      Marine protected areas or their cognates are another frequent topic of literature on sustainable fishing in the 20-teens literature. Although highly protected marine protected areas by definition do not include fishing within their boundaries, proponents of high marine protected areas coverage argue that they are essential for conservation and the spill-over benefits from marine protected areas can be an important component of sustainability in fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KOnfPh0i","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gjerde, 2016; Laffoley, 2021; Rochette, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Gjerde, 2016; Laffoley, 2021; Rochette, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30703,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y633W6RW"],"itemData":{"id":30703,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation-Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","language":"en","page":"45–60","title":"Protecting Earth's last conservation frontier: scientific, management and legal priorities for MPAs beyond national boundaries","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Gjerde","given":"K.M."},{"family":"Reeve","given":"L.L."},{"family":"Harden-Davies","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":30679,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TNDEXNX4"],"itemData":{"id":30679,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.3512","ISSN":"1052-7613, 1099-0755","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Aquatic Conserv: Mar Freshw Ecosyst","language":"en","page":"1512-1534","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Evolving the narrative for protecting a rapidly changing ocean, post?COVID?19","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/aqc.3512","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Laffoley","given":"D."},{"family":"Baxter","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Amon","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"J."},{"family":"Hall?Spencer","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Grorud?Colvert","given":"K."},{"family":"Levin","given":"L.A."},{"family":"Reid","given":"P.C."},{"family":"Rogers","given":"A.D."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"M.L."},{"family":"Woodall","given":"L.C."},{"family":"Andersen","given":"N.F."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",6]]}}},{"id":30516,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KZL3EGNU"],"itemData":{"id":30516,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation-Marine and Freshwater Ecosystem","issue":"31-43","language":"en","title":"Delivering the Aichi target 11: challenges and opportunities for marine areas beyond national jurisdiction","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Rochette","given":"J."},{"family":"Gjerde","given":"K."},{"family":"Druel","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other experts argue that marine protected areas are simply one of many tools available to deliver sustainable outcomes from fishing. That tool needs to be planned and located with substantial care to deliver desired outcomes, particularly because marine protected areas often incur significant social and or economic costs, which frequently are distributed in very inequitable ways ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VOarRKCu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kockel, 2020; Li, 2020; Mizrahi, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Kockel, 2020; Li, 2020; Mizrahi, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30678,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NZA3C68H"],"itemData":{"id":30678,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0233339","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e0233339","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Addressing distribution equity in spatial conservation prioritization for small-scale fisheries","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0233339","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Kockel","given":"Alessia"},{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C."},{"family":"Costa","given":"Maycira"},{"family":"Dearden","given":"Philip"}],"editor":[{"family":"Kimirei","given":"Ismael Aaron"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,19]]}}},{"id":30517,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CB3DIMBP"],"itemData":{"id":30517,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"244","title":"Rethinking marine conservation strategies to minimize socio-economic costs in a dynamic perspective","author":[{"family":"Li","given":"Yunzhou"},{"family":"Sun","given":"Ming"},{"family":"Evans","given":"Keith S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30677,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2ZGH92VJ"],"itemData":{"id":30677,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2020.108554","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"108554","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Mitigating negative livelihood impacts of no-take MPAs on small-scale fishers","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320719314132","volume":"245","author":[{"family":"Mizrahi","given":"Me'ira"},{"family":"Duce","given":"Stephanie"},{"family":"Khine","given":"Zin Lin"},{"family":"MacKeracher","given":"Tracy"},{"family":"Maung","given":"Khin May Chit"},{"family":"Phyu","given":"Ei Thal"},{"family":"Pressey","given":"Robert L."},{"family":"Simpfendorfer","given":"Colin"},{"family":"Diedrich","given":"Amy"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Consensus is lacking on whether highly protected marine areas are sufficient or necessary to produce sustainable fishing, and their role in conceptualization of sustainable fishing is still unresolved. 
Table 2.1. Literature for fishing. Each paper was scored first on whether it addressed primarily the target species (population), the ecosystem impacts or sensitivity of a fishery (ecological), the value and financial incentives of the fishery (economic), or the social context in which the fishery operates (social). Then each paper was scored for which aspects of the other factors and whether the primary thrust of the paper was the performance and/or participation in the fishery itself (fishery), analytical methods (analytical), the role of governance (governance), a review of a relatively long historical time series (history), or the use of additional knowledge systems in evaluating the factor (knowledge).
