2.2.3.3 Conceptualizations of sustainable gathering in the academic literature
      2.2.3.3.1 Introduction
Gathering encompasses a wide range of species (see Chapter 1), including plants and fungi, as well as animals such as frogs, turtles and crocodilians. Each of these is studied in disparate academic fields. The framing of gathering that has gained most attention in the academic literature is that of “non-timber forest products” or “non-wood products”. This review focuses largely, but not exclusively, on the literature in this framing since it forms the bulk of published research on the topic. The data management report for this review is available at 10.5281/zenodo.6472995. 
      Discussions about the sustainability of gathering in the academic literature emerged in the late 1980s. Prior to this, there was a long history of research on the ecology, harvest, processing and trade of species that are gathered, but little mention of sustainability. When it did come up, ideas centered around tragedy of the commons ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"naPsaCuv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sills, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Sills, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DTGL3ZZQ"],"itemData":{"id":30545,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Non-Timber Forest Products in the Global Context","event-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","ISBN":"978-3-642-17982-2","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_2","page":"23-51","publisher":"Springer Berlin Heidelberg","publisher-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Evolving Perspectives on Non-timber Forest Products","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_2","volume":"7","editor":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Sheona"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"}],"author":[{"family":"Sills","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Paumgarten","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Beer","given":"Jenne","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Pierce","given":"Alan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In the late 1980s the gathering of plants, algae and fungi began to be widely promoted in global conservation circles as a conservation strategy. It was considered an alternative to logging and livestock ranching – major causes of deforestation at the time – that could support local livelihoods while leaving the forest standing. As both governments and non-governmental organizations initiated programs to promote plants, algae and fungi commercialization, academic discussions about the sustainability of gathering ensued. At first these discussions focused only on economic criteria, because the widely held assumption was that the ecological impacts of gathering were minimal ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dDQbY3oW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sills, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Sills, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DTGL3ZZQ"],"itemData":{"id":30545,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Non-Timber Forest Products in the Global Context","event-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","ISBN":"978-3-642-17982-2","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_2","page":"23-51","publisher":"Springer Berlin Heidelberg","publisher-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Evolving Perspectives on Non-timber Forest Products","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_2","volume":"7","editor":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Sheona"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"}],"author":[{"family":"Sills","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Paumgarten","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Beer","given":"Jenne","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Pierce","given":"Alan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ideas and heated debates about sustainability centered on the economic contributions of gathering to rural livelihoods, including for subsistence, cash income and as safety-nets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BWpdPHkc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arnold & Perez, 2001; Belcher & Schreckenberg, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Arnold & Perez, 2001; Belcher & Schreckenberg, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30674,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NB4S8RF9"],"itemData":{"id":30674,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"437–47","title":"Can Non-Timber Forest Products Match Tropical Forest Conservation and Development Objectives?","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Perez","given":"M.Ruiz"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":30583,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZGRZ46E8"],"itemData":{"id":30583,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This article challenges the pervasive view that commercialisation of non-timber forest products can (easily) achieve ecosystem and species conservation as well as improving livelihoods. Following a brief review of who and what is involved, it focuses on the main ecological and livelihood risks of unconsidered promotion of NTFP commercialisation, drawing on a wide range of case studies from around the world. It concludes with some recommendations, emphasising the lack of ?magic-bullet? products, and the importance - among other things - of not ignoring national policy, taking an integrated view of the value chain, considering the implications of different production options, and improving both quality and quantity.","container-title":"Development Policy Review","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-7679.2007.00374.x","ISSN":"0950-6764","issue":"3","page":"355–377","title":"Commercialisation of Non-timber Forest Products: A Reality Check","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-7679.2007.00374.x","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Schreckenberg","given":"Kathrin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]],"season":"mai"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      By 2010 however, the conceptualization of sustainable gathering had evolved to include economic, ecological and social components, with governance and management understood to be key components of the latter ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qz1REvp0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arnold & Perez, 2001; Belcher & Schreckenberg, 2007; Sills, 2011; Ticktin, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Arnold & Perez, 2001; Belcher & Schreckenberg, 2007; Sills, 2011; Ticktin, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30674,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NB4S8RF9"],"itemData":{"id":30674,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"437–47","title":"Can Non-Timber Forest Products Match Tropical Forest Conservation and Development Objectives?","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Perez","given":"M.Ruiz"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":30583,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZGRZ46E8"],"itemData":{"id":30583,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This article challenges the pervasive view that commercialisation of non-timber forest products can (easily) achieve ecosystem and species conservation as well as improving livelihoods. Following a brief review of who and what is involved, it focuses on the main ecological and livelihood risks of unconsidered promotion of NTFP commercialisation, drawing on a wide range of case studies from around the world. It concludes with some recommendations, emphasising the lack of ?magic-bullet? products, and the importance - among other things - of not ignoring national policy, taking an integrated view of the value chain, considering the implications of different production options, and improving both quality and quantity.","container-title":"Development Policy Review","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-7679.2007.00374.x","ISSN":"0950-6764","issue":"3","page":"355–377","title":"Commercialisation of Non-timber Forest Products: A Reality Check","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-7679.2007.00374.x","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Schreckenberg","given":"Kathrin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]],"season":"mai"}}},{"id":30545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DTGL3ZZQ"],"itemData":{"id":30545,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Non-Timber Forest Products in the Global Context","event-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","ISBN":"978-3-642-17982-2","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_2","page":"23-51","publisher":"Springer Berlin Heidelberg","publisher-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Evolving Perspectives on Non-timber Forest Products","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_2","volume":"7","editor":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Sheona"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"}],"author":[{"family":"Sills","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Paumgarten","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Beer","given":"Jenne","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Pierce","given":"Alan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":966,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DVE4IFPW"],"itemData":{"id":966,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2664.2004.00859.x","ISSN":"00218901, 13652664","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"11-21","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The ecological implications of harvesting non-timber forest products: Ecological implications of non-timber harvesting","title-short":"The ecological implications of harvesting non-timber forest products","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1365-2664.2004.00859.x","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",2,12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These components included supportive national policies, resource tenure to ensure benefits captured by those managing the resource, and strong institutions governing resource use including organization among producers, as well as equitable access and benefit sharing. In terms of management, effective inventory and monitoring, including strong community or local involvement in decision-making for management and monitoring, including co-management, adaptable resource management practices, and inclusion of traditional ecological knowledge in management plans were widely conceived to be critical aspects of sustainable use. Finally, transparency and integration along the value chain among producers, and inclusion of women were also recognized as key conditions for sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EEfyBMqw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arnold & Perez, 2001; Belcher & Schreckenberg, 2007; Sills, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Arnold & Perez, 2001; Belcher & Schreckenberg, 2007; Sills, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30674,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NB4S8RF9"],"itemData":{"id":30674,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"437–47","title":"Can Non-Timber Forest Products Match Tropical Forest Conservation and Development Objectives?","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Perez","given":"M.Ruiz"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":30583,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZGRZ46E8"],"itemData":{"id":30583,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This article challenges the pervasive view that commercialisation of non-timber forest products can (easily) achieve ecosystem and species conservation as well as improving livelihoods. Following a brief review of who and what is involved, it focuses on the main ecological and livelihood risks of unconsidered promotion of NTFP commercialisation, drawing on a wide range of case studies from around the world. It concludes with some recommendations, emphasising the lack of ?magic-bullet? products, and the importance - among other things - of not ignoring national policy, taking an integrated view of the value chain, considering the implications of different production options, and improving both quality and quantity.","container-title":"Development Policy Review","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-7679.2007.00374.x","ISSN":"0950-6764","issue":"3","page":"355–377","title":"Commercialisation of Non-timber Forest Products: A Reality Check","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-7679.2007.00374.x","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Schreckenberg","given":"Kathrin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]],"season":"mai"}}},{"id":30545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DTGL3ZZQ"],"itemData":{"id":30545,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Non-Timber Forest Products in the Global Context","event-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","ISBN":"978-3-642-17982-2","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_2","page":"23-51","publisher":"Springer Berlin Heidelberg","publisher-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Evolving Perspectives on Non-timber Forest Products","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_2","volume":"7","editor":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Sheona"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"}],"author":[{"family":"Sills","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Paumgarten","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Beer","given":"Jenne","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Pierce","given":"Alan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      On the ecological side, most research conceptualized sustainability in terms of the maintenance of forest cover and/or the persistence of the harvested species. However, considerations of the effects of gathering on the broader ecological community, including of ecologically-related species and on measures of biodiversity and ecosystem processes were also discussed, if rarely measured ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yGf8CFFO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ticktin, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Ticktin, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":966,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DVE4IFPW"],"itemData":{"id":966,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2664.2004.00859.x","ISSN":"00218901, 13652664","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"11-21","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The ecological implications of harvesting non-timber forest products: Ecological implications of non-timber harvesting","title-short":"The ecological implications of harvesting non-timber forest products","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1365-2664.2004.00859.x","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",2,12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      The broad consensus however, which holds still today, was that given the vast array of species, life-histories, and types of use, and the widely different roles these play in local livelihoods, there is no one size fits all ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IPAMUicN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sills, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Sills, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DTGL3ZZQ"],"itemData":{"id":30545,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Non-Timber Forest Products in the Global Context","event-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","ISBN":"978-3-642-17982-2","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_2","page":"23-51","publisher":"Springer Berlin Heidelberg","publisher-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Evolving Perspectives on Non-timber Forest Products","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_2","volume":"7","editor":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Sheona"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"}],"author":[{"family":"Sills","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Paumgarten","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Beer","given":"Jenne","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Pierce","given":"Alan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      2.2.3.3.2 Post-2010
Dynamic social-ecological systems
Conceptualizations of sustainable gathering have shifted in the past 10 years in multiple ways. First, sustainable harvesting is now frequently conceptualized in terms of dynamic social-ecological systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jKEYFEsl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pezzuti, 2018; Shackleton, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Pezzuti, 2018; Shackleton, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30586,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5QMWQUAF"],"itemData":{"id":30586,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Community-based natural resource management (CBNRM) involves a system of local practices designed to regulate access to, and use of, natural resources through rules and norms shared by a set of users. These institutions are usually defined through rational motivations that drive collective action and well-delimited social and spatial boundaries. We discuss the shortcomings of these premises in dynamic ecological systems where the location of resource concentrations is ephemeral. We explore four cases of communitybased management of river turtle nesting sites on the lower Amazon floodplain. Despite the high ecological risks, monitoring costs, and limited material benefits, community residents remain motivated to engage in this collective activity. Based on information from numerous studies carried out over a period of two decades, we discuss how motivation to develop CBNRMs has changed over time and space and how intercommunity linkages have contributed to the endurance of this local institution.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-10254-230336","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art36","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Commoning in dynamic environments: community-based management of turtle nesting sites on the lower Amazon floodplain","title-short":"Commoning in dynamic environments","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol23/iss3/art36/","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Pezzuti","given":"Juarez"},{"family":"Castro","given":"Fábio","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"McGrath","given":"David G."},{"family":"Miorando","given":"Priscila Saikoski"},{"family":"Barboza","given":"Roberta Sá Leitão"},{"family":"Carneiro Romagnoli","given":"Fernanda"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":30539,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VHZHCXCJ"],"itemData":{"id":30539,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"There is growing knowledge about and appreciation of the importance of Non-timber Forest Products (NTFPs) to rural livelihoods in developing countries, and to a lesser extent, developed countries. However, there is also an assumption on the part of policy-makers that any harvesting of wild animal or plant products from the forests and other natural and modified ecosystems must be detrimental to the long-term viability of target populations and species. This book challenges this idea and shows that while examples of such negative impacts certainly exist, there are also many examples of sustainable harvesting systems for NTFPs. The chapters review and present coherent and scientifically sound information and case studies on the ecologically sustainable use of NTFPs. They also outline a general interdisciplinary approach for assessing the sustainability of NTFP harvesting systems at different scales. A wide range of case studies is included from Africa, Asia and South America, using plant and animal products for food, crafts, textiles, medicines and cosmetics\\"--","call-number":"SD543 .E46 2015","collection-title":"People and plants international conservation","event-place":"London ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-415-72859-1","number-of-pages":"280","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Ecological sustainability for non-timber forest products: dynamics and case studies of harvesting","title-short":"Ecological sustainability for non-timber forest products","editor":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C. M."},{"family":"Pandey","given":"A. K."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, where ecological, economic, political and socio-cultural dimensions of gathering are both interdependent and inseparable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HwU1XH6D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Mello, 2020)","plainCitation":"(de Mello, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33986,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TV355YM2"],"itemData":{"id":33986,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2020.102109","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"102109","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Social-ecological sustainability of non-timber forest products: A review and theoretical considerations for future research","title-short":"Social-ecological sustainability of non-timber forest products","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934119301303","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Mello","given":"Natália Girão Rodrigues","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Gulinck","given":"Hubert"},{"family":"Van den Broeck","given":"Pieter"},{"family":"Parra","given":"Constanza"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, sustainability is increasingly envisioned as a spatially and temporally dynamic phenomenon where harvest systems are in constant flux, with changes occurring at multiple levels and spatial scales and across the various components of the social-ecological system simultaneously ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZEgQyz9U","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pezzuti, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Pezzuti, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30586,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5QMWQUAF"],"itemData":{"id":30586,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Community-based natural resource management (CBNRM) involves a system of local practices designed to regulate access to, and use of, natural resources through rules and norms shared by a set of users. These institutions are usually defined through rational motivations that drive collective action and well-delimited social and spatial boundaries. We discuss the shortcomings of these premises in dynamic ecological systems where the location of resource concentrations is ephemeral. We explore four cases of communitybased management of river turtle nesting sites on the lower Amazon floodplain. Despite the high ecological risks, monitoring costs, and limited material benefits, community residents remain motivated to engage in this collective activity. Based on information from numerous studies carried out over a period of two decades, we discuss how motivation to develop CBNRMs has changed over time and space and how intercommunity linkages have contributed to the endurance of this local institution.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-10254-230336","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art36","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Commoning in dynamic environments: community-based management of turtle nesting sites on the lower Amazon floodplain","title-short":"Commoning in dynamic environments","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol23/iss3/art36/","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Pezzuti","given":"Juarez"},{"family":"Castro","given":"Fábio","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"McGrath","given":"David G."},{"family":"Miorando","given":"Priscila Saikoski"},{"family":"Barboza","given":"Roberta Sá Leitão"},{"family":"Carneiro Romagnoli","given":"Fernanda"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As such, conceptualizations of sustainability have shifted towards being context and scale-specific ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1i1dyxXU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shackleton, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Shackleton, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30539,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VHZHCXCJ"],"itemData":{"id":30539,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"There is growing knowledge about and appreciation of the importance of Non-timber Forest Products (NTFPs) to rural livelihoods in developing countries, and to a lesser extent, developed countries. However, there is also an assumption on the part of policy-makers that any harvesting of wild animal or plant products from the forests and other natural and modified ecosystems must be detrimental to the long-term viability of target populations and species. This book challenges this idea and shows that while examples of such negative impacts certainly exist, there are also many examples of sustainable harvesting systems for NTFPs. The chapters review and present coherent and scientifically sound information and case studies on the ecologically sustainable use of NTFPs. They also outline a general interdisciplinary approach for assessing the sustainability of NTFP harvesting systems at different scales. A wide range of case studies is included from Africa, Asia and South America, using plant and animal products for food, crafts, textiles, medicines and cosmetics\\"--","call-number":"SD543 .E46 2015","collection-title":"People and plants international conservation","event-place":"London ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-415-72859-1","number-of-pages":"280","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Ecological sustainability for non-timber forest products: dynamics and case studies of harvesting","title-short":"Ecological sustainability for non-timber forest products","editor":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C. M."},{"family":"Pandey","given":"A. K."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
Sustainability of multiple practices 
In the social-ecological system framing, the sustainability of gathering is no longer considered in isolation from that of other land and resources uses with which gathering co-occurs. For example, scholars argue that sustainability of gathering cannot be conceptualized in isolation of the sustainability of logging and hunting, due to the feedback loops among these practices across many landscapes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"W0407VbZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shackleton, 2015; Ticktin, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Shackleton, 2015; Ticktin, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30539,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VHZHCXCJ"],"itemData":{"id":30539,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"There is growing knowledge about and appreciation of the importance of Non-timber Forest Products (NTFPs) to rural livelihoods in developing countries, and to a lesser extent, developed countries. However, there is also an assumption on the part of policy-makers that any harvesting of wild animal or plant products from the forests and other natural and modified ecosystems must be detrimental to the long-term viability of target populations and species. This book challenges this idea and shows that while examples of such negative impacts certainly exist, there are also many examples of sustainable harvesting systems for NTFPs. The chapters review and present coherent and scientifically sound information and case studies on the ecologically sustainable use of NTFPs. They also outline a general interdisciplinary approach for assessing the sustainability of NTFP harvesting systems at different scales. A wide range of case studies is included from Africa, Asia and South America, using plant and animal products for food, crafts, textiles, medicines and cosmetics\\"--","call-number":"SD543 .E46 2015","collection-title":"People and plants international conservation","event-place":"London ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-415-72859-1","number-of-pages":"280","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Ecological sustainability for non-timber forest products: dynamics and case studies of harvesting","title-short":"Ecological sustainability for non-timber forest products","editor":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C. M."},{"family":"Pandey","given":"A. K."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":30685,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A2HIDN9M"],"itemData":{"id":30685,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Ecological Sustainability for Non-timber Forest Products. Dynamics and Case Studies of Harvesting","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-315-85158-7","language":"en","note":"type: Pages 31-52","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London","title":"The ecological sustainability of harvesting non-timber forest products: principles and methods","author":[{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."}],"editor":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C."},{"family":"Pandey","given":"A."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, sustainability of gathering is now frequently conceptualized in combination with that of interacting agricultural practices, including grazing and foraging of livestock, and associated fire regimes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"trAbJXZz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Groenendijk, 2012; Lybbert, 2011; Sampaio, 2012; Ticktin, 2012, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Groenendijk, 2012; Lybbert, 2011; Sampaio, 2012; Ticktin, 2012, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30547,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K59E4M4U"],"itemData":{"id":30547,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2664.2011.02078.x","ISSN":"00218901","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"164-173","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Limitations to sustainable frankincense production: blocked regeneration, high adult mortality and declining populations: <i>Sustainable frankincense production</i>","title-short":"Limitations to sustainable frankincense production","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1365-2664.2011.02078.x","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Groenendijk","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Eshete","given":"Abeje"},{"family":"Sterck","given":"Frank J."},{"family":"Zuidema","given":"Pieter A."},{"family":"Bongers","given":"Frans"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",2]]}}},{"id":30582,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S2NUHSXZ"],"itemData":{"id":30582,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Morocco's argan oil is now the most expensive edible oil in the world. High-value argan markets have sparked a bonanza of argan activity. Nongovernmental organizations, international and domestic development agencies, and argan oil cooperatives aggressively promote the win–win aim of simultaneously benefiting local people and the health of the argan forest. This paper tests some of these win–win claims. Analysis of a panel of detailed household data suggests that the boom has enabled some rural households to increase consumption, increase their goat herds (which bodes poorly for the argan forest), and send their girls to secondary school. The boom has predictably made households vigilant guardians of fruit on the tree, but it has not incited investments in longer term tree and forest health. We evaluate landscape-level impacts of these changes using commune-level data on educational enrollment and normalized difference vegetation index data over the period from 1981 to 2009. The results of the mesoanalysis of enrollment are consistent with the microanalysis: the argan boom seems to have improved educational outcomes, especially for girls. Our normalized difference vegetation index analysis, however, suggests that booming argan prices have not improved the forest and may have even induced degradation. We conclude by exploring the dynamic interactions between argan markets, local institutions, rural household welfare, and forest conservation and sustainability.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1106382108","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"34","journalAbbreviation":"PNAS","language":"en","note":"PMID: 21873185","page":"13963-13968","source":"www.pnas.org","title":"Booming markets for Moroccan argan oil appear to benefit some rural households while threatening the endemic argan forest","URL":"https://www.pnas.org/content/108/34/13963","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Lybbert","given":"Travis J."},{"family":"Aboudrare","given":"Abdellah"},{"family":"Chaloud","given":"Deborah"},{"family":"Magnan","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Nash","given":"Maliha"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8,23]]}}},{"id":30541,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WVX6PJWP"],"itemData":{"id":30541,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-012-9519-y","ISSN":"0300-7839, 1572-9915","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Hum Ecol","language":"en","page":"821-831","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effects of Socioeconomic Conditions on Multiple Uses of Swamp Forests in Central Brazil","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10745-012-9519-y","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Sampaio","given":"Maurício Bonesso"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Seixas","given":"Cristiana Simão"},{"family":"Santos","given":"Flavio Antonio Maës","non-dropping-particle":"dos"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",12]]}}},{"id":30671,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CZ7INENH"],"itemData":{"id":30671,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2664.2012.02156.x","ISSN":"00218901","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"774-784","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Disentangling the effects of multiple anthropogenic drivers on the decline of two tropical dry forest trees: Demographic effects of multiple disturbances","title-short":"Disentangling the effects of multiple anthropogenic drivers on the decline of two tropical dry forest trees","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1365-2664.2012.02156.x","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Ganesan","given":"Rengaian"},{"family":"Paramesha","given":"Mallegowda"},{"family":"Setty","given":"Siddappa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",8]]}}},{"id":30528,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4HNVTIRX"],"itemData":{"id":30528,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"648–654","title":"Disentangling, again, the drivers of decline for harvested tree species","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."},{"family":"Ganesan","given":"R."},{"family":"Paramesh","given":"R."},{"family":"Setty","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Consideration of feedbacks between gathering and invasive species has also emerged as a consideration for determining if a use is sustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KfapHiTf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Darabant, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Darabant, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32306,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3WB2RUJE"],"itemData":{"id":32306,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/1365-2664.12746","ISSN":"00218901","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Ecol","language":"en","page":"1613-1622","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Burning for enhanced non-timber forest product yield may jeopardize the resource base through interactive effects","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/1365-2664.12746","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Darabant","given":"András"},{"literal":"Dorji"},{"family":"Staudhammer","given":"Christina L."},{"family":"Rai","given":"Prem B."},{"family":"Gratzer","given":"Georg"}],"editor":[{"family":"Brando","given":"Paulo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
Sustainable use and ecosystem services
The effects of gathering on the provision of ecosystem services is now increasingly conceptualized as a component of sustainable use. This is usually framed as trade-offs across services, such as provisioning of plants, algae, fungi, timber, and carbon services ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nP84RYVc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Granath, 2018; Strengbom, 2018; Trivi\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}o, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Granath, 2018; Strengbom, 2018; Triviño, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30546,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AJGIZUJK"],"itemData":{"id":30546,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/1365-2664.13098","ISSN":"00218901","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Ecol","language":"en","page":"1658-1667","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trade-offs in berry production and biodiversity under prescribed burning and retention regimes in boreal forests","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/1365-2664.13098","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Granath","given":"Gustaf"},{"family":"Kouki","given":"Jari"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Samuel"},{"family":"Heikkala","given":"Osmo"},{"family":"Rodríguez","given":"Antonio"},{"family":"Strengbom","given":"Joachim"}],"editor":[{"family":"Mukul","given":"Sharif"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}},{"id":30535,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/72YM4A8P"],"itemData":{"id":30535,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/1365-2664.13019","ISSN":"00218901","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Ecol","language":"en","page":"958-966","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trade-offs in the multi-use potential of managed boreal forests","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/1365-2664.13019","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Strengbom","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Axelsson","given":"E. Petter"},{"family":"Lundmark","given":"Tomas"},{"family":"Nordin","given":"Annika"}],"editor":[{"family":"Villard","given":"Marc-André"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3]]}}},{"id":30538,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3FFRYGEQ"],"itemData":{"id":30538,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/1365-2664.12790","ISSN":"00218901","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Ecol","language":"en","page":"61-70","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Optimizing management to enhance multifunctionality in a boreal forest landscape","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/1365-2664.12790","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Triviño","given":"María"},{"family":"Pohjanmies","given":"Tähti"},{"family":"Mazziotta","given":"Adriano"},{"family":"Juutinen","given":"Artti"},{"family":"Podkopaev","given":"Dmitry"},{"family":"Le Tortorec","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Mönkkönen","given":"Mikko"}],"editor":[{"family":"Mori","given":"Akira"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and the cost of production ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DBVZtCy3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lambini, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Lambini, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30544,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7FDWE2QH"],"itemData":{"id":30544,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2018.01.029","ISSN":"09218009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"343-352","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Are Ecosystem Services Complementary or Competitive? An Econometric Analysis of Cost Functions of Private Forests in Vietnam","title-short":"Are Ecosystem Services Complementary or Competitive?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800916312423","volume":"147","author":[{"family":"Lambini","given":"Cosmas Kombat"},{"family":"Nguyen","given":"Trung Thanh"},{"family":"Abildtrup","given":"Jens"},{"family":"Pham","given":"Van Dien"},{"family":"Tenhunen","given":"John"},{"family":"Garcia","given":"Serge"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other authors argue that sustainable gathering could also include consideration of ecosystem services that have not been considered to date, for example the provision of services to other species, including food, shelter and resources used as medicine by non-humans ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gWkUR6Zb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shackleton, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Shackleton, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30536,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EJSMZKZL"],"itemData":{"id":30536,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-10469-230422","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art22","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Nontimber forest products as ecological and biocultural keystone species","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol23/iss4/art22/","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
Socio-cultural dimensions 
While the majority of studies on gathering that conceptualize sustainability from a social and economic science perspective emphasize economic and ecological trade-offs, more recent ideas about the sustainability of gathering include socio-cultural dimensions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"carn7k7o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Mello, 2020; Pezzuti, 2018)","plainCitation":"(de Mello, 2020; Pezzuti, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33986,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TV355YM2"],"itemData":{"id":33986,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2020.102109","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"102109","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Social-ecological sustainability of non-timber forest products: A review and theoretical considerations for future research","title-short":"Social-ecological sustainability of non-timber forest products","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934119301303","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Mello","given":"Natália Girão Rodrigues","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Gulinck","given":"Hubert"},{"family":"Van den Broeck","given":"Pieter"},{"family":"Parra","given":"Constanza"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}},{"id":30586,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5QMWQUAF"],"itemData":{"id":30586,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Community-based natural resource management (CBNRM) involves a system of local practices designed to regulate access to, and use of, natural resources through rules and norms shared by a set of users. These institutions are usually defined through rational motivations that drive collective action and well-delimited social and spatial boundaries. We discuss the shortcomings of these premises in dynamic ecological systems where the location of resource concentrations is ephemeral. We explore four cases of communitybased management of river turtle nesting sites on the lower Amazon floodplain. Despite the high ecological risks, monitoring costs, and limited material benefits, community residents remain motivated to engage in this collective activity. Based on information from numerous studies carried out over a period of two decades, we discuss how motivation to develop CBNRMs has changed over time and space and how intercommunity linkages have contributed to the endurance of this local institution.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-10254-230336","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art36","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Commoning in dynamic environments: community-based management of turtle nesting sites on the lower Amazon floodplain","title-short":"Commoning in dynamic environments","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol23/iss3/art36/","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Pezzuti","given":"Juarez"},{"family":"Castro","given":"Fábio","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"McGrath","given":"David G."},{"family":"Miorando","given":"Priscila Saikoski"},{"family":"Barboza","given":"Roberta Sá Leitão"},{"family":"Carneiro Romagnoli","given":"Fernanda"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Consistent with conceptualizations of sustainable use in indigenous peoples and local communities (see sections 2.2.4 and 2.2.8), the maintenance of social networks, including sharing networks and inter-community linkages, and social institutions are increasingly recognized in the academic literature as core elements of sustainable gathering. The relationship between gathering and health and wellbeing has also emerged as a critical element of social sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eKjiXQ1U","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sills, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Sills, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DTGL3ZZQ"],"itemData":{"id":30545,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Non-Timber Forest Products in the Global Context","event-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","ISBN":"978-3-642-17982-2","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_2","page":"23-51","publisher":"Springer Berlin Heidelberg","publisher-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Evolving Perspectives on Non-timber Forest Products","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_2","volume":"7","editor":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Sheona"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"}],"author":[{"family":"Sills","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Paumgarten","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Beer","given":"Jenne","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Pierce","given":"Alan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wellbeing can be generated in multiple ways, including through: the physical and spiritual act of gathering, connection to place, cultural symbolism, and consumption of the products gathered (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mdQZZnam","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Mello, 2020; Rapinski, 2018; Shackleton, 2018)","plainCitation":"(de Mello, 2020; Rapinski, 2018; Shackleton, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33986,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TV355YM2"],"itemData":{"id":33986,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2020.102109","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"102109","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Social-ecological sustainability of non-timber forest products: A review and theoretical considerations for future research","title-short":"Social-ecological sustainability of non-timber forest products","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934119301303","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Mello","given":"Natália Girão Rodrigues","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Gulinck","given":"Hubert"},{"family":"Van den Broeck","given":"Pieter"},{"family":"Parra","given":"Constanza"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}},{"id":30540,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y6B3BSBK"],"itemData":{"id":30540,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","DOI":"10.2993/0278-0771-38.3.333","ISSN":"0278-0771","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnobiology","page":"333","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Inuit Perception of Marine Organisms: From Folk Classification to Food Harvest","title-short":"Inuit Perception of Marine Organisms","URL":"https://bioone.org/journals/journal-of-ethnobiology/volume-38/issue-3/0278-0771-38.3.333/Inuit-Perception-of-Marine-Organisms--From-Folk-Classification-to/10.2993/0278-0771-38.3.333.full","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Rapinski","given":"Michel"},{"family":"Cuerrier","given":"Alain"},{"family":"Harris","given":"Cory"},{"family":"Ivujivik","given":"Elders","dropping-particle":"of"},{"family":"Kangiqsujuaq","given":"Elders","dropping-particle":"of"},{"family":"Lemire","given":"Mélanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",9,1]]}}},{"id":30536,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EJSMZKZL"],"itemData":{"id":30536,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-10469-230422","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art22","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Nontimber forest products as ecological and biocultural keystone species","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol23/iss4/art22/","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although the contribution of gathering to community health and nutrition has been well recognized for some time, especially as nutritional safety nets of both foods and medicines, these considerations are more frequently being conceptualized as considerations for sustainable use (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OX4xu3YS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Morsello, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Morsello, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30543,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2Z99N4PB"],"itemData":{"id":30543,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2014.01.021","ISSN":"09218009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"140-149","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Does trading non-timber forest products drive specialisation in products gathered for consumption? Evidence from the Brazilian Amazon","title-short":"Does trading non-timber forest products drive specialisation in products gathered for consumption?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800914000366","volume":"100","author":[{"family":"Morsello","given":"Carla"},{"family":"Delgado","given":"Juliana Aparecida da Silva"},{"family":"Fonseca-Morello","given":"Thiago"},{"family":"Brites","given":"Alice Dantas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Both food justice and sovereignty and health justice are viewed as aspects of sustainable gathering in indigenous peoples and local communities as well as in urban settings ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QXWKUPUB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Poe, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Poe, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30542,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CC79K4JB"],"itemData":{"id":30542,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-013-9572-1","ISSN":"0300-7839, 1572-9915","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Hum Ecol","language":"en","page":"409-422","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Urban Forest Justice and the Rights to Wild Foods, Medicines, and Materials in the City","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10745-013-9572-1","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
Coproduction
Finally, as discussed above, co-management approaches that include the integration of traditional and/or local ecological knowledge and science, have been recognized for some time as important for the sustainable gathering of commercialized species. However, sustainable gathering in changing contexts is now increasingly understood to depend not on the integration of knowledge systems, as was previously conceptualized, but rather on the coproduction of new knowledge (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vVlpJatp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Davidson-Hunt, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Davidson-Hunt, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30537,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WH2URDCS"],"itemData":{"id":30537,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"ABSTRACT. Interaction, negotiation, and sharing knowledge are at the heart of indigenous response to global environmental change. We consider Anishinaabe efforts to devise new institutional arrangements in response to the process of colonialism and changing global markets. Our findings are based on collaborative research undertaken with Anishinaabe colleagues from Pikangikum First Nation, northwestern Ontario. We worked with elders to understand their knowledge, preferences, and opinions regarding appropriate institutional arrangements for the co-production of knowledge required to develop nontimber forest products. We began our research by asking about the values, institutions, and conditions that guide plant harvesting, and then the conditions necessary to coproduce new knowledge regarding plant products with external partners. Results were discussed during focus groups and community meetings, and were modified based on that feedback. This research resulted in a framework based on the values, institutions, and conditions that are necessary for the coproduction of new knowledge. In this framework, Pikangikum people—through Anishinaabe teachings and collaborative partnerships—guide knowledge coproduction through meaningful participation as research advisors in the development of knowledge, institutions, and technologies. Coproducing knowledge in response to environmental change requires new institutional arrangements that provide community control, meaningful collaboration and partnerships, and significant benefit sharing with Pikangikum people.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"4","source":"JSTOR","title":"Anishinaabe Adaptation to Environmental Change in Northwestern Ontario: a Case Study in Knowledge Coproduction for Nontimber Forest Products","title-short":"Anishinaabe Adaptation to Environmental Change in Northwestern Ontario","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/26269415","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Davidson-Hunt","given":"Iain J."},{"family":"Idrobo","given":"C. Julián"},{"family":"Pengelly","given":"Ryan D."},{"family":"Sylvester","given":"Olivia"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The latter is understood to require institutional arrangements that provide community control, meaningful collaboration and partnerships, and significant benefit sharing.
2.2.3.4 Conceptualizations of sustainable terrestial animal harvesting (focus on hunting) in the academic literature
      2.2.3.4.1 Pre-2010 conceptualizations of hunting 
Hunting is defined as the act of searching, pursuing, collecting or killing wild animals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RydX0dBa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lindsey, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Lindsey, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30669,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDAFNMZ8"],"itemData":{"id":30669,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"There is a lack of consensus among conservationists as to whether trophy hunting represents a legitimate conservation tool in Africa. Hunting advocates stress that trophy hunting can create incentives for conservation where ecotourism is not possible. We assessed the hunting preferences of hunting clients who have hunted or plan to hunt in Africa (n=150), and the perception among African hunting operators (n=127) of client preferences at two US hunting conventions to determine whether this assertion is justified. Clients are most interested in hunting in well-known East and southern African hunting destinations, but some trophy species attract hunters to remote and unstable countries that might not otherwise derive revenues from hunting. Clients are willing to hunt in areas lacking high densities of wildlife or attractive scenery, and where people and livestock occur, stressing the potential for trophy hunting to generate revenues where ecotourism may not be viable. Hunting clients are more averse to hunting under conditions whereby conservation objectives are compromised than operators realize, suggesting that client preferences could potentially drive positive change in the hunting industry, to the benefit of conservation. However, the preferences and attitudes of some clients likely form the basis of some of the problems currently associated with the hunting industry in Africa, stressing the need for an effective regulatory framework.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","ISSN":"1469-1795","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","page":"283-291","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Potential of trophy hunting to create incentives for wildlife conservation in Africa where alternative wildlife-based land uses may not be viable","URL":"https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"P. A."},{"family":"Alexander","given":"R."},{"family":"Frank","given":"L. G."},{"family":"Mathieson","given":"A."},{"family":"Romañach","given":"S. S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hunting is one of the earliest forms of interaction between humans and the environment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9gmE0bef","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kittenberger, 1929)","plainCitation":"(Kittenberger, 1929)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30549,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VKTCM9AD"],"itemData":{"id":30549,"type":"book","publisher":"Longmans, Green & Company","title":"Big game hunting and collecting in East Africa, 1903-1926","URL":"https://scholar.googleusercontent.com/scholar.bib?q=info:n9IOTU5qVvMJ:scholar.google.com/&output=citation&scisdr=CgXsI6bnEK7v-6H2F7M:AAGBfm0AAAAAYEPzD7NwqsOFGttu_ZeMlxiBTVdLFgfI&scisig=AAGBfm0AAAAAYEPzD0lxBB8I0wez_Dc4aFmzLomLfzgC&scisf=4&ct=citation&cd=-1&hl=en","author":[{"family":"Kittenberger","given":"Kalman"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1929"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Reasons for hunting range from subsistence to management, recreation, sport (trophy hunting) and cultural heritage or a combination of these ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"y4rqIsb8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lindsey, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Lindsey, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30669,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDAFNMZ8"],"itemData":{"id":30669,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"There is a lack of consensus among conservationists as to whether trophy hunting represents a legitimate conservation tool in Africa. Hunting advocates stress that trophy hunting can create incentives for conservation where ecotourism is not possible. We assessed the hunting preferences of hunting clients who have hunted or plan to hunt in Africa (n=150), and the perception among African hunting operators (n=127) of client preferences at two US hunting conventions to determine whether this assertion is justified. Clients are most interested in hunting in well-known East and southern African hunting destinations, but some trophy species attract hunters to remote and unstable countries that might not otherwise derive revenues from hunting. Clients are willing to hunt in areas lacking high densities of wildlife or attractive scenery, and where people and livestock occur, stressing the potential for trophy hunting to generate revenues where ecotourism may not be viable. Hunting clients are more averse to hunting under conditions whereby conservation objectives are compromised than operators realize, suggesting that client preferences could potentially drive positive change in the hunting industry, to the benefit of conservation. However, the preferences and attitudes of some clients likely form the basis of some of the problems currently associated with the hunting industry in Africa, stressing the need for an effective regulatory framework.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","ISSN":"1469-1795","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","page":"283-291","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Potential of trophy hunting to create incentives for wildlife conservation in Africa where alternative wildlife-based land uses may not be viable","URL":"https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"P. A."},{"family":"Alexander","given":"R."},{"family":"Frank","given":"L. G."},{"family":"Mathieson","given":"A."},{"family":"Romañach","given":"S. S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hunting can also be conducted for the purpose of trade of animal derivatives for making jewellery and sometimes for medicinal purposes under various contexts.
      Prior to 2010, hunting was conceptualized in the literature as reflecting utilitarian and economic values ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jEabyuT8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eltringham, 1994; Sinclair, 1991)","plainCitation":"(Eltringham, 1994; Sinclair, 1991)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30570,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L275VXRW"],"itemData":{"id":30570,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605300028519","issue":"3","page":"163-168","title":"Can wildlife pay its way","URL":"https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/48e3/20d550f3e09dd0a74aa4d3e040ebfc6c8dea.pdf","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Eltringham","given":"S.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}},{"id":30514,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9KWCSBJP"],"itemData":{"id":30514,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"767–773","title":"Science and the Practice of Wildlife Management","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Sinclair","given":"A.R.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which could provide incentives for wild species conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3uod148G","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. G. Robinson & Bodmer, 1999)","plainCitation":"(J. G. Robinson & Bodmer, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30581,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E6PJ354Z"],"itemData":{"id":30581,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Hunting is ubiquitous in tropical forests around the world, and meat from wildlife species is an important source of animal protein for rural populations. Ungulates, primates, and rodents provide most of the biomass consumed, but a wide variety of wildlife species are hunted for both subsistence and commerce. Across the tropics, tens of millions of animals and millions of metric tons of meat are hunted and consumed each year. Is this harvest sustainable? Available information and the use of simple sustainability models suggest it is not. The supply of wildlife is limited, as annual production of large mammals in tropical forests is low when compared to other ecosystems. The demand is increasing as tropical forests become more accessible to hunters, effective human population densities increase, people become more sedentary, traditional hunting practices change, the meat trade becomes more commercial, and demand increases for wild meat from urban centers. If wildlife populations are to be sustained, then management institutions, be they government or community-based, need to be strengthened.","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","DOI":"10.2307/3802482","ISSN":"0022-541X","issue":"1","note":"publisher: [Wiley, Wildlife Society]","page":"1-13","source":"JSTOR","title":"Towards Wildlife Management in Tropical Forests","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/3802482","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"John G."},{"family":"Bodmer","given":"Richard E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Conceptualizations of hunting identified in the literature prior to 2010 are in line with international conventions and guidelines like the Convention on Biological Diversity and the Addis Ababa Principles and Guidelines for the Sustainable Use of Biodiversity, which confirm the right and the need for the sustainable use of natural resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dTmENwvm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2006)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1318,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DAANTGGC"],"itemData":{"id":1318,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"IUCN-ESUSG WISPER","title":"Guidelines on Sustainable Hunting in Europe","URL":"https://www.fundacioncazasostenible.org/app/download/5795770411/Guidelines+on+Sustainable+Hunting+in+Europe.pdf","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"editor":[{"family":"Casaer","given":"J."},{"family":"Kenward","given":"R."},{"family":"Lecocq","given":"Y."},{"family":"Reimoser","given":"F."},{"family":"Sharp","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The literature in this time period generally argued that sustainable use of wild species should contribute to both human needs and to the conservation of biological diversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PK7eXRo0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Baldus, 2008; McMichael, 2003; Robertson, 1991)","plainCitation":"(Baldus, 2008; McMichael, 2003; Robertson, 1991)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30534,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QJXXD37C"],"itemData":{"id":30534,"type":"book","event-place":"Budakeszi","ISBN":"978-963-87791-1-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: 931325384","publisher":"CIC","publisher-place":"Budakeszi","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Best practices in sustainable hunting a guide to best practices from around the world","URL":"http://webdoc.sub.gwdg.de/ebook/serien/yo/CIC/01.pdf","author":[{"family":"Baldus","given":"Rolf D"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":30522,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E7KHV7CK"],"itemData":{"id":30522,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","language":"en","page":"1919–1920","title":"New visions for addressing sustainability","volume":"302","author":[{"family":"McMichael","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Butler","given":"C.D."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":30711,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CCRQQXGU"],"itemData":{"id":30711,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Gibier Faune Sauvage","language":"fr","page":"379–388","title":"Wise use and conservation","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Robertson","given":"P.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Well-managed hunting with efficient legislative mechanisms and scientific input, such as the case of American hunting, were viewed as sustainable while also providing many incentives for conservation of species and landscapes. It was also argued that hunting is an important conservation tool because the social and economic benefits derived from it provide incentives for people to conserve the sources of those benefits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kKE3h3fp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2006)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1318,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DAANTGGC"],"itemData":{"id":1318,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"IUCN-ESUSG WISPER","title":"Guidelines on Sustainable Hunting in Europe","URL":"https://www.fundacioncazasostenible.org/app/download/5795770411/Guidelines+on+Sustainable+Hunting+in+Europe.pdf","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"editor":[{"family":"Casaer","given":"J."},{"family":"Kenward","given":"R."},{"family":"Lecocq","given":"Y."},{"family":"Reimoser","given":"F."},{"family":"Sharp","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This concept was instrumental in stimulating several conservation initiatives, particularly initiatives where indigenous people and local community engagement, equity and community benefits are crucial. Hunting was regarded as having the potential to support sustainable utilization of wild species, particularly if management took into account harvested species’ impacts on other species and on vegetation and was conducted in line with ecological principles applied across their natural ranges ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WaVmsRSj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2006)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1318,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DAANTGGC"],"itemData":{"id":1318,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"IUCN-ESUSG WISPER","title":"Guidelines on Sustainable Hunting in Europe","URL":"https://www.fundacioncazasostenible.org/app/download/5795770411/Guidelines+on+Sustainable+Hunting+in+Europe.pdf","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"editor":[{"family":"Casaer","given":"J."},{"family":"Kenward","given":"R."},{"family":"Lecocq","given":"Y."},{"family":"Reimoser","given":"F."},{"family":"Sharp","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
Hunting and wild species population management
Prior 2010, hunting was described as an important animal population control tool which played a crucial role in maintaining animal populations at sizes that prevent stress on the rangelands supporting them ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5wIC72ne","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Williams, 1996)","plainCitation":"(Williams, 1996)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30584,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PD9E2T23"],"itemData":{"id":30584,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-0-89272-383-6","language":"eng","number-of-pages":"318","publisher":"Silver Quill Press","source":"Internet Archive","title":"The insightful sportsman","URL":"http://archive.org/details/insightfulsports00will","author":[{"family":"Williams","given":"Ted"}],"contributor":[{"literal":"Internet Archive"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The occurrence of hunting around strict preservation areas such as national parks was accepted in terms of its ability to prevent the ballooning of wild species populations through the source sink relationship which occur between the hunting areas and non hunting areas. In addition, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"u1HyRHtM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Allendorf & Hard, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Allendorf & Hard, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30580,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E96T6IEQ"],"itemData":{"id":30580,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Human harvest of phenotypically desirable animals from wild populations imposes selection that can reduce the frequencies of those desirable phenotypes. Hunting and fishing contrast with agricultural and aquacultural practices in which the most desirable animals are typically bred with the specific goal of increasing the frequency of desirable phenotypes. We consider the potential effects of harvest on the genetics and sustainability of wild populations. We also consider how harvesting could affect the mating system and thereby modify sexual selection in a way that might affect recruitment. Determining whether phenotypic changes in harvested populations are due to evolution, rather than phenotypic plasticity or environmental variation, has been problematic. Nevertheless, it is likely that some undesirable changes observed over time in exploited populations (e.g., reduced body size, earlier sexual maturity, reduced antler size, etc.) are due to selection against desirable phenotypes—a process we call “unnatural” selection. Evolution brought about by human harvest might greatly increase the time required for over-harvested populations to recover once harvest is curtailed because harvesting often creates strong selection differentials, whereas curtailing harvest will often result in less intense selection in the opposing direction. We strongly encourage those responsible for managing harvested wild populations to take into account possible selective effects of harvest management and to implement monitoring programs to detect exploitation-induced selection before it seriously impacts viability.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.0901069106","ISSN":"0027-8424","issue":"Supplement 1","page":"9987-9994","title":"Human-induced evolution caused by unnatural selection through harvest of wild animals","URL":"https://www.pnas.org/content/106/Supplement_1/9987","volume":"106","author":[{"family":"Allendorf","given":"Fred W."},{"family":"Hard","given":"Jeffrey J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} also highlighted that the targeting of older animals past prime breeding age during hunting contributes to reducing pressure on resources leading to sustainable wild species habitats. The importance of hunting in controlling the population of animals such as elephants which may have significant undesirable impacts on habitats when their populations continuously grow, was generally accepted. In this regard, hunting was seen to have potential to contribute towards the conservation of several other species. 
 Hunting, economic development and tourism
The call for wild species to pay for their existence was present in early conservation narratives ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"usulmEWS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eltringham, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Eltringham, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30570,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L275VXRW"],"itemData":{"id":30570,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605300028519","issue":"3","page":"163-168","title":"Can wildlife pay its way","URL":"https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/48e3/20d550f3e09dd0a74aa4d3e040ebfc6c8dea.pdf","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Eltringham","given":"S.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Trophy hunting was presented as a wild species-based enterprise generating significant revenues for stakeholders and national economies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5yj6Z4kL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lindsey, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Lindsey, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30669,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDAFNMZ8"],"itemData":{"id":30669,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"There is a lack of consensus among conservationists as to whether trophy hunting represents a legitimate conservation tool in Africa. Hunting advocates stress that trophy hunting can create incentives for conservation where ecotourism is not possible. We assessed the hunting preferences of hunting clients who have hunted or plan to hunt in Africa (n=150), and the perception among African hunting operators (n=127) of client preferences at two US hunting conventions to determine whether this assertion is justified. Clients are most interested in hunting in well-known East and southern African hunting destinations, but some trophy species attract hunters to remote and unstable countries that might not otherwise derive revenues from hunting. Clients are willing to hunt in areas lacking high densities of wildlife or attractive scenery, and where people and livestock occur, stressing the potential for trophy hunting to generate revenues where ecotourism may not be viable. Hunting clients are more averse to hunting under conditions whereby conservation objectives are compromised than operators realize, suggesting that client preferences could potentially drive positive change in the hunting industry, to the benefit of conservation. However, the preferences and attitudes of some clients likely form the basis of some of the problems currently associated with the hunting industry in Africa, stressing the need for an effective regulatory framework.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","ISSN":"1469-1795","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","page":"283-291","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Potential of trophy hunting to create incentives for wildlife conservation in Africa where alternative wildlife-based land uses may not be viable","URL":"https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"P. A."},{"family":"Alexander","given":"R."},{"family":"Frank","given":"L. G."},{"family":"Mathieson","given":"A."},{"family":"Romañach","given":"S. S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Trophy hunting was viewed as an important foreign currency generating venture, contributing significantly to tourism revenues and gross domestic product for nations, and creating incentives for conservation where nature-based tourism was not viable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mFqNNzlE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Freeman, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Freeman, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30529,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4567SLJL"],"itemData":{"id":30529,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Alberta","publisher":"Occasional Publications Series, The University of Alberta Press","title":"Conservation hunting: People and wildlife in Canada’s North","author":[{"family":"Freeman","given":"M.M."},{"family":"Hudson","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Foote","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hunting was presented as a lucrative wild species business with the potential to generate extraordinarily high revenues with minimal off take of individual game animals. Hunting tourism was considered to present opportunities to develop into an economic and social force of considerable impact in remote rural and agriculturally marginal areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CmVIbKJA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2006)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1318,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DAANTGGC"],"itemData":{"id":1318,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"IUCN-ESUSG WISPER","title":"Guidelines on Sustainable Hunting in Europe","URL":"https://www.fundacioncazasostenible.org/app/download/5795770411/Guidelines+on+Sustainable+Hunting+in+Europe.pdf","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"editor":[{"family":"Casaer","given":"J."},{"family":"Kenward","given":"R."},{"family":"Lecocq","given":"Y."},{"family":"Reimoser","given":"F."},{"family":"Sharp","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, tourism in remote and peripheral areas can be rather volatile because it depends heavily on transportation and accessibility. The literature generally supported viewing hunting tourism as a tool to diversify local economies, but not a replacement for other sources of income ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"g99YFFP4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hall & Boyd, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Hall & Boyd, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30701,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BD65MVRC"],"itemData":{"id":30701,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Channel View Publications","title":"Nature-based tourism in peripheral areas: Development or disaster?","volume":"21","editor":[{"family":"Hall","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Boyd","given":"S.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition, many papers documented that hunting activity can be a useful mechanism for financing preservation of natural ecosystems, in a context of wise use in line with sustainable use principles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AVL29w44","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Foote & Wenzel, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Foote & Wenzel, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30700,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NPW47HB5"],"itemData":{"id":30700,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Tourism and the Consumption of Wildlife","language":"en","page":"137–150","publisher":"Routledge","title":"Conservation hunting concepts, Canada’s Inuit, and polar bear hunting","author":[{"family":"Foote","given":"L."},{"family":"Wenzel","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
Community-based conservation, incentives and hunting
The role of hunting in community development and poverty alleviation was discussed by a number of authors as a key benefit of trophy hunting, creating incentives for conservation among rural communities. Contributions of hunting towards community development were reported in African countries such as Tanzania and Zimbabwe, where it formed the backbone of community based natural resource management programs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"95vvgHu2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lindsey, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Lindsey, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30669,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDAFNMZ8"],"itemData":{"id":30669,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"There is a lack of consensus among conservationists as to whether trophy hunting represents a legitimate conservation tool in Africa. Hunting advocates stress that trophy hunting can create incentives for conservation where ecotourism is not possible. We assessed the hunting preferences of hunting clients who have hunted or plan to hunt in Africa (n=150), and the perception among African hunting operators (n=127) of client preferences at two US hunting conventions to determine whether this assertion is justified. Clients are most interested in hunting in well-known East and southern African hunting destinations, but some trophy species attract hunters to remote and unstable countries that might not otherwise derive revenues from hunting. Clients are willing to hunt in areas lacking high densities of wildlife or attractive scenery, and where people and livestock occur, stressing the potential for trophy hunting to generate revenues where ecotourism may not be viable. Hunting clients are more averse to hunting under conditions whereby conservation objectives are compromised than operators realize, suggesting that client preferences could potentially drive positive change in the hunting industry, to the benefit of conservation. However, the preferences and attitudes of some clients likely form the basis of some of the problems currently associated with the hunting industry in Africa, stressing the need for an effective regulatory framework.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","ISSN":"1469-1795","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","page":"283-291","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Potential of trophy hunting to create incentives for wildlife conservation in Africa where alternative wildlife-based land uses may not be viable","URL":"https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"P. A."},{"family":"Alexander","given":"R."},{"family":"Frank","given":"L. G."},{"family":"Mathieson","given":"A."},{"family":"Romañach","given":"S. S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. By contributing towards community development, hunting formed an important feature of models for sustainable wild species management linking trophy hunting, wild species conservation and community sustainability in rural areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"d5iIZR8L","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Freeman, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Freeman, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30529,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4567SLJL"],"itemData":{"id":30529,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Alberta","publisher":"Occasional Publications Series, The University of Alberta Press","title":"Conservation hunting: People and wildlife in Canada’s North","author":[{"family":"Freeman","given":"M.M."},{"family":"Hudson","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Foote","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The social and economic incentives from hunting promoted meaningful involvement of indigenous and local communities in wild species conservation, via incentives created by sustainable use for rural populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rOwxJRKl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nilsen & Solberg, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Nilsen & Solberg, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30563,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LWF5ALP2"],"itemData":{"id":30563,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"We used a simple life table approach to examine the age-specific patterns of harvest mortality in eight Norwegian moose populations during the last 15 years and tried to determine if the observed patterns were caused by hunter selectivity. The general opinion among local managers is that hunters prefer to shoot female moose not in company with calves to keep a high number of reproductive females in the population (and because of the emotional stress involved in leaving the calf/calves without a mother), and relatively large males because of the higher return with respect to meat and trophy. In support of the former view, we found the harvest mortality of adult females to be higher among pre-prime (1–3 years old) than prime-aged age classes (4–7 years old). This is probably because prime-aged females are more fecund and, therefore, more likely to be in company with one or two calves during the hunting season. As the season progressed, however, the selection pressure on barren females decreased, probably due to more productive females becoming ‘legal’ prey as their calf/calves were harvested. In males, we did not find any evidence of strong age-specific hunter selectivity, despite strong age-dependent variation in body mass and antler size. We suggest that this was due to the current strongly female-biased sex ratio in most Norwegian moose populations, which leaves the hunters with few opportunities to be selective within a relatively short and intensive hunting season. The management implications of these findings and to what extent the results are likely to affect the future evolution of life histories in Norwegian moose populations are discussed.","container-title":"European Journal of Wildlife Research","page":"153-163","title":"Patterns of hunting mortality in Norwegian moose ( Alces alces ) populations | SpringerLink","URL":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10344-005-0023-1","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Nilsen","given":"Erlend B."},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The social and economic benefits of trophy hunting also were acknowledged as cornerstones for incentive driven conservation practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CxSDpk2M","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hutton & Leader-Williams, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Hutton & Leader-Williams, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32738,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RQIIYJ5P"],"itemData":{"id":32738,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Discussions of sustainable use have become polarized. Welfarists oppose all use that involves killing animals. Among conservationists polarization arises in part from failure to distinguish between different ideas nestled under the umbrella term of ‘sustainable use’. These include direct use as an imperative or choice, the ideal of keeping any use within biologically sustainable limits, and use as a possible conservation strategy that can create positive incentives, which are key where land could otherwise be converted to biodiversity-unfriendly practices. People will continue to use wild living resources, which increasing human populations could further deplete. In response the conservation community can follow one of two approaches. On the one hand, it can try to stop use through the establishment of strictly protected areas and by enforcing legislation, although many would question the ethical position of imposing such an approach. On the other hand, it can work to introduce the wider management systems needed to deliver sustainable use and, if possible, incentive-driven conservation. Because most rural populations will continue using wild living resources in human-dominated landscapes, sustainable use and incentive-driven conservation should both be at the centre of the conservation agenda this century. Both species- and ecosystem-based management are likely to have a role in sustainable use. However, current enthusiasm for the ecosystem approach may throw up unexpected consequences, making the search for sustainability even more polarized. Nevertheless, direct use of species cannot provide sufficient incentives to ensure the continued delivery of ecosystem services, which need to be fully incorporated in the global accounting system.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605303000395","ISSN":"1365-3008, 0030-6053","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"215-226","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Sustainable use and incentive-driven conservation: realigning human and conservation interests","title-short":"Sustainable use and incentive-driven conservation","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/sustainable-use-and-incentivedriven-conservation-realigning-human-and-conservation-interests/5C8B0923D18E8FFBB39FA7DFA70F0B72","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Hutton","given":"Jon M."},{"family":"Leader-Williams","given":"Nigel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
Hunting, ethics and rights in sustainable use
By 2010, the previously asserted conservation values of hunting were deeply contested. Polarized debates emerged as conservationists differed in opinion as to whether trophy hunting is an ethically legitimate conservation tool ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MzTcP7ET","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lindsey, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Lindsey, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30669,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDAFNMZ8"],"itemData":{"id":30669,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"There is a lack of consensus among conservationists as to whether trophy hunting represents a legitimate conservation tool in Africa. Hunting advocates stress that trophy hunting can create incentives for conservation where ecotourism is not possible. We assessed the hunting preferences of hunting clients who have hunted or plan to hunt in Africa (n=150), and the perception among African hunting operators (n=127) of client preferences at two US hunting conventions to determine whether this assertion is justified. Clients are most interested in hunting in well-known East and southern African hunting destinations, but some trophy species attract hunters to remote and unstable countries that might not otherwise derive revenues from hunting. Clients are willing to hunt in areas lacking high densities of wildlife or attractive scenery, and where people and livestock occur, stressing the potential for trophy hunting to generate revenues where ecotourism may not be viable. Hunting clients are more averse to hunting under conditions whereby conservation objectives are compromised than operators realize, suggesting that client preferences could potentially drive positive change in the hunting industry, to the benefit of conservation. However, the preferences and attitudes of some clients likely form the basis of some of the problems currently associated with the hunting industry in Africa, stressing the need for an effective regulatory framework.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","ISSN":"1469-1795","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","page":"283-291","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Potential of trophy hunting to create incentives for wildlife conservation in Africa where alternative wildlife-based land uses may not be viable","URL":"https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00034.x","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"P. A."},{"family":"Alexander","given":"R."},{"family":"Frank","given":"L. G."},{"family":"Mathieson","given":"A."},{"family":"Romañach","given":"S. S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other polarized discussions hinged on whether strict protection strategies based on exclusion of extractive methods are sustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SN4Rfh0Z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Council of Europe, 2007; Kaltenborn, 2005; Kiringe, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Council of Europe, 2007; Kaltenborn, 2005; Kiringe, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30681,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/32XWV744"],"itemData":{"id":30681,"type":"document","language":"en","publisher":"Council of Europe Publishing","title":"European charter on hunting and biodiversity","URL":"https://esug.sycl.net/file_link/00064/Hunting_Charter_EN_636907530727699967.pdf","author":[{"family":"Council of Europe","given":"S.M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":30575,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EQDBM7M2"],"itemData":{"id":30575,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"European Journal of Wildlife Research","page":"213-222","title":"The nature of hunting around the Western Corridor of Serengeti National Park, Tanzania | SpringerLink","URL":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10344-005-0109-9","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Kaltenborn","given":"Bjørn P."},{"family":"Nyahongo","given":"Julius W."},{"family":"Tingstad","given":"Margrethe K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":30707,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DECSSEWJ"],"itemData":{"id":30707,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","language":"en","page":"314","title":"Managers’ perceptions of threats to the protected areas of Kenya: prioritization for effective management","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Kiringe","given":"J.W."},{"family":"Okello","given":"M.M."},{"family":"Ekajul","given":"S.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It was also argued that the sustainability of hunting is susceptible to abuse and malpractices, with hunting tourism inherently vulnerable to corruption, fraud, overshooting of best practices in quotas, bad management, and loss of wild species numbers and biodiversity. It was argued that community benefit from hunting revenues in community-based natural resource management programs where hunting is listed as a key use strategy, was also grossly affected by these misgovernance issues ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"htASJhqJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Balint & Mashinya, 2008; F. A. Johnson, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Balint & Mashinya, 2008; F. A. Johnson, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30687,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/INZQ6KY6"],"itemData":{"id":30687,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society and Natural Resources","issue":"9","language":"en","page":"783–796","title":"CAMPFIRE during Zimbabwe's national crisis: Local impacts and broader implications for community-based wildlife management","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Balint","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Mashinya","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":30568,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KKDM2GCZ"],"itemData":{"id":30568,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Those charged with regulating waterfowl harvests must cope with random environmental variations, incomplete control over harvest rates, and uncertainty about biological mechanisms operative in the population. Stochastic dynamic programming can be used effectively to account for these uncertainties if the probabilities associated with uncertain outcomes can be estimated. To use this approach managers must have clearly-stated objectives, a set of regulatory options, and a mathematical description of the managed system. We used dynamic programming to derive optimal harvest strategies for mallards (Anas platyrhynchos) in which we balanced the competing objectives of maximizing long-term cumulative harvest and achieving a specified population goal. Model-specific harvest strategies, which account for random variation in wetland conditions on the breeding grounds and for uncertainty about the relation between hunting regulations and harvest rates, are provided and compared. We also account for uncertainty in population dynamics with model probabilities, which express the relative confidence that alternative models adequately describe population responses to harvest and environmental conditions. Finally, we demonstrate how the harvest strategy thus derived can \\"evolve\\" as model probabilities are updated periodically using comparisons of model predictions and estimates of population size.","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","DOI":"10.2307/3802429","ISSN":"0022-541X","issue":"1","page":"202-216","source":"JSTOR","title":"Uncertainty and the Management of Mallard Harvests","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/3802429","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Johnson","given":"Fred A."},{"family":"Moore","given":"Clinton T."},{"family":"Kendall","given":"William L."},{"family":"Dubovsky","given":"James A."},{"family":"Caithamer","given":"David F."},{"family":"Kelley","given":"James R."},{"family":"Williams","given":"Byron K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
Hunting, trade and sustainability
A shift in the narratives about hunting and wild species trade reframed them as threatening the conservation of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"af0zSO7J","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Darimont, 2009; Zapata-R\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}os, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Darimont, 2009; Zapata-Ríos, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30576,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SKVG5MRN"],"itemData":{"id":30576,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The observable traits of wild populations are continually shaped and reshaped by the environment and numerous agents of natural selection, including predators. In stark contrast with most predators, humans now typically exploit high proportions of prey populations and target large, reproductive-aged adults. Consequently, organisms subject to consistent and strong ‘harvest selection’ by fishers, hunters, and plant harvesters may be expected to show particularly rapid and dramatic changes in phenotype. However, a comparison of the rate at which phenotypic changes in exploited taxa occurs relative to other systems has never been undertaken. Here, we show that average phenotypic changes in 40 human-harvested systems are much more rapid than changes reported in studies examining not only natural (n = 20 systems) but also other human-driven (n = 25 systems) perturbations in the wild, outpacing them by &gt;300% and 50%, respectively. Accordingly, harvested organisms show some of the most abrupt trait changes ever observed in wild populations, providing a new appreciation for how fast phenotypes are capable of changing. These changes, which include average declines of almost 20% in size-related traits and shifts in life history traits of nearly 25%, are most rapid in commercially exploited systems and, thus, have profound conservation and economic implications. Specifically, the widespread potential for transitively rapid and large effects on size- or life history-mediated ecological dynamics might imperil populations, industries, and ecosystems.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.0809235106","ISSN":"0027-8424","issue":"3","page":"952-954","title":"Human predators outpace other agents of trait change in the wild","URL":"https://www.pnas.org/content/106/3/952","volume":"106","author":[{"family":"Darimont","given":"Chris T."},{"family":"Carlson","given":"Stephanie M."},{"family":"Kinnison","given":"Michael T."},{"family":"Paquet","given":"Paul C."},{"family":"Reimchen","given":"Thomas E."},{"family":"Wilmers","given":"Christopher C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":33151,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9TXBSJMB"],"itemData":{"id":33151,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Although hunting is still critical to the subsistence of many people throughout Amazonia, this practice may not be sustainable under current socio-economic conditions. Native societies are rapidly undergoing socio-economic changes that exacerbate the pressure on wildlife and habitats, indicating the urgent need to assess the impacts of subsistence hunting. In a 12-month study we assessed hunting patterns in four Shuar native communities in the Ecuadorian Amazon. Hunting patterns and impact of hunting activities were documented using interviews, direct observations, self-monitoring records, community landscape mapping and mammal surveys. Although Shuar harvest a wide-range of wildlife species, including insects, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals we only report information about mammals. A total of 3,181 individuals (c. 26,000 kg) of 21 mammal species were hunted during the 12 months. We used three algorithms for assessing the sustainability of hunting: the production, stock-recruitment and harvest models. Of the 21 mammal species hunted there were sufficient data to assess 15, 12 of which were hunted above maximum sustainable levels within the 243 km2 hunting catchment area. The immediate need to conserve wildlife populations is not obvious to Shuar hunters who still enjoy what they perceive to be an inexhaustible source of wild meat. In this context management of Shuar hunting practices to control harvest levels is complex. The assessment presented here is the first step of what needs to be a long-term wildlife management process.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605309001914","ISSN":"1365-3008, 0030-6053","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"375-385","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Mammal hunting by the Shuar of the Ecuadorian Amazon: is it sustainable?","title-short":"Mammal hunting by the Shuar of the Ecuadorian Amazon","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/mammal-hunting-by-the-shuar-of-the-ecuadorian-amazon-is-it-sustainable/CB07BB9761FBBF09792EEB45D485C0D3","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Zapata-Ríos","given":"Galo"},{"family":"Urgilés","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Suárez","given":"Esteban"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The limitations of monitoring and control on wild species trade were highlighted as among the reasons commercial trade of wild species could be regarded as unsustainable. The contemporary and prehistoric extinction of thousands of wild species was attributed to hunting, including prominent species such as the quagga, woolly mammoth, sabre toothed cat and West African black rhinoceros. Populations of amphibian species have also declined as a result of collection and trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"64R8s6AG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Halliday, 2001; Kuzmin, 1996)","plainCitation":"(Halliday, 2001; Kuzmin, 1996)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30588,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WJYGII33"],"itemData":{"id":30588,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1046/j.1365-3008.2001.0188a.x","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Oryx","language":"en","page":"181-182","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The wider implications of amphibian population declines","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0030605300031811/type/journal_article","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Halliday","given":"Tim"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001",7]]}}},{"id":30565,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N4BG7UHB"],"itemData":{"id":30565,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Before the dissolution of the USSR in 1991, research and conservation of the region's amphibian fauna was co-ordsinated by a network of scientists. The use of similar survey methods made it possible to obtain comparable estimates of amphibian populations in different parts of the territory. Since 1991 the system of research and conservation has broken down as a result of political changes and economic crisis. The scientific community has become fragmented and there is a scarcity of funds available for essential research and conservation. There is an urgent need for the adoption and implementation of conservation measures, both within the territory of the former USSR and elsewhere, in order to prevent further declines in threatened amphibian populations. This paper presents a summary of the status of amphibians in the former USSR, the threats they face and recommended measures to protect them.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605300021359","ISSN":"1365-3008, 0030-6053","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"24-30","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Threatened amphibians in the former Soviet Union: the current situation and the main threats","title-short":"Threatened amphibians in the former Soviet Union","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/threatened-amphibians-in-the-former-soviet-union-the-current-situation-and-the-main-threats/CBAF664FFBE7905C6430FCE7332C6612","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Kuzmin","given":"Sergius L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Economic incentives such as the establishment of quotas without a scientific basis were observed to lead to unsustainable utilization patterns ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cGiYI7Wx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zhang, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Zhang, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30550,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DKHTCJY5"],"itemData":{"id":30550,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","page":"1493-1516","title":"Wildlife trade, consumption and conservation awareness in southwest China | SpringerLink","URL":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10531-008-9358-8","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Zhang","given":"Li"},{"family":"Hua","given":"Ning"},{"family":"Sun","given":"Shan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The motives for quota setting in trophy hunting were argued to be dominantly political and economic at the expense of conservation, with far-reaching consequences on the sustainable use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ibQuvWDl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rodrigues, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Rodrigues, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30515,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5VFTTEJI"],"itemData":{"id":30515,"type":"book","event-place":"Harare, Zimbabwe","language":"en","publisher":"Zimbabwe Conservation Task Force","publisher-place":"Harare, Zimbabwe","title":"The Myth of Trophy Hunting as Conservation","author":[{"family":"Rodrigues","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Persistence of wild species markets was also cited as a major hindrance to efforts to stop poaching ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fX0uYwk0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Darimont, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Darimont, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30576,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SKVG5MRN"],"itemData":{"id":30576,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The observable traits of wild populations are continually shaped and reshaped by the environment and numerous agents of natural selection, including predators. In stark contrast with most predators, humans now typically exploit high proportions of prey populations and target large, reproductive-aged adults. Consequently, organisms subject to consistent and strong ‘harvest selection’ by fishers, hunters, and plant harvesters may be expected to show particularly rapid and dramatic changes in phenotype. However, a comparison of the rate at which phenotypic changes in exploited taxa occurs relative to other systems has never been undertaken. Here, we show that average phenotypic changes in 40 human-harvested systems are much more rapid than changes reported in studies examining not only natural (n = 20 systems) but also other human-driven (n = 25 systems) perturbations in the wild, outpacing them by &gt;300% and 50%, respectively. Accordingly, harvested organisms show some of the most abrupt trait changes ever observed in wild populations, providing a new appreciation for how fast phenotypes are capable of changing. These changes, which include average declines of almost 20% in size-related traits and shifts in life history traits of nearly 25%, are most rapid in commercially exploited systems and, thus, have profound conservation and economic implications. Specifically, the widespread potential for transitively rapid and large effects on size- or life history-mediated ecological dynamics might imperil populations, industries, and ecosystems.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.0809235106","ISSN":"0027-8424","issue":"3","page":"952-954","title":"Human predators outpace other agents of trait change in the wild","URL":"https://www.pnas.org/content/106/3/952","volume":"106","author":[{"family":"Darimont","given":"Chris T."},{"family":"Carlson","given":"Stephanie M."},{"family":"Kinnison","given":"Michael T."},{"family":"Paquet","given":"Paul C."},{"family":"Reimchen","given":"Thomas E."},{"family":"Wilmers","given":"Christopher C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other negative impacts of commercial wild species trade such as the spread of invasive species and zoonotic diseases as a result of live animal sales were also highlighted by some authors (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zRXsuFbz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Smith, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Smith, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30566,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AE87LS3F"],"itemData":{"id":30566,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1174460","issue":"5927","page":"594-595","title":"Reducing the Risks of the Wildlife Trade | Science","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/324/5927/594","volume":"324","author":[{"family":"Smith","given":"Katherine F."},{"family":"Behrens","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Schloegel","given":"Lisa M."},{"family":"Marano","given":"Nina"},{"family":"Burgiel","given":"Stas"},{"family":"Daszak","given":"Peter"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Several authors suggested that there was need for conservationists and policy makers to find ways to reduce the magnitude of international wild species trade in order to save species from extinction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uhLqUYkg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. F. Smith, 2009; Vercauteren & Hygnstrom, 1998)","plainCitation":"(K. F. Smith, 2009; Vercauteren & Hygnstrom, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30566,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AE87LS3F"],"itemData":{"id":30566,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1174460","issue":"5927","page":"594-595","title":"Reducing the Risks of the Wildlife Trade | Science","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/324/5927/594","volume":"324","author":[{"family":"Smith","given":"Katherine F."},{"family":"Behrens","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Schloegel","given":"Lisa M."},{"family":"Marano","given":"Nina"},{"family":"Burgiel","given":"Stas"},{"family":"Daszak","given":"Peter"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":30557,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JBBHEXBR"],"itemData":{"id":30557,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Insight into the effects of agricultural activities and hunting on deer could be used to facilitate management. We examined home range characteristics of female white-tailed deer (Odocoileus virginianus) relative to corn development, corn harvest, and hunting seasons from 1991 to 1993. Among 30 radiomarked does, 53% (n = 16) remained residents of DeSoto National Wildlife Refuge (DNWR) throughout the study; 47% (n = 14) were transients. Among the transients, 30% (n = 9) permanently emigrated from DNWR and 17% (n = 5) migrated annually. Dispersal occured in April and May. Annual home ranges of resident does averaged 170 ha (CI = 38, n = 14). Home range centers shifted an average of 174 m (CI = 74, n = 14) closer to cornfields when corn was in the tasseling-silking stage of development. After corn harvest, home range centers shifted 157 m (CI = 63, n = 12) away from crop fields and into areas of permanent cover. Home range sizes increased 32% (9-64% CI) after corn harvest because does were forced to find and use other sources of cover and food. Resident does that caused local crop damage during the growing season were available for hunter harvest in the same areas where they caused damage. Migrators appeared especially vulnerable because they were exposed to state firearm and archery seasons and the DNWR 3-day muzzleloader hunt. Of all tagged deer, 20% of transients (3 of 15) were harvested legally, whereas 40% of residents (8 of 20) were harvested. Harvest or removal to reduce crop damage around large areas of permanent cover should be conducted in early to mid-fall, which can reduce deer densities in the immediate area but not impose additional hunting pressure on migratory deer.","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","DOI":"10.2307/3802289","ISSN":"0022-541X","issue":"1","page":"280-285","source":"JSTOR","title":"Effects of Agricultural Activities and Hunting on Home Ranges of Female White-Tailed Deer","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/3802289","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Vercauteren","given":"Kurt C."},{"family":"Hygnstrom","given":"Scott E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Approaches that were proposed to address the issue of wild species trade include awareness among governments to take proactive measures to address the impacts and risks of wild species trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4LJkDfU6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nijman, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Nijman, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32739,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMIG93TC"],"itemData":{"id":32739,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife trade is the very heart of biodiversity conservation and sustainable development providing an income for some of the least economically affluent people and it generates considerable revenue nationally. In Asia the unsustainable trade in wildlife has been identified as one of the main conservation challenges. Internationally, wildlife trade is regulated through the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of wild fauna and flora (CITES) to which all Southeast Asian nations are signatory. I obtained data on international trade in CITES-listed animals in the period 1998-2007. In all \\\\textgreater 35 million animals (0.3 million butterflies; 16.0 million seahorses; 0.1 million other fish; 17.4 million reptiles; 0.4 million mammals; 1.0 million birds) were exported in this period, 30 million (similar to 300 species) of them being wild-caught. In addition 18 million pieces and 2 million kg of live corals were exported. Malaysia, Vietnam, Indonesia and China are the major exporters of wild-caught animals and the European Union and Japan are the most significant importers. Over this period exports in birds significantly decreased, trade in the other taxa either increased or remained stable. For all taxa but butterflies the vast majority of individuals represent wild-caught individuals. Records of illegal or undeclared international trade are scant but can be significantly larger than levels of official exports. It is concluded that there is an urgent need for better assessments of what levels of exploitation are sustainable (including exploring appropriate proxies for Non Detriment Findings), for initiatives to make regulatory mechanisms more effective (including the introduction of minimum mandatory standards and monitoring selected wildlife trade hubs), and for better licensing and registration. Funding for at least some of these initiatives can be obtained by imposing small levies on exports of CITES-listed wildlife.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-009-9758-4","ISSN":"09603115","issue":"4","page":"1101–1114","title":"An overview of international wildlife trade from Southeast Asia","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
Effects of hunting on species populations and distribution
Simultaneously, the harvesting or removal of wild species through hunting was observed to have undesirable impacts on populations and the functioning and integrity of some ecosystems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yQF5rMev","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vermeulen, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Vermeulen, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30556,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WPQS7HGH"],"itemData":{"id":30556,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In central Africa, commercial poaching and local village hunting are still two major issues that logging companies must address through the implementation of effective management plans in order to certify their concessions. However, current problems in developing suitable hunting management schemes for dense tropical forests arise from (1) the difficulty associated with setting quotas which take into account indiscriminate local hunting practices (e.g. snare trapping) and the ill-defined modes of resource appropriation by local populations, (2) the difficulty associated with evaluating the effect of illegal hunting, i.e. poaching, and (3) the relative complexity of the main available model. To overcome this, we propose to develop alternative management models where village hunting is planned along the same lines as existing logging operation models, through the implementation of a system of spatio-temporal rotation of hunting areas. In practice, the logging concession, initially divided into annual logging areas, is divided into similar annual hunting areas (AHAs), which are opened to hunting during the year preceding the logging operations. A depletion of the wildlife stock is expected within the annually opened hunting areas, but the model assumes a progressive re-colonization of the depleted AHA in subsequent years from neighbouring ones. In this paper, an empirical model of such a controlled hunting system employing spatio-temporal rotation of hunting areas is tested within a Forestry Management Unit (FMU) covering 47,585 ha in the Dja region, in south-east Cameroon. The model, based on large forest areas, seems particularly well adapted to Cameroon’s dense forests because it fits within the existing legal framework of Community-Managed Zones of Hunting Interest (CMZHI) and is aligned with current logging concession operations. Preliminary results suggest that sustainable hunting can be achieved in the FMU, provided a management scheme of AHAs is strictly enforced through effective stakeholder commitment.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","page":"2705-2718","title":"Community hunting in logging concessions: towards a management model for Cameroon’s dense forests | SpringerLink","URL":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10531-009-9614-6","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Vermeulen","given":"Cédric"},{"family":"Julve","given":"Cecilia"},{"family":"Doucet","given":"Jean-Louis"},{"family":"Monticelli","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hunting, especially wild meat hunting, was often discussed as one of the major contributors to animal species population decline ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cLPTBIe1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brashares, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Brashares, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30578,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LA7BZIVF"],"itemData":{"id":30578,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The multibillion-dollar trade in bushmeat is among the most immediate threats to the persistence of tropical vertebrates, but our understanding of its underlying drivers and effects on human welfare is limited by a lack of empirical data. We used 30 years of data from Ghana to link mammal declines to the bushmeat trade and to spatial and temporal changes in the availability of fish. We show that years of poor fish supply coincided with increased hunting in nature reserves and sharp declines in biomass of 41 wildlife species. Local market data provide evidence of a direct link between fish supply and subsequent bushmeat demand in villages and show bushmeat's role as a dietary staple in the region. Our results emphasize the urgent need to develop cheap protein alternatives to bushmeat and to improve fisheries management by foreign and domestic fleets to avert extinctions of tropical wildlife.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1102425","issue":"5699","journalAbbreviation":"Science","page":"1180","title":"Bushmeat Hunting, Wildlife Declines, and Fish Supply in West Africa","URL":"http://science.sciencemag.org/content/306/5699/1180.abstract","volume":"306","author":[{"family":"Brashares","given":"Justin S."},{"family":"Arcese","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Sam","given":"Moses K."},{"family":"Coppolillo","given":"Peter B."},{"family":"Sinclair","given":"A. R. E."},{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]],"season":"12"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Breeding was argued to be negatively affected as a result of the selection during hunts which harvest males at a faster rate than females ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"krEuUYwU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fischer & Keith, 1974)","plainCitation":"(Fischer & Keith, 1974)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30569,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HTQEG6NV"],"itemData":{"id":30569,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"[During 1966-72, 1,132 ruffed grouse (Bonasa umbellus) were banded near Rochester, Alberta. Fifty-five (5 percent) were subsequently reported shot in the year of banding. Hunting along roads accounted for 96 percent of these shot birds. Loss of territorial adult males to hunters decreased significantly with increasing distance from banding location to the nearest road: 48, 13, 5, and 1 percent of those banded < 101, 101-200, 201-301, and ? 302 m, respectively, from the nearest road and surviving to 1 October were reported shot. Rates of hunting mortality also tended to decline among other adult cohorts and among juveniles with increased banding distance from roads. Mean annual survival of territorial adult males with drumming logs < 201 m from roads (0.23) was lower than that of males with logs at ? 201 m (0.36), suggesting that fall hunting increased total annual mortality in this cohort. There was no correlation, however, between ratios of population change from October to May and annual rates of total hunting mortality; it was therefore concluded that fall hunting had no measurable effect on spring population levels. Rates of hunting mortality were not significantly correlated with fall population densities. Forty-one of 67 (61 percent) territorial adult males were experimentally removed from two study areas near Rochester in spring 1971 and 1972 by shooting with a .22 caliber rifle. Midmating and postmating removals were conducted on different study areas. Such removals constituted additive mortality in one year but not in the next. Replacement of removed territorial males averaged 20 percent and was unrelated to population densities. Opportunities for shooting females and disturbing nesting hens were negligible. There was also little apparent effect on brood production.]","archive":"JSTOR","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","DOI":"10.2307/3800025","ISSN":"0022541X, 19372817","issue":"4","page":"585-600","title":"Population Responses of Central Alberta Ruffed Grouse to Hunting","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/3800025","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Fischer","given":"Charles A."},{"family":"Keith","given":"Lloyd B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1974"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Negative impacts of hunting were reported on small mammals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Wc2ABXJV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nixon, 1975)","plainCitation":"(Nixon, 1975)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30525,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H6IPK8L5"],"itemData":{"id":30525,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–25","title":"Effects of hunting and mast crops on a squirrel population","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Nixon","given":"C.M."},{"family":"McClain","given":"M.W."},{"family":"Donohoe","given":"R.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1975"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, amphibian and reptile species such as crocodile and turtle ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HEy0U45G","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(da N\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}brega Alves, 2008; Powell, 2000)","plainCitation":"(da Nóbrega Alves, 2008; Powell, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30577,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SY6M7A9V"],"itemData":{"id":30577,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The present work provides an overview of the global use of reptiles in traditional folk medicine and the implications for conservation. The results demonstrate that at least 165 reptile species belonging to 104 genera and 30 families are used in traditional folk medicine around the world. Some species are used as sources of drugs for modern medical science. Of the reptiles recorded, 53% are included on lists of endangered species, demonstrating the importance of understanding such medicinal uses in the context of reptile conservation as well as the need for considering socio-cultural factors when establishing management plans directed towards the sustainable use of these reptiles.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-007-9305-0","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity and Conservation","page":"2037-2049","title":"Reptiles used in traditional folk medicine: conservation implications","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10531-007-9305-0","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Nóbrega Alves","given":"Rômulo Romeu","non-dropping-particle":"da"},{"family":"Silva Vieira","given":"Washington Luiz","non-dropping-particle":"da"},{"family":"Santana","given":"Gindomar Gomes"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]],"season":"1"}}},{"id":30567,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GAYAURJR"],"itemData":{"id":30567,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Dominican Republic faces multiple threats to biodiversity. A list of native species of amphibians and reptiles (excluding sea turtles) is presented. Some may have become extinct recently, substantial populations of others have been extirpated, some have greatly reduced numbers, and others appear to be rare or have restricted ranges. Most of the 13 taxa listed are relatively large, vulnerable to human exploitation or introduced predators, and/or have limited distributions and specific habitat requirements. To be listed, evidence must exist that: (1) populations are dwindling, (2) the range is shrinking, or (3) a species must be vulnerable to exploitation and historically rare. Two iguanas (Cyclura cornuta, C. ricordii), two turtles (Trachemys decorata, T. stejnegeri vicina), and one crocodilian (Crocodylus acutus) have been exploited extensively and have long been recognized as threatened or endangered. The ranges of Cyclura ricordii and T. decorata are very localized and the previously widespread ranges of the others have shrunk or become fragmented. A toad (Bufo fluviaticus), a large galliwasp (Celestus anelpistus), and a snake (Alsophis melanichnus) have not been collected recently. Only a few specimens of another galliwasp (C. carraui) and a dwarf gecko (Sphaerodactylus cochranae) have been taken recently. In addition, extensive portions of the habitats of these species have been severely altered. Three other snakes (Alsophis anomalus, Ialtris agyrtes, I. dorsalis) are rare and may never have been common. Their size and habits render them vulnerable to predation by the introduced mongoose and to decimation by humans who fear and dislike them.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1046/j.1365-3008.2000.00103.x","issue":"2","page":"118-128","title":"Amphibians and reptiles of the Dominican Republic: species of special concern","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1046/j.1365-3008.2000.00103.x","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Powell","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Ottenwalder","given":"José A."},{"family":"Incháustegui","given":"Sixto J."},{"family":"Henderson","given":"Robert W."},{"family":"Glor","given":"Richard E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and several bird species in the 19th century ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uNiYW9jx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Madsen & Fox, 1995)","plainCitation":"(Madsen & Fox, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30526,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RI8KM8U8"],"itemData":{"id":30526,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Wildlife Biology","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"193–207","title":"Impacts of hunting disturbance on waterbirds-a review","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Madsen","given":"J."},{"family":"Fox","given":"A.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Hunting was also identified as a threat to tropical forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kpfSsFGA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bonaudo, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Bonaudo, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30689,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7X5B5Z76"],"itemData":{"id":30689,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"European Journal of Wildlife Research","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"199–206","title":"The effects of deforestation on wildfire along the transamazon highway","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Bonaudo","given":"T."},{"family":"Le Pendu","given":"Y."},{"family":"Faure","given":"J.F."},{"family":"Quanz","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Citing evidence from Malaysia, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"igdNfWSU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. G. Robinson & Bodmer, 1999)","plainCitation":"(J. G. Robinson & Bodmer, 1999)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30581,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E6PJ354Z"],"itemData":{"id":30581,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Hunting is ubiquitous in tropical forests around the world, and meat from wildlife species is an important source of animal protein for rural populations. Ungulates, primates, and rodents provide most of the biomass consumed, but a wide variety of wildlife species are hunted for both subsistence and commerce. Across the tropics, tens of millions of animals and millions of metric tons of meat are hunted and consumed each year. Is this harvest sustainable? Available information and the use of simple sustainability models suggest it is not. The supply of wildlife is limited, as annual production of large mammals in tropical forests is low when compared to other ecosystems. The demand is increasing as tropical forests become more accessible to hunters, effective human population densities increase, people become more sedentary, traditional hunting practices change, the meat trade becomes more commercial, and demand increases for wild meat from urban centers. If wildlife populations are to be sustained, then management institutions, be they government or community-based, need to be strengthened.","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","DOI":"10.2307/3802482","ISSN":"0022-541X","issue":"1","note":"publisher: [Wiley, Wildlife Society]","page":"1-13","source":"JSTOR","title":"Towards Wildlife Management in Tropical Forests","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/3802482","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"John G."},{"family":"Bodmer","given":"Richard E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} argued that hunting could lead to the loss of wild species that are pivotal in the maintenance of ecosystem processes such as pollination and seed dispersal. Forms of hunting such as trophy hunting were argued to negatively impact species due to lack of proper research and science-based decisions, which create an opportunity for unsustainable harvests and threatens wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"F8sStHFT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Salvatori, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Salvatori, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30712,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8BUCBARM"],"itemData":{"id":30712,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Wildlife Biology","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"3–10","title":"Hunting legislation in the Carpathian Mountains: implications for the conservation and management of large carnivores","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Salvatori","given":"V."},{"family":"Okarma","given":"H."},{"family":"Ionescu","given":"O."},{"family":"Dovhanych","given":"Y."},{"family":"Boitani","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In contrast, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2HhqOGzF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stork, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Stork, 2007)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30551,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VHN2E85B"],"itemData":{"id":30551,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"When it comes to understanding patterns of biodiversity, ours is a little-known planet. Large-scale sampling projects, as carried out in two investigations of insect diversity, show a way forward.","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/448657a","ISSN":"1476-4687","issue":"7154","language":"en","page":"657-658","source":"www.nature.com","title":"World of insects","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/448657a","volume":"448","author":[{"family":"Stork","given":"Nigel E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} described the importance of hunting as a habitat protection tool, which benefits tree dwelling insects and leads to stable insect populations. Most studies which had been conducted on coastal and wetland areas showed that hunting activities can greatly affect bird behavior and distribution as birds move to safer zones and alter known breeding, roosting or wintering sites ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Jg8lAfPM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barri, 2008; Pack, 1999; J. G. Robinson & Redford, 1994; Small, 1991)","plainCitation":"(Barri, 2008; Pack, 1999; J. G. Robinson & Redford, 1994; Small, 1991)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30554,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RC6LW44K"],"itemData":{"id":30554,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wild lesser rheas were studied during two reproductive seasons (2004–2005 and 2005–2006) in three adjacent ranches in Argentine Patagonia. These ranches differed in hunting pressure and egg harvest (protected, moderate and intensive use of the species), and livestock grazing conditions (with and without overgrazing). Density of individuals and nests, clutch size, brood size, hatching success, chick survival and recruitment rate were estimated in each area. At the ranch with intense hunting pressure, egg harvesting and livestock grazing, density of lesser rheas was the lowest and reproductive events were not recorded. None of the recorded demographic parameters differed significantly between the ranch where the species is protected and there is low livestock grazing intensity and the ranch where there is moderate use of the species and intensive grazing. Our work suggests that intense hunting and egg harvesting would have stronger effects than overgrazing on the density and reproductive success of wild lesser rhea populations in Patagonia.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605307000798","ISSN":"1365-3008","issue":"4","page":"607-610","title":"Effects of hunting, egg harvest and livestock grazing intensities on density and reproductive success of lesser rhea Rhea pennata pennata in Patagonia: implications for conservation","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/article/effects-of-hunting-egg-harvest-and-livestock-grazing-intensities-on-density-and-reproductive-success-of-lesser-rhea-rhea-pennata-pennata-in-patagonia-implications-for-conservation/A7F0C8A7416EB2ACED40105E810CCC15","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Barri","given":"Fernando R."},{"family":"Martella","given":"Mónica B."},{"family":"Navarro","given":"Joaquín L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":30562,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I84JQGD5"],"itemData":{"id":30562,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The effect of fall either-sex hunting on eastern wild turkey (Meleagris gallopavo silvestris) populations is a common concern of wildlife agencies. We examined the effect of fall either-sex hunting on survival of radiotagged female wild turkeys from 1989 to 1994 in Virginia and West Virginia. We tested the hypothesis that survival of female wild turkeys did not differ among areas closed to fall hunting, open to a 4-week fall season, or open to an 8- or 9-week fall hunting season. Mean annual survival rates were higher in the area closed to fall hunting than in regions where fall hunting occurred (P = 0.05). Mean annual survival rates varied within years (P ? 0.05). Differences in annual survival rate among areas were attributed to legal hunting (P ? 0.01). Poaching was a major mortality factor. Juvenile, yearling, and adult hens had similar survival rates in the areas closed to fall hunting and with 4 weeks of fall hunting (P = 0.39), but survival rates of juveniles were lower (P = 0.03) than those of yearling and adult females in the area with an 8-9-week fall hunting season. Harvest rates of female turkeys averaged 4.3% in the 4-week fall hunted area and 12.3% on the 8-9-week fall hunted area. Higher harvests were achieved on the study area opened only to spring hunting than the combined fall-spring harvests on the other study areas. Total harvest was negatively associated with survival (rs = - 0.90, P = 0.04) on the 8-9-week fall hunted area and positively associated with survival (rs = 0.90, P = 0.04) in the area closed to fall hunting. Spring gobbler-only hunting is suggested for maximum growth in a wild turkey population. Guidelines are presented for fall harvest programs.","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","DOI":"10.2307/3802811","ISSN":"0022-541X","issue":"3","page":"964-975","source":"JSTOR","title":"Effects of Fall Hunting on Wild Turkey Populations in Virginia and West Virginia","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/3802811","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Pack","given":"James C."},{"family":"Norman","given":"Gary W."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Curtis I."},{"family":"Steffen","given":"David E."},{"family":"Swanson","given":"David A."},{"family":"Pollock","given":"Kenneth H."},{"family":"Alpizar-Jara","given":"Russell"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":30560,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZV43AYLB"],"itemData":{"id":30560,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wild animals are an important source of protein for the rural peoples living in or near tropical forests. However, the resource can easily be overexploited and game species can become locally depleted, or even extinct. This paper discusses attempts to measure the sustainability of hunting in tropical forests. It examines five indices of sustainability in current use and two models that attempt to model sustainability.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605300028647","ISSN":"1365-3008, 0030-6053","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"249-256","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Measuring the sustainability of hunting in tropical forests","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/measuring-the-sustainability-of-hunting-in-tropical-forests/8D92491128C6A10AF03993FE4E8EFF0C","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"John G."},{"family":"Redford","given":"Kent H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}},{"id":30553,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7TSEXCUX"],"itemData":{"id":30553,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"We monitored 381 radio-marked ruffed grouse (Bonasa umbellus) on public and private wood-lands in central Wisconsin during 1982-88 to determine seasonal mortality rates due to predation and hunting. A total of 222 known mortalities were recorded: 159 from predation and 63 from hunting. Adult survival rates gradually decreased from 0.85 in summer to 0.57 in winter, then increased in spring to 0.73. Juvenile survival was also highest in summer (0.61), but remained relatively low through all seasons. Hunting mortality rates on public hunting areas were significantly (P < 0.005) higher than on private lands for both adults (0.73 vs. 0.13) and juveniles (0.56 vs. 0.09). In contrast, mortality (viz., predation) was similar during the nonhunting season on public and private lands for both adults (0.44 vs. 0.44) and juveniles (0.80 vs. 0.77, respectively), suggesting that hunting mortality was at least partially, if not completely, additive to natural mortality. Immigration from private lands sustained grouse numbers on public lands because overall population densities gradually increased from 5.8 to 7.8 drumming males per 100 ha during the 6-year study. We conclude that ruffed grouse numbers will be substantially reduced in areas with high hunting mortality and reduced immigration due to fragmented habitat.","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","DOI":"10.2307/3808983","ISSN":"0022-541X","issue":"3","page":"512-520","source":"JSTOR","title":"Predation and Hunting Mortality of Ruffed Grouse in Central Wisconsin","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/3808983","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Small","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Holzwart","given":"James C."},{"family":"Rusch","given":"Donald H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9QyeTHYX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Casas, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Casas, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30670,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7RCP77D7"],"itemData":{"id":30670,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract Hunting is one of the human activities that directly affect wildlife and has received increasing attention given its socioeconomic dimensions. Most studies have been conducted on coastal and wetland areas and showed that hunting activity can greatly affect bird behaviour and distribution. Hunting-free reserves for game species are zones where birds find an area of reduced disturbance. We evaluated the effect of hunting activities on the behaviour and use of hunting-free areas of lapwings Vanellus vanellus, golden plovers Pluvialis apricaria and little bustards Tetrax tetrax in agricultural areas. We compared the habitat use and behaviour of birds on days before, during and after hunting took place. All three studied species showed strong behavioural responses to hunting activities. Hunting activity increased flight probability and time spent vigilant (higher on hunting days than just before and after a hunting day), to the detriment of resting. We also found distributional (use of hunting-free reserve) responses to hunting activities, with hunting-free reserves being used more frequently during hunting days. Thus, reserves can mitigate the disturbance caused by hunting activities, benefiting threatened species in agricultural areas. Increasing the size or number of hunting-free areas might be an important management and conservation tool to reduce the impacts of hunting activities.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/j.1469-1795.2009.00259.x","ISSN":"1367-9430","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Animal Conservation","page":"346-354","title":"Effects of hunting on the behaviour and spatial distribution of farmland birds: importance of hunting-free refuges in agricultural areas","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2009.00259.x","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Casas","given":"F."},{"family":"Mougeot","given":"F."},{"family":"Viñuela","given":"J."},{"family":"Bretagnolle","given":"V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]],"season":"1"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} suggested that human predation alters animal behavior as the former come to be recognized as a threat. However, other articles emphasised the benefits of hunting for non-target species. For example, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FMETJ471","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mateo-Tomas & Olea, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Mateo-Tomas & Olea, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30573,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/78AJU8J8"],"itemData":{"id":30573,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"European Journal of Wildlife Research","page":"519-528","title":"When hunting benefits raptors: a case study of game species and vultures | SpringerLink","URL":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10344-009-0341-9","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Mateo-Tomas","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Olea","given":"Pedro P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} highlighted the importance of carcass meat for raptor and other carnivorous bird species success. In addition, the removal of individuals through hunting was argued to favor selection, thus maintaining balance and integrity of the ecosystem ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qH5lbso4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stenseth & Dunlop, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Stenseth & Dunlop, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30552,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5ANT3Z68"],"itemData":{"id":30552,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fishing and hunting by humans are the main causes of mortality in many populations of wild animals. The consequence is that large and rapid changes occur in certain characteristics that far exceed changes due to other agents.","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/457803a","ISSN":"1476-4687","issue":"7231","language":"en","page":"803-804","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Unnatural selection","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/457803a","volume":"457","author":[{"family":"Stenseth","given":"Nils Chr"},{"family":"Dunlop","given":"Erin S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Restrictive hunting regulations were credited with contributing to the stability and increase in survival rates of mallard duck populations in Canada and the United States of America, and goose populations in Europe as well as large scale habitat restoration by hunters ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ihUwhHL5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. W. Smith & Reynolds, 1992)","plainCitation":"(G. W. Smith & Reynolds, 1992)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30519,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E69DKB8N"],"itemData":{"id":30519,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"306–316","title":"Hunting and Mallard Survival, 1979-88","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Smith","given":"G.W."},{"family":"Reynolds","given":"R.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thus, it was argued that populations can thrive under well monitored and effectively managed hunting systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ChDwWWU9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Burnham, 1984; Casas, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Burnham, 1984; Casas, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30571,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4PEK5IZH"],"itemData":{"id":30571,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"[Management programs for waterfowl populations include rationale for, and establishment of, hunting regulations. These programs rest partially on the results of scientific studies on the effect of harvest rates on annual survival rates. The evidence of this relationship has changed markedly since the mid-1970's, and it is now widely believed that a largely compensatory relationship exists between hunting mortality and other forms of mortality for the mallard (Anas platyrhynchos). This paper employs a general probabilistic model formulated to include a parameter (b) representing a continuum between complete compensation (b = 0) and total additivity (b = 1). Maximum likelihood estimates of this parameter were computed for 47 data sets of adult mallards banded throughout North America before hunting commenced. We found additional evidence of a highly compensatory mortality process for adult male mallards, while the evidence for adult female mallards remains inconclusive. Effective harvest, land acquisition, and land management programs depend upon additional information on the chronology and mechanisms underlying a compensatory mortality process.]","archive":"JSTOR","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","DOI":"10.2307/3801166","ISSN":"0022541X, 19372817","issue":"2","page":"350-361","title":"Estimating the Effect of Hunting on Annual Survival Rates of Adult Mallards","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/3801166","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Burnham","given":"Kenneth P."},{"family":"White","given":"Gary C."},{"family":"Anderson","given":"David R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1984"]]}}},{"id":30670,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7RCP77D7"],"itemData":{"id":30670,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract Hunting is one of the human activities that directly affect wildlife and has received increasing attention given its socioeconomic dimensions. Most studies have been conducted on coastal and wetland areas and showed that hunting activity can greatly affect bird behaviour and distribution. Hunting-free reserves for game species are zones where birds find an area of reduced disturbance. We evaluated the effect of hunting activities on the behaviour and use of hunting-free areas of lapwings Vanellus vanellus, golden plovers Pluvialis apricaria and little bustards Tetrax tetrax in agricultural areas. We compared the habitat use and behaviour of birds on days before, during and after hunting took place. All three studied species showed strong behavioural responses to hunting activities. Hunting activity increased flight probability and time spent vigilant (higher on hunting days than just before and after a hunting day), to the detriment of resting. We also found distributional (use of hunting-free reserve) responses to hunting activities, with hunting-free reserves being used more frequently during hunting days. Thus, reserves can mitigate the disturbance caused by hunting activities, benefiting threatened species in agricultural areas. Increasing the size or number of hunting-free areas might be an important management and conservation tool to reduce the impacts of hunting activities.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/j.1469-1795.2009.00259.x","ISSN":"1367-9430","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Animal Conservation","page":"346-354","title":"Effects of hunting on the behaviour and spatial distribution of farmland birds: importance of hunting-free refuges in agricultural areas","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2009.00259.x","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Casas","given":"F."},{"family":"Mougeot","given":"F."},{"family":"Viñuela","given":"J."},{"family":"Bretagnolle","given":"V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]],"season":"1"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thus the literature includes good illustrations of the success of hunting as a management tool. However, the measures contributing to successes in conservation of species and habitats were recognized to be context specific and should not have a blanket application across populations.
      The development of assessment tools to measure the sustainability of hunting over the years was highlighted and the role of research acknowledged in a number of articles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QQ0KD4x1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bennett, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Bennett, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30587,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LVUN9QI8"],"itemData":{"id":30587,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605302000637","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"04","journalAbbreviation":"ORX","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Hunting the world's wildlife to extinction","URL":"http://www.journals.cambridge.org/abstract_S0030605302000637","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"Elizabeth L."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E. J."},{"family":"Bakarr","given":"Mohamed"},{"family":"Eves","given":"Heather E."},{"family":"Robinson","given":"John G."},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"David S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are many cases documenting the value of information from hunting in evaluating the status and trends of harvested populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GtOSotsb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. T. Robinson, 1971; Scillitani, 2010; Struebig, 2007; Tuttle, 1979)","plainCitation":"(P. T. Robinson, 1971; Scillitani, 2010; Struebig, 2007; Tuttle, 1979)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30561,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BM583AUM"],"itemData":{"id":30561,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In an eight-month field study of the pygmy hippopotamus, an endangered\\nRed Book\\nspecies, the author was able to make some assessment of the status of other animals in Liberia and Sierra Leone and shows how the danger is increasing for most of them. Chimpanzees in particular are exported in large numbers, and in order to catch the young animals whole family groups may be eliminated.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605300009704","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"2-3","journalAbbreviation":"Oryx","language":"en","page":"117-122","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wildlife Trends in Liberia and Sierra Leone","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0030605300009704/type/journal_article","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"P. T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1971"]]}}},{"id":30559,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9S2DFJI8"],"itemData":{"id":30559,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wild boar have been increasing in numbers all over Western Europe in the last 30 years. The species is a major pest for agriculture, but it has a high value as a game species, and in Italy, as in several other countries, it is traditionally hunted in drive hunts by hunting teams with several dogs. This hunting method can have disruptive effects on the demography and spatial behaviour of wild boar, especially family groups. We conducted a 2-year study (2003 and 2004) to determine the effects of drive hunt disturbance on the spatial behaviour of wild boar family groups in the Northern Apennines (central Italy). Twenty wild boar belonging to ten family groups were ear tagged with a radio device. We located resting sites daily and used intensive tracking sessions during drive hunts. Three seasons were determined: pre-hunting, hunting and post-hunting. A general pattern of increased spatial instability during the hunting season was shown. Resting ranges were larger, and resting sites were more interspersed. Distances between consecutive resting sites were greater during the hunting season and, especially, on hunting days. The displacement of family groups caused by drive hunts was generally short lived except for those groups that were repeatedly hunted and so abandoned their pre-hunt (native) range. During drive hunts, wild boar showed a moderate tolerance to hunting disturbance, and only family groups which were directly chased by dogs escaped or altered their behaviour. The response of wild boar to hunting disturbance seemed to be highly related to the degree of hunting pressure combined with individual variability. The impact on wild boar behaviour should be reduced, above all by avoiding repeated hunts in the same areas within a short period and by employing well-trained hounds.","container-title":"European Journal of Wildlife Research","DOI":"10.1007/s10344-009-0314-z","ISSN":"1439-0574","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Eur J Wildl Res","language":"en","page":"307-318","source":"Springer Link","title":"Do intensive drive hunts affect wild boar (Sus scrofa) spatial behaviour in Italy? Some evidences and management implications","title-short":"Do intensive drive hunts affect wild boar (Sus scrofa) spatial behaviour in Italy?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10344-009-0314-z","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Scillitani","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Monaco","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Toso","given":"Silvano"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]],"season":"1"}}},{"id":30513,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8ZYT9U8I"],"itemData":{"id":30513,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","language":"en","page":"390–393","title":"Intensive hunting of large flying foxes Pteropus vampyrus natunae in Central Kalimantan, Indonesian Borneo","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Struebig","given":"M.J."},{"family":"Harrison","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Cheyne","given":"S."},{"family":"Limin","given":"S.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":30558,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/772YVVAP"],"itemData":{"id":30558,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Twenty-two summer colonies of the endangered gray bat, Myotis grisescens, were censused in 1968-70 and 1976. A conservative estimate revealed a 54% decline in that time period and a 76% decline from known past maximum population levels. A strong association between decline and disturbance by people in caves was observed. Some major colonies disappeared entirely within the 6-year period. Gray bats are restricted to caves year-round and, due to specific temperature and foraging habitat requirements, they aggregate in large colonies in fewer than 5% of available caves. Management requires that the 9 known hibernation caves receive immediate protection, followed by protection of the most important summer caves used by bats from each protected winter cave. Adequate protection may prove impossible unless accompanied by public education. Environmental disturbances such as pesticide contamination, water pollution and siltation, and deforestation may pose serious threats and require further investigation.","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","DOI":"10.2307/3800631","ISSN":"0022-541X","issue":"1","page":"1-17","source":"JSTOR","title":"Status, Causes of Decline, and Management of Endangered Gray Bats","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/3800631","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Tuttle","given":"Merlin D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1979"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, particularly if using information from both hunters’ activities and removals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"90TpPkXL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alvard, 1995; Tallis, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Alvard, 1995; Tallis, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30579,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LN53EPW9"],"itemData":{"id":30579,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605300020883","issue":"1","page":"58-66","title":"Shotguns and sustainable hunting in the Neotropics","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Alvard","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}},{"id":31612,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HK9294D2"],"itemData":{"id":31612,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The core idea of the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment is that the human condition is tightly linked to environmental condition. This assertion suggests that conservation and development projects should be able to achieve both ecological and social progress without detracting from their primary objectives. Whereas “win–win” projects that achieve both conservation and economic gains are a commendable goal, they are not easy to attain. An analysis of World Bank projects with objectives of alleviating poverty and protecting biodiversity revealed that only 16% made major progress on both objectives. Here, we provide a framework for anticipating win–win, lose–lose, and win–lose outcomes as a result of how people manage their ecosystem services. This framework emerges from detailed explorations of several case studies in which biodiversity conservation and economic development coincide and cases in which there is joint failure. We emphasize that scientific advances around ecosystem service production functions, tradeoffs among multiple ecosystem services, and the design of appropriate monitoring programs are necessary for the implementation of conservation and development projects that will successfully advance both environmental and social goals. The potentially bright future of jointly advancing ecosystem services, conservation, and human well-being will be jeopardized unless a global monitoring effort is launched that uses the many ongoing projects as a grand experiment.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.0705797105","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"28","journalAbbreviation":"PNAS","language":"en","page":"9457-9464","source":"www.pnas.org","title":"An ecosystem services framework to support both practical conservation and economic development","URL":"https://www.pnas.org/content/105/28/9457","volume":"105","author":[{"family":"Tallis","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Kareiva","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Marvier","given":"Michelle"},{"family":"Chang","given":"Amy"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]],"season":"15"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
Multispecies hunting, wild meat consumption and perceived disease risk
The pre-2010 literature reported widespread wild meat hunting as one of the major threats to many mammals and birds in Africa, such as buffalo, kudu, and impala ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OeRVmXw9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Golden, 2009; Magige, 2009; Rao, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Golden, 2009; Magige, 2009; Rao, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33211,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2MLD3LK7"],"itemData":{"id":33211,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In the 2003 Durban Vision the Malagasy government committed to tripling the amount of protected areas in Madagascar by 2009. This extensive expansion needs to involve an assessment of the potential impacts on the people who rely on forest resources for subsistence. Wildlife for human consumption (bushmeat) is one such resource that has received great attention on mainland Africa but has largely...","container-title":"Oryx","ISSN":"0030-6053","issue":"3","language":"English","page":"386-392","source":"www.cabdirect.org","title":"Bushmeat hunting and use in the Makira Forest, north-eastern Madagascar: a conservation and livelihoods issue.","title-short":"Bushmeat hunting and use in the Makira Forest, north-eastern Madagascar","URL":"https://www.cabdirect.org/cabdirect/abstract/20093230670","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Golden","given":"C. D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":35191,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TE6ZQ89R"],"itemData":{"id":35191,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Widespread bushmeat hunting represents one of the major threats to many mammals and birds in Africa. We studied the influence of illegal bushmeat hunting on large grassland birds in the Serengeti National Park (SNP) and adjoining protected areas, by using the ostrich (Struthio camelus) as a case study. First, we documented illegal hunting of both small and large birds by using a questionnaire in the villages on the western and eastern side of the SNP. Second, we studied the effect of illegal hunting on density by driving 4,659 km of transects inside SNP and on the adjacent protected areas, where the data were analysed by DISTANCE sampling. Last, we used flight initiation distance (FID, i.e. the distance between an approaching predator (human) and prey when flight is started), to assess possible impacts on behaviour from illegal hunting. We found that people from the western side of the SNP admitted to hunting both small and large grassland birds, and collect ostrich feathers and eggs. Although the Maasai also hunted small birds, only ostrich feathers and eggs of the large grassland birds were used. Surprisingly, we found no significant differences in densities between the SNP and adjoining partially protected areas, but ostriches had longer FID to an approaching human outside the SNP. Currently illegal hunting does not appear to affect the ostrich population, but given the extensive use of birds for consumption more awareness educational programs accompanied by provision of agricultural incentives within the protected areas are needed.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","page":"1361-1373","title":"Does illegal hunting affect density and behaviour of African grassland birds? A case study on ostrich ( Struthio camelus ) | SpringerLink","URL":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10531-008-9481-6","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Magige","given":"Flora John"},{"family":"Holmern","given":"Tomas"},{"family":"Stokke","given":"Sigbjørn"},{"family":"Mlingwa","given":"Charles"},{"family":"Røskaft","given":"Eivin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":30533,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QZYVTCWU"],"itemData":{"id":30533,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ORYX","language":"en","page":"292–300","title":"Hunting patterns in tropical forests adjoining the Hkakaborazi National Park, north Myanmar","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Rao","given":"M."},{"family":"Myint","given":"T."},{"family":"Zaw","given":"T."},{"family":"Htun","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. According to ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"D2Cp8nzq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Golden, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Golden, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33211,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2MLD3LK7"],"itemData":{"id":33211,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In the 2003 Durban Vision the Malagasy government committed to tripling the amount of protected areas in Madagascar by 2009. This extensive expansion needs to involve an assessment of the potential impacts on the people who rely on forest resources for subsistence. Wildlife for human consumption (bushmeat) is one such resource that has received great attention on mainland Africa but has largely...","container-title":"Oryx","ISSN":"0030-6053","issue":"3","language":"English","page":"386-392","source":"www.cabdirect.org","title":"Bushmeat hunting and use in the Makira Forest, north-eastern Madagascar: a conservation and livelihoods issue.","title-short":"Bushmeat hunting and use in the Makira Forest, north-eastern Madagascar","URL":"https://www.cabdirect.org/cabdirect/abstract/20093230670","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Golden","given":"C. D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, this was particularly the case for illegal hunting for wild meat and rampant collection and harvesting of birds, amphibians, reptiles and edible insects. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0nPJgkfv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kumpel, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Kumpel, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30574,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQ79MEML"],"itemData":{"id":30574,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/j.1469-1795.2009.00279.x","title":"Trapper profiles and strategies: insights into sustainability from hunter behaviour - Kümpel - 2009 - Animal Conservation - Wiley Online Library","URL":"https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2009.00279.x","author":[{"family":"Kumpel","given":"N.F."},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"J. M."},{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"G."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} pointed out that hunters are the critical link between demand and supply of wild meat. Although wild meat hunting was acknowledged to present a potential threat to species conservation, demand for wild meat was also highlighted as continuously increasing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zjTBTpDW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barnes, 2002; J. G. Robinson & Bennett, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Barnes, 2002; J. G. Robinson & Bennett, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VK83YM22"],"itemData":{"id":30548,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Poor soils and high rainfall mean that the high productivity of the forests, an assumption that drives the development of the forest zone, is an illusion. The potential of the forests to produce meat, from wild or domestic herbivores, is limited. Growing human populations and shrinking forests accelerate pressures on forest resources faster than national statistics indicate. A simulation model demonstrates the effects of growing hunting pressure on one monkey and two duiker species. A version of this model that includes random variation shows that large harvests can be obtained for many years, but that a population collapse can happen suddenly; there is no period of gradually declining harvests. The accelerating hunting pressure in a zone of low productivity, shrinking habitat for monkeys and antelopes, the dynamics of non-linear systems, and natural environmental variation that affects reproduction and survival will lead to a collapse of hunted populations across the forest zone. We are now seeing the bushmeat boom and soon we will see the bushmeat bust.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605302000443","ISSN":"1365-3008","issue":"3","page":"236-242","title":"The bushmeat boom and bust in West and Central Africa","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/article/bushmeat-boom-and-bust-in-west-and-central-africa/8C0D39E358E7E07320762FAEE3D9660D","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Barnes","given":"Richard F. W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":31195,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3K96NT7L"],"itemData":{"id":31195,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable hunting of wildlife or bushmeat for human consumption across the tropics threatens both wildlife populations and the livelihoods of people who depend on these resources. The probability that hunting can be sustainable depends in part on ecological conditions that affect the ‘supply’ of and ‘demand’ for wildlife resources. In this study, supply is estimated across a number of tropical ecosystem types by calculating the theoretical ‘maximum sustainable offtake’ in kg/km2 for harvestable wildlife. Demand is estimated from observed harvests in kg/km2. We examine how supply and demand vary across relatively undisturbed ecosystems, indexed by annual rainfall. Supply is potentially highest in dry forests and wetter savannah grasslands and decreases in moist forests and more xeric grasslands. Demand tends to exceed supply in moist forests and xeric grasslands. Analogous to this ecological variation along the rainfall gradient is the gradient created by the conversion of tropical forests by humans. We hypothesise that the wild meat supply is greater in secondary forests and forest–farm–fallow mosaics than in undisturbed forests and test this with available data. We conclude that the probability that hunting will be sustainable varies with ecosystem type and degree of human disturbance and should in?uence where land is zoned for protected areas and where for wildlife harvests.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S1367943004001532","ISSN":"1367-9430, 1469-1795","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Animal Conservation","language":"en","page":"397-408","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Having your wildlife and eating it too: an analysis of hunting sustainability across tropical ecosystems","title-short":"Having your wildlife and eating it too","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1017/S1367943004001532","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"John G."},{"family":"Bennett","given":"Elizabeth L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There were also some articles that discussed negative impacts of wild meat hunting on both wild species populations through harvests of threatened species and people`s livelihoods through the transmission of zoonotic diseases which may have serious consequences for exposed people and their communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IEmSNebR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(LeBreton, 2006; Monroe & Willcox, 2006; Wilkie, 2006)","plainCitation":"(LeBreton, 2006; Monroe & Willcox, 2006; Wilkie, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30564,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BY6N9AVN"],"itemData":{"id":30564,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"There is a great need to determine the factors that influence the hunting, butchering and eating of bushmeat to better manage the important social, public health and conservation consequences of these activities. In particular, the hunting and butchering of wild animals can lead to the transmission of diseases that have potentially serious consequences for exposed people and their communities. Comprehension of these risks may lead to decreased levels of these activities. To investigate these issues, 3971 questionnaires were completed to examine the determinants of the hunting, butchering and eating of wild animals and perceptions of disease risk in 17 rural central African villages. A high proportion of individuals reported perceiving a risk of disease infection with bushmeat contact. Individuals who perceived risk were significantly less likely to butcher wild animals than those who perceived no risk. However, perception of risk was not associated with hunting and eating bushmeat (activities that, compared with butchering, involve less contact with raw blood and body fluids). This suggests that some individuals may act on perceived risk to avoid higher risk activity. These findings reinforce the notion that conservation programs in rural villages in central Africa should include health?risk education. This has the potential to reduce the levels of use of wild animals, particularly of certain endangered species (e.g. many non?human primates) that pose a particular risk to human health. However, as the use of wild game is likely to continue, people should be encouraged to undertake hunting and butchering more safely for their own and their community's health.","DOI":"10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00030.x","issue":"4","page":"357-363","title":"Patterns of bushmeat hunting and perceptions of disease risk among central African communities - LeBreton - 2006 - Animal Conservation - Wiley Online Library","URL":"https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00030.x","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"LeBreton","given":"M."},{"family":"Prosser","given":"A.T."},{"family":"Tamoufe","given":"U."},{"family":"Sateren","given":"W."},{"family":"Mpoudi-Ngole","given":"E."},{"family":"Diffo","given":"J.L.D."},{"family":"Burke","given":"D.S"},{"family":"Wolfe","given":"N.D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":30572,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R62LZKGV"],"itemData":{"id":30572,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00071.x","ISSN":"1367-9430, 1469-1795","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Animal Conservation","language":"en","page":"368-369","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Could risk of disease change bushmeat-butchering behavior?","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00071.x","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Monroe","given":"M. C."},{"family":"Willcox","given":"A. S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":30555,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5P9ZU4IM"],"itemData":{"id":30555,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00072.x","issue":"4","page":"370-371","title":"Bushmeat: a disease risk worth taking to put food on the table?","URL":"https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00072.x","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Wilkie","given":"D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

      2.2.3.4.2 Post-2010 conceptualization of hunting
The post 2010 literature review identified 222 papers which were coded according to different aspects of sustainable use that fell into the broad groupings of ecological, socio-economic, governance, and socio-cultural. The data management report for this review is available at: 10.5281/zenodo.6472995. In these papers, ecological aspects are the most represented and socio-economic aspects the least represented (Figure 2.1). 

 Figure 2.1. Distribution of average scores per group of aspects of sustainable hunting, presented in percentages. CC-BY

      Similar findings emerge when evaluating individual aspects of sustainable use. Among the ten most common aspects, half were ecological, followed by governance and socio-cultural aspects (Table 2.2). The most common aspect is an ecological focus at the population level, which was covered in 98% of all analyzed documents, followed by contributions to subsistence or culturally established livelihoods, which was discussed in 27% of the articles. 
