    ipbes:hasDescription """

The use of wild species includes three interacting systems: the wild species themselves, the human practices by which they are obtained from nature, and the uses for which they are intended (Chapter 1, Figure 1.6). Here the status and trends of the use of wild species are reported, organized according to the practices defined at length in Chapter 1: fishing (including lethal and non-lethal use), gathering, terrestrial animal harvesting (including lethal and non-lethal use), logging, and non-extractive practices. These practices are somewhat intuitive, but not always. Thus, readers should be attentive to the definitions and explanations of the practices and why certain organisms (e.g., living shellfish vs. shells) or certain parts of organisms (e.g., tree branches vs. tree fruits, leaves and sap) are discussed in a particular practice category.
      Each section begins with an overview presented in a format consistent with ways of thinking most prevalent in that field. This is followed by specific information relevant for the practice. The following section reviews uses according to the structure detailed in Chapter 1: ceremonial/cultural, decorative/aesthetic, energy, food/beverage, medicine/hygiene, recreation, science/education, shelter/construction, and other (see Chapter 1, Figure 1.6). Only the relevant uses are reported upon in each practice section. These categories are not exclusive, and many species have more than one use depending on a range of variables including their biology, habitat, life cycle, knowledge on utilization, existing rules, and regulations. There may thus be some overlap in the reporting. A selection of cases of multiple and complex use systems is discussed in section 3.4 to demonstrate some of the complexities of reporting on status and trends at national and international scales.
      When possible, the use categories have structured the reporting in this section. However, in many cases the knowledge about the sustainability of use is not organized according to these use categories. Therefore, in order to increase accessibility to policy makers, in sections where the bulk of knowledge is reported using a different system, hybrid organizing structures were created as an attempt to be attentive both to the organizing structure of this assessment, and the expectations of the readers. 
      
3.3.1. Fishing

3.3.1.1. Introduction

Prior to 1950 large-scale motorized fishing was mainly confined to the North Atlantic and Japan. Marine capture fishery has substantially expanded in the last 70 years in terms of geospatial and vertical distribution, and intensity of catch effort. Automatic identification systems data indicates that industrial fishing currently occurs in over 55% of the global ocean ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZFY6CDBs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kroodsma, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Kroodsma, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34094,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BTH4FGWY","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q46WLTTS"],"itemData":{"id":34094,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"More than half the fish in the sea\\nAs the human population has grown in recent decades, our dependence on ocean-supplied protein has rapidly increased. Kroodsma took advantage of the automatic identification system installed on all industrial fishing vessels to map and quantify fishing efforts across the world (see the Perspective by Poloczanska). More than half of the world's oceans are subject to industrial-scale harvest, spanning an area four times that covered by terrestrial agriculture. Furthermore, fishing efforts seem not to depend on economic or environmental drivers, but rather social and political schedules. Thus, more active measures will likely be needed to ensure sustainable use of ocean resources.\\nScience, this issue p. 904; see also p. 864\\nAlthough fishing is one of the most widespread activities by which humans harvest natural resources, its global footprint is poorly understood and has never been directly quantified. We processed 22 billion automatic identification system messages and tracked >70,000 industrial fishing vessels from 2012 to 2016, creating a global dynamic footprint of fishing effort with spatial and temporal resolution two to three orders of magnitude higher than for previous data sets. Our data show that industrial fishing occurs in >55% of ocean area and has a spatial extent more than four times that of agriculture. We find that global patterns of fishing have surprisingly low sensitivity to short-term economic and environmental variation and a strong response to cultural and political events such as holidays and closures.\\nMore than half of the ocean is exposed to industrial fishing activities.\\nMore than half of the ocean is exposed to industrial fishing activities.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aao5646","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6378","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2018 The Authors, some rights reserved; exclusive licensee American Association for the Advancement of Science. No claim to original U.S. Government Works. http://www.sciencemag.org/about/science-licenses-journal-article-reuseThis is an article distributed under the terms of the Science Journals Default License.","note":"PMID: 29472481","page":"904-908","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"Tracking the global footprint of fisheries","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/359/6378/904","volume":"359","author":[{"family":"Kroodsma","given":"David A."},{"family":"Mayorga","given":"Juan"},{"family":"Hochberg","given":"Timothy"},{"family":"Miller","given":"Nathan A."},{"family":"Boerder","given":"Kristina"},{"family":"Ferretti","given":"Francesco"},{"family":"Wilson","given":"Alex"},{"family":"Bergman","given":"Bjorn"},{"family":"White","given":"Timothy D."},{"family":"Block","given":"Barbara A."},{"family":"Woods","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Sullivan","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Costello","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Worm","given":"Boris"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2,23]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} although a much smaller footprint is estimated from the same data when a spatial grid of finer resolution is used in the calculation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ton0n4gmv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Amoroso, Parma, Pitcher, McConnaughey, & Jennings, 2018)","plainCitation":"(R. Amoroso, Parma, Pitcher, McConnaughey, & Jennings, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":159,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8W6XZ62C"],"itemData":{"id":159,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"6404","page":"eaat6713","title":"Comment on “Tracking the global footprint of fisheries”","volume":"361","author":[{"family":"Amoroso","given":"RO"},{"family":"Parma","given":"AM"},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"CR"},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"RA"},{"family":"Jennings","given":"S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Relative to coastal ecosystems, high seas ecosystems are much less affected ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OykPhPpW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Halpern, 2008; J. B. C. Jackson, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Halpern, 2008; J. B. C. Jackson, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9496,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9W3MMJA"],"itemData":{"id":9496,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1149345","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5865","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"948-952","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A Global Map of Human Impact on Marine Ecosystems","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1149345","volume":"319","author":[{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."},{"family":"Walbridge","given":"Shaun"},{"family":"Selkoe","given":"Kimberly A."},{"family":"Kappel","given":"Carrie V."},{"family":"Micheli","given":"Fiorenza"},{"family":"D'Agrosa","given":"Caterina"},{"family":"Bruno","given":"John F."},{"family":"Casey","given":"Kenneth S."},{"family":"Ebert","given":"Colin"},{"family":"Fox","given":"Helen E."},{"family":"Fujita","given":"Rod"},{"family":"Heinemann","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Lenihan","given":"Hunter S."},{"family":"Madin","given":"Elizabeth M. P."},{"family":"Perry","given":"Matthew T."},{"family":"Selig","given":"Elizabeth R."},{"family":"Spalding","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Steneck","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Reg"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",2,15]]}}},{"id":9899,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A7QV9IIY"],"itemData":{"id":9899,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1059199","page":"629-638","title":"Historical overfishing and the recent collapse of coastal ecosystems","volume":"293","author":[{"family":"Jackson","given":"J B C"},{"family":"Kirby","given":"M X"},{"family":"Berger","given":"W H"},{"family":"Bjorndal","given":"K A"},{"family":"Botsford","given":"L W"},{"family":"Bourque","given":"B J"},{"family":"Bradbury","given":"R H"},{"family":"Cooke","given":"R"},{"family":"Erlandson","given":"J"},{"family":"Estes","given":"J A"},{"family":"Hughes","given":"T P"},{"family":"Kidwell","given":"S"},{"family":"Lange","given":"C B"},{"family":"Lenihan","given":"H S"},{"family":"Pandolfi","given":"J M"},{"family":"Peterson","given":"C H"},{"family":"Steneck","given":"R S"},{"family":"Tegner","given":"M J"},{"family":"Warner","given":"R R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, reported landings from the high seas has been accelerating since the mid- 20th century with under two million tons in 1950 to over ten million tons in 2008 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"afti1f17a8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2010c)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2010c)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IC9ZVX93"],"itemData":{"id":30331,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229en","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"State of the worlds fisheries and aquaculture in 2010.","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The history of sustainable use of capture fisheries is closely tied with several key events and international agreements. Prominent among those is the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea ratified in 1982 by 157 parties. One of its most significant provisions was the establishment of 200-mile exclusive economic zones and introducing the concept of maximum sustainable yield as the default goal of fisheries management. The 200-mile exclusive economic zone allowed countries to exclude wide ranging foreign fishing fleets that earlier were able to legally fish within 12 miles of the national coastline. As a result, several countries established fisheries management systems (e.g., scientific assessment, regulation of harvest) for the newly expanded waters under their jurisdiction. This also led to expansion of many domestic fishing fleets.  
      The legal framework of the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea did not include several fish stocks across multiple exclusive economic zones or in the high seas. The United Nations fish stocks Agreement from 2001 provided international protocols for managing these “straddling stocks” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aldf5cpk5f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. R. Munro, 2000)","plainCitation":"(G. R. Munro, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30327,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9VPRWHNB"],"itemData":{"id":30327,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Resource Economics","ISSN":"0738-1360","issue":"4","page":"265-280","title":"The United Nations Fish Stocks Agreement of 1995: history and problems of implementation","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Munro","given":"Gordon R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It mandated the formation of Regional Fisheries Management Organizations (RFMO) to sustainably manage high seas and the straddling stocks. Following the Agreement, there are now 17 Regional Fisheries Management Organizations that cover almost all the high seas fisheries and associated straddling stocks outside national exclusive economic zones. Regional Fisheries Management Organizations are competent and mandated to establish binding conservation and management measures. They provide a formal mechanism for fishing states and states in whose jurisdiction fishery resources occur to meet their international obligation to cooperate to sustainably govern shared living marine resources throughout their distributions (the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea Articles 63, 66(5), 118; Code Articles 7.1.5, 6.12 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ya1ZDPQV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 1995)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":28897,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LT3AI4JH"],"itemData":{"id":28897,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","publisher":"Food and Agriculture ORganization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; Agreement on Port State Measure (PSMA) Article 4(1)(b) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1du4lti3mi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2010a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2010a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1296,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8YD3J8ZM"],"itemData":{"id":1296,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","page":"100","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"Agreement on Port State Measures to Prevent, Deter and Eliminate Illegal, Unreported and Unregulated Fishing","URL":"https://www.fao.org/3/i1644t/i1644t.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Regional Fisheries Management Organizations have played a critical role in multilateral fisheries governance of stocks that straddle or occur beyond national jurisdictions and are highly migratory. While spatial and taxonomic gaps remain, a large proportion of global marine fisheries are now managed by one or multiple Regional Fisheries Management Organizations, and they cover most areas of the high seas (Figure 3.15). 


Figure 3.15 Species-specific regional fisheries management organizations (top) and other regional fisheries management organizations (bottom). Abbreviations: ICCAT: International Commission for the Conservation of Atlantic Tunas; IATTC: Inter-American Tropical Tuna Commission, IPHC: International Pacific Halibut Commission; NPAFC: North Pacific Anadromous Fish Commission; WCPFC: Western and Central Pacific Fisheries Commission; CCSBT: Commission for the Conservation of Southern Bluefin Tuna; IOTC: Indian Ocean Tuna Commission; NASCO: North Atlantic Salmon Conservation Organization; PSC: Pacific Salmon Commission; IWC: International Whaling Commission; CACFISH: Central Asian and Caucuses Regional Fisheries and Aquaculture Commission; CTMFM: Joint Technical Commission of the Maritime Front; NAFO: Northwest Atlantic Fisheries Organization; NPFC: North Pacific Fisheries Commission; SEAFO: South East Atlantic Fisheries Organization; SPRFMO: South Pacific Regional Fisheries Management Organization; CCAMLR: Commission for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources; GFCM: General Fisheries Commission for the Mediterranean; NEAFC: North-East Atlantic Fisheries Commission; RECOFI: Regional Commission for Fisheries; SIOFA: Southern Indian Ocean Fisheries Agreement; LVFO: Lake Victoria Fisheries Organization. These maps are directly copied from its original source ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"agcr77gv3n","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L\\\\uc0\\\\u248{}bach, Petersson, Haberkon, & Mannini, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Løbach, Petersson, Haberkon, & Mannini, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1293,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K6374YVZ"],"itemData":{"id":1293,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","genre":"AO Fisheries and Aquaculture Technical Paper No. 651","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca7843en","publisher":"FAO","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Regional fisheries management organizations and advisory bodies. Activities and developments, 2000–2017.","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca7843en","author":[{"family":"Løbach","given":"Terje"},{"family":"Petersson","given":"T."},{"family":"Haberkon","given":"E."},{"family":"Mannini","given":"P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and was not modified by the assessment authors. The maps are copyrighted under license CC BY-NC-SA 3.0 IGO. The designations employed and the presentation of material on the maps used in the assessment do not imply the expression of any opinion whatsoever on the part of IPBES concerning the legal status of any country, territory, city or area or of its authorities, or concerning the delimitation of its frontiers or boundaries. These maps have been prepared or used for the sole purpose of facilitating the assessment of the broad biogeographical areas represented therein and for purposes of representing scientific data spatially.
      
      Fishing has impacts on marine ecosystems other than the target species. A range of international agreements have evolved to provide guidance on managing non-target species and vulnerable marine ecosystems (VMEs). Legal instruments establishing international responsibility to conserve associated and dependent species are relatively recent, which first became an obligation under the 1982 Law of the Sea Convention, and was reiterated and clarified further in subsequent United Nations resolutions (United Nations 1982 [Article 119], 1995 [Article 5(f), Article 10(d), and Annex 1]; 2006a, b). These provisions were elaborated further in subsequent instruments and guidance from other multilateral organizations. This includes the 1995 code of conduct for responsible fisheries of the FAO, which calls for the sustainable use of aquatic ecosystems and promotes the conservation of biodiversity and ecosystems by minimizing fisheries impacts on non-target species and the ecosystem in general ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CWXldXI7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 1995)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":28897,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LT3AI4JH"],"itemData":{"id":28897,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","publisher":"Food and Agriculture ORganization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. FAO has also produced a voluntary international plan of action on reducing the incidental capture of seabirds in longline fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mtlo8jRK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 1999a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 1999a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31430,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NS7U6M69"],"itemData":{"id":31430,"type":"book","call-number":"QL82 .I58 1999","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-104332-5","note":"OCLC: ocm43326999","number-of-pages":"26","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"International plan of action for reducing incidental catch of seabirds in longline fisheries.","title-short":"International plan of action for reducing incidental catch of seabirds in longline fisheries","editor":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, an international plan of action on the conservation and management of sharks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3II1BejD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 1999b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 1999b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2022,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8DL4HZ4Z"],"itemData":{"id":2022,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","language":"en","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"International plan of action for the conservation and management of sharks","editor":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, international guidelines on reducing marine turtle fishing mortality ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xUSX7psy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO Fisheries Department, 2009)","plainCitation":"(FAO Fisheries Department, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31431,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GART3PSB"],"itemData":{"id":31431,"type":"book","abstract":"Sea turtles are affected by a range of different factors, some natural and others\\ncaused by human activities, including fishing operations. As a result, all sea turtle\\nspecies whose conservation status has been assessed are considered to be\\nthreatened or endangered. These guidelines provide assistance for the\\npreparation of national or multilateral fisheries management measures and\\nindustry initiatives that may help to conserve sea turtles by reducing the negative\\nimpacts that fisheries may have on them. The guidelines are voluntary and nonbinding.\\nTheir scope is global, but when they are implemented, national and\\nregional diversity, including cultural and socio-economic differences, should be\\ntaken into account. These guidelines present our best understanding of how to\\nreduce interactions between sea turtles and fishing gear and reduce the\\nproportion of caught turtles that are killed as a result of interactions with marine\\ncapture fisheries. They include information about how to change fishing gear and\\nfishing methods and how the fishing industry can adopt voluntary approaches to\\nreduce sea turtle mortality. The guidelines make suggestions about implementing\\nmanagement actions, such as input and output controls and bycatch fees, and\\nthey cover subjects such as bycatch hotspot avoidance, best practices for the\\nhandling and release of caught turtles and reducing derelict fishing gear and other\\nmarine debris. They also identify fisheries and areas where fishing may be a\\nrelatively important cause of sea turtle deaths. Research, monitoring, information\\nexchange, capacity-building, financial support, socio-economic, cultural and legal\\naspects are also discussed","source":"ResearchGate","title":"Guidelines to Reduce Sea turtle Mortality in Fishing Operations. FAO Technical Guidelines for Responsible Fisheries","author":[{"family":"FAO Fisheries Department","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and broad guidelines on managing fisheries bycatch ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1vim2nujq0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2011)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1295,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/89NIARKU"],"itemData":{"id":1295,"type":"report","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","title":"International guidelines on bycatch management and reduction of discards","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These new instruments and international guidelines broadened the mandate of pre-existing Regional Fisheries Management Organizations, expanding their mandates from one target species to meet newer expectations for ecosystem-based management and precautionary approaches, i.e., establishing explicit limits of acceptable impacts on fish and non-fish bycatch species, associated or dependent and threatened species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"slpJ5edk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Japan, 2007; Lodge, Anderson, & Lobach, 2007; United Nations, 2006a, 2006b)","plainCitation":"(Japan, 2007; Lodge, Anderson, & Lobach, 2007; United Nations, 2006a, 2006b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":27180,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X8CP5YBY"],"itemData":{"id":27180,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Fisheries Agency of Japan","title":"Report of the Joint Meeting of Tuna RFMOs","author":[{"family":"Japan","given":"Fisheries Agency"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":9493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JS4942UD"],"itemData":{"id":9493,"type":"book","publisher":"Chatham House","title":"Recommended Best Practices for Regional Fisheries Management Organizations. Report of an Independent Panel to Develop a Model for Improved Governance by Regional Fisheries Management Organizations","author":[{"family":"Lodge","given":"M"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"D"},{"family":"Lobach","given":"T"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":28611,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J6EGDVGA"],"itemData":{"id":28611,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Resolution Adopted by the General Assembly. 61/105. Sustainable Fisheries, including through the 1995 Agreement for the Implementation of the Provisions of the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea of 10 December 1982 relating to the Conservation and Management of straddling Fish Stocks and Highly Migratory Fish Stocks, and Related Instruments (United Nations General Assembly Doc","author":[{"family":"United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":30319,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XUBCE538"],"itemData":{"id":30319,"type":"book","edition":"A/CONF.210/2006/15","publisher":"United Nations","title":"Review Conference on the Agreement for the Implementation of the Provisions of the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea of 10 December 1982 relating to the Conservation and Management of Straddling Fish Stocks and Highly Migratory Fish Stocks, New York, 22 to 26 May 2006","author":[{"family":"United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Fisheries targeting relatively fecund species can have profound impacts on co-occurring incidentally caught or bycatch species with delayed maturation, low fecundity and other life history traits that make them vulnerable to anthropogenic causes of mortality. While target stocks may be sustainable, the conservation status of bycatch species and other associated and dependent species is often not known. For instance, 47 of 68 fisheries that catch marine resources managed by Regional Fisheries Management Organizations have no observer coverage ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IXNnPPdX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Gilman, Passfield, & Nakamura, 2014)","plainCitation":"(E. Gilman, Passfield, & Nakamura, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R7EZ9ZUS","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IVW97XZW"],"itemData":{"id":31589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12021","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"327-351","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Performance of regional fisheries management organizations: ecosystem-based governance of bycatch and discards","title-short":"Performance of regional fisheries management organizations","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/faf.12021","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Gilman","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Passfield","given":"Kelvin"},{"family":"Nakamura","given":"Katrina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",6]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; for the vast majority of the ca. 4.6 million fishing vessels globally, information on non-retained catch is absent. In most fisheries, there are large gaps in understanding of life histories for many marine species. Information on total cumulative anthropogenic levels of fishery removals from an individual population, knowledge of the conservation status of individual populations, and deficits in monitoring are all unknown. Data collection protocols, observer coverage rates, and sufficient time-series to detect the response in absolute population abundance of long-lived species to this anthropogenic mortality source are also knowledge gaps in various global fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lgMso094","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Gilman, 2020; Lewison, Crowder, Read, & Freeman, 2004; Musick, 1999; P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez Roda, 2019)","plainCitation":"(E. Gilman, 2020; Lewison, Crowder, Read, & Freeman, 2004; Musick, 1999; Pérez Roda, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31590,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PCFC58VI"],"itemData":{"id":31590,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/s41598-020-71021-x","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"14017","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Benchmarking global fisheries discards","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-020-71021-x","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Gilman","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Perez Roda","given":"A."},{"family":"Huntington","given":"T."},{"family":"Kennelly","given":"S. J."},{"family":"Suuronen","given":"P."},{"family":"Chaloupka","given":"M."},{"family":"Medley","given":"P. A. H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}},{"id":31588,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GRQJITCS"],"itemData":{"id":31588,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2004.09.004","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"598-604","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Understanding impacts of fisheries bycatch on marine megafauna","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534704002642","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Lewison","given":"R"},{"family":"Crowder","given":"L"},{"family":"Read","given":"A"},{"family":"Freeman","given":"S"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",11]]}}},{"id":8635,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PMJQPPXD"],"itemData":{"id":8635,"type":"book","call-number":"QL121 .L53 1999","collection-number":"23","collection-title":"American Fisheries Society symposium","event-place":"Bethesda, Md","ISBN":"978-1-888569-15-5","number-of-pages":"265","publisher":"American Fisheries Society","publisher-place":"Bethesda, Md","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Life in the slow lane: ecology and conservation of long-lived marine animals","title-short":"Life in the slow lane","editor":[{"family":"Musick","given":"John A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":30328,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/392F7S9C"],"itemData":{"id":30328,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-92-5-131226-1","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1089014002","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"A third assessment of global marine fisheries discards / by Maria Amparo Pérez Roda, Eric Gilman, Tim Huntington, Steven J. Kennelly, Petri Suuronen, Milani Chaloupka, and Paul A. H. Medley.","author":[{"family":"Pérez Roda","given":"Maria Amparo"},{"family":"Gilman","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Huntington","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Kennelly","given":"Steven J"},{"family":"Suuronen","given":"Petri"},{"family":"Chaloupka","given":"Milani"},{"family":"Medley","given":"Paul A. H"},{"literal":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      United Nations Resolution 61/105 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a24lm24kfu8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNGA, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNGA, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1292,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7852NTZB"],"itemData":{"id":1292,"type":"legislation","container-title":"61/105","title":"United Nations General Assembly Resolution 61/105. Sustainable fisheries, including through the 1995 Agreement for the Implementation of the Provisions of the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea of 10 December 1982 relating to the Conservation and Management of Straddling Fish Stocks and Highly Migratory Fish Stocks, and related instruments","URL":"https://documents-dds-ny.un.org/doc/UNDOC/GEN/N06/500/73/PDF/N0650073.pdf?OpenElement","author":[{"family":"UNGA","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",12,8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} provides for responsible management of vulnerable marine ecosystems and non-target species as a legally binding instrument. It provides for collection of data on the impacts of fishing on vulnerable marine ecosystems and specific actions to protect them. Another important international protocol is the Agreement on Port State Measures ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ee9v7v6ug","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2016a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2016a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1297,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QTYVYMG2"],"itemData":{"id":1297,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"Agreement on Port State Measures to Prevent, Deter and Eliminate Illegal, Unreported and Unregulated Fishing","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} aimed at preventing, deterring and eliminating illegal, unreported and unregulated fishing by preventing vessels engaged in illegal, unreported and unregulated fishing from using ports and landing their catches ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2f6r8kdlm9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2021)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30332,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XULR83E2"],"itemData":{"id":30332,"type":"webpage","title":"Agreement on Port State Measures (PSMA) | Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","URL":"http://www.fao.org/port-state-measures/en/","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      Outside of formal international agreements, there have been many efforts to improve management both by non-governmental organizations and national governments. The 1990s were an era of greatly expanding concerns about overfishing, in many ways stimulated by the highly publicized collapse of the northern cod fishery in Canada ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2iftm22ik6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Finlayson, 1994; Kurlansky, 1997; J. C. Rice, Shelton, Rivard, Chouinard, & Fr\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}chet, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Finlayson, 1994; Kurlansky, 1997; J. C. Rice, Shelton, Rivard, Chouinard, & Fréchet, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34509,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HMXSG2LD"],"itemData":{"id":34509,"type":"book","event-place":"St. Johns, Newfoundland, Canada","number-of-pages":"176","publisher":"Institute of Social and Economic Research, Memorial University of Newfoundland","publisher-place":"St. Johns, Newfoundland, Canada","title":"Fishing for truth: a sociological analysis of northern cod stock assessments from 1977-1990","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Finlayson","given":"AC"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}},{"id":1290,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M5UTAS74"],"itemData":{"id":1290,"type":"book","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"0802713262","number-of-pages":"294","publisher":"Walker and Co.","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Cod: A Biography of the Fish that Changed the World","author":[{"family":"Kurlansky","given":"Mark"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}},{"id":30326,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XQ2RSS7Z"],"itemData":{"id":30326,"type":"report","event-place":"Copenhagen","number":"CM 2003/U:06","page":"23","publisher":"International Council for Exploration of the Seas","publisher-place":"Copenhagen","title":"Recovering Canadian Atlantic cod stocks: the shape of things to come?","author":[{"family":"Rice","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Shelton","given":"P.A."},{"family":"Rivard","given":"D."},{"family":"Chouinard","given":"G.A."},{"family":"Fréchet","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Marine Stewardship Council was formed in 1997 with the goal to use market pressure to improve fisheries sustainability, and now is a major force in market access, particularly in Europe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1kgc6iuru1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(MSC, 2021)","plainCitation":"(MSC, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30333,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M4JSIF4A"],"itemData":{"id":30333,"type":"webpage","abstract":"In the 1990s, overfishing was creating a damaging effect on the marine environment and seafood supplies. For over 20 years the MSC has worked to help reverse these effects and promote sustainable fishing.","language":"en","title":"History of the MSC | Marine Stewardship Council","URL":"https://www.msc.org/about-the-msc/our-history","author":[{"family":"MSC","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many environmental non-governmental organizations formed marine conservation divisions, and entirely new non-governmental organizations appeared with a focus on marine ecosystems. These were, to a great extent, funded by United States of America foundations with amounts up to 500 million United States dollars per year spent by environmental non-governmental organizations and foundations on marine conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15grnqfm44","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hilborn & Hilborn, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Hilborn & Hilborn, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30325,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UF5G3H9S"],"itemData":{"id":30325,"type":"book","ISBN":"0-19-257612-7","publisher":"Oxford University Press","title":"Ocean Recovery: A sustainable future for global fisheries?","author":[{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ulrike"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Since the 1990s national governments have expanded the science and management efforts through changes in legislations such as the United States of America Magnuson-Stevens act and revisions, and the creation of the Common Fisheries Policy in the European Union.  
      Finally, there has been increasing attention paid to consider impacts on fishing dependent coastal communities in almost all countries. As examples, Canada guarantees the first 90,000 tons of cod quota to small-scale inshore fishers, the United States of America allocates 8% of the allowable catch in the large industrial fisheries of the Bering Sea to local communities, and in Chile fishing cooperatives can apply for and be granted exclusive ownership of local inshore resources. 
      
3.3.1.2. Status and trends in global marine capture fisheries 

For the purposes of this assessment, in accordance with Chapter 1, fishing is defined as the harvest of entire organisms or parts of organisms that result in mortality of the aquatic animals, for example commercial fisheries or shark finning. Non-lethal fishing is defined as harvesting of entire or parts or products of organisms without intended mortality. Examples of non-lethal fishing include harvesting fish for the aquarium trade, catch and release fishing, or the extraction of blood from horseshoe crabs. 
      The status and trends of wild fish are estimated by a range of methods including scientific surveys, size or age distribution, catch per boat day and other estimates based on catch rate / fishing gear. A sophisticated method, known as “stock assessments”, combines all these types of data to provide scientific estimates of the trend in abundance and harvest rate for fish stocks. The most robust approaches now involve multispecies and ecosystem-level assessments, an improvement over conventional single stock assessments, even though single stock assessments remain the dominant approach. Produced by national fisheries agencies and international regional fisheries management organizations, these scientific assessments are publicly available for roughly half of the global fish catch. Considerable effort in recent years has been towards increasing understanding of the status of stocks that produce the other half of global marine catches. This effort is ongoing.
      The most cited stock status assessment comes from the state of world fisheries and aquaculture of the FAOITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1n5rcrcnkg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2020d)","plainCitation":"(2020d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which uses a sample of roughly 500 fish stocks from around the world to describe the status of stocks. When scientific assessments are not available, expert knowledge is often used to make some sort of assessment. The material presented below follows this approach.
      The status of fish stocks can be described in many ways. The most common approach is to compare the current abundance of the fish stock to target abundance, usually a target based on maximizing the long-term harvest, often called “maximum sustainable yield”. In FAO terminology, stocks that are above this target level are called “underfished”, stocks below the target are “overfished” and stocks with abundance close to the target are called “maximally sustainably fished." FAO uses the range 0.8 to 1.2 of the abundance as an indicator of maximum sustainable yield. Because fish stocks fluctuate naturally, sometimes over orders of magnitude of abundance, a better evaluation of the status of the fishery is to look at the fishing pressure relative to the targets. Fishing harder than the target rate is called “overfishing”. Some assessments of stock status are based solely on the trends in catch. When catch declines it is assumed that the stock is in poor shape. Comparisons may also be made between the current abundance of fish stocks to estimates from before significant fishing began, which is most commonly done using various kinds of ecosystem models (Figure 3.16).
      

Figure 3.16 The Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations’ estimates of the status of global fish stocks. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ufr1pp0m3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020d)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY-NC-SA 3.0 IGO.

      A common misinterpretation of the above data is that stocks that are “maximally sustainably fished” are somehow being pushed to the limit and this is an undesirable state. In fact, “maximally sustainably fished” means that stocks are at an abundance level that will provide long-term maximum sustainable yield. Another misinterpretation is that stocks that are overfished are headed towards extinction or necessarily declining.  “Overfished” simply means an abundance lower than would produce maximum sustainable yield, and many stocks remain at this level for decades; if fishing pressure is reduced these stocks can rebuild. Despite this common understanding, there is no agreed upon definition of what is overfished. The FAO defines overfished as the stock biomass being below 80% of the abundance that would produce maximum sustainable yield; the United States of America and New Zealand use a 50% cutoff, while many tunas’ Regional Fisheries Management Organizations define overfished as being below the target level.  
      From a conservation perspective, stocks that are fished to very low abundance, where recovery is often very slow, are a concern due to lack of knowledge of potential recovery. Neubauer ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1e6p28344o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2013)","plainCitation":"(2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30334,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LM4V8QP2"],"itemData":{"id":30334,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Recovery of overexploited marine populations has been slow, and most remain below target biomass levels. A key question is whether this is due to insufficient reductions in harvest rates or the erosion of population resilience. Using a global meta-analysis of overfished stocks, we find that resilience of those stocks subjected to moderate levels of overfishing is enhanced, not compromised, offering the possibility of swift recovery. However, prolonged intense overexploitation, especially for collapsed stocks, not only delays rebuilding but also substantially increases the uncertainty in recovery times, despite predictable influences of fishing and life history. Timely and decisive reductions in harvest rates could mitigate this uncertainty. Instead, current harvest and low biomass levels render recovery improbable for the majority of the world's depleted stocks.","archive_location":"WOS:000317657500054","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1230441","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"6130","page":"347-349","title":"Resilience and Recovery of Overexploited Marine Populations","URL":"://WOS:000317657500054","volume":"340","author":[{"family":"Neubauer","given":"P."},{"family":"Jensen","given":"O. P."},{"family":"Hutchings","given":"J. A."},{"family":"Baum","given":"J. K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} conclude that "prolonged intense overexploitation, especially for collapsed stocks, not only delays rebuilding but also substantially increases the uncertainty in recovery times. Furthermore, when stocks become depleted, catch rates are lower and therefore the effort needed to catch a given volume of fish is higher and so is its environmental footprint.
      For those fisheries that produce half of the world's marine catch for which good data is available, on average fish stocks are increasing because fishing pressure is lower than levels that would produce maximum long-term yield, and abundance is above target levels (Figure 3.17) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a13g67706d7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hilborn, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Hilborn, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34306,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C29NHAMV"],"itemData":{"id":34306,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Marine fish stocks are an important part of the world food system and are particularly important for many of the poorest people of the world. Most existing analyses suggest overfishing is increasing, and there is widespread concern that fish stocks are decreasing throughout most of the world. We assembled trends in abundance and harvest rate of stocks that are scientifically assessed, constituting half of the reported global marine fish catch. For these stocks, on average, abundance is increasing and is at proposed target levels. Compared with regions that are intensively managed, regions with less-developed fisheries management have, on average, 3-fold greater harvest rates and half the abundance as assessed stocks. Available evidence suggests that the regions without assessments of abundance have little fisheries management, and stocks are in poor shape. Increased application of area-appropriate fisheries science recommendations and management tools are still needed for sustaining fisheries in places where they are lacking.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1909726116","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"2218-2224","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effective fisheries management instrumental in improving fish stock status","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1909726116","volume":"117","author":[{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo Oscar"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Christopher M."},{"family":"Baum","given":"Julia K."},{"family":"Branch","given":"Trevor A."},{"family":"Costello","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Moor","given":"Carryn L.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Faraj","given":"Abdelmalek"},{"family":"Hively","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Olaf P."},{"family":"Kurota","given":"Hiroyuki"},{"family":"Little","given":"L. Richard"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"McClanahan","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Melnychuk","given":"Michael C."},{"family":"Minto","given":"Cóilín"},{"family":"Osio","given":"Giacomo Chato"},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Pons","given":"Maite"},{"family":"Segurado","given":"Susana"},{"family":"Szuwalski","given":"Cody S."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"Jono R."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Yimin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1,28]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.


Figure 3.17 Estimated abundance of global fish stocks 1970 – 2016 (A), European Union non-Mediterranean fish stocks (B), Mediterranean-Black Sea fish stocks (C), and Atlantic (D) and Pacific (E) ocean tuna fish stocks. Estimated abundance (B/BMSY -Biomass relative to the biomass that produces Maximum Sustainable Yield- in orange) and fishing pressure (U/UMSY -Fishing pressure or mortality relative to the fraction of the population harvested- in green) are shown for the stocks that are scientifically assessed around the world from 1970 to 2016 - shaded area is the confidence intervals. The biomass and fishing pressure are scaled to the level that would produce maximum sustainable yield. CC-BY. See data management report for the figure at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452917.

      Figure 3.17A shows the estimated abundance (B/BMSY -Biomass relative to the biomass that produces Maximum Sustainable Yield- in orange), fishing pressure (U/UMSY -Fishing pressure or mortality relative to the fraction of the population harvested- in green), and catch (in blue) for the stocks that are scientifically assessed around the world from 1970 to 2016. The biomass and fishing pressure are scaled to the level that would produce maximum sustainable yield. Abundance declined from 1970 to 1995, then leveled off for 10 years and about 2005 began to increase. This is consistent with increased fishing pressure from 1970 to the mid 1990s, then declining pressure since that time (Figure 3.17). When looking at different regions where there is good scientific understanding of stock status, one notes contrasting trends (Figure 3.17 B-E). The European Union (Figure 3.17B), Atlantic and Baltic stocks were already fished hard in 1970 and harvest rates increased up to about 1995, and then declined. Stocks were above target levels in 1970, declined to about 2005 and then began to increase. Mediterranean stocks (Figure 3.17C) have seen increasing fishing pressure since 1970 and declining abundance. Fishing pressure is far above target levels and abundance well below. One species specific estimate is included here (Figure 3.17 D & E). Global tuna fisheries were not fully developed in 1970 and saw generally increasing fishing pressure and declining abundance until recent years when abundance leveled off at or above target levels. Atlantic tuna fisheries were fished harder and earlier than Pacific (Figure 3.17) that would produce maximum sustainable yield.  
   In the FAO’s state of the world fisheries and aquaculture annual reports there are many stocks that are evaluated using expert knowledge because there is no scientific stock assessment. Melnychuk, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2l3sjsbimk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2017)","plainCitation":"(2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":352,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9FUEIPAW"],"itemData":{"id":352,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fisheries management systems around the world are highly diverse in their design, operation, and effectiveness at meeting objectives. A variety of management institutions, strategies, and tactics are used across disparate regions, fishing fleets, and taxonomic groups. At a global level, it is unclear which particular management attributes have greatest influence on the status of fished populations, and also unclear which external factors affect the overall success of fisheries management systems. We used expert surveys to characterize the management systems by species of 28 major fishing nations and examined influences of economic, geographic, and fishery-related factors. A Fisheries Management Index, which integrated research, management, enforcement, and socioeconomic attributes, showed wide variation among countries and was strongly affected by per capita gross domestic product (positively) and capacity-enhancing subsidies (negatively). Among 13 management attributes considered, three were particularly influential in whether stock size and fishing mortality are currently in or trending toward desirable states: extensiveness of stock assessments, strength of fishing pressure limits, and comprehensiveness of enforcement programs. These results support arguments that the key to successful fisheries management is the implementation and enforcement of science-based catch or effort limits, and that monetary investment into fisheries can help achieve management objectives if used to limit fishing pressure rather than enhance fishing capacity. Countries with currently less-effective management systems have the greatest potential for improving long-term stock status outcomes and should be the focus of efforts to improve fisheries management globally.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1609915114","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"178-183","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fisheries management impacts on target species status","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1609915114","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Melnychuk","given":"Michael C."},{"family":"Peterson","given":"Emily"},{"family":"Elliott","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",8,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} used an expert opinion survey of the 28 countries landing the most fish to determine the status of stocks and found that generally temperate stocks were considered to be in good shape while tropical stocks where not (Figure 3.18).       
   

Figure 3.18 Global abundance by coastline based on expert estimates.  Green indicates experts believe that most stocks are at abundance consistent with long term maximum sustainable yield, red indicates few stocks are at that level. Data from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ooqov71ht","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Melnychuk, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Melnychuk, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":352,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9FUEIPAW"],"itemData":{"id":352,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fisheries management systems around the world are highly diverse in their design, operation, and effectiveness at meeting objectives. A variety of management institutions, strategies, and tactics are used across disparate regions, fishing fleets, and taxonomic groups. At a global level, it is unclear which particular management attributes have greatest influence on the status of fished populations, and also unclear which external factors affect the overall success of fisheries management systems. We used expert surveys to characterize the management systems by species of 28 major fishing nations and examined influences of economic, geographic, and fishery-related factors. A Fisheries Management Index, which integrated research, management, enforcement, and socioeconomic attributes, showed wide variation among countries and was strongly affected by per capita gross domestic product (positively) and capacity-enhancing subsidies (negatively). Among 13 management attributes considered, three were particularly influential in whether stock size and fishing mortality are currently in or trending toward desirable states: extensiveness of stock assessments, strength of fishing pressure limits, and comprehensiveness of enforcement programs. These results support arguments that the key to successful fisheries management is the implementation and enforcement of science-based catch or effort limits, and that monetary investment into fisheries can help achieve management objectives if used to limit fishing pressure rather than enhance fishing capacity. Countries with currently less-effective management systems have the greatest potential for improving long-term stock status outcomes and should be the focus of efforts to improve fisheries management globally.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1609915114","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"178-183","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fisheries management impacts on target species status","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1609915114","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Melnychuk","given":"Michael C."},{"family":"Peterson","given":"Emily"},{"family":"Elliott","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",8,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. CC-BY. See data management report for the figure at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452953.

      Costello ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"acna7lpcae","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2012)","plainCitation":"(2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31424,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/55D9HWJC"],"itemData":{"id":31424,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1223389","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6106","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"517-520","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Status and Solutions for the World's Unassessed Fisheries","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1223389","volume":"338","author":[{"family":"Costello","given":"C."},{"family":"Ovando","given":"D."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"R."},{"family":"Gaines","given":"S. D."},{"family":"Deschenes","given":"O."},{"family":"Lester","given":"S. E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",10,26]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} attempted to estimate the status of the half of the world’s fisheries that are not scientifically assessed and combine this with the data from assessed stocks to provide a global estimate of status. They grouped stocks into four classes; (i) large assessed (large industrial fisheries of the world where a scientific assessment of status and trends is performed); (ii) large unassessed, (iii) small assessed and (iv) small unassessed stocks. The trends estimate showed that the large stocks, both assessed and unassessed, are on average about target levels, but small assessed stocks were declining and small unassessed stocks were well below target levels (Figure 3.19).
      

Figure 3.19 Trend estimates for global large and small stocks. The black lines are for stocks scientifically assessed and are generally the same stocks as used in Hilborn, 2020. The red lines are estimates of the trends for stocks not scientifically assessed. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1rqq0tp4ov","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Costello, 2012)","plainCitation":"(C. Costello, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31424,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/55D9HWJC"],"itemData":{"id":31424,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1223389","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6106","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"517-520","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Status and Solutions for the World's Unassessed Fisheries","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1223389","volume":"338","author":[{"family":"Costello","given":"C."},{"family":"Ovando","given":"D."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"R."},{"family":"Gaines","given":"S. D."},{"family":"Deschenes","given":"O."},{"family":"Lester","given":"S. E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",10,26]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} © 2012, American Association for the Advancement of Science. CC-BY NC.

      Rosenberg ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1hmuumnamq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2018)","plainCitation":"(2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31571,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VGPFMRNE"],"itemData":{"id":31571,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12363","ISSN":"1755263X","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"CONSERVATION LETTERS","language":"en","page":"e12363","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Applying a New Ensemble Approach to Estimating Stock Status of Marine Fisheries around the World: Estimating global fisheries status","title-short":"Applying a New Ensemble Approach to Estimating Stock Status of Marine Fisheries around the World","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/conl.12363","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Rosenberg","given":"Andrew A."},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin M."},{"family":"Afflerbach","given":"Jamie"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Sean C."},{"family":"Dickey-Collas","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Cooper","given":"Andrew B."},{"family":"Fogarty","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Fulton","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Gutiérrez","given":"Nicolás L."},{"family":"Hyde","given":"Kimberly J.W."},{"family":"Jardim","given":"Ernesto"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Olaf P."},{"family":"Kristiansen","given":"Trond"},{"family":"Longo","given":"Catherine"},{"family":"Minte-Vera","given":"Carolina V."},{"family":"Minto","given":"Cóilín"},{"family":"Mosqueira","given":"Iago"},{"family":"Osio","given":"Giacomo Chato"},{"family":"Ovando","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Selig","given":"Elizabeth R."},{"family":"Thorson","given":"James T."},{"family":"Walsh","given":"Jessica C."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Yimin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",8,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",1]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} combined four different methods (one being the Costello ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1dsv0ubs1m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2012)","plainCitation":"(2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31424,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/55D9HWJC"],"itemData":{"id":31424,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1223389","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6106","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"517-520","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Status and Solutions for the World's Unassessed Fisheries","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1223389","volume":"338","author":[{"family":"Costello","given":"C."},{"family":"Ovando","given":"D."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"R."},{"family":"Gaines","given":"S. D."},{"family":"Deschenes","given":"O."},{"family":"Lester","given":"S. E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",10,26]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}) to estimate the status of unassessed stocks using an approach called ensemble modelling (Figure 3.20). However, when the stock status was compared to the status for stocks that were scientifically assessed, the performance was rather poor and the ensemble method provided roughly similar status estimates both in regions where scientific assessment show stocks are in very poor shape such as the Mediterranean Sea, and also in regions where stocks are in very good shape such as the Northeast Pacific. Thus, we know the status of fish stocks which provide half of the world’s catch – largely from the temperate North, and do not know the status of the other half of the global catch - largely from Southeast Asia.
      Christensen ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ajkaao9kf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2014)","plainCitation":"(2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31570,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CCZ8BXL3"],"itemData":{"id":31570,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine ecology progress series","ISSN":"0171-8630","page":"155-166","title":"A century of fish biomass decline in the ocean","volume":"512","author":[{"family":"Christensen","given":"Villy"},{"family":"Coll","given":"Marta"},{"family":"Piroddi","given":"Chiara"},{"family":"Steenbeek","given":"Jeroen"},{"family":"Buszowski","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} examined 200 marine food web models covering the period 1880 to 2007 and compared the change in abundance of different trophic levels of fish. They estimated that high trophic level fish had declined by 2/3 (to roughly the level that would produce maximum sustainable yield) while the far more numerous low trophic level species would have more than doubled.
      

Figure 3.20 Estimation of the status of unassessed stocks by several data poor methods. Abbreviations: BMSY: Biomass that would support Maximum Sustainable Yield, CMSY:Catch Biomass that would support Maximum Sustainable Yield, COMSIR: catch-only-model with sampling-importance resampling, SSCOM: state-space catch-only model. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1pvne59mpr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rosenberg, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Rosenberg, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31571,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VGPFMRNE"],"itemData":{"id":31571,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12363","ISSN":"1755263X","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"CONSERVATION LETTERS","language":"en","page":"e12363","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Applying a New Ensemble Approach to Estimating Stock Status of Marine Fisheries around the World: Estimating global fisheries status","title-short":"Applying a New Ensemble Approach to Estimating Stock Status of Marine Fisheries around the World","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/conl.12363","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Rosenberg","given":"Andrew A."},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin M."},{"family":"Afflerbach","given":"Jamie"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Sean C."},{"family":"Dickey-Collas","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Cooper","given":"Andrew B."},{"family":"Fogarty","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Fulton","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Gutiérrez","given":"Nicolás L."},{"family":"Hyde","given":"Kimberly J.W."},{"family":"Jardim","given":"Ernesto"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Olaf P."},{"family":"Kristiansen","given":"Trond"},{"family":"Longo","given":"Catherine"},{"family":"Minte-Vera","given":"Carolina V."},{"family":"Minto","given":"Cóilín"},{"family":"Mosqueira","given":"Iago"},{"family":"Osio","given":"Giacomo Chato"},{"family":"Ovando","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Selig","given":"Elizabeth R."},{"family":"Thorson","given":"James T."},{"family":"Walsh","given":"Jessica C."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Yimin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",8,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY 4.0.

      The performance of marine fisheries in terms of providing food security can be measured by comparing levels of sustainable yield at the current fishing pressure and if people fished at rates that would provide maximum sustainable yield. This is only available for the assessed fish stocks of the world. The status of assessed stocks is maintained on-line in the RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a16tm474dbd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ricard, Minto, Jensen, & Baum, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Ricard, Minto, Jensen, & Baum, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30787,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DLTBUPQI"],"itemData":{"id":30787,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-2979.2011.00435.x","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"380-398","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Examining the knowledge base and status of commercially exploited marine species with the RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database: The RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database","title-short":"Examining the knowledge base and status of commercially exploited marine species with the RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1467-2979.2011.00435.x","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Ricard","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Minto","given":"Cóilín"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Olaf P"},{"family":"Baum","given":"Julia K"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Using the data from assessed stocks and calculation of lost yield ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XIRSmiyN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hilborn, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hilborn, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8756,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z4W7PFHG"],"itemData":{"id":8756,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsy138","issue":"1","page":"45-49","title":"Measuring fisheries performance using the “Goldilocks plot”","volume":"76","author":[{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} the figure below shows the amount of potential yield that is lost by fishing too hard (red), or too little (blue) and how much of the potential yield is achieved at current fishing pressures (blue). It is estimated that in 1950 when the data began, roughly half of the potential yield was lost by low fishing pressure and there was little loss from fishing too hard (overfishing). The loss from overfishing rose to between 10% and 20% during the 1980s and 1990s and has now declined to about 5%. Potential increase in yield by fishing harder is now about 17%, and across these stocks the current fishing pattern is achieving about 73% of potential yield (Figure 3.21). These calculations are based on the assumption that parameters that determine the productivity of fish stocks will remain unchanged at current estimated values. Note that fish production is not just a function of how hard people fish, but it depends on variable environmental conditions (temperature, food, ocean currents, etc.), including conditions affected by climate change.


Figure 3.21 The fraction of potential yield lost in each year by overfishing (red), and by fishing less than the Maximum Sustainable Yield (blue). Green shows the fraction of potential yield achieved at the fishing pressure for that year. CC-BY. See data management report for the figure at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6453019.

3.3.1.3. Status and trends in selected fisheries

As no satisfactory global reviews were found in the literature, significant effort was invested in a systematic review of small-scale fisheries because of their importance for local communities. Due to high variability, the review of marine and inland small-scale fisheries was made by geographic region (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Three other sections dedicated to distinct fisheries were also developed: (i) small to medium pelagic or forage fish fisheries that are mainly coastal and provide about 25% of world capture fisheries; (ii) tuna and tuna-like fisheries, which are of high economic value and are widely spatially distributed from coastal regions to the high seas; (iii) industrial demersal fisheries in coastal areas, which are a complex set of heterogeneous fishing fleets using diverse fishing gears active within the exclusive economic zones of coastal countries. When necessary, for taxonomic groups of special concern, we added information on their status and trends in dedicated boxes (e.g., Box 3.2).

Box 3.2 Status and trends of sharks, rays, and chimaeras: implications for species, the environment, and people 

There are approximately 1,250 species of sharks and rays found throughout the world’s marine, and some freshwater, habitats. Sharks and rays are relatively large-bodied predators and, hence, are both highly susceptible to a wide range of fishing gears (predominantly trawls, longlines, gill and tangle nets) and highly sensitive to fishing mortality because of their long generation lengths and low fecundity resulting in very low maximum population growth rates and low density-dependent compensation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a21jkll6fep","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Forrest & Walters, 2009; E. Gilman, 2008; Pardo, Kindsvater, Reynolds, & Dulvy, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Forrest & Walters, 2009; E. Gilman, 2008; Pardo, Kindsvater, Reynolds, & Dulvy, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31557,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AP95NA9Y"],"itemData":{"id":31557,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Canadian Journal of Fisheries and Aquatic Sciences","issue":"12","page":"2062–2080","title":"Estimating thresholds to optimal harvest rate for long-lived, low-fecundity sharks accounting for selectivity and density dependence in recruitment","volume":"66","author":[{"family":"Forrest","given":"Robyn E"},{"family":"Walters","given":"Carl J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":9871,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8ZXZX9W6","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HI3DFVIR"],"itemData":{"id":9871,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2007.05.001","ISSN":"0308597X","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"1-18","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Shark interactions in pelagic longline fisheries","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X07000565","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Gilman","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Clarke","given":"Shelley"},{"family":"Brothers","given":"Nigel"},{"family":"Alfaro-Shigueto","given":"Joanna"},{"family":"Mandelman","given":"John"},{"family":"Mangel","given":"Jeff"},{"family":"Petersen","given":"Samantha"},{"family":"Piovano","given":"Susanna"},{"family":"Thomson","given":"Nicola"},{"family":"Dalzell","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Donoso","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Goren","given":"Meidad"},{"family":"Werner","given":"Tim"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",1]]}}},{"id":31554,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4J4IRWPQ"],"itemData":{"id":31554,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Canadian journal of fisheries and aquatic sciences","DOI":"10.1139/cjfas-2016-0069","issue":"8","note":"publisher: NRC Research Press","page":"1159–1163","title":"Maximum intrinsic rate of population increase in sharks, rays, and chimaeras: the importance of survival to maturity","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Pardo","given":"Sebastián A"},{"family":"Kindsvater","given":"Holly K"},{"family":"Reynolds","given":"John D"},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Consequently, they are highly vulnerable to overfishing compared to the teleost fishes they are caught alongside and are particularly prone to disappearing prior to adequate monitoring ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a7l4bmj5hd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Myers & Worm, 2005; Yan, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Myers & Worm, 2005; Yan, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31555,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DIKWIHEF"],"itemData":{"id":31555,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences","DOI":"10.1098/rstb.2004.1573","issue":"1453","note":"publisher: The Royal Society","page":"13–20","title":"Extinction, survival or recovery of large predatory fishes","volume":"360","author":[{"family":"Myers","given":"Ransom A"},{"family":"Worm","given":"Boris"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":26774,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5WPRBIS7","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MVFYB24S"],"itemData":{"id":26774,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Extinctions on land are often inferred from sparse sightings over time, but this technique is ill-suited for wide-ranging species. We develop a space-for-time approach to track the spatial contraction and drivers of decline of sawfishes. These iconic and endangered shark-like rays were once found in warm, coastal waters of 90 nations and are now presumed extinct in more than half (\\n              n\\n              = 46). Using dynamic geography theory, we predict that sawfishes are gone from at least nine additional nations. Overfishing and habitat loss have reduced spatial occupancy, leading to local extinctions in 55 of the 90 nations, which equates to 58.7% of their historical distribution. Retention bans and habitat protections are urgently necessary to secure a future for sawfishes and similar species.","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.abb6026","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Sci. Adv.","language":"en","page":"eabb6026","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Overfishing and habitat loss drive range contraction of iconic marine fishes to near extinction","URL":"https://advances.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/sciadv.abb6026","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Yan","given":"Helen F."},{"family":"Kyne","given":"Peter M."},{"family":"Jabado","given":"Rima W."},{"family":"Leeney","given":"Ruth H."},{"family":"Davidson","given":"Lindsay N.K."},{"family":"Derrick","given":"Danielle H."},{"family":"Finucci","given":"Brittany"},{"family":"Freckleton","given":"Robert P."},{"family":"Fordham","given":"Sonja V."},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Global shark and ray catches reported to FAO rose to a peak in 2003 and declined at least 17% thereafter, likely due to overfishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1kmdn13jo2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Davidson, Krawchuk, & Dulvy, 2016; Dent & Clarke, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Davidson, Krawchuk, & Dulvy, 2016; Dent & Clarke, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31565,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KQZH6VME"],"itemData":{"id":31565,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000382495500008","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12119","ISSN":"1467-2960","issue":"2","page":"438-458","title":"Why have global shark and ray landings declined: improved management or overfishing?","URL":"://WOS:000382495500008","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Davidson","given":"L. N. K."},{"family":"Krawchuk","given":"M. A."},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"N. K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",6]]}}},{"id":31546,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BNQWH6PP"],"itemData":{"id":31546,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"FAO. Rome","title":"State of the global market for shark products. FAO Fisheries and Aquaculture, Technical Paper no. 590","author":[{"family":"Dent","given":"F"},{"family":"Clarke","given":"S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the global catch is underestimated and is likely to be two-to-four times greater ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aah7dln2pj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Clarke, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Clarke, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31547,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GLJ6P2SL"],"itemData":{"id":31547,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology letters","DOI":"10.1111/j.1461-0248.2006.00968.x","issue":"10","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"1115–1126","title":"Global estimates of shark catches using trade records from commercial markets","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Clarke","given":"Shelley C"},{"family":"McAllister","given":"Murdoch K"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"Eleanor J"},{"family":"Kirkwood","given":"GP"},{"family":"Michielsens","given":"Catherine GJ"},{"family":"Agnew","given":"David J"},{"family":"Pikitch","given":"Ellen K"},{"family":"Nakano","given":"Hideki"},{"family":"Shivji","given":"Mahmood S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Based on these FAO data and accounting for discards and illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing, it is possible that 63–273 million individuals were captured in the early 2000s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1eqii0ff2g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Boris Worm, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Boris Worm, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31577,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GYV37DHS"],"itemData":{"id":31577,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Adequate conservation and management of shark populations is becoming increasingly important on a global scale, especially because many species are exceptionally vulnerable to over?shing. Yet, reported catch statistics for sharks are incomplete, and mortality estimates have not been available for sharks as a group. Here, the global catch and mortality of sharks from reported and unreported landings, discards, and shark ?nning are being estimated at 1.44 million metric tons for the year 2000, and at only slightly less in 2010 (1.41 million tons). Based on an analysis of average shark weights, this translates into a total annual mortality estimate of about 100 million sharks in 2000, and about 97 million sharks in 2010, with a total range of possible values between 63 and 273 million sharks per year. Further, the exploitation rate for sharks as a group was calculated by dividing two independent mortality estimates by an estimate of total global biomass. As an alternative approach, exploitation rates for individual shark populations were compiled and averaged from stock assessments and other published sources. The resulting three independent estimates of the average exploitation rate ranged between 6.4% and 7.9% of sharks killed per year. This exceeds the average rebound rate for many shark populations, estimated from the life history information on 62 shark species (rebound rates averaged 4.9% per year), and explains the ongoing declines in most populations for which data exist. The consequences of these unsustainable catch and mortality rates for marine ecosystems could be substantial. Global total shark mortality, therefore, needs to be reduced drastically in order to rebuild depleted populations and restore marine ecosystems with functional top predators.","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2012.12.034","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"194-204","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Global catches, exploitation rates, and rebuilding options for sharks","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X13000055","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Worm","given":"Boris"},{"family":"Davis","given":"Brendal"},{"family":"Kettemer","given":"Lisa"},{"family":"Ward-Paige","given":"Christine A."},{"family":"Chapman","given":"Demian"},{"family":"Heithaus","given":"Michael R."},{"family":"Kessel","given":"Steven T."},{"family":"Gruber","given":"Samuel H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Only 4% of the global estimated catch is managed sustainably, based on 65 fisheries stock assessments from 47 species from Canada, United States of America, Australia, and New Zealand ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a126io2o31s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Simpfendorfer & Dulvy, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Simpfendorfer & Dulvy, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1229,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/96T7N8AC"],"itemData":{"id":1229,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Biology","DOI":"10.1016/j.cub.2016.12.017","ISSN":"09609822","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Current Biology","language":"en","page":"R97-R98","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Bright spots of sustainable shark fishing","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0960982216314646","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Simpfendorfer","given":"Colin A."},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Catch estimates of unassessed data-poor fisheries show that large coastal sharks have been very unsustainably fished since 1975 (B/Bmsy - Biomass relative to the biomass that produces Maximum Sustainable Yield < 0.5) (ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a6upe5u0i2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"\\\\uldash{(Costello, 2012)}","plainCitation":"(Costello, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31424,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/55D9HWJC"],"itemData":{"id":31424,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1223389","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6106","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"517-520","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Status and Solutions for the World's Unassessed Fisheries","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1223389","volume":"338","author":[{"family":"Costello","given":"C."},{"family":"Ovando","given":"D."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"R."},{"family":"Gaines","given":"S. D."},{"family":"Deschenes","given":"O."},{"family":"Lester","given":"S. E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",10,26]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Consequently, steep regional declines of coastal sharks have been documented ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a185rf7123","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ferretti, Osio, Jenkins, Rosenberg, & Lotze, 2013; MacNeil, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Ferretti, Osio, Jenkins, Rosenberg, & Lotze, 2013; MacNeil, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31558,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K8GMPN9G"],"itemData":{"id":31558,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep01057","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"1–11","title":"Long-term change in a meso-predator community in response to prolonged and heterogeneous human impact","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Ferretti","given":"Francesco"},{"family":"Osio","given":"Giacomo C"},{"family":"Jenkins","given":"Chris J"},{"family":"Rosenberg","given":"Andrew A"},{"family":"Lotze","given":"Heike K"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":31549,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7KWL8ITQ"],"itemData":{"id":31549,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-020-2519-y","issue":"7818","note":"publisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"801–806","title":"Global status and conservation potential of reef sharks","volume":"583","author":[{"family":"MacNeil","given":"M Aaron"},{"family":"Chapman","given":"Demian D"},{"family":"Heupel","given":"Michelle"},{"family":"Simpfendorfer","given":"Colin A"},{"family":"Heithaus","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Meekan","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Harvey","given":"Euan"},{"family":"Goetze","given":"Jordan"},{"family":"Kiszka","given":"Jeremy"},{"family":"Bond","given":"Mark E"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Oceanic sharks and rays have limited spatial refuge from fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ah6lo8dqrr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Queiroz, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Queiroz, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31553,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BEMPI7YF"],"itemData":{"id":31553,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-019-1444-4","issue":"7770","note":"publisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"461–466","title":"Global spatial risk assessment of sharks under the footprint of fisheries","volume":"572","author":[{"family":"Queiroz","given":"Nuno"},{"family":"Humphries","given":"Nicolas E"},{"family":"Couto","given":"Ana"},{"family":"Vedor","given":"Marisa"},{"family":"Da Costa","given":"Ivo"},{"family":"Sequeira","given":"Ana MM"},{"family":"Mucientes","given":"Gonzalo"},{"family":"Santos","given":"António M"},{"family":"Abascal","given":"Francisco J"},{"family":"Abercrombie","given":"Debra L"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and declined by 71% since 1970 due to an 18-fold increase in relative fishing pressure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1p4u6gusvu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pacoureau, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Pacoureau, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31564,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KZDH9PEF"],"itemData":{"id":31564,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-020-03173-9","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7843","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"567-571","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Half a century of global decline in oceanic sharks and rays","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-03173-9","volume":"589","author":[{"family":"Pacoureau","given":"Nathan"},{"family":"Rigby","given":"Cassandra L."},{"family":"Kyne","given":"Peter M."},{"family":"Sherley","given":"Richard B."},{"family":"Winker","given":"Henning"},{"family":"Carlson","given":"John K."},{"family":"Fordham","given":"Sonja V."},{"family":"Barreto","given":"Rodrigo"},{"family":"Fernando","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Francis","given":"Malcolm P."},{"family":"Jabado","given":"Rima W."},{"family":"Herman","given":"Katelyn B."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Kwang-Ming"},{"family":"Marshall","given":"Andrea D."},{"family":"Pollom","given":"Riley A."},{"family":"Romanov","given":"Evgeny V."},{"family":"Simpfendorfer","given":"Colin A."},{"family":"Yin","given":"Jamie S."},{"family":"Kindsvater","given":"Holly K."},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,28]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Sharks and rays from the tropical and subtropical coastal seas are currently at higher risk ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"buRTi0ZQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dulvy, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Dulvy, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31552,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NKGIABAH"],"itemData":{"id":31552,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Biology","DOI":"10.1016/j.cub.2021.08.062","issue":"21","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"4773–4787","title":"Overfishing drives over one-third of all sharks and rays toward a global extinction crisis","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K"},{"family":"Pacoureau","given":"Nathan"},{"family":"Rigby","given":"Cassandra L"},{"family":"Pollom","given":"Riley A"},{"family":"Jabado","given":"Rima W"},{"family":"Ebert","given":"David A"},{"family":"Finucci","given":"Brittany"},{"family":"Pollock","given":"Caroline M"},{"family":"Cheok","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Derrick","given":"Danielle H"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List provides a framework for integrating disparate data sources ranging from historical ecology, to catch data and stock assessments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a8mm20qvd6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN Standards and Petitions Committee, 2019; Mace, 2008; Sherley, 2020, p. 20)","plainCitation":"(IUCN Standards and Petitions Committee, 2019; Mace, 2008; Sherley, 2020, p. 20)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31576,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RK7PK87I"],"itemData":{"id":31576,"type":"document","publisher":"IUCN Standards and Petitions Committee","title":"Guidelines for Using the IUCN Red List Categories and Criteria. Version 14","URL":"http://www.iucnredlist.org/documents/RedListGuidelines.pdf","author":[{"family":"IUCN Standards and Petitions Committee","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":31550,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7CFNDMFD"],"itemData":{"id":31550,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2008.01044.x","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"1424–1442","title":"Quantification of extinction risk: IUCN's system for classifying threatened species","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Mace","given":"Georgina M"},{"family":"Collar","given":"Nigel J"},{"family":"Gaston","given":"Kevin J"},{"family":"Hilton-Taylor","given":"CRAIG"},{"family":"Akçakaya","given":"H Resit"},{"family":"Leader-Williams","given":"NIGEL"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"Eleanor Jane"},{"family":"Stuart","given":"Simon N"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":31560,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/23WD4PWG"],"itemData":{"id":31560,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12688","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"e12688","title":"Estimating IUCN Red List population reduction: JARA—a decision-support tool applied to pelagic sharks","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Sherley","given":"Richard B"},{"family":"Winker","given":"Henning"},{"family":"Rigby","given":"Cassandra L"},{"family":"Kyne","given":"Peter M"},{"family":"Pollom","given":"Riley"},{"family":"Pacoureau","given":"Nathan"},{"family":"Herman","given":"Katelyn"},{"family":"Carlson","given":"John K"},{"family":"Yin","given":"Jamie S"},{"family":"Kindsvater","given":"Holly K"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"locator":"20"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These comprehensive global assessments of sharks and rays offer a unique opportunity to calculate Living Plant and Red List indices to track progress toward the Convention on Biological Diversity and Sustainable Development Goals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a16ut0rmo9o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pacoureau, 2021; Walls & Dulvy, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Pacoureau, 2021; Walls & Dulvy, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31564,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KZDH9PEF"],"itemData":{"id":31564,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-020-03173-9","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7843","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"567-571","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Half a century of global decline in oceanic sharks and rays","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-03173-9","volume":"589","author":[{"family":"Pacoureau","given":"Nathan"},{"family":"Rigby","given":"Cassandra L."},{"family":"Kyne","given":"Peter M."},{"family":"Sherley","given":"Richard B."},{"family":"Winker","given":"Henning"},{"family":"Carlson","given":"John K."},{"family":"Fordham","given":"Sonja V."},{"family":"Barreto","given":"Rodrigo"},{"family":"Fernando","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Francis","given":"Malcolm P."},{"family":"Jabado","given":"Rima W."},{"family":"Herman","given":"Katelyn B."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Kwang-Ming"},{"family":"Marshall","given":"Andrea D."},{"family":"Pollom","given":"Riley A."},{"family":"Romanov","given":"Evgeny V."},{"family":"Simpfendorfer","given":"Colin A."},{"family":"Yin","given":"Jamie S."},{"family":"Kindsvater","given":"Holly K."},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,28]]}}},{"id":31559,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7XP8CEVM"],"itemData":{"id":31559,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific reports","DOI":"10.1038/s41598-021-94632-4","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"1–15","title":"Tracking the rising extinction risk of sharks and rays in the Northeast Atlantic Ocean and Mediterranean Sea","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Walls","given":"Rachel HL"},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Shark and ray extinction risk has been rising over the past half century (Pacoureau, 2021, Walls and Dulvy, 2021). Now, one one-third (391 of 1,199; 32.5%) of sharks and rays are classified as threatened (vulnerable, endangered, or critically endangered) (Dulvy, 2021). Assuming the 155 data deficient species are threatened in the same proportion to the other species then an estimated 449 species are threatened (37.5%, range 32.6–45.5%). Three species are critically endangered (possibly extinct), because they have not been recorded for over 80 years but there have been insufficient surveys to confirm their extinction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GWnp3hPE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dulvy, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Dulvy, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31552,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NKGIABAH"],"itemData":{"id":31552,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Biology","DOI":"10.1016/j.cub.2021.08.062","issue":"21","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"4773–4787","title":"Overfishing drives over one-third of all sharks and rays toward a global extinction crisis","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K"},{"family":"Pacoureau","given":"Nathan"},{"family":"Rigby","given":"Cassandra L"},{"family":"Pollom","given":"Riley A"},{"family":"Jabado","given":"Rima W"},{"family":"Ebert","given":"David A"},{"family":"Finucci","given":"Brittany"},{"family":"Pollock","given":"Caroline M"},{"family":"Cheok","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Derrick","given":"Danielle H"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A further eight species are regionally extinct in one or more countries and there have been at least 28 local extinctions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a18e3ds407q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dulvy, 2014, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Dulvy, 2014, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31574,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E8X5V6CI"],"itemData":{"id":31574,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The rapid expansion of human activities threatens ocean-wide biodiversity. Numerous marine animal populations have declined, yet it remains unclear whether these trends are symptomatic of a chronic accumulation of global marine extinction risk. We present the first systematic analysis of threat for a globally distributed lineage of 1,041 chondrichthyan fishes—sharks, rays, and chimaeras. We estimate that one-quarter are threatened according to IUCN Red List criteria due to overfishing (targeted and incidental). Large-bodied, shallow-water species are at greatest risk and five out of the seven most threatened families are rays. Overall chondrichthyan extinction risk is substantially higher than for most other vertebrates, and only one-third of species are considered safe. Population depletion has occurred throughout the world’s ice-free waters, but is particularly prevalent in the Indo-Pacific Biodiversity Triangle and Mediterranean Sea. Improved management of fisheries and trade is urgently needed to avoid extinctions and promote population recovery.\\n          , \\n            Ocean ecosystems are under pressure from overfishing, climate change, habitat destruction and pollution. These pressures have led to documented declines of some fishes in some places, such as those living in coral reefs and on the high seas. However, it is not clear whether these population declines are isolated one-off examples or, instead, if they are sufficiently widespread to risk the extinction of large numbers of species.\\n            Most fishes have a skeleton that is made of bone, but sharks and rays have a skeleton that is made of cartilage. A total of 1,041 species has such a skeleton and they are collectively known as the Chondrichthyes. To find out how well these fish are faring, Dulvy worked with more than 300 scientists around the world to assess the conservation status of all 1,041 species.\\n            Based on this, Dulvy estimate that one in four of these species are threatened with extinction, mainly as a result of overfishing. Moreover, just 389 species (37.4% of the total) are considered to be safe, which is the lowest fraction of safe species among all vertebrate groups studied to date.\\n            The largest sharks and rays are in the most peril, especially those living in shallow waters that are accessible to fisheries. A particular problem is the ‘fin trade’: the fins of sharks and shark-like rays are a delicacy in some Asian countries, and more than half of the chondrichthyans that enter the fin trade are under threat. Whether targeted or caught by boats fishing for other species, sharks and rays are used to supply a market that is largely unmonitored and unregulated. Habitat degradation and loss also pose considerable threats, particularly for freshwater sharks and rays.\\n            Dulvy identified three main hotspots where the biodiversity of sharks and rays was particularly seriously threatened—the Indo-Pacific Biodiversity Triangle, Red Sea, and the Mediterranean Sea—and argue that national and international action is needed to protect them from overfishing.","container-title":"eLife","DOI":"10.7554/eLife.00590","ISSN":"2050-084X","language":"en","page":"e00590","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Extinction risk and conservation of the world’s sharks and rays","URL":"https://elifesciences.org/articles/00590","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K"},{"family":"Fowler","given":"Sarah L"},{"family":"Musick","given":"John A"},{"family":"Cavanagh","given":"Rachel D"},{"family":"Kyne","given":"Peter M"},{"family":"Harrison","given":"Lucy R"},{"family":"Carlson","given":"John K"},{"family":"Davidson","given":"Lindsay NK"},{"family":"Fordham","given":"Sonja V"},{"family":"Francis","given":"Malcolm P"},{"family":"Pollock","given":"Caroline M"},{"family":"Simpfendorfer","given":"Colin A"},{"family":"Burgess","given":"George H"},{"family":"Carpenter","given":"Kent E"},{"family":"Compagno","given":"Leonard JV"},{"family":"Ebert","given":"David A"},{"family":"Gibson","given":"Claudine"},{"family":"Heupel","given":"Michelle R"},{"family":"Livingstone","given":"Suzanne R"},{"family":"Sanciangco","given":"Jonnell C"},{"family":"Stevens","given":"John D"},{"family":"Valenti","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"White","given":"William T"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",1,21]]}}},{"id":31552,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NKGIABAH"],"itemData":{"id":31552,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Biology","DOI":"10.1016/j.cub.2021.08.062","issue":"21","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"4773–4787","title":"Overfishing drives over one-third of all sharks and rays toward a global extinction crisis","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K"},{"family":"Pacoureau","given":"Nathan"},{"family":"Rigby","given":"Cassandra L"},{"family":"Pollom","given":"Riley A"},{"family":"Jabado","given":"Rima W"},{"family":"Ebert","given":"David A"},{"family":"Finucci","given":"Brittany"},{"family":"Pollock","given":"Caroline M"},{"family":"Cheok","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Derrick","given":"Danielle H"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The shark and ray extinction rate of 25 E/MSY (extinction per million spesies-year) is 25–250 times greater than the background fossil record extinction rate and 2.5 times greater than the proposed target rate of 10 E/MSY (extinction per million spesies-year) over the next century ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1r30hs3c13","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. D. Rounsevell, 2020)","plainCitation":"(M. D. Rounsevell, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31561,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QCMKV2HB"],"itemData":{"id":31561,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aba6592","issue":"6496","note":"publisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science","page":"1193–1195","title":"A biodiversity target based on species extinctions","volume":"368","author":[{"family":"Rounsevell","given":"Mark DA"},{"family":"Harfoot","given":"Mike"},{"family":"Harrison","given":"Paula A"},{"family":"Newbold","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Gregory","given":"Richard D"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Georgina M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nearly all (99.6%) species are taken incidentally, but are valuable and are retained for food: half (51.5%) for human consumption of the meat only, with remaining species used for food in combination with the production of animal feed, skins, and liver oil ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c4LgbvcI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dulvy, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Dulvy, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31552,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NKGIABAH"],"itemData":{"id":31552,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Biology","DOI":"10.1016/j.cub.2021.08.062","issue":"21","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"4773–4787","title":"Overfishing drives over one-third of all sharks and rays toward a global extinction crisis","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K"},{"family":"Pacoureau","given":"Nathan"},{"family":"Rigby","given":"Cassandra L"},{"family":"Pollom","given":"Riley A"},{"family":"Jabado","given":"Rima W"},{"family":"Ebert","given":"David A"},{"family":"Finucci","given":"Brittany"},{"family":"Pollock","given":"Caroline M"},{"family":"Cheok","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Derrick","given":"Danielle H"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The International Union for Conservation of Nature classification scheme does not record shark and ray fins or devil ray gill plates (Mobulidae), but these significant trades are subject to increasing international regulation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aclpd18v12","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carde\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}osa, Quinlan, Shea, & Chapman, 2018; Friedman, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Cardeñosa, Quinlan, Shea, & Chapman, 2018; Friedman, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GUN89YGK"],"itemData":{"id":733,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) is a multilateral environmental agreement to ensure that the international trade of threatened species is either prohibited (Appendix I listed species) or being conducted legally, sustainably, and transparently (Appendix II listed species). Twelve threatened shark species exploited for their fins, meat, and other products have been listed under CITES Appendix II. Sharks are often traded in high volumes, some of their products are visually indistinguishable, and most importing/exporting nations have limited capacity to detect illicit trade and enforce the regulations. High volume shipments often must be screened after only a short period of detainment (e.g., a maximum of 24?hours), which together with costs and capacity issues have limited the use of DNA approaches to identify illicit trade. Here, we present a reliable, field-based, fast (<4?hours), and cost effective ($0.94 USD per sample) multiplex real-time PCR protocol capable of detecting nine of the twelve sharks listed under CITES in a single reaction. This approach facilitates detection of illicit trade, with positive results providing probable cause to detain shipments for more robust forensic analysis. We also provide evidence of its application in real law enforcement scenarios in Hong Kong. Adoption of this approach can help parties meet their CITES requirements, avoiding potential international trade sanctions in the future.","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/s41598-018-34663-6","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"2018 The Author(s)","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"16313","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Multiplex real-time PCR assay to detect illegal trade of CITES-listed shark species","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-018-34663-6","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Cardeñosa","given":"Diego"},{"family":"Quinlan","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Shea","given":"Kwok Ho"},{"family":"Chapman","given":"Demian D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,5]]}}},{"id":31575,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LAT6GN7L"],"itemData":{"id":31575,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"CITES (the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora) aims to ensure that international trade in specimens of wild animals and plants does not threaten their survival. However, measuring the effectiveness and impacts of these trade regulations for commercially exploited aquatic species remains challenging. This study highlights observed or documented changes in elasmobranch fisheries in eight Southeast Asian countries before and after the listing of sharks and rays in CITES’ Appendix II, and the influence of CITES across five pillars or sectors of a “fishery assessment framework” developed especially for this purpose. Fisheries experts reported change was most common in the “governance” (e.g., policy, regulation and compliance) and “fisher(y)” sectors (e.g., structure and effort) of the assessment framework. The smallest change was recorded in “markets” (e.g., structures and prices) and “sociocultural” sectors (e.g., consumption, livelihoods and community awareness). Overall, the study demonstrates a measurable, albeit small, mostly positive influence of CITES in five of eight countries, while noting predominantly negative influences across two, and ongoing challenges for all in maintaining legal trade of these CITES-listed species. The study concludes by offering guidance on future needs: most notably, more effort for long-term collection of fundamental fisher-, stock- and market-related data to inform adaptive management and facilitation of legal trade where it is shown to be sustainable. Furthermore, as many of the shark and ray species under CITES provisions are transboundary stocks, increased support for communication and cooperation among regional fishery stakeholders is an ongoing need.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/faf.12281","ISSN":"1467-2979","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"© 2018 John Wiley & Sons Ltd","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/faf.12281","page":"662-676","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Examining the impact of CITES listing of sharks and rays in Southeast Asian fisheries","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/faf.12281","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Friedman","given":"K."},{"family":"Gabriel","given":"S."},{"family":"Abe","given":"O."},{"family":"Nuruddin","given":"A. Adnan"},{"family":"Ali","given":"A."},{"family":"Hassan","given":"R. Bidin Raja"},{"family":"Cadrin","given":"S. X."},{"family":"Cornish","given":"A."},{"family":"Meulenaer","given":"T. De"},{"family":"Dharmadi","given":""},{"family":"Fahmi","given":""},{"family":"Anh","given":"L. Huu Tuan"},{"family":"Kachelriess","given":"D."},{"family":"Kissol","given":"L."},{"family":"Krajangdara","given":"T."},{"family":"Wahab","given":"A. Rahman"},{"family":"Tanoue","given":"W."},{"family":"Tharith","given":"C."},{"family":"Torres","given":"F."},{"family":"Wanchana","given":"W."},{"family":"Win","given":"S."},{"family":"Yokawa","given":"K."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Y."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The global value of the shark and ray trade is worth 4.1 billion United States dollars, with the meat trade (2.6 billion United States dollars) exceeding the value of the global fin trade (1.5 billion United States dollars) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1nt5pqda67","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Niederm\\\\uc0\\\\u252{}ller, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Niedermüller, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31562,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VEY3MIW3"],"itemData":{"id":31562,"type":"report","publisher":"World Wildlife Fund.","title":"The shark and ray meat network: a deep dive into a global affair","author":[{"family":"Niedermüller","given":"S"},{"family":"Ainsworth","given":"G"},{"family":"Juan","given":"S","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Garcia","given":"R"},{"family":"Ospina-Alvarez","given":"A"},{"family":"Pita","given":"P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Widespread overfishing of sharks and rays will likely have profound consequences for the environment and people. The depletion and loss of sharks and rays, particularly in the tropics, does not bode well for the livelihoods of many coastal human populations, dependent on their meat and products for food and income ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"avgl3iolh9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(H. Booth, Squires, & Milner-Gulland, 2019; Seidu, 2022)","plainCitation":"(H. Booth, Squires, & Milner-Gulland, 2019; Seidu, 2022)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VTPHYY55"],"itemData":{"id":31548,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"104994","title":"The neglected complexities of shark fisheries, and priorities for holistic risk-based management","volume":"182","author":[{"family":"Booth","given":"Hollie"},{"family":"Squires","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"EJ"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":31563,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2TFTJWAE"],"itemData":{"id":31563,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2021.106157","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"106157","title":"Fishing for survival: importance of shark fisheries for the livelihoods of coastal communities in Western Ghana","volume":"246","author":[{"family":"Seidu","given":"Issah"},{"family":"Brobbey","given":"Lawrence K"},{"family":"Danquah","given":"Emmanuel"},{"family":"Oppong","given":"Samuel K"},{"family":"Beuningen","given":"David","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Seidu","given":"Moro"},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2022"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indeed, the depletion of sharks and rays reflects increasing evidence that the target teleost fisheries are overfished in South America, Africa, and Southeast Asia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2mmmnmckcc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dyhia Belhabib, Greer, & Pauly, 2018; V. W. Lam & Pauly, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Dyhia Belhabib, Greer, & Pauly, 2018; V. W. Lam & Pauly, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9797,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4QTF5WQY","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NIYKV52V"],"itemData":{"id":9797,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12360","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"e12360","title":"Trends in industrial and artisanal catch per effort in West African fisheries","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Belhabib","given":"Dyhia"},{"family":"Greer","given":"Krista"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":31556,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/794S9JIQ"],"itemData":{"id":31556,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Deep Sea Research Part II: Topical Studies in Oceanography","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"57–64","title":"Status of fisheries in 13 Asian large marine ecosystems","volume":"163","author":[{"family":"Lam","given":"Vicky WY"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

3.3.1.4. Small-scale fisheries

Small-scale fisheries are strongly anchored in local communities where fisheries represent a way of life ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1d0eoa3jlh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2015)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33286,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DEN3PTYZ"],"itemData":{"id":33286,"type":"book","abstract":"The Voluntary Guidelines for Securing Sustainable Small-Scale Fisheries in the Context of Food Security and Poverty Eradication (SSF Guidelines) represent the first ever internationl instrument dedicated to small-scale fisheries. They represent a global consensus on principles and guidance for small-scale fisheries governance and development. They were developed for small-scale fisheries in close collaboration with representatives of small-scale fisheries organizations in a participatory process between 2011-13, involving over 4000 stakeholders; facilitated by FAO, based on a mandate by COFI. They are directed at all those involved in the sector and intend to guide and encourage governments, fishing communities and other stakeholders to work together and ensure secure and sustainable small-scale fisheries for the benefit of small-scale fishers, fish workers and their communities as well as for society at large. They complement existing internatinoal instruments, such as the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries, the VG Tenure and the Right to Food Guidelines. Underpinned by a human rights approach, the SSF Guidelines represent a critical instrument to empower small-scale fishing communities - including vulnerable and marginalized groups - to participate in decision-making processes, and to assume responsibilities for sustainable use of fishery resources. The SSF Guidelines are already referred to in a number of ongoing policy processes (Committee on Global Food Security: Principles for responsible investment in agriculture and food systems 41st CFS recommendations; NEPAD's policy framework and reform strategy for fisheries and aquaculture in Africa; Western Central Atlantic Fishery Commission (WECAFC), Resolution WECAFC/15/2014/8)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-108704-6","language":"eng","number-of-pages":"18","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"K10plus ISBN","title":"Voluntary guidelines for securing sustainable small-scale fisheries in the context of food security and poverty eradication","editor":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Despite their importance, small-scale fisheries around the world are facing major challenges from the effects of global change, e.g., climate change, urbanization, industrialization, aquaculture intensification, and large-scale fisheries  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"adnmqv4mup","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2015; Chuenpagdee, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2015; Chuenpagdee, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34544,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TT2B3WNR"],"itemData":{"id":34544,"type":"book","call-number":"HT391 .B447 2015","edition":"1 Edition","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-1-138-77980-8","number-of-pages":"372","publisher":"Routledge, Taylor & Francis Group","publisher-place":"New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Coasts for people: interdisciplinary approaches to coastal and marine resource management","title-short":"Coasts for people","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":31545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6PD3RZ3G"],"itemData":{"id":31545,"type":"chapter","container-title":"World small-scale fisheries: contemporary visions","event-place":"Delft","ISBN":"978-90-5972-539-3","language":"eng","note":"OCLC: 934850466","page":"383–394","publisher":"Eburon Academic Publishers","publisher-place":"Delft","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Too big to ignore: global research network for the future of small-scale fisheries","editor":[{"family":"Chuenpagdee","given":"Ratana"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ongoing threats to small-scale fisheries affect entire production systems (harvest, processing, retail and transport) and create vulnerabilities that have no easy solution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1k6q7ij51j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chuenpagdee, 2011; Jentoft & Chuenpagdee, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Chuenpagdee, 2011; Jentoft & Chuenpagdee, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6PD3RZ3G"],"itemData":{"id":31545,"type":"chapter","container-title":"World small-scale fisheries: contemporary visions","event-place":"Delft","ISBN":"978-90-5972-539-3","language":"eng","note":"OCLC: 934850466","page":"383–394","publisher":"Eburon Academic Publishers","publisher-place":"Delft","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Too big to ignore: global research network for the future of small-scale fisheries","editor":[{"family":"Chuenpagdee","given":"Ratana"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":31542,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J79MJTUZ"],"itemData":{"id":31542,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2008.12.002","ISSN":"0308597X","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"553-560","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fisheries and coastal governance as a wicked problem","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X08001917","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Jentoft","given":"Svein"},{"family":"Chuenpagdee","given":"Ratana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In many cases, these challenges have placed the livelihoods, economy, food security, values and identity, and the viability of small-scale fisheries communities at risk ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aol2jdlhue","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bavinck, Jentoft, & Scholtens, 2018; Bundy, 2016; Jentoft & Chuenpagdee, 2015; Jentoft & Eide, 2011; Nayak & Armitage, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Bavinck, Jentoft, & Scholtens, 2018; Bundy, 2016; Jentoft & Chuenpagdee, 2015; Jentoft & Eide, 2011; Nayak & Armitage, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31569,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XAWEZNM5"],"itemData":{"id":31569,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Many social scientists in the field of fisheries display a strong concern for the social engineering of environmental sustainability, but also a tendency to identify with the concerns of government. This paper posits that social scientists have their own responsibility in the fisheries field, and that this responsibility includes more attention to the realm of social struggle and distributional justice. Social struggles within and over fisheries are argued to be globally intensifying, as a result of four trends: (1) the condition that inshore fisheries have now largely become a zero sum game; (2) the new sets of controls that are occurring in the fish value chain; (3) the incursion of new business interests into marine and coastal space; and (4) the increasing participation, if not interference, by governments in what used to be mainly fisher affairs. Not only does a reinvigorated social science agenda create attention to other, neglected domains of fisher society; the authors argue that addressing distributional justice concerns may be a precondition for achieving sustainable human-nature relations.","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2018.04.026","ISSN":"0308-597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"46-52","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Fisheries as social struggle: A reinvigorated social science research agenda","title-short":"Fisheries as social struggle","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308597X17309028","volume":"94","author":[{"family":"Bavinck","given":"Maarten"},{"family":"Jentoft","given":"Svein"},{"family":"Scholtens","given":"Joeri"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",8,1]]}}},{"id":31541,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7IH7DDSX"],"itemData":{"id":31541,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12110","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"1183-1193","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A decision support tool for response to global change in marine systems: the IMBER-ADApT Framework","title-short":"A decision support tool for response to global change in marine systems","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/faf.12110","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Bundy","given":"Alida"},{"family":"Chuenpagdee","given":"Ratana"},{"family":"Cooley","given":"Sarah R"},{"family":"Defeo","given":"Omar"},{"family":"Glaeser","given":"Bernhard"},{"family":"Guillotreau","given":"Patrice"},{"family":"Isaacs","given":"Moenieba"},{"family":"Mitsutaku","given":"Makino"},{"family":"Perry","given":"R Ian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}},{"id":32695,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R67GSZ5Y"],"itemData":{"id":32695,"type":"book","collection-title":"MARE Publication Series","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-319-17033-6","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-17034-3","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Interactive Governance for Small-Scale Fisheries","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-319-17034-3","volume":"13","editor":[{"family":"Jentoft","given":"Svein"},{"family":"Chuenpagdee","given":"Ratana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":31544,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S5NIDIYP"],"itemData":{"id":31544,"type":"book","event-place":"Dordrecht","ISBN":"978-94-007-1581-3","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-94-007-1582-0","publisher":"Springer Netherlands","publisher-place":"Dordrecht","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Poverty Mosaics: Realities and Prospects in Small-Scale Fisheries","title-short":"Poverty Mosaics","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-94-007-1582-0","editor":[{"family":"Jentoft","given":"Svein"},{"family":"Eide","given":"Arne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":31414,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NQZI3Z96"],"itemData":{"id":31414,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Regime shifts from one ecological state to another are often portrayed as sudden, dramatic, and difficult to reverse given the extent of substantial reorganizations in system structure, functions and feedbacks. However, most assessments of regime shifts in terrestrial and aquatic systems have emphasized their physical and/or biological dimensions. Our objective is to illustrate how equivalent concern with ecological and social processes can enhance our ability to understand and navigate ‘social-ecological’ regime shifts. We draw on two coastal lagoon systems experiencing rapid change to provide an empirical foundation for an initial analytical framework. Key issues we address include: 1) distinguishing underlying versus proximate drivers of rapid change (ecological and social); 2) considering appropriate scales of intervention; 3) considering the appropriate unit(s) for understanding regime shifts; 4) reflecting on social equity and the distribution of impacts (and benefits) of regime shifts; 5) assessing the influence of social power in the framing of and response to regime shifts; and 6) clarifying the role of management and governance in the context of rapid social-ecological change. Effective responses to social-ecological regime shifts will require a transition towards interdisciplinary research, inclusion of integrative and scale-specific suite of attributes for assessment, and interventions in management and governance approaches that are more multi-level, collaborative and adaptive.","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2018.04.020","ISSN":"09645691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"84-95","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Social-ecological regime shifts (SERS) in coastal systems","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0964569117309857","volume":"161","author":[{"family":"Nayak","given":"Prateep Kumar"},{"family":"Armitage","given":"Derek"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An estimated 5.8 million fishers in the world who earn less than 1 United States dollars per day ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"f25hDU8R","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2014b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2014b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12931,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7NI9KVSH"],"itemData":{"id":12931,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-108275-1","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 957283642","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"The state of the world fisheries and aquaculture 2014: opportunities and challenges.","title-short":"The state of the world fisheries and aquaculture 2014","URL":"https://www.fao.org/3/i3720e/i3720e.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ommer ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2bcr66mvem","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2007)","plainCitation":"(2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31543,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZKW2XKGY"],"itemData":{"id":31543,"type":"book","event-place":"Montreal","ISBN":"978-0-7735-7601-8","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 951204038","publisher":"McGill-Queen's University Press","publisher-place":"Montreal","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Coasts Under Stress: Restructuring and Social-Ecological Health.","title-short":"Coasts Under Stress","author":[{"family":"Ommer","given":"Rosemary E"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} characterize these large-scale, globalized processes as a crisis in social-ecological ‘health’, with dire consequences on small-scale fisheries communities.
      The COVID-19 pandemic will affect many small-scale fisheries and coastal communities worldwide, especially those more vulnerable, mainly through reduced (or closure of) markets, decreases in revenues from tourism, increases in health risks to fishers and traders and increased occurrence of illegal fishing due to lack of enforcement. Mitigation of these factors would likely require institutions to provide short- and long-term responses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uANuFMek","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. J. Bennett, 2020)","plainCitation":"(N. J. Bennett, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6386,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GC4FZBX3"],"itemData":{"id":6386,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1080/08920753.2020.1766937","ISSN":"0892-0753, 1521-0421","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"336-347","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The COVID-19 Pandemic, Small-Scale Fisheries and Coastal Fishing Communities","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/08920753.2020.1766937","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"Nathan J."},{"family":"Finkbeiner","given":"Elena M."},{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C."},{"family":"Belhabib","given":"Dyhia"},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."},{"family":"Kittinger","given":"John N."},{"family":"Mangubhai","given":"Sangeeta"},{"family":"Scholtens","given":"Joeri"},{"family":"Gill","given":"David"},{"family":"Christie","given":"Patrick"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,3]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There can be some positive outcomes from the pandemic crisis, including enhanced local cooperation among fishing communities and other institutions, valorization of local markets, food sharing and some recovery of fishing resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Wo5vGlh5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. J. Bennett, 2020)","plainCitation":"(N. J. Bennett, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6386,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GC4FZBX3"],"itemData":{"id":6386,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1080/08920753.2020.1766937","ISSN":"0892-0753, 1521-0421","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"336-347","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The COVID-19 Pandemic, Small-Scale Fisheries and Coastal Fishing Communities","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/08920753.2020.1766937","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"Nathan J."},{"family":"Finkbeiner","given":"Elena M."},{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C."},{"family":"Belhabib","given":"Dyhia"},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"Stacy D."},{"family":"Kittinger","given":"John N."},{"family":"Mangubhai","given":"Sangeeta"},{"family":"Scholtens","given":"Joeri"},{"family":"Gill","given":"David"},{"family":"Christie","given":"Patrick"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The state of inland capture-fishery resources that includes small-scale inland fisheries is more difficult to monitor ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"B3muOJ5F","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Welcomme, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Welcomme, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31578,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M8FMGU2T"],"itemData":{"id":31578,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","ISSN":"1095-9289","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","page":"1751-1756","title":"An overview of global catch statistics for inland fish","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Welcomme","given":"Robin L"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} for a number of reasons, including the diffused character of the practice due to: (i) large numbers of people being involved in the seasonal and subsistence nature of fisheries activities; (ii) much of the catch being consumed locally or traded informally; and (iii) fisher populations being greatly affected by activities other than fishing, including stocking from aquaculture and diversion of water for other uses such as agriculture and hydroelectric development ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1nis1g3i79","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2012c)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2012c)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31540,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EX4N8W95"],"itemData":{"id":31540,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome; London","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1010821422","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations ; Eurospan [distributor","publisher-place":"Rome; London","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture 2012.","URL":"https://www.fao.org/3/i2727e/i2727e00.htm","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	This section is based on a comprehensive review of 350 studies on small-scale fisheries from 107 countries worldwide (Figure 3.22). With regard to ecological sustainability, 39 studies indicate sustainable fisheries but almost half the studies (#165) indicate unsustainability. Whereas fisheries reported by 129 studies were considered to be partially sustainable; a few studies (#16) do not assess ecological sustainability but include some accounts on economic or social sustainability. Most of the reviewed literature on small-scale fisheries addresses the use of fish as food and feed, and is presented in detail below by major world regions. Other uses for fish are also mentioned in some regions (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). This review supports the text below, considering the available evidence from most of the revised studies (for details on the reviewed studies, see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651).


Figure 3.22 Global distribution of the 350 reviewed studies on small-scale fisheries among 107 countries (countries in gray means that no study was included). Under license CC-BY. See data management report for the figure at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6453056.

      A systematic literature review on small-scale fisheries was undertaken based on literature obtained through various combinations of a set of keywords: fisheries, sustainable, sustainability, small-scale, coastal, freshwater, catch, trend, success, local knowledge, use, fishers, co-management, increasing, review, catches, fish, and ecological. These keywords were selected to get a manageable number of hits (literature) to assess and to direct the search results to those articles analyzing sustainable fisheries, or at least to those showing trends of an increase in catches. The database SCOPUS was used for articles from the last 20 years (since 2000), which initially retrieved a total of 1635 articles. A complementary search was made on Google Scholar using a subset of these keywords. However, due to the large amount of literature retrieved (34300 hits), only the first 200 hits were reviewed on Google Scholar, including some of the more recent articles from the last 10 years. A total of 447 articles on small-scale fisheries were selected after an initial screening, including only articles that reported some data on fisheries, preferably trends and some kind of indicator, such as abundance, size or catch per unit of effort, or fishing effort among others. Articles addressing details of management or policy options which did not include data, or theoretical approaches and effects from drivers, such as climate change, pollution, or development projects, were not included. 
	These 447 articles were sorted by major regions and the case studies on small-scale fisheries were selected from these (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). This literature review was complemented with relevant articles inserted by the authors from their personal libraries, by suggested articles from internal and external reviewers, and through cross-reference from the selected articles. Our review did not retrieve a large number of articles dealing with uses other than food (ornamental, medicinal, etc.) and those addressing social and economic dimensions of sustainability in small-scale fisheries. 
      The selected studies were sorted across a gradient of ecological sustainability, ranging from fully sustainable (exploited populations stable, no habitat damage, no ecological filters or shifts in the composition of exploited species) to unsustainable (exploited populations declining or overfished). Intermediate or partial sustainability included situations in which current exploited populations are stable, but some higher valued species were depleted or extinct, which are considered here as ecological filters (see also section 3.3.1.4.2), or the fishing practice has caused habitat damage or bycatch. Fisheries lacking data on temporal trends to clearly indicate sustainable catches were also allocated to these partially sustainable categories (for details on the reviewed studies, see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651).
      A major challenge in evaluating the sustainability of small-scale fisheries is the lack of data on catches and measures of exploited stocks (size, proportion of juveniles caught, etc.), especially over broader spatial or temporal scales. Nevertheless, participatory research in collaboration with fishers and analyses of the fishers’ knowledge about fishing resources have contributed evidence to assess patterns of sustainability, catches and fishing effort.
      Relatively few studies have evaluated the economic sustainability of small-scale fisheries. A review on global marine fisheries indicates that well-managed and locally supported small-scale fisheries could be a more sustainable option to provide employment and food than the current subsidy-driven industrial fisheries, which may increase effort in spite of declining fishing resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RoRuFWWa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dirk Zeller & Pauly, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Dirk Zeller & Pauly, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33467,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DX8JIP2N"],"itemData":{"id":33467,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            We present a view on global marine fisheries that emphasizes mitigating the conflict between sustainability and the scale of industrial exploitation driven by the demand of continuous economic growth. We then summarize the current state of global fisheries. Finally, we advocate strongly for scaling back industrial fisheries, most of which are non-sustainable. This can be achieved through eliminating the harmful, capacity-enhancing subsidies that prop up industrial fisheries to continue operating despite declining fish stocks. Instead, we propose to support well-managed, locally owned and operated small-scale fisheries, which generally contribute more to local employment and food security. We stress that contrary to deep-seated opinion in the fishing industry and among politicians, reducing overfishing by eliminating overcapacity in fishing fleets will actually lead to greater, not reduced catches. This would address part of the increased global seafood demand over the coming decades, which is driven by population and wealth growth. This seems counterintuitive, but is supported by fisheries science, data and experiences. Thankfully, we are beginning to see that some of these changes are being pursued by a growing number of countries and international institutions.","container-title":"Global Sustainability","DOI":"10.1017/sus.2019.8","ISSN":"2059-4798","journalAbbreviation":"Glob. Sustain.","language":"en","page":"e11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Viewpoint: Back to the future for fisheries, where will we choose to go?","title-short":"Viewpoint","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S2059479819000085/type/journal_article","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, conventional economic models that have been applied to assess fisheries economic viability may not be appropriate to small-scale fisheries, which need inclusion of social and environmental variables to conduct economic viability analyses that go beyond profit maximization ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TT4vmtDh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schuhbauer & Sumaila, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Schuhbauer & Sumaila, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31537,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UQ3DRGAI"],"itemData":{"id":31537,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2016.01.018","page":"69-75","title":"Economic viability and small-scale fisheries - A review","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84959019054&doi=10.1016%2fj.ecolecon.2016.01.018&partnerID=40&md5=fda371dc967f93b92060db659f1536c6","volume":"124","author":[{"family":"Schuhbauer","given":"A."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The literature search retrieved 49 studies of global scope, which encompass multiple countries from more than one of the broad regions defined here, of which 18 studies were included in this review (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Among these, studies 15 address coastal fisheries, two address inland fisheries and two include both coastal and inland. These studies usually have a broad coverage in space or time, grouping data from many regions and communities and sometimes showing long time series of 50 up to 600 years (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). One of these studies, which brings data for over 1,900 coastal indigenous communities around the world, representing 27 million people across 87 countries, claims that sustainability depends on increased recognition and directed research regarding the marine knowledge and resource needs of indigenous peoples, whose needs must be explicitly incorporated into management policies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ak3oic96fq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, Pauly, Weatherdon, & Ota, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, Pauly, Weatherdon, & Ota, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34761,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZNQMHBSF"],"itemData":{"id":34761,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0166681","issue":"12","title":"A global estimate of seafood consumption by coastal indigenous peoples","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85002898790&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0166681&partnerID=40&md5=597d1afb26d002398d34360f6ed6117c","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"Andrés M."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."},{"family":"Weatherdon","given":"L.V."},{"family":"Ota","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Other studies point to the potential overfishing of marine invertebrates (including cephalopods, shellfish, lobsters, crabs, sea cucumbers) estimating that, in 2004, 34% of invertebrate fisheries were over-exploited, collapsed, or closed, as global invertebrate catches have increased 6-fold ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8fZC6RNz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S.C. Anderson, Flemming, Watson, & Lotze, 2011)","plainCitation":"(S.C. Anderson, Flemming, Watson, & Lotze, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31784,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A2LSAP98"],"itemData":{"id":31784,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0014735","issue":"3","title":"Rapid global expansion of invertebrate fisheries: Trends, drivers, and ecosystem effects","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-79952367034&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0014735&partnerID=40&md5=bf74cbff5b605bcd349de8641b0ab206","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Anderson","given":"S.C."},{"family":"Flemming","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Watson","given":"R."},{"family":"Lotze","given":"H.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This problem is especially severe for sea cucumber fisheries, 81% of which show population declines from overfishing, and 35% had declines in the average harvested body size. Harvesters moved from near- to off-shore regions in 51% of cases and from high- to low-value species in 76% of these fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jmOpomeP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sean C Anderson, Flemming, Watson, & Lotze, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Sean C Anderson, Flemming, Watson, & Lotze, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31786,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IFDPZZ9B"],"itemData":{"id":31786,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract In recent decades, invertebrate fisheries have expanded in catch and value worldwide. One increasingly harvested group is sea cucumbers (class Holothuroidea), which are highly valued in Asia and sold as trepang or bêche-de-mer. We compiled global landings, economic data, and country-specific assessment and management reports to synthesize global trends in sea cucumber fisheries, evaluate potential drivers, and test for local and global serial exploitation patterns. Although some sea cucumber fisheries have existed for centuries, catch trends of most individual fisheries followed boom-and-bust patterns since the 1950s, declining nearly as quickly as they expanded. New fisheries expanded five to six times faster in 1990 compared to 1960 and at an increasing distance from Asia, encompassing a global fishery by the 1990s. Global sea cucumber production was correlated to the Japanese yen at a leading lag. Regional assessments revealed that population declines from overfishing occurred in 81% of sea cucumber fisheries, average harvested body size declined in 35%, harvesters moved from near- to off-shore regions in 51% and from high- to low-value species in 76%. Thirty-eight per cent of sea cucumber fisheries remained unregulated, and illegal catches were of concern in half. Our results suggest that development patterns of sea cucumber fisheries are largely predictable, often unsustainable and frequently too rapid for effective management responses. We discuss potential ecosystem and human community consequences and urge for better monitoring and reporting of catch and abundance, proper scientific stock assessment and consideration of international trade regulations to ensure long-term and sustainable harvesting of sea cucumbers worldwide.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-2979.2010.00397.x","ISSN":"1467-2960","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Fish and Fisheries","note":"publisher: John Wiley & Sons, Ltd","page":"317-339","title":"Serial exploitation of global sea cucumber fisheries","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-2979.2010.00397.x","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Anderson","given":"Sean C"},{"family":"Flemming","given":"Joanna Mills"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Reg"},{"family":"Lotze","given":"Heike K"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",9,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, a global survey indicates that sawfishes (family Pristidae) have been heavily affected by intense harvesting and habitat degradation and these sawfish are now extinct in 55 of the 90 nations where they originally occurred ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GDwc7YlJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yan, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Yan, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26774,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5WPRBIS7","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MVFYB24S"],"itemData":{"id":26774,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Extinctions on land are often inferred from sparse sightings over time, but this technique is ill-suited for wide-ranging species. We develop a space-for-time approach to track the spatial contraction and drivers of decline of sawfishes. These iconic and endangered shark-like rays were once found in warm, coastal waters of 90 nations and are now presumed extinct in more than half (\\n              n\\n              = 46). Using dynamic geography theory, we predict that sawfishes are gone from at least nine additional nations. Overfishing and habitat loss have reduced spatial occupancy, leading to local extinctions in 55 of the 90 nations, which equates to 58.7% of their historical distribution. Retention bans and habitat protections are urgently necessary to secure a future for sawfishes and similar species.","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.abb6026","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Sci. Adv.","language":"en","page":"eabb6026","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Overfishing and habitat loss drive range contraction of iconic marine fishes to near extinction","URL":"https://advances.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/sciadv.abb6026","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Yan","given":"Helen F."},{"family":"Kyne","given":"Peter M."},{"family":"Jabado","given":"Rima W."},{"family":"Leeney","given":"Ruth H."},{"family":"Davidson","given":"Lindsay N.K."},{"family":"Derrick","given":"Danielle H."},{"family":"Finucci","given":"Brittany"},{"family":"Freckleton","given":"Robert P."},{"family":"Fordham","given":"Sonja V."},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	A study comparing the fisheries in Florida (Atlantic) and Hawaii (Pacific) over a period of 600 years indicated that, although fishing had been sustainable in Hawaii for 400 years, landings have declined and some species are recorded as overexploited in both the study regions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GVOjDYRA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mcclenachan & Kittinger, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Mcclenachan & Kittinger, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31783,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9SDADU63"],"itemData":{"id":31783,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-2979.2012.00465.x","issue":"3","page":"239-255","title":"Multicentury trends and the sustainability of coral reef fisheries in Hawai'i and Florida","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84880641202&doi=10.1111%2fj.1467-2979.2012.00465.x&partnerID=40&md5=3dbd2b38f3b1108ba3d0e1a277219d5f","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Mcclenachan","given":"L."},{"family":"Kittinger","given":"J.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A study reviewing context and attributes of co-management initiatives in small-scale fisheries concludes that more research is needed to discern when co-management initiatives can transform pre-existing conflicts, challenge power asymmetries and distribute benefits more equitably ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yqyMO09d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(d\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Armengol, Prieto Castillo, Ruiz-Mall\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}n, & Corbera, 2018)","plainCitation":"(d’Armengol, Prieto Castillo, Ruiz-Mallén, & Corbera, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31538,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RK95J9NR"],"itemData":{"id":31538,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2018.07.009","page":"212-225","title":"A systematic review of co-managed small-scale fisheries: Social diversity and adaptive management improve outcomes","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85050666947&doi=10.1016%2fj.gloenvcha.2018.07.009&partnerID=40&md5=55c5192341e607678449a7623cd3fcf8","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Armengol","given":"L.","non-dropping-particle":"d'"},{"family":"Prieto Castillo","given":"M."},{"family":"Ruiz-Mallén","given":"I."},{"family":"Corbera","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, another study indicates that fishers perceived improved livelihoods and compliance in co-managed sites, thus evidencing contributions of co-management to improve social sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6rWseb9I","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cinner, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Cinner, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31572,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6NV2JEUT"],"itemData":{"id":31572,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1121215109","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"14","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","language":"en","page":"5219-5222","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Comanagement of coral reef social-ecological systems","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/doi/10.1073/pnas.1121215109","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Cinner","given":"J. E."},{"family":"McClanahan","given":"T. R."},{"family":"MacNeil","given":"M. A."},{"family":"Graham","given":"N. A. J."},{"family":"Daw","given":"T. M."},{"family":"Mukminin","given":"A."},{"family":"Feary","given":"D. A."},{"family":"Rabearisoa","given":"A. L."},{"family":"Wamukota","given":"A."},{"family":"Jiddawi","given":"N."},{"family":"Campbell","given":"S. J."},{"family":"Baird","given":"A. H."},{"family":"Januchowski-Hartley","given":"F. A."},{"family":"Hamed","given":"S."},{"family":"Lahari","given":"R."},{"family":"Morove","given":"T."},{"family":"Kuange","given":"J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",4,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

* Europe
 Out of the 56 papers reviewed for Europe (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). The vast majority cover mainly the coastal and marine/oceanic fisheries in Europe or in European archipelagos in the Atlantic Ocean, the Mediterranean Sea (and its internal seas, like the Adriatic, the Aegean, the Marmara) or in the Black Sea. Ocean or marine small-scale fisheries is discussed in 48 papers, whereas only a small number of those (eight) investigated the European inland small-scale fisheries. A majority of these papers focused on Iberian freshwater fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IsKo1EpX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Antunes, Cobo, & Ara\\\\uc0\\\\u250{}jo, 2015; Braga, Pereira, Morgado, Soares, & Azeiteiro, 2019; Marcos, Torres, L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez-Capel, & P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez-Ruzafa, 2015; F. Maynou, Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}nez-Ba\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}os, Demestre, & Franquesa, 2014)","plainCitation":"(C. Antunes, Cobo, & Araújo, 2015; Braga, Pereira, Morgado, Soares, & Azeiteiro, 2019; Marcos, Torres, López-Capel, & Pérez-Ruzafa, 2015; F. Maynou, Martínez-Baños, Demestre, & Franquesa, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31782,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C4RFSYFY"],"itemData":{"id":31782,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Freshwater Fisheries Ecology","event-place":"Oxford, UK","ISBN":"978-1-118-39442-7","note":"DOI: 10.1002/9781118394380.ch23","page":"268-282","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","publisher-place":"Oxford, UK","title":"Iberian inland fisheries","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84984844857&doi=10.1002%2f9781118394380.ch23&partnerID=40&md5=c6a7af8f3dd1d871e5423260a9b0aae9","author":[{"family":"Antunes","given":"C."},{"family":"Cobo","given":"F."},{"family":"Araújo","given":"M.J."}],"editor":[{"family":"Craig","given":"J.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":31802,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3GP2S3IU"],"itemData":{"id":31802,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-019-0345-9","issue":"1","title":"Ethnozoological knowledge of traditional fishing villages about the anadromous sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus) in the Minho river, Portugal","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85077285236&doi=10.1186%2fs13002-019-0345-9&partnerID=40&md5=6dd7f95f450acf4eac9bd04130185fb5","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Braga","given":"H.O."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"M.J."},{"family":"Morgado","given":"F."},{"family":"Soares","given":"A.M.V.M."},{"family":"Azeiteiro","given":"U.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31789,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EG2KICEF"],"itemData":{"id":31789,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1007/s11160-015-9397-7","issue":"4","page":"689-713","title":"Long term evolution of fisheries in a coastal lagoon related to changes in lagoon ecology and human pressures","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84946496133&doi=10.1007%2fs11160-015-9397-7&partnerID=40&md5=984fc1cd13dad2c2b46957e1109be817","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Marcos","given":"C."},{"family":"Torres","given":"I."},{"family":"López-Capel","given":"A."},{"family":"Pérez-Ruzafa","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":34075,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I6SD7P3S"],"itemData":{"id":34075,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ichthyology","DOI":"10.1111/jai.12460","issue":"5","page":"978-985","title":"Bio-economic analysis of the Mar Menor (Murcia, SE Spain) small-scale lagoon fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84908251441&doi=10.1111%2fjai.12460&partnerID=40&md5=de31f1e791a98aca1cd7ebfa70c485f8","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Maynou","given":"F."},{"family":"Martínez-Baños","given":"P."},{"family":"Demestre","given":"M."},{"family":"Franquesa","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, although there are other very important fishing practices, such as the trout fisheries, taking place in many different countries of the region ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oy9hh014","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shephard, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Shephard, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31809,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9L6CR29K"],"itemData":{"id":31809,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2019.04.073","page":"96-106","title":"Combining empirical indicators and expert knowledge for surveillance of data-limited sea trout stocks","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85064906024&doi=10.1016%2fj.ecolind.2019.04.073&partnerID=40&md5=1891ff272b2971e96ccadb921df1dfbb","volume":"104","author":[{"family":"Shephard","given":"S."},{"family":"Josset","given":"Q."},{"family":"Davidson","given":"I."},{"family":"Kennedy","given":"R."},{"family":"Magnusson","given":"K."},{"family":"Gargan","given":"P.G."},{"family":"Walker","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Poole","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
