7"],"itemData":{"id":31741,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-008-9178-1","issue":"4","page":"581-598","title":"Mapping cod: Fisheries science, fish harvesters' ecological knowledge and cod migrations in the Northern Gulf of St. Lawrence","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-48749120074&doi=10.1007%2fs10745-008-9178-1&partnerID=40&md5=5019c38c24b474170933c230b4198760","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Murray","given":"G."},{"family":"Neis","given":"B."},{"family":"Palmer","given":"C.T."},{"family":"Schneider","given":"D.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1090,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZEF9RTGP"],"itemData":{"id":1090,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2014.07.025","page":"103-110","title":"Harvesters' perspectives on the management of British Columbia's giant red sea cucumber fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84906500216&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2014.07.025&partnerID=40&md5=b988fe097a05bddd512f0496ad485587","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"O'Regan","given":"S.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}Six studies focus on economic and 12 studies highlight social considerations in small-scale fisheries of Canada and the United States of America (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Some of these studies underscore the high economic value of the recreational fisheries practice in Florida, which provides jobs and revenues ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YIvskBsZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ault, Bohnsack, Smith, & Luo, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Ault, Bohnsack, Smith, & Luo, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1072,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7WDGPK8M"],"itemData":{"id":1072,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bulletin of Marine Science","issue":"2","page":"595-622","title":"Towards sustainable multispecies fisheries in the Florida, USA, coral reef ecosystem","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-17444364515&partnerID=40&md5=c2dcc3a13595289f5d7e95e1fbccdda4","volume":"76","author":[{"family":"Ault","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Bohnsack","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Smith","given":"S.G."},{"family":"Luo","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, the catch-and-release fishery in South Florida (and the Caribbean) has an estimated value of at least half a billion dollars per year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AMzCm99f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kroloff, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Kroloff, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1075,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/87KQJRFM"],"itemData":{"id":1075,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environmental Biology of Fishes","DOI":"10.1007/s10641-018-0812-5","issue":"2","page":"319-328","title":"Understanding the decline of catch-and-release fishery with angler knowledge: a key informant approach applied to South Florida bonefish","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85055945724&doi=10.1007%2fs10641-018-0812-5&partnerID=40&md5=c67809c0efaf0039684b82adf1ba0ac2","volume":"102","author":[{"family":"Kroloff","given":"E.K.N."},{"family":"Heinen","given":"J.T."},{"family":"Braddock","given":"K.N."},{"family":"Rehage","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Santos","given":"R.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, the lobster (Homarus americanus) fishery is very important to the region of the Gulf of Maine in Canada  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hI6I5hQd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Boudreau & Worm, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Boudreau & Worm, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1099,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3CFU3TBU"],"itemData":{"id":1099,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Ecology Progress Series","DOI":"10.3354/meps08473","page":"181-191","title":"Top-down control of lobster in the Gulf of Maine: Insights from local ecological knowledge and research surveys","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-77954105646&doi=10.3354%2fmeps08473&partnerID=40&md5=b27c208390e1c0a22f2c17d64481585e","volume":"403","author":[{"family":"Boudreau","given":"S.A."},{"family":"Worm","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some studies indicate potential negative interactions among economic activities. For example, commercial fishing coupled with the expansion of sports (recreational) fishing in the last decades may had affected yelloweye rockfish (Sebastes ruberrimus) populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1kad8b29vo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eckert, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Eckert, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31727,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WE8ZZ9T4"],"itemData":{"id":31727,"type":"article-journal","call-number":"Esub 21f","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2834","ISSN":"10527613","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Aquatic Conserv: Mar Freshw Ecosyst","language":"English","page":"158-166","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Diving back in time: Extending historical baselines for yelloweye rockfish with Indigenous knowledge","title-short":"Diving back in time","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/aqc.2834","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Eckert","given":"Lauren E."},{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C."},{"family":"Frid","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"McGreer","given":"Madeleine"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, food security in Alaska has been negatively affected by the development of export-oriented commercial fisheries and tourism-oriented sport fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fqVnUFLD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(H. L. Harrison & Loring, 2016)","plainCitation":"(H. L. Harrison & Loring, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31732,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7W5MNFN2"],"itemData":{"id":31732,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Agriculture and Food Security","DOI":"10.1186/s40066-016-0065-5","issue":"1","title":"Urban harvests: Food security and local fish and shellfish in Southcentral Alaska","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84996615321&doi=10.1186%2fs40066-016-0065-5&partnerID=40&md5=c09c5e701ade23047e796e8653bd2014","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Harrison","given":"H.L."},{"family":"Loring","given":"P.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another study reports changes in fishing area or practices in response to changing market infrastructure (e.g., switch to frozen from salt cod), besides changes in economic factors external to the fishery, such as loss of other income generating activities, which can affect the economic sustainability of cod (Gadus morhua) in Newfoundland, Canada ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9lX55d7l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Murray, Neis, & Schneider, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Murray, Neis, & Schneider, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31717,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3TEFHVDL"],"itemData":{"id":31717,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1080/08920750701682056","issue":"1","page":"81-108","title":"Lessons from a multi-scale historical reconstruction of newfoundland and labrador fisheries","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-37749051797&doi=10.1080%2f08920750701682056&partnerID=40&md5=28af0836ff2534b3580e732e73c89faa","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Murray","given":"G."},{"family":"Neis","given":"B."},{"family":"Schneider","given":"D.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Some of the studies that mention social characteristics of small-scale fisheries comment on the relevance of fishing resources to local peoples’ livelihoods and food security (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). For example, fishing of Arctic char (Salvelinus alpinus) has high value for food security, cultural identity and local economic development among Arctic communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KHWaPQys","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Roux, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Roux, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IKKLUCWV"],"itemData":{"id":31740,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2018.01.016","page":"177-186","title":"Small-scale fisheries in Canada's Arctic: Combining science and fishers knowledge towards sustainable management","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85044539689&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2018.01.016&partnerID=40&md5=c9b73f49544b72fe63f8e513f9b23971","volume":"101","author":[{"family":"Roux","given":"M.-J."},{"family":"Tallman","given":"R.F."},{"family":"Martin","given":"Z.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Conversely, the observed decline in the catches of the Dungeness crab may compromise the ability of indigenous fishers to access traditional foods in Canada ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DkzGWrUT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ban, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Ban, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31728,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GT85P9S4"],"itemData":{"id":31728,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Introduction: Fisheries management is often data-limited, and conducted at spatial scales that are too large to address the needs of Indigenous peoples, whose cultures depend upon the local availability of marine resources.","call-number":"Esub 21h","container-title":"Ecosystem Health and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1080/20964129.2017.1379887","ISSN":"2096-4129, 2332-8878","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystem Health and Sustainability","language":"English","page":"1379887","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Indigenous knowledge as data for modern fishery management: a case study of Dungeness crab in Pacific Canada","title-short":"Indigenous knowledge as data for modern fishery management","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/20964129.2017.1379887","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C"},{"family":"Eckert","given":"Lauren"},{"family":"McGreer","given":"Madeleine"},{"family":"Frid","given":"Alejandro"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other studies emphasize the relevance and benefits of integrating multiple knowledge sources in fisheries assessments, including fishers’ indigenous and local knowledge, which may improve dialogue, cooperation and social relations between fishers and scientists ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nuc1TEFw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ambrose, 2014; Ban, 2017; Murray, Neis, Palmer,, 2008; Murray, Neis, & Schneider, 2008; Rehage, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Ambrose, 2014; Ban, 2017; Murray, Neis, Palmer,, 2008; Murray, Neis, & Schneider, 2008; Rehage, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31726,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/69CPRGBZ"],"itemData":{"id":31726,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2014.00040","issue":"SEP","title":"Interpreting environmental change in coastal Alaska using traditional and scientific ecological knowledge","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85008930053&doi=10.3389%2ffmars.2014.00040&partnerID=40&