tors and expert knowledge for surveillance of data-limited sea trout stocks","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85064906024&doi=10.1016%2fj.ecolind.2019.04.073&partnerID=40&md5=1891ff272b2971e96ccadb921df1dfbb","volume":"104","author":[{"family":"Shephard","given":"S."},{"family":"Josset","given":"Q."},{"family":"Davidson","given":"I."},{"family":"Kennedy","given":"R."},{"family":"Magnusson","given":"K."},{"family":"Gargan","given":"P.G."},{"family":"Walker","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Poole","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":9087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RC5QGUYI"],"itemData":{"id":9087,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Canadian Journal of Fisheries and Aquatic Sciences","DOI":"10.1139/cjfas-2018-0423","issue":"1","page":"124-131","title":"Two sympatric, not externally discernible, and heavily exploited deepwater species with coastal migration during spawning season: Implications for sustainable stocks management of Aphanopus carbo and Aphanopus intermedius around madeira","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85077890087&doi=10.1139%2fcjfas-2018-0423&partnerID=40&md5=cf2179b01d56024c7c53a4cf354f3532","volume":"77","author":[{"family":"Vasconcelos","given":"J."},{"family":"Sousa","given":"R."},{"family":"Henriques","given":"P."},{"family":"Amorim","given":"A."},{"family":"Delgado","given":"J."},{"family":"Riera","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, also in association with other methods and indicators. However, as expected, most of the ecological assessments reviewed (41 out of 63 papers) support their conclusions with landing statistics (production/catch biomass, catch composition) and related parameters to measure fishing effort and catch-per-unit-of-effort.
