ed":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31522,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UYFV3W2S"],"itemData":{"id":31522,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2017.11.020","page":"119-126","title":"Is it sustainable fishing for Octopus maya Voss and Solis, 1966, during the breeding season using a bait-based fishing technique?","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85037340186&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2017.11.020&partnerID=40&md5=b557a5950e74007aae0a26fe42530559","volume":"199","author":[{"family":"Duarte","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Hernández-Flores","given":"A."},{"family":"Salas","given":"S."},{"family":"Seijo","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3UQSPIR8"],"itemData":{"id":31795,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2020.105526","title":"Use of historical data to assess changes in the vulnerability of sharks","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85079187749&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2020.105526&partnerID=40&md5=653a68f2be7bd84597b6da8d3c7ae717","volume":"226","author":[{"family":"Martínez-Candelas","given":"I.A."},{"family":"Pérez-Jiménez","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Espinoza-Tenorio","given":"A."},{"family":"McClenachan","given":"L."},{"family":"Méndez-Loeza","given":"I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31752,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WXQ88SU8"],"itemData":{"id":31752,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Boletim do Instituto de Pesca","DOI":"10.20950/1678-2305.2019.45.2.423","ISSN":"00469939, 16782305","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Bol. Inst. Pesca","page":"e.423","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishery parameters and population dynamics of silver croaker on the Xingu river, Brazilian Amazon","URL":"https://www.pesca.sp.gov.br/FISHERY_PARAMETERS_AND_POPULATION_DYNAMICS_OF_SILVER_CROAKER_ON_THE_XINGU_RIVER_BRAZILIAN_AMAZON.html","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Mesquita","given":"Esther Mirian Cardoso"},{"family":"Cruz","given":"Rivetla Edipo Araújo"},{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Isaac","given":"Victoria Judith"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,4]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The reviewed studies applied a varied set of indicators, often in combination, including total catches or landings (58), catch-per-unit-of-effort (22), and size of exploited fishing resources (37). Catch composition and its variation through time, measures of fishing effort, such as number of fishers, vessels and the distribution of effort in space and time, economic indicators (revenues and costs), and overall abundance trends estimated from indigenous and local knowledge were also used as indicators of sustainable use (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651).
      A few studies calculated and compared sustainability indicators based on ecological, economic and social data ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pO59svVw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ciss\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, 2014; Robotham, 2019; Torres-Guevara, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Cissé, 2014; Robotham, 2019; Torres-Guevara, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31532,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/379TV9EU"],"itemData":{"id":31532,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2013.10.003","page":"397-405","title":"Sustainability of tropical small-scale fisheries: Integrated assessment in French Guiana","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84889591238&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2013.10.003&partnerID=40&md5=7c2314b9d2cb4de5683fcc4924d33b5b","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Cissé","given":"A.A."},{"family":"Blanchard","given":"F."},{"family":"Guyader","given":"O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31746,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PJX4EFPL"],"itemData":{"id":31746,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2019.103514","title":"Contribution to the study of sustainability of small-scale artisanal fisheries in Chile","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85065445876&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2019.103514&partnerID=40&md5=ed3f45f0456c7053e749393f7714379d","volume":"106","author":[{"family":"Robotham","given":"H."},{"family":"Bustos","given":"E."},{"family":"Ther-Rios","given":"F."},{"family":"Avila","given":"M."},{"family":"Robotham","given":"M."},{"family":"Hidalgo","given":"C."},{"family":"Muñoz","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31531,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/56G8BD8N"],"itemData":{"id":31531,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Sustainability (Switzerland)","DOI":"10.3390/su8060549","issue":"6","title":"Understanding artisanal fishers' behaviors: The case of Ciénaga Grande de Santa Marta, Colombia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84976287037&doi=10.3390%2fsu8060549&partnerID=40&md5=b648244e55b422c7dc8c3ca3d2e186ca","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Torres-Guevara","given":"L.E."},{"family":"Lopez","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Schlüter","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, most of the reported trends are based on total catches only. The lack of effort or catch-per-unit-of-effort data makes it more difficult to properly assess the sustainability of these fisheries. Furthermore, while some species are preferred, most of these fisheries are multi-species and multi-gear (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). This imposes further challenges to sustainability assessments, as exploited species may differ regarding their resilience to fishing pressure and stock status. These challenges were addressed by most of the reviewed studies through two main, non-mutually exclusive, approaches. First, to rely on a variety of the indicators described above and second, to include fishers’ knowledge about catches, trends, details of fishing effort in combination with fisheries data, biological surveys or modelling. Indeed, indigenous and local knowledge was included in the majority (69) of studies reviewed (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651).
      Many studies stressed the important economic role of both coastal and inland small-scale fisheries in the studied regions, but relatively few studies included economic indicators (profits, revenues), analyzed market chains, or evaluated the economic sustainability of the studied fisheries (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Economic considerations were mentioned by 42% of the 78 studies on coastal small-scale fisheries and by 45% of the 29 studies on inland small-scale fisheries, sometimes linked to the analysis of catch trends and ecological sustainability.
      
* North America
The indicators adopted in the reviewed studies include catch (landings data), population and stock parameters, environmental or ecological indicators, productivity susceptibility analysis and various modelling approaches (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Even considering that both countries have a well-developed fisheries science and management with strong financial and technical capacity, the majority of the reviewed studies (17) include fishers’ knowledge or indigenous and local knowledge, usually in combination with the above-mentioned fisheries and ecological indicators (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Moreover, fishers’ knowledge has been included in these studies on various forms or manifestations, from traditional knowledge of indigenous people, usually from the Artic ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QOdfyYlM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ambrose, 2014; Ban, 2017; Eckert, 2018; Roux, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Ambrose, 2014; Ban, 2017; Eckert, 2018; Roux, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31726,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/69CPRGBZ"],"itemData":{"id":31726,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2014.00040","issue":"SEP","title":"Interpreting environmental change in coastal Alaska using traditional and scientific ecological knowledge","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85008930053&doi=10.3389%2ffmars.2014.00040&partnerID=40&md5=f732daa05d107ffc2e54303f706a29a7","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Ambrose","given":"W.G."},{"family":"Clough","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Johnson","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Greenacre","given":"M."},{"family":"Griffith","given":"D.C."},{"family":"Carroll","given":"M.L."},{"family":"Whiting","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31728,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GT85P9S4"],"itemData":{"id":31728,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Introduction: Fisheries management is often data-limited, and conducted at spatial scales that are too large to address the needs of Indigenous peoples, whose cultures depend upon the local availability of marine resources.","call-number":"Esub 21h","container-title":"Ecosystem Health and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1080/20964129.2017.1379887","ISSN":"2096-4129, 2332-8878","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystem Health and Sustainability","language":"English","page":"1379887","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Indigenous knowledge as data for modern fishery management: a case study of Dungeness crab in Pacific Canada","title-short":"Indigenous knowledge as data for modern fishery management","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/20964129.2017.1379887","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C"},{"family":"Eckert","given":"Lauren"},{"family":"McGreer","given":"Madeleine"},{"family":"Frid","given":"Alejandro"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8,3]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31727,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WE8ZZ9T4"],"itemData":{"id":31727,"type":"article-journal","call-number":"Esub 21f","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2834","ISSN":"10527613","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Aquatic Conserv: Mar Freshw Ecosyst","language":"English","page":"158-166","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Diving back in time: Extending historical baselines for yelloweye rockfish with Indigenous knowledge","title-short":"Diving back in time","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/aqc.2834","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Eckert","given":"Lauren E."},{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C."},{"family":"Frid","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"McGreer","given":"Madeleine"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",