al","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environmental Biology of Fishes","DOI":"10.1007/s10641-018-0812-5","issue":"2","page":"319-328","title":"Understanding the decline of catch-and-release fishery with angler knowledge: a key informant approach applied to South Florida bonefish","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85055945724&doi=10.1007%2fs10641-018-0812-5&partnerID=40&md5=c67809c0efaf0039684b82adf1ba0ac2","volume":"102","author":[{"family":"Kroloff","given":"E.K.N."},{"family":"Heinen","given":"J.T."},{"family":"Braddock","given":"K.N."},{"family":"Rehage","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Santos","given":"R.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31741,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NUXGVBY7"],"itemData":{"id":31741,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-008-9178-1","issue":"4","page":"581-598","title":"Mapping cod: Fisheries science, fish harvesters' ecological knowledge and cod migrations in the Northern Gulf of St. Lawrence","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-48749120074&doi=10.1007%2fs10745-008-9178-1&partnerID=40&md5=5019c38c24b474170933c230b4198760","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Murray","given":"G."},{"family":"Neis","given":"B."},{"family":"Palmer","given":"C.T."},{"family":"Schneider","given":"D.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1090,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZEF9RTGP"],"itemData":{"id":1090,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2014.07.025","page":"103-110","title":"Harvesters' perspectives on the management of British Columbia's giant red sea cucumber fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84906500216&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2014.07.025&partnerID=40&md5=b988fe097a05bddd512f0496ad485587","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"O'Regan","given":"S.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

* Asia-Pacific
The reviewed studies employed a wide range of indicators, most commonly catches (landings data), catch-per-unit-of-effort, fishing effort, abundance (density) and size of exploited fishing resources, besides socioeconomic indicators (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Almost two thirds (66) of the reviewed studies included indigenous and local knowledge-based indicators to inform fish abundance trends, catches, catch-per-unit-of-effort, sizes, fishing effort, perceptions on management or socioeconomic status, thus indicating the relevance of indigenous and local knowledge and collaboration with fishers for research on these small-scale fisheries.

3.3.1.4.2. The role of indigenous and local knowledge in small-scale fisheries

Despite the review provided here, it is also widely acknowledged that most small-scale fisheries remain unreported and unmonitored, resulting in the lack of longer time series data to evaluate their sustainability. This is especially the case in tropical countries and the Arctic, where small-scale fisheries are widespread. These data gaps can be overcome through collaborative research to record and analyze fishers’ local ecological knowledge, a form of indigenous and local knowledge based on an experiential understanding of one’s environment coupled with communal and historical use. Fishers’ local ecological knowledge contributes to estimates on temporal trends in abundance of fisheries resources, and can extend the time series available for the analysis to periods before scientific monitoring ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GXzPLD19","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Giglio, Luiz, & Gerhardinger, 2015; G. Hallwass, Schiavetti, & Silvano, 2019; Jahan, 2017; Maia, 2018; Stocks, Foster, Bat, Ha, & Vincent, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Giglio, Luiz, & Gerhardinger, 2015; G. Hallwass, Schiavetti, & Silvano, 2019; Jahan, 2017; Maia, 2018; Stocks, Foster, Bat, Ha, & Vincent, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1096,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CMEYXT5G"],"itemData":{"id":1096,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/acv.12178","issue":"4","page":"348-358","title":"Depletion of marine megafauna and shifting baselines among artisanal fishers in eastern Brazil","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84938979370&doi=10.1111%2facv.12178&partnerID=40&md5=5daebc5767b7e57c0d850bf3fd3998cd","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Giglio","given":"V.J."},{"family":"Luiz","given":"O.J."},{"family":"Gerhardinger","given":"L.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":382,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2T4NL3H7"],"itemData":{"id":382,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The local ecological knowledge (LEK) of ?shermen has been applied to reconstruct temporal patterns of resource abundance and to assess resource exploitation, especially in tropical ecosystems. Protected areas (PAs) are the main conservation measure in the Amazon, but their ef?cacy to improve ?sheries is poorly known. The main goal of this study was to apply ?shermen’ LEK to evaluate temporal changes in composition and abundance of exploited ?sh species among three different categories of PAs in the Tapajo s River, Brazilian Amazon. We interviewed 203 ?shers about their highest ?sh catches, and the main ?sh caught at two time periods: the beginning of their ?shing careers and currently. Fishers’ LEK indicated decreases in citations of larger-bodied ?shes and in reported catches over time, evidencing a gradient of conservation according to PA’s time of establishment and management rules. We noticed higher changes (reduced catches and smaller ?sh exploited) in the Environmental PA, established 10 years ago and lacking management plan and buffer zone. In National Forest, which was created 40 years ago with more restrictive management rules, ?sh composition and catches included larger commercial ?sh. The Extractive Reserve, which was created 15 years ago and lacks speci?c ?shing rules, may be in an intermediate stage, as large ?sh and highest catches decreased over time, but medium and large ?sh are still exploited. Fishers’ LEK can be an invaluable but neglected source of data on temporal patterns of ?shing pressure and conservation needs, especially in tropical megadiverse, poorly known and threatened rivers.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/acv.12504","ISSN":"1367-9430, 1469-1795","journalAbbreviation":"Anim Conserv","language":"en","page":"acv.12504","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers’ knowledge indicates temporal changes in composition and abundance of fishing resources in Amazon protected areas","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/acv.12504","author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"G."},{"family":"Schiavetti","given":"A."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"R. A. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,11]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31720,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KT6V78SQ"],"itemData":{"id":31720,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1007/s10668-015-9740-0","issue":"2","page":"461-478","title":"Fishers’ local knowledge on impact of climate change and anthropogenic interferences on Hilsa fishery in South Asia: evidence from Bangladesh","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84949506409&doi=10.1007%2fs10668-015-9740-0&partnerID=40&md5=3805100345ebc784b4cd141a9c618a65","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Jahan","given":"I."},{"family":"Ahsan","given":"D."},{"family":"Farque","given":"M.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":35160,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NZGZBQFV"],"itemData":{"id":35160,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2018.01.006","page":"133-142","title":"Shifting baselines among traditional fishers in São Tomé and Príncipe islands, Gulf of Guinea","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85041474163&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2018.01.006&partnerID=40&md5=6441cfd6731bcf1bc8fdf26bd1fe0344","volume":"154","author":[{"family":"Maia","given":"H.A."},{"family":"Morais","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Siqueira","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Hanazaki","given":"N."},{"family":"Floeter","given":"S.R."},{"family":"Bender","given":"M.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1084,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q3KYCIAX"],"itemData":{"id":1084,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-019-0073-8","issue":"3","page":"397-408","title":"Local Fishers’ Knowledge of Target and Incidental Seahorse Catch in Southern Vietnam","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85066014179&doi=10.1007%2fs10745-019-0073-8&partnerID=40&md5=e646d71a034db6d7d5c49c59e4974ea5","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Stocks","given":"A.P."},{"family":"Foster","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Bat","given":"N.K."},{"family":"Ha","given":"N.M."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A.C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or data ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YF2VVMBA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}enz\\\\uc0\\\\u8211{}Arroyo, Roberts, Torre, & Cari\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}o\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Olvera, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Sáenz–Arroyo, Roberts, Torre, & Cariño?Olvera, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31754,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LV6VR48N"],"itemData":{"id":31754,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","ISSN":"1467-2960","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Fish and Fisheries","page":"121-133","title":"Using fishers’ anecdotes, naturalists’ observations and grey literature to reassess marine species at risk: the case of the Gulf grouper in the Gulf of California, Mexico","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Sáenz–Arroyo","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"Callum M"},{"family":"Torre","given":"Jorge"},{"family":"Cariño?Olvera","given":"Micheline"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} were available. Indeed, in many cases worldwide fishers’ knowledge is the only available knowledge source. 
	In the last 20 years, several studies have recorded fisher indigenous and local knowledge and local ecological knowledge through using qualitative methods, such as interviews with fishers, to reconstruct temporal trends in fisheries resources. This was the case in 56 of the studies reviewed here. Through these studies data were collected from an aggregated total of 13,565 fishers (through interviews), on approximately 454 fish species in 32 countries worldwide (Table 3.3). All the studies further quantitatively analyzed fishers’ local ecological knowledge to identify trends in abundance, size and composition of fisheries resources through a series of indicators such as estimated abundance categories (declined, same, increased), catch per unit of effort, amounts of regular, poor and best catches, and size (length or weight) of largest ever caught (Table 3.3). 
