"DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2009.01395.x","issue":"3","page":"778-787","title":"Rapidly shifting baselines in yangtze fishing communities and local memory of extinct species","title-short":"Cambios rápidos en las directrices en comunidades de pescadores de yangtze y en la memoria local de especies extintas","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-77956587275&doi=10.1111%2fj.1523-1739.2009.01395.x&partnerID=40&md5=6fba0c49e423bd89f5d8031385ddfcbc","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Turvey","given":"S.T."},{"family":"Barrett","given":"L.A."},{"family":"Yujiang","given":"H."},{"family":"Lei","given":"Z."},{"family":"Xinqiao","given":"Z."},{"family":"Xianyan","given":"W."},{"family":"Yadong","given":"H."},{"family":"Kaiya","given":"Z."},{"family":"Hart","given":"T."},{"family":"Ding","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":"ERpTB65M/8Ejy5W82","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5932248/items/X3Q2PMC7"],"itemData":{"id":1989,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2016.05.013","page":"74-79","title":"Making history count: The shifting baselines of Turkish fisheries","volume":"183","author":[{"family":"Ulman","given":"A."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, further studies show that this is not always the case, and both older and younger fishers may hold similar perceptions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"abgNyGp9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barbosa-Filho, 2020; G. Hallwass, 2019; A. R. Ribeiro, 2021; Thurstan, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Barbosa-Filho, 2020; G. Hallwass, 2019; A. R. Ribeiro, 2021; Thurstan, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34918,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BAITNNUM"],"itemData":{"id":34918,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2019.105025","title":"Evidence of shifting baseline and Fisher judgment on lane snapper (Lutjanus synagris) management in a Brazilian marine protected area","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85074530717&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2019.105025&partnerID=40&md5=3c216b9020fa44d107f70dc930e50806","volume":"183","author":[{"family":"Barbosa-Filho","given":"M.L.V."},{"family":"Souza","given":"G.B.G.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Lopes","given":"S.D.F."},{"family":"Siciliano","given":"S."},{"family":"Hauser Davis","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Mourão","given":"J.D.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":382,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2T4NL3H7"],"itemData":{"id":382,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The local ecological knowledge (LEK) of ?shermen has been applied to reconstruct temporal patterns of resource abundance and to assess resource exploitation, especially in tropical ecosystems. Protected areas (PAs) are the main conservation measure in the Amazon, but their ef?cacy to improve ?sheries is poorly known. The main goal of this study was to apply ?shermen’ LEK to evaluate temporal changes in composition and abundance of exploited ?sh species among three different categories of PAs in the Tapajo s River, Brazilian Amazon. We interviewed 203 ?shers about their highest ?sh catches, and the main ?sh caught at two time periods: the beginning of their ?shing careers and currently. Fishers’ LEK indicated decreases in citations of larger-bodied ?shes and in reported catches over time, evidencing a gradient of conservation according to PA’s time of establishment and management rules. We noticed higher changes (reduced catches and smaller ?sh exploited) in the Environmental PA, established 10 years ago and lacking management plan and buffer zone. In National Forest, which was created 40 years ago with more restrictive management rules, ?sh composition and catches included larger commercial ?sh. The Extractive Reserve, which was created 15 years ago and lacks speci?c ?shing rules, may be in an intermediate stage, as large ?sh and highest catches decreased over time, but medium and large ?sh are still exploited. Fishers’ LEK can be an invaluable but neglected source of data on temporal patterns of ?shing pressure and conservation needs, especially in tropical megadiverse, poorly known and threatened rivers.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/acv.12504","ISSN":"1367-9430, 1469-1795","journalAbbreviation":"Anim Conserv","language":"en","page":"acv.12504","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers’ knowledge indicates temporal changes in composition and abundance of fishing resources in Amazon protected areas","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/acv.12504","author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"G."},{"family":"Schiavetti","given":"A."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"R. A. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,11]]}}},{"id":31772,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N9LG9VE2"],"itemData":{"id":31772,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2021.105543","ISSN":"09645691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"105543","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers’ ecological knowledge to support conservation of reef fish (groupers) in the tropical Atlantic","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0964569121000284","volume":"204","author":[{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"Ariane R."},{"family":"Damasio","given":"Ludmila M.A."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato A.M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",4]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1086,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NXEGHAJ6"],"itemData":{"id":1086,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12184","issue":"2","page":"98-105","title":"Setting the Record Straight: Assessing the Reliability of Retrospective Accounts of Change","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84930606535&doi=10.1111%2fconl.12184&partnerID=40&md5=4f47f3cb3afe567c2edb385c92566cf2","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Thurstan","given":"R.H."},{"family":"Buckley","given":"S.M."},{"family":"Ortiz","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Pandolfi","given":"J.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Furthermore, fishers also report stable catches or sizes of at least some fish species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dktwXsvJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. R. Ribeiro, 2021a; Renato Azevedo Matias Silvano & Hallwass, 2020)","plainCitation":"(A. R. Ribeiro, 2021a; Renato Azevedo Matias Silvano & Hallwass, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31772,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N9LG9VE2"],"itemData":{"id":31772,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2021.105543","ISSN":"09645691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"105543","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers’ ecological knowledge to support conservation of reef fish (groupers) in the tropical Atlantic","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0964569121000284","volume":"204","author":[{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"Ariane R."},{"family":"Damasio","given":"Ludmila M.A."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato A.M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",4]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31770,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C48UQ3I7"],"itemData":{"id":31770,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Freshwater small-scale fisheries sustain millions of livelihoods worldwide, but a lack of monitoring makes it difficult to check the sustainability of these fisheries. We aim to compare and describe participatory research methods used in studies with fishers in the Tapajos River, a poorly known tropical river in the Brazilian Amazon. We address three interview approaches, two ways to do fisheries monitoring and two approaches for georeferenced mapping based on fishers’ knowledge, which can provide data about at least 16 topics related to fisheries. We highlight major advantages and shortcomings of these methods and illustrate their potential with examples of results on fisheries and fish biology of Peacock bass (Cichla spp. tucunaré in Brazil), an important commercial fish in the Brazilian Amazon. The interviews, participatory monitoring and mapping revealed which fish are more valued by local communities, how fish abundance and sizes varied over time, when fish are more often caught and show reproductive activity, and which sites or habitats fish need to reproduce. In addition to providing useful data from many sites in a cost-effective way, participatory methods can bring the additional benefit of including local stakeholders in the monitoring, management, and research activities.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su12114487","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","language":"en","page":"4487","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Participatory Research with Fishers to Improve Knowledge on Small-Scale Fisheries in Tropical Rivers","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/12/11/4487","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato Azevedo Matias"},{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Limitations related to the application of fishers’ local ecological knowledge to estimate abundance trends include heavy reliance on fishers’ memories, which at time may be inaccurate or biased due to memory illusion or shifting baseline syndrome ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a69av5k5o5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tim M Daw, 2011; O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Donnell, Molloy, & Vincent, 2012b; Papworth, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Tim M Daw, 2011; O’Donnell, Molloy, & Vincent, 2012b; Papworth, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1056,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DMS2SLKC"],"itemData":{"id":1056,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","ISSN":"1469-4387","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Conservation","page":"75-88","title":"Perceptions of trends in Seychelles artisanal trap fisheries: comparing catch monitoring, underwater visual census and fishers' knowledge","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Daw","given":"Tim M"},{"family":"Robinson","given":"JAN"},{"family":"Graham","given":"Nicholas AJ"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":31716,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IMNC98Q2"],"itemData":{"id":31716,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1080/08920753.2012.727734","issue":"6","page":"594-611","title":"Comparing Fisher Interviews, Logbooks, and Catch Landings Estimates of Extraction Rates in a Small-Scale Fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84868265819&doi=10.1080%2f08920753.2012.727734&partnerID=40&md5=ad8d271ce5cfe17e3269d685d23c03d3","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"O'Donnell","given":"K.P."},{"family":"Molloy","given":"P.P."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A.C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31755,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V6FSTJV3"],"itemData":{"id":31755,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/j.1755-263X.2009.00049.x","ISSN":"1755263X, 1755263X","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Evidence for shifting baseline syndrome in conservation","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1755-263X.2009.00049.x","author":[{"family":"Papworth","given":"S.K."},{"family":"Rist","given":"J."},{"family":"Coad","given":"L."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, it should be noted that the ways in which local ecological knowledge and indigenous and local knowledge data are collected include methods for minimizing bias such as data triangulation amongst community members, data comparisons with archival and spatial data, and sampling techniques intended to identify the most robust knowledge holders. It is also quite common to search for points of comparison between indigenous and local knowledge/ local ecological knowledge and scientific knowledge. For example, more than half (29) of the reviewed studies included conventional scientific databases, such as biological sampling, fish catches, or governmental monitoring, which were compared with data gathered from fishers (Table 3.3). Although disagreements or partial agreements were observed in eight studies, most studies (21) showed high levels of agreement between trends based on local ecological knowledge and those based on scientific data (Table 3.3). This further reinforces the usefulness and reliability of fishers’ local ecological knowledge to evaluate temporal trends in fisheries. 
	Other studies integrated fishers’ local ecological knowledge and conventional scientific data in models to show fisheries trends ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1qoteaqafr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ainsworth, 2011; Ban, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Ainsworth, 2011; Ban, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1073,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RR3EFF6E"],"itemData":{"id":1073,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine and Coastal Fisheries","DOI":"10.1080/19425120.2010.549047","issue":"1","page":"190-218","title":"Quantifying species abundance trends in the Northern Gulf of California using local ecological knowledge","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84855928642&doi=10.1080%2f19425120.2010.549047&partnerID=40&md5=348459b7653242a2240a4ee2d13e81a2","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Ainsworth","given":"C.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":31728,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GT85P9S4"],"itemData":{"id":31728,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Introduction: Fisheries management is often data-limited, and conducted at spatial scales that are too large to address the needs of Indigenous peoples, whose cultures depend upon the local availability of marine resources.","call-number":"Esub 21h","container-title":"Ecosystem Health and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1080/20964129.2017.1379887","ISSN":"2096-4129, 2332-8878","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystem Health and Sustainability","language":"English","page":"1379887","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Indigenous knowledge as data for modern fishery management: a case study of Dungeness crab in Pacific Canada","title-short":"Indigenous knowledge as data for modern fishery management","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/20964129.2017.1379887","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C"},{"family":"Eckert","given":"Lauren"},{"family":"McGreer","given":"Madeleine"},{"family":"Frid","given":"Alejandro"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A few studies also analyzed and observed temporal changes in the composition of fishing resources (Table 3.3), usually indicating a shift from the exploitation of more valuable large fish to less valuable smaller fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1m0dfeiur8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Coll, 2014; Godoy, Gelcich, Vasquez, & Castilla, 2010; G. Hallwass, 2019; Jaiteh, Hordyk, Braccini, Warren, & Loneragan, 2017; Strieder Philippsen, Minte-Vera, Okada, Carvalho, & Angelini, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Coll, 2014; Godoy, Gelcich, Vasquez, & Castilla, 2010; G. Hallwass, 2019; Jaiteh, Hordyk, Braccini, Warren, & Loneragan, 2017; Strieder Philippsen, Minte-Vera, Okada, Carvalho, & Angelini, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1050,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8NI83NV9"],"itemData":{"id":1050,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0085670","issue":"1","title":"Assessing fishing and marine biodiversity changes using fishers' perceptions: The Spanish Mediterranean and Gulf of Cadiz case study","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84896126576&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0085670&partnerID=40&md5=2745efc7ff84323cc6812d9d7b14f52e","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Coll","given":"M."},{"family":"Carreras","given":"M."},{"family":"Ciércoles","given":"C."},{"family":"Cornax","given":"M.-J."},{"family":"Gorelli","given":"G."},{"family":"Morote","given":"E."},{"family":"Sáez","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":1078,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3N87JPA4"],"itemData":{"id":1078,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ecological Applications","DOI":"10.1890/09-1806.1","issue":"6","page":"1504-1511","title":"Spearfishing to depletion: Evidence from temperate reef fishes in Chile","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-77956444151&doi=10.1890%2f09-1806.1&partnerID=40&md5=ba4f262bec2f87464ce70138d8ebbe05","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Godoy","given":"N."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Vasquez","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Castilla","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":382,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2T4NL3H7"],"itemData":{"id":382,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The local ecological knowledge (LEK) of ?shermen has been applied to reconstruct temporal patterns of resource abundance and to assess resource exploitation, especially in tropical ecosystems. Protected areas (PAs) are the main conservation measure in the Amazon, but their ef?cacy to improve ?sheries is poorly known. The main goal of this study was to apply ?shermen’ LEK to evaluate temporal changes in composition and abundance of exploited ?sh species among three different categories of PAs in the Tapajo s River, Brazilian Amazon. We interviewed 203 ?shers about their highest ?sh catches, and the main ?sh caught at two time periods: the beginning of their ?shing careers and currently. Fishers’ LEK indicated decreases in citations of larger-bodied ?shes and in reported catches over time, evidencing a gradient of conservation according to PA’s time of establishment and management rules. We noticed higher changes (reduced catches and smaller ?sh exploited) in the Environmental PA, established 10 years ago and lacking management plan and buffer zone. In National Forest, which was created 40 years ago with more restrictive management rules, ?sh composition and catches included larger commercial ?sh. The Extractive Reserve, which was created 15 years ago and lacks speci?c ?shing rules, may be in an intermediate stage, as large ?sh and highest catches decreased over time, but medium and large ?sh are still exploited. Fishers’ LEK can be an invaluable but neglected source of data on temporal patterns of ?shing pressure and conservation needs, especially in tropical megadiverse, poorly known and threatened rivers.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/acv.12504","ISSN":"1367-9430, 1469-1795","journalAbbreviation":"Anim Conserv","language":"en","page":"acv.12504","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers’ knowledge indicates temporal changes in composition and abundance of fishing resources in Amazon protected areas","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/acv.12504","author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"G."},{"family":"Schiavetti","given":"A."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"R. A. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,11]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1053,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2KGS695T"],"itemData":{"id":1053,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsw170","issue":"1","page":"242-253","title":"Shark finning in eastern Indonesia: Assessing the sustainability of a data-poor fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85020604458&doi=10.1093%2ficesjms%2ffsw170&partnerID=40&md5=ce2394ff4f77b9989abadbd6d039e806","volume":"74","author":[{"family":"Jaiteh","given":"V.F."},{"family":"Hordyk","given":"A.R."},{"family":"Braccini","given":"M."},{"family":"Warren","given":"C."},{"family":"Loneragan","given":"N.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M3NVXDYA"],"itemData":{"id":1087,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine and Freshwater Research","DOI":"10.1071/MF16053","issue":"5","page":"980-992","title":"Fishers' and scientific histories: An example of consensus from an inland fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85018262780&doi=10.1071%2fMF16053&partnerID=40&md5=6e52ce85acf3e8f7dedaf166b96e8e14","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Strieder Philippsen","given":"J."},{"family":"Minte-Vera","given":"C.V."},{"family":"Okada","given":"E.K."},{"family":"Carvalho","given":"A.R."},{"family":"Angelini","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or the disappearance of some species altogether ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a25cg2v4vo8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Damasio, Lopes, Guariento, & Carvalho, 2015; Katikiro, 2014; Lavides, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Damasio, Lopes, Guariento, & Carvalho, 2015; Katikiro, 2014; Lavides, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31730,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BJUS6UF3"],"itemData":{"id":31730,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Background In small-scale fishery, information provided by fishers has been useful to complement current and past lack of knowledge on species and environment.\\nMethodology Through interviews, 82 fishers from the largest fishing communities on the north and south borders of a Brazilian northeastern coastal state provided estimates of the catch per unit effort (CPUE) and rank of species abundance of their main target fishes for three time points: current year (2013 at the time of the research), 10, and 20 years past. This information was contrasted to other available data sources: scientific sampling of fish landing (2013), governmental statistics (2003), and information provided by expert fishers (1993), respectively. Principal Findings Fishers were more accurate when reporting information about their maximum CPUE for 2013, but except for three species, which they estimated accurately, fishers overestimated their mean CPUE per species. Fishers were also accurate at establishing ranks of abundance of their main target species for all periods. Fishers' beliefs that fish abundance has not changed over the last 10 years (2003–2013) were corroborated by governmental and scientific landing data.\\nConclusions The comparison between official and formal landing records and fishers' perceptions revealed that fishers are accurate when reporting maximum CPUE, but not when reporting mean CPUE. Moreover, fishers are less precise the less common a species is in their","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0133122","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e0133122","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Matching Fishers’ Knowledge and Landing Data to Overcome Data Missing in Small-Scale Fisheries","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0133122","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Damasio","given":"Ludmila de Melo Alves"},{"family":"Lopes","given":"Priscila F. M."},{"family":"Guariento","given":"Rafael D."},{"family":"Carvalho","given":"Adriana R."}],"editor":[{"family":"Stergiou","given":"Konstantinos I"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",7,15]]}}},{"id":1095,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4DZCGZPB"],"itemData":{"id":1095,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2014.01.009","page":"23-31","title":"Perceptions on the shifting baseline among coastal fishers of Tanga, Northeast Tanzania","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84894104446&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2014.01.009&partnerID=40&md5=30155e564b8000d55420d52f131dac00","volume":"91","author":[{"family":"Katikiro","given":"R.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1065,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7HUHCJDP"],"itemData":{"id":1065,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0155752","issue":"5","title":"Patterns of coral-reef finfish species disappearances inferred from fishers' knowledge in global epicentre of marine shorefish diversity","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84969794078&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0155752&partnerID=40&md5=5537fabe2d29a70ac4c56d7670e59cdf","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Lavides","given":"M.N."},{"family":"Molina","given":"E.P.V."},{"family":"De La Rosa","given":"G.E."},{"family":"Mill","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Rushton","given":"S.P."},{"family":"Stead","given":"S.M."},{"family":"Polunin","given":"N.V.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These temporal changes in catch composition (Table 3.3) suggest that fisheries may have experienced ‘ecological filters’ in some freshwater and marine ecosystems, indicating genetic selection through specific forms of harvesting activities. The extent to which species diversity or specific species characteristics are affected in this way is uncertain.
	Some of the reviewed studies have also provided useful information based on fishers’ local ecological knowledge related to drivers or consequences of observed trends, including protected areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LHNVR01I","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. Hallwass, 2020)","plainCitation":"(G. Hallwass, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":382,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2T4NL3H7"],"itemData":{"id":382,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The local ecological knowledge (LEK) of ?shermen has been applied to reconstruct temporal patterns of resource abundance and to assess resource exploitation, especially in tropical ecosystems. Protected areas (PAs) are the main conservation measure in the Amazon, but their ef?cacy to improve ?sheries is poorly known. The main goal of this study was to apply ?shermen’ LEK to evaluate temporal changes in composition and abundance of exploited ?sh species among three different categories of PAs in the Tapajo s River, Brazilian Amazon. We interviewed 203 ?shers about their highest ?sh catches, and the main ?sh caught at two time periods: the beginning of their ?shing careers and currently. Fishers’ LEK indicated decreases in citations of larger-bodied ?shes and in reported catches over time, evidencing a gradient of conservation according to PA’s time of establishment and management rules. We noticed higher changes (reduced catches and smaller ?sh exploited) in the Environmental PA, established 10 years ago and lacking management plan and buffer zone. In National Forest, which was created 40 years ago with more restrictive management rules, ?sh composition and catches included larger commercial ?sh. The Extractive Reserve, which was created 15 years ago and lacks speci?c ?shing rules, may be in an intermediate stage, as large ?sh and highest catches decreased over time, but medium and large ?sh are still exploited. Fishers’ LEK can be an invaluable but neglected source of data on temporal patterns of ?shing pressure and conservation needs, especially in tropical megadiverse, poorly known and threatened rivers.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/acv.12504","ISSN":"1367-9430, 1469-1795","journalAbbreviation":"Anim Conserv","language":"en","page":"acv.12504","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers’ knowledge indicates temporal changes in composition and abundance of fishing resources in Amazon protected areas","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/acv.12504","author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"G."},{"family":"Schiavetti","given":"A."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"R. A. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, environmental impacts including dams or pollution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2Xwd59OF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Dey, Choudhary, Dey, Deshpande, & Kelkar, 2019; Frezza & Clem, 2015; Gustavo Hallwass, Lopes, Juras, & Silvano, 2013; Jahan, 2017; Strieder Philippsen, 2017)","plainCitation":"(S. Dey, Choudhary, Dey, Deshpande, & Kelkar, 2019; Frezza & Clem, 2015; Gustavo Hallwass, Lopes, Juras, & Silvano, 2013; Jahan, 2017; Strieder Philippsen, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31722,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B7LY9ZDB"],"itemData":{"id":31722,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/fme.12390","title":"Identifying potential causes of fish declines through local ecological knowledge of fishers in the Ganga River, eastern Bihar, India","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85073981596&doi=10.1111%2ffme.12390&partnerID=40&md5=d83c7fb55176c2a9a0f52522ab60b13d","author":[{"family":"Dey","given":"S."},{"family":"Choudhary","given":"S.K."},{"family":"Dey","given":"S."},{"family":"Deshpande","given":"K."},{"family":"Kelkar","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1079,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QIJBCCEH"],"itemData":{"id":1079,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environmental Biology of Fishes","DOI":"10.1007/s10641-015-0442-0","issue":"11","page":"2187-2202","title":"Using local fishers’ knowledge to characterize historical trends in the Florida Bay bonefish population and fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84943663521&doi=10.1007%2fs10641-015-0442-0&partnerID=40&md5=39b27769a2bf29f9dd34fa965ba20478","volume":"98","author":[{"family":"Frezza","given":"P.E."},{"family":"Clem","given":"S.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31729,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4TNV83FK"],"itemData":{"id":31729,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The long-term impacts of large hydroelectric dams on small-scale ?sheries in tropical rivers are poorly known. A promising way to investigate such impacts is to compare and integrate the local ecological knowledge (LEK) of resource users with biological data for the same region. We analyzed the accuracy of ?shers’ LEK to investigate ?sheries dynamics and environmental changes in the Lower Tocantins River (Brazilian Amazon) downstream from a large dam. We estimated ?shers’ LEK through interviews with 300 ?shers in nine villages and collected data on 601 ?sh landings in ?ve of these villages, 22 years after the dam’s establishment (2006–2008). We compared these two databases with each other and with data on ?sh landings from before the dam’s establishment (1981) gathered from the literature. The data obtained based on the ?shers’ LEK (interviews) and from ?sheries agreed regarding the primary ?sh species caught, the most commonly used type of ?shing gear (gill nets) and even the most often used gill net mesh sizes but disagreed regarding seasonal ?sh abundance. According to the interviewed ?shers, the primary environmental changes that occurred after the impoundment were an overall decrease in ?sh abundance, an increase in the abundance of some ?sh species and, possibly, the local extinction of a commercial ?sh species (Semaprochilodus brama). These changes were corroborated by comparing ?sh landings sampled before and 22 years after the impoundment, which indicated changes in the composition of ?sh landings and a decrease in the total annual ?sh production. Our results reinforce the hypothesis that large dams may adversely affect small-scale ?sheries downstream and establish a feasible approach for applying ?shers’ LEK to ?sheries management, especially in regions with a low research capacity.","container-title":"Ecological Applications","DOI":"10.1890/12-0429.1","ISSN":"1051-0761","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Applications","language":"en","page":"392-407","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers' knowledge identifies environmental changes and fish abundance trends in impounded tropical rivers","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1890/12-0429.1","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Lopes","given":"Priscila F."},{"family":"Juras","given":"Anastácio A."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato A. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",3]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31720,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KT6V78SQ"],"itemData":{"id":31720,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1007/s10668-015-9740-0","issue":"2","page":"461-478","title":"Fishers’ local knowledge on impact of climate change and anthropogenic interferences on Hilsa fishery in South Asia: evidence from Bangladesh","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84949506409&doi=10.1007%2fs10668-015-9740-0&partnerID=40&md5=3805100345ebc784b4cd141a9c618a65","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Jahan","given":"I."},{"family":"Ahsan","given":"D."},{"family":"Farque","given":"M.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M3NVXDYA"],"itemData":{"id":1087,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine and Freshwater Research","DOI":"10.1071/MF16053","issue":"5","page":"980-992","title":"Fishers' and scientific histories: An example of consensus from an inland fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85018262780&doi=10.1071%2fMF16053&partnerID=40&md5=6e52ce85acf3e8f7dedaf166b96e8e14","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Strieder Philippsen","given":"J."},{"family":"Minte-Vera","given":"C.V."},{"family":"Okada","given":"E.K."},{"family":"Carvalho","given":"A.R."},{"family":"Angelini","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, climate change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2iepftqe6a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ernesto Azzurro, Moschella, & Maynou, 2011; Eckert, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Ernesto Azzurro, Moschella, & Maynou, 2011; Eckert, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31777,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CQPBZEUH"],"itemData":{"id":31777,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0024885","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e24885","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Tracking Signals of Change in Mediterranean Fish Diversity Based on Local Ecological Knowledge","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0024885","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Azzurro","given":"Ernesto"},{"family":"Moschella","given":"Paula"},{"family":"Maynou","given":"Francesc"}],"editor":[{"family":"Merenlender","given":"Adina Maya"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",9,22]]}}},{"id":31727,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WE8ZZ9T4"],"itemData":{"id":31727,"type":"article-journal","call-number":"Esub 21f","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2834","ISSN":"10527613","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Aquatic Conserv: Mar Freshw Ecosyst","language":"English","page":"158-166","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Diving back in time: Extending historical baselines for yelloweye rockfish with Indigenous knowledge","title-short":"Diving back in time","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/aqc.2834","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Eckert","given":"Lauren E."},{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C."},{"family":"Frid","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"McGreer","given":"Madeleine"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, distribution and ecology of invasive species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YZhQWZ2q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Araujo Catelani, Petry, Mayer Pelicice, & Azevedo Matias Silvano, 2021; Ernesto Azzurro & Cerri, 2021; Boughedir, 2015; van Putten, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Araujo Catelani, Petry, Mayer Pelicice, & Azevedo Matias Silvano, 2021; Ernesto Azzurro & Cerri, 2021; Boughedir, 2015; van Putten, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31762,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JFP7KPGK"],"itemData":{"id":31762,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ethnobiology and Conservation","DOI":"10.15451/ec2020-09-10.04-1-16","ISSN":"22384782","journalAbbreviation":"Ethnobio Conserv","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers’ knowledge on the ecology, impacts and benefits of the non-native peacock bass Cichla kelberi in a coastal river in southeastern Brazil","URL":"https://ethnobioconservation.com/index.php/ebc/article/view/319/297","author":[{"family":"Araujo Catelani","given":"Paula"},{"family":"Petry","given":"Ana Cristina"},{"family":"Mayer Pelicice","given":"Fernando"},{"family":"Azevedo Matias Silvano","given":"Renado"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,26]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31776,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SMIZBXRB"],"itemData":{"id":31776,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2021.104412","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"104412","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Participatory mapping of invasive species: A demonstration in a coastal lagoon","title-short":"Participatory mapping of invasive species","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X21000221","volume":"126","author":[{"family":"Azzurro","given":"Ernesto"},{"family":"Cerri","given":"Jacopo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",4]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31775,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/URTE3XKP"],"itemData":{"id":31775,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Local Ecological Knowledge of 92 professional fishers from Tunisia and Libya was used to investigate on the occurrence and establishment of the exotic Hemiramphus far and Saurida undosquamis along the southern Mediterranean coasts. According to fisherman’s knowledge, H. far and S. undosquamis appeared in Libya, in 1980 and 1989 respectively. In Tunisia these species were observed afterwards, in 2004 and 2007. Currently both H. far and S. undosquamis result to be well distributed and established, along the entire surveyed area, from Tubrouk (eastern Libya) to Tabarka (western Tunisia). A statistical analysis of the qualitative trends in abundance perceived by the respondents show that both species have significantly increased in abundance in Tunisia. In Libya an increase of the abundance of H. far was also apparent during the first decade of the 21st century but the current abundance of S. undosquamis resulted to be stable at the level of occasional captures. Given the lack of regular environmental monitoring programmes in the area, these findings provide information that could not have been obtained otherwise. Besides improving our understanding on the status and chronology of these invasions, this approach highlights the value of fisherman’s knowledge to reconstruct ecological process in the course of rapid historical modifications.","container-title":"Mediterranean Marine Science","DOI":"10.12681/mms.1179","ISSN":"1791-6763, 1108-393X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Medit. Mar. Sci.","page":"628","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Tracking the invasion of Hemiramphus far and Saurida undosquamis along the southern Mediterranean coasts: A Local Ecological Knowledge study","title-short":"Tracking the invasion of Hemiramphus far and Saurida undosquamis along the southern Mediterranean coasts","URL":"https://ejournals.epublishing.ekt.gr/index.php/hcmr-med-mar-sc/article/view/13011","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Boughedir","given":"W."},{"family":"Rifi","given":"M."},{"family":"Shakman","given":"E."},{"family":"Maynou","given":"F."},{"family":"Ghanem","given":"R."},{"family":"Ben Souissi","given":"J."},{"family":"Azzurro","given":"E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",10,13]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31774,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G6H7QAT6"],"itemData":{"id":31774,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            The changing geographical distribution of species, or range shift, is one of the better documented fingerprints of climate change in the marine environment. Range shifts may also lead to dramatic changes in the distribution of economic, social, and cultural opportunities. These challenge marine resource users' capacity to adapt to a changing climate and managers' ability to implement adaptation plans. In particular, a reluctance to attribute marine range shift to climate change can undermine the effectiveness of climate change communications and pose a potential barrier to successful adaptation. Attribution is a known powerful predictor of behavioural intention. Understanding the cognitive processes that underpin the formation of marine resource users' beliefs about the cause of observed marine range shift phenomena is therefore an important topic for research. An examination of the attribution by marine resource users of three types of range shifts experienced in a marine climate change hotspot in southeast Australia to various climate and non-climate drivers indicates the existence of at least three contributing cognitions. These are: (i) engrained mental representations of environmental phenomena, (ii) scientific complexity in the attribution pathway, and (iii) dissonance from the positive or negative nature of the impact. All three play a part in explaining the complex pattern of attribution of marine climate change range shifts, and should be considered when planning for engagement with stakeholders and managers around adaptation to climate change.","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsv192","ISSN":"1054-3139, 1095-9289","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"1306-1318","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Empirical evidence for different cognitive effects in explaining the attribution of marine range shifts to climate change","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/icesjms/article/73/5/1306/2240649","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Putten","given":"Ingrid E.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Frusher","given":"Stewart"},{"family":"Fulton","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Hobday","given":"Alistair J."},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Sarah M."},{"family":"Metcalf","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Pecl","given":"Gretta T."},{"literal":"Handling editor: Sarah Kraak"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",5,1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, or trophic cascades associated with fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pmtc2dyW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Boudreau & Worm, 2010; Ulman & Pauly, 2016b)","plainCitation":"(Boudreau & Worm, 2010; Ulman & Pauly, 2016b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1099,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3CFU3TBU"],"itemData":{"id":1099,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Ecology Progress Series","DOI":"10.3354/meps08473","page":"181-191","title":"Top-down control of lobster in the Gulf of Maine: Insights from local ecological knowledge and research surveys","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-77954105646&doi=10.3354%2fmeps08473&partnerID=40&md5=b27c208390e1c0a22f2c17d64481585e","volume":"403","author":[{"family":"Boudreau","given":"S.A."},{"family":"Worm","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1044,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9F96IVMS"],"itemData":{"id":1044,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2016.05.013","page":"74-79","title":"Making history count: The shifting baselines of Turkish fisheries","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84969921503&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2016.05.013&partnerID=40&md5=60729f97b02cad008a35d8873df8a3ab","volume":"183","author":[{"family":"Ulman","given":"A."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
