Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/fme.12006","issue":"1","page":"58-67","title":"Do traditional fishers recognise reef fish species declines? Shifting environmental baselines in Eastern Brazil","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84872554669&doi=10.1111%2ffme.12006&partnerID=40&md5=0bd0067395f01905acbcff8ba284b983","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Bender","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Floeter","given":"S.R."},{"family":"Hanazaki","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; [50] ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"P51KTs8F","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Zapelini, 2019)","plainCitation":"(C. Zapelini, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31714,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M7YTU3FC"],"itemData":{"id":31714,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.3224","issue":"12","page":"2092-2106","title":"Tracking interactions: Shifting baseline and fisheries networks in the largest Southwestern Atlantic reef system","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85075235046&doi=10.1002%2faqc.3224&partnerID=40&md5=eeaaed583f0f9e4cf22aecb4d48babf4","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Zapelini","given":"C."},{"family":"Bender","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Giglio","given":"V.J."},{"family":"Schiavetti","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; [51] ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4WWbr8Cr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bender, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Bender, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1069,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N38WH2T5"],"itemData":{"id":1069,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0110332","issue":"10","title":"Local ecological knowledge and scientific data reveal overexploitation by multigear artisanal fisheries in the Southwestern Atlantic","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84908036980&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0110332&partnerID=40&md5=1042537e25634433482a32f14519a77e","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Bender","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Machado","given":"G.R."},{"family":"De Azevedo Silva","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Floeter","given":"S.R."},{"family":"Monteiro-Netto","given":"C."},{"family":"Luiz","given":"O.J."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"C.E.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; [52] ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2R8U59Zq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cleverson Zapelini, Giglio, Carvalho, Bender, & Gerhardinger, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Cleverson Zapelini, Giglio, Carvalho, Bender, & Gerhardinger, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1062,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5ICRCVGS"],"itemData":{"id":1062,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","ISSN":"0278-0771","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnobiology","page":"478-493","title":"Assessing Fishing Experts' Knowledge to Improve Conservation Strategies for an Endangered Grouper in the Southwestern Atlantic","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Zapelini","given":"Cleverson"},{"family":"Giglio","given":"Vinicius J"},{"family":"Carvalho","given":"Renata C"},{"family":"Bender","given":"Mariana G"},{"family":"Gerhardinger","given":"Leopoldo C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; [53] ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LSVgjDnb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Castello, Arantes, Mcgrath, Stewart, & Sousa, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Castello, Arantes, Mcgrath, Stewart, & Sousa, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34932,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GCXDDA7J","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V275GCVY"],"itemData":{"id":34932,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2491","ISSN":"10527613","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Aquatic Conserv: Mar. Freshw. Ecosyst.","language":"en","page":"587-598","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Understanding fishing-induced extinctions in the Amazon: FISHING-INDUCED EXTINCTIONS IN THE AMAZON","title-short":"Understanding fishing-induced extinctions in the Amazon","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/aqc.2491","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Arantes","given":"Caroline Chaves"},{"family":"Mcgrath","given":"David Gibbs"},{"family":"Stewart","given":"Donald James"},{"family":"Sousa","given":"Fabio Sarmento De"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; [54] ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xJPkAExu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gustavo Hallwass, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Gustavo Hallwass, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31729,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4TNV83FK"],"itemData":{"id":31729,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The long-term impacts of large hydroelectric dams on small-scale ?sheries in tropical rivers are poorly known. A promising way to investigate such impacts is to compare and integrate the local ecological knowledge (LEK) of resource users with biological data for the same region. We analyzed the accuracy of ?shers’ LEK to investigate ?sheries dynamics and environmental changes in the Lower Tocantins River (Brazilian Amazon) downstream from a large dam. We estimated ?shers’ LEK through interviews with 300 ?shers in nine villages and collected data on 601 ?sh landings in ?ve of these villages, 22 years after the dam’s establishment (2006–2008). We compared these two databases with each other and with data on ?sh landings from before the dam’s establishment (1981) gathered from the literature. The data obtained based on the ?shers’ LEK (interviews) and from ?sheries agreed regarding the primary ?sh species caught, the most commonly used type of ?shing gear (gill nets) and even the most often used gill net mesh sizes but disagreed regarding seasonal ?sh abundance. According to the interviewed ?shers, the primary environmental changes that occurred after the impoundment were an overall decrease in ?sh abundance, an increase in the abundance of some ?sh species and, possibly, the local extinction of a commercial ?sh species (Semaprochilodus brama). These changes were corroborated by comparing ?sh landings sampled before and 22 years after the impoundment, which indicated changes in the composition of ?sh landings and a decrease in the total annual ?sh production. Our results reinforce the hypothesis that large dams may adversely affect small-scale ?sheries downstream and establish a feasible approach for applying ?shers’ LEK to ?sheries management, especially in regions with a low research capacity.","container-title":"Ecological Applications","DOI":"10.1890/12-0429.1","ISSN":"1051-0761","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Applications","language":"en","page":"392-407","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers' knowledge identifies environmental changes and fish abundance trends in impounded tropical rivers","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1890/12-0429.1","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Lopes","given":"Priscila F."},{"family":"Juras","given":"Anastácio A."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato A. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; [55] ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"keYbVTLq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Godoy, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Godoy, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1078,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3N87JPA4"],"itemData":{"id":1078,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ecological Applications","DOI":"10.1890/09-1806.1","issue":"6","page":"1504-1511","title":"Spearfishing to depletion: Evidence from temperate reef fishes in Chile","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-77956444151&doi=10.1890%2f09-1806.1&partnerID=40&md5=ba4f262bec2f87464ce70138d8ebbe05","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Godoy","given":"N."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Vasquez","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Castilla","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; [56] ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1tNirO2D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Castellanos-Galindo, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Castellanos-Galindo, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34936,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V3RSFWM9"],"itemData":{"id":34936,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2871","issue":"2","page":"305-314","title":"Using landing statistics and fishers’ traditional ecological knowledge to assess conservation threats to Pacific goliath grouper in Colombia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85040966191&doi=10.1002%2faqc.2871&partnerID=40&md5=7c716bd7c7f8caccba490fdee8f0de1a","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Castellanos-Galindo","given":"G.A."},{"family":"Chong-Montenegro","given":"C."},{"family":"Baos E","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Zapata","given":"L.A."},{"family":"Tompkins","given":"P."},{"family":"Graham","given":"R.T."},{"family":"Craig","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
3.3.1.4.3. Pelagic fisheries for forage fish

Small pelagic fish populations, also called forage fish, such as sardine, capelin, anchovy, herring and mackerel, provide about 25% of the total annual production of capture fisheries worldwide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"anbupj09bu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020d)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These resources contribute significantly to the well-being of coastal communities around the world, particularly in developing countries. Small pelagic fish are plankton feeders and represent the main prey items for several predators (piscivorous fish including sharks, mammals and birds), and play a key role in marine ecosystems by sustaining numerous higher trophic level species, many of which are commercially targeted ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a8l75q92ev","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alder, Campbell, Karpouzi, Kaschner, & Pauly, 2008; Bakun, Babcock, Lluch-Cota, Santora, & Salvadeo, 2010; Essington, 2015; A. D. M. Smith, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Alder, Campbell, Karpouzi, Kaschner, & Pauly, 2008; Bakun, Babcock, Lluch-Cota, Santora, & Salvadeo, 2010; Essington, 2015; A. D. M. Smith, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1257,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HS2H3KTH"],"itemData":{"id":1257,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fisheries targeting small-to-medium pelagic, so-called forage ?sh, impact on human food security and marine ecosystems. Because their operations are shrouded by the myth that forage ?sh are unsuitable for human consumption, the role of these ?sheries in intensive food production is not well understood or appreciated. Thus, although they account for over 30% of global landings of marine ?sh annually, our knowledge of how these levels of removal impact on marine ecosystems is limited. Nevertheless, there is considerable scope for policy makers to change the current management of these ?sheries and to enhance their contribution to food security and economic development. Industry and consumers also have an important role in ?nding the balance between these ?sheries contributing to human food security and poverty alleviation on the one hand, and sustaining intensive animal food production systems, especially aquaculture, on the other.","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev.environ.33.020807.143204","ISSN":"1543-5938, 1545-2050","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"153-166","source":"Crossref","title":"Forage Fish: From Ecosystems to Markets","title-short":"Forage Fish","URL":"http://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev.environ.33.020807.143204","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Alder","given":"Jacqueline"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"Brooke"},{"family":"Karpouzi","given":"Vasiliki"},{"family":"Kaschner","given":"Kristin"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}},{"id":1256,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KCT2ASEU"],"itemData":{"id":1256,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1007/s11160-009-9118-1","ISSN":"0960-3166, 1573-5184","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"9-29","source":"Crossref","title":"Issues of ecosystem-based management of forage fisheries in “open” non-stationary ecosystems: the example of the sardine fishery in the Gulf of California","title-short":"Issues of ecosystem-based management of forage fisheries in “open” non-stationary ecosystems","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11160-009-9118-1","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Bakun","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Babcock","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Lluch-Cota","given":"Salvador E."},{"family":"Santora","given":"Christine"},{"family":"Salvadeo","given":"Christian J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",3]]}}},{"id":1258,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZTCQYR6J"],"itemData":{"id":1258,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1422020112","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"21","language":"en","page":"6648-6652","source":"Crossref","title":"Fishing amplifies forage fish population collapses","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1422020112","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Essington","given":"Timothy E."},{"family":"Moriarty","given":"Pamela E."},{"family":"Froehlich","given":"Halley E."},{"family":"Hodgson","given":"Emma E."},{"family":"Koehn","given":"Laura E."},{"family":"Oken","given":"Kiva L."},{"family":"Siple","given":"Margaret C."},{"family":"Stawitz","given":"Christine C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",5,26]]}}},{"id":1262,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HKH3K4ZZ"],"itemData":{"id":1262,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1209395","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6046","language":"en","page":"1147-1150","source":"Crossref","title":"Impacts of Fishing Low-Trophic Level Species on Marine Ecosystems","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1209395","volume":"333","author":[{"family":"Smith","given":"A. D. M."},{"family":"Brown","given":"C. J."},{"family":"Bulman","given":"C. M."},{"family":"Fulton","given":"E. A."},{"family":"Johnson","given":"P."},{"family":"Kaplan","given":"I. C."},{"family":"Lozano-Montes","given":"H."},{"family":"Mackinson","given":"S."},{"family":"Marzloff","given":"M."},{"family":"Shannon","given":"L. J."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y.-J."},{"family":"Tam","given":"J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8,26]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fisheries for small pelagic fish have a high economic value because of their use for human consumption and for the production of fish meal and fish oil. These fisheries are not only critically important in terms of future global food security but are also pivotal to the economies of small-scale fisheries communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a27d5oa0tlr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pikitch, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Pikitch, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1272,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NYGK25DI"],"itemData":{"id":1272,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Forage ?sh play a pivotal role in marine ecosystems and economies worldwide by sustaining many predators and ?sheries directly and indirectly. We estimate global forage ?sh contributions to marine ecosystems through a synthesis of 72 published Ecopath models from around the world. Three distinct contributions of forage ?sh were examined: (i) the ecological support service of forage ?sh to predators in marine ecosystems, (ii) the total catch and value of forage ?sheries and (iii) the support service of forage ?sh to the catch and value of other commercially targeted predators. Forage ?sh use and value varied and exhibited patterns across latitudes and ecosystem types. Forage ?sh supported many kinds of predators, including ?sh, seabirds, marine mammals and squid. Overall, forage ?sh contribute a total of about $16.9 billion USD to global ?sheries values annually, i.e. 20% of the global ex-vessel catch values of all marine ?sheries combined. While the global catch value of forage ?sheries was $5.6 billion, ?sheries supported by forage ?sh were more than twice as valuable ($11.3 billion). These estimates provide important information for evaluating the trade-offs of various uses of forage ?sh across ecosystem types, latitudes and globally. We did not estimate a monetary value for supportive contributions of forage ?sh to recreational ?sheries or to uses unrelated to ?sheries, and thus the estimates of economic value reported herein understate the global value of forage ?shes.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12004","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"43-64","source":"Crossref","title":"The global contribution of forage fish to marine fisheries and ecosystems","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/faf.12004","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Pikitch","given":"Ellen K"},{"family":"Rountos","given":"Konstantine J"},{"family":"Essington","given":"Timothy E"},{"family":"Santora","given":"Christine"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Reg"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"Ussif R"},{"family":"Boersma","given":"P Dee"},{"family":"Boyd","given":"Ian L"},{"family":"Conover","given":"David O"},{"family":"Cury","given":"Philippe"},{"family":"Heppell","given":"Selina S"},{"family":"Houde","given":"Edward D"},{"family":"Mangel","given":"Marc"},{"family":"Plagányi","given":"Éva"},{"family":"Sainsbury","given":"Keith"},{"family":"Steneck","given":"Robert S"},{"family":"Geers","given":"Tess M"},{"family":"Gownaris","given":"Natasha"},{"family":"Munch","given":"Stephan B"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It has been estimated that fisheries supported by forage fish are actually more than twice as valuable as forage fisheries themselves, providing a strong economic argument for their conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1oaga89eag","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pikitch, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Pikitch, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1273,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X2ZY8K5S"],"itemData":{"id":1273,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1505403112","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"21","language":"en","page":"6529-6530","source":"Crossref","title":"Stop-loss order for forage fish fisheries","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1505403112","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Pikitch","given":"Ellen K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",5,26]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Populations of small pelagic fish exhibit extreme fluctuations in abundance and geographic distribution due to the impact of environmental factors, which are often amplified by anthropogenic influences ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"am4elobeae","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Essington, 2015; Izquierdo-Pe\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}a, Lluch-Cota, Hernandez-Rivas, & Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}nez-Rinc\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}n, 2019; Stephenson & Smedbol, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Essington, 2015; Izquierdo-Peña, Lluch-Cota, Hernandez-Rivas, & Martínez-Rincón, 2019; Stephenson & Smedbol, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1258,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZTCQYR6J"],"itemData":{"id":1258,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1422020112","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"21","language":"en","page":"6648-6652","source":"Crossref","title":"Fishing amplifies forage fish population collapses","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1422020112","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Essington","given":"Timothy E."},{"family":"Moriarty","given":"Pamela E."},{"family":"Froehlich","given":"Halley E."},{"family":"Hodgson","given":"Emma E."},{"family":"Koehn","given":"Laura E."},{"family":"Oken","given":"Kiva L."},{"family":"Siple","given":"Margaret C."},{"family":"Stawitz","given":"Christine C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",5,26]]}}},{"id":1271,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TKB3KELJ"],"itemData":{"id":1271,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Small pelagic ?sh species have been a central research topic for more than 60 years because of their ecological role, their economic importance, and the apparent multidecadal large ?uctuations in catches, synchronic ?uctuations from remote regions, and the alternation between the abundance of sardine and anchovies within each system. Strong evidence supports the observation that low-frequency ?uctuations of small pelagics occur naturally. However, the synchrony and the alternation have been questioned. In this study, the Regime Problem hypothesis is revisited by updating the ?sh catch time series to 2015, and by incorporating into the analyses stocks not originally considered due to the time series lengths. The speci?c questions addressed here are: 1) whether the synchronic ?uctuations of small pelagics catches from remote regions and the species alternation between species within each system, observed for the last century in the Regime Problem literature, remains after updating the time series, and 2) If a multidecadal signal can also be detected in regions that were not considered in the original Regime hypothesis framework. Results indicate that the multidecadal synchrony between systems and alternation between species remains clear for the Kuroshio and Humboldt systems. The California small pelagics cannot be considered to be in phase with the other systems, that in Benguela alternation is not evident during at least the last two decades, and that there is not a single worldwide mode of variability teleconnecting all small pelagics.","container-title":"Deep Sea Research Part II: Topical Studies in Oceanography","DOI":"10.1016/j.dsr2.2018.11.003","ISSN":"09670645","language":"en","page":"4-10","source":"Crossref","title":"Revisiting the Regime Problem hypothesis: 25 years later","title-short":"Revisiting the Regime Problem hypothesis","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0967064517303594","volume":"159","author":[{"family":"Izquierdo-Peña","given":"Vanessa"},{"family":"Lluch-Cota","given":"Salvador E."},{"family":"Hernandez-Rivas","given":"Martín E."},{"family":"Martínez-Rincón","given":"Raul O."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1]]}}},{"id":1260,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KBISY3XD"],"itemData":{"id":1260,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Encyclopedia of Ocean Sciences","ISBN":"978-0-12-813082-7","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1016/B978-0-12-409548-9.11491-5","page":"503-509","publisher":"Elsevier","source":"Crossref","title":"Small Pelagic Species Fisheries","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/B9780124095489114915","author":[{"family":"Stephenson","given":"R.L."},{"family":"Smedbol","given":"R.K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The exploitation of many stocks of pelagic fishes has exhibited a pattern of sharply increasing catches followed by an even more rapid decline (Figure 3.23), leading in several cases to closure of the fishery ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"at198autje","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stephenson & Smedbol, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Stephenson & Smedbol, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1260,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KBISY3XD"],"itemData":{"id":1260,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Encyclopedia of Ocean Sciences","ISBN":"978-0-12-813082-7","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1016/B978-0-12-409548-9.11491-5","page":"503-509","publisher":"Elsevier","source":"Crossref","title":"Small Pelagic Species Fisheries","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/B9780124095489114915","author":[{"family":"Stephenson","given":"R.L."},{"family":"Smedbol","given":"R.K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nonetheless, Froehlich ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1TWcjWOo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Froehlich, Jacobsen, Essington, Clavelle, & Halpern, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Froehlich, Jacobsen, Essington, Clavelle, & Halpern, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1259,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S69FI9FA"],"itemData":{"id":1259,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Sustainability","DOI":"10.1038/s41893-018-0077-1","ISSN":"2398-9629","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"298-303","source":"Crossref","title":"Avoiding the ecological limits of forage fish for fed aquaculture","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41893-018-0077-1","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Froehlich","given":"Halley E."},{"family":"Jacobsen","given":"Nis Sand"},{"family":"Essington","given":"Timothy E."},{"family":"Clavelle","given":"Tyler"},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} calculated the maximum sustainable yield for 401 stocks that comprise 99% of global forage fish catch, and estimated that the average small pelagic fish catch could increase by 30% from 2012 levels, which would correspond to raising the average (post-1980) small pelagic fish limit by 1.8 million tons per year.


Figure 3.23 Standardized catch time series for sardines and anchovies from the four largest small pelagics fisheries: Japan, Humboldt, Benguela, and California ecosystems. Data from the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations’ fishstat data base. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2kc2bkisgc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Izquierdo-Pe\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}a, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Izquierdo-Peña, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1271,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TKB3KELJ"],"itemData":{"id":1271,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Small pelagic ?sh species have been a central research topic for more than 60 years because of their ecological role, their economic importance, and the apparent multidecadal large ?uctuations in catches, synchronic ?uctuations from remote regions, and the alternation between the abundance of sardine and anchovies within each system. Strong evidence supports the observation that low-frequency ?uctuations of small pelagics occur naturally. However, the synchrony and the alternation have been questioned. In this study, the Regime Problem hypothesis is revisited by updating the ?sh catch time series to 2015, and by incorporating into the analyses stocks not originally considered due to the time series lengths. The speci?c questions addressed here are: 1) whether the synchronic ?uctuations of small pelagics catches from remote regions and the species alternation between species within each system, observed for the last century in the Regime Problem literature, remains after updating the time series, and 2) If a multidecadal signal can also be detected in regions that were not considered in the original Regime hypothesis framework. Results indicate that the multidecadal synchrony between systems and alternation between species remains clear for the Kuroshio and Humboldt systems. The California small pelagics cannot be considered to be in phase with the other systems, that in Benguela alternation is not evident during at least the last two decades, and that there is not a single worldwide mode of variability teleconnecting all small pelagics.","container-title":"Deep Sea Research Part II: Topical Studies in Oceanography","DOI":"10.1016/j.dsr2.2018.11.003","ISSN":"09670645","language":"en","page":"4-10","source":"Crossref","title":"Revisiting the Regime Problem hypothesis: 25 years later","title-short":"Revisiting the Regime Problem hypothesis","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0967064517303594","volume":"159","author":[{"family":"Izquierdo-Peña","given":"Vanessa"},{"family":"Lluch-Cota","given":"Salvador E."},{"family":"Hernandez-Rivas","given":"Martín E."},{"family":"Martínez-Rincón","given":"Raul O."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} © 2018 Elsevier Ltd., license number 5153140108259. CC-BY NC.

3.3.1.4.4. Pelagic fisheries for billfishes, tuna and tuna-like species

Fisheries targeting tuna and tuna-like species and billfishes are of great socioeconomic importance due to high economic value and extensive international trade and are therefore highlighted in the sustainable use assessment. Tuna accounts for over 9% of total marine fisheries catch, is the fourth most valuable globally traded fishery product, and is about 8% of the 129 billion United States dollars value of internationally traded fishery products ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HeYeuWVo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2014b, 2018d)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2014b, 2018d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12931,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7NI9KVSH"],"itemData":{"id":12931,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-108275-1","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 957283642","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"The state of the world fisheries and aquaculture 2014: opportunities and challenges.","title-short":"The state of the world fisheries and aquaculture 2014","URL":"https://www.fao.org/3/i3720e/i3720e.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":7733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M2WPUUSR"],"itemData":{"id":7733,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development\\n(2030 Agenda for short) offers a vision of a\\nfairer, more peaceful world in which no one is\\nleft behind. The 2030 Agenda also sets aims for\\nthe contribution and conduct of fisheries and\\naquaculture towards food security and nutrition,\\nand the sector’s use of natural resources, in a\\nway that ensures sustainable development in\\neconomic, social and environmental terms,\\nwithin the context of the FAO Code of Conduct\\nfor Responsible Fisheries (FAO, 1995). A major\\nchallenge to implementation of the 2030 Agenda\\nis the sustainability divide between developed\\nand developing countries which has partially\\nresulted from increased economic\\ninterdependencies, coupled with limited\\nmanagement and governance capacity in\\ndeveloping countries. To eliminate this disparity\\nwhile making progress towards the target for\\nrestoration of overfished stocks set by the 2030\\nAgenda, the global community needs to support\\ndeveloping nations to achieve their full fisheries\\nand aquaculture potential. \\n\\nReport is rich in data.","event-place":"Rome","language":"English","note":"978-92-5-130562-1","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture 2018 - Meeting the sustainable development goals","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/I9540EN/i9540en.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fisheries targeting these species provide substantial economic revenue, employment and food security to fishing and coastal states ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sGdGwsHY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J.D. Bell & Pacific Community, 2011; FAO, 2018d; Gillett, 2009)","plainCitation":"(J.D. Bell & Pacific Community, 2011; FAO, 2018d; Gillett, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":27052,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C6JQBRCR"],"itemData":{"id":27052,"type":"book","event-place":"Noumea, New Caledonia","language":"en","publisher":"Secretariat of the Pacific Community","publisher-place":"Noumea, New Caledonia","title":"Vulnerability of tropical Pacific fisheries and aquaculture to climate change: Summary for Pacific island countries and territories","editor":[{"family":"Bell","given":"J.D."},{"family":"Pacific Community","given":"Secretariat"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":7733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M2WPUUSR"],"itemData":{"id":7733,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development\\n(2030 Agenda for short) offers a vision of a\\nfairer, more peaceful world in which no one is\\nleft behind. The 2030 Agenda also sets aims for\\nthe contribution and conduct of fisheries and\\naquaculture towards food security and nutrition,\\nand the sector’s use of natural resources, in a\\nway that ensures sustainable development in\\neconomic, social and environmental terms,\\nwithin the context of the FAO Code of Conduct\\nfor Responsible Fisheries (FAO, 1995). A major\\nchallenge to implementation of the 2030 Agenda\\nis the sustainability divide between developed\\nand developing countries which has partially\\nresulted from increased economic\\ninterdependencies, coupled with limited\\nmanagement and governance capacity in\\ndeveloping countries. To eliminate this disparity\\nwhile making progress towards the target for\\nrestoration of overfished stocks set by the 2030\\nAgenda, the global community needs to support\\ndeveloping nations to achieve their full fisheries\\nand aquaculture potential. \\n\\nReport is rich in data.","event-place":"Rome","language":"English","note":"978-92-5-130562-1","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture 2018 - Meeting the sustainable development goals","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/I9540EN/i9540en.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":1241,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3QAF76NH"],"itemData":{"id":1241,"type":"book","publisher":"Asian Development Bank","title":"Fisheries in the Economies of Pacific Island Countries and Territories","author":[{"family":"Gillett","given":"R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Tunas and billfishes have been an important food source since ancient times, and are target species of fisheries worldwide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dPM72ATI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Majkowski, 2007; Miyake, Guillotreau, & Sun, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Majkowski, 2007; Miyake, Guillotreau, & Sun, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1236,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8IXZ8JHM"],"itemData":{"id":1236,"type":"book","edition":"FAO Fisheries Technical Paper 483","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","title":"Global Fishery Resources of Tuna and Tuna-like Species","author":[{"family":"Majkowski","given":"J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":9483,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EFTVGYBX","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XFRF6NSW"],"itemData":{"id":9483,"type":"book","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","title":"Recent Developments in the Tuna Industry. Stocks, Fisheries, Management, Processing, Trade and Markets","author":[{"family":"Miyake","given":"M"},{"family":"Guillotreau","given":"P"},{"family":"Sun","given":"C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the 19th century, most tuna fisheries were coastal, conducted by locally-based fleets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dXVUrEv0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Majkowski, n.d., 2007)","plainCitation":"(Majkowski, n.d., 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9455,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XJZ8ZZN8","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SM7EBBFQ"],"itemData":{"id":9455,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Review of the State of World Marine Fishery Resources","edition":"FAO Fisheries Technical Paper 457","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","title":"Tuna and tuna-like species","author":[{"family":"Majkowski","given":""}]}},{"id":1236,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8IXZ8JHM"],"itemData":{"id":1236,"type":"book","edition":"FAO Fisheries Technical Paper 483","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","title":"Global Fishery Resources of Tuna and Tuna-like Species","author":[{"family":"Majkowski","given":"J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Industrial tuna fisheries began in the 1940s. Over the next few decades, fishing grounds quickly expanded as did the number of countries with large-scale coastal and distant-water tuna fleets. About 82% of world tuna is consumed as canned product, and 18% as fresh product (including as sashimi) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0Tps8kM1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Miyake, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Miyake, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9483,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EFTVGYBX","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XFRF6NSW"],"itemData":{"id":9483,"type":"book","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","title":"Recent Developments in the Tuna Industry. Stocks, Fisheries, Management, Processing, Trade and Markets","author":[{"family":"Miyake","given":"M"},{"family":"Guillotreau","given":"P"},{"family":"Sun","given":"C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Japan consumes an estimated 78% of the fresh tuna ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3DZ0QUeZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Miyake, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Miyake, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9483,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EFTVGYBX","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XFRF6NSW"],"itemData":{"id":9483,"type":"book","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","title":"Recent Developments in the Tuna Industry. Stocks, Fisheries, Management, Processing, Trade and Markets","author":[{"family":"Miyake","given":"M"},{"family":"Guillotreau","given":"P"},{"family":"Sun","given":"C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Demand for both canned and fresh tuna has increased rapidly, with reported landings of principal market tunas increasing from about 700 thousand tons in 1960 to almost 4.8 million tons in 2014 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0Sm5iiJo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(SPC, 2015)","plainCitation":"(SPC, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1233,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2872I2SP"],"itemData":{"id":1233,"type":"report","genre":"Oceanic Fisheries Programme, Secretariat of the Pacific Community, Noumea, New Caledonia.","title":"Western and Central Pacific Fisheries Commission Tuna Fishery Yearbook 2014.","URL":"https://www.wcpfc.int/doc/wcpfc-tuna-fishery-yearbook-2015","author":[{"family":"SPC","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Figure 3.24).
      Since 2006, over half of principal market tunas have come from the western and central Pacific Ocean ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qp8yMBni","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(SPC, 2015)","plainCitation":"(SPC, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1233,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2872I2SP"],"itemData":{"id":1233,"type":"report","genre":"Oceanic Fisheries Programme, Secretariat of the Pacific Community, Noumea, New Caledonia.","title":"Western and Central Pacific Fisheries Commission Tuna Fishery Yearbook 2014.","URL":"https://www.wcpfc.int/doc/wcpfc-tuna-fishery-yearbook-2015","author":[{"family":"SPC","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Several Pacific Island countries and territories obtain a large proportion of their gross domestic product through revenue from tuna fisheries, as high as 63% of total government revenue in some cases ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"J7B6orlM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aqorau, 2009; Johann D. Bell, 2015; FFA, 2015; Gillett, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Aqorau, 2009; Johann D. Bell, 2015; FFA, 2015; Gillett, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1247,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A4KM4HGZ"],"itemData":{"id":1247,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The International Journal of Marine and Coastal Law","DOI":"10.1163/157180809X455647","ISSN":"09273522, 15718085","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"int j mar coast law","language":"en","page":"557-581","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recent Developments in Pacific Tuna Fisheries: The Palau Arrangement and the Vessel Day Scheme","title-short":"Recent Developments in Pacific Tuna Fisheries","URL":"http://booksandjournals.brillonline.com/content/journals/10.1163/157180809x455647","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Aqorau","given":"Transform"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",9,1]]}}},{"id":1239,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B7D68E2Z"],"itemData":{"id":1239,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2014.10.005","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"584-591","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Diversifying the use of tuna to improve food security and public health in Pacific Island countries and territories","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X1400267X","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Bell","given":"Johann D."},{"family":"Allain","given":"Valerie"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Andréfouët","given":"Serge"},{"family":"Andrew","given":"Neil L."},{"family":"Batty","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Blanc","given":"Michel"},{"family":"Dambacher","given":"Jeffrey M."},{"family":"Hampton","given":"John"},{"family":"Hanich","given":"Quentin"},{"family":"Harley","given":"Shelton"},{"family":"Lorrain","given":"Anne"},{"family":"McCoy","given":"Michael"},{"family":"McTurk","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Nicol","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Pilling","given":"Graham"},{"family":"Point","given":"David"},{"family":"Sharp","given":"Michael K."},{"family":"Vivili","given":"Paula"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Peter"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1]]}}},{"id":9451,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7XSF4CHM"],"itemData":{"id":9451,"type":"book","publisher":"Pacific Islands Forum Fisheries Agency","title":"Economic Indicators Report","author":[{"family":"FFA","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":1241,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3QAF76NH"],"itemData":{"id":1241,"type":"book","publisher":"Asian Development Bank","title":"Fisheries in the Economies of Pacific Island Countries and Territories","author":[{"family":"Gillett","given":"R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This includes licensing, fees, and granting access to foreign purse seine and longline tuna fisheries to fish in their exclusive economic zones. Capture and processing practices generate additional revenue and substantial employment in the Pacific Islands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"su1MqPRV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Johann D. Bell, 2015; FFA, 2015; Gillett, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Johann D. Bell, 2015; FFA, 2015; Gillett, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1239,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B7D68E2Z"],"itemData":{"id":1239,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2014.10.005","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"584-591","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Diversifying the use of tuna to improve food security and public health in Pacific Island countries and territories","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X1400267X","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Bell","given":"Johann D."},{"family":"Allain","given":"Valerie"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Andréfouët","given":"Serge"},{"family":"Andrew","given":"Neil L."},{"family":"Batty","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Blanc","given":"Michel"},{"family":"Dambacher","given":"Jeffrey M."},{"family":"Hampton","given":"John"},{"family":"Hanich","given":"Quentin"},{"family":"Harley","given":"Shelton"},{"family":"Lorrain","given":"Anne"},{"family":"McCoy","given":"Michael"},{"family":"McTurk","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Nicol","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Pilling","given":"Graham"},{"family":"Point","given":"David"},{"family":"Sharp","given":"Michael K."},{"family":"Vivili","given":"Paula"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Peter"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":9451,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7XSF4CHM"],"itemData":{"id":9451,"type":"book","publisher":"Pacific Islands Forum Fisheries Agency","title":"Economic Indicators Report","author":[{"family":"FFA","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":1241,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3QAF76NH"],"itemData":{"id":1241,"type":"book","publisher":"Asian Development Bank","title":"Fisheries in the Economies of Pacific Island Countries and Territories","author":[{"family":"Gillett","given":"R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In 2014, the Pacific sslands forum fisheries agency (15 Pacific small islands developing states, Australia and New Zealand) obtained an estimated 556 million United States dollars of their combined gross domestic product from the tuna fisheries, and employed over 22,000 people in processing and various other tuna-practice related positions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wHp5yTfN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FFA, 2015)","plainCitation":"(FFA, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9451,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7XSF4CHM"],"itemData":{"id":9451,"type":"book","publisher":"Pacific Islands Forum Fisheries Agency","title":"Economic Indicators Report","author":[{"family":"FFA","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some locally-based tuna fisheries supply largely low-value fishes (smaller tunas, incidental tuna-like species) to local markets in Pacific Island countries and territories, contributing to local food security and tourism industries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ac08vtfcoj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Johann D. Bell, 2015; Gillett, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Johann D. Bell, 2015; Gillett, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1239,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B7D68E2Z"],"itemData":{"id":1239,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2014.10.005","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"584-591","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Diversifying the use of tuna to improve food security and public health in Pacific Island countries and territories","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X1400267X","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Bell","given":"Johann D."},{"family":"Allain","given":"Valerie"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Andréfouët","given":"Serge"},{"family":"Andrew","given":"Neil L."},{"family":"Batty","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Blanc","given":"Michel"},{"family":"Dambacher","given":"Jeffrey M."},{"family":"Hampton","given":"John"},{"family":"Hanich","given":"Quentin"},{"family":"Harley","given":"Shelton"},{"family":"Lorrain","given":"Anne"},{"family":"McCoy","given":"Michael"},{"family":"McTurk","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Nicol","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Pilling","given":"Graham"},{"family":"Point","given":"David"},{"family":"Sharp","given":"Michael K."},{"family":"Vivili","given":"Paula"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Peter"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1]]}}},{"id":1241,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3QAF76NH"],"itemData":{"id":1241,"type":"book","publisher":"Asian Development Bank","title":"Fisheries in the Economies of Pacific Island Countries and Territories","author":[{"family":"Gillett","given":"R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 	


Figure 3.24 Global reported landings of principal market species of tunas by region, 1960-2014. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2dkbsmp79e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Gilman, Allain, Collette, Hampton, & Lehodey, 2016; SPC, 2015)","plainCitation":"(E. Gilman, Allain, Collette, Hampton, & Lehodey, 2016; SPC, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30790,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DW5MSBX7"],"itemData":{"id":30790,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Explaining Ocean Warming: Causes, scale, effects and consequences","ISBN":"978-2-8317-1806-4","note":"DOI: 10.2305/IUCN.CH.2016.08.en","page":"255-272","publisher":"IUCN, International Union for Conservation of Nature","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effects of Ocean Warming on Pelagic Tunas, a Review","URL":"https://portals.iucn.org/library/node/46254","editor":[{"family":"Laffoley","given":"D."},{"family":"Baxter","given":"J.M."}],"author":[{"family":"Gilman","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Allain","given":"Valerie"},{"family":"Collette","given":"B. B."},{"family":"Hampton","given":"John"},{"family":"Lehodey","given":"P"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",8,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":1233,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2872I2SP"],"itemData":{"id":1233,"type":"report","genre":"Oceanic Fisheries Programme, Secretariat of the Pacific Community, Noumea, New Caledonia.","title":"Western and Central Pacific Fisheries Commission Tuna Fishery Yearbook 2014.","URL":"https://www.wcpfc.int/doc/wcpfc-tuna-fishery-yearbook-2015","author":[{"family":"SPC","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} © 2016 International Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural Resources. CC-BY.

	Single-stock assessment models are the most common approach used by fisheries management authorities to assess the sustainability of stocks of principal market species of tuna, tuna-like species and billfishes. The four regional fisheries management organizations for tropical tunas have recently adopted and implemented single-stock harvest strategies. The main elements of harvest strategies are outlined in the following literature: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"npNFCdli","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sainsbury, 2000; WCPFC, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Sainsbury, 2000; WCPFC, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9499,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YNATRJ49"],"itemData":{"id":9499,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1006/jmsc.2000.0737","ISSN":"10543139","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","language":"en","page":"731-741","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Design of operational management strategies for achieving fishery ecosystem objectives","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/icesjms/article-lookup/doi/10.1006/jmsc.2000.0737","volume":"57","author":[{"family":"Sainsbury","given":"K"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",6]]}}},{"id":28542,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WR8SU2U2","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8J8BE6JI"],"itemData":{"id":28542,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Western and Central Pacific Fisheries Commission","title":"Conservation and Management Measure on Establishing a Harvest Strategy for Key Fisheries and Stocks in the Western and Central Pacific Ocean","author":[{"literal":"WCPFC"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	The status of most but not all stocks of principal market tunas and billfishes is relatively certain ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TLRJ75VS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(ISSF, 2020; Juan-Jord\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}, Mosqueira, Freire, & Dulvy, 2013; Pons, 2017)","plainCitation":"(ISSF, 2020; Juan-Jordá, Mosqueira, Freire, & Dulvy, 2013; Pons, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":27684,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4D6P8RG9","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FF8NNJK4"],"itemData":{"id":27684,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"International Seafood Sustainability Foundation","title":"Status of the World Fisheries for Tuna","author":[{"literal":"ISSF"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":1248,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3CX9NZMQ"],"itemData":{"id":1248,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Scombrids (tunas, bonitos, Spanish mackerels and mackerels) support important fisheries in tropical, subtropical and temperate waters around the world, being one of the most economically- and socially-important marine species globally. Their sustainable exploitation, management and conservation depend on accurate life history information for the development of quantitative fisheries stock assessments, and in the fishery data-poor situations for the identification of vulnerable species. Here, we assemble life history traits (maximum size, growth, longevity, maturity, fecundity, spawning duration and spawning interval) for the 51 species of scombrids globally. We identify major biological gaps in knowledge and prioritize life history research needs in scombrids based on their biological gaps in knowledge, the importance of their fisheries and their current conservation status according to the International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List. We find that the growth and reproductive biology of tunas and mackerel species have been more extensively studied than for Spanish mackerels and bonitos, although there are notable exceptions in all groups. We also reveal that reproductive biology of species, particular fecundity, is the least studied biological aspect in scombrids. We identify two priority groups, including 32 species of scombrids, and several populations of principal market tunas, for which life history research should be prioritized following the species-specific life history gaps identified in this study in the coming decades. By highlighting the important gaps in biological knowledge and providing a priority setting for life history research in scombrid species this study provides guidance for management and conservation and serves as a guide for biologists and resource managers interested in the biology, ecology, and management of scombrid species.","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0070405","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"PLOS ONE","language":"en","page":"e70405","source":"PLoS Journals","title":"The Conservation and Management of Tunas and Their Relatives: Setting Life History Research Priorities","title-short":"The Conservation and Management of Tunas and Their Relatives","URL":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0070405","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Juan-Jordá","given":"Maria José"},{"family":"Mosqueira","given":"Iago"},{"family":"Freire","given":"Juan"},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",8,8]]}}},{"id":1244,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R6PAQLQN"],"itemData":{"id":1244,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12163","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"1-21","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effects of biological, economic and management factors on tuna and billfish stock status","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/faf.12163","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Pons","given":"Maite"},{"family":"Branch","given":"Trevor A"},{"family":"Melnychuk","given":"Michael C"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Olaf P"},{"family":"Brodziak","given":"Jon"},{"family":"Fromentin","given":"Jean M"},{"family":"Harley","given":"Shelton J"},{"family":"Haynie","given":"Alan C"},{"family":"Kell","given":"Laurie T"},{"family":"Maunder","given":"Mark N"},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M"},{"family":"Restrepo","given":"Victor R"},{"family":"Sharma","given":"Rishi"},{"family":"Ahrens","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Direct mortality caused by pelagic marine fisheries is the main driver of reductions in the size and abundance of pelagic apex predators, including target stocks and incidentally caught species. Many target species are considered to be above limit thresholds and near targets. However, as discussed earlier in this chapter, the fisheries that catch these principal market species also intentionally or accidentally capture species that are highly vulnerable to anthropogenic mortality sources. There is extremely high uncertainty of the status of stocks and populations of these other species. 
      Fisheries that target tuna and tuna-like species, billfishes and other relatively fecund species can have large impacts on incidentally caught species that, due to their lower reproduction rates and other life history traits, are relatively vulnerable to increased mortality. This includes seabirds, sea turtles, marine mammals, elasmobranchs and some teleosts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MsgvJDdy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Branch, Lobo, & Purcell, 2013; M. A. Hall, Alverson, & Metuzals, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Branch, Lobo, & Purcell, 2013; M. A. Hall, Alverson, & Metuzals, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1240,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/22KR456T"],"itemData":{"id":1240,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Pollution Bulletin","DOI":"10.1016/S0025-326X(00)00111-9","ISSN":"0025326X","issue":"1-6","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Pollution Bulletin","language":"en","page":"204-219","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"By-Catch: Problems and Solutions","title-short":"By-Catch","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0025326X00001119","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Hall","given":"Martin A."},{"family":"Alverson","given":"Dayton L."},{"family":"Metuzals","given":"Kaija I."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",1]]}}},{"id":1281,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/726ZH8FR"],"itemData":{"id":1281,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2013.03.003","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"409-413","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Opportunistic exploitation: an overlooked pathway to extinction","title-short":"Opportunistic exploitation","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534713000712","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Branch","given":"Trevor A."},{"family":"Lobo","given":"Aaron S."},{"family":"Purcell","given":"Steven W."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"k1tcm05R","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. L. Gilman, 2011)","plainCitation":"(E. L. Gilman, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8636,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QC5GXFH2"],"itemData":{"id":8636,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2011.01.021","ISSN":"0308597X","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"590-609","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Bycatch governance and best practice mitigation technology in global tuna fisheries","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X11000224","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Gilman","given":"Eric L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Pelagic fisheries selectively remove individuals based on certain traits (e.g., behavioral traits for boldness; life-history traits for size-at-age; physiological traits for visual acuity; morphological traits for mouth dimensions), reducing intraspecific genetic diversity and altering fitness and evolutionary processes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7w6SbGOk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Heino, D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az Pauli, & Dieckmann, 2015; Hollins, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Heino, Díaz Pauli, & Dieckmann, 2015; Hollins, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1245,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S9XNRT5F"],"itemData":{"id":1245,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Ecology, Evolution, and Systematics","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-ecolsys-112414-054339","ISSN":"1543-592X, 1545-2069","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Ecol. Evol. Syst.","language":"en","page":"461-480","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fisheries-Induced Evolution","URL":"http://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev-ecolsys-112414-054339","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Heino","given":"Mikko"},{"family":"Díaz Pauli","given":"Beatriz"},{"family":"Dieckmann","given":"Ulf"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",12,4]]}}},{"id":9486,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RPI69VN8"],"itemData":{"id":9486,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Evolutionary Applications","DOI":"10.1111/eva.12597","ISSN":"17524571","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Evol Appl","language":"en","page":"561-576","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A physiological perspective on fisheries-induced evolution","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/eva.12597","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Hollins","given":"Jack"},{"family":"Thambithurai","given":"Davide"},{"family":"Koeck","given":"Barbara"},{"family":"Crespel","given":"Amelie"},{"family":"Bailey","given":"David M."},{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."},{"family":"Lindström","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Parsons","given":"Kevin J."},{"family":"Killen","given":"Shaun S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fishing gear can alter and damage habitat ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KTisZIbH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dagorn, Holland, Restrepo, & Moreno, 2013; Escalle, Brouwer, Phillips, Pilling, & PNA, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Dagorn, Holland, Restrepo, & Moreno, 2013; Escalle, Brouwer, Phillips, Pilling, & PNA, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1249,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W3ZNB32S"],"itemData":{"id":1249,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The use of fish aggregating devices (FADs) by purse seine fisheries has come under increasing criticism for its potential deleterious impacts on tuna stocks, for high levels of by-catch and threats to the biodiversity of tropical pelagic ecosystems. Here, we review the current state of scientific knowledge of this fishing technique and current management strategies. Our intent is to encourage objective discussion of the topic and highlight areas worthy of future research. We show that catching juvenile tuna around FADs does not necessarily result in overfishing of stocks, although more selective fishing techniques would likely help obtain higher yield. Levels of non-tuna by-catch are comparable to or less than in other commercial tuna fisheries and are primarily comprised of species that are not considered threatened. Accordingly, to minimize impacts on ecosystem balance, there is merit in considering that all species captured in purse seine fisheries (excluding vulnerable species such as turtles and sharks) should be retained, but the consequences of such a measure should be carefully examined before implementation. The take of vulnerable species could be further reduced by introduction of additional mitigation measures, but their potential benefits would be limited without parallel efforts with other gears. Finally, there is no unequivocal empirical evidence that FADs represent an ‘ecological trap’ that inherently disrupts tuna biology although further research should focus on this issue. We encourage RFMOs to expand and improve their FAD management plans. Under appropriate management regimes, FAD fishing could be an ecologically and economically sensible fishing method.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-2979.2012.00478.x","ISSN":"1467-2979","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"391-415","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Is it good or bad to fish with FADs? What are the real impacts of the use of drifting FADs on pelagic marine ecosystems?","title-short":"Is it good or bad to fish with FADs?","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1467-2979.2012.00478.x","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Dagorn","given":"Laurent"},{"family":"Holland","given":"Kim N."},{"family":"Restrepo","given":"Victor"},{"family":"Moreno","given":"Gala"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":9452,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F3FQRF5F"],"itemData":{"id":9452,"type":"book","edition":"WCPFC-SC13-2017/MI-WP-05.","publisher":"Western and Central Pacific Fisheries Commission","title":"Preliminary Analyses of PNA FAD Tracking Data from 2016 and 2017. WCPFC-SC13-2017/MI-WP-05.","author":[{"family":"Escalle","given":"L"},{"family":"Brouwer","given":"S"},{"family":"Phillips","given":"J"},{"family":"Pilling","given":"G"},{"family":"PNA","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). Thus, fisheries targeting large, highly migratory pelagic predators of high trophic levels (total lenght > 4.0) indirectly modify trophic food web structure and processes and functionally-linked systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xeIeYNuw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pace, Cole, Carpenter, & Kitchell, 1999; Polovina, Abecassis, Howell, & Woodworth, 2009; J. Stevens, 2000; Ward & Myers, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Pace, Cole, Carpenter, & Kitchell, 1999; Polovina, Abecassis, Howell, & Woodworth, 2009; J. Stevens, 2000; Ward & Myers, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1235,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7GXQIIJM"],"itemData":{"id":1235,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Bulletin","page":"523-531","title":"Increases in the relative abundance of mid-trophic level fishes concurrent with declines in apex predators in the subtropical North Pacific, 1996-2006","volume":"107","author":[{"family":"Polovina","given":"J"},{"family":"Abecassis","given":"M"},{"family":"Howell","given":"E"},{"family":"Woodworth","given":"P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":1242,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q2I6PGTY"],"itemData":{"id":1242,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology","DOI":"10.1890/03-0746","ISSN":"0012-9658","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Ecology","language":"en","page":"835-847","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"SHIFTS IN OPEN-OCEAN FISH COMMUNITIES COINCIDING WITH THE COMMENCEMENT OF COMMERCIAL FISHING","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1890/03-0746","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Ward","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Myers","given":"Ransom A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",4]]}}},{"id":1243,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CS7PQU9T"],"itemData":{"id":1243,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1006/jmsc.2000.0724","ISSN":"10543139","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","language":"en","page":"476-494","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The effects of fishing on sharks, rays, and chimaeras (chondrichthyans), and the implications for marine ecosystems","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/icesjms/article-lookup/doi/10.1006/jmsc.2000.0724","volume":"57","author":[{"family":"Stevens","given":"J"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",6]]}}},{"id":1238,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H4BKPNCQ"],"itemData":{"id":1238,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/S0169-5347(99)01723-1","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"483-488","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trophic cascades revealed in diverse ecosystems","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534799017231","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Pace","given":"Michael L."},{"family":"Cole","given":"Jonathan J."},{"family":"Carpenter","given":"Stephen R."},{"family":"Kitchell","given":"James F."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5JdZCeln","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. A. Estes, 2011)","plainCitation":"(J. A. Estes, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34391,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ASWXWTL3"],"itemData":{"id":34391,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1205106","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6040","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"301-306","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trophic Downgrading of Planet Earth","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1205106","volume":"333","author":[{"family":"Estes","given":"James A."},{"family":"Terborgh","given":"John"},{"family":"Brashares","given":"Justin S."},{"family":"Power","given":"Mary E."},{"family":"Berger","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Bond","given":"William J."},{"family":"Carpenter","given":"Stephen R."},{"family":"Essington","given":"Timothy E."},{"family":"Holt","given":"Robert D."},{"family":"Jackson","given":"Jeremy B. C."},{"family":"Marquis","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Oksanen","given":"Lauri"},{"family":"Oksanen","given":"Tarja"},{"family":"Paine","given":"Robert T."},{"family":"Pikitch","given":"Ellen K."},{"family":"Ripple","given":"William J."},{"family":"Sandin","given":"Stuart A."},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"Marten"},{"family":"Schoener","given":"Thomas W."},{"family":"Shurin","given":"Jonathan B."},{"family":"Sinclair","given":"Anthony R. E."},{"family":"Soulé","given":"Michael E."},{"family":"Virtanen","given":"Risto"},{"family":"Wardle","given":"David A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",7,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At this latter broad level, there is limited understanding of what magnitudes of interacting natural (e.g., large scale climate variability) and anthropogenic pressures (including from fishing) cause pelagic ecosystems to reach a tipping point where they undergo a protracted or permanent regime shift, and how altered components of the state of pelagic ecosystems affect functionally-linked systems (Box 3.3; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c5Gnp338","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ortu\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}o Crespo & Dunn, 2017; Pace, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Ortuño Crespo & Dunn, 2017; Pace, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9480,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z3ATTPAT","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LC9MWP6G"],"itemData":{"id":9480,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract \\n            Open?ocean fisheries expanded rapidly from the 1960s through the 1980s, when global fish catches peaked, plateaued and possibly began to decline. While catches remain at best stagnant, fishing effort globally continues to increase. The likelihood of ecosystem impacts occurring due to fishing is related to fishing effort and is thus also expected to be increasing. Despite this rapid growth, ecological research into the impacts of fisheries on open?ocean environments has lagged behind coastal and deep-sea environments. This review addresses this knowledge gap by considering the roles fisheries play in controlling the open-ocean at three ecological scales: (i) species (population or stock); (ii) biological community; and (iii) ecosystem. We find significant evidence for top-down control at the species and community scales. While evidence of ecosystem-level impacts in the open-ocean were not explicit in the literature, we provide examples of these impacts in several marine pelagic systems and encourage further research at this ecological scale. At the species level, fishing can reduce abundance, and alter physiology and life history traits, which, in turn, affect the functional role of the species within the biological community. Fishing may also induce changes to open-ocean community trophodynamics, and reduce biodiversity and resilience in open-ocean ecosystems. Our ability to manage open-ocean ecosystems has significant implications for provisioning of ecosystem services and food security. However, we posit that the monitoring required to assure the sustainability of open-ocean ecosystems is not being undertaken, and will require coordination with the Global Ocean Observing System, industry, and academia.","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsx084","ISSN":"1054-3139, 1095-9289","issue":"9","language":"en","page":"2283-2297","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A review of the impacts of fisheries on open-ocean ecosystems","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/icesjms/article/74/9/2283/3855115","volume":"74","author":[{"family":"Ortuño Crespo","given":"Guillermo"},{"family":"Dunn","given":"Daniel C"}],"editor":[{"family":"Travers-Trolet","given":"Morgane"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12,1]]}}},{"id":1238,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H4BKPNCQ"],"itemData":{"id":1238,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/S0169-5347(99)01723-1","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"483-488","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trophic cascades revealed in diverse ecosystems","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534799017231","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Pace","given":"Michael L."},{"family":"Cole","given":"Jonathan J."},{"family":"Carpenter","given":"Stephen R."},{"family":"Kitchell","given":"James F."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999",12]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Box 3.3 Ecosystem effects resulting from combined natural and anthropogenic impacts and their influence on the fisheries 

Although populated and exploited since the Neolithic, the Black Sea has undergone dramatic ecosystem changes in the last half century, mainly related to anthropogenic impacts such as uncontrolled fishing, cultural eutrophication and invasions by alien species. Fisheries collapses, harmful algal and jellyfish blooms, benthic community loss, and upper shelf hypoxia have had dire consequences for ecosystems and human livelihood depending on them. Recent research studies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a18f5m6nd3d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. Daskalov, 2003; Georgi M. Daskalov, 2017; Oguz & Gilbert, 2007)","plainCitation":"(G. Daskalov, 2003; Georgi M. Daskalov, 2017; Oguz & Gilbert, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1218,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JVGV2224"],"itemData":{"id":1218,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Ecology Progress Series","DOI":"10.3354/meps255259","ISSN":"0171-8630, 1616-1599","journalAbbreviation":"Mar. Ecol. Prog. Ser.","language":"en","page":"259-270","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Long-term changes in fish abundance and environmental indices in the Black Sea","URL":"http://www.int-res.com/abstracts/meps/v255/p259-270/","volume":"255","author":[{"family":"Daskalov","given":"Gm"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":1228,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5HCH8HFF"],"itemData":{"id":1228,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Biology","DOI":"10.1111/gcb.13508","ISSN":"1354-1013, 1365-2486","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Glob Change Biol","language":"en","page":"1486-1498","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Architecture of collapse: regime shift and recovery in an hierarchically structured marine ecosystem","title-short":"Architecture of collapse","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/gcb.13508","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Daskalov","given":"Georgi M."},{"family":"Boicenco","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Grishin","given":"Alexandre N."},{"family":"Lazar","given":"Luminita"},{"family":"Mihneva","given":"Vesselina"},{"family":"Shlyakhov","given":"Vladislav A."},{"family":"Zengin","given":"Mustafa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",4]]}}},{"id":1224,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S6DP9FWU"],"itemData":{"id":1224,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Deep-Sea Research I","DOI":"10.1016/j.dsr.200609.010","title":"Abrupt transitions of the top-down controlled Black Sea pelagic ecosystem during 1960–2000: evidence for regime-shifts under strong fishery exploitation and nutrient enrichment modulated by climate-induced variations","author":[{"family":"Oguz","given":"T"},{"family":"Gilbert","given":"D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} have demonstrated that these major changes resulted from synergistic effects of climate forcing, trophic interactions and anthropogenic pressures (overfishing, eutrophication and introduction of invasive species).

Historical trends in fishing and environmental change in the Black Sea
Following the development of the fisheries, the pelagic top-predators have declined by the early 1970s in the Black Sea. For instance, the large population of dolphins diminished about tenfold through overexploitation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a10lc47hul7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u214{}zturk, 1996; Sirotenko, Danilevskiy, & Shlyakhov, 1979)","plainCitation":"(Özturk, 1996; Sirotenko, Danilevskiy, & Shlyakhov, 1979)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1223,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7DIABH8Z"],"itemData":{"id":1223,"type":"book","publisher":"UNEP, Istanbul","title":"Proceedings of the First International Symposium on the Marine Mammals of the Black Sea","editor":[{"family":"Özturk","given":"B"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}},{"id":1219,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FGGYXP7J"],"itemData":{"id":1219,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Resources and Raw Materials in the Black Sea","event-place":"Moskow","page":"242-246","publisher":"AztcherNIRO, Pishtchevaya promishlenist","publisher-place":"Moskow","title":"Dolphins","editor":[{"family":"Tkatcheva","given":"K S"},{"family":"Benko","given":"Y K"}],"author":[{"family":"Sirotenko","given":"M D"},{"family":"Danilevskiy","given":"M M"},{"family":"Shlyakhov","given":"V A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1979"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Before 1970, the fishery targeted mainly large, valuable migratory species, such as bonito, mackerel, bluefin tuna and swordfish. All of these important fisheries collapsed mainly due to heavy and unregulated fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a12jr32rpsv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G M Daskalov, Demirel, Ulman, Georgieva, & Zengin, 2020; G M Daskalov, Prodanov, & Zengin, 2008)","plainCitation":"(G M Daskalov, Demirel, Ulman, Georgieva, & Zengin, 2020; G M Daskalov, Prodanov, & Zengin, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1226,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XVFMTWH2"],"itemData":{"id":1226,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES JMS","title":"Stock dynamics and predator-prey effects of bonito and bluefish as top predators in the Black Sea","volume":"in print","author":[{"family":"Daskalov","given":"G M"},{"family":"Demirel","given":"N"},{"family":"Ulman","given":"A"},{"family":"Georgieva","given":"Y"},{"family":"Zengin","given":"M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":1227,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2FE59TUU"],"itemData":{"id":1227,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Freshwater Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2427.2008.02115.x","event-title":"Reconciling fisheries with conservation: Proceedings of the Fourth World Fisheries Congress","language":"en","page":"635-636","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Black Seas fisheries and ecosystem change: discriminating between natural variability and human-related effects","title-short":"Reconciling fisheries with conservation","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1365-2427.2008.02115.x","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Daskalov","given":"G M"},{"family":"Prodanov","given":"K"},{"family":"Zengin","given":"M"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the early 1970s, the stocks of planktivorous fishes (sprat, anchovy and horse mackerel) increased considerably and became a target for the industrial fishery ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a29b998uvoa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barange, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Barange, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30800,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DJXFYCH9"],"itemData":{"id":30800,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Climate change and small pelagic fish","page":"191–255","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Current trends in the assessment and management of stocks","author":[{"family":"Barange","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Bernal","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Cercole","given":"Maria Christina"},{"family":"Cubillos","given":"Luis A"},{"family":"Daskalov","given":"Georgi M"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Carryn L"},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"Jose AA","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Dickey-Collas","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Gaughan","given":"Daniel J"},{"family":"Hill","given":"Kevin"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Their increase in biomass and catch promoted the expansion of powerful trawl and purse seine fishing fleets and a steady increase in fishing effort ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15vjk5bamt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gucu, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Gucu, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1220,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UGJRUS9R"],"itemData":{"id":1220,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Sensitivity to Change: Black Sea, Baltic Sea and North Sea","event-place":"Dordrecht","ISBN":"978-94-010-6429-3","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-94-011-5758-2","publisher":"Springer Netherlands","publisher-place":"Dordrecht","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Role of fishing in the Black Sea ecosystem","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-94-011-5758-2","editor":[{"family":"Özsoy","given":"Emin"},{"family":"Mikaelyan","given":"Alexander"}],"author":[{"family":"Gucu","given":"A C"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The highest catch and fishing mortality were recorded in the late 1980s, but biomasses of exploited populations were declining due to recruitment failures in the previous years. Sharp reductions in biomass and catch in the early 1990s were described as stock collapses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1dmhgkb6r2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G M Daskalov, 2008)","plainCitation":"(G M Daskalov, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1227,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2FE59TUU"],"itemData":{"id":1227,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Freshwater Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2427.2008.02115.x","event-title":"Reconciling fisheries with conservation: Proceedings of the Fourth World Fisheries Congress","language":"en","page":"635-636","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Black Seas fisheries and ecosystem change: discriminating between natural variability and human-related effects","title-short":"Reconciling fisheries with conservation","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1365-2427.2008.02115.x","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Daskalov","given":"G M"},{"family":"Prodanov","given":"K"},{"family":"Zengin","given":"M"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. After 1990, the fishing effort decreased and a slow recovery of small pelagic fishes occurred during the 2000s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"an9ne9cfca","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Georgi M. Daskalov, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Georgi M. Daskalov, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1228,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5HCH8HFF"],"itemData":{"id":1228,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Biology","DOI":"10.1111/gcb.13508","ISSN":"1354-1013, 1365-2486","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Glob Change Biol","language":"en","page":"1486-1498","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Architecture of collapse: regime shift and recovery in an hierarchically structured marine ecosystem","title-short":"Architecture of collapse","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/gcb.13508","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Daskalov","given":"Georgi M."},{"family":"Boicenco","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Grishin","given":"Alexandre N."},{"family":"Lazar","given":"Luminita"},{"family":"Mihneva","given":"Vesselina"},{"family":"Shlyakhov","given":"Vladislav A."},{"family":"Zengin","given":"Mustafa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Starting in the 1970s several human activities further induced a deterioration of the environmental conditions. Intensive bottom trawling on the shelf provoked dispersal of sediment, which severely decreased water transparency, and its re-sedimentation buried demersal life under thick silt layer ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a21gpl5gott","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Samyshev & Rubinstein, 1988)","plainCitation":"(Samyshev & Rubinstein, 1988)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1222,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/54LQPVFC"],"itemData":{"id":1222,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Proceedings of the III Vsecoyuznaya konferentziya po morskoy biologii, Sevastopol, octobre 1988, volume 2, Kiev.","page":"137-139","title":"Change in structure of plankton and benthos in the Black Sea by the anthropogenic factors","author":[{"family":"Samyshev","given":"E Z"},{"family":"Rubinstein","given":"I G"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1988"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Increased nutrient loading from rivers and coastal sources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a26fgp9gqne","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zaitsev & Mamaev, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Zaitsev & Mamaev, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1221,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G48BDVXV"],"itemData":{"id":1221,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Colonial Waterbirds","DOI":"10.2307/1521749","ISSN":"07386028","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Colonial Waterbirds","page":"113","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Marine Biological Diversity in the Black Sea: A Study of Change and Decline","title-short":"Marine Biological Diversity in the Black Sea","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/1521749?origin=crossref","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Zaitsev","given":"Y."},{"family":"Mamaev","given":"V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} favoured frequent plankton blooms, equally contributing decreasing transparency and ventilation leading to benthic life kills. The degradation of massive phytoplankton blooms by aerobic bacteria that pump oxygen from the water further promoted hypoxia, especially near the bottom. By the 1990s, biological invasion of the ctenophore Mnemiopsis leidyi (brought in ship ballast water) has contributed to depletion of zooplankton and collapses of small pelagic fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2mgjcvm40a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Knowler, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Knowler, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2Y3B52T2"],"itemData":{"id":1225,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2004.06.013","ISSN":"09218009","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"187-199","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Reassessing the costs of biological invasion: Mnemiopsis leidyi in the Black sea","title-short":"Reassessing the costs of biological invasion","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800904002927","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Knowler","given":"D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Driving factors and mechanisms of trophic cascades and regime shifts
Ecosystem shifts cascading down from top-predators to primary producers and affecting water quality were registered along the 1970s and 1990s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2po6rppq1i","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G M Daskalov, 2008)","plainCitation":"(G M Daskalov, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1227,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2FE59TUU"],"itemData":{"id":1227,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Freshwater Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2427.2008.02115.x","event-title":"Reconciling fisheries with conservation: Proceedings of the Fourth World Fisheries Congress","language":"en","page":"635-636","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Black Seas fisheries and ecosystem change: discriminating between natural variability and human-related effects","title-short":"Reconciling fisheries with conservation","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1365-2427.2008.02115.x","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Daskalov","given":"G M"},{"family":"Prodanov","given":"K"},{"family":"Zengin","given":"M"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The first shift followed the depletion of top predators from the 1950-1970, after which the ecosystem stabilized at low abundance of top predators, high abundance of planktivores, low zooplankton biomass and high phytoplankton biomasses during the 1970s and 1980s. The second shift was associated with the collapse of planktivorous fish and outburst of M. leidyi in the early 1990s, which resulted in a second system-wide trophic cascade, with similar alternating effects on zoo- and phytoplankton, and on water chemistry. Overfishing was recognised as the structuring factor affecting not only fish stocks, but the whole ecosystem and held responsible for the system shifts to unhealthy states ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2986hnv44j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G M Daskalov, 2008)","plainCitation":"(G M Daskalov, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1227,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2FE59TUU"],"itemData":{"id":1227,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Freshwater Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2427.2008.02115.x","event-title":"Reconciling fisheries with conservation: Proceedings of the Fourth World Fisheries Congress","language":"en","page":"635-636","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Black Seas fisheries and ecosystem change: discriminating between natural variability and human-related effects","title-short":"Reconciling fisheries with conservation","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1365-2427.2008.02115.x","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Daskalov","given":"G M"},{"family":"Prodanov","given":"K"},{"family":"Zengin","given":"M"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Overfishing also contributed to hypoxia by cascading increase of phytoplankton and subsequently bacteria activity. Regional and global climate change, eutrophication, and invasive species were also reported to synergistically contribute to ecosystem shifts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a21ifben7vk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Georgi M. Daskalov, 2017; Oguz & Gilbert, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Georgi M. Daskalov, 2017; Oguz & Gilbert, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1228,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5HCH8HFF"],"itemData":{"id":1228,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Biology","DOI":"10.1111/gcb.13508","ISSN":"1354-1013, 1365-2486","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Glob Change Biol","language":"en","page":"1486-1498","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Architecture of collapse: regime shift and recovery in an hierarchically structured marine ecosystem","title-short":"Architecture of collapse","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/gcb.13508","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Daskalov","given":"Georgi M."},{"family":"Boicenco","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Grishin","given":"Alexandre N."},{"family":"Lazar","given":"Luminita"},{"family":"Mihneva","given":"Vesselina"},{"family":"Shlyakhov","given":"Vladislav A."},{"family":"Zengin","given":"Mustafa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",4]]}}},{"id":1224,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S6DP9FWU"],"itemData":{"id":1224,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Deep-Sea Research I","DOI":"10.1016/j.dsr.200609.010","title":"Abrupt transitions of the top-down controlled Black Sea pelagic ecosystem during 1960–2000: evidence for regime-shifts under strong fishery exploitation and nutrient enrichment modulated by climate-induced variations","author":[{"family":"Oguz","given":"T"},{"family":"Gilbert","given":"D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Effects of trophic cascades and regime shifts on fisheries
The cascading shifts have affected the whole food web from top-predators to primary producers, with repercussions on water chemistry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ase7fsr693","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G M Daskalov, 2008)","plainCitation":"(G M Daskalov, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1227,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2FE59TUU"],"itemData":{"id":1227,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Freshwater Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2427.2008.02115.x","event-title":"Reconciling fisheries with conservation: Proceedings of the Fourth World Fisheries Congress","language":"en","page":"635-636","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Black Seas fisheries and ecosystem change: discriminating between natural variability and human-related effects","title-short":"Reconciling fisheries with conservation","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1365-2427.2008.02115.x","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Daskalov","given":"G M"},{"family":"Prodanov","given":"K"},{"family":"Zengin","given":"M"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The environmental degradation has naturally affected fish stocks and fisheries relying on them ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"akhm3bqvvj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G M Daskalov, 2008; Zaitsev & Mamaev, 1998)","plainCitation":"(G M Daskalov, 2008; Zaitsev & Mamaev, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1227,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2FE59TUU"],"itemData":{"id":1227,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Freshwater Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2427.2008.02115.x","event-title":"Reconciling fisheries with conservation: Proceedings of the Fourth World Fisheries Congress","language":"en","page":"635-636","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Black Seas fisheries and ecosystem change: discriminating between natural variability and human-related effects","title-short":"Reconciling fisheries with conservation","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1365-2427.2008.02115.x","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Daskalov","given":"G M"},{"family":"Prodanov","given":"K"},{"family":"Zengin","given":"M"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":1221,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G48BDVXV"],"itemData":{"id":1221,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Colonial Waterbirds","DOI":"10.2307/1521749","ISSN":"07386028","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Colonial Waterbirds","page":"113","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Marine Biological Diversity in the Black Sea: A Study of Change and Decline","title-short":"Marine Biological Diversity in the Black Sea","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/1521749?origin=crossref","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Zaitsev","given":"Y."},{"family":"Mamaev","given":"V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The effect of 1970s trophic cascade on fisheries catches has been positive as small pelagic stocks boomed after being released from predation. The 1990s shift however entrained small pelagic stock and fisheries collapses and substantial socio-economic losses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a20c8avqgha","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Knowler, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Knowler, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2Y3B52T2"],"itemData":{"id":1225,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2004.06.013","ISSN":"09218009","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"187-199","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Reassessing the costs of biological invasion: Mnemiopsis leidyi in the Black sea","title-short":"Reassessing the costs of biological invasion","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800904002927","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Knowler","given":"D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although recovery of previous states is unlikely, some components of the ecosystem have been subject to partial recoveries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"abvi220hf2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Georgi M. Daskalov, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Georgi M. Daskalov, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1228,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5HCH8HFF"],"itemData":{"id":1228,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Biology","DOI":"10.1111/gcb.13508","ISSN":"1354-1013, 1365-2486","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Glob Change Biol","language":"en","page":"1486-1498","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Architecture of collapse: regime shift and recovery in an hierarchically structured marine ecosystem","title-short":"Architecture of collapse","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/gcb.13508","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Daskalov","given":"Georgi M."},{"family":"Boicenco","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Grishin","given":"Alexandre N."},{"family":"Lazar","given":"Luminita"},{"family":"Mihneva","given":"Vesselina"},{"family":"Shlyakhov","given":"Vladislav A."},{"family":"Zengin","given":"Mustafa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The overall state on the marine environment has improved with the reduction of the nutrient load, partial control over M. leidyi, and more intense turnover rates related to warmer sea water. Following reduction in the fishing pressure, stocks and catches of small pelagic species recovered to intermediate levels, but large valuable species such as turbot, bonito and bluefish remain scarce according to historical abundances. Current single-species based management practices seem insufficient to deal with consequences of ecosystem regime shifts. At present the existing management bodies at national and international levels fail to implement ecosystem-based management. Recovery of resilient ecosystems should mean restoring all important components (including top-predators) into a desirable state with reduced anthropogenic impacts, normalized species interactions, buffered trophic cascades, increased biodiversity and improved environmental quality. This ecosystem state would provide strategic benefits, such as a clean marine environment, abundant and diverse fish stocks and sustainable economic activities (e.g., fishing, tourism), to a range of stakeholders and society as a whole.

	Of the 23 stocks of the seven principal market tuna species, 9 have biomass levels that are below a level estimated to produce maximum sustainable yields or similar thresholds. The fishing mortality rate exceeds a maximum sustainable yield-based or similar reference point, indicating that the stock is not rebuilding its biomass, or both ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"60nG0p3U","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(ISSF, 2016)","plainCitation":"(ISSF, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9453,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BVPX9ANE"],"itemData":{"id":9453,"type":"document","note":"ISSF Technical Report 2016-05","publisher":"International Seafood Sustainability Foundation","title":"Tuna Stock Status Update – 2016","author":[{"family":"ISSF","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Most tuna stocks are either under-exploited or fully-exploited, dominated by skipjack, albacore and yellowfin tunas. As discussed above, while the use of some of these principal market species is considered sustainable when assessed against certain metrics such as the FAO’s definition of overexploited (3.3.1), vulnerable species bycatch accompanies the fishing activity. As political attention to problematic bycatch in marine capture fisheries has increased over recent decades, more resources have been allocated to assess the status of incidentally captured stocks and populations that are of relatively high risk, including, for example, silky and oceanic whitetip sharks, false killer whales, leatherback and loggerhead sea turtles, and several pelagic seabirds including albatrosses and large petrels. These assessments have included semi-quantitative ecological risk assessments using productivity-susceptibility analysis that informs the relative risk of affected stocks and populations, and quantitative, model-based and data-intensive stock assessments and population models that provide information on the absolute risks to affected stocks and populations.
	The International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List global species-level categorizations do not provide information on the status of individual populations/stocks. Of the 61 species belonging to Suborder Scombroidei, species assessed against the International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List criteria, 13% were listed as Threatened and 7% as Near Threatened ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"epOV6gyw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Collette, 2011; IUCN, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Collette, 2011; IUCN, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1246,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DNJ3HCPL"],"itemData":{"id":1246,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1208730","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6040","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"291-292","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"High Value and Long Life—Double Jeopardy for Tunas and Billfishes","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1208730","volume":"333","author":[{"family":"Collette","given":"B. B."},{"family":"Carpenter","given":"K. E."},{"family":"Polidoro","given":"B. A."},{"family":"Juan-Jordá","given":"M. J."},{"family":"Boustany","given":"A."},{"family":"Die","given":"D. J."},{"family":"Elfes","given":"C."},{"family":"Fox","given":"W."},{"family":"Graves","given":"J."},{"family":"Harrison","given":"L. R."},{"family":"McManus","given":"R."},{"family":"Minte-Vera","given":"C. V."},{"family":"Nelson","given":"R."},{"family":"Restrepo","given":"V."},{"family":"Schratwieser","given":"J."},{"family":"Sun","given":"C.-L."},{"family":"Amorim","given":"A."},{"family":"Brick Peres","given":"M."},{"family":"Canales","given":"C."},{"family":"Cardenas","given":"G."},{"family":"Chang","given":"S.-K."},{"family":"Chiang","given":"W.-C."},{"family":"Oliveira Leite","given":"N.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Harwell","given":"H."},{"family":"Lessa","given":"R."},{"family":"Fredou","given":"F. L."},{"family":"Oxenford","given":"H. A."},{"family":"Serra","given":"R."},{"family":"Shao","given":"K.-T."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"R."},{"family":"Wang","given":"S.-P."},{"family":"Watson","given":"R."},{"family":"Yáñez","given":"E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",7,15]]}}},{"id":9481,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NXW29KMD"],"itemData":{"id":9481,"type":"dataset","DOI":"10.2305/IUCN.UK.2014-3.RLTS.T170341A65166749.en","language":"en","publisher":"International Union for Conservation of Nature","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Thunnus orientalis: Collette, B., Fox, W., Juan Jorda, M., Nelson, R., Pollard, D., Suzuki, N. & Teo, S.: The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2014: e.T170341A65166749","title-short":"Thunnus orientalis","URL":"http://www.iucnredlist.org/details/170341/0","author":[{"literal":"IUCN"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",8,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Of the Scombroidei, Pacific bluefin, Southern bluefin, Atlantic bluefin and bigeye tuna were categorized as Threatened. The characteristics that these four species of threatened tunas have in common are long generational lengths, longer-lived and later maturity. When combined these traits results in longer time to recover from population declines ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1iv4l6jets","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Collette, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Collette, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1246,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DNJ3HCPL"],"itemData":{"id":1246,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1208730","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6040","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"291-292","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"High Value and Long Life—Double Jeopardy for Tunas and Billfishes","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1208730","volume":"333","author":[{"family":"Collette","given":"B. B."},{"family":"Carpenter","given":"K. E."},{"family":"Polidoro","given":"B. A."},{"family":"Juan-Jordá","given":"M. J."},{"family":"Boustany","given":"A."},{"family":"Die","given":"D. J."},{"family":"Elfes","given":"C."},{"family":"Fox","given":"W."},{"family":"Graves","given":"J."},{"family":"Harrison","given":"L. R."},{"family":"McManus","given":"R."},{"family":"Minte-Vera","given":"C. V."},{"family":"Nelson","given":"R."},{"family":"Restrepo","given":"V."},{"family":"Schratwieser","given":"J."},{"family":"Sun","given":"C.-L."},{"family":"Amorim","given":"A."},{"family":"Brick Peres","given":"M."},{"family":"Canales","given":"C."},{"family":"Cardenas","given":"G."},{"family":"Chang","given":"S.-K."},{"family":"Chiang","given":"W.-C."},{"family":"Oliveira Leite","given":"N.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Harwell","given":"H."},{"family":"Lessa","given":"R."},{"family":"Fredou","given":"F. L."},{"family":"Oxenford","given":"H. A."},{"family":"Serra","given":"R."},{"family":"Shao","given":"K.-T."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"R."},{"family":"Wang","given":"S.-P."},{"family":"Watson","given":"R."},{"family":"Yáñez","given":"E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",7,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These threatened tuna species also have higher economic values per unit of weight relative to the other market tunas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vAWjp78Z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Miyake, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Miyake, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9483,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EFTVGYBX","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XFRF6NSW"],"itemData":{"id":9483,"type":"book","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","title":"Recent Developments in the Tuna Industry. Stocks, Fisheries, Management, Processing, Trade and Markets","author":[{"family":"Miyake","given":"M"},{"family":"Guillotreau","given":"P"},{"family":"Sun","given":"C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      While there were some early concerns over their application to exploited fishes this largely reflects a misunderstanding of how the criteria work ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1q1k9kqkca","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mace & Hudson, 1999; Reynolds & Mace, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Mace & Hudson, 1999; Reynolds & Mace, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1213,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RT4ERFQS"],"itemData":{"id":1213,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1046/j.1523-1739.1999.013002242.x","ISSN":"08888892, 15231739","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"242-246","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Attitudes toward Sustainability and Extinction","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1046/j.1523-1739.1999.013002242.x","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Mace","given":"Georgina M."},{"family":"Hudson","given":"Elodie J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999",4]]}}},{"id":1209,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/633YS3XI"],"itemData":{"id":1209,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/S0169-5347(99)01629-8","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"215-217","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Risk assessments of threatened species","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534799016298","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Reynolds","given":"John D"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Georgina M"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List assessments are global in scale whereas fisheries assessments are regional in scale and hence these different assessment processes are used for different purposes. However residual concerns about the applicability of the International Union for Conservation of Nature criteria have been refuted by extensive empirical evidence that consistently show strong alignment and harmony with fisheries management reference points, based on simulations using data from the global population dynamics database ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"alk91pvv1j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Connors, Cooper, Peterman, & Dulvy, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Connors, Cooper, Peterman, & Dulvy, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1217,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UAWJ23LA"],"itemData":{"id":1217,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abundance trends are the basis for many classifications of threat and recovery status, but they can be a challenge to interpret because of observation error, stochastic variation in abundance (process noise) and temporal autocorrelation in that process noise. To measure the frequency of incorrectly detecting a decline (false-positive or false alarm) and failing to detect a true decline (false-negative), we simulated stable and declining abundance time series across several magnitudes of observation error and autocorrelated process noise. We then empirically estimated the magnitude of observation error and autocorrelated process noise across a broad range of taxa and mapped these estimates onto the simulated parameter space. Based on the taxa we examined, at low classification thresholds (30% decline in abundance) and short observation windows (10 years), false alarms would be expected to occur, on average, about 40% of the time assuming density-independent dynamics, whereas false-negatives would be expected to occur about 60% of the time. However, false alarms and failures to detect true declines were reduced at higher classification thresholds (50% or 80% declines), longer observation windows (20, 40, 60 years), and assuming density-dependent dynamics. The lowest false-positive and false-negative rates are likely to occur for large-bodied, long-lived animal species.","container-title":"Proceedings of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences","DOI":"10.1098/rspb.2013.2935","ISSN":"0962-8452, 1471-2954","issue":"1787","journalAbbreviation":"Proc. R. Soc. B.","language":"en","page":"20132935","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The false classification of extinction risk in noisy environments","URL":"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rspb.2013.2935","volume":"281","author":[{"family":"Connors","given":"B. M."},{"family":"Cooper","given":"A. B."},{"family":"Peterman","given":"R. M."},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"N. K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",7,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and multiple global meta-analyses of all fisheries stock assessments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a36i88tjjt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(e.g. Davies & Baum, 2012; d\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Eon-Eggertson, Dulvy, & Peterman, 2015; P. G. Fernandes, 2017; Porszt, Peterman, Dulvy, Cooper, & Irvine, 2012)","plainCitation":"(e.g. Davies & Baum, 2012; d’Eon-Eggertson, Dulvy, & Peterman, 2015; P. G. Fernandes, 2017; Porszt, Peterman, Dulvy, Cooper, & Irvine, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1212,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D4GNVCFU"],"itemData":{"id":1212,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep00561","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"561","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Extinction Risk and Overfishing: Reconciling Conservation and Fisheries Perspectives on the Status of Marine Fishes","title-short":"Extinction Risk and Overfishing","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/srep00561","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Davies","given":"Trevor D."},{"family":"Baum","given":"Julia K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",12]]}},"prefix":"e.g. "},{"id":1216,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NP6CU47X"],"itemData":{"id":1216,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12123","ISSN":"1755263X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Letters","language":"en","page":"86-96","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Reliable Identification of Declining Populations in an Uncertain World: Identifying declines in an uncertain world","title-short":"Reliable Identification of Declining Populations in an Uncertain World","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/conl.12123","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Eggertson","given":"Faye","non-dropping-particle":"d’Eon-"},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K."},{"family":"Peterman","given":"Randall M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",3]]}}},{"id":1211,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B6Z4GYX8"],"itemData":{"id":1211,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1038/s41559-017-0170","ISSN":"2397-334X","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Ecol Evol","language":"en","page":"0170","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Coherent assessments of Europe’s marine fishes show regional divergence and megafauna loss","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41559-017-0170","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Fernandes","given":"Paul G."},{"family":"Ralph","given":"Gina M."},{"family":"Nieto","given":"Ana"},{"family":"García Criado","given":"Mariana"},{"family":"Vasilakopoulos","given":"Paraskevas"},{"family":"Maravelias","given":"Christos D."},{"family":"Cook","given":"Robin M."},{"family":"Pollom","given":"Riley A."},{"family":"Kova?i?","given":"Marcelo"},{"family":"Pollard","given":"David"},{"family":"Farrell","given":"Edward D."},{"family":"Florin","given":"Ann-Britt"},{"family":"Polidoro","given":"Beth A."},{"family":"Lawson","given":"Julia M."},{"family":"Lorance","given":"Pascal"},{"family":"Uiblein","given":"Franz"},{"family":"Craig","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Allen","given":"David J."},{"family":"Fowler","given":"Sarah L."},{"family":"Walls","given":"Rachel H. L."},{"family":"Comeros-Raynal","given":"Mia T."},{"family":"Harvey","given":"Michael S."},{"family":"Dureuil","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Biscoito","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Pollock","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"McCully Phillips","given":"Sophy R."},{"family":"Ellis","given":"Jim R."},{"family":"Papaconstantinou","given":"Constantinos"},{"family":"Soldo","given":"Alen"},{"family":"Keskin","given":"Çetin"},{"family":"Knudsen","given":"Steen Wilhelm"},{"family":"Gil de Sola","given":"Luís"},{"family":"Serena","given":"Fabrizio"},{"family":"Collette","given":"Bruce B."},{"family":"Nedreaas","given":"Kjell"},{"family":"Stump","given":"Emilie"},{"family":"Russell","given":"Barry C."},{"family":"Garcia","given":"Silvia"},{"family":"Afonso","given":"Pedro"},{"family":"Jung","given":"Armelle B. J."},{"family":"Alvarez","given":"Helena"},{"family":"Delgado","given":"João"},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K."},{"family":"Carpenter","given":"Kent E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",7]]}}},{"id":1210,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6GIP2BCF"],"itemData":{"id":1210,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2012.01882.x","ISSN":"08888892","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"894-904","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Reliability of Indicators of Decline in Abundance: Reliability of Indicators of Decline","title-short":"Reliability of Indicators of Decline in Abundance","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2012.01882.x","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Porszt","given":"Erin J."},{"family":"Peterman","given":"Randall M."},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K."},{"family":"Cooper","given":"Andrew B."},{"family":"Irvine","given":"James R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The greatest concerns were raised for the highly fecund broad cast-spawning fishes, yet the International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List categories and criteria have been shown to highly aligned with fisheries assessment. As a result, marine fishes assessed by the International Union for Conservation of Nature as being Endangered or Critically Endangered are consistently fished beyond target and limit reference points ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a18ts21u127","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dulvy, Jennings, Goodwin, Grant, & Reynolds, 2005; Simpfendorfer & Dulvy, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Dulvy, Jennings, Goodwin, Grant, & Reynolds, 2005; Simpfendorfer & Dulvy, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1215,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R2I27SBU"],"itemData":{"id":1215,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2664.2005.01063.x","ISSN":"00218901, 13652664","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"883-891","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Comparison of threat and exploitation status in North-East Atlantic marine populations: Do threat criteria raise false alarms?","title-short":"Comparison of threat and exploitation status in North-East Atlantic marine populations","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1365-2664.2005.01063.x","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K."},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Goodwin","given":"Nicholas B."},{"family":"Grant","given":"Alastair"},{"family":"Reynolds","given":"John D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",7,28]]}}},{"id":1229,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/96T7N8AC"],"itemData":{"id":1229,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Biology","DOI":"10.1016/j.cub.2016.12.017","ISSN":"09609822","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Current Biology","language":"en","page":"R97-R98","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Bright spots of sustainable shark fishing","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0960982216314646","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Simpfendorfer","given":"Colin A."},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For species that are not subject to fisheries assessments, the International Union for Conservation of Nature assessments offer valuable information on the need for fisheries management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a7sh7g2iti","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(ICES, 2018)","plainCitation":"(ICES, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1214,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M9W7IZW8"],"itemData":{"id":1214,"type":"report","event-place":"Copenhagen, Denmark","publisher":"International Council for the Exploration of the Seas (ICES)","publisher-place":"Copenhagen, Denmark","title":"EU request on analysis of the IUCN process for the assessment of the conservation status of marine species in comparison to the process used by fisheries management bodies","author":[{"family":"ICES","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

3.3.1.4.5. Whaling

Aquatic mammals are an important hunting target species for subsistence, culture and identity of some indigenous and local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2hp18cuqk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IWC, 2021; S. L. Newell & Doubleday, 2020)","plainCitation":"(IWC, 2021; S. L. Newell & Doubleday, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KB5HW8HM"],"itemData":{"id":30811,"type":"report","archive":"https://archive.iwc.int/pages/view.php?ref=17766&search=%21collection29+&order_by=title&offset=0&restypes=&starsearch=&archive=&per_page=240&default_sort_direction=DESC&sort=DESC&context=Root&k=&curpos=&go=previous&","event-place":"Cambrige, UK","number":"19276","publisher-place":"Cambrige, UK","title":"Report Of The Scientific Committee SC68C","URL":"https://archive.iwc.int/pages/view.php?ref=17766&search=%21collection29+&order_by=title&offset=0&restypes=&starsearch=&archive=&per_page=240&default_sort_direction=DESC&sort=DESC&context=Root&k=&curpos=&go=previous&","author":[{"family":"IWC","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":1275,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HMMKN6IL"],"itemData":{"id":1275,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Food security is a complex topic defined not just by having enough nutritious food to eat but also by cost, safety and cultural considerations. In Arctic Inuit communities, food security is intimately connected to culture through traditional methods of harvesting country food. In Chesterfield Inlet, Nunavut, community-based research was conducted in collaboration with Chesterfield Inlet community members using interviews and community engagement. Community members were consulted about the design of the interview guide, recruitment of participants, analysis and validation of results. This study aims to develop a theoretical framework of how food security, cultural continuity and community health and well-being are interconnected to allow for a richer understanding of how increased shipping, climate change and social changes are impacting community members. In Chesterfield Inlet, harvesting and consuming country food (e.g., seal) is perceived as the mechanism that connects food, culture and community health. Sharing of freshly harvested country food supports the food security of community members without hunters in their families, aligns with hunters’ cultural beliefs and promotes community health and well-being. Changes that reduce a hunter’s success in harvesting country food limit her or his ability to share country food, which negatively impacts community health and well-being. The results of this study support existing community efforts to adapt to changes that impact harvesting success.","container-title":"Polar Research","DOI":"10.33265/polar.v39.3755","ISSN":"1751-8369","language":"en","license":"Copyright (c) 2020 Sarah L. Newell, Nancy C. Doubleday, & Community of Chesterfield Inlet,  Nunavut","source":"polarresearch.net","title":"Sharing country food: connecting health, food security and cultural continuity in Chesterfield Inlet, Nunavut","title-short":"Sharing country food","URL":"https://polarresearch.net/index.php/polar/article/view/3755","author":[{"family":"Newell","given":"Sarah L."},{"family":"Doubleday","given":"Nancy C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,23]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Box 3.4). Across South America and West Africa hunted aquatic mammals includes 33 small cetaceans and all three manatee species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aulm1rurgd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cosentino & Fisher, 2016; Porter & Lai, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Cosentino & Fisher, 2016; Porter & Lai, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1231,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2H4U64UE"],"itemData":{"id":1231,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Aquatic bushmeat can be de?ned as the products derived from wild aquatic megafauna (e.g., marine mammals) that are used for human consumption and non-food purposes, including traditional medicine. It is obtained through illegal or unregulated hunts as well as from stranded (dead or alive) and bycaught animals. In most South American and West African countries aquatic mammals are or have been taken for bushmeat, including 33 small cetaceans and all three manatee species. Of these, two cetacean species are listed in the IUCN red list as “near threatened,” and one as “vulnerable,” as are all manatee species. Additionally, 22 cetacean species are listed as “data de?cient,” hence some of these species may also be at risk. No reports (recent or otherwise) were found for some countries, caution is needed in concluding that aquatic bushmeat is not utilized in these nations. Moreover, although aquatic bushmeat is mostly obtained opportunistically and was likely originally taken only for local consumption, directed catches occur in most countries and may have reached unsustainable levels in some areas. For example, in Peru and Nigeria, thousands of small cetaceans are illegally hunted annually. Reliable, recent data and a better overall understanding of the drivers of aquatic bushmeat will be essential in the development of effective mitigation measures.","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2016.00163","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Utilization of Aquatic Bushmeat from Small Cetaceans and Manatees in South America and West Africa","URL":"http://journal.frontiersin.org/Article/10.3389/fmars.2016.00163/abstract","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Cosentino","given":"A. Mel"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Sue"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",9,16]]}}},{"id":1205,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NVCBU7CA"],"itemData":{"id":1205,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2017.00047","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Marine Mammals in Asian Societies; Trends in Consumption, Bait, and Traditional Use","URL":"http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fmars.2017.00047/full","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Porter","given":"Lindsay"},{"family":"Lai","given":"Hong Yu"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2,28]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The vast majority of whales hunted for aboriginal subsistence in Denmark, Norway and Iceland (Figure 3.25A, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1o6hr7db58","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(International Whaling Commission., 2021)","plainCitation":"(International Whaling Commission., 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1266,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EEGZKBG6"],"itemData":{"id":1266,"type":"webpage","title":"Total catches. Available at https://iwc.int/total-catches, Downloaded August 2021.","author":[{"family":"International Whaling Commission.","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}) are common minkes. Besides, Greenland (Denmark) has been conducting aboriginal subsistence whaling targeting fin, bowhead and humpback whales as well as commercial whaling targeting narwhal and other small cetaceans. Faroe Islands (Denmark) has been conducting the drive fishery targeting pilot whales. Norway and Iceland have been conducting commercial whaling on fin whales. In these countries local hunters often sell whale meat to foreign tourists or in European Union markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ecrds7hql","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eklund T., 2017)","plainCitation":"(Eklund T., 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1277,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XWQADGQC"],"itemData":{"id":1277,"type":"thesis","genre":"Master's thesis","number-of-pages":"84","publisher":"Management and Economy in the International Forest Sector, Tampere University of Applied Sciences","title":"Possibilities, Challenges and International Demand for Commercial Hunting Services in Finland - New economic growth from the Finnish bioeconomy","URL":"https://www.theseus.fi/bitstream/handle/10024/132914/Eklund_Tiina.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y","author":[{"family":"Eklund T.","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indigenous communities in the Russian Federation mostly hunt the gray whale ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2irob99pb5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IWC, 2019a)","plainCitation":"(IWC, 2019a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1207,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMDT6PMT"],"itemData":{"id":1207,"type":"webpage","container-title":"International Whaling Commission","title":"Description of the aboriginal subsistence hunt in Chukotka, Russian Federation","URL":"https://iwc.int/russian-federation","author":[{"family":"IWC","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"literal":"2019a"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and in the United States of America, the bowhead whale ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2o7n1kdgut","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IWC, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IWC, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1208,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5F4KKEXZ"],"itemData":{"id":1208,"type":"webpage","container-title":"International Whaling Commission","title":"Description of the USA aboriginal subsistence hunt: Makah Tribe","URL":"https://iwc.int/makah-tribe","author":[{"family":"IWC","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"literal":"2019b"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In India, Pakistan and Sri Lanka cetaceans are also hunted (often illegally) for use as bait in other fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1rmhucqdg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Porter & Lai, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Porter & Lai, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1205,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NVCBU7CA"],"itemData":{"id":1205,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2017.00047","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Marine Mammals in Asian Societies; Trends in Consumption, Bait, and Traditional Use","URL":"http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fmars.2017.00047/full","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Porter","given":"Lindsay"},{"family":"Lai","given":"Hong Yu"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2,28]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Aquatic wild species can also be utilized on a commercial basis (Figure 3.25). Since 1982, the International Whaling Commission which regulates commercial whaling has maintained a "zero quota" on commercial whaling (with the exception of catches set by countries under objection or reservation) because of historical overexploitation and the challenge of managing whaling sustainably. The organization currently has 88 members. Japan suspended commercial whaling in 1988 and began whaling for scientific research in 1987 to gather population data in accordance with the paragraph 10e of the Schedule of the International Convention for the Regulation of Whaling ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ifmrofpdn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cosentino & Fisher, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Cosentino & Fisher, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1231,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2H4U64UE"],"itemData":{"id":1231,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Aquatic bushmeat can be de?ned as the products derived from wild aquatic megafauna (e.g., marine mammals) that are used for human consumption and non-food purposes, including traditional medicine. It is obtained through illegal or unregulated hunts as well as from stranded (dead or alive) and bycaught animals. In most South American and West African countries aquatic mammals are or have been taken for bushmeat, including 33 small cetaceans and all three manatee species. Of these, two cetacean species are listed in the IUCN red list as “near threatened,” and one as “vulnerable,” as are all manatee species. Additionally, 22 cetacean species are listed as “data de?cient,” hence some of these species may also be at risk. No reports (recent or otherwise) were found for some countries, caution is needed in concluding that aquatic bushmeat is not utilized in these nations. Moreover, although aquatic bushmeat is mostly obtained opportunistically and was likely originally taken only for local consumption, directed catches occur in most countries and may have reached unsustainable levels in some areas. For example, in Peru and Nigeria, thousands of small cetaceans are illegally hunted annually. Reliable, recent data and a better overall understanding of the drivers of aquatic bushmeat will be essential in the development of effective mitigation measures.","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2016.00163","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Utilization of Aquatic Bushmeat from Small Cetaceans and Manatees in South America and West Africa","URL":"http://journal.frontiersin.org/Article/10.3389/fmars.2016.00163/abstract","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Cosentino","given":"A. Mel"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Sue"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",9,16]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} 2016). In accordance with the provisions of the International Convention for the Regulation of Whaling (Article 8), all meat taken from whales caught for scientific whaling was processed and sold in stores and restaurants, and the proceeds obtained from the sales were used for the research activities in the following years in accordance with the direction by the Government of Japan. The International Court of Justice, using various criteria, ruled that Japan?s whaling was not “for purposes of scientific research” as required by Article VIII of the International Convention for the Regulation of Whaling, and ordered Japan to immediately cease its JARPA II whaling program (JARPA II: second phase of Japan's whale research program under special permit in the Antarctic) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2i0grfvqt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Clapham, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Clapham, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30812,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R9U7THE8"],"itemData":{"id":30812,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Since 1987, Japan has conducted extensive special permit whaling (“scientific whaling”) in the Antarctic and North Pacific. This has been viewed by many as a way to circumvent the International Whaling Commission?s (IWC) moratorium on commercial whaling, which was implemented in 1985. Recently, Australia took Japan to the International Court of Justice (ICJ) over this issue. Using various criteria, the Court ruled that Japan?s whaling was not “for purposes of scientific research” as required by Article VIII of the International Convention for the Regulation of Whaling, and ordered Japan to immediately cease its JARPA II whaling program in the Antarctic. Despite optimism that the Court?s ruling might spell the end of Japanese whaling in the Antarctic and even elsewhere, Japan has indicated that it will redesign its whaling programs and continue operations. Based upon Japan?s history at the IWC, I argue here that this was an expected outcome; I predict the course of events over the next months, and suggest that the ICJ ruling, while satisfying as an independent vindication of Japan?s critics, represents little more than a temporary setback for that nation?s whaling enterprise.","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2014.08.011","ISSN":"0308-597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"238-241","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Japan?s whaling following the International Court of Justice ruling: Brave New World – Or business as usual?","title-short":"Japan?s whaling following the International Court of Justice ruling","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308597X14002279","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Clapham","given":"Phillip J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	In 2019, Japan withdrew from the International Convention for the Regulation of Whaling, in line with Japan’s basic policy of promoting sustainable use of aquatic living resources based on scientific evidence, and resumed commercial whaling after 31 years of suspension ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QGLQJk0B","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Holm, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Holm, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9576,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5BBNVAJF"],"itemData":{"id":9576,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Asia - Pacific Journal : Japan Focus","DOI":"10.5167/uzh-179152","ISSN":"1557-4660","issue":"4, 4","language":"eng","license":"info:eu-repo/semantics/openAccess","note":"number: 4, 4\\npublisher: Japan Focus","page":"1-16","source":"www.zora.uzh.ch","title":"After Withdrawal from the IWC: The Future of Japanese Whaling","title-short":"After Withdrawal from the IWC","URL":"https://www.zora.uzh.ch/id/eprint/179152/","volume":"vol. 17","author":[{"family":"Holm","given":"Fynn"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Norway and Iceland are members of the International Whaling Commission, but have continued to commercially hunt whales either under objection to the moratorium decision or under reservation to it ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1l1mvu9ci8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IWC, 2021)","plainCitation":"(IWC, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KB5HW8HM"],"itemData":{"id":30811,"type":"report","archive":"https://archive.iwc.int/pages/view.php?ref=17766&search=%21collection29+&order_by=title&offset=0&restypes=&starsearch=&archive=&per_page=240&default_sort_direction=DESC&sort=DESC&context=Root&k=&curpos=&go=previous&","event-place":"Cambrige, UK","number":"19276","publisher-place":"Cambrige, UK","title":"Report Of The Scientific Committee SC68C","URL":"https://archive.iwc.int/pages/view.php?ref=17766&search=%21collection29+&order_by=title&offset=0&restypes=&starsearch=&archive=&per_page=240&default_sort_direction=DESC&sort=DESC&context=Root&k=&curpos=&go=previous&","author":[{"family":"IWC","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Russian Federation has also objected to the moratorium, but has not resumed whaling.  Countries members of the Illegal Whaling Commission that take whales are obliged to provide statistical, scientific and other pertinent information to the International Whaling Commission. While the Western North Pacific stock of common minke and Bryde’s whales are confirmed by the Illegal Whaling Commission Scientific Committee to be relatively abundant, abundance estimate of North Pacific stock of sei whale is still under examination by the Illegal Whaling Commission Scientific Committee although it has been substantially recovered. Thus, sei whales as a whole are still classified as endangered by the International Union for Conservation of Nature.
      In 2018,  despite a substantial number of opposition, the International Whaling Commission adopted a resolution which reaffirms "that the moratorium on commercial whaling, which has been in effect since 1986, has contributed to the recovery of some cetacean populations, and aware of the cumulative effects of multiple, existing and emerging threats to cetacean populations such as entanglement, bycatch, underwater noise, ship strikes, marine debris and climate change" and "agrees that the role of the International Whaling Commission in the 21st century includes inter-alia its responsibility to ensure the recovery of cetacean populations to their pre-industrial levels, and in this context reaffirms the importance in maintaining the moratorium on commercial whaling" (Figure 3.25A, B and C).


Figure 3.25 (A, B & C). Global catches of whales according to the International Whaling Commission, 1985-2018 (a) Aboriginal subsistence catches (b) special permit catches (c) commercial catches taken under objection or reservation. CC-BY-NC. See data management report for the figure at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6453071.

Box 3.4 Small-scale indigenous whaling in the North

Many northern Indigenous peoples continue traditional whale hunting, a practice dating back centuries or more ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qrtdwHdy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stoker & Krupnik, 1993)","plainCitation":"(Stoker & Krupnik, 1993)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9507,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3L8RM5DB","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V74YPVI9"],"itemData":{"id":9507,"type":"chapter","abstract":"Stoker, S.W., and Krupnik, I.I. 1993. Subsistence whaling. In: J.J. Burns, J.J. Montague, and C.J. Cowles, eds. The bowhead whale. Special Publication Number 2. Lawrence, Kansas: The Society for Marine Mammalogy. p. 579-629.","container-title":"bowhead whale. Special Publication Number 2.","edition":"J.J. Burns, J.J. Montague, and C.J. Cowles, eds.","publisher":"Lawrence, Kansas: The Society for Marine Mammalogy","title":"Subsistence whaling","author":[{"family":"Stoker","given":"S.W."},{"family":"Krupnik","given":"I. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Whaling provides substantial quantities of food, is a central part of community activities and culture, and a source of fulfillment and identity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MUYKxUhC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sakakibara, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Sakakibara, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9504,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KRUSGAB6"],"itemData":{"id":9504,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-0-8165-4214-7","note":"DOI: 10.2307/j.ctv1595kz4","publisher":"University of Arizona Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Whale Snow: Iñupiat, Climate Change, and Multispecies Resilience in Arctic Alaska","title-short":"Whale Snow","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/10.2307/j.ctv1595kz4","author":[{"family":"Sakakibara","given":"Chie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Collaborative hunts and sharing of the products promote social cohesion, an essential component of thriving in a challenging environment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eFNWA1lK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Huntington, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Huntington, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6396,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CAPY7MV4","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NFDMCSDG"],"itemData":{"id":6396,"type":"chapter","abstract":"Bowhead whaling has attracted a great deal of scholarly attention, from archeology to ethnography to political science. Such research has traced the development and spread of bowhead whaling through the Arctic, the significance of not just the hunt but also the festivals and activities that occur throughout the year, and the battles of whalers to assert their knowledge of the whale and their rights to continue their practices on their own terms. This chapter provides a brief review of the literature on bowhead whaling, and then presents five accounts of Indigenous whaling by Indigenous authors, from two communities in Alaska, one in Chukotka, and one in Nunavut, together with a broader overview of the history of bowhead whaling in Greenland. These accounts, written specifically for this book, demonstrate the vigor of contemporary whaling cultures and the significance that bowhead whaling continues to have for the peoples of Arctic coasts.","container-title":"The Bowhead Whale","ISBN":"978-0-12-818969-6","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1016/B978-0-12-818969-6.00031-5","page":"501-517","publisher":"Academic Press","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Whale hunting in Indigenous Arctic cultures","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780128189696000315","author":[{"family":"Huntington","given":"H. P."},{"family":"Sakakibara","given":"C."},{"family":"Noongwook","given":"G."},{"family":"Kanayurak","given":"N."},{"family":"Skhauge","given":"V."},{"family":"Zdor","given":"E."},{"family":"Inutiq","given":"S."},{"family":"Lyberth","given":"B."}],"editor":[{"family":"George","given":"J. C."},{"family":"Thewissen","given":"J. G. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some places whale products are sold in local markets, which occasionally creates conflicts among users ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Uwu4ttst","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sejersen, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Sejersen, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9510,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8CWCTCH9"],"itemData":{"id":9510,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Modernization, as well as the rapid socioeconomic and political changes that have taken place in Greenland in the 20th century, have altered the interests and concerns of Greenland's hunters. For example, these changes can be observed in the way hunters divide a catch of beluga whales. This article focuses specifically on how beluga hunters have negotiated new ways of dividing the catch in order to respond to new needs and demands. Today, Greenland is a heterogeneous society with a number of different socioeconomic groups: a situation that has intensified conflicts and strategies based on social compartmentalization. The chosen strategies lead us to question the emphasis that social scientists usually place on community integration. Apart from considering local problems, hunters must also relate to a number of elaborate Home Rule regulations that influence their rights and control their activity. The Home Rule government has strengthened the regulations because biologists and international/regional management commissions have concluded that the stock of beluga whales is substantially depleted. The regional management commission, NAMMCO, thus warns that the present harvests are several times the sustainable yield, and, if continued, will likely lead to stock extinction within 20 years. This new concern has made it even more necessary to redefine the rules for catch division and make them locally flexible. The Home Rule regulations are discussed and compared to local ways of dealing with new concerns and interests. /// La modernisation, tout comme les changements brusques qui ont eu lieu au Groenland au cours du XXe siècle aux niveaux socio-économique et politique, ont modifié les intérêts et les préoccupations des chasseurs groenlandais. On peut ainsi observer ces changements dans la façon dont les chasseurs partagent une prise de béluga. Cet article se penche en particulier sur la façon dont les chasseurs de bélugas ont négocié de nouveaux modes de partage des prises afin de répondre aux demandes et aux besoins actuels. Aujourd'hui, le Groenland est une société hétérogène formée de plusieurs groupes socio-économiques différents, situation qui a intensifié les conflits et les stratégies fondés sur la compartimentation sociale. Les stratégies choisies nous amènent à remettre en question l'insistance généralement placée par les spécialistes des sciences sociales sur l'intégration au sein de la collectivité. En plus de tenir compte des problèmes locaux, les chasseurs doivent composer avec plusieurs règlements complexes de la Loi d'autonomie du Groenland, qui influencent leurs droits et contrôlent leur activité. Le gouvernement autonome a renforcé les règlements après que les biologistes et les commissions de gestion internationale et régionale ont conclu que le stock de bélugas connaît un important déclin. La commission de gestion régionale, NAMMCO, prédit ainsi que les prélèvements actuels représentent plusieurs fois un rendement équilibré et que, s'ils se poursuivent, ils mèneront probablement à l'extinction des stocks d'ici vingt ans. Ce nouveau sujet de préoccupation rend encore plus nécessaires une redéfinition des règles pour le partage des prises et leur assouplissement au niveau local. On discute les règlements du gouvernement autonome et on les compare à la façon dont les communautés abordent leurs intérêts et préoccupations actuels.","container-title":"Arctic","ISSN":"0004-0843","issue":"4","note":"publisher: Arctic Institute of North America","page":"431-443","source":"JSTOR","title":"Hunting and Management of Beluga Whales (Delphinapterus Leucas) in Greenland: Changing Strategies to Cope with New National and Local Interests","title-short":"Hunting and Management of Beluga Whales (Delphinapterus Leucas) in Greenland","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/40512399","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Sejersen","given":"Frank"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but rarely leading to excessive exploitation. The legacy of large-scale commercial whaling continues to affect some whale populations in the Arctic, but most stocks appear to have recovered and concerns about unsustainable takes at present are limited ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TYXA1atk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Givens & Heide-J\\\\uc0\\\\u248{}rgensen, 2021; NAMMCO, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Givens & Heide-Jørgensen, 2021; NAMMCO, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5914,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SFU9LCXS","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6QSIQ9DJ"],"itemData":{"id":5914,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The Bowhead Whale","page":"77-86","publisher":"Elsevier","title":"Abundance","author":[{"family":"Givens","given":"Geof H"},{"family":"Heide-Jørgensen","given":"Mads Peter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":9508,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7SYG43EU"],"itemData":{"id":9508,"type":"document","abstract":"Report of the NAMMCO global review of monodontids","publisher":"Hillerød, Denmark. Tromsø, Norway: North Atlantic Marine Mammal Commission.","title":"Report of the NAMMCO global review of monodontids","URL":"https://nammco.no/wp-content/uploads/2018/05/report-global-review-of-monodontids-nammco-2018_after-erratum-060518­_2.pdf","author":[{"family":"NAMMCO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The bowhead whale (Balaena mysticetus; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"biYxY1Z9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Huntington, 2021; Suydam & George, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Huntington, 2021; Suydam & George, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6396,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CAPY7MV4","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NFDMCSDG"],"itemData":{"id":6396,"type":"chapter","abstract":"Bowhead whaling has attracted a great deal of scholarly attention, from archeology to ethnography to political science. Such research has traced the development and spread of bowhead whaling through the Arctic, the significance of not just the hunt but also the festivals and activities that occur throughout the year, and the battles of whalers to assert their knowledge of the whale and their rights to continue their practices on their own terms. This chapter provides a brief review of the literature on bowhead whaling, and then presents five accounts of Indigenous whaling by Indigenous authors, from two communities in Alaska, one in Chukotka, and one in Nunavut, together with a broader overview of the history of bowhead whaling in Greenland. These accounts, written specifically for this book, demonstrate the vigor of contemporary whaling cultures and the significance that bowhead whaling continues to have for the peoples of Arctic coasts.","container-title":"The Bowhead Whale","ISBN":"978-0-12-818969-6","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1016/B978-0-12-818969-6.00031-5","page":"501-517","publisher":"Academic Press","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Whale hunting in Indigenous Arctic cultures","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780128189696000315","author":[{"family":"Huntington","given":"H. P."},{"family":"Sakakibara","given":"C."},{"family":"Noongwook","given":"G."},{"family":"Kanayurak","given":"N."},{"family":"Skhauge","given":"V."},{"family":"Zdor","given":"E."},{"family":"Inutiq","given":"S."},{"family":"Lyberth","given":"B."}],"editor":[{"family":"George","given":"J. C."},{"family":"Thewissen","given":"J. G. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,1]]}}},{"id":6395,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AK5CTE9J","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MGNDYUJI"],"itemData":{"id":6395,"type":"chapter","abstract":"The current subsistence harvest of bowhead whales (Balaena mysticetus) allows Inuit from Greenland, Canada, Alaska, and Chukotka to help fill important nutritional and cultural needs. Greenland and Canada primarily hunt from the East Canada–West Greenland stock and Alaska and Chukotka hunt from the Bering–Chukchi–Beaufort (BCB) Seas stock. There is no hunting on the Spitzbergen and Sea of Okhotsk stocks. The hunts for bowheads in Greenland, Canada, and Chukotka are small, numbering only one or two per year. The hunts on the BCB stock are considerably larger with a landed quota of 56 whales per year. The quota is established by the International Whaling Commission based on the cultural and nutritional needs of Inuit communities and on the population size and trend of bowheads. Data on harvested BCB whales have been collected since the mid-1970s. Between 1974 and 2018, hunters from 12 villages in Alaska, extending from the Bering Sea to the eastern Alaskan Beaufort Sea, harvested 1493 bowheads. Hunters at Barrow landed the most whales (n=748), while Shaktoolik only landed one and Little Diomede landed two. Some villages hunt only in the spring, some only in the autumn, while Barrow, Wainwright, and the Saint Lawrence Island villages (Gambell and Savoonga) hunt in both the spring and autumn/winter. The average size of whales differs among the villages. Gambell, Savoonga, and Wainwright harvest larger whales than do Point Hope and Barrow. It is not clear whether these differences are due to hunter selectivity, whale availability, or both. The size of landed whales changes during the migration in at least some villages. During the spring, larger whales tend to be taken more frequently near the end of the migration. The opposite is true for the autumn whereby larger whales tend to be taken early while small whales tend to be taken later. Overall, the sex ratio is equal. Generally, males and females do not appear to segregate during the spring or autumn migration. However, large females appear to comprise much of the tail end of the migration as they tend to be more common in late May in northern Chukchi Sea. The efficiency (no. of whales landed/no. of whales struck) of the hunt has increased markedly over this period. Currently, the efficiency is about 0.80. The bowhead harvests in West Greenland, Canada, Chukotka, and Alaska represent only a fraction of the estimated population size, indicating that the hunts are sustainable.","container-title":"The Bowhead Whale","ISBN":"978-0-12-818969-6","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1016/B978-0-12-818969-6.00032-7","page":"519-535","publisher":"Academic Press","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Current indigenous whaling","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780128189696000327","author":[{"family":"Suydam","given":"Robert"},{"family":"George","given":"J. C."}],"editor":[{"family":"George","given":"J. C."},{"family":"Thewissen","given":"J. G. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} is hunted primarily by Iñupiaq and Yupik whalers in Alaska under a quota of 67 whales per year established by the International Whaling Commission based on population status and also cultural need. An annual hunt is conducted by Inuit in Nunavut of about one whale per year, with the hunt rotating among communities. The bowhead is also occasionally hunted in Chukotka, Russia, and in Greenland. The beluga whale (Delphinapterus leucas) is hunted in Greenland, Canada, Alaska, and Chukotka by Inuit, Inuvialuit, Gwichin, Iñupiat, Yupik, Yup’ik, and Chukchi. Worldwide, over 1000 beluga are taken per year on average, and the hunt is regarded as sustainable in nearly all locations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2Hhhw9bv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hobbs, 2019; NAMMCO, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hobbs, 2019; NAMMCO, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7739,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WRW97IJH"],"itemData":{"id":7739,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Fisheries Review","ISSN":"0090-1830","issue":"3-4","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Fisheries Review","page":"1-62","title":"Global review of the conservation status of monodontid stocks","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"Hobbs","given":"Roderick C"},{"family":"Reeves","given":"Randall R"},{"family":"Prewitt","given":"Jill S"},{"family":"Desportes","given":"Genevieve"},{"family":"Breton-Honeyman","given":"Kaitlin"},{"family":"Christensen","given":"Tom"},{"family":"Citta","given":"John J"},{"family":"Ferguson","given":"Steven H"},{"family":"Frost","given":"Kathryn J"},{"family":"Garde","given":"Eva"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":9508,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7SYG43EU"],"itemData":{"id":9508,"type":"document","abstract":"Report of the NAMMCO global review of monodontids","publisher":"Hillerød, Denmark. Tromsø, Norway: North Atlantic Marine Mammal Commission.","title":"Report of the NAMMCO global review of monodontids","URL":"https://nammco.no/wp-content/uploads/2018/05/report-global-review-of-monodontids-nammco-2018_after-erratum-060518­_2.pdf","author":[{"family":"NAMMCO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The narwhal (Monodon monoceros) is hunted in Canada and Greenland by Inuit ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1vl4qvdbd1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. S. Lee, 2017)","plainCitation":"(D. S. Lee, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1237,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6VXXGM94"],"itemData":{"id":1237,"type":"chapter","abstract":"Lee, D.S., 2017. Inuit and narwhal. In: W.W. Fitzhugh and M.T. Nweeia, eds. Narwhal: revealing and Arctic legend. Hanover, NH: IPI Press and Smithsonian Institution. p. 105-120.","container-title":"Narwhal: revealing and Arctic legend","page":"105-120","publisher":"Hanover, NH: IPI Press and Smithsonian Institution","title":"Inuit and narwhal","author":[{"family":"Lee","given":"D.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The worldwide annual harvest is similar to that for beluga whales and is considered sustainable for most populations currently hunted ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pYfYTZ5a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hobbs, 2019; NAMMCO, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hobbs, 2019; NAMMCO, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7739,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WRW97IJH"],"itemData":{"id":7739,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Fisheries Review","ISSN":"0090-1830","issue":"3-4","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Fisheries Review","page":"1-62","title":"Global review of the conservation status of monodontid stocks","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"Hobbs","given":"Roderick C"},{"family":"Reeves","given":"Randall R"},{"family":"Prewitt","given":"Jill S"},{"family":"Desportes","given":"Genevieve"},{"family":"Breton-Honeyman","given":"Kaitlin"},{"family":"Christensen","given":"Tom"},{"family":"Citta","given":"John J"},{"family":"Ferguson","given":"Steven H"},{"family":"Frost","given":"Kathryn J"},{"family":"Garde","given":"Eva"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":9508,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7SYG43EU"],"itemData":{"id":9508,"type":"document","abstract":"Report of the NAMMCO global review of monodontids","publisher":"Hillerød, Denmark. Tromsø, Norway: North Atlantic Marine Mammal Commission.","title":"Report of the NAMMCO global review of monodontids","URL":"https://nammco.no/wp-content/uploads/2018/05/report-global-review-of-monodontids-nammco-2018_after-erratum-060518­_2.pdf","author":[{"family":"NAMMCO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Chukchi and Yupik whalers in Chukotka hunt about 125 gray whales per year (Eschrichtius robustus; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1sjkt1b6eb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IWC, 2019a)","plainCitation":"(IWC, 2019a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1207,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMDT6PMT"],"itemData":{"id":1207,"type":"webpage","container-title":"International Whaling Commission","title":"Description of the aboriginal subsistence hunt in Chukotka, Russian Federation","URL":"https://iwc.int/russian-federation","author":[{"family":"IWC","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"literal":"2019a"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}) under an Illegal Whaling Commission quota. The harvest is considered sustainable. In 1999, Makah whalers in the American state of Washington resumed a cultural tradition of gray whale hunting that had been interrupted by colonization and its disruptions, but since 2002 domestic regulations have prevented the hunt from taking place ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1596h2ee8a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IWC, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IWC, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1208,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5F4KKEXZ"],"itemData":{"id":1208,"type":"webpage","container-title":"International Whaling Commission","title":"Description of the USA aboriginal subsistence hunt: Makah Tribe","URL":"https://iwc.int/makah-tribe","author":[{"family":"IWC","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"literal":"2019b"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Greenland ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"adligbpa6v","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IWC, n.d.)","plainCitation":"(IWC, n.d.)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1230,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZRGX3B9U"],"itemData":{"id":1230,"type":"webpage","container-title":"International Whaling Commission","title":"Management and utilization of large whales in Greenland","URL":"https://iwc.int/makah-tribe","author":[{"family":"IWC","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, hunters take approximately 150 minke whales (Balaenoptera acutorostrata), 11 fin whales (Balaena physalus), and 7 humpback whales (Megaptera novaeangliae) per year. All of these Greenland large whale harvests are under an Illegal Whaling Commission quota and are considered sustainable. In addition to larger cetaceans, some dolphins and porpoises are taken in Arctic communities. Although not indigenous, Faroe Islanders in the North Atlantic hunt long-finned pilot whales (Globicephala melas) each year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ftf79v717","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Statbank, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Statbank, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1274,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YGUHALPG"],"itemData":{"id":1274,"type":"webpage","title":"Whale hunts, pilot whales, and skinn (1951-2020). Statistics Faroe Islands.","author":[{"family":"Statbank","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, a small-scale traditional harvest dating back centuries, which has averaged around 650 whales per year over the last decade.

3.3.1.4.6. Industrial demersal fisheries in coastal areas

The status of demersal fisheries in temperate countries is well documented in the RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1mig95uegk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Christopher Costello, 2016; RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database, 2018; Ricard, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Christopher Costello, 2016; RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database, 2018; Ricard, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3HP9MBRY"],"itemData":{"id":354,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Data from 4,713 fisheries worldwide, representing 78% of global reported fish catch, are analyzed to estimate the status, trends, and benefits of alternative approaches to recovering depleted fisheries. For each fishery, we estimate current biological status and forecast the impacts of contrasting management regimes on catch, profit, and biomass of fish in the sea. We estimate unique recovery targets and trajectories for each fishery, calculate the year-by-year effects of alternative recovery approaches, and model how alternative institutional reforms affect recovery outcomes. Current status is highly heterogeneous—the median fishery is in poor health (overfished, with further overfishing occurring), although 32% of fisheries are in good biological, although not necessarily economic, condition. Our business-as-usual scenario projects further divergence and continued collapse for many of the world’s fisheries. Applying sound management reforms to global fisheries in our dataset could generate annual increases exceeding 16 million metric tons (MMT) in catch, $53 billion in profit, and 619 MMT in biomass relative to business as usual. We also find that, with appropriate reforms, recovery can happen quickly, with the median fishery taking under 10 y to reach recovery targets. Our results show that commonsense reforms to fishery management would dramatically improve overall fish abundance while increasing food security and profits.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1520420113","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"5125-5129","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Global fishery prospects under contrasting management regimes","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1520420113","volume":"113","author":[{"family":"Costello","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Ovando","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Clavelle","given":"Tyler"},{"family":"Strauss","given":"C. Kent"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Melnychuk","given":"Michael C."},{"family":"Branch","given":"Trevor A."},{"family":"Gaines","given":"Steven D."},{"family":"Szuwalski","given":"Cody S."},{"family":"Cabral","given":"Reniel B."},{"family":"Rader","given":"Douglas N."},{"family":"Leland","given":"Amanda"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",5,3]]}}},{"id":30339,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6XFR3ARY"],"itemData":{"id":30339,"type":"dataset","abstract":"RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database version 4.44.","DOI":"10.5281/ZENODO.2542919","license":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International, Open Access","publisher":"Zenodo","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database v4.44","URL":"https://zenodo.org/record/2542919","author":[{"family":"RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",8,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12,22]]}}},{"id":30787,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DLTBUPQI"],"itemData":{"id":30787,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-2979.2011.00435.x","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"380-398","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Examining the knowledge base and status of commercially exploited marine species with the RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database: The RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database","title-short":"Examining the knowledge base and status of commercially exploited marine species with the RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1467-2979.2011.00435.x","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Ricard","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Minto","given":"Cóilín"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Olaf P"},{"family":"Baum","given":"Julia K"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, where approximately 53% of global reported catch is counted. Those consist of three dominant taxonomic groups, gadids (cod, haddock, pollock and hake), pleuronectids (flatfish), and sebastids (rockfish). While many of these fisheries underwent a historical phase of overexploitation, recent evidence suggests that many of these fisheries have been managed since the 1990s and 2000s in ways that reduced fishing mortality rates ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1mn3jjgt7m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Christopher Costello & Ovando, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Christopher Costello & Ovando, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1254,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H5WQPIAD"],"itemData":{"id":1254,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"We compile global data to examine the current status, trends, threats, and opportunities in the world's wild-capture fisheries. We find that global fisheries have largely diverged—well-managed, often industrial fisheries tend to be in reasonably good health, while coastal fisheries, often from low-governance regions, tend to be in poor health. Good governance seems to play a central role, and we summarize key findings from the literature on how effective fishery management can simultaneously increase food security, livelihoods, and conservation outcomes. Other solutions, such as marine protected areas and big data, can be useful but will not, by themselves, solve the main fishery challenges. We conclude by examining notorious threats, such as climate change and lack of governance on the high seas, and find that these can be largely neutralized with good fishery management, suggesting that overall, the future of wild fisheries can be bright with effective fishery management interventions.","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033310","ISSN":"1543-5938, 1545-2050","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Environ. Resour.","language":"en","page":"177-200","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Status, Institutions, and Prospects for Global Capture Fisheries","URL":"https://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033310","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Costello","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Ovando","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In many cases these measures improved stock status ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2i99ivk7j9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hilborn & Ovando, 2014; B. Worm, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Hilborn & Ovando, 2014; B. Worm, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1278,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RE96M9NN"],"itemData":{"id":1278,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The argument persists that the continued overexploitation by many fisheries around the world is evidence that current approaches to fisheries management are failing, and that more precautionary management approaches are needed. We review the available estimates of the status of fish stocks from three sources: the FAO's “State of Marine Resources”, a database on scientific stock assessments, and recent estimates from statistical models designed to determine the status of unassessed fish stocks. The two key results are (i) that stocks that are scientifically assessed are in better shape and indeed are not typically declining but rebuilding, and (ii) that large stocks appear to be in better shape than small stocks. These results support the view that stocks that are managed are improving, while stocks that are not managed are not. Large stocks receive far more management attention than small stocks in jurisdictions that have active fisheries management systems, and most unassessed stocks are simply not managed. We assert that fisheries management as currently practised can (and often does) lead to sustainable fisheries, and what is needed is to actively manage the unassessed fisheries of the world. More precautionary management is not necessarily needed to ensure the sustainability of managed fisheries.","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsu034","ISSN":"1054-3139","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","page":"1040-1046","source":"Silverchair","title":"Reflections on the success of traditional fisheries management","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1093/icesjms/fsu034","volume":"71","author":[{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Ovando","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",8,1]]}}},{"id":1252,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZVDB8VTI"],"itemData":{"id":1252,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1132294","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5800","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"787-790","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impacts of Biodiversity Loss on Ocean Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1132294","volume":"314","author":[{"family":"Worm","given":"B."},{"family":"Barbier","given":"E. B."},{"family":"Beaumont","given":"N."},{"family":"Duffy","given":"J. E."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"Halpern","given":"B. S."},{"family":"Jackson","given":"J. B. C."},{"family":"Lotze","given":"H. K."},{"family":"Micheli","given":"F."},{"family":"Palumbi","given":"S. R."},{"family":"Sala","given":"E."},{"family":"Selkoe","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Stachowicz","given":"J. J."},{"family":"Watson","given":"R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",11,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and increased biomass to the point that some authors now focus on underfishing of some key stocks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1daue86vud","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hilborn, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Hilborn, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1270,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4RIZQXAG"],"itemData":{"id":1270,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Most reporting of stock status accumulated at a national or regional level gives statistics on what proportion of the stocks are below some abundance threshold or above some fishing mortality rate threshold. This approach does not convey useful information on the performance of the fisheries management system in maximizing long-term sustainable yield, which is the primary objective of most national and international fisheries legislation. In this paper, I present a graphical approach for representing how much yield is being lost as a consequence of current suboptimal abundance and fishing pressure. Using the EU stocks assessed by ICES as an example, I show how traditional criteria for overfished and overfishing fail to display realistic information about the performance of the fishery. This approach provides much more useful information for the public and policy makers.","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsy138","ISSN":"1054-3139","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","page":"45-49","source":"Silverchair","title":"Measuring fisheries performance using the “Goldilocks plot”","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1093/icesjms/fsy138","volume":"76","author":[{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The figure below shows the trend in abundance and fishing mortality for these species in temperate areas (Europe, North America, Japan, Chile, New Zealand and Australia). Stock abundance tends to be above the level that would produce long-term maximum sustainable yield and fishing pressure is lower. This has resulted in increasing general stock abundance (Figure 3.26). 


Figure 3.26 Stock abundance trends. These figures describe the trend in ratios of B/Bmsy (Biomass relative to the biomass that produces Maximum Sustainable Yield), U/Umsy (Fishing pressure or mortality relative to the fraction of the population harvested), and catch/(mean catch) across assessed stocks in the taxonomic group. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15ovvi6776","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database, 2018)","plainCitation":"(RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30339,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6XFR3ARY"],"itemData":{"id":30339,"type":"dataset","abstract":"RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database version 4.44.","DOI":"10.5281/ZENODO.2542919","license":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International, Open Access","publisher":"Zenodo","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database v4.44","URL":"https://zenodo.org/record/2542919","author":[{"family":"RAM Legacy Stock Assessment Database","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",8,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC-BY 4.0.

	The status of demersal fisheries in the rest of the world is much less documented. A quarter of the remaining global reported catch has undergone some form of data-limited stock assessment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"anib1ofpn0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2016b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2016b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1251,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QC88ITIT"],"itemData":{"id":1251,"type":"book","abstract":"FAO (2016). The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2016. Contributing\\nto food security and nutrition for all. Rome.","publisher":"FAO, Rome.","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2016. Contributing to food security and nutrition for all","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} while 22% remains unassessed, with little information about population status or risk of over-fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cd5MQXNJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Christopher Costello & Ovando, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Christopher Costello & Ovando, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1254,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H5WQPIAD"],"itemData":{"id":1254,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"We compile global data to examine the current status, trends, threats, and opportunities in the world's wild-capture fisheries. We find that global fisheries have largely diverged—well-managed, often industrial fisheries tend to be in reasonably good health, while coastal fisheries, often from low-governance regions, tend to be in poor health. Good governance seems to play a central role, and we summarize key findings from the literature on how effective fishery management can simultaneously increase food security, livelihoods, and conservation outcomes. Other solutions, such as marine protected areas and big data, can be useful but will not, by themselves, solve the main fishery challenges. We conclude by examining notorious threats, such as climate change and lack of governance on the high seas, and find that these can be largely neutralized with good fishery management, suggesting that overall, the future of wild fisheries can be bright with effective fishery management interventions.","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033310","ISSN":"1543-5938, 1545-2050","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Environ. Resour.","language":"en","page":"177-200","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Status, Institutions, and Prospects for Global Capture Fisheries","URL":"https://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033310","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Costello","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Ovando","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These data limited stocks make up an increasing proportion of globally reported catch over time, from 20% to 47% in the last 60 years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ice8opio3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vasconcellos & Cochrane, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Vasconcellos & Cochrane, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1250,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GYJ7UGE5"],"itemData":{"id":1250,"type":"chapter","abstract":"Vasconcellos, M., & Cochrane, K. (2005). Overview of world status of da-\\nta-limited fisheries: Inferences from landings statistics. In V. F. Kruse\\nGallucci, (Eds.),  (pp. 1–20). Anchorage, AK: LowellWakefield Symposium, 21.","container-title":"Fisheries assessment and management in data-limited situations","edition":"Anchorage, AK: LowellWakefield Symposium, 21.","page":"1–20","publisher":"V. F. Kruse Gallucci, (Eds.)","title":"Overview of world status of data-limited fisheries: Inferences from landings statistics.","author":[{"family":"Vasconcellos","given":"M"},{"family":"Cochrane","given":"K"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. From two areas for which information is available, the Mediterranean and Western Africa, the evidence is that these stocks are very heavily exploited and almost certainly over-fished and subject to over-fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1hgrrdobk1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hilborn, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Hilborn, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34306,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C29NHAMV"],"itemData":{"id":34306,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Marine fish stocks are an important part of the world food system and are particularly important for many of the poorest people of the world. Most existing analyses suggest overfishing is increasing, and there is widespread concern that fish stocks are decreasing throughout most of the world. We assembled trends in abundance and harvest rate of stocks that are scientifically assessed, constituting half of the reported global marine fish catch. For these stocks, on average, abundance is increasing and is at proposed target levels. Compared with regions that are intensively managed, regions with less-developed fisheries management have, on average, 3-fold greater harvest rates and half the abundance as assessed stocks. Available evidence suggests that the regions without assessments of abundance have little fisheries management, and stocks are in poor shape. Increased application of area-appropriate fisheries science recommendations and management tools are still needed for sustaining fisheries in places where they are lacking.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1909726116","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"2218-2224","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effective fisheries management instrumental in improving fish stock status","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1909726116","volume":"117","author":[{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo Oscar"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Christopher M."},{"family":"Baum","given":"Julia K."},{"family":"Branch","given":"Trevor A."},{"family":"Costello","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Moor","given":"Carryn L.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Faraj","given":"Abdelmalek"},{"family":"Hively","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Olaf P."},{"family":"Kurota","given":"Hiroyuki"},{"family":"Little","given":"L. Richard"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"McClanahan","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Melnychuk","given":"Michael C."},{"family":"Minto","given":"Cóilín"},{"family":"Osio","given":"Giacomo Chato"},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Pons","given":"Maite"},{"family":"Segurado","given":"Susana"},{"family":"Szuwalski","given":"Cody S."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"Jono R."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Yimin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1,28]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The demersal species from the regions not well covered in the RAM Legacy Stock Assessment database, along with the small pelagic fisheries of the same regions, constitute the dominant component of the unassessed fish stocks of the world.
      Most of those demersal stocks belong to coastal fisheries which contribute much, if not most, of global catches, but quantitative estimates of the extent of their contribution depend on how coastal fisheries are defined, especially in relation to small scale fisheries. Palomares and Pauly ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a27sadplvm0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Palomares & Pauly, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Palomares & Pauly, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33973,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EFGHQL7E"],"itemData":{"id":33973,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Palomares, M. L. D., and D. Pauly. 2019. On the creeping increase of vessels’ fishing power. Ecology and Society 24(3):31. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-11136-240331","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-11136-240331","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","language":"en","license":"© 2019 by the author(s)","note":"publisher: The Resilience Alliance","source":"www.ecologyandsociety.org","title":"On the creeping increase of vessels’ fishing power","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol24/iss3/art31/","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Palomares","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",9,16]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} used the “Sea Around Us” reconstructed catch database ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1chslt2pqv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Zeller, 2016)","plainCitation":"(D. Zeller, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1253,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3BBNLID"],"itemData":{"id":1253,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2016.04.046","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"145-152","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Still catching attention: Sea Around Us reconstructed global catch data, their spatial expression and public accessibility","title-short":"Still catching attention","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X16302421","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Zeller","given":"D."},{"family":"Palomares","given":"M.L.D."},{"family":"Tavakolie","given":"A."},{"family":"Ang","given":"M."},{"family":"Belhabib","given":"D."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W.L."},{"family":"Lam","given":"V.W.Y."},{"family":"Sy","given":"E."},{"family":"Tsui","given":"G."},{"family":"Zylich","given":"K."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} to estimate the catch in an area at most 50 km from inhabited coastlines or down to a depth of 200 m (Figure 3.27), considered to be the area in which small scale fisheries (artisanal, subsistence, and recreational) are located. Coastal fisheries made up an average of 55% of global marine fisheries in the 5-year period from 2010 to 2014, while small-scale fisheries in the same period contributed 36% of the marine catches consumed directly by people (Figure 3.28). 



Figure 3.27 Catches from 1950-2014 of (A) the world’s marine fisheries (B) coastal fisheries (areas <=50 km from inhabited coastlines or to a depth of <=200 m). Based on ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a5qdlnq1ib","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Palomares & Pauly, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Palomares & Pauly, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33973,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EFGHQL7E"],"itemData":{"id":33973,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Palomares, M. L. D., and D. Pauly. 2019. On the creeping increase of vessels’ fishing power. Ecology and Society 24(3):31. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-11136-240331","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-11136-240331","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","language":"en","license":"© 2019 by the author(s)","note":"publisher: The Resilience Alliance","source":"www.ecologyandsociety.org","title":"On the creeping increase of vessels’ fishing power","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol24/iss3/art31/","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Palomares","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",9,16]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license number 5278770226780. CC-BY NC.

Figure 3.28 Global catch data as reported to the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations by fishing countries (reported catch: black line) (1950-2016). Source:ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2j2s0g599c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dirk Zeller, Cashion, Palomares, & Pauly, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Dirk Zeller, Cashion, Palomares, & Pauly, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1267,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/94JGEEFF"],"itemData":{"id":1267,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As part of the global marine fisheries catch reconstruction project conducted by the Sea Around Us over the last decade, estimates were derived for discards in all major fisheries in the world. The reconstruction process derives conservative but non-­zero time-­series estimates for every fisheries component known to exist, and relies on a wide variety of data and information sources and on conservative assumptions to ensure comprehensive and complete time-­series coverage. Globally, estimated discards increased from under 5 million t/year (t = 1,000 kg) in the early 1950s to a peak of 18.8 million t in 1989, and gradually declined thereafter to levels of the late 1950s of less than 10 million t/year. Thus, estimated discards represented between 10% and 20% of total reconstructed catches (reported landings + unreported landings + unreported discards) per year up to the year 2000, after which estimated discards accounted for slightly less than 10% of total annual catches. Most discards were generated by industrial (i.e. large-­scale) fisheries. Discarding occurred predominantly in northern Atlantic waters in the earlier decades (1950s–1980s), after which discarding off the West Coast of Africa dominated. More recently, fleets operating in Northwest Pacific and Western Central Pacific waters generated the most discards. In most areas, discards consist essentially of marketable taxa, suggesting a combination of poor fishing practices and poor management procedures is largely responsible for the waste discarding represents. This is important in an era of increasing food security and human nutritional health concerns, especially in developing countries.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12233","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"30-39","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Global marine fisheries discards: A synthesis of reconstructed data","title-short":"Global marine fisheries discards","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/faf.12233","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"},{"family":"Cashion","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Palomares","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC-BY NC.
	
	Lower-income countries lack the capacity to industrially harvest fish populations off their shores, and thus frequently host foreign fishing fleets through fishing access agreements or joint venture operations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1562h3ldgl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Belhabib, 2015; Kaczynski & Fluharty, 2002)","plainCitation":"(D. Belhabib, 2015; Kaczynski & Fluharty, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30788,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LFP3LD9Y"],"itemData":{"id":30788,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2015.04.010","page":"72-81","title":"Feeding the poor: Contribution of West African fisheries toemployment and food security","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84927739945&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2015.04.010&partnerID=40&md5=9138f3370f5844c2eea61c4f0cfff9b7","volume":"111","author":[{"family":"Belhabib","given":"D."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U.R."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":1280,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S5DHRFL2"],"itemData":{"id":1280,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fishery cooperation agreements with the Sub-Saharan West African coastal states are considered by the European Union as purely commercial deals that are designed to maximize access to coastal state fishery resources, secure employment for European harvesting and processing industries and supply European seafood consumption markets at the lowest possible cost. Financial compensation paid by Brussels to the West African countries for fishing rights covers two-thirds or more of the license fees and is a subsidy for European vessel owners. This subsidy puts EU in position of a preferred user of the coastal resources. That displaces foreign investors and local entrepreneurs in the coastal states, distorts economics of the European fishing enterprises and promotes excessive pressure on the resources that greatly harms the marine environment in the West African region. Analysis of EU's relations with Guinea-Bissau shows that together with manipulation of the size of fishing fleet used by EU in this country's waters, there were significant irregularities resulting from excessive by-catch, underpayment of tuna license fees and denial of timely statistical information for the coastal state. Continuation of this type of relations with Sub-Saharan West Africa is against the long-term interests of the coastal states and sustainability of the coastal resources. Unless significant changes in fishing policies of the EU are made, West African coastal countries will face severe overexploitation of their resources and subsequent drop in license revenues. The EU's departure from purely business approach in fisheries relations with the West African coastal countries and termination of subsidization of the European fleets should be considered as important steps toward new fisheries relations with the region On other hand, coastal states should undertake more coordinated approach in dealing with foreign pressures on their resources and harmonize negotiation of the fisheries agreements with the EU. They also must improve the investment climate so foreign fleet operators would be encouraged to integrate their offshore activity with the coastal states’ economies.","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/S0308-597X(01)00039-2","ISSN":"0308-597X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"75-93","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"European policies in West Africa: who benefits from fisheries agreements?","title-short":"European policies in West Africa","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308597X01000392","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Kaczynski","given":"Vlad M."},{"family":"Fluharty","given":"David L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The higher capacity and improved technology of higher-income nations has enabled these countries to build and operate distant water fishing fleets, and often to subsidize those fleets heavily ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1be0tdped9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sala, Aburto-Oropeza, Reza, Paredes, & L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez-Lemus, 2004; Dirk Zeller & Pauly, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Sala, Aburto-Oropeza, Reza, Paredes, & López-Lemus, 2004; Dirk Zeller & Pauly, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1206,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2SHU3476"],"itemData":{"id":1206,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries","DOI":"10.1577/1548-8446(2004)29[19:FDCFWI]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0363-2415, 1548-8446","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries","language":"en","page":"19-25","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishing Down Coastal Food Webs in the Gulf of California","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1577/1548-8446(2004)29[19:FDCFWI]2.0.CO;2","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Sala","given":"Enric"},{"family":"Aburto-Oropeza","given":"Octavio"},{"family":"Reza","given":"Miriam"},{"family":"Paredes","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"López-Lemus","given":"Luis G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",3]]}}},{"id":33467,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DX8JIP2N"],"itemData":{"id":33467,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            We present a view on global marine fisheries that emphasizes mitigating the conflict between sustainability and the scale of industrial exploitation driven by the demand of continuous economic growth. We then summarize the current state of global fisheries. Finally, we advocate strongly for scaling back industrial fisheries, most of which are non-sustainable. This can be achieved through eliminating the harmful, capacity-enhancing subsidies that prop up industrial fisheries to continue operating despite declining fish stocks. Instead, we propose to support well-managed, locally owned and operated small-scale fisheries, which generally contribute more to local employment and food security. We stress that contrary to deep-seated opinion in the fishing industry and among politicians, reducing overfishing by eliminating overcapacity in fishing fleets will actually lead to greater, not reduced catches. This would address part of the increased global seafood demand over the coming decades, which is driven by population and wealth growth. This seems counterintuitive, but is supported by fisheries science, data and experiences. Thankfully, we are beginning to see that some of these changes are being pursued by a growing number of countries and international institutions.","container-title":"Global Sustainability","DOI":"10.1017/sus.2019.8","ISSN":"2059-4798","journalAbbreviation":"Glob. Sustain.","language":"en","page":"e11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Viewpoint: Back to the future for fisheries, where will we choose to go?","title-short":"Viewpoint","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S2059479819000085/type/journal_article","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Describing fishing patterns of those industrial fleets in comprehensive and quantitative terms is challenging due to the lack of open access to detailed records on the behavior of fishing vessels. However, McCauley, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1g18hft78e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McCauley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(McCauley, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1279,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PYZ9MFR7"],"itemData":{"id":1279,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.aau2161","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Sci. Adv.","language":"en","page":"eaau2161","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wealthy countries dominate industrial fishing","URL":"https://advances.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/sciadv.aau2161","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"McCauley","given":"Douglas J."},{"family":"Jablonicky","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Joyce","given":"Francis H."},{"family":"Mayorga","given":"Juan"},{"family":"Kroodsma","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} produced fishing patterns of industrial fishing vessels (>24m) based on high-resolution fishing vessel activity information derived from automatic identification systems data (Figures 3.29 and 3.30). Such patterns address one of the fundamental issues of fisheries sustainability, namely direct and collateral impacts by fishing gear on habitats, target and non-target species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HWV03LUy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. O. Amoroso, 2018, 2018; Lewison, 2004; Palomares & Pauly, 2019)","plainCitation":"(R. O. Amoroso, 2018, 2018; Lewison, 2004; Palomares & Pauly, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":162,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/48CUWG7D"],"itemData":{"id":162,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Bottom trawlers land around 19 million tons of fish and invertebrates annually, almost one-quarter of wild marine landings. The extent of bottom trawling footprint (seabed area trawled at least once in a specified region and time period) is often contested but poorly described. We quantify footprints using high-resolution satellite vessel monitoring system (VMS) and logbook data on 24 continental shelves and slopes to 1,000-m depth over at least 2 years. Trawling footprint varied markedly among regions: from <10% of seabed area in Australian and New Zealand waters, the Aleutian Islands, East Bering Sea, South Chile, and Gulf of Alaska to >50% in some European seas. Overall, 14% of the 7.8 million-km2 study area was trawled, and 86% was not trawled. Trawling activity was aggregated; the most intensively trawled areas accounting for 90% of activity comprised 77% of footprint on average. Regional swept area ratio (SAR; ratio of total swept area trawled annually to total area of region, a metric of trawling intensity) and footprint area were related, providing an approach to estimate regional trawling footprints when high-resolution spatial data are unavailable. If SAR was ?0.1, as in 8 of 24 regions, there was >95% probability that >90% of seabed was not trawled. If SAR was 7.9, equal to the highest SAR recorded, there was >95% probability that >70% of seabed was trawled. Footprints were smaller and SAR was ?0.25 in regions where fishing rates consistently met international sustainability benchmarks for fish stocks, implying collateral environmental benefits from sustainable fishing.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1802379115","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"43","journalAbbreviation":"PNAS","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2018 the Author(s). Published by PNAS.. https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/This open access article is distributed under Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives License 4.0 (CC BY-NC-ND).","note":"PMID: 30297399","page":"E10275-E10282","source":"www.pnas.org","title":"Bottom trawl fishing footprints on the world’s continental shelves","URL":"https://www.pnas.org/content/115/43/E10275","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo O."},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"C. Roland"},{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"Adriaan D."},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"Robert A."},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Suuronen","given":"Petri"},{"family":"Eigaard","given":"Ole R."},{"family":"Bastardie","given":"Francois"},{"family":"Hintzen","given":"Niels T."},{"family":"Althaus","given":"Franziska"},{"family":"Baird","given":"Susan Jane"},{"family":"Black","given":"Jenny"},{"family":"Buhl-Mortensen","given":"Lene"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"Alexander B."},{"family":"Catarino","given":"Rui"},{"family":"Collie","given":"Jeremy"},{"family":"Cowan","given":"James H."},{"family":"Durholtz","given":"Deon"},{"family":"Engstrom","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Fairweather","given":"Tracey P."},{"family":"Fock","given":"Heino O."},{"family":"Ford","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Gálvez","given":"Patricio A."},{"family":"Gerritsen","given":"Hans"},{"family":"Góngora","given":"María Eva"},{"family":"González","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Hiddink","given":"Jan G."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Kathryn M."},{"family":"Intelmann","given":"Steven S."},{"family":"Jenkins","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Jonsson","given":"Patrik"},{"family":"Kainge","given":"Paulus"},{"family":"Kangas","given":"Mervi"},{"family":"Kathena","given":"Johannes N."},{"family":"Kavadas","given":"Stefanos"},{"family":"Leslie","given":"Rob W."},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Steve G."},{"family":"Lundy","given":"Mathieu"},{"family":"Makin","given":"David"},{"family":"Martin","given":"Julie"},{"family":"Mazor","given":"Tessa"},{"family":"Gonzalez-Mirelis","given":"Genoveva"},{"family":"Newman","given":"Stephen J."},{"family":"Papadopoulou","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Posen","given":"Paulette E."},{"family":"Rochester","given":"Wayne"},{"family":"Russo","given":"Tommaso"},{"family":"Sala","given":"Antonello"},{"family":"Semmens","given":"Jayson M."},{"family":"Silva","given":"Cristina"},{"family":"Tsolos","given":"Angelo"},{"family":"Vanelslander","given":"Bart"},{"family":"Wakefield","given":"Corey B."},{"family":"Wood","given":"Brent A."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Kaiser","given":"Michel J."},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10,23]]}}},{"id":162,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/48CUWG7D"],"itemData":{"id":162,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Bottom trawlers land around 19 million tons of fish and invertebrates annually, almost one-quarter of wild marine landings. The extent of bottom trawling footprint (seabed area trawled at least once in a specified region and time period) is often contested but poorly described. We quantify footprints using high-resolution satellite vessel monitoring system (VMS) and logbook data on 24 continental shelves and slopes to 1,000-m depth over at least 2 years. Trawling footprint varied markedly among regions: from <10% of seabed area in Australian and New Zealand waters, the Aleutian Islands, East Bering Sea, South Chile, and Gulf of Alaska to >50% in some European seas. Overall, 14% of the 7.8 million-km2 study area was trawled, and 86% was not trawled. Trawling activity was aggregated; the most intensively trawled areas accounting for 90% of activity comprised 77% of footprint on average. Regional swept area ratio (SAR; ratio of total swept area trawled annually to total area of region, a metric of trawling intensity) and footprint area were related, providing an approach to estimate regional trawling footprints when high-resolution spatial data are unavailable. If SAR was ?0.1, as in 8 of 24 regions, there was >95% probability that >90% of seabed was not trawled. If SAR was 7.9, equal to the highest SAR recorded, there was >95% probability that >70% of seabed was trawled. Footprints were smaller and SAR was ?0.25 in regions where fishing rates consistently met international sustainability benchmarks for fish stocks, implying collateral environmental benefits from sustainable fishing.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1802379115","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"43","journalAbbreviation":"PNAS","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2018 the Author(s). Published by PNAS.. https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/This open access article is distributed under Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives License 4.0 (CC BY-NC-ND).","note":"PMID: 30297399","page":"E10275-E10282","source":"www.pnas.org","title":"Bottom trawl fishing footprints on the world’s continental shelves","URL":"https://www.pnas.org/content/115/43/E10275","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo O."},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"C. Roland"},{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"Adriaan D."},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"Robert A."},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Suuronen","given":"Petri"},{"family":"Eigaard","given":"Ole R."},{"family":"Bastardie","given":"Francois"},{"family":"Hintzen","given":"Niels T."},{"family":"Althaus","given":"Franziska"},{"family":"Baird","given":"Susan Jane"},{"family":"Black","given":"Jenny"},{"family":"Buhl-Mortensen","given":"Lene"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"Alexander B."},{"family":"Catarino","given":"Rui"},{"family":"Collie","given":"Jeremy"},{"family":"Cowan","given":"James H."},{"family":"Durholtz","given":"Deon"},{"family":"Engstrom","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Fairweather","given":"Tracey P."},{"family":"Fock","given":"Heino O."},{"family":"Ford","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Gálvez","given":"Patricio A."},{"family":"Gerritsen","given":"Hans"},{"family":"Góngora","given":"María Eva"},{"family":"González","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Hiddink","given":"Jan G."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Kathryn M."},{"family":"Intelmann","given":"Steven S."},{"family":"Jenkins","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Jonsson","given":"Patrik"},{"family":"Kainge","given":"Paulus"},{"family":"Kangas","given":"Mervi"},{"family":"Kathena","given":"Johannes N."},{"family":"Kavadas","given":"Stefanos"},{"family":"Leslie","given":"Rob W."},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Steve G."},{"family":"Lundy","given":"Mathieu"},{"family":"Makin","given":"David"},{"family":"Martin","given":"Julie"},{"family":"Mazor","given":"Tessa"},{"family":"Gonzalez-Mirelis","given":"Genoveva"},{"family":"Newman","given":"Stephen J."},{"family":"Papadopoulou","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Posen","given":"Paulette E."},{"family":"Rochester","given":"Wayne"},{"family":"Russo","given":"Tommaso"},{"family":"Sala","given":"Antonello"},{"family":"Semmens","given":"Jayson M."},{"family":"Silva","given":"Cristina"},{"family":"Tsolos","given":"Angelo"},{"family":"Vanelslander","given":"Bart"},{"family":"Wakefield","given":"Corey B."},{"family":"Wood","given":"Brent A."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Kaiser","given":"Michel J."},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10,23]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31588,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GRQJITCS"],"itemData":{"id":31588,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2004.09.004","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"598-604","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Understanding impacts of fisheries bycatch on marine megafauna","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534704002642","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Lewison","given":"R"},{"family":"Crowder","given":"L"},{"family":"Read","given":"A"},{"family":"Freeman","given":"S"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",11]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":33973,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EFGHQL7E"],"itemData":{"id":33973,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Palomares, M. L. D., and D. Pauly. 2019. On the creeping increase of vessels’ fishing power. Ecology and Society 24(3):31. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-11136-240331","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-11136-240331","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","language":"en","license":"© 2019 by the author(s)","note":"publisher: The Resilience Alliance","source":"www.ecologyandsociety.org","title":"On the creeping increase of vessels’ fishing power","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol24/iss3/art31/","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Palomares","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",9,16]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, directly related to the amount of gear deployed rather than to the amount of target yield extracted coming from catch data ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a16iehv1om1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. R. Stewart, 2010)","plainCitation":"(K. R. Stewart, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1204,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E2FZDGUP"],"itemData":{"id":1204,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0014451","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e14451","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Characterizing Fishing Effort and Spatial Extent of Coastal Fisheries","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0014451","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Stewart","given":"Kelly R."},{"family":"Lewison","given":"Rebecca L."},{"family":"Dunn","given":"Daniel C."},{"family":"Bjorkland","given":"Rhema H."},{"family":"Kelez","given":"Shaleyla"},{"family":"Halpin","given":"Patrick N."},{"family":"Crowder","given":"Larry B."}],"editor":[{"family":"Unsworth","given":"Richard Kazimierz Frank"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",12,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 


Figure 3.29 Distribution of industrial fishing effort by vessels flagged to nations from different income classes as measured using automatic identification systems data and convolutional neural network models. (A) The percent of fishing effort (measured in fishing hours) detected globally on the high seas and in all exclusive economic zones for vessels flagged to nations from four different World Bank income groups. (B) The percent of automatic identification systems-detected industrial fishing effort in all exclusive economic zones, grouped by the World Bank income groups of the exclusive economic zones. Here, the category Domestic fishing is included, which refers to instances when a fishing country was fishing in its own exclusive economic zone. Other categories represent foreign fishing effort conducted within an exclusive economic zone by a nation flagged to one of the four World Bank income classes. “Invalid identity” refers to vessels with a Maritime Mobile Service Identity (MMSI) number that did not accurately refer to an individual country. “Unclassified” refers to fishing entities that were fishing in an exclusive economic zone but did not have a World Bank income group. All data presented here are summarized from the year 2016. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a11qmj2gd4t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McCauley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(McCauley, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1279,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PYZ9MFR7"],"itemData":{"id":1279,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.aau2161","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Sci. Adv.","language":"en","page":"eaau2161","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wealthy countries dominate industrial fishing","URL":"https://advances.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/sciadv.aau2161","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"McCauley","given":"Douglas J."},{"family":"Jablonicky","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Joyce","given":"Francis H."},{"family":"Mayorga","given":"Juan"},{"family":"Kroodsma","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY 4.0.


Figure 3.30 Density distribution of global industrial fishing effort, derived using automatic identification systems data. (A) Vessels flagged to higher-income countries and (B) vessels flagged to lower-­income countries. Industrial fishing effort is estimated using convolutional neural network models and plotted as the log10 number of fishing hours. This map is directly copied from its original source ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5IXSxuPB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McCauley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(McCauley, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1279,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PYZ9MFR7"],"itemData":{"id":1279,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.aau2161","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Sci. Adv.","language":"en","page":"eaau2161","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wealthy countries dominate industrial fishing","URL":"https://advances.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/sciadv.aau2161","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"McCauley","given":"Douglas J."},{"family":"Jablonicky","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Joyce","given":"Francis H."},{"family":"Mayorga","given":"Juan"},{"family":"Kroodsma","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and was not modified by the assessment authors. The map is copyrighted under license CC BY 4.0. The designations employed and the presentation of material on the maps used in the assessment do not imply the expression of any opinion whatsoever on the part of IPBES concerning the legal status of any country, territory, city or area or of its authorities, or concerning the delimitation of its frontiers or boundaries. These maps have been prepared or used for the sole purpose of facilitating the assessment of the broad biogeographical areas represented therein and for purposes of representing scientific data spatially.
      
      The density distribution of global industrial fishing effort reveals global dominance of industrial fishing by wealthy nations (www.worldbank.org; using 2016 classifications). Vessels flagged to higher-income nations are responsible for 97% of the trackable industrial fishing on the high seas and 78% of such effort within the national waters of lower-income countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JVOCgh04","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McCauley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(McCauley, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1279,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PYZ9MFR7"],"itemData":{"id":1279,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.aau2161","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Sci. Adv.","language":"en","page":"eaau2161","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wealthy countries dominate industrial fishing","URL":"https://advances.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/sciadv.aau2161","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"McCauley","given":"Douglas J."},{"family":"Jablonicky","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Joyce","given":"Francis H."},{"family":"Mayorga","given":"Juan"},{"family":"Kroodsma","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      While legal, these arrangements raise many challenges regarding their sustainability and equity. For instance, the expected benefits of these partnerships, such as revenues and investments in local infrastructure and technologies, have not always materialized  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1p9ei3g0nt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Antonova, 2016; Crona, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Antonova, 2016; Crona, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1268,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U3YGXXBY"],"itemData":{"id":1268,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Over the past two decades, European Union rhetoric has communicated a desire to take on a normative power role in advancing human rights and sustainable development approaches in the context of global fisheries policy. Officials have propagated an image of a “new Europe,” committed to promoting good maritime governance and ensuring responsible fishing worldwide as part of its global responsibility to human rights and sustainable development. These normative principles have at times been framed as an integral part of the European Union's legal and political identity. In practice, however, the European Union's bilateral fishing agreements with developing states have come short of European Union aspirations, facing criticism for hindering rather than aiding local development. This paper explores the bilateral agreements from an international law perspective, engaging in grounded theory, discourse analysis, and a detailed case study on European Union-Senegal fishing relations. For the European Union, the article raises questions about conflicts between national and supranational fishing goals and about the challenges these conflicts present to its goal of normative leadership. More generally, the study suggests implications for enacting international law principles on the ground, as well as for the inherent power dynamics of post-colonial relations.","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2016.04.008","ISSN":"0308-597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"77-84","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The rhetoric of “responsible fishing”: Notions of human rights and sustainability in the European Union's bilateral fishing agreements with developing states","title-short":"The rhetoric of “responsible fishing”","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308597X16301749","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Antonova","given":"Anna S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",8,1]]}}},{"id":1269,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GLDRX6CK"],"itemData":{"id":1269,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fish and fish-related products are among the most highly traded commodities globally and the proportion of globally harvested fish that is internationally traded has steadily risen over time. Views on the benefits of international seafood trade diverge, partly as a result from adopting either an aggregate national focus or a focus on local market actors. However, both views generally assume that the trade in question is characterized by export of fisheries resources to international markets. This is potentially misleading as empirical evidence suggests that import of seafood can also have impacts on local SSF dynamics. A systematic analysis of the different ways in which local production systems connect to international seafood markets can therefore help shed more light on why small-scale fisheries exhibit such differences in outcomes as they engage in an increasingly global seafood trade. This paper conducts a synthesis across 24 cases from around the world and develops a typology of small-scale fisheries and how they connect to and interact with international seafood trade. The analysis is based on key features drawn from trade theory regarding how trade interacts with local production. The implications of the findings for social and ecological sustainability of small-scale fisheries are discussed with the aim of identifying further research topics which deserve attention to better inform trade policy for more sustainable fisheries and more just wealth distribution from their trade.","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2015.11.016","ISSN":"0308-597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"1-10","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Towards a typology of interactions between small-scale fisheries and global seafood trade","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308597X15003607","volume":"65","author":[{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice I."},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Squires","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Daw","given":"Tim M."},{"family":"Khan","given":"Ahmed"},{"family":"Havice","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Chomo","given":"Victoria"},{"family":"Troell","given":"Max"},{"family":"Buchary","given":"Eny A."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Distant water fleets are also involved in illegal, unreported and unregulated fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1c4k3cg08b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pauly, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Pauly, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1203,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BGFR79WP"],"itemData":{"id":1203,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12032","ISSN":"1467-2960, 1467-2979","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"474-488","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"China's distant?water fisheries in the 21st century","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/faf.12032","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Belhabib","given":"Dyhia"},{"family":"Blomeyer","given":"Roland"},{"family":"Cheung","given":"William W W L"},{"family":"Cisneros?Montemayor","given":"Andrés M"},{"family":"Copeland","given":"Duncan"},{"family":"Harper","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Lam","given":"Vicky W Y"},{"family":"Mai","given":"Yining"},{"family":"Manach","given":"Frédéric"},{"family":"Österblom","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Mok","given":"Ka Man"},{"family":"Meer","given":"Liesbeth"},{"family":"Sanz","given":"Antonio"},{"family":"Shon","given":"Soohyun"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U Rashid"},{"family":"Swartz","given":"Wilf"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Reg"},{"family":"Zhai","given":"Yunlei"},{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which are considered as a serious threat to fisheries and fisheries-dependent communities, marine ecosystems and societies at large ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a144ts29tj0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hutniczak, Delpeuch, & Leroy, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Hutniczak, Delpeuch, & Leroy, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1255,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PCQKFD9Y"],"itemData":{"id":1255,"type":"report","abstract":"Barbara Hutniczak, Claire Delpeuch,\\nAntonia Leroy","genre":"OECD Food, Agriculture and Fisheries Papers","language":"en","note":"collection-title: OECD Food, Agriculture and Fisheries Papers\\nvolume: 120\\nDOI: 10.1787/9b86ba08-en","number":"120","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Closing Gaps in National Regulations Against IUU Fishing","URL":"https://www.oecd-ilibrary.org/agriculture-and-food/closing-gaps-in-national-regulations-against-iuu-fishing_9b86ba08-en","author":[{"family":"Hutniczak","given":"Barbara"},{"family":"Delpeuch","given":"Claire"},{"family":"Leroy","given":"Antonia"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",2,14]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Agnew ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1qJX74wT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2009)","plainCitation":"(2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34952,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/74MHS39C","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IKRUYGRE"],"itemData":{"id":34952,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0004570","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e4570","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Estimating the Worldwide Extent of Illegal Fishing","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0004570","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Agnew","given":"David J."},{"family":"Pearce","given":"John"},{"family":"Pramod","given":"Ganapathiraju"},{"family":"Peatman","given":"Tom"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Reg"},{"family":"Beddington","given":"John R."},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"Tony J."}],"editor":[{"family":"Sandin","given":"Stuart A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",2,25]]}},"label":"page","suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} estimated that 11–26 million tons (from exclusive economic zones and high seas), or roughly one-quarter of the world catch of fish goes to illegal, unreported and unregulated fishing every year. The same authors found a correspondence between their regional estimates of illegal and unreported fishing and the number of depleted stocks in those regions. As exemplified in the case study in Box 3.5, the relationship between industrial fisheries, small scale fisheries, population status, food security, and livelihoods is a complex one indeed.

Box 3.5 Bottom trawling: assessing seabed habitat and biota impacts

The recognition that sustainability of fisheries not only involves maintaining target stocks at productive levels, but also minimizing wider ecosystem impacts of fishing has turned increasing attention to the evaluation of the environmental footprint of different fishing methods. In particular, the use of bottom-contact mobile gears as a means of catching fish has sparked heated debates in fishery and conservation sciences. On the one hand, bottom trawling contributes close to 20 million tons of fish and invertebrates per year to the global food supply and provides food and livelihoods for millions of people as well as significant export revenues to many countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1nebkm326s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Amoroso, 2018)","plainCitation":"(R. Amoroso, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":159,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8W6XZ62C"],"itemData":{"id":159,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"6404","page":"eaat6713","title":"Comment on “Tracking the global footprint of fisheries”","volume":"361","author":[{"family":"Amoroso","given":"RO"},{"family":"Parma","given":"AM"},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"CR"},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"RA"},{"family":"Jennings","given":"S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. On the other hand, bottom trawling impacts seabed habitats, damaging biogenic structures and altering sediment composition and its biogeochemical dynamics, kills benthic organisms and alters ecosystem functions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1vq986i3no","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. R. Clark, 2016; De Borger, Tiano, Braeckman, Rijnsdorp, & Soetaert, 2021; Hiddink, 2017; O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Neill & Ivanovi\\\\uc0\\\\u263{}, 2016; Pusceddu, 2014)","plainCitation":"(M. R. Clark, 2016; De Borger, Tiano, Braeckman, Rijnsdorp, & Soetaert, 2021; Hiddink, 2017; O’Neill & Ivanovi?, 2016; Pusceddu, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30803,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TZSGLWF7"],"itemData":{"id":30803,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","issue":"suppl_1","note":"publisher: Oxford University Press","page":"i51–i69","title":"The impacts of deep-sea fisheries on benthic communities: a review","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Clark","given":"Malcolm R"},{"family":"Althaus","given":"Franziska"},{"family":"Schlacher","given":"Thomas A"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Alan"},{"family":"Bowden","given":"David A"},{"family":"Rowden","given":"Ashley A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":30802,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MSRJ2YXS"],"itemData":{"id":30802,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract. Bottom trawling in shelf seas can occur more than 10 times per year for a\\ngiven location. This affects the benthic metabolism, through a mortality of\\nthe macrofauna, resuspension of organic matter from the sediment, and\\nalterations of the physical sediment structure. However, the trawling\\nimpacts on organic carbon mineralization and associated processes are not\\nwell known. Using a modelling approach, the effects of increasing trawling\\nfrequencies on early diagenesis were studied in five different sedimentary\\nenvironments, simulating the effects of a deeper-penetrating gear (e.g. a\\ntickler chain beam trawl) versus a shallower, more variable penetrating gear\\n(e.g. an electric pulse trawl). Trawling events strongly increased oxygen\\nand nitrate concentrations in surface sediment layers and led to\\nsignificantly lower amounts of ammonium (43?%–99?% reduction) and\\norganic carbon in the top 10?cm of the sediment (62?%–96?% reduction).\\nAs a result, total mineralization rates in the sediment were decreased by up\\nto 28?%. The effect on different mineralization processes differed both\\nbetween sediment types and between trawling frequencies. The shallow-penetrating gear had a slightly smaller effect on benthic denitrification\\nthan the deeper-penetrating gear, but there were no statistically different\\nresults between gear types for all other parameters. Denitrification was\\nreduced by 69?% in a fine sandy sediment, whereas nitrogen removal nearly\\ndoubled in a highly eutrophic mud. This suggests that even relatively low\\npenetration depths from bottom fishing gears generate significant\\nbiogeochemical alterations. Physical organic carbon removal through\\ntrawl-induced resuspension of sediments, exacerbated by a removal of\\nbioturbating macrofauna, was identified as the main cause of the changes in\\nthe mineralization process.","container-title":"Biogeosciences","DOI":"10.5194/bg-18-2539-2021","ISSN":"1726-4189","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Biogeosciences","language":"en","page":"2539-2557","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impact of bottom trawling on sediment biogeochemistry: a modelling approach","title-short":"Impact of bottom trawling on sediment biogeochemistry","URL":"https://bg.copernicus.org/articles/18/2539/2021/","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"De Borger","given":"Emil"},{"family":"Tiano","given":"Justin"},{"family":"Braeckman","given":"Ulrike"},{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"Adriaan D."},{"family":"Soetaert","given":"Karline"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,22]]}}},{"id":30809,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SCCG7W9R"],"itemData":{"id":30809,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"31","note":"publisher: National Acad Sciences","page":"8301–8306","title":"Global analysis of depletion and recovery of seabed biota after bottom trawling disturbance","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Hiddink","given":"Jan Geert"},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Sciberras","given":"Marija"},{"family":"Szostek","given":"Claire L"},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Kathryn M"},{"family":"Ellis","given":"Nick"},{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"Adriaan D"},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"Robert A"},{"family":"Mazor","given":"Tessa"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":30810,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8BU46TBE"],"itemData":{"id":30810,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            An improved understanding of the physical interaction of towed demersal fishing gears with the seabed has been developed in recent years, and there is a clearer view of the underpinning mechanical processes that lead to the modification and alteration of the benthic environment. The physical impact of these gears on soft sediments can be classified broadly as being either geotechnical or hydrodynamic in nature: penetration and piercing of the substrate, lateral displacement of sediment, and the influence of the pressure field transmitted through the sediment can be considered geotechnical, whereas the mobilization of sediment into the water column can be considered hydrodynamic. A number of experimental and numerical approaches have been used to gain better insights of these physical processes. These include small-scale modelling in towing tanks and sand channels; large-scale modelling in the field; measurements behind full-scale towed gears at sea; numerical/mathematical modelling of sediment mechanics; and numerical/mathematical modelling of hydrodynamics. Here, we will review this research, and that in associated fields, and show how it can form the basis of predictive models of the benthic impact of trawl gears.","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsv125","ISSN":"1095-9289, 1054-3139","issue":"suppl_1","language":"en","page":"i5-i14","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The physical impact of towed demersal fishing gears on soft sediments","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/icesjms/article/73/suppl_1/i5/2573993","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"O'Neill","given":"F. G."},{"family":"Ivanovi?","given":"A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",1,1]]}}},{"id":30804,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YPUMF8QE"],"itemData":{"id":30804,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1405454111","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"24","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","language":"en","page":"8861-8866","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Chronic and intensive bottom trawling impairs deep-sea biodiversity and ecosystem functioning","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/doi/10.1073/pnas.1405454111","volume":"111","author":[{"family":"Pusceddu","given":"A."},{"family":"Bianchelli","given":"S."},{"family":"Martin","given":"J."},{"family":"Puig","given":"P."},{"family":"Palanques","given":"A."},{"family":"Masque","given":"P."},{"family":"Danovaro","given":"R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",6,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Pusceddu 2014, Clark 2016, O'Neill and Ivanovi? 2016; Hiddink 2017, De Borger 2021). Concerns about environmental impacts of bottom trawling have fueled strong public campaigns and resulted to its ban in some countries and regions. Less extreme approaches for reducing the negative impacts of trawling have been pursued, including changes in gear design and fishing operations, prevention of further expansion of trawled area, ocean zoning, bycatch and habitat quotas and the closure of large areas to protect sensitive habitats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"af1u92mpis","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McConnaughey, 2020; A. Williams, 2020)","plainCitation":"(McConnaughey, 2020; A. Williams, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1276,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KC45AWCF"],"itemData":{"id":1276,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12431","ISSN":"1467-2960","issue":"2","note":"type: Journal Article","page":"319-337","title":"Choosing best practices for managing impacts of trawl fishing on seabed habitats and biota","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1111/faf.12431","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"Robert A."},{"family":"Hiddink","given":"Jan G."},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"C. Roland"},{"family":"Kaiser","given":"Michel J."},{"family":"Suuronen","given":"Petri"},{"family":"Sciberras","given":"Marija"},{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"Adriaan D."},{"family":"Collie","given":"Jeremy S."},{"family":"Mazor","given":"Tessa"},{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo O."},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30806,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E9HA7AS7"],"itemData":{"id":30806,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2020.567002","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","page":"567002","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Fate of Deep-Sea Coral Reefs on Seamounts in a Fishery-Seascape: What Are the Impacts, What Remains, and What Is Protected?","title-short":"The Fate of Deep-Sea Coral Reefs on Seamounts in a Fishery-Seascape","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fmars.2020.567002/full","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Williams","given":"Alan"},{"family":"Althaus","given":"Franziska"},{"family":"Maguire","given":"Kylie"},{"family":"Green","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Untiedt","given":"Candice"},{"family":"Alderslade","given":"Phil"},{"family":"Clark","given":"Malcolm R."},{"family":"Bax","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Schlacher","given":"Thomas A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,25]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. United Nations General Assembly Resolutions 61/105 (2007) and 64/72 (2010) required Regional Fisheries Management Organizations to identify vulnerable marine ecosystems on the seabed within their jurisdictions and ensure that fisheries did not cause serious adverse impacts to them. Of particular concern has been the expansion of trawling into deeper areas, leading for example to the ban on bottom trawling in deep waters (below 800 m) and in areas with vulnerable marine ecosystems (below 400 m) adopted by the European Union in 2016.  
      Assessments of the global and regional seabed impacts of bottom trawling require information on the distribution and intensity of trawling, the direct impact of the gear on the swept habitats and communities, and their capacity to recover from trawling disturbances ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"am7n7qncv9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mazor, 2021; McConnaughey, 2020; Pitcher, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Mazor, 2021; McConnaughey, 2020; Pitcher, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30805,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TV59B5AF"],"itemData":{"id":30805,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12506","ISSN":"1467-2960, 1467-2979","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"72-86","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trawl fishing impacts on the status of seabed fauna in diverse regions of the globe","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/faf.12506","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Mazor","given":"Tessa"},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"C. Roland"},{"family":"Rochester","given":"Wayne"},{"family":"Kaiser","given":"Michel J."},{"family":"Hiddink","given":"Jan G."},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"Robert A."},{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"Adriaan D."},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Suuronen","given":"Petri"},{"family":"Collie","given":"Jeremy"},{"family":"Sciberras","given":"Marija"},{"family":"Atkinson","given":"Lara"},{"family":"Durholtz","given":"Deon"},{"family":"Ellis","given":"Jim R"},{"family":"Bolam","given":"Stefan G."},{"family":"Schratzberger","given":"Michaela"},{"family":"Couce","given":"Elena"},{"family":"Eggleton","given":"Jacqueline"},{"family":"Garcia","given":"Clement"},{"family":"Kainge","given":"Paulus"},{"family":"Paulus","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Kathena","given":"Johannes N."},{"family":"Gogina","given":"Mayya"},{"family":"Denderen","given":"P. Daniël","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Keller","given":"Aimee A."},{"family":"Horness","given":"Beth H."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1]]}}},{"id":1276,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KC45AWCF"],"itemData":{"id":1276,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12431","ISSN":"1467-2960","issue":"2","note":"type: Journal Article","page":"319-337","title":"Choosing best practices for managing impacts of trawl fishing on seabed habitats and biota","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1111/faf.12431","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"Robert A."},{"family":"Hiddink","given":"Jan G."},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"C. Roland"},{"family":"Kaiser","given":"Michel J."},{"family":"Suuronen","given":"Petri"},{"family":"Sciberras","given":"Marija"},{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"Adriaan D."},{"family":"Collie","given":"Jeremy S."},{"family":"Mazor","given":"Tessa"},{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo O."},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30807,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VNRAKVVA"],"itemData":{"id":30807,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Methods in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.1111/2041-210X.12705","ISSN":"2041-210X, 2041-210X","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Methods Ecol Evol","language":"en","page":"472-480","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Estimating the sustainability of towed fishing?gear impacts on seabed habitats: a simple quantitative risk assessment method applicable to data?limited fisheries","title-short":"Estimating the sustainability of towed fishing?gear impacts on seabed habitats","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/2041-210X.12705","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Pitcher","given":"C. Roland"},{"family":"Ellis","given":"Nick"},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Hiddink","given":"Jan G."},{"family":"Mazor","given":"Tessa"},{"family":"Kaiser","given":"Michel J."},{"family":"Kangas","given":"Mervi I."},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"Robert A."},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"Adriaan D."},{"family":"Suuronen","given":"Petri"},{"family":"Collie","given":"Jeremy S."},{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Kathryn M."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"}],"editor":[{"family":"Freckleton","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A study using high-resolution satellite vessel monitoring system and logbook data on 24 continental shelves and slopes to 1,000-m depth (covering 7.8 million-km2 in total) showed that 14% of the overall study area was trawled and 86% was not trawled ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"arlmjvg136","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Amoroso, 2018)","plainCitation":"(R. Amoroso, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":159,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8W6XZ62C"],"itemData":{"id":159,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"6404","page":"eaat6713","title":"Comment on “Tracking the global footprint of fisheries”","volume":"361","author":[{"family":"Amoroso","given":"RO"},{"family":"Parma","given":"AM"},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"CR"},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"RA"},{"family":"Jennings","given":"S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the seabed proportion impacted by trawling varied markedly among and within regions, from less than 1% in southern Chile to a maximum of 80% in the Adriatic Sea and from areas (within region) trawled several times per year and others only disturbed sporadically. Trawling activity was aggregated; the most intensively trawled areas accounting for 90% of activity comprised 77% of footprint on average trawled ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jSqtfHyR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Amoroso, 2018)","plainCitation":"(R. Amoroso, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":159,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8W6XZ62C"],"itemData":{"id":159,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"6404","page":"eaat6713","title":"Comment on “Tracking the global footprint of fisheries”","volume":"361","author":[{"family":"Amoroso","given":"RO"},{"family":"Parma","given":"AM"},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"CR"},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"RA"},{"family":"Jennings","given":"S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In most heavily trawled areas of Europe a large fraction of the area (e.g., North Sea, West Iberia and Skagerrak and Kattegat) was trawled at least once per year, while more than half of the seabed was not trawled during the 2-6-year study period in 20 of 24 regions examined. Trawling footprints were also smaller in regions where fishing rates met sustainability benchmarks trawled ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VcpjZtbW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Amoroso, 2018)","plainCitation":"(R. Amoroso, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":159,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8W6XZ62C"],"itemData":{"id":159,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"6404","page":"eaat6713","title":"Comment on “Tracking the global footprint of fisheries”","volume":"361","author":[{"family":"Amoroso","given":"RO"},{"family":"Parma","given":"AM"},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"CR"},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"RA"},{"family":"Jennings","given":"S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      To evaluate biotic impacts, the frequency of trawling events further needs to be compared to the rate of recovery of the different types of organisms inhabiting seabeds. Recent meta-analyses of more than three decades of published results for sedimentary habitats have shown that the immediate mortality of animals in the path of the trawl is correlated with the penetration depth of the gear in the sediment, which vary with the type of gear ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a21tf3poqe0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hiddink, 2017; Sciberras, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hiddink, 2017; Sciberras, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30809,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SCCG7W9R"],"itemData":{"id":30809,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"31","note":"publisher: National Acad Sciences","page":"8301–8306","title":"Global analysis of depletion and recovery of seabed biota after bottom trawling disturbance","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Hiddink","given":"Jan Geert"},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Sciberras","given":"Marija"},{"family":"Szostek","given":"Claire L"},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Kathryn M"},{"family":"Ellis","given":"Nick"},{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"Adriaan D"},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"Robert A"},{"family":"Mazor","given":"Tessa"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":30801,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8YJHNZJU"],"itemData":{"id":30801,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12283","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"698-715","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Response of benthic fauna to experimental bottom fishing: A global meta-analysis","title-short":"Response of benthic fauna to experimental bottom fishing","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/faf.12283","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Sciberras","given":"Marija"},{"family":"Hiddink","given":"Jan Geert"},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Szostek","given":"Claire L"},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Kathryn M"},{"family":"Kneafsey","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Clarke","given":"Leo J"},{"family":"Ellis","given":"Nick"},{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"Adriaan D"},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"Robert A"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Collie","given":"Jeremy S"},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"C. Roland"},{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo O"},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M"},{"family":"Suuronen","given":"Petri"},{"family":"Kaiser","given":"Michel J"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The most commonly used trawl gear (otter trawls) kills 6% of the biomass per pass, whereas the most destructive gear (hydraulic dredges) kills 41% of the seabed biota present. Estimated recovery rates after trawling ranged from 1.9 to 6.4 years on average, depending on the type of sediment, trawl gear and benthic  species longevity (with longer-lived animals showing larger depletion effects in comparative studies, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a25bd88p94n","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hiddink, 2019, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Hiddink, 2019, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30808,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4ZMA8DI9"],"itemData":{"id":30808,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/1365-2664.13278","ISSN":"0021-8901, 1365-2664","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Ecol","language":"en","page":"1075-1084","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Assessing bottom trawling impacts based on the longevity of benthic invertebrates","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/1365-2664.13278","volume":"56","autho