shing on seabed habitats and biota","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1111/faf.12431","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"Robert A."},{"family":"Hiddink","given":"Jan G."},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"C. Roland"},{"family":"Kaiser","given":"Michel J."},{"family":"Suuronen","given":"Petri"},{"family":"Sciberras","given":"Marija"},{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"Adriaan D."},{"family":"Collie","given":"Jeremy S."},{"family":"Mazor","given":"Tessa"},{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo O."},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Selective effects linked to chronic trawling are likely to be much stronger for long-lived sessile epifauna, such as sponges and corals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1abfborgcq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hiddink, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Hiddink, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30809,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SCCG7W9R"],"itemData":{"id":30809,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"31","note":"publisher: National Acad Sciences","page":"8301–8306","title":"Global analysis of depletion and recovery of seabed biota after bottom trawling disturbance","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Hiddink","given":"Jan Geert"},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Sciberras","given":"Marija"},{"family":"Szostek","given":"Claire L"},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Kathryn M"},{"family":"Ellis","given":"Nick"},{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"Adriaan D"},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"Robert A"},{"family":"Mazor","given":"Tessa"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      By combining known distribution of trawl intensity from Amoroso ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aoak28tkh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Amoroso, 2018)","plainCitation":"(R. Amoroso, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":159,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8W6XZ62C"],"itemData":{"id":159,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"6404","page":"eaat6713","title":"Comment on “Tracking the global footprint of fisheries”","volume":"361","author":[{"family":"Amoroso","given":"RO"},{"family":"Parma","given":"AM"},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"CR"},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"RA"},{"family":"Jennings","given":"S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} with predicted abundance distributions of different benthos groups for 13 diverse regions of the globe, Mazor ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a234a32kkjc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mazor, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Mazor, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30805,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TV59B5AF"],"itemData":{"id":30805,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12506","ISSN":"1467-2960, 1467-2979","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"72-86","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trawl fishing impacts on the status of seabed fauna in diverse regions of the globe","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/faf.12506","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Mazor","given":"Tessa"},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"C. Roland"},{"family":"Rochester","given":"Wayne"},{"family":"Kaiser","given":"Michel J."},{"family":"Hiddink","given":"Jan G."},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Amoroso","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"Robert A."},{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"Adriaan D."},{"family":"Parma","given":"Ana M."},{"family":"Suuronen","given":"Petri"},{"family":"Collie","given":"Jeremy"},{"family":"Sciberras","given":"Marija"},{"family":"Atkinson","given":"Lara"},{"family":"Durholtz","given":"Deon"},{"family":"Ellis","given":"Jim R"},{"family":"Bolam","given":"Stefan G."},{"family":"Schratzberger","given":"Michaela"},{"family":"Couce","given":"Elena"},{"family":"Eggleton","given":"Jacqueline"},{"family":"Garcia","given":"Clement"},{"family":"Kainge","given":"Paulus"},{"family":"Paulus","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Kathena","given":"Johannes N."},{"family":"Gogina","given":"Mayya"},{"family":"Denderen","given":"P. Daniël","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Keller","given":"Aimee A."},{"family":"Horness","given":"Beth H."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found that expected benthic community status ranged between 86% and 100% of untrawled status (mean 99%), with more than three-quarters of benthic groups predicted to be at 95% or more of their benchmarks. Mean benthos status was lowest in regions of Europe and Africa and for taxonomic classes Bivalvia and Gastropoda. Communities prevalent in sedimentary habitats of the continental shelves could thus sustain moderate levels of trawling, provided that target fishing mortalities are maintained within accepted sustainability benchmarks. Biogenic habitats, such as coral reefs, maerl beds and sea mounts habitats (not covered by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a24ntv6cpac","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hiddink, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Hiddink, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30809,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SCCG7W9R"],"itemData":{"id":30809,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"31","note":"publisher: National Acad Sciences","page":"8301–8306","title":"Global analysis of depletion and recovery of seabed biota after bottom trawling disturbance","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Hiddink","given":"Jan Geert"},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Sciberras","given":"Marija"},{"family":"Szostek","given":"Claire L"},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Kathryn M"},{"family":"Ellis","given":"Nick"},{"family":"Rijnsdorp","given":"Adriaan D"},{"family":"McConnaughey","given":"Robert A"},{"family":"Mazor","given":"Tessa"},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}) are nonetheless expected to be the much more sensitive to trawling impacts due to their long recovery times. The limited data available for long-lived habitat-forming species indicate that post-trawling recovery may take decades ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ahr0vohhnr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kaiser, Hormbrey, Booth, Hinz, & Hiddink, 2018; A. Williams, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Kaiser, Hormbrey, Booth, Hinz, & Hiddink, 2018; A. Williams, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1202,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/57VMFHLI"],"itemData":{"id":1202,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/1365-2664.13087","ISSN":"00218901","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Ecol","language":"en","page":"1060-1070","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recovery linked to life history of sessile epifauna following exclusion of towed mobile fishing gear","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/1365-2664.13087","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Kaiser","given":"Michel J."},{"family":"Hormbrey","given":"Samantha"},{"family":"Booth","given":"Jonathan R."},{"family":"Hinz","given":"Hilmar"},{"family":"Hiddink","given":"Jan G."}],"editor":[{"family":"Januchowski-Hartley","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",5]]}}},{"id":1201,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NQICERP7"],"itemData":{"id":1201,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1439-0485.2010.00385.x","ISSN":"01739565","language":"en","page":"183-199","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Seamount megabenthic assemblages fail to recover from trawling impacts: Trawling impacts","title-short":"Seamount megabenthic assemblages fail to recover from trawling impacts","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1439-0485.2010.00385.x","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Williams","given":"Alan"},{"family":"Schlacher","given":"Thomas A."},{"family":"Rowden","given":"Ashley A."},{"family":"Althaus","given":"Franziska"},{"family":"Clark","given":"Malcolm R."},{"family":"Bowden","given":"David A."},{"family":"Stewart","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Bax","given":"Nicholas J."},{"family":"Consalvey","given":"Mireille"},{"family":"Kloser","given":"Rudy J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and be unachievable within acceptable timeframes; spatial closures are therefore essential ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ah1me5n3p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. R. Clark, 2016)","plainCitation":"(M. R. Clark, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30803,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TZSGLWF7"],"itemData":{"id":30803,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","issue":"suppl_1","note":"publisher: Oxford University Press","page":"i51–i69","title":"The impacts of deep-sea fisheries on benthic communities: a review","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Clark","given":"Malcolm R"},{"family":"Althaus","given":"Franziska"},{"family":"Schlacher","given":"Thomas A"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Alan"},{"family":"Bowden","given":"David A"},{"family":"Rowden","given":"Ashley A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The studies discussed above highlight the importance for policy analysis and implementation of collecting local data on the intensity and distribution of trawling, and the distribution of sediment types and vulnerable marine habitats. These data are needed to identify local best practices and most effective approaches to reduce habitat impacts of fishing, and to allow quantification of trade-offs between fish production for food and the environmental costs associated with different fishing methods and marine policies.

3.3.1.5. Uses of wild caught aquatic organisms

Regarding fishing practices, the following uses are well-documented in the literature and available data sources: food and feed (3.3.1.5.1), medicine and hygiene (3.3.1.5.2), recreational fishing (3.3.1.5.3), decorative and aesthetic (3.3.1.5.4), and ceremony and cultural uses (3.3.1.5.5). The following uses are not relevant to this practice or were not documented: energy, education and learning, and materials and shelter. With regards to non-lethal uses of wild aquatic organisms, a review of catch and release recreational fishing (3.3.1.6.1) and ornamental and aquarium fisheries (3.3.1.6.2) are included.

3.3.1.5.1. Food and Feed

Fish and seafood products are important for human diet, providing about 3.1 billion people with almost 20 percent of their average daily animal protein intake ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QwLQLscC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sunderland, Chan, & Cheung, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Sunderland, Chan, & Cheung, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6804,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N5N4QVA3"],"itemData":{"id":6804,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Predicting Future Oceans","ISBN":"978-0-12-817945-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1016/B978-0-12-817945-1.00017-4","page":"175-179","publisher":"Elsevier","source":"Crossref","title":"Fisheries and seafood security under changing oceans","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/B9780128179451000174","author":[{"family":"Sunderland","given":"Elsie M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"Hing Man"},{"family":"Cheung","given":"William W.L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Human consumption of fish in 2018 totaled 96.4 M tons ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1950sdv6l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020d)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Of the landed catch of industrial fisheries, about 80% is used for direct human consumption, and close to 100% of the retained catch of small-scale fisheries is eaten by people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3BaVcTXl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2018d)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2018d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M2WPUUSR"],"itemData":{"id":7733,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development\\n(2030 Agenda for short) offers a vision of a\\nfairer, more peaceful world in which no one is\\nleft behind. The 2030 Agenda also sets aims for\\nthe contribution and conduct of fisheries and\\naquaculture towards food security and nutrition,\\nand the sector’s use of natural resources, in a\\nway that ensures sustainable development in\\neconomic, social and environmental terms,\\nwithin the context of the FAO Code of Conduct\\nfor Responsible Fisheries (FAO, 1995). A major\\nchallenge to implementation of the 2030 Agenda\\nis the sustainability divide between developed\\nand developing countries which has partially\\nresulted from increased economic\\ninterdependencies, coupled with limited\\nmanagement and governance capacity in\\ndeveloping countries. To eliminate this disparity\\nwhile making progress towards the target for\\nrestoration of overfished stocks set by the 2030\\nAgenda, the global community needs to support\\ndeveloping nations to achieve their full fisheries\\nand aquaculture potential. \\n\\nReport is rich in data.","event-place":"Rome","language":"English","note":"978-92-5-130562-1","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture 2018 - Meeting the sustainable development goals","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/I9540EN/i9540en.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is important to note that some of these estimates include wild fish and farmed fish from aquaculture. For different indicators available at a global scale, especially fish consumption, much of the available literature does not clearly distinguish between farmed and wild caught fish. Further, in several data sets the information on both wild and farmed fish is so intricately mixed up that it is impossible to distinguish between the two. Indeed, this lack of clarity makes proper assessment of the sustainable use of wild fish species extremely challenging and presents a serious issue for accurate reporting and tracking. This issue is discussed in more detail in the knowledge gaps section (3.5). Thus, despite the focus of this assessment on wild species, assessment experts consistently evaluated the various material they reviewed in relation to two options: (i) select not to use available data and literature on farmed fish and exclude the state of the knowledge on this topic or (ic) include available data on farmed fish in combination with wild fish.  
      Since 1973, the global consumption of fish has doubled, due to increased demand in developed and developing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mwbfgSPa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Delgado, International Food Policy Research Institute, & WorldFish Center, 2003; FAO, 2018d)","plainCitation":"(Delgado, International Food Policy Research Institute, & WorldFish Center, 2003; FAO, 2018d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1264,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TLASMK2B"],"itemData":{"id":1264,"type":"book","call-number":"HD9450.5 .F56 2003","event-place":"Washington, D.C. : Penang, Malaysia","ISBN":"978-0-89629-725-8","number-of-pages":"226","publisher":"International Food Policy Research Institute ; WorldFish Center","publisher-place":"Washington, D.C. : Penang, Malaysia","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Fish to 2020: supply and demand in changing global markets","title-short":"Fish to 2020","editor":[{"family":"Delgado","given":"Christopher L."},{"family":"International Food Policy Research Institute","given":""},{"family":"WorldFish Center","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":7733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M2WPUUSR"],"itemData":{"id":7733,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development\\n(2030 Agenda for short) offers a vision of a\\nfairer, more peaceful world in which no one is\\nleft behind. The 2030 Agenda also sets aims for\\nthe contribution and conduct of fisheries and\\naquaculture towards food security and nutrition,\\nand the sector’s use of natural resources, in a\\nway that ensures sustainable development in\\neconomic, social and environmental terms,\\nwithin the context of the FAO Code of Conduct\\nfor Responsible Fisheries (FAO, 1995). A major\\nchallenge to implementation of the 2030 Agenda\\nis the sustainability divide between developed\\nand developing countries which has partially\\nresulted from increased economic\\ninterdependencies, coupled with limited\\nmanagement and governance capacity in\\ndeveloping countries. To eliminate this disparity\\nwhile making progress towards the target for\\nrestoration of overfished stocks set by the 2030\\nAgenda, the global community needs to support\\ndeveloping nations to achieve their full fisheries\\nand aquaculture potential. \\n\\nReport is rich in data.","event-place":"Rome","language":"English","note":"978-92-5-130562-1","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture 2018 - Meeting the sustainable development goals","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/I9540EN/i9540en.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Consumption grew from approximately 9.0 kg per capita in 1961 to 20.2 kg per capita in 2015, at an average rate of about 1.5 percent per year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YwzIxs3P","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2018d)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2018d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M2WPUUSR"],"itemData":{"id":7733,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development\\n(2030 Agenda for short) offers a vision of a\\nfairer, more peaceful world in which no one is\\nleft behind. The 2030 Agenda also sets aims for\\nthe contribution and conduct of fisheries and\\naquaculture towards food security and nutrition,\\nand the sector’s use of natural resources, in a\\nway that ensures sustainable development in\\neconomic, social and environmental terms,\\nwithin the context of the FAO Code of Conduct\\nfor Responsible Fisheries (FAO, 1995). A major\\nchallenge to implementation of the 2030 Agenda\\nis the sustainability divide between developed\\nand developing countries which has partially\\nresulted from increased economic\\ninterdependencies, coupled with limited\\nmanagement and governance capacity in\\ndeveloping countries. To eliminate this disparity\\nwhile making progress towards the target for\\nrestoration of overfished stocks set by the 2030\\nAgenda, the global community needs to support\\ndeveloping nations to achieve their full fisheries\\nand aquaculture potential. \\n\\nReport is rich in data.","event-place":"Rome","language":"English","note":"978-92-5-130562-1","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture 2018 - Meeting the sustainable development goals","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/I9540EN/i9540en.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A higher rate of 2.4 percent is observed in developing countries for the same period. The growth of world per capita fish consumption from 18 kg in 2008 to 20 kg in 2013 was due to an increase in per capita consumption of freshwater & diadromous fish (migrate between freshwater and saltwater, ex: salmon, eel, etc.), crustaceans and shell molluscs, whereas that of marine fish and cephalopods declined ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"amc57ic5mq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cai & Leung, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Cai & Leung, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1261,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7C4Q2PVI"],"itemData":{"id":1261,"type":"book","collection-number":"607","collection-title":"FAO fisheries and aquaculture technical paper","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-109857-8","language":"en","note":"OCLC: on1013590907","number-of-pages":"114","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Short-term projection of global fish demand and supply gaps","author":[{"family":"Cai","given":"Junning"},{"family":"Leung","given":"PingSun"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The importance of global fish production for nutrition and food security varies geographically across regions, countries, and communities dependent on fish at rates far above the global average (Box 3.6). Some of the most fish-dependent populations are located in countries in which the contribution of fish is relatively low at the national level ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1lebo9jav8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Bennett, 2018)","plainCitation":"(A. Bennett, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1232,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GJ93RJIV"],"itemData":{"id":1232,"type":"report","event-place":"Durham, NC: Duke University","genre":"NI Report 18-02","publisher-place":"Durham, NC: Duke University","title":"Contribution of Fisheries to Food and Nutrition Security: Current Knowledge, Policy, and Research","URL":"https://nicholasinstitute.duke.edu/sites/default/files/publications/contribution_of_fisheries_to_food_and_nutrition_security_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"Abigail"},{"family":"Patil","given":"Pawan"},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin"},{"family":"Rader","given":"Doug"},{"family":"Virdin","given":"John"},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At sub-national scales, individual communities can be almost entirely dependent on seafood for protein. Fish is crucial for coastal indigenous groups, who on average consume fish at a rate that is 15 times higher than the global average (Figure 3.31). 
      

Figure 3.31 Fish dependency around the world. This map is directly copied from its original source ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ahfieqlm0r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Bennett, 2018)","plainCitation":"(A. Bennett, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1232,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GJ93RJIV"],"itemData":{"id":1232,"type":"report","event-place":"Durham, NC: Duke University","genre":"NI Report 18-02","publisher-place":"Durham, NC: Duke University","title":"Contribution of Fisheries to Food and Nutrition Security: Current Knowledge, Policy, and Research","URL":"https://nicholasinstitute.duke.edu/sites/default/files/publications/contribution_of_fisheries_to_food_and_nutrition_security_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"Abigail"},{"family":"Patil","given":"Pawan"},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin"},{"family":"Rader","given":"Doug"},{"family":"Virdin","given":"John"},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and was not modified by the assessment authors. The map is copyrighted under license CC BY 4.0. The designations employed and the presentation of material on the maps used in the assessment do not imply the expression of any opinion whatsoever on the part of IPBES concerning the legal status of any country, territory, city or area or of its authorities, or concerning the delimitation of its frontiers or boundaries. These maps have been prepared or used for the sole purpose of facilitating the assessment of the broad biogeographical areas represented therein and for purposes of representing scientific data spatially. 

Box 3.6  Dried fish in Asian countries

Dried fish is an important part of small-scale fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a12mkskksom","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2018b; Kawarazuka & B\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}n\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, 2010)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2018b; Kawarazuka & Béné, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1265,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A8CVAVI2"],"itemData":{"id":1265,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","collection-title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130562-1","language":"en","number-of-pages":"210","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Meeting the sustainable development goals","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":30789,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6RJISMIL"],"itemData":{"id":30789,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Food Security","DOI":"10.1007/s12571-010-0079-y","ISSN":"1876-4517, 1876-4525","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Food Sec.","language":"en","page":"343-357","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Linking small-scale fisheries and aquaculture to household nutritional security: an overview","title-short":"Linking small-scale fisheries and aquaculture to household nutritional security","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12571-010-0079-y","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Kawarazuka","given":"Nozomi"},{"family":"Béné","given":"Christophe"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and includes fish that has been cured, dried, salted, brined, fermented, or smoked fish (see Supplementary material Table S3.1). These are often small and low market value fish from capture fisheries. Approximately 12% of fisheries are prepared and preserved, and 12% are cured. In some countries dried fish consumption is significantly higher ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1kshaanbst","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2018b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2018b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1265,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A8CVAVI2"],"itemData":{"id":1265,"type":"book","collection-number":"2018","collection-title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-130562-1","language":"en","number-of-pages":"210","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Meeting the sustainable development goals","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The voluntary guidelines for securing sustainable small-scale fisheries considers fishing and fish processing as important drivers of food security and poverty eradication ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a18n84kneup","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2015)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33286,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DEN3PTYZ"],"itemData":{"id":33286,"type":"book","abstract":"The Voluntary Guidelines for Securing Sustainable Small-Scale Fisheries in the Context of Food Security and Poverty Eradication (SSF Guidelines) represent the first ever internationl instrument dedicated to small-scale fisheries. They represent a global consensus on principles and guidance for small-scale fisheries governance and development. They were developed for small-scale fisheries in close collaboration with representatives of small-scale fisheries organizations in a participatory process between 2011-13, involving over 4000 stakeholders; facilitated by FAO, based on a mandate by COFI. They are directed at all those involved in the sector and intend to guide and encourage governments, fishing communities and other stakeholders to work together and ensure secure and sustainable small-scale fisheries for the benefit of small-scale fishers, fish workers and their communities as well as for society at large. They complement existing internatinoal instruments, such as the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries, the VG Tenure and the Right to Food Guidelines. Underpinned by a human rights approach, the SSF Guidelines represent a critical instrument to empower small-scale fishing communities - including vulnerable and marginalized groups - to participate in decision-making processes, and to assume responsibilities for sustainable use of fishery resources. The SSF Guidelines are already referred to in a number of ongoing policy processes (Committee on Global Food Security: Principles for responsible investment in agriculture and food systems 41st CFS recommendations; NEPAD's policy framework and reform strategy for fisheries and aquaculture in Africa; Western Central Atlantic Fishery Commission (WECAFC), Resolution WECAFC/15/2014/8)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-108704-6","language":"eng","number-of-pages":"18","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"K10plus ISBN","title":"Voluntary guidelines for securing sustainable small-scale fisheries in the context of food security and poverty eradication","editor":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Asia and Africa wide varieties of species are dried ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a23l7f9hhk9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ruddle & Ishige, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Ruddle & Ishige, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30799,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AJ248D3R"],"itemData":{"id":30799,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Globalization, food and social identities in the Asia Pacific Region","note":"publisher: Sophia University Institute of Comparative Culture Tokyo","page":"1–17","title":"On the origins, diffusion and cultural context of fermented fish products in Southeast Asia","author":[{"family":"Ruddle","given":"Kenneth"},{"family":"Ishige","given":"Naomichi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, including many small pelagic species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2mr3ulimo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"\\\\uldash{(Doe, 2017)}","plainCitation":"(Doe, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1234,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FZQYYSZT"],"itemData":{"id":1234,"type":"book","edition":"Routlege","ISBN":"1-351-44850-1","title":"Fish Drying and Smoking: Production and Quality","author":[{"family":"Doe","given":"Peter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Bangladesh, dried fish are eaten more frequently than any other type of fish. The contribution of dried products to total fish consumption is disproportionately important for low-income consumers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"avd3muff7t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Belton & Thilsted, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Belton & Thilsted, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1263,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WXYTX95T"],"itemData":{"id":1263,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fisheries and ?sh supply are undergoing a fundamental structural transition, as indicated by a ten country analysis. Aquaculture now provides around half the ?sh for direct human consumption and is set to grow further, but capture ?sheries continue to make essential contributions to food and nutrition security throughout the global South. Capture ?sheries provide diverse, nutritionally valuable ?sh and ?sh products which are often culturally preferred and easily accessed by the poor. Technological changes in aquaculture have dramatically increased ?sh supply, lowered relative ?sh prices, and reigned in price volatility. Policies that recognize and safeguard the diversity and complementarity of roles played by capture ?sheries and aquaculture are needed to ensure that the transition in ?sheries sustainably improves food and nutrition security in the global South.","container-title":"Global Food Security","DOI":"10.1016/j.gfs.2013.10.001","ISSN":"22119124","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"59-66","source":"Crossref","title":"Fisheries in transition: Food and nutrition security implications for the global South","title-short":"Fisheries in transition","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2211912413000515","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Belton","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"Shakuntala Haraksingh"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although dry fish is not cheap, the quantity needed for a meal is less and therefore economical and may explain popularity in rural areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"atof5mi3l0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Samaranayaka, Perera, & Warnasuriya, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Samaranayaka, Perera, & Warnasuriya, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30794,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6ED9P6H9"],"itemData":{"id":30794,"type":"paper-conference","title":"Food Habits among Adolescents in Colombo , Sri Lanka","author":[{"family":"Samaranayaka","given":"Shyamalee"},{"family":"Perera","given":"A. N. F."},{"family":"Warnasuriya","given":"Narada"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Dried fish contribute to food and nutrition security in both coastal and arid mountainous regions of low-income countries as they are a concentrated source of animal protein, rich in calcium and other micronutrients and fats, easily transportable and have a long self-life ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ats309o0gn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Belton, Hossain, & Thilsted, 2018; Thilsted, James, Toppe, Subasinghe, & Karunasagar, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Belton, Hossain, & Thilsted, 2018; Thilsted, James, Toppe, Subasinghe, & Karunasagar, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30798,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8ZCG5PAN"],"itemData":{"id":30798,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Social Wellbeing and the Values of Small-scale Fisheries","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-319-60749-8","note":"collection-title: MARE Publication Series\\nDOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-60750-4_10","page":"217-241","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Labour, Identity and Wellbeing in Bangladesh’s Dried Fish Value Chains","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-319-60750-4_10","volume":"17","editor":[{"family":"Johnson","given":"Derek S."},{"family":"Acott","given":"Tim G."},{"family":"Stacey","given":"Natasha"},{"family":"Urquhart","given":"Julie"}],"author":[{"family":"Belton","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Hossain","given":"Mostafa A. R."},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"Shakuntala H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":30797,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BLKFTZ24"],"itemData":{"id":30797,"type":"article-journal","note":"publisher: FAO","title":"Maximizing the contribution of fish to human nutrition","author":[{"family":"Thilsted","given":"Shakuntala Haraksingh"},{"family":"James","given":"David"},{"family":"Toppe","given":"Jogeir"},{"family":"Subasinghe","given":"Rohana"},{"family":"Karunasagar","given":"Iddya"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, in Malawi, a serving of 24 g of small dried fish twice a day provides an intake of calcium, zinc and iron which is 327%, 152% and 22% higher, respectively than a daily diet without fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1o69ffante","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. S. Gibson & Hotz, 2001; Kawarazuka & B\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}n\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, 2010)","plainCitation":"(R. S. Gibson & Hotz, 2001; Kawarazuka & Béné, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30793,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8GT6YV25"],"itemData":{"id":30793,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Both cereal staples and household diets can be manipulated to enhance the content of micronutrients and/or alter the levels of absorption modifiers to improve micronutrient bioavailability. Strategies described range from plant breeding, use of fertilizers and genetic engineering to changes in food preparation and processing methods at the household level involving soaking, fermentation and germination. The impact of five household strategies designed to enhance the content and bioavailability of iron, zinc and calcium in a representative daily menu for rural Malawian preschool children has been calculated using food composition data. In the five strategies, relishes based on small dried fish replaced plant-based relishes, maize-based porridges prepared with maize flour soaked to reduce its hexa (IP-6)- and penta (IP-5)-inositol phosphate content replaced conventional porridges; and a pumpkin-leaf relish replaced sweet potato to increase the retinol content of the daily menu. Comparison of the calculated energy, nutrient, and phytate content, and [phytate]:[zinc] molar ratios of the five modified menus compared with the unmodified menu emphasizes that to ensure that the estimated requirements for iron and zinc are met, the optimal strategy includes dried fish relish twice daily together with porridges prepared using soaked (or fermented) maize flour to reduce their hexa- and penta-inositol phosphate content. Implementation of these household strategies has the potential to increase the bioavailability of iron and zinc in rural Malawian diets from low to high.","container-title":"British Journal of Nutrition","DOI":"10.1079/BJN2001309","ISSN":"0007-1145, 1475-2662","issue":"S2","journalAbbreviation":"Br J Nutr","language":"en","page":"S159-S166","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Dietary diversification/modification strategies to enhance micronutrient content and bioavailability of diets in developing countries","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0007114501001064/type/journal_article","volume":"85","author":[{"family":"Gibson","given":"Rosalind S."},{"family":"Hotz","given":"Christine"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001",5]]}}},{"id":30789,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6RJISMIL"],"itemData":{"id":30789,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Food Security","DOI":"10.1007/s12571-010-0079-y","ISSN":"1876-4517, 1876-4525","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Food Sec.","language":"en","page":"343-357","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Linking small-scale fisheries and aquaculture to household nutritional security: an overview","title-short":"Linking small-scale fisheries and aquaculture to household nutritional security","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12571-010-0079-y","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Kawarazuka","given":"Nozomi"},{"family":"Béné","given":"Christophe"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Since low end processing activities are mostly done by women ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15rmfam8sl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Samanta, Bhaumik, & Patra, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Samanta, Bhaumik, & Patra, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30792,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SXSMKZBB"],"itemData":{"id":30792,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Current Research and Academic Review","issue":"5","page":"84–100","title":"Socio-economic status of the fish curers of the dry fish industry of the coastal areas of West Bengal, India","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Samanta","given":"C"},{"family":"Bhaumik","given":"U"},{"family":"Patra","given":"BC"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, their control over family income directly affects household food security and nutritional outcomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1jh7o7ecj9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kawarazuka & B\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}n\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Kawarazuka & Béné, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30789,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6RJISMIL"],"itemData":{"id":30789,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Food Security","DOI":"10.1007/s12571-010-0079-y","ISSN":"1876-4517, 1876-4525","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Food Sec.","language":"en","page":"343-357","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Linking small-scale fisheries and aquaculture to household nutritional security: an overview","title-short":"Linking small-scale fisheries and aquaculture to household nutritional security","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12571-010-0079-y","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Kawarazuka","given":"Nozomi"},{"family":"Béné","given":"Christophe"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Women have been involved in the dried fish sector in developed countries and regions as well. Historically, and for centuries (until the 1960s) dried fish processing was a major activity in places such as Newfoundland and other Eastern North American locales and was undertaken significantly by women ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1hm8d8dfvc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Doe, 2017; Neis, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Doe, 2017; Neis, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1234,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FZQYYSZT"],"itemData":{"id":1234,"type":"book","edition":"Routlege","ISBN":"1-351-44850-1","title":"Fish Drying and Smoking: Production and Quality","author":[{"family":"Doe","given":"Peter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":30791,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DIBDZ5L3"],"itemData":{"id":30791,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Fishing Places, Fishing People","event-place":"Toronto","ISBN":"978-1-4426-7493-6","note":"DOI: 10.3138/9781442674936-005","publisher":"University of Toronto Press","publisher-place":"Toronto","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"3. Familial and Social Patriarchy in the Newfoundland Fishing Industry","URL":"https://www.degruyter.com/document/doi/10.3138/9781442674936-005/html","editor":[{"family":"Newell","given":"Dianne"},{"family":"Ommer","given":"Rosemary"}],"author":[{"family":"Neis","given":"Barbara"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999",1,31]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Men were engaged in the pursuit and capture of fish; women in the spreading, turning and drying of fish. This produced food and income security for workers in this profession.
      Subsistence and artisanal fisherfolk communities in Asia mostly belong to socially and economically marginalized groups ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2oi8dasf9g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hapke, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Hapke, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30796,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V4X6CILF"],"itemData":{"id":30796,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Professional Geographer","DOI":"10.1111/0033-0124.00287","ISSN":"0033-0124, 1467-9272","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"The Professional Geographer","language":"en","page":"313-331","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Gender, Work, and Household Survival in South Indian Fishing Communities: A Preliminary Analysis","title-short":"Gender, Work, and Household Survival in South Indian Fishing Communities","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1111/0033-0124.00287","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Hapke","given":"Holly M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, with those engaged in dried fish activity even more marginalized among fisher communities. Hence, the importance of wild marine species in life and livelihoods of the poorest of the poor is immense. At the same time the fisher communities draw life satisfaction by engaging in fishing activities they find challenging and skillfully providing them with a different identity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1fqdqldt9q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nayak, Dias, & Pradhan, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Nayak, Dias, & Pradhan, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/34RNIJ63"],"itemData":{"id":30795,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Life Below Water","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-319-71064-8","language":"en","note":"collection-title: Encyclopedia of the UN Sustainable Development Goals\\nDOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-71064-8_88-1","page":"1-18","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Traditional Fishing Community and Sustainable Development","URL":"https://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-319-71064-8_88-1","editor":[{"family":"Leal Filho","given":"Walter"},{"family":"Azul","given":"Anabela Marisa"},{"family":"Brandli","given":"Luciana"},{"family":"Lange Salvia","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Wall","given":"Tony"}],"author":[{"family":"Nayak","given":"Prateep Kumar"},{"family":"Dias","given":"Ana Carolina Esteves"},{"family":"Pradhan","given":"Sisir Kanta"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
      Marine fish used to be the largest species group in world fish consumption, but its share declined from 53% in 1993 to 37% in 2013. Marine fish are still the dominant species consumed in many countries. Indeed, in 2013 marine fish accounted for more than half of fish consumption in more than 170 countries. Over the same period freshwater & diadromous fish consumption grew rapidly, increasing from 3.2 kg in 1993 to 7.5 kg in 2013 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a29dufls2jc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Bennett, 2018)","plainCitation":"(A. Bennett, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1232,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GJ93RJIV"],"itemData":{"id":1232,"type":"report","event-place":"Durham, NC: Duke University","genre":"NI Report 18-02","publisher-place":"Durham, NC: Duke University","title":"Contribution of Fisheries to Food and Nutrition Security: Current Knowledge, Policy, and Research","URL":"https://nicholasinstitute.duke.edu/sites/default/files/publications/contribution_of_fisheries_to_food_and_nutrition_security_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"Abigail"},{"family":"Patil","given":"Pawan"},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin"},{"family":"Rader","given":"Doug"},{"family":"Virdin","given":"John"},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Crustaceans accounted for nearly 10% of world fish consumption in 2013, increasing from 8% in 1993. Shell molluscs accounted for 13% of world fish consumption in 2013; nearly the same as in 1993. Cephalopods accounted for 2.6% in 2013; down from 3.5% in 1993 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1724sp5sn7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cai & Leung, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Cai & Leung, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1261,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7C4Q2PVI"],"itemData":{"id":1261,"type":"book","collection-number":"607","collection-title":"FAO fisheries and aquaculture technical paper","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-109857-8","language":"en","note":"OCLC: on1013590907","number-of-pages":"114","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Short-term projection of global fish demand and supply gaps","author":[{"family":"Cai","given":"Junning"},{"family":"Leung","given":"PingSun"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Figure 3.32).


Figure 3.32 Species composition of world per capita fish consumption. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a16uqcriiqs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cai & Leung, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Cai & Leung, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1261,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7C4Q2PVI"],"itemData":{"id":1261,"type":"book","collection-number":"607","collection-title":"FAO fisheries and aquaculture technical paper","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-109857-8","language":"en","note":"OCLC: on1013590907","number-of-pages":"114","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Short-term projection of global fish demand and supply gaps","author":[{"family":"Cai","given":"Junning"},{"family":"Leung","given":"PingSun"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} © FAO, 2017. CC-BY NC. 

      Hatchery-based aquaculture relies on the use of wild fish as feed. The share of fed species in total aquaculture production accounts for the majority (69.5%) of “food fish” production from aquaculture ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hsriJemx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Clavelle, Lester, Gentry, & Froehlich, 2019; FAO, 2018d)","plainCitation":"(Clavelle, Lester, Gentry, & Froehlich, 2019; FAO, 2018d)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1028,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LRJT6E5K"],"itemData":{"id":1028,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Aquaculture surpassed wild fisheries as the largest supplier of fish for human consumption in 2014 and is expected to supply the majority of seafood for future increases in demand. Marine and coastal aquaculture, collectively referred to as mariculture, currently represents just 36% of aquaculture production but is poised to expand in the decades ahead. One of the most commonly cited concerns regarding this likely expansion is ecological and socioeconomic interactions with wild-­ capture fisheries. While attention has largely been drawn to high-­profile negative externalities from fed finfish and crustacean mariculture, not all marine-­based practices are equivalent. Empirical evidence for the different interactions between mariculture and wild fisheries is often sparse. While negative consequences can arise, positive synergies can also occur. By considering mariculture development in the context of fisheries interactions, we suggest that it is possible to minimize conflicts and maximize positive connections between the two sectors. We provide the first comprehensive synthesis of the interactions between mariculture and wild fisheries, characterizing the types of interactions, evaluating available empirical evidence and identifying where management (sector-­specific and cooperative) can play an important role. We highlight potential effects of mariculture on the efficiency, sustainability, and equity of seafood production and identify remaining knowledge gaps.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12351","ISSN":"1467-2960, 1467-2979","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"368-388","source":"Crossref","title":"Interactions and management for the future of marine aquaculture and capture fisheries","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/faf.12351","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Clavelle","given":"Tyler"},{"family":"Lester","given":"Sarah E."},{"family":"Gentry","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Froehlich","given":"Halley E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",3]]}}},{"id":7733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M2WPUUSR"],"itemData":{"id":7733,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development\\n(2030 Agenda for short) offers a vision of a\\nfairer, more peaceful world in which no one is\\nleft behind. The 2030 Agenda also sets aims for\\nthe contribution and conduct of fisheries and\\naquaculture towards food security and nutrition,\\nand the sector’s use of natural resources, in a\\nway that ensures sustainable development in\\neconomic, social and environmental terms,\\nwithin the context of the FAO Code of Conduct\\nfor Responsible Fisheries (FAO, 1995). A major\\nchallenge to implementation of the 2030 Agenda\\nis the sustainability divide between developed\\nand developing countries which has partially\\nresulted from increased economic\\ninterdependencies, coupled with limited\\nmanagement and governance capacity in\\ndeveloping countries. To eliminate this disparity\\nwhile making progress towards the target for\\nrestoration of overfished stocks set by the 2030\\nAgenda, the global community needs to support\\ndeveloping nations to achieve their full fisheries\\nand aquaculture potential. \\n\\nReport is rich in data.","event-place":"Rome","language":"English","note":"978-92-5-130562-1","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"The state of world fisheries and aquaculture 2018 - Meeting the sustainable development goals","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/I9540EN/i9540en.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Capture-based mariculture depends on wild-caught juveniles for “seed,” which are then raised and fattened in captivity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a16a9ave4hq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Boyd, 2020; Ottolenghi, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Boyd, 2020; Ottolenghi, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1030,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CFBBUEZI"],"itemData":{"id":1030,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of the World Aquaculture Society","DOI":"10.1111/jwas.12714","ISSN":"0893-8849, 1749-7345","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"578-633","source":"Crossref","title":"Achieving sustainable aquaculture: Historical and current perspectives and future needs and challenges","title-short":"Achieving sustainable aquaculture","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/jwas.12714","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Boyd","given":"Claude E."},{"family":"D'Abramo","given":"Louis R."},{"family":"Glencross","given":"Brent D."},{"family":"Huyben","given":"David C."},{"family":"Juarez","given":"Lorenzo M."},{"family":"Lockwood","given":"George S."},{"family":"McNevin","given":"Aaron A."},{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Teletchea","given":"Fabrice"},{"family":"Tomasso","given":"Joseph R."},{"family":"Tucker","given":"Craig S."},{"family":"Valenti","given":"Wagner C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",6]]}}},{"id":1018,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z53XN7GJ"],"itemData":{"id":1018,"type":"book","publisher":"FAO","title":"Capture-based aquaculture: the fattening of eels, groupers, tunas and yellowtails.","author":[{"family":"Ottolenghi","given":"Francesca"},{"family":"Silvestri","given":"Cecilia"},{"family":"Giordano","given":"Paola"},{"family":"Lovatelli","given":"Alessandro"},{"family":"New","given":"Michael B"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This practice, sometimes referred to as “ranching,” is widespread and an important source of production for many species, including tuna, shrimp, lobster, grouper and eels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a16tvvcnmu5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lorenzen, Leber, & Blankenship, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Lorenzen, Leber, & Blankenship, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1034,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JHTQ22MR"],"itemData":{"id":1034,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1080/10641262.2010.491564","ISSN":"1064-1262, 1547-6553","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"189-210","source":"Crossref","title":"Responsible Approach to Marine Stock Enhancement: An Update","title-short":"Responsible Approach to Marine Stock Enhancement","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10641262.2010.491564","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Lorenzen","given":"Kai"},{"family":"Leber","given":"Kenneth M."},{"family":"Blankenship","given":"H. Lee"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",8,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, no current estimates of the extent of capture-­based mariculture exist.
      Production of fed species depends on feeds containing high concentrations of proteins and lipids traditionally sourced from fishmeal and oil rendered from wild-caught forage fish, such as herring, sardines and menhaden ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2d0ud43mhu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tacon, Hasan, & Metian, 2011; Tacon & Metian, 2008b, 2008a, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Tacon, Hasan, & Metian, 2011; Tacon & Metian, 2008b, 2008a, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1042,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H8VYXQVK"],"itemData":{"id":1042,"type":"book","abstract":"The rise into global prominence and rapid growth of finfish and crustacean aquaculture has been due, in part, to the availability and on-farm provision of feed inputs within the major producing countries. More than 46 percent of the total global aquaculture production in 2008 was dependent upon the supply of external feed inputs. For the aquaculture sector to maintain its current average growth rate of 8 to 10 percent per year to 2025, the supply of nutrient and feed inputs will have to grow at a similar rate. This had been readily attainable when the industry was young. It may not be the case anymore as the sector has grown into a major consumer of and competitor for feed resources. This paper reviews the dietary feeding practices employed for the production of the major cultured fed species, the total global production and market availability of the major feed ingredient sources used and the major constraints to feed ingredient usage, and recommends approaches to feed ingredient selection and usage for the major species of cultivated fish and crustacean. Emphasis is placed on the need for major producing countries to maximize the use of locally available feed-grade ingredient sources, and, in particular, to select and use those nutritionally sound and safe feed ingredient sources whose production and growth can keep pace with the 8 to 10 percent annual average annual growth of the fed finfish and crustacean aquaculture sector","call-number":"SH156 .T326 2011","collection-number":"564","collection-title":"FAO fisheries and aquaculture technical paper","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-106933-2","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn792750597","number-of-pages":"87","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Demand and supply of feed ingredients for farmed fish and crustaceans","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Hasan","given":"Mohammad R."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33788,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2NTQX6BN"],"itemData":{"id":33788,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The ?n?sh and crustacean aquaculture sector is still highly dependent upon marine capture ?sheries for sourcing key dietary nutrient inputs, including ?sh meal and ?sh oil. This dependency is particularly strong within compound aquafeeds for farmed carnivorous ?n?sh species and marine shrimp.","container-title":"Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1016/j.aquaculture.2008.08.015","ISSN":"00448486","issue":"1-4","language":"en","page":"146-158","source":"Crossref","title":"Global overview on the use of fish meal and fish oil in industrially compounded aquafeeds: Trends and future prospects","title-short":"Global overview on the use of fish meal and fish oil in industrially compounded aquafeeds","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S004484860800567X","volume":"285","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G.J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",12]]}}},{"id":1043,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BFLIQW2E"],"itemData":{"id":1043,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1196/annals.1454.003","ISSN":"00778923, 17496632","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"50-59","source":"Crossref","title":"Aquaculture Feed and Food Safety","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1196/annals.1454.003","volume":"1140","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",10]]}}},{"id":33789,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LGDYCGI4"],"itemData":{"id":33789,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The rise of aquaculture has attracted a great deal of attention and this has increased since the sector is now providing more ?sh and crustaceans than capture ?sheries. This global prominence has been partly facilitated by the availability and onfarm provision of feed inputs within the major aquaculture producing countries. More than 70% of the total global aquaculture production is dependent upon the supply of external feed inputs. For the aquaculture sector to maintain its current growth rate, the supply of nutrient and feed inputs will have to grow at a similar rate, while aquatic ingredients production remains static and other sectors compete for same feed resources. This paper attempts to make a global analysis of aquaculture growth, its role in global food production, and to update the estimates of compound feed dependent ?sh and crustacean species.","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science & Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1080/23308249.2014.987209","ISSN":"2330-8249, 2330-8257","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1-10","source":"Crossref","title":"Feed Matters: Satisfying the Feed Demand of Aquaculture","title-short":"Feed Matters","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23308249.2014.987209","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The total annual production of fish meal was 4.5 million tons, and the total annual production of fish oils was 0.9 million tons in 2016, of which 69% and 75%, respectively, were used in aquafeeds ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ce4VEArO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. Hua, 2019)","plainCitation":"(K. Hua, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8472,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N5ZEJFIZ"],"itemData":{"id":8472,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"One Earth","DOI":"10.1016/j.oneear.2019.10.018","ISSN":"25903322","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"316-329","source":"Crossref","title":"The Future of Aquatic Protein: Implications for Protein Sources in Aquaculture Diets","title-short":"The Future of Aquatic Protein","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2590332219301320","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Hua","given":"Katheline"},{"family":"Cobcroft","given":"Jennifer M."},{"family":"Cole","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Condon","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Jerry","given":"Dean R."},{"family":"Mangott","given":"Arnold"},{"family":"Praeger","given":"Christina"},{"family":"Vucko","given":"Matthew J."},{"family":"Zeng","given":"Chaoshu"},{"family":"Zenger","given":"Kyall"},{"family":"Strugnell","given":"Jan M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",11]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An additional 23% and 5% of this fish meal is used in pig and chicken feeds. The aquaculture industry is making important gains in improving feed conversion ratios, reducing the inclusion of fishmeal in feed and developing substitutes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1f0ecvfhp8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2016b; Klinger & Naylor, 2012; R. L. Naylor, 2009)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2016b; Klinger & Naylor, 2012; R. L. Naylor, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1251,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QC88ITIT"],"itemData":{"id":1251,"type":"book","abstract":"FAO (2016). The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2016. Contributing\\nto food security and nutrition for all. Rome.","publisher":"FAO, Rome.","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2016. Contributing to food security and nutrition for all","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":1037,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H7BRRMYZ"],"itemData":{"id":1037,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Aquaculture is currently the fastest growing animal food production sector and will soon supply more than half of the world’s seafood for human consumption. Continued growth in aquaculture production is likely to come from intensi?cation of ?sh, shell?sh, and algae production. Intensi?cation is often accompanied by a range of resource and environmental problems. We review several potential solutions to these problems, including novel culture systems, alternative feed strategies, and species choices. We examine the problems addressed; the stage of adoption; and the bene?ts, costs, and constraints of each solution. Policies that provide incentives for innovation and environmental improvement are also explored. We end the review by identifying easily adoptable solutions and promising technologies worth further investment.","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-021111-161531","ISSN":"1543-5938, 1545-2050","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"247-276","source":"Crossref","title":"Searching for Solutions in Aquaculture: Charting a Sustainable Course","title-short":"Searching for Solutions in Aquaculture","URL":"http://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev-environ-021111-161531","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Klinger","given":"Dane"},{"family":"Naylor","given":"Rosamond"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",11,21]]}}},{"id":1041,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QERIRVTF"],"itemData":{"id":1041,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.0905235106","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"36","language":"en","page":"15103-15110","source":"Crossref","title":"Feeding aquaculture in an era of finite resources","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/doi/10.1073/pnas.0905235106","volume":"106","author":[{"family":"Naylor","given":"R. L."},{"family":"Hardy","given":"R. W."},{"family":"Bureau","given":"D. P."},{"family":"Chiu","given":"A."},{"family":"Elliott","given":"M."},{"family":"Farrell","given":"A. P."},{"family":"Forster","given":"I."},{"family":"Gatlin","given":"D. M."},{"family":"Goldburg","given":"R. J."},{"family":"Hua","given":"K."},{"family":"Nichols","given":"P. D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",9,8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nonetheless, the use of wild fish for feed by the aquaculture sector is increasing as a result of overall growth, intensification of farming practices, and from the rising share of higher trophic level species in total production menhaden ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gBWnpq9X","properties":{"formattedCitation":"\\\\uldash{(Tacon, Hasan, & Metian, 2011; Tacon & Metian, 2008b, 2008a, 2015)}","plainCitation":"(Tacon, Hasan, & Metian, 2011; Tacon & Metian, 2008b, 2008a, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1042,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H8VYXQVK"],"itemData":{"id":1042,"type":"book","abstract":"The rise into global prominence and rapid growth of finfish and crustacean aquaculture has been due, in part, to the availability and on-farm provision of feed inputs within the major producing countries. More than 46 percent of the total global aquaculture production in 2008 was dependent upon the supply of external feed inputs. For the aquaculture sector to maintain its current average growth rate of 8 to 10 percent per year to 2025, the supply of nutrient and feed inputs will have to grow at a similar rate. This had been readily attainable when the industry was young. It may not be the case anymore as the sector has grown into a major consumer of and competitor for feed resources. This paper reviews the dietary feeding practices employed for the production of the major cultured fed species, the total global production and market availability of the major feed ingredient sources used and the major constraints to feed ingredient usage, and recommends approaches to feed ingredient selection and usage for the major species of cultivated fish and crustacean. Emphasis is placed on the need for major producing countries to maximize the use of locally available feed-grade ingredient sources, and, in particular, to select and use those nutritionally sound and safe feed ingredient sources whose production and growth can keep pace with the 8 to 10 percent annual average annual growth of the fed finfish and crustacean aquaculture sector","call-number":"SH156 .T326 2011","collection-number":"564","collection-title":"FAO fisheries and aquaculture technical paper","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-106933-2","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn792750597","number-of-pages":"87","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Demand and supply of feed ingredients for farmed fish and crustaceans","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Hasan","given":"Mohammad R."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33788,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2NTQX6BN"],"itemData":{"id":33788,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The ?n?sh and crustacean aquaculture sector is still highly dependent upon marine capture ?sheries for sourcing key dietary nutrient inputs, including ?sh meal and ?sh oil. This dependency is particularly strong within compound aquafeeds for farmed carnivorous ?n?sh species and marine shrimp.","container-title":"Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1016/j.aquaculture.2008.08.015","ISSN":"00448486","issue":"1-4","language":"en","page":"146-158","source":"Crossref","title":"Global overview on the use of fish meal and fish oil in industrially compounded aquafeeds: Trends and future prospects","title-short":"Global overview on the use of fish meal and fish oil in industrially compounded aquafeeds","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S004484860800567X","volume":"285","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G.J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",12]]}}},{"id":1043,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BFLIQW2E"],"itemData":{"id":1043,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1196/annals.1454.003","ISSN":"00778923, 17496632","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"50-59","source":"Crossref","title":"Aquaculture Feed and Food Safety","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1196/annals.1454.003","volume":"1140","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",10]]}}},{"id":33789,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LGDYCGI4"],"itemData":{"id":33789,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The rise of aquaculture has attracted a great deal of attention and this has increased since the sector is now providing more ?sh and crustaceans than capture ?sheries. This global prominence has been partly facilitated by the availability and onfarm provision of feed inputs within the major aquaculture producing countries. More than 70% of the total global aquaculture production is dependent upon the supply of external feed inputs. For the aquaculture sector to maintain its current growth rate, the supply of nutrient and feed inputs will have to grow at a similar rate, while aquatic ingredients production remains static and other sectors compete for same feed resources. This paper attempts to make a global analysis of aquaculture growth, its role in global food production, and to update the estimates of compound feed dependent ?sh and crustacean species.","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science & Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1080/23308249.2014.987209","ISSN":"2330-8249, 2330-8257","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1-10","source":"Crossref","title":"Feed Matters: Satisfying the Feed Demand of Aquaculture","title-short":"Feed Matters","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23308249.2014.987209","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}(Figure 3.33a).
	Forage fish have been captured and reduced into fishmeal and oil for decades (reduction fisheries), supporting production of terrestrially farmed species, such as pigs and poultry. Aquaculture did not become the dominant user of rendered forage fish until the 2000s, well after global catches of forage fish had plateaued ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a13ap5u83aa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. J. Shepherd & Jackson, 2013)","plainCitation":"(C. J. Shepherd & Jackson, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1029,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J9LYZKPN"],"itemData":{"id":1029,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.12224","ISSN":"00221112","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"1046-1066","source":"Crossref","title":"Global fishmeal and fish-oil supply: inputs, outputs and markets <sup>a</sup>: global production of fishmeal and fish-oil","title-short":"Global fishmeal and fish-oil supply","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/jfb.12224","volume":"83","author":[{"family":"Shepherd","given":"C. J."},{"family":"Jackson","given":"A. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These pelagic species now help support over 70% of aquaculture production ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2odloatj4b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2016b; Tacon & Metian, 2015)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2016b; Tacon & Metian, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1251,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QC88ITIT"],"itemData":{"id":1251,"type":"book","abstract":"FAO (2016). The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2016. Contributing\\nto food security and nutrition for all. Rome.","publisher":"FAO, Rome.","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2016. Contributing to food security and nutrition for all","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33789,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LGDYCGI4"],"itemData":{"id":33789,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The rise of aquaculture has attracted a great deal of attention and this has increased since the sector is now providing more ?sh and crustaceans than capture ?sheries. This global prominence has been partly facilitated by the availability and onfarm provision of feed inputs within the major aquaculture producing countries. More than 70% of the total global aquaculture production is dependent upon the supply of external feed inputs. For the aquaculture sector to maintain its current growth rate, the supply of nutrient and feed inputs will have to grow at a similar rate, while aquatic ingredients production remains static and other sectors compete for same feed resources. This paper attempts to make a global analysis of aquaculture growth, its role in global food production, and to update the estimates of compound feed dependent ?sh and crustacean species.","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science & Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1080/23308249.2014.987209","ISSN":"2330-8249, 2330-8257","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1-10","source":"Crossref","title":"Feed Matters: Satisfying the Feed Demand of Aquaculture","title-short":"Feed Matters","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23308249.2014.987209","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} acting as feed for carnivorous species (for example, salmon, tuna) and increasingly non-obligate carnivores (for example, carps, shrimp) alike ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1jl7eso234","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tacon & Metian, 2008b, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Tacon & Metian, 2008b, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33788,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2NTQX6BN"],"itemData":{"id":33788,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The ?n?sh and crustacean aquaculture sector is still highly dependent upon marine capture ?sheries for sourcing key dietary nutrient inputs, including ?sh meal and ?sh oil. This dependency is particularly strong within compound aquafeeds for farmed carnivorous ?n?sh species and marine shrimp.","container-title":"Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1016/j.aquaculture.2008.08.015","ISSN":"00448486","issue":"1-4","language":"en","page":"146-158","source":"Crossref","title":"Global overview on the use of fish meal and fish oil in industrially compounded aquafeeds: Trends and future prospects","title-short":"Global overview on the use of fish meal and fish oil in industrially compounded aquafeeds","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S004484860800567X","volume":"285","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G.J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",12]]}}},{"id":33789,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LGDYCGI4"],"itemData":{"id":33789,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The rise of aquaculture has attracted a great deal of attention and this has increased since the sector is now providing more ?sh and crustaceans than capture ?sheries. This global prominence has been partly facilitated by the availability and onfarm provision of feed inputs within the major aquaculture producing countries. More than 70% of the total global aquaculture production is dependent upon the supply of external feed inputs. For the aquaculture sector to maintain its current growth rate, the supply of nutrient and feed inputs will have to grow at a similar rate, while aquatic ingredients production remains static and other sectors compete for same feed resources. This paper attempts to make a global analysis of aquaculture growth, its role in global food production, and to update the estimates of compound feed dependent ?sh and crustacean species.","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science & Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1080/23308249.2014.987209","ISSN":"2330-8249, 2330-8257","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1-10","source":"Crossref","title":"Feed Matters: Satisfying the Feed Demand of Aquaculture","title-short":"Feed Matters","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23308249.2014.987209","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The added demand from the rapid growth of aquaculture resulted in terrestrial husbandry substituting forage fish with alternative feed sources, reducing fishmeal and oil use by pigs and poultry to roughly 25% of total forage fish use (Figure 3.33b). 


Figure 3.33 Animal production (livestock, poultry and fed aquaculture species) and forage fish use trends. (a) Time-series of biomass of primary farmed animal groups (poultry and pig, fed aquaculture) that consume forage fish. (b) Proportion of fishmeal use by the primary consumer groups over time. (c) Proportion (mean ± 95% confidence interval) of forage fish in global feed relative to maximum fishmeal price per year (see color scale). (d) Difference in proportion of inclusion in total animal feed over time compared to 1961 for crops, forage fish (also depicted in panel c), other aquatic inputs and terrestrial meat-sourced contributions (by-products) in livestock and aquaculture feed, combined. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a277r9nhj4b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Froehlich, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Froehlich, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1259,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S69FI9FA"],"itemData":{"id":1259,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Sustainability","DOI":"10.1038/s41893-018-0077-1","ISSN":"2398-9629","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"298-303","source":"Crossref","title":"Avoiding the ecological limits of forage fish for fed aquaculture","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41893-018-0077-1","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Froehlich","given":"Halley E."},{"family":"Jacobsen","given":"Nis Sand"},{"family":"Essington","given":"Timothy E."},{"family":"Clavelle","given":"Tyler"},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} © 2018, Springer Nature under license CC BY-NC-SA 4.0.

      To date, two factors have helped avoid resource limitations of forage fish affecting aquaculture growth. First, forage fish have become an increasingly smaller fraction of fish feed inputs over the decades, driven in part by price (Figure 3.33c). Most aquaculture (and agricultural) feed is now largely crop-based (for example, soy), and this trend continues to increase (Figure 3.33d). Additionally, some countries use trimmings (fish by-products) from aquaculture and fisheries, as well as other aquatic species, as forage fish alternatives (Figure 3.33d). Second, aquaculture of selected species is continuously becoming more efficient, as measured by feed conversion ratios. Together, these factors contribute to lower fish-in-fish-out ratios (weight of forage fish used relative to fed cultured species produced). 
      The issue of fishmeal and oil use from aquaculture is continuing to raise diverging views and the sustainability of such practices remains dispersed in the literature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1rsbavq440","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Natale, Hofherr, Fiore, & Virtanen, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Natale, Hofherr, Fiore, & Virtanen, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1039,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3L32K6D9"],"itemData":{"id":1039,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Aquaculture is increasingly interacting with ?sheries from a socio-economic perspective at the level of the global food market. In addition, since 36% of landings from ?sheries are used for the production of ?shmeal and ?sh oil, which are essential feed elements for many aquaculture species, another source of interaction is at the level of the aqua feed market. The relevance of the interactions between the two sectors is becoming more evident as the process of transition from ?shing to farming has reached a pivotal point, with almost 50% of the ?sh food supply now coming from aquaculture. The analyses on aquaculture and ?sheries interactions often result in diverging views. This paper reviews the main positions in the recent scienti?c literature, structuring the discussion along the following themes: food market interactions; comparison of resources use by means of Life Cycle Assessment; use of ?shmeal and ?sh oil and aqua feed markets; impacts on small pelagic ?sh stocks; food security; technical substitution of ?shmeal and ?sh oil with alternative ingredients. The review is not aimed at taking a de?nitive position in respect of these complex issues but rather at summarising the different scienti?c contributions and indicating possible areas for further research. Research on ?sheries and aquaculture interactions is considered bene?cial in terms of policy support especially now that the reform of European Common Fishery Policy is assigning greater relevance to the growth of aquaculture and to its role in terms of socio-economic development for coastal and rural communities. In a more global perspective, the understanding of interactions between aquaculture and ?sheries is important to evaluate the respective contributions to food security goals and the implications in terms of environmental sustainability and resource use ef?ciency.","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2012.05.037","ISSN":"0308597X","language":"en","page":"205-213","source":"Crossref","title":"Interactions between aquaculture and fisheries","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X12001352","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Natale","given":"Fabrizio"},{"family":"Hofherr","given":"Johann"},{"family":"Fiore","given":"Gianluca"},{"family":"Virtanen","given":"Jarno"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Cashion ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1u8q9q446c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cashion, Le Manach, Zeller, & Pauly, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Cashion, Le Manach, Zeller, & Pauly, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1035,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I8ASNFBI"],"itemData":{"id":1035,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Marine fisheries target and catch fish both for direct human consumption (DHC) as well as for fishmeal and fish oil, and other products. We derived the fractions used for each for 1950–2010 by fishing country, and thus provide a factual foundation for discussions of the optimal use of fisheries resources. From 1950 to 2010, 27% (~20 million tonnes annually) of globally reconstructed marine fisheries landings were destined for uses other than DHC. Importantly, 90% of fish destined for uses other than DHC are food-­grade or prime food-­grade fish, while fish without a ready market for DHC make up a much smaller proportion. These findings have implications for how we are using fish to feed ourselves or, more appropriately, how we are not using fish to feed ourselves.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12209","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"837-844","source":"Crossref","title":"Most fish destined for fishmeal production are food-grade fish","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/faf.12209","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Cashion","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Le Manach","given":"Frédéric"},{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} underscore the concerns around directing ~20 million tons of wild fish every year towards feeding farmed fish, pigs and chickens instead of humans ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"auuhn892vv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Belton & Thilsted, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Belton & Thilsted, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1263,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WXYTX95T"],"itemData":{"id":1263,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fisheries and ?sh supply are undergoing a fundamental structural transition, as indicated by a ten country analysis. Aquaculture now provides around half the ?sh for direct human consumption and is set to grow further, but capture ?sheries continue to make essential contributions to food and nutrition security throughout the global South. Capture ?sheries provide diverse, nutritionally valuable ?sh and ?sh products which are often culturally preferred and easily accessed by the poor. Technological changes in aquaculture have dramatically increased ?sh supply, lowered relative ?sh prices, and reigned in price volatility. Policies that recognize and safeguard the diversity and complementarity of roles played by capture ?sheries and aquaculture are needed to ensure that the transition in ?sheries sustainably improves food and nutrition security in the global South.","container-title":"Global Food Security","DOI":"10.1016/j.gfs.2013.10.001","ISSN":"22119124","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"59-66","source":"Crossref","title":"Fisheries in transition: Food and nutrition security implications for the global South","title-short":"Fisheries in transition","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2211912413000515","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Belton","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"Shakuntala Haraksingh"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Importantly, 90% of fish destined for uses other than direct human consumption are food-­grade or prime food-grade fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VzaowRf9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cashion, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Cashion, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1035,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I8ASNFBI"],"itemData":{"id":1035,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Marine fisheries target and catch fish both for direct human consumption (DHC) as well as for fishmeal and fish oil, and other products. We derived the fractions used for each for 1950–2010 by fishing country, and thus provide a factual foundation for discussions of the optimal use of fisheries resources. From 1950 to 2010, 27% (~20 million tonnes annually) of globally reconstructed marine fisheries landings were destined for uses other than DHC. Importantly, 90% of fish destined for uses other than DHC are food-­grade or prime food-­grade fish, while fish without a ready market for DHC make up a much smaller proportion. These findings have implications for how we are using fish to feed ourselves or, more appropriately, how we are not using fish to feed ourselves.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12209","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"837-844","source":"Crossref","title":"Most fish destined for fishmeal production are food-grade fish","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/faf.12209","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Cashion","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Le Manach","given":"Frédéric"},{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Tacon & Metian ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a22b31toqip","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tacon & Metian, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Tacon & Metian, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1040,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EWBZZJAF"],"itemData":{"id":1040,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In a world where nearly 30% of humanity is suffering from malnutrition and over 70% of the planet is covered with water, aquatic foods represent an essential component of the global food basket to improve the nutrition, health, and well being of all peoples. It is not by chance that Japan, the country with one of the world’s highest reported life expectancies and lowest incidences of obesity and deaths from heart related illnesses, is also one of the world’s top consumers of captured and farmed aquatic animal food products and aquatic plants. According to the FAO, in 2009, total captured and farmed aquatic animal food products accounted for 16.6% of the global population’s intake of animal protein, providing more than three billion people with almost 20% of their average per capita intake of animal protein, and 4.3 billion people with at least 15% of such protein. This article reviews the nutritional composition of different farmed and captured aquatic food products and compares these with conventional terrestrial meat products. In addition to the superior nutritional pro?le and bene?ts of aquatic animal food products, small-sized marine pelagic ?sh play an important role in the nutrition of the poor as an affordable and much needed source of high quality animal protein and essential amino acids, omega-3 fatty acids, vitamins, minerals, and trace elements. As one of the best aquatic animal foods from a nutritional perspective, the direct consumption of small pelagic ?sh should be encouraged and promoted, as apposed to the continued targeted use of these species for reduction into ?shmeal and ?sh oil for use in animal feeds.","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1080/10641262.2012.753405","ISSN":"1064-1262, 1547-6553","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"22-38","source":"Crossref","title":"Fish Matters: Importance of Aquatic Foods in Human Nutrition and Global Food Supply","title-short":"Fish Matters","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/10641262.2012.753405","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} indicate that feed use of small pelagic fish competes with its use for food especially in developing countries. Much of the literature warns against aquaculture’s reliance on forage fish, citing the fully exploited, over-exploited or recovering status of many forage fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2n95bvn0dd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rosamond L. Naylor, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Rosamond L. Naylor, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1038,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YJD5BGHC"],"itemData":{"id":1038,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Global production of farmed fish and shellfish has more than doubled in the past 15 years. Many people believe that such growth relieves pressure on ocean fisheries, but the opposite is true for some types of aquaculture. Farming carnivorous species requires large inputs of wild fish for feed. Some aquaculture systems also reduce wild fish supplies through habitat modification, wild seedstock collection and other ecological impacts. On balance, global aquaculture production still adds to world fish supplies; however, if the growing aquaculture industry is to sustain its contribution to world fish supplies, it must reduce wild fish inputs in feed and adopt more ecologically sound management practices.","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/35016500","ISSN":"1476-4687","issue":"6790","language":"En","license":"2000 Macmillan Magazines Ltd.","page":"1017","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Effect of aquaculture on world fish supplies","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/35016500","volume":"405","author":[{"family":"Naylor","given":"Rosamond L."},{"family":"Goldburg","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Primavera","given":"Jurgenne H."},{"family":"Kautsky","given":"Nils"},{"family":"Beveridge","given":"Malcolm C. M."},{"family":"Clay","given":"Jason"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Lubchenco","given":"Jane"},{"family":"Mooney","given":"Harold"},{"family":"Troell","given":"Max"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, though the current global amount being extracted appears below maximum sustainable levels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ilfn9djch","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Froehlich, 2018; Hilborn & Costello, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Froehlich, 2018; Hilborn & Costello, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1259,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S69FI9FA"],"itemData":{"id":1259,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Sustainability","DOI":"10.1038/s41893-018-0077-1","ISSN":"2398-9629","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"298-303","source":"Crossref","title":"Avoiding the ecological limits of forage fish for fed aquaculture","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41893-018-0077-1","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Froehlich","given":"Halley E."},{"family":"Jacobsen","given":"Nis Sand"},{"family":"Essington","given":"Timothy E."},{"family":"Clavelle","given":"Tyler"},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6]]}}},{"id":1036,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CWMJZSEI"],"itemData":{"id":1036,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The oceans provide food, employment and income for billions of people. We analyzed data from scienti?c stock assessments, and from a statistical model for other ?sh stocks, to summarize the past and present status, and the potential catch, abundance and pro?t for 4713 ?sh stocks constituting 78% of global ?sheries. Three major scenarios of future trends are considered; business as usual (BAU) in which largely unmanaged ?sheries move towards bioeconomic equilibrium but where well-managed ?sheries maintain their management, maximum sustainable yield (MSY) in which ?sheries are managed to maximize yield, and ?sheries reform (REF) where the competitive race to ?sh is eliminated and ?sheries are managed to maximize pro?t. The future prospects di?er greatly based on region of the world and product type. This analysis forecasts that yield in major tuna and forage ?sh species will remain roughly the same as current levels under all three scenarios, while there does appear to be potential for increased yield of white?sh. There is considerable room for increased pro?t in most of these ?sheries from better management. Increased yield will come from rebuilding overexploited stocks, reducing ?shing mortality on stocks that are still abundant but ?shed at high rates, and surprisingly from ?shing some stocks harder. Indeed in Europe and North America the primary potential for increased yield comes from fully exploiting stocks that are now lightly exploited. Asia provides the greatest opportunity for increased ?sh abundance and increased pro?t by ?sheries reform that would lead to reduced ?shing pressure.","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.02.003","ISSN":"0308597X","language":"en","page":"350-355","source":"Crossref","title":"The potential for blue growth in marine fish yield, profit and abundance of fish in the ocean","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X1730074X","volume":"87","author":[{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Costello","given":"Chris"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Switching from feed fish to direct human food would depend upon affordability and development of low-cost conserved products. A regional approach is needed to assess the consequences of using more feed fish for human consumption. While there are possible benefits of switching at least part of the catches of forage fish to food in South American countries, in Asia this is a less clearly understood, since cheap fish and trash fish contribute to the development of small-scale aquaculture, which reportedly has positive effects on livelihood and human consumption. In sub-Saharan Africa the effects would be limited since feed fisheries are an exception and aquaculture is not yet widespread or dependent on compound feed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aeno78bq0d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hasan & Halwart, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Hasan & Halwart, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1017,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZR8NW9TC"],"itemData":{"id":1017,"type":"report","publisher":"FAO, Roma (Italia).","title":"Fish as feed inputs for aquaculture: Practices, sustainability and implications.","author":[{"family":"Hasan","given":"MR"},{"family":"Halwart","given":"M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Nonetheless, the use of wild fish for feed by the aquaculture sector is increasing as a result of overall growth, intensification of farming practices, and the rising share of fed, higher trophic level species in total production  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2aemtck8rt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tacon, 2011; Tacon & Metian, 2008b, 2008a, 2009, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Tacon, 2011; Tacon & Metian, 2008b, 2008a, 2009, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1042,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H8VYXQVK"],"itemData":{"id":1042,"type":"book","abstract":"The rise into global prominence and rapid growth of finfish and crustacean aquaculture has been due, in part, to the availability and on-farm provision of feed inputs within the major producing countries. More than 46 percent of the total global aquaculture production in 2008 was dependent upon the supply of external feed inputs. For the aquaculture sector to maintain its current average growth rate of 8 to 10 percent per year to 2025, the supply of nutrient and feed inputs will have to grow at a similar rate. This had been readily attainable when the industry was young. It may not be the case anymore as the sector has grown into a major consumer of and competitor for feed resources. This paper reviews the dietary feeding practices employed for the production of the major cultured fed species, the total global production and market availability of the major feed ingredient sources used and the major constraints to feed ingredient usage, and recommends approaches to feed ingredient selection and usage for the major species of cultivated fish and crustacean. Emphasis is placed on the need for major producing countries to maximize the use of locally available feed-grade ingredient sources, and, in particular, to select and use those nutritionally sound and safe feed ingredient sources whose production and growth can keep pace with the 8 to 10 percent annual average annual growth of the fed finfish and crustacean aquaculture sector","call-number":"SH156 .T326 2011","collection-number":"564","collection-title":"FAO fisheries and aquaculture technical paper","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-106933-2","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn792750597","number-of-pages":"87","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Demand and supply of feed ingredients for farmed fish and crustaceans","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Hasan","given":"Mohammad R."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33788,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2NTQX6BN"],"itemData":{"id":33788,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The ?n?sh and crustacean aquaculture sector is still highly dependent upon marine capture ?sheries for sourcing key dietary nutrient inputs, including ?sh meal and ?sh oil. This dependency is particularly strong within compound aquafeeds for farmed carnivorous ?n?sh species and marine shrimp.","container-title":"Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1016/j.aquaculture.2008.08.015","ISSN":"00448486","issue":"1-4","language":"en","page":"146-158","source":"Crossref","title":"Global overview on the use of fish meal and fish oil in industrially compounded aquafeeds: Trends and future prospects","title-short":"Global overview on the use of fish meal and fish oil in industrially compounded aquafeeds","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S004484860800567X","volume":"285","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G.J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",12]]}}},{"id":1043,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BFLIQW2E"],"itemData":{"id":1043,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1196/annals.1454.003","ISSN":"00778923, 17496632","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"50-59","source":"Crossref","title":"Aquaculture Feed and Food Safety","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1196/annals.1454.003","volume":"1140","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",10]]}}},{"id":33790,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TDJJGRG7"],"itemData":{"id":33790,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1080/10641260802677074","ISSN":"1064-1262, 1547-6553","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"305-317","source":"Crossref","title":"Fishing for Aquaculture: Non-Food Use of Small Pelagic Forage Fish—A Global Perspective","title-short":"Fishing for Aquaculture","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10641260802677074","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",6,17]]}}},{"id":33789,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LGDYCGI4"],"itemData":{"id":33789,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The rise of aquaculture has attracted a great deal of attention and this has increased since the sector is now providing more ?sh and crustaceans than capture ?sheries. This global prominence has been partly facilitated by the availability and onfarm provision of feed inputs within the major aquaculture producing countries. More than 70% of the total global aquaculture production is dependent upon the supply of external feed inputs. For the aquaculture sector to maintain its current growth rate, the supply of nutrient and feed inputs will have to grow at a similar rate, while aquatic ingredients production remains static and other sectors compete for same feed resources. This paper attempts to make a global analysis of aquaculture growth, its role in global food production, and to update the estimates of compound feed dependent ?sh and crustacean species.","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science & Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1080/23308249.2014.987209","ISSN":"2330-8249, 2330-8257","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1-10","source":"Crossref","title":"Feed Matters: Satisfying the Feed Demand of Aquaculture","title-short":"Feed Matters","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23308249.2014.987209","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Tacon","given":"Albert G. J."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Furthermore, fishmeal and oil are also used in terrestrial livestock feed, and their demand is increasing for pet food, and human food and medicine. Given current trends in aquaculture and demand for seafood and terrestrial meat, estimates suggest that ecological limits of forage fish could be reached as soon as 2037, or even sooner if precautionary measures do not further limit access to the wild resource (e.g., Atlantic herring, Clupea harengus, Clupeidae) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2mm1hmmu3j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Froehlich, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Froehlich, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1259,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S69FI9FA"],"itemData":{"id":1259,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Sustainability","DOI":"10.1038/s41893-018-0077-1","ISSN":"2398-9629","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"298-303","source":"Crossref","title":"Avoiding the ecological limits of forage fish for fed aquaculture","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41893-018-0077-1","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Froehlich","given":"Halley E."},{"family":"Jacobsen","given":"Nis Sand"},{"family":"Essington","given":"Timothy E."},{"family":"Clavelle","given":"Tyler"},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Small Scale Fisheries contributing to Food and feed uses
This section was written following the methods used for the systematic review described in 3.3.1.3. (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651).

* Europe
Small-scale fishing is still the most important component of commercial fishing in the European Union with special relevance in Southern Europe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a22amvk1488","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Lloret, 2018)","plainCitation":"(J. Lloret, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":392,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4LPJBJKI"],"itemData":{"id":392,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2016.11.007","page":"176-186","title":"Small-scale coastal fisheries in European Seas are not what they were: Ecological, social and economic changes","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85007195924&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2016.11.007&partnerID=40&md5=7099a107b633d84556c70468ec43c71b","volume":"98","author":[{"family":"Lloret","given":"J."},{"family":"Cowx","given":"I.G."},{"family":"Cabral","given":"H."},{"family":"Castro","given":"M."},{"family":"Font","given":"T."},{"family":"Gonçalves","given":"J.M.S."},{"family":"Gordoa","given":"A."},{"family":"Hoefnagel","given":"E."},{"family":"Mati?-Skoko","given":"S."},{"family":"Mikkelsen","given":"E."},{"family":"Morales-Nin","given":"B."},{"family":"Moutopoulos","given":"D.K."},{"family":"Muñoz","given":"M."},{"family":"Santos","given":"M.N.","non-dropping-particle":"dos"},{"family":"Pintassilgo","given":"P."},{"family":"Pita","given":"C."},{"family":"Stergiou","given":"K.I."},{"family":"Ünal","given":"V."},{"family":"Veiga","given":"P."},{"family":"Erzini","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is a highly diversified fishery, involving fishing systems of many forms and sizes, and targeting a wide range of taxonomic groups. Small-scale fisheries in Europe are responsible for a catch equivalent to one large-scale fishery when it comes to human consumption ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CixW9cbT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Leleu, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Leleu, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31521,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XLGNPZN8"],"itemData":{"id":31521,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2014.02.006","page":"93-101","title":"Métiers, effort and catches of a Mediterranean small-scale coastal fishery: The case of the CÔte Bleue Marine Park","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84896038880&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2014.02.006&partnerID=40&md5=d244730e67af2e4a12134ae037326074","volume":"154","author":[{"family":"Leleu","given":"K."},{"family":"Pelletier","given":"D."},{"family":"Charbonnel","given":"E."},{"family":"Letourneur","given":"Y."},{"family":"Alban","given":"F."},{"family":"Bachet","given":"F."},{"family":"Boudouresque","given":"C.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Traditional European small-scale fisheries are more than 2,000 years old ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1t19g0bbti","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Antunes, 2015)","plainCitation":"(C. Antunes, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31782,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C4RFSYFY"],"itemData":{"id":31782,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Freshwater Fisheries Ecology","event-place":"Oxford, UK","ISBN":"978-1-118-39442-7","note":"DOI: 10.1002/9781118394380.ch23","page":"268-282","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","publisher-place":"Oxford, UK","title":"Iberian inland fisheries","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84984844857&doi=10.1002%2f9781118394380.ch23&partnerID=40&md5=c6a7af8f3dd1d871e5423260a9b0aae9","author":[{"family":"Antunes","given":"C."},{"family":"Cobo","given":"F."},{"family":"Araújo","given":"M.J."}],"editor":[{"family":"Craig","given":"J.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and data on some fisheries can be found as far back as 400 years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"25cKL3Wm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Marcos, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Marcos, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31789,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EG2KICEF"],"itemData":{"id":31789,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1007/s11160-015-9397-7","issue":"4","page":"689-713","title":"Long term evolution of fisheries in a coastal lagoon related to changes in lagoon ecology and human pressures","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84946496133&doi=10.1007%2fs11160-015-9397-7&partnerID=40&md5=984fc1cd13dad2c2b46957e1109be817","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Marcos","given":"C."},{"family":"Torres","given":"I."},{"family":"López-Capel","given":"A."},{"family":"Pérez-Ruzafa","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} . Stocks of small pelagic fish species and of larger demersal fish species have been exploited since the Middle Ages and are still important today ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mKjmcpVg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Almeida, 2014; Bastari, 2017; Battaglia, 2017; Braga, Azeiteiro, Oliveira, & Pardal, 2017b)","plainCitation":"(Almeida, 2014; Bastari, 2017; Battaglia, 2017; Braga, Azeiteiro, Oliveira, & Pardal, 2017b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31800,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZAYFLGJM"],"itemData":{"id":31800,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Life Cycle Assessment","DOI":"10.1007/s11367-013-0646-5","issue":"2","page":"297-306","title":"Environmental assessment of sardine (Sardina pilchardus) purse seine fishery in Portugal with LCA methodology including bio