* Africa	
It is well established that most of the non-artisanal small-scale fishing in Africa is unsustainable (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). This general statement applies equally to inland, coastal and marine/oceanic fishing.
	Small species dominate the African fishing practice for both food and feed that involves the inland and coastal fisheries driven by tradition, species displacement, and substitution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aju6924pji","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jamu, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Jamu, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31528,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9LAI5MQ5"],"itemData":{"id":31528,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Journal of Great Lakes Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.jglr.2010.11.017","issue":"SUPPL. 1","page":"3-14","title":"Challenges to sustainable management of the lakes of Malawi","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-79952695965&doi=10.1016%2fj.jglr.2010.11.017&partnerID=40&md5=13fdc8fe7d639007c33e2f09e241cb28","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Jamu","given":"D."},{"family":"Banda","given":"M."},{"family":"Njaya","given":"F."},{"family":"Hecky","given":"R.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A transition from large piscivorous species to small omnivorous species took place during the last half century, when larger fish were almost extinguished from the catch as a result of increasing numbers of fishers, fishing fleets and gear efficiency. A limited number of high value species continue to be targeted, often for export and for higher prices in international markets. There are many cases in the literature reporting on local problems with foreign industrial fleets competing with small-scale fleets, and both affect the artisanal fishing systems. Consequently, these systems show many indicators of unsustainability, such as declining stocks, catches, average size of fishes, catch-per-unit-of-effort, and so on (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Many official fish landing data series available are underestimated, and important attempts for reconstruction are taking place. Researchers have tried to uncover records of unregulated artisanal catches to produce realistic series of data. These reconstructed series also show the same declining trends.
	In comparison with commercial fleet fisheries, artisanal fisheries present more diverse scenarios and different trends (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Some artisanal fishery systems show signs of reduction of catch volume and size over the decades due to excessive fishing pressures ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1bthrdrotv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dyhia Belhabib, 2018; Tuda & Wolff, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Dyhia Belhabib, 2018; Tuda & Wolff, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9797,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4QTF5WQY","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NIYKV52V"],"itemData":{"id":9797,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12360","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"e12360","title":"Trends in industrial and artisanal catch per effort in West African fisheries","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Belhabib","given":"Dyhia"},{"family":"Greer","given":"Krista"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31907,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9ZWSGWJ4"],"itemData":{"id":31907,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2014.11.016","page":"36-44","title":"Evolving trends in the Kenyan artisanal reef fishery and its implications for fisheries management","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84920063117&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2014.11.016&partnerID=40&md5=42678cd397224f4f999fcfe400b8fbb7","volume":"104","author":[{"family":"Tuda","given":"P.M."},{"family":"Wolff","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, mainly because fishing pressure has stayed high. Control of fishing pressure is, sometimes, an inherent trait of a system. Systems based on indigenous and local knowledge use the available habitats and fishing grounds ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1vooqmhnam","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mirera, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Mirera, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31804,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JVXJTS9D"],"itemData":{"id":31804,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2013.08.002","page":"119-129","title":"Heredity or traditional knowledge: Fishing tactics and dynamics of artisanal mangrove crab (Scylla serrata) fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84883530091&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2013.08.002&partnerID=40&md5=5ca3d6bd021a727acea9e28297b81734","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Mirera","given":"D.O."},{"family":"Ochiewo","given":"J."},{"family":"Munyi","given":"F."},{"family":"Muriuki","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} to distribute fishing pressure among a large number of species, or to focus the pressure on specific cohorts or in specific times ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WtTSwtAK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Musembi, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Musembi, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31534,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8FLQRXIR"],"itemData":{"id":31534,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Journal of the Indian Ocean Region","DOI":"10.1080/19480881.2019.1603608","issue":"2","page":"139-156","title":"Species composition, abundance and fishing methods of small-scale fisheries in the seagrass meadows of Gazi Bay, Kenya","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85065732359&doi=10.1080%2f19480881.2019.1603608&partnerID=40&md5=a6c1bc9e0c0760d4cd987c065d5f1659","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Musembi","given":"P."},{"family":"Fulanda","given":"B."},{"family":"Kairo","given":"J."},{"family":"Githaiga","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2a2ls3d4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Musembi, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Musembi, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31534,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8FLQRXIR"],"itemData":{"id":31534,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Journal of the Indian Ocean Region","DOI":"10.1080/19480881.2019.1603608","issue":"2","page":"139-156","title":"Species composition, abundance and fishing methods of small-scale fisheries in the seagrass meadows of Gazi Bay, Kenya","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85065732359&doi=10.1080%2f19480881.2019.1603608&partnerID=40&md5=a6c1bc9e0c0760d4cd987c065d5f1659","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Musembi","given":"P."},{"family":"Fulanda","given":"B."},{"family":"Kairo","given":"J."},{"family":"Githaiga","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}All of these are effective measures of controlling effort and off-take.

* Latin America
In Latin America, almost all studies (99) analyze the use of fisheries resources as food, either for subsistence, commerce or both. Overall, 15% of these studies report sustainable use, 48% report unsustainable use (exploited populations declining and other sustainability problems), and 37% indicated partially sustainable use (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Considering 76 studies of coastal small-scale fisheries (including oceanic islands, bays and estuaries), about 13% (10 studies) mention sustainable use, while 53% indicate unsustainable fishing (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Among 23 studies on inland or freshwater small-scale fisheries, 22% indicate sustainable and 30% unsustainable uses, whereas the remaining majority of studies point to partially sustainable use, suggesting less data availability for inland fisheries compared to coastal cases (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Sustainable coastal small-scale fisheries examples include co-management systems through territorial rights granted to fishing communities and well-established rules to exploit mainly shellfish, oysters and lobsters in Chile and Mexico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9KgZErMU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u193{}lvarez, 2018; Castilla, Espinosa, Yamashiro, Melo, & Gelcich, 2016; De la Cruz-Gonz\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}lez, 2018; Defeo, 2016; Gelcich, 2017, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Álvarez, 2018; Castilla, Espinosa, Yamashiro, Melo, & Gelcich, 2016; De la Cruz-González, 2018; Defeo, 2016; Gelcich, 2017, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZKJFMIDT"],"itemData":{"id":359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2018.08.029","page":"203-214","title":"Environmental history of Mexican North Pacific fishing communities","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85052975797&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2018.08.029&partnerID=40&md5=7b1889198ba3e82c414b1cbb823188d6","volume":"165","author":[{"family":"Álvarez","given":"P."},{"family":"Espejel","given":"I."},{"family":"Bocco","given":"G."},{"family":"Cariño","given":"M."},{"family":"Seingier","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":1001,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3V97D8HA"],"itemData":{"id":1001,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Journal of Shellfish Research","DOI":"10.2983/035.035.0223","issue":"2","page":"499-506","title":"Telecoupling between Catch, Farming, and International Trade for the Gastropods Concholepas concholepas (Loco) and Haliotis spp. (Abalone)","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84987657083&doi=10.2983%2f035.035.0223&partnerID=40&md5=c1280dbfb5d51bcbf1d1a9de515584ac","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Castilla","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Espinosa","given":"J."},{"family":"Yamashiro","given":"C."},{"family":"Melo","given":"O."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":358,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NAZ4F7DS"],"itemData":{"id":358,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2018.01.008","page":"96-102","title":"Self-empowerment and successful co-management in an artisanal fishing community: Santa Cruz de Miramar, Mexico","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85041435659&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2018.01.008&partnerID=40&md5=2e93d9232595fcefceb19189c999a30c","volume":"154","author":[{"family":"De la Cruz-González","given":"F.J."},{"family":"Patiño-Valencia","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Luna-Raya","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"A.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":34288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MVWWMNWF"],"itemData":{"id":34288,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Co-management (Co-M), de?ned as the sharing of management tasks and responsibilities between governments and local users, is emerging as a powerful institutional arrangement to redress ?sheries paradigm failures, yet long-term assessments of its performance are lacking. A comparative analysis of ?ve smallscale Latin American shell?sheries was conducted to identify factors suggesting success and failure. In Chile, Uruguay and Mexico Co-M produced positive effects, including stabilization of landings at low levels, increase in abundance, CPUE, unit prices and revenues per unit of effort, and reduced interannual variability in several ?shery indicators, particularly in landings. Co-M was successful because it was mainly bottom-up implemented and accompanied by-catch shares (spatial property rights and community quotas). By contrast, Co-M implementation was unable to prevent the collapse of the Galapagos sea cucumber ?shery, as re?ected by a decrease in abundance and CPUE. Negative effects were also observed in the Galapagos spiny lobster ?shery during Co-M implementation. However, recovery was observed in recent years, re?ected in a stabilization of ?shing effort and the highest CPUE and economic revenues observed since the beginning of the Co-M implementation phase. The combined effects of market forces, climate variability and a moratorium on ?shing effort were critical in ?shery recovery. We conclude that Co-M is not a blueprint that can be applied to all shell?sheries to enhance their governability. These social–ecological systems need to be managed by jointly addressing problems related to the resources, their marine environment and the people targeting them, accounting for their socioeconomic and cultural contexts.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12101","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"176-192","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Co-management in Latin American small-scale shellfisheries: assessment from long-term case studies","title-short":"Co-management in Latin American small-scale shellfisheries","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/faf.12101","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Defeo","given":"Omar"},{"family":"Castrejón","given":"Mauricio"},{"family":"Pérez-Castañeda","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Castilla","given":"Juan C"},{"family":"Gutiérrez","given":"Nicolás L"},{"family":"Essington","given":"Timothy E"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",3]]}}},{"id":364,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KY3M28JI"],"itemData":{"id":364,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Bulletin of Marine Science","DOI":"10.5343/bms.2015.1082","issue":"1","page":"53-67","title":"Fishers' perceptions on the Chilean coastal TURF system after two decades: Problems, benefits, and emerging needs","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85009956088&doi=10.5343%2fbms.2015.1082&partnerID=40&md5=282619c33be9c7354daa0444ec5ce5c2","volume":"93","author":[{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Cinner","given":"J."},{"family":"Donlan","given":"C.J."},{"family":"Tapia-Lewin","given":"S."},{"family":"Godoy","given":"N."},{"family":"Castilla","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":363,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EVV9MAHV"],"itemData":{"id":363,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1012021107","issue":"39","page":"16794-16799","title":"Navigating transformations in governance of Chilean marine coastal resources","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-78049276265&doi=10.1073%2fpnas.1012021107&partnerID=40&md5=0a50cd77256a5b88f2095e7c9f9f915c","volume":"107","author":[{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"T.P."},{"family":"Olsson","given":"P."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"Defeo","given":"O."},{"family":"Fernández","given":"M."},{"family":"Foale","given":"S."},{"family":"Gunderson","given":"L.H."},{"family":"Rodríguez-Sickert","given":"C."},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"M."},{"family":"Steneck","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Castilla","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some of these co-management systems were effective in supporting recovery of resources after a fishery collapse, such as the shellfish Concholepas concholepas in Chile ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1i0u16td2t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Castilla, 2016; Defeo, 2016; Gelcich, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Castilla, 2016; Defeo, 2016; Gelcich, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1001,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3V97D8HA"],"itemData":{"id":1001,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Journal of Shellfish Research","DOI":"10.2983/035.035.0223","issue":"2","page":"499-506","title":"Telecoupling between Catch, Farming, and International Trade for the Gastropods Concholepas concholepas (Loco) and Haliotis spp. (Abalone)","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84987657083&doi=10.2983%2f035.035.0223&partnerID=40&md5=c1280dbfb5d51bcbf1d1a9de515584ac","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Castilla","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Espinosa","given":"J."},{"family":"Yamashiro","given":"C."},{"family":"Melo","given":"O."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":34288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MVWWMNWF"],"itemData":{"id":34288,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Co-management (Co-M), de?ned as the sharing of management tasks and responsibilities between governments and local users, is emerging as a powerful institutional arrangement to redress ?sheries paradigm failures, yet long-term assessments of its performance are lacking. A comparative analysis of ?ve smallscale Latin American shell?sheries was conducted to identify factors suggesting success and failure. In Chile, Uruguay and Mexico Co-M produced positive effects, including stabilization of landings at low levels, increase in abundance, CPUE, unit prices and revenues per unit of effort, and reduced interannual variability in several ?shery indicators, particularly in landings. Co-M was successful because it was mainly bottom-up implemented and accompanied by-catch shares (spatial property rights and community quotas). By contrast, Co-M implementation was unable to prevent the collapse of the Galapagos sea cucumber ?shery, as re?ected by a decrease in abundance and CPUE. Negative effects were also observed in the Galapagos spiny lobster ?shery during Co-M implementation. However, recovery was observed in recent years, re?ected in a stabilization of ?shing effort and the highest CPUE and economic revenues observed since the beginning of the Co-M implementation phase. The combined effects of market forces, climate variability and a moratorium on ?shing effort were critical in ?shery recovery. We conclude that Co-M is not a blueprint that can be applied to all shell?sheries to enhance their governability. These social–ecological systems need to be managed by jointly addressing problems related to the resources, their marine environment and the people targeting them, accounting for their socioeconomic and cultural contexts.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12101","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"176-192","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Co-management in Latin American small-scale shellfisheries: assessment from long-term case studies","title-short":"Co-management in Latin American small-scale shellfisheries","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/faf.12101","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Defeo","given":"Omar"},{"family":"Castrejón","given":"Mauricio"},{"family":"Pérez-Castañeda","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Castilla","given":"Juan C"},{"family":"Gutiérrez","given":"Nicolás L"},{"family":"Essington","given":"Timothy E"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",3]]}}},{"id":363,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EVV9MAHV"],"itemData":{"id":363,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1012021107","issue":"39","page":"16794-16799","title":"Navigating transformations in governance of Chilean marine coastal resources","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-78049276265&doi=10.1073%2fpnas.1012021107&partnerID=40&md5=0a50cd77256a5b88f2095e7c9f9f915c","volume":"107","author":[{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"T.P."},{"family":"Olsson","given":"P."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"Defeo","given":"O."},{"family":"Fernández","given":"M."},{"family":"Foale","given":"S."},{"family":"Gunderson","given":"L.H."},{"family":"Rodríguez-Sickert","given":"C."},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"M."},{"family":"Steneck","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Castilla","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Atrina maura, A. tuberculosa, Pinna rugosa, oyster (Crassostrea iridescens), lobster and fish in Mexico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2qjhgkiso5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u193{}lvarez, 2018; De la Cruz-Gonz\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}lez, 2018; Palacios-Abrantes, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Álvarez, 2018; De la Cruz-González, 2018; Palacios-Abrantes, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZKJFMIDT"],"itemData":{"id":359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2018.08.029","page":"203-214","title":"Environmental history of Mexican North Pacific fishing communities","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85052975797&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2018.08.029&partnerID=40&md5=7b1889198ba3e82c414b1cbb823188d6","volume":"165","author":[{"family":"Álvarez","given":"P."},{"family":"Espejel","given":"I."},{"family":"Bocco","given":"G."},{"family":"Cariño","given":"M."},{"family":"Seingier","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":358,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NAZ4F7DS"],"itemData":{"id":358,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2018.01.008","page":"96-102","title":"Self-empowerment and successful co-management in an artisanal fishing community: Santa Cruz de Miramar, Mexico","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85041435659&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2018.01.008&partnerID=40&md5=2e93d9232595fcefceb19189c999a30c","volume":"154","author":[{"family":"De la Cruz-González","given":"F.J."},{"family":"Patiño-Valencia","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Luna-Raya","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"A.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N8NFP5ZV"],"itemData":{"id":32548,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0209431","issue":"12","title":"Evaluating the bio-economic performance of a Callo de hacha (Atrina maura, Atrina tuberculosa & Pinna rugosa) fishery restoration plan in La Paz, Mexico","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85058775470&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0209431&partnerID=40&md5=0cc5599bb16631c6354484f1b8836f86","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Palacios-Abrantes","given":"J."},{"family":"Herrera-Correal","given":"J."},{"family":"Rodríguez","given":"S."},{"family":"Brunkow","given":"J."},{"family":"Molina","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	In association with small-scale fisheries, a management strategy called “Territorial Users’ Rights Fisheries management” (TURF) has been implemented with varying success in Chile ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a244l9e5l5m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Defeo, 2016; Gelcich, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Defeo, 2016; Gelcich, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MVWWMNWF"],"itemData":{"id":34288,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Co-management (Co-M), de?ned as the sharing of management tasks and responsibilities between governments and local users, is emerging as a powerful institutional arrangement to redress ?sheries paradigm failures, yet long-term assessments of its performance are lacking. A comparative analysis of ?ve smallscale Latin American shell?sheries was conducted to identify factors suggesting success and failure. In Chile, Uruguay and Mexico Co-M produced positive effects, including stabilization of landings at low levels, increase in abundance, CPUE, unit prices and revenues per unit of effort, and reduced interannual variability in several ?shery indicators, particularly in landings. Co-M was successful because it was mainly bottom-up implemented and accompanied by-catch shares (spatial property rights and community quotas). By contrast, Co-M implementation was unable to prevent the collapse of the Galapagos sea cucumber ?shery, as re?ected by a decrease in abundance and CPUE. Negative effects were also observed in the Galapagos spiny lobster ?shery during Co-M implementation. However, recovery was observed in recent years, re?ected in a stabilization of ?shing effort and the highest CPUE and economic revenues observed since the beginning of the Co-M implementation phase. The combined effects of market forces, climate variability and a moratorium on ?shing effort were critical in ?shery recovery. We conclude that Co-M is not a blueprint that can be applied to all shell?sheries to enhance their governability. These social–ecological systems need to be managed by jointly addressing problems related to the resources, their marine environment and the people targeting them, accounting for their socioeconomic and cultural contexts.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12101","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"176-192","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Co-management in Latin American small-scale shellfisheries: assessment from long-term case studies","title-short":"Co-management in Latin American small-scale shellfisheries","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/faf.12101","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Defeo","given":"Omar"},{"family":"Castrejón","given":"Mauricio"},{"family":"Pérez-Castañeda","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Castilla","given":"Juan C"},{"family":"Gutiérrez","given":"Nicolás L"},{"family":"Essington","given":"Timothy E"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",3]]}}},{"id":363,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EVV9MAHV"],"itemData":{"id":363,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1012021107","issue":"39","page":"16794-16799","title":"Navigating transformations in governance of Chilean marine coastal resources","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-78049276265&doi=10.1073%2fpnas.1012021107&partnerID=40&md5=0a50cd77256a5b88f2095e7c9f9f915c","volume":"107","author":[{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"T.P."},{"family":"Olsson","given":"P."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"Defeo","given":"O."},{"family":"Fernández","given":"M."},{"family":"Foale","given":"S."},{"family":"Gunderson","given":"L.H."},{"family":"Rodríguez-Sickert","given":"C."},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"M."},{"family":"Steneck","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Castilla","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, population and catches of the clam (Mesodesma donacium) declined over time after the establishment of a territorial users’ rights fisheries system, causing the collapse of the clam fishery. However, this was at least in part due to management restrictions preventing fishers from moving fishing grounds to cope with natural variability of clam abundance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ynMoHl4G","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aburto & Stotz, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Aburto & Stotz, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":390,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8ZMQRYKF"],"itemData":{"id":390,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2012.10.013","page":"88-98","title":"Learning about TURFs and natural variability: Failure of surf clam management in Chile","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84869869570&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2012.10.013&partnerID=40&md5=4aebaba544b49db1ea191ef249232eda","volume":"71","author":[{"family":"Aburto","given":"J."},{"family":"Stotz","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A study across 500 km of the Chilean coast indicates effects of displacement caused by Territorial Users’ Rights Fisheries, which intensify fishing efforts and thereby reduce shellfish abundance in open access areas which have been reduced in size compared to surrounding areas which have entered into government management. This creates conflict and resource shortages for fishers not engaged Territorial Users’ Rights Fisheries management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ccNCN13C","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garmendia, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Garmendia, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34279,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/29U5ZLPX"],"itemData":{"id":34279,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2021.104425","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"104425","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The use of fishers’ knowledge to assess benthic resource abundance across management regimes in Chilean artisanal fisheries","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X2100035X","volume":"127","author":[{"family":"Garmendia","given":"Vladimir"},{"family":"Subida","given":"Maria Dulce"},{"family":"Aguilar","given":"Ainara"},{"family":"Fernández","given":"Miriam"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Other cases of sustainable coastal small-scale fisheries include two fish species (Paralabrax nebulifer, Caulolatilus princeps) that have been fished sustainably mostly because they are only occasionally fished, when preferable resources are unavailable in Baja California Sur, Mexico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nriw1B8H","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cavieses N\\\\uc0\\\\u250{}\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}ez, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Cavieses Núñez, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31742,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KQ5NL6SP"],"itemData":{"id":31742,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsy065","issue":"6","page":"2088-2096","title":"Deep learning models for the prediction of small-scale fisheries catches: Finfish fishery in the region of \\"bahiá Magadalena-Almejas\\"","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85068175906&doi=10.1093%2ficesjms%2ffsy065&partnerID=40&md5=ce827529a931860d84c977d53567ec81","volume":"75","author":[{"family":"Cavieses Núñez","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Ojeda Ruiz De La Penã","given":"M.Á."},{"family":"Flores Irigollen","given":"A."},{"family":"Rodríguez Rodríguez","given":"M."},{"family":"Jardim","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ytYxFlcf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Duarte, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Duarte, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31522,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UYFV3W2S"],"itemData":{"id":31522,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2017.11.020","page":"119-126","title":"Is it sustainable fishing for Octopus maya Voss and Solis, 1966, during the breeding season using a bait-based fishing technique?","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85037340186&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2017.11.020&partnerID=40&md5=b557a5950e74007aae0a26fe42530559","volume":"199","author":[{"family":"Duarte","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Hernández-Flores","given":"A."},{"family":"Salas","given":"S."},{"family":"Seijo","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}The sustainability of the important fishery of octopus (Octopus maya) in Yucatan (Mexico) is unresolved ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"d44xtqGi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Duarte, 2018; Rosales Raya & Berdugo, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Duarte, 2018; Rosales Raya & Berdugo, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31522,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UYFV3W2S"],"itemData":{"id":31522,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2017.11.020","page":"119-126","title":"Is it sustainable fishing for Octopus maya Voss and Solis, 1966, during the breeding season using a bait-based fishing technique?","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85037340186&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2017.11.020&partnerID=40&md5=b557a5950e74007aae0a26fe42530559","volume":"199","author":[{"family":"Duarte","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Hernández-Flores","given":"A."},{"family":"Salas","given":"S."},{"family":"Seijo","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":9110,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XQK7D7HF"],"itemData":{"id":9110,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Maritime Studies","DOI":"10.1007/s40152-018-0127-3","issue":"1","page":"91-101","title":"Decision Making in the Campeche Maya Octopus fishery in two fishing communities","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85065904885&doi=10.1007%2fs40152-018-0127-3&partnerID=40&md5=feacda4875444d7c225d2e481da4c33e","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Rosales Raya","given":"M.L."},{"family":"Berdugo","given":"J.E.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. From one hand, the regulations, including fishing season, applied over 30 years and the interaction of fishing gear (baits) with the reproductive behavior of parental care without feeding performed by females, of may have contributed to maintain stocks of this octopus, even in face of intense fishing pressure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BqYYZo4y","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Duarte, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Duarte, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31522,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UYFV3W2S"],"itemData":{"id":31522,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2017.11.020","page":"119-126","title":"Is it sustainable fishing for Octopus maya Voss and Solis, 1966, during the breeding season using a bait-based fishing technique?","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85037340186&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2017.11.020&partnerID=40&md5=b557a5950e74007aae0a26fe42530559","volume":"199","author":[{"family":"Duarte","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Hernández-Flores","given":"A."},{"family":"Salas","given":"S."},{"family":"Seijo","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, an increased market demand and search for profit maximization may have pushed fishers to adopt a combination of legal and illegal, furtive, and undeclared fishing tacticts (including diving), which may undermine sustainability and threatens the long-term viability of the octopus' fishery in the Yucatan Peninsula ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mIsLD9XJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rosales Raya & Berdugo, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Rosales Raya & Berdugo, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9110,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XQK7D7HF"],"itemData":{"id":9110,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Maritime Studies","DOI":"10.1007/s40152-018-0127-3","issue":"1","page":"91-101","title":"Decision Making in the Campeche Maya Octopus fishery in two fishing communities","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85065904885&doi=10.1007%2fs40152-018-0127-3&partnerID=40&md5=feacda4875444d7c225d2e481da4c33e","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Rosales Raya","given":"M.L."},{"family":"Berdugo","given":"J.E.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Most of the studies identifying partially sustainable coastal small-scale fisheries include those lacking temporal series of data to estimate species declines, showing distinct trends among exploited species (i.e., some declining, others stable or increasing) or other indicators (e.g., catch volume and size), besides some studies indicating shifts in composition of fished species (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Several studies on coastal finfish fisheries fall in this partially sustainable category in Brazil ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2108soa2nu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barbosa-Filho, 2020a; Damasio, 2015; E. G. Lima, 2016; Renato A.M. Silvano, Nora, Andreoli, Lopes, & Begossi, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Barbosa-Filho, 2020a; Damasio, 2015; E. G. Lima, 2016; Renato A.M. Silvano, Nora, Andreoli, Lopes, & Begossi, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34918,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BAITNNUM"],"itemData":{"id":34918,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2019.105025","title":"Evidence of shifting baseline and Fisher judgment on lane snapper (Lutjanus synagris) management in a Brazilian marine protected area","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85074530717&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2019.105025&partnerID=40&md5=3c216b9020fa44d107f70dc930e50806","volume":"183","author":[{"family":"Barbosa-Filho","given":"M.L.V."},{"family":"Souza","given":"G.B.G.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Lopes","given":"S.D.F."},{"family":"Siciliano","given":"S."},{"family":"Hauser Davis","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Mourão","given":"J.D.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31730,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BJUS6UF3"],"itemData":{"id":31730,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Background In small-scale fishery, information provided by fishers has been useful to complement current and past lack of knowledge on species and environment.\\nMethodology Through interviews, 82 fishers from the largest fishing communities on the north and south borders of a Brazilian northeastern coastal state provided estimates of the catch per unit effort (CPUE) and rank of species abundance of their main target fishes for three time points: current year (2013 at the time of the research), 10, and 20 years past. This information was contrasted to other available data sources: scientific sampling of fish landing (2013), governmental statistics (2003), and information provided by expert fishers (1993), respectively. Principal Findings Fishers were more accurate when reporting information about their maximum CPUE for 2013, but except for three species, which they estimated accurately, fishers overestimated their mean CPUE per species. Fishers were also accurate at establishing ranks of abundance of their main target species for all periods. Fishers' beliefs that fish abundance has not changed over the last 10 years (2003–2013) were corroborated by governmental and scientific landing data.\\nConclusions The comparison between official and formal landing records and fishers' perceptions revealed that fishers are accurate when reporting maximum CPUE, but not when reporting mean CPUE. Moreover, fishers are less precise the less common a species is in their","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0133122","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e0133122","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Matching Fishers’ Knowledge and Landing Data to Overcome Data Missing in Small-Scale Fisheries","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0133122","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Damasio","given":"Ludmila de Melo Alves"},{"family":"Lopes","given":"Priscila F. M."},{"family":"Guariento","given":"Rafael D."},{"family":"Carvalho","given":"Adriana R."}],"editor":[{"family":"Stergiou","given":"Konstantinos I"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",7,15]]}}},{"id":31757,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AV76QX5G"],"itemData":{"id":31757,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","ISSN":"0308-597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","page":"111-120","title":"Fishers’ knowledge indicates short-term temporal changes in the amount and composition of catches in the southwestern Atlantic","volume":"71","author":[{"family":"Lima","given":"Evelyn G"},{"family":"Begossi","given":"Alpina"},{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato AM"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":1023,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5HRF3ESU"],"itemData":{"id":1023,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2016.10.002","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"125-132","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The ‘ghost of past fishing’: Small-scale fisheries and conservation of threatened groupers in subtropical islands","title-short":"The ‘ghost of past fishing’","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X16306261","volume":"75","author":[{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato A.M."},{"family":"Nora","given":"Vinícius"},{"family":"Andreoli","given":"Tainá B."},{"family":"Lopes","given":"Priscila F.M."},{"family":"Begossi","given":"Alpina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Mexico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6b4g1Jhl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Erisman, 2010; Rife, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Erisman, 2010; Rife, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31797,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EEUSS7ZM"],"itemData":{"id":31797,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","ISSN":"0165-7836","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Research","page":"279-288","title":"Seasonal, annual, and long-term trends in commercial fisheries for aggregating reef fishes in the Gulf of California, Mexico","volume":"106","author":[{"family":"Erisman","given":"Brad"},{"family":"Mascarenas","given":"Ismael"},{"family":"Paredes","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Mitcheson","given":"Yvonne Sadovy","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Aburto-Oropeza","given":"Octavio"},{"family":"Hastings","given":"Philip"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31908,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BNCXPVNM"],"itemData":{"id":31908,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of environmental management","ISSN":"0301-4797","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of environmental management","page":"276-283","title":"Long-term effectiveness of a multi-use marine protected area on reef fish assemblages and fisheries landings","volume":"117","author":[{"family":"Rife","given":"Alexis N"},{"family":"Aburto-Oropeza","given":"Octavio"},{"family":"Hastings","given":"Philip A"},{"family":"Erisman","given":"Brad"},{"family":"Ballantyne","given":"Ford"},{"family":"Wielgus","given":"Jeffrey"},{"family":"Sala","given":"Enric"},{"family":"Gerber","given":"Leah"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Colombia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ywwFmVac","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez-Angarita, 2018)","plainCitation":"(López-Angarita, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1033,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VY5682H7"],"itemData":{"id":1033,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2018.00023","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","page":"23","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Winners and Losers in Area-Based Management of a Small-Scale Fishery in the Colombian Pacific","URL":"http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fmars.2018.00023/full","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"López-Angarita","given":"Juliana"},{"family":"Tilley","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Díaz","given":"Juan M."},{"family":"Hawkins","given":"Julie P."},{"family":"Cagua","given":"E. Fernando"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"Callum M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2,6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, besides the fishery for king crab (Lithodes santolla) in Chile ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4duXtD7Z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bozzeda, Mar\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}n, & Nahuelhual, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Bozzeda, Marín, & Nahuelhual, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1010,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z9V7IZ3R"],"itemData":{"id":1010,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Progress in Oceanography","DOI":"10.1016/j.pocean.2018.10.013","page":"64-71","title":"An uncertainty-based decision support tool to evaluate the southern king crab (Lithodes santolla) fishery in a scarce information context","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85056287298&doi=10.1016%2fj.pocean.2018.10.013&partnerID=40&md5=bd13c7ed83ae06953ce157128aabdc71","volume":"174","author":[{"family":"Bozzeda","given":"F."},{"family":"Marín","given":"S.L."},{"family":"Nahuelhual","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some of these partially sustainable cases involve a temporal shift in the exploited fishing resources, in the form of a decline (or even disappearance) in catches of large, slow growing and high valued fish, such as reef predators, coupled with an increase in catches of smaller, fast growing and usually less valued fishery resources, such as shrimp, reef herbivores, or pelagic fish, as indicated in Brazil ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MECFcRnN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Damasio, 2015; A. R. Ribeiro, 2021; C. Zapelini, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Damasio, 2015; A. R. Ribeiro, 2021; C. Zapelini, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31730,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BJUS6UF3"],"itemData":{"id":31730,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Background In small-scale fishery, information provided by fishers has been useful to complement current and past lack of knowledge on species and environment.\\nMethodology Through interviews, 82 fishers from the largest fishing communities on the north and south borders of a Brazilian northeastern coastal state provided estimates of the catch per unit effort (CPUE) and rank of species abundance of their main target fishes for three time points: current year (2013 at the time of the research), 10, and 20 years past. This information was contrasted to other available data sources: scientific sampling of fish landing (2013), governmental statistics (2003), and information provided by expert fishers (1993), respectively. Principal Findings Fishers were more accurate when reporting information about their maximum CPUE for 2013, but except for three species, which they estimated accurately, fishers overestimated their mean CPUE per species. Fishers were also accurate at establishing ranks of abundance of their main target species for all periods. Fishers' beliefs that fish abundance has not changed over the last 10 years (2003–2013) were corroborated by governmental and scientific landing data.\\nConclusions The comparison between official and formal landing records and fishers' perceptions revealed that fishers are accurate when reporting maximum CPUE, but not when reporting mean CPUE. Moreover, fishers are less precise the less common a species is in their","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0133122","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e0133122","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Matching Fishers’ Knowledge and Landing Data to Overcome Data Missing in Small-Scale Fisheries","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0133122","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Damasio","given":"Ludmila de Melo Alves"},{"family":"Lopes","given":"Priscila F. M."},{"family":"Guariento","given":"Rafael D."},{"family":"Carvalho","given":"Adriana R."}],"editor":[{"family":"Stergiou","given":"Konstantinos I"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",7,15]]}}},{"id":31772,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N9LG9VE2"],"itemData":{"id":31772,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2021.105543","ISSN":"09645691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"105543","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers’ ecological knowledge to support conservation of reef fish (groupers) in the tropical Atlantic","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0964569121000284","volume":"204","author":[{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"Ariane R."},{"family":"Damasio","given":"Ludmila M.A."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato A.M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",4]]}}},{"id":31714,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M7YTU3FC"],"itemData":{"id":31714,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.3224","issue":"12","page":"2092-2106","title":"Tracking interactions: Shifting baseline and fisheries networks in the largest Southwestern Atlantic reef system","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85075235046&doi=10.1002%2faqc.3224&partnerID=40&md5=eeaaed583f0f9e4cf22aecb4d48babf4","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Zapelini","given":"C."},{"family":"Bender","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Giglio","given":"V.J."},{"family":"Schiavetti","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Ecuador ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lYDF4JPI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schiller, Alava, Grove, Reck, & Pauly, 2015a)","plainCitation":"(Schiller, Alava, Grove, Reck, & Pauly, 2015a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31901,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M7W55M28"],"itemData":{"id":31901,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2458","issue":"3","page":"431-446","title":"The demise of Darwin's fishes: Evidence of fishing down and illegal shark finning in the Galápagos Islands","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84932196852&doi=10.1002%2faqc.2458&partnerID=40&md5=c12e2c5ccc32c701c0cdabd50d2df948","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Schiller","given":"L."},{"family":"Alava","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Grove","given":"J."},{"family":"Reck","given":"G."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Costa Rica ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citati