g pressure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qcHK6Pik","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ram\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}rez-Amaro & Galv\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}n-Maga\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}a, 2019; Salda\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}a-Ruiz, Sosa-Nishizaki, & Cartamil, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Ramírez-Amaro & Galván-Magaña, 2019; Saldaña-Ruiz, Sosa-Nishizaki, & Cartamil, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31902,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DJLA2RRH"],"itemData":{"id":31902,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2019.02.001","page":"105-116","title":"Effect of gillnet selectivity on elasmobranchs off the northwestern coast of Mexico","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85061381140&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2019.02.001&partnerID=40&md5=e496acea1abf2129c770ddf4c39a51b7","volume":"172","author":[{"family":"Ramírez-Amaro","given":"S."},{"family":"Galván-Magaña","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31898,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZKQNPAIY"],"itemData":{"id":31898,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2017.07.011","page":"116-129","title":"Historical reconstruction of Gulf of California shark fishery landings and species composition, 1939–2014, in a data-poor fishery context","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85024892126&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2017.07.011&partnerID=40&md5=c2ea1ccdaa1d1199b4b5928f39677d1f","volume":"195","author":[{"family":"Saldaña-Ruiz","given":"L.E."},{"family":"Sosa-Nishizaki","given":"O."},{"family":"Cartamil","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Although benthic invertebrates have been usually among the more sustainable fisheries (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651), there are some cases of overexploitation and even fisheries collapses of high valued and easy to catch invertebrates, such as the abalones (Haliotis spp.) or sea cucumbers (Isostichopus badionotus, among other species), in Chile ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"exYiTWmj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}enz-Arroyo & Revollo-Fern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Sáenz-Arroyo & Revollo-Fernández, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31715,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CUBYP332"],"itemData":{"id":31715,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2016.06.006","page":"217-221","title":"Local ecological knowledge concurs with fishing statistics: An example from the abalone fishery in Baja California, Mexico","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84974633250&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2016.06.006&partnerID=40&md5=1f20d0d3a9bff0270b75350ae493a16a","volume":"71","author":[{"family":"Sáenz-Arroyo","given":"A."},{"family":"Revollo-Fernández","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Ecuador ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9HpzyutQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schiller, Alava, Grove, Reck, & Pauly, 2015b)","plainCitation":"(Schiller, Alava, Grove, Reck, & Pauly, 2015b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":28278,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B7ABY33X"],"itemData":{"id":28278,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Scopus","page":"431–446","title":"The demise of Darwin’s fishes: Evidence of fishing down and illegal shark finning in the Galápagos Islands","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Schiller","given":"L."},{"family":"Alava","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Grove","given":"J."},{"family":"Reck","given":"G."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Mexico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DCe02RAt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gamboa-\\\\uc0\\\\u193{}lvarez, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Gamboa-Álvarez, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31812,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XHV2QXF9"],"itemData":{"id":31812,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2019.105011","title":"Rise and decline of the sea cucumber fishery in Campeche Bank, Mexico","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85072996710&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2019.105011&partnerID=40&md5=3da2ed754775df91c7ac83f992a36908","volume":"184","author":[{"family":"Gamboa-Álvarez","given":"M.Á."},{"family":"López-Rocha","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Poot-López","given":"G.R."},{"family":"Aguilar-Perera","given":"A."},{"family":"Villegas-Hernández","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The size and density of the shellfish Queen conch (Lobatus gigas) had declined over a 15-year period in Belize, raising concerns of recruitment or overfishing, but deep water and protected areas may provide a refuge from fishing pressure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pUvxI0N5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tewfik, Babcock, Appeldoorn, & Gibson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Tewfik, Babcock, Appeldoorn, & Gibson, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8829,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q2UFKZNX"],"itemData":{"id":8829,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.3147","issue":"10","page":"1587-1607","title":"Declining size of adults and juvenile harvest threatens sustainability of a tropical gastropod, Lobatus gigas, fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85068157952&doi=10.1002%2faqc.3147&partnerID=40&md5=df0a7327008d3c79da57e34f99bd78e1","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Tewfik","given":"A."},{"family":"Babcock","given":"E.A."},{"family":"Appeldoorn","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Gibson","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Only a few studies were considered to be partially sustainable or unsustainable because of side effects from some fishing practices that would cause habitat damage or by-catch, for example, trawling to catch shrimp ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4ZxMpaW7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. M. Martins, 2018; Rosa, 2011; S\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}nchez-Jim\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}nez, 2019)","plainCitation":"(I. M. Martins, 2018; Rosa, 2011; Sánchez-Jiménez, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1092,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PJANHHJ8"],"itemData":{"id":1092,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2017.10.008","page":"109-116","title":"What fishers’ local ecological knowledge can reveal about the changes in exploited fish catches","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85031758836&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2017.10.008&partnerID=40&md5=17650c8b3de493fbb68729baa0e16c7d","volume":"198","author":[{"family":"Martins","given":"I.M."},{"family":"Medeiros","given":"R.P."},{"family":"Di Domenico","given":"M."},{"family":"Hanazaki","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1002,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AU2DTERU"],"itemData":{"id":1002,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity and Conservation","page":"1951-1971","title":"Fisheries and trade of seahorses in Brazil: historical perspective, current trends, and future directions","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Rosa","given":"Ierecê L"},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"Tacyana PR"},{"family":"Osório","given":"Frederico M"},{"family":"Moraes","given":"Luiz E"},{"family":"Castro","given":"André LC"},{"family":"Barros","given":"Glaura ML"},{"family":"Alves","given":"Rômulo RN"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31912,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4T3UA4EE"],"itemData":{"id":31912,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2019.00126","issue":"MAR","title":"Connecting a trophic model and local ecological knowledge to improve fisheries management: The case of gulf of Nicoya, Costa Rica","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85065447567&doi=10.3389%2ffmars.2019.00126&partnerID=40&md5=cc6b9d8430168b28ab488e8b224ff5d8","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Sánchez-Jiménez","given":"A."},{"family":"Fujitani","given":"M."},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"D."},{"family":"Schlüter","given":"A."},{"family":"Wolff","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, pufferfish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jS4WkDWk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eduardo, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Eduardo, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1012,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EGQNW4GE"],"itemData":{"id":1012,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.3278","title":"Biodiversity, ecology, fisheries, and use and trade of Tetraodontiformes fishes reveal their socio-ecological significance along the tropical Brazilian continental shelf","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85078737222&doi=10.1002%2faqc.3278&partnerID=40&md5=3669cbfecdeeab9f657558d415d3734c","author":[{"family":"Eduardo","given":"L.N."},{"family":"Bertrand","given":"A."},{"family":"Frédou","given":"T."},{"family":"Lira","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Lima","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"B.P."},{"family":"Menard","given":"F."},{"family":"Lucena-Frédou","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or jellyfish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Kj8HUjYU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brotz, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brotz, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31519,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZT6AHU57"],"itemData":{"id":31519,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1007/s11160-016-9445-y","issue":"1","title":"Jellyfish fisheries in the Americas: origin, state of the art, and perspectives on new fishing grounds","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84988429381&doi=10.1007%2fs11160-016-9445-y&partnerID=40&md5=9db4717398ca57dd1b5242882d9ff5ff","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Brotz","given":"L."},{"family":"Schiariti","given":"A."},{"family":"López-Martínez","given":"J."},{"family":"Álvarez-Tello","given":"J."},{"family":"Peggy Hsieh","given":"Y.-H."},{"family":"Jones","given":"R.P."},{"family":"Quiñones","given":"J."},{"family":"Dong","given":"Z."},{"family":"Morandini","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Preciado","given":"M."},{"family":"Laaz","given":"E."},{"family":"Mianzan","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This may be partially due to two factors that may have reduced the number of articles on trawling in this review: first, some or most of these trawling fisheries may be considered to be large-scale to have made it into the small-scale fisheries review. Second, some studies were perhaps not retrieved in a review using search words as ‘sustainable, sustainability, success and increasing’.
	The sustainable cases of inland small-scale fisheries (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651) are mostly related to the successful co-management of the large and valuable commercial fish pirarucu (Arapaima gigas) in the Brazilian Amazon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1v3ieksf5o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Campos-Silva & Peres, 2016; L. Castello, 2009; Petersen, Brum, Rossoni, Silveira, & Castello, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Campos-Silva & Peres, 2016; L. Castello, 2009; Petersen, Brum, Rossoni, Silveira, & Castello, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11559,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PZ8SCRFZ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVPJDIXP"],"itemData":{"id":11559,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep34745","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"34745","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community-based management induces rapid recovery of a high-value tropical freshwater fishery","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/srep34745","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Campos-Silva","given":"João Vitor"},{"family":"Peres","given":"Carlos A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}},{"id":34927,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CYJQYLNZ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TQF9WJ9U"],"itemData":{"id":34927,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-008-9220-5","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"197-209","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Lessons from Integrating Fishers of Arapaima in Small-Scale Fisheries Management at the Mamirauá Reserve, Amazon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-008-9220-5","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Viana","given":"João P."},{"family":"Watkins","given":"Graham"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Luzadis","given":"Valerie A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",2]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1026,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2USVXXEF"],"itemData":{"id":1026,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.12968","ISSN":"00221112","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"241-248","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recovery of <i>Arapaima</i> sp. populations by community-based management in floodplains of the Purus River, Amazon: recovery of <i>arapaima</i> sp. populations","title-short":"Recovery of <i>Arapaima</i> sp. populations by community-based management in floodplains of the Purus River, Amazon","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/jfb.12968","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Petersen","given":"T. A."},{"family":"Brum","given":"S. M"},{"family":"Rossoni","given":"F."},{"family":"Silveira","given":"G. F. V."},{"family":"Castello","given":"L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",7]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, (see also box 6.5 on community-based fishery of pirarucu in the Amazon in Chapter 6). Other mechanisms that could lead to sustainable fisheries are the exploitation of fish resilient to either fishing pressure or environmental change (e.g., dams). For example, the fishing of Plagioscion squamosissimus, especially in communities within extractive reserves or other kinds of protected areas, as observed in several rivers in the Brazilian Amazon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3zr0ozFi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gustavo Hallwass, Lu\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}s Henrique Tomazoni da Silva, Paula Nagl, Mariana Clauzet, & Alpina Begossi, 2020; Gustavo Hallwass, 2011; G. Hallwass, 2020; Gustavo Hallwass & Silvano, 2016; F.W. Keppeler, 2017; Mesquita, 2019; R.A.M. Silvano, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Gustavo Hallwass, Luís Henrique Tomazoni da Silva, Paula Nagl, Mariana Clauzet, & Alpina Begossi, 2020; Gustavo Hallwass, 2011; G. Hallwass, 2020; Gustavo Hallwass & Silvano, 2016; F.W. Keppeler, 2017; Mesquita, 2019; R.A.M. Silvano, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1021,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SE4KUVVR"],"itemData":{"id":1021,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries in the Brazilian Amazon: People, Ecology and Conservation in Black and Clear Water Rivers","event-place":"São Paulo, Brazil","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"São Paulo, Brazil","title":"Small-scale fisheries, livelihoods and food security of riverine people","author":[{"literal":"Gustavo Hallwass"},{"literal":"Luís Henrique Tomazoni da Silva"},{"literal":"Paula Nagl"},{"literal":"Mariana Clauzet"},{"literal":"Alpina Begossi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]],"season":"In press"}},"label":"page"},{"id":1014,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVUAIFL8"],"itemData":{"id":1014,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The management of small-scale freshwater ?sheries in Amazon has been based usually on surveys of urban markets, while ?sheries of rural villages have gone unnoticed. We compared the ?shing characteristics (catch, effort and selectivity) between an urban market and ?ve small villages in the Lower Tocantins River (Brazilian Amazon), downstream from a large reservoir. We recorded 86 and 601 ?sh landings in the urban market and villages, respectively, using the same methodology. The urban ?shers showed higher catch per unit of effort, higher amount of ice (related to a higher ?shing effort, as ice is used to store ?sh catches) and larger crew size per ?shing trip, but village ?shers had a higher estimated annual ?sh production. Conversely, urban and village ?shers used similar ?shing gear (gillnets) and the main ?sh species caught were the same. However, village ?shers showed more diverse strategies regarding gear, habitats and ?sh caught. Therefore, although it underestimated the total amount of ?sh caught in the Lower Tocantins River region, the data from the urban market could be a reliable indicator of main ?sh species exploited and ?shing gear used by village ?shers. Monitoring and management should consider the differences and similarities between urban and rural ?sheries, in Amazon and in other tropical regions.","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-010-9584-1","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"188-200","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishing Effort and Catch Composition of Urban Market and Rural Villages in Brazilian Amazon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-010-9584-1","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Lopes","given":"Priscila Fabiana"},{"family":"Juras","given":"Anastacio Afonso"},{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato Azevedo Matias"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",2]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":382,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2T4NL3H7"],"itemData":{"id":382,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The local ecological knowledge (LEK) of ?shermen has been applied to reconstruct temporal patterns of resource abundance and to assess resource exploitation, especially in tropical ecosystems. Protected areas (PAs) are the main conservation measure in the Amazon, but their ef?cacy to improve ?sheries is poorly known. The main goal of this study was to apply ?shermen’ LEK to evaluate temporal changes in composition and abundance of exploited ?sh species among three different categories of PAs in the Tapajo s River, Brazilian Amazon. We interviewed 203 ?shers about their highest ?sh catches, and the main ?sh caught at two time periods: the beginning of their ?shing careers and currently. Fishers’ LEK indicated decreases in citations of larger-bodied ?shes and in reported catches over time, evidencing a gradient of conservation according to PA’s time of establishment and management rules. We noticed higher changes (reduced catches and smaller ?sh exploited) in the Environmental PA, established 10 years ago and lacking management plan and buffer zone. In National Forest, which was created 40 years ago with more restrictive management rules, ?sh composition and catches included larger commercial ?sh. The Extractive Reserve, which was created 15 years ago and lacks speci?c ?shing rules, may be in an intermediate stage, as large ?sh and highest catches decreased over time, but medium and large ?sh are still exploited. Fishers’ LEK can be an invaluable but neglected source of data on temporal patterns of ?shing pressure and conservation needs, especially in tropical megadiverse, poorly known and threatened rivers.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/acv.12504","ISSN":"1367-9430, 1469-1795","journalAbbreviation":"Anim Conserv","language":"en","page":"acv.12504","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers’ knowledge indicates temporal changes in composition and abundance of fishing resources in Amazon protected areas","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/acv.12504","author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"G."},{"family":"Schiavetti","given":"A."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"R. A. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,11]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1011,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J7HQ2ZNE"],"itemData":{"id":1011,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Planning and Management","ISSN":"0964-0568","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Environmental Planning and Management","page":"1537-1559","title":"Patterns of selectiveness in the Amazonian freshwater fisheries: implications for management","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato AM"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":391,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F8E9M956"],"itemData":{"id":391,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"ORYX","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605316000247","issue":"2","page":"268-279","title":"Influence of protected areas on fish assemblages and fisheries in a large tropical river","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84976553035&doi=10.1017%2fS0030605316000247&partnerID=40&md5=0ea9128733605cda73567a7b54828141","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Keppeler","given":"F.W."},{"family":"Hallwass","given":"G."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"R.A.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31752,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WXQ88SU8"],"itemData":{"id":31752,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Boletim do Instituto de Pesca","DOI":"10.20950/1678-2305.2019.45.2.423","ISSN":"00469939, 16782305","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Bol. Inst. Pesca","page":"e.423","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishery parameters and population dynamics of silver croaker on the Xingu river, Brazilian Amazon","URL":"https://www.pesca.sp.gov.br/FISHERY_PARAMETERS_AND_POPULATION_DYNAMICS_OF_SILVER_CROAKER_ON_THE_XINGU_RIVER_BRAZILIAN_AMAZON.html","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Mesquita","given":"Esther Mirian Cardoso"},{"family":"Cruz","given":"Rivetla Edipo Araújo"},{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Isaac","given":"Victoria Judith"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,4]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":366,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VNKH3NDI"],"itemData":{"id":366,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1007/s10021-013-9722-8","ISSN":"1432-9840, 1435-0629","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystems","language":"en","page":"271-285","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Co-management and Spatial Features Contribute to Secure Fish Abundance and Fishing Yields in Tropical Floodplain Lakes","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10021-013-9722-8","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato A. M."},{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Lopes","given":"Priscila F."},{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"Ariane R."},{"family":"Lima","given":"Renata P."},{"family":"Hasenack","given":"Heinrich"},{"family":"Juras","given":"Anastácio A."},{"family":"Begossi","given":"Alpina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",3]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Small-scale fisheries have cultural and socioeconomic relevance to indigenous Tacana people in the Beni River, Bolivian Amazon, where a participatory survey indicated that this fishery, which exploits 43 species for food and income, has been ecologically and economically sustainable as catches and sizes of exploited fish remained unchanged for a period of seven years, providing a regular source of revenues to local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"T9xcDpkv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Salinas, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Salinas, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1005,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KUAF683U"],"itemData":{"id":1005,"type":"report","abstract":"Protected areas and indigenous territories together make up 45.5% of the Amazon Basin (RAISG, 2015) and represent 60% (FAO & OIMT, 2011) of the world´s tropical forest. In the case of Bolivia, protected areas and indigenous territories of the Amazon Basin make up 43.6% of the country´s surface area (WCS, 2016). Indigenous territories overlap or border protected areas and harbor most of Bolivia´s rich biodiversity. They contribute to landscape integrity through the zoning\\nof areas for different purposes such as communal use, wildlife corridors, and watershed protection. This approach is\\nfundamental for the preservation of cultural values, and the creation of opportunities for the sustainable management of natural resources. \\nThe Tacana and Leco Indigenous Peoples developed land management experiences since the early 2000´s, which resulted in outcomes in participatory planning (technical and spatial), institutional strengthening, the development of economic enterprises and biodiversity conservation. Their traditional territories are located in the northern Amazon, within the La Paz Department, which contains one of the most biologically diverse landscapes in the world. The regions varied\\ntopography, climate and elevations (altitudes between 190 and 6040 m above sea level) resulted in the development of a high diversity of habitats and wildlife species, with the highest concentration of plants and animals in Bolivia: 60% of the flora and 66% of the vertebrates, with a very high number of bird species (77%), and 10% of the world’s birds.\\nThe regions conservation value led to the creation of national protected areas of Madidi, Pilón Lajas and Apolobamba in\\nBolivia, administered by the National Protected Areas Service (SERNAP). These protected areas are vital for the protection\\nof watersheds, forest preservation and the maintenance of wildlife species. These protected areas complement the activities of nine indigenous territories that strengthen integral planning at the landscape level. As a transboundary area, with southeastern Peru, its importance for conservation is even greater, contributing to ecosystem connectivity and the coordination of protection actions.","call-number":"Sub 169c","event-place":"La Paz, Bolivia","language":"English","page":"50","publisher":"CIPTA, CIPLA and WCS","publisher-place":"La Paz, Bolivia","title":"The Environmental, Economic and Sociocultural Value of Indigenous Territorial Management in the Greater Madidi Landscape Executive Summary","author":[{"family":"Salinas","given":"E."},{"family":"Wallace","given":"R."},{"family":"Painter","given":"L."},{"family":"Lehm","given":"Z."},{"family":"Loayza","given":"O."},{"family":"Pabón","given":"C."},{"family":"Ramírez","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, a study conducted 20 years ago indicates that the large frugivorous fish Colossoma macropomum in the Bolivian Amazon supports a sustainable fishery partly due to its linkages with a small population and a well-preserved floodplain forest habitat ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"L2oclx15","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Reinert & Winter, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Reinert & Winter, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1019,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AHE7A96Z"],"itemData":{"id":1019,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","ISSN":"0888-8892","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","page":"1344-1351","title":"Sustainability of harvested pacú (Colossoma macropomum) populations in the northeastern Bolivian Amazon","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Reinert","given":"Thomas R"},{"family":"Winter","given":"Kimberly A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Unsustainable cases include migratory fish, such as Prochilodus species among others, which have suffered intense fishing pressure, sometimes aggravated by environmental impacts (e.g., dams in rivers in Brazil) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5Zr9LSDJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Catarino, 2019; R. E. Santos, Pinto-Coelho, Fonseca, Sim\\\\uc0\\\\u245{}es, & Zanchi, 2018; Strieder Philippsen, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Catarino, 2019; R. E. Santos, Pinto-Coelho, Fonseca, Simões, & Zanchi, 2018; Strieder Philippsen, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31747,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YR7IUL3U"],"itemData":{"id":31747,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Acta Ichthyologica et Piscatoria","DOI":"10.3750/AIEP/02623","issue":"4","page":"373-380","title":"Stock assessment of prochilodus nigricans (Actinopterygii: Characiformes: Prochilodontidae) using two distinct algorithms, in the context of a small-scale amazonian fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85077909841&doi=10.3750%2fAIEP%2f02623&partnerID=40&md5=155a014f9686ec6c5da1a0b90f5ed89f","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Catarino","given":"M.F."},{"family":"Kahn","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Freitas","given":"C.E.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1024,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GTU57A9D"],"itemData":{"id":1024,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/fme.12305","ISSN":"0969997X","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Manag Ecol","language":"en","page":"380-391","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The decline of fisheries on the Madeira River, Brazil: The high cost of the hydroelectric dams in the Amazon Basin","title-short":"The decline of fisheries on the Madeira River, Brazil","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/fme.12305","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Santos","given":"Rangel E."},{"family":"Pinto-Coelho","given":"Ricardo M."},{"family":"Fonseca","given":"Rogério"},{"family":"Simões","given":"Nadson R."},{"family":"Zanchi","given":"Fabrício B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M3NVXDYA"],"itemData":{"id":1087,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine and Freshwater Research","DOI":"10.1071/MF16053","issue":"5","page":"980-992","title":"Fishers' and scientific histories: An example of consensus from an inland fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85018262780&doi=10.1071%2fMF16053&partnerID=40&md5=6e52ce85acf3e8f7dedaf166b96e8e14","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Strieder Philippsen","given":"J."},{"family":"Minte-Vera","given":"C.V."},{"family":"Okada","given":"E.K."},{"family":"Carvalho","given":"A.R."},{"family":"Angelini","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Argentina ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0a40K4Gr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Baig\\\\uc0\\\\u250{}n, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Baigún, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31724,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MD6C8WUE"],"itemData":{"id":31724,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Neotropical Ichthyology","DOI":"10.1590/S1679-62252013000100023","issue":"1","page":"199-210","title":"Assessment of sábalo (Prochilodus lineatus) fisheries in the lower Paraná river basin (Argentina) based on hydrological, biological, and fishery indicators","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84876386598&doi=10.1590%2fS1679-62252013000100023&partnerID=40&md5=fb86b4090e39f29776b2d4d04d930b85","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Baigún","given":"C."},{"family":"Minotti","given":"P."},{"family":"Oldani","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another unsustainable pattern in the Brazilian Amazon refers to the decrease in catches and size of some of the larger and most valuable commercial fishes, such as Colossoma macropomum and large catfish (Pimelodidae), among others ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5bc6zlgc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Castello, McGrath, & Beck, 2011; Garcez Costa Sousa & de Carvalho Freitas, 2011; G. Hallwass, 2019; Tregidgo, Barlow, Pompeu, de Almeida Rocha, & Parry, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Castello, McGrath, & Beck, 2011; Garcez Costa Sousa & de Carvalho Freitas, 2011; G. Hallwass, 2019; Tregidgo, Barlow, Pompeu, de Almeida Rocha, & Parry, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34945,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8Z5MJ63Q"],"itemData":{"id":34945,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Resource assessments of small-scale ?sheries in the Amazon are rare, making it dif?cult to improve ?sheries management. This study assessed the sustainability of small-scale ?shery resources in the Lower Amazon region using data from over 20,000 interviews collected in three ?oodplain ?shing communities between 1992 and 2007. The data were analyzed with respect to theoretical maxima of catches and historical trends of (i) catch per unit effort (CPUE), (ii) species composition of the catch, and (iii) mean body length of the most-caught species. The most important results were: First, observed catches have been about 54–58% of theoretical maxima in all communities. Second, CPUE has remained stable over time in all communities. Third, there has been no substitution of ?sh species in the catch. Fourth, mean body lengths of ?ve of the nine most-caught species were below length-at-?rst-reproduction, and the body lengths of some have increased overtime while that of others have remained stable or decreased. Overall, ?shery resources in the region appear to be moderately exploited, with some key species showing typical signs of overexploitation. This would indicate that the principal threat to the sustainability of ?shery resources is excessive concentration of ?shing effort on a few target species combined with unsustainable ?shing practices. Management and research recommendations are proposed.","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2011.05.002","ISSN":"01657836","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Research","language":"en","page":"356-364","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Resource sustainability in small-scale fisheries in the Lower Amazon floodplains","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0165783611001913","volume":"110","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"McGrath","given":"David G."},{"family":"Beck","given":"Pieter S.A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",7]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1025,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YN9ET5F7"],"itemData":{"id":1025,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ichthyology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1439-0426.2010.01521.x","ISSN":"01758659","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"118-121","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Seasonal catch distribution of tambaqui (Colossoma macropomum), Characidae in a central Amazon floodplain lake: implications for sustainable fisheries management: Seasonal catch distribution of tambaqui","title-short":"Seasonal catch distribution of tambaqui (Colossoma macropomum), Characidae in a central Amazon floodplain lake","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1439-0426.2010.01521.x","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Garcez Costa Sousa","given":"R."},{"family":"Carvalho Freitas","given":"C. E.","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",2]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":382,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2T4NL3H7"],"itemData":{"id":382,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The local ecological knowledge (LEK) of ?shermen has been applied to reconstruct temporal patterns of resource abundance and to assess resource exploitation, especially in tropical ecosystems. Protected areas (PAs) are the main conservation measure in the Amazon, but their ef?cacy to improve ?sheries is poorly known. The main goal of this study was to apply ?shermen’ LEK to evaluate temporal changes in composition and abundance of exploited ?sh species among three different categories of PAs in the Tapajo s River, Brazilian Amazon. We interviewed 203 ?shers about their highest ?sh catches, and the main ?sh caught at two time periods: the beginning of their ?shing careers and currently. Fishers’ LEK indicated decreases in citations of larger-bodied ?shes and in reported catches over time, evidencing a gradient of conservation according to PA’s time of establishment and management rules. We noticed higher changes (reduced catches and smaller ?sh exploited) in the Environmental PA, established 10 years ago and lacking management plan and buffer zone. In National Forest, which was created 40 years ago with more restrictive management rules, ?sh composition and catches included larger commercial ?sh. The Extractive Reserve, which was created 15 years ago and lacks speci?c ?shing rules, may be in an intermediate stage, as large ?sh and highest catches decreased over time, but medium and large ?sh are still exploited. Fishers’ LEK can be an invaluable but neglected source of data on temporal patterns of ?shing pressure and conservation needs, especially in tropical megadiverse, poorly known and threatened rivers.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/acv.12504","ISSN":"1367-9430, 1469-1795","journalAbbreviation":"Anim Conserv","language":"en","page":"acv.12504","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers’ knowledge indicates temporal changes in composition and abundance of fishing resources in Amazon protected areas","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/acv.12504","author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"G."},{"family":"Schiavetti","given":"A."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"R. A. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,11]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1022,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UCEGDHJE"],"itemData":{"id":1022,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","ISSN":"0027-8424","issue":"32","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","page":"8655-8659","title":"Rainforest metropolis casts 1,000-km defaunation shadow","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Tregidgo","given":"Daniel J"},{"family":"Barlow","given":"Jos"},{"family":"Pompeu","given":"Paulo S"},{"family":"Almeida Rocha","given":"Mayana","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Parry","given":"Luke"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Even the pirarucu (Arapaima gigas) that increased in co-managed small-scale fisheries is considered to be unsustainably exploited in non-managed Amazonian rivers, where the abundance of this fish has reportedly reduced ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"w8ZQEGEJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Castello, 2015; G. Hallwass, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Castello, 2015; G. Hallwass, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34932,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GCXDDA7J","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V275GCVY"],"itemData":{"id":34932,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2491","ISSN":"10527613","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Aquatic Conserv: Mar. Freshw. Ecosyst.","language":"en","page":"587-598","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Understanding fishing-induced extinctions in the Amazon: FISHING-INDUCED EXTINCTIONS IN THE AMAZON","title-short":"Understanding fishing-induced extinctions in the Amazon","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/aqc.2491","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Castello","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Arantes","given":"Caroline Chaves"},{"family":"Mcgrath","given":"David Gibbs"},{"family":"Stewart","given":"Donald James"},{"family":"Sousa","given":"Fabio Sarmento De"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",10]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":382,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2T4NL3H7"],"itemData":{"id":382,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The local ecological knowledge (LEK) of ?shermen has been applied to reconstruct temporal patterns of resource abundance and to assess resource exploitation, especially in tropical ecosystems. Protected areas (PAs) are the main conservation measure in the Amazon, but their ef?cacy to improve ?sheries is poorly known. The main goal of this study was to apply ?shermen’ LEK to evaluate temporal changes in composition and abundance of exploited ?sh species among three different categories of PAs in the Tapajo s River, Brazilian Amazon. We interviewed 203 ?shers about their highest ?sh catches, and the main ?sh caught at two time periods: the beginning of their ?shing careers and currently. Fishers’ LEK indicated decreases in citations of larger-bodied ?shes and in reported catches over time, evidencing a gradient of conservation according to PA’s time of establishment and management rules. We noticed higher changes (reduced catches and smaller ?sh exploited) in the Environmental PA, established 10 years ago and lacking management plan and buffer zone. In National Forest, which was created 40 years ago with more restrictive management rules, ?sh composition and catches included larger commercial ?sh. The Extractive Reserve, which was created 15 years ago and lacks speci?c ?shing rules, may be in an intermediate stage, as large ?sh and highest catches decreased over time, but medium and large ?sh are still exploited. Fishers’ LEK can be an invaluable but neglected source of data on temporal patterns of ?shing pressure and conservation needs, especially in tropical megadiverse, poorly known and threatened rivers.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/acv.12504","ISSN":"1367-9430, 1469-1795","journalAbbreviation":"Anim Conserv","language":"en","page":"acv.12504","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers’ knowledge indicates temporal changes in composition and abundance of fishing resources in Amazon protected areas","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/acv.12504","author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"G."},{"family":"Schiavetti","given":"A."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"R. A. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,11]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, mainly due to widespread illegal fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QQYybT1q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cavole, Arantes, & Castello, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Cavole, Arantes, & Castello, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1013,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HZ3Y2H2F"],"itemData":{"id":1013,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2015.03.012","page":"1-5","title":"How illegal are tropical small-scale fisheries? An estimate for arapaima in the Amazon","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84926181648&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2015.03.012&partnerID=40&md5=ed3403e90a97686a80f7573be1f1323f","volume":"168","author":[{"family":"Cavole","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Arantes","given":"C.C."},{"family":"Castello","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	An interesting exception of this pattern is the fishing of some large and migratory catfish (Brachyplatystoma spp.) in the Brazilian Amazon, as catches of some of these species have increased in some rivers either due to successful regulations, improved fishing technologies (larger nets, motorized boats) or to market opportunities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Smlxaob0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cruz, 2020; Gustavo Hallwass, 2020; G. Hallwass, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Cruz, 2020; Gustavo Hallwass, 2020; G. Hallwass, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1027,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KKKS76V8"],"itemData":{"id":1027,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine and Freshwater Research","DOI":"10.1071/MF20098","ISSN":"1323-1650","journalAbbreviation":"Mar. Freshwater Res.","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trends and environmental drivers of giant catfish catch in the lower Amazon River","URL":"http://www.publish.csiro.au/?paper=MF20098","author":[{"family":"Cruz","given":"R. E. A."},{"family":"Kaplan","given":"D. A."},{"family":"Santos","given":"P. B."},{"family":"Ávila-da-Silva","given":"A. O."},{"family":"Marques","given":"E. E."},{"family":"Isaac","given":"V. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1021,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SE4KUVVR"],"itemData":{"id":1021,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries in the Brazilian Amazon: People, Ecology and Conservation in Black and Clear Water Rivers","event-place":"São Paulo, Brazil","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"São Paulo, Brazil","title":"Small-scale fisheries, livelihoods and food security of riverine people","author":[{"literal":"Gustavo Hallwass"},{"literal":"Luís Henrique Tomazoni da Silva"},{"literal":"Paula Nagl"},{"literal":"Mariana Clauzet"},{"literal":"Alpina Begossi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]],"season":"In press"}},"label":"page"},{"id":382,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2T4NL3H7"],"itemData":{"id":382,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The local ecological knowledge (LEK) of ?shermen has been applied to reconstruct temporal patterns of resource abundance and to assess resource exploitation, especially in tropical ecosystems. Protected areas (PAs) are the main conservation measure in the Amazon, but their ef?cacy to improve ?sheries is poorly known. The main goal of this study was to apply ?shermen’ LEK to evaluate temporal changes in composition and abundance of exploited ?sh species among three different categories of PAs in the Tapajo s River, Brazilian Amazon. We interviewed 203 ?shers about their highest ?sh catches, and the main ?sh caught at two time periods: the beginning of their ?shing careers and currently. Fishers’ LEK indicated decreases in citations of larger-bodied ?shes and in reported catches over time, evidencing a gradient of conservation according to PA’s time of establishment and management rules. We noticed higher changes (reduced catches and smaller ?sh exploited) in the Environmental PA, established 10 years ago and lacking management plan and buffer zone. In National Forest, which was created 40 years ago with more restrictive management rules, ?sh composition and catches included larger commercial ?sh. The Extractive Reserve, which was created 15 years ago and lacks speci?c ?shing rules, may be in an intermediate stage, as large ?sh and highest catches decreased over time, but medium and large ?sh are still exploited. Fishers’ LEK can be an invaluable but neglected source of data on temporal patterns of ?shing pressure and conservation needs, especially in tropical megadiverse, poorly known and threatened rivers.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/acv.12504","ISSN":"1367-9430, 1469-1795","journalAbbreviation":"Anim Conserv","language":"en","page":"acv.12504","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fishers’ knowledge indicates temporal changes in composition and abundance of fishing resources in Amazon protected areas","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/acv.12504","author":[{"family":"Hallwass","given":"G."},{"family":"Schiavetti","given":"A."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"R. A. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,11]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, these catfish fisheries are difficult to manage due to the long migrations (more than 1,000 km) that these fish perform along the main Amazon River and its tributaries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nD9IRmkh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barthem, 2017; Nunes, 2019; Petrere, Barthem, C\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}rdoba, & G\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}mez, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Barthem, 2017; Nunes, 2019; Petrere, Barthem, Córdoba, & Gómez, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1031,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZWBKA9C3"],"itemData":{"id":1031,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep41784","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"41784","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Goliath catfish spawning in the far western Amazon confirmed by the distribution of mature adults, drifting larvae and migrating juveniles","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/srep41784","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Barthem","given":"Ronaldo B."},{"family":"Goulding","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Leite","given":"Rosseval G."},{"family":"Cañas","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Forsberg","given":"Bruce"},{"family":"Venticinque","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Petry","given":"Paulo"},{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"Mauro L. de B."},{"family":"Chuctaya","given":"Junior"},{"family":"Mercado","given":"Armando"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",3]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31764,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GJKVIDEF"],"itemData":{"id":31764,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Hydrobiologia","ISSN":"0018-8158","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Hydrobiologia","page":"197-215","title":"Fishers’ local ecological knowledge indicate migration patterns of tropical freshwater fish in an Amazonian river","volume":"833","author":[{"family":"Nunes","given":"Moisés Ubiratã Schmitz"},{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato Azevedo Matias"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1020,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IIC3V5MW"],"itemData":{"id":1020,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","ISSN":"0960-3166","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","page":"403-414","title":"Review of the large catfish fisheries in the upper Amazon and the stock depletion of piraíba (Brachyplatystoma filamentosumLichtenstein)","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Petrere","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Barthem","given":"Ronaldo Borges"},{"family":"Córdoba","given":"Edwin Agudelo"},{"family":"Gómez","given":"Bernardo Corrales"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which make these fishes especially susceptible to impacts caused by dams ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"f986Z8Up","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. E. Santos, 2018)","plainCitation":"(R. E. Santos, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1024,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GTU57A9D"],"itemData":{"id":1024,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/fme.12305","ISSN":"0969997X","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Manag Ecol","language":"en","page":"380-391","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The decline of fisheries on the Madeira River, Brazil: The high cost of the hydroelectric dams in the Amazon Basin","title-short":"The decline of fisheries on the Madeira River, Brazil","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/fme.12305","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Santos","given":"Rangel E."},{"family":"Pinto-Coelho","given":"Ricardo M."},{"family":"Fonseca","given":"Rogério"},{"family":"Simões","given":"Nadson R."},{"family":"Zanchi","given":"Fabrício B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and may thus require basin wide or even transboundary international management approachesITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oC24fhjZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Doria, 2020; Goulding, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Doria, 2020; Goulding, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30322,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y8FTCCNR"],"itemData":{"id":30322,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"10.1080/08941920.2020.1771492","ISSN":"0894-1920, 1521-0723","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Society & Natural Resources","language":"en","page":"1213-1231","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Challenges for the Governance of Small-Scale Fisheries on the Brazil-Bolivia Transboundary Region","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/08941920.2020.1771492","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Doria","given":"Carolina R. C."},{"family":"Athayde","given":"Simone"},{"family":"Lima","given":"Haissa Melo","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Carvajal-Vallejos","given":"Fernando M."},{"family":"Dutka-Gianelli","given":"Jynessa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,2]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1032,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SZGF8ZSM"],"itemData":{"id":1032,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12328","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"138-158","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ecosystem-based management of Amazon fisheries and wetlands","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/faf.12328","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Goulding","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Venticinque","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"Mauro L. de B."},{"family":"Barthem","given":"Ronaldo B."},{"family":"Leite","given":"Rosseval G."},{"family":"Forsberg","given":"Bruce"},{"family":"Petry","given":"Paulo"},{"family":"Lopes da Silva-Júnior","given":"Urbano"},{"family":"Ferraz","given":"Polliana Santos"},{"family":"Cañas","given":"Carlos"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

* North America
In North America, most of the reviewed studies focus fish and invertebrates as food (15) were from the Artic and Alaska (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651), whereas fewer studies address recreational (3), or both uses (4). Among the studies on use of fishing resources as food, only one reported a sustainable lobster fishery in California (United States of America), where a collaborative approach improved the ecological assessment and feedback with human dimensions of the system ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lFO5wv24","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kay, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Kay, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31896,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K2ZQWDD3"],"itemData":{"id":31896,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ecological Applications","DOI":"10.1890/11-0155.1","issue":"1","page":"322-335","title":"Collaborative assessment of California spiny lobster population and fishery responses to a marine reserve network","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84863337702&doi=10.1890%2f11-0155.1&partnerID=40&md5=fdfbc98e2e8e4d496db5f2629492f258","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Kay","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Lenihan","given":"H.S."},{"family":"Guenther","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Miller","given":"C.J."},{"family":"Shrout","given":"S.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, a study including fishers’ knowledge indicated that the depletion of Atlantic cod caused an increase in the population of lobsters, thus improving the sustainability of lobster fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FK9tc8ME","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Boudreau & Worm, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Boudreau & Worm, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1099,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3CFU3TBU"],"itemData":{"id":1099,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Ecology Progress Series","DOI":"10.3354/meps08473","page":"181-191","title":"Top-down control of lobster in the Gulf of Maine: Insights from local ecological knowledge and research surveys","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-77954105646&doi=10.3354%2fmeps08473&partnerID=40&md5=b27c208390e1c0a22f2c17d64481585e","volume":"403","author":[{"family":"Boudreau","given":"S.A."},{"family":"Worm","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another study indicated that Pacific salmon species have been increasing in recent years (1990s and early 2000s) in the Beaufort Sea ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HvKVfKph","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carothers, Sformo, Cotton, George, & Westley, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Carothers, Sformo, Cotton, George, & Westley, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31895,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DNAHRSHG"],"itemData":{"id":31895,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic","DOI":"10.14430/arctic68876","issue":"3","page":"273-288","title":"Pacific salmon in the rapidly changing arctic: Exploring local knowledge and emerging fisheries in Utqia?vik and Nuiqsut, Alaska","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85073480543&doi=10.14430%2farctic68876&partnerID=40&md5=72ec8321b3679ee71578d54f02aa928e","volume":"72","author":[{"family":"Carothers","given":"C."},{"family":"Sformo","given":"T.L."},{"family":"Cotton","given":"S."},{"family":"George","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Westley","given":"P.A.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 	Some problems affecting the sustainability of coastal small-scale fisheries are the potential serial depletion and regional overfishing of the rock crab fishery in California ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"S9AoTNzq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fitzgerald, Wilson, & Lenihan, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Fitzgerald, Wilson, & Lenihan, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1007,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NHW3GNPZ"],"itemData":{"id":1007,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2018.07.012","page":"133-144","title":"Detecting a need for improved management in a data-limited crab fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85050880217&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2018.07.012&partnerID=40&md5=6094cf807df19962f91aeb45fc385dd1","volume":"208","author":[{"family":"Fitzgerald","given":"S.P."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Lenihan","given":"H.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, severe declines in the abundance and catches of the Dungeness crab (Cancer magister) in Canada ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ET8Nyeig","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ban, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Ban, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31728,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GT85P9S4"],"itemData":{"id":31728,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Introduction: Fisheries management is often data-limited, and conducted at spatial scales that are too large to address the needs of Indigenous peoples, whose cultures depend upon the local availability of marine resources.","call-number":"Esub 21h","container-title":"Ecosystem Health and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1080/20964129.2017.1379887","ISSN":"2096-4129, 2332-8878","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystem Health and Sustainability","language":"English","page":"1379887","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Indigenous knowledge as data for modern fishery management: a case study of Dungeness crab in Pacific Canada","title-short":"Indigenous knowledge as data for modern fishery management","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/20964129.2017.1379887","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C"},{"family":"Eckert","given":"Lauren"},{"family":"McGreer","given":"Madeleine"},{"family":"Frid","given":"Alejandro"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and overfishing and declines of stocks of salmon in Alaska ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Qv2jNMze","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(H. L. Harrison & Loring, 2016; Loring, Harrison, & Gerlach, 2014)","plainCitation":"(H. L. Harrison & Loring, 2016; Loring, Harrison, & Gerlach, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31732,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7W5MNFN2"],"itemData":{"id":31732,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Agriculture and Food Security","DOI":"10.1186/s40066-016-0065-5","issue":"1","title":"Urban harvests: Food security and local fish and shellfish in Southcentral Alaska","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84996615321&doi=10.1186%2fs40066-016-0065-5&partnerID=40&md5=c09c5e701ade23047e796e8653bd2014","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Harrison","given":"H.L."},{"family":"Loring","given":"P.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1006,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MBN67IFU"],"itemData":{"id":1006,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Society and Natural Resources","DOI":"10.1080/08941920.2013.819955","issue":"2","page":"185-199","title":"Local Perceptions of the Sustainability of Alaska's Highly Contested Cook Inlet Salmon Fisheries","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84895067349&doi=10.1080%2f08941920.2013.819955&partnerID=40&md5=6d0e1a417e94657c018a65fb8bedfde5","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Loring","given":"P.A."},{"family":"Harrison","given":"H.L."},{"family":"Gerlach","given":"S.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and sea cucumber in Canada ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lt2dq8V8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Regan, 2015)","plainCitation":"(O’Regan, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1090,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZEF9RTGP"],"itemData":{"id":1090,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2014.07.025","page":"103-110","title":"Harvesters' perspectives on the management of British Columbia's giant red sea cucumber fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84906500216&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2014.07.025&partnerID=40&md5=b988fe097a05bddd512f0496ad485587","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"O'Regan","given":"S.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. One study that combines traditional and scientific ecological knowledge showed that two exploited shellfish species were also impacted by local and regional environmental factors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wk8kVXrg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ambrose, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Ambrose, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31726,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/69CPRGBZ"],"itemData":{"id":31726,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2014.00040","issue":"SEP","title":"Interpreting environmental change in coastal Alaska using traditional and scientific ecological knowledge","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85008930053&doi=10.3389%2ffmars.2014.00040&partnerID=40&md5=f732daa05d107ffc2e54303f706a29a7","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Ambrose","given":"W.G."},{"family":"Clough","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Johnson","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Greenacre","given":"M."},{"family":"Griffith","given":"D.C."},{"family":"Carroll","given":"M.L."},{"family":"Whiting","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, other studies have shown that the involvement of indigenous peoples and local communities were critical to the reversion of a declining trend in local populations of lake sturgeons (Acipenser fulvescens) in Wisconsin and the Great Lakes region (United States of America), a very relevant social and economic traditional small-scale fishery ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IQuJ4jDW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kline, Bruch, & Binkowski, 2012; Runstrom, Bruch, Reiter, & Cox, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Kline, Bruch, & Binkowski, 2012; Runstrom, Bruch, Reiter, & Cox, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9761,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/67T6FJME"],"itemData":{"id":9761,"type":"book","ISBN":"0-87020-546-3","publisher":"Wisconsin Historical Society","title":"People of the sturgeon: Wisconsin's love affair with an ancient fish","author":[{"family":"Kline","given":"Kathleen Schmitt"},{"family":"Bruch","given":"Ronald M"},{"family":"Binkowski","given":"Frederick P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":9743,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UYFWK2HR"],"itemData":{"id":9743,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ichthyology","DOI":"10.1046/j.1439-0426.2002.00426.x","ISSN":"0175-8659, 1439-0426","issue":"4-6","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Ichthyol","language":"en","page":"481-485","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Lake sturgeon (Acipenser fulvescens) on the Menominee Indian Reservation: an effort toward co-management and population restoration","title-short":"Lake sturgeon (Acipenser fulvescens) on the Menominee Indian Reservation","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1046/j.1439-0426.2002.00426.x","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Runstrom","given":"A."},{"family":"Bruch","given":"R. M."},{"family":"Reiter","given":"D."},{"family":"Cox","given":"D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002",12]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The previous population decline of this species was due to unsustainable practices, such as overfishing and habitat loss or transformation, trends also observed in other parts of the world, including the large-scale fishing of sturgeons ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LDQ5MZXF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tavakoli, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Tavakoli, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9732,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L82NH7J3"],"itemData":{"id":9732,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science & Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1080/23308249.2021.1873244","ISSN":"2330-8249, 2330-8257","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Reviews in Fisheries Science & Aquaculture","language":"en","page":"753-768","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Sturgeon, Caviar, and Caviar Substitutes: From Production, Gastronomy, Nutrition, and Quality Change to Trade and Commercial Mimicry","title-short":"Sturgeon, Caviar, and Caviar Substitutes","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23308249.2021.1873244","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Tavakoli","given":"Samad"},{"family":"Luo","given":"Yongkang"},{"family":"Regenstein","given":"Joe M."},{"family":"Daneshvar","given":"Ehsan"},{"family":"Bhatnagar","given":"Amit"},{"family":"Tan","given":"Yuqing"},{"family":"Hong","given":"Hui"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",10,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the Great Lakes region, the local community-built co-management rules across the last six decades for the sturgeon fishery, including fishing festivals and competitions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"31kzsHFN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kline, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Kline, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9761,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/67T6FJME"],"itemData":{"id":9761,"type":"book","ISBN":"0-87020-546-3","publisher":"Wisconsin Historical Society","title":"People of the sturgeon: Wisconsin's love affair with an ancient fish","author":[{"family":"Kline","given":"Kathleen Schmitt"},{"family":"Bruch","given":"Ronald M"},{"family":"Binkowski","given":"Frederick P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and this fishery is also important for the Menominee Nation (an indigenous tribe in upper Midwestern in the United States of America). Together with local authorities and researchers, the local community build a successful restoration program to reintroduce lake sturgeon larvae to areas where they could no longer be found ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XrUUMpWA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Runstrom, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Runstrom, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9743,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UYFWK2HR"],"itemData":{"id":9743,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ichthyology","DOI":"10.1046/j.1439-0426.2002.00426.x","ISSN":"0175-8659, 1439-0426","issue":"4-6","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Ichthyol","language":"en","page":"481-485","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Lake sturgeon (Acipenser fulvescens) on the Menominee Indian Reservation: an effort toward co-management and population restoration","title-short":"Lake sturgeon (Acipenser fulvescens) on the Menominee Indian Reservation","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1046/j.1439-0426.2002.00426.x","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Runstrom","given":"A."},{"family":"Bruch","given":"R. M."},{"family":"Reiter","given":"D."},{"family":"Cox","given":"D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

* Asia-Pacific
In Asia-Pacific, the majority of studies (77) address the use of fishing resources as food, either as subsistence, commercial or to support livelihoods. Only 6 studies report sustainable use of fishing resources for food in coastal small-scale fisheries, including reef fish and invertebrates in the American Samoa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yTQZjndk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Craig, 2008)","plainCitation":"(P. Craig, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1047,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A875WIT3"],"itemData":{"id":1047,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2007.08.018","issue":"3","page":"230-240","title":"Subsistence harvest of coral reef resources in the outer islands of American Samoa: Modern, historic and prehistoric catches","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-39149126693&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2007.08.018&partnerID=40&md5=3b51a3fc615d2c20af3fa5e686a4d167","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Craig","given":"P."},{"family":"Green","given":"A."},{"family":"Tuilagi","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Solomon Islands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"s9xFGf7V","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. J. Cohen, 2013)","plainCitation":"(P. J. Cohen, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1051,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RZETPG5C"],"itemData":{"id":1051,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2013.08.010","issue":"6","page":"1702-1713","title":"Fishing dynamics associated with periodically harvested marine closures","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84885425703&doi=10.1016%2fj.gloenvcha.2013.08.010&partnerID=40&md5=ef02c031492d506b97dbc6bc1ff869c6","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Cohen","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Cinner","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Foale","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the Torres Strait Islands in Australia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qodDwcN1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Busilacchi, Russ, Williams, Begg, & Sutton, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Busilacchi, Russ, Williams, Begg, & Sutton, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":379,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3LZ8AZLF"],"itemData":{"id":379,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2012.08.017","page":"50-58","title":"Quantifying changes in the subsistence reef fishery of indigenous communities in Torres Strait, Australia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84867017341&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2012.08.017&partnerID=40&md5=36a0db1b9b09c53ff9adb6036973fc7b","volume":"137","author":[{"family":"Busilacchi","given":"S."},{"family":"Russ","given":"G.R."},{"family":"Williams","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Begg","given":"G.A."},{"family":"Sutton","given":"S.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, besides fisheries of shrimp in Indonesia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"p0CJ78PK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Anna, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Anna, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1048,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P4HDFX28"],"itemData":{"id":1048,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversitas","DOI":"10.13057/biodiv/d180132","issue":"1","page":"248-256","title":"Indonesian shrimp resource accounting for sustainable stock management","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85007575346&doi=10.13057%2fbiodiv%2fd180132&partnerID=40&md5=40ae790b6fbb4d30fd250764e43122ae","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Anna","given":"Z."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, abalone (Haliotis spp.) in Australia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XNWWHcCi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mayfield, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Mayfield, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":361,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IKS7P457"],"itemData":{"id":361,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1080/10641262.2012.725434","issue":"4","page":"220-250","title":"Fifty years of sustained production from the Australian abalone fisheries","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84883494979&doi=10.1080%2f10641262.2012.725434&partnerID=40&md5=e454e226ff4869e559bf765e641c92b0","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Mayfield","given":"S."},{"family":"Mundy","given":"C."},{"family":"Gorfine","given":"H."},{"family":"Hart","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Worthington","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and co-managed finfish fisheries in Bangladesh ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bfyoaCBa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mazumder, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Mazumder, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1015,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D8ETAJCP"],"itemData":{"id":1015,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"AACL Bioflux","issue":"3","page":"466-482","title":"Role of co-management in wetland productivity: A case study from Hail haor in Bangladesh","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84969920189&partnerID=40&md5=1a574cfedbc3368f0fa826c272d917c3","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Mazumder","given":"S.K."},{"family":"Das","given":"S.K."},{"family":"Ghaffar","given":"M.A."},{"family":"Rahman","given":"M.H."},{"family":"Majumder","given":"M.K."},{"family":"Basak","given":"L.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The analysis of a long time series of 3,000 years involving both indigenous and local knowledge from fishers and archaeological data indicates no major changes in catch composition of fish and invertebrates exploited in the American Samoa, where catches are at lower levels than the estimated stock sizes of reef fish and fishing yields (kg/ha) correspond to those of less fished Pacific Islands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2d8bblpar","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Craig, 2008)","plainCitation":"(P. Craig, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1047,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A875WIT3"],"itemData":{"id":1047,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2007.08.018","issue":"3","page":"230-240","title":"Subsistence harvest of coral reef resources in the outer islands of American Samoa: Modern, historic and prehistoric catches","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-39149126693&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2007.08.018&partnerID=40&md5=3b51a3fc615d2c20af3fa5e686a4d167","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Craig","given":"P."},{"family":"Green","given":"A."},{"family":"Tuilagi","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This sustainable pattern may be related to a relatively small population of fishers who fish primarily for subsistence and, even considering that sales increased over time, other economic opportunities may have reduced reliance on fishing and hence fishing pressure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"atp515dtic","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Craig, 2008)","plainCitation":"(P. Craig, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1047,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A875WIT3"],"itemData":{"id":1047,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2007.08.018","issue":"3","page":"230-240","title":"Subsistence harvest of coral reef resources in the outer islands of American Samoa: Modern, historic and prehistoric catches","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-39149126693&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2007.08.018&partnerID=40&md5=3b51a3fc615d2c20af3fa5e686a4d167","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Craig","given":"P."},{"family":"Green","given":"A."},{"family":"Tuilagi","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	The observed fisheries’ sustainability in the Torres Strait Islands could also be partially related to more subsistence-oriented fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FjpMUfIa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Busilacchi, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Busilacchi, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":379,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3LZ8AZLF"],"itemData":{"id":379,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2012.08.017","page":"50-58","title":"Quantifying changes in the subsistence reef fishery of indigenous communities in Torres Strait, Australia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84867017341&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2012.08.017&partnerID=40&md5=36a0db1b9b09c53ff9adb6036973fc7b","volume":"137","author":[{"family":"Busilacchi","given":"S."},{"family":"Russ","given":"G.R."},{"family":"Williams","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Begg","given":"G.A."},{"family":"Sutton","given":"S.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, in French Polynesia catches of reef fish have been stable for nine years, even after major natural disturbances including a cyclone. This could be partially due to government subsidy that reduced poverty among fishers, who are mostly part time ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SG6oRCye","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rassweiler, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Rassweiler, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1064,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IK2I4JXB"],"itemData":{"id":1064,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ambio","DOI":"10.1007/s13280-019-01154-5","issue":"1","page":"130-143","title":"Perceptions and responses of Pacific Island fishers to changing coral reefs","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85062647426&doi=10.1007%2fs13280-019-01154-5&partnerID=40&md5=99d42c652a32f18cf8c2a4018bb0a5f3","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Rassweiler","given":"A."},{"family":"Lauer","given":"M."},{"family":"Lester","given":"S.E."},{"family":"Holbrook","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Schmitt","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Madi Moussa","given":"R."},{"family":"Munsterman","given":"K.S."},{"family":"Lenihan","given":"H.S."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Wencélius","given":"J."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, these are exceptions among the Pacific Island countries, where most cases of sustainable or potentially sustainable fisheries are usually linked to some form of co-management or customary management system, such as periodic harvest closures ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uhcpw2f5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. J. Cohen & Alexander, 2013; P. J. Cohen, 2013; P. J. Cohen & Foale, 2013)","plainCitation":"(P. J. Cohen & Alexander, 2013; P. J. Cohen, 2013; P. J. Cohen & Foale, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":372,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UB5ASWYE"],"itemData":{"id":372,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0073383","issue":"9","title":"Catch Rates, Composition and Fish Size from Reefs Managed with Periodically-Harvested Closures","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84884187782&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0073383&partnerID=40&md5=a226a13dc7a683b628fea812c4c68bf5","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Cohen","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Alexander","given":"T.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1051,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RZETPG5C"],"itemData":{"id":1051,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2013.08.010","issue":"6","page":"1702-1713","title":"Fishing dynamics associated with periodically harvested marine closures","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84885425703&doi=10.1016%2fj.gloenvcha.2013.08.010&partnerID=40&md5=ef02c031492d506b97dbc6bc1ff869c6","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Cohen","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Cinner","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Foale","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":386,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P5WMZSU7"],"itemData":{"id":386,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2012.05.010","issue":"1","page":"278-287","title":"Sustaining small-scale fisheries with periodically harvested marine reserves","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84867331391&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2012.05.010&partnerID=40&md5=97e90d359d0fb32c61f04cd63be1a4a1","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Cohen","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Foale","given":"S.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although promising, these co-management systems have shown variable results depending on the life history of exploited species, the size of managed area and the regime of opening and closing the area to fishing, which regulates the fishing pressure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nywIP053","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. J. Cohen & Foale, 2013)","plainCitation":"(P. J. Cohen & Foale, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":386,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P5WMZSU7"],"itemData":{"id":386,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2012.05.010","issue":"1","page":"278-287","title":"Sustaining small-scale fisheries with periodically harvested marine reserves","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84867331391&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2012.05.010&partnerID=40&md5=97e90d359d0fb32c61f04cd63be1a4a1","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Cohen","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Foale","given":"S.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Therefore, some of these co-management systems improved fisheries yields for fast-growing exploited species in a context of moderate or low fishing intensity. Others have shown a decline of larger and slow growing species (reef fish), usually associated with smaller closed areas or more intense fishing promoted by shorter closed intervals (less than one year) and longer opening periods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IQUTBjPb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. J. Cohen & Foale, 2013; Goetze, Langlois, Claudet, Januchowski-Hartley, & Jupiter, 2016; Richard J. Hamilton, Hughes, Brown, Leve, & Kama, 2019; Rhodes, 2008; D. Yang & Pomeroy, 2017)","plainCitation":"(P. J. Cohen & Foale, 2013; Goetze, Langlois, Claudet, Januchowski-Hartley, & Jupiter, 2016; Richard J. Hamilton, Hughes, Brown, Leve, & Kama, 2019; Rhodes, 2008; D. Yang & Pomeroy, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":386,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P5WMZSU7"],"itemData":{"id":386,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2012.05.010","issue":"1","page":"278-287","title":"Sustaining small-scale fisheries with periodically harvested marine reserves","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84867331391&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2012.05.010&partnerID=40&md5=97e90d359d0fb32c61f04cd63be1a4a1","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Cohen","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Foale","given":"S.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":362,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CTC8IKS4"],"itemData":{"id":362,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2016.08.038","page":"67-74","title":"Periodically harvested closures require full protection of vulnerable species and longer closure periods","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84988028108&doi=10.1016%2fj.biocon.2016.08.038&partnerID=40&md5=b457eefad14ff12523d64b23090daf36","volume":"203","author":[{"family":"Goetze","given":"J."},{"family":"Langlois","given":"T."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"J."},{"family":"Januchowski-Hartley","given":"F."},{"family":"Jupiter","given":"S.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":34296,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2FA5ZCMN"],"itemData":{"id":34296,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Coral Reefs","DOI":"10.1007/s00338-019-01801-z","ISSN":"0722-4028, 1432-0975","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Coral Reefs","language":"en","page":"455-465","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community-based management fails to halt declines of bumphead parrotfish and humphead wrasse in Roviana Lagoon, Solomon Islands","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00338-019-01801-z","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Hamilton","given":"Richard J."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Alec"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Christopher J."},{"family":"Leve","given":"Tingo"},{"family":"Kama","given":"Warren"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1063,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QBHMV6WH"],"itemData":{"id":1063,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Coral Reefs","DOI":"10.1007/s00338-007-0331-x","issue":"2","page":"443-454","title":"Characterization and management of the commercial sector of the Pohnpei coral reef fishery, Micronesia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-42549094240&doi=10.1007%2fs00338-007-0331-x&partnerID=40&md5=1ec86efa3ed300b20f805f7df8cedfc3","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Rhodes","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Tupper","given":"M.H."},{"family":"Wichilmel","given":"C.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":360,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QQW4K4VF"],"itemData":{"id":360,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.09.027","page":"173-181","title":"The impact of community-based fisheries management (CBFM) on equity and sustainability of small-scale coastal fisheries in the Philippines","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85032488364&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2017.09.027&partnerID=40&md5=76d0c3c7aaacb7248e14d9e8598dda8d","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Yang","given":"D."},{"family":"Pomeroy","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Even the more sustainable reef fisheries observed in American Samoa and French Polynesia show a lack of larger piscivorous reef fish, suggesting these larger predators may have been intensively fished in the past ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ejb5jh44v","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Craig, 2008; Rassweiler, 2020)","plainCitation":"(P. Craig, 2008; Rassweiler, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1047,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A875WIT3"],"itemData":{"id":1047,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2007.08.018","issue":"3","page":"230-240","title":"Subsistence harvest of coral reef resources in the outer islands of American Samoa: Modern, historic and prehistoric catches","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-39149126693&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2007.08.018&partnerID=40&md5=3b51a3fc615d2c20af3fa5e686a4d167","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Craig","given":"P."},{"family":"Green","given":"A."},{"family":"Tuilagi","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":1064,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IK2I4JXB"],"itemData":{"id":1064,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ambio","DOI":"10.1007/s13280-019-01154-5","issue":"1","page":"130-143","title":"Perceptions and responses of Pacific Island fishers to changing coral reefs","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85062647426&doi=10.1007%2fs13280-019-01154-5&partnerID=40&md5=99d42c652a32f18cf8c2a4018bb0a5f3","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Rassweiler","given":"A."},{"family":"Lauer","given":"M."},{"family":"Lester","given":"S.E."},{"family":"Holbrook","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Schmitt","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Madi Moussa","given":"R."},{"family":"Munsterman","given":"K.S."},{"family":"Lenihan","given":"H.S."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Wencélius","given":"J."},{"family":"Claudet","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some Pacific Island countries, fisheries for small pelagic fish could be a sustainable alternative for food production, as these fish seem to be more resilient to fishing pressure compared to larger reef fish, as observed in the Solomon Islands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Td0WKnhI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Roeger, Foale, & Sheaves, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Roeger, Foale, & Sheaves, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31897,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7UREMR88"],"itemData":{"id":31897,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2015.10.016","page":"250-259","title":"When 'fishing down the food chain' results in improved food security: Evidence from a small pelagic fishery in Solomon Islands","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84945291842&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2015.10.016&partnerID=40&md5=9645b9e1badd807b6b652be9db8c28de","volume":"174","author":[{"family":"Roeger","given":"J."},{"family":"Foale","given":"S."},{"family":"Sheaves","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Timor Leste, where fishing aggregation devices and co-management has helped to improve sustainability of coastal and reef fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XT3fOX7d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tilley, Hunnam,, 2019; Tilley, Wilkinson,, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Tilley, Hunnam,, 2019; Tilley, Wilkinson,, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31731,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7MYGCJQ8"],"itemData":{"id":31731,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2019.00392","issue":"JUL","title":"Evaluating the fit of co-management for small-scale fisheries governance in timor-leste","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85069781680&doi=10.3389%2ffmars.2019.00392&partnerID=40&md5=33e93601ea449dd2863fd1ce49cfa089","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Tilley","given":"A."},{"family":"Hunnam","given":"K.J."},{"family":"Mills","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Steenbergen","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Govan","given":"H."},{"family":"Alonso-Poblacion","given":"E."},{"family":"Roscher","given":"M."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"M."},{"family":"Rodrigues","given":"P."},{"family":"Amador","given":"T."},{"family":"Duarte","given":"A."},{"family":"Gomes","given":"M."},{"family":"Cohen","given":"P.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":384,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4KN94SQG"],"itemData":{"id":384,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2019.00487","issue":"JUL","title":"Nearshore fish aggregating devices show positive outcomes for sustainable fisheries development in Timor-Leste","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85069789103&doi=10.3389%2ffmars.2019.00487&partnerID=40&md5=8ac7cdd40a9618e7325eafd5d6fe2e52","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Tilley","given":"A."},{"family":"Wilkinson","given":"S.P."},{"family":"Kolding","given":"J."},{"family":"López-Angarita","given":"J."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"M."},{"family":"Mills","given":"D.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	There are examples of co-management measures that helped to recover the abundance and hence to improve sustainability of fisheries resources, such as shellfish in Fiji ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EfCXvfBl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Thaman, Thaman, Balawa, & Veitayaki, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Thaman, Thaman, Balawa, & Veitayaki, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":380,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3RFSXGW5"],"itemData":{"id":380,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","DOI":"10.2993/0278-0771-37.3.494","issue":"3","page":"494-513","title":"The Recovery of a Tropical Marine Mollusk Fishery: A Transdisciplinary Community-Based Approach in Navakavu, Fiji","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85029575009&doi=10.2993%2f0278-0771-37.3.494&partnerID=40&md5=5b5163569be33036d1fc3cfd860050af","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Thaman","given":"B."},{"family":"Thaman","given":"R.R."},{"family":"Balawa","given":"A."},{"family":"Veitayaki","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and reef fish in Hawaii ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Z7RIIvOs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Friedlander, Shackeroff, & Kittinger, 2013; Friedlander, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Friedlander, Shackeroff, & Kittinger, 2013; Friedlander, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":385,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3SJTUDTK"],"itemData":{"id":385,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pacific Science","DOI":"10.2984/67.3.10","issue":"3","page":"441-460","title":"Customary marine resource knowledge and use in contemporary Hawai'i","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84879875991&doi=10.2984%2f67.3.10&partnerID=40&md5=e842484c286f53c124cf9aa880a6b290","volume":"67","author":[{"family":"Friedlander","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Shackeroff","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Kittinger","given":"J.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1282,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L6WFDPKD"],"itemData":{"id":1282,"type":"book","collection-title":"Advances in Marine Biology","note":"page: 203\\nDOI: 10.1016/B978-0-12-800214-8.00005-0","number-of-pages":"153","title":"Understanding the Scale of Marine Protection in Hawai'i: From Community-Based Management to the Remote Northwestern Hawaiian Islands","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84908161069&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-12-800214-8.00005-0&partnerID=40&md5=24e69576bd7f0bfe52fe4f247ae8a08f","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Friedlander","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Stamoulis","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Kittinger","given":"J.N."},{"family":"Drazen","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Tissot","given":"B.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, some studies also indicated declines in catches of Hawaiian fisheries for octopus and reef fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fvCKqjXM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Delaney, 2017; Kittinger, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Delaney, 2017; Kittinger, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31906,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AMLLKR76"],"itemData":{"id":31906,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.4089","issue":"12","title":"Patterns in artisanal coral reef fisheries revealed through local monitoring efforts","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85036473855&doi=10.7717%2fpeerj.4089&partnerID=40&md5=fee592bbea576f809a1e6ea4b35d0e66","volume":"2017","author":[{"family":"Delaney","given":"D.G."},{"family":"Teneva","given":"L.T."},{"family":"Stamoulis","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Giddens","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Koike","given":"H."},{"family":"Ogawa","given":"T."},{"family":"Friedlander","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Kittinger","given":"J.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31909,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GBER3CVP"],"itemData":{"id":31909,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0123856","issue":"8","title":"From reef to table: Social and ecological factors affecting coral reef fisheries, artisanal seafood supply chains, and seafood security","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84941979160&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0123856&partnerID=40&md5=df6bcb78935871037f484bc6ab256ad2","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Kittinger","given":"J.N."},{"family":"Teneva","given":"L.T."},{"family":"Koike","given":"H."},{"family":"Stamoulis","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Kittinger","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Oleson","given":"K.L.L."},{"family":"Conklin","given":"E."},{"family":"Gomes","given":"M."},{"family":"Wilcox","given":"B."},{"family":"Friedlander","given":"A.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An analysis of bioeconomic modelling, which included stock parameters in addition to data on catch, effort, revenues and costs, indicated that shrimp fisheries could be sustainable in Indonesia by showing increased catches over a period of 27 years and surplus stocks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2bkvb1sq6a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Anna, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Anna, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1048,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P4HDFX28"],"itemData":{"id":1048,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversitas","DOI":"10.13057/biodiv/d180132","issue":"1","page":"248-256","title":"Indonesian shrimp resource accounting for sustainable stock management","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85007575346&doi=10.13057%2fbiodiv%2fd180132&partnerID=40&md5=40ae790b6fbb4d30fd250764e43122ae","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Anna","given":"Z."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, potential side effects or impacts from shrimp fisheries, such as by-catch or habitat damage, were not included in this study ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ilusgc245","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Anna, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Anna, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1048,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P4HDFX28"],"itemData":{"id":1048,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversitas","DOI":"10.13057/biodiv/d180132","issue":"1","page":"248-256","title":"Indonesian shrimp resource accounting for sustainable stock management","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85007575346&doi=10.13057%2fbiodiv%2fd180132&partnerID=40&md5=40ae790b6fbb4d30fd250764e43122ae","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Anna","given":"Z."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which could compromise the overall sustainability of this fishery. A moratorium of one year imposed on large scale fisheries for tuna in Indonesia had mixed effects on catches of the small-scale pole and line tuna fisheries, but fishers considered that the moratorium was positive and increased their catches, indicating the potential conflicts and competition between large- and small-scale fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FdSDGlut","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Khan, Gray, Mill, & Polunin, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Khan, Gray, Mill, & Polunin, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1008,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6J6Y5L9U"],"itemData":{"id":1008,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2018.05.014","page":"143-149","title":"Impact of a fishing moratorium on a tuna pole-and-line fishery in eastern Indonesia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85047055887&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2018.05.014&partnerID=40&md5=8fdc93fbf293ac7abb3e6bae795c0f16","volume":"94","author":[{"family":"Khan","given":"A.M.A."},{"family":"Gray","given":"T.S."},{"family":"Mill","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Polunin","given":"N.V.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Some cases of unsustainable coastal small-scale fisheries include sharks in Indonesia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qXU6p1KJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ainsworth, Pitcher, & Rotinsulu, 2008; Ferse, 2014; Jaiteh, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Ainsworth, Pitcher, & Rotinsulu, 2008; Ferse, 2014; Jaiteh, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1004,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RKZECRA5"],"itemData":{"id":1004,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2008.01.006","issue":"3","page":"848-859","title":"Evidence of fishery depletions and shifting cognitive baselines in Eastern Indonesia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-40349101811&doi=10.1016%2fj.biocon.2008.01.006&partnerID=40&md5=e033ef546b879204cbc643fdad3b5117","volume":"141","author":[{"family":"Ainsworth","given":"C.H."},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"T.J."},{"family":"Rotinsulu","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":8795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S78WWUR9","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6WNBIHG"],"itemData":{"id":8795,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Small-scale fisheries in coral reef areas support the livelihoods of millions of people worldwide. Anthropogenic impacts such as overfishing and climate change increasingly threaten both the reef ecosystem and the livelihood security of the people that depend on the reefs. Adaptive management strategies are needed to adequately deal with these threats, but they require an understanding of the underlying drivers, which often originate and act on multiple levels. Using a social-ecological system approach, the coral reef fishery of the Spermonde Archipelago in South Sulawesi/Indonesia is assessed to identify key drivers and strategic leverage points for management. Under the influence of international markets and technological changes, several export-oriented fisheries have developed in the area that led to distinct subsequent peaks in fishing activity in a pattern of sequential marine resource exploitation. In response to stressors such as seasonality and overfishing of individual locations or species, a number of coping strategies have developed locally. These include extensive borrowing from fishing patrons, diversification of fishing methods, fishing migrations, and the crafting of local institutions to regulate fishing activity. However, the coping strategies hinder, and even decrease, the capacity of the system to adapt to future stressors and undermine the sustainability of the fishery. Potential strategies that target different levels of the fishery system in order to strengthen adaptive management are identified.","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10113-012-0342-1","ISSN":"1436-378X","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Reg Environ Change","language":"en","page":"2053-2065","source":"Springer Link","title":"To cope or to sustain? Eroding long-term sustainability in an Indonesian coral reef fishery","title-short":"To cope or to sustain?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10113-012-0342-1","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Neil","given":"Muhammad"},{"family":"Schwerdtner Máñez","given":"Kathleen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12,1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1053,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2KGS695T"],"itemData":{"id":1053,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsw170","issue":"1","page":"242-253","title":"Shark finning in eastern Indonesia: Assessing the sustainability of a data-poor fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85020604458&doi=10.1093%2ficesjms%2ffsw170&partnerID=40&md5=ce2394ff4f77b9989abadbd6d039e806","volume":"74","author":[{"family":"Jaiteh","given":"V.F."},{"family":"Hordyk","given":"A.R."},{"family":"Braccini","given":"M."},{"family":"Warren","given":"C."},{"family":"Loneragan","given":"N.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and China ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"w3YF4jJy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. Y. Y. Lam & Sadovy de Mitcheson, 2011)","plainCitation":"(V. Y. Y. Lam & Sadovy de Mitcheson, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1422,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZAFMXGR4","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UAY6LMNN"],"itemData":{"id":1422,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-2979.2010.00383.x","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"51-74","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The sharks of South East Asia - unknown, unmonitored and unmanaged: Sharks of South East Asia","title-short":"The sharks of South East Asia - unknown, unmonitored and unmanaged","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1467-2979.2010.00383.x","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Lam","given":"Vivian Y Y"},{"family":"Sadovy de Mitcheson","given":"Yvonne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",3]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and sawfish in Bangladesh ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"u1iybKXT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hossain, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Hossain, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1016,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7VCJJTFB"],"itemData":{"id":1016,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2466","issue":"6","page":"781-799","title":"Sawfish exploitation and status in Bangladesh","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84954198622&doi=10.1002%2faqc.2466&partnerID=40&md5=c653e3f4de86b672158cf5307938a7e4","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Hossain","given":"M.A."},{"family":"Thompson","given":"B.S."},{"family":"Chowdhury","given":"G.W."},{"family":"Mohsanin","given":"S."},{"family":"Fahad","given":"Z.H."},{"family":"Koldewey","given":"H.J."},{"family":"Islam","given":"M.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In China, a comprehensive study including market surveys and interviews with fishers in Hong Kong and mainland southern China indicates an overall depletion of sharks in the South China sea, where shark fisheries collapsed between 1970s and 1990s, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"if7wRLJH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. Y. Y. Lam & Sadovy de Mitcheson, 2011)","plainCitation":"(V. Y. Y. Lam & Sadovy de Mitcheson, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1422,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZAFMXGR4","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UAY6LMNN"],"itemData":{"id":1422,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-2979.2010.00383.x","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"51-74","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The sharks of South East Asia - unknown, unmonitored and unmanaged: Sharks of South East Asia","title-short":"The sharks of South East Asia - unknown, unmonitored and unmanaged","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1467-2979.2010.00383.x","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Lam","given":"Vivian Y Y"},{"family":"Sadovy de Mitcheson","given":"Yvonne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Notwithstanding management measures implemented by the Chinese government, a recent study analyzing landings’ data and fishing effort from China for the period of 1955–2019, shows variable decadal trends with an overall adverse effect from increased fishing intensity on piscivorous fishes, including sharks and rays ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"L7dwuK51","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Liu, 2021)","plainCitation":"(D. Liu, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AZNU4WS7"],"itemData":{"id":9749,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2021.581952","title":"Long-Term Variability of Piscivorous Fish in China Seas Under Climate Change With Implication for Fisheries Management","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85108996597&doi=10.3389%2ffmars.2021.581952&partnerID=40&md5=7449e14510e359c975700cd095083e87","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"D."},{"family":"Tian","given":"Y."},{"family":"Ma","given":"S."},{"family":"Li","given":"J."},{"family":"Sun","given":"P."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Z."},{"family":"Fu","given":"C."},{"family":"Lan","given":"K."},{"family":"Zhou","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Effects from overfishing can interact synergistically with effects from climate change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WZFxIvkk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Liu, 2021)","plainCitation":"(D. Liu, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AZNU4WS7"],"itemData":{"id":9749,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2021.581952","title":"Long-Term Variability of Piscivorous Fish in China Seas Under Climate Change With Implication for Fisheries Management","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85108996597&doi=10.3389%2ffmars.2021.581952&partnerID=40&md5=7449e14510e359c975700cd095083e87","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"D."},{"family":"Tian","given":"Y."},{"family":"Ma","given":"S."},{"family":"Li","given":"J."},{"family":"Sun","given":"P."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Z."},{"family":"Fu","given":"C."},{"family":"Lan","given":"K."},{"family":"Zhou","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Moreover, China, especially Hong Kong, is the world largest market for shark fins, thus driving exploitation and trade of sharks worldwide, usually at unsustainable levels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YdxXf8gQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eriksson & Clarke, 2015; Fields, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Eriksson & Clarke, 2015; Fields, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9771,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZQU3DUFX"],"itemData":{"id":9771,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2015.01.018","page":"163-173","title":"Chinese market responses to overexploitation of sharks and sea cucumbers","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84922513945&doi=10.1016%2fj.biocon.2015.01.018&partnerID=40&md5=2176d741ed1d1b35dd3f7476a642bcf7","volume":"184","author":[{"family":"Eriksson","given":"H."},{"family":"Clarke","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":734,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YQTP4BTA"],"itemData":{"id":734,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The shark fin trade is a major driver of shark exploitation in fisheries all over the world, most of which are not managed on a species-specific basis. Species-specific trade information highlights taxa of particular concern and can be used to assess the efficacy of management measures and anticipate emerging threats. The species composition of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region of China, one of the world's largest fin trading hubs, was partially assessed in 1999–2001. We randomly selected and genetically identified fin trimmings (n = 4800), produced during fin processing, from the retail market of Hong Kong in 2014–2015 to assess contemporary species composition of the fin trade. We used nonparametric species estimators to determine that at least 76 species of sharks, batoids, and chimaeras supplied the fin trade and a Bayesian model to determine their relative proportion in the market. The diversity of traded species suggests species substitution could mask depletion of vulnerable species; one-third of identified species are threatened with extinction. The Bayesian model suggested that 8 species each comprised >1% of the fin trimmings (34.1–64.2% for blue [Prionace glauca], 0.2–1.2% for bull [Carcharhinus leucas] and shortfin mako [Isurus oxyrinchus]); thus, trade was skewed to a few globally distributed species. Several other coastal sharks, batoids, and chimaeras are in the trade but poorly managed. Fewer than 10 of the species we modeled have sustainably managed fisheries anywhere in their range, and the most common species in trade, the blue shark, was not among them. Our study and approach serve as a baseline to track changes in composition of species in the fin trade over time to better understand patterns of exploitation and assess the effects of emerging management actions for these animals.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/cobi.13043","ISSN":"1523-1739","issue":"2","language":"en","license":"© 2017 The Authors. Conservation Biology published by Wiley Periodicals, Inc. on behalf of Society for Conservation Biology.","note":"_eprint: https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/cobi.13043","page":"376-389","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Species composition of the international shark fin trade assessed through a retail-market survey in Hong Kong","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/cobi.13043","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Fields","given":"Andrew T."},{"family":"Fischer","given":"Gunter A."},{"family":"Shea","given":"Stanley K. H."},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Huarong"},{"family":"Abercrombie","given":"Debra L."},{"family":"Feldheim","given":"Kevin A."},{"family":"Babcock","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Chapman","given":"Demian D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, shark fisheries can be at least partially sustainable in the Great Barrier Reef (Australia), where susceptibility to fisheries vary among species and their life histories, as smaller species are caught mainly as adults (less vulnerable), whereas larger ones are regularly caught as juveniles, and thus more vulnerable to fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zd7TW6GP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harry, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Harry, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31903,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B94ARV8Q"],"itemData":{"id":31903,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine and Freshwater Research","ISSN":"1448-6059","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Marine and Freshwater Research","page":"710-721","title":"Evaluating catch and mitigating risk in a multispecies, tropical, inshore shark fishery within the Great Barrier Reef World Heritage Area","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Harry","given":"Alastair V"},{"family":"Tobin","given":"Andrew J"},{"family":"Simpfendorfer","given":"Colin A"},{"family":"Welch","given":"David J"},{"family":"Mapleston","given":"Amos"},{"family":"White","given":"Jimmy"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Ashley J"},{"family":"Stapley","given":"Jason"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Furthermore, in Eastern Indonesia the whale shark (Rhincodon typus) is not commercially exploited due to customary beliefs among Bajao people that prohibit harvesting this fish, low market values of shark meat and skin, and a lack of technology to harvest such a large fish. No catch data is provided for other regions of Indonesia where this shark could be commercially fished ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UKZcjlnV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stacey, Karam, Meekan, Pickering, & Ninef, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Stacey, Karam, Meekan, Pickering, & Ninef, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31900,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RZWZXL4R"],"itemData":{"id":31900,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Conservation and Society","DOI":"10.4103/0972-4923.92197","issue":"1","page":"63-75","title":"Prospects for whale shark conservation in Eastern Indonesia through bajo traditional ecological knowledge and community-based monitoring","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84856595278&doi=10.4103%2f0972-4923.92197&partnerID=40&md5=a2c1807324a5caaac644ce3987b691c7","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Stacey","given":"N.E."},{"family":"Karam","given":"J."},{"family":"Meekan","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Pickering","given":"S."},{"family":"Ninef","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Unsustainable patterns have been also observed in several countries for fisheries of sea cucumbers (Holoturidae), which usually shows a typical cycle of boom and bust typically ending in sharp declines ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mhQLlSZv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eriksson, 2018; Hair, 2016; Prescott, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Eriksson, 2018; Hair, 2016; Prescott, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31738,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8FU766DT"],"itemData":{"id":31738,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12255","issue":"2","page":"308-320","title":"Geography limits island small-scale fishery production","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85032841558&doi=10.1111%2ffaf.12255&partnerID=40&md5=de9686482b5c54c9b7d1cb955681b733","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Eriksson","given":"H."},{"family":"Friedman","given":"K."},{"family":"Amos","given":"M."},{"family":"Bertram","given":"I."},{"family":"Pakoa","given":"K."},{"family":"Fisher","given":"R."},{"family":"Andrew","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31735,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DRJLLXTX"],"itemData":{"id":31735,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Regional Studies in Marine Science","DOI":"10.1016/j.rsma.2016.02.001","page":"69-79","title":"Beyond boom, bust and ban: The sandfish (Holothuria scabra) fishery in the Tigak Islands, Papua New Guinea","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84958748927&doi=10.1016%2fj.rsma.2016.02.001&partnerID=40&md5=e116f18e258f57cafe4342028c36f58c","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Hair","given":"C."},{"family":"Foale","given":"S."},{"family":"Kinch","given":"J."},{"family":"Yaman","given":"L."},{"family":"Southgate","given":"P.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31737,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F54LKFKK"],"itemData":{"id":31737,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.03.033","page":"197-205","title":"The money side of livelihoods: Economics of an unregulated small-scale Indonesian sea cucumber fishery in the Timor Sea","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85017452886&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2017.03.033&partnerID=40&md5=570c9d1e6fa2a6653d74f32bff10e791","volume":"82","author":[{"family":"Prescott","given":"J."},{"family":"Riwu","given":"J."},{"family":"Prasetyo","given":"A.P."},{"family":"Stacey","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The large reef fish that form predictable spawning or feeding aggregations, such as groupers or large herbivores, may be negatively affected by unsustainable practices, such as night spearfishing and catches of juveniles, as these slow growing fish are vulnerable to intense fishing during aggregation periods, even in regions under co-management systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xXYiceaT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Richard J. Hamilton, 2019; R.J. Hamilton, 2012; Rhodes, 2008; J. Robinson, Cinner, & Graham, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Richard J. Hamilton, 2019; R.J. Hamilton, 2012; Rhodes, 2008; J. Robinson, Cinner, & Graham, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34296,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2FA5ZCMN"],"itemData":{"id":34296,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Coral Reefs","DOI":"10.1007/s00338-019-01801-z","ISSN":"0722-4028, 1432-0975","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Coral Reefs","language":"en","page":"455-465","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community-based management fails to halt declines of bumphead parrotfish and humphead wrasse in Roviana Lagoon, Solomon Islands","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00338-019-01801-z","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Hamilton","given":"Richard J."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Alec"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Christopher J."},{"family":"Leve","given":"Tingo"},{"family":"Kama","given":"Warren"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1052,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ECS8MMJR"],"itemData":{"id":1052,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2011.11.020","issue":"1","page":"246-257","title":"Fishing in the dark-local knowledge, night spearfishing and spawning aggregations in the Western Solomon Islands","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84856227224&doi=10.1016%2fj.biocon.2011.11.020&partnerID=40&md5=0ea587a3825f0b737a091b4d49bcb3e9","volume":"145","author":[{"family":"Hamilton","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Giningele","given":"M."},{"family":"Aswani","given":"S."},{"family":"Ecochard","given":"J.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1063,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QBHMV6WH"],"itemData":{"id":1063,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Coral Reefs","DOI":"10.1007/s00338-007-0331-x","issue":"2","page":"443-454","title":"Characterization and management of the commercial sector of the Pohnpei coral reef fishery, Micronesia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-42549094240&doi=10.1007%2fs00338-007-0331-x&partnerID=40&md5=1ec86efa3ed300b20f805f7df8cedfc3","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Rhodes","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Tupper","given":"M.H."},{"family":"Wichilmel","given":"C.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31905,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TJJEXW5Q"],"itemData":{"id":31905,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0091296","issue":"3","title":"The influence of fisher knowledge on the susceptibility of reef fish aggregations to fishing","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84898656797&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0091296&partnerID=40&md5=13e5eb9afcc86fc7625d09860b65abc9","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"J."},{"family":"Cinner","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Graham","given":"N.A.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Unsustainable nearshore coastal and reef fisheries have been observed in Southeast Asian countries located in biodiversity hotspots, such as Indonesia and Philippines, among others (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Multiple factors, including increased population, poverty, lack of economic alternatives other than fishing, pressure from domestic and international markets, open access and illegal fishing by using destructive practices (bombs, cyanide) lead to unsustainable levels of fishing effort and overall declines in catches of many fishing resources, such as reef and coastal fish, sharks, rays, sea cucumbers and lobsters in these biodiversity rich countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Q1HcsG81","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Acebes, Barr, Pereda, & Santos, 2016; Ainsworth, 2008; Ferse, 2014; Jaiteh, 2017; Khasanah, Nurdin, Sadovy de Mitcheson, & Jompa, 2020; Macusi, 2019; Muallil, Mamauag, Cababaro,, 2014; Muallil, Mamauag, Cabral, Celeste-Dizon, & Ali\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}o, 2014; Prescott, 2017; Selgrath, 2018a, 2018b)","plainCitation":"(Acebes, Barr, Pereda, & Santos, 2016; Ainsworth, 2008; Ferse, 2014; Jaiteh, 2017; Khasanah, Nurdin, Sadovy de Mitcheson, & Jompa, 2020; Macusi, 2019; Muallil, Mamauag, Cababaro,, 2014; Muallil, Mamauag, Cabral, Celeste-Dizon, & Aliño, 2014; Prescott, 2017; Selgrath, 2018a, 2018b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31911,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UCKN4HAG"],"itemData":{"id":31911,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Biodiversity Records","DOI":"10.1186/s41200-016-0098-2","issue":"1","title":"Characteristics of a previously undescribed fishery and habitat for Manta alfredi in the Philippines","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85052333537&doi=10.1186%2fs41200-016-0098-2&partnerID=40&md5=d9abc2a8a74e0ba8f466f6fd4f3a8e68","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Acebes","given":"J.M.V."},{"family":"Barr","given":"Y."},{"family":"Pereda","given":"J.M.R."},{"family":"Santos","given":"M.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1004,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RKZECRA5"],"itemData":{"id":1004,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2008.01.006","issue":"3","page":"848-859","title":"Evidence of fishery depletions and shifting cognitive baselines in Eastern Indonesia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-40349101811&doi=10.1016%2fj.biocon.2008.01.006&partnerID=40&md5=e033ef546b879204cbc643fdad3b5117","volume":"141","author":[{"family":"Ainsworth","given":"C.H."},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"T.J."},{"family":"Rotinsulu","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":8795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S78WWUR9","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6WNBIHG"],"itemData":{"id":8795,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Small-scale fisheries in coral reef areas support the livelihoods of millions of people worldwide. Anthropogenic impacts such as overfishing and climate change increasingly threaten both the reef ecosystem and the livelihood security of the people that depend on the reefs. Adaptive management strategies are needed to adequately deal with these threats, but they require an understanding of the underlying drivers, which often originate and act on multiple levels. Using a social-ecological system approach, the coral reef fishery of the Spermonde Archipelago in South Sulawesi/Indonesia is assessed to identify key drivers and strategic leverage points for management. Under the influence of international markets and technological changes, several export-oriented fisheries have developed in the area that led to distinct subsequent peaks in fishing activity in a pattern of sequential marine resource exploitation. In response to stressors such as seasonality and overfishing of individual locations or species, a number of coping strategies have developed locally. These include extensive borrowing from fishing patrons, diversification of fishing methods, fishing migrations, and the crafting of local institutions to regulate fishing activity. However, the coping strategies hinder, and even decrease, the capacity of the system to adapt to future stressors and undermine the sustainability of the fishery. Potential strategies that target different levels of the fishery system in order to strengthen adaptive management are identified.","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10113-012-0342-1","ISSN":"1436-378X","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Reg Environ Change","language":"en","page":"2053-2065","source":"Springer Link","title":"To cope or to sustain? Eroding long-term sustainability in an Indonesian coral reef fishery","title-short":"To cope or to sustain?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10113-012-0342-1","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Neil","given":"Muhammad"},{"family":"Schwerdtner Máñez","given":"Kathleen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12,1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1053,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2KGS695T"],"itemData":{"id":1053,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsw170","issue":"1","page":"242-253","title":"Shark finning in eastern Indonesia: Assessing the sustainability of a data-poor fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85020604458&doi=10.1093%2ficesjms%2ffsw170&partnerID=40&md5=ce2394ff4f77b9989abadbd6d039e806","volume":"74","author":[{"family":"Jaiteh","given":"V.F."},{"family":"Hordyk","given":"A.R."},{"family":"Braccini","given":"M."},{"family":"Warren","given":"C."},{"family":"Loneragan","given":"N.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31910,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/29P2UECY"],"itemData":{"id":31910,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science and Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1080/23308249.2018.1542420","issue":"1","page":"1-15","title":"Management of the Grouper Export Trade in Indonesia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85073809562&doi=10.1080%2f23308249.2018.1542420&partnerID=40&md5=ed9f907e22d6541c5a36ae0806519327","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Khasanah","given":"M."},{"family":"Nurdin","given":"N."},{"family":"Sadovy de Mitcheson","given":"Y."},{"family":"Jompa","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CSY2ZMI9"],"itemData":{"id":31733,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2018.11.010","page":"340-349","title":"Wild lobster (Panulirus ornatus) fry fishery in Balete bay, Davao Oriental: Catch trends and implications to fisheries management","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85058150654&doi=10.1016%2fj.ocecoaman.2018.11.010&partnerID=40&md5=b153ada27a88e3a5b98d74a94710095f","volume":"168","author":[{"family":"Macusi","given":"E.D."},{"family":"Laya-og","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Abreo","given":"N.A.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31718,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QMLNCTQ3"],"itemData":{"id":31718,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2014.02.008","page":"110-117","title":"Catch trends in Philippine small-scale fisheries over the last five decades: The fishers perspectives","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84896754839&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2014.02.008&partnerID=40&md5=bf977bf1b312e0bba9f84f16d3bc9fb0","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Muallil","given":"R.N."},{"family":"Mamauag","given":"S.S."},{"family":"Cababaro","given":"J.T."},{"family":"Arceo","given":"H.O."},{"family":"Aliño","given":"P.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1003,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7K67U9C3"],"itemData":{"id":1003,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2013.08.026","page":"212-221","title":"Status, trends and challenges in the sustainability of small-scale fisheries in the Philippines: Insights from FISHDA (Fishing Industries' Support in Handling Decisions Application) model","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84889593125&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2013.08.026&partnerID=40&md5=1d69ada8e73398b6163d1737187be3c7","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Muallil","given":"R.N."},{"family":"Mamauag","given":"S.S."},{"family":"Cabral","given":"R.B."},{"family":"Celeste-Dizon","given":"E.O."},{"family":"Aliño","given":"P.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31737,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F54LKFKK"],"itemData":{"id":31737,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.03.033","page":"197-205","title":"The money side of livelihoods: Economics of an unregulated small-scale Indonesian sea cucumber fishery in the Timor Sea","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85017452886&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2017.03.033&partnerID=40&md5=570c9d1e6fa2a6653d74f32bff10e791","volume":"82","author":[{"family":"Prescott","given":"J."},{"family":"Riwu","given":"J."},{"family":"Prasetyo","given":"A.P."},{"family":"Stacey","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1088,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WC32I4MK"],"itemData":{"id":1088,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsx108","issue":"1","page":"210-220","title":"Incorporating spatial dynamics greatly increases estimates of long-term fishing effort: A participatory mapping approach","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85041291260&doi=10.1093%2ficesjms%2ffsx108&partnerID=40&md5=5387b3854a4261b170a53b2a3b5155c2","volume":"75","author":[{"family":"Selgrath","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Gergel","given":"S.E."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A.C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31734,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YVZ7ELEA"],"itemData":{"id":31734,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0190232","issue":"3","title":"Shifting gears: Diversification, intensification, and effort increases in small-scale fisheries (1950-2010)","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85043760660&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0190232&partnerID=40&md5=7a9dfca520f8b4c3b3dfe6fe4602ebdc","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Selgrath","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Gergel","given":"S.E."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A.C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      None of the 10 studies addressing use of fish or invertebrates for food in coastal and inland small-scale fisheries in Asia Pacific indicate sustainable fisheries (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). These fisheries are usually considered unsustainable due to multiple and interacting effects of overfishing, lack of proper management, illegal or destructive fishing practices, coupled with habitat alteration by river dams, deforestation, pollution and increased water temperature, as observed in Bangladesh ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5m7JPMlQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ahmed, Rahman, Bunting, & Brugere, 2013; Jahan, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Ahmed, Rahman, Bunting, & Brugere, 2013; Jahan, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31817,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G7BBQSI6"],"itemData":{"id":31817,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Singapore Journal of Tropical Geography","DOI":"10.1111/sjtg.12015","issue":"1","page":"86-102","title":"Socio-economic and ecological challenges of small-scale fishing and strategies for its sustainable management: A case study of the Old Brahmaputra River, Bangladesh","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84874919647&doi=10.1111%2fsjtg.12015&partnerID=40&md5=989b044591a6501f7faf435139ca1730","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Ahmed","given":"N."},{"family":"Rahman","given":"S."},{"family":"Bunting","given":"S.W."},{"family":"Brugere","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31720,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KT6V78SQ"],"itemData":{"id":31720,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1007/s10668-015-9740-0","issue":"2","page":"461-478","title":"Fishers’ local knowledge on impact of climate change and anthropogenic interferences on Hilsa fishery in South Asia: evidence from Bangladesh","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84949506409&doi=10.1007%2fs10668-015-9740-0&partnerID=40&md5=3805100345ebc784b4cd141a9c618a65","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Jahan","given":"I."},{"family":"Ahsan","given":"D."},{"family":"Farque","given":"M.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Laos ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JbgURHzf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. N. E. Gray, 2017; Millar, 2019)","plainCitation":"(T. N. E. Gray, 2017; Millar, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31721,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LXJQBVHQ"],"itemData":{"id":31721,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0183247","issue":"8","title":"Using local ecological knowledge to monitor threatened Mekong megafauna in Lao PDR","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85027891924&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0183247&partnerID=40&md5=a43ebe8ea0d7138380a3cbc47b4b7108","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Gray","given":"T.N.E."},{"family":"Phommachak","given":"A."},{"family":"Vannachomchan","given":"K."},{"family":"Guegan","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31815,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JRFB5GSS"],"itemData":{"id":31815,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1007/s10668-018-0105-3","issue":"4","page":"1835-1852","title":"Local perceptions of changes in the use and management of floodplain fisheries commons: the case of Pak Peung wetland in Lao PDR","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85041908407&doi=10.1007%2fs10668-018-0105-3&partnerID=40&md5=028ca63770da18a18e028c1262fec095","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Millar","given":"J."},{"family":"Robinson","given":"W."},{"family":"Baumgartner","given":"L."},{"family":"Homsombath","given":"K."},{"family":"Chittavong","given":"M."},{"family":"Phommavong","given":"T."},{"family":"Singhanouvong","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and India ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"e0su3u7q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Dey, 2019; Keskar, Raghavan, Kumkar, Padhye, & Dahanukar, 2017)","plainCitation":"(S. Dey, 2019; Keskar, Raghavan, Kumkar, Padhye, & Dahanukar, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31722,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B7LY9ZDB"],"itemData":{"id":31722,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/fme.12390","title":"Identifying potential causes of fish declines through local ecological knowledge of fishers in the Ganga River, eastern Bihar, India","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85073981596&doi=10.1111%2ffme.12390&partnerID=40&md5=d83c7fb55176c2a9a0f52522ab60b13d","author":[{"family":"Dey","given":"S."},{"family":"Choudhary","given":"S.K."},{"family":"Dey","given":"S."},{"family":"Deshpande","given":"K."},{"family":"Kelkar","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31818,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HKNFD7PL"],"itemData":{"id":31818,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Aquatic Living Resources","DOI":"10.1051/alr/2016036","title":"Assessing the sustainability of subsistence fisheries of small indigenous fish species: Fishing mortality and exploitation of hill stream loaches in India","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85019033860&doi=10.1051%2falr%2f2016036&partnerID=40&md5=a1bbe2ee105e3adc5ef4d799e2e60f9d","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Keskar","given":"A."},{"family":"Raghavan","given":"R."},{"family":"Kumkar","given":"P."},{"family":"Padhye","given":"A."},{"family":"Dahanukar","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, an increase in low value fish has been observed in Cambodia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bEyPvkCp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Enomoto, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Enomoto, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":998,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X8WWWEKL"],"itemData":{"id":998,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1007/s12562-011-0378-z","issue":"5","page":"713-722","title":"Data mining and stock assessment of fisheries resources in Tonle Sap Lake, Cambodia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-80052403552&doi=10.1007%2fs12562-011-0378-z&partnerID=40&md5=65a2474efa259ba0c70827e1bf0c1563","volume":"77","author":[{"family":"Enomoto","given":"K."},{"family":"Ishikawa","given":"S."},{"family":"Hori","given":"M."},{"family":"Sitha","given":"H."},{"family":"Song","given":"S.L."},{"family":"Thuok","given":"N."},{"family":"Kurokura","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and co-management initiatives including fishers’ indigenous and local knowledge could be strategic and promising for recovery of fish stocks in the Mekong River Basin ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Pmr7eSOA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. G. Baird & Flaherty, 2005)","plainCitation":"(I. G. Baird & Flaherty, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31816,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I9Y8Z72Z"],"itemData":{"id":31816,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-005-3093-7","issue":"3","page":"439-454","title":"Mekong River Fish Conservation Zones in southern Laos: Assessing effectiveness using local ecological knowledge","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-26244439711&doi=10.1007%2fs00267-005-3093-7&partnerID=40&md5=ec2363b513e6b18a74f4a7b8d0d75794","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Baird","given":"I.G."},{"family":"Flaherty","given":"M.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and through community-based freshwater reserves in Thailand ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"75AQLeYi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Koning, Perales, Fluet-Chouinard, & McIntyre, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Koning, Perales, Fluet-Chouinard, & McIntyre, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31860,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SU92NRBX"],"itemData":{"id":31860,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-020-2944-y","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7839","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"631-635","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A network of grassroots reserves protects tropical river fish diversity","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-2944-y","volume":"588","author":[{"family":"Koning","given":"Aaron A."},{"family":"Perales","given":"K. Martin"},{"family":"Fluet-Chouinard","given":"Etienne"},{"family":"McIntyre","given":"Peter B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The widespread small-scale coastal fisheries in Japan have a long history of a strong bottom-up, co-management system of governance, actively including local fishers through the fishery cooperative associations, which cooperate with scientists and government to regulate fishing activity and allocate fishing grounds among coastal fishers, among other management activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Rhr77yeE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ganseforth, 2021; Makino, Matsuda, & Sakurai, 2009; Matsuda, Makino, & Sakurai, 2009; Teh, Teh, Abe, Ishimura, & Roman, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Ganseforth, 2021; Makino, Matsuda, & Sakurai, 2009; Matsuda, Makino, & Sakurai, 2009; Teh, Teh, Abe, Ishimura, & Roman, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9766,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TXBBSGQY"],"itemData":{"id":9766,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Geographical Journal","DOI":"10.1111/geoj.12414","title":"Blue revitalization or dispossession? Reform of common resource management in Japanese small-scale fisheries","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85117865491&doi=10.1111%2fgeoj.12414&partnerID=40&md5=9916aece19ef374ec3b1ef94eda36667","author":[{"family":"Ganseforth","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":9752,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RHPHBQN9"],"itemData":{"id":9752,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2008.05.013","issue":"2","page":"207-214","title":"Expanding fisheries co-management to ecosystem-based management: A case in the Shiretoko World Natural Heritage area, Japan","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-56949097451&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2008.05.013&partnerID=40&md5=90777bfc914f1508add42a790be5572a","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Makino","given":"M."},{"family":"Matsuda","given":"H."},{"family":"Sakurai","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":9748,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F54S432A"],"itemData":{"id":9748,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2009.03.017","issue":"9","page":"1937-1942","title":"Development of an adaptive marine ecosystem management and co-management plan at the Shiretoko World Natural Heritage Site","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-67949092783&doi=10.1016%2fj.biocon.2009.03.017&partnerID=40&md5=723facbd422503863d6c4eebab018bed","volume":"142","author":[{"family":"Matsuda","given":"H."},{"family":"Makino","given":"M."},{"family":"Sakurai","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":9726,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2N43L9EL"],"itemData":{"id":9726,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2020.104274","title":"Small-scale fisheries in developed countries: Looking beyond developing country narratives through Japan's perspective","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85094885737&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2020.104274&partnerID=40&md5=cb8865657a212f7767c9e58343d9e93f","volume":"122","author":[{"family":"Teh","given":"L.C.L."},{"family":"Teh","given":"L.S.L."},{"family":"Abe","given":"K."},{"family":"Ishimura","given":"G."},{"family":"Roman","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This co-management system can contribute to the sustainability of small-scale fisheries and to marine conservation in Japan, to the extent that local communities can implement fishery regulations to cope with declining fishing resources. This may include protected areas, gear modifications and restrictions on fishing effort (number of boats), as observed in the Shiretoko World Natural Heritage Site where the management plan considers fishers as part of the ecosystem ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Z78758Q6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Makino, 2009; Matsuda, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Makino, 2009; Matsuda, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9752,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RHPHBQN9"],"itemData":{"id":9752,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2008.05.013","issue":"2","page":"207-214","title":"Expanding fisheries co-management to ecosystem-based management: A case in the Shiretoko World Natural Heritage area, Japan","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-56949097451&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2008.05.013&partnerID=40&md5=90777bfc914f1508add42a790be5572a","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Makino","given":"M."},{"family":"Matsuda","given":"H."},{"family":"Sakurai","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":9748,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F54S432A"],"itemData":{"id":9748,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2009.03.017","issue":"9","page":"1937-1942","title":"Development of an adaptive marine ecosystem management and co-management plan at the Shiretoko World Natural Heritage Site","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-67949092783&doi=10.1016%2fj.biocon.2009.03.017&partnerID=40&md5=723facbd422503863d6c4eebab018bed","volume":"142","author":[{"family":"Matsuda","given":"H."},{"family":"Makino","given":"M."},{"family":"Sakurai","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, the social and economic sustainability of the Japanese small-scale fisheries face some challenges, such as limited workforce due to an ageing population and lower incomes from fishing compared to other activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RLrvx58I","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Teh, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Teh, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9726,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2N43L9EL"],"itemData":{"id":9726,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2020.104274","title":"Small-scale fisheries in developed countries: Looking beyond developing country narratives through Japan's perspective","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85094885737&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2020.104274&partnerID=40&md5=cb8865657a212f7767c9e58343d9e93f","volume":"122","author":[{"family":"Teh","given":"L.C.L."},{"family":"Teh","given":"L.S.L."},{"family":"Abe","given":"K."},{"family":"Ishimura","given":"G."},{"family":"Roman","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, besides institutional changes that may reduce participation of local fishing association in fisheries management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qDCUMsZH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ganseforth, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Ganseforth, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9766,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TXBBSGQY"],"itemData":{"id":9766,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Geographical Journal","DOI":"10.1111/geoj.12414","title":"Blue revitalization or dispossession? Reform of common resource management in Japanese small-scale fisheries","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85117865491&doi=10.1111%2fgeoj.12414&partnerID=40&md5=9916aece19ef374ec3b1ef94eda36667","author":[{"family":"Ganseforth","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
3.3.1.5.2. Medicine and hygiene

Aquatic organisms provide diverse sources of bioactive compounds of interest for nutraceutical, pharmaceutical, and cosmeceutical industries (Table 3.4). Fish, crustaceans and molluscs produce a variety of biologically active compounds that have been characterized by their antimicrobial, antiviral, anti-inflammatory, antioxidant, anti-cancer/antitumor, antihypertensive, anti-atherosclerotic, anticoagulant, and immunomodulatory properties and other medicinal functions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1qm0lbecjn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chbel, Asmaa, Delgado, Aurelio Serrano, Soukri, Abdelaziz, & El Khalfi, Bouchra, 2021; Nistic\\\\uc0\\\\u242{}, 2017; \\\\uc0\\\\u352{}imat, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Chbel, Asmaa, Delgado, Aurelio Serrano, Soukri, Abdelaziz, & El Khalfi, Bouchra, 2021; Nisticò, 2017; Šimat, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31884,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GWFV24W6"],"itemData":{"id":31884,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The marine environment is characterized by a wide diversity of microorganisms among which marine bacteria. To insure their survival in hostile conditions where they face high competition with pathogenic microorganisms, they produce various kinds of bioactive molecules within biofilms with unique structural and functional features. As example: marine peptides which provide a broad spectrum of antimicrobial, antitumoral, antiviral and anti-inflammatory activities, in addition to marine exopolysaccharides showing antifouling and antifungal activities, immunomodulatory properties, emulsion stabilization capacity with other various potentials. Some biofilms have shown a beneficial role for aquaculture, among which enhancement of growth performance and improvement of water quality, while others are threatening not only aquaculture and maritime fields, but also medicine and food industry. Thus, marine bioactive compounds are promising preventing agents for the establishment and growth of fouling microorganisms, which may be useful in different fields in order to decrease economic losses and avoid foodborne illnesses.","DOI":"10.5281/ZENODO.4384158","ISSN":"2449-8955","license":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International, Open Access","note":"publisher: Zenodo","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"Marine biomolecules: a promising approach in therapy and biotechnology","title-short":"Marine biomolecules","URL":"https://zenodo.org/record/4384158","author":[{"family":"Chbel, Asmaa","given":""},{"family":"Delgado, Aurelio Serrano","given":""},{"family":"Soukri, Abdelaziz","given":""},{"family":"El Khalfi, Bouchra","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,3]]}}},{"id":31883,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AI3ZAG9M"],"itemData":{"id":31883,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Resources","DOI":"10.3390/resources6040065","ISSN":"2079-9276","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Resources","language":"en","page":"65","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Aquatic-Derived Biomaterials for a Sustainable Future: A European Opportunity","title-short":"Aquatic-Derived Biomaterials for a Sustainable Future","URL":"http://www.mdpi.com/2079-9276/6/4/65","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Nisticò","given":"Roberto"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",11,12]]}}},{"id":31885,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U394T99R"],"itemData":{"id":31885,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The oceans have been the Earth’s most valuable source of food. They have now also become a valuable and versatile source of bioactive compounds. The significance of marine organisms as a natural source of new substances that may contribute to the food sector and the overall health of humans are expanding. This review is an update on the recent studies of functional seafood compounds (chitin and chitosan, pigments from algae, fish lipids and omega-3 fatty acids, essential amino acids and bioactive proteins/peptides, polysaccharides, phenolic compounds, and minerals) focusing on their potential use as nutraceuticals and health benefits.","container-title":"Marine Drugs","DOI":"10.3390/md18120627","ISSN":"1660-3397","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Drugs","language":"en","page":"627","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recent Advances in Marine-Based Nutraceuticals and Their Health Benefits","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/1660-3397/18/12/627","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Šimat","given":"Vida"},{"family":"Elabed","given":"Nariman"},{"family":"Kulawik","given":"Piotr"},{"family":"Ceylan","given":"Zafer"},{"family":"Jamroz","given":"Ewelina"},{"family":"Yazgan","given":"Hatice"},{"family":"?agalj","given":"Martina"},{"family":"Regenstein","given":"Joe M."},{"family":"Özogul","given":"Fatih"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fish oil, chitin, peptides, polysaccharides, gelatin, pigments, polyphenols, vitamins and minerals are examples of the compounds that have been used as functional food ingredients ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1glc70cetr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Venugopal, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Venugopal, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31882,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GRGGBH8M"],"itemData":{"id":31882,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Sweeteners","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-319-26478-3","language":"en","note":"collection-title: Reference Series in Phytochemistry\\nDOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-54528-8_36-2","page":"1-45","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Nutrients and Nutraceuticals from Seafood","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-319-54528-8_36-2","editor":[{"family":"Merillon","given":"Jean-Michel"},{"family":"Ramawat","given":"Kishan Gopal"}],"author":[{"family":"Venugopal","given":"V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} with health benefits. For a number of countries, especially in the tropics, nutrients such as zinc, calcium and iron available from marine fish are essential to the health of local populations, especially for children under five years old ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"afnlu3q8ag","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hicks, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Hicks, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33783,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E3GIZU2D"],"itemData":{"id":33783,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-019-1592-6","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7776","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"95-98","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Harnessing global fisheries to tackle micronutrient deficiencies","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-019-1592-6","volume":"574","author":[{"family":"Hicks","given":"Christina C."},{"family":"Cohen","given":"Philippa J."},{"family":"Graham","given":"Nicholas A. J."},{"family":"Nash","given":"Kirsty L."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"D’Lima","given":"Coralie"},{"family":"Mills","given":"David J."},{"family":"Roscher","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Thilsted","given":"Shakuntala H."},{"family":"Thorne-Lyman","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"MacNeil","given":"M. Aaron"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Biological properties of fish have also been used to treat or prevent different kinds of health disorders. 
	The food industry introduced several components to improve the properties of foods (i.e., emulsifier, stabilizer, texture modifier, coating or thickening agent) or to enrich foods with functional components and allow their application in health-promoting foods for direct consumption ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a61f7qbuqi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u352{}imat, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Šimat, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31885,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U394T99R"],"itemData":{"id":31885,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The oceans have been the Earth’s most valuable source of food. They have now also become a valuable and versatile source of bioactive compounds. The significance of marine organisms as a natural source of new substances that may contribute to the food sector and the overall health of humans are expanding. This review is an update on the recent studies of functional seafood compounds (chitin and chitosan, pigments from algae, fish lipids and omega-3 fatty acids, essential amino acids and bioactive proteins/peptides, polysaccharides, phenolic compounds, and minerals) focusing on their potential use as nutraceuticals and health benefits.","container-title":"Marine Drugs","DOI":"10.3390/md18120627","ISSN":"1660-3397","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Drugs","language":"en","page":"627","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recent Advances in Marine-Based Nutraceuticals and Their Health Benefits","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/1660-3397/18/12/627","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Šimat","given":"Vida"},{"family":"Elabed","given":"Nariman"},{"family":"Kulawik","given":"Piotr"},{"family":"Ceylan","given":"Zafer"},{"family":"Jamroz","given":"Ewelina"},{"family":"Yazgan","given":"Hatice"},{"family":"?agalj","given":"Martina"},{"family":"Regenstein","given":"Joe M."},{"family":"Özogul","given":"Fatih"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are many papers promoting the benefits of biologically active components from wild caught animals, but little data were found on the number of wild animals caught and used in pharmaceuticals, nutraceuticals and hygiene products. Thus, the below review focuses on selected uses for which there is enough information to provide an overall assessment.

