* Fish oil as a source of omega-3 long chain polyunsaturated fatty acids
Fish oils contain high levels of omega-3 long chain polyunsaturated fatty acids (n-3 LC-PUFA), including those known as EPA and DHA (eicosapentaenoic acid [20:5n-3] and docosahexaenoic acid [22:6n-3]). Those components are well accepted as being essential for a healthy and balanced diet, and a large number of studies demonstrate the positive effects of food supplementation with fish oil on human health and the prevention of certain diseases (see ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1rsla7ce8a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ghasemi Fard, Wang, Sinclair, Elliott, & Turchini, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Ghasemi Fard, Wang, Sinclair, Elliott, & Turchini, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31893,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RXQSRA7E"],"itemData":{"id":31893,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Critical Reviews in Food Science and Nutrition","DOI":"10.1080/10408398.2018.1425978","ISSN":"1040-8398, 1549-7852","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Critical Reviews in Food Science and Nutrition","language":"en","page":"1684-1727","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"How does high DHA fish oil affect health? A systematic review of evidence","title-short":"How does high DHA fish oil affect health?","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10408398.2018.1425978","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Ghasemi Fard","given":"Samaneh"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Fenglei"},{"family":"Sinclair","given":"Andrew J."},{"family":"Elliott","given":"Glenn"},{"family":"Turchini","given":"Giovanni M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} for a review). 
      The vast majority of n-3 LC-PUFA is produced by marine micro-organisms, predominantly microalgae ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7atoV36D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harwood & Guschina, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Harwood & Guschina, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8470,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LD5UW3SL"],"itemData":{"id":8470,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biochimie","DOI":"10.1016/j.biochi.2008.11.004","ISSN":"03009084","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Biochimie","language":"en","page":"679-684","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The versatility of algae and their lipid metabolism","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0300908408003131","volume":"91","author":[{"family":"Harwood","given":"John L."},{"family":"Guschina","given":"Irina A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, whereas terrestrial wild plants do not produce EPA or DHA (eicosapentaenoic acid [20:5n-3] and docosahexaenoic acid [22:6n-3]). ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2n5uri3pj7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harwood, 1996)","plainCitation":"(Harwood, 1996)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31892,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8ZBUFG2H"],"itemData":{"id":31892,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biochimica et Biophysica Acta (BBA) - Lipids and Lipid Metabolism","DOI":"10.1016/0005-2760(95)00242-1","ISSN":"00052760","issue":"1-2","journalAbbreviation":"Biochimica et Biophysica Acta (BBA) - Lipids and Lipid Metabolism","language":"en","page":"7-56","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recent advances in the biosynthesis of plant fatty acids","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/0005276095002421","volume":"1301","author":[{"family":"Harwood","given":"John L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Therefore, the supply of these components for humans come from the ocean, and predominantly from capture fisheries (almost 90%), whether as food fish or via fish oil and fishmeal, with relatively small additional amounts estimated from seafood by-products and recycling, unfed aquaculture and traditional macroalgal sources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2jjha34q68","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tocher, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Tocher, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31891,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BFPBGAL6"],"itemData":{"id":31891,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Lipids","DOI":"10.1007/s11745-006-5051-4","ISSN":"0024-4201, 1558-9307","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Lipids","language":"en","page":"1003-1016","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Highly unsaturated fatty acid synthesis in marine fish: Cloning, functional characterization, and nutritional regulation of fatty acyl ?6 desaturase of Atlantic cod (Gadus morhua L.)","title-short":"Highly unsaturated fatty acid synthesis in marine fish","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1007/s11745-006-5051-4","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Tocher","given":"Douglas R."},{"family":"Zheng","given":"Xiaozhong"},{"family":"Schlechtriem","given":"Christian"},{"family":"Hastings","given":"Nicola"},{"family":"Dick","given":"James R."},{"family":"Teale","given":"Alan J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The global supply of fish oil remains relatively stable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ahuj26s712","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020d; J. Shepherd & Bachis, 2014)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020d; J. Shepherd & Bachis, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":31872,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5CU7JWYD"],"itemData":{"id":31872,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquaculture Economics & Management","DOI":"10.1080/13657305.2014.959212","ISSN":"1365-7305, 1551-8663","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Aquaculture Economics & Management","language":"en","page":"395-416","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"CHANGING SUPPLY AND DEMAND FOR FISH OIL","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/13657305.2014.959212","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Shepherd","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Bachis","given":"Enrico"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",10,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, constrained largely by natural supply constraints in the fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a16n2djdp6s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Misund, Oglend, & Pincinato, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Misund, Oglend, & Pincinato, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31873,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KJFI78R3"],"itemData":{"id":31873,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquaculture Economics & Management","DOI":"10.1080/13657305.2017.1284942","ISSN":"1365-7305, 1551-8663","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Aquaculture Economics & Management","language":"en","page":"185-210","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The rise of fish oil: From feed to human nutritional supplement","title-short":"The rise of fish oil","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13657305.2017.1284942","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Misund","given":"Bård"},{"family":"Oglend","given":"Atle"},{"family":"Pincinato","given":"Ruth Beatriz Mezzalira"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",4,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Figure 3.34). Supplements in the food industry use 20 to 25 percent of globally available fish oil (2017), up from only 5% in 1990 (Figure 3.35). While Fish oil is currently the only economically viable source of n-3 LC-PUFA for feed purposes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xkfnewny","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Misund, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Misund, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31873,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KJFI78R3"],"itemData":{"id":31873,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquaculture Economics & Management","DOI":"10.1080/13657305.2017.1284942","ISSN":"1365-7305, 1551-8663","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Aquaculture Economics & Management","language":"en","page":"185-210","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The rise of fish oil: From feed to human nutritional supplement","title-short":"The rise of fish oil","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13657305.2017.1284942","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Misund","given":"Bård"},{"family":"Oglend","given":"Atle"},{"family":"Pincinato","given":"Ruth Beatriz Mezzalira"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",4,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the growing demand from the human nutritional supplement industry has tightened the competition noticeably ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aih2sjfbj7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Shepherd & Bachis, 2014)","plainCitation":"(J. Shepherd & Bachis, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31872,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5CU7JWYD"],"itemData":{"id":31872,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquaculture Economics & Management","DOI":"10.1080/13657305.2014.959212","ISSN":"1365-7305, 1551-8663","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Aquaculture Economics & Management","language":"en","page":"395-416","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"CHANGING SUPPLY AND DEMAND FOR FISH OIL","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/13657305.2014.959212","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Shepherd","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Bachis","given":"Enrico"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",10,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Based on the recommended dose for cardiac health, the total demand for n?3 LC-PUFA is over 1.25 million metric tonnes (mt) whereas total supply is optimistically estimated at just over 0.8 million mt indicating a shortfall of over 0.4 million mt ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"agd37k5gud","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tocher, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Tocher, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31863,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YKXBTNZE"],"itemData":{"id":31863,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1016/j.aquaculture.2015.01.010","ISSN":"00448486","journalAbbreviation":"Aquaculture","language":"en","page":"94-107","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Omega-3 long-chain polyunsaturated fatty acids and aquaculture in perspective","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0044848615000137","volume":"449","author":[{"family":"Tocher","given":"Douglas R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      


