Figure 3.37 The International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List population trends of all elasmobranch species reported in the liver oil trade. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FP8DfHLY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Macdonald & Soll, 2020)","plainCitation":"(C. Macdonald & Soll, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31874,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F5MB995Y"],"itemData":{"id":31874,"type":"report","abstract":"Abstract\\n          The COVID-19 pandemic may create new demand for wildlife-generated products for human health, including a shark-derived ingredient used in some vaccines. Adjuvants are a vaccine component that increases efficacy, and some adjuvants contain squalene, a natural compound derived from shark liver oil which is found most abundantly in deep-sea sharks. In recent decades, there has been growing conservation concern associated with the sustainability of many shark fisheries. The need for a potentially massive number of adjuvant-containing SARS-CoV-2 vaccines may increase global demand for shark-derived squalene, with possible consequences for shark conservation, especially of vulnerable and understudied deep-sea species. A shift to non-animal-derived sources of squalene, which are similar in cost and identical in effectiveness, or an emphasis on increasing traceability and sustainability of shark-derived squalene from existing well-managed fisheries, could better support conservation and public health goals.","genre":"preprint","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1101/2020.10.14.338053","publisher":"Ecology","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Shark conservation risks associated with the use of shark liver oil in SARS-CoV-2 vaccine development","URL":"http://biorxiv.org/lookup/doi/10.1101/2020.10.14.338053","author":[{"family":"Macdonald","given":"Catherine"},{"family":"Soll","given":"Joshua"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY-NC-ND 4.0. 

	Deep-sea sharks offer larger volumes of liver oil compared to other shark species and are therefore of greater interest to the shark liver oil trade (Figure 3.38). The knowledge on these species remains relatively poor due to low research priority added to the difficulties to conduct research in the deep sea ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"anod7pnd3p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kyne & Simpfendorfer, 2007; Neiva, Coelho, & Erzini, 2006; Verissimo, MacMillan, & Smith, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Kyne & Simpfendorfer, 2007; Neiva, Coelho, & Erzini, 2006; Verissimo, MacMillan, & Smith, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31880,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NKUPXSJL"],"itemData":{"id":31880,"type":"book","abstract":"Kyne P.M., Simpfendorfer, C.A., 2007. A collation and summarization of available data on\\ndeepwater Chondrichthyans: biodiversity, life history and fisheries. A report prepared by the\\nIUCN SSC Shark Specialist Group for the Marine Conservation Biology Institute.\\nhttps://www.iucnssg.org/uploads/5/4/1/2/54120303/deepwater_chondrichthyans.pdf (accessed 23\\nFeb 2021).","publisher":"IUCN SSC Shark Specialist Group for the Marine Conservation Biology Institute","title":"A collation and summarization of available data on deepwater Chondrichthyans: biodiversity, life history and fisheries.","author":[{"family":"Kyne","given":"Peter M"},{"family":"Simpfendorfer","given":"C. A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":31879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9E2DGLDP"],"itemData":{"id":31879,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Etmopterus spinax\\n              is one of the most abundant predators of the upper continental slope off the Algarve (southern Portugal), where it is captured in large quantities in deep-water fisheries. The feeding habits of\\n              E. spinax\\n              off the Algarve were investigated through the analysis of stomach contents of 376 individuals. Prey composition was described and maturity, sex and size related variations in the diet analysed. The overall diet of\\n              E. spinax\\n              suggested a fairly generalized benthopelagic foraging behaviour primarily tuned to pelagic macroplankton/microneckton, teleost fish and cephalopods. Sex and maturity related differences in the diet were not significant. Two main ontogenic diet shifts were observed at about 17 and 28?cm total length. Small and medium sized immature sharks had a diet dominated by eurybathic crustaceans, chiefly\\n              Meganyctiphanes norvegica\\n              and\\n              Pasiphaea sivado\\n              . Larger individuals consumed more teleosts and cephalopods, in part associated with scavenging as a new feeding strategy. With increasing shark size the diet diversified both in terms of resources exploited and prey size.","container-title":"Journal of the Marine Biological Association of the United Kingdom","DOI":"10.1017/S0025315406013762","ISSN":"0025-3154, 1469-7769","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"J. Mar. Biol. Ass.","language":"en","page":"835-841","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Feeding habits of the velvet belly lanternshark <i>Etmopterus spinax</i> (Chondrichthyes: Etmopteridae) off the Algarve, southern Portugal","title-short":"Feeding habits of the velvet belly lanternshark <i>Etmopterus spinax</i> (Chondrichthyes","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0025315406013762/type/journal_article","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Neiva","given":"João"},{"family":"Coelho","given":"Rui"},{"family":"Erzini","given":"Karim"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",8]]}}},{"id":31877,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FBVLAHDJ"],"itemData":{"id":31877,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/j.1755-263X.2010.00151.x","ISSN":"1755263X","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1-8","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Toward a systematic approach for identifying conservation flagships: Identifying conservation flagships","title-short":"Toward a systematic approach for identifying conservation flagships","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1755-263X.2010.00151.x","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Verissimo","given":"Diogo"},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"Douglas C."},{"family":"Smith","given":"Robert J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Therefore, little is known about population structure, habitat use and reproduction of many of these species. Nevertheless, shark reproductive rates and recovery potential are known to decline when depth increases, and population depletion risks exist even when exploitation (targeted or incidental) rates are low ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1uhovm2edr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Simpfendorfer & Kyne, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Simpfendorfer & Kyne, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31878,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D8AYYB9H"],"itemData":{"id":31878,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"SUMMARY\\n            As global fishing effort increasingly expands into deeper water, concerns exist over the ability of deep-sea fishes to sustain fisheries. There is however little quantitative evidence to support these concerns for the deep-sea cartilaginous fishes (Chondrichthyes: sharks, rays and chimaeras). This paper compiled available life history data for this group to analyse their ability to rebound from population declines relative to continental shelf and pelagic species. Deep-sea cartilaginous fishes have rates of population increase that are on average less than half those of shelf and pelagic species, and include the lowest levels observed to date. Population doubling times indicate that once a stock has been depleted, it will take decades, and potentially centuries, before it will recover. Furthermore, population recovery rates decrease with increasing depth, suggesting species that occur deepest are those most vulnerable to fishing. These results provide the first assessment of the productivity of deep-sea chondrichthyans, highlighting that precautionary management of developing deep-sea fisheries is essential if stocks and biodiversity are to be maintained.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892909990191","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Conserv.","language":"en","page":"97-103","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Limited potential to recover from overfishing raises concerns for deep-sea sharks, rays and chimaeras","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0376892909990191/type/journal_article","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Simpfendorfer","given":"Colin A."},{"family":"Kyne","given":"Peter M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For these reasons, deep-sea sharks have been identified as a conservation priority ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1t2nfl12j2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dulvy, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Dulvy, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31574,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E8X5V6CI"],"itemData":{"id":31574,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The rapid expansion of human activities threatens ocean-wide biodiversity. Numerous marine animal populations have declined, yet it remains unclear whether these trends are symptomatic of a chronic accumulation of global marine extinction risk. We present the first systematic analysis of threat for a globally distributed lineage of 1,041 chondrichthyan fishes—sharks, rays, and chimaeras. We estimate that one-quarter are threatened according to IUCN Red List criteria due to overfishing (targeted and incidental). Large-bodied, shallow-water species are at greatest risk and five out of the seven most threatened families are rays. Overall chondrichthyan extinction risk is substantially higher than for most other vertebrates, and only one-third of species are considered safe. Population depletion has occurred throughout the world’s ice-free waters, but is particularly prevalent in the Indo-Pacific Biodiversity Triangle and Mediterranean Sea. Improved management of fisheries and trade is urgently needed to avoid extinctions and promote population recovery.\\n          , \\n            Ocean ecosystems are under pressure from overfishing, climate change, habitat destruction and pollution. These pressures have led to documented declines of some fishes in some places, such as those living in coral reefs and on the high seas. However, it is not clear whether these population declines are isolated one-off examples or, instead, if they are sufficiently widespread to risk the extinction of large numbers of species.\\n            Most fishes have a skeleton that is made of bone, but sharks and rays have a skeleton that is made of cartilage. A total of 1,041 species has such a skeleton and they are collectively known as the Chondrichthyes. To find out how well these fish are faring, Dulvy worked with more than 300 scientists around the world to assess the conservation status of all 1,041 species.\\n            Based on this, Dulvy estimate that one in four of these species are threatened with extinction, mainly as a result of overfishing. Moreover, just 389 species (37.4% of the total) are considered to be safe, which is the lowest fraction of safe species among all vertebrate groups studied to date.\\n            The largest sharks and rays are in the most peril, especially those living in shallow waters that are accessible to fisheries. A particular problem is the ‘fin trade’: the fins of sharks and shark-like rays are a delicacy in some Asian countries, and more than half of the chondrichthyans that enter the fin trade are under threat. Whether targeted or caught by boats fishing for other species, sharks and rays are used to supply a market that is largely unmonitored and unregulated. Habitat degradation and loss also pose considerable threats, particularly for freshwater sharks and rays.\\n            Dulvy identified three main hotspots where the biodiversity of sharks and rays was particularly seriously threatened—the Indo-Pacific Biodiversity Triangle, Red Sea, and the Mediterranean Sea—and argue that national and international action is needed to protect them from overfishing.","container-title":"eLife","DOI":"10.7554/eLife.00590","ISSN":"2050-084X","language":"en","page":"e00590","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Extinction risk and conservation of the world’s sharks and rays","URL":"https://elifesciences.org/articles/00590","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K"},{"family":"Fowler","given":"Sarah L"},{"family":"Musick","given":"John A"},{"family":"Cavanagh","given":"Rachel D"},{"family":"Kyne","given":"Peter M"},{"family":"Harrison","given":"Lucy R"},{"family":"Carlson","given":"John K"},{"family":"Davidson","given":"Lindsay NK"},{"family":"Fordham","given":"Sonja V"},{"family":"Francis","given":"Malcolm P"},{"family":"Pollock","given":"Caroline M"},{"family":"Simpfendorfer","given":"Colin A"},{"family":"Burgess","given":"George H"},{"family":"Carpenter","given":"Kent E"},{"family":"Compagno","given":"Leonard JV"},{"family":"Ebert","given":"David A"},{"family":"Gibson","given":"Claudine"},{"family":"Heupel","given":"Michelle R"},{"family":"Livingstone","given":"Suzanne R"},{"family":"Sanciangco","given":"Jonnell C"},{"family":"Stevens","given":"John D"},{"family":"Valenti","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"White","given":"William T"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",1,21]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 


Figure 3.38 The total reported net weight (tons) of annual trade in shark liver oil reported to the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2kt7sm0u3s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Macdonald & Soll, 2020)","plainCitation":"(C. Macdonald & Soll, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31874,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F5MB995Y"],"itemData":{"id":31874,"type":"report","abstract":"Abstract\\n          The COVID-19 pandemic may create new demand for wildlife-generated products for human health, including a shark-derived ingredient used in some vaccines. Adjuvants are a vaccine component that increases efficacy, and some adjuvants contain squalene, a natural compound derived from shark liver oil which is found most abundantly in deep-sea sharks. In recent decades, there has been growing conservation concern associated with the sustainability of many shark fisheries. The need for a potentially massive number of adjuvant-containing SARS-CoV-2 vaccines may increase global demand for shark-derived squalene, with possible consequences for shark conservation, especially of vulnerable and understudied deep-sea species. A shift to non-animal-derived sources of squalene, which are similar in cost and identical in effectiveness, or an emphasis on increasing traceability and sustainability of shark-derived squalene from existing well-managed fisheries, could better support conservation and public health goals.","genre":"preprint","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1101/2020.10.14.338053","publisher":"Ecology","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Shark conservation risks associated with the use of shark liver oil in SARS-CoV-2 vaccine development","URL":"http://biorxiv.org/lookup/doi/10.1101/2020.10.14.338053","author":[{"family":"Macdonald","given":"Catherine"},{"family":"Soll","given":"Joshua"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY-NC-ND 4.0.

	The cosmetics industry in Europe and the United States of America has decreased its use of shark-based squalene in recent years, under pressure from non-profit organizations and consumers. Independent tests conducted by the French organization “Bloom” determined that most cosmetics (>90% of products tested) sold in Europe or the United States of America no longer contain shark-derived ingredients, although shark-derived squalene is still commonly used in cosmetics elsewhere ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1gg3kpldf2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ducos, Guillonneau, Le Manach, & Nouvian, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Ducos, Guillonneau, Le Manach, & Nouvian, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31881,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G48RT3B2"],"itemData":{"id":31881,"type":"book","publisher":"BLOOM NGO","title":"La Belle et la Bête, du requin dans nos crèmes de beauté !","URL":"http://www.bloomassociation.org/la-belle-et-la-bete-etude-exclusive-du-requin-dans-nos-cremes-de-beaute/ (accessed 23 feb 2021)","author":[{"family":"Ducos","given":"Laure"},{"family":"Guillonneau","given":"Victoire"},{"family":"Le Manach","given":"Frédéric"},{"family":"Nouvian","given":"Claire"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Covid-19 pandemic has reinvigorated the debate on using shark squalene-derived products in the production of potential SARS-CoV-2 vaccines ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1pon0evb5f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Macdonald & Soll, 2020)","plainCitation":"(C. Macdonald & Soll, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31874,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F5MB995Y"],"itemData":{"id":31874,"type":"report","abstract":"Abstract\\n          The COVID-19 pandemic may create new demand for wildlife-generated products for human health, including a shark-derived ingredient used in some vaccines. Adjuvants are a vaccine component that increases efficacy, and some adjuvants contain squalene, a natural compound derived from shark liver oil which is found most abundantly in deep-sea sharks. In recent decades, there has been growing conservation concern associated with the sustainability of many shark fisheries. The need for a potentially massive number of adjuvant-containing SARS-CoV-2 vaccines may increase global demand for shark-derived squalene, with possible consequences for shark conservation, especially of vulnerable and understudied deep-sea species. A shift to non-animal-derived sources of squalene, which are similar in cost and identical in effectiveness, or an emphasis on increasing traceability and sustainability of shark-derived squalene from existing well-managed fisheries, could better support conservation and public health goals.","genre":"preprint","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1101/2020.10.14.338053","publisher":"Ecology","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Shark conservation risks associated with the use of shark liver oil in SARS-CoV-2 vaccine development","URL":"http://biorxiv.org/lookup/doi/10.1101/2020.10.14.338053","author":[{"family":"Macdonald","given":"Catherine"},{"family":"Soll","given":"Joshua"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

* Bioactive compounds from wild caught species and seafood processing by-products
Fish and shellfish, including crustaceans, are sources of a wide range of bioactive compounds (Box 3.7) that can be recovered from commercial fish processing waste (scales, shells, frames, backbones, viscera, head, liver, skin, belly flaps, dark muscle, roe, and others) and bycatch (unwanted fish and fish of poor economic value). A large corpus of grey literature promotes the use of such material for the production of nutrients, nutraceuticals and pharmaceuticals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a3evscbntu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Venugopal, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Venugopal, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31882,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GRGGBH8M"],"itemData":{"id":31882,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Sweeteners","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-319-26478-3","language":"en","note":"collection-title: Reference Series in Phytochemistry\\nDOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-54528-8_36-2","page":"1-45","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Nutrients and Nutraceuticals from Seafood","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-319-54528-8_36-2","editor":[{"family":"Merillon","given":"Jean-Michel"},{"family":"Ramawat","given":"Kishan Gopal"}],"author":[{"family":"Venugopal","given":"V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, however most processed fish by-products are reduced to fish meal, fish oil and fish silage ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a19ivciqhcm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Jackson & Newton, 2016; Venugopal, 2018)","plainCitation":"(A. Jackson & Newton, 2016; Venugopal, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31886,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5RWZSSQ2"],"itemData":{"id":31886,"type":"report","abstract":"Jackson, A., & Newton, R. W. (2016). Project to Model the Use of Fisheries by-products in the Production of Marine Ingredients with Special Reference to omega-3 Fatty Acids EPA and DHA. Institute of Aquaculture, University of Stirling & IFFO, the Marine Ingredients Organisation.","publisher":"Institute of Aquaculture, University of Stirling & IFFO, the Marine Ingredients Organisation.","title":"Project to Model the Use of Fisheries by-products in the Production of Marine Ingredients with Special Reference to omega-3 Fatty Acids EPA and DHA","author":[{"family":"Jackson","given":"A."},{"family":"Newton","given":"R. W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":31882,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GRGGBH8M"],"itemData":{"id":31882,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Sweeteners","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-319-26478-3","language":"en","note":"collection-title: Reference Series in Phytochemistry\\nDOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-54528-8_36-2","page":"1-45","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Nutrients and Nutraceuticals from Seafood","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-319-54528-8_36-2","editor":[{"family":"Merillon","given":"Jean-Michel"},{"family":"Ramawat","given":"Kishan Gopal"}],"author":[{"family":"Venugopal","given":"V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	The potential of using these byproducts is important. Jackson and Newton ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a34hpoje7l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Jackson & Newton, 2016)","plainCitation":"(A. Jackson & Newton, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31886,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5RWZSSQ2"],"itemData":{"id":31886,"type":"report","abstract":"Jackson, A., & Newton, R. W. (2016). Project to Model the Use of Fisheries by-products in the Production of Marine Ingredients with Special Reference to omega-3 Fatty Acids EPA and DHA. Institute of Aquaculture, University of Stirling & IFFO, the Marine Ingredients Organisation.","publisher":"Institute of Aquaculture, University of Stirling & IFFO, the Marine Ingredients Organisation.","title":"Project to Model the Use of Fisheries by-products in the Production of Marine Ingredients with Special Reference to omega-3 Fatty Acids EPA and DHA","author":[{"family":"Jackson","given":"A."},{"family":"Newton","given":"R. W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} estimate that the collection and processing of all byproducts not currently used for fish oil extraction would yield around 50,000 tons of EPA and DHA (eicosapentaenoic acid [20:5n-3] and docosahexaenoic acid [22:6n-3]) with around 80% coming from wild capture fisheries. This additional tonnage of EPA and DHA would increase the global supply by around 25%.

Box 3.7  The promising potential of cone snails

Molluscs have long been used in traditional medicine and scientists often rely on local knowledge to identify bioactive compounds with potential therapeutic applications ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ai72rectte","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Benkendorff, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Benkendorff, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31862,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MEWFJQLU"],"itemData":{"id":31862,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Drugs","DOI":"10.3390/md13085237","ISSN":"1660-3397","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Drugs","language":"en","page":"5237-5275","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Are the Traditional Medical Uses of Muricidae Molluscs Substantiated by Their Pharmacological Properties and Bioactive Compounds?","URL":"http://www.mdpi.com/1660-3397/13/8/5237","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Benkendorff","given":"Kirsten"},{"family":"Rudd","given":"David"},{"family":"Nongmaithem","given":"Bijayalakshmi"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Lei"},{"family":"Young","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Edwards","given":"Vicki"},{"family":"Avila","given":"Cathy"},{"family":"Abbott","given":"Catherine"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",8,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In this context, one of the most studied groups of organisms are the cone snails, renowned for their capacity to produce venoms used to capture their prey or deter predators ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a4atnbh0ga","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dutertre, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Dutertre, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J2I5B9NI"],"itemData":{"id":31833,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Venomous animals are thought to inject the same combination of toxins for both predation and defence, presumably exploiting conserved target pharmacology across prey and predators. Remarkably, cone snails can rapidly switch between distinct venoms in response to predatory or defensive stimuli. Here, we show that the defence-evoked venom of Conus geographus contains high levels of paralytic toxins that potently block neuromuscular receptors, consistent with its lethal effects on humans. In contrast, C. geographus predation-evoked venom contains prey-specific toxins mostly inactive at human targets. Predation- and defence-evoked venoms originate from the distal and proximal regions of the venom duct, respectively, explaining how different stimuli can generate two distinct venoms. A specialized defensive envenomation strategy is widely evolved across worm, mollusk and fish-hunting cone snails. We propose that defensive toxins, originally evolved in ancestral worm-hunting cone snails to protect against cephalopod and fish predation, have been repurposed in predatory venoms to facilitate diversification to fish and mollusk diets.","container-title":"Nature Communications","DOI":"10.1038/ncomms4521","ISSN":"2041-1723","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"2014 Nature Publishing Group, a division of Macmillan Publishers Limited. All Rights Reserved.","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"3521","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Evolution of separate predation- and defence-evoked venoms in carnivorous cone snails","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/ncomms4521","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Dutertre","given":"Sébastien"},{"family":"Jin","given":"Ai-Hua"},{"family":"Vetter","given":"Irina"},{"family":"Hamilton","given":"Brett"},{"family":"Sunagar","given":"Kartik"},{"family":"Lavergne","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Dutertre","given":"Valentin"},{"family":"Fry","given":"Bryan G."},{"family":"Antunes","given":"Agostinho"},{"family":"Venter","given":"Deon J."},{"family":"Alewood","given":"Paul F."},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Richard J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",3,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Cone snails are only the tip of the iceberg: order Neogastropoda, has at least 15,000 recorded species, most of which are suspected to be venomous ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1vtjosuo5h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. Puillandre, 2011)","plainCitation":"(N. Puillandre, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31861,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5SVTN9JG"],"itemData":{"id":31861,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Molluscan Studies","DOI":"10.1093/mollus/eyr015","ISSN":"0260-1230, 1464-3766","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Molluscan Studies","language":"en","page":"259-272","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The dragon tamed? A molecular phylogeny of the Conoidea (Gastropoda)","title-short":"The dragon tamed?","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/mollus/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/mollus/eyr015","volume":"77","author":[{"family":"Puillandre","given":"N."},{"family":"Kantor","given":"Y. I."},{"family":"Sysoev","given":"A."},{"family":"Couloux","given":"A."},{"family":"Meyer","given":"C."},{"family":"Rawlings","given":"T."},{"family":"Todd","given":"J. A."},{"family":"Bouchet","given":"P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Venoms produced by cone snails (termed “conotoxins”) have been studied since the end of the 1970s, and constitute an inexhaustible reservoir of toxins, with more than 1,000 species and up to 200 unique toxins produced by each of them ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ao9vvpvj7h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Olivera, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Olivera, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31888,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/89ZSL8MT"],"itemData":{"id":31888,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Biological Chemistry","DOI":"10.1074/jbc.R600020200","ISSN":"0021-9258","issue":"42","journalAbbreviation":"J Biol Chem","language":"eng","note":"PMID: 16905531","page":"31173-31177","source":"PubMed","title":"Conus peptides: biodiversity-based discovery and exogenomics","title-short":"Conus peptides","volume":"281","author":[{"family":"Olivera","given":"Baldomero M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",10,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. One toxin of cone snail has been approved to be used as an analgesic to treat chronic pain (PRIALT®). Several others are engaged at various steps of the process of drug approval, with applications such as epilepsy, cardioprotection and diabetes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a27ht2u4peq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bj\\\\uc0\\\\u248{}rn-Yoshimoto, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Bjørn-Yoshimoto, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31890,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QACBD5TK"],"itemData":{"id":31890,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Conotoxins form a diverse group of peptide toxins found in the venom of predatory marine cone snails. Decades of conotoxin research have provided numerous measurable scientific and societal benefits. These include their use as a drug, diagnostic agent, drug leads, and research tools in neuroscience, pharmacology, biochemistry, structural biology, and molecular evolution. Human envenomations by cone snails are rare but can be fatal. Death by envenomation is likely caused by a small set of toxins that induce muscle paralysis of the diaphragm, resulting in respiratory arrest. The potency of these toxins led to concerns regarding the potential development and use of conotoxins as biological weapons. To address this, various regulatory measures have been introduced that limit the use and access of conotoxins within the research community. Some of these regulations apply to all of the &asymp;200,000 conotoxins predicted to exist in nature of which less than 0.05% are estimated to have any significant toxicity in humans. In this review we provide an overview of the many benefits of conotoxin research, and contrast these to the perceived biosecurity concerns of conotoxins and research thereof.","container-title":"Biomedicines","DOI":"10.3390/biomedicines8080235","issue":"8","language":"en","license":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","note":"number: 8\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"235","source":"www.mdpi.com","title":"Curses or Cures: A Review of the Numerous Benefits Versus the Biosecurity Concerns of Conotoxin Research","title-short":"Curses or Cures","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2227-9059/8/8/235","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Bjørn-Yoshimoto","given":"Walden E."},{"family":"Ramiro","given":"Iris Bea L."},{"family":"Yandell","given":"Mark"},{"family":"McIntosh","given":"J. Michael"},{"family":"Olivera","given":"Baldomero M."},{"family":"Ellgaard","given":"Lars"},{"family":"Safavi-Hemami","given":"Helena"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Such promising applications make the cone snails (and relatives) an attractive group of organisms for pharmacological companies. However, the only source of toxins is natural populations (cone snails are highly difficult to reproduce in captivity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1vjoncr9k0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Perron, 1981)","plainCitation":"(Perron, 1981)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31887,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N9LZE7BI"],"itemData":{"id":31887,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The planktotrophic larvae of Conus lividus, C. quercinus, C. \\nflavidus, C. striatus, and C. marmoreus were reared through metamorphosis in \\nthe laboratory and were described and figured . Minimum planktonic periods \\nof these species were found to be 50, 30, 23, 20, and 10 days, respectively. The \\nlecithotrophic larvae of C. pennaceus metamorphosed within 24 hours of \\nescape from their egg capsules. Early post-hatching growth rate s of planktotrophic \\nveligers were related to hatching size and the developmental state of \\nthe velum at hat ching. In C. pennaceus, metamorphosis was induced by the \\npresence of a biological film. Substratum texture had no effect on rates of \\nmetamorphosis. The lecithotrophic larvae of C. pennaceus were observed to \\ningest unicellular algae. Experiments showed that this facultative feeding may \\nimprove the survival of C. pennaceus when the larval period is artificially \\nprolonged.","ISSN":"0030-8870","language":"en-US","note":"Accepted: 2008-02-23T23:51:18Z\\npublisher: University of Hawai'i Press","source":"scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu","title":"Larval Growth and Metamorphosis of Conus (Castropoda: Toxoglossa) in Hawaii","title-short":"Larval Growth and Metamorphosis of Conus (Castropoda","URL":"http://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/handle/10125/536","author":[{"family":"Perron","given":"Frank E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1981",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Researchers are now looking for sustainable solutions to preserve the biodiversity. 
      The Nagoya protocol regulates access to genetic resources to guarantee fair benefit sharing with local populations. This is the case, for example, with cone snails that mostly live in tropical shallow waters of emerging countries. Indeed, the highest diversity of cone snails is encountered in the Indo-Pacific ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a75oqf949q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nicolas Puillandre, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Nicolas Puillandre, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":993,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E24EX75K"],"itemData":{"id":993,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract Cone snails have long been studied both by taxonomists for the diversity of their shells and by biochemists for the potential therapeutic applications of their toxins. Phylogenetic approaches have revealed that different lineages of Conus evolved divergent venoms, a property that is exploited to enhance the discovery of new conotoxins, but is rarely used in taxonomy. Specimens belonging to the Indo-West Pacific Conus lividus species complex were analyzed using phenetic and phylogenetic methods based on shell morphology, COI and 28S rRNA gene sequences and venom mRNA expression and protein composition. All methods converged to reveal a new species, C. conco n. sp. (described in Supplementary data), restricted to the Marquesas Islands, where it diverged recently (?3 mya) from C. lividus. The geographical distribution of C. conco and C. lividus and their phylogenetic relationships suggest that the two species diverged in allopatry. Furthermore, the diversity of the transcript sequences and toxin molecular masses suggest that C. conco evolved unique toxins, presumably in response to new selective pressure, such as the availability of new preys and ecological niches. Furthermore, this new species evolved new transcripts giving rise to original toxin structures, probably each carrying specific biological activity.","container-title":"Molecular Phylogenetics and Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.ympev.2014.06.024","ISSN":"1055-7903","issue":"0","license":"All rights reserved","page":"186–192","title":"When everything converges: Integrative taxonomy with shell, DNA and venomic data reveals Conus conco, a new species of cone snails (Gastropoda: Conoidea)","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1055790314002371","volume":"80","author":[{"family":"Puillandre","given":"Nicolas"},{"family":"Stöcklin","given":"Reto"},{"family":"Favreau","given":"Philippe"},{"family":"Bianchi","given":"Estelle"},{"family":"Perret","given":"Frédéric"},{"family":"Rivasseau","given":"Audrey"},{"family":"Limpalaër","given":"Loïc"},{"family":"Monnier","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Bouchet","given":"Philippe"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, specifically in the Southwest Pacific (e.g., Philippines, Indonesia, Papua New Guinea, New Caledonia), and the most studied species, such as Conus textile or Conus geographus, the latter being the only deadly species for humans, with a fatality rate of 50% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a23l9fe4is3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kohn, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Kohn, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31838,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2H3UXU9T"],"itemData":{"id":31838,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Prominent hallmarks of the widely distributed, mainly tropical marine snail genus Conus are: (1) its unusually high species diversity; it is the largest genus of animals in the sea, with more than 800 recognized species; and (2) its specialized feeding behavior of overcoming prey by injection with potent neurotoxic, paralytic venoms, and swallowing the victim whole. Including the first report of a human fatality from a Conus sting nearly 350 years ago, at least 141 human envenomations have been recorded, of which 36 were fatal. Most Conus species are quite specialized predators that can be classified in one of three major feeding guilds: they prey exclusively or nearly so on worms, primarily polychaete annelids, other gastropods, sometimes including other Conus species, or fishes. These differences are shown to relate to the severity of human envenomations, with the danger increasing generally in the order listed above and a strong likelihood that all of the known human fatalities may be attributable solely to the single piscivorous species C. geographus.","container-title":"Toxins","DOI":"10.3390/toxins11010010","ISSN":"2072-6651","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Toxins","language":"en","page":"10","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Conus Envenomation of Humans: In Fact and Fiction","title-short":"Conus Envenomation of Humans","URL":"http://www.mdpi.com/2072-6651/11/1/10","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Kohn","given":"Alan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, live in these regions. There, cone snails are harvested for aesthetic reasons, and if local populations harvest common species to sell them to tourists, rare species are subject to an active international market reserved to specialists. Restrictions are applied regardless of intent. Strict application of the Nagoya protocol in a growing number of countries also affects scientific study of biodiversity. The impact of sampling in the field for scientific purpose has been claimed to be negligible compared to the impact of tourists and collectors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1jd6scgn7r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Duda, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Duda, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31889,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KWUGK56Q"],"itemData":{"id":31889,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science (New York, N.Y.)","DOI":"10.1126/science.303.5660.955","ISSN":"1095-9203","issue":"5660","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"eng","note":"PMID: 14963310","page":"955-957; author reply 955-957","source":"PubMed","title":"How much at risk are cone snails?","volume":"303","author":[{"family":"Duda","given":"Thomas F."},{"family":"Bingham","given":"Jon-Paul"},{"family":"Livett","given":"Bruce G."},{"family":"Kohn","given":"Alan J."},{"family":"Massilia","given":"Gabriella Raybaudi"},{"family":"Schultz","given":"Joseph R."},{"family":"Down","given":"John"},{"family":"Sandall","given":"David"},{"family":"Sweedler","given":"Jonathan V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",2,13]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the latter being itself considered to be negligible in regard to the impact of human-mediated environmental changes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ah7hbmn10c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Peters, O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Leary, Hawkins, & Roberts, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Peters, O’Leary, Hawkins, & Roberts, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33295,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XCPRA2ID"],"itemData":{"id":33295,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Cape Verde in the Eastern Atlantic is typical of many island groups in supporting a wealth of endemic species both terrestrial and marine. Marine gastropod molluscs of the genus Conus, commonly known as cone snails, occur in coastal tropical waters throughout the globe, but in Cape Verde their endemism reaches its apogee with 53 out of 56 species occurring nowhere else, the majority of which are restricted to single islands and frequently to single bays. However, Cape Verde is rapidly moving to a tourism-based economy with a projected boom in infrastructure development often coincidental with the shallow-water habitat of many range-restricted Conus. The conservation assessment of Conus to standards of the International Union for the Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List of Endangered Species, found that 45.3% of 53 species assessed from Cape Verde are threatened or near-threatened with extinction compared to 7.4% of 579 species in the rest of the world. The only three Conus species globally assessed as Critically Endangered and on the cusp of extinction are all endemic to Cape Verde. Our analysis of Conus species distribution, together with spatial data of coastal protected areas and tourism development zones, identify important areas for future research and new marine protection. Our findings show that endemism with its associated risks for Conus in Cape Verde has worldwide parallels with many non-marine taxa, while our proposed strategy for Conus conservation extends beyond the confines of the country and this taxonomic group.","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2016.06.006","ISSN":"2351-9894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"201-213","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The cone snails of Cape Verde: Marine endemism at a terrestrial scale","title-short":"The cone snails of Cape Verde","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2351989416300439","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Peters","given":"Howard"},{"family":"O’Leary","given":"Bethan C."},{"family":"Hawkins","given":"Julie P."},{"family":"Roberts","given":"Callum M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",7,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

3.3.1.5.3. Recreational fisheries

Recreational fisheries are defined as the fishing of aquatic animals that do not constitute the individual’s primary source of nutrition and are not sold or traded on any market ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1gn49p4l5s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2012b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2012b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31864,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CMETXVK5"],"itemData":{"id":31864,"type":"report","abstract":"Recreational fishing is defined as fishing of aquatic animals (mainly fish) that do not constitute the individual's primary resource to meet basic nutritional needs and are not generally sold or otherwise traded on export, domestic or black markets. Recreational fishing constitutes the dominant use of wild fish stocks in all freshwaters of industrialized countries, and it is prominent in many coastal ecosystems. The importance of recreational fisheries is increasing rapidly in many transitional economies. The present Technical Guidelines for Responsible Fisheries are focused on recreational fisheries and describe strategies to promote environmentally sustainable and socially responsible management of such fisheries. To this end, the document details policy, management and behavioural recommendations for sustainable recreational fisheries that are an increasingly important component of global fisheries. Specifically, the Guidelines translate the relevant provisions of the FAO Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries into specific advice for recreational fisheries. The concept of aquatic stewardship is introduced as an overarching ethical framework needed to achieve ecologically sustainable recreational fisheries on a global scale. Within this normative mindset, the adaptive management philosophy based on quantifiable and transparent objectives and continuous learning and feedback loops is proposed along with the acknowledgement of principles such as the ecosystem approach and the precautionary approach. Detailed sections on policy and institutional frameworks (tailored towards policy-makers), recreational fisheries management actions and strategies (tailored towards fisheries managers), recreational fisheries practices (tailored towards individual recreational fishers) and recreational fisheries research (tailored to researchers and managers) provide tangible advice for responsible recreational fisheries. The special considerations necessary for recreational fisheries in developing countries and economies in transition are acknowledged. Adherence to the guidelines and recommendations presented in the present document will enable policy-makers, managers and the entire recreational fisheries sector to orient recreational fisheries towards maintaining or achieving sustainability","call-number":"SH331 .A74 2012","event-place":"Rome","note":"OCLC: ocn812531706","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Recreational fisheries","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Recreational fishing is one of the most popular leisure activities in inland waters and coastal zones worldwide, with about 11.5% of the world’s population involved ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2oo3knuvi6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Arlinghaus, Tillner, & Bork, 2015; Steven J. Cooke & Cowx, 2004; Kelleher, 2012)","plainCitation":"(R. Arlinghaus, Tillner, & Bork, 2015; Steven J. Cooke & Cowx, 2004; Kelleher, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33784,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W2Q6QY4P"],"itemData":{"id":33784,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/fme.12075","ISSN":"0969997X","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Manag Ecol","language":"en","page":"45-55","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Explaining participation rates in recreational fishing across industrialised countries","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/fme.12075","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"R."},{"family":"Tillner","given":"R."},{"family":"Bork","given":"M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",2]]}}},{"id":243,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JFSRA8QY"],"itemData":{"id":243,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1641/0006-3568(2004)054[0857:TRORFI]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0006-3568","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"BioScience","language":"en","page":"857","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Role of Recreational Fishing in Global Fish Crises","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/bioscience/article/54/9/857-859/252977","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."},{"family":"Cowx","given":"Ian G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":31859,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EVZSQ2LY"],"itemData":{"id":31859,"type":"book","publisher":"Worldbank; WorldFish","title":"Hidden harvest: The global contribution of capture fisheries","author":[{"family":"Kelleher","given":"K"},{"family":"Westlund","given":"L"},{"family":"Hoshino","given":"E"},{"family":"Mills","given":"D"},{"family":"Willmann","given":"R"},{"family":"Graaf","given":"G","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Brummett","given":"R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In industrialized countries, this proportion can be much higher, exceeding 30% (e.g., Norway) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1kluaolrj8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Arlinghaus, 2015)","plainCitation":"(R. Arlinghaus, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33784,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W2Q6QY4P"],"itemData":{"id":33784,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/fme.12075","ISSN":"0969997X","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Manag Ecol","language":"en","page":"45-55","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Explaining participation rates in recreational fishing across industrialised countries","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/fme.12075","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"R."},{"family":"Tillner","given":"R."},{"family":"Bork","given":"M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Benefits derived from recreational fisheries include substantial economic benefits in the form of expenditures and related infrastructure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a4vqk0pjc7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, Sumaila, Kaschner, & Pauly, 2010; Potts, Childs, Sauer, & Duarte, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, Sumaila, Kaschner, & Pauly, 2010; Potts, Childs, Sauer, & Duarte, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34767,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EQNETNQI"],"itemData":{"id":34767,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2010.05.005","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"1273–1278","title":"The global potential for whale watching","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"Andrés M"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"Ussif Rashid"},{"family":"Kaschner","given":"Kristin"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"David"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":31858,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MWKRP524"],"itemData":{"id":31858,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2400.2008.00617.x","ISSN":"0969997X, 13652400","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"14-20","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Characteristics and economic contribution of a developing recreational fishery in southern Angola","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1365-2400.2008.00617.x","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Potts","given":"W. M."},{"family":"Childs","given":"A.-R."},{"family":"Sauer","given":"W. H. H."},{"family":"Duarte","given":"A. D. C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, an increase in the stability of the employment buffer through increased year-round or seasonal tourism employment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"adsbfia6tm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. E. D. Smith, Khoa, & Lorenzen, 2005)","plainCitation":"(L. E. D. Smith, Khoa, & Lorenzen, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31857,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/86QVARZM"],"itemData":{"id":31857,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"An improved analytical framework and typology of fishers are provided to improve understanding of the diverse livelihood functions of inland fishing in development policy making. Inland fisheries make an important but often neglected contribution to rural livelihoods in developing countries. A holistic and widely applicable analysis of the possible livelihood functions of such fisheries is presented, focusing on fishing as one activity within diverse livelihoods. Four different livelihood strategies are identified, involving fishing as: (i) a primary livelihood of last resort, (ii) part of a diversified semi-subsistence livelihood, (iii) a specialist occupation and (iv) part of a diversified accumulation strategy. The policy implications of these strategies are found to be differentiated and poorly represented in practice by socio-economic analysis that either undfervalues fisheries or treats them solely as livelihoods of last resort and by traditional approaches to fisheries management centred on stock conservation. The need for a more diverse and flexible range of measures, tailored to local priorities and conditions and ensuring that poor people can access the benefits of inland fisheries whilst achieving conservation objectives, is identified.","container-title":"Water Policy","DOI":"10.2166/wp.2005.0023","ISSN":"1366-7017, 1996-9759","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"359-383","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Livelihood functions of inland fisheries: policy implications in developing countries","title-short":"Livelihood functions of inland fisheries","URL":"https://iwaponline.com/wp/article/7/4/359/20258/Livelihood-functions-of-inland-fisheries-policy","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Smith","given":"Laurence E. D."},{"family":"Khoa","given":"S. Nguyen"},{"family":"Lorenzen","given":"K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",8,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, psycho-social benefits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a19l67a6u69","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Floyd, Nicholas, Lee, Lee, & Scott, 2006; Parkkila, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Floyd, Nicholas, Lee, Lee, & Scott, 2006; Parkkila, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31853,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NGLQ3PLB"],"itemData":{"id":31853,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Leisure Sciences","DOI":"10.1080/01490400600745860","ISSN":"0149-0400, 1521-0588","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Leisure Sciences","language":"en","page":"351-368","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Social Stratification in Recreational Fishing Participation: Research and Policy Implications","title-short":"Social Stratification in Recreational Fishing Participation","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/01490400600745860","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Floyd","given":"Myron F."},{"family":"Nicholas","given":"Lorraine"},{"family":"Lee","given":"Injae"},{"family":"Lee","given":"Jin-Hyung"},{"family":"Scott","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",9]]}}},{"id":31856,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4A78SIJA"],"itemData":{"id":31856,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EIFAAC Occasional Paper","issue":"46","note":"publisher: Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","page":"I","title":"Methodologies for assessing socio-economic benefits of European inland recreational fisheries","author":[{"family":"Parkkila","given":"K"},{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"R"},{"family":"Artell","given":"Janne"},{"family":"Gentner","given":"B"},{"family":"Haider","given":"W"},{"family":"Aas","given":"Ø"},{"family":"Barton","given":"D"},{"family":"Roth","given":"Eva"},{"family":"Sipponen","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Hickley","given":"Phil"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and recreational fisher involvement in conservation efforts such as habitat restoration, citizen science, and research ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1b95eulgak","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Copeland, Baker, Koehn, Morris, & Cowx, 2017; Tufts, Holden, & DeMille, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Copeland, Baker, Koehn, Morris, & Cowx, 2017; Tufts, Holden, & DeMille, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31854,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7H3HU9Z6"],"itemData":{"id":31854,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/fme.12204","ISSN":"0969997X","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Manag Ecol","language":"en","page":"82-92","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Motivations of recreational fishers involved in fish habitat management","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/fme.12204","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Copeland","given":"C."},{"family":"Baker","given":"E."},{"family":"Koehn","given":"J. D."},{"family":"Morris","given":"S. G."},{"family":"Cowx","given":"I. G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}},{"id":31855,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/39ZLC8S8"],"itemData":{"id":31855,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Environmental Studies","DOI":"10.1080/00207233.2015.1022987","ISSN":"0020-7233, 1029-0400","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Environmental Studies","language":"en","page":"850-868","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Benefits arising from sustainable use of North America’s fishery resources: economic and conservation impacts of recreational angling","title-short":"Benefits arising from sustainable use of North America’s fishery resources","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00207233.2015.1022987","volume":"72","author":[{"family":"Tufts","given":"B.L."},{"family":"Holden","given":"J."},{"family":"DeMille","given":"M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",9,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      While commercial fisheries catch by country are documented since 1950 by the FAO, data for global marine recreational catches remains scarce. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1iu1tql1mn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Freire, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Freire, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31843,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XE7RRYWS"],"itemData":{"id":31843,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2020.00012","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","page":"12","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Estimating Global Catches of Marine Recreational Fisheries","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fmars.2020.00012/full","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Freire","given":"Kátia Meirelles Felizola"},{"family":"Belhabib","given":"Dyhia"},{"family":"Espedido","given":"Jeniffer C."},{"family":"Hood","given":"Lincoln"},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin M."},{"family":"Lam","given":"Vicky W. L."},{"family":"Machado","given":"Michel L."},{"family":"Mendonça","given":"Jocemar Tomasino"},{"family":"Meeuwig","given":"Jessica J."},{"family":"Moro","given":"Pietro S."},{"family":"Motta","given":"Fábio S."},{"family":"Palomares","given":"Maria-Lourdes D."},{"family":"Smith","given":"Nicola"},{"family":"Teh","given":"Lydia"},{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"},{"family":"Zylich","given":"Kyrstn"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} reported three published estimates, one of 0.5 million tons per year from FAO approximated recreational catches (marine and inland) based on a questionnaire answered by people in 30 mostly developed countries. A second estimate reached 10.9 million tons per year was derived from an extrapolation of Canadian recreational participation and catch rates, and included both marine and inland areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"abd5kr8d59","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Steven J. Cooke & Cowx, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Steven J. Cooke & Cowx, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":243,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JFSRA8QY"],"itemData":{"id":243,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1641/0006-3568(2004)054[0857:TRORFI]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0006-3568","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"BioScience","language":"en","page":"857","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Role of Recreational Fishing in Global Fish Crises","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/bioscience/article/54/9/857-859/252977","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."},{"family":"Cowx","given":"Ian G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Freire ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6Eg24Rwr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Freire, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Freire, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31843,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XE7RRYWS"],"itemData":{"id":31843,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2020.00012","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","page":"12","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Estimating Global Catches of Marine Recreational Fisheries","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fmars.2020.00012/full","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Freire","given":"Kátia Meirelles Felizola"},{"family":"Belhabib","given":"Dyhia"},{"family":"Espedido","given":"Jeniffer C."},{"family":"Hood","given":"Lincoln"},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin M."},{"family":"Lam","given":"Vicky W. L."},{"family":"Machado","given":"Michel L."},{"family":"Mendonça","given":"Jocemar Tomasino"},{"family":"Meeuwig","given":"Jessica J."},{"family":"Moro","given":"Pietro S."},{"family":"Motta","given":"Fábio S."},{"family":"Palomares","given":"Maria-Lourdes D."},{"family":"Smith","given":"Nicola"},{"family":"Teh","given":"Lydia"},{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"},{"family":"Zylich","given":"Kyrstn"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} describe estimates of likely marine recreational catches for 1950-2014, based on independent reconstructions for 125 countries. Those estimates of marine recreational fisheries show that catches grew globally until the early 1980s, stabilized during the 1990s, and began increasing again thereafter, amounting to around 900,000 tons in 2014. Marine recreational catches account therefore for slightly less than 1% of total global marine catches (Figure 3.39). Trends vary regionally, decreasing strongly in North America, slightly decreasing in Europe and Oceania, while increasing in Asia, South America and Africa. The derived taxonomic composition indicates that recent catches were dominated by Sparidae (12% of total catches), followed by Scombridae (10%), Carangidae (6%), Gadidae (5%), and Sciaenidae (4%). The importance of Elasmobranchii (sharks and rays) in recreational fisheries in some regions is of concern, given the life-history traits of these taxa. Preliminary catch reconstruction, despite high data uncertainty, should encourage efforts to improve national data reporting of recreational catches (Figure 3.40). 


Figure 3.39 Global marine catches from recreational fisheries by major geographic region for 1950–2014 for all countries with marine recreational fisheries. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pDK49Sbk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Freire, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Freire, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31843,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XE7RRYWS"],"itemData":{"id":31843,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2020.00012","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","page":"12","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Estimating Global Catches of Marine Recreational Fisheries","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fmars.2020.00012/full","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Freire","given":"Kátia Meirelles Felizola"},{"family":"Belhabib","given":"Dyhia"},{"family":"Espedido","given":"Jeniffer C."},{"family":"Hood","given":"Lincoln"},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin M."},{"family":"Lam","given":"Vicky W. L."},{"family":"Machado","given":"Michel L."},{"family":"Mendonça","given":"Jocemar Tomasino"},{"family":"Meeuwig","given":"Jessica J."},{"family":"Moro","given":"Pietro S."},{"family":"Motta","given":"Fábio S."},{"family":"Palomares","given":"Maria-Lourdes D."},{"family":"Smith","given":"Nicola"},{"family":"Teh","given":"Lydia"},{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"},{"family":"Zylich","given":"Kyrstn"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY 4.0.



Figure 3.40 Taxonomic composition of global recreational catches by the nine most represented families or higher groupings. ‘Marine fishes nei’ (nei, not elsewhere included) comprises a large contribution of taxonomically unidentified catches; while ‘Others’ comprises all additional taxa with minor contributions pooled. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sjsu8qWv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Freire, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Freire, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31843,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XE7RRYWS"],"itemData":{"id":31843,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2020.00012","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","page":"12","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Estimating Global Catches of Marine Recreational Fisheries","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fmars.2020.00012/full","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Freire","given":"Kátia Meirelles Felizola"},{"family":"Belhabib","given":"Dyhia"},{"family":"Espedido","given":"Jeniffer C."},{"family":"Hood","given":"Lincoln"},{"family":"Kleisner","given":"Kristin M."},{"family":"Lam","given":"Vicky W. L."},{"family":"Machado","given":"Michel L."},{"family":"Mendonça","given":"Jocemar Tomasino"},{"family":"Meeuwig","given":"Jessica J."},{"family":"Moro","given":"Pietro S."},{"family":"Motta","given":"Fábio S."},{"family":"Palomares","given":"Maria-Lourdes D."},{"family":"Smith","given":"Nicola"},{"family":"Teh","given":"Lydia"},{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"},{"family":"Zylich","given":"Kyrstn"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY 4.0

      In Europe, the majority of recreational and tourism fishing is carried out in the Mediterranean Sea ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1ThLarnK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Antunes, 2015; Cillari, 2012; J. Lloret, 2018; Marengo, 2015; Mavruk, Saygu, Bengil, Alan, & Azzurro, 2018; Ulman, 2015a; Ulman & Pauly, 2016b)","plainCitation":"(C. Antunes, 2015; Cillari, 2012; J. Lloret, 2018; Marengo, 2015; Mavruk, Saygu, Bengil, Alan, & Azzurro, 2018; Ulman, 2015a; Ulman & Pauly, 2016b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31782,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C4RFSYFY"],"itemData":{"id":31782,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Freshwater Fisheries Ecology","event-place":"Oxford, UK","ISBN":"978-1-118-39442-7","note":"DOI: 10.1002/9781118394380.ch23","page":"268-282","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","publisher-place":"Oxford, UK","title":"Iberian inland fisheries","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84984844857&doi=10.1002%2f9781118394380.ch23&partnerID=40&md5=c6a7af8f3dd1d871e5423260a9b0aae9","author":[{"family":"Antunes","given":"C."},{"family":"Cobo","given":"F."},{"family":"Araújo","given":"M.J."}],"editor":[{"family":"Craig","given":"J.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5CSYQBEV"],"itemData":{"id":383,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The crisis of small-scale fisheries is causing the progressive abandonment of ancient fishing traditions and customs along a coastal area of the Strait of Sicily. For instance, worthwhile mentioning is the rapid decline in the use of bottom longline, which used to be one of the most popular gears in this area in past times. This study aims at investigating the potential of bottom longline for tourism purposes, in the perspective of recovering ancient fishing crafts. Experimental surveys were carried out to explore the effectiveness of this gear on soft bottoms, which are not usually exploited by local fishermen. An analysis of catches was carried out to assess their suitability within fishing tourism actions. The results gathered during the period of investigation demonstrate a reasonable variety of catches, with large-size specimens representing the bulk of captures. The highest values in terms of abundance and biomass were recorded for bogue Boops boops (L.) and axillary seabream Pagellus acarne (Risso). The possible enhancement of these two low commercial value species is discussed. Results also highlight the suitability of the use of bottom longline during tourism low seasons. The use of longline would produce several positive impacts in the framework of fishing tourism in the study area, such as for instance the creation of alternative sources of income for artisanal fishers, the enhancement of underexploited species in the perspective of environmental sustainability, the recovery and re-launch of a disappearing fishing gear, thus contributing to safeguard cultural diversity.","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2011.10.007","ISSN":"0964-5691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"20-26","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The use of bottom longline on soft bottoms: An opportunity of development for fishing tourism along a coastal area of the Strait of Sicily (Mediterranean Sea)","title-short":"The use of bottom longline on soft bottoms","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0964569111001608","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Cillari","given":"Tiziana"},{"family":"Falautano","given":"Manuela"},{"family":"Castriota","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Marino","given":"Vanessa"},{"family":"Vivona","given":"Pietro"},{"family":"Andaloro","given":"Franco"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",1,1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":392,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4LPJBJKI"],"itemData":{"id":392,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2016.11.007","page":"176-186","title":"Small-scale coastal fisheries in European Seas are not what they were: Ecological, social and economic changes","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85007195924&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2016.11.007&partnerID=40&md5=7099a107b633d84556c70468ec43c71b","volume":"98","author":[{"family":"Lloret","given":"J."},{"family":"Cowx","given":"I.G."},{"family":"Cabral","given":"H."},{"family":"Castro","given":"M."},{"family":"Font","given":"T."},{"family":"Gonçalves","given":"J.M.S."},{"family":"Gordoa","given":"A."},{"family":"Hoefnagel","given":"E."},{"family":"Mati?-Skoko","given":"S."},{"family":"Mikkelsen","given":"E."},{"family":"Morales-Nin","given":"B."},{"family":"Moutopoulos","given":"D.K."},{"family":"Muñoz","given":"M."},{"family":"Santos","given":"M.N.","non-dropping-particle":"dos"},{"family":"Pintassilgo","given":"P."},{"family":"Pita","given":"C."},{"family":"Stergiou","given":"K.I."},{"family":"Ünal","given":"V."},{"family":"Veiga","given":"P."},{"family":"Erzini","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31798,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2BB3P5VE"],"itemData":{"id":31798,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/fme.12110","issue":"3","page":"249-260","title":"Comparative analysis of artisanal and recreational fisheries for Dentex dentex in a Marine Protected Area","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84929265099&doi=10.1111%2ffme.12110&partnerID=40&md5=3d583f397f90edd8f42106c112c87d15","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Marengo","given":"M."},{"family":"Culioli","given":"J.-M."},{"family":"Santoni","given":"M.-C."},{"family":"Marchand","given":"B."},{"family":"Durieux","given":"E.D.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":9093,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CE9DJUI3"],"itemData":{"id":9093,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.10.018","page":"141-148","title":"Grouper fishery in the Northeastern Mediterranean: An assessment based on interviews on resource users","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85033683562&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2017.10.018&partnerID=40&md5=b17632ce333e8d7be1596b3198cd602b","volume":"87","author":[{"family":"Mavruk","given":"S."},{"family":"Saygu","given":"?."},{"family":"Bengil","given":"F."},{"family":"Alan","given":"V."},{"family":"Azzurro","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":28319,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RWXKVBM6"],"itemData":{"id":28319,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Development and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1007/s10668-014-9576-z","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"801–821","title":"Unifying the catch data of a divided island: Cyprus’s marine fisheries catches","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10668-014-9576-z","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Ulman","given":"A."},{"family":"Çiçek","given":"B.A."},{"family":"Salihoglu","given":"I."},{"family":"Petrou","given":"A."},{"family":"Patsalidou","given":"M."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."},{"family":"Zeller","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":1044,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9F96IVMS"],"itemData":{"id":1044,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2016.05.013","page":"74-79","title":"Making history count: The shifting baselines of Turkish fisheries","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84969921503&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2016.05.013&partnerID=40&md5=60729f97b02cad008a35d8873df8a3ab","volume":"183","author":[{"family":"Ulman","given":"A."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, although some of these fishing practices take place in the Atlantic coast or its islands and archipelagos ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1hvc7hm0pp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carvalho, 2017; Das & Afonso, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Carvalho, 2017; Das & Afonso, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":376,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L5VUDIJC"],"itemData":{"id":376,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.01.008","page":"34-44","title":"Socio-economic, operational and technical characterisation of the harvesting of gooseneck barnacle (Pollicipes pollicipes) in SW Portugal: Insights towards fishery co-management","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85009170677&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2017.01.008&partnerID=40&md5=7811606bf0e2b057b997b0e29ed391f9","volume":"78","author":[{"family":"Carvalho","given":"A.N."},{"family":"Vasconcelos","given":"P."},{"family":"Piló","given":"D."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"F."},{"family":"Gaspar","given":"M.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":31904,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZWAGA585"],"itemData":{"id":31904,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2017.00354","issue":"NOV","title":"Review of the diversity, ecology, and conservation of elasmobranchs in the Azores region, Mid-North Atlantic","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85034106000&doi=10.3389%2ffmars.2017.00354&partnerID=40&md5=78cadf00ad1d949a7e0cbf54c082a93f","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Das","given":"D."},{"family":"Afonso","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is well established in the literature reviewed that the recreational small-scale fisheries performed in Europe is not sustainable, and only 30% of the studies reviewed show any level of sustainable exploitation of recreational small-scale fishing activities (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Unsustainability is assumed due to the lack of regulation of the recreational fishing activity in general. 
      The available information shows that the majority of this practice is not necessarily linked with the tourism industry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"am6ahmhufr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cillari, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Cillari, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5CSYQBEV"],"itemData":{"id":383,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The crisis of small-scale fisheries is causing the progressive abandonment of ancient fishing traditions and customs along a coastal area of the Strait of Sicily. For instance, worthwhile mentioning is the rapid decline in the use of bottom longline, which used to be one of the most popular gears in this area in past times. This study aims at investigating the potential of bottom longline for tourism purposes, in the perspective of recovering ancient fishing crafts. Experimental surveys were carried out to explore the effectiveness of this gear on soft bottoms, which are not usually exploited by local fishermen. An analysis of catches was carried out to assess their suitability within fishing tourism actions. The results gathered during the period of investigation demonstrate a reasonable variety of catches, with large-size specimens representing the bulk of captures. The highest values in terms of abundance and biomass were recorded for bogue Boops boops (L.) and axillary seabream Pagellus acarne (Risso). The possible enhancement of these two low commercial value species is discussed. Results also highlight the suitability of the use of bottom longline during tourism low seasons. The use of longline would produce several positive impacts in the framework of fishing tourism in the study area, such as for instance the creation of alternative sources of income for artisanal fishers, the enhancement of underexploited species in the perspective of environmental sustainability, the recovery and re-launch of a disappearing fishing gear, thus contributing to safeguard cultural diversity.","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2011.10.007","ISSN":"0964-5691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"20-26","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The use of bottom longline on soft bottoms: An opportunity of development for fishing tourism along a coastal area of the Strait of Sicily (Mediterranean Sea)","title-short":"The use of bottom longline on soft bottoms","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0964569111001608","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Cillari","given":"Tiziana"},{"family":"Falautano","given":"Manuela"},{"family":"Castriota","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Marino","given":"Vanessa"},{"family":"Vivona","given":"Pietro"},{"family":"Andaloro","given":"Franco"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Instead, it is usually carried out by locals as cultural practices that maintain important connections between communities and nature. This allows for some territorial overlap, and consequently for some level of competition with other fishing practices, mainly the commercial fishing for food and feed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a18e95gpaum","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carvalho, 2017; Das & Afonso, 2017; Marengo, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Carvalho, 2017; Das & Afonso, 2017; Marengo, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":376,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L5VUDIJC"],"itemData":{"id":376,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.01.008","page":"34-44","title":"Socio-economic, operational and technical characterisation of the harvesting of gooseneck barnacle (Pollicipes pollicipes) in SW Portugal: Insights towards fishery co-management","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85009170677&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2017.01.008&partnerID=40&md5=7811606bf0e2b057b997b0e29ed391f9","volume":"78","author":[{"family":"Carvalho","given":"A.N."},{"family":"Vasconcelos","given":"P."},{"family":"Piló","given":"D."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"F."},{"family":"Gaspar","given":"M.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":31904,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZWAGA585"],"itemData":{"id":31904,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2017.00354","issue":"NOV","title":"Review of the diversity, ecology, and conservation of elasmobranchs in the Azores region, Mid-North Atlantic","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85034106000&doi=10.3389%2ffmars.2017.00354&partnerID=40&md5=78cadf00ad1d949a7e0cbf54c082a93f","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Das","given":"D."},{"family":"Afonso","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":31798,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2BB3P5VE"],"itemData":{"id":31798,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/fme.12110","issue":"3","page":"249-260","title":"Comparative analysis of artisanal and recreational fisheries for Dentex dentex in a Marine Protected Area","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84929265099&doi=10.1111%2ffme.12110&partnerID=40&md5=3d583f397f90edd8f42106c112c87d15","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Marengo","given":"M."},{"family":"Culioli","given":"J.-M."},{"family":"Santoni","given":"M.-C."},{"family":"Marchand","given":"B."},{"family":"Durieux","given":"E.D.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although the European regulation of fisheries in general tends to be very widespread, most of the recreational fishing practices are not formal, and are therefore unregulated ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a29do100fnr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Lloret, 2018)","plainCitation":"(J. Lloret, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":392,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4LPJBJKI"],"itemData":{"id":392,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2016.11.007","page":"176-186","title":"Small-scale coastal fisheries in European Seas are not what they were: Ecological, social and economic changes","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85007195924&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2016.11.007&partnerID=40&md5=7099a107b633d84556c70468ec43c71b","volume":"98","author":[{"family":"Lloret","given":"J."},{"family":"Cowx","given":"I.G."},{"family":"Cabral","given":"H."},{"family":"Castro","given":"M."},{"family":"Font","given":"T."},{"family":"Gonçalves","given":"J.M.S."},{"family":"Gordoa","given":"A."},{"family":"Hoefnagel","given":"E."},{"family":"Mati?-Skoko","given":"S."},{"family":"Mikkelsen","given":"E."},{"family":"Morales-Nin","given":"B."},{"family":"Moutopoulos","given":"D.K."},{"family":"Muñoz","given":"M."},{"family":"Santos","given":"M.N.","non-dropping-particle":"dos"},{"family":"Pintassilgo","given":"P."},{"family":"Pita","given":"C."},{"family":"Stergiou","given":"K.I."},{"family":"Ünal","given":"V."},{"family":"Veiga","given":"P."},{"family":"Erzini","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. On the other hand, sustainable recreational fishing practices in Europe are probably due to the use of more selective gears ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"af48bh471r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cillari, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Cillari, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5CSYQBEV"],"itemData":{"id":383,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The crisis of small-scale fisheries is causing the progressive abandonment of ancient fishing traditions and customs along a coastal area of the Strait of Sicily. For instance, worthwhile mentioning is the rapid decline in the use of bottom longline, which used to be one of the most popular gears in this area in past times. This study aims at investigating the potential of bottom longline for tourism purposes, in the perspective of recovering ancient fishing crafts. Experimental surveys were carried out to explore the effectiveness of this gear on soft bottoms, which are not usually exploited by local fishermen. An analysis of catches was carried out to assess their suitability within fishing tourism actions. The results gathered during the period of investigation demonstrate a reasonable variety of catches, with large-size specimens representing the bulk of captures. The highest values in terms of abundance and biomass were recorded for bogue Boops boops (L.) and axillary seabream Pagellus acarne (Risso). The possible enhancement of these two low commercial value species is discussed. Results also highlight the suitability of the use of bottom longline during tourism low seasons. The use of longline would produce several positive impacts in the framework of fishing tourism in the study area, such as for instance the creation of alternative sources of income for artisanal fishers, the enhancement of underexploited species in the perspective of environmental sustainability, the recovery and re-launch of a disappearing fishing gear, thus contributing to safeguard cultural diversity.","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2011.10.007","ISSN":"0964-5691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"20-26","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The use of bottom longline on soft bottoms: An opportunity of development for fishing tourism along a coastal area of the Strait of Sicily (Mediterranean Sea)","title-short":"The use of bottom longline on soft bottoms","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0964569111001608","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Cillari","given":"Tiziana"},{"family":"Falautano","given":"Manuela"},{"family":"Castriota","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Marino","given":"Vanessa"},{"family":"Vivona","given":"Pietro"},{"family":"Andaloro","given":"Franco"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and those that are carried out in marine protected areas or other specific areas designated by local management arrangements ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a4j9hkigo8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Marengo, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Marengo, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31798,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2BB3P5VE"],"itemData":{"id":31798,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/fme.12110","issue":"3","page":"249-260","title":"Comparative analysis of artisanal and recreational fisheries for Dentex dentex in a Marine Protected Area","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84929265099&doi=10.1111%2ffme.12110&partnerID=40&md5=3d583f397f90edd8f42106c112c87d15","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Marengo","given":"M."},{"family":"Culioli","given":"J.-M."},{"family":"Santoni","given":"M.-C."},{"family":"Marchand","given":"B."},{"family":"Durieux","given":"E.D.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
 	In Africa, despite the small number of studies on small-scale recreation and tourism fisheries, the reviewed scientific literature suggested that this type of fishing is unsustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aefa6s8suf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Belhabib, 2016; Leeney, 2016, 2017; Leeney & Poncelet, 2015; McCafferty, 2012)","plainCitation":"(D. Belhabib, 2016; Leeney, 2016, 2017; Leeney & Poncelet, 2015; McCafferty, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31799,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4ET9F84J"],"itemData":{"id":31799,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Palgrave Communications","DOI":"10.1057/palcomms.2015.50","title":"Best for pleasure, not for business: Evaluating recreational marine fisheries in West Africa using unconventional sources of data","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84983558177&doi=10.1057%2fpalcomms.2015.50&partnerID=40&md5=23c6d71a2db9f25411d5f14b7cb9c3e2","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Belhabib","given":"D."},{"family":"Campredon","given":"P."},{"family":"Lazar","given":"N."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U.R."},{"family":"Baye","given":"B.C."},{"family":"Kane","given":"E.A."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":997,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4V9V5NLL"],"itemData":{"id":997,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2542","issue":"2","page":"381-385","title":"Fishers' ecological knowledge of sawfishes in Lake Piso, Liberia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84922572107&doi=10.1002%2faqc.2542&partnerID=40&md5=1fd25f910f2f63377457591d49ee3224","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Leeney","given":"R.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":995,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/42KPLHZG"],"itemData":{"id":995,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.2950","issue":"2","title":"Are sawfishes still present in Mozambique? A baseline ecological study","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85013214701&doi=10.7717%2fpeerj.2950&partnerID=40&md5=5f48501279c24c43e086c4892c40af21","volume":"2017","author":[{"family":"Leeney","given":"R.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1094,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/33KIW5I9"],"itemData":{"id":1094,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2419","issue":"3","page":"411-430","title":"Using fishers' ecological knowledge to assess the status and cultural importance of sawfish in Guinea-Bissau","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84932198453&doi=10.1002%2faqc.2419&partnerID=40&md5=c34873c32ee92a66b6d05e6ecbc1860b","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Leeney","given":"R.H."},{"family":"Poncelet","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31785,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TXQYQZJY"],"itemData":{"id":31785,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Water SA","DOI":"10.4314/wsa.v38i2.18","issue":"2","page":"327-344","title":"The use of water resources for inland fisheries in South Africa","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84860523720&doi=10.4314%2fwsa.v38i2.18&partnerID=40&md5=f1201a95504d83ff6f4e1d70e8b49619","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"McCafferty","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Ellender","given":"B.R."},{"family":"Weyl","given":"O.L.F."},{"family":"Britz","given":"P.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This unsustainability is assumed due to strong fishing pressure, and lack of regulation and monitoring which means there is a relative lack of data available. The assessments cover most of the West Coast, encompassing many fish species and also a good part of the East Coast for the recreational fishing industry, mainly targeting the sawfish (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). This later fishing practice is experiencing a strong decline in the last decades and in some areas the sawfish is now rarely detected.
 	In Latin America only two studies evaluated recreational fisheries, but in both cases these fisheries co-occur with artisanal commercial fisheries that exploit fish for food and none were considered as being fully sustainable (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). The increase in recreational spear fishing caused an unsustainable decline on catches and sizes of three reef fish species in Chile ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YJ2WhKIx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Godoy, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Godoy, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1078,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3N87JPA4"],"itemData":{"id":1078,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Ecological Applications","DOI":"10.1890/09-1806.1","issue":"6","page":"1504-1511","title":"Spearfishing to depletion: Evidence from temperate reef fishes in Chile","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-77956444151&doi=10.1890%2f09-1806.1&partnerID=40&md5=ba4f262bec2f87464ce70138d8ebbe05","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Godoy","given":"N."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."},{"family":"Vasquez","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Castilla","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The tourism related to fishing, either in the form of tourists fishing for recreation or eating fish in hotels and restaurants, has increased over time and is an important economic activity in the Bahamas and other Caribbean Island countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IGd0i4ve","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. S. Smith & Zeller, 2016)","plainCitation":"(N. S. Smith & Zeller, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":996,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MUCQ2J7Z"],"itemData":{"id":996,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fishery Bulletin","ISSN":"0090-0656","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fishery Bulletin","title":"Unreported catch and tourist demand on local fisheries of small island states: the case of The Bahamas, 1950-2010.","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Smith","given":"Nicola S"},{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the recreational catches related to tourism, about half of total catches in the Bahamas, have been unreported and poorly regulated, which is again assumed to compromise sustainability over time ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"o0RGMrqe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. S. Smith & Zeller, 2016)","plainCitation":"(N. S. Smith & Zeller, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":996,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MUCQ2J7Z"],"itemData":{"id":996,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fishery Bulletin","ISSN":"0090-0656","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fishery Bulletin","title":"Unreported catch and tourist demand on local fisheries of small island states: the case of The Bahamas, 1950-2010.","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Smith","given":"Nicola S"},{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In North America, most of the reviewed studies address the recreational coastal fisheries in the United States of America, especially in Florida (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Some of these studies on recreational fisheries of the bonefish (Albula vulpes) indicate an unsustainable pattern of decline in abundance and size of this fish species, which has suffered increased fishing effort and post-release mortality, leading to an overexploited catch-and-release fishery with negative population effects ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DtU5wzT3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Frezza & Clem, 2015; Rehage, 2019; R. O. Santos, Rehage, Kroloff, Heinen, & Adams, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Frezza & Clem, 2015; Rehage, 2019; R. O. Santos, Rehage, Kroloff, Heinen, & Adams, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1079,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QIJBCCEH"],"itemData":{"id":1079,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environmental Biology of Fishes","DOI":"10.1007/s10641-015-0442-0","issue":"11","page":"2187-2202","title":"Using local fishers’ knowledge to characterize historical trends in the Florida Bay bonefish population and fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84943663521&doi=10.1007%2fs10641-015-0442-0&partnerID=40&md5=39b27769a2bf29f9dd34fa965ba20478","volume":"98","author":[{"family":"Frezza","given":"P.E."},{"family":"Clem","given":"S.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1077,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WG5KLWZ9"],"itemData":{"id":1077,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environmental Biology of Fishes","DOI":"10.1007/s10641-018-0831-2","issue":"2","page":"285-298","title":"How has the quality of bonefishing changed over the past 40 years? Using local ecological knowledge to quantitatively inform population declines in the South Florida flats fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85058004526&doi=10.1007%2fs10641-018-0831-2&partnerID=40&md5=3b421e92ae21ab85b81c4f2accf875ae","volume":"102","author":[{"family":"Rehage","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Santos","given":"R.O."},{"family":"Kroloff","given":"E.K.N."},{"family":"Heinen","given":"J.T."},{"family":"Lai","given":"Q."},{"family":"Black","given":"B.D."},{"family":"Boucek","given":"R.E."},{"family":"Adams","given":"A.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":994,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YUDMDC3P"],"itemData":{"id":994,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environmental Biology of Fishes","DOI":"10.1007/s10641-018-0828-x","issue":"2","page":"299-317","title":"Combining data sources to elucidate spatial patterns in recreational catch and effort: fisheries-dependent data and local ecological knowledge applied to the South Florida bonefish fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85055867654&doi=10.1007%2fs10641-018-0828-x&partnerID=40&md5=4bddfcea6267ab5f75e48158a4b1428b","volume":"102","author":[{"family":"Santos","given":"R.O."},{"family":"Rehage","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Kroloff","given":"E.K.N."},{"family":"Heinen","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Adams","given":"A.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another study showed anglers and guides are environmentally conscientious and self-aware of potential anthropogenic drivers of bonefish decline, which may have also been influenced by climate and water quality ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IMnJyF7q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kroloff, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Kroloff, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1075,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/87KQJRFM"],"itemData":{"id":1075,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environmental Biology of Fishes","DOI":"10.1007/s10641-018-0812-5","issue":"2","page":"319-328","title":"Understanding the decline of catch-and-release fishery with angler knowledge: a key informant approach applied to South Florida bonefish","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85055945724&doi=10.1007%2fs10641-018-0812-5&partnerID=40&md5=c67809c0efaf0039684b82adf1ba0ac2","volume":"102","author":[{"family":"Kroloff","given":"E.K.N."},{"family":"Heinen","given":"J.T."},{"family":"Braddock","given":"K.N."},{"family":"Rehage","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Santos","given":"R.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A study analyzing 22 fish species of the snapper-grouper reef fish complex in the Florida Keys reported that the majority of these species have been fished unsustainably, though overfishing appears most severe for those long-lived, slow-growing fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dHjk7XOI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ault, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Ault, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1072,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7WDGPK8M"],"itemData":{"id":1072,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bulletin of Marine Science","issue":"2","page":"595-622","title":"Towards sustainable multispecies fisheries in the Florida, USA, coral reef ecosystem","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-17444364515&partnerID=40&md5=c2dcc3a13595289f5d7e95e1fbccdda4","volume":"76","author":[{"family":"Ault","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Bohnsack","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Smith","given":"S.G."},{"family":"Luo","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The only inland study that provided a comprehensive review of recreational and other fisheries in the region of Great Lakes and Mississippi River (United States of America and Canada). It describes internal threats such as overexploitation and bycatch/release mortality, as well as external threats such as inter-sectoral conflicts, environmental change (e.g., habitat alteration and fragmentation), water availability, and introduction of non-native species and pollution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"v5P6qLSf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S.J. Cooke & Murchie, 2015)","plainCitation":"(S.J. Cooke & Murchie, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1076,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8ZW6Z25M"],"itemData":{"id":1076,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/fme.12005","issue":"1","page":"1-13","title":"Status of aboriginal, commercial and recreational inland fisheries in North America: Past, present and future","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84921899044&doi=10.1111%2ffme.12005&partnerID=40&md5=8fb48b0fa2a71e9f519b46338bafa65d","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Cooke","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Murchie","given":"K.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In Asia-Pacific, only four reviewed studies address recreational coastal or inland fisheries (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Although accounting for only a small fraction of the total reconstructed fisheries catches in 25 Pacific Island countries, recreational fisheries have economic relevance to local coastal communities, as these fisheries are related to tourism ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2RH3P8Mm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Zeller, 2015)","plainCitation":"(D. Zeller, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31756,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7Z73PVY9"],"itemData":{"id":31756,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Coral Reefs","DOI":"10.1007/s00338-014-1219-1","ISSN":"0722-4028, 1432-0975","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Coral Reefs","language":"en","page":"25-39","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesis of underreported small-scale fisheries catch in Pacific island waters","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00338-014-1219-1","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Zeller","given":"D."},{"family":"Harper","given":"S."},{"family":"Zylich","given":"K."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The occurrence of whale sharks, which are not commercially exploited and are regularly sighted by fishers, indicates opportunities for the development of non-extractive tourism activities based on observation of whale sharks and promoting collaboration and use of fisher indigenous and local knowledge in Eastern Indonesia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jhW1YiSt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stacey, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Stacey, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31900,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RZWZXL4R"],"itemData":{"id":31900,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Conservation and Society","DOI":"10.4103/0972-4923.92197","issue":"1","page":"63-75","title":"Prospects for whale shark conservation in Eastern Indonesia through bajo traditional ecological knowledge and community-based monitoring","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84856595278&doi=10.4103%2f0972-4923.92197&partnerID=40&md5=a2c1807324a5caaac644ce3987b691c7","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Stacey","given":"N.E."},{"family":"Karam","given":"J."},{"family":"Meekan","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Pickering","given":"S."},{"family":"Ninef","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The two cases of inland recreational fisheries indicate potentially overfished populations of crayfish (Euastacus armatus) in Australia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FkqvjR5H","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zukowski, Curtis, & Watts, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Zukowski, Curtis, & Watts, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1000,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ITP8HN4Q"],"itemData":{"id":1000,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2011.03.020","issue":"1","page":"120-127","title":"Using fisher local ecological knowledge to improve management: The Murray crayfish in Australia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-79955965744&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2011.03.020&partnerID=40&md5=e20ff2cbca0b898aca784bd5227e2fb8","volume":"110","author":[{"family":"Zukowski","given":"S."},{"family":"Curtis","given":"A."},{"family":"Watts","given":"R.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and more sustainable fisheries of migratory fish in the lower Mekong River basin ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"p0W0gzwO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mattson, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Mattson, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GBAKVSJ5"],"itemData":{"id":31749,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"International Journal of Ecology and Environmental Sciences","issue":"1","page":"109-117","title":"Conservation and enhancement of fisheries: The case of the Lower Mekong Basin","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-33745043289&partnerID=40&md5=65dbcc1ebfc1ce026fc9f27ba7e6c13e","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Mattson","given":"N.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Manta rays (Manta alfredi) have been exploited possibly at unsustainable levels for food and medicinal use in the Philippines ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PRCJd00q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Acebes, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Acebes, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31911,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UCKN4HAG"],"itemData":{"id":31911,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Biodiversity Records","DOI":"10.1186/s41200-016-0098-2","issue":"1","title":"Characteristics of a previously undescribed fishery and habitat for Manta alfredi in the Philippines","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85052333537&doi=10.1186%2fs41200-016-0098-2&partnerID=40&md5=d9abc2a8a74e0ba8f466f6fd4f3a8e68","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Acebes","given":"J.M.V."},{"family":"Barr","given":"Y."},{"family":"Pereda","given":"J.M.R."},{"family":"Santos","given":"M.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Recreational fisheries are of concern as fishers concentrate their effort on specific areas, times, species and sizes, leading to greater impacts on targeted stocks. For instance, the nearshore zones more intensively exploited by marine recreational fishers are often critical habitats for multiple life stages of many fish (e.g., spawning, nursery), and immature life stages may be targeted in these areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1rf8j3pt2s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Steven J. Cooke & Cowx, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Steven J. Cooke & Cowx, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":243,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JFSRA8QY"],"itemData":{"id":243,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1641/0006-3568(2004)054[0857:TRORFI]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0006-3568","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"BioScience","language":"en","page":"857","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Role of Recreational Fishing in Global Fish Crises","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/bioscience/article/54/9/857-859/252977","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."},{"family":"Cowx","given":"Ian G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Recreational fishers also selectively target larger and older “trophy” fish, often of keystone, top-predatory species, with life-history characteristics that make them vulnerable to exploitation (late age-at-maturity, low fecundity), which can lead to demographic or evolutionary effects on fish populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a6rcvkk7m7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Robert Arlinghaus & Cooke, 2009; Lewin, Arlinghaus, & Mehner, 2006; J Lloret, 2020; Prato, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Robert Arlinghaus & Cooke, 2009; Lewin, Arlinghaus, & Mehner, 2006; J Lloret, 2020; Prato, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31866,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PE2ELQJV"],"itemData":{"id":31866,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Recreational Hunting, Conservation and Rural Livelihoods","event-place":"Oxford, UK","ISBN":"978-1-4443-0317-9","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1002/9781444303179.ch3","page":"39-58","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","publisher-place":"Oxford, UK","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recreational Fisheries: Socioeconomic Importance, Conservation Issues and Management Challenges","title-short":"Recreational Fisheries","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/9781444303179.ch3","editor":[{"family":"Dickson","given":"Barney"},{"family":"Hutton","given":"Jon"},{"family":"Adams","given":"William M."}],"author":[{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",1,30]]}}},{"id":31865,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A5C9385G"],"itemData":{"id":31865,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1080/10641260600886455","ISSN":"1064-1262, 1547-6553","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","language":"en","page":"305-367","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Documented and Potential Biological Impacts of Recreational Fishing: Insights for Management and Conservation","title-short":"Documented and Potential Biological Impacts of Recreational Fishing","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10641260600886455","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Lewin","given":"Wolf-Christian"},{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Mehner","given":"Thomas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",12]]}}},{"id":31851,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E45VHAHW"],"itemData":{"id":31851,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            This study evaluates the fishing pressure exerted by the most common recreational and professional, small-scale fishing practices on vulnerable target and bycatch species in coastal and offshore waters of the western Mediterranean. By combining multiple data sources, we assembled a unique dataset on catches at multiple sites in these areas by recreational (RF) and small-scale fisheries (SSF), covering the period from 1997 to 2015. Furthermore, a framework with which to identify the vulnerable species among all the species caught is provided; it is based on the IUCN Red List, international conventions for the protection of flora and fauna, the Habitats Directive and the intrinsic vulnerability index of marine fish. Overall, about a quarter of exploited species targeted by SSF and RF in coastal waters were vulnerable, making up nearly 50% of the total SSF catch and nearly 20% of the total recreational catch. In offshore waters, 100% of the RF and SSF catch was made up of vulnerable species. Among the species caught as bycatch in both areas by SSF and RF, there was a total of 27 vulnerable vertebrate species, which included birds, cetaceans, elasmobranchs and sea turtles. Our results highlight the need to differentiate between different fishing methods or gears when studying the fishing impacts on vulnerable species. The results also indicate that, although RF and SSF are often considered to have a relatively low ecological impact, a range of different fishing methods are affecting vulnerable species in coastal or offshore waters in the western Mediterranean Sea, be they targeted or taken unintentionally as bycatch.","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.1093/icesjms/fsz071","ISSN":"1054-3139, 1095-9289","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"2255-2264","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recreational and small-scale fisheries may pose a threat to vulnerable species in coastal and offshore waters of the western Mediterranean","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/icesjms/article/77/6/2255/5486184","volume":"77","author":[{"family":"Lloret","given":"J"},{"family":"Biton-Porsmoguer","given":"S"},{"family":"Carreño","given":"A"},{"family":"Di Franco","given":"A"},{"family":"Sahyoun","given":"R"},{"family":"Melià","given":"P"},{"family":"Claudet","given":"J"},{"family":"Sève","given":"C"},{"family":"Ligas","given":"A"},{"family":"Belharet","given":"M"},{"family":"Calò","given":"A"},{"family":"Carbonara","given":"P"},{"family":"Coll","given":"M"},{"family":"Corrales","given":"X"},{"family":"Lembo","given":"G"},{"family":"Sartor","given":"P"},{"family":"Bitetto","given":"I"},{"family":"Vilas","given":"D"},{"family":"Piroddi","given":"C"},{"family":"Prato","given":"G"},{"family":"Charbonnel","given":"E"},{"family":"Bretton","given":"O"},{"family":"Hartmann","given":"V"},{"family":"Prats","given":"L"},{"family":"Font","given":"T"}],"editor":[{"family":"Browman","given":"Howard"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,1]]}}},{"id":31852,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y4ALXW8V"],"itemData":{"id":31852,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosphere","DOI":"10.1002/ecs2.1601","ISSN":"2150-8925, 2150-8925","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosphere","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Assessing interacting impacts of artisanal and recreational fisheries in a small Marine Protected Area (Portofino, NW Mediterranean Sea)","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ecs2.1601","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Prato","given":"Giulia"},{"family":"Barrier","given":"Celine"},{"family":"Francour","given":"Patrice"},{"family":"Cappanera","given":"Valentina"},{"family":"Markantonatou","given":"Vasiliki"},{"family":"Guidetti","given":"Paolo"},{"family":"Mangialajo","given":"Luisa"},{"family":"Cattaneo?Vietti","given":"Riccardo"},{"family":"Gascuel","given":"Didier"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and community changes (e.g., successful invasion by non-native species) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2bo6lo3ie","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2012b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2012b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31864,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CMETXVK5"],"itemData":{"id":31864,"type":"report","abstract":"Recreational fishing is defined as fishing of aquatic animals (mainly fish) that do not constitute the individual's primary resource to meet basic nutritional needs and are not generally sold or otherwise traded on export, domestic or black markets. Recreational fishing constitutes the dominant use of wild fish stocks in all freshwaters of industrialized countries, and it is prominent in many coastal ecosystems. The importance of recreational fisheries is increasing rapidly in many transitional economies. The present Technical Guidelines for Responsible Fisheries are focused on recreational fisheries and describe strategies to promote environmentally sustainable and socially responsible management of such fisheries. To this end, the document details policy, management and behavioural recommendations for sustainable recreational fisheries that are an increasingly important component of global fisheries. Specifically, the Guidelines translate the relevant provisions of the FAO Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries into specific advice for recreational fisheries. The concept of aquatic stewardship is introduced as an overarching ethical framework needed to achieve ecologically sustainable recreational fisheries on a global scale. Within this normative mindset, the adaptive management philosophy based on quantifiable and transparent objectives and continuous learning and feedback loops is proposed along with the acknowledgement of principles such as the ecosystem approach and the precautionary approach. Detailed sections on policy and institutional frameworks (tailored towards policy-makers), recreational fisheries management actions and strategies (tailored towards fisheries managers), recreational fisheries practices (tailored towards individual recreational fishers) and recreational fisheries research (tailored to researchers and managers) provide tangible advice for responsible recreational fisheries. The special considerations necessary for recreational fisheries in developing countries and economies in transition are acknowledged. Adherence to the guidelines and recommendations presented in the present document will enable policy-makers, managers and the entire recreational fisheries sector to orient recreational fisheries towards maintaining or achieving sustainability","call-number":"SH331 .A74 2012","event-place":"Rome","note":"OCLC: ocn812531706","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Recreational fisheries","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Recreational fishers can be regarded as keystone top-predators ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a254pctopem","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hilborn & Walters, 1992)","plainCitation":"(Hilborn & Walters, 1992)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31819,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/89JUWZS2"],"itemData":{"id":31819,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Quantitative Fisheries Stock Assessment","event-place":"Boston, MA","ISBN":"978-1-4020-1845-9","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-1-4615-3598-0_7","page":"241-296","publisher":"Springer US","publisher-place":"Boston, MA","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Stock and Recruitment","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-1-4615-3598-0_7","container-author":[{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Walters","given":"Carl J."}],"author":[{"family":"Hilborn","given":"Ray"},{"family":"Walters","given":"Carl J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} with increasing efficiency, as knowledge (techniques, areas, seasons, species, etc.) is becoming more accessible and technology (GPS, sounders, braided lines, etc.) more affordable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2nibm06tjv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. P. Griffiths, 2010)","plainCitation":"(S. P. Griffiths, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31824,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NQVAGJMG"],"itemData":{"id":31824,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-2979.2010.00354.x","ISSN":"14672960, 14672979","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"220-228","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Following the chain to elusive anglers: Following the chain to elusive anglers","title-short":"Following the chain to elusive anglers","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1467-2979.2010.00354.x","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Shane P."},{"family":"Pollock","given":"Kenneth H."},{"family":"Lyle","given":"Jeremy M."},{"family":"Pepperell","given":"Julian G."},{"family":"Tonks","given":"Mark L."},{"family":"Sawynok","given":"William"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",3,10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Hence, recreational fisheries are now widely recognized as a significant component of marine capture fisheries and a potentially significant contributor to fish declines along with the commercial fleets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ana2oinb7b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Agius Darmanin & Vella, 2019; Robert Arlinghaus & Cooke, 2009; Herfaut, Levrel, Th\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}baud, & V\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}ron, 2013; Pawson, Glenn, & Padda, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Agius Darmanin & Vella, 2019; Robert Arlinghaus & Cooke, 2009; Herfaut, Levrel, Thébaud, & Véron, 2013; Pawson, Glenn, & Padda, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31821,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A2Z8NQ3V"],"itemData":{"id":31821,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2019.00390","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","page":"390","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"First Central Mediterranean Scientific Field Study on Recreational Fishing Targeting the Ecosystem Approach to Sustainability","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fmars.2019.00390/full","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Agius Darmanin","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Vella","given":"Adriana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,25]]}}},{"id":31866,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PE2ELQJV"],"itemData":{"id":31866,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Recreational Hunting, Conservation and Rural Livelihoods","event-place":"Oxford, UK","ISBN":"978-1-4443-0317-9","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1002/9781444303179.ch3","page":"39-58","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","publisher-place":"Oxford, UK","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recreational Fisheries: Socioeconomic Importance, Conservation Issues and Management Challenges","title-short":"Recreational Fisheries","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/9781444303179.ch3","editor":[{"family":"Dickson","given":"Barney"},{"family":"Hutton","given":"Jon"},{"family":"Adams","given":"William M."}],"author":[{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",1,30]]}}},{"id":31822,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WDHLWY7C"],"itemData":{"id":31822,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2013.02.026","ISSN":"09645691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"121-131","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The nationwide assessment of marine recreational fishing: A French example","title-short":"The nationwide assessment of marine recreational fishing","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0964569113000586","volume":"78","author":[{"family":"Herfaut","given":"Johanna"},{"family":"Levrel","given":"Harold"},{"family":"Thébaud","given":"Olivier"},{"family":"Véron","given":"Gérard"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",6]]}}},{"id":31823,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IVYK88DA"],"itemData":{"id":31823,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2007.07.001","ISSN":"0308597X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"339-350","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The definition of marine recreational fishing in Europe","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X07000802","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Pawson","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Glenn","given":"H."},{"family":"Padda","given":"G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. To achieve sustainable fisheries management, it appears essential to incorporate recreational fisheries stock assessments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a11hdg1g98l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gordoa, Dedeu, & Boada, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Gordoa, Dedeu, & Boada, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31830,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q9E4AHK8"],"itemData":{"id":31830,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2018.10.026","ISSN":"01657836","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Research","language":"en","page":"1-12","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recreational fishing in Spain: First national estimates of fisher population size, fishing activity and fisher social profile","title-short":"Recreational fishing in Spain","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0165783618302947","volume":"211","author":[{"family":"Gordoa","given":"Ana"},{"family":"Dedeu","given":"Arnau L."},{"family":"Boada","given":"Jordi"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      
3.3.1.5.4. Decorative and aesthetic

Some animal parts are used to make perfumes, mainly as a fixative substance that includes musk, ambergris, civet and castoreum. Of these four animal products, ambergris is jetsam coprolite which originates from the sperm whale ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2c54srrhug","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Macleod, Sinding, Olsen, Collins, & Rowland, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Macleod, Sinding, Olsen, Collins, & Rowland, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":992,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8RY2343U"],"itemData":{"id":992,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biology Letters","DOI":"ARTN 20190819 10.1098/rsbl.2019.0819","ISSN":"1744-9561","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Biol Letters","language":"English","title":"DNA preserved in jetsam whale ambergris","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Macleod","given":"R."},{"family":"Sinding","given":"M. H. S."},{"family":"Olsen","given":"M. T."},{"family":"Collins","given":"M. J."},{"family":"Rowland","given":"S. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",2,26]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It has been found rarely but this is in practice for centuries all over the world. It is difficult to estimate the sustainability of the ambergris gathering, as some samples have been present in the environment for about a thousand years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ah19ge44u","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rowland, Sutton, & Knowles, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Rowland, Sutton, & Knowles, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":991,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8FQJWYQH"],"itemData":{"id":991,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Natural Product Research","DOI":"10.1080/14786419.2018.1523163","ISSN":"1478-6419","issue":"21","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Prod Res","language":"English","page":"3134-3142","title":"The age of ambergris","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Rowland","given":"S. J."},{"family":"Sutton","given":"P. A."},{"family":"Knowles","given":"T. D. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",11,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	The rest of this section was written following the methods used for the systematic review described in 3.3.1.3 (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651).
      In Europe, only a fraction of the small-scale fisheries exploits aquatic animals for uses other than food. These organisms are usually benthic invertebrate species, which are not only fished for food and feed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a13kq0velrm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Duncan, 2016; Pita, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Duncan, 2016; Pita, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31535,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G25LWCTK"],"itemData":{"id":31535,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","collection-title":"Developments in Aquaculture and Fisheries Science","container-title":"Developments in Aquaculture and Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1016/B978-0-444-62710-0.00019-5","note":"DOI: 10.1016/B978-0-444-62710-0.00019-5","page":"781-858","title":"The European Scallop Fisheries for Pecten maximus, Aequipecten opercularis, Chlamys islandica, and Mimachlamys varia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84978427384&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-444-62710-0.00019-5&partnerID=40&md5=a029bcf90c5b4e83078f6f8a3c5e3d72","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Duncan","given":"P.F."},{"family":"Brand","given":"A.R."},{"family":"Strand","given":"Ø."},{"family":"Foucher","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":31787,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4LT7EXU3"],"itemData":{"id":31787,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2018.09.018","page":"208-224","title":"Socioecological changes in data-poor S-fisheries: A hidden shellfisheries crisis in Galicia (NW Spain)","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85054149091&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2018.09.018&partnerID=40&md5=bedbc67ea33abb4d2203c9dca2c8f17b","volume":"101","author":[{"family":"Pita","given":"P."},{"family":"Fernández-Márquez","given":"D."},{"family":"Antelo","given":"M."},{"family":"Macho","given":"G."},{"family":"Villasante","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but also for a limited number of other uses. Some Porifera are traditionally used and sold as sponges for baths, for instance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2o6ppr3mdj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fourt, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Fourt, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31807,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E4MG39TK"],"itemData":{"id":31807,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10113-020-01581-1","issue":"1","title":"Past and present of a Mediterranean small-scale fishery: the Greek sponge fishery—its resilience and sustainability","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85078476313&doi=10.1007%2fs10113-020-01581-1&partnerID=40&md5=3093b42cd59d219ab6e4b9d61cd168ee","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Fourt","given":"M."},{"family":"Faget","given":"D."},{"family":"Dailianis","given":"T."},{"family":"Koutsoubas","given":"D."},{"family":"Pérez","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The literature is unresolved on the sustainability of these practices. In recent decades, traditional gear was replaced by modern technologies, such as trawls ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DhkTqSQE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pita, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Pita, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31787,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4LT7EXU3"],"itemData":{"id":31787,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2018.09.018","page":"208-224","title":"Socioecological changes in data-poor S-fisheries: A hidden shellfisheries crisis in Galicia (NW Spain)","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85054149091&doi=10.1016%2fj.marpol.2018.09.018&partnerID=40&md5=bedbc67ea33abb4d2203c9dca2c8f17b","volume":"101","author":[{"family":"Pita","given":"P."},{"family":"Fernández-Márquez","given":"D."},{"family":"Antelo","given":"M."},{"family":"Macho","given":"G."},{"family":"Villasante","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which in combination with increased demand, led to overfishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a94tl13ued","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fourt, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Fourt, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31807,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E4MG39TK"],"itemData":{"id":31807,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10113-020-01581-1","issue":"1","title":"Past and present of a Mediterranean small-scale fishery: the Greek sponge fishery—its resilience and sustainability","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85078476313&doi=10.1007%2fs10113-020-01581-1&partnerID=40&md5=3093b42cd59d219ab6e4b9d61cd168ee","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Fourt","given":"M."},{"family":"Faget","given":"D."},{"family":"Dailianis","given":"T."},{"family":"Koutsoubas","given":"D."},{"family":"Pérez","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some stocks collapsed, although when this happened is unclear. However, more recently strong control of the catch along with other introduced management measures have resulted in the sustainability of this fishing practice being slowly rebuilt ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1oj2q4ba9m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fourt, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Fourt, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31807,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E4MG39TK"],"itemData":{"id":31807,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10113-020-01581-1","issue":"1","title":"Past and present of a Mediterranean small-scale fishery: the Greek sponge fishery—its resilience and sustainability","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85078476313&doi=10.1007%2fs10113-020-01581-1&partnerID=40&md5=3093b42cd59d219ab6e4b9d61cd168ee","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Fourt","given":"M."},{"family":"Faget","given":"D."},{"family":"Dailianis","given":"T."},{"family":"Koutsoubas","given":"D."},{"family":"Pérez","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, in most places the uncontrolled use of trawls is still a severe threat to the sustainability of megabenthic fauna, either for the exploited stocks or for other species of demersal fish, which are equally important for the European economy ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1g711uv722","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Duncan, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Duncan, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31535,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G25LWCTK"],"itemData":{"id":31535,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","collection-title":"Developments in Aquaculture and Fisheries Science","container-title":"Developments in Aquaculture and Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1016/B978-0-444-62710-0.00019-5","note":"DOI: 10.1016/B978-0-444-62710-0.00019-5","page":"781-858","title":"The European Scallop Fisheries for Pecten maximus, Aequipecten opercularis, Chlamys islandica, and Mimachlamys varia","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84978427384&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-444-62710-0.00019-5&partnerID=40&md5=a029bcf90c5b4e83078f6f8a3c5e3d72","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Duncan","given":"P.F."},{"family":"Brand","given":"A.R."},{"family":"Strand","given":"Ø."},{"family":"Foucher","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
 	In Latin America, few studies address other uses than food, including ornamental fish to aquarium trade, decorative (handcrafts) or medicinal uses, and often these alternative uses can be made of the same organisms, some of which are also used as food (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). None of the 14 studies that focused on these alternative uses addressed the sustainability of the practices. Some studies indicated partially sustainable uses of medicinal or decorative fish species on the Brazilian coast, which may occur at a local scale (low fishing pressure), but may sometimes include threatened species or be linked to trawling and by-catch ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Gj9KksFF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eduardo, 2020; Pinto, Mour\\\\uc0\\\\u227{}o, & Alves, 2015; Rosa, 2011; C. A. B. Santos & N\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}brea Alves, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Eduardo, 2020; Pinto, Mourão, & Alves, 2015; Rosa, 2011; C. A. B. Santos & Nóbrea Alves, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1012,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EGQNW4GE"],"itemData":{"id":1012,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.3278","title":"Biodiversity, ecology, fisheries, and use and trade of Tetraodontiformes fishes reveal their socio-ecological significance along the tropical Brazilian continental shelf","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85078737222&doi=10.1002%2faqc.3278&partnerID=40&md5=3669cbfecdeeab9f657558d415d3734c","author":[{"family":"Eduardo","given":"L.N."},{"family":"Bertrand","given":"A."},{"family":"Frédou","given":"T."},{"family":"Lira","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Lima","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"B.P."},{"family":"Menard","given":"F."},{"family":"Lucena-Frédou","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31813,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MUX3VXJ8"],"itemData":{"id":31813,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-015-0007-5","issue":"1","title":"Use of ichthyofauna by artisanal fishermen at two protected areas along the coast of Northeast Brazil","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84924916689&doi=10.1186%2fs13002-015-0007-5&partnerID=40&md5=b72fd009d2941c4ef02ac8e6c5690adc","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Pinto","given":"M.F."},{"family":"Mourão","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Alves","given":"R.R.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1002,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AU2DTERU"],"itemData":{"id":1002,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity and Conservation","page":"1951-1971","title":"Fisheries and trade of seahorses in Brazil: historical perspective, current trends, and future directions","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Rosa","given":"Ierecê L"},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"Tacyana PR"},{"family":"Osório","given":"Frederico M"},{"family":"Moraes","given":"Luiz E"},{"family":"Castro","given":"André LC"},{"family":"Barros","given":"Glaura ML"},{"family":"Alves","given":"Rômulo RN"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31814,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CXFQETMX"],"itemData":{"id":31814,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-015-0076-5","issue":"1","title":"Ethnoichthyology of the indigenous Truká people, Northeast Brazil","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84953219148&doi=10.1186%2fs13002-015-0076-5&partnerID=40&md5=7da5f5d72df4808dea7d531885ba7fc7","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Santos","given":"C.A.B."},{"family":"Nóbrea Alves","given":"R.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The medicinal or decorative use of parts of sharks (mostly by finning) and sawfish are regarded as unsustainable, leading to declines in the exploited species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8D2YHiq4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barbosa-Filho, 2019; Bonfil, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Barbosa-Filho, 2019; Bonfil, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34914,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2CKBEEQG"],"itemData":{"id":34914,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ethnobiology Letters","DOI":"10.14237/ebl.10.1.2019.1560","issue":"1","page":"97-103","title":"Historical shark meat consumption and trade trends in a global richness hotspot","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85077912499&doi=10.14237%2febl.10.1.2019.1560&partnerID=40&md5=049227e121a3f3f153e4fe59c99c5e13","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Barbosa-Filho","given":"M.L.V."},{"family":"Hauser-Davis","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Siciliano","given":"S."},{"family":"Dias","given":"T.L.P."},{"family":"Alves","given":"R.R.N."},{"family":"Costa-Neto","given":"E.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":999,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QY8VVVYS"],"itemData":{"id":999,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Endangered Species Research","DOI":"10.3354/esr00899","page":"213-228","title":"Tapping into local ecological knowledge to assess the former importance and current status of sawfishes in Mexico","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85054697288&doi=10.3354%2fesr00899&partnerID=40&md5=6ebb8037e2e8de61edee94e6eb7357f1","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Bonfil","given":"R."},{"family":"Ricaño-Soriano","given":"M."},{"family":"Mendoza-Vargas","given":"O.U."},{"family":"Méndez-Loeza","given":"I."},{"family":"Pérez-Jiménez","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Bolaño-Martínez","given":"N."},{"family":"Palacios-Barreto","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fisheries exploiting jellyfish mostly for food, but including many occasional uses as food for livestock or aquaculture, bait, medicine or aesthetic (collagen) have developed at different stages in several South American countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"porBNBPB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brotz, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brotz, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31519,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZT6AHU57"],"itemData":{"id":31519,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1007/s11160-016-9445-y","issue":"1","title":"Jellyfish fisheries in the Americas: origin, state of the art, and perspectives on new fishing grounds","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84988429381&doi=10.1007%2fs11160-016-9445-y&partnerID=40&md5=9db4717398ca57dd1b5242882d9ff5ff","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Brotz","given":"L."},{"family":"Schiariti","given":"A."},{"family":"López-Martínez","given":"J."},{"family":"Álvarez-Tello","given":"J."},{"family":"Peggy Hsieh","given":"Y.-H."},{"family":"Jones","given":"R.P."},{"family":"Quiñones","given":"J."},{"family":"Dong","given":"Z."},{"family":"Morandini","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Preciado","given":"M."},{"family":"Laaz","given":"E."},{"family":"Mianzan","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These fisheries may be considered as partially sustainable, or potentially sustainable, given limited data on landings, potential problems of bycatch and habitat damage (depending on fishing technique) and coastal pollution from jellyfish processing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XwxKiHgz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brotz, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brotz, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31519,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZT6AHU57"],"itemData":{"id":31519,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1007/s11160-016-9445-y","issue":"1","title":"Jellyfish fisheries in the Americas: origin, state of the art, and perspectives on new fishing grounds","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84988429381&doi=10.1007%2fs11160-016-9445-y&partnerID=40&md5=9db4717398ca57dd1b5242882d9ff5ff","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Brotz","given":"L."},{"family":"Schiariti","given":"A."},{"family":"López-Martínez","given":"J."},{"family":"Álvarez-Tello","given":"J."},{"family":"Peggy Hsieh","given":"Y.-H."},{"family":"Jones","given":"R.P."},{"family":"Quiñones","given":"J."},{"family":"Dong","given":"Z."},{"family":"Morandini","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Preciado","given":"M."},{"family":"Laaz","given":"E."},{"family":"Mianzan","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
 	In North America, no uses other than food and recreation were observed among the reviewed studies in this region (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651).
 	In Asia-Pacific, only a few studies (8) from the reviewed coastal and inland small-scale fisheries mention uses other than food, such as ornamental or decorative (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). The use of marine shellfish shells as ornaments and handicrafts in Fiji is likely partially sustainable due to a recovery of exploited shellfish populations as a result of co-management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0Mbp1tsP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Thaman, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Thaman, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":380,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3RFSXGW5"],"itemData":{"id":380,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","DOI":"10.2993/0278-0771-37.3.494","issue":"3","page":"494-513","title":"The Recovery of a Tropical Marine Mollusk Fishery: A Transdisciplinary Community-Based Approach in Navakavu, Fiji","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85029575009&doi=10.2993%2f0278-0771-37.3.494&partnerID=40&md5=5b5163569be33036d1fc3cfd860050af","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Thaman","given":"B."},{"family":"Thaman","given":"R.R."},{"family":"Balawa","given":"A."},{"family":"Veitayaki","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

3.3.1.5.5. Ceremony and cultural expression

For many small-scale fishing societies, successful fishing does not depend only on technical procedures, but rather on religious and cultural rituals and practices. Good fishing implies that propitiatory practices, such as fasting, specific diets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a3818fcfgi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Teiwaki, 1988)","plainCitation":"(Teiwaki, 1988)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31844,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UJBW6XIM"],"itemData":{"id":31844,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Micronesian politics","page":"1-37","title":"Kiribati: Nation of water","author":[{"family":"Teiwaki","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1988"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or sex avoidance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2nh93ble1r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Deb, Haque, & Thompson, 2015; Hoeppe, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Deb, Haque, & Thompson, 2015; Hoeppe, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31841,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FF4ZM8KK"],"itemData":{"id":31841,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Gender, Place & Culture","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1080/0966369X.2013.855626","issue":"3","page":"305-324","title":"‘Man can’t give birth, woman can't fish’: gender dynamics in the small-scale fisheries of Bangladesh","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/0966369X.2013.855626","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Deb","given":"A.K."},{"family":"Haque","given":"C.E."},{"family":"Thompson","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":31845,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QQI44EBQ"],"itemData":{"id":31845,"type":"book","collection-title":"Studies in environmental anthropology and ethnobiology","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-1-84545-015-1","number-of-pages":"232","publisher":"Berghahn Books","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Conversations on the beach. Fishermen’s knowledge, metaphor and environmental change in South India","author":[{"family":"Hoeppe","given":"Gotz"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, accompany the various stages of the technical process, including the manufacture of canoes used in fishing practice ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2nr22vjv7v","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Foale, Cohen, Januchowski\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Hartley, Wenger, & Macintyre, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Foale, Cohen, Januchowski?Hartley, Wenger, & Macintyre, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31837,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V2CPKXMC"],"itemData":{"id":31837,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and fisheries","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-2979.2010.00395.x","issue":"4","page":"357-369","title":"Tenure and taboos: origins and implications for fisheries in the Pacific","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/j.1467-2979.2010.00395.x","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Foale","given":"S."},{"family":"Cohen","given":"P."},{"family":"Januchowski?Hartley","given":"S."},{"family":"Wenger","given":"A."},{"family":"Macintyre","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Rituals may also be led by shamans ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1adrdh5djn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ivanoff, 1992; Laugrand, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Ivanoff, 1992; Laugrand, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31828,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AZU6JMB6"],"itemData":{"id":31828,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bulletin de l'École française d'Extrême-Orient","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.3406/befeo.1992.1874","ISSN":"0336-1519 - online 1760-737X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"BEFEO","page":"103-130","title":"Équilibre paradoxal: sédentarité et sacralité chez les nomades marins moken","URL":"https://www.persee.fr/docAsPDF/befeo_0336-1519_1992_num_79_2_1874.pdf","volume":"79","author":[{"family":"Ivanoff","given":"Jacques"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}},{"id":31835,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CZ2BU72M"],"itemData":{"id":31835,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"La lettre du Collège de France","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.4000/annuaire-cdf.12036","page":"986-988","title":"L’ontologie sur la glace. Les Inuit de l’Arctique central canadien et leurs animaux","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Laugrand","given":"Frédéric"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or marabouts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ar4ad97eqo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Artaud, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Artaud, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31831,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RU2T7HGS"],"itemData":{"id":31831,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Senses and Society","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1080/17458927.2016.1195109","ISSN":"1745-8935","issue":"3","page":"262-274","title":"Spelling out Sensations: Reflections on the ways in which the Natural Environment can infiltrate Meaning-Making","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17458927.2016.1195109","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Artaud","given":"Hélène"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thus, many taboos are meant to favour 'luck' or prevent the breach of rules and ward off the ontological imbalances resulting from a non-respect of the rules ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a563pdotti","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Artaud, 2016, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Artaud, 2016, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31831,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RU2T7HGS"],"itemData":{"id":31831,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Senses and Society","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1080/17458927.2016.1195109","ISSN":"1745-8935","issue":"3","page":"262-274","title":"Spelling out Sensations: Reflections on the ways in which the Natural Environment can infiltrate Meaning-Making","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17458927.2016.1195109","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Artaud","given":"Hélène"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":31839,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TIXJ3UXB"],"itemData":{"id":31839,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Billebaude","page":"48-56","title":"Piéger la rencontre","volume":"16 - L'art du leurre","author":[{"family":"Artaud","given":"Hélène"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In fishing societies these rituals play an important part because marine species are perceived as 'partners' ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2jm50tbbkt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Astuti, 1995; Bataille-Benguigui, 1981; D\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Arcy, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Astuti, 1995; Bataille-Benguigui, 1981; D’Arcy, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33455,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/86XT2PT5"],"itemData":{"id":33455,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"American Ethnologist","issue":"3","page":"464-482","title":"\\"The Vezo are not a kind of people”: identity, difference, and “ethnicity” among a fishing people of western Madagascar","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/645967","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Astuti","given":"Rita"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}},{"id":31842,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H7WWT6FV"],"itemData":{"id":31842,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal de la Société des océanistes","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.3406/jso.1981.3064","issue":"72-73","page":"239-250","title":"Bataille-Benguigui M-C. (1981) « La capture au requin du nœud coulant aux îles Tonga : persistance et changements dans l'observation des interdits », Journal de la Société des océanistes, n°72-73, tome 37, 1981. La pêche traditionnelle en Océanie. pp. 239-250.","URL":"https://www.persee.fr/doc/jso_0300-953x_1981_num_37_72_3064","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Bataille-Benguigui","given":"Marie-Claire"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1981"]]}}},{"id":31848,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4Q4ZTZ4M"],"itemData":{"id":31848,"type":"book","event-place":"Honolulu","ISBN":"978-0-8248-3297-1","number-of-pages":"312","publisher":"University of Hawaii Press","publisher-place":"Honolulu","title":"The people of the sea: environment, identity, and history in Oceania.","author":[{"family":"D'Arcy","given":"Paul"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} rather than simply as 'prey' or 'resources'. Bonds of seduction or alliances ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aebt84m3jq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Robert Earle Johannes, 1981; Zerner, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Robert Earle Johannes, 1981; Zerner, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32714,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H9EVID4H"],"itemData":{"id":32714,"type":"book","event-place":"Berkeley/Los Angeles/London","ISBN":"0-520-03929-7","language":"English","number-of-pages":"245","publisher":"University of California Press","publisher-place":"Berkeley/Los Angeles/London","title":"Words of the lagoon: fishing and marine lore in the Palau district of Micronesia","URL":"https://books.google.fr/books?id=TloVDfV7QLoC&pg=PR3&hl=fr&source=gbs_selected_pages&cad=3#v=onepage&q&f=false","author":[{"family":"Johannes","given":"Robert Earle"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1981"]]}}},{"id":31850,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AKIUK2T9"],"itemData":{"id":31850,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Culture and the question of rights. Forests, coasts, and seas in Southeast Asia","edition":"Zerner C. (ed)","event-place":"Durham & London","ISBN":"978-0-8223-2802-5","page":"56-108","publisher":"Duke University Press","publisher-place":"Durham & London","title":"Sounding the Makassar Strait: the poetics and politics of an Indonesian marine environment","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1515/9780822383819-005","author":[{"family":"Zerner","given":"Charles"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, fraternity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2nhcdd8tg2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grimble, 1989; Lewis, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Grimble, 1989; Lewis, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31846,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZWZ3VKAA"],"itemData":{"id":31846,"type":"book","collection-title":"Pacific Islands Monograph Series","edition":"Maude H.E. (ed)","event-place":"Honolulu","ISBN":"978-0-8248-8223-5","number-of-pages":"392","publisher":"University of Hawaii Press","publisher-place":"Honolulu","title":"Writings on the Atoll Culture of the Gilbert Islands","URL":"https://core.ac.uk/download/pdf/211329399.pdf","author":[{"family":"Grimble","given":"Arthur F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1989"]]}}},{"id":31849,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6WMUUGVF"],"itemData":{"id":31849,"type":"book","event-place":"Honolulu","ISBN":"978-0-8248-1582-0","number-of-pages":"464","publisher":"University of Hawaii Press","publisher-place":"Honolulu","title":"We, the Navigators: The Ancient Art of Landfinding in the Pacific","author":[{"family":"Lewis","given":"David"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, co-substantiality ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a11a4gi80pp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Laugrand, 2015; N. Peterson & Rigsby, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Laugrand, 2015; N. Peterson & Rigsby, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31835,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CZ2BU72M"],"itemData":{"id":31835,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"La lettre du Collège de France","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.4000/annuaire-cdf.12036","page":"986-988","title":"L’ontologie sur la glace. Les Inuit de l’Arctique central canadien et leurs animaux","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Laugrand","given":"Frédéric"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":31847,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PCFBTQ28"],"itemData":{"id":31847,"type":"book","event-place":"Sydney","ISBN":"978-1-74332-389-2","number-of-pages":"303","publisher":"Sydney University Press","publisher-place":"Sydney","title":"Customary marine tenure in Australia","URL":"https://dx.doi.org/10.30722/sup.9781743323892","author":[{"family":"Peterson","given":"Nicolas"},{"family":"Rigsby","given":"Bruce"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or consanguinity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1d2btp2ibt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ivanoff, 1992)","plainCitation":"(Ivanoff, 1992)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31828,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AZU6JMB6"],"itemData":{"id":31828,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bulletin de l'École française d'Extrême-Orient","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.3406/befeo.1992.1874","ISSN":"0336-1519 - online 1760-737X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"BEFEO","page":"103-130","title":"Équilibre paradoxal: sédentarité et sacralité chez les nomades marins moken","URL":"https://www.persee.fr/docAsPDF/befeo_0336-1519_1992_num_79_2_1874.pdf","volume":"79","author":[{"family":"Ivanoff","given":"Jacques"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} unite human and aquatic communities. Beyond these relationships, fishing rituals aim at strengthening the ties between people, social groups or clans. For instance, for the Tao people, fishing flying fish (Family Exocoetidae) is an opportunity to renew a set of cultural and identity principles and values ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1gvcobl10l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berger, 2019; Fan, 2019; Gaffric, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Berger, 2019; Fan, 2019; Gaffric, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31826,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K3C7GUAZ"],"itemData":{"id":31826,"type":"book","edition":"Berger, David Nathaniel","event-place":"Copenhagen, Denmark","ISBN":"978-87-92786-93-7","language":"English","number-of-pages":"680","publisher":"The International Work Group for Indigenous Affairs (IWGIA)","publisher-place":"Copenhagen, Denmark","title":"The Indigenous World 2019","URL":"https://www.iwgia.org/en/documents-and-publications/documents/publications-pdfs/english-publications/4-the-indigenous-world-2019/file.html","author":[{"family":"Berger","given":"David Nathaniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":31829,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q6XRI3UI"],"itemData":{"id":31829,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Planning and Management","DOI":"10.1080/09640568.2017.1418303","ISSN":"Print ISSN: 0964-0568 Online ISSN: 1360-0559","issue":"2","page":"327-341","title":"Risk discourses and governance of high-level radioactive waste storage in Taiwan","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/09640568.2017.1418303","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Fan","given":"Mei-Fang"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":31827,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X2JUA3FA"],"itemData":{"id":31827,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"TRANS - Revue de littérature générale et comparée","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.4000/trans.867","title":"Do Waves Have Memories? Human and Ocean Issues in Taiwan Indigenous Writer Syaman Rapongan’s Writing","URL":"https://journals.openedition.org/trans/pdf/867","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Gaffric","given":"Gwennaël"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The same is true of whale hunting, which for several indigenous communities constitutes a means of regulating group relations or asserting their singularity within a State (see section 3.3.1.4.6) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2qpjkptfa1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Adamson, 2012; Deutsch, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Adamson, 2012; Deutsch, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31832,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BNF8SD4T"],"itemData":{"id":31832,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Revista Canaria de Estudios Ingleses","ISSN":"0211-5913","page":"29-45","title":"Whale as cosmos: Multi-species ethnography and contemporary indigenous cosmopolitics","URL":"https://riull.ull.es/xmlui/bitstream/handle/915/12267/RCEI_64_%28%202012%29_03.pdf?sequence=1","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Adamson","given":"Joni"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":31840,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EHICA2ID"],"itemData":{"id":31840,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Sociology","DOI":"10.1080/23251042.2017.1298183","issue":"3","page":"186-196","title":"The struggle of a marginalized community for ethnic renewal: the whale hunters of Neah Bay","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Deutsch","given":"Sierra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is also the case for salmon fishing among the Ainu ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1i9luqpepg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Iwasaki-Goodman & Nomoto, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Iwasaki-Goodman & Nomoto, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31836,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RUA6IGWZ"],"itemData":{"id":31836,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Senri ethnological studies","DOI":"http://doi.org/10.15021/00002785","page":"27-46","title":"Revitalizing the relationship between Ainu and salmon: Salmon rituals in the present.","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Iwasaki-Goodman","given":"Masami"},{"family":"Nomoto","given":"Masahiro"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Items from aquatic species, such as sea-shells, are used in some rituals, for instance in the candomblé, an Afro-Brazilian religion ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a24t5c7c492","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Neto, Voeks, Dias, & Alves, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Neto, Voeks, Dias, & Alves, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31834,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D9I3G9D4"],"itemData":{"id":31834,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/1746-4269-8-10","issue":"article n° 10","page":"1-10","title":"Mollusks of Candomblé: symbolic and ritualistic importance","URL":"http://www.ethnobiomed.com/content/8/1/10","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Neto","given":"N.A.L."},{"family":"Voeks","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Dias","given":"T.L."},{"family":"Alves","given":"R.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

3.3.1.6.  “Non-lethal” fishing practices and uses

3.3.1.6.1. Catch and release recreational fishing

Recreational fishing can involve a variety of gear types but catch-and-release fishing is most typically focused on fish caught by hook and line ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tM0tKhFx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2012b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2012b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31864,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CMETXVK5"],"itemData":{"id":31864,"type":"report","abstract":"Recreational fishing is defined as fishing of aquatic animals (mainly fish) that do not constitute the individual's primary resource to meet basic nutritional needs and are not generally sold or otherwise traded on export, domestic or black markets. Recreational fishing constitutes the dominant use of wild fish stocks in all freshwaters of industrialized countries, and it is prominent in many coastal ecosystems. The importance of recreational fisheries is increasing rapidly in many transitional economies. The present Technical Guidelines for Responsible Fisheries are focused on recreational fisheries and describe strategies to promote environmentally sustainable and socially responsible management of such fisheries. To this end, the document details policy, management and behavioural recommendations for sustainable recreational fisheries that are an increasingly important component of global fisheries. Specifically, the Guidelines translate the relevant provisions of the FAO Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries into specific advice for recreational fisheries. The concept of aquatic stewardship is introduced as an overarching ethical framework needed to achieve ecologically sustainable recreational fisheries on a global scale. Within this normative mindset, the adaptive management philosophy based on quantifiable and transparent objectives and continuous learning and feedback loops is proposed along with the acknowledgement of principles such as the ecosystem approach and the precautionary approach. Detailed sections on policy and institutional frameworks (tailored towards policy-makers), recreational fisheries management actions and strategies (tailored towards fisheries managers), recreational fisheries practices (tailored towards individual recreational fishers) and recreational fisheries research (tailored to researchers and managers) provide tangible advice for responsible recreational fisheries. The special considerations necessary for recreational fisheries in developing countries and economies in transition are acknowledged. Adherence to the guidelines and recommendations presented in the present document will enable policy-makers, managers and the entire recreational fisheries sector to orient recreational fisheries towards maintaining or achieving sustainability","call-number":"SH331 .A74 2012","event-place":"Rome","note":"OCLC: ocn812531706","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Recreational fisheries","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Therefore, this discussion is focused on angled fish. 
      With respect to recreational catch-and-release fishing, it is difficult to disentangle the socio-economic benefits of harvest versus release-oriented recreational fishing, which collectively generates over 100 billion United States dollars annually while creating opportunities for anglers to connect with nature and spend time with friends and family ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OVyjqkeD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Robert Arlinghaus & Cooke, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Robert Arlinghaus & Cooke, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31866,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PE2ELQJV"],"itemData":{"id":31866,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Recreational Hunting, Conservation and Rural Livelihoods","event-place":"Oxford, UK","ISBN":"978-1-4443-0317-9","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1002/9781444303179.ch3","page":"39-58","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","publisher-place":"Oxford, UK","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recreational Fisheries: Socioeconomic Importance, Conservation Issues and Management Challenges","title-short":"Recreational Fisheries","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/9781444303179.ch3","editor":[{"family":"Dickson","given":"Barney"},{"family":"Hutton","given":"Jon"},{"family":"Adams","given":"William M."}],"author":[{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",1,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Recreational fisheries certainly can and do involve harvest for personal consumption ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qfjQMvG7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Steven J Cooke, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Steven J Cooke, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":271,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9QRPZAS5"],"itemData":{"id":271,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12246","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"201-224","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The nexus of fun and nutrition: Recreational fishing is also about food","title-short":"The nexus of fun and nutrition","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/faf.12246","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J"},{"family":"Twardek","given":"William M"},{"family":"Lennox","given":"Robert J"},{"family":"Zolderdo","given":"Aaron J"},{"family":"Bower","given":"Shannon D"},{"family":"Gutowsky","given":"Lee F G"},{"family":"Danylchuk","given":"Andy J"},{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Beard","given":"Douglas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but harvest rates vary markedly among regions, species, and angler typologies. To emphasize that variation, recreational harvest rates of species like muskellunge (Esox masquinongy) and bonefish (Albula spp) are around 1% while species like walleye (Sander vitreus) and Atlantic cod (Gadus morhua) have harvest rates that typically exceed 60% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1fIkCCrf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Robert Arlinghaus, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Robert Arlinghaus, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":273,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YSICL3QA"],"itemData":{"id":273,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1080/10641260601149432","ISSN":"1064-1262, 1547-6553","issue":"1-2","journalAbbreviation":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","language":"en","page":"75-167","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Understanding the Complexity of Catch-and-Release in Recreational Fishing: An Integrative Synthesis of Global Knowledge from Historical, Ethical, Social, and Biological Perspectives","title-short":"Understanding the Complexity of Catch-and-Release in Recreational Fishing","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10641260601149432","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."},{"family":"Lyman","given":"Jon"},{"family":"Policansky","given":"David"},{"family":"Schwab","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Suski","given":"Cory"},{"family":"Sutton","given":"Stephen G."},{"family":"Thorstad","given":"Eva B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",4,6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  In some cases, release of fish is dictated by regulations (e.g., closed seasons, bag limits, size limits) but it can also be voluntary. Where there are long term trend data available, there is evidence that fish release rates have crept up slowly over time ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dYDIzqpn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brownscombe, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Brownscombe, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":980,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MIKF38JA"],"itemData":{"id":980,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries","DOI":"10.1080/03632415.2014.915811","ISSN":"0363-2415, 1548-8446","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries","language":"en","page":"251-260","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Canadian Recreational Fisheries: 35 Years of Social, Biological, and Economic Dynamics from a National Survey","title-short":"Canadian Recreational Fisheries","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1080/03632415.2014.915811","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Brownscombe","given":"Jacob W."},{"family":"Bower","given":"Shannon D."},{"family":"Bowden","given":"William"},{"family":"Nowell","given":"Liane"},{"family":"Midwood","given":"Jonathan D."},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Neville"},{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",6,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      The release of angled fish requires proper handling and not all fish survive ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yBqaxIo3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. J. Cooke & Schramm, 2007)","plainCitation":"(S. J. Cooke & Schramm, 2007)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":977,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JW5RRH5K"],"itemData":{"id":977,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Management and Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2400.2007.00527.x","ISSN":"0969-997X, 1365-2400","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Manage","language":"en","page":"73-79","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Catch-and-release science and its application to conservation and management of recreational fisheries","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1365-2400.2007.00527.x","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Cooke","given":"S. J."},{"family":"Schramm","given":"H. L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. From a sustainability perspective, it is irrelevant whether fishing mortality arises from harvest (i.e., from an extractive fishery) or from release mortality (i.e., in a non-extractive fishery). Catch and release mortality rates are highly variable and can range from near total mortality to near total survival (recognizing that zero mortality is never attainable).  Several syntheses suggest that the bulk of recreational fisheries exhibit release mortality rates that are less than 10% (ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Tfj4mT7l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arlinghaus, 2007; Bartholomew & Bohnsack, 2005; Muoneke & Childress, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Arlinghaus, 2007; Bartholomew & Bohnsack, 2005; Muoneke & Childress, 1994)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":273,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YSICL3QA"],"itemData":{"id":273,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1080/10641260601149432","ISSN":"1064-1262, 1547-6553","issue":"1-2","journalAbbreviation":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","language":"en","page":"75-167","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Understanding the Complexity of Catch-and-Release in Recreational Fishing: An Integrative Synthesis of Global Knowledge from Historical, Ethical, Social, and Biological Perspectives","title-short":"Understanding the Complexity of Catch-and-Release in Recreational Fishing","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10641260601149432","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."},{"family":"Lyman","given":"Jon"},{"family":"Policansky","given":"David"},{"family":"Schwab","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Suski","given":"Cory"},{"family":"Sutton","given":"Stephen G."},{"family":"Thorstad","given":"Eva B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",4,6]]}}},{"id":272,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8LMBNLF6"],"itemData":{"id":272,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1007/s11160-005-2175-1","ISSN":"0960-3166, 1573-5184","issue":"1-2","journalAbbreviation":"Rev Fish Biol Fisheries","language":"en","page":"129-154","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A Review of Catch-and-Release Angling Mortality with Implications for No-take Reserves","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11160-005-2175-1","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Bartholomew","given":"Aaron"},{"family":"Bohnsack","given":"James A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",2]]}}},{"id":973,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8I966MYJ"],"itemData":{"id":973,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1080/10641269409388555","ISSN":"1064-1262, 1547-6553","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","language":"en","page":"123-156","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Hooking mortality: A review for recreational fisheries","title-short":"Hooking mortality","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10641269409388555","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Muoneke","given":"Maurice I."},{"family":"Childress","given":"W. Michael"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although mortality rates are informative, alone they provide little information on the population-level consequences of release mortality ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iYfbcyZn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kerns, Allen, & Harris, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Kerns, Allen, & Harris, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":978,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/II47PUEB"],"itemData":{"id":978,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries","DOI":"10.1080/03632415.2012.731878","ISSN":"0363-2415, 1548-8446","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries","language":"en","page":"502-503","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Importance of Assessing Population-Level Impact of Catch-and-Release Mortality","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1080/03632415.2012.731878","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Kerns","given":"Janice A."},{"family":"Allen","given":"Micheal S."},{"family":"Harris","given":"Julianne E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",12,10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Information on fishing effort, life history characteristics, population status, and the role of other fisheries practices dictate whether catch and release mortality threatens the sustainability of fish populations. Mortality arising from catch and release is often cryptic and has been implicated in fisheries collapse ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6C9yrSWX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Post, 2002; D. M. Schroeder & Love, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Post, 2002; D. M. Schroeder & Love, 2002)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":975,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IRSRA9AW"],"itemData":{"id":975,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries","DOI":"10.1577/1548-8446(2002)027<0006:CRF>2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0363-2415","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"null","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"6-17","title":"Canada's Recreational Fisheries: The Invisible Collapse?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1577/1548-8446(2002)027<0006:CRF>2.0.CO;2","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Post","given":"John R."},{"family":"Sullivan","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Cox","given":"Sean"},{"family":"Lester","given":"Nigel P."},{"family":"Walters","given":"Carl J."},{"family":"Parkinson","given":"Eric A."},{"family":"Paul","given":"Andrew J."},{"family":"Jackson","given":"Leyland"},{"family":"Shuter","given":"Brian J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002",1,1]]}}},{"id":972,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/52VW7MYY"],"itemData":{"id":972,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"California Cooperative Oceanic Fisheries Investigations Report","note":"publisher: California Cooperative Oceanic Fisheries Investigations","page":"182–190","title":"Recreational fishing and marine fish populations in California","URL":"https://lovelab.msi.ucsb.edu/Schroeder_Love2002.pdf","author":[{"family":"Schroeder","given":"Donna M"},{"family":"Love","given":"Milton S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are many factors that determine whether an individual fish will survive a catch and release event. The single biggest driver of mortality is anatomical hooking location with fish hooked in the jaw region having comparatively low mortality relative to fish hooked more deeply in areas such as the gills or esophagus ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hQJSrVhC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Robert Arlinghaus, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Robert Arlinghaus, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":273,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YSICL3QA"],"itemData":{"id":273,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1080/10641260601149432","ISSN":"1064-1262, 1547-6553","issue":"1-2","journalAbbreviation":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","language":"en","page":"75-167","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Understanding the Complexity of Catch-and-Release in Recreational Fishing: An Integrative Synthesis of Global Knowledge from Historical, Ethical, Social, and Biological Perspectives","title-short":"Understanding the Complexity of Catch-and-Release in Recreational Fishing","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10641260601149432","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."},{"family":"Lyman","given":"Jon"},{"family":"Policansky","given":"David"},{"family":"Schwab","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Suski","given":"Cory"},{"family":"Sutton","given":"Stephen G."},{"family":"Thorstad","given":"Eva B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",4,6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      Recreational catch and release fishing can have consequences for aquatic and coastal habitats. Issues include tackle loss (e.g., lead sinkers, hooks, line), littering, trampling of shoreline vegetation and in-water habitats (e.g., coral, gravel spawning sites), erosion, noise pollution, and hydrocarbon release from boats, and accidental or intentional release of exotic species (e.g., bait bucket transfers, stocking), among others (reviewed in ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c5Cqet9l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Steven J. Cooke & Cowx, 2006; Lewin, 2006; P. McPhee, 2002; Venohr, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Steven J. Cooke & Cowx, 2006; Lewin, 2006; P. McPhee, 2002; Venohr, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":976,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/22HB6B86"],"itemData":{"id":976,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2005.09.019","ISSN":"00063207","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"93-108","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Contrasting recreational and commercial fishing: Searching for common issues to promote unified conservation of fisheries resources and aquatic environments","title-short":"Contrasting recreational and commercial fishing","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320705003800","volume":"128","author":[{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."},{"family":"Cowx","given":"Ian G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",2]]}}},{"id":31865,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A5C9385G"],"itemData":{"id":31865,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1080/10641260600886455","ISSN":"1064-1262, 1547-6553","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","language":"en","page":"305-367","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Documented and Potential Biological Impacts of Recreational Fishing: Insights for Management and Conservation","title-short":"Documented and Potential Biological Impacts of Recreational Fishing","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10641260600886455","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Lewin","given":"Wolf-Christian"},{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Mehner","given":"Thomas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",12]]}}},{"id":974,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XCGIJ6BU"],"itemData":{"id":974,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Recreational fishing is a growing component of the total fishery harvest in many countries, but the impacts of this sector on aquatic resources are often ignored in the management of aquatic systems. Recreational fishing is open-access, and in many inshore regions, the recreational harvest exceeds the commercial harvest. The environmental impacts from recreational angling can be both ecologically significant and broad in scope and include: the removal of a considerable biomass of a wide variety of species; discarded by-catch; possible trophic cascades through the removal of higher order carnivores; impacts on habitat through bait harvesting; impacts of introduced and translocated species to support angling fisheries; direct impacts on sea-birds, marine mammals and reptiles; and angler generated pollution. Management, for several reasons, has largely ignored these environmental impacts from recreational fishing. Recreational fishing impacts are cumulative, whereas there is a tendency for consideration of impacts in isolation. Recreational fishing lobbyists have generally been successful in focusing public and political attention on other impacts such as commercial fishing, and recreational fishing has tended not to come under close scrutiny from conservation and environmental groups. Without changes to the monitoring and management of recreational fisheries that incorporate the broad ecological impacts from the activity, it may not be ecologically sustainable in the long term and Australia will not meet its international obligations of protecting aquatic biodiversity. The definition of property rights and appropriate measures to prevent or manage large scale marine restocking are two emerging issues that also need to be addressed.","container-title":"Pacific Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1071/PC020040","ISSN":"1038-2097","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Pac. Conserv. Biol.","language":"en","page":"40","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Swallowing the bait: is recreational fishing in Australia ecologically sustainable?","title-short":"Swallowing the bait","URL":"http://www.publish.csiro.au/?paper=PC020040","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"P. McPhee","given":"D."},{"family":"Leadbitter","given":"D."},{"family":"A. Skilleter","given":"G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":990,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EHQFVG29"],"itemData":{"id":990,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Recreational activities on, in, and along freshwaters (e.g., boating, bathing, angling) positively contribute to human well-being but can also concurrently stress aquatic ecosystems. While outdoor recreation, aquatic ecosystems, and human well-being form coupled social-ecological systems, inherent fluxes and interactions between these have rarely been properly quantified. This paper synthesizes information on links between water-based recreational activities, effects on freshwater ecosystems integrity and recreational quality, and proposes a novel framework for assessment and integrated management. This framework is based on understanding relationships between recreational quality, demand and use, and recreational use-induced impacts on ecosystem state and function, as well as ecological and social carrying capacities. Current management approaches of freshwater ecosystems addressing economic, environmental, or recreational aspects are poorly linked and harmonized, and are further constrained by inadequate information on the dynamics and densities of recreational uses. Novel assessment and monitoring methods are needed to capture the short-term peak dynamics of water-based recreational uses, and we argue social media could play an increasingly important role here. An integrative recreation ecology management concept combined with peak usage information has great potential to form the basis for next-generation management approaches of freshwater and other ecosystems.","container-title":"Environmental Reviews","DOI":"10.1139/er-2017-0024","ISSN":"1181-8700, 1208-6053","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Environ. Rev.","language":"en","page":"199-213","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The underestimated dynamics and impacts of water-based recreational activities on freshwater ecosystems","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/er-2017-0024","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Venohr","given":"Markus"},{"family":"Langhans","given":"Simone D."},{"family":"Peters","given":"Oliver"},{"family":"Hölker","given":"Franz"},{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Mitchell","given":"Lewis"},{"family":"Wolter","given":"Christian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Many fishing guides and outfitters pride themselves on their operations being catch and release focused and use that in marketing. A number of non-governmental organizations focus on educating anglers on how to engage in responsible catch and release. Moreover, governments routinely apply harvest regulations as part of their fisheries management initiatives in an effort to create sustainable fisheries that benefit aquatic ecosystems and the humans that use them. Thus, catch and release activities and the associated tour operators contribute to creating responsible and sustainable recreational fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vYZVWhQ8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Steven J. Cooke, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Steven J. Cooke, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":979,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ADSAR97E"],"itemData":{"id":979,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.13935","ISSN":"00221112","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"845-856","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Searching for responsible and sustainable recreational fisheries in the Anthropocene","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/jfb.13935","volume":"94","author":[{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."},{"family":"Twardek","given":"William M."},{"family":"Reid","given":"Andrea J."},{"family":"Lennox","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Danylchuk","given":"Sascha C."},{"family":"Brownscombe","given":"Jacob W."},{"family":"Bower","given":"Shannon D."},{"family":"Arlinghaus","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Hyder","given":"Kieran"},{"family":"Danylchuk","given":"Andy J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
3.3.1.6.2. Ornamental or aquarium Fish
      
Ornamental fish trade is a global, multibillion-dollar industry, involving over 125 countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ldiogsop8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Evers, 2019a)","plainCitation":"(Evers, 2019a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31539,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6NA2GXDA"],"itemData":{"id":31539,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.13930","ISSN":"0022-1112, 1095-8649","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"jfb.13930","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Where are they all from? – sources and sustainability in the ornamental freshwater fish trade","title-short":"Where are they all from?","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/jfb.13930","author":[{"family":"Evers","given":"Hans?Georg"},{"family":"Pinnegar","given":"John K."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Martin I."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and worth billions of United States Dollars. Ornamental fisheries are divided into marine and freshwater fisheries. Some of the original representative sustainable gathering projects of ornamental fishes are losing their competitiveness due to the rise of off-site aquaculture. 
      The freshwater ornamental fish trade involves about 125 countries worldwide, is worth approximately 15-30 billion United States dollars ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qKJEo0Kr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Evers, Pinnegar, & Taylor, 2019b; Penning, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Evers, Pinnegar, & Taylor, 2019b; Penning, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"ERpTB65M/TVkNM0Ur","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5932248/items/MUZWHFRU"],"itemData":{"id":8738,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.13930","ISSN":"0022-1112, 1095-8649","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"jfb.13930","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Where are they all from? – sources and sustainability in the ornamental freshwater fish trade","title-short":"Where are they all from?","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/jfb.13930","author":[{"family":"Evers","given":"Hans?Georg"},{"family":"Pinnegar","given":"John K."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Martin I."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,8]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":"ERpTB65M/JxBj2nV0","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5932248/items/4TUZZSXP"],"itemData":{"id":17028,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Association of Zoos and Aquariums, Berna, Suiza","journalAbbreviation":"World Association of Zoos and Aquariums, Berna, Suiza","title":"Turning the tide: a global aquarium strategy for conservation and sustainability","author":[{"family":"Penning","given":"M"},{"family":"Reid","given":"G"},{"family":"Koldewey","given":"H"},{"family":"Dick","given":"G"},{"family":"Andrews","given":"B"},{"family":"Arai","given":"K"},{"family":"Garratt","given":"P"},{"family":"Gendron","given":"S"},{"family":"Lange","given":"J"},{"family":"Tanner","given":"K"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and trading around 1.5 billion specimens per year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1glkT7le","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. H. Stevens, Croft, Paull, & Tyler, 2017)","plainCitation":"(C. H. Stevens, Croft, Paull, & Tyler, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DBZM64ZP"],"itemData":{"id":9733,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.13377","ISSN":"00221112","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"409-428","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Stress and welfare in ornamental fishes: what can be learned from aquaculture?: stress and welfare in ornamental fishes","title-short":"Stress and welfare in ornamental fishes","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/jfb.13377","volume":"91","author":[{"family":"Stevens","given":"C. H."},{"family":"Croft","given":"D. P."},{"family":"Paull","given":"G. C."},{"family":"Tyler","given":"C. R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Roughly 1,000 of the over 5,300 freshwater fish species traded are widely available in commercial numbers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"f36XmjsY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Evers, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(Evers, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"ERpTB65M/TVkNM0Ur","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5932248/items/MUZWHFRU"],"itemData":{"id":8738,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.13930","ISSN":"0022-1112, 1095-8649","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"jfb.13930","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Where are they all from? – sources and sustainability in the ornamental freshwater fish trade","title-short":"Where are they all from?","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/jfb.13930","author":[{"family":"Evers","given":"Hans?Georg"},{"family":"Pinnegar","given":"John K."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Martin I."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A big difference is that around 90% of freshwater ornamental fishes are farmed, usually in Asia or South America, but also in Israel, the United States of America and Europe. Although a smaller portion of freshwater ornamental fishes are still sourced from the wild, in comparison to marine ornamental fishes, it is still a challenge to determine the volume due to lack of reliable data.
	The marine aquarium trade supplies public and private aquariums with a large diversity of organisms ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"76V6SFlV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. Dey, 2016; Wabnitz, 2003)","plainCitation":"(V. Dey, 2016; Wabnitz, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9774,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6GZK677A"],"itemData":{"id":9774,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Infofish International","issue":"16","journalAbbreviation":"Infofish International","page":"23-29","title":"The global trade in ornamental fish","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Dey","given":"VK"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":34335,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3W6LTK77"],"itemData":{"id":34335,"type":"book","ISBN":"92-807-2363-4","publisher":"UNEP/Earthprint","title":"From ocean to aquarium: the global trade in marine ornamental species","author":[{"family":"Wabnitz","given":"Colette"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A review found that an estimated 15-30 million specimens of coral reef fishes are extracted each year from tropical coral reefs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZrqByNAv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biondo & Burki, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Biondo & Burki, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34338,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FTSYRERF"],"itemData":{"id":34338,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The multi-billion dollar trade in ornamental fishes has rarely been reliably monitored. Almost all coral reef fishes are wild-caught, and few scientific analyses have attempted to elicit exact quantities and identify species involved. The consequences of the removal of millions of these fishes are poorly understood. This article collates and examines available information, including scientific studies and formal publications, in order to create a more accurate picture of this commerce. We demonstrate that it is almost impossible to analyse the trade in marine ornamental fishes due to a lack of data, and that available data for marine species is frequently combined with that for freshwater species. Figures range from 15 to 30 million coral reef fishes being traded annually, but could be as high as 150 million specimens. The global value of this trade was only estimated for 1976 and 1999 between USD 28&ndash;40 million. This review highlights the urgent need to introduce a specific harmonised system tariff code and for a global monitoring system, such as the Trade Control and Expert System already in use in Europe, in order to gather accurate and timely information on the number and species of marine ornamental fishes in commerce, where specimens originated, and whether they were wild-caught or captive-bred.","container-title":"Animals","DOI":"10.3390/ani10112014","issue":"11","language":"en","license":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","note":"number: 11\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"2014","source":"www.mdpi.com","title":"A Systematic Review of the Ornamental Fish Trade with Emphasis on Coral Reef Fishes—An Impossible Task","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-2615/10/11/2014","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."},{"family":"Burki","given":"Rainer P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The review did not assess mortality rates ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JXkqlfJo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. H. Stevens, 2017)","plainCitation":"(C. H. Stevens, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DBZM64ZP"],"itemData":{"id":9733,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.13377","ISSN":"00221112","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"409-428","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Stress and welfare in ornamental fishes: what can be learned from aquaculture?: stress and welfare in ornamental fishes","title-short":"Stress and welfare in ornamental fishes","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/jfb.13377","volume":"91","author":[{"family":"Stevens","given":"C. H."},{"family":"Croft","given":"D. P."},{"family":"Paull","given":"G. C."},{"family":"Tyler","given":"C. R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, making proper harvest estimates more challenging since they cannot be based on trade data ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MxevRYql","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. P. A. Cohen, Valenti, & Calado, 2013; Militz, Kinch, Foale, & Southgate, 2016; Monticini, 2010; Olivier, 2001; C. H. Stevens, 2017; Thornhill, 2012)","plainCitation":"(F. P. A. Cohen, Valenti, & Calado, 2013; Militz, Kinch, Foale, & Southgate, 2016; Monticini, 2010; Olivier, 2001; C. H. Stevens, 2017; Thornhill, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9775,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XVBBVA4N"],"itemData":{"id":9775,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1080/10641262.2012.760522","ISSN":"1064-1262, 1547-6553","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","language":"en","page":"98-111","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Traceability Issues in the Trade of Marine Ornamental Species","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/10641262.2012.760522","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Cohen","given":"Felipe P. A."},{"family":"Valenti","given":"Wagner C."},{"family":"Calado","given":"Ricardo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4,3]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":9751,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UCYU5SF8"],"itemData":{"id":9751,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS One","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS One","page":"e0151624","title":"Fish rejections in the marine aquarium trade: an initial case study raises concern for village-based fisheries","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Militz","given":"Thane A"},{"family":"Kinch","given":"Jeff"},{"family":"Foale","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Southgate","given":"Paul C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":9747,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PZFEB7YZ"],"itemData":{"id":9747,"type":"article-journal","title":"The ornamental fish trade: production and commerce of ornamental fish: technical-managerial and legislative aspects","author":[{"family":"Monticini","given":"Pierluigi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":9745,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MXLVSUJX","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C4ECLFJS"],"itemData":{"id":9745,"type":"article-journal","title":"The ornamental fish market","author":[{"family":"Olivier","given":"Katia"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":9733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DBZM64ZP"],"itemData":{"id":9733,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.13377","ISSN":"00221112","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"409-428","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Stress and welfare in ornamental fishes: what can be learned from aquaculture?: stress and welfare in ornamental fishes","title-short":"Stress and welfare in ornamental fishes","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/jfb.13377","volume":"91","author":[{"family":"Stevens","given":"C. H."},{"family":"Croft","given":"D. P."},{"family":"Paull","given":"G. C."},{"family":"Tyler","given":"C. R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8]]}}},{"id":9731,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GQWHLYM4"],"itemData":{"id":9731,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Defenders of Wildlife","journalAbbreviation":"Defenders of Wildlife","title":"Ecological impacts and practices of the coral reef wildlife trade","volume":"187","author":[{"family":"Thornhill","given":"Daniel J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Most marine ornamental species are being collected from the wild ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MREE77VN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biondo, 2017, 2018; Biondo & Burki, 2019; V. Dey, 2016; Rhyne, 2012; Rhyne, Tlusty, Szczebak, & Holmberg, 2017; Wabnitz, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Biondo, 2017, 2018; Biondo & Burki, 2019; V. Dey, 2016; Rhyne, 2012; Rhyne, Tlusty, Szczebak, & Holmberg, 2017; Wabnitz, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9780,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/56FNPDH9"],"itemData":{"id":9780,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2017.05.006","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"95-105","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Quantifying the trade in marine ornamental fishes into Switzerland and an estimation of imports from the European Union","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S235198941730094X","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",7]]}}},{"id":9784,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JWA4M2Q3"],"itemData":{"id":9784,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2018.e00418","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e00418","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Importation of marine ornamental fishes to Switzerland","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2351989418301197","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}},{"id":9779,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/47DVBCYM"],"itemData":{"id":9779,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2019.103620","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"103620","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Monitoring the trade in marine ornamental fishes through the European Trade Control and Expert System TRACES: Challenges and possibilities","title-short":"Monitoring the trade in marine ornamental fishes through the European Trade Control and Expert System TRACES","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X19300193","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."},{"family":"Burki","given":"Rainer P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}},{"id":9774,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6GZK677A"],"itemData":{"id":9774,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Infofish International","issue":"16","journalAbbreviation":"Infofish International","page":"23-29","title":"The global trade in ornamental fish","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Dey","given":"VK"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":9744,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GH7P8PXX"],"itemData":{"id":9744,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0035808","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e35808","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Revealing the Appetite of the Marine Aquarium Fish Trade: The Volume and Biodiversity of Fish Imported into the United States","title-short":"Revealing the Appetite of the Marine Aquarium Fish Trade","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0035808","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Schofield","given":"Pamela J."},{"family":"Kaufman","given":"Les"},{"family":"Morris","given":"James A."},{"family":"Bruckner","given":"Andrew W."}],"editor":[{"family":"Clifton","given":"Julian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",5,21]]}}},{"id":740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GEBU3NUX"],"itemData":{"id":740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The trade of live marine animals for home and public aquaria has grown into a major global industry. Millions of marine fishes and invertebrates are removed from coral reefs and associated habitats each year. The majority are imported into the United States, with the remainder sent to Europe, Japan, and a handful of other countries. Despite the recent growth and diversification of the aquarium trade, to date, data collection is not mandatory, and hence comprehensive information on species volume and diversity is lacking. This lack of information makes it impossible to study trade pathways. Without species-specific volume and diversity data, it is unclear how importing and exporting governments can oversee this industry effectively or how sustainability should be encouraged. To expand our knowledge and understanding of the trade, and to effectively communicate this new understanding, we introduce the publically-available Marine Aquarium Biodiversity and Trade Flow online database (https://www.aquariumtradedata.org/). This tool was created to communicate the volume and diversity of marine fishes and/or invertebrates imported into the US over three complete years (2008, 2009, and 2011) and three partial years (2000, 2004, 2005). To create this tool, invoices pertaining to shipments of live marine fishes and invertebrates were scanned and analyzed for species name, species quantities, country of origin, port of entry, and city of import destination. Here we focus on the analysis of the later three years of data and also produce an estimate for the entirety of 2000, 2004, and 2005. The three-year aggregate totals (2008, 2009, 2011) indicate that just under 2,300 fish and 725 invertebrate species were imported into the US cumulatively, although just under 1,800 fish and 550 invertebrate species were traded annually. Overall, the total number of live marine animals decreased between 2008 and 2011. In 2008, 2009, and 2011, the total number of individual fish (8.2, 7.3, and 6.9 million individuals) and invertebrates (4.2, 3.7, and 3.6 million individuals) assessed by analyzing the invoice data are roughly 60% of the total volumes recorded through the Law Enforcement Management Information System (LEMIS) dataset. Using these complete years, we back-calculated the number of individuals of both fishes and invertebrates imported in 2000, 2004, and 2005. These estimates (9.3, 10.8, and 11.2 million individual fish per year) were consistent with the three years of complete data. We also use these data to understand the global trade in two species (Banggai cardinalfish, Pterapogon kauderni, and orange clownfish, Amphiprion ocellaris / percula) recently considered for Endangered Species Act listing. Aquariumtradedata.org can help create more effective management plans for the traded species, and ideally could be implemented at key trade ports to better assess the global trade of aquatic wildlife.","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.2949","ISSN":"2167-8359","journalAbbreviation":"PeerJ","language":"en","note":"publisher: PeerJ Inc.","page":"e2949","source":"peerj.com","title":"Expanding our understanding of the trade in marine aquarium animals","URL":"https://peerj.com/articles/2949","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Szczebak","given":"Joseph T."},{"family":"Holmberg","given":"Robert J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,26]]}}},{"id":34335,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3W6LTK77"],"itemData":{"id":34335,"type":"book","ISBN":"92-807-2363-4","publisher":"UNEP/Earthprint","title":"From ocean to aquarium: the global trade in marine ornamental species","author":[{"family":"Wabnitz","given":"Colette"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} including species that are listed as endangered by the International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List, such as the Banggai cardinalfish (Pterapogon kauderni). Of the approximately 4,000 marine ornamental fishes known to date ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Cl3ZNcBf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Froese & Pauly, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Froese & Pauly, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6262,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DRE63WW8"],"itemData":{"id":6262,"type":"dataset","abstract":"Froese, R. and D. Pauly. Editors. 2018. FishBase.\\nWorld Wide Web electronic publication.\\nwww.fishbase.org, version (06/2018).","title":"FishBase","URL":"http://www.fishbase.org/glossary/Glossary.php?q=charismatic+species","author":[{"family":"Froese","given":"R."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, about 2,500 species are in trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"T6U7cLIt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rhyne, 2012, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Rhyne, 2012, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9744,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GH7P8PXX"],"itemData":{"id":9744,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0035808","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e35808","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Revealing the Appetite of the Marine Aquarium Fish Trade: The Volume and Biodiversity of Fish Imported into the United States","title-short":"Revealing the Appetite of the Marine Aquarium Fish Trade","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0035808","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Schofield","given":"Pamela J."},{"family":"Kaufman","given":"Les"},{"family":"Morris","given":"James A."},{"family":"Bruckner","given":"Andrew W."}],"editor":[{"family":"Clifton","given":"Julian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",5,21]]}}},{"id":740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GEBU3NUX"],"itemData":{"id":740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The trade of live marine animals for home and public aquaria has grown into a major global industry. Millions of marine fishes and invertebrates are removed from coral reefs and associated habitats each year. The majority are imported into the United States, with the remainder sent to Europe, Japan, and a handful of other countries. Despite the recent growth and diversification of the aquarium trade, to date, data collection is not mandatory, and hence comprehensive information on species volume and diversity is lacking. This lack of information makes it impossible to study trade pathways. Without species-specific volume and diversity data, it is unclear how importing and exporting governments can oversee this industry effectively or how sustainability should be encouraged. To expand our knowledge and understanding of the trade, and to effectively communicate this new understanding, we introduce the publically-available Marine Aquarium Biodiversity and Trade Flow online database (https://www.aquariumtradedata.org/). This tool was created to communicate the volume and diversity of marine fishes and/or invertebrates imported into the US over three complete years (2008, 2009, and 2011) and three partial years (2000, 2004, 2005). To create this tool, invoices pertaining to shipments of live marine fishes and invertebrates were scanned and analyzed for species name, species quantities, country of origin, port of entry, and city of import destination. Here we focus on the analysis of the later three years of data and also produce an estimate for the entirety of 2000, 2004, and 2005. The three-year aggregate totals (2008, 2009, 2011) indicate that just under 2,300 fish and 725 invertebrate species were imported into the US cumulatively, although just under 1,800 fish and 550 invertebrate species were traded annually. Overall, the total number of live marine animals decreased between 2008 and 2011. In 2008, 2009, and 2011, the total number of individual fish (8.2, 7.3, and 6.9 million individuals) and invertebrates (4.2, 3.7, and 3.6 million individuals) assessed by analyzing the invoice data are roughly 60% of the total volumes recorded through the Law Enforcement Management Information System (LEMIS) dataset. Using these complete years, we back-calculated the number of individuals of both fishes and invertebrates imported in 2000, 2004, and 2005. These estimates (9.3, 10.8, and 11.2 million individual fish per year) were consistent with the three years of complete data. We also use these data to understand the global trade in two species (Banggai cardinalfish, Pterapogon kauderni, and orange clownfish, Amphiprion ocellaris / percula) recently considered for Endangered Species Act listing. Aquariumtradedata.org can help create more effective management plans for the traded species, and ideally could be implemented at key trade ports to better assess the global trade of aquatic wildlife.","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.2949","ISSN":"2167-8359","journalAbbreviation":"PeerJ","language":"en","note":"publisher: PeerJ Inc.","page":"e2949","source":"peerj.com","title":"Expanding our understanding of the trade in marine aquarium animals","URL":"https://peerj.com/articles/2949","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Szczebak","given":"Joseph T."},{"family":"Holmberg","given":"Robert J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,26]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Of all these species only around 25 species (1%), can be bred in commercial numbers and about 300 have been bred successfully in research stages ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Evkvkcpu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pouil, Tlusty, Rhyne, & Metian, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Pouil, Tlusty, Rhyne, & Metian, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PUXTRC7Y","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HBPBP86E"],"itemData":{"id":9740,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1111/raq.12381","ISSN":"1753-5123, 1753-5131","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Rev Aquacult","language":"en","page":"1217-1230","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Aquaculture of marine ornamental fish: overview of the production trends and the role of academia in research progress","title-short":"Aquaculture of marine ornamental fish","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/raq.12381","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Pouil","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",5]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	The International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List category is a starting point to warrant protection of a species, but many species of reef fishes are currently labelled ‘not evaluated’ and ‘data deficient’: 73.3% in 2014 and 44.8% in 2018, meaning that the conservation states for almost half of the species is still unknown ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NQEKScD5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biondo, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Biondo, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9784,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JWA4M2Q3"],"itemData":{"id":9784,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2018.e00418","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e00418","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Importation of marine ornamental fishes to Switzerland","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2351989418301197","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Protection from international trade would come through the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species but only few species are listed on its appendices (e.g., Hippocampus spp. Cheilinus undulatus, Holacanthus clarionensis) thus very little specific trade data is collected (https://cites.org/eng/app/appendices.php, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Zt7DhDdd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CITES, 2012)","plainCitation":"(CITES, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30877,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QH2ZDVAP"],"itemData":{"id":30877,"type":"document","publisher":"UNEP?WCMC","title":"CITES Trade: recent trends in international trade in Appendix II?listed species (1996?2010)","author":[{"family":"CITES","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	It is estimated that over 50 countries are actively involved in the marine aquarium industry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fyZHqZ58","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biondo & Burki, 2020; Rhyne, 2012, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Biondo & Burki, 2020; Rhyne, 2012, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34338,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FTSYRERF"],"itemData":{"id":34338,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The multi-billion dollar trade in ornamental fishes has rarely been reliably monitored. Almost all coral reef fishes are wild-caught, and few scientific analyses have attempted to elicit exact quantities and identify species involved. The consequences of the removal of millions of these fishes are poorly understood. This article collates and examines available information, including scientific studies and formal publications, in order to create a more accurate picture of this commerce. We demonstrate that it is almost impossible to analyse the trade in marine ornamental fishes due to a lack of data, and that available data for marine species is frequently combined with that for freshwater species. Figures range from 15 to 30 million coral reef fishes being traded annually, but could be as high as 150 million specimens. The global value of this trade was only estimated for 1976 and 1999 between USD 28&ndash;40 million. This review highlights the urgent need to introduce a specific harmonised system tariff code and for a global monitoring system, such as the Trade Control and Expert System already in use in Europe, in order to gather accurate and timely information on the number and species of marine ornamental fishes in commerce, where specimens originated, and whether they were wild-caught or captive-bred.","container-title":"Animals","DOI":"10.3390/ani10112014","issue":"11","language":"en","license":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","note":"number: 11\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"2014","source":"www.mdpi.com","title":"A Systematic Review of the Ornamental Fish Trade with Emphasis on Coral Reef Fishes—An Impossible Task","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-2615/10/11/2014","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."},{"family":"Burki","given":"Rainer P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",11]]}}},{"id":9744,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GH7P8PXX"],"itemData":{"id":9744,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0035808","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e35808","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Revealing the Appetite of the Marine Aquarium Fish Trade: The Volume and Biodiversity of Fish Imported into the United States","title-short":"Revealing the Appetite of the Marine Aquarium Fish Trade","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0035808","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Schofield","given":"Pamela J."},{"family":"Kaufman","given":"Les"},{"family":"Morris","given":"James A."},{"family":"Bruckner","given":"Andrew W."}],"editor":[{"family":"Clifton","given":"Julian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",5,21]]}}},{"id":740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GEBU3NUX"],"itemData":{"id":740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The trade of live marine animals for home and public aquaria has grown into a major global industry. Millions of marine fishes and invertebrates are removed from coral reefs and associated habitats each year. The majority are imported into the United States, with the remainder sent to Europe, Japan, and a handful of other countries. Despite the recent growth and diversification of the aquarium trade, to date, data collection is not mandatory, and hence comprehensive information on species volume and diversity is lacking. This lack of information makes it impossible to study trade pathways. Without species-specific volume and diversity data, it is unclear how importing and exporting governments can oversee this industry effectively or how sustainability should be encouraged. To expand our knowledge and understanding of the trade, and to effectively communicate this new understanding, we introduce the publically-available Marine Aquarium Biodiversity and Trade Flow online database (https://www.aquariumtradedata.org/). This tool was created to communicate the volume and diversity of marine fishes and/or invertebrates imported into the US over three complete years (2008, 2009, and 2011) and three partial years (2000, 2004, 2005). To create this tool, invoices pertaining to shipments of live marine fishes and invertebrates were scanned and analyzed for species name, species quantities, country of origin, port of entry, and city of import destination. Here we focus on the analysis of the later three years of data and also produce an estimate for the entirety of 2000, 2004, and 2005. The three-year aggregate totals (2008, 2009, 2011) indicate that just under 2,300 fish and 725 invertebrate species were imported into the US cumulatively, although just under 1,800 fish and 550 invertebrate species were traded annually. Overall, the total number of live marine animals decreased between 2008 and 2011. In 2008, 2009, and 2011, the total number of individual fish (8.2, 7.3, and 6.9 million individuals) and invertebrates (4.2, 3.7, and 3.6 million individuals) assessed by analyzing the invoice data are roughly 60% of the total volumes recorded through the Law Enforcement Management Information System (LEMIS) dataset. Using these complete years, we back-calculated the number of individuals of both fishes and invertebrates imported in 2000, 2004, and 2005. These estimates (9.3, 10.8, and 11.2 million individual fish per year) were consistent with the three years of complete data. We also use these data to understand the global trade in two species (Banggai cardinalfish, Pterapogon kauderni, and orange clownfish, Amphiprion ocellaris / percula) recently considered for Endangered Species Act listing. Aquariumtradedata.org can help create more effective management plans for the traded species, and ideally could be implemented at key trade ports to better assess the global trade of aquatic wildlife.","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.2949","ISSN":"2167-8359","journalAbbreviation":"PeerJ","language":"en","note":"publisher: PeerJ Inc.","page":"e2949","source":"peerj.com","title":"Expanding our understanding of the trade in marine aquarium animals","URL":"https://peerj.com/articles/2949","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Szczebak","given":"Joseph T."},{"family":"Holmberg","given":"Robert J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,26]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, this trade lacks sufficient monitoring, and the specific geographic origin of most specimens uncertain ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0BjZ3Vlg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biondo & Burki, 2019, 2020; Biondo & Calado, 2021; F. P. A. Cohen, 2013; Ploeg, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Biondo & Burki, 2019, 2020; Biondo & Calado, 2021; F. P. A. Cohen, 2013; Ploeg, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9779,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/47DVBCYM"],"itemData":{"id":9779,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2019.103620","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"103620","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Monitoring the trade in marine ornamental fishes through the European Trade Control and Expert System TRACES: Challenges and possibilities","title-short":"Monitoring the trade in marine ornamental fishes through the European Trade Control and Expert System TRACES","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X19300193","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."},{"family":"Burki","given":"Rainer P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}},{"id":34338,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FTSYRERF"],"itemData":{"id":34338,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The multi-billion dollar trade in ornamental fishes has rarely been reliably monitored. Almost all coral reef fishes are wild-caught, and few scientific analyses have attempted to elicit exact quantities and identify species involved. The consequences of the removal of millions of these fishes are poorly understood. This article collates and examines available information, including scientific studies and formal publications, in order to create a more accurate picture of this commerce. We demonstrate that it is almost impossible to analyse the trade in marine ornamental fishes due to a lack of data, and that available data for marine species is frequently combined with that for freshwater species. Figures range from 15 to 30 million coral reef fishes being traded annually, but could be as high as 150 million specimens. The global value of this trade was only estimated for 1976 and 1999 between USD 28&ndash;40 million. This review highlights the urgent need to introduce a specific harmonised system tariff code and for a global monitoring system, such as the Trade Control and Expert System already in use in Europe, in order to gather accurate and timely information on the number and species of marine ornamental fishes in commerce, where specimens originated, and whether they were wild-caught or captive-bred.","container-title":"Animals","DOI":"10.3390/ani10112014","issue":"11","language":"en","license":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","note":"number: 11\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"2014","source":"www.mdpi.com","title":"A Systematic Review of the Ornamental Fish Trade with Emphasis on Coral Reef Fishes—An Impossible Task","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-2615/10/11/2014","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."},{"family":"Burki","given":"Rainer P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",11]]}}},{"id":34334,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8XT4B27Z"],"itemData":{"id":34334,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The EU is one of the main importers of marine ornamental species sourced from tropical coral reefs around the world. While the entrance of live organisms into the EU, along with their intra-EU circulation, is framed within stringent control mechanisms, to date, no reliable figures exist concerning which marine ornamental species are imported, in what numbers, and where they are sourced from. This lack of reliable data in the EU on the trade of marine ornamental species is puzzling if one considers that all these imported specimens must be controlled at customs offices located in international airports. Such data deficiency favors the prevalence of blurry supply chains and a “business as usual” mindset that hampers any serious effort to promote sustainability in the marine aquarium industry. To safeguard the collection of findable, accessible, interoperable, and reusable (FAIR) data, we suggest that the EU platform Trade Control and Expert System (TRACES) refines its surveillance on the trade of marine ornamental species. The detailed survey of marine ornamental fishes alone can be used as a proof of concept to validate the use of TRACES for this purpose and, if successful, it can later be expanded to all other taxonomic groups of marine ornamental species.","container-title":"Animals","DOI":"10.3390/ani11061668","ISSN":"2076-2615","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Animals","language":"en","page":"1668","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The European Union Is Still Unable to Find Nemo and Dory-Time for a Reliable Traceability System for the Marine Aquarium Trade","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-2615/11/6/1668","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."},{"family":"Calado","given":"Ricardo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",6,3]]}}},{"id":9775,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XVBBVA4N"],"itemData":{"id":9775,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","DOI":"10.1080/10641262.2012.760522","ISSN":"1064-1262, 1547-6553","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Reviews in Fisheries Science","language":"en","page":"98-111","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Traceability Issues in the Trade of Marine Ornamental Species","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/10641262.2012.760522","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Cohen","given":"Felipe P. A."},{"family":"Valenti","given":"Wagner C."},{"family":"Calado","given":"Ricardo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4,3]]}}},{"id":9741,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WRQW7LRK"],"itemData":{"id":9741,"type":"chapter","container-title":"International Transport of Live Fish in the Ornamental Aquatic Industry: OFI Educational Publication; Ornamental Fish International: Maarsen, The Netherlands","title":"The volume of the ornamental fish trade","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Ploeg","given":"Alex"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The largest exporting markets are Indonesia, the Philippines and Sri Lanka ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"opH2js8D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rhyne, 2012, 2017; Wabnitz, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Rhyne, 2012, 2017; Wabnitz, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9744,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GH7P8PXX"],"itemData":{"id":9744,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0035808","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e35808","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Revealing the Appetite of the Marine Aquarium Fish Trade: The Volume and Biodiversity of Fish Imported into the United States","title-short":"Revealing the Appetite of the Marine Aquarium Fish Trade","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0035808","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Schofield","given":"Pamela J."},{"family":"Kaufman","given":"Les"},{"family":"Morris","given":"James A."},{"family":"Bruckner","given":"Andrew W."}],"editor":[{"family":"Clifton","given":"Julian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",5,21]]}}},{"id":740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GEBU3NUX"],"itemData":{"id":740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The trade of live marine animals for home and public aquaria has grown into a major global industry. Millions of marine fishes and invertebrates are removed from coral reefs and associated habitats each year. The majority are imported into the United States, with the remainder sent to Europe, Japan, and a handful of other countries. Despite the recent growth and diversification of the aquarium trade, to date, data collection is not mandatory, and hence comprehensive information on species volume and diversity is lacking. This lack of information makes it impossible to study trade pathways. Without species-specific volume and diversity data, it is unclear how importing and exporting governments can oversee this industry effectively or how sustainability should be encouraged. To expand our knowledge and understanding of the trade, and to effectively communicate this new understanding, we introduce the publically-available Marine Aquarium Biodiversity and Trade Flow online database (https://www.aquariumtradedata.org/). This tool was created to communicate the volume and diversity of marine fishes and/or invertebrates imported into the US over three complete years (2008, 2009, and 2011) and three partial years (2000, 2004, 2005). To create this tool, invoices pertaining to shipments of live marine fishes and invertebrates were scanned and analyzed for species name, species quantities, country of origin, port of entry, and city of import destination. Here we focus on the analysis of the later three years of data and also produce an estimate for the entirety of 2000, 2004, and 2005. The three-year aggregate totals (2008, 2009, 2011) indicate that just under 2,300 fish and 725 invertebrate species were imported into the US cumulatively, although just under 1,800 fish and 550 invertebrate species were traded annually. Overall, the total number of live marine animals decreased between 2008 and 2011. In 2008, 2009, and 2011, the total number of individual fish (8.2, 7.3, and 6.9 million individuals) and invertebrates (4.2, 3.7, and 3.6 million individuals) assessed by analyzing the invoice data are roughly 60% of the total volumes recorded through the Law Enforcement Management Information System (LEMIS) dataset. Using these complete years, we back-calculated the number of individuals of both fishes and invertebrates imported in 2000, 2004, and 2005. These estimates (9.3, 10.8, and 11.2 million individual fish per year) were consistent with the three years of complete data. We also use these data to understand the global trade in two species (Banggai cardinalfish, Pterapogon kauderni, and orange clownfish, Amphiprion ocellaris / percula) recently considered for Endangered Species Act listing. Aquariumtradedata.org can help create more effective management plans for the traded species, and ideally could be implemented at key trade ports to better assess the global trade of aquatic wildlife.","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.2949","ISSN":"2167-8359","journalAbbreviation":"PeerJ","language":"en","note":"publisher: PeerJ Inc.","page":"e2949","source":"peerj.com","title":"Expanding our understanding of the trade in marine aquarium animals","URL":"https://peerj.com/articles/2949","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Szczebak","given":"Joseph T."},{"family":"Holmberg","given":"Robert J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,26]]}}},{"id":34335,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3W6LTK77"],"itemData":{"id":34335,"type":"book","ISBN":"92-807-2363-4","publisher":"UNEP/Earthprint","title":"From ocean to aquarium: the global trade in marine ornamental species","author":[{"family":"Wabnitz","given":"Colette"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While some analyses have tried to estimate trading figures for large importing markets, such as the United States of America ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jKZKTMhe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rhyne, 2012, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Rhyne, 2012, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9744,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GH7P8PXX"],"itemData":{"id":9744,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0035808","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e35808","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Revealing the Appetite of the Marine Aquarium Fish Trade: The Volume and Biodiversity of Fish Imported into the United States","title-short":"Revealing the Appetite of the Marine Aquarium Fish Trade","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0035808","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Schofield","given":"Pamela J."},{"family":"Kaufman","given":"Les"},{"family":"Morris","given":"James A."},{"family":"Bruckner","given":"Andrew W."}],"editor":[{"family":"Clifton","given":"Julian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",5,21]]}}},{"id":740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GEBU3NUX"],"itemData":{"id":740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The trade of live marine animals for home and public aquaria has grown into a major global industry. Millions of marine fishes and invertebrates are removed from coral reefs and associated habitats each year. The majority are imported into the United States, with the remainder sent to Europe, Japan, and a handful of other countries. Despite the recent growth and diversification of the aquarium trade, to date, data collection is not mandatory, and hence comprehensive information on species volume and diversity is lacking. This lack of information makes it impossible to study trade pathways. Without species-specific volume and diversity data, it is unclear how importing and exporting governments can oversee this industry effectively or how sustainability should be encouraged. To expand our knowledge and understanding of the trade, and to effectively communicate this new understanding, we introduce the publically-available Marine Aquarium Biodiversity and Trade Flow online database (https://www.aquariumtradedata.org/). This tool was created to communicate the volume and diversity of marine fishes and/or invertebrates imported into the US over three complete years (2008, 2009, and 2011) and three partial years (2000, 2004, 2005). To create this tool, invoices pertaining to shipments of live marine fishes and invertebrates were scanned and analyzed for species name, species quantities, country of origin, port of entry, and city of import destination. Here we focus on the analysis of the later three years of data and also produce an estimate for the entirety of 2000, 2004, and 2005. The three-year aggregate totals (2008, 2009, 2011) indicate that just under 2,300 fish and 725 invertebrate species were imported into the US cumulatively, although just under 1,800 fish and 550 invertebrate species were traded annually. Overall, the total number of live marine animals decreased between 2008 and 2011. In 2008, 2009, and 2011, the total number of individual fish (8.2, 7.3, and 6.9 million individuals) and invertebrates (4.2, 3.7, and 3.6 million individuals) assessed by analyzing the invoice data are roughly 60% of the total volumes recorded through the Law Enforcement Management Information System (LEMIS) dataset. Using these complete years, we back-calculated the number of individuals of both fishes and invertebrates imported in 2000, 2004, and 2005. These estimates (9.3, 10.8, and 11.2 million individual fish per year) were consistent with the three years of complete data. We also use these data to understand the global trade in two species (Banggai cardinalfish, Pterapogon kauderni, and orange clownfish, Amphiprion ocellaris / percula) recently considered for Endangered Species Act listing. Aquariumtradedata.org can help create more effective management plans for the traded species, and ideally could be implemented at key trade ports to better assess the global trade of aquatic wildlife.","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.2949","ISSN":"2167-8359","journalAbbreviation":"PeerJ","language":"en","note":"publisher: PeerJ Inc.","page":"e2949","source":"peerj.com","title":"Expanding our understanding of the trade in marine aquarium animals","URL":"https://peerj.com/articles/2949","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Szczebak","given":"Joseph T."},{"family":"Holmberg","given":"Robert J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,26]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Australia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8kgnn5Dk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Trujillo-Gonz\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}lez & Militz, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Trujillo-González & Militz, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9729,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5SMLMIRI"],"itemData":{"id":9729,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Context\\nBiological resource use represents the most common direct threat to biodiversity. Despite this, there is a paucity of comprehensive and overarching data relating to the biological resource use. The global aquarium trade encompasses millions of individual live fishes representing thousands of marine and freshwater species traded on an annual basis. The lack of specific data systems for recording information where fish are exported or imported has resulted in limited accessible trade data. An evaluation of the data-reporting frameworks presently employed by countries engaged in the aquarium trade is warranted to better understand the means by which comprehensive data on the aquarium trade can be made more accessible.\\n\\nAims\\nThis study examines the data-reporting framework of The Australian Government Department of Agriculture and Water Resources (DAWR) used to collate aquarium fish import data, and its capacity to inform on the aquarium trade biodiversity imported to Australia.\\n\\nMethods\\nAquarium import records from 2010–16 were provided by DAWR and used to determine the quantity of individual fishes and consignments imported to Australia. The potential biodiversity of imports was determined from the Australian Government’s List of Permitted Live Freshwater/Marine Fish Suitable for Import 2018 (Number 69, F2017C00079), the legislative document identifying species permitted import to Australia for the aquarium trade. Species permitted import were cross-referenced with the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List to address whether the Australian aquarium trade is importing threatened species.\\n\\nKey results\\nA total of 10320 consignments encompassing more than 78.6 million aquarium fishes were imported to Australia between 2010 and 2016. A total of 4628 species of fishes were permitted import to Australia for the aquarium trade with 73 of the marine species (2.0%) and 81 of the freshwater species (7.5%) found to be threatened with some degree of extinction risk. The data-reporting framework for aquarium fish imports offered limited capacity to taxonomically differentiate imports and only 12.5% of all aquarium fishes imported could be identified to species.\\n\\nConclusions\\nThe aquarium fish import records provided by DAWR had limited taxonomic resolution and, consequently, limited capacity to contribute to an improved understanding of the biodiversity imported to Australia for the aquarium fish trade. While more detailed information is available than is presently collated by DAWR, the availability of this information is constrained by the laws around protected information and the resources available to DAWR.\\n\\nImplications\\nAccessible, detailed information on aquarium fish imports is necessary to support comprehensive research capable of addressing threats to biodiversity loss from the aquarium trade. To this end, the means by which Australian aquarium import data can be reported at greater taxonomic resolution under the existing legislative and resource restraints should be explored further.","container-title":"Wildlife Research","DOI":"10.1071/WR18135","ISSN":"1035-3712","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Wildl. Res.","language":"en","page":"355","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Taxonomically constrained reporting framework limits biodiversity data for aquarium fish imports to Australia","URL":"http://www.publish.csiro.au/?paper=WR18135","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Trujillo-González","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Militz","given":"Thane A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and Europe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lfzLOZZH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biondo, 2017, 2018; Biondo & Burki, 2019; Leal, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Biondo, 2017, 2018; Biondo & Burki, 2019; Leal, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9780,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/56FNPDH9"],"itemData":{"id":9780,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2017.05.006","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"95-105","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Quantifying the trade in marine ornamental fishes into Switzerland and an estimation of imports from the European Union","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S235198941730094X","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",7]]}}},{"id":9784,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JWA4M2Q3"],"itemData":{"id":9784,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2018.e00418","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e00418","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Importation of marine ornamental fishes to Switzerland","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2351989418301197","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}},{"id":9779,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/47DVBCYM"],"itemData":{"id":9779,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2019.103620","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"103620","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Monitoring the trade in marine ornamental fishes through the European Trade Control and Expert System TRACES: Challenges and possibilities","title-short":"Monitoring the trade in marine ornamental fishes through the European Trade Control and Expert System TRACES","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X19300193","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."},{"family":"Burki","given":"Rainer P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}},{"id":9760,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2CS84GTA"],"itemData":{"id":9760,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12120","ISSN":"14672960","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Fish Fish","language":"en","page":"459-468","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Marine ornamental fish imports in the European Union: an economic perspective","title-short":"Marine ornamental fish imports in the European Union","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/faf.12120","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Leal","given":"Miguel Costa"},{"family":"Vaz","given":"Marcela Carraro Melo"},{"family":"Puga","given":"João"},{"family":"Rocha","given":"Rui Jorge Miranda"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Rosa","given":"Rui"},{"family":"Calado","given":"Ricardo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, they all represent approximations and the figures presented are most likely underestimates. Japan is mentioned in the literature as a large importer, but with no recent trade figures available ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"62cLkI66","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biondo, 2017, 2018; Biondo & Burki, 2019, 2020; Rhyne, 2012, 2017; Wabnitz, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Biondo, 2017, 2018; Biondo & Burki, 2019, 2020; Rhyne, 2012, 2017; Wabnitz, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9780,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/56FNPDH9"],"itemData":{"id":9780,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2017.05.006","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"95-105","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Quantifying the trade in marine ornamental fishes into Switzerland and an estimation of imports from the European Union","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S235198941730094X","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",7]]}}},{"id":9784,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JWA4M2Q3"],"itemData":{"id":9784,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2018.e00418","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e00418","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Importation of marine ornamental fishes to Switzerland","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2351989418301197","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}},{"id":9779,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/47DVBCYM"],"itemData":{"id":9779,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2019.103620","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"103620","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Monitoring the trade in marine ornamental fishes through the European Trade Control and Expert System TRACES: Challenges and possibilities","title-short":"Monitoring the trade in marine ornamental fishes through the European Trade Control and Expert System TRACES","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X19300193","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."},{"family":"Burki","given":"Rainer P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}},{"id":34338,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FTSYRERF"],"itemData":{"id":34338,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The multi-billion dollar trade in ornamental fishes has rarely been reliably monitored. Almost all coral reef fishes are wild-caught, and few scientific analyses have attempted to elicit exact quantities and identify species involved. The consequences of the removal of millions of these fishes are poorly understood. This article collates and examines available information, including scientific studies and formal publications, in order to create a more accurate picture of this commerce. We demonstrate that it is almost impossible to analyse the trade in marine ornamental fishes due to a lack of data, and that available data for marine species is frequently combined with that for freshwater species. Figures range from 15 to 30 million coral reef fishes being traded annually, but could be as high as 150 million specimens. The global value of this trade was only estimated for 1976 and 1999 between USD 28&ndash;40 million. This review highlights the urgent need to introduce a specific harmonised system tariff code and for a global monitoring system, such as the Trade Control and Expert System already in use in Europe, in order to gather accurate and timely information on the number and species of marine ornamental fishes in commerce, where specimens originated, and whether they were wild-caught or captive-bred.","container-title":"Animals","DOI":"10.3390/ani10112014","issue":"11","language":"en","license":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","note":"number: 11\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"2014","source":"www.mdpi.com","title":"A Systematic Review of the Ornamental Fish Trade with Emphasis on Coral Reef Fishes—An Impossible Task","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-2615/10/11/2014","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."},{"family":"Burki","given":"Rainer P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",11]]}}},{"id":9744,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GH7P8PXX"],"itemData":{"id":9744,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0035808","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e35808","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Revealing the Appetite of the Marine Aquarium Fish Trade: The Volume and Biodiversity of Fish Imported into the United States","title-short":"Revealing the Appetite of the Marine Aquarium Fish Trade","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0035808","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Schofield","given":"Pamela J."},{"family":"Kaufman","given":"Les"},{"family":"Morris","given":"James A."},{"family":"Bruckner","given":"Andrew W."}],"editor":[{"family":"Clifton","given":"Julian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",5,21]]}}},{"id":740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GEBU3NUX"],"itemData":{"id":740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The trade of live marine animals for home and public aquaria has grown into a major global industry. Millions of marine fishes and invertebrates are removed from coral reefs and associated habitats each year. The majority are imported into the United States, with the remainder sent to Europe, Japan, and a handful of other countries. Despite the recent growth and diversification of the aquarium trade, to date, data collection is not mandatory, and hence comprehensive information on species volume and diversity is lacking. This lack of information makes it impossible to study trade pathways. Without species-specific volume and diversity data, it is unclear how importing and exporting governments can oversee this industry effectively or how sustainability should be encouraged. To expand our knowledge and understanding of the trade, and to effectively communicate this new understanding, we introduce the publically-available Marine Aquarium Biodiversity and Trade Flow online database (https://www.aquariumtradedata.org/). This tool was created to communicate the volume and diversity of marine fishes and/or invertebrates imported into the US over three complete years (2008, 2009, and 2011) and three partial years (2000, 2004, 2005). To create this tool, invoices pertaining to shipments of live marine fishes and invertebrates were scanned and analyzed for species name, species quantities, country of origin, port of entry, and city of import destination. Here we focus on the analysis of the later three years of data and also produce an estimate for the entirety of 2000, 2004, and 2005. The three-year aggregate totals (2008, 2009, 2011) indicate that just under 2,300 fish and 725 invertebrate species were imported into the US cumulatively, although just under 1,800 fish and 550 invertebrate species were traded annually. Overall, the total number of live marine animals decreased between 2008 and 2011. In 2008, 2009, and 2011, the total number of individual fish (8.2, 7.3, and 6.9 million individuals) and invertebrates (4.2, 3.7, and 3.6 million individuals) assessed by analyzing the invoice data are roughly 60% of the total volumes recorded through the Law Enforcement Management Information System (LEMIS) dataset. Using these complete years, we back-calculated the number of individuals of both fishes and invertebrates imported in 2000, 2004, and 2005. These estimates (9.3, 10.8, and 11.2 million individual fish per year) were consistent with the three years of complete data. We also use these data to understand the global trade in two species (Banggai cardinalfish, Pterapogon kauderni, and orange clownfish, Amphiprion ocellaris / percula) recently considered for Endangered Species Act listing. Aquariumtradedata.org can help create more effective management plans for the traded species, and ideally could be implemented at key trade ports to better assess the global trade of aquatic wildlife.","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.2949","ISSN":"2167-8359","journalAbbreviation":"PeerJ","language":"en","note":"publisher: PeerJ Inc.","page":"e2949","source":"peerj.com","title":"Expanding our understanding of the trade in marine aquarium animals","URL":"https://peerj.com/articles/2949","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Szczebak","given":"Joseph T."},{"family":"Holmberg","given":"Robert J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,26]]}}},{"id":34335,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3W6LTK77"],"itemData":{"id":34335,"type":"book","ISBN":"92-807-2363-4","publisher":"UNEP/Earthprint","title":"From ocean to aquarium: the global trade in marine ornamental species","author":[{"family":"Wabnitz","given":"Colette"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Furthermore, there is no information at all for growing markets, such as those located in Southeast Asia, Africa, and South America ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YQgIzuYG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biondo & Calado, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Biondo & Calado, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34334,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8XT4B27Z"],"itemData":{"id":34334,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The EU is one of the main importers of marine ornamental species sourced from tropical coral reefs around the world. While the entrance of live organisms into the EU, along with their intra-EU circulation, is framed within stringent control mechanisms, to date, no reliable figures exist concerning which marine ornamental species are imported, in what numbers, and where they are sourced from. This lack of reliable data in the EU on the trade of marine ornamental species is puzzling if one considers that all these imported specimens must be controlled at customs offices located in international airports. Such data deficiency favors the prevalence of blurry supply chains and a “business as usual” mindset that hampers any serious effort to promote sustainability in the marine aquarium industry. To safeguard the collection of findable, accessible, interoperable, and reusable (FAIR) data, we suggest that the EU platform Trade Control and Expert System (TRACES) refines its surveillance on the trade of marine ornamental species. The detailed survey of marine ornamental fishes alone can be used as a proof of concept to validate the use of TRACES for this purpose and, if successful, it can later be expanded to all other taxonomic groups of marine ornamental species.","container-title":"Animals","DOI":"10.3390/ani11061668","ISSN":"2076-2615","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Animals","language":"en","page":"1668","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The European Union Is Still Unable to Find Nemo and Dory-Time for a Reliable Traceability System for the Marine Aquarium Trade","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-2615/11/6/1668","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."},{"family":"Calado","given":"Ricardo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",6,3]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      With regards to the literature focused on small scale fishing, in Latin America the nine studies addressing small-scale fisheries of ornamental fish for aquarium trade included only three studies in the Brazilian coast ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7ShUCxFb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eduardo, 2020; Monteiro-Neto, 2003; Rosa, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Eduardo, 2020; Monteiro-Neto, 2003; Rosa, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1012,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EGQNW4GE"],"itemData":{"id":1012,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.3278","title":"Biodiversity, ecology, fisheries, and use and trade of Tetraodontiformes fishes reveal their socio-ecological significance along the tropical Brazilian continental shelf","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85078737222&doi=10.1002%2faqc.3278&partnerID=40&md5=3669cbfecdeeab9f657558d415d3734c","author":[{"family":"Eduardo","given":"L.N."},{"family":"Bertrand","given":"A."},{"family":"Frédou","given":"T."},{"family":"Lira","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Lima","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"B.P."},{"family":"Menard","given":"F."},{"family":"Lucena-Frédou","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":969,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7SW3T2JH"],"itemData":{"id":969,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity & Conservation","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity & Conservation","page":"1287-1295","title":"Analysis of the marine ornamental fish trade at Ceará State, northeast Brazil","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Monteiro-Neto","given":"Cassiano"},{"family":"Cunha","given":"Francisca Edna De Andrade"},{"family":"Nottingham","given":"Mara Carvalho"},{"family":"Araújo","given":"Maria Elizabeth"},{"family":"Rosa","given":"Ierecê Lucena"},{"family":"Barros","given":"Glaura Maria Leite"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1002,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AU2DTERU"],"itemData":{"id":1002,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity and Conservation","page":"1951-1971","title":"Fisheries and trade of seahorses in Brazil: historical perspective, current trends, and future directions","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Rosa","given":"Ierecê L"},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"Tacyana PR"},{"family":"Osório","given":"Frederico M"},{"family":"Moraes","given":"Luiz E"},{"family":"Castro","given":"André LC"},{"family":"Barros","given":"Glaura ML"},{"family":"Alves","given":"Rômulo RN"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, while all others focus on freshwater fisheries in the Peruvian and Brazilian Amazon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"g5GQ9pgO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ara\\\\uc0\\\\u250{}jo, 2020; Evers, 2019a; Gerstner, Ortega, Sanchez, & Graham, 2006; Guzm\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}n Maldonado, 2017; Ladislau, 2020; Moreau & Coomes, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Araújo, 2020; Evers, 2019a; Gerstner, Ortega, Sanchez, & Graham, 2006; Guzmán Maldonado, 2017; Ladislau, 2020; Moreau & Coomes, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":967,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G4RG52RK"],"itemData":{"id":967,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2019.105483","ISSN":"01657836","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Research","language":"en","page":"105483","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Economic analysis of the threats posed to the harvesting of ornamental fish by the operation of the Belo Monte hydroelectric dam in northern Brazil","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0165783619303388","volume":"225","author":[{"family":"Araújo","given":"Janayna Galvão","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Santos","given":"Marcos Antônio Souza","dropping-particle":"dos"},{"family":"Rebello","given":"Fabrício Khoury"},{"family":"Prang","given":"Gregor"},{"family":"Almeida","given":"Morgana Carvalho","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Isaac","given":"Victoria Judith"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",5]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31539,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6NA2GXDA"],"itemData":{"id":31539,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.13930","ISSN":"0022-1112, 1095-8649","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"jfb.13930","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Where are they all from? – sources and sustainability in the ornamental freshwater fish trade","title-short":"Where are they all from?","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/jfb.13930","author":[{"family":"Evers","given":"Hans?Georg"},{"family":"Pinnegar","given":"John K."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Martin I."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,8]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":917,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HA7N52RV"],"itemData":{"id":917,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.0022-1112.2006.00978.x","issue":"3","page":"862-875","title":"Effects of the freshwater aquarium trade on wild fish populations in differentially-fished areas of the Peruvian Amazon","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-33644654313&doi=10.1111%2fj.0022-1112.2006.00978.x&partnerID=40&md5=02137b2f71f6a355823c006f80e1b978","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Gerstner","given":"C.L."},{"family":"Ortega","given":"H."},{"family":"Sanchez","given":"H."},{"family":"Graham","given":"D.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31567,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/52MQ2ZHY"],"itemData":{"id":31567,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2016.11.021","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"192-199","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Transboundary fisheries management in the Amazon: Assessing current policies for the management of the ornamental silver arawana (Osteoglossum bicirrhosum)","title-short":"Transboundary fisheries management in the Amazon","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X16305772","volume":"76","author":[{"family":"Guzmán Maldonado","given":"Adriana"},{"family":"Macedo Lopes","given":"Priscila Fabiana"},{"family":"Rodríguez Fernández","given":"Carlos Alberto"},{"family":"Lasso Alcala","given":"Carlos Andrés"},{"family":"Sumalia","given":"Ussif Rashid"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31573,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/249IDV5Z"],"itemData":{"id":31573,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract The Negro river basin is considered the largest area of extractive of ornamental fish in Brazil. This area has fundamental importance for the populations from the Amazon. The present study aimed to describe socioeconomic profile of ornamental fishermen known as “piabeiros” in the Municipality of Barcelos, as well as the ornamental fishery, fisheries area, target species, environments, fishing techniques, equipment, capture techniques and main difficulties faced by the current activity. This study was carried out in municipality of Barcelos, through semi - structured interviews, with artisanal ornamental fishermen (N= 89). The main families of ornamental fish caught and traded were: Characidae, Lebiasinidae, Gasteropelecidae, Cichilidae, Anostomidae, Loricaridae, Potamotrygonidae and Gymnotidae. The main catchment areas were igarapés, lakes, flooded fields, beaches, river banks and igapó forest. Rapiché was the most used equipment in the fisheries both by the fishermen of the urban areas (43.81%) and rural (41.89%). Most of the fishermen are associated with the colony of fishermen of Barcelos (Z33). The data showed that the dynamics of ornamental fishing have changed in a short time and directly affected fishermen, in addition to the low age renewal with the participation of younger fishermen, threatening the transmission of ecological knowledge to future generations. As a result, the increase of the problems related to the productive chain and absence of public power to the activity, since ornamental fishing has already been treated as one of the main economic activities more important for the local communities and for the State of Amazonas.\\n          , \\n            Resumo A bacia do rio Negro é considerada a maior área de extrativismo de peixes ornamentais do Brasil. Esta área possui importância fundamental para as populações da Amazônia. O presente estudo objetivou descrever o perfil socioeconômico de pescadores conhecidos como “piabeiros” no município de Barcelos, bem como a pesca ornamental, área de pesca, espécies-alvo, ambientes, técnicas de pesca, equipamentos, técnicas de captura e dificuldades enfrentadas nesta atividade. O estudo foi realizado por meio de entrevistas semi - estruturadas, com pescadores artesanais ornamentais (N= 89). Os dados mostram que as famílias de peixes ornamentais capturados e comercializados foram: Characidae, Lebiasinidae, Gasteropelecidae, Cichilidae, Anostomidae, Loricaridae, Potamotrygonidae e Gymnotidae. As áreas de captura descritas foram igarapés, lagos, alagados, praias, margens de rios e floresta de igapó. O Rapiché foi o equipamento mais utilizado nas pescarias tanto pelos pescadores das áreas urbanas (43,81%) como rurais (41,89%). A maioria dos pescadores está associada à colônia de pescadores de Barcelos (Z33). Os dados coletados mostraram que a dinâmica da pesca ornamental mudou em pouco tempo e afetou diretamente os pescadores. Além da baixa taxa de renovação, com a participação de pescadores mais jovens, ameaçando a transmissão de conhecimento ecológico para as gerações futuras. Como resultado, notamos o aumento dos problemas relacionados à cadeia produtiva e a ausência de poder público na atividade. A pesca ornamental já foi tratada como uma das principais atividades econômicas para as comunidades locais e para o Estado do Amazonas.","container-title":"Brazilian Journal of Biology","DOI":"10.1590/1519-6984.215806","ISSN":"1678-4375, 1519-6984","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Braz. J. Biol.","page":"544-556","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ornamental fishing in the region of Barcelos, Amazonas: socioeconomic description and scenario of activity in the view of “piabeiros”","title-short":"Ornamental fishing in the region of Barcelos, Amazonas","URL":"http://www.scielo.br/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S1519-69842020000300544&tlng=en","volume":"80","author":[{"family":"Ladislau","given":"D. S."},{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"M. W. S."},{"family":"Castro","given":"P. D. S."},{"family":"Aride","given":"P. H. R."},{"family":"Paiva","given":"A. J. V."},{"family":"Polese","given":"M. F."},{"family":"Souza","given":"A. B."},{"family":"Bassul","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Lavander","given":"H. D."},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"A. T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":968,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HSU8IAZZ"],"itemData":{"id":968,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Amazon basin is a key supplier of wild freshwater fishes to the multi-billion US$ global aquarium market, yet limited information exists on the organization of the regional trade, its importance to local economies or conservation impacts. Through field interviews and review of government statistics, this paper describes the state of the industry in Peru, reporting on the scale and value of the trade, the nature of the fishery and the characteristics and roles of key actors in regional supply networks. An economically important industry is revealed, with 28 firms officially exporting over nine million fishes worth US$ 2.5 million to 24 countries in 2001, and involving fish species from 36 families transported from rainforest catchments up to 1100 km distant from the export centre of Iquitos. Most fish are however collected close to the city, with 10 species representing >70% of trade volume. Some 10 000 people earn income from the trade, among them many rural poor who depend on aquarium fish collection as a primary or supplementary source of cash income. The industry is currently undergoing an important transition towards supplying new Asian and European markets, increasing exports of species biologically unsuited to heavy exploitation as a result, and highlighting the conservation need for improved knowledge and management of the trade in Amazonia.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892907003566","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Conserv.","language":"en","page":"12-22","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Aquarium fish exploitation in western Amazonia: conservation issues in Peru","title-short":"Aquarium fish exploitation in western Amazonia","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0376892907003566/type/journal_article","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Moreau","given":"Marie-Annick"},{"family":"Coomes","given":"Oliver T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",3]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A study in the Brazilian coast shows an increasing trend in the number of fish (mainly native reef species) caught and traded, mostly for export, but there are no data on fishing effort or population status of exploited fish to check for the sustainability of such large trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KMOIkot6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Monteiro-Neto, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Monteiro-Neto, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":969,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7SW3T2JH"],"itemData":{"id":969,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity & Conservation","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity & Conservation","page":"1287-1295","title":"Analysis of the marine ornamental fish trade at Ceará State, northeast Brazil","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Monteiro-Neto","given":"Cassiano"},{"family":"Cunha","given":"Francisca Edna De Andrade"},{"family":"Nottingham","given":"Mara Carvalho"},{"family":"Araújo","given":"Maria Elizabeth"},{"family":"Rosa","given":"Ierecê Lucena"},{"family":"Barros","given":"Glaura Maria Leite"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other studies in the Peruvian and Brazilian Amazon indicate that this activity may be unsustainable due to illegal fishing, rapid expansion of fishing effort, reduced fish abundance in more heavily fished regions compared to protected areas and synergic effects of intense exploitation, market pressure (increased sale prices) and habitat change caused by dams ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FiAKVuJ6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Evers, 2019a; Gerstner, 2006; Guzm\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}n Maldonado, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Evers, 2019a; Gerstner, 2006; Guzmán Maldonado, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31539,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6NA2GXDA"],"itemData":{"id":31539,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.13930","ISSN":"0022-1112, 1095-8649","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"jfb.13930","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Where are they all from? – sources and sustainability in the ornamental freshwater fish trade","title-short":"Where are they all from?","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/jfb.13930","author":[{"family":"Evers","given":"Hans?Georg"},{"family":"Pinnegar","given":"John K."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Martin I."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,8]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":917,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HA7N52RV"],"itemData":{"id":917,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.0022-1112.2006.00978.x","issue":"3","page":"862-875","title":"Effects of the freshwater aquarium trade on wild fish populations in differentially-fished areas of the Peruvian Amazon","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-33644654313&doi=10.1111%2fj.0022-1112.2006.00978.x&partnerID=40&md5=02137b2f71f6a355823c006f80e1b978","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Gerstner","given":"C.L."},{"family":"Ortega","given":"H."},{"family":"Sanchez","given":"H."},{"family":"Graham","given":"D.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31567,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/52MQ2ZHY"],"itemData":{"id":31567,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2016.11.021","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"192-199","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Transboundary fisheries management in the Amazon: Assessing current policies for the management of the ornamental silver arawana (Osteoglossum bicirrhosum)","title-short":"Transboundary fisheries management in the Amazon","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X16305772","volume":"76","author":[{"family":"Guzmán Maldonado","given":"Adriana"},{"family":"Macedo Lopes","given":"Priscila Fabiana"},{"family":"Rodríguez Fernández","given":"Carlos Alberto"},{"family":"Lasso Alcala","given":"Carlos Andrés"},{"family":"Sumalia","given":"Ussif Rashid"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Studies addressing either coastal or inland ornamental small-scale fisheries expressed concerns on unreported and unknown fish mortality during collection and transportation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MJF1SCox","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Monteiro-Neto, 2003; Moreau & Coomes, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Monteiro-Neto, 2003; Moreau & Coomes, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":969,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7SW3T2JH"],"itemData":{"id":969,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity & Conservation","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity & Conservation","page":"1287-1295","title":"Analysis of the marine ornamental fish trade at Ceará State, northeast Brazil","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Monteiro-Neto","given":"Cassiano"},{"family":"Cunha","given":"Francisca Edna De Andrade"},{"family":"Nottingham","given":"Mara Carvalho"},{"family":"Araújo","given":"Maria Elizabeth"},{"family":"Rosa","given":"Ierecê Lucena"},{"family":"Barros","given":"Glaura Maria Leite"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":968,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HSU8IAZZ"],"itemData":{"id":968,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Amazon basin is a key supplier of wild freshwater fishes to the multi-billion US$ global aquarium market, yet limited information exists on the organization of the regional trade, its importance to local economies or conservation impacts. Through field interviews and review of government statistics, this paper describes the state of the industry in Peru, reporting on the scale and value of the trade, the nature of the fishery and the characteristics and roles of key actors in regional supply networks. An economically important industry is revealed, with 28 firms officially exporting over nine million fishes worth US$ 2.5 million to 24 countries in 2001, and involving fish species from 36 families transported from rainforest catchments up to 1100 km distant from the export centre of Iquitos. Most fish are however collected close to the city, with 10 species representing >70% of trade volume. Some 10 000 people earn income from the trade, among them many rural poor who depend on aquarium fish collection as a primary or supplementary source of cash income. The industry is currently undergoing an important transition towards supplying new Asian and European markets, increasing exports of species biologically unsuited to heavy exploitation as a result, and highlighting the conservation need for improved knowledge and management of the trade in Amazonia.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892907003566","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Conserv.","language":"en","page":"12-22","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Aquarium fish exploitation in western Amazonia: conservation issues in Peru","title-short":"Aquarium fish exploitation in western Amazonia","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0376892907003566/type/journal_article","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Moreau","given":"Marie-Annick"},{"family":"Coomes","given":"Oliver T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",3]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In Africa, only a small number of papers dealt with small-scale fisheries for ornamental trade, both in the coral reefs off the coast of Kenya. Some of the many species studied proved to be at low risk of overexploitation, mainly because there is large and disseminated use of very selective gears to capture the fish in this type of fishing. This selectivity allows the removal of mature, large (and colorful) individuals above the maturation size ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"d96LKhys","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gomes, Erzini, & Mcclanahan, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Gomes, Erzini, & Mcclanahan, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":912,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3YYALMVV"],"itemData":{"id":912,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2389","issue":"5","page":"680-695","title":"Trap modification opens new gates to achieve sustainable coral reef fisheries","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84908010329&doi=10.1002%2faqc.2389&partnerID=40&md5=b13caf6c062ce797e1820ea7c143efb4","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Gomes","given":"I."},{"family":"Erzini","given":"K."},{"family":"Mcclanahan","given":"T.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. On the other hand, some other species are vulnerable to overfishing and other species are probably already overfished ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Kg8TYcOl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Okemwa, Kaunda-Arara, Kimani, & Ogutu, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Okemwa, Kaunda-Arara, Kimani, & Ogutu, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":918,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T5P49RG3"],"itemData":{"id":918,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","ISSN":"0165-7836","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Research","page":"19-31","title":"Catch composition and sustainability of the marine aquarium fishery in Kenya","volume":"183","author":[{"family":"Okemwa","given":"GM"},{"family":"Kaunda-Arara","given":"B"},{"family":"Kimani","given":"EN"},{"family":"Ogutu","given":"B"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This overfishing is due to low natural abundance and long-term intense fishing pressure. In those cases, more active management measures could mitigate threats to vulnerable species.
      In Asia-Pacific, data from both fishers’ knowledge and recordings of fish landings (logbooks) of seahorses (Hippocampus comes), which are exploited as ornamental and medicinal fish in the Philippines, indicate that catch-per-unit-of-effort did not change over a period of nine years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UetFcaNO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Donnell, 2012)","plainCitation":"(O’Donnell, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31716,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IMNC98Q2"],"itemData":{"id":31716,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1080/08920753.2012.727734","issue":"6","page":"594-611","title":"Comparing Fisher Interviews, Logbooks, and Catch Landings Estimates of Extraction Rates in a Small-Scale Fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84868265819&doi=10.1080%2f08920753.2012.727734&partnerID=40&md5=ad8d271ce5cfe17e3269d685d23c03d3","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"O'Donnell","given":"K.P."},{"family":"Molloy","given":"P.P."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A.C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fishers’ logbooks that included zero catches (fishing trips on which no seahorse was caught) showed the lowest catch-per-unit-of-effort values, and a previous study based on fishers’ indigenous and local knowledge indicated declines of seahorse catches from 1970 to 2005 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PUOy5w9q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Donnell, Pajaro, & Vincent, 2010)","plainCitation":"(O’Donnell, Pajaro, & Vincent, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":916,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IYFMJSUT"],"itemData":{"id":916,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/j.1469-1795.2010.00377.x","issue":"6","page":"526-533","title":"How does the accuracy of fisher knowledge affect seahorse conservation status?","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-78650142240&doi=10.1111%2fj.1469-1795.2010.00377.x&partnerID=40&md5=20efedbeb1e753637c504543ac8b7e47","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"O'Donnell","given":"K.P."},{"family":"Pajaro","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A.C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some studies point to unsustainable rates of exploitation of sea horses (Hippocampus spp.) on the coast of Vietnam ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BICAmT0q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stocks, 2019; Stocks, Foster, Bat, & Vincent, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Stocks, 2019; Stocks, Foster, Bat, & Vincent, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1084,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q3KYCIAX"],"itemData":{"id":1084,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-019-0073-8","issue":"3","page":"397-408","title":"Local Fishers’ Knowledge of Target and Incidental Seahorse Catch in Southern Vietnam","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85066014179&doi=10.1007%2fs10745-019-0073-8&partnerID=40&md5=e646d71a034db6d7d5c49c59e4974ea5","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Stocks","given":"A.P."},{"family":"Foster","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Bat","given":"N.K."},{"family":"Ha","given":"N.M."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A.C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":919,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AN37CSQD"],"itemData":{"id":919,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2017.07.021","page":"27-33","title":"Catch as catch can: Targeted and indiscriminate small-scale fishing of seahorses in Vietnam","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85027858544&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2017.07.021&partnerID=40&md5=a901fd632fbf0cded3868e651fef8243","volume":"196","author":[{"family":"Stocks","given":"A.P."},{"family":"Foster","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Bat","given":"N.K."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A.C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In India, nearly 50% of marine aquarium fish and corals, considered highly financially valuable species, have not been assessed for their extinction risk ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wJ5hy0EZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nepal, Abt, Skog, Prestemon, & Abt, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Nepal, Abt, Skog, Prestemon, & Abt, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":436,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6U682U25"],"itemData":{"id":436,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Science","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Oxford University Press US","page":"14–26","title":"Projected market competition for wood biomass between traditional products and energy: A simulated interaction of us regional, national, and global forest product markets","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1093/forsci/fxy031","volume":"65","author":[{"family":"Nepal","given":"Prakash"},{"family":"Abt","given":"Karen L"},{"family":"Skog","given":"Kenneth E"},{"family":"Prestemon","given":"Jeffrey P"},{"family":"Abt","given":"Robert C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The ornamental fisheries of corals (many species) and the coastal fish (Pterapogon kauderni) in Indonesia are considered to be unsustainable, due to intensive fishing pressure, habitat damage, or overestimated quotas beyond ecological capacity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bHRqP7tE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sebastian C. A. Ferse, 2012; Kolm & Berglund, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Sebastian C. A. Ferse, 2012; Kolm & Berglund, 2003)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1074,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KAG8PLBI"],"itemData":{"id":1074,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Collection of live coral for the marine aquarium trade has increased significantly in recent years, causing concerns over the ecological implications of this activity for the source regions. The Spermonde Archipelago off Southwest Sulawesi is one of the four major ornamental coral collection sites in Indonesia, the country supplying most corals in the trade. Management of the fishery remains ineffective, and overharvesting is widespread. Conservation strategies for marine resources increasingly emphasize the socioeconomic dimensions of resource use, yet little information is available on the livelihoods of fishermen involved in the ornamental fishery. Here, a first analysis of the livelihoods of coral fishermen in the Spermonde Archipelago is presented, along with a discussion of possibilities for future management interventions. The reef fishery of the study area is characterized by a high diversity of target organisms, with none of the fishermen relying exclusively on ornamental corals for income. Moreover, fishermen are organized in a tightly knit web of patron–client relationships, leaving little scope for them to initiate changes in livelihood strategies. The patron–client system emerged as a key institution of the fishery. In order to support sustainable marine resource use, management policies should simultaneously target the fishermen and their patrons as well as the regional coral trade network, broadening the livelihood portfolio of island communities and improving the regulation of the associated trade network.","container-title":"Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1080/08920753.2012.694801","ISSN":"0892-0753","issue":"5","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/08920753.2012.694801","page":"525-555","source":"Taylor and Francis+NEJM","title":"Livelihoods of Ornamental Coral Fishermen in South Sulawesi/Indonesia: Implications for Management","title-short":"Livelihoods of Ornamental Coral Fishermen in South Sulawesi/Indonesia","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/08920753.2012.694801","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."},{"family":"Knittweis","given":"Leyla"},{"family":"Krause","given":"Gesche"},{"family":"Maddusila","given":"Andi"},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",9,1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":914,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/223WQWH6"],"itemData":{"id":914,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1046/j.1523-1739.2003.01522.x","issue":"3","page":"910-914","title":"Wild populations of a reef fish suffer from the \\"nondestructive\\" aquarium trade fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-0038580550&doi=10.1046%2fj.1523-1739.2003.01522.x&partnerID=40&md5=58aa72ff84ab14dc1c548082ddc377f1","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Kolm","given":"N."},{"family":"Berglund","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A recent monitoring survey of Banggai Cardinalfish populations shows mixed trends from 2004 to 2018 among seven sites in Indonesia: recovery or partial recovery in three sites, stable in one, increase in one and decline in two sites, indicating potential effects from conservation measures in some sites and the relevance of microhabitats (sea urchins and sea anemones) to juveniles and adults of this reef fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"u9CFgGPY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wiadnyana, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Wiadnyana, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":28524,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/64K7ZPRW"],"itemData":{"id":28524,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Central Sulawesi, Indonesia","language":"en","note":"publisher: IOP Publishing","page":"012033","title":"Population trends of Banggai cardinalfish in the Banggai Islands","volume":"420","author":[{"family":"Wiadnyana","given":"N."},{"family":"Suharti","given":"S."},{"family":"Ndobe","given":"S."},{"family":"Triharyuni","given":"S."},{"family":"Lilley","given":"G."},{"family":"Risuana","given":"S."},{"family":"Moore","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lIU9SsYA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sebastian C. A. Ferse, 2012; Kolm & Berglund, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Sebastian C. A. Ferse, 2012; Kolm & Berglund, 2003)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1074,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KAG8PLBI"],"itemData":{"id":1074,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Collection of live coral for the marine aquarium trade has increased significantly in recent years, causing concerns over the ecological implications of this activity for the source regions. The Spermonde Archipelago off Southwest Sulawesi is one of the four major ornamental coral collection sites in Indonesia, the country supplying most corals in the trade. Management of the fishery remains ineffective, and overharvesting is widespread. Conservation strategies for marine resources increasingly emphasize the socioeconomic dimensions of resource use, yet little information is available on the livelihoods of fishermen involved in the ornamental fishery. Here, a first analysis of the livelihoods of coral fishermen in the Spermonde Archipelago is presented, along with a discussion of possibilities for future management interventions. The reef fishery of the study area is characterized by a high diversity of target organisms, with none of the fishermen relying exclusively on ornamental corals for income. Moreover, fishermen are organized in a tightly knit web of patron–client relationships, leaving little scope for them to initiate changes in livelihood strategies. The patron–client system emerged as a key institution of the fishery. In order to support sustainable marine resource use, management policies should simultaneously target the fishermen and their patrons as well as the regional coral trade network, broadening the livelihood portfolio of island communities and improving the regulation of the associated trade network.","container-title":"Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1080/08920753.2012.694801","ISSN":"0892-0753","issue":"5","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/08920753.2012.694801","page":"525-555","source":"Taylor and Francis+NEJM","title":"Livelihoods of Ornamental Coral Fishermen in South Sulawesi/Indonesia: Implications for Management","title-short":"Livelihoods of Ornamental Coral Fishermen in South Sulawesi/Indonesia","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/08920753.2012.694801","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."},{"family":"Knittweis","given":"Leyla"},{"family":"Krause","given":"Gesche"},{"family":"Maddusila","given":"Andi"},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",9,1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":914,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/223WQWH6"],"itemData":{"id":914,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1046/j.1523-1739.2003.01522.x","issue":"3","page":"910-914","title":"Wild populations of a reef fish suffer from the \\"nondestructive\\" aquarium trade fishery","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-0038580550&doi=10.1046%2fj.1523-1739.2003.01522.x&partnerID=40&md5=58aa72ff84ab14dc1c548082ddc377f1","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Kolm","given":"N."},{"family":"Berglund","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}Morevoer, some marine protected areas, which were created to protect reef fish for ornamental aquarium trade in Hawaii, have increased abundance of some exploited species and thus possibly improved the sustainability of these commercially valuable ornamental fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3L186RBa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Friedlander, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Friedlander, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1282,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L6WFDPKD"],"itemData":{"id":1282,"type":"book","collection-title":"Advances in Marine Biology","note":"page: 203\\nDOI: 10.1016/B978-0-12-800214-8.00005-0","number-of-pages":"153","title":"Understanding the Scale of Marine Protection in Hawai'i: From Community-Based Management to the Remote Northwestern Hawaiian Islands","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84908161069&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-12-800214-8.00005-0&partnerID=40&md5=24e69576bd7f0bfe52fe4f247ae8a08f","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Friedlander","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Stamoulis","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Kittinger","given":"J.N."},{"family":"Drazen","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Tissot","given":"B.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The few studies on inland ornamental fisheries indicate potential unsustainable harvest, due mostly to intense fishing effort and weak regulations (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). The increase on fishing effort had caused overexploitation of wild caught populations of the clown loach or tiger botia fish Chromobotia macracanthus in Indonesia, which directed fishers to catch fish larvae to be reared in captivity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ng3zvgZm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Evers, 2019a)","plainCitation":"(Evers, 2019a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31539,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6NA2GXDA"],"itemData":{"id":31539,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.13930","ISSN":"0022-1112, 1095-8649","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"jfb.13930","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Where are they all from? – sources and sustainability in the ornamental freshwater fish trade","title-short":"Where are they all from?","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/jfb.13930","author":[{"family":"Evers","given":"Hans?Georg"},{"family":"Pinnegar","given":"John K."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Martin I."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In India, thousands of individuals of threatened and endemic freshwater fish species have been regularly caught and sold for high values in the export market, stimulating an intense fishing pressure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ul9P5e6G","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Raghavan, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Raghavan, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":988,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UXMWWM3E"],"itemData":{"id":988,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2013.04.019","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"158-169","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Uncovering an obscure trade: Threatened freshwater fishes and the aquarium pet markets","title-short":"Uncovering an obscure trade","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320713001225","volume":"164","author":[{"family":"Raghavan","given":"Rajeev"},{"family":"Dahanukar","given":"Neelesh"},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Krishna Kumar","given":"K."},{"family":"Molur","given":"Sanjay"},{"family":"Rosser","given":"Alison M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The aquarium trade of ornamental fishes is usually considered as a profitable, rapidly developing, but somewhat unpredictable economic activity, which is subjected to sudden fluctuations in the international market and may involve high operational costs, either for coastal ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DCi48JXC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Monteiro-Neto, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Monteiro-Neto, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":969,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7SW3T2JH"],"itemData":{"id":969,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity & Conservation","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity & Conservation","page":"1287-1295","title":"Analysis of the marine ornamental fish trade at Ceará State, northeast Brazil","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Monteiro-Neto","given":"Cassiano"},{"family":"Cunha","given":"Francisca Edna De Andrade"},{"family":"Nottingham","given":"Mara Carvalho"},{"family":"Araújo","given":"Maria Elizabeth"},{"family":"Rosa","given":"Ierecê Lucena"},{"family":"Barros","given":"Glaura Maria Leite"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or inland fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7efJhI0s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ara\\\\uc0\\\\u250{}jo, 2020; Moreau & Coomes, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Araújo, 2020; Moreau & Coomes, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":967,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G4RG52RK"],"itemData":{"id":967,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2019.105483","ISSN":"01657836","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Research","language":"en","page":"105483","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Economic analysis of the threats posed to the harvesting of ornamental fish by the operation of the Belo Monte hydroelectric dam in northern Brazil","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0165783619303388","volume":"225","author":[{"family":"Araújo","given":"Janayna Galvão","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Santos","given":"Marcos Antônio Souza","dropping-particle":"dos"},{"family":"Rebello","given":"Fabrício Khoury"},{"family":"Prang","given":"Gregor"},{"family":"Almeida","given":"Morgana Carvalho","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Isaac","given":"Victoria Judith"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",5]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":968,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HSU8IAZZ"],"itemData":{"id":968,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Amazon basin is a key supplier of wild freshwater fishes to the multi-billion US$ global aquarium market, yet limited information exists on the organization of the regional trade, its importance to local economies or conservation impacts. Through field interviews and review of government statistics, this paper describes the state of the industry in Peru, reporting on the scale and value of the trade, the nature of the fishery and the characteristics and roles of key actors in regional supply networks. An economically important industry is revealed, with 28 firms officially exporting over nine million fishes worth US$ 2.5 million to 24 countries in 2001, and involving fish species from 36 families transported from rainforest catchments up to 1100 km distant from the export centre of Iquitos. Most fish are however collected close to the city, with 10 species representing >70% of trade volume. Some 10 000 people earn income from the trade, among them many rural poor who depend on aquarium fish collection as a primary or supplementary source of cash income. The industry is currently undergoing an important transition towards supplying new Asian and European markets, increasing exports of species biologically unsuited to heavy exploitation as a result, and highlighting the conservation need for improved knowledge and management of the trade in Amazonia.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892907003566","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Conserv.","language":"en","page":"12-22","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Aquarium fish exploitation in western Amazonia: conservation issues in Peru","title-short":"Aquarium fish exploitation in western Amazonia","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0376892907003566/type/journal_article","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Moreau","given":"Marie-Annick"},{"family":"Coomes","given":"Oliver T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",3]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Trade includes many dealers with large differences in prices paid between fishers and final retailers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fKdSepjU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rosa, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Rosa, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1002,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AU2DTERU"],"itemData":{"id":1002,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity and Conservation","page":"1951-1971","title":"Fisheries and trade of seahorses in Brazil: historical perspective, current trends, and future directions","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Rosa","given":"Ierecê L"},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"Tacyana PR"},{"family":"Osório","given":"Frederico M"},{"family":"Moraes","given":"Luiz E"},{"family":"Castro","given":"André LC"},{"family":"Barros","given":"Glaura ML"},{"family":"Alves","given":"Rômulo RN"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, the well-established aquarium trade in the Negro River (Brazilian Amazon), which exploits mainly the small cardinal tetra fish Paracheirodon axelrodi for the international market, has experienced problems related to the productive chain, such as competition with international producers, absence of local buyers, decrease on sales and lower profits, making some fishers abandon this activity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CXjosTAi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Evers, 2019a; Ladislau, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Evers, 2019a; Ladislau, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31539,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6NA2GXDA"],"itemData":{"id":31539,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fish Biology","DOI":"10.1111/jfb.13930","ISSN":"0022-1112, 1095-8649","journalAbbreviation":"J Fish Biol","language":"en","page":"jfb.13930","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Where are they all from? – sources and sustainability in the ornamental freshwater fish trade","title-short":"Where are they all from?","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/jfb.13930","author":[{"family":"Evers","given":"Hans?Georg"},{"family":"Pinnegar","given":"John K."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Martin I."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,8]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31573,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/249IDV5Z"],"itemData":{"id":31573,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract The Negro river basin is considered the largest area of extractive of ornamental fish in Brazil. This area has fundamental importance for the populations from the Amazon. The present study aimed to describe socioeconomic profile of ornamental fishermen known as “piabeiros” in the Municipality of Barcelos, as well as the ornamental fishery, fisheries area, target species, environments, fishing techniques, equipment, capture techniques and main difficulties faced by the current activity. This study was carried out in municipality of Barcelos, through semi - structured interviews, with artisanal ornamental fishermen (N= 89). The main families of ornamental fish caught and traded were: Characidae, Lebiasinidae, Gasteropelecidae, Cichilidae, Anostomidae, Loricaridae, Potamotrygonidae and Gymnotidae. The main catchment areas were igarapés, lakes, flooded fields, beaches, river banks and igapó forest. Rapiché was the most used equipment in the fisheries both by the fishermen of the urban areas (43.81%) and rural (41.89%). Most of the fishermen are associated with the colony of fishermen of Barcelos (Z33). The data showed that the dynamics of ornamental fishing have changed in a short time and directly affected fishermen, in addition to the low age renewal with the participation of younger fishermen, threatening the transmission of ecological knowledge to future generations. As a result, the increase of the problems related to the productive chain and absence of public power to the activity, since ornamental fishing has already been treated as one of the main economic activities more important for the local communities and for the State of Amazonas.\\n          , \\n            Resumo A bacia do rio Negro é considerada a maior área de extrativismo de peixes ornamentais do Brasil. Esta área possui importância fundamental para as populações da Amazônia. O presente estudo objetivou descrever o perfil socioeconômico de pescadores conhecidos como “piabeiros” no município de Barcelos, bem como a pesca ornamental, área de pesca, espécies-alvo, ambientes, técnicas de pesca, equipamentos, técnicas de captura e dificuldades enfrentadas nesta atividade. O estudo foi realizado por meio de entrevistas semi - estruturadas, com pescadores artesanais ornamentais (N= 89). Os dados mostram que as famílias de peixes ornamentais capturados e comercializados foram: Characidae, Lebiasinidae, Gasteropelecidae, Cichilidae, Anostomidae, Loricaridae, Potamotrygonidae e Gymnotidae. As áreas de captura descritas foram igarapés, lagos, alagados, praias, margens de rios e floresta de igapó. O Rapiché foi o equipamento mais utilizado nas pescarias tanto pelos pescadores das áreas urbanas (43,81%) como rurais (41,89%). A maioria dos pescadores está associada à colônia de pescadores de Barcelos (Z33). Os dados coletados mostraram que a dinâmica da pesca ornamental mudou em pouco tempo e afetou diretamente os pescadores. Além da baixa taxa de renovação, com a participação de pescadores mais jovens, ameaçando a transmissão de conhecimento ecológico para as gerações futuras. Como resultado, notamos o aumento dos problemas relacionados à cadeia produtiva e a ausência de poder público na atividade. A pesca ornamental já foi tratada como uma das principais atividades econômicas para as comunidades locais e para o Estado do Amazonas.","container-title":"Brazilian Journal of Biology","DOI":"10.1590/1519-6984.215806","ISSN":"1678-4375, 1519-6984","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Braz. J. Biol.","page":"544-556","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ornamental fishing in the region of Barcelos, Amazonas: socioeconomic description and scenario of activity in the view of “piabeiros”","title-short":"Ornamental fishing in the region of Barcelos, Amazonas","URL":"http://www.scielo.br/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S1519-69842020000300544&tlng=en","volume":"80","author":[{"family":"Ladislau","given":"D. S."},{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"M. W. S."},{"family":"Castro","given":"P. D. S."},{"family":"Aride","given":"P. H. R."},{"family":"Paiva","given":"A. J. V."},{"family":"Polese","given":"M. F."},{"family":"Souza","given":"A. B."},{"family":"Bassul","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Lavander","given":"H. D."},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"A. T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	The global trade of marine ornamental fishes has always lagged behind in terms of transparency, as there is a multitude of stakeholders involved from the fishers at location of capture to the (many) intermediaries and traders, the exporters and importers and the intermediaries in the importing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"p9aPCuPL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Amos & Claussen, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Amos & Claussen, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":156,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QG627MMS"],"itemData":{"id":156,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Turnstone Consulting and Starling Resources Report","journalAbbreviation":"Turnstone Consulting and Starling Resources Report","title":"Certification as a conservation tool in the marine aquarium trade: challenges to effectiveness","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Amos","given":"Amy Mathews"},{"family":"Claussen","given":"John D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some attempts have been made to increase transparency in the marine ornamental fish industry. The Global Marine Data Base (GMAD) was introduced in 2002 and collected importer and exporter data but with only 41 contributing companies and unfortunately, only for one year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RzwiyMme","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Green, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Green, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9759,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XS94ZFMU"],"itemData":{"id":9759,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine ornamental species: collection, culture & conservation","journalAbbreviation":"Marine ornamental species: collection, culture & conservation","page":"29-48","title":"International trade in marine aquarium species: using the global marine aquarium database","author":[{"family":"Green","given":"Edmund"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another attempt was the Marine Aquarium Council label that was established in 1998 to ensure traceability, good practice, and sustainable schemes of ecologically and socially responsible fishing, but has been inactive since 2008 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"U75ZJ4Km","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dee, Horii, & Thornhill, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Dee, Horii, & Thornhill, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9773,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PYF2L26G"],"itemData":{"id":9773,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2013.11.025","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"225-237","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Conservation and management of ornamental coral reef wildlife: Successes, shortcomings, and future directions","title-short":"Conservation and management of ornamental coral reef wildlife","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320713004047","volume":"169","author":[{"family":"Dee","given":"Laura E."},{"family":"Horii","given":"Stephanie S."},{"family":"Thornhill","given":"Daniel J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	
3.3.2. Gathering

3.3.2.1. Introduction

Wild algae, fungi and plants provide food, income and nutritional diversity for an estimated one in five people around the world, in particular women, children, landless farmers and others in vulnerable situations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a13q8p0ec0k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sorrenti & Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Sorrenti & Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":987,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U496QJQB"],"itemData":{"id":987,"type":"book","abstract":"Non-wood forest products (NWFPs) such as nuts, mushrooms, herbs, spices, aromatic plants and game have been used for food, health and cultural purposes for millennia, yet there is a tendency to underestimate their role because they are poorly represented in international statistics, as in most cases their use and trade are confined to the informal sector. Recent studies show that NWFPs still form the basis of lives and livelihoods in many parts of the world and play a much more significant role in food and nutritional than previously thought (FAO, 2016; Rowland, 2016; Ickowitz, 2014). For this reason, the FAO Forestry Statistics Programme has taken renewed interest in addressing the existing data gap on NWFPs to provide a sound evidence evidence base for decision making regarding their use. As a point of departure, a systematic review of NWFPs in the existing international classification systems used for the collection and dissemination of data on production, trade and economic activities was initiated in an effort to ultimately improve data collection on NWFPs. A number of reports, journal articles, working papers, statistical publications and databases were also reviewed. Experts were invited over a period of four months (July to October 2016) to provide suggestions to help improve the study. A selection of \\"major\\" NWFPs were identified, including: edible mushrooms and truffles; forest berries; maple products; edible nuts; bamboo and rattan; cork; bark; latexes; gums and resins; hides; skins and trophies; game meat and edible insects. Although some important product categories were excluded from this initial review, the study should be seen as a first step towards improving data collection on what is an extremely vast category of products. Not surprisingly, the report found that data on international trade and production of NWFPs is partial and not comparable across countries and over time. A major difficulty for statistics gathering is that NWFPs are often classified under agricultural categories without any distinction between wild and farmed produce. Nevertheless, many NWFPs -- such as maple products, cork, bamboo and rattan, gums and resins -- are recognizable under a wide range of categories in the three international schemes, the Harmonized Commodity Description (HS), the Central Products Classification (CPC) and the International Standard Industrial Classification of All Economic Activities (ISIC), with the HS enabling the highest level of product detail. This is a good basis for improving data on NWFP production and trade. The report also found that information is available in national databases to varying degrees, with countries reporting on products that have value to them. Thailand, for example, is the only country that has detailed coes (four) on edible insects, likely due to the growing economic importance of rearing insects in the country over the past two decades; Canada and the United States of America have rich databases on maple products; Finland gathers statistics on wild edible mushrooms and wild berries; Japan collects data on a suite of \\"minor forest products\\", including mushrooms. The findings suggest that there is scope to improve data collection by further clarifying terminology and classification issues and strengthening collaboration with the United Nations Statistics Division (UNSD) and the World Customs Organization (WCO) who are responsible for revising classification of products, as well as with other agencies such as the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES)5. They also point to the conclusion that no matter how well these methodologies are refined, these figures will only capture a part of the picture, leaving out many products that are traded informally, produced for subsistence or for cultural purposes. Targeted household surveys are needed to complement these figures and are recommended to capture the full value of NWFPs.","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-109602-4","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 980879700","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Non-wood forest products in international statistical systems","author":[{"family":"Sorrenti","given":"Simona"},{"literal":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Plant List (http://www.theplantlist.org/) and the World Flora Online (WFO, http://www.worldfloraonline.org/) list around 360,000 species with accepted names (accessed January 2021). The world checklist of vascular plants includes approximately 350,000 accepted species. With regards to fungi, 148,000 species have been scientifically identified, but it is believed that more than 90% of species remain unknown to science ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a29b7oflfrs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Antonelli, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Antonelli, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30901,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G9883U5U"],"itemData":{"id":30901,"type":"report","note":"DOI: 10.34885/172","publisher":"Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"State of the World's Plants and Fungi 2020","URL":"