,"dropping-particle":"da"},{"family":"Sampaio","given":"Paulo","dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}There are many wild plants whose roots are harvested for medicinal use. Some of the most well-known are ginseng (Panax sp.), Nardostachys grandiflora ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2339rhikkg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Ghimire, McKey, & Aumeeruddy-Thomas, 2005)","plainCitation":"(S. Ghimire, McKey, & Aumeeruddy-Thomas, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31000,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M4Y29NQT"],"itemData":{"id":31000,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2005.02.005","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"4","page":"463-475","title":"Conservation of Himalayan medicinal plants: Harvesting patterns and ecology of two threatened species, Nardostachys grandiflora DC. and Neopicrorhiza scrophulariiflora (Pennell) Hong","volume":"124","author":[{"family":"Ghimire","given":"Suresh"},{"family":"McKey","given":"Doyle"},{"family":"Aumeeruddy-Thomas","given":"Yildiz"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Oshá (Ligusticum porteri) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1if2nlo4jg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kindscher, Martin, & Long, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Kindscher, Martin, & Long, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33999,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BYL4M64W"],"itemData":{"id":33999,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-019-09456-1","ISSN":"0013-0001","page":"1–16","title":"The Sustainable Harvest of Wild Populations of Oshá (Ligusticum porteri) in Southern Colorado for the Herbal Products Trade","author":[{"family":"Kindscher","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Martin","given":"Leanne M."},{"family":"Long","given":"Quinn"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Black Cohosh (Actaea racemosa L.) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a12thg5dcir","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Small, Chamberlain, & Mathews, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Small, Chamberlain, & Mathews, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31005,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F99M8G2U"],"itemData":{"id":31005,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The American Midland Naturalist","DOI":"10.1674/0003-0031-166.2.339","ISSN":"0003-0031","issue":"2","page":"339–348","title":"Recovery of Black Cohosh (Actaea racemosa L.) Following Experimental Harvests","volume":"166","author":[{"family":"Small","given":"Christine J."},{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"James L."},{"family":"Mathews","given":"Derrick S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Cryptolepis sanguinolenta ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2jum5shrqh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Amissah, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Amissah, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":173,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PVIDGHMA"],"itemData":{"id":173,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Research on Medicinal and Aromatic Plants","DOI":"10.1016/j.jarmap.2015.12.005","ISSN":"2214-7861","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Res Medicinal Aromatic Plants","page":"34-41","title":"Genetic diversity and cryptolepine concentration of Cryptolepis sanguinolenta (Lindl). Schlt. from selected regions of Ghana","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Amissah","given":"J. N."},{"family":"Spiller","given":"M."},{"family":"Oppong","given":"A."},{"family":"Osei-Safo","given":"D."},{"family":"Owusu-Darko","given":"R."},{"family":"Debener","given":"T."},{"family":"Danquah","given":"E. Y."},{"family":"Addae-Mensah","given":"I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Stemona tuberosa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2h37mh60l4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. Chen, Sun, Wang, Kongkiatpaiboon, & Cai, 2019)","plainCitation":"(G. Chen, Sun, Wang, Kongkiatpaiboon, & Cai, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30721,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EE56FMFI"],"itemData":{"id":30721,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-018-1648-1","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","page":"213–227","title":"Conserving threatened widespread species: a case study using a traditional medicinal plant in Asia","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Chen","given":"Gao"},{"family":"Sun","given":"Weibang"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Xiaoxun"},{"family":"Kongkiatpaiboon","given":"Sumet"},{"family":"Cai","given":"Xianghai"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Eurycoma longifolia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2gcbo5hsp6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Susilowati, Rachmat, Elfiati, & Hasibuan, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Susilowati, Rachmat, Elfiati, & Hasibuan, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":989,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZJP5AHL3"],"itemData":{"id":989,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversitas Journal of Biological Diversity","DOI":"10.13057/biodiv/d200215","ISSN":"1412-033X","issue":"2","page":"413-418","title":"The composition and diversity of plant species in pasak bumi’s (Eurycoma longifolia) habitat in Batang Lubu Sutam forest, North Sumatra, Indonesia","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Susilowati","given":"ARIDA"},{"family":"Rachmat","given":"HENTI"},{"family":"Elfiati","given":"DENI"},{"family":"Hasibuan","given":"HABIBI M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The gathering of major parts of wild plants such as stems and bulbs is also common in herbaceous plants like orchids. These types of gathering activities may kill the plant and are therefore a focus for species conservation and sustainable management efforts. Sustainable harvest programs for gathering flowers, fruit and leaves for medicinal use have secondary benefits for habitat protection. 
      To study the sustainability of the use and gathering of wild plants a literature review was conducted and studies on the ecological aspect of specific species were collected, based on the parts gathered and the life form of plants used. Note that separate literature reviews were conducted on the sustainable use of wild fungi and for urban gathering (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). For the literature review on wild plants, in accordance with the requirements of the systematic literature review, key word combinations were used such as: #gather/pick/collect# + #plant# + #wild# + #terms of the aim of uses + sustainable# and searched primarily in google scholar, Web of Science SCI (Science Citation Index Expanded) and CNKI (China National Knowledge Infrastructure). A total of 89,400 materials were identified, but most only described how the wild plants were used. Eight hundred and fourteen (814) relevant articles and reports went through the initial screening. Fifty-one (51) cases of specific plant species or groups met the search criteria for inclusion in the study of sustainable use by gathering. The relevant papers were carefully reviewed to determine the credibility of the conclusions of each set of research (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Cases of sustainable use by gathering wild plants are from all IPBES regions, including Africa (13), America (21), Asia and Pacific (11) and Europe and central Asia (6). Of the 51 cases of the use and gathering of wild plants retrieved, more than two-third of tree/shrub gathering were sustainably managed, while more than half of the gathered herbs assessed were considered unsustainable. For trees, the existing cases show that the gathering of bark for uses, mainly medicinal aims, are not sustainable due to the lack of management and regulatory systems. For herbs, gathering root for medicines from perennial herbs led to more unsustainability concerns (Table 3.6).
      
