      In Europe, changing patterns in wild plant and fungi use vary by country and region, associated with changing lifestyles, urbanization, large-scale farming, less periods of famine and economic hardship in recent years and changing outdoor recreation patterns. At the same time, large increases in immigrant populations are affecting what is harvested, by whom and for what purposes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1vob65oi8o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u321{}uczaj, 2012)","plainCitation":"(?uczaj, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U6XCGXXT"],"itemData":{"id":30765,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The aim of this review is to present an overview of changes in the contemporary use of wild food plants in Europe, mainly using the examples of our home countries: Poland, Italy, Spain, Estonia and Sweden. We set the scene referring to the nutrition of 19th century peasants, involving many famine and emergency foods. Later we discuss such issues as children's wild snacks, the association between the decline of plant knowledge and the disappearance of plant use, the effects of over-exploitation, the decrease of the availability of plants due to ecosystem changes, land access rights for foragers and intoxication dangers. We also describe the 20th and 21st century vogues in wild plant use, particularly their shift into the domain of haute-cuisine.","container-title":"Acta Societatis Botanicorum Poloniae","DOI":"10.5586/asbp.2012.031","ISSN":"2083-9480","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"Copyright (c) 2012 ?ukasz ?uczaj, Andrea Pieroni, Javier Tardío, Manuel Pardo-de-Santayana, Renata Sõukand, Ingvar Svanberg, Raivo Kalle","note":"number: 4","page":"359-370","source":"pbsociety.org.pl","title":"Wild food plant use in 21st century Europe: the disappearance of old traditions and the search for new cuisines involving wild edibles","title-short":"Wild food plant use in 21st century Europe","URL":"https://pbsociety.org.pl/journals/index.php/asbp/article/view/asbp.2012.031","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"?uczaj","given":"?ukasz"},{"family":"Pieroni","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Tardío","given":"Javier"},{"family":"Pardo-de-Santayana","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Sõukand","given":"Renata"},{"family":"Svanberg","given":"Ingvar"},{"family":"Kalle","given":"Raivo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",11,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In France, 728 algae, fungi and plants species are extracted from the wild, of which 100 are commonly used ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a7h2e66pib","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lescure, Th\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}venin, Garreta, & Morisson, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Lescure, Thévenin, Garreta, & Morisson, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":928,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZTISC2NP"],"itemData":{"id":928,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The ecologist, ethnologist and gatherers, authors of this work have established an almost exhaustive list of plants subject to commercial pickings in mainland France. 728 species or subspecies are thus listed, and forest species or those frequently associated with forest processions represent a quarter of them.\\n\\nAmong the 74 species cited by more than half of the collectors surveyed, more than half can be found in the forest.\\n\\nFor all species, the status \\"wild\\" and / or \\"cultivated\\" is specified. The authors also informed for all species whether they benefited from protection measures and the number of departments concerned.","container-title":"Le Monde des Plantes","page":"19-39","title":"Les plantes faisant l’objet de cueillettes commerciales sur le territoire métropolitain. Une liste commentée","URL":"https://oppla.eu/casestudy/20464","volume":"517","author":[{"family":"Lescure","given":"J.P."},{"family":"Thévenin","given":"T."},{"family":"Garreta","given":"R."},{"family":"Morisson","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Recent research in Norway found a total of 273 wild edible plants from 67 botanical families were identified by collectors, with the majority of harvested material coming from seven families and ten taxa. Fruits and berries, leaves and flowers were the most popular and important plant parts that were foraged by study respondents ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1vfrpt7e7o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Giraud, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Giraud, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":911,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IZHJDX76"],"itemData":{"id":911,"type":"thesis","abstract":"Globally, the role of wild edible plants (WEPs) in providing culturally appropriate nutrition and food security, added economic value, and ecological benefits, is now well recognized. In Europe, the use of WEPs appears both as an ancient and on-going subsistence activity, but also as an emerging trend in high-end gastronomy and luxury foods. Yet, WEPs remain underutilized and mostly neglected, and the conservation of knowledge systems and practices associated to them is threatened. It is also unknown if there are any threats on the plant populations through harvesting. In Norway, overharvesting of some wild edible species has been observed recently around urban centres. Although research has been conducted on the documentation of traditional knowledge associated to WEPs, little is known about how new foraging practices affect biological diversity. The purpose of this study was to investigate if and when foraging activities in Norway can be unsustainable and what foraging practices may threaten plant communities. Different socio-cultural constructs around WEP harvesting were analysed, notably comparing chefs and professional foragers with amateurs. In collaboration with the Norwegian Association for Mycology and Foraging, ethnographic methods were used and 19 key stakeholders were interviewed within the Norwegian foraging community including foragers, chefs, association leaders, and conservation experts. Ethnobotanical data was collected through an online questionnaire (219 responses mainly from amateur foragers), and combined with available information on species’ ecology and their conservation status to assess foraging impact on WEPs. Results show that foraging WEPs in Norway poses no immediate threat to plant conservation, yet risks exist. These are discussed in the context of developing local guidelines for the sustainable use of WEPs in Norway in a participatory manner.  Keywords: Biocultural Diversity ?Foraging ?Sustainability ?New Nordic Food Movements ?Wild Edible Plants","call-number":"Esub45","language":"English","publisher":"Norwegian University of Life Sciences (NMBU)    and    Institut supérieur d'agriculture Rhône-Alpes (ISARA-Lyon)","title":"Sustainable foraging of wild edible plants in Norway: A Biocultural Approach","author":[{"family":"Giraud","given":"Nicolas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,23]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Research suggests dozens to hundreds of wild algae, fungi and plants are gathered in urban, rural, and wilderness ecosystems throughout the continental United States of America, Alaska, Hawai'i and United States of America territories. Of these, a small subset enters into large-scale trade with maple syrup (Acer sp.), wild blueberries (Vaccinium sp.), and medicinal species such as American ginseng (Panax quinquefolius) noteworthy among them. Estimates of the number of United States of America residents who gather at least occasionally range from 18% to 36%, with the vast majority (>80%) gathering for personal use only. It seems likely, then, that a majority of United States of America residents who gather do so for personal use, while a few species gathered for commercial purposes account for the majority of biomass removed. Wild algae, fungi and plants gathering plays an important cultural role for many indigenous peoples and local communities in the United States of America including, but not confined to, those formally recognized as indigenous. Rights of access to wild algae, fungi and plants for subsistence purposes are provided for by law in the United States of America’s state of Alaska (for rural residents of that state), Hawai’i (for Native Hawaiians), and under the terms of many treaties between tribes and the federal government ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2eai2dgepe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chamberlain, Emery, & Patel-Weynand, 2018; Cordell, 2012; M. R. Emery & Pierce, 2005; M. R. Emery, Pierce, & Schroeder, 2004; Hurley, Grabbatin, Goetcheus, & Halfacre, 2013; Robbins, Emery, & Rice, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Chamberlain, Emery, & Patel-Weynand, 2018; Cordell, 2012; M. R. Emery & Pierce, 2005; M. R. Emery, Pierce, & Schroeder, 2004; Hurley, Grabbatin, Goetcheus, & Halfacre, 2013; Robbins, Emery, & Rice, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30752,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PQNPRAMM"],"itemData":{"id":30752,"type":"report","collection-title":"General Technical Report SRS-GTR-232","language":"English","page":"267","publisher":"USDA Forest Service, Southern Research Station","title":"Assessment of Nontimber Forest Products in the United States Under Changing Conditions. A report for the United Sates Department of Agriculture","URL":"https://doi.org/10.2737/SRS-GTR-232","author":[{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"James. L."},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R."},{"family":"Patel-Weynand","given":"Toral"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6]]}}},{"id":965,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JKZYQ3CJ"],"itemData":{"id":965,"type":"report","event-place":"Newtown Square, PA","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.2737/NRS-GTR-100","number":"NRS-GTR-100","page":"NRS-GTR-100","publisher":"U.S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service, Northern Research Station","publisher-place":"Newtown Square, PA","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Outdoor Recreation in the Northern United States","URL":"https://www.fs.usda.gov/treesearch/pubs/41528","author":[{"family":"Cordell","given":"H. Ken"},{"family":"Betz","given":"Carter J."},{"family":"Mou","given":"Shela H."},{"family":"Gormanson","given":"Dale D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":34123,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FC3WS7MW","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P9FRQ7IL"],"itemData":{"id":34123,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"People continue to hunt, fish, trap, and gather for subsistence purposes in the contemporary United States. This fact has implications for forest policy, as suggested by an international convention on temperate and boreal forests, commonly known as the Montréal Process. Three canons of law provide a legal basis for subsistence activities by designated social groups in Alaska and Hawaii and by American Indians with treaty rights in the coterminous forty-eight states. A literature review also presents evidence of such practices by people from a variety of ethnic backgrounds throughout the nation. Teleological notions of development espoused by both neoliberal and Marxist scholars suggest that subsistence activities should not persist in a First World setting except as failures of the officially sanctioned economic system. However, alternative economic perspectives from peasant studies and economic geography offer a conceptual framework for viewing at least some subsistence activities as having a logic and values outside of, if articulated with, market structures. Meeting the Montréal Process goal of providing for subsistence use of forests will require research focused on local practices and terms of access to resources as well as their relationship to state and capital processes. We outline the basics of a research agenda on subsistence for an emerging First World political ecology.","container-title":"Environment and Planning A: Economy and Space","DOI":"10.1068/a36263","ISSN":"0308-518X, 1472-3409","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Environ Plan A","language":"en","page":"981-993","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Interrupting the Telos: Locating Subsistence in Contemporary US Forests","title-short":"Interrupting the Telos","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1068/a36263","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R."},{"family":"Pierce","given":"Alan R"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",6]]}}},{"id":984,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3JDTW8S7"],"itemData":{"id":984,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"In: Darr, David R., coord. Data report: a supplement of the national report on sustainable forests-2003. FS-766A. Washington, DC: U.S. Department of Agriculture.","language":"en","source":"www.fs.usda.gov","title":"Criterion 6, indicator 47 area and percent of forest land used for subsistence purposes","URL":"https://www.fs.usda.gov/treesearch/pubs/18428","author":[{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R."},{"family":"Pierce","given":"Alan R."},{"family":"Schroeder","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":963,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I3VX5L8T"],"itemData":{"id":963,"type":"chapter","container-title":"African Ethnobotany in the Americas","event-place":"New York, NY","ISBN":"978-1-4614-0835-2","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-1-4614-0836-9_6","page":"153-173","publisher":"Springer New York","publisher-place":"New York, NY","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Gathering, Buying, and Growing Sweetgrass (Muhlenbergia sericea): Urbanization and Social Networking in the Sweetgrass Basket-Making Industry of Lowcountry South Carolina","title-short":"Gathering, Buying, and Growing Sweetgrass (Muhlenbergia sericea)","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-1-4614-0836-9_6","editor":[{"family":"Voeks","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Rashford","given":"John"}],"author":[{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T."},{"family":"Grabbatin","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Goetcheus","given":"Cari"},{"family":"Halfacre","given":"Angela"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":960,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XVBDGLWD"],"itemData":{"id":960,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Area","DOI":"10.1111/j.1475-4762.2008.00794.x","ISSN":"0004-0894, 1475-4762","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Area","language":"en","page":"265-277","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Gathering in Thoreau's backyard: nontimber forest product harvesting as practice","title-short":"Gathering in Thoreau's backyard","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1475-4762.2008.00794.x","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Robbins","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla"},{"family":"Rice","given":"Jennifer L"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Gatherers have different identities and sources of knowledge in gathering networks. For example, in Austria, organic certification for wild plants has been issued to three types of gatherers: regular, diversified and single-plant gatherers. Among them, regular gatherers are the principal knowledge sources of traditional and local knowledge, and the diversified gatherers who are less common and learning knowledge from formal courses or self-learning, may be more worried by the loss of traditional knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2dqgbo4hf9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schunko & Vogl, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Schunko & Vogl, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33054,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H56TNN47"],"itemData":{"id":33054,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"European countries are split over the appreciation of wild berries, fruits, mushrooms, and herbs. While some countries provide public statistics on wild plants, others seem to neglect wild plant gathering and commercialization. In this study, we aimed to understand if wild plant commercialization is neglected or irrelevant in Austria, a country that does not provide statistics. We focus on organic producers, because organic certification of wild plant gathering might have potential for countering frequent concerns about commercial gathering, including destructive gathering and overharvesting. Using a mixed-methods approach with a concurrent triangulation design, databases of six organic certification bodies were analysed concurrently with semi-structured expert interviews of their representatives. We found that organic certification for gathering was issued to 1.5% of organic producers in the year 2016 in Austria and is relevant for three distinct gatherer types: regular, diversified, and single-plant gatherers. Organic gathering is most frequently part of agricultural or horticultural farms and rarely an isolated commercial activity. It is related to mixed farming, deepening on-farm diversification, and contributes to maintaining traditions, as well as the local socio-ecological memory of wild plant products. Organic wild plants are directly marketed to consumers as traditional and innovative products, but also supplied to mass markets. We conclude that from a socio-cultural perspective and a focus on regional economies, organic gathering is neglected in Austria, whereas from an income perspective, wild plant gathering seems to be indeed relevant for few organic producers, although exhibiting potential.","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"11","page":"1-14","title":"Is the Commercialization of Wild Plants by Organic Producers in Austria Neglected or Irrelevant?","URL":"https://ideas.repec.org/a/gam/jsusta/v10y2018i11p3989-d179612.html","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Schunko","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Vogl","given":"Christian R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In France, present professional gatherers are of multiple origins, urban or rural, and hold their knowledge from different sources. They care for the sustainability of the plants and ecosystems more than occasional opportunistic gatherers. Through their associations or cooperatives, they establish rules of good gathering practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"arsqsa15sg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lescure, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Lescure, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":928,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZTISC2NP"],"itemData":{"id":928,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The ecologist, ethnologist and gatherers, authors of this work have established an almost exhaustive list of plants subject to commercial pickings in mainland France. 728 species or subspecies are thus listed, and forest species or those frequently associated with forest processions represent a quarter of them.\\n\\nAmong the 74 species cited by more than half of the collectors surveyed, more than half can be found in the forest.\\n\\nFor all species, the status \\"wild\\" and / or \\"cultivated\\" is specified. The authors also informed for all species whether they benefited from protection measures and the number of departments concerned.","container-title":"Le Monde des Plantes","page":"19-39","title":"Les plantes faisant l’objet de cueillettes commerciales sur le territoire métropolitain. Une liste commentée","URL":"https://oppla.eu/casestudy/20464","volume":"517","author":[{"family":"Lescure","given":"J.P."},{"family":"Thévenin","given":"T."},{"family":"Garreta","given":"R."},{"family":"Morisson","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ogobhtl40","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Julliand, Pinton, Garreta, & Lescure, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Julliand, Pinton, Garreta, & Lescure, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":962,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9VLNUEUN"],"itemData":{"id":962,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EchoGéo","DOI":"10.4000/echogeo.16987","ISSN":"1963-1197","issue":"47","journalAbbreviation":"echogeo","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Normaliser le sauvage : l'expérience française des cueilleurs professionnels","title-short":"Normaliser le sauvage","URL":"http://journals.openedition.org/echogeo/16987","author":[{"family":"Julliand","given":"Claire"},{"family":"Pinton","given":"Florence"},{"family":"Garreta","given":"Raphaële"},{"family":"Lescure","given":"Jean-Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,21]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
3.3.2.2.2. Urban gathering

Urban gathering is an activity which supports biodiversity and sustainable human-nature interactions, but it is under-recognized as a global activity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a27p2bhs4d6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. J. McLain, 2012; A. Russo, Escobedo, Cirella, & Zerbe, 2017; Tiwary, Vilhar, Zhiyanski, Stojanovski, & Dinca, 2020)","plainCitation":"(R. J. McLain, 2012; A. Russo, Escobedo, Cirella, & Zerbe, 2017; Tiwary, Vilhar, Zhiyanski, Stojanovski, & Dinca, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":950,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YHVD6VPJ"],"itemData":{"id":950,"type":"report","event-place":"Portland, OR","language":"en","number":"PNW-GTR-849","page":"PNW-GTR-849","publisher":"U.S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service, Pacific Northwest Research Station","publisher-place":"Portland, OR","title":"Gathering in the city: an annotated bibliography and review of the literature about human-plant interactions in urban ecosystems","title-short":"Gathering in the city","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"R. J."},{"family":"MacFarland","given":"K."},{"family":"Brody","given":"L."},{"family":"Hebert","given":"J."},{"family":"Hurley","given":"P."},{"family":"Poe","given":"M."},{"family":"Buttolph","given":"L. P."},{"family":"Gabriel","given":"N."},{"family":"Dzuna","given":"M."},{"family":"Emery","given":"M. R."},{"family":"Charnley","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":926,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WTXR89BV"],"itemData":{"id":926,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Recently published green infrastructure, nature-based solutions, and ecosystem disservices (ED) literature have focused primarily on the supply of urban regulating and cultural ecosystem services (ES). Other literature on urban and peri-urban agriculture has mostly studied the role of localized, intensive agricultural practices in providing food to inhabitants. The aim of this review is to raise awareness and stress the knowledge gap on the importance of urban provisioning ES, particularly when implementing an edible green infrastructure (EGI) approach as it can o?er improved resilience and quality of life in cities. We compiled and systematically analyzed studies on urban ES and ED related to a number of EGI typologies. Our systematic review of the relevant literature via an EGI framework, identi?ed more than 80 peer-reviewed publications that focused on ES and food production in urban areas. An EGI approach can contribute socially, economically, and environmentally to urban sustainability and food security. However, such bene?ts must be weighed against ED trade-o?s, including: potential health risks caused by human exposure to heavy metals and organic chemical contaminants often present in urban surroundings. We conclude with recommendations and guidelines for incorporating EGI into urban planning and design, and discuss novel areas for future research.","container-title":"Agriculture, Ecosystems & Environment","DOI":"10.1016/j.agee.2017.03.026","ISSN":"01678809","journalAbbreviation":"Agriculture, Ecosystems & Environment","language":"en","page":"53-66","title":"Edible green infrastructure: An approach and review of provisioning ecosystem services and disservices in urban environments","title-short":"Edible green infrastructure","volume":"242","author":[{"family":"Russo","given":"Alessio"},{"family":"Escobedo","given":"Francisco J."},{"family":"Cirella","given":"Giuseppe T."},{"family":"Zerbe","given":"Stefan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":938,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UWW6782I"],"itemData":{"id":938,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The role of the urban common (i.e. shared space and resources) in sustainable provisioning of goods and services to city dwellers is discussed in this paper. Focusing on tree-based green infrastructure, the study scope includes three categories of provisioning (woody biomass, food/fibre, and non-timber forest products, i.e. NTFPs), alongside three categories of supporting services (fresh water replenishment, soil nutrient restoration, building preservation). As a first step, prospects of utilizing the urban common as facilitator of nature-based solution to the earmarked provisioning services are evaluated through dedicated literature survey and expert elicitation on perceived impact of environmental change triggers and management interventions (planning and/or governance). This is followed by a structured review of the state of affairs in four European cities (London, Amsterdam, Sofia, Ljubljana), representing different macro-geographical regions with distinct socio-economic drivers in managing these provisioning services. The pan-European expert elicitation exercise noted active management of the urban common as positively impacting on the performance of the majority of provisioning services, while environmental change impacts were found to be overriding and adversely influencing the provisioning of material resources (mainly NTFPs and woody biomass). The four-city case study highlighted some regional peculiarities in connecting the city dwellers to the urban common and identified the need to overcome socio-cultural barriers for enhancing pan-European best practice sharing in the management of goods and services provisioning. This is deemed essential to pave way for an emerging perspective on sustainable utilization of the urban common as an enabler for nature-based solution, making it fit for purpose in meeting the astronomical demands of future urban living.","container-title":"Urban Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1007/s11252-020-00951-1","ISSN":"1083-8155, 1573-1642","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Ecosyst","language":"en","page":"645-657","title":"Management of nature-based goods and services provisioning from the urban common: a pan-European perspective","title-short":"Management of nature-based goods and services provisioning from the urban common","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Tiwary","given":"Abhishek"},{"family":"Vilhar","given":"Ursa"},{"family":"Zhiyanski","given":"Miglena"},{"family":"Stojanovski","given":"Vladimir"},{"family":"Dinca","given":"Lucian"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urban gathering promotes positive cultural, ecological, economic, and health outcomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"agtekc1svf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Shackleton, Hurley, Dahlberg, Emery, & Nagendra, 2017; Synk, 2017)","plainCitation":"(C. Shackleton, Hurley, Dahlberg, Emery, & Nagendra, 2017; Synk, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33678,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ENAQFCLB"],"itemData":{"id":33678,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Although hardly noticed or formally recognised, urban foraging by humans probably occurs in all urban settings around the world. We draw from research in India, South Africa, Sweden, and the United States to demonstrate the ubiquity and varied nature of urban foraging in different contexts. Across these different contexts, we distil seven themes that characterise and thereby advance thinking about research and the understanding of urban foraging. We show that it is widespread and occurs across a variety of urban spaces and places. The species used and the local practices vary between contexts, and are in constant ?ux as urban ecological and social settings change. This requires that urban foragers are knowledgeable about diverse species, harvest locations, and rights of access, and that their practices are adaptable to changing contexts. Despite its ubiquity, most cities have some forms of regulations that prohibit or discourage urban foraging. We highlight a few important exceptions that can provide prototypes and lessons for other cities regarding supportive policy frameworks and initiatives. The formulation of dynamic policy, design, and management strategies in support of urban foraging will bene?t from understanding the common characteristics of foraging in cities worldwide, but also will require comprehension of the speci?c and dynamic contexts in which they would be implemented.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su9101884","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"10","language":"en","page":"1884","title":"Urban Foraging: A Ubiquitous Human Practice Overlooked by Urban Planners, Policy, and Research","title-short":"Urban Foraging","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Dahlberg","given":"Annika"},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",10,20]]}}},{"id":932,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VURR83Q6"],"itemData":{"id":932,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As a component of urban food systems, foraging—the collection of plant or fungal materials, such as berries and nuts, not deliberately cultivated for human use—may promote positive cultural, ecological, economic, and health outcomes. Foraging behaviors, motivations, and barriers in the urban context remain under-characterized despite emerging literature on the subject.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2017.10.007","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"97-102","title":"Gathering Baltimore’s bounty: Characterizing behaviors, motivations, and barriers of foragers in an urban ecosystem","title-short":"Gathering Baltimore’s bounty","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Synk","given":"Colleen M."},{"family":"Kim","given":"Brent F."},{"family":"Davis","given":"Charles A."},{"family":"Harding","given":"James"},{"family":"Rogers","given":"Virginia"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T."},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R."},{"family":"Nachman","given":"Keeve E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As a global phenomenon, it provides three categories of provisioning (woody biomass, food/fibre, and non-timber forest products), and it supports a ‘green economy’ ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2qga0k7r48","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Shackleton, Chinyimba, Hebinck, Shackleton, & Kaoma, 2015; Tiwary, 2020)","plainCitation":"(S. Shackleton, Chinyimba, Hebinck, Shackleton, & Kaoma, 2015; Tiwary, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":952,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F4C7GJ9X"],"itemData":{"id":952,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","DOI":"10.1016/j.landurbplan.2014.12.004","ISSN":"01692046","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape and Urban Planning","language":"en","page":"76-86","title":"Multiple benefits and values of trees in urban landscapes in two towns in northern South Africa","volume":"136","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Sheona"},{"family":"Chinyimba","given":"Abby"},{"family":"Hebinck","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Kaoma","given":"Humphrey"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":938,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UWW6782I"],"itemData":{"id":938,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The role of the urban common (i.e. shared space and resources) in sustainable provisioning of goods and services to city dwellers is discussed in this paper. Focusing on tree-based green infrastructure, the study scope includes three categories of provisioning (woody biomass, food/fibre, and non-timber forest products, i.e. NTFPs), alongside three categories of supporting services (fresh water replenishment, soil nutrient restoration, building preservation). As a first step, prospects of utilizing the urban common as facilitator of nature-based solution to the earmarked provisioning services are evaluated through dedicated literature survey and expert elicitation on perceived impact of environmental change triggers and management interventions (planning and/or governance). This is followed by a structured review of the state of affairs in four European cities (London, Amsterdam, Sofia, Ljubljana), representing different macro-geographical regions with distinct socio-economic drivers in managing these provisioning services. The pan-European expert elicitation exercise noted active management of the urban common as positively impacting on the performance of the majority of provisioning services, while environmental change impacts were found to be overriding and adversely influencing the provisioning of material resources (mainly NTFPs and woody biomass). The four-city case study highlighted some regional peculiarities in connecting the city dwellers to the urban common and identified the need to overcome socio-cultural barriers for enhancing pan-European best practice sharing in the management of goods and services provisioning. This is deemed essential to pave way for an emerging perspective on sustainable utilization of the urban common as an enabler for nature-based solution, making it fit for purpose in meeting the astronomical demands of future urban living.","container-title":"Urban Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1007/s11252-020-00951-1","ISSN":"1083-8155, 1573-1642","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Ecosyst","language":"en","page":"645-657","title":"Management of nature-based goods and services provisioning from the urban common: a pan-European perspective","title-short":"Management of nature-based goods and services provisioning from the urban common","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Tiwary","given":"Abhishek"},{"family":"Vilhar","given":"Ursa"},{"family":"Zhiyanski","given":"Miglena"},{"family":"Stojanovski","given":"Vladimir"},{"family":"Dinca","given":"Lucian"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Of the 43 studies related to urban gathering retrieved for this assessment, 70% are from the Americas, Europe and Central Asia, 20% are from Africa, and the remaining are from Asia and the Pacific. Common characteristics of gathering, such as health risks, ecological conditions, and pressures on wild algae, fungi and plants species are likely not the same between in rural and urban contexts, making further research on urban gathering a knowledge gap on the sustainable use of wild species for nature’s contributions to people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ajneldk173","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. K. Fischer & Kowarik, 2020; Rupprecht, Byrne, Garden, & Hero, 2015; C. Shackleton, 2017; Short Gianotti & Hurley, 2016)","plainCitation":"(L. K. Fischer & Kowarik, 2020; Rupprecht, Byrne, Garden, & Hero, 2015; C. Shackleton, 2017; Short Gianotti & Hurley, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":941,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3Y9K2TNY"],"itemData":{"id":941,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As global population becomes increasingly urban, opportunities for people to experience nature have decreased. Counteracting this trend is a key challenge for future urban development as interactions of urban people with biodiversity support human health and wellbeing, and may also result in positive attitudes towards biodiversity conservation. Collecting edible plants in urban surroundings, especially outside of gardens (“urban foraging”) is a traditional interaction with nature, based on knowledge about multiple uses of plants. Although some studies exist from different cities around the world, urban foraging has been revealed as a critically understudied phenomenon. We now analyze (i) the relevance of this human-nature interaction in Berlin, one of Europe’s metropolises, (ii) how people’s sociocultural background matters in attitudes of urban foragers vs. non-foragers towards this activity, and (iii) whether urban foraging may lead to conflicts with biodiversity conservation. Our survey revealed urban foraging as a relevant human-nature interaction with a high potential to grow: 33% of 535 respondents already collected edible plants outside of gardens and another 38% would be doing so given certain conditions, e.g. when contamination risks can be excluded. Many sociocultural groups (differing, e.g. on gender, age, childhood experience) shared attitudes towards foraging and existing barriers. Risks to biodiversity seem to be manageable as neither native species nor rare species were over-foraged in relation to species’ abundance in the local flora, with more abundant species being collected more frequently. We conclude that urban foraging can be a powerful tool for connecting urban people to nature without putting native biodiversity at risk. We make a claim for integrated approaches towards environmental policy, environmental education and greenspace management: these should aim on keeping potential health risks at a minimum, and should support urban foraging as a biodiversity-friendly and sustainable human-nature interaction in the cities of tomorrow.","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2020.106087","ISSN":"1470160X","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Indicators","language":"en","page":"106087","title":"Connecting people to biodiversity in cities of tomorrow: Is urban foraging a powerful tool?","title-short":"Connecting people to biodiversity in cities of tomorrow","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Fischer","given":"Leonie K."},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":924,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VVNGJPW4"],"itemData":{"id":924,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2015.08.009","ISSN":"16188667","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"883-908","title":"Informal urban green space: A trilingual systematic review of its role for biodiversity and trends in the literature","title-short":"Informal urban green space","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Rupprecht","given":"Christoph D. D."},{"family":"Byrne","given":"Jason A."},{"family":"Garden","given":"Jenni G."},{"family":"Hero","given":"Jean-Marc"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":33678,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ENAQFCLB"],"itemData":{"id":33678,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Although hardly noticed or formally recognised, urban foraging by humans probably occurs in all urban settings around the world. We draw from research in India, South Africa, Sweden, and the United States to demonstrate the ubiquity and varied nature of urban foraging in different contexts. Across these different contexts, we distil seven themes that characterise and thereby advance thinking about research and the understanding of urban foraging. We show that it is widespread and occurs across a variety of urban spaces and places. The species used and the local practices vary between contexts, and are in constant ?ux as urban ecological and social settings change. This requires that urban foragers are knowledgeable about diverse species, harvest locations, and rights of access, and that their practices are adaptable to changing contexts. Despite its ubiquity, most cities have some forms of regulations that prohibit or discourage urban foraging. We highlight a few important exceptions that can provide prototypes and lessons for other cities regarding supportive policy frameworks and initiatives. The formulation of dynamic policy, design, and management strategies in support of urban foraging will bene?t from understanding the common characteristics of foraging in cities worldwide, but also will require comprehension of the speci?c and dynamic contexts in which they would be implemented.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su9101884","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"10","language":"en","page":"1884","title":"Urban Foraging: A Ubiquitous Human Practice Overlooked by Urban Planners, Policy, and Research","title-short":"Urban Foraging","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Dahlberg","given":"Annika"},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",10,20]]}}},{"id":922,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2XJIZR2I"],"itemData":{"id":922,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land Use Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.landusepol.2016.06.019","ISSN":"02648377","journalAbbreviation":"Land Use Policy","language":"en","page":"555-563","title":"Gathering plants and fungi along the urban-rural gradient: Uncovering differences in the attitudes and practices among urban, suburban, and rural landowners","title-short":"Gathering plants and fungi along the urban-rural gradient","volume":"57","author":[{"family":"Short Gianotti","given":"Anne G."},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The use of Geographic Information Systems (GIS) and spatial modelling in digital platforms and apps shows promise in quantifying urban natures as baselines for this additional research ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a25e8v2o79f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arrington, 2021; Moss, Voigt, & Becker, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Arrington, 2021; Moss, Voigt, & Becker, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":920,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MMAVNVT3"],"itemData":{"id":920,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2020.126896","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126896","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Urban foraging of five non-native plants in NYC: Balancing ecosystem services and invasive species management","title-short":"Urban foraging of five non-native plants in NYC","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1618866720307135","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Arrington","given":"Austin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3]]}}},{"id":942,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IW2S73EA"],"itemData":{"id":942,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"City","DOI":"10.1080/13604813.2021.1935513","ISSN":"1360-4813, 1470-3629","issue":"3-4","journalAbbreviation":"City","language":"en","page":"255-276","title":"Digital urban nature: Probing a void in the smart city discourse","title-short":"Digital urban nature","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Moss","given":"Timothy"},{"family":"Voigt","given":"Friederike"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Sören"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",7,4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Dozens to hundreds of feral and wild plant and fungi species are gathered for food, medicine, firewood, decoration, and cultural practices in urban ecosystems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1u00l08b25","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kaoma & Shackleton, 2015; Landor-Yamagata, Kowarik, & Fischer, 2018; \\\\uc0\\\\u321{}uczaj, Wilde, & Townsend, 2021; R. J. McLain, 2012; R. J. McLain, Poe, Urgenson, Blahna, & Buttolph, 2017; Palliwoda, Kowarik, & von der Lippe, 2017; M. R. Poe, LeCompte, McLain, & Hurley, 2014; C. Shackleton, 2017; S. Shackleton, 2015; Somesh, Rao, Murali, & Nagendra, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Kaoma & Shackleton, 2015; Landor-Yamagata, Kowarik, & Fischer, 2018; ?uczaj, Wilde, & Townsend, 2021; R. J. McLain, 2012; R. J. McLain, Poe, Urgenson, Blahna, & Buttolph, 2017; Palliwoda, Kowarik, & von der Lippe, 2017; M. R. Poe, LeCompte, McLain, & Hurley, 2014; C. Shackleton, 2017; S. Shackleton, 2015; Somesh, Rao, Murali, & Nagendra, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":953,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P2QST5TY"],"itemData":{"id":953,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Valuation of the contribution of non-timber forest products (NTFPs) to household incomes has been well researched in the rural and remote areas of the developing world. In comparison, there has been little investigation of the contribution of NTFPs in urban areas and amongst the urban poor. This paper reports results from a survey of 450 households across three towns on the use and value of tree NTFPs collected by households in the poorer areas from their own homesteads, open spaces within towns and at the urban periphery. Collection (and purchase) of tree NTFPs was widespread, especially of ?rewood and fruits. The ratio collected from homesteads relative to other urban spaces differed between products and the amount required. Overall, approximately 20% of household income was derived from urban tree NTFPs. The highest contribution (33%) was amongst the poorest sectors represented by mostly recent migrants to towns who were living in informal settlements whilst trying to establish a foothold in the urban economy. In the formal housing areas the contribution was at least 14%, which has been overlooked by standard income surveys in urban areas.","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2015.08.005","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy Econ","language":"en","page":"104-112","title":"The direct-use value of urban tree non-timber forest products to household income in poorer suburbs in South African towns","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Kaoma","given":"Humphrey"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":931,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V2X4DZN4"],"itemData":{"id":931,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Gathering wild plants in cities (urban foraging) is likely an important, but understudied human-nature interaction globally. As large European cities are critically understudied in this regard, we performed in-depth ethnography-based interviews in Berlin, Germany, to shed light on the cultural background of foragers, their motivations and which plants and fungi are gathered for which purposes. Results demonstrate multiple uses of 125 taxa, mostly frequently-occurring species but also some Red List species, from a range of formal and informal greenspace types. Both native and non-native species were gathered, with signi?cant differences in use patterns. Use for food was most common, followed by medicinal uses, and personal enjoyment was a frequent motivation, indicating that urban foraging combines provisioning and cultural ecosystem services. Familial and childhood foraging exposure were common, pointing to in?uences of early-in-life exposure on later-in-life activities and transgenerational aspects of the practice. Results further suggest legacy effects from the post-war and communist eras on foraging knowledge. Although non-commercial foraging is allowed in Berlin, over-harvesting was not evident. Interviews indicate that stewardship of urban biodiversity is common among foragers. Results thus suggest considering urban foraging as a promising vehicle for linking humans with nature when developing a biodiverse urban green infrastructure.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su10061873","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1873","title":"Urban Foraging in Berlin: People, Plants and Practices within the Metropolitan Green Infrastructure","title-short":"Urban Foraging in Berlin","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Landor-Yamagata","given":"Jonah"},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"},{"family":"Fischer","given":"Leonie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,4]]}}},{"id":937,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N8FQZKRQ"],"itemData":{"id":937,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Foraging in the British Isles is an increasingly popular activity for both personal consumption and for commercial purposes. While legislation and guidelines exist regulating the sustainable collection of wild edibles, the founding principles of the British foraging movement are not well documented. For this research, 36 of the most active foraging instructors of the Association of Foragers were interviewed to understand their background, species collected, sources of knowledge, and problems faced during collection. Altogether, 102 species of leafy vegetables, fruits, fungi, and seaweeds were mentioned as frequently used, while 34 species of roadkill animals were listed, mostly for personal consumption. Instructors reported learning from wild food guidebooks, other foragers, or personal experience. Frequent contact among foragers has led to the standardisation of knowledge and practices among them forming a “new tradition”, partly based on old British traditions but modi?ed by in?uences from other countries and cultures, both in terms of choice of species and processing techniques. Contrary to expectations, foragers rarely reported clashes with nature conservation or forestry managers. The authors argue that knowledge and practice developed by the Association of Foragers (AoF) are sustainable and could be integrated into the British food and nature conservation system.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su13063478","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"3478","title":"The Ethnobiology of Contemporary British Foragers: Foods They Teach, Their Sources of Inspiration and Impact","title-short":"The Ethnobiology of Contemporary British Foragers","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"?uczaj","given":"?ukasz"},{"family":"Wilde","given":"Monica"},{"family":"Townsend","given":"Leanne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,21]]}}},{"id":950,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YHVD6VPJ"],"itemData":{"id":950,"type":"report","event-place":"Portland, OR","language":"en","number":"PNW-GTR-849","page":"PNW-GTR-849","publisher":"U.S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service, Pacific Northwest Research Station","publisher-place":"Portland, OR","title":"Gathering in the city: an annotated bibliography and review of the literature about human-plant interactions in urban ecosystems","title-short":"Gathering in the city","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"R. J."},{"family":"MacFarland","given":"K."},{"family":"Brody","given":"L."},{"family":"Hebert","given":"J."},{"family":"Hurley","given":"P."},{"family":"Poe","given":"M."},{"family":"Buttolph","given":"L. P."},{"family":"Gabriel","given":"N."},{"family":"Dzuna","given":"M."},{"family":"Emery","given":"M. R."},{"family":"Charnley","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":921,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VFC5RUFZ"],"itemData":{"id":921,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Our research seeks to expand the concept of urban environmental stewardship to include the everyday stewardship practices of urban nontimber forest products foragers. Ethnographic data from 58 urban foragers and 18 land stewards in the city of Seattle (USA) revealed that foragers reported using a variety of practices to enhance and minimize negative desirable species and their habitats. Many of these practices were identical to those practiced by restoration volunteers in formal programs and align with Seattle Parks Department management objectives. Foragers actively sought to learn more about what practices are sustainable; many mentored others in sustainable harvesting practices. Most foragers emphasized the importance of treating plants and their environment with respect. The land stewards voiced some concerns about foraging in city parks, but most were cautiously supportive of opening up the parks to foragers. The study results suggest that an opportunity exists for park managers to develop alliances with foragers so as to leverage foragers’ everyday stewardship practices and accomplish some of their park restoration objectives. Doing so will require park managers to acknowledge the positive contributions that humans can to make novel ecosystems and foragers to develop or strengthen selfregulation norms.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2017.10.005","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"36-42","title":"Urban non-timber forest products stewardship practices among foragers in Seattle, washington (USA)","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"R. J."},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R."},{"family":"Urgenson","given":"Lauren S."},{"family":"Blahna","given":"Dale J."},{"family":"Buttolph","given":"Lita P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":933,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9TB76EG"],"itemData":{"id":933,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","DOI":"10.1016/j.landurbplan.2016.09.003","ISSN":"01692046","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape and Urban Planning","language":"en","page":"394-406","title":"Human-biodiversity interactions in urban parks: The species level matters","title-short":"Human-biodiversity interactions in urban parks","volume":"157","author":[{"family":"Palliwoda","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"},{"family":"Lippe","given":"Moritz","non-dropping-particle":"von der"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":31615,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BBMRWGK2"],"itemData":{"id":31615,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Social & Cultural Geography","DOI":"10.1080/14649365.2014.908232","ISSN":"1464-9365, 1470-1197","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Social & Cultural Geography","language":"en","page":"901-919","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Urban foraging and the relational ecologies of belonging","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/14649365.2014.908232","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R."},{"family":"LeCompte","given":"Joyce"},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",11,17]]}}},{"id":33678,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ENAQFCLB"],"itemData":{"id":33678,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Although hardly noticed or formally recognised, urban foraging by humans probably occurs in all urban settings around the world. We draw from research in India, South Africa, Sweden, and the United States to demonstrate the ubiquity and varied nature of urban foraging in different contexts. Across these different contexts, we distil seven themes that characterise and thereby advance thinking about research and the understanding of urban foraging. We show that it is widespread and occurs across a variety of urban spaces and places. The species used and the local practices vary between contexts, and are in constant ?ux as urban ecological and social settings change. This requires that urban foragers are knowledgeable about diverse species, harvest locations, and rights of access, and that their practices are adaptable to changing contexts. Despite its ubiquity, most cities have some forms of regulations that prohibit or discourage urban foraging. We highlight a few important exceptions that can provide prototypes and lessons for other cities regarding supportive policy frameworks and initiatives. The formulation of dynamic policy, design, and management strategies in support of urban foraging will bene?t from understanding the common characteristics of foraging in cities worldwide, but also will require comprehension of the speci?c and dynamic contexts in which they would be implemented.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su9101884","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"10","language":"en","page":"1884","title":"Urban Foraging: A Ubiquitous Human Practice Overlooked by Urban Planners, Policy, and Research","title-short":"Urban Foraging","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Dahlberg","given":"Annika"},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",10,20]]}}},{"id":952,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F4C7GJ9X"],"itemData":{"id":952,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","DOI":"10.1016/j.landurbplan.2014.12.004","ISSN":"01692046","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape and Urban Planning","language":"en","page":"76-86","title":"Multiple benefits and values of trees in urban landscapes in two towns in northern South Africa","volume":"136","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Sheona"},{"family":"Chinyimba","given":"Abby"},{"family":"Hebinck","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Kaoma","given":"Humphrey"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":947,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D6XJCKVT"],"itemData":{"id":947,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Urban foraging is widely prevalent globally, and can provide foragers benefits like additional income and nutritional supplements. However, urban foraging is largely understudied and we have little information on types of species foraged, patterns of foraging, socio-economic profile of foragers, and impact of urbanization on foraging. In this study, we aimed to identify patterns of urban foraging in one of the fastest growing cities in the world, Bengaluru in India. Our specific objectives were to identify a) the foragers b) species foraged and foraging sites c) impact of urbanization on foraging and d) the barriers to foraging. We collected our information through semi-structured interviews with residents from four sites selected across a gradient of urbanization. Sixteen per cent of the respondents reported that they foraged, most of who were women (97 %) belonging to socially (90 %) and economically (81 %) disadvantaged groups. Seventy-six species were foraged across the four sites for food (76 %), medicine (26 %), and cultural practices (18 %). Foraging occurred in unused land (32 %), parks (32 %), farmlands (12 %), native villages (12 %), nearby woodlands (9%) and home gardens (3%). We found significant differences in foragers along a gradient of urbanization (chi square = 34.56, p = 1.5 × 10? 7, df = 3) with most foragers at the least urbanized site (40 % of the respondents) and least foragers at the most urbanized site (2% of the respondents). Lack of knowledge of foraged species (66 %) and lack of access (55 %) were the biggest barriers to foraging. Our study demonstrates that foraging occurs in a metropolis like Bengaluru with foragers generally belonging to lower socio-economic backgrounds, who are most vulnerable to changes due to urbanization. Redesigning urban environments to create green spaces for forgeable species to flourish, that are made accessible to marginalized communities, is essential for their nutritional and cultural well-being.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2020.126940","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126940","title":"Patterns of urban foraging in Bengaluru city","volume":"57","author":[{"family":"Somesh","given":"Dhruthi"},{"family":"Rao","given":"Rohit"},{"family":"Murali","given":"Ranjini"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some cases, for example in Uganda, New Zealand, French Guiana, Haiti and India, wild plants are primarily gathered for medicinal purposes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1h891v8kv5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dejouhanet & de Bercegol, 2019; Mollee, Pouliot, & McDonald, 2017; Tareau, Dejouhanet, Odonne, Palisse, & Ansoe, 2019; Wehi & Wehi, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Dejouhanet & de Bercegol, 2019; Mollee, Pouliot, & McDonald, 2017; Tareau, Dejouhanet, Odonne, Palisse, & Ansoe, 2019; Wehi & Wehi, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":925,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7EA4FG6V"],"itemData":{"id":925,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EchoGéo [Online]","DOI":"10.4000/echogeo.17468","title":"New Geographies of Collection: Crossed perspectives on modern ”gatherers” - Introduction","URL":"https://journals.openedition.org/echogeo/17468","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Dejouhanet","given":"Lucie"},{"family":"Bercegol","given":"Rémi","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":927,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V5VAYSM3"],"itemData":{"id":927,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land Use Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.landusepol.2017.01.020","ISSN":"02648377","journalAbbreviation":"Land Use Policy","language":"en","page":"67-77","title":"Into the urban wild: Collection of wild urban plants for food and medicine in Kampala, Uganda","title-short":"Into the urban wild","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Mollee","given":"Eefke"},{"family":"Pouliot","given":"Mariève"},{"family":"McDonald","given":"Morag A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":958,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PGLPEPK6"],"itemData":{"id":958,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EchoGéo","DOI":"10.4000/echogeo.17260","ISSN":"1963-1197","issue":"47","journalAbbreviation":"echogeo","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild medicinal plant collection in transitional societies: A case Analysis from French Guiana","title-short":"Wild medicinal plant collection in transitional societies","URL":"http://journals.openedition.org/echogeo/17260","author":[{"family":"Tareau","given":"Marc-Alexandre"},{"family":"Dejouhanet","given":"Lucie"},{"family":"Odonne","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Palisse","given":"Marianne"},{"family":"Ansoe","given":"Clarisse"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,21]]}}},{"id":915,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AJVE4E5X"],"itemData":{"id":915,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ecosystem fragmentation and destruction can lead to restrictive administration policies on traditional harvesting by indigenous peoples from remaining ecosystem tracts. In New Zealand, concerns about endangered species and governmental policies that focus on species and ecosystem preservation have resulted in severely curtailed traditional harvesting rights. Although provision has been made for limited gathering of traditional plants from government-administered conservation lands, it is unclear how much harvesting is undertaken on these lands and elsewhere and what this harvest might consist of. We interviewed seven expert Maori elders from the Waikato, New Zealand, to identify plant species they currently harvested and from where. We compared these data with the data we collected on permits issued for plant collecting on conservation lands in the same region. We sought to gain information on indigenous plant harvesting to determine the extent of permitted harvesting from conservation lands in the Waikato and to identify issues that might affect plant harvesting and management. Elders identified 58 species they harvest regularly or consider culturally important; over 50% of these species are harvested for medicinal use. Permit data from 1996 to 2006 indicated no apparent relationship between species of reported cultural significance and the number of permits issued for each of these species. Currently, few plant species are harvested from conservation lands, although some unofficial harvesting occurs. Elders instead reported that medicinal plants are frequently collected from urban and other public areas. They reported that plant species used for dyeing, carving, and weaving are difficult to access. Elders also discussed concerns such as spraying of roadsides, which resulted in the death of medicinal species, and use of commercial hybrids in urban planning. Local government may have an increasingly important role in supporting native traditions through urban planning, which takes account of cultural harvesting needs while potentially reducing future harvesting pressure on conservation lands. We suggest that active participation by the Maori community in the development and management of urban harvesting resources will result in positive outcomes.","container-title":"Conserv Biol","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2009.01376.x","ISSN":"0888-8892","issue":"2","language":"English","page":"594-604","title":"Traditional Plant Harvesting in Contemporary Fragmented and Urban Landscapes","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Wehi","given":"P. M."},{"family":"Wehi","given":"W. L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, in major urban spaces in these countries gathering wild edible plants and fungi was most commonly for food, followed by medicinal uses and personal enjoyment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2lboasl2lo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Amato-Lourenco, 2020; Garekae & Shackleton, 2020a; Landor-Yamagata, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Amato-Lourenco, 2020; Garekae & Shackleton, 2020a; Landor-Yamagata, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":935,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CLABPN4M"],"itemData":{"id":935,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Foraging wild-growing edible plants (WEPs) is a re-emerging practice with increasing popularity worldwide, including in urban areas. However, in cities, this practice raises questions about the safety of foraging these plants for human consumption, due to the potential exposure of plants to higher levels of pollutants. In this study, the concentration of 12 elements (Al, V, Cr, Mn, Co, Ni, Zn, As, Rb, Cd, Ba and Pb) in three different WEPs (Amaranthus spp., Plantago tomentosa and Taraxacum of?cinale) were determined according to different traf?c categories in the municipality of São Paulo. Additionally, plants were sampled within the inner areas of three municipal parks in the same study region. Different gradients of elemental concentrations were obtained according to the traf?c categories. Freeways presented higher concentrations of several elements than local roads or parks. For the WEPs collected along freeways and some plants along arterial roads, the concentrations of Pb exceeded safety levels for human consumption. Our data suggest that foraging in large urban centres should be performed preferentially in low-traf?c areas.","container-title":"Science of The Total Environment","DOI":"10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.133967","ISSN":"00489697","journalAbbreviation":"Science of The Total Environment","language":"en","page":"133967","title":"Edible weeds: Are urban environments fit for foraging?","title-short":"Edible weeds","volume":"698","author":[{"family":"Amato-Lourenco","given":"Luís Fernando"},{"family":"Ranieri","given":"Guilherme Reis"},{"family":"Oliveira Souza","given":"Vanessa Cristina","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Junior","given":"Fernando Barbosa"},{"family":"Saldiva","given":"Paulo Hilário Nascimento"},{"family":"Mauad","given":"Thais"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":934,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NSVCVCND"],"itemData":{"id":934,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Globally, approximately one billion people bene?t from contributions of wild foods to their food security and dietary diversity. Wild foods are known to be important in rural communities in terms of food and micronutrient provision, diversifying diets, reducing vulnerability to non-communicable diseases and overall health. However, the potential contribution of wild foods towards food security and dietary diversity in urban food systems has been largely overlooked. This study examined the contribution of wild foods to household dietary diversity in two towns in South Africa, based on a survey of 137 households. Household diets were quite diverse, with half (51%) having consumed ? 8 food groups, 39% consumed 6 or 7 food groups, and only 10% recorded ? 5 food groups in the previous 48 h. Wild foods were prevalent across the sample, with 62% of the households consuming them to some degree. Wild vegetables and fruits were the most common wild foods, consumed by 96 and 79% of the households, respectively. Although wild foods had limited signi?cance on overall dietary diversity, they exhibited substantial contributions within particular food groups. For example, the consumption of vegetables and oil and fats was most prevalent among households consuming wild foods than those who did not. The ?ndings show that wild foods could contribute towards diversifying urban diets at a micro-level, within particular food groups consumed, such as vegetables and fruits. Hence, wild foods are important in ameliorating the monotonous diets of some households and in turn promoting dietary diversity.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su12020678","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"678","title":"Foraging Wild Food in Urban Spaces: The Contribution of Wild Foods to Urban Dietary Diversity in South Africa","title-short":"Foraging Wild Food in Urban Spaces","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Garekae","given":"Hesekia"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1,16]]}}},{"id":931,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V2X4DZN4"],"itemData":{"id":931,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Gathering wild plants in cities (urban foraging) is likely an important, but understudied human-nature interaction globally. As large European cities are critically understudied in this regard, we performed in-depth ethnography-based interviews in Berlin, Germany, to shed light on the cultural background of foragers, their motivations and which plants and fungi are gathered for which purposes. Results demonstrate multiple uses of 125 taxa, mostly frequently-occurring species but also some Red List species, from a range of formal and informal greenspace types. Both native and non-native species were gathered, with signi?cant differences in use patterns. Use for food was most common, followed by medicinal uses, and personal enjoyment was a frequent motivation, indicating that urban foraging combines provisioning and cultural ecosystem services. Familial and childhood foraging exposure were common, pointing to in?uences of early-in-life exposure on later-in-life activities and transgenerational aspects of the practice. Results further suggest legacy effects from the post-war and communist eras on foraging knowledge. Although non-commercial foraging is allowed in Berlin, over-harvesting was not evident. Interviews indicate that stewardship of urban biodiversity is common among foragers. Results thus suggest considering urban foraging as a promising vehicle for linking humans with nature when developing a biodiverse urban green infrastructure.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su10061873","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1873","title":"Urban Foraging in Berlin: People, Plants and Practices within the Metropolitan Green Infrastructure","title-short":"Urban Foraging in Berlin","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Landor-Yamagata","given":"Jonah"},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"},{"family":"Fischer","given":"Leonie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wild edibles, including berries, fruits, nuts, greens, and young shoots, were by far the most frequently mentioned type of product, contributing to diversifying urban diets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xmzT8c6R","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garekae & Shackleton, 2020a; Rebecca J McLain, Hurley, Emery, & Poe, 2014; M. Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2020; C. Shackleton, 2017; Somesh, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Garekae & Shackleton, 2020a; Rebecca J McLain, Hurley, Emery, & Poe, 2014; M. Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2020; C. Shackleton, 2017; Somesh, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":934,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NSVCVCND"],"itemData":{"id":934,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Globally, approximately one billion people bene?t from contributions of wild foods to their food security and dietary diversity. Wild foods are known to be important in rural communities in terms of food and micronutrient provision, diversifying diets, reducing vulnerability to non-communicable diseases and overall health. However, the potential contribution of wild foods towards food security and dietary diversity in urban food systems has been largely overlooked. This study examined the contribution of wild foods to household dietary diversity in two towns in South Africa, based on a survey of 137 households. Household diets were quite diverse, with half (51%) having consumed ? 8 food groups, 39% consumed 6 or 7 food groups, and only 10% recorded ? 5 food groups in the previous 48 h. Wild foods were prevalent across the sample, with 62% of the households consuming them to some degree. Wild vegetables and fruits were the most common wild foods, consumed by 96 and 79% of the households, respectively. Although wild foods had limited signi?cance on overall dietary diversity, they exhibited substantial contributions within particular food groups. For example, the consumption of vegetables and oil and fats was most prevalent among households consuming wild foods than those who did not. The ?ndings show that wild foods could contribute towards diversifying urban diets at a micro-level, within particular food groups consumed, such as vegetables and fruits. Hence, wild foods are important in ameliorating the monotonous diets of some households and in turn promoting dietary diversity.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su12020678","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"678","title":"Foraging Wild Food in Urban Spaces: The Contribution of Wild Foods to Urban Dietary Diversity in South Africa","title-short":"Foraging Wild Food in Urban Spaces","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Garekae","given":"Hesekia"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1,16]]}}},{"id":957,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEIARWXM"],"itemData":{"id":957,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Local environment","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1080/13549839.2013.841659","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"220–240","title":"Gathering “wild” food in the city: rethinking the role of foraging in urban ecosystem planning and management","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T"},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R"},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":28291,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6JQJDH3Q"],"itemData":{"id":28291,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-020-00185-x","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"665–677","title":"Fruits of the Veld: Ecological and Socioeconomic Patterns of Natural Resource Use across South Africa","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10745-020-00185-x","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Sardeshpande","given":"M."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":33678,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ENAQFCLB"],"itemData":{"id":33678,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Although hardly noticed or formally recognised, urban foraging by humans probably occurs in all urban settings around the world. We draw from research in India, South Africa, Sweden, and the United States to demonstrate the ubiquity and varied nature of urban foraging in different contexts. Across these different contexts, we distil seven themes that characterise and thereby advance thinking about research and the understanding of urban foraging. We show that it is widespread and occurs across a variety of urban spaces and places. The species used and the local practices vary between contexts, and are in constant ?ux as urban ecological and social settings change. This requires that urban foragers are knowledgeable about diverse species, harvest locations, and rights of access, and that their practices are adaptable to changing contexts. Despite its ubiquity, most cities have some forms of regulations that prohibit or discourage urban foraging. We highlight a few important exceptions that can provide prototypes and lessons for other cities regarding supportive policy frameworks and initiatives. The formulation of dynamic policy, design, and management strategies in support of urban foraging will bene?t from understanding the common characteristics of foraging in cities worldwide, but also will require comprehension of the speci?c and dynamic contexts in which they would be implemented.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su9101884","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"10","language":"en","page":"1884","title":"Urban Foraging: A Ubiquitous Human Practice Overlooked by Urban Planners, Policy, and Research","title-short":"Urban Foraging","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Dahlberg","given":"Annika"},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",10,20]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":947,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D6XJCKVT"],"itemData":{"id":947,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Urban foraging is widely prevalent globally, and can provide foragers benefits like additional income and nutritional supplements. However, urban foraging is largely understudied and we have little information on types of species foraged, patterns of foraging, socio-economic profile of foragers, and impact of urbanization on foraging. In this study, we aimed to identify patterns of urban foraging in one of the fastest growing cities in the world, Bengaluru in India. Our specific objectives were to identify a) the foragers b) species foraged and foraging sites c) impact of urbanization on foraging and d) the barriers to foraging. We collected our information through semi-structured interviews with residents from four sites selected across a gradient of urbanization. Sixteen per cent of the respondents reported that they foraged, most of who were women (97 %) belonging to socially (90 %) and economically (81 %) disadvantaged groups. Seventy-six species were foraged across the four sites for food (76 %), medicine (26 %), and cultural practices (18 %). Foraging occurred in unused land (32 %), parks (32 %), farmlands (12 %), native villages (12 %), nearby woodlands (9%) and home gardens (3%). We found significant differences in foragers along a gradient of urbanization (chi square = 34.56, p = 1.5 × 10? 7, df = 3) with most foragers at the least urbanized site (40 % of the respondents) and least foragers at the most urbanized site (2% of the respondents). Lack of knowledge of foraged species (66 %) and lack of access (55 %) were the biggest barriers to foraging. Our study demonstrates that foraging occurs in a metropolis like Bengaluru with foragers generally belonging to lower socio-economic backgrounds, who are most vulnerable to changes due to urbanization. Redesigning urban environments to create green spaces for forgeable species to flourish, that are made accessible to marginalized communities, is essential for their nutritional and cultural well-being.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2020.126940","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126940","title":"Patterns of urban foraging in Bengaluru city","volume":"57","author":[{"family":"Somesh","given":"Dhruthi"},{"family":"Rao","given":"Rohit"},{"family":"Murali","given":"Ranjini"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Urban green spaces where gathering happens are promising pathways towards biodiversity conservation in cities because they facilitate interactions between people and nature which support physical and mental health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"apv6erkc0s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Palliwoda, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Palliwoda, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":933,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9TB76EG"],"itemData":{"id":933,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","DOI":"10.1016/j.landurbplan.2016.09.003","ISSN":"01692046","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape and Urban Planning","language":"en","page":"394-406","title":"Human-biodiversity interactions in urban parks: The species level matters","title-short":"Human-biodiversity interactions in urban parks","volume":"157","author":[{"family":"Palliwoda","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"},{"family":"Lippe","given":"Moritz","non-dropping-particle":"von der"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Equitable access to cultural ecosystem services from urban green space helps overcome sociocultural barriers, strengthens social relationships, maintains knowledge and traditions of families and communities, increases shares in the management of goods and services, and increases healthy food intake and personal participation in healthy behaviors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a20kdsvbe1a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Askerlund & Almers, 2016; V. Jennings, Larson, & Yun, 2016; Landor-Yamagata, 2018; R. J. McLain, 2012; \\\\uc0\\\\u352{}iftov\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}, 2020; Tiwary, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Askerlund & Almers, 2016; V. Jennings, Larson, & Yun, 2016; Landor-Yamagata, 2018; R. J. McLain, 2012; Šiftová, 2020; Tiwary, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":951,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CXBBCC4A"],"itemData":{"id":951,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2016.08.007","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"187-197","title":"Forest gardens – new opportunities for urban children to understand and develop relationships with other organisms","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Askerlund","given":"Per"},{"family":"Almers","given":"Ellen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":923,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GJJXHQEZ"],"itemData":{"id":923,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Urban green spaces provide an array of bene?ts, or ecosystem services, that support our physical, psychological, and social health. In many cases, however, these bene?ts are not equitably distributed across diverse urban populations. In this paper, we explore relationships between cultural ecosystem services provided by urban green space and the social determinants of health outlined in the United States Healthy People 2020 initiative. Speci?cally, we: (1) explore connections between cultural ecosystem services and social determinants of health; (2) examine cultural ecosystem services as nature-based health amenities to promote social equity; and (3) recommend areas for future research examining links between urban green space and public health within the context of environmental justice.","container-title":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","DOI":"10.3390/ijerph13020196","ISSN":"1660-4601","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"IJERPH","language":"en","page":"196","title":"Advancing Sustainability through Urban Green Space: Cultural Ecosystem Services, Equity, and Social Determinants of Health","title-short":"Advancing Sustainability through Urban Green Space","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Jennings","given":"Viniece"},{"family":"Larson","given":"Lincoln"},{"family":"Yun","given":"Jessica"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",2,5]]}}},{"id":931,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V2X4DZN4"],"itemData":{"id":931,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Gathering wild plants in cities (urban foraging) is likely an important, but understudied human-nature interaction globally. As large European cities are critically understudied in this regard, we performed in-depth ethnography-based interviews in Berlin, Germany, to shed light on the cultural background of foragers, their motivations and which plants and fungi are gathered for which purposes. Results demonstrate multiple uses of 125 taxa, mostly frequently-occurring species but also some Red List species, from a range of formal and informal greenspace types. Both native and non-native species were gathered, with signi?cant differences in use patterns. Use for food was most common, followed by medicinal uses, and personal enjoyment was a frequent motivation, indicating that urban foraging combines provisioning and cultural ecosystem services. Familial and childhood foraging exposure were common, pointing to in?uences of early-in-life exposure on later-in-life activities and transgenerational aspects of the practice. Results further suggest legacy effects from the post-war and communist eras on foraging knowledge. Although non-commercial foraging is allowed in Berlin, over-harvesting was not evident. Interviews indicate that stewardship of urban biodiversity is common among foragers. Results thus suggest considering urban foraging as a promising vehicle for linking humans with nature when developing a biodiverse urban green infrastructure.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su10061873","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1873","title":"Urban Foraging in Berlin: People, Plants and Practices within the Metropolitan Green Infrastructure","title-short":"Urban Foraging in Berlin","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Landor-Yamagata","given":"Jonah"},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"},{"family":"Fischer","given":"Leonie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,4]]}}},{"id":950,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YHVD6VPJ"],"itemData":{"id":950,"type":"report","event-place":"Portland, OR","language":"en","number":"PNW-GTR-849","page":"PNW-GTR-849","publisher":"U.S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service, Pacific Northwest Research Station","publisher-place":"Portland, OR","title":"Gathering in the city: an annotated bibliography and review of the literature about human-plant interactions in urban ecosystems","title-short":"Gathering in the city","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"R. J."},{"family":"MacFarland","given":"K."},{"family":"Brody","given":"L."},{"family":"Hebert","given":"J."},{"family":"Hurley","given":"P."},{"family":"Poe","given":"M."},{"family":"Buttolph","given":"L. P."},{"family":"Gabriel","given":"N."},{"family":"Dzuna","given":"M."},{"family":"Emery","given":"M. R."},{"family":"Charnley","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":944,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2G386ZQ4"],"itemData":{"id":944,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The article ?lls the gap in the geographical literature on foraging in a Central and Eastern European context. Its goals are to investigate the extent and character of foraging practice and the population of foragers in Czechia, as well as to uncover what motivates the practice of foraging for non-timber forest products. A mixed methods approach was used in the reported study, which combined the triangulation of data from o?cial statistics, three waves of a representative data survey, and a case study of mushroom pickers in Prague, the capital city of Czechia. The results show that foraging is a widespread practice in Czechia with stable but ?uctuating harvest volumes. Czechs gather mainly mushrooms and edible berries. The results also show that foraging is a traditional practice of people with di?erent demographic characteristics and from diverse socio-economic backgrounds, with a myriad of diverse motivations and connotations far from mere economic ones. Strolling outside in the fresh air and in forests is clearly a dominant motive. Thus, the author concludes that foraging in Czechia is far from being limited to livelihood strategies to tackle food insecurity.","container-title":"Norsk Geografisk Tidsskrift - Norwegian Journal of Geography","DOI":"10.1080/00291951.2020.1851757","ISSN":"0029-1951, 1502-5292","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Norsk Geografisk Tidsskrift - Norwegian Journal of Geography","language":"en","page":"310-320","title":"Foraging in Czechia: The nation’s precious hobby","title-short":"Foraging in Czechia","volume":"74","author":[{"family":"Šiftová","given":"Jana"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,19]]}}},{"id":938,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UWW6782I"],"itemData":{"id":938,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The role of the urban common (i.e. shared space and resources) in sustainable provisioning of goods and services to city dwellers is discussed in this paper. Focusing on tree-based green infrastructure, the study scope includes three categories of provisioning (woody biomass, food/fibre, and non-timber forest products, i.e. NTFPs), alongside three categories of supporting services (fresh water replenishment, soil nutrient restoration, building preservation). As a first step, prospects of utilizing the urban common as facilitator of nature-based solution to the earmarked provisioning services are evaluated through dedicated literature survey and expert elicitation on perceived impact of environmental change triggers and management interventions (planning and/or governance). This is followed by a structured review of the state of affairs in four European cities (London, Amsterdam, Sofia, Ljubljana), representing different macro-geographical regions with distinct socio-economic drivers in managing these provisioning services. The pan-European expert elicitation exercise noted active management of the urban common as positively impacting on the performance of the majority of provisioning services, while environmental change impacts were found to be overriding and adversely influencing the provisioning of material resources (mainly NTFPs and woody biomass). The four-city case study highlighted some regional peculiarities in connecting the city dwellers to the urban common and identified the need to overcome socio-cultural barriers for enhancing pan-European best practice sharing in the management of goods and services provisioning. This is deemed essential to pave way for an emerging perspective on sustainable utilization of the urban common as an enabler for nature-based solution, making it fit for purpose in meeting the astronomical demands of future urban living.","container-title":"Urban Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1007/s11252-020-00951-1","ISSN":"1083-8155, 1573-1642","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Ecosyst","language":"en","page":"645-657","title":"Management of nature-based goods and services provisioning from the urban common: a pan-European perspective","title-short":"Management of nature-based goods and services provisioning from the urban common","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Tiwary","given":"Abhishek"},{"family":"Vilhar","given":"Ursa"},{"family":"Zhiyanski","given":"Miglena"},{"family":"Stojanovski","given":"Vladimir"},{"family":"Dinca","given":"Lucian"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urban gathering can also support identity, place attachment, or mobility and agency of people and communities in the city ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aq3n341arg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. R. Poe, 2014)","plainCitation":"(M. R. Poe, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31615,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BBMRWGK2"],"itemData":{"id":31615,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Social & Cultural Geography","DOI":"10.1080/14649365.2014.908232","ISSN":"1464-9365, 1470-1197","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Social & Cultural Geography","language":"en","page":"901-919","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Urban foraging and the relational ecologies of belonging","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/14649365.2014.908232","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R."},{"family":"LeCompte","given":"Joyce"},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",11,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Gender and income level affect urban gathering activities differently in different regions. They may be evenly distributed along gender or income categories in the United States of America, Germany and the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Norhtern Ireland ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2o8jiuaa39","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. K. Fischer & Kowarik, 2020; \\\\uc0\\\\u321{}uczaj, 2021; R. J. McLain, 2012; Rebecca J McLain, 2014)","plainCitation":"(L. K. Fischer & Kowarik, 2020; ?uczaj, 2021; R. J. McLain, 2012; Rebecca J McLain, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":941,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3Y9K2TNY"],"itemData":{"id":941,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As global population becomes increasingly urban, opportunities for people to experience nature have decreased. Counteracting this trend is a key challenge for future urban development as interactions of urban people with biodiversity support human health and wellbeing, and may also result in positive attitudes towards biodiversity conservation. Collecting edible plants in urban surroundings, especially outside of gardens (“urban foraging”) is a traditional interaction with nature, based on knowledge about multiple uses of plants. Although some studies exist from different cities around the world, urban foraging has been revealed as a critically understudied phenomenon. We now analyze (i) the relevance of this human-nature interaction in Berlin, one of Europe’s metropolises, (ii) how people’s sociocultural background matters in attitudes of urban foragers vs. non-foragers towards this activity, and (iii) whether urban foraging may lead to conflicts with biodiversity conservation. Our survey revealed urban foraging as a relevant human-nature interaction with a high potential to grow: 33% of 535 respondents already collected edible plants outside of gardens and another 38% would be doing so given certain conditions, e.g. when contamination risks can be excluded. Many sociocultural groups (differing, e.g. on gender, age, childhood experience) shared attitudes towards foraging and existing barriers. Risks to biodiversity seem to be manageable as neither native species nor rare species were over-foraged in relation to species’ abundance in the local flora, with more abundant species being collected more frequently. We conclude that urban foraging can be a powerful tool for connecting urban people to nature without putting native biodiversity at risk. We make a claim for integrated approaches towards environmental policy, environmental education and greenspace management: these should aim on keeping potential health risks at a minimum, and should support urban foraging as a biodiversity-friendly and sustainable human-nature interaction in the cities of tomorrow.","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2020.106087","ISSN":"1470160X","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Indicators","language":"en","page":"106087","title":"Connecting people to biodiversity in cities of tomorrow: Is urban foraging a powerful tool?","title-short":"Connecting people to biodiversity in cities of tomorrow","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Fischer","given":"Leonie K."},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":937,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N8FQZKRQ"],"itemData":{"id":937,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Foraging in the British Isles is an increasingly popular activity for both personal consumption and for commercial purposes. While legislation and guidelines exist regulating the sustainable collection of wild edibles, the founding principles of the British foraging movement are not well documented. For this research, 36 of the most active foraging instructors of the Association of Foragers were interviewed to understand their background, species collected, sources of knowledge, and problems faced during collection. Altogether, 102 species of leafy vegetables, fruits, fungi, and seaweeds were mentioned as frequently used, while 34 species of roadkill animals were listed, mostly for personal consumption. Instructors reported learning from wild food guidebooks, other foragers, or personal experience. Frequent contact among foragers has led to the standardisation of knowledge and practices among them forming a “new tradition”, partly based on old British traditions but modi?ed by in?uences from other countries and cultures, both in terms of choice of species and processing techniques. Contrary to expectations, foragers rarely reported clashes with nature conservation or forestry managers. The authors argue that knowledge and practice developed by the Association of Foragers (AoF) are sustainable and could be integrated into the British food and nature conservation system.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su13063478","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"3478","title":"The Ethnobiology of Contemporary British Foragers: Foods They Teach, Their Sources of Inspiration and Impact","title-short":"The Ethnobiology of Contemporary British Foragers","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"?uczaj","given":"?ukasz"},{"family":"Wilde","given":"Monica"},{"family":"Townsend","given":"Leanne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,21]]}}},{"id":950,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YHVD6VPJ"],"itemData":{"id":950,"type":"report","event-place":"Portland, OR","language":"en","number":"PNW-GTR-849","page":"PNW-GTR-849","publisher":"U.S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service, Pacific Northwest Research Station","publisher-place":"Portland, OR","title":"Gathering in the city: an annotated bibliography and review of the literature about human-plant interactions in urban ecosystems","title-short":"Gathering in the city","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"R. J."},{"family":"MacFarland","given":"K."},{"family":"Brody","given":"L."},{"family":"Hebert","given":"J."},{"family":"Hurley","given":"P."},{"family":"Poe","given":"M."},{"family":"Buttolph","given":"L. P."},{"family":"Gabriel","given":"N."},{"family":"Dzuna","given":"M."},{"family":"Emery","given":"M. R."},{"family":"Charnley","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":957,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEIARWXM"],"itemData":{"id":957,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Local environment","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1080/13549839.2013.841659","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"220–240","title":"Gathering “wild” food in the city: rethinking the role of foraging in urban ecosystem planning and management","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T"},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R"},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urban gathering in developing countries tends to be more female-dominated in some countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2np5ic4bkp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garekae & Shackleton, 2020a; Somesh, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Garekae & Shackleton, 2020a; Somesh, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":934,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NSVCVCND"],"itemData":{"id":934,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Globally, approximately one billion people bene?t from contributions of wild foods to their food security and dietary diversity. Wild foods are known to be important in rural communities in terms of food and micronutrient provision, diversifying diets, reducing vulnerability to non-communicable diseases and overall health. However, the potential contribution of wild foods towards food security and dietary diversity in urban food systems has been largely overlooked. This study examined the contribution of wild foods to household dietary diversity in two towns in South Africa, based on a survey of 137 households. Household diets were quite diverse, with half (51%) having consumed ? 8 food groups, 39% consumed 6 or 7 food groups, and only 10% recorded ? 5 food groups in the previous 48 h. Wild foods were prevalent across the sample, with 62% of the households consuming them to some degree. Wild vegetables and fruits were the most common wild foods, consumed by 96 and 79% of the households, respectively. Although wild foods had limited signi?cance on overall dietary diversity, they exhibited substantial contributions within particular food groups. For example, the consumption of vegetables and oil and fats was most prevalent among households consuming wild foods than those who did not. The ?ndings show that wild foods could contribute towards diversifying urban diets at a micro-level, within particular food groups consumed, such as vegetables and fruits. Hence, wild foods are important in ameliorating the monotonous diets of some households and in turn promoting dietary diversity.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su12020678","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"678","title":"Foraging Wild Food in Urban Spaces: The Contribution of Wild Foods to Urban Dietary Diversity in South Africa","title-short":"Foraging Wild Food in Urban Spaces","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Garekae","given":"Hesekia"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1,16]]}}},{"id":947,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D6XJCKVT"],"itemData":{"id":947,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Urban foraging is widely prevalent globally, and can provide foragers benefits like additional income and nutritional supplements. However, urban foraging is largely understudied and we have little information on types of species foraged, patterns of foraging, socio-economic profile of foragers, and impact of urbanization on foraging. In this study, we aimed to identify patterns of urban foraging in one of the fastest growing cities in the world, Bengaluru in India. Our specific objectives were to identify a) the foragers b) species foraged and foraging sites c) impact of urbanization on foraging and d) the barriers to foraging. We collected our information through semi-structured interviews with residents from four sites selected across a gradient of urbanization. Sixteen per cent of the respondents reported that they foraged, most of who were women (97 %) belonging to socially (90 %) and economically (81 %) disadvantaged groups. Seventy-six species were foraged across the four sites for food (76 %), medicine (26 %), and cultural practices (18 %). Foraging occurred in unused land (32 %), parks (32 %), farmlands (12 %), native villages (12 %), nearby woodlands (9%) and home gardens (3%). We found significant differences in foragers along a gradient of urbanization (chi square = 34.56, p = 1.5 × 10? 7, df = 3) with most foragers at the least urbanized site (40 % of the respondents) and least foragers at the most urbanized site (2% of the respondents). Lack of knowledge of foraged species (66 %) and lack of access (55 %) were the biggest barriers to foraging. Our study demonstrates that foraging occurs in a metropolis like Bengaluru with foragers generally belonging to lower socio-economic backgrounds, who are most vulnerable to changes due to urbanization. Redesigning urban environments to create green spaces for forgeable species to flourish, that are made accessible to marginalized communities, is essential for their nutritional and cultural well-being.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2020.126940","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126940","title":"Patterns of urban foraging in Bengaluru city","volume":"57","author":[{"family":"Somesh","given":"Dhruthi"},{"family":"Rao","given":"Rohit"},{"family":"Murali","given":"Ranjini"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and male-dominated in other countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2jb2s5d3on","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garekae & Shackleton, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(Garekae & Shackleton, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":940,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B47KJTK8"],"itemData":{"id":940,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Urban ecosystems provide goods and services critical to livelihood sustenance, environmental protection and sustainability. However, the potential role of some urban ecosystem services such as the provisioning services, to promote liveable cities and livelihood resilience is seldom considered. This study reports on the prevalence and pattern of wild plant foraging and uses in the towns of Potchefstroom and Thabazimbi, South Africa, based on a random sample of 374 households. Descriptive and inferential statistics were used for analysing data. Foraging wild plants was integral to livelihoods and traditions and provided household energy, dietary diversity, health, and cultural affirmation, among others. About 68 % of the respondents reported foraging wild plants, albeit at varying degrees between and within towns. The prevalence of foraging differed significantly between and within towns, being higher in Thabazimbi and for residents on the outskirts of town. Although urban foraging transcends demographic and socio-economic backgrounds, the likelihood of foraging significantly differed in relation to childhood foraging background, perception towards the practice, location of residence in town and household affluence. Nevertheless, foraging was not limited to poorer households, as one-third of the more affluent households also foraged. Foraging occurred in both public and private green spaces, with public spaces being the most frequently visited. Motivations towards foraging were quite diverse and varied across space, context and over time. Overall, the high prevalence of foraging exhibited in this study and that from elsewhere should not be overlooked in urban landscape planning and development.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2020.126581","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126581","title":"Urban foraging of wild plants in two medium-sized South African towns: People, perceptions and practices","title-short":"Urban foraging of wild plants in two medium-sized South African towns","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Garekae","given":"Hesekia"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Residents with lower income and predominantly living or growing up in rural areas or peri-urban areas are more likely to be urban foragers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ag8ufkgl93","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garekae & Shackleton, 2020b, 2020a; Mollee, 2017; Short Gianotti & Hurley, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Garekae & Shackleton, 2020b, 2020a; Mollee, 2017; Short Gianotti & Hurley, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":940,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B47KJTK8"],"itemData":{"id":940,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Urban ecosystems provide goods and services critical to livelihood sustenance, environmental protection and sustainability. However, the potential role of some urban ecosystem services such as the provisioning services, to promote liveable cities and livelihood resilience is seldom considered. This study reports on the prevalence and pattern of wild plant foraging and uses in the towns of Potchefstroom and Thabazimbi, South Africa, based on a random sample of 374 households. Descriptive and inferential statistics were used for analysing data. Foraging wild plants was integral to livelihoods and traditions and provided household energy, dietary diversity, health, and cultural affirmation, among others. About 68 % of the respondents reported foraging wild plants, albeit at varying degrees between and within towns. The prevalence of foraging differed significantly between and within towns, being higher in Thabazimbi and for residents on the outskirts of town. Although urban foraging transcends demographic and socio-economic backgrounds, the likelihood of foraging significantly differed in relation to childhood foraging background, perception towards the practice, location of residence in town and household affluence. Nevertheless, foraging was not limited to poorer households, as one-third of the more affluent households also foraged. Foraging occurred in both public and private green spaces, with public spaces being the most frequently visited. Motivations towards foraging were quite diverse and varied across space, context and over time. Overall, the high prevalence of foraging exhibited in this study and that from elsewhere should not be overlooked in urban landscape planning and development.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2020.126581","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126581","title":"Urban foraging of wild plants in two medium-sized South African towns: People, perceptions and practices","title-short":"Urban foraging of wild plants in two medium-sized South African towns","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Garekae","given":"Hesekia"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":934,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NSVCVCND"],"itemData":{"id":934,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Globally, approximately one billion people bene?t from contributions of wild foods to their food security and dietary diversity. Wild foods are known to be important in rural communities in terms of food and micronutrient provision, diversifying diets, reducing vulnerability to non-communicable diseases and overall health. However, the potential contribution of wild foods towards food security and dietary diversity in urban food systems has been largely overlooked. This study examined the contribution of wild foods to household dietary diversity in two towns in South Africa, based on a survey of 137 households. Household diets were quite diverse, with half (51%) having consumed ? 8 food groups, 39% consumed 6 or 7 food groups, and only 10% recorded ? 5 food groups in the previous 48 h. Wild foods were prevalent across the sample, with 62% of the households consuming them to some degree. Wild vegetables and fruits were the most common wild foods, consumed by 96 and 79% of the households, respectively. Although wild foods had limited signi?cance on overall dietary diversity, they exhibited substantial contributions within particular food groups. For example, the consumption of vegetables and oil and fats was most prevalent among households consuming wild foods than those who did not. The ?ndings show that wild foods could contribute towards diversifying urban diets at a micro-level, within particular food groups consumed, such as vegetables and fruits. Hence, wild foods are important in ameliorating the monotonous diets of some households and in turn promoting dietary diversity.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su12020678","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"678","title":"Foraging Wild Food in Urban Spaces: The Contribution of Wild Foods to Urban Dietary Diversity in South Africa","title-short":"Foraging Wild Food in Urban Spaces","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Garekae","given":"Hesekia"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1,16]]}}},{"id":927,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V5VAYSM3"],"itemData":{"id":927,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land Use Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.landusepol.2017.01.020","ISSN":"02648377","journalAbbreviation":"Land Use Policy","language":"en","page":"67-77","title":"Into the urban wild: Collection of wild urban plants for food and medicine in Kampala, Uganda","title-short":"Into the urban wild","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Mollee","given":"Eefke"},{"family":"Pouliot","given":"Mariève"},{"family":"McDonald","given":"Morag A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":922,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2XJIZR2I"],"itemData":{"id":922,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land Use Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.landusepol.2016.06.019","ISSN":"02648377","journalAbbreviation":"Land Use Policy","language":"en","page":"555-563","title":"Gathering plants and fungi along the urban-rural gradient: Uncovering differences in the attitudes and practices among urban, suburban, and rural landowners","title-short":"Gathering plants and fungi along the urban-rural gradient","volume":"57","author":[{"family":"Short Gianotti","given":"Anne G."},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Most urban gathering in the developed world is not commercially oriented; products are mainly for personal consumption and gifting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1r0q48t8el","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Charnley, McLain, & Poe, 2018; Rebecca J McLain, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Charnley, McLain, & Poe, 2018; Rebecca J McLain, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":956,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N8A22WKY"],"itemData":{"id":956,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1080/08941920.2017.1413696","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"734–750","title":"Natural resource access rights and wrongs: Nontimber forest products gathering in urban environments","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Charnley","given":"Susan"},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J"},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":957,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEIARWXM"],"itemData":{"id":957,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Local environment","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1080/13549839.2013.841659","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"220–240","title":"Gathering “wild” food in the city: rethinking the role of foraging in urban ecosystem planning and management","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T"},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R"},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In countries in the Global South, rapid urbanization, unplanned settlements, and poor service delivery mean that it remains vital to gather for self-provisioning and income. A substantial contribution of total household income can be generated from urban gathering, particularly in poorer households ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a9oqq6effr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Borelli, 2020; Dejouhanet & de Bercegol, 2019; Kaoma & Shackleton, 2015; Somesh, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Borelli, 2020; Dejouhanet & de Bercegol, 2019; Kaoma & Shackleton, 2015; Somesh, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":929,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZK5U5I4K"],"itemData":{"id":929,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Overlooked in national reports and in conservation programs, wild food plants (WFPs) have been a vital component of food and nutrition security for centuries. Recently, several countries have reported on the widespread and regular consumption of WFPs, particularly by rural and indigenous communities but also in urban contexts. They are reported as critical for livelihood resilience and for providing essential micronutrients to people enduring food shortages or other emergency situations. However, threats derived from changes in land use and climate, overexploitation and urbanization are reducing the availability of these biological resources in the wild and contributing to the loss of traditional knowledge associated with their use. Meanwhile, few policy measures are in place explicitly targeting their conservation and sustainable use. This can be partially attributed to a lack of scienti?c evidence and awareness among policymakers and relevant stakeholders of the untapped potential of WFPs, accompanied by market and non-market barriers limiting their use. This paper reviews recent e?orts being undertaken in several countries to build evidence of the importance of WFPs, while providing examples of cross-sectoral cooperation and multi-stakeholder approaches that are contributing to advance their conservation and sustainable use. An integrated conservation approach is proposed contributing to secure their availability for future generations.","container-title":"Plants","DOI":"10.3390/plants9101299","ISSN":"2223-7747","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Plants","language":"en","page":"1299","title":"Born to Eat Wild: An Integrated Conservation Approach to Secure Wild Food Plants for Food Security and Nutrition","title-short":"Born to Eat Wild","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Borelli","given":"Teresa"},{"family":"Hunter","given":"Danny"},{"family":"Powell","given":"Bronwen"},{"family":"Ulian","given":"Tiziana"},{"family":"Mattana","given":"Efisio"},{"family":"Termote","given":"Céline"},{"family":"Pawera","given":"Lukas"},{"family":"Beltrame","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Penafiel","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Tan","given":"Ayfer"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Mary"},{"family":"Engels","given":"Johannes"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,1]]}}},{"id":925,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7EA4FG6V"],"itemData":{"id":925,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EchoGéo [Online]","DOI":"10.4000/echogeo.17468","title":"New Geographies of Collection: Crossed perspectives on modern ”gatherers” - Introduction","URL":"https://journals.openedition.org/echogeo/17468","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Dejouhanet","given":"Lucie"},{"family":"Bercegol","given":"Rémi","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":953,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P2QST5TY"],"itemData":{"id":953,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Valuation of the contribution of non-timber forest products (NTFPs) to household incomes has been well researched in the rural and remote areas of the developing world. In comparison, there has been little investigation of the contribution of NTFPs in urban areas and amongst the urban poor. This paper reports results from a survey of 450 households across three towns on the use and value of tree NTFPs collected by households in the poorer areas from their own homesteads, open spaces within towns and at the urban periphery. Collection (and purchase) of tree NTFPs was widespread, especially of ?rewood and fruits. The ratio collected from homesteads relative to other urban spaces differed between products and the amount required. Overall, approximately 20% of household income was derived from urban tree NTFPs. The highest contribution (33%) was amongst the poorest sectors represented by mostly recent migrants to towns who were living in informal settlements whilst trying to establish a foothold in the urban economy. In the formal housing areas the contribution was at least 14%, which has been overlooked by standard income surveys in urban areas.","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2015.08.005","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy Econ","language":"en","page":"104-112","title":"The direct-use value of urban tree non-timber forest products to household income in poorer suburbs in South African towns","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Kaoma","given":"Humphrey"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":947,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D6XJCKVT"],"itemData":{"id":947,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Urban foraging is widely prevalent globally, and can provide foragers benefits like additional income and nutritional supplements. However, urban foraging is largely understudied and we have little information on types of species foraged, patterns of foraging, socio-economic profile of foragers, and impact of urbanization on foraging. In this study, we aimed to identify patterns of urban foraging in one of the fastest growing cities in the world, Bengaluru in India. Our specific objectives were to identify a) the foragers b) species foraged and foraging sites c) impact of urbanization on foraging and d) the barriers to foraging. We collected our information through semi-structured interviews with residents from four sites selected across a gradient of urbanization. Sixteen per cent of the respondents reported that they foraged, most of who were women (97 %) belonging to socially (90 %) and economically (81 %) disadvantaged groups. Seventy-six species were foraged across the four sites for food (76 %), medicine (26 %), and cultural practices (18 %). Foraging occurred in unused land (32 %), parks (32 %), farmlands (12 %), native villages (12 %), nearby woodlands (9%) and home gardens (3%). We found significant differences in foragers along a gradient of urbanization (chi square = 34.56, p = 1.5 × 10? 7, df = 3) with most foragers at the least urbanized site (40 % of the respondents) and least foragers at the most urbanized site (2% of the respondents). Lack of knowledge of foraged species (66 %) and lack of access (55 %) were the biggest barriers to foraging. Our study demonstrates that foraging occurs in a metropolis like Bengaluru with foragers generally belonging to lower socio-economic backgrounds, who are most vulnerable to changes due to urbanization. Redesigning urban environments to create green spaces for forgeable species to flourish, that are made accessible to marginalized communities, is essential for their nutritional and cultural well-being.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2020.126940","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126940","title":"Patterns of urban foraging in Bengaluru city","volume":"57","author":[{"family":"Somesh","given":"Dhruthi"},{"family":"Rao","given":"Rohit"},{"family":"Murali","given":"Ranjini"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the potential of urban gathering to affect food sovereignty and security is not evenly distributed across socioeconomic strata ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a13h5qv9t4q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bunge, Diemont, Bunge, & Harris, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Bunge, Diemont, Bunge, & Harris, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":930,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6RPADJ5"],"itemData":{"id":930,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Urban foraging is an under-explored facet of the alternative food movement. Foraging can improve urban food systems by contributing to nutrition (food security) and cultural appropriateness and community engagement (food sovereignty). This study quanti?es the nutrition available from foraged products in Syracuse, New York, using four common urban species: serviceberry, mulberry, apple and black walnut. Fruit from trees of each species was harvested and weighed weekly during the 2016 growing season. Seasonal mean yield estimates were low relative to orchard crop yields, as many trees produced no fruit. Overall, large variation was present in yield. Tree diameter was positively correlated with yield. Reasons for low yields in general included late spring frost, summer drought and herbivory, as well as other species-speci?c factors. Edible fruit tree availability throughout Syracuse was also compared to neighborhood household income levels. Edible trees are overall more available in high income neighborhoods. During sampling, foraging was also informally discussed with homeowners and passersby; strong interest in the practice was found across demographics. We conclude this study with recommendations for improving yields and designing a forage-rich urban forest.","container-title":"Journal of Urban Ecology","DOI":"10.1093/jue/juy028","ISSN":"2058-5543","issue":"1","language":"en","title":"Urban foraging for food security and sovereignty: quantifying edible forest yield in Syracuse, New York using four common fruit- and nut-producing street tree species","title-short":"Urban foraging for food security and sovereignty","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Bunge","given":"Avalon"},{"family":"Diemont","given":"Stewart A. W."},{"family":"Bunge","given":"John A."},{"family":"Harris","given":"Stephen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Most gatherers acquire and pass on knowledge about gathering practices through family and friends or gathering trips ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2m85209mfp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garekae & Shackleton, 2020b, 2020a; Rebecca J McLain, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Garekae & Shackleton, 2020b, 2020a; Rebecca J McLain, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":940,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B47KJTK8"],"itemData":{"id":940,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Urban ecosystems provide goods and services critical to livelihood sustenance, environmental protection and sustainability. However, the potential role of some urban ecosystem services such as the provisioning services, to promote liveable cities and livelihood resilience is seldom considered. This study reports on the prevalence and pattern of wild plant foraging and uses in the towns of Potchefstroom and Thabazimbi, South Africa, based on a random sample of 374 households. Descriptive and inferential statistics were used for analysing data. Foraging wild plants was integral to livelihoods and traditions and provided household energy, dietary diversity, health, and cultural affirmation, among others. About 68 % of the respondents reported foraging wild plants, albeit at varying degrees between and within towns. The prevalence of foraging differed significantly between and within towns, being higher in Thabazimbi and for residents on the outskirts of town. Although urban foraging transcends demographic and socio-economic backgrounds, the likelihood of foraging significantly differed in relation to childhood foraging background, perception towards the practice, location of residence in town and household affluence. Nevertheless, foraging was not limited to poorer households, as one-third of the more affluent households also foraged. Foraging occurred in both public and private green spaces, with public spaces being the most frequently visited. Motivations towards foraging were quite diverse and varied across space, context and over time. Overall, the high prevalence of foraging exhibited in this study and that from elsewhere should not be overlooked in urban landscape planning and development.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2020.126581","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126581","title":"Urban foraging of wild plants in two medium-sized South African towns: People, perceptions and practices","title-short":"Urban foraging of wild plants in two medium-sized South African towns","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Garekae","given":"Hesekia"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":934,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NSVCVCND"],"itemData":{"id":934,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Globally, approximately one billion people bene?t from contributions of wild foods to their food security and dietary diversity. Wild foods are known to be important in rural communities in terms of food and micronutrient provision, diversifying diets, reducing vulnerability to non-communicable diseases and overall health. However, the potential contribution of wild foods towards food security and dietary diversity in urban food systems has been largely overlooked. This study examined the contribution of wild foods to household dietary diversity in two towns in South Africa, based on a survey of 137 households. Household diets were quite diverse, with half (51%) having consumed ? 8 food groups, 39% consumed 6 or 7 food groups, and only 10% recorded ? 5 food groups in the previous 48 h. Wild foods were prevalent across the sample, with 62% of the households consuming them to some degree. Wild vegetables and fruits were the most common wild foods, consumed by 96 and 79% of the households, respectively. Although wild foods had limited signi?cance on overall dietary diversity, they exhibited substantial contributions within particular food groups. For example, the consumption of vegetables and oil and fats was most prevalent among households consuming wild foods than those who did not. The ?ndings show that wild foods could contribute towards diversifying urban diets at a micro-level, within particular food groups consumed, such as vegetables and fruits. Hence, wild foods are important in ameliorating the monotonous diets of some households and in turn promoting dietary diversity.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su12020678","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"678","title":"Foraging Wild Food in Urban Spaces: The Contribution of Wild Foods to Urban Dietary Diversity in South Africa","title-short":"Foraging Wild Food in Urban Spaces","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Garekae","given":"Hesekia"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1,16]]}}},{"id":957,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEIARWXM"],"itemData":{"id":957,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Local environment","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1080/13549839.2013.841659","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"220–240","title":"Gathering “wild” food in the city: rethinking the role of foraging in urban ecosystem planning and management","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T"},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R"},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Oral transmission, amateur society outings, professional scientists, books, and field guides help counteract the decline in more traditional outdoor gathering activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ncbv1qk49","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u321{}uczaj, 2021; Rebecca J McLain, 2014; Palliwoda, 2017)","plainCitation":"(?uczaj, 2021; Rebecca J McLain, 2014; Palliwoda, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":937,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N8FQZKRQ"],"itemData":{"id":937,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Foraging in the British Isles is an increasingly popular activity for both personal consumption and for commercial purposes. While legislation and guidelines exist regulating the sustainable collection of wild edibles, the founding principles of the British foraging movement are not well documented. For this research, 36 of the most active foraging instructors of the Association of Foragers were interviewed to understand their background, species collected, sources of knowledge, and problems faced during collection. Altogether, 102 species of leafy vegetables, fruits, fungi, and seaweeds were mentioned as frequently used, while 34 species of roadkill animals were listed, mostly for personal consumption. Instructors reported learning from wild food guidebooks, other foragers, or personal experience. Frequent contact among foragers has led to the standardisation of knowledge and practices among them forming a “new tradition”, partly based on old British traditions but modi?ed by in?uences from other countries and cultures, both in terms of choice of species and processing techniques. Contrary to expectations, foragers rarely reported clashes with nature conservation or forestry managers. The authors argue that knowledge and practice developed by the Association of Foragers (AoF) are sustainable and could be integrated into the British food and nature conservation system.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su13063478","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"3478","title":"The Ethnobiology of Contemporary British Foragers: Foods They Teach, Their Sources of Inspiration and Impact","title-short":"The Ethnobiology of Contemporary British Foragers","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"?uczaj","given":"?ukasz"},{"family":"Wilde","given":"Monica"},{"family":"Townsend","given":"Leanne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,21]]}}},{"id":957,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEIARWXM"],"itemData":{"id":957,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Local environment","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1080/13549839.2013.841659","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"220–240","title":"Gathering “wild” food in the city: rethinking the role of foraging in urban ecosystem planning and management","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T"},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R"},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":933,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9TB76EG"],"itemData":{"id":933,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","DOI":"10.1016/j.landurbplan.2016.09.003","ISSN":"01692046","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape and Urban Planning","language":"en","page":"394-406","title":"Human-biodiversity interactions in urban parks: The species level matters","title-short":"Human-biodiversity interactions in urban parks","volume":"157","author":[{"family":"Palliwoda","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"},{"family":"Lippe","given":"Moritz","non-dropping-particle":"von der"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Stakeholders exchange information on the nature of green spaces, species and ecosystems and allied activities. City managers can make use of gatherers' extensive local ecological knowledge to inform more formal management practices and support the overall management of urban natural areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a66pq1unfa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. J. McLain, 2017; Mallika Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2020)","plainCitation":"(R. J. McLain, 2017; Mallika Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":921,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VFC5RUFZ"],"itemData":{"id":921,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Our research seeks to expand the concept of urban environmental stewardship to include the everyday stewardship practices of urban nontimber forest products foragers. Ethnographic data from 58 urban foragers and 18 land stewards in the city of Seattle (USA) revealed that foragers reported using a variety of practices to enhance and minimize negative desirable species and their habitats. Many of these practices were identical to those practiced by restoration volunteers in formal programs and align with Seattle Parks Department management objectives. Foragers actively sought to learn more about what practices are sustainable; many mentored others in sustainable harvesting practices. Most foragers emphasized the importance of treating plants and their environment with respect. The land stewards voiced some concerns about foraging in city parks, but most were cautiously supportive of opening up the parks to foragers. The study results suggest that an opportunity exists for park managers to develop alliances with foragers so as to leverage foragers’ everyday stewardship practices and accomplish some of their park restoration objectives. Doing so will require park managers to acknowledge the positive contributions that humans can to make novel ecosystems and foragers to develop or strengthen selfregulation norms.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2017.10.005","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"36-42","title":"Urban non-timber forest products stewardship practices among foragers in Seattle, washington (USA)","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"R. J."},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R."},{"family":"Urgenson","given":"Lauren S."},{"family":"Blahna","given":"Dale J."},{"family":"Buttolph","given":"Lita P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":945,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/56PGVHNQ"],"itemData":{"id":945,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Gathering of uncultivated food from green spaces, also known as foraging, is observed in urban areas across the world, but the literature focuses predominantly on the global north. Our study examines the existing urban land management structure and its approach to urban foraging in the eastern coastal region of South Africa. Through interviews with municipal officials in nine cities, we identified different stakeholders and their roles in urban green space management. We then used network analysis to represent interactions and influence of these stakeholders, and environmental worldviews to determine organisational and perceptual barriers to and enablers of foraging in urban green spaces. The policy on urban green space management, as well as land managers themselves are amenable to the concept of foraging in public spaces. Lack of knowledge on wild indigenous species and sustainable offtake, ambiguous, coarse, or lacking policy, and normative views of pristine nature may hinder foraging. We recommend pathways for policy and stakeholder partnerships to incorporate sustainable foraging in their biodiversity conservation and land stewardship strategies. Received: February 20, 2020 Accepted: March 5, 2020 Published: April 7, 2020 Copyright: © 2020 Sardeshpande, Shackleton. This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original author and source are credited.","container-title":"PLoS One","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0230693","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"e0230693","title":"Urban foraging: Land management policy, perspectives, and potential","title-short":"Urban foraging","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Sardeshpande","given":"Mallika"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"}],"editor":[{"family":"Rozylowicz","given":"Laurentiu"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",4,7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Voluntary codes of conduct may be the best way to manage urban gathering to prevent over-harvesting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2aq52eiha0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Charnley, 2018; R. J. McLain, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Charnley, 2018; R. J. McLain, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":956,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N8A22WKY"],"itemData":{"id":956,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1080/08941920.2017.1413696","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"734–750","title":"Natural resource access rights and wrongs: Nontimber forest products gathering in urban environments","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Charnley","given":"Susan"},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J"},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":921,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VFC5RUFZ"],"itemData":{"id":921,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Our research seeks to expand the concept of urban environmental stewardship to include the everyday stewardship practices of urban nontimber forest products foragers. Ethnographic data from 58 urban foragers and 18 land stewards in the city of Seattle (USA) revealed that foragers reported using a variety of practices to enhance and minimize negative desirable species and their habitats. Many of these practices were identical to those practiced by restoration volunteers in formal programs and align with Seattle Parks Department management objectives. Foragers actively sought to learn more about what practices are sustainable; many mentored others in sustainable harvesting practices. Most foragers emphasized the importance of treating plants and their environment with respect. The land stewards voiced some concerns about foraging in city parks, but most were cautiously supportive of opening up the parks to foragers. The study results suggest that an opportunity exists for park managers to develop alliances with foragers so as to leverage foragers’ everyday stewardship practices and accomplish some of their park restoration objectives. Doing so will require park managers to acknowledge the positive contributions that humans can to make novel ecosystems and foragers to develop or strengthen selfregulation norms.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2017.10.005","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"36-42","title":"Urban non-timber forest products stewardship practices among foragers in Seattle, washington (USA)","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"R. J."},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R."},{"family":"Urgenson","given":"Lauren S."},{"family":"Blahna","given":"Dale J."},{"family":"Buttolph","given":"Lita P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urban gatherers usually select common wild plant species and plant parts that have little impact on the reproduction of plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ar9b438cek","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schunko, Wild, & Brandner, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Schunko, Wild, & Brandner, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":948,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/74ZCMI3X"],"itemData":{"id":948,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wild food foraging in urban areas can have considerable benefits, but concerns have been raised by public authorities about its potential negative ecological impacts. In this study, our aim was to explore experts’ per­ spectives on the foraging practices that prevent, limit, or cause negative ecological impacts in urban areas, and how different levels of local ecological knowledge among foragers translate into the (non-)adoption of preventive or limiting measures. We conducted twenty-one semi-structured expert interviews with urban foragers, envi­ ronmental educators, managers of public urban green spaces, open space planners and representatives of research institutions and non-governmental institutions in Vienna, Austria, and analysed the data using quali­ tative content analysis. A range of preventive, limiting, and damaging practices were reported as taking place at the same time. The ecological impact of foraging practices related to the selection of plant species and foraging locations, the quantities foraged, the foraging techniques applied, foraging dates and the care taken when foraging. The experts suggested that two different types of impacts can be harmful: first, urban foragers can cause negative ecological impacts to plant populations if they are not familiar with the species being foraged or with foraging in public urban green spaces; second, urban foragers can harm the integrity of public urban green spaces in foraging hotspots in particular. The biospheric versus utilitarian mindsets of urban foragers and their nature relatedness were also associated with the ecological impacts. We argue that medium and long-term measures to limit damage to urban plant populations should include population studies of plant populations observed to be at risk, clear legal frameworks for wild plant foraging, and a revitalisation of the declining traditional transmission of local ecological knowledge combined with comprehensive and innovative educational measures.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2021.127164","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"127164","title":"Exploring and limiting the ecological impacts of urban wild food foraging in Vienna, Austria","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Schunko","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Wild","given":"Anna-Sophie"},{"family":"Brandner","given":"Anjoulie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many gatherers have adopted the “principles of practice” and appropriate techniques for preventing or limiting negative ecological impacts; meanwhile, they teach and promulgate sustainable and responsible harvesting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a10o3pq06p5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u321{}uczaj, 2021; Schunko, 2021)","plainCitation":"(?uczaj, 2021; Schunko, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":937,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N8FQZKRQ"],"itemData":{"id":937,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Foraging in the British Isles is an increasingly popular activity for both personal consumption and for commercial purposes. While legislation and guidelines exist regulating the sustainable collection of wild edibles, the founding principles of the British foraging movement are not well documented. For this research, 36 of the most active foraging instructors of the Association of Foragers were interviewed to understand their background, species collected, sources of knowledge, and problems faced during collection. Altogether, 102 species of leafy vegetables, fruits, fungi, and seaweeds were mentioned as frequently used, while 34 species of roadkill animals were listed, mostly for personal consumption. Instructors reported learning from wild food guidebooks, other foragers, or personal experience. Frequent contact among foragers has led to the standardisation of knowledge and practices among them forming a “new tradition”, partly based on old British traditions but modi?ed by in?uences from other countries and cultures, both in terms of choice of species and processing techniques. Contrary to expectations, foragers rarely reported clashes with nature conservation or forestry managers. The authors argue that knowledge and practice developed by the Association of Foragers (AoF) are sustainable and could be integrated into the British food and nature conservation system.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su13063478","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"3478","title":"The Ethnobiology of Contemporary British Foragers: Foods They Teach, Their Sources of Inspiration and Impact","title-short":"The Ethnobiology of Contemporary British Foragers","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"?uczaj","given":"?ukasz"},{"family":"Wilde","given":"Monica"},{"family":"Townsend","given":"Leanne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,21]]}}},{"id":948,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/74ZCMI3X"],"itemData":{"id":948,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wild food foraging in urban areas can have considerable benefits, but concerns have been raised by public authorities about its potential negative ecological impacts. In this study, our aim was to explore experts’ per­ spectives on the foraging practices that prevent, limit, or cause negative ecological impacts in urban areas, and how different levels of local ecological knowledge among foragers translate into the (non-)adoption of preventive or limiting measures. We conducted twenty-one semi-structured expert interviews with urban foragers, envi­ ronmental educators, managers of public urban green spaces, open space planners and representatives of research institutions and non-governmental institutions in Vienna, Austria, and analysed the data using quali­ tative content analysis. A range of preventive, limiting, and damaging practices were reported as taking place at the same time. The ecological impact of foraging practices related to the selection of plant species and foraging locations, the quantities foraged, the foraging techniques applied, foraging dates and the care taken when foraging. The experts suggested that two different types of impacts can be harmful: first, urban foragers can cause negative ecological impacts to plant populations if they are not familiar with the species being foraged or with foraging in public urban green spaces; second, urban foragers can harm the integrity of public urban green spaces in foraging hotspots in particular. The biospheric versus utilitarian mindsets of urban foragers and their nature relatedness were also associated with the ecological impacts. We argue that medium and long-term measures to limit damage to urban plant populations should include population studies of plant populations observed to be at risk, clear legal frameworks for wild plant foraging, and a revitalisation of the declining traditional transmission of local ecological knowledge combined with comprehensive and innovative educational measures.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2021.127164","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"127164","title":"Exploring and limiting the ecological impacts of urban wild food foraging in Vienna, Austria","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Schunko","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Wild","given":"Anna-Sophie"},{"family":"Brandner","given":"Anjoulie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Despite these benefits, urban gathering is not extensive enough to be considered as a solution to multiple challenges within the food system ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aoeidi5vq5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nyman, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Nyman, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":936,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BZQADFBW"],"itemData":{"id":936,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"While e?orts have been made to reframe cities as food-producing landscapes, it remains important to attend to the contingent and relational matter of food, and the potentially ambiguous role that urban space itself may play in its ?gurative and material de?nitions. Urban food scholarship or practice that focuses uniquely on concerns of production and provision tends to overlook the blurry edges and ontological uncertainty of what food is, what is food and how matter becomes food. This paper partly draws on existing work connected to the relational materialism of food and its various modes of becoming, and re-examines this with reference to the practices of ‘urban food foraging’ in London, UK. It builds on mixed-method ?eldwork in London and develops a series of vignettes concerning unattended and unintended plants in the city and their uncertain status as potential foodstu?s. The central argument is that the ontological status of ‘food’ cannot be found in its edibleness or bare matter but through the varying material, cultural, legal, environmental, scienti?c and spatial assemblages which give rise to it. ‘Urban foraging’ helps reveal this tension through the practices’ transgressive qualities and the disruptive in?uence of the proximity, density and diversity of urban space. In so doing, it presents the moment of picking or acquisition as an additional – but not de?nitive – moment of ‘meaning-making’ in the food system, serving to widen our focus too often ?xated on speci?c moments in linear renderings of production, consumption and disposal. As a challenge to our otherwise shared understanding of food, it speaks to those seeking to recon?gure the position of cities in the food system (and food production within cities), those concerned with the greening of urban environments, and those seeking alternative modes of generating and distributing food.","container-title":"Geoforum","DOI":"10.1016/j.geoforum.2018.10.009","ISSN":"00167185","journalAbbreviation":"Geoforum","language":"en","page":"170-180","title":"Food, meaning-making and ontological uncertainty: Exploring ‘urban foraging’ and productive landscapes in London","title-short":"Food, meaning-making and ontological uncertainty","volume":"99","author":[{"family":"Nyman","given":"Marcus"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. With some exceptions (e.g., cities in the Pacific region ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1hah3r9n8b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Borelli, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Borelli, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":929,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZK5U5I4K"],"itemData":{"id":929,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Overlooked in national reports and in conservation programs, wild food plants (WFPs) have been a vital component of food and nutrition security for centuries. Recently, several countries have reported on the widespread and regular consumption of WFPs, particularly by rural and indigenous communities but also in urban contexts. They are reported as critical for livelihood resilience and for providing essential micronutrients to people enduring food shortages or other emergency situations. However, threats derived from changes in land use and climate, overexploitation and urbanization are reducing the availability of these biological resources in the wild and contributing to the loss of traditional knowledge associated with their use. Meanwhile, few policy measures are in place explicitly targeting their conservation and sustainable use. This can be partially attributed to a lack of scienti?c evidence and awareness among policymakers and relevant stakeholders of the untapped potential of WFPs, accompanied by market and non-market barriers limiting their use. This paper reviews recent e?orts being undertaken in several countries to build evidence of the importance of WFPs, while providing examples of cross-sectoral cooperation and multi-stakeholder approaches that are contributing to advance their conservation and sustainable use. An integrated conservation approach is proposed contributing to secure their availability for future generations.","container-title":"Plants","DOI":"10.3390/plants9101299","ISSN":"2223-7747","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Plants","language":"en","page":"1299","title":"Born to Eat Wild: An Integrated Conservation Approach to Secure Wild Food Plants for Food Security and Nutrition","title-short":"Born to Eat Wild","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Borelli","given":"Teresa"},{"family":"Hunter","given":"Danny"},{"family":"Powell","given":"Bronwen"},{"family":"Ulian","given":"Tiziana"},{"family":"Mattana","given":"Efisio"},{"family":"Termote","given":"Céline"},{"family":"Pawera","given":"Lukas"},{"family":"Beltrame","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Penafiel","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Tan","given":"Ayfer"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Mary"},{"family":"Engels","given":"Johannes"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}), the average contribution of wild algae, fungi and plants to diets is low ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2pdgfjpgd5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Shackleton, 2017)","plainCitation":"(C. Shackleton, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33678,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ENAQFCLB"],"itemData":{"id":33678,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Although hardly noticed or formally recognised, urban foraging by humans probably occurs in all urban settings around the world. We draw from research in India, South Africa, Sweden, and the United States to demonstrate the ubiquity and varied nature of urban foraging in different contexts. Across these different contexts, we distil seven themes that characterise and thereby advance thinking about research and the understanding of urban foraging. We show that it is widespread and occurs across a variety of urban spaces and places. The species used and the local practices vary between contexts, and are in constant ?ux as urban ecological and social settings change. This requires that urban foragers are knowledgeable about diverse species, harvest locations, and rights of access, and that their practices are adaptable to changing contexts. Despite its ubiquity, most cities have some forms of regulations that prohibit or discourage urban foraging. We highlight a few important exceptions that can provide prototypes and lessons for other cities regarding supportive policy frameworks and initiatives. The formulation of dynamic policy, design, and management strategies in support of urban foraging will bene?t from understanding the common characteristics of foraging in cities worldwide, but also will require comprehension of the speci?c and dynamic contexts in which they would be implemented.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su9101884","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"10","language":"en","page":"1884","title":"Urban Foraging: A Ubiquitous Human Practice Overlooked by Urban Planners, Policy, and Research","title-short":"Urban Foraging","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Dahlberg","given":"Annika"},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",10,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} due to lower tree density in urban spaces, the relatively low proportion of edible parts, or both ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2pvfuisa00","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bunge, 2019; Estela, Ghermandi, & Margutti, 1995)","plainCitation":"(Bunge, 2019; Estela, Ghermandi, & Margutti, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":930,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6RPADJ5"],"itemData":{"id":930,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Urban foraging is an under-explored facet of the alternative food movement. Foraging can improve urban food systems by contributing to nutrition (food security) and cultural appropriateness and community engagement (food sovereignty). This study quanti?es the nutrition available from foraged products in Syracuse, New York, using four common urban species: serviceberry, mulberry, apple and black walnut. Fruit from trees of each species was harvested and weighed weekly during the 2016 growing season. Seasonal mean yield estimates were low relative to orchard crop yields, as many trees produced no fruit. Overall, large variation was present in yield. Tree diameter was positively correlated with yield. Reasons for low yields in general included late spring frost, summer drought and herbivory, as well as other species-speci?c factors. Edible fruit tree availability throughout Syracuse was also compared to neighborhood household income levels. Edible trees are overall more available in high income neighborhoods. During sampling, foraging was also informally discussed with homeowners and passersby; strong interest in the practice was found across demographics. We conclude this study with recommendations for improving yields and designing a forage-rich urban forest.","container-title":"Journal of Urban Ecology","DOI":"10.1093/jue/juy028","ISSN":"2058-5543","issue":"1","language":"en","title":"Urban foraging for food security and sovereignty: quantifying edible forest yield in Syracuse, New York using four common fruit- and nut-producing street tree species","title-short":"Urban foraging for food security and sovereignty","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Bunge","given":"Avalon"},{"family":"Diemont","given":"Stewart A. W."},{"family":"Bunge","given":"John A."},{"family":"Harris","given":"Stephen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1,1]]}}},{"id":955,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q2YQDTBS"],"itemData":{"id":955,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bulletin of the Ecological Society of America","language":"en","page":"4","title":"Edible Weeds: A Scarcely Used Resource","author":[{"family":"Estela","given":"Eduardo H. Rapoport"},{"family":"Ghermandi","given":"Raffaele Luciana"},{"family":"Margutti","given":"Laura"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are also concerns and potentially physical health risks from eating wild plants or fungi grown on contaminated urban land ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ad4ml4qusk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. J. McLain, 2012; A. Russo, 2017)","plainCitation":"(R. J. McLain, 2012; A. Russo, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":950,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YHVD6VPJ"],"itemData":{"id":950,"type":"report","event-place":"Portland, OR","language":"en","number":"PNW-GTR-849","page":"PNW-GTR-849","publisher":"U.S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service, Pacific Northwest Research Station","publisher-place":"Portland, OR","title":"Gathering in the city: an annotated bibliography and review of the literature about human-plant interactions in urban ecosystems","title-short":"Gathering in the city","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"R. J."},{"family":"MacFarland","given":"K."},{"family":"Brody","given":"L."},{"family":"Hebert","given":"J."},{"family":"Hurley","given":"P."},{"family":"Poe","given":"M."},{"family":"Buttolph","given":"L. P."},{"family":"Gabriel","given":"N."},{"family":"Dzuna","given":"M."},{"family":"Emery","given":"M. R."},{"family":"Charnley","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":926,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WTXR89BV"],"itemData":{"id":926,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Recently published green infrastructure, nature-based solutions, and ecosystem disservices (ED) literature have focused primarily on the supply of urban regulating and cultural ecosystem services (ES). Other literature on urban and peri-urban agriculture has mostly studied the role of localized, intensive agricultural practices in providing food to inhabitants. The aim of this review is to raise awareness and stress the knowledge gap on the importance of urban provisioning ES, particularly when implementing an edible green infrastructure (EGI) approach as it can o?er improved resilience and quality of life in cities. We compiled and systematically analyzed studies on urban ES and ED related to a number of EGI typologies. Our systematic review of the relevant literature via an EGI framework, identi?ed more than 80 peer-reviewed publications that focused on ES and food production in urban areas. An EGI approach can contribute socially, economically, and environmentally to urban sustainability and food security. However, such bene?ts must be weighed against ED trade-o?s, including: potential health risks caused by human exposure to heavy metals and organic chemical contaminants often present in urban surroundings. We conclude with recommendations and guidelines for incorporating EGI into urban planning and design, and discuss novel areas for future research.","container-title":"Agriculture, Ecosystems & Environment","DOI":"10.1016/j.agee.2017.03.026","ISSN":"01678809","journalAbbreviation":"Agriculture, Ecosystems & Environment","language":"en","page":"53-66","title":"Edible green infrastructure: An approach and review of provisioning ecosystem services and disservices in urban environments","title-short":"Edible green infrastructure","volume":"242","author":[{"family":"Russo","given":"Alessio"},{"family":"Escobedo","given":"Francisco J."},{"family":"Cirella","given":"Giuseppe T."},{"family":"Zerbe","given":"Stefan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the spraying of chemical herbicides and pesticides ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a49aqjlp60","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rebecca J McLain, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Rebecca J McLain, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":957,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SEIARWXM"],"itemData":{"id":957,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Local environment","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1080/13549839.2013.841659","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"220–240","title":"Gathering “wild” food in the city: rethinking the role of foraging in urban ecosystem planning and management","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T"},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R"},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and mistaking potentially toxic species with edible species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a16m74o3gks","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. K. Fischer & Kowarik, 2020)","plainCitation":"(L. K. Fischer & Kowarik, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":941,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3Y9K2TNY"],"itemData":{"id":941,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As global population becomes increasingly urban, opportunities for people to experience nature have decreased. Counteracting this trend is a key challenge for future urban development as interactions of urban people with biodiversity support human health and wellbeing, and may also result in positive attitudes towards biodiversity conservation. Collecting edible plants in urban surroundings, especially outside of gardens (“urban foraging”) is a traditional interaction with nature, based on knowledge about multiple uses of plants. Although some studies exist from different cities around the world, urban foraging has been revealed as a critically understudied phenomenon. We now analyze (i) the relevance of this human-nature interaction in Berlin, one of Europe’s metropolises, (ii) how people’s sociocultural background matters in attitudes of urban foragers vs. non-foragers towards this activity, and (iii) whether urban foraging may lead to conflicts with biodiversity conservation. Our survey revealed urban foraging as a relevant human-nature interaction with a high potential to grow: 33% of 535 respondents already collected edible plants outside of gardens and another 38% would be doing so given certain conditions, e.g. when contamination risks can be excluded. Many sociocultural groups (differing, e.g. on gender, age, childhood experience) shared attitudes towards foraging and existing barriers. Risks to biodiversity seem to be manageable as neither native species nor rare species were over-foraged in relation to species’ abundance in the local flora, with more abundant species being collected more frequently. We conclude that urban foraging can be a powerful tool for connecting urban people to nature without putting native biodiversity at risk. We make a claim for integrated approaches towards environmental policy, environmental education and greenspace management: these should aim on keeping potential health risks at a minimum, and should support urban foraging as a biodiversity-friendly and sustainable human-nature interaction in the cities of tomorrow.","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2020.106087","ISSN":"1470160X","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Indicators","language":"en","page":"106087","title":"Connecting people to biodiversity in cities of tomorrow: Is urban foraging a powerful tool?","title-short":"Connecting people to biodiversity in cities of tomorrow","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Fischer","given":"Leonie K."},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, the wild edible food gathered along freeways and arterial roads often have concentrations of lead exceeding safety levels for human consumption ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1qgk29go96","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Amato-Lourenco, 2020; von Hoffen & S\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}umel, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Amato-Lourenco, 2020; von Hoffen & Säumel, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":935,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CLABPN4M"],"itemData":{"id":935,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Foraging wild-growing edible plants (WEPs) is a re-emerging practice with increasing popularity worldwide, including in urban areas. However, in cities, this practice raises questions about the safety of foraging these plants for human consumption, due to the potential exposure of plants to higher levels of pollutants. In this study, the concentration of 12 elements (Al, V, Cr, Mn, Co, Ni, Zn, As, Rb, Cd, Ba and Pb) in three different WEPs (Amaranthus spp., Plantago tomentosa and Taraxacum of?cinale) were determined according to different traf?c categories in the municipality of São Paulo. Additionally, plants were sampled within the inner areas of three municipal parks in the same study region. Different gradients of elemental concentrations were obtained according to the traf?c categories. Freeways presented higher concentrations of several elements than local roads or parks. For the WEPs collected along freeways and some plants along arterial roads, the concentrations of Pb exceeded safety levels for human consumption. Our data suggest that foraging in large urban centres should be performed preferentially in low-traf?c areas.","container-title":"Science of The Total Environment","DOI":"10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.133967","ISSN":"00489697","journalAbbreviation":"Science of The Total Environment","language":"en","page":"133967","title":"Edible weeds: Are urban environments fit for foraging?","title-short":"Edible weeds","volume":"698","author":[{"family":"Amato-Lourenco","given":"Luís Fernando"},{"family":"Ranieri","given":"Guilherme Reis"},{"family":"Oliveira Souza","given":"Vanessa Cristina","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Junior","given":"Fernando Barbosa"},{"family":"Saldiva","given":"Paulo Hilário Nascimento"},{"family":"Mauad","given":"Thais"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":954,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RMY75L9Q"],"itemData":{"id":954,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Today's urban gardening focuses mainly on vegetable production and rarely includes fruit trees. Health effects of consuming urban crops are questioned due to high local pollution loads. Here, we determined cadmium and lead content in the edible parts of nuts, berries, pome, and stone fruits harvested from fruit trees and shrubs within inner city neighbourhoods of Berlin, Germany. We analysed how local settings at sampling sites shaped the trace metal content. We revealed signi?cant differences in trace metal content depending on species, fruit type, local traf?c, and parameters related to barriers between the sampling site and neighbouring roads. Higher overall traf?c burden and proximity to roads increased whereas buildings or vegetation as barriers reduced trace metal content in the edible biomass. We demonstrate, that the consumption of non-vegetable fruits growing in inner city sites in Berlin does not pose a risk on human health as long as the fruits are thoroughly washed and it is provided that site pollutions and impacts are considered in garden concepts and guidelines.","container-title":"Ecotoxicology and Environmental Safety","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoenv.2013.11.023","ISSN":"01476513","journalAbbreviation":"Ecotoxicology and Environmental Safety","language":"en","page":"233-239","title":"Orchards for edible cities: Cadmium and lead content in nuts, berries, pome and stone fruits harvested within the inner city neighbourhoods in Berlin, Germany","title-short":"Orchards for edible cities","volume":"101","author":[{"family":"Hoffen","given":"Laura Pauline","non-dropping-particle":"von"},{"family":"Säumel","given":"Ina"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Tensions sometimes exist between urban gatherers and land managers, and between gatherers and other citizens over gathering, particularly in public spaces ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"arc2kn36t7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. J. McLain, 2012)","plainCitation":"(R. J. McLain, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":950,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YHVD6VPJ"],"itemData":{"id":950,"type":"report","event-place":"Portland, OR","language":"en","number":"PNW-GTR-849","page":"PNW-GTR-849","publisher":"U.S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service, Pacific Northwest Research Station","publisher-place":"Portland, OR","title":"Gathering in the city: an annotated bibliography and review of the literature about human-plant interactions in urban ecosystems","title-short":"Gathering in the city","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"R. J."},{"family":"MacFarland","given":"K."},{"family":"Brody","given":"L."},{"family":"Hebert","given":"J."},{"family":"Hurley","given":"P."},{"family":"Poe","given":"M."},{"family":"Buttolph","given":"L. P."},{"family":"Gabriel","given":"N."},{"family":"Dzuna","given":"M."},{"family":"Emery","given":"M. R."},{"family":"Charnley","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This varies by region. Gathering in many African cities, for example, is permissible in open urban areas, with tacit support from policy and land managers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ao7lu925o3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mallika Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Mallika Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":945,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/56PGVHNQ"],"itemData":{"id":945,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Gathering of uncultivated food from green spaces, also known as foraging, is observed in urban areas across the world, but the literature focuses predominantly on the global north. Our study examines the existing urban land management structure and its approach to urban foraging in the eastern coastal region of South Africa. Through interviews with municipal officials in nine cities, we identified different stakeholders and their roles in urban green space management. We then used network analysis to represent interactions and influence of these stakeholders, and environmental worldviews to determine organisational and perceptual barriers to and enablers of foraging in urban green spaces. The policy on urban green space management, as well as land managers themselves are amenable to the concept of foraging in public spaces. Lack of knowledge on wild indigenous species and sustainable offtake, ambiguous, coarse, or lacking policy, and normative views of pristine nature may hinder foraging. We recommend pathways for policy and stakeholder partnerships to incorporate sustainable foraging in their biodiversity conservation and land stewardship strategies. Received: February 20, 2020 Accepted: March 5, 2020 Published: April 7, 2020 Copyright: © 2020 Sardeshpande, Shackleton. This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original author and source are credited.","container-title":"PLoS One","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0230693","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"e0230693","title":"Urban foraging: Land management policy, perspectives, and potential","title-short":"Urban foraging","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Sardeshpande","given":"Mallika"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"}],"editor":[{"family":"Rozylowicz","given":"Laurentiu"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",4,7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, in many cities in Europe and North America urban gathering is not widely recognized or encouraged, although it is happening. Many cities have some form of regulations that prohibit or discourage urban foraging ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1supipn4nd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Landor-Yamagata, 2018; Ortez, 2021; C. Shackleton, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Landor-Yamagata, 2018; Ortez, 2021; C. Shackleton, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":931,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V2X4DZN4"],"itemData":{"id":931,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Gathering wild plants in cities (urban foraging) is likely an important, but understudied human-nature interaction globally. As large European cities are critically understudied in this regard, we performed in-depth ethnography-based interviews in Berlin, Germany, to shed light on the cultural background of foragers, their motivations and which plants and fungi are gathered for which purposes. Results demonstrate multiple uses of 125 taxa, mostly frequently-occurring species but also some Red List species, from a range of formal and informal greenspace types. Both native and non-native species were gathered, with signi?cant differences in use patterns. Use for food was most common, followed by medicinal uses, and personal enjoyment was a frequent motivation, indicating that urban foraging combines provisioning and cultural ecosystem services. Familial and childhood foraging exposure were common, pointing to in?uences of early-in-life exposure on later-in-life activities and transgenerational aspects of the practice. Results further suggest legacy effects from the post-war and communist eras on foraging knowledge. Although non-commercial foraging is allowed in Berlin, over-harvesting was not evident. Interviews indicate that stewardship of urban biodiversity is common among foragers. Results thus suggest considering urban foraging as a promising vehicle for linking humans with nature when developing a biodiverse urban green infrastructure.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su10061873","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1873","title":"Urban Foraging in Berlin: People, Plants and Practices within the Metropolitan Green Infrastructure","title-short":"Urban Foraging in Berlin","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Landor-Yamagata","given":"Jonah"},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"},{"family":"Fischer","given":"Leonie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,4]]}}},{"id":949,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TBQHACBU"],"itemData":{"id":949,"type":"thesis","publisher":"The University of Arizona","title":"Foraging in Tucson's Parks: Interest, Barriers, and Opportunities","author":[{"family":"Ortez","given":"Paola"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":33678,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ENAQFCLB"],"itemData":{"id":33678,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Although hardly noticed or formally recognised, urban foraging by humans probably occurs in all urban settings around the world. We draw from research in India, South Africa, Sweden, and the United States to demonstrate the ubiquity and varied nature of urban foraging in different contexts. Across these different contexts, we distil seven themes that characterise and thereby advance thinking about research and the understanding of urban foraging. We show that it is widespread and occurs across a variety of urban spaces and places. The species used and the local practices vary between contexts, and are in constant ?ux as urban ecological and social settings change. This requires that urban foragers are knowledgeable about diverse species, harvest locations, and rights of access, and that their practices are adaptable to changing contexts. Despite its ubiquity, most cities have some forms of regulations that prohibit or discourage urban foraging. We highlight a few important exceptions that can provide prototypes and lessons for other cities regarding supportive policy frameworks and initiatives. The formulation of dynamic policy, design, and management strategies in support of urban foraging will bene?t from understanding the common characteristics of foraging in cities worldwide, but also will require comprehension of the speci?c and dynamic contexts in which they would be implemented.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su9101884","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"10","language":"en","page":"1884","title":"Urban Foraging: A Ubiquitous Human Practice Overlooked by Urban Planners, Policy, and Research","title-short":"Urban Foraging","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Dahlberg","given":"Annika"},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",10,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Urban gathering is growing in popularity. Many scholars agree that more people would like to gather wild algae, fungi and plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1d66cs2qms","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. K. Fischer & Kowarik, 2020)","plainCitation":"(L. K. Fischer & Kowarik, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":941,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3Y9K2TNY"],"itemData":{"id":941,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As global population becomes increasingly urban, opportunities for people to experience nature have decreased. Counteracting this trend is a key challenge for future urban development as interactions of urban people with biodiversity support human health and wellbeing, and may also result in positive attitudes towards biodiversity conservation. Collecting edible plants in urban surroundings, especially outside of gardens (“urban foraging”) is a traditional interaction with nature, based on knowledge about multiple uses of plants. Although some studies exist from different cities around the world, urban foraging has been revealed as a critically understudied phenomenon. We now analyze (i) the relevance of this human-nature interaction in Berlin, one of Europe’s metropolises, (ii) how people’s sociocultural background matters in attitudes of urban foragers vs. non-foragers towards this activity, and (iii) whether urban foraging may lead to conflicts with biodiversity conservation. Our survey revealed urban foraging as a relevant human-nature interaction with a high potential to grow: 33% of 535 respondents already collected edible plants outside of gardens and another 38% would be doing so given certain conditions, e.g. when contamination risks can be excluded. Many sociocultural groups (differing, e.g. on gender, age, childhood experience) shared attitudes towards foraging and existing barriers. Risks to biodiversity seem to be manageable as neither native species nor rare species were over-foraged in relation to species’ abundance in the local flora, with more abundant species being collected more frequently. We conclude that urban foraging can be a powerful tool for connecting urban people to nature without putting native biodiversity at risk. We make a claim for integrated approaches towards environmental policy, environmental education and greenspace management: these should aim on keeping potential health risks at a minimum, and should support urban foraging as a biodiversity-friendly and sustainable human-nature interaction in the cities of tomorrow.","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2020.106087","ISSN":"1470160X","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Indicators","language":"en","page":"106087","title":"Connecting people to biodiversity in cities of tomorrow: Is urban foraging a powerful tool?","title-short":"Connecting people to biodiversity in cities of tomorrow","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Fischer","given":"Leonie K."},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but safety concerns, lack of knowledge, perceived social stigma, and lack of access remain significant barriers to urban gathering for many ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a10ea1pfind","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ortez, 2021; Somesh, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Ortez, 2021; Somesh, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":949,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TBQHACBU"],"itemData":{"id":949,"type":"thesis","publisher":"The University of Arizona","title":"Foraging in Tucson's Parks: Interest, Barriers, and Opportunities","author":[{"family":"Ortez","given":"Paola"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":947,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D6XJCKVT"],"itemData":{"id":947,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Urban foraging is widely prevalent globally, and can provide foragers benefits like additional income and nutritional supplements. However, urban foraging is largely understudied and we have little information on types of species foraged, patterns of foraging, socio-economic profile of foragers, and impact of urbanization on foraging. In this study, we aimed to identify patterns of urban foraging in one of the fastest growing cities in the world, Bengaluru in India. Our specific objectives were to identify a) the foragers b) species foraged and foraging sites c) impact of urbanization on foraging and d) the barriers to foraging. We collected our information through semi-structured interviews with residents from four sites selected across a gradient of urbanization. Sixteen per cent of the respondents reported that they foraged, most of who were women (97 %) belonging to socially (90 %) and economically (81 %) disadvantaged groups. Seventy-six species were foraged across the four sites for food (76 %), medicine (26 %), and cultural practices (18 %). Foraging occurred in unused land (32 %), parks (32 %), farmlands (12 %), native villages (12 %), nearby woodlands (9%) and home gardens (3%). We found significant differences in foragers along a gradient of urbanization (chi square = 34.56, p = 1.5 × 10? 7, df = 3) with most foragers at the least urbanized site (40 % of the respondents) and least foragers at the most urbanized site (2% of the respondents). Lack of knowledge of foraged species (66 %) and lack of access (55 %) were the biggest barriers to foraging. Our study demonstrates that foraging occurs in a metropolis like Bengaluru with foragers generally belonging to lower socio-economic backgrounds, who are most vulnerable to changes due to urbanization. Redesigning urban environments to create green spaces for forgeable species to flourish, that are made accessible to marginalized communities, is essential for their nutritional and cultural well-being.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2020.126940","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126940","title":"Patterns of urban foraging in Bengaluru city","volume":"57","author":[{"family":"Somesh","given":"Dhruthi"},{"family":"Rao","given":"Rohit"},{"family":"Murali","given":"Ranjini"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Conservation practitioners had a negative or ambivalent view about the desirability of allowing or encouraging more foraging, particularly in parks or natural areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1qdgo9rmma","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wehi & Wehi, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Wehi & Wehi, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":915,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AJVE4E5X"],"itemData":{"id":915,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ecosystem fragmentation and destruction can lead to restrictive administration policies on traditional harvesting by indigenous peoples from remaining ecosystem tracts. In New Zealand, concerns about endangered species and governmental policies that focus on species and ecosystem preservation have resulted in severely curtailed traditional harvesting rights. Although provision has been made for limited gathering of traditional plants from government-administered conservation lands, it is unclear how much harvesting is undertaken on these lands and elsewhere and what this harvest might consist of. We interviewed seven expert Maori elders from the Waikato, New Zealand, to identify plant species they currently harvested and from where. We compared these data with the data we collected on permits issued for plant collecting on conservation lands in the same region. We sought to gain information on indigenous plant harvesting to determine the extent of permitted harvesting from conservation lands in the Waikato and to identify issues that might affect plant harvesting and management. Elders identified 58 species they harvest regularly or consider culturally important; over 50% of these species are harvested for medicinal use. Permit data from 1996 to 2006 indicated no apparent relationship between species of reported cultural significance and the number of permits issued for each of these species. Currently, few plant species are harvested from conservation lands, although some unofficial harvesting occurs. Elders instead reported that medicinal plants are frequently collected from urban and other public areas. They reported that plant species used for dyeing, carving, and weaving are difficult to access. Elders also discussed concerns such as spraying of roadsides, which resulted in the death of medicinal species, and use of commercial hybrids in urban planning. Local government may have an increasingly important role in supporting native traditions through urban planning, which takes account of cultural harvesting needs while potentially reducing future harvesting pressure on conservation lands. We suggest that active participation by the Maori community in the development and management of urban harvesting resources will result in positive outcomes.","container-title":"Conserv Biol","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2009.01376.x","ISSN":"0888-8892","issue":"2","language":"English","page":"594-604","title":"Traditional Plant Harvesting in Contemporary Fragmented and Urban Landscapes","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Wehi","given":"P. M."},{"family":"Wehi","given":"W. L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Risks to biodiversity seem manageable as overharvesting has not been documented ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"abt3pofgoj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Landor-Yamagata, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Landor-Yamagata, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":931,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V2X4DZN4"],"itemData":{"id":931,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Gathering wild plants in cities (urban foraging) is likely an important, but understudied human-nature interaction globally. As large European cities are critically understudied in this regard, we performed in-depth ethnography-based interviews in Berlin, Germany, to shed light on the cultural background of foragers, their motivations and which plants and fungi are gathered for which purposes. Results demonstrate multiple uses of 125 taxa, mostly frequently-occurring species but also some Red List species, from a range of formal and informal greenspace types. Both native and non-native species were gathered, with signi?cant differences in use patterns. Use for food was most common, followed by medicinal uses, and personal enjoyment was a frequent motivation, indicating that urban foraging combines provisioning and cultural ecosystem services. Familial and childhood foraging exposure were common, pointing to in?uences of early-in-life exposure on later-in-life activities and transgenerational aspects of the practice. Results further suggest legacy effects from the post-war and communist eras on foraging knowledge. Although non-commercial foraging is allowed in Berlin, over-harvesting was not evident. Interviews indicate that stewardship of urban biodiversity is common among foragers. Results thus suggest considering urban foraging as a promising vehicle for linking humans with nature when developing a biodiverse urban green infrastructure.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su10061873","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1873","title":"Urban Foraging in Berlin: People, Plants and Practices within the Metropolitan Green Infrastructure","title-short":"Urban Foraging in Berlin","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Landor-Yamagata","given":"Jonah"},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"},{"family":"Fischer","given":"Leonie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and in fact many urban greenspaces conserve considerable biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a4jh21egrg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rupprecht, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Rupprecht, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":924,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VVNGJPW4"],"itemData":{"id":924,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2015.08.009","ISSN":"16188667","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"883-908","title":"Informal urban green space: A trilingual systematic review of its role for biodiversity and trends in the literature","title-short":"Informal urban green space","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Rupprecht","given":"Christoph D. D."},{"family":"Byrne","given":"Jason A."},{"family":"Garden","given":"Jenni G."},{"family":"Hero","given":"Jean-Marc"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fruit gathering was likely to be least damaging ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a10naji0r42","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mallika Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Mallika Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":945,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/56PGVHNQ"],"itemData":{"id":945,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Gathering of uncultivated food from green spaces, also known as foraging, is observed in urban areas across the world, but the literature focuses predominantly on the global north. Our study examines the existing urban land management structure and its approach to urban foraging in the eastern coastal region of South Africa. Through interviews with municipal officials in nine cities, we identified different stakeholders and their roles in urban green space management. We then used network analysis to represent interactions and influence of these stakeholders, and environmental worldviews to determine organisational and perceptual barriers to and enablers of foraging in urban green spaces. The policy on urban green space management, as well as land managers themselves are amenable to the concept of foraging in public spaces. Lack of knowledge on wild indigenous species and sustainable offtake, ambiguous, coarse, or lacking policy, and normative views of pristine nature may hinder foraging. We recommend pathways for policy and stakeholder partnerships to incorporate sustainable foraging in their biodiversity conservation and land stewardship strategies. Received: February 20, 2020 Accepted: March 5, 2020 Published: April 7, 2020 Copyright: © 2020 Sardeshpande, Shackleton. This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original author and source are credited.","container-title":"PLoS One","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0230693","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"e0230693","title":"Urban foraging: Land management policy, perspectives, and potential","title-short":"Urban foraging","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Sardeshpande","given":"Mallika"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"}],"editor":[{"family":"Rozylowicz","given":"Laurentiu"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",4,7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and more abundant species are collected more frequently ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1c43ipo03t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. K. Fischer & Kowarik, 2020)","plainCitation":"(L. K. Fischer & Kowarik, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":941,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3Y9K2TNY"],"itemData":{"id":941,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As global population becomes increasingly urban, opportunities for people to experience nature have decreased. Counteracting this trend is a key challenge for future urban development as interactions of urban people with biodiversity support human health and wellbeing, and may also result in positive attitudes towards biodiversity conservation. Collecting edible plants in urban surroundings, especially outside of gardens (“urban foraging”) is a traditional interaction with nature, based on knowledge about multiple uses of plants. Although some studies exist from different cities around the world, urban foraging has been revealed as a critically understudied phenomenon. We now analyze (i) the relevance of this human-nature interaction in Berlin, one of Europe’s metropolises, (ii) how people’s sociocultural background matters in attitudes of urban foragers vs. non-foragers towards this activity, and (iii) whether urban foraging may lead to conflicts with biodiversity conservation. Our survey revealed urban foraging as a relevant human-nature interaction with a high potential to grow: 33% of 535 respondents already collected edible plants outside of gardens and another 38% would be doing so given certain conditions, e.g. when contamination risks can be excluded. Many sociocultural groups (differing, e.g. on gender, age, childhood experience) shared attitudes towards foraging and existing barriers. Risks to biodiversity seem to be manageable as neither native species nor rare species were over-foraged in relation to species’ abundance in the local flora, with more abundant species being collected more frequently. We conclude that urban foraging can be a powerful tool for connecting urban people to nature without putting native biodiversity at risk. We make a claim for integrated approaches towards environmental policy, environmental education and greenspace management: these should aim on keeping potential health risks at a minimum, and should support urban foraging as a biodiversity-friendly and sustainable human-nature interaction in the cities of tomorrow.","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2020.106087","ISSN":"1470160X","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Indicators","language":"en","page":"106087","title":"Connecting people to biodiversity in cities of tomorrow: Is urban foraging a powerful tool?","title-short":"Connecting people to biodiversity in cities of tomorrow","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Fischer","given":"Leonie K."},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Even among those favoring gathering, sustainability assessment and adoption of appropriate rules was a precondition ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ai6hk6si7k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mallika Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Mallika Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":945,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/56PGVHNQ"],"itemData":{"id":945,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Gathering of uncultivated food from green spaces, also known as foraging, is observed in urban areas across the world, but the literature focuses predominantly on the global north. Our study examines the existing urban land management structure and its approach to urban foraging in the eastern coastal region of South Africa. Through interviews with municipal officials in nine cities, we identified different stakeholders and their roles in urban green space management. We then used network analysis to represent interactions and influence of these stakeholders, and environmental worldviews to determine organisational and perceptual barriers to and enablers of foraging in urban green spaces. The policy on urban green space management, as well as land managers themselves are amenable to the concept of foraging in public spaces. Lack of knowledge on wild indigenous species and sustainable offtake, ambiguous, coarse, or lacking policy, and normative views of pristine nature may hinder foraging. We recommend pathways for policy and stakeholder partnerships to incorporate sustainable foraging in their biodiversity conservation and land stewardship strategies. Received: February 20, 2020 Accepted: March 5, 2020 Published: April 7, 2020 Copyright: © 2020 Sardeshpande, Shackleton. This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original author and source are credited.","container-title":"PLoS One","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0230693","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"e0230693","title":"Urban foraging: Land management policy, perspectives, and potential","title-short":"Urban foraging","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Sardeshpande","given":"Mallika"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"}],"editor":[{"family":"Rozylowicz","given":"Laurentiu"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",4,7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Gathering may support invasive species management in urban ecosystems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"al7targ3ir","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arrington, 2021; R. J. McLain, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Arrington, 2021; R. J. McLain, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":920,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MMAVNVT3"],"itemData":{"id":920,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2020.126896","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126896","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Urban foraging of five non-native plants in NYC: Balancing ecosystem services and invasive species management","title-short":"Urban foraging of five non-native plants in NYC","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1618866720307135","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Arrington","given":"Austin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3]]}}},{"id":921,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VFC5RUFZ"],"itemData":{"id":921,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Our research seeks to expand the concept of urban environmental stewardship to include the everyday stewardship practices of urban nontimber forest products foragers. Ethnographic data from 58 urban foragers and 18 land stewards in the city of Seattle (USA) revealed that foragers reported using a variety of practices to enhance and minimize negative desirable species and their habitats. Many of these practices were identical to those practiced by restoration volunteers in formal programs and align with Seattle Parks Department management objectives. Foragers actively sought to learn more about what practices are sustainable; many mentored others in sustainable harvesting practices. Most foragers emphasized the importance of treating plants and their environment with respect. The land stewards voiced some concerns about foraging in city parks, but most were cautiously supportive of opening up the parks to foragers. The study results suggest that an opportunity exists for park managers to develop alliances with foragers so as to leverage foragers’ everyday stewardship practices and accomplish some of their park restoration objectives. Doing so will require park managers to acknowledge the positive contributions that humans can to make novel ecosystems and foragers to develop or strengthen selfregulation norms.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2017.10.005","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"36-42","title":"Urban non-timber forest products stewardship practices among foragers in Seattle, washington (USA)","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"R. J."},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R."},{"family":"Urgenson","given":"Lauren S."},{"family":"Blahna","given":"Dale J."},{"family":"Buttolph","given":"Lita P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although most utilized species are native ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1pfquktemo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Charnley, 2018; Palliwoda, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Charnley, 2018; Palliwoda, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":956,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N8A22WKY"],"itemData":{"id":956,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1080/08941920.2017.1413696","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"734–750","title":"Natural resource access rights and wrongs: Nontimber forest products gathering in urban environments","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Charnley","given":"Susan"},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J"},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":933,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9TB76EG"],"itemData":{"id":933,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","DOI":"10.1016/j.landurbplan.2016.09.003","ISSN":"01692046","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape and Urban Planning","language":"en","page":"394-406","title":"Human-biodiversity interactions in urban parks: The species level matters","title-short":"Human-biodiversity interactions in urban parks","volume":"157","author":[{"family":"Palliwoda","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"},{"family":"Lippe","given":"Moritz","non-dropping-particle":"von der"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, a species’ status as invasive or non-invasive can influence gathering practice ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1lcmtr1l5k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. J. McLain, 2017)","plainCitation":"(R. J. McLain, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":921,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VFC5RUFZ"],"itemData":{"id":921,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Our research seeks to expand the concept of urban environmental stewardship to include the everyday stewardship practices of urban nontimber forest products foragers. Ethnographic data from 58 urban foragers and 18 land stewards in the city of Seattle (USA) revealed that foragers reported using a variety of practices to enhance and minimize negative desirable species and their habitats. Many of these practices were identical to those practiced by restoration volunteers in formal programs and align with Seattle Parks Department management objectives. Foragers actively sought to learn more about what practices are sustainable; many mentored others in sustainable harvesting practices. Most foragers emphasized the importance of treating plants and their environment with respect. The land stewards voiced some concerns about foraging in city parks, but most were cautiously supportive of opening up the parks to foragers. The study results suggest that an opportunity exists for park managers to develop alliances with foragers so as to leverage foragers’ everyday stewardship practices and accomplish some of their park restoration objectives. Doing so will require park managers to acknowledge the positive contributions that humans can to make novel ecosystems and foragers to develop or strengthen selfregulation norms.","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2017.10.005","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"36-42","title":"Urban non-timber forest products stewardship practices among foragers in Seattle, washington (USA)","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"R. J."},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R."},{"family":"Urgenson","given":"Lauren S."},{"family":"Blahna","given":"Dale J."},{"family":"Buttolph","given":"Lita P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Since many invasive wild plants have a history of cultivation as food, medicine, and materials, providing some socio-economic values, the gathering and use of edible weeds as a complementary resource has promising possibilities. For example, bracken fern (Pteridium aquilinum), a native plant in the Pacific Northwest region of the United States of America, has been classified and gathered as an edible ‘weed’ ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a22jhanlhm5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. R. Poe, 2014)","plainCitation":"(M. R. Poe, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31615,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BBMRWGK2"],"itemData":{"id":31615,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Social & Cultural Geography","DOI":"10.1080/14649365.2014.908232","ISSN":"1464-9365, 1470-1197","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Social & Cultural Geography","language":"en","page":"901-919","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Urban foraging and the relational ecologies of belonging","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/14649365.2014.908232","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R."},{"family":"LeCompte","given":"Joyce"},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",11,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      An emerging approach is to consider urban forests as nature-based solutions in the urban environment and include them in city management and planning ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a63f8rq1bc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Roeland, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Roeland, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":913,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LNLJEYJX"],"itemData":{"id":913,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As a Nature-Based Solution, urban forests deliver a number of environmental ecosystem services (EESs). To quantify these EESs, well-defined, reliable, quantifiable and stable indicators are needed. With literature analysis and expert knowledge gathered within COST Action FP1204 GreenInUrbs, we proposed a classification of urban forest EESs into three categories: (A) regulation of air, water, soil and climate; (B) provisioning of habitat quality; and (C) provisioning of other goods and services. Each category is divided into EES types: (a) amelioration of air quality; restoration of soil and water; amelioration of the microclimate; removal of CO2 from the air; (b) provision of habitat for biodiversity; support for resilient urban ecosystems; provision of genetic diversity; and (c) provision of energy and nutrients; provision of grey infrastructure resilience. Each EES type provides one or more benefits. For each of these 12 benefits, we propose a set of indicators to be used when analyzing the impacts on the identified EESs. Around half of the 36 indicators are relevant to more than one single benefit, which highlights complex interrelationships. The indicators of wider applicability are tree and stand characteristics, followed by leaf physical traits and tree species composition. This knowledge is needed for the optimization of the EESs delivered by urban forests, now and in the future.","container-title":"Journal of Forestry Research","DOI":"10.1007/s11676-019-00916-x","ISSN":"1007-662x","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"J Forestry Res","language":"English","page":"1981-1996","title":"Towards an integrative approach to evaluate the environmental ecosystem services provided by urban forest","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Roeland","given":"S."},{"family":"Moretti","given":"M."},{"family":"Amorim","given":"J. H."},{"family":"Branquinho","given":"C."},{"family":"Fares","given":"S."},{"family":"Morelli","given":"F."},{"family":"Niinemets","given":"U."},{"family":"Paoletti","given":"E."},{"family":"Pinho","given":"P."},{"family":"Sgrigna","given":"G."},{"family":"Stojanovski","given":"V."},{"family":"Tiwary","given":"A."},{"family":"Sicard","given":"P."},{"family":"Calfapietra","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Trees are welcomed for their products and regulating services like shade and windbreaks, also their less tangible aesthetic and cultural values ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ancr5gvoeb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Shackleton, 2015)","plainCitation":"(S. Shackleton, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":952,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F4C7GJ9X"],"itemData":{"id":952,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","DOI":"10.1016/j.landurbplan.2014.12.004","ISSN":"01692046","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape and Urban Planning","language":"en","page":"76-86","title":"Multiple benefits and values of trees in urban landscapes in two towns in northern South Africa","volume":"136","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Sheona"},{"family":"Chinyimba","given":"Abby"},{"family":"Hebinck","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Kaoma","given":"Humphrey"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urban gathering creates ties between people and the surrounding nature, in fact encouraging people to see urban vegetation and green space as natural ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"alfrs83a9b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Landor-Yamagata, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Landor-Yamagata, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":931,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V2X4DZN4"],"itemData":{"id":931,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Gathering wild plants in cities (urban foraging) is likely an important, but understudied human-nature interaction globally. As large European cities are critically understudied in this regard, we performed in-depth ethnography-based interviews in Berlin, Germany, to shed light on the cultural background of foragers, their motivations and which plants and fungi are gathered for which purposes. Results demonstrate multiple uses of 125 taxa, mostly frequently-occurring species but also some Red List species, from a range of formal and informal greenspace types. Both native and non-native species were gathered, with signi?cant differences in use patterns. Use for food was most common, followed by medicinal uses, and personal enjoyment was a frequent motivation, indicating that urban foraging combines provisioning and cultural ecosystem services. Familial and childhood foraging exposure were common, pointing to in?uences of early-in-life exposure on later-in-life activities and transgenerational aspects of the practice. Results further suggest legacy effects from the post-war and communist eras on foraging knowledge. Although non-commercial foraging is allowed in Berlin, over-harvesting was not evident. Interviews indicate that stewardship of urban biodiversity is common among foragers. Results thus suggest considering urban foraging as a promising vehicle for linking humans with nature when developing a biodiverse urban green infrastructure.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su10061873","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1873","title":"Urban Foraging in Berlin: People, Plants and Practices within the Metropolitan Green Infrastructure","title-short":"Urban Foraging in Berlin","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Landor-Yamagata","given":"Jonah"},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"},{"family":"Fischer","given":"Leonie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urban planners may consider these benefits of green spaces and issues of access to nature in the city ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ebdg5kmja","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Charnley, 2018; C. M. Shackleton, Drescher, & Schlesinger, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Charnley, 2018; C. M. Shackleton, Drescher, & Schlesinger, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":956,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N8A22WKY"],"itemData":{"id":956,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1080/08941920.2017.1413696","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"734–750","title":"Natural resource access rights and wrongs: Nontimber forest products gathering in urban environments","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Charnley","given":"Susan"},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J"},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":939,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9DJP4EI3"],"itemData":{"id":939,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Most sub-Saharan African countries are experiencing accelerating rates of urban migration, which bring with it many opportunities and challenges for migrating households, urban planners, governments and local and national economies. At the household level urbanisation may drive changes in the allocation of household labour and consequently social and economic relations. Yet how these are manifest in land-based livelihood strategies of arable cropping, livestock husbandry and the collection of wild products in urban settings has rarely been considered. Here we examine the changing participation rates in land-based livelihood activities by male- and female-headed households along the rural–urban continua of six sub-Saharan African secondary towns. To capture the gendered changes in land-based livelihood strategies along the urban–rural continuum, 1156 randomly selected households were sampled along transects radiating out from the urban through the periurban to rural surrounds. Participation in different land-based livelihood strategies was recorded by means of a questionnaire. We show that engagement in one or more land-based livelihood activities is widespread (92% of all households), even amongst urban households (82%). We also show that there are marked changes in gendered participation for many landbased activities along the urbanisation gradient, with a general narrowing of differences in participation rates between male- and female-headed households with increasing urbanisation. These results indicate that land-based activities are components of not only rural livelihoods in Africa, but are also practiced by most urban households too, and that urbanisation reshapes some of the gendered dimensions of roles more typical of rural contexts.","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2020.104946","ISSN":"0305750X","language":"en","page":"104946","title":"Urbanisation reshapes gendered engagement in land-based livelihood activities in mid-sized African towns","volume":"130","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C. M."},{"family":"Drescher","given":"A."},{"family":"Schlesinger","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In summary, the combination of edible green infrastructure and urban beautification contributes to urban food production, as well as co-benefits nutrition, socioeconomics, and environment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a10gp8turap","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Russo, 2017)","plainCitation":"(A. Russo, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":926,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WTXR89BV"],"itemData":{"id":926,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Recently published green infrastructure, nature-based solutions, and ecosystem disservices (ED) literature have focused primarily on the supply of urban regulating and cultural ecosystem services (ES). Other literature on urban and peri-urban agriculture has mostly studied the role of localized, intensive agricultural practices in providing food to inhabitants. The aim of this review is to raise awareness and stress the knowledge gap on the importance of urban provisioning ES, particularly when implementing an edible green infrastructure (EGI) approach as it can o?er improved resilience and quality of life in cities. We compiled and systematically analyzed studies on urban ES and ED related to a number of EGI typologies. Our systematic review of the relevant literature via an EGI framework, identi?ed more than 80 peer-reviewed publications that focused on ES and food production in urban areas. An EGI approach can contribute socially, economically, and environmentally to urban sustainability and food security. However, such bene?ts must be weighed against ED trade-o?s, including: potential health risks caused by human exposure to heavy metals and organic chemical contaminants often present in urban surroundings. We conclude with recommendations and guidelines for incorporating EGI into urban planning and design, and discuss novel areas for future research.","container-title":"Agriculture, Ecosystems & Environment","DOI":"10.1016/j.agee.2017.03.026","ISSN":"01678809","journalAbbreviation":"Agriculture, Ecosystems & Environment","language":"en","page":"53-66","title":"Edible green infrastructure: An approach and review of provisioning ecosystem services and disservices in urban environments","title-short":"Edible green infrastructure","volume":"242","author":[{"family":"Russo","given":"Alessio"},{"family":"Escobedo","given":"Francisco J."},{"family":"Cirella","given":"Giuseppe T."},{"family":"Zerbe","given":"Stefan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ecosystem services provided by urban green space create urban gardening and gathering opportunities that contribute to healthy lifestyles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aiolv54nd2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. Jennings, 2016)","plainCitation":"(V. Jennings, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":923,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GJJXHQEZ"],"itemData":{"id":923,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Urban green spaces provide an array of bene?ts, or ecosystem services, that support our physical, psychological, and social health. In many cases, however, these bene?ts are not equitably distributed across diverse urban populations. In this paper, we explore relationships between cultural ecosystem services provided by urban green space and the social determinants of health outlined in the United States Healthy People 2020 initiative. Speci?cally, we: (1) explore connections between cultural ecosystem services and social determinants of health; (2) examine cultural ecosystem services as nature-based health amenities to promote social equity; and (3) recommend areas for future research examining links between urban green space and public health within the context of environmental justice.","container-title":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","DOI":"10.3390/ijerph13020196","ISSN":"1660-4601","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"IJERPH","language":"en","page":"196","title":"Advancing Sustainability through Urban Green Space: Cultural Ecosystem Services, Equity, and Social Determinants of Health","title-short":"Advancing Sustainability through Urban Green Space","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Jennings","given":"Viniece"},{"family":"Larson","given":"Lincoln"},{"family":"Yun","given":"Jessica"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",2,5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Traditional tropical home gardens serve as a model for biocultural diversity in small-scale urban green spaces ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lnQkQWkz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hemmelgarn & Munsell, 2021; M. Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Hemmelgarn & Munsell, 2021; M. Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":943,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KM2792XD"],"itemData":{"id":943,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Urban agroforestry efforts have focused primarily on food production, but these dynamic, multiple-strata systems can host a much broader set of functions. This exploratory synthesis describes opportunities and considerations for urban forest gardens’ (UFG) capacity to include diverse, biologically important, and culturally relevant nontimber forest species that have medicinal properties, provide decorative and landscaping products, supply raw material for crafts, and other valuable outputs. Literature relevant to social and ecological aspects of design for ‘beyond-food’ specialty forest crops in urban forest gardens reveals a need for collaborative, participatory, and culturally relevant UFG decision making that addresses access inequities, potential contamination from urban pollutants, and a continued need for education and awareness of UFG multifunctionality. These production spaces can serve as both a biological and cultural repository for species that may be otherwise overlooked in a narrowly oriented food garden, though case study examples indicating contextual elements of implementation are needed to understand specific cultural and health sovereignty benefits. Currently, traditional tropical homegardens serve as a model for biocultural diversity in small-scale urban green spaces. Incorporating conservation goals into urban agroforestry initiatives at varying scales holds potential for growing interest in and commitment to building capacity for this emergent land use.","container-title":"Urban Agriculture & Regional Food Systems","DOI":"10.1002/uar2.20009","ISSN":"2575-1220, 2575-1220","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Urban agric. region. food syst.","language":"en","title":"Exploring ‘beyond?food’ opportunities for biocultural conservation in urban forest gardens","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Hemmelgarn","given":"Hannah L."},{"family":"Munsell","given":"John F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":28291,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6JQJDH3Q"],"itemData":{"id":28291,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-020-00185-x","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"665–677","title":"Fruits of the Veld: Ecological and Socioeconomic Patterns of Natural Resource Use across South Africa","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10745-020-00185-x","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Sardeshpande","given":"M."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The forest garden helps urban children develop environmental, scientific, and possibly other values ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ka6r9nm50","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Askerlund & Almers, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Askerlund & Almers, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":951,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CXBBCC4A"],"itemData":{"id":951,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2016.08.007","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"187-197","title":"Forest gardens – new opportunities for urban children to understand and develop relationships with other organisms","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Askerlund","given":"Per"},{"family":"Almers","given":"Ellen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The use of edible green infrastructure areas and gardens are playing an important role in the COVID-19 pandemic and post-lockdown period as people have spent more time at home and demonstrated an increased awareness of the need for self- reliance and resilience to emerging threats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aelkgrgo22","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Russo & Cirella, 2020)","plainCitation":"(A. Russo & Cirella, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":946,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G5Q8IAIP"],"itemData":{"id":946,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ten identi?ed edible green infrastructure (EGI)-related urban regeneration case studies within the Campania region, Italy, are explored in relation to local community development, involvement, and education. Urban space and agriculture are promoted as sustainably planned networks for edible food components and structures. Within an urban ecosystem, city planners are actively promoting urban agriculture after an increase in the availability of unused land. Advantages for public health include stress reduction and physical activity, as well as sustainability of urban gardens by way of far-sighted urban planning. Case studies within the Campania region illustrate EGI know-hows and awareness, and they elucidate upon a number of bene?cial reasons for its implementation. Within the Campania region, all ?ve provinces showed positive impacts when using EGI for urban regeneration and well-being. Recent developments from the COVID-19 pandemic are reinforcing a rethink of food security and food supply chains.","container-title":"Agriculture","DOI":"10.3390/agriculture10080358","ISSN":"2077-0472","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Agriculture","language":"en","page":"358","title":"Edible Green Infrastructure for Urban Regeneration and Food Security: Case Studies from the Campania Region","title-short":"Edible Green Infrastructure for Urban Regeneration and Food Security","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Russo","given":"Alessio"},{"family":"Cirella","given":"Giuseppe T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,14]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

3.3.2.2.3. Gender trends

Gathering wild products is a gendered activity in many parts of the world, depending on cultural rules, on the type of harvested wild algae, fungi and plants and the places where they are harvested. In many countries, women perform the bulk of the labor for gathering and processing wild plants for food, medicine, fuel and handicrafts, as well as for other subsistence purposes, and often sell wild products at local markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a27pmaa06m7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Howard, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Howard, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ETQRTMX8"],"itemData":{"id":32087,"type":"book","call-number":"QK46.5.H85 W66 2003","event-place":"New York : Eschborn, Germany","ISBN":"978-1-84277-156-3","number-of-pages":"298","publisher":"Zed Books ; Deutsche Gesellschaft für Technische Zusammenarbeit","publisher-place":"New York : Eschborn, Germany","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Women & plants: gender relations in biodiversity management and conservation","title-short":"Women & plants","editor":[{"family":"Howard","given":"Patricia L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some gathering activities are specific to men, some others are conducted equally by men or women, as well as children, or involve the whole family. Today, commercial gathering is done by men and women who make it their primary profession ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a60caa7tlb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Julliand, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Julliand, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":962,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9VLNUEUN"],"itemData":{"id":962,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EchoGéo","DOI":"10.4000/echogeo.16987","ISSN":"1963-1197","issue":"47","journalAbbreviation":"echogeo","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Normaliser le sauvage : l'expérience française des cueilleurs professionnels","title-short":"Normaliser le sauvage","URL":"http://journals.openedition.org/echogeo/16987","author":[{"family":"Julliand","given":"Claire"},{"family":"Pinton","given":"Florence"},{"family":"Garreta","given":"Raphaële"},{"family":"Lescure","given":"Jean-Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,21]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In low-income households, women are often responsible for gathering for self-consumption and to sell ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a10vlmp79t0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sabater, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Sabater, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32040,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/75IEQH5Z"],"itemData":{"id":32040,"type":"report","abstract":"This document provides a review of existing literature around women’s roles in local economies, cultural preservation and environmental conservation in the Mediterranean Basin. The paper further identifies data gaps and economic opportunities to support cultural practices in the region’s cultural landscapes.","genre":"IUCN: International Union for Conservation of Nature","language":"en","note":"publisher: IUCN: International Union for Conservation of Nature","source":"policycommons.net","title":"Gender, culture, and sustainability in the Mediterranean","URL":"https://policycommons.net/artifacts/1368648/gender-culture-and-sustainability-in-the-mediterranean/1982816/","author":[{"family":"Sabater","given":"L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	A range of examples show a variety of gender dynamics in gathering around the world. In the 1980s, farmers in the mountains of central France, men and women, harvested wild plants and mushrooms for their own consumption, to share with family and for commercial purposes. Children, teenagers and elders dedicated more time to gathering than adults, the latter being busy with agricultural activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1g8jh69kfq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Larr\\\\uc0\\\\u232{}re & La Soudi\\\\uc0\\\\u232{}re, 1985)","plainCitation":"(Larrère & La Soudière, 1985)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32070,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8X4WILDQ"],"itemData":{"id":32070,"type":"book","call-number":"MLCM 90/05574 (Q)","collection-title":"L'Homme et la nature","event-place":"Lyon","ISBN":"978-2-904638-31-2","number-of-pages":"253","publisher":"Manufacture","publisher-place":"Lyon","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Cueillir la montagne: plantes, fleurs, champignons en Gévaudan, Auvergne, et Limousin","title-short":"Cueillir la montagne","author":[{"family":"Larrère","given":"Raphaël"},{"family":"La Soudière","given":"Martin","dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1985"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Turkey, gathering practices between men and women differ in that woman prefer to gather in social groups, and distribute some of the wild plants such as edible greens that they have gathered as gifts to friends and neighbors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a25habltjlr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ertug, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Ertug, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32069,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/72BNDFAT"],"itemData":{"id":32069,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Women & Plants","event-place":"London, U.K. & USA","ISBN":"1-84277-156-6","page":"183-196","publisher":"Zed Books","publisher-place":"London, U.K. & USA","title":"Gendering the tradition of gathering in central Anatolia (Turkey)","author":[{"family":"Ertug","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the tropical forests of French Guiana, the Maroon Ndjuka women gather wild plants close to the village and the fields, while men gather wild plants in the deep forest ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2engehrd4m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tareau, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Tareau, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":958,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PGLPEPK6"],"itemData":{"id":958,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EchoGéo","DOI":"10.4000/echogeo.17260","ISSN":"1963-1197","issue":"47","journalAbbreviation":"echogeo","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild medicinal plant collection in transitional societies: A case Analysis from French Guiana","title-short":"Wild medicinal plant collection in transitional societies","URL":"http://journals.openedition.org/echogeo/17260","author":[{"family":"Tareau","given":"Marc-Alexandre"},{"family":"Dejouhanet","given":"Lucie"},{"family":"Odonne","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Palisse","given":"Marianne"},{"family":"Ansoe","given":"Clarisse"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,21]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the savannas of central Brazil, the Xavante women gather wild plants while the men hunt, but men sometimes join them ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2hd6ha3pp7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Flowers, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Flowers, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32051,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G5X8ES43"],"itemData":{"id":32051,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Antropologia e História Xavante em Perspectiva","edition":"Coimbra Jr.  CEA, Welch JR. (ed)","event-place":"Rio de Janeiro","page":"67-86.","publisher":"Museu do Índio/FUNAI","publisher-place":"Rio de Janeiro","title":"Economia, Subsistência e Trabalho: Sistema em Mudança","URL":"https://acervo.socioambiental.org/acervo/livros/antropologia-e-historia-xavante-em-perspectiva","author":[{"family":"Flowers","given":"Nancy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Extractivism with long expeditions in the forest is usually practiced by men, for instance rubber or piassava collectors in the Amazon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ao0ud3uppv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schmink & Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Schmink & García, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32072,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T88HQPV9"],"itemData":{"id":32072,"type":"book","note":"DOI: 10.17528/cifor/005505","publisher":"Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR)","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Under the canopy: Gender and forests in Amazonia","title-short":"Under the canopy","URL":"http://www.cifor.org/library/5505/under-the-canopy-gender-and-forests-in-amazonia/","author":[{"family":"Schmink","given":"M"},{"family":"García","given":"MAG"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, eaglewood (Aquilaria sp.) collectors in Borneo or Papua; in these last regions, some traders even organize expeditions where they drop a group of several men in the middle of the forest from a helicopter ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a17cdc2v1sl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mittelman, Lai, Byron, Michon, & Katz, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Mittelman, Lai, Byron, Michon, & Katz, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32046,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PA8NXMNC"],"itemData":{"id":32046,"type":"book","collection-number":"Working paper n° APFSOS/WP/28","collection-title":"FAO Asia-Pacific Forestry Sector Outlook Study. Working Paper Series","event-place":"Rome/Bangkok","number-of-pages":"50","publisher":"FAO. Forest Policy and Planning Division / Regional Office for Asia and the Pacific","publisher-place":"Rome/Bangkok","title":"Non-wood forest products outlook study for Asia and the Pacific: Towards 2010","URL":"https://www.fao.org/publications/card/fr/c/e041130d-dd94-5502-8b16-317e11afb26c","author":[{"family":"Mittelman","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Lai","given":"C.K."},{"family":"Byron","given":"N."},{"family":"Michon","given":"G."},{"family":"Katz","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the dry and semi-dry areas of Africa, gums and resins such as gum arabic (Acacia senegal, A. seyal), myrrh (Commiphora myrrha) and frankincense (Boswellia spp.) are usually gathered by men, pastoralists who fulfil this activity while taking their cattle to graze ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aidlij2u28","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mugah, Chikamai, Mbiru, & Casadei, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Mugah, Chikamai, Mbiru, & Casadei, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32049,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GG7H966W"],"itemData":{"id":32049,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome/Nairobi","publisher":"FAO, Forestry Department, Forest Products Division/ KEFRI/ TWAS/ AIDGUM/ GTZ","publisher-place":"Rome/Nairobi","title":"Conservation, management and utilization of plant gums, resins and essential oils. Proccedings of a regional conference for Africa held in Nairobi, Kenya (6-10/10/97)","author":[{"family":"Mugah","given":"J.O."},{"family":"Chikamai","given":"B.N."},{"family":"Mbiru","given":"S.S."},{"family":"Casadei","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Tapping resins in general is a male task, especially when it is necessary to climb on trees. Batak benzoin tappers in North Sumatra, Indonesia, describe the benzoin tree (Styrax paralleloneurum) as a woman who gets pregnant of the resin after the tapping, a symbolic sexual act ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1l12bhitn3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Esther Katz, Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a, & Goloubinoff, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Esther Katz, García, & Goloubinoff, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32056,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2SIU8MA6"],"itemData":{"id":32056,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Tapping the Green Market. Certification and Management of Non-Timber Forest Products","edition":"Guillen A, Laird S, Shanley P, Pierce A. (ed)","event-place":"United Kingdom.","ISBN":"1-85383-810-1","page":"182-190","publisher":"Earthscan/WWF/UNESCO People and Plants/Kew Gardens","publisher-place":"United Kingdom.","title":"Sumatra Benzoin (Styrax spp.)","URL":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/272743208_Tapping_the_Green_Market_Certification_and_Management_of_Non-timber_Forest_Products","author":[{"family":"Katz","given":"Esther"},{"family":"García","given":"Carmen"},{"family":"Goloubinoff","given":"Marina"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

3.3.2.3. Uses of wild plants, algae, and fungi, including the leaves and fruits of trees  

Unlike the case for some of the other practices, where only selected uses are relevant, all of the uses outlined in Chapter 1 of the assessment are relevant for gathering practices. In fact, in several subsections of 3.3.2.3 the diversity of species gathered for the various uses are so extensive that additional subdivisions have been created. The sections are as follows: Ceremony and cultural expression (3.3.2.3.1); decorative and aesthetic (3.3.2.3.2) with subsections on ornamental, natural cloth and dyes, handicrafts, and perfume and incense; energy (3.3.2.3.3); food (3.3.2.3.4) with subsections on nuts & seeds, starchy fruits, juicy fruits, beverages, syrups, gums, and resins, wild edible mushrooms, and wild vegetables; medicine and hygiene (3.3.2.3.5); recreation (3.3.2.3.6); science and education (3.3.2.3.7); and materials and shelter (3.3.2.3.8). Importantly, the text is not an inventory of all species gathered for various practices. Rather, the focus is on those species of particular interest in relation to sustainable use which emerged through the systematic literature review and those that were highlighted through various rounds of expert discussion and review.   

3.3.2.3.1. Ceremony and cultural expression

The world’s major cultures and ritual practices observe conservation of species and nature as essentials for human well-being. Cultural expression may take the form of song, stories, dances, art, designs, crafts, rituals, ceremonies, and more. Many wild species, especially wild plants and fungi, perform critical roles in ceremonies of various cultures around the world. They are harvested for use in spiritual observances and practices, and are highly valued for their role in maintaining cultural identity in formal ways ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"atckbrok1s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. C. Hamilton, 2004)","plainCitation":"(A. C. Hamilton, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32109,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WDY5F38I"],"itemData":{"id":32109,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Many types of action can be taken in favour of the conservation and sustainable use of medicinal plants. Some of these are undertaken directly at the places where the plants are found, while others are less direct, such as some of those relating to commercial systems, ex situ conservation and bioprospecting. In the latter cases, actions taken will not lead to in situ conservation unless they feedback to improvements in the ?eld. Probably the single most important role for medicinal plants in biological conservation is their ‘use’ to achieve conservation of natural habitats more generally. This stems from the special meanings that medicinal plants have to people, related to the major contributions that they make to many people’s lives in terms of health support, ?nancial income, cultural identity and livelihood security. Problems associated with biopiracy or (the other side of the coin) excessive restrictions on research have come to assume policy prominence in the general thematic area of ‘medicinal plant conservation and use’. The fair and equitable sharing of bene?ts from bioprospecting is required under the Convention on Biological Diversity, but it is not always easy to achieve these ideals in practice. While experience is accumulating in how this may practically be achieved, it is important, at the present time, that controls imposed on scienti?c research to prevent biopiracy or theft of local and indigenous intellectual property do not unduly restrict research that has little or nothing to do with these matters.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1023/B:BIOC.0000021333.23413.42","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity and Conservation","language":"en","page":"1477-1517","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Medicinal plants, conservation and livelihoods","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1023/B:BIOC.0000021333.23413.42","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Hamilton","given":"Alan C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Millennium Ecosystem Assessment highlighted that impeding religious and social ceremonies by denying people access to required wild plants or fungi could harm social relations as “many cultures attach spiritual and religious values to ecosystems or their components” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a18qpftmb79","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30336,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WFNSCJUR"],"itemData":{"id":30336,"type":"book","event-place":"Washington DC","ISBN":"1-59726-040-1","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington DC","title":"Ecosystems and Human Well-Being: Synthesis","URL":"http://www.who.int/entity/globalchange/ecosystems/ecosys.pdf","author":[{"literal":"Millennium Ecosystem Assessment"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      Research on gathering for ceremony and cultural expression focus more on the cultural dimensions, such as the types of rituals (e.g., marriage, birth, death, important memorial points and specific religious rituals) than they do on the sustainable use of the species per se. Wild species are sometimes mixed with horticultural plants, and used for decoration, smoking, dyeing, as non-pharmacological medicine or for energy and nutrition. Flowers and incenses made out of dried plants or resins such as frankincense or myrrh are often used in rituals. It is difficult to make a complete list of species used for ceremonies, as many ethnobotanists make inventories of dozens to hundreds of wild plants from local surveys ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"apep456nap","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barcel\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}, But\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}, Gras, Orriols, & Vall\\\\uc0\\\\u232{}s, 2019; Des, Rizki, & Fitri, 2019; Yanfei Geng, 2017; Rangel-Landa, Casas, Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a-Frapolli, & Lira, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Barceló, Butí, Gras, Orriols, & Vallès, 2019; Des, Rizki, & Fitri, 2019; Yanfei Geng, 2017; Rangel-Landa, Casas, García-Frapolli, & Lira, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32098,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7GM4C9D5"],"itemData":{"id":32098,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-019-09474-z","ISSN":"0013-0001","page":"1–8","title":"Ethnobotany in a “Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity”: Plants in “la Patum” Festivity (Berga, Catalonia, Iberian Peninsula)1","author":[{"family":"Barceló","given":"Carme M."},{"family":"Butí","given":"Elisenda"},{"family":"Gras","given":"Airy"},{"family":"Orriols","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Vallès","given":"Joan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":907,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MMKV69AY"],"itemData":{"id":907,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Physics: Conference Series","DOI":"10.1088/1742-6596/1317/1/012098","ISSN":"1742-6588","issue":"1","page":"012098","title":"Plants used in the traditional ceremony in kanagarian tiku","volume":"1317","author":[{"family":"Des","given":"M."},{"family":"Rizki","given":""},{"family":"Fitri","given":"Melisa"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":910,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F53EYL6Q"],"itemData":{"id":910,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Background: Conservation of biodiversity is primary important of today’s critically vulnerable environment. Efficient conservation can be possible only with the long-term participation and understanding of the communities. Ritual beliefs of the indigenous people are one of the important tools to understand the local communities and aid the nature conservation. In this paper, we documented contemporary ritual practices and ritual plant uses among the Naxi people and discussed the importance of traditional knowledge on ritual practice in the conservation of plants in the mountains presenting a case study of the Dongba culture.\\nMethods: This study was carried out from July in 2013 to July in 2014. To document and analyze the present state of the ritual plant used by the Naxi people we conducted an ethnobotanical survey. We interviewed local people including Dongba priests using the semi-structured questionnaire. During the field study, we participated in the local religious activities to witness the use of different plants in ritual activities of the Naxi people. We interviewed twenty-two key informants and eleven of them were male. All the specimens of documented species were collected and deposited at the herbarium of Kunming Institute of Botany.\\nResults: The survey results revealed the Naxi people possessed sound knowledge of the traditional ritual plants and great diversity of plants used in many of Naxi rituals and festivals. From the survey, we documented 32 ritual plant species belonging to 24 genera of 17 families used in various ritual activities. The ritual plants were grouped into two categories, namely those burned as incense, and those used for decoration. The incense plants like Olea europaea subsp. cuspidata and Pistacia weinmanniifolia were probably promising natural aromatic resource. Plants of genus Quercus were the most frequently used species. The places for ritual activities were diverse, such as the incense burners inside and outside the house and sacred trees at the Baishuitai. Local people except the young generation had an abundant of traditional knowledge.\\nConclusions: Our study shows the live ritual activities and the beliefs of the residents are keeping the plant diversity and the entire forest preserved as sacred mountains. Our study emphasizes traditional belief and an alternative view of conservation that is not led mainly by governmental policies, as local practices and ritual plants uses play as constant reminders to the Naxi on nature conservation. However, further research is recommended for in-depth understanding the role of traditional belief in biodiversity conservation.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-017-0186-3","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"58","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The implications of ritual practices and ritual plant uses on nature conservation: a case study among the Naxi in Yunnan Province, Southwest China","title-short":"The implications of ritual practices and ritual plant uses on nature conservation","URL":"http://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-017-0186-3","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Geng","given":"Yanfei"},{"family":"Hu","given":"Guoxiong"},{"family":"Ranjitkar","given":"Sailesh"},{"family":"Shi","given":"Yinxian"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Yu"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Yuhua"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}},{"id":32096,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6KSVDJUA"],"itemData":{"id":32096,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-017-0185-4","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","page":"59","title":"Sociocultural and ecological factors influencing management of edible and non-edible plants: the case of Ixcatlán, Mexico.","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Rangel-Landa","given":"Selene"},{"family":"Casas","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"García-Frapolli","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Lira","given":"Rafael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Some of the wild species used for rituals are unusual and rare ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1i3uk4itoe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Naegel, 2004; Rangel-Landa, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Naegel, 2004; Rangel-Landa, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32037,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N7S6AU9Z"],"itemData":{"id":32037,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Shellfish Research","ISSN":"0730-8000","page":"211–214","title":"Plicopurpura pansa (Gould, 1853) from the Pacific coast of Mexico and Central America: A traditional source of Tyrian purple","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Naegel","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":32096,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6KSVDJUA"],"itemData":{"id":32096,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-017-0185-4","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","page":"59","title":"Sociocultural and ecological factors influencing management of edible and non-edible plants: the case of Ixcatlán, Mexico.","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Rangel-Landa","given":"Selene"},{"family":"Casas","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"García-Frapolli","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Lira","given":"Rafael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Gatherers give them as presents to the organizers of the ceremony or communitarian feast, and commercialization is uncommon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1qc60nclkv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barcel\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}, 2019; Rangel-Landa, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Barceló, 2019; Rangel-Landa, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32098,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7GM4C9D5"],"itemData":{"id":32098,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-019-09474-z","ISSN":"0013-0001","page":"1–8","title":"Ethnobotany in a “Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity”: Plants in “la Patum” Festivity (Berga, Catalonia, Iberian Peninsula)1","author":[{"family":"Barceló","given":"Carme M."},{"family":"Butí","given":"Elisenda"},{"family":"Gras","given":"Airy"},{"family":"Orriols","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Vallès","given":"Joan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32096,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6KSVDJUA"],"itemData":{"id":32096,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-017-0185-4","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","page":"59","title":"Sociocultural and ecological factors influencing management of edible and non-edible plants: the case of Ixcatlán, Mexico.","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Rangel-Landa","given":"Selene"},{"family":"Casas","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"García-Frapolli","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Lira","given":"Rafael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, because of important traditional culture, there is often concern about the disappearance of these particular species in studies of national culture. Rare species are harvested at levels just enough to satisfy the needs of the community ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a23s0f31fao","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rangel-Landa, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Rangel-Landa, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32096,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6KSVDJUA"],"itemData":{"id":32096,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-017-0185-4","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","page":"59","title":"Sociocultural and ecological factors influencing management of edible and non-edible plants: the case of Ixcatlán, Mexico.","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Rangel-Landa","given":"Selene"},{"family":"Casas","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"García-Frapolli","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Lira","given":"Rafael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and in some cases substitutions are developed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2a32uaucp8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Des, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Des, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":907,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MMKV69AY"],"itemData":{"id":907,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Physics: Conference Series","DOI":"10.1088/1742-6596/1317/1/012098","ISSN":"1742-6588","issue":"1","page":"012098","title":"Plants used in the traditional ceremony in kanagarian tiku","volume":"1317","author":[{"family":"Des","given":"M."},{"family":"Rizki","given":""},{"family":"Fitri","given":"Melisa"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The ritual practices of Naxi people in Yunnan, China for example, pay high respect to conserving natural resources, although these beliefs and cultural expressions receive less attention from younger generations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a4h15cmp4g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yanfei Geng, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Yanfei Geng, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":910,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F53EYL6Q"],"itemData":{"id":910,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Background: Conservation of biodiversity is primary important of today’s critically vulnerable environment. Efficient conservation can be possible only with the long-term participation and understanding of the communities. Ritual beliefs of the indigenous people are one of the important tools to understand the local communities and aid the nature conservation. In this paper, we documented contemporary ritual practices and ritual plant uses among the Naxi people and discussed the importance of traditional knowledge on ritual practice in the conservation of plants in the mountains presenting a case study of the Dongba culture.\\nMethods: This study was carried out from July in 2013 to July in 2014. To document and analyze the present state of the ritual plant used by the Naxi people we conducted an ethnobotanical survey. We interviewed local people including Dongba priests using the semi-structured questionnaire. During the field study, we participated in the local religious activities to witness the use of different plants in ritual activities of the Naxi people. We interviewed twenty-two key informants and eleven of them were male. All the specimens of documented species were collected and deposited at the herbarium of Kunming Institute of Botany.\\nResults: The survey results revealed the Naxi people possessed sound knowledge of the traditional ritual plants and great diversity of plants used in many of Naxi rituals and festivals. From the survey, we documented 32 ritual plant species belonging to 24 genera of 17 families used in various ritual activities. The ritual plants were grouped into two categories, namely those burned as incense, and those used for decoration. The incense plants like Olea europaea subsp. cuspidata and Pistacia weinmanniifolia were probably promising natural aromatic resource. Plants of genus Quercus were the most frequently used species. The places for ritual activities were diverse, such as the incense burners inside and outside the house and sacred trees at the Baishuitai. Local people except the young generation had an abundant of traditional knowledge.\\nConclusions: Our study shows the live ritual activities and the beliefs of the residents are keeping the plant diversity and the entire forest preserved as sacred mountains. Our study emphasizes traditional belief and an alternative view of conservation that is not led mainly by governmental policies, as local practices and ritual plants uses play as constant reminders to the Naxi on nature conservation. However, further research is recommended for in-depth understanding the role of traditional belief in biodiversity conservation.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-017-0186-3","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"58","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The implications of ritual practices and ritual plant uses on nature conservation: a case study among the Naxi in Yunnan Province, Southwest China","title-short":"The implications of ritual practices and ritual plant uses on nature conservation","URL":"http://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-017-0186-3","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Geng","given":"Yanfei"},{"family":"Hu","given":"Guoxiong"},{"family":"Ranjitkar","given":"Sailesh"},{"family":"Shi","given":"Yinxian"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Yu"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Yuhua"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Hallucinogenic plants and fungi harvested in the wild are used by shamans or mediums, in religious or curing ceremonies, in particular on the American continent, but also in Siberia (Amanita muscaria) and Africa (iboga, Tabernante iboga, in Gabon). For the Jotï, an indigenous group in the Venezuelan Amazon, mushrooms play a central role in their religious and spiritual beliefs and are fundamental to their cosmology ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Xgq6VVa8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zent, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Zent, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30782,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q7TDSQLI"],"itemData":{"id":30782,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9039-2","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"471-481","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Mushrooms for Life among the Jotï in the Venezuelan Guayana","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9039-2","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Zent","given":"Egleé L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Among the most known in the Americas are ayahuasca (Banisteropsis sp.) from the Amazon, and Psylocybe mushrooms and peyote (Lophophora williamsii) from Mexico and the United States of America Southwest ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a24kh8usfrk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Furst, 1972; Heim & Wasson, 1958; Schultes & Hofmann, 1979)","plainCitation":"(Furst, 1972; Heim & Wasson, 1958; Schultes & Hofmann, 1979)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32050,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M3DV5BCD"],"itemData":{"id":32050,"type":"book","event-place":"New York","number-of-pages":"304","publisher":"Praeger Publishers","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Flesh of the Gods : the ritual Use of Hallucinogens","URL":"https://archive.org/details/fleshofgodsritua0000furs","author":[{"family":"Furst","given":"Peter T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1972"]]}}},{"id":32048,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VC6F5CQ4"],"itemData":{"id":32048,"type":"book","collection-title":"Archives du Muséum","event-place":"Paris","language":"French","number-of-pages":"445","publisher":"Muséum National d'Histoire Naturelle","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Heim R, Wasson RG. 1958. Les champignons hallucinogènes du Mexique.","URL":"https://sciencepress.mnhn.fr/sites/default/files/articles/pdf/archives_du_museum_serie_7_tome_6_-_les_champignons_hallucinogenes_du_mexique_-_etudes_ethnologiques_taxinomiques_biologiques_physiologiques_et_chimiques_-_med.pdf","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Heim","given":"Roger"},{"family":"Wasson","given":"R. Gordon"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1958"]]}}},{"id":32047,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X6D47AVP"],"itemData":{"id":32047,"type":"book","event-place":"London","publisher":"McGraw & Hill,","publisher-place":"London","title":"Plants of the gods","URL":"https://archive.org/details/SchultesHofmannPlantsOfTheGodsHealingArts2001","author":[{"family":"Schultes","given":"Richard Evans"},{"family":"Hofmann","given":"Albert"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1979"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the 1960s, after Wasson’s discovery of hallucinogenic Psylocybe mushrooms in Mexico, “hippies” rushed to that country to experience these fungi. There has also been a development of shamanistic tourism among the Mazatecs in Mexico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"avkb8bmlhu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Demanget, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Demanget, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32054,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CHE5RMGF"],"itemData":{"id":32054,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ethnologies","DOI":"10.7202/1006310ar","issue":"2","page":"199-232","title":"Aux sources d’une communauté imaginée. Le tourisme chamanique à Huautla de Jimenez (Indiens mazatèques, Mexique)","URL":"https://www.erudit.org/fr/revues/ethno/2010-v32-n2-ethno5003573/1006310ar/","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Demanget","given":"Magali"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and in the Peruvian Amazon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a185hv92b00","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fotiou, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Fotiou, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32068,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2JVW54D3"],"itemData":{"id":32068,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Anthropology of Consciousness","DOI":"10.1111/anoc.12056","ISSN":"10534202","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Anthropol Conscious","language":"en","page":"151-179","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Globalization of Ayahuasca Shamanism and the Erasure of Indigenous Shamanism","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/anoc.12056","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Fotiou","given":"Evgenia"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Peyote is still traded in the United States of America ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15ps0d1o5k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Feeney, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Feeney, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32064,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NRJ43Y89"],"itemData":{"id":32064,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Organization","DOI":"10.17730/0018-7259.76.1.59","ISSN":"0018-7259, 1938-3525","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Human Organization","language":"en","page":"59-72","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Peyote as Commodity: An Examination of Market Actors and Access Mechanisms","title-short":"Peyote as Commodity","URL":"http://sfaajournals.net/doi/10.17730/0018-7259.76.1.59","volume":"76","author":[{"family":"Feeney","given":"Kevin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Overall, 216 species of fungi are thought to be hallucinogenic, and of these 116 species belong to the Genus Psilocybe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a56hpnceb8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Willis, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Willis, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30357,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MX282NHI"],"itemData":{"id":30357,"type":"book","event-place":"Richmond, UK","publisher":"Kew Publishing","publisher-place":"Richmond, UK","title":"State of the world's fungi 2018. Report.","author":[{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."}],"editor":[{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Since rituals are not a daily need, there are few relevant management measures that are directly applied to species specifically relating to ceremonial use, and it is recommended that maintaining traditional and cultural practices can complement management strategies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15ectfv512","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kideghesho, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Kideghesho, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34285,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BZ7ENXP7"],"itemData":{"id":34285,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science & Management","DOI":"10.1080/17451590903065579","ISSN":"1745-1604, 1745-1590","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science & Management","language":"en","page":"83-94","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The potentials of traditional African cultural practices in mitigating overexploitation of wildlife species and habitat loss: experience of Tanzania","title-short":"The potentials of traditional African cultural practices in mitigating overexploitation of wildlife species and habitat loss","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17451590903065579","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Kideghesho","given":"Jafari R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",7,21]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In fact, conserving biodiversity based on cultural and religious faiths may be often more efficient and sustainable than government legislation or regulations given peoples’ long-term relationships with the particular species. 
      
3.3.2.3.2. Decorative and aesthetic

Wild species are harvested for crafts and decorative use for personal consumption, as gifts, and for sale as raw or value-added items ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"arqs74vt73","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. R. Emery, 1999)","plainCitation":"(M. R. Emery, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32111,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ECS8424C"],"itemData":{"id":32111,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"In: Josiah, Scott J., ed. Proceedings of the North American Conference on Enterprise Development Through Agroforestry: Farming the Forest for Specialty Products. Minneapolis, MN. 25-32","language":"en","source":"www.fs.usda.gov","title":"Social values of specialty forest products to rural communities","URL":"https://www.fs.usda.gov/treesearch/pubs/18989","author":[{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The gathering of wild species like orchids, Bromeliads, succulents, and wild fungi are important sources of money and livelihood for collectors at local and regional scales and may also enter into global trade. Hence the sustainability of their wild populations, habitat, economies and communities is a subject of concern. Many wild species harvested for crafts are usually listed in inventories as parts of general ethnobotanical research. It can be challenging to distinguish among uses at the local level, as one collection may result in the gathering one species for food, medicine, ritual decoration, and transplant into the home garden. There is a lack of research on the sustainability of this kind of mixed use.

* Ornamental wild plants
When the acquisition is part of the organism and managed well, gathering wild plants for the use of decoration may not have too many negative effects. For example, although there is lack of conservation assessment of the 80% of wild harvested Indian plants to make potpourri, the gathering of such 455 species provides a supplementary income to rural poor and is considered as a sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a21ln4isa6a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cook, Leon, & Nesbitt, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Cook, Leon, & Nesbitt, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32094,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IWWAVBNL"],"itemData":{"id":32094,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-015-9325-8","ISSN":"0013-0001","issue":"4","page":"330–344","title":"Potpourri as a Sustainable Plant Product: Identity, Origin, and Conservation Status1","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Cook","given":"Frances E. M."},{"family":"Leon","given":"Christine J."},{"family":"Nesbitt","given":"Mark"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Minas Gerais, central Brazil, the gatherers of everlasting (sempre-vivas) flowers of the Serra do Espinhaço Meridional enrich the native pastures where the flowers grow with the seeds fallen from the collected flowers and stimulate their growth by fire management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"alv26nb9qv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Monteiro, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Monteiro, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32053,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MK9VRMTB"],"itemData":{"id":32053,"type":"chapter","collection-number":"3","collection-title":"Povos e comunidades tradicionais","container-title":"Sistemas agrícolas tradicionais no Brasil","edition":"Simoni Eidt J., Udry C. (ed).","event-place":"Brasilia","ISBN":"978-85-7035-893-6","language":"portuguese","page":"93-140","publisher":"Embrapa","publisher-place":"Brasilia","title":"Sistema agrícola tradicional da Serra do Espinhaço Meridional, MG: transumância, biodiversidade e cultura nas paisagens manejadas pelos(as) apanhadores(as) de flores sempre-vivas","URL":"https://www.embrapa.br/busca-de-publicacoes/-/publicacao/1109452/sistemas-agricolas-tradicionais-no-brasil","author":[{"family":"Monteiro","given":"Fernanda T."},{"family":"Fávero","given":"Claudenir"},{"family":"Costa Filho","given":"Aderval"},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"Maria Neudes S."},{"family":"Soldati","given":"Gustavo T."},{"family":"Duque-Brasil","given":"Reinaldo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, demonstrating a form of traditional management and care which supports sustainable use. Their agro-extrative system was recognized by FAO in 2020 as a Globally Important Agricultural Heritage System (GIAHS) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2b38jj8pig","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(GIAHS, 2020)","plainCitation":"(GIAHS, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32041,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HIBUACM9"],"itemData":{"id":32041,"type":"webpage","container-title":"Globally Important Agricultural heritage Systems","title":"Traditional Agricultural System in the Southern Espinhaço Range, Minas Gerais, Brazil","URL":"https://www.fao.org/giahs/giahsaroundtheworld/designated-sites/latin-america-and-the-caribbean/semprevivas-minasgerais/en","author":[{"family":"GIAHS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Trade in exotic wild plants increased in North America and Europe after the Second World War and demand for wild plants increased pressure on wild populations and even drove the extinction of some rare species in the late 1970s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"arcbaja8u0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lavorgna, Rutherford, Vaglica, Smith, & Sajeva, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Lavorgna, Rutherford, Vaglica, Smith, & Sajeva, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":902,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q8EX4J94"],"itemData":{"id":902,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"European Journal on Criminal Policy and Research","DOI":"10.1007/s10610-017-9354-1","ISSN":"0928-1371, 1572-9869","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Eur J Crim Policy Res","language":"en","page":"269-288","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"CITES, wild plants, and opportunities for crime","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10610-017-9354-1","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Lavorgna","given":"Anita"},{"family":"Rutherford","given":"Catherine"},{"family":"Vaglica","given":"Valentina"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Matthew J."},{"family":"Sajeva","given":"Maurizio"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Twenty-two European countries reported the total value of “ornamental plants” at almost 1,400 million euros, which amounts to 49.6% and the highest of the marketed plant products from forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1u3uur338l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Forest Europe, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Forest Europe, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30938,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XNL3I4JK"],"itemData":{"id":30938,"type":"report","title":"State of Europe’s Forests 2020","URL":"www.foresteurope.org","author":[{"family":"Forest Europe","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora has listed more than 32,000 species of ornamental plants in its Appendices, most in Appendix II (Table 3.8). 
      The gathering for sale of cut flower or foliage of Bromeliads, or ornamental plants like Aloe and Orchids are considered to negatively affect species survival ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aj81oegn0r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Flores-Palacios, Bustamante-Molina, Corona-L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez, & Valencia-D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az, 2015; Mondrag\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}n Chaparro & Ticktin, 2011; Mondrag\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}n, M\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}ndez-Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a, & Morillo, 2016; Negrelle & Anacleto, 2012; Phelps & Webb, 2015; Sakai, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Flores-Palacios, Bustamante-Molina, Corona-López, & Valencia-Díaz, 2015; Mondragón Chaparro & Ticktin, 2011; Mondragón, Méndez-García, & Morillo, 2016; Negrelle & Anacleto, 2012; Phelps & Webb, 2015; Sakai, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32092,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4I2SCXUL"],"itemData":{"id":32092,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientia Horticulturae","DOI":"10.1016/j.scienta.2015.03.003","ISSN":"0304-4238","page":"72–79","title":"Seed number, germination and longevity in wild dry forest Tillandsia species of horticultural value","volume":"187","author":[{"family":"Flores-Palacios","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Bustamante-Molina","given":"Angelita"},{"family":"Corona-López","given":"Angélica"},{"family":"Valencia-Díaz","given":"Susana"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":32063,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZPX7P4WB"],"itemData":{"id":32063,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2011.01691.x","ISSN":"08888892","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"797-807","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Demographic Effects of Harvesting Epiphytic Bromeliads and an Alternative Approach to Collection: Use and Conservation of Epiphytic Bromeliads","title-short":"Demographic Effects of Harvesting Epiphytic Bromeliads and an Alternative Approach to Collection","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2011.01691.x","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Mondragón Chaparro","given":"Demetria"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8]]}}},{"id":32099,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DYRUQQT7"],"itemData":{"id":32099,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-016-9332-4","ISSN":"0013-0001","issue":"1","page":"29–36","title":"Prioritizing the Conservation of Epiphytic Bromeliads Using Ethnobotanical Information from a Traditional Mexican Market","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Mondragón","given":"Demetria"},{"family":"Méndez-García","given":"Elia","dropping-particle":"del"},{"family":"Morillo","given":"Ivón"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32039,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/84W9UUSX"],"itemData":{"id":32039,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ciência Rural, Santa Maria","ISSN":"0103-8478","issue":"6","page":"981–986","title":"Bromeliads wild harvesting in State of Parana","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Negrelle","given":"B. R. R."},{"family":"Anacleto","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":32526,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5BUYUBQK"],"itemData":{"id":32526,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Conservation and environmental management often rely heavily on official statistics, but there are often concerns over accuracy and reliability, particularly true when dealing with sensitive issues such as illegal harvest and trade. A growing number of cases highlight commercial trades in wild flora and fauna that have been undocumented in official data. Here we present the first in-depth study of the trade of wild-collected ornamental plants in continental Southeast Asia, focusing on the four largest wildlife markets in Thailand. Botanical surveys revealed a massive, previously undocumented commercial trade in wild, protected ornamental plants involving Thailand, Lao PDR and Myanmar, focusing primarily on the Orchidaceae. The results indicate that illegal trade threatens not only charismatic Southeast Asian mammals, reptiles and amphibians, but also hundreds of tropical plant species. Trade surveys identified 347 orchid species in 93 genera, including many listed as threatened. The observed cross-border trade moves plants at a rate orders of magnitude larger than government-reported statistics, and directly conflicts with official policy statement. Harvester interviews strongly indicated that wild collection was negatively affecting the majority of species they traded. Despite three decades of broad restrictions on the international trade of all wild orchids, these results highlight a major conservation challenge that has been almost completely overlooked. There is urgent need to improve botanical trade monitoring, to operationalize existing conservation commitments, and as part of a broader, multifaceted response to illegal trade. We call for increased attention to botanical trade and conservation in Southeast Asia, and argue that efforts to tackle illegal wildlife trade must ensure they include flora.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2015.03.030","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","page":"296-305","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"“Invisible” wildlife trades: Southeast Asia’s undocumented illegal trade in wild ornamental plants","title-short":"“Invisible” wildlife trades","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S000632071500141X","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Phelps","given":"J."},{"family":"Webb","given":"Edward L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",6,1]]}}},{"id":32030,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D483A67U"],"itemData":{"id":32030,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2016.10.022","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"Plants 54 2009","page":"340–349","title":"Social and ecological factors associated with the use of non-timber forest products by people in rural Borneo","volume":"204","author":[{"family":"Sakai","given":"Shoko"},{"family":"Choy","given":"Yee Keong"},{"family":"Kishimoto-Yamada","given":"Keiko"},{"family":"Takano","given":"Kohei T."},{"family":"Ichikawa","given":"Masahiro"},{"family":"Samejima","given":"Hiromitsu"},{"family":"Kato","given":"Yumi"},{"family":"Soda","given":"Ryoji"},{"family":"Ushio","given":"Masayuki"},{"family":"Saizen","given":"Izuru"},{"family":"Nakashizuka","given":"Tohru"},{"family":"Itioka","given":"Takao"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many of these species are also cultivated, but no data was available at the time of this assessment on the share of global market sales from wild versus cultivated plants. Sale prices vary between species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a29tviqjn7c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mondrag\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}n, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Mondragón, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32099,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DYRUQQT7"],"itemData":{"id":32099,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-016-9332-4","ISSN":"0013-0001","issue":"1","page":"29–36","title":"Prioritizing the Conservation of Epiphytic Bromeliads Using Ethnobotanical Information from a Traditional Mexican Market","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Mondragón","given":"Demetria"},{"family":"Méndez-García","given":"Elia","dropping-particle":"del"},{"family":"Morillo","given":"Ivón"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but the origin of the plants (wild vs farmed) did not affect price, since cultured plants have better physical variables than wild-harvested plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2c6sk31fvq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Elps, Carrasco, & Webb, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Elps, Carrasco, & Webb, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32090,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/99LRDWL9"],"itemData":{"id":32090,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12160","ISSN":"1523-1739","issue":"1","title":"A Framework for Assessing Supply-Side Wildlife Conservation","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Elps","given":"J."},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L. R."},{"family":"Webb","given":"E. L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some researchers believe that the supply-side measures to ensure the sustainable use may lack effectiveness. Consumer preferences may help to reduce the market driven push to overharvest ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EvAOGUtJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Elps, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Elps, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32090,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/99LRDWL9"],"itemData":{"id":32090,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12160","ISSN":"1523-1739","issue":"1","title":"A Framework for Assessing Supply-Side Wildlife Conservation","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Elps","given":"J."},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L. R."},{"family":"Webb","given":"E. L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
