      More than a half of all cactus species (57%) are used by people. Cacti are prized for their aesthetic qualities. The most common use is for ornamental horticulture (674 species), which in most cases is related to gathering wild plants and seeds for specialized collections. Cacti comprise about 130 genera and 1,500 species distributed mainly in North and South America; however, several species of Rhipsalis (mistletoe cactus) occur in tropical Africa. Some species of Opuntia (prickly pear) have been introduced in Africa, Australia and South Asia (India). Nearly all genera are cultivated as ornamentals; some of the more common are Opuntia and Carnegiea (giant saguaro), Cereus (hedge cactus, cereus), Echinopsis (sea-urchin cactus), Epiphyllum (orchid cactus), Hylocereus (night-blooming cereus), Mammilaria (pincushion cactus), Melocactus (Turk’s cap cactus), Rhipsalis, and Schlumbergera (Christmas cactus) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ahnro0271f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Judd, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Judd, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32106,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EMQ5JV9W"],"itemData":{"id":32106,"type":"book","call-number":"QK95 .P548 1999","event-place":"Sunderland, Mass","ISBN":"978-0-87893-404-1","language":"en","number-of-pages":"464","publisher":"Sinauer Associates","publisher-place":"Sunderland, Mass","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Plant systematics: a phylogenetic approach","title-short":"Plant systematics","editor":[{"family":"Judd","given":"Walter S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Native people of the Americas propagate branches, seeds or transplant complete individuals from the wild to their agroforestry systems and home gardens ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ptfg1g4te","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Casas & Barbera, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Casas & Barbera, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30779,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6NEAZCK5"],"itemData":{"id":30779,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Cacti. Biology and uses","edition":"Nobel, Park S.","event-place":"Berkeley & Los Angeles","page":"143-162","publisher":"University of California Press,","publisher-place":"Berkeley & Los Angeles","title":"Mesoamerican domestication and diffusion","author":[{"family":"Casas","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Barbera","given":"Giuseppe"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. People occasionally harvest useful parts of several species of cacti for use in traditional medicine. Cactus pears from Opuntia stricta are also considered as a potential source of natural colorants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1jggo3ims9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Casas & Barbera, 2002; Goettsch, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Casas & Barbera, 2002; Goettsch, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30779,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6NEAZCK5"],"itemData":{"id":30779,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Cacti. Biology and uses","edition":"Nobel, Park S.","event-place":"Berkeley & Los Angeles","page":"143-162","publisher":"University of California Press,","publisher-place":"Berkeley & Los Angeles","title":"Mesoamerican domestication and diffusion","author":[{"family":"Casas","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Barbera","given":"Giuseppe"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":32062,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G9MWISD6"],"itemData":{"id":32062,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Plants","DOI":"10.1038/nplants.2015.142","ISSN":"2055-0278","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Nature Plants","language":"en","page":"15142","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"High proportion of cactus species threatened with extinction","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nplants2015142","volume":"1","author":[