      Due to their popularity and the commercialization of so many wild species, poaching entire plants from the wild is a growing problem. Most species are regulated by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (Table 3.8). Among the threatened cacti species, 64% are utilized by humans in some form and 57% (236 species) are used in horticulture ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a35es7s0a7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Goettsch, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Goettsch, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32062,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G9MWISD6"],"itemData":{"id":32062,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Plants","DOI":"10.1038/nplants.2015.142","ISSN":"2055-0278","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Nature Plants","language":"en","page":"15142","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"High proportion of cactus species threatened with extinction","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nplants2015142","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Goettsch","given":"Bárbara"},{"family":"Hilton-Taylor","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Cruz-Piñón","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Duffy","given":"James P."},{"family":"Frances","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Hernández","given":"Héctor M."},{"family":"Inger","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Pollock","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Schipper","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Superina","given":"Mariella"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Nigel P."},{"family":"Tognelli","given":"Marcelo"},{"family":"Abba","given":"Agustín M."},{"family":"Arias","given":"Salvador"},{"family":"Arreola-Nava","given":"Hilda J."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Marc A."},{"family":"Bárcenas","given":"Rolando T."},{"family":"Barrios","given":"Duniel"},{"family":"Braun","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Butterworth","given":"Charles A."},{"family":"Búrquez","given":"Alberto"},{"family":"Caceres","given":"Fátima"},{"family":"Chazaro-Basañez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Corral-Díaz","given":"Rafael"},{"family":"Valle Perea","given":"Mario","non-dropping-particle":"del"},{"family":"Demaio","given":"Pablo H."},{"family":"Duarte de Barros","given":"Williams A."},{"family":"Durán","given":"Rafael"},{"family":"Yancas","given":"Luis Faúndez"},{"family":"Felger","given":"Richard S."},{"family":"Fitz-Maurice","given":"Betty"},{"family":"Fitz-Maurice","given":"Walter A."},{"family":"Gann","given":"George"},{"family":"Gómez-Hinostrosa","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Gonzales-Torres","given":"Luis R."},{"family":"Patrick Griffith","given":"M."},{"family":"Guerrero","given":"Pablo C."},{"family":"Hammel","given":"Barry"},{"family":"Heil","given":"Kenneth D."},{"family":"Hernández-Oria","given":"José Guadalupe"},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Ishihara","given":"Mario Ishiki"},{"family":"Kiesling","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Larocca","given":"João"},{"family":"León-de la Luz","given":"José Luis"},{"family":"Loaiza S.","given":"Christian R."},{"family":"Lowry","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Machado","given":"Marlon C."},{"family":"Majure","given":"Lucas C."},{"family":"Ávalos","given":"José Guadalupe Martínez"},{"family":"Martorell","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Maschinski","given":"Joyce"},{"family":"Méndez","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Mittermeier","given":"Russell A."},{"family":"Nassar","given":"Jafet M."},{"family":"Negrón-Ortiz","given":"Vivian"},{"family":"Oakley","given":"Luis J."},{"family":"Ortega-Baes","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"Ana Beatriz Pin"},{"family":"Pinkava","given":"Donald J."},{"family":"Porter","given":"J. Mark"},{"family":"Puente-Martinez","given":"Raul"},{"family":"Gamarra","given":"José Roque"},{"family":"Pérez","given":"Patricio Saldivia"},{"family":"Martínez","given":"Emiliano Sánchez"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Manuel Sotomayor M. del C.","given":"J."},{"family":"Stuart","given":"Simon N."},{"family":"Muñoz","given":"José Luis Tapia"},{"family":"Terrazas","given":"Teresa"},{"family":"Terry","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Trevisson","given":"Marcelo"},{"family":"Valverde","given":"Teresa"},{"family":"Van Devender","given":"Thomas R."},{"family":"Véliz-Pérez","given":"Mario Esteban"},{"family":"Walter","given":"Helmut E."},{"family":"Wyatt","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"Zappi","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Alejandro Zavala-Hurtado","given":"J."},{"family":"Gaston","given":"Kevin J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There is growing concern that a high proportion of cactus species may be threatened with extinction in the near future, mainly due to growing illegal trade.
      Orchids are a prominent group of the global horticultural trade. While large numbers of orchids are grown commercially, there are still large numbers taken directly from the wild. Over-harvesting of wild orchids associated with floral and medicinal trade is a serious concern for their long-term survival ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aokfg2pf1m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SU6WGFBJ"],"itemData":{"id":32354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society","DOI":"10.1093/botlinnean/box083","ISSN":"0024-4074, 1095-8339","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"435-455","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A review of the trade in orchids and its implications for conservation","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/botlinnean/article/186/4/435/4736317","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Boer","given":"Hugo J","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Fay","given":"Michael F"},{"family":"Gale","given":"Stephan W"},{"family":"Gardiner","given":"Lauren M"},{"family":"Gunasekara","given":"Rajasinghe S"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Pankaj"},{"family":"Masters","given":"Susanne"},{"family":"Metusala","given":"Destario"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L"},{"family":"Veldman","given":"Sarina"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Shan"},{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Cross-border trade of orchids is well recognized as a threat to orchid conservation and regulated by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. However, domestic trade may not be regulated or poorly enforced in some orchid-rich countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ip0505q8u","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Phelps & Webb, 2015; Tamara Ticktin, 2020; Wong & Liu, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Phelps & Webb, 2015; Tamara Ticktin, 2020; Wong & Liu, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32526,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5BUYUBQK"],"itemData":{"id":32526,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Conservation and environmental management often rely heavily on official statistics, but there are often concerns over accuracy and reliability, particularly true when dealing with sensitive issues such as illegal harvest and trade. A growing number of cases highlight commercial trades in wild flora and fauna that have been undocumented in official data. Here we present the first in-depth study of the trade of wild-collected ornamental plants in continental Southeast Asia, focusing on the four largest wildlife markets in Thailand. Botanical surveys revealed a massive, previously undocumented commercial trade in wild, protected ornamental plants involving Thailand, Lao PDR and Myanmar, focusing primarily on the Orchidaceae. The results indicate that illegal trade threatens not only charismatic Southeast Asian mammals, reptiles and amphibians, but also hundreds of tropical plant species. Trade surveys identified 347 orchid species in 93 genera, including many listed as threatened. The observed cross-border trade moves plants at a rate orders of magnitude larger than government-reported statistics, and directly conflicts with official policy statement. Harvester interviews strongly indicated that wild collection was negatively affecting the majority of species they traded. Despite three decades of broad restrictions on the international trade of all wild orchids, these results highlight a major conservation challenge that has been almost completely overlooked. There is urgent need to improve botanical trade monitoring, to operationalize existing conservation commitments, and as part of a broader, multifaceted response to illegal trade. We call for increased attention to botanical trade and conservation in Southeast Asia, and argue that efforts to tackle illegal wildlife trade must ensure they include flora.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2015.03.030","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","page":"296-305","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"“Invisible” wildlife trades: Southeast Asia’s undocumented illegal trade in wild ornamental plants","title-short":"“Invisible” wildlife trades","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S000632071500141X","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Phelps","given":"J."},{"family":"Webb","given":"Edward L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",6,1]]}}},{"id":30988,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4WHFB3HN"],"itemData":{"id":30988,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12697","ISSN":"1755-263X, 1755-263X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"CONSERVATION LETTERS","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesis of wild orchid trade and demography provides new insight on conservation strategies","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/conl.12697","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Mondragón","given":"Demetria"},{"family":"Lopez?Toledo","given":"Leonel"},{"family":"Dutra?Elliott","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Aguirre?León","given":"Ernesto"},{"family":"Hernández?Apolinar","given":"Mariana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}},{"id":32067,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2Q3JVZQM"],"itemData":{"id":32067,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-019-09463-2","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"357-374","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild-Orchid Trade in a Chinese E-Commerce Market","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-019-09463-2","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Wong","given":"Shan"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Hong"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This legal and illegal domestic trade of wild orchids can be larger than cross-border trade and can also pose serious threats to species survival, but receive far less attention from orchid conservationists ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TDfDjliA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Phelps & Webb, 2015; Tamara Ticktin, 2020; Wong & Liu, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Phelps & Webb, 2015; Tamara Ticktin, 2020; Wong & Liu, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32526,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5BUYUBQK"],"itemData":{"id":32526,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Conservation and environmental management often rely heavily on official statistics, but there are often concerns over accuracy and reliability, particularly true when dealing with sensitive issues such as illegal harvest and trade. A growing number of cases highlight commercial trades in wild flora and fauna that have been undocumented in official data. Here we present the first in-depth study of the trade of wild-collected ornamental plants in continental Southeast Asia, focusing on the four largest wildlife markets in Thailand. Botanical surveys revealed a massive, previously undocumented commercial trade in wild, protected ornamental plants involving Thailand, Lao PDR and Myanmar, focusing primarily on the Orchidaceae. The results indicate that illegal trade threatens not only charismatic Southeast Asian mammals, reptiles and amphibians, but also hundreds of tropical plant species. Trade surveys identified 347 orchid species in 93 genera, including many listed as threatened. The observed cross-border trade moves plants at a rate orders of magnitude larger than government-reported statistics, and directly conflicts with official policy statement. Harvester interviews strongly indicated that wild collection was negatively affecting the majority of species they traded. Despite three decades of broad restrictions on the international trade of all wild orchids, these results highlight a major conservation challenge that has been almost completely overlooked. There is urgent need to improve botanical trade monitoring, to operationalize existing conservation commitments, and as part of a broader, multifaceted response to illegal trade. We call for increased attention to botanical trade and conservation in Southeast Asia, and argue that efforts to tackle illegal wildlife trade must ensure they include flora.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2015.03.030","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","page":"296-305","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"“Invisible” wildlife trades: Southeast Asia’s undocumented illegal trade in wild ornamental plants","title-short":"“Invisible” wildlife trades","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S000632071500141X","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Phelps","given":"J."},{"family":"Webb","given":"Edward L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",6,1]]}}},{"id":30988,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4WHFB3HN"],"itemData":{"id":30988,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12697","ISSN":"1755-263X, 1755-263X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"CONSERVATION LETTERS","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesis of wild orchid trade and demography provides new insight on conservation strategies","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/conl.12697","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Mondragón","given":"Demetria"},{"family":"Lopez?Toledo","given":"Leonel"},{"family":"Dutra?Elliott","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Aguirre?León","given":"Ernesto"},{"family":"Hernández?Apolinar","given":"Mariana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}},{"id":32067,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2Q3JVZQM"],"itemData":{"id":32067,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-019-09463-2","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"357-374","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild-Orchid Trade in a Chinese E-Commerce Market","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-019-09463-2","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Wong","given":"Shan"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Hong"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Snowdrops (Galanthus sp.) is a relatively small genus of perennial herbaceous plants distributed throughout Europe and central Asia, threatened in the wild due to habitat destruction, illegal gathering and climate change. A cherished garden plant with beautiful flowers blooming in winter and early spring, Galanthus is the world’s most traded wild-sourced ornamental bulb genus. To implement the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora regulations, Turkey sets annual export quotas of wild bulbs at 2.5-5.0 million for G. elwesii and 2-4 million G. woronowii. Georgia sets an export quota of wild G. woronowii at 15 million a year to ensure the trade and gathering do not endanger the survival of wild populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aslidg5hen","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R\\\\uc0\\\\u248{}nsted, Zubov, Bruun-Lund, & Davis, 2013; UNEP, 2021, p. 2021)","plainCitation":"(Rønsted, Zubov, Bruun-Lund, & Davis, 2013; UNEP, 2021, p. 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32066,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J9NVKMEK"],"itemData":{"id":32066,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Molecular Phylogenetics and Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.ympev.2013.05.019","ISSN":"10557903","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Molecular Phylogenetics and Evolution","language":"en","page":"205-217","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Snowdrops falling slowly into place: An improved phylogeny for Galanthus (Amaryllidaceae)","title-short":"Snowdrops falling slowly into place","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1055790313002194","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Rønsted","given":"Nina"},{"family":"Zubov","given":"Dimitri"},{"family":"Bruun-Lund","given":"Sam"},{"family":"Davis","given":"Aaron P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",10]]}}},{"id":32059,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/48RLMDK6"],"itemData":{"id":32059,"type":"webpage","title":"The Species+ Website. Nairobi, Kenya. Compiled by UNEP-WCMC, Cambridge, UK. Available at: www.speciesplus.net. [Accessed 25/02/2021].","URL":"https://speciesplus.net/","author":[{"family":"UNEP","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}},"locator":"2021"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      
* Natural cloth and dye
Numerous wild plants, lichens, and mushrooms have been used as natural dyes for centuries. Some of them, such as Brazil wood (Caesalpinia echinata), were traded across continents. Most natural dyes were substituted by chemical dyes from the 19th century on, but some remained in use in local arts and crafts, and have been revived recently. Some species are not only on textiles but also in the cosmetic and food industries. For instance, the lichen Rocella canariensis is used as a food coloring known as E121 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1k2j1pjv8r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cardon, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Cardon, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32061,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/96ERK9YZ"],"itemData":{"id":32061,"type":"book","call-number":"TP897 .C15 2007","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-904982-00-5","language":"eng","note":"OCLC: ocn123114906","number-of-pages":"778","publisher":"Archetype","publisher-place":"London","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Natural dyes: sources, tradition, technology and science","title-short":"Natural dyes","author":[{"family":"Cardon","given":"Dominique"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Cotton, linen, silk, wool and artificial fiber and dyes have replaced many wild sources. Uganda bark cloth was derived from the wild fig or mutuba tree (Ficus natalensis) and has been recognized by UNESCO as a masterpiece of the 'Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity'. The production process requires collaboration among local laborers, specific skills and specially designed tools. In recent years bark cloth has been explored as a sustainable fashion luxury textile, providing jobs to local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1sj40j2c8a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Venkatraman, Scott, & Liauw, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Venkatraman, Scott, & Liauw, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32033,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8Q5FSL7C"],"itemData":{"id":32033,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainable Materials and Technologies","DOI":"ARTN e00136 10.1016/j.susmat.2019.e00136","ISSN":"2214-9937","journalAbbreviation":"Sustain Mater Techno","language":"English","title":"Environmentally friendly and sustainable bark cloth for garment applications: Evaluation of fabric properties and apparel development","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Venkatraman","given":"P. D."},{"family":"Scott","given":"K."},{"family":"Liauw","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The use of bark facilitates scattered planting of mutuba trees in agroforestry systems, which in turn protects crops and soil from erosion on windy hill slopes. World Overview on Conservation Approaches and Technologies (WOCAT) has developed a guide on the use and propagation of the tree. This example highlights the value of this specialized knowledge. However, traditional knowledge on unique dying sources and processes is vanishing fast, and represents a knowledge gap which may become impossible to address in the near future.
      Many wild fungi and lichens are also harvested for use in dye making. For example, Emery, Martin and Dyke ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dSd1friB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Emery, 2006)","plainCitation":"(M. Emery, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":982,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DQ5PEQ2U"],"itemData":{"id":982,"type":"report","event-place":"Edinburgh","genre":"government report","language":"English","page":"i-viii + 1-40","publisher":"Forestry Commission","publisher-place":"Edinburgh","title":"Wild harvests from Scottish Woodlands: social, cultural, and economic values of contemporary non-timber forest products","author":[{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla"},{"family":"Martin","given":"Suzanne"},{"family":"Dyke","given":"Alison"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found that of the over 200 species harvested from the wild in Scotland, 76 of them were non-vascular species. Of these, 16 were harvested for crafting purposes such as the production of dyes for homespun wool. A group of lichens known collectively as ‘orchil’ has been used as a dyestuff since the Bronze Age in Europe. Trade in orchil declined as manufactured, synthetic and cheaper alternatives were found. It continues at low levels for artisanal use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2q5vgi5sg3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. Wolfslehner, 2019)","plainCitation":"(B. Wolfslehner, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30991,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IVV5KSPW"],"itemData":{"id":30991,"type":"report","publisher":"European  Forest Institute","title":"Non-wood forest products in Europe: Seeing the forest  around the trees. What Science Can Tell Us 10.","URL":"https://efi.int/sites/default/files/files/publication-bank/2019/efi_wsctu_10_2019.pdf","author":[{"family":"Wolfslehner","given":"B."},{"family":"Prokofieva","given":"I."},{"family":"Mavsar","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some firms specialized in plant dyes aim at meeting standards of environmentally and socially responsible manufacturing and have applied to a certification, but as of 2010 this issue remained unresolved ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1p5n80m46l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cardon, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Cardon, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":908,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NENU26QC"],"itemData":{"id":908,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Symposium Proceedings Textile Society of America","edition":"Textile Society of America","event-place":"Lincoln","page":"1-10","publisher":"University of Nebraska","publisher-place":"Lincoln","title":"Cardon D. 2010. Natural Dyes, Our Global Heritage of Colors.  . University of Nebraska, Lincoln.","URL":"https://digitalcommons.unl.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1011&context=tsaconf","author":[{"family":"Cardon","given":"Dominique"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
* Handicrafts
The following is not meant to be an exhaustive inventory of all wild algae, fungi and plants used for handicrafts. Rather, it is a review of the wild species of interest with regards to sustainable use which appeared in the systematic literature searches.
	A wild plant material called golden grass (Syngonanthus nitens) is used to produce golden handicraft articles in Brazil. Rural communities harvest, process and knit the scapes of Syngonanthus nitens, which has been an important source of income for them since the late 1990s. The survival of plant populations was once affected by the increase in community demand for scapes. The Brazilian federal environmental agency (Ibama) has proposed management techniques to prevent overexploitation of the species. For example, the harvest time was set precisely to ensure the removal of inflorescences after seed production or full maturation. Furthermore, returning the capitula of inflorescences used in handicraft to the field represents another important tool for the sustainable management of golden grass ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1lok2iakf2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Oliveira, Cruz, Sousa, Moreira, & Tanaka, 2014; I. B. Schmidt, Figueiredo, & Scariot, 2007; I. B. Schmidt & Ticktin, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Oliveira, Cruz, Sousa, Moreira, & Tanaka, 2014; I. B. Schmidt, Figueiredo, & Scariot, 2007; I. B. Schmidt & Ticktin, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32091,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4R3AVFFB"],"itemData":{"id":32091,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Brazilian Journal of Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s40415-014-0049-2","ISSN":"0100-8404","issue":"2","page":"95-103","title":"Implications of the harvest time on Syngonanthus nitens (Bong.) Ruhland (Eriocaulaceae) management in the state of Minas Gerais","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Oliveira","given":"Maria Neudes Sousa","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Cruz","given":"Sara Michelly"},{"family":"Sousa","given":"Amanda Miranda","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Moreira","given":"Fernanda da Conceição"},{"family":"Tanaka","given":"Mário Kiichiro"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":32031,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F7SK9RMC"],"itemData":{"id":32031,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"Doi 10.1663/0013-0001(2007)61[73:Eaeoho]2.0.Co;2","ISSN":"0013-0001","issue":"1","language":"English","page":"73-85","title":"Ethnobotany and effects of harvesting on the population ecology of Syngonanthus nitens (Bong.) Ruhland (Eriocaulaceae), a NTFP from Jalapao Region, Central Brazil","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Schmidt","given":"I. B."},{"family":"Figueiredo","given":"I. B."},{"family":"Scariot","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]],"season":"Spr"}}},{"id":32032,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ACWPIFU2"],"itemData":{"id":32032,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.03.018","ISSN":"0006-3207","language":"English","page":"187-195","title":"When lessons from population models and local ecological knowledge coincide - Effects of flower stalk harvesting in the Brazilian savanna","volume":"152","author":[{"family":"Schmidt","given":"I. B."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      There are several types of wild plants in the United States of America called Sweetgrass, that can be used to make handcrafts. Hierochloe odorata is native to Northern North America and is commonly used as incense and fragrance by Native Americans. It is used traditionally to craft or decorate baskets and bowls ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a4r7uh7uj1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Leif, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Leif, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32045,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5E37H89B"],"itemData":{"id":32045,"type":"report","event-place":"Rose Lake Plant Materials Center, East Lansing, MI 48823","publisher":"USDA-Natural Resources Conservation Service","publisher-place":"Rose Lake Plant Materials Center, East Lansing, MI 48823","title":"Plant fact sheet for sweetgrass [Hierochloe odorata (L.) P. Beauv","author":[{"family":"Leif","given":"J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In South Carolina, gulfhairawn muhly (Muhlenbergia filipes) is also called Sweetgrass. Its leaves are gathered by the Gullah community, descendants of enslaved Africans, to make a form of coiled basketry. The Gullah basket is now recognized as an artform and a major source of income for the local people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a4vco1up1s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(USDA & NRCS, 2009)","plainCitation":"(USDA & NRCS, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32043,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DD5RR2TJ"],"itemData":{"id":32043,"type":"report","event-place":"National Plant Data Center, Baton Rouge, LA.","publisher-place":"National Plant Data Center, Baton Rouge, LA.","title":"The PLANTS Database for gulfhairawn muhly (Muhlenbergia filipes)","URL":"http://plants.usda.gov/","author":[{"family":"USDA","given":""},{"family":"NRCS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This native coastal grass on which the basket makers depend has become increasingly scarce due to urbanization and limited access to the resource. Basket makers have to develop social-economical strategies, such as purchasing raw materials from other states, or negotiating access to the grass to maintain the traditional artform and their livelihood ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"anc8h6dl2f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grabbatin, Hurley, & Halfacre, 2011; Hurley, 2013; USDA & NRCS, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Grabbatin, Hurley, & Halfacre, 2011; Hurley, 2013; USDA & NRCS, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33679,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CW8A5AHJ"],"itemData":{"id":33679,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Scholars working around the world have drawn attention to the physical and social changes associated with rural gentri?cation. Case studies from the United States have focused on how these patterns lead to the cultural displacement and replacement of land-based livelihoods, including non-timber forest product (NTFP) practices. Scholars have also documented the persistence of culturally and economically important NTFP practices in urban and suburban areas. We reconcile these disparate outcomes, displacement on the one hand and persistence on the other, by focusing on the social relationships that co-produce land use and livelihood change. Our case investigates how African American sweetgrass basketmakers in Mount Pleasant South Carolina negotiate the complex terrain of a rapidly urbanizing and gentrifying landscape.","container-title":"Geoforum","DOI":"10.1016/j.geoforum.2011.06.007","ISSN":"00167185","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Geoforum","language":"en","page":"638-649","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"“I Still Have the Old Tradition”: The co-production of sweetgrass basketry and coastal development","title-short":"“I Still Have the Old Tradition”","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0016718511001096","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Grabbatin","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T."},{"family":"Halfacre","given":"Angela"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",11]]}}},{"id":963,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I3VX5L8T"],"itemData":{"id":963,"type":"chapter","container-title":"African Ethnobotany in the Americas","event-place":"New York, NY","ISBN":"978-1-4614-0835-2","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-1-4614-0836-9_6","page":"153-173","publisher":"Springer New York","publisher-place":"New York, NY","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Gathering, Buying, and Growing Sweetgrass (Muhlenbergia sericea): Urbanization and Social Networking in the Sweetgrass Basket-Making Industry of Lowcountry South Carolina","title-short":"Gathering, Buying, and Growing Sweetgrass (Muhlenbergia sericea)","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-1-4614-0836-9_6","editor":[{"family":"Voeks","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Rashford","given":"John"}],"author":[{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T."},{"family":"Grabbatin","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Goetcheus","given":"Cari"},{"family":"Halfacre","given":"Angela"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":32043,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DD5RR2TJ"],"itemData":{"id":32043,"type":"report","event-place":"National Plant Data Center, Baton Rouge, LA.","publisher-place":"National Plant Data Center, Baton Rouge, LA.","title":"The PLANTS Database for gulfhairawn muhly (Muhlenbergia filipes)","URL":"http://plants.usda.gov/","author":[{"family":"USDA","given":""},{"family":"NRCS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Many species of wild fungi are harvested for craft purposes. Turkey tail mushrooms (Trametes versicolor) grow throughout North American forests, and also across Europe and Asia. Turkey tail is a very colorful bracket fungus that grows throughout the year on dead or rotting wood. Pieces of the fruiting body are often harvested for use by artists and jewelry makers, who most commonly use them in earrings and necklaces ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aa3t3tkfmd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Spahr, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Spahr, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VRPEE2JE"],"itemData":{"id":32102,"type":"book","event-place":"Berkeley, CA USA","ISBN":"978-1-55643-795-3","publisher":"North Atlantic Books","publisher-place":"Berkeley, CA USA","title":"Edible and medicinal mushrooms of New England and Eastern Canada: A photographic Guidebook to Finding and Using Key Species","URL":"https://www.google.com/search?q=turkey+tail+jewelry&rlz=1C5CHFA_enNO863NO864&oq=turkey+tail+jewelry&aqs=chrome..69i57.5967j0j7&sourceid=chrome&ie=UTF-8","author":[{"family":"Spahr","given":"David L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ganoderma applanatum (commonly known as the artist’s conk) is also a bracket fungus with a cosmopolitan distribution. It is sometimes used as a medicinal tea, but it is most commonly known in North America for its use as an artist’s canvas of sorts, where burning or carving into the underflesh of a dried polypore leaves behind brown markings to create images ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aft93t6ll3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wetzel, Duchesne, & Laporte, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Wetzel, Duchesne, & Laporte, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32103,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W5DMS5GI"],"itemData":{"id":32103,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Bioproducts from canada's forests: New Partnerships in the Bioeconomy","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"147–162","publisher":"Springer","title":"Decorative and Aesthetic Products","author":[{"family":"Wetzel","given":"Suzanne"},{"family":"Duchesne","given":"Luc C"},{"family":"Laporte","given":"Michael F"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In this case, while some mycelia live on in the dying or decaying wood medium, polypores take so long to grow that when the fruiting body is harvested, functionally almost the entire the organism is harvested. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Kmsk1rBv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Emery, 2006)","plainCitation":"(M. Emery, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":982,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DQ5PEQ2U"],"itemData":{"id":982,"type":"report","event-place":"Edinburgh","genre":"government report","language":"English","page":"i-viii + 1-40","publisher":"Forestry Commission","publisher-place":"Edinburgh","title":"Wild harvests from Scottish Woodlands: social, cultural, and economic values of contemporary non-timber forest products","author":[{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla"},{"family":"Martin","given":"Suzanne"},{"family":"Dyke","given":"Alison"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} 
      The long-term sustainability of wild mushroom, wild fungi and wild lichen gathering varies depending on several factors. First, how much of the organism is harvested is paramount. In most cases, it is actually only the fruiting body that is taken, leaving the mycelium behind in its substrate. However, if the fruiting body is harvested before the spores are released the reproductive potential is essentially removed. Despite variation across species and regions in what is harvested and how, there is general agreement that most fungi harvested for crafts purposes are harvested at sustainable levels. 
      Bark is a popular handicraft item. Otomi people in Mexico use barks of Trema micrantha and several Ficus species for handmaking paper crafts. With the color paintings by the Nahua people, Amate bark paper has been traded nationally and internationally. Bark harvesters include indigenous and non-indigenous peoples, often of low-income. From the 1980s to 1995, the bark supply increased dramatically from only 4 main harvesters to around 200 people in an area of 1500 km2. As the main source and preferred species of bark paper, Trema micrantha are fast growing, occur within all vegetation and can be harvested throughout the year. The species is recommended for amelioration of degraded lands. It is planted as a shade tree in coffee plantations. When it reaches five to eight years of age it is removed as part of the management of the coffee plantation. With the expansion of the harvest area, including the above factors, this use of bark to make Amate handicrafts is considered to be growing and sustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"af4cubi9ui","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez, 2005)","plainCitation":"(López, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32057,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PWWWTPIC"],"itemData":{"id":32057,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"case studies of non-timber forest product system","container-title":"Forest Products, Livelihoods and Conservation. Case Studies of Non-Timber Forest Product Systems. Volume 3 – Latin America","edition":"Alexiades M.N., Shanley P. (ed)","event-place":"Bogor (Indonesia).","ISBN":"979-3361-24-7","page":"365-390","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor (Indonesia).","title":"Amate, Mexican bark paper: resourceful harvest strategies to meet market demands","URL":"hhttps://doi.org/10.17528/cifor/002281","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"López","given":"Citlali"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Birch bark is also harvested throughout central North America and northern Europe and used for a variety of handicrafts including baskets and ornaments. According to Emery ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a8viarnn6f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2014)","plainCitation":"(2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32108,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/INP4KW7N"],"itemData":{"id":32108,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Forestry","issue":"2","language":"English","page":"207-214","title":"Using traditional ecological knowledge as a basis for targeted forest inventories: Paper birch (Betula papyrifera) in the US Great Lakes region","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R."},{"family":"Wrobel","given":"Alexandra"},{"family":"Hansen","given":"Matthew H."},{"family":"Dockry","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Moser","given":"W. Keith"},{"family":"Stark","given":"Kekek Jason"},{"family":"Gilbert","given":"Jonathan H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, “Paper birch (Betula papyrifera) is a cultural keystone species for the Anishinaabe in the United States of America Great Lakes region” specifically because of its bark. 
      
* Perfume and incense
Aromatic plants often have medicinal values and face the same stress and sustainability problems as medicinal plants. Numerous resins are used as incense around the world, either for local use and small-scale trade or for international trade, such as frankincense (Boswellia spp.) or myrrh (Commiphora spp.). Frankincense and myrrh products also have wide ranges of other industrial uses such as for food and beverages, and are used as traditional medicines in China. The first two quality grades of final products are sold in international markets and the least quality graded items are for domestic use like in churches, coffee ceremonies, etc. Tapping and gathering of frankincense is carried out around the dry season. It follows a specific pattern including shaving a thin layer of the bark, the moderate widening of the wound one month later, and then the gathering the gum. An average of 500 g of frankincense is obtained from each tree each season after three to four months of continued tapping ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2deqs1mu37","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(W. Tadesse, Desalegn, & Alia, 2007)","plainCitation":"(W. Tadesse, Desalegn, & Alia, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32065,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/89QRICMN"],"itemData":{"id":32065,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Investigación Agraria: Sistemas y Recursos Forestales","DOI":"10.5424/srf/2007163-01010","ISSN":"2340-3578, 1131-7965","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Invest. Agrar.: Sist. Recur. For.","page":"211","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Natural gum and resin bearing species of Ethiopia and their potential applications","URL":"http://revistas.inia.es/index.php/fs/article/view/1010","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Tadesse","given":"W."},{"family":"Desalegn","given":"G."},{"family":"Alia","given":"R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Total world export demand is estimated at around 2500-10,000 tons/year with much uncertainty, since the European Union and the United States of America have a broader classification of natural gums and resins in the harmonized system code. The principal exporters are Ethiopia, Kenya, Somalia and Eritrea ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2jtt4r046h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Coppen, 2020b; Wubalem Tadesse, Dejene, Zeleke, & Desalegn, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Coppen, 2020b; Wubalem Tadesse, Dejene, Zeleke, & Desalegn, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30984,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BJZEG35V"],"itemData":{"id":30984,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The drylands of Ethiopia are well known for their natural gum and resin producing tree and shrub species such as Acacia, Boswellia, Commiphora and Sterculia. The production and trade volumes of gums and resins in the country showed a declining trend since 2010. The present review work is focused on availability of alternative underutilized tree and shrub species to indicate the diverse gum and resin market opportunities. Several Sub-Saharan African countries are producing gum and resin products from diversified species. Gum arabic is collected from Acacia senegal (L) Willd, Acacia seyal DEL, and Acacia polyacantha Willd species. Three countries namely Sudan, Nigeria, Chad contribute about 97% to the international market, while Ethiopia's contribution is 0.9%. World demand for karaya gum from Sterculia setigera DEL is about 7,000 tonne and in Africa, Senegal is the leading exporter. Despite the huge resources of A. senegal, A. seyal and A. polyacantha, Ethiopia producing very low quantity, and gum is collected from natural oozes of trunks or branches. S. setigera is also found in the country, although gum karaya is not yet under production. South and south eastern parts of Ethiopia hosts abundant species of Acacia, Boswellia and Commiphora. Gum-resin products are collected from natural exudates by herdsmen, women and children while herding and doing other activities, indicating its adverse effects on quality and quantity. Very small proportions of Myrrh and gum opopanax enter the local market. Other constraints are, lack of appropriate institutions, infrastructure, tapping technologies and market information. Therefore, appropriate policy formulation, research and development interventions, are recommended for supporting sustainable management, production and marketing of products.","container-title":"World Journal of Agricultural Research","DOI":"10.12691/wjar-8-2-2","issue":"2","page":"32-38","source":"www.sciagriculturalresearch.com","title":"Underutilized Natural Gum and Resin Resources in Ethiopia for Future Directions and Commercial Utilization","URL":"http://www.sciagriculturalresearch.com/articles/8/2/2","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Tadesse","given":"Wubalem"},{"family":"Dejene","given":"Tatek"},{"family":"Zeleke","given":"Gizachew"},{"family":"Desalegn","given":"Getachew"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,15]]}}},{"id":32078,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AEPB87SJ"],"itemData":{"id":32078,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Production and Marketing of Gum Resins 2","publisher":"The Network for Natural Gums & Resins in Africa","title":"Overview of International Trade and Markets","URL":"https://ngara.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/Production-and-Marketing-of-Gum-Resins_2.pdf","author":[{"family":"Coppen","given":"J. J. W."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Boswellia papyrifera which is the main source (70% of the Ethiopia's natural gum and resins production) is declining at alarming rates, due to expansion of agricultural lands, overgrazing, population increase, growing demand for construction and fuel wood, forest fires, and pests and diseases. Recent increases in demand of frankincense have also led overharvesting. The lack of traceability in the supply chain and the ineffectiveness of organic certification also affects populations of substitute frankincense species. Studies suggested cultivation and substitution to mitigate the impact and sustain this historical activity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aie2c0toeq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brendler, Brinckmann, & Schippmann, 2018; S. Johnson, 2019; Wubalem Tadesse, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Brendler, Brinckmann, & Schippmann, 2018; S. Johnson, 2019; Wubalem Tadesse, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32097,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KJ54W6SS"],"itemData":{"id":32097,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2018.07.017","ISSN":"0378-8741","issue":"DITSL GmbH, Bonn 2011","page":"279–286","title":"Sustainable supply, a foundation for natural product development: The case of Indian frankincense (Boswellia serrata Roxb. ex Colebr.).","volume":"225","author":[{"family":"Brendler","given":"T."},{"family":"Brinckmann","given":"J. A."},{"family":"Schippmann","given":"U."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":905,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/53LJRFRJ"],"itemData":{"id":905,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Plants","DOI":"10.3390/plants8040088","ISSN":"2223-7747","issue":"4","page":"88","title":"Organic Certification is Not Enough: The Case of the Methoxydecane Frankincense","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Johnson","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"DeCarlo","given":"Anjanette"},{"family":"Satyal","given":"Prabodh"},{"family":"Dosoky","given":"Noura S."},{"family":"Sorensen","given":"Aaron"},{"family":"Setzer","given":"William N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":30984,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BJZEG35V"],"itemData":{"id":30984,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The drylands of Ethiopia are well known for their natural gum and resin producing tree and shrub species such as Acacia, Boswellia, Commiphora and Sterculia. The production and trade volumes of gums and resins in the country showed a declining trend since 2010. The present review work is focused on availability of alternative underutilized tree and shrub species to indicate the diverse gum and resin market opportunities. Several Sub-Saharan African countries are producing gum and resin products from diversified species. Gum arabic is collected from Acacia senegal (L) Willd, Acacia seyal DEL, and Acacia polyacantha Willd species. Three countries namely Sudan, Nigeria, Chad contribute about 97% to the international market, while Ethiopia's contribution is 0.9%. World demand for karaya gum from Sterculia setigera DEL is about 7,000 tonne and in Africa, Senegal is the leading exporter. Despite the huge resources of A. senegal, A. seyal and A. polyacantha, Ethiopia producing very low quantity, and gum is collected from natural oozes of trunks or branches. S. setigera is also found in the country, although gum karaya is not yet under production. South and south eastern parts of Ethiopia hosts abundant species of Acacia, Boswellia and Commiphora. Gum-resin products are collected from natural exudates by herdsmen, women and children while herding and doing other activities, indicating its adverse effects on quality and quantity. Very small proportions of Myrrh and gum opopanax enter the local market. Other constraints are, lack of appropriate institutions, infrastructure, tapping technologies and market information. Therefore, appropriate policy formulation, research and development interventions, are recommended for supporting sustainable management, production and marketing of products.","container-title":"World Journal of Agricultural Research","DOI":"10.12691/wjar-8-2-2","issue":"2","page":"32-38","source":"www.sciagriculturalresearch.com","title":"Underutilized Natural Gum and Resin Resources in Ethiopia for Future Directions and Commercial Utilization","URL":"http://www.sciagriculturalresearch.com/articles/8/2/2","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Tadesse","given":"Wubalem"},{"family":"Dejene","given":"Tatek"},{"family":"Zeleke","given":"Gizachew"},{"family":"Desalegn","given":"Getachew"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1sjndv29hh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Coppen, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Coppen, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32060,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IHG42GDH"],"itemData":{"id":32060,"type":"report","collection-title":"Production and Marketing of Gum Resins_2","publisher":"The Network for Natural Gums and Resins in Africa (NGARA).","title":"Chapter 1.0 Overview of International Trade and Markets.","author":[{"family":"Coppen","given":"J.J.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"au54eblho4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Jensen & Meilby, 2008; Anders Jensen & Meilby, 2010; Paoli, Peart, Leighton, & Samsoedin, 2001; Soehartono & Newton, 2001)","plainCitation":"(A. Jensen & Meilby, 2008; Anders Jensen & Meilby, 2010; Paoli, Peart, Leighton, & Samsoedin, 2001; Soehartono & Newton, 2001)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32029,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YRE3T52A"],"itemData":{"id":32029,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605308007825","ISSN":"0030-6053","issue":"2","language":"English","page":"214-221","title":"Does commercialization of a non-timber forest product reduce ecological impact? A case study of the Critically Endangered Aquilaria crassna in Lao PDR","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Jensen","given":"A."},{"family":"Meilby","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",4]]}}},{"id":33208,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GAPRK9J6"],"itemData":{"id":33208,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Returns from Harvesting a Commercial Non-timber Forest Product andParticular Characteristics of Harvesters and Their Strategies:Aquilaria crassnaand Agarwood in Lao PDR. This paper explores associations between the returns from harvesting a commercial non-timber forest product and particular characteristics of harvesters and their strategies. Data originate from a survey in Laos of the harvest and trade of agarwood, a highly valuable commodity harvested mainly from tree species in the genus Aquilaria. Five groups of harvesters were identified, with considerable differences in returns between them. Foreign harvesters, i.e., ethnic Vietnamese, earned approximately USD 13 per day on average, while nonlocal full-time harvesters earned approximately USD 9, and nonlocal part-time harvesters USD 7 per day. Local people earn approximately USD 5 per day and only USD 4 if harvesting only took place in village forests. Factors observed to determine returns are market access, especially access to national and international markets, and knowledge and experience in trading, harvesting, and the organization of harvesting trips. It is suggested that interventions should be made to address these factors, to level out returns from harvesting, and to increase the share of returns captured by local people.","archive":"Readcube","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-010-9108-1","ISSN":"0013-0001","issue":"1","page":"34-45","title":"Returns from Harvesting a Commercial Non-timber Forest Product and Particular Characteristics of Harvesters and Their Strategies: Aquilaria crassna and Agarwood in Lao PDR1","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/s12231-010-9108-1","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Jensen","given":"Anders"},{"family":"Meilby","given":"Henrik"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32095,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RRGZPHM3"],"itemData":{"id":32095,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1046/j.1523-1739.2001.98586.x","ISSN":"1523-1739","issue":"6","page":"1721–1732","title":"An Ecological and Economic Assessment of the Nontimber Forest Product Gaharu Wood in Gunung Palung National Park, West Kalimantan, Indonesia","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Paoli","given":"Gary D."},{"family":"Peart","given":"David R."},{"family":"Leighton","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Samsoedin","given":"Ismayadi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":971,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WXDWUGM6"],"itemData":{"id":971,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/S0006-3207(00)00089-6","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"1","page":"29-41","title":"Conservation and sustainable use of tropical trees in the genus Aquilaria II. The impact of gaharu harvesting in Indonesia","volume":"97","author":[{"family":"Soehartono","given":"Tonny"},{"family":"Newton","given":"Adrian C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}The Spikenard, also called Jatamansi, is made from the rhizomes of Nardostachys jatamansi distributed in the Qinhai-tibet Plateau and Himalayas in Asia. It is vulnerable to harvesting and on the verge of extinction due to overexploitation and habitat destruction in some areas. It was evaluated as critically endangered in India but is common in Himalayas of China and Nepal. Sustainability of harvest is related to the harvesting practices. The sensitivity is higher in outcrop than in meadow habitats. Positive effects are possible with low harvesting levels under strict management conditions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2pi0scc8gb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Ghimire, Gimenez, Pradel, McKey, & Aumeeruddy\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Thomas, 2008; S. Ghimire, 2005; Kamini & Raina, 2013; Larsen, 2005)","plainCitation":"(S. Ghimire, Gimenez, Pradel, McKey, & Aumeeruddy?Thomas, 2008; S. Ghimire, 2005; Kamini & Raina, 2013; Larsen, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32093,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BEWIM2BV"],"itemData":{"id":32093,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2664.2007.01375.x","ISSN":"1365-2664","issue":"1","page":"41-51","title":"Demographic variation and population viability in a threatened Himalayan medicinal and aromatic herb Nardostachys grandiflora: matrix modelling of harvesting effects in two contrasting habitats","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Ghimire","given":"Suresh"},{"family":"Gimenez","given":"Olivier"},{"family":"Pradel","given":"Roger"},{"family":"McKey","given":"Doyle"},{"family":"Aumeeruddy?Thomas","given":"Yildiz"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":31000,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M4Y29NQT"],"itemData":{"id":31000,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2005.02.005","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"4","page":"463-475","title":"Conservation of Himalayan medicinal plants: Harvesting patterns and ecology of two threatened species, Nardostachys grandiflora DC. and Neopicrorhiza scrophulariiflora (Pennell) Hong","volume":"124","author":[{"family":"Ghimire","given":"Suresh"},{"family":"McKey","given":"Doyle"},{"family":"Aumeeruddy-Thomas","given":"Yildiz"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32089,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/94WS3JV3"],"itemData":{"id":32089,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Products Journal","DOI":"10.13073/FPJ-D-12-00092","ISSN":"0015-7473","issue":"1","page":"67-71","title":"Review of Nardostachys grandiflora: An Important Endangered Medicinal and Aromatic Plant of Western Himalaya","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Kamini","given":""},{"family":"Raina","given":"Ravinder"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":32088,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U53SSQ39"],"itemData":{"id":32088,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1663/0013-0001(2005)059[0213:IORORO]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0013-0001","issue":"3","page":"213-220","title":"Impact of replanting on regeneration of the medicinal plantNardostachys grandiflora DC. (Valerianaceae)","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Larsen","given":"Helle"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
3.3.2.3.3. Energy

As renewable sources of bioenergy, wild plants and fungi have a huge contribution to make to reducing both carbon emissions and energy poverty. Many African countries have high proportions of fuel species. In East Africa, the indigenous tree species Croton megalocarpus supports a sustainable seed oil industry that provides biofuel for electricity. One microenterprise, EcoFuels Kenya, sources more than 3,000 tonnes of wild-harvested nuts each year. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ajdnofgr9o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Antonelli, 2020; Grace, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Antonelli, 2020; Grace, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30901,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G9883U5U"],"itemData":{"id":30901,"type":"report","note":"DOI: 10.34885/172","publisher":"Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"State of the World's Plants and Fungi 2020","URL":"https://kew.iro.bl.uk/work/23394899-864d-4ef0-b864-857efea500c5","author":[{"family":"Antonelli","given":"A."},{"family":"Fry","given":"C."},{"family":"Smith","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Simmonds","given":"M.S.J."},{"family":"Kersey","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Pritchard","given":"H.W."},{"family":"Abbo","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Acedo","given":"C."},{"family":"Adams","given":"J."},{"family":"Ainsworth","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Allkin","given":"B."},{"family":"Annecke","given":"W."},{"family":"Bachman","given":"S.P."},{"family":"Bacon","given":"K."},{"family":"Bárrios","given":"S."},{"family":"Barstow","given":"C."},{"family":"Battison","given":"A."},{"family":"Bell","given":"E."},{"family":"Bensusan","given":"K."},{"family":"Bidartondo","given":"M.I."},{"family":"Blackhall-Miles","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Borrell","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Brearley","given":"F.Q."},{"family":"Breman","given":"E."},{"family":"Brewer","given":"R.F.A."},{"family":"Brodie","given":"J."},{"family":"Cámara-Leret","given":"R."},{"family":"Campostrini Forzza","given":"R."},