	Switchgrass (Panicum virgatum) is native to North and Central America, can grow in many different soils, has low fertilizer requirements and can in some cases promote biodiversity depending on the land use being displaced ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ad34e9hcrt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cheng & Timilsina, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Cheng & Timilsina, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32081,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IFIQNDVQ"],"itemData":{"id":32081,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Renewable Energy","DOI":"10.1016/j.renene.2011.04.031","ISSN":"09601481","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Renewable Energy","language":"en","page":"3541-3549","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Status and barriers of advanced biofuel technologies: A review","title-short":"Status and barriers of advanced biofuel technologies","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0960148111002035","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Cheng","given":"Jay J."},{"family":"Timilsina","given":"Govinda R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It can be used as a biofuel source and has potential economic benefits especially in the United States of America. Despite this potential, the environmental consequences of converting to crop grasslands and large land use needs must be addressed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2b6ss4gsop","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barney & DiTomaso, 2010; R. A. Brown, Rosenberg, Hays, Easterling, & Mearns, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Barney & DiTomaso, 2010; R. A. Brown, Rosenberg, Hays, Easterling, & Mearns, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32086,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6KGIYH97"],"itemData":{"id":32086,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biomass and Bioenergy","DOI":"10.1016/j.biombioe.2009.10.009","ISSN":"09619534","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Biomass and Bioenergy","language":"en","page":"124-133","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Bioclimatic predictions of habitat suitability for the biofuel switchgrass in North America under current and future climate scenarios","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0961953409002128","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Barney","given":"Jacob N."},{"family":"DiTomaso","given":"Joseph M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",1]]}}},{"id":32105,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2D8H8FCG"],"itemData":{"id":32105,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agriculture, Ecosystems & Environment","DOI":"10.1016/S0167-8809(99)00115-2","ISSN":"01678809","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Agriculture, Ecosystems & Environment","language":"en","page":"31-47","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Potential production and environmental effects of switchgrass and traditional crops under current and greenhouse-altered climate in the central United States: a simulation study","title-short":"Potential production and environmental effects of switchgrass and traditional crops under current and greenhouse-altered climate in the central United States","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0167880999001152","volume":"78","author":[{"family":"Brown","given":"R.A"},{"family":"Rosenberg","given":"N.J"},{"family":"Hays","given":"C.J"},{"family":"Easterling","given":"W.E"},{"family":"Mearns","given":"L.O"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Switchgrass has been shown to have the potential to decrease soil erosion rates 30 times during the first year of growth, and up to 600 times during the second and third years when the root system has been established ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ad540b5g3c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McLaughlin, 2002; P. R. D. Williams, Inman, Aden, & Heath, 2009)","plainCitation":"(McLaughlin, 2002; P. R. D. Williams, Inman, Aden, & Heath, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32104,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VY38BS9V"],"itemData":{"id":32104,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science & Technology","DOI":"10.1021/es010963d","ISSN":"0013-936X, 1520-5851","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Environ. Sci. Technol.","language":"en","page":"2122-2129","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"High-Value Renewable Energy from Prairie Grasses","URL":"https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/es010963d","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"McLaughlin","given":"S. B."},{"family":"Torre Ugarte","given":"D. G.","non-dropping-particle":"de la"},{"family":"Garten,","given":"C. T."},{"family":"Lynd","given":"L. R."},{"family":"Sanderson","given":"M. A."},{"family":"Tolbert","given":"V. R."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"D. D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002",5]]}}},{"id":32085,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AVEDYREK"],"itemData":{"id":32085,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science & Technology","DOI":"10.1021/es900250d","ISSN":"0013-936X, 1520-5851","issue":"13","journalAbbreviation":"Environ. Sci. Technol.","language":"en","page":"4763-4775","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Environmental and Sustainability Factors Associated With Next-Generation Biofuels in the U.S.: What Do We Really Know?","title-short":"Environmental and Sustainability Factors Associated With Next-Generation Biofuels in the U.S.","URL":"https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/es900250d","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Williams","given":"Pamela R. D."},{"family":"Inman","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Aden","given":"Andy"},{"family":"Heath","given":"Garvin A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Werling ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1cui48rain","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2014)","plainCitation":"(2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J7XES8T5"],"itemData":{"id":32107,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1309492111","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","language":"en","page":"1652-1657","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Perennial grasslands enhance biodiversity and multiple ecosystem services in bioenergy landscapes","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/doi/10.1073/pnas.1309492111","volume":"111","author":[{"family":"Werling","given":"B. P."},{"family":"Dickson","given":"T. L."},{"family":"Isaacs","given":"R."},{"family":"Gaines","given":"H."},{"family":"Gratton","given":"C."},{"family":"Gross","given":"K. L."},{"family":"Liere","given":"H."},{"family":"Malmstrom","given":"C. M."},{"family":"Meehan","given":"T. D."},{"family":"Ruan","given":"L."},{"family":"Robertson","given":"B. A."},{"family":"Robertson","given":"G. P."},{"family":"Schmidt","given":"T. M."},{"family":"Schrotenboer","given":"A. C."},{"family":"Teal","given":"T. K."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"J. K."},{"family":"Landis","given":"D. A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",1,28]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found that perennial grasslands that contained switchgrass and prairie plantings have significantly higher biodiversity than maize lands, as arthropods, grassland birds, soil-living methanotrophic bacteria and pollination-insects were found, among others.
	Two other interesting wild plant species are Miscanthus spp., which is native to Southeast Asia, and Bermudagrass (Cynodon dactylon), native along the United States of America coast. All three grass species are very interesting as biofuel plants, as they grow in the wild but can also be cultivated ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"d7LPzxSz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cheng & Timilsina, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Cheng & Timilsina, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32081,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IFIQNDVQ"],"itemData":{"id":32081,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Renewable Energy","DOI":"10.1016/j.renene.2011.04.031","ISSN":"09601481","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Renewable Energy","language":"en","page":"3541-3549","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Status and barriers of advanced biofuel technologies: A review","title-short":"Status and barriers of advanced biofuel technologies","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0960148111002035","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Cheng","given":"Jay J."},{"family":"Timilsina","given":"Govinda R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The grass genus Miscanthus is among the first crops for which bilateral agreements have been developed under the Convention on Biological Diversity to guide breeding of new varieties from wild germplasm collections from Asia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bP0FSbew","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Antonelli, 2020; Grace, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Antonelli, 2020; Grace, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30901,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G9883U5U"],"itemData":{"id":30901,"type":"report","note":"DOI: 10.34885/172","publisher":"Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"State of the World's Plants and Fungi 2020","URL":"https://kew.