ily":"Smith","given":"D."},{"family":"Smith","given":"P."},{"family":"Smith","given":"S.R."},{"family":"Sofo","given":"A."},{"family":"Spence","given":"N."},{"family":"Stanworth","given":"A."},{"family":"Stara","given":"K."},{"family":"Stevenson","given":"P.C."},{"family":"Stroh","given":"P."},{"family":"Suz","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Tambam","given":"B.B."},{"family":"Tatsis","given":"E.C."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"I."},{"family":"Thiers","given":"B."},{"family":"Thormann","given":"I."},{"family":"Vaglica","given":"V."},{"family":"Vásquez-Londoño","given":"C."},{"family":"Victor","given":"J."},{"family":"Viruel","given":"J."},{"family":"Walker","given":"B.E."},{"family":"Walker","given":"K."},{"family":"Walsh","given":"A."},{"family":"Way","given":"M."},{"family":"Wilbraham","given":"J."},{"family":"Wilkin","given":"P."},{"family":"Wilkinson","given":"T."},{"family":"Williams","given":"C."},{"family":"Winterton","given":"D."},{"family":"Wong","given":"K.M."},{"family":"Woodfield-Pascoe","given":"N."},{"family":"Woodman","given":"J."},{"family":"Wyatt","given":"L."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"R."},{"family":"Zhang","given":"B.G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}}},{"id":906,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/48WC3ZWP"],"itemData":{"id":906,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Bioenergy is a major component of the global transition to renewable energy technologies. The plant and fungal kingdoms offer great potential but remain mostly untapped. Their increased use could contribute to the renewable energy transition and addressing the United Nations Sustainable Development Goal 7 “Ensure access to affordable, reliable, sustainable and modern energy for all.” Current research focuses on species cultivated at scale in temperate regions, overlooking the wealth of potential new sources of small-scale energy where they are most urgently needed. A shift towards diversified, accessible bioenergy technologies will help to mitigate and adapt to the threats of climate change, decrease energy poverty, improve human health by reducing indoor pollution, increase energy resilience of communities, and decrease greenhouse gas emissions from fossil fuels.","container-title":"PLANTS, PEOPLE, PLANET","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ppp3.10147","issue":"5","page":"446-462","title":"Plant Power: Opportunities and challenges for meeting sustainable energy needs from the plant and fungal kingdoms","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Grace","given":"Olwen M."},{"family":"Lovett","given":"Jon C."},{"family":"Gore","given":"Charles J. N."},{"family":"Moat","given":"Justin"},{"family":"Ondo","given":"Ian"},{"family":"Pironon","given":"Samuel"},{"family":"Langat","given":"Moses K."},{"family":"Pérez-Escobar","given":"Oscar A."},{"family":"Ross","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Suzan Abbo","given":"Mary"},{"family":"Shrestha","given":"Krishna K."},{"family":"Gowda","given":"Balakrishna"},{"family":"Farrar","given":"Kerrie"},{"family":"Adams","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Cámara-Leret","given":"Rodrigo"},{"family":"Diazgranados","given":"Mauricio"},{"family":"Ulian","given":"Tiziana"},{"family":"Sagala","given":"Saut"},{"family":"Rianawati","given":"Elisabeth"},{"family":"Hazra","given":"Amit"},{"family":"Masera","given":"Omar R."},{"family":"Antonelli","given":"Alexandre"},{"family":"Wilkin","given":"Paul"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Certain natural grasslands are found in some climate zones and it may be beneficial for future biofuel production to come from grassland as the root system in the soil can prevent erosion.
	Jatropha is a group of non-edible plants found mostly in America that includes 66 species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a16km6ip1v2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dehgan, 1984; Goel, Makkar, Francis, & Becker, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Dehgan, 1984; Goel, Makkar, Francis, & Becker, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32084,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WBA8TGEI"],"itemData":{"id":32084,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Systematic Botany","DOI":"10.2307/2418796","ISSN":"03636445","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Systematic Botany","page":"467","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Phylogenetic Significance of Interspecific Hybridization in Jatropha (Euphorbiaceae)","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/2418796?origin=crossref","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Dehgan","given":"Bijan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1984",10]]}}},{"id":32083,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9IEW69L3"],"itemData":{"id":32083,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Phorbol esters are the tetracyclic diterpenoids generally known for their tumor promoting activity. The phorbol esters mimic the action of diacyl glycerol (DAG), activator of protein kinase C, which regulates different signal transduction pathways and other cellular metabolic activities. They occur naturally in many plants of the family Euphorbiacaeae and Thymelaeaceae. The biological activities of the phorbol esters are highly structure specific. The phorbol esters, even at very low concentrations, show toxicological manifestations in animals fed diets containing them. This toxicity limits the use of many nutritive plants and agricultural by-products containing phorbol esters to be used as animal feed. Therefore, various chemical and physical treatments have been evaluated to extract or inactivate phorbol esters so that seed meals rich in proteins could be used as feed resources. However, not much progress has been reported so far. The detoxifying ability has also been reported in some molluscs and in liver homogenate of mice. Besides, possessing antinutritional and toxic effects, few derivatives of the phorbol esters are also known for their antimicrobial and antitumor activities. The molluscicidal and insecticidal properties of phorbol esters indicate its potential to be used as an effective biopesticide and insecticide.","container-title":"International Journal of Toxicology","DOI":"10.1080/10915810701464641","ISSN":"1091-5818, 1092-874X","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Int J Toxicol","language":"en","page":"279-288","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Phorbol Esters: Structure, Biological Activity, and Toxicity in Animals","title-short":"Phorbol Esters","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1080/10915810701464641","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Goel","given":"Gunjan"},{"family":"Makkar","given":"Harinder P. S."},{"family":"Francis","given":"George"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Klaus"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The most common species, Jatropha curcas, is a multipurpose plant species useful to control soil erosion, improve soil infiltration, reclaim wasteland and phytoremediation of contaminated soil, and prepare green manure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2mblorc7vn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Subedi, Chaudhary, Kunwar, Bussmann, & Oaniagua-Zambrana, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Subedi, Chaudhary, Kunwar, Bussmann, & Oaniagua-Zambrana, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32042,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GWD9CV52"],"itemData":{"id":32042,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Ethnobotany of Mountain Regions","container-title":"Ethnobotany of the Himalayas","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-030-57407-9","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-030-57408-6","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Jatropha curcas L. (Euphorbiaceae)","URL":"https://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-030-57408-6","editor":[{"family":"Kunwar","given":"Ripu M."},{"family":"Sher","given":"Hassan"},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"Rainer W."}],"author":[{"family":"Subedi","given":"C. K."},{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"R. P."},{"family":"Kunwar","given":"Ripu M."},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"W"},{"family":"Oaniagua-Zambrana","given":"N. Y."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The species has a high core nonvolatile oil content, between 25 and 35% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ai9q07aiko","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. G. D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az, 2017; R. S. Kumar, Parthiban, Hemalatha, Kalaiselvi, & Rao, 2009)","plainCitation":"(B. G. Díaz, 2017; R. S. Kumar, Parthiban, Hemalatha, Kalaiselvi, & Rao, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":901,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J9G67VQN"],"itemData":{"id":901,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Genetics and Molecular Research","DOI":"10.4238/gmr16029683","ISSN":"16765680","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Genet. Mol. Res.","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"High genetic diversity of Jatropha curcas assessed by ISSR","URL":"http://www.funpecrp.com.br/gmr/year2017/vol16-2/pdf/gmr-16-02-gmr.16029683.pdf","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"B.G."},{"family":"Argollo","given":"D.M."},{"family":"Franco","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Nucci","given":"S.M."},{"family":"Siqueira","given":"W.J."},{"family":"Laat","given":"D.M.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Colombo","given":"C.A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":32077,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AVW69SRW"],"itemData":{"id":32077,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Crop Science","DOI":"10.2135/cropsci2008.10.0601","ISSN":"0011183X","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Crop Sci.","language":"en","page":"1667-1674","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Investigation on Cross-Compatibility Barriers in the Biofuel Crop <i>Jatropha curcas</i> L. with Wild <i>Jatropha</i> Species","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.2135/cropsci2008.10.0601","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Kumar","given":"R. Senthil"},{"family":"Parthiban","given":"K. T."},{"family":"Hemalatha","given":"P."},{"family":"Kalaiselvi","given":"T."},{"family":"Rao","given":"M. Govinda"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and is the most domesticated species of Jatropha used today. It was created through a combination of systematic selection, inter-hybridization (between J. curcas and J. integerrima) and breeding programs and has a higher oil content ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ai6miar0ss","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sujatha & Prabakaran, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Sujatha & Prabakaran, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32080,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JTM5L4Q7"],"itemData":{"id":32080,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Genetic Resources and Crop Evolution","DOI":"10.1023/A:1022961028064","ISSN":"09259864","issue":"1","page":"75-82","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"New ornamental Jatropha hybrids through  interspecific hybridization","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1023/A:1022961028064","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Sujatha","given":"M."},{"family":"Prabakaran","given":"A.J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but Jatropha is still a wild plant grown as live fence around agricultural fields ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"afdd5hsij4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Becker & Makkar, 2008; R. S. Kumar, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Becker & Makkar, 2008; R. S. Kumar, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32082,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UYBTEWAX"],"itemData":{"id":32082,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Lipid Technology","DOI":"10.1002/lite.200800023","ISSN":"0956666X, 18635377","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Lipid Technology","language":"en","page":"104-107","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Jatropha curcas: A potential source for tomorrow' s oil and biodiesel","title-short":"Jatropha curcas","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/lite.200800023","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Becker","given":"Klaus"},{"family":"Makkar","given":"H. P. S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",5]]}}},{"id":32077,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AVW69SRW"],"itemData":{"id":32077,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Crop Science","DOI":"10.2135/cropsci2008.10.0601","ISSN":"0011183X","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Crop Sci.","language":"en","page":"1667-1674","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Investigation on Cross-Compatibility Barriers in the Biofuel Crop <i>Jatropha curcas</i> L. with Wild <i>Jatropha</i> Species","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.2135/cropsci2008.10.0601","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Kumar","given":"R. Senthil"},{"family":"Parthiban","given":"K. T."},{"family":"Hemalatha","given":"P."},{"family":"Kalaiselvi","given":"T."},{"family":"Rao","given":"M. Govinda"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and is regularly used by indigenous people Subedi, 2021). The other plant with oil content—Croton megalocarpus— is native to eastern Africa and can have a seed oil content of 30-45% on a mass basis ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a188n20sp0o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aliyu, Agnew, & Douglas, 2010; Hines & Eckman, 1993)","plainCitation":"(Aliyu, Agnew, & Douglas, 2010; Hines & Eckman, 1993)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32074,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SMGCD622"],"itemData":{"id":32074,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biomass and Bioenergy","DOI":"10.1016/j.biombioe.2010.04.026","ISSN":"09619534","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Biomass and Bioenergy","language":"en","page":"1495-1499","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Croton megalocarpus (Musine) seeds as a potential source of bio-diesel","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0961953410001595","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Aliyu","given":"B."},{"family":"Agnew","given":"B."},{"family":"Douglas","given":"S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",10]]}}},{"id":32079,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5PU646VV"],"itemData":{"id":32079,"type":"book","event-place":"Ottawa","ISBN":"978-0-9697075-0-9","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 476111910","publisher":"Cultural Survival Canada","publisher-place":"Ottawa","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Indigenous multipurpose trees of Tanzania: uses and economic benefits for people","title-short":"Indigenous multipurpose trees of Tanzania","author":[{"family":"Hines","given":"Deborah A"},{"family":"Eckman","given":"Karlyn"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Another interesting wild plant rich with oil is the Beauty Leaf Tree (Calophyllum inophyllum), which can carry 10,000 fruits per tree a year and the seeds contain up to 60-70% useful oil ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2k8d2ed4q5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Friday & Okano, 2006; Jahirul, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Friday & Okano, 2006; Jahirul, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32076,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V98625WN"],"itemData":{"id":32076,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Species profiles for Pacific Island agroforestry","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Permanent Agriculture Resources Hawaii","page":"1–17","title":"Calophyllum inophyllum (kamani)","URL":"https://www.doc-developpement-durable.org/file/Fabrications-Objets-Outils-Produits/Huiles-essentielles/FICHES_PLANTES&HUILES/calophyllum%20inophyllum/Calophyllum%20inophyllum%20(Kamani).pdf","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Friday","given":"JB"},{"family":"Okano","given":"Dana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":32112,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L38SEJAX"],"itemData":{"id":32112,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Procedia Engineering","DOI":"10.1016/j.proeng.2013.03.168","ISSN":"18777058","journalAbbreviation":"Procedia Engineering","language":"en","page":"619-624","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Optimisation of Bio-Oil Extraction Process from Beauty Leaf (Calophyllum Inophyllum) Oil Seed as a Second Generation Biodiesel Source","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1877705813005225","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Jahirul","given":"M.I."},{"family":"Brown","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Senadeera","given":"W."},{"family":"Ashwath","given":"N."},{"family":"Laing","given":"C."},{"family":"Leski-Taylor","given":"J."},{"family":"Rasul","given":"M.G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The tree is native to Australia but has been introduced to Southeast Asia and India and started to use as biofuel plant at small-scale ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"acmn1hlrdm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Friday & Okano, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Friday & Okano, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32076,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V98625WN"],"itemData":{"id":32076,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Species profiles for Pacific Island agroforestry","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Permanent Agriculture Resources Hawaii","page":"1–17","title":"Calophyllum inophyllum (kamani)","URL":"https://www.doc-developpement-durable.org/file/Fabrications-Objets-Outils-Produits/Huiles-essentielles/FICHES_PLANTES&HUILES/calophyllum%20inophyllum/Calophyllum%20inophyllum%20(Kamani).pdf","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Friday","given":"JB"},{"family":"Okano","given":"Dana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Brock ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a22bpp1fo1f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2018)","plainCitation":"(2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32073,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B2NKNMLR"],"itemData":{"id":32073,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Molecular Phylogenetics and Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.ympev.2018.06.031","ISSN":"10557903","journalAbbreviation":"Molecular Phylogenetics and Evolution","language":"en","page":"834-842","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Phylogenetics of Camelina Crantz. (Brassicaceae) and insights on the origin of gold-of-pleasure (Camelina sativa)","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1055790318302446","volume":"127","author":[{"family":"Brock","given":"Jordan R."},{"family":"Dönmez","given":"Ali A."},{"family":"Beilstein","given":"Mark A."},{"family":"Olsen","given":"Kenneth M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} noted the gold-of-pleasure (Camelina sativa), which is an old-world oilseed crop that went out of use in the mid-20th century but has now gained renewed interest as a biofuel source.
	There are various studies about wild-living plants and crops and even Yang ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a292fanc0ni","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2013)","plainCitation":"(2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32075,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JBMU4GTC"],"itemData":{"id":32075,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In order to reveal the potentials of unknown and lessknown herbaceous wild plant (potential diesel plant) from saline-alkali wasteland for development biodiesel production, seed and plant samples from 33 species were collected and plant oil content, seed oil content, seed yield per plants and saline-alkali tolerance of the plants were analyzed. The result show that oil content in plants ranged from 1.31-15.01%. Euphorbia heyneana (15.01%) had the highest oil content followed by Ricinus communis (13.9%), Cirsium setosum (12.5%), Euphorbia nutans (11.02%), Cirsiu japonicum (9.27%). About fifty percent species were found have more seed oil content within the range of 21 to 48.5%. Maximum of 48.5% was observed in one wild species of Ricinus communis followed by Euphorbia esula (34.2%), Euphorbia nutans (29.3%), Xanthium sibiricum (28.5%), Euphorbia humifusa (28.1%), Euphorbia heyneana (24.1%), Capsella bursa-pastoris (24%), Suaeda glauca (23.6%), Artemisia argyi (23.5%), Lepidium apetalum (23%). Highest level of seed field per plant was observed in Ricinus communis, Glycine soja and the higher level of seed field was in Humulus scandens, Sonchus oleraceus, Gynura crepidioides, Artemisia argyi, Abutilon theophrasti, Cirsiu japonicum, Inula japonica, Comnyza canadensis. 14 species grew in moderate saline-alkali soil and only one species (Suaeda glauca) was in severe saline-alkali soil. Comprehensive analysis show that most potential herbaceous diesel plants are Ricinus communis, Euphorbia esula, Glycine soja, Gynura crepidioides, Cirsiu japonicum and Artemisia argyi, based on 4 values of oil content in plants, and seed, seed yield per plants and saline-alkali tolerance.","container-title":"Advanced Materials Research","DOI":"10.4028/www.scientific.net/AMR.641-642.578","ISSN":"1662-8985","journalAbbreviation":"AMR","page":"578-582","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Potential Species and Character of Wild Diesel Plant in Tianjin","URL":"https://www.scientific.net/AMR.641-642.578","volume":"641-642","author":[{"family":"Yang","given":"Jing Hui"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Yan Jun"},{"family":"Li","given":"Jian Ke"},{"family":"Huang","given":"Jun Xun"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Wei Yu"},{"family":"Li","given":"Shuang Yue"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",1]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} have studied possible wild plants for biofuel production and to avoid competition of using of edible plants for food industry. They studied wild plants from salt-alkali wastelands, which often occur in many arid and semi-arid regions of the world. They note that “[…] the direct competition with food production should be avoided and a much wider range of plants possible sources of biomass should be made or screened so that they are able to be grown on marginal lands. The non-edible biofuel plant species with fewer inputs, higher tolerant are required so that the diesel plants can be planted in the desert or on the saline-alkali land.” They listed several wild herbaceous plants rich in oil from stems and leaves in China: Euphorbia heyneana (15.01%), Ricinus communis (13.9%), Cirsium setosum (12.5%), Euphorbia nutans (11.02%), Cirsiu japonicum (9.27%), Metaplexis japonica (8.27%), Taraxacum officnala (7.75%), Lactuca raddeana (7.63%), Euphorbia humifusa (6.88%), Euphorbia thymifolia (6.81%), Euphorbia esula (6.57%) and Aster tataricus (5.64%). It is possible to develop a method to extract biofuel from these herbaceous plants and at the same time use the semi-alkali wasteland as possible cultivation land and avoid competition with crops for food production. 

3.3.2.3.4. Food and beverage

Food consumption is the most common form of use for gathering wild species. Foraging is the oldest productive activity of people, but it keeps being practiced, in rural as in urban environments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a168hpbj67f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Svizzero, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Svizzero, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32058,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RVS992R5"],"itemData":{"id":32058,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Although human foraging behavior, i.e., the method used to get food procurement from the wild, is the economic criterion usually used in the academic literature in order to define hunter-gatherer societies, it is restricted neither to these societies nor to this goal. It consists of the extraction of natural resources by means of various techniques, such as hunting, fishing and gathering. It is applied to a broad range of wild resources – aquatic and terrestrial, plants, animals and minerals – even though in some cases it could be limited only to some of these resources such as the non-timber forest products (NTFPs). The aim of this paper is to demonstrate that while foraging is an ubiquitous human behavior, its goals are evolving with the passage of time. More precisely these goals that exist today have been present in some form in the past, only their importance and emphasis has changed over time and with the historical, sociological and ecological contexts. While subsistence seems naturally the most obvious motivation of human foraging behavior, the latter also occurs in various contexts such as in mixed economies. Moreover, other goals – different from the biological one – also exist. Indeed, foraging can be a mean to obtain a – primary or secondary – source of income provided through trade of harvested wild products. Socio-cultural goals may also motivate human foraging behavior. They are related to culture and heritage, recreational values, or to environmental conservation and sustainability, the latter being exemplified for instance by the recent movement of urban foragers.","container-title":"Anthropology","DOI":"10.4172/2332-0915.1000161","ISSN":"23320915","issue":"01","journalAbbreviation":"Anthropol","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Foraging Wild Resources: Evolving Goals of an Ubiquitous Human Behavior","title-short":"Foraging Wild Resources","URL":"https://www.omicsonline.org/open-access/foraging-wild-resources-evolving-goals-of-an-ubiquitous-human-behavior-2332-0915-1000161.php?aid=69754","volume":"04","author":[{"family":"Svizzero","given":"Serge"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Information on wild species used for food historically came from ethnobiological/ethnobotanical inventories. It is more recently increasing in the scientific literature due to renewed interest in gathering and sustainable use. The most important sources of human food are almost all vascular plants (flowering plants, conifers and other gymnosperms, ferns, horsetails and clubmosses), accounting for 7,014 species of the 7,039 included in the reviews cited. The remainder are bryophytes (mosses, liverworts and hornworts), and green and red algae ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"an4lct1d6c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Antonelli, 2020; Ulian, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Antonelli, 2020; Ulian, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30901,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G9883U5U"],"itemData":{"id":30901,"type":"report","note":"DOI: 10.34885/172","publisher":"Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"State of the World's Plants and Fungi 2020","URL":"https://kew.iro.bl.uk/work/23394899-864d-4ef0-b864-857efea500c5","author":[{"family":"Antonelli","given":"A."},{"family":"Fry","given":"C."},{"family":"Smith","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Simmonds","given":"