"},{"family":"Farlow","given":"A."},{"family":"Farrar","given":"K."},{"family":"Fay","given":"M.F."},{"family":"Felix","given":"J."},{"family":"Forest","given":"F."},{"family":"Forrest","given":"L.L."},{"family":"Fulcher","given":"T."},{"family":"Gafforov","given":"Y."},{"family":"Gardiner","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Gâteblé","given":"G."},{"family":"Gaya","given":"E."},{"family":"Geslin","given":"B."},{"family":"Gonçalves","given":"S.C."},{"family":"Gore","given":"C.J.N."},{"family":"Govaerts","given":"R."},{"family":"Gowda","given":"B."},{"family":"Grace","given":"O.M."},{"family":"Grall","given":"A."},{"family":"Haelewaters","given":"D."},{"family":"Halley","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Hamilton","given":"M.A."},{"family":"Hazra","given":"A."},{"family":"Heller","given":"T."},{"family":"Hollingsworth","given":"P.M."},{"family":"Holstein","given":"N."},{"family":"Howes","given":"M.-J.R."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"M."},{"family":"Hunter","given":"D."},{"family":"Hutchinson","given":"N."},{"family":"Hyde","given":"K."},{"family":"Iganci","given":"J."},{"family":"Jones","given":"M."},{"family":"Kelly","given":"L.J."},{"family":"Kirk","given":"P."},{"family":"Koch","given":"H."},{"family":"Grisai-Greilhuber","given":"I."},{"family":"Lall","given":"N."},{"family":"Langat","given":"M.K."},{"family":"Leaman","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Leão","given":"T.C."},{"family":"Lee","given":"M.A."},{"family":"Leitch","given":"I.J."},{"family":"Leon","given":"C."},{"family":"Lettice","given":"E."},{"family":"Lewis","given":"G.P."},{"family":"Li","given":"L."},{"family":"Lindon","given":"H."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Liu","given":"U."},{"family":"Llewellyn","given":"T."},{"family":"Looney","given":"B."},{"family":"Lovett","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Luczaj","given":"L."},{"family":"Lulekal","given":"E."},{"family":"Maggassouba","given":"S."},{"family":"Malécot","given":"V."},{"family":"Martin","given":"C."},{"family":"Masera","given":"O.R."},{"family":"Mattana","given":"E."},{"family":"Maxted","given":"N."},{"family":"Mba","given":"C."},{"family":"McGinn","given":"K.J."},{"family":"Metheringham","given":"C."},{"family":"Miles","given":"S."},{"family":"Miller","given":"J."},{"family":"Milliken","given":"W."},{"family":"Moat","given":"J."},{"family":"Moore","given":"P.G.P."},{"family":"Morim","given":"M.P."},{"family":"Mueller","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Muminjanov","given":"H."},{"family":"Negrão","given":"R."},{"family":"Nic Lughadha","given":"E."},{"family":"Nicholson","given":"N."},{"family":"Niskanen","given":"T."},{"family":"Nono Womdim","given":"R."},{"family":"Noorani","given":"A."},{"family":"Obreza","given":"M."},{"family":"O'Donnell","given":"K."},{"family":"O'Hanlon","given":"R."},{"family":"Onana","given":"J.-M."},{"family":"Ondo","given":"I."},{"family":"Padulosi","given":"S."},{"family":"Paton","given":"A."},{"family":"Pearce","given":"T."},{"family":"Pérez Escobar","given":"O.A."},{"family":"Pieroni","given":"A."},{"family":"Pironon","given":"S."},{"family":"Prescott","given":"T.A.K."},{"family":"Qi","given":"Y.D."},{"family":"Qin","given":"H."},{"family":"Quave","given":"C.L."},{"family":"Rajaovelona","given":"L."},{"family":"Razanajatovo","given":"H."},{"family":"Reich","given":"P.B."},{"family":"Rianawati","given":"E."},{"family":"Rich","given":"T.C.G."},{"family":"Richards","given":"S.L."},{"family":"Rivers","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Ross","given":"A."},{"family":"Rumsey","given":"F."},{"family":"Ryan","given":"M."},{"family":"Ryan","given":"P."},{"family":"Sagala","given":"S."},{"family":"Sanchez","given":"M.D."},{"family":"Sharrock","given":"S."},{"family":"Shrestha","given":"K.K."},{"family":"Sim","given":"J."},{"family":"Sirakaya","given":"A."},{"family":"Sjöman","given":"H."},{"family":"Smidt","given":"E.C."},{"family":"Smith","given":"D."},{"family":"Smith","given":"P."},{"family":"Smith","given":"S.R."},{"family":"Sofo","given":"A."},{"family":"Spence","given":"N."},{"family":"Stanworth","given":"A."},{"family":"Stara","given":"K."},{"family":"Stevenson","given":"P.C."},{"family":"Stroh","given":"P."},{"family":"Suz","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Tambam","given":"B.B."},{"family":"Tatsis","given":"E.C."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"I."},{"family":"Thiers","given":"B."},{"family":"Thormann","given":"I."},{"family":"Vaglica","given":"V."},{"family":"Vásquez-Londoño","given":"C."},{"family":"Victor","given":"J."},{"family":"Viruel","given":"J."},{"family":"Walker","given":"B.E."},{"family":"Walker","given":"K."},{"family":"Walsh","given":"A."},{"family":"Way","given":"M."},{"family":"Wilbraham","given":"J."},{"family":"Wilkin","given":"P."},{"family":"Wilkinson","given":"T."},{"family":"Williams","given":"C."},{"family":"Winterton","given":"D."},{"family":"Wong","given":"K.M."},{"family":"Woodfield-Pascoe","given":"N."},{"family":"Woodman","given":"J."},{"family":"Wyatt","given":"L."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"R."},{"family":"Zhang","given":"B.G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}}},{"id":30885,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GKT7MWFS"],"itemData":{"id":30885,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Societal Impact Statement Biodiversity is essential to food security and nutrition locally and globally. By reviewing the global state of edible plants and highlighting key neglected and underutilized species (NUS), we attempt to unlock plant food resources and explore the role of fungi, which along with the wealth of traditional knowledge about their uses and practices, could help support sustainable agriculture while ensuring better protection of the environment and the continued delivery of its ecosystem services. This work will inform a wide range of user communities, including scientists, conservation and development organizations, policymakers, and the public of the importance of biodiversity beyond mainstream crops. Summary As the world's population is increasing, humanity is facing both shortages (hunger) and excesses (obesity) of calorie and nutrient intakes. Biodiversity is fundamental to addressing this double challenge, which involves a far better understanding of the global state of food resources. Current estimates suggest that there are at least 7,039 edible plant species, in a broad taxonomic sense, which includes 7,014 vascular plants. This is in striking contrast to the small handful of food crops that provide the majority of humanity's calorie and nutrient intake. Most of these 7,039 edible species have additional uses, the most common being medicines (70%), materials (59%), and environmental uses (40%). Species of major food crops display centers of diversity, as previously proposed, while the rest of edible plants follow latitudinal distribution patterns similarly to the total plant diversity, with higher species richness at lower latitudes. The International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List includes global conservation assessments for at least 30% of edible plants, with ca. 86% of them conserved ex situ. However, at least 11% of those species recorded are threatened. We highlight multipurpose NUS of plants from different regions of the world, which could be key for a more resilient, sustainable, biodiverse, and community participation-driven new “green revolution.” Furthermore, we explore how fungi could diversify and increase the nutritional value of our diets. NUS, along with the wealth of traditional knowledge about their uses and practices, offer a largely untapped resource to support food security and sustainable agriculture. However, for these natural resources to be unlocked, enhanced collaboration among stakeholders is vital.","container-title":"PLANTS, PEOPLE, PLANET","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ppp3.10145","issue":"5","page":"421-445","title":"Unlocking plant resources to support food security and promote sustainable agriculture","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Ulian","given":"Tiziana"},{"family":"Diazgranados","given":"Mauricio"},{"family":"Pironon","given":"Samuel"},{"family":"Padulosi","given":"Stefano"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Udayangani"},{"family":"Davies","given":"Lee"},{"family":"Howes","given":"Melanie-Jayne R."},{"family":"Borrell","given":"James S."},{"family":"Ondo","given":"Ian"},{"family":"Pérez-Escobar","given":"Oscar A."},{"family":"Sharrock","given":"Suzanne"},{"family":"Ryan","given":"Philippa"},{"family":"Hunter","given":"Danny"},{"family":"Lee","given":"Mark A."},{"family":"Barstow","given":"Charles"},{"family":"?uczaj","given":"?ukasz"},{"family":"Pieroni","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Cámara-Leret","given":"Rodrigo"},{"family":"Noorani","given":"Arshiya"},{"family":"Mba","given":"Chikelu"},{"family":"Nono Womdim","given":"Rémi"},{"family":"Muminjanov","given":"Hafiz"},{"family":"Antonelli","given":"Alexandre"},{"family":"Pritchard","given":"Hugh W."},{"family":"Mattana","given":"Efisio"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In agricultural and forager communities in Asian and African countries, the mean use of wild foods is 90-100 species per location, and in indigenous communities there are an estimated 120 wild species used as food in communities in both industrialized and developing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1o85cdgfk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bharucha & Pretty, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Bharucha & Pretty, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32071,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TB7HWFZ5"],"itemData":{"id":32071,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Almost every ecosystem has been amended so that plants and animals can be used as food, fibre, fodder, medicines, traps and weapons. Historically, wild plants and animals were sole dietary components for hunter–gatherer and forager cultures. Today, they remain key to many agricultural communities. The mean use of wild foods by agricultural and forager communities in 22 countries of Asia and Africa (36 studies) is 90–100 species per location. Aggregate country estimates can reach 300–800 species (e.g. India, Ethiopia, Kenya). The mean use of wild species is 120 per community for indigenous communities in both industrialized and developing countries. Many of these wild foods are actively managed, suggesting there is a false dichotomy around ideas of the agricultural and the wild: hunter–gatherers and foragers farm and manage their environments, and cultivators use many wild plants and animals. Yet, provision of and access to these sources of food may be declining as natural habitats come under increasing pressure from development, conservation-exclusions and agricultural expansion. Despite their value, wild foods are excluded from official statistics on economic values of natural resources. It is clear that wild plants and animals continue to form a significant proportion of the global food basket, and while a variety of social and ecological drivers are acting to reduce wild food use, their importance may be set to grow as pressures on agricultural productivity increase.","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences","DOI":"10.1098/rstb.2010.0123","ISSN":"0962-8436, 1471-2970","issue":"1554","journalAbbreviation":"Phil. Trans. R. Soc. B","language":"en","page":"2913-2926","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The roles and values of wild foods in agricultural systems","URL":"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rstb.2010.0123","volume":"365","author":[{"family":"Bharucha","given":"Zareen"},{"family":"Pretty","given":"Jules"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",9,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      With economic and social development, the acquisition of wild food through gathering has been gradually marginalized. In some places, the harvest and consumption of wild foods is considered antiquated behavior and may even be denigrated and abandoned ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1f920ihtug","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garcia, 2006; \\\\uc0\\\\u321{}uczaj, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Garcia, 2006; ?uczaj, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32890,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I6VFNN3U"],"itemData":{"id":32890,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"Artn 39 10.1186/1746-4269-2-39","ISSN":"1746-4269","language":"English","title":"The mother - child nexus. Knowledge and valuation of wild food plants in Wayanad, Western Ghats, India","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Garcia","given":"G. S. C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":30765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U6XCGXXT"],"itemData":{"id":30765,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The aim of this review is to present an overview of changes in the contemporary use of wild food plants in Europe, mainly using the examples of our home countries: Poland, Italy, Spain, Estonia and Sweden. We set the scene referring to the nutrition of 19th century peasants, involving many famine and emergency foods. Later we discuss such issues as children's wild snacks, the association between the decline of plant knowledge and the disappearance of plant use, the effects of over-exploitation, the decrease of the availability of plants due to ecosystem changes, land access rights for foragers and intoxication dangers. We also describe the 20th and 21st century vogues in wild plant use, particularly their shift into the domain of haute-cuisine.","container-title":"Acta Societatis Botanicorum Poloniae","DOI":"10.5586/asbp.2012.031","ISSN":"2083-9480","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"Copyright (c) 2012 ?ukasz ?uczaj, Andrea Pieroni, Javier Tardío, Manuel Pardo-de-Santayana, Renata Sõukand, Ingvar Svanberg, Raivo Kalle","note":"number: 4","page":"359-370","source":"pbsociety.org.pl","title":"Wild food plant use in 21st century Europe: the disappearance of old traditions and the search for new cuisines involving wild edibles","title-short":"Wild food plant use in 21st century Europe","URL":"https://pbsociety.org.pl/journals/index.php/asbp/article/view/asbp.2012.031","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"?uczaj","given":"?ukasz"},{"family":"Pieroni","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Tardío","given":"Javier"},{"family":"Pardo-de-Santayana","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Sõukand","given":"Renata"},{"family":"Svanberg","given":"Ingvar"},{"family":"Kalle","given":"Raivo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",11,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, islands of Western Oceania are particularly rich in native fruit and nut trees; in Vanuatu, out of 40 of these native species, 30 are not cultivated; they used to play an important part in local diets but presently are often substituted by industrial food ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ac92k7v1rn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Walter & Sam, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Walter & Sam, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32055,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IWS7G2WQ"],"itemData":{"id":32055,"type":"book","collection-title":"Didsctiques","event-place":"Paris","publisher":"IRD Editions","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Fruits d’Océanie","author":[{"family":"Walter","given":"Annie"},{"family":"Sam","given":"Chanel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In places where gathering persists, it has been suggested that some people consider it an optimal alternative to farming. This may include trading foraged goods with farmers. This is recognized to be the case in places where gatherers refrain from practicing agriculture for cultural, social, or institutional reasons. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1sr9sk5ss1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Tisdell & Svizzero, 2015)","plainCitation":"(C. Tisdell & Svizzero, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33566,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IHGLYXQY"],"itemData":{"id":33566,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Social Evolution & History","issue":"2","note":"publisher: ???????? ? ???????????? ???????????????? «???????????? «???????»","title":"The persistence of hunting and gathering economies","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Tisdell","given":"Clem"},{"family":"Svizzero","given":"Serge"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, it is now valued again in some countries as health food and in haute cuisine ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2k5m3m21o9","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u321{}uczaj, 2012; Doyon, 2019)","plainCitation":"(?uczaj, 2012; Doyon, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U6XCGXXT"],"itemData":{"id":30765,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The aim of this review is to present an overview of changes in the contemporary use of wild food plants in Europe, mainly using the examples of our home countries: Poland, Italy, Spain, Estonia and Sweden. We set the scene referring to the nutrition of 19th century peasants, involving many famine and emergency foods. Later we discuss such issues as children's wild snacks, the association between the decline of plant knowledge and the disappearance of plant use, the effects of over-exploitation, the decrease of the availability of plants due to ecosystem changes, land access rights for foragers and intoxication dangers. We also describe the 20th and 21st century vogues in wild plant use, particularly their shift into the domain of haute-cuisine.","container-title":"Acta Societatis Botanicorum Poloniae","DOI":"10.5586/asbp.2012.031","ISSN":"2083-9480","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"Copyright (c) 2012 ?ukasz ?uczaj, Andrea Pieroni, Javier Tardío, Manuel Pardo-de-Santayana, Renata Sõukand, Ingvar Svanberg, Raivo Kalle","note":"number: 4","page":"359-370","source":"pbsociety.org.pl","title":"Wild food plant use in 21st century Europe: the disappearance of old traditions and the search for new cuisines involving wild edibles","title-short":"Wild food plant use in 21st century Europe","URL":"https://pbsociety.org.pl/journals/index.php/asbp/article/view/asbp.2012.031","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"?uczaj","given":"?ukasz"},{"family":"Pieroni","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Tardío","given":"Javier"},{"family":"Pardo-de-Santayana","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Sõukand","given":"Renata"},{"family":"Svanberg","given":"Ingvar"},{"family":"Kalle","given":"Raivo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",11,19]]}}},{"id":32052,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7N44HQTZ"],"itemData":{"id":32052,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EchoGéo [online]","DOI":":10.4000/echogeo.16873","title":"Les cueillettes commerciales au Québec?: capter la diversité socio-environnementale","URL":"http://journals.openedition.org/echogeo/16873","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Doyon","given":"Sabrina"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There is also a growing demand for wild plants in the food and aromatics industry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UPO7aF0p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lescure, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Lescure, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":928,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZTISC2NP"],"itemData":{"id":928,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The ecologist, ethnologist and gatherers, authors of this work have established an almost exhaustive list of plants subject to commercial pickings in mainland France. 728 species or subspecies are thus listed, and forest species or those frequently associated with forest processions represent a quarter of them.\\n\\nAmong the 74 species cited by more than half of the collectors surveyed, more than half can be found in the forest.\\n\\nFor all species, the status \\"wild\\" and / or \\"cultivated\\" is specified. The authors also informed for all species whether they benefited from protection measures and the number of departments concerned.","container-title":"Le Monde des Plantes","page":"19-39","title":"Les plantes faisant l’objet de cueillettes commerciales sur le territoire métropolitain. Une liste commentée","URL":"https://oppla.eu/casestudy/20464","volume":"517","author":[{"family":"Lescure","given":"J.P."},{"family":"Thévenin","given":"T."},{"family":"Garreta","given":"R."},{"family":"Morisson","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In addition to being a food source, evidence shows that for some indigenous peoples and local communities, during times of food shortage wild foods provide nutritional supplements of important vitamins and minerals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"adkjl06oln","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harris & Mohammed, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Harris & Mohammed, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32034,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SHB9AKCE"],"itemData":{"id":32034,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ambio","DOI":"Doi 10.1639/0044-7447(2003)032[0024:Ronwfi]2.0.Co;2","ISSN":"0044-7447","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ambio","language":"English","page":"24-29","title":"Relying on nature: Wild foods in Northern Nigeria","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Harris","given":"F. M. A."},{"family":"Mohammed","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This finding extends to urban dwellers in developed countries. Gathering wild foods sustains dietary traditions and supports community livelihoods. Trends in consumption of wild foods in Europe vary according to regions and countries, and according to categories of species. One study found that across European Union countries at least 27 species of mushrooms and 81 species of vascular plants are harvested and consumed as wild food ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2jcltsqbsv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schulp, Thuiller, & Verburg, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Schulp, Thuiller, & Verburg, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32038,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KHBC39S6"],"itemData":{"id":32038,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2014.06.018","ISSN":"0921-8009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Econ","language":"English","page":"292-305","title":"Wild food in Europe: A synthesis of knowledge and data of terrestrial wild food as an ecosystem service","volume":"105","author":[{"family":"Schulp","given":"C. J. E."},{"family":"Thuiller","given":"W."},{"family":"Verburg","given":"P. H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Gathering for food is not a static process; some wild plants are consistently gathered, others are forgotten or re-emerge after periods of unpopularity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"atiok8qc84","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u321{}uczaj, 2012)","plainCitation":"(?uczaj, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U6XCGXXT"],"itemData":{"id":30765,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The aim of this review is to present an overview of changes in the contemporary use of wild food plants in Europe, mainly using the examples of our home countries: Poland, Italy, Spain, Estonia and Sweden. We set the scene referring to the nutrition of 19th century peasants, involving many famine and emergency foods. Later we discuss such issues as children's wild snacks, the association between the decline of plant knowledge and the disappearance of plant use, the effects of over-exploitation, the decrease of the availability of plants due to ecosystem changes, land access rights for foragers and intoxication dangers. We also describe the 20th and 21st century vogues in wild plant use, particularly their shift into the domain of haute-cuisine.","container-title":"Acta Societatis Botanicorum Poloniae","DOI":"10.5586/asbp.2012.031","ISSN":"2083-9480","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"Copyright (c) 2012 ?ukasz ?uczaj, Andrea Pieroni, Javier Tardío, Manuel Pardo-de-Santayana, Renata Sõukand, Ingvar Svanberg, Raivo Kalle","note":"number: 4","page":"359-370","source":"pbsociety.org.pl","title":"Wild food plant use in 21st century Europe: the disappearance of old traditions and the search for new cuisines involving wild edibles","title-short":"Wild food plant use in 21st century Europe","URL":"https://pbsociety.org.pl/journals/index.php/asbp/article/view/asbp.2012.031","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"?uczaj","given":"?ukasz"},{"family":"Pieroni","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Tardío","given":"Javier"},{"family":"Pardo-de-Santayana","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Sõukand","given":"Renata"},{"family":"Svanberg","given":"Ingvar"},{"family":"Kalle","given":"Raivo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",11,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Scientific studies have focused on the analysis of dietary conditions related to human health, such as the nutritional content of wild species, toxic side effects, heavy metal concentration (mainly fungi) and health risk assessment. Recorded indigenous and local knowledge combined with scientific analysis, is promoting new resources for crop development, the protection of crop wild relatives, and the provision of new solutions or ideas to address global hunger and protein sources. Because the number of wild plants (and fungi) gathered for food is so extensive, we have further divided this section into sub-sections.
	Wild fruits are important source of nutrition, medicine, materials for cosmetics, crafts, fiber, and fuel and are the most widely used wild algae, fungi and plants. Clement ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ckpblp96e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Clement, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Clement, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32044,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ECPGHDP7"],"itemData":{"id":32044,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Studies in Historical Ecology","container-title":"Time and Complexity in the Neotropical Lowlands","edition":"Balée W., Erickson C.L. (ed)","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-0-231-13562-7","page":"165-185","publisher":"Columbia University Press","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Fruit trees and the transition to food production in Amazonia","URL":"https://www.academia.edu/778447/Fruit_trees_and_the_transition_to_food_production_in_Amazonia","author":[{"family":"Clement","given":"Charles R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} distinguishes three types of fruits: (i) nuts and seeds, which contain oil and are rich in proteins and so can play an important part in the diet, (ii) starchy fruits rich in oil and starch (such as palm fruits) and (iii) juicy fruits, such as berries, rich in vitamins. In the United States of America alone, permitted harvest volumes of edible fruits, nuts, and berries were as follows: 303, 748 gallons and 670,726 pounds ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"avat6d776r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30981,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5ZULX6G7"],"itemData":{"id":30981,"type":"book","collection-number":"232","collection-title":"General Technical Report SRS","ISBN":"978-0-16-094588-5","number-of-pages":"276","publisher":"Government Printing Office","title":"Assessment of Nontimber Forest Prodcuts in the United States Under Changing Conditions","URL":"https://books.google.co.za/books?id=L8SIGPD_pv0C&dq=Chamberlain+2018+NTFP+assessment&lr=&source=gbs_navlinks_s","author":[{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"James L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While these figures represent the best available data, they likely do not represent total harvest of popular species black walnut (Juglans negra L.), pine nuts, and low-bush blueberries.
      A recent literature review on wild edible fruits found that studies have increased over the last three decades, a majority of it reports ethnobotanical and taxonomic descriptions with relatively few studies on their landscape ecology, economics, and conservation. Among them, a third of retrieved articles were based on studies in Africa and a quarter were from South America. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"r01vYATP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mallika Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Mallika Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32110,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VB4FL976"],"itemData":{"id":32110,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wild edible fruits (WEFs) are among the most widely used non-timber forest products (NTFPs), and important sources of nutrition, medicine, and income for their users. In addition to their use as food, WEF species may also yield ?ber, fuel, and a range of processed products. Besides forests, WEF species also thrive in diverse environments, such as agroforestry and urban landscapes, deserts, fallows, natural lands, and plantations. Given the multifunctional, ubiquitous nature of WEFs, we conducted a systematic review on the literature speci?c to WEFs and highlighted links between di?erent domains of the wider knowledge on NTFPs. We found that literature speci?c to WEFs was limited, and a majority of it reported ethnobotanical and taxonomic descriptions, with relatively few studies on landscape ecology, economics, and conservation of WEFs. Our review identi?es priorities and emerging avenues for research and policymaking to promote sustainable WEF management and use, and subsequent biodiversity and habitat conservation. In particular, we recommend that ecosystem services, economic incentives, market innovations, and stakeholder synergies are incorporated into WEF conservation strategies.","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f10060467","ISSN":"1999-4907","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Forests","language":"en","page":"467","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild Edible Fruits: A Systematic Review of an Under-Researched Multifunctional NTFP (Non-Timber Forest Product)","title-short":"Wild Edible Fruits","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/1999-4907/10/6/467","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Sardeshpande","given":"Mallika"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",5,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Different fruit species respond differently to harvesting and other disturbances, such as fire and herbivory. Although the review by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1cfaup1mu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stanley, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Stanley, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33998,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S5QGTRKX"],"itemData":{"id":33998,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ethnobiology and Conservation","ISSN":"2238-4782","journalAbbreviation":"Ethnobio Conserv","language":"en","license":"Copyright (c)","source":"www.ethnobioconservation.com","title":"Is Non-Timber Forest Product Harvest Sustainable in the Less Developed World? A Systematic Review of the Recent Economic and Ecological Literature","title-short":"Is Non-Timber Forest Product Harvest Sustainable in the Less Developed World?","URL":"https://www.ethnobioconservation.com/index.php/ebc/article/view/19","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Stanley","given":"Denise"},{"family":"Voeks","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Short","given":"Leaa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",10,11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} concludes that the majority of case studies surmise that wild algae, fungi and plants harvests are ecologically sustainable, Sardeshpande and Shackleton ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wH0Su4Ww","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32110,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VB4FL976"],"itemData":{"id":32110,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wild edible fruits (WEFs) are among the most widely used non-timber forest products (NTFPs), and important sources of nutrition, medicine, and income for their users. In addition to their use as food, WEF species may also yield ?ber, fuel, and a range of processed products. Besides forests, WEF species also thrive in diverse environments, such as agroforestry and urban landscapes, deserts, fallows, natural lands, and plantations. Given the multifunctional, ubiquitous nature of WEFs, we conducted a systematic review on the literature speci?c to WEFs and highlighted links between di?erent domains of the wider knowledge on NTFPs. We found that literature speci?c to WEFs was limited, and a majority of it reported ethnobotanical and taxonomic descriptions, with relatively few studies on landscape ecology, economics, and conservation of WEFs. Our review identi?es priorities and emerging avenues for research and policymaking to promote sustainable WEF management and use, and subsequent biodiversity and habitat conservation. In particular, we recommend that ecosystem services, economic incentives, market innovations, and stakeholder synergies are incorporated into WEF conservation strategies.","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f10060467","ISSN":"1999-4907","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Forests","language":"en","page":"467","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild Edible Fruits: A Systematic Review of an Under-Researched Multifunctional NTFP (Non-Timber Forest Product)","title-short":"Wild Edible Fruits","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/1999-4907/10/6/467","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Sardeshpande","given":"Mallika"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",5,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found 14 of the 25 studies explicitly addressing harvest sustainability illustrated overexploitation beyond recovery to optimal vitality. In some cases, extraction in a commercial scale is the attempt to make benefits to avoid tree logging and deforestation, such as the marula (Sclerocarya birrea) fruit, the Brazil nut (Bertholletia excelsa, Lecythidaceae) and the bush mango (Irvingia gabonensis Baill. ex Lanen.). When harvest is lethal to the plant or market demand is high which drives to intensive production, the species of wild edible fruits is domesticated and cultivated as tree crops. Certification is considered to ensure the sustainability of gathering under the influence of the trade chain and to promote socio-economic conditions for harvesters and forest communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"48UlOE1R","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mallika Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Mallika Sardeshpande & Shackleton, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32110,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VB4FL976"],"itemData":{"id":32110,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wild edible fruits (WEFs) are among the most widely used non-timber forest products (NTFPs), and important sources of nutrition, medicine, and income for their users. In addition to their use as food, WEF species may also yield ?ber, fuel, and a range of processed products. Besides forests, WEF species also thrive in diverse environments, such as agroforestry and urban landscapes, deserts, fallows, natural lands, and plantations. Given the multifunctional, ubiquitous nature of WEFs, we conducted a systematic review on the literature speci?c to WEFs and highlighted links between di?erent domains of the wider knowledge on NTFPs. We found that literature speci?c to WEFs was limited, and a majority of it reported ethnobotanical and taxonomic descriptions, with relatively few studies on landscape ecology, economics, and conservation of WEFs. Our review identi?es priorities and emerging avenues for research and policymaking to promote sustainable WEF management and use, and subsequent biodiversity and habitat conservation. In particular, we recommend that ecosystem services, economic incentives, market innovations, and stakeholder synergies are incorporated into WEF conservation strategies.","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f10060467","ISSN":"1999-4907","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Forests","language":"en","page":"467","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild Edible Fruits: A Systematic Review of an Under-Researched Multifunctional NTFP (Non-Timber Forest Product)","title-short":"Wild Edible Fruits","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/1999-4907/10/6/467","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Sardeshpande","given":"Mallika"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",5,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The following examples collate several species of wild edible fruits are a complement to the aforementioned review that are mainly gathered from the wild and also support a certain scale of trade in a medium term.
      
* Nuts and Seeds
In the United States of America, pine nuts (Pinus monophyla Torr. & Frém.) are highly prized nuts harvested primarily from natural stands on public lands in the western half of the country. These forests are usually not actively managed for pine nut production and in fact are a forest complex (pinyon-juniper) that was historically seen as without much value and eradicated in favor of range lands. However, pine nuts have a long history of use among indigenous peoples and local communities in the southwestern United States of America, where pine nuts continue to be harvested for local markets and for export. While the United States of America exported approximately 20,000 United States Dollars worth of pine nuts in 2007, it imported about 54 million United States Dollars worth ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1l0ptk8lh2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30981,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5ZULX6G7"],"itemData":{"id":30981,"type":"book","collection-number":"232","collection-title":"General Technical Report SRS","ISBN":"978-0-16-094588-5","number-of-pages":"276","publisher":"Government Printing Office","title":"Assessment of Nontimber Forest Prodcuts in the United States Under Changing Conditions","URL":"https://books.google.co.za/books?id=L8SIGPD_pv0C&dq=Chamberlain+2018+NTFP+assessment&lr=&source=gbs_navlinks_s","author":[{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"James L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, suggesting that the majority of pine nuts harvested are for personal and local use. Also in the United States of America, approximately 25 million pounds of black walnut (Juglans nigra L.) were harvested from natural populations in 1998, although it is unknown if this constitutes a sustainable harvest amount ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1o23v658io","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30981,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5ZULX6G7"],"itemData":{"id":30981,"type":"book","collection-number":"232","collection-title":"General Technical Report SRS","ISBN":"978-0-16-094588-5","number-of-pages":"276","publisher":"Government Printing Office","title":"Assessment of Nontimber Forest Prodcuts in the United States Under Changing Conditions","URL":"https://books.google.co.za/books?id=L8SIGPD_pv0C&dq=Chamberlain+2018+NTFP+assessment&lr=&source=gbs_navlinks_s","author":[{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"James L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	The Brazil nut tree is an iconic tree occurring in terra firme (non?flooded) forests throughout the Amazon basin. It can reach up to 50 meters tall and live for hundreds of years. Brazil nut seed harvesting from natural forests is a cornerstone algae, fungi and plants economies in Amazonia. Brazil nuts are the only globally traded seed gathered from the wild by tens of thousands of rural households and are an integral component of the extractivist culture of many indigenous peoples and local communities in the area. In the Brazilian Amazon alone, over 45,000 tons of Brazil nuts are gathered annually, with sales of over 33 million United States dollars ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"apt87p916","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Guariguata, Cronkleton, Duchelle, & Zuidema, 2017; Peres, 2003; Wadt, Kainer, Staudhammer, & Serrano, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Guariguata, Cronkleton, Duchelle, & Zuidema, 2017; Peres, 2003; Wadt, Kainer, Staudhammer, & Serrano, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":903,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2PPLPL9Y"],"itemData":{"id":903,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Brazil nut (the seeds of the rainforest tree Bertholletia excelsa) is the only globally traded seed collected from the wild by forest-based harvesters across the Amazon basin. The large geographic scale of Brazil nut exploitation and the significant contributions to local livelihoods, national economies, and forest-based development over the last decades, merit a review of the “conservation-through-use” paradigm. We use Elinor Ostrom’s framework for assessing sustainability in socioecological systems: (1) resource unit, (2) users, (3) governance system, and (4) resource system, to determine how different contexts and external developments generate specific conservation and development outcomes. We find that the resource unit reacts robustly to the type and level of extraction currently practiced; that resource users have built on a self-organized system that had defined boundaries and access to the resource; that linked production chains, market networks and informal financing work to supply global markets; and that local harvesters have used supporting alliances with NGOs and conservationists to formalize and secure their endogenous governance system and make it more equitable. As a result, the Brazil nut model represents a socioecological system that may not require major changes to sustain productivity. Yet since long-term Brazil nut production seems inextricably tied to a continuous forest cover, and because planted Brazil nut trees currently provide a minimal contribution to total nut production basin-wide, we call to preserve, diversify and intensify production in Brazil nut-rich forests that will inevitably become ever more integrated within human-modified landscapes over time.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-017-1355-3","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","page":"2007-2027","title":"Revisiting the ‘cornerstone of Amazonian conservation’: a socioecological assessment of Brazil nut exploitation","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Guariguata","given":"Manuel R."},{"family":"Cronkleton","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Duchelle","given":"Amy E."},{"family":"Zuidema","given":"Pieter A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8,1]]}}},{"id":909,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BCE7W5DT"],"itemData":{"id":909,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"A comparative analysis of 23 populations of the Brazil nut tree (Bertholletia excelsa) across the Brazilian, Peruvian, and Bolivian Amazon shows that the history and intensity of Brazil nut exploitation are major determinants of population size structure. Populations subjected to persistent levels of harvest lack juvenile trees less than 60 centimeters in diameter at breast height; only populations with a history of either light or recent exploitation contain large numbers of juvenile trees. A harvesting model confirms that intensive exploitation levels over the past century are such that juvenile recruitment is insufficient to maintain populations over the long term. Without management, intensively harvested populations will succumb to a process of senescence and demographic collapse, threatening this cornerstone of the Amazonian extractive economy.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1091698","issue":"5653","page":"2112-2114","title":"Demographic Threats to the Sustainability of Brazil Nut Exploitation","volume":"302","author":[{"family":"Peres","given":"Carlos A."},{"family":"Baider","given":"Claudia"},{"family":"Zuidema","given":"Pieter A."},{"family":"Wadt","given":"Lúcia H. O."},{"family":"Kainer","given":"Karen A."},{"family":"Gomes-Silva","given":"Daisy A. P."},{"family":"Salomão","given":"Rafael P."},{"family":"Simões","given":"Luciana L."},{"family":"Franciosi","given":"Eduardo R. N."},{"family":"Cornejo Valverde","given":"Fernando"},{"family":"Gribel","given":"Rogério"},{"family":"Shepard","given":"Glenn H."},{"family":"Kanashiro","given":"Milton"},{"family":"Coventry","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Yu","given":"Douglas W."},{"family":"Watkinson","given":"Andrew R."},{"family":"Freckleton","given":"Robert P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":32035,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PK3GSQ6H"],"itemData":{"id":32035,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The emergence of Brazilian extractive reserves reinforced the notion that sustainable forest use could play a central role in conservation. Brazil nut is considered a model non-timber product for promoting conservation through use. Demographic studies, however, have demonstrated differential impacts of nut harvest on Brazil nut population structure. Comparing three populations with different forest use histories, degrees of road access, and recent levels of nut harvest, we asked: (1) Are they exhibiting regeneration failure? and (2) Are seedling/sapling densities explained by adult Brazil nut structure, fruit fate and/or overall forest structure? We installed four 9-ha plots in each site to census Brazil nut trees ?10cm dbh, and within each plot, 36 subplots (25×25m each) to quantify regeneration, overall forest structure, and open and closed fruit counts. Approximately 29–55% of fruits was unharvested, and more than 90% of these was opened by scatterhoarding dispersers. Population structure approximated a reverse-J size class distribution, with seedling densities varying from 3.2 to 5.8individualsha?1. We conclude that within the current harvest context, regeneration is sufficient for population persistence in our sites, at least over the medium term. The socioeconomics of sustainably managing Brazil nut is the greater challenge, involving interlinked competitive land uses, nut quality, and substantial increases in local income from harvest. Some of these challenges are being addressed in Brazil, Bolivia and Peru, providing hope that this cornerstone extractive species will continue to play a prominent role in the ecological and economic landscape of Amazonia.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biocon.2007.10.007","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"1","page":"332-346","title":"Sustainable forest use in Brazilian extractive reserves: Natural regeneration of Brazil nut in exploited populations","volume":"141","author":[{"family":"Wadt","given":"L. H. O."},{"family":"Kainer","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Staudhammer","given":"C. L."},{"family":"Serrano","given":"R. O. P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Brazil nut is organized in a concession system and the supply chain includes three certification schemes: (i) organic certification; (ii) Fairtrade certification; and (iii) Forest Stewardship Council certification. It is considered a model of the use of wild species for promoting “conservation-through-use”. Extensive research suggests the Brazil nut tree reacts robustly to the type and level of extraction currently practiced in the medium term ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ah7cj0094t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Guariguata, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Guariguata, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":903,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2PPLPL9Y"],"itemData":{"id":903,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Brazil nut (the seeds of the rainforest tree Bertholletia excelsa) is the only globally traded seed collected from the wild by forest-based harvesters across the Amazon basin. The large geographic scale of Brazil nut exploitation and the significant contributions to local livelihoods, national economies, and forest-based development over the last decades, merit a review of the “conservation-through-use” paradigm. We use Elinor Ostrom’s framework for assessing sustainability in socioecological systems: (1) resource unit, (2) users, (3) governance system, and (4) resource system, to determine how different contexts and external developments generate specific conservation and development outcomes. We find that the resource unit reacts robustly to the type and level of extraction currently practiced; that resource users have built on a self-organized system that had defined boundaries and access to the resource; that linked production chains, market networks and informal financing work to supply global markets; and that local harvesters have used supporting alliances with NGOs and conservationists to formalize and secure their endogenous governance system and make it more equitable. As a result, the Brazil nut model represents a socioecological system that may not require major changes to sustain productivity. Yet since long-term Brazil nut production seems inextricably tied to a continuous forest cover, and because planted Brazil nut trees currently provide a minimal contribution to total nut production basin-wide, we call to preserve, diversify and intensify production in Brazil nut-rich forests that will inevitably become ever more integrated within human-modified landscapes over time.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-017-1355-3","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","page":"2007-2027","title":"Revisiting the ‘cornerstone of Amazonian conservation’: a socioecological assessment of Brazil nut exploitation","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Guariguata","given":"Manuel R."},{"family":"Cronkleton","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Duchelle","given":"Amy E."},{"family":"Zuidema","given":"Pieter A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Given the importance of this species to the local economy, this assessment highlights some specific concerns that may affect future status and trends of Brazil nut production. Without active management, in the past extensive and intensive exploitation led to insufficient juvenile recruitment to maintain populations, and harvested populations went into a process of senescence and demographic collapse. Rainfall is also a key factor in determining tree performance and demography and the forecasted declines in pollinator diversity may threaten the long?term resilience of the Brazil nut trees. Climate change therefore could potentially negatively impact B. excelsa populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2lg6ckjdg6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Peres, 2003; Thomas, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Peres, 2003; Thomas, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":909,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BCE7W5DT"],"itemData":{"id":909,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"A comparative analysis of 23 populations of the Brazil nut tree (Bertholletia excelsa) across the Brazilian, Peruvian, and Bolivian Amazon shows that the history and intensity of Brazil nut exploitation are major determinants of population size structure. Populations subjected to persistent levels of harvest lack juvenile trees less than 60 centimeters in diameter at breast height; only populations with a history of either light or recent exploitation contain large numbers of juvenile trees. A harvesting model confirms that intensive exploitation levels over the past century are such that juvenile recruitment is insufficient to maintain populations over the long term. Without management, intensively harvested populations will succumb to a process of senescence and demographic collapse, threatening this cornerstone of the Amazonian extractive economy.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1091698","issue":"5653","page":"2112-2114","title":"Demographic Threats to the Sustainability of Brazil Nut Exploitation","volume":"302","author":[{"family":"Peres","given":"Carlos A."},{"family":"Baider","given":"Claudia"},{"family":"Zuidema","given":"Pieter A."},{"family":"Wadt","given":"Lúcia H. O."},{"family":"Kainer","given":"Karen A."},{"family":"Gomes-Silva","given":"Daisy A. P."},{"family":"Salomão","given":"Rafael P."},{"family":"Simões","given":"Luciana L."},{"family":"Franciosi","given":"Eduardo R. N."},{"family":"Cornejo Valverde","given":"Fernando"},{"family":"Gribel","given":"Rogério"},{"family":"Shepard","given":"Glenn H."},{"family":"Kanashiro","given":"Milton"},{"family":"Coventry","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Yu","given":"Douglas W."},{"family":"Watkinson","given":"Andrew R."},{"family":"Freckleton","given":"Robert P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":32100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P7ZPEKTC"],"itemData":{"id":32100,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0183743","issue":"8","page":"e0183743","title":"NTFP harvesters as citizen scientists: Validating traditional and crowdsourced knowledge on seed production of Brazil nut trees in the Peruvian Amazon","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Thomas","given":"Evert"},{"family":"Valdivia","given":"Jheyson"},{"family":"Caicedo","given":"Carolina Alcázar"},{"family":"Quaedvlieg","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Wadt","given":"Lucia Helena O."},{"family":"Corvera","given":"Ronald"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Changes in human use of the forested landscape are also an immediate concern. Brazil nut extraction is accompanied by unsustainable forestry activities outside the gathering seasons in a given year. Due to development pressures, Brazil nut forests have been gradually destroyed and transformed into market-oriented agricultural areas to support global beef markets. Land conversion in the basin has also sparked violent conflicts and led to decreased sustainable management of Brazil nut producing areas. Some of these challenges are being addressed in Brazil, Bolivia and Peru ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1bgcn2lrtg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bertwell, Kainer, Cropper Jr, Staudhammer, & de Oliveira Wadt, 2018; Escobal & Aldana, 2003; C. S. Simmons, 2019; Wadt, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Bertwell, Kainer, Cropper Jr, Staudhammer, & de Oliveira Wadt, 2018; Escobal & Aldana, 2003; C. S. Simmons, 2019; Wadt, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":904,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5R4FU8AY"],"itemData":{"id":904,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract Harvest of Brazil nuts from the large, iconic tree Bertholletia excelsa generates substantial income for smallholders, providing a strong incentive to conserve the mature forests where it grows. Although much previous work has focused on the impact of nut harvest on new seedling recruits into B. excelsa populations, the connection between harvest rates and long-term population stability is still unclear. Moreover, there is additional uncertainty for Brazil nut management in terms of population response to climate change and other anthropogenic influences. We drew on 14 years of research in two sites in Acre, Brazil with different B. excelsa nut harvest intensities (39% and 81%), to produce stochastic and deterministic matrix population models which incorporated parameter uncertainty in vital rates. Adult abundance was projected to remain close to the current observed abundance or higher through the next 50 years. Elasticity analyses revealed that the asymptotic population growth rate (?) was most sensitive to stasis vital rates in sapling, juvenile, and adult stages. Deterministic transition matrices calculated using diameter growth rates dependent on rainfall yielded average ? values around 1.0 under extreme high, extreme low, and average annual rainfall. While sustained high rates of Brazil nut harvest and climate change could potentially negatively impact B. excelsa populations, changes in human use of the forested landscape are more immediate concern. To reduce the risk of population decline, smallholders and managers of B. excelsa rich forests should focus on conservation of pre-mature and mature individuals.","container-title":"Biotropica","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/btp.12505","ISSN":"0006-3606","issue":"1","page":"50-59","title":"Are Brazil nut populations threatened by fruit harvest?","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Bertwell","given":"Todd D."},{"family":"Kainer","given":"Karen A."},{"family":"Cropper Jr","given":"Wendell P."},{"family":"Staudhammer","given":"Christina L."},{"family":"Oliveira Wadt","given":"Lúcia H.","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32036,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GFVFNRUG"],"itemData":{"id":32036,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This study explores the relationship between poverty and Amazonian forest management by Brazil nut harvesters in southeastern Peru. Although the poor rely more upon natural resource-based income than the rich, wealthier households use more forest wood and land than poorer ones. Contrary to the belief that exploitation of nontimber forest products can alleviate deforestation, this study finds that seasonal Brazil nut extraction is accompanied by unsustainable forestry activities during the rest of the year. Only those who find employment in activities unrelated to forestry manage to break the link between poverty and environmental degradation.","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.worlddev.2003.08.001","ISSN":"0305-750X","issue":"11","page":"1873-1887","title":"Are Nontimber Forest Products the Antidote to Rainforest Degradation? Brazil Nut Extraction in Madre De Dios, Peru","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Escobal","given":"Javier"},{"family":"Aldana","given":"Ursula"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",11,1]]}}},{"id":32101,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IZHCN3ZT"],"itemData":{"id":32101,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of the American Association of Geographers","DOI":"10.1080/24694452.2018.1489215","ISSN":"2469-4452","issue":"1","page":"242-265","title":"Discipline and Develop: Destruction of the Brazil Nut Forest in the Lower Amazon Basin","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Simmons","given":"Cynthia S."},{"family":"Walker","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Aldrich","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Arima","given":"Eugenio"},{"family":"Pereira","given":"Ritaumaria"},{"family":"Castro","given":"Edna Maria Ramos","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Michelotti","given":"Fernando"},{"family":"Waylen","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Antunes","given":"Aghane"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1,2]]}}},{"id":32035,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PK3GSQ6H"],"itemData":{"id":32035,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The emergence of Brazilian extractive reserves reinforced the notion that sustainable forest use could play a central role in conservation. Brazil nut is considered a model non-timber product for promoting conservation through use. Demographic studies, however, have demonstrated differential impacts of nut harvest on Brazil nut population structure. Comparing three populations with different forest use histories, degrees of road access, and recent levels of nut harvest, we asked: (1) Are they exhibiting regeneration failure? and (2) Are seedling/sapling densities explained by adult Brazil nut structure, fruit fate and/or overall forest structure? We installed four 9-ha plots in each site to census Brazil nut trees ?10cm dbh, and within each plot, 36 subplots (25×25m each) to quantify regeneration, overall forest structure, and open and closed fruit counts. Approximately 29–55% of fruits was unharvested, and more than 90% of these was opened by scatterhoarding dispersers. Population structure approximated a reverse-J size class distribution, with seedling densities varying from 3.2 to 5.8individualsha?1. We conclude that within the current harvest context, regeneration is sufficient for population persistence in our sites, at least over the medium term. The socioeconomics of sustainably managing Brazil nut is the greater challenge, involving interlinked competitive land uses, nut quality, and substantial increases in local income from harvest. Some of these challenges are being addressed in Brazil, Bolivia and Peru, providing hope that this cornerstone extractive species will continue to play a prominent role in the ecological and economic landscape of Amazonia.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biocon.2007.10.007","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"1","page":"332-346","title":"Sustainable forest use in Brazilian extractive reserves: Natural regeneration of Brazil nut in exploited populations","volume":"141","author":[{"family":"Wadt","given":"L. H. O."},{"family":"Kainer","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Staudhammer","given":"C. L."},{"family":"Serrano","given":"R. O. P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      
* Starchy Fruits
At least 30 Amazonian palm species are used as food, most of them for their fruits (Attalea spp., Euterpe spp., Mauritia flexuosa, Oenocarpus ssp.), consumed raw, cooked or processed into drinks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aoi8rp7ktd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kahn, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Kahn, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30976,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BZ7V3M9R"],"itemData":{"id":30976,"type":"book","collection-title":"Didactiques","event-place":"Paris","ISBN":"2-7099-1359-3","publisher":"ORSTOM","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Les palmiers de l'Eldorado","author":[{"family":"Kahn","given":"Francis"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Oenocarpus bataua is the seventh most abundant tree in the Amazon and one of the most used palms in neotropical forests in the Americas. Once, felling adult palms was the most common technique used to harvest fruits, which negatively affected the demography of its population. Inconsistent regulations on O. bataua harvesting across different countries contributes to confusion and threatens sustainable use of this species. Colombia has a harvest quota; Ecuador requires management plans; Peru and Bolivia forbid killing the tree. However, in all cases enforcement is difficult. To support sustainable use, in some villages, adult palms are climbed when they are not too tall to cut racemes with ripe fruits, and such non-destructive harvest techniques may meet the increasing demand and maintain the populations. 
	Pequi (Caryocar brasiliense) is a native fruit from Brazil, found in the Amazon, Caatinga, Cerrado, and Atlantic rainforest regions, and has high potential for sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2052gg3336","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Guedes, Antoniassi, & de Faria-Machado, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Guedes, Antoniassi, & de Faria-Machado, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30764,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CEXEPQ69"],"itemData":{"id":30764,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Pequi is a native fruit from Brazil, found in the Amazon, Caatinga, Cerrado and Atlantic Rain Forest regions. It is one of the main plants with great potential for sustainable use in Central Brazil. Among 16 species comprising Caryocar genus, three are highlighted: C. brasiliense, C. villosum, and C. coriaceum, of economic importance for families in small communities of Brazilian Cerrado. They are generally organized in cooperatives and use the leaves for preparing medicinal extracts, and the fruits for culinary purposes. When the sale of fresh fruits is reduced, they produce and commercialize fruit preserves, and liqueur products, as well as extract the oil. Harvesting of mature fruits after falling from the tree while keeping some fruits at the plant is the best way for its sustainable production. The internal mesocarp contains from 36 to 66% dry weight of oil having traditional culinary, medicinal and cosmetic uses. This oil has 60% of oleic acid and 35% palmitic acid, thus suitable for industrial fat hardstocks. The presence of the triacylglycerols POO and POP makes the oil of interest for cosmetic and food industries. Both pulp and kernel oils have been studied for their health effects. By physical, enzymatic or chemical modi?cation, the oil has shown potential uses as cocoa butter substitute and zero trans fat product.","container-title":"OCL","DOI":"10.1051/ocl/2017040","ISSN":"2272-6977, 2257-6614","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"OCL","language":"en","page":"D507","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Pequi: a Brazilian fruit with potential uses for the fat industry","title-short":"Pequi","URL":"http://www.ocl-journal.org/10.1051/ocl/2017040","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Guedes","given":"Andréa Madalena Maciel"},{"family":"Antoniassi","given":"Rosemar"},{"family":"Faria-Machado","given":"Adelia Ferreira","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Pequi was harvested in 265 municipalities in the Cerrado ecoregion, which produced approximately 76 thousand tons. Finally, 42 thousand tons of pequi were harvested from 2012 to 2017.

* ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2npo6botvb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yetman, B\\\\uc0\\\\u250{}rquez Montijo, Hultine, Sanderson, & Crosswhite, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Yetman, Búrquez Montijo, Hultine, Sanderson, & Crosswhite, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30985,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PZEULKDS"],"itemData":{"id":30985,"type":"book","call-number":"QK495.C11","collection-title":"The Southwest Center series","event-place":"Tucson","ISBN":"978-0-8165-4125-6","number-of-pages":"1","publisher":"The University of Arizona Press","publisher-place":"Tucson","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The saguaro cactus: a natural history","title-short":"The saguaro cactus","author":[{"family":"Yetman","given":"David"},{"family":"Búrquez Montijo","given":"Alberto"},{"family":"Hultine","given":"Kevin"},{"family":"Sanderson","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Crosswhite","given":"Frank S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"asm0kos8pj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Paye, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Paye, 2000)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30761,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W2G55K2V"],"itemData":{"id":30761,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-0-89327-422-1","language":"en","note":"Google-Books-ID: 9_jqAAAAMAAJ","number-of-pages":"228","publisher":"New York Botanical Garden PressDept","source":"Google Books","title":"Cultural Uses of Plants: A Guide to Learning About Ethnobotany","title-short":"Cultural Uses of Plants","author":[{"family":"Paye","given":"Gabriell DeBear"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1uqv35fmsn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yetman, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Yetman, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30985,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PZEULKDS"],"itemData":{"id":30985,"type":"book","call-number":"QK495.C11","collection-title":"The Southwest Center series","event-place":"Tucson","ISBN":"978-0-8165-4125-6","number-of-pages":"1","publisher":"The University of Arizona Press","publisher-place":"Tucson","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The saguaro cactus: a natural history","title-short":"The saguaro cactus","author":[{"family":"Yetman","given":"David"},{"family":"Búrquez Montijo","given":"Alberto"},{"family":"Hultine","given":"Kevin"},{"family":"Sanderson","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Crosswhite","given":"Frank S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}Juicy Fruits
Berries and juicy tree fruits are harvested all over the world for personal, informal economic and formal economic use. In the United States of America people commonly harvest wild low-bush blueberries, wild raspberries, wild strawberries, and less commonly serviceberries (Amelanchier spp.), chokecherries (Prunus virginiana), and other species of wild cherry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1a13ss5nuj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30752,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PQNPRAMM"],"itemData":{"id":30752,"type":"report","collection-title":"General Technical Report SRS-GTR-232","language":"English","page":"267","publisher":"USDA Forest Service, Southern Research Station","title":"Assessment of Nontimber Forest Products in the United States Under Changing Conditions. A report for the United Sates Department of Agriculture","URL":"https://doi.org/10.2737/SRS-GTR-232","author":[{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"James. L."},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R."},{"family":"Patel-Weynand","given":"Toral"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Lingonberry or cowberry (Vaccinium vitis-idaea) is one of the most popular berries in American and European Nordic countries, and it is widely used in the human diet. It is a perennial evergreen shrub distributed in circumboreal regions of northern Eurasia and North America. Lingonberries are most commonly harvested by hand with berry rakes. Lingonberry is an important element of coniferous forests understories in terms of nature’s contributions to people, and it also has cultural and economic importance, linked to a rural lifestyle. Major lingonberry-exporting countries are Sweden, Finland, and the Russian Federation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a216s4j6lb6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Padmanabhan, Correa-Betanzo, & Paliyath, 2016; Pouta, Siev\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}nen, & Neuvonen, 2006; Woziwoda, Dyderski, & Jagodzi\\\\uc0\\\\u324{}ski, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Padmanabhan, Correa-Betanzo, & Paliyath, 2016; Pouta, Sievänen, & Neuvonen, 2006; Woziwoda, Dyderski, & Jagodzi?ski, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30937,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SYXCJUJY"],"itemData":{"id":30937,"type":"chapter","abstract":"Provides users with a solid bridge of current and accurate information spanning food production and processing, from distribution and consumption to health effects--","call-number":"RA784 .E476 2016","container-title":"Encyclopedia of food and health","event-place":"Amsterdam ; Boston","ISBN":"978-0-12-384947-2","note":"OCLC: ocn936554893","publisher":"Academic Press is an imprint of Elsevier","publisher-place":"Amsterdam ; Boston","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Berries and Related Fruits","editor":[{"family":"Caballero","given":"Benjamin"},{"family":"Finglas","given":"Paul M."},{"family":"Toldrá","given":"Fidel"}],"author":[{"family":"Padmanabhan","given":"P"},{"family":"Correa-Betanzo","given":"J"},{"family":"Paliyath","given":"P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33683,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DWNRZE39"],"itemData":{"id":33683,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"10.1080/08941920500519156","ISSN":"0894-1920, 1521-0723","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Society & Natural Resources","language":"en","page":"285-304","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recreational Wild Berry Picking in Finland—Reflection of a Rural Lifestyle","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/08941920500519156","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Pouta","given":"Eija"},{"family":"Sievänen","given":"Tuija"},{"family":"Neuvonen","given":"Marjo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",4]]}}},{"id":30983,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T8JIFYJJ"],"itemData":{"id":30983,"type":"report","abstract":"Abstract\\n          \\n            Background:\\n            Multi-purpose use of forests in a sustainable way forces a recognition of how alien woody species introduction in forests with different land use history affect native plants other than trees. Lingonberry\\n            Vaccinium vitis-idaea\\n            is an important understory component of temperate and boreal forests and provider of valuable non-wood forest products. Here we studied effects of land use changes and introduction of Northern red oak\\n            Quercus rubra\\n            on lingonberry in a mesic Scots pine (\\n            Pinus sylvestris\\n            ) forests (in central Poland). We measured lingonberry cover, height of shoots, biomass of stems and leaves, and fruit productivity. Shoots were collected within 200 research plots located in recent and ancient Scots pine forests, with and without\\n            Q. rubra\\n            .\\n            Results:\\n            We found that\\n            V. vitis-idaea\\n            reached lower cover, aboveground biomass and fruit production in recent than ancient forests and in forests with than without\\n            Q. rubra\\n            . The fruit production in recent pine forest was only 2% of that reported in ancient pine forest, and\\n            V. vitis-idaea\\n            did not reproduce generatively in forests with\\n            Q. rubra\\n            . Biomass and carbon sequestration of\\n            V. vitis-idaea\\n            in forests with alien (invasive) tree decreased by 75% compared to ancient pine forest. Effects were also clear at the individual shoot level – in less suitable conditions we found taller heights and higher biomass allocation into stems than foliage. Biomass allocation in fruiting and non-fruiting shoots in pine forests was also different – less of the dry biomass of fruiting shoots was allocated to leaves than to stems.\\n            Conclusions:\\n            In the age of high interest in ecosystem services and discussions about usage of alien tree species as alternatives in forest management, our results clearly indicate disruption of ecosystem services provided by\\n            V. vitis-idaea\\n            in the presence of\\n            Q. rubra\\n            . Lingonberry benefited from the continuity of forest land use, however, regardless of land-use legacy, alien tree introduction, led to decline in abundance of species crucial for ecosystem functioning. Therefore, to maintain valuable native species and for conservation of ecosystem services delivery, we suggest limiting the introducing\\n            Q. rubra\\n            in areas with abundant\\n            V. vitis-idaea\\n            , especially in forests with continuous forest land-use history.","genre":"preprint","note":"DOI: 10.21203/rs.3.rs-38732/v1","publisher":"In Review","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forest land use discontinuity and northern red oak Quercus rubra introduction change biomass allocation and life strategy of lingonberry Vaccinium vitis-idaea","URL":"https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-38732/v1","author":[{"family":"Woziwoda","given":"Beata"},{"family":"Dyderski","given":"Marcin K."},{"family":"Jagodzi?ski","given":"Andrzej M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A set of criteria and indicators were involved in assessing the commercial supply chain of bilberry in Finland, and suggested a lack of social sustainability due to decreasing involvement and consultations with forest owners and the local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a28r962jv2m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hamunen, Kurttila, Miina, Peltola, & Tikkanen, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Hamunen, Kurttila, Miina, Peltola, & Tikkanen, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30904,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K3WMC4Q5"],"itemData":{"id":30904,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"ARTN 102002 10.1016/j.forpol.2019.102002","ISSN":"1389-9341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy Econ","language":"English","title":"Sustainability of Nordic non-timber forest product-related businesses - A case study on bilberry","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Hamunen","given":"K."},{"family":"Kurttila","given":"M."},{"family":"Miina","given":"J."},{"family":"Peltola","given":"R."},{"family":"Tikkanen","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Lingonberries are most commonly harvested by hand with berry rakes. In Finland, 11-26 million kg of bilberries and lingonberries were gathered in the 1990s. It is estimated that over half of the population still participates in berry picking based on the Nordic allemannsretten or “everyman’s right”, which is a long-standing right to move through and share resources on both private and public lands, including the right to pick berries and mushrooms in communal areas. 
      Some estimates suggest utilization rates of the two most common berries, bilberry (Vaccinium myrtillus L.) and lingonberry are low (4–15% of the total annual yield of wild berries), making this a very sustainable activity. One study found that approximately 32% of the total harvested of berries were for commercial sale ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"an6r4946dv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Turtiainen, Salo, & Saastamoinen, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Turtiainen, Salo, & Saastamoinen, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33682,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T7TV39LG"],"itemData":{"id":33682,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Silva Fennica","DOI":"10.14214/sf.115","ISSN":"22424075","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Silva Fenn.","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Variations of yield and utilisation of bilberries (Vaccinium myrtillus L.) and cowberries (V. vitis-idaea L.) in Finland","URL":"http://www.silvafennica.fi/article/115","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Turtiainen","given":"Marjut"},{"family":"Salo","given":"Kauko"},{"family":"Saastamoinen","given":"Olli"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the demand for so called “super foods” has accelerated exports for global markets, and the volume of the Nordic wild berry harvest has doubled during the past two decades. Along with an increase in the market demand, lingonberry has been domesticated and commercially cultivated in several locations across Europe, Scandinavia, and also recently in the United States of America ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a9e886emlu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Forest Europe, 2020; Padmanabhan, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Forest Europe, 2020; Padmanabhan, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30938,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XNL3I4JK"],"itemData":{"id":30938,"type":"report","title":"State of Europe’s Forests 2020","URL":"www.foresteurope.org","author":[{"family":"Forest Europe","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30937,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SYXCJUJY"],"itemData":{"id":30937,"type":"chapter","abstract":"Provides users with a solid bridge of current and accurate information spanning food production and processing, from distribution and consumption to health effects--","call-number":"RA784 .E476 2016","container-title":"Encyclopedia of food and health","event-place":"Amsterdam ; Boston","ISBN":"978-0-12-384947-2","note":"OCLC: ocn936554893","publisher":"Academic Press is an imprint of Elsevier","publisher-place":"Amsterdam ; Boston","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Berries and Related Fruits","editor":[{"family":"Caballero","given":"Benjamin"},{"family":"Finglas","given":"Paul M."},{"family":"Toldrá","given":"Fidel"}],"author":[{"family":"Padmanabhan","given":"P"},{"family":"Correa-Betanzo","given":"J"},{"family":"Paliyath","given":"P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The land area covered in bilberry (Vaccinium myrtillus) bushes and locally harvested berries have declined in Central Italian Apennine regions in recent last decades ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1bcps7lqto","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nin, Petrucci, Del Bubba, Ancillotti, & Giordani, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Nin, Petrucci, Del Bubba, Ancillotti, & Giordani, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30911,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5334TSYQ"],"itemData":{"id":30911,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientia Horticulturae","DOI":"10.1016/j.scienta.2017.08.049","ISSN":"0304-4238","language":"English","page":"241-249","title":"Effects of environmental factors on seed germination and seedling establishment in bilberry (Vaccinium myrtillus L.)","volume":"226","author":[{"family":"Nin","given":"S."},{"family":"Petrucci","given":"W. A."},{"family":"Del Bubba","given":"M."},{"family":"Ancillotti","given":"C."},{"family":"Giordani","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Regular gatherers of bilberry in Estonia use clearly delineated picking areas, and typically do not share their areas outside close family relations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aefcf69h1s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Remm, Runkla, & Lohmus, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Remm, Runkla, & Lohmus, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30903,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q9DSBLSH"],"itemData":{"id":30903,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Baltic Forestry","ISSN":"1392-1355","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Balt For","language":"English","page":"287-295","title":"How Bilberry Pickers Use Estonian Forests: Implications for Sustaining a Non-Timber Value","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Remm","given":"L."},{"family":"Runkla","given":"M."},{"family":"Lohmus","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Bilberries are also a popular wild food in the Czech Republic, where the number of households involved in the gathering of wild fruits has increased in recent years. The ratio of participants and yield of bilberries are the highest in wild fruit ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"af2i69tipp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. Wolfslehner, 2019)","plainCitation":"(B. Wolfslehner, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30991,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IVV5KSPW"],"itemData":{"id":30991,"type":"report","publisher":"European  Forest Institute","title":"Non-wood forest products in Europe: Seeing the forest  around the trees. What Science Can Tell Us 10.","URL":"https://efi.int/sites/default/files/files/publication-bank/2019/efi_wsctu_10_2019.pdf","author":[{"family":"Wolfslehner","given":"B."},{"family":"Prokofieva","given":"I."},{"family":"Mavsar","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There is also a high demand on bilberries in France. The boom started in the late 1960s. At that time some gatherers in the Massif Central area increased the quantity of berries they were gathering by 500%. This gathering is regulated for non-residents ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2jhpaivc3r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Larr\\\\uc0\\\\u232{}re, 1982)","plainCitation":"(Larrère, 1982)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30978,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7J4KUUWS"],"itemData":{"id":30978,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Etudes Rurales","ISSN":"0014-2182","issue":"La chasse et la cueillette aujourd'hui","page":"191-208","title":"Des cueillettes, des conflits, des contrôles","URL":"https://www.persee.fr/doc/rural_0014-2182_1982_num_87_1_2883","volume":"87-88","author":[{"family":"Larrère","given":"Raphaël"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1982"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Most cacti produce edible fruit for humans but prickly pears of Opuntia species and fruits from Stenocereus, Cereus, Carnegiea and Pachycereus species are the most important for numerous peoples of the arid Americas (Box 3.8). Fruits are gathered from the wild, semi-cultivated and cultivated stands. People commonly make use of a tool called “chicole” which is a long stick with a kind of basket in the top. With “chicole” people reach and pull fruits without damaging them or injuring themselves or the wild plant. The fruits harvested are stored in a basket or bucket for transporting them to homes and markets. In agroforestry systems, people sometimes leave fruit-producing cacti because they favor their propagation and take special care of these valued plants. Some species, mainly Stenocereus, Cereus, Lemairocereus, are cultivated, and processes of domestication and generation of varieties associated to human selection have been documented in Mexico  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"am98s038k5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Casas & Barbera, 2002; Casas, Otero-Arnaiz, P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez-Negr\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}n, & Valiente-Banuet, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Casas & Barbera, 2002; Casas, Otero-Arnaiz, Pérez-Negrón, & Valiente-Banuet, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30779,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6NEAZCK5"],"itemData":{"id":30779,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Cacti. Biology and uses","edition":"Nobel, Park S.","event-place":"Berkeley & Los Angeles","page":"143-162","publisher":"University of California Press,","publisher-place":"Berkeley & Los Angeles","title":"Mesoamerican domestication and diffusion","author":[{"family":"Casas","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Barbera","given":"Giuseppe"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":30780,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q2BD6IIK"],"itemData":{"id":30780,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of Botany","DOI":"10.1093/aob/mcm126","issue":"5","page":"1101–1115","title":"In situ Management and Domestication of Plants in Mesoamerica","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/aob/article/100/5/1101/136433","volume":"100","author":[{"family":"Casas","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Otero-Arnaiz","given":"Adriana"},{"family":"Pérez-Negrón","given":"Edgar"},{"family":"Valiente-Banuet","given":"Alfonso"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Box 3.8 The many lives of a single plant. Based on ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2k3hc52614","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Paye, 2000, p. 142; Yetman, 2020, p. 69)","plainCitation":"(Paye, 2000, p. 142; Yetman, 2020, p. 69)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30761,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W2G55K2V"],"itemData":{"id":30761,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-0-89327-422-1","language":"en","note":"Google-Books-ID: 9_jqAAAAMAAJ","number-of-pages":"228","publisher":"New York Botanical Garden PressDept","source":"Google Books","title":"Cultural Uses of Plants: A Guide to Learning About Ethnobotany","title-short":"Cultural Uses of Plants","author":[{"family":"Paye","given":"Gabriell DeBear"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}},"locator":"142"},{"id":30985,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PZEULKDS"],"itemData":{"id":30985,"type":"book","call-number":"QK495.C11","collection-title":"The Southwest Center series","event-place":"Tucson","ISBN":"978-0-8165-4125-6","number-of-pages":"1","publisher":"The University of Arizona Press","publisher-place":"Tucson","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The saguaro cactus: a natural history","title-short":"The saguaro cactus","author":[{"family":"Yetman","given":"David"},{"family":"Búrquez Montijo","given":"Alberto"},{"family":"Hultine","given":"Kevin"},{"family":"Sanderson","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Crosswhite","given":"Frank S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"locator":"69"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

The Saguaro cactus (Carnegiea gigantea) grows in desert of Arizona, California, and Mexico, and can reach up to height of 15 meters (50 feet) with a life span of about 200 years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nwVNabFO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yetman, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Yetman, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30985,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PZEULKDS"],"itemData":{"id":30985,"type":"book","call-number":"QK495.C11","collection-title":"The Southwest Center series","event-place":"Tucson","ISBN":"978-0-8165-4125-6","number-of-pages":"1","publisher":"The University of Arizona Press","publisher-place":"Tucson","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The saguaro cactus: a natural history","title-short":"The saguaro cactus","author":[{"family":"Yetman","given":"David"},{"family":"Búrquez Montijo","given":"Alberto"},{"family":"Hultine","given":"Kevin"},{"family":"Sanderson","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Crosswhite","given":"Frank S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The fruits are harvested by O’odham Indians, who cook the pulp to make jam, candy, syrup, and wine, but the wild plant also plays an important role in the lives of many other organisms in these environments of which humans are a part. It takes 50 years for this cactus to bear flowers and fruits. The cactus provides shelter and food for numerous organisms throughout its life span. Carpenter birds and elf owls make nests in the fleshy body of the cactus, and Harris’s hawks build nests in the branches. Bats, doves, butterflies and bees enjoy the nectar when the cactus blooms during May. Many animals such as curved bill thrashers, horned lizards, coyotes, and javelin pigs also eat the fruits. As the cactus nears the end of its lifespan, aquatic beetles swim through the decomposing plant flesh. When the cactus is dead, it is home to termites, spiders, giant centipedes, banded geckos, cactus mice, and spotted night snakes.

* Beverages
Mead, Hyssop, Salep, teas, and wild coffees from dandelion greens and chicory are some of the many beverages people make from wild plants. The English term “tea” refers the infusion made of the leaves of Camellia sinensis but there are kinds of aromatic and refreshing beverages around the world. In Europe, 142 taxa of plants belonging to 99 genera and 40 families are reported the use of recreational tea ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a90s9283jo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S\\\\uc0\\\\u245{}ukand, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Sõukand, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31009,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SVXT3Y5E"],"itemData":{"id":31009,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper is a review of local plants used in water infusions as aromatic and refreshing hot beverages (recreational tea) consumed in food-related settings in Europe, and not for specific medicinal purposes. The reviewed 29 areas are located across Europe, covering the post-Soviet countries, eastern and Mediterranean Europe. Altogether, 142 taxa belonging to 99 genera and 40 families were reported. The most important families for making herbal tea in all research areas were Lamiaceae and Asteraceae, while Rosaceae was popular only in eastern and central Europe. With regards to botanical genera, the dominant taxa included Mentha, Tilia, Thymus, Origanum, Rubus and Matricaria. The clear favorite was Origanum vulgare L., mentioned in 61% of the regions. Regionally, other important taxa included Rubus idaeus L. in eastern Europe, Chamaemelum nobile (L.) All. in southern Europe and Rosa canina L. in central Europe. Future research on the pharmacological, nutritional and chemical properties of the plants most frequently used in the tea-making process is essential to ensure their safety and appropriateness for daily consumption. Moreover, regional studies dedicated to the study of local plants used for making recreational tea are important to improve our understanding of their selection criteria, cultural importance and perceived properties in Europe and abroad.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/1746-4269-9-58","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","page":"58","title":"Plants used for making recreational tea in Europe: a review based on specific research sites","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Sõukand","given":"Renata"},{"family":"Quave","given":"Cassandra L."},{"family":"Pieroni","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Pardo-de-Santayana","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Tardío","given":"Javier"},{"family":"Kalle","given":"Raivo"},{"family":"?uczaj","given":"?ukasz"},{"family":"Svanberg","given":"Ingvar"},{"family":"Kolosova","given":"Valeria"},{"family":"Aceituno-Mata","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Menendez-Baceta","given":"Gorka"},{"family":"Ko?odziejska-Degórska","given":"Iwona"},{"family":"Piro?nikow","given":"Ewa"},{"family":"Petkevi?ius","given":"Rolandas"},{"family":"Hajdari","given":"Avni"},{"family":"Mustafa","given":"Behxhet"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",8,13]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In China, 759 plant species have documented for use as teas, and a market survey identified an additional 23 species used as herbal tea ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2p5rg29jgi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fu, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Fu, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30918,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H977Q3IR"],"itemData":{"id":30918,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ethnopharmacological relevance Herbal teas have long been consumed by Chinese people for preventive and/or therapeutic healthcare. Although herbal teas are widely consumed by many cultural groups in different regions of China, no thorough review has been undertaken to assess the diversity of the country's herbal tea usage. This literature review, complemented by a quantitative survey in an important tea market in Kunming, begins to fill this knowledge gap. Aims of the study The study aims to summarize the current knowledge of plant species used as herbal teas by different cultural groups in different regions of China, with a focus on the teas’ perceived traditional healthcare functions, related phytochemical/pharmaceutical research, and safety issues. Materials and methods The study involved a comprehensive literature review and a market survey. The literature review was based on published ethnobotanical studies of herbal teas in China. We searched the Web of Science™, ELSEVIER, the China National Knowledge Infrastructure (CNKI) and the China Science and Technology Journal Database to locate relevant studies (including journal articles, Masters/PhD dissertations and books) that were published before March 2017. A species list was compiled based on the review and supplemented with information retrieved from the Scifinder database (https://scifinder.cas.org) and the Chinese Pharmacopoeia (2010). A Use Value Index was employed for ranking the most cited species. Based on the 29 most cited species, we discussed the current research status in relation to healthcare benefits and safety concerns of herbal teas in China. To better understand the current status of the herbal tea market in China, we also surveyed 136 tea vendors at the Xiongda Tea Market in Kunming. Information gathered from the survey included the species sold, the sale prices and the form of the herbal tea product. Results The literature identified 759 plant species used as herbal tea in China and the market survey identified an additional 23 species. Most of the species used were from the Leguminosae, Compositae and Lamiaceae families. Twenty two provinces and fourteen ethnic minority groups have records on the consumption of herbal teas. Southern China uses up to 82% of the total species, and 211 out of 759 species are used by minority groups. Thirty categories of traditional healthcare functions are linked with herbal teas, with clearing away heat, relieving toxicity and suppressing cough being the most important functions. There is phytochemical/pharmaceutical evidence to support the claimed healthcare benefits of some Chinese herbal teas. Although Chinese herbal teas are generally safe to consume, overdoses of some herbal teas and some unapproved mixtures of species may cause health risks. Based on our market survey, the prices of most herbal teas range between 100 and 200 RMB (US$15–30) per kg. Conclusions A rich array of herbal tea species with various traditional healthcare functions have long been used in China, and as such there is a huge market potential for Chinese herbal teas. More pharmaceutical/phytochemical research is needed to assess a wide range of perceived healthcare benefits of Chinese herbal teas. Our research highlights the need to study herbal teas through an ethnopharmacological perspective and by employing a holistic approach, which requires greater consideration of traditional knowledge in the pharmacological research design. Product safety and sustainability issues should also be considered, so the traditional applications of herbal teas can be transformed to efficient health boosting functional products.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jep.2018.04.026","ISSN":"0378-8741","page":"217-228","title":"A billion cups: The diversity, traditional uses, safety issues and potential of Chinese herbal teas","volume":"222","author":[{"family":"Fu","given":"Yao"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Jin-chao"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony B."},{"family":"Towns","given":"Alexandra Maria"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Yu"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Hua-ying"},{"family":"Li","given":"Jian-wen"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Xue-fei"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",8,10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The majority of wild plants used are perceived as medicinal plants in local folk medicine or “folk functional foods”. The status of the use of herbal tea is dependent on access to the natural resources, cultural and social contexts, and the habit of its use in the region and personal preferences of the consumer. 
	Salep is a beverage made from orchid tubers in Europe and central Asia. Harvesting wild orchid tubers for this purpose dates back to the medieval period. Six species of orchids are named as components of Salep. Tuber gathering for Salep has been cited as a cause of orchid population decline and causes conservation concern in Turkey and neighboring countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1l11mc7gvi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Charitonidou, Stara, Kougioumoutzis, & Halley, 2019; Ghorbani, Gravendeel, Naghibi, & de Boer, 2014; Kreziou, de Boer, & Gravendeel, 2016; Masters, van Andel, de Boer, Heijungs, & Gravendeel, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Charitonidou, Stara, Kougioumoutzis, & Halley, 2019; Ghorbani, Gravendeel, Naghibi, & de Boer, 2014; Kreziou, de Boer, & Gravendeel, 2016; Masters, van Andel, de Boer, Heijungs, & Gravendeel, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30909,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NUYYMAXN"],"itemData":{"id":30909,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Biological Research-Thessaloniki","DOI":"ARTN 18 10.1186/s40709-019-0110-1","ISSN":"1790-045x","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Biol Res-Thessalon","language":"English","title":"Implications of salep collection for the conservation of the Elder-flowered orchid (Dactylorhiza sambucina) in Epirus, Greece","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Charitonidou","given":"M."},{"family":"Stara","given":"K."},{"family":"Kougioumoutzis","given":"K."},{"family":"Halley","given":"J. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12,30]]}}},{"id":30907,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BKG5AICX"],"itemData":{"id":30907,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-014-0746-y","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"English","page":"2749-2760","title":"Wild orchid tuber collection in Iran: a wake-up call for conservation","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Ghorbani","given":"A."},{"family":"Gravendeel","given":"B."},{"family":"Naghibi","given":"F."},{"family":"Boer","given":"H.","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",10]]}}},{"id":30905,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9A68WKSD"],"itemData":{"id":30905,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605315000265","ISSN":"0030-6053","issue":"3","language":"English","page":"393-396","title":"Harvesting of salep orchids in north-western Greece continues to threaten natural populations","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Kreziou","given":"A."},{"family":"Boer","given":"H.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Gravendeel","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",7]]}}},{"id":30906,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TGPMBV92"],"itemData":{"id":30906,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"UNSP 108454 10.1016/j.biocon.2020.108454","ISSN":"0006-3207","language":"English","title":"Patent analysis as a novel method for exploring commercial interest in wild harvested species","volume":"243","author":[{"family":"Masters","given":"S."},{"family":"Andel","given":"T.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Boer","given":"H. J.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Heijungs","given":"R."},{"family":"Gravendeel","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Scientists and conservationists recommend cessation of wild orchids harvest for this purpose ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aj1gg09sis","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ghorbani, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Ghorbani, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30907,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BKG5AICX"],"itemData":{"id":30907,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-014-0746-y","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"English","page":"2749-2760","title":"Wild orchid tuber collection in Iran: a wake-up call for conservation","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Ghorbani","given":"A."},{"family":"Gravendeel","given":"B."},{"family":"Naghibi","given":"F."},{"family":"Boer","given":"H.","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

* Syrups, Gums and Resins
Indigenous tribes in Eastern North America know the sap of maples (Acer spp.) and call it “sweet water.” When the first European explorers and colonists arrived, they learned of maple sap and boiling the sap down to produce syrup or sugar. Sugar maple (Acer saccharum) is the species most frequently tapped for sap production. Under the best conditions, sugar maples reach a tappable size in about 40 years and can continue to produce sap for a century ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1gb2h7tqle","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ciesla, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Ciesla, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30961,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DEBM9I8K"],"itemData":{"id":30961,"type":"book","collection-number":"15","collection-title":"Non-wood forest products","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-104855-9","language":"eng","number-of-pages":"125","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"K10plus ISBN","title":"Non-wood forest products from temperate broad-leaved trees","author":[{"family":"Ciesla","given":"William M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. During the maple sugaring season, which lasts about six weeks in spring, an average maple tree will yield between 35 and 50 liters of sap, which will produce between 1 and 1.5 liter of pure maple syrup ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yerVvBj3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ciesla, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Ciesla, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30961,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DEBM9I8K"],"itemData":{"id":30961,"type":"book","collection-number":"15","collection-title":"Non-wood forest products","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-104855-9","language":"eng","number-of-pages":"125","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"K10plus ISBN","title":"Non-wood forest products from temperate broad-leaved trees","author":[{"family":"Ciesla","given":"William M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Maple syrup is produced only in the Eastern United States of America and Canada. Maple syrup production is a hobby that connects people to nature, provides supplementary income for farmers, and is an important cultural practice for indigenous peoples ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2545n70obb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Weiss, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Weiss, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30934,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WL4K9XD2"],"itemData":{"id":30934,"type":"chapter","collection-number":"No 10","collection-title":"What science can tell us","container-title":"Non-wood forest products in Europe: seeing the forest around the trees","event-place":"Joensuu","ISBN":"978-952-5980-77-6","language":"eng","publisher":"European Forest Institute","publisher-place":"Joensuu","source":"K10plus ISBN","title":"Rendering NWFPs innovative","editor":[{"family":"Wolfslehner","given":"Bernhard"},{"family":"Prokofieva","given":"Irina"},{"family":"Mavsar","given":"Robert"}],"author":[{"family":"Weiss","given":"G"},{"family":"Ludvig","given":"A"},{"family":"Asamer-Handler","given":"M"},{"family":"Fischer","given":"C R"},{"family":"Vacik","given":"H"},{"family":"Zivojinovic","given":"I"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As a large-scale commodity, maple syrup is a luxury item consumed worldwide (Figure 3.41). The largest market for syrup is in the United States of America. Since the late 19th century, maple production in the United States of America has declined while that in Canada has increased. With sugar maple (Acer saccharum Marshall) often distributed throughout the region’s forests, only a small percentage of potentially tappable trees are in use for maple syrup production (an estimated 0.4% in the United States of America; Ciesla 2002, Farrell and Chabot 2012). Maple syrup production is weather dependent and expected to be heavily affected by climate change, with the potential for it to be eliminated in southern reaches of its current distribution peaked in the 19th century, reaching a record 25,032,928 liters of maple syrup in 1860. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a242d4bhb8q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Iverson & Matthews, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Iverson & Matthews, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30936,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NNAX866L"],"itemData":{"id":30936,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Chamberlain J, Emery MR, Patel-Waynand T (eds)","page":"249–251","title":"Appendix 2: Assessment of risk due to climate change: Sugar maple (Acer saccharum Marshall)","author":[{"family":"Iverson","given":"L"},{"family":"Matthews","given":"S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.


Figure 3.41 Maple Syrup Production in the United States of America and Canada 1860-2010. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"au6bv34ihc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Farrell & Chabot, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Farrell & Chabot, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30754,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FAPHDSQ7"],"itemData":{"id":30754,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Agriculture, Food Systems, and Community Development","DOI":"10.5304/jafscd.2012.022.009","ISSN":"21520801","journalAbbreviation":"JAFSCD","page":"11-27","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Assessing the growth potential and economic impact of the U.S. maple syrup industry","URL":"https://foodsystemsjournal.org/index.php/fsj/article/view/79","author":[{"family":"Farrell","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Chabot","given":"Brian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",8,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",3,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY 4.0.

      In Europe, the main sources of tree sap are silver and downy birch trees (Betula pendula Roth and Betula pubescens Ehrh). Birch sap is colorless or slightly opalescent. It is used as a traditional drink, in traditional medicine, in veterinary medicine and as a cosmetic product. Gathering sap from birch and other trees was more widespread in earlier times. In Russia, Ukraine, Belarus, Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania it remains a more common practice. The most productive silver birch trees for sap gathering are those taller than 28 m. A birch tree can produce 36l gallons of sap in nine days. Experiments conducted in Estonia in the 1970s showed that the profit gained from the sap was six times the profit gained from timber. More recently birch sap is becoming a more commercial product, and is of interest to pharmaceutical companies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ap4npil8bh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grabek-Lejko, Kasprzyk, Zagu\\\\\\\\la, & Puchalski, 2017; Mingaila, 2020; Svanberg, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Grabek-Lejko, Kasprzyk, Zagu\\\\la, & Puchalski, 2017; Mingaila, 2020; Svanberg, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30932,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RPA9M2JH"],"itemData":{"id":30932,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Baltic Forestry","issue":"1","title":"The bioactive and mineral compounds in birch sap collected in different types of habitats","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Grabek-Lejko","given":"Dorota"},{"family":"Kasprzyk","given":"Idalia"},{"family":"Zagu\\\\la","given":"GRZEGORZ"},{"family":"Puchalski","given":"CZES\\\\LAW"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":30935,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HJNTAX7K"],"itemData":{"id":30935,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Birch sap is colourless or slightly opalescent and is traditionally drunk in spring. Currently, birch sap is becoming more important in the market sector as well as to pharmacy companies due to its biochemical composition and use in a wide variety of products. To extract good quality sap using birch resources in a sustainable way, there is a need to investigate the influence of the dendrometric parameters of birch trees and soil properties on the quantity and chemical composition of birch sap. This study is performed in five silver birch (Betula pendula Roth) forest stands growing in Histosol, Luvisol and Arenosol with different moisture and nutrient contents. The results indicated that the most productive silver birch trees for sap harvesting were taller than 28 m, had a diameter at breast height over 40 cm and a crown base height greater than 19 m. Additionally, the highest quantity of birch sap was harvested from trees growing in well-aerated mineral soils (Arenosol and Luvisol) with normal moisture content. However, the sweetest birch sap was harvested from trees growing in nutrient-rich organic (undrained peatland Histosol) and temporarily flooded mineral (Luvisol) soils.","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f11040365","ISSN":"1999-4907","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Forests","language":"en","page":"365","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Quantity and Biochemical Composition of Sap Collected from Silver Birch (Betula pendula Roth) Trees Growing in Different Soils","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/1999-4907/11/4/365","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Mingaila","given":"Justas"},{"family":"?iuldien?","given":"Dovil?"},{"family":"Viškelis","given":"Pranas"},{"family":"Bartkevi?ius","given":"Edmundas"},{"family":"Vilimas","given":"Vladas"},{"family":"Armolaitis","given":"K?stutis"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3,25]]}}},{"id":30933,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7B993ZJA"],"itemData":{"id":30933,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Acta Societatis Botanicorum Poloniae","issue":"4","note":"publisher: -","title":"Uses of tree saps in northern and eastern parts of Europe","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"Svanberg","given":"Ingvar"},{"family":"Sõukand","given":"Renata"},{"family":"Luczaj","given":"Lukasz"},{"family":"Kalle","given":"Raivo"},{"family":"Zyryanova","given":"Olga"},{"family":"Dénes","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Papp","given":"Nóra"},{"family":"Nedelcheva","given":"Aneli"},{"family":"Seskauskaite","given":"Daiva"},{"family":"Kolodziejska-Degorska","given":"Iwona"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Gum Arabic or acacia gum is a tree gum exudate gathered from a number of Acacia species and has been an important part of commerce since ancient times. Gum Arabic is used in food and drink industries, in pharmaceuticals and in printing and textile industries as thickening, stabilizing, binding and sizing agents. Gum resin products are harvested from natural exudates by herdsmen, women and children while herding and doing other activities. Yields of gum Arabic from individual trees are very variable. A tree yields an average of 250g of gum per season. Very small proportions of gum enter the local market, but some is directly sold as a road-side snack in some West African countries, in Niger for example (E.S. Barron personal observation). African countries export about 100,000 tons of gum Arabic annually, and demand was previously projected to reach 150,000 tons by 2020. The European Union is responsible for 80% of global trade in gum Arabic, worth around 125 million euros. Sudan is one of the biggest gum Arabic producers in the world and produces more than 80% of the total world gum Arabic ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"asa1ltn71t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wubalem Tadesse, 2020; B. Wolfslehner, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Wubalem Tadesse, 2020; B. Wolfslehner, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30984,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BJZEG35V"],"itemData":{"id":30984,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The drylands of Ethiopia are well known for their natural gum and resin producing tree and shrub species such as Acacia, Boswellia, Commiphora and Sterculia. The production and trade volumes of gums and resins in the country showed a declining trend since 2010. The present review work is focused on availability of alternative underutilized tree and shrub species to indicate the diverse gum and resin market opportunities. Several Sub-Saharan African countries are producing gum and resin products from diversified species. Gum arabic is collected from Acacia senegal (L) Willd, Acacia seyal DEL, and Acacia polyacantha Willd species. Three countries namely Sudan, Nigeria, Chad contribute about 97% to the international market, while Ethiopia's contribution is 0.9%. World demand for karaya gum from Sterculia setigera DEL is about 7,000 tonne and in Africa, Senegal is the leading exporter. Despite the huge resources of A. senegal, A. seyal and A. polyacantha, Ethiopia producing very low quantity, and gum is collected from natural oozes of trunks or branches. S. setigera is also found in the country, although gum karaya is not yet under production. South and south eastern parts of Ethiopia hosts abundant species of Acacia, Boswellia and Commiphora. Gum-resin products are collected from natural exudates by herdsmen, women and children while herding and doing other activities, indicating its adverse effects on quality and quantity. Very small proportions of Myrrh and gum opopanax enter the local market. Other constraints are, lack of appropriate institutions, infrastructure, tapping technologies and market information. Therefore, appropriate policy formulation, research and development interventions, are recommended for supporting sustainable management, production and marketing of products.","container-title":"World Journal of Agricultural Research","DOI":"10.12691/wjar-8-2-2","issue":"2","page":"32-38","source":"www.sciagriculturalresearch.com","title":"Underutilized Natural Gum and Resin Resources in Ethiopia for Future Directions and Commercial Utilization","URL":"http://www.sciagriculturalresearch.com/articles/8/2/2","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Tadesse","given":"Wubalem"},{"family":"Dejene","given":"Tatek"},{"family":"Zeleke","given":"Gizachew"},{"family":"Desalegn","given":"Getachew"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,15]]}}},{"id":30991,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IVV5KSPW"],"itemData":{"id":30991,"type":"report","publisher":"European  Forest Institute","title":"Non-wood forest products in Europe: Seeing the forest  around the trees. What Science Can Tell Us 10.","URL":"https://efi.int/sites/default/files/files/publication-bank/2019/efi_wsctu_10_2019.pdf","author":[{"family":"Wolfslehner","given":"B."},{"family":"Prokofieva","given":"I."},{"family":"Mavsar","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Table 3.9).
      
Table 3.9 Exports of Gum Arabic (tons) from different African countries 2001-2010. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a12d0q6io04","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wubalem Tadesse, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Wubalem Tadesse, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30984,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BJZEG35V"],"itemData":{"id":30984,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The drylands of Ethiopia are well known for their natural gum and resin producing tree and shrub species such as Acacia, Boswellia, Commiphora and Sterculia. The production and trade volumes of gums and resins in the country showed a declining trend since 2010. The present review work is focused on availability of alternative underutilized tree and shrub species to indicate the diverse gum and resin market opportunities. Several Sub-Saharan African countries are producing gum and resin products from diversified species. Gum arabic is collected from Acacia senegal (L) Willd, Acacia seyal DEL, and Acacia polyacantha Willd species. Three countries namely Sudan, Nigeria, Chad contribute about 97% to the international market, while Ethiopia's contribution is 0.9%. World demand for karaya gum from Sterculia setigera DEL is about 7,000 tonne and in Africa, Senegal is the leading exporter. Despite the huge resources of A. senegal, A. seyal and A. polyacantha, Ethiopia producing very low quantity, and gum is collected from natural oozes of trunks or branches. S. setigera is also found in the country, although gum karaya is not yet under production. South and south eastern parts of Ethiopia hosts abundant species of Acacia, Boswellia and Commiphora. Gum-resin products are collected from natural exudates by herdsmen, women and children while herding and doing other activities, indicating its adverse effects on quality and quantity. Very small proportions of Myrrh and gum opopanax enter the local market. Other constraints are, lack of appropriate institutions, infrastructure, tapping technologies and market information. Therefore, appropriate policy formulation, research and development interventions, are recommended for supporting sustainable management, production and marketing of products.","container-title":"World Journal of Agricultural Research","DOI":"10.12691/wjar-8-2-2","issue":"2","page":"32-38","source":"www.sciagriculturalresearch.com","title":"Underutilized Natural Gum and Resin Resources in Ethiopia for Future Directions and Commercial Utilization","URL":"http://www.sciagriculturalresearch.com/articles/8/2/2","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Tadesse","given":"Wubalem"},{"family":"Dejene","given":"Tatek"},{"family":"Zeleke","given":"Gizachew"},{"family":"Desalegn","given":"Getachew"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC-BY 4.0.
