      Karaya gum is produced as an exudate from the genus Sterculia including Sterculia urens tree found in India and Sterculia setigera found in Africa and is used for many industries. World demand for karaya gum is about 7,000 tons, and Senegal is the leading exporter in Africa. The population of karaya trees once markedly declined due to crude traditional tapping methods which lead to the death of the tapped trees and over exploitation. Scientific tapping and proper harvesting methods are now priorities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a5b6plhme9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nair, 2004; Wubalem Tadesse, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Nair, 2004; Wubalem Tadesse, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30931,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A7QT6SPT"],"itemData":{"id":30931,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sterculiaceae using Ethephon. US Forest Service Pacific Northwest Research Station General Technical Report PNW GTR","page":"69–73","title":"Gum tapping in Sterculia urens Roxb","volume":"604","author":[{"family":"Nair","given":"MNB"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":30984,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BJZEG35V"],"itemData":{"id":30984,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The drylands of Ethiopia are well known for their natural gum and resin producing tree and shrub species such as Acacia, Boswellia, Commiphora and Sterculia. The production and trade volumes of gums and resins in the country showed a declining trend since 2010. The present review work is focused on availability of alternative underutilized tree and shrub species to indicate the diverse gum and resin market opportunities. Several Sub-Saharan African countries are producing gum and resin products from diversified species. Gum arabic is collected from Acacia senegal (L) Willd, Acacia seyal DEL, and Acacia polyacantha Willd species. Three countries namely Sudan, Nigeria, Chad contribute about 97% to the international market, while Ethiopia's contribution is 0.9%. World demand for karaya gum from Sterculia setigera DEL is about 7,000 tonne and in Africa, Senegal is the leading exporter. Despite the huge resources of A. senegal, A. seyal and A. polyacantha, Ethiopia producing very low quantity, and gum is collected from natural oozes of trunks or branches. S. setigera is also found in the country, although gum karaya is not yet under production. South and south eastern parts of Ethiopia hosts abundant species of Acacia, Boswellia and Commiphora. Gum-resin products are collected from natural exudates by herdsmen, women and children while herding and doing other activities, indicating its adverse effects on quality and quantity. Very small proportions of Myrrh and gum opopanax enter the local market. Other constraints are, lack of appropriate institutions, infrastructure, tapping technologies and market information. Therefore, appropriate policy formulation, research and development interventions, are recommended for supporting sustainable management, production and marketing of products.","container-title":"World Journal of Agricultural Research","DOI":"10.12691/wjar-8-2-2","issue":"2","page":"32-38","source":"www.sciagriculturalresearch.com","title":"Underutilized Natural Gum and Resin Resources in Ethiopia for Future Directions and Commercial Utilization","URL":"http://www.sciagriculturalresearch.com/articles/8/2/2","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Tadesse","given":"Wubalem"},{"family":"Dejene","given":"Tatek"},{"family":"Zeleke","given":"Gizachew"},{"family":"Desalegn","given":"Getachew"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
* Wild edible mushrooms 
More than 350 species coming from 18 orders of fungi are commonly eaten as food ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qWm9ygIw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Willis, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Willis, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30357,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MX282NHI"],"itemData":{"id":30357,"type":"book","event-place":"Richmond, UK","publisher":"Kew Publishing","publisher-place":"Richmond, UK","title":"State of the world's fungi 2018. Report.","author":[{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."}],"editor":[{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The number of used wild edible mushrooms is likely much higher than that based on lists and assessments from individual countries, e.g., over 1000 species of edible mushrooms are listed in China ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2L5SZxWp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. Wu, 2019)","plainCitation":"(F. Wu, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33297,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/32SRJ9EQ"],"itemData":{"id":33297,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fungal Diversity","DOI":"10.1007/s13225-019-00432-7","ISSN":"1560-2745, 1878-9129","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fungal Diversity","language":"en","page":"1-76","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Resource diversity of Chinese macrofungi: edible, medicinal and poisonous species","title-short":"Resource diversity of Chinese macrofungi","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s13225-019-00432-7","volume":"98","author":[{"family":"Wu","given":"Fang"},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Li-Wei"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Zhu-Liang"},{"family":"Bau","given":"Tolgor"},{"family":"Li","given":"Tai-Hui"},{"family":"Dai","given":"Yu-Cheng"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, 371 in Mexico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aec8l7h8vv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Moreno Fuentes, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Moreno Fuentes, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30995,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9US8DQDM"],"itemData":{"id":30995,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Anales de Antropología","DOI":"10.1016/S0185-1225(14)70496-5","ISSN":"01851225","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Anales de Antropología","language":"es","page":"241-272","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Un recurso alimentario de los grupos originarios y mestizos de méxico: los hongos silvestres","title-short":"Un recurso alimentario de los grupos originarios y mestizos de méxico","URL":"http://www.revistas.unam.mx/index.php/antropologia/article/view/44200","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Moreno Fuentes","given":"Ángel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and 268 species are traded in Europe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OOYqCRmY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Peintner, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Peintner, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30361,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDHTF9ZC"],"itemData":{"id":30361,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0063926","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e63926","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Mycophilic or Mycophobic? Legislation and Guidelines on Wild Mushroom Commerce Reveal Different Consumption Behaviour in European Countries","title-short":"Mycophilic or Mycophobic?","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0063926","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Peintner","given":"Ursula"},{"family":"Schwarz","given":"Stefanie"},{"family":"Meši?","given":"Armin"},{"family":"Moreau","given":"Pierre-Arthur"},{"family":"Moreno","given":"Gabriel"},{"family":"Saviuc","given":"Philippe"}],"editor":[{"family":"Fairhead","given":"Cecile"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",5,21]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The last comprehensive global assessment was conducted in 2004 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oLVTIMva","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Boa, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Boa, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30355,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8JG5XKPZ"],"itemData":{"id":30355,"type":"book","abstract":"Paper discusses traditional and contemporary uses of fungi as food or in medicine. Reviews the charactersitics of fungi biology and ecology, as well as fungi management","call-number":"QK617 .B713 2004","collection-number":"17","collection-title":"Non-wood forest products","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-105157-3","note":"OCLC: ocm57121299","number-of-pages":"147","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild edible fungi: a global overview of their use and importance to people","title-short":"Wild edible fungi","author":[{"family":"Boa","given":"E. R."}],"contributor":[{"family":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and given the high rates of taxonomic discovery among fungi, including of useful species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WW7wbj8h","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Dentinger & Suz, 2014; Willis, 2018; F. Wu, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Dentinger & Suz, 2014; Willis, 2018; F. Wu, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FHS25U8T"],"itemData":{"id":30354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.570","ISSN":"2167-8359","language":"en","page":"e570","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"What’s for dinner? Undescribed species of porcini in a commercial packet","title-short":"What’s for dinner?","URL":"https://peerj.com/articles/570","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Dentinger","given":"Bryn T.M."},{"family":"Suz","given":"Laura M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",9,16]]}}},{"id":30357,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MX282NHI"],"itemData":{"id":30357,"type":"book","event-place":"Richmond, UK","publisher":"Kew Publishing","publisher-place":"Richmond, UK","title":"State of the world's fungi 2018. Report.","author":[{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."}],"editor":[{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":33297,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/32SRJ9EQ"],"itemData":{"id":33297,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fungal Diversity","DOI":"10.1007/s13225-019-00432-7","ISSN":"1560-2745, 1878-9129","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fungal Diversity","language":"en","page":"1-76","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Resource diversity of Chinese macrofungi: edible, medicinal and poisonous species","title-short":"Resource diversity of Chinese macrofungi","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s13225-019-00432-7","volume":"98","author":[{"family":"Wu","given":"Fang"},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Li-Wei"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Zhu-Liang"},{"family":"Bau","given":"Tolgor"},{"family":"Li","given":"Tai-Hui"},{"family":"Dai","given":"Yu-Cheng"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, a re-evaluation is overdue. Wild mushrooms are harvested for food in over 80 countries worldwide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aavpt0lgt3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pieroni, Nebel, Santoro, & Heinrick, 2005a)","plainCitation":"(Pieroni, Nebel, Santoro, & Heinrick, 2005a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30751,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8BSN6BKA"],"itemData":{"id":30751,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Food Sciences and Nutrition","page":"245-272","title":"Food for Two Seasons: Culinary Uses of Non-Cultivated Local Vegetables and Mushrooms in a South Italian Village","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Pieroni","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Nebel","given":"Sabine"},{"family":"Santoro","given":"Franco R."},{"family":"Heinrick","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Among wild-harvested fungi, most commonly consumed and traded are Chanterelles (Cantharellus spp.), Porcini (Boletus spp.). Truffles (Tuber spp.) Morels (Morchella spp.), Brittlegills (Russula spp.), Milkcaps (Lactarius spp.), Button mushroom (Agaricus spp.), and Matsutake (Tricoloma spp.). Wild edible mushrooms can be found in over 200 genera, and grow in a wide variety of habitats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WOklWeDP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Boa, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Boa, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30355,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8JG5XKPZ"],"itemData":{"id":30355,"type":"book","abstract":"Paper discusses traditional and contemporary uses of fungi as food or in medicine. Reviews the charactersitics of fungi biology and ecology, as well as fungi management","call-number":"QK617 .B713 2004","collection-number":"17","collection-title":"Non-wood forest products","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-105157-3","note":"OCLC: ocm57121299","number-of-pages":"147","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild edible fungi: a global overview of their use and importance to people","title-short":"Wild edible fungi","author":[{"family":"Boa","given":"E. R."}],"contributor":[{"family":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many of the most popular used species form symbiotic relationships, making them difficult if not impossible to cultivate. For example, all of the above-listed genera (with the exception of button mushrooms) form ectomycorrhizal symbioses with trees, while Termitomyces spp. which are widely consumed across Africa and Asia are symbionts of termites ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"au08ckn53i","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Boa, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Boa, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30355,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8JG5XKPZ"],"itemData":{"id":30355,"type":"book","abstract":"Paper discusses traditional and contemporary uses of fungi as food or in medicine. Reviews the charactersitics of fungi biology and ecology, as well as fungi management","call-number":"QK617 .B713 2004","collection-number":"17","collection-title":"Non-wood forest products","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-105157-3","note":"OCLC: ocm57121299","number-of-pages":"147","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild edible fungi: a global overview of their use and importance to people","title-short":"Wild edible fungi","author":[{"family":"Boa","given":"E. R."}],"contributor":[{"family":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Popular saprotrophic species include button mushrooms, straw mushrooms (Volvariella spp.), shitake (Letinula edodes) and oyster mushrooms (Pleurotus spp.), although these species are cultivated at large scale ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ahie13s41b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Boa, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Boa, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30355,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8JG5XKPZ"],"itemData":{"id":30355,"type":"book","abstract":"Paper discusses traditional and contemporary uses of fungi as food or in medicine. Reviews the charactersitics of fungi biology and ecology, as well as fungi management","call-number":"QK617 .B713 2004","collection-number":"17","collection-title":"Non-wood forest products","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-105157-3","note":"OCLC: ocm57121299","number-of-pages":"147","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild edible fungi: a global overview of their use and importance to people","title-short":"Wild edible fungi","author":[{"family":"Boa","given":"E. R."}],"contributor":[{"family":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, they are also frequently harvested in the wild, for example in Malaysia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a7ig17godp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fui, Saikim, Kulip, & Seelan, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Fui, Saikim, Kulip, & Seelan, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30744,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MHJUEV2T"],"itemData":{"id":30744,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Tropical Biology & Conservation (JTBC)","page":"203â–222","title":"Distribution and ethnomycological knowledge of wild edible mushrooms in Sabah (Northern Borneo), Malaysia","author":[{"family":"Fui","given":"Foo She"},{"family":"Saikim","given":"Fiffy Hanisdah"},{"family":"Kulip","given":"Julius"},{"family":"Seelan","given":"Jaya Seelan Sathiya"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Benin ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1qsrjrq76r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Codjia & Yorou, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Codjia & Yorou, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30742,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ITIX6IMV"],"itemData":{"id":30742,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Biosciences","DOI":"10.4314/jab.v78i1.14","ISSN":"1997-5902","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J. App. Bioscience.","page":"6729","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ethnicity and gender variability in the diversity, recognition and exploitation of Wild Useful Fungi in Pobè region (Benin, West Africa)","URL":"http://www.ajol.info/index.php/jab/article/view/105853","volume":"78","author":[{"family":"Codjia","given":"J E"},{"family":"Yorou","given":"N S"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",7,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Mexico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2q10cdt6ru","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Haro-Luna, Ruan-Soto, & Guzm\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}n-D\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}valos, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Haro-Luna, Ruan-Soto, & Guzmán-Dávalos, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30743,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EQXYQ5MT"],"itemData":{"id":30743,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              The relationship between humans and nature is defined by culture. Accordingly, the use, conceptions, and perceptions of resources differ among cultural groups, even among those inhabiting the same region or those who come into contact with the same biota. In particular, mushrooms evoke a wide range of sentiments. During ethnobiological tours in Mexico, semi-structured interviews were carried out with 37 individuals of each community, from ten Wixarika and mestizo communities, living in the same locality and sharing similar resources, in the municipality of Villa Guerrero in northern Jalisco, Mexico. Furthermore, informal interviews with four Wixarika and five mestizo key informants were conducted. The topics treated were regarding the traditional nomenclature and classification, uses, and knowledge of mushrooms and related practices. Wixarika names of 37 mushroom species with edible, medicinal, and recreational uses were recorded. In addition, the Wixaritari were found to associate toxic mushrooms with the divine, as evidenced by one case of the use of mushrooms as a hierophanic agent. Each culture’s knowledge of the phenology and ecology of mushrooms was recorded in addition to data highlighting the cultural exchange between the Wixaritari and mestizos. However, a loss in the knowledge and practices concerning mushrooms was observed as a result of social changes. Even so, both cultures prefer mushrooms to other foods, including meat, especially\\n              Volvariella bombycina\\n              and\\n              Pleurotus djamor\\n              .","container-title":"IMA Fungus","DOI":"10.1186/s43008-019-0014-6","ISSN":"2210-6359","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"IMA Fungus","language":"en","page":"16","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Traditional knowledge, uses, and perceptions of mushrooms among the Wixaritari and mestizos of Villa Guerrero, Jalisco, Mexico","URL":"https://IMAFungus.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s43008-019-0014-6","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Haro-Luna","given":"Mara Ximena"},{"family":"Ruan-Soto","given":"Felipe"},{"family":"Guzmán-Dávalos","given":"Laura"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or Italy ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"atbae1in6h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pieroni, Nebel, Santoro, & Heinrick, 2005b)","plainCitation":"(Pieroni, Nebel, Santoro, & Heinrick, 2005b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"ERpTB65M/1DzRVql9","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B9KJZGKJ"],"itemData":{"id":"lOfcmtfD/i7qKzhnm","type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Food Sciences and Nutrition","issue":"4","page":"245-272","title":"Food for Two Seasons: Culinary Uses of Non-Cultivated Local Vegetables and Mushrooms in a South Italian Village","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Pieroni","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Nebel","given":"Sabine"},{"family":"Santoro","given":"Franco R."},{"family":"Heinrick","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      To assess status and trends of wild useful fungi, literature searches were conducted via a variety of search engines (Google Scholar, EBSCO Host and SCOPUS). To this end a Google Scholar search with the terms “(gathering OR collecting OR picking OR hunting OR foraging) AND (mushroom OR lichen OR fungi) AND sustainable AND wild” served as the basis and variations in the combinations of these terms, as well as supplementation with the different use categories (e.g., ceremonial, medicinal, food) were used until 50% saturation of articles already in the database were reached. In total, 112 sources were reviewed (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review for the gathering of fungi at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4659811).  
      The extent of usage of different species varies widely. Typically, ethnomycological studies report the use of tens to hundreds of species where a majority is harvested for personal use, gifting or barter (based on 20 articles from the literature review). A smaller number of popular species is sold at local and regional markets, while select species, often global commodities, are sold on to middlemen and traders to enter national and international markets (based on 9 articles from the literature review). This phenomenon is particularly well-documented in Mexico. For example, the Mazahua people use 31 species of wild mushrooms, of which 18 are sold in local or regional markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3Ui7PDKW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Farf\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}n, Casas, Ibarra-Manr\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}quez, & P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez-Negr\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}n, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Farfán, Casas, Ibarra-Manríquez, & Pérez-Negrón, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30734,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4FEYVGM9"],"itemData":{"id":30734,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1663/0013-0001(2007)61[173:MEASIT]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0013-0001","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Economic Botany","language":"en","page":"173-191","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Mazahua Ethnobotany and Subsistence in the Monarch Butterfly Biosphere Reserve, Mexico","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1663/0013-0001(2007)61[173:MEASIT]2.0.CO;2","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Farfán","given":"Berenice"},{"family":"Casas","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Ibarra-Manríquez","given":"Guillermo"},{"family":"Pérez-Negrón","given":"Edgar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The less popular species are also sometimes sold in mixed species bags, while a handful of highly-prized species including Amanita caesarea complex, porcini, morels, chanterelles and matsutake are targeted for export ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SXpy8rhG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Montoya, Hern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez, Mapes, Kong, & Estrada\\\\uc0\\\\u8211{}Torres, 2008; P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez-Moreno, Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}nez-Reyes, Yescas-P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez, Delgado-Alvarado, & Xoconostle-C\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}zares, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Montoya, Hernández, Mapes, Kong, & Estrada–Torres, 2008; Pérez-Moreno, Martínez-Reyes, Yescas-Pérez, Delgado-Alvarado, & Xoconostle-Cázares, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QU5SVZSE"],"itemData":{"id":30733,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9021-z","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"413-424","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Collection and Sale of Wild Mushrooms in a Community of Tlaxcala, Mexico","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9021-z","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Montoya","given":"Adriana"},{"family":"Hernández","given":"Nuri"},{"family":"Mapes","given":"Cristina"},{"family":"Kong","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Estrada–Torres","given":"Arturo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}},{"id":30741,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVE6RPTK"],"itemData":{"id":30741,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9043-6","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"425-436","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild Mushroom Markets in Central Mexico and a Case Study at Ozumba","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9043-6","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Pérez-Moreno","given":"Jesús"},{"family":"Martínez-Reyes","given":"Magdalena"},{"family":"Yescas-Pérez","given":"Angélica"},{"family":"Delgado-Alvarado","given":"Adriana"},{"family":"Xoconostle-Cázares","given":"Beatriz"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A similar imbalance in usage among taxa also exists at larger geographical scales as indicated by a comparison among European guidelines and legislations, where on lists from 24 countries with an average length of 55 taxa and a total of 268, only two taxa were listed in all countries: porcinis (Boletus edulis complex) and chanterelles (Cantharellus cibarius). A further five (Lactarius deliciosus, Morchella esculenta, Boletus badius, Agaricus campestris and Craterellus cornucopioides) were listed in more than 70% of countries, while 134 (about 50%) were listed in only one or two countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"17hLVWUB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Peintner, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Peintner, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30361,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDHTF9ZC"],"itemData":{"id":30361,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0063926","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e63926","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Mycophilic or Mycophobic? Legislation and Guidelines on Wild Mushroom Commerce Reveal Different Consumption Behaviour in European Countries","title-short":"Mycophilic or Mycophobic?","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0063926","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Peintner","given":"Ursula"},{"family":"Schwarz","given":"Stefanie"},{"family":"Meši?","given":"Armin"},{"family":"Moreau","given":"Pierre-Arthur"},{"family":"Moreno","given":"Gabriel"},{"family":"Saviuc","given":"Philippe"}],"editor":[{"family":"Fairhead","given":"Cecile"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",5,21]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Finally, species preferences and use may shift over time as is highlighted by Russula virescens which was highly appreciated in the Southwest of France in the 18th century but is no longer consumed nowadays, while the chanterelle increased in popularity in this region ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1es6taji3q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Duhart, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Duhart, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30994,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I4J3HMVK"],"itemData":{"id":30994,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Revue d’ethnoécologie","DOI":"10.4000/ethnoecologie.917","ISSN":"2267-2419","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"ethnoecologie","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Contribution à l’anthropologie de la consommation de champignons à partir du cas du sud-ouest de la France (xvie-xxi","URL":"http://journals.openedition.org/ethnoecologie/917","author":[{"family":"Duhart","given":"Frédéric"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",12,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The use and appreciation of different mushroom species is deeply cultural, and whether a species is used and what for is often due to a multitude of factors, including language, geography, cultural and culinary traditions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"apVSRYDl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Comandini & Rinaldi, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Comandini & Rinaldi, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30732,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/89MDEA7R"],"itemData":{"id":30732,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Mushrooms, Humans and Nature in a Changing World","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-030-37377-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-030-37378-8_13","page":"341-364","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ethnomycology in Europe: The Past, the Present, and the Future","title-short":"Ethnomycology in Europe","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-030-37378-8_13","editor":[{"family":"Pérez-Moreno","given":"Jesús"},{"family":"Guerin-Laguette","given":"Alexis"},{"family":"Flores Arzú","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Yu","given":"Fu-Qiang"}],"author":[{"family":"Comandini","given":"Ornella"},{"family":"Rinaldi","given":"Andrea C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, regions in Europe with similar occurrence of mushroom species (e.g., Southeast Europe versus Southwest Europe, or Eastern Europe versus the nordic countries) favor different species and the use of species is more strongly influenced by local tastes, traditions and commerce with neighbors than climatic variables or vegetation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VodTkQWe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Peintner, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Peintner, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30361,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDHTF9ZC"],"itemData":{"id":30361,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0063926","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e63926","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Mycophilic or Mycophobic? Legislation and Guidelines on Wild Mushroom Commerce Reveal Different Consumption Behaviour in European Countries","title-short":"Mycophilic or Mycophobic?","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0063926","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Peintner","given":"Ursula"},{"family":"Schwarz","given":"Stefanie"},{"family":"Meši?","given":"Armin"},{"family":"Moreau","given":"Pierre-Arthur"},{"family":"Moreno","given":"Gabriel"},{"family":"Saviuc","given":"Philippe"}],"editor":[{"family":"Fairhead","given":"Cecile"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",5,21]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In line with this, usage frequently reflects cultural interactions, for example in Finland, gatherers in Eastern parts of the country with stronger cultural influence from Russia prefer milk caps (Lactarius spp.), while those in Southwestern regions where French cuisine permeated through Swedish influence prefer porcinis and chanterelles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"d4UAFLjc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Comandini & Rinaldi, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Comandini & Rinaldi, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30732,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/89MDEA7R"],"itemData":{"id":30732,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Mushrooms, Humans and Nature in a Changing World","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-030-37377-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-030-37378-8_13","page":"341-364","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ethnomycology in Europe: The Past, the Present, and the Future","title-short":"Ethnomycology in Europe","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-030-37378-8_13","editor":[{"family":"Pérez-Moreno","given":"Jesús"},{"family":"Guerin-Laguette","given":"Alexis"},{"family":"Flores Arzú","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Yu","given":"Fu-Qiang"}],"author":[{"family":"Comandini","given":"Ornella"},{"family":"Rinaldi","given":"Andrea C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Immigrant populations often bring culinary traditions and preferences to their new homes, nicely illustrated in the Western United States of America, where a culture and tradition of gathering, along with different species preferences, was established by early immigrants from Europe, Asia and Russia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gULnjXv3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arora, 2008a; Parks & Schmitt, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Arora, 2008a; Parks & Schmitt, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34978,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4S9VIB8Z"],"itemData":{"id":34978,"type":"book","event-place":"Portland, OR","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.2737/PNW-GTR-393","publisher":"U.S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service, Pacific Northwest Research Station","publisher-place":"Portland, OR","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild edible mushrooms in the Blue Mountains: resource and issues.","title-short":"Wild edible mushrooms in the Blue Mountains","URL":"https://www.fs.usda.gov/treesearch/pubs/5106","author":[{"family":"Parks","given":"Catherine G."},{"family":"Schmitt","given":"Craig L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}},{"id":30731,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KHWPEJU7"],"itemData":{"id":30731,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9050-7","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"356-375","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"California Porcini: Three New Taxa, Observations on Their Harvest, and the Tragedy of No Commons","title-short":"California Porcini","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9050-7","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Arora","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another salient example illustrating, fine-grained, context dependence of use are the false morels (Gyromitra esculenta), which are consumed at quantity in Finland ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AZr1JTw5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Turtiainen, Saastamoinen, Kangas, & Vaara, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Turtiainen, Saastamoinen, Kangas, & Vaara, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30723,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3ZTHAK86"],"itemData":{"id":30723,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Silva Fennica","DOI":"10.14214/sf.911","ISSN":"22424075","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Silva Fenn.","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Picking of wild edible mushrooms in Finland in 1997–1999 and 2011","URL":"http://www.silvafennica.fi/article/911","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Turtiainen","given":"Marjut"},{"family":"Saastamoinen","given":"Olli"},{"family":"Kangas","given":"Kari"},{"family":"Vaara","given":"Matti"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and Gyromitra infula, which is harvested both in Nepal ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gGv7xaPt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Christensen, Bhattarai, Devkota, & Larsen, 2008)","plainCitation":"(M. Christensen, Bhattarai, Devkota, & Larsen, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30722,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KLQLXNH5"],"itemData":{"id":30722,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-007-9000-9","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"12-23","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Collection and Use of Wild Edible Fungi in Nepal","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-007-9000-9","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Christensen","given":"Morten"},{"family":"Bhattarai","given":"Sanjeeb"},{"family":"Devkota","given":"Shiva"},{"family":"Larsen","given":"Helle O."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Mexico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VkrWePi7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez-Moreno, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Pérez-Moreno, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30741,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVE6RPTK"],"itemData":{"id":30741,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9043-6","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"425-436","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild Mushroom Markets in Central Mexico and a Case Study at Ozumba","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9043-6","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Pérez-Moreno","given":"Jesús"},{"family":"Martínez-Reyes","given":"Magdalena"},{"family":"Yescas-Pérez","given":"Angélica"},{"family":"Delgado-Alvarado","given":"Adriana"},{"family":"Xoconostle-Cázares","given":"Beatriz"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These species are largely considered toxic and safe consumption rests on the knowledge of correct preparation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ky3r2TGG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Peintner, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Peintner, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30361,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDHTF9ZC"],"itemData":{"id":30361,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0063926","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e63926","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Mycophilic or Mycophobic? Legislation and Guidelines on Wild Mushroom Commerce Reveal Different Consumption Behaviour in European Countries","title-short":"Mycophilic or Mycophobic?","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0063926","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Peintner","given":"Ursula"},{"family":"Schwarz","given":"Stefanie"},{"family":"Meši?","given":"Armin"},{"family":"Moreau","given":"Pierre-Arthur"},{"family":"Moreno","given":"Gabriel"},{"family":"Saviuc","given":"Philippe"}],"editor":[{"family":"Fairhead","given":"Cecile"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",5,21]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, highlighting the importance of indigenous and local knowledge in shaping use of individual species.
      The trade of edible fungi has been valued at 42 billion United States dollars in 2018 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mGzvBLhJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Willis, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Willis, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30357,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MX282NHI"],"itemData":{"id":30357,"type":"book","event-place":"Richmond, UK","publisher":"Kew Publishing","publisher-place":"Richmond, UK","title":"State of the world's fungi 2018. Report.","author":[{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."}],"editor":[{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, this estimate includes mostly cultivable species and only two (porcinis and morels) out of the nine species evaluated are exclusively gathered in the wild, while other economically important wild taxa such as truffles, chanterelles and matsutake are omitted. Data on trade volumes also is often aggregated at higher levels that include both taxa from cultivation and wild gathering. For example ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oLbcCB6K","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Frutos, 2020)","plainCitation":"(de Frutos, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30356,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GULFJJ7B"],"itemData":{"id":30356,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"International trade in edible wild mushrooms has increased spectacularly in recent years. Data point to structural changes in world markets in the purchase and sale of this non-wood forestry resource, which is becoming increasingly important in the society of many countries. The reasons why countries are exchanging these harvested products seem to be changing, making it necessary to interpret such changes on the basis of alternative explanations of the theory of international trade. The present research o?ers a world-wide study of these changes, analyzing the development of intra-industry trade indices of edible wild mushrooms from a wide array of countries, using foreign commercial transaction data from the United Nations Organization. The main results show that what is driving these changes seems to be the shift in world demand for these products, with changes in consumption habits that entail a major diversi?cation in demand.","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2020.102093","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"102093","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Changes in world patterns of wild edible mushrooms use measured through international trade flows","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934119306045","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Frutos","given":"Pablo","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} estimated international trade for edible fungi at 1.2 million tons for 2017 based on United Nations Comtrade data (https://comtrade.un.org/), using harmonized customs codes that include all species, except the genus Agaricus. Agaricus spp. constitute approximately 30% of the cultivated mushroom trade volume, so this figure is likely still influenced by the other four taxa cultivated at large scale [Pleurotus, Lentinula, Auricularia and Flammulina; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zTcLNRLF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Royse, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Royse, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30729,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C5FZZ7U6"],"itemData":{"id":30729,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Proceedings of the 8th International Conference on Mushroom Biology and Mushroom Products (ICMBMP8)","publisher":"Citeseer","title":"A global perspective on the high five: Agaricus, Pleurotus, Lentinula, Auricularia & Flammulina","author":[{"family":"Royse","given":"Daniel J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}]. FAOSTAT aggregates data for all fungi into a “mushrooms and truffles” category, yielding a production of 10.9 million tons for 2017 (http://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QC; Accessed 27.02.2021). Comparison with the international trade figure suggests that as much as 90% of trade volume may be based on cultivated fungi, although again it is unclear what proportion can be attributed to wild species. The FAO estimates also include Agaricus data and other cultivated species. The heterogeneity in taxonomic granularity of data accumulation and aggregated reporting for both cultivated and wild species makes it challenging to produce meaningful estimates of production and trade volumes of wild edible fungi. This problem constitutes an active area of work within the FAO, as reflected by the introduction of new harmonized system codes for widely traded wild plants algae and fungi coming into effect in January 2022 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"al5l7jq3qv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Customs Organization, 2019)","plainCitation":"(World Customs Organization, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30960,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TJV3BQZS"],"itemData":{"id":30960,"type":"document","title":"INTERNATIONAL CONVENTION ON THE HARMONIZED COMMODITY DESCRIPTION AND CODING SYSTEM - AMENDMENTS TO THE NOMENCLATURE APPENDED AS AN ANNEX TO THE CONVENTION","URL":"http://www.wcoomd.org/-/media/wco/public/global/pdf/topics/nomenclature/instruments-and-tools/hs-nomenclature-2022/ng0262b1.pdf?db=web","author":[{"family":"World Customs Organization","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, a body of literature focusing on specific regions or target species clearly highlights the economic importance and development potential of wild mushroom trade, especially for rural areas. 
      Our literature review yielded 24 studies that highlight a contribution of gathering and selling wild fungi to incomes of rural populations worldwide (3 Africa, 5 Americas, 8 Europe and Central Asia, 7 Asia Pacific). China, and Yunnan in particular, provides an excellent example of how the gathering of wild edible fungi can fuel economic development in rural areas. Yunnan harbors a large diversity of edible fungi and is the center of the wild edible mushroom industry in China ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WtyTV7KN","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(R. Hua, Chen, & Fu, 2017; Dongyang Liu, 2018)","plainCitation":"(R. Hua, Chen, & Fu, 2017; Dongyang Liu, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30728,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FGSS7RKW"],"itemData":{"id":30728,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As one of the province with the most abundant bio-diversity in China, Yunnan is also one of the most-concerned region in the world which is famous for its bio-diversity. The wild edible fungi resources in Yunnan are extremely rich and diverse, which account for nearly 40.7% of the world’s known resources and 90% of China’s known resource respectively. Serving as one important type of forest understory resource and product, the wild edible fungi has a large market due to its unique flavor, texture or special health care function. Therefore, sustainable utilization of the wild edible fungi resource is of paramount importance to develop the rural livelihood and furthermore conserve forest and animal resources in Yunnan. Integrating a large number of previous researches, this paper has summarized the current situations of edible fungi resources as well as the existing problems on production, food, medicine utilization, processing, trade and resource conservation inYunnan. Data showed that the wild edible fungi resources are playing extremely important roles on the peasant household incomes in the vast mountainous areas in Yunnan. For example, the fungi collection incomes can account for 50% to 80% of the household income in Xishuangbanna. However, the wild edible fungi resources are facing the problems of the reductions in resource types and quantities, unclear ownership, improper collection and conservation measures, unscientific management as well as other outstanding issues. The author point out that it is quite essential to clear the ownership, implement the community-based conservation strategies as well as to strengthen the researches on the scientific collection, conservation and breeding technologies in order to solve the problems in the effective conservation and sustainable utilization of the wild edible fungi resources in the future.","container-title":"Journal of Agricultural Science","DOI":"10.5539/jas.v9n5p158","ISSN":"1916-9760, 1916-9752","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JAS","page":"158","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An Overview of Wild Edible Fungi Resource Conservation and Its Utilization in Yunnan","URL":"http://www.ccsenet.org/journal/index.php/jas/article/view/65927","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Hua","given":"Rong"},{"family":"Chen","given":"Zhen"},{"family":"Fu","given":"Wen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",4,12]]}}},{"id":8765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UMGCVYCT","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6B4AVFTW"],"itemData":{"id":8765,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"42","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An ethnobotanical survey of edible fungi in Chuxiong City, Yunnan, China","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Dongyang"},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Hong"},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"Rainer W."},{"family":"Guo","given":"Zhiyong"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Bo"},{"family":"Long","given":"Chunlin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Especially in the more remote areas of Yunnan, the contribution from gathering of wild fungi can reach up to 90% of annual household income ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Lfu3yGwk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arora, 2008b; R. Hua, 2017; Huber, Ineichen, Yang, & Weckerle, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Arora, 2008b; R. Hua, 2017; Huber, Ineichen, Yang, & Weckerle, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30726,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JRQYNWK4"],"itemData":{"id":30726,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9048-1","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"278-290","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Houses That Matsutake Built","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9048-1","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Arora","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}},{"id":33205,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5JAIGFVQ"],"itemData":{"id":33205,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-010-9126-z","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"189-204","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Livelihood and Conservation Aspects of Non-wood Forest Product Collection in the Shaxi Valley, Southwest China","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-010-9126-z","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Huber","given":"Franz K."},{"family":"Ineichen","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Yongping"},{"family":"Weckerle","given":"Caroline S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"da