ungi resources in the future.","container-title":"Journal of Agricultural Science","DOI":"10.5539/jas.v9n5p158","ISSN":"1916-9760, 1916-9752","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JAS","page":"158","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An Overview of Wild Edible Fungi Resource Conservation and Its Utilization in Yunnan","URL":"http://www.ccsenet.org/journal/index.php/jas/article/view/65927","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Hua","given":"Rong"},{"family":"Chen","given":"Zhen"},{"family":"Fu","given":"Wen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",4,12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Nanhua county alone, the yearly production of wild fungi amounted to 7677 tons, valued at 80 million United States dollars ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CXonSQnA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dongyang Liu, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Dongyang Liu, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UMGCVYCT","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6B4AVFTW"],"itemData":{"id":8765,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"42","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An ethnobotanical survey of edible fungi in Chuxiong City, Yunnan, China","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Dongyang"},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Hong"},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"Rainer W."},{"family":"Guo","given":"Zhiyong"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Bo"},{"family":"Long","given":"Chunlin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In 2015, the total yield of wild edible fungi for the whole province amounted to 0.17 million tons, with Yunnan being a major supplier of porcini (Boletus spp.) which are primarily exported to Europe and matsutake (Tricholoma matsutake) which are exported to Japan ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Faccw6VI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Hua, 2017; Huber, 2010)","plainCitation":"(R. Hua, 2017; Huber, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33205,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5JAIGFVQ"],"itemData":{"id":33205,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-010-9126-z","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"189-204","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Livelihood and Conservation Aspects of Non-wood Forest Product Collection in the Shaxi Valley, Southwest China","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-010-9126-z","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Huber","given":"Franz K."},{"family":"Ineichen","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Yongping"},{"family":"Weckerle","given":"Caroline S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",9]]}}},{"id":30728,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FGSS7RKW"],"itemData":{"id":30728,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As one of the province with the most abundant bio-diversity in China, Yunnan is also one of the most-concerned region in the world which is famous for its bio-diversity. The wild edible fungi resources in Yunnan are extremely rich and diverse, which account for nearly 40.7% of the world’s known resources and 90% of China’s known resource respectively. Serving as one important type of forest understory resource and product, the wild edible fungi has a large market due to its unique flavor, texture or special health care function. Therefore, sustainable utilization of the wild edible fungi resource is of paramount importance to develop the rural livelihood and furthermore conserve forest and animal resources in Yunnan. Integrating a large number of previous researches, this paper has summarized the current situations of edible fungi resources as well as the existing problems on production, food, medicine utilization, processing, trade and resource conservation inYunnan. Data showed that the wild edible fungi resources are playing extremely important roles on the peasant household incomes in the vast mountainous areas in Yunnan. For example, the fungi collection incomes can account for 50% to 80% of the household income in Xishuangbanna. However, the wild edible fungi resources are facing the problems of the reductions in resource types and quantities, unclear ownership, improper collection and conservation measures, unscientific management as well as other outstanding issues. The author point out that it is quite essential to clear the ownership, implement the community-based conservation strategies as well as to strengthen the researches on the scientific collection, conservation and breeding technologies in order to solve the problems in the effective conservation and sustainable utilization of the wild edible fungi resources in the future.","container-title":"Journal of Agricultural Science","DOI":"10.5539/jas.v9n5p158","ISSN":"1916-9760, 1916-9752","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JAS","page":"158","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An Overview of Wild Edible Fungi Resource Conservation and Its Utilization in Yunnan","URL":"http://www.ccsenet.org/journal/index.php/jas/article/view/65927","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Hua","given":"Rong"},{"family":"Chen","given":"Zhen"},{"family":"Fu","given":"Wen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",4,12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In all papers surveyed, gathering wild fungi was a supplemental activity to other forms of subsistence, primarily agriculture due to the seasonality of mushroom fruiting and year to year fluctuations in abundance and price. However, due to the highly perishable nature of the product that requires fast processing, the establishment of mushroom supply chains has led to lasting economic diversification in rural areas with the involvement of middlemen, mushroom traders and processing facilities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"E4x9UgUq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arora, 2008b; Huber, 2010; Dongyang Liu, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Arora, 2008b; Huber, 2010; Dongyang Liu, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30726,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JRQYNWK4"],"itemData":{"id":30726,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9048-1","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"278-290","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Houses That Matsutake Built","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9048-1","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Arora","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}},{"id":33205,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5JAIGFVQ"],"itemData":{"id":33205,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-010-9126-z","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"189-204","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Livelihood and Conservation Aspects of Non-wood Forest Product Collection in the Shaxi Valley, Southwest China","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-010-9126-z","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Huber","given":"Franz K."},{"family":"Ineichen","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Yongping"},{"family":"Weckerle","given":"Caroline S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",9]]}}},{"id":8765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UMGCVYCT","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6B4AVFTW"],"itemData":{"id":8765,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"42","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An ethnobotanical survey of edible fungi in Chuxiong City, Yunnan, China","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Dongyang"},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Hong"},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"Rainer W."},{"family":"Guo","given":"Zhiyong"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Bo"},{"family":"Long","given":"Chunlin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Shangri-la, Diqing Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture, matsutake are often bought and sold several times before leaving the city, spreading the income not only to middlemen, who often do not have access to matsutake habitats themselves, but also to shops, restaurants and other facilities that were established near the mushroom markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IBgoPauo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arora, 2008b)","plainCitation":"(Arora, 2008b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30726,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JRQYNWK4"],"itemData":{"id":30726,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9048-1","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"278-290","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Houses That Matsutake Built","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9048-1","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Arora","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Mexico mushrooms are also sold to traders and middlemen, although here there was a greater emphasis on sale at local and regional markets for generation of income ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"C6mYUZOc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Farf\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}n-Heredia, Casas, Moreno-Calles, Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a-Frapolli, & Castilleja, 2018; P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez-Moreno, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Farfán-Heredia, Casas, Moreno-Calles, García-Frapolli, & Castilleja, 2018; Pérez-Moreno, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30741,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVE6RPTK"],"itemData":{"id":30741,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9043-6","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"425-436","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild Mushroom Markets in Central Mexico and a Case Study at Ozumba","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9043-6","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Pérez-Moreno","given":"Jesús"},{"family":"Martínez-Reyes","given":"Magdalena"},{"family":"Yescas-Pérez","given":"Angélica"},{"family":"Delgado-Alvarado","given":"Adriana"},{"family":"Xoconostle-Cázares","given":"Beatriz"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}},{"id":30727,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6FHZITAK"],"itemData":{"id":30727,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-018-0205-z","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"5","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ethnoecology of the interchange of wild and weedy plants and mushrooms in Phurépecha markets of Mexico: economic motives of biotic resources management","title-short":"Ethnoecology of the interchange of wild and weedy plants and mushrooms in Phurépecha markets of Mexico","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-018-0205-z","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Farfán-Heredia","given":"Berenice"},{"family":"Casas","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Moreno-Calles","given":"Ana I."},{"family":"García-Frapolli","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Castilleja","given":"Aída"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      None of the papers reviewed mentioned commercial export of fungi from Africa, but highlighted informal, local sale as the main form of generating income ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6lYHhpVx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Osarenkhoe, John, & Theophilus, 2014; Wendiro, Wacoo, & Wise, 2019; Yorou, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Osarenkhoe, John, & Theophilus, 2014; Wendiro, Wacoo, & Wise, 2019; Yorou, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30777,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3GEVS89Y"],"itemData":{"id":30777,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Advances in Microbiology","DOI":"10.4236/aim.2014.41008","ISSN":"2165-3402, 2165-3410","issue":"01","journalAbbreviation":"AiM","page":"39-54","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ethnomycological Conspectus of West African Mushrooms: An Awareness Document","title-short":"Ethnomycological Conspectus of West African Mushrooms","URL":"http://www.scirp.org/journal/doi.aspx?DOI=10.4236/aim.2014.41008","volume":"04","author":[{"family":"Osarenkhoe","given":"Osemwegie Omorefosa"},{"family":"John","given":"Okhuoya Aroye"},{"family":"Theophilus","given":"Dania Akande"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":30725,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P4WDDPXK"],"itemData":{"id":30725,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Ectomycorrhizal Symbioses in Tropical and Neotropical Forests","publisher":"CRC Press","title":"Biodiversity and Sustainable Use of Wild Edible Fungi in the Sudanian Centre of Endemism: A Plea for Valorisation","author":[{"family":"Yorou","given":"N. S."},{"family":"Koné","given":"N."},{"family":"Guissou","given":"Marie-Laure"},{"family":"Guelly","given":"A. K."},{"family":"Maba","given":"D. L."},{"family":"Ekué","given":"M. R. M."},{"family":"Kesel","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":30776,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7WZ5FQCN"],"itemData":{"id":30776,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-019-0342-z","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"64","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Identifying indigenous practices for cultivation of wild saprophytic mushrooms: responding to the need for sustainable utilization of natural resources","title-short":"Identifying indigenous practices for cultivation of wild saprophytic mushrooms","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-019-0342-z","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Wendiro","given":"Deborah"},{"family":"Wacoo","given":"Alex Paul"},{"family":"Wise","given":"Graham"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, small scale export of porcini to Italy and the United States of America, primarily from Southern Africa, were indicated based on personal communication ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Upxs6vnz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Boa, 2004; Sitta & Floriani, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Boa, 2004; Sitta & Floriani, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30355,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8JG5XKPZ"],"itemData":{"id":30355,"type":"book","abstract":"Paper discusses traditional and contemporary uses of fungi as food or in medicine. Reviews the charactersitics of fungi biology and ecology, as well as fungi management","call-number":"QK617 .B713 2004","collection-number":"17","collection-title":"Non-wood forest products","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-105157-3","note":"OCLC: ocm57121299","number-of-pages":"147","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild edible fungi: a global overview of their use and importance to people","title-short":"Wild edible fungi","author":[{"family":"Boa","given":"E. R."}],"contributor":[{"family":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":30737,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B34XCFD6"],"itemData":{"id":30737,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9037-4","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"307-322","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Nationalization and Globalization Trends in the Wild Mushroom Commerce of Italy with Emphasis on Porcini (Boletus edulis and Allied Species)","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9037-4","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Sitta","given":"Nicola"},{"family":"Floriani","given":"Marco"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Besides direct contributions to household income, wild mushrooms provide a rich source of protein and can help to bridge periods of food scarcity which often fall into the rainy season, e.g., in Ethiopia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xJoBYy9s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dejene, Oria-de-Rueda, & Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}n-Pinto, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Dejene, Oria-de-Rueda, & Martín-Pinto, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30724,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XL7W9CBG"],"itemData":{"id":30724,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Aim of study: To review and provide all-purpose information about wild mushrooms in Ethiopia and to create awareness for conservation and use of mycological resources.Area of study: We focused mainly on Ethiopia, where information about wild mushrooms is scanty and their status is unknown under the rampant degradation of the habitats.Main results: We reviewed all relevant references related to wild mushrooms and their ecological niches, cultural practices and species used for cultivation as well as the anthropogenic factors affecting the conservation of fungal diversity.Research highlights: This review summarizes issues related to the diversity of wild mushrooms, the main ecological niches and their associated fungal species, and mushroom cultivation practices in Ethiopia. Moreover, threats and the need for future conservation of wild mushrooms in the country are also reported. This review paper can serve as base line information and indicator for further mycological studies in Ethiopia as well as in other developing countries with similar scenarios.","container-title":"Forest Systems","DOI":"10.5424/fs/2017261-10790","ISSN":"2171-9845","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Syst","page":"eR02","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild mushrooms in Ethiopia: A review and synthesis for future perspective","title-short":"Wild mushrooms in Ethiopia","URL":"http://revistas.inia.es/index.php/fs/article/view/10790","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Dejene","given":"Tatek"},{"family":"Oria-de-Rueda","given":"Juan Andrés"},{"family":"Martín-Pinto","given":"Pablo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",5,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, West Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8WYMYyN7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yorou, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Yorou, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30725,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P4WDDPXK"],"itemData":{"id":30725,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Ectomycorrhizal Symbioses in Tropical and Neotropical Forests","publisher":"CRC Press","title":"Biodiversity and Sustainable Use of Wild Edible Fungi in the Sudanian Centre of Endemism: A Plea for Valorisation","author":[{"family":"Yorou","given":"N. S."},{"family":"Koné","given":"N."},{"family":"Guissou","given":"Marie-Laure"},{"family":"Guelly","given":"A. K."},{"family":"Maba","given":"D. L."},{"family":"Ekué","given":"M. R. M."},{"family":"Kesel","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Mexico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QnrsUknT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Farf\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}n, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Farfán, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30734,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4FEYVGM9"],"itemData":{"id":30734,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1663/0013-0001(2007)61[173:MEASIT]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0013-0001","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Economic Botany","language":"en","page":"173-191","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Mazahua Ethnobotany and Subsistence in the Monarch Butterfly Biosphere Reserve, Mexico","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1663/0013-0001(2007)61[173:MEASIT]2.0.CO;2","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Farfán","given":"Berenice"},{"family":"Casas","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Ibarra-Manríquez","given":"Guillermo"},{"family":"Pérez-Negrón","given":"Edgar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In addition to the 27 sources mentioned above, a further ten studies were found where wild mushrooms were important contributors to a healthy diet and subsistence of people in economically marginalized positions. The use of wild mushrooms was also reported among several indigenous groups in the Amazon, most prominently the Jotï ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TXJ2OIvg","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Zent, Zent, & Iturriaga, 2004; Zent, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Zent, Zent, & Iturriaga, 2004; Zent, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30781,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V9DEVIT2"],"itemData":{"id":30781,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1663/0013-0001(2004)058[0214:KAUOFB]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0013-0001","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Economic Botany","language":"en","page":"214-226","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Knowledge and Use of Fungi by a Mycophilic Society of the Venezuelan Amazon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1663/0013-0001(2004)058[0214:KAUOFB]2.0.CO;2","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Zent","given":"Egleé L."},{"family":"Zent","given":"Stanford"},{"family":"Iturriaga","given":"Teresa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",4]]}}},{"id":30782,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q7TDSQLI"],"itemData":{"id":30782,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9039-2","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"471-481","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Mushrooms for Life among the Jotï in the Venezuelan Guayana","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9039-2","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Zent","given":"Egleé L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and the Yanomami people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"alqi98vl8k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fidalgo & Prance, 1976; Oscar Ipoko Sanuma, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Fidalgo & Prance, 1976; Oscar Ipoko Sanuma, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30993,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UUHKKJNB"],"itemData":{"id":30993,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mycologia","DOI":"10.1080/00275514.1976.12019902","ISSN":"0027-5514, 1557-2536","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Mycologia","language":"en","page":"201-210","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Ethnomycology of the Sanama Indians","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00275514.1976.12019902","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Fidalgo","given":"Oswaldo"},{"family":"Prance","given":"Ghillean T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1976",1]]}}},{"id":30992,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y5ZTWYIC"],"itemData":{"id":30992,"type":"book","call-number":"F2520.1.Y3 S26 2016","collection-number":"volume 4 = Saberes da floresta Yanomami ; volume 4","collection-title":"Uli tä uli naha tökö kupai ? tökö pewö taö wi","event-place":"Boa Vista, Roraima, Brasil : São Paulo, SP, Brasil","ISBN":"978-85-8226-039-5","language":"por sai","number-of-pages":"108","publisher":"Hutukara Associação Yanomami ; Instituto Socioambiental","publisher-place":"Boa Vista, Roraima, Brasil : São Paulo, SP, Brasil","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Sanöma samakönö sama tökö nii pewö oa wi ? tökö waheta. Ana amopö = Enciclopédia dos alimentos Yanomami (Sanöma). Cogumelos","editor":[{"family":"Sanuma","given":"Oscar Ipoko"},{"family":"Sanuma","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Martins","given":"Moreno Saraiva"},{"family":"Tokimoto","given":"Keisuke"},{"family":"Instituto Socioambiental (Brazil)","given":""},{"family":"Hutukara Associação Yanomami","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Recently, the Yanomami in Brazil started trading some mushrooms as a niche market ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a11h59bmuug","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(O. I. Sanuma, 2016)","plainCitation":"(O. I. Sanuma, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30972,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KCQ84JF8"],"itemData":{"id":30972,"type":"book","collection-title":"Saberes da floresta Yanomami/Urihi anë thëpëã pouwi","event-place":"São Paulo/Boa Vista","language":"Portuguese / Yanomami (Sanöma)","number-of-pages":"108","publisher":"Instituto Sociambiental/Hutukara Associação Yanomami","publisher-place":"São Paulo/Boa Vista","title":"Cogumelos. Enciclopédia dos Alimentos Yanomami (Sanöma)/Ana amopö. Sanöma samakönö sama tökö nii pewö oa wi i tökö waheta","URL":"https://acervo.socioambiental.org/acervo/publicacoes-isa/enciclopedia-dos-alimentos-yanomami-sanoma-cogumelos","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Sanuma","given":"O. I."},{"family":"Tokimoto","given":"K."},{"family":"Sanuma","given":"C."},{"family":"Autuori","given":"J."},{"family":"Sanuma","given":"L.R."},{"family":"Sanuma","given":"M."},{"family":"Martins","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Menolli Junior","given":"N."},{"family":"Ishikawa","given":"N.K."},{"family":"Apiamö","given":"R.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Although often considered the “meat of the poor” or emergency foods that can cover protein nutritional needs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1u9qa2ddeo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Christensen, 2008; Guissou, Lykke, Sankara, & Guinko, 2008; Oyetayo, 2011; Redzic, Barudanovic, & Pilipovic, 2010)","plainCitation":"(M. Christensen, 2008; Guissou, Lykke, Sankara, & Guinko, 2008; Oyetayo, 2011; Redzic, Barudanovic, & Pilipovic, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30722,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KLQLXNH5"],"itemData":{"id":30722,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-007-9000-9","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"12-23","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Collection and Use of Wild Edible Fungi in Nepal","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-007-9000-9","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Christensen","given":"Morten"},{"family":"Bhattarai","given":"Sanjeeb"},{"family":"Devkota","given":"Shiva"},{"family":"Larsen","given":"Helle O."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",5]]}}},{"id":30750,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QR4T25YY"],"itemData":{"id":30750,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9028-5","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"530-539","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Declining Wild Mushroom Recognition and Usage in Burkina Faso","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9028-5","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Guissou","given":"K. Marie Laure"},{"family":"Lykke","given":"Anne Mette"},{"family":"Sankara","given":"Philippe"},{"family":"Guinko","given":"Sita"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}},{"id":30766,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KU4VUXDC"],"itemData":{"id":30766,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"For centuries, mushrooms have been appreciated as sources of food nutrients and pharmacologically important compounds useful in medicine. Yet not all the medicinal properties of mushrooms have been exploited. The above statement is more pertinent to mushrooms that are indigenous to Nigeria. There are inadequate data on the identity and medicinal properties of these wild mushrooms. Information on the ethnomedicinal uses of some mushrooms such as Pleurotus tuber-regium used for headache, stomach pain fever, cold, constipation; Lentinus squarullosus for mumps, heart diseases; Termitomyces microcarpus for gonorrhea; Calvatia cyathiformis for leucorrhea, barreness; Ganoderma lucidum for treating arthritis, neoplasia; G. resinaceum used for hyperglycemia, liver diseases (hepatoprotector); G. applanatum used as antioxidant and for diabetes had been gathered through survey. The above information is mostly obtained from traditional herbalists who in most cases will not disclose their preparation compositions. A lot of these mushrooms are obtained only in the wild. Scientific documents of the identities and medicinal properties are still scanty. Preliminary studies on some species of Temitomyces, Lenzites and Lentinus species showed that they possess appreciable antimicrobial and antioxidant properties. Moreover, molecular characterization also reveals that they are not 100% homologous with existing sequences under the same name in GenBank. It is therefore pertinent that well structured studies on their ecology, identification and medicinal uses be carried out. This will make the full exploitation of the medicinal potentials of mushrooms indigenous to Nigeria realizable.","container-title":"African journal of traditional, complementary, and alternative medicines: AJTCAM","DOI":"10.4314/ajtcam.v8i3.65289","ISSN":"2505-0044","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Afr J Tradit Complement Altern Med","language":"eng","note":"PMID: 22468005\\nPMCID: PMC3252220","page":"267-274","source":"PubMed","title":"Medicinal uses of mushrooms in Nigeria: towards full and sustainable exploitation","title-short":"Medicinal uses of mushrooms in Nigeria","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Oyetayo","given":"Olusegun V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":30948,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z35IUJLW"],"itemData":{"id":30948,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology Review","note":"publisher: JSTOR","page":"175–187","title":"Wild mushrooms and lichens used as human food for survival in war conditions; Podrinje-Zepa Region (Bosnia and Herzegovina, W. Balkan)","author":[{"family":"Redzic","given":"Sulejman"},{"family":"Barudanovic","given":"Senka"},{"family":"Pilipovic","given":"Sasa"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, this view diminishes the cultural importance of wild edible fungi. In some communities in Mexico, for example, mushrooms are considered delicacies with great flavor that are superior to meat ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"I0E95Cra","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Farf\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}n-Heredia, 2018; Haro-Luna, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Farfán-Heredia, 2018; Haro-Luna, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30727,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6FHZITAK"],"itemData":{"id":30727,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-018-0205-z","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"5","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ethnoecology of the interchange of wild and weedy plants and mushrooms in Phurépecha markets of Mexico: economic motives of biotic resources management","title-short":"Ethnoecology of the interchange of wild and weedy plants and mushrooms in Phurépecha markets of Mexico","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-018-0205-z","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Farfán-Heredia","given":"Berenice"},{"family":"Casas","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Moreno-Calles","given":"Ana I."},{"family":"García-Frapolli","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Castilleja","given":"Aída"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}},{"id":30743,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EQXYQ5MT"],"itemData":{"id":30743,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              The relationship between humans and nature is defined by culture. Accordingly, the use, conceptions, and perceptions of resources differ among cultural groups, even among those inhabiting the same region or those who come into contact with the same biota. In particular, mushrooms evoke a wide range of sentiments. During ethnobiological tours in Mexico, semi-structured interviews were carried out with 37 individuals of each community, from ten Wixarika and mestizo communities, living in the same locality and sharing similar resources, in the municipality of Villa Guerrero in northern Jalisco, Mexico. Furthermore, informal interviews with four Wixarika and five mestizo key informants were conducted. The topics treated were regarding the traditional nomenclature and classification, uses, and knowledge of mushrooms and related practices. Wixarika names of 37 mushroom species with edible, medicinal, and recreational uses were recorded. In addition, the Wixaritari were found to associate toxic mushrooms with the divine, as evidenced by one case of the use of mushrooms as a hierophanic agent. Each culture’s knowledge of the phenology and ecology of mushrooms was recorded in addition to data highlighting the cultural exchange between the Wixaritari and mestizos. However, a loss in the knowledge and practices concerning mushrooms was observed as a result of social changes. Even so, both cultures prefer mushrooms to other foods, including meat, especially\\n              Volvariella bombycina\\n              and\\n              Pleurotus djamor\\n              .","container-title":"IMA Fungus","DOI":"10.1186/s43008-019-0014-6","ISSN":"2210-6359","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"IMA Fungus","language":"en","page":"16","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Traditional knowledge, uses, and perceptions of mushrooms among the Wixaritari and mestizos of Villa Guerrero, Jalisco, Mexico","URL":"https://IMAFungus.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s43008-019-0014-6","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Haro-Luna","given":"Mara Ximena"},{"family":"Ruan-Soto","given":"Felipe"},{"family":"Guzmán-Dávalos","given":"Laura"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The strong appreciation and deep cultural traditions associated with gathering and consumption of fungi are reflected in the fact that many papers explicitly mention recreation, social bonding and stress release as a major reason why people gathered wild mushrooms (9 sources). Gifting and exchange of gathered fungi or products prepared from them among friends, family and members of the community were also mentioned several times ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kJHbPTdc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garibay-Orijel, Cifuentes, & Estrada-Torres, 2006; Haro-Luna, 2019; Pieroni, 2005b)","plainCitation":"(Garibay-Orijel, Cifuentes, & Estrada-Torres, 2006; Haro-Luna, 2019; Pieroni, 2005b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"ERpTB65M/1DzRVql9","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B9KJZGKJ"],"itemData":{"id":9792,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Food Sciences and Nutrition","issue":"4","page":"245-272","title":"Food for Two Seasons: Culinary Uses of Non-Cultivated Local Vegetables and Mushrooms in a South Italian Village","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Pieroni","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Nebel","given":"Sabine"},{"family":"Santoro","given":"Franco R."},{"family":"Heinrick","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",6]]}}},{"id":30738,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3KXCEMRD"],"itemData":{"id":30738,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fungal Diversity","page":"46-67","title":"People using macro-fungal diversity in Oaxaca, Mexico","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Garibay-Orijel","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Cifuentes","given":"J."},{"family":"Estrada-Torres","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":30743,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EQXYQ5MT"],"itemData":{"id":30743,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              The relationship between humans and nature is defined by culture. Accordingly, the use, conceptions, and perceptions of resources differ among cultural groups, even among those inhabiting the same region or those who come into contact with the same biota. In particular, mushrooms evoke a wide range of sentiments. During ethnobiological tours in Mexico, semi-structured interviews were carried out with 37 individuals of each community, from ten Wixarika and mestizo communities, living in the same locality and sharing similar resources, in the municipality of Villa Guerrero in northern Jalisco, Mexico. Furthermore, informal interviews with four Wixarika and five mestizo key informants were conducted. The topics treated were regarding the traditional nomenclature and classification, uses, and knowledge of mushrooms and related practices. Wixarika names of 37 mushroom species with edible, medicinal, and recreational uses were recorded. In addition, the Wixaritari were found to associate toxic mushrooms with the divine, as evidenced by one case of the use of mushrooms as a hierophanic agent. Each culture’s knowledge of the phenology and ecology of mushrooms was recorded in addition to data highlighting the cultural exchange between the Wixaritari and mestizos. However, a loss in the knowledge and practices concerning mushrooms was observed as a result of social changes. Even so, both cultures prefer mushrooms to other foods, including meat, especially\\n              Volvariella bombycina\\n              and\\n              Pleurotus djamor\\n              .","container-title":"IMA Fungus","DOI":"10.1186/s43008-019-0014-6","ISSN":"2210-6359","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"IMA Fungus","language":"en","page":"16","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Traditional knowledge, uses, and perceptions of mushrooms among the Wixaritari and mestizos of Villa Guerrero, Jalisco, Mexico","URL":"https://IMAFungus.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s43008-019-0014-6","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Haro-Luna","given":"Mara Ximena"},{"family":"Ruan-Soto","given":"Felipe"},{"family":"Guzmán-Dávalos","given":"Laura"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Gathering and eating wild foods and engaging in culinary traditions provides a sense of place, identity and a connection with nature that is celebrated at festivals, e.g., in Spain ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eg8YGOk0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fust\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}-Forn\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Fusté-Forné, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HP3TPZ7H"],"itemData":{"id":30740,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Geographies","DOI":"10.1080/14616688.2018.1558453","ISSN":"1461-6688, 1470-1340","journalAbbreviation":"Tourism Geographies","language":"en","page":"1-21","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Seasonality in food tourism: wild foods in peripheral areas","title-short":"Seasonality in food tourism","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/14616688.2018.1558453","author":[{"family":"Fusté-Forné","given":"Francesc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or China ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LgJ1NmGp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dongyang Liu, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Dongyang Liu, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UMGCVYCT","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6B4AVFTW"],"itemData":{"id":8765,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"42","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An ethnobotanical survey of edible fungi in Chuxiong City, Yunnan, China","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Dongyang"},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Hong"},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"Rainer W."},{"family":"Guo","given":"Zhiyong"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Bo"},{"family":"Long","given":"Chunlin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and has developed into a sizeable foraging tourism industry worth 800,000 euros per year in Spain ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rVIP9vH9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fust\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}-Forn\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Fusté-Forné, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HP3TPZ7H"],"itemData":{"id":30740,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Geographies","DOI":"10.1080/14616688.2018.1558453","ISSN":"1461-6688, 1470-1340","journalAbbreviation":"Tourism Geographies","language":"en","page":"1-21","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Seasonality in food tourism: wild foods in peripheral areas","title-short":"Seasonality in food tourism","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/14616688.2018.1558453","author":[{"family":"Fusté-Forné","given":"Francesc"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Finally, a study comparing rural populations in Sweden, Ukraine and Russia showed that a high proportion of people engaged in gathering, irrespective of economic status ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WD0oW8mV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stryamets, Elbakidze, Ceuterick, Angelstam, & Axelsson, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Stryamets, Elbakidze, Ceuterick, Angelstam, & Axelsson, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30739,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VG6PBS2V"],"itemData":{"id":30739,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-015-0036-0","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"53","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"From economic survival to recreation: contemporary uses of wild food and medicine in rural Sweden, Ukraine and NW Russia","title-short":"From economic survival to recreation","URL":"http://www.ethnobiomed.com/content/11/1/53","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Stryamets","given":"Nataliya"},{"family":"Elbakidze","given":"Marine"},{"family":"Ceuterick","given":"Melissa"},{"family":"Angelstam","given":"Per"},{"family":"Axelsson","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Instead, the importance of commercial harvesting to supplement income increased or decreased inversely proportional to the standard of living and employment opportunities, highlighting that the social importance of gathering wild fungi may often be masked by economic necessity and reasons for gathering can shift over time.
      In the International Union for Conservation of Nature Red list, 116 of 545 species of evaluated Fungi are used as human food, and 16 species of edible fungi are evaluated as threatened. With the exception of Africa, the distribution of the species assessed is relatively balanced in the other three IPBES regions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a292hgnmfp4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2021)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":883,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CBPTIH6I"],"itemData":{"id":883,"type":"webpage","abstract":"Established in 1964, the IUCN Red List of Threatened Species has evolved to become the world’s most comprehensive information source on the global conservation status of animal, fungi and plant species.","container-title":"IUCN Red List of Threatened Species","title":"The IUCN Red List Data","URL":"https://www.iucnredlist.org/en","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Figure 3.42; Figure 3.43; Table 3.10). Less than 14% of edible fungi are threatened, which is lower compared to the overall level of the species assessed by the International Union for Conservation of Nature red lists (28%). However, due to the limited number of fungi assessed, the figure may not be representative of the global status. With regards to figures 3.42 and 3.43, it is important to note that the majority of fungal conservation related inventory and monitoring has historically been based in Europe, hence the density of data from that region ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ajt1m1iv1p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barron, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Barron, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30762,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CN4Y2YN9"],"itemData":{"id":30762,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fungal conservation emerged in the 1980s when mycologists gained considerable insight into fluctuations and trends in fungal populations throughout Europe and the United States of America. Concerns arose over the possible decline of many macrofungi and likely consequences for ecosystem function and global change. Increased harvesting of edible species for international markets also sparked concerns about overharvesting and associated habitat disturbance, particularly in the U.S.A. This paper identifies two social networks that have coalesced around these concerns. They are: (1) professional, and (2) non-professional. Professional mycologists are active in societies, international forums, and governments. A wide range of non-professionals engage with fungi for recreational, scientific, economic and subsistence purposes. The paper identifies areas of collaboration and disconnection among these social networks and their composites, and suggests benefits from further integration that will strengthen this nascent field.","collection-title":"Conservation Underground: Fungi in a Changing World","container-title":"Fungal Ecology","DOI":"10.1016/j.funeco.2010.09.009","ISSN":"1754-5048","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Fungal Ecology","language":"en","page":"124-133","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The emergence and coalescence of fungal conservation social networks in Europe and the U.S.A.","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1754504810000681","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Barron","given":"Elizabeth S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",4,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 


