Figure 3.43 The threatened status and threats of edible fungal species. At the top, the threatened status of all assessed 116 species and at the bottom, the threats of all assessed 116 species. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c9XQFTE4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2021)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":883,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CBPTIH6I"],"itemData":{"id":883,"type":"webpage","abstract":"Established in 1964, the IUCN Red List of Threatened Species has evolved to become the world’s most comprehensive information source on the global conservation status of animal, fungi and plant species.","container-title":"IUCN Red List of Threatened Species","title":"The IUCN Red List Data","URL":"https://www.iucnredlist.org/en","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} © IUCN Red List Data. This figure was made using the International Union for Conservation of Nature website https://www.iucnredlist.org/search/stats, by selecting “Fungi” in the tab “Taxonomy” and then by selecting “Food-human” in the tab “Use and Trade”. 

      In a regional assessment, take China as an example, the threatened species list of China’s macrofungi assesses the overall threat status of 9302 species and 1.04% of the total number of species (97 species) is assessed as threatened ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a17h6jjs82j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yijian, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Yijian, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30944,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U6TSIFBL"],"itemData":{"id":30944,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity Science","DOI":"10.17520/biods.2019173","ISSN":"1005-0094","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"4-10","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Development of red list assessment of macrofungi in China","URL":"http://www.biodiversity-science.net/EN/10.17520/biods.2019173","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Yijian","given":"Yao"},{"family":"Jiangchun","given":"Wei"},{"family":"Wenying","given":"Zhuang"},{"family":"Lei","given":"Cai"},{"family":"Dongmei","given":"Liu"},{"family":"Junsheng","given":"Li"},{"family":"Tiezheng","given":"Wei"},{"family":"Yi","given":"Li"},{"family":"Ke","given":"Wang"},{"family":"Haijun","given":"Wu"},{"literal":"1 State Key Laboratory of Mycology, Institute of Microbiology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100101"},{"literal":"2 University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049"},{"literal":"3 Department of Nature and Ecology Conservation, Ministry of Ecology and Environment, Beijing 100035"},{"literal":"4 State Environmental Protection Key Laboratory of Regional Eco-process and Function Assessment, Chinese Research Academy of Environmental Sciences, Beijing 100012"},{"literal":"5 School of Food Science and Engineering, Yangzhou University, Yangzhou, Jiangsu 225127"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Among the 97 threatened fungi, there are 13 species used as food, 8 species are medicinal use, and other 8 species are used both for food and medicine (Figure 3.44). 


Figure 3.44 China threatened fungi used as food and medicine. Based on ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1te83kuet1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ministry of Ecology and Environment of the People\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}s Republic of China & Chinese Academy of Sciences, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Ministry of Ecology and Environment of the People’s Republic of China & Chinese Academy of Sciences, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30928,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B5J5DMZC"],"itemData":{"id":30928,"type":"report","title":"???????????—????? - China's Red List of Biodiversity - MacroFungal Assessment Report","URL":"http://www.mee.gov.cn/xxgk2018/xxgk/xxgk01/201805/W020180926382629921552.pdf","author":[{"family":"Ministry of Ecology and Environment of the People’s Republic of China","given":""},{"family":"Chinese Academy of Sciences","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Abbreviations: EN: Endangered, VU: Vulnerable. See data management report for the figure at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6453079.

      Based on the literature survey, land use change (10 sources), timber harvesting, deforestation (8 sources) and climate change (8 sources) were listed as the most common ecological threats that likely affect a broad range of edible fungi irrespective of their economic importance. Overharvesting was primarily reported on in the context of species gathered for commercial purposes (8 sources), with a particular focus on matsutake ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LuJQf8bU","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}nez Carrera, 2002; J. S. Brooks & Tshering, 2010; Dongyang Liu, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Martínez Carrera, 2002; J. S. Brooks & Tshering, 2010; Dongyang Liu, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30917,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H5A6DF6R"],"itemData":{"id":30917,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Micología Aplicada International","ISSN":"1534-2581","language":"Español","title":"Studies on the traditional management, and processing of matsutake mushrooms In Oaxaca, Mexico","URL":"https://www.redalyc.org/articulo.oa?id=68514203","author":[{"family":"Martínez Carrera","given":"M.","suffix":"D.Morales, P.Pellicer González, E.León, H.Aguilar, A.Ramírez, P.Ortega, P.Largo, A.Bonilla, M.Gómez"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":33196,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FHMQTPBW"],"itemData":{"id":33196,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"SUMMARY\\n            Despite sound logic supporting decentralized resource management, the results of community-based natural resource management (CBNRM) efforts have been mixed. Many conditions are thought to contribute to the sustainable use of common pool resources, but as practitioners evaluate the likelihood of CBNRM success, it is necessary to understand which particular conditions have the greatest impact and how these differ across contexts. This paper describes the harvest of the matsutake mushroom and its decline in two rural communities in Bhutan that possess many of the conditions thought to facilitate resource management. Data from surveys, informal interviews and focus group meetings suggest the decline in the matsutake harvest can be attributed to the absence of a small number of enabling characteristics and an additional factor that is often overlooked in the CBNRM literature. Factors contributing to the decline include environmental dynamics, lack of leadership, and the difficulty of monitoring and enforcing harvesting guidelines. However, communities are reluctant to absorb the costs of developing institutions owing to the lack of perceived scarcity and salience of matsutake and, perhaps most importantly, to a historical dependence on a paternalistic government. This reliance on the government may preclude communities from assuming the responsibilities of matsutake management and enforcing rules to assure a sustainable harvest, a trend seen elsewhere in Bhutan. CBNRM may succeed if governments can simultaneously build capacity in communities while empowering them to take ownership over resource management. Though a relatively small number of factors have impeded CBNRM in this case, many of the obstacles can be overcome and these efforts should be considered a work in progress in Bhutan.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892910000573","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Conserv.","language":"en","page":"336-346","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A respected central government and other obstacles to community-based management of the matsutake mushroom in Bhutan","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0376892910000573/type/journal_article","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Brooks","given":"Jeremy S."},{"family":"Tshering","given":"Doley"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",9]]}}},{"id":8765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UMGCVYCT","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6B4AVFTW"],"itemData":{"id":8765,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"42","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An ethnobotanical survey of edible fungi in Chuxiong City, Yunnan, China","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Dongyang"},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Hong"},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"Rainer W."},{"family":"Guo","given":"Zhiyong"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Bo"},{"family":"Long","given":"Chunlin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and truffles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LXWNsqVq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garcia-Barreda, 2018; Radomir, Mesud, & \\\\uc0\\\\u381{}aklina, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Garcia-Barreda, 2018; Radomir, Mesud, & Žaklina, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30775,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VCAUYVFA"],"itemData":{"id":30775,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.3897/natureconservation.25.21919","ISSN":"1314-3301, 1314-6947","journalAbbreviation":"NC","page":"31-53","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Conservation and trade of wild edible mushrooms of Serbia – history, state of the art and perspectives","URL":"https://natureconservation.pensoft.net/articles.php?id=21919","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Radomir","given":"Mandi?"},{"family":"Mesud","given":"Adžemovi?"},{"family":"Žaklina","given":"Marjanovi?"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2,7]]}}},{"id":34870,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4GX7DAUC"],"itemData":{"id":34870,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-017-0973-6","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"535-544","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Black Truffle Harvesting in Spanish Forests: Trends, Current Policies and Practices, and Implications on its Sustainability","title-short":"Black Truffle Harvesting in Spanish Forests","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-017-0973-6","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Garcia-Barreda","given":"Sergi"},{"family":"Forcadell","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Sánchez","given":"Sergio"},{"family":"Martín-Santafé","given":"María"},{"family":"Marco","given":"Pedro"},{"family":"Camarero","given":"J. Julio"},{"family":"Reyna","given":"Santiago"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Long-term scientific studies monitoring the effect of different harvesting techniques (picking versus cutting) showed no adverse effects of gathering fruitbodies on future production of epigeous (aboveground) fruitbodies using either technique, but instead identified trampling associated with gathering activities as reducing the number of fruitbodies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YxsAlsft","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Egli, Peter, Buser, Stahel, & Ayer, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Egli, Peter, Buser, Stahel, & Ayer, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30719,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NTHRWNQZ"],"itemData":{"id":30719,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2005.10.042","ISSN":"00063207","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"271-276","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Mushroom picking does not impair future harvests – results of a long-term study in Switzerland","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320705004726","volume":"129","author":[{"family":"Egli","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Peter","given":"Martina"},{"family":"Buser","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Stahel","given":"Werner"},{"family":"Ayer","given":"François"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another study focused on harvesting techniques of the American matsutake (Tricholoma magnivelare) and also found no adverse effects of gathering on the number and weight of fruitbodies produced when mushrooms were picked using best practice methods (no soil removal, careful plucking of fruitbodies using a small tool) over the course of ten years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TMhb3CiM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Luoma, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Luoma, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30736,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UWTI3EF5"],"itemData":{"id":30736,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2006.08.342","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"65-75","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effects of mushroom harvest technique on subsequent American matsutake production","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112706008632","volume":"236","author":[{"family":"Luoma","given":"Daniel L."},{"family":"Eberhart","given":"Joyce L."},{"family":"Abbott","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Moore","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Amaranthus","given":"Michael P."},{"family":"Pilz","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Box 3.9). However, more disruptive harvesting methods using raking and soil removal resulted in fewer and lighter fruitbodies in the nine years following treatment, especially if soil was not replaced. This is in line with reports by Yi gatherers who expressed concerns about younger gatherers uprooting entire fruitbodies instead of using the more careful traditional gathering techniques ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tSKiq3Vb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dongyang Liu, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Dongyang Liu, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UMGCVYCT","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6B4AVFTW"],"itemData":{"id":8765,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"42","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An ethnobotanical survey of edible fungi in Chuxiong City, Yunnan, China","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Dongyang"},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Hong"},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"Rainer W."},{"family":"Guo","given":"Zhiyong"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Bo"},{"family":"Long","given":"Chunlin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Overall, however, the long-term studies reconcile reports of overharvesting with those where no influence was reported despite commercial scale gathering ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DgTOIcaS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arora, 2008b; M. Christensen, 2008; Huber, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Arora, 2008b; M. Christensen, 2008; Huber, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30722,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KLQLXNH5"],"itemData":{"id":30722,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-007-9000-9","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"12-23","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Collection and Use of Wild Edible Fungi in Nepal","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-007-9000-9","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Christensen","given":"Morten"},{"family":"Bhattarai","given":"Sanjeeb"},{"family":"Devkota","given":"Shiva"},{"family":"Larsen","given":"Helle O."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",5]]}}},{"id":30726,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JRQYNWK4"],"itemData":{"id":30726,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9048-1","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"278-290","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Houses That Matsutake Built","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9048-1","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Arora","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}},{"id":33205,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5JAIGFVQ"],"itemData":{"id":33205,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-010-9126-z","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"189-204","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Livelihood and Conservation Aspects of Non-wood Forest Product Collection in the Shaxi Valley, Southwest China","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-010-9126-z","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Huber","given":"Franz K."},{"family":"Ineichen","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Yongping"},{"family":"Weckerle","given":"Caroline S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, indicating that a good balance of commercial development and sustainable use is achievable with appropriate management practices. Species most at risk appear to be those subject to disruptive gathering practices such as matsutake and truffles, both of which are developing belowground, although structured research for a larger variety of species is currently lacking. 
      Indigenous peoples and local communities are both the main sources of knowledge with respect to status and management approaches (7 out of 8 articles reporting overharvesting) and key stakeholders in the use of wild edible fungi. Integrative research articulating local scale indigenous and local knowledge with other sources of knowledge that can incorporate the large year to year fluctuations in fruiting due to climatic variables and the impact of other environmental factors are required to better understand the multidimensional drivers influencing sustainable use. Consequently, the erosion and loss of indigenous and local knowledge also presents a major threat to sustainable use of wild fungi. This was reported in twelve studies reviewed and across all IPBES regions (5 Africa, 2 Americas, 2 Europe and Central Asia and 3 Asia Pacific). Indigenous and local knowledge is usually transmitted orally within families, often while engaging in gathering activities, so factors such as increased urbanization and associated cultural changes can decrease interest in gathering and the opportunity to do so ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2dkdumqh24","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. R. Emery & Barron, 2010)","plainCitation":"(M. R. Emery & Barron, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30982,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SZVLXFJP"],"itemData":{"id":30982,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-010-9127-y","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"205-216","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Using Local Ecological Knowledge to Assess Morel Decline in the U.S. Mid–Atlantic Region","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-010-9127-y","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R."},{"family":"Barron","given":"Elizabeth S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In three cases societal changes coincided with decline of wild edible fungi through deforestation and land use change and scarcity was one of the major reasons cited why people did not engage in gathering, e.g., in Burkina Faso ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aBB0Q6s6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Guissou, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Guissou, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30750,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QR4T25YY"],"itemData":{"id":30750,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9028-5","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"530-539","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Declining Wild Mushroom Recognition and Usage in Burkina Faso","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9028-5","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Guissou","given":"K. Marie Laure"},{"family":"Lykke","given":"Anne Mette"},{"family":"Sankara","given":"Philippe"},{"family":"Guinko","given":"Sita"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or Nigeria ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"He7DbXCX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Oyetayo, 2011; Uzoebo, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Oyetayo, 2011; Uzoebo, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30766,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KU4VUXDC"],"itemData":{"id":30766,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"For centuries, mushrooms have been appreciated as sources of food nutrients and pharmacologically important compounds useful in medicine. Yet not all the medicinal properties of mushrooms have been exploited. The above statement is more pertinent to mushrooms that are indigenous to Nigeria. There are inadequate data on the identity and medicinal properties of these wild mushrooms. Information on the ethnomedicinal uses of some mushrooms such as Pleurotus tuber-regium used for headache, stomach pain fever, cold, constipation; Lentinus squarullosus for mumps, heart diseases; Termitomyces microcarpus for gonorrhea; Calvatia cyathiformis for leucorrhea, barreness; Ganoderma lucidum for treating arthritis, neoplasia; G. resinaceum used for hyperglycemia, liver diseases (hepatoprotector); G. applanatum used as antioxidant and for diabetes had been gathered through survey. The above information is mostly obtained from traditional herbalists who in most cases will not disclose their preparation compositions. A lot of these mushrooms are obtained only in the wild. Scientific documents of the identities and medicinal properties are still scanty. Preliminary studies on some species of Temitomyces, Lenzites and Lentinus species showed that they possess appreciable antimicrobial and antioxidant properties. Moreover, molecular characterization also reveals that they are not 100% homologous with existing sequences under the same name in GenBank. It is therefore pertinent that well structured studies on their ecology, identification and medicinal uses be carried out. This will make the full exploitation of the medicinal potentials of mushrooms indigenous to Nigeria realizable.","container-title":"African journal of traditional, complementary, and alternative medicines: AJTCAM","DOI":"10.4314/ajtcam.v8i3.65289","ISSN":"2505-0044","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Afr J Tradit Complement Altern Med","language":"eng","note":"PMID: 22468005\\nPMCID: PMC3252220","page":"267-274","source":"PubMed","title":"Medicinal uses of mushrooms in Nigeria: towards full and sustainable exploitation","title-short":"Medicinal uses of mushrooms in Nigeria","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Oyetayo","given":"Olusegun V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":30773,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W2369Q4A"],"itemData":{"id":30773,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Mushrooms has a long history of use as foods and for traditional medicines in many countries of the world. In the olden times, mushrooms sourcing is entirely from the wild. Identification of mushrooms in those days were typically based on individual knowledge and conversance acquired in the course of exploration and utilization. Nowadays, as a result of advancement in mushroom science, cultivators now produce edible mushrooms commercially, a development which has tremendously impacted the industry. This paper reviews the agelong hunting history and identification practises of mushroom in Nigeria with a view to providing useful information to individuals and scientists who may have interest in studying or carrying out research in the area in the future.","container-title":"Journal of Medicinal Plants Studies","ISSN":"2320-3862","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"J. Med. Plants Stud.","language":"en","note":"publisher: AkiNik Publications","page":"89-91","source":"www.plantsjournal.com","title":"History of mushroom hunting and identification in Nigeria","URL":"https://www.plantsjournal.com/archives/?year=2019&vol=7&issue=6&part=B&ArticleId=1076","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Uzoebo","given":"CP"},{"family":"Azeez","given":"AA"},{"family":"Akeredolu","given":"OA"},{"family":"Adetunji","given":"AS"},{"family":"Bolaji","given":"OO"},{"family":"Abdulkadir","given":"AK"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. One of the latter also cited social stigmas associated with gathering, which all together can lead to a situation of rapidly declining indigenous and local knowledge. 

Box 3.9 Matsutake and sustainable management

Matsutake (Tricholoma matsutake) and the closely-related species T. magnivelare and T. caligatum) are subject to some of the richest literature available with regards to management practices for wild edible fungi ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aBhgo3sp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tsing, Satsuka, & for the Matsutake Worlds Research Group, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Tsing, Satsuka, & for the Matsutake Worlds Research Group, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30748,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4PQJPXYT"],"itemData":{"id":30748,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9035-6","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"244-253","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Diverging Understandings of Forest Management in Matsutake Science","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9035-6","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Tsing","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Satsuka","given":"Shiho"},{"literal":"for the Matsutake Worlds Research Group"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Matsutake are highly appreciated in Japan where productivity has been in decline since the 1940s. T. matsutake grows as an ectomycorrhizal symbiont of Japanese red pine, a pioneer species that is commonly found around settlements ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NAzQieLj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Saito & Mitsumata, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Saito & Mitsumata, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W23CV6KD"],"itemData":{"id":30749,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9034-7","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"257-268","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Bidding Customs and Habitat Improvement for Matsutake (Tricholoma matsutake) in Japan","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9034-7","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Saito","given":"Haruo"},{"family":"Mitsumata","given":"Gaku"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For centuries people have been coppicing the satoyama (village forests) to harvest wood for fuel and other uses which created a favorable habitat for matsutake. A low point in the matsutake production was reached in the 1970s when many households switched to propane gas and oil fuels, which was considered the main reason for the decline in productivity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0zvC6gMo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Saito & Mitsumata, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Saito & Mitsumata, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W23CV6KD"],"itemData":{"id":30749,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9034-7","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"257-268","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Bidding Customs and Habitat Improvement for Matsutake (Tricholoma matsutake) in Japan","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9034-7","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Saito","given":"Haruo"},{"family":"Mitsumata","given":"Gaku"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Due to the high market prices, especially for Japanese matsutake which can reach over 400 United States dollars per kg (2006), research has focused on silvicultural approaches for habitat improvement to increase matsutake yields ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xbkEXvof","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Saito & Mitsumata, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Saito & Mitsumata, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W23CV6KD"],"itemData":{"id":30749,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9034-7","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"257-268","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Bidding Customs and Habitat Improvement for Matsutake (Tricholoma matsutake) in Japan","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9034-7","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Saito","given":"Haruo"},{"family":"Mitsumata","given":"Gaku"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In a comparison of different land management practices, the most successful one was rooted in the traditional irai system, where wild algae, fungi and plants are considered a communal resource of the village. This management practice involves joint habitat improvement sessions and days where everyone can gather which not only improved matsutake production, but also provided community-building social activities and ultimately a virtuous cycle where increased matsutake production and the social aspects leads to increased interest in participating in management activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Mwoa0ik0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Saito & Mitsumata, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Saito & Mitsumata, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W23CV6KD"],"itemData":{"id":30749,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9034-7","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"257-268","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Bidding Customs and Habitat Improvement for Matsutake (Tricholoma matsutake) in Japan","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9034-7","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Saito","given":"Haruo"},{"family":"Mitsumata","given":"Gaku"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similar community-based management practices have proven successful in Shangri-la, Diqing Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture, China where matsutake harvest rights are organized by village and overharvesting and competition between gatherers are mitigated by instituting “rest days” of 3 to 5 consecutive days where gathering is prohibited once the quantity of matsutake sales declines ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"956ak07j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arora, 2008b)","plainCitation":"(Arora, 2008b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30726,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JRQYNWK4"],"itemData":{"id":30726,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9048-1","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"278-290","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Houses That Matsutake Built","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9048-1","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Arora","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although the measure was implemented as a means to maximize profit, it also prevents harvest of very young specimens and may thereby benefit the reproductive potential of the fungus. Conflict among gatherers was further minimized by charging high fees for gathering permits for outsiders. Despite matsutake contributing the majority of household income in the region, there were no concerns voiced about declining numbers of mushrooms ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"feckbKv0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arora, 2008b)","plainCitation":"(Arora, 2008b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30726,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JRQYNWK4"],"itemData":{"id":30726,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9048-1","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"278-290","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Houses That Matsutake Built","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9048-1","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Arora","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
      Case studies from the United States of America and Europe highlight policies that are rooted in different philosophies of nature and perspectives on resource management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VFS5PPWg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tsing, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Tsing, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30748,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4PQJPXYT"],"itemData":{"id":30748,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9035-6","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"244-253","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Diverging Understandings of Forest Management in Matsutake Science","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9035-6","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Tsing","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Satsuka","given":"Shiho"},{"literal":"for the Matsutake Worlds Research Group"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. When market demand for wild edible mushrooms increased in the United States of America in the 1980s, restrictive gathering laws were put into place, either requiring permits for gathering, selling and buying mushrooms ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"in0c4NEm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rebecca J. McLain, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Rebecca J. McLain, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30747,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TU2E2G7P"],"itemData":{"id":30747,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9025-8","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"343-355","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Constructing a Wild Mushroom Panopticon: The Extension of Nation-State Control over the Forest Understory in Oregon, USA","title-short":"Constructing a Wild Mushroom Panopticon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9025-8","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, or forbidding gathering outright ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Cf3J0KJL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arora, 2008a)","plainCitation":"(Arora, 2008a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30731,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KHWPEJU7"],"itemData":{"id":30731,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9050-7","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"356-375","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"California Porcini: Three New Taxa, Observations on Their Harvest, and the Tragedy of No Commons","title-short":"California Porcini","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9050-7","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Arora","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This led to de facto criminalization of gathering and gatherers that often had a long history of engaging in this activity but were not involved in the process of developing meaningful regulation. Consequences were increased volumes of mushrooms sold via black and grey markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PesEBEPI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arora, 2008a; Parks & Schmitt, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Arora, 2008a; Parks & Schmitt, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34978,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4S9VIB8Z"],"itemData":{"id":34978,"type":"book","event-place":"Portland, OR","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.2737/PNW-GTR-393","publisher":"U.S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service, Pacific Northwest Research Station","publisher-place":"Portland, OR","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild edible mushrooms in the Blue Mountains: resource and issues.","title-short":"Wild edible mushrooms in the Blue Mountains","URL":"https://www.fs.usda.gov/treesearch/pubs/5106","author":[{"family":"Parks","given":"Catherine G."},{"family":"Schmitt","given":"Craig L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}},{"id":30731,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KHWPEJU7"],"itemData":{"id":30731,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9050-7","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"356-375","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"California Porcini: Three New Taxa, Observations on Their Harvest, and the Tragedy of No Commons","title-short":"California Porcini","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9050-7","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Arora","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and a reframing of gathering from a family activity as work or an outright illegal activity, threatening transmission of indigenous and local knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"se7uSJcS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arora, 2008a; Rebecca J. McLain, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Arora, 2008a; Rebecca J. McLain, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30731,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KHWPEJU7"],"itemData":{"id":30731,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9050-7","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"356-375","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"California Porcini: Three New Taxa, Observations on Their Harvest, and the Tragedy of No Commons","title-short":"California Porcini","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9050-7","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Arora","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}},{"id":30747,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TU2E2G7P"],"itemData":{"id":30747,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9025-8","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"343-355","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Constructing a Wild Mushroom Panopticon: The Extension of Nation-State Control over the Forest Understory in Oregon, USA","title-short":"Constructing a Wild Mushroom Panopticon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9025-8","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Similarly, mushroom gatherers and traders were not included as stakeholders in the development of the Forest Development Strategy in Serbia, where only commercial entities can apply for permits to gather wild plants, algae and, fungi ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7k2X1CTm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Radomir, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Radomir, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30775,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VCAUYVFA"],"itemData":{"id":30775,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.3897/natureconservation.25.21919","ISSN":"1314-3301, 1314-6947","journalAbbreviation":"NC","page":"31-53","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Conservation and trade of wild edible mushrooms of Serbia – history, state of the art and perspectives","URL":"https://natureconservation.pensoft.net/articles.php?id=21919","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Radomir","given":"Mandi?"},{"family":"Mesud","given":"Adžemovi?"},{"family":"Žaklina","given":"Marjanovi?"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2,7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Serbia houses a rich variety of popular edible mushrooms, most prominently black truffles (Tuber melanosporum) which can fetch a market price of up to 4000 euros per kg. Due to high taxes levied on gathering and selling truffles, and above-mentioned restrictions on permitting, there is a flourishing black market and the majority of truffle export is purportedly going through illegal routes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7rUokiPD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Radomir, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Radomir, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30775,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VCAUYVFA"],"itemData":{"id":30775,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.3897/natureconservation.25.21919","ISSN":"1314-3301, 1314-6947","journalAbbreviation":"NC","page":"31-53","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Conservation and trade of wild edible mushrooms of Serbia – history, state of the art and perspectives","URL":"https://natureconservation.pensoft.net/articles.php?id=21919","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Radomir","given":"Mandi?"},{"family":"Mesud","given":"Adžemovi?"},{"family":"Žaklina","given":"Marjanovi?"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2,7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, a situation that neither benefits stakeholders nor allows for a realistic assessment of how to balance gathering activities with a healthy forest ecosystem. 
      In Southern Europe, wild truffle populations have been in decline, largely due to habitat degradation and climate change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OeDC4Kb9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B\\\\uc0\\\\u252{}ntgen, 2012; Garcia-Barreda, 2018; Pieroni, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Büntgen, 2012; Garcia-Barreda, 2018; Pieroni, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30746,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FU9W4VWB"],"itemData":{"id":30746,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Climate Change","DOI":"10.1038/nclimate1733","ISSN":"1758-678X, 1758-6798","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Nature Clim Change","language":"en","page":"827-829","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Drought-induced decline in Mediterranean truffle harvest","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nclimate1733","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Büntgen","given":"Ulf"},{"family":"Egli","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Camarero","given":"J. Julio"},{"family":"Fischer","given":"Erich M."},{"family":"Stobbe","given":"Ulrich"},{"family":"Kauserud","given":"Håvard"},{"family":"Tegel","given":"Willy"},{"family":"Sproll","given":"Ludger"},{"family":"Stenseth","given":"Nils C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",12]]}}},{"id":30745,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TCKUFW4L"],"itemData":{"id":30745,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-016-0088-9","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"18","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The changing ethnoecological cobweb of white truffle (Tuber magnatum Pico) gatherers in South Piedmont, NW Italy","URL":"http://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-016-0088-9","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Pieroni","given":"Andrea"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}},{"id":34870,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4GX7DAUC"],"itemData":{"id":34870,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-017-0973-6","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"535-544","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Black Truffle Harvesting in Spanish Forests: Trends, Current Policies and Practices, and Implications on its Sustainability","title-short":"Black Truffle Harvesting in Spanish Forests","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-017-0973-6","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Garcia-Barreda","given":"Sergi"},{"family":"Forcadell","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Sánchez","given":"Sergio"},{"family":"Martín-Santafé","given":"María"},{"family":"Marco","given":"Pedro"},{"family":"Camarero","given":"J. Julio"},{"family":"Reyna","given":"Santiago"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, an assessment of policies and regulations relating to truffle gathering in Spain suggest that lack of appropriate management strategies may further exacerbate this trend ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aYpraoN1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garcia-Barreda, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Garcia-Barreda, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34870,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4GX7DAUC"],"itemData":{"id":34870,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-017-0973-6","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"535-544","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Black Truffle Harvesting in Spanish Forests: Trends, Current Policies and Practices, and Implications on its Sustainability","title-short":"Black Truffle Harvesting in Spanish Forests","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-017-0973-6","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Garcia-Barreda","given":"Sergi"},{"family":"Forcadell","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Sánchez","given":"Sergio"},{"family":"Martín-Santafé","given":"María"},{"family":"Marco","given":"Pedro"},{"family":"Camarero","given":"J. Julio"},{"family":"Reyna","given":"Santiago"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Spain, gathering rights in public forests are auctioned off for terms of two to six years to private gatherers, which creates little incentive to invest in long-term strategies to maintain harvests. The situation becomes more acute as productivity declines and bidding becomes economically unattractive to commercial entities. In this case harvesting rights are purchased by municipalities which often leads to overexploitation, excessive trampling and damaging picking techniques as a larger number of gatherers face stiff competition with each other ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eAvvq4B3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garcia-Barreda, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Garcia-Barreda, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34870,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4GX7DAUC"],"itemData":{"id":34870,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-017-0973-6","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"535-544","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Black Truffle Harvesting in Spanish Forests: Trends, Current Policies and Practices, and Implications on its Sustainability","title-short":"Black Truffle Harvesting in Spanish Forests","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-017-0973-6","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Garcia-Barreda","given":"Sergi"},{"family":"Forcadell","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Sánchez","given":"Sergio"},{"family":"Martín-Santafé","given":"María"},{"family":"Marco","given":"Pedro"},{"family":"Camarero","given":"J. Julio"},{"family":"Reyna","given":"Santiago"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, overall truffle production in Spain has increased in recent years due to the contribution of truffles grown in plantations, whose share rose from 10% in 1998 to 60% in 2012 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Zgi5YDZl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garcia-Barreda, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Garcia-Barreda, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34870,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4GX7DAUC"],"itemData":{"id":34870,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-017-0973-6","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"535-544","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Black Truffle Harvesting in Spanish Forests: Trends, Current Policies and Practices, and Implications on its Sustainability","title-short":"Black Truffle Harvesting in Spanish Forests","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-017-0973-6","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Garcia-Barreda","given":"Sergi"},{"family":"Forcadell","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Sánchez","given":"Sergio"},{"family":"Martín-Santafé","given":"María"},{"family":"Marco","given":"Pedro"},{"family":"Camarero","given":"J. Julio"},{"family":"Reyna","given":"Santiago"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, indicating that cultivation and silvicultural approaches are important avenues towards sustainable use for highly prized and highly commercialized species. 
      
* Wild vegetables
Wild vegetables are an important part of the human diet. The gathering and consumption of wild vegetables are of great value in three ways. (i) They contribute to food security and famine, (ii) they are playing an increasingly important role as health food, and (iii) diets including wild vegetables pass on traditional flavors and cultural influences. Wild fruits and mushrooms are more frequently gathered than wild vegetables, and many wild vegetables have been forgotten. Herbicides have also contributed to their disappearance. Some species are regaining popularity as gourmet or health foods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2osfb4evhd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u321{}uczaj, 2012)","plainCitation":"(?uczaj, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U6XCGXXT"],"itemData":{"id":30765,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The aim of this review is to present an overview of changes in the contemporary use of wild food plants in Europe, mainly using the examples of our home countries: Poland, Italy, Spain, Estonia and Sweden. We set the scene referring to the nutrition of 19th century peasants, involving many famine and emergency foods. Later we discuss such issues as children's wild snacks, the association between the decline of plant knowledge and the disappearance of plant use, the effects of over-exploitation, the decrease of the availability of plants due to ecosystem changes, land access rights for foragers and intoxication dangers. We also describe the 20th and 21st century vogues in wild plant use, particularly their shift into the domain of haute-cuisine.","container-title":"Acta Societatis Botanicorum Poloniae","DOI":"10.5586/asbp.2012.031","ISSN":"2083-9480","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"Copyright (c) 2012 ?ukasz ?uczaj, Andrea Pieroni, Javier Tardío, Manuel Pardo-de-Santayana, Renata Sõukand, Ingvar Svanberg, Raivo Kalle","note":"number: 4","page":"359-370","source":"pbsociety.org.pl","title":"Wild food plant use in 21st century Europe: the disappearance of old traditions and the search for new cuisines involving wild edibles","title-short":"Wild food plant use in 21st century Europe","URL":"https://pbsociety.org.pl/journals/index.php/asbp/article/view/asbp.2012.031","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"?uczaj","given":"?ukasz"},{"family":"Pieroni","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Tardío","given":"Javier"},{"family":"Pardo-de-Santayana","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Sõukand","given":"Renata"},{"family":"Svanberg","given":"Ingvar"},{"family":"Kalle","given":"Raivo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",11,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
    Wild vegetables were commonly eaten in the past, especially in times of scarcity. In non-famine times, they diversified monotonous diets. Children ate some wild vegetables with an acidic taste (Rumex, Oxalis) as snacks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2n9kc4ppqc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u321{}uczaj, 2012)","plainCitation":"(?uczaj, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U6XCGXXT"],"itemData":{"id":30765,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The aim of this review is to present an overview of changes in the contemporary use of wild food plants in Europe, mainly using the examples of our home countries: Poland, Italy, Spain, Estonia and Sweden. We set the scene referring to the nutrition of 19th century peasants, involving many famine and emergency foods. Later we discuss such issues as children's wild snacks, the association between the decline of plant knowledge and the disappearance of plant use, the effects of over-exploitation, the decrease of the availability of plants due to ecosystem changes, land access rights for foragers and intoxication dangers. We also describe the 20th and 21st century vogues in wild plant use, particularly their shift into the domain of haute-cuisine.","container-title":"Acta Societatis Botanicorum Poloniae","DOI":"10.5586/asbp.2012.031","ISSN":"2083-9480","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"Copyright (c) 2012 ?ukasz ?uczaj, Andrea Pieroni, Javier Tardío, Manuel Pardo-de-Santayana, Renata Sõukand, Ingvar Svanberg, Raivo Kalle","note":"number: 4","page":"359-370","source":"pbsociety.org.pl","title":"Wild food plant use in 21st century Europe: the disappearance of old traditions and the search for new cuisines involving wild edibles","title-short":"Wild food plant use in 21st century Europe","URL":"https://pbsociety.org.pl/journals/index.php/asbp/article/view/asbp.2012.031","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"?uczaj","given":"?ukasz"},{"family":"Pieroni","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Tardío","given":"Javier"},{"family":"Pardo-de-Santayana","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Sõukand","given":"Renata"},{"family":"Svanberg","given":"Ingvar"},{"family":"Kalle","given":"Raivo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",11,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some species are still gathered, such as Asparagus acutifolius or Scolymus hispanicus, as a part of traditional diets (Table 3.11). The Mediterranean diet is a model of healthy dietary patterns and has been recognized on the UNESCO Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity for Italy, Spain, Portugal, Morocco, Greece, Cyprus, and Croatia. Cultural and historical factors diversify the use of wild vegetables ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1fsivb2khi","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u321{}uczaj, Zovko Kon\\\\uc0\\\\u269{}i\\\\uc0\\\\u263{}, Mili\\\\uc0\\\\u269{}evi\\\\uc0\\\\u263{}, Dolina, & Pand\\\\uc0\\\\u382{}a, 2013; \\\\uc0\\\\u321{}uczaj & Dolina, 2015; \\\\uc0\\\\u321{}uczaj, \\\\uc0\\\\u321{}ukasz, Jug-Dujakovi\\\\uc0\\\\u263{}, Dolina, Jeri\\\\uc0\\\\u269{}evi\\\\uc0\\\\u263{}, & Vitasovi\\\\uc0\\\\u263{}-Kosi\\\\uc0\\\\u263{}, 2019; Geraci, Amato, Di Noto, Bazan, & Schicchi, 2018)","plainCitation":"(?uczaj, Zovko Kon?i?, Mili?evi?, Dolina, & Pandža, 2013; ?uczaj & Dolina, 2015; ?uczaj, ?ukasz, Jug-Dujakovi?, Dolina, Jeri?evi?, & Vitasovi?-Kosi?, 2019; Geraci, Amato, Di Noto, Bazan, & Schicchi, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30963,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9Q57HLSJ"],"itemData":{"id":30963,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Background: Dalmatia is an interesting place to study the use of wild greens as it lies at the intersection of influence of Slavs, who do not usually use many species of wild greens, and Mediterranean culinary culture, where the use of multiple wild greens is common. The aim of the study was to document the mixtures of wild green vegetables which are sold in all the vegetable markets of Dalmatia.\\nMethods: All vendors (68) in all 11 major markets of the Dalmatian coast were interviewed. The piles of wild vegetables they sold were searched and herbarium specimens taken from them.\\nResults: The mean number of species in the mix was 5.7. The most commonly sold wild plants are: Sonchus oleraceus L., Allium ampeloprasum L., Foeniculum vulgare Mill., Urospermum picroides F.W.Schmidt, Papaver rhoeas L., Daucus carota L., Taraxacum sp., Picris echioides L., Silene latifolia Poir. and Crepis spp. Also the cultivated beet (Beta vulgaris L.) and a few cultivated Brassicaceae varieties are frequent components. Wild vegetables from the mix are usually boiled for 20–30 minutes and dressed with olive oil and salt. Altogether at least 37 wild taxa and 13 cultivated taxa were recorded. Apart from the mixes, Asparagus acutifolius L. and Tamus communis L. shoots are sold in separate bunches (they are usually eaten with eggs), as well as some Asteraceae species, the latter are eaten raw or briefly boiled.\\nConclusions: The rich tradition of eating many wild greens may result both from strong Venetian and Greek influences and the necessity of using all food resources available in the barren, infertile land in the past. Although the number of wild-collected green vegetables is impressive we hypothesize that it may have decreased over the years, and that further in-depth local ethnobotanical studies are needed in Dalmatia to record the disappearing knowledge of edible plants.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/1746-4269-9-2","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"2","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild vegetable mixes sold in the markets of Dalmatia (southern Croatia)","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1746-4269-9-2","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"?uczaj","given":"?ukasz"},{"family":"Zovko Kon?i?","given":"Marijana"},{"family":"Mili?evi?","given":"Tihomir"},{"family":"Dolina","given":"Katija"},{"family":"Pandža","given":"Marija"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",12]]}}},{"id":30962,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/USHXMQJU"],"itemData":{"id":30962,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ethnopharmacological relevance: Wild vegetable mixes used in southern Europe are interesting from the pharmacological point of view, as they contain many species which have considerable nutritional and medicinal value, but some are potentially toxic to humans. Although many ethnobotanical studies document the rich tradition of using wild vegetables at the turn of the 20th and 21st century in the Mediterranean region, there is a dearth of older historical studies which could help us to assess the extent of Traditional Knowledge loss.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2015.02.033","ISSN":"03788741","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","language":"en","page":"297-304","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A hundred years of change in wild vegetable use in southern Herzegovina","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378874115001117","volume":"166","author":[{"family":"?uczaj","given":"?ukasz"},{"family":"Dolina","given":"Katija"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",5]]}}},{"id":30975,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2AFP4PMI"],"itemData":{"id":30975,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-019-0297-0","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"article n° 18","page":"1-17","title":"The ethnobotany and biogeography of wild vegetables in the Adriatic islands Jug-Dujakovi?, M., Dolina, K., Jeri?evi? M., Vitasovi?-Kosi? I.","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/track/pdf/10.1186/s13002-019-0297-0.pdf","volume":"15","author":[{"literal":"?uczaj, ?ukasz"},{"family":"Jug-Dujakovi?","given":"Marija"},{"family":"Dolina","given":"Katija"},{"family":"Jeri?evi?","given":"Mirjana"},{"family":"Vitasovi?-Kosi?","given":"Ivana"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":30980,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DM8NPY8M"],"itemData":{"id":30980,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-018-0215-x","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"14","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The wild taxa utilized as vegetables in Sicily (Italy): a traditional component of the Mediterranean diet","title-short":"The wild taxa utilized as vegetables in Sicily (Italy)","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-018-0215-x","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Geraci","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Amato","given":"Filippo"},{"family":"Di Noto","given":"Giuseppe"},{"family":"Bazan","given":"Giuseppe"},{"family":"Schicchi","given":"Rosario"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
    
Table 3.11 Comparison of the use of wild vegetables among Mediterranean countries. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1f8iq3mjr9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Geraci, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Geraci, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30980,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DM8NPY8M"],"itemData":{"id":30980,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-018-0215-x","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"14","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The wild taxa utilized as vegetables in Sicily (Italy): a traditional component of the Mediterranean diet","title-short":"The wild taxa utilized as vegetables in Sicily (Italy)","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-018-0215-x","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Geraci","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Amato","given":"Filippo"},{"family":"Di Noto","given":"Giuseppe"},{"family":"Bazan","given":"Giuseppe"},{"family":"Schicchi","given":"Rosario"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC-BY 4.0.
