      Wild vegetables can be important local commodities and are sold at high prices in local and regional markets. However, if they are not gathered as wild vegetables, they are often considered weeds because they need little attention or management and are gathered from the wild, agricultural or disturbed spaces. Wild vegetables are often associated with traditional production systems and a long history of local selection and usage. In France, at least until the 1980s, people in some rural areas ate wild vegetables at the turn of the seasons, bitter salads in spring to purify the blood, and diuretic vegetables in autumn to prevent winter rheumatism ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ktf1ru4if","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}densieu, 1988; Schaal, 1993)","plainCitation":"(Fédensieu, 1988; Schaal, 1993)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30966,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DJSGKNU6"],"itemData":{"id":30966,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Savoirs","language":"French","page":"66-83","title":"Cures saisonnières et plantes de cueillette en milieu cévenol","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Fédensieu","given":"Annick"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1988"]]}}},{"id":30977,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2MHW24AE"],"itemData":{"id":30977,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Écologie humaine","issue":"1","language":"French","page":"23-45","title":"Schaal B. 1993.  Des remèdes et des corps, gérer ‘la force’ : aspects d'une approche ethnobotanique dans une vallée vosgienne. Écologie humaine, 11 (1) : 23-45.","URL":"http://hdl.handle.net/2042/41374","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Schaal","given":"Benoist"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Local and traditional knowledge is an important factor in maintaining the sustainability of wild vegetable gathering, cooking and consumption. This knowledge of wild vegetables may serve as baseline data for sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aspgljmu2r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ahmad, Ahmad, & Weckerle, 2013; Konsam, Thongam, & Handique, 2016; Maroyi, 2013; Wujisguleng & Khasbagen, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Ahmad, Ahmad, & Weckerle, 2013; Konsam, Thongam, & Handique, 2016; Maroyi, 2013; Wujisguleng & Khasbagen, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30753,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XFSJ2XRI"],"itemData":{"id":30753,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Studies have been carried out in the proposed area to asses, record and report the Ethno-botanical potential of the area. Forty five individuals were selected through snow ball sampling and interviewed in details, equally distributed in the three main sites of the area. A total of 66 ethnobotanically important species were found, belonging to 37 families, in which one was Pterodophyte family and the remaining were of angiosperm, distributed in 2 monocots and 34 dicot families. Regarding habit, 39 herbs, 11 shrubs and 16 were trees. 63.6% species were having medicinal uses including 02 as veterinary medicinal, 59.09% as fodder, 43.93% as fuel, 24.24% species as technological including timber and agricultural tools and 25.57% as edible including wild vegetables/pot herbs/salad and wild fruits and 33.33% were included in the category “Others” including miscellaneous uses. Regarding use reports the overall average use value was 2.5 for each species, 4.5 for tree species, 2.54 for shrubs and 1.69 for herbs. Mode of administration of medicinal plants was as, 21 plants were applied externally, 10 with internal usage and 11 with both internal and external application. 9 different parts of plants were reported to be used for medicinal purposes. There is a need to educate the locals for sustainable harvesting of plant resources and also to carry out detailed qualitative and quantitative studies in all the ethnic groups surrounding Thakht-e-Sulaiman hills complex, especially gathering the valuable knowledge of the inhabitants residing on hill sites of this area.","container-title":"PAKISTAN JOURNAL OF BOTANY","ISSN":"0556-3321","issue":"1","language":"English","page":"197-205","title":"ETHNOBOTANICAL STUDIES OF THE EASTERN PLAINS OF TAKHT-E-SULAIMAN HILLS","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Ahmad","given":"Khalid"},{"family":"Ahmad","given":"Mushtaq"},{"family":"Weckerle","given":"Caroline"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]],"season":"Enero"}}},{"id":30947,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LRYHD2RR"],"itemData":{"id":30947,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-016-0083-1","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Assessment of wild leafy vegetables traditionally consumed by the ethnic communities of Manipur, northeast India","title-short":"Erratum to","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-016-0083-1","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Konsam","given":"Surjata"},{"family":"Thongam","given":"Biseshwori"},{"family":"Handique","given":"Arun Kumar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}},{"id":30943,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3E4YVNLW"],"itemData":{"id":30943,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              Background\\n              Most agricultural weeds are usually regarded as undesirable and targeted for eradication. However, weeds are useful to human beings as food and traditional medicines. Few studies have been done to document the uses of weeds as traditional vegetables. This study was therefore, done to document indigenous knowledge related to the diversity and use of agricultural weeds as traditional vegetables in Shurugwi District, Zimbabwe, emphasizing their role in food security and livelihoods of the local people.\\n            \\n            \\n              Materials and methods\\n              Semi-structured interviews, observation and guided field walks with 147 participants were employed between December 2011 and January 2012 to obtain ethnobotanical data on the use of edible weeds as traditional vegetables. Based on ethnobotanical information provided by the participants, botanical specimens were collected, numbered, pressed and dried for identification.\\n            \\n            \\n              Results\\n              \\n                A total of 21 edible weeds belonging to 11 families and 15 genera, mostly from Amaranthaceae (19%), Asteraceae and Tiliaceae (14.3%), Capparaceae, Cucurbitaceae and Solanaceae (9.5% each) were identified. Of the documented edible weeds, 52.4% are indigenous while 47.6% are exotic to Zimbabwe; either semi-cultivated or growing naturally as agricultural weeds in farmlands, fallow land and home gardens. Among the main uses of edible weeds were leafy vegetables (81%), followed by edible fruits (19%), edible corms (9.5%), edible flowers and seeds (4.8% each). The most important edible weeds were\\n                Cleome gynandra\\n                , cited by 93.9% of the participants,\\n                Cucumis metuliferus\\n                (90.5%),\\n                Cucumis anguria\\n                (87.8%),\\n                Corchorus tridens\\n                (50.3%) and\\n                Amaranthus hybridus\\n                (39.5%). All edible weeds were available during rainy and harvest period with\\n                Cleome gynandra\\n                ,\\n                Corchorus tridens, Cucumis anguria, Cucumis metuliferus\\n                and\\n                Moringa oleifera\\n                also available during the dry season, enabling households to obtain food outputs in different times of the year. The importance of edible weeds for local livelihoods was ubiquitously perceived, with all participants reporting their contribution towards food security and nutrition.\\n              \\n            \\n            \\n              Conclusion\\n              The present study confirm findings from similar studies conducted elsewhere that rural households engage in harvesting of wild edible vegetables and other non-timber forest products (NTFPs) as a survival strategy. Based on their potential nutritional and medicinal value, edible weeds could contribute in a major way to food security, basic primary health care and balanced diets of rural households and possibly urban households as well.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/1746-4269-9-60","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"60","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Use of weeds as traditional vegetables in Shurugwi District, Zimbabwe","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1746-4269-9-60","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Maroyi","given":"Alfred"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",12]]}}},{"id":30942,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P3AMJL95"],"itemData":{"id":30942,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              Background\\n              This paper was based on ethnobotanical investigations conducted from 2004-2006 in Inner Mongolian Autonomous Region of northern China. Today, due to their nutritious and relatively pollution-free characteristics, wild vegetables are playing an increasingly important role in peoples' health and well-being. This paper aims to provide scientific clues for the selection of special and high quality wild vegetables species.\\n            \\n            \\n              Methods\\n              An ethnobotanical study, consisting of a literature survey, open-ended and semi-structured interviews, and collection and identification of voucher specimens was carried out to gather information on wild vegetables in Inner Mongolia. Next, an integrated assessment of 90 species of wild vegetables was performed using the linearity weighted integrative mathematical analysis method.\\n            \\n            \\n              Results\\n              According to an integrated assessment of 90 species of wild vegetables in Inner Mongolia, there are 5 species with the highest integrated value, 40 species of high-integrated value, 43 species of general integrated value, and 2 species of low value. The results indicate that the vast majority of wild vegetables have high value in Inner Mongolia.\\n            \\n            \\n              Conclusions\\n              Inner Mongolia is rich in wild vegetable resources. A comprehensive assessment indicates that the vast majority of wild vegetables are of high value. However, these wild vegetables are seldom collected or cultivated by local people. Most of the collected species require further research and investigation into their nutrient content, toxicity and ethnobotany to illuminate their potential as new cultivars or products.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/1746-4269-6-34","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"34","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An integrated assessment of wild vegetable resources in Inner Mongolian Autonomous Region, China","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1746-4269-6-34","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Wujisguleng","given":"Wujisguleng"},{"family":"Khasbagen","given":"Khasbagen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Knowledge on food plants is, however, eroding in various parts of the world. In Mexico, rural indigenous and mestizo populations commonly eat wild greens called quelites, mainly gathered when weeding the fields; the most common species are Amaranthus hybridus, Chenopodium berlandieri, Anoda cristata, Porophyllum ruderale ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aolem1ednj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bye, 1981)","plainCitation":"(Bye, 1981)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30778,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GRSFAE44"],"itemData":{"id":30778,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","issue":"1","page":"109-123","title":"Quelites. Ethnoecology of edible greens. Past, present and future","URL":"https://ethnobiology.org/sites/default/files/pdfs/JoE/1-1/Bye1981.pdf","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Bye","given":"Robert A. Jr"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1981"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Perceived as poor people’s food, they are disappearing from peoples’ diets, but there are actions to promote them ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ajrop7oi6e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mera Ovando, Castro Lara, & Bye Boettler, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Mera Ovando, Castro Lara, & Bye Boettler, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30921,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F7PLHNEB"],"itemData":{"id":30921,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-607-02-2589-5","language":"In Spanish; abstracts in Spanish and English","note":"OCLC: 885361112","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Especies vegetales poco valoradas: una alternativa para la seguridad alimentaria","title-short":"Especies vegetales poco valoradas","author":[{"family":"Mera Ovando","given":"Luz Mar??a"},{"family":"Castro Lara","given":"Delia"},{"family":"Bye Boettler","given":"Robert Arthur"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Weeds from rice fields are especially consumed in Asia and still play an important part in the diet in Northern Thailand ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a18pgs9q9rk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cruz-Garcia & Price, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Cruz-Garcia & Price, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30979,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FPZWDPPG"],"itemData":{"id":30979,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Background: Wild food plants are a critical component in the subsistence system of rice farmers in Northeast Thailand. One of the important characteristics of wild plant foods among farming households is that the main collection locations are increasingly from anthropogenic ecosystems such as agricultural areas rather than pristine ecosystems. This paper provides selected results from a study of wild food conducted in several villages in Northeast Thailand. A complete botanical inventory of wild food plants from these communities and surrounding areas is provided including their diversity of growth forms, the different anthropogenic locations were these species grow and the multiplicity of uses they have.\\nMethods: Data was collected using focus groups and key informant interviews with women locally recognized as knowledgeable about contemporarily gathered plants. Plant species were identified by local taxonomists.\\nResults: A total of 87 wild food plants, belonging to 47 families were reported, mainly trees, herbs (terrestrial and aquatic) and climbers. Rice fields constitute the most important growth location where 70% of the plants are found, followed by secondary woody areas and home gardens. The majority of species (80%) can be found in multiple growth locations, which is partly explained by villagers moving selected species from one place to another and engaging in different degrees of management. Wild food plants have multiple edible parts varying from reproductive structures to vegetative organs. More than two thirds of species are reported as having diverse additional uses and more than half of them are also regarded as medicine.\\nConclusions: This study shows the remarkable importance of anthropogenic areas in providing wild food plants. This is reflected in the great diversity of species found, contributing to the food and nutritional security of rice farmers in Northeast Thailand.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/1746-4269-7-33","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"33","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ethnobotanical investigation of 'wild' food plants used by rice farmers in Kalasin, Northeast Thailand","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1746-4269-7-33","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Cruz-Garcia","given":"Gisella S"},{"family":"Price","given":"Lisa L"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Laos ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a25l3l9tm8n","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kosaka, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Kosaka, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30967,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UB38GEAE"],"itemData":{"id":30967,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-013-9251-6","ISSN":"0013-0001","issue":"4","page":"335–349.","title":"Wild Edible Herbs in Paddy Fields and Their Sale in a Mixture in Houaphan Province, the Lao People’s Democratic Republic","URL":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12231-013-9251-6","volume":"67","author":[{"family":"Kosaka","given":"Yasuyuki"},{"family":"Xayvongsa","given":"Lamphoune"},{"family":"Vilayphone","given":"Anoulom"},{"family":"Chanthavong","given":"Houngphet"},{"family":"Takeda","given":"Shinya"},{"family":"Kato","given":"Makoto"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There was little evidence of wild greens consumption in South America. In the Amazon, most people are not keen on greens; the few wild species occasionally consumed are Phytolacca rivinoides and Talinum spp. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1m20qhkptc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Katz, 2012)","plainCitation":"(E. Katz, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30920,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B8NLY4FS"],"itemData":{"id":30920,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The consumption of greens is reported as being very minor among Amazonian Indians. The authors of this article present a new review of this subject, based on fieldwork with Amerindians and other populations in different parts of the Brazilian Amazon and French Guiana. Written sources on Brazilian, Peruvian, Columbian and Venezuelan Amazon were also reviewed. The consumption of cultivated, semi-cultivated and wild species of greens was taken into account here, as the data specific to wild greens is very scarce. It is confirmed that greens are not commonly eaten among native Amazonians and that some ethnic groups do not consume them at all. The consumed species are usually young shoots of weeds or cassava leaves. Common in the Belém region are some specific aromatic plants, which have been diffused to other parts of the Amazon, together with introduced plants such as kale and coriander. Migrants from Northeastern Brazil settled in the Amazon consume some cultivated greens, especially aromatic plants. Maroons are the ones who use more greens in their diet. Native Amazonian people, who supplement agriculture with game and fish, follow a hunter-gatherer pattern, preferring wild fruit and tubers to greens. © The Author(s) 2012.","container-title":"Acta Societatis Botanicorum Poloniae","DOI":"10.5586/asbp.2012.048","ISSN":"20839480","issue":"4","title":"No greens in the forest? Note on the limited consumption of greens in the Amazon","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"Katz","given":"E."},{"family":"López","given":"C.L."},{"family":"Fleury","given":"M."},{"family":"Miller","given":"R.P."},{"family":"Payê","given":"V."},{"family":"Dias","given":"T."},{"family":"Silva","given":"F."},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"Z."},{"family":"Moreira","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Africa, a large number of indigenous or naturalized vegetables, such as baobab leaves or spider plant (Cleome gynandra), contribute to dietary diversity and food security, but have been neglected in some areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2lmli3fvlb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Towns & Shackleton, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Towns & Shackleton, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30941,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q3DFUWJE"],"itemData":{"id":30941,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-019-09448-1","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"461-477","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Traditional, Indigenous, or Leafy? A Definition, Typology, and Way Forward for African Vegetables","title-short":"Traditional, Indigenous, or Leafy?","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-019-09448-1","volume":"72","author":[{"family":"Towns","given":"Alexandra M."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Two widely consumed and popular wild vegetables in the United States of America are fiddlehead ferns and ramps (wild onions). Fiddleheads are newly emerging and immature fronds of the ostrich fern (Matteuccia struthiopteris (L.) Todaro), which occurs throughout temperate areas of the country with high soil moisture. For many they are an early food of spring, are also part of rural, local economies and can sometimes be found in larger grocery store chains in regions where they are popular. Total yields are estimated at 100,000 pounds annually, which is believed to be a sustainable yield. Ramps (Allium tricoccum) are a spring ephemeral species popular in the Eastern and central northern United States of America. Like fiddleheads, they are an early spring wild crop that is highly prized and celebrated as part of the return of spring. There is a long history of local and subsistence use of this species, which became nationally recognized in the 1990s due to a growing interest in it as a specialty food product. Now sold nationally in restaurants and health food stores, the accompanying market expansion has led to concerns regarding sustainable harvesting. Total quantities harvested are undocumented ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a14bd1jcc5g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Chamberlain, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30981,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5ZULX6G7"],"itemData":{"id":30981,"type":"book","collection-number":"232","collection-title":"General Technical Report SRS","ISBN":"978-0-16-094588-5","number-of-pages":"276","publisher":"Government Printing Office","title":"Assessment of Nontimber Forest Prodcuts in the United States Under Changing Conditions","URL":"https://books.google.co.za/books?id=L8SIGPD_pv0C&dq=Chamberlain+2018+NTFP+assessment&lr=&source=gbs_navlinks_s","author":[{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"James L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Seaweeds, or “ocean vegetables”, are collected throughout coastal areas all over the world. Historically, coastal people have been gathering and using seaweeds and seagrasses for a variety of purposes, including food, feed, fertilizer, medicine, fibers, biofuel and materials; they are included here as food is a primary reason for collection. Globally, total macroalgal production has increased by approximately 5.7% per annum (including harvest of wild species and cultivation) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CceZ7oOM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2014d; Rebours, Friis Pedersen, \\\\uc0\\\\u216{}vsthus, & Roleda, 2014)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2014d; Rebours, Friis Pedersen, Øvsthus, & Roleda, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30986,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DBZX43N8"],"itemData":{"id":30986,"type":"book","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":30940,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MC7AMNZD"],"itemData":{"id":30940,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bioforsk Fokus","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Bioforsk","page":"107","title":"Seaweed-a resource for organic farming","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Rebours","given":"Céline"},{"family":"Friis Pedersen","given":"Susanne"},{"family":"Øvsthus","given":"Ingunn"},{"family":"Roleda","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. By volume, production is dominated by aquaculture (>96%), which resulted in 27.3 million tons of annual global production in 2014 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2o232v4okp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lotze, Milewski, Fast, Kay, & Worm, 2019; Mac Monagail, Cornish, Morrison, Araujo, & Critchley, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Lotze, Milewski, Fast, Kay, & Worm, 2019; Mac Monagail, Cornish, Morrison, Araujo, & Critchley, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33715,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NNNVNWGC"],"itemData":{"id":33715,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanica Marina","DOI":"10.1515/bot-2019-0027","ISSN":"0006-8055","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Bot Mar","language":"English","page":"395-409","title":"Ecosystem-based management of seaweed harvesting","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Lotze","given":"H. K."},{"family":"Milewski","given":"I."},{"family":"Fast","given":"J."},{"family":"Kay","given":"L."},{"family":"Worm","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}},{"id":30908,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MVTBG2SP"],"itemData":{"id":30908,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"European Journal of Phycology","DOI":"10.1080/09670262.2017.1365273","ISSN":"0967-0262","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Eur J Phycol","language":"English","page":"371-390","title":"Sustainable harvesting of wild seaweed resources","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Mac Monagail","given":"M."},{"family":"Cornish","given":"L."},{"family":"Morrison","given":"L."},{"family":"Araujo","given":"R."},{"family":"Critchley","given":"A. T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Despite the large scale of production from aquaculture, wild seaweed harvesting still plays an important role in many cultures. Thirty-two countries report active harvesting of seaweeds from the wild, with over 800,000 tons harvested annually from natural beds. Methods, regulations and management regimes vary widely across species and countries. European, Canadian and Latin American seaweed production still comes from harvesting wild populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a13oscfa5uf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Buschmann, 2017; Rebours, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Buschmann, 2017; Rebours, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30939,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3WN9LCST"],"itemData":{"id":30939,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"European Journal of Phycology","DOI":"10.1080/09670262.2017.1365175","ISSN":"0967-0262, 1469-4433","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"European Journal of Phycology","language":"en","page":"391-406","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Seaweed production: overview of the global state of exploitation, farming and emerging research activity","title-short":"Seaweed production","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/09670262.2017.1365175","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Buschmann","given":"Alejandro H."},{"family":"Camus","given":"Carolina"},{"family":"Infante","given":"Javier"},{"family":"Neori","given":"Amir"},{"family":"Israel","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Hernández-González","given":"María C."},{"family":"Pereda","given":"Sandra V."},{"family":"Gomez-Pinchetti","given":"Juan Luis"},{"family":"Golberg","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Tadmor-Shalev","given":"Niva"},{"family":"Critchley","given":"Alan T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",10,2]]}}},{"id":30940,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MC7AMNZD"],"itemData":{"id":30940,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bioforsk Fokus","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Bioforsk","page":"107","title":"Seaweed-a resource for organic farming","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Rebours","given":"Céline"},{"family":"Friis Pedersen","given":"Susanne"},{"family":"Øvsthus","given":"Ingunn"},{"family":"Roleda","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Chile, China and Norway lead in exploitation of wild seaweed stocks. The Chilean harvest by artisanal fishers has been around 400,000 tons over the last 10 years, and there is concern about the environmental impacts of kelp removals. The marine license vetting committee of Ireland grants licenses to mechanically harvest seaweed and considers the potential negative impact on the marine environment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aari2iccmo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mac Monagail, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Mac Monagail, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30908,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MVTBG2SP"],"itemData":{"id":30908,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"European Journal of Phycology","DOI":"10.1080/09670262.2017.1365273","ISSN":"0967-0262","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Eur J Phycol","language":"English","page":"371-390","title":"Sustainable harvesting of wild seaweed resources","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Mac Monagail","given":"M."},{"family":"Cornish","given":"L."},{"family":"Morrison","given":"L."},{"family":"Araujo","given":"R."},{"family":"Critchley","given":"A. T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Seaweed has been harvested in Brittany for several centuries, where this activity became industrial in the 18th century ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"af33vsh2pj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arzel, 1987)","plainCitation":"(Arzel, 1987)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30974,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2MH7NN7R"],"itemData":{"id":30974,"type":"book","event-place":"Douarnenez (France)","number-of-pages":"305","publisher":"Le Chasse-marée","publisher-place":"Douarnenez (France)","title":"Les Goémoniers","author":[{"family":"Arzel","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1987"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Box 3.10). 
      In Hawai’i seaweeds (Limu) are used for food, medicine, and ceremony as a traditional wild green. In recent years, more young Hawaiian men than women reported gathering wild seaweeds, indicating a cultural shift from pre-Contact Hawai’i, when women were the predominant gatherers and consumers of limu. Knowledgeable adults report a decline in the abundance of wild seaweeds driven by over-picking and pollution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a18f535pch0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hart, Ticktin, Kelman, Wright, & Tabandera, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Hart, Ticktin, Kelman, Wright, & Tabandera, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30915,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HQ4XGGYI"],"itemData":{"id":30915,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-014-9258-7","ISSN":"0013-0001","issue":"1","language":"English","page":"30-43","title":"Contemporary Gathering Practice and Antioxidant Benefit of Wild Seaweeds in Hawai'i","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Hart","given":"G. M."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."},{"family":"Kelman","given":"D."},{"family":"Wright","given":"A. D."},{"family":"Tabandera","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
Box 3.10 Seaweeds harvest in Brittany (Western France)

The tip of the Brittany peninsula is particularly rich in seaweed, where over 330 species of macroalgae have been reported. There are two types of seaweed harvesting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a25hfvg90n7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garineaud, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Garineaud, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30969,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NH3K9VWX"],"itemData":{"id":30969,"type":"book","collection-title":"PhD Thesis","edition":"Museum National d’Histoire Naturelle","event-place":"Paris","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Récolter la mer : des savoirs et des pratiques des collecteurs d'algues à la gestion durable des ressources côtières en Bretagne","author":[{"family":"Garineaud","given":"Clément"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Kelp is harvested from the sub-tidal sea in the archipelago of Molène-Ouessant (off the tip) and on the northern coast of the tip (from Le Conquet to Roscoff). This activity is locally considered as part of small-scale fisheries, with environmental knowledge transmitted within the family ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ckqh0kpf8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garineaud, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Garineaud, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30970,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q9ZXRC3K"],"itemData":{"id":30970,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ethnographiques.org","ISSN":"1961-9162","language":"French","title":"Pratiques manuelles ou mécanisées : la part de la main dans les perceptions sensorielles et dans les savoirs écologiques. Exemple des récoltants d’algues en Bretagne","URL":"https://www.ethnographiques.org/2015/Garineaud","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Garineaud","given":"Clément"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Two types of kelp are harvested: Laminaria digitata (40 000 tons/year) and Laminaria hyperborea (25 000 tons/year) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a218n2ecc6r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mesnildrey, Jacob, Frangoudes, Reunavot, & Lesueur, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Mesnildrey, Jacob, Frangoudes, Reunavot, & Lesueur, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30964,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/65FTPDBC"],"itemData":{"id":30964,"type":"book","event-place":"Rennes","publisher":"Les publications du Pôle halieutique Agrocampus Ouest","publisher-place":"Rennes","title":"La filière des macro-algues en France. Rapport d’étude. Netalgae.","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Mesnildrey","given":"L."},{"family":"Jacob","given":"C"},{"family":"Frangoudes","given":"K."},{"family":"Reunavot","given":"M."},{"family":"Lesueur","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. They are used to produce alginate, a gelling-thickening agent used in industry, especially the food industry. Two companies buy 95% of the harvest. This exploitation is considered sustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1runb72fbb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Frangoudes & Garineaud, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Frangoudes & Garineaud, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30971,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HXIC59HN"],"itemData":{"id":30971,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Interactive governance for small-scale fisheries","edition":"Jentoft S., Chuenpagdee R. (ed)","event-place":"Amsterdam","page":"101-115","publisher":"MARE Publication Series","publisher-place":"Amsterdam","title":"Governability of kelp forest small-scale harvesting in Iroise sea, France","URL":"https://vdoc.pub/documents/interactive-governance-for-small-scale-fisheries-global-reflections-7ev51kilp040","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Frangoudes","given":"Katia"},{"family":"Garineaud","given":"Clément"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} because it is followed and controlled by a scientific institution, IFREMER (Institut Français de Recherche pour l'Exploitation de la Mer), in collaboration with the kelp collectors and industrial companies. However, the economic dependence on two companies and the lack of diversification of trade makes the practice vulnerable. 
     About 30 species are harvested on shore, in quantities of a few kilograms to several tons per year, reaching a total of about 10,000 tons. Around 300 collectors are involved in this activity, with different status, from seasonal workers to small processing companies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1q8m5c694d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garineaud, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Garineaud, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30969,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NH3K9VWX"],"itemData":{"id":30969,"type":"book","collection-title":"PhD Thesis","edition":"Museum National d’Histoire Naturelle","event-place":"Paris","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Récolter la mer : des savoirs et des pratiques des collecteurs d'algues à la gestion durable des ressources côtières en Bretagne","author":[{"family":"Garineaud","given":"Clément"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The most harvested species are Fucales and edible seaweeds such as Palmaria palmata, Himanthalia elongata or “pioka” (Chondrus crispus and Mastocarpus stellatus). The seaweeds are harvested by hand, or with scissors when clinging to a rock. Then they are dried, either preserved in salt or processed and sold fresh, depending on the species, the use and the collector. They are mainly used in food, industrial and pharmaceutical products. It is difficult to analyze this exploitation because of the diversity of harvested species, outlets and stakeholders. The lack of scientific knowledge, follow up, and control of this activity makes it vulnerable to changing conditions. It is difficult to establish administrative frameworks, exploitation regulations and labels matching with the stakeholders and their practices. The main risk with regards to sustainable use would be to turn this small-scale exploitation into a more intensive, more industrial and less diversified trade. Climate change is also likely to have an impact on seaweed harvesting and increase variability of the resource. Some species have already been displaced ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a6o0kfjmps","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gallon, 2014; Raybaud, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Gallon, 2014; Raybaud, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30973,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6V8JBIN8"],"itemData":{"id":30973,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Biogeography","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/jbi.12380","ISSN":"1365-2699","issue":"12","page":"2293–2306","title":"Twenty years of observed and predicted changes in subtidal red seaweed assemblages along a biogeographical transition zone: inferring potential causes from environmental data","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/jbi.12380","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Gallon","given":"R. K."},{"family":"Robuchon","given":"M."},{"family":"Leroy","given":"B."},{"family":"Le Gall","given":"L."},{"family":"Valero","given":"M."},{"family":"Feunteun","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":30968,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PPPIMN23"],"itemData":{"id":30968,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0066044","issue":"6","page":"1-10","title":"Decline in kelp in West Europe and climate.  8(6): 1–10.","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Raybaud","given":"V."},{"family":"Beaugrand","given":"G."},{"family":"Goberville","given":"E."},{"family":"Delebecq","given":"G."},{"family":"Destombe","given":"C."},{"family":"Valero","given":"M."},{"family":"Davoult","given":"D."},{"family":"Morin","given":"P."},{"family":"Gevaert","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is unclear how companies will adapt to variability and changing environmental and social conditions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2k61760lvb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garineaud, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Garineaud, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30969,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NH3K9VWX"],"itemData":{"id":30969,"type":"book","collection-title":"PhD Thesis","edition":"Museum National d’Histoire Naturelle","event-place":"Paris","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Récolter la mer : des savoirs et des pratiques des collecteurs d'algues à la gestion durable des ressources côtières en Bretagne","author":[{"family":"Garineaud","given":"Clément"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Finally, the lack of information, transparency and accessible data makes understanding the social dimensions more difficult.

* Protista and blue-green algae
The terrestrial cyanobacterial species Nostoc flagelliforme, commonly called Fai-Cai (Fat Choy), lives in desert or semi-arid grasslands in the Asia Pacific region, and is used as a vegetable in Chinese cuisine ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aa3ilcssis","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dai, 1992; Gao, 1998; YL Geng & Jiang, 1991)","plainCitation":"(Dai, 1992; Gao, 1998; YL Geng & Jiang, 1991)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30945,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B5U58XFF"],"itemData":{"id":30945,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"J. Ning xia Univ","page":"71–77","title":"Review on the research of Nostoc flagelliforme","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Dai","given":"ZJ"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}},{"id":30958,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/94ME9D8T"],"itemData":{"id":30958,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Phycology","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"37–49","title":"Chinese studies on the edible blue-green alga, Nostoc flagelliforme: a review","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Gao","given":"Kunshan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":30946,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8AEQ8893"],"itemData":{"id":30946,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Chinese Wild Plant","page":"37–38","title":"Resource of Nostoc flagelliforme and its utilization in Ningxia","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Geng","given":"YL"},{"family":"Jiang","given":"ZH"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Herders scrape the vegetable with rakes. Indigenous and local knowledge suggests one must forage over approximately 10 acres of grassland to harvest 100g of dry fat choy. The raking can destroy the delicate grasslands and accelerate desertification. Therefore, the species was up-listed into the Class I of state key protected wild plants (even though it is not a plant) in 2000 and harvest and trade were banned at that time ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1p9rd046he","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(But, Cheng, Chan, Lau, & But, 2002)","plainCitation":"(But, Cheng, Chan, Lau, & But, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30957,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LAMGX8XZ"],"itemData":{"id":30957,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Phycology","DOI":"10.1023/A:1019518329032","ISSN":"09218971","issue":"2","page":"143-145","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Nostoc flagelliforme and faked items retailed in Hong Kong","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1023/A:1019518329032","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"But","given":"Paul Pui-Hay"},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Ling"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Pui Kwan"},{"family":"Lau","given":"David Tai-Wai"},{"family":"But","given":"Joyce Wing-Hin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Nostoc commune or Ge-Xian-Mi (Rice of Immortal Ge) is the second edible species of Nostoc, originally listed for use in the The Compendium of Materia Medica ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a13p0gr8icn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Li, 1596)","plainCitation":"(S. Li, 1596)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30956,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2I6FFR5R"],"itemData":{"id":30956,"type":"document","publisher":"Nanjin, China","title":"Compendium of Materia Medica","author":[{"family":"Li","given":"Shizhen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1596"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} by Shi-Zhen Li (1518–93?) of the Ming Dynasty. The name of Ge-Xian-Mi is related to Ge Hong (AD 284–364), a Taoist theoretician of the Eastern Jin Dynasty, who used N. commune as food during periods of famine and later introduced it to the emperor. It is used for health food and herbal medicine however the wild type of N. commune has been decreasing as a result of recent increases in market demand and environmental pollution. Artificial culture of the blue green algae generates economic benefits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ak33803q83","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Diao & Yang, 2014; Nazih & Bard, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Diao & Yang, 2014; Nazih & Bard, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30955,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YHVX5MDK"],"itemData":{"id":30955,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Biology","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Triveni Enterprises","page":"485","title":"Evaluation of morphological variation and biomass growth of Nostoc commune under laboratory conditions","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Diao","given":"Yi"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Zujun"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":30954,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/362ZWNB6"],"itemData":{"id":30954,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Microalgae in health and disease prevention","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"211–226","title":"Microalgae in human health: interest as a functional food","author":[{"family":"Nazih","given":"Hassan"},{"family":"Bard","given":"Jean-Marie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nostoc species are still consumed, not only in China, but also in various countries such as the Philippines, Thailand, Japan, Fiji, Peru, Ecuador, Mexico, Mongolia, and Siberia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a21r4k5rqb1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Borowitzka, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Borowitzka, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30927,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7WZUX8FA"],"itemData":{"id":30927,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Microalgae in Health and Disease Prevention","ISBN":"978-0-12-811405-6","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1016/B978-0-12-811405-6.00009-8","page":"195-210","publisher":"Elsevier","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Microalgae in Medicine and Human Health","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/B9780128114056000098","author":[{"family":"Borowitzka","given":"Michael A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      High-quality agar and agarose for bacteriology and pharmaceuticals originated from wild harvested Pterocladiella capillacea. A report reveals a decline in biomass coupled with a peak in wholesale prices, which have resulted in overharvesting in some countries in due to this increased economic exploitation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a24uq1co58o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Patarra, Iha, Pereira, & Neto, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Patarra, Iha, Pereira, & Neto, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30914,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5TTPCCT2"],"itemData":{"id":30914,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Phycology","DOI":"10.1007/s10811-019-02009-y","ISSN":"0921-8971","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Phycol","language":"English","title":"Concise review of the species Pterocladiella capillacea (SG Gmelin) Santelices & Hommersand","author":[{"family":"Patarra","given":"R. F."},{"family":"Iha","given":"C."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"L."},{"family":"Neto","given":"A. I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12,23]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ongoing unsustainable commercial harvest of the algae could result in further marine ecological damage; thus, the future of the industry is uncertain.
      
3.3.2.3.5. Medicine and hygiene 

Humans use wild plants and fungi for medicinal purposes all over the world. Gathering wild species for medicines is motivated by a range of factors. These include poverty or difficulty accessing medical assistance, traditional knowledge and beliefs, cultural heritage, or for profit due to commercialization. There is also a growing demand for products produced at least in part from wild harvested plants and fungi, to complement chemical medicines in many high-income countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2lovttivt4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lamrani-Alaoui & Hassikou, 2018; Lanker, 2010; H. Liu, Luo, Heinen, Bhat, & Liu, 2014; Nekratova & Shurupova, 2016; L. Petersen, Reid, Moll, & Hockings, 2017; K. M. Stewart, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Lamrani-Alaoui & Hassikou, 2018; Lanker, 2010; H. Liu, Luo, Heinen, Bhat, & Liu, 2014; Nekratova & Shurupova, 2016; L. Petersen, Reid, Moll, & Hockings, 2017; K. M. Stewart, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30718,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EVDFDI8Q"],"itemData":{"id":30718,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-018-1565-3","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","page":"2729–2745","title":"Rapid risk assessment to harvesting of wild medicinal and aromatic plant species in Morocco for conservation and sustainable management purposes","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Lamrani-Alaoui","given":"Mostafa"},{"family":"Hassikou","given":"Rachida"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":31004,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XWK9H552"],"itemData":{"id":31004,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science, Ecosystem Services & Management","DOI":"10.1080/21513732.2010.527302","ISSN":"2151-3732","issue":"1-2","page":"20-27","title":"Natural regeneration status of the endangered medicinal plant, Taxus baccata Hook. F. syn. T. wallichiana, in northwest Himalaya","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Lanker","given":"Ubeed"},{"family":"Malik","given":"A. R."},{"family":"Gupta","given":"N. K."},{"family":"Butola","given":"Jitendra S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":30919,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6T5HGCZV"],"itemData":{"id":30919,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-014-0661-2","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"English","page":"1215-1228","title":"Eat your orchid and have it too: a potentially new conservation formula for Chinese epiphytic medicinal orchids","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"H."},{"family":"Luo","given":"Y. B."},{"family":"Heinen","given":"J."},{"family":"Bhat","given":"M."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Z. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",5]]}}},{"id":31010,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XRF48FSQ"],"itemData":{"id":31010,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Key Engineering Materials","DOI":"10.4028/www.scientific.net/kem.683.433","page":"433–439","title":"Natural Resources of Medicinal Plants: Estimation of Reserves on Example of <i>Rhaponticum carthamoides</i>","volume":"683","author":[{"family":"Nekratova","given":"Natalya"},{"family":"Shurupova","given":"Margarita"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":30715,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EV8SB8QU"],"itemData":{"id":30715,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"South African Journal of Science","DOI":"10.17159/sajs.2017/20160260","ISSN":"1996-7489","issue":"9/10","page":"8-8","title":"Perspectives of wild medicine harvesters from Cape Town, South Africa","volume":"113","author":[{"family":"Petersen","given":"Leif"},{"family":"Reid","given":"Andrew M."},{"family":"Moll","given":"Eugene J."},{"family":"Hockings","given":"Marc T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33462,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UCD2984J"],"itemData":{"id":33462,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2003.08.002","ISSN":"0378-8741","issue":"1","page":"3-13","title":"The African cherry (Prunus africana): Can lessons be learned from an over-exploited medicinal tree?","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Stewart","given":"K. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      A large number of ethnobotanical studies have generated inventories and analysis of medicinal and hygienic uses of wild plants. Online databases summarize information on medicinal plants. For example, the Kew royal botanical garden has established the Medicinal Plant Names Service (https://mpns.science.kew.org), the Africa Museum in Brussels runs the Prelude Medicinal Plants Database (https://www.africamuseum.be), and databases like Native American Ethnobotany (http://naeb.brit.org/), the Indian Medicinal Plants Database (http://www.medicinalplants.in/) and the China National Genebank (https://db.cngb.org) all include information on medicinal uses. 
      These inventories of medicinal plants outline the threat level to the species, conservation status, or priority of conservation for further actions. In South Africa for example, 2,062 indigenous plant species (10% of the total national flora) have been documented for use as traditional medicine. Of these, 82 wild medicinal plant species (0.4% of the total national flora) are considered threatened with extinction at a national level ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1lhicu9jke","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. L. Williams, Victor, & Crouch, 2013)","plainCitation":"(V. L. Williams, Victor, & Crouch, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31006,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J5L652W6"],"itemData":{"id":31006,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"South African Journal of Botany","DOI":"10.1016/j.sajb.2013.01.006","ISSN":"0254-6299","page":"23-35","title":"Red Listed medicinal plants of South Africa: Status, trends, and assessment challenges","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Williams","given":"V. L."},{"family":"Victor","given":"J. E."},{"family":"Crouch","given":"N. R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thirteen percent of Myanmar medicinal plant species are considered threatened in the International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List of Threatened Species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a13el86673j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(DeFilipps, Krupnick, & Krupnick, 2018)","plainCitation":"(DeFilipps, Krupnick, & Krupnick, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31011,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VZQAY4RS"],"itemData":{"id":31011,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PhytoKeys","DOI":"10.3897/phytokeys.102.24380","ISSN":"1314-2011","issue":"102","page":"1–341","title":"The medicinal plants of Myanmar","volume":"102","author":[{"family":"DeFilipps","given":"Robert A."},{"family":"Krupnick","given":"Gary A."},{"family":"Krupnick","given":"Gary A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These data suggest possibilities for future research, conservation programs, sustainable harvesting projects, management and regulations. 
      When and how wild plants or plant parts are gathered have important effects on their medicinal value. Each category of medicinal plant has its specific collection time to maintain not only efficacy, but also sustainability. In this regard, Kletter and Kriechbaum ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aagsodcaek","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2001, p. 12)","plainCitation":"(2001, p. 12)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30952,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7QS2FWK9"],"itemData":{"id":30952,"type":"book","publisher":"CRC Press","title":"Tibetan medicinal plants","author":[{"family":"Kletter","given":"Christa"},{"family":"Kriechbaum","given":"Monika"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}},"locator":"12","suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} remarks “a plant has medicinal value when it is harvested at the right time, but is mere grass when harvested during the wrong season”. 
      Local and traditional knowledge is a key to the sustainable gathering of wild medicinal plants. Of the articles retrieved in the Web of Science published between 2000-2020, more than one third (n=117/349) mentioned “traditional knowledge”. By its very nature, traditional knowledge is holistic in nature, thus in these articles it was not always distinguished as being specifically for medical use, and could also be related consumption for food or aromatic uses. This is consistent with the fact that many wild medicinal plants have multiple uses at the same time. Like in Angola, 35% of the 127 Leguminosae plants are only used medicinally by the local communities, while the remaining species were reported to have many other uses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aqjtv6kibc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Catarino, Duarte, Costa, Carrero, & Romeiras, 2019)","plainCitation":"(S. Catarino, Duarte, Costa, Carrero, & Romeiras, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30713,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8LNRCBGI"],"itemData":{"id":30713,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.6736","ISSN":"2167-8359","page":"e6736","title":"Conservation and sustainable use of the medicinal Leguminosae plants from Angola.","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Catarino","given":"Silvia"},{"family":"Duarte","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Costa","given":"Esperança"},{"family":"Carrero","given":"Paula"},{"family":"Romeiras","given":"Maria M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Wild plant species are chosen for pharmaceutical studies through different methods. One method what has come to be known as bioprospecting: the investigation of indigenous uses of wild plant species based on indigenous local knowledge that can offer strong clues to the biological activities of those plants. There are many examples of this knowledge being used by companies who either do not financially compensate local people at all, or do not do so in proportion to the value of their resultant profits. This is commonly known as biopiracy, and is a major issue is many developing countries and with indigenous communities around the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a14c1a9l9mp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Benjaminsen & Svarstad, 2021; Shiva, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Benjaminsen & Svarstad, 2021; Shiva, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30990,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2R3WLT8Z"],"itemData":{"id":30990,"type":"chapter","abstract":"Starting out with Hollywood’s classical movie Medicine Man, this chapter gives an introduction to bioprospecting—an activity often implying that corporations in the Global North use specific plants and indigenous knowledge from the Global South in the development of modern medicines. This example also illustrates how social constructivism and realism may be combined in political ecology studies. The chapter introduces concepts such as ‘leading discourses’ that influence policy-making on issues and ‘narratives’ about specific cases. The chapter shows how realist approaches can provide critical examinations of claims that powerful actors make in their production of discourses and narratives. Case studies are presented about bioprospecting in Tanzania, and about how the history of a best-selling medicine began on a mountain road in Norway.","container-title":"Political Ecology: A Critical Engagement with Global Environmental Issues","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-030-56036-2","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-030-56036-2_3","page":"59-87","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","title":"Discourses and Narratives on Environment and Development: The Example of Bioprospecting","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-56036-2_3","author":[{"family":"Benjaminsen","given":"Tor A."},{"family":"Svarstad","given":"Hanne"}],"editor":[{"family":"Benjaminsen","given":"Tor A."},{"family":"Svarstad","given":"Hanne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":30763,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/32RMK6W4"],"itemData":{"id":30763,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Signs: Journal of Women in Culture and Society","DOI":"10.1086/508502","ISSN":"0097-9740","issue":"2","note":"publisher: The University of Chicago Press","page":"307-313","source":"journals.uchicago.edu (Atypon)","title":"Bioprospecting as Sophisticated Biopiracy","URL":"https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/full/10.1086/508502","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Shiva","given":"Vandana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In relation to the definitions of sustainable use reviewed in Chapter 2, this form of exploitation is considered as a form of unsustainable use. Scientific experimentation is another method through which medicinal knowledge of natural products has over the last few centuries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2lqm42ko59","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. A. Dias, Urban, & Roessner, 2012)","plainCitation":"(D. A. Dias, Urban, & Roessner, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30924,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/82QB44DW"],"itemData":{"id":30924,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Metabolites","DOI":"10.3390/metabo2020303","ISSN":"2218-1989","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Metabolites","language":"en","page":"303-336","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A Historical Overview of Natural Products in Drug Discovery","URL":"http://www.mdpi.com/2218-1989/2/2/303","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Dias","given":"Daniel A."},{"family":"Urban","given":"Sylvia"},{"family":"Roessner","given":"Ute"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",4,16]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. About a quarter of all Food and Drug Administration and/or the European Medical Agency approved drugs are plant based ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ghqv9esu1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Thomford, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Thomford, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30923,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PJF39YWJ"],"itemData":{"id":30923,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Molecular Sciences","DOI":"10.3390/ijms19061578","ISSN":"1422-0067","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"IJMS","language":"en","page":"1578","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Natural Products for Drug Discovery in the 21st Century: Innovations for Novel Drug Discovery","title-short":"Natural Products for Drug Discovery in the 21st Century","URL":"http://www.mdpi.com/1422-0067/19/6/1578","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Thomford","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Senthebane","given":"Dimakatso"},{"family":"Rowe","given":"Arielle"},{"family":"Munro","given":"Daniella"},{"family":"Seele","given":"Palesa"},{"family":"Maroyi","given":"Alfred"},{"family":"Dzobo","given":"Kevin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",5,25]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. From 1981 to 2002, around 49% of the small-molecule new chemical entities that were introduced were from natural products or based on natural-products. The utilization of natural products in order to discover and develop new drugs is an active area of research ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a26m0hu8ldd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Koehn & Carter, 2005; Newman & Cragg, 2007, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Koehn & Carter, 2005; Newman & Cragg, 2007, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30926,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/85Y5IWU2"],"itemData":{"id":30926,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Reviews Drug Discovery","DOI":"10.1038/nrd1657","ISSN":"1474-1776, 1474-1784","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Rev Drug Discov","language":"en","page":"206-220","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The evolving role of natural products in drug discovery","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nrd1657","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Koehn","given":"Frank E."},{"family":"Carter","given":"Guy T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",3]]}}},{"id":30965,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9ZF8QXHM"],"itemData":{"id":30965,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Natural Products","page":"461–477","title":"Natural Products as Sources of New Drugs over the Last 25 Years","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Newman","given":"D. J."},{"family":"Cragg","given":"G. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":30965,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9ZF8QXHM"],"itemData":{"id":30965,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Natural Products","page":"461–477","title":"Natural Products as Sources of New Drugs over the Last 25 Years","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Newman","given":"D. J."},{"family":"Cragg","given":"G. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
* Medicinal Fungi
Fungi are also widely used for medical purposes, especially in the Asia Pacific Region. Our literature review yielded 33 studies that detailed the use of medicinal fungi from all IPBES regions (Africa 8, Americas 7, Europe and Central Asia 8 and Asia Pacific 8). Of these, 90% also reported on species used for food, so many of the aspects pertaining to sustainable use are shared with wild edible mushrooms (see section 3.3.2.3). All studies reporting on wild species and their uses (12 in total) reported fewer medicinal species than species used for food and often species were used both as food and medicine. The largest number of medicinal species was reported from China, with 692 species with medicinal properties with 277 species considered as both food and medicine ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NV1uDMTQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. Wu, 2019)","plainCitation":"(F. Wu, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33297,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/32SRJ9EQ"],"itemData":{"id":33297,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fungal Diversity","DOI":"10.1007/s13225-019-00432-7","ISSN":"1560-2745, 1878-9129","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fungal Diversity","language":"en","page":"1-76","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Resource diversity of Chinese macrofungi: edible, medicinal and poisonous species","title-short":"Resource diversity of Chinese macrofungi","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s13225-019-00432-7","volume":"98","author":[{"family":"Wu","given":"Fang"},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Li-Wei"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Zhu-Liang"},{"family":"Bau","given":"Tolgor"},{"family":"Li","given":"Tai-Hui"},{"family":"Dai","given":"Yu-Cheng"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Mexico also hosts a large variety of medicinal fungi with a survey reporting the use of 70 species to treat over 40 different conditions, again many with dual use as food and medicine ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vbjkk1Ax","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Guzm\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}n, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Guzmán, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30786,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7YRW5GS6"],"itemData":{"id":30786,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Medicinal Mushrooms","DOI":"10.1615/IntJMedMushr.v10.i3.20","ISSN":"1521-9437","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Int J Med Mushr","language":"en","page":"209-217","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Diversity and Use of Traditional Mexican Medicinal Fungi. A Review","URL":"http://www.dl.begellhouse.com/journals/708ae68d64b17c52,1b05160a735f1b4e,016f015768d8c7b5.html","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Guzmán","given":"Gaston"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Medicinal fungi also have a long history of use in Europe, where interest in traditional medicines has been increasing again recently after a decline in use in the 20th century ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MBPd9FgS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Comandini & Rinaldi, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Comandini & Rinaldi, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30732,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/89MDEA7R"],"itemData":{"id":30732,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Mushrooms, Humans and Nature in a Changing World","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-030-37377-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-030-37378-8_13","page":"341-364","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ethnomycology in Europe: The Past, the Present, and the Future","title-short":"Ethnomycology in Europe","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-030-37378-8_13","editor":[{"family":"Pérez-Moreno","given":"Jesús"},{"family":"Guerin-Laguette","given":"Alexis"},{"family":"Flores Arzú","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Yu","given":"Fu-Qiang"}],"author":[{"family":"Comandini","given":"Ornella"},{"family":"Rinaldi","given":"Andrea C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	The most common medicinal fungi include Ophiocordyceps sinensis (caterpillar fungus), Ganoderma lucidum (lingzhi or reishi), Lariciformis officinalis, Lentinula edidodes (shitake), Trametes versicolor (turkey tail), Schizophyllum commune (the split gill) and Pleurotus spp., especially Pleurotus tuber-regium which is used medicinally across Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"k4dY3yJT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Milenge Kamalebo, Nshimba Seya Wa Malale, Masumbuko Ndabaga, Degreef, & De Kesel, 2018; Oyetayo, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Milenge Kamalebo, Nshimba Seya Wa Malale, Masumbuko Ndabaga, Degreef, & De Kesel, 2018; Oyetayo, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30766,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KU4VUXDC"],"itemData":{"id":30766,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"For centuries, mushrooms have been appreciated as sources of food nutrients and pharmacologically important compounds useful in medicine. Yet not all the medicinal properties of mushrooms have been exploited. The above statement is more pertinent to mushrooms that are indigenous to Nigeria. There are inadequate data on the identity and medicinal properties of these wild mushrooms. Information on the ethnomedicinal uses of some mushrooms such as Pleurotus tuber-regium used for headache, stomach pain fever, cold, constipation; Lentinus squarullosus for mumps, heart diseases; Termitomyces microcarpus for gonorrhea; Calvatia cyathiformis for leucorrhea, barreness; Ganoderma lucidum for treating arthritis, neoplasia; G. resinaceum used for hyperglycemia, liver diseases (hepatoprotector); G. applanatum used as antioxidant and for diabetes had been gathered through survey. The above information is mostly obtained from traditional herbalists who in most cases will not disclose their preparation compositions. A lot of these mushrooms are obtained only in the wild. Scientific documents of the identities and medicinal properties are still scanty. Preliminary studies on some species of Temitomyces, Lenzites and Lentinus species showed that they possess appreciable antimicrobial and antioxidant properties. Moreover, molecular characterization also reveals that they are not 100% homologous with existing sequences under the same name in GenBank. It is therefore pertinent that well structured studies on their ecology, identification and medicinal uses be carried out. This will make the full exploitation of the medicinal potentials of mushrooms indigenous to Nigeria realizable.","container-title":"African journal of traditional, complementary, and alternative medicines: AJTCAM","DOI":"10.4314/ajtcam.v8i3.65289","ISSN":"2505-0044","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Afr J Tradit Complement Altern Med","language":"eng","note":"PMID: 22468005\\nPMCID: PMC3252220","page":"267-274","source":"PubMed","title":"Medicinal uses of mushrooms in Nigeria: towards full and sustainable exploitation","title-short":"Medicinal uses of mushrooms in Nigeria","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Oyetayo","given":"Olusegun V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":30785,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LQDULP22"],"itemData":{"id":30785,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-017-0203-6","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"13","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Uses and importance of wild fungi: traditional knowledge from the Tshopo province in the Democratic Republic of the Congo","title-short":"Uses and importance of wild fungi","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-017-0203-6","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Milenge Kamalebo","given":"Héritier"},{"family":"Nshimba Seya Wa Malale","given":"Hippolyte"},{"family":"Masumbuko Ndabaga","given":"Cephas"},{"family":"Degreef","given":"Jérôme"},{"family":"De Kesel","given":"André"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Medicinal fungi produce a range of active compounds, many of which have been shown to have anti-oxidant, anti-tumor or anti-microbial properties ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nzLuIK1U","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. Wu, 2019)","plainCitation":"(F. Wu, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33297,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/32SRJ9EQ"],"itemData":{"id":33297,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fungal Diversity","DOI":"10.1007/s13225-019-00432-7","ISSN":"1560-2745, 1878-9129","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fungal Diversity","language":"en","page":"1-76","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Resource diversity of Chinese macrofungi: edible, medicinal and poisonous species","title-short":"Resource diversity of Chinese macrofungi","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s13225-019-00432-7","volume":"98","author":[{"family":"Wu","given":"Fang"},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Li-Wei"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Zhu-Liang"},{"family":"Bau","given":"Tolgor"},{"family":"Li","given":"Tai-Hui"},{"family":"Dai","given":"Yu-Cheng"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. To this end, G. lucidum is probably the most intensively studied species. It produces over 400 bioactive compounds and has been dubbed “the mushroom of immortality” in China where it has been used for over 2,400 years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nwfkeJbk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}r, Knez, & Knez Hrn\\\\uc0\\\\u269{}i\\\\uc0\\\\u269{}, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Cör, Knez, & Knez Hrn?i?, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30784,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DY68V72N"],"itemData":{"id":30784,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Molecules","DOI":"10.3390/molecules23030649","ISSN":"1420-3049","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Molecules","language":"en","page":"649","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Antitumour, Antimicrobial, Antioxidant and Antiacetylcholinesterase Effect of Ganoderma Lucidum Terpenoids and Polysaccharides: A Review","title-short":"Antitumour, Antimicrobial, Antioxidant and Antiacetylcholinesterase Effect of Ganoderma Lucidum Terpenoids and Polysaccharides","URL":"http://www.mdpi.com/1420-3049/23/3/649","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Cör","given":"Darija"},{"family":"Knez","given":"Željko"},{"family":"Knez Hrn?i?","given":"Maša"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3,13]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nowadays it is widely used to supplement cancer treatment both in China and Western countries. Several records indicating the medicinal use of lichens in Spain and Nepal were also found ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aAb51k3q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shiva Devkota, Chaudhary, Werth, & Scheidegger, 2017; Gonz\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}lez-Tejero, Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}nez-Lirola, Casares-Porcel, & Molero-Mesa, 1995)","plainCitation":"(Shiva Devkota, Chaudhary, Werth, & Scheidegger, 2017; González-Tejero, Martínez-Lirola, Casares-Porcel, & Molero-Mesa, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30783,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M8YJ3ICP"],"itemData":{"id":30783,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/BF02862281","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"96-98","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Three lichens used in popular medicine in Eastern Andalucia (Spain)","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/BF02862281","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"González-Tejero","given":"M. R."},{"family":"Martínez-Lirola","given":"M. J."},{"family":"Casares-Porcel","given":"M."},{"family":"Molero-Mesa","given":"J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995",1]]}}},{"id":30735,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MSAMI6L2"],"itemData":{"id":30735,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-017-0142-2","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"15","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Indigenous knowledge and use of lichens by the lichenophilic communities of the Nepal Himalaya","URL":"http://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-017-0142-2","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Devkota","given":"Shiva"},{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"Ram Prasad"},{"family":"Werth","given":"Silke"},{"family":"Scheidegger","given":"Christoph"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Perhaps the most valuable species globally is the illusive caterpillar fungus (Ophiocordyceps sinensis), which grows only in the Himalayan mountains (Box 3.11). 
	Gathering of caterpillar fungus (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) has dramatically increased over the last 20 years. A short seasonal and rotational approach for gathering is useful for its sustainability. Caterpillar fungus extraction provides up to 72% of household income in the area, and estimates of households involved in the short seasonal gathering range from 52% to 98%. Understanding of local commercial harvest and trade supports sustainable management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1iteom3bcv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. He, 2018; Kuniyal & Sundriyal, 2013; Woodhouse, McGowan, & Milner-Gulland, 2014)","plainCitation":"(J. He, 2018; Kuniyal & Sundriyal, 2013; Woodhouse, McGowan, & Milner-Gulland, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":748,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F7R3X8Y2"],"itemData":{"id":748,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ethnopharmacological relevance\\nCaterpillar mushroom (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) is a unique medicinal fungi which is only found in alpine grasslands in Himalayan mountain regions and the Tibetan Plateau. Known locally as Yartsa Gunbu, it has been widely used in Tibetan and Chinese Medicine for centuries. It is crucial to understand local commercial harvest and trade practices of caterpillar mushroom to support the sustainable management of this valuable resource. However, data derived from empirically grounded research is currently limited, particularly in China.\\nAim of this study\\nThe research aims to provide the most up-to-date insights into caterpillar mushroom harvest and trade in the main production area of the Tibet Region in Southwest China and to generate policy recommendations for sustainable use.\\nMaterials and methods\\nThe research was conducted in 2015–2016 in six Tibetan communities located in two counties in Diqing Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture, Southwest China. Quantitative and qualitative data were collected from in-depth interviews with local households engaged in caterpillar mushroom harvesting (n?=?157), local caterpillar mushroom traders (n?=?14), and from focus groups discussions (n?=?5) with regional caterpillar mushroom industry stakeholders.\\nResults\\nThe research found large regional- and community-level differences in caterpillar mushroom harvest practices. The harvest practices of communities involved in the co-management of a Nature Reserve were more sustainable than those communities not involved in such a scheme, and this was due to the external support and training provided via the co-management scheme. Moreover, a customary tenure system was proving effective for avoiding competition over caterpillar mushroom collection. However, in both counties, narrow marketing channel and non-grading system in trade limits the possibility of improving the local benefits generated from the commercial harvest of caterpillar mushroom. Meanwhile, the local traders play an important bridging role in the value chain and generate greater benefits from product grading.\\nConclusion\\nTo support the sustainable management of the caterpillar mushroom industry in Southwest China, the prefectural governments should invest in training on appropriate harvesting techniques and the dissemination of market information. It is also critical that prefectural governments recognize and support the customary tenure system of mushroom collection to avoid competition between collectors.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2018.04.022","ISSN":"0378-8741","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","language":"en","page":"86-90","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Harvest and trade of caterpillar mushroom (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) and the implications for sustainable use in the Tibet Region of Southwest China","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378874118302459","volume":"221","author":[{"family":"He","given":"Jun"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7,15]]}}},{"id":31008,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PNQ2AHPY"],"itemData":{"id":31008,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2012.12.004","ISSN":"2212-0416","page":"e40-e43","title":"Conservation salvage of Cordyceps sinensis collection in the Himalayan mountains is neglected","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Kuniyal","given":"Chandra P."},{"family":"Sundriyal","given":"Rakesh C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":30910,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QBFNMA4"],"itemData":{"id":30910,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605312001330","ISSN":"0030-6053","issue":"1","language":"English","page":"30-38","title":"Fungal gold and firewood on the Tibetan plateau: examining access to diverse ecosystem provisioning services within a rural community","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Woodhouse","given":"E."},{"family":"McGowan","given":"P."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Box 3.11 Status and trends of caterpillar fungus in the Nepalese Himalayas 

Ophiocordyceps sinensis (Berk.) G.H.Sung, J.M.Sung, Hywel-Jones & Spatafora, (Hypocreales, Ophiocordycipitaceae) is a high-altitude fungus reported only from the alpine meadows in Nepal, India, Bhutan and China. Locally called Yar-tsa-gunbu (summer grass, winter insect), it occurs from 3,540 m to 5,050 m above sea level across 24 different northern districts in Nepal ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ae7b6a92bg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Devkota, 2008)","plainCitation":"(S. Devkota, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30987,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2Q75BW6U"],"itemData":{"id":30987,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Medicinal plants in Nepal: An Anthology of Contemporary Research","event-place":"Kathmandu, Nepal","page":"90-96","publisher":"Ecological Society (ECOS)","publisher-place":"Kathmandu, Nepal","title":"Approach towards the harvesting of Cordyceps sinensis (Berk.) SACC. in pastures of Dolpa, Nepal","author":[{"family":"Devkota","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and up to 5,200 m in Bhutan ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ak6qnl0k1d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cannon, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Cannon, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30759,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HEAKLTTW"],"itemData":{"id":30759,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The insect-pathogenic fungus Ophiocordyceps sinensis (better known as Cordyceps sinensis) is harvested over much of the Himalayan plateau as a highly prized remedy in traditional Oriental medicine. Over the past 10 years its ?nancial value has increased dramatically, with collectors paid as much as US $12,500 kg?1 for top-quality material. This is causing signi?cant distortion to local economies, and there is widespread concern that the current rate of collection is unsustainable. This paper introduces the fungus and its insect hosts, documents some of the biological and social constraints to achieving sustainability, describes the socioeconomic climate within which harvest and sale occurs in Bhutan, and details the measures put in place by the Royal Government of Bhutan to promote wise management of this valuable natural resource.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-009-9587-5","ISSN":"0960-3115, 1572-9710","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"2263-2281","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Steps towards sustainable harvest of Ophiocordyceps sinensis in Bhutan","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10531-009-9587-5","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Cannon","given":"Paul F."},{"family":"Hywel-Jones","given":"Nigel L."},{"family":"Maczey","given":"Norbert"},{"family":"Norbu","given":"Lungten"},{"literal":"Tshitila"},{"family":"Samdup","given":"Tashi"},{"family":"Lhendup","given":"Phurba"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is an entomopathogenic fungus that parasitizes over 50 species of Thitarodes (Hepialidae) moth larvae ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1jolmuv7a2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(X.-L. Wang & Yao, 2011)","plainCitation":"(X.-L. Wang & Yao, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30757,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S7PWVKFZ"],"itemData":{"id":30757,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ophiocordyceps sinensis (? Cordyceps sinensis) is one of the most valued medicinal fungi in China, used for its invigorating effects in strengthening the body and restoring energy. The fungus parasitizes larvae of moths and converts them into sclerotia from which the fungus fruiting body grows. Since the late 1950s, considerable effort has been devoted to the study of host insects related to the fungus. In the present paper, the research history of insect species associated with O. sinensis is briefly reviewed and an extensive literature survey is presented. Ninety-one insect names, spanning 13 genera, related to host insects of O. sinensis are investigated. T??he relationships between the reported insect species and O. sinensis are analyzed. Fiftyseven of these are considered as recognizable potential host species of the fungus distributed throughout the Tibetan Plateau, whilst eight are considered as indeterminate hosts and 26 as non-hosts. Among the names of recognizable potential host insects, three are invalid (nomen nudum) and require further study. This work provides basic information for management of the insect resources and for the conservation and sustainable use of O. sinensis.","container-title":"ZooKeys","DOI":"10.3897/zookeys.127.802","ISSN":"1313-2970, 1313-2989","journalAbbreviation":"ZK","language":"en","page":"43-59","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Host insect species of Ophiocordyceps sinensis: a review","title-short":"Host insect species of Ophiocordyceps sinensis","URL":"http://zookeys.pensoft.net/articles.php?id=2711","volume":"127","author":[{"family":"Wang","given":"Xiao-Liang"},{"family":"Yao","given":"Yi-Jian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",9,8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
     In the gathering season (May – July) and particularly when the snow melts, gathering is extensive. As many as 70,000 collectors (men, women, and children) have been reported across 25 principal gathering pastures in a single district (Dolpa of Nepal), living in temporary tent camps for about two months ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2dgr7csmai","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S Devkota, 2009)","plainCitation":"(S Devkota, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30760,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FURW26K6"],"itemData":{"id":30760,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ophiocordyceps sinensis (Berk.) G.H. Sung, J.M. Sung, Hywell-Jones & Spatafora is a highly valuable medicinal fungus. Biologically it is an entomopathogenic, entomophagous or entomophilous fungus. In order to investigate different floral associations with O. sinensis, and to know different threats to pasture biodiversity, research was conducted in three pastures of Raha and Majphal Village Development Committees of Dolpa district. The study revealed that Juncus thomsonii and Bistorta macrophylla were the principal plant associates with O. sinensis as they dominated the alpine pasture vegetation. This research also highlighted the need for some proactive solutions along with conservation awareness program as important management initiatives to ensure ecological balance of O. sinensis.  Key words: Alpine zone, flora, Ophiocordyceps sinensis, pasture management, Yarsagumba doi: 10.3126/banko.v19i1.2180 Banko Janakari, Vol. 19, No. 1, 29-36","container-title":"Banko Janakari","DOI":"10.3126/banko.v19i1.2180","ISSN":"2631-2301, 1016-0582","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Banko","language":"en","page":"29-36","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The frequency and relationship of flowering plants on the distribution pattern of <i>Ophiocordyceps sinensis</i> (Yarchagunbu) in the highlands of Dolpa district, Nepal","URL":"https://www.nepjol.info/index.php/BANKO/article/view/2180","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Devkota","given":"S"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The fungus provides a substantial source of cash income for many households: 21.1% contribution to the total household income and 53.3% to the total cash income among rural inhabitants and helping to fund childrens’ education, food purchasing, household construction and debt repayments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a13s3fmfqp2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pouliot, Pyakurel, & Smith-Hall, 2018; Shrestha & Bawa, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Pouliot, Pyakurel, & Smith-Hall, 2018; Shrestha & Bawa, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30755,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TQWMY992"],"itemData":{"id":30755,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ethnopharmacological relevance Ophiocordyceps sinensis (Berk.) G.H.Sung, J.M.Sung, Hywel-Jones & Spatafora, a high altitude Himalayan fungus-caterpillar product found in alpine meadows in China, Bhutan, Nepal, and India, has been used in the Traditional Chinese Medicine system for over 2000 years. Heightened demand in China over the past 15 years, coupled with limited production, has led to a price hike and increased economic importance of harvests to rural households throughout the species’ range. There is, however, limited knowledge on the actors and profit distribution in the O. sinensis production network , especially from the distribution areas on the southern flanks of the Himalayas. Filling in this knowledge gap is essential to the identification and design of public interventions.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2018.02.028","ISSN":"03788741","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","language":"en","page":"59-68","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"High altitude organic gold: The production network for Ophiocordyceps sinensis from far-western Nepal","title-short":"High altitude organic gold","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S037887411734059X","volume":"218","author":[{"family":"Pouliot","given":"Mariève"},{"family":"Pyakurel","given":"Dipesh"},{"family":"Smith-Hall","given":"Carsten"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",5]]}}},{"id":33685,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/32BMJDRE"],"itemData":{"id":33685,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Harvesting of Chinese caterpillar fungus, one of the most expensive biological commodities in the world, has become an important livelihood strategy for mountain communities of Nepal. However, very little is known about the role of Chinese caterpillar fungus in household economy. We estimated the economic contribution of Chinese caterpillar fungus to the household income, quanti?ed the extent of ‘‘Chinese caterpillar fungus dependence’’ among households with different economic and social characteristics, and assessed the role of cash income from the Chinese caterpillar fungus harvest in meeting various household needs including education, debt payments, and food security. Results show that Chinese caterpillar fungus income is the second largest contributor to the total household income after farm income with 21.1% contribution to the total household income and 53.3% to the total cash income. The contribution of Chinese caterpillar fungus income to total household income decreases as the household income increases making its contribution highest for the poorest households. There is signi?cant correlation between Chinese caterpillar fungus dependency and percentage of family members involved in harvesting, number of food-suf?cient months, and total income without Chinese caterpillar fungus income. Income from Chinese caterpillar fungus is helping the poorest to educate children, purchase food, and pay debts. However, reported decline of Chinese caterpillar fungus from its natural habitat might threaten local livelihoods that depend on the Chinese caterpillar fungus in future. Therefore, sustainable management of Chinese caterpillar fungus through partnership among local institutions and the state is critical in conserving the species and the sustained ?ow of bene?ts to local communities.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2014.06.019","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"194-202","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Economic contribution of Chinese caterpillar fungus to the livelihoods of mountain communities in Nepal","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320714002468","volume":"177","author":[{"family":"Shrestha","given":"Uttam Babu"},{"family":"Bawa","given":"Kamaljit S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Apart from this, subsidiary incomes in mountain communities come from farming, animal husbandry, collection and trade of other medicinal and aromatic plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"heQqyJxT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Olsen & Larsen, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Olsen & Larsen, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30758,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7SUDBRGN"],"itemData":{"id":30758,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Commercial alpine medicinal plants are collected from the wild by local rural households throughout the Himalaya and sold in order to increase household incomes. Recent studies indicate that this annual trade amounts to thousands of tonnes of roots, rhizomes, tubers, leaves, etc., worth millions of US dollars. The main market is in India. Based on a national survey, including the most commonly traded species, and a village study this paper investigates the importance of the alpine medicinal plant trade at national and local levels in Nepal. The national survey included standardized openended interviews with 232 harvesters, 64 local traders, 66 central wholesalers, 47 regional wholesalers, and 16 production companies. The village survey is based on the daily records of household activities in 15 households in a one-year period. The annual Nepalese alpine and sub-alpine medicinal plant trade is conservatively estimated to vary from 480 to 2500 t with a total harvester value of US$0.8–3.3 million; the average harvester value is estimated at US$66.0 ± 99.0. The trade in 1997/98 amounted to 1600 t with a harvester value of US$2.3 million and an export value equivalent to 2.5% of total export from Nepal. Medicinal plant harvesting was found to constitute an integrated part of local livelihood strategies, contributing from 3 to 44% (average of 12%) of the annual household income. Importance at household level depended on land and animal holdings, and the availability of adult male labour. The validity and reliability of methods and analyses is evaluated, and issues of conservation and management of alpine medicinal plant species under the community forestry scheme are discussed.","language":"en","page":"12","source":"Zotero","title":"Alpine medicinal plant trade and Himalayan 5BlackwellPublishingLtd mountain livelihood strategies","author":[{"family":"Olsen","given":"Carsten Smith"},{"family":"Larsen","given":"Helle Overgaard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
     The global annual collection of caterpillar fungus is roughly estimated at 85-185 metric tons ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2bvolmim1i","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Winkler, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Winkler, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30756,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M2SWTSIR"],"itemData":{"id":30756,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Cordyceps sinensis is a mushroom that parasitizes larvae of Thitarodes (Hepialus) moths, which inhabit the alpine grasslands of the Tibetan Plateau. Tibetans have used the mushroom, which they call yartsa gunbu (“summer-grass, winter-worm”) for many centuries, if not millennia. A 350% increase in the price paid to pickers between 1997 and 2004 has turned this tiny mushroom into the single most important source of cash for rural households in contemporary Tibet. On average, 40% of the rural cash income in the Tibet Autonomous Region is derived from its collection, which government statistics ?gured at 50,000 kg in 2004, contributing at least CNY (Chinese yuan) 1.8 billion (USD 225 million) to the Tibet Autonomous Region’s GDP. A dramatic fungal commodi?cation of the rural Tibetan economy is occurring, as the income from sale of Cordyceps often accounts for 70%–90% of a family’s annual cash income in areas where it grows. The ever-increasing harvesting pressure raises the question of sustainability. The fact that Cordyceps has been collected for centuries and is still common argues for its resilience, but the lack of harvest studies for C. sinensis precludes a de?nite answer as to whether the harvest can be sustained at its current level.","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-008-9038-3","ISSN":"0013-0001, 1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"291-305","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Yartsa Gunbu (Cordyceps sinensis) and the Fungal Commodification of Tibet’s Rural Economy","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12231-008-9038-3","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Winkler","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indigenous peoples and local communities living in the Nepalese Himalayas use it for the treatment of different diseases like diarrhea, headache, cough, rheumatism, liver disease, and also as an aphrodisiac and tonic ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ak4h37gnno","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Devkota, 2006)","plainCitation":"(S. Devkota, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32889,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BT48RECB"],"itemData":{"id":32889,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Cordyceps sinensis (Berk.) Sacc., a well-known and valued traditional medicine, is also called winter worm summer grass. The product deserves high potential to generate income opportunities, enhance rural income and raise the national revenue. The study of C. sinensis was carried out in Dolpa district, Western Nepal to document ethnomycological uses and local practices. Data were collected using open ended questionnaires administered to collectors/users (n = 74), traders (n = 25) and local healers or amchis (n = 3). Indigenous peoples are utilizing this Himalayan treasure for the treatment of different diseases like diarrhea, headache, cough, rheumatism, liver disease, and also as an aphrodisiac and tonic. Internationally it is regarded as Himalayan Viagra. The present study therefore aims at highlighting the ethnomycological uses of C. sinensis resource of the Trans-Himalayan region in their niche.","container-title":"Our Nature","DOI":"10.3126/on.v4i1.502","ISSN":"2091-2781, 1991-2951","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Our Nature","language":"en","page":"48-52","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Yarsagumba [<i>Cordyceps sinensis</i> (Berk.) Sacc.]; Traditional Utilization in Dolpa District, Western Nepal","URL":"https://www.nepjol.info/index.php/ON/article/view/502","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Devkota","given":"S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the main market is China, where there are several reasons behind increasing demand. Many consider the species as valuable medicinal fungi in accordance with traditional Chinese medicine. It is traded as the “Himalayan Viagra” and prices have exceeded 140,000 United States dollars per kg for the best quality in Chinese markets, depending upon size, color, aroma, and region of origin ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"apfq0enh7c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shrestha & Bawa, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Shrestha & Bawa, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33685,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/32BMJDRE"],"itemData":{"id":33685,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Harvesting of Chinese caterpillar fungus, one of the most expensive biological commodities in the world, has become an important livelihood strategy for mountain communities of Nepal. However, very little is known about the role of Chinese caterpillar fungus in household economy. We estimated the economic contribution of Chinese caterpillar fungus to the household income, quanti?ed the extent of ‘‘Chinese caterpillar fungus dependence’’ among households with different economic and social characteristics, and assessed the role of cash income from the Chinese caterpillar fungus harvest in meeting various household needs including education, debt payments, and food security. Results show that Chinese caterpillar fungus income is the second largest contributor to the total household income after farm income with 21.1% contribution to the total household income and 53.3% to the total cash income. The contribution of Chinese caterpillar fungus income to total household income decreases as the household income increases making its contribution highest for the poorest households. There is signi?cant correlation between Chinese caterpillar fungus dependency and percentage of family members involved in harvesting, number of food-suf?cient months, and total income without Chinese caterpillar fungus income. Income from Chinese caterpillar fungus is helping the poorest to educate children, purchase food, and pay debts. However, reported decline of Chinese caterpillar fungus from its natural habitat might threaten local livelihoods that depend on the Chinese caterpillar fungus in future. Therefore, sustainable management of Chinese caterpillar fungus through partnership among local institutions and the state is critical in conserving the species and the sustained ?ow of bene?ts to local communities.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2014.06.019","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"194-202","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Economic contribution of Chinese caterpillar fungus to the livelihoods of mountain communities in Nepal","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320714002468","volume":"177","author":[{"family":"Shrestha","given":"Uttam Babu"},{"family":"Bawa","given":"Kamaljit S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The high number of collectors, their trampling effects on fragile subalpine and alpine landscapes, wild species poaching, improper garbage disposal and annual large harvested volumes have raised several sustainability concerns ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a3vfv8kui4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Byers, Byers, Shrestha, Thapa, & Sharma, 2020; S Devkota, 2009; Pouliot, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Byers, Byers, Shrestha, Thapa, & Sharma, 2020; S Devkota, 2009; Pouliot, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30989,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/49QXQYBC"],"itemData":{"id":30989,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Around 2003, the highly valuable medicinal fungi Ophiocordyceps sinensis (Nepali: yartsa gunbu) began to be commercially harvested in the remote Barun valley of the Makalu-Barun National Park and Bufer Zone, eastern Nepal. Since then, an estimated 3,000 collectors per year have visited the valley each harvesting season, placing new pressures upon its subalpine and alpine landscapes. A review of the yartsa gunbu literature suggested that its harvesting throughout highland India, Nepal, Bhutan, and China has brought important economic benefts, but that it has ofen been accompanied by a corresponding increase in negative environmental impacts such as alpine shrub destruction, wildlife poaching, and improper garbage disposal. Adverse social impacts reported have included an increase in violence, occasional murder, and the erosion of traditional values. In an atempt to determine if similar phenomena were occurring within the Barun valley, east Nepal, we conducted a month-long study of yartsa gunbu harvesting practices between May and June of 2016. Unlike other regions of the Himalaya, we found that violence and social unrest due to harvesting competition were unheard of in the Barun, which we link to the (a) lower market value of yartsa gunbu harvested there when compared to other regions, and (b) the recognized role of yartsa gunbu as a supplemental and livelihood diversifying income generation opportunity instead of a sole source of new income. Since its collection and sale were legalized by the Government of Nepal in 2001, the concurrent development of locally responsive yartsa gunbu harvesting policies and practices can also be linked to the general absence of environmental disturbance that we found.","container-title":"Himalaya","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"44-59","source":"Zotero","title":"Impacts of Yartsa Gunbu Harvesting on Alpine Ecosystems in the Barun Valley, Makalu-Barun National Park, Nepal","URL":"https://asu.pure.elsevier.com/en/publications/impacts-of-yartsa-gunbu-harvesting-on-alpine-ecosystems-in-the-ba","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Byers","given":"Alton C"},{"family":"Byers","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Shrestha","given":"Milan"},{"family":"Thapa","given":"Dambar"},{"family":"Sharma","given":"Bidhya"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":30760,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FURW26K6"],"itemData":{"id":30760,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ophiocordyceps sinensis (Berk.) G.H. Sung, J.M. Sung, Hywell-Jones & Spatafora is a highly valuable medicinal fungus. Biologically it is an entomopathogenic, entomophagous or entomophilous fungus. In order to investigate different floral associations with O. sinensis, and to know different threats to pasture biodiversity, research was conducted in three pastures of Raha and Majphal Village Development Committees of Dolpa district. The study revealed that Juncus thomsonii and Bistorta macrophylla were the principal plant associates with O. sinensis as they dominated the alpine pasture vegetation. This research also highlighted the need for some proactive solutions along with conservation awareness program as important management initiatives to ensure ecological balance of O. sinensis.  Key words: Alpine zone, flora, Ophiocordyceps sinensis, pasture management, Yarsagumba doi: 10.3126/banko.v19i1.2180 Banko Janakari, Vol. 19, No. 1, 29-36","container-title":"Banko Janakari","DOI":"10.3126/banko.v19i1.2180","ISSN":"2631-2301, 1016-0582","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Banko","language":"en","page":"29-36","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The frequency and relationship of flowering plants on the distribution pattern of <i>Ophiocordyceps sinensis</i> (Yarchagunbu) in the highlands of Dolpa district, Nepal","URL":"https://www.nepjol.info/index.php/BANKO/article/view/2180","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Devkota","given":"S"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":30755,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TQWMY992"],"itemData":{"id":30755,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ethnopharmacological relevance Ophiocordyceps sinensis (Berk.) G.H.Sung, J.M.Sung, Hywel-Jones & Spatafora, a high altitude Himalayan fungus-caterpillar product found in alpine meadows in China, Bhutan, Nepal, and India, has been used in the Traditional Chinese Medicine system for over 2000 years. Heightened demand in China over the past 15 years, coupled with limited production, has led to a price hike and increased economic importance of harvests to rural households throughout the species’ range. There is, however, limited knowledge on the actors and profit distribution in the O. sinensis production network , especially from the distribution areas on the southern flanks of the Himalayas. Filling in this knowledge gap is essential to the identification and design of public interventions.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2018.02.028","ISSN":"03788741","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","language":"en","page":"59-68","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"High altitude organic gold: The production network for Ophiocordyceps sinensis from far-western Nepal","title-short":"High altitude organic gold","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S037887411734059X","volume":"218","author":[{"family":"Pouliot","given":"Mariève"},{"family":"Pyakurel","given":"Dipesh"},{"family":"Smith-Hall","given":"Carsten"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
     The Chinese government has supported been thoroughly making efforts to reduce dependence on wild Ophiocordyceps sinensis through cultivation and fermentation technologies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2i5tat2l11","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yue, Ye, Lin, & Zhou, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Yue, Ye, Lin, & Zhou, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30953,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KKJVSUXP"],"itemData":{"id":30953,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Medicinal Mushrooms","DOI":"10.1615/IntJMedMushr.v15.i5.10","ISSN":"1521-9437","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Int J Med Mushr","language":"en","page":"425-434","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Artificial Cultivation of Medicinal Caterpillar Fungus, Ophiocordyceps sinensis (Ascomycetes): A Review","title-short":"The Artificial Cultivation of Medicinal Caterpillar Fungus, Ophiocordyceps sinensis (Ascomycetes)","URL":"http://www.dl.begellhouse.com/journals/708ae68d64b17c52,7310c9d720cd3def,3201d8e775f2581c.html","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Yue","given":"Kai"},{"family":"Ye","given":"Meng"},{"family":"Lin","given":"Xiao"},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Zuji"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Advanced biotechnology is being applied to cultivate Paecilomyces hepialid (fermentation mycelium) with active ingredients from the natural caterpillar fungus as well as compounds of its equivalent medicinal value ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1neksipgm5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ji, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Ji, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30951,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5PF5TRAB"],"itemData":{"id":30951,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Functional Foods","DOI":"10.1016/j.jff.2020.103901","ISSN":"17564646","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Functional Foods","language":"en","page":"103901","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Comparison of bioactive constituents and effects on gut microbiota by in vitro fermentation between Ophicordyceps sinensis and Cordyceps militaris","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1756464620301250","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Ji","given":"Yang"},{"family":"Su","given":"Anxiang"},{"family":"Ma","given":"Gaoxing"},{"family":"Tao","given":"Tianyi"},{"family":"Fang","given":"Donglu"},{"family":"Zhao","given":"Liyan"},{"family":"Hu","given":"Qiuhui"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  There has been intensive focus on the artificial cultivation of the caterpillar fungus which has yielded successful approaches for its propagation and breeding ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"arhec2gs5b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(X. Li, 2019)","plainCitation":"(X. Li, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30950,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TM4SWXU9"],"itemData":{"id":30950,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Critical Reviews in Biotechnology","DOI":"10.1080/07388551.2018.1531820","ISSN":"0738-8551, 1549-7801","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Critical Reviews in Biotechnology","language":"en","page":"181-191","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A breakthrough in the artificial cultivation of Chinese cordyceps on a large-scale and its impact on science, the economy, and industry","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/07388551.2018.1531820","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Li","given":"Xiao"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Qing"},{"family":"Li","given":"Wenjia"},{"family":"Li","given":"Quanping"},{"family":"Qian","given":"Zhengming"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Xingzhong"},{"family":"Dong","given":"Caihong"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",2,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The emergence and application of culture-based techniques as a substitute for wild caterpillar fungus and the development of artificially bred varieties are a promising path towards protection and sustainable use of wild caterpillar fungus resources.

* Seeds, Leaves and fruits for medicinal use
The gathering of seeds, leaves and fruits for medicinal use is usually non-lethal and seasonal. In some cases, the average annual harvest is high but the population size is consistent, such as with Aloe ferox in South Africa and Euphorbia antisyphilitica in Mexico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2hdh2ptimk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Martinez-Balleste & Mandujano, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Martinez-Balleste & Mandujano, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30916,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RGG5CA5Y"],"itemData":{"id":30916,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-013-9229-4","ISSN":"0013-0001","issue":"2","language":"English","page":"121-136","title":"The Consequences of Harvesting on Regeneration of a Non-timber Wax Producing Species (Euphorbia antisyphilitica Zucc.) of the Chihuahuan Desert","volume":"67","author":[{"family":"Martinez-Balleste","given":"A."},{"family":"Mandujano","given":"M. C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These species were once included in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, but after assessing the sustainability of harvest and trade, their products have been exempted from strict control. In order to sustain the trade, improving the techniques of wax extraction and promoting fair trade pricing structures that benefit local harvesters have been suggested ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WyfuzboR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Martinez-Balleste & Mandujano, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Martinez-Balleste & Mandujano, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30916,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RGG5CA5Y"],"itemData":{"id":30916,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-013-9229-4","ISSN":"0013-0001","issue":"2","language":"English","page":"121-136","title":"The Consequences of Harvesting on Regeneration of a Non-timber Wax Producing Species (Euphorbia antisyphilitica Zucc.) of the Chihuahuan Desert","volume":"67","author":[{"family":"Martinez-Balleste","given":"A."},{"family":"Mandujano","given":"M. C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Certification schemes can support management for sustainable use. For example, harvesting of the fruits of Schisandra sphenanthera in Chinese forests meet sustainable wild harvesting standards, with an incentive to maintain habitat outside formal protected areas based on FairWild Standards ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2pvj88vqss","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2010)","plainCitation":"(2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30959,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q5I7CNZ2"],"itemData":{"id":30959,"type":"report","event-place":"Weinfelden, Switzerland","publisher":"FairWild Foundation","publisher-place":"Weinfelden, Switzerland","title":"FairWild Standard, Version 2.0","author":[{"family":"FairWild Foundation","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Giant Panda Friendly Products Standards ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ani2fqn07p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2012)","plainCitation":"(2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30949,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XEQFW5TB"],"itemData":{"id":30949,"type":"report","event-place":"WWF China Chengdu Programme Office, Chengdu","publisher":"WWF China","publisher-place":"WWF China Chengdu Programme Office, Chengdu","title":"Standards for Giant Panda Friendly Products (Version March 2012)","author":[{"family":"WWF China","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"af118o9fpj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brinckmann, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Brinckmann, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34280,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NQWAAZE4"],"itemData":{"id":34280,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2018.05.042","ISSN":"0378-8741","page":"522–534","title":"Sustainable harvest, people and pandas: Assessing a decade of managed wild harvest and trade in Schisandra sphenanthera.","volume":"224","author":[{"family":"Brinckmann","given":"J. A."},{"family":"Luo","given":"W."},{"family":"Xu","given":"Q."},{"family":"He","given":"X."},{"family":"Wu","given":"J."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"A. B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the absence of effective management, gathering of leaves, seeds and fruit is stressful for some sensitive species such as Aloe peglerae, Cola nitida and C. millenii which are endemic to Africa and currently endangered. Studies suggest developing silvicultural techniques to improve domestication through ex situ cultivation in gardens and orchards ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ats5ebnb5r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chungu, 2007; Lawin, 2019; Pfab & Scholes, 2004; Savi, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Chungu, 2007; Lawin, 2019; Pfab & Scholes, 2004; Savi, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30997,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CCD435TF"],"itemData":{"id":30997,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Southern Hemisphere Forestry Journal","DOI":"10.2989/SHFJ.2007.69.3.4.354","ISSN":"1991-931X","issue":"3","page":"157-163","title":"Bark removal for medicinal use predisposes indigenous forest trees to wood degradation in Zambia","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Chungu","given":"D."},{"family":"Muimba-Kankolongo","given":"A."},{"family":"Roux","given":"J."},{"family":"Malambo","given":"F. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":30912,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7JJ8UJNS"],"itemData":{"id":30912,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bois Et Forets Des Tropiques","DOI":"10.19182/bft2019.339.a31716","ISSN":"0006-579x","issue":"339","journalAbbreviation":"Bois for Trop","language":"French","page":"61-74","title":"Knowledge and uses of Cola millenii K. Schum. (Malvaceae) in the Guinean and Sudano-Guinean zones of Benin","author":[{"family":"Lawin","given":"I. F."},{"family":"Houetchegnon","given":"T."},{"family":"Fandohan","given":"A. B."},{"family":"Salako","given":"V. K."},{"family":"Assogbadjo","given":"A. E."},{"family":"Ouinsavi","given":"C. A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":30999,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PLKARUGQ"],"itemData":{"id":30999,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2003.10.018","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"5","page":"695-701","title":"Is the collection of Aloe peglerae from the wild sustainable? An evaluation using stochastic population modelling","volume":"118","author":[{"family":"Pfab","given":"Michèle F."},{"family":"Scholes","given":"Mavis A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":30767,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/45SJLKHB"],"itemData":{"id":30767,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1007/s10668-018-0091-5","ISSN":"1573-2975","issue":"3","page":"1357-1368","title":"Synergy between traditional knowledge of use and tree population structure for sustainability of Cola nitida (Vent.) Schott. & Endl in Benin (West Africa)","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Savi","given":"Merveille Koissi"},{"family":"Noumonvi","given":"Raoul"},{"family":"Chadaré","given":"Flora Josiane"},{"family":"Daïnou","given":"Kasso"},{"family":"Salako","given":"Valère Kolawolé"},{"family":"Idohou","given":"Rodrigue"},{"family":"Assogbadjo","given":"Achille Ephrem"},{"family":"Glèlè Kakaï","given":"Romain"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

* Barks and stems
Bark harvesting for medicinal purposes is widespread in Africa as a form of local and free medicine. Julbernardia paniculate and Isoberlinia angolensis are two species severely negatively affected by bark removal. Traditionally, there have been measures to reduce injuries to the tree. One form of local tree protection is to cover the wound site with mud, which protects the tree from wood deterioration and insect damage ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2cn9nmdlmk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chungu, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Chungu, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30997,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CCD435TF"],"itemData":{"id":30997,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Southern Hemisphere Forestry Journal","DOI":"10.2989/SHFJ.2007.69.3.4.354","ISSN":"1991-931X","issue":"3","page":"157-163","title":"Bark removal for medicinal use predisposes indigenous forest trees to wood degradation in Zambia","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Chungu","given":"D."},{"family":"Muimba-Kankolongo","given":"A."},{"family":"Roux","given":"J."},{"family":"Malambo","given":"F. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition to practical measures, domestic legislation can also offer local protection. For example, Warburgia salutaris is endangered and overexploited in many regions and deemed threatened throughout its range. South Africa's environmental legislation now prohibits the harvesting of protected wild plants or plant parts (e.g., the bark and leaves of Warburgia salutaris) and recommends the use of alternative species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2dikclit2h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rasethe, Semenya, & Maroyi, 2019; Senkoro, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Rasethe, Semenya, & Maroyi, 2019; Senkoro, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30768,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/69H956G7"],"itemData":{"id":30768,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine","DOI":"Artn 2609532 10.1155/2019/2609532","ISSN":"1741-427x","journalAbbreviation":"Evid-Based Compl Alt","language":"English","title":"Medicinal Plants Traded in Informal Herbal Medicine Markets of the Limpopo Province, South Africa","author":[{"family":"Rasethe","given":"M. T."},{"family":"Semenya","given":"S. S."},{"family":"Maroyi","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":30720,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/598PB5FS"],"itemData":{"id":30720,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-019-09468-x","ISSN":"0013-0001","issue":"3","page":"304–324","title":"Uses, Knowledge, and Management of the Threatened Pepper-Bark Tree (Warburgia salutaris) in Southern Mozambique","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Senkoro","given":"Annae M."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."},{"family":"Voeks","given":"Robert A."},{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"Ana I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Harvesting bark to meet medicinal demands is becoming less sustainable for some species due to increasing demand. Prunus africana in Africa and The Himalayan yew (Taxus wallichiana) are greatly threatened with unsustainable harvest. Wild-gathering of barks of Prunus africana is no longer sustainable. The population has been declining over much of its geographical range in sub-Saharan Africa in recent decades. Only recently have existing standing crop inventories and scientifically based annual quotas being determined ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1efeq950k4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fashing, 2004; K. Stewart, 2009; K. M. Stewart, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Fashing, 2004; K. Stewart, 2009; K. M. Stewart, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30730,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BWQ25FE7"],"itemData":{"id":30730,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2004.03.018","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"4","page":"449-459","title":"Mortality trends in the African cherry (Prunus africana) and the implications for colobus monkeys (Colobus guereza) in Kakamega Forest, Kenya","volume":"120","author":[{"family":"Fashing","given":"Peter J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":31002,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GEM2ZXN4"],"itemData":{"id":31002,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2009.05.039","ISSN":"0378-1127","issue":"7","page":"1121-1128","title":"Effects of bark harvest and other human activity on populations of the African cherry (Prunus africana) on Mount Oku, Cameroon","volume":"258","author":[{"family":"Stewart","given":"Kristine"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":33462,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UCD2984J"],"itemData":{"id":33462,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2003.08.002","ISSN":"0378-8741","issue":"1","page":"3-13","title":"The African cherry (Prunus africana): Can lessons be learned from an over-exploited medicinal tree?","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Stewart","given":"K. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Himalayan yew (Taxus wallichiana ) is very slow growing species with poor natural regeneration. Most wild populations in Asia Pacific forests are threatened with extinction and are endangered in the Himalaya due to over-harvesting of their barks and leaves in combination with low seed production and germination. In situ conservation and management and artificial regeneration using efficient biotechnological tools have been proposed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2g9jr4hrth","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lanker, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Lanker, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31004,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XWK9H552"],"itemData":{"id":31004,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science, Ecosystem Services & Management","DOI":"10.1080/21513732.2010.527302","ISSN":"2151-3732","issue":"1-2","page":"20-27","title":"Natural regeneration status of the endangered medicinal plant, Taxus baccata Hook. F. syn. T. wallichiana, in northwest Himalaya","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Lanker","given":"Ubeed"},{"family":"Malik","given":"A. R."},{"family":"Gupta","given":"N. K."},{"family":"Butola","given":"Jitendra S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Both of these species are included in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora to restrict international trade, with the intention to develop tools and methods for sustainable gathering or promote alternative source including cultivation.
      
* Roots, Rhizome, Tuber and Bulbils
Gathering roots, rhizome, tuber and bulbils usually does harm to plants. It is fatal to dig out all the roots and tuber. Such gathering, if not managed properly, is unsustainable. Destructive overharvesting is the key threat to Stemona tuberosa, Gymnadenia conopsea in Asia and Siphonochilus aethiopicus and Dioscorea bulbifera in Africa driven by high market demand ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aqoqbhn3ho","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. Chen, 2019; Ikiriza, 2019; Kala, 2009; Shao, 2017; Xego, Kambizi, & Nchu, 2016)","plainCitation":"(G. Chen, 2019; Ikiriza, 2019; Kala, 2009; Shao, 2017; Xego, Kambizi, & Nchu, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30721,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EE56FMFI"],"itemData":{"id":30721,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-018-1648-1","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","page":"213–227","title":"Conserving threatened widespread species: a case study using a traditional medicinal plant in Asia","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Chen","given":"Gao"},{"family":"Sun","given":"Weibang"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Xiaoxun"},{"family":"Kongkiatpaiboon","given":"Sumet"},{"family":"Cai","given":"Xianghai"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":30714,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5PSFKC8R"],"itemData":{"id":30714,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Cogent Biology","DOI":"10.1080/23312025.2019.1631561","issue":"1","title":"Dioscorea bulbifera, a highly threatened African medicinal plant, a review","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Ikiriza","given":"Hilda"},{"family":"Engeu","given":"Ogwang"},{"family":"Peter","given":"Emanuel L."},{"family":"Hedmon","given":"Okella"},{"family":"Umba","given":"Casim"},{"family":"Abubaker","given":"Muwonge"},{"family":"Abdalla","given":"Ali"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":31001,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4JXKQUUV"],"itemData":{"id":31001,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/1746-4269-5-20","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","page":"20","title":"Aboriginal uses and management of ethnobotanical species in deciduous forests of Chhattisgarh state in India.","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Kala","given":"Chandra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":30717,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B6UMTMC4"],"itemData":{"id":30717,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Plant Science","DOI":"10.3389/fpls.2017.00888","page":"888","title":"Using In Situ Symbiotic Seed Germination to Restore Over-collected Medicinal Orchids in Southwest China","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Shao","given":"Shi-Cheng"},{"family":"Burgess","given":"Kevin S."},{"family":"Cruse-Sanders","given":"Jennifer M."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Qiang"},{"family":"Fan","given":"Xu-Li"},{"family":"Huang","given":"Hui"},{"family":"Gao","given":"Jiang-Yun"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":31007,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z8NUCRSD"],"itemData":{"id":31007,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"African Journal of Traditional, Complementary and Alternative Medicines","DOI":"10.4314/ajtcam.v13i3.20","ISSN":"0189-6016","issue":"3","page":"169-180","title":"Threatened medicinal plants of South Africa: Case of the family hyacinthaceae","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Xego","given":"S."},{"family":"Kambizi","given":"L."},{"family":"Nchu","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The increasing demand on stems and roots coupled with non-sustainable harvesting methods has resulted in a substantial decline of Cryptolepis sanguinolenta in its wild populations in Africa forests. The development of domestication protocols has been suggested as one way to protect the species and decrease rates of decline ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2et973hk4c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. He, 2018; Kuniyal & Sundriyal, 2013; Woodhouse, 2014)","plainCitation":"(J. He, 2018; Kuniyal & Sundriyal, 2013; Woodhouse, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":748,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F7R3X8Y2"],"itemData":{"id":748,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ethnopharmacological relevance\\nCaterpillar mushroom (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) is a unique medicinal fungi which is only found in alpine grasslands in Himalayan mountain regions and the Tibetan Plateau. Known locally as Yartsa Gunbu, it has been widely used in Tibetan and Chinese Medicine for centuries. It is crucial to understand local commercial harvest and trade practices of caterpillar mushroom to support the sustainable management of this valuable resource. However, data derived from empirically grounded research is currently limited, particularly in China.\\nAim of this study\\nThe research aims to provide the most up-to-date insights into caterpillar mushroom harvest and trade in the main production area of the Tibet Region in Southwest China and to generate policy recommendations for sustainable use.\\nMaterials and methods\\nThe research was conducted in 2015–2016 in six Tibetan communities located in two counties in Diqing Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture, Southwest China. Quantitative and qualitative data were collected from in-depth interviews with local households engaged in caterpillar mushroom harvesting (n?=?157), local caterpillar mushroom traders (n?=?14), and from focus groups discussions (n?=?5) with regional caterpillar mushroom industry stakeholders.\\nResults\\nThe research found large regional- and community-level differences in caterpillar mushroom harvest practices. The harvest practices of communities involved in the co-management of a Nature Reserve were more sustainable than those communities not involved in such a scheme, and this was due to the external support and training provided via the co-management scheme. Moreover, a customary tenure system was proving effective for avoiding competition over caterpillar mushroom collection. However, in both counties, narrow marketing channel and non-grading system in trade limits the possibility of improving the local benefits generated from the commercial harvest of caterpillar mushroom. Meanwhile, the local traders play an important bridging role in the value chain and generate greater benefits from product grading.\\nConclusion\\nTo support the sustainable management of the caterpillar mushroom industry in Southwest China, the prefectural governments should invest in training on appropriate harvesting techniques and the dissemination of market information. It is also critical that prefectural governments recognize and support the customary tenure system of mushroom collection to avoid competition between collectors.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2018.04.022","ISSN":"0378-8741","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","language":"en","page":"86-90","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Harvest and trade of caterpillar mushroom (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) and the implications for sustainable use in the Tibet Region of Southwest China","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378874118302459","volume":"221","author":[{"family":"He","given":"Jun"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7,15]]}}},{"id":31008,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PNQ2AHPY"],"itemData":{"id":31008,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2012.12.004","ISSN":"2212-0416","page":"e40-e43","title":"Conservation salvage of Cordyceps sinensis collection in the Himalayan mountains is neglected","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Kuniyal","given":"Chandra P."},{"family":"Sundriyal","given":"Rakesh C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":30910,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QBFNMA4"],"itemData":{"id":30910,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605312001330","ISSN":"0030-6053","issue":"1","language":"English","page":"30-38","title":"Fungal gold and firewood on the Tibetan plateau: examining access to diverse ecosystem provisioning services within a rural community","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Woodhouse","given":"E."},{"family":"McGowan","given":"P."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Panax quinquefolius (American wild ginseng) is a highly valued wild root collected extensively in the United State of America s. Populations have declined significantly over time in six northern states in the United States of America and harvest pressure is now restricting harvestable stocks. Annual wild ginseng harvests decreased from the high point in the late 1980s to early 1990s, but subsequently increased after 2005. Natural rates of population recovery are slow. Market prices for this species do seem to operate on a supply and demand logic such that quantities supplied are negatively related to prices, theoretically providing economic incentives for forest retention. A federal regulation has banned exports of roots from plants under five years old in effect since 1999. Management includes stewardship-oriented harvest restrictions such as delays in the opening of the permitted harvest season by two weeks, self-limits on harvest intensity, and planting ginseng seeds at the time of harvest ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1as573clff","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Burkhart & Jacobson, 2009; Burkhart, Jacobson, & Finley, 2012; Case, Flinn, Jancaitis, Alley, & Paxton, 2007; Frey, Chamberlain, & Prestemon, 2018; J. Schmidt, Cruse-Sanders, Chamberlain, Ferreira, & Young, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Burkhart & Jacobson, 2009; Burkhart, Jacobson, & Finley, 2012; Case, Flinn, Jancaitis, Alley, & Paxton, 2007; Frey, Chamberlain, & Prestemon, 2018; J. Schmidt, Cruse-Sanders, Chamberlain, Ferreira, & Young, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30771,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/94VCYKA6"],"itemData":{"id":30771,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agroforestry Systems","DOI":"10.1007/s10457-008-9173-y","ISSN":"0167-4366","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Agroforest Syst","page":"437-453","title":"Transitioning from wild collection to forest cultivation of indigenous medicinal forest plants in eastern North America is constrained by lack of profitability","volume":"76","author":[{"family":"Burkhart","given":"Eric P."},{"family":"Jacobson","given":"Michael G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":30998,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IFESZJRZ"],"itemData":{"id":30998,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-012-0389-9","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"14","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","page":"3657–3679","title":"A case study of stakeholder perspective and experience with wild American ginseng (Panax quinquefolius) conservation efforts in Pennsylvania, U.S.A.: limitations to a CITES driven, top-down regulatory approach","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Burkhart","given":"Eric P."},{"family":"Jacobson","given":"Michael G."},{"family":"Finley","given":"James"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":30772,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WR5SQMBS"],"itemData":{"id":30772,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2006.07.018","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"1","page":"22-30","title":"Declining abundance of American ginseng (Panax quinquefolius L.) documented by herbarium specimens","volume":"134","author":[{"family":"Case","given":"Martha A."},{"family":"Flinn","given":"Kathryn M."},{"family":"Jancaitis","given":"Jean"},{"family":"Alley","given":"Ashley"},{"family":"Paxton","given":"Amy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":31012,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FE4ZCCHP"],"itemData":{"id":31012,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2018.09.011","ISSN":"1389-9341","issue":"World Dev. 29 2001","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy Econ","page":"97-109","title":"The potential for a backward-bending supply curve of non-timber forest products: An empirical case study of wild American ginseng production","volume":"97","author":[{"family":"Frey","given":"Gregory E."},{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"James L."},{"family":"Prestemon","given":"Jeffrey P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":30769,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EFUSN59B"],"itemData":{"id":30769,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2019.01.006","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"Nature 452 2008","page":"139–149","title":"Explaining harvests of wild-harvested herbaceous plants: American ginseng as a case study","volume":"231","author":[{"family":"Schmidt","given":"John"},{"family":"Cruse-Sanders","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"James L."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"Susana"},{"family":"Young","given":"John A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Some perennial wild plants can tolerate a certain degree of gathering activities. Populations of Neopicrorhiza scrophulariiflora were heavily exploited in one area of the alpine Himalayas but appear more resilient to extraction than other commercially exploited populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ac2k7m1anm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Ghimire, 2005; Poudeyal, Meilby, Shrestha, & Ghimire, 2019)","plainCitation":"(S. Ghimire, 2005; Poudeyal, Meilby, Shrestha, & Ghimire, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31000,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M4Y29NQT"],"itemData":{"id":31000,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2005.02.005","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"4","page":"463-475","title":"Conservation of Himalayan medicinal plants: Harvesting patterns and ecology of two threatened species, Nardostachys grandiflora DC. and Neopicrorhiza scrophulariiflora (Pennell) Hong","volume":"124","author":[{"family":"Ghimire","given":"Suresh"},{"family":"McKey","given":"Doyle"},{"family":"Aumeeruddy-Thomas","given":"Yildiz"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":30770,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MU8946BZ"],"itemData":{"id":30770,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecol Evol","DOI":"10.1002/ece3.5355","ISSN":"2045-7758","issue":"13","journalAbbreviation":"Ecology and evolution","page":"7726–7740","title":"Harvest effects on density and biomass of Neopicrorhiza scrophulariiflora vary along environmental gradients in the Nepalese Himalayas","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Poudeyal","given":"Mukti"},{"family":"Meilby","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Shrestha","given":"Bharat"},{"family":"Ghimire","given":"Suresh"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the American highlands, the local risk index for conservation status of Oxalis adenophylla was medium, driven by changes in its environment and not directly related to gathering. In fact, gathering of leaves and roots of this species is thought to promote its conservation through the understanding of its sensitivity to harvesting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1rdato8hnh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"\\\\uldash{(Ochoa & Ladio, 2014)}","plainCitation":"(Ochoa & Ladio, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30913,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DW2PR85M"],"itemData":{"id":30913,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2014.05.058","ISSN":"0378-8741","issue":"1","language":"English","page":"533-542","title":"Ethnoecology of Oxalis adenophylla Gillies ex Hook. & Arn.","volume":"155","author":[{"family":"Ochoa","given":"J. J."},{"family":"Ladio","given":"A. H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",8,8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}
	The rhizome of Hydrastis canadensis (goldenseal) is widely harvested in America's woodlands and has been used for traditional medicine by native peoples. Regeneration time varies between populations, making it difficult to predict overall abundance. Late-summer and fall are the ideal periods when goldenseal rhizomes are traditionally gathered ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2e6t21uteu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Albrecht & McCarthy, 2006; Burkhart & Jacobson, 2009; D. L. Christensen & Gorchov, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Albrecht & McCarthy, 2006; Burkhart & Jacobson, 2009; D. L. Christensen & Gorchov, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30996,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BTDIYDXL"],"itemData":{"id":30996,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The American Midland Naturalist","DOI":"10.1674/0003-0031(2006)156[229:CAOGHC]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0003-0031","issue":"2","page":"229-236","title":"Comparative Analysis of Goldenseal (Hydrastis canadensis L.) Population Re-growth Following Human Harvest: Implications for Conservation","volume":"156","author":[{"family":"Albrecht","given":"Matthew A."},{"family":"McCarthy","given":"Brian C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":30771,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/94VCYKA6"],"itemData":{"id":30771,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agroforestry Systems","DOI":"10.1007/s10457-008-9173-y","ISSN":"0167-4366","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Agroforest Syst","page":"437-453","title":"Transitioning from wild collection to forest cultivation of indigenous medicinal forest plants in eastern North America is constrained by lack of profitability","volume":"76","author":[{"family":"Burkhart","given":"Eric P."},{"family":"Jacobson","given":"Michael G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":31003,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z6E9I22C"],"itemData":{"id":31003,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Plant Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s11258-010-9749-2","ISSN":"1385-0237","issue":"2","page":"195-211","title":"Population dynamics of goldenseal (Hydrastis canadensis) in the core of its historical range","volume":"210","author":[{"family":"Christensen","given":"Deanna L."},{"family":"Gorchov","given":"David L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In the majority of cases, proper management and predictable harvest volumes are required to ensure that root gathering meets the need of regeneration and renewal, but habitat conditions are also critical. For example, black cohosh (Actaea racemose) is highly responsive to harvest intensity in the United States of America. Low to moderate harvest intensities and/or longer recovery periods will be necessary for prolonged and sustainable harvests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a296cle86v9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Small, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Small, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31005,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F99M8G2U"],"itemData":{"id":31005,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The American Midland Naturalist","DOI":"10.1674/0003-0031-166.2.339","ISSN":"0003-0031","issue":"2","page":"339–348","title":"Recovery of Black Cohosh (Actaea racemosa L.) Following Experimental Harvests","volume":"166","author":[{"family":"Small","given":"Christine J."},{"family":"Chamberlain","given":"James L."},{"family":"Mathews","given":"Derrick S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A low harvest rate, for example 50% of mature plants every 10 years, may be sustainable for the harvest of osha or wild parsnip (Ligusticum porter) in America's highlands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"avilk49uuu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kindscher, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Kindscher, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33999,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BYL4M64W"],"itemData":{"id":33999,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-019-09456-1","ISSN":"0013-0001","page":"1–16","title":"The Sustainable Harvest of Wild Populations of Oshá (Ligusticum porteri) in Southern Colorado for the Herbal Products Trade","author":[{"family":"Kindscher","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Martin","given":"Leanne M."},{"family":"Long","given":"Quinn"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Harvesting of Rheum acuminatum R. australe and Rhaponticum carthamoides in central Nepal can be considered sustainable under optimal management. Predictable exploitable reserves and volume of harvesting, however, partly differ between species and strongly depends on habitat conditions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a18g12n2e6k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nekratova & Shurupova, 2016; Rokaya, M\\\\uc0\\\\u252{}nzbergov\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}, & Dost\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}lek, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Nekratova & Shurupova, 2016; Rokaya, Münzbergová, & Dostálek, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31010,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XRF48FSQ"],"itemData":{"id":31010,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Key Engineering Materials","DOI":"10.4028/www.scientific.net/kem.683.433","page":"433–439","title":"Natural Resources of Medicinal Plants: Estimation of Reserves on Example of <i>Rhaponticum carthamoides</i>","volume":"683","author":[{"family":"Nekratova","given":"Natalya"},{"family":"Shurupova","given":"Margarita"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":30716,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CGU43RZP"],"itemData":{"id":30716,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Folia Geobotanica","DOI":"10.1007/s12224-017-9287-y","ISSN":"1211-9520","issue":"2","page":"239-252","title":"Sustainable harvesting strategy of medicinal plant species in Nepal – results of a six-year study","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Rokaya","given":"Maan B."},{"family":"Münzbergová","given":"Zuzana"},{"family":"Dostálek","given":"Tomáš"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Management including wild cultivation can also protect habitats. Micro-propagation can aid in re-establishing plants in their natural habitats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2a30urb9pr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ikiriza, 2019; Kala, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Ikiriza, 2019; Kala, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30714,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5PSFKC8R"],"itemData":{"id":30714,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Cogent Biology","DOI":"10.1080/23312025.2019.1631561","issue":"1","title":"Dioscorea bulbifera, a highly threatened African medicinal plant, a review","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Ikiriza","given":"Hilda"},{"family":"Engeu","given":"Ogwang"},{"family":"Peter","given":"Emanuel L."},{"family":"Hedmon","given":"Okella"},{"family":"Umba","given":"Casim"},{"family":"Abubaker","given":"Muwonge"},{"family":"Abdalla","given":"Ali"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":31001,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4JXKQUUV"],"itemData":{"id":31001,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/1746-4269-5-20","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","page":"20","title":"Aboriginal uses and management of ethnobotanical species in deciduous forests of Chhattisgarh state in India.","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Kala","given":"Chandra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Overexploitation for traditional medicine and health food sipplements, combined with habitat destruction, has resulted in the rapid decrease of Dendrobium sp. in Asia. However, epiphytic orchids planted in natural forests as part of in situ cultivation are facilitating more sustainable harvesting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2f3t64rc61","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(H. Liu, 2014, 2014; Shao, 2017)","plainCitation":"(H. Liu, 2014, 2014; Shao, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30919,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6T5HGCZV"],"itemData":{"id":30919,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-014-0661-2","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"English","page":"1215-1228","title":"Eat your orchid and have it too: a potentially new conservation formula for Chinese epiphytic medicinal orchids","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"H."},{"family":"Luo","given":"Y. B."},{"family":"Heinen","given":"J."},{"family":"Bhat","given":"M."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Z. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",5]]}}},{"id":30919,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6T5HGCZV"],"itemData":{"id":30919,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-014-0661-2","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"English","page":"1215-1228","title":"Eat your orchid and have it too: a potentially new conservation formula for Chinese epiphytic medicinal orchids","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"H."},{"family":"Luo","given":"Y. B."},{"family":"Heinen","given":"J."},{"family":"Bhat","given":"M."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Z. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",5]]}}},{"id":30717,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B6UMTMC4"],"itemData":{"id":30717,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Plant Science","DOI":"10.3389/fpls.2017.00888","page":"888","title":"Using In Situ Symbiotic Seed Germination to Restore Over-collected Medicinal Orchids in Southwest China","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Shao","given":"Shi-Cheng"},{"family":"Burgess","given":"Kevin S."},{"family":"Cruse-Sanders","given":"Jennifer M."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Qiang"},{"family":"Fan","given":"Xu-Li"},{"family":"Huang","given":"Hui"},{"family":"Gao","given":"Jiang-Yun"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Box 3.12). 

Box 3.12  The sustainable use of wild Orchids in traditional Chinese medicine

In China, orchids are traded for both ornamental and medicinal purposes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"akv18rlofa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hong Liu, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Hong Liu, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30925,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SR68WJKJ"],"itemData":{"id":30925,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2020.108484","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"108484","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Assessing conservation efforts against threats to wild orchids in China","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320719317549","volume":"243","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Hong"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Zhongjian"},{"family":"Jin","given":"Xiaohua"},{"family":"Gao","given":"Jiangyun"},{"family":"Chen","given":"Yan"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Qiang"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Di-Yang"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. About a quarter of all Chinese wild orchid species are considered traditional Chinese medicine and market demands for some of them have been extremely high ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a25p3hafjbb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(H. Liu, 2014)","plainCitation":"(H. Liu, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30919,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6T5HGCZV"],"itemData":{"id":30919,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-014-0661-2","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"English","page":"1215-1228","title":"Eat your orchid and have it too: a potentially new conservation formula for Chinese epiphytic medicinal orchids","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"H."},{"family":"Luo","given":"Y. B."},{"family":"Heinen","given":"J."},{"family":"Bhat","given":"M."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Z. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wild populations of some traditional Chinese medicinal orchids, such as those in the genus of Dendrobium, have either been extirpated or reduced to small, isolated populations. Augmentations or reintroductions are required to bring these populations back to a healthy state. 
      Recognizing the issue of high demand on exhausted natural resources, the Chinese government has embraced the conservation intervention to increase supply by farming ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a24galmaitj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hong Liu, Gale, Cheuk, & Fischer, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Hong Liu, Gale, Cheuk, & Fischer, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30922,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ET2H75T5"],"itemData":{"id":30922,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13216","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"288-299","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Conservation impacts of commercial cultivation of endangered and overharvested plants","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/cobi.13216","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Hong"},{"family":"Gale","given":"Stephan W."},{"family":"Cheuk","given":"Mang Lung"},{"family":"Fischer","given":"Gunter A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and has been very successful in encouraging massive shade house commercial cultivation of threatened traditional Chinese medicinal orchids. The total shadehouse products of Dendrobium officinale, one of the most used medicinal orchid species in China, was more than 6.4 billion United States dollars in 2011 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ps43f1bbi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(H. Liu, 2014)","plainCitation":"(H. Liu, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30919,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6T5HGCZV"],"itemData":{"id":30919,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-014-0661-2","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"English","page":"1215-1228","title":"Eat your orchid and have it too: a potentially new conservation formula for Chinese epiphytic medicinal orchids","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"H."},{"family":"Luo","given":"Y. B."},{"family":"Heinen","given":"J."},{"family":"Bhat","given":"M."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Z. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, it appears as though large commercial shadehouse cultivations have not alleviated pressure on wild populations. One reason for this is related to the public perception that cultivated products are considered to be less potent than wild harvested orchids, and so wild harvested products are considered to be of higher quality and are sold at premium prices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ce0och52j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(H. Liu, 2014)","plainCitation":"(H. Liu, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30919,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6T5HGCZV"],"itemData":{"id":30919,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-014-0661-2","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"English","page":"1215-1228","title":"Eat your orchid and have it too: a potentially new conservation formula for Chinese epiphytic medicinal orchids","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"H."},{"family":"Luo","given":"Y. B."},{"family":"Heinen","given":"J."},{"family":"Bhat","given":"M."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Z. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition, orchids growing in industrial shade houses are subject to synthetic fertilizers and pesticides, which also make the cultivated product less desirable.
      A semi-wild cultivation approach in which specimens are outplanted into native wooded areas specifically for harvesting has been implemented in a few places in southern China, such as Renhua County in northern Guangdong province, Xingyi County in Southwestern Guizhou province, and Leye County in northwestern Guangxi province. These areas are relatively undeveloped compared to the Pearl River Delta area in southern China and are within the native ranges of several medicinal Dendrobium orchids. These cultivation operations are a hybrid between commercial cultivation and population restoration because farmers can harvest certain number of stems (pseudobulbs) without killing the plants, and allow some plants to flower and fruit. Seeds produced from these plants are potential sources of population recovery and thus this form of outplanting is called “restoration-friendly cultivation” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1govmeq01p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(H. Liu, 2014)","plainCitation":"(H. Liu, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30919,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6T5HGCZV"],"itemData":{"id":30919,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-014-0661-2","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"English","page":"1215-1228","title":"Eat your orchid and have it too: a potentially new conservation formula for Chinese epiphytic medicinal orchids","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"H."},{"family":"Luo","given":"Y. B."},{"family":"Heinen","given":"J."},{"family":"Bhat","given":"M."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Z. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The market share of these semi-wild products is unknown. 
      This semi-wild or restoration-friendly cultivation approach has been suggested for other epiphytic orchids that are harvested for cultural and religious festivals and ceremonies in Latin American Countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1e48no189t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tamara Ticktin, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Tamara Ticktin, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30988,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4WHFB3HN"],"itemData":{"id":30988,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12697","ISSN":"1755-263X, 1755-263X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"CONSERVATION LETTERS","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesis of wild orchid trade and demography provides new insight on conservation strategies","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/conl.12697","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Mondragón","given":"Demetria"},{"family":"Lopez?Toledo","given":"Leonel"},{"family":"Dutra?Elliott","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Aguirre?León","given":"Ernesto"},{"family":"Hernández?Apolinar","given":"Mariana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, Mexico has more than 1,300 species of orchids ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1tnncug488","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(H\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}gsater, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Hágsater, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30930,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GBEEF96Y"],"itemData":{"id":30930,"type":"report","event-place":"Mexico City, Mexico","license":"In copyright. Digitized with the permission of the rights holder.","note":"publisher: México : Instituto de Ecología, A C., 1988-","page":"101–103","publisher":"Instituto Chinoín","publisher-place":"Mexico City, Mexico","title":"Las orquídeas de México","URL":"https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/part/267080","author":[{"family":"Hágsater","given":"E"},{"family":"Soto-Arenas","given":"M A"},{"family":"Salazar-Chávez","given":"G A"},{"family":"Jiménez-Machorro","given":"R"},{"family":"López-Rosas","given":"M A"},{"family":"Dressler","given":"R L"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and among these more than 300 orchid species in 90 genera were used for religious and cultural celebrations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"asdtothgd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Menchaca Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a, Lozano Rodr\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}guez, & S\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}nchez Morales, 2012; Tamara Ticktin, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Menchaca García, Lozano Rodríguez, & Sánchez Morales, 2012; Tamara Ticktin, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30929,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CPREJAUZ"],"itemData":{"id":30929,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Revista mexicana de ciencias forestales","issue":"13","page":"09–16","title":"Strategies for the sustainable harvesting of Mexican orchids","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Menchaca García","given":"Rebeca Alicia"},{"family":"Lozano Rodríguez","given":"Miguel Ángel"},{"family":"Sánchez Morales","given":"Lorena"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":30988,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4WHFB3HN"],"itemData":{"id":30988,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12697","ISSN":"1755-263X, 1755-263X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"CONSERVATION LETTERS","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesis of wild orchid trade and demography provides new insight on conservation strategies","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/conl.12697","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Mondragón","given":"Demetria"},{"family":"Lopez?Toledo","given":"Leonel"},{"family":"Dutra?Elliott","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Aguirre?León","given":"Ernesto"},{"family":"Hernández?Apolinar","given":"Mariana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. About a dozen of these species (e.g., Laelia speciosa, Euchile karwinskii, Barkeria vanneriana) are traded in high volumes legally and illegally ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1bm2b2s7ot","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tamara Ticktin, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Tamara Ticktin, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30988,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4WHFB3HN"],"itemData":{"id":30988,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12697","ISSN":"1755-263X, 1755-263X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"CONSERVATION LETTERS","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesis of wild orchid trade and demography provides new insight on conservation strategies","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/conl.12697","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Mondragón","given":"Demetria"},{"family":"Lopez?Toledo","given":"Leonel"},{"family":"Dutra?Elliott","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Aguirre?León","given":"Ernesto"},{"family":"Hernández?Apolinar","given":"Mariana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There have been attempts to establish a rural community nursery system in the relevant areas, in which farmers were encouraged to plant these orchids in their backyard and adjacent community forests. The nurseries were then registered as Environmental Management Units (UMA, for their acronym in Spanish) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ickdc15ea","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Menchaca Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Menchaca García, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30929,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CPREJAUZ"],"itemData":{"id":30929,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Revista mexicana de ciencias forestales","issue":"13","page":"09–16","title":"Strategies for the sustainable harvesting of Mexican orchids","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Menchaca García","given":"Rebeca Alicia"},{"family":"Lozano Rodríguez","given":"Miguel Ángel"},{"family":"Sánchez Morales","given":"Lorena"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The nursery system was intended to promote sustainable harvesting in rural communities, as non-lethal harvesting can be done sustainably from the nurseries which then in turn allow wild populations to recover, as shown in population viability simulation models in Ticktin ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1qc1q2kn6m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2020)","plainCitation":"(2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30988,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4WHFB3HN"],"itemData":{"id":30988,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12697","ISSN":"1755-263X, 1755-263X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"CONSERVATION LETTERS","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesis of wild orchid trade and demography provides new insight on conservation strategies","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/conl.12697","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Mondragón","given":"Demetria"},{"family":"Lopez?Toledo","given":"Leonel"},{"family":"Dutra?Elliott","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Aguirre?León","given":"Ernesto"},{"family":"Hernández?Apolinar","given":"Mariana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Semi-wild or restoration-friendly cultivation operations have positive impacts on sustainable use, but are not widespread in comparison with harvest quantities. Ecological and socioecological infrastructure needs to be developed and supported to achieve orchid conservation and support livelihoods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2qm1ipja98","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(H. Liu, 2014)","plainCitation":"(H. Liu, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30919,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6T5HGCZV"],"itemData":{"id":30919,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-014-0661-2","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"English","page":"1215-1228","title":"Eat your orchid and have it too: a potentially new conservation formula for Chinese epiphytic medicinal orchids","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"H."},{"family":"Luo","given":"Y. B."},{"family":"Heinen","given":"J."},{"family":"Bhat","given":"M."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Z. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, mass reproduction centers coordinated with farmers to deliver enough plants for semi-wild planting requires support ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a276h2cqeoi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Menchaca Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Menchaca García, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30929,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CPREJAUZ"],"itemData":{"id":30929,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Revista mexicana de ciencias forestales","issue":"13","page":"09–16","title":"Strategies for the sustainable harvesting of Mexican orchids","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Menchaca García","given":"Rebeca Alicia"},{"family":"Lozano Rodríguez","given":"Miguel Ángel"},{"family":"Sánchez Morales","given":"Lorena"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These centers can also provide technical support on growing and harvesting and marketing support. Restoration-friendly cultivation can directly facilitate the recovery of threatened species, encourage protection of natural forests, and benefit marginalized rural communities. However, it is unclear exactly what ecological growth conditions, harvesting regimes, and market conditions are suitable to achieve population restoration while generating enough income for participants, and what policies are needed to enable marginalized rural small holders to engage with the centers.

3.3.2.3.6. Recreation

Many of the other uses covered throughout section 3.3.2 include some sort of recreational component. Only a few examples are provided here that stand out in terms of their recreational value.
      A trend has been observed in recent years to promote forest management and sustainable use by combining gathering of wild algae, fungi and plants with non-extractive practices such as tourism. For example, mycological tourism is growing in popularity, often associated with amateur societies in North America and Europe, where people go mushroom gathering, harvest wild mushrooms and then identify them with the help of professionals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a28lhggbm32","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barron, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Barron, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31160,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CEHLMDKQ"],"itemData":{"id":31160,"type":"book","publisher":"Rutgers The State University of New Jersey-New Brunswick","title":"Situated knowledge and fungal conservation: Morel mushroom management in the mid-Atlantic region of the United States","author":[{"family":"Barron","given":"Elizabeth S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. On one hand it is considered professional exploitation of wild resources, o the other hand it is a form of forest management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a17ib5upi6p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jim\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}nez-Ruiz, Thom\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}-Ortiz, Espinoza-Ortega, & Vizcarra Bordi, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Jiménez-Ruiz, Thomé-Ortiz, Espinoza-Ortega, & Vizcarra Bordi, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30871,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6UGENDMH"],"itemData":{"id":30871,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bosque (Valdivia)","DOI":"10.4067/S0717-92002017000300002","ISSN":"0717-9200","issue":"3","page":"447-456","source":"SciELO","title":"Aprovechamiento recreativo de los hongos comestibles silvestres: casos de micoturismo en el mundo con énfasis en México","title-short":"Aprovechamiento recreativo de los hongos comestibles silvestres","URL":"https://scielo.conicyt.cl/scielo.php?script=sci_abstract&pid=S0717-92002017000300002&lng=en&nrm=iso&tlng=es","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Jiménez-Ruiz","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Thomé-Ortiz","given":"Humberto"},{"family":"Espinoza-Ortega","given":"Angélica"},{"family":"Vizcarra Bordi","given":"Ivonne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In fact, amateur mycological associations continue to grow and are considered a valuable resource by mycologists for everything from taxonomic assistance to data collection ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a257lj5rslj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barron, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Barron, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30762,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CN4Y2YN9"],"itemData":{"id":30762,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fungal conservation emerged in the 1980s when mycologists gained considerable insight into fluctuations and trends in fungal populations throughout Europe and the United States of America. Concerns arose over the possible decline of many macrofungi and likely consequences for ecosystem function and global change. Increased harvesting of edible species for international markets also sparked concerns about overharvesting and associated habitat disturbance, particularly in the U.S.A. This paper identifies two social networks that have coalesced around these concerns. They are: (1) professional, and (2) non-professional. Professional mycologists are active in societies, international forums, and governments. A wide range of non-professionals engage with fungi for recreational, scientific, economic and subsistence purposes. The paper identifies areas of collaboration and disconnection among these social networks and their composites, and suggests benefits from further integration that will strengthen this nascent field.","collection-title":"Conservation Underground: Fungi in a Changing World","container-title":"Fungal Ecology","DOI":"10.1016/j.funeco.2010.09.009","ISSN":"1754-5048","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Fungal Ecology","language":"en","page":"124-133","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The emergence and coalescence of fungal conservation social networks in Europe and the U.S.A.","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1754504810000681","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Barron","given":"Elizabeth S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",4,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Many cultural services and values support recreational gathering of wild species. In the Northeastern United States of America, gathering wild edible huckleberries has been related to maintaining social relations, recreational use and commercial purposes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a20o9ehpr4s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carroll, Blatner, & Cohn, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Carroll, Blatner, & Cohn, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30889,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYC5652C"],"itemData":{"id":30889,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Rural Sociology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1549-0831.2003.tb00140.x","ISSN":"1549-0831","issue":"3","page":"319-342","title":"Somewhere Between: Social Embeddedness and the Spectrum of Wild Edible Huckleberry Harvest and Use","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Carroll","given":"Matthew S."},{"family":"Blatner","given":"Keith A."},{"family":"Cohn","given":"Patricia J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Spain, while the gathering and consumption of wild edible plants is generally decreasing, there is an increase in the harvest of foods with high cultural value ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a16htbrgbgo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Reyes-Garcia, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Reyes-Garcia, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31141,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7KLXTWXH"],"itemData":{"id":31141,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2015.11.003","ISSN":"0921-8009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Econ","language":"English","page":"303-311","title":"From famine foods to delicatessen: Interpreting trends in the use of wild edible plants through cultural ecosystem services","volume":"120","author":[{"family":"Reyes-Garcia","given":"V."},{"family":"Menendez-Baceta","given":"G."},{"family":"Aceituno-Mata","given":"L."},{"family":"Acosta-Naranjo","given":"R."},{"family":"Calvet-Mir","given":"L."},{"family":"Dominguez","given":"P."},{"family":"Garnatje","given":"T."},{"family":"Gomez-Baggethun","given":"E."},{"family":"Molina-Bustamante","given":"M."},{"family":"Molina","given":"M."},{"family":"Rodriguez-Franco","given":"R."},{"family":"Serrasolses","given":"G."},{"family":"Valles","given":"J."},{"family":"Pardo-de-Santayana","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Austria, interviews in 2008-2009 reveal the multiple motivations for gathering wild plants; women, older respondents and home gardeners gather wild plants more often for fun ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a143e68irpm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schunko, Grasser, & Vogl, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Schunko, Grasser, & Vogl, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31172,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4HRZE22H"],"itemData":{"id":31172,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Background: Wild plant gathering becomes again a popular and fashionable activity in Europe after gathering practices have been increasingly abandoned over the last decades. Recent ethnobotanical research documented a diversity of gathering practices from people of diverse socio-economic and cultural backgrounds who gather in urban and rural areas. Few efforts were though made to study the motivations for gathering wild plants and to understand the resurgent popularity of wild plant gathering. This paper addresses the following research questions: (1) which motivations activate wild plant gatherers? (2) which motivation-types of gatherers exist in the Grosses Walsertal? (3) how do the motivations for gathering relate to the socio-demographic background of gatherers?\\nMethods: Field research was conducted in the Grosses Walsertal, Austria in the years 2008 and 2009 in two field research periods. Thirty-six local farmers were first interviewed with semi-structured interviews. The motivations identified in these interviews were then included in a structured questionnaire, which was used to interview 353 residents of the valley. Pupils of local schools participated in the data collection as interviewers. Principal Component Analysis was used to categorize the motivations and to identify motivation-types of wild plant gatherers. Generalized Linear Models were calculated to identify relations between motivations and the socio-demographic background of gatherers.\\nResults: The respondents listed 13 different motivations for gathering wild plants and four motivations for not gathering. These 17 motivations were grouped in five motivation-types of wild plant gatherers, which are in decreasing importance: product quality, fun, tradition, not-gathering, income. Women, older respondents and homegardeners gather wild plants more often for fun; older respondents gather more often for maintaining traditions; non-homegardeners more frequently mention motivations for not gathering.\\nConclusions: The resurgent popularity of wild plant gathering comes along with an internalization of motivations: the main motivations for wild plant gathering changed from the external extrinsic motivation of gathering because of necessity towards the internalized extrinsic motivation of gathering for the highly esteemed product quality and the intrinsic motivation of gathering for the pleasure of the activity itself. This internalization of motivations supports the persistence of wild plant gathering, a positive self-perception of gatherers and good quality of engagement with wild plant gathering.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-015-0032-4","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"55","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Explaining the resurgent popularity of the wild: motivations for wild plant gathering in the Biosphere Reserve Grosses Walsertal, Austria","title-short":"Explaining the resurgent popularity of the wild","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-015-0032-4","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Schunko","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Grasser","given":"Susanne"},{"family":"Vogl","given":"Christian R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
3.3.2.3.7. Science and education 

Around the world, gathering wild specimens continues to generate information of scientific value. This includes dried plants and fungi for herbaria and fungaria, living plants and fungal cultures grown by botanical gardens and mycological institutes, and seeds stored in seed banks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2cjjsq2e5g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Antonelli, 2020; Paton, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Antonelli, 2020; Paton, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30901,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G9883U5U"],"itemData":{"id":30901,"type":"report","note":"DOI: 10.34885/172","publisher":"Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"State of the World's Plants and Fungi 2020","URL":"https://kew.iro.bl.uk/work/23394899-864d-4ef0-b864-857efea500c5","author":[{"family":"Antonelli","given":"A."},{"family":"Fry","given":"C."},{"family":"Smith","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Simmonds","given":"M.S.J."},{"family":"Kersey","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Pritchard","given":"H.W."},{"family":"Abbo","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Acedo","given":"C."},{"family":"Adams","given":"J."},{"family":"Ainsworth","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Allkin","given":"B."},{"family":"Annecke","given":"W."},{"family":"Bachman","given":"S.P."},{"family":"Bacon","given":"K."},{"family":"Bárrios","given":"S."},{"family":"Barstow","given":"C."},{"family":"Battison","given":"A."},{"family":"Bell","given":"E."},{"family":"Bensusan","given":"K."},{"family":"Bidartondo","given":"M.I."},{"family":"Blackhall-Miles","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Borrell","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Brearley","given":"F.Q."},{"family":"Breman","given":"E."},{"family":"Brewer","given":"R.F.A."},{"family":"Brodie","given":"J."},{"family":"Cámara-Leret","given":"R."},{"family":"Campostrini Forzza","given":"R."},{"family":"Cannon","given":"P."},{"family":"Carine","given":"M."},{"family":"Carretero","given":"J."},{"family":"Cavagnaro","given":"T.R."},{"family":"Cazar","given":"M.-E."},{"family":"C