ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a11oos420dr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Epstein, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Epstein, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31086,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2G5SHEXY"],"itemData":{"id":31086,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wolves are protected by law in both the United States (US) and European Union (EU). These laws restrict the harming or killing of individual members of protected species, but allow it in selective circumstances, such as when killing some individuals would benefit the species. In both unions, some states have argued that allowing the public hunting of wolves would in fact benefit the species by improving social tolerance for wolves, a claim that is currently the subject of controversy among scientists. In the absence of clear evidence that hunting is favourable for wolf populations, US courts have repeatedly struck down policies that allowed it. While hunting wolves to achieve their social acceptability is likely to also violate EU law, the EU court has not yet resolved the question and hunting for social acceptance continues in some Member States, such as Sweden and Finland. This article contrasts these legal responses to social ‘tolerance hunting’ and argues that the Habitats Directive should not be interpreted to allow tolerance hunting of strictly protected species. It then uses the contrasting legal situations to engage with the claim that the EU has become more ‘precautionary’ than the US on environmental matters.","container-title":"Review of European, Comparative & International Environmental Law","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/reel.12188","ISSN":"2050-0394","issue":"1","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/reel.12188","page":"19-29","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Killing Wolves to Save Them? Legal Responses to ‘Tolerance Hunting’ in the European Union and United States","title-short":"Killing Wolves to Save Them?","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/reel.12188","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Epstein","given":"Yaffa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}
Waterfowl 
LC – least concern 
North America 
ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"25cj0du6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. G. Anderson & Padding, 2015; Hirschfeld, 2019; P. Mahoney & Geist, 2019; Mustin, 2012; Reimoser & Reimoser, 2016)","plainCitation":"(M. G. Anderson & Padding, 2015; Hirschfeld, 2019; P. Mahoney & Geist, 2019; Mustin, 2012; Reimoser & Reimoser, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31071,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YBCCKZEL"],"itemData":{"id":31071,"type":"article-journal","language":"en","page":"72","source":"Zotero","title":"Biodiversity impacts of game bird hunting and associated management practices in Europe and North America","author":[{"family":"Mustin","given":"Karen"},{"family":"Newey","given":"Scott"},{"family":"Irvine","given":"Justin"},{"family":"Arroyo","given":"Beatriz"},{"family":"Redpath","given":"Steve"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":31075,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/47G7LCLE"],"itemData":{"id":31075,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Environmental Studies","DOI":"10.1080/00207233.2015.1019296","ISSN":"0020-7233, 1029-0400","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Environmental Studies","language":"en","page":"810-829","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The North American approach to waterfowl management: synergy of hunting and habitat conservation","title-short":"The North American approach to waterfowl management","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00207233.2015.1019296","volume":"72","author":[{"family":"Anderson","given":"Michael G."},{"family":"Padding","given":"Paul I."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",9,3]]}}},{"id":1177,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LGDKNFDB"],"itemData":{"id":1177,"type":"article-journal","collection-title":"Beiträge zur Jagd- und Wildforschung","language":"en","page":"29-43","source":"Zotero","title":"Long-term trends of hunting bags and wildlife populations in Central Europe","URL":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/312072090_Long-term_trends_of_hunting_bags_and_wildlife_populations_in_Central_Europe","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Reimoser","given":"Friedrich"},{"family":"Reimoser","given":"Susanne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":31052,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R3ZC63ME"],"itemData":{"id":31052,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Of?cial hunting statistics from 24 EU member states as well as Switzerland and Norway were collated. Data on 82 permissible quarry species of bird (listed in Annex II of the EU Birds Directive), mostly from the hunting season 2014/15 but in some cases from earlier years, were evaluated and compared with a previous study. The results show a total annual hunting bag of at least 52 million birds. In addition, a signi?cant number of birds are killed, legally or illegally, in areas just beyond the study area and in European countries for which no data were available (Greece, Ireland, the Netherlands and the UK). Despite a long-term decrease in the gross number of killings compared with older data, the bag ?gures for certain species, such as Common Pochard Aythya ferina, Northern Lapwing Vanellus vanellus, Turtle Dove Streptopelia turtur and Skylark Alauda arvensis, remain high in proportion to their declining populations in Europe. Against a backdrop of declining populations of many affected species, hunting pressures may be undermining conservation efforts undertaken for these species in other countries.","container-title":"British Birds","language":"en","page":"14","source":"Zotero","title":"an analysis of bag ?gures and the potential impact on the conservation of threatened species","author":[{"family":"Hirschfeld","given":"Axel"},{"family":"Attard","given":"Geraldine"},{"family":"Scott","given":"Lloyd"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":26,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HNUMASNM"],"itemData":{"id":26,"type":"book","number-of-pages":"176","publisher":"Johns Hopkins University Press Books","title":"The North American Model of Wildlife Conservation","URL":"https://jhupbooks.press.jhu.edu/title/north-american-model-wildlife-conservation","author":[{"family":"Mahoney","given":"P."},{"family":"Geist","given":"V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}
Mallard (Anas platyrhynchos) 
LC – least concern, 
increasing 
Europe, North America 
ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"t4nK9F9c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Mahoney & Geist, 2019; J. D. Nichols, Runge, Johnson, & Williams, 2007)","plainCitation":"(P. Mahoney & Geist, 2019; J. D. Nichols, Runge, Johnson, & Williams, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31103,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CUZPVNF7"],"itemData":{"id":31103,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Since 1995, the US Fish and Wildlife Service has used an adaptive approach to the management of sport harvest of mid-continent Mallard ducks (Anas platyrhynchos) in North America. This approach differs from many current approaches to conservation and management in requiring close collaboration between managers and scientists. Key elements of this process are objectives, alternative management actions, models permitting prediction of system responses, and a monitoring program. The iterative process produces optimal management decisions and leads to reduction in uncertainty about response of populations to management. This general approach to management has a number of desirable features and is recommended for use in many other programs of management and conservation.","container-title":"Journal of Ornithology","DOI":"10.1007/s10336-007-0256-8","ISSN":"1439-0361","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"J Ornithol","language":"en","page":"343-349","source":"Springer Link","title":"Adaptive harvest management of North American waterfowl populations: a brief history and future prospects","title-short":"Adaptive harvest management of North American waterfowl populations","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10336-007-0256-8","volume":"148","author":[{"family":"Nichols","given":"James D."},{"family":"Runge","given":"Michael C."},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Fred A."},{"family":"Williams","given":"Byron K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",12,1]]}}},{"id":26,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HNUMASNM"],"itemData":{"id":26,"type":"book","number-of-pages":"176","publisher":"Johns Hopkins University Press Books","title":"The North American Model of Wildlife Conservation","URL":"https://jhupbooks.press.jhu.edu/title/north-american-model-wildlife-conservation","author":[{"family":"Mahoney","given":"P."},{"family":"Geist","given":"V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}
