	Selective timber-harvesting is one of the dominant logging practices that contributes nearly 15 percent of global timber needs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1aj5n9f2rm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Martin, Newton, Pfeifer, Khoo, & Bullock, 2015; B. H. Poudyal, 2018)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Martin, Newton, Pfeifer, Khoo, & Bullock, 2015; B. H. Poudyal, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31978,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YDA7YEKL"],"itemData":{"id":31978,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2015.07.010","ISSN":"03781127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"224-233","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impacts of tropical selective logging on carbon storage and tree species richness: A meta-analysis","title-short":"Impacts of tropical selective logging on carbon storage and tree species richness","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112715003837","volume":"356","author":[{"family":"Martin","given":"Philip A."},{"family":"Newton","given":"Adrian C."},{"family":"Pfeifer","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Khoo","given":"MinSheng"},{"family":"Bullock","given":"James M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",11]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":30341,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S65S5IDN","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2B39WUKE"],"itemData":{"id":30341,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2018.08.006","ISSN":"03781127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"166-175","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Evolutionary dynamics of selective logging in the tropics: A systematic review of impact studies and their effectiveness in sustainable forest management","title-short":"Evolutionary dynamics of selective logging in the tropics","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112718310806","volume":"430","author":[{"family":"Poudyal","given":"Bishnu Hari"},{"family":"Maraseni","given":"Tek"},{"family":"Cockfield","given":"Geoff"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Selective timber-harvesting can be low impact timber-harvesting when it involves harvesting 1-2 species and 1-2 individuals per hectare. It can be moderate when 5-15 species are harvested and 1-3 individuals per hectare ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aq073l0luc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Uhl, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Uhl, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32014,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GV6IUILT"],"itemData":{"id":32014,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.2307/1313035","ISSN":"00063568, 15253244","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"BioScience","language":"en","page":"160-168","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Natural Resource Management in the Brazilian Amazon","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/bioscience/article-lookup/doi/10.2307/1313035","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Uhl","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Barreto","given":"Paulo"},{"family":"Vidal","given":"Edson"},{"family":"Amaral","given":"Paulo"},{"family":"Barros","given":"Ana Cristina"},{"family":"Souza,","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Johns","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Gerwing","given":"Jeffrey"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The practice is done on a large scale through mechanized tree extraction or on a small scale through manual extraction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a5q96el474","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rend\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}n-Carmona, Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}nez-Yr\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}zar, Balvanera, & P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez-Salicrup, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Rendón-Carmona, Martínez-Yrízar, Balvanera, & Pérez-Salicrup, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31955,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7E5JHKDE"],"itemData":{"id":31955,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2008.09.031","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"567-579","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Selective cutting of woody species in a Mexican tropical dry forest: Incompatibility between use and conservation","title-short":"Selective cutting of woody species in a Mexican tropical dry forest","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S037811270800724X","volume":"257","author":[{"family":"Rendón-Carmona","given":"Humberto"},{"family":"Martínez-Yrízar","given":"Angelina"},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Pérez-Salicrup","given":"Diego"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The practice can also be carried out by local people who hand harvest wood in exchange for staples, while large distant companies do the wood processing	
	Globally selective logging is practiced on about 20.3% (3.9 million km2) of humid tropical forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a119aviah6n","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Asner, Rudel, Aide, Defries, & Emerson, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Asner, Rudel, Aide, Defries, & Emerson, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31979,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ISL57MS2"],"itemData":{"id":31979,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In recent decades the rate and geographic extent of land-use and land-cover change has increased throughout the world’s humid tropical forests. The pan-tropical geography of forest change is a challenge to assess, and improved estimates of the human footprint in the tropics are critical to understanding potential changes in biodiversity. We combined recently published and new satellite observations, along with images from Google Earth and a literature review, to estimate the contemporary global extent of deforestation, selective logging, and secondary regrowth in humid tropical forests. Roughly 1.4% of the biome was deforested between 2000 and 2005. As of 2005, about half of the humid tropical forest biome contained 50% or less tree cover. Although not directly comparable to deforestation, geographic estimates of selective logging indicate that at least 20% of the humid tropical forest biome was undergoing some level of timber harvesting between 2000 and 2005. Forest recovery estimates are even less certain, but a compilation of available reports suggests that at least 1.2% of the humid tropical forest biome was in some stage of long-term secondary regrowth in 2000. Nearly 70% of the regrowth reports indicate forest regeneration in hilly, upland, and mountainous environments considered marginal for large-scale agriculture and ranching. Our estimates of the human footprint are conservative because they do not resolve very small-scale deforestation, low-intensity logging, and unreported secondary regrowth, nor do they incorporate other impacts on tropical forest ecosystems, such as fire and hunting. Our results highlight the enormous geographic extent of forest change throughout the humid tropics and the considerable limitations of the science and technology available for such a synthesis.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2009.01333.x","ISSN":"08888892, 15231739","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1386-1395","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A Contemporary Assessment of Change in Humid Tropical Forests","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2009.01333.x","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Asner","given":"Gregory P."},{"family":"Rudel","given":"Thomas K."},{"family":"Aide","given":"T. Mitchell"},{"family":"Defries","given":"Ruth"},{"family":"Emerson","given":"Ruth"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Selective logging is considered unsustainable when it is carried out the conventional way without measures to reduce damage to the residual forest stand. It is considered sustainable when specific planning and techniques are used to minimize damage to the residual stand. Several of these techniques are included in guidelines which are referred to as Reduced Impact Logging (RIL) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ed4su92cf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arets, 2011; Dykstra & Heinrich, 1996; Pinard, Putz, Tay, & Sullivan, 1995; F. E. Putz, Sist, Fredericksen, & Dykstra, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Arets, 2011; Dykstra & Heinrich, 1996; Pinard, Putz, Tay, & Sullivan, 1995; F. E. Putz, Sist, Fredericksen, & Dykstra, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31977,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AULDBN8M"],"itemData":{"id":31977,"type":"report","event-place":"Alterra","number":"1808","publisher":"Wageningen-UR","publisher-place":"Alterra","title":"Global wood production: assessment of industrial round wood supply from forest management systems in different global regions (No. 1808). Alterra, Wageningen-UR.","author":[{"family":"Arets","given":"E. J. M. M"},{"family":"Van der Meer","given":"P. J"},{"family":"Verwer","given":"C. C"},{"family":"Hengeveld","given":"G. M"},{"family":"Tolkamp","given":"G. W"},{"family":"Nabuurs","given":"G. J"},{"family":"Van Oorschot","given":"M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31964,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VY4CIA6L"],"itemData":{"id":31964,"type":"book","call-number":"SD538 .D94 1996","event-place":"Rome : Lanham, MD","ISBN":"978-92-5-103690-7","number-of-pages":"85","publisher":"FAO","publisher-place":"Rome : Lanham, MD","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"FAO model code of forest harvesting practice","author":[{"family":"Dykstra","given":"Dennis P."},{"family":"Heinrich","given":"R."}],"contributor":[{"family":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32015,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZNA2BG8R"],"itemData":{"id":32015,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Forestry","issue":"10","page":"41-45","title":"Creating timber harvest guidelines for a reduced-impact logging project in Malaysia.","URL":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/279674351_Creating_timber_harvest_guidelines_for_a_Reduced-Impact_Logging_Project_in_Malaysia","volume":"93","author":[{"family":"Pinard","given":"Michelle A"},{"family":"Putz","given":"Francis E"},{"family":"Tay","given":"John"},{"family":"Sullivan","given":"Thomas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31264,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R3GAA65W"],"itemData":{"id":31264,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2008.03.036","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"1427-1433","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Reduced-impact logging: Challenges and opportunities","title-short":"Reduced-impact logging","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112708002892","volume":"256","author":[{"family":"Putz","given":"Francis E."},{"family":"Sist","given":"P."},{"family":"Fredericksen","given":"T."},{"family":"Dykstra","given":"D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Reduced impact logging is implemented at the operational level by planning skid trails, practicing carefully controlled felling and skidding, and reducing damage to soils and residual trees ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mRVb9v4g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Sist & Ferreira, 2007)","plainCitation":"(P. Sist & Ferreira, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":28485,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P852IRJR"],"itemData":{"id":28485,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2007.02.014","issue":"2–3","language":"en","page":"199–209","title":"Sustainability of reduced-impact logging in the Eastern Amazon","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.foreco.2007.02.014","volume":"243","author":[{"family":"Sist","given":"P."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"F.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Implementation of reduced impact logging is still limited ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"avfr1g6v1v","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arets, 2011; P. A. Martin, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Arets, 2011; P. A. Martin, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31977,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AULDBN8M"],"itemData":{"id":31977,"type":"report","event-place":"Alterra","number":"1808","publisher":"Wageningen-UR","publisher-place":"Alterra","title":"Global wood production: assessment of industrial round wood supply from forest management systems in different global regions (No. 1808). Alterra, Wageningen-UR.","author":[{"family":"Arets","given":"E. J. M. M"},{"family":"Van der Meer","given":"P. J"},{"family":"Verwer","given":"C. C"},{"family":"Hengeveld","given":"G. M"},{"family":"Tolkamp","given":"G. W"},{"family":"Nabuurs","given":"G. J"},{"family":"Van Oorschot","given":"M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31978,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YDA7YEKL"],"itemData":{"id":31978,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2015.07.010","ISSN":"03781127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"224-233","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impacts of tropical selective logging on carbon storage and tree species richness: A meta-analysis","title-short":"Impacts of tropical selective logging on carbon storage and tree species richness","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112715003837","volume":"356","author":[{"family":"Martin","given":"Philip A."},{"family":"Newton","given":"Adrian C."},{"family":"Pfeifer","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Khoo","given":"MinSheng"},{"family":"Bullock","given":"James M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",11]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and conventional logging practices continue to dominate ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a11sdd50lrm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. E. Putz, Dykstra, & Heinrich, 2000)","plainCitation":"(F. E. Putz, Dykstra, & Heinrich, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31963,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/54GGJRH5"],"itemData":{"id":31963,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1046/j.1523-1739.2000.99137.x","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"951-956","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Why Poor Logging Practices Persist in the Tropics","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1046/j.1523-1739.2000.99137.x","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Putz","given":"Francis E."},{"family":"Dykstra","given":"Dennis P."},{"family":"Heinrich","given":"Rudolf"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",8,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Among the major factors hindering adaptation of reduced impact logging is the expense in comparison with conventional timber-harvesting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ad7dhaec6b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. E. Putz, 2000, 2008)","plainCitation":"(F. E. Putz, 2000, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31963,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/54GGJRH5"],"itemData":{"id":31963,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1046/j.1523-1739.2000.99137.x","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"951-956","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Why Poor Logging Practices Persist in the Tropics","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1046/j.1523-1739.2000.99137.x","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Putz","given":"Francis E."},{"family":"Dykstra","given":"Dennis P."},{"family":"Heinrich","given":"Rudolf"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",8,15]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31264,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R3GAA65W"],"itemData":{"id":31264,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2008.03.036","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"1427-1433","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Reduced-impact logging: Challenges and opportunities","title-short":"Reduced-impact logging","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112708002892","volume":"256","author":[{"family":"Putz","given":"Francis E."},{"family":"Sist","given":"P."},{"family":"Fredericksen","given":"T."},{"family":"Dykstra","given":"D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition, evidence that reduced impact logging achieves the desired objectives is also contradictory. Some studies do suggest a reduction in the negative impacts of logging activities when reduced impact logging guidelines are followed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a19ru502ou1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bicknell, Struebig, Edwards, & Davies, 2014; R. Pereira, Zweede, Asner, & Keller, 2002; F. E. Putz, 2012; T. A. P. West, Vidal, & Putz, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Bicknell, Struebig, Edwards, & Davies, 2014; R. Pereira, Zweede, Asner, & Keller, 2002; F. E. Putz, 2012; T. A. P. West, Vidal, & Putz, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32013,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WX984ISM"],"itemData":{"id":32013,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Biology","DOI":"10.1016/j.cub.2014.10.067","ISSN":"09609822","issue":"23","journalAbbreviation":"Current Biology","language":"en","page":"R1119-R1120","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Improved timber harvest techniques maintain biodiversity in tropical forests","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0960982214014158","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Bicknell","given":"Jake E."},{"family":"Struebig","given":"Matthew J."},{"family":"Edwards","given":"David P."},{"family":"Davies","given":"Zoe G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31954,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DQGFZWSC"],"itemData":{"id":31954,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/S0378-1127(01)00732-0","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"1-3","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"77-89","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forest canopy damage and recovery in reduced-impact and conventional selective logging in eastern Para, Brazil","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112701007320","volume":"168","author":[{"family":"Pereira","given":"Rodrigo"},{"family":"Zweede","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Asner","given":"Gregory P."},{"family":"Keller","given":"Michael"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002",9]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31257,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LKYRNHVA"],"itemData":{"id":31257,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/j.1755-263X.2012.00242.x","ISSN":"1755263X","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"296-303","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Sustaining conservation values in selectively logged tropical forests: the attained and the attainable: Sustaining tropical forests with forestry","title-short":"Sustaining conservation values in selectively logged tropical forests","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1755-263X.2012.00242.x","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Putz","given":"Francis E."},{"family":"Zuidema","given":"Pieter A."},{"family":"Synnott","given":"Timothy"},{"family":"Peña-Claros","given":"Marielos"},{"family":"Pinard","given":"Michelle A."},{"family":"Sheil","given":"Douglas"},{"family":"Vanclay","given":"Jerome K."},{"family":"Sist","given":"Plinio"},{"family":"Gourlet-Fleury","given":"Sylvie"},{"family":"Griscom","given":"Bronson"},{"family":"Palmer","given":"John"},{"family":"Zagt","given":"Roderick"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",8]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31952,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EE27ZST4"],"itemData":{"id":31952,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2013.11.022","ISSN":"03781127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"59-63","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forest biomass recovery after conventional and reduced-impact logging in Amazonian Brazil","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112713007779","volume":"314","author":[{"family":"West","given":"Thales A.P."},{"family":"Vidal","given":"Edson"},{"family":"Putz","given":"Francis E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",2]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, other studies suggest that positive effects of reduced impact logging are in fact more closely related to differences in harvesting intensity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a48oa2m64d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Griscom, Ellis, & Putz, 2014; Johns, 1992; Picard, Gourlet-Fleury, & Forni, 2012; Plinio Sist, 2000; Plinio Sist, Fimbel, Sheil, Nasi, & Chevallier, 2003; Plinio Sist, Nolan, Bertault, & Dykstra, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Griscom, Ellis, & Putz, 2014; Johns, 1992; Picard, Gourlet-Fleury, & Forni, 2012; Plinio Sist, 2000; Plinio Sist, Fimbel, Sheil, Nasi, & Chevallier, 2003; Plinio Sist, Nolan, Bertault, & Dykstra, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31948,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9AQHD3I3"],"itemData":{"id":31948,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Biology","DOI":"10.1111/gcb.12386","ISSN":"13541013","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Glob Change Biol","language":"en","page":"923-937","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Carbon emissions performance of commercial logging in East Kalimantan, Indonesia","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/gcb.12386","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Griscom","given":"Bronson"},{"family":"Ellis","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Putz","given":"Francis E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",3]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31950,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4JPBCMQ9"],"itemData":{"id":31950,"type":"article-journal","collection-title":"B: Biological Sciences","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London.","issue":"1275","language":"en","page":"7","source":"Zotero","title":"Vertebrate Responses to Selective Logging: Implications for the Design of Logging Systems","volume":"335","author":[{"family":"Johns","given":"Andrew D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31951,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6VBELXMN"],"itemData":{"id":31951,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The proportion of stems damaged by logging is a key parameter for the management of natural productive forests in the three tropical continents (Africa, Neotropics, and southeastern Asia). Based on a review of the literature and on a meta-analysis of published data, we estimated this logging damage rate for conventional logging and compared it across continents. Scaling coefficients were estimated to convert damage rate and logging intensity from one unit to another. Felled trees were smaller in the Neotropics (61 cm diameter at breast height (dbh) on average) than in Africa or Asia (92 cm dbh). A pantropical equation relating the proportion of trees damaged (?, no unit) to logging intensity (N\\n              log\\n              , in ha\\n              –1\\n              ) was fitted: ? = 1 – (1 + 0.09135N\\n              log\\n              )\\n              –0.70461\\n              . A significant residual continent effect was found, with lower damage in the Neotropics than in Africa or Asia for the same level of logging intensity, in agreement with the size of felled trees. The damage rate varied with the size of damaged trees and divided equally between destroyed and injured trees, with injured trees experiencing a threefold mortality rate during 5–10 years. Taking account at least of the relationship between logging damage and logging intensity would improve the accuracy of forecasts in forest management.","container-title":"Canadian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1139/x2012-018","ISSN":"0045-5067, 1208-6037","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Can. J. For. Res.","language":"en","page":"605-613","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Estimating damage from selective logging and implications for tropical forest management","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/x2012-018","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Picard","given":"Nicolas"},{"family":"Gourlet-Fleury","given":"Sylvie"},{"family":"Forni","given":"Éric"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",3]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31962,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3SGTGI4Q"],"itemData":{"id":31962,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The International Forestry Review","page":"3-10","title":"Reduced-impact logging in the tropics: objectives, principles and impacts.","URL":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/230661109_Reduced-Impact_logging_in_the_tropics_objectives_principles_and_impact_of_research","author":[{"family":"Sist","given":"Plinio"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31949,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8H9YWIEF"],"itemData":{"id":31949,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Selective logging applied in tropical forests is based on one universal criterion: a minimum diameter cutting limit for all commercial timber species. Minimum diameter cutting limits in mixed dipterocarp forests of the Malesia region lead to high felling intensities (10–20+ trees ha\\n              ?1\\n              ). Such extraction rates create massive stand damage (>50% of the remaining tree population), which has a negative impact on the regeneration and growth of many harvested dipterocarp species. As such, the minimum diameter cutting limit approach is seldom compatible with sustainable forest management. Where basic ecological characteristics of the commercial species are considered in timber harvesting prescriptions, mixed dipterocarp forests appear capable of sustained timber yields, habitat conservation, and providing other goods and services. This paper first presents the main silvicultural systems developed in mixed dipterocarp forests of Western Malesia and then reviews current knowledge of dipterocarp biology to finally develop guidelines aimed at improving the ecological sustainability of production forests of Western Malesia. These guidelines, a pragmatic reflection of science and ‘best guess’ judgement, include: (1) integration of reduced-impact logging practices into normal management operations; (2) cutting of eight trees ha\\n              ?1\\n              or less (with a felling cycle of 40–60 years to be determined according to local conditions); (3) defining minimum diameter cutting limits according to the structure, density and diameter at reproduction of target species; (4) avoiding harvesting species with less than one adult tree ha\\n              ?1\\n              (diameter at breast height [dbh] ? 50 cm over an area of 50–100 ha); (5) minimizing the size and connectivity of gaps (<600 m\\n              2\\n              whenever possible); (6) refraining from treatments such as understorey clearing; and (7) providing explicit protection for key forest species and the ecological processes they perform. Further refinement is encouraged to allow for local conditions, and for other forest types.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892903000389","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Conserv.","language":"en","page":"364-374","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Towards sustainable management of mixed dipterocarp forests of South-east Asia: moving beyond minimum diameter cutting limits","title-short":"Towards sustainable management of mixed dipterocarp forests of South-east Asia","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0376892903000389/type/journal_article","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Sist","given":"Plinio"},{"family":"Fimbel","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Sheil","given":"Douglas"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Chevallier","given":"Marie-Hélène"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",12]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31953,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AXNXL97Y"],"itemData":{"id":31953,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/S0378-1127(98)00228-X","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"251-260","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Harvesting intensity versus sustainability in Indonesia","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S037811279800228X","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Sist","given":"Plinio"},{"family":"Nolan","given":"Timothy"},{"family":"Bertault","given":"Jean-Guy"},{"family":"Dykstra","given":"Dennis"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998",8]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Reduced impact logging provides guidelines to reduce environmental impacts of logging, however its lack of specificity regarding intensity can sometimes result in perverse effects. Therefore, more research is recommended to clarify whether reduced impact logging should be practiced in a way which incorporates harvesting intensity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1pl6sut5d7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Martin, 2015)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Martin, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31978,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YDA7YEKL"],"itemData":{"id":31978,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2015.07.010","ISSN":"03781127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"224-233","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impacts of tropical selective logging on carbon storage and tree species richness: A meta-analysis","title-short":"Impacts of tropical selective logging on carbon storage and tree species richness","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112715003837","volume":"356","author":[{"family":"Martin","given":"Philip A."},{"family":"Newton","given":"Adrian C."},{"family":"Pfeifer","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Khoo","given":"MinSheng"},{"family":"Bullock","given":"James M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.   
      Low intensity harvesting that is recommended in reduced impact logging may encourage expansion into previously unlogged areas in order to distribute the impact more widely  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5nOJOzHE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Martin, 2015)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Martin, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31978,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YDA7YEKL"],"itemData":{"id":31978,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2015.07.010","ISSN":"03781127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"224-233","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impacts of tropical selective logging on carbon storage and tree species richness: A meta-analysis","title-short":"Impacts of tropical selective logging on carbon storage and tree species richness","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112715003837","volume":"356","author":[{"family":"Martin","given":"Philip A."},{"family":"Newton","given":"Adrian C."},{"family":"Pfeifer","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Khoo","given":"MinSheng"},{"family":"Bullock","given":"James M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition, the recovery rate of commercial trees after reduced impact logging is very low. One study in tropical rain forests, revealed that only 50% of the commercial stand was predicted to recover after a period of 30 years, creating a major reduction in stock for the next harvesting cycle in that area. This is not compatible with sustainable yield production on a long-term basis ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dXhXNGT3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Sist & Ferreira, 2007)","plainCitation":"(P. Sist & Ferreira, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":28485,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P852IRJR"],"itemData":{"id":28485,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2007.02.014","issue":"2–3","language":"en","page":"199–209","title":"Sustainability of reduced-impact logging in the Eastern Amazon","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.foreco.2007.02.014","volume":"243","author":[{"family":"Sist","given":"P."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"F.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Sist and Ferreira ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2YQTBxc8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2007)","plainCitation":"(2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":28485,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P852IRJR"],"itemData":{"id":28485,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2007.02.014","issue":"2–3","language":"en","page":"199–209","title":"Sustainability of reduced-impact logging in the Eastern Amazon","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.foreco.2007.02.014","volume":"243","author":[{"family":"Sist","given":"P."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"F.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}},"label":"page","suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} suggest that more sophisticated silvicultural systems are required to ensure sustainable management of the forests on a long-term basis. There is evidence suggesting that reduced-impact logging practices, if actually employed, could increase future timber yields ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"p6gBnpwF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Griscom, 2014; Johns, 1992; Picard, 2012; Plinio Sist, 2000; Plinio Sist, 2003, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Griscom, 2014; Johns, 1992; Picard, 2012; Plinio Sist, 2000; Plinio Sist, 2003, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31948,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9AQHD3I3"],"itemData":{"id":31948,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Biology","DOI":"10.1111/gcb.12386","ISSN":"13541013","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Glob Change Biol","language":"en","page":"923-937","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Carbon emissions performance of commercial logging in East Kalimantan, Indonesia","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/gcb.12386","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Griscom","given":"Bronson"},{"family":"Ellis","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Putz","given":"Francis E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",3]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31950,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4JPBCMQ9"],"itemData":{"id":31950,"type":"article-journal","collection-title":"B: Biological Sciences","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London.","issue":"1275","language":"en","page":"7","source":"Zotero","title":"Vertebrate Responses to Selective Logging: Implications for the Design of Logging Systems","volume":"335","author":[{"family":"Johns","given":"Andrew D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31951,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6VBELXMN"],"itemData":{"id":31951,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The proportion of stems damaged by logging is a key parameter for the management of natural productive forests in the three tropical continents (Africa, Neotropics, and southeastern Asia). Based on a review of the literature and on a meta-analysis of published data, we estimated this logging damage rate for conventional logging and compared it across continents. Scaling coefficients were estimated to convert damage rate and logging intensity from one unit to another. Felled trees were smaller in the Neotropics (61 cm diameter at breast height (dbh) on average) than in Africa or Asia (92 cm dbh). A pantropical equation relating the proportion of trees damaged (?, no unit) to logging intensity (N\\n              log\\n              , in ha\\n              –1\\n              ) was fitted: ? = 1 – (1 + 0.09135N\\n              log\\n              )\\n              –0.70461\\n              . A significant residual continent effect was found, with lower damage in the Neotropics than in Africa or Asia for the same level of logging intensity, in agreement with the size of felled trees. The damage rate varied with the size of damaged trees and divided equally between destroyed and injured trees, with injured trees experiencing a threefold mortality rate during 5–10 years. Taking account at least of the relationship between logging damage and logging intensity would improve the accuracy of forecasts in forest management.","container-title":"Canadian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1139/x2012-018","ISSN":"0045-5067, 1208-6037","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Can. J. For. Res.","language":"en","page":"605-613","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Estimating damage from selective logging and implications for tropical forest management","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/x2012-018","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Picard","given":"Nicolas"},{"family":"Gourlet-Fleury","given":"Sylvie"},{"family":"Forni","given":"Éric"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",3]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31962,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3SGTGI4Q"],"itemData":{"id":31962,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The International Forestry Review","page":"3-10","title":"Reduced-impact logging in the tropics: objectives, principles and impacts.","URL":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/230661109_Reduced-Impact_logging_in_the_tropics_objectives_principles_and_impact_of_research","author":[{"family":"Sist","given":"Plinio"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31953,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AXNXL97Y"],"itemData":{"id":31953,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/S0378-1127(98)00228-X","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"251-260","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Harvesting intensity versus sustainability in Indonesia","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S037811279800228X","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Sist","given":"Plinio"},{"family":"Nolan","given":"Timothy"},{"family":"Bertault","given":"Jean-Guy"},{"family":"Dykstra","given":"Dennis"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998",8]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31949,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8H9YWIEF"],"itemData":{"id":31949,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Selective logging applied in tropical forests is based on one universal criterion: a minimum diameter cutting limit for all commercial timber species. Minimum diameter cutting limits in mixed dipterocarp forests of the Malesia region lead to high felling intensities (10–20+ trees ha\\n              ?1\\n              ). Such extraction rates create massive stand damage (>50% of the remaining tree population), which has a negative impact on the regeneration and growth of many harvested dipterocarp species. As such, the minimum diameter cutting limit approach is seldom compatible with sustainable forest management. Where basic ecological characteristics of the commercial species are considered in timber harvesting prescriptions, mixed dipterocarp forests appear capable of sustained timber yields, habitat conservation, and providing other goods and services. This paper first presents the main silvicultural systems developed in mixed dipterocarp forests of Western Malesia and then reviews current knowledge of dipterocarp biology to finally develop guidelines aimed at improving the ecological sustainability of production forests of Western Malesia. These guidelines, a pragmatic reflection of science and ‘best guess’ judgement, include: (1) integration of reduced-impact logging practices into normal management operations; (2) cutting of eight trees ha\\n              ?1\\n              or less (with a felling cycle of 40–60 years to be determined according to local conditions); (3) defining minimum diameter cutting limits according to the structure, density and diameter at reproduction of target species; (4) avoiding harvesting species with less than one adult tree ha\\n              ?1\\n              (diameter at breast height [dbh] ? 50 cm over an area of 50–100 ha); (5) minimizing the size and connectivity of gaps (<600 m\\n              2\\n              whenever possible); (6) refraining from treatments such as understorey clearing; and (7) providing explicit protection for key forest species and the ecological processes they perform. Further refinement is encouraged to allow for local conditions, and for other forest types.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892903000389","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Conserv.","language":"en","page":"364-374","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Towards sustainable management of mixed dipterocarp forests of South-east Asia: moving beyond minimum diameter cutting limits","title-short":"Towards sustainable management of mixed dipterocarp forests of South-east Asia","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0376892903000389/type/journal_article","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Sist","given":"Plinio"},{"family":"Fimbel","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Sheil","given":"Douglas"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Chevallier","given":"Marie-Hélène"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",12]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There is not much change on the ground in spite of these recommended practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"as3skfmcvb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. Putz, 2018)","plainCitation":"(F. Putz, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31947,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K8R46ADL"],"itemData":{"id":31947,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"JOURNAL OF TROPICAL FOREST SCIENCE","DOI":"10.26525/jtfs2018.30.5.415417","ISSN":"01281283, 25219847","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JTFS","page":"415-417","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"SUSTAINABLE = GOOD, BETTER, OR RESPONSIBLE","URL":"https://www.frim.gov.my/v1/jtfsonline/jtfs/v30n5/415-417.pdf","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Putz","given":"Fe"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,14]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	The status of illegal logging and associated timber trade as well as its trends in harvesting practice, constitute complex and serious challenges in the sustainable use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1enenrcmkp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Liu, Yong, Choi, & Gibson, 2020)","plainCitation":"(J. Liu, Yong, Choi, & Gibson, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31997,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U23DGV5R"],"itemData":{"id":31997,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2020.03.004","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"679-690","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Transboundary Frontiers: An Emerging Priority for Biodiversity Conservation","title-short":"Transboundary Frontiers","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534720300793","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Jiajia"},{"family":"Yong","given":"Ding Li"},{"family":"Choi","given":"Chi-Yeung"},{"family":"Gibson","given":"Luke"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although illegal timber trade and unsustainable logging that threaten sustainable use are rampant and not well documented, it is promoting large-scale forest destruction, especially in the tropics ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2bjn9l4m25","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Laurance, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Laurance, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31992,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LUHP7366"],"itemData":{"id":31992,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2004.06.001","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"399-401","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The perils of payoff: corruption as a threat to global biodiversity","title-short":"The perils of payoff","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534704001685","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Laurance","given":"W"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Illegal timber trade is highly international, which may result in substantial loss of large old trees, for example that along the China-Russia border ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1kqmcvhbur","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Laurance, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Laurance, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31992,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LUHP7366"],"itemData":{"id":31992,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2004.06.001","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"399-401","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The perils of payoff: corruption as a threat to global biodiversity","title-short":"The perils of payoff","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534704001685","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Laurance","given":"W"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Owing to the higher prices of timber in India and China, smugglers are motivated to export timber from Nepal to the Tibetan Autonomous Region of China ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aact7tqsng","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. P. Chaudhary, Uprety, & Rimal, 2016)","plainCitation":"(R. P. Chaudhary, Uprety, & Rimal, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31610,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9MC5TAJV"],"itemData":{"id":31610,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological and Environmental hazards, risks, and disasters","note":"publisher: Elsevier Amsterdam","page":"335–372","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Deforestation in Nepal: Causes, consequences and responses","title-short":"Deforestation in Nepal","author":[{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"R. P."},{"family":"Uprety","given":"Y."},{"family":"Rimal","given":"S. K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; and Nepal-India border ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1emhc57c1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. P. Chaudhary, 2016; Regmi, 1994)","plainCitation":"(R. P. Chaudhary, 2016; Regmi, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31610,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9MC5TAJV"],"itemData":{"id":31610,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological and Environmental hazards, risks, and disasters","note":"publisher: Elsevier Amsterdam","page":"335–372","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Deforestation in Nepal: Causes, consequences and responses","title-short":"Deforestation in Nepal","author":[{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"R. P."},{"family":"Uprety","given":"Y."},{"family":"Rimal","given":"S. K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":31990,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JYAPJE3P"],"itemData":{"id":31990,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The PDF of this file is 2,485 kbytes in size and therefore will take a long time to download if you click on the PDF link below. If you would like the file to be sent to you by email, please send a request to info@nepjol.info. Please include the citation below in your request. DOI: 10.3126/opsa.v4i0.1086Occasional Papers in Sociology and Anthropology Vol.4 1994 p.72-89","container-title":"Occasional Papers in Sociology and Anthropology","DOI":"10.3126/opsa.v4i0.1086","ISSN":"2091-0312","journalAbbreviation":"Occ.Pap.Soc.&Anth.","language":"en","page":"72-89","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Deforestation and rural society in the Nepalese Terai","URL":"https://www.nepjol.info/index.php/OPSA/article/view/1086","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Regmi","given":"Rishikeshab Raj"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
	Regarding data on logging, a common understanding is that it is hard to obtain accurate data on the scope of illegal logging. Scientific studies as well as reports present conflicting views on whether illegal logging is declining or not ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a66h8mmlql","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kleinschmit, Mansourian, Wildburger, & Purret, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Kleinschmit, Mansourian, Wildburger, & Purret, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":16226,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZ2A8BTY","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FHXRX6U9"],"itemData":{"id":16226,"type":"book","collection-number":"35","collection-title":"IUFRO world series","event-place":"Vienna","ISBN":"978-3-902762-70-2","language":"en","number-of-pages":"145","publisher":"IUFRO","publisher-place":"Vienna","source":"K10plus ISBN","title":"Illegal logging and related timber trade: dimensions, drivers, impacts and responses ; a global scientific rapid response assessment report","title-short":"Illegal logging and related timber trade","editor":[{"family":"Kleinschmit","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Mansourian","given":"Stephanie"},{"family":"Wildburger","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Purret","given":"Andre"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. According to Hoare ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2l46nm92d6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2015)","plainCitation":"(2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31968,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LVJSVYIQ"],"itemData":{"id":31968,"type":"book","event-place":"London: Chatham House","publisher-place":"London: Chatham House","title":"Tackling illegal logging and the related trade. What progress and where next.","author":[{"family":"Hoare","given":"Alison"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",7]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, there has been important progress made in reducing illegality in the forest sector over the last decades. However, another report published three years earlier claims that illegal logging has remained high in many regions, even increased in some areas, and become more advanced with better organized activities also comprising criminal activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1tqs1in5ms","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nellemann, International Criminal Police Organization, & GRID--Arendal, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Nellemann, International Criminal Police Organization, & GRID--Arendal, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31995,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XBA5XW3L"],"itemData":{"id":31995,"type":"book","abstract":"Environmental crime and the illegal grabbing of natural resources is becoming an ever more sophisticated activity requiring national authorities and law enforcement agencies to develop responses commensurate with the scale and the complexity of the challenge to keep one step ahead. This report - Green Carbon, Black Trade - by UNEP and INTERPOL focuses on illegal logging and its impacts on the lives and livelihoods of often some of the poorest people in the world set aside the environmental damage. It underlines how criminals are combining old fashioned methods such as bribes with high tech methods such as computer hacking of government web sites to obtain transportation and other permits. The report spotlights the increasingly sophisticated tactics being deployed to launder illegal logs through a web of palm oil plantations, road networks and saw mills.","ISBN":"978-82-7701-102-8","language":"en","note":"OCLC: 880396786","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Green carbon, black trade: illegal logging, tax fraud and laundering in the worlds tropical forests : a rapid response assessment","title-short":"Green carbon, black trade","author":[{"family":"Nellemann","given":"C"},{"literal":"International Criminal Police Organization"},{"literal":"GRID--Arendal"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. China (importing more than 50%, of total illegal export value), Vietnam, India, the European Union, Thailand and the United States of America are among the major importers of illegal timber accounting for 84% of the total value of imports. Southeast Asia (mainly Cambodia, Laos, Myanmar Indonesia and Malaysia), the Russian Federation, Papua New Guinea and the Congo Basin (Democratic Republic of Congo, the Republic of Congo and Cameroon are among the main exporters with Southeast Asia accounting for 55% of the exports ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1edkgm8dl0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ram P. Chaudhary, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Ram P. Chaudhary, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1300,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5MD2A7T4"],"itemData":{"id":1300,"type":"book","call-number":"QC903.2.N35 W67 2008","event-place":"Kathmandu : Norway : University of Bergen","ISBN":"978-99933-52-94-5","note":"event-title: Workshop on Bergen-Tribhuvan Human Ecology Programme","number-of-pages":"199","publisher":"Tribhuvan University","publisher-place":"Kathmandu : Norway : University of Bergen","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Local effects of global changes in the Himalayas: Manang, Nepal","title-short":"Local effects of global changes in the Himalayas","editor":[{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"Ram P."},{"family":"Aase","given":"T. H."},{"family":"Vetaas","given":"O. R."},{"family":"Subedi","given":"B. P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.   
	There has also been an observed geographic shift in illegal logging and related timber trade. Illegal logging in Brazil, Indonesia and Malaysia has declined in recent years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a3f2icf6q7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoare, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Hoare, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31968,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LVJSVYIQ"],"itemData":{"id":31968,"type":"book","event-place":"London: Chatham House","publisher-place":"London: Chatham House","title":"Tackling illegal logging and the related trade. What progress and where next.","author":[{"family":"Hoare","given":"Alison"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. After decades of conservation efforts, forests along the China-Russia border have been recolonized ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2jfvf987mk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. Wang, 2016)","plainCitation":"(T. Wang, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31989,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J3CWJUCM"],"itemData":{"id":31989,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Context The Amur tiger and leopard, once roaming over the Eurasian continent, are now endangered and con?ned to the Sikhote-Alin Mountains, Russia—a landscape that has been increasingly fragmented due to human activities. The ultimate fate of these big cats depends on whether they can resettle in their previous main historical range in NE China. Recent sightings of these animals along the China–Russia border have aroused new hope, but direct evidence is lacking.","container-title":"Landscape Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10980-015-0278-1","ISSN":"0921-2973, 1572-9761","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape Ecol","language":"en","page":"491-503","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Amur tigers and leopards returning to China: direct evidence and a landscape conservation plan","title-short":"Amur tigers and leopards returning to China","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10980-015-0278-1","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Wang","given":"Tianming"},{"family":"Feng","given":"Limin"},{"family":"Mou","given":"Pu"},{"family":"Wu","given":"Jianguo"},{"family":"Smith","given":"James L. D."},{"family":"Xiao","given":"Wenhong"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Haitao"},{"family":"Dou","given":"Hailong"},{"family":"Zhao","given":"Xiaodan"},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Yanchao"},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Bo"},{"family":"Wu","given":"Hongyan"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Li"},{"family":"Tian","given":"Yu"},{"family":"Guo","given":"Qingxi"},{"family":"Kou","given":"Xiaojun"},{"family":"Han","given":"Xuemei"},{"family":"Miquelle","given":"Dale G."},{"family":"Oliver","given":"Chadwick D."},{"family":"Xu","given":"Rumei"},{"family":"Ge","given":"Jianping"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In recent years the smuggling of timber as well as other forest resources has declined along Nepal-China and Nepal-India borders due to improved monitoring and collaborative transboundary conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1blgg5t4cr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. P. Chaudhary, 2016)","plainCitation":"(R. P. Chaudhary, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31610,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9MC5TAJV"],"itemData":{"id":31610,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological and Environmental hazards, risks, and disasters","note":"publisher: Elsevier Amsterdam","page":"335–372","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Deforestation in Nepal: Causes, consequences and responses","title-short":"Deforestation in Nepal","author":[{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"R. P."},{"family":"Uprety","given":"Y."},{"family":"Rimal","given":"S. K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; personal communication with Bishnu Lama, indigenous people and local community member and chairman of the Namkha rural municipality - Humla District, Nepal, December 2020). However, Russia, other Southeast Asian countries (e.g., Cambodia, Laos and Myanmar), Papua New Guinea and some African countries have also witnessed increases in illegal forest activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1k6266ssid","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Guan, 2016)","plainCitation":"(J. Guan, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31940,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V7EWZ6PP"],"itemData":{"id":31940,"type":"chapter","collection-number":"35","collection-title":"IUFRO World Series","container-title":"Illegal logging and related timber trade: dimensions, drivers, impacts and responses : a global scientific rapid response assessment report","ISBN":"978-3-902762-70-2","language":"en","note":"OCLC: 972337413","page":"37-59","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Quantifying Illegal Logging and Related Timber Trade","URL":"http://www.iufro.org/science/gfep/illegal-timber-trade-rapid-response/report/","author":[{"family":"Guan","given":"Jianbang"},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Masiero","given":"Mauro"},{"family":"Pettenella","given":"Davide"},{"family":"Andrighetto","given":"Nicola"},{"family":"Dawson","given":"Tim"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Russia (primarily in its Far East region) is one among rising timber producer countries and exports timber mainly to China ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2p2v73t4jn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Guan, 2016)","plainCitation":"(J. Guan, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31940,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V7EWZ6PP"],"itemData":{"id":31940,"type":"chapter","collection-number":"35","collection-title":"IUFRO World Series","container-title":"Illegal logging and related timber trade: dimensions, drivers, impacts and responses : a global scientific rapid response assessment report","ISBN":"978-3-902762-70-2","language":"en","note":"OCLC: 972337413","page":"37-59","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Quantifying Illegal Logging and Related Timber Trade","URL":"http://www.iufro.org/science/gfep/illegal-timber-trade-rapid-response/report/","author":[{"family":"Guan","given":"Jianbang"},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Masiero","given":"Mauro"},{"family":"Pettenella","given":"Davide"},{"family":"Andrighetto","given":"Nicola"},{"family":"Dawson","given":"Tim"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. China has become the world’s largest importer of tropical timber since a ban on domestic logging was implemented in 1998. It is also a key processing country, for example, it is the leading manufacturer of furniture worldwide, occupying 40 percent of the global market share ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ai80n7ik1f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Richer, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Richer, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32007,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UBWNTUJ2"],"itemData":{"id":32007,"type":"post-weblog","abstract":"By value, nearly 83 percent of Chinese furniture exports are shipped to the US, EU, and Australia -- three markets that are actively regulating the trade in illegal timber. According to a March 2016 Forest","container-title":"Forest Trends","language":"en-US","title":"Chinese Furniture Exports Reach All-Time High in 2015","URL":"https://www.forest-trends.org/blog/chinese-furniture-exports-reach-all-time-high-in-2015/","author":[{"family":"Richer","given":"Eve"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",6,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; much is exported to the United States of America and Europe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2insp34pbe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tacconi, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Tacconi, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31944,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MF284VAX"],"itemData":{"id":31944,"type":"chapter","collection-number":"35","container-title":"Illegal Logging and Related Timber Trade – Dimensions, Drivers, Impacts and Responses: A Global Scientific Rapid Response Assessment Report","ISBN":"978-3-902762-70-2","page":"23-35","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","title":"Defining Illegal Forest Activities and Illegal Logging","author":[{"family":"Tacconi","given":"L."},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"P.O."},{"family":"Leipold","given":"S."},{"family":"Rodrigues","given":"R. J."},{"family":"Savaresi","given":"A."},{"family":"To","given":"P."},{"family":"Weng","given":"X."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
	Commercial logging is illegal in Afghanistan which leaves a massive smuggling industry to satisfy international demand. Local communities have lost control over the resources on which they depend for their survival, and forest resources are now largely used for immediate profit by organized crime syndicates and traders ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a188j8nc7o9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Milbrandt & Overend, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Milbrandt & Overend, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31946,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/66SGGZL3"],"itemData":{"id":31946,"type":"report","genre":"Technical Report","number":"NREL/TP-6A20-49358.","publisher":"National Renewable Energy Laboratory","title":"Assessment of biomass resources in Afghanistan. No.  National Renewable Energy Lab.(NREL), Golden, CO (United States), 2011.","author":[{"family":"Milbrandt","given":"Anelia"},{"family":"Overend","given":"Ralph"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Additionally, poor forest management, lack of incentives for reforestation, lack of community involvement and awareness, and agricultural and urban encroachments on forest land also contributed to the severe decline of forest cover in Afghanistan ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a24qpkpk9k9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Milbrandt & Overend, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Milbrandt & Overend, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31946,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/66SGGZL3"],"itemData":{"id":31946,"type":"report","genre":"Technical Report","number":"NREL/TP-6A20-49358.","publisher":"National Renewable Energy Laboratory","title":"Assessment of biomass resources in Afghanistan. No.  National Renewable Energy Lab.(NREL), Golden, CO (United States), 2011.","author":[{"family":"Milbrandt","given":"Anelia"},{"family":"Overend","given":"Ralph"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The results have been that rangelands have deteriorated, forests have been felled, and wild species populations have greatly diminished from uncontrolled hunting and habitat degradation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ql4aon4rr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNDP, 2014)","plainCitation":"(UNDP, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31218,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CG9JNN7N"],"itemData":{"id":31218,"type":"report","title":"United Nations Development Programme Country: Afghanistan PROJECT DOCUMENT “Establishing integrated models for protected areas and their co-management in Afghanistan","author":[{"family":"UNDP","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	In 2006, an executive order that was issued by then President Hamid Karzai banned illegal timber-harvesting and felling of trees and shrubs in natural forests in Afghanistan. After that, Afghanistan’s Forest Management Law, passed in 2012, declared natural forests and woodlands as public property owned by the national government. The law also has a provision to support community-based forest management, allowing indigenous communities to utilize and manage the forest in collaboration with the Department of Natural Resources. However, the deterioration of overall law and order situation in Afghanistan means that the 2012 forest law has only been partially implemented.  
	Curbing illegal timber extraction and trade poses special challenges because of the need for cooperation among sovereign states. In order to support producer countries, bilateral arrangements have emerged, either between neighboring countries or between primary export and import countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ah79vav9dl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kleinschmit, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Kleinschmit, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":16226,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZ2A8BTY","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FHXRX6U9"],"itemData":{"id":16226,"type":"book","collection-number":"35","collection-title":"IUFRO world series","event-place":"Vienna","ISBN":"978-3-902762-70-2","language":"en","number-of-pages":"145","publisher":"IUFRO","publisher-place":"Vienna","source":"K10plus ISBN","title":"Illegal logging and related timber trade: dimensions, drivers, impacts and responses ; a global scientific rapid response assessment report","title-short":"Illegal logging and related timber trade","editor":[{"family":"Kleinschmit","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Mansourian","given":"Stephanie"},{"family":"Wildburger","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Purret","given":"Andre"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Imports of illegal tropical hardwood timber in China with the republic of Congo, Ghana, Papua New Guinea, Laos, Brazil and Malaysia; India with Brazil, and Paua New Guinea; Japan with Republic of Congo, Cameroon, Malaysia and Paua New Guinea; and South Korea with Malaysia are considerable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a26fke2laoq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Z. Guan, Chen, Xu, & Liu, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Z. Guan, Chen, Xu, & Liu, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31928,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/79LZD44R"],"itemData":{"id":31928,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Illegal logging has caused serious damage to the global forest environment. China, Japan, India and South Korea have been criticized for their imports of illegal timber. Based on a trade balance analysis method, this paper evaluates the imports of illegal timber of China, Japan, India and South Korea. The results show that China has serious illegal timber imports with the Republic of Congo, Ghana, Papua New Guinea, Laos, Brazil and Malaysia. India has serious illegal timber imports with Brazil and Papua New Guinea. Japan has serious illegal timber imports with the Republic of the Congo, Cameroon, Malaysia and Papua New Guinea. South Korea has serious illegal timber imports with Malaysia. In order to control the imports of illegal timber, there is a need to further strengthen international cooperation and domestic legislation, enhance the protection and cultivation of forest resources and reduce dependence on imported timber.","container-title":"International Wood Products Journal","DOI":"10.1080/20426445.2020.1785604","ISSN":"2042-6445, 2042-6453","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"International Wood Products Journal","language":"en","page":"211-225","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Are imports of illegal timber in China, India, Japan and South Korea considerable? based on a historic trade balance analysis method","title-short":"Are imports of illegal timber in China, India, Japan and South Korea considerable?","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/20426445.2020.1785604","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Guan","given":"Zhijie"},{"family":"Chen","given":"Xiaoyu"},{"family":"Xu","given":"Yan"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Yaofei"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Bilateral actions that also include transboundary cooperation have been initiated at the national level ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1subf85na2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tacconi, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Tacconi, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31944,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MF284VAX"],"itemData":{"id":31944,"type":"chapter","collection-number":"35","container-title":"Illegal Logging and Related Timber Trade – Dimensions, Drivers, Impacts and Responses: A Global Scientific Rapid Response Assessment Report","ISBN":"978-3-902762-70-2","page":"23-35","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","title":"Defining Illegal Forest Activities and Illegal Logging","author":[{"family":"Tacconi","given":"L."},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"P.O."},{"family":"Leipold","given":"S."},{"family":"Rodrigues","given":"R. J."},{"family":"Savaresi","given":"A."},{"family":"To","given":"P."},{"family":"Weng","given":"X."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Besides scientists, transboundary conservation deserves more attention from policymakers too ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ufsm4acfe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Liu, 2020)","plainCitation":"(J. Liu, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31997,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U23DGV5R"],"itemData":{"id":31997,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2020.03.004","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"679-690","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Transboundary Frontiers: An Emerging Priority for Biodiversity Conservation","title-short":"Transboundary Frontiers","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534720300793","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Jiajia"},{"family":"Yong","given":"Ding Li"},{"family":"Choi","given":"Chi-Yeung"},{"family":"Gibson","given":"Luke"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Policy in one country can easily have a major impact in other countries. For example, logging bans in Thailand and China have led to increased logging and forest loss in the neighboring countries including Lao People's Democratic Republic, Cambodia, Indonesia, the Russian Far East and Mongolia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ib451j57r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fisher, Maginnis, Jackson, Barrow, & Jeanrenaud, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Fisher, Maginnis, Jackson, Barrow, & Jeanrenaud, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31945,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/88UR5BUF"],"itemData":{"id":31945,"type":"book","call-number":"HC79.P6 P6826 2008","event-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","ISBN":"978-1-84407-635-2","number-of-pages":"146","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Linking conservation and poverty reduction: landscapes, people and power","title-short":"Linking conservation and poverty reduction","editor":[{"family":"Fisher","given":"R."},{"family":"Maginnis","given":"S."},{"family":"Jackson","given":"W."},{"family":"Barrow","given":"E."},{"family":"Jeanrenaud","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hence, there is need to further strengthen international cooperation and domestic legislation in order to control the imports of illegal timber, enhance the protection and cultivation of forest resources and reduce dependence on imported timber ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2gi3enrhjg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Z. Guan, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Z. Guan, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31928,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/79LZD44R"],"itemData":{"id":31928,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Illegal logging has caused serious damage to the global forest environment. China, Japan, India and South Korea have been criticized for their imports of illegal timber. Based on a trade balance analysis method, this paper evaluates the imports of illegal timber of China, Japan, India and South Korea. The results show that China has serious illegal timber imports with the Republic of Congo, Ghana, Papua New Guinea, Laos, Brazil and Malaysia. India has serious illegal timber imports with Brazil and Papua New Guinea. Japan has serious illegal timber imports with the Republic of the Congo, Cameroon, Malaysia and Papua New Guinea. South Korea has serious illegal timber imports with Malaysia. In order to control the imports of illegal timber, there is a need to further strengthen international cooperation and domestic legislation, enhance the protection and cultivation of forest resources and reduce dependence on imported timber.","container-title":"International Wood Products Journal","DOI":"10.1080/20426445.2020.1785604","ISSN":"2042-6445, 2042-6453","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"International Wood Products Journal","language":"en","page":"211-225","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Are imports of illegal timber in China, India, Japan and South Korea considerable? based on a historic trade balance analysis method","title-short":"Are imports of illegal timber in China, India, Japan and South Korea considerable?","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/20426445.2020.1785604","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Guan","given":"Zhijie"},{"family":"Chen","given":"Xiaoyu"},{"family":"Xu","given":"Yan"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Yaofei"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a22847h67ok","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kleinschmit, Mansourian, Wildburger, & Purret, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Kleinschmit, Mansourian, Wildburger, & Purret, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":16226,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZ2A8BTY","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FHXRX6U9"],"itemData":{"id":16226,"type":"book","collection-number":"35","collection-title":"IUFRO world series","event-place":"Vienna","ISBN":"978-3-902762-70-2","language":"en","number-of-pages":"145","publisher":"IUFRO","publisher-place":"Vienna","source":"K10plus ISBN","title":"Illegal logging and related timber trade: dimensions, drivers, impacts and responses ; a global scientific rapid response assessment report","title-short":"Illegal logging and related timber trade","editor":[{"family":"Kleinschmit","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Mansourian","given":"Stephanie"},{"family":"Wildburger","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Purret","given":"Andre"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}
	The spread of illegal logging and other forest crimes into protected areas occurs because valuable timber is still available in commercial volumes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1tc8f3atcm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wardojo, Suhariyanto, & Purnama, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Wardojo, Suhariyanto, & Purnama, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31943,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GDULBHG9"],"itemData":{"id":31943,"type":"paper-conference","event-place":"Bali, Indonesia","event-title":"East Asia Ministerial Conferenceon Forest Law Enforcement and Governance (FLEG)","publisher-place":"Bali, Indonesia","title":"Law enforcement and forest protection in Indonesia: a retrospect andprospect.","URL":"http://siteresources.worldbank.org/INTINDONESIA/FLEG/20171554/Law_Enforcement.pdf","author":[{"family":"Wardojo","given":"W"},{"family":"Suhariyanto","given":""},{"family":"Purnama","given":"B.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Timber felling in protected areas in Indonesia involve multiple stakeholders, including local people, logging companies, military personal and forestry officials ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2jcsmptrok","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barber & Talbott, 2003; Hiller, 2004; Laurance, 2004; McCarthy, 2002; Ravenel, 2004; Robertson & van Schaik, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Barber & Talbott, 2003; Hiller, 2004; Laurance, 2004; McCarthy, 2002; Ravenel, 2004; Robertson & van Schaik, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31982,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AUVZRL6I"],"itemData":{"id":31982,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","DOI":"10.1300/J091v16n03_07","ISSN":"1054-9811, 1540-756X","issue":"3-4","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","language":"en","page":"131-160","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Chainsaw and the Gun: The Role of the Military in Deforesting Indonesia","title-short":"The Chainsaw and the Gun","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1300/J091v16n03_07","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Barber","given":"Charles Victor"},{"family":"Talbott","given":"Kirk"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",3]]}}},{"id":31998,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J43U4B8H"],"itemData":{"id":31998,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","DOI":"10.1300/J091v19n01_09","ISSN":"1054-9811, 1540-756X","issue":"1-3","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","language":"en","page":"181-212","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recent Trends in Illegal Logging and a Brief Discussion of Their Causes: A Case Study from Gunung Palung National Park, Indonesia","title-short":"Recent Trends in Illegal Logging and a Brief Discussion of Their Causes","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1300/J091v19n01_09","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Hiller","given":"Marc A."},{"family":"Jarvis","given":"Benjamin C."},{"family":"Lisa","given":"Hikma"},{"family":"Paulson","given":"Laura J."},{"family":"Pollard","given":"Edward H. B."},{"family":"Stanley","given":"Scott A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",11,8]]}}},{"id":31992,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LUHP7366"],"itemData":{"id":31992,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2004.06.001","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"399-401","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The perils of payoff: corruption as a threat to global biodiversity","title-short":"The perils of payoff","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534704001685","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Laurance","given":"W"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",8]]}}},{"id":31996,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/THS8S5GW"],"itemData":{"id":31996,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This article examines the institutional matrix associated with logging and forest pioneering in a district on Sumatra. It draws together theoretical approaches to develop a framework for analysing the operation of competing forms of institutional power and control. The article argues that the governance of local natural resources and the current epidemic of ‘illegal logging’ can be understood in terms of the particular institutional or socio-political structures found in remote forested areas.","container-title":"Journal of Southeast Asian Studies","DOI":"10.1017/S0022463402000048","ISSN":"0022-4634, 1474-0680","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J. Southeast Asian stud.","language":"en","page":"77-106","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Power and Interest on Sumatra's Rainforest Frontier: Clientelist Coalitions, Illegal Logging and Conservation in the Alas Valley","title-short":"Power and Interest on Sumatra's Rainforest Frontier","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0022463402000048/type/journal_article","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"McCarthy","given":"John F."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002",2]]}}},{"id":31991,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4EEDPI9M"],"itemData":{"id":31991,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","DOI":"10.1300/J091v19n01_10","ISSN":"1054-9811, 1540-756X","issue":"1-3","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","language":"en","page":"213-237","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community-Based Logging and <i>De Facto</i> Decentralization: Illegal Logging in the Gunung Palung Area of West Kalimantan, Indonesia","title-short":"Community-Based Logging and <i>De Facto</i> Decentralization","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1300/J091v19n01_10","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Ravenel","given":"Ramsay M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",11,8]]}}},{"id":31994,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UPP93JMZ"],"itemData":{"id":31994,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract The Sumatran orang-utan is in dramatic decline, including the population in its main stronghold, the Leuser Ecosystem, in Sumatra, Indonesia (C. P. van Schaik (2001) Oryx 35, 14±25). The major threats to the survival of Sumatran orang-utans are identi®ed as habitat loss (mainly from conversion to oil palm plantations), habitat degradation and habitat fragmentation. The immediate causes of this are identi®ed as weak compliance with regulations and laws; weak law enforcement and the weak legal environment. Corruption is identi®ed as the ultimate causal factor underlying these three immediate causal factors, along with a frontier mentality and bureaucratic constraints. Together, they have resulted in the destruction of prime orang-utan habitat. Several political actions are recommended to improve the effectiveness of habitat conservation for the orang-utan and several technical challenges are to be overcome once the policy context is right. The most crucial problem to solve is the lack of regular funds for enforcement operations and establishing a new system of enforcement that is effective. In addition, the Gunung Leuser National Park needs to be redesigned by enlarging it to cover all high biodiversity areas within the Leuser Ecosystem. Moreover, habitat corridors between important forest tracts need to be re-established.","language":"en","page":"13","source":"Zotero","title":"Causal factors underlying the dramatic decline of the Sumatran orang-utan","author":[{"family":"Robertson","given":"J M Yarrow"},{"family":"Schaik","given":"Carel P","non-dropping-particle":"van"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Illegal logging provides immediate income for local communities and may aid in day-to-day survival ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1slack10ph","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schroeder-Wildberg & Carius, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Schroeder-Wildberg & Carius, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31971,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MVXDLQ2Y"],"itemData":{"id":31971,"type":"book","collection-title":"Environment, natural resources and food","event-place":"Berlin","ISBN":"978-3-937235-33-2","language":"eng","note":"OCLC: 218056858","number-of-pages":"76","publisher":"InWEnt [u.a.]","publisher-place":"Berlin","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Illegal logging, conflict and the business sector in Indonesia","author":[{"family":"Schroeder-Wildberg","given":"Esther"},{"family":"Carius","given":"Alexander"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some places illegal forestry activity is a function of local livelihood context such as reduced income from farming ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a26qbma33sj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yonariza & Webb, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Yonariza & Webb, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31993,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YZXI3RUR"],"itemData":{"id":31993,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Illegal logging is a threat to tropical forests in protected areas, yet the factors motivating it need to be better understood. Here, rural household participation in timber felling in the Barisan I Nature Reserve (West Sumatra) was described, the household contextual factors relating to this participation analysed and the importance of these activities to the household income assessed. Nearly 19% of the sampled households cut or hauled timber in the Reserve, and the demand for cash encouraged participation. Alternative livelihoods such as livestock raising and agroforestry intensi?cation outside the Reserve could reduce the need for timber felling. Knowledge about legal status of the Reserve did not affect the odds of a household engaging in timber harvesting, but greater awareness of the Reserve resulted in lower levels of income from timber. Development policy that seeks to provide livelihood alternatives to timber felling households could reduce dependence on timber and contribute to forest conservation in the Reserve. Moreover, conservation outcomes should improve if control over the protection and enforcement of the Reserve is co-managed between the government and the local communities.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892907003542","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Conserv.","language":"en","page":"73-82","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Rural household participation in illegal timber felling in a protected area of West Sumatra, Indonesia","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0376892907003542/type/journal_article","volume":"34","author":[{"literal":"Yonariza"},{"family":"Webb","given":"Edward L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

3.3.4.3. A stratified typology on sustainable use of wild species in logging 

Forests are owned either publicly by the state for the benefit of the citizens or privately by individuals, local, tribal and indigenous communities, or business entities and institutions. The proportion of forests under public ownership has declined, while those under private ownership increased between 1990 and 2015. In all regions, public administration holds management rights to most of the publicly owned forests. Globally, individuals own most privately owned forests, followed by local, tribal and indigenous communities and the least are owned by business entities and institutions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1u8ujkiunj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"ERpTB65M/5xO8ZqnJ","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A6FP6AF5"],"itemData":{"id":12535,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132974-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1195470916","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Global Forest Resources Assessment (FRA) 2020: main report","title-short":"GLOBAL FOREST RESOURCES ASSESSMENT (FRA) 2020","URL":"https://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8753en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Table 3.17). 

