These logging operations are further differentiated by key aspects of use, identified here as harvest regime, governance, and economy:
1. Harvesting regime refers to the species harvested, species characteristics which affect volume of harvest such as growth and regeneration rates, and the techniques and equipment used. 
2. Governance refers to different forms of access to forests and timber. It also refers to individual and collective rights, which range from diffuse and well-defined customary rights to full formal ownership of private lands and long-term usufruct rights in public lands. Legality is also considered a governance issue. 
3. Economy refers to ways in which benefits are accumulated by actors. These include subsistence needs, and produce goods for the formal and informal economies. There are differential distributions of benefits depending on the form of capital accumulation and capital distribution. 

3.3.4.3.1. Smallholder Logging Practice

Estimates suggest that 1.3 billion people live in or around the world’s remaining forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a7a5h177oo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chao, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Chao, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32148,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9VXJ6Y25"],"itemData":{"id":32148,"type":"book","event-place":"United Kingdom","number-of-pages":"27","publisher":"Forest Peoples Programme","publisher-place":"United Kingdom","title":"Forest Peoples: Numbers across the World","URL":"https://www.forestpeoples.org/sites/fpp/files/publication/2012/05/forest-peoples-numbers-across-world-final_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"Chao","given":"Sophie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These include right holders with individual and collective access to forests, either formal or informal. Rights holders may include individual landowners, indigenous traditional communities, local communities with established land tenure and historical access, and naturalized immigrant communities (for example from settler colonial expansion). In many cases different individuals and communities may co-exist in the same locations. For example, in the Brazilian Amazon, traditional dwellers are comprised by caboclos or local people who descended from immigrants who followed the several waves of resource exploitation into the region and mixed with indigenous residents ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1gpj4ne738","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Adams, Murrieta, Neves, & Harris, 2009)","plainCitation":"(C. Adams, Murrieta, Neves, & Harris, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32163,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FQR56UKJ"],"itemData":{"id":32163,"type":"book","call-number":"F2659.C32 S6313 2009","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-1-4020-9282-4","number-of-pages":"358","publisher":"Springer","publisher-place":"New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Amazon peasant societies in a changing environment: political ecology, invisibility and modernity in the rainforest","title-short":"Amazon peasant societies in a changing environment","editor":[{"family":"Adams","given":"Cristina"},{"family":"Murrieta","given":"Rui"},{"family":"Neves","given":"Walter A."},{"family":"Harris","given":"Mark"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the north central United States of America, the indigenous Menominee and Ojibwe peoples manage their tribal forests independently of the surrounding state and federally owned lands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ar01bh07s6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mausel, Waupochick, & Pecore, 2017; Ronald L. Trosper, 2012; Waller & Reo, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Mausel, Waupochick, & Pecore, 2017; Ronald L. Trosper, 2012; Waller & Reo, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32133,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7BWE5C8C"],"itemData":{"id":32133,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Forestry","DOI":"10.5849/jof.16-046","ISSN":"0022-1201","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Forestry","language":"en","page":"366-369","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Menominee Forestry: Past, Present, Future","title-short":"Menominee Forestry","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/jof/article/115/5/366-369/4599888","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Mausel","given":"David L."},{"family":"Waupochick","given":"Anthony"},{"family":"Pecore","given":"Marshall"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",9,24]]}}},{"id":32132,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KVDWJZRN"],"itemData":{"id":32132,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2012.08.002","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"56-61","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Menominee implementation of the Chichilnisky criterion for sustainable forest management","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934112001657","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Trosper","given":"Ronald L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",12]]}}},{"id":33478,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YWZ6QGJJ"],"itemData":{"id":33478,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Indigenous peoples manage forestlands and wildlife differently than public and private forestland managers. To evaluate ecological outcomes from these differences, we compared the structure, composition, and diversity of Ojibwe and Menominee tribal forests to nearby nontribal forestlands in northern Wisconsin. These indigenous peoples seek to manage forests for mature conditions, accommodate wolves and other predators, and hunt deer to sustain traditional livelihood values. Their forests are often more mature with higher tree volume, higher rates of tree regeneration, more plant diversity, and fewer invasive species than nearby nontribal forestlands. In contrast, nontribal forestlands lost appreciable plant diversity in the 20th century and have failed to regenerate tree species sensitive to deer herbivory. Ensuing shifts in forest composition and wildlife populations have jeopardized the ability of managers to sustain wildlife and meet certification standards on nontribal forestlands. Lessons from tribal forestlands could help improve the sustainable management of nontribal public forestlands.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-09865-230145","ISSN":"17083087","title":"First stewards: Ecological outcomes of forest and wildlife stewardship by indigenous peoples of Wisconsin, USA","author":[{"family":"Waller","given":"Donald M."},{"family":"Reo","given":"Nicholas J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Naturalized communities and migrants are more recent arrivals into forest zones who settled spontaneously or followed government-sponsored programs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"acbh7k1m0f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. M. Fernandes, 2004)","plainCitation":"(B. M. Fernandes, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32144,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FSBNDD2A"],"itemData":{"id":32144,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agrária (São Paulo. Online)","DOI":"10.11606/issn.1808-1150.v0i1p16-36","ISSN":"1808-1150","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Agrária (São Paulo, Online)","page":"16","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Espaços agrários de inclusão e exclusão social: novas configurações do campo brasileiro","title-short":"Espaços agrários de inclusão e exclusão social","URL":"http://revistas.usp.br/agraria/article/view/75","volume":"0","author":[{"family":"Fernandes","given":"Bernardo Mançano"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",12,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Southeast Asia, immigrants followed state-driven immigration programs but also followed the development of plantations that attracted rural labor to forest landscapes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a90o1v4rrf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Budidarsono, Susanti, & Zoomers, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Budidarsono, Susanti, & Zoomers, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31918,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/295BPMKB"],"itemData":{"id":31918,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Biofuels - Economy, Environment and Sustainability","ISBN":"978-953-51-0950-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.5772/53586","publisher":"InTech","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Oil Palm Plantations in Indonesia: The Implications for Migration, Settlement/Resettlement and Local Economic Development","title-short":"Oil Palm Plantations in Indonesia","URL":"http://www.intechopen.com/books/biofuels-economy-environment-and-sustainability/oil-palm-plantations-in-indonesia-the-implications-for-migration-settlement-resettlement-and-local-e","editor":[{"family":"Fang","given":"Zhen"}],"author":[{"family":"Budidarsono","given":"Suseno"},{"family":"Susanti","given":"Ari"},{"family":"Zoomers","given":"Annelies"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",1,23]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. All these local groups undertake some type of small-scale logging, along with landless people trying to make a living, a portion of which may carry out logging operations on smallholder lands through different arrangements. 
	Smallholder plot sizes range widely across world regions. Plots in more remote areas tend to have more independent logging activities. For example, 60% of family forest owners in the United States of America have an area ranging between 0.4 - 4.0 ha ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a13me3fu26s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Snyder, Butler, & Markowski-Lindsay, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Snyder, Butler, & Markowski-Lindsay, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32135,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SYSDVZEE"],"itemData":{"id":32135,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Small-scale Forestry","DOI":"10.1007/s11842-018-9410-9","ISSN":"1873-7617, 1873-7854","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Small-scale Forestry","language":"en","page":"127-147","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Small-Area Family Forest Ownerships in the USA","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11842-018-9410-9","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Snyder","given":"Stephanie A."},{"family":"Butler","given":"Brett J."},{"family":"Markowski-Lindsay","given":"Marla"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many smallholder farmers in the Amazon have access to larger pieces of land of up to roughly 100 ha ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2g6j320gcv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Siegmund-Schultze, Rischkowsky, da Veiga, & King, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Siegmund-Schultze, Rischkowsky, da Veiga, & King, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32183,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WF9P8JQW"],"itemData":{"id":32183,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agricultural Systems","DOI":"10.1016/j.agsy.2007.03.005","ISSN":"0308521X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Agricultural Systems","language":"en","page":"738-749","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Cattle are cash generating assets for mixed smallholder farms in the Eastern Amazon","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308521X07000492","volume":"94","author":[{"family":"Siegmund-Schultze","given":"M."},{"family":"Rischkowsky","given":"B."},{"family":"Veiga","given":"J.B.","non-dropping-particle":"da"},{"family":"King","given":"J.M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a9l5heodf2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Budiman, Fujiwara, Sato, & Pamungkas, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Budiman, Fujiwara, Sato, & Pamungkas, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31919,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4P2VTMMJ"],"itemData":{"id":31919,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Local wisdom has been coexisting with the state system in several places in Indonesia. The Mountain Mutis Nature Reserve in East Nusa Tenggara province is the strict nature reserves, but a customary land tenure system, called suf, exists so far in the nature reserve. The objectives of this study are (1) to organize the historical territorialization process, (2) to clarify the customary land tenure system and activities for livelihoods by local people, and (3) to discuss the challenges of its land tenure system to manage forests sustainably as well as policy methods to harmonize legal pluralism in Mutis Area. Field observation and in-depth interviews with key informants were employed for data collection, and the collected data were analyzed by a qualitative descriptive method. The findings showed the traditional reward and punishment systems regarding extracting non-timber forest products, grazing livestock, and preventing forest fires were working well for sustainable forest management. However, increased pressure on forests due to future population growth appears to have an impact on the traditional system. It also showed the government officers and local people started some discussions to recognize the suf in the formal legal order. However, there were institutional problems to introduce current state systems. Therefore, it is required to flexibly operate or revise the state laws according to the actual situation to harmonize society between state and people.","container-title":"Jurnal Manajemen Hutan Tropika (Journal of Tropical Forest Management)","DOI":"10.7226/jtfm.26.3.244","ISSN":"20870469, 20892063","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"jtfm","page":"244-253","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Another Law in Indonesia: Customary Land Tenure System Coexisting with State Order in Mutis Forest","title-short":"Another Law in Indonesia","URL":"http://journal.ipb.ac.id/index.php/jmht/article/view/31447/20554","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Budiman","given":"I"},{"family":"Fujiwara","given":"T"},{"family":"Sato","given":"N"},{"family":"Pamungkas","given":"D"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the Amazon, the more remote farms are, the higher the probability that they still have some primary forest remnants stocking their property. These remote farmers often operate more independently regardless of the status of their tenure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ao4hhp2duo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Serra, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Serra, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32174,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AA498I6K"],"itemData":{"id":32174,"type":"thesis","event-place":"Freiburg","genre":"Doctoral Thesis","publisher":"University of Freiburg","publisher-place":"Freiburg","title":"Family Farming in the Amazon: A Dead End or the Way Ahead for Sustainable Development? A Case Study from the Trans-Amazon Highway in Brazil","URL":"https://d1wqtxts1xzle7.cloudfront.net/63141872/Thesis_Serra_20_02_202020200429-90069-niu049.pdf?1588207416=&response-content-disposition=inline%3B+filename%3DFamily_Farming_in_the_Amazon_A_Dead_End.pdf&Expires=1617126611&Signature=HB~sfM6ESgjCMwTxCeQGEHM~NV0Z8yyL9uwwriAVfZc5XADWhGB1cKsta0VFD0fHR5OwdeJ6CySv-b7Q2BgOGsUEDRmXZxmsK013iZ2OwTHJGV5uKzmALybhgJU-r-qiupq3wRAj2vW9bDeK00jOBERjsq3l0ML5zZ7AiBDJ6PbZbvn4tQ1oANPj8cO4ScqheZxpb18mX0a3Ot5BWfgRgr55mNTx0VEn24Szawd8aiLpWbmWuFkkoQyLiTKv9QnwquuP0ZymqX7uf31AkGZmXReTNn0aHs0QDY5h5ennMX~QxivwZRKTlQYeREAya9EwsAbGW8AtFVYWLj5UQ12kIA__&Key-Pair-Id=APKAJLOHF5GGSLRBV4ZA","author":[{"family":"Serra","given":"Anderson Borges"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the Amazon, most of the forests on the land occupied by immigrant smallholders are already degraded from fires or former harvesting by commercial loggers. Smallholders may harvest trees from their plots, but, due to the immense logistical and legal challenges, this is rarely carried out for commercial purposes. Accordingly, for most farmers, forests are a reserve for agricultural land, or provide materials for subsistence needs e.g., fences, fuel wood) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a229sg47j2a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pacheco, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Pacheco, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32191,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XIYKC28C"],"itemData":{"id":32191,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-009-9220-y","ISSN":"0300-7839, 1572-9915","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Hum Ecol","language":"en","page":"27-41","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Smallholder Livelihoods, Wealth and Deforestation in the Eastern Amazon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10745-009-9220-y","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Pacheco","given":"Pablo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. If marketable timber is still available, they may also extract trees for commercial purposes when quick cash is needed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a73fi4eki5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pokorny, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Pokorny, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EN7YYVSN"],"itemData":{"id":31225,"type":"book","event-place":"Place of publication not identified","ISBN":"978-1-138-57317-8","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 993640230","publisher":"ROUTLEDGE","publisher-place":"Place of publication not identified","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Smallholders, forest management and rural development in the amazon.","author":[{"family":"Pokorny","given":"Benno"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While smallholder farmers selectively extract high-value timber from remnant forests, they may also sell timber that originated from secondary forests emerging in agricultural fallows, often to local markets. At the same time, growing trees is an essential component of most smallholders' production systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a16bquvet3a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoch, Pokorny, & de Jong, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Hoch, Pokorny, & de Jong, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32185,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9ARZZPRG"],"itemData":{"id":32185,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agroforestry Systems","DOI":"10.1007/s10457-012-9480-1","ISSN":"0167-4366, 1572-9680","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Agroforest Syst","language":"en","page":"361-375","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Financial attractiveness of smallholder tree plantations in the Amazon: bridging external expectations and local realities","title-short":"Financial attractiveness of smallholder tree plantations in the Amazon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10457-012-9480-1","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Hoch","given":"L."},{"family":"Pokorny","given":"B."},{"family":"Jong","given":"W.","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Accordingly, over time, landscapes occupied by smallholders develop complex land-use mosaics that include swidden fields, fallows, agroforestry plots and forest patches ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2h466kqgbu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Denevan & Padoch, 1987; Padoch & Pinedo-V\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}squez, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Denevan & Padoch, 1987; Padoch & Pinedo-Vásquez, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32146,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GBZVK8IF"],"itemData":{"id":32146,"type":"book","call-number":"F2270.2.B6 S95 1988","collection-number":"v. 5","collection-title":"Advances in economic botany","event-place":"Bronx, N.Y., U.S.A","ISBN":"978-0-89327-325-5","number-of-pages":"107","publisher":"New York Botanical Garden","publisher-place":"Bronx, N.Y., U.S.A","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Swidden-fallow agroforestry in the Peruvian Amazon","editor":[{"family":"Denevan","given":"William M."},{"family":"Padoch","given":"Christine"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1987"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32192,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UPRYP9XR"],"itemData":{"id":32192,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Human Impacts on Amazonia","ISBN":"978-0-231-10589-7","note":"DOI: 10.7312/pose10588-013","page":"172-180","publisher":"Columbia University Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"10. Concurrent Activities and Invisible Technologies: An Example of Timber Management in Amazonia","title-short":"10. Concurrent Activities and Invisible Technologies","URL":"https://www.degruyter.com/document/doi/10.7312/pose10588-013/html","editor":[{"family":"Posey","given":"Darrell A."},{"family":"Balick","given":"Michael J."}],"author":[{"family":"Padoch","given":"Christine"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vásquez","given":"Miguel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",12,31]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	The small-scale logging industry is characterized by stakeholders that may or may not have a felling permit, often use chainsaws (sometimes mobile saw) for felling and processing in the forest, have smaller numbers of trees per operation, often produce lower quality sawnwood for national market and neighboring countries and is largely informal ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ofabo9jte","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cerutti & Lescuyer, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Cerutti & Lescuyer, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FNHXP5NB"],"itemData":{"id":31288,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia.","ISBN":"978-602-8693-44-8","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 786191932","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia.","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"The domestic market for small-scale chainsaw milling in Cameroon: present situation, opportunities and challenges","title-short":"The domestic market for small-scale chainsaw milling in Cameroon","author":[{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition to chainsaws, winches and canoes with outboard motors are often used when water transport is involved. Chain saw milling requires a relatively small investment as the equipment is readily available and inexpensive to buy or rent, is portable and efficient ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"atu6a5khto","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pinard, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Pinard, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32205,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F7JMDYYC"],"itemData":{"id":32205,"type":"chapter","container-title":"A Cut for the Poor, Proceedings of the International Conference on Managing Forests for Poverty Reduction: Capturing Opportunities in Forest Harvesting and Wood Processing for the Benefit of the Poor","event-place":"Ho Chi Min City, Vietnam","page":"3-6","publisher-place":"Ho Chi Min City, Vietnam","title":"Processing lumber with chainsaws: relevance for households in the forest zone of Ghana.","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/ag131e/ag131e19.htm","author":[{"family":"Pinard","given":"Michelle A"},{"family":"Adam","given":"Kwame A"},{"family":"Cobbinah","given":"Joseph R"},{"family":"Nutukor","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Damnyang","given":"Lawrence"},{"family":"Nyarko","given":"Charles"},{"family":"Nketiah","given":"Samuel K"},{"family":"Boatang","given":"Kyereh"},{"family":"Abrebesse","given":"Owusu M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Among the products of chainsaw milling are boards and planks for personal use, those that are sold directly to the market, and blocks or scantlings that are further processed in sawmills ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1a0selev54","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wit, van Dam, Omar Cerutti, Lescuyer, & Mckeown, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Wit, van Dam, Omar Cerutti, Lescuyer, & Mckeown, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32206,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z7YI4UX3"],"itemData":{"id":32206,"type":"article-journal","language":"en","page":"16","source":"Zotero","title":"Chainsaw milling: supplier to local markets — a synthesis","URL":"https://agritrop.cirad.fr/559325/1/document_559325.pdf","author":[{"family":"Wit","given":"Marieke"},{"family":"Dam","given":"Jinke","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Omar Cerutti","given":"Paolo"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Mckeown","given":"James Parker"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The logging team consists of a few individuals who could be part of an entitled community or recruited from elsewhere, and they may own their own equipment or operate equipment owned by others ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a12aokvfvjl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Salo, Sir\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}n, & Kalliola, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Salo, Sirén, & Kalliola, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31913,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N4Z54KP9"],"itemData":{"id":31913,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Diagnosing Wild Species Harvest","ISBN":"978-0-12-397204-0","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1016/B978-0-12-397204-0.00009-7","page":"161-177","publisher":"Elsevier","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Changing the Law of the Jungle","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/B9780123972040000097","author":[{"family":"Salo","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Sirén","given":"Anders"},{"family":"Kalliola","given":"Risto"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. They harvest significantly smaller volumes of timber. The practice is usually very selective, concentrating only on the most valuable commercial species such as, in the tropics, mahogany, cedar, teak and tornillo (Cedrelinga catanaeformis). Chainsaw systems persist especially in areas with more rugged terrain. Across the United States of America, one-third to over three-quarters of loggers used chainsaw felling ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"agosqua1fa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Conrad, Greene, & Hiesl, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Conrad, Greene, & Hiesl, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32134,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P4I2BSPA"],"itemData":{"id":32134,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Forestry","DOI":"10.1093/jofore/fvx014","ISSN":"0022-1201, 1938-3746","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"291-303","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A Review of Changes in US Logging Businesses 1980s–Present","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/jof/article/116/3/291/4980796","volume":"116","author":[{"family":"Conrad","given":"Joseph L"},{"family":"Greene","given":"W Dale"},{"family":"Hiesl","given":"Patrick"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",5,4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wherever possible, small-scale chainsaw millers target large trees to maximize their output ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6kG2lojq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cerutti & Lescuyer, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Cerutti & Lescuyer, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FNHXP5NB"],"itemData":{"id":31288,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia.","ISBN":"978-602-8693-44-8","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 786191932","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia.","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"The domestic market for small-scale chainsaw milling in Cameroon: present situation, opportunities and challenges","title-short":"The domestic market for small-scale chainsaw milling in Cameroon","author":[{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Wood production by small-scale chain saw operators can be for personal use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ul3kmdork","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Snyder, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Snyder, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32135,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SYSDVZEE"],"itemData":{"id":32135,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Small-scale Forestry","DOI":"10.1007/s11842-018-9410-9","ISSN":"1873-7617, 1873-7854","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Small-scale Forestry","language":"en","page":"127-147","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Small-Area Family Forest Ownerships in the USA","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11842-018-9410-9","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Snyder","given":"Stephanie A."},{"family":"Butler","given":"Brett J."},{"family":"Markowski-Lindsay","given":"Marla"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and to supply domestic markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a94c8r5kd9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rozemeijer & Aggrey, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Rozemeijer & Aggrey, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32211,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PD3TS9JA"],"itemData":{"id":32211,"type":"book","event-place":"Wageningen","ISBN":"978-90-5113-106-2","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 798003055","publisher":"Tropenbos International","publisher-place":"Wageningen","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Securing legal domestic lumber supply through multi-stakeholder dialogue in Ghana","URL":"http://edepot.wur.nl/214614","author":[{"family":"Rozemeijer","given":"Nico"},{"family":"Aggrey","given":"Jane"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some instances, the wood is for social or community purposes and not sold or exchanged ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aaegua26vf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lesniewska & McDermott, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Lesniewska & McDermott, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32212,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HJHJ85G7"],"itemData":{"id":32212,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2014.01.005","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"16-23","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"FLEGT VPAs: Laying a pathway to sustainability via legality lessons from Ghana and Indonesia","title-short":"FLEGT VPAs","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934114000185","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Lesniewska","given":"Feja"},{"family":"McDermott","given":"Constance L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. When entering into formal markets, the timber is usually purchased by middlemen at cheaper prices who then sell it to the timber industries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wiFJbh4X","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Salo, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Salo, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31913,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N4Z54KP9"],"itemData":{"id":31913,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Diagnosing Wild Species Harvest","ISBN":"978-0-12-397204-0","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1016/B978-0-12-397204-0.00009-7","page":"161-177","publisher":"Elsevier","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Changing the Law of the Jungle","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/B9780123972040000097","author":[{"family":"Salo","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Sirén","given":"Anders"},{"family":"Kalliola","given":"Risto"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The industry is rapidly growing in tropical countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a25afl4uv67","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoare, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Hoare, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31968,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LVJSVYIQ"],"itemData":{"id":31968,"type":"book","event-place":"London: Chatham House","publisher-place":"London: Chatham House","title":"Tackling illegal logging and the related trade. What progress and where next.","author":[{"family":"Hoare","given":"Alison"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, representing approximately 30-40% (in Guyana, Republic of Congo, Democratic Republic of Congo and Uganda), more than 50% (in Ghana, Cameroon and Peru), and almost 100% (in Liberia) of total timber trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a378h2spbg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wit, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Wit, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32206,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z7YI4UX3"],"itemData":{"id":32206,"type":"article-journal","language":"en","page":"16","source":"Zotero","title":"Chainsaw milling: supplier to local markets — a synthesis","URL":"https://agritrop.cirad.fr/559325/1/document_559325.pdf","author":[{"family":"Wit","given":"Marieke"},{"family":"Dam","given":"Jinke","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Omar Cerutti","given":"Paolo"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Mckeown","given":"James Parker"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, wood for timber is only a small part of the total domestic market, with most locally traded wood in the tropics being used for fuel or made into charcoal ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Z6LNySqy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wit, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Wit, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32206,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z7YI4UX3"],"itemData":{"id":32206,"type":"article-journal","language":"en","page":"16","source":"Zotero","title":"Chainsaw milling: supplier to local markets — a synthesis","URL":"https://agritrop.cirad.fr/559325/1/document_559325.pdf","author":[{"family":"Wit","given":"Marieke"},{"family":"Dam","given":"Jinke","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Omar Cerutti","given":"Paolo"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Mckeown","given":"James Parker"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
	In many Amazonian countries (e.g., Bolivia, Peru, Ecuador), smallholders are allowed to extract timber from their properties for commercial purpose, yet they have to obtain permits, often through simplified processes including simpler management plans ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aabns42kc4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cerutti & Lescuyer, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Cerutti & Lescuyer, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FNHXP5NB"],"itemData":{"id":31288,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia.","ISBN":"978-602-8693-44-8","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 786191932","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia.","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"The domestic market for small-scale chainsaw milling in Cameroon: present situation, opportunities and challenges","title-short":"The domestic market for small-scale chainsaw milling in Cameroon","author":[{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). That said, few smallholders, such as those in the Peruvian Amazon, have secured formal property rights ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ntkimrrmh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cronkleton & Larson, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Cronkleton & Larson, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32145,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6LPVXP4Y"],"itemData":{"id":32145,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"10.1080/08941920.2015.1014609","ISSN":"0894-1920, 1521-0723","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Society & Natural Resources","language":"en","page":"496-512","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Formalization and Collective Appropriation of Space on Forest Frontiers: Comparing Communal and Individual Property Systems in the Peruvian and Ecuadoran Amazon","title-short":"Formalization and Collective Appropriation of Space on Forest Frontiers","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/08941920.2015.1014609","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Cronkleton","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Larson","given":"Anne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",5,4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For those with formal usufruct rights to households occupying forest lands, they may be able to register a formal forest management plan to carry out selective timber-harvesting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a233ki56spb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Robiglio, Acevedo, & Simauchi, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Robiglio, Acevedo, & Simauchi, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32166,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A4839AZX"],"itemData":{"id":32166,"type":"book","event-place":"Lima, Perú.","publisher":"ICRAF Oficina Regional para América Latina","publisher-place":"Lima, Perú.","title":"Diagnóstico de los productores familiares en la Amazonía Peruana","author":[{"family":"Robiglio","given":"Valentina"},{"family":"Acevedo","given":"Martín Reyes"},{"family":"Simauchi","given":"Elena Castro"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In spite of the options allowing for the use of simplified plans, only a small portion of smallholders formally apply for forest permits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"atjaokjskp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pacheco, Mej\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a, Cano, & de Jong, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Pacheco, Mejía, Cano, & de Jong, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31916,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PUF7FNLN"],"itemData":{"id":31916,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f7090193","ISSN":"1999-4907","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Forests","language":"en","page":"193","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Smallholder Forestry in the Western Amazon: Outcomes from Forest Reforms and Emerging Policy Perspectives","title-short":"Smallholder Forestry in the Western Amazon","URL":"http://www.mdpi.com/1999-4907/7/9/193","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Pacheco","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Mejía","given":"Elena"},{"family":"Cano","given":"Walter"},{"family":"Jong","given":"Wil","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",8,31]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Box 3.15 Smallholder logging in Ucayali, Peruvian Amazon

There are approximately 440,000 smallholder producers (i.e., plots <115 hectares) in Ucayali region in the Peruvian Amazon, with approximately 80% holding less than 20 hectares of land ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ap7bif35r8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Robiglio, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Robiglio, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32166,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A4839AZX"],"itemData":{"id":32166,"type":"book","event-place":"Lima, Perú.","publisher":"ICRAF Oficina Regional para América Latina","publisher-place":"Lima, Perú.","title":"Diagnóstico de los productores familiares en la Amazonía Peruana","author":[{"family":"Robiglio","given":"Valentina"},{"family":"Acevedo","given":"Martín Reyes"},{"family":"Simauchi","given":"Elena Castro"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is estimated that mosaic production systems of these smallholders cover more the 4.5 million hectares in the Peruvian Amazon, with approximately 90,000 ha under fallow-forestry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a13movbn06e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sears, Pinedo-Vasquez, & Padoch, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Sears, Pinedo-Vasquez, & Padoch, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32170,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CF57NKQS"],"itemData":{"id":32170,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The Social Lives of Forests: Past, Present, and Future of Woodland Resurgence","ISBN":"978-0-226-32268-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.7208/chicago/9780226024134.001.0001","page":"336-347","publisher":"University of Chicago Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"26. From Fallow Timber to Urban Housing: Family Forestry and Tablilla Production in Peru","URL":"http://www.bibliovault.org/BV.landing.epl?ISBN=9780226322681","author":[{"family":"Sears","given":"Robin R"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Padoch","given":"Christine"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Farmers in the Ucayali region of Peru produce 950,000 m3 of sawn wood annually ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1q58jc4dlu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sears, Cronkleton, Polo Villanueva, Miranda Ruiz, & P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez-Ojeda del Arco, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Sears, Cronkleton, Polo Villanueva, Miranda Ruiz, & Pérez-Ojeda del Arco, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32173,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J45AN4D5"],"itemData":{"id":32173,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2017.11.004","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"49-58","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Farm-forestry in the Peruvian Amazon and the feasibility of its regulation through forest policy reform","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934117302666","volume":"87","author":[{"family":"Sears","given":"Robin R."},{"family":"Cronkleton","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Polo Villanueva","given":"Fredy"},{"family":"Miranda Ruiz","given":"Medardo"},{"family":"Pérez-Ojeda del Arco","given":"Matías"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is based on production of 38 m3 ha-1 of sawnwood on a stand of 7 years. Smallholder farmers extract timber from remnant standing forests but also from secondary forests growing from fallows. The main product from the fallow-forestry system is small dimension lumber from Guazuma crinita (Sterculiaceae) and Calycophyllum spruceanum and Rubiaceae ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sUqpDIQv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sears, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Sears, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32170,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CF57NKQS"],"itemData":{"id":32170,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The Social Lives of Forests: Past, Present, and Future of Woodland Resurgence","ISBN":"978-0-226-32268-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.7208/chicago/9780226024134.001.0001","page":"336-347","publisher":"University of Chicago Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"26. From Fallow Timber to Urban Housing: Family Forestry and Tablilla Production in Peru","URL":"http://www.bibliovault.org/BV.landing.epl?ISBN=9780226322681","author":[{"family":"Sears","given":"Robin R"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Padoch","given":"Christine"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	In tropical regions, forest concessions occupy more than 20% of public forests in west and central Africa and Southeast Asia and about 4% in Latin America. In the tropics 15% of forests are managed by communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2h9jalkv6o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arts & de Koning, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Arts & de Koning, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32202,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8SDYBCTS"],"itemData":{"id":32202,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2017.03.014","ISSN":"0305750X","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","language":"en","page":"315-325","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community Forest Management: An Assessment and Explanation of its Performance Through QCA","title-short":"Community Forest Management","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0305750X17300761","volume":"96","author":[{"family":"Arts","given":"Bas"},{"family":"Koning","given":"Jessica","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  All these communities manage forests through different socio-ecological systems with very peculiar characteristics that are associated with traditional knowledge and community identity. 

	Informal logging by smallholders provides thousands of jobs in Central African countries. In the Congo Basin, countries have embraced forest policies that mainly targeted the sustainable management of timber in large-scale timber-harvesting concessions targeting export markets and overlooked small-scale production. Yet small-scale chainsaw milling, which is chiefly informal, has undergone rapid development to meet the domestic demand for cheap timber in Central African countries and other nearby countries, as well as the interests of stakeholders all along the chain of custody ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2c483mr36","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eba\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}a Atyi, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Eba’a Atyi, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32196,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A3PYI6U3"],"itemData":{"id":32196,"type":"report","event-place":"Yaoundé, Cameroon","number":"4","publisher":"CIFOR report for ITTO","publisher-place":"Yaoundé, Cameroon","title":"Domestic markets, cross-border trade and the role of the informal sector in Cote d’Ivoire, Cameroon and the Democratic Republic of Congo.","author":[{"family":"Eba’a Atyi","given":"R"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"G"},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"P.O."},{"family":"Tsanga","given":"R"},{"family":"Essiane Mendoula","given":"E"},{"family":"Collins","given":"F"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Over the last decade, in Central Africa, the annual volume of timber from informal chainsaw milling consumed domestically or unofficially exported to nearby countries is greater than that of timber from the industrial sector ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"asprckn66a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Guillaume Lescuyer & Cerutti, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Guillaume Lescuyer & Cerutti, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32181,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B483PAZ4"],"itemData":{"id":32181,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Perspective","issue":"21","language":"fr","page":"4","source":"Zotero","title":"Politiques de Gestion Durable Des Forêts En Afrique Centrale?: Prendre En Compte Le Secteur Informel.","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Cameroon, around 45,000 people find their main employment in this sector ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uwXosGtb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cerutti & Lescuyer, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Cerutti & Lescuyer, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FNHXP5NB"],"itemData":{"id":31288,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia.","ISBN":"978-602-8693-44-8","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 786191932","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia.","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"The domestic market for small-scale chainsaw milling in Cameroon: present situation, opportunities and challenges","title-short":"The domestic market for small-scale chainsaw milling in Cameroon","author":[{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the cities of Congo, the Central African Republic and Gabon, more than 1,000 people have jobs directly linked to the sale of small-scale timber production ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1umf6vkid0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Guillaume Lescuyer, Cerutti, & Robiglio, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Guillaume Lescuyer, Cerutti, & Robiglio, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32177,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TKGKJENT"],"itemData":{"id":32177,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2013.02.010","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"68-77","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Artisanal chainsaw milling to support decentralized management of timber in Central Africa? An analysis through the theory of access","title-short":"Artisanal chainsaw milling to support decentralized management of timber in Central Africa?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934113000488","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Robiglio","given":"Valentina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
 	Small-scale chainsaw milling is an important source of income for rural stakeholders, and accepted by urban consumers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a263cuo1clq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Guillaume Lescuyer, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Guillaume Lescuyer, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32182,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T84R5WCR"],"itemData":{"id":32182,"type":"article-journal","language":"en","page":"72","source":"Zotero","title":"National demand for sawnwood in Cameroon","author":[{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Tsanga","given":"Raphaël"},{"family":"Mendoula","given":"Edouard Essiane"},{"family":"Ahanda","given":"Barthélémy Xavier Embolo"},{"family":"Ouedraogo","given":"Hadji Adama"},{"family":"Fung","given":"Obed"},{"family":"Dubiez","given":"Emilien"},{"family":"Logo","given":"Patrice Bigombe"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, who gain access to materials at prices three to four times lower than those from industrial timber ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a28pc2ci705","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Guillaume Lescuyer, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Guillaume Lescuyer, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32177,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TKGKJENT"],"itemData":{"id":32177,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2013.02.010","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"68-77","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Artisanal chainsaw milling to support decentralized management of timber in Central Africa? An analysis through the theory of access","title-short":"Artisanal chainsaw milling to support decentralized management of timber in Central Africa?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934113000488","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Robiglio","given":"Valentina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In remote areas, smallholders, when in need to harvest and sell timber often face distorted market conditions, mainly for two reasons. They may suffer from elevated transport costs, due to long distances, bad roads, and small quantities, or, to avoid logistical challenges depend on intermediaries or sawmill operators that tend to underprice the timber ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"at1gt2qmam","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pacheco, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Pacheco, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33829,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I549BUNA"],"itemData":{"id":33829,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation and Society","DOI":"10.4103/0972-4923.97484","ISSN":"0972-4923","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Conservat Soc","language":"en","page":"114","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Smallholders and communities in timber markets: Conditions shaping diverse forms of engagement in tropical Latin America","title-short":"Smallholders and communities in timber markets","URL":"http://www.conservationandsociety.org/text.asp?2012/10/2/114/97484","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Pacheco","given":"Pablo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In locations closer to the markets, smallholders who still dispose on forests, are better engaged to extend market networks managed by intermediaries who organize the extraction in response to orders from end-buyers in the cities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2pk2q4n5rt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mejia, Pacheco, Muzo, & Torres, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Mejia, Pacheco, Muzo, & Torres, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32179,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PHVZVQFA"],"itemData":{"id":32179,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Smallholders control an important portion of forestlands in the Ecuadorian Amazon. Timber is not the main source of income but contributes to complement relatively diversified livelihood strategies of smallholders. Timber extraction is stimulated by a vigorous network of intermediaries linked to the end-markets in the cities. Most small-scale operations are undertaken informally since smallholders require complying with still cumbersome procedures in spite of state efforts for simplifying the forestry regulations. Benefits for smallholders from timber extraction depend on how the harvesting operations are organized and whether they involve the participation of smallholders as well as on the species harvested, and whether these operations are conducted with a formal permit or not. This paper assesses the role of timber in smallholders’ livelihoods, the influence of how timber operations are undertaken has on benefits distribution, and the role that urban markets have on shaping the dynamics of timber supply.","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554815814668954","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"38-50","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Smallholders and timber extraction in the Ecuadorian Amazon: amidst market opportunities and regulatory constraints","title-short":"Smallholders and timber extraction in the Ecuadorian Amazon","URL":"http://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/146554815814668954","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Mejia","given":"E."},{"family":"Pacheco","given":"P."},{"family":"Muzo","given":"A."},{"family":"Torres","given":"B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Main markets are for construction such as in Central Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2bf4asf5f1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eba\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}a Atyi, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Eba’a Atyi, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32196,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A3PYI6U3"],"itemData":{"id":32196,"type":"report","event-place":"Yaoundé, Cameroon","number":"4","publisher":"CIFOR report for ITTO","publisher-place":"Yaoundé, Cameroon","title":"Domestic markets, cross-border trade and the role of the informal sector in Cote d’Ivoire, Cameroon and the Democratic Republic of Congo.","author":[{"family":"Eba’a Atyi","given":"R"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"G"},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"P.O."},{"family":"Tsanga","given":"R"},{"family":"Essiane Mendoula","given":"E"},{"family":"Collins","given":"F"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and the furniture industry such as in Jepara district, Indonesia (Box 3.16). In some places in the Amazon, the broader value chain for small-dimension lumber supports hundreds of other actors involved in the harvest, transport, transformation, and wholesale activities within marketing networks stretching from remote areas of the Amazon to major urban centers in Peru’s coast and highlands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1uk589hg1n","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pokorny, 2013; Sears, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Pokorny, 2013; Sears, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EN7YYVSN"],"itemData":{"id":31225,"type":"book","event-place":"Place of publication not identified","ISBN":"978-1-138-57317-8","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 993640230","publisher":"ROUTLEDGE","publisher-place":"Place of publication not identified","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Smallholders, forest management and rural development in the amazon.","author":[{"family":"Pokorny","given":"Benno"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32173,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J45AN4D5"],"itemData":{"id":32173,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2017.11.004","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"49-58","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Farm-forestry in the Peruvian Amazon and the feasibility of its regulation through forest policy reform","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934117302666","volume":"87","author":[{"family":"Sears","given":"Robin R."},{"family":"Cronkleton","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Polo Villanueva","given":"Fredy"},{"family":"Miranda Ruiz","given":"Medardo"},{"family":"Pérez-Ojeda del Arco","given":"Matías"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	In the Amazon, for most smallholders, primary forests play only a little role for income generation, whenever wood products such as firewood, poles, or for construction are regularly used by the families ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1g4hcjjh1c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Porro, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Porro, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32194,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NIJ5CPYR"],"itemData":{"id":32194,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tropics","DOI":"10.3759/tropics.23.47","ISSN":"0917-415X, 1882-5729","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Tropics","language":"en","page":"47-62","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forest Use and Agriculture in Ucayali, Peruvian Amazon: Interactions Among Livelihood Strategies, Income and Environmental Outcomes","title-short":"Forest Use and Agriculture in Ucayali, Peruvian Amazon","URL":"http://jlc.jst.go.jp/DN/JST.JSTAGE/tropics/23.47?lang=en&from=CrossRef&type=abstract","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Porro","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Lopez-Feldman","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"W. Vela-Alvarado","given":"Jorge"},{"family":"QuiÑonez-Ruíz","given":"Lourdes"},{"family":"P. Seijas-Cardenas","given":"Zully"},{"family":"Vásquez-Macedo","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Salazar-Arista","given":"Clemente"},{"family":"I. NúÑez-Paredes","given":"Vladimir"},{"family":"Cardenas-Ruiz","given":"Jefferson"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition, forest fallows have a potential to generate income if the production areas are located near to roads and markets. In such conditions, farmers benefit from additional incomes from selling wood ranging from 35 to 1870 United States dollars per hectare ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a29o5rpep39","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoch, 2012; Sears, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Hoch, 2012; Sears, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32185,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9ARZZPRG"],"itemData":{"id":32185,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agroforestry Systems","DOI":"10.1007/s10457-012-9480-1","ISSN":"0167-4366, 1572-9680","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Agroforest Syst","language":"en","page":"361-375","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Financial attractiveness of smallholder tree plantations in the Amazon: bridging external expectations and local realities","title-short":"Financial attractiveness of smallholder tree plantations in the Amazon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10457-012-9480-1","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Hoch","given":"L."},{"family":"Pokorny","given":"B."},{"family":"Jong","given":"W.","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",3]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32170,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CF57NKQS"],"itemData":{"id":32170,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The Social Lives of Forests: Past, Present, and Future of Woodland Resurgence","ISBN":"978-0-226-32268-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.7208/chicago/9780226024134.001.0001","page":"336-347","publisher":"University of Chicago Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"26. From Fallow Timber to Urban Housing: Family Forestry and Tablilla Production in Peru","URL":"http://www.bibliovault.org/BV.landing.epl?ISBN=9780226322681","author":[{"family":"Sears","given":"Robin R"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Padoch","given":"Christine"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, with multiplier income effects associated with the processing. In Central Africa, chainsaw milling also constitutes an important source of employment for rural population, which translates into a relatively regular income stream on the lack of other job opportunities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dUxvvnoI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eba\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}a Atyi, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Eba’a Atyi, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32196,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A3PYI6U3"],"itemData":{"id":32196,"type":"report","event-place":"Yaoundé, Cameroon","number":"4","publisher":"CIFOR report for ITTO","publisher-place":"Yaoundé, Cameroon","title":"Domestic markets, cross-border trade and the role of the informal sector in Cote d’Ivoire, Cameroon and the Democratic Republic of Congo.","author":[{"family":"Eba’a Atyi","given":"R"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"G"},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"P.O."},{"family":"Tsanga","given":"R"},{"family":"Essiane Mendoula","given":"E"},{"family":"Collins","given":"F"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Box 3.16 The furniture industry in Indonesia

Furniture is an important industry within the forest-based sectors for several reasons. First, micro, small and medium enterprises play a significant role in creating employment. The furniture sector provides direct employment to approximately 500,000 individuals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15pvssoiug","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Munadi, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Munadi, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32180,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D4MUS545"],"itemData":{"id":32180,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Info Komoditi Furniture","event-place":"Jakarta","publisher":"Indonesia: Kementerian Perdagangan","publisher-place":"Jakarta","title":"Furnitur, Produk Berdaya Saing Yang Butuh Perhatian.","URL":"http://bppp.kemendag.go.id/media_content/2017/10/Isi_BRIK_FURNITUR.pdf","author":[{"family":"Munadi","given":"Ernawati"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Second, the industry contributes significantly in terms of foreign exchange. In 2019, Indonesia exported 1.7 billion United States dollars from the furniture exports ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aup5jkdr65","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bank Indonesia, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Bank Indonesia, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32187,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/75RPR5FU"],"itemData":{"id":32187,"type":"report","event-place":"Jakarta, Indonesia","publisher":"Bank Indonesia","publisher-place":"Jakarta, Indonesia","title":"Table V.13. Value of Non-Oil and Gas Export by Commodity.","author":[{"family":"Bank Indonesia","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Third, the furniture industry also represents Indonesian identity in international markets since Jepara is a furniture producing district with global recognition as furniture and woodcraft center ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1vjh16db6o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pujiati, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Pujiati, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32171,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RN3YTAFL"],"itemData":{"id":32171,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Info Komoditi Furniture","event-place":"Jakarta, Indonesia","page":"7-36","publisher-place":"Jakarta, Indonesia","title":"Produksi Furniture Indonesia","URL":"http://bppp.kemendag.go.id/media_content/2017/10/Isi_BRIK_FURNITUR.pdf","author":[{"family":"Pujiati","given":"Riska"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Ironically, the performance of the industry at the national level is not well-known, and national estimates regarding the size of the industry are based on limited data. The most valuable data comes from the Central Statistical Agency, which reported that by 2019, there were 145,000 furniture micro, small and medium enterprises in Indonesia (wooden and nonwooden-based), representing about 3.3% of the total sample of 4.4 million micro, small, and medium enterprises ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15q593f2el","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(BPS, 2020)","plainCitation":"(BPS, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32137,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FED9GCTE"],"itemData":{"id":32137,"type":"report","event-place":"Jakarta, Indonesia","publisher":"Badan Pusat Statistik","publisher-place":"Jakarta, Indonesia","title":"Profil Industri Mikro Dan Kecil 2019","URL":"https://www.bps.go.id/publication/2020/11/16/db2fdf158825afb80a113b6a/profil-industri-mikro-dan-kecil-2019.html","author":[{"family":"BPS","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A Ministry of Industry report shows that wooden-based furniture producers represent about 80% of the total furniture producers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a916k8n106","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Munadi, 2017; Pujiati, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Munadi, 2017; Pujiati, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32180,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D4MUS545"],"itemData":{"id":32180,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Info Komoditi Furniture","event-place":"Jakarta","publisher":"Indonesia: Kementerian Perdagangan","publisher-place":"Jakarta","title":"Furnitur, Produk Berdaya Saing Yang Butuh Perhatian.","URL":"http://bppp.kemendag.go.id/media_content/2017/10/Isi_BRIK_FURNITUR.pdf","author":[{"family":"Munadi","given":"Ernawati"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32171,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RN3YTAFL"],"itemData":{"id":32171,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Info Komoditi Furniture","event-place":"Jakarta, Indonesia","page":"7-36","publisher-place":"Jakarta, Indonesia","title":"Produksi Furniture Indonesia","URL":"http://bppp.kemendag.go.id/media_content/2017/10/Isi_BRIK_FURNITUR.pdf","author":[{"family":"Pujiati","given":"Riska"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. From this information and the Central Statistical Agency data, there was an estimated 116,000 wooden-based furniture producers in Indonesia. 
      A survey of furniture producers in Jepara and Pasuruan in 2020 by the Center for International Forestry Research ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2op3gm5q5t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dermawan, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dermawan, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32188,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I8IHEFDM"],"itemData":{"id":32188,"type":"report","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","title":"Wood Legality Verification Systems and Furniture Producers in Indonesia: Lessons from Jepara and Pasuruan.","author":[{"family":"Dermawan","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} estimated that one producer uses about 71 m3 of wood annually. Multiplying this number with the estimated total national producers, the wood consumption by the furniture industry in Indonesia could reach approximately 8.2 million m3 of wood. A high segment of wooden furniture in Indonesia uses teak as the primary raw material. Teak is mainly available in Java and some areas in other islands, such as Sulawesi. With the mean annual increment of 10 m3/ha/year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a46c4tdsml","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kallio, Kanninen, & Krisnawati, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Kallio, Kanninen, & Krisnawati, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32161,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L5MJASRD"],"itemData":{"id":32161,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","DOI":"10.1080/14728028.2012.734127","ISSN":"1472-8028, 2164-3075","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","language":"en","page":"158-175","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Smallholder teak plantations in two villages in Central Java: Silvicultural activity and stand performance","title-short":"Smallholder teak plantations in two villages in Central Java","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/14728028.2012.734127","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Kallio","given":"Maarit H."},{"family":"Kanninen","given":"Markku"},{"family":"Krisnawati","given":"Haruni"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, meeting the need for 8.2 million m3 of wood would require approximately 820,000 hectares of teak forests. 

	In the Amazon, much timber harvest takes place at the scale of individual households, even within communal properties granted to indigenous communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2dqgq7cd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cronkleton & Larson, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Cronkleton & Larson, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32145,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6LPVXP4Y"],"itemData":{"id":32145,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"10.1080/08941920.2015.1014609","ISSN":"0894-1920, 1521-0723","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Society & Natural Resources","language":"en","page":"496-512","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Formalization and Collective Appropriation of Space on Forest Frontiers: Comparing Communal and Individual Property Systems in the Peruvian and Ecuadoran Amazon","title-short":"Formalization and Collective Appropriation of Space on Forest Frontiers","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/08941920.2015.1014609","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Cronkleton","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Larson","given":"Anne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",5,4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The smallholders may harvest the timber themselves with chainsaws and then process the log to produce planks, which are easier to transport, they hire specialized loggers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1b7m8i2bnh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pokorny, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Pokorny, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EN7YYVSN"],"itemData":{"id":31225,"type":"book","event-place":"Place of publication not identified","ISBN":"978-1-138-57317-8","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 993640230","publisher":"ROUTLEDGE","publisher-place":"Place of publication not identified","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Smallholders, forest management and rural development in the amazon.","author":[{"family":"Pokorny","given":"Benno"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Yet, this informal practice is generally penalized by law, except in some countries like Ecuador ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2a3id1tqba","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sears, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Sears, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32170,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CF57NKQS"],"itemData":{"id":32170,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The Social Lives of Forests: Past, Present, and Future of Woodland Resurgence","ISBN":"978-0-226-32268-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.7208/chicago/9780226024134.001.0001","page":"336-347","publisher":"University of Chicago Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"26. From Fallow Timber to Urban Housing: Family Forestry and Tablilla Production in Peru","URL":"http://www.bibliovault.org/BV.landing.epl?ISBN=9780226322681","author":[{"family":"Sears","given":"Robin R"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Padoch","given":"Christine"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some cases, smallholders sell standing timber to professional loggers that have better connection with sawmills, which often approach a larger number of farmers so to compensate for the use of heavy machinery ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a20au3n3uj9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mejia, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Mejia, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32179,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PHVZVQFA"],"itemData":{"id":32179,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Smallholders control an important portion of forestlands in the Ecuadorian Amazon. Timber is not the main source of income but contributes to complement relatively diversified livelihood strategies of smallholders. Timber extraction is stimulated by a vigorous network of intermediaries linked to the end-markets in the cities. Most small-scale operations are undertaken informally since smallholders require complying with still cumbersome procedures in spite of state efforts for simplifying the forestry regulations. Benefits for smallholders from timber extraction depend on how the harvesting operations are organized and whether they involve the participation of smallholders as well as on the species harvested, and whether these operations are conducted with a formal permit or not. This paper assesses the role of timber in smallholders’ livelihoods, the influence of how timber operations are undertaken has on benefits distribution, and the role that urban markets have on shaping the dynamics of timber supply.","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554815814668954","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"38-50","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Smallholders and timber extraction in the Ecuadorian Amazon: amidst market opportunities and regulatory constraints","title-short":"Smallholders and timber extraction in the Ecuadorian Amazon","URL":"http://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/146554815814668954","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Mejia","given":"E."},{"family":"Pacheco","given":"P."},{"family":"Muzo","given":"A."},{"family":"Torres","given":"B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the Peruvian Amazon, commercial harvest of fallow timber is done with a chainsaw portable mill with a circular saw set up on the farm for in situ primary transformation. It is often the case that the rough-hewn planks are planed into the finished product in either lumber yards or workshops in the urban centers.  
      Systems for permitting are quite different and greatly vary in many countries in temperate zones, where land tenure systems tend to be more closely regulated with regards to private vs. public ownership. In cases of private ownership, there is variation in the freedom to decide the amount of timber to harvest, approvals required to harvest and freedom of owners to perform the actual harvesting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2n68qif04n","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nichiforel, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Nichiforel, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32136,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IZWRMI7N"],"itemData":{"id":32136,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land Use Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.landusepol.2018.02.034","ISSN":"02648377","journalAbbreviation":"Land Use Policy","language":"en","page":"535-552","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"How private are Europe’s private forests? A comparative property rights analysis","title-short":"How private are Europe’s private forests?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0264837717305999","volume":"76","author":[{"family":"Nichiforel","given":"Liviu"},{"family":"Keary","given":"Kevin"},{"family":"Deuffic","given":"Philippe"},{"family":"Weiss","given":"Gerhard"},{"family":"Thorsen","given":"Bo Jellesmark"},{"family":"Winkel","given":"Georg"},{"family":"Avdibegovi?","given":"Mersudin"},{"family":"Dobšinská","given":"Zuzana"},{"family":"Feliciano","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Gatto","given":"Paola"},{"family":"Gorriz Mifsud","given":"Elena"},{"family":"Hoogstra-Klein","given":"Marjanke"},{"family":"Hrib","given":"Michal"},{"family":"Hujala","given":"Teppo"},{"family":"Jager","given":"Laszlo"},{"family":"Jarský","given":"Vilém"},{"family":"Jod?owski","given":"Krzysztof"},{"family":"Lawrence","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Lukmine","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Pezdevšek Malovrh","given":"Špela"},{"family":"Nedeljkovi?","given":"Jelena"},{"family":"Noni?","given":"Dragan"},{"family":"Krajter Ostoi?","given":"Silvija"},{"family":"Pukall","given":"Klaus"},{"family":"Rondeux","given":"Jacques"},{"family":"Samara","given":"Theano"},{"family":"Sarvašová","given":"Zuzana"},{"family":"Scriban","given":"Ramona Elena"},{"family":"Šilingien?","given":"Rita"},{"family":"Sinko","given":"Milan"},{"family":"Stojanovska","given":"Makedonka"},{"family":"Stojanovski","given":"Vladimir"},{"family":"Stoyanov","given":"Nickola"},{"family":"Teder","given":"Meelis"},{"family":"Vennesland","given":"Birger"},{"family":"Vilkriste","given":"Lelde"},{"family":"Wilhelmsson","given":"Erik"},{"family":"Wilkes-Allemann","given":"Jerylee"},{"family":"Bouriaud","given":"Laura"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Freedom to decide the amount of timber to harvest can be based on a framework of general silvicultural restrictions (e.g., Norway, Austria, Denmark, Ireland, Latvia, Portugal and Sweden) and size/quantity provided for in the legislation. For example, in France the forest owners maximumly harvest 50% of the standing timber on their property in comparison to Estonia, where one can harvest 20m3 per year and Bulgaria where one can harvest 10m3 per year. These ranges are a result of forest management planning in combination with owners' decisions. In some cases, such as in Finland and Netherlands, more restrictions apply. In other countries owners are generally required to ask for approvals and adhere to the conditions of approval (Bulgaria, Greece, Romania). Approvals may be required when forest management plans do not apply (e.g., France and Czeck Republic) or when there are special circumstances such as exceeding a given size of clear cut. There is little regulation on private forests in Denmark, and in Estonia no formal approval is required for personal use. In the majority of the countries, forest owners have the freedom to cut down trees without any restrictions, others restrict the quantity an individual can harvest by him/herself (for example in Romania where owners can harvest less than 20m3 without a permit). Several countries in Eastern Europe only grant licenses to individuals with harvesting skills, and others such as Greece require the owner to contract a special firm for harvesting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1frbtuoh4j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nichiforel, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Nichiforel, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32136,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IZWRMI7N"],"itemData":{"id":32136,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land Use Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.landusepol.2018.02.034","ISSN":"02648377","journalAbbreviation":"Land Use Policy","language":"en","page":"535-552","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"How private are Europe’s private forests? A comparative property rights analysis","title-short":"How private are Europe’s private forests?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0264837717305999","volume":"76","author":[{"family":"Nichiforel","given":"Liviu"},{"family":"Keary","given":"Kevin"},{"family":"Deuffic","given":"Philippe"},{"family":"Weiss","given":"Gerhard"},{"family":"Thorsen","given":"Bo Jellesmark"},{"family":"Winkel","given":"Georg"},{"family":"Avdibegovi?","given":"Mersudin"},{"family":"Dobšinská","given":"Zuzana"},{"family":"Feliciano","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Gatto","given":"Paola"},{"family":"Gorriz Mifsud","given":"Elena"},{"family":"Hoogstra-Klein","given":"Marjanke"},{"family":"Hrib","given":"Michal"},{"family":"Hujala","given":"Teppo"},{"family":"Jager","given":"Laszlo"},{"family":"Jarský","given":"Vilém"},{"family":"Jod?owski","given":"Krzysztof"},{"family":"Lawrence","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Lukmine","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Pezdevšek Malovrh","given":"Špela"},{"family":"Nedeljkovi?","given":"Jelena"},{"family":"Noni?","given":"Dragan"},{"family":"Krajter Ostoi?","given":"Silvija"},{"family":"Pukall","given":"Klaus"},{"family":"Rondeux","given":"Jacques"},{"family":"Samara","given":"Theano"},{"family":"Sarvašová","given":"Zuzana"},{"family":"Scriban","given":"Ramona Elena"},{"family":"Šilingien?","given":"Rita"},{"family":"Sinko","given":"Milan"},{"family":"Stojanovska","given":"Makedonka"},{"family":"Stojanovski","given":"Vladimir"},{"family":"Stoyanov","given":"Nickola"},{"family":"Teder","given":"Meelis"},{"family":"Vennesland","given":"Birger"},{"family":"Vilkriste","given":"Lelde"},{"family":"Wilhelmsson","given":"Erik"},{"family":"Wilkes-Allemann","given":"Jerylee"},{"family":"Bouriaud","given":"Laura"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Ecological impacts of small-scale logging range from minimal to long-term. In Central Africa, when smallholder logging is driven by demand, chainsaw millers may penetrate deeper into the forest, and apply more effective tools such as portable saws in order to meet with a growing urban demand ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aqq81dd1dg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cerruti, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Cerruti, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31381,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DD732L9U"],"itemData":{"id":31381,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554817822295920","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"50-63","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Social impacts of the Forest Stewardship Council certification in the Congo basin","URL":"https://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/146554817822295920","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Cerruti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"G."},{"family":"Tacconi","given":"L."},{"family":"Eba'a Atyi","given":"R."},{"family":"Essiane","given":"E."},{"family":"Nasi","given":"R."},{"family":"Tabi Eckebil","given":"P.P."},{"family":"Tsanga","given":"R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fallow-forestry allows the use of timber and other non-timber forest resources, while providing multiple contributions to people to regenerate soil fertility and conserve biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a7t88k1ic9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pattanayak & Sills, 2001; Pyh\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}l\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}, Brown, & Neil Adger, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Pattanayak & Sills, 2001; Pyhälä, Brown, & Neil Adger, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31914,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P33WLUGA"],"itemData":{"id":31914,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land Economics","DOI":"10.2307/3146943","ISSN":"0023-7639, 1543-8325","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Land Economics","language":"en","page":"595-612","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Do Tropical Forests Provide Natural Insurance? The Microeconomics of Non-Timber Forest Product Collection in the Brazilian Amazon","title-short":"Do Tropical Forests Provide Natural Insurance?","URL":"http://le.uwpress.org/cgi/doi/10.2307/3146943","volume":"77","author":[{"family":"Pattanayak","given":"Subhrendu K."},{"family":"Sills","given":"Erin O."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001",11]]}}},{"id":33202,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BIMGK2PA"],"itemData":{"id":33202,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1007/s10021-005-0154-y","ISSN":"1432-9840, 1435-0629","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystems","language":"en","page":"1328-1341","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Implications of Livelihood Dependence on Non-Timber Products in Peruvian Amazonia","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10021-005-0154-y","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Pyhälä","given":"Aili"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Katrina"},{"family":"Neil Adger","given":"W."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In such systems, smallholder farmers often conserve scarce timber species, such as Cedrela odorata, Swietenia macrophylla, and Dipteryx spp.), among others ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1gaskms5d6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Putzel, Padoch, & Ricse, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Putzel, Padoch, & Ricse, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32172,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JKNL3STC"],"itemData":{"id":32172,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Small-scale Forestry","DOI":"10.1007/s11842-012-9221-3","ISSN":"1873-7617, 1873-7854","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Small-scale Forestry","language":"en","page":"421-436","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Putting Back the Trees: Smallholder Silvicultural Enrichment of Post-Logged Concession Forest in Peruvian Amazonia","title-short":"Putting Back the Trees","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11842-012-9221-3","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Putzel","given":"Louis"},{"family":"Padoch","given":"Christine"},{"family":"Ricse","given":"Auberto"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
            Due to the individualized living schemes of small-scale farmers in the Amazon, there are not many social impacts of forest management. However, natural and planted forests are frequently affected by accidental fires caused during field preparation, which further reduces the attractiveness of forest investments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1de4d6sr3o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoch, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Hoch, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32185,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9ARZZPRG"],"itemData":{"id":32185,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agroforestry Systems","DOI":"10.1007/s10457-012-9480-1","ISSN":"0167-4366, 1572-9680","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Agroforest Syst","language":"en","page":"361-375","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Financial attractiveness of smallholder tree plantations in the Amazon: bridging external expectations and local realities","title-short":"Financial attractiveness of smallholder tree plantations in the Amazon","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10457-012-9480-1","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Hoch","given":"L."},{"family":"Pokorny","given":"B."},{"family":"Jong","given":"W.","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and may lead to conflicts. Less frequent are wood robbery, and forest tenure conflicts in the remoter, less accessible forest parts of smallholder properties. Although not often discussed, smallholder logging often does not involve women in the operations, which may lead to some unequal distribution of benefits in the households undertaking logging, although women develop other activities in the farm and gardens ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1j59mko3j9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Colfer, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Colfer, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32147,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UDCF3455"],"itemData":{"id":32147,"type":"book","call-number":"SD247 .E58 2005","event-place":"Washington, DC : Bogor, Indonesia","ISBN":"978-1-891853-77-7","number-of-pages":"335","publisher":"Resources for the Future ; Center for International Forestry Research","publisher-place":"Washington, DC : Bogor, Indonesia","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The equitable forest: diversity, community, and resource management","title-short":"The equitable forest","editor":[{"family":"Colfer","given":"Carol J. Pierce"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
            Thousands of households manage forest fallows and trees as part of their customary livelihoods strategy that meets both subsistence and income needs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"alkpv2utje","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pokorny & De Jong, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Pokorny & De Jong, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32138,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2SZQSBAH"],"itemData":{"id":32138,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554815814668981","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"1-19","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Smallholders and forest landscape transitions: locally devised development strategies of the tropical Americas","title-short":"Smallholders and forest landscape transitions","URL":"http://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/146554815814668981","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Pokorny","given":"Benno"},{"family":"De Jong","given":"W."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Smallholder logging is only sustainable when it is done for subsistence or at low intensities. It constitutes a complementary activity that is shrinking over time due to the expansion of agriculture. Even with forest fallows and re-growing secondary forest, tree species composition and tree growth are affected from soil degradation caused by agricultural uses and fire.  

3.3.4.3.2. Community Logging Practice

Community forest management involves the use and management of forests by communities. While community forestry often involves the management of large areas of forest relative to the average size of that managed by individual smallholders, the areas are still small relative to most industrial estates (100s of hectares compared with 1000s of hectares). Furthermore, the focus on multiple use management is strong in both community and smallholder forestry compared with the focus on timber production in industrial estates. 
      Forest areas that are owned or managed by local communities have been increasing in the last decades and account for up to 15% of total forest area worldwide (513 million hectares) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a232dk0at7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Putraditama, Kim, & Baral, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Putraditama, Kim, & Baral, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32201,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D8DP79D8"],"itemData":{"id":32201,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trees, Forests and People","DOI":"10.1016/j.tfp.2021.100062","ISSN":"26667193","journalAbbreviation":"Trees, Forests and People","language":"en","page":"100062","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Where to put community-based forestry?: Reconciling conservation and livelihood in Lampung, Indonesia","title-short":"Where to put community-based forestry?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2666719321000017","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Putraditama","given":"Andika"},{"family":"Kim","given":"Yeon-Su"},{"family":"Baral","given":"Himlal"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Collective forest tenure reforms in countries such as China ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2573hbd1o6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yiwen, Kant, & Long, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Yiwen, Kant, & Long, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31923,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JBK2J73J"],"itemData":{"id":31923,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Devolution is a promising tool to enhance forest management. The literature has discussed many factors that affect the outcomes of forest devolution policies; however, insufficient attention has been paid to the role of exogenous socio-economic changes. Using the longitudinal case study method, we focus on how socio-economic changes affect the effectiveness of forest devolution policies using a case from Southeast China. We find that in this case, although forest devolution succeeded in granting farmers sufficient forest rights, it failed to incentivize farmers to contribute to managing forests because of the dramatic changes in socio-economic contexts. Economic development and outmigration reduced farmers’ dependence on forest income, elevated the costs of silvicultural operations, and posed market risks, thereby reducing farmers’ enthusiasm about managing forests; outmigration also weakened community leadership and impeded the collective action of making forest investments. Eventually, socio-economic changes compromised the positive stimulus caused by forest devolution and contributed to the collective action dilemma of managing forests after the reform. We argue that operationalizing forest devolution in developing countries needs to consider the exogenous socio-economic changes that may enhance or counteract the effects of devolution policies, and that more autonomy should be granted to communities to make policies adaptative to their local socio-economic dynamics.","container-title":"Land","DOI":"10.3390/land9020058","ISSN":"2073-445X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Land","language":"en","page":"58","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Collective Action Dilemma after China’s Forest Tenure Reform: Operationalizing Forest Devolution in a Rapidly Changing Society","title-short":"Collective Action Dilemma after China’s Forest Tenure Reform","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2073-445X/9/2/58","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Yiwen","given":"Zhang"},{"family":"Kant","given":"Shashi"},{"family":"Long","given":"Hexing"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",2,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Indonesia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"edD6c1ov","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Putraditama, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Putraditama, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32201,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D8DP79D8"],"itemData":{"id":32201,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trees, Forests and People","DOI":"10.1016/j.tfp.2021.100062","ISSN":"26667193","journalAbbreviation":"Trees, Forests and People","language":"en","page":"100062","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Where to put community-based forestry?: Reconciling conservation and livelihood in Lampung, Indonesia","title-short":"Where to put community-based forestry?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2666719321000017","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Putraditama","given":"Andika"},{"family":"Kim","given":"Yeon-Su"},{"family":"Baral","given":"Himlal"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, although criticized in terms of effectiveness ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9pflKHgM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yiwen, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Yiwen, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31923,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JBK2J73J"],"itemData":{"id":31923,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Devolution is a promising tool to enhance forest management. The literature has discussed many factors that affect the outcomes of forest devolution policies; however, insufficient attention has been paid to the role of exogenous socio-economic changes. Using the longitudinal case study method, we focus on how socio-economic changes affect the effectiveness of forest devolution policies using a case from Southeast China. We find that in this case, although forest devolution succeeded in granting farmers sufficient forest rights, it failed to incentivize farmers to contribute to managing forests because of the dramatic changes in socio-economic contexts. Economic development and outmigration reduced farmers’ dependence on forest income, elevated the costs of silvicultural operations, and posed market risks, thereby reducing farmers’ enthusiasm about managing forests; outmigration also weakened community leadership and impeded the collective action of making forest investments. Eventually, socio-economic changes compromised the positive stimulus caused by forest devolution and contributed to the collective action dilemma of managing forests after the reform. We argue that operationalizing forest devolution in developing countries needs to consider the exogenous socio-economic changes that may enhance or counteract the effects of devolution policies, and that more autonomy should be granted to communities to make policies adaptative to their local socio-economic dynamics.","container-title":"Land","DOI":"10.3390/land9020058","ISSN":"2073-445X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Land","language":"en","page":"58","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Collective Action Dilemma after China’s Forest Tenure Reform: Operationalizing Forest Devolution in a Rapidly Changing Society","title-short":"Collective Action Dilemma after China’s Forest Tenure Reform","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2073-445X/9/2/58","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Yiwen","given":"Zhang"},{"family":"Kant","given":"Shashi"},{"family":"Long","given":"Hexing"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",2,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, are likely contributing to this upward trend in community forest area. The trend in moving away from industrial forestry towards landholder-based forest management and community forestry may be due to increased support for community forests as a form of sustainable development. 
      From an ecological perspective, indigenous, low-intensity forest use has little negative impact on forest ecosystems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1tkvbkgdol","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}mez-Pompa, Whitmore, & Hadley, 1991)","plainCitation":"(Gómez-Pompa, Whitmore, & Hadley, 1991)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32176,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TSXAEEHE"],"itemData":{"id":32176,"type":"book","collection-number":"Vol. 6","collection-title":"Man and the biosphere series","event-place":"Paris","ISBN":"978-1-85070-261-0","language":"eng","note":"OCLC: 299745773","number-of-pages":"457","publisher":"Unesco","publisher-place":"Paris","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Rain forest regeneration and management","editor":[{"family":"Gómez-Pompa","given":"Arturo"},{"family":"Whitmore","given":"Timothy Charles"},{"family":"Hadley","given":"Malcolm"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The effects of informal, more intensive timber harvest by the community in more forested landscapes in the Amazon and Central Africa, are limited to the easily accessible parts of the forests, where, after a while, the valuable species tend to disappear ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1rm555kgh9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ferreira, Cunha, & Parolin, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Ferreira, Cunha, & Parolin, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32207,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CUKD7KY6"],"itemData":{"id":32207,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Virola surinamensis is a dioecious timber species of Amazonian floodplain forests. It is threatened by extinction and a priority species in the conservation program of genetic resources with high economic value for Brazil. The present study was aimed to assess the population structure of Virola surinamensis and the impact of logging in the estuary region of Brazilian Amazonia. Our data suggest that altering the dioecious population structure by logging likely creates the imbalance of male and female individuals in the population. New policies are needed to protect Virola trees with a special regard on reproductive matriarch trees.","container-title":"Environment Conservation Journal","DOI":"10.36953/ECJ.2014.15301","ISSN":"2278-5124, 0972-3099","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"ECJ","page":"1-8","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effects of logging on Virola surinamensis in an Amazonian floodplain forest","URL":"https://journal.environcj.in/index.php/ecj/article/view/519","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Ferreira","given":"Leandro V."},{"family":"Cunha","given":"Denise A."},{"family":"Parolin","given":"Pia"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The environmental damage becomes stronger with the involvement of professional loggers, as they have the means for investments into infrastructure and heavy machinery. Although timber-harvesting is highly selective, the damage to the forest could be immense as it damages the remaining stand and changes its structure and tree composition in the long run ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2cdu202pso","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Avila, 2017)","plainCitation":"(de Avila, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32165,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T2YQRYIT"],"itemData":{"id":32165,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2016.11.039","ISSN":"03781127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"225-235","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recruitment, growth and recovery of commercial tree species over 30 years following logging and thinning in a tropical rain forest","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112716304935","volume":"385","author":[{"family":"Avila","given":"Angela Luciana","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Schwartz","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Ruschel","given":"Ademir Roberto"},{"family":"Lopes","given":"José do Carmo"},{"family":"Silva","given":"José Natalino Macedo"},{"family":"Carvalho","given":"João Olegário Pereira","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Dormann","given":"Carsten F."},{"family":"Mazzei","given":"Lucas"},{"family":"Soares","given":"Marcio Hofmann Mota"},{"family":"Bauhus","given":"Jürgen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the basic ecological functions of the forest remain as long as it is not converted for agricultural purposes. 
      Independently of the type of ownership or management goals, community forest management has been supported across the globe by governments and donors as a way of combining socio-economic development with forest conservation. Transferring responsibilities from public (e.g., governments) or private (e.g., companies) entities to forest communities is believed to create conditions for better conservation and more sustainable use of forest ecosystems, as well as fostering social well-being and gender equity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a3jmn97hq7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nandigama, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Nandigama, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32204,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6QPDX7XS"],"itemData":{"id":32204,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land Use Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.landusepol.2018.05.061","ISSN":"02648377","journalAbbreviation":"Land Use Policy","language":"en","page":"103445","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Performance of success and failure in grassroots conservation and development interventions: Gender dynamics in participatory forest management in India","title-short":"Performance of success and failure in grassroots conservation and development interventions","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0264837717305136","volume":"97","author":[{"family":"Nandigama","given":"Sailaja"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There has been a high level of support for community forests managed under communal property rights, which suggests participatory engagement in common property resource management promotes environmental sustainability through improved livelihoods for the rural poor ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2l2d8vfs07","properties":{"formattedCitation":"\\\\uldash{(Bluffstone, 2018; Okumu & Muchapondwa, 2020; 2008, 2009)}","plainCitation":"(Bluffstone, 2018; Okumu & Muchapondwa, 2020; 2008, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31922,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4YKSA996"],"itemData":{"id":31922,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2017.07.030","ISSN":"0305750X","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","language":"en","page":"133-141","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Does Collective Action Sequester Carbon? Evidence from the Nepal Community Forestry Program","title-short":"Does Collective Action Sequester Carbon?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0305750X17302590","volume":"101","author":[{"family":"Bluffstone","given":"Randall A."},{"family":"Somanathan","given":"E."},{"family":"Jha","given":"Prakash"},{"family":"Luintel","given":"Harisharan"},{"family":"Bista","given":"Rajesh"},{"family":"Toman","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Paudel","given":"Naya"},{"family":"Adhikari","given":"Bhim"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",1]]}}},{"id":32200,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AW9EMCTK"],"itemData":{"id":32200,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2020.104890","ISSN":"0305750X","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","language":"en","page":"104890","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Welfare and forest cover impacts of incentive based conservation: Evidence from Kenyan community forest associations","title-short":"Welfare and forest cover impacts of incentive based conservation","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0305750X20300164","volume":"129","author":[{"family":"Okumu","given":"Boscow"},{"family":"Muchapondwa","given":"Edwin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",5]]}}},{"id":31915,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I2B9BA86"],"itemData":{"id":31915,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Affairs","DOI":"10.1111/j.1468-0270.2008.00840.x","ISSN":"02650665, 14680270","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"24-31","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"INSTITUTIONS AND THE ENVIRONMENT","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1468-0270.2008.00840.x","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}},"label":"page","suppress-author":true},{"id":36,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2NACCRB4"],"itemData":{"id":36,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1172133","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5939","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"419-422","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A General Framework for Analyzing Sustainability of Social-Ecological Systems","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1172133","volume":"325","author":[{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",7,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and decreases the costs of management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1rf9ctm47q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Guti\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rrez-Zamora & Hern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez Estrada, 2020; Nandigama, 2020; Shumsky, Hickey, Johns, Pelletier, & Galaty, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Gutiérrez-Zamora & Hernández Estrada, 2020; Nandigama, 2020; Shumsky, Hickey, Johns, Pelletier, & Galaty, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32203,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A8D8V7TB"],"itemData":{"id":32203,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2020.102188","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"102188","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Responsibilization and state territorialization: Governing socio-territorial conflicts in community forestry in Mexico","title-short":"Responsibilization and state territorialization","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934119302011","volume":"116","author":[{"family":"Gutiérrez-Zamora","given":"Violeta"},{"family":"Hernández Estrada","given":"Mara"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32204,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6QPDX7XS"],"itemData":{"id":32204,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land Use Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.landusepol.2018.05.061","ISSN":"02648377","journalAbbreviation":"Land Use Policy","language":"en","page":"103445","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Performance of success and failure in grassroots conservation and development interventions: Gender dynamics in participatory forest management in India","title-short":"Performance of success and failure in grassroots conservation and development interventions","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0264837717305136","volume":"97","author":[{"family":"Nandigama","given":"Sailaja"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32199,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FZBUVDRQ"],"itemData":{"id":32199,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land Use Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.landusepol.2013.10.014","ISSN":"02648377","journalAbbreviation":"Land Use Policy","language":"en","page":"48-69","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Institutional factors affecting wild edible plant (WEP) harvest and consumption in semi-arid Kenya","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0264837713002093","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Shumsky","given":"Stephanie"},{"family":"Hickey","given":"Gordon M."},{"family":"Johns","given":"Timothy"},{"family":"Pelletier","given":"Bernard"},{"family":"Galaty","given":"John"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",5]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Community forests also increase local resilience and enable better disaster preparedness for emergencies ranging from earthquakes to the COVID-19 pandemic ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a22a2pp6a27","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gentle, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Gentle, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"ERpTB65M/1Rrx5zWh","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GAKVT37W"],"itemData":{"id":16760,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Research in Globalization","DOI":"10.1016/j.resglo.2020.100025","ISSN":"2590051X","journalAbbreviation":"Research in Globalization","language":"en","page":"100025","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effectiveness of community forest user groups (CFUGs) in responding to the 2015 earthquakes and COVID-19 in Nepal","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Gentle","given":"Popular"},{"family":"Maraseni","given":"Tek N."},{"family":"Paudel","given":"Dinesh"},{"family":"Dahal","given":"Ganga R."},{"family":"Kanel","given":"Tara"},{"family":"Pathak","given":"Bharati"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Communities can have full, partial, or no formal ownership of the forests they manage. In the cases that communities hold ownership of the forest land, they often share forest management responsibilities, including its costs and benefits, with governments, non-governmental organizations, etc., via different legal arrangements ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"asnu2h6orh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hyde, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Hyde, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32131,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SWJLGP9Z"],"itemData":{"id":32131,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Devolution in forestry generally refers to the transfer of some degree of central government authority to local responsibility. It commonly takes the form of recommendations by external advisors in developing country situations who encourage formally organized decision making by a local political community. This paper reviews the literature on local tenure, the summaries of case studies in forestry, and the broader history of experience with collectives and cooperatives with the objective of identifying the characteristic cases where some form of collective or community forest management might be successful. The paper identifies four cases. It concludes that, at some level, smaller groups of managers may cooperate in labor and capital intensive activities like fire control or harvesting, or in specialized activities beyond their personal expertise, activities like processing and marketing their products. Successful and sustainable community-wide collective enterprises seldom include agricultural or forest land—with the possible, and likely impermanent, exception of low-valued open access land beyond the perimeter of agricultural crops and managed forests.","container-title":"New Frontiers of Forest Economics: Forest Economics beyond the Perfectly Competitive Commodity Markets","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2016.06.018","ISSN":"1389-9341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","page":"85-91","title":"Whereabouts devolution and collective forest management?","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S138993411630137X","volume":"72","author":[{"family":"Hyde","given":"William F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The mechanisms that transfer rights to use and management of forests from public or private land to communities greatly vary across the world. Legal arrangements range from the forestry regime of “baldios” in Portugal or the “montes comunales en mano commum” in Galiza/Spain ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1c815a74kv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carvalho Ribeiro, S\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}nia Maria, 1998; Skulska, Duarte, Rego, & Montiel-Molina, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Carvalho Ribeiro, Sónia Maria, 1998; Skulska, Duarte, Rego, & Montiel-Molina, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32209,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FBKKMS5G"],"itemData":{"id":32209,"type":"book","publisher":"UTAD, Universidade de Trás os Montes e Alto Douro, licenciatura em Eng Florestal","title":"A participação dos compartes na gestão do baldio: Estudo de caso no Baldio da Ermida, concelho de Terras de Bouro, Parque Nacional da Peneda Gerês, Portugal","author":[{"literal":"Carvalho Ribeiro, Sónia Maria"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":32198,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FJEXQNHA"],"itemData":{"id":32198,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Small-scale Forestry","DOI":"10.1007/s11842-020-09445-6","ISSN":"1873-7617, 1873-7854","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Small-scale Forestry","language":"en","page":"231-251","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Relationships Between Wildfires, Management Modalities of Community Areas, and Ownership Types in Pine Forests of Mainland Portugal","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11842-020-09445-6","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Skulska","given":"I."},{"family":"Duarte","given":"I."},{"family":"Rego","given":"F. C."},{"family":"Montiel-Molina","given":"C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and the “van panchayats” in the Himalayas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ah9sb23bnb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Thakur, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Thakur, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32210,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XP73DJVQ"],"itemData":{"id":32210,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2019.117674","ISSN":"03781127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"117674","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Indicator based integrated vulnerability assessment of community forests in Indian west Himalaya","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112719309417","volume":"457","author":[{"family":"Thakur","given":"Shinny"},{"family":"Negi","given":"Vikram S."},{"family":"Pathak","given":"Ravi"},{"family":"Dhyani","given":"Rupesh"},{"family":"Durgapal","given":"Kamini"},{"family":"Rawal","given":"Ranbeer S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Mediterranean European countries, the existence of common property institutions and community forests in particular dates to at least a thousand years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2cf3mv14gd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cullotta, 2015; Skulska, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Cullotta, 2015; Skulska, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32208,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LM829FNT"],"itemData":{"id":32208,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Planning and Management","DOI":"10.1080/09640568.2014.927754","ISSN":"0964-0568, 1360-0559","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Environmental Planning and Management","language":"en","page":"1384-1411","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forest planning across Europe: the spatial scale, tools, andinter-sectoral integration in land-use planning","title-short":"Forest planning across Europe","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/09640568.2014.927754","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Cullotta","given":"Sebastiano"},{"family":"Bon?ina","given":"Andrej"},{"family":"Carvalho-Ribeiro","given":"Sonia M."},{"family":"Chauvin","given":"Christophe"},{"family":"Farcy","given":"Christine"},{"family":"Kurttila","given":"Mikko"},{"family":"Maetzke","given":"Federico G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",8,3]]}}},{"id":32198,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FJEXQNHA"],"itemData":{"id":32198,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Small-scale Forestry","DOI":"10.1007/s11842-020-09445-6","ISSN":"1873-7617, 1873-7854","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Small-scale Forestry","language":"en","page":"231-251","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Relationships Between Wildfires, Management Modalities of Community Areas, and Ownership Types in Pine Forests of Mainland Portugal","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11842-020-09445-6","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Skulska","given":"I."},{"family":"Duarte","given":"I."},{"family":"Rego","given":"F. C."},{"family":"Montiel-Molina","given":"C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Community forest management is also associated with use of forests in indigenous reserves or designated sustainable use areas including for example the sustainable use extractive reserves (called RESEXs in Brazil), some of which were created over the last decades, granting conditional local use rights on state lands for vast areas. In South America, often communities also manage land through forest concessions. Forest concessions are defined as a formal legal agreement signed with a concessionaire for the occupation and use of a territory. In these agreements, space units are demarcated for the use and management of ecosystems for specific uses and for a fixed time. There are at least 122 million hectares of tropical forests under concessions, equivalent to 14% of the world's public forests some of which are managed by forest communities. 
	The industry is operated by small and medium forest enterprises which are largely left out of the forest statistics, planning and management, and yet it is growing rapidly in many tropical countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a18t350jnk9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoare, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Hoare, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31968,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LVJSVYIQ"],"itemData":{"id":31968,"type":"book","event-place":"London: Chatham House","publisher-place":"London: Chatham House","title":"Tackling illegal logging and the related trade. What progress and where next.","author":[{"family":"Hoare","given":"Alison"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The small and medium forest enterprises are characterized by low level capital, informally trained workers and having potential for value addition ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a21jtufs4lm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Osei-Tutu, Nketiah, Kyereh, Owusu-Ansah, & Faniyan, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Osei-Tutu, Nketiah, Kyereh, Owusu-Ansah, & Faniyan, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31289,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FBGBXV3E"],"itemData":{"id":31289,"type":"book","collection-number":"27","collection-title":"IIED small and medium forest enterprise series","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-84369-454-0","language":"eng","note":"OCLC: 837767006","number-of-pages":"72","publisher":"International Institute for Environment and Development","publisher-place":"London","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Hidden forestry revealed: characteristics, constraints and opportunities for small and medium forest enterprises in Ghana","title-short":"Hidden forestry revealed","author":[{"family":"Osei-Tutu","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Nketiah","given":"K"},{"family":"Kyereh","given":"B"},{"family":"Owusu-Ansah","given":"M"},{"family":"Faniyan","given":"J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The industry contributes directly to the local economy in the form of improved livelihoods and cheap lumber for urban consumers. Small and medium forest enterprises are the main, additional or alternative income sources for a greater proportion of the local population as compared to the large-scale formal forest subsector in countries where the forestry sector is among the major income earner ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2p4vnf1aj7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cerutti & Lescuyer, 2011; Osei-Tutu, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Cerutti & Lescuyer, 2011; Osei-Tutu, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FNHXP5NB"],"itemData":{"id":31288,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia.","ISBN":"978-602-8693-44-8","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 786191932","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia.","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"The domestic market for small-scale chainsaw milling in Cameroon: present situation, opportunities and challenges","title-short":"The domestic market for small-scale chainsaw milling in Cameroon","author":[{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31289,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FBGBXV3E"],"itemData":{"id":31289,"type":"book","collection-number":"27","collection-title":"IIED small and medium forest enterprise series","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-84369-454-0","language":"eng","note":"OCLC: 837767006","number-of-pages":"72","publisher":"International Institute for Environment and Development","publisher-place":"London","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Hidden forestry revealed: characteristics, constraints and opportunities for small and medium forest enterprises in Ghana","title-short":"Hidden forestry revealed","author":[{"family":"Osei-Tutu","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Nketiah","given":"K"},{"family":"Kyereh","given":"B"},{"family":"Owusu-Ansah","given":"M"},{"family":"Faniyan","given":"J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is because small and medium forest enterprises tend to accrue wealth locally, empower local entrepreneurship and seek local approval to operate ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UFa9bXi4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Osei-Tutu, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Osei-Tutu, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31289,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FBGBXV3E"],"itemData":{"id":31289,"type":"book","collection-number":"27","collection-title":"IIED small and medium forest enterprise series","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-84369-454-0","language":"eng","note":"OCLC: 837767006","number-of-pages":"72","publisher":"International Institute for Environment and Development","publisher-place":"London","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Hidden forestry revealed: characteristics, constraints and opportunities for small and medium forest enterprises in Ghana","title-short":"Hidden forestry revealed","author":[{"family":"Osei-Tutu","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Nketiah","given":"K"},{"family":"Kyereh","given":"B"},{"family":"Owusu-Ansah","given":"M"},{"family":"Faniyan","given":"J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Small-scale enterprises tend not to be adapted to landlocked, low population density, remote markets and high transportation, costs but can compete and replace forest concessions when public road infrastructures allow them easier access to the market ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"abtdpeu84q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Karsenty, Drigo, Piketty, & Singer, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Karsenty, Drigo, Piketty, & Singer, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31287,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T28AZ5ZC"],"itemData":{"id":31287,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2008.07.001","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"1498-1508","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Regulating industrial forest concessions in Central Africa and South America","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112708005471","volume":"256","author":[{"family":"Karsenty","given":"Alain"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Isabel Garcia"},{"family":"Piketty","given":"Marie-Gabrielle"},{"family":"Singer","given":"Benjamin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Box 3.15). 
      Globally, about 15% of tropical forests are managed by communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"avl4h7efc2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arts & de Koning, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Arts & de Koning, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32202,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8SDYBCTS"],"itemData":{"id":32202,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2017.03.014","ISSN":"0305750X","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","language":"en","page":"315-325","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community Forest Management: An Assessment and Explanation of its Performance Through QCA","title-short":"Community Forest Management","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0305750X17300761","volume":"96","author":[{"family":"Arts","given":"Bas"},{"family":"Koning","given":"Jessica","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, many managed by indigenous peoples and local communities. As of 2020, indigenous peoples and local communities in Africa, South America and Asia, customarily managed at least 31% of land area corresponding to 571 M hectares ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a29d1m8h909","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Khare, White, & Frechette, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Khare, White, & Frechette, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":481,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3NQXMSWZ"],"itemData":{"id":481,"type":"report","genre":"Technical Report","page":"32","publisher":"Rights and Resources Initiative (RRI)","title":"Estimate of the area of land and territories of Indigenous Peoples, local communities, and Afro- descendants where their rights have not been recognized","URL":"https://rightsandresources.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/Area-Study-Final-1.pdf","author":[{"family":"Khare","given":"Arvind"},{"family":"White","given":"Andy"},{"family":"Frechette","given":"Alain"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As of 2016, in Latin America nearly 33% of forests (232 million ha) were under some type of collective tenure regime owned by communities, most of which are of indigenous peoples, and another 8% of the area had been designated for their use. An important portion of these forests are used for meeting subsistence needs, but few of the communities undertake commercial logging operations, formally or informally. Traditional forest management for subsistence uses tends to be informed by traditional knowledge and customary local regulations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"afcksganvu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. C. Gibson, McKean, & Ostrom, 2000)","plainCitation":"(C. C. Gibson, McKean, & Ostrom, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32195,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NGBHFTKJ"],"itemData":{"id":32195,"type":"book","call-number":"SD387.S55 P46 2000","collection-title":"Politics, science, and the environment","event-place":"Cambridge, Mass","ISBN":"978-0-262-07201-4","number-of-pages":"274","publisher":"MIT Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge, Mass","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"People and forests: communities, institutions, and governance","title-short":"People and forests","editor":[{"family":"Gibson","given":"Clark C."},{"family":"McKean","given":"Margaret A."},{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In turn, community forestry for commercial purposes is informed by management plans that are based on scientific forestry with no obvious role for indigenous knowledge. Often, these plans are inspired by large-scale industrial timber-harvesting schemes. 
	Since informal management schemes are considered by some to be ineffective or degrading, the management of forests by communities on the basis of the Reduced-Impact-Logging principles and formally authorized management plans has been widely promoted given assumptions that it would lead to sustainable outcomes in terms of biodiversity and local income. Accordingly, hundreds of initiatives across the tropics have promoted community forestry, in some cases also labelled as social forestry or collaborative forest management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ai27jg09kq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hajjar, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Hajjar, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32186,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BMNHNQM7"],"itemData":{"id":32186,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Sustainability","DOI":"10.1038/s41893-020-00633-y","ISSN":"2398-9629","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Sustain","language":"en","page":"216-224","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A global analysis of the social and environmental outcomes of community forests","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41893-020-00633-y","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Hajjar","given":"Reem"},{"family":"Oldekop","given":"Johan A."},{"family":"Cronkleton","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Newton","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Russell","given":"Aaron J. M."},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Wen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	The most developed cases of community forest management in Mesoamerica include Quintana Roo, Mexico, and Peten, Guatemala (see Supplementary material Box S3.1), as well as community forestry in the Amazon including Brazil, Bolivia, Peru. In Central Africa, community forestry has mainly developed in Cameroon, and to a lesser extent in Democratic Republic of the Congo. Especially in Latin America, the promotion of community forestry was accompanied by the formal recognition of tenure rights to indigenous peoples ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"at1fb43klu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(RRI, 2015)","plainCitation":"(RRI, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32168,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E7ASD3NX"],"itemData":{"id":32168,"type":"report","event-place":"Washington DC","publisher":"Rights and Resources Initiative","publisher-place":"Washington DC","title":"Who Owns the World’s Land? A Global Baseline of Formally Recognized Indigenous and Community Land Rights.","URL":"https://rightsandresources.org/wp-content/uploads/GlobalBaseline_web.pdf","author":[{"family":"RRI","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which has been understood as a critical condition for achieving positive outcomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"avtvrpkulq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Baynes, Herbohn, Smith, Fisher, & Bray, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Baynes, Herbohn, Smith, Fisher, & Bray, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32164,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GP24JR3V"],"itemData":{"id":32164,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2015.09.011","ISSN":"09593780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"226-238","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Key factors which influence the success of community forestry in developing countries","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0959378015300479","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Baynes","given":"Jack"},{"family":"Herbohn","given":"John"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Bray","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	In developed countries, community forestry is less well established than in developing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a8i9g43jl1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bullock & Hanna, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Bullock & Hanna, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32156,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F6APEW6X"],"itemData":{"id":32156,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"10.1080/08941920701561007","ISSN":"0894-1920, 1521-0723","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Society & Natural Resources","language":"en","page":"77-85","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community Forestry: Mitigating or Creating Conflict in British Columbia?","title-short":"Community Forestry","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/08941920701561007","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Bullock","given":"Ryan"},{"family":"Hanna","given":"Kevin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",12,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Charnley and Poe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a17p84emsu3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2007)","plainCitation":"(2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32139,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H4DN6XI5"],"itemData":{"id":32139,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Community forestry refers to forest management that has ecological sustainability and local community benefits as central goals, with some degree of responsibility and authority for forest management formally vested in the community This review provides an overview of where the field of community forestry is today. We describe four case examples from the Americas: Canada, the United States, Mexico, and Bolivia. We also identify five hypotheses embedded in the concept of community forestry and examine the evidence supporting them. We conclude that community forestry holds promise as a viable approach to forest conservation and community development. Major gaps remain, however, between community forestry in theory and in practice. For example, devolution of forest management authority from states to communities has been partial and disappointing, and local control over forest management appears to have more ecological than socioeconomic benefits. We suggest ways that anthropologists can contribute to the field.","collection-title":"Annual Review of Anthropology","container-title":"Annual Review of Anthropology","DOI":"10.1146/annurev.anthro.35.081705.123143","ISSN":"0084-6570","page":"301–336","title":"Community forestry in theory and practice: Where are we now?","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Charnley","given":"Susan"},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} reported only 2% community and indigenous ownership of forests in developed countries in comparison with the approximately 14% of community and indigenous owned forests in developing countries. Community forestry began to be implemented in the 1990s in Canada as a result of public controversies surrounding large-scale industrial forestry, and as of 2007 existed in Ontario, Quebec, and British Columbia (Box 3.17). In the Canadian context, forests remain state property but communities receive key management rights and responsibilities. In the United States of America community forestry initiatives have been supported through joint efforts across private, tribal, and public lands across the country. Despite widespread support for increased public participation in environmental decision-making in the United States of America, there has been resistance from the government and environmental groups to yielding actual control over land to local communities. Thus, in the United States of America collaborations between state and federal forest management agencies and local communities has been more common ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"am326i4mf9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Charnley & Poe, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Charnley & Poe, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32139,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H4DN6XI5"],"itemData":{"id":32139,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Community forestry refers to forest management that has ecological sustainability and local community benefits as central goals, with some degree of responsibility and authority for forest management formally vested in the community This review provides an overview of where the field of community forestry is today. We describe four case examples from the Americas: Canada, the United States, Mexico, and Bolivia. We also identify five hypotheses embedded in the concept of community forestry and examine the evidence supporting them. We conclude that community forestry holds promise as a viable approach to forest conservation and community development. Major gaps remain, however, between community forestry in theory and in practice. For example, devolution of forest management authority from states to communities has been partial and disappointing, and local control over forest management appears to have more ecological than socioeconomic benefits. We suggest ways that anthropologists can contribute to the field.","collection-title":"Annual Review of Anthropology","container-title":"Annual Review of Anthropology","DOI":"10.1146/annurev.anthro.35.081705.123143","ISSN":"0084-6570","page":"301–336","title":"Community forestry in theory and practice: Where are we now?","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Charnley","given":"Susan"},{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Box 3.17 Community forestry on public lands in Canada

Community forestry has been a legally recognized form of forestry governance in Canada for over 50 years. While area in community forestry is small compared with industrial tenures, it makes important contributions to community development and diversifying the beneficiaries of forestry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a96ai06am1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bullock & Hanna, 2007; McIlveen & Rhodes, 2016; Teitelbaum, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Bullock & Hanna, 2007; McIlveen & Rhodes, 2016; Teitelbaum, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32156,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F6APEW6X"],"itemData":{"id":32156,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"10.1080/08941920701561007","ISSN":"0894-1920, 1521-0723","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Society & Natural Resources","language":"en","page":"77-85","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community Forestry: Mitigating or Creating Conflict in British Columbia?","title-short":"Community Forestry","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/08941920701561007","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Bullock","given":"Ryan"},{"family":"Hanna","given":"Kevin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",12,3]]}}},{"id":32151,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UH86NJ92"],"itemData":{"id":32151,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Community forestry: Lessons from policy and practice","event-place":"Vancouver, BC","page":"179-209","publisher":"UBC Press","publisher-place":"Vancouver, BC","title":"Community forestry in an age of crisis: Structural change, the mountain pine beetle, and the evolution of the Burns Lake community forest","author":[{"family":"McIlveen","given":"K"},{"family":"Rhodes","given":"M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32149,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EZ6ED8FR"],"itemData":{"id":32149,"type":"book","call-number":"SD567 .C66 2016","event-place":"Vancouver ; Toronto","ISBN":"978-0-7748-3188-8","note":"OCLC: ocn933273555","number-of-pages":"405","publisher":"UBC Press","publisher-place":"Vancouver ; Toronto","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Community forestry in Canada: lessons from policy and practice","title-short":"Community forestry in Canada","editor":[{"family":"Teitelbaum","given":"Sara"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
 	Three provinces in Canada have institutionalized forms of community forestry on public land: British Columbia, Ontario, and Quebec. Since 1998, British Columbia has granted 25-year renewable licenses to more than 50 organizations and indigenous communities under the British Columbia Community Forest Agreement ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1flp5i95fb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Government of British Columbia, 2020a)","plainCitation":"(Government of British Columbia, 2020a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32153,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/694ZVNCT"],"itemData":{"id":32153,"type":"report","publisher":"Government of British Columbia","title":"Community Forest Agreements. Issued and Invited Community Forests (as of April 24, 2020)","URL":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/farming-natural-resources-and-industry/forestry/timber-tenures/community-forest-agreements/issued_cfa_status_report_april-24-2020.pdf","author":[{"family":"Government of British Columbia","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. British Columbia also has a tenure specific to indigenous communities, the First Nations Woodland Licence ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a5lj3bjn48","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Government of British Columbia, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(Government of British Columbia, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32152,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CSHLS5WV"],"itemData":{"id":32152,"type":"report","publisher":"Government of British Columbia","title":"Community Forest Agreements. Issued First Nations Woodland Licences (as of April 24, 2020)","URL":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/farming-natural-resources-and-industry/forestry/timber-tenures/first-nations-woodland-licence/issued_fnwl_april_24_2020.pdf","author":[{"family":"Government of British Columbia","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
 	Quebec was the second province to adopt a community forestry policy. Although implementation has been slow ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2p1bphvet9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Minist\\\\uc0\\\\u232{}re des Ressources naturelles et de la Faune, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Ministère des Ressources naturelles et de la Faune, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32150,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NKILV5AY"],"itemData":{"id":32150,"type":"report","event-place":"Quebec","publisher-place":"Quebec","title":"Proposals for the selection , establishment and operation of local forests. Consultation paper","URL":"https://mffp.gouv.qc.ca/forets/gestion/pdf/document-consultation-proximite-ang.pdf","author":[{"family":"Ministère des Ressources naturelles et de la Faune","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, a number of community forests have been created across the province ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15h08tvsmr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bissonnette, Blouin, Bouthillier, & Teitelbaum, 2020; Teitelbaum, Beckley, & Nadeau, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Bissonnette, Blouin, Bouthillier, & Teitelbaum, 2020; Teitelbaum, Beckley, & Nadeau, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32158,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8TV8LMNA"],"itemData":{"id":32158,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Revue Gouvernance","DOI":"10.7202/1073111ar","ISSN":"1912-0362","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Revue Gouvernance","language":"fr","page":"52","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Vers des forêts de proximité en terres publiques ? Le « bricolage » institutionnel comme vecteur d’innovation en foresterie communautaire au Québec, Canada","title-short":"Vers des forêts de proximité en terres publiques ?","URL":"http://id.erudit.org/iderudit/1073111ar","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Bissonnette","given":"Jean-François"},{"family":"Blouin","given":"Denis"},{"family":"Bouthillier","given":"Luc"},{"family":"Teitelbaum","given":"Sara"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32142,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PLW2IB4N"],"itemData":{"id":32142,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Despite the interest community forestry generates, there is little published literature on the array of initiatives currently taking place across Canada. This paper presents the results of nationwide survey of community forestry initiatives on public land. The survey focused on basic characteristics such as objectives, organizational structures, and tenure types. The research revealed that there are over one hundred community forest initiatives currently taking place on public land, mainly in Ontario, Quebec, and British Columbia. Most of them are run through local government organizations. Approximately 60% operate on Crown land while the remaining 40% operate on land owned fee simple by local governments. The median landbase is 4200 ha. The average age of community forests is 10 years.","container-title":"The Forestry Chronicle","DOI":"10.5558/tfc82416-3","ISSN":"0015-7546, 1499-9315","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"The Forestry Chronicle","language":"en","page":"416-428","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A national portrait of community forestry on public land in Canada","URL":"http://pubs.cif-ifc.org/doi/10.5558/tfc82416-3","volume":"82","author":[{"family":"Teitelbaum","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Beckley","given":"Tom"},{"family":"Nadeau","given":"Solange"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",5,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many are located in proximity to small rural communities and are run by municipal or regional governments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a10svmm4bpj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chiasson & Leclerc, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Chiasson & Leclerc, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32155,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GZEYGZVS"],"itemData":{"id":32155,"type":"book","call-number":"SD568.Q4 G68 2013","collection-number":"13","collection-title":"Science régionale","event-place":"Québec (Québec)","ISBN":"978-2-7605-3616-6","number-of-pages":"252","publisher":"Presses de l'Université du Québec","publisher-place":"Québec (Québec)","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"La gouvernance locale des forêts publiques québécoises: une avenue de développement des régions périphériques?","title-short":"La gouvernance locale des forêts publiques québécoises","editor":[{"family":"Chiasson","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Leclerc","given":"Édith"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A handful have also been allocated to indigenous communities and are largely run by the band council.  
 	The province of Ontario has a network of county and municipal forests, as well as forests owned and managed by Conservation Authorities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"asooukotkn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Teitelbaum & Bullock, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Teitelbaum & Bullock, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32157,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8QM7YB92"],"itemData":{"id":32157,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ontario’s County, Municipal and Conservation Authority forests have received little attention within the academic literature on community forestry in Canada. These “Agreement Forests”, as they were once called, are a product of the early 20th century and have been under local government management since the 1990s. Most are situated in Southern Ontario. In this article we investigate the extent to which community forestry principles are at work in these forests. Three principles—participatory governance, local benefits and multiple forest use—are analyzed using a composite score approach derived from survey data collected from nearly all of these forest organizations (response rate = 80%). Results indicate that most of these organizations do display attributes associated with community forestry principles, including a local governance process, public participation activities, local employment and multiple-use management. Traditional forestry employment is less strong than in similar studies of Crown land community forests; however, there is an important emphasis on non-timber activities. The article concludes that the County, Municipal and Conservation Authority forests represents a unique approach, which reflects the specific geographic and socio-economic context in which it resides.","container-title":"The Forestry Chronicle","DOI":"10.5558/tfc2012-136","ISSN":"0015-7546, 1499-9315","issue":"06","journalAbbreviation":"The Forestry Chronicle","language":"en","page":"697-707","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Are community forestry principles at work in Ontario’s County, Municipal, and Conservation Authority forests?","URL":"http://pubs.cif-ifc.org/doi/10.5558/tfc2012-136","volume":"88","author":[{"family":"Teitelbaum","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Bullock","given":"Ryan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Under this model, forestlands are owned outright by local government entities and have strong authority over management decisions. In contrast, the provinces of Quebec and British Columbia retain considerable control over management decisions such as allowable timber cut, wild species management and gathering. Community forestry entities in Quebec and Ontario also face substantial administrative burdens from a regulatory system designed for much larger operations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1as2djalef","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ambus & Hoberg, 2011; R.L Trosper & Tindall, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Ambus & Hoberg, 2011; R.L Trosper & Tindall, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32159,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IHMBCYVK"],"itemData":{"id":32159,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"10.1080/08941920.2010.520078","ISSN":"0894-1920, 1521-0723","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Society & Natural Resources","language":"en","page":"933-950","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Evolution of Devolution: A Critical Analysis of the Community Forest Agreement in British Columbia","title-short":"The Evolution of Devolution","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/08941920.2010.520078","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Ambus","given":"Lisa"},{"family":"Hoberg","given":"George"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",9]]}}},{"id":32141,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5P2TCDEL"],"itemData":{"id":32141,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Aboriginal Peoples and forest lands in Canada","event-place":"Vancouver, BC","page":"313-325","publisher":"UBC Press","publisher-place":"Vancouver, BC","title":"Consultation and accommodation : Making losses visible.","author":[{"family":"Trosper","given":"R.L"},{"family":"Tindall","given":"D.B"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
 	Timber harvesting is a main objective for many community forests and, in at least one case, generates significant employment in its region of British Columbia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2507l7qgl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McIlveen & Rhodes, 2016)","plainCitation":"(McIlveen & Rhodes, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32151,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UH86NJ92"],"itemData":{"id":32151,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Community forestry: Lessons from policy and practice","event-place":"Vancouver, BC","page":"179-209","publisher":"UBC Press","publisher-place":"Vancouver, BC","title":"Community forestry in an age of crisis: Structural change, the mountain pine beetle, and the evolution of the Burns Lake community forest","author":[{"family":"McIlveen","given":"K"},{"family":"Rhodes","given":"M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, there is considerable diversity in management values and priorities, with some strongly focused on protection of ecological functions and nature’s contributions to people. Some community forests have diversified their activities through development of recreation and/or alternative forest products. For example, one community forestry initiative in Quebec developed an innovative approach combining timber and wild blueberry production. Revenues have been sufficient to support research on optimal conditions for co-habitation of trees and blueberries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aeo6r2iiha","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Fournier, 2013)","plainCitation":"(J. Fournier, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32154,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VG397GHV"],"itemData":{"id":32154,"type":"thesis","genre":"Master’s thesis","publisher":"Université du Québec à Montréal","title":"Facteurs de succès et de contraintes à la foresterie communautaire : Étude de cas et évaluation de deux initiatives","URL":"https://core.ac.uk/download/pdf/18491099.pdf","author":[{"family":"Fournier","given":"JASON"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.   

	The specific outcomes of community forestry initiatives largely depend on the biophysical conditions, tenure right situation, community characteristics, and the type of intervention. For the majority of cases, positive environmental and income-related outcomes are reported, but the need for formalization and the related bureaucratic and technical requirements negatively affect forest access and resource rights ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Q2OL8MRk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hajjar, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Hajjar, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32186,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BMNHNQM7"],"itemData":{"id":32186,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Sustainability","DOI":"10.1038/s41893-020-00633-y","ISSN":"2398-9629","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Sustain","language":"en","page":"216-224","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A global analysis of the social and environmental outcomes of community forests","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41893-020-00633-y","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Hajjar","given":"Reem"},{"family":"Oldekop","given":"Johan A."},{"family":"Cronkleton","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Newton","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Russell","given":"Aaron J. M."},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Wen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and the attractiveness for the local resource users ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a20qc4jo8ks","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pokorny, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Pokorny, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EN7YYVSN"],"itemData":{"id":31225,"type":"book","event-place":"Place of publication not identified","ISBN":"978-1-138-57317-8","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 993640230","publisher":"ROUTLEDGE","publisher-place":"Place of publication not identified","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Smallholders, forest management and rural development in the amazon.","author":[{"family":"Pokorny","given":"Benno"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Accordingly, the long-term success of community forestry initiatives largely relies on continuous external support, but only in some limited cases ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"af3g2c2d81","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pokorny, Johnson, Medina, & Hoch, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Pokorny, Johnson, Medina, & Hoch, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32193,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HE3B7KCW"],"itemData":{"id":32193,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geoforum","DOI":"10.1016/j.geoforum.2010.08.002","ISSN":"00167185","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Geoforum","language":"en","page":"387-401","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Market-based conservation of the Amazonian forests: Revisiting win–win expectations","title-short":"Market-based conservation of the Amazonian forests","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0016718510000928","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Pokorny","given":"Benno"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"James"},{"family":"Medina","given":"Gabriel"},{"family":"Hoch","given":"Lisa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
	Traditional forest management for subsistence uses tends to be informed by traditional knowledge and customary local regulations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hQz8eIqk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. C. Gibson, 2000)","plainCitation":"(C. C. Gibson, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32195,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NGBHFTKJ"],"itemData":{"id":32195,"type":"book","call-number":"SD387.S55 P46 2000","collection-title":"Politics, science, and the environment","event-place":"Cambridge, Mass","ISBN":"978-0-262-07201-4","number-of-pages":"274","publisher":"MIT Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge, Mass","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"People and forests: communities, institutions, and governance","title-short":"People and forests","editor":[{"family":"Gibson","given":"Clark C."},{"family":"McKean","given":"Margaret A."},{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In turn, community forestry for commercial purposes is informed by management plans that are based on scientific forestry with no obvious role for indigenous knowledge. Often, these plans are inspired by large-scale industrial timber-harvesting schemes.
      In the Amazon, there are four general schemes of community timber harvesting: (1) traditional harvesting of forest products aimed to meet subsistence needs; (2) locally devised schemes to carry out commercial timber harvesting; (3) harvesting agreements between communities and loggers; and (4) formal community forestry on the basis of legally authorized management plans as described above ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ajlfb0hoee","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sabogal, de Jong, Pokorny, & Louman, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Sabogal, de Jong, Pokorny, & Louman, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32169,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PDCD382P"],"itemData":{"id":32169,"type":"book","abstract":"Antecedentes, realidad y oportunidades del manejo forestal comunitario en América Latina; Capacidades técnicas y desafios del manejo forestal comunitario; Capacidades empresariales para el desarrollo de empresas forestales comunitarias; Capacidades organizativas para el manejo forestal comunitario frente a las demandas y expectativas oficiales; Políticas públicas que afectam el manejo forestal comunitario; Estrategias de acompanãmiento al manejo forestal comunitario; Síntesis y recomendaciones.","event-place":"Belém, PA","ISBN":"978-979-1412-73-5","language":"Spanish","note":"OCLC: 709201134","publisher":"CIFOR: CATIE","publisher-place":"Belém, PA","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Manejo forestal comunitario en América Latina experiencias, lecciones aprendidas y retos para el futuro.","editor":[{"family":"Sabogal","given":"César"},{"family":"Jong","given":"Wil","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Pokorny","given":"Benno"},{"family":"Louman","given":"Bastiaan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The species, volumes, areas, and management schemes of the forest operations vary strongly between and within these schemes and the context under which they occur. A key contextual factor is tenure. For example, some indigenous people and communities have been granted collective tenure, and others hold collective rights in extractive reserves, yet others have not been recognized with customary collective or individual tenure, which affects the community’s possibility to legally use timber. 
      Informal logging operations tend to be highly selective of high-value species such as for example Swietenia macrophylla, Manilkara huberi, Mezilaurus itauba, Handroanthus serratifolia. While traditional logging practiced by communities works with motor-manual practices and concentrates on small areas up to 20 hectares with extraction volumes of around 50m³ in the entire area, formalized community forestry operations may cover extraction areas of up to 1,000 hectares and volumes extracted range from 5-20m³ per hectare. In accordance with Reduced-Impact-Logging principles, many in the Amazon region follow formally approved management plans drawing on timber inventories and respect defined cutting cycles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a23jrru7ffa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sabogal, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Sabogal, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32169,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PDCD382P"],"itemData":{"id":32169,"type":"book","abstract":"Antecedentes, realidad y oportunidades del manejo forestal comunitario en América Latina; Capacidades técnicas y desafios del manejo forestal comunitario; Capacidades empresariales para el desarrollo de empresas forestales comunitarias; Capacidades organizativas para el manejo forestal comunitario frente a las demandas y expectativas oficiales; Políticas públicas que afectam el manejo forestal comunitario; Estrategias de acompanãmiento al manejo forestal comunitario; Síntesis y recomendaciones.","event-place":"Belém, PA","ISBN":"978-979-1412-73-5","language":"Spanish","note":"OCLC: 709201134","publisher":"CIFOR: CATIE","publisher-place":"Belém, PA","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Manejo forestal comunitario en América Latina experiencias, lecciones aprendidas y retos para el futuro.","editor":[{"family":"Sabogal","given":"César"},{"family":"Jong","given":"Wil","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Pokorny","given":"Benno"},{"family":"Louman","given":"Bastiaan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, most frequently, timber on communal lands is harvested by local loggers on the basis of informal arrangements that pay the communities or the communitarian leader a lump sum for the right to harvest the forests. While these arrangements typically are unfair and often the logger doesn’t hold his promise, it provides communities the opportunity for an easy income ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"alfjushpqc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Medina, Pokorny, & Campbell, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Medina, Pokorny, & Campbell, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32178,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DDZNMGAP"],"itemData":{"id":32178,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Many believe that Amazonian communities could bene?t from the growing market for timber through self-governed approaches to forest management. However, there is no clear understanding of how communities are to develop such approaches given logging frontiers that are characterized by informal negotiations with loggers and community forestry initiatives that are promoted by development agents. This article reports on research from four study areas in Bolivia, Brazil and Peru which reveals that external players exercise considerable power over communities. First, loggers and development agents impose forest management schemes directly on communities, hindering them from developing their own approaches. Second, paternalistic relationships with loggers and development agents prevent communities from identifying common interests and expressing these through their representative organizations. Finally, loggers and development agents use powerful discourses to shape acceptable schemes in forest management, silencing communities’ voices in debates. Through these different power mechanisms, external agents thwart the emergence of self-governed community management approaches.","container-title":"Development and Change","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-7660.2009.01570.x","ISSN":"0012155X, 14677660","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"745-767","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Loggers, Development Agents and the Exercise of Power in Amazonia","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1467-7660.2009.01570.x","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Medina","given":"Gabriel"},{"family":"Pokorny","given":"Benno"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"Bruce"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Boxes 3.18 and 3.19). 

Box 3.18 Coomflona in Flona Tapajos, Para, Brazil 

Tapajós National Forest is a government-owned land with community use designated as protected area with sustainable use of natural resources. Located in the state of Pará, in the Brazilian Amazon, Tapajós National Forest occupies an area of 527,319 ha of mostly dense tropical forest characterized by the dominance of large trees under a climatic regime of high temperatures and intense precipitation distributed throughout the year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1980bai3un","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Humphries, Andrade, & McGrath, 2015; IBAMA, 2004; J. N. M. Silva, de Carvalho, & Lopes, 1985)","plainCitation":"(Humphries, Andrade, & McGrath, 2015; IBAMA, 2004; J. N. M. Silva, de Carvalho, & Lopes, 1985)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32197,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8PHZFLHC"],"itemData":{"id":32197,"type":"report","event-place":"San Francisco, CA, USA","publisher-place":"San Francisco, CA, USA","title":"COOMFLONA?: A Successful Community-Based Forest Enterprise in Brazil","author":[{"family":"Humphries","given":"Shoana"},{"family":"Andrade","given":"Darlison"},{"family":"McGrath","given":"David"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32190,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XLSW59XQ"],"itemData":{"id":32190,"type":"report","event-place":"Brasília, DF, Brasil","publisher":"Instituto Brasileiro de Meio Ambiente e dos Recursos Naturais Renováveis.","publisher-place":"Brasília, DF, Brasil","title":"Floresta Nacional Do Tapajós: Plano de Manejo.” Vol. I-Inform.","URL":"http://www.icmbio.gov.br/portal/images/stories/imgs-unidades-coservacao/flona_tapajoss.pdf","author":[{"family":"IBAMA","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32184,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8BWPGKWF"],"itemData":{"id":32184,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The results of a forest inventory carried out in area of 144 hectares in the Tapajós National Forest, in Santarem, State of Pará, Brazil, are reported. The gross standing volume of the whole area was 31,738.484 cubic meters, with an average of 220.406 m3 per hectare. The volume of commercial trees was 113.296 m3 per hectare, which corresponds to 51.41% of the stand average volume.","container-title":"Embrapa Amazônia Oriental-Artigo em periódico indexado (ALICE)","issue":"10","language":"pt","page":"69","source":"Zotero","title":"INVENTÁRIO FLORESTAL DE UMA ÁREA EXPERIMENTAL NA FLORESTA NACIONAL DO TAPAJÓS","author":[{"family":"Silva","given":"José Natalino Macedo"},{"family":"Carvalho","given":"João Olegário Pereira","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Lopes","given":"José do Carmo Alves"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1985"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Over 24 forest-based communities are based in the area. Approximately 500 indigenous people and 5000 local people live within Tapajós National Forest. They have diversified livelihood strategies which include agriculture, non-timber forest products, timber, and fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"askmv5h48d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Andrade, de Carvalho, da Silva-Ribeiro, & Dantas, 2014; ICMBio, 2015)","plainCitation":"(de Andrade, de Carvalho, da Silva-Ribeiro, & Dantas, 2014; ICMBio, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30071,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P6363K4T","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PJDA92TU"],"itemData":{"id":30071,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Simpósio Nacional de Áreas Protegidas","event-place":"Viçosa","ISBN":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/336881171_MANEJO_FLORESTAL_COMUNITARIO_COMO_ESTRATEGIA_DE_GESTAO_E_MELHORIA_DE_VIDA_DA_POPULACAO_TRADICIONAL_DA_FLORESTA_NACIONAL_DO_TAPAJOS","page":"249-56","publisher":"Universidade Federal de Viçosa","publisher-place":"Viçosa","title":"Manejo Florestal Comunitário Como Estratégia de Gestão e Melhoria Da Qualidade de Vida Da População Tradicional Da Floresta Nacional Do Tapajós","author":[{"family":"Andrade","given":"Dárlison Fernandes","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Carvalho","given":"Fábio Menezes","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Silva-Ribeiro","given":"Renato Bezerra","non-dropping-particle":"da"},{"family":"Dantas","given":"Jeremias Batista"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32160,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RP9ADS9M"],"itemData":{"id":32160,"type":"report","event-place":"Santarém, PA, Brasil","publisher":"Instituto Chico Mendes de Conservação da Biodiversidade","publisher-place":"Santarém, PA, Brasil","title":"Relatório: Levantamento de Famílias Da Floresta Nacional Do Tapajós.","author":[{"family":"ICMBio","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
Tapajós National Forest residents founded a local timber cooperative, the Mixed Cooperative of the Tapajós National Forest (Coomflona) to manage tropical timber resources. The members include 150 forest residents from the 24 communities. With few exceptions, Coomflona hires external labor for work such as lawyer, forest engineer, and forestry machinery operators. The access to forest is collective, since every cooperative-member has the right to vote and make decisions over forest resource management. Decisions are made during general assemblies, held during the first three months of the year, and a cooperative executive committee operationalizes management decisions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a61ui1j8u2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Espada & Vasconcellos Sobrinho, 2019; Humphries, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Espada & Vasconcellos Sobrinho, 2019; Humphries, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31920,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EA6MZE3J"],"itemData":{"id":31920,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Over the last few years, forest-based communities have faced two different but related phenomena. On the one hand, they have become more integrated with global economies, accessing regional and international markets. On the other, they have been pressured by economic groups into becoming part of the ecologically unequal exchange that exports natural resources and generates social and environmental problems at a local level. However, within new approaches to managing common-pool resources in common properties such as sustainable-use protected areas, communities are finding their own ways to be resilient and to face the two phenomena that are part of the same global economic system. Communities have built a multi-partner governance system for forest management and community development that involves agents from the civil society, state and market. Accordingly, multi-partner governance has proven to be a strategy to protect community-based forests against increasing timber market pressure. The question that then emerges is, to what extent has multi-partner governance been effective in supporting forest-based communities to be resilient and to face pressures from the global timber market in forests under community use? The aim of this paper is to analyze forest-based community resilience to the global economic system in situations where common properties are under governance of multiple stakeholders. The research is based on a singular case study in the Tapajós National Forest, Brazilian Amazon, which is a sustainable-use protected area with 24 communities involved in a multi-partner governance system. The article shows that forest-based communities under pressure have been resilient, and facing the global economic system have created a community-based cooperative for managing timber and engaging all partners in the process to improve their collective action. The cooperative provides timber sales revenue that supports community development both through diversification of agroforestry production and building of infrastructure as collective benefits.","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f10060461","ISSN":"1999-4907","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Forests","language":"en","page":"461","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Logging Community-Based Forests in the Amazon: An Analysis of External Influences, Multi-Partner Governance, and Resilience","title-short":"Logging Community-Based Forests in the Amazon","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/1999-4907/10/6/461","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Espada","given":"Ana Luiza Violato"},{"family":"Vasconcellos Sobrinho","given":"Mário"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",5,28]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32189,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9TGJMIS"],"itemData":{"id":32189,"type":"report","event-place":"San Francisco","publisher-place":"San Francisco","title":"Financial Viability and Income Generation for a Community Forestry Cooperative in Brazil.","author":[{"family":"Humphries","given":"Shoana"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Coomflona has a permit to manage timber with non-onerous (zero-cost) concession from the federal government, and every year it has to submit an operational plan to get the approval from the government to execute timber-harvesting operations. Currently, the total timber harvest area covers 44,000 ha, and represents 8% of the total area of the Tapajós National Forest. Annually, Coomflona now manages an area of 1,500 ha, which has steadily increased since its first year of timber-harvesting operations in 2006. They manage for a cutting cycle of 30 years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"H7GIH2XM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Espada & Vasconcellos Sobrinho, 2019; Humphries, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Espada & Vasconcellos Sobrinho, 2019; Humphries, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31920,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EA6MZE3J"],"itemData":{"id":31920,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Over the last few years, forest-based communities have faced two different but related phenomena. On the one hand, they have become more integrated with global economies, accessing regional and international markets. On the other, they have been pressured by economic groups into becoming part of the ecologically unequal exchange that exports natural resources and generates social and environmental problems at a local level. However, within new approaches to managing common-pool resources in common properties such as sustainable-use protected areas, communities are finding their own ways to be resilient and to face the two phenomena that are part of the same global economic system. Communities have built a multi-partner governance system for forest management and community development that involves agents from the civil society, state and market. Accordingly, multi-partner governance has proven to be a strategy to protect community-based forests against increasing timber market pressure. The question that then emerges is, to what extent has multi-partner governance been effective in supporting forest-based communities to be resilient and to face pressures from the global timber market in forests under community use? The aim of this paper is to analyze forest-based community resilience to the global economic system in situations where common properties are under governance of multiple stakeholders. The research is based on a singular case study in the Tapajós National Forest, Brazilian Amazon, which is a sustainable-use protected area with 24 communities involved in a multi-partner governance system. The article shows that forest-based communities under pressure have been resilient, and facing the global economic system have created a community-based cooperative for managing timber and engaging all partners in the process to improve their collective action. The cooperative provides timber sales revenue that supports community development both through diversification of agroforestry production and building of infrastructure as collective benefits.","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f10060461","ISSN":"1999-4907","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Forests","language":"en","page":"461","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Logging Community-Based Forests in the Amazon: An Analysis of External Influences, Multi-Partner Governance, and Resilience","title-short":"Logging Community-Based Forests in the Amazon","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/1999-4907/10/6/461","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Espada","given":"Ana Luiza Violato"},{"family":"Vasconcellos Sobrinho","given":"Mário"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",5,28]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32189,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9TGJMIS"],"itemData":{"id":32189,"type":"report","event-place":"San Francisco","publisher-place":"San Francisco","title":"Financial Viability and Income Generation for a Community Forestry Cooperative in Brazil.","author":[{"family":"Humphries","given":"Shoana"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Coomflona implements reduced impact harvesting techniques, removing 3 to 4 whole trees per hectare. The main log extraction equipment in a skidder, and around of 30,000 meters cube of roundwood are harvest every year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ji2r7q168","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Humphries, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Humphries, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32197,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8PHZFLHC"],"itemData":{"id":32197,"type":"report","event-place":"San Francisco, CA, USA","publisher-place":"San Francisco, CA, USA","title":"COOMFLONA?: A Successful Community-Based Forest Enterprise in Brazil","author":[{"family":"Humphries","given":"Shoana"},{"family":"Andrade","given":"Darlison"},{"family":"McGrath","given":"David"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Coomflona, with the support of its partner organizations, achieved several notable accomplishments. First, the cooperative has secured financial resources for forestry operation costs, critical in community forestry. Second, the cooperative created an innovative system of funds in which to allocate net profit from timber sales to benefit timber workers, their families, and communities, and beyond, local people that do not participate directly in the cooperative. Third, Coomflona invested in a portable sawmill and small-scale carpentry to verticalize timber production, aggregate value to timber products, expand market strategies, and engage additional community members in timber production. Fourth, Coomflona has established long-term and strong partnerships with diverse organizations. Fifth, Coomflona has become a model for other community-based groups aiming to manage timber resources in sustainable-use protected areas, as in the cases of extractive reserves. Sixth, Coomflona is running timber management with good practices considered in the forestry sector; the Forest Stewardship Council certification, for instance, certifies that Coomflona is maintaining forest health and ecosystem functions while provisioning both local social and economic benefits. 

Box 3.19 Ejido Petcacab-Quintana Roo, Mexico, drawn from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15jabvg83p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wilshusen, 2005a, 2005b)","plainCitation":"(Wilshusen, 2005a, 2005b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32175,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YFYBB4XM"],"itemData":{"id":32175,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The community forests of Mexico: Managing for sustainable landscapes","event-place":"Austin","page":"151-179","publisher":"University of Texas Press","publisher-place":"Austin","title":"Community adaptation or collective breakdown? The emergence of ‘work groups’ in two forestry ejidos in Quintana Roo, Mexico.","author":[{"family":"Wilshusen","given":"Peter R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32167,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BTQ3R6XV"],"itemData":{"id":32167,"type":"report","title":"ITTO Country Case Study: Petcacab. Sociedad de Productores Forestales Ejidales de Quintana Roo (SPFEQR), Quintana Roo, México.","URL":"https://rightsandresources.org/wp-content/exported-pdf/petcacabqr.pdf","author":[{"family":"Wilshusen","given":"Peter R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}

Local communities own approximately 45% of Mexico's forests and have relative autonomy to manage them. Some of these communities have established community forest enterprises in order to generate benefits, such as jobs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a191llr42vk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Frey, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Frey, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32140,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZF4TH2BI"],"itemData":{"id":32140,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2019.05.009","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"101923","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Competitiveness, certification, and support of timber harvest by community forest enterprises in Mexico","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934119302813","volume":"107","author":[{"family":"Frey","given":"Gregory E."},{"family":"Cubbage","given":"Frederick W."},{"family":"Holmes","given":"Thomas P."},{"family":"Reyes-Retana","given":"Graciela"},{"family":"Davis","given":"Robert R."},{"family":"Megevand","given":"Carole"},{"family":"Rodríguez-Paredes","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Kraus-Elsin","given":"Yoanna"},{"family":"Hernández-Toro","given":"Berenice"},{"family":"Chemor-Salas","given":"Diana Nacibe"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the Mexican state of Quintana Roo, tropical forest ecosystems dominate the landscape. Forest types vary by soil, topography and local climate: medium-stature forests (15 to 25 meters) are present on well-drained soils, while shorter forests occur on seasonally inundated wetlands depressions. Mahogany (Swietenia macrophylla) and Spanish cedar (Cedrela odorata) were historically the most important commercial tree species, but in recent decades lesser-known tropical species have come to constitute around 70% of the harvest ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ani2l57gj0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ellis, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Ellis, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31921,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A3R77CTX"],"itemData":{"id":31921,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f6114295","ISSN":"1999-4907","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Forests","language":"en","page":"4295-4327","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Endurance and Adaptation of Community Forest Management in Quintana Roo, Mexico","URL":"http://www.mdpi.com/1999-4907/6/11/4295","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Ellis","given":"Edward"},{"family":"Kainer","given":"Karen"},{"family":"Sierra-Huelsz","given":"José"},{"family":"Negreros-Castillo","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Rodriguez-Ward","given":"Dawn"},{"family":"DiGiano","given":"Maria"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",11,23]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As of 1992, the harvest was managed by four associations of forestry ejidos through community forest enterprises with a combined allowable cut of 10,580 m3 per year of mahogany and cedar from 393,481 ha of permanent forest areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1gro4801ru","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Flachsenberg & Galleti, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Flachsenberg & Galleti, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32143,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7T6BPZGP"],"itemData":{"id":32143,"type":"chapter","container-title":"In La Selva Maya: Conservación y Desarrollo","ISBN":"968-23-2194-8","publisher":"island press","title":"El Manejo Forestal de La Selva En Quintana Roo, México.","URL":"http://repositorio.cenpat-conicet.gob.ar:8081/xmlui/bitstream/handle/123456789/469/laSelvaMaya.pdf?sequence=1","author":[{"family":"Flachsenberg","given":"Henning"},{"family":"Galleti","given":"Hugo Alfredo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Up until 1983, logging was carried out first by small private concessionaires and later by a parastatal company, with wildly fluctuating annual volumes between 10,000 and 50,000 m3. Beginning in 1984, community management produced a striking reduction and stabilization of harvests of mahogany and cedar going from 10,000 m3 annually, a 78% reduction from the last five years of the parastatal to around 5,000 m3 in 2018, and foresters consider this to be sustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1u5bps45c4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bray, 2020; Navarro-Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}nez, Ellis, Hern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez-G\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}mez, Romero-Montero, & S\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}nchez-S\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}nchez, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Bray, 2020; Navarro-Martínez, Ellis, Hernández-Gómez, Romero-Montero, & Sánchez-Sánchez, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32162,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7DGJIPBE"],"itemData":{"id":32162,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"David Barton Bray has spent more than thirty years researching and studying Mexican Community Forestry Enterprises (CFEs). In this book he shares the scientific evidence for Mexico's social and environmental achievements and how, in its most successful manifestations, it became a global model for common property forest management, sustainable social-ecological systems, and climate change mitigation in developing countries\\"--","call-number":"SD147 .B73 2020","event-place":"Tucson","ISBN":"978-0-8165-4112-6","publisher":"The University of Arizona Press","publisher-place":"Tucson","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Mexico's community forest enterprises: success on the commons and the seeds of a good anthropocene","title-short":"Mexico's community forest enterprises","author":[{"family":"Bray","given":"David Barton"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31917,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3QRQ4G7R"],"itemData":{"id":31917,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tropical Conservation Science","DOI":"10.1177/1940082918766875","ISSN":"1940-0829, 1940-0829","journalAbbreviation":"Tropical Conservation Science","language":"en","page":"194008291876687","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Distribution and Abundance of Big-Leaf Mahogany ( <i>Swietenia macrophylla</i> ) on the Yucatan Peninsula, Mexico","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1940082918766875","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Navarro-Martínez","given":"Angélica"},{"family":"Ellis","given":"Edward A."},{"family":"Hernández-Gómez","given":"Irving"},{"family":"Romero-Montero","given":"José Arturo"},{"family":"Sánchez-Sánchez","given":"Odilón"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Petcacab is an ejido, a common property land grant in Mexico’s agrarian system, inhabited by Mayan indigenous peoples. It is located in Central Quintana Roo, with an estimated population of 947 and 206 legal members of the ejido. The property regime is communal with a total land area of 46,000 hectares and permanent forest area of 32,500 hectares.  Petcacab’s community forest enterprise was initially organized in the mid-1980s with external support from the Forest Pilot Plan, supported by the Mexican government and German foreign assistance. Petcacab initially organized its community forest enterprise as an entirely community-administered operation, supervised by community authorities.  However, due to concerns about corruption, in 1996 Petcacab reorganized its community forest enterprise to be administered by what are termed “work groups” or coalitions of community members based on family clans and individual families. Access to communal lands by community groups, approved by the community assembly, was permitted by a 1992 reform to agrarian law. By 2000, Petcacab had 11 work groups who each received a proportional share of the annual authorized volume, and essentially managed themselves as small, separate community forest enterprises or microenterprises. 
      All of the work groups operated under a single management program prepared by a professional forester and approved by the Mexican environmental agency. In the 2000s Petcacab had authorized harvest volumes of 1,499 m3 of mahogany, 2,545 m3 of tropical softwoods, 3, 927 m3 of tropical hardwoods and 10, 328 m3 of polewood, with a decline in the volume of mahogany in more recent years. Production is small-scale industrial, with the use of tractors and skidders for extraction and logwood is sold to a community sawmill or intermediaries. Harvests are regulated by Mexican forest and environmental laws and compliance is considered good.  
      The work groups sell both logwood and sawnwood, after processing at a community sawmill. Most timber is sold domestically in Mexico. Benefits go to the individual work groups, with little or no reinvestment in the community or the community sawmill. Harvests are conducted according to the management programs with little or no input from indigenous knowledge. Harvests of mahogany have declined in recent years but are considered sustainable at current more reduced levels.  Socially, the work groups have allowed for increased incomes at the work group and household level, but with a corresponding decline in community investments in public goods. The apportionment of the authorized volumes to individuals has led to a market in the shares of authorized volumes and increasing economic inequality with the ejido as some members purchase others shares. Economically the work groups appear to be profitable, and the work group arrangements appear to be sustainable. 
      
      In protected areas an important timber management experience operated collectively by community-based enterprises takes place in the Mayan Biosphere Reserve, a protected area of 2.1 million ha established in 1990s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2qqg7p9gj5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Radachowsky, Ramos, McNab, Baur, & Kazakov, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Radachowsky, Ramos, McNab, Baur, & Kazakov, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31228,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DWMENDH9"],"itemData":{"id":31228,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2011.08.043","ISSN":"03781127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"18-28","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forest concessions in the Maya Biosphere Reserve, Guatemala: A decade later","title-short":"Forest concessions in the Maya Biosphere Reserve, Guatemala","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112711005354","volume":"268","author":[{"family":"Radachowsky","given":"Jeremy"},{"family":"Ramos","given":"Victor H."},{"family":"McNab","given":"Roan"},{"family":"Baur","given":"Erick H."},{"family":"Kazakov","given":"Nikolay"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A total of 12-community concession contracts (for areas ranging from 7,000 ha to 85,000 ha for a total of 390,000 ha) were signed between 1994 and 2001 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1l5tqle65n","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stoian, Rodas, Butler, Monterroso, & Hodgdon, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Stoian, Rodas, Butler, Monterroso, & Hodgdon, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31229,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EGK7IKJ9"],"itemData":{"id":31229,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"CIFOR","language":"en","page":"8","source":"Zotero","title":"Forest concessions in Petén, Guatemala: A Systematic Analysis of the Socioeconomic Performance of Community Enterprises in the Maya Biosphere Reserve.","author":[{"family":"Stoian","given":"Dietmar"},{"family":"Rodas","given":"Aldo"},{"family":"Butler","given":"Megan"},{"family":"Monterroso","given":"Iliana"},{"family":"Hodgdon","given":"Benjamin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. All concession contracts required collective organization: three forms emerged i) limited liability companies or civil societies (Sociedades Civiles), ii) civil associations, and iii) cooperatives. Community concession contracts are legal agreements between the state and an organized group composed of members living in a given community. These 25-year concession contracts allowed concessionaire members rights to manage and extract timber and non-timber forest products recognizing also rights to implement nature-based tourism activities in protected areas. This system of community concessions in the Multiple Use Zone (MUZ) represents about 15% of the country’s total forest cover, including national parks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a130jsm1fri","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IARNA/URL/ILA, 2006)","plainCitation":"(IARNA/URL/ILA, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31406,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9Q23HPAZ"],"itemData":{"id":31406,"type":"report","event-place":"Guatemala","publisher":"Instituto de Agricultura, Recursos Naturales y Abiente, Universidad Rafael Landivar and Asociacion Instituto de Incidencia Ambiental","publisher-place":"Guatemala","title":"Perfil Ambiental de Guatemala: tendencias y reflexiones sobre la gestion ambiental","author":[{"family":"IARNA/URL/ILA","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The area under forest concessions covers more than 480,000 hectares. To date, nine community concession contracts remain active (around 350,000 hectares) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a28el05425v","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stoian, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Stoian, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31229,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EGK7IKJ9"],"itemData":{"id":31229,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"CIFOR","language":"en","page":"8","source":"Zotero","title":"Forest concessions in Petén, Guatemala: A Systematic Analysis of the Socioeconomic Performance of Community Enterprises in the Maya Biosphere Reserve.","author":[{"family":"Stoian","given":"Dietmar"},{"family":"Rodas","given":"Aldo"},{"family":"Butler","given":"Megan"},{"family":"Monterroso","given":"Iliana"},{"family":"Hodgdon","given":"Benjamin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Compared to Latin America and South Asia, relatively little information on Africa was available. In Central Africa, the number of communities formally embracing community forest management has greatly increased over the last twenty years as all countries have included this management option in their forest legal frameworks. There are now more than a thousand of them, about 90% of which are in Cameroon. However, most of the community forestry operations validated by the authorities are either inactive, as in Cameroon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a9712rt413","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. Lescuyer, Cerutti, & Tsanga, 2016)","plainCitation":"(G. Lescuyer, Cerutti, & Tsanga, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":850,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SZTFYKAN"],"itemData":{"id":850,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In Cameroon, sustainable timber management relies on the model of large logging concession. However, over the past fifteen years, small-scale logging has become a common activity, with two different forms. First, the creation of community forests in the late 1990s allowed village associations to legally harvest, process and trade timber, almost always with the support of external actors such as NGOs or private operators. Second, individual chainsaw milling, almost always informal, has grown considerably. The article compares the economic, social and environmental impacts of these two options of small-scale logging. Although much focus has been put on community forestry over the latest two decades, it remains a marginal activity with a turnover of less than € 2 million per year and a small impact on rural economies. Conversely, informal chainsaw milling represents an annual turnover of € 93 million, with a flow of revenues around € 30 million for the benefit of rural population. From an environmental perspective, none of the two options seems to substantially conserve or degrade forest resources, but more research is needed on the issue.","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554816819683744","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"40-51","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Contributions of community and individual small-scale logging to sustainable timber management in Cameroon","URL":"http://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/146554816819683744","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"G."},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"P.O."},{"family":"Tsanga","given":"R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",10,13]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, or oriented towards conservation, as in the Democratic Republic of Congo ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a20k138a86u","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bauer, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Bauer, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31222,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I883XXXP"],"itemData":{"id":31222,"type":"report","event-place":"Kinshasa, DRC","publisher":"GIZ - Programme Biodiversité et Forêts","publisher-place":"Kinshasa, DRC","title":"Cartographie Des Acteurs de La Foresterie Communautaire En RDC – Un Aperçu Des Intervenants, de La Vision et Les Défis Dans Sa Mise En Œuvre","URL":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Tina_Bauer2/publication/334729658_Cartographie_des_acteurs_de_la_Foresterie_Communautaire_en_RDC_-_un_apercu_des_intervenants_de_la_vision_et_les_defis_dans_sa_mise_en_oeuvre/links/5d3d3c16a6fdcc370a660f69/Cartographie-des-acteurs-de-la-Foresterie-Communautaire-en-RDC-un-apercu-des-intervenants-de-la-vision-et-les-defis-dans-sa-mise-en-oeuvre.pdf","author":[{"family":"Bauer","given":"Tina"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In total, only about 150 forestry communities are authorized in Cameroon, and about a hundred are created or in the process of being created in both Gabon and the Democratic Republic of Congo. There are no regional statistics on timber production from community forestry in Central Africa. Yet, based on case studies in each country, a maximum of 50,000m3 would be legally extracted from community forestry harvesting operations in the Congo Basin ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ak1dp7tfav","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Beauchamp & Ingram, 2011; Julve, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Beauchamp & Ingram, 2011; Julve, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31221,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QT5D8SLC"],"itemData":{"id":31221,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Community forestry is considered a tool for decentralisation and devolution and as efficient strategy to achieve the multiple goals of sustainable resource management and poverty alleviation. However, evidence worldwide has shown mixed results. A financial, economic and environmental cost-benefit analysis of two community forests in Cameroon revealed that community forests are economically and environmentally profitable, and benefit communities more, compared to a baseline situation. Sharp differences between the economic and financial returns highlight the importance of conditional factors. These include the communities’ technical and managerial skills, access to finance, legal resources and market information, and the communities’ capacity for vertical integration. The cases highlight the limitations of the current regulatory and policy framework as a determining influence on the exploitation of community forests and conclude there is a pressing need for institutional and organizational reforms within the governmental and support apparatus to increase the profitability and equity of community forestry.","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554811798811371","ISSN":"14655489","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Int. Forest. Rev.","language":"en","page":"389-403","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impacts of community forests on livelihoods in Cameroon: lessons from two case studies","title-short":"Impacts of community forests on livelihoods in Cameroon","URL":"http://openurl.ingenta.com/content/xref?genre=article&issn=1465-5489&volume=13&issue=4&spage=389","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Beauchamp","given":"E."},{"family":"Ingram","given":"V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12,1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31250,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FPYC33Y4"],"itemData":{"id":31250,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bois et forêts des tropiques","issue":"3","page":"71-80","title":"Forêts communautaires camerounaises et Plan d’action «Forest Law Enforcement, Governance and Trade»(FLEGT): quel prix pour la légalité?.\\" Bois et forêts des tropiques 317, no. 3 (2013): 71-80.","URL":"https://orbi.uliege.be/bitstream/2268/159801/1/BFT%20Julve%20et%20al%202013.pdf","volume":"317","author":[{"family":"Julve","given":"Cécilia"},{"family":"Eckebil","given":"Tabi Pamela"},{"family":"Nadège","given":"Nzoyem Saha"},{"family":"Tchantchouang","given":"Jean-Claude"},{"family":"Kerkohf","given":"Benoit"},{"family":"Beauquin","given":"Antoine"},{"family":"Mbarga","given":"Jean-Paul Mbarga"},{"family":"Vermeulen","given":"Cédric"},{"family":"Cerruti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Due to inadequate regulatory texts that are costly to apply for ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2frgppgjnk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cuny, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Cuny, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31403,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3TGP9ZPV"],"itemData":{"id":31403,"type":"book","event-place":"Wageningen","ISBN":"978-90-5113-104-8","language":"French","note":"OCLC: 776130972","publisher":"Tropenbos International","publisher-place":"Wageningen","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Etat des lieux de la foresterie communautaire et communale au Cameron","author":[{"family":"Cuny","given":"Pascal"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the vast majority of community forests that exploit timber do so illegally, destined for domestic markets that do not require timber of legal origin. There are no statistics on these illegal practices, but numerous reports from Cameroon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2c0ago398m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nzoyem, Vabi, Kouokam, & Azanga, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Nzoyem, Vabi, Kouokam, & Azanga, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31226,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3HJABHYR"],"itemData":{"id":31226,"type":"paper-conference","event-title":"conference ‘Taking stock of smallholder and community forestry: where do we go from here","page":"24-26","title":"Forêts communautaires contre la pauvreté, la déforestation et la dégradation des forêts: en faire une réalité au Cameroun.","author":[{"family":"Nzoyem","given":"N"},{"family":"Vabi","given":"M"},{"family":"Kouokam","given":"R"},{"family":"Azanga","given":"C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Gabon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1mk358ibm7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ondo, Medik, Mijola, & Boussougou, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Ondo, Medik, Mijola, & Boussougou, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31227,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F2ZKY2KD"],"itemData":{"id":31227,"type":"report","event-place":"Libreville, DRC","publisher":"UE-FAO FLEGT.","publisher-place":"Libreville, DRC","title":"Légalité et Traçabilité Des Bois Des Forêts Communautaires Du Gabon (Province de l’Ogooue Ivindo)","URL":"http://www.keva-in.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/L%C3%A9galit%C3%A9-et-tra%C3%A7abilit%C3%A9-des-bois-des-for%C3%AAts-communautaires-au-Gabon.pdf","author":[{"family":"Ondo","given":"Rose"},{"family":"Medik","given":"Annie"},{"family":"Mijola","given":"Joana"},{"family":"Boussougou","given":"Aubin Claude"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} indicate that informal timber-harvesting from community forestry operations far exceeds the volume legally extracted. 
	Generally, South Asia’s forest dependent communities are the ones who are also the more disadvantaged in the region. Two categories of forest dependent people are identifiable. First, those who are traditionally the forest communities residing in and around forest areas for generations, such as tribal communities in India. Altogether, the population of this group is estimated to be 150 million in the region ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1vec30kaan","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Bank, 2005)","plainCitation":"(World Bank, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31382,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GPL8QGDY"],"itemData":{"id":31382,"type":"report","event-place":"Washington DC","publisher":"World Bank","publisher-place":"Washington DC","title":"India : Unlocking Opportunities for Forest-Dependent People in India, Volume 2, Appendixes.","URL":"https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/handle/10986/8414","author":[{"family":"World Bank","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Second, people who depend on forests for a variety of products and nature’s contributions to people but do not directly reside inside or in the vicinity of the forest. There are around 400 million forest users in this category ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"anum2pbk4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Poffenberger, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Poffenberger, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31217,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3JBRL73B"],"itemData":{"id":31217,"type":"book","publisher":"IUCN","title":"Communities and forest management in South Asia.","author":[{"family":"Poffenberger","given":"Mark"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. More recently, rapid rural and urban and even overseas migration of youths has led to change in the conventional patterns of forest dependence, with reduced use of forest products in livelihoods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aais2q4tme","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ojha, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Ojha, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31369,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/28TAEMH2"],"itemData":{"id":31369,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Rural Studies","DOI":"10.1016/j.jrurstud.2017.05.012","ISSN":"07430167","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Rural Studies","language":"en","page":"156-172","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Agricultural land underutilisation in the hills of Nepal: Investigating socio-environmental pathways of change","title-short":"Agricultural land underutilisation in the hills of Nepal","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0743016716303394","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Ojha","given":"Hemant R."},{"family":"Shrestha","given":"Krishna K."},{"family":"Subedi","given":"Yuba R."},{"family":"Shah","given":"Racchya"},{"family":"Nuberg","given":"Ian"},{"family":"Heyojoo","given":"Binod"},{"family":"Cedamon","given":"Edwin"},{"family":"Rigg","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Tamang","given":"Sujata"},{"family":"Paudel","given":"Krishna P."},{"family":"Malla","given":"Yam"},{"family":"McManus","given":"Phil"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	In terms of policy shifts, South Asia has notable community forestry initiatives in terms of scale and demonstrated outcomes, although the actual form and operational modalities vary greatly across the countries and sub-national regions. Likewise, a variety of local regimes of community forestry are found: formally handed over state forests, jointly managed forests, ‘sacred groves’ with cultural values, community plantations, and other forms of collective land use for trees and pastures. The beliefs and rituals linked to sacred groves have helped to conserve biodiversity, although they are under threat due to changing values and perceptions (see section 3.3.5).  
	Forest harvesting status and trends data for community forestry are not readily available for most countries in Sout Asia. Available evidence suggests all the countries and their sub-national authorities are struggling to optimize forest harvesting in a sustainable, efficient, and equitable way. A significant part of the forest landscape is under protected area management, and there have been participatory and co-management shifts in this regime too, especially since early 1990s. Studies show that such participatory shift in protected area management has resulted in more active use of resources, as found by a study in Bangladesh ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ar5l6jq0dp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. Islam, Nath, Jashimuddin, & Rahman, 2019)","plainCitation":"(K. Islam, Nath, Jashimuddin, & Rahman, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31408,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XDTEDNY2"],"itemData":{"id":31408,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.envdev.2019.100456","ISSN":"22114645","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Development","language":"en","page":"100456","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forest dependency, co-management and improvement of peoples’ livelihood capital: Evidence from Chunati Wildlife Sanctuary, Bangladesh","title-short":"Forest dependency, co-management and improvement of peoples’ livelihood capital","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2211464518302689","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Islam","given":"Kamrul"},{"family":"Nath","given":"Tapan Kumar"},{"family":"Jashimuddin","given":"Mohammed"},{"family":"Rahman","given":"Md. Farhadur"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
	In Bhutan, conservation rather than sustainable use mindsets dominate forest management policy and programs, and strategies and methodologies to promote sustainable harvesting are slow to develop ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1lrkku5286","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Phuntsho, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Phuntsho, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31214,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IBRUGDW9"],"itemData":{"id":31214,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Community forestry in Bhutan: Putting people at the heart of poverty reduction","event-place":"Bhutan","page":"1-3","publisher":"Ugyen Wangchuck Institute for Conservation and Environment (UWICE)","publisher-place":"Bhutan","title":"Forests, community forestry and their significance in Bhutan.","author":[{"family":"Phuntsho","given":"Sonam"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Despite having nearly 70% of area under forest, Bhutan has kept harvesting level to a minimum, favoring import of forest products. The most significant wood-based import item is charcoal. In 2012, Bhutan imported charcoal worth 16.8 million United States dollars, comprising 1.4 percent of total imports and 60 percent of wood-based imports (MoF 2013, cited in World Bank ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15jckjst4s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2019)","plainCitation":"(2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31220,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BG8U734R"],"itemData":{"id":31220,"type":"report","event-place":"Washington, DC","publisher":"World Bank","publisher-place":"Washington, DC","title":"Bhutan Forest Note : Pathways for Sustainable Forest Management and Socio-equitable Economic Development","URL":"https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/handle/10986/32047 License: CC BY 3.0 IGO.","author":[{"family":"World Bank","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The first national forest inventory published in 2017 and provides detailed data and information on Bhutan’s forests, showing the Bhutan is under harvesting its forest below the potential ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ptsvbjq7r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Bank, 2019)","plainCitation":"(World Bank, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31220,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BG8U734R"],"itemData":{"id":31220,"type":"report","event-place":"Washington, DC","publisher":"World Bank","publisher-place":"Washington, DC","title":"Bhutan Forest Note : Pathways for Sustainable Forest Management and Socio-equitable Economic Development","URL":"https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/handle/10986/32047 License: CC BY 3.0 IGO.","author":[{"family":"World Bank","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A bottom-up approach to forest management in Bhutan began after the 1979 royal decree that called for the involvement of local people in tree planting activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1g6nkh27h4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Phuntsho, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Phuntsho, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31214,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IBRUGDW9"],"itemData":{"id":31214,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Community forestry in Bhutan: Putting people at the heart of poverty reduction","event-place":"Bhutan","page":"1-3","publisher":"Ugyen Wangchuck Institute for Conservation and Environment (UWICE)","publisher-place":"Bhutan","title":"Forests, community forestry and their significance in Bhutan.","author":[{"family":"Phuntsho","given":"Sonam"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Bhutan’s community forestry policy emphasizes protection, conservation and sustainable use of forest resources in the country, together with contributions to poverty reduction and local democratization ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cljgBfec","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Phuntsho, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Phuntsho, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31214,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IBRUGDW9"],"itemData":{"id":31214,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Community forestry in Bhutan: Putting people at the heart of poverty reduction","event-place":"Bhutan","page":"1-3","publisher":"Ugyen Wangchuck Institute for Conservation and Environment (UWICE)","publisher-place":"Bhutan","title":"Forests, community forestry and their significance in Bhutan.","author":[{"family":"Phuntsho","given":"Sonam"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The level of harvesting in community forestry is no different than the national scenario. Following the adoption of a more decentralized and people-centered approach to forestry in the early 2000s, the number of community forestry management groups has increased rapidly since 2007. By 2018, there were 781 community forest management groups involving 32,402 rural households managing 92,165 hectares (3.0 percent) of forest land ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ereucglco","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(MoAF, 2018; World Bank, 2019)","plainCitation":"(MoAF, 2018; World Bank, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31374,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4V6FBHQK"],"itemData":{"id":31374,"type":"report","event-place":"Thimpu, Bhutan","publisher":"Ministry of Agriculture and Forests","publisher-place":"Thimpu, Bhutan","title":"Statistics on Community Forests (CFs) as of December 2017.","author":[{"family":"MoAF","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":31220,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BG8U734R"],"itemData":{"id":31220,"type":"report","event-place":"Washington, DC","publisher":"World Bank","publisher-place":"Washington, DC","title":"Bhutan Forest Note : Pathways for Sustainable Forest Management and Socio-equitable Economic Development","URL":"https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/handle/10986/32047 License: CC BY 3.0 IGO.","author":[{"family":"World Bank","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although the government is supportive to the implementation of the community forestry program, community forest management groups continue to face administrative hurdles with regard to timber marketing, leading to under-harvesting of forest stock ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a12hb3ssflf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Samdrup, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Samdrup, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31215,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ECPKQJ2W"],"itemData":{"id":31215,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Community forestry in Bhutan: Putting people at the heart of poverty reduction","page":"5-16","publisher":"Ugyen Wangchuck Institute for Conservation and Environment and Social Forestry Division,","title":"Improving the contribution of community forestry to poverty reduction in Bhutan.","URL":"http://www.uwice.gov.bt/admin_uwice/publications/publication_files/Reports/2011/UWICER-CFIB.pdf","author":[{"family":"Samdrup","given":"Tashi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Community forestry in Nepal emerged in the mid-1970s and led to the devolution of management and user rights to forest user groups, largely as a shift in the approach to conserve the hill forests in the context of degradation and deforestation. During the last three decades Nepal’s community forestry programme has evolved in terms of coverage and institutional innovation, supported through appropriate changes in policies and legislation. Substantial international support has also helped to sustain community participation. Community forestry has contributed to livelihoods of nearly one-third of the country’s total population, improved forest conditions and biodiversity, and above all, developed itself as a self-sustaining system involving a strong base of policy champions, service providers, and critical action researchers. By the end of 2019, over 22,000 community forestry user groups had been registered across the country, with rights granted to manage nearly two million hectares of forest areas (about a third of total forest areas of the country. 	Community forests are vital components of environmental resilience and nature’s contributions to people not only to the people close by but also to large populations downstream. Dissipating fears of desertification, community forestry has led to improvement in forest ecology, with 74% of the forest area managed by community forestry user groups reported as in “good” condition, compared to 19% in “degraded” condition ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ag5sd68sj9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kanel & Kandel, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Kanel & Kandel, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31411,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/58UIJANK"],"itemData":{"id":31411,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Forest and Livelihood","issue":"1","title":"Community Forestry in Nepal: Achievements and Challenges","URL":"https://www.forestaction.org/app/webroot/vendor/tinymce/editor/plugins/filemanager/files/8.%20CF_policy_Kanel%20and%20Kandel%20final_june%2029.pdf","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Kanel","given":"Keshav R."},{"family":"Kandel","given":"Bala Ram"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Community forestry user groups also compare favorably to government forests in terms of change in forest condition ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"agqv1figa3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nagendra, Pareeth, Sharma, Schweik, & Adhikari, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Nagendra, Pareeth, Sharma, Schweik, & Adhikari, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31412,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HCJ5JZBY"],"itemData":{"id":31412,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10980-007-9162-y","ISSN":"0921-2973, 1572-9761","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape Ecol","language":"en","page":"41-54","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forest fragmentation and regrowth in an institutional mosaic of community, government and private ownership in Nepal","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10980-007-9162-y","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"},{"family":"Pareeth","given":"Sajid"},{"family":"Sharma","given":"Bhawna"},{"family":"Schweik","given":"Charles M."},{"family":"Adhikari","given":"Keshav R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. More recent empirical evidence confirms improved biodiversity outcomes from community forestry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1da3o9t2nm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Luintel, Bluffstone, & Scheller, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Luintel, Bluffstone, & Scheller, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1128,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L9ZCM45T"],"itemData":{"id":1128,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0199526","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e0199526","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The effects of the Nepal community forestry program on biodiversity conservation and carbon storage","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0199526","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Luintel","given":"Harisharan"},{"family":"Bluffstone","given":"Randall A."},{"family":"Scheller","given":"Robert M."}],"editor":[{"family":"Yue","given":"Bi-Song"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,26]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nepal has a strong legislation that allows communities to enjoy perpetual rights over designated community forest areas. Such perpetual and sustained rights of access to forests have been key for the success of community forestry in Nepal ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a24ird0okll","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Acharya, Adhikari, & Khanal, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Acharya, Adhikari, & Khanal, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31397,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XZGQUULZ"],"itemData":{"id":31397,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Forest land tenure patterns have rapidly changed in Nepal in the past. The historically dominant land tenures are disappearing, and new tenures are emerging. This paper describes and analyses this dynamic nature of forest tenure, forest management modalities and the tenurial pattern they embody as well as their impact on livelihood, income, forest condition and equity in benefit distribution. Here, tenure means all options and opportunities-legal, non-legal and customary-to own, use or share any forest products and trees. Nepal’s forests can broadly be divided into two types from ownership perspective, viz., national forest and private forest. Within the national forest there are different forest management regimes encompassing different rights and responsibilities given to the people. The forest or trees held in common or managed by communities seem to be more sustainable than other forms of tenurial arrangement. Increasing numbers of community-based management modalities have developed innovative means of tenure arrangements to contribute positively to livelihood and environmental sustainability. It demonstrates that decentralized and democratic governance system strengthens land tenure in favour of local people’s livelihood. The paper argues that secure tenure is important for the conservation of forest resources and for encouraging investment in conservation and management. Which tenure regime is suitable at a particular time and place or location is more of a contextual matter, and thus, depending upon people’s participation, tenurial modalities need to be determined.","container-title":"Journal of Forest and Livelihood","language":"en","page":"14","source":"Zotero","title":"Forest Tenure Regimes and Their Impact on Livelihoods in Nepal","author":[{"family":"Acharya","given":"Krishna P"},{"family":"Adhikari","given":"Jagannath"},{"family":"Khanal","given":"Dilraj"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Though the land ownership remains with the government, the tenure of forest biomass is transferred to the community through a detailed approval process. Community forestry user groups retain 100 percent of revenues generated from their forest, but they have to allocate 25% of the income to forest development activities and 35% to programs that directly benefit the poorest households within the community forestry user group. The existing forest law provides communities with enough rights to choose their objectives of forest management and harvesting, but too often the actual practices of regulation and bureaucratic oversights hinder active management of forests beyond subsistence use. Nepal has achieved massive scale community forestry development in terms of enabling policy and institutional development, but the actual use of forest is less than 30% of the annual sustainable harvest level. Despite having 45% of the country’s area under forests, the contribution of the forest sector to local and national economy has remained much less than the potential in Nepal ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a254328k4kp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Banjade, Paudel, Karki, Sunam, & Paudyal, 2011; Chhetri, Lund, & Nielsen, 2012; Luintel, Bluffstone, Scheller, & Adhikari, 2017; Thoms, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Banjade, Paudel, Karki, Sunam, & Paudyal, 2011; Chhetri, Lund, & Nielsen, 2012; Luintel, Bluffstone, Scheller, & Adhikari, 2017; Thoms, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31386,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J4KYFRBP"],"itemData":{"id":31386,"type":"report","event-place":"Kathmandu, Nepal","genre":"Discussion Paper Series 11: 2. Forest Action","page":"16","publisher-place":"Kathmandu, Nepal","title":"Putting timber into the hot seat: discourse, policy and contestations over timber in Nepal. Discussion Paper Series 11: 2. Forest Action, . 16 p.","author":[{"family":"Banjade","given":"M"},{"family":"Paudel","given":"N. S."},{"family":"Karki","given":"R"},{"family":"Sunam","given":"R"},{"family":"Paudyal","given":"B"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31398,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N544EWLW"],"itemData":{"id":31398,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2011.09.023","ISSN":"09218009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"113-121","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The public finance potential of community forestry in Nepal","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800911003995","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Chhetri","given":"Bir Bahadur Khanal"},{"family":"Lund","given":"Jens Friis"},{"family":"Nielsen","given":"Øystein Juul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31409,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8IP4CIWC"],"itemData":{"id":31409,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Environment & Development","DOI":"10.1177/1070496517707305","ISSN":"1070-4965, 1552-5465","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"The Journal of Environment & Development","language":"en","page":"297-321","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Effect of the Nepal Community Forestry Program on Equity in Benefit Sharing","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1070496517707305","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Luintel","given":"Harisharan"},{"family":"Bluffstone","given":"Randall A."},{"family":"Scheller","given":"Robert M."},{"family":"Adhikari","given":"Bhim"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",9]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31387,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QYKX7C5M"],"itemData":{"id":31387,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geoforum","DOI":"10.1016/j.geoforum.2008.01.006","ISSN":"00167185","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Geoforum","language":"en","page":"1452-1465","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community control of resources and the challenge of improving local livelihoods: A critical examination of community forestry in Nepal","title-short":"Community control of resources and the challenge of improving local livelihoods","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0016718508000080","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Thoms","given":"Christopher A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",5]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As the national mood has recently shifted towards active forest management, several attempts have been made to develop and scale up silviculture innovations. These have stimulated debates in scientific forest management, though outcomes on the ground have remained limited. 		
      Community forestry in Sri Lanka has developed somewhat similarly to Nepal, but began in the 1990s. The community forestry project was initiated in Sri Lanka after a series of forest policy reforms and decentralization arrangements during the 1980s. Since 2003, the Department of Forest Conservation, a government department responsible for forestry in Sri Lanka, has been testing and trialing various approaches using the community forestry model ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a29b0dm5uf2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ekanayake, Xie, Ahmad, Geldard, & Nissanka, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Ekanayake, Xie, Ahmad, Geldard, & Nissanka, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31407,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/URRZFJRE"],"itemData":{"id":31407,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","DOI":"10.1080/10549811.2020.1794906","ISSN":"1054-9811, 1540-756X","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","language":"en","page":"1-17","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community Forestry for livelihood Improvement: evidence from the intermediate zone, Sri lanka","title-short":"Community Forestry for livelihood Improvement","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10549811.2020.1794906","author":[{"family":"Ekanayake","given":"E.M.B.P"},{"family":"Xie","given":"Yi"},{"family":"Ahmad","given":"Shahzad"},{"family":"Geldard","given":"R.P."},{"family":"Nissanka","given":"A.H.S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Community-based forest management in Sri Lanka encompasses community-owned forests and agro-forests as well as government-owned forests managed by communities. Forests managed by communities produce timber and wood products in agroforestry systems, on agricultural lands and community lands including farmer woodlots and silvopastoral systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1p196r71n7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(De Zoysa, 2017)","plainCitation":"(De Zoysa, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31405,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q83BE3CX"],"itemData":{"id":31405,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Community-based forest management in Sri Lanka encompasses community-owned forests and agro-forests as well as government-owned forests managed by communities. Low carbon, resource-efficient and socially inclusive, the ideal characteristics of a green economy are embodied in community-managed forests. This study reviews the literature and analyzes the community-based forest management approach of Sri Lanka in terms of: contribution to the green economy and environment; major challenges limiting the contribution; and strategies to promote the contribution. Community-based-forest-management contributions to the green economy and environment include: a foundation for grassroots democracy; granted lands as acts of social justice; social stability of rural communities; employment generation and livelihood development with community-based forest enterprises and out-grower schemes purchasing the forest products and paying market prices; creation of markets for ecosystem services and payments for environmental services; and sustainable production practices combined with broad distribution of benefits and production of timber and wood products. Environmental contributions include biodiversity conservation and adaptation to climatic change and unpredictable climate events. Community-based forest management protects and restores forest ecosystems while creating green employment. The main challenges are recognized as: changes in the forest resource base; poor communication and marketing imperfection; lack of secure tenure or access rights to forest resources; weak community governance; and unfair laws of the government on community forest related activities. Among strategies to promote the contribution are: incorporation of ethical criteria and bio-cultural heritage in forest management; greater land tenure security and change in the political and economic environment to restore governability to local communities; multi-functional rather than monoculture forests for community-managed forest ecosystems and technological advances towards a green bio-economy within the community-based forest enterprises; creation of an enabling business environment through financial provisions; integration of communities into key partners of forest-based industrial development; and removal of regulatory barriers and support of community rights to manage forest-based enterprises. Community based forestry management in Sri Lanka has a great potential for promotion of green economy and environment creating viable forest-based enterprises with strong backing of favorable policies.","container-title":"The Sri Lanka Forester","language":"en","page":"1-23","source":"Zotero","title":"Community-based forest management in Sri Lanka: Approaching a green economy and environment","URL":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Mangala-De-Zoysa/publication/326913485_Community-based_forest_management_in_Sri_Lanka_Approaching_a_green_economy_and_environment/links/5b97a513458515310578aff8/Community-based-forest-management-in-Sri-Lanka-Approaching-a-green-economy-and-environment.pdf","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"De Zoysa","given":"Mangala"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The home gardens, outside natural and planted forests supply more than 70% of the timber and 80% of the fuel wood in Sri Lanka ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tqkYunYo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(De Zoysa, 2017)","plainCitation":"(De Zoysa, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31405,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q83BE3CX"],"itemData":{"id":31405,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Community-based forest management in Sri Lanka encompasses community-owned forests and agro-forests as well as government-owned forests managed by communities. Low carbon, resource-efficient and socially inclusive, the ideal characteristics of a green economy are embodied in community-managed forests. This study reviews the literature and analyzes the community-based forest management approach of Sri Lanka in terms of: contribution to the green economy and environment; major challenges limiting the contribution; and strategies to promote the contribution. Community-based-forest-management contributions to the green economy and environment include: a foundation for grassroots democracy; granted lands as acts of social justice; social stability of rural communities; employment generation and livelihood development with community-based forest enterprises and out-grower schemes purchasing the forest products and paying market prices; creation of markets for ecosystem services and payments for environmental services; and sustainable production practices combined with broad distribution of benefits and production of timber and wood products. Environmental contributions include biodiversity conservation and adaptation to climatic change and unpredictable climate events. Community-based forest management protects and restores forest ecosystems while creating green employment. The main challenges are recognized as: changes in the forest resource base; poor communication and marketing imperfection; lack of secure tenure or access rights to forest resources; weak community governance; and unfair laws of the government on community forest related activities. Among strategies to promote the contribution are: incorporation of ethical criteria and bio-cultural heritage in forest management; greater land tenure security and change in the political and economic environment to restore governability to local communities; multi-functional rather than monoculture forests for community-managed forest ecosystems and technological advances towards a green bio-economy within the community-based forest enterprises; creation of an enabling business environment through financial provisions; integration of communities into key partners of forest-based industrial development; and removal of regulatory barriers and support of community rights to manage forest-based enterprises. Community based forestry management in Sri Lanka has a great potential for promotion of green economy and environment creating viable forest-based enterprises with strong backing of favorable policies.","container-title":"The Sri Lanka Forester","language":"en","page":"1-23","source":"Zotero","title":"Community-based forest management in Sri Lanka: Approaching a green economy and environment","URL":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Mangala-De-Zoysa/publication/326913485_Community-based_forest_management_in_Sri_Lanka_Approaching_a_green_economy_and_environment/links/5b97a513458515310578aff8/Community-based-forest-management-in-Sri-Lanka-Approaching-a-green-economy-and-environment.pdf","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"De Zoysa","given":"Mangala"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Recent studies have shown that impact of community forestry development has led to positive outcomes on livelihoods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"esQx0LeI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ekanayake, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Ekanayake, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31407,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/URRZFJRE"],"itemData":{"id":31407,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","DOI":"10.1080/10549811.2020.1794906","ISSN":"1054-9811, 1540-756X","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","language":"en","page":"1-17","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community Forestry for livelihood Improvement: evidence from the intermediate zone, Sri lanka","title-short":"Community Forestry for livelihood Improvement","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10549811.2020.1794906","author":[{"family":"Ekanayake","given":"E.M.B.P"},{"family":"Xie","given":"Yi"},{"family":"Ahmad","given":"Shahzad"},{"family":"Geldard","given":"R.P."},{"family":"Nissanka","given":"A.H.S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	In India, large scale shifts from state control of forest to joint management with local communities has led to a large area of forest being managed under joint forest management. Joint forest management covers more than 22 million hectares which is about third of the forest land in India, engaging 25 million people through 104, in 729 committees across more than 100,000 villages ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"auss28v21m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sundar, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Sundar, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31216,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BGSP2BHW"],"itemData":{"id":31216,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/1465548822272329","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"495-511","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Joint forest management in India – an assessment","URL":"https://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/1465548822272329","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Sundar","given":"B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Like Nepal and Bhutan, a conservative approach to forest harvesting dominates forest management practices across all regimes of public forests. 	India’s average annual yield of forest is estimated as 85.65 million m3, whereas the annual removal of only 5.85 million m3, which is 6.82% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a5hupjm6gp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FSI, 2019)","plainCitation":"(FSI, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31410,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2K9YJGSJ"],"itemData":{"id":31410,"type":"report","publisher":"Deheradun, Forest Survey of India, Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change","title":"India State of Forest Report 2019","author":[{"family":"FSI","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Total growing stock is estimated to be 5915 million m3 and the growing stock of trees outside forest is 1642 m3 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ad91cp0gp8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FSI, 2019, p. 117)","plainCitation":"(FSI, 2019, p. 117)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31410,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2K9YJGSJ"],"itemData":{"id":31410,"type":"report","publisher":"Deheradun, Forest Survey of India, Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change","title":"India State of Forest Report 2019","author":[{"family":"FSI","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"locator":"117","label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Total forest coverage between 2010 and 2020 increased in India by 0.38% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ce4hlp9vn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"ERpTB65M/5xO8ZqnJ","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A6FP6AF5"],"itemData":{"id":12535,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132974-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1195470916","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Global Forest Resources Assessment (FRA) 2020: main report","title-short":"GLOBAL FOREST RESOURCES ASSESSMENT (FRA) 2020","URL":"https://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8753en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Timber production from public forest meets only the 3.35% of the total demand, while trees from outside areas officially classified as forests provide 45% of the demand ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2kvlv8ircf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ghosh & Sinha, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Ghosh & Sinha, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1129,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MLDXFN5U"],"itemData":{"id":1129,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Natural Resources Forum","DOI":"10.1111/1477-8947.12094","ISSN":"01650203","issue":"1-2","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Resour Forum","language":"en","page":"62-76","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impact of forest policies on timber production in India: a review: Mili Ghosh and Bhaskar Sinha / Natural Resources Forum","title-short":"Impact of forest policies on timber production in India","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/1477-8947.12094","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Ghosh","given":"Mili"},{"family":"Sinha","given":"Bhaskar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is suggested that community forests managed by indigenous people and local communities are more likely to harvest in sustainable ways than those managed by the government ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Iumx86vy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sundar, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Sundar, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31216,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BGSP2BHW"],"itemData":{"id":31216,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/1465548822272329","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"495-511","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Joint forest management in India – an assessment","URL":"https://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/1465548822272329","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Sundar","given":"B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Despite government efforts to raise domestic productivity, India’s overall timber production remains low. This is especially true for the tree species preferred by consumers such as teak, sheesham and pine ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ae7qqbbsin","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Norman & Canby, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Norman & Canby, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31213,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X955ETQA"],"itemData":{"id":31213,"type":"book","publisher":"Forest Trends","title":"India's wooden furniture and wood handicrafts: risk of trade in illegally harvested woods","URL":"https://www.forest-trends.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/India_Report_FINAL.pdf","author":[{"family":"Norman","given":"Marigold"},{"family":"Canby","given":"Kerstin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The International Union of Forest Research Organizations estimates that India was the third-largest importer of illegally logged timber in the world in 2016, after China and Vietnam ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1mnhhopp5u","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kleinschmit, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Kleinschmit, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":16226,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZ2A8BTY","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FHXRX6U9"],"itemData":{"id":16226,"type":"book","collection-number":"35","collection-title":"IUFRO world series","event-place":"Vienna","ISBN":"978-3-902762-70-2","language":"en","number-of-pages":"145","publisher":"IUFRO","publisher-place":"Vienna","source":"K10plus ISBN","title":"Illegal logging and related timber trade: dimensions, drivers, impacts and responses ; a global scientific rapid response assessment report","title-short":"Illegal logging and related timber trade","editor":[{"family":"Kleinschmit","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Mansourian","given":"Stephanie"},{"family":"Wildburger","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Purret","given":"Andre"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While its own forests are under harvested, India is emerging as a major importer of timber ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a103479mg8o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vanam, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Vanam, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31219,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/46SAQ7BP"],"itemData":{"id":31219,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EPRA International Journal of Multidisciplinary Research (IJMR)","ISSN":"24553662","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"EPRA","page":"119-122","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Timber trade in India-challenges and policies","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Vanam","given":"B"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The demand for timber is growing from the current gross value added of 606 billion United States dollars in 2011 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ic95kfrfd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2014a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2014a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1294,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8HSFDJDP","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ANLFLBA4"],"itemData":{"id":1294,"type":"book","title":"State of the World‘s Forests Enhancing the socioeconomic benefits from forests","URL":"https://www.fao.org/3/i3710e/i3710e.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, restrictive policy and regulatory barriers inhibit community forestry groups from harvesting and selling surplus timber from their forests even when supported by sustainable forest harvesting protocols ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a26cssj2vsq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shyamsundar, Ahlroth, Kristjanson, & Onder, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Shyamsundar, Ahlroth, Kristjanson, & Onder, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1137,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8UBHGNFS"],"itemData":{"id":1137,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2019.104622","ISSN":"0305750X","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","language":"en","page":"104622","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Supporting pathways to prosperity in forest landscapes – A PRIME framework","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0305750X19302682","volume":"125","author":[{"family":"Shyamsundar","given":"Priya"},{"family":"Ahlroth","given":"Sofia"},{"family":"Kristjanson","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Onder","given":"Stefanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. India’s joint forest management is an arrangement for co-management between local communities and the Department of Forest. Typically, the joint forest management committee holds a joint account in the local public sector bank with the chairperson, vice-chairperson and the district forest officer or her nominee as joint signatories through which financial aid from donor and the government is channeled ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RLhGr4Ms","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sundar, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Sundar, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31216,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BGSP2BHW"],"itemData":{"id":31216,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/1465548822272329","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"495-511","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Joint forest management in India – an assessment","URL":"https://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/1465548822272329","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Sundar","given":"B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The district forest officer prepares forest management plans in consultation with the communities. 

3.3.4.3.3. Industrial Logging Practice

In practice there are three major types of industrial logging: (i) most frequently, so-called private concessions grounded on an agreement between a private landholder and the logger or by the landowner himself, mostly on a short-term basis and sizes of some few hundred to thousand hectares; (ii) government granted concessions in public forests (~1.5 M hectares by 2019) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1b13lh1fko","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. R. Ribeiro, Azevedo-Ramos, & Nascimento dos Santos, 2020)","plainCitation":"(J. R. Ribeiro, Azevedo-Ramos, & Nascimento dos Santos, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1102,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F75CPJ22"],"itemData":{"id":1102,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trees, Forests and People","DOI":"10.1016/j.tfp.2020.100021","ISSN":"26667193","journalAbbreviation":"Trees, Forests and People","language":"en","page":"100021","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impact of forest concessions on local jobs in central amazon","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2666719320300212","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"Jime Rodrigues"},{"family":"Azevedo-Ramos","given":"Claudia"},{"family":"Nascimento dos Santos","given":"Ricardo Bruno"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} based on a set of technical, financial, and administrative requirements, that comprise several ten thousand hectares for an entire timber-harvesting cycle; (iii) the legal use of timber from authorized forest conversion areas in private landholdings. Nearly all industrial logging is organized by sawmills to secure their supply. 
      Large-scale industrial logging involves felling large numbers of trees in areas of more than 50,000 ha. The loggers have felling permits, use heavy machinery, have a processing plant and sell a number of wood products including logs, sawnwood, veneer, plywood, and wooden floors, almost exclusively for export ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a17lg62ic79","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cerutti & Lescuyer, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Cerutti & Lescuyer, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FNHXP5NB"],"itemData":{"id":31288,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia.","ISBN":"978-602-8693-44-8","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 786191932","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia.","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"The domestic market for small-scale chainsaw milling in Cameroon: present situation, opportunities and challenges","title-short":"The domestic market for small-scale chainsaw milling in Cameroon","author":[{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the last three to four decades, industrial logging has been the major source of globally traded wood products (ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1xFIuKjl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2019a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2019a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":28646,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HSS6Y2MA"],"itemData":{"id":28646,"type":"report","language":"en","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","title":"Global forest products facts and figures 2018","URL":"https://www.fao.org/3/ca7415en/ca7415en.pdf","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). For example, in Papua New Guinea, large-scale industrial logging companies export approximately 90% of the logs harvested in the country ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a278bksjmpr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(PNGF, 2009)","plainCitation":"(PNGF, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31389,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DZBQYGEZ"],"itemData":{"id":31389,"type":"report","publisher":"Papua New Guinea Forest Authority","title":"PAPUA NEW GUINEA FORESTRY OUTLOOK STUDY.","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/am614e/am614e00.pdf","author":[{"family":"PNGF","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
	Large scale forestry operations occurring within managed forests and tree plantations were estimated to cover 26% of global forest area between 2001 and 2015 (mainly in America and Europe) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aenot0f1d6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Curtis, Slay, Harris, Tyukavina, & Hansen, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Curtis, Slay, Harris, Tyukavina, & Hansen, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31290,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HKH2Y84I"],"itemData":{"id":31290,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Global maps of forest loss depict the scale and magnitude of forest disturbance, yet companies, governments, and nongovernmental organizations need to distinguish permanent conversion (i.e., deforestation) from temporary loss from forestry or wildfire. Using satellite imagery, we developed a forest loss classification model to determine a spatial attribution of forest disturbance to the dominant drivers of land cover and land use change over the period 2001 to 2015. Our results indicate that 27% of global forest loss can be attributed to deforestation through permanent land use change for commodity production. The remaining areas maintained the same land use over 15 years; in those areas, loss was attributed to forestry (26%), shifting agriculture (24%), and wildfire (23%). Despite corporate commitments, the rate of commodity-driven deforestation has not declined. To end deforestation, companies must eliminate 5 million hectares of conversion from supply chains each year.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aau3445","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6407","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"1108-1111","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Classifying drivers of global forest loss","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.aau3445","volume":"361","author":[{"family":"Curtis","given":"Philip G."},{"family":"Slay","given":"Christy M."},{"family":"Harris","given":"Nancy L."},{"family":"Tyukavina","given":"Alexandra"},{"family":"Hansen","given":"Matthew C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",9,14]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Within some tropical countries, a small number of large-scale companies who source much of their timber from small and medium sized enterprises dominate the export sector ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a5k6lv0cj7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Osei-Tutu, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Osei-Tutu, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31289,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FBGBXV3E"],"itemData":{"id":31289,"type":"book","collection-number":"27","collection-title":"IIED small and medium forest enterprise series","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-84369-454-0","language":"eng","note":"OCLC: 837767006","number-of-pages":"72","publisher":"International Institute for Environment and Development","publisher-place":"London","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Hidden forestry revealed: characteristics, constraints and opportunities for small and medium forest enterprises in Ghana","title-short":"Hidden forestry revealed","author":[{"family":"Osei-Tutu","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Nketiah","given":"K"},{"family":"Kyereh","given":"B"},{"family":"Owusu-Ansah","given":"M"},{"family":"Faniyan","given":"J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Allocation of forests or trees for large scale industrial logging in public and large-scale privately owned forests is predominantly done through the provision of forest concessions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a4sespg33b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vilanova, Ram\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}rez-Angulo, Ram\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}rez, & Torres-Lezama, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Vilanova, Ramírez-Angulo, Ramírez, & Torres-Lezama, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31295,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MF83IPJV"],"itemData":{"id":31295,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2011.11.001","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"3-12","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Compliance with sustainable forest management guidelines in three timber concessions in the Venezuelan Guayana: Analysis and implications","title-short":"Compliance with sustainable forest management guidelines in three timber concessions in the Venezuelan Guayana","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934111001833","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Vilanova","given":"Emilio"},{"family":"Ramírez-Angulo","given":"Hirma"},{"family":"Ramírez","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Torres-Lezama","given":"Armando"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which is a common legal tool among forest policy decision-makers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a5vfe67fct","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Karsenty, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Karsenty, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31287,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T28AZ5ZC"],"itemData":{"id":31287,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2008.07.001","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"1498-1508","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Regulating industrial forest concessions in Central Africa and South America","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112708005471","volume":"256","author":[{"family":"Karsenty","given":"Alain"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Isabel Garcia"},{"family":"Piketty","given":"Marie-Gabrielle"},{"family":"Singer","given":"Benjamin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Forest concessions have been carried out for hundreds of years in boreal, temperate and tropical public forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2fnlltfapt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Van Hensbergen, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Van Hensbergen, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31283,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ATQLGHFX"],"itemData":{"id":31283,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome, Italy","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome, Italy","title":"Forest Concessions—Past Present and Future. Forestry and Institutions Working Paper, 36.","URL":"http://www.fao.org/forestry/45024-0c63724580ace381a8f8104cf24a3cff3.pdf","author":[{"family":"Van Hensbergen","given":"Berty"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Forest concessions have been carried out in many of the Central African forests for over a century (since the colonial rule), and in South American countries for over three decades ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a18l2tef17g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Karsenty, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Karsenty, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31287,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T28AZ5ZC"],"itemData":{"id":31287,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2008.07.001","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"1498-1508","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Regulating industrial forest concessions in Central Africa and South America","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112708005471","volume":"256","author":[{"family":"Karsenty","given":"Alain"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Isabel Garcia"},{"family":"Piketty","given":"Marie-Gabrielle"},{"family":"Singer","given":"Benjamin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Within Latin America, Southeast Asia and West & Central Africa, forest concessions cover about 123 million ha accounting for approximately 14% of the publicly owned forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1eb1oaela6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Van Hensbergen, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Van Hensbergen, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31283,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ATQLGHFX"],"itemData":{"id":31283,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome, Italy","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome, Italy","title":"Forest Concessions—Past Present and Future. Forestry and Institutions Working Paper, 36.","URL":"http://www.fao.org/forestry/45024-0c63724580ace381a8f8104cf24a3cff3.pdf","author":[{"family":"Van Hensbergen","given":"Berty"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.   
	Conventional logging is highly selective, sometimes concentrating on only one or two species. Selecting and felling trees often occurs without a complete inventory or thorough spatial planning. In large-scale conventional logging there may be issues with incorrect identification, poor labor conditions, insufficient training in best practices, and overly high felling rates. These conditions can lead to immense damage and economic losses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2dbgsr0i01","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Piponiot, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Piponiot, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1140,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I47YJCMY"],"itemData":{"id":1140,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Research Letters","DOI":"10.1088/1748-9326/ab195e","ISSN":"1748-9326","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Environ. Res. Lett.","page":"064014","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Can timber provision from Amazonian production forests be sustainable?","URL":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/ab195e","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Piponiot","given":"Camille"},{"family":"Rödig","given":"Edna"},{"family":"Putz","given":"Francis E"},{"family":"Rutishauser","given":"Ervan"},{"family":"Sist","given":"Plinio"},{"family":"Ascarrunz","given":"Nataly"},{"family":"Blanc","given":"Lilian"},{"family":"Derroire","given":"Géraldine"},{"family":"Descroix","given":"Laurent"},{"family":"Guedes","given":"Marcelino Carneiro"},{"family":"Coronado","given":"Euridice Honorio"},{"family":"Huth","given":"Andreas"},{"family":"Kanashiro","given":"Milton"},{"family":"Licona","given":"Juan Carlos"},{"family":"Mazzei","given":"Lucas"},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"Marcus Vinicio Neves","non-dropping-particle":"d’"},{"family":"Peña-Claros","given":"Marielos"},{"family":"Rodney","given":"Ken"},{"family":"Shenkin","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Souza","given":"Cintia Rodrigues","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Vidal","given":"Edson"},{"family":"West","given":"Thales A P"},{"family":"Wortel","given":"Verginia"},{"family":"Hérault","given":"Bruno"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6,12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Since the 1950s, clear-cutting involves the use of heavy timber machinery ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a24trh21i68","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Boucher, Auger, No\\\\uc0\\\\u235{}l, Grondin, & Arseneault, 2017; Maleki, Nguema Allogo, & Lafleur, 2020; Mohr, Coppus, Iroum\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, Huber, & Bronstert, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Boucher, Auger, Noël, Grondin, & Arseneault, 2017; Maleki, Nguema Allogo, & Lafleur, 2020; Mohr, Coppus, Iroumé, Huber, & Bronstert, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31294,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4T3P47P4"],"itemData":{"id":31294,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Vegetation Science","DOI":"10.1111/jvs.12466","ISSN":"11009233","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Veg Sci","language":"en","page":"57-68","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fire is a stronger driver of forest composition than logging in the boreal forest of eastern Canada","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/jvs.12466","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Boucher","given":"Yan"},{"family":"Auger","given":"Isabelle"},{"family":"Noël","given":"Jean"},{"family":"Grondin","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Arseneault","given":"Dominique"}],"editor":[{"family":"Collins","given":"Beverly"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1142,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z4PA4WKM"],"itemData":{"id":1142,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Over the last three decades, the ecological basis for the generalized use of even-aged silviculture in boreal forests has been increasingly challenged. In boreal mixed-wood landscapes, the diminishing proportion of conifers, to the benefit of intolerant hardwoods, has been a primary concern, coupled with the general rarefication of old-growth conifer-dominated stands. In this context, partial cutting, extended rotations and forest renewal techniques that eliminate or reduce regenerating hardwoods have been proposed as means of regaining greater conifer cover. As a result, experimentation and industrial application of various forms of both variable retention and partial harvesting are occurring across the commercial Canadian boreal forest. In this study, we compared the effects of two harvesting intensities, clearcutting and low-intensity partial cutting (removal of 25–31% of tree basal area), on hardwood and conifer regeneration levels 7–19 years following treatments in aspen (Populus tremuloides)-dominated stands and verified whether regeneration differences existed between micro-sites on and off machinery trails. The abundance of aspen regeneration increased with percent basal area removal and was positively correlated to the abundance of mature aspen prior to harvesting. The abundance of fir (Abies balsamea) regeneration after partial cutting was similar to controls and higher than after clear-cutting and was positively correlated with ground cover of mixed litter (i.e., mixture of needles and leaves) and negatively correlated with ground cover of broadleaf litter. These results suggest that it is possible in boreal mixed-woods to control aspen abundance and promote or maintain conifer regeneration through silvicultural treatments that limit canopy opening and promote mixed forest floor litter.","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f11070741","ISSN":"1999-4907","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Forests","language":"en","page":"741","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Natural Regeneration Following Partial and Clear-Cut Harvesting in Mature Aspen-Jack Pine Stands in Eastern Canada","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/1999-4907/11/7/741","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Maleki","given":"Kobra"},{"family":"Nguema Allogo","given":"Freddy"},{"family":"Lafleur","given":"Benoit"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,8]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31302,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9YS68UEA"],"itemData":{"id":31302,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Geophysical Research: Earth Surface","DOI":"10.1002/jgrf.20047","ISSN":"21699003","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"J. Geophys. Res. Earth Surf.","language":"en","page":"814-831","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Runoff generation and soil erosion processes after clear cutting","title-short":"Runoff generation and soil erosion processes after clear cutting","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/jgrf.20047","volume":"118","author":[{"family":"Mohr","given":"Christian H."},{"family":"Coppus","given":"Ruben"},{"family":"Iroumé","given":"Andrés"},{"family":"Huber","given":"Anton"},{"family":"Bronstert","given":"Axel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",6]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which may lead to changes in tree composition and oversimplification of stand structure and species diversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"as9j1gghvu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Boucher, Arseneault, Sirois, & Blais, 2009; Boucher, 2017; Lena Gustafsson, Kouki, & Sverdrup-Thygeson, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Boucher, Arseneault, Sirois, & Blais, 2009; Boucher, 2017; Lena Gustafsson, Kouki, & Sverdrup-Thygeson, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31293,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GDAT6MR4"],"itemData":{"id":31293,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10980-008-9294-8","ISSN":"0921-2973, 1572-9761","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape Ecol","language":"en","page":"171-184","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Logging pattern and landscape changes over the last century at the boreal and deciduous forest transition in Eastern Canada","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10980-008-9294-8","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Boucher","given":"Yan"},{"family":"Arseneault","given":"Dominique"},{"family":"Sirois","given":"Luc"},{"family":"Blais","given":"Louis"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",2]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31294,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4T3P47P4"],"itemData":{"id":31294,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Vegetation Science","DOI":"10.1111/jvs.12466","ISSN":"11009233","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Veg Sci","language":"en","page":"57-68","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fire is a stronger driver of forest composition than logging in the boreal forest of eastern Canada","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/jvs.12466","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Boucher","given":"Yan"},{"family":"Auger","given":"Isabelle"},{"family":"Noël","given":"Jean"},{"family":"Grondin","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Arseneault","given":"Dominique"}],"editor":[{"family":"Collins","given":"Beverly"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31268,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HMGHGZ5Q"],"itemData":{"id":31268,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1080/02827581.2010.497495","ISSN":"0282-7581, 1651-1891","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","language":"en","page":"295-308","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Tree retention as a conservation measure in clear-cut forests of northern Europe: a review of ecological consequences","title-short":"Tree retention as a conservation measure in clear-cut forests of northern Europe","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/02827581.2010.497495","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Gustafsson","given":"Lena"},{"family":"Kouki","given":"Jari"},{"family":"Sverdrup-Thygeson","given":"Anne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",8]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These activities can have negative effects on wild plant and animal populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"alvaql67if","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berg, 1994; G\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}rdenfors, 2010; Hyv\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}rinen, Jusl\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}n, Kemppainen, Uddstr\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}m, & Liukko, 2019; K\\\\uc0\\\\u229{}l\\\\uc0\\\\u229{}s, Viken, Henriksen, & Skjelseth, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Berg, 1994; Gärdenfors, 2010; Hyvärinen, Juslén, Kemppainen, Uddström, & Liukko, 2019; Kålås, Viken, Henriksen, & Skjelseth, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31266,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IVSDMDL4"],"itemData":{"id":31266,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1046/j.1523-1739.1994.08030718.x","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"718-731","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Threatened Plant, Animal, and Fungus Species in Swedish Forests: Distribution and Habitat Associations","title-short":"Threatened Plant, Animal, and Fungus Species in Swedish Forests","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1046/j.1523-1739.1994.08030718.x","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Berg","given":"Ake"},{"family":"Ehnstrom","given":"Beng"},{"family":"Gustafsson","given":"Lena"},{"family":"Hallingback","given":"Tomas"},{"family":"Jonsell","given":"Mats"},{"family":"Weslien","given":"Jan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994",9]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31267,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BGN43VZ7"],"itemData":{"id":31267,"type":"book","event-place":"Uppsala","ISBN":"978-91-88506-35-1","language":"Parallel text in Swedish and English.","note":"OCLC: 764569119","publisher":"ArtDatabanken-SLU i samarbete med Naturvårdsverket","publisher-place":"Uppsala","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Rödlistade arter i Sverige 2010 = The 2010 red list of Swedish species","author":[{"family":"Gärdenfors","given":"Ulf"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31261,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BWVTQE9R"],"itemData":{"id":31261,"type":"book","event-place":"Helsinki","number-of-pages":"704","publisher":"Ympäristöministeriö & Suomen ympäristökeskus","publisher-place":"Helsinki","title":"Suomen lajien uhanalaisuus 2019-Punainen kirja: The 2019 Red List of Finnish Species.","author":[{"family":"Hyvärinen","given":"Esko"},{"family":"Juslén","given":"Aino Kaisa"},{"family":"Kemppainen","given":"Eija"},{"family":"Uddström","given":"Annika"},{"family":"Liukko","given":"Ulla-Maija"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31265,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y6N4STVW"],"itemData":{"id":31265,"type":"book","event-place":"Trondheim","ISBN":"978-82-92838-26-6","language":"Text engl. und norweg.","note":"OCLC: 918259516","publisher":"Artsdatabanken","publisher-place":"Trondheim","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Norsk rødliste for arter 2010 = The 2010 Norwegian red list for species","author":[{"family":"Kålås","given":"John Atle"},{"family":"Viken","given":"Åslaug"},{"family":"Henriksen","given":"Snorre"},{"family":"Skjelseth","given":"Sigrun"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Log skidding, done with heavy machinery, can also be damaging if done during improper weather conditions or during the wrong season when soils are especially vulnerable. 
	Industrial logging is done with and without legally authorized management plans. A correct tracing of the logs to their point of origin varies widely so loggers may use management plans to justify harvesting adjunct areas technically not under the plan. The vast majority of sawmills in the tropics work with this kind of timber-harvesting. They have small teams of mostly non-local seasonal workers and use tractors for both the construction of access roads, secondary roads, and landings as well as for the skidding ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1eiq61l31k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pokorny & Steinbrenner, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Pokorny & Steinbrenner, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31243,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FD3JHGM6"],"itemData":{"id":31243,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Timber companies and policy makers in the Brazilian Amazon urgently need financial information on forest management. Results from a few experiments, case studies, and surveys have been groundbreaking, but are insufficient. A strategic partnership between timber companies and research organizations is needed to generate additional information. This paper presents a tool for monitoring production and costs of forest operations to facilitate such collaboration. The tool provides useful information for companies and, at the same time, generates reliable data for research. Selected results are presented on production, capacity, and costs to demonstrate the usefulness of the information that can be generated. These results are based on the first 2 years of implementation by a company in the State of Pará, Brazil. This pilot project confirmed that the tool is simple and relevant. Its successful implementation requires significant investment, and will be applicable only to companies interested in changing from conventional logging to reduced-impact logging, especially those seeking Forest Stewardship Council certification. Successful implementation of the tool will also depend on it generating readily understood and highly relevant results for the companies, and receiving extensive support during the first 2 years.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-01224-100103","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art3","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Collaborative Monitoring of Production and Costs of Timber Harvest Operations in the Brazilian Amazon","URL":"http://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol10/iss1/art3/","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Pokorny","given":"Benno"},{"family":"Steinbrenner","given":"Max"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Larger companies may also use skidders and stackers for loading. Sometimes machinery is owned by the sawmill, sometimes services are subcontracted. Transport distances from the forest to the sawmill may reach up to nearly 100 km ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3NLk7m7T","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pokorny & Steinbrenner, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Pokorny & Steinbrenner, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31243,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FD3JHGM6"],"itemData":{"id":31243,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Timber companies and policy makers in the Brazilian Amazon urgently need financial information on forest management. Results from a few experiments, case studies, and surveys have been groundbreaking, but are insufficient. A strategic partnership between timber companies and research organizations is needed to generate additional information. This paper presents a tool for monitoring production and costs of forest operations to facilitate such collaboration. The tool provides useful information for companies and, at the same time, generates reliable data for research. Selected results are presented on production, capacity, and costs to demonstrate the usefulness of the information that can be generated. These results are based on the first 2 years of implementation by a company in the State of Pará, Brazil. This pilot project confirmed that the tool is simple and relevant. Its successful implementation requires significant investment, and will be applicable only to companies interested in changing from conventional logging to reduced-impact logging, especially those seeking Forest Stewardship Council certification. Successful implementation of the tool will also depend on it generating readily understood and highly relevant results for the companies, and receiving extensive support during the first 2 years.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-01224-100103","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art3","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Collaborative Monitoring of Production and Costs of Timber Harvest Operations in the Brazilian Amazon","URL":"http://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol10/iss1/art3/","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Pokorny","given":"Benno"},{"family":"Steinbrenner","given":"Max"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, if the distance becomes too large, the saw lines are dismantled and rebuilt closer to the forest. 
      Alternative “sustainable forest management” schemes are meant to ameliorate several of the concerns raised regarding species identification, spatial planning, proper use of equipment, and proper application of management plans. More sustainable forest management is well planned so as to minimize damage on the remaining stand while effectively making use of costly heavy machinery. This includes infrastructure planning, spatial planning of harvesting operations, harvest planning based on an inventory of all commercially valuable trees, and skid-trail planning ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"afds43jri","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. E. Putz, 2012)","plainCitation":"(F. E. Putz, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31257,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LKYRNHVA"],"itemData":{"id":31257,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/j.1755-263X.2012.00242.x","ISSN":"1755263X","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"296-303","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Sustaining conservation values in selectively logged tropical forests: the attained and the attainable: Sustaining tropical forests with forestry","title-short":"Sustaining conservation values in selectively logged tropical forests","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1755-263X.2012.00242.x","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Putz","given":"Francis E."},{"family":"Zuidema","given":"Pieter A."},{"family":"Synnott","given":"Timothy"},{"family":"Peña-Claros","given":"Marielos"},{"family":"Pinard","given":"Michelle A."},{"family":"Sheil","given":"Douglas"},{"family":"Vanclay","given":"Jerome K."},{"family":"Sist","given":"Plinio"},{"family":"Gourlet-Fleury","given":"Sylvie"},{"family":"Griscom","given":"Bronson"},{"family":"Palmer","given":"John"},{"family":"Zagt","given":"Roderick"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In tropical and subtropical regions such harvesting is done by larger companies with the necessary human and financial resources and engaged in export activities often linked to Forest Stewardship Council (FSC) certification ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hFh2Cfbe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pokorny & Steinbrenner, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Pokorny & Steinbrenner, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31243,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FD3JHGM6"],"itemData":{"id":31243,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Timber companies and policy makers in the Brazilian Amazon urgently need financial information on forest management. Results from a few experiments, case studies, and surveys have been groundbreaking, but are insufficient. A strategic partnership between timber companies and research organizations is needed to generate additional information. This paper presents a tool for monitoring production and costs of forest operations to facilitate such collaboration. The tool provides useful information for companies and, at the same time, generates reliable data for research. Selected results are presented on production, capacity, and costs to demonstrate the usefulness of the information that can be generated. These results are based on the first 2 years of implementation by a company in the State of Pará, Brazil. This pilot project confirmed that the tool is simple and relevant. Its successful implementation requires significant investment, and will be applicable only to companies interested in changing from conventional logging to reduced-impact logging, especially those seeking Forest Stewardship Council certification. Successful implementation of the tool will also depend on it generating readily understood and highly relevant results for the companies, and receiving extensive support during the first 2 years.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-01224-100103","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art3","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Collaborative Monitoring of Production and Costs of Timber Harvest Operations in the Brazilian Amazon","URL":"http://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol10/iss1/art3/","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Pokorny","given":"Benno"},{"family":"Steinbrenner","given":"Max"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Certification requires the demarcation of protected areas, and the timber-harvesting of a wider range of tree species, including the ones with lower commercial value, so as to reduce the pressure on the most valuable tree species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2b3tjj6hgs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. E. Putz, 2008)","plainCitation":"(F. E. Putz, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31264,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R3GAA65W"],"itemData":{"id":31264,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2008.03.036","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"1427-1433","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Reduced-impact logging: Challenges and opportunities","title-short":"Reduced-impact logging","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112708002892","volume":"256","author":[{"family":"Putz","given":"Francis E."},{"family":"Sist","given":"P."},{"family":"Fredericksen","given":"T."},{"family":"Dykstra","given":"D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The engagement of the certifier has positive effects on the quality of the operation, the treatment of the workers and the local resource users living around the management unit. However, certified companies tend to work as enclaves in the forest landscape and prefer to work with non-local workers. They prioritize fast timber-harvesting and hesitate to invest in the long-term security of the management unit once the area has been logged. These practices place the long-term sustainability of these certified operations in question.
	Since the 1980s, variable retention forestry is being promoted as a sustainable forest management practice in temperate and boreal forests as opposed to clear-cutting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"akmcrtv9d6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fedrowitz, 2014; Franklin, Berg, Thornburgh, & Tappeiner, 1997; Harkema & Scott, 2002; Kuuluvainen & Grenfell, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Fedrowitz, 2014; Franklin, Berg, Thornburgh, & Tappeiner, 1997; Harkema & Scott, 2002; Kuuluvainen & Grenfell, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1133,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XHMDXW6M"],"itemData":{"id":1133,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/1365-2664.12289","ISSN":"0021-8901, 1365-2664","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Ecol","language":"en","page":"1669-1679","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"REVIEW: Can retention forestry help conserve biodiversity? A meta?analysis","title-short":"REVIEW","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/1365-2664.12289","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Fedrowitz","given":"Katja"},{"family":"Koricheva","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Baker","given":"Susan C."},{"family":"Lindenmayer","given":"David B."},{"family":"Palik","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Rosenvald","given":"Raul"},{"family":"Beese","given":"William"},{"family":"Franklin","given":"Jerry F."},{"family":"Kouki","given":"Jari"},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"Ellen"},{"family":"Messier","given":"Christian"},{"family":"Sverdrup?Thygeson","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Gustafsson","given":"Lena"}],"editor":[{"family":"Baraloto","given":"Christopher"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31392,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RJDTHS5A"],"itemData":{"id":31392,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Creating a forestry for the 21st century: the science of ecosystem management","event-place":"Washington DC","page":"111-139","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington DC","title":"Alternative silvicultural approaches to timber harvest: variable retention harvest systems. Pages 111–139 in K.A. Kohm & J.F. Franklin, editors. Creating a forestry for the 21st century . Island Press, Covelo , California","author":[{"family":"Franklin","given":"Jerry F"},{"family":"Berg","given":"Dean E"},{"family":"Thornburgh","given":"Dale A."},{"family":"Tappeiner","given":"John C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31269,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S7GE2QAR"],"itemData":{"id":31269,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ministry of Forests, Forest Practices Branch. British Columbia","title":"The Retention Systemt: Maintaining Forest Ecosystem Diversity.","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Harkema","given":"J"},{"family":"Scott","given":"M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31388,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8JVEFJAC"],"itemData":{"id":31388,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Natural disturbance emulation (NDE) has been proposed as a general approach to ecologically sustainable forest management. We reviewed the concepts, theories, and strategies related to NDE in boreal forest management. We also reviewed publications that discussed NDE in the boreal forest in general and those that specifically compared NDE-based management with conventional even-aged management. The papers generally focused on northern North America and landscape-scale wildfire as the main disturbance factor, whereas information from Eurasia was exclusively theoretical. Within this limited scope, NDE was generally found to have a positive effect on biodiversity in terms of forest structure and species diversity when compared with conventional even-aged management. Studies on timber supply and social implications of NDE were so few that they preclude generalizations. We conclude that the ecological and economic performance of NDE as a management approach still remains poorly examined. To advance the development of NDE, particular attention should be given to (1) augmenting the knowledge base on natural range of variability of unmanaged forest ecosystems and evaluating the validity of this information in a changing climate, (2) fostering multidisciplinary research with better integration of ecological theory to both integrative and analytical research on NDE, and (3) better integration of socioeconomic concerns, adaptive management schemes, and international collaboration into NDE initiatives.","container-title":"Canadian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1139/x2012-064","ISSN":"0045-5067, 1208-6037","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Can. J. For. Res.","language":"en","page":"1185-1203","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Natural disturbance emulation in boreal forest ecosystem management — theories, strategies, and a comparison with conventional even-aged management <sup>1</sup> This article is one of a selection of papers from the 7th International Conference on Disturbance Dynamics in Boreal Forests.","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/x2012-064","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Kuuluvainen","given":"Timo"},{"family":"Grenfell","given":"Russell"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",7]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is a system in which key structural components of the original stand are retained at the time of logging through selection cutting, gap cutting and modifications of clear cutting, and become part of a new stand that regrows after logging ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2hfr2n7rat","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Franklin, 1997; Lena Gustafsson, 2010; Koivula, 2014; Koivula, Silvennoinen, Koivula, Tikkanen, & Tyrv\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}inen, 2020; Puettmann, Messier, & Coates, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Franklin, 1997; Lena Gustafsson, 2010; Koivula, 2014; Koivula, Silvennoinen, Koivula, Tikkanen, & Tyrväinen, 2020; Puettmann, Messier, & Coates, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31392,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RJDTHS5A"],"itemData":{"id":31392,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Creating a forestry for the 21st century: the science of ecosystem management","event-place":"Washington DC","page":"111-139","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington DC","title":"Alternative silvicultural approaches to timber harvest: variable retention harvest systems. Pages 111–139 in K.A. Kohm & J.F. Franklin, editors. Creating a forestry for the 21st century . Island Press, Covelo , California","author":[{"family":"Franklin","given":"Jerry F"},{"family":"Berg","given":"Dean E"},{"family":"Thornburgh","given":"Dale A."},{"family":"Tappeiner","given":"John C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31268,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HMGHGZ5Q"],"itemData":{"id":31268,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1080/02827581.2010.497495","ISSN":"0282-7581, 1651-1891","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","language":"en","page":"295-308","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Tree retention as a conservation measure in clear-cut forests of northern Europe: a review of ecological consequences","title-short":"Tree retention as a conservation measure in clear-cut forests of northern Europe","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/02827581.2010.497495","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Gustafsson","given":"Lena"},{"family":"Kouki","given":"Jari"},{"family":"Sverdrup-Thygeson","given":"Anne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",8]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31390,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LBZSJU73"],"itemData":{"id":31390,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1080/02827581.2014.938110","ISSN":"0282-7581, 1651-1891","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","language":"en","page":"579-592","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forest management inspired by natural disturbance dynamics (DISTDYN) – a long-term research and development project in Finland","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/02827581.2014.938110","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Koivula","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Kuuluvainen","given":"Timo"},{"family":"Hallman","given":"Erkki"},{"family":"Kouki","given":"Jari"},{"family":"Siitonen","given":"Juha"},{"family":"Valkonen","given":"Sauli"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",8,18]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31296,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5I5WRG77"],"itemData":{"id":31296,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Forest management, characterized in many northern countries by the predominance of clear cutting and growing even-aged and -sized trees, has simplified the structure of boreal forests. Consequences include alterations in cultural ecosystem services such as forest attractiveness, i.e., combined aesthetic and recreational values. Continuous-cover forestry might mitigate these effects through the use of selection and gap cutting, but these methods have been little studied, particularly from the attractiveness viewpoint. We used photo surveys to assess Finnish citizens’ perceptions of attractiveness of in-stand scenery of Scots pine (Pinus sylvestris L.) forests logged using different methods. (i) The attractiveness scores, given by respondents, declined steadily from unharvested forest through continuous-cover methods to seed-tree and clear-cut methods. (ii) Respondents with a negative attitude to forest management gave lower scores than respondents with a positive attitude, but the declining slopes of attractiveness against logging intensity were similar. (iii) In unharvested and less intensively managed stands, summer photos received higher scores than corresponding winter photos. (iv) Background variables (gender, education, living environment, memberships in recreational or nature NGOs, forestry profession, and forest ownership) had negligible effects on the scores. We recommend the use of continuous-cover logging methods in settlement and recreational areas.","container-title":"Canadian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1139/cjfr-2019-0431","ISSN":"0045-5067, 1208-6037","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Can. J. For. Res.","language":"en","page":"819-828","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Continuous-cover management and attractiveness of managed Scots pine forests","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/cjfr-2019-0431","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Koivula","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Silvennoinen","given":"Harri"},{"family":"Koivula","given":"Hanna"},{"family":"Tikkanen","given":"Jukka"},{"family":"Tyrväinen","given":"Liisa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31391,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5NGX2QEN"],"itemData":{"id":31391,"type":"book","call-number":"QH353 .D38 2009","event-place":"Washington D.C.","ISBN":"978-0-19-921875-2","language":"en","note":"OCLC: 234430671","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington D.C.","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"A Critique of Silviculture: Managing For Complexity","author":[{"family":"Puettmann","given":"Klaus"},{"family":"Messier","given":"Christian"},{"family":"Coates","given":"K. D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Emerging research reveals that tree retention has the potential to reduce impacts of logging on forest biodiversity through creating favourable conditions that allow for complex and uneven forest structures similar to those of natural forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2iofmt69jn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lena Gustafsson, 2020; Moussaoui, Leduc, Fenton, Lafleur, & Bergeron, 2019; Opoku-Nyame, Leduc, & Fenton, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Lena Gustafsson, 2020; Moussaoui, Leduc, Fenton, Lafleur, & Bergeron, 2019; Opoku-Nyame, Leduc, & Fenton, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CT8UIWCJ"],"itemData":{"id":31383,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              Retention approaches in forest management are today common in several North European countries, integrated into the clearcutting practice as a way to promote biodiversity and maintain ecosystem functions. Individual green trees and retention patches (tree groups) are retained at final harvest, and deadwood is left at site or created. Here, we review research on retention in Sweden, Finland, Norway, the Baltic States, and NW Russia, with special focus on biodiversity. Following the first publication in 1994, about 180 peer-reviewed articles have been published. We present results from a systematic search of the retention literature, separated into the following topics: buffer zones, retention patches, high stumps, other types of deadwood, European aspen\\n              Populus tremula\\n              , and cost-efficiency. Russian literature is synthesized separately since studies from this region have so far almost exclusively been published in the Russian language. Furthermore, we describe six ongoing large-scale, replicated experiments with varying retention levels, five in Finland and one in Sweden, and summarize their main results. Among main conclusions for practice from the literature and experiments are that retention patches as large as 0.5 ha and 10-m-wide buffers to watercourses are not enough to maintain pre-harvest species composition but survival of forest species is still larger than on conventional clearcuts. Deadwood on clearcuts may present important habitats to saproxylic species, including rare and red-listed ones and a prioritization of tree species per stand is recommended. We identify several important future research directions including switch of focus towards the landscape as well as the species population level. Surveys in parts of European Russia where retention has been unintentionally implemented already for a century would indicate possible future trajectories of biodiversity and their drivers in other regions of Northern Europe. A stronger link to ecological theory would help in study designs and in the formulation of predicted outcomes.","container-title":"Ecological Processes","DOI":"10.1186/s13717-019-0208-2","ISSN":"2192-1709","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Process","language":"en","page":"3","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Research on retention forestry in Northern Europe","URL":"https://ecologicalprocesses.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s13717-019-0208-2","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Gustafsson","given":"Lena"},{"family":"Hannerz","given":"Mats"},{"family":"Koivula","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Shorohova","given":"Ekaterina"},{"family":"Vanha-Majamaa","given":"Ilkka"},{"family":"Weslien","given":"Jan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31385,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2P83TRDM"],"itemData":{"id":31385,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2018.09.059","ISSN":"1470160X","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Indicators","language":"en","page":"89-99","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Changes in forest structure along a chronosequence in the black spruce boreal forest: Identifying structures to be reproduced through silvicultural practices","title-short":"Changes in forest structure along a chronosequence in the black spruce boreal forest","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1470160X1830760X","volume":"97","author":[{"family":"Moussaoui","given":"Louiza"},{"family":"Leduc","given":"Alain"},{"family":"Fenton","given":"Nicole J."},{"family":"Lafleur","given":"Benoit"},{"family":"Bergeron","given":"Yves"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",2]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31384,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5KZYITQV"],"itemData":{"id":31384,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Clear cut harvest simplifies and eliminates old growth forest structure, negatively impacting biodiversity. Partial cut harvest has been hypothesized (1) to have less impact on biodiversity than clear cut harvest, and (2) to encourage old growth forest structures. Long-term studies are required to test this hypothesis as most studies are conducted soon after harvest. Using epixylic bryophytes as indicators, this study addresses this knowledge gap. Fourteen years after harvest, we examined changes in epixylic bryophyte community composition richness and traits, and their microhabitats (coarse woody debris characteristics and microclimate) along an unharvested, partial cuts and clear cuts harvest treatment in 30 permanent plots established in the boreal black spruce (\\n              Picea mariana\\n              ) forests of northwestern Quebec, Canada. Our results were compared to those of an initial post-harvest study (year 5) and to a chronosequence of old growth forests to examine species changes over time and the similarity of bryophyte communities in partial cut and old growth forests. Coarse woody debris (CWD) volume by decay class varied among harvest treatments with partial cuts and clear cuts recording lower volumes of early decay CWD. The epixylic community was richer in partial cuts than in mature unharvested forests and clear cuts. In addition, species richness and overall abundance doubled in partial and clear cuts between years 5 and 14. Species composition also differed among treatments between years 5 and 14. Furthermore, conditions in partial cut stands supported small, drought sensitive, and old growth confined species that are threatened by conditions in clear cut stands. Lastly, over time, species composition in partial cuts became more similar to old growth forests. Partial cuts reduced harvest impacts by continuing to provide favorable microhabitat conditions that support epixylic bryophytes. Also, partial cut harvest has the potential to encourage old growth species assemblages, which has been a major concern for biodiversity conservation in managed forest landscapes. Our findings support the promotion of partial cut harvest as an effective strategy to achieve species and habitat conservation goals.","container-title":"Frontiers in Forests and Global Change","DOI":"10.3389/ffgc.2021.674887","ISSN":"2624-893X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. For. Glob. Change","page":"674887","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Bryophyte Conservation in Managed Boreal Landscapes: Fourteen-Year Impacts of Partial Cuts on Epixylic Bryophytes","title-short":"Bryophyte Conservation in Managed Boreal Landscapes","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/ffgc.2021.674887/full","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Opoku-Nyame","given":"Jeffrey"},{"family":"Leduc","given":"Alain"},{"family":"Fenton","given":"Nicole J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",6,3]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The practice is being adopted with rather modest retention levels ranging from 30 to 40% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1cr1s5oq0h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Beese, Deal, Dunsworth, Mitchell, & Philpott, 2019; R. E. Scott, Neyland, & Baker, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Beese, Deal, Dunsworth, Mitchell, & Philpott, 2019; R. E. Scott, Neyland, & Baker, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1132,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TGUGZAAT"],"itemData":{"id":1132,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Processes","DOI":"10.1186/s13717-019-0181-9","ISSN":"2192-1709","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Process","language":"en","page":"33","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Two decades of variable retention in British Columbia: a review of its implementation and effectiveness for biodiversity conservation","title-short":"Two decades of variable retention in British Columbia","URL":"https://ecologicalprocesses.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s13717-019-0181-9","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Beese","given":"William J."},{"family":"Deal","given":"John"},{"family":"Dunsworth","given":"B. Glen"},{"family":"Mitchell","given":"Stephen J."},{"family":"Philpott","given":"Timothy J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32310,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4TW5SJEU"],"itemData":{"id":32310,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Processes","DOI":"10.1186/s13717-019-0174-8","ISSN":"2192-1709","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Process","language":"en","page":"23","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Variable retention in Tasmania, Australia: trends over 16 years of monitoring and adaptive management","title-short":"Variable retention in Tasmania, Australia","URL":"https://ecologicalprocesses.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s13717-019-0174-8","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Scott","given":"Robyn E."},{"family":"Neyland","given":"Mark G."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Susan C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Though retaining small amounts of trees or patches is better than traditional clearfelling ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2j25avtc8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lena Gustafsson, 2020; Koivula & Vanha-Majamaa, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Lena Gustafsson, 2020; Koivula & Vanha-Majamaa, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CT8UIWCJ"],"itemData":{"id":31383,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              Retention approaches in forest management are today common in several North European countries, integrated into the clearcutting practice as a way to promote biodiversity and maintain ecosystem functions. Individual green trees and retention patches (tree groups) are retained at final harvest, and deadwood is left at site or created. Here, we review research on retention in Sweden, Finland, Norway, the Baltic States, and NW Russia, with special focus on biodiversity. Following the first publication in 1994, about 180 peer-reviewed articles have been published. We present results from a systematic search of the retention literature, separated into the following topics: buffer zones, retention patches, high stumps, other types of deadwood, European aspen\\n              Populus tremula\\n              , and cost-efficiency. Russian literature is synthesized separately since studies from this region have so far almost exclusively been published in the Russian language. Furthermore, we describe six ongoing large-scale, replicated experiments with varying retention levels, five in Finland and one in Sweden, and summarize their main results. Among main conclusions for practice from the literature and experiments are that retention patches as large as 0.5 ha and 10-m-wide buffers to watercourses are not enough to maintain pre-harvest species composition but survival of forest species is still larger than on conventional clearcuts. Deadwood on clearcuts may present important habitats to saproxylic species, including rare and red-listed ones and a prioritization of tree species per stand is recommended. We identify several important future research directions including switch of focus towards the landscape as well as the species population level. Surveys in parts of European Russia where retention has been unintentionally implemented already for a century would indicate possible future trajectories of biodiversity and their drivers in other regions of Northern Europe. A stronger link to ecological theory would help in study designs and in the formulation of predicted outcomes.","container-title":"Ecological Processes","DOI":"10.1186/s13717-019-0208-2","ISSN":"2192-1709","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Process","language":"en","page":"3","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Research on retention forestry in Northern Europe","URL":"https://ecologicalprocesses.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s13717-019-0208-2","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Gustafsson","given":"Lena"},{"family":"Hannerz","given":"Mats"},{"family":"Koivula","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Shorohova","given":"Ekaterina"},{"family":"Vanha-Majamaa","given":"Ilkka"},{"family":"Weslien","given":"Jan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31291,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5SEVK8H3"],"itemData":{"id":31291,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Processes","DOI":"10.1186/s13717-019-0209-1","ISSN":"2192-1709","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Process","language":"en","page":"11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Experimental evidence on biodiversity impacts of variable retention forestry, prescribed burning, and deadwood manipulation in Fennoscandia","URL":"https://ecologicalprocesses.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s13717-019-0209-1","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Koivula","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Vanha-Majamaa","given":"Ilkka"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, only retaining a minor proportion of the volume of harvestable timber (often 1-10%) makes it practically impossible to avoid edge effects and random demographic effects on the forest stands. Maintaining more of the mature forest characteristics in production forests would require lower harvest intensities in some areas than is currently typical. Therefore, this low level of retention is still considered by some scholars as clear-felling ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ph02rjgks","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fedrowitz, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Fedrowitz, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1133,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XHMDXW6M"],"itemData":{"id":1133,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/1365-2664.12289","ISSN":"0021-8901, 1365-2664","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Ecol","language":"en","page":"1669-1679","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"REVIEW: Can retention forestry help conserve biodiversity? A meta?analysis","title-short":"REVIEW","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/1365-2664.12289","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Fedrowitz","given":"Katja"},{"family":"Koricheva","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Baker","given":"Susan C."},{"family":"Lindenmayer","given":"David B."},{"family":"Palik","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Rosenvald","given":"Raul"},{"family":"Beese","given":"William"},{"family":"Franklin","given":"Jerry F."},{"family":"Kouki","given":"Jari"},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"Ellen"},{"family":"Messier","given":"Christian"},{"family":"Sverdrup?Thygeson","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Gustafsson","given":"Lena"}],"editor":[{"family":"Baraloto","given":"Christopher"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Prevailing retention practices have been reported to lack ecological credibility in safeguarding biodiversity and there are calls for their further development ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ap476k00of","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kuuluvainen, Lindberg, Vanha-Majamaa, Keto-Tokoi, & Punttila, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Kuuluvainen, Lindberg, Vanha-Majamaa, Keto-Tokoi, & Punttila, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1141,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/APH2FMY9"],"itemData":{"id":1141,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            In managed forests, leaving retention trees during final harvesting has globally become a common approach to reconciling the often conflicting goals of timber production and safeguarding biodiversity and delivery of several ecosystem services. In Finland, the dominant certification scheme requires leaving low levels of retention that can benefit some specific species. However, species responses are dependent on the level of retention and the current low amounts of retention clearly do not provide the habitat quality and continuity needed for declining and red-listed forest species which are dependent on old living trees and coarse woody debris. Several factors contribute to this situation. First, the ecological benefits of the current low retention levels are further diminished by monotonous standwise use of retention, resulting in low variability of retention habitat at the landscape scale. Second, the prevailing timber-oriented management thinking may regard retention trees as an external cost to be minimized, rather than as part of an integrated approach to managing the ecosystem for specific goals. Third, the main obstacles of development may still be institutional and policy-related. The development of retention practices in Finland indicates that the aim has not been to use ecological understanding to attain specific ecological sustainability goals, but rather to define the lowest level of retention that still allows access to the market. We conclude that prevailing retention practices in Finland currently lack ecological credibility in safeguarding biodiversity and they should urgently be developed based on current scientific knowledge to meet ecological sustainability goals.","container-title":"Ecological Processes","DOI":"10.1186/s13717-019-0198-0","ISSN":"2192-1709","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Process","language":"en","page":"47","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Low-level retention forestry, certification, and biodiversity: case Finland","title-short":"Low-level retention forestry, certification, and biodiversity","URL":"https://ecologicalprocesses.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s13717-019-0198-0","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Kuuluvainen","given":"Timo"},{"family":"Lindberg","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Vanha-Majamaa","given":"Ilkka"},{"family":"Keto-Tokoi","given":"Petri"},{"family":"Punttila","given":"Pekka"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other studies have reported that it is not necessarily the level of retention of living trees, but rather, the microclimatic continuity, and maintenance and active increase of legacies such as existing coarse woody debris, very old trees, and tree species mixtures that significantly contribute to the conservation of forest species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2g2ldvft52","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Koivula & Vanha-Majamaa, 2020; Siitonen, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Koivula & Vanha-Majamaa, 2020; Siitonen, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31291,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5SEVK8H3"],"itemData":{"id":31291,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Processes","DOI":"10.1186/s13717-019-0209-1","ISSN":"2192-1709","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Process","language":"en","page":"11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Experimental evidence on biodiversity impacts of variable retention forestry, prescribed burning, and deadwood manipulation in Fennoscandia","URL":"https://ecologicalprocesses.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s13717-019-0209-1","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Koivula","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Vanha-Majamaa","given":"Ilkka"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31260,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ANXXZQYK"],"itemData":{"id":31260,"type":"article-journal","language":"en","page":"32","source":"Zotero","title":"Forest Management, Coarse Woody Debris and Saproxylic Organisms: Fennoscandian Boreal Forests as an Example","author":[{"family":"Siitonen","given":"Juha"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Diversification of silvicultural harvesting techniques is recommended to enhance specific structural or compositional elements and the diversity of species in forest stands. Either clear-cutting, partial cutting or selective cutting can be carried out to match variations in stand conditions and effects of natural disturbances, biophysical site characteristics and succession processes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ah6j6c7b53","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bergeron, Gauthier, Kafka, Lefort, & Lesieur, 2001; Harvey & Bergeron, 1989; Maleki, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Bergeron, Gauthier, Kafka, Lefort, & Lesieur, 2001; Harvey & Bergeron, 1989; Maleki, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31259,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3GUYQ4GN"],"itemData":{"id":31259,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Given that fire is the most important disturbance of the boreal forest, climatically induced changes in fire frequency (i.e., area burnt per year) can have important consequences on the resulting forest mosaic age-class distribution and composition. Using archives and dendroecological data we reconstructed the fire frequency in four large sectors along a transect from eastern Ontario to central Quebec. Results showed a dramatic decrease in fire frequency that began in the mid-19th century and has been accentuated during the 20th century. Although all areas showed a similar temporal decrease in area burned, we observed a gradual increase in fire frequency from the west to Abitibi east, followed by a slight decrease in central Quebec. The global warming that has been occurring since the end of the Little Ice Age (~1850) may have created a climate less prone to large forest fires in the eastern boreal forest of North America. This interpretation is corroborated by predictions of a decrease in forest fires for that region of the boreal forest in the future. A longer fire cycle (i.e., the time needed to burn an area equivalent to the study area) has important consequences for sustainable forest management of the boreal forest of eastern Canada. When considering the important proportion of overmature and old-growth stands in the landscape resulting from the elongation of the fire cycles, it becomes difficult to justify clear-cutting practices over all the entire area as well as short rotations as a means to emulate natural disturbances. Alternative practices involving the uses of variable proportion of clear, partial, and selective cutting are discussed.","container-title":"Canadian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1139/x00-178","ISSN":"0045-5067, 1208-6037","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Can. J. For. Res.","language":"en","page":"384-391","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Natural fire frequency for the eastern Canadian boreal forest: consequences for sustainable forestry","title-short":"Natural fire frequency for the eastern Canadian boreal forest","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/x00-178","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Bergeron","given":"Yves"},{"family":"Gauthier","given":"Sylvie"},{"family":"Kafka","given":"Victor"},{"family":"Lefort","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Lesieur","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001",3,1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31258,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P6YJDBFK"],"itemData":{"id":31258,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Pre- and post-harvest regeneration levels were compared for Abiesbalsamea (L.) Mill. –Betulapapyrifera Marsh. –Picea spp. forests in an area of the southern clay belt of northwestern Quebec. Results revealed abundant advance softwood regeneration (mean?=?65?000 stems/ha), almost entirely of Abies prior to harvest. The survey following mechanical and manual whole-tree harvesting suggested a 92% reduction of softwood regeneration and a shift from softwood to a mixed or hardwood-shrub dominated regeneration. Ninety percent of softwood seedlings collected after harvest were pre-established. Destruction of advance regeneration was generally greater on fine-textured soils. Hierarchic cluster analysis of ecological types based on softwood, hardwood, and shrub tree regeneration data as variables, revealed nine groups that could serve as a basis for operational silvicultural decision making. In general, Salix spp. and Alnusrugosa (Du Roi) Spreng. are the major competitors on poorly drained sites; Betula sp., Acerspicatum Lam., and Prunuspensylvanica L.f. dominate on thin organic deposits and coarse deposits, whereas Populustremuloides Michx. and Acerspicatum dominate on fine-textured deposits. These findings suggest that a good understanding of physical site factors can provide useful information for harvesting and silvicultural planning.","container-title":"Canadian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1139/x89-222","ISSN":"0045-5067, 1208-6037","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Can. J. For. Res.","language":"en","page":"1458-1469","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Site patterns of natural regeneration following clear-cutting in northwestern Quebec","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/x89-222","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Harvey","given":"B. D."},{"family":"Bergeron","given":"Y."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1989",11,1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1142,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z4PA4WKM"],"itemData":{"id":1142,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Over the last three decades, the ecological basis for the generalized use of even-aged silviculture in boreal forests has been increasingly challenged. In boreal mixed-wood landscapes, the diminishing proportion of conifers, to the benefit of intolerant hardwoods, has been a primary concern, coupled with the general rarefication of old-growth conifer-dominated stands. In this context, partial cutting, extended rotations and forest renewal techniques that eliminate or reduce regenerating hardwoods have been proposed as means of regaining greater conifer cover. As a result, experimentation and industrial application of various forms of both variable retention and partial harvesting are occurring across the commercial Canadian boreal forest. In this study, we compared the effects of two harvesting intensities, clearcutting and low-intensity partial cutting (removal of 25–31% of tree basal area), on hardwood and conifer regeneration levels 7–19 years following treatments in aspen (Populus tremuloides)-dominated stands and verified whether regeneration differences existed between micro-sites on and off machinery trails. The abundance of aspen regeneration increased with percent basal area removal and was positively correlated to the abundance of mature aspen prior to harvesting. The abundance of fir (Abies balsamea) regeneration after partial cutting was similar to controls and higher than after clear-cutting and was positively correlated with ground cover of mixed litter (i.e., mixture of needles and leaves) and negatively correlated with ground cover of broadleaf litter. These results suggest that it is possible in boreal mixed-woods to control aspen abundance and promote or maintain conifer regeneration through silvicultural treatments that limit canopy opening and promote mixed forest floor litter.","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f11070741","ISSN":"1999-4907","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Forests","language":"en","page":"741","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Natural Regeneration Following Partial and Clear-Cut Harvesting in Mature Aspen-Jack Pine Stands in Eastern Canada","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/1999-4907/11/7/741","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Maleki","given":"Kobra"},{"family":"Nguema Allogo","given":"Freddy"},{"family":"Lafleur","given":"Benoit"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,8]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Clear-cutting tends to allow cycling of early successional species into a single species dominant stand, while partial cutting and extended rotations can enable maintenance of a mixed species stand or stands that have some characteristics of older forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a8kogof6o6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ruel, Fortin, & Pothier, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Ruel, Fortin, & Pothier, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BPMMHAHG"],"itemData":{"id":31402,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The uncut boreal forest of eastern Québec is largely composed of stands with an irregular structure. Traditionally, even-aged silvicultural systems have been used for these forests but a strong interest has developed in alternative approaches. In 2004, an integrated experiment was established to provide a general assessment of harvesting uneven-aged boreal forest stands with a wide variety of treatments. Here, we summarize the key results of this experiment, which involved four silvicultural treatments differing in the level of tree retention: a clearcut with advance growth protection, a severe partial cut protecting small vigorous merchantable stems (75%–90% basal area removed), and two patterns of selection cutting (35% basal area removed). We evaluated treatment effects on vegetation attributes and animal species assemblages. We also assessed whether or not selection cutting approaches could become broadly used on an operational basis by examining simple forms of application and assessing their economic profitability. We found that many attributes of old-growth forests can be maintained with selection cutting, even with simple approaches that do not invest in marking trees to cut. Unlike more severe cuts, silvicultural treatments with more than 55% tree retention largely maintain the animal assemblages associated with old forests. Financial analysis showed that selection cutting is profitable over the long time frame, but clearcutting remains more profitable. This greater profitability is related to the first entry, whereas future entries will be more profitable with selection cutting.","container-title":"The Forestry Chronicle","DOI":"10.5558/tfc2013-066","ISSN":"0015-7546, 1499-9315","issue":"03","journalAbbreviation":"The Forestry Chronicle","language":"en","page":"360-369","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Partial cutting in old-growth boreal stands: An integrated experiment","title-short":"Partial cutting in old-growth boreal stands","URL":"http://pubs.cif-ifc.org/doi/10.5558/tfc2013-066","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Ruel","given":"Jean-Claude"},{"family":"Fortin","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Pothier","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Post-logging forest restoration greatly relies on post logging seedling generation. The ability of a species to be sustained through rotations depends on the growth and reproduction of surviving adults, juveniles and seedling regeneration (smith, 1997). However, many of the high-value timber species are nonpioneer light demanders whose seedlings occur at low densities in the forest understory due to limited shade tolerance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a268j9lids2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grogan, Landis, Ashton, & Galv\\\\uc0\\\\u227{}o, 2005; Gullison & Hubbell, 1992; J. S. Hall, Medjibe, Berlyn, & Ashton, 2003; Jones, 1956; Lamprecht, 1989; Medjibe & Hall, 2002; M Schulze, Vidal, Grogan, Zweede, & Zarin, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Grogan, Landis, Ashton, & Galvão, 2005; Gullison & Hubbell, 1992; J. S. Hall, Medjibe, Berlyn, & Ashton, 2003; Jones, 1956; Lamprecht, 1989; Medjibe & Hall, 2002; M Schulze, Vidal, Grogan, Zweede, & Zarin, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31244,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FWIK7DXB"],"itemData":{"id":31244,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2004.09.013","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"2-3","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"399-412","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Growth response by big-leaf mahogany (Swietenia macrophylla) advance seedling regeneration to overhead canopy release in southeast Pará, Brazil","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112704006899","volume":"204","author":[{"family":"Grogan","given":"James"},{"family":"Landis","given":"R. Matthew"},{"family":"Ashton","given":"Mark S."},{"family":"Galvão","given":"Jurandir"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",1]]}}},{"id":31245,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SL623HTD"],"itemData":{"id":31245,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecologia en  Bolivia","page":"43-56","title":"Regeneracion natural de la mara  (Swietenia macrophylla) en el bosque Chimanes, Bol","URL":"https://ecologiaenbolivia.com/documents/GullisonHubbell19.pdf","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Gullison","given":"R. E."},{"family":"Hubbell","given":"S.P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}},{"id":31246,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/95SYMVVL"],"itemData":{"id":31246,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/S0378-1127(02)00488-7","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"1-3","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"135-144","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Seedling growth of three co-occurring Entandrophragma species (Meliaceae) under simulated light environments: implications for forest management in central Africa","title-short":"Seedling growth of three co-occurring Entandrophragma species (Meliaceae) under simulated light environments","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112702004887","volume":"179","author":[{"family":"Hall","given":"Jefferson S."},{"family":"Medjibe","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Berlyn","given":"Graeme P."},{"family":"Ashton","given":"P.Mark S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",7]]}}},{"id":1127,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6BEL9KQI"],"itemData":{"id":1127,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Ecology","DOI":"10.2307/2257155","ISSN":"00220477","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"The Journal of Ecology","language":"en","page":"83","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ecological Studies on the Rain Forest of Southern Nigeria: IV (Continued). The Plateau Forest of the Okomu Forest Reserve","title-short":"Ecological Studies on the Rain Forest of Southern Nigeria","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/2257155?origin=crossref","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Jones","given":"E. W."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1956",1]]}}},{"id":31248,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WR3IM5DU"],"itemData":{"id":31248,"type":"book","event-place":"Eschborn","publisher":"Federal Republic of Germany","publisher-place":"Eschborn","title":"Silviculture in the Tropics: Tropical Forest Ecosystems and Their Tree Species-Possibilities and Methods for Their Long-Term Utilization","author":[{"family":"Lamprecht","given":"Hans"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1989"]]}}},{"id":31247,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TSDPYUI8"],"itemData":{"id":31247,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/S0378-1127(01)00769-1","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"1-3","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"249-257","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Seed dispersal and its implications for silviculture of African mahogany (Entandrophragma spp.) in undisturbed forest in the Central African Republic","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112701007691","volume":"170","author":[{"family":"Medjibe","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Hall","given":"Jefferson S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002",10]]}}},{"id":31233,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YBIKN3EF"],"itemData":{"id":31233,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Revista Ciência Hoje","issue":"36","page":"66-69","title":"Madeiras nobres em perigo: Práticas e leis atuais de manejo florestal não garantem exploração sustentável","volume":"241","author":[{"family":"Schulze","given":"M"},{"family":"Vidal","given":"E"},{"family":"Grogan","given":"J"},{"family":"Zweede","given":"J"},{"family":"Zarin","given":"D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, such as wind-dispersed mahoganies and related genera in the family Meliaceae (Swietenia, Cedrela, Chukrasia, Entandrophragma, Khaya, Toona), Amburana, Cedrelinga, Couratari, Dinizia, Hymenolobium, and Tabebuia. These usually have limited post-logging regeneration ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a175cgeasbt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dickinson & Whigham, 1999; Grogan, Galv\\\\uc0\\\\u227{}o, Sim\\\\uc0\\\\u245{}es, & Ver\\\\uc0\\\\u205{}ssimo, 2003; Gullison, Panfil, Strouse, & Hubbell, 1996; M. D. Schulze, 2003, p. 2003; Ver\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}ssimo, Barreto, Tarifa, & Uhl, 1995)","plainCitation":"(Dickinson & Whigham, 1999; Grogan, Galvão, Simões, & VerÍssimo, 2003; Gullison, Panfil, Strouse, & Hubbell, 1996; M. D. Schulze, 2003, p. 2003; Veríssimo, Barreto, Tarifa, & Uhl, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1126,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZK94WJUB"],"itemData":{"id":1126,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Low rates of natural regeneration of big-leaf mahogany (Swietenia macrophylla King) were found in gaps due to felling and natural treefalls in the Yucatan. This finding is consistent with low post-logging abundances of reproductive-sized trees as well as relatively low growth rates of planted mahogany seedlings in small felling gaps. High growth rates of seedlings in large felling gaps and landings provides optimism that the extensive plantings done in the region will provide timber in the future. The long-term effects of shoot borers, which predominantly attack the fastest growing seedlings, are uncertain.","container-title":"International Forestry Review","language":"en","page":"35-39","source":"Zotero","title":"Regeneration of mahogany (Swietenia macrophylla) in the Yucatan","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Dickinson","given":"Matthew B"},{"family":"Whigham","given":"Dennis F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":31239,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5TWHV6FS"],"itemData":{"id":31239,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Big-Leaf Mahogany","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-0-387-98837-5","language":"en","note":"collection-title: Ecological Studies\\nDOI: 10.1007/0-387-21778-9_10","page":"193-208","publisher":"Springer-Verlag","publisher-place":"New York","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Regeneration of Big-Leaf Mahogany in Closed and Logged Forests of Southeastern Pará, Brazil","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/0-387-21778-9_10","volume":"159","editor":[{"family":"Lugo","given":"Ariel E."},{"family":"Figueroa Colón","given":"Julio C."},{"family":"Alayón","given":"Mildred"}],"author":[{"family":"Grogan","given":"James"},{"family":"Galvão","given":"Jurandir"},{"family":"Simões","given":"Luciana"},{"family":"VerÍssimo","given":"Adalberto"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":31242,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VDA7T4CG"],"itemData":{"id":31242,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society","DOI":"10.1111/j.1095-8339.1996.tb02060.x","ISSN":"00244074","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"9-34","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ecology and management of mahogany (Swietenia macrophylla King) in the Chimanes Forest, Beni, Bolivia","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/botlinnean/article-lookup/doi/10.1111/j.1095-8339.1996.tb02060.x","volume":"122","author":[{"family":"Gullison","given":"R.E."},{"family":"Panfil","given":"S.N."},{"family":"Strouse","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Hubbell","given":"S.P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996",9]]}}},{"id":31234,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XRJA2F2W"],"itemData":{"id":31234,"type":"thesis","publisher":"University Park: The Pennsylvania State University.","title":"Ecology and Behavior of Nine Timber Tree Species in Pará, Brazil: Links between Species Life History and Forest Management and Conservation.","author":[{"family":"Schulze","given":"Mark D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",12]]}},"locator":"2003"},{"id":31285,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/55UZUE6Q"],"itemData":{"id":31285,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/0378-1127(94)03432-V","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"39-60","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Extraction of a high-value natural resource in Amazonia: the case of mahogany","title-short":"Extraction of a high-value natural resource in Amazonia","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/037811279403432V","volume":"72","author":[{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Adalberto"},{"family":"Barreto","given":"Paulo"},{"family":"Tarifa","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Uhl","given":"Christopher"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and thus require adjustment in logging and silvicultural practices to promote their regeneration. To ensure sustained yield timber production from such timber species across the tropics, there are a number of silvicultural practices that should be taken into consideration ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a19dfmkdfj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grogan & Galv\\\\uc0\\\\u227{}o, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Grogan & Galvão, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31292,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7ERU79PE"],"itemData":{"id":31292,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biotropica","DOI":"10.1111/j.1744-7429.2006.00121.x","ISSN":"00063606","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"219-228","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Factors Limiting Post-logging Seedling Regeneration by Big-leaf Mahogany ( <i>Swietenia macrophylla</i> ) in Southeastern Amazonia, Brazil, and Implications for Sustainable Management <sup>1</sup>: Mahogany Seed Availability","title-short":"Factors Limiting Post-logging Seedling Regeneration by Big-leaf Mahogany ( <i>Swietenia macrophylla</i> ) in Southeastern Amazonia, Brazil, and Implications for Sustainable Management <sup>1</sup>","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1744-7429.2006.00121.x","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Grogan","given":"James"},{"family":"Galvão","given":"Jurandir"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Economically, timber-harvesting is most profitable for the traders, particularly if engaged in export markets. Conventional, particularly illegal, timber-harvesting, is also profitable for the owner of the sawmill, but also provides urgently required income opportunities for local people, not so much in the forest operations, but in the sawmills ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aqvb7fas5d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pokorny, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Pokorny, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EN7YYVSN"],"itemData":{"id":31225,"type":"book","event-place":"Place of publication not identified","ISBN":"978-1-138-57317-8","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 993640230","publisher":"ROUTLEDGE","publisher-place":"Place of publication not identified","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Smallholders, forest management and rural development in the amazon.","author":[{"family":"Pokorny","given":"Benno"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The benefits of large-scale industrial logging to the local economy are usually limited ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"anr8eo2ea","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Gray, 1999)","plainCitation":"(J. Gray, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31284,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MRYSGYIV"],"itemData":{"id":31284,"type":"report","event-place":"Brasília, Brazil","publisher":"Ministério do Meio Ambiente","publisher-place":"Brasília, Brazil","title":"Regime de propriedade florestal e valoração de floresta públicas no Brasil. Programa Nacional de Florestas.","author":[{"family":"Gray","given":"J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} to some low-paid work, but loss of non-timber forest products which many local people often rely on for subsistence or livelihood diversification can have serious negative impacts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a204arh9bik","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(W. M. Adams, 2009)","plainCitation":"(W. M. Adams, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31279,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9I4H6FUF"],"itemData":{"id":31279,"type":"book","call-number":"HC59.7 .A714 2009","edition":"3rd ed","event-place":"London ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-415-39507-6","note":"OCLC: ocn191010559","number-of-pages":"449","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Green development: environment and sustainability in a developing world","title-short":"Green development","author":[{"family":"Adams","given":"W. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Benefits to the national economy are restricted, because while value is added to the timber when it is sawn and made into products, this typically takes place elsewhere ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1wCTnkN5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(W. M. Adams, 2009)","plainCitation":"(W. M. Adams, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31279,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9I4H6FUF"],"itemData":{"id":31279,"type":"book","call-number":"HC59.7 .A714 2009","edition":"3rd ed","event-place":"London ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-415-39507-6","note":"OCLC: ocn191010559","number-of-pages":"449","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Green development: environment and sustainability in a developing world","title-short":"Green development","author":[{"family":"Adams","given":"W. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The main products obtained are round logs which are directly exported with very little in-country downstream processing. In instances where companies obtain concessions from private or community forests, these give royalties to the owners which depend on the tree species harvested. Nevertheless, large concessions seem to be a suitable tenure model in low-density areas where central or local governments are not capable of creating or maintaining adequate infrastructure to support regional economic issues and where only large-scale companies have the potential to do so ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1l4heb24eb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Karsenty, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Karsenty, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31287,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T28AZ5ZC"],"itemData":{"id":31287,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2008.07.001","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"1498-1508","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Regulating industrial forest concessions in Central Africa and South America","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112708005471","volume":"256","author":[{"family":"Karsenty","given":"Alain"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Isabel Garcia"},{"family":"Piketty","given":"Marie-Gabrielle"},{"family":"Singer","given":"Benjamin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Social impacts are felt due to large amounts of migrant labor associated with industrial logging. A larger proportion of the workers in large-scale industrial timber-harvesting operations are permanent, but are brought from other regions and seldom become settled in a region. Concessionaires, especially including certified companies, have to effectively protect their management unit against informal harvest and encroachment. Accordingly, local resource users living in and around concessions suffer from restricted access to forest management areas. In Central Africa, the social impact of industrial timber-harvesting remains a contested issue. Taxation systems and services due to workers and the local populations are clear on paper, but there is limited transparency or availability of information about how much of the due amounts or promised services are actually paid into the State coffers or delivered locally. And while there seems to be a bit more clarity on the amounts of money that are collected and redistributed to local councils and villages, e.g., in Cameroon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a26vfcfh0nq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cerutti, Lescuyer, Assembe-Mvondo, & Tacconi, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Cerutti, Lescuyer, Assembe-Mvondo, & Tacconi, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31373,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IUK4YP9Z"],"itemData":{"id":31373,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Forestry Review","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Forestry Review","page":"130-138","title":"The challenges of redistributing forest-related monetary benefits to local governments: a decade of logging area fees in Cameroon.","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"G"},{"family":"Assembe-Mvondo","given":"S"},{"family":"Tacconi","given":"L"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and the Democratic Republic of Congo ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1e60i7gqhr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tsanga, Cerutti, Bolika, Tibaldeschi, & Inkonkoy, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Tsanga, Cerutti, Bolika, Tibaldeschi, & Inkonkoy, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31223,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GQWGRV46"],"itemData":{"id":31223,"type":"report","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","title":"Independent monitoring of social clauses in the Democratic Republic of Congo. Report.","author":[{"family":"Tsanga","given":"R"},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"P O"},{"family":"Bolika","given":"J M"},{"family":"Tibaldeschi","given":"P"},{"family":"Inkonkoy","given":"F"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} much of the burden of redistributing benefits to local populations remains within the concessionaires themselves, which are not always willing or capable of playing that role ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ltujgmlh7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cerruti, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Cerruti, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31381,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DD732L9U"],"itemData":{"id":31381,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554817822295920","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"50-63","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Social impacts of the Forest Stewardship Council certification in the Congo basin","URL":"https://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/146554817822295920","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Cerruti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"G."},{"family":"Tacconi","given":"L."},{"family":"Eba'a Atyi","given":"R."},{"family":"Essiane","given":"E."},{"family":"Nasi","given":"R."},{"family":"Tabi Eckebil","given":"P.P."},{"family":"Tsanga","given":"R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Ecological, economic and social sustainability can perhaps be achieved through continuous-cover forest management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1psh8ilrht","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(e.g., Fedrowitz, 2014; Franklin, 1997; Kuuluvainen & Grenfell, 2012)","plainCitation":"(e.g., Fedrowitz, 2014; Franklin, 1997; Kuuluvainen & Grenfell, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1133,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XHMDXW6M"],"itemData":{"id":1133,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/1365-2664.12289","ISSN":"0021-8901, 1365-2664","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Ecol","language":"en","page":"1669-1679","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"REVIEW: Can retention forestry help conserve biodiversity? A meta?analysis","title-short":"REVIEW","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/1365-2664.12289","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Fedrowitz","given":"Katja"},{"family":"Koricheva","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Baker","given":"Susan C."},{"family":"Lindenmayer","given":"David B."},{"family":"Palik","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Rosenvald","given":"Raul"},{"family":"Beese","given":"William"},{"family":"Franklin","given":"Jerry F."},{"family":"Kouki","given":"Jari"},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"Ellen"},{"family":"Messier","given":"Christian"},{"family":"Sverdrup?Thygeson","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Gustafsson","given":"Lena"}],"editor":[{"family":"Baraloto","given":"Christopher"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12]]}},"prefix":"e.g., "},{"id":31392,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RJDTHS5A"],"itemData":{"id":31392,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Creating a forestry for the 21st century: the science of ecosystem management","event-place":"Washington DC","page":"111-139","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington DC","title":"Alternative silvicultural approaches to timber harvest: variable retention harvest systems. Pages 111–139 in K.A. Kohm & J.F. Franklin, editors. Creating a forestry for the 21st century . Island Press, Covelo , California","author":[{"family":"Franklin","given":"Jerry F"},{"family":"Berg","given":"Dean E"},{"family":"Thornburgh","given":"Dale A."},{"family":"Tappeiner","given":"John C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}},{"id":31388,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8JVEFJAC"],"itemData":{"id":31388,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Natural disturbance emulation (NDE) has been proposed as a general approach to ecologically sustainable forest management. We reviewed the concepts, theories, and strategies related to NDE in boreal forest management. We also reviewed publications that discussed NDE in the boreal forest in general and those that specifically compared NDE-based management with conventional even-aged management. The papers generally focused on northern North America and landscape-scale wildfire as the main disturbance factor, whereas information from Eurasia was exclusively theoretical. Within this limited scope, NDE was generally found to have a positive effect on biodiversity in terms of forest structure and species diversity when compared with conventional even-aged management. Studies on timber supply and social implications of NDE were so few that they preclude generalizations. We conclude that the ecological and economic performance of NDE as a management approach still remains poorly examined. To advance the development of NDE, particular attention should be given to (1) augmenting the knowledge base on natural range of variability of unmanaged forest ecosystems and evaluating the validity of this information in a changing climate, (2) fostering multidisciplinary research with better integration of ecological theory to both integrative and analytical research on NDE, and (3) better integration of socioeconomic concerns, adaptive management schemes, and international collaboration into NDE initiatives.","container-title":"Canadian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1139/x2012-064","ISSN":"0045-5067, 1208-6037","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Can. J. For. Res.","language":"en","page":"1185-1203","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Natural disturbance emulation in boreal forest ecosystem management — theories, strategies, and a comparison with conventional even-aged management <sup>1</sup> This article is one of a selection of papers from the 7th International Conference on Disturbance Dynamics in Boreal Forests.","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/x2012-064","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Kuuluvainen","given":"Timo"},{"family":"Grenfell","given":"Russell"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This regime applies logging methods other than clear cutting and thus varies the amount and spatial distribution of retained trees, and the size of harvested openings. The logging methods include selection cutting, gap cutting and modifications of clear cutting, all characterized by maintaining a significant proportion of trees throughout the logging cycle ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1rni612lf3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(e.g., Koivula, 2014; Puettmann, 2009)","plainCitation":"(e.g., Koivula, 2014; Puettmann, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31390,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LBZSJU73"],"itemData":{"id":31390,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1080/02827581.2014.938110","ISSN":"0282-7581, 1651-1891","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","language":"en","page":"579-592","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forest management inspired by natural disturbance dynamics (DISTDYN) – a long-term research and development project in Finland","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/02827581.2014.938110","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Koivula","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Kuuluvainen","given":"Timo"},{"family":"Hallman","given":"Erkki"},{"family":"Kouki","given":"Jari"},{"family":"Siitonen","given":"Juha"},{"family":"Valkonen","given":"Sauli"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",8,18]]}},"prefix":"e.g., "},{"id":31391,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5NGX2QEN"],"itemData":{"id":31391,"type":"book","call-number":"QH353 .D38 2009","event-place":"Washington D.C.","ISBN":"978-0-19-921875-2","language":"en","note":"OCLC: 234430671","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington D.C.","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"A Critique of Silviculture: Managing For Complexity","author":[{"family":"Puettmann","given":"Klaus"},{"family":"Messier","given":"Christian"},{"family":"Coates","given":"K. D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Experimental evidence suggests that even modest retention of living trees in harvested blocks is beneficial for biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aqgfd5mvhg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Koivula & Vanha-Majamaa, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Koivula & Vanha-Majamaa, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31291,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5SEVK8H3"],"itemData":{"id":31291,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Processes","DOI":"10.1186/s13717-019-0209-1","ISSN":"2192-1709","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Process","language":"en","page":"11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Experimental evidence on biodiversity impacts of variable retention forestry, prescribed burning, and deadwood manipulation in Fennoscandia","URL":"https://ecologicalprocesses.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s13717-019-0209-1","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Koivula","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Vanha-Majamaa","given":"Ilkka"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, based on landscape preference research, retention methods may be preferred over clear cutting by citizens who use forests for aesthetic pleasure, recreation, hunting, or harvesting (see 3.3.4.4). Clear cutting decreases the aesthetic and recreational values of forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a13juqoo39g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(e.g., Arnberger, 2018; Karjalainen, 2006; Tyrv\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}inen, Silvennoinen, & Hallikainen, 2017)","plainCitation":"(e.g., Arnberger, 2018; Karjalainen, 2006; Tyrväinen, Silvennoinen, & Hallikainen, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31377,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H3AVVEAI"],"itemData":{"id":31377,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-017-0975-4","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"209-223","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Visitor Preferences for Visual Changes in Bark Beetle-Impacted Forest Recreation Settings in the United States and Germany","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-017-0975-4","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Arnberger","given":"Arne"},{"family":"Ebenberger","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Schneider","given":"Ingrid E."},{"family":"Cottrell","given":"Stuart"},{"family":"Schlueter","given":"Alexander C."},{"family":"Ruschkowski","given":"Eick","non-dropping-particle":"von"},{"family":"Venette","given":"Robert C."},{"family":"Snyder","given":"Stephanie A."},{"family":"Gobster","given":"Paul H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}},"prefix":"e.g., "},{"id":31378,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U5MK3JS9"],"itemData":{"id":31378,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Dissertationes Forestales","DOI":"10.14214/df.31","ISSN":"17957389","issue":"31","journalAbbreviation":"Diss. For.","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The visual preferences for forest regeneration and field afforestation - four case studies in Finland","URL":"http://www.dissertationesforestales.fi/article/1815","volume":"2006","author":[{"family":"Karjalainen","given":"Eeva"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":31380,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7D9L85RE"],"itemData":{"id":31380,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1080/02827581.2016.1241892","ISSN":"0282-7581, 1651-1891","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","language":"en","page":"349-359","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effect of the season and forest management on the visual quality of the nature-based tourism environment: a case from Finnish Lapland","title-short":"Effect of the season and for