ht us?","title-short":"The aesthetics of forestry","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/BF01867587","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Ribe","given":"Robert G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1989",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Citizens prefer forests with diverse tree ages, species, and sizes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a17ms807jqq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Silvennoinen, Alho, Kolehmainen, & Pukkala, 2001; Silvennoinen, Pukkala, & Tahvanainen, 2002; Tyrv\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}inen, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Silvennoinen, Alho, Kolehmainen, & Pukkala, 2001; Silvennoinen, Pukkala, & Tahvanainen, 2002; Tyrväinen, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31376,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RVDYQKF3"],"itemData":{"id":31376,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","DOI":"10.1016/S0169-2046(01)00163-3","ISSN":"01692046","issue":"1-2","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape and Urban Planning","language":"en","page":"11-20","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Prediction models of landscape preferences at the forest stand level","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169204601001633","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Silvennoinen","given":"Harri"},{"family":"Alho","given":"Juha"},{"family":"Kolehmainen","given":"Osmo"},{"family":"Pukkala","given":"Timo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001",9]]}}},{"id":31370,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4JX5EW2Q"],"itemData":{"id":31370,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1080/028275802753742936","ISSN":"0282-7581, 1651-1891","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","language":"en","page":"263-273","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effect of Cuttings on the Scenic Beauty of a Tree Stand","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/028275802753742936","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Silvennoinen","given":"Harri"},{"family":"Pukkala","given":"Timo"},{"family":"Tahvanainen","given":"Liisa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002",1]]}}},{"id":31380,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7D9L85RE"],"itemData":{"id":31380,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1080/02827581.2016.1241892","ISSN":"0282-7581, 1651-1891","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","language":"en","page":"349-359","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effect of the season and forest management on the visual quality of the nature-based tourism environment: a case from Finnish Lapland","title-short":"Effect of the season and forest management on the visual quality of the nature-based tourism environment","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/02827581.2016.1241892","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Tyrväinen","given":"Liisa"},{"family":"Silvennoinen","given":"Harri"},{"family":"Hallikainen","given":"Ville"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",5,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} with not too densely spaced trees ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1pg75pld7o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ribe, 1989; Silvennoinen, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Ribe, 1989; Silvennoinen, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31375,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XVAN3L2E"],"itemData":{"id":31375,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/BF01867587","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"55-74","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The aesthetics of forestry: What has empirical preference research taught us?","title-short":"The aesthetics of forestry","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/BF01867587","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Ribe","given":"Robert G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1989",1]]}}},{"id":31379,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PW9UEKZZ"],"itemData":{"id":31379,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Dissertationes Forestales","DOI":"10.14214/df.242","ISSN":"17957389","issue":"242","journalAbbreviation":"Diss. For.","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Metsämaiseman kauneus ja metsänhoidon vaikutus koettuun maisemaan metsikkötasolla","URL":"http://www.dissertationesforestales.fi/article/7732","volume":"2017","author":[{"family":"Silvennoinen","given":"Harri"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Industrial logging is quite extensive in the tropics (Box 3.20). It takes place legally on public and private lands, and illegally on public forests designated for conservation. Logging also occurs on indigenous people and local communities’ lands and territories. Forest concessions have been widely used to allow companies to undertake large-scale timber-harvesting operations, yet these areas have been shrinking over time, particularly in the Amazon (e.g., Bolivia, Peru) and Southeast Asia (e.g., Malaysia and Indonesia). There are significant areas of public lands granted as concessions on all continents. In 2009, small properties accounted for 28% of production, medium-sized properties extracted 41% of wood and large properties supplied 31% of roundwood ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2kipaj13e1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Pereira, Santos, Vedoveto, Guimar\\\\uc0\\\\u227{}es, & Ver\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}ssimo, 2010)","plainCitation":"(D. Pereira, Santos, Vedoveto, Guimarães, & Veríssimo, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33135,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J7TZF49W"],"itemData":{"id":33135,"type":"book","publisher":"IMAZON-Instituto do Homem e Meio Ambiente da Amazônia","title":"Fatos Florestais da Amazônia","author":[{"family":"Pereira","given":"Denys"},{"family":"Santos","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Vedoveto","given":"Mariana"},{"family":"Guimarães","given":"Jayne"},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Adalberto"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Only 29% of production in 2009 came from areas owned or leased by the timber industries. The remainder (71%) originated in third party areas. 

Box 3.20 Industrial logging in the Amazon

In 1998, the Brazilian Amazon generated 10.8 million cubic meters of native wood. Twenty years later, only 57% of this volume was produced (~ 6.2 million m3 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aigauquh3l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lentini, Sobral, & Vieira, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Lentini, Sobral, & Vieira, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31251,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AKYCAMNH"],"itemData":{"id":31251,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Boletim TIMBERFlow","issue":"02","language":"pt","page":"11","source":"Zotero","title":"Como o Mercado Dos Produtos Madeireiros Da Amazônia Evoluiu Nas Últimas Duas Décadas (1998-2018)?","URL":"https://www.imaflora.org/public/media/biblioteca/boletim_timberflow_2_abril_2020.pdf","author":[{"family":"Lentini","given":"Marco"},{"family":"Sobral","given":"Leonardo"},{"family":"Vieira","given":"Robson"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This was due to increasing competition with cheap supplies of forest products from tree plantations and contractions in the domestic market, as well as replacement with other materials such as plastics, steel and aluminum. An estimated 95% of the sawmills in the region are small family enterprises with very limited managerial capacity. Despite operations based on legally approved management plans, it is unclear whether this logging is sustainable. The extraction (cutting and skid trails) is performed mostly (61%) by third parties, while the rest (39%) is extracted by the processing industries themselves ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1mjpd6h6qu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Pereira, 2010)","plainCitation":"(D. Pereira, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33135,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J7TZF49W"],"itemData":{"id":33135,"type":"book","publisher":"IMAZON-Instituto do Homem e Meio Ambiente da Amazônia","title":"Fatos Florestais da Amazônia","author":[{"family":"Pereira","given":"Denys"},{"family":"Santos","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Vedoveto","given":"Mariana"},{"family":"Guimarães","given":"Jayne"},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Adalberto"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Amazon has more than 300 species of trees considered commercially valuable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a5gb0ors86","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Martini, Rosa, & Uhl, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Martini, Rosa, & Uhl, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31280,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XJQ7TXZY"],"itemData":{"id":31280,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The impacts of logging on Amazonian ecosystems has been the focus of considerable attention both within and outside of Amazonia. However, the impacts of logging on individual timber species has not been at all adequately investigated. Logging affects timber species by: (1) removing mature individuals that are important sources of seeds; (2) damaging seedlings and saplings (\\n              i.e\\n              future generations of canopy adults); and (3) creating conditions that favour fire by opening the canopy and leaving slash as fuel on the forest floor.\\n            \\n            In this study we summarize information on the ecological characteristics of 305 timber species in Brazilian Amazonia. We identify seven ecological parameters that are useful in evaluating a species' ability to resist the negative impacts of logging. These characteristics are: (1) effective long-distance dispersal ability; (2) abundance of saplings in forest regeneration; (3) rapid growth; (4) ability to resprout; (5) capacity to withstand fire; (6) broad geographic distribution; and (7) high density of adults. We hypothesize thai species with characteristics opposed to these parameters and subjected to intense logging pressure will have difficulty in maintaining their populations in logging regions.\\n            \\n              We use a simple scoring system to rank species with regard to their hypothesized ability to withstand logging impacts. Among the species that are potentially susceptible to logging impacts are\\n              Euxylophora paraensis\\n              (‘Pau Amarelo’) and\\n              Swietenia macrophylla\\n              (American Mahogany). The sawn lumber from these two species goes principally to European and North American buyers, revealing a direct link between First World consumption and possible biodiversity impoverishment in the Brazilian rain-forest. These two species, and others that might experience population reductions as a result of logging, merit special study.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892900024589","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Conserv.","language":"en","page":"152-162","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An Attempt to Predict Which Amazonian Tree Species May be Threatened by Logging Activities","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0376892900024589/type/journal_article","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Martini","given":"Adriana M. Z."},{"family":"Rosa","given":"Nelson de A."},{"family":"Uhl","given":"Christopher"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, for decades the very same 15 to 20 species of commercial interest were harvested. Some of the most strained and consequently most pressured species are: Hymenaea courbaril, Handroanthus sp, Apuleia leiocarpa, Goupia glabra Aubl., Manilkara alata, Himenolobium petreum , Couratari sp., Dinizia excelsa  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kmpqx3ZJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lentini, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Lentini, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31251,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AKYCAMNH"],"itemData":{"id":31251,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Boletim TIMBERFlow","issue":"02","language":"pt","page":"11","source":"Zotero","title":"Como o Mercado Dos Produtos Madeireiros Da Amazônia Evoluiu Nas Últimas Duas Décadas (1998-2018)?","URL":"https://www.imaflora.org/public/media/biblioteca/boletim_timberflow_2_abril_2020.pdf","author":[{"family":"Lentini","given":"Marco"},{"family":"Sobral","given":"Leonardo"},{"family":"Vieira","given":"Robson"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Manifold attempts to broadening this range have not been too successful. Only very few large companies have the interest and capacity to comply with the Forest Stewardship Council certification standard. It is estimated that less than a quarter of the timber produced in the Amazon is exported, as most of the timber is consumed in the big cities at the coast. Depending on the forest type and the market situation, between 10 to 25 m³ per hectare is harvested. Increments of commercial timber are low around 0.5 to 1.5 m³ per year and hectare, which mathematically result in harvesting cycles of around 25 to 35 years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vDyC9jC6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Pereira, 2010)","plainCitation":"(D. Pereira, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33135,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J7TZF49W"],"itemData":{"id":33135,"type":"book","publisher":"IMAZON-Instituto do Homem e Meio Ambiente da Amazônia","title":"Fatos Florestais da Amazônia","author":[{"family":"Pereira","given":"Denys"},{"family":"Santos","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Vedoveto","given":"Mariana"},{"family":"Guimarães","given":"Jayne"},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Adalberto"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The regulations established in the Brazilian Amazon assume that a minimum harvest cycle of 25 to 30 years would guarantee the long-term sustainability of forest management. The legal requirements for industrial timber harvest include clarified tenure arrangements for the forest management unit, the formulation of a sustainable forest management plan, and annual operational plans. The volumes and products of harvested wood have to be reported in a document of forest origin (DFO) designed to accompany legally harvested wood at all stages of the transport and production chain ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ckglgni2c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Waldhoff & Vidal, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Waldhoff & Vidal, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31224,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L7R55FCH"],"itemData":{"id":31224,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2014.08.005","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"311-318","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community loggers attempting to legalize traditional timber harvesting in the Brazilian Amazon: An endless path","title-short":"Community loggers attempting to legalize traditional timber harvesting in the Brazilian Amazon","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934114001725","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Waldhoff","given":"Philippe"},{"family":"Vidal","given":"Edson"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The regulations foresee two categories of forest management: 1) Low-intensity forest management, normally by local communities, with a maximum harvest volume of up to 10 m3 per ha, a minimum harvest cycle of 10 years, and restrictions on the use of heavy machinery; 2) Complete forest management, which allows a maximum harvest volume of 30 m3 per hectare and year, minimum harvest cycles of 25 to 35 years, and without machinery restrictions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1gr78vl7p2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Pereira, 2010)","plainCitation":"(D. Pereira, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33135,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J7TZF49W"],"itemData":{"id":33135,"type":"book","publisher":"IMAZON-Instituto do Homem e Meio Ambiente da Amazônia","title":"Fatos Florestais da Amazônia","author":[{"family":"Pereira","given":"Denys"},{"family":"Santos","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Vedoveto","given":"Mariana"},{"family":"Guimarães","given":"Jayne"},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Adalberto"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

	Throughout the tropics, forestry regulations commonly grant rights to industrial, large-scale, export-oriented timber-harvesting concessions. These concessions require management plans, which are presumed to maintain forest cover and biodiversity. All countries in Central Africa follow the concessionary model, with the Ministries of Forests granting rights and responsibilities to the concessionaire (i.e., a private entity is given permission to manage a public property) either through public auctions or directly. The duration of the contract varies. In the Central African Republic, the concession is granted for the entire lifespan of the company, in all other countries there exist legal temporal limitations to the contractual agreement, which is 15 years in Cameroon, Republic of Congo and Equatorial Guinea, 25 years in the Democratic Republic of Congo, and 30 years in Gabon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"s6dilPwi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cerutti, Nasi, & Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR), Kenya and Indonesia, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Cerutti, Nasi, & Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR), Kenya and Indonesia, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31404,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FCV483RM"],"itemData":{"id":31404,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-1-78676-248-1","note":"DOI: 10.19103/AS.2020.0074.41","publisher":"Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR), Kenya and Indonesia","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Sustainable forest management (SFM) of tropical moist forests: the Congo Basin","title-short":"Sustainable forest management (SFM) of tropical moist forests","URL":"https://shop.bdspublishing.com/store/bds/detail/product/3-190-9781786766007","author":[{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"Robert"},{"literal":"Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR), Kenya and Indonesia"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Timber-harvesting concessions comprise a total area of 50 million ha in the Congo Basin, of which about half had approved management plans by 2020 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2k7tn4h6mb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cerutti, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Cerutti, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31404,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FCV483RM"],"itemData":{"id":31404,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-1-78676-248-1","note":"DOI: 10.19103/AS.2020.0074.41","publisher":"Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR), Kenya and Indonesia","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Sustainable forest management (SFM) of tropical moist forests: the Congo Basin","title-short":"Sustainable forest management (SFM) of tropical moist forests","URL":"https://shop.bdspublishing.com/store/bds/detail/product/3-190-9781786766007","author":[{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo Omar"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"Robert"},{"literal":"Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR), Kenya and Indonesia"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Management plans are generally based on a rotation period of about 30 years, with annual allowable cuts authorized for timber-harvesting each year by the forest administration.  
	Logging in the boreal and temperate forests is mainly industrial in scale ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a22mcg6np1h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Safford & Vallejo, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Safford & Vallejo, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1101,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WA2MBV5C"],"itemData":{"id":1101,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Developments in Soil Science","ISBN":"978-0-444-63998-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1016/B978-0-444-63998-1.00012-4","page":"259-308","publisher":"Elsevier","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ecosystem management and ecological restoration in the Anthropocene: integrating global change, soils, and disturbance in boreal and Mediterranean forests","title-short":"Ecosystem management and ecological restoration in the Anthropocene","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/B9780444639981000124","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Safford","given":"Hugh D."},{"family":"Vallejo","given":"V. Ramón"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Approximately 90% of the forest in Fennoscandia, and perhaps 40% and 60% of Canadian and Russian forests, respectively are subject to industrial tree harvest ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1p3jkubdus","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gauthier, Bernier, Kuuluvainen, Shvidenko, & Schepaschenko, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Gauthier, Bernier, Kuuluvainen, Shvidenko, & Schepaschenko, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32307,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LSF3CWFM"],"itemData":{"id":32307,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aaa9092","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6250","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"819-822","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Boreal forest health and global change","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.aaa9092","volume":"349","author":[{"family":"Gauthier","given":"S."},{"family":"Bernier","given":"P."},{"family":"Kuuluvainen","given":"T."},{"family":"Shvidenko","given":"A. Z."},{"family":"Schepaschenko","given":"D. G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",8,21]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Prior to the 20th century, selective cutting was the dominant logging practice in the temperate and boreal forests. An intensive era of clear-cutting targeting mainly conifer trees began in the 20th century due to economic factors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1lsr8fpv8n","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dupuis, Danneyrolles, Laflamme, Boucher, & Arseneault, 2020; Koivula & Vanha-Majamaa, 2020; Lundmark, Josefsson, & \\\\uc0\\\\u214{}stlund, 2013; Storaunet, Rolstad, Gjerde, & Gundersen, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Dupuis, Danneyrolles, Laflamme, Boucher, & Arseneault, 2020; Koivula & Vanha-Majamaa, 2020; Lundmark, Josefsson, & Östlund, 2013; Storaunet, Rolstad, Gjerde, & Gundersen, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1144,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LLKERKLS"],"itemData":{"id":1144,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.3389/fevo.2020.00257","ISSN":"2296-701X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Ecol. Evol.","page":"257","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forest Transformation Following European Settlement in the Saguenay-Lac-St-Jean Valley in Eastern Québec, Canada","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fevo.2020.00257/full","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Dupuis","given":"Sébastien"},{"family":"Danneyrolles","given":"Victor"},{"family":"Laflamme","given":"Jason"},{"family":"Boucher","given":"Yan"},{"family":"Arseneault","given":"Dominique"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,5]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31291,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5SEVK8H3"],"itemData":{"id":31291,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Processes","DOI":"10.1186/s13717-019-0209-1","ISSN":"2192-1709","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Process","language":"en","page":"11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Experimental evidence on biodiversity impacts of variable retention forestry, prescribed burning, and deadwood manipulation in Fennoscandia","URL":"https://ecologicalprocesses.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s13717-019-0209-1","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Koivula","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Vanha-Majamaa","given":"Ilkka"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31263,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/23ZRMTYD"],"itemData":{"id":31263,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2013.07.003","ISSN":"03781127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"112-122","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The history of clear-cutting in northern Sweden – Driving forces and myths in boreal silviculture","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112713004350","volume":"307","author":[{"family":"Lundmark","given":"Hanna"},{"family":"Josefsson","given":"Torbjörn"},{"family":"Östlund","given":"Lars"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",11]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31303,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/THLFGFNY"],"itemData":{"id":31303,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Silva Fennica","DOI":"10.14214/sf.479","ISSN":"22424075","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Silva Fenn.","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Historical logging, productivity, and structural characteristics of boreal coniferous forests in Norway","URL":"http://www.silvafennica.fi/article/479","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Storaunet","given":"Ken"},{"family":"Rolstad","given":"Jørund"},{"family":"Gjerde","given":"Ivar"},{"family":"Gundersen","given":"Vegard"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Clear-cutting continues to be the dominant logging practice ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a21hplo30sb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Curtis, 2018; K\\\\uc0\\\\u229{}l\\\\uc0\\\\u229{}s, 2010; Siitonen, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Curtis, 2018; Kålås, 2010; Siitonen, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31290,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HKH2Y84I"],"itemData":{"id":31290,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Global maps of forest loss depict the scale and magnitude of forest disturbance, yet companies, governments, and nongovernmental organizations need to distinguish permanent conversion (i.e., deforestation) from temporary loss from forestry or wildfire. Using satellite imagery, we developed a forest loss classification model to determine a spatial attribution of forest disturbance to the dominant drivers of land cover and land use change over the period 2001 to 2015. Our results indicate that 27% of global forest loss can be attributed to deforestation through permanent land use change for commodity production. The remaining areas maintained the same land use over 15 years; in those areas, loss was attributed to forestry (26%), shifting agriculture (24%), and wildfire (23%). Despite corporate commitments, the rate of commodity-driven deforestation has not declined. To end deforestation, companies must eliminate 5 million hectares of conversion from supply chains each year.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aau3445","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6407","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"1108-1111","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Classifying drivers of global forest loss","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.aau3445","volume":"361","author":[{"family":"Curtis","given":"Philip G."},{"family":"Slay","given":"Christy M."},{"family":"Harris","given":"Nancy L."},{"family":"Tyukavina","given":"Alexandra"},{"family":"Hansen","given":"Matthew C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",9,14]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31265,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y6N4STVW"],"itemData":{"id":31265,"type":"book","event-place":"Trondheim","ISBN":"978-82-92838-26-6","language":"Text engl. und norweg.","note":"OCLC: 918259516","publisher":"Artsdatabanken","publisher-place":"Trondheim","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Norsk rødliste for arter 2010 = The 2010 Norwegian red list for species","author":[{"family":"Kålås","given":"John Atle"},{"family":"Viken","given":"Åslaug"},{"family":"Henriksen","given":"Snorre"},{"family":"Skjelseth","given":"Sigrun"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31260,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ANXXZQYK"],"itemData":{"id":31260,"type":"article-journal","language":"en","page":"32","source":"Zotero","title":"Forest Management, Coarse Woody Debris and Saproxylic Organisms: Fennoscandian Boreal Forests as an Example","author":[{"family":"Siitonen","given":"Juha"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, although trials for partial cutting practices, such as retention silviculture have been established to test their operational and biological feasibility ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a9g3hpo244","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bose, Harvey, Brais, Beaudet, & Leduc, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Bose, Harvey, Brais, Beaudet, & Leduc, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31400,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZAMRK9H8"],"itemData":{"id":31400,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forestry","DOI":"10.1093/forestry/cpt047","ISSN":"0015-752X, 1464-3626","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Forestry","language":"en","page":"11-28","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Constraints to partial cutting in the boreal forest of Canada in the context of natural disturbance-based management: a review","title-short":"Constraints to partial cutting in the boreal forest of Canada in the context of natural disturbance-based management","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/forestry/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/forestry/cpt047","volume":"87","author":[{"family":"Bose","given":"A. K."},{"family":"Harvey","given":"B. D."},{"family":"Brais","given":"S."},{"family":"Beaudet","given":"M."},{"family":"Leduc","given":"A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In clear-cutting, mature trees are usually completely removed, followed by regeneration through site preparation, sowing or planting, tending of the emerging cohort of even-aged trees, and often a relatively short logging rotation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2kk7l9brj8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Koivula, 2020; Safford & Vallejo, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Koivula, 2020; Safford & Vallejo, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31296,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5I5WRG77"],"itemData":{"id":31296,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Forest management, characterized in many northern countries by the predominance of clear cutting and growing even-aged and -sized trees, has simplified the structure of boreal forests. Consequences include alterations in cultural ecosystem services such as forest attractiveness, i.e., combined aesthetic and recreational values. Continuous-cover forestry might mitigate these effects through the use of selection and gap cutting, but these methods have been little studied, particularly from the attractiveness viewpoint. We used photo surveys to assess Finnish citizens’ perceptions of attractiveness of in-stand scenery of Scots pine (Pinus sylvestris L.) forests logged using different methods. (i) The attractiveness scores, given by respondents, declined steadily from unharvested forest through continuous-cover methods to seed-tree and clear-cut methods. (ii) Respondents with a negative attitude to forest management gave lower scores than respondents with a positive attitude, but the declining slopes of attractiveness against logging intensity were similar. (iii) In unharvested and less intensively managed stands, summer photos received higher scores than corresponding winter photos. (iv) Background variables (gender, education, living environment, memberships in recreational or nature NGOs, forestry profession, and forest ownership) had negligible effects on the scores. We recommend the use of continuous-cover logging methods in settlement and recreational areas.","container-title":"Canadian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1139/cjfr-2019-0431","ISSN":"0045-5067, 1208-6037","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Can. J. For. Res.","language":"en","page":"819-828","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Continuous-cover management and attractiveness of managed Scots pine forests","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/cjfr-2019-0431","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Koivula","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Silvennoinen","given":"Harri"},{"family":"Koivula","given":"Hanna"},{"family":"Tikkanen","given":"Jukka"},{"family":"Tyrväinen","given":"Liisa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1101,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WA2MBV5C"],"itemData":{"id":1101,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Developments in Soil Science","ISBN":"978-0-444-63998-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1016/B978-0-444-63998-1.00012-4","page":"259-308","publisher":"Elsevier","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ecosystem management and ecological restoration in the Anthropocene: integrating global change, soils, and disturbance in boreal and Mediterranean forests","title-short":"Ecosystem management and ecological restoration in the Anthropocene","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/B9780444639981000124","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Safford","given":"Hugh D."},{"family":"Vallejo","given":"V. Ramón"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An underlying rationale of clear-cutting is economic because it is seen as highly efficient and leading to sustained yields of timber ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a188htci1mm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Koivula, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Koivula, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31296,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5I5WRG77"],"itemData":{"id":31296,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Forest management, characterized in many northern countries by the predominance of clear cutting and growing even-aged and -sized trees, has simplified the structure of boreal forests. Consequences include alterations in cultural ecosystem services such as forest attractiveness, i.e., combined aesthetic and recreational values. Continuous-cover forestry might mitigate these effects through the use of selection and gap cutting, but these methods have been little studied, particularly from the attractiveness viewpoint. We used photo surveys to assess Finnish citizens’ perceptions of attractiveness of in-stand scenery of Scots pine (Pinus sylvestris L.) forests logged using different methods. (i) The attractiveness scores, given by respondents, declined steadily from unharvested forest through continuous-cover methods to seed-tree and clear-cut methods. (ii) Respondents with a negative attitude to forest management gave lower scores than respondents with a positive attitude, but the declining slopes of attractiveness against logging intensity were similar. (iii) In unharvested and less intensively managed stands, summer photos received higher scores than corresponding winter photos. (iv) Background variables (gender, education, living environment, memberships in recreational or nature NGOs, forestry profession, and forest ownership) had negligible effects on the scores. We recommend the use of continuous-cover logging methods in settlement and recreational areas.","container-title":"Canadian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1139/cjfr-2019-0431","ISSN":"0045-5067, 1208-6037","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Can. J. For. Res.","language":"en","page":"819-828","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Continuous-cover management and attractiveness of managed Scots pine forests","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/cjfr-2019-0431","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Koivula","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Silvennoinen","given":"Harri"},{"family":"Koivula","given":"Hanna"},{"family":"Tikkanen","given":"Jukka"},{"family":"Tyrväinen","given":"Liisa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The concept of sustained yield has been criticized for only concentrating on the maintenance of timber stocks over time, while other forest resources that are protected with site-specific practices are not explicitly considered in the management plans, consequently leading to their decline ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a48i5sb6n5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berg, 1994; Cyr, Gauthier, Bergeron, & Carcaillet, 2009; Luckert & Williamson, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Berg, 1994; Cyr, Gauthier, Bergeron, & Carcaillet, 2009; Luckert & Williamson, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31266,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IVSDMDL4"],"itemData":{"id":31266,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1046/j.1523-1739.1994.08030718.x","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"718-731","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Threatened Plant, Animal, and Fungus Species in Swedish Forests: Distribution and Habitat Associations","title-short":"Threatened Plant, Animal, and Fungus Species in Swedish Forests","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1046/j.1523-1739.1994.08030718.x","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Berg","given":"Ake"},{"family":"Ehnstrom","given":"Beng"},{"family":"Gustafsson","given":"Lena"},{"family":"Hallingback","given":"Tomas"},{"family":"Jonsell","given":"Mats"},{"family":"Weslien","given":"Jan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994",9]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1131,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NETZLH64"],"itemData":{"id":1131,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","DOI":"10.1890/080088","ISSN":"1540-9295","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","language":"en","page":"519-524","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forest management is driving the eastern North American boreal forest outside its natural range of variability","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1890/080088","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Cyr","given":"Dominic"},{"family":"Gauthier","given":"Sylvie"},{"family":"Bergeron","given":"Yves"},{"family":"Carcaillet","given":"Christopher"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",12]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31262,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JBYEUSG5"],"itemData":{"id":31262,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper considers the question of whether sustainable forest management (SFM) should continue to incorporate sustained yield (SY) requirements, as it currently does in many jurisdictions. We evaluate the extent to which SY and SFM are consistent with notions of weak and (or) strong sustainability. Strong sustainability implies placing constraints on the reduction of stocks of natural capital to prevent irreversibility and (or) protect flows of services that have public good characteristics. In contrast, weak sustainability may allow market forces to draw down stocks of natural capital so long as levels of total capital (including human-made and natural capital) are maintained. We argue that with SY policies, we have probably chosen to attach strong sustainability policies to the only forest resource that does not need such protection (i.e., timber), while we have excluded other resources that could well need such protection (e.g., biodiversity) for pursuing SFM. Thus, the concept of allowable annual cuts could be dropped from SFM to be replaced by safe minimum standards on components of forest capital that are subject to irreversibility and (or) that have public good features. In other words, if we truly wish to pursue SFM, it may be necessary to leave SY behind.","container-title":"Canadian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1139/x04-172","ISSN":"0045-5067, 1208-6037","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Can. J. For. Res.","language":"en","page":"356-364","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Should sustained yield be part of sustainable forest management?","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/x04-172","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Luckert","given":"MK (Marty)"},{"family":"Williamson","given":"T"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",2,1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Tree retention is an emerging alternative to clear cut harvesting, practiced on several continents including North and South America, Oceania, and Europe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a249noqu7aq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lena Gustafsson, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Lena Gustafsson, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CT8UIWCJ"],"itemData":{"id":31383,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              Retention approaches in forest management are today common in several North European countries, integrated into the clearcutting practice as a way to promote biodiversity and maintain ecosystem functions. Individual green trees and retention patches (tree groups) are retained at final harvest, and deadwood is left at site or created. Here, we review research on retention in Sweden, Finland, Norway, the Baltic States, and NW Russia, with special focus on biodiversity. Following the first publication in 1994, about 180 peer-reviewed articles have been published. We present results from a systematic search of the retention literature, separated into the following topics: buffer zones, retention patches, high stumps, other types of deadwood, European aspen\\n              Populus tremula\\n              , and cost-efficiency. Russian literature is synthesized separately since studies from this region have so far almost exclusively been published in the Russian language. Furthermore, we describe six ongoing large-scale, replicated experiments with varying retention levels, five in Finland and one in Sweden, and summarize their main results. Among main conclusions for practice from the literature and experiments are that retention patches as large as 0.5 ha and 10-m-wide buffers to watercourses are not enough to maintain pre-harvest species composition but survival of forest species is still larger than on conventional clearcuts. Deadwood on clearcuts may present important habitats to saproxylic species, including rare and red-listed ones and a prioritization of tree species per stand is recommended. We identify several important future research directions including switch of focus towards the landscape as well as the species population level. Surveys in parts of European Russia where retention has been unintentionally implemented already for a century would indicate possible future trajectories of biodiversity and their drivers in other regions of Northern Europe. A stronger link to ecological theory would help in study designs and in the formulation of predicted outcomes.","container-title":"Ecological Processes","DOI":"10.1186/s13717-019-0208-2","ISSN":"2192-1709","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Process","language":"en","page":"3","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Research on retention forestry in Northern Europe","URL":"https://ecologicalprocesses.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s13717-019-0208-2","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Gustafsson","given":"Lena"},{"family":"Hannerz","given":"Mats"},{"family":"Koivula","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Shorohova","given":"Ekaterina"},{"family":"Vanha-Majamaa","given":"Ilkka"},{"family":"Weslien","given":"Jan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Emerging research reveals that tree retention has the potential to reduce impacts of logging on forest biodiversity through creating favourable conditions that allow for complex and uneven forest structures similar to those of natural forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SZO3Fkp0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lena Gustafsson, 2020; Moussaoui, 2019; Opoku-Nyame, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Lena Gustafsson, 2020; Moussaoui, 2019; Opoku-Nyame, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CT8UIWCJ"],"itemData":{"id":31383,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              Retention approaches in forest management are today common in several North European countries, integrated into the clearcutting practice as a way to promote biodiversity and maintain ecosystem functions. Individual green trees and retention patches (tree groups) are retained at final harvest, and deadwood is left at site or created. Here, we review research on retention in Sweden, Finland, Norway, the Baltic States, and NW Russia, with special focus on biodiversity. Following the first publication in 1994, about 180 peer-reviewed articles have been published. We present results from a systematic search of the retention literature, separated into the following topics: buffer zones, retention patches, high stumps, other types of deadwood, European aspen\\n              Populus tremula\\n              , and cost-efficiency. Russian literature is synthesized separately since studies from this region have so far almost exclusively been published in the Russian language. Furthermore, we describe six ongoing large-scale, replicated experiments with varying retention levels, five in Finland and one in Sweden, and summarize their main results. Among main conclusions for practice from the literature and experiments are that retention patches as large as 0.5 ha and 10-m-wide buffers to watercourses are not enough to maintain pre-harvest species composition but survival of forest species is still larger than on conventional clearcuts. Deadwood on clearcuts may present important habitats to saproxylic species, including rare and red-listed ones and a prioritization of tree species per stand is recommended. We identify several important future research directions including switch of focus towards the landscape as well as the species population level. Surveys in parts of European Russia where retention has been unintentionally implemented already for a century would indicate possible future trajectories of biodiversity and their drivers in other regions of Northern Europe. A stronger link to ecological theory would help in study designs and in the formulation of predicted outcomes.","container-title":"Ecological Processes","DOI":"10.1186/s13717-019-0208-2","ISSN":"2192-1709","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Process","language":"en","page":"3","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Research on retention forestry in Northern Europe","URL":"https://ecologicalprocesses.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s13717-019-0208-2","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Gustafsson","given":"Lena"},{"family":"Hannerz","given":"Mats"},{"family":"Koivula","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Shorohova","given":"Ekaterina"},{"family":"Vanha-Majamaa","given":"Ilkka"},{"family":"Weslien","given":"Jan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31385,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2P83TRDM"],"itemData":{"id":31385,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2018.09.059","ISSN":"1470160X","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Indicators","language":"en","page":"89-99","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Changes in forest structure along a chronosequence in the black spruce boreal forest: Identifying structures to be reproduced through silvicultural practices","title-short":"Changes in forest structure along a chronosequence in the black spruce boreal forest","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1470160X1830760X","volume":"97","author":[{"family":"Moussaoui","given":"Louiza"},{"family":"Leduc","given":"Alain"},{"family":"Fenton","given":"Nicole J."},{"family":"Lafleur","given":"Benoit"},{"family":"Bergeron","given":"Yves"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",2]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31384,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5KZYITQV"],"itemData":{"id":31384,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Clear cut harvest simplifies and eliminates old growth forest structure, negatively impacting biodiversity. Partial cut harvest has been hypothesized (1) to have less impact on biodiversity than clear cut harvest, and (2) to encourage old growth forest structures. Long-term studies are required to test this hypothesis as most studies are conducted soon after harvest. Using epixylic bryophytes as indicators, this study addresses this knowledge gap. Fourteen years after harvest, we examined changes in epixylic bryophyte community composition richness and traits, and their microhabitats (coarse woody debris characteristics and microclimate) along an unharvested, partial cuts and clear cuts harvest treatment in 30 permanent plots established in the boreal black spruce (\\n              Picea mariana\\n              ) forests of northwestern Quebec, Canada. Our results were compared to those of an initial post-harvest study (year 5) and to a chronosequence of old growth forests to examine species changes over time and the similarity of bryophyte communities in partial cut and old growth forests. Coarse woody debris (CWD) volume by decay class varied among harvest treatments with partial cuts and clear cuts recording lower volumes of early decay CWD. The epixylic community was richer in partial cuts than in mature unharvested forests and clear cuts. In addition, species richness and overall abundance doubled in partial and clear cuts between years 5 and 14. Species composition also differed among treatments between years 5 and 14. Furthermore, conditions in partial cut stands supported small, drought sensitive, and old growth confined species that are threatened by conditions in clear cut stands. Lastly, over time, species composition in partial cuts became more similar to old growth forests. Partial cuts reduced harvest impacts by continuing to provide favorable microhabitat conditions that support epixylic bryophytes. Also, partial cut harvest has the potential to encourage old growth species assemblages, which has been a major concern for biodiversity conservation in managed forest landscapes. Our findings support the promotion of partial cut harvest as an effective strategy to achieve species and habitat conservation goals.","container-title":"Frontiers in Forests and Global Change","DOI":"10.3389/ffgc.2021.674887","ISSN":"2624-893X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. For. Glob. Change","page":"674887","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Bryophyte Conservation in Managed Boreal Landscapes: Fourteen-Year Impacts of Partial Cuts on Epixylic Bryophytes","title-short":"Bryophyte Conservation in Managed Boreal Landscapes","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/ffgc.2021.674887/full","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Opoku-Nyame","given":"Jeffrey"},{"family":"Leduc","given":"Alain"},{"family":"Fenton","given":"Nicole J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",6,3]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Though even leaving small amounts of trees or patches is better than traditional clear-felling ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"upqwpYHf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lena Gustafsson, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Lena Gustafsson, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CT8UIWCJ"],"itemData":{"id":31383,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              Retention approaches in forest management are today common in several North European countries, integrated into the clearcutting practice as a way to promote biodiversity and maintain ecosystem functions. Individual green trees and retention patches (tree groups) are retained at final harvest, and deadwood is left at site or created. Here, we review research on retention in Sweden, Finland, Norway, the Baltic States, and NW Russia, with special focus on biodiversity. Following the first publication in 1994, about 180 peer-reviewed articles have been published. We present results from a systematic search of the retention literature, separated into the following topics: buffer zones, retention patches, high stumps, other types of deadwood, European aspen\\n              Populus tremula\\n              , and cost-efficiency. Russian literature is synthesized separately since studies from this region have so far almost exclusively been published in the Russian language. Furthermore, we describe six ongoing large-scale, replicated experiments with varying retention levels, five in Finland and one in Sweden, and summarize their main results. Among main conclusions for practice from the literature and experiments are that retention patches as large as 0.5 ha and 10-m-wide buffers to watercourses are not enough to maintain pre-harvest species composition but survival of forest species is still larger than on conventional clearcuts. Deadwood on clearcuts may present important habitats to saproxylic species, including rare and red-listed ones and a prioritization of tree species per stand is recommended. We identify several important future research directions including switch of focus towards the landscape as well as the species population level. Surveys in parts of European Russia where retention has been unintentionally implemented already for a century would indicate possible future trajectories of biodiversity and their drivers in other regions of Northern Europe. A stronger link to ecological theory would help in study designs and in the formulation of predicted outcomes.","container-title":"Ecological Processes","DOI":"10.1186/s13717-019-0208-2","ISSN":"2192-1709","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Process","language":"en","page":"3","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Research on retention forestry in Northern Europe","URL":"https://ecologicalprocesses.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s13717-019-0208-2","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Gustafsson","given":"Lena"},{"family":"Hannerz","given":"Mats"},{"family":"Koivula","given":"Matti"},{"family":"Shorohova","given":"Ekaterina"},{"family":"Vanha-Majamaa","given":"Ilkka"},{"family":"Weslien","given":"Jan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, tree retention comprising a minor proportion of the volume of harvestable timber (often 1-10%), which makes it practically impossible to avoid edge effects and random demographic effects on the forest stands. Maintaining more of the mature forest characteristics in production forests would require lower harvest intensities in some areas than is currently typical. Determining exact levels that are required to secure long-term viable populations of different species, as well as the most cost-efficient implementation of these conservation measures, remains a major challenge for future research ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ahsknlq2tn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lena Gustafsson, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Lena Gustafsson, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31268,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HMGHGZ5Q"],"itemData":{"id":31268,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","DOI":"10.1080/02827581.2010.497495","ISSN":"0282-7581, 1651-1891","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","language":"en","page":"295-308","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Tree retention as a conservation measure in clear-cut forests of northern Europe: a review of ecological consequences","title-short":"Tree retention as a conservation measure in clear-cut forests of northern Europe","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/02827581.2010.497495","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Gustafsson","given":"Lena"},{"family":"Kouki","given":"Jari"},{"family":"Sverdrup-Thygeson","given":"Anne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	There is a continued increase in the amount of wood removals globally through industrial logging practices (Figure 3.56). In 2019, global wood removals were estimated at 3.97 billion m3, of which 2.02 billion m3 was industrial roundwood and 1.95 billion m3 wood fuel. The year 2018 had the highest level of production and trade values for global wood removals and all major wood-based products since 1947 (data from the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, http://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data). The demand for and the consumption of wood products is escalating in line with growing populations and incomes, a trend expected to continue in the coming decades ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1o80cabrcp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2010b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2010b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34891,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BGL5M5XF"],"itemData":{"id":34891,"type":"book","collection-number":"163","collection-title":"FAO forestry paper","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-106654-6","language":"eng","note":"OCLC: 838335300","number-of-pages":"340","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Global forest resources assessment 2010: Main report","title-short":"Global forest resources assessment 2010","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. North American and European countries have the highest global wood yields ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a6930i10ua","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Chaudhary, Carrasco, & Kastner, 2017)","plainCitation":"(A. Chaudhary, Carrasco, & Kastner, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31278,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ACTG7B2Z"],"itemData":{"id":31278,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science of The Total Environment","DOI":"10.1016/j.scitotenv.2017.02.078","ISSN":"00489697","journalAbbreviation":"Science of The Total Environment","language":"en","page":"985-994","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Linking national wood consumption with global biodiversity and ecosystem service losses","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0048969717303194","volume":"586","author":[{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"Abhishek"},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L. Roman"},{"family":"Kastner","given":"Thomas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which is partly attributed to clear-felling regimes prevalent in temperate Europe/North American countries with high yields compared with low-yield selective logging in the tropics ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a8o0s5jci6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Chaudhary, Burivalova, Koh, & Hellweg, 2016)","plainCitation":"(A. Chaudhary, Burivalova, Koh, & Hellweg, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1139,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IAFKS9J3"],"itemData":{"id":1139,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep23954","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"23954","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impact of Forest Management on Species Richness: Global Meta-Analysis and Economic Trade-Offs","title-short":"Impact of Forest Management on Species Richness","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/srep23954","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"Abhishek"},{"family":"Burivalova","given":"Zuzana"},{"family":"Koh","given":"Lian Pin"},{"family":"Hellweg","given":"Stefanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are also large-scale imports of timber products by a limited number of countries especially China and the United States of America. The globalization of trade has enabled such countries to reduce local forest exploitation and achieve forest transitions from net deforestation to net reforestation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2kc9goiu9o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kastner, Erb, & Nonhebel, 2011; Meyfroidt, Rudel, & Lambin, 2010; Mills Busa, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Kastner, Erb, & Nonhebel, 2011; Meyfroidt, Rudel, & Lambin, 2010; Mills Busa, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31393,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N5BRU7WM"],"itemData":{"id":31393,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2011.05.003","ISSN":"09593780","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"947-956","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"International wood trade and forest change: A global analysis","title-short":"International wood trade and forest change","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S095937801100080X","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Kastner","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Erb","given":"Karl-Heinz"},{"family":"Nonhebel","given":"Sanderine"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31253,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UKFRPM2E"],"itemData":{"id":31253,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1014773107","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"49","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","language":"en","page":"20917-20922","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forest transitions, trade, and the global displacement of land use","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/doi/10.1073/pnas.1014773107","volume":"107","author":[{"family":"Meyfroidt","given":"P."},{"family":"Rudel","given":"T. K."},{"family":"Lambin","given":"E. F."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",12,7]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31255,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FBJG5BAU"],"itemData":{"id":31255,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/j.1755-263X.2012.00304.x","ISSN":"1755263X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Letters","language":"en","page":"192-199","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Deforestation beyond borders: Addressing the disparity between production and consumption of global resources: Deforestation beyond borders","title-short":"Deforestation beyond borders","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1755-263X.2012.00304.x","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Mills Busa","given":"Julianne H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",6]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.


Figure 3.56 Global wood removals 1990-2019. Data from FAO database ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a149808cefm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2017a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2017a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26775,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HEL9AT6T","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4QJ596GD"],"itemData":{"id":26775,"type":"webpage","language":"it","title":"FAOSTAT statistical database","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY-NC- SA 3.0 IGO. See data management report for the figure at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6453131.

	Some of the logging practices in species-rich tropical forests have been reported to resemble mining operations at the species level ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a20puhkoq3b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}mez Pompa, 1989; N. Johnson & Cabarle, 1993; Moad & Whitmore, 1994; M Schulze, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Gómez Pompa, 1989; N. Johnson & Cabarle, 1993; Moad & Whitmore, 1994; M Schulze, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31249,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L96QNIPZ"],"itemData":{"id":31249,"type":"book","event-place":"Cáceres","ISBN":"978-84-7723-044-1","language":"Spanish","note":"OCLC: 801872337","publisher":"Universidad de Extremadura. Servicio de Publicaciones","publisher-place":"Cáceres","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Colección de ejercicios de ingeniería rural (hidráulica)","author":[{"family":"Gómez Pompa","given":"Pedro"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1989"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31297,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZ4526UA"],"itemData":{"id":31297,"type":"book","call-number":"SD247 .J64 1993","event-place":"Washington, D.C., USA","ISBN":"978-0-915825-90-5","number-of-pages":"71","publisher":"World Resources Institute","publisher-place":"Washington, D.C., USA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Surviving the cut: natural forest management in the humid tropics","title-short":"Surviving the cut","author":[{"family":"Johnson","given":"Nels"},{"family":"Cabarle","given":"Bruce"}],"contributor":[{"family":"World Resources Institute","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31231,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2EH64FRG"],"itemData":{"id":31231,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","DOI":"10.1300/J091v01n04_02","ISSN":"1054-9811, 1540-756X","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","language":"en","page":"25-63","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Tropical Forest Management in the Asia-Pacific Region","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1300/J091v01n04_02","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Moad","given":"Alexander S."},{"family":"Whitmore","given":"Jacob L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994",5,23]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31233,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YBIKN3EF"],"itemData":{"id":31233,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Revista Ciência Hoje","issue":"36","page":"66-69","title":"Madeiras nobres em perigo: Práticas e leis atuais de manejo florestal não garantem exploração sustentável","volume":"241","author":[{"family":"Schulze","given":"M"},{"family":"Vidal","given":"E"},{"family":"Grogan","given":"J"},{"family":"Zweede","given":"J"},{"family":"Zarin","given":"D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, where a single, or group, or wider community of high value timber species are targeted for extraction. In the past, major target species included the big leaf Mahogany (Swietenia macrophylla), Brazilwood or Pau-brasil (Caesalpinia echinata), Brazil-nuts (Bertholletia excelsa), rosewood (Dalbergia nigra and Aniba rosaeodora) and others ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1fe56rnfqs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Martini, 1994; Mark Schulze, Grogan, Landis, & Vidal, 2008; Ver\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}ssimo, 1995)","plainCitation":"(Martini, 1994; Mark Schulze, Grogan, Landis, & Vidal, 2008; Veríssimo, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31280,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XJQ7TXZY"],"itemData":{"id":31280,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The impacts of logging on Amazonian ecosystems has been the focus of considerable attention both within and outside of Amazonia. However, the impacts of logging on individual timber species has not been at all adequately investigated. Logging affects timber species by: (1) removing mature individuals that are important sources of seeds; (2) damaging seedlings and saplings (\\n              i.e\\n              future generations of canopy adults); and (3) creating conditions that favour fire by opening the canopy and leaving slash as fuel on the forest floor.\\n            \\n            In this study we summarize information on the ecological characteristics of 305 timber species in Brazilian Amazonia. We identify seven ecological parameters that are useful in evaluating a species' ability to resist the negative impacts of logging. These characteristics are: (1) effective long-distance dispersal ability; (2) abundance of saplings in forest regeneration; (3) rapid growth; (4) ability to resprout; (5) capacity to withstand fire; (6) broad geographic distribution; and (7) high density of adults. We hypothesize thai species with characteristics opposed to these parameters and subjected to intense logging pressure will have difficulty in maintaining their populations in logging regions.\\n            \\n              We use a simple scoring system to rank species with regard to their hypothesized ability to withstand logging impacts. Among the species that are potentially susceptible to logging impacts are\\n              Euxylophora paraensis\\n              (‘Pau Amarelo’) and\\n              Swietenia macrophylla\\n              (American Mahogany). The sawn lumber from these two species goes principally to European and North American buyers, revealing a direct link between First World consumption and possible biodiversity impoverishment in the Brazilian rain-forest. These two species, and others that might experience population reductions as a result of logging, merit special study.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892900024589","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Conserv.","language":"en","page":"152-162","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An Attempt to Predict Which Amazonian Tree Species May be Threatened by Logging Activities","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0376892900024589/type/journal_article","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Martini","given":"Adriana M. Z."},{"family":"Rosa","given":"Nelson de A."},{"family":"Uhl","given":"Christopher"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31286,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4WWB7E6H"],"itemData":{"id":31286,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2008.02.051","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"1443-1457","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"How rare is too rare to harvest?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0378112708002302","volume":"256","author":[{"family":"Schulze","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Grogan","given":"James"},{"family":"Landis","given":"R. Matthew"},{"family":"Vidal","given":"Edson"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31285,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/55UZUE6Q"],"itemData":{"id":31285,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/0378-1127(94)03432-V","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"39-60","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Extraction of a high-value natural resource in Amazonia: the case of mahogany","title-short":"Extraction of a high-value natural resource in Amazonia","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/037811279403432V","volume":"72","author":[{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Adalberto"},{"family":"Barreto","given":"Paulo"},{"family":"Tarifa","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Uhl","given":"Christopher"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995",3]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Due to these practices, some species were reported endangered and added to Appendix II of Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. With scarcity and restrictions in extraction and trade of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listed species, new species are targeted. This practice has led to severe and dense reductions of adult populations or old growth timber stocks, often at large spatial scales ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a7dhnt8oj0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Uhl, Ver\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}ssimo, Mattos, Brandino, & Vieira, 1991; Ver\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}ssimo, Barreto, Mattos, Tarifa, & Uhl, 1992; Ver\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}ssimo, 1995)","plainCitation":"(Uhl, Veríssimo, Mattos, Brandino, & Vieira, 1991; Veríssimo, Barreto, Mattos, Tarifa, & Uhl, 1992; Veríssimo, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31232,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SHKB2GK4"],"itemData":{"id":31232,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest ecology and management","issue":"3-4","page":"243-273","title":"Social, economic, and ecological consequences of selective logging in an Amazon frontier: the case of Tailândia","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Uhl","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Adalberto"},{"family":"Mattos","given":"Marli Maria"},{"family":"Brandino","given":"Zeni"},{"family":"Vieira","given":"Ima Célia Guimarães"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31230,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ICHECVBJ"],"itemData":{"id":31230,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/0378-1127(92)90099-U","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"1-4","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"169-199","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Logging impacts and prospects for sustainable forest management in an old Amazonian frontier: The case of Paragominas","title-short":"Logging impacts and prospects for sustainable forest management in an old Amazonian frontier","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/037811279290099U","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Adalberto"},{"family":"Barreto","given":"Paulo"},{"family":"Mattos","given":"Marli"},{"family":"Tarifa","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Uhl","given":"Christopher"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992",12]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31285,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/55UZUE6Q"],"itemData":{"id":31285,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/0378-1127(94)03432-V","ISSN":"03781127","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"39-60","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Extraction of a high-value natural resource in Amazonia: the case of mahogany","title-short":"Extraction of a high-value natural resource in Amazonia","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/037811279403432V","volume":"72","author":[{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Adalberto"},{"family":"Barreto","given":"Paulo"},{"family":"Tarifa","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Uhl","given":"Christopher"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995",3]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Land occupation and timber extraction through conventional industrial logging has generated a culture of timber mining in many forest landscapes in the tropics, which has proved to be very persistent among some local stakeholders making an income from industrial timber extraction, which translates into low investments in operations or forest recovery. These cultural aspects of timber extraction in the tropics have been little studied, as well as shifts in social perceptions over time. 
      
3.3.4.4. Uses  

Like with the other practices reviewed in section 3.3, available knowledge on logging for a variety of uses was reviewed. In the case of logging, the relevant uses include decorative and aesthetic, energy, and shelter and construction. While it is certainly the case that many wood and tree products are used for ceremonial and cultural expression, food and feed, and medicine and hygiene, based on the definition of logging used in this assessment, these other uses (and the associated tree products) are discussed in the section on gathering (3.3.2).  

3.3.4.4.1. Decorative and aesthetic 

Harvesting timber for wood carvings is mainly a destructive process. The entire tree is felled at the trunk between 5 and 50 cm from the ground using a metal axe or chain saw and machetes, and the artists cut different lengths of timber from the fallen tree for their carvings ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aemk67d3sf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. D. Griffiths, Philips, & Godjuwa, 2003; Koenig, Altman, & Griffiths, 2011; Purata, Brosi, & Chibnik, 2004)","plainCitation":"(A. D. Griffiths, Philips, & Godjuwa, 2003; Koenig, Altman, & Griffiths, 2011; Purata, Brosi, & Chibnik, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1145,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B47JWXG2"],"itemData":{"id":1145,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Aboriginal people in remote Australian communities have limited opportunity to participate in the market economy. The Aboriginal arts and craft industry offers an important option to generate cash income and the sustained supply of native plants used in production is vital. We assessed the current harvest of a rainforest tree species commonly used for sculpture, Bombax ceiba. Information from field surveys, a regional rainforest inventory and sales figures from the Maningrida region were used to estimate the population size and structure of B. ceiba, the harvest intensity of patches and the economic value of the harvest. We estimate the density of B. ceiba to be 105.10±12.32 stems ha?1 and a regional population of 76615±14063 stems. A cumulative harvest over 20 years of 6% suggests approximately 4596 stems harvested, and the harvest was worth about $200,000 over the last three years. The majority (80%) of harvested stems coppiced and were suitable for harvesting. Logistic regression suggests both distance to outstation and township influence harvesting intensity, with some localised over-harvest. Rainforests provide a valuable resource to Aboriginal artists in Maningrida and their maintenance provides important conservation benefits to the Australian community.","archive":"Readcube","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/s0006-3207(02)00419-6","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"2","page":"295-305","title":"Harvest of Bombax ceiba for the Aboriginal arts industry, central Arnhem Land, Australia","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S0006-3207(02)00419-6","volume":"113","author":[{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Anthony D."},{"family":"Philips","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Godjuwa","given":"Charles"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":33047,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W65DVVJY"],"itemData":{"id":33047,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-011-9413-z","ISSN":"0300-7839, 1572-9915","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Hum Ecol","language":"en","page":"407-419","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Artists as Harvesters: Natural Resource Use by Indigenous Woodcarvers in Central Arnhem Land, Australia","title-short":"Artists as Harvesters","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10745-011-9413-z","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Koenig","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Altman","given":"Jon C."},{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Anthony D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1134,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4GDV2D4K"],"itemData":{"id":1134,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Riches of the forest: fruits, remedies and handicrafts in Latin America","event-place":"Desa Putra, Indonesia","language":"en","page":"5","publisher":"Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR)","publisher-place":"Desa Putra, Indonesia","source":"Zotero","title":"Alebrijes, wood carvings","URL":"http://www.cifor.org/library/1612/riches-of-the-forest-fruits-remedies-and-handicrafts-in-latin-america/","author":[{"family":"Purata","given":"Silvia E"},{"family":"Brosi","given":"Berry J"},{"family":"Chibnik","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In other instances, only the prime section of the stem is removed, leaving the rest of wood in the forest. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2nhgbfohr0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B Belcher, 2002)","plainCitation":"(B Belcher, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31399,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZUB6YY2F"],"itemData":{"id":31399,"type":"report","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.17528/cifor/001164","publisher":"Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR)","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Planning for woodcarving in the 21st century","URL":"http://www.cifor.org/library/1164/planning-for-woodcarving-in-the-21st-century","author":[{"family":"Belcher","given":"B"},{"family":"Braedt","given":"O"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"B"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"A"},{"family":"Choge","given":"S"},{"family":"De Jong","given":"W"},{"family":"Höft","given":"R"},{"family":"Ndoye","given":"O"},{"family":"Omeja","given":"P"},{"family":"Permadi","given":"P"},{"family":"Purata","given":"S"},{"family":"Rohadi","given":"D"},{"family":"Schmitt","given":"S"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"S"},{"family":"Standa-Gunda","given":"W"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Tree sizes are selected based on the size and nature of the sculpture to be made. This can depend on the cultural subject matter ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2f84l71qcn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Koenig, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Koenig, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33047,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W65DVVJY"],"itemData":{"id":33047,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-011-9413-z","ISSN":"0300-7839, 1572-9915","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Hum Ecol","language":"en","page":"407-419","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Artists as Harvesters: Natural Resource Use by Indigenous Woodcarvers in Central Arnhem Land, Australia","title-short":"Artists as Harvesters","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10745-011-9413-z","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Koenig","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Altman","given":"Jon C."},{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Anthony D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The average diameter of trees harvested for small sculptures such as birds would be smaller than those harvested to make canoes. However, the average diameter of trees harvested has significant implications on the sustainability of the tree species. Cutting down smaller sized trees before they produce and disperse seeds could affect the population of the tree species.  
	Wood for woodcarvings continues to be mainly harvested from the wild ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1befelt2gc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ellery, Cunningham, & Choge, 2005; A. D. Griffiths, 2003; Purata, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Ellery, Cunningham, & Choge, 2005; A. D. Griffiths, 2003; Purata, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31241,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y6GEQLQ8"],"itemData":{"id":31241,"type":"chapter","abstract":"The woodcarving industry in Kenya, characterized primarily by carved bowls, rhinoceroses and giraffes, is the largest of its kind in Africa, generating an estimated US$20 million per year in export revenue (Obunga, 1995; Choge, 2002). This enormous industry relies on indigenous forests for its raw materials since the favoured species include Brachylaena huillensis (muhugu, muhuhu or 'mahogany') and Combretum schumannii (mkongolo). However, less than 2 per cent of Kenya is covered by closed-canopy indigenous forest, partly due to a range of activities supporting livelihoods through the use of forest products. For example, most Dalbergia melanoxylon (African blackwood, or mpingo) stocks in woodland areas have been depleted because this species is so highly favoured for carving. Commercial carving is consequently characterized by over-exploitation of favoured tree species, use of trees in smaller and smaller size classes, long-distance log trade, and carvers constantly shifting to new resource-rich frontiers for raw materials (see Figure 3.1). While carvers are increasingly using a number of alternative species, the question remains whether alternative woods can support the growing industry, This case study focuses on the area surrounding Arabuko Sokoke Forest Reserve, one of the larger forest reserves on the Kenya coast. Wood scarcity affects not only the woodcarving industry, but the furniture and other industries as well. As a result, there is a need to diversify the species used and to promote· on-farm cultivation of fast-growing trees for a woodcarving supply. The change to alternative carving wood has intro<iuced a number of challenges to the industry. The carving qualities of many species are poorly known, and poor seasoning results in cracking and fungal infection of finished carvings, leading to wastage· and loss of income and market share.","container-title":"Carving out a Future","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-84977-070-5","page":"320","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London","title":"Chasing the wooden rhino: the case of woodcarving in Kenya.","URL":"http://197.248.75.118:8282/jspui/bitstream/123456789/481/1/CHASING%20THE%20WOODEN%20RHINO.pdf","author":[{"family":"Ellery","given":"William"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony"},{"family":"Choge","given":"Simon K."}],"editor":[{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"Bruce M."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1145,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B47JWXG2"],"itemData":{"id":1145,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Aboriginal people in remote Australian communities have limited opportunity to participate in the market economy. The Aboriginal arts and craft industry offers an important option to generate cash income and the sustained supply of native plants used in production is vital. We assessed the current harvest of a rainforest tree species commonly used for sculpture, Bombax ceiba. Information from field surveys, a regional rainforest inventory and sales figures from the Maningrida region were used to estimate the population size and structure of B. ceiba, the harvest intensity of patches and the economic value of the harvest. We estimate the density of B. ceiba to be 105.10±12.32 stems ha?1 and a regional population of 76615±14063 stems. A cumulative harvest over 20 years of 6% suggests approximately 4596 stems harvested, and the harvest was worth about $200,000 over the last three years. The majority (80%) of harvested stems coppiced and were suitable for harvesting. Logistic regression suggests both distance to outstation and township influence harvesting intensity, with some localised over-harvest. Rainforests provide a valuable resource to Aboriginal artists in Maningrida and their maintenance provides important conservation benefits to the Australian community.","archive":"Readcube","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/s0006-3207(02)00419-6","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"2","page":"295-305","title":"Harvest of Bombax ceiba for the Aboriginal arts industry, central Arnhem Land, Australia","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S0006-3207(02)00419-6","volume":"113","author":[{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Anthony D."},{"family":"Philips","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Godjuwa","given":"Charles"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":1134,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4GDV2D4K"],"itemData":{"id":1134,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Riches of the forest: fruits, remedies and handicrafts in Latin America","event-place":"Desa Putra, Indonesia","language":"en","page":"5","publisher":"Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR)","publisher-place":"Desa Putra, Indonesia","source":"Zotero","title":"Alebrijes, wood carvings","URL":"http://www.cifor.org/library/1612/riches-of-the-forest-fruits-remedies-and-handicrafts-in-latin-america/","author":[{"family":"Purata","given":"Silvia E"},{"family":"Brosi","given":"Berry J"},{"family":"Chibnik","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  These include forests on public land, communal and private forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ad2fulrq15","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ellery, 2005; A. D. Griffiths, 2003; Koenig, 2011; Matose, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Ellery, 2005; A. D. Griffiths, 2003; Koenig, 2011; Matose, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31241,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y6GEQLQ8"],"itemData":{"id":31241,"type":"chapter","abstract":"The woodcarving industry in Kenya, characterized primarily by carved bowls, rhinoceroses and giraffes, is the largest of its kind in Africa, generating an estimated US$20 million per year in export revenue (Obunga, 1995; Choge, 2002). This enormous industry relies on indigenous forests for its raw materials since the favoured species include Brachylaena huillensis (muhugu, muhuhu or 'mahogany') and Combretum schumannii (mkongolo). However, less than 2 per cent of Kenya is covered by closed-canopy indigenous forest, partly due to a range of activities supporting livelihoods through the use of forest products. For example, most Dalbergia melanoxylon (African blackwood, or mpingo) stocks in woodland areas have been depleted because this species is so highly favoured for carving. Commercial carving is consequently characterized by over-exploitation of favoured tree species, use of trees in smaller and smaller size classes, long-distance log trade, and carvers constantly shifting to new resource-rich frontiers for raw materials (see Figure 3.1). While carvers are increasingly using a number of alternative species, the question remains whether alternative woods can support the growing industry, This case study focuses on the area surrounding Arabuko Sokoke Forest Reserve, one of the larger forest reserves on the Kenya coast. Wood scarcity affects not only the woodcarving industry, but the furniture and other industries as well. As a result, there is a need to diversify the species used and to promote· on-farm cultivation of fast-growing trees for a woodcarving supply. The change to alternative carving wood has intro<iuced a number of challenges to the industry. The carving qualities of many species are poorly known, and poor seasoning results in cracking and fungal infection of finished carvings, leading to wastage· and loss of income and market share.","container-title":"Carving out a Future","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-84977-070-5","page":"320","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London","title":"Chasing the wooden rhino: the case of woodcarving in Kenya.","URL":"http://197.248.75.118:8282/jspui/bitstream/123456789/481/1/CHASING%20THE%20WOODEN%20RHINO.pdf","author":[{"family":"Ellery","given":"William"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony"},{"family":"Choge","given":"Simon K."}],"editor":[{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"Bruce M."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1145,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B47JWXG2"],"itemData":{"id":1145,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Aboriginal people in remote Australian communities have limited opportunity to participate in the market economy. The Aboriginal arts and craft industry offers an important option to generate cash income and the sustained supply of native plants used in production is vital. We assessed the current harvest of a rainforest tree species commonly used for sculpture, Bombax ceiba. Information from field surveys, a regional rainforest inventory and sales figures from the Maningrida region were used to estimate the population size and structure of B. ceiba, the harvest intensity of patches and the economic value of the harvest. We estimate the density of B. ceiba to be 105.10±12.32 stems ha?1 and a regional population of 76615±14063 stems. A cumulative harvest over 20 years of 6% suggests approximately 4596 stems harvested, and the harvest was worth about $200,000 over the last three years. The majority (80%) of harvested stems coppiced and were suitable for harvesting. Logistic regression suggests both distance to outstation and township influence harvesting intensity, with some localised over-harvest. Rainforests provide a valuable resource to Aboriginal artists in Maningrida and their maintenance provides important conservation benefits to the Australian community.","archive":"Readcube","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/s0006-3207(02)00419-6","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"2","page":"295-305","title":"Harvest of Bombax ceiba for the Aboriginal arts industry, central Arnhem Land, Australia","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S0006-3207(02)00419-6","volume":"113","author":[{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Anthony D."},{"family":"Philips","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Godjuwa","given":"Charles"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":33047,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W65DVVJY"],"itemData":{"id":33047,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-011-9413-z","ISSN":"0300-7839, 1572-9915","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Hum Ecol","language":"en","page":"407-419","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Artists as Harvesters: Natural Resource Use by Indigenous Woodcarvers in Central Arnhem Land, Australia","title-short":"Artists as Harvesters","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10745-011-9413-z","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Koenig","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Altman","given":"Jon C."},{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Anthony D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1125,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YMPMXRFA"],"itemData":{"id":1125,"type":"paper-conference","abstract":"The paper uses commodity chain analysis to examine the transformation of access and control in woodcraft markets around Victoria Falls in the context of changing policies in the last two decades. The mushrooming of a transnational market for curios has begun to transform the use and management of forests. The paper discusses the distribution of benefits along the chain. It also elaborates on the relationship between property rights operating at a variety of scales beginning with trees on individual farmland or private property, trees on communal woodlands or common property and trees on state land (protected forests and national parks) or state property with other strategies that actors use to gain and control access at different stages of the commodity chain including harvesting, processing, and marketing. In conclusion, the implications for policy are discussed.","container-title":"In survival of the commons: Mounting challenges and new realities, the 11th conference of the International Association for the Study of Common Property","event-place":"Bali, Indonesia","language":"en","page":"16","publisher-place":"Bali, Indonesia","source":"Zotero","title":"Access mapping and chains: The woodcraft curio market around Victoria Falls, Zimbabwe","author":[{"family":"Matose","given":"Frank"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, there are no certain global statistics of volumes of wood extracted for wood carvings as this is primarily an informal activity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2j0t63aic6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ellery, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Ellery, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31241,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y6GEQLQ8"],"itemData":{"id":31241,"type":"chapter","abstract":"The woodcarving industry in Kenya, characterized primarily by carved bowls, rhinoceroses and giraffes, is the largest of its kind in Africa, generating an estimated US$20 million per year in export revenue (Obunga, 1995; Choge, 2002). This enormous industry relies on indigenous forests for its raw materials since the favoured species include Brachylaena huillensis (muhugu, muhuhu or 'mahogany') and Combretum schumannii (mkongolo). However, less than 2 per cent of Kenya is covered by closed-canopy indigenous forest, partly due to a range of activities supporting livelihoods through the use of forest products. For example, most Dalbergia melanoxylon (African blackwood, or mpingo) stocks in woodland areas have been depleted because this species is so highly favoured for carving. Commercial carving is consequently characterized by over-exploitation of favoured tree species, use of trees in smaller and smaller size classes, long-distance log trade, and carvers constantly shifting to new resource-rich frontiers for raw materials (see Figure 3.1). While carvers are increasingly using a number of alternative species, the question remains whether alternative woods can support the growing industry, This case study focuses on the area surrounding Arabuko Sokoke Forest Reserve, one of the larger forest reserves on the Kenya coast. Wood scarcity affects not only the woodcarving industry, but the furniture and other industries as well. As a result, there is a need to diversify the species used and to promote· on-farm cultivation of fast-growing trees for a woodcarving supply. The change to alternative carving wood has intro<iuced a number of challenges to the industry. The carving qualities of many species are poorly known, and poor seasoning results in cracking and fungal infection of finished carvings, leading to wastage· and loss of income and market share.","container-title":"Carving out a Future","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-84977-070-5","page":"320","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London","title":"Chasing the wooden rhino: the case of woodcarving in Kenya.","URL":"http://197.248.75.118:8282/jspui/bitstream/123456789/481/1/CHASING%20THE%20WOODEN%20RHINO.pdf","author":[{"family":"Ellery","given":"William"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony"},{"family":"Choge","given":"Simon K."}],"editor":[{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"Bruce M."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, however that does not mean it is small in scale and scope. In 2002, woodcarving in Kenya were estimated to consume 50,000 trees per year (0.7% of the total round wood market share in Kenya). But although the amount of wood extracted for the purpose seems low, the wood carving practice relies on a selected number of species with desired qualities such as close grain, tensile strength and resistance to cracking or insect attack. In addition, a small range of different timbers are often favored as a result of social, cultural and historical factors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a6cmeafnnj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. B. Cunningham, 2005)","plainCitation":"(A. B. Cunningham, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31237,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PEXC25RM"],"itemData":{"id":31237,"type":"book","call-number":"HD9774.W652 C37 2005","collection-title":"People and plants conservation series","event-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","ISBN":"978-1-84407-045-9","note":"OCLC: ocm57579353","number-of-pages":"293","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Carving out a future: forests, livelihoods and the international woodcarving trade","title-short":"Carving out a future","editor":[{"family":"Cunningham","given":"A. B."},{"family":"Campbell","given":"B. M."},{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian M."},{"family":"World Wildlife Fund","given":""},{"family":"Unesco","given":""},{"family":"Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which end up being over exploited. This has led to over exploitation of the particular wild species, especially those with other purposes, of which some are listed among the endangered species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"akqfahlgst","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. B. Cunningham, 2005; Ellery, 2005)","plainCitation":"(A. B. Cunningham, 2005; Ellery, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31241,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y6GEQLQ8"],"itemData":{"id":31241,"type":"chapter","abstract":"The woodcarving industry in Kenya, characterized primarily by carved bowls, rhinoceroses and giraffes, is the largest of its kind in Africa, generating an estimated US$20 million per year in export revenue (Obunga, 1995; Choge, 2002). This enormous industry relies on indigenous forests for its raw materials since the favoured species include Brachylaena huillensis (muhugu, muhuhu or 'mahogany') and Combretum schumannii (mkongolo). However, less than 2 per cent of Kenya is covered by closed-canopy indigenous forest, partly due to a range of activities supporting livelihoods through the use of forest products. For example, most Dalbergia melanoxylon (African blackwood, or mpingo) stocks in woodland areas have been depleted because this species is so highly favoured for carving. Commercial carving is consequently characterized by over-exploitation of favoured tree species, use of trees in smaller and smaller size classes, long-distance log trade, and carvers constantly shifting to new resource-rich frontiers for raw materials (see Figure 3.1). While carvers are increasingly using a number of alternative species, the question remains whether alternative woods can support the growing industry, This case study focuses on the area surrounding Arabuko Sokoke Forest Reserve, one of the larger forest reserves on the Kenya coast. Wood scarcity affects not only the woodcarving industry, but the furniture and other industries as well. As a result, there is a need to diversify the species used and to promote· on-farm cultivation of fast-growing trees for a woodcarving supply. The change to alternative carving wood has intro<iuced a number of challenges to the industry. The carving qualities of many species are poorly known, and poor seasoning results in cracking and fungal infection of finished carvings, leading to wastage· and loss of income and market share.","container-title":"Carving out a Future","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-84977-070-5","page":"320","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London","title":"Chasing the wooden rhino: the case of woodcarving in Kenya.","URL":"http://197.248.75.118:8282/jspui/bitstream/123456789/481/1/CHASING%20THE%20WOODEN%20RHINO.pdf","author":[{"family":"Ellery","given":"William"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony"},{"family":"Choge","given":"Simon K."}],"editor":[{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"Bruce M."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31237,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PEXC25RM"],"itemData":{"id":31237,"type":"book","call-number":"HD9774.W652 C37 2005","collection-title":"People and plants conservation series","event-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","ISBN":"978-1-84407-045-9","note":"OCLC: ocm57579353","number-of-pages":"293","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Carving out a future: forests, livelihoods and the international woodcarving trade","title-short":"Carving out a future","editor":[{"family":"Cunningham","given":"A. B."},{"family":"Campbell","given":"B. M."},{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian M."},{"family":"World Wildlife Fund","given":""},{"family":"Unesco","given":""},{"family":"Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
	From carving small household items, to carving the interior and exterior of houses and temples, ritual objects and decorative pieces, fashioning idols for various articles of furniture and for ceremonial objects ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a14jlrhc6k1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Saville, 1925)","plainCitation":"(Saville, 1925)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31240,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QTLBTAPS"],"itemData":{"id":31240,"type":"book","language":"en","number-of-pages":"248","publisher":"New York, Museum of the American Inidan, Heye Foundation","source":"Google Books","title":"The Wood-Carver ?s Art in Ancient Mexico","URL":"https://archive.org/details/woodcarversartin00savi/page/n21/mode/2up","author":[{"family":"Saville","given":"Marshall H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1925"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, carving traditions have mainly been associated with culture, technology and change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2tp23tk7d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. B. Cunningham, 2005)","plainCitation":"(A. B. Cunningham, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31237,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PEXC25RM"],"itemData":{"id":31237,"type":"book","call-number":"HD9774.W652 C37 2005","collection-title":"People and plants conservation series","event-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","ISBN":"978-1-84407-045-9","note":"OCLC: ocm57579353","number-of-pages":"293","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Carving out a future: forests, livelihoods and the international woodcarving trade","title-short":"Carving out a future","editor":[{"family":"Cunningham","given":"A. B."},{"family":"Campbell","given":"B. M."},{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian M."},{"family":"World Wildlife Fund","given":""},{"family":"Unesco","given":""},{"family":"Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The practice was mainly associated with particular communities stretching back many generations, carving particular types of pieces that were mainly associated with long standing cultural significance. For example, in the tropics, subtropics, pre-industrial societies of Europe and some northern temperate regions, woodcarvings were and for some, are still the major sources of social and cultural materials ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2q0n9j71eu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. B. Cunningham, 2005)","plainCitation":"(A. B. Cunningham, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31237,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PEXC25RM"],"itemData":{"id":31237,"type":"book","call-number":"HD9774.W652 C37 2005","collection-title":"People and plants conservation series","event-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","ISBN":"978-1-84407-045-9","note":"OCLC: ocm57579353","number-of-pages":"293","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Carving out a future: forests, livelihoods and the international woodcarving trade","title-short":"Carving out a future","editor":[{"family":"Cunningham","given":"A. B."},{"family":"Campbell","given":"B. M."},{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian M."},{"family":"World Wildlife Fund","given":""},{"family":"Unesco","given":""},{"family":"Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Whereas the practices have been socially and culturally sustainable among some woodcarving communities such as the Aboriginal wood carvers of Australia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1f87qiimom","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Koenig, Altman, Griffiths, & Kohen, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Koenig, Altman, Griffiths, & Kohen, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1136,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FLLVUA5Q"],"itemData":{"id":1136,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The growth of the Aboriginal Arts Industry in Australia over the last few decades has seen an increase in the number of indigenous communities producing woodcarvings. The timber used for carving is derived from locally available tree species. Whilst the practice relies on the continued supply of timber from native forests, very little is known about the harvesters and their production of sculpture. A long-term community art centre sales database from the Maningrida region of central Amhem Land was used to examine the changes the sculpture sector has experienced over the last 20 years. The results show that sculpture production has grown rapidly over the last two decades. Arts production is a flexible economic activity and carving is a popular artistic medium.","container-title":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","DOI":"10.1080/14728028.2007.9752580","ISSN":"1472-8028, 2164-3075","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","language":"en","page":"43-60","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"20 Years of Aboriginal Woodcarving in Arnhem land, Australia: Using art sales records to examine the Dynamics of Sculpture Production","title-short":"20 YEARS OF ABORIGINAL WOODCARVING IN ARNHEM LAND, AUSTRALIA","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/14728028.2007.9752580","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Koenig","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Altman","given":"Jon C."},{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Anthony D."},{"family":"Kohen","given":"Apolline"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, some communities such as those in the Mexican state of Oaxaca are engaged in carving novel creations without longstanding cultural significance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"apo8v3gcb1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Purata, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Purata, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1134,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4GDV2D4K"],"itemData":{"id":1134,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Riches of the forest: fruits, remedies and handicrafts in Latin America","event-place":"Desa Putra, Indonesia","language":"en","page":"5","publisher":"Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR)","publisher-place":"Desa Putra, Indonesia","source":"Zotero","title":"Alebrijes, wood carvings","URL":"http://www.cifor.org/library/1612/riches-of-the-forest-fruits-remedies-and-handicrafts-in-latin-america/","author":[{"family":"Purata","given":"Silvia E"},{"family":"Brosi","given":"Berry J"},{"family":"Chibnik","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	The wood carving industry has grown tremendously over the years, extending beyond the local and national to the international markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ain3403gsg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Altman, 2005; Ellery, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Altman, 2005; Ellery, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1135,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5SF7IUGN"],"itemData":{"id":1135,"type":"book","call-number":"N7401 .A725 2005","collection-title":"Kenneth Myer Lecture in Art & Entertainment Management","event-place":"Geelong, Vic","ISBN":"978-1-74156-010-7","language":"en","number-of-pages":"24","publisher":"Deakin University","publisher-place":"Geelong, Vic","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Brokering Aboriginal art: a critical perspective on marketing, institutions, and the state","title-short":"Brokering Aboriginal art","author":[{"family":"Altman","given":"Jon C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31241,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y6GEQLQ8"],"itemData":{"id":31241,"type":"chapter","abstract":"The woodcarving industry in Kenya, characterized primarily by carved bowls, rhinoceroses and giraffes, is the largest of its kind in Africa, generating an estimated US$20 million per year in export revenue (Obunga, 1995; Choge, 2002). This enormous industry relies on indigenous forests for its raw materials since the favoured species include Brachylaena huillensis (muhugu, muhuhu or 'mahogany') and Combretum schumannii (mkongolo). However, less than 2 per cent of Kenya is covered by closed-canopy indigenous forest, partly due to a range of activities supporting livelihoods through the use of forest products. For example, most Dalbergia melanoxylon (African blackwood, or mpingo) stocks in woodland areas have been depleted because this species is so highly favoured for carving. Commercial carving is consequently characterized by over-exploitation of favoured tree species, use of trees in smaller and smaller size classes, long-distance log trade, and carvers constantly shifting to new resource-rich frontiers for raw materials (see Figure 3.1). While carvers are increasingly using a number of alternative species, the question remains whether alternative woods can support the growing industry, This case study focuses on the area surrounding Arabuko Sokoke Forest Reserve, one of the larger forest reserves on the Kenya coast. Wood scarcity affects not only the woodcarving industry, but the furniture and other industries as well. As a result, there is a need to diversify the species used and to promote· on-farm cultivation of fast-growing trees for a woodcarving supply. The change to alternative carving wood has intro<iuced a number of challenges to the industry. The carving qualities of many species are poorly known, and poor seasoning results in cracking and fungal infection of finished carvings, leading to wastage· and loss of income and market share.","container-title":"Carving out a Future","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-84977-070-5","page":"320","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London","title":"Chasing the wooden rhino: the case of woodcarving in Kenya.","URL":"http://197.248.75.118:8282/jspui/bitstream/123456789/481/1/CHASING%20THE%20WOODEN%20RHINO.pdf","author":[{"family":"Ellery","given":"William"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony"},{"family":"Choge","given":"Simon K."}],"editor":[{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"Bruce M."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} which has increased demand of the wood carvings. The carvings are sold in a number of arenas including family workshops, markets and craft shops, either within the villages or other cities and countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2fadd1iupd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Purata, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Purata, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1134,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4GDV2D4K"],"itemData":{"id":1134,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Riches of the forest: fruits, remedies and handicrafts in Latin America","event-place":"Desa Putra, Indonesia","language":"en","page":"5","publisher":"Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR)","publisher-place":"Desa Putra, Indonesia","source":"Zotero","title":"Alebrijes, wood carvings","URL":"http://www.cifor.org/library/1612/riches-of-the-forest-fruits-remedies-and-handicrafts-in-latin-america/","author":[{"family":"Purata","given":"Silvia E"},{"family":"Brosi","given":"Berry J"},{"family":"Chibnik","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An activity that was once predominantly a men’s activity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1db1rer7sr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. B. Cunningham, 2005)","plainCitation":"(A. B. Cunningham, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31237,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PEXC25RM"],"itemData":{"id":31237,"type":"book","call-number":"HD9774.W652 C37 2005","collection-title":"People and plants conservation series","event-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","ISBN":"978-1-84407-045-9","note":"OCLC: ocm57579353","number-of-pages":"293","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Carving out a future: forests, livelihoods and the international woodcarving trade","title-short":"Carving out a future","editor":[{"family":"Cunningham","given":"A. B."},{"family":"Campbell","given":"B. M."},{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian M."},{"family":"World Wildlife Fund","given":""},{"family":"Unesco","given":""},{"family":"Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} has progressively increased number of women and youths involved, becoming a family activity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2i381enhh2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Koenig, 2007; Purata, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Koenig, 2007; Purata, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1136,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FLLVUA5Q"],"itemData":{"id":1136,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The growth of the Aboriginal Arts Industry in Australia over the last few decades has seen an increase in the number of indigenous communities producing woodcarvings. The timber used for carving is derived from locally available tree species. Whilst the practice relies on the continued supply of timber from native forests, very little is known about the harvesters and their production of sculpture. A long-term community art centre sales database from the Maningrida region of central Amhem Land was used to examine the changes the sculpture sector has experienced over the last 20 years. The results show that sculpture production has grown rapidly over the last two decades. Arts production is a flexible economic activity and carving is a popular artistic medium.","container-title":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","DOI":"10.1080/14728028.2007.9752580","ISSN":"1472-8028, 2164-3075","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","language":"en","page":"43-60","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"20 Years of Aboriginal Woodcarving in Arnhem land, Australia: Using art sales records to examine the Dynamics of Sculpture Production","title-short":"20 YEARS OF ABORIGINAL WOODCARVING IN ARNHEM LAND, AUSTRALIA","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/14728028.2007.9752580","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Koenig","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Altman","given":"Jon C."},{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Anthony D."},{"family":"Kohen","given":"Apolline"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1134,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4GDV2D4K"],"itemData":{"id":1134,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Riches of the forest: fruits, remedies and handicrafts in Latin America","event-place":"Desa Putra, Indonesia","language":"en","page":"5","publisher":"Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR)","publisher-place":"Desa Putra, Indonesia","source":"Zotero","title":"Alebrijes, wood carvings","URL":"http://www.cifor.org/library/1612/riches-of-the-forest-fruits-remedies-and-handicrafts-in-latin-america/","author":[{"family":"Purata","given":"Silvia E"},{"family":"Brosi","given":"Berry J"},{"family":"Chibnik","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The women involved are mainly spouses and children of prominent wood carvers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a11njqm088k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Koenig, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Koenig, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1136,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FLLVUA5Q"],"itemData":{"id":1136,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The growth of the Aboriginal Arts Industry in Australia over the last few decades has seen an increase in the number of indigenous communities producing woodcarvings. The timber used for carving is derived from locally available tree species. Whilst the practice relies on the continued supply of timber from native forests, very little is known about the harvesters and their production of sculpture. A long-term community art centre sales database from the Maningrida region of central Amhem Land was used to examine the changes the sculpture sector has experienced over the last 20 years. The results show that sculpture production has grown rapidly over the last two decades. Arts production is a flexible economic activity and carving is a popular artistic medium.","container-title":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","DOI":"10.1080/14728028.2007.9752580","ISSN":"1472-8028, 2164-3075","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","language":"en","page":"43-60","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"20 Years of Aboriginal Woodcarving in Arnhem land, Australia: Using art sales records to examine the Dynamics of Sculpture Production","title-short":"20 YEARS OF ABORIGINAL WOODCARVING IN ARNHEM LAND, AUSTRALIA","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/14728028.2007.9752580","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Koenig","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Altman","given":"Jon C."},{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Anthony D."},{"family":"Kohen","given":"Apolline"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, these are mainly involved in the less labor-intensive activities such as sanding, polishing and painting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1o8c7lvpc6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Matose, 2006; Purata, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Matose, 2006; Purata, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1125,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YMPMXRFA"],"itemData":{"id":1125,"type":"paper-conference","abstract":"The paper uses commodity chain analysis to examine the transformation of access and control in woodcraft markets around Victoria Falls in the context of changing policies in the last two decades. The mushrooming of a transnational market for curios has begun to transform the use and management of forests. The paper discusses the distribution of benefits along the chain. It also elaborates on the relationship between property rights operating at a variety of scales beginning with trees on individual farmland or private property, trees on communal woodlands or common property and trees on state land (protected forests and national parks) or state property with other strategies that actors use to gain and control access at different stages of the commodity chain including harvesting, processing, and marketing. In conclusion, the implications for policy are discussed.","container-title":"In survival of the commons: Mounting challenges and new realities, the 11th conference of the International Association for the Study of Common Property","event-place":"Bali, Indonesia","language":"en","page":"16","publisher-place":"Bali, Indonesia","source":"Zotero","title":"Access mapping and chains: The woodcraft curio market around Victoria Falls, Zimbabwe","author":[{"family":"Matose","given":"Frank"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":1134,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4GDV2D4K"],"itemData":{"id":1134,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Riches of the forest: fruits, remedies and handicrafts in Latin America","event-place":"Desa Putra, Indonesia","language":"en","page":"5","publisher":"Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR)","publisher-place":"Desa Putra, Indonesia","source":"Zotero","title":"Alebrijes, wood carvings","URL":"http://www.cifor.org/library/1612/riches-of-the-forest-fruits-remedies-and-handicrafts-in-latin-america/","author":[{"family":"Purata","given":"Silvia E"},{"family":"Brosi","given":"Berry J"},{"family":"Chibnik","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Other than the aesthetic values, these products have earned households, communities and national economies income. Wood carving is a major source of income through facilitating purchase of livelihood needs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2i5krbfpht","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Purata, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Purata, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1134,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4GDV2D4K"],"itemData":{"id":1134,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Riches of the forest: fruits, remedies and handicrafts in Latin America","event-place":"Desa Putra, Indonesia","language":"en","page":"5","publisher":"Center for International Forestry Research (CIFOR)","publisher-place":"Desa Putra, Indonesia","source":"Zotero","title":"Alebrijes, wood carvings","URL":"http://www.cifor.org/library/1612/riches-of-the-forest-fruits-remedies-and-handicrafts-in-latin-america/","author":[{"family":"Purata","given":"Silvia E"},{"family":"Brosi","given":"Berry J"},{"family":"Chibnik","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} especially among communities in dry environments that suffer from lack of agricultural opportunities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1hcpes0fkq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Matose, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Matose, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1125,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YMPMXRFA"],"itemData":{"id":1125,"type":"paper-conference","abstract":"The paper uses commodity chain analysis to examine the transformation of access and control in woodcraft markets around Victoria Falls in the context of changing policies in the last two decades. The mushrooming of a transnational market for curios has begun to transform the use and management of forests. The paper discusses the distribution of benefits along the chain. It also elaborates on the relationship between property rights operating at a variety of scales beginning with trees on individual farmland or private property, trees on communal woodlands or common property and trees on state land (protected forests and national parks) or state property with other strategies that actors use to gain and control access at different stages of the commodity chain including harvesting, processing, and marketing. In conclusion, the implications for policy are discussed.","container-title":"In survival of the commons: Mounting challenges and new realities, the 11th conference of the International Association for the Study of Common Property","event-place":"Bali, Indonesia","language":"en","page":"16","publisher-place":"Bali, Indonesia","source":"Zotero","title":"Access mapping and chains: The woodcraft curio market around Victoria Falls, Zimbabwe","author":[{"family":"Matose","given":"Frank"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, it is not possible to obtain exact numbers of people involved ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1hlntm35ff","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ellery, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Ellery, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31241,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y6GEQLQ8"],"itemData":{"id":31241,"type":"chapter","abstract":"The woodcarving industry in Kenya, characterized primarily by carved bowls, rhinoceroses and giraffes, is the largest of its kind in Africa, generating an estimated US$20 million per year in export revenue (Obunga, 1995; Choge, 2002). This enormous industry relies on indigenous forests for its raw materials since the favoured species include Brachylaena huillensis (muhugu, muhuhu or 'mahogany') and Combretum schumannii (mkongolo). However, less than 2 per cent of Kenya is covered by closed-canopy indigenous forest, partly due to a range of activities supporting livelihoods through the use of forest products. For example, most Dalbergia melanoxylon (African blackwood, or mpingo) stocks in woodland areas have been depleted because this species is so highly favoured for carving. Commercial carving is consequently characterized by over-exploitation of favoured tree species, use of trees in smaller and smaller size classes, long-distance log trade, and carvers constantly shifting to new resource-rich frontiers for raw materials (see Figure 3.1). While carvers are increasingly using a number of alternative species, the question remains whether alternative woods can support the growing industry, This case study focuses on the area surrounding Arabuko Sokoke Forest Reserve, one of the larger forest reserves on the Kenya coast. Wood scarcity affects not only the woodcarving industry, but the furniture and other industries as well. As a result, there is a need to diversify the species used and to promote· on-farm cultivation of fast-growing trees for a woodcarving supply. The change to alternative carving wood has intro<iuced a number of challenges to the industry. The carving qualities of many species are poorly known, and poor seasoning results in cracking and fungal infection of finished carvings, leading to wastage· and loss of income and market share.","container-title":"Carving out a Future","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-84977-070-5","page":"320","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London","title":"Chasing the wooden rhino: the case of woodcarving in Kenya.","URL":"http://197.248.75.118:8282/jspui/bitstream/123456789/481/1/CHASING%20THE%20WOODEN%20RHINO.pdf","author":[{"family":"Ellery","given":"William"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony"},{"family":"Choge","given":"Simon K."}],"editor":[{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"Bruce M."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and the value of the industry as a whole is hard to determine ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2fvjl0174f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. D. Griffiths, 2003)","plainCitation":"(A. D. Griffiths, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1145,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B47JWXG2"],"itemData":{"id":1145,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Aboriginal people in remote Australian communities have limited opportunity to participate in the market economy. The Aboriginal arts and craft industry offers an important option to generate cash income and the sustained supply of native plants used in production is vital. We assessed the current harvest of a rainforest tree species commonly used for sculpture, Bombax ceiba. Information from field surveys, a regional rainforest inventory and sales figures from the Maningrida region were used to estimate the population size and structure of B. ceiba, the harvest intensity of patches and the economic value of the harvest. We estimate the density of B. ceiba to be 105.10±12.32 stems ha?1 and a regional population of 76615±14063 stems. A cumulative harvest over 20 years of 6% suggests approximately 4596 stems harvested, and the harvest was worth about $200,000 over the last three years. The majority (80%) of harvested stems coppiced and were suitable for harvesting. Logistic regression suggests both distance to outstation and township influence harvesting intensity, with some localised over-harvest. Rainforests provide a valuable resource to Aboriginal artists in Maningrida and their maintenance provides important conservation benefits to the Australian community.","archive":"Readcube","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/s0006-3207(02)00419-6","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"2","page":"295-305","title":"Harvest of Bombax ceiba for the Aboriginal arts industry, central Arnhem Land, Australia","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S0006-3207(02)00419-6","volume":"113","author":[{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Anthony D."},{"family":"Philips","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Godjuwa","given":"Charles"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} due to its dynamic nature. The wood carving industry in Kenya generates about 20 million United States dollars per year in export revenue ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1ge8i9taam","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Choge, 2002; Obunga, 1995)","plainCitation":"(Choge, 2002; Obunga, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31235,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W5GY8PK8"],"itemData":{"id":31235,"type":"thesis","event-place":"South Africa","genre":"Masters thesis","publisher":", University of Natal","publisher-place":"South Africa","title":"Study of Economic aspects of the Wood Carving Industry in Kenya: Implications for policy development to make the industry more sustainable","author":[{"family":"Choge","given":"Simon K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31236,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W5GZ6LRY"],"itemData":{"id":31236,"type":"report","genre":"Technical progress report","title":"Sustainable Development Of Wood Carving Industry In Kenya","author":[{"family":"Obunga","given":"R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, employing about 40% of thef national formal timber industry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1v7tql934h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ellery, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Ellery, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31241,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y6GEQLQ8"],"itemData":{"id":31241,"type":"chapter","abstract":"The woodcarving industry in Kenya, characterized primarily by carved bowls, rhinoceroses and giraffes, is the largest of its kind in Africa, generating an estimated US$20 million per year in export revenue (Obunga, 1995; Choge, 2002). This enormous industry relies on indigenous forests for its raw materials since the favoured species include Brachylaena huillensis (muhugu, muhuhu or 'mahogany') and Combretum schumannii (mkongolo). However, less than 2 per cent of Kenya is covered by closed-canopy indigenous forest, partly due to a range of activities supporting livelihoods through the use of forest products. For example, most Dalbergia melanoxylon (African blackwood, or mpingo) stocks in woodland areas have been depleted because this species is so highly favoured for carving. Commercial carving is consequently characterized by over-exploitation of favoured tree species, use of trees in smaller and smaller size classes, long-distance log trade, and carvers constantly shifting to new resource-rich frontiers for raw materials (see Figure 3.1). While carvers are increasingly using a number of alternative species, the question remains whether alternative woods can support the growing industry, This case study focuses on the area surrounding Arabuko Sokoke Forest Reserve, one of the larger forest reserves on the Kenya coast. Wood scarcity affects not only the woodcarving industry, but the furniture and other industries as well. As a result, there is a need to diversify the species used and to promote· on-farm cultivation of fast-growing trees for a woodcarving supply. The change to alternative carving wood has intro<iuced a number of challenges to the industry. The carving qualities of many species are poorly known, and poor seasoning results in cracking and fungal infection of finished carvings, leading to wastage· and loss of income and market share.","container-title":"Carving out a Future","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-84977-070-5","page":"320","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London","title":"Chasing the wooden rhino: the case of woodcarving in Kenya.","URL":"http://197.248.75.118:8282/jspui/bitstream/123456789/481/1/CHASING%20THE%20WOODEN%20RHINO.pdf","author":[{"family":"Ellery","given":"William"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony"},{"family":"Choge","given":"Simon K."}],"editor":[{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"Bruce M."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Around the Victoria falls in Zimbabwe, the industry provides a source of livelihood to nearly a thousand households in a dry part of the country with households getting around 14 to 60 United States dollars a month 
	The timber trade and woodcarving are closely linked, particularly in Asia, where timber is intricately carved to make buildings, doors or furniture. Trade in carvings is not new. It is bigger than ever before; however, it has spread internationally, rather than regionally, and has focused on a far smaller resource base ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2c4uoa58c0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. B. Cunningham, 2005)","plainCitation":"(A. B. Cunningham, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31237,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PEXC25RM"],"itemData":{"id":31237,"type":"book","call-number":"HD9774.W652 C37 2005","collection-title":"People and plants conservation series","event-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","ISBN":"978-1-84407-045-9","note":"OCLC: ocm57579353","number-of-pages":"293","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Sterling, VA","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Carving out a future: forests, livelihoods and the international woodcarving trade","title-short":"Carving out a future","editor":[{"family":"Cunningham","given":"A. B."},{"family":"Campbell","given":"B. M."},{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian M."},{"family":"World Wildlife Fund","given":""},{"family":"Unesco","given":""},{"family":"Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Since many of the species used for wood carvings are endangered/threatened, their use and trade are restricted by both national regulations (for example sandalwood is restricted by the Kingdom of Tonga sandalwood regulations 2016, Tamil Nadu sandalwood possession rules, 1970, and the sandalwood (limitation of removal of sandalwood) order 1996 in Western Australia) and the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aq3n24ttab","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Groves & Rutherford, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Groves & Rutherford, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31401,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ESAUNRYD"],"itemData":{"id":31401,"type":"book","call-number":"582.16","event-place":"Richmond (GB)","ISBN":"978-1-84246-592-9","language":"eng","publisher":"Kew publishing","publisher-place":"Richmond (GB)","source":"BnF ISBN","title":"CITES and timber guide: a guide to CITES-listed tree species","title-short":"CITES and timber guide","URL":"https://jordbruksverket.se/download/18.7d044c501710eb9f893e4656/1585320243840/CITES-and-Timber-a-guide-to-CITES-listed-tree-species.pdf","author":[{"family":"Groves","given":"Madeleine"},{"family":"Rutherford","given":"Catherine"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, there are some initiatives to ensure sustainability of these species by both community and corporations. For example, some species have been in cultivation through plantation establishment and agroforestry practices; In Bali woodcarving has been put on a sound basis through shifts to a fast-growing species like Paraserianthus falcataria. In India there is the roadside, village-level and plantation production of Dalbergia sissoo. In coastal Kenya there are plantations of the neem trees. There is recommendation and adoption of community/corporate tree plantations for sandalwood ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cMBZ8Hyi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. N. A. Kumar, Joshi, & Ram, 2012)","plainCitation":"(A. N. A. Kumar, Joshi, & Ram, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6397,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XCMU6KFB"],"itemData":{"id":6397,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Sandalwood (Santalum album L.) is a valuable tree associated with Indian culture. It is the second most expensive wood in the world. The heartwood of the tree is treasured for its aroma and is one of the finest natural materials for carving. Sandalwood oil is used in perfumes, cosmetics, aromatherapy and pharmaceuticals. The monopoly of sandalwood trade by the Governments of Karnataka, Tamil Nadu and Kerala and its consequences have resulted in severe exploitation, pushing S. album into the vulnerable category of the IUCN Red List. Extensive research has shown that sandalwood exhibits considerable genetic diversity for different traits. However, information pertaining to heartwood and oil content is meagre mainly because of non-availability of sandalwood plantations. Carrying out further research on these two important traits is difficult as natural populations have dwindled rapidly. We strongly urge that it is essential to encourage the establishment of community/corporate sandalwood plantations in different parts of India with appropriate incentives and adequate protective measures. These plantations can form the base population sources to regain the leadership of India in the sandalwood industry for perfumery and the precious art of carving.","container-title":"CURRENT SCIENCE","issue":"12","language":"en","page":"10","source":"Zotero","title":"Sandalwood: history, uses, present status and the future","volume":"103","author":[{"family":"Kumar","given":"A N Arun"},{"family":"Joshi","given":"Geeta"},{"family":"Ram","given":"H Y Mohan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} in different parts of India with appropriate incentives and adequate protective measures. Australia has been raising large sandalwood plantations, and may be able to meet the global demands, with the world’s largest plantation of S. album established in the Kimberly, Western Australia.  

3.3.4.4.2. Energy

Energy security is one of the requirements for a good quality of life, and this includes availability and access to clean, reliable, affordable and sustainable energy without compromising health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a18ejulc7hq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UN, 2015)","plainCitation":"(UN, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1117,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RWKME4MZ"],"itemData":{"id":1117,"type":"webpage","container-title":"Sustainable Development Goals","genre":"International","title":"The Sustainable Development Agenda. Accessed from:  https://www.un.org/sustainabledevelopment/development-agenda/,","URL":"The Sustainable Development Agenda. Accessed from:  https://www.un.org/sustainabledevelopment/development-agenda/","author":[{"family":"UN","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Yet globally, 1.1 billion (14%) people do not have access to electricity and 2.8 billion (38%) people rely on unclean ‘traditional biomass’ for energy (including charcoal, coal, crop waste, dung, kerosene and wood), with the associated health implications from household air pollution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a26qmtslc7n","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IEA, 2017)","plainCitation":"(IEA, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1123,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/356RTL52"],"itemData":{"id":1123,"type":"report","event-place":"Paris","genre":"World Energy Outlook Special Report","publisher":"International Energy Agency","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Energy Access Outlook 2017: From poverty to prosperity","URL":"https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-access-outlook-2017","author":[{"family":"IEA","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Figure 3.57a). Wood energy contributes 75-90% of sub-Saharan Africa’s household energy mix ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"air947mbok","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoffmann, Br\\\\uc0\\\\u252{}ntrup, & Dewes, 2016; World Bank, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Hoffmann, Brüntrup, & Dewes, 2016; World Bank, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31372,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4C56HQXI"],"itemData":{"id":31372,"type":"book","publisher":"World Bank","title":"Wood-based biomass energy development for sub-Saharan Africa: issues and approaches","author":[{"family":"World Bank","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":31371,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GVFUNQPV"],"itemData":{"id":31371,"type":"report","publisher":"Briefing Paper","title":"Wood energy in sub-Saharan Africa: how to make a shadow business sustainable","author":[{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Harry"},{"family":"Brüntrup","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Dewes","given":"Clara"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An estimated 880 million people globally log firewood or produce charcoal ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0NU8CnJc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO & UNEP, 2020)","plainCitation":"(FAO & UNEP, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30347,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GVLDA89Q"],"itemData":{"id":30347,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132419-6","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca8642en","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The State of the World’s Forests 2020: Forests, Biodiversity and People","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8642en","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"UNEP","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Reliance on wood biomass for cooking is highest in developing Asian countries and sub-Saharan Africa (ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Rh82Kjt9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IEA, 2017)","plainCitation":"(IEA, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1123,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/356RTL52"],"itemData":{"id":1123,"type":"report","event-place":"Paris","genre":"World Energy Outlook Special Report","publisher":"International Energy Agency","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Energy Access Outlook 2017: From poverty to prosperity","URL":"https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-access-outlook-2017","author":[{"family":"IEA","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. One third of the world’s population (2.4 billion people) use wood fuel for cooking - which provides more nutrients than raw food - and other food preservation processes (e.g., smoking, drying), and one in ten people use wood fuel for boiling and sterilizing water ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0zQu1hT5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO & UNEP, 2020)","plainCitation":"(FAO & UNEP, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30347,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GVLDA89Q"],"itemData":{"id":30347,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132419-6","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca8642en","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The State of the World’s Forests 2020: Forests, Biodiversity and People","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8642en","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"UNEP","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Most wild biomass energy is derived from wood, with implications for social and natural systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zqJcC2Jm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arnold, 2006; Bailis, 2005; Holdren, 2000; Miah, 2009; Munalula & Meincken, 2009; K. R. Smith & others, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Arnold, 2006; Bailis, 2005; Holdren, 2000; Miah, 2009; Munalula & Meincken, 2009; K. R. Smith & others, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1122,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8N3A8MKI"],"itemData":{"id":1122,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Summary\\nIn the 1970s, it appeared that fuelwood use was growing rapidly, and this could have major adverse impacts on the resource and poor users. By the mid-1980s, revised assessments indicated that there was less of a problem than had been foreseen, and much less of a need for forestry interventions to maintain supplies. As a result many of the latter were sharply scaled back. Additional information and analysis that has become available since the 1980s support the view that there is no need for large scale interventions devoted just to provision of fuelwood. However, declining access to supplies or markets can raise significant problems in some areas, and for particular categories of user. Growing urban demand for charcoal can also cause local problems, in particular in Africa. The potentials for appropriate forms of intervention are discussed.","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2005.08.008","ISSN":"0305-750X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","page":"596-611","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Woodfuels, livelihoods, and policy interventions: Changing Perspectives","title-short":"Woodfuels, livelihoods, and policy interventions","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305750X05002263","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"J. E. Michael"},{"family":"Köhlin","given":"Gunnar"},{"family":"Persson","given":"Reidar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",3,1]]}}},{"id":1118,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QZ3L5K8J"],"itemData":{"id":1118,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"We analyzed the mortality impacts and greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions produced by household energy use in Africa. Under a business-as-usual (BAU) scenario, household indoor air pollution will cause an estimated 9.8 million premature deaths by the year 2030. Gradual and rapid transitions to charcoal would delay 1.0 million and 2.8 million deaths, respectively; similar transitions to petroleum fuels would delay 1.3 million and 3.7 million deaths. Cumulative BAU GHG emissions will be 6.7 billion tons of carbon by 2050, which is 5.6% of Africa’s total emissions. Large shifts to the use of fossil fuels would reduce GHG emissions by 1 to 10%. Charcoal-intensive future scenarios using current practices increase emissions by 140 to 190%; the increase can be reduced to 5 to 36% using currently available technologies for sustainable production or potentially reduced even more with investment in technological innovation.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1106881","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"5718","page":"98–103","title":"Mortality and Greenhouse Gas Impacts of Biomass and Petroleum Energy Futures in Africa","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/308/5718/98","volume":"308","author":[{"family":"Bailis","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Ezzati","given":"Majid"},{"family":"Kammen","given":"Daniel M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":34825,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MJXSXUYI"],"itemData":{"id":34825,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"World Energy Assessment","container-title":"Energy and the challenge of sustainability","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"92-1-126126-0","publisher":"United Nations Development Programme","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Energy, the Environment, and Health","URL":"http://large.stanford.edu/courses/2017/ph240/fleming2/docs/wea-2000.pdf#page=74","author":[{"family":"Holdren","given":"John P"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Kirk R"},{"family":"Kjellstrom","given":"Tord"},{"family":"Streets","given":"David"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Xiaodong"},{"family":"Fischer","given":"S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":1119,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P2YBJBSU"],"itemData":{"id":1119,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biomass and Bioenergy","issue":"1","page":"70–78","title":"Wood fuel use in the traditional cooking stoves in the rural floodplain areas of Bangladesh: a socio-environmental perspective","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0961953408001025","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Miah","given":"Md Danesh"},{"family":"Al Rashid","given":"Harun"},{"family":"Shin","given":"Man Yong"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":1121,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KFKSG6LP"],"itemData":{"id":1121,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biomass and bioenergy","issue":"3","page":"415–420","title":"An evaluation of South African fuelwood with regards to calorific value and environmental impact","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0961953408002109","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Munalula","given":"Francis"},{"family":"Meincken","given":"Martina"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":1120,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KEYZQ5H4"],"itemData":{"id":1120,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"UNASYLVA-FAO-","issue":"2","page":"41","title":"Health impacts of household fuelwood use in developing countries","URL":"https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/92e3/c13f1f5242d27c11099375009d9850beb30d.pdf","volume":"57","author":[{"family":"Smith","given":"Kirk R"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Logging for energy accounts for 50% of all wood consumed globally, and accounts for 90% of logged timber in Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OUEIsmhn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO & UNEP, 2020)","plainCitation":"(FAO & UNEP, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30347,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GVLDA89Q"],"itemData":{"id":30347,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132419-6","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca8642en","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The State of the World’s Forests 2020: Forests, Biodiversity and People","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8642en","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"UNEP","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. According to the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations’ statistics (http://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/FO), global wood fuel removals have increased over time with approximately 2 billion m3 produced in 2019. There is great variation in wood fuel production and use in the different regions. Production is highest in Asia and Africa at approximately 713 million m3 and 706 million m3 respectively (Figure 3.57a). Whereas production levels are increasing in Africa (from 445 million m3 in 1990), the opposite is happening in Asia whose production has decreased from 897 million m3 in 1990. Latin America and the Caribbean have a fairly high level of production (268 million m3). Oceania has the lowest production of approximately 10 million m3 in 2019. Although absolute wood fuel consumption is increasing, especially in sub-Saharan Africa, per capita consumption is decreasing across all regions (Figure 3.57b). All regions reported minimal trade in wood fuels, implying that wood fuels are mainly consumed locally and in domestic markets. However, wood-based energy industry has the potential to grow in a number of countries. This has motivated the investment in biomass-based energy generation, and research and development of new energy products such as biodiesel ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5Nr6YyNq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Asikainen, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Asikainen, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31271,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HBAHLYBW"],"itemData":{"id":31271,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"IUFRO World Series Vol. 25","container-title":"Forests and Society-Responding to Global Drivers of Change","event-place":"Vienna","page":"183-200","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","publisher-place":"Vienna","title":"Forests and bioenergy production (Vol. 25, pp. 183-200). IUFRO","author":[{"family":"Asikainen","given":"Antti"},{"family":"Anttila","given":"Perttu"},{"family":"Heinimö","given":"Jussi"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Tattersall"},{"family":"Stupak","given":"Inge"},{"family":"Quirino","given":"Waldir Ferreira"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although alternative energy sources reduce demand for wood fuel, in some areas wood fuel use persists due to habits, taste and custom ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LSJXjTU0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, Schure, Ingram, & Yoo, 2017)","plainCitation":"(FAO, Schure, Ingram, & Yoo, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31270,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GNU9K89D"],"itemData":{"id":31270,"type":"report","abstract":"\\"With food insecurity, climate change and deforestation and forest degradation remaining key global issues, this paper highlights the role of sustainable woodfuel in improving food security. Food insecurity and a high dependence on woodfuel as a primary cooking fuel are characteristics common to vulnerable groups of people in developing regions of the world.With adequate policy and legal frameworks in place, woodfuel production and harvesting can be sustainable and a main source of green energy. Moreover, the widespread availability of woodfuel, and the enormous market for it, presents opportunities for employment and for sustainable value chains, providing further rationale for promoting this source of energy. This paper explains how sustainable woodfuel is closely linked to food security and provides insights in how the linkages could be strengthened at all stages of woodfuel production, trade and use.\\"--Publisher's description","event-place":"Rome","language":"en","note":"OCLC: on1013590908","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Sustainable woodfuel for food security: a smart choice: green, renewable and affordable","title-short":"Sustainable woodfuel for food security","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"Schure","given":"Jolien"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Yoo","given":"Byoung Il"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 


Figure 3.57 Total a) and per capita b) wood fuel consumption trends by region. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"alahropfgh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2017c)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2017c)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31252,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GR9F483P"],"itemData":{"id":31252,"type":"report","event-place":"Rome","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","title":"Incentivising sustainable wood energy in sub-Saharan Africa - a way forward for policy makers","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/i6815e/i6815e.pdf","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} © FAO, 2017.

	In several industrialized countries, wood energy provides nearly 25% of total energy supply, and the leading renewable energy source in Europe accounting for about 45% of primary energy from renewable sources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iWpUXy18","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Francisco X. Aguilar, FAO, & UNECE, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Francisco X. Aguilar, FAO, & UNECE, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31301,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YHWX3Y6E"],"itemData":{"id":31301,"type":"book","abstract":"Wood Energy in the ECE Region highlights the use of wood for energy and includes the most recent statistics on wood energy markets across the UNECE region. It aims to communicate the relevance of wood energy in the region and help bridge information about the forest and energy sectors. It also intends to offer some of the best-available information on the role that wood energy can play in various sectors to support environmental, energy, and socio-economic strategies toward a greener economy. It focuses on commercial wood energy uses in the UNECE inclusive of energy generated for cooking, heating and electricity generation.--Page ii","call-number":"TP324 .W66 2018","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-92-1-117154-9","note":"OCLC: on1055657712","number-of-pages":"93","publisher":"United Nations","publisher-place":"New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wood energy in the ECE Region: Data, trends and outlook in Europe, the Commonwealth of Independent States and North America","title-short":"Wood energy in the ECE Region","URL":"https://unece.org/DAM/timber/publications/SP-42-Interactive.pdf","editor":[{"family":"Aguilar","given":"Francisco X."},{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"UNECE","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. With the requirement of European Union states to have 27% of their energy generated from renewable energy by 2030 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8A6cycIR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(European Commission, 2014)","plainCitation":"(European Commission, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31305,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9TTQPM35"],"itemData":{"id":31305,"type":"report","event-place":"Brussels, Belgium.","publisher":"European Commission","publisher-place":"Brussels, Belgium.","title":"A policy framework for climate and energy in the period from 2020 to 2030.","URL":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/ALL/?uri=CELEX%3A52014DC0015","author":[{"family":"European Commission","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Europe’s wood consumption for energy generation is expected to grow and reach 752 million m3 in 2030 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"O8uFENZn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mantau, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Mantau, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31304,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HDG5QC46"],"itemData":{"id":31304,"type":"report","event-place":"Hamburg/Germany, June 2010","genre":"Methodology report","publisher-place":"Hamburg/Germany, June 2010","title":"Real potential for changes in growth and use of EU forests. Hamburg: EUwood,","URL":"http://www.unece.org/fileadmin/DAM/timber/meetings/20110321/euwood_final_report.pdf","author":[{"family":"Mantau","given":"Udo"},{"family":"Saal","given":"U"},{"family":"Prins","given":"K"},{"family":"Steierer","given":"F"},{"family":"Lindner","given":"M"},{"family":"Verkerk","given":"H"},{"family":"Anttila","given":"P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Logging for energy in North and Central America has been growing to meet increasing export demand for wood pellets.
	In Europe and North America, wood energy utilization is commonly integrated in forest management practices and the wood products industry. Wood energy feedstocks can be considered a co-product of forest management as part of silvicultural treatments inclusive of thinning, final integrated harvests and salvage logging, as well as a by-product of the forest industry during the production of sawn goods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JnI3MbQy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Asikainen, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Asikainen, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31271,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HBAHLYBW"],"itemData":{"id":31271,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"IUFRO World Series Vol. 25","container-title":"Forests and Society-Responding to Global Drivers of Change","event-place":"Vienna","page":"183-200","publisher":"International Union of Forest Research Organizations (IUFRO)","publisher-place":"Vienna","title":"Forests and bioenergy production (Vol. 25, pp. 183-200). IUFRO","author":[{"family":"Asikainen","given":"Antti"},{"family":"Anttila","given":"Perttu"},{"family":"Heinimö","given":"Jussi"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Tattersall"},{"family":"Stupak","given":"Inge"},{"family":"Quirino","given":"Waldir Ferreira"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Most of the wood used for energy comes indirectly through the forest industry as a co-product (58%) and a little over of a third of the wood mobilized for energy comes directly from forests (36%). Data from the Joint Wood Energy Enquiry for 2013 shows that the forest-based industry was the largest consumer of wood energy (44%), followed by the residential (36%) and combined heat and power (17%) sectors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2pr6i4htir","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. X. Aguilar, Glavonji\\\\uc0\\\\u263{}, Hartkamp, Mabee, & Skog, 2015)","plainCitation":"(F. X. Aguilar, Glavonji?, Hartkamp, Mabee, & Skog, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31238,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2VWJ9MNC"],"itemData":{"id":31238,"type":"chapter","container-title":"United Nations Forest Products Annual Market Review","page":"91-104","title":"Chapter 9: Wood Energy Market, 2014-2015. In: United Nations Forest Products Annual Market Review","URL":"http:// www.unece.org/fileadmin/DAM/timber/publications/2015- FPAMR-E.pdf","author":[{"family":"Aguilar","given":"F. X."},{"family":"Glavonji?","given":"B."},{"family":"Hartkamp","given":"R"},{"family":"Mabee","given":"W"},{"family":"Skog","given":"K"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Use of wood for energy creates job opportunities not only along the supply-chain of woody biomass feedstocks, but also through investments in technology development and energy conversion and final consumption ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"t6qRmCcg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Francisco X. Aguilar, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Francisco X. Aguilar, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31301,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YHWX3Y6E"],"itemData":{"id":31301,"type":"book","abstract":"Wood Energy in the ECE Region highlights the use of wood for energy and includes the most recent statistics on wood energy markets across the UNECE region. It aims to communicate the relevance of wood energy in the region and help bridge information about the forest and energy sectors. It also intends to offer some of the best-available information on the role that wood energy can play in various sectors to support environmental, energy, and socio-economic strategies toward a greener economy. It focuses on commercial wood energy uses in the UNECE inclusive of energy generated for cooking, heating and electricity generation.--Page ii","call-number":"TP324 .W66 2018","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-92-1-117154-9","note":"OCLC: on1055657712","number-of-pages":"93","publisher":"United Nations","publisher-place":"New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wood energy in the ECE Region: Data, trends and outlook in Europe, the Commonwealth of Independent States and North America","title-short":"Wood energy in the ECE Region","URL":"https://unece.org/DAM/timber/publications/SP-42-Interactive.pdf","editor":[{"family":"Aguilar","given":"Francisco X."},{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"UNECE","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The FAO and the United Nations Environment Program ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nj3y1Uhn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2020)","plainCitation":"(2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30347,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GVLDA89Q"],"itemData":{"id":30347,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132419-6","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca8642en","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The State of the World’s Forests 2020: Forests, Biodiversity and People","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8642en","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"UNEP","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} estimated that over 40 million people are involved in commercial wood fuel activities to supply urban centers. Furthermore, wood fuel production generated an estimated 33 billion United States dollars in 2011 global revenue ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OK3eMhhq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO & UNEP, 2020)","plainCitation":"(FAO & UNEP, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30347,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GVLDA89Q"],"itemData":{"id":30347,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132419-6","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca8642en","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The State of the World’s Forests 2020: Forests, Biodiversity and People","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8642en","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"UNEP","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The number of jobs and net earnings is influenced by production method and organization of energy systems. For instance, the utilization of 390,000 dry tons of woody biomass estimated to feed a 100 megawatt power facility in the southern United States of America has been estimated to support 585 direct and 481 indirect jobs through the recovery of logging co-products, while direct and indirect employment associated with operation of the power plant were 281 and 115, respectively ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8JDa9vz7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Perez-Verdin, Grebner, Munn, Sun, & Grado, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Perez-Verdin, Grebner, Munn, Sun, & Grado, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31254,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6K5WBMWE"],"itemData":{"id":31254,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This study examined the economic impacts of woody biomass utilization for bioenergy conversion in Mississippi. Analysis of economic impacts was organized around three groups of events: (1) recovery of logging and thinning residues, (2) electricity generation from cofiring systems, and (3) construction and operation of biofuel facilities. Input–output analysis was used to simulate economic impacts in terms of gross output, value-added, and employment. Cost information and woody biomass inventories were obtained from the literature, a statewide forest inventory, and Impact Analysis for Planning (IMPLAN) database. Results showed that the single activity of recovery of all available logging and thinning residues would create a considerable number of jobs and stimulate the rural economy with more resources coming to local industries and households. Due to construction and operation costs, economic impacts of biofuels were higher than biopower. However, biofuels reported the lowest employment and value-added multipliers of all three groups. This may be due, in part, to equipment and materials manufactured outside of the state. It is expected that as technology, equipment, and human capital were gradually attracted to the area, the multiplicative effect and expenditure retention should increase. These results can help decision-makers evaluate Mississippi’s potential for future bioenergy development.","container-title":"FOREST PRODUCTS JOURNAL","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"10","source":"Zotero","title":"Economic impacts of woody biomass utilization for bioenergy in Mississippi","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Perez-Verdin","given":"Gustavo"},{"family":"Grebner","given":"Donald L"},{"family":"Munn","given":"Ian A"},{"family":"Sun","given":"Changyou"},{"family":"Grado","given":"Stephen C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
	Global wood fuel demand peaked in the mid-1990s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BRaAVUXc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J.E. Michael Arnold, K\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}hlin, Persson, & Shepherd, 2003)","plainCitation":"(J.E. Michael Arnold, Köhlin, Persson, & Shepherd, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"ERpTB65M/j13oGxnZ","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BGRQ39PR"],"itemData":{"id":4644,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"\\"The impact of woodfuel collection on forests has been controversial and its role in rural livelihoods and deforestation is the subject of considerable debate. This study reviews the main dimensions of this discourse and the resulting responses form the forestry sector. It assesses new information that has come to light over the past decade, looking at national and global trends on woodfuel production and use and the evolution of patterns of urban and rural demand and supply. It examines livelihood and environmental dimensions of relevance to forestry and outlines some of the main issues that warrant additional attention. It is thus intended more as a foundation for further discussion, rather than being a set of prescriptions for action by forestry, though where these are evident they are identified.\\n\\n\\"The available evidence does not substantiate earlier concerns that woodfuel demand has been outpacing sustainable supply on a scale that makes it a major cause of deforestation. It appears the balance between the two seldom an issue requiring forestry intervention on a national scale. However, the rapid rise in charcoal production and its concentration, to supply large urban markets, certainly warrants further investigation. Overall, the woodfuels situation is an important consideration for particular areas within a country and for particular groups of users and suppliers. Globally, fuelwood consumption appears to have peaked (although charcoal consumption is continuing to rise) and in some developing countries, it now appears to be in decline. However, the total quantities of woodfuels being used, and the number of people using them, are still huge. In poor households almost everywhere, woodfuels are among the main forest related inputs, although the level of attention they receive does not currently reflect this, despite the growing focus on giving forestry a stronger livelihood orientation. Forestry initiatives need to be compatible with the energy sector’s objective of helping poor users move up the energy ladder to greater fuel efficiency and alternative fuels. The main task though, is likely to be facilitating access to supplies for those who continue to depend on biomass fuels, for their own use or as an important source of income. Forestry measures will need to integrate meeting this demand into wider forestry objectives, rather than, as in the past, developing responses focusing on the fuelwood issue alone.\\"","issue":"CIFOR Occasional Paper, no. 39","title":"Fuelwood Revisited: What Has Changed in the Last Decade?","URL":"http://hdl.handle.net/10535/4692","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"J.E. Michael"},{"family":"Köhlin","given":"Gunnar"},{"family":"Persson","given":"Reidar"},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"Gill"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, instigating a declaration of a ‘fuelwood crisis’. However, the projected wood fuel supply-demand models predicting wood fuel stock collapse were an overestimation due to limited data and an incomplete understanding of social, economic and ecological interactions around wood energy ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ECWUUXpV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dewees, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dewees, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31311,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JCVVVCNG"],"itemData":{"id":31311,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Forestry Review","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Commonwealth Forestry Association","page":"55–64","title":"Whose problem is it anyway? Narratives and counter-narratives and their impact on woodfuel policy formulation","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Dewees","given":"PA"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, the available amounts of wood fuel may not be sufficient to meet local energy needs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UcMnSymA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fabian, Volkmer, & Wiedemann, 2011; Swinkels, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Fabian, Volkmer, & Wiedemann, 2011; Swinkels, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31256,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JI3KL6UZ"],"itemData":{"id":31256,"type":"report","archive":"2 April 2021","genre":"Support for microfinance services in rural regions\\" and GTZ/DED/CIM project \\"Sustainable Management of Natural Resources in Gorno-Badakhshan","publisher":"GIZ","title":"Microloans for Thermal Insulation: A Product Documentation Based on Experience in Tajik Gorno-Badakhshan.","URL":"https://archnet.org/collections/378/publications/6830","author":[{"family":"Fabian","given":"A"},{"family":"Volkmer","given":"H"},{"family":"Wiedemann","given":"C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31300,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZMPCI4IY"],"itemData":{"id":31300,"type":"report","event-place":"Washington D.C","language":"English","publisher":"World Bank","publisher-place":"Washington D.C","title":"Assessment of household energy deprivation in Tajikistan : policy options for socially responsible reform in the energy sector","URL":"http://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/944321468341064427/pdf/888370ESW0whit0n0Energy0Deprivation.pdf","author":[{"family":"Swinkels","given":"Rob"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",6,19]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Household energy consumption is usually higher than wood fuel reported in official statistics, which mainly refer to wood from forests sources while leaving out other forms of wood biomass that contribute to household energy production. These include (for example in Europe) all by-products (sawmill by-products, other industrial wood residues and black liquor), solid wood fuels and post-consumer wood ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BxZ1UWZc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mantau, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Mantau, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31304,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HDG5QC46"],"itemData":{"id":31304,"type":"report","event-place":"Hamburg/Germany, June 2010","genre":"Methodology report","publisher-place":"Hamburg/Germany, June 2010","title":"Real potential for changes in growth and use of EU forests. Hamburg: EUwood,","URL":"http://www.unece.org/fileadmin/DAM/timber/meetings/20110321/euwood_final_report.pdf","author":[{"family":"Mantau","given":"Udo"},{"family":"Saal","given":"U"},{"family":"Prins","given":"K"},{"family":"Steierer","given":"F"},{"family":"Lindner","given":"M"},{"family":"Verkerk","given":"H"},{"family":"Anttila","given":"P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Although wood fuel demand can be met at a global, national or even regional scale, when comparing supply-demand balances, localized wood fuel scarcity persists ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TtJXwyeu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J.E.M. Arnold, K\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}hlin, Persson, & Shepherd, 2003; FAO, 2017; Masera, Bailis, Drigo, Ghilardi, & Ruiz-Mercado, 2015)","plainCitation":"(J.E.M. Arnold, Köhlin, Persson, & Shepherd, 2003; FAO, 2017; Masera, Bailis, Drigo, Ghilardi, & Ruiz-Mercado, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26738,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UCKMNQT3"],"itemData":{"id":26738,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"type: (CIFOR Occasional Paper, 39).","title":"Fuelwood Revisited: What Has Changed in the Last Decade?","URL":"http://hdl.handle.net/10535/4692","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"J.E.M."},{"family":"Köhlin","given":"G."},{"family":"Persson","given":"R."},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":31270,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GNU9K89D"],"itemData":{"id":31270,"type":"report","abstract":"\\"With food insecurity, climate change and deforestation and forest degradation remaining key global issues, this paper highlights the role of sustainable woodfuel in improving food security. Food insecurity and a high dependence on woodfuel as a primary cooking fuel are characteristics common to vulnerable groups of people in developing regions of the world.With adequate policy and legal frameworks in place, woodfuel production and harvesting can be sustainable and a main source of green energy. Moreover, the widespread availability of woodfuel, and the enormous market for it, presents opportunities for employment and for sustainable value chains, providing further rationale for promoting this source of energy. This paper explains how sustainable woodfuel is closely linked to food security and provides insights in how the linkages could be strengthened at all stages of woodfuel production, trade and use.\\"--Publisher's description","event-place":"Rome","language":"en","note":"OCLC: on1013590908","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Sustainable woodfuel for food security: a smart choice: green, renewable and affordable","title-short":"Sustainable woodfuel for food security","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"Schure","given":"Jolien"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Yoo","given":"Byoung Il"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":31394,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z88NFHWT"],"itemData":{"id":31394,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-102014-021318","page":"121–150","title":"Environmental burden of traditional bioenergy use","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Masera","given":"Omar R"},{"family":"Bailis","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Rudi"},{"family":"Ghilardi","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Ruiz-Mercado","given":"Ilse"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wood fuel-scarcity ‘hotspots’ occur in areas where wood fuel is crucial for subsistence use and household-level well-being (Figure 3.58a) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XxdjqY0j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. E. Michael Arnold, 2006; Sampson, 2005)","plainCitation":"(J. E. Michael Arnold, 2006; Sampson, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1122,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8N3A8MKI"],"itemData":{"id":1122,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Summary\\nIn the 1970s, it appeared that fuelwood use was growing rapidly, and this could have major adverse impacts on the resource and poor users. By the mid-1980s, revised assessments indicated that there was less of a problem than had been foreseen, and much less of a need for forestry interventions to maintain supplies. As a result many of the latter were sharply scaled back. Additional information and analysis that has become available since the 1980s support the view that there is no need for large scale interventions devoted just to provision of fuelwood. However, declining access to supplies or markets can raise significant problems in some areas, and for particular categories of user. Growing urban demand for charcoal can also cause local problems, in particular in Africa. The potentials for appropriate forms of intervention are discussed.","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2005.08.008","ISSN":"0305-750X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","page":"596-611","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Woodfuels, livelihoods, and policy interventions: Changing Perspectives","title-short":"Woodfuels, livelihoods, and policy interventions","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305750X05002263","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"J. E. Michael"},{"family":"Köhlin","given":"Gunnar"},{"family":"Persson","given":"Reidar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",3,1]]}}},{"id":1124,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L9PVR3KX"],"itemData":{"id":1124,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Current State and Trends","container-title":"Millienium Ecosystem Assessment","event-place":"Washington DC","ISBN":"978-1-55963-227-0","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington DC","title":"Timber, Fuel, and Fiber","URL":"https://www.millenniumassessment.org/documents/document.278.aspx.pdf","author":[{"family":"Sampson","given":"R. Neil"},{"family":"Bystriakova","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Sanda"},{"family":"Gonzalez","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Irland","given":"Lloyd L."},{"family":"Kauppi","given":"Pekka"},{"family":"Sedjo","given":"Roger"},{"family":"Thompson","given":"Ian D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In these areas, wood fuel users have few to no alternatives for cooking and heating, posing localized wood fuel driven challenges as most wood fuel (particularly firewood) is produced, harvested and consumed at a local level ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"D9vEc7kW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sampson, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Sampson, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1124,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L9PVR3KX"],"itemData":{"id":1124,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Current State and Trends","container-title":"Millienium Ecosystem Assessment","event-place":"Washington DC","ISBN":"978-1-55963-227-0","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington DC","title":"Timber, Fuel, and Fiber","URL":"https://www.millenniumassessment.org/documents/document.278.aspx.pdf","author":[{"family":"Sampson","given":"R. Neil"},{"family":"Bystriakova","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Sanda"},{"family":"Gonzalez","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Irland","given":"Lloyd L."},{"family":"Kauppi","given":"Pekka"},{"family":"Sedjo","given":"Roger"},{"family":"Thompson","given":"Ian D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition, regions where logging rates exceed growth rates are likely to cause degradation or deforestation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IMj74LHm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Robert Bailis, Drigo, Ghilardi, & Masera, 2015; Masera, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Robert Bailis, Drigo, Ghilardi, & Masera, 2015; Masera, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31396,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LF4D53G7"],"itemData":{"id":31396,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Climate Change","DOI":"10.1038/nclimate2491","ISSN":"1758-678X, 1758-6798","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Nature Clim Change","language":"en","page":"266-272","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The carbon footprint of traditional woodfuels","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nclimate2491","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Bailis","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Rudi"},{"family":"Ghilardi","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Masera","given":"Omar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",3]]}}},{"id":31394,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z88NFHWT"],"itemData":{"id":31394,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-102014-021318","page":"121–150","title":"Environmental burden of traditional bioenergy use","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Masera","given":"Omar R"},{"family":"Bailis","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Rudi"},{"family":"Ghilardi","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Ruiz-Mercado","given":"Ilse"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In 2009, 27-34% of wood fuel logging exceeded growth rates, predominantly in hotspots in South Asia and East Africa, affecting over 250 million rural people reliant on wood energy (Figure 3.58b) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZwQfOcIv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Masera, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Masera, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31394,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z88NFHWT"],"itemData":{"id":31394,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-102014-021318","page":"121–150","title":"Environmental burden of traditional bioenergy use","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Masera","given":"Omar R"},{"family":"Bailis","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Rudi"},{"family":"Ghilardi","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Ruiz-Mercado","given":"Ilse"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The FAO estimate that one third of wood fuel logging was unsustainable and a major cause of forest degradation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sV7xHf6O","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2017; FAO & UNEP, 2020)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2017; FAO & UNEP, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30347,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GVLDA89Q"],"itemData":{"id":30347,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132419-6","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca8642en","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The State of the World’s Forests 2020: Forests, Biodiversity and People","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8642en","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"UNEP","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":31270,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GNU9K89D"],"itemData":{"id":31270,"type":"report","abstract":"\\"With food insecurity, climate change and deforestation and forest degradation remaining key global issues, this paper highlights the role of sustainable woodfuel in improving food security. Food insecurity and a high dependence on woodfuel as a primary cooking fuel are characteristics common to vulnerable groups of people in developing regions of the world.With adequate policy and legal frameworks in place, woodfuel production and harvesting can be sustainable and a main source of green energy. Moreover, the widespread availability of woodfuel, and the enormous market for it, presents opportunities for employment and for sustainable value chains, providing further rationale for promoting this source of energy. This paper explains how sustainable woodfuel is closely linked to food security and provides insights in how the linkages could be strengthened at all stages of woodfuel production, trade and use.\\"--Publisher's description","event-place":"Rome","language":"en","note":"OCLC: on1013590908","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Sustainable woodfuel for food security: a smart choice: green, renewable and affordable","title-short":"Sustainable woodfuel for food security","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"Schure","given":"Jolien"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Yoo","given":"Byoung Il"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the link between wood fuel logging and deforestation or forest degradation is challenging to quantify and varies temporally and geographically ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sSXF9NRx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rob Bailis, Wang, Drigo, Ghilardi, & Masera, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Rob Bailis, Wang, Drigo, Ghilardi, & Masera, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31395,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NXJJKKSD"],"itemData":{"id":31395,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Research Letters","DOI":"10.1088/1748-9326/aa83ed","ISSN":"1748-9326","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Environ. Res. Lett.","page":"115002","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Getting the numbers right: revisiting woodfuel sustainability in the developing world","title-short":"Getting the numbers right","URL":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/aa83ed","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Bailis","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Yiting"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Rudi"},{"family":"Ghilardi","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Masera","given":"Omar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Demand depends inter alia on household level preferences and economic context, vegetation species composition and physiognomy, the availability and cost of alternative energy sources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IrDxpIwn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rob Bailis, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Rob Bailis, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31395,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NXJJKKSD"],"itemData":{"id":31395,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Research Letters","DOI":"10.1088/1748-9326/aa83ed","ISSN":"1748-9326","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Environ. Res. Lett.","page":"115002","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Getting the numbers right: revisiting woodfuel sustainability in the developing world","title-short":"Getting the numbers right","URL":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/aa83ed","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Bailis","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Yiting"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Rudi"},{"family":"Ghilardi","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Masera","given":"Omar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Supply may vary with land use, productivity (and associated edaphic and climatic determinants), and accessibility of wood ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BhuxEbwq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rob Bailis, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Rob Bailis, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31395,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NXJJKKSD"],"itemData":{"id":31395,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Research Letters","DOI":"10.1088/1748-9326/aa83ed","ISSN":"1748-9326","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Environ. Res. Lett.","page":"115002","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Getting the numbers right: revisiting woodfuel sustainability in the developing world","title-short":"Getting the numbers right","URL":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/aa83ed","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Bailis","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Yiting"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Rudi"},{"family":"Ghilardi","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Masera","given":"Omar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. To further complicate quantification of wood fuel extraction, logging locations are not always from forests but are derived from many types of land cover (e.g., farms, roadside commons, home gardens), and may be a primary (logging specifically for wood fuel) or secondary activity (e.g., wood cleared from farms) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"h5Of2OeL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rob Bailis, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Rob Bailis, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31395,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NXJJKKSD"],"itemData":{"id":31395,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Research Letters","DOI":"10.1088/1748-9326/aa83ed","ISSN":"1748-9326","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Environ. Res. Lett.","page":"115002","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Getting the numbers right: revisiting woodfuel sustainability in the developing world","title-short":"Getting the numbers right","URL":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/aa83ed","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Bailis","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Yiting"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Rudi"},{"family":"Ghilardi","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Masera","given":"Omar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thus, wood fuel logging is often not the sole cause of forest degradation, but unsustainable wood fuel logging in Africa, particularly charcoal logging in open access systems with uncertain or unclear forest tenure, can be the primary driver of forest degradation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vGoa6JLr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2017)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31270,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GNU9K89D"],"itemData":{"id":31270,"type":"report","abstract":"\\"With food insecurity, climate change and deforestation and forest degradation remaining key global issues, this paper highlights the role of sustainable woodfuel in improving food security. Food insecurity and a high dependence on woodfuel as a primary cooking fuel are characteristics common to vulnerable groups of people in developing regions of the world.With adequate policy and legal frameworks in place, woodfuel production and harvesting can be sustainable and a main source of green energy. Moreover, the widespread availability of woodfuel, and the enormous market for it, presents opportunities for employment and for sustainable value chains, providing further rationale for promoting this source of energy. This paper explains how sustainable woodfuel is closely linked to food security and provides insights in how the linkages could be strengthened at all stages of woodfuel production, trade and use.\\"--Publisher's description","event-place":"Rome","language":"en","note":"OCLC: on1013590908","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Sustainable woodfuel for food security: a smart choice: green, renewable and affordable","title-short":"Sustainable woodfuel for food security","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"Schure","given":"Jolien"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Yoo","given":"Byoung Il"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The FAO found that wood fuel sustainability is strongly related to forest management rights and access, especially through permitting and/or taxation systems developed with local participation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SzmeAD3p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2017)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31270,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GNU9K89D"],"itemData":{"id":31270,"type":"report","abstract":"\\"With food insecurity, climate change and deforestation and forest degradation remaining key global issues, this paper highlights the role of sustainable woodfuel in improving food security. Food insecurity and a high dependence on woodfuel as a primary cooking fuel are characteristics common to vulnerable groups of people in developing regions of the world.With adequate policy and legal frameworks in place, woodfuel production and harvesting can be sustainable and a main source of green energy. Moreover, the widespread availability of woodfuel, and the enormous market for it, presents opportunities for employment and for sustainable value chains, providing further rationale for promoting this source of energy. This paper explains how sustainable woodfuel is closely linked to food security and provides insights in how the linkages could be strengthened at all stages of woodfuel production, trade and use.\\"--Publisher's description","event-place":"Rome","language":"en","note":"OCLC: on1013590908","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Sustainable woodfuel for food security: a smart choice: green, renewable and affordable","title-short":"Sustainable woodfuel for food security","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"Schure","given":"Jolien"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Yoo","given":"Byoung Il"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, beyond these areas of localized shortages, sustainably logged wood fuel has the potential to be a viable, renewable, energy source that provides income ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"x5SFSgr1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2017)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31270,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GNU9K89D"],"itemData":{"id":31270,"type":"report","abstract":"\\"With food insecurity, climate change and deforestation and forest degradation remaining key global issues, this paper highlights the role of sustainable woodfuel in improving food security. Food insecurity and a high dependence on woodfuel as a primary cooking fuel are characteristics common to vulnerable groups of people in developing regions of the world.With adequate policy and legal frameworks in place, woodfuel production and harvesting can be sustainable and a main source of green energy. Moreover, the widespread availability of woodfuel, and the enormous market for it, presents opportunities for employment and for sustainable value chains, providing further rationale for promoting this source of energy. This paper explains how sustainable woodfuel is closely linked to food security and provides insights in how the linkages could be strengthened at all stages of woodfuel production, trade and use.\\"--Publisher's description","event-place":"Rome","language":"en","note":"OCLC: on1013590908","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Sustainable woodfuel for food security: a smart choice: green, renewable and affordable","title-short":"Sustainable woodfuel for food security","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"Schure","given":"Jolien"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Yoo","given":"Byoung Il"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and may be the preferred fuel source for cultural and economic reasons ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eNtM24vl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Munro, van der Horst, & Healy, 2017)","plainCitation":"(P. Munro, van der Horst, & Healy, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31272,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UQGEPD5Z"],"itemData":{"id":31272,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy Policy","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"635–641","title":"Energy justice for all? Rethinking sustainable development goal 7 through struggles over traditional energy practices in Sierra Leone","volume":"105","author":[{"family":"Munro","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Horst","given":"Greg","non-dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Healy","given":"Stephen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, provided air quality (indoor and outdoor) and climate change emissions are mitigated ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rqMynXio","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rob Bailis, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Rob Bailis, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31395,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NXJJKKSD"],"itemData":{"id":31395,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Research Letters","DOI":"10.1088/1748-9326/aa83ed","ISSN":"1748-9326","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Environ. Res. Lett.","page":"115002","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Getting the numbers right: revisiting woodfuel sustainability in the developing world","title-short":"Getting the numbers right","URL":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/aa83ed","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Bailis","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Yiting"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Rudi"},{"family":"Ghilardi","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Masera","given":"Omar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.


Figure 3.58 a) Global population reliant on traditional biomass, including wood fuel and animal waste, and b) wood fuel supply/demand balance with circles on major deficit “hotspots”. See data management report for the figure at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6453135.

	In low-income countries, wood fuel use occurs predominantly at the household scale for lighting, cooking and heating, but can support local and village-level industry. Commercial involvement with wood fuels, both firewood and charcoal, provides supplemental or an occasional income source ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QSluTZvE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. E. Michael Arnold, 2006)","plainCitation":"(J. E. Michael Arnold, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1122,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8N3A8MKI"],"itemData":{"id":1122,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Summary\\nIn the 1970s, it appeared that fuelwood use was growing rapidly, and this could have major adverse impacts on the resource and poor users. By the mid-1980s, revised assessments indicated that there was less of a problem than had been foreseen, and much less of a need for forestry interventions to maintain supplies. As a result many of the latter were sharply scaled back. Additional information and analysis that has become available since the 1980s support the view that there is no need for large scale interventions devoted just to provision of fuelwood. However, declining access to supplies or markets can raise significant problems in some areas, and for particular categories of user. Growing urban demand for charcoal can also cause local problems, in particular in Africa. The potentials for appropriate forms of intervention are discussed.","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2005.08.008","ISSN":"0305-750X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","page":"596-611","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Woodfuels, livelihoods, and policy interventions: Changing Perspectives","title-short":"Woodfuels, livelihoods, and policy interventions","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305750X05002263","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"J. E. Michael"},{"family":"Köhlin","given":"Gunnar"},{"family":"Persson","given":"Reidar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, or an activity to fall back on as a ‘safety net’. For example, firewood trading (and household subsistence use) increased in South Africa during economic shocks, such as loss of urban employment or breadwinner death as a result of HIV/AIDS (Human Immunodeficiency Virus) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0OZaZRy5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Shackleton & Shackleton, 2004)","plainCitation":"(C. Shackleton & Shackleton, 2004)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34362,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AF68QQ3J"],"itemData":{"id":34362,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"South African Journal of Science","issue":"11","page":"658–664","title":"The importance of non-timber forest products in rural livelihood security and as safety nets: a review of evidence from South Africa","volume":"100","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Sheona"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or in response to the covid-19 pandemic, such as the switch from liquefied petroleum gas to wood fuel in Kenya and Malawi ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a3gnedko46","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shupler, 2020; Zalengera, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Shupler, 2020; Zalengera, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1138,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A5JX2XS7"],"itemData":{"id":1138,"type":"report","abstract":"Abstract\\n          A COVID-19 lockdown may impact household fuel use and food security for ?700 million sub-Saharan Africans who rely on polluting fuels (e.g. wood, kerosene) for household energy and typically work in the informal economy. In an informal settlement in Nairobi, surveys administered before (n=474) and after (n=194) a mandatory COVID-19-related community lockdown documented socioeconomic/household energy impacts. During lockdown, 95% of participants indicated income decline or cessation and 88% reported being food insecure. Three quarters of participants cooked less frequently and half altered their diet. One quarter (27%) of households primarily using liquefied petroleum gas (LPG) for cooking before lockdown switched to kerosene (14%) or wood (13%). These results indicate the livelihoods of urban Kenyan families were deleteriously affected by COVID-19 lockdown, with a likely rise in household air pollution from community-level increases in polluting fuel use. To safeguard public health, policies should prioritize enhancing clean fuel and food access among the urban poor.","genre":"preprint","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1101/2020.05.27.20115113","publisher":"Public and Global Health","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"COVID-19 Lockdown in a Kenyan Informal Settlement: Impacts on Household Energy and Food Security","title-short":"COVID-19 Lockdown in a Kenyan Informal Settlement","URL":"http://medrxiv.org/lookup/doi/10.1101/2020.05.27.20115113","author":[{"family":"Shupler","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Mwitari","given":"James"},{"family":"Gohole","given":"Arthur"},{"family":"Cuevas","given":"Rachel Anderson","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Puzzolo","given":"Elisa"},{"family":"?uki?","given":"Iva"},{"family":"Nix","given":"Emily"},{"family":"Pope","given":"Dan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,29]]}}},{"id":1143,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VN8785EB"],"itemData":{"id":1143,"type":"report","genre":"Workshop Report","page":"1-12","publisher":"Energy and Economic Growth: Applied Research Programme","title":"Impacts and Coping Mechanisms for the Covid-19 Pandemic in Malawi’s Energy Sector","URL":"pstorage-loughborough-53465.s3.amazonaws.com/25189643/MzuzuUniWorkshopReport.pdf","author":[{"family":"Zalengera","given":"Collen"},{"family":"Chitedze","given":"Isaac"},{"family":"To","given":"Long Seng"},{"family":"Chitawo","given":"Maxon"},{"family":"Mwale","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Maroyi","given":"Timeyo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Eastern Afghanistan’s forests have been an important energy resource during conflict-related crises in the region, although the forest wood stocks have been severely depleted ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a23dfskj1ie","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNDP, 2014)","plainCitation":"(UNDP, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31218,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CG9JNN7N"],"itemData":{"id":31218,"type":"report","title":"United Nations Development Programme Country: Afghanistan PROJECT DOCUMENT “Establishing integrated models for protected areas and their co-management in Afghanistan","author":[{"family":"UNDP","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Firewood trade can occur in conjunction with farmland clearing, with wood fuel being sold to wealthy urban clients through formal channels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2gvfimoelv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013; FAO, 2017; Gandar, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013; FAO, 2017; Gandar, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3WICGITY"],"itemData":{"id":1107,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy for Sustainable Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.esd.2012.07.004","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"86–94","title":"The environmental impacts of charcoal production in tropical ecosystems of the world: A synthesis","URL":"https://0-www.sciencedirect.com.wam.seals.ac.za/science/article/pii/S0973082612000476","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Chidumayo","given":"Emmanuel N"},{"family":"Gumbo","given":"Davison J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":31270,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GNU9K89D"],"itemData":{"id":31270,"type":"report","abstract":"\\"With food insecurity, climate change and deforestation and forest degradation remaining key global issues, this paper highlights the role of sustainable woodfuel in improving food security. Food insecurity and a high dependence on woodfuel as a primary cooking fuel are characteristics common to vulnerable groups of people in developing regions of the world.With adequate policy and legal frameworks in place, woodfuel production and harvesting can be sustainable and a main source of green energy. Moreover, the widespread availability of woodfuel, and the enormous market for it, presents opportunities for employment and for sustainable value chains, providing further rationale for promoting this source of energy. This paper explains how sustainable woodfuel is closely linked to food security and provides insights in how the linkages could be strengthened at all stages of woodfuel production, trade and use.\\"--Publisher's description","event-place":"Rome","language":"en","note":"OCLC: on1013590908","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"Gemeinsamer Bibliotheksverbund ISBN","title":"Sustainable woodfuel for food security: a smart choice: green, renewable and affordable","title-short":"Sustainable woodfuel for food security","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""},{"family":"Schure","given":"Jolien"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Yoo","given":"Byoung Il"}],"contributor":[{"family":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":1115,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X8N8D23P"],"itemData":{"id":1115,"type":"report","collection-title":"South African Energy Policy Research and Training Project","event-place":"Cape Town","number":"9","publisher":"Energy for Development Research Centre","publisher-place":"Cape Town","title":"Afforestation and woodland management in South Africa","URL":"https://open.uct.ac.za/bitstream/handle/11427/22671/Gandar_1994.pdf?sequence=6","author":[{"family":"Gandar","given":"Mark"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Firewood is normally logged on foot, limiting the logging radius to 1-3km, although this distance can be higher in arid regions with low tree cover ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"p4PWIY2v","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M.B. Cardoso, Ladio, & Lozada, 2013)","plainCitation":"(M.B. Cardoso, Ladio, & Lozada, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31298,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FH6WTGDX"],"itemData":{"id":31298,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Arid Environments","DOI":"10.1016/j.jaridenv.2012.09.013","ISSN":"01401963","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Arid Environments","language":"en","page":"146-152","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fuelwood consumption patterns and resilience in two rural communities of the northwest Patagonian steppe, Argentina","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S014019631200256X","volume":"98","author":[{"family":"Cardoso","given":"M.B."},{"family":"Ladio","given":"A.H."},{"family":"Lozada","given":"M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Increasing firewood demand, locally and in urban areas has resulted in logged wood being collected and transported vehicle or horseback ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wBlwxVqF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M.B. Cardoso, 2013; Matsika, Erasmus, & Twine, 2012)","plainCitation":"(M.B. Cardoso, 2013; Matsika, Erasmus, & Twine, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31298,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FH6WTGDX"],"itemData":{"id":31298,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Arid Environments","DOI":"10.1016/j.jaridenv.2012.09.013","ISSN":"01401963","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Arid Environments","language":"en","page":"146-152","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fuelwood consumption patterns and resilience in two rural communities of the northwest Patagonian steppe, Argentina","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S014019631200256X","volume":"98","author":[{"family":"Cardoso","given":"M.B."},{"family":"Ladio","given":"A.H."},{"family":"Lozada","given":"M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",11]]}}},{"id":31306,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BZEC2QFA"],"itemData":{"id":31306,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892912000264","ISSN":"0376-8929","issue":"01","page":"71–83","title":"A tale of two villages: assessing the dynamics of fuelwood supply in communal landscapes in South Africa","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Matsika","given":"Ruwadzano"},{"family":"Erasmus","given":"Barend F.N. N"},{"family":"Twine","given":"Wayne C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and may be by ‘outsiders’ ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Xpe4u7XL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(W. Twine, Saphugu, & Moshe, 2003)","plainCitation":"(W. Twine, Saphugu, & Moshe, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1116,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K8N8GTU5"],"itemData":{"id":1116,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The International Journal of Sustainable Development & World Ecology","issue":"3","page":"263–274","title":"Harvesting of communal resources by ‘outsiders’ in rural South Africa: a case of xenophobia or a real threat to sustainability?","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/13504500309469804","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Twine","given":"Wayne"},{"family":"Saphugu","given":"Victor"},{"family":"Moshe","given":"Dineo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Localized shortages have also resulted in increased harvest time, changes in collected species or size classes, or harvest of live wood during deadwood shortages, often in violation of local traditional knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CGT5exsd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Findlay & Twine, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Findlay & Twine, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"ERpTB65M/30Alku3o","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A7Z5TWGK"],"itemData":{"id":4574,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land","issue":"1","page":"35","title":"Chiefs in a democracy: A case study of the ‘new’systems of regulating firewood harvesting in post-apartheid South Africa","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Findlay","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Twine","given":"Wayne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Higher income is associated with a reduction in both firewood and charcoal use, although there is substantial variation between countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"klsHTBzc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J.E.M. Arnold, 2003)","plainCitation":"(J.E.M. Arnold, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26738,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UCKMNQT3"],"itemData":{"id":26738,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"type: (CIFOR Occasional Paper, 39).","title":"Fuelwood Revisited: What Has Changed in the Last Decade?","URL":"http://hdl.handle.net/10535/4692","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"J.E.M."},{"family":"Köhlin","given":"G."},{"family":"Persson","given":"R."},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The expectation that provisions of cleaner, more efficient energies and stoves would result in traditional energy users transitioning up the ‘energy ladder’ have largely not occurred, with households ‘stacking’ fuel, i.e., using multiple devices and fuels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nqWBufb3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. E. Michael Arnold, 2006; Hiemstra-Van der Horst & Hovorka, 2008; Masera, 2015; van der Kroon, Brouwer, & Van Beukering, 2013)","plainCitation":"(J. E. Michael Arnold, 2006; Hiemstra-Van der Horst & Hovorka, 2008; Masera, 2015; van der Kroon, Brouwer, & Van Beukering, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1122,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8N3A8MKI"],"itemData":{"id":1122,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Summary\\nIn the 1970s, it appeared that fuelwood use was growing rapidly, and this could have major adverse impacts on the resource and poor users. By the mid-1980s, revised assessments indicated that there was less of a problem than had been foreseen, and much less of a need for forestry interventions to maintain supplies. As a result many of the latter were sharply scaled back. Additional information and analysis that has become available since the 1980s support the view that there is no need for large scale interventions devoted just to provision of fuelwood. However, declining access to supplies or markets can raise significant problems in some areas, and for particular categories of user. Growing urban demand for charcoal can also cause local problems, in particular in Africa. The potentials for appropriate forms of intervention are discussed.","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2005.08.008","ISSN":"0305-750X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","page":"596-611","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Woodfuels, livelihoods, and policy interventions: Changing Perspectives","title-short":"Woodfuels, livelihoods, and policy interventions","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305750X05002263","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"J. E. Michael"},{"family":"Köhlin","given":"Gunnar"},{"family":"Persson","given":"Reidar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",3,1]]}}},{"id":1114,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LP9P4HYG"],"itemData":{"id":1114,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy Policy","issue":"9","page":"3333–3344","title":"Reassessing the “energy ladder”: household energy use in Maun, Botswana","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301421508002280","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Hiemstra-Van der Horst","given":"Greg"},{"family":"Hovorka","given":"Alice J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":31394,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z88NFHWT"],"itemData":{"id":31394,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-102014-021318","page":"121–150","title":"Environmental burden of traditional bioenergy use","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Masera","given":"Omar R"},{"family":"Bailis","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Rudi"},{"family":"Ghilardi","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Ruiz-Mercado","given":"Ilse"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":1113,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EZEB59RV"],"itemData":{"id":1113,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews","page":"504–513","title":"The energy ladder: Theoretical myth or empirical truth? Results from a meta-analysis","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1364032112006594","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Kroon","given":"Bianca","non-dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Brouwer","given":"Roy"},{"family":"Van Beukering","given":"Pieter Jh"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wood fuel use at household level is inelastic for a variety of reasons, including cultural and household taste preferences, high capital cost of appliances and energy, poor infrastructure and reflects dynamic, complex decision making at a household level ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LVF9AXQO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. E. Michael Arnold, 2006; Hiemstra-Van der Horst & Hovorka, 2008; IEA, 2017; Masera, 2015; van der Kroon, 2013)","plainCitation":"(J. E. Michael Arnold, 2006; Hiemstra-Van der Horst & Hovorka, 2008; IEA, 2017; Masera, 2015; van der Kroon, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1122,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8N3A8MKI"],"itemData":{"id":1122,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Summary\\nIn the 1970s, it appeared that fuelwood use was growing rapidly, and this could have major adverse impacts on the resource and poor users. By the mid-1980s, revised assessments indicated that there was less of a problem than had been foreseen, and much less of a need for forestry interventions to maintain supplies. As a result many of the latter were sharply scaled back. Additional information and analysis that has become available since the 1980s support the view that there is no need for large scale interventions devoted just to provision of fuelwood. However, declining access to supplies or markets can raise significant problems in some areas, and for particular categories of user. Growing urban demand for charcoal can also cause local problems, in particular in Africa. The potentials for appropriate forms of intervention are discussed.","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2005.08.008","ISSN":"0305-750X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","page":"596-611","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Woodfuels, livelihoods, and policy interventions: Changing Perspectives","title-short":"Woodfuels, livelihoods, and policy interventions","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305750X05002263","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"J. E. Michael"},{"family":"Köhlin","given":"Gunnar"},{"family":"Persson","given":"Reidar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",3,1]]}}},{"id":1114,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LP9P4HYG"],"itemData":{"id":1114,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy Policy","issue":"9","page":"3333–3344","title":"Reassessing the “energy ladder”: household energy use in Maun, Botswana","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301421508002280","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Hiemstra-Van der Horst","given":"Greg"},{"family":"Hovorka","given":"Alice J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":1123,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/356RTL52"],"itemData":{"id":1123,"type":"report","event-place":"Paris","genre":"World Energy Outlook Special Report","publisher":"International Energy Agency","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Energy Access Outlook 2017: From poverty to prosperity","URL":"https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-access-outlook-2017","author":[{"family":"IEA","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":31394,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z88NFHWT"],"itemData":{"id":31394,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-102014-021318","page":"121–150","title":"Environmental burden of traditional bioenergy use","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Masera","given":"Omar R"},{"family":"Bailis","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Rudi"},{"family":"Ghilardi","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Ruiz-Mercado","given":"Ilse"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":1113,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EZEB59RV"],"itemData":{"id":1113,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews","page":"504–513","title":"The energy ladder: Theoretical myth or empirical truth? Results from a meta-analysis","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1364032112006594","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Kroon","given":"Bianca","non-dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Brouwer","given":"Roy"},{"family":"Van Beukering","given":"Pieter Jh"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are indications that energy stacking, and the availability of a diversity of energy resources represents the adaptive capacity of communities, favors the conservation of local species and contributes to broader social-ecological resilience ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Z5GUZGl0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M.B. Cardoso, 2013)","plainCitation":"(M.B. Cardoso, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31298,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FH6WTGDX"],"itemData":{"id":31298,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Arid Environments","DOI":"10.1016/j.jaridenv.2012.09.013","ISSN":"01401963","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Arid Environments","language":"en","page":"146-152","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fuelwood consumption patterns and resilience in two rural communities of the northwest Patagonian steppe, Argentina","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S014019631200256X","volume":"98","author":[{"family":"Cardoso","given":"M.B."},{"family":"Ladio","given":"A.H."},{"family":"Lozada","given":"M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Energy stacking is a complex phenomenon, illustrated by a case in Patagonia, Argentina. A local village reliant on costly firewood, received subsidized liquefied petroleum gas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hYsCK3Xs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Betina Cardoso & Gonz\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}lez, 2019)","plainCitation":"(M. Betina Cardoso & González, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31299,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8BEY9JQ"],"itemData":{"id":31299,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy for Sustainable Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.esd.2019.03.007","ISSN":"09730826","journalAbbreviation":"Energy for Sustainable Development","language":"en","page":"82-90","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Residential energy transition and thermal efficiency in an arid environment of northeast Patagonia, Argentina","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S097308261831370X","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Cardoso","given":"M. Betina"},{"family":"González","given":"Alejandro D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This drastically reduced the amount of wood fuel collected in the region and reduced household air pollution, but not only did households continue to use wood burning stoves, their gas consumption to heat poorly insulated houses was extremely high (650 kilowatt-hour/m2) incurring substantial operational and environmental costs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pztYo7t2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Betina Cardoso & Gonz\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}lez, 2019)","plainCitation":"(M. Betina Cardoso & González, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31299,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8BEY9JQ"],"itemData":{"id":31299,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy for Sustainable Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.esd.2019.03.007","ISSN":"09730826","journalAbbreviation":"Energy for Sustainable Development","language":"en","page":"82-90","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Residential energy transition and thermal efficiency in an arid environment of northeast Patagonia, Argentina","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S097308261831370X","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Cardoso","given":"M. Betina"},{"family":"González","given":"Alejandro D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The study’s recommendations were two-fold: insulate the houses and receive a return on investment in liquefied petroleum gas savings in 2.2 years, and consider subsiding a household preference-determined mix of cheaper fire wood and gas to reduce subsidization costs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IglPHRhY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Betina Cardoso & Gonz\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}lez, 2019)","plainCitation":"(M. Betina Cardoso & González, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31299,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8BEY9JQ"],"itemData":{"id":31299,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy for Sustainable Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.esd.2019.03.007","ISSN":"09730826","journalAbbreviation":"Energy for Sustainable Development","language":"en","page":"82-90","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Residential energy transition and thermal efficiency in an arid environment of northeast Patagonia, Argentina","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S097308261831370X","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Cardoso","given":"M. Betina"},{"family":"González","given":"Alejandro D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This example clearly demonstrates the complexities in altering relative energy mixes and the potential trade-offs to social, economic and the environmental conditions.
	Although firewood use is slowly decreasing, charcoal demand in urban areas is growing, doubling over 25 years to about 207 million m3 wood for charcoal per annum in 2000 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1HhsxQFT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J.E.M. Arnold, 2003)","plainCitation":"(J.E.M. Arnold, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26738,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UCKMNQT3"],"itemData":{"id":26738,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"type: (CIFOR Occasional Paper, 39).","title":"Fuelwood Revisited: What Has Changed in the Last Decade?","URL":"http://hdl.handle.net/10535/4692","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"J.E.M."},{"family":"Köhlin","given":"G."},{"family":"Persson","given":"R."},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In tropical South America charcoal use varies across the region with Brazilian use mainly for manufacturing and Central American for the food industry and limited domestic use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6TXOWcv9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3WICGITY"],"itemData":{"id":1107,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy for Sustainable Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.esd.2012.07.004","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"86–94","title":"The environmental impacts of charcoal production in tropical ecosystems of the world: A synthesis","URL":"https://0-www.sciencedirect.com.wam.seals.ac.za/science/article/pii/S0973082612000476","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Chidumayo","given":"Emmanuel N"},{"family":"Gumbo","given":"Davison J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In sub-Saharan Africa charcoal is mainly used for household cooking, particularly in urban areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qQibV9Gr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3WICGITY"],"itemData":{"id":1107,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy for Sustainable Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.esd.2012.07.004","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"86–94","title":"The environmental impacts of charcoal production in tropical ecosystems of the world: A synthesis","URL":"https://0-www.sciencedirect.com.wam.seals.ac.za/science/article/pii/S0973082612000476","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Chidumayo","given":"Emmanuel N"},{"family":"Gumbo","given":"Davison J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Rural-urban charcoal trade is increasing as wealthy, urban firewood users ‘transition’ to charcoal ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GTbNLOS4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. E. Michael Arnold, 2006)","plainCitation":"(J. E. Michael Arnold, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1122,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8N3A8MKI"],"itemData":{"id":1122,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Summary\\nIn the 1970s, it appeared that fuelwood use was growing rapidly, and this could have major adverse impacts on the resource and poor users. By the mid-1980s, revised assessments indicated that there was less of a problem than had been foreseen, and much less of a need for forestry interventions to maintain supplies. As a result many of the latter were sharply scaled back. Additional information and analysis that has become available since the 1980s support the view that there is no need for large scale interventions devoted just to provision of fuelwood. However, declining access to supplies or markets can raise significant problems in some areas, and for particular categories of user. Growing urban demand for charcoal can also cause local problems, in particular in Africa. The potentials for appropriate forms of intervention are discussed.","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2005.08.008","ISSN":"0305-750X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","page":"596-611","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Woodfuels, livelihoods, and policy interventions: Changing Perspectives","title-short":"Woodfuels, livelihoods, and policy interventions","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305750X05002263","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"J. E. Michael"},{"family":"Köhlin","given":"Gunnar"},{"family":"Persson","given":"Reidar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, 81% of energy use in Mozambique is wood fuel, with charcoal the predominant use in urban areas with the capital city, Maputo, garnering the highest prices for charcoal ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UPvF2EVC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cuvilas, Jirjis, & Lucas, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Cuvilas, Jirjis, & Lucas, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1104,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YAD2LH6T"],"itemData":{"id":1104,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews","DOI":"10.1016/j.rser.2010.02.002","issue":"7","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"2139–2146","title":"Energy situation in Mozambique: A review","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Cuvilas","given":"Carlos Alberto"},{"family":"Jirjis","given":"Raida"},{"family":"Lucas","given":"Carlos"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is estimated that 91-99% of charcoal production is illegal ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aqf3reend8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cuvilas, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Cuvilas, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1104,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YAD2LH6T"],"itemData":{"id":1104,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews","DOI":"10.1016/j.rser.2010.02.002","issue":"7","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"2139–2146","title":"Energy situation in Mozambique: A review","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Cuvilas","given":"Carlos Alberto"},{"family":"Jirjis","given":"Raida"},{"family":"Lucas","given":"Carlos"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The high value and demand of charcoal in urban areas further incentivizes increased production ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HsQO9JRm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cuvilas, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Cuvilas, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1104,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YAD2LH6T"],"itemData":{"id":1104,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews","DOI":"10.1016/j.rser.2010.02.002","issue":"7","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"2139–2146","title":"Energy situation in Mozambique: A review","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Cuvilas","given":"Carlos Alberto"},{"family":"Jirjis","given":"Raida"},{"family":"Lucas","given":"Carlos"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Charcoal production from plantations is increasing in the global tropics and charcoal is still predominantly derived from wild species in natural forests, and frequently related to deforestation or forest degradation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"L88BOjqY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3WICGITY"],"itemData":{"id":1107,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy for Sustainable Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.esd.2012.07.004","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"86–94","title":"The environmental impacts of charcoal production in tropical ecosystems of the world: A synthesis","URL":"https://0-www.sciencedirect.com.wam.seals.ac.za/science/article/pii/S0973082612000476","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Chidumayo","given":"Emmanuel N"},{"family":"Gumbo","given":"Davison J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Charcoal logging alone have resulted in the loss of 3 million hectares of forest cover in 2009 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wwgoBbwZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3WICGITY"],"itemData":{"id":1107,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy for Sustainable Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.esd.2012.07.004","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"86–94","title":"The environmental impacts of charcoal production in tropical ecosystems of the world: A synthesis","URL":"https://0-www.sciencedirect.com.wam.seals.ac.za/science/article/pii/S0973082612000476","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Chidumayo","given":"Emmanuel N"},{"family":"Gumbo","given":"Davison J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Southern Africa charcoal production is valued at about 2-3% of gross domestic product ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rj3Ect1k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Malimbwi, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Malimbwi, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31308,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S27F5LQ6"],"itemData":{"id":31308,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The dry forests and woodlands of Africa: managing for products and services","event-place":"London, UK","ISBN":"978-1-84977-654-7","page":"155-178","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London, UK","title":"Woodfuel","editor":[{"family":"Chidumayo","given":"Emmanuel N"},{"family":"Gumbo","given":"Davison J"}],"author":[{"family":"Malimbwi","given":"Rogers"},{"family":"Chidumayo","given":"Emmanuel"},{"family":"Zahabu","given":"Eliakim"},{"family":"Kingazi","given":"Stephano"},{"family":"Misana","given":"Salome"},{"family":"Luoga","given":"Emmanuel"},{"family":"Nduwamungu","given":"Jean"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and forms a significant income source with households able to earn 1000 to 10,000 United States dollars per annum although studies suggest this income is not sustained over the long-term and does not provide improvements in human well-being ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zgIrDEy7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Baumert, 2016; H. E. Smith, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Baumert, 2016; H. E. Smith, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31307,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V5PCUNCG"],"itemData":{"id":31307,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy for Sustainable Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.esd.2016.06.003","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"129–138","title":"Charcoal supply chains from Mabalane to Maputo: Who benefits?","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Baumert","given":"Sophia"},{"family":"Luz","given":"Ana Catarina"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Janet"},{"family":"Vollmer","given":"Frank"},{"family":"Ryan","given":"Casey M"},{"family":"Patenaude","given":"Genevieve"},{"family":"Zorrilla-Miras","given":"Pedro"},{"family":"Artur","given":"Luis"},{"family":"Nhantumbo","given":"Isilda"},{"family":"Macqueen","given":"Duncan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":31312,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MKZCPLB7"],"itemData":{"id":31312,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2019.101976","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"101976","title":"Impacts of land use intensification on human wellbeing: Evidence from rural Mozambique","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Smith","given":"Harriet Elizabeth"},{"family":"Ryan","given":"Casey M"},{"family":"Vollmer","given":"Frank"},{"family":"Woollen","given":"Emily"},{"family":"Keane","given":"Aidan"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Janet A"},{"family":"Baumert","given":"Sophia"},{"family":"Grundy","given":"Isla M"},{"family":"Carvalho","given":"Mariana"},{"family":"Lisboa","given":"Sá N"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Like firewood, the broad extent of charcoal logging and impacts remain unknown due to the informal and dynamic use of the resource. 
	Wood for firewood and charcoal are usually cut from main branches or the main stem, leaving the stump rooted in the ground. Many tree and shrub species logged for energy regenerate vegetatively, sprouting from the cut or damaged trunk, although the rate of coppice growth varies across species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JqIz1ci2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Neke, Owen-Smith, & Witkowski, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Neke, Owen-Smith, & Witkowski, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1109,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7U83L43H"],"itemData":{"id":1109,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2006.05.051","issue":"1-3","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"114–123","title":"Comparative resprouting response of Savanna woody plant species following harvesting: the value of persistence","volume":"232","author":[{"family":"Neke","given":"Kirsten S"},{"family":"Owen-Smith","given":"Norman"},{"family":"Witkowski","given":"Ed TF"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, environmental context, post-logged land-use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Jw36ZfKl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3WICGITY"],"itemData":{"id":1107,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy for Sustainable Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.esd.2012.07.004","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"86–94","title":"The environmental impacts of charcoal production in tropical ecosystems of the world: A synthesis","URL":"https://0-www.sciencedirect.com.wam.seals.ac.za/science/article/pii/S0973082612000476","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Chidumayo","given":"Emmanuel N"},{"family":"Gumbo","given":"Davison J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and the type of logging ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"O8GEBtJB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. M. Shackleton, 2000)","plainCitation":"(C. M. Shackleton, 2000)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1108,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7FGK49WD"],"itemData":{"id":1108,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"South African Journal of Botany","DOI":"10.1016/S0254-6299(15)31074-7","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"124–127","title":"Stump size and the number of coppice shoots for selected savanna tree species","volume":"66","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Resprouting is a major source of regeneration in dry tropical forests and woodlands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iOqbkla2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chidumayo, 2013; Tredennick & Hanan, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Chidumayo, 2013; Tredennick & Hanan, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1106,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PQNZD75A"],"itemData":{"id":1106,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2012.11.031","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"154–161","title":"Forest degradation and recovery in a miombo woodland landscape in Zambia: 22 years of observations on permanent sample plots","volume":"291","author":[{"family":"Chidumayo","given":"Emmanuel N"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":1105,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M52FWPFF"],"itemData":{"id":1105,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The American Naturalist","DOI":"10.1086/680475","issue":"5","note":"publisher: University of Chicago Press Chicago, IL","page":"E153–E165","title":"Effects of tree harvest on the stable-state dynamics of savanna and forest","volume":"185","author":[{"family":"Tredennick","given":"Andrew T"},{"family":"Hanan","given":"Niall P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and temperate forest regions, forming part of rotational logging management. A review of charcoal production reported 9-12 years logging rotations for Mali, Niger and Burkina Faso, 10-15 years for Mexico, 20-30 years in Zambia, and a wide 8–23-year range in Tanzania ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7Q6uAT39","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3WICGITY"],"itemData":{"id":1107,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy for Sustainable Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.esd.2012.07.004","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"86–94","title":"The environmental impacts of charcoal production in tropical ecosystems of the world: A synthesis","URL":"https://0-www.sciencedirect.com.wam.seals.ac.za/science/article/pii/S0973082612000476","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Chidumayo","given":"Emmanuel N"},{"family":"Gumbo","given":"Davison J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Underestimated coppice regeneration post-firewood and charcoal logging is one of the reasons that the ‘fuelwood crisis’ in which biomass stocks were predicted to collapse, has not occurred ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2UZGUc3X","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J.E.M. Arnold, 2003; Mograbi, 2019; W. C. Twine & Holdo, 2016)","plainCitation":"(J.E.M. Arnold, 2003; Mograbi, 2019; W. C. Twine & Holdo, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26738,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UCKMNQT3"],"itemData":{"id":26738,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"type: (CIFOR Occasional Paper, 39).","title":"Fuelwood Revisited: What Has Changed in the Last Decade?","URL":"http://hdl.handle.net/10535/4692","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"J.E.M."},{"family":"Köhlin","given":"G."},{"family":"Persson","given":"R."},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":31310,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HAMI5ZGX"],"itemData":{"id":31310,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land Degradation & Development","DOI":"10.1002/ldr.3210","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"190–201","title":"Fuelwood extraction intensity drives compensatory regrowth in African savanna communal lands","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Mograbi","given":"Penelope J"},{"family":"Witkowski","given":"Ed TF"},{"family":"Erasmus","given":"Barend FN"},{"family":"Asner","given":"Gregory P"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Jolene T"},{"family":"Mathieu","given":"Renaud"},{"family":"Wessels","given":"Konrad J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":1110,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HITE67PE"],"itemData":{"id":1110,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/1365-2664.12713","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"1766–1776","title":"Fuelwood sustainability revisited: integrating size structure and resprouting into a spatially realistic fuelshed model","URL":"https://0-besjournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com.wam.seals.ac.za/doi/abs/10.1111/1365-2664.12713?sid=vendor%3Adatabase","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Twine","given":"Wayne C"},{"family":"Holdo","given":"Ricardo M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Despite the significant productivity of woodlands and forests, wood fuel logging can alter floristic composition and vegetation structure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"maqHmC8o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mograbi, 2015; Tredennick & Hanan, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Mograbi, 2015; Tredennick & Hanan, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31309,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/84EXZ4ZR"],"itemData":{"id":31309,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS One","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0127093","issue":"5","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e0127093","title":"Biomass increases go under cover: woody vegetation dynamics in South African rangelands","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Mograbi","given":"Penelope J"},{"family":"Erasmus","given":"Barend FN"},{"family":"Witkowski","given":"ETF"},{"family":"Asner","given":"Gregory P"},{"family":"Wessels","given":"Konrad J"},{"family":"Mathieu","given":"Renaud"},{"family":"Knapp","given":"David E"},{"family":"Martin","given":"Roberta E"},{"family":"Main","given":"Russell"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":1105,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M52FWPFF"],"itemData":{"id":1105,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The American Naturalist","DOI":"10.1086/680475","issue":"5","note":"publisher: University of Chicago Press Chicago, IL","page":"E153–E165","title":"Effects of tree harvest on the stable-state dynamics of savanna and forest","volume":"185","author":[{"family":"Tredennick","given":"Andrew T"},{"family":"Hanan","given":"Niall P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Depending on the wood fuel logging intensity, these ecosystem changes can alter the amount or type of nature’s contributions to people derived from the forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YeyOB9ea","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Chidumayo & Gumbo, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3WICGITY"],"itemData":{"id":1107,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy for Sustainable Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.esd.2012.07.004","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"86–94","title":"The environmental impacts of charcoal production in tropical ecosystems of the world: A synthesis","URL":"https://0-www.sciencedirect.com.wam.seals.ac.za/science/article/pii/S0973082612000476","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Chidumayo","given":"Emmanuel N"},{"family":"Gumbo","given":"Davison J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, in Mozambique, charcoal production led to a reduction in firewood and construction material resources, with other natural resources such as wild food, medicinal plants and grazing mostly unaffected, although these trade-offs were mediated by village position and woodland resource characteristics ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PKtu5YHE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Woollen, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Woollen, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1130,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QM5LZCTH"],"itemData":{"id":1130,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Africanwoodlands forma major part of the tropical grassy biomeand support the livelihoods of millions of rural and urban people. Charcoal production in particular is a major economic activity, but its impact on other ecosystem services is little studied. To address this, our study collected biophysical and social datasets, which were combined in ecological production functions, to assess ecosystem service provision and its change under different charcoal production scenarios in Gaza Province, southern Mozambique. We found that villages with longer histories of charcoal production had experienced declines in wood suitable for charcoal, firewood and construction, and tended to have lower perceived availabilities of these services. Scenarios of future charcoal impacts indicated that firewood and woody construction ser- vices were likely to trade-off with charcoal production. However, even under the most extreme charcoal scenario, these services were not completely lost. Other provisioning services, such as wild food, medicinal plants and grass, were largely unaffected by charcoal production. To reduce the future impacts of charcoal production, producers must avoid increased intensification of charcoal extraction by avoiding the expansion of species and sizes of trees used for charcoal production. This is a major challenge to land managers and policymakers in the area.","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B-Biological Sciences","DOI":"10.1098/rstb.2015.0315","ISSN":"0962-8436","note":"PMID: 27502380","page":"1–14","title":"Charcoal production in the Mopane woodlands of Mozambique: what are the trade-offs with other ecosystem services?","volume":"371","author":[{"family":"Woollen","given":"Emily"},{"family":"Ryan","given":"Casey M"},{"family":"Baumert","given":"Sophia"},{"family":"Vollmer","given":"Frank"},{"family":"Grundy","given":"Isla"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Janet"},{"family":"Fernando","given":"Jone"},{"family":"Luz","given":"Ana"},{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"Natasha"},{"family":"Lisboa","given":"Sá N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Variable ecosystem regeneration potential and context-specificity of environmental impacts and trade-offs in wood fuel logging are challenging to incorporate into large scale policy and management plans because of the social-ecological complexity and non-linear responses occurring across spatial and temporal scales.
	Gender is one of the predominant features of traditional energy harvest, use and management of wood energy, including their (lack of) involvement in trade of wood fuels. While much of the research and interventions on inequality in forest resource use and management, many of the same challenges and barriers are faced by other vulnerable groups, such as minority ethnic groups, migrants, indigenous peoples, youths, landless people and other socially-differentiated groups such as lower castes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YgV49VjF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Chaudhary, McGregor, Houston, & Chettri, 2018; Kristjanson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(S. Chaudhary, McGregor, Houston, & Chettri, 2018; Kristjanson, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31274,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HHQILHTI"],"itemData":{"id":31274,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2017.10.020","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"99–115","title":"Environmental justice and ecosystem services: A disaggregated analysis of community access to forest benefits in Nepal","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"Sunita"},{"family":"McGregor","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Houston","given":"Donna"},{"family":"Chettri","given":"Nakul"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":1112,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7AKTXWVS"],"itemData":{"id":1112,"type":"report","collection-title":"Program on Forests","genre":"Working Paper","publisher":"PROFOR","title":"Taking action on gender gaps in forest landscapes","URL":"https://www.profor.info/sites/profor.info/files/Working%20Paper_Taking%20Action%20on%20Gender%20Gaps%20in%20Forest%20Landscapes.pdf","author":[{"family":"Kristjanson","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Bah","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Kuriakose","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Shakirova","given":"Meerim"},{"family":"Segura","given":"Gerardo"},{"family":"Siegmann","given":"Katharina"},{"family":"Granat","given":"Margaux"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Gender gaps exist in almost aspects of natural resource use and management, including: disparities in participation; leadership; resource, land access, and tenure; forest use; division of labor and workloads; skills; access to technologies and inputs; access to information; access to forest services; access to benefits; access to credit; access to markets; policy engagement; and forest laws and regulations  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"l2OCOFra","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kristjanson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Kristjanson, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1112,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7AKTXWVS"],"itemData":{"id":1112,"type":"report","collection-title":"Program on Forests","genre":"Working Paper","publisher":"PROFOR","title":"Taking action on gender gaps in forest landscapes","URL":"https://www.profor.info/sites/profor.info/files/Working%20Paper_Taking%20Action%20on%20Gender%20Gaps%20in%20Forest%20Landscapes.pdf","author":[{"family":"Kristjanson","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Bah","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Kuriakose","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Shakirova","given":"Meerim"},{"family":"Segura","given":"Gerardo"},{"family":"Siegmann","given":"Katharina"},{"family":"Granat","given":"Margaux"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. With respect to the use of wood fuel for energy, women bear the majority of the responsibility for logging and using wood fuel ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SCyARKsg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Clancy, Ummar, Shakya, & Kelkar, 2007; IEA, 2017; Murphy, Berazneva, & Lee, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Clancy, Ummar, Shakya, & Kelkar, 2007; IEA, 2017; Murphy, Berazneva, & Lee, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31276,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XHNG8IYS"],"itemData":{"id":31276,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Gender & Development","DOI":"10.1080/13552070701391102","ISSN":"1355-2074, 1364-9221","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Gender & Development","language":"en","page":"241-257","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Appropriate gender-analysis tools for unpacking the gender-energy-poverty nexus","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13552070701391102","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Clancy","given":"Joy"},{"family":"Ummar","given":"Fareeha"},{"family":"Shakya","given":"Indira"},{"family":"Kelkar","given":"Govind"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",7]]}}},{"id":1123,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/356RTL52"],"itemData":{"id":1123,"type":"report","event-place":"Paris","genre":"World Energy Outlook Special Report","publisher":"International Energy Agency","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Energy Access Outlook 2017: From poverty to prosperity","URL":"https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-access-outlook-2017","author":[{"family":"IEA","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":31275,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KBZBVH2L"],"itemData":{"id":31275,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            Fuelwood scarcity creates a widespread environmental problem that places a major burden on women and children in the rural areas of developing countries. Consequently, many governments, donors and non-governmental organizations have encouraged on-farm fuelwood production and agroforestry practices. Whether, however, fuelwood from different sources can be easily substituted is an important empirical question as the degree of substitutability can depend on local markets and households' resource endowments and incomes. In this paper, we examine the substitution between three fuelwood sources among rural households in western Kenya: fuelwood collected off-farm, fuelwood produced on-farm, and that which is purchased. Using household-specific shadow prices for fuelwood and male and female wages, we find that strict gender divisions in household labor result in limited substitution between fuelwood sources. Among the implications are that programs and policies promoting agroforestry will have limited success without first addressing the structural differences in labor markets.","container-title":"Environment and Development Economics","DOI":"10.1017/S1355770X1800027X","ISSN":"1355-770X, 1469-4395","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Dev. Econ.","language":"en","page":"655-678","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fuelwood source substitution, gender, and shadow prices in western Kenya","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S1355770X1800027X/type/journal_article","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Murphy","given":"David M. A."},{"family":"Berazneva","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Lee","given":"David R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Households spend 1.4 hours a day harvesting fuel - a significant amount of time for women and children that could be used on other livelihood activities and education ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Pn83DEVU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IEA, 2017)","plainCitation":"(IEA, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1123,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/356RTL52"],"itemData":{"id":1123,"type":"report","event-place":"Paris","genre":"World Energy Outlook Special Report","publisher":"International Energy Agency","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Energy Access Outlook 2017: From poverty to prosperity","URL":"https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-access-outlook-2017","author":[{"family":"IEA","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The physical burden of headloads is not insignificant with a bundle weighing between 25-50 kg ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0AxryBVV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IEA, 2017)","plainCitation":"(IEA, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1123,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/356RTL52"],"itemData":{"id":1123,"type":"report","event-place":"Paris","genre":"World Energy Outlook Special Report","publisher":"International Energy Agency","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Energy Access Outlook 2017: From poverty to prosperity","URL":"https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-access-outlook-2017","author":[{"family":"IEA","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Lack of access to clean cooking methods also has implications for household health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hJ0PWLwV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IEA, 2017)","plainCitation":"(IEA, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1123,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/356RTL52"],"itemData":{"id":1123,"type":"report","event-place":"Paris","genre":"World Energy Outlook Special Report","publisher":"International Energy Agency","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Energy Access Outlook 2017: From poverty to prosperity","URL":"https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-access-outlook-2017","author":[{"family":"IEA","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, with women and children the most vulnerable to household air pollution which is a major cause of death and illness in low-income countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wtAr4dFY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Masera, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Masera, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31394,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z88NFHWT"],"itemData":{"id":31394,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-102014-021318","page":"121–150","title":"Environmental burden of traditional bioenergy use","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Masera","given":"Omar R"},{"family":"Bailis","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Drigo","given":"Rudi"},{"family":"Ghilardi","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Ruiz-Mercado","given":"Ilse"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	An innovative approach to track how women are benefitting from interventions in forest resource use, trade and management is the W+ certification standard ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GLwbIVn2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(WOCAN, 2020)","plainCitation":"(WOCAN, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31273,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4MUYXH5I"],"itemData":{"id":31273,"type":"article-newspaper","container-title":"Women Organizing for Change in Agriculture and Natural Resource Management (WOCAN)","title":"About the W+ Standard","URL":"https://www.wocan.org/what-we-do/wstandard","author":[{"family":"WOCAN","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The standard was created to measure women’s empowerment and to accelerate investment to address gender inequality in access to resources and capital, specifically targeting improvements in: time, income and assets, health, leadership, education and knowledge, and food security ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Md2H4Ud6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(WOCAN, 2020)","plainCitation":"(WOCAN, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31273,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4MUYXH5I"],"itemData":{"id":31273,"type":"article-newspaper","container-title":"Women Organizing for Change in Agriculture and Natural Resource Management (WOCAN)","title":"About the W+ Standard","URL":"https://www.wocan.org/what-we-do/wstandard","author":[{"family":"WOCAN","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The standard provides certification for economic development and environment projects that improve socio-economic conditions for women. Benefits accrue to women through involvement in certified projects as well as from direct payments to women from the sale of W+ certification credits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GnP1wLBj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(WOCAN, 2020)","plainCitation":"(WOCAN, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31273,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4MUYXH5I"],"itemData":{"id":31273,"type":"article-newspaper","container-title":"Women Organizing for Change in Agriculture and Natural Resource Management (WOCAN)","title":"About the W+ Standard","URL":"https://www.wocan.org/what-we-do/wstandard","author":[{"family":"WOCAN","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Successful application of the W+ programme has demonstrated that interventions that save time and improve wood fuel efficiency are especially beneficial to women ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LjY6OBEZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kristjanson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Kristjanson, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1112,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7AKTXWVS"],"itemData":{"id":1112,"type":"report","collection-title":"Program on Forests","genre":"Working Paper","publisher":"PROFOR","title":"Taking action on gender gaps in forest landscapes","URL":"https://www.profor.info/sites/profor.info/files/Working%20Paper_Taking%20Action%20on%20Gender%20Gaps%20in%20Forest%20Landscapes.pdf","author":[{"family":"Kristjanson","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Bah","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Kuriakose","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Shakirova","given":"Meerim"},{"family":"Segura","given":"Gerardo"},{"family":"Siegmann","given":"Katharina"},{"family":"Granat","given":"Margaux"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. W+ certification involving biogas digester projects in Nepal and Indonesia have resulted in tangible time and energy savings for women with improvements in income, assets and leadership capacity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oENqUAyB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kristjanson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Kristjanson, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1112,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7AKTXWVS"],"itemData":{"id":1112,"type":"report","collection-title":"Program on Forests","genre":"Working Paper","publisher":"PROFOR","title":"Taking action on gender gaps in forest landscapes","URL":"https://www.profor.info/sites/profor.info/files/Working%20Paper_Taking%20Action%20on%20Gender%20Gaps%20in%20Forest%20Landscapes.pdf","author":[{"family":"Kristjanson","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Bah","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Kuriakose","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Shakirova","given":"Meerim"},{"family":"Segura","given":"Gerardo"},{"family":"Siegmann","given":"Katharina"},{"family":"Granat","given":"Margaux"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Uptake of more fuel-efficient stoves has the potential for environmental benefits too. A case study in China documents a successful social media campaign to improve fuel-efficient stove uptake ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZM43KMPT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(DeWan, Green, Li, & Hayden, 2013)","plainCitation":"(DeWan, Green, Li, & Hayden, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1111,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z2TS3ZCX"],"itemData":{"id":1111,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fuel  wood  is  a  key  source  of  energy  for  many  families  in  developing  areas  of  China.    Fuel  efficient stoves    are    often    identified    as    a    win-win    solution    for    forest    protections    and    public health/development  in  China  and  across  the  globe.  However,  the  communication  and  connection between stoves and biodiversity conservation has been less clear, by both those who are promoting their use as well as those adopting the technology. Social  marketing is the application  of marketing principles used to sell products applied to “sell” ideas, attitudes, and behaviours to benefit the public good. The Campaign to Protect the Sichuan Golden Snub-nosed Monkey in the Yuhe Nature Reserve, Gansu  Province,  China,  was  initiated  in  2008  in  an  effort  to  inspire  communities  to  protect forest habitat in the reserve, and quickly adopt fuel-efficient stoves.  Results of this study show significant increases in knowledge, attitudes, and interpersonal communication pre and post campaign (16 –49 percentage  points).    Post-campaign  (within  1  year)  results  concluded  28.0%  and  43.1%  of  those surveyed within 1 year of and 2.5 years adopted the technology.  For those households that adopted fuel-efficient   stoves,   consumption   and   gathering   time   were   reducedby40.1%   and   38.2% respectively.  Finally,preliminary research suggests that adoption of fuel-efficient stoves also lead to a  reduction  in  forest  destruction,  with  a  23.7  %  reduction  in  the  number  of  newly  felled  trees  in areas where the stoves had been adopted by greater than half of the surrounding community.  The results of this study suggest that social marketing can be an effective tool for improving community knowledge  and  attitudes,  decreasing  destructive  behaviour,  and  reducing  threats  to  biological important forests in China.","container-title":"Conservation Evidence","issue":"1","page":"32-36","title":"Using social marketing tools to increase fuel-efficient stove adoption for conservation of the golden snub-nosed monkey, Gansu Province, China","URL":"https://www.changewildlifeconsumers.org/site/assets/files/1355/golden-snub-nosed-monkey-case-study.pdf","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"DeWan","given":"Amielle"},{"family":"Green","given":"Kevin"},{"family":"Li","given":"Xiaohong"},{"family":"Hayden","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. After two years, 43% of households had incorporated the stoves into their use, saving 40.1% on gathering time, and in the process saw a 23.7% reduction in newly felled trees in areas crucial to the conservation of the Sichuan Golden Snub-nosed Monkey ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lCx6sCj9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(DeWan, 2013)","plainCitation":"(DeWan, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1111,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z2TS3ZCX"],"itemData":{"id":1111,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fuel  wood  is  a  key  source  of  energy  for  many  families  in  developing  areas  of  China.    Fuel  efficient stoves    are    often    identified    as    a    win-win    solution    for    forest    protections    and    public health/development  in  China  and  across  the  globe.  However,  the  communication  and  connection between stoves and biodiversity conservation has been less clear, by both those who are promoting their use as well as those adopting the technology. Social  marketing is the application  of marketing principles used to sell products applied to “sell” ideas, attitudes, and behaviours to benefit the public good. The Campaign to Protect the Sichuan Golden Snub-nosed Monkey in the Yuhe Nature Reserve, Gansu  Province,  China,  was  initiated  in  2008  in  an  effort  to  inspire  communities  to  protect forest habitat in the reserve, and quickly adopt fuel-efficient stoves.  Results of this study show significant increases in knowledge, attitudes, and interpersonal communication pre and post campaign (16 –49 percentage  points).    Post-campaign  (within  1  year)  results  concluded  28.0%  and  43.1%  of  those surveyed within 1 year of and 2.5 years adopted the technology.  For those households that adopted fuel-efficient   stoves,   consumption   and   gathering   time   were   reducedby40.1%   and   38.2% respectively.  Finally,preliminary research suggests that adoption of fuel-efficient stoves also lead to a  reduction  in  forest  destruction,  with  a  23.7  %  reduction  in  the  number  of  newly  felled  trees  in areas where the stoves had been adopted by greater than half of the surrounding community.  The results of this study suggest that social marketing can be an effective tool for improving community knowledge  and  attitudes,  decreasing  destructive  behaviour,  and  reducing  threats  to  biological important forests in China.","container-title":"Conservation Evidence","issue":"1","page":"32-36","title":"Using social marketing tools to increase fuel-efficient stove adoption for conservation of the golden snub-nosed monkey, Gansu Province, China","URL":"https://www.changewildlifeconsumers.org/site/assets/files/1355/golden-snub-nosed-monkey-case-study.pdf","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"DeWan","given":"Amielle"},{"family":"Green","given":"Kevin"},{"family":"Li","given":"Xiaohong"},{"family":"Hayden","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Whilst gender inequalities are certainly a rights-based issue ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2hvYG2el","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Chaudhary, 2018; Clancy, 2007; Rights and Resources Initiative, 2014)","plainCitation":"(S. Chaudhary, 2018; Clancy, 2007; Rights and Resources Initiative, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31274,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HHQILHTI"],"itemData":{"id":31274,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2017.10.020","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"99–115","title":"Environmental justice and ecosystem services: A disaggregated analysis of community access to forest benefits in Nepal","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"Sunita"},{"family":"McGregor","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Houston","given":"Donna"},{"family":"Chettri","given":"Nakul"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":31276,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XHNG8IYS"],"itemData":{"id":31276,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Gender & Development","DOI":"10.1080/13552070701391102","ISSN":"1355-2074, 1364-9221","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Gender & Development","language":"en","page":"241-257","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Appropriate gender-analysis tools for unpacking the gender-energy-poverty nexus","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13552070701391102","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Clancy","given":"Joy"},{"family":"Ummar","given":"Fareeha"},{"family":"Shakya","given":"Indira"},{"family":"Kelkar","given":"Govind"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",7]]}}},{"id":31277,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H6GFE7QV"],"itemData":{"id":31277,"type":"report","event-place":"Washington DC","publisher-place":"Washington DC","title":"What future for reform? Progress and slowdown in forest tenure reform since 2002","URL":"https://rightsandresources.org/en/publication/view/what-future-for-reform/","author":[{"family":"Rights and Resources Initiative","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, investment in targeted programmes for women are opportunities for the sustainable management of forests and poverty relief ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rN2cNXSg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kristjanson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Kristjanson, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1112,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7AKTXWVS"],"itemData":{"id":1112,"type":"report","collection-title":"Program on Forests","genre":"Working Paper","publisher":"PROFOR","title":"Taking action on gender gaps in forest landscapes","URL":"https://www.profor.info/sites/profor.info/files/Working%20Paper_Taking%20Action%20on%20Gender%20Gaps%20in%20Forest%20Landscapes.pdf","author":[{"family":"Kristjanson","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Bah","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Kuriakose","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Shakirova","given":"Meerim"},{"family":"Segura","given":"Gerardo"},{"family":"Siegmann","given":"Katharina"},{"family":"Granat","given":"Margaux"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ingram ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TgbpBgf0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ingram, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Ingram, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33463,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZKYGLITC"],"itemData":{"id":33463,"type":"chapter","call-number":"SD247 .G46 2016","collection-title":"The earthscan forest library","container-title":"Gender and forests: climate change, tenure, value chains and emerging issues","event-place":"London ; New York","ISBN":"978-1-138-95503-5","publisher":"Routledge, Taylor & Francis Group","publisher-place":"London ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Gender and Forest, Tree and Agroforestry Value Chains: Evidence from Literature","editor":[{"family":"Colfer","given":"Carol J. Pierce"},{"family":"Sijapati Basnett","given":"Bimbika"},{"family":"Elias","given":"Marlene"}],"author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"V"},{"family":"Haverhals","given":"M"},{"family":"Petersen","given":"S"},{"family":"Elias","given":"M"},{"family":"Sijapati Basnett","given":"B"},{"family":"Sola","given":"P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} document cases where male and female headed households harvest the same amount of wood, yet male households earned over three times more. In a Kenya study, women earn less than men in trading wood, and woodlots were mainly managed by men ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fN1y2EUr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Murphy, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Murphy, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31275,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KBZBVH2L"],"itemData":{"id":31275,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            Fuelwood scarcity creates a widespread environmental problem that places a major burden on women and children in the rural areas of developing countries. Consequently, many governments, donors and non-governmental organizations have encouraged on-farm fuelwood production and agroforestry practices. Whether, however, fuelwood from different sources can be easily substituted is an important empirical question as the degree of substitutability can depend on local markets and households' resource endowments and incomes. In this paper, we examine the substitution between three fuelwood sources among rural households in western Kenya: fuelwood collected off-farm, fuelwood produced on-farm, and that which is purchased. Using household-specific shadow prices for fuelwood and male and female wages, we find that strict gender divisions in household labor result in limited substitution between fuelwood sources. Among the implications are that programs and policies promoting agroforestry will have limited success without first addressing the structural differences in labor markets.","container-title":"Environment and Development Economics","DOI":"10.1017/S1355770X1800027X","ISSN":"1355-770X, 1469-4395","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Dev. Econ.","language":"en","page":"655-678","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fuelwood source substitution, gender, and shadow prices in western Kenya","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S1355770X1800027X/type/journal_article","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Murphy","given":"David M. A."},{"family":"Berazneva","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Lee","given":"David R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Yet women’s expenditures and increased roles in household expenditure decisions are associated with improvements in household nutrition, health and education ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fmuKVGkY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. Ingram, 2016)","plainCitation":"(V. Ingram, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33463,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZKYGLITC"],"itemData":{"id":33463,"type":"chapter","call-number":"SD247 .G46 2016","collection-title":"The earthscan forest library","container-title":"Gender and forests: climate change, tenure, value chains and emerging issues","event-place":"London ; New York","ISBN":"978-1-138-95503-5","publisher":"Routledge, Taylor & Francis Group","publisher-place":"London ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Gender and Forest, Tree and Agroforestry Value Chains: Evidence from Literature","editor":[{"family":"Colfer","given":"Carol J. Pierce"},{"family":"Sijapati Basnett","given":"Bimbika"},{"family":"Elias","given":"Marlene"}],"author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"V"},{"family":"Haverhals","given":"M"},{"family":"Petersen","given":"S"},{"family":"Elias","given":"M"},{"family":"Sijapati Basnett","given":"B"},{"family":"Sola","given":"P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Women’s income is a major determinant of household fuel choice and use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sWCOHHE0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(van der Kroon, 2013)","plainCitation":"(van der Kroon, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1113,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EZEB59RV"],"itemData":{"id":1113,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews","page":"504–513","title":"The energy ladder: Theoretical myth or empirical truth? Results from a meta-analysis","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1364032112006594","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Kroon","given":"Bianca","non-dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Brouwer","given":"Roy"},{"family":"Van Beukering","given":"Pieter Jh"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thus, gender responsive interventions in training and enabling women to access markets and boost income can serve as leverage points for improving community well-being ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IbetPglh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Groot, Mohlakoana, Knox, & Bressers, 2017; V. Ingram, 2016)","plainCitation":"(de Groot, Mohlakoana, Knox, & Bressers, 2017; V. Ingram, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31281,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SATVSSFJ"],"itemData":{"id":31281,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energy Research & Social Science","DOI":"10.1016/j.erss.2017.04.004","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"86–97","title":"Fuelling women's empowerment? An exploration of the linkages between gender, entrepreneurship and access to energy in the informal food sector","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Groot","given":"Jiska","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Mohlakoana","given":"Nthabiseng"},{"family":"Knox","given":"Abigail"},{"family":"Bressers","given":"Hans"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33463,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZKYGLITC"],"itemData":{"id":33463,"type":"chapter","call-number":"SD247 .G46 2016","collection-title":"The earthscan forest library","container-title":"Gender and forests: climate change, tenure, value chains and emerging issues","event-place":"London ; New York","ISBN":"978-1-138-95503-5","publisher":"Routledge, Taylor & Francis Group","publisher-place":"London ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Gender and Forest, Tree and Agroforestry Value Chains: Evidence from Literature","editor":[{"family":"Colfer","given":"Carol J. Pierce"},{"family":"Sijapati Basnett","given":"Bimbika"},{"family":"Elias","given":"Marlene"}],"author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"V"},{"family":"Haverhals","given":"M"},{"family":"Petersen","given":"S"},{"family":"Elias","given":"M"},{"family":"Sijapati Basnett","given":"B"},{"family":"Sola","given":"P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, opportunities for improved ecosystem health as empowering women’s leadership and technical capacity building have been found to improve sustainable management of forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VoYmfncB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mwangi & Mai, 2011; Mwangi, Meinzen-Dick, & Sun, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Mwangi & Mai, 2011; Mwangi, Meinzen-Dick, & Sun, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1103,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S4AIDJMC"],"itemData":{"id":1103,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-03873-160117","issue":"1","note":"publisher: JSTOR","title":"Gender and sustainable forest management in East Africa and Latin America","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Mwangi","given":"Esther"},{"family":"Meinzen-Dick","given":"Ruth"},{"family":"Sun","given":"Yan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":31282,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4QZIZ4EW"],"itemData":{"id":31282,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554811797406561","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"119-122","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Introduction to the Special Issue on Forests and Gender","URL":"http://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/146554811797406561","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Mwangi","given":"Esther"},{"family":"Mai","given":"Y.H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",6,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, if wood fuel demand declines significantly, there are many women reliant on wood fuel sale income that will have reduced earnings in the event of a lack of alternate opportunities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3QmHyi9T","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IEA, 2017)","plainCitation":"(IEA, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1123,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/356RTL52"],"itemData":{"id":1123,"type":"report","event-place":"Paris","genre":"World Energy Outlook Special Report","publisher":"International Energy Agency","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Energy Access Outlook 2017: From poverty to prosperity","URL":"https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-access-outlook-2017","author":[{"family":"IEA","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

3.3.4.4.3. Material and Construction 

To have an idea of the amount of timber converted into wood for different purposes (sawn wood, energy, industrial round wood and paper and paper board), the assessment utilizes statistics on the production and trade of forest products over 245 countries and territories ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1vljiduvvb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2017a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2017a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26775,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HEL9AT6T","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4QJ596GD"],"itemData":{"id":26775,"type":"webpage","language":"it","title":"FAOSTAT statistical database","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, this does not include products from illegal timber trade. 

* Industrial round wood 
Industrial round wood is all roundwood used for any purpose other than energy. It comprises pulpwood, sawlogs and veneer logs. Global industrial roundwood removals have increased from 1.7 billion m3 in 1990 to 2.0 billion m3 in 2019 (Figure 3.59). 


Figure 3.59 Global trends in industrial roundwood use. Data from FAO database ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bFhleJWm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2017a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2017a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26775,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HEL9AT6T","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4QJ596GD"],"itemData":{"id":26775,"type":"webpage","language":"it","title":"FAOSTAT statistical database","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY-NC- SA 3.0 IGO. See data management report for the figure at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6453131.
      
      The increase in production is across all the regions except North America. Europe and North America had significant decreases in production in 1995 and 2010 respectively, while Asia had its greatest increase in production in 2010. There is a slight increase in trade of industrial roundwood. In 2019, approximately 144 million m3 and 138 million m3 were imported and exported respectively, while 83 million m3 and 83 million m3 were imported and exported respectively in 1990. Asia is a net importer, importing about 30 million cubic meters higher in 2019 than in 1990. Other regions are net exporters. Europe is the main exporter followed by Oceania. Africa and the Latin America and the Caribbean import and export very minimal quantities of industrial round wood (data from the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations; http://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data).  
      
* Sawnwood
Sawnwood encompasses planks, sleepers (cross-ties), beams, joists, boards, rafters, scantlings, laths, boxboards and “lumber”. There was an increase in sawnwood production from 463 million m3 in 1990 to 488 million m3 in 2019, with the largest increase in Asia (Figure 3.60). There are significant decreases in production between the two points in time happening in 2000 in Asia, 1995 in Europe and 2010 in North America. There is an increase in trade of sawnwood with 149 million m3 and 156 million m3 imported and exported respectively in 2019 as compared to 84 million m3 and 78 million m3 imported and exported respectively in 1990. Asia and Africa are net importer of sawnwood while the rest of the regions are net exporters. Asia is the major importer, importing about 47 million m3 more in 2019 than in 1990, while Europe is the major exporter, exporting 71 million m3 more in 2019 than it exported in 1990 (data from the FAO; http://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data). 


Figure 3.60 Global trends in sawnwood. Data from FAO database ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oBrMNRhl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2017a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2017a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26775,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HEL9AT6T","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4QJ596GD"],"itemData":{"id":26775,"type":"webpage","language":"it","title":"FAOSTAT statistical database","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY-NC- SA 3.0 IGO. See data management report for the figure at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6453131.

* Wood based panels
The wood-based panels’ product category consists of plywood (including blockboard and laminated veneer lumber), particle board, oriented strand board and fibreboard. In 2019, approximately 358 million m3 of wood-based panels were produced globally (Figure 3.61). This is an increase of 234 million m3 from a volume of 124 million m3 reported in 1990. The major producers of wood-based panels are Asia, Europe and North America, with Asia reporting the most tremendous increase of production from 25 million m3 in 1990 to 196 million m3 in 2019. Trade in wood-based products has also increased between 1990 to 2019 from approximately 28 million m3 of imports and exports in 1990 to 88 million m3 of imports and exports in 2019. Europe is the major trader of the product, followed by Asia and North America (data from the FAO; http://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data). 


Figure 3.61 Global trends in wood based panel production. Data from FAO database ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yCu3QA7N","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2017a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2017a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26775,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HEL9AT6T","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4QJ596GD"],"itemData":{"id":26775,"type":"webpage","language":"it","title":"FAOSTAT statistical database","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY-NC- SA 3.0 IGO. See data management report for the figure at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6453131.

* Paper and paper board 
The paper and paperboard product group comprises graphic papers (newsprint, printing and writing paper) and other paper and paperboard. There is an increase in global production of paper and paper boards (Figure 3.62). In 2019, approximately 404 million tons were produced, an increase of 165 million m3 from production volumes of 1990. The major producers and traders of paper and paper boards are Asia, followed by Europe and North America. Production levels of North America have fallen by approximately 11 million tons between 1990 and 2019, while those of Asia and Europe have increased by 138 million tons and 25 million tons respectively within the same time intervals. Trade in paper and paper boards has also increased with 110 million tons and 113 million tons imported and exported respectively. Asia is a net importer while Europe and North America are net exporters (data from the FAO; http://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data).  


Figure 3.62 Global trends in paper and paperboard production. Data from FAO database ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"itfENYFD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2017a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2017a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26775,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HEL9AT6T","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4QJ596GD"],"itemData":{"id":26775,"type":"webpage","language":"it","title":"FAOSTAT statistical database","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY-NC- SA 3.0 IGO. See data management report for the figure at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6453131.

3.3.4.5. Emerging issues in logging and timber management

3.3.4.5.1. Covid-19 Pandemic

The COVID-19 pandemic has led to disruptions in international trade and supply chains of timber and its products globally. Many developing countries are heavily dependent on international trade of these products and the pandemic is having a significant effect on production and consumption patterns. For example, recent developments in the timber markets have increased the dependency on Chinese demand. With the pandemic triggered decline of exported round timber to China, stockpiles of export products are being built up in some places. This is further exacerbated by limited demand in typically strong markets such as Austria and Germany, while export markets in France, Italy and Spain are essentially at a standstill. Together these factors have resulted in a decrease in export incomes in developing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1lqt0ldrtu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020c)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020c)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31448,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZFCSDBR3"],"itemData":{"id":31448,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132532-2","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca8844en","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The impacts of COVID-19 on the forest sector: How to respond?","title-short":"The impacts of COVID-19 on the forest sector","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8844en","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As a result, the least developed timber-producing countries, in particular, may suffer directly from plummeting export volumes of roundwood and other wood products ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nBHIfcsR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020c)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020c)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31448,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZFCSDBR3"],"itemData":{"id":31448,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132532-2","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca8844en","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The impacts of COVID-19 on the forest sector: How to respond?","title-short":"The impacts of COVID-19 on the forest sector","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca8844en","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, in the post-COVID-19 environment, the trade and consumption of legal and sustainable wood products may be promoted through sustainable forest management for wood production, and can play a crucial role in economic recovery, especially considering efforts to promote a circular bioeconomy and climate change mitigation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"akm38fkeh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32344,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9TQ8X64G"],"itemData":{"id":32344,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-133587-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.4060/cb1987en","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impacts of COVID-19 on wood value chains and forest sector response","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/cb1987en","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      For indigenous people and local communities, negative effects of the COVID-19 pandemic on vulnerable communities, including women have been observed. Although, there has been steady progress made to date to empower women by supporting their participation in legal and sustainable fuelwood and charcoal production, the COVID-19 crisis is expected to put increasing pressure on forest resources through illegal charcoal production. Situations where livelihoods are put under significant pressure often tend to result in a shift towards activities with quick economic gains at the sacrifice of legal activities. In some countries, restrictions on travel and movements may affect the transportation and trade of fuelwood (particularly charcoal) from production sites to market centers (mostly urban areas). This may affect reliable access to energy for cooking in urban areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vTodRKIJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32344,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9TQ8X64G"],"itemData":{"id":32344,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-133587-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.4060/cb1987en","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impacts of COVID-19 on wood value chains and forest sector response","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/cb1987en","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The COVID-19 pandemic also set the progress of universal access to electricity and clean cooking back, with the number of people without electricity access forecast to rise by 2% in 2021 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2jruadbeto","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IEA, 2021)","plainCitation":"(IEA, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31444,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3EQR5JPZ"],"itemData":{"id":31444,"type":"report","event-place":"Paris","language":"en","page":"386","publisher":"International Energy Agency","publisher-place":"Paris","source":"Zotero","title":"World Energy Outlook 2021","URL":"https://www.iea.org/reports/world-energy-outlook-2021","author":[{"family":"IEA","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The economic shock of the pandemic also resulted in a return to wood fuels, with many people unable to pay for modern, clean fuels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1mhmmsk7cj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IEA, 2021)","plainCitation":"(IEA, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31444,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3EQR5JPZ"],"itemData":{"id":31444,"type":"report","event-place":"Paris","language":"en","page":"386","publisher":"International Energy Agency","publisher-place":"Paris","source":"Zotero","title":"World Energy Outlook 2021","URL":"https://www.iea.org/reports/world-energy-outlook-2021","author":[{"family":"IEA","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

3.3.5. Non-extractive practices

3.3.5.1. Introduction: Significance of non-extractive practices

Non-extractive practices are widespread across the globe, occur in all ecoregions, and are essential to maintaining inter alia human relaxation, spiritual and cultural identity, connection to nature, belonging, sense of place, physical and psychological health, and inspiration. 
      The contributions of wild species to people from non-extractive practices are often intangible and resist commodity-based valuation (with the exception of recreational tourism). Yet many of the non-extractive contributions from nature are core to the human experience and contribute to the well-being of people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uC93DoYV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, 2005; Russell, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, 2005; Russell, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30336,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WFNSCJUR"],"itemData":{"id":30336,"type":"book","event-place":"Washington DC","ISBN":"1-59726-040-1","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington DC","title":"Ecosystems and Human Well-Being: Synthesis","URL":"http://www.who.int/entity/globalchange/ecosystems/ecosys.pdf","author":[{"literal":"Millennium Ecosystem Assessment"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32723,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EF73QVSJ"],"itemData":{"id":32723,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-012312-110838","note":"publisher: Annual Reviews","page":"473–502","title":"Humans and nature: how knowing and experiencing nature affect well-being","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Russell","given":"Roly"},{"family":"Guerry","given":"Anne D"},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Gould","given":"Rachelle K"},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai MA"},{"family":"Klain","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Levine","given":"Jordan"},{"family":"Tam","given":"Jordan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Knowing and experiencing nature is the foundation of cultural expression and identity; is inherent in the concept of biocultural diversity; forms the backdrop for social connections, religious experience and beauty; as well as contributing substantially to gross domestic product and local livelihoods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xwyH1GZ7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Russell, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Russell, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32723,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EF73QVSJ"],"itemData":{"id":32723,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-012312-110838","note":"publisher: Annual Reviews","page":"473–502","title":"Humans and nature: how knowing and experiencing nature affect well-being","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Russell","given":"Roly"},{"family":"Guerry","given":"Anne D"},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Gould","given":"Rachelle K"},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai MA"},{"family":"Klain","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Levine","given":"Jordan"},{"family":"Tam","given":"Jordan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Although extractive practices are often the focus in the debate on what constitutes sustainable use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NLedmujk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Abensperg-Traun, 2009; Di Minin, 2019; Link & Watson, 2019; Nijman, 2010; Zeller & Pauly, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Abensperg-Traun, 2009; Di Minin, 2019; Link & Watson, 2019; Nijman, 2010; Zeller & Pauly, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34640,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WZBYGGSA"],"itemData":{"id":34640,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2008.12.034","ISSN":"00063207","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"948-963","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"CITES, sustainable use of wild species and incentive-driven conservation in developing countries, with an emphasis on southern Africa","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320709000615","volume":"142","author":[{"family":"Abensperg-Traun","given":"Max"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",5]]}}},{"id":31368,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L3XGLTJ8"],"itemData":{"id":31368,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"\\\\textlessp\\\\textgreaterOverexploitation is one of the main threats to biodiversity, but the intensity of this threat varies geographically. We identified global concentrations, on land and at sea, of 4543 species threatened by unsustainable commercial harvesting. Regions under high-intensity threat (based on accessibility on land and on fishing catch at sea) cover 4.3% of the land and 6.1% of the seas and contain 82% of all species threatened by unsustainable harvesting and \\\\textgreater80% of the ranges of Critically Endangered species threatened by unsustainable harvesting. Currently, only 16% of these regions are covered by protected areas on land and just 6% at sea. Urgent actions are needed in these centers of unsustainable harvesting to ensure that use of species is sustainable and to prevent further species' extinctions.\\\\textless/p\\\\textgreater","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.aau2879","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"4","page":"eaau2879","title":"Identifying global centers of unsustainable commercial harvesting of species","URL":"http://advances.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/sciadv.aau2879","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Di Minin","given":"Enrico"},{"family":"Brooks","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Toivonen","given":"Tuuli"},{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H. M."},{"family":"Heikinheimo","given":"Vuokko"},{"family":"Watson","given":"James E. M."},{"family":"Burgess","given":"Neil D."},{"family":"Challender","given":"Daniel W. S."},{"family":"Goettsch","given":"Bárbara"},{"family":"Jenkins","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Moilanen","given":"Atte"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4]]}}},{"id":31354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2ZB9FTXC"],"itemData":{"id":31354,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The well-documented value of marine fisheries is threatened by overfishing. Management typically focuses on target populations but lacks effective tools to document or restrain overexploitation of marine ecosystems. Here, we present three indices and accompanying thresholds to detect and delineate ecosystem overfishing (EOF): the Fogarty, Friedland, and Ryther indices. These are based on widely available and readily interpreted catch and satellite data that link fisheries landings to primary production using known limits of trophic transfer efficiency. We propose theoretically and empirically based thresholds for each of those indices; with these criteria, several ecosystems are fished sustainably, but nearly 40 to 50% of tropical and temperate ecosystems exceed even extreme thresholds. Applying these criteria to global fisheries data results in strong evidence for two specific instances of EOF, increases in both pressure on tropical fish and a climate-mediated polar shift. Here, we show that these two patterns represent evidence for global EOF.","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.aav0474","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Sci. Adv.","language":"en","page":"eaav0474","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Global ecosystem overfishing: Clear delineation within real limits to production","title-short":"Global ecosystem overfishing","URL":"https://advances.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/sciadv.aav0474","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Link","given":"Jason S."},{"family":"Watson","given":"Reg A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",6]]}}},{"id":32739,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMIG93TC"],"itemData":{"id":32739,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife trade is the very heart of biodiversity conservation and sustainable development providing an income for some of the least economically affluent people and it generates considerable revenue nationally. In Asia the unsustainable trade in wildlife has been identified as one of the main conservation challenges. Internationally, wildlife trade is regulated through the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of wild fauna and flora (CITES) to which all Southeast Asian nations are signatory. I obtained data on international trade in CITES-listed animals in the period 1998-2007. In all \\\\textgreater 35 million animals (0.3 million butterflies; 16.0 million seahorses; 0.1 million other fish; 17.4 million reptiles; 0.4 million mammals; 1.0 million birds) were exported in this period, 30 million (similar to 300 species) of them being wild-caught. In addition 18 million pieces and 2 million kg of live corals were exported. Malaysia, Vietnam, Indonesia and China are the major exporters of wild-caught animals and the European Union and Japan are the most significant importers. Over this period exports in birds significantly decreased, trade in the other taxa either increased or remained stable. For all taxa but butterflies the vast majority of individuals represent wild-caught individuals. Records of illegal or undeclared international trade are scant but can be significantly larger than levels of official exports. It is concluded that there is an urgent need for better assessments of what levels of exploitation are sustainable (including exploring appropriate proxies for Non Detriment Findings), for initiatives to make regulatory mechanisms more effective (including the introduction of minimum mandatory standards and monitoring selected wildlife trade hubs), and for better licensing and registration. Funding for at least some of these initiatives can be obtained by imposing small levies on exports of CITES-listed wildlife.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-009-9758-4","ISSN":"09603115","issue":"4","page":"1101–1114","title":"An overview of international wildlife trade from Southeast Asia","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":33467,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DX8JIP2N"],"itemData":{"id":33467,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            We present a view on global marine fisheries that emphasizes mitigating the conflict between sustainability and the scale of industrial exploitation driven by the demand of continuous economic growth. We then summarize the current state of global fisheries. Finally, we advocate strongly for scaling back industrial fisheries, most of which are non-sustainable. This can be achieved through eliminating the harmful, capacity-enhancing subsidies that prop up industrial fisheries to continue operating despite declining fish stocks. Instead, we propose to support well-managed, locally owned and operated small-scale fisheries, which generally contribute more to local employment and food security. We stress that contrary to deep-seated opinion in the fishing industry and among politicians, reducing overfishing by eliminating overcapacity in fishing fleets will actually lead to greater, not reduced catches. This would address part of the increased global seafood demand over the coming decades, which is driven by population and wealth growth. This seems counterintuitive, but is supported by fisheries science, data and experiences. Thankfully, we are beginning to see that some of these changes are being pursued by a growing number of countries and international institutions.","container-title":"Global Sustainability","DOI":"10.1017/sus.2019.8","ISSN":"2059-4798","journalAbbreviation":"Glob. Sustain.","language":"en","page":"e11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Viewpoint: Back to the future for fisheries, where will we choose to go?","title-short":"Viewpoint","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S2059479819000085/type/journal_article","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, non-extractive practices may also have sustainability implications, both for wild species and for human well-being. Although, non-extractive practices, by its very definition, are viewed as having less of a direct impact on wild species and ecosystems than extractive practices, there are many documented detrimental impacts and sustainability concerns in this practice. This is particularly well-documented for the use of wild species for tourism and recreation (see Section 3.3.5.2.3.). However, many of the adverse impacts may be avoided or mitigated through context-based understanding and collaborative engagement with all stakeholders. 
      Non-extractive benefits from wild species and nature are similar conceptually to the definitions of cultural nature’s contributions to people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QWtRbrsh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Costanza, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Costanza, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33838,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NJJDGUH9"],"itemData":{"id":33838,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Theservices of ecological systemsandthe natural capitalstocksthat producethemarecritical to the functioning ofthe Earth's life-support system. They contribute to human welfare, both directly and indirectly, and therefore represent part of the total economic value of the planet.We have estimated the current economic value of17ecosystem services for 16 biomes, based on published studies and a few original calculations. For the entire biosphere, the value (most of which is outside the market) is estimated to be in the range of US$16–54 trillion (1012) per year, with an average of US$33trillion peryear.Becauseofthe natureofthe uncertainties, thismustbeconsideredaminimumestimate. Global gross national product total is around US$18 trillion per year.","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.ijrobp.2010.07.1349","ISSN":"0360-3016","issue":"May","note":"PMID: 19880551","page":"253–260","title":"The Value of the World's Ecosystem Services and Natural Capital","volume":"387","author":[{"family":"Costanza","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Arge","given":"Ralph"},{"family":"Groot","given":"Rudolf De"},{"family":"Farber","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Hannon","given":"Bruce"},{"family":"Limburg","given":"Karin"},{"family":"Naeem","given":"Shahid"},{"family":"Neill","given":"Robert V O"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and non-material benefits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EsJ5PTqm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30336,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WFNSCJUR"],"itemData":{"id":30336,"type":"book","event-place":"Washington DC","ISBN":"1-59726-040-1","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington DC","title":"Ecosystems and Human Well-Being: Synthesis","URL":"http://www.who.int/entity/globalchange/ecosystems/ecosys.pdf","author":[{"literal":"Millennium Ecosystem Assessment"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In this assessment, non-extractive practices are defined based on the observation of wild species in a way that does not involve the harvest or removal of any part of the organism. The observation can imply some interaction with the wild species, such as the activities of wild species tourism and whale watching or no interaction with the wild species, such as photography (see Chapter 1). 
      Just as in extractive practices, the social contextual heterogeneity in the contributions from wild species to human well-being through their non-extractive use has implications for equitable environmental decision making ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"u9yW45yT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}n-L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Martín-López, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31348,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KUUJZKAJ"],"itemData":{"id":31348,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS one","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0038970","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e38970","title":"Uncovering ecosystem service bundles through social preferences","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Martín-López","given":"Berta"},{"family":"Iniesta-Arandia","given":"Irene"},{"family":"García-Llorente","given":"Marina"},{"family":"Palomo","given":"Ignacio"},{"family":"Casado-Arzuaga","given":"Izaskun"},{"family":"Del Amo","given":"David García"},{"family":"Gómez-Baggethun","given":"Erik"},{"family":"Oteros-Rozas","given":"Elisa"},{"family":"Palacios-Agundez","given":"Igone"},{"family":"Willaarts","given":"Bárbara"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The contributions from wild species to human well-being are perceived and valued differently, which influences the type and extent of use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Eye3Fr5G","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pascual, 2017; Satz, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Pascual, 2017; Satz, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31367,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B4GJWYZ6"],"itemData":{"id":31367,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Nature is perceived and valued in starkly different and often conflicting ways. This paper presents the rationale for the inclusive valuation of nature's contributions to people (NCP) in decision making, as well as broad methodological steps for doing so. While developed within the context of the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES), this approach is more widely applicable to initiatives at the knowledge???policy interface, which require a pluralistic approach to recognizing the diversity of values. We argue that transformative practices aiming at sustainable futures would benefit from embracing such diversity, which require recognizing and addressing power relationships across stakeholder groups that hold different values on human nature-relations and NCP.","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2016.12.006","ISSN":"18773435","note":"PMID: 25811845","page":"7–16","title":"Valuing nature's contributions to people: the IPBES approach","volume":"26-27","author":[{"family":"Pascual","given":"Unai"},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Diaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Pataki","given":"G"},{"family":"Roth","given":"Eva"},{"family":"Stenseke","given":"Marie"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Robert T."},{"family":"Basak Dessane","given":"Esra"},{"family":"Islar","given":"Mine"},{"family":"Kelemen","given":"Eszter"},{"family":"Maris","given":"Virginie"},{"family":"Quaas","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"Suneetha M."},{"family":"Wittmer","given":"Heidi"},{"family":"Adlan","given":"Asia"},{"family":"Ahn","given":"So Eun"},{"family":"Al-Hafedh","given":"Yousef S."},{"family":"Amankwah","given":"Edward"},{"family":"Asah","given":"Stanley T."},{"family":"Berry","given":"Pam"},{"family":"Bilgin","given":"Adem"},{"family":"Breslow","given":"Sara J."},{"family":"Bullock","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Caceres","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Daly-Hassen","given":"Hamed"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Eugenio"},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Gomez-Baggethun","given":"Erik"},{"family":"Gonzalez-Jimenez","given":"David"},{"family":"Houdet","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Keune","given":"Hans"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Ritesh"},{"family":"Ma","given":"Keping"},{"family":"May","given":"Peter H."},{"family":"Mead","given":"Aroha"},{"family":"O'Farrell","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Pandit","given":"Ram"},{"family":"Pengue","given":"Walter"},{"family":"Pichis-Madruga","given":"R"},{"family":"Popa","given":"Florin"},{"family":"Preston","given":"Susan"},{"family":"Pacheco-Balanza","given":"Diego"},{"family":"Saarikoski","given":"Heli"},{"family":"Strassburg","given":"Bernardo B."},{"family":"Belt","given":"Marjan","non-dropping-particle":"van den"},{"family":"Verma","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Wickson","given":"Fern"},{"family":"Yagi","given":"Noboyuki"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33827,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YUBN858K"],"itemData":{"id":33827,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ambio","DOI":"10.1007/s13280-013-0386-6","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"675–684","title":"The challenges of incorporating cultural ecosystem services into environmental assessment","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Satz","given":"Debra"},{"family":"Gould","given":"Rachelle K"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai MA"},{"family":"Guerry","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Norton","given":"Bryan"},{"family":"Satterfield","given":"Terre"},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S"},{"family":"Levine","given":"Jordan"},{"family":"Woodside","given":"Ulalia"},{"family":"Hannahs","given":"Neil"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This also means that there may be conflict between different users of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1ADeAYii","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pascual, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Pascual, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31367,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B4GJWYZ6"],"itemData":{"id":31367,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Nature is perceived and valued in starkly different and often conflicting ways. This paper presents the rationale for the inclusive valuation of nature's contributions to people (NCP) in decision making, as well as broad methodological steps for doing so. While developed within the context of the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES), this approach is more widely applicable to initiatives at the knowledge???policy interface, which require a pluralistic approach to recognizing the diversity of values. We argue that transformative practices aiming at sustainable futures would benefit from embracing such diversity, which require recognizing and addressing power relationships across stakeholder groups that hold different values on human nature-relations and NCP.","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2016.12.006","ISSN":"18773435","note":"PMID: 25811845","page":"7–16","title":"Valuing nature's contributions to people: the IPBES approach","volume":"26-27","author":[{"family":"Pascual","given":"Unai"},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Diaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Pataki","given":"G"},{"family":"Roth","given":"Eva"},{"family":"Stenseke","given":"Marie"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Robert T."},{"family":"Basak Dessane","given":"Esra"},{"family":"Islar","given":"Mine"},{"family":"Kelemen","given":"Eszter"},{"family":"Maris","given":"Virginie"},{"family":"Quaas","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"Suneetha M."},{"family":"Wittmer","given":"Heidi"},{"family":"Adlan","given":"Asia"},{"family":"Ahn","given":"So Eun"},{"family":"Al-Hafedh","given":"Yousef S."},{"family":"Amankwah","given":"Edward"},{"family":"Asah","given":"Stanley T."},{"family":"Berry","given":"Pam"},{"family":"Bilgin","given":"Adem"},{"family":"Breslow","given":"Sara J."},{"family":"Bullock","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Caceres","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Daly-Hassen","given":"Hamed"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Eugenio"},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Gomez-Baggethun","given":"Erik"},{"family":"Gonzalez-Jimenez","given":"David"},{"family":"Houdet","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Keune","given":"Hans"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Ritesh"},{"family":"Ma","given":"Keping"},{"family":"May","given":"Peter H."},{"family":"Mead","given":"Aroha"},{"family":"O'Farrell","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Pandit","given":"Ram"},{"family":"Pengue","given":"Walter"},{"family":"Pichis-Madruga","given":"R"},{"family":"Popa","given":"Florin"},{"family":"Preston","given":"Susan"},{"family":"Pacheco-Balanza","given":"Diego"},{"family":"Saarikoski","given":"Heli"},{"family":"Strassburg","given":"Bernardo B."},{"family":"Belt","given":"Marjan","non-dropping-particle":"van den"},{"family":"Verma","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Wickson","given":"Fern"},{"family":"Yagi","given":"Noboyuki"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, one study documented interpersonal conflicts both within and between two recreational user groups in Hawaii, scuba divers and snorkelers, that held different nature-oriented values, those who valued nature intrinsically and held protectionist beliefs versus an anthropocentric-utilitarian value ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pkk5wXFD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Philips, Szuster, & Needham, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Philips, Szuster, & Needham, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31454,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TT32J8WM"],"itemData":{"id":31454,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Tourism Research","DOI":"10.1002/jtr.2311","ISSN":"1099-2340, 1522-1970","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Int J Tourism Res","language":"en","page":"868-881","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Tourist value orientations and conflicts at a marine protected area in Hawaii","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/jtr.2311","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Philips","given":"Li P."},{"family":"Szuster","given":"Brian W."},{"family":"Needham","given":"Mark D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, local residents near North American ski resorts placed high emphasis on recreational access and came into conflict with city residents who preferred that the area remain pristine wilderness, unaffected by tourism activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eQfUySie","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Saremba & Gill, 1991)","plainCitation":"(Saremba & Gill, 1991)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":487,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/42VCSHLX"],"itemData":{"id":487,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of Tourism Research","DOI":"10.1016/0160-7383(91)90052-D","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"455–472","title":"Value conflicts in mountain park settings","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Saremba","given":"John"},{"family":"Gill","given":"Alison"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. One proposed solution to avoid these types of conflicts is to spatially or temporally partition regions that can cater for different stakeholder’s values (e.g., demarcated fishing and diving zones).
      There can also be a disconnect between the importance placed on non-extractive practices of nature at a local level, where they are used on a daily basis, and the level to which they are incorporated into regional, national and global decisions on ecosystem management, which are made from a more distanced level ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RbbN3Ili","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brondizio, Ostrom, & Young, 2009; S. Chaudhary, McGregor, Houston, & Chettri, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Brondizio, Ostrom, & Young, 2009; S. Chaudhary, McGregor, Houston, & Chettri, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RZMWHIBF"],"itemData":{"id":33833,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual review of environment and resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev.environ.020708.100707","title":"Connectivity and the governance of multilevel social-ecological systems: the role of social capital","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Brondizio","given":"Eduardo S"},{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"},{"family":"Young","given":"Oran R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":33826,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5K74A8FS"],"itemData":{"id":33826,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2019.100972","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"100972","title":"Spiritual enrichment or ecological protection?: A multi-scale analysis of cultural ecosystem services at the Mai Pokhari, a Ramsar site of Nepal","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"Sunita"},{"family":"McGregor","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Houston","given":"Donna"},{"family":"Chettri","given":"Nakul"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thus, governance systems play a large role in which non-extractive contributions from nature are delivered to people, by identifying which stakeholder group’s expectations and values are recognized ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GcDRtXF0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gladkikh, Gould, & Coleman, 2019; Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}n-L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez, 2012; Pert, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Gladkikh, Gould, & Coleman, 2019; Martín-López, 2012; Pert, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31349,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FBGPX9W2"],"itemData":{"id":31349,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2019.101036","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"101036","title":"Cultural ecosystem services and the well-being of refugee communities","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Gladkikh","given":"Tatiana M"},{"family":"Gould","given":"Rachelle K"},{"family":"Coleman","given":"Kimberly J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":31348,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KUUJZKAJ"],"itemData":{"id":31348,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS one","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0038970","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e38970","title":"Uncovering ecosystem service bundles through social preferences","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Martín-López","given":"Berta"},{"family":"Iniesta-Arandia","given":"Irene"},{"family":"García-Llorente","given":"Marina"},{"family":"Palomo","given":"Ignacio"},{"family":"Casado-Arzuaga","given":"Izaskun"},{"family":"Del Amo","given":"David García"},{"family":"Gómez-Baggethun","given":"Erik"},{"family":"Oteros-Rozas","given":"Elisa"},{"family":"Palacios-Agundez","given":"Igone"},{"family":"Willaarts","given":"Bárbara"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":31353,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DZZJ963L"],"itemData":{"id":31353,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2014.10.012","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"41–56","title":"Mapping cultural ecosystem services with rainforest aboriginal peoples: integrating biocultural diversity, governance and social variation","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Pert","given":"Petina L"},{"family":"Hill","given":"Rosemary"},{"family":"Maclean","given":"Kirsten"},{"family":"Dale","given":"Allan"},{"family":"Rist","given":"Phil"},{"family":"Schmider","given":"Joann"},{"family":"Talbot","given":"Leah"},{"family":"Tawake","given":"Lavenie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
3.3.5.2. Uses

Regarding non-extractive practices, the following uses are well-documented in the literature and available data sources: ceremony and cultural expression (section 3.3.5.2.1), medicine and hygiene (section 3.3.5.2.2.), recreation (section 3.3.5.2.3.), education and learning (section 3.3.5.2.4). 
      The documentation of the non-extractive practices of nature, especially the use by indigenous peoples and local communities, often does not include species described at a species level, but frequently as part of a functional group (e.g., trees in urban green spaces; worship of sacred groves). For many indigenous and local communities their worldview and experiences are intimately connected with nature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HvTmGGvA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Klain, Satterfield, & Chan, 2014; Pert, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Klain, Satterfield, & Chan, 2014; Pert, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31347,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZQ9PWI8U"],"itemData":{"id":31347,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2014.09.003","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"310–320","title":"What matters and why? Ecosystem services and their bundled qualities","volume":"107","author":[{"family":"Klain","given":"Sarah C"},{"family":"Satterfield","given":"Terre A"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai MA"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":31353,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DZZJ963L"],"itemData":{"id":31353,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2014.10.012","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"41–56","title":"Mapping cultural ecosystem services with rainforest aboriginal peoples: integrating biocultural diversity, governance and social variation","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Pert","given":"Petina L"},{"family":"Hill","given":"Rosemary"},{"family":"Maclean","given":"Kirsten"},{"family":"Dale","given":"Allan"},{"family":"Rist","given":"Phil"},{"family":"Schmider","given":"Joann"},{"family":"Talbot","given":"Leah"},{"family":"Tawake","given":"Lavenie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indigenous and local knowledge is premised on the interdependence of what scientific knowledge may identify as distinct components of nature and culture, such that worship of sacred groves is a holistic practice that includes the species in the grove (e.g., forest plant and animal species community), the ecosystem processes (e.g., primary production, pollination), the landscape features (e.g., rocks, rivers), and the particular cultural practices and language of the human community. However, in order to keep the scope of this section pragmatic and practical, literature that deals with quantifiable and measurable use of wild species up to the taxa level (e.g., trees) has been included in this review on non-extractive practices and literature on landscapes and landscape components (e.g., sacred pools, sacred mountains) was excluded. 
      The following uses are not relevant to this practice and/or were not documented: decorative and aesthetic, energy, food and feed, materials and shelter. Although aesthetic beauty and inspiration of nature are a form of non-extractive practice, this was excluded to maintain the scope of the assessment to quantifiable and measurable impacts of sustainable use. Keyword searches and methods for each review are detailed in each subsection.
      
3.3.5.2.1. Ceremony and cultural expression

Ceremony and cultural expression refer to the use of wild species in spiritual observances and practices, valued for their role in maintaining cultural identity (Chapter 1). In the context of non-extractive practices, the use of wild species can be through worship of religious or culturally important species. In urban areas, similarly urban green spaces have become important analogues for worship and ceremonial rituals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QQGuRu8q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ngulani & Shackleton, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Ngulani & Shackleton, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31352,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ARK5CWL2"],"itemData":{"id":31352,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2018.11.009","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"97–104","title":"Use of public urban green spaces for spiritual services in Bulawayo, Zimbabwe","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Ngulani","given":"T"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"CM"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thus, wild species can underpin cultural and religious identity by supporting spiritual, intellectual and emotional features and contribute to literature, lifestyles, value systems, traditions and beliefs, and ways of living together. The use of wild species for ceremony and cultural expression supports social cohesion, belonging and identity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0KaPCpTa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Satz, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Satz, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33827,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YUBN858K"],"itemData":{"id":33827,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ambio","DOI":"10.1007/s13280-013-0386-6","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"675–684","title":"The challenges of incorporating cultural ecosystem services into environmental assessment","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Satz","given":"Debra"},{"family":"Gould","given":"Rachelle K"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai MA"},{"family":"Guerry","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Norton","given":"Bryan"},{"family":"Satterfield","given":"Terre"},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S"},{"family":"Levine","given":"Jordan"},{"family":"Woodside","given":"Ulalia"},{"family":"Hannahs","given":"Neil"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wild species form part of history and cultural narratives ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"V8aRGOyM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pascual, 2017; Satz, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Pascual, 2017; Satz, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31367,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B4GJWYZ6"],"itemData":{"id":31367,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Nature is perceived and valued in starkly different and often conflicting ways. This paper presents the rationale for the inclusive valuation of nature's contributions to people (NCP) in decision making, as well as broad methodological steps for doing so. While developed within the context of the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES), this approach is more widely applicable to initiatives at the knowledge???policy interface, which require a pluralistic approach to recognizing the diversity of values. We argue that transformative practices aiming at sustainable futures would benefit from embracing such diversity, which require recognizing and addressing power relationships across stakeholder groups that hold different values on human nature-relations and NCP.","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2016.12.006","ISSN":"18773435","note":"PMID: 25811845","page":"7–16","title":"Valuing nature's contributions to people: the IPBES approach","volume":"26-27","author":[{"family":"Pascual","given":"Unai"},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Diaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Pataki","given":"G"},{"family":"Roth","given":"Eva"},{"family":"Stenseke","given":"Marie"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Robert T."},{"family":"Basak Dessane","given":"Esra"},{"family":"Islar","given":"Mine"},{"family":"Kelemen","given":"Eszter"},{"family":"Maris","given":"Virginie"},{"family":"Quaas","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"Suneetha M."},{"family":"Wittmer","given":"Heidi"},{"family":"Adlan","given":"Asia"},{"family":"Ahn","given":"So Eun"},{"family":"Al-Hafedh","given":"Yousef S."},{"family":"Amankwah","given":"Edward"},{"family":"Asah","given":"Stanley T."},{"family":"Berry","given":"Pam"},{"family":"Bilgin","given":"Adem"},{"family":"Breslow","given":"Sara J."},{"family":"Bullock","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Caceres","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Daly-Hassen","given":"Hamed"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Eugenio"},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Gomez-Baggethun","given":"Erik"},{"family":"Gonzalez-Jimenez","given":"David"},{"family":"Houdet","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Keune","given":"Hans"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Ritesh"},{"family":"Ma","given":"Keping"},{"family":"May","given":"Peter H."},{"family":"Mead","given":"Aroha"},{"family":"O'Farrell","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Pandit","given":"Ram"},{"family":"Pengue","given":"Walter"},{"family":"Pichis-Madruga","given":"R"},{"family":"Popa","given":"Florin"},{"family":"Preston","given":"Susan"},{"family":"Pacheco-Balanza","given":"Diego"},{"family":"Saarikoski","given":"Heli"},{"family":"Strassburg","given":"Bernardo B."},{"family":"Belt","given":"Marjan","non-dropping-particle":"van den"},{"family":"Verma","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Wickson","given":"Fern"},{"family":"Yagi","given":"Noboyuki"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33827,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YUBN858K"],"itemData":{"id":33827,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ambio","DOI":"10.1007/s13280-013-0386-6","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"675–684","title":"The challenges of incorporating cultural ecosystem services into environmental assessment","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Satz","given":"Debra"},{"family":"Gould","given":"Rachelle K"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai MA"},{"family":"Guerry","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Norton","given":"Bryan"},{"family":"Satterfield","given":"Terre"},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S"},{"family":"Levine","given":"Jordan"},{"family":"Woodside","given":"Ulalia"},{"family":"Hannahs","given":"Neil"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thus, unsustainable use of wild species central to cultural and ceremonial engagement can harm social relations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Q31UZBr3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30336,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WFNSCJUR"],"itemData":{"id":30336,"type":"book","event-place":"Washington DC","ISBN":"1-59726-040-1","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington DC","title":"Ecosystems and Human Well-Being: Synthesis","URL":"http://www.who.int/entity/globalchange/ecosystems/ecosys.pdf","author":[{"literal":"Millennium Ecosystem Assessment"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Inversely, restoration of degraded forests and landscapes provides an opportunity for cultural and indigenous value revival ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JKmEMQGG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Constant & Taylor, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Constant & Taylor, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31496,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H4XM2UPV"],"itemData":{"id":31496,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2020.102222","ISSN":"13899341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"102222","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Restoring the forest revives our culture: Ecosystem services and values for ecological restoration across the rural-urban nexus in South Africa","title-short":"Restoring the forest revives our culture","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1389934119305271","volume":"118","author":[{"family":"Constant","given":"Natasha Louise"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Peter John"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The text below is based on a literature review (Web of Science) using the following strings of terms ("non-extractive use*" OR "cultural ecosystem service*" OR "non-material contribution*" OR “non-consumptive use”) AND (spiritual* OR ceremon* OR religion* OR ritual*), generating 51 hits (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Articles that were recommended by citation databases were also considered, as well as harvested from personal libraries and recommendations from experts. The scope of this section is limited to the non-extractive practices of wild species for ceremonial and cultural expression where the impact of the use can be measured or assessed. After reviewing the title and abstract, 36 papers were selected for a full-text read. Relevance was determined by mention of either the status (current), trend (historical), or impacts of use of wild species (or taxa) in at least one dimension of sustainability (social, economic, or ecological). After a full text read of these papers, eight were deemed relevant and assessed for the literature review. These data form the basis of the text below.  
      Of the reviewed articles, half (4 out of 8) covered the importance of trees for ceremonial use, particularly sacred groves in Africa. Research on sacred groves was mostly anthropological and social data of long-term (>10 years) trends on a regional (<100 km) scale. 
      Sacred natural sites, such as sacred groves and burial sites, are an important feature across the world that can play a central role in biodiversity and resource conservation. These sites exhibit large diversity in their form and function, showing strong local context of both ecology and culture ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Q4sbxe8O","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011; Juh\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}-Beaulaton & Salpeteur, 2017)","plainCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011; Juhé-Beaulaton & Salpeteur, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33825,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GFIJURLB"],"itemData":{"id":33825,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-011-0065-5","issue":"9","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1895–1910","title":"Consequences of wooded shrine rituals on vegetation conservation in West Africa: a case study from the Bwaba cultural area (West Burkina Faso)","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Fournier","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":"ERpTB65M/Se9tCzSz","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JTPDDELI"],"itemData":{"id":10023,"type":"book","publisher":"Routledge Research in Landscape and Environmental Design, Taylor & Francis Ltd","title":"“Sacred groves” in African contexts (Benin, Cameroon): insights from history and anthropology","author":[{"family":"Juhé-Beaulaton","given":"Dominique"},{"family":"Salpeteur","given":"Matthieu"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Sacred groves are places of spiritual and cultural importance, protected by the authority of tribal taboos and spiritual “caretakers”. In general, restrictions forbid cutting down or harvesting any part of the trees, including dead wood, to burn or harm the fauna and flora, or to remove soil or stones ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UOTEUnPn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011)","plainCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33825,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GFIJURLB"],"itemData":{"id":33825,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-011-0065-5","issue":"9","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1895–1910","title":"Consequences of wooded shrine rituals on vegetation conservation in West Africa: a case study from the Bwaba cultural area (West Burkina Faso)","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Fournier","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Depending on the tribal custom, picking herbaceous plants and grazing livestock near the sacred trees may be permitted ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Pw1MRs6B","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011)","plainCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33825,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GFIJURLB"],"itemData":{"id":33825,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-011-0065-5","issue":"9","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1895–1910","title":"Consequences of wooded shrine rituals on vegetation conservation in West Africa: a case study from the Bwaba cultural area (West Burkina Faso)","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Fournier","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Taboos also vary depending on the type of sacred grove. For example, the Tandroy clan in Madagascar allows the use of fire in honey groves - kept for medicinal, food, and spiritual purposes – but has more stringent taboos in burial forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cBaIbbl0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(von Heland & Folke, 2014)","plainCitation":"(von Heland & Folke, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33828,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9C23Q9AF"],"itemData":{"id":33828,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2013.11.003","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"251–264","title":"A social contract with the ancestors—Culture and ecosystem services in southern Madagascar","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Heland","given":"Jacob","non-dropping-particle":"von"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The current status of sacred groves, or indeed of any wild species used for ceremonial and cultural purposes, is not well documented in the literature. But the limited data that do exist are mixed, with some evidence that taboos and traditional beliefs have protected sacred groves. Sacred groves can an important role in community-based conservation of biodiversity, acting as refugia for species. For example, India possesses relict populations of certain threatened tree species (Actinodaphne lawsonii, Hopea ponga, Madhuca neriifoli, and Syzygium zeylanicum, Myristica fatua and Gymnacranthera canarica) in numerous riparian groves. Sacred groves in the Karnataka state also shelter a high diversity of macrofungi, 49 out of 163 species are unique to sacred groves ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"S1CJKjtC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bhagwat & Rutte, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Bhagwat & Rutte, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34431,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G4HHBP6H"],"itemData":{"id":34431,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","DOI":"10.1890/1540-9295(2006)4[519:SGPFBM]2.0.CO;2","issue":"10","page":"519-524","title":"Sacred groves: potential for biodiversity management Journal Item","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Bhagwat","given":"Shonil A"},{"family":"Rutte","given":"Claudia"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, in central Tanzania, greater woody plant species richness was found in sacred groves than in a state-managed forest reserve ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"I5dE2lqJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mgumia & Oba, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Mgumia & Oba, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34705,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/THNGFBXU"],"itemData":{"id":34705,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892903000250","ISSN":"201809:33:55","issue":"3","page":"259-265","title":"Potential role of sacred groves in biodiversity conservation in Tanzania","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/pdf/44520689.pdf?refreqid=excelsior%3A59d5b9001526c3885181d58114928c53","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Mgumia","given":"F H"},{"family":"Oba","given":"G"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Despite droughts and pressure to use resources inside sacred forests, the ancestral forests in Ambonaivo have been preserved whereas elsewhere in Madagascar, sacred groves have been cut down for charcoal production ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9gVhxjku","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(von Heland & Folke, 2014)","plainCitation":"(von Heland & Folke, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33828,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9C23Q9AF"],"itemData":{"id":33828,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2013.11.003","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"251–264","title":"A social contract with the ancestors—Culture and ecosystem services in southern Madagascar","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Heland","given":"Jacob","non-dropping-particle":"von"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, sacred groves in Morocco have been effectively conserved as a result of their sacred status ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"omupLQAN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Frosch & Deil, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Frosch & Deil, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"ERpTB65M/JMzY1DQY","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QLHLS3D2"],"itemData":{"id":14577,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Phytocoenologia","DOI":"10.1127/0340-269X/2011/0041-0503","ISSN":"0340-269X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"phyto","language":"en","page":"153-181","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forest vegetation on sacred sites of the Tangier Peninsula (NW Morocco) – discussed in a SW-Mediterranean context","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Frosch","given":"Birgit"},{"family":"Deil","given":"Ulrich"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Despite their significance, the protection offered to wooded shrines may be limited in extent and may only be for a certain period of time ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RqBZieVW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011)","plainCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33825,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GFIJURLB"],"itemData":{"id":33825,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-011-0065-5","issue":"9","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1895–1910","title":"Consequences of wooded shrine rituals on vegetation conservation in West Africa: a case study from the Bwaba cultural area (West Burkina Faso)","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Fournier","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Burkino Faso, the clearing of wooded shrines has also been blamed on “foreigners” fleeing worsening climatic conditions in the Sahel, who are (either intentionally or not) ignorant of local traditions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"O1eX9dCl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011)","plainCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33825,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GFIJURLB"],"itemData":{"id":33825,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-011-0065-5","issue":"9","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1895–1910","title":"Consequences of wooded shrine rituals on vegetation conservation in West Africa: a case study from the Bwaba cultural area (West Burkina Faso)","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Fournier","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Benin most sacred groves have been neglected or cut down, but a few have been restored and are being managed for nature’s contributions to people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mVpimDAM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Juh\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}-Beaulaton & Salpeteur, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Juhé-Beaulaton & Salpeteur, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"ERpTB65M/Se9tCzSz","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JTPDDELI"],"itemData":{"id":10023,"type":"book","publisher":"Routledge Research in Landscape and Environmental Design, Taylor & Francis Ltd","title":"“Sacred groves” in African contexts (Benin, Cameroon): insights from history and anthropology","author":[{"family":"Juhé-Beaulaton","given":"Dominique"},{"family":"Salpeteur","given":"Matthieu"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A vegetation assessment of wooded shrines in West Africa found more groves were cut down than restored and although they were less used for extractive purposes than similar secular forests, they were still being used for extractive purposes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ofnlEkTh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011)","plainCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33825,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GFIJURLB"],"itemData":{"id":33825,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-011-0065-5","issue":"9","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1895–1910","title":"Consequences of wooded shrine rituals on vegetation conservation in West Africa: a case study from the Bwaba cultural area (West Burkina Faso)","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Fournier","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Sacred groves are also not necessarily ecologically ‘pristine’ by conservation standards. Whilst the preference by locals is for sacred groves to “have trees”, preferably dense vegetation, the species of tree is considered unimportant and wooded shrines range from almost natural to highly modified ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"i7R2bzwl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011)","plainCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33825,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GFIJURLB"],"itemData":{"id":33825,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-011-0065-5","issue":"9","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1895–1910","title":"Consequences of wooded shrine rituals on vegetation conservation in West Africa: a case study from the Bwaba cultural area (West Burkina Faso)","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Fournier","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The literature suggests the future of sacred groves is strongly dependent on how spiritual and religious practices adapt to changing socio-political conditions. The degrading social contract with nature and the erosion of ancestral and natural connections threatens the sustainability of sacred groves. Cultural trends show taboos around sacred groves are eroding as the elder “spiritual caretakers” who play an active role in supervising use of the groves, are not replaced ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZsZO2ImG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011; von Heland & Folke, 2014)","plainCitation":"(A. Fournier, 2011; von Heland & Folke, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33825,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GFIJURLB"],"itemData":{"id":33825,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-011-0065-5","issue":"9","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1895–1910","title":"Consequences of wooded shrine rituals on vegetation conservation in West Africa: a case study from the Bwaba cultural area (West Burkina Faso)","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Fournier","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33828,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9C23Q9AF"],"itemData":{"id":33828,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2013.11.003","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"251–264","title":"A social contract with the ancestors—Culture and ecosystem services in southern Madagascar","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Heland","given":"Jacob","non-dropping-particle":"von"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are also changes to “social-ancestor contracts” which are being modernized, and more of the local people have converted to global religions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qsLVXYvO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Juh\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}-Beaulaton & Salpeteur, 2017; von Heland & Folke, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Juhé-Beaulaton & Salpeteur, 2017; von Heland & Folke, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"ERpTB65M/Se9tCzSz","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JTPDDELI"],"itemData":{"id":10023,"type":"book","publisher":"Routledge Research in Landscape and Environmental Design, Taylor & Francis Ltd","title":"“Sacred groves” in African contexts (Benin, Cameroon): insights from history and anthropology","author":[{"family":"Juhé-Beaulaton","given":"Dominique"},{"family":"Salpeteur","given":"Matthieu"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33828,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9C23Q9AF"],"itemData":{"id":33828,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2013.11.003","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"251–264","title":"A social contract with the ancestors—Culture and ecosystem services in southern Madagascar","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Heland","given":"Jacob","non-dropping-particle":"von"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The increasing assimilation of local peoples’ moral order into one more closely aligned with modern, Western, democratic ideals governed by the nation-state has eroded the traditional and ancestral social-ecological system central to their identity, as well as the associated protection afforded to their land and the species it contains ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ph2CLabC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Findlay & Twine, 2018; von Heland & Folke, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Findlay & Twine, 2018; von Heland & Folke, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"ERpTB65M/30Alku3o","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A7Z5TWGK"],"itemData":{"id":9345,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land","issue":"1","page":"35","title":"Chiefs in a democracy: A case study of the ‘new’systems of regulating firewood harvesting in post-apartheid South Africa","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Findlay","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Twine","given":"Wayne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":33828,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9C23Q9AF"],"itemData":{"id":33828,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2013.11.003","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"251–264","title":"A social contract with the ancestors—Culture and ecosystem services in southern Madagascar","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Heland","given":"Jacob","non-dropping-particle":"von"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As local protection erodes for sacred sites, there is an opportunity for more formal protection, such as the promising case developing in Estonia where Estonian indigenous peoples and local communities (Maausk) and the government are planning to confer legal protection to approximately 550 sacred groves ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CMy8eL1V","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kaasik, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Kaasik, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":496,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KS4837VR"],"itemData":{"id":496,"type":"chapter","call-number":"BV896.E85 D45 2010","container-title":"The diversity of sacred lands in Europe: proceedings of the Third Workshop of the Delos Initiative, Inari/Aanaar, Finland, 1-3 July 2010","event-place":"Gland, Switzerland","ISBN":"978-2-8317-1423-3","note":"OCLC: ocn793221858","publisher":"IUCN","publisher-place":"Gland, Switzerland","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Conserving sacred natural sites in Estonia","editor":[{"family":"Mallarach i Carrera","given":"Josep Maria"},{"family":"Papagiann?s","given":"Thymios"},{"family":"Väisänen","given":"Rauno"}],"author":[{"family":"Kaasik","given":"Ahto"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are also opportunities to conserve sacred groves for purposes other than cultural worship provided the other uses are compatible and respectful of the sacred status. This has occurred in West Africa where sacred groves are also used for heritage and cultural tourism ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fftRM0wN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Juh\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}-Beaulaton & Salpeteur, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Juhé-Beaulaton & Salpeteur, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"ERpTB65M/Se9tCzSz","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JTPDDELI"],"itemData":{"id":10023,"type":"book","publisher":"Routledge Research in Landscape and Environmental Design, Taylor & Francis Ltd","title":"“Sacred groves” in African contexts (Benin, Cameroon): insights from history and anthropology","author":[{"family":"Juhé-Beaulaton","given":"Dominique"},{"family":"Salpeteur","given":"Matthieu"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The literature review on the use of wild species for ceremonial and cultural expression also described the use of urban green spaces for religious worship. However, increasing urbanization threatens the loss of green spaces used for worship, especially as these sacred sites are not associated with formal religious structures or buildings ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zf3CnJoA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(W. Jackson & Ormsby, 2017)","plainCitation":"(W. Jackson & Ormsby, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31350,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9SHVQ4JP"],"itemData":{"id":31350,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1007/s11252-016-0623-4","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"675–681","title":"Urban sacred natural sites–a call for research","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Jackson","given":"Wendy"},{"family":"Ormsby","given":"Alison"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Use of urban green space for ceremonial purposes has been documented in Zimbabwe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UOZKdWqQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ngulani & Shackleton, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Ngulani & Shackleton, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31352,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ARK5CWL2"],"itemData":{"id":31352,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2018.11.009","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"97–104","title":"Use of public urban green spaces for spiritual services in Bulawayo, Zimbabwe","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Ngulani","given":"T"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"CM"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Accra ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oePTF8ig","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Okyerefo & Fiaveh, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Okyerefo & Fiaveh, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31351,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NVTI72A9"],"itemData":{"id":31351,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Health Sociology Review","DOI":"10.1080/14461242.2016.1257360","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"308–320","title":"Prayer and health-seeking beliefs in Ghana: understanding the ‘religious space’of the urban forest","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Okyerefo","given":"Michael Perry Kweku"},{"family":"Fiaveh","given":"Daniel Yaw"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and India ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GEqeAPzh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gopal, von der Lippe, & Kowarik, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Gopal, von der Lippe, & Kowarik, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31346,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8I8XAAMI"],"itemData":{"id":31346,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban ecosystems","DOI":"10.1007/s11252-018-0804-4","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"161–172","title":"Sacred sites, biodiversity and urbanization in an Indian megacity","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Gopal","given":"Divya"},{"family":"Lippe","given":"Moritz","non-dropping-particle":"von der"},{"family":"Kowarik","given":"Ingo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but it is an underreported form of use of either formal or informal urban green spaces and has not received adequate research or policy attention ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bg2EyM14","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(W. Jackson & Ormsby, 2017; Ngulani & Shackleton, 2019)","plainCitation":"(W. Jackson & Ormsby, 2017; Ngulani & Shackleton, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31350,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9SHVQ4JP"],"itemData":{"id":31350,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1007/s11252-016-0623-4","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"675–681","title":"Urban sacred natural sites–a call for research","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Jackson","given":"Wendy"},{"family":"Ormsby","given":"Alison"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":31352,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ARK5CWL2"],"itemData":{"id":31352,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2018.11.009","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"97–104","title":"Use of public urban green spaces for spiritual services in Bulawayo, Zimbabwe","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Ngulani","given":"T"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"CM"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. No information on the use of urban green spaces for worship described whether this was an increasing phenomenon, or the sustainability of this use.
      Overall, the use of wild species for ceremonial and cultural purposes is likely widespread but poorly documented. There are little to no data on the status and trends, or sustainability of this use. However, the literature on sacred groves do suggest that cultural erosion is driving a decreasing trend of ceremonial use, and thus also an erosion of traditional protection that the use afforded these species.
      
3.3.5.2.2. Medicine and hygiene

This section relates to the non-extractive practices of wild species for human health, both psychological and physical. The scope of this section is limited to the non-extractive practice of wild species for restorative and/or preventative effects and the impact this use has in terms of a measurable effect on the species. The text below is based on a literature review (Google Scholar) including the following search terms: ("non-extractive use*" OR "cultural ecosystem service*" OR "non-material contribution*" OR “non-consumptive use”) AND (sustainab* OR "forest therapy" OR "human well-being" OR "human health" OR stress OR happiness OR dose-response) generating over 1 million hits (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). Articles that were recommended by citation databases were also considered, as well as collected from personal libraries and recommendations by experts After a title and abstract read, 24 papers were selected for a full-text read. After a full text read of these papers, 13 were deemed relevant and assessed for the literature review. Relevance was determined by mention of either the status (current), trend (historical) or impacts of use of wild species (or taxa) in at least one dimension of sustainability (social, economic, or ecological). These data form the basis of the text below.
      Relevant material from the review covered mostly the use of trees (10 papers) for health purposes, with a few mentions of terrestrial mammals and birds (4 papers). 46% of the studies on this topic were global overviews, with regional studies mostly representing Asia-Pacific and Europe Central. Relevant research was overwhelmingly short-term studies (<1 year), but spanned a variety of spatial scales: global, national, regional and local. 
      There was an absence of information in the literature on trends in the non-extractive practices of wild species for health and hygiene. From the review, only one paper tracked trends in use for health over time. This was a paper that documented current and past trends in the use of forests for forest therapy in Korea ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NRd6gZRy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shin, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Shin, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":483,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4XJXLJX3"],"itemData":{"id":483,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Net. J. Agric. Sci","page":"53–57","title":"Paradigm shift on forest utilization: Forest service for health promotion in the Republic of Korea","URL":"http://www.netjournals.org/pdf/NJAS/2017/2/17-018.pdf","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Shin","given":"Won Sop"},{"family":"Kim","given":"Jae-Jun"},{"family":"Lim","given":"Sang Seop"},{"family":"Yoo","given":"Rhee-Hwa"},{"family":"Jeong","given":"Mi-Ae"},{"family":"Lee","given":"Jeonghee"},{"family":"Park","given":"Sujin"},{"literal":"others"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, no information was found reporting on the sustainability of health-based use of wild species on species or ecosystems. Although undocumented, negative impacts on wild species used for medicine and health likely include the effects of trampling during nature visits (see section 3.3.5.2.3. Recreational). It is possible that the health benefits obtained from wild species motivate people to support and protect their natural spaces. Research on environmental education supports that the more people learn from (and in) nature, the more likely they are to develop pro-nature behavior ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EW7qAtKT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Richardson, Cormack, McRobert, & Underhill, 2016)","plainCitation":"(M. Richardson, Cormack, McRobert, & Underhill, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31344,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LK8SF2J5"],"itemData":{"id":31344,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PloS one","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0149777","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science San Francisco, CA USA","page":"e0149777","title":"30 days wild: Development and evaluation of a large-scale nature engagement campaign to improve well-being","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Richardson","given":"Miles"},{"family":"Cormack","given":"Adam"},{"family":"McRobert","given":"Lucy"},{"family":"Underhill","given":"Ralph"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. But this has not yet been documented for the non-extractive practices of wild species for medicine and hygiene use. It is not a given that the benefits provided by wild species always confer protection. For instance, street tree vandalism is a significant driver of urban tree mortality ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Sm0Y8u4Q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Richardson & Shackleton, 2014)","plainCitation":"(E. Richardson & Shackleton, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31470,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HQE8VIBK"],"itemData":{"id":31470,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2014.04.003","ISSN":"16188667","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"425-432","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The extent and perceptions of vandalism as a cause of street tree damage in small towns in the Eastern Cape, South Africa","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1618866714000375","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Richardson","given":"Emma"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} despite the numerous benefits provided by urban greening.
      The literature on this topic extensively deals with the beneficial impacts of nature, especially forests, on individual human well-being. A significant research gap exists on the impacts of health-based use of wild species on human community health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zjhq4rC9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nesbitt, Hotte, Barron, Cowan, & Sheppard, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Nesbitt, Hotte, Barron, Cowan, & Sheppard, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31462,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M5VGC7AJ"],"itemData":{"id":31462,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2017.05.005","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"103-111","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The social and economic value of cultural ecosystem services provided by urban forests in North America: A review and suggestions for future research","title-short":"The social and economic value of cultural ecosystem services provided by urban forests in North America","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1618866717300456","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Nesbitt","given":"Lorien"},{"family":"Hotte","given":"Ngaio"},{"family":"Barron","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Cowan","given":"Judith"},{"family":"Sheppard","given":"Stephen R.J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The rest of this section will describe the evidence and examples of the impacts of health use of wild species on human individual’s well-being.
      Rapid urbanization and industrialization have been related to the rise in chronic mental and physical health problems, mostly associated with stress ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"I6HMS5j6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ashworth, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Ashworth, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31322,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y5SBHWLU"],"itemData":{"id":31322,"type":"webpage","container-title":"PsychCentral","title":"How does stress affect us?","URL":"https://psychcentral.com/lib/how-does-stress-affect-us#1","author":[{"family":"Ashworth","given":"Michael"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, costing millions in healthcare-related expenses and lost work days ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zuIKm1a3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Moore, Gould, & Keary, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Moore, Gould, & Keary, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31463,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IC5FBYHA"],"itemData":{"id":31463,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Hygiene and Environmental Health","DOI":"10.1078/1438-4639-00223","ISSN":"14384639","issue":"4-5","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Hygiene and Environmental Health","language":"en","page":"269-278","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Global urbanization and impact on health","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1438463904702230","volume":"206","author":[{"family":"Moore","given":"Melinda"},{"family":"Gould","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Keary","given":"Barbara S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thus, preventive measures, including nature-based remedies, to deal with the modern-day health crisis are economically prudent, and are supported formally by some governments, such as the legislation passed by the Korean government for the use of forests for health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rTA5gfgy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kotte, Li, & Shin, 2019; Shin, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Kotte, Li, & Shin, 2019; Shin, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31469,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XNDEBZ4S"],"itemData":{"id":31469,"type":"book","abstract":"The first International Handbook of Forest Therapy defines the scientific domain of this innovative, evidence-based and timely public health approach. More than 50 authors from around the world are brought together to offer their expertise and insights about forest therapy from a variety of research perspectives. The theoretical discussion of the effects related to the biophilia hypothesis presented here is complemented by research results compiled across the last three decades in the fields of forest medicine and biochemistry from Asia. The book also highlights the latest developments with re.","event-place":"Newcastle-upon-Tyne","ISBN":"978-1-5275-4174-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1125112771","publisher":"Cambridge Scholars Publisher","publisher-place":"Newcastle-upon-Tyne","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"International Handbook of Forest Therapy","URL":"https://public.ebookcentral.proquest.com/choice/publicfullrecord.aspx?p=5962801","author":[{"family":"Kotte","given":"Dieter"},{"family":"Li","given":"Qing"},{"family":"Shin","given":"Won Sop"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":483,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4XJXLJX3"],"itemData":{"id":483,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Net. J. Agric. Sci","page":"53–57","title":"Paradigm shift on forest utilization: Forest service for health promotion in the Republic of Korea","URL":"http://www.netjournals.org/pdf/NJAS/2017/2/17-018.pdf","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"S