      Reviews on the effects of forest therapy on human health found that most research reported positive effects ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HFyRHeq1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Frumkin, 2017; Kotte, 2019; Rajoo, 2020; K. L. Wolf, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Frumkin, 2017; Kotte, 2019; Rajoo, 2020; K. L. Wolf, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31464,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WQ8ERAHC"],"itemData":{"id":31464,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Health Perspectives","DOI":"10.1289/EHP1663","ISSN":"0091-6765, 1552-9924","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Environ Health Perspect","language":"en","page":"075001","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Nature Contact and Human Health: A Research Agenda","title-short":"Nature Contact and Human Health","URL":"https://ehp.niehs.nih.gov/doi/10.1289/EHP1663","volume":"125","author":[{"family":"Frumkin","given":"Howard"},{"family":"Bratman","given":"Gregory N."},{"family":"Breslow","given":"Sara Jo"},{"family":"Cochran","given":"Bobby"},{"family":"Kahn Jr","given":"Peter H."},{"family":"Lawler","given":"Joshua J."},{"family":"Levin","given":"Phillip S."},{"family":"Tandon","given":"Pooja S."},{"family":"Varanasi","given":"Usha"},{"family":"Wolf","given":"Kathleen L."},{"family":"Wood","given":"Spencer A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",7,24]]}}},{"id":31469,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XNDEBZ4S"],"itemData":{"id":31469,"type":"book","abstract":"The first International Handbook of Forest Therapy defines the scientific domain of this innovative, evidence-based and timely public health approach. More than 50 authors from around the world are brought together to offer their expertise and insights about forest therapy from a variety of research perspectives. The theoretical discussion of the effects related to the biophilia hypothesis presented here is complemented by research results compiled across the last three decades in the fields of forest medicine and biochemistry from Asia. The book also highlights the latest developments with re.","event-place":"Newcastle-upon-Tyne","ISBN":"978-1-5275-4174-0","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1125112771","publisher":"Cambridge Scholars Publisher","publisher-place":"Newcastle-upon-Tyne","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"International Handbook of Forest Therapy","URL":"https://public.ebookcentral.proquest.com/choice/publicfullrecord.aspx?p=5962801","author":[{"family":"Kotte","given":"Dieter"},{"family":"Li","given":"Qing"},{"family":"Shin","given":"Won Sop"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":31461,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3NKRR7PR"],"itemData":{"id":31461,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2020.126744","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126744","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The physiological and psychosocial effects of forest therapy: A systematic review","title-short":"The physiological and psychosocial effects of forest therapy","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1618866720304039","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Rajoo","given":"Keeren Sundara"},{"family":"Karam","given":"Daljit Singh"},{"family":"Abdullah","given":"Mohd Zaki"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}},{"id":31360,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y5MY6YPY"],"itemData":{"id":31360,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The urban forest is a green infrastructure system that delivers multiple environmental, economic, social and health services, and functions in cities. Environmental benefits of urban trees are well understood, but no review to date has examined how urban trees affect human health. This review provides a comprehensive summary of existing literature on the health impacts of urban trees that can inform future research, policy, and nature-based public health interventions. A systematic search used keywords representing human health, environmental health, and urban forestry. Following screening and appraisal of several thousand articles, 201 studies were conceptually sorted into a three-part framework. Reducing Harm, representing 41% of studies, includes topics such as air pollution, ultraviolet radiation, heat exposure, and pollen. Restoring Capacities, at 31%, includes attention restoration, mental health, stress reduction, and clinical outcomes. Building Capacities, at 28%, includes topics such as birth outcomes, active living, and weight status. The studies that were reviewed show substantial heterogeneity in purpose and method yet indicate important health outcomes associated with people’s exposure to trees. This review will help inform future research and practice, and demonstrates why urban forest planning and management should strategically promote trees as a social determinant of public health.","container-title":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","DOI":"10.3390/ijerph17124371","ISSN":"1660-4601","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"IJERPH","language":"en","page":"4371","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Urban Trees and Human Health: A Scoping Review","title-short":"Urban Trees and Human Health","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/1660-4601/17/12/4371","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Wolf","given":"Kathleen L."},{"family":"Lam","given":"Sharon T."},{"family":"McKeen","given":"Jennifer K."},{"family":"Richardson","given":"Gregory R.A."},{"family":"Bosch","given":"Matilda","non-dropping-particle":"van den"},{"family":"Bardekjian","given":"Adrina C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The benefits of natural settings for restorative effects, such as stress relief, decreased cognitive fatigue, and happiness (see Chapter 1), have been documented in both urban and non-urban settings. Natural settings have been associated with, amongst others, better cognitive functioning, fewer symptoms of depression and lower antidepressant use, reduced stress and psychiatric disorders, reduced diabetes, and improved immune function ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a3VsF8eZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Frumkin, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Frumkin, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31464,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WQ8ERAHC"],"itemData":{"id":31464,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Health Perspectives","DOI":"10.1289/EHP1663","ISSN":"0091-6765, 1552-9924","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Environ Health Perspect","language":"en","page":"075001","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Nature Contact and Human Health: A Research Agenda","title-short":"Nature Contact and Human Health","URL":"https://ehp.niehs.nih.gov/doi/10.1289/EHP1663","volume":"125","author":[{"family":"Frumkin","given":"Howard"},{"family":"Bratman","given":"Gregory N."},{"family":"Breslow","given":"Sara Jo"},{"family":"Cochran","given":"Bobby"},{"family":"Kahn Jr","given":"Peter H."},{"family":"Lawler","given":"Joshua J."},{"family":"Levin","given":"Phillip S."},{"family":"Tandon","given":"Pooja S."},{"family":"Varanasi","given":"Usha"},{"family":"Wolf","given":"Kathleen L."},{"family":"Wood","given":"Spencer A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",7,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Exposure to nature has also been shown to have a positive effect on infant birth weights, reductions in childhood obesity, and improved blood pressure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"to0kXP5O","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aerts, Honnay, & Van Nieuwenhuyse, 2018; Frumkin, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Aerts, Honnay, & Van Nieuwenhuyse, 2018; Frumkin, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31466,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J2LG2JQI"],"itemData":{"id":31466,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"British Medical Bulletin","DOI":"10.1093/bmb/ldy021","ISSN":"0007-1420, 1471-8391","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"5-22","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Biodiversity and human health: mechanisms and evidence of the positive health effects of diversity in nature and green spaces","title-short":"Biodiversity and human health","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/bmb/article/127/1/5/5051732","volume":"127","author":[{"family":"Aerts","given":"Raf"},{"family":"Honnay","given":"Olivier"},{"family":"Van Nieuwenhuyse","given":"An"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",9,1]]}}},{"id":31464,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WQ8ERAHC"],"itemData":{"id":31464,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Health Perspectives","DOI":"10.1289/EHP1663","ISSN":"0091-6765, 1552-9924","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Environ Health Perspect","language":"en","page":"075001","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Nature Contact and Human Health: A Research Agenda","title-short":"Nature Contact and Human Health","URL":"https://ehp.niehs.nih.gov/doi/10.1289/EHP1663","volume":"125","author":[{"family":"Frumkin","given":"Howard"},{"family":"Bratman","given":"Gregory N."},{"family":"Breslow","given":"Sara Jo"},{"family":"Cochran","given":"Bobby"},{"family":"Kahn Jr","given":"Peter H."},{"family":"Lawler","given":"Joshua J."},{"family":"Levin","given":"Phillip S."},{"family":"Tandon","given":"Pooja S."},{"family":"Varanasi","given":"Usha"},{"family":"Wolf","given":"Kathleen L."},{"family":"Wood","given":"Spencer A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",7,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Exceptions include the negative effects of plant pollen and volatile organic compounds from trees ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fFLlc4yi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. L. Wolf, 2020)","plainCitation":"(K. L. Wolf, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31360,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y5MY6YPY"],"itemData":{"id":31360,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The urban forest is a green infrastructure system that delivers multiple environmental, economic, social and health services, and functions in cities. Environmental benefits of urban trees are well understood, but no review to date has examined how urban trees affect human health. This review provides a comprehensive summary of existing literature on the health impacts of urban trees that can inform future research, policy, and nature-based public health interventions. A systematic search used keywords representing human health, environmental health, and urban forestry. Following screening and appraisal of several thousand articles, 201 studies were conceptually sorted into a three-part framework. Reducing Harm, representing 41% of studies, includes topics such as air pollution, ultraviolet radiation, heat exposure, and pollen. Restoring Capacities, at 31%, includes attention restoration, mental health, stress reduction, and clinical outcomes. Building Capacities, at 28%, includes topics such as birth outcomes, active living, and weight status. The studies that were reviewed show substantial heterogeneity in purpose and method yet indicate important health outcomes associated with people’s exposure to trees. This review will help inform future research and practice, and demonstrates why urban forest planning and management should strategically promote trees as a social determinant of public health.","container-title":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","DOI":"10.3390/ijerph17124371","ISSN":"1660-4601","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"IJERPH","language":"en","page":"4371","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Urban Trees and Human Health: A Scoping Review","title-short":"Urban Trees and Human Health","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/1660-4601/17/12/4371","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Wolf","given":"Kathleen L."},{"family":"Lam","given":"Sharon T."},{"family":"McKeen","given":"Jennifer K."},{"family":"Richardson","given":"Gregory R.A."},{"family":"Bosch","given":"Matilda","non-dropping-particle":"van den"},{"family":"Bardekjian","given":"Adrina C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Findings on the benefits wild species and ecosystems provide for mental and physical health have motivated for technology to provide this form of health benefit through virtual reality, and although exposure to nature through photographs or video does improve stress levels and reduce cognitive fatigue, the real experiences in nature significantly outperform virtual experiences for restorative benefits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CzovNog1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Calogiuri, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Calogiuri, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31343,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/57FI6PI7"],"itemData":{"id":31343,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in psychology","DOI":"10.3389/fpsyg.2017.02321","note":"publisher: Frontiers","page":"2321","title":"Experiencing nature through immersive virtual environments: environmental perceptions, physical engagement, and affective responses during a simulated nature walk","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Calogiuri","given":"Giovanna"},{"family":"Litleskare","given":"Sigbjørn"},{"family":"Fagerheim","given":"Kaia A"},{"family":"Rydgren","given":"Tore L"},{"family":"Brambilla","given":"Elena"},{"family":"Thurston","given":"Miranda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The studies mentioned above used a variety of self-reported measures to assess human well-being, with little research being done on clinical outcomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0OVca9ti","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aerts, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Aerts, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31466,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J2LG2JQI"],"itemData":{"id":31466,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"British Medical Bulletin","DOI":"10.1093/bmb/ldy021","ISSN":"0007-1420, 1471-8391","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"5-22","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Biodiversity and human health: mechanisms and evidence of the positive health effects of diversity in nature and green spaces","title-short":"Biodiversity and human health","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/bmb/article/127/1/5/5051732","volume":"127","author":[{"family":"Aerts","given":"Raf"},{"family":"Honnay","given":"Olivier"},{"family":"Van Nieuwenhuyse","given":"An"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",9,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The research was also mostly based on a limited set of variables to describe nature. The majority of studies were based on proximity to nature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"m0H5UW7S","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Xiao, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Xiao, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31486,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6X9HKY8B"],"itemData":{"id":31486,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","DOI":"10.1016/j.landurbplan.2018.09.013","ISSN":"01692046","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape and Urban Planning","language":"en","page":"80-91","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Exploring the disparities in park access through mobile phone data: Evidence from Shanghai, China","title-short":"Exploring the disparities in park access through mobile phone data","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169204618309897","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Xiao","given":"Yang"},{"family":"Wang","given":"De"},{"family":"Fang","given":"Jia"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, or the number or cover of trees ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cM5KCJOA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. L. Wolf, 2020)","plainCitation":"(K. L. Wolf, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31360,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y5MY6YPY"],"itemData":{"id":31360,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The urban forest is a green infrastructure system that delivers multiple environmental, economic, social and health services, and functions in cities. Environmental benefits of urban trees are well understood, but no review to date has examined how urban trees affect human health. This review provides a comprehensive summary of existing literature on the health impacts of urban trees that can inform future research, policy, and nature-based public health interventions. A systematic search used keywords representing human health, environmental health, and urban forestry. Following screening and appraisal of several thousand articles, 201 studies were conceptually sorted into a three-part framework. Reducing Harm, representing 41% of studies, includes topics such as air pollution, ultraviolet radiation, heat exposure, and pollen. Restoring Capacities, at 31%, includes attention restoration, mental health, stress reduction, and clinical outcomes. Building Capacities, at 28%, includes topics such as birth outcomes, active living, and weight status. The studies that were reviewed show substantial heterogeneity in purpose and method yet indicate important health outcomes associated with people’s exposure to trees. This review will help inform future research and practice, and demonstrates why urban forest planning and management should strategically promote trees as a social determinant of public health.","container-title":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","DOI":"10.3390/ijerph17124371","ISSN":"1660-4601","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"IJERPH","language":"en","page":"4371","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Urban Trees and Human Health: A Scoping Review","title-short":"Urban Trees and Human Health","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/1660-4601/17/12/4371","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Wolf","given":"Kathleen L."},{"family":"Lam","given":"Sharon T."},{"family":"McKeen","given":"Jennifer K."},{"family":"Richardson","given":"Gregory R.A."},{"family":"Bosch","given":"Matilda","non-dropping-particle":"van den"},{"family":"Bardekjian","given":"Adrina C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There were fewer studies on the diversity of wild species for human well-being ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mxEnhbXU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Methorst, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Methorst, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":484,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NVCNGXE7"],"itemData":{"id":484,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2020.106917","ISSN":"09218009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"106917","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The importance of species diversity for human well-being in Europe","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800920322084","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Methorst","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Rehdanz","given":"Katrin"},{"family":"Mueller","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Hansjürgens","given":"Bernd"},{"family":"Bonn","given":"Aletta"},{"family":"Böhning-Gaese","given":"Katrin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and none was identified on specific wild species, rather focusing on functional groups such as trees or birds. The limited research on the effects of ecological quality (e.g., species richness) of trees suggests lower correlation with life satisfaction than overall abundance and denser tree cover, suggesting people were less affected by species diversity and more by the mere presence of trees ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RQjOJCwO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Marselle, 2020; Methorst, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Marselle, 2020; Methorst, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31468,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F4MCWJC5"],"itemData":{"id":31468,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              Growing urbanisation is a threat to both mental health and biodiversity. Street trees are an important biodiversity component of urban greenspace, but little is known about their effects on mental health. Here, we analysed the association of street tree density and species richness with antidepressant prescribing for 9751 inhabitants of Leipzig, Germany. We examined spatial scale effects of street trees at different distances around participant’s homes, using Euclidean buffers of 100, 300, 500, and 1000 m. Employing generalised additive models, we found a lower rate of antidepressant prescriptions for people living within 100 m of higher density of street trees—although this relationship was marginally significant (\\n              p\\n              ?=?0.057) when confounding factors were considered. Density of street trees at further spatial distances, and species richness of street trees at any distance, were not associated with antidepressant prescriptions. However, for individuals with low socio-economic status, high density of street trees at 100 m around the home significantly reduced the probability of being prescribed antidepressants. The study suggests that unintentional daily contact to nature through street trees close to the home may reduce the risk of depression, especially for individuals in deprived groups. This has important implications for urban planning and nature-based health interventions in cities.","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/s41598-020-79924-5","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"22445","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Urban street tree biodiversity and antidepressant prescriptions","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-020-79924-5","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Marselle","given":"Melissa R."},{"family":"Bowler","given":"Diana E."},{"family":"Watzema","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Eichenberg","given":"David"},{"family":"Kirsten","given":"Toralf"},{"family":"Bonn","given":"Aletta"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}},{"id":484,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NVCNGXE7"],"itemData":{"id":484,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2020.106917","ISSN":"09218009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"106917","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The importance of species diversity for human well-being in Europe","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800920322084","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Methorst","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Rehdanz","given":"Katrin"},{"family":"Mueller","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Hansjürgens","given":"Bernd"},{"family":"Bonn","given":"Aletta"},{"family":"Böhning-Gaese","given":"Katrin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, although the state of knowledge in this field is still mixed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dPjCCln7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aerts, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Aerts, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31466,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J2LG2JQI"],"itemData":{"id":31466,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"British Medical Bulletin","DOI":"10.1093/bmb/ldy021","ISSN":"0007-1420, 1471-8391","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"5-22","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Biodiversity and human health: mechanisms and evidence of the positive health effects of diversity in nature and green spaces","title-short":"Biodiversity and human health","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/bmb/article/127/1/5/5051732","volume":"127","author":[{"family":"Aerts","given":"Raf"},{"family":"Honnay","given":"Olivier"},{"family":"Van Nieuwenhuyse","given":"An"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",9,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Certainly, people have expressed preference for particular species, especially those that were aesthetically pleasing or reminded them of their childhood home ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PFwn7qKE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C.M. Shackleton & Mograbi, 2020)","plainCitation":"(C.M. Shackleton & Mograbi, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31467,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NVNHASGG"],"itemData":{"id":31467,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2019.126507","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126507","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Meeting a diversity of needs through a diversity of species: Urban residents’ favourite and disliked tree species across eleven towns in South Africa and Zimbabwe","title-short":"Meeting a diversity of needs through a diversity of species","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1618866719303887","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Mograbi","given":"P.J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Dose-response effects of wild species on human health have been demonstrated with trees and with birds. People living within 100m of higher street tree density had lower antidepressant prescriptions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"X0zLnWPL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Marselle, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Marselle, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31468,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F4MCWJC5"],"itemData":{"id":31468,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              Growing urbanisation is a threat to both mental health and biodiversity. Street trees are an important biodiversity component of urban greenspace, but little is known about their effects on mental health. Here, we analysed the association of street tree density and species richness with antidepressant prescribing for 9751 inhabitants of Leipzig, Germany. We examined spatial scale effects of street trees at different distances around participant’s homes, using Euclidean buffers of 100, 300, 500, and 1000 m. Employing generalised additive models, we found a lower rate of antidepressant prescriptions for people living within 100 m of higher density of street trees—although this relationship was marginally significant (\\n              p\\n              ?=?0.057) when confounding factors were considered. Density of street trees at further spatial distances, and species richness of street trees at any distance, were not associated with antidepressant prescriptions. However, for individuals with low socio-economic status, high density of street trees at 100 m around the home significantly reduced the probability of being prescribed antidepressants. The study suggests that unintentional daily contact to nature through street trees close to the home may reduce the risk of depression, especially for individuals in deprived groups. This has important implications for urban planning and nature-based health interventions in cities.","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/s41598-020-79924-5","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"22445","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Urban street tree biodiversity and antidepressant prescriptions","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-020-79924-5","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Marselle","given":"Melissa R."},{"family":"Bowler","given":"Diana E."},{"family":"Watzema","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Eichenberg","given":"David"},{"family":"Kirsten","given":"Toralf"},{"family":"Bonn","given":"Aletta"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This effect was even more pronounced for individuals with low socio-economic status ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"n486VI2b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Marselle, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Marselle, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31468,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F4MCWJC5"],"itemData":{"id":31468,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              Growing urbanisation is a threat to both mental health and biodiversity. Street trees are an important biodiversity component of urban greenspace, but little is known about their effects on mental health. Here, we analysed the association of street tree density and species richness with antidepressant prescribing for 9751 inhabitants of Leipzig, Germany. We examined spatial scale effects of street trees at different distances around participant’s homes, using Euclidean buffers of 100, 300, 500, and 1000 m. Employing generalised additive models, we found a lower rate of antidepressant prescriptions for people living within 100 m of higher density of street trees—although this relationship was marginally significant (\\n              p\\n              ?=?0.057) when confounding factors were considered. Density of street trees at further spatial distances, and species richness of street trees at any distance, were not associated with antidepressant prescriptions. However, for individuals with low socio-economic status, high density of street trees at 100 m around the home significantly reduced the probability of being prescribed antidepressants. The study suggests that unintentional daily contact to nature through street trees close to the home may reduce the risk of depression, especially for individuals in deprived groups. This has important implications for urban planning and nature-based health interventions in cities.","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/s41598-020-79924-5","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"22445","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Urban street tree biodiversity and antidepressant prescriptions","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-020-79924-5","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Marselle","given":"Melissa R."},{"family":"Bowler","given":"Diana E."},{"family":"Watzema","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Eichenberg","given":"David"},{"family":"Kirsten","given":"Toralf"},{"family":"Bonn","given":"Aletta"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A study exploring self-reported life satisfaction across Europe in relation to several taxonomic groups and socio-economic indicators found that bird species richness was highly correlated with life satisfaction, comparable with that of net household income (Figure 3.64) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YZNAinAb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Methorst, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Methorst, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":484,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NVCNGXE7"],"itemData":{"id":484,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2020.106917","ISSN":"09218009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"106917","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The importance of species diversity for human well-being in Europe","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800920322084","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Methorst","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Rehdanz","given":"Katrin"},{"family":"Mueller","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Hansjürgens","given":"Bernd"},{"family":"Bonn","given":"Aletta"},{"family":"Böhning-Gaese","given":"Katrin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Methorst, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AauwIrzd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2021)","plainCitation":"(2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":484,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NVCNGXE7"],"itemData":{"id":484,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2020.106917","ISSN":"09218009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"106917","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The importance of species diversity for human well-being in Europe","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800920322084","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Methorst","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Rehdanz","given":"Katrin"},{"family":"Mueller","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Hansjürgens","given":"Bernd"},{"family":"Bonn","given":"Aletta"},{"family":"Böhning-Gaese","given":"Katrin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} hypothesize that the direct multisensory experience of birds and/or the supporting landscape properties that support bird diversity benefit human life satisfaction. Another study found that vegetation cover and afternoon bird abundance was positively associated with lower depression, anxiety and stress ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ah75s1wc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. T. C. Cox, 2017)","plainCitation":"(D. T. C. Cox, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31321,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F2NKM8IH"],"itemData":{"id":31321,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1093/biosci/biw173","ISSN":"0006-3568, 1525-3244","journalAbbreviation":"BioScience","language":"en","page":"biw173","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Doses of Neighborhood Nature: The Benefits for Mental Health of Living with Nature","title-short":"Doses of Neighborhood Nature","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/bioscience/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/biosci/biw173","author":[{"family":"Cox","given":"Daniel T. C."},{"family":"Shanahan","given":"Danielle F."},{"family":"Hudson","given":"Hannah L."},{"family":"Plummer","given":"Kate E."},{"family":"Siriwardena","given":"Gavin M."},{"family":"Fuller","given":"Richard A."},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Karen"},{"family":"Hancock","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Gaston","given":"Kevin J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,25]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Cox, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cnRMXZLG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2017)","plainCitation":"(2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31321,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F2NKM8IH"],"itemData":{"id":31321,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1093/biosci/biw173","ISSN":"0006-3568, 1525-3244","journalAbbreviation":"BioScience","language":"en","page":"biw173","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Doses of Neighborhood Nature: The Benefits for Mental Health of Living with Nature","title-short":"Doses of Neighborhood Nature","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/bioscience/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/biosci/biw173","author":[{"family":"Cox","given":"Daniel T. C."},{"family":"Shanahan","given":"Danielle F."},{"family":"Hudson","given":"Hannah L."},{"family":"Plummer","given":"Kate E."},{"family":"Siriwardena","given":"Gavin M."},{"family":"Fuller","given":"Richard A."},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Karen"},{"family":"Hancock","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Gaston","given":"Kevin J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,25]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} modelled neighborhood vegetation cover thresholds at which population prevalence of mental health issues were significantly lower: more than 20% for depression and stress, and more than 30% for anxiety. A dose-response model suggested that visits to nature of 30 minutes or more a week could reduce population prevalence of depression by 7% and high blood pressure by 9% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"emfbcoZW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shanahan, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Shanahan, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31460,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RZSB9F2U"],"itemData":{"id":31460,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep28551","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"28551","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Health Benefits from Nature Experiences Depend on Dose","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/srep28551","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Shanahan","given":"Danielle F."},{"family":"Bush","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Gaston","given":"Kevin J."},{"family":"Lin","given":"Brenda B."},{"family":"Dean","given":"Julie"},{"family":"Barber","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Fuller","given":"Richard A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A significant reduction, especially considering that depression alone in Australia, where this study was conducted, was estimated at 12.6 billion Asutralian dollars per year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nmiB506L","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shanahan, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Shanahan, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31460,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RZSB9F2U"],"itemData":{"id":31460,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep28551","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"28551","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Health Benefits from Nature Experiences Depend on Dose","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/srep28551","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Shanahan","given":"Danielle F."},{"family":"Bush","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Gaston","given":"Kevin J."},{"family":"Lin","given":"Brenda B."},{"family":"Dean","given":"Julie"},{"family":"Barber","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Fuller","given":"Richard A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A study from the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland found that individuals spending at least 120 minutes a week in nature reported better health and well-being relative to people spending no time outdoors; positive associations peaked at 200-300 minutes a week ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IkoFs0un","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. P. White, 2019)","plainCitation":"(M. P. White, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31361,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VDYX6XHZ"],"itemData":{"id":31361,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/s41598-019-44097-3","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"7730","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Spending at least 120 minutes a week in nature is associated with good health and wellbeing","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-019-44097-3","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"White","given":"Mathew P."},{"family":"Alcock","given":"Ian"},{"family":"Grellier","given":"James"},{"family":"Wheeler","given":"Benedict W."},{"family":"Hartig","given":"Terry"},{"family":"Warber","given":"Sara L."},{"family":"Bone","given":"Angie"},{"family":"Depledge","given":"Michael H."},{"family":"Fleming","given":"Lora E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These “Green Prescriptions” highlight the importance of species presence and diversity to human well-being, a cost-effective means of supporting a healthy population. 
      
      
Figure 3.64 Estimated life-satisfaction increase correlates to bird species richness and income across Europe. Estimates are based on the coefficients for log-transformed mean bird species richness and log-transformed net household income, both corrected for socio- and macro-economic factors. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fi4mEYyT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Methorst, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Methorst, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":484,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NVCNGXE7"],"itemData":{"id":484,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2020.106917","ISSN":"09218009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"106917","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The importance of species diversity for human well-being in Europe","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800920322084","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Methorst","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Rehdanz","given":"Katrin"},{"family":"Mueller","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Hansjürgens","given":"Bernd"},{"family":"Bonn","given":"Aletta"},{"family":"Böhning-Gaese","given":"Katrin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY-NC-ND 4.0. 

      There are significant socio-economic disparities in urban green space access, both as a result of restricted access (e.g., private space) and as a consequence of socio-economic class differentiation in urban planning ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jsqEiD4h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Venter, Shackleton, Van Staden, Selomane, & Masterson, 2020; J. Wu, He, Chen, Lin, & Wang, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Venter, Shackleton, Van Staden, Selomane, & Masterson, 2020; J. Wu, He, Chen, Lin, & Wang, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31366,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6W6SRQC"],"itemData":{"id":31366,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","DOI":"10.1016/j.landurbplan.2020.103889","ISSN":"01692046","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape and Urban Planning","language":"en","page":"103889","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Green Apartheid: Urban green infrastructure remains unequally distributed across income and race geographies in South Africa","title-short":"Green Apartheid","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169204620303947","volume":"203","author":[{"family":"Venter","given":"Zander S."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."},{"family":"Van Staden","given":"Francini"},{"family":"Selomane","given":"Odirilwe"},{"family":"Masterson","given":"Vanessa A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",11]]}}},{"id":31489,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MTDBUVJD"],"itemData":{"id":31489,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Gated communities have become a common feature in recent decades and have been shown to lead to social inequality to the detriment of the poorest social classes. Because access to urban green space is crucial for both physical health and spiritual pleasure, it is often regarded as an indicator of social justice; however, there are many references to the current inequity in urban green space accessibility. Our study aimed first to measure the potential spatial accessibility of green space in the central urban area of Beijing; then to evaluate the socio-economic disparities in green space accessibility; and finally, to assess the effect of the policy of “opening up gated residential communities” on urban green space accessibility. We adopt the Gaussian-based two-step floating catchment to assess the spatial accessibility of green spaces in each residential zone in the central area of Beijing, and the ordinary least squares model was used to evaluate the inequity in accessibility caused by socio-economic disparities. The results reveal that lower income residential zones have remarkably lower access to green spaces. Next, by comparing the differences in accessibility equity between two comparable scenarios in which all communities have dismantled their fences, we unexpectedly find that the inequity of access to urban green space does not improve but becomes more pronounced. We attribute this result to socio-spatial polarization. Our findings can be used by urban planners to target current urban planning system reform and by policymakers to focus closely on the gradual spatial polarization between the rich and the poor.","container-title":"Environment and Planning B: Urban Analytics and City Science","DOI":"10.1177/2399808318793139","ISSN":"2399-8083, 2399-8091","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Environment and Planning B: Urban Analytics and City Science","language":"en","page":"626-644","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Dismantling the fence for social justice? Evidence based on the inequity of urban green space accessibility in the central urban area of Beijing","title-short":"Dismantling the fence for social justice?","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/2399808318793139","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Wu","given":"Jiayu"},{"family":"He","given":"Qingsong"},{"family":"Chen","given":"Yunwen"},{"family":"Lin","given":"Jian"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Shantong"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Gentrification, while making cities more attractive to wealthy residents and attracting investment, has environmental justice implications, especially on urban green space access by lower class or income communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZPQCLUZW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kronenberg, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Kronenberg, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31494,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B5GMYUAH"],"itemData":{"id":31494,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Cities","DOI":"10.1016/j.cities.2020.102862","ISSN":"02642751","journalAbbreviation":"Cities","language":"en","page":"102862","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Environmental justice in the context of urban green space availability, accessibility, and attractiveness in postsocialist cities","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0264275120312105","volume":"106","author":[{"family":"Kronenberg","given":"Jakub"},{"family":"Haase","given":"Annegret"},{"family":"?aszkiewicz","given":"Edyta"},{"family":"Antal","given":"Attila"},{"family":"Baravikova","given":"Aliaksandra"},{"family":"Biernacka","given":"Magdalena"},{"family":"Dushkova","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Fil?ak","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Haase","given":"Dagmar"},{"family":"Ignatieva","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Khmara","given":"Yaryna"},{"family":"Ni??","given":"Mihai Razvan"},{"family":"Onose","given":"Diana Andreea"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Public space is also increasingly being ‘corporatized’, where public space maintenance is sponsored by private interests, and the urban green space is redesigned and highly controlled to meet the needs of the ‘owner’ rather than the general public ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TdSCDHpF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Schmidt, 2004)","plainCitation":"(S. Schmidt, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EQI8XC5G"],"itemData":{"id":31493,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"IEEE Technology and Society Magazine","issue":"3","note":"publisher: IEEE","page":"17–18","title":"World wide plaza: The corporatization of urban public space","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Schmidt","given":"Stephan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Research in North American cities on parks, urban forests and tree canopy cover found race, ethnicity and income disparities in tree distribution with non-white communities living in areas of lower tree density and lower quantity and quality of parks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"K68AuRgC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Heynen, Perkins, & Roy, 2006; Rigolon, Browning, & Jennings, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Heynen, Perkins, & Roy, 2006; Rigolon, Browning, & Jennings, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31488,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3XS3AAGI"],"itemData":{"id":31488,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Affairs Review","DOI":"10.1177/1078087406290729","ISSN":"1078-0874, 1552-8332","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Affairs Review","language":"en","page":"3-25","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Political Ecology of Uneven Urban Green Space: The Impact of Political Economy on Race and Ethnicity in Producing Environmental Inequality in Milwaukee","title-short":"The Political Ecology of Uneven Urban Green Space","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1078087406290729","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Heynen","given":"Nik"},{"family":"Perkins","given":"Harold A."},{"family":"Roy","given":"Parama"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",9]]}}},{"id":31487,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TQWQGCPW"],"itemData":{"id":31487,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","DOI":"10.1016/j.landurbplan.2018.05.026","ISSN":"01692046","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape and Urban Planning","language":"en","page":"156-169","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Inequities in the quality of urban park systems: An environmental justice investigation of cities in the United States","title-short":"Inequities in the quality of urban park systems","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169204618304316","volume":"178","author":[{"family":"Rigolon","given":"Alessandro"},{"family":"Browning","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Viniece"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The disproportionate access to and distribution of urban green spaces creates inequitable health benefits derived from exposure to nature, with lower income and minority communities in cities more likely to live in “riskscapes” – environments that increase the vulnerability of these communities to pollutants and hazards ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a10lonav7or","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. Jennings, Johnson Gaither, & Gragg, 2012)","plainCitation":"(V. Jennings, Johnson Gaither, & Gragg, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31495,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HIT788NG"],"itemData":{"id":31495,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Justice","DOI":"10.1089/env.2011.0007","ISSN":"1939-4071, 1937-5174","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Justice","language":"en","page":"1-7","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Promoting Environmental Justice Through Urban Green Space Access: A Synopsis","title-short":"Promoting Environmental Justice Through Urban Green Space Access","URL":"http://www.liebertpub.com/doi/10.1089/env.2011.0007","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Jennings","given":"Viniece"},{"family":"Johnson Gaither","given":"Cassandra"},{"family":"Gragg","given":"Richard Schulterbrandt"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      “Green prescriptions” such as “forest bathing” are increasing as they improve human health, but there are also win-win opportunities for people and ecosystems through “reciprocal restoration”. Pilot initiatives with urban youth working in habitat restoration programs have shown greater anti-inflammatory capacity, cardiovascular fitness, resistance to endoparasites, resistance to infectious diseases, reduced sensitivity to allergens, reduced frequency of nervous and musculoskeletal disorders and a wide range of positive effects on mental health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1rihjipb0v","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nabhan, Orlando, Smith Monti, & Aronson, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Nabhan, Orlando, Smith Monti, & Aronson, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31362,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G7RDVFXD"],"itemData":{"id":31362,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecopsychology","DOI":"10.1089/eco.2020.0003","ISSN":"1942-9347","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Ecopsychology","language":"en","page":"195-202","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Hands-On Ecological Restoration as a Nature-Based Health Intervention: Reciprocal Restoration for People and Ecosystems","title-short":"Hands-On Ecological Restoration as a Nature-Based Health Intervention","URL":"https://www.liebertpub.com/doi/10.1089/eco.2020.0003","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Nabhan","given":"Gary P."},{"family":"Orlando","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Smith Monti","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Aronson","given":"James"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Concurrently, habitats are restored including vegetation cover and soil microbial content ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1t8acj6hfp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nabhan, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Nabhan, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31362,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G7RDVFXD"],"itemData":{"id":31362,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecopsychology","DOI":"10.1089/eco.2020.0003","ISSN":"1942-9347","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Ecopsychology","language":"en","page":"195-202","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Hands-On Ecological Restoration as a Nature-Based Health Intervention: Reciprocal Restoration for People and Ecosystems","title-short":"Hands-On Ecological Restoration as a Nature-Based Health Intervention","URL":"https://www.liebertpub.com/doi/10.1089/eco.2020.0003","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Nabhan","given":"Gary P."},{"family":"Orlando","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Smith Monti","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Aronson","given":"James"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The hypothesized mechanisms for the documented improvements in mental and physical health include the Microbiome Rewilding Hypothesis where restoring soil microbial diversity enhances human gut microbiome health and boosts immune functioning, and the Psycho-Evolutionary Restoration Hypothesis where humans exposed to forested systems exposes them to phytoncides that may reduce depression and lower cortisol levels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eK8GFE3R","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nabhan, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Nabhan, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31362,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G7RDVFXD"],"itemData":{"id":31362,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecopsychology","DOI":"10.1089/eco.2020.0003","ISSN":"1942-9347","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Ecopsychology","language":"en","page":"195-202","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Hands-On Ecological Restoration as a Nature-Based Health Intervention: Reciprocal Restoration for People and Ecosystems","title-short":"Hands-On Ecological Restoration as a Nature-Based Health Intervention","URL":"https://www.liebertpub.com/doi/10.1089/eco.2020.0003","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Nabhan","given":"Gary P."},{"family":"Orlando","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Smith Monti","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Aronson","given":"James"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In a critical review of the effects of environmental diversity on human health, Sandifer, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"py4QvrL9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2015)","plainCitation":"(2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31455,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F2ZASMCA"],"itemData":{"id":31455,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2014.12.007","ISSN":"22120416","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystem Services","language":"en","page":"1-15","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Exploring connections among nature, biodiversity, ecosystem services, and human health and well-being: Opportunities to enhance health and biodiversity conservation","title-short":"Exploring connections among nature, biodiversity, ecosystem services, and human health and well-being","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2212041614001648","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Sandifer","given":"Paul A."},{"family":"Sutton-Grier","given":"Ariana E."},{"family":"Ward","given":"Bethney P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",4]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found the only unambiguous causal relationship was the maintenance of a healthy immune system and reduction of inflammatory diseases through exposure to environmental microbial diversity. There is also a limit in research on the sustained, long-term effects of nature-based therapies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JawKP3fn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rajoo, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Rajoo, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31461,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3NKRR7PR"],"itemData":{"id":31461,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","DOI":"10.1016/j.ufug.2020.126744","ISSN":"16188667","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","language":"en","page":"126744","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The physiological and psychosocial effects of forest therapy: A systematic review","title-short":"The physiological and psychosocial effects of forest therapy","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1618866720304039","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Rajoo","given":"Keeren Sundara"},{"family":"Karam","given":"Daljit Singh"},{"family":"Abdullah","given":"Mohd Zaki"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, despite the limited information about the causal nature underlying the benefits of nature and biodiversity to human health, protecting and restoring a diversity of natural habitats seems crucial for maintaining human health in a developing world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DiaPrLRy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sandifer, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Sandifer, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31455,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F2ZASMCA"],"itemData":{"id":31455,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2014.12.007","ISSN":"22120416","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystem Services","language":"en","page":"1-15","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Exploring connections among nature, biodiversity, ecosystem services, and human health and well-being: Opportunities to enhance health and biodiversity conservation","title-short":"Exploring connections among nature, biodiversity, ecosystem services, and human health and well-being","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2212041614001648","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Sandifer","given":"Paul A."},{"family":"Sutton-Grier","given":"Ariana E."},{"family":"Ward","given":"Bethney P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indeed, the improvement of human health is a powerful tool to leverage support from multiple stakeholders to enhance social-ecological health in a variety of ways.
      
3.3.5.2.3. Recreation

Wildlife watching is an activity that involves the watching of wild species (animals and plants). Watching wild species is essentially an observational activity, although in some cases it can involve interactions with the animals being watched, such as touching or feeding them ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rAYaAcm0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These recreational activities include nature-based tourism, hiking and nature walks, photography and cinematography, game watching and safaris, and snorkeling and scuba diving. The use of wild species for recreation is primarily for enjoyment but may also provide relaxation, restoration, physical exercise (see section 3.3.5.2.2. Medicine and hygiene), and educational experiences (see section 3.3.5.2.4. Education and learning). 
      The scope of this section is limited to the non-extractive practices of wild species for recreation where the impact of the use can be measured or assessed. The text below is based on a literature review (Web of Science) using the following strings of terms ("non-extractive use*" OR “non-consumptive” OR "cultural ecosystem service*" OR "non-material contribution*" OR “touris*” OR “community based tourism*” OR “ecotourism” OR “eco-tourism” OR “sustainable tourism” OR “recreational” OR “nature-based tourism” OR “wildlife watching” OR “wildlife viewing”) AND (sustainab* OR trend*), generating 16117 hits. Articles that were recommended by citation databases were also considered, as well as collected from personal libraries and recommendations from experts. After a title and abstract read, 82 papers were selected for a full text read. After a full text read of these papers, 27 were deemed relevant and assessed for the literature review. Relevance was determined by mention of the status (current), trend (historical) or impacts of use of a wild species (or taxa) in at least one dimension of sustainability (social, economic, or ecological). These data form the basis of the text below. 
      The literature covered fairly equally (4-6 papers each): vegetation (trees and shrubs); terrestrial mammals; birds (terrestrial and marine); marine mammals; fish, rays and sharks; and arthropods (marine and terrestrial). The temporal scale of the research articles was 10 short term (<1 year), 1 medium term (1-10 years), and 9 long term (>10 years) studies. The review included articles from every IPBES region.
      Most of the information in the text below relates to wildlife watching tourism, as 74% of relevant articles focused on tourism specifically. Wildlife watching does occur around people’s homes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7zcA4zwd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Wilkinson, Waitt, & Gibbs, 2014; Zaraz\\\\uc0\\\\u250{}a-Carbajal, 2020)","plainCitation":"(C. Wilkinson, Waitt, & Gibbs, 2014; Zarazúa-Carbajal, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AYS6JCWV"],"itemData":{"id":31359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Australian Geographer","DOI":"10.1080/00049182.2014.899029","ISSN":"0004-9182, 1465-3311","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Australian Geographer","language":"en","page":"205-220","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Understanding Place as ‘Home’ and ‘Away’ through Practices of Bird-watching","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/00049182.2014.899029","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Wilkinson","given":"Carrie"},{"family":"Waitt","given":"Gordon"},{"family":"Gibbs","given":"Leah"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",4,3]]}}},{"id":31436,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BBTU7QWZ"],"itemData":{"id":31436,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Background: Interactions between humans and fauna lay in the heart of the history of human subsistence. In Mesoamerica, the Tehuacán-Cuicatlán Valley (TCV) harbours a high biodiversity with archaeological and ethnoecological evidence of its use by people inhabiting the area since at least 12,000 B.P. It is recognized as one of the most ancient areas of agriculture in the Americas, and a broad spectrum of management practices aimed to ensure the availability of desirable plants has been documented, but it has not been analysed for animals. This study aimed to investigate the use and management practices directed to wild animals along current settlements within the TCV and neighbouring areas.\\nMethods: We conducted an extensive search, review and analysis of documental sources for the period between 1967 and 2018. We found 38 documents providing information about the presence of animal species and 15 describing their use and/or management. We included our own observations from four case studies among the Ixcatec, Cuicatec, Nahua and Mestizo people, as well as from regional studies of biodiversity. We used unconstrained multivariate data analysis to describe the management typology of the animals in the region.\\nResults: Hitherto, 652 vertebrate species and 765 species of insects have been recorded in this area; and until present, 107 wild animal species have been reported to be used in 11 use-type categories, mostly for food (65.42%), ornamental (27.52%) and medicinal (21.10%) purposes by the Nahua, Cuicatec, Popolocan, Ixcatec, Mazatec and Mestizo people. Their extraction entails manual capture and gathering as well as hunting and trapping strategies, some of them involving planning in time or space and communitarian regulations; in addition, relocation actions and care in captivity were recorded. Nearly 178 of the species distributed in the region with no reports of local use are used in other localities of Mesoamerica. Ethnozoological information is still lacking for the Mixtec, Chinantec and Chocholtec people in the area.\\nConclusions: Wild fauna is still a valuable resource for the inhabitants of the TCV. Animals are obtained through extractive practices, which vary from one another in their qualitative attributes. With this work, we provide a context for further research priorities on fauna management in a region of high biocultural significance.","call-number":"Sub158","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-020-0354-8","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"English","page":"4","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Use and management of wild fauna by people of the Tehuacán-Cuicatlán Valley and surrounding areas, Mexico","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-020-0354-8","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Zarazúa-Carbajal","given":"Mariana"},{"family":"Chávez-Gutiérrez","given":"Michelle"},{"family":"Romero-Bautista","given":"Yessica"},{"family":"Rangel-Landa","given":"Selene"},{"family":"Moreno-Calles","given":"Ana Isabel"},{"family":"Ramos","given":"Luis Fernando Alvarado"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Sandra E."},{"family":"Blancas","given":"José"},{"family":"Val","given":"Ek","non-dropping-particle":"del"},{"family":"Coro Arizmendi","given":"María","non-dropping-particle":"del"},{"family":"Casas","given":"Alejandro"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but this is less well documented than wildlife watching tourism. Wildlife watching tourism overlaps with various types of tourism, such as tours focused on seeing a specific kind of wild taxa (Table 3.19) and tourism where wildlife watching is an added advantage but not the main focus of the activity (e.g., adventure and sports tourism) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hDMBknS9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, a specific type of nature-based tourism is eco-tourism, where the tourism activity aims to contribute to the conservation of natural and cultural heritage through the involvement of indigenous peoples and local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jEOPqwFg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Eco-tourism has relatively low numbers of tourism and is suited to small groups and independent tourists ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hUKXSmlp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      
