* Social aspects
Enjoyment of nature for tourism and recreation is recognized as the most prominent cultural nature’s contributions to people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"76n3cviJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Balmford, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Balmford, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQW7BDCB"],"itemData":{"id":30359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS Biol","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.1002074","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e1002074","title":"Walk on the wild side: estimating the global magnitude of visits to protected areas","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Green","given":"Jonathan MH"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Beresford","given":"James"},{"family":"Huang","given":"Charles"},{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Walpole","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Manica","given":"Andrea"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Over the last half a century the demand for nature-based tourism experiences has been on the rise, with the ever-increasing breadth and depth of its global penetration, integrating more and more natural areas into commercial processes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JNlHOdhn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Balmford, 2009, 2015; Elmahdy, Haukeland, & Fredman, 2017; C. M. Hall, Harrison, Weaver, & Wall, 2013; Mowforth & Munt, 2015; D. Scott & G\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}ssling, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Balmford, 2009, 2015; Elmahdy, Haukeland, & Fredman, 2017; C. M. Hall, Harrison, Weaver, & Wall, 2013; Mowforth & Munt, 2015; D. Scott & Gössling, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31345,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/47Y4F5CW"],"itemData":{"id":31345,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS Biol","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.1000144","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e1000144","title":"A global perspective on trends in nature-based tourism","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Beresford","given":"James"},{"family":"Green","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Walpole","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Manica","given":"Andrea"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":30359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQW7BDCB"],"itemData":{"id":30359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS Biol","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.1002074","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e1002074","title":"Walk on the wild side: estimating the global magnitude of visits to protected areas","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Green","given":"Jonathan MH"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Beresford","given":"James"},{"family":"Huang","given":"Charles"},{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Walpole","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Manica","given":"Andrea"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":33823,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZEGATTT"],"itemData":{"id":33823,"type":"article-journal","note":"publisher: Norwegian University of Life Sciences, \\\\AAs","title":"Tourism megatrends: A literature review focused on nature-based tourism","URL":"https://hdl.handle.net/11250/2648159","author":[{"family":"Elmahdy","given":"Yasmine Mounir"},{"family":"Haukeland","given":"Jan Vidar"},{"family":"Fredman","given":"Peter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":31341,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2MVWYJZC"],"itemData":{"id":31341,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Recreation Research","DOI":"10.1080/02508281.2013.11081730","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"71–92","title":"Vanishing peripheries: Does tourism consume places?","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Hall","given":"C Michael"},{"family":"Harrison","given":"David"},{"family":"Weaver","given":"David"},{"family":"Wall","given":"Geoffrey"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":31339,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T6KH2PEP"],"itemData":{"id":31339,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-1-317-74723-9","publisher":"routledge","title":"Tourism and sustainability: Development, globalisation and new tourism in the third world","author":[{"family":"Mowforth","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Munt","given":"Ian"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":31340,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/63B88RSH"],"itemData":{"id":31340,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Recreation Research","DOI":"10.1080/02508281.2015.1075739","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"269–285","title":"What could the next 40 years hold for global tourism?","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Scott","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Gössling","given":"Stefan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, according to rough estimations, world terrestrial protected nature areas currently receive approximately 8 billion visits per year, of which 80% are in Europe and North America ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UeN8pQOq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Balmford, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Balmford, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQW7BDCB"],"itemData":{"id":30359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS Biol","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.1002074","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e1002074","title":"Walk on the wild side: estimating the global magnitude of visits to protected areas","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Green","given":"Jonathan MH"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Beresford","given":"James"},{"family":"Huang","given":"Charles"},{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Walpole","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Manica","given":"Andrea"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In general, nature-based tourism and recreation are affected by the following global drivers of change, i.e.,  megatrends: social trends (population growth, urbanization, changes in household composition, aging population, health and well-being, changing work patterns, gender equality, values and lifestyle); technological (transportation, high-tech equipment, information and communication technologies); economic trends (economic growth; sharing economy; fuel costs); environmental (climate change; land use and landscape change); political (political turbulence; changes in border regulations; health risks; geopolitics) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vyvEYut5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Elmahdy, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Elmahdy, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33823,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZEGATTT"],"itemData":{"id":33823,"type":"article-journal","note":"publisher: Norwegian University of Life Sciences, \\\\AAs","title":"Tourism megatrends: A literature review focused on nature-based tourism","URL":"https://hdl.handle.net/11250/2648159","author":[{"family":"Elmahdy","given":"Yasmine Mounir"},{"family":"Haukeland","given":"Jan Vidar"},{"family":"Fredman","given":"Peter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The complexities of these drivers are discussed in Chapter 4 of this assessment. For the purposes of Chapter 3, it is important to point out that a combination of these interconnected global trends is and will be significantly affecting demand for nature-based tourist experiences and the way people engage with nature. 
      There is concern that the aforementioned global trends contribute to increasing disconnectedness of large masses of populations from natural phenomena and processes in their daily life, which generates interest to experience nature as a leisure activity in an organized, often commercialized setting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c6oXgAKt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Buckley, 2000; Buckley, Gretzel, Scott, Weaver, & Becken, 2015; Curtin, 2005; Dwyer, 2003; Elmahdy, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Buckley, 2000; Buckley, Gretzel, Scott, Weaver, & Becken, 2015; Curtin, 2005; Dwyer, 2003; Elmahdy, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33822,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FCQK9AY7"],"itemData":{"id":33822,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Tourism Research","DOI":"10.1002/1522-1970(200011/12)2:63.3.CO;2-R","issue":"6","note":"publisher: John Wiley & Sons, Ltd. Chichester, UK","page":"437–444","title":"Neat trends: current issues in nature, eco-and adventure tourism","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Buckley","given":"Ralf"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":33824,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ECYWPMBP"],"itemData":{"id":33824,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Recreation Research","DOI":"10.1080/02508281.2015.1005942","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"59–70","title":"Tourism megatrends","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Buckley","given":"Ralf"},{"family":"Gretzel","given":"Ulrike"},{"family":"Scott","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Weaver","given":"David"},{"family":"Becken","given":"Susanne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":33818,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QXMHWEF7"],"itemData":{"id":33818,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ecotourism","DOI":"10.1080/14724040508668434","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Routledge\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/14724040508668434","page":"1-15","title":"Nature, Wild Animals and Tourism: An Experiential View","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/14724040508668434","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Curtin","given":"Susanna"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":33832,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M7VU3PPW"],"itemData":{"id":33832,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Tourism Sciences","DOI":"10.1080/15980634.2003.11434550","ISSN":"1598-0634, 2377-0058","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Tourism Sciences","language":"en","page":"61-77","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trends Underpinning Tourism to 2015: An Analysis of Key Drivers for Change","title-short":"Trends Underpinning Tourism to 2015","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/15980634.2003.11434550","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dwyer","given":"Larry"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",1]]}}},{"id":33823,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZEGATTT"],"itemData":{"id":33823,"type":"article-journal","note":"publisher: Norwegian University of Life Sciences, \\\\AAs","title":"Tourism megatrends: A literature review focused on nature-based tourism","URL":"https://hdl.handle.net/11250/2648159","author":[{"family":"Elmahdy","given":"Yasmine Mounir"},{"family":"Haukeland","given":"Jan Vidar"},{"family":"Fredman","given":"Peter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It has been observed that nature-based tourism and recreation are increasingly characterized by the importance of experiences, achievement, adventure and well-planned activities rather than simple leisure and social interaction. Many studies indicate that tourism and recreation in nature are becoming more specialized, diversified, motorized, sportified and adventurized ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VZzKIQXV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u214{}hman, \\\\uc0\\\\u214{}hman, & Sandell, 2016; Sandell, Arneg\\\\uc0\\\\u229{}rd, & Backman, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Öhman, Öhman, & Sandell, 2016; Sandell, Arnegård, & Backman, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33821,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PF7R7HSQ"],"itemData":{"id":33821,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Adventure Education and Outdoor Learning","DOI":"10.1080/14729679.2016.1147965","issue":"4","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"285–302","title":"Outdoor recreation in exergames: a new step in the detachment from nature?","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Öhman","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Öhman","given":"Marie"},{"family":"Sandell","given":"Klas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33819,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KBZ6CMDQ"],"itemData":{"id":33819,"type":"book","event-place":"Lund","publisher":"Studentlitteratur","publisher-place":"Lund","title":"Friluftssport och äventyrsidrott: Utmaningar för lärare, ledare och miljö i en föränderlig värld [Outdoor sport and adventure sport – Challenges for teachers, leaders and environments in a changing world]","editor":[{"family":"Sandell","given":"K"},{"family":"Arnegård","given":"J"},{"family":"Backman","given":"E"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In this context nature is transformed into a setting, a scenic backdrop for tourist experiences ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2KfI39Lu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fossgard & Fredman, 2019; Margaryan, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Fossgard & Fredman, 2019; Margaryan, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31336,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UA2TBH4D"],"itemData":{"id":31336,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Outdoor Recreation and Tourism","DOI":"10.1016/j.jort.2019.04.001","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"100219","title":"Dimensions in the nature-based tourism experiencescape: An explorative analysis","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Fossgard","given":"Knut"},{"family":"Fredman","given":"Peter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":31337,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/984IWS7V"],"itemData":{"id":31337,"type":"thesis","genre":"PhD Thesis","publisher":"Mid Sweden University","title":"Commercialization of nature through tourism","URL":"https://www.diva-portal.org/smash/record.jsf?pid=diva2%3A1147748&dswid=-2503","author":[{"family":"Margaryan","given":"Lusine"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This also affects tourists’ expectations regarding the availability of tourism-related services in nature. There is a growing demand for ‘wild’, ‘unspoiled’, ‘pristine’ nature in combination with high levels of comfort, accessibility and high-quality experiences ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ePg6W9wR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Elmahdy, 2017; Fredman, Wall-Reinius, & Grund\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}n, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Elmahdy, 2017; Fredman, Wall-Reinius, & Grundén, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33823,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZEGATTT"],"itemData":{"id":33823,"type":"article-journal","note":"publisher: Norwegian University of Life Sciences, \\\\AAs","title":"Tourism megatrends: A literature review focused on nature-based tourism","URL":"https://hdl.handle.net/11250/2648159","author":[{"family":"Elmahdy","given":"Yasmine Mounir"},{"family":"Haukeland","given":"Jan Vidar"},{"family":"Fredman","given":"Peter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33820,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XMWMB5BK"],"itemData":{"id":33820,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scandinavian Journal of Hospitality and Tourism","DOI":"10.1080/15022250.2012.752893","issue":"4","note":"publisher: Routledge\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/15022250.2012.752893","page":"289-309","title":"The Nature of Nature in Nature-based Tourism","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/15022250.2012.752893","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Fredman","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Wall-Reinius","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Grundén","given":"Anna"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These pristine landscapes are advertised for tourism in brochures with backgrounds of teeming game, but absent of the human communities that live alongside wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZnmWOza3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Montgomery, Borona, Kasozi, Mudumba, & Ogada, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Montgomery, Borona, Kasozi, Mudumba, & Ogada, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33814,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LSF2SJ35"],"itemData":{"id":33814,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13483","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"1122-1130","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Positioning human heritage at the center of conservation practice","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/cobi.13483","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Montgomery","given":"Robert A."},{"family":"Borona","given":"Kendi"},{"family":"Kasozi","given":"Herbert"},{"family":"Mudumba","given":"Tutilo"},{"family":"Ogada","given":"Mordecai"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      This marketing perpetuates that indigenous peoples and local communities are separate from the social-ecological system, constitute a threat to wild species conservation, and drives the alienation and displacement of indigenous peoples and local communities, often with indigenous peoples and local communities on the boundaries of conservation areas receiving few benefits from tourism activities taking place ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OYmJTuOe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Montgomery, 2020; Saarinen, Moswete, Atlhopheng, & Hambira, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Montgomery, 2020; Saarinen, Moswete, Atlhopheng, & Hambira, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33814,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LSF2SJ35"],"itemData":{"id":33814,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13483","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"1122-1130","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Positioning human heritage at the center of conservation practice","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/cobi.13483","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Montgomery","given":"Robert A."},{"family":"Borona","given":"Kendi"},{"family":"Kasozi","given":"Herbert"},{"family":"Mudumba","given":"Tutilo"},{"family":"Ogada","given":"Mordecai"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}},{"id":31512,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CWUUJWGE"],"itemData":{"id":31512,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Development Southern Africa","DOI":"10.1080/0376835X.2020.1809997","ISSN":"0376-835X, 1470-3637","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Development Southern Africa","language":"en","page":"855-870","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Changing socio-ecologies of Kalahari: Local perceptions towards environmental change and tourism in Kgalagadi, Botswana","title-short":"Changing socio-ecologies of Kalahari","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/0376835X.2020.1809997","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Saarinen","given":"Jarkko"},{"family":"Moswete","given":"Naomi"},{"family":"Atlhopheng","given":"Julius R."},{"family":"Hambira","given":"Wame L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indigenous peoples and local communities also suffer from the negative aspects of tourism, for example disease and predation adjacent to protected areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rMBU9uqZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Swemmer, Mmethi, & Twine, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Swemmer, Mmethi, & Twine, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31505,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z2W5R253"],"itemData":{"id":31505,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2017.09.002","ISSN":"22120416","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystem Services","language":"en","page":"162-172","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Tracing the cost/benefit pathway of protected areas: A case study of the Kruger National Park, South Africa","title-short":"Tracing the cost/benefit pathway of protected areas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2212041616304417","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Swemmer","given":"Louise"},{"family":"Mmethi","given":"Helen"},{"family":"Twine","given":"Wayne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, or tourist-related disturbance of their activities (e.g., snow mobile recreation in the vicinity of Saami reindeer herders in Lapland ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AcQ2X24L","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kluwe & Krumpe, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Kluwe & Krumpe, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31506,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GIHNA5QM"],"itemData":{"id":31506,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Wilderness","issue":"3","page":"28-33","title":"Interpersonal and societal aspects of use conflicts","URL":"https://www.webpages.uidaho.edu/css496/Readings/Kluwe_Krumpe_Use_Conflicts_W_AK_Finland.pdf","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Kluwe","given":"Joan"},{"family":"Krumpe","given":"Edwin E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, or rock climbers disturbing Native American rituals on Devils Tower/Mato Tipila in Wyoming (Taylor and Geffen 2004). There may also be a conflict in values between recreational and non-recreational users, especially around expected behavior in sacred areas or around traditional hunting practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jLeuqtwK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zeppel, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Zeppel, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":499,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VLHTWZXZ"],"itemData":{"id":499,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism and Hospitality Research","DOI":"10.1057/thr.2009.28","ISSN":"1467-3584, 1742-9692","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Tour Hosp Res","page":"93-104","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Managing cultural values in sustainable tourism: Conflicts in protected areas","title-short":"Managing cultural values in sustainable tourism","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1057/thr.2009.28","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Zeppel","given":"Heather"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Tourists can also cause degradation of culturally important sites through or illegal removal of cultural heritage items ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1dk7gietkp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(INTOSAI WGEA, 2013)","plainCitation":"(INTOSAI WGEA, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32782,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQDLDNH8"],"itemData":{"id":32782,"type":"report","page":"44","title":"Impact of Tourism on Wildlife Conservation","URL":"http://iced.cag.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2014/02/2013_wgea_Wild-Life_view.pdf","author":[{"family":"INTOSAI WGEA","given":""}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Karis","given":"Alar"},{"family":"Utouh","given":"Ludovick"},{"family":"Liphafa","given":"Lucy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Tourism may change local identities and values, through commercialization of local culture and standardization to meet tourists' expectations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2ola5ece7l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(INTOSAI WGEA, 2013)","plainCitation":"(INTOSAI WGEA, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32782,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQDLDNH8"],"itemData":{"id":32782,"type":"report","page":"44","title":"Impact of Tourism on Wildlife Conservation","URL":"http://iced.cag.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2014/02/2013_wgea_Wild-Life_view.pdf","author":[{"family":"INTOSAI WGEA","given":""}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Karis","given":"Alar"},{"family":"Utouh","given":"Ludovick"},{"family":"Liphafa","given":"Lucy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      As the demand for wild species-related experiences is on the rise, wild species-related content on social media and wild species documentaries have become more popular than ever. For example, the British Broadcasting Corporation (BBC) Planet Earth I and II have been among the most watched documentaries worldwide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pvCAifWZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Jackson, 2016)","plainCitation":"(J. Jackson, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33816,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SAT7BSP3"],"itemData":{"id":33816,"type":"article-newspaper","container-title":"The Guardian","title":"Planet Earth II most watched natural history show for 15 years","URL":"https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2016/nov/07/planet-earth-ii-bbc1-most-watched-natural-history-show-for-15-years","author":[{"family":"Jackson","given":"J"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",7,11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The growth of media attention and circulation of wild species-related content in the social media further stimulates demand to experience wild species in real life, as well as photograph and share ‘selfies’ with wild species. Between 2014 and 2017 there has been a documented increase of nearly 300% in the quantity of wild species selfies shared on the Instagram platform ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KhcPYu12","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Animal Protection, 2017)","plainCitation":"(World Animal Protection, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33817,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TDB4MW37"],"itemData":{"id":33817,"type":"report","title":"A close up on cruelty: The harmful impact of wildlife selfies in the Amazon","URL":"https://www.worldanimalprotection.org/sites/default/files/int_files/amazon_selfies_report.pd","author":[{"family":"World Animal Protection","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Of these, over 40% could be classified as inappropriate wild species selfies – featuring handling, hugging, touching, feeding or other potentially detrimental interactions between humans and wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Mh8FNEwT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Animal Protection, 2017)","plainCitation":"(World Animal Protection, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33817,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TDB4MW37"],"itemData":{"id":33817,"type":"report","title":"A close up on cruelty: The harmful impact of wildlife selfies in the Amazon","URL":"https://www.worldanimalprotection.org/sites/default/files/int_files/amazon_selfies_report.pd","author":[{"family":"World Animal Protection","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Tourism marketing and social media sharing influences the demand for extremely close interactions with wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"z0MfpHUo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GERJ6WN8"],"itemData":{"id":493,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Purpose\\n              This paper aims to synthesize the key findings of prior research on the topic of human-wildlife interactions (HWI) in natural places for tourism.\\n            \\n            \\n              Design/methodology/approach\\n              The methodology of systematic review was used to search for, appraise and analyze the relevant research evidence. A total of 47 English-language academic journal articles, published between 2003 and 2018, with free online access to full texts in the database of Hospitality and Tourism Complete have been reviewed. Thematic analysis was adopted to synthesize the textual data.\\n            \\n            \\n              Findings\\n              The reviewed articles cover a wide geographical spread, diverse wildlife species and interaction types, and various research focuses including ecological impacts, human dimensions and management issues of wildlife tourism. The interactions between wildlife and human systems in the context of tourism constitute a complicated social-ecological system, in which both the humans and animals can be affected positively and negatively. Management and scientific research provide the nexus between the ecological and human dimensions of wildlife tourism. While opportunities for sustainable development abound, challenges are not to be neglected.\\n            \\n            \\n              Originality/value\\n              Due to the complexity of wildlife encounters for tourist purposes, the extant literature indicates a diverse and fragmented view from which integrated implications are difficult to obtain. This paper presents the first overarching review in English of the literature on human-wildlife interactions for tourism and provides a big picture understanding of what has been and what is needed to be done in terms of both wildlife tourism research and practices.","container-title":"Journal of Hospitality and Tourism Insights","DOI":"10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007","ISSN":"2514-9792","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JHTI","language":"en","page":"529-547","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism: a systematic review","title-short":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism","URL":"https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007/full/html","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dou","given":"Xueting"},{"family":"Day","given":"Jonathon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, research has shown the dichotomy of tourists’ desires for intimate encounters with wild species and recognition of the detrimental effects on animal welfare as a result of these interactions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GkietM1E","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GERJ6WN8"],"itemData":{"id":493,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Purpose\\n              This paper aims to synthesize the key findings of prior research on the topic of human-wildlife interactions (HWI) in natural places for tourism.\\n            \\n            \\n              Design/methodology/approach\\n              The methodology of systematic review was used to search for, appraise and analyze the relevant research evidence. A total of 47 English-language academic journal articles, published between 2003 and 2018, with free online access to full texts in the database of Hospitality and Tourism Complete have been reviewed. Thematic analysis was adopted to synthesize the textual data.\\n            \\n            \\n              Findings\\n              The reviewed articles cover a wide geographical spread, diverse wildlife species and interaction types, and various research focuses including ecological impacts, human dimensions and management issues of wildlife tourism. The interactions between wildlife and human systems in the context of tourism constitute a complicated social-ecological system, in which both the humans and animals can be affected positively and negatively. Management and scientific research provide the nexus between the ecological and human dimensions of wildlife tourism. While opportunities for sustainable development abound, challenges are not to be neglected.\\n            \\n            \\n              Originality/value\\n              Due to the complexity of wildlife encounters for tourist purposes, the extant literature indicates a diverse and fragmented view from which integrated implications are difficult to obtain. This paper presents the first overarching review in English of the literature on human-wildlife interactions for tourism and provides a big picture understanding of what has been and what is needed to be done in terms of both wildlife tourism research and practices.","container-title":"Journal of Hospitality and Tourism Insights","DOI":"10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007","ISSN":"2514-9792","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JHTI","language":"en","page":"529-547","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism: a systematic review","title-short":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism","URL":"https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007/full/html","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dou","given":"Xueting"},{"family":"Day","given":"Jonathon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Environmental education and increased awareness of wildlife watching sustainability can and does play a role in changing tourist behavior, such as that demonstrated in dolphin-watching tours where tourists were willing to trade-off close interactions for the purposes of dolphin welfare ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"g90D9VIq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GERJ6WN8"],"itemData":{"id":493,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Purpose\\n              This paper aims to synthesize the key findings of prior research on the topic of human-wildlife interactions (HWI) in natural places for tourism.\\n            \\n            \\n              Design/methodology/approach\\n              The methodology of systematic review was used to search for, appraise and analyze the relevant research evidence. A total of 47 English-language academic journal articles, published between 2003 and 2018, with free online access to full texts in the database of Hospitality and Tourism Complete have been reviewed. Thematic analysis was adopted to synthesize the textual data.\\n            \\n            \\n              Findings\\n              The reviewed articles cover a wide geographical spread, diverse wildlife species and interaction types, and various research focuses including ecological impacts, human dimensions and management issues of wildlife tourism. The interactions between wildlife and human systems in the context of tourism constitute a complicated social-ecological system, in which both the humans and animals can be affected positively and negatively. Management and scientific research provide the nexus between the ecological and human dimensions of wildlife tourism. While opportunities for sustainable development abound, challenges are not to be neglected.\\n            \\n            \\n              Originality/value\\n              Due to the complexity of wildlife encounters for tourist purposes, the extant literature indicates a diverse and fragmented view from which integrated implications are difficult to obtain. This paper presents the first overarching review in English of the literature on human-wildlife interactions for tourism and provides a big picture understanding of what has been and what is needed to be done in terms of both wildlife tourism research and practices.","container-title":"Journal of Hospitality and Tourism Insights","DOI":"10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007","ISSN":"2514-9792","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JHTI","language":"en","page":"529-547","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism: a systematic review","title-short":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism","URL":"https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007/full/html","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dou","given":"Xueting"},{"family":"Day","given":"Jonathon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Research on Mozambiquan tourists showed low awareness of cetacean-based regulations, but the tourists were supportive of well-regulated activities, therefore educated tourists could increase operator compliance with regulations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6KYiqKjr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Rocha, 2020)","plainCitation":"(D. Rocha, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31314,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/375XZ4NC"],"itemData":{"id":31314,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2020.104048","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"104048","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Moving towards a sustainable cetacean-based tourism industry – A case study from Mozambique","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X19305767","volume":"120","author":[{"family":"Rocha","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Drakeford","given":"Benjamin"},{"family":"Marley","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"Potts","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Hale","given":"Michelle"},{"family":"Gullan","given":"Angie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In their review on wildlife watching sustainability, Dou and Day ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pmp1Vsrn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GERJ6WN8"],"itemData":{"id":493,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Purpose\\n              This paper aims to synthesize the key findings of prior research on the topic of human-wildlife interactions (HWI) in natural places for tourism.\\n            \\n            \\n              Design/methodology/approach\\n              The methodology of systematic review was used to search for, appraise and analyze the relevant research evidence. A total of 47 English-language academic journal articles, published between 2003 and 2018, with free online access to full texts in the database of Hospitality and Tourism Complete have been reviewed. Thematic analysis was adopted to synthesize the textual data.\\n            \\n            \\n              Findings\\n              The reviewed articles cover a wide geographical spread, diverse wildlife species and interaction types, and various research focuses including ecological impacts, human dimensions and management issues of wildlife tourism. The interactions between wildlife and human systems in the context of tourism constitute a complicated social-ecological system, in which both the humans and animals can be affected positively and negatively. Management and scientific research provide the nexus between the ecological and human dimensions of wildlife tourism. While opportunities for sustainable development abound, challenges are not to be neglected.\\n            \\n            \\n              Originality/value\\n              Due to the complexity of wildlife encounters for tourist purposes, the extant literature indicates a diverse and fragmented view from which integrated implications are difficult to obtain. This paper presents the first overarching review in English of the literature on human-wildlife interactions for tourism and provides a big picture understanding of what has been and what is needed to be done in terms of both wildlife tourism research and practices.","container-title":"Journal of Hospitality and Tourism Insights","DOI":"10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007","ISSN":"2514-9792","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JHTI","language":"en","page":"529-547","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism: a systematic review","title-short":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism","URL":"https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007/full/html","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dou","given":"Xueting"},{"family":"Day","given":"Jonathon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} caution that environmental awareness does not occur automatically from increased exposure to wild species, but rather from focused environmental education with targeted, actionable messages on biodiversity conservation. 
      Wildlife watching has emerged as a widespread and lucrative tourist activity and its popularity is growing rapidly ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aDjVQN7K","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Lima & Green, 2017; Dybsand, 2020; Hassan & Sharma, 2017; Karanth, 2017; Mowforth & Munt, 2015; World Animal Protection, 2017)","plainCitation":"(de Lima & Green, 2017; Dybsand, 2020; Hassan & Sharma, 2017; Karanth, 2017; Mowforth & Munt, 2015; World Animal Protection, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31437,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2TK4XTS4"],"itemData":{"id":31437,"type":"book","collection-title":"Geoheritage, Geoparks and Geotourism book series","note":"publisher: Springer","publisher":"Springer","title":"Wildlife Tourism, Environmental Learning and Ethical Encounters","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-55574-4","editor":[{"family":"Lima","given":"Ismar Borges","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Green","given":"Ronda J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":31335,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y3I8SSEF"],"itemData":{"id":31335,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.tourman.2020.104097","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"104097","title":"In the absence of a main attraction–Perspectives from polar bear watching tourism participants","volume":"79","author":[{"family":"Dybsand","given":"Hilde Nikoline Hambro"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":31338,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NAICQVFJ"],"itemData":{"id":31338,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Geoheritage, Geoparks and Geotourism book series","container-title":"Wildlife Tourism, Environmental Learning and Ethical Encounters","ISBN":"978-3-319-55574-4","page":"155–168","publisher":"Springer","title":"Wildlife Tourism for Visitors’ Learning Experiences: Some Evidences on the Royal Bengal Tiger in Bangladesh and India","author":[{"family":"Hassan","given":"Azizul"},{"family":"Sharma","given":"Anukrati"}],"editor":[{"family":"Lima","given":"Ismar Borges","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Green","given":"R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":30864,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TK46WBPJ"],"itemData":{"id":30864,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Contemporary Geographies of Leisure, Tourism and Mobility","container-title":"Nature tourism","ISBN":"978-1-317-33466-8","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"220","publisher":"Routledge","title":"14 Emerging Trends in Wildlife and Tiger Tourism in India","author":[{"family":"Karanth","given":"Krithi K"},{"family":"Jain","given":"Shivangi"},{"family":"Mariyam","given":"Dincy"}],"editor":[{"family":"Chen","given":"Joseph S"},{"family":"Prebensen","given":"Nina K"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":31339,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T6KH2PEP"],"itemData":{"id":31339,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-1-317-74723-9","publisher":"routledge","title":"Tourism and sustainability: Development, globalisation and new tourism in the third world","author":[{"family":"Mowforth","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Munt","given":"Ian"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":33817,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TDB4MW37"],"itemData":{"id":33817,"type":"report","title":"A close up on cruelty: The harmful impact of wildlife selfies in the Amazon","URL":"https://www.worldanimalprotection.org/sites/default/files/int_files/amazon_selfies_report.pd","author":[{"family":"World Animal Protection","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. International tourism has grown year after year for the last decade, driven in part by nature-based tourism (including extractive tourism activities) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6rdleZ0h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNWTO, 2019)","plainCitation":"(UNWTO, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31485,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q5XARPE3"],"itemData":{"id":31485,"type":"book","abstract":"International Tourism Highlights presents a concise overview of international tourism in the world based on the results for the year 2018.\\"","event-place":"Madrid","ISBN":"978-92-844-2115-2","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1128006896","publisher":"World Tourism Organization","publisher-place":"Madrid","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"International tourism highlights 2019","author":[{"literal":"UNWTO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Between 1990 and 2000, average annual international tourism growth was 4.4%, but wild species-rich countries like Madagascar, Brazil, Cuba and South Africa all experienced averages between 10-20% annually and Vietnam and Laos between 24-36% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Z23YfGnF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Regional share of wildlife watching tourism relative to overall tourism varies globally, from 36.3% in Africa to 1.6% in Europe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"koN6r5La","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(WTTC, 2019a)","plainCitation":"(WTTC, 2019a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":482,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K9XD2NW5"],"itemData":{"id":482,"type":"report","publisher":"World Travel & Tourism Council","title":"The economic impact of global wildlife tourism: Travel & tourism as an economic tool for the protection of wildlife","URL":"https://wttc.org/Portals/0/Documents/Reports/2019/Sustainable%20Growth-Economic%20Impact%20of%20Global%20Wildlife%20Tourism-Aug%202019.pdf?ver=2021-02-25-182802-167","author":[{"family":"WTTC","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Domestic tourism is estimated to be ten times the scale, but the numbers are uncertain. Similarly, the proportion of non-extractive nature-based tourism in recreation and leisure tourism is difficult to unpack as increasingly tourism trends have seen a blend in various types of tourism, such as family holidays that involve urban, adventure tours and wildlife watching ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"w9nKM5RQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, or visits that combine trophy hunting and wildlife watching. But comparisons of protected area visitation rates mirror overall tourism rates in low-income countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"u8we1R5z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Balmford, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Balmford, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31345,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/47Y4F5CW"],"itemData":{"id":31345,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS Biol","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.1000144","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e1000144","title":"A global perspective on trends in nature-based tourism","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Beresford","given":"James"},{"family":"Green","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Walpole","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Manica","given":"Andrea"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      A global study estimates that protected areas receive 8 billion visits per annum, generating 600 billion United States dollars ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iu5GqlhH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Balmford, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Balmford, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQW7BDCB"],"itemData":{"id":30359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS Biol","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.1002074","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e1002074","title":"Walk on the wild side: estimating the global magnitude of visits to protected areas","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Green","given":"Jonathan MH"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Beresford","given":"James"},{"family":"Huang","given":"Charles"},{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Walpole","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Manica","given":"Andrea"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Revenue generated from tourism in protected areas far exceeds the cost of managing these areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AOZujaqz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Balmford, 2015; WTTC, 2019a)","plainCitation":"(Balmford, 2015; WTTC, 2019a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQW7BDCB"],"itemData":{"id":30359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS Biol","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.1002074","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e1002074","title":"Walk on the wild side: estimating the global magnitude of visits to protected areas","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Green","given":"Jonathan MH"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Beresford","given":"James"},{"family":"Huang","given":"Charles"},{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Walpole","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Manica","given":"Andrea"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":482,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K9XD2NW5"],"itemData":{"id":482,"type":"report","publisher":"World Travel & Tourism Council","title":"The economic impact of global wildlife tourism: Travel & tourism as an economic tool for the protection of wildlife","URL":"https://wttc.org/Portals/0/Documents/Reports/2019/Sustainable%20Growth-Economic%20Impact%20of%20Global%20Wildlife%20Tourism-Aug%202019.pdf?ver=2021-02-25-182802-167","author":[{"family":"WTTC","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Surveyed governments and tour operators overwhelming rank nature, national parks and wild species as their largest assets for tourism, a practice that is labor intensive and employs local communities, especially in remote areas where developing regions do not have many other employment options ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"E2gxNAgp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Tourism Organization, 2014)","plainCitation":"(World Tourism Organization, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31342,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V2YAVGY3"],"itemData":{"id":31342,"type":"book","event-place":"Madrid","ISBN":"978-92-844-1675-2","publisher":"UNWTO","publisher-place":"Madrid","title":"Towards measuring the economic value of wildlife watching tourism in Africa - briefing paper","author":[{"family":"World Tourism Organization","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nature-based tourism has been increasing over the last decade as a result of increased demand (increased knowledge of wild species from media and the internet) and shrinking supply (reduced habitats and wild species scarcity) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FxDOGGhb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(The World Bank, 2018)","plainCitation":"(The World Bank, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":495,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X63KD9FV"],"itemData":{"id":495,"type":"document","title":"Growing Wildlife-Based Tourism Sustainably: A New Report and Q&A","URL":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2018/03/01/growing-wildlife-based-tourism-sustainably-a-new-report-and-qahttps://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2018/03/01/growing-wildlife-based-tourism-sustainably-a-new-report-and-qa","author":[{"family":"The World Bank","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is apparent in visitation data for the iconic nature-based tourism destination, the Galapagos Islands, which has recorded an increasing trend in visitors from 1989 (<50,000 visitors) to 2019 (about 271,000 visitors) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0AS0505G","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az-S\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}nchez & Obaco, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Díaz-Sánchez & Obaco, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31482,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CZZKPREZ"],"itemData":{"id":31482,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Policy Research in Tourism, Leisure and Events","DOI":"10.1080/19407963.2020.1813149","ISSN":"1940-7963, 1940-7971","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Policy Research in Tourism, Leisure and Events","language":"en","page":"1-5","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The effects of Coronavirus (COVID-19) on expected tourism revenues for natural preservation. The case of the Galapagos Islands","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/19407963.2020.1813149","author":[{"family":"Díaz-Sánchez","given":"Juan Pablo"},{"family":"Obaco","given":"Moisés"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Recreational use of wild species also generates significant revenue, particularly through nature-based tourism. Wildlife watching contributed 120.1 billion United States dollars in 2018 (343.6 billion United States dollars with multiplier effects) to global gross domestic product, five times the estimated value of the illegal wild species trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Z7UW3SUk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(WTTC, 2019a)","plainCitation":"(WTTC, 2019a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":482,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K9XD2NW5"],"itemData":{"id":482,"type":"report","publisher":"World Travel & Tourism Council","title":"The economic impact of global wildlife tourism: Travel & tourism as an economic tool for the protection of wildlife","URL":"https://wttc.org/Portals/0/Documents/Reports/2019/Sustainable%20Growth-Economic%20Impact%20of%20Global%20Wildlife%20Tourism-Aug%202019.pdf?ver=2021-02-25-182802-167","author":[{"family":"WTTC","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wildlife watching also sustained 21.8 million jobs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3lD0mfBI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(WTTC, 2019a)","plainCitation":"(WTTC, 2019a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":482,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K9XD2NW5"],"itemData":{"id":482,"type":"report","publisher":"World Travel & Tourism Council","title":"The economic impact of global wildlife tourism: Travel & tourism as an economic tool for the protection of wildlife","URL":"https://wttc.org/Portals/0/Documents/Reports/2019/Sustainable%20Growth-Economic%20Impact%20of%20Global%20Wildlife%20Tourism-Aug%202019.pdf?ver=2021-02-25-182802-167","author":[{"family":"WTTC","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The global monetary potential value of whale watching was estimated at over 2.5 billion United States dollars in 2009 and supporting 19,000 jobs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3lIKvojF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34767,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EQNETNQI"],"itemData":{"id":34767,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2010.05.005","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"1273–1278","title":"The global potential for whale watching","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"Andrés M"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"Ussif Rashid"},{"family":"Kaschner","given":"Kristin"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"David"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Shark and ray watching generated over 314 million United States dollars per annum, directly supporting 10,000 jobs and is expected to double by 2033, generating over 780 million United States dollars globally ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AJqFWiRN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, Barnes-Mauthe, Al-Abdulrazzak, Navarro-Holm, & Sumaila, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, Barnes-Mauthe, Al-Abdulrazzak, Navarro-Holm, & Sumaila, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34759,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W2R5S64E"],"itemData":{"id":34759,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            Amid declining shark populations because of overfishing, a burgeoning shark watching industry, already well established in some locations, generates benefits from shark protection. We compile reported economic benefits at shark watching locations and use a meta-analytical approach to estimate benefits at sites without available data. Results suggest that, globally, c. 590,000 shark watchers expend > USD 314 million per year, directly supporting 10,000 jobs. By comparison, the landed value of global shark fisheries is currently c. USD 630 million and has been in decline for most of the past decade. Based on current observed trends, numbers of shark watchers could more than double within the next 20 years, generating > USD 780 million in tourist expenditures around the world. This supports optimistic projections at new sites, including those in an increasing number of shark sanctuaries established primarily for shark conservation and enacted in recognition of the ecological and economic importance of living sharks.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605312001718","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Oryx","language":"en","page":"381-388","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Global economic value of shark ecotourism: implications for conservation","title-short":"Global economic value of shark ecotourism","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0030605312001718/type/journal_article","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"Andrés M."},{"family":"Barnes-Mauthe","given":"Michele"},{"family":"Al-Abdulrazzak","given":"Dalal"},{"family":"Navarro-Holm","given":"Estrella"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U. Rashid"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In contrast the value of shark fisheries was estimated at 630 million United States dollars and has been on the decline over the last decade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VqIOFX90","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34759,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W2R5S64E"],"itemData":{"id":34759,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            Amid declining shark populations because of overfishing, a burgeoning shark watching industry, already well established in some locations, generates benefits from shark protection. We compile reported economic benefits at shark watching locations and use a meta-analytical approach to estimate benefits at sites without available data. Results suggest that, globally, c. 590,000 shark watchers expend > USD 314 million per year, directly supporting 10,000 jobs. By comparison, the landed value of global shark fisheries is currently c. USD 630 million and has been in decline for most of the past decade. Based on current observed trends, numbers of shark watchers could more than double within the next 20 years, generating > USD 780 million in tourist expenditures around the world. This supports optimistic projections at new sites, including those in an increasing number of shark sanctuaries established primarily for shark conservation and enacted in recognition of the ecological and economic importance of living sharks.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605312001718","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Oryx","language":"en","page":"381-388","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Global economic value of shark ecotourism: implications for conservation","title-short":"Global economic value of shark ecotourism","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0030605312001718/type/journal_article","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"Andrés M."},{"family":"Barnes-Mauthe","given":"Michele"},{"family":"Al-Abdulrazzak","given":"Dalal"},{"family":"Navarro-Holm","given":"Estrella"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U. Rashid"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The expected revenue from entrance tickets to the Galapagos Islands in 2020 was about 18 million United States dollars, although significant losses have been predicted as a result of the COVID-19 pandemic ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MmY1K4y2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az-S\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}nchez & Obaco, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Díaz-Sánchez & Obaco, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31482,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CZZKPREZ"],"itemData":{"id":31482,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Policy Research in Tourism, Leisure and Events","DOI":"10.1080/19407963.2020.1813149","ISSN":"1940-7963, 1940-7971","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Policy Research in Tourism, Leisure and Events","language":"en","page":"1-5","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The effects of Coronavirus (COVID-19) on expected tourism revenues for natural preservation. The case of the Galapagos Islands","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/19407963.2020.1813149","author":[{"family":"Díaz-Sánchez","given":"Juan Pablo"},{"family":"Obaco","given":"Moisés"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This revenue is mainly allocated to Galapagos Island conservation programs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lVudeNOz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az-S\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}nchez & Obaco, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Díaz-Sánchez & Obaco, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31482,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CZZKPREZ"],"itemData":{"id":31482,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Policy Research in Tourism, Leisure and Events","DOI":"10.1080/19407963.2020.1813149","ISSN":"1940-7963, 1940-7971","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Policy Research in Tourism, Leisure and Events","language":"en","page":"1-5","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The effects of Coronavirus (COVID-19) on expected tourism revenues for natural preservation. The case of the Galapagos Islands","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/19407963.2020.1813149","author":[{"family":"Díaz-Sánchez","given":"Juan Pablo"},{"family":"Obaco","given":"Moisés"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. International tourism arrivals in Africa, in large part for wild species tourism (including extractive recreational tourism), in 2013 were 56 million people, generating 34.2 billion United States dollars, and 134 million tourists are expected in 2030 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7HlFyOMb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Tourism Organization, 2014)","plainCitation":"(World Tourism Organization, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31342,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V2YAVGY3"],"itemData":{"id":31342,"type":"book","event-place":"Madrid","ISBN":"978-92-844-1675-2","publisher":"UNWTO","publisher-place":"Madrid","title":"Towards measuring the economic value of wildlife watching tourism in Africa - briefing paper","author":[{"family":"World Tourism Organization","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. During 2000 in East Africa alone, 1 billion United States dollars was generated from foreign tourist arrivals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HyhAk97O","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the United States of America, wildlife watching engaged 86 million people in the vicinity of their homes, and 81.1 million people travelled away from home to view wild species, generating 75.9 billion United States dollars ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TZhhbZIt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(U.S. Department of the Interior, U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, U.S. Department of Commerce, & U.S. Census Bureau, 2018)","plainCitation":"(U.S. Department of the Interior, U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, U.S. Department of Commerce, & U.S. Census Bureau, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31504,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZXISNTQB"],"itemData":{"id":31504,"type":"report","number":"FHW/16-NAT","title":"2016 National Survey of Fishing, Hunting and Wildlife-Associated Recreation","URL":"https://www.fws.gov/wsfrprograms/subpages/nationalsurvey/nat_survey2016.pdf","author":[{"family":"U.S. Department of the Interior","given":""},{"family":"U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service","given":""},{"family":"U.S. Department of Commerce","given":""},{"family":"U.S. Census Bureau","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Recreation represents over 75% of the value of the United States of America national forests, higher than the value of timber extracted ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RkOkLm4n","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Groom, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Groom, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34560,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RLLUNZ2M"],"itemData":{"id":34560,"type":"book","edition":"3rd Edition","title":"Principles of Conservation Biology","author":[{"family":"Groom","given":"Martha J."},{"family":"Meffe","given":"Gary K"},{"family":"Carroll","given":"C Ronald"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      Although tourism revenue is significant at the national level, for economic benefits to alleviate poverty, the World Tourism Organization (UNWTO) found local level employment, infrastructure benefits, supply of goods and services and support by the tourism enterprises, as well as other pro-poor approaches were important ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cV2t3iyn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. If local communities and suppliers are able to meet the standard needed to cater to international tourists, considerable benefits can accrue to the local economies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IxXWERfq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Twining-Ward, Li, Bhammar, & Wright, 2018; UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Twining-Ward, Li, Bhammar, & Wright, 2018; UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33813,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8ZD26HHH"],"itemData":{"id":33813,"type":"report","event-place":"Washington DC, USA","genre":"Tourism for Development","publisher":"The World Bank","publisher-place":"Washington DC, USA","title":"Supporting sustainable livelihoods through wildlife tourism","URL":"https://econpapers.repec.org/scripts/redir.pf?u=https%3A%2F%2Fopenknowledge.worldbank.org%2Fbitstream%2Fhandle%2F10986%2F29417%2F123765-WP-P157432-PUBLIC.pdf%3Fsequence%3D6;h=repec:wbk:wboper:29417","author":[{"family":"Twining-Ward","given":"Louise"},{"family":"Li","given":"Wendy"},{"family":"Bhammar","given":"Hasita"},{"family":"Wright","given":"Elisson"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, if supplies and expertise are sourced on imports, then 50% or more of the tourism revenue “leaks” from the local and national economies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"I1qiER20","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Wild species which have the biggest importance for the tourism and recreation practices are those which attract interest from the widest spectrum of tourists, i.e.,  the ‘flagship’ species – most often the megafauna, ‘charismatic’ mammals and birds, the ‘cute and cuddly’, dangerous predators, threatened species, and species that are believed to display intelligence ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NFVpUjkQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aguilera-Alcal\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}, Morales-Reyes, Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}n-L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez, Mole\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}n, & S\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}nchez-Zapata, 2020; Carr & Broom, 2018; Devillers & Beudels-Jamar, 2008; Higginbottom, 2004; Newsome, Moore, & Dowling, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Aguilera-Alcalá, Morales-Reyes, Martín-López, Moleón, & Sánchez-Zapata, 2020; Carr & Broom, 2018; Devillers & Beudels-Jamar, 2008; Higginbottom, 2004; Newsome, Moore, & Dowling, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31484,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VZH3TPN3"],"itemData":{"id":31484,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2020.106643","ISSN":"1470160X","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Indicators","language":"en","page":"106643","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Role of scavengers in providing non-material contributions to people","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1470160X2030580X","volume":"117","author":[{"family":"Aguilera-Alcalá","given":"Natividad"},{"family":"Morales-Reyes","given":"Zebensui"},{"family":"Martín-López","given":"Berta"},{"family":"Moleón","given":"Marcos"},{"family":"Sánchez-Zapata","given":"José Antonio"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}},{"id":31330,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PQFT9ATY"],"itemData":{"id":31330,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-1-78639-185-8","number-of-pages":"188","publisher":"CABI","title":"Tourism and animal welfare","author":[{"family":"Carr","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Broom","given":"Donald M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":31329,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LAY3RMKK"],"itemData":{"id":31329,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The Future of Drylands","ISBN":"978-1-4020-6970-3","page":"101–113","publisher":"Springer","title":"The Role of Megafauna Restoration in Dryland, Natural and Cultural Heritage Conservation","author":[{"family":"Devillers","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Beudels-Jamar","given":"Roseline C"}],"editor":[{"family":"Lee","given":"Cathy"},{"family":"Schaaf","given":"Thomas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":31190,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4LB2ZAP7"],"itemData":{"id":31190,"type":"book","call-number":"G156.5.E26 W54 2003","event-place":"Altona, Vic","ISBN":"978-1-86335-545-2","number-of-pages":"277","publisher":"Common Ground Publishing","publisher-place":"Altona, Vic","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wildlife tourism: impacts, management and planning","title-short":"Wildlife tourism","editor":[{"family":"Higginbottom","given":"Karen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":31334,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ELJ2RKD6"],"itemData":{"id":31334,"type":"book","ISBN":"1 853150-24-5","publisher":"Channel view publications","title":"Natural area tourism: Ecology, impacts and management","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Newsome","given":"David"},{"family":"Moore","given":"Susan A"},{"family":"Dowling","given":"Ross Kingston"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The growing awareness of biodiversity loss has created a demand to see places and wild species that might disappear, including “endangered experiences”, highly exclusive tourism packages offering unique opportunities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2j079dc37t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(WTTC, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(WTTC, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":494,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMAX4XCC"],"itemData":{"id":494,"type":"report","publisher":"World Travel & Tourism Council","title":"World, Transformed: Megatrends and their implications for travel and tourism","URL":"https://tourismknowledgecenter.com/publication/world-transformed-megatrends-and-their-implications-for-travel-tourism","author":[{"family":"WTTC","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, in Eurasia and Africa, national parks that hold large mammals have much higher visitation rates than those which do not ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"p21VT5xh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Devillers & Beudels-Jamar, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Devillers & Beudels-Jamar, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31329,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LAY3RMKK"],"itemData":{"id":31329,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The Future of Drylands","ISBN":"978-1-4020-6970-3","page":"101–113","publisher":"Springer","title":"The Role of Megafauna Restoration in Dryland, Natural and Cultural Heritage Conservation","author":[{"family":"Devillers","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Beudels-Jamar","given":"Roseline C"}],"editor":[{"family":"Lee","given":"Cathy"},{"family":"Schaaf","given":"Thomas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Difference in preferences for wild species has its roots in a range of evolutionary as well as cultural predispositions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"trjIgh69","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jacobs, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Jacobs, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31324,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QR9D8T3T"],"itemData":{"id":31324,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","DOI":"10.1080/10871200802545765","ISSN":"1087-1209","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","note":"publisher: Routledge","page":"1-11","title":"Why Do We Like or Dislike Animals?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/10871200802545765","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Jacobs","given":"Maarten H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While some countries have a long history of wildlife watching tourism (e.g., in the East and South Africa), recent rapid growth of this business has been observed in many new destinations, for example in Southeast Asia and the Amazon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"k6tyLGby","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Karanth, 2017; World Animal Protection, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Karanth, 2017; World Animal Protection, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30864,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TK46WBPJ"],"itemData":{"id":30864,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Contemporary Geographies of Leisure, Tourism and Mobility","container-title":"Nature tourism","ISBN":"978-1-317-33466-8","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"220","publisher":"Routledge","title":"14 Emerging Trends in Wildlife and Tiger Tourism in India","author":[{"family":"Karanth","given":"Krithi K"},{"family":"Jain","given":"Shivangi"},{"family":"Mariyam","given":"Dincy"}],"editor":[{"family":"Chen","given":"Joseph S"},{"family":"Prebensen","given":"Nina K"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33817,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TDB4MW37"],"itemData":{"id":33817,"type":"report","title":"A close up on cruelty: The harmful impact of wildlife selfies in the Amazon","URL":"https://www.worldanimalprotection.org/sites/default/files/int_files/amazon_selfies_report.pd","author":[{"family":"World Animal Protection","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Overall, natural areas of high value for wildlife watching tourism tend to be characterized by (i) abundance of large animals, (ii) presence of charismatic species, and (iii) high biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TXT6I6jV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Higginbottom, 2004; Newsome, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Higginbottom, 2004; Newsome, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31190,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4LB2ZAP7"],"itemData":{"id":31190,"type":"book","call-number":"G156.5.E26 W54 2003","event-place":"Altona, Vic","ISBN":"978-1-86335-545-2","number-of-pages":"277","publisher":"Common Ground Publishing","publisher-place":"Altona, Vic","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wildlife tourism: impacts, management and planning","title-short":"Wildlife tourism","editor":[{"family":"Higginbottom","given":"Karen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":31334,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ELJ2RKD6"],"itemData":{"id":31334,"type":"book","ISBN":"1 853150-24-5","publisher":"Channel view publications","title":"Natural area tourism: Ecology, impacts and management","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Newsome","given":"David"},{"family":"Moore","given":"Susan A"},{"family":"Dowling","given":"Ross Kingston"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is expected that presence of tourism in such areas will only be increasing, so special attention needs to be paid to aspects of sustainability in these processes. 
      Wildlife watching activities and tourism accrue considerable funds for conservation projects, as well as raising public awareness of the need for conservation. A case in point is Projeto Tamar which, through working with local communities and fishers, successfully promoted turtle conservation along the Brazilian coastline, improving turtle hatching success through protecting hatchery sites and establishing alternative employment and income opportunities based on tourism and turtle protection ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DrEkc74j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, a public-private partnership in a heavily poached region resulted in increased revenue for local communities and provided alternative revenue, to such a degree that wild species are again abundant in Majete Wildlife Reserve, Malawi ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bi8c2k8W","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Twining-Ward, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Twining-Ward, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33813,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8ZD26HHH"],"itemData":{"id":33813,"type":"report","event-place":"Washington DC, USA","genre":"Tourism for Development","publisher":"The World Bank","publisher-place":"Washington DC, USA","title":"Supporting sustainable livelihoods through wildlife tourism","URL":"https://econpapers.repec.org/scripts/redir.pf?u=https%3A%2F%2Fopenknowledge.worldbank.org%2Fbitstream%2Fhandle%2F10986%2F29417%2F123765-WP-P157432-PUBLIC.pdf%3Fsequence%3D6;h=repec:wbk:wboper:29417","author":[{"family":"Twining-Ward","given":"Louise"},{"family":"Li","given":"Wendy"},{"family":"Bhammar","given":"Hasita"},{"family":"Wright","given":"Elisson"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Conservation of one of the last remaining nesting sites of Little Penguins (Eudyptula minor) on Australia’s Phillip Island Nature Park, on Bunurong Aboriginal Land, is funded by an inclusive, collaborative business plan for tourism ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Cn7jAZTG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The business plan is revised every five years with the community and stakeholders and the Bunurong community representatives are involved in education programs and project supervision of a high-quality, high-volume tourist enterprise ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7V7Beq6s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
* Ecological aspects
Wildlife watching can have unintended consequences for wild species in three ways: changes to species behavior, changes to physiology, or damage to habitats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cdJVRxb7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Behavioral changes to wild species include changes to feeding or resting time, expending energy to try and move away from the disturbance, altering interactions between different species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CA1M5srd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, aggressive behavior, increased stress, or alternatively a reduction in fear towards humans, and dependency on non-natural and supplemental food sources especially at feeding sites ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wrrQeMKH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GERJ6WN8"],"itemData":{"id":493,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Purpose\\n              This paper aims to synthesize the key findings of prior research on the topic of human-wildlife interactions (HWI) in natural places for tourism.\\n            \\n            \\n              Design/methodology/approach\\n              The methodology of systematic review was used to search for, appraise and analyze the relevant research evidence. A total of 47 English-language academic journal articles, published between 2003 and 2018, with free online access to full texts in the database of Hospitality and Tourism Complete have been reviewed. Thematic analysis was adopted to synthesize the textual data.\\n            \\n            \\n              Findings\\n              The reviewed articles cover a wide geographical spread, diverse wildlife species and interaction types, and various research focuses including ecological impacts, human dimensions and management issues of wildlife tourism. The interactions between wildlife and human systems in the context of tourism constitute a complicated social-ecological system, in which both the humans and animals can be affected positively and negatively. Management and scientific research provide the nexus between the ecological and human dimensions of wildlife tourism. While opportunities for sustainable development abound, challenges are not to be neglected.\\n            \\n            \\n              Originality/value\\n              Due to the complexity of wildlife encounters for tourist purposes, the extant literature indicates a diverse and fragmented view from which integrated implications are difficult to obtain. This paper presents the first overarching review in English of the literature on human-wildlife interactions for tourism and provides a big picture understanding of what has been and what is needed to be done in terms of both wildlife tourism research and practices.","container-title":"Journal of Hospitality and Tourism Insights","DOI":"10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007","ISSN":"2514-9792","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JHTI","language":"en","page":"529-547","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism: a systematic review","title-short":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism","URL":"https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007/full/html","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dou","given":"Xueting"},{"family":"Day","given":"Jonathon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, or preventing optimal spatial distribution relative to resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1s97g6009d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Blanc, Guillemain, Mouronval, Desmonts, & Fritz, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Blanc, Guillemain, Mouronval, Desmonts, & Fritz, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31452,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C59NUU9E"],"itemData":{"id":31452,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Revue d'écologie","note":"publisher: Société nationale de protection de la nature et d'acclimatation de France …","title":"Effects of non-consumptive leisure disturbance to wildlife","author":[{"family":"Blanc","given":"Romain"},{"family":"Guillemain","given":"Matthieu"},{"family":"Mouronval","given":"Jean-Baptiste"},{"family":"Desmonts","given":"Diane"},{"family":"Fritz","given":"Herve"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Short-term changes in animal behavior as a result of human-wild species interactions in tourism contexts are easier to detect and well-studied, but long-term changes are under researched ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CvUBNZlc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GERJ6WN8"],"itemData":{"id":493,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Purpose\\n              This paper aims to synthesize the key findings of prior research on the topic of human-wildlife interactions (HWI) in natural places for tourism.\\n            \\n            \\n              Design/methodology/approach\\n              The methodology of systematic review was used to search for, appraise and analyze the relevant research evidence. A total of 47 English-language academic journal articles, published between 2003 and 2018, with free online access to full texts in the database of Hospitality and Tourism Complete have been reviewed. Thematic analysis was adopted to synthesize the textual data.\\n            \\n            \\n              Findings\\n              The reviewed articles cover a wide geographical spread, diverse wildlife species and interaction types, and various research focuses including ecological impacts, human dimensions and management issues of wildlife tourism. The interactions between wildlife and human systems in the context of tourism constitute a complicated social-ecological system, in which both the humans and animals can be affected positively and negatively. Management and scientific research provide the nexus between the ecological and human dimensions of wildlife tourism. While opportunities for sustainable development abound, challenges are not to be neglected.\\n            \\n            \\n              Originality/value\\n              Due to the complexity of wildlife encounters for tourist purposes, the extant literature indicates a diverse and fragmented view from which integrated implications are difficult to obtain. This paper presents the first overarching review in English of the literature on human-wildlife interactions for tourism and provides a big picture understanding of what has been and what is needed to be done in terms of both wildlife tourism research and practices.","container-title":"Journal of Hospitality and Tourism Insights","DOI":"10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007","ISSN":"2514-9792","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JHTI","language":"en","page":"529-547","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism: a systematic review","title-short":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism","URL":"https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007/full/html","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dou","given":"Xueting"},{"family":"Day","given":"Jonathon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, tourism effects on wild species individuals are more detectable and better documented than the repercussions of these individual effects at the population level ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ah689a4qp4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Blanc, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Blanc, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31452,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C59NUU9E"],"itemData":{"id":31452,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Revue d'écologie","note":"publisher: Société nationale de protection de la nature et d'acclimatation de France …","title":"Effects of non-consumptive leisure disturbance to wildlife","author":[{"family":"Blanc","given":"Romain"},{"family":"Guillemain","given":"Matthieu"},{"family":"Mouronval","given":"Jean-Baptiste"},{"family":"Desmonts","given":"Diane"},{"family":"Fritz","given":"Herve"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}
      The evasive nature of wild species together with tourists’ expectations for a close contact with wild species creates a strong incentive for tourist destination managers to minimize sighting uncertainty and decrease the watching distance through invasive practices ranging from baiting, attracting, and habituating, to capturing animals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GEW67g5D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dybsand, 2020; Knight, 2009; Margaryan & Wall-Reinius, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Dybsand, 2020; Knight, 2009; Margaryan & Wall-Reinius, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31335,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y3I8SSEF"],"itemData":{"id":31335,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.tourman.2020.104097","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"104097","title":"In the absence of a main attraction–Perspectives from polar bear watching tourism participants","volume":"79","author":[{"family":"Dybsand","given":"Hilde Nikoline Hambro"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":31328,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M738I4IE"],"itemData":{"id":31328,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Animals","DOI":"10.1163/156853009X418091","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Brill","page":"167–184","title":"Making wildlife viewable: habituation and attraction","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Knight","given":"John"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":31333,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QTIJ5RMP"],"itemData":{"id":31333,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","DOI":"10.1080/10871209.2017.1334842","issue":"5","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"406–421","title":"Commercializing the unpredictable: perspectives from wildlife watching tourism entrepreneurs in Sweden","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Margaryan","given":"Lusine"},{"family":"Wall-Reinius","given":"Sandra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and driving off-road ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6DZOmed2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nortje, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Nortje, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31315,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KK8PUINL"],"itemData":{"id":31315,"type":"thesis","event-place":"Pretoria","genre":"PhD Thesis","publisher":"University of Pretoria","publisher-place":"Pretoria","title":"Studies on the impacts of off-road driving and the influence of tourists' consciousness and attitudes on soil compactionand associated vegetation in the Makuleke Contractual Park, Kruger National Park","URL":"http://hdl.handle.net/2263/40223","author":[{"family":"Nortje","given":"Gerhardus Petrus"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Commercial wildlife watching activities rely on wild species being made viewable, which is often achieved through highly unsustainable and unethical practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xC60SnDY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dybsand, 2020; Knight, 2009; World Animal Protection, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Dybsand, 2020; Knight, 2009; World Animal Protection, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31335,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y3I8SSEF"],"itemData":{"id":31335,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.tourman.2020.104097","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"104097","title":"In the absence of a main attraction–Perspectives from polar bear watching tourism participants","volume":"79","author":[{"family":"Dybsand","given":"Hilde Nikoline Hambro"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":31328,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M738I4IE"],"itemData":{"id":31328,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Animals","DOI":"10.1163/156853009X418091","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Brill","page":"167–184","title":"Making wildlife viewable: habituation and attraction","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Knight","given":"John"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":33817,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TDB4MW37"],"itemData":{"id":33817,"type":"report","title":"A close up on cruelty: The harmful impact of wildlife selfies in the Amazon","URL":"https://www.worldanimalprotection.org/sites/default/files/int_files/amazon_selfies_report.pd","author":[{"family":"World Animal Protection","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, high tourist volumes in the Serengeti have created serious disturbance for wild species and the large area of the park makes it challenging to enforce responsible game watching behavior ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mzW8xDl7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In one case, the cubs of a cheetah were scared away by 15 vehicles and assumed to be predated on by lions as they were never seen again ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mrFPsa7D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Snorkeling and diving may also disturb the aquatic habitat and influence species behavior ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QSgDDa6S","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Teresa, Romero, Casatti, & Sabino, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Teresa, Romero, Casatti, & Sabino, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31357,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PE3BSLCF"],"itemData":{"id":31357,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-011-9641-4","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"960-968","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fish as Indicators of Disturbance in Streams Used for Snorkeling Activities in a Tourist Region","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-011-9641-4","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Teresa","given":"Fabricio Barreto"},{"family":"Romero","given":"Renato de Mei"},{"family":"Casatti","given":"Lilian"},{"family":"Sabino","given":"José"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The practice of fish feeding during diving may affect fish communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a7lJWUMm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ilarri, Souza, Medeiros, Grempel, & Rosa, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Ilarri, Souza, Medeiros, Grempel, & Rosa, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31355,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3GC54UP9"],"itemData":{"id":31355,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The effects of tourist visitation and food provisioning on fish assemblages were assessed by visual censuses (stationary technique) carried out in a tropical reef in Northeastern Brazil. Comparisons of species abundance, richness, equitability, and trophic structure in the presence (PT) and absence (AT) of tourists suggest that tourist visitation and supplementary food influenced the structure of the fish assemblage, as follows: (a) diversity, equitability and species richness were significantly higher on the AT period, while the abundance of a particular species was significantly higher during PT; (b) trophic structure differed between the AT and PT periods, omnivores being more abundant during the latter period, while mobile invertivores, piscivores, roving herbivores and territorial herbivores were significantly more abundant on AT. Reef tourism is increasingly being regarded as an alternative to generate income for human coastal communities in the tropics. Therefore, closer examination of the consequences of the various components of this activity to reef system is a necessary step to assist conservation and management initiatives.\\n          , \\n            Os efeitos da visitação turística e da alimentação suplementar sobre a ictiocenose foram avaliados por meio de censos visuais (técnica estacionária) em um recife tropical no nordeste do Brasil. Comparações entre a abundância das espécies, riqueza, equitabilidade e estrutura trófica na presença (PT) e na ausência (AT) de turistas sugerem que a visitação turística e a alimentação suplementar influenciam a estrutura da ictiocenose, como segue: (a) diversidade, equitabilidade e riqueza de espécies foram significativamente maiores no período AT, enquanto a abundância de uma única espécie foi significativamente maior durante o período PT; (b) a estrutura trófica foi diferente entre os períodos AT e PT, com os onívoros sendo mais abundantes no último período, enquanto invertívoros móveis, piscívoros, herbívoros errantes e herbívoros territoriais foram significativamente mais abundantes no período AT. O turismo em ambientes recifais é cada vez mais uma opção na geração de renda para diversas comunidades costeiras nos trópicos. Conseqüentemente, investigações mais detalhadas sobre as conseqüências dos vários componentes desta atividade sobre o sistema recifal são necessárias para subsidiar iniciativas de manejo e conservação.","container-title":"Neotropical Ichthyology","DOI":"10.1590/S1679-62252008000400014","ISSN":"1679-6225","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Neotrop. ichthyol.","language":"en","page":"651-656","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effects of tourist visitation and supplementary feeding on fish assemblage composition on a tropical reef in the Southwestern Atlantic","URL":"http://www.scielo.br/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S1679-62252008000400014&lng=en&tlng=en","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Ilarri","given":"Martina Di Iulio"},{"family":"Souza","given":"Allan Tainá","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Medeiros","given":"Paulo Roberto","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Grempel","given":"Renato Grotta"},{"family":"Rosa","given":"Ierecê Maria de Lucena"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A long-term, intensive study of the detrimental impacts on wild species from even well-managed, low level, commercial watching and controlled feeding of bottle-nosed dolphins at Monkey Mia, Western Australia documented long-term dolphin responses to human-wild species interaction. Over decades of monitoring, dolphin abundance (immigration and mortality) and fecundity declined at the tourism sites but not the control sites ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4mqImPHt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Higham & Bejder, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Higham & Bejder, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31483,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JZM6BPJN"],"itemData":{"id":31483,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Issues in Tourism","DOI":"10.2167/cit345.0","ISSN":"1368-3500, 1747-7603","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Current Issues in Tourism","language":"en","page":"75-83","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Managing Wildlife-based Tourism: Edging Slowly Towards Sustainability?","title-short":"Managing Wildlife-based Tourism","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.2167/cit345.0","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Higham","given":"James E.S."},{"family":"Bejder","given":"Lars"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Highly responsive management interventions were implemented based on the research recommendations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3DEpwhBa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Higham & Bejder, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Higham & Bejder, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31483,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JZM6BPJN"],"itemData":{"id":31483,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Issues in Tourism","DOI":"10.2167/cit345.0","ISSN":"1368-3500, 1747-7603","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Current Issues in Tourism","language":"en","page":"75-83","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Managing Wildlife-based Tourism: Edging Slowly Towards Sustainability?","title-short":"Managing Wildlife-based Tourism","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.2167/cit345.0","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Higham","given":"James E.S."},{"family":"Bejder","given":"Lars"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and impacts were reduced after regulations limiting the duration of feeding events ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7uc45dKV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Foroughirad & Mann, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Foroughirad & Mann, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31358,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4D2ETSQT"],"itemData":{"id":31358,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"To promote close encounters with wildlife, humans sometimes provision wild animals with food. However such practices can be harmful, and the impacts of human provisioning on wild animals can be dif?cult to determine, especially indirect effects such as those on the offspring of provisioned animals. In Shark Bay, Australia, a small subset of the resident population of bottlenose dolphins is regularly provisioned with ?sh handouts under the supervision of the West Australian Department of Environment and Conservation (DEC). Previous studies have shown that calves born to provisioned females experienced reduced care and higher mortality relative to calves of non-provisioned mothers. These results led to changes in the management practices in 1994, which we assessed the ef?cacy of by comparing (1) calf mortality before and after the intervention and (2) behavior of provisioned with non-provisioned dolphins in the population. Although calves born to provisioned females exhibited higher survivorship (86.7%) than before the intervention (23.1%, v2 = 9.05, df = 1, p = 0.003, N = 28), group differences in maternal and calf activity budgets were still observed over the course of calf development. Provisioned mothers provided less care to their calves and their calves appeared to compensate by foraging more and separating more from their mothers compared to their non-provisioned counterparts (N = 114 calves). Our study shows that careful regulation and reduced wildlife provisioning can increase calf survivorship, but behavioral development continues to be affected.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2013.01.001","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"242-249","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Long-term impacts of fish provisioning on the behavior and survival of wild bottlenose dolphins","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320713000037","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Foroughirad","given":"Vivienne"},{"family":"Mann","given":"Janet"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, findings of this nature are of great concern for the unknown long-term sustainability at other, especially high-intensity and/or low management tourism sites, for cetaceans and other wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"r4E4zPRe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020; Higham & Bejder, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020; Higham & Bejder, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GERJ6WN8"],"itemData":{"id":493,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Purpose\\n              This paper aims to synthesize the key findings of prior research on the topic of human-wildlife interactions (HWI) in natural places for tourism.\\n            \\n            \\n              Design/methodology/approach\\n              The methodology of systematic review was used to search for, appraise and analyze the relevant research evidence. A total of 47 English-language academic journal articles, published between 2003 and 2018, with free online access to full texts in the database of Hospitality and Tourism Complete have been reviewed. Thematic analysis was adopted to synthesize the textual data.\\n            \\n            \\n              Findings\\n              The reviewed articles cover a wide geographical spread, diverse wildlife species and interaction types, and various research focuses including ecological impacts, human dimensions and management issues of wildlife tourism. The interactions between wildlife and human systems in the context of tourism constitute a complicated social-ecological system, in which both the humans and animals can be affected positively and negatively. Management and scientific research provide the nexus between the ecological and human dimensions of wildlife tourism. While opportunities for sustainable development abound, challenges are not to be neglected.\\n            \\n            \\n              Originality/value\\n              Due to the complexity of wildlife encounters for tourist purposes, the extant literature indicates a diverse and fragmented view from which integrated implications are difficult to obtain. This paper presents the first overarching review in English of the literature on human-wildlife interactions for tourism and provides a big picture understanding of what has been and what is needed to be done in terms of both wildlife tourism research and practices.","container-title":"Journal of Hospitality and Tourism Insights","DOI":"10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007","ISSN":"2514-9792","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JHTI","language":"en","page":"529-547","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism: a systematic review","title-short":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism","URL":"https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007/full/html","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dou","given":"Xueting"},{"family":"Day","given":"Jonathon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,2]]}}},{"id":31483,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JZM6BPJN"],"itemData":{"id":31483,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Issues in Tourism","DOI":"10.2167/cit345.0","ISSN":"1368-3500, 1747-7603","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Current Issues in Tourism","language":"en","page":"75-83","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Managing Wildlife-based Tourism: Edging Slowly Towards Sustainability?","title-short":"Managing Wildlife-based Tourism","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.2167/cit345.0","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Higham","given":"James E.S."},{"family":"Bejder","given":"Lars"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      A similar activity has been conducted in the Negro River, in the Brazilian Amazon, directed to feeding the freshwater pink (or red) dolphin (Inia geoffrensis), but the potential effects of this activity on dolphin’s behavior are not well known, but potentially increase dolphin aggression and may be harmful to both the dolphins and tourists ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XqRBxioj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pinto de S\\\\uc0\\\\u225{} Alves, Andriolo, Orams, & de Freitas Azevedo, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Pinto de Sá Alves, Andriolo, Orams, & de Freitas Azevedo, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31500,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J5XKEI4L"],"itemData":{"id":31500,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Acta Ethologica","DOI":"10.1007/s10211-012-0132-2","ISSN":"0873-9749, 1437-9546","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"acta ethol","language":"en","page":"9-19","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Resource defence and dominance hierarchy in the boto (Inia geoffrensis) during a provisioning program","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10211-012-0132-2","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Pinto de Sá Alves","given":"Luiz Cláudio"},{"family":"Andriolo","given":"Artur"},{"family":"Orams","given":"Mark Bryan"},{"family":"Freitas Azevedo","given":"Alexandre","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. White sharks (Carcharodon carcharias) watching activities elicited curiosity and aggressive behaviors associated with feeding, leading the authors to advise against intentional feeding to avoid human-shark-cage associated incidents and the conditioning of sharks to boats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"D7e35xQo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Becerril\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a, Hoyos\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Padilla, Micarelli, Galv\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}n\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Maga\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}a, & Sperone, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Becerril?García, Hoyos?Padilla, Micarelli, Galván?Magaña, & Sperone, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31318,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/37ETNXT9"],"itemData":{"id":31318,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.3057","ISSN":"1052-7613, 1099-0755","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Aquatic Conserv: Mar Freshw Ecosyst","language":"en","page":"773-782","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The surface behaviour of white sharks during ecotourism: A baseline for monitoring this threatened species around Guadalupe Island, Mexico","title-short":"The surface behaviour of white sharks during ecotourism","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/aqc.3057","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Becerril?García","given":"Edgar E."},{"family":"Hoyos?Padilla","given":"Edgar M."},{"family":"Micarelli","given":"Primo"},{"family":"Galván?Magaña","given":"Felipe"},{"family":"Sperone","given":"Emilio"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Even relatively innocuous recreational activities can have an impact on animal behavior. Research using camera traps to assess the prevalence of human recreational activities in association with terrestrial mammal occurrence in a Canadian protected area showed avoidance of mountain biking by moose (Alces spp.) and grizzly bears (Ursus arctos), although all recorded mammal species avoided humans on trails, especially mountain bikes and motorized vehicles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pjgDrFDf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Naidoo & Burton, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Naidoo & Burton, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31364,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BU28ZKP4"],"itemData":{"id":31364,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Science and Practice","DOI":"10.1111/csp2.271","ISSN":"2578-4854, 2578-4854","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Conservat Sci and Prac","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Relative effects of recreational activities on a temperate terrestrial wildlife assemblage","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/csp2.271","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Burton","given":"A. Cole"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Even “silent activities” such as windsurfing may have impacts as they enable off-path access to otherwise “sanctuary” areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1o25nbhk92","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Blanc, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Blanc, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31452,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C59NUU9E"],"itemData":{"id":31452,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Revue d'écologie","note":"publisher: Société nationale de protection de la nature et d'acclimatation de France …","title":"Effects of non-consumptive leisure disturbance to wildlife","author":[{"family":"Blanc","given":"Romain"},{"family":"Guillemain","given":"Matthieu"},{"family":"Mouronval","given":"Jean-Baptiste"},{"family":"Desmonts","given":"Diane"},{"family":"Fritz","given":"Herve"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. But the presence of tourists and vehicles can be reduced through spatial or temporal zonation to provide sanctuary for wild species. The adverse impacts of high volumes of tourists and vehicles on wild species is managed in the Serengeti through strict park zonation, where certain areas are designated “No-Go” zones where no wildlife watching is allowed, “Intensive” and “Low Use” zones have designated tourism activities and “Wilderness” zones where no vehicles are allowed and low numbers of tourists do walking tours ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"P08g0dS9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Habituation (stress response decreases with repeated exposure to humans) or sensitization (stress response increases with repeated exposure to humans) varies across and within species, and with the type of stressor, the type of tourism, spatiotemporal aspects, life history traits and intraspecific characteristics ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Eo4IFgsn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Geffroy, Samia, Bessa, & Blumstein, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Geffroy, Samia, Bessa, & Blumstein, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31458,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VJRR89R6"],"itemData":{"id":31458,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2015.09.010","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"755-765","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"How Nature-Based Tourism Might Increase Prey Vulnerability to Predators","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534715002463","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Geffroy","given":"Benjamin"},{"family":"Samia","given":"Diogo S.M."},{"family":"Bessa","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Blumstein","given":"Daniel T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, African penguins (Spheniscus demersus) and Magellanic penguins (Sphenicus magellanicus) habituate to humans but yellow-eyed penguins (Megadyptes antipodes) sensitize to humans ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ikCVx39i","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Geffroy, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Geffroy, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31458,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VJRR89R6"],"itemData":{"id":31458,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2015.09.010","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"755-765","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"How Nature-Based Tourism Might Increase Prey Vulnerability to Predators","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534715002463","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Geffroy","given":"Benjamin"},{"family":"Samia","given":"Diogo S.M."},{"family":"Bessa","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Blumstein","given":"Daniel T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Thus, the impacts of repeated exposure to humans are extremely context-dependent and need to be assessed locally, as well as monitored over the long-term. This has important repercussions for wild species, as behavioral changes as a result of tourist-exposure may compromise their susceptibility to poaching or their risk of predation by other animals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zFx5uFzL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Geffroy, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Geffroy, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31458,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VJRR89R6"],"itemData":{"id":31458,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2015.09.010","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"755-765","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"How Nature-Based Tourism Might Increase Prey Vulnerability to Predators","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534715002463","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Geffroy","given":"Benjamin"},{"family":"Samia","given":"Diogo S.M."},{"family":"Bessa","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Blumstein","given":"Daniel T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Wild species’ physiology may be affected by tourism activities even though their behavioral patterns have not altered ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KDTxkCyt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GERJ6WN8"],"itemData":{"id":493,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Purpose\\n              This paper aims to synthesize the key findings of prior research on the topic of human-wildlife interactions (HWI) in natural places for tourism.\\n            \\n            \\n              Design/methodology/approach\\n              The methodology of systematic review was used to search for, appraise and analyze the relevant research evidence. A total of 47 English-language academic journal articles, published between 2003 and 2018, with free online access to full texts in the database of Hospitality and Tourism Complete have been reviewed. Thematic analysis was adopted to synthesize the textual data.\\n            \\n            \\n              Findings\\n              The reviewed articles cover a wide geographical spread, diverse wildlife species and interaction types, and various research focuses including ecological impacts, human dimensions and management issues of wildlife tourism. The interactions between wildlife and human systems in the context of tourism constitute a complicated social-ecological system, in which both the humans and animals can be affected positively and negatively. Management and scientific research provide the nexus between the ecological and human dimensions of wildlife tourism. While opportunities for sustainable development abound, challenges are not to be neglected.\\n            \\n            \\n              Originality/value\\n              Due to the complexity of wildlife encounters for tourist purposes, the extant literature indicates a diverse and fragmented view from which integrated implications are difficult to obtain. This paper presents the first overarching review in English of the literature on human-wildlife interactions for tourism and provides a big picture understanding of what has been and what is needed to be done in terms of both wildlife tourism research and practices.","container-title":"Journal of Hospitality and Tourism Insights","DOI":"10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007","ISSN":"2514-9792","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JHTI","language":"en","page":"529-547","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism: a systematic review","title-short":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism","URL":"https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007/full/html","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dou","given":"Xueting"},{"family":"Day","given":"Jonathon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Yellow-eyed penguins (Megadyptes antipodes) at unregulated tourism sites showed significantly higher stress-induced corticosterone concentrations, with lower breeding success and lower fledgling weights than penguins visited for monitoring purposes only ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gvycfeoG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ellenberg, Setiawan, Cree, Houston, & Seddon, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Ellenberg, Setiawan, Cree, Houston, & Seddon, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31481,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8NCD4WG5"],"itemData":{"id":31481,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"General and Comparative Endocrinology","DOI":"10.1016/j.ygcen.2007.02.022","ISSN":"00166480","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"General and Comparative Endocrinology","language":"en","page":"54-63","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Elevated hormonal stress response and reduced reproductive output in Yellow-eyed penguins exposed to unregulated tourism","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0016648007000858","volume":"152","author":[{"family":"Ellenberg","given":"Ursula"},{"family":"Setiawan","given":"Alvin N."},{"family":"Cree","given":"Alison"},{"family":"Houston","given":"David M."},{"family":"Seddon","given":"Philip J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The presence of roads and traffic can also increase animal stress levels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0F41zxQb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lunde, Bech, Fyumagwa, Jackson, & R\\\\uc0\\\\u248{}skaft, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Lunde, Bech, Fyumagwa, Jackson, & Røskaft, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31319,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2PKRCBW9"],"itemData":{"id":31319,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"African Journal of Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/aje.12302","ISSN":"01416707","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Afr. J. Ecol.","language":"en","page":"434-441","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Assessing the effect of roads on impala ( <i>Aepyceros melampus</i> ) stress levels using faecal glucocorticoid metabolites","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/aje.12302","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Lunde","given":"Even Tvede"},{"family":"Bech","given":"Claus"},{"family":"Fyumagwa","given":"Robert D."},{"family":"Jackson","given":"Craig R."},{"family":"Røskaft","given":"Eivin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A well-studied intensive tourism site at the Grand Cayman Islands where stingrays (Hypanus americanus) are visited and fed by recreational scuba divers since 1986 have shown haematological changes, increased parasite loads, high injury rates and open wounds from boat collisions, and major behavioral changes from being normally solitary to forming schools of 12-15 individuals, as well as switching to diurnal feeding at the dive sites ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MVU9OeC6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Most of the stingrays’ food now comes from divers and the reduced dietary diversity has compromised their disease resistance and immune response ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fDB12bCz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, these kinds of examples of poor tolerance of tourist activities by species are species, habitat, tour operator and regulator specific. For example, a comparison between the effects of provisioning and viewing on the Cayman stingrays, which has been shown as detrimental, against the highly self-regulated and limited number of shark-feeding tour operators in Fiji suggests no effects on bull shark (Carcharhinus leucas) fitness and health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XR4OKDrm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Healy, Hill, Barnett, & Chin, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Healy, Hill, Barnett, & Chin, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31320,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XMR5B5VL"],"itemData":{"id":31320,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2020.103964","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"103964","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A global review of elasmobranch tourism activities, management and risk","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X19302143","volume":"118","author":[{"family":"Healy","given":"Teleah Joy"},{"family":"Hill","given":"Nicholas James"},{"family":"Barnett","given":"Adam"},{"family":"Chin","given":"Andrew"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The trend in using wild species as photo props for “selfies” as photographic souvenirs has driven an increase in captive and handling of wild species, like slow lorises (Nycticebus spp.) in Asia, which have their teeth clipped to reduce the risk of injury to tourists, and results in early death ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WKwzvnVq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Osterberg & Nekaris, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Osterberg & Nekaris, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31507,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IH87JD4R"],"itemData":{"id":31507,"type":"report","genre":"TRAFFIC Bulletin","number":"27","page":"13-18","title":"The use of animals as photo props to attract tourists in Thailand: a case study of the slow loris Nycticebus spp.","URL":"https://www.traffic.org/site/assets/files/3008/traffic_pub_bulletin_27_1.pdf#page=17","author":[{"family":"Osterberg","given":"Petra"},{"family":"Nekaris","given":"K. A. I."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A study of three-toed sloths (Bradypus variegatus) in Brazil and Peru found each sloth was held by on average five tourists, often by the claws and had their limbs stretched and manipulated ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nq6wFKjr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carder, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Carder, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31508,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5LWSWLC9"],"itemData":{"id":31508,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The use of wild animals as photo props is prevalent across the globe and is widely recognised to represent a potential animal welfare concern. However, detailed information regarding the specific impacts of such activity on wild animal behaviour is currently lacking. Herein, we investigated how brown-throated three-toed sloths (Bradypus variegatus) were handled by tourists, and how sloths behaved during wildlife ‘selfies’ taken in Manaus, Brazil and Puerto Alegria and Iquitos in Peru. In total, we observed 17 sloths (during 70 focal observations) that were provided for use in wildlife selfies on 34 different tours. We found that an average number of 5 people held each sloth during each focal observation. For 48.6% of the time the sloths were handled in a way which involved physical manipulation of the sloths’ head and/or limbs and/or being held by the claws. From the eight different types of sloth behaviour observed, we found that the two types performed for the longest average duration of time were surveillance (55.3%) and limb stretching (12.6%). Our findings show that when being handled sloths were frequently held in ways that may compromise their welfare. Although to date the behaviour of sloths while being handled has not been reported in any published literature, in this study we document certain behaviours which may act as indicators of compromised welfare. We suggest that our data provides a potential baseline for future study into the behaviour and welfare of sloths.","container-title":"Animals","DOI":"10.3390/ani8110216","ISSN":"2076-2615","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Animals","language":"en","page":"216","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Impact of ‘Selfie’ Tourism on the Behaviour and Welfare of Brown-Throated Three-Toed Sloths","URL":"http://www.mdpi.com/2076-2615/8/11/216","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Carder","given":"Gemma"},{"family":"Plese","given":"Tinka"},{"family":"Machado","given":"Fernando"},{"family":"Paterson","given":"Suzanne"},{"family":"Matthews","given":"Neil"},{"family":"McAnea","given":"Laura"},{"family":"D’Cruze","given":"Neil"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wild species handled for long durations have been shown to display increased behavioral and physiological stress responses, leading to injury, stress and death ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CQFStuLU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. A. Baird, 2016)","plainCitation":"(B. A. Baird, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31510,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3KAF3V7P"],"itemData":{"id":31510,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Applied Animal Behaviour Science","DOI":"10.1016/j.applanim.2015.12.004","ISSN":"01681591","journalAbbreviation":"Applied Animal Behaviour Science","language":"en","page":"150-162","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Program animal welfare: Using behavioral and physiological measures to assess the well-being of animals used for education programs in zoos","title-short":"Program animal welfare","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0168159115003469","volume":"176","author":[{"family":"Baird","given":"Bonnie A."},{"family":"Kuhar","given":"Christopher W."},{"family":"Lukas","given":"Kristen E."},{"family":"Amendolagine","given":"Laura A."},{"family":"Fuller","given":"Grace A."},{"family":"Nemet","given":"James"},{"family":"Willis","given":"Mark A."},{"family":"Schook","given":"Mandi W."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Tourists and other recreational users of nature, especially in high volumes, can damage the environment and species habitats. Trampling vegetation and the creation of informal trails both damages the environment and reduces the appeal and restorative impact on human health and well-being of these areas to other recreational users ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"g8PS6Iij","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Taff, Benfield, Miller, D\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Antonio, & Schwartz, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Taff, Benfield, Miller, D’Antonio, & Schwartz, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31365,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VXIBNM2D"],"itemData":{"id":31365,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Parks and protected areas are recognized for the important ecosystem services, or benefits, they provide society. One emerging but understudied component is the cultural ecosystem services that parks and protected areas provide. These cultural ecosystem services include a variety of benefits, such as cultural heritage, spiritual value, recreation opportunities, and human health and well-being. However, many of these services can only be provided if people visit these parks and protected areas through tourism opportunities. However, with this tourism use comes a variety of inevitable resource impacts. This current research connects potential impacts from tourism in parks and protected areas to the health and well-being aspect of cultural ecosystem services. We used an MTurk sample to record affective responses across a range of resource conditions. Results demonstrate that as tourism-related ecological impacts increased, positive affect decreased. Decreases in positive affect were more severe for park and protected area scenes featuring informal and/or undesignated social trails when compared to scenes with increasing levels of trampling/vegetation loss. Collectively, the results show that managing tourism in parks and protected areas in a manner that reduces impact is essential to providing beneficial cultural ecosystem services related to human health and well-being.","container-title":"Environments","DOI":"10.3390/environments6040043","ISSN":"2076-3298","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Environments","language":"en","page":"43","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Role of Tourism Impacts on Cultural Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3298/6/4/43","volume":"6","author":[{"literal":"Taff"},{"literal":"Benfield"},{"literal":"Miller"},{"literal":"D'Antonio"},{"literal":"Schwartz"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There is evidence that scuba diving, even without fish feeding, may cause unintentional damage to aquatic organisms, such as corals and algae, which may be hit by divers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jjm3jtLI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Di Franco, Milazzo, Baiata, Tomasello, & Chemello, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Di Franco, Milazzo, Baiata, Tomasello, & Chemello, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31356,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U3I9DA4T"],"itemData":{"id":31356,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The effects of diving activity in different Mediterranean subtidal habitats are scarcely known. This study evaluates diver behaviour (for example time spent in each habitat), use (contacts made with the substrate) and immediate effects of diver contact on benthic species in a marine protected area (MPA) in Sicily. Over a two-year period, intentions of 105 divers were observed within seven subtidal habitats: algae on horizontal substrate, algae on vertical substrate, Posidonia oceanica, encrusted walls, caves, sand and pebbles. Divers selected a habitat in proportion to its availability along the scuba trail. On average, each diver made 2.52 contacts every seven minutes, and no differences were detected among the levels of diver scuba certi?cation. The highest rates of total and unintentional contacts were recorded on caves and encrusted walls, and the slow growing species Eunicella singularis and Astroides calycularis were the most frequently injured by divers. Most of the contacts were concentrated in the ?rst minutes of the dives. The identi?cation of diving effects in different habitats will enable management strategies to speci?cally control this impact at a habitat scale, for example restricting the start of the dive to low vulnerability habitats would reduce damage to benthic organisms, allowing sustainable use of MPAs.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892909005426","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"01","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Conserv.","language":"en","page":"32","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Scuba diver behaviour and its effects on the biota of a Mediterranean marine protected area","URL":"http://www.journals.cambridge.org/abstract_S0376892909005426","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Di Franco","given":"Antonio"},{"family":"Milazzo","given":"Marco"},{"family":"Baiata","given":"Pasquale"},{"family":"Tomasello","given":"Agostino"},{"family":"Chemello","given":"Renato"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The sunscreen from divers and swimmers has been associated with bleaching of coral reefs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XZLFu8wR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Danovaro, 2008; Downs, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Danovaro, 2008; Downs, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GQYIJH6Z"],"itemData":{"id":31331,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental health perspectives","DOI":"10.1289/ehp.10966","issue":"4","note":"publisher: National Institute of Environmental Health Sciences","page":"441–447","title":"Sunscreens cause coral bleaching by promoting viral infections","volume":"116","author":[{"family":"Danovaro","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Bongiorni","given":"Lucia"},{"family":"Corinaldesi","given":"Cinzia"},{"family":"Giovannelli","given":"Donato"},{"family":"Damiani","given":"Elisabetta"},{"family":"Astolfi","given":"Paola"},{"family":"Greci","given":"Lucedio"},{"family":"Pusceddu","given":"Antonio"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":31326,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WMYP2MK4"],"itemData":{"id":31326,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecotoxicology","DOI":"10.1007/s10646-014-1211-0.","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"175–191","title":"Toxicological effects of the sunscreen UV filter, benzophenone-2, on planulae and in vitro cells of the coral, Stylophora pistillata","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Downs","given":"CA"},{"family":"Kramarsky-Winter","given":"Esti"},{"family":"Fauth","given":"John E"},{"family":"Segal","given":"Roee"},{"family":"Bronstein","given":"Omri"},{"family":"Jeger","given":"Rina"},{"family":"Lichtenfeld","given":"Yona"},{"family":"Woodley","given":"Cheryl M"},{"family":"Pennington","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Kushmaro","given":"Ariel"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Hawaii has banned the use of sunscreens containing oxybenzone or octinoxate from the 1st of January 2021 with similar bans predicted to follow in other coral-reef containing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IRZMimB4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Raffa, Pergolizzi Jr, Taylor Jr, Kitzen, & Group, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Raffa, Pergolizzi Jr, Taylor Jr, Kitzen, & Group, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31323,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AYJRLDIX"],"itemData":{"id":31323,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Clinical Pharmacy and Therapeutics","DOI":"10.1111/jcpt.12778","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"134–139","title":"Sunscreen bans: Coral reefs and skin cancer","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Raffa","given":"Robert B"},{"family":"Pergolizzi Jr","given":"Joseph V"},{"family":"Taylor Jr","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Kitzen","given":"Jan M"},{"family":"Group","given":"NEMA Research"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Even a single vehicle driving on sandy beaches has been estimated to crush up to 0.75% of the intertidal population ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4QF04y6a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schlacher, Thompson, & Price, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Schlacher, Thompson, & Price, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31316,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q9SLYLPQ"],"itemData":{"id":31316,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1439-0485.2007.00156.x","ISSN":"0173-9565, 1439-0485","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Ecology","language":"en","page":"354-367","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Vehicles versus conservation of invertebrates on sandy beaches: mortalities inflicted by off-road vehicles on ghost crabs","title-short":"Vehicles versus conservation of invertebrates on sandy beaches","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1439-0485.2007.00156.x","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Schlacher","given":"Thomas A."},{"family":"Thompson","given":"Luke"},{"family":"Price","given":"Sam"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and beach camping zones show a 20.2% reduction in dune vegetation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1jrXeyUG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Thompson & Schlacher, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Thompson & Schlacher, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31509,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U5C7QFI8"],"itemData":{"id":31509,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Coastal Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s11852-008-0032-9","ISSN":"1400-0350, 1874-7841","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"J Coast Conserv","language":"en","page":"67-82","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Physical damage to coastal dunes and ecological impacts caused by vehicle tracks associated with beach camping on sandy shores: a case study from Fraser Island, Australia","title-short":"Physical damage to coastal dunes and ecological impacts caused by vehicle tracks associated with beach camping on sandy shores","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11852-008-0032-9","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Thompson","given":"Luke M. C."},{"family":"Schlacher","given":"Thomas A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      A review of winter recreational activities in Alpine areas found ski resorts and associated infrastructure have negative impacts across all studied taxa, independent of geographic region or ski modification ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1OhqhUTt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sato, Wood, & Lindenmayer, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Sato, Wood, & Lindenmayer, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31511,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/55KH9MBY"],"itemData":{"id":31511,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0064282","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e64282","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Effects of Winter Recreation on Alpine and Subalpine Fauna: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis","title-short":"The Effects of Winter Recreation on Alpine and Subalpine Fauna","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0064282","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Sato","given":"Chloe F."},{"family":"Wood","given":"Jeff T."},{"family":"Lindenmayer","given":"David B."}],"editor":[{"family":"Lindborg","given":"Regina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",5,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is concerning as the area affected in Europe by ski-runs is large and increasing, currently spanning about 4000 km across Italy, Switzerland and Austria, although there is a suggestion that environmentally-friendly ski-run design could mitigate many of these impacts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SJ1AMaBS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rolando, Caprio, Rinaldi, & Ellena, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Rolando, Caprio, Rinaldi, & Ellena, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31313,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T7E2W9CI"],"itemData":{"id":31313,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2664.2006.01253.x","ISSN":"00218901, 13652664","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"210-219","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The impact of high-altitude ski-runs on alpine grassland bird communities: Ski-runs and alpine grassland bird communities","title-short":"The impact of high-altitude ski-runs on alpine grassland bird communities","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1365-2664.2006.01253.x","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Rolando","given":"Antonio"},{"family":"Caprio","given":"Enrico"},{"family":"Rinaldi","given":"Enrico"},{"family":"Ellena","given":"Ivan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",11,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Tourism facilities (e.g., lodges, ablutions) and impacts from waste, as well as high water usage are concerns in the nature-based tourism industry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"s1jK9MET","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Despite initiatives to foster sustainable travel behaviors (e.g., carbon offsetting for unavoidable travel emissions) and attempts to improve the eco-efficiencies of tourism industries, tourism carbon emissions have increased at 3.3% annually ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oj8izJxq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sun, Lin, & Higham, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Sun, Lin, & Higham, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31363,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q49YFP6M"],"itemData":{"id":31363,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.tourman.2020.104161","ISSN":"02615177","journalAbbreviation":"Tourism Management","language":"en","page":"104161","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Managing tourism emissions through optimizing the tourism demand mix: Concept and analysis","title-short":"Managing tourism emissions through optimizing the tourism demand mix","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0261517720300856","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"Sun","given":"Ya-Yen"},{"family":"Lin","given":"Pei-Chun"},{"family":"Higham","given":"James"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, driven by increased travel frequency, long-haul flights and shorter stays per trip ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OhmFHNY6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sun, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Sun, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31363,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q49YFP6M"],"itemData":{"id":31363,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.tourman.2020.104161","ISSN":"02615177","journalAbbreviation":"Tourism Management","language":"en","page":"104161","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Managing tourism emissions through optimizing the tourism demand mix: Concept and analysis","title-short":"Managing tourism emissions through optimizing the tourism demand mix","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0261517720300856","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"Sun","given":"Ya-Yen"},{"family":"Lin","given":"Pei-Chun"},{"family":"Higham","given":"James"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Altering resource availability to wild species to increase watching potential can have unintended consequences on the surrounding ecosystem. The provision of artificial water points in Kruger National Park, South Africa, although intended to maintain herbivore numbers during droughts expanded the range of water-dependent species (e.g., zebra and wildebeest), and in association their predators (e.g., lions) to the detriment of less common species (e.g., roan antelope) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2IfBDzOa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harrington, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Harrington, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31503,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UFW6RCPE"],"itemData":{"id":31503,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/S0006-3207(98)00120-7","ISSN":"00063207","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"69-78","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Establishing the causes of the roan antelope decline in the Kruger National Park, South Africa","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320798001207","volume":"90","author":[{"family":"Harrington","given":"Rhidian"},{"family":"Owen-Smith","given":"Norman"},{"family":"Viljoen","given":"Petri C."},{"family":"Biggs","given":"Harry C."},{"family":"Mason","given":"Darryl R."},{"family":"Funston","given":"Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The widespread availability of surface water has also been implicated in the reduction of vegetation structure by homogenizing elephant impacts across the landscape ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"daQABTs5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gaylard, Owen-Smith, & Redfern, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Gaylard, Owen-Smith, & Redfern, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31502,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7FKZMWYU"],"itemData":{"id":31502,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The Kruger Experience: Ecology and Management of Savanna Heterogeneity.","event-place":"Washington DC, USA","ISBN":"978-1-59726-299-6","language":"English.","note":"OCLC: 1154833548","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington DC, USA","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Surface water availability: Implications for heterogeneity and ecosystem processes","URL":"https://catalogue.solent.ac.uk/openurl/44SSU_INST/44SSU_INST:VU1?u.ignore_date_coverage=true&rft.mms_id=9997124104904796","editor":[{"family":"Du Toit","given":"Johan T."},{"family":"Rogers","given":"Kevin H."},{"family":"Biggs","given":"Harry C."}],"author":[{"family":"Gaylard","given":"Angela"},{"family":"Owen-Smith","given":"Norman"},{"family":"Redfern","given":"Jessica"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      These unintended effects to facilitate watching wild species demonstrate the complexity of tourism impacts on populations and ecosystems. As these impacts are species and context specific, there is much to be discovered about the potential of tourism impacts. Even under the best code of conduct, there might still be detrimental, often cryptic, effects on animal reproduction and long-term survival ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Pn4o4r1I","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carr & Broom, 2018; Szott, Pretorius, Ganswindt, & Koyama, 2020; Tyagi, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Carr & Broom, 2018; Szott, Pretorius, Ganswindt, & Koyama, 2020; Tyagi, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31330,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PQFT9ATY"],"itemData":{"id":31330,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-1-78639-185-8","number-of-pages":"188","publisher":"CABI","title":"Tourism and animal welfare","author":[{"family":"Carr","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Broom","given":"Donald M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":31327,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8UGJI3RX"],"itemData":{"id":31327,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Wildlife Research","DOI":"10.1071/WR19045","issue":"1","note":"publisher: CSIRO","page":"34–43","title":"Physiological stress response of African elephants to wildlife tourism in Madikwe Game Reserve, South Africa","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Szott","given":"Isabelle D"},{"family":"Pretorius","given":"Yolanda"},{"family":"Ganswindt","given":"Andre"},{"family":"Koyama","given":"Nicola F"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":31332,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CGAPYQIB"],"itemData":{"id":31332,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Physiology","DOI":"10.1093/conphys/coz045","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Oxford University Press","page":"coz045","title":"Physiological stress responses of tigers due to anthropogenic disturbance especially tourism in two central Indian tiger reserves","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Tyagi","given":"Abhinav"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Vinod"},{"family":"Kittur","given":"Sagar"},{"family":"Reddy","given":"Mahender"},{"family":"Naidenko","given":"Sergey"},{"family":"Ganswindt","given":"Andre"},{"family":"Umapathy","given":"Govindhaswamy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A review of tourist impacts on wild species cautions that although the literature overwhelmingly reports on negative impacts, the findings are strongly dependent on the methods used and many findings (especially behavioral responses) could be interpreted as short-term coping strategies that do not necessarily have long-term repercussions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bo8CP4WY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bateman & Fleming, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Bateman & Fleming, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31501,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RLSPMATN"],"itemData":{"id":31501,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2017.05.003","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"10-19","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Are negative effects of tourist activities on wildlife over-reported? A review of assessment methods and empirical results","title-short":"Are negative effects of tourist activities on wildlife over-reported?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320717307711","volume":"211","author":[{"family":"Bateman","given":"Philip W."},{"family":"Fleming","given":"Patricia A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Considerable variation exists between and within species and locations, in tourism operator methods and regulations. Therefore, more serious and coordinated global multi-stakeholder efforts to regulate this practice, involving tourism businesses, local communities, science, governmental and non-governmental organizations, are needed. 
      
* Considerations for sustainable recreational use 
Based on the current trends one can expect further growth in demand for wildlife watching experiences and, consequently, an increasing number of wild species integrated into tourism operations. Particularly vulnerable in this perspective are the megafauna and ‘charismatic’ wild species, which, however, also receive the most media attention and conservation support ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iKNywmuN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carr & Broom, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Carr & Broom, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31330,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PQFT9ATY"],"itemData":{"id":31330,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-1-78639-185-8","number-of-pages":"188","publisher":"CABI","title":"Tourism and animal welfare","author":[{"family":"Carr","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Broom","given":"Donald M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Megafauna are the best studied taxa of animals, whereas there is a lack of research on the impacts of tourism on the lesser fauna, e.g., ground-dwelling mammals, small reptiles, insects, etc. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GABbmwXp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. D. Wolf, Croft, & Green, 2019)","plainCitation":"(I. D. Wolf, Croft, & Green, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31325,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZH5VK5XU"],"itemData":{"id":31325,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environments","DOI":"10.3390/environments6090104","issue":"9","note":"publisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"104","title":"Nature Conservation and Nature-Based Tourism: A Paradox?","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Wolf","given":"Isabelle D"},{"family":"Croft","given":"David B"},{"family":"Green","given":"Ronda J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The interlinkages between tourism, representations of wild species on social media, conservation and sustainability have acquired great importance and need further research and policy attention. Likewise, the role of environmental education in changing tourist attitudes and behavior requires further research attention ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"t8ozHymc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GERJ6WN8"],"itemData":{"id":493,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Purpose\\n              This paper aims to synthesize the key findings of prior research on the topic of human-wildlife interactions (HWI) in natural places for tourism.\\n            \\n            \\n              Design/methodology/approach\\n              The methodology of systematic review was used to search for, appraise and analyze the relevant research evidence. A total of 47 English-language academic journal articles, published between 2003 and 2018, with free online access to full texts in the database of Hospitality and Tourism Complete have been reviewed. Thematic analysis was adopted to synthesize the textual data.\\n            \\n            \\n              Findings\\n              The reviewed articles cover a wide geographical spread, diverse wildlife species and interaction types, and various research focuses including ecological impacts, human dimensions and management issues of wildlife tourism. The interactions between wildlife and human systems in the context of tourism constitute a complicated social-ecological system, in which both the humans and animals can be affected positively and negatively. Management and scientific research provide the nexus between the ecological and human dimensions of wildlife tourism. While opportunities for sustainable development abound, challenges are not to be neglected.\\n            \\n            \\n              Originality/value\\n              Due to the complexity of wildlife encounters for tourist purposes, the extant literature indicates a diverse and fragmented view from which integrated implications are difficult to obtain. This paper presents the first overarching review in English of the literature on human-wildlife interactions for tourism and provides a big picture understanding of what has been and what is needed to be done in terms of both wildlife tourism research and practices.","container-title":"Journal of Hospitality and Tourism Insights","DOI":"10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007","ISSN":"2514-9792","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JHTI","language":"en","page":"529-547","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism: a systematic review","title-short":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism","URL":"https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007/full/html","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dou","given":"Xueting"},{"family":"Day","given":"Jonathon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      Specific attention needs to be paid to the emergence of the so-called tourist-driven destinations, which appear spontaneously based on a spike in media popularity and uncontrolled tourist demand, rather than coordinated marketing efforts of the local tourism actors. In addition, the expansion of tourism into remote, ‘pristine’ areas needs to be managed and monitored to avoid detrimental impacts to sensitive and vulnerable species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DhU2dESD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As tourists prefer areas that are deemed ‘pristine’ (i.e., more ecologically and aesthetically sound), there are opportunities to increase recreational tourism by restoring ecosystems. For example, work in RAMSAR (the Convention of wetlands of international importance) listed wetlands in India suggest that annual recreational visits could increase by 13% if the water quality could be improved to maintain wild species and fisheries diversity and abundance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nS7fm98h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sinclair, Ghermandi, Moses, & Joseph, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Sinclair, Ghermandi, Moses, & Joseph, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":500,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FM7FF5C5"],"itemData":{"id":500,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.tourman.2018.10.018","ISSN":"02615177","journalAbbreviation":"Tourism Management","language":"en","page":"179-186","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recreation and environmental quality of tropical wetlands: A social media based spatial analysis","title-short":"Recreation and environmental quality of tropical wetlands","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S026151771830253X","volume":"71","author":[{"family":"Sinclair","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Ghermandi","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Moses","given":"Sheela A."},{"family":"Joseph","given":"Sabu"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Researchers have also highlighted the need for studies that integrate the ecological and social aspects of human-wild species interactions to inform the sustainable development of the tourism industry, local communities and wild species conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XO9mdUSW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GERJ6WN8"],"itemData":{"id":493,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Purpose\\n              This paper aims to synthesize the key findings of prior research on the topic of human-wildlife interactions (HWI) in natural places for tourism.\\n            \\n            \\n              Design/methodology/approach\\n              The methodology of systematic review was used to search for, appraise and analyze the relevant research evidence. A total of 47 English-language academic journal articles, published between 2003 and 2018, with free online access to full texts in the database of Hospitality and Tourism Complete have been reviewed. Thematic analysis was adopted to synthesize the textual data.\\n            \\n            \\n              Findings\\n              The reviewed articles cover a wide geographical spread, diverse wildlife species and interaction types, and various research focuses including ecological impacts, human dimensions and management issues of wildlife tourism. The interactions between wildlife and human systems in the context of tourism constitute a complicated social-ecological system, in which both the humans and animals can be affected positively and negatively. Management and scientific research provide the nexus between the ecological and human dimensions of wildlife tourism. While opportunities for sustainable development abound, challenges are not to be neglected.\\n            \\n            \\n              Originality/value\\n              Due to the complexity of wildlife encounters for tourist purposes, the extant literature indicates a diverse and fragmented view from which integrated implications are difficult to obtain. This paper presents the first overarching review in English of the literature on human-wildlife interactions for tourism and provides a big picture understanding of what has been and what is needed to be done in terms of both wildlife tourism research and practices.","container-title":"Journal of Hospitality and Tourism Insights","DOI":"10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007","ISSN":"2514-9792","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JHTI","language":"en","page":"529-547","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism: a systematic review","title-short":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism","URL":"https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007/full/html","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dou","given":"Xueting"},{"family":"Day","given":"Jonathon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Financial resources and operational experience are sorely needed at the human-wild species interaction interface, with many wild species populations attractive to tourists in countries least able to afford the research, management and monitoring needed in these sites ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JgfZvZsC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GERJ6WN8"],"itemData":{"id":493,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Purpose\\n              This paper aims to synthesize the key findings of prior research on the topic of human-wildlife interactions (HWI) in natural places for tourism.\\n            \\n            \\n              Design/methodology/approach\\n              The methodology of systematic review was used to search for, appraise and analyze the relevant research evidence. A total of 47 English-language academic journal articles, published between 2003 and 2018, with free online access to full texts in the database of Hospitality and Tourism Complete have been reviewed. Thematic analysis was adopted to synthesize the textual data.\\n            \\n            \\n              Findings\\n              The reviewed articles cover a wide geographical spread, diverse wildlife species and interaction types, and various research focuses including ecological impacts, human dimensions and management issues of wildlife tourism. The interactions between wildlife and human systems in the context of tourism constitute a complicated social-ecological system, in which both the humans and animals can be affected positively and negatively. Management and scientific research provide the nexus between the ecological and human dimensions of wildlife tourism. While opportunities for sustainable development abound, challenges are not to be neglected.\\n            \\n            \\n              Originality/value\\n              Due to the complexity of wildlife encounters for tourist purposes, the extant literature indicates a diverse and fragmented view from which integrated implications are difficult to obtain. This paper presents the first overarching review in English of the literature on human-wildlife interactions for tourism and provides a big picture understanding of what has been and what is needed to be done in terms of both wildlife tourism research and practices.","container-title":"Journal of Hospitality and Tourism Insights","DOI":"10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007","ISSN":"2514-9792","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JHTI","language":"en","page":"529-547","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism: a systematic review","title-short":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism","URL":"https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007/full/html","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dou","given":"Xueting"},{"family":"Day","given":"Jonathon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Finding ways to mobilize the power of new communication technologies and channel them towards sustainable tourism practices will be crucial in achieving more sustainable wildlife watching operations.
      Sustainable nature-based tourism needs to make a positive impact to the natural and social setting that tourism takes place in, and should generate benefit for the host communities and indigenous peoples and local communities in a manner that does not compromise the future human well-being needs of indigenous peoples and local communities or the ecosystems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KamyLfWX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Well-managed wildlife watching is a significant boon to community development and revenue, as well as an important source of funding for wild species conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5wE981JR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, tourism is only sustainable where the habitats and species being used recreationally are sufficiently resilient to the impacts related to the use, where tourism and the associated development is kept within manageable limits, where the tourism experience attracts a long-term and viable tourism economy, and where local communities and the local economy benefit from the activity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IxoqZrEP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The direct benefits range from increased income and employment through education and access to many new facilities, up to perception of pride and recognition. Although the direct economic benefits are most important to local residents ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eivjrT7d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Akis, Peristianis, & Warner, 1996)","plainCitation":"(Akis, Peristianis, & Warner, 1996)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31498,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MHLS9ZIY"],"itemData":{"id":31498,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Management","DOI":"10.1016/S0261-5177(96)00066-0","ISSN":"02615177","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Tourism Management","language":"en","page":"481-494","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Residents' attitudes to tourism development: the case of Cyprus","title-short":"Residents' attitudes to tourism development","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0261517796000660","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Akis","given":"Sevgin"},{"family":"Peristianis","given":"Nicos"},{"family":"Warner","given":"Jonathan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the indirect benefits such as improved public infrastructure, education, public safety and healthcare facilities may reach even wider groups of people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"v6l56o5H","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Afenyo & Amuquandoh, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Afenyo & Amuquandoh, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6FUM3SIN"],"itemData":{"id":31497,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Planning & Development","DOI":"10.1080/21568316.2013.864994","ISSN":"2156-8316, 2156-8324","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Tourism Planning & Development","language":"en","page":"179-190","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Who Benefits from Community-based Ecotourism Development? Insights from Tafi Atome, Ghana","title-short":"Who Benefits from Community-based Ecotourism Development?","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/21568316.2013.864994","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Afenyo","given":"Ewoenam Afua"},{"family":"Amuquandoh","given":"Francis Eric"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",4,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and gain the support for tourism among those who do not benefit directly from the activity. Addressing the above points to plan and manage sustainable nature-based tourism requires stakeholder engagement in a process that helps identify diverse interests, provides expertise, and facilitates local commitment to managing tourism ventures and impacts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Nqws8XB5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      An exemplary case study of stakeholder engagement in wild species tourism is that of Bunaken national marine park, Indonesia. Bunaken national marine park pioneered a co-management approach that is being modelled by other protected areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CEgKDwmD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Bunaken national marine park is a popular dive site for international tourists, as well as the home of 30,000 people whose livelihoods depend on fishing. Park management is overseen by a multistakeholder advisory board, including governmental, non-governmental organizations, representations of the villages within the park, park authorities, Tourism and Fisheries Departments, the local universities and the private tourism sector ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kGSqBrOa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Local community elders advised on the location of the marine sanctuaries and no-take zones, the local community is involved in reef restoration efforts, and advised where to place marine sanctuaries which replenish both diving and fishing sites. Proceeds from park fees are managed by the multistakeholder board and are used for conservation and development programs, village development schemes, plastic and waste disposal, environmental education of villagers, rehabilitation and restoration projects, and law enforcement and patrols for destructive fishing and tourism practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lcph9qbx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Stakeholder needs have evolved as the social and environmental landscape has changed, and management has recognized the need to be adaptive in this regard. In Bunaken, the growing popularity for tourism is placing additional stress on the reefs and the large numbers of dive operators are not all members of the stakeholder association. They are considering a mandatory license system rather than voluntary compliance to manage the number of divers, dive operators and boats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BuY26a4D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In another example, an unexpected repercussion of a successful public-private partnership in a heavily poached reserve has resulted in a tourism and revenue boom in Malawi’s Majete wildlife reserve, but the now abundant wild species are affecting local communities' resources, increasing human-wildlife conflict ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kHKiw1dh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Twining-Ward, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Twining-Ward, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33813,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8ZD26HHH"],"itemData":{"id":33813,"type":"report","event-place":"Washington DC, USA","genre":"Tourism for Development","publisher":"The World Bank","publisher-place":"Washington DC, USA","title":"Supporting sustainable livelihoods through wildlife tourism","URL":"https://econpapers.repec.org/scripts/redir.pf?u=https%3A%2F%2Fopenknowledge.worldbank.org%2Fbitstream%2Fhandle%2F10986%2F29417%2F123765-WP-P157432-PUBLIC.pdf%3Fsequence%3D6;h=repec:wbk:wboper:29417","author":[{"family":"Twining-Ward","given":"Louise"},{"family":"Li","given":"Wendy"},{"family":"Bhammar","given":"Hasita"},{"family":"Wright","given":"Elisson"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Stakeholders differing needs and perspectives need to be negotiated, as power imbalances between stakeholders can undercut effective collective management actions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uiAS73s4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Meza-Arce, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Meza-Arce, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31317,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JVZLL3F3"],"itemData":{"id":31317,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2020.104056","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"104056","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Unraveling the white shark observation tourism at Guadalupe Island, Mexico: Actors, needs and sustainability","title-short":"Unraveling the white shark observation tourism at Guadalupe Island, Mexico","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X19307353","volume":"119","author":[{"family":"Meza-Arce","given":"Mercedes I."},{"family":"Malpica-Cruz","given":"Luis"},{"family":"Hoyos-Padilla","given":"Mauricio E."},{"family":"Mojica","given":"Francisco J."},{"family":"Arredondo-García","given":"María Concepción"},{"family":"Leyva","given":"Claudia"},{"family":"Zertuche-Chanes","given":"Rebeca"},{"family":"Santana-Morales","given":"Omar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Recreational use may also be at odds with the extractive natural resource use needs of the local communities. This highlights the need to manage both physical and cultural conflicts between recreational users and indigenous peoples and local communities, through temporal or spatial zoning as well as by addressing the disparate cultural and social values of the respective stakeholders sensitively ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kKUZv6ej","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zeppel, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Zeppel, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":499,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VLHTWZXZ"],"itemData":{"id":499,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism and Hospitality Research","DOI":"10.1057/thr.2009.28","ISSN":"1467-3584, 1742-9692","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Tour Hosp Res","page":"93-104","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Managing cultural values in sustainable tourism: Conflicts in protected areas","title-short":"Managing cultural values in sustainable tourism","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1057/thr.2009.28","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Zeppel","given":"Heather"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Significant opportunities exist for tourism revenue to support indigenous peoples and local communities that are already involved in conservation practices through local and traditional practices. The Entim e Naimina Enkiyio (Forest of the Lost Child) is one of few ungazetted forests in Kenya supporting abundant wild species, including threatened and highly endemic species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"o7TQoXoz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tebtebba  Foundation, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Tebtebba  Foundation, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31453,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GFZ8XXJJ"],"itemData":{"id":31453,"type":"book","call-number":"SD411 .S87 2010","collection-title":"Indigenous Peoples, Forests & REDD plus","event-place":"Baguio City, Philippines","ISBN":"978-971-0186-01-3","number-of-pages":"2","publisher":"Tebtebba Foundation","publisher-place":"Baguio City, Philippines","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Sustaining & enhancing forests through traditional resource management","editor":[{"family":"Tebtebba  Foundation","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This site is estimated to have tourism potential of up to 40,000 United States dollars per annum, notwithstanding the other benefits supporting conservation would ensure, such as catchment protection, wild algae, fungi and plants, grazing, and spiritual and cultural value ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bHigv1LM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tebtebba  Foundation, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Tebtebba  Foundation, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31453,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GFZ8XXJJ"],"itemData":{"id":31453,"type":"book","call-number":"SD411 .S87 2010","collection-title":"Indigenous Peoples, Forests & REDD plus","event-place":"Baguio City, Philippines","ISBN":"978-971-0186-01-3","number-of-pages":"2","publisher":"Tebtebba Foundation","publisher-place":"Baguio City, Philippines","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Sustaining & enhancing forests through traditional resource management","editor":[{"family":"Tebtebba  Foundation","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The communities conserving biodiversity, as well as managing the natural resources for their subsistence, should be supported and strengthened where appropriate. 
      However, the benefits of tourism should not be overstated and require careful consideration of what is realistic ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qhD7RTMt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, in a survey of World Bank Global Environnement Facility projects, most projects had positioned tourism as key to sustainable resource management and wild species conservation, but only 8% of the projects analyzed the tourism-derived income potential ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aBA41lmz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A key finding of this World Bank survey was that although tourism did generate revenue, it could not be solely relied on and was not even the most important source of funding ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bjuR9kCO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      An economic model of the impacts of increased tourism revenue in the Philippines demonstrated that although economic benefits are accrued to all local households in the short-term, over the long-term increased demand for natural resources driven by the tourism industry eroded local household incomes, particularly for households directly involved in the natural resources economy ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bPeNVhAj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gilliland, Sanchirico, & Taylor, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Gilliland, Sanchirico, & Taylor, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31457,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DN22C7DW"],"itemData":{"id":31457,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.jenvman.2020.110968","ISSN":"03014797","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"110968","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Market-driven bioeconomic general equilibrium impacts of tourism on resource-dependent local economies: A case from the western Philippines","title-short":"Market-driven bioeconomic general equilibrium impacts of tourism on resource-dependent local economies","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0301479720308963","volume":"271","author":[{"family":"Gilliland","given":"Ted E."},{"family":"Sanchirico","given":"James N."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"J. Edward"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, tourism in Latin America was associated with increased agricultural expansion and deforestation to service tourist consumption ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iuvlfk34","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gunter & Ceddia, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Gunter & Ceddia, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31456,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T4IWA7N5"],"itemData":{"id":31456,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The present study investigates the role of ecotourism as a potential catalyst for land sparing in Latin America, with a particular focus on indigenous and community-based ecotourism. The research question is investigated within a comprehensive empirical land sparing–agricultural expansion framework, which uses the Jevons paradox as its theoretical foundation. It also allows for environmental governance and includes several socioeconomic control variables. In doing so, a panel data set based on secondary data from institutional sources and comprising 10 Latin American countries for the period from 1995 to 2015 is employed, which resulted in 209 observations in total. Panel estimation results show that there is only moderate evidence of land sparing associated with ecotourism, when it occurs on indigenous peoples’ and local communities’ land. To achieve land sparing through ecotourism, titling land to indigenous peoples and local communities as stakeholders is therefore crucial, but this beneficial effect should not be overestimated.","container-title":"Journal of Travel Research","DOI":"10.1177/0047287520949687","ISSN":"0047-2875, 1552-6763","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Travel Research","language":"en","page":"004728752094968","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Can Indigenous and Community-Based Ecotourism Serve as a Catalyst for Land Sparing in Latin America?","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0047287520949687","author":[{"family":"Gunter","given":"Ulrich"},{"family":"Ceddia","given":"M. Graziano"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Providing indigenous peoples and local communities with title deeds and land rights seemed to mitigate this effect, although the research authors caution this effect should not be overstated ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fr8nLzjT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gunter & Ceddia, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Gunter & Ceddia, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31456,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T4IWA7N5"],"itemData":{"id":31456,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The present study investigates the role of ecotourism as a potential catalyst for land sparing in Latin America, with a particular focus on indigenous and community-based ecotourism. The research question is investigated within a comprehensive empirical land sparing–agricultural expansion framework, which uses the Jevons paradox as its theoretical foundation. It also allows for environmental governance and includes several socioeconomic control variables. In doing so, a panel data set based on secondary data from institutional sources and comprising 10 Latin American countries for the period from 1995 to 2015 is employed, which resulted in 209 observations in total. Panel estimation results show that there is only moderate evidence of land sparing associated with ecotourism, when it occurs on indigenous peoples’ and local communities’ land. To achieve land sparing through ecotourism, titling land to indigenous peoples and local communities as stakeholders is therefore crucial, but this beneficial effect should not be overestimated.","container-title":"Journal of Travel Research","DOI":"10.1177/0047287520949687","ISSN":"0047-2875, 1552-6763","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Travel Research","language":"en","page":"004728752094968","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Can Indigenous and Community-Based Ecotourism Serve as a Catalyst for Land Sparing in Latin America?","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0047287520949687","author":[{"family":"Gunter","given":"Ulrich"},{"family":"Ceddia","given":"M. Graziano"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The Monarch Butterfly Forest Project is often lauded as a win-win-win success for tourism-livelihoods-conservation. Established in Mexico at forest locations where monarch butterflies (Danaus plexippus) congregate in winter, the project promoted recreation centers, established butterfly visitor centers and implemented tourism management at butterfly sanctuaries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jv8OgaWx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The project focuses on livelihood solutions for a region characterized by high unemployment, and has provided tourism training for local people, is involved in reforestation of areas critical to the butterfly habitat, and spans to managing logging impacts in Canada and the United States of America which threaten monarch summer habitat ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mbF8O0lB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Without detracting from the immense strides the Monarch Butterfly Project has made in livelihoods and conservation, in some areas there is evidence of local residents returning to extractive activities as the project failed to yield the expected employment opportunities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jfgBhfOA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barkin, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Barkin, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31499,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BECLBGLC"],"itemData":{"id":31499,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","DOI":"10.1023/A:1025725012903","ISSN":"1387585X","issue":"3/4","page":"371-382","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Alleviating Poverty Through Ecotourism: Promises and Reality in the Monarch Butterfly Reserve of Mexico","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1023/A:1025725012903","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Barkin","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although the rate of logging within the core areas of the Monarch Butterfly Biosphere Reserve have declined, logging is still present ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2627snvrkl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Flores-Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}nez, 2019; Vidal, L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a, & Rend\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}n\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Salinas, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Flores-Martínez, 2019; Vidal, López?García, & Rendón?Salinas, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31451,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZZ4YPZEU"],"itemData":{"id":31451,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Environmental Science","DOI":"10.3389/fenvs.2019.00167","ISSN":"2296-665X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Environ. Sci.","page":"167","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recent Forest Cover Loss in the Core Zones of the Monarch Butterfly Biosphere Reserve in Mexico","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fenvs.2019.00167/full","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Flores-Martínez","given":"José Juan"},{"family":"Martínez-Pacheco","given":"Anuar"},{"family":"Rendón-Salinas","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Rickards","given":"Jorge"},{"family":"Sarkar","given":"Sahotra"},{"family":"Sánchez-Cordero","given":"Víctor"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",11,1]]}}},{"id":31447,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BEKVCTK7"],"itemData":{"id":31447,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12138","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"177-186","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trends in Deforestation and Forest Degradation after a Decade of Monitoring in the Monarch Butterfly Biosphere Reserve in Mexico","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/cobi.12138","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Vidal","given":"Omar"},{"family":"López?García","given":"José"},{"family":"Rendón?Salinas","given":"Eduardo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, particularly small-scale logging ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a15nvpiebku","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez-Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a & Navarro-Cerrillo, 2020)","plainCitation":"(López-García & Navarro-Cerrillo, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31446,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SPFBLBYB"],"itemData":{"id":31446,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Forestry Research","DOI":"10.1007/s11676-019-00964-3","ISSN":"1007-662X, 1993-0607","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"J. For. Res.","language":"en","page":"1551-1566","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Disturbance and forest recovery in the Monarch Butterfly Biosphere Reserve, Mexico","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11676-019-00964-3","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"López-García","given":"José"},{"family":"Navarro-Cerrillo","given":"Rafael M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Monarch Biosphere Reserve zonation policies restricting community use of natural resources and the subsequent compensation for lost legal logging permits through payment for ecosystem services has had unintended consequences through provoking social conflict, often armed, in some areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a29h4rcj74o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gonzalez-Duarte, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Gonzalez-Duarte, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31445,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/84FE4Z37"],"itemData":{"id":31445,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2021.105420","ISSN":"0305750X","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","language":"en","page":"105420","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Butterflies, organized crime, and “sad trees”: A critique of the Monarch Butterfly Biosphere Reserve Program in a context of rural violence","title-short":"Butterflies, organized crime, and “sad trees”","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0305750X21000322","volume":"142","author":[{"family":"Gonzalez-Duarte","given":"Columba"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indeed, the local communities who were ancestral inhabitants of what is now core biosphere areas do not share the biosphere reserve paradigm of a binary use/non-use landscape, but instead view the relationship with the forest ecosystem as a continuum of co-inhabitation and Gonzalez-Duarte (2021) suggests the enforced split in ancestral ecological practices has supported a fractured social compact, fostered illicit extractive activities, undermined community forest management, encouraged organized crime and has created a disregard for core areas where monarch butterflies do not overwinter. For an overview of the challenges facing Mexico’s biosphere reserves, see Sada ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ata5p7mndf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2019)","plainCitation":"(2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31450,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KX7BNDUT"],"itemData":{"id":31450,"type":"chapter","container-title":"UNESCO Biosphere Reserves","ISBN":"978-0-429-42874-6","page":"47–48","publisher":"Routledge","title":"The Mexican biosphere reserves: Landscape and sustainability","author":[{"family":"Sada","given":"Sergio Guevara"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). These challenges are by no means unique to the Monarch Butterfly Biosphere Reserve and occur in many of the global biospheres reserves where integration of core conservation areas is not adequately incorporated into the multi-use, social-ecological system that the core reserves and local communities exist within ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a2c1rnk2ofa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Coetzer, Witkowski, & Erasmus, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Coetzer, Witkowski, & Erasmus, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31449,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5XE3AT8I"],"itemData":{"id":31449,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Reviews","DOI":"10.1111/brv.12044","ISSN":"14647931","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Biol Rev","language":"en","page":"82-104","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Reviewing Biosphere Reserves globally: effective conservation action or bureaucratic label?: Reviewing Biosphere Reserves globally","title-short":"Reviewing Biosphere Reserves globally","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/brv.12044","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Coetzer","given":"Kaera L."},{"family":"Witkowski","given":"Edward T. F."},{"family":"Erasmus","given":"Barend F. N."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Often local livelihoods are believed to be in conflicting relation with conservation and therefore they are highly restricted by the rules and regulation that impede local economic development ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HdLlP44R","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stone, 2015; P. West, Igoe, & Brockington, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Stone, 2015; P. West, Igoe, & Brockington, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PFPWI2JP"],"itemData":{"id":34354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ecotourism","DOI":"10.1080/14724049.2015.1023309","ISSN":"1472-4049, 1747-7638","issue":"2-3","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ecotourism","language":"en","page":"166-184","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community-based ecotourism: a collaborative partnerships perspective","title-short":"Community-based ecotourism","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/14724049.2015.1023309","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Stone","given":"Moren Tibabo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",9,2]]}}},{"id":492,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P2NBTA3B"],"itemData":{"id":492,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Anthropology","DOI":"10.1146/annurev.anthro.35.081705.123308","ISSN":"0084-6570, 1545-4290","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Anthropol.","language":"en","page":"251-277","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Parks and Peoples: The Social Impact of Protected Areas","title-short":"Parks and Peoples","URL":"http://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev.anthro.35.081705.123308","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"West","given":"Paige"},{"family":"Igoe","given":"James"},{"family":"Brockington","given":"Dan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Cases of prohibition of traditional activities that involve unsustainable use of natural resources in favor of conservation were reported in Tanzania ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qJWLIOAP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Charnley, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Charnley, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31480,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KQBA83ZJ"],"itemData":{"id":31480,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Organization","DOI":"00 18-7259/05/010075-14$1.90/1","issue":"1","title":"From Nature Tourism to Ecotourism? The case of the Ngorongoro Conservation Area, Tanzania","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Charnley","given":"Susan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Bangladesh ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"h89ZkVbO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Md. W. Islam, Rahman, Iftekhar, & Rakkibu, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Md. W. Islam, Rahman, Iftekhar, & Rakkibu, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31491,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2TER4JKT"],"itemData":{"id":31491,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ecotourism","DOI":"10.1080/14724049.2013.820309","ISSN":"1472-4049, 1747-7638","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ecotourism","language":"en","page":"119-129","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Can community-based tourism facilitate conservation of the Bangladesh Sundarbans?","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/14724049.2013.820309","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Islam","given":"Md. Wasiul"},{"family":"Rahman","given":"Md. Mozahidur"},{"family":"Iftekhar","given":"Md. Sayed"},{"family":"Rakkibu","given":"Md. Golam"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Botswana ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pNj7V3gj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sebele, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Sebele, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31492,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9KCEHFN3"],"itemData":{"id":31492,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.tourman.2009.01.005","ISSN":"02615177","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Tourism Management","language":"en","page":"136-146","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community-based tourism ventures, benefits and challenges: Khama Rhino Sanctuary Trust, Central District, Botswana","title-short":"Community-based tourism ventures, benefits and challenges","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0261517709000120","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Sebele","given":"Lesego S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and Nicaragua ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mI65c58T","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de los Angeles Somarriba-Chang & Gunnarsdotter, 2012)","plainCitation":"(de los Angeles Somarriba-Chang & Gunnarsdotter, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31490,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KAWCDQGN"],"itemData":{"id":31490,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Tourism","DOI":"10.1080/09669582.2012.681786","ISSN":"0966-9582, 1747-7646","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Sustainable Tourism","language":"en","page":"1025-1043","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Local community participation in ecotourism and conservation issues in two nature reserves in Nicaragua","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/09669582.2012.681786","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Angeles Somarriba-Chang","given":"Matilde","non-dropping-particle":"de los"},{"family":"Gunnarsdotter","given":"Yvonne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The high dependence on natural resource for self-subsistence ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GqhPNHCO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Belsky, 2009; Moswete, Thapa, & Lacey, 2009; Prachvuthy, 2006; Rozemeijer, 2000; Wunder, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Belsky, 2009; Moswete, Thapa, & Lacey, 2009; Prachvuthy, 2006; Rozemeijer, 2000; Wunder, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":491,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6GPY4DNL"],"itemData":{"id":491,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Rural Sociology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1549-0831.1999.tb00382.x","ISSN":"00360112","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"641-666","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Misrepresenting Communities: The Politics of Community-Based Rural Ecotourism in Gales Point Manatee, Belize","title-short":"Misrepresenting Communities","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/j.1549-0831.1999.tb00382.x","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Belsky","given":"JiU M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",10,22]]}}},{"id":490,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SF65BS7Y"],"itemData":{"id":490,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Sustainable tourism in Southern Africa: Local communities and natural resources in transition","event-place":"Bristol, U.K.","ISBN":"978-1-84541-108-4","note":"publisher: Clevedon, Channel View Publications: Bristol, UK","page":"189–209","publisher":"Channel view publications","publisher-place":"Bristol, U.K.","title":"Village-based tourism and community participation: A case study of the Matsheng villages in southwest Botswana","author":[{"family":"Moswete","given":"Naomi"},{"family":"Thapa","given":"Brijesh"},{"family":"Lacey","given":"Gary"}],"editor":[{"family":"Saarinen","given":"Jarkko"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":489,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U92ZDHZ8"],"itemData":{"id":489,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of GMS Development Studies","note":"publisher: Asian Development Bank","page":"25-40","title":"Tourism, Poverty, and Income Distribution: Chambok Community-based Ecotourism Development, Kirirom National Park, Kompong Speu Province, Cambodia","URL":"http://hdl.handle.net/11540/1742","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Prachvuthy","given":"Men"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":488,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/977U2QUF"],"itemData":{"id":488,"type":"chapter","container-title":"SNV/IUCN CBNRM Support Programme: Gaborone, Botswana","page":"17-20","title":"Community-based tourism in Botswana: The SNV experience in 3 community tourism projects","URL":"http://www.bibalex.org/Search4Dev/files/284060/116197.pdf","author":[{"family":"Rozemeijer","given":"Nico"}],"editor":[{"family":"Rozemeijer","given":"Nico"},{"family":"Gujadhur","given":"Tara"},{"family":"Motshubi","given":"Charles"},{"family":"Berg","given":"Elvia","non-dropping-particle":"van den"},{"family":"Flyman","given":"Michael Vosa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":485,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K6287AIC"],"itemData":{"id":485,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"CIFOR Occasional paper","ISSN":"0854-9818","issue":"21","page":"24","title":"Promoting forest conservation through ecotourism income: A case study from the Ecuadorian Amazon region","author":[{"family":"Wunder","given":"Sven"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} often give communities no choice but to engage in illegal activities. For example, in a case study in Central Amazonian Rainforest, Brazil, some of the families were reported to risk starving because fishing became very difficult and the large-scale agriculture was prohibited in the conservation area ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"E5zzOQtq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. B. Lima & d\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Hauteserre, 2011)","plainCitation":"(I. B. Lima & d’Hauteserre, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31478,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3DD2AB6I"],"itemData":{"id":31478,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Anatolia","DOI":"10.1080/13032917.2011.597933","ISSN":"1303-2917, 2156-6909","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Anatolia","language":"en","page":"184-203","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community capitals and ecotourism for enhancing Amazonian forest livelihoods","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/13032917.2011.597933","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Lima","given":"Ismar Borges"},{"family":"Hauteserre","given":"Anne-Marie","non-dropping-particle":"d'"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      It should be highlighted that nature-based tourism as a complementary activity that substitutes completely, or partially, unsustainable use of natural resources requires a fundamental re-organization of a community’s economic and social structure, which might trigger ideological opposition of those communities that have been relying on those activities for generations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"y4lIq0Dx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schweinsberg, Darcy, & Wearing, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Schweinsberg, Darcy, & Wearing, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31473,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XJUJJD49"],"itemData":{"id":31473,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ecotourism","DOI":"10.1080/14724049.2018.1502936","ISSN":"1472-4049, 1747-7638","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ecotourism","language":"en","page":"239-251","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Repertory grids and the measurement of levels of community support for rural ecotourism development","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/14724049.2018.1502936","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Schweinsberg","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Darcy","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Wearing","given":"Stephen L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Local communities who participate in nature-based tourism and receive tangible benefits tend to become cautious in their use of natural resources and, therefore, more likely to support tourism and conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DDll7te7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lindberg, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Lindberg, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31479,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I39S3BJN"],"itemData":{"id":31479,"type":"chapter","call-number":"G156.5.E26 E53 2001","container-title":"The encyclopedia of ecotourism","event-place":"Oxon, UK ; New York, NY","ISBN":"978-0-85199-368-3","publisher":"CABI Pub","publisher-place":"Oxon, UK ; New York, NY","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Economic impacts","editor":[{"family":"Weaver","given":"David B."}],"author":[{"family":"Lindberg","given":"K"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the employment in tourism must be high enough in terms of demand to maintain the workforce, and the financial benefits must be higher than gains from unsustainable activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wna0vL79","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kiss, 2004; Mbaiwa & Stronza, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Kiss, 2004; Mbaiwa & Stronza, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34282,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PY53E6DB"],"itemData":{"id":34282,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2004.03.010","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"232-237","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Is community-based ecotourism a good use of biodiversity conservation funds?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534704000655","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Kiss","given":"Agnes"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",5]]}}},{"id":31472,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MEZYND5W"],"itemData":{"id":31472,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Tourism","DOI":"10.1080/09669581003653500","ISSN":"0966-9582, 1747-7646","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Sustainable Tourism","language":"en","page":"635-656","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The effects of tourism development on rural livelihoods in the Okavango Delta, Botswana","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/09669581003653500","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Mbaiwa","given":"Joseph E."},{"family":"Stronza","given":"Amanda L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",5,25]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In destinations where community-based tourism is already in place, but it does not provide enough employment, the unsustainable use of resources is a common practice. The limited economic opportunities reduce or disable any incentives for conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RNqxqPYe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. G. Simmons, 1994)","plainCitation":"(D. G. Simmons, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31477,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/65Z74JR8"],"itemData":{"id":31477,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Management","DOI":"10.1016/0261-5177(94)90003-5","ISSN":"02615177","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Tourism Management","language":"en","page":"98-108","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community participation in tourism planning","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/0261517794900035","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Simmons","given":"David G"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Immediately after the incentives for tourism development or benefits from it decrease, local residents go back to previous livelihood-supporting extractive activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dl04Jfls","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. F. Wilkinson & Pratiwi, 1995)","plainCitation":"(P. F. Wilkinson & Pratiwi, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31471,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SI7C3I5"],"itemData":{"id":31471,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of Tourism Research","DOI":"10.1016/0160-7383(94)00077-8","ISSN":"01607383","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Annals of Tourism Research","language":"en","page":"283-299","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Gender and tourism in an Indonesian village","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/0160738394000778","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Wilkinson","given":"Paul F"},{"family":"Pratiwi","given":"Wiwik"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Direct employment is one of the most common limitations of many community-based tourism initiatives as often a small-scale project is not able to employ many people and still remain profitable. A study by Zapata ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FfCl2Ocv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2011)","plainCitation":"(2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31476,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JJ8RHJ5R"],"itemData":{"id":31476,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Issues in Tourism","DOI":"10.1080/13683500.2011.559200","ISSN":"1368-3500, 1747-7603","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Current Issues in Tourism","language":"en","page":"725-749","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Can community-based tourism contribute to development and poverty alleviation? Lessons from Nicaragua","title-short":"Can community-based tourism contribute to development and poverty alleviation?","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/13683500.2011.559200","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Zapata","given":"María José"},{"family":"Hall","given":"C. Michael"},{"family":"Lindo","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Vanderschaeghe","given":"Mieke"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",11]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} on 34 community-based tourism projects in Nicaragua reported that they were able to generate an average of 6.8 permanent jobs and 12.2 part-time positions. However, it should be stressed that what is considered low or high employment is highly situational as it depends on the size of the community and their direct needs. When resource consumption is prohibited within the protected area, the high dependence on resource extractive activities may also have adverse effect on resources surrounding the area, as demands intensify due to a shrinking resource base ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wIW4qIW3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Durbin & Ralambo, 1994; Parry & Campbell, 1992)","plainCitation":"(Durbin & Ralambo, 1994; Parry & Campbell, 1992)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31474,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FBC8JC9V"],"itemData":{"id":31474,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","ISSN":"03768929, 14694387","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"115–120","title":"The Role of Local People in the Successful Maintenance of Protected Areas in Madagascar","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/44518936","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Durbin","given":"Joanna C."},{"family":"Ralambo","given":"Jonny A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}},{"id":31475,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XQP8XT6W"],"itemData":{"id":31475,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental conservation","issue":"3","note":"publisher: JSTOR","page":"245–252","title":"Attitudes of rural communities to animal wildlife and its utilization in Chobe Enclave and Mababe Depression, Botswana","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/44518723","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Parry","given":"David"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"Bruce"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This might also have a negative effect on tourism itself that is based on supply of pristine landscape, biodiversity of animal and plant species. For example, in Wolong Nature Reserve, China, activities such as logging and clearing for fuelwood, agriculture, gathering of herbal medicinal plants, and ranching have significantly degraded and fragmented giant panda habitat that is the main tourism attraction offered by the local community-based tourism initiative ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"n9CRj9rd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. He, 2008)","plainCitation":"(G. He, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V9SCATWN"],"itemData":{"id":34493,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-008-9214-3","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"1017-1025","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Distribution of Economic Benefits from Ecotourism: A Case Study of Wolong Nature Reserve for Giant Pandas in China","title-short":"Distribution of Economic Benefits from Ecotourism","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-008-9214-3","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"He","given":"Guangming"},{"family":"Chen","given":"Xiaodong"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Wei"},{"family":"Bearer","given":"Scott"},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Shiqiang"},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Lily Yeqing"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Hemin"},{"family":"Ouyang","given":"Zhiyun"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Jianguo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Nature-based tourism, and the associated reliance on tourism-derived funds for conservation, is also sensitive to economic shocks. For example, as a result of the COVID-19 pandemic, the predicted loss in park entrance fees to the Galapagos Islands is expected to cost between 35-55% of total revenue, monies mainly allocated for conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CAJ7Gpfu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az-S\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}nchez & Obaco, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Díaz-Sánchez & Obaco, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31482,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CZZKPREZ"],"itemData":{"id":31482,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Policy Research in Tourism, Leisure and Events","DOI":"10.1080/19407963.2020.1813149","ISSN":"1940-7963, 1940-7971","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Policy Research in Tourism, Leisure and Events","language":"en","page":"1-5","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The effects of Coronavirus (COVID-19) on expected tourism revenues for natural preservation. The case of the Galapagos Islands","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/19407963.2020.1813149","author":[{"family":"Díaz-Sánchez","given":"Juan Pablo"},{"family":"Obaco","given":"Moisés"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Continued conservation in the Galapagos will require alternate sources of funding or loans ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XAlDUCsM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az-S\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}nchez & Obaco, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Díaz-Sánchez & Obaco, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31482,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CZZKPREZ"],"itemData":{"id":31482,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Policy Research in Tourism, Leisure and Events","DOI":"10.1080/19407963.2020.1813149","ISSN":"1940-7963, 1940-7971","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Policy Research in Tourism, Leisure and Events","language":"en","page":"1-5","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The effects of Coronavirus (COVID-19) on expected tourism revenues for natural preservation. The case of the Galapagos Islands","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/19407963.2020.1813149","author":[{"family":"Díaz-Sánchez","given":"Juan Pablo"},{"family":"Obaco","given":"Moisés"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The potential for detrimental effects of human-wild species interactions needs to be closely managed, requiring local community empowerment, supportive and cooperation from tour operators and enterprises, and buy-in from tourists ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"USQW3bbi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GERJ6WN8"],"itemData":{"id":493,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Purpose\\n              This paper aims to synthesize the key findings of prior research on the topic of human-wildlife interactions (HWI) in natural places for tourism.\\n            \\n            \\n              Design/methodology/approach\\n              The methodology of systematic review was used to search for, appraise and analyze the relevant research evidence. A total of 47 English-language academic journal articles, published between 2003 and 2018, with free online access to full texts in the database of Hospitality and Tourism Complete have been reviewed. Thematic analysis was adopted to synthesize the textual data.\\n            \\n            \\n              Findings\\n              The reviewed articles cover a wide geographical spread, diverse wildlife species and interaction types, and various research focuses including ecological impacts, human dimensions and management issues of wildlife tourism. The interactions between wildlife and human systems in the context of tourism constitute a complicated social-ecological system, in which both the humans and animals can be affected positively and negatively. Management and scientific research provide the nexus between the ecological and human dimensions of wildlife tourism. While opportunities for sustainable development abound, challenges are not to be neglected.\\n            \\n            \\n              Originality/value\\n              Due to the complexity of wildlife encounters for tourist purposes, the extant literature indicates a diverse and fragmented view from which integrated implications are difficult to obtain. This paper presents the first overarching review in English of the literature on human-wildlife interactions for tourism and provides a big picture understanding of what has been and what is needed to be done in terms of both wildlife tourism research and practices.","container-title":"Journal of Hospitality and Tourism Insights","DOI":"10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007","ISSN":"2514-9792","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JHTI","language":"en","page":"529-547","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism: a systematic review","title-short":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism","URL":"https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007/full/html","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dou","given":"Xueting"},{"family":"Day","given":"Jonathon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The management of the recreational use of wild species needs complementary enforcement and voluntary compliance measures, especially in the tourism context, managing human-wild species interactions is in effect managing people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3UTVD3GD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dou & Day, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GERJ6WN8"],"itemData":{"id":493,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Purpose\\n              This paper aims to synthesize the key findings of prior research on the topic of human-wildlife interactions (HWI) in natural places for tourism.\\n            \\n            \\n              Design/methodology/approach\\n              The methodology of systematic review was used to search for, appraise and analyze the relevant research evidence. A total of 47 English-language academic journal articles, published between 2003 and 2018, with free online access to full texts in the database of Hospitality and Tourism Complete have been reviewed. Thematic analysis was adopted to synthesize the textual data.\\n            \\n            \\n              Findings\\n              The reviewed articles cover a wide geographical spread, diverse wildlife species and interaction types, and various research focuses including ecological impacts, human dimensions and management issues of wildlife tourism. The interactions between wildlife and human systems in the context of tourism constitute a complicated social-ecological system, in which both the humans and animals can be affected positively and negatively. Management and scientific research provide the nexus between the ecological and human dimensions of wildlife tourism. While opportunities for sustainable development abound, challenges are not to be neglected.\\n            \\n            \\n              Originality/value\\n              Due to the complexity of wildlife encounters for tourist purposes, the extant literature indicates a diverse and fragmented view from which integrated implications are difficult to obtain. This paper presents the first overarching review in English of the literature on human-wildlife interactions for tourism and provides a big picture understanding of what has been and what is needed to be done in terms of both wildlife tourism research and practices.","container-title":"Journal of Hospitality and Tourism Insights","DOI":"10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007","ISSN":"2514-9792","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"JHTI","language":"en","page":"529-547","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism: a systematic review","title-short":"Human-wildlife interactions for tourism","URL":"https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JHTI-01-2020-0007/full/html","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dou","given":"Xueting"},{"family":"Day","given":"Jonathon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Instilling and supporting a sense of pride and custodianship of wild species amongst tour operators can facilitate responsible tourism. 
In summary, for sustainable recreational use of wild species there needs to be: 
1. low impact on the wild species being used
2. long-term monitoring of wild species populations and habitats 
3. long-term improvement in the livelihoods of local communities 
4. awareness and support for conservation from all stakeholders 
5. adaptive management and limits on “acceptable change” for wild species tourism, conservation and local communities, including the ability to limit further development 
6. supportive regulatory frameworks from local and national government ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BboLKWDb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} 
      As every tourism initiative is different, there is no single set of suitable conditions that enable both conservation and local livelihoods to flourish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1l5a7sqi38","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Beeton, 2008a; Faulkner & Tideswell, 1997; Etsuko Okazaki, 2008; Reimer & Walter, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Beeton, 2008a; Faulkner & Tideswell, 1997; Etsuko Okazaki, 2008; Reimer & Walter, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31439,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IZD8MWME"],"itemData":{"id":31439,"type":"book","event-place":"Collingwood, Victoria","publisher":"Landlinks Press","publisher-place":"Collingwood, Victoria","title":"Community development through tourism","author":[{"family":"Beeton","given":"S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":31443,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G6XL7DMZ"],"itemData":{"id":31443,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of sustainable tourism","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"3–28","title":"A framework for monitoring community impacts of tourism","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Faulkner","given":"Bill"},{"family":"Tideswell","given":"Carmen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}},{"id":31438,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/74RI7W9D"],"itemData":{"id":31438,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Tourism","DOI":"10.1080/09669580802159594","ISSN":"0966-9582, 1747-7646","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Sustainable Tourism","language":"en","page":"511-529","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A Community-Based Tourism Model: Its Conception and Use","title-short":"A Community-Based Tourism Model","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/09669580802159594","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Okazaki","given":"Etsuko"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9,8]]}}},{"id":31442,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AY52X9ET"],"itemData":{"id":31442,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tourism Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.tourman.2012.04.002","ISSN":"02615177","journalAbbreviation":"Tourism Management","language":"en","page":"122-132","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"How do you know it when you see it? Community-based ecotourism in the Cardamom Mountains of southwestern Cambodia","title-short":"How do you know it when you see it?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0261517712000659","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Reimer","given":"J.K. (Kila)"},{"family":"Walter","given":"Pierre"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, a number of factors emerged from a global analysis of over 100 community-based tourism case studies in natural areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1g3heduot3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yanes, Zielinski, Diaz Cano, & Kim, 2019; S. Zielinski, Kim, Botero, & Yanes, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Yanes, Zielinski, Diaz Cano, & Kim, 2019; S. Zielinski, Kim, Botero, & Yanes, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31441,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZYRARTHH"],"itemData":{"id":31441,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Many developing countries have recognized the potential of their natural resources for the development of tourism. However, the policies designed to provide a framework for socially inclusive and ecologically sound tourism turn out to be weak in fostering community-based tourism (CBT). There is a spectrum of conditions that lead to success or failure of CBT initiatives that should be accounted for by national policies to ensure meaningful community engagement. This paper proposes a framework for the evaluation of tourism policy documents based on factors that facilitate and constrain CBT in developing countries. The framework was applied to seven tourism policy documents in Colombia using a content analysis approach. The results showed that the policies are weak in providing a base for community engagement in CBT. International experiences indicate that the barriers to CBT are similar throughout the world; thus, the framework is useful in the evaluation of policy documents in other developing countries.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su11092506","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","language":"en","page":"2506","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community-Based Tourism in Developing Countries: A Framework for Policy Evaluation","title-short":"Community-Based Tourism in Developing Countries","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/11/9/2506","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Yanes","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Zielinski","given":"Seweryn"},{"family":"Diaz Cano","given":"Marlenny"},{"family":"Kim","given":"Seong-il"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4,29]]}}},{"id":31440,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HQ8DK6UV"],"itemData":{"id":31440,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Issues in Tourism","DOI":"10.1080/13683500.2018.1543254","ISSN":"1368-3500, 1747-7603","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Current Issues in Tourism","language":"en","page":"723-739","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Factors that facilitate and inhibit community-based tourism initiatives in developing countries","URL":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13683500.2018.1543254","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Zielinski","given":"Seweryn"},{"family":"Kim","given":"Seong-il"},{"family":"Botero","given":"Camilo"},{"family":"Yanes","given":"Andrea"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Aspects that are critical for a success are: 
1. the availability of financial resources 
2. skills and technical expertise 
3. political influence 
4. local control over land and resources 
5. community cohesion
6. involvement in local planning and management
      The external support provided by non-governmental organizations and governmental organization is crucial for ensuring the abovementioned conditions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pl89WDZN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Beeton, 2008b; E. Okazaki, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Beeton, 2008b; E. Okazaki, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":27057,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RQY6X94Y"],"itemData":{"id":27057,"type":"book","event-place":"Collingwood, Victoria","language":"en","publisher":"Landlinks Press","publisher-place":"Collingwood, Victoria","title":"Community development through tourism","author":[{"family":"Beeton","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":1866,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G6V8J6R3"],"itemData":{"id":1866,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Tourism","DOI":"10.1080/09669580802159594","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"511–529","title":"A Community-Based Tourism Model: Its Conception and Use","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/09669580802159594","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Okazaki","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The external factors enabling community-based tourism are the political will and decentralization of power and control to the community. 
      The main barriers for successful community-based tourism development are:
1. the lack of skills and expertise in areas required for tourism 
2. lack of noticeable improvement of quality of life in the community (health, education, economy)
3. lack of independence in the decision-making process
4. lack of participative decision making
5. lack of community control over land and resources
6. low level of control over tourism activities in the area
7. internal conflict within community
8. high dependence on resource consumptive activities
9. lack of significant employment in tourism, among others.

3.3.5.2.4. Education and learning

This section deals with the non-extractive practices of wild species for the production of knowledge (Chapter 1). The scope of this section is limited to the non-extractive practices of wild species for learning and education where the impact of this use has a measurable effect on the species. The text below is based on a literature review (Google Scholar) using the following keywords: wildlife, nature, environmental, education, and learning generating 119 000 hits. Articles that were recommended by citation databases were also considered, as well as collected from personal libraries and recommendations from experts. After a title and abstract read, 18 papers were selected for a full text read. After a full text read of these papers, 12 were deemed relevant and assessed for the literature review. Relevance was determined by mention of either the status (current), trend (historical) or impacts of use of a wild species (or taxa) in at least one dimension of sustainability (social, economic, or ecological) (see the data management report for Chapter 3 systematic literature review at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6452651). These data form the basis of the text below.
      Although the use of wild species and ecosystems for scientific research and environmental education, amongst other purposes, is certainly widespread, there is no indication whether this has increased over time or on the current status of use. Relevant articles represented all IPBES regions and most ecosystem types. The literature mostly addressed the use of ‘nature’ for education and learning, rather than a species/taxa specific approach, but where taxa were mentioned, they were either mammals (terrestrial and marine) or birds. There was little to no information in the literature about the sustainability or the effects of use on wild species or ecosystems. The exception was one article which mentioned concern over the routine use of outdoor teaching sites and their management plan to rotate use of environmentally sensitive areas as needed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ewOw1y3J","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ernst & Stanek, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Ernst & Stanek, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31694,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X6WPCWZM"],"itemData":{"id":31694,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","DOI":"10.1080/10871200600803010","ISSN":"1087-1209, 1533-158X","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","language":"en","page":"255-265","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Prairie Science Class: A Model for Re-Visioning Environmental Education within the National Wildlife Refuge System","title-short":"The Prairie Science Class","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/10871200600803010","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Ernst","given":"Julie (Athman)"},{"family":"Stanek","given":"Donna"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although undocumented, the non-extractive practices of wild species are likely to experience similar impacts to recreational watching of wild species such as stress-related responses from wild species and habitat damage through trampling (see section 3.3.5.2.3. Ecological aspects of recreational use). 
      There are two main methods of using wild species for learning and education. The first is via scientific research and the second through environmental education, mostly to school children and tourists, although a significant amount of informal, experiential learning and knowledge transfer occurs through other practices and uses of wild species, such as birdwatching (recreational use of wild species) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1r3c5fcc8f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zvonar & Weidensaul, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Zvonar & Weidensaul, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31644,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QZWVJLJV"],"itemData":{"id":31644,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Urban Environmental Education","page":"95-99","publisher":"Cornell university Civic Ecology Lab, NAAEE, EECapacity","title":"Bird study in urban environmental education","URL":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Angelique-Hjarding/publication/302877963_Urban_Planning_and_Environmental_Education/links/5732474208ae9ace84047dd9/Urban-Planning-and-Environmental-Education.pdf#page=97","author":[{"family":"Zvonar","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Weidensaul","given":"Amy"}],"editor":[{"family":"Russ","given":"Alex"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and urban foraging (gathering) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1qj967kaci","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. R. Poe, 2014)","plainCitation":"(M. R. Poe, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31615,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BBMRWGK2"],"itemData":{"id":31615,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Social & Cultural Geography","DOI":"10.1080/14649365.2014.908232","ISSN":"1464-9365, 1470-1197","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Social & Cultural Geography","language":"en","page":"901-919","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Urban foraging and the relational ecologies of belonging","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/14649365.2014.908232","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Poe","given":"Melissa R."},{"family":"LeCompte","given":"Joyce"},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",11,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Scientific use of wild species is generated through measuring faunal and floral diversity, and population structure and ecological processes. A review of “intellectual ecosystem services” generated by South African National Parks showed a bias towards research on animals, particularly mammals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FiTuQYac","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Smit, Roux, Swemmer, Boshoff, & Novellie, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Smit, Roux, Swemmer, Boshoff, & Novellie, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31691,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RH6PDQRF"],"itemData":{"id":31691,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2017.05.003","ISSN":"22120416","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystem Services","language":"en","page":"238-250","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Protected areas as outdoor classrooms and global laboratories: Intellectual ecosystem services flowing to-and-from a National Park","title-short":"Protected areas as outdoor classrooms and global laboratories","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2212041616302479","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Smit","given":"Izak P.J."},{"family":"Roux","given":"Dirk J."},{"family":"Swemmer","given":"Louise K."},{"family":"Boshoff","given":"Nelius"},{"family":"Novellie","given":"Peter"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, the journals that published research from protected areas were mostly mammal dominated, with little to no focus on social science, environmental governance or social-ecological studies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KTZCpv8j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Smit, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Smit, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31691,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RH6PDQRF"],"itemData":{"id":31691,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2017.05.003","ISSN":"22120416","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystem Services","language":"en","page":"238-250","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Protected areas as outdoor classrooms and global laboratories: Intellectual ecosystem services flowing to-and-from a National Park","title-short":"Protected areas as outdoor classrooms and global laboratories","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2212041616302479","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Smit","given":"Izak P.J."},{"family":"Roux","given":"Dirk J."},{"family":"Swemmer","given":"Louise K."},{"family":"Boshoff","given":"Nelius"},{"family":"Novellie","given":"Peter"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wild species use in education in Europe was dominated by threatened and charismatic species, such as wolves (Canis lupus), brown bears (Ursus arctos) and Imperial Eagles (Aquila adalberti) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KlPXfsf2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aguilera-Alcal\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Aguilera-Alcalá, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31484,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VZH3TPN3"],"itemData":{"id":31484,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2020.106643","ISSN":"1470160X","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Indicators","language":"en","page":"106643","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Role of scavengers in providing non-material contributions to people","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1470160X2030580X","volume":"117","author":[{"family":"Aguilera-Alcalá","given":"Natividad"},{"family":"Morales-Reyes","given":"Zebensui"},{"family":"Martín-López","given":"Berta"},{"family":"Moleón","given":"Marcos"},{"family":"Sánchez-Zapata","given":"José Antonio"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These cases highlight the paucity of research conducted on less “popular” taxa, such as fungi and invertebrates, forbs and shrubs. Notwithstanding, the public’s interest in charismatic species has been harnessed effectively for scientific research, such as in the analysis of data such as camera traps (e.g., https://www.zooniverse.org/) or in data collection such as atlas projects (e.g., http://sabap2.birdmap.africa/). These citizen science projects both solve significant big-data processing and collecting challenges facing scientists, as well as providing enjoyment and ecological education to interested citizens.
      The second major use of wild species for education and learning is environmental education. Here environmental education is defined as a process that allows individuals to explore environmental issues, engage in problem solving, and take action to improve the environment. As a result, individuals develop a deeper understanding of environmental issues and have the skills to make informed and responsible decisions (EPA 2018: https://www.epa.gov/education/what-environmental-education Accessed on 9 January 2021). Most of this literature focuses on education and on nature rather than wild species per se. 
      Most children today have more access to environmental knowledge through nature documentaries and films than all previous generations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8C22i3fb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hudson, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Hudson, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31680,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2WEMMB77"],"itemData":{"id":31680,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1641/0006-3568(2001)051[0283:CFEEIA]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0006-3568","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"BioScience","language":"en","page":"283","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Challenges for Environmental Education: Issues and Ideas for the 21st Century","title-short":"Challenges for Environmental Education","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/bioscience/article/51/4/283-288/247100","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Hudson","given":"Stewart J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ironically, such media-educated children in developed countries may fervently campaign for saving polar bears, cheetahs and whales, while they have almost no contact with wild animals or plants common in their own country ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ENzYNPsJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hudson, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Hudson, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31680,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2WEMMB77"],"itemData":{"id":31680,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1641/0006-3568(2001)051[0283:CFEEIA]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0006-3568","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"BioScience","language":"en","page":"283","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Challenges for Environmental Education: Issues and Ideas for the 21st Century","title-short":"Challenges for Environmental Education","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/bioscience/article/51/4/283-288/247100","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Hudson","given":"Stewart J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There was consensus in the research on environmental education, especially for school children, that educational programs that use the environment for learning supported improved attitudes toward the environment and a desire to look after the environment. An education program specifically on wild Bornean orangutans (Pongo pygmaeus wurmbii) led to 13.6-40.4% increase in student knowledge and more positive attitudes towards conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"btGgaLYH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Freund, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Freund, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31692,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P8JKTHGV"],"itemData":{"id":31692,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/acv.12513","ISSN":"1367-9430, 1469-1795","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Anim Conserv","language":"en","page":"18-27","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Conserving orangutans one classroom at a time: evaluating the effectiveness of a wildlife education program for school?aged children in Indonesia","title-short":"Conserving orangutans one classroom at a time","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/acv.12513","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Freund","given":"C. A."},{"family":"Achmad","given":"M."},{"family":"Kanisius","given":"P."},{"family":"Naruri","given":"R."},{"family":"Tang","given":"E."},{"family":"Knott","given":"C. D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In a study on primary and secondary school children in an environmental education program, 41% of students indicated their feelings about the environment had changed as a result of the nature-based excursion through a combination of observation and instruction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"j5DRHVOK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Ballantyne & Packer, 2002)","plainCitation":"(R. Ballantyne & Packer, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31696,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H2Y9G9HA"],"itemData":{"id":31696,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Research in Geographical and Environmental Education","DOI":"10.1080/10382040208667488","ISSN":"1038-2046, 1747-7611","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"International Research in Geographical and Environmental Education","language":"en","page":"218-236","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Nature-based Excursions: School Students' Perceptions of Learning in Natural Environments","title-short":"Nature-based Excursions","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/10382040208667488","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Ballantyne","given":"Roy"},{"family":"Packer","given":"Jan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Responses include: “I had a better understanding of the impact of people on the forests.” (15-year-old) and “Don’t feed the native wildlife.” (15-year-old).
      The benefits of using wild species for learning and education are considerable. In terms of ecological benefits, scientific research on wild species is applied by wild species managers to improve sustainable conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EMLYmmCr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Smit, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Smit, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31691,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RH6PDQRF"],"itemData":{"id":31691,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2017.05.003","ISSN":"22120416","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystem Services","language":"en","page":"238-250","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Protected areas as outdoor classrooms and global laboratories: Intellectual ecosystem services flowing to-and-from a National Park","title-short":"Protected areas as outdoor classrooms and global laboratories","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2212041616302479","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Smit","given":"Izak P.J."},{"family":"Roux","given":"Dirk J."},{"family":"Swemmer","given":"Louise K."},{"family":"Boshoff","given":"Nelius"},{"family":"Novellie","given":"Peter"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Learning in (and from) nature engenders pro-nature behaviors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nUR4WSKL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Richardson, 2016)","plainCitation":"(M. Richardson, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31344,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LK8SF2J5"],"itemData":{"id":31344,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PloS one","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0149777","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science San Francisco, CA USA","page":"e0149777","title":"30 days wild: Development and evaluation of a large-scale nature engagement campaign to improve well-being","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Richardson","given":"Miles"},{"family":"Cormack","given":"Adam"},{"family":"McRobert","given":"Lucy"},{"family":"Underhill","given":"Ralph"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} such as fostering a sense of responsibility and stewardship, and changing attitudes and behavior via increased ecological knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BZODnQPJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kwan, Cheung, Law, Cheung, & Shin, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Kwan, Cheung, Law, Cheung, & Shin, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31682,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/84523AY4"],"itemData":{"id":31682,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2016.12.002","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"53-65","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Conservation education program for threatened Asian horseshoe crabs: A step towards reducing community apathy to environmental conservation","title-short":"Conservation education program for threatened Asian horseshoe crabs","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1617138116302539","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Kwan","given":"Billy K.Y."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"Joe H.Y."},{"family":"Law","given":"Anniqa C.K."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"S.G."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Paul K.S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This knowledge can ripple outwards from the primary recipients and be transmitted to parents and neighbors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"26Gr856N","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vaughan, Gack, Solorazano, & Ray, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Vaughan, Gack, Solorazano, & Ray, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31690,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MHBAD2FA"],"itemData":{"id":31690,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Environmental Education","DOI":"10.1080/00958960309603489","ISSN":"0095-8964, 1940-1892","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"The Journal of Environmental Education","language":"en","page":"12-21","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Effect of Environmental Education on Schoolchildren, Their Parents, and Community Members: A Study of Intergenerational and Intercommunity Learning","title-short":"The Effect of Environmental Education on Schoolchildren, Their Parents, and Community Members","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/00958960309603489","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Vaughan","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Gack","given":"Julie"},{"family":"Solorazano","given":"Humberto"},{"family":"Ray","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Educational courses and formal training on wild species and nature can build constituencies with neighboring communities, indigenous peoples and local communities and other stakeholders, as well as capacity building for future wild species research and management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AEL2Mb7d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Smit, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Smit, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31691,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RH6PDQRF"],"itemData":{"id":31691,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Services","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoser.2017.05.003","ISSN":"22120416","journalAbbreviation":"Ecosystem Services","language":"en","page":"238-250","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Protected areas as outdoor classrooms and global laboratories: Intellectual ecosystem services flowing to-and-from a National Park","title-short":"Protected areas as outdoor classrooms and global laboratories","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2212041616302479","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Smit","given":"Izak P.J."},{"family":"Roux","given":"Dirk J."},{"family":"Swemmer","given":"Louise K."},{"family":"Boshoff","given":"Nelius"},{"family":"Novellie","given":"Peter"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Imparting environmental knowledge to tourists and students also provides employment, especially important when this is in local communities involved in these practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c8sv6C8e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ternes, Gerhardinger, & Schiavetti, 2016; UNEP/CMS, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Ternes, Gerhardinger, & Schiavetti, 2016; UNEP/CMS, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31426,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3FG8PB4V"],"itemData":{"id":31426,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-016-0125-8","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"52","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Seahorses in focus: local ecological knowledge of seahorse-watching operators in a tropical estuary","title-short":"Seahorses in focus","URL":"http://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-016-0125-8","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Ternes","given":"Maria L. F."},{"family":"Gerhardinger","given":"Leopoldo C."},{"family":"Schiavetti","given":"Alexandre"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}},{"id":497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5QRXFHQ"],"itemData":{"id":497,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","number":"CMS/ScS14/Inf.8","page":"86","publisher":"UNEP /   CMS Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Wildlife watching and tourism: A study on the benefits and risks of a fast growing tourism activity and its impacts on species","URL":"https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/document/ScC14_Inf_08_Wildlife_Watching_E_0.pdf","author":[{"family":"UNEP/CMS","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The aspects of engaging with wild species that contribute significantly to conservation education in the public include: watching wild species in their natural habitat, opportunities for close encounters with wild species, opportunities to observe natural wild species behavior, engaging the public emotionally, connection with the public’s prior knowledge and experiences, convincing communication, and establishing a link between everyday actions and changes to these actions people can make to foster conservation outcomes, and providing incentives and activities to support behavior change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jsIOTCJu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Ballantyne, Packer, Hughes, & Dierking, 2007)","plainCitation":"(R. Ballantyne, Packer, Hughes, & Dierking, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31695,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UY52E4BJ"],"itemData":{"id":31695,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Education Research","DOI":"10.1080/13504620701430604","ISSN":"1350-4622, 1469-5871","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Education Research","language":"en","page":"367-383","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Conservation learning in wildlife tourism settings: lessons from research in zoos and aquariums","title-short":"Conservation learning in wildlife tourism settings","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/13504620701430604","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Ballantyne","given":"Roy"},{"family":"Packer","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Hughes","given":"K."},{"family":"Dierking","given":"L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Beyond generating knowledge and awareness, there is concern on whether knowledge translates into action, and the longevity of pro-environmental awareness and behavior changes. In terms of longevity of pro-environmental awareness and attitudes, there is limited longitudinal research on this aspect. One study on the influence of a six-week bird feeding and monitoring program on school grounds showed that a year later, several schools had continued the program themselves, suggesting that such interventions have the potential to be maintained in the longer term ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MKAzTc2F","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. L. White, Eberstein, & Scott, 2018)","plainCitation":"(R. L. White, Eberstein, & Scott, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32114,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BCUK8PVJ"],"itemData":{"id":32114,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0193993","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e0193993","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Birds in the playground: Evaluating the effectiveness of an urban environmental education project in enhancing school children’s awareness, knowledge and attitudes towards local wildlife","title-short":"Birds in the playground","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0193993","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"White","given":"Rachel L."},{"family":"Eberstein","given":"Katie"},{"family":"Scott","given":"Dawn M."}],"editor":[{"family":"Ito","given":"Etsuro"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3,6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another example illustrates the benefits of a close engagement with a wild species over the longer term. Here secondary school students reared captive-born juvenile threatened Asian horseshoe crabs (Tachypleus tridentatus) for 14 months, which were then released into the wild ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cTB757z4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kwan, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Kwan, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31682,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/84523AY4"],"itemData":{"id":31682,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2016.12.002","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"53-65","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Conservation education program for threatened Asian horseshoe crabs: A step towards reducing community apathy to environmental conservation","title-short":"Conservation education program for threatened Asian horseshoe crabs","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1617138116302539","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Kwan","given":"Billy K.Y."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"Joe H.Y."},{"family":"Law","given":"Anniqa C.K."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"S.G."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Paul K.S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Rearing involved training students to collect data, test water conditions, and provided opportunities to improve on the protocols through experimentation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UP4QgPXT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kwan, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Kwan, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31682,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/84523AY4"],"itemData":{"id":31682,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2016.12.002","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"53-65","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Conservation education program for threatened Asian horseshoe crabs: A step towards reducing community apathy to environmental conservation","title-short":"Conservation education program for threatened Asian horseshoe crabs","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1617138116302539","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Kwan","given":"Billy K.Y."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"Joe H.Y."},{"family":"Law","given":"Anniqa C.K."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"S.G."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Paul K.S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The students were also tasked with presentations on the importance of horseshoe crabs and after the horseshoe crabs were released, tagged individuals could be tracked by students to monitor their movements and growth ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RSMVkKIr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kwan, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Kwan, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31682,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/84523AY4"],"itemData":{"id":31682,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2016.12.002","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"53-65","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Conservation education program for threatened Asian horseshoe crabs: A step towards reducing community apathy to environmental conservation","title-short":"Conservation education program for threatened Asian horseshoe crabs","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1617138116302539","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Kwan","given":"Billy K.Y."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"Joe H.Y."},{"family":"Law","given":"Anniqa C.K."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"S.G."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Paul K.S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The extended period of rearing and engagement with the horseshoe crabs engendered a strong emotional attachment and fostered a sense of responsibility, resulting in more pro-environmental attitudes and behavior ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KbpZwnuL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kwan, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Kwan, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31682,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/84523AY4"],"itemData":{"id":31682,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2016.12.002","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"53-65","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Conservation education program for threatened Asian horseshoe crabs: A step towards reducing community apathy to environmental conservation","title-short":"Conservation education program for threatened Asian horseshoe crabs","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1617138116302539","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Kwan","given":"Billy K.Y."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"Joe H.Y."},{"family":"Law","given":"Anniqa C.K."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"S.G."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Paul K.S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Both Ernst and Stank ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Kv7d8zeB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2006)","plainCitation":"(2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31694,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X6WPCWZM"],"itemData":{"id":31694,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","DOI":"10.1080/10871200600803010","ISSN":"1087-1209, 1533-158X","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","language":"en","page":"255-265","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Prairie Science Class: A Model for Re-Visioning Environmental Education within the National Wildlife Refuge System","title-short":"The Prairie Science Class","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/10871200600803010","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Ernst","given":"Julie (Athman)"},{"family":"Stanek","given":"Donna"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",8]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Freund, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4pAY42cc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2020)","plainCitation":"(2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31692,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P8JKTHGV"],"itemData":{"id":31692,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/acv.12513","ISSN":"1367-9430, 1469-1795","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Anim Conserv","language":"en","page":"18-27","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Conserving orangutans one classroom at a time: evaluating the effectiveness of a wildlife education program for school?aged children in Indonesia","title-short":"Conserving orangutans one classroom at a time","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/acv.12513","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Freund","given":"C. A."},{"family":"Achmad","given":"M."},{"family":"Kanisius","given":"P."},{"family":"Naruri","given":"R."},{"fam