    ipbes:hasDescription """
The Chapter is based on the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services framework of nature's contribution to people and further develops ideas within this framework that are relevant to understanding the drivers of use of wild species. This chapter in particular assesses the status and trends of drivers of use of wild species that lead to sustainable or unsustainable outcomes. The most important factors that contribute to ecosystem degradation are technological advances and changing social dynamics and may be equated with the five major indirect drivers of ecosystem degradation identified by the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment, namely changes in population, economic activity (which increased nearly sevenfold between 1950 and 2000), socio-political factors, cultural factors, and technological changes. These factors do not directly degrade ecosystems but operate more diffusely by amplifying and promoting the direct drivers of ecosystem degradation.

4.2.1  Environmental drivers 
4.2.1.1 Overview
This section discusses drivers that are directly linked with the so-called “natural” environment: climate change and hydrometeorological hazards, land and ecosystem degradation, invasive alien species, land and seascape change, pollution, and environmental hazards. Globally, climate change and related hazards have adversely impacted biodiversity and terrestrial, marine, and freshwater ecosystems. Although there is little direct evidence to show how climate change has, and will, affect wild species use, climate change and associated hazards have already, and are expected to further affect, food production systems, energy systems, water availability, and human health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oMftHWkB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoegh-Guldberg, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hoegh-Guldberg, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32304,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DC9XB7R5"],"itemData":{"id":32304,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Global Warming of 1.5°C","language":"English","publisher":"IPCC","title":"Impacts of 1.5ºC global warming on natural and human systems","author":[{"family":"Hoegh-Guldberg","given":"Ove"},{"family":"Jacob","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Bindi","given":"Marco"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Sally"},{"family":"Camilloni","given":"Ines"},{"family":"Diedhiou","given":"Arona"},{"family":"Djalante","given":"Riyanti"},{"family":"Ebi","given":"Kristie L."},{"family":"Engelbrecht","given":"Francois"},{"family":"Guiot","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Hijioka","given":"Yasuaki"},{"family":"Mehrotra","given":"Shagun"},{"family":"Payne","given":"Antony"},{"family":"Seneviratne","given":"Sonia I."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Adelle"},{"family":"Warren","given":"Rachel F."},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Guangsheng"},{"family":"Tschakert","given":"Petra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which in turn impact how wild species are used. Land degradation, especially degradation of forests, rangelands, and croplands worldwide has affected the capacity of nature to produce provisioning services and the availability and abundance of wild species. Conversion of grassland, savanna, and forests mainly tropical forests to agriculture, aquaculture, and urban development has destroyed the primary habitats of species causing many species to be endangered. It also leads to the decline of commercially traded species. The expansion and intensification of agricultural lands and grazing lands have positive and negative impacts on sustainable use of wild species. Intensification increases production capacity and reduces the dependence on wild resources while it also has costs—release of pollutants into the environment and increased greenhouse gases. In marine and freshwater ecosystems, many pollutants accumulate in organisms and when humans consume food from polluted waters, they are exposed to pollutants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9FxDz43L","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sonne, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Sonne, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32302,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R2YTNFMD"],"itemData":{"id":32302,"type":"book","source":"ResearchGate","title":"Biological effects of contaminant exposure in Arctic wildlife and fish","author":[{"family":"Sonne","given":"Christian"},{"family":"Basu","given":"Niladri"},{"family":"Desforges","given":"jean-pierre"},{"family":"Dietz","given":"Rune"},{"family":"Eulaers","given":"Igor"},{"family":"Jenssen","given":"Bjørn"},{"family":"Letcher","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Barst","given":"Benjamin"},{"family":"Bustnes","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Bytingsvik","given":"Jenny"},{"family":"Ciesielski","given":"Tomasz"},{"family":"Drevnick","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Haarr","given":"Ane"},{"family":"Hylaand","given":"Ketil"},{"family":"Mallory","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Pedersen","given":"Kathrine"},{"family":"Provencher","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Tartu","given":"Sabrina"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10,16]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This has serious implications to people who consume large amounts of fish and sea food, for example indigenous peoples and local communities who consume the blubber of marine mammals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tgMdfkvi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Donaldson, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Donaldson, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32303,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CLR9ERIS"],"itemData":{"id":32303,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science of The Total Environment","DOI":"10.1016/j.scitotenv.2012.05.008","journalAbbreviation":"Science of The Total Environment","page":"437–438","title":"Corrigendum to “Environmental contaminants and human health in the Canadian Arctic” [Sci. Total Environ. 408 (2010) 5165–5234]","volume":"431","author":[{"family":"Donaldson","given":"S.G."},{"family":"Van Oostdam","given":"Jay"},{"family":"Tikhonov","given":"Constantine"},{"family":"Feeley","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Armstrong","given":"B."},{"family":"Ayotte","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Boucher","given":"Olivier"},{"family":"Bowers","given":"Wayne"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Laurie"},{"family":"Dallaire","given":"Frederic"},{"family":"Dallaire","given":"R."},{"family":"Dewailly","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Edwards","given":"J."},{"family":"Egeland","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Fontaine","given":"J."},{"family":"Furgal","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Leech","given":"T."},{"family":"Loring","given":"E."},{"family":"Muckle","given":"Gina"},{"family":"Shearer","given":"R.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",8,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Globally, urban areas are expanding at twice the rate of their population, and residential development is a leading driver of land-use change that has severe implications for biodiversity and wild species populations. Urbanization has negatively affected the abundance of species and caused even micro-evolutionary changes. Urbanization occurring in coastal areas has adversely affected the diversity, life history, survival, reproduction and growth of many aquatic species. Likewise, building of dams has substantially impacted on riverine ecosystems and affected forests and caused species losses.
      On the other hand, there has been a growth in urban greenery, an increase of production and consumption of organic foods, and agroforestry practice worldwide. This has a positive impact on resource use, but the scale is very small, compared to the degradation of natural habitats. Natural hazards, including geological or geophysical hazards that originate from internal earth processes (earthquakes, volcanic activities, landslides, tsunamis), as well as biological hazards including zoonotic diseases, have had significant impacts on ecosystems and species and by consequence use of wild species.  These hazard events arise from, or are exacerbated by, increased human interactions with their environment, and prompt us to re-examine the relationship between people and wild species. 
      
 4.2.1.2. Climate change and hydrometeorological hazards
4.2.1.2.1. Overview
It is estimated that our planet has experienced human-induced warming of approximately 1°C since 1880 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HHLsaoRr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoegh-Guldberg, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hoegh-Guldberg, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32304,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DC9XB7R5"],"itemData":{"id":32304,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Global Warming of 1.5°C","language":"English","publisher":"IPCC","title":"Impacts of 1.5ºC global warming on natural and human systems","author":[{"family":"Hoegh-Guldberg","given":"Ove"},{"family":"Jacob","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Bindi","given":"Marco"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Sally"},{"family":"Camilloni","given":"Ines"},{"family":"Diedhiou","given":"Arona"},{"family":"Djalante","given":"Riyanti"},{"family":"Ebi","given":"Kristie L."},{"family":"Engelbrecht","given":"Francois"},{"family":"Guiot","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Hijioka","given":"Yasuaki"},{"family":"Mehrotra","given":"Shagun"},{"family":"Payne","given":"Antony"},{"family":"Seneviratne","given":"Sonia I."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Adelle"},{"family":"Warren","given":"Rachel F."},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Guangsheng"},{"family":"Tschakert","given":"Petra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, with widespread impacts on biodiversity which are accelerating in marine, freshwater, and terrestrial ecosystems, as well as increased frequency and intensity of extreme weather events resulting in hydrometeorological hazards ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WS51YDhJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Further, climate change is projected to become an increasingly significant direct driver of change in nature and human well-being. Although predictions vary depending on the scenario and by geographic region, the adverse impacts of climate change on biodiversity will become more pronounced in the next decades and will worsen with global warming ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1zdvUJds","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Adverse impacts on biodiversity and ecosystem functioning are expected to profoundly alter habitat for wild species, as species ranges shrink, and thus greatly influence the risk of global extinctions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"p3ArCjLu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, according to the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, with an average increase in temperature of 1.5°C above pre-industrial levels, 6% of insects, 8% of plants and 4% of vertebrates will lose over half of their climatically determined geographic range. These figures will increase to 18% of insects, 16% of plants and 8% of vertebrates for global warming of 2°C. Coastal ecosystems such as mangroves, tidal marshes and seagrass meadows are affected by the multiple impacts of ocean warming, including sea-level rise, with particular impact on loss of biodiversity and ecosystem services ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LRE4KGLu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPCC, 2019)","plainCitation":"(IPCC, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32308,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TCQLBKC7"],"itemData":{"id":32308,"type":"chapter","container-title":"IPCC Special Report on the Ocean and Cryosphere in a Changing Climate","event-place":"In press","page":"35","publisher-place":"In press","title":"Summary for Policymakers","author":[{"family":"IPCC","given":""}],"editor":[{"family":"Pörtner","given":"H.-O."},{"family":"Roberts","given":"D.C."},{"family":"Masson-Delmotte","given":"V."},{"family":"Zhai","given":"P."},{"family":"Tignor","given":"M."},{"family":"Poloczanska","given":"E."},{"family":"Mintenbeck","given":"K."},{"family":"Alegría","given":"A."},{"family":"Nicolai","given":"M."},{"family":"Okem","given":"A."},{"family":"Petzold","given":"J."},{"family":"Rama","given":"B."},{"family":"Weyer","given":"N. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Evidence for marine ecosystems indicate that the majority (70-90%) of tropical coral reefs will go extinct, even if global warming is constrained to 1.5°C ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"z3tGHphC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoegh-Guldberg, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hoegh-Guldberg, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32304,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DC9XB7R5"],"itemData":{"id":32304,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Global Warming of 1.5°C","language":"English","publisher":"IPCC","title":"Impacts of 1.5ºC global warming on natural and human systems","author":[{"family":"Hoegh-Guldberg","given":"Ove"},{"family":"Jacob","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Bindi","given":"Marco"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Sally"},{"family":"Camilloni","given":"Ines"},{"family":"Diedhiou","given":"Arona"},{"family":"Djalante","given":"Riyanti"},{"family":"Ebi","given":"Kristie L."},{"family":"Engelbrecht","given":"Francois"},{"family":"Guiot","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Hijioka","given":"Yasuaki"},{"family":"Mehrotra","given":"Shagun"},{"family":"Payne","given":"Antony"},{"family":"Seneviratne","given":"Sonia I."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Adelle"},{"family":"Warren","given":"Rachel F."},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Guangsheng"},{"family":"Tschakert","given":"Petra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, with dire consequences for biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kVxCyPVl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPCC, 2019)","plainCitation":"(IPCC, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32308,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TCQLBKC7"],"itemData":{"id":32308,"type":"chapter","container-title":"IPCC Special Report on the Ocean and Cryosphere in a Changing Climate","event-place":"In press","page":"35","publisher-place":"In press","title":"Summary for Policymakers","author":[{"family":"IPCC","given":""}],"editor":[{"family":"Pörtner","given":"H.-O."},{"family":"Roberts","given":"D.C."},{"family":"Masson-Delmotte","given":"V."},{"family":"Zhai","given":"P."},{"family":"Tignor","given":"M."},{"family":"Poloczanska","given":"E."},{"family":"Mintenbeck","given":"K."},{"family":"Alegría","given":"A."},{"family":"Nicolai","given":"M."},{"family":"Okem","given":"A."},{"family":"Petzold","given":"J."},{"family":"Rama","given":"B."},{"family":"Weyer","given":"N. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Increased frequency, intensity, and amount of hydrometeorological hazards—such as heatwaves, heavy rainfall, drought, flooding, storms, and cold spells—are further expected at both 1.5 °C and 2 °C global warming, with risks projected to be lower at 1.5 °C compared to those at 2 °C ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QFS39o8m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoegh-Guldberg, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hoegh-Guldberg, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32304,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DC9XB7R5"],"itemData":{"id":32304,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Global Warming of 1.5°C","language":"English","publisher":"IPCC","title":"Impacts of 1.5ºC global warming on natural and human systems","author":[{"family":"Hoegh-Guldberg","given":"Ove"},{"family":"Jacob","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Bindi","given":"Marco"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Sally"},{"family":"Camilloni","given":"Ines"},{"family":"Diedhiou","given":"Arona"},{"family":"Djalante","given":"Riyanti"},{"family":"Ebi","given":"Kristie L."},{"family":"Engelbrecht","given":"Francois"},{"family":"Guiot","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Hijioka","given":"Yasuaki"},{"family":"Mehrotra","given":"Shagun"},{"family":"Payne","given":"Antony"},{"family":"Seneviratne","given":"Sonia I."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Adelle"},{"family":"Warren","given":"Rachel F."},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Guangsheng"},{"family":"Tschakert","given":"Petra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Storms and severe weather events can destroy or severely damage infrastructure and productive assets such as boats, landing sites, post-harvesting facilities and roads. This can lead to decreased harvesting capacity and access to markets, reducing the availability of food products as well as increasing their prices, resulting in higher incidences of malnutrition in communities, thus having serious consequences for food security, nutrition and health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bTsw6u9c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Niiya, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Niiya, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3IZPVJHU"],"itemData":{"id":32288,"type":"article-newspaper","title":"El fenomeno de El Niño 1997-1998: Problema de seguridad alimentaria en la poblacion local. Estudio de caso en el area marginal urbana de Piura, 1998, pp. 247-281 Peru y El Niño: Aprendiendo de la Naturaleza, PromPeru, PIEDUL S.R.L, Lima.","author":[{"family":"Niiya","given":"","suffix":"Y.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This in turn would affect livelihood activities, including harvesting of wild species. Although hydrometeorological hazards differ from slow-onset climate change impacts in temporal scale, this section reviews climate change and associated hazards together as a driver affecting the use of wild species.
      Although climate change and associated hazards will be a significant driver of biodiversity and wild species loss, there is little direct evidence to show how this driver will affect wild species use. What is clear is that climate change will impact not only different ecosystems and their biodiversity, but also, food production systems, energy systems, water availability, and human health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"V97cnB00","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoegh-Guldberg, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hoegh-Guldberg, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32304,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DC9XB7R5"],"itemData":{"id":32304,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Global Warming of 1.5°C","language":"English","publisher":"IPCC","title":"Impacts of 1.5ºC global warming on natural and human systems","author":[{"family":"Hoegh-Guldberg","given":"Ove"},{"family":"Jacob","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Bindi","given":"Marco"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Sally"},{"family":"Camilloni","given":"Ines"},{"family":"Diedhiou","given":"Arona"},{"family":"Djalante","given":"Riyanti"},{"family":"Ebi","given":"Kristie L."},{"family":"Engelbrecht","given":"Francois"},{"family":"Guiot","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Hijioka","given":"Yasuaki"},{"family":"Mehrotra","given":"Shagun"},{"family":"Payne","given":"Antony"},{"family":"Seneviratne","given":"Sonia I."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Adelle"},{"family":"Warren","given":"Rachel F."},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Guangsheng"},{"family":"Tschakert","given":"Petra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which will all affect use of wild species. In terms of food systems, there is well-established evidence to support that climate change is likely to have negative impacts on agricultural productivity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JTu502Od","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Challinor, 2007; Chavas, 2009; Cline, 2007; R\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}tter & van de Geijn, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Challinor, 2007; Chavas, 2009; Cline, 2007; Rötter & van de Geijn, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32290,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EV49LJV6"],"itemData":{"id":32290,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Climatic Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10584-007-9249-0","ISSN":"0165-0009, 1573-1480","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Climatic Change","language":"en","page":"381-399","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Assessing the vulnerability of food crop systems in Africa to climate change","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10584-007-9249-0","volume":"83","author":[{"family":"Challinor","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Wheeler","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Garforth","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Craufurd","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Kassam","given":"Amir"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",6,27]]}}},{"id":32287,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NICRW7YE"],"itemData":{"id":32287,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agricultural and Forest Meteorology","DOI":"10.1016/j.agrformet.2009.02.001","ISSN":"01681923","issue":"6-7","journalAbbreviation":"Agricultural and Forest Meteorology","language":"en","page":"1118-1128","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Long-term climate change impacts on agricultural productivity in eastern China","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0168192309000380","volume":"149","author":[{"family":"Chavas","given":"Daniel R."},{"family":"Izaurralde","given":"R. César"},{"family":"Thomson","given":"Allison M."},{"family":"Gao","given":"Xuejie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",6]]}}},{"id":32289,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MDR94DL2"],"itemData":{"id":32289,"type":"book","abstract":"How will global warming affect developing countries, which rely heavily on agriculture as a source of economic growth? William Cline asserts that developing countries have more at risk than industrial countries as global warming worsens. Using general circulation and agricultural impact models, Cline boldly examines 2070-99 to forecast the effects of global warming and its economic impact. This detailed study: * outlines existing studies on the agricultural impact of climate change; * estimates projected changes in temperature, precipitation, and agricultural capacity; and * concludes with policy recommendations. * Cline finds that agricultural production in developing countries may fall between 10 and 25 percent, and if global warming progresses unabated, India's agricultural capacity could fall as much as 40 percent. Thus, policymakers should address this phenomenon now before the world's developing countries are adversely and irreversibly affected.","publisher":"Peterson Institute for International Economics","title":"Global Warming and Agriculture: Impact Estimates by Country","URL":"https://EconPapers.repec.org/RePEc:iie:ppress:4037","author":[{"family":"Cline","given":"William"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":32286,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PT7SYMQI"],"itemData":{"id":32286,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Climatic Change","DOI":"10.1023/A:1005541132734","ISSN":"01650009","issue":"4","page":"651-681","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"[No title found]","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1023/A:1005541132734","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Rötter","given":"R."},{"family":"Geijn","given":"S.C.","non-dropping-particle":"van de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, though exactly how agricultural production will change has not been reliably quantified ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XUitwWQy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gornall, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Gornall, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32291,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I3PDZ3GH"],"itemData":{"id":32291,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper reviews recent literature concerning a wide range of processes through which climate change could potentially impact global-scale agricultural productivity, and presents projections of changes in relevant meteorological, hydrological and plant physiological quantities from a climate model ensemble to illustrate key areas of uncertainty. Few global-scale assessments have been carried out, and these are limited in their ability to capture the uncertainty in climate projections, and omit potentially important aspects such as extreme events and changes in pests and diseases. There is a lack of clarity on how climate change impacts on drought are best quantified from an agricultural perspective, with different metrics giving very different impressions of future risk. The dependence of some regional agriculture on remote rainfall, snowmelt and glaciers adds to the complexity. Indirect impacts via sea-level rise, storms and diseases have not been quantified. Perhaps most seriously, there is high uncertainty in the extent to which the direct effects of CO\\n              2\\n              rise on plant physiology will interact with climate change in affecting productivity. At present, the aggregate impacts of climate change on global-scale agricultural productivity cannot be reliably quantified.","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences","DOI":"10.1098/rstb.2010.0158","ISSN":"0962-8436, 1471-2970","issue":"1554","journalAbbreviation":"Phil. Trans. R. Soc. B","language":"en","page":"2973-2989","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Implications of climate change for agricultural productivity in the early twenty-first century","URL":"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rstb.2010.0158","volume":"365","author":[{"family":"Gornall","given":"Jemma"},{"family":"Betts","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Burke","given":"Eleanor"},{"family":"Clark","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Camp","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Willett","given":"Kate"},{"family":"Wiltshire","given":"Andrew"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",9,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Along with other drivers, the impacts of climate change and associated hazards will increasingly exacerbate negative impacts on use of wild species.
 
4.2.1.2.2. Fishing
