stems","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"McLean","given":"M."},{"family":"Mouillot","given":"D."},{"family":"Lindegren","given":"M."},{"family":"Engelhard","given":"G."},{"family":"Villéger","given":"S."},{"family":"Marchal","given":"P."},{"family":"Auber","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32310,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4TW5SJEU"],"itemData":{"id":32310,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Processes","DOI":"10.1186/s13717-019-0174-8","ISSN":"2192-1709","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Process","language":"en","page":"23","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Variable retention in Tasmania, Australia: trends over 16 years of monitoring and adaptive management","title-short":"Variable retention in Tasmania, Australia","URL":"https://ecologicalprocesses.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s13717-019-0174-8","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Scott","given":"Robyn E."},{"family":"Neyland","given":"Mark G."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Susan C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and these impacts vary considerably within and between populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eG4iqNCs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fernandes, 2016; Genner, 2010; A. R. Hughes, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Fernandes, 2016; Genner, 2010; A. R. Hughes, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32121,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UKAD6YH9"],"itemData":{"id":32121,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Biology","issue":"2","language":"nl","page":"517–527","title":"Body size?dependent responses of a marine fish assemblage to climate change and fishing over a century?long scale","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Genner","given":"M.J."},{"family":"Sims","given":"D.W."},{"family":"Southward","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Budd","given":"G.C."},{"family":"Masterson","given":"P."},{"family":"Mchugh","given":"M."},{"family":"Hawkins","given":"S.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32268,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GTZN8X9G"],"itemData":{"id":32268,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"1357–1369","title":"Projecting marine fish production and catch potential in Bangladesh in the 21st century under long-term environmental change and management scenarios","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Fernandes","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Kay","given":"S."},{"family":"Hossain","given":"M.A."},{"family":"Ahmed","given":"M."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W."},{"family":"Lazar","given":"A.N."},{"family":"Barange","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32406,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y2LH3B9F"],"itemData":{"id":32406,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","DOI":"10.1002/fee.1986","ISSN":"1540-9295, 1540-9309","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Front Ecol Environ","language":"en","page":"17-24","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Predicting the sensitivity of marine populations to rising temperatures","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/fee.1986","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Hughes","given":"A Randall"},{"family":"Hanley","given":"Torrance C"},{"family":"Moore","given":"Althea FP"},{"family":"Ramsay?Newton","given":"Christine"},{"family":"Zerebecki","given":"Robyn A"},{"family":"Sotka","given":"Erik E"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} as well as life stages ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vhCwiGo0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dahlke, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dahlke, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32117,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2YWGZB9D"],"itemData":{"id":32117,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Some cope better than others\\nIncreasingly, research is revealing how organisms may, or may not, adapt to a changing climate. Understanding the limitations placed by a species's physiology can help to determine whether it has an immediate potential to deal with rapid change. Many studies have looked at physiological tolerance to climate change in fishes, with results indicating a range of responses. Dahlke conducted a meta-analysis to explore how life stage may influence a species's ability to tolerate temperature change (see the Perspective by Sunday). They found that embryos and breeding adult fishes are much more susceptible to temperature change than those in other life stages and that this factor must therefore be considered in evaluations of susceptibility.\\nScience, this issue p. 65; see also p. 35\\nSpecies’ vulnerability to climate change depends on the most temperature-sensitive life stages, but for major animal groups such as fish, life cycle bottlenecks are often not clearly defined. We used observational, experimental, and phylogenetic data to assess stage-specific thermal tolerance metrics for 694 marine and freshwater fish species from all climate zones. Our analysis shows that spawning adults and embryos consistently have narrower tolerance ranges than larvae and nonreproductive adults and are most vulnerable to climate warming. The sequence of stage-specific thermal tolerance corresponds with the oxygen-limitation hypothesis, suggesting a mechanistic link between ontogenetic changes in cardiorespiratory (aerobic) capacity and tolerance to temperature extremes. A logarithmic inverse correlation between the temperature dependence of physiological rates (development and oxygen consumption) and thermal tolerance range is proposed to reflect a fundamental, energetic trade-off in thermal adaptation. Scenario-based climate projections considering the most critical life stages (spawners and embryos) clearly identify the temperature requirements for reproduction as a critical bottleneck in the life cycle of fish. By 2100, depending on the Shared Socioeconomic Pathway (SSP) scenario followed, the percentages of species potentially affected by water temperatures exceeding their tolerance limit for reproduction range from ~10% (SSP 1–1.9) to ~60% (SSP 5–8.5). Efforts to meet ambitious climate targets (SSP 1–1.9) could therefore benefit many fish species and people who depend on healthy fish stocks.\\nThermal tolerance metrics for 694 marine and freshwater fish species reveal that breeding stages are most vulnerable to climate warming.\\nThermal tolerance metrics for 694 marine and freshwater fish species reveal that breeding stages are most vulnerable to climate warming.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aaz3658","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6499","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2020, American Association for the Advancement of Science. https://www.sciencemag.org/about/science-licenses-journal-article-reuseThis is an article distributed under the terms of the Science Journals Default License.","note":"publisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science\\nsection: Research Article","page":"65-70","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"Thermal bottlenecks in the life cycle define climate vulnerability of fish","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/369/6499/65","volume":"369","author":[{"family":"Dahlke","given":"Flemming T."},{"family":"Wohlrab","given":"Sylke"},{"family":"Butzin","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Pörtner","given":"Hans-Otto"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The evidence is also well-established that these changes affect how commercially important wild species are exploited through fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2PjA3LTy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Howell, 2013; Meynecke, 2006; Pinsky & Fogarty, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Howell, 2013; Meynecke, 2006; Pinsky & Fogarty, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32238,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LRSZABFB"],"itemData":{"id":32238,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Estuarine, Coastal and Shelf Science","issue":"3-4","language":"en","page":"491–504","title":"Effect of rainfall as a component of climate change on estuarine fish production in Queensland, Australia","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Meynecke","given":"J.O."},{"family":"Lee","given":"S.Y."},{"family":"Duke","given":"N.C."},{"family":"Warnken","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":32221,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MSUSKNAE"],"itemData":{"id":32221,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Climatic change","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"883–891","title":"Lagged social-ecological responses to climate and range shifts in fisheries","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Pinsky","given":"M.L."},{"family":"Fogarty","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":32248,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F7AQSX47"],"itemData":{"id":32248,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Climatic Change","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"79–93","title":"Climate-induced primary productivity change and fishing impacts on the Central North Pacific ecosystem and Hawaii-based pelagic longline fishery","volume":"119","author":[{"family":"Howell","given":"E.A."},{"family":"Wabnitz","given":"C.C."},{"family":"Dunne","given":"J.P."},{"family":"Polovina","given":"J.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and as a result, the global seafood market and food security ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OobhzWti","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hobday, 2016; Paukert, 2017; Reygondeau, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Hobday, 2016; Paukert, 2017; Reygondeau, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32243,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XGJBGHZS"],"itemData":{"id":32243,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"249–264","title":"Planning adaptation to climate change in fast-warming marine regions with seafood-dependent coastal communities","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Hobday","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Cochrane","given":"K."},{"family":"Downey-Breedt","given":"N."},{"family":"Howard","given":"J."},{"family":"Aswani","given":"S."},{"family":"Byfield","given":"V."},{"family":"Putten","given":"E.I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32309,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BELRXMU6"],"itemData":{"id":32309,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"To date, there are few comprehensive assessments of how climate change affects inland finfish, fisheries, and aquaculture at a global scale, but one is necessary to identify research needs and commonalities across regions and to help guide decision making and funding priorities. Broadly, the consequences of climate change on inland fishes will impact global food security, the livelihoods of people who depend on inland capture and recreational fisheries. However, understanding how climate change will affect inland fishes and fisheries has lagged behind marine assessments. Building from a North American inland fish assessment, we convened an expert panel from seven countries to provide a first-step to a framework for determining how to approach an assessment of how climate change may affect inland fishes, capture fisheries, and aquaculture globally. Starting with the small group helped frame the key questions (e.g., who is the audience? What is the best approach and spatial scale?). Data gaps identified by the group include: the tolerances of inland fisheries to changes in temperature, stream flows, salinity, and other environmental factors linked to climate change, and the adaptive capacity of fishes and fisheries to adjust to these changes. These questions are difficult to address, but long-term and large-scale datasets are becoming more readily available as a means to test hypotheses related to climate change. We hope this perspective will help researchers and decision makers identify research priorities and provide a framework to help sustain inland fish populations and fisheries for the diversity of users around the globe.","container-title":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1007/s11160-017-9477-y","ISSN":"0960-3166, 1573-5184","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Rev Fish Biol Fisheries","language":"en","page":"393-409","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Designing a global assessment of climate change on inland fishes and fisheries: knowns and needs","title-short":"Designing a global assessment of climate change on inland fishes and fisheries","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s11160-017-9477-y","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Paukert","given":"Craig P."},{"family":"Lynch","given":"Abigail J."},{"family":"Beard","given":"T. Douglas"},{"family":"Chen","given":"Yushun"},{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."},{"family":"Cooperman","given":"Michael S."},{"family":"Cowx","given":"Ian G."},{"family":"Ibengwe","given":"Lilian"},{"family":"Infante","given":"Dana M."},{"family":"Myers","given":"Bonnie J. E."},{"family":"Nguy?n","given":"Hòa Phú"},{"family":"Winfield","given":"Ian J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",6]]}}},{"id":32226,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QLVY8QL7"],"itemData":{"id":32226,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Predicting future oceans","language":"en","page":"87–101","publisher":"Elsevier","title":"Current and future biogeography of exploited marine groups under climate change","author":[{"family":"Reygondeau","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hindcast models indicate that the maximum sustainable yield of marine fish populations decreased globally by 4.1% from 1930 to 2010, with some regions experiencing losses of 15 to 35% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ixjSacJr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Free, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Free, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34011,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YBILDXFV"],"itemData":{"id":34011,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"6430","language":"en","page":"979–983","title":"Impacts of historical warming on marine fisheries production","volume":"363","author":[{"family":"Free","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Thorson","given":"J.T."},{"family":"Pinsky","given":"M.L."},{"family":"Oken","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Wiedenmann","given":"J."},{"family":"Jensen","given":"O.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Between 2000 and 2050, maximum catch potential is projected to decrease globally by 7.7%, with global fisheries revenue projected to decrease by 10.4%; under high carbon dioxide emission scenarios. Figures in the tropics are projected to decrease by 38% and 33%, respectively ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OsDa1QBx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lam, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Lam, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32292,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QEDQGZWR"],"itemData":{"id":32292,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep32607","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"32607","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Projected change 