fisheries after an extreme marine heat wave in 2011, leading to fishery closure the following year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hXfEKJYa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chandrapavan, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Chandrapavan, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32276,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H7UHW746"],"itemData":{"id":32276,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","language":"en","page":"510","title":"The decline and recovery of a crab population from an extreme marine heatwave and a changing climate","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Chandrapavan","given":"A."},{"family":"Caputi","given":"N."},{"family":"Kangas","given":"M.I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, while a study in the Denmark showed the rapid decline of eel fishery after a winter storm in the 19th Century ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Pla8L70g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Poulsen, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Poulsen, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32223,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PP3RBQIR"],"itemData":{"id":32223,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","issue":"2-3","language":"en","page":"181–195","title":"A long-term (1667–1860) perspective on impacts of fishing and environmental variability on fisheries for herring, eel, and whitefish in the Limfjord, Denmark","volume":"87","author":[{"family":"Poulsen","given":"B."},{"family":"Holm","given":"P."},{"family":"MacKenzie","given":"B.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Moreover, studies show how coastal communities in Vanuatu that suffered from a tropical storm and El-Nino-induced prolonged drought ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qHI7v3Ic","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eriksson, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Eriksson, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32266,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UFZ7S9AN"],"itemData":{"id":32266,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science & Policy","language":"en","page":"50–58","title":"The role of fish and fisheries in recovering from natural hazards: Lessons learned from Vanuatu","volume":"76","author":[{"family":"Eriksson","given":"H."},{"family":"Albert","given":"J."},{"family":"Albert","given":"S."},{"family":"Warren","given":"R."},{"family":"Pakoa","given":"K."},{"family":"Andrew","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and lakeside communities in Tanzania whose economy changed as a result of drought ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"g75wzFZS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kimirei, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Kimirei, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32263,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V9IRMY3D"],"itemData":{"id":32263,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic ecosystem health & management","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"29–35","title":"Changes in species composition and abundance of commercially important pelagic fish species in Kigoma area, Lake Tanganyika, Tanzania","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Kimirei","given":"I.A."},{"family":"Mgaya","given":"Y.D."},{"family":"Chande","given":"A.I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} increased their reliance on fishing for their livelihoods. In Antigua and Barbuda, 16% of fishing fleet was destroyed or lost and 18% damaged due to Hurricane Luis in 1995, resulting in an estimated decrease of 24% in gross revenues ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BS9aM7sK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mahon, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Mahon, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35165,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MQ34FST3"],"itemData":{"id":35165,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Issue Paper – Draft","event-place":"Washington, Mainstreaming","language":"en","publisher-place":"Washington, Mainstreaming","title":"Adaptation of fisheries and fishing communities to the impacts of climate change in the CARICOM region","author":[{"family":"Mahon","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. During Hurricane Katrina in 2005, the businesses of about 95% of the 62 seafood dealers in Mississippi were destroyed or their infrastructure so severely damaged that commercial fisher folk were unable to sell their catch or buy fuel or ice from them ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5ZvmfCg6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Buck, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Buck, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32284,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CB5JUU8T"],"itemData":{"id":32284,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Hurricanes Katrina and Rita: fishing and aquaculture industries damage and recovery.Congressional Research Service Report for Congress 4","author":[{"family":"Buck","given":"E.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
4.2.1.2.3. Gathering 
Evidence of the linkage between climate change and hydrometeorological hazards and gathering of wild species is inconclusive. In conjunction with other factors such as land use change and overharvesting, climate change has led to, for example, changes in communities and geographical distribution of seaweed due to warming sea temperatures in Australia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WAgmHXQV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wernberg, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Wernberg, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32129,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZEH5SKFH"],"itemData":{"id":32129,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Experimental Marine Biology and Ecology","issue":"1-2","language":"cs","page":"264–271","title":"Biogenic habitat structure of seaweeds change along a latitudinal gradient in ocean temperature","volume":"400","author":[{"family":"Wernberg","given":"T."},{"family":"Thomsen","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Tuya","given":"F."},{"family":"Kendrick","given":"G.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and in Japan ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a9J22Ooh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kumagai, 2018; Verg\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}s, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Kumagai, 2018; Vergés, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32124,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8TJAMKN2"],"itemData":{"id":32124,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proc. R. Soc. B","DOI":"10.1098/rspb.2014.0846","issue":"1789","language":"en","page":"1–10","title":"The tropicalization of temperate marine ecosystems: climate-mediated changes in herbivory and community phase shifts","volume":"281","author":[{"family":"Vergés","given":"A."},{"family":"Steinberg","given":"P.D."},{"family":"Hay","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Poore","given":"A.G.B."},{"family":"Campbell","given":"A.H."},{"family":"Ballesteros","given":"E."},{"family":"Heck","given":"K.L."},{"family":"Booth","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Coleman","given":"M.A."},{"family":"Feary","given":"D.A."},{"family":"Figueira","given":"W."},{"family":"Langlois","given":"T."},{"family":"Marzinelli","given":"E.M."},{"family":"Mizerek","given":"T."},{"family":"Mumby","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Nakamura","given":"Y."},{"family":"Roughan","given":"M."},{"family":"Sebille","given":"E."},{"family":"Gupta","given":"A.Sen"},{"family":"Roughan","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":32265,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E59JXWLB"],"itemData":{"id":32265,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1716826115.","issue":"36","language":"en","page":"8990–8995","title":"Ocean currents and herbivory drive macroalgae-to-coral community shift under climate warming","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Kumagai","given":"N.H."},{"family":"García Molinos","given":"J."},{"family":"Yamano","given":"H."},{"family":"Takao","given":"S."},{"family":"Fujii","given":"M."},{"family":"Yamanaka","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and decrease in populations of medicinal plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lc3e0vYW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hopping, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hopping, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":275,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R2KCG8X4"],"itemData":{"id":275,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Demand for traditional medicine ingredients is causing species declines globally. Due to this trade, Himalayan caterpillar fungus (\\n              Ophiocordyceps sinensis\\n              ) has become one of the world’s most valuable biological commodities, providing a crucial source of income for hundreds of thousands of collectors. However, the resulting harvesting boom has generated widespread concern over the sustainability of its collection. We investigate whether caterpillar fungus production is decreasing—and if so, why—across its entire range. To overcome the limitations of sparse quantitative data, we use a multiple evidence base approach that makes use of complementarities between local knowledge and ecological modeling. We find that, according to collectors across four countries, caterpillar fungus production has decreased due to habitat degradation, climate change, and especially overexploitation. Our statistical models corroborate that climate change is contributing to this decline. They indicate that caterpillar fungus is more productive under colder conditions, growing in close proximity to areas likely to have permafrost. With significant warming already underway throughout much of its range, we conclude that caterpillar fungus populations have been negatively affected by a combination of overexploitation and climate change. Our results underscore that harvesting is not the sole threat to economically valuable species, and that a collapse of the caterpillar fungus system under ongoing warming and high collection pressure would have serious implications throughout the Himalayan region.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1811591115","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"45","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"11489-11494","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The demise of caterpillar fungus in the Himalayan region due to climate change and overharvesting","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.1811591115","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Hopping","given":"Kelly A."},{"family":"Chignell","given":"Stephen M."},{"family":"Lambin","given":"Eric F."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and thus, loss of associated indigenous knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"I0Z80D91","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hong, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Hong, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32246,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AK4GCQTG"],"itemData":{"id":32246,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-015-0019-1","language":"pt","page":"32","title":"Ethnobotanical study on medicinal plants used by Maonan people in China","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Hong","given":"L."},{"family":"Guo","given":"Z."},{"family":"Huang","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These impacts can have serious consequences for people with long histories of interaction with their natural surroundings, such as indigenous peoples and local communities in particular pastoralists, who have had to adapt their livelihood strategies. A study points to climate change and drought as reasons why pastoralists in Tanzania have had to start farming ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lrlUvfhl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tibuhwa, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Tibuhwa, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32398,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HGYRAEYP"],"itemData":{"id":32398,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–9","title":"Folk taxonomy and use of mushrooms in communities around Ngorongoro and Serengeti National Park, Tanzania","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Tibuhwa","given":"D.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, while other studies point to conflict induced by climate change especially droughts and floods as the primary reason behind changes in livelihood activities of pastoralists in Kenya ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ofzu5NCW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Omolo, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Omolo, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32116,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5U7KRFV7"],"itemData":{"id":32116,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Clitmate change-induced conflict is a major global threat to human security and the environment. It has been projected that there is going to be an increase in climate changes resulting in increased droughts and floods in northern Kenya. Climate change impacts will be differently distributed among different regions, ages, income groups, occupations and gender. People living in poverty are more vulnerable to environmental changes. In relation to these concerns, this article discusses the following issues: climate change, pastoralism and conflicts, gender issues in Turkana, and the future of pastoralism in relation to changing climate conditions. Specifically, the first section looks at the impacts of climate change on pastoralism and the livelihoods of pastoralists, and at the types of climate change-induced conflicts in Turkana. The next section focuses on the impact of climate change-induced conflict on women and men’s livelihoods, including discussion of the roles and participation in decision making. Finally, the future of pastoralism in relation to changing climate is discussed. The focus will be on scenarios of the past and future projections of rainfall patterns in Turkana, the future of pastoralism and the possibility of climate-induced conflicts in the future.","container-title":"African Journal on Conflict Resolution","DOI":"10.4314/ajcr.v10i2.63312","ISSN":"1562-6997","issue":"2","language":"en","license":"Copyright (c)","note":"number: 2","source":"www.ajol.info","title":"Gender and climate change-induced conflict in pastoral communities: Case study of Turkana in northwestern Kenya","title-short":"Gender and climate change-induced conflict in pastoral communities","URL":"https://www.ajol.info/index.php/ajcr/article/view/63312","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Omolo","given":"N. A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

4.2.1.2.4 Terrestrial and marine animal harvesting
The evidence that link climate change and trends in climatic conditions with population densities, growth, diseases, mortality, and distribution of hunted wild species is well-established. Changes in, for example, population densities of hares ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VliYbUgQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schai-Braun, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Schai-Braun, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32395,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QIL6XCT4"],"itemData":{"id":32395,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 10\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"2837","title":"Estimating sustainable harvest rates for European hare (Lepus Europaeus) populations","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Schai-Braun","given":"Stéphanie C"},{"family":"Kowalczyk","given":"Christine"},{"family":"Klansek","given":"Erich"},{"family":"Hackländer","given":"Klaus"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} quail phenology ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6cGqG49W","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nadal, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Nadal, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32228,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3NJTYPHV"],"itemData":{"id":32228,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science of the total environment","language":"en","page":"1295–1301","title":"Synchronizing biological cycles as key to survival under a scenario of global change: The Common quail (Coturnix coturnix) strategy","volume":"613","author":[{"family":"Nadal","given":"J."},{"family":"Ponz","given":"C."},{"family":"Margalida","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, breeding patterns of ducks, increased biomass of seals and belugas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qI8tvo8C","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Hoover, 2013)","plainCitation":"(C. Hoover, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32249,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5679MNKH"],"itemData":{"id":32249,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem model future projections. Ecological modelling","language":"en","page":"143–156","title":"Effects of hunting, fishing and climate change on the Hudson Bay marine ecosystem: II","volume":"264","author":[{"family":"Hoover","given":"C."},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"T."},{"family":"Christensen","given":"V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, mortality and distribution of walrus ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8FnbBoSk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(MacCracken, 2012)","plainCitation":"(MacCracken, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32242,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/78CE85EK"],"itemData":{"id":32242,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and evolution","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"2072–2090","title":"Pacific W alrus and climate change: observations and predictions","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"MacCracken","given":"J.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, imply a link between climate change and how wild species are hunted, but the evidence on this is incomplete.  For example, studies in the Arctic region show a clear link between impacts of climate change, such as changes in weather, ice, and oceanographic conditions, with variation in hunting season of walrus and whales ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ij0gQ214","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Metcalf & Robards, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Metcalf & Robards, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32239,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/69GW34D2"],"itemData":{"id":32239,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","issue":"sp2","language":"en","page":"148– 156","title":"Sustaining a healthy human–walrus relationship in a dynamic environment: challenges for comanagement","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Metcalf","given":"V."},{"family":"Robards","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and melting sea ice for the increased catch of narwhals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1caGHw64","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nielsen, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Nielsen, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32231,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M4GRJAZV"],"itemData":{"id":32231,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Polar Research","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"238–245","title":"Is climate change causing the increasing narwhal (Monodon monoceros) catches in Smith Sound, Greenland?","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Nielsen","given":"M.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The relationship between climate change, hunting, and conservation of polar bears has received more attention than other hunted wild species; the evidence is clear that the melting of the sea ice due to climate change threatens the habitat of polar bears.  
      In some cases, changes in habitat are affecting bear distribution with increased incidence of human-bear conflict as polar bears move nearer to human settlements searching for food. Indigenous knowledge is clear on the current and potential for sustainable harvesting of bears, however, scientific evidence is unresolved. Whether polar bears can be sustainably hunted ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GAPPezt5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Regehr, 2017; Stirling, 2008, 2011; Tyrrell & Clark, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Regehr, 2017; Stirling, 2008, 2011; Tyrrell & Clark, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":353,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L9J5PQHC"],"itemData":{"id":353,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"363–372","source":"Google Scholar","title":"What happened to climate change? CITES and the reconfiguration of polar bear conservation discourse","title-short":"What happened to climate change?","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Tyrrell","given":"Martina"},{"family":"Clark","given":"Douglas A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":32225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XNB4ZPRJ"],"itemData":{"id":32225,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"1534–1543","title":"Harvesting wildlife affected by climate change: a modelling and management approach for polar bears","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Regehr","given":"Eric V."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"Ryan R."},{"family":"Rode","given":"Karyn D."},{"family":"Runge","given":"Michael C."},{"family":"Stern","given":"Harry L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":32411,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E9LYLEQN"],"itemData":{"id":32411,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"568–574","title":"Quantitative support for a subjective fatness index for immobilized polar bears","volume":"72","author":[{"family":"Stirling","given":"I."},{"family":"Thiemann","given":"G.W."},{"family":"Richardson","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":32396,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LXKGWN8H"],"itemData":{"id":32396,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Canada","page":"859–876","title":"Polar bear population status in the northern Beaufort Sea","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Stirling","given":"I."},{"family":"McDonald","given":"T.L."},{"family":"Richardson","given":"E.S."},{"family":"Regehr","given":"E.V."},{"family":"Amstrup","given":"S.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Evidence that hydrometeorological hazards have contributed to the use of wild species through hunting is scarce. A study conducted after a tropical cyclone in Tonga shows the relationship between tropical cyclones and increased mortality of fruit bats due to increased hunting, and destruction of trees that bats feed from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"d8CCTKoP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McConkey, 2004)","plainCitation":"(McConkey, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32236,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2N7DFAAR"],"itemData":{"id":32236,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Tropical Ecology","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"555–561","title":"Effects of Cyclone Waka on flying foxes (Pteropus tonganus) in the Vava'u Islands of Tonga","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"McConkey","given":"K.R."},{"family":"Drake","given":"D.R."},{"family":"Franklin","given":"J."},{"family":"Tonga","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. What can be inferred from this study is another link between wild species use and hazards: that changes in the climate and the frequency and intensity of hydrometeorological hazards will have negative impacts on agriculture, aquaculture and other livelihood activities, which would then lead to communities increasing other activities to obtain food, most notably by hunting, fishing, and gathering wild species. But the evidence on this is inconclusive. 
      
4.2.1.2.5 Logging
The evidence of the link between climate change and logging is established but incomplete. Although agricultural land encroachment and unsustainable forest management practices are the biggest threats to forests and their biodiversity, forests are impacted by changes in the climate, and thus, how products are harvested from them. Models predict a decline in commercially important trees in the taiga ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CGdd1cQu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bu, 2008; Ma, 2019; Steenberg, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Bu, 2008; Ma, 2019; Steenberg, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32393,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4W5LVC4T"],"itemData":{"id":32393,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Chinese Journal of Plant Ecology","ISSN":"1005-264X","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Chinese Journal of Plant Ecology","note":"number: 1","page":"80","title":"Sensitivity of coniferous trees to environmental factors at different scales in the Small Xing'an Mountains, China","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Bu","given":"Ren-Cang"},{"family":"Chang","given":"Yu"},{"family":"Hu","given":"Yuan-Man"},{"family":"Li","given":"Xiu-Zhen"},{"family":"He","given":"Hong-Shi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":32394,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J9ZHI5BV"],"itemData":{"id":32394,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global change biology","ISSN":"1354-1013","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Global change biology","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"351-360","title":"Divergent trends in the risk of spring frost damage to trees in Europe with recent warming","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Ma","given":"Qianqian"},{"family":"Huang","given":"Jian?Guo"},{"family":"Hänninen","given":"Heikki"},{"family":"Berninger","given":"Frank"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32410,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XKZN99U5"],"itemData":{"id":32410,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of forest science","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"61–73","title":"Modelling the effects of climate change and timber harvest on the forests of central Nova Scotia, Canada","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Steenberg","given":"J.W."},{"family":"Duinker","given":"P.N."},{"family":"Bush","given":"P.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} while the opposite is predicted in warmer parts of the world (increased southern species in Northeastern China increase in some temperate and pioneer species in the Canadian Maritimes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BCz4cnhi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Steenberg, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Steenberg, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32410,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XKZN99U5"],"itemData":{"id":32410,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of forest science","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"61–73","title":"Modelling the effects of climate change and timber harvest on the forests of central Nova Scotia, Canada","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Steenberg","given":"J.W."},{"family":"Duinker","given":"P.N."},{"family":"Bush","given":"P.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; increased forest productivity in the Pacific Northwest ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5R4XkIa9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Creutzburg, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Creutzburg, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32283,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DHRY7GD6"],"itemData":{"id":32283,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"503–518","title":"Forest management scenarios in a changing climate: trade?offs between carbon, timber, and old forest","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Creutzburg","given":"M.K."},{"family":"Scheller","given":"R.M."},{"family":"Lucash","given":"M.S."},{"family":"LeDuc","given":"S.D."},{"family":"Johnson","given":"M.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; higher profits from forestry predicted in Lithuania ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0U0i8glF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mozgeris, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Mozgeris, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32241,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P4DHDK7G"],"itemData":{"id":32241,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests","issue":"9","language":"en","page":"809","title":"Spatial pattern of climate change effects on Lithuanian forestry","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Mozgeris","given":"G."},{"family":"Brukas","given":"V."},{"family":"Pivori?nas","given":"N."},{"family":"?inga","given":"G."},{"family":"Makrickien?","given":"E."},{"family":"By?enkien?","given":"S."},{"family":"Augustaitis","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, while estimates from models in other studies are more ambiguous ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XSrqNMmn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(De Cauwer, 2014; Halofsky, 2014)","plainCitation":"(De Cauwer, 2014; Halofsky, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32270,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FKBBBYGX"],"itemData":{"id":32270,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"211–226","title":"Potential, realised, future distribution and environmental suitability for Pterocarpus angolensis DC in southern Africa","volume":"315","author":[{"family":"De Cauwer","given":"V."},{"family":"Muys","given":"B."},{"family":"Revermann","given":"R."},{"family":"Trabucco","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":32257,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z5RZ76CP"],"itemData":{"id":32257,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"1908–1925","title":"Dry forest resilience varies under simulated climate?management scenarios in a central Oregon, USA landscape","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Halofsky","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Halofsky","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Burcsu","given":"T."},{"family":"Hemstrom","given":"M.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      Natural disturbances have shaped the development of structure and function of forest ecosystems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"d1a6Y0ZR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Attiwill, 1994b)","plainCitation":"(Attiwill, 1994b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32294,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8WUJQW6I"],"itemData":{"id":32294,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and management","issue":"2-3","language":"en","page":"247–300","title":"The disturbance of forest ecosystems: the ecological basis for conservative management","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Attiwill","given":"P.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). The link between hydrometeorological hazards and logging  is well-established. Wildfires, floods and droughts are hazards most likely to impact sustainable use of wild species on both land and in water, and there is sufficient evidence on how wildfires in particular define the supply of trees and non-timber forest products, thus impacting their use. Fire has been used as a forest management tool in many parts of the world to maintain, for example, a balance between vegetation in desert grasslands and stimulate herbs and seeds production ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DZiDw6y3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bock & Block, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Bock & Block, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32300,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4YUHJC6H"],"itemData":{"id":32300,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fire and avian ecology in North America. Studies in avian biology","issue":"30","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Camarillo, Calif\\npublisher: Cooper Ornithological Society","page":"14–32","title":"Fire and birds in the southwestern United States","author":[{"family":"Bock","given":"C.E."},{"family":"Block","given":"W.M."}],"editor":[{"family":"Saab","given":"V."},{"family":"Powell","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, impact tree distributions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3FDVdCUj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Halofsky, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Halofsky, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32257,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z5RZ76CP"],"itemData":{"id":32257,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"1908–1925","title":"Dry forest resilience varies under simulated climate?management scenarios in a central Oregon, USA landscape","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Halofsky","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Halofsky","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Burcsu","given":"T."},{"family":"Hemstrom","given":"M.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and species composition ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"owrxwfK2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Attiwill, 1994a)","plainCitation":"(Attiwill, 1994a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32293,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/STK5SY3F"],"itemData":{"id":32293,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest ecology and management","issue":"2-3","language":"en","page":"301–346","title":"Ecological disturbance and the conservative management of eucalypt forests in Australia","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Attiwill","given":"P.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and rehabilitate forest diversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QY1AMTT9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kelly & L., 2017; Vanha-Majamaa, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Kelly & L., 2017; Vanha-Majamaa, 2007)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32125,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KKGMX8ZD"],"itemData":{"id":32125,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","issue":"1-2","language":"en","page":"77–88","title":"Rehabilitating boreal forest structure and species composition in Finland through logging, dead wood creation and fire: the EVO experiment","volume":"250","author":[{"family":"Vanha-Majamaa","given":"I."},{"family":"Lilja","given":"S."},{"family":"Ryömä","given":"R."},{"family":"Kotiaho","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Laaka-Lindberg","given":"S."},{"family":"Lindberg","given":"H."},{"family":"Kuuluvainen","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":32261,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4TVHTXDG"],"itemData":{"id":32261,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","language":"en","page":"1264–1265","title":"Using fire to promote biodiversity","volume":"355","author":[{"family":"Kelly","given":"Land Brotons"},{"literal":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Regular fire regimes that are natural part of the lifecycle of some forests have positive impacts on use of wild species in forests, for example, to increase essential oil content of lemongrass ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZR4HkL1m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Darabant, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Darabant, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32306,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3WB2RUJE"],"itemData":{"id":32306,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/1365-2664.12746","ISSN":"00218901","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"J Appl Ecol","language":"en","page":"1613-1622","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Burning for enhanced non-timber forest product yield may jeopardize the resource base through interactive effects","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/1365-2664.12746","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Darabant","given":"András"},{"literal":"Dorji"},{"family":"Staudhammer","given":"Christina L."},{"family":"Rai","given":"Prem B."},{"family":"Gratzer","given":"Georg"}],"editor":[{"family":"Brando","given":"Paulo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, to maintain abundance of morel mushrooms ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RFjgWNXM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. R. Larson, 2016)","plainCitation":"(E. R. Larson, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32305,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N2XQXPUW"],"itemData":{"id":32305,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PeerJ","ISSN":"2167-8359","page":"e1915","title":"Phylogenetic species delimitation for crayfishes of the genus Pacifastacus","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Larson","given":"Eric R"},{"family":"Castelin","given":"Magalie"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Bronwyn W"},{"family":"Olden","given":"Julian D"},{"family":"Abbott","given":"Cathryn L"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, to sustain large hardwood trees ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yELRAhfx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Long, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Long, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32385,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VPKI5SDZ"],"itemData":{"id":32385,"type":"chapter","abstract":"In this chapter, we review scientific information regarding the conservation and restoration of forest ecosystems on public lands within the Northwest Forest Plan (NWFP, or Plan) area that harbor special value for American Indian tribes and individuals. We highlight advances in understanding how changes in climate, fire, hydrology, vegetation, and resource management regimes have affected tribal ecocultural resources and how land management can promote ecocultural resources in the future. In particular, we examine how distinctive strategies for engaging tribes in restoring ecocultural resources can uphold both tribal rights and federal responsibilities, while supporting other federal land management goals.","call-number":"Sub 43c","collection-title":"General Technical Report (GTR)","container-title":"Synthesis of science to inform land management within the Northwest Forest Plan area","event-place":"Portland, Oregon","ISBN":"Gen. Tech. Rep. PNW-GTR-966","language":"en","number-of-volumes":"3","page":"851-918","publisher":"Department of Agriculture, Forest Service, Pacific Northwest Research Station","publisher-place":"Portland, Oregon","source":"Zotero","title":"Tribal Ecocultural Resources and Engagement","URL":"https://www.fs.fed.us/pnw/pubs/pnw_gtr966.pdf","volume":"3, Chapter 11","author":[{"family":"Long","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Lake","given":"Frank K"},{"family":"Lynn","given":"Kathy"},{"family":"Viles","given":"Carson"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, for natural regeneration in forests rich in brazil nut ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ikD6rjMp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Porcher, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Porcher, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32220,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A4G3RP78"],"itemData":{"id":32220,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"fr","page":"52–59","title":"Fire-and distance-dependent recruitment of the Brazil nut in the Peruvian Amazon","volume":"427","author":[{"family":"Porcher","given":"V."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"E."},{"family":"Gomringer","given":"R.C."},{"family":"Lozano","given":"R.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and to regenerate eucalyptus forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qjS0IwM8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Attiwill, 1994a; Burton, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Attiwill, 1994a; Burton, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32293,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/STK5SY3F"],"itemData":{"id":32293,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest ecology and management","issue":"2-3","language":"en","page":"301–346","title":"Ecological disturbance and the conservative management of eucalypt forests in Australia","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Attiwill","given":"P.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}},{"id":32285,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NT684GCL"],"itemData":{"id":32285,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"436","title":"Shifting states, altered fates: divergent fuel moisture responses after high frequency wildfire in an obligate seeder eucalypt forest","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Burton","given":"J."},{"family":"Cawson","given":"J."},{"family":"Noske","given":"P."},{"family":"Sheridan","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, evidence is well-established that altered or intensified disturbance regimes, most notably fire but also floods and droughts—which are expected to increase in intensity and frequency with climate change—will have negative impacts on forests and its products, and thus, how they are used. Frequent and severe wildfires will be devastating for particular vegetation types, causing land degradation, loss of habitats, deforestation and support the proliferation of alien invasive plant species. Frequent wildfires, especially during prolonged dry conditions, may result in the disturbance of forest and savanna ecosystems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"B0xsTnX5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kganyago & Shikwambana, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Kganyago & Shikwambana, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32262,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G9C5HBCV"],"itemData":{"id":32262,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","language":"en","page":"6811","title":"Assessing Spatio-Temporal Variability of Wildfires and their Impact on Sub-Saharan Ecosystems and Air Quality Using Multisource Remotely Sensed Data and Trend Analysis","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Kganyago","given":"M."},{"family":"Shikwambana","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Examples of negative impacts of changes in disturbance regimes on use of wild species include a record-breaking flood in the Amazon that killed Brazil nut trees (Harraiz 2017), while an extreme drought in the Amazon in 2010 decreased biomass and timber volumes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oSe9bTGI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vidal, 2016a)","plainCitation":"(Vidal, 2016a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32311,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z63QSNNG"],"itemData":{"id":32311,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Concerns about the sustainability of tropical forestry motivated this study on post-logging timber and carbon dynamics over a 20-year period in Paragominas, Pará, Brazil. Previously unlogged forest was subjected to conventional logging (CL), reduced-impact logging (RIL), or was set aside as an unlogged control. All trees ?25cm DBH and all trees of commercial species ?10cm DBH were monitored in a 24.5ha plot in each treatment, with a 5.25ha subplot in each for monitoring all trees ?10cm DBH. Above-ground biomass and bole volumes of merchantable species were tracked based on 10 inventories made between 1993 and 2014. Pre-logging biomass and bole volumes of commercial species were estimated as 237, 231, and 211Mgha?1, and 78, 80, and 70m3ha?1, in the RIL, CL, and unlogged plots, respectively. One year after logging, biomass was reduced 14% by RIL and 24% by CL with corresponding merchantable species volume reductions of 21% and 31%. By 2014, biomass and bole volumes of commercial species had recovered 95% and 98% of their pre-logging stocks in the RIL plot but only 76% and 72% in the CL plot, respectively; timber volumes from large trees (?50cm DBH) were only recovered to 81% in the RIL plot and to 53% in the CL plot. Over the first twenty years after logging, average volume increments from commercial species were substantially higher in the RIL plot (0.72m3ha?1year?1) than in the CL plot (0.08m3ha?1year?1). Recovery of both biomass and timber volumes were temporarily reversed between 2009 and 2014 due to a 4-fold increase in annual mortality rates in the RIL plot and a 5.5-fold increase in the CL plot (as well as a 3-fold increase in the control plot), all presumably related to the extreme drought of 2010. Our findings support the claim that use of RIL techniques accelerates rates of biomass and timber stock recovery after selective logging.","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2016.06.003","ISSN":"0378-1127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"1-8","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Recovery of biomass and merchantable timber volumes twenty years after conventional and reduced-impact logging in Amazonian Brazil","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378112716303000","volume":"376","author":[{"family":"Vidal","given":"Edson"},{"family":"West","given":"Thales A. P."},{"family":"Putz","given":"Francis E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",9,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A model predicts that increased frequency of fires in India's Western Ghats will decrease the recruitment of a traditional medicinal plant ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8olUN7f1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Varghese, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Varghese, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34010,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVLWWHTI"],"itemData":{"id":34010,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS One","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"0119634","title":"Assessing the effects of multiple stressors on the recruitment of fruit harvested trees in a tropical dry forest, Western Ghats, India","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Varghese","given":"A."},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"T."},{"family":"Mandle","given":"L."},{"family":"Nath","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These will impact how these forest products are used; a model for timber production in the Brazilian Amazon shows that fire losses can reach up to US$183 +/- 30 ha/year in areas hit by recurrent fires that would be harvested between 2012 and 2041 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pUMEcz78","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Oliveira, 2019)","plainCitation":"(de Oliveira, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32386,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XZBWR6FH"],"itemData":{"id":32386,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Background: Recognition of the diversity of living beings, including the classification and naming of species, is a fundamental condition for biological literacy with the aim of developing critical awareness of human relationships with nature, and for which formal education plays an important role. The present study aimed to analyze the representation that urban/rural students have for wild vertebrates and their main sources of knowledge.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-018-0283-y","ISSN":"17464269","issue":"1","page":"1–23","title":"Wild vertebrates and their representation by urban/rural students in a region of northeast Brazil","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Oliveira","given":"José Valberto","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Faria Lopes","given":"Sérgio","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Barboza","given":"Raynner Rilke Duarte"},{"family":"Melo Brito Trovão","given":"Dilma Maria","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Ramos","given":"Maiara Bezerra"},{"family":"Nóbrega Alves","given":"Rômulo Romeu"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, while another estimates the vulnerability of timber supply in Canadian forest management areas to increase in some areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jakqq8F2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gauthier, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Gauthier, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32307,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LSF3CWFM"],"itemData":{"id":32307,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aaa9092","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6250","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"819-822","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Boreal forest health and global change","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.aaa9092","volume":"349","author":[{"family":"Gauthier","given":"S."},{"family":"Bernier","given":"P."},{"family":"Kuuluvainen","given":"T."},{"family":"Shvidenko","given":"A. Z."},{"family":"Schepaschenko","given":"D. G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",8,21]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another model estimated that using the most extreme projected climate scenario, timber supply could be reduced by up to 79% due to climate change and fire in the eastern Canadian boreal forest ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2ALEdB6i","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dhital, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Dhital, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32273,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9WQDQHNK"],"itemData":{"id":32273,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Planning and Management","issue":"12","language":"en","page":"2228–2249","title":"Adaptation potential of ecosystem-based management to climate change in the eastern Canadian boreal forest","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Dhital","given":"N."},{"family":"Raulier","given":"F."},{"family":"Bernier","given":"P.Y."},{"family":"Lapointe-Garant","given":"M.P."},{"family":"Berninger","given":"F."},{"family":"Bergeron","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      On the other hand, there is established but incomplete evidence that climate change and hydrometeorological hazards have contributed to sustainable use of forests and its products.  Efforts to mitigate climate change have focused on forests, in the global North through sustainable forest management while in the global South this has taken the form of Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation (REDD/REDD+) projects. These have contributed to sustainable use of wild species. Positive impacts of Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation (REDD/REDD+) include minimizing human-wild species conflicts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"z6cJE7nV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Entenmann, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Entenmann, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32267,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E85TDDP2"],"itemData":{"id":32267,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","language":"en","page":"3561–3586","title":"REDD+-related activities in Kenya: actors’ views on biodiversity and monitoring in a broader policy context","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Entenmann","given":"S.K."},{"family":"Schmitt","given":"C.B."},{"family":"Konold","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and on biodiversity conservation and livelihoods, while concerns have been raised about the  rights and access of local communities, including indigenous peoples and local communities, to these forests (see also Chapter 2 of this assessment). Further, in recognition of the important role forests can play to reduce impacts of hazards such as storms, floods, and landslides, as well as impacts of climate change in coastal areas such as sea-level rise and coastal erosion ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Fz0HhYvo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ghosh, 2016; Hiwasaki, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Ghosh, 2016; Hiwasaki, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32120,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9ZBFLFTF"],"itemData":{"id":32120,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests","issue":"12","language":"en","page":"305","title":"Mapping long-term changes in mangrove species composition and distribution in the Sundarbans","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Ghosh","given":"M.K."},{"family":"Kumar","given":"L."},{"family":"Roy","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32245,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2ISBL53D"],"itemData":{"id":32245,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Climatic Change","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"35–56","title":"Local and indigenous knowledge on climate-related hazards of coastal and small island communities in Southeast Asia","volume":"128","author":[{"family":"Hiwasaki","given":"L."},{"family":"Luna","given":"E."},{"family":"Marçal","given":"J.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, efforts to preserve forests have been implemented, for example, the Natural Forest Conservation Project in China, which put in place measures to ban commercial logging in some forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"i5kvodr7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Zhu, 2018)","plainCitation":"(J. Zhu, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32403,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IBYLEHF4"],"itemData":{"id":32403,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bulletin of Chinese Academy of Sciences (Chinese Version","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"107–118","title":"Support ecological restoration and sustainable management of forests in Northeast China based on research of forest ecology and demonstrations","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Zhu","given":"Jiaojun"},{"family":"Yan","given":"Qiaoling"},{"family":"Yu","given":"Lizhong"},{"family":"Zhan","given":"Jinxin"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Kai"},{"family":"Gao","given":"Tian"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
4.2.1.2.6 Non-extractive uses
There is increasing amount of work that explores the impacts that climate change and hydrometeorological hazards will have on tourism, especially nature-based tourism ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kzdzgsfd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Amelung, 2007; Becken & Hay, 2007; C. M. Hall & Higham, 2005; Hamilton, 2005; D. Scott, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Amelung, 2007; Becken & Hay, 2007; C. M. Hall & Higham, 2005; Hamilton, 2005; D. Scott, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":181,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZEYUFU85","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P8BXUPLB"],"itemData":{"id":181,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Travel Research","DOI":"10.1177/0047287506295937","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"285–296","title":"Implications of Global Climate Change for Tourism Flows and Seasonality","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Amelung","given":"B."},{"family":"Nicholls","given":"S."},{"family":"Viner","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":32387,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMY4AECW"],"itemData":{"id":32387,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Multilingual Matters","title":"Tourism and climate change: Risks and opportunities","author":[{"family":"Becken","given":"S."},{"family":"Hay","given":"J.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":32256,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PC4IRMCG"],"itemData":{"id":32256,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Channel View Publications","title":"Tourism, recreation and climate change","editor":[{"family":"Hall","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Higham","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32254,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FXRGCRHB"],"itemData":{"id":32254,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global environmental change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2004.12.009","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"253–266","title":"Climate change and international tourism: a simulation study","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Hamilton","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Maddison","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Tol","given":"R.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32408,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QSV3KFQY"],"itemData":{"id":32408,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Routledge","title":"Tourism and climate change: Impacts, adaptation and mitigation","author":[{"family":"Scott","given":"D."},{"family":"Hall","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Stefan","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The implications for use of wild species through wild species tourism is established but incomplete, with emerging evidence of negative impacts of climate change on Wild species tourism among pastoralists in Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kVxPGEFz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barnes, 2012; Bedelian & Ogutu, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Barnes, 2012; Bedelian & Ogutu, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32388,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JSAKNAW6"],"itemData":{"id":32388,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pastoralism: Research, Policy and Practice","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"22","title":"Expected climate change impacts on land and natural resource use in Namibia: exploring economically efficient responses","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Barnes","given":"J.I."},{"family":"MacGregor","given":"J."},{"family":"Alberts","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":32389,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JTV6T2HS"],"itemData":{"id":32389,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pastoralism","DOI":"10.1186/s13570-017-0085-1","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"10","title":"Trade-offs for climate-resilient pastoral livelihoods in wildlife conservancies in the Mara ecosystem, Kenya","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Bedelian","given":"C."},{"family":"Ogutu","given":"J.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

4.2.1.2.7 Mediating factors
Climate change is a driver that is increasingly exacerbating the impact of other stressors on nature and human well-being ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KNOw2RyP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The difficulty of isolating impacts of climate change on species with those of other stressors is well established from the literature.  For the case of fishing, other stressors include overfishing, invasive species, habitat degradation and loss, pollution and eutrophication, and shipping ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tHKr4GPJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cardinale, 2008; Collingsworth, 2017; Diop & Scheren, 2016; Halpern, 2019; P. C. Jacobson, 2017; McGreavy, 2018; R. I. Perry, 2010; Pratchett, 2011; Ustin, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Cardinale, 2008; Collingsworth, 2017; Diop & Scheren, 2016; Halpern, 2019; P. C. Jacobson, 2017; McGreavy, 2018; R. I. Perry, 2010; Pratchett, 2011; Ustin, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32275,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NI3PG6WR"],"itemData":{"id":32275,"type":"book","language":"pt","number-of-pages":"221–237","publisher":"Alaska Sea Grant College Program","title":"Disentangling the effect of adult biomass and temperature on the recruitment dynamics of fishes. Resiliency of Gadid Stocks to Fishing and Climate Change","author":[{"family":"Cardinale","given":"M."},{"family":"Hjelm","given":"J."},{"family":"Casini","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":32222,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JKMTR8FI"],"itemData":{"id":32222,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","issue":"5-6","language":"en","page":"356–363","title":"The challenge of adapting marine social–ecological systems to the additional stress of climate change","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Perry","given":"R.I."},{"family":"Ommer","given":"R.E."},{"family":"Barange","given":"M."},{"family":"Werner","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32224,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YTAAC7ID"],"itemData":{"id":32224,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine and Freshwater Research","issue":"9","language":"en","page":"1062–1081","title":"Contribution of climate change to degradation and loss of critical fish habitats in Australian marine and freshwater environments","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Pratchett","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Bay","given":"L.K."},{"family":"Gehrke","given":"P.C."},{"family":"Koehn","given":"J.D."},{"family":"Osborne","given":"K."},{"family":"Pressey","given":"R.L."},{"family":"Wachenfeld","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":32123,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S2JQ94YF"],"itemData":{"id":32123,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Evolutionary Ecology Research","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"529–550","title":"Developing the capacity to monitor climate change impacts in Mediterranean estuaries","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Ustin","given":"S.L."},{"family":"Santos","given":"M.J."},{"family":"Hestir","given":"E.L."},{"family":"Khanna","given":"S."},{"family":"Casas","given":"A."},{"family":"Greenberg","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":32274,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2XQTU55G"],"itemData":{"id":32274,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Estuarine, Coastal and Shelf Science","language":"en","page":"327–339","title":"Sustainable oceans and coasts: Lessons learnt from Eastern and Western Africa","volume":"183","author":[{"family":"Diop","given":"S."},{"family":"Scheren","given":"P.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32282,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZL5FIFUS"],"itemData":{"id":32282,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"363–391","title":"Climate change as a long-term stressor for the fisheries of the Laurentian Great Lakes of North America","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Collingsworth","given":"P.D."},{"family":"Bunnell","given":"D.B."},{"family":"Murray","given":"M.W."},{"family":"Kao","given":"Y.C."},{"family":"Feiner","given":"Z.S."},{"family":"Claramunt","given":"R.M."},{"family":"Ludsin","given":"S.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":32251,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LW7I7HWX"],"itemData":{"id":32251,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PloS one","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"0182667","title":"Disentangling the effects of a century of eutrophication and climate warming on freshwater lake fish assemblages","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Jacobson","given":"P.C."},{"family":"Hansen","given":"G.J."},{"family":"Bethke","given":"B.J."},{"family":"Cross","given":"T.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":32235,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KC5C8Y2Z"],"itemData":{"id":32235,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean & coastal management","language":"en","page":"240–253","title":"Enhancing adaptive capacities in coastal communities through engaged communication research: Insights from a statewide study of shellfish co-management","volume":"163","author":[{"family":"McGreavy","given":"B."},{"family":"Randall","given":"S."},{"family":"Quiring","given":"T."},{"family":"Hathaway","given":"C."},{"family":"Hillyer","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32255,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XZNSVE9E"],"itemData":{"id":32255,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific reports","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–8","title":"Recent pace of change in human impact on the world’s ocean","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Halpern","given":"B.S."},{"family":"Frazier","given":"M."},{"family":"Afflerbach","given":"J."},{"family":"Lowndes","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Micheli","given":"F."},{"family":"O’Hara","given":"C."},{"family":"Selkoe","given":"K.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Studies in the Atlantic ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"e4yuzn4C","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mullon, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Mullon, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32227,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RUY5MZGA"],"itemData":{"id":32227,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological modelling","language":"en","page":"273–291","title":"Quantitative pathways for Northeast Atlantic fisheries based on climate, ecological–economic and governance modelling scenarios","volume":"320","author":[{"family":"Mullon","given":"C."},{"family":"Steinmetz","given":"F."},{"family":"Merino","given":"G."},{"family":"Fernandes","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W.L."},{"family":"Butenschön","given":"M."},{"family":"Barange","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and in California ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GKU6vGkK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aguilera, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Aguilera, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32130,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PXLQJQ4N"],"itemData":{"id":32130,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS One","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS One","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Public Library of Science San Francisco, CA USA","page":"e0118992","title":"Managing small-scale commercial fisheries for adaptive capacity: insights from dynamic social-ecological drivers of change in Monterey Bay","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Aguilera","given":"Stacy E"},{"family":"Cole","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Finkbeiner","given":"Elena M"},{"family":"Le Cornu","given":"Elodie"},{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C"},{"family":"Carr","given":"Mark H"},{"family":"Cinner","given":"Joshua E"},{"family":"Crowder","given":"Larry B"},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Hicks","given":"Christina C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} predict that governance, trade and market decisions will have a bigger impact on sustainable use of marine species than climate change, while another scenario predicts that the sustainable use of small pelagic fish depends more on how people respond to climate change rather than the change itself ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"C0VrzJyO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Le Bris, 2018; Merino, 2010; Niiranen, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Le Bris, 2018; Merino, 2010; Niiranen, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32240,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DGYBG7AJ"],"itemData":{"id":32240,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Marine Systems","issue":"1-2","language":"en","page":"196–205","title":"Climate variability and change scenarios for a marine commodity: modelling small pelagic fish, fisheries and fishmeal in a globalized market","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"Merino","given":"G."},{"family":"Barange","given":"M."},{"family":"Mullon","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32404,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4YCBYXAT"],"itemData":{"id":32404,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Biology","DOI":"10.1111/gcb.12309","ISSN":"13541013","journalAbbreviation":"Glob Change Biol","language":"en","page":"n/a-n/a","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Combined effects of global climate change and regional ecosystem drivers on an exploited marine food web","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/gcb.12309","author":[{"family":"Niiranen","given":"Susa"},{"family":"Yletyinen","given":"Johanna"},{"family":"Tomczak","given":"Maciej T."},{"family":"Blenckner","given":"Thorsten"},{"family":"Hjerne","given":"Olle"},{"family":"MacKenzie","given":"Brian R."},{"family":"Müller-Karulis","given":"Bärbel"},{"family":"Neumann","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Meier","given":"H. E. Markus"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",8]]}}},{"id":32405,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LZFCGTIG"],"itemData":{"id":32405,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Significance\\n            Climate change is impacting global fisheries and societies that depend on them. Identifying climate adaptation measures requires understanding how environmental changes and management policies interact in driving fishery productivity. Coincident with the recent exceptional warming of the northwest Atlantic Ocean, the American lobster has become the most valuable fishery resource in North America. Here we show that interactions between warming waters, ecosystem changes, and differences in conservation efforts led to the simultaneous collapse of lobster fishery in southern New England and record-breaking landings in the Gulf of Maine. Our results demonstrate that sound, widely adopted fishery conservation measures based on fundamental biological principles can help capitalize on gains and mitigate losses caused by global climate change.\\n          , \\n            Managing natural resources in an era of increasing climate impacts requires accounting for the synergistic effects of climate, ecosystem changes, and harvesting on resource productivity. Coincident with recent exceptional warming of the northwest Atlantic Ocean and removal of large predatory fish, the American lobster has become the most valuable fishery resource in North America. Using a model that links ocean temperature, predator density, and fishing to population productivity, we show that harvester-driven conservation efforts to protect large lobsters prepared the Gulf of Maine lobster fishery to capitalize on favorable ecosystem conditions, resulting in the record-breaking landings recently observed in the region. In contrast, in the warmer southern New England region, the absence of similar conservation efforts precipitated warming-induced recruitment failure that led to the collapse of the fishery. Population projections under expected warming suggest that the American lobster fishery is vulnerable to future temperature increases, but continued efforts to preserve the stock's reproductive potential can dampen the negative impacts of warming. This study demonstrates that, even though global climate change is severely impacting marine ecosystems, widely adopted, proactive conservation measures can increase the resilience of commercial fisheries to climate change.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1711122115","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U.S.A.","language":"en","page":"1831-1836","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Climate vulnerability and resilience in the most valuable North American fishery","URL":"https://pnas.org/doi/full/10.1073/pnas.1711122115","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Le Bris","given":"Arnault"},{"family":"Mills","given":"Katherine E."},{"family":"Wahle","given":"Richard A."},{"family":"Chen","given":"Yong"},{"family":"Alexander","given":"Michael A."},{"family":"Allyn","given":"Andrew J."},{"family":"Schuetz","given":"Justin G."},{"family":"Scott","given":"James D."},{"family":"Pershing","given":"Andrew J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. What emerges from the existing literature is that climate change will interact with other environmental, socio-cultural, political, and economic drivers to negatively impact the marine, freshwater, and forest ecosystems, which in turn affect how humans use wild species.  As a complex, major driver of change, climate change needs to be considered together with other multiple and interacting drivers of sustainable use, with possibilities resulting in “non-linear abrupt change”ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DVGGd5HA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. H. Schneider, 2004)","plainCitation":"(S. H. Schneider, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32219,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N3CGMMC7"],"itemData":{"id":32219,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"245–258","title":"Abrupt non-linear climate change, irreversibility and surprise","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Schneider","given":"S.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, so-called “tipping points”. 
      The negative impacts of climate change and hydrometeorological hazards on sustainable use of wild species will have more serious implications for countries in the global South. Increased intensity and frequency of extreme climate and weather events from global warming will disproportionately affect vulnerable and poor people, especially in Africa and Asia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Xy9zVvQc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoegh-Guldberg, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hoegh-Guldberg, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32304,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DC9XB7R5"],"itemData":{"id":32304,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Global Warming of 1.5°C","language":"English","publisher":"IPCC","title":"Impacts of 1.5ºC global warming on natural and human systems","author":[{"family":"Hoegh-Guldberg","given":"Ove"},{"family":"Jacob","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Bindi","given":"Marco"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Sally"},{"family":"Camilloni","given":"Ines"},{"family":"Diedhiou","given":"Arona"},{"family":"Djalante","given":"Riyanti"},{"family":"Ebi","given":"Kristie L."},{"family":"Engelbrecht","given":"Francois"},{"family":"Guiot","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Hijioka","given":"Yasuaki"},{"family":"Mehrotra","given":"Shagun"},{"family":"Payne","given":"Antony"},{"family":"Seneviratne","given":"Sonia I."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Adelle"},{"family":"Warren","given":"Rachel F."},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Guangsheng"},{"family":"Tschakert","given":"Petra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In particular, Small Island Development States are expected to be particularly at risk from impacts of multiple hazards, which can compound the effects of each other. Small Island Development States and populations in the global South have limited capacities to adapt, thus making impacts from climate change and related hazards more serious ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oUlp74r7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoegh-Guldberg, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hoegh-Guldberg, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32304,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DC9XB7R5"],"itemData":{"id":32304,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Global Warming of 1.5°C","language":"English","publisher":"IPCC","title":"Impacts of 1.5ºC global warming on natural and human systems","author":[{"family":"Hoegh-Guldberg","given":"Ove"},{"family":"Jacob","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Bindi","given":"Marco"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Sally"},{"family":"Camilloni","given":"Ines"},{"family":"Diedhiou","given":"Arona"},{"family":"Djalante","given":"Riyanti"},{"family":"Ebi","given":"Kristie L."},{"family":"Engelbrecht","given":"Francois"},{"family":"Guiot","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Hijioka","given":"Yasuaki"},{"family":"Mehrotra","given":"Shagun"},{"family":"Payne","given":"Antony"},{"family":"Seneviratne","given":"Sonia I."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Adelle"},{"family":"Warren","given":"Rachel F."},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Guangsheng"},{"family":"Tschakert","given":"Petra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This will have particularly serious implications for the case for fishing, for which there are not many studies being done, and thus insufficient data, for countries in the global South ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ld9bu1TS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Comte & Pendleton, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Comte & Pendleton, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32281,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TZIMWYZ5"],"itemData":{"id":32281,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of environmental management","language":"en","page":"462–474","title":"Management strategies for coral reefs and people under global environmental change: 25 years of scientific research","volume":"209","author":[{"family":"Comte","given":"A."},{"family":"Pendleton","given":"L.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many fisheries-dependent tropical regions are more at risk to climate change impacts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CcQ1BXwW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(W. W. Cheung, 2018; Lam, 2016; A. L. Perry, 2010; Reygondeau, 2019)","plainCitation":"(W. W. Cheung, 2018; Lam, 2016; A. L. Perry, 2010; Reygondeau, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32113,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PTUVYK4I"],"itemData":{"id":32113,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","issue":"4","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"464–475","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Fisheries, large-scale trade, and conservation of seahorses in Malaysia and Thailand","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Perry","given":"Allison L."},{"family":"Lunn","given":"Kristin E."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"Amanda CJ"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32292,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QEDQGZWR"],"itemData":{"id":32292,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep32607","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"32607","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Projected change in global fisheries revenues under climate change","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/srep32607","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Lam","given":"Vicky W. Y."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"William W. L."},{"family":"Reygondeau","given":"Gabriel"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U. Rashid"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}},{"id":32280,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DVNAMV7I"],"itemData":{"id":32280,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global change biology","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"5149–5163","title":"Opportunities for climate?risk reduction through effective fisheries management","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Cheung","given":"W.W."},{"family":"Jones","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Reygondeau","given":"G."},{"family":"Frölicher","given":"T.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32226,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QLVY8QL7"],"itemData":{"id":32226,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Predicting future oceans","language":"en","page":"87–101","publisher":"Elsevier","title":"Current and future biogeography of exploited marine groups under climate change","author":[{"family":"Reygondeau","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and have experienced major declines in fish stocks since the 1990s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kaPV5mLW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Golden, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Golden, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32115,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G2I5SN3J"],"itemData":{"id":32115,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/534317a","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7607","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"317-320","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Nutrition: Fall in fish catch threatens human health","title-short":"Nutrition","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/534317a","volume":"534","author":[{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"William W. L."},{"family":"Dey","given":"Madan M."},{"family":"Halpern","given":"Benjamin S."},{"family":"McCauley","given":"Douglas J."},{"family":"Smith","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Vaitla","given":"Bapu"},{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"},{"family":"Myers","given":"Samuel S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, with the biggest projected decrease in catch potential and revenue decrease in the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5gT8CpXk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lam, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Lam, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32292,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QEDQGZWR"],"itemData":{"id":32292,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep32607","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"32607","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Projected change in global fisheries revenues under climate change","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/srep32607","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Lam","given":"Vicky W. Y."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"William W. L."},{"family":"Reygondeau","given":"Gabriel"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U. Rashid"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Food security is thus expected to be an issue in countries in the global South as a result of climate change's impact on fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QpVscitp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ficke, 2007; R. L. White, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Ficke, 2007; R. L. White, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32269,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IUW2AC72"],"itemData":{"id":32269,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"581–613","title":"Potential impacts of global climate change on freshwater fisheries","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Ficke","given":"A.D."},{"family":"Myrick","given":"C.A."},{"family":"Hansen","given":"L.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":32114,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BCUK8PVJ"],"itemData":{"id":32114,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0193993","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e0193993","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Birds in the playground: Evaluating the effectiveness of an urban environmental education project in enhancing school children’s awareness, knowledge and attitudes towards local wildlife","title-short":"Birds in the playground","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0193993","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"White","given":"Rachel L."},{"family":"Eberstein","given":"Katie"},{"family":"Scott","given":"Dawn M."}],"editor":[{"family":"Ito","given":"Etsuro"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3,6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, it is well-established that communities that rely on fishing for their livelihoods would suffer more from the impacts of climate change, such as smaller fishing communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CeLhxQrm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Frusher, 2016; Tull, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Frusher, 2016; Tull, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32118,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/REVCINDS"],"itemData":{"id":32118,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Oceanography","language":"en","page":"19–28","title":"From physics to fish to folk: supporting coastal regional communities to understand their vulnerability to climate change in Australia","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Frusher","given":"S."},{"family":"Putten","given":"I."},{"family":"Haward","given":"M."},{"family":"Hobday","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Holbrook","given":"N.J."},{"family":"Jennings","given":"S."},{"family":"Tull","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32400,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3N5DGWH2"],"itemData":{"id":32400,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"1437–1446","title":"The economic and social impacts of environmental change on fishing towns and coastal communities: a historical case study of Geraldton, Western Australia","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Tull","given":"M."},{"family":"Metcalf","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Gray","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and traditional fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cPcqC426","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vivekanandan, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Vivekanandan, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32127,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VTNSYZ8R"],"itemData":{"id":32127,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental development","language":"en","page":"46–56","title":"Climate change effects in the Bay of Bengal large marine ecosystem","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Vivekanandan","given":"E."},{"family":"Hermes","given":"R."},{"family":"O’Brien","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is also the case for low-income food deficit countries, which heavily depend on fisheries for their national ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1LH8xEAa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lam, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Lam, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32292,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QEDQGZWR"],"itemData":{"id":32292,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep32607","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"32607","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Projected change in global fisheries revenues under climate change","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/srep32607","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Lam","given":"Vicky W. Y."},{"family":"Cheung","given":"William W. L."},{"family":"Reygondeau","given":"Gabriel"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U. Rashid"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The increasing vulnerability of coastal fishing communities especially in the global South point to a need for adaptation planning to mitigate the impacts of climate change and related hazards on unsustainable use of wild species.  Multi-level coordination across stakeholders on conserving fisheries and associated species and ecosystems would need to include interventions on land as well as in the ocean and freshwater water bodies, using, for example, ecosystem-based approaches ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6lgSqzxY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Furthermore, it is necessary to consider how climate change impacts indigenous peoples, who usually live in areas that are more exposed to impacts of climate change and associated hazards, and thus, are experiencing profound, negative impacts on their cultures, health, well-being, livelihoods, rights and ultimately, their own survival ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JZGZJTQs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Galloway McLean, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Galloway McLean, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32119,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X7H3VSLF"],"itemData":{"id":32119,"type":"book","event-place":"Anchorage","language":"en","publisher":"Alaska. United Nations University – Traditional Knowledge Initiative","publisher-place":"Anchorage","title":"Report of the Indigenous Peoples’ Global Summit on Climate Change","author":[{"family":"Galloway McLean","given":"K."},{"family":"Ramos-Castillo","given":"A."},{"family":"Gross","given":"T."},{"family":"Johnston","given":"S."},{"family":"Vierros","given":"M."},{"family":"Noa","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  While the well-established evidence of indigenous communities using their traditional ecological knowledge to adapt how they harvest wild species as the climate changes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zr99ZTFB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes & Jolly, 2002; Berman & Kofinas, 2004; Sabo, 1991)","plainCitation":"(Berkes & Jolly, 2002; Berman & Kofinas, 2004; Sabo, 1991)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32215,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DU9YQU3I"],"itemData":{"id":32215,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Garland, New York, New York, USA","title":"Long term adaptations among Arctic hunter-gatherers: a case study from southern Baffin Island","author":[{"family":"Sabo","given":"G.","suffix":"III"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}},{"id":32298,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YF7R333Q"],"itemData":{"id":32298,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation ecology","issue":"2","language":"en","title":"Adapting to climate change: social-ecological resilience in a Canadian western Arctic community","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Jolly","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":32297,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KMJPJXSD"],"itemData":{"id":32297,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"31–46","title":"Hunting for models: grounded and rational choice approaches to analyzing climate effects on subsistence hunting in an Arctic community","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Berman","given":"M."},{"family":"Kofinas","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or how they could adapt ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tsJqbGOf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gautam, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Gautam, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32122,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6GXZWCEI"],"itemData":{"id":32122,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Climate Change and Indigenous Peoples in the United States","event-place":"Cham","language":"en","page":"77–91","publisher":"Springer","publisher-place":"Cham","title":"Climate change in arid lands and Native American socioeconomic vulnerability: The case of the Pyramid Lake Paiute Tribe","author":[{"family":"Gautam","given":"M.R."},{"family":"Chief","given":"K."},{"family":"Smith","given":"W.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, impacts of climate change—along with other endogenous and exogenous factors—on how wild species can be used will have serious repercussions especially for indigenous peoples and local communities who rely on these species for their nutritional needs, as well as for social and economic well-being, heath, and cultural survival ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"y0QLqjQX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nuttall, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Nuttall, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32230,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UPCAA7N7"],"itemData":{"id":32230,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Arctic: Arctic Climate Impact Assessment, by Arctic Climate Impact Assessment (ACIA","language":"en","page":"649–690","publisher":"ACIA Overview Report. Cambridge University Press","title":"Hunting, Herding, Fishing, and Gathering: Indigenous Peoples and Renewable Resource Use in the Arctic","URL":"https://www.amap.no/documents/doc/arctic-arctic-climate-impact-assessment/796.","author":[{"family":"Nuttall","given":"Mark"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Further, there is established but incomplete evidence that indigenous and local knowledge can play a role to mitigate the negative impacts of unsustainable use of wild species exacerbated by climate change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"huhY4xcB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schmitt, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Schmitt, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32217,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NPNL9X4L"],"itemData":{"id":32217,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Viet Nam. Journal of Coastal Conservation","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"545–558","title":"Site-specific and integrated adaptation to climate change in the coastal mangrove zone of Soc Trang Province","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Schmitt","given":"K."},{"family":"Albers","given":"T."},{"family":"Pham","given":"T.T."},{"family":"Dinh","given":"S.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, thus underlining the importance of co-management of areas with local communities and integrating local and indigenous knowledge to develop strategies to build resilience.
      
4.2.1.3. Land/ecosystem degradation 
According to the IPBES assessment “Land degradation” is defined as the human-caused processes that drive the decline or loss in biodiversity, ecosystem functions or ecosystem services in any terrestrial and associated aquatic ecosystems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Mh7N4mjL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2018b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2018b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":86,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KXQGTCY8"],"itemData":{"id":86,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-09-6","number-of-pages":"744","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"The IPBES assessment report on Land Degradation and Restoration","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3237392","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"Montanarella","given":"L"},{"family":"Scholes","given":"R"},{"family":"Brainich","given":"A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Specifically, land degradation is the reduction or loss of biological or economic productivity and complexity (including soil erosion, deterioration in physical, chemical, biological or economic properties of soils and long term loss of vegetation) of cropland, rangeland, pastureland, forest and woodlands in arid, semi-arid and dry sub-humid areas, that results from land uses or from a combination of processes, including those arising from human activities and habitation patterns ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SnLyq3ec","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2018b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2018b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":86,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KXQGTCY8"],"itemData":{"id":86,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-09-6","number-of-pages":"744","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"The IPBES assessment report on Land Degradation and Restoration","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3237392","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"Montanarella","given":"L"},{"family":"Scholes","given":"R"},{"family":"Brainich","given":"A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The land degradation is occurring in all land cover, land use and landscape types, in all countries.
	Although a national level framework for assessing and reversing ecosystem degradation to support the national implementation of Aichi Biodiversity Target 15 and EU Biodiversity Strategy Target 2 is available ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dRJqOk8w","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kotiaho, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Kotiaho, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":700,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8GV4SYMJ"],"itemData":{"id":700,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7597","language":"en","page":"37","title":"Land use: A global baseline for ecosystem recovery","volume":"532","author":[{"family":"Kotiaho","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Ten Brink","given":"B."},{"family":"Harris","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, unfortunately, assessing ecosystem degradation and recovery at the global scale, is not feasible ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"I3Tlm5Pr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2018b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2018b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":86,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KXQGTCY8"],"itemData":{"id":86,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-09-6","number-of-pages":"744","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"The IPBES assessment report on Land Degradation and Restoration","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3237392","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"Montanarella","given":"L"},{"family":"Scholes","given":"R"},{"family":"Brainich","given":"A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. To assess anthropogenic ecosystem degradation, the reference condition of pre-degradation state also known as its natural state is necessary, but it is a challenge to establish the natural state for an ecosystem because humans have been influencing the system for such a long time.
Multiple drivers including land use change, agricultural intensification, pollution, and invasive alien species drive land degradation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZxjPWsvc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2018b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2018b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":86,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KXQGTCY8"],"itemData":{"id":86,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-09-6","number-of-pages":"744","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"The IPBES assessment report on Land Degradation and Restoration","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3237392","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"Montanarella","given":"L"},{"family":"Scholes","given":"R"},{"family":"Brainich","given":"A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For instance, the damage cost of environmental degradation in Middle East and North Africa is estimated at US$ 9 billion per year, with a mean estimate of 5.7% of gross domestic product ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VKkAeAur","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hussein, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Hussein, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":693,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X6U5DPAR"],"itemData":{"id":693,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Management of Environmental Quality","language":"en","page":"305–317","title":"Costs of Environmental Degradation: An Analysis in the Middle East and North Africa Region","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Hussein","given":"M.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The land degradation in Syria due to high soil salinity resulted in a 37% decline in yields of cotton and wheat (the main irrigated crops), which represents the total annual loss in agricultural productivity at around US$80 million. The bushmeat hunting for food and medicinal products is driving a global crisis whereby 301 terrestrial mammal species are threatened with extinction, which is exacerbated by threats include deforestation, agricultural expansion, human encroachment and competition with livestock ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Y2uJpQoK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ripple, Chapron,, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Ripple, Chapron,, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32523,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ETTD9UU9"],"itemData":{"id":32523,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1093/biosci/biw092","ISSN":"0006-3568","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"BioScience","page":"807-812","source":"Silverchair","title":"Saving the World's Terrestrial Megafauna","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1093/biosci/biw092","volume":"66","author":[{"family":"Ripple","given":"William J."},{"family":"Chapron","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"López-Bao","given":"José Vicente"},{"family":"Durant","given":"Sarah M."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."},{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter A."},{"family":"Bennett","given":"Elizabeth L."},{"family":"Beschta","given":"Robert L."},{"family":"Bruskotter","given":"Jeremy T."},{"family":"Campos-Arceiz","given":"Ahimsa"},{"family":"Corlett","given":"Richard T."},{"family":"Darimont","given":"Chris T."},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy J."},{"family":"Dirzo","given":"Rodolfo"},{"family":"Dublin","given":"Holly T."},{"family":"Estes","given":"James A."},{"family":"Everatt","given":"Kristoffer T."},{"family":"Galetti","given":"Mauro"},{"family":"Goswami","given":"Varun R."},{"family":"Hayward","given":"Matt W."},{"family":"Hedges","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Hunter","given":"Luke T. B."},{"family":"Kerley","given":"Graham I. H."},{"family":"Letnic","given":"Mike"},{"family":"Levi","given":"Taal"},{"family":"Maisels","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Morrison","given":"John C."},{"family":"Nelson","given":"Michael Paul"},{"family":"Newsome","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Painter","given":"Luke"},{"family":"Pringle","given":"Robert M."},{"family":"Sandom","given":"Christopher J."},{"family":"Terborgh","given":"John"},{"family":"Treves","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Van Valkenburgh","given":"Blaire"},{"family":"Vucetich","given":"John A."},{"family":"Wirsing","given":"Aaron J."},{"family":"Wallach","given":"Arian D."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Woodroffe","given":"Rosie"},{"family":"Young","given":"Hillary"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Li"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",10,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Degradation of habitat negatively impacts faunal community as a whole in Southeast Asia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lbtJwIhh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tilker, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Tilker, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":646,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D23WBJ8R"],"itemData":{"id":646,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Communications biology","ISSN":"2399-3642","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Communications biology","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"1-11","title":"Habitat degradation and indiscriminate hunting differentially impact faunal communities in the Southeast Asian tropical biodiversity hotspot","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Tilker","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Abrams","given":"Jesse F"},{"family":"Mohamed","given":"Azlan"},{"family":"Nguyen","given":"An"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Seth T"},{"family":"Sollmann","given":"Rahel"},{"family":"Niedballa","given":"Jürgen"},{"family":"Bhagwat","given":"Tejas"},{"family":"Gray","given":"Thomas NE"},{"family":"Rawson","given":"Benjamin M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which results in a decrease of hunting practice of communities. Further, spatially explicit models at the global scale revealed 121–219 species in Borneo, the central Amazon and the Congo Basin will become threatened under current rates of forest loss over the next 30 years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"z5XzDfe2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Betts, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Betts, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33131,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VNTPK5VD"],"itemData":{"id":33131,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7664","language":"en","page":"441","title":"Global forest loss disproportionately erodes biodiversity in intact landscapes","volume":"547","author":[{"family":"Betts","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"C."},{"family":"Ripple","given":"W.J."},{"family":"Phalan","given":"B."},{"family":"Millers","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Duarte","given":"A."},{"family":"Levi","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In marine system, coral habitat degradation due to anthropogenic pressures can have varying degrees of effects on reef fisheries. For instance, habitat degradation along with plastic pollution compounds effects of fishing on coral reefs as increased fishing reduces large-bodied target species, while habitat loss results in fewer small-bodied juveniles and prey that replenish stocks and provide dietary resources for predatory target species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cnkzfPMw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. K. Wilson, 2010)","plainCitation":"(S. K. Wilson, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":627,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QLNWZZ37"],"itemData":{"id":627,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","language":"en","page":"442–451","title":"Habitat degradation and fishing effects on the size 789 structure of coral reef fish communities","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Wilson","given":"S.K."},{"family":"Fisher","given":"R."},{"family":"Pratchett","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Graham","given":"N.A.J."},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"N.K."},{"family":"Turner","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Cakacaka","given":"A."},{"family":"Polunin","given":"N.V.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Drylands around the world are undergoing rapid land degradation and shifts in vegetation composition in response to climate change and anthropogenic disturbances. Accelerated hydrological–aeolian erosion processes and rapid vegetation shifts are recognized as important drivers of land degradation. Soil erosion is a major concern for environment and natural resources leading to the reduction in field productivity and soil quality resulting in land degradation. The erosion reduces biomass and productivity by diminishing soil organic matter and soil quality which ultimately influences the diversity of plants, animals, and microbes present in an entire ecosystem. It is estimated that each year about 10 million ha of cropland worldwide is lost due to soil erosion thus reducing the cropland available for food production ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TTUQ2tgd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pimentel, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Pimentel, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":681,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ATKT657T"],"itemData":{"id":681,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","language":"en","page":"119–137","title":"Soil erosion: A food and environmental threat","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Pimentel","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the rate of erosion differs across the continents. For example, soil erosion has little impact on crop productivity in Europe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pOnZYx4I","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bakker, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Bakker, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":629,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AU4PIGTZ"],"itemData":{"id":629,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystems","language":"en","page":"1209–1219","title":"The effect of soil erosion on Europe's crop yields","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Bakker","given":"M.M."},{"family":"Govers","given":"G."},{"family":"Jones","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Rounsevell","given":"M.D.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, while losses associated with erosion are highest in agroecosystems of Asia, Africa, and South America (30-40 tonnes per hectare every year; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ohtT0JdG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Taddese, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Taddese, 2001)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":628,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A6XM92WD"],"itemData":{"id":628,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","language":"fr","page":"815–824","title":"Land Degradation: A Challenge to Ethiopia","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Taddese","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

4.2.1.4 Invasive alien species 
      Invasive alien species are plants, animals, pathogens and other organisms that are non-native to an ecosystem, and which may cause economic or environmental harm or adversely affect human health (https://www.cbd.int/idb/2009/about/what/). They can impact on the sustainable use of wild species, both positively and negatively, by altering the abundance of the wild species, by providing a substitute to the species that have historically been used by indigenous peoples and local communities, and by impacting on alternative resources such as when agricultural crops or livestock are negatively affected by invasive species. 
      Invasive alien species are considered as key drivers of human-caused global environmental change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XpkhRSxn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vitousek, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Vitousek, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33149,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HUSXGET2"],"itemData":{"id":33149,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"New Zealand Journal of Ecology","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–16","title":"Introduced species: a significant component of human-caused global change","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Vitousek","given":"P.M."},{"family":"D'antonio","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Loope","given":"L.L."},{"family":"Rejmanek","given":"M."},{"family":"Westbrooks","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The number of invasive alien species belonging to the different taxonomic groups except for microorganisms and many invertebrates is relatively well known now. Almost 4% of the global plants (~14000 plant species) has become naturalized in ecosystems other than their native ones ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RiwmmPuV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Py\\\\uc0\\\\u353{}ek, 2020; Van Kleunen, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Pyšek, 2020; Van Kleunen, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":683,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KZHNQV6B"],"itemData":{"id":683,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Reviews","DOI":"10.1111/brv.12627.","language":"en","title":"Scientists' warning on invasive alien species","volume":"95","editor":[{"family":"Pyšek","given":"Petr"},{"family":"Hulme","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Simberloff","given":"Dan"},{"family":"Bacher","given":"Sven"},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Carlton","given":"James"},{"family":"Dawson","given":"Wayne"},{"family":"Essl","given":"Franz"},{"family":"Foxcroft","given":"Llewellyn"},{"family":"Genovesi","given":"Piero"},{"family":"Jeschke","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Kühn","given":"Ingolf"},{"family":"Liebhold","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Mandrak","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Meyerson","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Pauchard","given":"Anibal"},{"family":"Pergl","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Roy","given":"Helen"},{"family":"Seebens","given":"Hanno"},{"family":"Richardson","given":"David"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":33147,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QLATMBNR"],"itemData":{"id":33147,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7567","language":"en","page":"100","title":"Global exchange and accumulation of non-native plants","volume":"525","author":[{"family":"Van Kleunen","given":"M."},{"family":"Dawson","given":"W."},{"family":"Essl","given":"F."},{"family":"Pergl","given":"J."},{"family":"Winter","given":"M."},{"family":"Weber","given":"E."},{"family":"Antonova","given":"L.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Of the 1,517 recorded invasive alien species, 39% were introduced intentionally and 26% unintentionally, 22% both intentionally and unintentionally, while 13% had no information available ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XJXuoVU2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Turbelin, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Turbelin, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":667,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/39GA3QVF"],"itemData":{"id":667,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Biogeography","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"78–92","title":"Mapping the global state of invasive alien species: patterns of invasion and policy responses","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Turbelin","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Malamud","given":"B.D."},{"family":"Francis","given":"R.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. About 0.5% and 0.7% of the world's tree and shrub species (622 species) are currently invasive outside their natural range but woody plant invasions are rapidly increasing around the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"y6MsFZoo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. M. Richardson & Rejm\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}nek, 2011)","plainCitation":"(D. M. Richardson & Rejmánek, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":686,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S5MNJZAT"],"itemData":{"id":686,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Diversity and distributions","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"788–809","title":"Trees and shrubs as invasive alien species–a global review","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Richardson","given":"D.M."},{"family":"Rejmánek","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Alien insect species outnumber invasions of all other animal taxa; North America alone has 3,200 species of non-native insects ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3h6SCM9m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Liebhold, 2018; Py\\\\uc0\\\\u353{}ek, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Liebhold, 2018; Pyšek, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":645,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UCWG8SCE"],"itemData":{"id":645,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Scientific Reports","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"1-5","title":"Plant diversity drives global patterns of insect invasions","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Liebhold","given":"Andrew M"},{"family":"Yamanaka","given":"Takehiko"},{"family":"Roques","given":"Alain"},{"family":"Augustin","given":"Sylvie"},{"family":"Chown","given":"Steven L"},{"family":"Brockerhoff","given":"Eckehard G"},{"family":"Pyšek","given":"Petr"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":683,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KZHNQV6B"],"itemData":{"id":683,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Reviews","DOI":"10.1111/brv.12627.","language":"en","title":"Scientists' warning on invasive alien species","volume":"95","editor":[{"family":"Pyšek","given":"Petr"},{"family":"Hulme","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Simberloff","given":"Dan"},{"family":"Bacher","given":"Sven"},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Carlton","given":"James"},{"family":"Dawson","given":"Wayne"},{"family":"Essl","given":"Franz"},{"family":"Foxcroft","given":"Llewellyn"},{"family":"Genovesi","given":"Piero"},{"family":"Jeschke","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Kühn","given":"Ingolf"},{"family":"Liebhold","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Mandrak","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Meyerson","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Pauchard","given":"Anibal"},{"family":"Pergl","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Roy","given":"Helen"},{"family":"Seebens","given":"Hanno"},{"family":"Richardson","given":"David"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At least 175 species of gastropods have become established across 56 countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"w8ZfhbMr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Capinha, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Capinha, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":610,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LRGUVFGQ"],"itemData":{"id":610,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Emerging patterns in species distribution\\n            \\n              Human influences are leading to a shift in the geographical distribution of animal species. Capinha\\n             \\n              compared the distributions of native mollusk species with those introduced to new areas by human activities. The ranges of native species are still broadly constrained by limitations on their capacity for dispersal, whereas those of the introduced aliens are affected more by climate and patterns of human movement. As humans continue to break down barriers to dispersal, more species' distributions will come to be limited by their environmental tolerances.\\n            \\n            \\n              Science\\n              , this issue p.\\n              1248\\n            \\n          , \\n            Human-mediated dispersal of mollusks is changing the way they can colonize new environments.\\n          , \\n            It has been argued that globalization in human-mediated dispersal of species breaks down biogeographic boundaries, yet empirical tests are still missing. We used data on native and alien ranges of terrestrial gastropods to analyze dissimilarities in species composition among 56 globally distributed regions. We found that native ranges confirm the traditional biogeographic realms, reflecting natural dispersal limitations. However, the distributions of gastropods after human transport are primarily explained by the prevailing climate and, to a smaller extent, by distance and trade relationships. Our findings show that human-mediated dispersal is causing a breakdown of biogeographic barriers, and that climate and to some extent socioeconomic relationships will define biogeography in an era of global change.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aaa8913","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6240","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"1248-1251","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The dispersal of alien species redefines biogeography in the Anthropocene","URL":"https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aaa8913","volume":"348","author":[{"family":"Capinha","given":"César"},{"family":"Essl","given":"Franz"},{"family":"Seebens","given":"Hanno"},{"family":"Moser","given":"Dietmar"},{"family":"Pereira","given":"Henrique Miguel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",6,12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and 745 alien species of freshwater fish species have led to established alien populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VzhW5fri","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tedesco, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Tedesco, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":609,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GJPM7BMK"],"itemData":{"id":609,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Data","DOI":"10.1038/sdata.2017.141","ISSN":"2052-4463","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Data","language":"en","page":"170141","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A global database on freshwater fish species occurrence in drainage basins","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/sdata2017141","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Tedesco","given":"Pablo A."},{"family":"Beauchard","given":"Olivier"},{"family":"Bigorne","given":"Rémy"},{"family":"Blanchet","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Buisson","given":"Laëtitia"},{"family":"Conti","given":"Lorenza"},{"family":"Cornu","given":"Jean-François"},{"family":"Dias","given":"Murilo S."},{"family":"Grenouillet","given":"Gaël"},{"family":"Hugueny","given":"Bernard"},{"family":"Jézéquel","given":"Céline"},{"family":"Leprieur","given":"Fabien"},{"family":"Brosse","given":"Sébastien"},{"family":"Oberdorff","given":"Thierry"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At least 78 species of alien amphibian and 198 species of alien reptiles were established outside their native range ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"970TrfFY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Capinha, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Capinha, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":613,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UH63AUNV"],"itemData":{"id":613,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Diversity and Distributions","DOI":"10.1111/ddi.12617","ISSN":"1366-9516, 1472-4642","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Divers Distrib","language":"en","page":"1313-1322","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Diversity, biogeography and the global flows of alien amphibians and reptiles","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ddi.12617","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Capinha","given":"César"},{"family":"Seebens","given":"Hanno"},{"family":"Cassey","given":"Phillip"},{"family":"García?Díaz","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Lenzner","given":"Bernd"},{"family":"Mang","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Moser","given":"Dietmar"},{"family":"Pyšek","given":"Petr"},{"family":"Rödder","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Scalera","given":"Riccardo"},{"family":"Winter","given":"Marten"},{"family":"Dullinger","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Essl","given":"Franz"}],"editor":[{"family":"Nori","given":"Javier"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Likewise, a total of 3,661 alien bird introduction records was reported of which 37% of these species have become established ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wlP8NMkk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dyer, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Dyer, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":612,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KK7B2UM8"],"itemData":{"id":612,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLOS Biology","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.2000942","ISSN":"1545-7885","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS Biol","language":"en","page":"e2000942","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Global Distribution and Drivers of Alien Bird Species Richness","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.2000942","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Dyer","given":"Ellie E."},{"family":"Cassey","given":"Phillip"},{"family":"Redding","given":"David W."},{"family":"Collen","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Franks","given":"Victoria"},{"family":"Gaston","given":"Kevin J."},{"family":"Jones","given":"Kate E."},{"family":"Kark","given":"Salit"},{"family":"Orme","given":"C. David L."},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim M."}],"editor":[{"family":"Ricklefs","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The cumulative number of alien species richness across six taxonomic groups is given in Figure 4.2.
      

Figure 4.2. Trends in Cumulative in established alien species across six taxonomic groups in four regions of the world. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Pr1K0K8B","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Py\\\\uc0\\\\u353{}ek, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Pyšek, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":683,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KZHNQV6B"],"itemData":{"id":683,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Reviews","DOI":"10.1111/brv.12627.","language":"en","title":"Scientists' warning on invasive alien species","volume":"95","editor":[{"family":"Pyšek","given":"Petr"},{"family":"Hulme","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Simberloff","given":"Dan"},{"family":"Bacher","given":"Sven"},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Carlton","given":"James"},{"family":"Dawson","given":"Wayne"},{"family":"Essl","given":"Franz"},{"family":"Foxcroft","given":"Llewellyn"},{"family":"Genovesi","given":"Piero"},{"family":"Jeschke","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Kühn","given":"Ingolf"},{"family":"Liebhold","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Mandrak","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Meyerson","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Pauchard","given":"Anibal"},{"family":"Pergl","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Roy","given":"Helen"},{"family":"Seebens","given":"Hanno"},{"family":"Richardson","given":"David"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license CC BY-4.0.
      
      A wide range of invasive alien species either introduced deliberately (fish farming, pet trade, horticulture, forestry, agriculture, biocontrol) or unintentionally (transportation, travel and scientific research) through human transport and commerce has caused a loss in global economy, human health, agriculture and overall wellbeing of humans (https://www.cbd.int/undb/media/factsheets/undb-factsheet-ias-en.pdf). There are six broad introduction mechanism of alien species: a) deliberate release (e.g. game animals, sport fishes, pets); b) escape from captivity (e.g. ornamental garden plants, pets); c) contaminants of commodities (e.g. weed seeds, pest insects, microbial pathogens); d) stowaways on transport vectors (e.g. marine organisms fouling ship hulls or in ballast water, latent endophytic pathogens in plants); e) via anthropogenic corridors (such as through the Suez and Panama Canals); or f) unaided spread from other invaded regions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6MmJpbVq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. E. Hulme, 2009a)","plainCitation":"(P. E. Hulme, 2009a)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":692,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/427NIZSB"],"itemData":{"id":692,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of applied ecology","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"10–18","title":"Trade, transport and trouble: managing invasive species pathways in an era of globalization","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Hulme","given":"P.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Species escape from Horticulture, forestry and agroforestry and the nursery are the dominant pathway for species invasions accounting for 31% of the species introduced outside of their natural geographical range ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dKKw9cJh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Turbelin, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Turbelin, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":667,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/39GA3QVF"],"itemData":{"id":667,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Biogeography","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"78–92","title":"Mapping the global state of invasive alien species: patterns of invasion and policy responses","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Turbelin","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Malamud","given":"B.D."},{"family":"Francis","given":"R.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The recent growth in the trade of exotic pets is another growing threat to species biological invasion  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IYyLus4G","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lockwood, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Lockwood, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":611,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S9IEE3UZ"],"itemData":{"id":611,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","DOI":"10.1002/fee.2059","ISSN":"1540-9295, 1540-9309","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Front Ecol Environ","language":"en","page":"323-330","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"When pets become pests: the role of the exotic pet trade in producing invasive vertebrate animals","title-short":"When pets become pests","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/fee.2059","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Lockwood","given":"Julie L"},{"family":"Welbourne","given":"Dustin J"},{"family":"Romagosa","given":"Christina M"},{"family":"Cassey","given":"Phillip"},{"family":"Mandrak","given":"Nicholas E"},{"family":"Strecker","given":"Angela"},{"family":"Leung","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Stringham","given":"Oliver C"},{"family":"Udell","given":"Bradley"},{"family":"Episcopio?Sturgeon","given":"Diane J"},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F"},{"family":"Sinclair","given":"James"},{"family":"Springborn","given":"Michael R"},{"family":"Pienaar","given":"Elizabeth F"},{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L"},{"family":"Keller","given":"Reuben"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some of these pet species cause zoonotic diseases, particularly those pets sourced from the wild ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HYhXoFx2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Day, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Day, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33121,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G8K8AZBU"],"itemData":{"id":33121,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Parasites & vectors","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–6","title":"One health: the importance of companion animal vector-borne diseases","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Day","given":"M.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Invasive species generally have higher values of performance-related traits characterizing physiology, leaf?area allocation, shoot allocation, growth rate, size, and fitness than non?invasive plant species do, and these traits promote invasiveness under many different circumstances ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CvF5fJhs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Van Kleunen, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Van Kleunen, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33146,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DT2B4FJ2"],"itemData":{"id":33146,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology letters","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"235–245","title":"A meta?analysis of trait differences between invasive and non?invasive plant species","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Van Kleunen","given":"M."},{"family":"Weber","given":"E."},{"family":"Fischer","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Invasive species also have greater plasticity in their response to greater resource availability than non?invasive, but this plasticity was only sometimes associated with a fitness benefit ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VgIphjd3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Davidson, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Davidson, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33123,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5AJP6FFV"],"itemData":{"id":33123,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology letters","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"419–431","title":"Do invasive species show higher phenotypic plasticity than native species and, if so, is it adaptive? A meta?analysis","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Davidson","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Jennions","given":"M."},{"family":"Nicotra","given":"A.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Individual plants and animals are also generally less frequently infected (prevalence minus percent individuals infected) in introduced compared to native conspecific populations; and introduced animals and plants may escape 75% or more of the parasite and pathogen species from their native range ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6UgneGxw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Torchin & Mitchell, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Torchin & Mitchell, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/URQ9BBCW"],"itemData":{"id":666,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"183–190","title":"Parasites, pathogens, and invasions by plants and animals","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Torchin","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Mitchell","given":"C.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Invasive species may not have equal impacts on the sustainable use of wild species across all environments. Substantial variation in the spatial patterns of invasion was observed, small tropical islands and coastal mainland regions are the main recipients of invasive alien species and hotspots of established alien species richness across multiple taxonomic groups ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6jweicCn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dawson, 2017; Turbelin, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Dawson, 2017; Turbelin, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":644,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IVA8PMSG"],"itemData":{"id":644,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","ISSN":"2397-334X","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","note":"number: 7\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"1-7","title":"Global hotspots and correlates of alien species richness across taxonomic groups","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Dawson","given":"Wayne"},{"family":"Moser","given":"Dietmar"},{"family":"Van Kleunen","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Kreft","given":"Holger"},{"family":"Pergl","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Pyšek","given":"Petr"},{"family":"Weigelt","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Winter","given":"Marten"},{"family":"Lenzner","given":"Bernd"},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":667,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/39GA3QVF"],"itemData":{"id":667,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Biogeography","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"78–92","title":"Mapping the global state of invasive alien species: patterns of invasion and policy responses","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Turbelin","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Malamud","given":"B.D."},{"family":"Francis","given":"R.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the impacts of invasions on nature’s contributions to people ecosystem services and human wellbeing are high in developing countries as those countries have limited options for preventing and managing invasive species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hifTKklX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Py\\\\uc0\\\\u353{}ek, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Pyšek, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":683,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KZHNQV6B"],"itemData":{"id":683,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Reviews","DOI":"10.1111/brv.12627.","language":"en","title":"Scientists' warning on invasive alien species","volume":"95","editor":[{"family":"Pyšek","given":"Petr"},{"family":"Hulme","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Simberloff","given":"Dan"},{"family":"Bacher","given":"Sven"},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Carlton","given":"James"},{"family":"Dawson","given":"Wayne"},{"family":"Essl","given":"Franz"},{"family":"Foxcroft","given":"Llewellyn"},{"family":"Genovesi","given":"Piero"},{"family":"Jeschke","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Kühn","given":"Ingolf"},{"family":"Liebhold","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Mandrak","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Meyerson","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Pauchard","given":"Anibal"},{"family":"Pergl","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Roy","given":"Helen"},{"family":"Seebens","given":"Hanno"},{"family":"Richardson","given":"David"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Regions with some newly industrialized countries in the Global North with high population densities and large surface areas support the most established alien species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KUGJy7c6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Py\\\\uc0\\\\u353{}ek, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Pyšek, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":683,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KZHNQV6B"],"itemData":{"id":683,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Reviews","DOI":"10.1111/brv.12627.","language":"en","title":"Scientists' warning on invasive alien species","volume":"95","editor":[{"family":"Pyšek","given":"Petr"},{"family":"Hulme","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Simberloff","given":"Dan"},{"family":"Bacher","given":"Sven"},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Carlton","given":"James"},{"family":"Dawson","given":"Wayne"},{"family":"Essl","given":"Franz"},{"family":"Foxcroft","given":"Llewellyn"},{"family":"Genovesi","given":"Piero"},{"family":"Jeschke","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Kühn","given":"Ingolf"},{"family":"Liebhold","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Mandrak","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Meyerson","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Pauchard","given":"Anibal"},{"family":"Pergl","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Roy","given":"Helen"},{"family":"Seebens","given":"Hanno"},{"family":"Richardson","given":"David"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the world’s islands, where valued species have often evolved in the absence of strong competition, herbivory, parasitism or predation, biological invasions cause a loss of biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0yKJysvL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Courchamp, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Courchamp, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33128,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TYKR82IK"],"itemData":{"id":33128,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Reviews","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"347–383","title":"Mammal invaders on islands: impact, control and control impact","volume":"78","author":[{"family":"Courchamp","given":"F."},{"family":"Chapuis","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Pascal","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Island endemics have limited experience of mammalian predators or herbivores and nowhere to escape ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VPmdY9nt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Py\\\\uc0\\\\u353{}ek, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Pyšek, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":643,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KIF3AQH5"],"itemData":{"id":643,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Preslia.","ISSN":"0032-7786","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Preslia.","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Czech Botanical Society","page":"203-274","title":"Naturalized alien flora of the world.","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Pyšek","given":"Petr"},{"family":"Pergl","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Essl","given":"Franz"},{"family":"Lenzner","given":"Bernd"},{"family":"Dawson","given":"Wayne"},{"family":"Kreft","given":"Holger"},{"family":"Weigelt","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Winter","given":"Marten"},{"family":"Kartesz","given":"John"},{"family":"Nishino","given":"Misako"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. On islands, the biological invasion is considered as the second greatest agent of species endangerment and extinction after habitat destruction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bb2t4IAk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pejchar & Mooney, 2009; Wilcove, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Pejchar & Mooney, 2009; Wilcove, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":668,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UAANE3VZ"],"itemData":{"id":668,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"607–615","title":"Quantifying threats to imperiled species in the United States","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Wilcove","given":"David S."},{"family":"Rothstein","given":"David"},{"family":"Dubow","given":"Jason"},{"family":"Phillips","given":"Ali"},{"family":"Losos","given":"Elizabeth"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":680,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZUUSAMX5"],"itemData":{"id":680,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in ecology & evolution","issue":"9","language":"en","page":"497–504","title":"Invasive species, ecosystem services and human well-being","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Pejchar","given":"L."},{"family":"Mooney","given":"H.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, about half of New Zealand’s flora comprises alien plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"I5Td5ocl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. E. Hulme, 2020)","plainCitation":"(P. E. Hulme, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":652,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3LFX68R5"],"itemData":{"id":652,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Invasions","ISSN":"1573-1464","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Invasions","note":"number: 5\\npublisher: Springer","page":"1539-1562","title":"Plant invasions in New Zealand: global lessons in prevention, eradication and control","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Hulme","given":"Philip E"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In freshwater ecosystem, the invasion rates are likely to continue to be high ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"J3LvHBHn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Strayer, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Strayer, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":662,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7QEIBV5C"],"itemData":{"id":662,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Freshwater biology","language":"en","page":"152–174","title":"Alien species in fresh waters: ecological effects, interactions with other stressors, and prospects for the future","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Strayer","given":"D.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The number of marine non-indigenous fishes is increasing alarmingly within a short period of time causing structural changes and the decline of native species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NUyBxWsz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arndt, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Arndt, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":605,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XU8AC8VP"],"itemData":{"id":605,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Hydrobiologia","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"457–474","title":"Ecological impact of alien marine fishes: insights from freshwater systems based on a comparative review","volume":"817","author":[{"family":"Arndt","given":"E."},{"family":"Marchetti","given":"M.P."},{"family":"Schembri","given":"P.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Invasive species also disrupt trophic cascades causing the mismatch of evolutionarily based strategies among predators and prey ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1PRyURMo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kimbro, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Kimbro, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":699,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7IY4Z8MM"],"itemData":{"id":699,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oecologia","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"563–575","title":"Invasive species cause large-scale loss of native California oyster habitat by disrupting trophic cascades","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Kimbro","given":"D.L."},{"family":"Grosholz","given":"E.D."},{"family":"Baukus","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Nesbitt","given":"N.J."},{"family":"Travis","given":"N.M."},{"family":"Attoe","given":"S."},{"family":"Coleman-Hulbert","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Invasive alien species have devastating impacts on biodiversity and ecosystem health causing decline or even extinction of native species and impairment of  nature’s contributions to people worldwide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YKQjLUkj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pejchar & Mooney, 2009; Traveset & Richardson, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Pejchar & Mooney, 2009; Traveset & Richardson, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":680,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZUUSAMX5"],"itemData":{"id":680,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in ecology & evolution","issue":"9","language":"en","page":"497–504","title":"Invasive species, ecosystem services and human well-being","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Pejchar","given":"L."},{"family":"Mooney","given":"H.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":665,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2MIHB457"],"itemData":{"id":665,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in ecology & evolution","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"208–216","title":"Biological invasions as disruptors of plant reproductive mutualisms","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Traveset","given":"A."},{"family":"Richardson","given":"D.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Alien invasion is regarded as one of the major drivers of biotic homogenization  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nnFmWbLj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Coll\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}ony & Shwartz, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Colléony & Shwartz, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":608,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B9HSA5V5"],"itemData":{"id":608,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2019.108314","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"108314","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"When the winners are the losers: Invasive alien bird species outcompete the native winners in the biotic homogenization process","title-short":"When the winners are the losers","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S000632071931434X","volume":"241","author":[{"family":"Colléony","given":"Agathe"},{"family":"Shwartz","given":"Assaf"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and wild species extinction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XcJ6Z1hz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bellard, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Bellard, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":607,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7LJ89YD4"],"itemData":{"id":607,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"We assessed the prevalence of alien species as a driver of recent extinctions in five major taxa (plants, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals), using data from the IUCN Red List. Our results show that alien species are the second most common threat associated with species that have gone completely extinct from these taxa since AD 1500. Aliens are the most common threat associated with extinctions in three of the five taxa analysed, and for vertebrate extinctions overall.","container-title":"Biology Letters","DOI":"10.1098/rsbl.2015.0623","ISSN":"1744-9561, 1744-957X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Biol. Lett.","language":"en","page":"20150623","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Alien species as a driver of recent extinctions","URL":"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rsbl.2015.0623","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Bellard","given":"Céline"},{"family":"Cassey","given":"Phillip"},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} mainly through the introduction of novel traits, genes and behaviors by new alien species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sR6kldzy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McGeoch & Jetz, 2019)","plainCitation":"(McGeoch & Jetz, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":616,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GBPTIJUF"],"itemData":{"id":616,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"One Earth","DOI":"10.1016/j.oneear.2019.10.003","ISSN":"25903322","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"One Earth","language":"en","page":"171-174","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Measure and Reduce the Harm Caused by Biological Invasions","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2590332219300843","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"McGeoch","given":"Melodie"},{"family":"Jetz","given":"Walter"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LAdsGup5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bellard, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Bellard, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":607,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7LJ89YD4"],"itemData":{"id":607,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"We assessed the prevalence of alien species as a driver of recent extinctions in five major taxa (plants, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals), using data from the IUCN Red List. Our results show that alien species are the second most common threat associated with species that have gone completely extinct from these taxa since AD 1500. Aliens are the most common threat associated with extinctions in three of the five taxa analysed, and for vertebrate extinctions overall.","container-title":"Biology Letters","DOI":"10.1098/rsbl.2015.0623","ISSN":"1744-9561, 1744-957X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Biol. Lett.","language":"en","page":"20150623","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Alien species as a driver of recent extinctions","URL":"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rsbl.2015.0623","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Bellard","given":"Céline"},{"family":"Cassey","given":"Phillip"},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} reported that 1372 vertebrates are threatened by more than 200 invasive alien species mainly in the Americas, India, Indonesia, Australia and New Zealand. Invasive species proliferation on primate habitats cause habitat loss and population declines and extirpation of primate species in addition to hunting for meat and culturally valued body parts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WBboIlyU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Estrada, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Estrada, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33139,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B6WFTDUT"],"itemData":{"id":33139,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Primates occur in 90 countries, but four—Brazil, Madagascar, Indonesia, and the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC)—harbor 65% of the world's primate species (439) and 60% of these primates are Threatened, Endangered, or Critically Endangered (IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2017-3). Considering their importance for global primate conservation, we examine the anthropogenic pressures each country is facing that place their primate populations at risk. Habitat loss and fragmentation are main threats to primates in Brazil, Madagascar, and Indonesia. However, in DRC hunting for the commercial bushmeat trade is the primary threat. Encroachment on primate habitats driven by local and global market demands for food and non-food commodities hunting, illegal trade, the proliferation of invasive species, and human and domestic-animal borne infectious diseases cause habitat loss, population declines, and extirpation. Modeling agricultural expansion in the 21st century for the four countries under a worst-case-scenario, showed a primate range contraction of 78% for Brazil, 72% for Indonesia, 62% for Madagascar, and 32% for DRC. These pressures unfold in the context of expanding human populations with low levels of development. Weak governance across these four countries may limit effective primate conservation planning. We examine landscape and local approaches to effective primate conservation policies and assess the distribution of protected areas and primates in each country. Primates in Brazil and Madagascar have 38% of their range inside protected areas, 17% in Indonesia and 14% in DRC, suggesting that the great majority of primate populations remain vulnerable. We list the key challenges faced by the four countries to avert primate extinctions now and in the future. In the short term, effective law enforcement to stop illegal hunting and illegal forest destruction is absolutely key. Long-term success can only be achieved by focusing local and global public awareness, and actively engaging with international organizations, multinational businesses and consumer nations to reduce unsustainable demands on the environment. Finally, the four primate range countries need to ensure that integrated, sustainable land-use planning for economic development includes the maintenance of biodiversity and intact, functional natural ecosystems.","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.4869","page":"e4869","title":"Primates in peril: the significance of Brazil, Madagascar, Indonesia and the Democratic Republic of the Congo for global primate conservation","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Estrada","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Garber","given":"Paul A."},{"family":"Mittermeier","given":"Russell A."},{"family":"Wich","given":"Serge"},{"family":"Gouveia","given":"Sidney"},{"family":"Dobrovolski","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Nekaris","given":"K.A.I."},{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Rylands","given":"Anthony B."},{"family":"Maisels","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Williamson","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Bicca-Marques","given":"Julio"},{"family":"Fuentes","given":"Agustin"},{"family":"Jerusalinsky","given":"Leandro"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Steig"},{"family":"Rodrigues de Melo","given":"Fabiano"},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"Leonardo"},{"family":"Schwitzer","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Roos","given":"Christian"},{"family":"Cheyne","given":"Susan M."},{"family":"Martins Kierulff","given":"Maria Cecilia"},{"family":"Raharivololona","given":"Brigitte"},{"family":"Talebi","given":"Mauricio"},{"family":"Ratsimbazafy","given":"Jonah"},{"family":"Supriatna","given":"Jatna"},{"family":"Boonratana","given":"Ramesh"},{"family":"Wedana","given":"Made"},{"family":"Setiawan","given":"Arif"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Based on a recent analysis of data on global extinction in the International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List database ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XyTZQnEe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2017)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33209,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SPIJQTCL"],"itemData":{"id":33209,"type":"report","publisher":"IUCN","title":"IUCN 2017 Annual Report","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, invasive alien species contributed to the extinction of 261 (39%) out of 782 species of terrestrial and freshwater animal and 39 (25%) species of total 153 plant species worldwide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dqPJdHvL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. M. Blackburn, 2019)","plainCitation":"(T. M. Blackburn, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":651,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CGUZBJUR"],"itemData":{"id":651,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","ISSN":"1540-9295","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"203-207","title":"Alien versus native species as drivers of recent extinctions","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim M"},{"family":"Bellard","given":"Céline"},{"family":"Ricciardi","given":"Anthony"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Invasive species negatively affect native species richness, abundance, fitness, productivity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DmMuo6qv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cameron, 2016; Py\\\\uc0\\\\u353{}ek, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Cameron, 2016; Pyšek, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":656,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CDJUNN23"],"itemData":{"id":656,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Biogeography","ISSN":"1466-822X","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Biogeography","note":"number: 5\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"596-606","title":"Global meta?analysis of the impacts of terrestrial invertebrate invaders on species, communities and ecosystems","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Cameron","given":"Erin K"},{"family":"Vilà","given":"Montserrat"},{"family":"Cabeza","given":"Mar"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":618,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5W8DXJYE"],"itemData":{"id":618,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Biology","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"1725–1737","title":"A global assessment of invasive plant impacts on resident species, communities and ecosystems: the interaction of impact measures, invading species' traits and environment","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Pyšek","given":"P."},{"family":"Jarošík","given":"V."},{"family":"Hulme","given":"P.E."},{"family":"Pergl","given":"J."},{"family":"Hejda","given":"M."},{"family":"Schaffner","given":"U."},{"family":"Vilà","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and hinder ecosystem functioning such as regime shifts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fJ3DRlU1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gaertner, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Gaertner, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":655,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NMJDJQWJ"],"itemData":{"id":655,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Diversity and Distributions","ISSN":"1366-9516","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Diversity and Distributions","note":"number: 7\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"733-744","title":"Invasive plants as drivers of regime shifts: identifying high?priority invaders that alter feedback relationships","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Gaertner","given":"Mirijam"},{"family":"Biggs","given":"Reinette"},{"family":"Te Beest","given":"Mariska"},{"family":"Hui","given":"Cang"},{"family":"Molofsky","given":"Jane"},{"family":"Richardson","given":"David M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and provision of ecosystem services ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1DVq6Clq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Castro\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}ez, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Castro?Díez, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":650,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LMPUWKQA"],"itemData":{"id":650,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Reviews","ISSN":"1464-7931","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Reviews","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"1477-1501","title":"Global effects of non?native tree species on multiple ecosystem services","volume":"94","author":[{"family":"Castro?Díez","given":"Pilar"},{"family":"Vaz","given":"Ana Sofia"},{"family":"Silva","given":"Joaquim S"},{"family":"Van Loo","given":"Marcela"},{"family":"Alonso","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Aponte","given":"Cristina"},{"family":"Bayón","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Bellingham","given":"Peter J"},{"family":"Chiuffo","given":"Mariana C"},{"family":"DiManno","given":"Nicole"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other notable impacts of invasive alien species are eutrophication, expansion of natural fire regime, increased soil erosion, hydrological regime, alteration of soil stability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UJ58I2Mj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. K. Rai & Singh, 2020)","plainCitation":"(P. K. Rai & Singh, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":649,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GQ7CLD87"],"itemData":{"id":649,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological indicators","ISSN":"1470-160X","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological indicators","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"106020","title":"Invasive alien plant species: Their impact on environment, ecosystem services and human health","volume":"111","author":[{"family":"Rai","given":"Prabhat Kumar"},{"family":"Singh","given":"JS"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	The impacts of invasive alien species on local livelihoods are both negative and positive. The negative impacts of invasive alien species on local livelihood includes decrease supply of natural resources particularly non-timber forest products due to the loss of biodiversity and change in abundance of species, reduce agricultural production (livestock and/or crops and fisheries), harm to human health and safety, and reduce the cultural value of landscapes resulting in reduced resilience and adaptive capacity of households and communities along with loss of incomes (financial capital) and increased labor times ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rqJyK9bi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. T. Shackleton, 2019)","plainCitation":"(R. T. Shackleton, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":672,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2DJLXUNF"],"itemData":{"id":672,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of environmental management","language":"en","page":"145–157","title":"The role of invasive alien species in shaping local livelihoods and human well-being: A review","volume":"229","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"R.T."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Kull","given":"C.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, people abandoned farming ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tBOmfP73","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zavaleta Arango, n.d.)","plainCitation":"(Zavaleta Arango, n.d.)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33137,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SXL7BRCE"],"itemData":{"id":33137,"type":"graphic","abstract":"From contributor: La Micro-region de los Ozolotepec, se encuentra localizado en el sur del estado de Oaxaca, se encuentra localizado en la plena sierra madre del sur. Entre montaÃ±as, Rios, Lagos, Lagunas, Flora y Fauna, Fiestas, usos y costumbres y una gran diversidad de ecosistemas que destacan a esta Region de los Ozolotepc. //\\nThe Micro-region of the Ozolotepec, located in the south of the state of Oaxaca, is located in the full Sierra Madre of the south. Between mountains, Rivers, Lakes, Lagoons, Flora and Fauna, festivals, customs and customs and a great diversity of ecosystems that stand out to this Region of the Ozolotepc.","call-number":"Sub116","title":"Desde la Tierra de los Osos (Ozolotepec) // From the Land of bears (Ozolotepec)","author":[{"family":"Zavaleta Arango","given":"Libia"}]}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YpJCVSo5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cho & Tifuh, 2012; J. Travis, 1993)","plainCitation":"(Cho & Tifuh, 2012; J. Travis, 1993)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":648,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PJJQ3H3Q"],"itemData":{"id":648,"type":"article-journal","title":"Quantification of the impacts of water hyacinth on riparian communities in Cameroon and assessment of an appropriate method of control: the case of the Wouri River Basin","author":[{"family":"Cho","given":"Mujingni Epse"},{"family":"Tifuh","given":"Jenette"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":654,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q7I9HM9Z"],"itemData":{"id":654,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"5138","journalAbbreviation":"Science","note":"number: 5138\\npublisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science","page":"1366-1367","title":"Invader threatens black, Azov seas","volume":"262","author":[{"family":"Travis","given":"John"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and emigrate from their areas due to the negative effects of invasive species. A global analysis of the threat to crop production by almost 1300 known invasive insect pests and pathogens, on country-by-country basis for 124 countries revealed significant variations in countries in terms of potential threat from invasive species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NF7njxD9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Paini, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Paini, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":678,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DEPITYKB"],"itemData":{"id":678,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"27","language":"en","page":"7575–7579","title":"Global threat to agriculture from invasive species","volume":"113","author":[{"family":"Paini","given":"D.R."},{"family":"Sheppard","given":"A.W."},{"family":"Cook","given":"D.C."},{"family":"De Barro","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Worner","given":"S.P."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"M.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Introduced species have positive impacts on economy and livelihoods. For example, introduced species also act as hosts, food sources, pollinators and seed dispersers for native species as well as providing herbivory predatory or parasite release ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NIZB8EiE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Goodenough, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Goodenough, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":647,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NGK6WWT5"],"itemData":{"id":647,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Community Ecology","ISSN":"1588-2756","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Community Ecology","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Springer","page":"13-21","title":"Are the ecological impacts of alien species misrepresented? A review of the “native good, alien bad” philosophy","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Goodenough","given":"Anne E"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Invasive species provide the provision of fuelwood, fodder, food products, timber and medicinal products as well as other benefits for livelihoods such as soil improvement through green manure and nitrogen fixation, live fencing, and cultural services, such as recreation and aesthetic values ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mO5VE5NW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. T. Shackleton, 2019)","plainCitation":"(R. T. Shackleton, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":672,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2DJLXUNF"],"itemData":{"id":672,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of environmental management","language":"en","page":"145–157","title":"The role of invasive alien species in shaping local livelihoods and human well-being: A review","volume":"229","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"R.T."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Kull","given":"C.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}
      Introduced species may also have positive impacts on ecosystems, agriculture and food security. For example, an invasive tree in Florida (Melaleuca quinquenervia) has increased honey production that is worth US$ 15 million per year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KtQC4XHG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Serbesoff-King, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Serbesoff-King, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":670,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TMFL3U6L"],"itemData":{"id":670,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Aquatic Plant Management","issue":"1","language":"fr","title":"Melaleuca in Florida: a literature review on the taxonomy, distribution, biology, ecology, economic importance and control measures","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Serbesoff-King","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The introduction of brush-tailed possums (Trichosurus vulpecula) to New Zealand was considered profitable for ‘eco-friendly’ industry (at least US $ 20 million per year) although it has resulted in massive defoliation and negative impacts on biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pHRvBUKB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Clout & Barlow, 1982; Forsyth, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Clout & Barlow, 1982; Forsyth, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33127,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8GUPQBVD"],"itemData":{"id":33127,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"New Zealand journal of ecology","language":"en","page":"29–35","title":"Exploitation of brushtail possum populations in theory and practice","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Clout","given":"M.N."},{"family":"Barlow","given":"N.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1982"]]}}},{"id":33141,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MAXM45ZQ"],"itemData":{"id":33141,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Invasions","DOI":"10.1007/s10530-018-1697-0.","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"2209–2225","title":"Control history, longitude and multiple abiotic and biotic variables predict the abundances of invasive brushtail possums in New Zealand forests","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Forsyth","given":"D.M."},{"family":"Ramsey","given":"D.S.L."},{"family":"Perry","given":"M."},{"family":"McKay","given":"M."},{"family":"Wright","given":"E.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fur and skins of brush-tailed possums are also used by indigenous M?ori people of New Zealand for economic gain but the most economically-sustainable possum fur harvest strategy is unlikely to achieve even modest conservation outcomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3dhbBs9V","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Jones, 2012)","plainCitation":"(C. Jones, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":695,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KIX95GWV"],"itemData":{"id":695,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Serving two masters: Reconciling economic and biodiversity outcomes of brushtail","author":[{"family":"Jones","given":"C."},{"family":"Barron","given":"M."},{"family":"Warburton","given":"B."},{"family":"Coleman","given":"M."},{"family":"Lyver","given":"P.O.B."},{"family":"Nugent","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} hence a program to eradicate possum has started ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oj3WUCc1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Owens, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Owens, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":677,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EJY39EQE"],"itemData":{"id":677,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/541148a","language":"nl","page":"148–150","title":"Behind New Zealand’s wild plan to purge all pests","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1038/541148a","volume":"541","author":[{"family":"Owens","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Locals in Nepal used several species of invasive alien plants for producing compost, charcoal, and bio-briquette, forage for livestock and medicinal purpose ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7iBg9XII","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shrestha, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Shrestha, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32533,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9PG7GG3W"],"itemData":{"id":32533,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              Products obtained from forests or other natural environments play a crucial role in sustaining the livelihoods of poor people in developing countries through income generation and the creation of employment opportunities. Although studies have been carried out to evaluate the dependence of local livelihoods on environmental products, quantify the linkage between poverty and biodiversity, and assess the impacts of resource depletion on household economics, most have been focused geographically on the tropics. Our study was conducted in the mountain village of Jumla, Nepal, to quantify the economic contribution of Chinese caterpillar fungus\\n              Ophiocordyceps sinensis\\n              (yarsagumba) to local households. Income from yarsagumba accounted for up to 65% of the total household cash income, on average, and its contribution was highest in the poorest households. It contributed to reducing income inequality by 38%, and the income was utilized to purchase food and clothes, celebrate festivals, pay for medical treatment and children's education, and for savings. There was a mean annual decline of 25 pieces in the per capita harvest of yarsagumba during 2010–2014. However, the decline had no adverse impact on household income, as the price increased.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605317000461","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Oryx","language":"en","page":"256-264","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Economic dependence of mountain communities on Chinese caterpillar fungus <i>Ophiocordyceps sinensis</i> (yarsagumba): a case from western Nepal","title-short":"Economic dependence of mountain communities on Chinese caterpillar fungus <i>Ophiocordyceps sinensis</i> (yarsagumba)","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0030605317000461/type/journal_article","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Shrestha","given":"Uttam Babu"},{"family":"Dhital","given":"Krishna Ram"},{"family":"Gautam","given":"Ambika Prasad"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Overall, positive impacts of invasive alien species include provisioning services (fuelwood, fodder, timber and food products), regulating services (soil improvement and shade), and cultural services (recreation and spiritual values) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MwGJVKQ1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. T. Shackleton, 2019)","plainCitation":"(R. T. Shackleton, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":672,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2DJLXUNF"],"itemData":{"id":672,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of environmental management","language":"en","page":"145–157","title":"The role of invasive alien species in shaping local livelihoods and human well-being: A review","volume":"229","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"R.T."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Kull","given":"C.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} For example, there are examples of small initiatives to make use of an invasive like Lantana camara as a substitute for rattan in making furniture in Male Mahadeshwara Hills, Karnataka ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Sb1g6fss","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kannan, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Kannan, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":653,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XCTJBIK7"],"itemData":{"id":653,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","ISSN":"0378-1127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"166-173","title":"Can local use assist in controlling invasive alien species in tropical forests? The case of Lantana camara in southern India","volume":"376","author":[{"family":"Kannan","given":"Ramesh"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M"},{"family":"Krishnan","given":"Smitha"},{"family":"Shaanker","given":"R Uma"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the number of species causing negative impacts is double (37%) the number of beneficial species (16%) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xRNtMGV3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. T. Shackleton, 2019)","plainCitation":"(R. T. Shackleton, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":672,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2DJLXUNF"],"itemData":{"id":672,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of environmental management","language":"en","page":"145–157","title":"The role of invasive alien species in shaping local livelihoods and human well-being: A review","volume":"229","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"R.T."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Kull","given":"C.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some of the invasive species (e.g., green crab in Canada, Chinese mitten crab in China) have become a source of meat, common food and an omega-6 fatty acid and they can enhance fishing industry's value chain, improve profitability while addressing issues of waste management and environmental sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7azvvM3M","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dave & Routray, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Dave & Routray, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33124,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WGBA4CVG"],"itemData":{"id":33124,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Cleaner Production","language":"en","page":"617–641","title":"Current scenario of Canadian fishery and corresponding underutilized species and fishery byproducts: A potential source of omega-3 fatty acids","volume":"180","author":[{"family":"Dave","given":"D."},{"family":"Routray","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many other species such as wild pigs (Sus scrofa) are considered as the desired species for hunting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hbJCYwGE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Engeman, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Engeman, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":661,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UNZE9HAG"],"itemData":{"id":661,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science and Pollution Research","ISSN":"1614-7499","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Science and Pollution Research","note":"number: 11\\npublisher: Springer","page":"8077-8091","title":"Monitoring wild pig populations: a review of methods","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Engeman","given":"RM"},{"family":"Massei","given":"G"},{"family":"Sage","given":"M"},{"family":"Gentle","given":"Matthew N"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hunting of invasive or introduced mammals pigs and monkeys may provide benefits to native fauna and flora ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"phXN6npB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carvalho, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Carvalho, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33136,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMG7VN2S"],"itemData":{"id":33136,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Hunting and invasive species are amongst the main causes of species extinctions on oceanic islands. On the island of São Tomé hunting of introduced mammals (monkeys, civets and pigs) and endemic bird species has contrasting effects in terms of conservation. Hunting of introduced mammals may benefit native fauna and flora but pressure on endemic birds poses a threat to some species. We conducted semi-structured interviews with 119 hunters to assess the relative importance of native and introduced prey species, gathering information on personal profiles, preference and practice, and hunters’ perceptions of trends in prey populations. Eleven species were hunted, including five invasive mammals and five endemic birds of high conservation importance. Based on species hunted, techniques used and final destination of the quarry we distinguished three groups of hunters, using cluster analyses: bird, monkey and feral-pig hunters. Bird hunters were all from urban areas but most pig hunters were rural. Monkey hunters were mostly rural workers but a small proportion were from urban areas. Bird and monkey hunters were primarily motivated by commercial gains but they also hunted for enjoyment and food. In general, hunting of mammals is an opportunistic activity that, if regulated, can be sustainable and contribute to mitigating the effects of invasive species on local fauna as well contributing to local livelihoods. Given the economic drivers involved in hunting of birds, intervention to reduce or eliminate this form of hunting will require enforcement of legislation and raising awareness of the issues involved.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605313000550","ISSN":"1365-3008","issue":"2","page":"278-286","title":"What motivates hunters to target exotic or endemic species on the island of São Tomé, Gulf of Guinea?","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/article/what-motivates-hunters-to-target-exotic-or-endemic-species-on-the-island-of-sao-tome-gulf-of-guinea/E1728FC9D03047BB8684D42B417E6E8A","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Carvalho","given":"Mariana"},{"family":"Palmeirim","given":"Jorge M."},{"family":"Rego","given":"Francisco C."},{"family":"Sole","given":"Nelson"},{"family":"Santana","given":"Aristides"},{"family":"Fa","given":"John E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the ranches of Mexico, exotic species were introduced to provide year-round hunting opportunities including tourists ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9D6O62vd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barthel & Schuett, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Barthel & Schuett, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33130,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WWHDY4KJ"],"itemData":{"id":33130,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sociedad, Estado y Territorio","language":"es","title":"Perception of mexican hunters toward exotic game species","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Barthel","given":"G.G."},{"family":"Schuett","given":"M.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Biological invasions and wild species disease are inextricably linked. Biological invasions can drive the spread of new diseases to wild species and humans. Biological invasions lead to novel parasite–host interactions and transmission opportunities, with the potential to affect human, wild species, and ecosystem health and resilience ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"txzoTzlZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Py\\\\uc0\\\\u353{}ek & Richardson, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Pyšek & Richardson, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":684,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5C5C7SR8"],"itemData":{"id":684,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual review of environment and resources","language":"en","page":"25–55","title":"Invasive species, environmental change and management, and health","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Pyšek","given":"P."},{"family":"Richardson","given":"D.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Several potential zoonoses have originated from biological invasion in Europe and potentially elsewhere ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fLKLf5yo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. E. Hulme, 2014)","plainCitation":"(P. E. Hulme, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":658,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EEDAJKH7"],"itemData":{"id":658,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in parasitology","ISSN":"1471-4922","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in parasitology","note":"number: 6\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"267-270","title":"Invasive species challenge the global response to emerging diseases","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Hulme","given":"Philip E"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Zoonotic pathogens and parasites that are transmitted from animals to humans are a major public health risk and three-quarters of emerging human pathogens are zoonotic ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HEB1cDEW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. J. White & Razgour, 2020)","plainCitation":"(R. J. White & Razgour, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":620,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WGR3S2JK"],"itemData":{"id":620,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mammal Review","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"336–352","title":"Emerging zoonotic diseases originating in mammals: a systematic review of effects of anthropogenic land?use change","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"White","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Razgour","given":"O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Invasive species directly affect human health; several species of the invasive alien plants cause allergies, phytotoxicity, disease, eczematous dermatitis and asthma to humans ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"j9Q5tkyN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. K. Rai & Singh, 2020)","plainCitation":"(P. K. Rai & Singh, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":649,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GQ7CLD87"],"itemData":{"id":649,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological indicators","ISSN":"1470-160X","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological indicators","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"106020","title":"Invasive alien plant species: Their impact on environment, ecosystem services and human health","volume":"111","author":[{"family":"Rai","given":"Prabhat Kumar"},{"family":"Singh","given":"JS"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. On the other hand, invasive species act as a vector transmitting several diseases. Several human diseases and their sudden outbreak across continents are linked to biological invasions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sbIO5dmq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Py\\\\uc0\\\\u353{}ek & Richardson, 2010; P. K. Rai & Singh, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Pyšek & Richardson, 2010; P. K. Rai & Singh, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":684,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5C5C7SR8"],"itemData":{"id":684,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual review of environment and resources","language":"en","page":"25–55","title":"Invasive species, environmental change and management, and health","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Pyšek","given":"P."},{"family":"Richardson","given":"D.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":649,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GQ7CLD87"],"itemData":{"id":649,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological indicators","ISSN":"1470-160X","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological indicators","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"106020","title":"Invasive alien plant species: Their impact on environment, ecosystem services and human health","volume":"111","author":[{"family":"Rai","given":"Prabhat Kumar"},{"family":"Singh","given":"JS"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, invasive mosquito the Asian tiger mosquito (Aedes albopictus) spread through the transportation of eggs via the international trade of used tire, is a vector for transmission of many viruses, including dengue, LaCrosse, Yellow fever, chikungunya and West Nile ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Sl0ugFxt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Benedict, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Benedict, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26020,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WKMGVK7X","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RELU4NBZ"],"itemData":{"id":26020,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Vector-borne and zoonotic Diseases","ISSN":"1530-3667","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Vector-borne and zoonotic Diseases","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Mary Ann Liebert, Inc. 2 Madison Avenue Larchmont, NY 10538 USA","page":"76-85","title":"Spread of the tiger: global risk of invasion by the mosquito Aedes albopictus","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Benedict","given":"Mark Q"},{"family":"Levine","given":"Rebecca S"},{"family":"Hawley","given":"William A"},{"family":"Lounibos","given":"L Philip"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some invasive species affect human health through environmental contamination such as air pollutants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5gFhIPfO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. R. Jones, 2018b)","plainCitation":"(K. R. Jones, 2018b)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33138,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PKDHVNTN"],"itemData":{"id":33138,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Protected yet pressured\\nProtected areas are increasingly recognized as an essential way to safeguard biodiversity. Although the percentage of land included in the global protected area network has increased from 9 to 15%, Jones found that a third of this area is influenced by intensive human activity. Thus, even landscapes that are protected are experiencing some human pressure, with only the most remote northern regions remaining almost untouched.\\nScience, this issue p. 788\\nIn an era of massive biodiversity loss, the greatest conservation success story has been the growth of protected land globally. Protected areas are the primary defense against biodiversity loss, but extensive human activity within their boundaries can undermine this. Using the most comprehensive global map of human pressure, we show that 6 million square kilometers (32.8%) of protected land is under intense human pressure. For protected areas designated before the Convention on Biological Diversity was ratified in 1992, 55% have since experienced human pressure increases. These increases were lowest in large, strict protected areas, showing that they are potentially effective, at least in some nations. Transparent reporting on human pressure within protected areas is now critical, as are global targets aimed at efforts required to halt biodiversity loss.\\nHuman pressure is present in a third of the land designated as protected, globally.\\nHuman pressure is present in a third of the land designated as protected, globally.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aap9565","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6390","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2018 The Authors, some rights reserved; exclusive licensee American Association for the Advancement of Science. No claim to original U.S. Government Works. http://www.sciencemag.org/about/science-licenses-journal-article-reuseThis is an article distributed under the terms of the Science Journals Default License.","note":"publisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science\\nsection: Report\\nPMID: 29773750","page":"788-791","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"One-third of global protected land is under intense human pressure","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/360/6390/788","volume":"360","author":[{"family":"Jones","given":"Kendall R."},{"family":"Venter","given":"Oscar"},{"family":"Fuller","given":"Richard A."},{"family":"Allan","given":"James R."},{"family":"Maxwell","given":"Sean L."},{"family":"Negret","given":"Pablo Jose"},{"family":"Watson","given":"James E. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",5,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Invasive species also cause disease to native animal species. For example, the worldwide amphibian decline is driven by the emerging infectious diseases chytridiomycosis caused by Batrachochtrium dendrobatidis, an invasive fungus ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"D603K28D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. J. Crawford, 2010)","plainCitation":"(A. J. Crawford, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":660,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JS4TXX4E"],"itemData":{"id":660,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","ISSN":"0027-8424","issue":"31","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","note":"number: 31\\npublisher: National Acad Sciences","page":"13777-13782","title":"Epidemic disease decimates amphibian abundance, species diversity, and evolutionary history in the highlands of central Panama","volume":"107","author":[{"family":"Crawford","given":"Andrew J"},{"family":"Lips","given":"Karen R"},{"family":"Bermingham","given":"Eldredge"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Invasive species also increase the outbreak of fungal pathogens which adversely affect the health of native plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Rq7FN038","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Beckstead, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Beckstead, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":602,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QFW8PX6H"],"itemData":{"id":602,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ecology","ISSN":"0022-0477","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ecology","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"168-177","title":"Cheatgrass facilitates spillover of a seed bank pathogen onto native grass species","volume":"98","author":[{"family":"Beckstead","given":"Julie"},{"family":"Meyer","given":"Susan E"},{"family":"Connolly","given":"Brian M"},{"family":"Huck","given":"Michael B"},{"family":"Street","given":"Laura E"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, the amphibian pet trade is linked with the global spread of chytrid fungus (Batrachochyrtium dendrobatidis) which has led to significant decline of amphibians ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZlTvJIEy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alroy, 2015; Auliya, Altherr,, 2016; Thumsov\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Alroy, 2015; Auliya, Altherr,, 2016; Thumsová, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33129,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5Z9PU6WE"],"itemData":{"id":33129,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"42","language":"en","page":"13003–13008","title":"Current extinction rates of reptiles and amphibians","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Alroy","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":32746,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R29PGGD2"],"itemData":{"id":32746,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Of the 10,272 currently recognized reptile species, the trade of fewer than 8% are regulated by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and the European Wildlife Trade Regulations (EWTR). However, the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List has assessed 45% of the world's reptile species and determined that at least 1390 species are threatened by “biological resource use”. Of these, 355 species are intentionally targeted by collectors, including 194 non-CITES-listed species. Herein we review the global reptile pet trade, its impacts, and its contribution to the over-harvesting of species and populations, in light of current international law. Findings are based on an examination of relevant professional observations, online sources, and literature (e.g., applicable policies, taxonomy [reptile database], trade statistics [EUROSTAT], and conservation status [IUCN Red List]). Case studies are presented from the following countries and regions: Australia, Central America, China, Galapagos Islands (Ecuador), Germany, Europe, India, Indonesia (Kalimantan), Islamic Republic of Iran, Japan, Madagascar, Mexico, New Zealand, the Philippines, South Africa, Sri Lanka, Vietnam, Western Africa, and Western Asia. The European Union (EU) plays a major role in reptile trade. Between 2004 and 2014 (the period under study), the EU member states officially reported the import of 20,788,747 live reptiles. This review suggests that illegal trade activities involve species regulated under CITES, as well as species that are not CITES-regulated but nationally protected in their country of origin and often openly offered for sale in the EU. Further, these case studies demonstrate that regulations and enforcement in several countries are inadequate to prevent the overexploitation of species and to halt illegal trade activities.","collection-title":"Advancing reptile conservation: Addressing knowledge gaps and mitigating key drivers of extinction risk","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2016.05.017","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"103-119","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Trade in live reptiles, its impact on wild populations, and the role of the European market","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320716301987","volume":"204","author":[{"family":"Auliya","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Altherr","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Ariano-Sanchez","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Baard","given":"Ernst H."},{"family":"Brown","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Rafe M."},{"family":"Cantu","given":"Juan-Carlos"},{"family":"Gentile","given":"Gabriele"},{"family":"Gildenhuys","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Henningheim","given":"Evert"},{"family":"Hintzmann","given":"Jürgen"},{"family":"Kanari","given":"Kahoru"},{"family":"Krvavac","given":"Milivoje"},{"family":"Lettink","given":"Marieke"},{"family":"Lippert","given":"Jörg"},{"family":"Luiselli","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Nilson","given":"Göran"},{"family":"Nguyen","given":"Truong Quang"},{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Parham","given":"James F."},{"family":"Pasachnik","given":"Stesha A."},{"family":"Pedrono","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Rauhaus","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Córdova","given":"Danny Rueda"},{"family":"Sanchez","given":"Maria-Elena"},{"family":"Schepp","given":"Ulrich"},{"family":"Schingen","given":"Mona","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Schneeweiss","given":"Norbert"},{"family":"Segniagbeto","given":"Gabriel H."},{"family":"Somaweera","given":"Ruchira"},{"family":"Sy","given":"Emerson Y."},{"family":"Türkozan","given":"Oguz"},{"family":"Vinke","given":"Sabine"},{"family":"Vinke","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Vyas","given":"Raju"},{"family":"Williamson","given":"Stuart"},{"family":"Ziegler","given":"Thomas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12,1]]}}},{"id":663,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DBAXXAYC"],"itemData":{"id":663,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Herpetology Notes","language":"en","page":"777–784","title":"Incidence of emerging pathogens in legal and illegal amphibian trade in Spain","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Thumsová","given":"B."},{"family":"Bosch","given":"J."},{"family":"Martinez-Silvestre","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, a few positive health impacts of invasive species are reported. For instance, extracts from Lantana camara is used as a mosquito repellent ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MwIByzle","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mng\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}ong\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}o, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Mng’ong’o, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":659,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RNU78SJI"],"itemData":{"id":659,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS One","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS One","note":"number: 10\\npublisher: Public Library of Science San Francisco, USA","page":"e25927","title":"Repellent plants provide affordable natural screening to prevent mosquito house entry in tropical rural settings—results from a pilot efficacy study","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Mng'ong'o","given":"Frank C"},{"family":"Sambali","given":"Joseph J"},{"family":"Sabas","given":"Eustachkius"},{"family":"Rubanga","given":"Justine"},{"family":"Magoma","given":"Jaka"},{"family":"Ntamatungiro","given":"Alex J"},{"family":"Turner","given":"Elizabeth L"},{"family":"Nyogea","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Ensink","given":"Jeroen HJ"},{"family":"Moore","given":"Sarah J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Additionally, ethnobotanical surveys on invasive alien plant species, can provide benefits (e.g., Lantana camara, Opuntia ficus-indica and Ricinus communis) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zQiuOcBE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rahmatullah, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Rahmatullah, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":657,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HKB8JDLP"],"itemData":{"id":657,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Advances in natural and applied sciences","ISSN":"1995-0772","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Advances in natural and applied sciences","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: American-Eurasian Network for Scientific Information","page":"236-253","title":"An ethnomedicinal, pharmacological and phytochemical review of some Bignoniaceae family plants and a description of Bignoniaceae plants in folk medicinal uses in Bangladesh","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Rahmatullah","given":"Mohammed"},{"family":"Samarrai","given":"Walied"},{"family":"Jahan","given":"Rownak"},{"family":"Rahman","given":"Shahnaz"},{"family":"Sharmin","given":"Nasima"},{"family":"Miajee","given":"EU"},{"family":"Chowdhury","given":"Majeedul H"},{"family":"Bari","given":"Sazzadul"},{"family":"Jamal","given":"Farhana"},{"family":"Bashar","given":"ABMA"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hunting and biological invasion are interconnected to each other. Removing invasive species affects hunting practices. 
      Hunting has been a significant pathway for the introduction of invasive species into Europe in the last century. About 24.3% of the mammals (36 species) and 30.2% of the birds (63 species) introduced into Europe during the last century were released primarily for hunting purposes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"G2v7DNyv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carpio, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Carpio, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":633,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GME36B52"],"itemData":{"id":633,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Invasions","ISSN":"1573-1464","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Invasions","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Springer","page":"1197-1211","title":"Hunting as a source of alien species: a European review","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Carpio","given":"Antonio J"},{"family":"Guerrero-Casado","given":"Jose"},{"family":"Barasona","given":"Jose A"},{"family":"Tortosa","given":"Francisco S"},{"family":"Vicente","given":"Joaquin"},{"family":"Hillström","given":"Lars"},{"family":"Delibes-Mateos","given":"Miguel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Likewise, around 30% species of invasive introduced mammals in southern South America were introduced for hunting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EKij7Oc8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ballari, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Ballari, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33311,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N46N6RU6"],"itemData":{"id":33311,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mammal Review","ISSN":"0305-1838","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Mammal Review","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"229-240","title":"Understanding trends in biological invasions by introduced mammals in southern S outh A merica: a review of research and management","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Ballari",