a (Trematoda: Fasciolidae), a liver parasite of ruminants","title-short":"Multiple origins of European populations of the giant liver fluke Fascioloides magna (Trematoda","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0020751910003814","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Králová-Hromadová","given":"Ivica"},{"family":"Bazsalovicsová","given":"Eva"},{"family":"Štefka","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Špakulová","given":"Marta"},{"family":"Vávrová","given":"Silvia"},{"family":"Szemes","given":"Tomáš"},{"family":"Tkach","given":"Vasyl"},{"family":"Trudgett","given":"Alan"},{"family":"Pybus","given":"Margo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, hybridisation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"42mnuao2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Baker, 2014; Barbanera, 2010)","plainCitation":"(J. Baker, 2014; Barbanera, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":625,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KWFZZ8TC"],"itemData":{"id":625,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Emu-Austral Ornithology","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–12","title":"Threats from introduced birds to native birds","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Baker","given":"J."},{"family":"Harvey","given":"K.J."},{"family":"French","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":33312,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FFH436SD"],"itemData":{"id":33312,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological conservation","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biological conservation","note":"number: 5\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"1259-1268","title":"Genetic consequences of intensive management in game birds","volume":"143","author":[{"family":"Barbanera","given":"Filippo"},{"family":"Pergams","given":"Oliver RW"},{"family":"Guerrini","given":"Monica"},{"family":"Forcina","given":"Giovanni"},{"family":"Panayides","given":"Panicos"},{"family":"Dini","given":"Fernando"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and habitat alteration ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WDGR0iuq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kumschick, 2011b)","plainCitation":"(Kumschick, 2011b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":619,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FHZKV3JV"],"itemData":{"id":619,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Diversity and Distributions","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"663–672","title":"Weak or strong invaders? A comparison of impact between the native and invaded ranges of mammals and birds alien to Europe","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Kumschick","given":"S."},{"family":"Alba","given":"C."},{"family":"Hufbauer","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Nentwig","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In many places of the world, hunting including professional and recreational hunting, vehicle-mounted shooting, aerial or ground-based shooting has been used to reduce populations of invasive animals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zTjZUvNf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barron, 2011; Bengsen & Sparkes, 2016; Capizzi, 2020; McLeod & Saunders, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Barron, 2011; Bengsen & Sparkes, 2016; Capizzi, 2020; McLeod & Saunders, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33134,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VSXTLAZL"],"itemData":{"id":33134,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fungal Ecology","DOI":"10.1016/j.funeco.2010.09.009","ISSN":"17545048","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Fungal Ecology","language":"en","page":"124-133","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The emergence and coalescence of fungal conservation social networks in Europe and the U.S.A.","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1754504810000681","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Barron","given":"Elizabeth S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",4]]}}},{"id":622,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SSAVZ3B6"],"itemData":{"id":622,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mammal Review","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"297–310","title":"Can recreational hunting contribute to pest mammal control on public land in Australia?","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Bengsen","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Sparkes","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":637,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4AJ25CEA"],"itemData":{"id":637,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mammal Review","ISSN":"0305-1838","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Mammal Review","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"124-135","title":"A review of mammal eradications on Mediterranean islands","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Capizzi","given":"Dario"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":638,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GTTPAWNW"],"itemData":{"id":638,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Australasian Journal of Environmental Management","ISSN":"1448-6563","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Australasian Journal of Environmental Management","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"248-259","title":"Can legislation improve the effectiveness of fox control in NSW?","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"McLeod","given":"Lynette J"},{"family":"Saunders","given":"Glen R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some cases, the ground-based shooting effort is able to control overabundant mammal population and bird population such as white-headed duck in UK and France ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"L7Wb18Qb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Henderson, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Henderson, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":636,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L5TPGSWU"],"itemData":{"id":636,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"British Birds","ISSN":"0007-0335","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"British Birds","note":"number: 12","page":"680","title":"Progress of the UK Ruddy Duck eradication programme","volume":"102","author":[{"family":"Henderson","given":"Iain"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In New Zealand, hunting is a primary strategy to control introduced wild deer ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4ecOs4F5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Latham, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Latham, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33114,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RIBUTTJE"],"itemData":{"id":33114,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Invasions","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"251–266","title":"Assessing the efficacy of aerial culling of introduced wild deer in New Zealand with analytical decomposition of predation risk","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Latham","given":"A.D.M."},{"family":"Latham","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Herries","given":"D."},{"family":"Barron","given":"M."},{"family":"Cruz","given":"J."},{"family":"Anderson","given":"D.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, such killing or lethal control of predators does not produce a desire outcome ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6Bjqy4S5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Doherty & Ritchie, 2017; Dubois, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Doherty & Ritchie, 2017; Dubois, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33125,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5DWTZ4MB"],"itemData":{"id":33125,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"15–22","title":"Stop jumping the gun: a call for evidence?based invasive predator management","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Doherty","given":"T.S."},{"family":"Ritchie","given":"E.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33116,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XJJ4SBFH"],"itemData":{"id":33116,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"753–760","title":"International consensus principles for ethical wildlife control","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Dubois","given":"S."},{"family":"Fenwick","given":"N."},{"family":"Ryan","given":"E.A."},{"family":"Baker","given":"L."},{"family":"Baker","given":"S.E."},{"family":"Beausoleil","given":"N.J."},{"family":"Fraser","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and non-lethal killing is more effective than lethal killing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"inZ1DFyw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Treves, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Treves, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":664,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HNLVFXM7"],"itemData":{"id":664,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","issue":"7","language":"en","page":"380–388","title":"Predator control should not be a shot in the dark","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Treves","given":"A."},{"family":"Krofel","given":"M."},{"family":"McManus","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Globally, the introduction and spread of marine non-indigenous fishes  is facilitated by a number of anthropogenic factors such as building canals, shipping, intentionally introduced for fishery purpose, aquarium trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rnhXJf1x","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arndt, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Arndt, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":605,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XU8AC8VP"],"itemData":{"id":605,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Hydrobiologia","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"457–474","title":"Ecological impact of alien marine fishes: insights from freshwater systems based on a comparative review","volume":"817","author":[{"family":"Arndt","given":"E."},{"family":"Marchetti","given":"M.P."},{"family":"Schembri","given":"P.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In a freshwater environment, the introduction of non-native freshwater fishes for economic purposes, including aquaculture and aquarium trade, as well as improvement for wild stocks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gMINoOgo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wei, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Wei, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":621,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GHE7K76Q"],"itemData":{"id":621,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Ecosystem Health & Management","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"396–407","title":"A review of freshwater fish introductions to the Guangdong province, China","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Wei","given":"H."},{"family":"Hu","given":"Y."},{"family":"Li","given":"S."},{"family":"Chen","given":"F."},{"family":"Luo","given":"D."},{"family":"Gu","given":"D."},{"family":"Yang","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. About 23.6% of the freshwater fish introduced into Europe during the last century were released primarily for angling purposes suggesting that angling was a significant pathway for the introduction of invasive fish species into Europe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"P33doJTg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carpio, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Carpio, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":641,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9JLCLCX9"],"itemData":{"id":641,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Invasions","ISSN":"1573-1464","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Invasions","note":"number: 11\\npublisher: Springer","page":"3233-3248","title":"Angling as a source of non-native freshwater fish: a European review","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Carpio","given":"Antonio J"},{"family":"De Miguel","given":"RJ"},{"family":"Oteros","given":"José"},{"family":"Hillström","given":"Lars"},{"family":"Tortosa","given":"Francisco S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some islands like Puerto Rico, around 80% (46 species) of fish in the inland waters are non-native and are imported for sport fishing and pet trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Zysjpoa8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rodr\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}guez-Barreras, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Rodríguez-Barreras, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":640,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HRKBG88M"],"itemData":{"id":640,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Neotropical Biodiversity","ISSN":"2376-6808","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Neotropical Biodiversity","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"42-59","title":"An island invaded by exotics: a review of freshwater fish in Puerto Rico","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Rodríguez-Barreras","given":"Ruber"},{"family":"Zapata-Arroyo","given":"Camille"},{"family":"Falcón L","given":"Wilfredo"},{"family":"Olmeda","given":"María De Lourdes"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The introduction of non-native freshwater fishes has adverse environmental and socio-economic effects ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"T1aFIujQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wei, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Wei, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":621,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GHE7K76Q"],"itemData":{"id":621,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Ecosystem Health & Management","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"396–407","title":"A review of freshwater fish introductions to the Guangdong province, China","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Wei","given":"H."},{"family":"Hu","given":"Y."},{"family":"Li","given":"S."},{"family":"Chen","given":"F."},{"family":"Luo","given":"D."},{"family":"Gu","given":"D."},{"family":"Yang","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Invasive aquatic species eliminate native species of amphipod in freshwater habitats in Europe and North America ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IAeZJaWS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dick & Platvoet, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Dick & Platvoet, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":639,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4V3FKW4I"],"itemData":{"id":639,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the Royal Society of London. Series B: Biological Sciences","ISSN":"0962-8452","issue":"1447","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the Royal Society of London. Series B: Biological Sciences","note":"number: 1447\\npublisher: The Royal Society","page":"977-983","title":"Invading predatory crustacean Dikerogammarus villosus eliminates both native and exotic species","volume":"267","author":[{"family":"Dick","given":"Jaimie TA"},{"family":"Platvoet","given":"Dirk"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The introduction of invasive species also introduces novel pathogens to the new areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"USjJhRd2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bacela\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Spychalska, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Bacela?Spychalska, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26049,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3KU4E8D8","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SPI2DDZS"],"itemData":{"id":26049,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","ISSN":"1052-7613","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"606-618","title":"The ‘killer shrimp’Dikerogammarus villosus (Crustacea, Amphipoda) invading Alpine lakes: overland transport by recreational boats and scuba?diving gear as potential entry vectors?","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Bacela?Spychalska","given":"Karolina"},{"family":"Grabowski","given":"Michal"},{"family":"Rewicz","given":"Tomasz"},{"family":"Konopacka","given":"Alicja"},{"family":"Wattier","given":"Remi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Alien invasive species are the second most prevalent threat after habitat loss and degradation for freshwater fisheries in Canada, affecting 26 of 41 listed fish species and 6 of 11 listed mollusc species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vydBL9kj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dextrase & Mandrak, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Dextrase & Mandrak, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":626,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WZBELIXA"],"itemData":{"id":626,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Invasions","ISSN":"1573-1464","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Invasions","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Springer","page":"13-24","title":"Impacts of alien invasive species on freshwater fauna at risk in Canada","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Dextrase","given":"Alan J"},{"family":"Mandrak","given":"Nicholas E"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some regions, utilization of invasive species provides economic benefits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ad0CJeTo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pienkowski, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Pienkowski, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":631,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JIQVYYLP"],"itemData":{"id":631,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","ISSN":"0921-8009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"68-77","title":"Alien invasions and livelihoods: Economic benefits of invasive Australian Red Claw crayfish in Jamaica","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Pienkowski","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"McLaren","given":"Kurt"},{"family":"Wilson","given":"Byron"},{"family":"Hockley","given":"Neal"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} while in some areas invasive species has become a problem for commercial fishing in the United States of America ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2GXAVKDR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dudgeon, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Dudgeon, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33119,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDGJYFPW"],"itemData":{"id":33119,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Reviews of the Cambridge Philosophical Society","language":"en","page":"163–82","title":"Freshwater biodiversity: importance, threats, status and conservation challenges","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"Dudgeon","given":"D."},{"family":"Arthington","given":"A.H."},{"family":"Gessner","given":"M.O."},{"family":"Kawabata","given":"Z."},{"family":"Knowler","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Lévêque","given":"C."},{"family":"Naiman","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Prieur-Richard","given":"A.H."},{"family":"Soto","given":"D."},{"family":"Stiassny","given":"M.L.J."},{"family":"Sullivan","given":"C.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Despite the beneficial effects of angling by introduced fish making angling sustainable, it has a negative impact on native fish species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ADkpfR7I","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carpio, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Carpio, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":641,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9JLCLCX9"],"itemData":{"id":641,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Invasions","ISSN":"1573-1464","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Invasions","note":"number: 11\\npublisher: Springer","page":"3233-3248","title":"Angling as a source of non-native freshwater fish: a European review","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Carpio","given":"Antonio J"},{"family":"De Miguel","given":"RJ"},{"family":"Oteros","given":"José"},{"family":"Hillström","given":"Lars"},{"family":"Tortosa","given":"Francisco S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Micropterus salmoides normally introduced for sport fishing purposes worldwide is now listed by the International Union for Conservation of Nature as one of the 100 of the world's worst invasive alien species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LJgK7HbA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pereira, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Pereira, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33135,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J7TZF49W"],"itemData":{"id":33135,"type":"book","publisher":"IMAZON-Instituto do Homem e Meio Ambiente da Amazônia","title":"Fatos Florestais da Amazônia","author":[{"family":"Pereira","given":"Denys"},{"family":"Santos","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Vedoveto","given":"Mariana"},{"family":"Guimarães","given":"Jayne"},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Adalberto"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This species causes negative impacts on local population such as local extirpation of native species and food web changes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XSfIgiWt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pereira, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Pereira, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33135,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J7TZF49W"],"itemData":{"id":33135,"type":"book","publisher":"IMAZON-Instituto do Homem e Meio Ambiente da Amazônia","title":"Fatos Florestais da Amazônia","author":[{"family":"Pereira","given":"Denys"},{"family":"Santos","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Vedoveto","given":"Mariana"},{"family":"Guimarães","given":"Jayne"},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Adalberto"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Invasive species in the marine environment may cause an alteration of benthic habitat structure leading to the disruption of food webs, changes in nutrient cycles and energy transfer, or changes in the community structure, population decline and local replacement of native species through competition and predation and transmission of disease and potential hosts of parasites to native fishes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"i0dA11ZX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arndt, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Arndt, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":605,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XU8AC8VP"],"itemData":{"id":605,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Hydrobiologia","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"457–474","title":"Ecological impact of alien marine fishes: insights from freshwater systems based on a comparative review","volume":"817","author":[{"family":"Arndt","given":"E."},{"family":"Marchetti","given":"M.P."},{"family":"Schembri","given":"P.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Negative impacts of invasive species in aquatic ecosystems ranging from an abundance of aquatic communities particularly macrophytes, zooplankton and fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rR13Tv5A","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gallardo, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Gallardo, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":634,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZDMV9Y6M"],"itemData":{"id":634,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global change biology","ISSN":"1354-1013","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Global change biology","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"151-163","title":"Global ecological impacts of invasive species in aquatic ecosystems","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Gallardo","given":"Belinda"},{"family":"Clavero","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Sánchez","given":"Marta I"},{"family":"Vilà","given":"Montserrat"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Aquaculture also has negative impacts such as elevated input of nutrients and organic matter in habitat and water quality, spread of diseases, biotic homogenization, loss of population viability resulting from hybridization and outbreeding depression, and the local extirpation of native species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1hXhYUHE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lima, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Lima, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":614,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LZHPWD2J"],"itemData":{"id":614,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reviews in Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1111/raq.12150","ISSN":"17535123","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Rev Aquacult","language":"en","page":"111-122","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Expansion of aquaculture parks and the increasing risk of non-native species invasions in Brazil","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/raq.12150","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Lima","given":"Luciano B."},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"Fagner Junior M."},{"family":"Giacomini","given":"Henrique C."},{"family":"Lima-Junior","given":"Dilermando P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and the conversion of natural infrastructure such as coastal wetlands into (fish, shrimp, etc.) farm ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HgnZGUBh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoanh, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Hoanh, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":691,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3EWJ9LHB"],"itemData":{"id":691,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Wallingford\\nvolume: 2","title":"Environment and livelihoods in tropical coastal zones : managing agriculture-fishery-aquaculture conflicts","author":[{"family":"Hoanh","given":"C.T."},{"family":"Tuong","given":"T.P."},{"family":"Gowing","given":"J.W."},{"family":"Hardy","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Additionally, an increase in water turbidity, nitrogen and organic matter concentration due to invasive species is consistent across the habitats and scales, although there was no general evidence for a decline of species diversity in invaded habitats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GwkeE7dz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gallardo, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Gallardo, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":634,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZDMV9Y6M"],"itemData":{"id":634,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global change biology","ISSN":"1354-1013","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Global change biology","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"151-163","title":"Global ecological impacts of invasive species in aquatic ecosystems","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Gallardo","given":"Belinda"},{"family":"Clavero","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Sánchez","given":"Marta I"},{"family":"Vilà","given":"Montserrat"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Salmon farming in Chile has faced several challenges such as sanitary crisis, social conflicts, market problem, lack of good governance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"edq99ew7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chavez Carrillo 2019)","plainCitation":"(Chavez Carrillo 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33310,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2BCZN3VZ"],"itemData":{"id":33310,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Costa Rica is supporting Marine Areas of Responsible Fishing (AMPRs) to enable small-scale fishing communities to apply for exclusive harvesting and management rights within spatially delimited areas under a co-management policy framework. Communities need to self-organize their own fishing association and develop a fishing management plan (POP) to apply. Seven AMPRs have been established in the Gulf of Nicoya, but many face collective action challenges to develop and achieve common goals and implementation. In this article, we conduct a qualitative comparative analysis by applying the Social-Ecological System Framework (SESF) as a tool to identify the social and ecological conditions influencing collective action and co-management in three AMPRs in the Gulf of Nicoya, and we compare the similarities and differences between them Our results show that all three AMPRs face collective action challenges for different reasons. Nonetheless, some commonalities exist. Common drivers motivating collective action include the desire to restrict certain types of fishing gears due to perceptions of resource scarcity and high dependence on local resources. Monitoring and sanctioning mechanisms, the presence of effective leadership and the economic heterogeneity of actors all positively influence collective action in two cases. However, in all three cases, there are more variables hindering collective action such as mistrust among actors, internal conflicts, non-rule compliance, lack of governmental support and resource unit mobility. Our findings suggest that AMPRs are a promising and potentially effective governance strategy because they can empower marginalized small-scale fishing communities and bring them into national development processes. However, there is an evident need for more state and local community investment into capacity building for self-organization and deliberation processes that can better enable the development of mutually accepted goals and implementation tools such as communication and enforcement strategies. Maintaining the perceptions among local communities that AMPRs are an acceptable and legitimate form of governance is a key challenge for continued collective action and achieving associated conservation goals. This research shows that adapting governance institutions to fit the social-ecological context of each AMPR is critical for success, despite the perceived similarity between the AMPRs.","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","DOI":"10.18352/ijc.923","ISSN":"1875-0281","issue":"1","page":"705","title":"Do responsible fishing areas work? Comparing collective action challenges in three small-scale fisheries in Costa Rica","URL":"https://www.thecommonsjournal.org/article/10.18352/ijc.923/","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Chavez Carrillo","given":"Isis Ivania"},{"family":"Partelow","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Madrigal-Ballestero","given":"Roger"},{"family":"Schlüter","given":"Achim"},{"family":"Gutierrez-Montes","given":"Isabel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} along with ecological and environment problems including eutrophication, negative effect of pesticides on non-target species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"L9Rehjhg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gerhart, 2017; Qui\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}ones, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Gerhart, 2017; Quiñones, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":682,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NFMB67C2"],"itemData":{"id":682,"type":"document","language":"it","note":"page: 375–402\\nDOI: 10.1111/raq.12337","title":"Environmental issues in Chilean salmon farming: a review","author":[{"family":"Quiñones","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Fuentes","given":"M."},{"family":"Montes","given":"R.M."},{"family":"Soto","given":"D."},{"family":"León-Muñoz","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":33144,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYD2W77Q"],"itemData":{"id":33144,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Political Ecology","DOI":"10.2458/v24i1.20963","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"726–742","title":"Petri dishes of an archipelago: The ecological rubble of the Chilean salmon farming industry","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Gerhart","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and disease outbreak ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jYHLupgN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mardones, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Mardones, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":690,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TZVBVC2Z"],"itemData":{"id":690,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquaculture","DOI":"10.1016/j.aquaculture.2017.09.048","issue":"October 2017","language":"en","page":"211–220","title":"Identification of research gaps for highly infectious diseases in aquaculture: The case of the endemic Piscirickettsia salmonis in the Chilean salmon farming industry","volume":"482","author":[{"family":"Mardones","given":"F.O."},{"family":"Paredes","given":"F."},{"family":"Medina","given":"M."},{"family":"Tello","given":"A."},{"family":"Valdivia","given":"V."},{"family":"Ibarra","given":"R."},{"family":"Correa","given":"J."},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The knowledge on the ecological impact of invasive marine fishes is still rudimentary globally despite the extensive literature on the identification of new records, geographic spread, pathways ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"g4ONdK7K","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arndt, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Arndt, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":605,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XU8AC8VP"],"itemData":{"id":605,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Hydrobiologia","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"457–474","title":"Ecological impact of alien marine fishes: insights from freshwater systems based on a comparative review","volume":"817","author":[{"family":"Arndt","given":"E."},{"family":"Marchetti","given":"M.P."},{"family":"Schembri","given":"P.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This might be the reason that biological invasions are being widely disregarded when planning for conservation in the marine environment across local to global scales ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fTsYHND0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Giakoumi, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Giakoumi, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":630,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DYFAT8E7"],"itemData":{"id":630,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Diversity and Distributions","ISSN":"1366-9516","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Diversity and Distributions","note":"number: 12\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"1220-1231","title":"Space invaders; biological invasions in marine conservation planning","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Giakoumi","given":"Sylvaine"},{"family":"Guilhaumon","given":"François"},{"family":"Kark","given":"Salit"},{"family":"Terlizzi","given":"Antonio"},{"family":"Claudet","given":"Joachim"},{"family":"Felline","given":"Serena"},{"family":"Cerrano","given":"Carlo"},{"family":"Coll","given":"Marta"},{"family":"Danovaro","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Fraschetti","given":"Simonetta"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
4.2.1.5 Land and seascape change
This section will provide an overview of major changes in landscape/seascape. It will start with an introduction that provides a synopsis of where (urban, forest, agricultural and rangelands) major changes have been occurring and how these have affected biodiversity and sustainable use of wild species. 
4.2.1.5.1. Change in urban areas and impacts on biodiversity and sustainable use
Although urban areas cover less than 3% of the earth’s surface, urbanization is a major driver of global environmental change such as climate change, pollution, alteration of both abiotic and biotic ecosystem properties within, surrounding, and even at a great distance from urban areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FJgpa0MC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grimm, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Grimm, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":642,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ILS38KQB"],"itemData":{"id":642,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"264–272","title":"The changing landscape: ecosystem responses to urbanization and pollution across climatic and societal gradients","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Grimm","given":"N.B."},{"family":"Foster","given":"D."},{"family":"Groffman","given":"P."},{"family":"Grove","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Hopkinson","given":"C.S."},{"family":"Nadelhoffer","given":"K.J."},{"family":"Peters","given":"D.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Globally, the urban areas are expanding at twice the rate of their population ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Q5Vmq9cd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Seto, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Seto, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":671,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8TCIL956"],"itemData":{"id":671,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"40","language":"en","page":"16083–16088","title":"Global forecasts of urban expansion to 2030 and direct impacts on biodiversity and carbon pools","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Seto","given":"K.C."},{"family":"Güneralp","given":"B."},{"family":"Hutyra","given":"L.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The urban population has increased from ~200 million in 1900 to about 4 billion in 2014 and is expected to reach 5 billion in 2030 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LVuBtEye","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations, 2014)","plainCitation":"(United Nations, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":617,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XPKAD6ZX"],"itemData":{"id":617,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"United Nations, Department of Economic and Social Affairs","language":"en","title":"World Urbanization Prospects, the 2014 Revision: Highlights","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Currently, more than two-third (75%) of the population of the high-income countries live in urban areas, but the rapid growth of urban population is observed in the low-income and lower-middle-income countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PPGCZ098","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Residential development is a leading driver of land use change, with important implications for biodiversity, ecosystem processes, and human well-being ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RFU2iJRK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pejchar, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Pejchar, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":679,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UHIBR5F5"],"itemData":{"id":679,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"146–153","title":"Consequences of residential development for biodiversity and human well?being","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Pejchar","given":"L."},{"family":"Reed","given":"S.E."},{"family":"Bixler","given":"P."},{"family":"Ex","given":"L."},{"family":"Mockrin","given":"M.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urban land expansion modifies habitats causing biodiversity loss, alters biomass, natural processes, biogeochemistry, hydrology, land cover and surface energy balance, carbon storage, and causes climate change and pollution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GHk4l0l7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(d\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Amour, 2017; Seto, 2012)","plainCitation":"(d’Amour, 2017; Seto, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":624,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2GCAN7PS"],"itemData":{"id":624,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"34","language":"en","page":"8939–8944","title":"Future urban land expansion and implications for global croplands","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"Amour","given":"C.B.","non-dropping-particle":"d’"},{"family":"Reitsma","given":"F."},{"family":"Baiocchi","given":"G."},{"family":"Barthel","given":"S."},{"family":"Güneralp","given":"B."},{"family":"Erb","given":"K.H."},{"family":"Seto","given":"K.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":671,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8TCIL956"],"itemData":{"id":671,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"40","language":"en","page":"16083–16088","title":"Global forecasts of urban expansion to 2030 and direct impacts on biodiversity and carbon pools","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Seto","given":"K.C."},{"family":"Güneralp","given":"B."},{"family":"Hutyra","given":"L.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Light pollution in urban areas also has behavioral and ecological effects on wildlife ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eFllUNgy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schirmer, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Schirmer, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":675,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6ZDQD5IH"],"itemData":{"id":675,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific reports","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–13","title":"Mapping behaviorally relevant light pollution levels to improve urban habitat planning","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Schirmer","given":"A.E."},{"family":"Gallemore","given":"C."},{"family":"Liu","given":"T."},{"family":"Magle","given":"S."},{"family":"DiNello","given":"E."},{"family":"Ahmed","given":"H."},{"family":"Gilday","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urbanization affects primary production in terrestrial and aquatic ecosystems by replacing and fragmenting natural areas with impervious cover, increasing nutrient supply, changing hydrological regimes, and altering the composition (decreasing the abundance of apex predators) and seasonality of primary producers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YS2t5iU7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(El\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Sabaawi, 2018)","plainCitation":"(El?Sabaawi, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33120,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LHNGTXN8"],"itemData":{"id":33120,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Functional ecology","issue":"7","language":"en","page":"1718–1728","title":"Trophic structure in a rapidly urbanizing planet","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"El?Sabaawi","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urbanization has both lethal (e.g., vehicle collisions and bird strikes) and sub-lethal effects (physiological and behavioral changes) on animals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"o4KeksDm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Birnie-Gauvin, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Birnie-Gauvin, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33132,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J9S34WMF"],"itemData":{"id":33132,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Reviews","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"416–425","title":"Sublethal consequences of urban life for wild vertebrates","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Birnie-Gauvin","given":"K."},{"family":"Peiman","given":"K.S."},{"family":"Gallagher","given":"A.J."},{"family":"De Bruijn","given":"R."},{"family":"Cooke","given":"S.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The increasing urban sprawl has contributed to the extensive fragmentation and reduction of natural habitat worldwide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jL24vGDT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gelmi-Candusso & H\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}m\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}l\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}inen, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Gelmi-Candusso & Hämäläinen, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33145,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BXLCQ4BM"],"itemData":{"id":33145,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","language":"en","page":"41","title":"Seeds and the city: the interdependence of zoochory and ecosystem dynamics in urban environments","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Gelmi-Candusso","given":"T.A."},{"family":"Hämäläinen","given":"A.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urbanization has a range of adverse effects on ecosystem functioning, including the disruption of plant dispersal processes that are mediated by animals across the landscape ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NI0JGBoG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gelmi-Candusso & H\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}m\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}l\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}inen, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Gelmi-Candusso & Hämäläinen, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33145,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BXLCQ4BM"],"itemData":{"id":33145,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","language":"en","page":"41","title":"Seeds and the city: the interdependence of zoochory and ecosystem dynamics in urban environments","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Gelmi-Candusso","given":"T.A."},{"family":"Hämäläinen","given":"A.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The density and extent of housing is a strong predictor of the decline of native species of birds ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AQZwibdH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lepczyk, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Lepczyk, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33115,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GAAGV53D"],"itemData":{"id":33115,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"405–416","title":"Human impacts on regional avian diversity and abundance","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Lepczyk","given":"C.A."},{"family":"Flather","given":"C.H."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V.C."},{"family":"Pidgeon","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Hammer","given":"R.B."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} Likewise, urbanization is cited as a potential contributor to amphibian population declines ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ypcZBZhS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. P. Riley, 2005; Scheffers, 2012)","plainCitation":"(S. P. Riley, 2005; Scheffers, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":685,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I9VR9K9K"],"itemData":{"id":685,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1894–1907","title":"Effects of urbanization on the distribution and abundance of amphibians and invasive species in southern California streams","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Riley","given":"S.P."},{"family":"Busteed","given":"G.T."},{"family":"Kats","given":"L.B."},{"family":"Vandergon","given":"T.L."},{"family":"Lee","given":"L.F."},{"family":"Dagit","given":"R.G."},{"family":"Sauvajot","given":"R.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":33133,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A97FATP9"],"itemData":{"id":33133,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in ecology & evolution","ISSN":"0169-5347","issue":"9","page":"501–510","title":"What we know and don’t know about Earth's missing biodiversity","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Scheffers","given":"Brett R"},{"family":"Joppa","given":"Lucas N"},{"family":"Pimm","given":"Stuart L"},{"family":"Laurance","given":"William F"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urbanization influences species traits and micro-evolutionary changes in many species of mammals, birds, fishes, and insects ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"e6ETxhOS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alberti, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Alberti, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":603,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3GMDSZRN"],"itemData":{"id":603,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences","issue":"1712","language":"en","page":"20160029","title":"Urban driven phenotypic changes: empirical observations and theoretical implications for eco-evolutionary feedback","volume":"372","author":[{"family":"Alberti","given":"M."},{"family":"Marzluff","given":"J."},{"family":"Hunt","given":"V.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In response to urbanization, some plant traits (e.g., woodiness, seed mass, and height) tended to increase ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yWdoU87V","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. S. Williams, Hahs, and Vesk 2015)","plainCitation":"(N. S. Williams, Hahs, and Vesk 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":669,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R477DPRX"],"itemData":{"id":669,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Perspectives in Plant Ecology, Evolution and Systematics","issue":"1","language":"fr","page":"78–86","title":"Urbanisation, plant traits and the composition of urban floras","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Williams","given":"N.S."},{"family":"Hahs","given":"A.K."},{"family":"Vesk","given":"P.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Urban sprawl is expanding into marine environments with the construction of artificial structures. In Europe, the United States, Australia, and Asia, more than 50% of the shoreline is now modified by hard engineering, including groins and breakwaters to protect against erosion and wave action ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"w1sLhVjB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dafforn, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Dafforn, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33122,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X4WD3K94"],"itemData":{"id":33122,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"82–90","title":"Marine urbanization: an ecological framework for designing multifunctional artificial structures","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Dafforn","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Glasby","given":"T.M."},{"family":"Airoldi","given":"L."},{"family":"Rivero","given":"N.K."},{"family":"Mayer-Pinto","given":"M."},{"family":"Johnston","given":"E.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urbanization particularly coastal urbanization increased pressure on the surf zones of ocean beaches that provide habitat for a diversity of fishes and are prime sites for recreational angling and commercial net fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cp95gDxQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Olds, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Olds, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":676,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6H6QEBVF"],"itemData":{"id":676,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"78–89","title":"The ecology of fish in the surf zones of ocean beaches: a global review","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Olds","given":"Andrew D."},{"family":"Vargas?Fonseca","given":"Elena"},{"family":"Connolly","given":"Rod M."},{"family":"Gilby","given":"Ben L."},{"family":"Huijbers","given":"Chantal M."},{"family":"Hyndes","given":"Glenn A."},{"family":"Layman","given":"Craig A."},{"family":"Whitfield","given":"Alan K."},{"family":"Schlacher","given":"Thomas A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Coastal development including buildings construction also poses risks to marine turtles coming to beaches to lay eggs (e.g., Rushikulia and Gaharmatha in Odisha/Orissa where Olive Ridleys lay eggs). In an aquatic ecosystem, urban point sources of nutrients are the leading cause of hypoxia (decline in oxygen supply) resulting in adverse impacts on species’ physiology, life history, survival, reproduction, growth of aquatic invertebrates ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xArPF54t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Galic, 2018; Jenny, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Galic, 2018; Jenny, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33143,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MKF7D5IW"],"itemData":{"id":33143,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science of the Total Environment","language":"en","title":"Adverse impacts of hypoxia on aquatic invertebrates: A meta-analysis","author":[{"family":"Galic","given":"N."},{"family":"Hawkins","given":"T."},{"family":"Forbes","given":"V.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":696,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ME4C47GQ"],"itemData":{"id":696,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"45","language":"en","page":"12655–12660","title":"Urban point sources of nutrients were the leading cause for the historical spread of hypoxia across European lakes","volume":"113","author":[{"family":"Jenny","given":"J.P."},{"family":"Normandeau","given":"A."},{"family":"Francus","given":"P."},{"family":"Taranu","given":"Z.E."},{"family":"Gregory-Eaves","given":"I."},{"family":"Lapointe","given":"F."},{"family":"Zolitschka","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In recent years, sustainable urbanization, the concept of nature-based solution and urban nature have been increasing as urban landscapes constitute the future environment for most of the world’s human population. Urban forest, which is a dynamic system of trees, shrubs, green space, soil, and water, provides many functions, services and benefits needed for sustainable development of urban areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CQMq1EyC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Solomou, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Solomou, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":673,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GK5UK7L3"],"itemData":{"id":673,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Notulae Botanicae Horti Agrobotanici Cluj-Napoca","issue":"1","language":"es","page":"10–16","title":"Importance, Utilization and Health of Urban Forests: A Review","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Solomou","given":"A.D."},{"family":"Topalidou","given":"E.T."},{"family":"Germani","given":"R."},{"family":"Argiri","given":"A."},{"family":"Karetsos","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urban nature has the potential to improve air and water quality, mitigate flooding, enhance physical and mental health and promote social and cultural well-being ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GCHRqrrr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Keeler, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Keeler, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":698,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FLMGQHIC"],"itemData":{"id":698,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Sustainability","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"29","title":"Social-ecological and technological factors moderate the value of urban nature","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Keeler","given":"B.L."},{"family":"Hamel","given":"P."},{"family":"McPhearson","given":"T."},{"family":"Hamann","given":"M.H."},{"family":"Donahue","given":"M.L."},{"family":"Prado","given":"K.A.M."},{"family":"Guerry","given":"A.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urban greenery plays a significant role in reducing energy use for both heating and cooling ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bECxqNZt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ko, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Ko, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33113,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5JJJCIPU"],"itemData":{"id":33113,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban forestry & urban greening","language":"en","page":"318–335","title":"Trees and vegetation for residential energy conservation: A critical review for evidence-based urban greening in North America","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Ko","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urban green and blue spaces promote health by offering areas for physical activity, stress relief and social interaction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"noV82EBd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kabisch, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Kabisch, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":697,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IP6R4EKL"],"itemData":{"id":697,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental research","language":"en","page":"362–373","title":"The health benefits of nature-based solutions to urbanization challenges for children and the elderly–A systematic review","volume":"159","author":[{"family":"Kabisch","given":"N."},{"family":"Bosch","given":"M."},{"family":"Lafortezza","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Green roofs in urban areas also deliver selected nature’s contributions to people such as removal of pollution and reduced annual energy consumption ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oqjoQ0UY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. F. M. Francis & Jensen, 2017)","plainCitation":"(L. F. M. Francis & Jensen, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33140,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SZEWMYZK"],"itemData":{"id":33140,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban forestry & urban greening","language":"en","page":"167–176","title":"Benefits of green roofs: A systematic review of the evidence for three ecosystem services","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Francis","given":"L.F.M."},{"family":"Jensen","given":"M.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urban trees and grassland also provide habitats for different species of animals including birds, bees, butterflies and hoverflies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BCsiIf9L","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dylewski, 2019; Y. Han, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Dylewski, 2019; Y. Han, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33118,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IC5IQI5W"],"itemData":{"id":33118,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Entomology","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"678–689","title":"Are all urban green spaces a favourable habitat for pollinator communities? Bees, butterflies and hoverflies in different urban green areas","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Dylewski","given":"?."},{"family":"Ma?kowiak","given":"?."},{"family":"Banaszak?Cibicka","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":694,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H8X5TUHC"],"itemData":{"id":694,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science of The Total Environment","language":"en","page":"748–759","title":"Nest site selection for five common birds and their coexistence in an urban habitat","volume":"690","author":[{"family":"Han","given":"Y."},{"family":"Bai","given":"J."},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Z."},{"family":"Wu","given":"T."},{"family":"Chen","given":"P."},{"family":"Sun","given":"G."},{"family":"Zhao","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Overall, urban trees provide nature’s contributions to people including carbon sequestration, air quality improvement, storm water attenuation, food production, microclimate control, soil infiltration, visual quality, recreation, social capital, and energy conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"W5PLRWyT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kabisch, 2017; Lovell & Taylor, 2013; Roy, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Kabisch, 2017; Lovell & Taylor, 2013; Roy, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":674,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D9ZWME65"],"itemData":{"id":674,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Forestry & Urban Greening","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"351–363","title":"A systematic quantitative review of urban tree benefits, costs, and assessment methods across cities in different climatic zones","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Roy","given":"S."},{"family":"Byrne","given":"J."},{"family":"Pickering","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":697,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IP6R4EKL"],"itemData":{"id":697,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental research","language":"en","page":"362–373","title":"The health benefits of nature-based solutions to urbanization challenges for children and the elderly–A systematic review","volume":"159","author":[{"family":"Kabisch","given":"N."},{"family":"Bosch","given":"M."},{"family":"Lafortezza","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":689,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2WLYC7HY"],"itemData":{"id":689,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape ecology","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"1447–1463","title":"Supplying urban ecosystem services through multifunctional green infrastructure in the United States","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Lovell","given":"S.T."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"J.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, little is known how these trends affect sustainable use of wild species.
      More recently, rooftop gardens are becoming an important part of the urban agriculture and urban rooftop agriculture can improve various nature’s contributions to people, enrich urban biodiversity and reduce food insecurity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZD7vh1cu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Walters & Stoelzle Midden, 2018)","plainCitation":"(S. Walters & Stoelzle Midden, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33148,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QG8BWCSD"],"itemData":{"id":33148,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agriculture","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"168","title":"Sustainability of Urban Agriculture: Vegetable Production on Green Roofs","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Walters","given":"S."},{"family":"Stoelzle Midden","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urban and peri-urban agriculture contribute to 10 key societal challenges of urbanization: climate change, food security, biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people ecosystem services, agricultural intensification, resource efficiency, urban renewal and regeneration, land management, public health, social cohesion, and economic growth ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"szykAHfp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Artmann & Sartison, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Artmann & Sartison, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":606,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T68RD5J9"],"itemData":{"id":606,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"6","language":"en","title":"The role of urban agriculture as a nature-based solution: a review for developing a systemic assessment framework","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Artmann","given":"M."},{"family":"Sartison","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urban agriculture has impacts on food security, nutrition outcomes, physical and mental health outcomes, and social capital ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Tkwn6D6t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Audate, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Audate, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":604,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NEQZ3NTC"],"itemData":{"id":604,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BMC public health","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"672","title":"Scoping review of the impacts of urban agriculture on the determinants of health","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Audate","given":"P.P."},{"family":"Fernandez","given":"M.A."},{"family":"Cloutier","given":"G."},{"family":"Lebel","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Although urban development in developing counties is seemingly chaotic with high levels of poverty, there are opportunities to realize urban green infrastructure in those areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pvg18CL0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lindley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Lindley, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33117,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BUQ829EW"],"itemData":{"id":33117,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","language":"en","page":"328–338","title":"Rethinking urban green infrastructure and ecosystem services from the perspective of sub-Saharan African cities","volume":"180","author":[{"family":"Lindley","given":"S."},{"family":"Pauleit","given":"S."},{"family":"Yeshitela","given":"K."},{"family":"Cilliers","given":"S."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Urban development (population growth, in-migration) also leads to decongestion and depopulation of rural areas that may have a salutary impact on biodiversity, ecosystem services and populations of certain wild species in the rural areas. In and around urban areas, human-wild species conflict is responsible for billions of dollars of damage and costs association with mitigation and prevention ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"n6NUbLAB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Conover, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Conover, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33126,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IGK2D3DB"],"itemData":{"id":33126,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"CRC press","title":"Resolving human-wildlife conflicts: the science of wildlife damage management","author":[{"family":"Conover","given":"M.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Urban environments are notorious source of mortality of Wild species including roads, collision with buildings, depredation and disease ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6f7av1pU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Forman & Alexander, 1998; Loss, 2014; Nyhus, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Forman & Alexander, 1998; Loss, 2014; Nyhus, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33142,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ILC9WBSX"],"itemData":{"id":33142,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual review of ecology and systematics","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"207–231","title":"Roads and their major ecological effects","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Forman","given":"R.T."},{"family":"Alexander","given":"L.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":688,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XJGSHSJL"],"itemData":{"id":688,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"763–771","title":"Estimation of bird?vehicle collision mortality on US roads","volume":"78","author":[{"family":"Loss","given":"S.R."},{"family":"Will","given":"T."},{"family":"Marra","given":"P.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":687,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S4HQDPL6"],"itemData":{"id":687,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","language":"en","page":"143–171","title":"Human–wildlife conflict and coexistence","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Nyhus","given":"P.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
      4.2.1.5.2. Infrastructure development (dams and roads construction)
Humans have been modifying rivers for thousands of years for flood regulation, water supply, transportation, irrigation and more recently for settlements and industries, recreation, hydropower generation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gtmx8jgK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ripl, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Ripl, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33405,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PDWJVUQ3"],"itemData":{"id":33405,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London. Series B: Biological Sciences","issue":"1440","language":"en","page":"1921–1934","title":"Water: the bloodstream of the biosphere","volume":"358","author":[{"family":"Ripl","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Globally the number of dam constructions has increased dramatically over the past six decades to meet the energy demands and flood control. Dams have altered flows (fragmentation, flow regulation or both) of 48% of the rivers at various degrees worldwide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"k1HauaOa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grill, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Grill, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33329,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L9YIRZKR"],"itemData":{"id":33329,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Research Letters","DOI":"10.1088/1748-9326/10/1/015001","ISSN":"1748-9326","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Environ. Res. Lett.","page":"015001","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An index-based framework for assessing patterns and trends in river fragmentation and flow regulation by global dams at multiple scales","URL":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/10/1/015001","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Grill","given":"Günther"},{"family":"Lehner","given":"Bernhard"},{"family":"Lumsdon","given":"Alexander E"},{"family":"MacDonald","given":"Graham K"},{"family":"Zarfl","given":"Christiane"},{"family":"Reidy Liermann","given":"Catherine"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Only 37 per cent of rivers longer than 1,000 kilometers remain free-flowing over their entire length and 23 per cent flow uninterrupted to the ocean ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3TuMEKGV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grill, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Grill, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33330,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/559LCJX3"],"itemData":{"id":33330,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-019-1111-9","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7755","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"215-221","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Mapping the world’s free-flowing rivers","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-019-1111-9","volume":"569","author":[{"family":"Grill","given":"G."},{"family":"Lehner","given":"B."},{"family":"Thieme","given":"M."},{"family":"Geenen","given":"B."},{"family":"Tickner","given":"D."},{"family":"Antonelli","given":"F."},{"family":"Babu","given":"S."},{"family":"Borrelli","given":"P."},{"family":"Cheng","given":"L."},{"family":"Crochetiere","given":"H."},{"family":"Ehalt Macedo","given":"H."},{"family":"Filgueiras","given":"R."},{"family":"Goichot","given":"M."},{"family":"Higgins","given":"J."},{"family":"Hogan","given":"Z."},{"family":"Lip","given":"B."},{"family":"McClain","given":"M. E."},{"family":"Meng","given":"J."},{"family":"Mulligan","given":"M."},{"family":"Nilsson","given":"C."},{"family":"Olden","given":"J. D."},{"family":"Opperman","given":"J. J."},{"family":"Petry","given":"P."},{"family":"Reidy Liermann","given":"C."},{"family":"Sáenz","given":"L."},{"family":"Salinas-Rodríguez","given":"S."},{"family":"Schelle","given":"P."},{"family":"Schmitt","given":"R. J. P."},{"family":"Snider","given":"J."},{"family":"Tan","given":"F."},{"family":"Tockner","given":"K."},{"family":"Valdujo","given":"P. H."},{"family":"Soesbergen","given":"A.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Zarfl","given":"C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Currently, about 50,000 large dams (higher than 15 m), and an estimated 16.7 million reservoirs (larger than 0.01 ha) hold approximately 8,070 km3 of water  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8Xgm85eW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lehner, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Lehner, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33253,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2B3SPSUA"],"itemData":{"id":33253,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","issue":"9","language":"en","page":"494–502","title":"High?resolution mapping of the world's reservoirs and dams for sustainable river?flow management","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Lehner","given":"B."},{"family":"Liermann","given":"C.R."},{"family":"Revenga","given":"C."},{"family":"Vörösmarty","given":"C."},{"family":"Fekete","given":"B."},{"family":"Crouzet","given":"P."},{"family":"Nilsson","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Dam building has substantial impact on riverine ecosystems and freshwater biodiversity causing population loss ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MDdKXawK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dynesius & Nilsson, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Dynesius & Nilsson, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33256,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EGTJEIAY"],"itemData":{"id":33256,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"5186","language":"en","page":"753–762","title":"Fragmentation and flow regulation of river systems in the northern third of the world","volume":"266","author":[{"family":"Dynesius","given":"M."},{"family":"Nilsson","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Dams substantially modified lotic ecosystems causing extirpation of fish migration ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pPhC20yS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Y. Liu, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Y. Liu, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33199,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XPVV3BC2"],"itemData":{"id":33199,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"ABSTRACT This paper explores the ecosystem services provided by anadromous brown trout (often termed sea trout) populations in Norway. Sea trout is an important species in both freshwater and marine ecosystems and provides important demand-driven ecological provisioning and socio-cultural services. While the sea trout once provided an important provisioning service through a professional fishery and subsistence fishing, fishing for sea trout in the near shore coastal areas and in rivers is today a very popular and accessible recreational activity and generates primarily socio-cultural services. The recreational fishery contributes to local cultural heritage, its folkways and lore, to the development and transfer of local ecological knowledge and fishing experience to the young and to human well-being. As a salmonid species, the sea trout is sensitive to negative environmental conditions in both freshwater and marine coastal areas and is in general decline. A recent decision to expand production of farmed salmon may increase pressure on stocks. Good management of recreational fishing is accordingly important for the species to thrive, but knowledge of what fishers shall value with respect to fishing sea trout and what management measures they will accept is limited. Researchers sought to capture information about non-extractive direct use value (non-monetary) of the sea trout recreational fishery using questionnaire surveys targeting Norwegian anglers around the country. Results indicate that the most important ecosystem services delivered by recreational sea trout fisheries are social-cultural ecosystem services at the level of individual fishers; fishing sea trout most likely also has important social functions. Fishers are prepared to accept stricter management measures that reduce catches and allow fishing to continue but they oppose paying higher fees.","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2019.00178","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","language":"English","source":"Frontiers","title":"Social-Cultural Ecosystem Services of Sea Trout Recreational Fishing in Norway","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fmars.2019.00178/full","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Yajie"},{"family":"Bailey","given":"Jennifer L."},{"family":"Davidsen","given":"Jan G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, loss of native fish species and increase of non-native fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TQmNZgaf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Loures & Pompeu, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Loures & Pompeu, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33221,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/856437V5"],"itemData":{"id":33221,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Freshwater Biology","issue":"10","language":"en","page":"1806–1820","title":"Temporal changes in fish diversity in lotic and lentic environments along a reservoir cascade","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Loures","given":"R.C."},{"family":"Pompeu","given":"P.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and decrease in diversity of benthic macro invertebrate ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FZ6SCJVL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Linares, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Linares, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33255,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3AYEE75F"],"itemData":{"id":33255,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"River Research and Applications","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"725–735","title":"Small hydropower dam alters the taxonomic composition of benthic macroinvertebrate assemblages in a neotropical river","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Linares","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Assis","given":"W."},{"family":"Castro Solar","given":"R.R."},{"family":"Leitão","given":"R.P."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"R.M."},{"family":"Callisto","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Himalayan region particularly in China and India has approximately 55% of the world’s larger dams and is considered as the region with the highest dam density in the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"h934d1jB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grumbine & Pandit, 2013; Pandit & Grumbine, 2012; W.C.D., 2000)","plainCitation":"(Grumbine & Pandit, 2013; Pandit & Grumbine, 2012; W.C.D., 2000)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33239,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V472AC4H"],"itemData":{"id":33239,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"6115","language":"en","page":"36–37","title":"Threats from India's Himalaya dams","volume":"339","author":[{"family":"Grumbine","given":"R.E."},{"family":"Pandit","given":"M.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33391,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SYT5F6M3"],"itemData":{"id":33391,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1061–1071","title":"Potential effects of ongoing and proposed hydropower development on terrestrial biological diversity in the Indian Himalaya","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Pandit","given":"M.K."},{"family":"Grumbine","given":"R.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":33338,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UC6LHPWY"],"itemData":{"id":33338,"type":"book","event-place":"London, UK","language":"en","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London, UK","title":"Dams and Development: A New Framework for Decision-Making, World Commission on Dams","author":[{"literal":"W.C.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The proposed dam locations in the Indian Himalaya are situated in areas of high species richness for angiosperms, birds, fishes, and butterflies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yWuEYRT9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grumbine & Pandit, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Grumbine & Pandit, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33239,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V472AC4H"],"itemData":{"id":33239,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"6115","language":"en","page":"36–37","title":"Threats from India's Himalaya dams","volume":"339","author":[{"family":"Grumbine","given":"R.E."},{"family":"Pandit","given":"M.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Models showed that dam building could lead to loss of 22 angiosperm and 7 vertebrate taxa by 2025 due to the submergence and habitat degradation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hviUCmSj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pandit & Grumbine, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Pandit & Grumbine, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33391,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SYT5F6M3"],"itemData":{"id":33391,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1061–1071","title":"Potential effects of ongoing and proposed hydropower development on terrestrial biological diversity in the Indian Himalaya","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Pandit","given":"M.K."},{"family":"Grumbine","given":"R.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Dam construction alters survival, phenology and growth of floodplain vegetation and reduces field yields below the dams ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"22PhEEjO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Forsberg, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Forsberg, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33340,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BTTE6BID"],"itemData":{"id":33340,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PloS one","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"PloS one","note":"number: 8\\npublisher: Public Library of Science San Francisco, CA USA","page":"e0182254","title":"The potential impact of new Andean dams on Amazon fluvial ecosystems","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Forsberg","given":"Bruce R"},{"family":"Melack","given":"John M"},{"family":"Dunne","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Barthem","given":"Ronaldo B"},{"family":"Goulding","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Paiva","given":"Rodrigo CD"},{"family":"Sorribas","given":"Mino V"},{"family":"Silva Jr","given":"Urbano L"},{"family":"Weisser","given":"Sabine"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The reported effects of dams on fish and other aquatic mammals include blocking migration route, habitat fragmentation and changing from lotic to lentic water in the impounded areas, changes of water flow in downstream reaches ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"x7H456aN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wu, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Wu, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33297,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/32SRJ9EQ"],"itemData":{"id":33297,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fungal Diversity","DOI":"10.1007/s13225-019-00432-7","ISSN":"1560-2745, 1878-9129","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fungal Diversity","language":"en","page":"1-76","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Resource diversity of Chinese macrofungi: edible, medicinal and poisonous species","title-short":"Resource diversity of Chinese macrofungi","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s13225-019-00432-7","volume":"98","author":[{"family":"Wu","given":"Fang"},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Li-Wei"},{"family":"Yang","given":"Zhu-Liang"},{"family":"Bau","given":"Tolgor"},{"family":"Li","given":"Tai-Hui"},{"family":"Dai","given":"Yu-Cheng"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although reservoir fish yields will compensate for some downstream losses, increase in mercury contamination due to dams could offset the benefits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fksBX1PT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Forsberg, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Forsberg, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33340,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BTTE6BID"],"itemData":{"id":33340,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PloS one","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"PloS one","note":"number: 8\\npublisher: Public Library of Science San Francisco, CA USA","page":"e0182254","title":"The potential impact of new Andean dams on Amazon fluvial ecosystems","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Forsberg","given":"Bruce R"},{"family":"Melack","given":"John M"},{"family":"Dunne","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Barthem","given":"Ronaldo B"},{"family":"Goulding","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Paiva","given":"Rodrigo CD"},{"family":"Sorribas","given":"Mino V"},{"family":"Silva Jr","given":"Urbano L"},{"family":"Weisser","given":"Sabine"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Roads are the seeds of tropical forest destruction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oVte9wif","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(W. Laurance, 2012)","plainCitation":"(W. Laurance, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33337,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U2XEJDQY"],"itemData":{"id":33337,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Yale Environment 360 Magazine","language":"it","page":"1–6","title":"As roads spread in rainforests, the environmental toll grows","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Laurance","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Rapid road constructions in the tropics affect many species particularly specialist ones that are susceptible to hunting, roadkill, elevated predation and species invasions near roads. Road building increases forest disturbances, edge effects, facilitates legal and illegal logging and increases hunting pressure on wild speciesITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"REYZI2QY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Laporte, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Laporte, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33339,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L3KGIH26"],"itemData":{"id":33339,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"5830","journalAbbreviation":"Science","note":"number: 5830\\npublisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science","page":"1451-1451","title":"Expansion of industrial logging in Central Africa","volume":"316","author":[{"family":"Laporte","given":"Nadine T"},{"family":"Stabach","given":"Jared A"},{"family":"Grosch","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Lin","given":"Tiffany S"},{"family":"Goetz","given":"Scott J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Linear infrastructure such as roads, fences, walls, railways and pipelines create barriers that prevent species movements ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IDv1H61N","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wingard, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Wingard, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33369,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HN4ZZI9M"],"itemData":{"id":33369,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"CMS","issue":"Doc","language":"en","page":"2","title":"Guidelines on mitigating the impact of linear infrastructure and related disturbance on mammals in Central Asia","volume":"P11","author":[{"family":"Wingard","given":"J."},{"family":"Zahler","given":"P."},{"family":"Victurine","given":"R."},{"family":"Bayasgalan","given":"O."},{"family":"Buuveibaatar","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Linear infrastructure has caused habitat fragmentation, splited populations, changed migration, nomadism and dispersal and altered behaviors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bcOMcrKG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mueller & Fagan, 2008; Olson, 2011; Wingard, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Mueller & Fagan, 2008; Olson, 2011; Wingard, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33219,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/INA7SBK4"],"itemData":{"id":33219,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oikos","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"654–664","title":"Search and navigation in dynamic environments–from individual behaviors to population distributions","volume":"117","author":[{"family":"Mueller","given":"T."},{"family":"Fagan","given":"W.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":33369,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HN4ZZI9M"],"itemData":{"id":33369,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"CMS","issue":"Doc","language":"en","page":"2","title":"Guidelines on mitigating the impact of linear infrastructure and related disturbance on mammals in Central Asia","volume":"P11","author":[{"family":"Wingard","given":"J."},{"family":"Zahler","given":"P."},{"family":"Victurine","given":"R."},{"family":"Bayasgalan","given":"O."},{"family":"Buuveibaatar","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":33389,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QJIH3TC8"],"itemData":{"id":33389,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"304–312","title":"Death by a thousand huts? Effects of household presence on density and distribution of Mongolian gazelles","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Olson","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Mueller","given":"T."},{"family":"Kerby","given":"J.T."},{"family":"Bolortsetseg","given":"S."},{"family":"Leimgruber","given":"P."},{"family":"Nicolson","given":"C.R."},{"family":"Fuller","given":"T.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Structure like border fences cause direct mortality of wildlife particularly large carnivores and large herbivores due to entanglement ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ENY0k23q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Trouwborst, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Trouwborst, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33288,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N4Z5VSMJ"],"itemData":{"id":33288,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000411522900005","container-title":"Nature Conservation-Bulgaria","DOI":"10.3897/natureconservation.21.13690","ISSN":"1314-6947","issue":"21","page":"83-128","title":"International law and lions (Panthera leo): understanding and improving the contribution of wildlife treaties to the conservation and sustainable use of an iconic carnivore","URL":"://WOS:000411522900005","author":[{"family":"Trouwborst","given":"A."},{"family":"Lewis","given":"M."},{"family":"Burnham","given":"D."},{"family":"Dickman","given":"A."},{"family":"Hinks","given":"A."},{"family":"Hodgetts","given":"T."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"E. A."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"D. W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The border wall between US-Mexico reduces area, quality, and connectivity of plant and animal habitats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"waUQIdkb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(H. Peters, 2016)","plainCitation":"(H. Peters, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33295,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XCPRA2ID"],"itemData":{"id":33295,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Cape Verde in the Eastern Atlantic is typical of many island groups in supporting a wealth of endemic species both terrestrial and marine. Marine gastropod molluscs of the genus Conus, commonly known as cone snails, occur in coastal tropical waters throughout the globe, but in Cape Verde their endemism reaches its apogee with 53 out of 56 species occurring nowhere else, the majority of which are restricted to single islands and frequently to single bays. However, Cape Verde is rapidly moving to a tourism-based economy with a projected boom in infrastructure development often coincidental with the shallow-water habitat of many range-restricted Conus. The conservation assessment of Conus to standards of the International Union for the Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List of Endangered Species, found that 45.3% of 53 species assessed from Cape Verde are threatened or near-threatened with extinction compared to 7.4% of 579 species in the rest of the world. The only three Conus species globally assessed as Critically Endangered and on the cusp of extinction are all endemic to Cape Verde. Our analysis of Conus species distribution, together with spatial data of coastal protected areas and tourism development zones, identify important areas for future research and new marine protection. Our findings show that endemism with its associated risks for Conus in Cape Verde has worldwide parallels with many non-marine taxa, while our proposed strategy for Conus conservation extends beyond the confines of the country and this taxonomic group.","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2016.06.006","ISSN":"2351-9894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"201-213","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The cone snails of Cape Verde: Marine endemism at a terrestrial scale","title-short":"The cone snails of Cape Verde","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2351989416300439","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Peters","given":"Howard"},{"family":"O’Leary","given":"Bethan C."},{"family":"Hawkins","given":"Julie P."},{"family":"Roberts","given":"Callum M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",7,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

4.2.1.5.3. Change in agricultural land and impacts on biodiversity and sustainable use
The expansion and intensification of agriculture are growing rapidly across the globe since 1700 to provide food, fiber, and biofuels, through livestock farming, aquaculture and the cultivation of trees ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"y7tQ5rAF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dudley & Sasha, 2017; Grassini, 2013; Ramankutty, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Dudley & Sasha, 2017; Grassini, 2013; Ramankutty, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33240,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XLJKUWUS"],"itemData":{"id":33240,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature communications","language":"en","page":"2918","title":"Distinguishing between yield advances and yield plateaus in historical crop production trends","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Grassini","given":"P."},{"family":"Eskridge","given":"K.M."},{"family":"Cassman","given":"K.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33258,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VTYZCHY2"],"itemData":{"id":33258,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity","issue":"2-3","language":"en","page":"45–49","title":"Agriculture and biodiversity: a review","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Dudley","given":"N."},{"family":"Sasha","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33399,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PMRACK5C"],"itemData":{"id":33399,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual review of plant biology","language":"en","page":"789–815","title":"Trends in global agricultural land use: implications for environmental health and food security","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Ramankutty","given":"N."},{"family":"Mehrabi","given":"Z."},{"family":"Waha","given":"K."},{"family":"Jarvis","given":"L."},{"family":"Kremen","given":"C."},{"family":"Herrero","given":"M."},{"family":"Rieseberg","given":"L.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Worldwide, agriculture has already cleared or converted 70% of grassland, 50% of savanna, 45% of the temperate deciduous forest, and 27% of the tropical forest ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5l2Fp1mT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. A. Foley, 2011)","plainCitation":"(J. A. Foley, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33299,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WS9ZCEXR"],"itemData":{"id":33299,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7369","language":"en","page":"337","title":"Solutions for a cultivated planet","volume":"478","author":[{"family":"Foley","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Ramankutty","given":"N."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Cassidy","given":"E.S."},{"family":"Gerber","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Johnston","given":"M."},{"family":"Balzer","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Between 1980 and 2000, more than 55% of new agricultural land came at the expense of intact forests, and another 28% came from disturbed forests across the tropics ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5cHOE4Pp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gibbs, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Gibbs, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33304,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JNIRSJGS"],"itemData":{"id":33304,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"38","language":"en","page":"16732–16737","title":"Tropical forests were the primary sources of new agricultural land in the 1980s and 1990s","volume":"107","author":[{"family":"Gibbs","given":"H.K."},{"family":"Ruesch","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Achard","given":"F."},{"family":"Clayton","given":"M.K."},{"family":"Holmgren","given":"P."},{"family":"Ramankutty","given":"N."},{"family":"Foley","given":"J.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The demand for food, feed and other products is expected to increase in the future resulting in 10 billion hectares of natural ecosystems that would be converted to agriculture by 2050 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fmUvaNtt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alexandratos & Bruinsma, 2012; Tilman, 2001b)","plainCitation":"(Alexandratos & Bruinsma, 2012; Tilman, 2001b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33351,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YDT8LGYJ"],"itemData":{"id":33351,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forecasting agriculturally driven global environmental change. science","issue":"5515","language":"en","page":"281–284","volume":"292","author":[{"family":"Tilman","given":"D."},{"family":"Fargione","given":"J."},{"family":"Wolff","given":"B."},{"family":"D'antonio","given":"C."},{"family":"Dobson","given":"A."},{"family":"Howarth","given":"R."},{"family":"Swackhamer","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":26067,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CIH8N36G","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GMSHFUV4"],"itemData":{"id":26067,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agric. Dev. Econ. Div., Food Agric. Organ","issue":"12–03","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: United Nations, Rome","title":"World agriculture towards 2030/2050: the 2012 revision","author":[{"family":"Alexandratos","given":"N."},{"family":"Bruinsma","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Agriculture as conventionally practised is one of the greatest environmental threats causing loss of wild species habitat, soil erosion, pollution, water?stress, sedimentation of waterways, pesticide poisoning of humans and non-target species, and greenhouse?gas emissions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CuD0eP09","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Power, 2010; Tayleur, 2017; Tilman, 2001a)","plainCitation":"(Power, 2010; Tayleur, 2017; Tilman, 2001a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33398,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JB5UYUZW"],"itemData":{"id":33398,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Philosophical transactions of the royal society B: biological sciences","issue":"1554","language":"en","page":"2959–2971","title":"Ecosystem services and agriculture: tradeoffs and synergies","volume":"365","author":[{"family":"Power","given":"A.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":33349,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GP4EZRIW"],"itemData":{"id":33349,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"610–618","title":"Global coverage of agricultural sustainability standards, and their role in conserving biodiversity","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Tayleur","given":"C."},{"family":"Balmford","given":"A."},{"family":"Buchanan","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S.H."},{"family":"Ducharme","given":"H."},{"family":"Green","given":"R.E."},{"family":"Phalan","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33345,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V26VD2ND"],"itemData":{"id":33345,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"science","issue":"5515","language":"en","page":"281–284","title":"Forecasting agriculturally driven global environmental change","volume":"292","author":[{"family":"Tilman","given":"D."},{"family":"Fargione","given":"J."},{"family":"Wolff","given":"B."},{"family":"D'antonio","given":"C."},{"family":"Dobson","given":"A."},{"family":"Howarth","given":"R."},{"family":"Swackhamer","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Agriculture affects biodiversity mainly through the conversion of natural habitats into cropland and pasture, intensification of management in long-established cultural landscapes and release of pesticides and pollutants, including greenhouse gases ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bqFGqAgH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dudley & Sasha, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Dudley & Sasha, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33258,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VTYZCHY2"],"itemData":{"id":33258,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity","issue":"2-3","language":"en","page":"45–49","title":"Agriculture and biodiversity: a review","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Dudley","given":"N."},{"family":"Sasha","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Across biomes and taxonomic groups, conversion to farms and ranches results in losses of average reduction of 13.6% of local species richness and 10.7% of species abundance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OaoIazBi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Newbold, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Newbold, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32462,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7NGLHL22"],"itemData":{"id":32462,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7545","language":"en","page":"45","title":"Global effects of land use on local terrestrial biodiversity","volume":"520","author":[{"family":"Newbold","given":"T."},{"family":"Hudson","given":"L.N."},{"family":"Hill","given":"S.L."},{"family":"Contu","given":"S."},{"family":"Lysenko","given":"I."},{"family":"Senior","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Day","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"h