d the consequences for pollination","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Brittain","given":"C."},{"family":"Potts","given":"S.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33273,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KQULG44S"],"itemData":{"id":33273,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agriculture, ecosystems & environment","DOI":"10.1016/j.agee.2013.12.013","language":"en","page":"153–160","title":"Pesticide doses, landscape structure and their relative effects on farmland birds","volume":"185","author":[{"family":"Chiron","given":"F."},{"family":"Chargé","given":"R."},{"family":"Julliard","given":"R."},{"family":"Jiguet","given":"F."},{"family":"Muratet","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":33272,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KIZFJXCL"],"itemData":{"id":33272,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science and Pollution Research","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"119–134","title":"Risks of large-scale use of systemic insecticides to ecosystem functioning and services","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Chagnon","given":"M."},{"family":"Kreutzweiser","given":"D."},{"family":"Mitchell","given":"E.A."},{"family":"Morrissey","given":"C.A."},{"family":"Noome","given":"D.A."},{"family":"Sluijs","given":"J.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":33342,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UFC48EF7"],"itemData":{"id":33342,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science of the Total Environment","ISSN":"0048-9697","journalAbbreviation":"Science of the Total Environment","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"146-153","title":"Assessment of the exposure to organochlorine pesticides, PCBs and PAHs in six species of predatory birds of the Canary Islands, Spain","volume":"472","author":[{"family":"Luzardo","given":"Octavio P"},{"family":"Ruiz-Suárez","given":"Norberto"},{"family":"Henríquez-Hernández","given":"Luis Alberto"},{"family":"Valerón","given":"Pilar F"},{"family":"Camacho","given":"María"},{"family":"Zumbado","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Boada","given":"Luis D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Agriculture is a major cause of global endangerment of flora and fauna, including pollinators ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nOzi5vIm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alfaro-Shigueto, 2005; R. E. Green, 2005; Norris, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Alfaro-Shigueto, 2005; R. E. Green, 2005; Norris, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32507,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AE6VFQ6J"],"itemData":{"id":32507,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the Twenty-first Annual Symposium on Sea Turtle Biology and Conservation","issue":"4","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Philadelphia, PA\\npublisher: NOAA","page":"2009–2041","title":"Diet analysis from green turtle Chelonia mydas agassizii from central Peruvian Coast","volume":"368","author":[{"family":"Alfaro-Shigueto","given":"J."},{"family":"Montes","given":"D."},{"family":"Acleto","given":"C."},{"family":"Zuñiga","given":"R."},{"family":"Huamán","given":"P."},{"family":"Ali","given":"H."},{"family":"Qaiser","given":"M."}],"editor":[{"family":"Coyne","given":"M."},{"family":"Clark","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32479,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I46XAHD4"],"itemData":{"id":32479,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"5709","language":"en","title":"Farming and the Fate of Wild Nature","volume":"307","author":[{"family":"Green","given":"Rhys E."},{"family":"Cornell","given":"Stephen J."},{"family":"Scharlemann","given":"Jörn P.W."},{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":33409,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9H6BGHHC"],"itemData":{"id":33409,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation letters","DOI":"10.1111/j.1755-263X.2008.00007.x","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"2–11","title":"Agriculture and biodiversity conservation: opportunity knocks","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Norris","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The deforestation caused by agricultural expansion in tropics has led to population declines of commercially traded wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8gTsBSfG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Symes, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Symes, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32549,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6VE22I2Z"],"itemData":{"id":32549,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable trade in wildlife products both legally and illegally is a leading cause of population declines and increased extinction risk in commercially valuable species. However due to the clandestine nature of illegal trade and paucity of overarching studies of legal trade our understanding on international trade networks is patchy. We develop a gravity–underreporting modelling framework to analyse and compare: (i) data on the legal trade in mammalian, avian and reptilian products from recorded by The Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and (ii) to data on the seizures of illegal products entering the USA between 2004 and 2013. We find substantial differences in the factors driving legal trade for the 3 taxonomic groups considered, indicating different drivers for different product markets. Illegal imports for all groups were associated with increasing exporter GDP. We found higher probabilities of underreporting for avian and reptile products, and in general central Africa, central Asia, Eastern Europe and Pacific Island states showed higher underreporting than other regions, indicating the existence of complex trade networks and the potential for the laundering of illegal products through legal markets. Our results show the important regional and economic trends driving wildlife trade. Our new modelling framework can also help illuminate previously unseen aspects of illegal and legal wildlife trade, which can help with the implementation of interventions to curb the impact of trade on wild populations.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2017.11.007","ISSN":"00063207","page":"268–276","title":"The gravity of wildlife trade","volume":"218","author":[{"family":"Symes","given":"William S."},{"family":"McGrath","given":"Francesca L."},{"family":"Rao","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L. Roman"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Sharp decline or threatened with total extinction of several species of farmland birds in Europe was attributed to the sorts of agricultural intensification encouraged by the Common Agricultural Policy ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0cB9XNE7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pain & Pienkowski, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Pain & Pienkowski, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33388,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U4RUZUKZ"],"itemData":{"id":33388,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Academic Press","title":"Farming and birds in Europe: the common agricultural policy and its implications for bird conservation","author":[{"family":"Pain","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Pienkowski","given":"M.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The combined effect of deforestation and commercial trade of wild species further increases the average losses of species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c2MdiTZa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Symes, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Symes, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32549,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6VE22I2Z"],"itemData":{"id":32549,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable trade in wildlife products both legally and illegally is a leading cause of population declines and increased extinction risk in commercially valuable species. However due to the clandestine nature of illegal trade and paucity of overarching studies of legal trade our understanding on international trade networks is patchy. We develop a gravity–underreporting modelling framework to analyse and compare: (i) data on the legal trade in mammalian, avian and reptilian products from recorded by The Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and (ii) to data on the seizures of illegal products entering the USA between 2004 and 2013. We find substantial differences in the factors driving legal trade for the 3 taxonomic groups considered, indicating different drivers for different product markets. Illegal imports for all groups were associated with increasing exporter GDP. We found higher probabilities of underreporting for avian and reptile products, and in general central Africa, central Asia, Eastern Europe and Pacific Island states showed higher underreporting than other regions, indicating the existence of complex trade networks and the potential for the laundering of illegal products through legal markets. Our results show the important regional and economic trends driving wildlife trade. Our new modelling framework can also help illuminate previously unseen aspects of illegal and legal wildlife trade, which can help with the implementation of interventions to curb the impact of trade on wild populations.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2017.11.007","ISSN":"00063207","page":"268–276","title":"The gravity of wildlife trade","volume":"218","author":[{"family":"Symes","given":"William S."},{"family":"McGrath","given":"Francesca L."},{"family":"Rao","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L. Roman"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Species loss has effect on productivity (biomass production by plants) and decomposition (mass loss of plant litter) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VmHE1OWn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hooper, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Hooper, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33227,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HWASABV3"],"itemData":{"id":33227,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7401","language":"en","page":"105","title":"A global synthesis reveals biodiversity loss as a major driver of ecosystem change","volume":"486","author":[{"family":"Hooper","given":"D.U."},{"family":"Adair","given":"E.C."},{"family":"Cardinale","given":"B.J."},{"family":"Byrnes","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Hungate","given":"B.A."},{"family":"Matulich","given":"K.L."},{"family":"O’Connor","given":"M.I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} hence has an impact on the sustainable use of wild species. However, the plant species identity is also important in affecting decomposition ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KAlBv0IX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vivanco & Austin, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Vivanco & Austin, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33305,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9HYVUME4"],"itemData":{"id":33305,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Argentina. Journal of Ecology","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"727–736","title":"Tree species identity alters forest litter decomposition through long?term plant and soil interactions in Patagonia","volume":"96","author":[{"family":"Vivanco","given":"L."},{"family":"Austin","given":"A.T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 


Figure 4.3.  IUCN red-listed species threatened by agriculture (i.e., CR= Critically Endangered, EN= Endangered, VU= Vulnerable) in a range of biodiversity groups. Data are from the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List database (http://www.iucnredlist.org/search/search?basic). Least concern (LC) species were excluded from the analysis. IUCN threat codes used to assess agricultural threats were 1.1, 6.2.1, 6.3.1, and 6.3.7. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BEH3WOgV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Norris, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Norris, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33409,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9H6BGHHC"],"itemData":{"id":33409,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation letters","DOI":"10.1111/j.1755-263X.2008.00007.x","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"2–11","title":"Agriculture and biodiversity conservation: opportunity knocks","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Norris","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} @ 2008 Blackwell Publishing, Inc. license number 515479115045.

      This agriculture expansion, particularly in the tropics, has negative impacts on biodiversity and the territories occupied by indigenous people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VAzI5B09","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(W. F. Laurance, 2014; Scariot, 2013)","plainCitation":"(W. F. Laurance, 2014; Scariot, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U9XP6RJ4"],"itemData":{"id":33383,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"177–178","title":"Land sparing or land sharing: the missing link","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Scariot","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33254,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DTMY3HSY"],"itemData":{"id":33254,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in ecology & evolution","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"107–116","title":"Agricultural expansion and its impacts on tropical nature","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Laurance","given":"W.F."},{"family":"Sayer","given":"J."},{"family":"Cassman","given":"K.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Approximately 11% of the world's forested lands are included within territories occupied by indigenous peoples ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OEQHuJW8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sobrevila, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Sobrevila, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33375,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VLT9AI4C"],"itemData":{"id":33375,"type":"book","event-place":"Washington, DC","language":"en","publisher":"The World Bank","publisher-place":"Washington, DC","title":"The role of indigenous peoples in biodiversity conservation: the natural but often forgotten partners","author":[{"family":"Sobrevila","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and at least 36% intact forest landscapes are within indigenous people’s lands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"P685lsLq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fa, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Fa, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33290,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HMHFQU63"],"itemData":{"id":33290,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","DOI":"10.1002/fee.2148","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"135–140","title":"Importance of Indigenous Peoples’ lands for the conservation of Intact Forest Landscapes","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Fa","given":"John E"},{"family":"Watson","given":"James EM"},{"family":"Leiper","given":"Ian"},{"family":"Potapov","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Evans","given":"Tom D"},{"family":"Burgess","given":"Neil D"},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Zsolt"},{"family":"Fernández-Llamazares","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Duncan","given":"Tom"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Stephanie"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Such peoples, as well as smallholder farmers and traditional peoples, derive life's necessities and monetary income from subsistence farming and hunting, fishing, harvesting of wild products including non-timber forest products from those indigenous lands maintaining biodiversity and ecosystem services ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ammwtS8D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Scariot, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Scariot, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U9XP6RJ4"],"itemData":{"id":33383,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"177–178","title":"Land sparing or land sharing: the missing link","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Scariot","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Diverse agricultural systems exist, though, with combinations of short-lived and perennial crops together with timber and non-timber products developed over centuries in rural areas, including by indigenous peoples and local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OkyfySl3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some indigenous communities such as Australian aborigines maintained a complex system of land management using seasonal calendar inform adaptive savanna burning and the life cycles of native plants to ensure plentiful of wildlife and plant foods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"z5vSM1HV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gammage, 2011; M. McKemey, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Gammage, 2011; M. McKemey, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33302,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FWUQZBVD"],"itemData":{"id":33302,"type":"book","event-place":"Australia","language":"en","publisher":"NSW","publisher-place":"Australia","title":"The Biggest Estate on Earth; Allen & Unwin: Crows Nest","author":[{"family":"Gammage","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33319,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9Z4MG9XF"],"itemData":{"id":33319,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Indigenous fire management is experiencing a resurgence worldwide. Northern Australia is the world leader in Indigenous savanna burning, delivering social, cultural, environmental and economic benefits. In 2016, a greenhouse gas abatement fire program commenced in the savannas of south-eastern Arnhem Land in the Northern Territory, managed by the Indigenous Yugul Mangi rangers. We undertook participatory action research and semi-structured interviews with rangers and Elders during 2016 and 2019 to investigate Indigenous knowledge and obtain local feedback about fire management. Results indicated that Indigenous rangers effectively use cross-cultural science (including local and Traditional Ecological Knowledge alongside western science) to manage fire. Fire management is a key driver in the production of bush tucker (wild food) resources and impacts other cultural and ecological values. A need for increased education and awareness about Indigenous burning was consistently emphasized. To address this, the project participants developed the Yugul Mangi Faiya En Sisen Kelenda (Yugul Mangi Fire and Seasons Calendar) that drew on Indigenous knowledge of seasonal biocultural indicators to guide the rangers&rsquo; fire management planning. The calendar has potential for application in fire management planning, intergenerational transfer of Indigenous knowledge and locally driven adaptive fire management.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su12030995","issue":"3","language":"en","license":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","page":"995","title":"Indigenous Knowledge and Seasonal Calendar Inform Adaptive Savanna Burning in Northern Australia","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/12/3/995","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"McKemey","given":"Michelle"},{"family":"Ens","given":"Emilie"},{"family":"Rangers","given":"Yugul Mangi"},{"family":"Costello","given":"Oliver"},{"family":"Reid","given":"Nick"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In order to reduce the social and environmental impacts of agriculture, there has been a call for improved practices and certification, and the codification of sustainable agricultural standards are growing, albeit worldwide coverage of certification is relatively small ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EqaM4pMy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Milder, 2015; Tayleur, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Milder, 2015; Tayleur, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33217,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6Y8F7LBE"],"itemData":{"id":33217,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation biology","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"309–320","title":"An agenda for assessing and improving conservation impacts of sustainability standards in tropical agriculture","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Milder","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Arbuthnot","given":"M."},{"family":"Blackman","given":"A."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"S.E."},{"family":"Giovannucci","given":"D."},{"family":"Gross","given":"L."},{"family":"Meyer","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":33349,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GP4EZRIW"],"itemData":{"id":33349,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"610–618","title":"Global coverage of agricultural sustainability standards, and their role in conserving biodiversity","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Tayleur","given":"C."},{"family":"Balmford","given":"A."},{"family":"Buchanan","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S.H."},{"family":"Ducharme","given":"H."},{"family":"Green","given":"R.E."},{"family":"Phalan","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Certification for sustainable agriculture has reduced inputs of chemical pesticides, fertilizers, and herbicides, and increased adoption of shade trees and soil conservation practices, reduced water use and pollution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bRrKi2pq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Blackman & Naranjo, 2012; Ibanez & Blackman, 2016; Rueda, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Blackman & Naranjo, 2012; Ibanez & Blackman, 2016; Rueda, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33282,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/857I4AEC"],"itemData":{"id":33282,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","language":"pt","page":"58–66","title":"Does eco-certification have environmental benefits? Organic coffee in Costa Rica","volume":"83","author":[{"family":"Blackman","given":"A."},{"family":"Naranjo","given":"M.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":33232,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZDD4YIFZ"],"itemData":{"id":33232,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","language":"en","page":"14–27","title":"Is eco-certification a win–win for developing country agriculture? Organic coffee certification in Colombia","volume":"82","author":[{"family":"Ibanez","given":"M."},{"family":"Blackman","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33381,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S7R3Q2AH"],"itemData":{"id":33381,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Regional environmental change","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"25–33","title":"Eco-certification and coffee cultivation enhance tree cover and forest connectivity in the Colombian coffee landscapes","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Rueda","given":"X."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"N.E."},{"family":"Lambin","given":"E.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A project such as Future Resources, Agriculture and Nature Conservation in Germany was initiated to develop and test measures to preserve and increase biodiversity in agricultural landscape (https://www.franz-projekt.de/website/english-summary). Positive and potentially indirect effects of certification on biodiversity were reported ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zNNrVgzz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tscharntke, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Tscharntke, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33358,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6DKZ6CPY"],"itemData":{"id":33358,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"14–23","title":"Conserving biodiversity through certification of tropical agroforestry crops at local and landscape scales","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Tscharntke","given":"T."},{"family":"Milder","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Schroth","given":"G."},{"family":"Clough","given":"Y."},{"family":"DeClerck","given":"F."},{"family":"Waldron","given":"A."},{"family":"Ghazoul","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, sustainable certification schemes of oil palm significantly reduce deforestation but not fire or peatland clearance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gqC3Iobs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carlson, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Carlson, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":249,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CYM9FUHS"],"itemData":{"id":249,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-09923-230135","issue":"1","note":"type: Journal Article","title":"Peruvian anchoveta as a telecoupled fisheries system","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol23/iss1/art35/","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Carlson","given":"Andrew K."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"William W."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Jianguo"},{"family":"Orlic","given":"Ivan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hunting of small game is also increasingly becoming a driver of de-intensification of farming in ways which improve the food chains of the hunted species and many other species, notably pollinators ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zWNRxBhc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Brewin, 2020; Ewald, 2012; Sotherton, 1991)","plainCitation":"(J. Brewin, 2020; Ewald, 2012; Sotherton, 1991)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33376,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9SII7896"],"itemData":{"id":33376,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"page: 373–397","title":"Conservation headlands: a practical combination of intensive cereal farming and conservation. The Ecology of Temperate Cereal Fields","author":[{"family":"Sotherton","given":"N.W."}],"editor":[{"family":"Firbank","given":"L.G."},{"family":"Carter","given":"N."},{"family":"JF","given":"Darbyshire"},{"family":"Potts","given":"G.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}},{"id":33259,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AIP3E6BB"],"itemData":{"id":33259,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Animal Biodiversity and conservation","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"363–369","title":"Restoration of a wild grey partridge shoot: a major development in the Sussex study, UK","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Ewald","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Potts","given":"G.R."},{"family":"Aebischer","given":"N.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":33283,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9GTLR5JM"],"itemData":{"id":33283,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"The Game and Wildlife Conservation Trust, Fordingbridge","title":"Farming with nature–promoting biodiversity across Europe through partridge conservation","author":[{"family":"Brewin","given":"J."},{"family":"Buner","given":"F."},{"family":"Ewald","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, some complex trade-offs and unintended consequences of certification were also reported ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zFqvFBkP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tayleur, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Tayleur, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33349,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GP4EZRIW"],"itemData":{"id":33349,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"610–618","title":"Global coverage of agricultural sustainability standards, and their role in conserving biodiversity","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Tayleur","given":"C."},{"family":"Balmford","given":"A."},{"family":"Buchanan","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S.H."},{"family":"Ducharme","given":"H."},{"family":"Green","given":"R.E."},{"family":"Phalan","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Despite being considered as inefficient and ideologically driven ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6xNfBlKi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Trewavas, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Trewavas, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33356,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WAI44RVM"],"itemData":{"id":33356,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"6827","language":"et","page":"409","title":"Urban myths of organic farming","volume":"410","author":[{"family":"Trewavas","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, trends of organic farming, the extent of organically farmed land and the market size of organic foods has been growing recently worldwide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SItkLbhg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Willer & Lernoud, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Willer & Lernoud, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33364,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PQL4KUY3"],"itemData":{"id":33364,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Statistics and emerging trends 2017","language":"en","page":"1–336","publisher":"Research Institute of Organic Agriculture FiBL and IFOAM-Organics International","title":"The world of organic agriculture","author":[{"family":"Willer","given":"H."},{"family":"Lernoud","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although organic farming systems produce lower yields compared to the conventional agriculture, they are more profitable and environmentally friendly and deliver equally or more nutritious foods that contain less (or no) pesticide residues, compared with conventional farming (Regnold & Wachter, 2016). On average organic farming increased species richness by about 30%ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eG8HWSnB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tuck, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Tuck, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33357,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YG2ERJK9"],"itemData":{"id":33357,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of applied ecology","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"746–755","title":"Land?use intensity and the effects of organic farming on biodiversity: a hierarchical meta?analysis","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Tuck","given":"S.L."},{"family":"Winqvist","given":"C."},{"family":"Mota","given":"F."},{"family":"Ahnström","given":"J."},{"family":"Turnbull","given":"L.A."},{"family":"Bengtsson","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Agroforestry and small-scale farms (<2ha) also play a crucial role in maintaining the genetic diversity of managed species and agrobiodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zA8cItij","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2018b; Ricciardi, 2018)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2018b; Ricciardi, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33404,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GCDMGMXW"],"itemData":{"id":33404,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Food Security","language":"en","page":"64–72","title":"How much of the world’s food do smallholders produce?","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Ricciardi","given":"V."},{"family":"Ramankutty","given":"N."},{"family":"Mehrabi","given":"Z."},{"family":"Jarvis","given":"L."},{"family":"Chookolingo","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":86,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KXQGTCY8"],"itemData":{"id":86,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-09-6","number-of-pages":"744","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"The IPBES assessment report on Land Degradation and Restoration","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3237392","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"Montanarella","given":"L"},{"family":"Scholes","given":"R"},{"family":"Brainich","given":"A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Agroforestry has the potential for providing habitats outside formally protected land, connecting nature reserves and alleviating resource-use pressure on conservation areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"F9lCumGO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bhagwat, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Bhagwat, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33281,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RBK5TRLT"],"itemData":{"id":33281,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in ecology & evolution","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"261–267","title":"Agroforestry: a refuge for tropical biodiversity?","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Bhagwat","given":"S.A."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K.J."},{"family":"Birks","given":"H.J.B."},{"family":"Whittaker","given":"R.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Agroforestry also enhances functional biodiversity, carbon sequestration, soil fertility, drought resistance, as well as weed and biological pest control ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eFfLvyJR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tscharntke, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Tscharntke, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C2EGG7J5"],"itemData":{"id":33359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"On the Possibility of Life in Capitalist Ruins","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Princeton\\npublisher: Princeton University Press","page":"619–629 ,","title":"Multifunctional shade?tree management in tropical agroforestry landscapes–a review","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Tscharntke","given":"T."},{"family":"Clough","given":"Y."},{"family":"Bhagwat","given":"S.A."},{"family":"Buchori","given":"D."},{"family":"Faust","given":"H."},{"family":"Hertel","given":"D."},{"family":"Scherber","given":"C."},{"family":"L","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the species selection is important; agroforestry can promote undesirable species including invasive species if proper measures were not implemented ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vXSrEhSD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Udawatta, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Udawatta, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33361,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QQIPWN5K"],"itemData":{"id":33361,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su11102879","language":"en","page":"2879","title":"Agroforestry and Biodiversity","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Udawatta","given":"R.P."},{"family":"Rankoth","given":"L."},{"family":"Jose","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Traditional ecological knowledge influences the decision for preserving biodiversity in agroforestry systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yszYsVtw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vallejo-Ramos, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Vallejo-Ramos, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33303,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZYH5TZAJ"],"itemData":{"id":33303,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"31","title":"TEK and biodiversity management in agroforestry systems of different socio-ecological contexts of the Tehuacan Valley","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Vallejo-Ramos","given":"M."},{"family":"Moreno-Calles","given":"A.I."},{"family":"Casas","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the direct evidence of how agroforestry affects sustainable and unsustainable use is lacking. Similarly, although cultivation of medicinal plants is widely considered as a means for relieving harvesting pressure on wild populations by fulfilling demand for large volume production of plant-based drugs and herbal remedies, a clear analysis of their contribution remains still unclear ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"J6w70T1U","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. L. Chen, 2016; Schippmann, 2002)","plainCitation":"(S. L. Chen, 2016; Schippmann, 2002)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33287,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7VEBHCFR"],"itemData":{"id":33287,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Biodiversity and the Ecosystem Approach in Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","page":"142-167","title":"Impact of Cultivation and Gathering of Medicinal Plants on Biodiversity: Global Trends and Issues","author":[{"family":"Schippmann","given":"Uwe"},{"family":"Leaman","given":"Danna"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":34085,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/23XMD24P"],"itemData":{"id":34085,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Chinese medicine","issue":"1","language":"fr","page":"1–10","title":"Conservation and sustainable use of medicinal plants: problems, progress, and prospects","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Chen","given":"S.L."},{"family":"Yu","given":"H."},{"family":"Luo","given":"H.M."},{"family":"Wu","given":"Q."},{"family":"Li","given":"C.F."},{"family":"Steinmetz","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      
4.2.1.5.4. Change in forest areas and impacts on biodiversity and sustainable use
Forested ecosystems support a large number of terrestrial biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vxVyRSWf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pimm, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Pimm, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33394,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MP9CZWCD"],"itemData":{"id":33394,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"6187","language":"en","page":"1246752","title":"The biodiversity of species and their rates of extinction, distribution, and protection","volume":"344","author":[{"family":"Pimm","given":"S.L."},{"family":"Jenkins","given":"C.N."},{"family":"Abell","given":"R."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"T.M."},{"family":"Gittleman","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Joppa","given":"L.N."},{"family":"Sexton","given":"J.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. About 25% (40 million km2) of the earth’s terrestrial surface is covered by forests of which as much as 82% is now degraded ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ImQoPUQq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(MacDicken, 2016; Watson, 2018)","plainCitation":"(MacDicken, 2016; Watson, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33346,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KV5794CC"],"itemData":{"id":33346,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Global forest resources assessment 2015: how are the world's forests changing?","author":[{"family":"MacDicken","given":"K."},{"family":"Jonsson","given":"Ö."},{"family":"Piña","given":"L."},{"family":"Maulo","given":"S."},{"family":"Contessa","given":"V."},{"family":"Adikari","given":"Y."},{"family":"D’Annunzio","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33294,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W4LHZQQF"],"itemData":{"id":33294,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"In: Chamberlain, James L.; Emery, Marla R.; Patel-Weynand, Toral, eds. 2018. Assessment of nontimber forest products in the United States under changing conditions. Gen. Tech. Rep. SRS–232. Asheville, NC: U.S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service, Southern Research Station","language":"en","page":"102-117","source":"www.fs.usda.gov","title":"Social dimensions of nontimber forest products","URL":"https://www.fs.usda.gov/treesearch/pubs/57299","volume":"2018","author":[{"family":"Watson","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Christian","given":"Colmore S."},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R."},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wilmsen","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Globally tree cover has increased by 2.24 million km2 (7.1% relative to the 1982 level) during the period 1982-2016 as a result of a net loss in the tropics being outweighed by a net gain in the extratropics ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wjzlBIKe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Song, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Song, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33377,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NZXUJ22G"],"itemData":{"id":33377,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7720","language":"en","page":"639","title":"Global land change from 1982 to 2016","volume":"560","author":[{"family":"Song","given":"X.P."},{"family":"Hansen","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Stehman","given":"S.V."},{"family":"Potapov","given":"P.V."},{"family":"Tyukavina","given":"A."},{"family":"Vermote","given":"E.F."},{"family":"Townshend","given":"J.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The deforestation rates of tropical dense forests continue to be high 74,400 km2/ year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3bHEIkDH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hansen, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Hansen, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33241,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AGSA4T6C"],"itemData":{"id":33241,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"6160","language":"en","page":"850–853","title":"High-resolution global maps of 21st-century forest cover change","volume":"342","author":[{"family":"Hansen","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Potapov","given":"P.V."},{"family":"Moore","given":"R."},{"family":"Hancher","given":"M."},{"family":"Turubanova","given":"S.A."},{"family":"Tyukavina","given":"A."},{"family":"Thau","given":"D."},{"family":"Stehman","given":"S.V."},{"family":"Goetz","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Loveland","given":"T.R."},{"family":"Kommareddy","given":"A."},{"family":"Egorov","given":"A."},{"family":"Chini","given":"L."},{"family":"Justice","given":"C.O."},{"family":"Townshend","given":"J.R.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, about 129 million ha of global forest have been lost since 1990 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rbkTkkWM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2015)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33286,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DEN3PTYZ"],"itemData":{"id":33286,"type":"book","abstract":"The Voluntary Guidelines for Securing Sustainable Small-Scale Fisheries in the Context of Food Security and Poverty Eradication (SSF Guidelines) represent the first ever internationl instrument dedicated to small-scale fisheries. They represent a global consensus on principles and guidance for small-scale fisheries governance and development. They were developed for small-scale fisheries in close collaboration with representatives of small-scale fisheries organizations in a participatory process between 2011-13, involving over 4000 stakeholders; facilitated by FAO, based on a mandate by COFI. They are directed at all those involved in the sector and intend to guide and encourage governments, fishing communities and other stakeholders to work together and ensure secure and sustainable small-scale fisheries for the benefit of small-scale fishers, fish workers and their communities as well as for society at large. They complement existing internatinoal instruments, such as the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries, the VG Tenure and the Right to Food Guidelines. Underpinned by a human rights approach, the SSF Guidelines represent a critical instrument to empower small-scale fishing communities - including vulnerable and marginalized groups - to participate in decision-making processes, and to assume responsibilities for sustainable use of fishery resources. The SSF Guidelines are already referred to in a number of ongoing policy processes (Committee on Global Food Security: Principles for responsible investment in agriculture and food systems 41st CFS recommendations; NEPAD's policy framework and reform strategy for fisheries and aquaculture in Africa; Western Central Atlantic Fishery Commission (WECAFC), Resolution WECAFC/15/2014/8)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-108704-6","language":"eng","number-of-pages":"18","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"K10plus ISBN","title":"Voluntary guidelines for securing sustainable small-scale fisheries in the context of food security and poverty eradication","editor":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the intact forest landscapes, which are critical for stabilizing terrestrial carbon sequestration and storage, harboring biodiversity, water provision, indigenous culture and the maintenance of human health, shrunk globally by 7.2% from 2000–2013 due to industrial logging, agricultural expansion, fire, and mining/resource extraction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ux8hkyAL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Potapov, 2017; Watson, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Potapov, 2017; Watson, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33289,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3DS7YN3E"],"itemData":{"id":33289,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.1600821","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci. Adv.","language":"en","page":"e1600821","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The last frontiers of wilderness: Tracking loss of intact forest landscapes from 2000 to 2013","title-short":"The last frontiers of wilderness","URL":"https://advances.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/sciadv.1600821","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Potapov","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Hansen","given":"Matthew C."},{"family":"Laestadius","given":"Lars"},{"family":"Turubanova","given":"Svetlana"},{"family":"Yaroshenko","given":"Alexey"},{"family":"Thies","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Wynet"},{"family":"Zhuravleva","given":"Ilona"},{"family":"Komarova","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Minnemeyer","given":"Susan"},{"family":"Esipova","given":"Elena"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1]]}}},{"id":33294,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W4LHZQQF"],"itemData":{"id":33294,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"In: Chamberlain, James L.; Emery, Marla R.; Patel-Weynand, Toral, eds. 2018. Assessment of nontimber forest products in the United States under changing conditions. Gen. Tech. Rep. SRS–232. Asheville, NC: U.S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service, Southern Research Station","language":"en","page":"102-117","source":"www.fs.usda.gov","title":"Social dimensions of nontimber forest products","URL":"https://www.fs.usda.gov/treesearch/pubs/57299","volume":"2018","author":[{"family":"Watson","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Christian","given":"Colmore S."},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R."},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wilmsen","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Deforestation substantially increased the odds of a species being listed as threatened, undergoing recent upgrading to a higher threat category and exhibiting declining populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BJW2nD2Z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Betts, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Betts, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33131,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VNTPK5VD"],"itemData":{"id":33131,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7664","language":"en","page":"441","title":"Global forest loss disproportionately erodes biodiversity in intact landscapes","volume":"547","author":[{"family":"Betts","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"C."},{"family":"Ripple","given":"W.J."},{"family":"Phalan","given":"B."},{"family":"Millers","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Duarte","given":"A."},{"family":"Levi","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Forest or habitat loss also coincide with overhunting, wildfires, selective logging, biological invasions, and other stressors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1qE0fQ8c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barlow, 2016; Betts, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Barlow, 2016; Betts, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26035,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZZPBEE3","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D6WRKPHD"],"itemData":{"id":26035,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7610","language":"en","page":"144–147","title":"Anthropogenic disturbance in tropical forests can double biodiversity loss from deforestation","volume":"535","author":[{"family":"Barlow","given":"J."},{"family":"Lennox","given":"G.D."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"J."},{"family":"Berenguer","given":"E."},{"family":"Lees","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Mac Nally","given":"R."},{"family":"Thomson","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Barros Ferraz","given":"S.F."},{"family":"Louzada","given":"J."},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"V.H.F."},{"family":"Parry","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33131,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VNTPK5VD"],"itemData":{"id":33131,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7664","language":"en","page":"441","title":"Global forest loss disproportionately erodes biodiversity in intact landscapes","volume":"547","author":[{"family":"Betts","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"C."},{"family":"Ripple","given":"W.J."},{"family":"Phalan","given":"B."},{"family":"Millers","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Duarte","given":"A."},{"family":"Levi","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      These risks were disproportionately high in relatively intact landscapes; even minimal deforestation has had severe consequences for vertebrate biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"r9zp5ID8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Betts, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Betts, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33131,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VNTPK5VD"],"itemData":{"id":33131,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7664","language":"en","page":"441","title":"Global forest loss disproportionately erodes biodiversity in intact landscapes","volume":"547","author":[{"family":"Betts","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"C."},{"family":"Ripple","given":"W.J."},{"family":"Phalan","given":"B."},{"family":"Millers","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Duarte","given":"A."},{"family":"Levi","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In high-risk hotspot such as Amazon, Borneo and Congo Basin, 121–219 species will become threatened under current rates of forest loss over the next 30 years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qNqbAk0Q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Betts, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Betts, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33131,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VNTPK5VD"],"itemData":{"id":33131,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7664","language":"en","page":"441","title":"Global forest loss disproportionately erodes biodiversity in intact landscapes","volume":"547","author":[{"family":"Betts","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"C."},{"family":"Ripple","given":"W.J."},{"family":"Phalan","given":"B."},{"family":"Millers","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Duarte","given":"A."},{"family":"Levi","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Likewise, there is a positive relationship between the global extinction risk of forest-dependent birds and the global intact forest landscapes although only 22.5% of global hotspots of range?rarity for forest?dependent birds is found within intact forests (Donald, 2018). Compared to primary humid tropical forest extent for the year 2001, primary forest loss from 2002–2014 has been the highest for rainforest countries of Brazil, Democratic Republic of the Congo, and Indonesia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hMRwE8Cg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Turubanova, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Turubanova, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33362,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F9Q45DCV"],"itemData":{"id":33362,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Research Letters","issue":"7","language":"en","page":"074028","title":"Ongoing primary forest loss in Brazil, Democratic Republic of the Congo, and Indonesia","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Turubanova","given":"S."},{"family":"Potapov","given":"P.V."},{"family":"Tyukavina","given":"A."},{"family":"Hansen","given":"M.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
A study predicted substantial declines in suitable habitat for approximately 17000 species out of 19400 species of amphibians, birds and mammals studied due to land-use change alone ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TZlZL80s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Powers & Jetz, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Powers & Jetz, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33397,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E3DZ83WC"],"itemData":{"id":33397,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Climate Change","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"323","title":"Global habitat loss and extinction risk of terrestrial vertebrates under future land-use-change scenarios","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Powers","given":"R.P."},{"family":"Jetz","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The decline is disproportionately higher in South American, Southeast Asian and African countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pT99Tp4d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Powers & Jetz, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Powers & Jetz, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33397,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E3DZ83WC"],"itemData":{"id":33397,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Climate Change","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"323","title":"Global habitat loss and extinction risk of terrestrial vertebrates under future land-use-change scenarios","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Powers","given":"R.P."},{"family":"Jetz","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Deforestation and forest fragmentation negatively affect the abundance and availability of non-timber forest products and decrease the rate of hunting success. Fragmentation has negative impacts on genetic diversity of birds ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"p91QB5c8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Athrey, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Athrey, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33280,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CA9MNVTY"],"itemData":{"id":33280,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Evolutionary applications","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"540–552","title":"Birds in space and time: Genetic changes accompanying anthropogenic habitat fragmentation in the endangered black?capped vireo (Vireo atricapilla","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Athrey","given":"G."},{"family":"Barr","given":"K.R."},{"family":"Lance","given":"R.F."},{"family":"Leberg","given":"P.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and fish population ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uDfBrzw3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pavlova, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Pavlova, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33392,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BMWVDK7H"],"itemData":{"id":33392,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Evolutionary Applications","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"531–550","title":"Severe consequences of habitat fragmentation on genetic diversity of an endangered Australian freshwater fish: A call for assisted gene flow","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Pavlova","given":"A."},{"family":"Beheregaray","given":"L.B."},{"family":"Coleman","given":"R."},{"family":"Gilligan","given":"D."},{"family":"Harrisson","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Ingram","given":"B.A."},{"family":"Sunnucks","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and population fitness threatening endangered species in land ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DeuLu0ow","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Athrey, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Athrey, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33280,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CA9MNVTY"],"itemData":{"id":33280,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Evolutionary applications","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"540–552","title":"Birds in space and time: Genetic changes accompanying anthropogenic habitat fragmentation in the endangered black?capped vireo (Vireo atricapilla","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Athrey","given":"G."},{"family":"Barr","given":"K.R."},{"family":"Lance","given":"R.F."},{"family":"Leberg","given":"P.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} whereas in aquatic ecosystem, alteration of habitat such as dam building is associated with change in genetic composition ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MHnl07YG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. Q. Thompson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(T. Q. Thompson, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33352,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/737CV2EQ"],"itemData":{"id":33352,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"177–186","title":"Anthropogenic habitat alteration leads to rapid loss of adaptive variation and restoration potential in wild salmon populations","volume":"116","author":[{"family":"Thompson","given":"T.Q."},{"family":"Bellinger","given":"M.R."},{"family":"O’Rourke","given":"S.M."},{"family":"Prince","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Stevenson","given":"A.E."},{"family":"Rodrigues","given":"A.T."},{"family":"Miller","given":"M.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Deforestation restricts the availability of non-timber forest products hence affects negatively the local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SjxIq2WI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. Schmidt, 2020)","plainCitation":"(L. Schmidt, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33386,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PPTFIY6P"],"itemData":{"id":33386,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"NJAS-Wageningen Journal of Life Sciences","language":"en","page":"100325","title":"The impact of deforestation on collection and domestication of Jernang (Daemonorops spp.) and other NTFPs in southern Sumatra, Indonesia","volume":"92","author":[{"family":"Schmidt","given":"L."},{"family":"Widianingsih","given":"N.N."},{"family":"Kaad","given":"A.P."},{"family":"Theilade","given":"I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Commercial deforestation negatively affects the rural people who depend on wild animals like snail, wild meat, wild honey and wild and cultivated vegetables for subsistence ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ev5c1UqA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Appiah, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Appiah, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33322,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MUYRSMHR"],"itemData":{"id":33322,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment, Development and Sustainability","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"471–487","title":"Dependence on forest resources and tropical deforestation in Ghana","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Appiah","given":"M."},{"family":"Blay","given":"D."},{"family":"Damnyag","given":"L."},{"family":"Dwomoh","given":"F.K."},{"family":"Pappinen","given":"A."},{"family":"Luukkanen","given":"O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A fragmented or declining environmental quality is likely to support lower populations and produce lower yields of some plants, algae and fungi and simultaneously affect what land use and livelihood options might be viable or not. Deforestation is also associated with the declines in consumption and diversity of nutritious fruits and wild foods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1bX79hdd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ickowitz, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Ickowitz, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33231,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QS567QQS"],"itemData":{"id":33231,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Glob. Environ. Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2013.12.001","language":"en","page":"287–294","title":"Dietary quality and tree cover in Africa","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Ickowitz","given":"A."},{"family":"Powell","given":"B."},{"family":"Salim","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Sunderland","given":"T.C.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indigenous local community reported a decline in hunting success rates, fruit harvests after logging ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c3bigccw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Araujo Lima Constantino, 2016; Menton, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Araujo Lima Constantino, 2016; Menton, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33321,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XN4H5FDR"],"itemData":{"id":33321,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"2","language":"en","title":"Deforestation and hunting effects on wildlife across Amazonian indigenous lands","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Araujo Lima Constantino","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33218,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NK6BRWAD"],"itemData":{"id":33218,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"1","language":"en","title":"Company–community timber harvesting contracts in Amazonian settlements: impacts on livelihoods and NTFP harvests","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Menton","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Merry","given":"F.D."},{"family":"Lawrence","given":"A."},{"family":"Brown","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Deforestation causes a reduction in the generation of hunting products particularly the diversity of bushmeat that in turn reduction of household dietary intake and cash income ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xVQ2wvLx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gillet, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Gillet, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33236,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P4LF6PGF"],"itemData":{"id":33236,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"106","title":"What are the impacts of deforestation on the harvest of non-timber forest products in Central Africa?","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Gillet","given":"P."},{"family":"Vermeulen","given":"C."},{"family":"Doucet","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Codina","given":"E."},{"family":"Lehnebach","given":"C."},{"family":"Feintrenie","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, bushmeat hunting is more widespread in fragmented forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QBfWFejK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Torres, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Torres, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33360,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q5UVC7QF"],"itemData":{"id":33360,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"315–323","title":"Landscape correlates of bushmeat consumption and hunting in a post-frontier Amazonian region","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Torres","given":"P.C."},{"family":"Morsello","given":"C."},{"family":"Parry","given":"L."},{"family":"Barlow","given":"J."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"J."},{"family":"Gardner","given":"T."},{"family":"Pardini","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Infrastructure development makes remote forested areas accessible exacerbating the hunting and trapping pressure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ImrZ8NqV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barlow, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Barlow, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26035,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZZPBEE3","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D6WRKPHD"],"itemData":{"id":26035,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7610","language":"en","page":"144–147","title":"Anthropogenic disturbance in tropical forests can double biodiversity loss from deforestation","volume":"535","author":[{"family":"Barlow","given":"J."},{"family":"Lennox","given":"G.D."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"J."},{"family":"Berenguer","given":"E."},{"family":"Lees","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Mac Nally","given":"R."},{"family":"Thomson","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Barros Ferraz","given":"S.F."},{"family":"Louzada","given":"J."},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"V.H.F."},{"family":"Parry","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Additionally, hunting practice has evolved to meet urban market demand after urbanization and the opening of the roads ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jkdHPn5k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ickowitz, 2013; Torres, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Ickowitz, 2013; Torres, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33231,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QS567QQS"],"itemData":{"id":33231,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Glob. Environ. Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2013.12.001","language":"en","page":"287–294","title":"Dietary quality and tree cover in Africa","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Ickowitz","given":"A."},{"family":"Powell","given":"B."},{"family":"Salim","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Sunderland","given":"T.C.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33360,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q5UVC7QF"],"itemData":{"id":33360,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"315–323","title":"Landscape correlates of bushmeat consumption and hunting in a post-frontier Amazonian region","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Torres","given":"P.C."},{"family":"Morsello","given":"C."},{"family":"Parry","given":"L."},{"family":"Barlow","given":"J."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"J."},{"family":"Gardner","given":"T."},{"family":"Pardini","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Forest expansion continues to occur in most industrialized countries, on lands abandoned by farming and animal husbandry and areas that continue to mature on land that was deforested in the past century but have not been converted to a different land use since then ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UuGehyDn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Keenan, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Keenan, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33250,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I4TNYTQP"],"itemData":{"id":33250,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2015.06.014","language":"en","page":"9–20","title":"Dynamics of global forest area: Results from the FAO Global Forest Resources Assessment 2015.Forest","volume":"352","author":[{"family":"Keenan","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Reams","given":"G.A."},{"family":"Achard","given":"F."},{"family":"De Freitas","given":"J.V."},{"family":"Grainger","given":"A."},{"family":"Lindquist","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Planted forests account for 25-100% of gains and increasingly substitute for natural forests, particularly in Africa, Southeast Asia, and Europe. The global rate of planted-forest expansion since 1990 is close to a target of 2.4% per annum necessary to replace wood supplied from natural forests in the medium term, although the rate had declined to 1.5% since 2005 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yZUCOdOq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Sloan & Sayer, 2015)","plainCitation":"(S. Sloan & Sayer, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33341,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FHFQ8829"],"itemData":{"id":33341,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","ISSN":"0378-1127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"134-145","title":"Forest Resources Assessment of 2015 shows positive global trends but forest loss and degradation persist in poor tropical countries","volume":"352","author":[{"family":"Sloan","given":"Sean"},{"family":"Sayer","given":"Jeffrey A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although natural forests are usually more suitable as biodiversity habitat for a wider range of native forest species than plantation, plantation forests can provide valuable habitat even for some threatened and endangered species and may contribute to the conservation of biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2OQC9Se0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brockerhoff, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Brockerhoff, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33284,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQBJ4HTW"],"itemData":{"id":33284,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","issue":"5","language":"fr","page":"925–951","title":"Plantation forests and biodiversity: oxymoron or opportunity?","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Brockerhoff","given":"E.G."},{"family":"Jactel","given":"H."},{"family":"Parrotta","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Quine","given":"C.P."},{"family":"Sayer","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, large scale tree plantations also have negative impacts on biodiversity, quality and quantality of water and livelihoods particularly loss of or restriction to previous livelihoods, reduced access ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Wpd9IhSi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Malkam\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}ki, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Malkamäki, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9ESKMP4X"],"itemData":{"id":33225,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"90–103","title":"A systematic review of the socio-economic impacts of large-scale tree plantations, worldwide","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Malkamäki","given":"A."},{"family":"D’Amato","given":"D."},{"family":"Hogarth","given":"N.J."},{"family":"Kanninen","given":"M."},{"family":"Pirard","given":"R."},{"family":"Toppinen","given":"A."},{"family":"Zhou","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Furthermore, tree plantations, particularly monocultures displaced native forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yeybg2xe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hua, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hua, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33230,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8W9GS3WG"],"itemData":{"id":33230,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"113–124","title":"Tree plantations displacing native forests: The nature and drivers of apparent forest recovery on former croplands in Southwestern China from 2000 to 2015","volume":"222","author":[{"family":"Hua","given":"F."},{"family":"Wang","given":"L."},{"family":"Fisher","given":"B."},{"family":"Zheng","given":"X."},{"family":"Wang","given":"X."},{"family":"Douglas","given":"W.Y."},{"family":"Wilcove","given":"D.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
4.2.1.5.5. Change in rangelands and impacts on biodiversity and sustainable use
The rangeland condition affects biodiversity directly and indirectly because rangeland comprises high biodiversity values ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nhKdTBE2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. B. Harris 2010)","plainCitation":"(R. B. Harris 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33242,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YEDEQMM7"],"itemData":{"id":33242,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Arid Environments","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–12","title":"Rangeland degradation on the Qinghai-Tibetan plateau: a review of the evidence of its magnitude and causes","volume":"74","author":[{"family":"Harris","given":"R.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Rangelands are the most dominant land cover types on Earth, covering 25-45% of the land surface depending on how these lands are defined ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"h2DwyErW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Reid, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Reid, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33403,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BQX5ATNL"],"itemData":{"id":33403,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","language":"en","page":"217–242","title":"Dynamics and resilience of rangelands and pastoral peoples around the globe","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Reid","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Fernández-Giménez","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Galvin","given":"K.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Globally, 4,734 mammal species are less endangered in rangelands and wildlands, compared to croplands and urban areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"48fXnz06","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pekin & Pijanowski, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Pekin & Pijanowski, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33393,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G6RXMMGI"],"itemData":{"id":33393,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Diversity and Distributions","issue":"9","language":"en","page":"909–918","title":"Global land use intensity and the endangerment status of mammal species","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Pekin","given":"B.K."},{"family":"Pijanowski","given":"B.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Grasslands are among the ecosystems with the highest species richness in the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"k9u19KDf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. B. Wilson, 2012)","plainCitation":"(J. B. Wilson, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33367,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5STZ7YRG"],"itemData":{"id":33367,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of vegetation Science","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"796–802","title":"Plant species richness: the world records","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Wilson","given":"J.B."},{"family":"Peet","given":"R.K."},{"family":"Dengler","given":"J."},{"family":"Pärtel","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Globally both expansion of rangeland and intensification of rangelands are caused by the increasing demand for livestock production that depends on grazing systems requiring grazing lands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tsxqRW5u","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Godde, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Godde, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33234,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UWI9M26Q"],"itemData":{"id":33234,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global food security","language":"en","page":"93–105","title":"Grazing systems expansion and intensification: drivers, dynamics, and trade-offs","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Godde","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Garnett","given":"T."},{"family":"Thornton","given":"P.K."},{"family":"Ash","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Herrero","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This has led to unsustainable use exemplified by deforestation and land degradation. At a global scale, almost half (49%) of grassland ecosystems were degraded and nearly 5% of these grassland experiences strong to extreme levels of degradation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"64Jo7byn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gang, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Gang, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33301,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8474FV8T"],"itemData":{"id":33301,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Earth Sciences","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"4273–4282","title":"Quantitative assessment of the contributions of climate change and human activities on global grassland degradation","volume":"72","author":[{"family":"Gang","given":"C."},{"family":"Zhou","given":"W."},{"family":"Chen","given":"Y."},{"family":"Wang","given":"Z."},{"family":"Sun","given":"Z."},{"family":"Li","given":"J."},{"family":"Odeh","given":"I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The major causes of the rangeland degradations are climate change, overgrazing, land-use change (converting pastureland to cropland), and shrub encroachment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iKfQtEUU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. B. Harris, 2010; Knapp, 2008; Reid, 2014; Tiscornia, 2019; M. D. Walker, 2006)","plainCitation":"(R. B. Harris, 2010; Knapp, 2008; Reid, 2014; Tiscornia, 2019; M. D. Walker, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33242,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YEDEQMM7"],"itemData":{"id":33242,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Arid Environments","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–12","title":"Rangeland degradation on the Qinghai-Tibetan plateau: a review of the evidence of its magnitude and causes","volume":"74","author":[{"family":"Harris","given":"R.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":33344,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SC6HWJ8K"],"itemData":{"id":33344,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global change biology","ISSN":"1354-1013","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Global change biology","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"615-623","title":"Shrub encroachment in North American grasslands: shifts in growth form dominance rapidly alters control of ecosystem carbon inputs","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Knapp","given":"Alan K"},{"family":"Briggs","given":"John M"},{"family":"Collins","given":"Scott L"},{"family":"Archer","given":"Steven R"},{"family":"BRET?HARTE","given":"M SYNDONIA"},{"family":"Ewers","given":"Brent E"},{"family":"Peters","given":"Debra P"},{"family":"Young","given":"Donald R"},{"family":"Shaver","given":"Gaius R"},{"family":"Pendall","given":"Elise"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":33403,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BQX5ATNL"],"itemData":{"id":33403,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","language":"en","page":"217–242","title":"Dynamics and resilience of rangelands and pastoral peoples around the globe","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Reid","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Fernández-Giménez","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Galvin","given":"K.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":33354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4HNVE3JV"],"itemData":{"id":33354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agronomy","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"239","title":"Drivers, Process, and Consequences of Native Grassland Degradation: Insights from a Literature Review and a Survey in Río de la Plata Grasslands","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Tiscornia","given":"G."},{"family":"Jaurena","given":"M."},{"family":"Baethgen","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":33306,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FYNVIHWS"],"itemData":{"id":33306,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"1342–1346","title":"Plant community responses to experimental warming across the tundra biome","volume":"103","author":[{"family":"Walker","given":"M.D."},{"family":"Wahren","given":"C.H."},{"family":"Hollister","given":"R.D."},{"family":"Henry","given":"G.H."},{"family":"Ahlquist","given":"L.E."},{"family":"Alatalo","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Epstein","given":"H.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Overgrazing, that occurs when stocking rates exceed the carrying capacity of grassland particularly in developing countries cause the rangeland degradation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nvVWCMEY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bai, 2002; Fedrigo, 2018; J. Liu, 2013; Yao, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Bai, 2002; Fedrigo, 2018; J. Liu, 2013; Yao, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33325,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FJSZVUG6"],"itemData":{"id":33325,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ying yong sheng tai xue bao= The journal of applied ecology","issue":"7","language":"en","page":"823–826","title":"Analysis of formation causes of grassland degradation in Maduo County in the source region of Yellow River","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Bai","given":"W."},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Y."},{"family":"Xie","given":"G."},{"family":"Shen","given":"Z."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":33296,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5SYULKTV"],"itemData":{"id":33296,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","ISSN":"17083087","issue":"2","note":"type: Journal Article","title":"Framing Sustainability in a Telecoupled World","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/26269331","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Jianguo"},{"family":"Hull","given":"Vanessa"},{"family":"Batistella","given":"Mateus"},{"family":"DeFries","given":"Ruth"},{"family":"Dietz","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Fu","given":"Feng"},{"family":"Hertel","given":"Thomas W."},{"family":"Izaurralde","given":"R. Cesar"},{"family":"Lambin","given":"Eric F."},{"family":"Li","given":"Shuxin"},{"family":"Martinelli","given":"Luiz A."},{"family":"McConnell","given":"William J."},{"family":"Moran","given":"Emilio F."},{"family":"Naylor","given":"Rosamond"},{"family":"Ouyang","given":"Zhiyun"},{"family":"Polenske","given":"Karen R."},{"family":"Reenberg","given":"Anette"},{"family":"Miranda Rocha","given":"Gilberto","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Simmons","given":"Cynthia S."},{"family":"Verburg","given":"Peter H."},{"family":"Vitousek","given":"Peter M."},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Fusuo"},{"family":"Zhu","given":"Chunquan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33372,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SMYEBH7S"],"itemData":{"id":33372,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Catena","language":"en","page":"494–500","title":"Alpine grassland degradation in the Qilian Mountains, China—A case study in Damaying Grassland","volume":"137","author":[{"family":"Yao","given":"Z."},{"family":"Zhao","given":"C."},{"family":"Yang","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"W."},{"family":"Li","given":"Y."},{"family":"You","given":"J."},{"family":"Xiao","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33263,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CD82HMM6"],"itemData":{"id":33263,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Restoration ecology","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"677–685","title":"Temporary grazing exclusion promotes rapid recovery of species richness and productivity in a long?term overgrazed Campos grassland","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Fedrigo","given":"J.K."},{"family":"Ataide","given":"P.F."},{"family":"Filho","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"L.V."},{"family":"Jaurena","given":"M."},{"family":"Laca","given":"E.A."},{"family":"Nabinger","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In contrast, in developed countries (e.g., Europe), contraction of extensive grazing is allowing forest that was cleared historically for farming and grazing to re-establish itself ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1rNNgKxN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Navarro & Pereira, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Navarro & Pereira, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33292,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZRF7DNRH"],"itemData":{"id":33292,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Rewilding European Landscapes","page":"3–23","publisher":"Springer, Cham","title":"Rewilding abandoned landscapes in Europe","author":[{"family":"Navarro","given":"Laetitia M"},{"family":"Pereira","given":"Henrique M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Warming and the increased frequency of prolonged droughts are the major climatic changes that cause rangeland degradation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"p1lpr1Ua","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gang, 2014; Tiscornia, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Gang, 2014; Tiscornia, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4HNVE3JV"],"itemData":{"id":33354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agronomy","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"239","title":"Drivers, Process, and Consequences of Native Grassland Degradation: Insights from a Literature Review and a Survey in Río de la Plata Grasslands","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Tiscornia","given":"G."},{"family":"Jaurena","given":"M."},{"family":"Baethgen","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":33301,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8474FV8T"],"itemData":{"id":33301,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Earth Sciences","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"4273–4282","title":"Quantitative assessment of the contributions of climate change and human activities on global grassland degradation","volume":"72","author":[{"family":"Gang","given":"C."},{"family":"Zhou","given":"W."},{"family":"Chen","given":"Y."},{"family":"Wang","given":"Z."},{"family":"Sun","given":"Z."},{"family":"Li","given":"J."},{"family":"Odeh","given":"I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Rangeland degradation reduces various ecosystem services but particularly affect sustainable use of wild species as it has decreased capacity to provide forage for large herbivores including domestic livestock ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"N4T5gQcT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoppe, 2016; J. Liu, 2013; Paudel, 2010; Yao, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Hoppe, 2016; J. Liu, 2013; Paudel, 2010; Yao, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33296,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5SYULKTV"],"itemData":{"id":33296,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","ISSN":"17083087","issue":"2","note":"type: Journal Article","title":"Framing Sustainability in a Telecoupled World","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/26269331","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Jianguo"},{"family":"Hull","given":"Vanessa"},{"family":"Batistella","given":"Mateus"},{"family":"DeFries","given":"Ruth"},{"family":"Dietz","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Fu","given":"Feng"},{"family":"Hertel","given":"Thomas W."},{"family":"Izaurralde","given":"R. Cesar"},{"family":"Lambin","given":"Eric F."},{"family":"Li","given":"Shuxin"},{"family":"Martinelli","given":"Luiz A."},{"family":"McConnell","given":"William J."},{"family":"Moran","given":"Emilio F."},{"family":"Naylor","given":"Rosamond"},{"family":"Ouyang","given":"Zhiyun"},{"family":"Polenske","given":"Karen R."},{"family":"Reenberg","given":"Anette"},{"family":"Miranda Rocha","given":"Gilberto","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Simmons","given":"Cynthia S."},{"family":"Verburg","given":"Peter H."},{"family":"Vitousek","given":"Peter M."},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Fusuo"},{"family":"Zhu","given":"Chunquan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33372,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SMYEBH7S"],"itemData":{"id":33372,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Catena","language":"en","page":"494–500","title":"Alpine grassland degradation in the Qilian Mountains, China—A case study in Damaying Grassland","volume":"137","author":[{"family":"Yao","given":"Z."},{"family":"Zhao","given":"C."},{"family":"Yang","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"W."},{"family":"Li","given":"Y."},{"family":"You","given":"J."},{"family":"Xiao","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32665,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/85SLPXGQ"],"itemData":{"id":32665,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Forests for People: Community Rights and Forest Tenure Reform, Earthscan","event-place":"London, UK","lang