":[{"family":"Li","given":"X."},{"family":"Weng","given":"J.K."},{"family":"Chapple","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":33390,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SX7KSX8Q"],"itemData":{"id":33390,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"FAO","language":"en","page":"171–219","title":"The south american campos ecosystem","author":[{"family":"Pallarés","given":"O.R."},{"family":"Berretta","given":"E.J."},{"family":"Maraschin","given":"G.E."},{"family":"Suttie","given":"J."},{"family":"Reynolds","given":"S.G."},{"family":"Batello","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Rangeland management systems are very diverse, ranging from nomadic pastoral activities in sub-Saharan native savannas to sedentary Dutch dairy farming to industrial-scale farming in North America and Australia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"60iM8gvw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Godde, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Godde, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33234,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UWI9M26Q"],"itemData":{"id":33234,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global food security","language":"en","page":"93–105","title":"Grazing systems expansion and intensification: drivers, dynamics, and trade-offs","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Godde","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Garnett","given":"T."},{"family":"Thornton","given":"P.K."},{"family":"Ash","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Herrero","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Most global rangelands are still common pool resources (except some privately owned in North America and Australia) and are used by indigenous pastoralists/ranchers, agropastoralists, hunters, conservationists, recreationists, and others ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7bAQ7fL3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Reid, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Reid, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33403,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BQX5ATNL"],"itemData":{"id":33403,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","language":"en","page":"217–242","title":"Dynamics and resilience of rangelands and pastoral peoples around the globe","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Reid","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Fernández-Giménez","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Galvin","given":"K.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There have been shifts in pastoral land-use practices causing diverse changes in rangelands themselves from contraction, loss, and fragmentation to expansion and reaggregation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Brzt8kLX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Reid, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Reid, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33403,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BQX5ATNL"],"itemData":{"id":33403,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","language":"en","page":"217–242","title":"Dynamics and resilience of rangelands and pastoral peoples around the globe","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Reid","given":"R.S."},{"family":"Fernández-Giménez","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Galvin","given":"K.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. All of these changes lead to either income diversification at the household level ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Blf7Kxto","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Homewood, 2009; Reid, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Homewood, 2009; Reid, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33229,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ACDPYJNJ"],"itemData":{"id":33229,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Springer Science & Business Media","title":"Staying Maasai?: Livelihoods, conservation and development in East African rangelands","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Homewood","given":"K."},{"family":"Kristjanson","given":"P."},{"family":"Trench","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":33402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6X3N9KIB"],"itemData":{"id":33402,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Univ of California Press","title":"Savannas of our birth: people, wildlife, and change in East Africa","author":[{"family":"Reid","given":"R.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or intensification of rangelands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rena93Gi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(BurnSilver, 2009; Nkedianye, 2009)","plainCitation":"(BurnSilver, 2009; Nkedianye, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33277,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KZQYEZLE"],"itemData":{"id":33277,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Staying Maasai?","event-place":"New York, NY","language":"en","page":"161–207","publisher":"Springer","publisher-place":"New York, NY","title":"Pathways of continuity and change: Maasai livelihoods in Amboseli, Kajiado District, Kenya","author":[{"family":"BurnSilver","given":"S.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":33412,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NKU562JM"],"itemData":{"id":33412,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Staying Maasai?","event-place":"New York, NY","language":"nl","page":"115–149","publisher":"Springer","publisher-place":"New York, NY","title":"Assessing returns to land and changing livelihood strategies in Kitengela","author":[{"family":"Nkedianye","given":"D."},{"family":"Radeny","given":"M."},{"family":"Kristjanson","given":"P."},{"family":"Herrero","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Rangeland changes causes species decline. In North American grasslands bird populations have experienced drastic declines over the past half century particularly due to the land-use change and a rapid loss of habitat ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Yn07CQZ5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Correll, 2019; Grand, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Correll, 2019; Grand, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33237,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/39IKN6L9"],"itemData":{"id":33237,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Science and Practice","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"20","title":"The future of North American grassland birds: Incorporating persistent and emergent threats into full annual cycle conservation priorities","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Grand","given":"J."},{"family":"Wilsey","given":"C."},{"family":"Wu","given":"J.X."},{"family":"Michel","given":"N.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":33276,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F8BKA9FR"],"itemData":{"id":33276,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosphere","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"02523","title":"Quantifying specialist avifaunal decline in grassland birds of the Northern Great Plains","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Correll","given":"M.D."},{"family":"Strasser","given":"E.H."},{"family":"Green","given":"A.W."},{"family":"Panjabi","given":"A.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Conversion of grasslands into other land uses is the major threat for grassland birds in Brazil ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JwI8Wuw3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jacoboski, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Jacoboski, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33244,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CYAAEA7T"],"itemData":{"id":33244,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Perspectives in Ecology and Conservation","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"109–114","title":"Bird-grassland associations in protected and non-protected areas in southern Brazil","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Jacoboski","given":"L.I."},{"family":"Paulsen","given":"R.K."},{"family":"Hartz","given":"S.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Future climate change added vulnerability to these grassland birds in North America ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tpAEhvZI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wilsey, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Wilsey, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33365,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HKCNNQ9Y"],"itemData":{"id":33365,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Science and Practice","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"21","title":"Climate policy action needed to reduce vulnerability of conservation?reliant grassland birds in North America","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Wilsey","given":"C."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"L."},{"family":"Bateman","given":"B."},{"family":"Jensen","given":"C."},{"family":"Michel","given":"N."},{"family":"Panjabi","given":"A."},{"family":"Langham","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      There is some evidence that indigenous pastoralist communities are displaced to create protected areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OaoH89Y8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Reid, 2012; Tang & Gavin, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Reid, 2012; Tang & Gavin, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33350,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B7IJJ4QS"],"itemData":{"id":33350,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society and Natural Resources","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"193–206","title":"Traditional ecological knowledge informing resource management: Saxoul conservation in inner Mongolia, China","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Tang","given":"R."},{"family":"Gavin","given":"M.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":33402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6X3N9KIB"],"itemData":{"id":33402,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Univ of California Press","title":"Savannas of our birth: people, wildlife, and change in East Africa","author":[{"family":"Reid","given":"R.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and to prohibit traditional management practices such as burning grassland to produce a new flush of nutrient rich grass and remove old moribund grass material ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"crCLOS5M","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez-Gim\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}nez & Estaque, 2012; M. U. Johansson, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Fernández-Giménez & Estaque, 2012; M. U. Johansson, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33347,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CGY6VADA"],"itemData":{"id":33347,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"287–300","title":"Pyrenean pastoralists’ ecological knowledge: documentation and application to natural resource management and adaptation","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Fernández-Giménez","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Estaque","given":"F.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":33245,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JHYP5AUW"],"itemData":{"id":33245,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"3","language":"en","title":"Tending for cattle: traditional fire management in Ethiopian montane heathlands","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Johansson","given":"M.U."},{"family":"Fetene","given":"M."},{"family":"Malmer","given":"A."},{"family":"Granström","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The forced eviction of pastoralist communities to establish protected areas sometimes has led to an increase in wild species poaching ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qmdg9bA8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Reid, 2012; Scharf, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Reid, 2012; Scharf, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33402,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6X3N9KIB"],"itemData":{"id":33402,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Univ of California Press","title":"Savannas of our birth: people, wildlife, and change in East Africa","author":[{"family":"Reid","given":"R.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":33387,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2D9GN9L6"],"itemData":{"id":33387,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Wild Rangelands–Conserving Wildlife While Maintaining Livestock in Semi-Arid Ecosystems","event-place":"Wiley-Blackwell, Oxford, UK","language":"en","page":"312–339","publisher-place":"Wiley-Blackwell, Oxford, UK","title":"Herders and hunters in a transitional economy: The challenge of wildlife and rangeland management in post-socialist Mongolia","author":[{"family":"Scharf","given":"K."},{"family":"Fernández-Giménez","given":"M."},{"family":"Batbuyan","given":"B."},{"family":"Enkhbold","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      
4.2.1.5.6. Habitat conversion 
The conversion of natural ecosystems into anthropogenic ecosystems (such as farmlands, pastures, and plantations) is the most important direct driver of change in terrestrial ecosystems. This is driven indirectly by changing social dynamics–notably the drive for economic development. This significant expansion of agricultural land has been the critical factor that has enabled the human population to continue growing. While an estimated 1 billion people remain malnourished, the supply of food per capita has continued to increase steadily, a result largely of improved technology as well as intensification of cropland. Poor governance however, has prevented more equitable distribution of the benefits from food producing plants. Additionally, about 1.3 billion tons of food produced for human consumption is wasted per year and the loss on a per capita basis is higher in industrialized world than in developing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"01bv8C7c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2011b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2011b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33328,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CK96YQRK"],"itemData":{"id":33328,"type":"document","title":"Food wastage footprint and climate change. Retrieved from: http://www.fao.org/3/a-bb144e.pdf.","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":"Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) of the United Nations"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Habitat loss and fragmentation, and the consequent reduction of much of the Earth’s biodiversity, has been caused by the increasing human population density and energy use, mainly after the 19th and 20th centuries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2Xe84XhQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sala 2000; Fahrig, n.d.)","plainCitation":"(Sala 2000; Fahrig, n.d.)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33318,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KAMEYCQB"],"itemData":{"id":33318,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","page":"1770–1774","title":"Biodiversity: Global Biodiversity Scenarios for the Year 2100","volume":"287","author":[{"family":"Sala","given":"O E"},{"family":"Chapin","given":"F S"},{"family":"Armesto","given":"J J"},{"family":"Berlow","given":"E"},{"family":"Bloomfield","given":"J"},{"family":"Dirzo","given":"R"},{"family":"Huber-Sanwald","given":"E"},{"family":"Huenneke","given":"L F"},{"family":"Jackson","given":"R B"},{"family":"Kinzig","given":"A"},{"family":"Leemans","given":"R"},{"family":"Lodge","given":"D M"},{"family":"Mooney","given":"H A"},{"family":"Oesterheld","given":"M"},{"family":"Poff","given":"N L"},{"family":"Sykes","given":"M T"},{"family":"Walker","given":"B"},{"family":"Walker","given":"M"},{"family":"Wall","given":"D H"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":33264,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AE2E4CRA"],"itemData":{"id":33264,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Ecology, Evolution and Systematics","language":"fr","page":"487–515","title":"Effects of habitat fragmentation on biodiversity","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Fahrig","given":"L."}]}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The drivers of species loss include pollution, and habitat conversion. Habitat conversion for human development, directly trades economic profit for habitat loss. Economic activity that causes habitat loss includes urbanization, mining, water development, and agriculture. However, a leading cause of global change is likely the growing human modification of environments for agriculture ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mWqHnafD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Keitt, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Keitt, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33249,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B4UXG32N"],"itemData":{"id":33249,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","language":"en","page":"1561–1573","title":"Habitat conversion, extinction thresholds, and pollination services in agroecosystems","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Keitt","given":"T.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Cerrado is the richest savanna ecosystem in the world, but the intensive human occupation process has also transformed it into one of the most important regions for cattle ranching and commodity crops in Brazil ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3pTAFeum","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Myers, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Myers, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33293,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K3IB5HKN"],"itemData":{"id":33293,"type":"report","abstract":"Conservationists are far from able to assist all species under threat, if only for lack of funding. This places a premium on priorities: how can we support the most species at the least cost? One way is to identify`biodiversityidentify`biodiversity hotspots' where exceptional concentrations of endemic species are undergoing exceptional loss of habitat. As many as 44% of all species of vascular plants and 35% of all species in four vertebrate groups are con®ned to 25 hotspots comprising only 1.4% of the land surface of the Earth. This opens the way for a `silver bullet' strategy on the part of conservation planners, focusing on these hotspots in proportion to their share of the world's species at risk. The number of species threatened with extinction far outstrips available conservation resources, and the situation looks set to become rapidly worse 1±4. This places a premium on identifying priorities. How can we protect the most species per dollar invested? This key question is at the forefront of conservation planning, and forms the focus of this article. By concentrating on areas where there is greatest need and where the payoff from safeguard measures would also be greatest, conservationists can engage in a systematic response to the challenge of large-scale extinctions ahead. A promising approach is to identify`hotspotsidentify`hotspots' , or areas featuring exceptional concentrations of endemic species and experiencing exceptional loss of habitat 5±9. Here we focus on species, rather than populations or other taxa, as the most prominent and readily recognizable form of biodiversity. This is not to suggest that populations and even ecological processes are not important manifestations of biodiversity, but they do not belong in this assessment. There are other types of hotspot 10,11 , featuring richness of, for example, rare 12,13 or taxonomically unusual species 14,15. This article considers only hotspots as de®ned above. Concentrating a large proportion of conservation support on these areas would go far to stem the mass extinction of species that is now underway. The hotspots' boundaries have been determined by`biologicalby`biological commonalities'. Each of the areas features a separate biota or community of species that ®ts together as a biogeographic unit. This is apparent in the case of islands or island groups such as New Caledonia, New Zealand, the Caribbean, Polynesia/Micronesia, Madagascar and the Philippines. Much the same applies tòeco-logical islands' in clearly de®ned continental units such as the Cape Figure 1 The 25 hotspots. The hotspot expanses comprise 30±3% of the red areas.","title":"Biodiversity hotspots for conservation priorities","URL":"www.nature.com","author":[{"family":"Myers","given":"Norman"},{"family":"Mittermeier²","given":"Russell A"},{"family":"Mittermeier²","given":"Cristina G"},{"family":"Da Fonseca³","given":"Gustavo A B"},{"family":"Kent","given":"Jennifer"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The proportion of remaining habitats in the Cerrado varies from 39% to 55% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LohhaItF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eva, 2004; Machado, 2004; Mantovani & Pereira, 1998; Sano, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Eva, 2004; Machado, 2004; Mantovani & Pereira, 1998; Sano, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33335,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LA3YXYIY"],"itemData":{"id":33335,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Biology","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"731–744","title":"A land cover map of South America","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Eva","given":"H.D."},{"family":"Belward","given":"A.S."},{"family":"De Miranda","given":"E.E."},{"family":"Di Bella","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Gond","given":"V."},{"family":"Huber","given":"O."},{"family":"Fritz","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":33223,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8WVX6VPL"],"itemData":{"id":33223,"type":"report","genre":"(technical report)","language":"pt","title":"Estimativas de perda da área do Cerrado Brasileiro. Conservation International","author":[{"family":"Machado","given":"R.B."},{"family":"Ramos Neto","given":"M.B."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"P.G.P."},{"family":"Caldas","given":"E.F."},{"family":"Goncalves","given":"D.A."},{"family":"Santos","given":"N.S."},{"family":"Tabor","given":"K."},{"family":"teininger","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":33224,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FDFXKDYI"],"itemData":{"id":33224,"type":"chapter","container-title":"SIMPÓSIO BRASILEIRO DE SENSORIAMENTO","language":"en","title":"Estimating the integrity of the Cerrado vegetation cover through the Landsat-TM data","author":[{"family":"Mantovani","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Pereira","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":33382,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RLTQTSGA"],"itemData":{"id":33382,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pesquisa Agropecuária Brasileira","language":"pt","page":"153–156","title":"semidetalhado do uso da terra do Bioma Cerrado","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Sano","given":"E.E.Rosa"},{"family":"R.","given":"Brito"},{"family":"J.L.S.","given":"Ferreira"},{"literal":"L.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Cattle ranching and intensive farming in the Cerrado ecoregion have caused tremendous decline in natural habitat cover ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GPkZePyk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Diniz-Filho, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Diniz-Filho, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33343,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D43GMVEA"],"itemData":{"id":33343,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientia Agricola","ISSN":"0103-9016","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Scientia Agricola","note":"number: 6\\npublisher: SciELO Brasil","page":"764-771","title":"Agriculture, habitat loss and spatial patterns of human occupation in a biodiversity hotspot","volume":"66","author":[{"family":"Diniz-Filho","given":"José Alexandre Felizola"},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"Guilherme","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Lobo","given":"Fábio"},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"Laerte Guimarães"},{"family":"Bini","given":"Luis Mauricio"},{"family":"Rangel","given":"Thiago Fernando Lopes Valle Brito"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. According to a study by Hermann and colleagues, land conversion rates far outweigh preservation attempts in the area. Collecting data from satellite images, silviculture in the area was expanded by 94% over the six-year study, and grassland was the main target for agricultural land conversion. On a larger scale, this reflects global developments in temperate grasslands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ptXI9wMv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hermann, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Hermann, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33334,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8B37WCYS"],"itemData":{"id":33334,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Health and Sustainability","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"01224","title":"Forest–grassland biodiversity hotspot under siege: land conversion counteracts nature conservation","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Hermann","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Lang","given":"M."},{"family":"Gonçalves","given":"J."},{"family":"Hasenack","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An overwhelming number of studies have looked at the impact of agricultural habitat conversion on birds, which are often used as indicators of biodiversity status. They play vital roles in many ecosystems, ranging from pollination and seed dispersal to insect control and nutrient cycling. It has been estimated that approximately a fifth to a quarter of pre-agricultural bird numbers have been lost due to agricultural development ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"icaBFdjT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gaston, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Gaston, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33333,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H6K7VAP3"],"itemData":{"id":33333,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the Royal Society of London. Series B: Biological Sciences","issue":"1521","language":"en","page":"1293–1300","title":"Habitat conversion and global avian biodiversity loss","volume":"270","author":[{"family":"Gaston","given":"K.J."},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"T.M."},{"family":"Goldewijk","given":"K.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In particular, avian breeding success is impacted by agricultural land conversion. 
	A study conducted by Cartwright, concluded that the formerly critically endangered Mauritius kestrel Falco punctatus experiences a decline in breeding success as the area of agriculture near a nest site is increased ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hKhgk03i","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cartwright, n.d.)","plainCitation":"(Cartwright, n.d.)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33271,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NK28G9IV"],"itemData":{"id":33271,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"J Appl Ecol. Oct","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"1387–1395","title":"Agriculture modifies the seasonal decline of breeding success in a tropical wild bird population","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Cartwright","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Nicoll","given":"M.A.C."},{"family":"Jones","given":"C.G."},{"family":"Tatayah","given":"V."},{"family":"Norris","given":"K."}]}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This may be attributable to the increasing spatial variation in the availability of native prey, which is reduced by land conversion. In addition, loss of farmland bird populations has been observed in Europe. For example, farmland bird populations that are dependent on key aspects of these agro-ecosystems experienced a 40% decline between 1980 and 2000 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7M7M9KGV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. Cao 2010)","plainCitation":"(L. Cao 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33268,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CIBYZRWF"],"itemData":{"id":33268,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"21","language":"en","page":"9513–9518","title":"Importance of carbon dioxide physiological forcing to future climate change","volume":"107","author":[{"family":"Cao","given":"L."},{"family":"Bala","given":"G."},{"family":"Caldeira","given":"K."},{"family":"Nemani","given":"R."},{"family":"Ban-Weiss","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Cattle ranching and intensive farming in the Cerrado ecoregion have caused tremendous decline in natural habitat cover ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PYfJbZ9B","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Diniz-Filho, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Diniz-Filho, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33343,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D43GMVEA"],"itemData":{"id":33343,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientia Agricola","ISSN":"0103-9016","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Scientia Agricola","note":"number: 6\\npublisher: SciELO Brasil","page":"764-771","title":"Agriculture, habitat loss and spatial patterns of human occupation in a biodiversity hotspot","volume":"66","author":[{"family":"Diniz-Filho","given":"José Alexandre Felizola"},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"Guilherme","dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Lobo","given":"Fábio"},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"Laerte Guimarães"},{"family":"Bini","given":"Luis Mauricio"},{"family":"Rangel","given":"Thiago Fernando Lopes Valle Brito"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The drastic loss of spatially consistent natural cover in the Cerrado ecoregion denotes the decline of many endemic plant species, which is also mirrored in the Atlantic rain forest ecoregion. The forest-grassland mosaic of Rio Grande do Sul, Brazil has been largely converted for agri- and silviculture. Due to extensive logging practices, the area’s Araucaria broadleaf forest is only a mere fraction of its original extension, and the Araucaria angustifolia species has been recently placed on the International Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural Resources Red List of Endangered Species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9gWsFKAs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hermann, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Hermann, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33334,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8B37WCYS"],"itemData":{"id":33334,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecosystem Health and Sustainability","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"01224","title":"Forest–grassland biodiversity hotspot under siege: land conversion counteracts nature conservation","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Hermann","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Lang","given":"M."},{"family":"Gonçalves","given":"J."},{"family":"Hasenack","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

4.2.1.6. Pollution and eutrophication
Key messages :
* Pollution, be it from anthropogenic or natural sources, brings negative consequences on the abundance, distribution, availability, harvesting, gathering, and value chain of wild species in different ways and at different spatial and temporal scales (well established). 
* The interaction of state, indigenous peoples and local communities, different forms of conservation bodies (national and international, governmental and non-governmental organizations, and community-based organizations, and other stakeholders is central to the safeguarding of wild species and minimizing every possible cause and threat posed by pollution (established but incomplete).
* Acts and regulations are often inadequate or poorly addressed in terms of local evaluation methods such as Initial Environmental Examination and Environmental Impact Assessment - where in majority of cases, effects of pollution on wild species are hardly understood. Better understanding of pollution-induced changes in wild species dynamics, effective implementation of regulations, building capacity and awareness are paramount (established but incomplete).   
Key points for policymaking: 
* Sufficient support by governments for more research and better understanding of the pollution-induced changes in wild species and their sustainable use is paramount.
* More serious international pollution mitigation commitments and emission curtailment agreements are needed vis-a-vis checking the unsustainable exploitation of wild species, including their illegal trade.
* Developing a global policy framework for post-2020 achievable targets, with specific regional guidelines, to minimize and eventually stop the impact of pollution on sustainable use of wild species is important.

4.2.1.6.1. Overview
This section reviews environmental pollution, specifically, air, water and land pollution vis-à-vis the impacts on sustainable use of wild species. The pollution could either be anthropogenic or natural and is characterized as being due to chemical, physical or biological pollutants emanating from point or non-point sources. 
      Air pollution decreases the native populations of animals with serious negative impacts on wild birds, insects, reptiles as well as wild mammals. The anthropogenic variables of air pollution are also responsible for decline in lichens, wild growing medicinal plants and many other wild species. Air pollution from industry harms wild species in different ways, such as bioaccumulation, causing diseases, mortality and physiological stress. The pollutants originate from human activities such as combustion of burnable waste, fossil fuels in thermal power plants and automobiles, which increases the concentration of gaseous and particulate pollutants in the atmosphere. The resulting air pollution and subsequent acid deposition changes the chemistry of the lakes and damages wild plants. Air pollutants affect wild species by entering the food chain and damaging the supply and quality of food through bioaccumulation. The oxides of sulphur (sulphur oxide, sulphur dioxide, and sulphate), noxious nitrogen gas (nitrogen monoxide, nitrous oxide, nitrogen dioxide) ammonia (NH3), volatile organic compounds, and carbon monoxide (CO) are the important chemical pollutants emitted from various anthropogenic activities, the effects of which on ecosystems and wild species therein are growing with the growth of urbanization and industrialization. 
      A major threat to wild aquatic species is the contamination of water bodies by different pollutants (physical, biological, chemical and radioactive) resulting from many sources (mining activities, industrial ef?uents, domestic sewage and agricultural runoff). Domestic and industrial waste, livestock waste, and agrochemicals are predominant pollutants in waterbodies. Oil pollution enters the ocean and severely contaminates beaches and sediment and causes serious harm to marine wild species. Oil spills in freshwater ecosystems too have grave impacts on wild species. Phosphorus, nitrogen and many other nutrients are added to aquatic ecosystems continuously by agriculture and urban activities, which in turn cause diverse problems, such as oxygen limitation, toxic algal blooms, loss of biodiversity and threat to important wild species of recreational value.  It affects the quality of the environment or habitat in which they live and the availability and quality of the food supply. Acid rain, heavy metals, persistent organic pollutants and other toxic substances are the issues of major concern, the effects of which on fish, fisheries and other wild aquatic species is felt across the world, especially in developing world due to increasing population, urbanization and modernization. The elevated concentration of nutrients in freshwater bodies (eutrophication) triggers blooms, invasions and biodiversity loss in lakes, rivers and wetlands thereby pushing the useful wild species to the brink of rarity or extinction. Plastic accumulation in the oceans has severe impacts on marine life, increasing the likelihood of coral reefs being a?ected by diseases, and threatening overall ecosystem health and human livelihoods. Ingestion of microplastics is being reported in a number of marine invertebrate species. Microfibre ingestion in crabs affects food consumption and energy balance, while in marine worms the ingestion of microscopic unplasticized polyvinylchloride reduces growth and energy reserves. 
      The soil pollution caused by myriad of human activities, leakage of oil and chemicals, excessive use of chemical pesticides and fertilizers etc., negatively effects soil health, and consequently the soil biota. Excessive levels of fertilizers, pesticides, and even nutrients, especially nitrogen, phosphorous and heavy metals composition in terrestrial soils, freshwater sediments and coastal ecosystems with cascading effects on wild species diversity, ecosystem function and human wellbeing. In the last few decades, there is some evidence that various types of xenobiotics have adversely affected wild species through soil-based impacts thereby contributing quite a great deal to push these species to the brink of extinction. Whether soil microbes also go extinct many a times without even being ever identified, and how such extinctions are related to the extinction of wild species above-ground, are some unanswered questions.
      
4.2.1.6.2. Key Gaps :
* Some extremely harmful pollutants such as polychlorinated biphenyls, despite a near global ban over more than past three decades, are still found to bioaccumulate in some wild species thereby indicating their presence and use in the environment. 
* The rising global decline of insects, especially pollinators, due to air pollution-induced mortality or climate change driven plant-pollinator mismatch needs more evidence, and effective intervention, in view of its huge implications for horticulture and agriculture sectors that comprise the backbone of the economy of developing countries. 
* Lack of focused in-depth studies on the impact of pollution on keystone wild species despite the dire need of understanding their biology, ecology and conservation in the context of growing pollution, especially in the developing countries. 

4.2.1.6.3. Methodology
The experts used about 30 keywords in different permutations and combinations to search the relevant literature. Each term pertaining to different types and forms of pollution was paired with different words or terms used for wild species.  For instance, terms such as pollution, pollutants, toxic chemicals, contaminants, environmental pollution, air pollution, water pollution, soil pollution, noise pollution, toxic elements, heavy metals, organic contaminants, inorganic pollutants, automobile pollution, biotic pollution etc. were paired with terms such as sustainable use of wild species, wild species, wild resources, birds, fish, reptiles, freshwater life, marine species, forest species, wilderness, extinction, habitat modification, keystone species, global patterns of impact of pollution on wild species, regional changes in wild resources by pollution etc. Over 200 searches were performed that resulted in a large number of research articles, policy documents, book chapters and other papers, which in turn were refined to select the most appropriate articles/sources in English since 1950 for inclusion. References cited in these perused articles were also considered, if appropriate, in order to ensure as wide coverage of related research as possible.
Figure 4.4. Typology of pollution and causes. The impact of pollution on sustainable use of wild species are quite varied and a suite of mitigation measures against pollution vis-à-vis wild species could be used, in isolation or combination, in context specific situations. License: CC-BY.

4.2.1.6.4. Air pollution
Air pollution is one of the major global environmental concerns, with not only serious human health impacts but also equally serious effects on myriad of wild species. Gaseous air pollutants are emitted from various natural sources, such as volcanoes and forest fires, or anthropogenic activities that have significantly increased with population growth and industrialization ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jpBs9TuC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kemp, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Kemp, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33248,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WX4KHHDM"],"itemData":{"id":33248,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA","language":"en","page":"11017–1102","title":"Climate related sea-level variations over the past two millennia","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Kemp","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Horton","given":"B.P."},{"family":"Donnelly","given":"J.P."},{"family":"Mann","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Vermeer","given":"M."},{"family":"Rahmstorf","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Air pollution comprises of a mixture of gases and particles (such as CO2, CO, O3, SO2, CH3, smoke and mixture of urban and industrial emissions) in undesirable and harmful amounts. Human beings have produced facilities that use many of the Earth’s energy resources to make their life easier. Burning fuels such as coal, oil and natural gases result in pollution by the release of harmful substances into the environment. Burning of fossil fuels in industries and transport sector, industrialization and urbanization have led to increase in concentrations of gaseous and particulate pollutants in the atmosphere leading to air pollution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5edKjkbl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tripathi and Gautam, n.d.; Dwivedi and Tripathi 2007)","plainCitation":"(Tripathi and Gautam, n.d.; Dwivedi and Tripathi 2007)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33355,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F95GBVB7"],"itemData":{"id":33355,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"J. Environ. Biol","language":"fr","page":"127–132","title":"M(2007).Biochemical parameters of plants as indicators of air pollution","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Tripathi","given":"A.K."},{"literal":"Gautam"}]}},{"id":33257,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BJH6CIZH"],"itemData":{"id":33257,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"J. Environ. Biol","language":"en","page":"257–263","title":"Pollution tolerance and distribution pattern of plants in surrounding area of coal-fired industries","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Dwivedi","given":"A.K."},{"family":"Tripathi","given":"B.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These constituents interact with reactants present in the atmosphere and result in secondary pollutants such as acid deposition. Acid rain causes negative impact on aquatic organisms including fishes. The aquatic ecosystems found in Belgium, Denmark, West Germany and the Netherland ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8thrBV5H","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Whelpdale, 1983)","plainCitation":"(Whelpdale, 1983)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33307,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U9MYBBDZ"],"itemData":{"id":33307,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Water Quality Bulletin","language":"en","page":"72–80","title":"Acid deposition, distribution and impact","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Whelpdale","given":"D.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1983"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, are affected by acid rain. Acidification of surface waters had been reported in many including Great Britain, Northern, Central and Eastern Europe, southwestern China, southeastern Canada, the Northeast, Upper Midwest and Appalachian Mountain regions of the United States of America. Large portions of the high elevation western North America are also potentially sensitive to acidic deposition, however, atmospheric deposition to this region is relatively low. Concern over effects of acidic deposition in the Mountain West and California may be overshadowed by potential effects of elevated nitrogen deposition, including eutrophication of naturally nitrogen-limited lakes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"A32nilPd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fenn, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Fenn, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33262,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I9WYPLCY"],"itemData":{"id":33262,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1641/0006-3568(2003)053(0404:EEONDI)2.0.CO;2","language":"fr","page":"404– 420","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Fenn","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Baron","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allen","given":"E.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Decreases in pH and elevated concentrations of dissolved inorganic aluminum have resulted in physiological changes to organisms, direct mortality of sensitive life history stages, and reduced the species diversity and abundance of aquatic life in many streams and lakes in acid-impacted areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xCV1xBrM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u8220{}DriscolI , C.T.and Wang,Z (2019).Ecosystem Effects of Acidic Deposition,\\\\uc0\\\\u8221{} n.d.)","plainCitation":"(“DriscolI , C.T.and Wang,Z (2019).Ecosystem Effects of Acidic Deposition,” n.d.)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33265,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YTRVCLR9"],"itemData":{"id":33265,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Encyclopedia of Water: Science, Technology, and Society","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1002/9781119300762.wsts0043","title":"DriscolI , C.T.and Wang,Z (2019).Ecosystem Effects of Acidic Deposition"}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Top level predators such as bears and eagles, among many others, are particularly susceptible to the bioaccumulation of these types of air pollutants. Changes in the abundance of any species because of air pollution can dramatically influence the abundance and health of dependent species. The loss of some species of fish because of higher levels of aluminum may allow insect populations to increase, which may benefit certain types of ducks that feed on insects. But the same loss of fish could be detrimental to eagles, ospreys and many other animals that depend on fish as a source of food.  
      The major effects of industrial air pollution on wild species include direct mortality, debilitating industrial-related injury and disease, physiological stress, anemia, and bioaccumulation. Some air pollutants have caused a change in the distribution of certain wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0Ys1Zd9M","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. J. Newman, 1979)","plainCitation":"(R. J. Newman, 1979)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33411,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MNFI3Y7K"],"itemData":{"id":33411,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"181–190","title":").Effects of industrial air pollution on wildlife","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Newman","given":"R.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1979"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The African urban centers have grown tremendously in the last thirty years and are still on a continuous rise ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3qkslYEx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Obeng-Odoom, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Obeng-Odoom, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33215,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HQVRMZNG"],"itemData":{"id":33215,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Cities","language":"en","page":"425–429","title":"The state of African cities 2010: Governance, inequality and urban land markets","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Obeng-Odoom","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Motor vehicles, power generation plants and other industrial machinery produce toxic gases. Nitrous oxide, Sulphur oxide and Carbon monoxide pollution in the tropics may exert more adverse effects on sensitive species of diverse plants and animals, and photo-chemicals have resulted in shifts of vegetation from ozone sensitive to ozone tolerant ones ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Pnln0g6Z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barker & Tingey, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Barker & Tingey, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33320,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JPVX3CRY"],"itemData":{"id":33320,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Berlin, Germany","publisher":"Springer Science and Business Media","title":"Air pollution effects on biodiversity","URL":"http://www.unece.org/environmental-policy/conventions/","author":[{"family":"Barker","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Tingey","given":"D.T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Sulphur dioxide and hydrogen sulphide emitted from power plants, especially the coal fired ones and paper and mill factories, have already resulted to acid rains in various parts of Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MYacwA0J","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Europe & W.H.O., 2006; Josipovic, 2010; Nduka, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Europe & W.H.O., 2006; Josipovic, 2010; Nduka, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33261,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I2VNNMQ7"],"itemData":{"id":33261,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global update","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Denmark\\npublisher: WHO","title":"Air quality guidelines for particulate matter, ozone, nitrogen dioxide and sulfur dioxide","URL":"http://www.euro.who.","author":[{"literal":"Europe"},{"literal":"W.H.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":33407,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NEZQNFSG"],"itemData":{"id":33407,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Scientific World Journal","language":"en","page":"811–818","title":"Acid rain phenomenon in Niger delta region of Nigeria: Economic, biodiversity, and public health concern","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Nduka","given":"J.K.C."},{"family":"Orisakwe","given":"O.E."},{"family":"Ezenweke","given":"L.O."},{"family":"Ezenwa","given":"T.E."},{"family":"N.","given":"Chendo M."},{"family":"Ezeabalisi","given":"N.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":33247,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L54HNL9K"],"itemData":{"id":33247,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Monitoring and Assessment","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"181–196","title":"Concentrations, distributions and critical level exceedance assessment of SO2, NO2 and O3 in South Africa","volume":"171","author":[{"family":"Josipovic","given":"M."},{"family":"Annegarn","given":"H.J."},{"family":"Kneen","given":"M.A."},{"family":"Pienaar","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Piketh","given":"S.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The sources of NH3 in Africa are municipal effluent, farmyard/feedlot manure, and inorganic mineral fertilizers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jG2jZglU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carmichael, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Carmichael, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33267,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2R9H83XB"],"itemData":{"id":33267,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Atmospheric Environment","issue":"9","language":"en","page":"1293–1308","title":"Measurements of sulfur dioxide, ozone and ammonia concentrations in Asia, Africa, and South America using passive samplers","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Carmichael","given":"G.R."},{"family":"Ferm","given":"M."},{"family":"Thongboonchoo","given":"N."},{"family":"Woo","given":"J.H."},{"family":"Chan","given":"L.Y."},{"family":"Murano","given":"K."},{"family":"Viet","given":"P.H."},{"family":"Mossberg","given":"C."},{"family":"Bala","given":"R."},{"family":"Boonjawat","given":"J."},{"family":"Upatum","given":"P."},{"family":"Mohan","given":"M."},{"family":"Adhikary","given":"S.P."},{"family":"Shrestha","given":"A.B."},{"family":"Pienaar","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Brunke","given":"E.B."},{"family":"Chen","given":"T."},{"family":"Jie","given":"T."},{"family":"Guoan","given":"D."},{"family":"Peng","given":"L.C."},{"family":"Dhiharto","given":"S."},{"family":"Harjanto","given":"H."},{"family":"Jose","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Kimani","given":"W."},{"family":"Kirouane","given":"A."},{"family":"Lacaux","given":"J."},{"family":"Richard","given":"S."},{"family":"Barturen","given":"O."},{"family":"Cerda","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Athayde","given":"A."},{"family":"Tavares","given":"T."},{"family":"Cotrina","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Bilici","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There have been many reported air pollution episodes involving injury or death to animals since the end of the nineteenth century ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yjPxFxCz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. R. Newman, 1980; J. R. Newman & Schreiber, 1985)","plainCitation":"(J. R. Newman, 1980; J. R. Newman & Schreiber, 1985)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33410,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B4UAI2MN"],"itemData":{"id":33410,"type":"chapter","container-title":"U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, Biological Services Program, National Power Plant Team","language":"en","page":"–80 40 1 32","title":"Effects of air emissions on wildlife resources","author":[{"family":"Newman","given":"J.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1980"]]}}},{"id":33213,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MMES2IGD"],"itemData":{"id":33213,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Veterinary and Human Toxicology","language":"en","page":"394–401","title":"Effects of acidic deposition and other energy emissions on wildlife: A compendium","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Newman","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Schreiber","given":"R.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1985"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some of the incidents involving the adverse effects of these airborne pollutants on mammals and birds have been recorded quite earlier. For instance, the earliest incident involving arsenic poisoning of fallow deer (Dama dama) in Germany was recorded in 1887. Among birds, there are examples of granivores, insectivores, and carnivores being affected in various ways by air emissions. Effects may range from quite subtle, such as a reduction in genetic diversity, to dramatic, such as a change in population numbers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gptBCFvu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. R. Newman, 1980; J. R. Newman & Schreiber, 1985)","plainCitation":"(J. R. Newman, 1980; J. R. Newman & Schreiber, 1985)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33410,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B4UAI2MN"],"itemData":{"id":33410,"type":"chapter","container-title":"U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, Biological Services Program, National Power Plant Team","language":"en","page":"–80 40 1 32","title":"Effects of air emissions on wildlife resources","author":[{"family":"Newman","given":"J.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1980"]]}}},{"id":33213,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MMES2IGD"],"itemData":{"id":33213,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Veterinary and Human Toxicology","language":"en","page":"394–401","title":"Effects of acidic deposition and other energy emissions on wildlife: A compendium","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Newman","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Schreiber","given":"R.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1985"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A review of decades of research on lead contamination in vultures across the world found 72% of articles from North America and Europe, with the rest corresponding to Asia (13%), South America (8%), and Africa (7%). Of these 88% studies showed the lead concentration beyond threshold limits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"n1ZZjnxu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Plaza & Lambertucci, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Plaza & Lambertucci, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33395,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NB27UTG5"],"itemData":{"id":33395,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science of the Total Environment","language":"en","page":"409–417","title":"What do we know about lead contamination in wild vultures and condors? A review of decades of research","volume":"654","author":[{"family":"Plaza","given":"P.I."},{"family":"Lambertucci","given":"S.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This corroborates with a series of case studies that demonstrate huge and sustained impact of contaminants, such as dichloro-diphenyl-trichloroethane, dieldrin and declofenac on vultures ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pqvdaKUZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shore & Taggart, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Shore & Taggart, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33374,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FF6Y5BIR"],"itemData":{"id":33374,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Science & Health","language":"en","page":"11,65–70,","title":"Population-level impacts of chemical contaminants on apex avian species","author":[{"family":"Shore","given":"R.F."},{"family":"Taggart","given":"M.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The rising global decline of insects, especially pollinators, and birds due to air pollution-induced mortality is evidenced by significant correlation between increase in pollutants such as respirable suspended particulate matter and changes in pollinator bee survival ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wQtlreLa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Thimmegowda, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Thimmegowda, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33353,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RBELUX5I"],"itemData":{"id":33353,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U.S.A","language":"en","page":"20653–20661","title":"A field-based quantitative analysis of sublethal effects of air pollution on pollinators","volume":"117","author":[{"family":"Thimmegowda","given":"G.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, high avian mortality due to fly ash calls for controlling sources such as atmospheric geo-engineering and industrial emissions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jw7vlKMz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dutta, 2017; Whiteside & Herndon, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Dutta, 2017; Whiteside & Herndon, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QLFZYLFZ"],"itemData":{"id":33331,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Energ. Ecol. Environ","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"329–349","title":"Insights into the impacts of four current environmental problems on flying birds","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Dutta","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33309,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EIFR2IT9"],"itemData":{"id":33309,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Populations and Species Asian Journal of","language":"en","page":"1–21","title":"Coal Fly Ash: A Previously Unrecognized Primary Factor in the Catastrophic Global Demise of Bird","volume":"Biology6(4","author":[{"family":"Whiteside","given":"M."},{"family":"Herndon","given":"J.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GRfqzGE8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sanderfoot and Holloway 2017)","plainCitation":"(Sanderfoot and Holloway 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33384,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/77BXQL2A"],"itemData":{"id":33384,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environ. Res. Lett","language":"en","page":"083002","title":"Air pollution impacts on avian species via inhalation exposure and associated outcomes","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Sanderfoot","given":"O.V."},{"family":"Holloway","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found consistent evidence for the adverse effect of air pollution on birds, primarily attributable to CO, O3, SO2, smoke and mixture of urban and industrial emissions. One of the first case reports using monkeys as model systems recently linked animal social conflict to air pollution and global warming ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QFLX2yV1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Xu, 2021)","plainCitation":"(A. Xu, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33371,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KHACMTSL"],"itemData":{"id":33371,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"654","title":"Monkeys fight more in polluted air","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Xu","given":"A."},{"family":"Liu","given":"C."},{"family":"Wan","given":"Y."},{"family":"Bai","given":"Y."},{"family":"Li","given":"Z."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the results of which clearly indicate more daily social fighting behaviors under the condition of polluted air.  Even mate choice at different stages can be affected by pollution, which in turn can influence individual fitness, population dynamics and community structure of wild animals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"H6n7M73C","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Candolin & Wong, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Candolin & Wong, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33270,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I3U2TAWT"],"itemData":{"id":33270,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"B","language":"fr","page":"20180055","title":"Mate choice in a polluted world: consequences for individuals, populations and communities","volume":"374","author":[{"family":"Candolin","given":"U."},{"family":"Wong","given":"B.B.M."}],"translator":[{"family":"Soc","given":"Phil R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The anthropogenic variables of air pollution are responsible for lichen decline ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"74l5Bbmn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Giordani, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Giordani, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33235,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RVILK45D"],"itemData":{"id":33235,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environ. Pollut","language":"en","page":"317–323","title":"Is the diversity of epiphytic lichens a reliable indicator of air pollution? A case study from Italy","volume":"146","author":[{"family":"Giordani","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The lichens are able to indiscriminately absorb a large range from the ambient air through their entire surface ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"09jNn802","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aznar, 2008; Conti, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Aznar, 2008; Conti, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33324,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B6XN6E4R"],"itemData":{"id":33324,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Pollution","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"76–81","title":"Metal contamination in the lichen Alectoria sarmentosa near the copper smelter of Murdochville, Quebec","volume":"156","author":[{"family":"Aznar","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Richer-Lafleche","given":"M."},{"family":"Cluis","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":33274,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/84WM6AH3"],"itemData":{"id":33274,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Monitoring and Assessment","language":"it","page":"1–12","title":"Atmospheric background trace elements deposition in Tierra del Fuego region (Patagonia, Argentina), using transplanted Usnea barbata lichens","author":[{"family":"Conti","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Finoia","given":"M."},{"family":"Bocca","given":"B."},{"family":"Mele","given":"G."},{"family":"Alimonti","given":"A."},{"family":"Pino","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Accumulated pollutants in their thallus in line with atmospheric concentrations show a close correlation with their atmospheric levels and have proved the lichen’s capability as an effective biomonitor ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ddg9ydf9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Adamo, 2008; Godinho, 2009; Wolterbeek, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Adamo, 2008; Godinho, 2009; Wolterbeek, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33348,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KJJ7265D"],"itemData":{"id":33348,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Atmospheric Environment","language":"en","page":"2 ,346–357","title":"Geochemical properties of airborne particulate matter (PM10) collected by automatic device and biomonitors in a Mediterranean urban environment","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Adamo","given":"P."},{"family":"Giordano","given":"S."},{"family":"Naimo","given":"D."},{"literal":"Bargagli"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":33233,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P2FSF3R5"],"itemData":{"id":33233,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Pollution","issue":"1","language":"fr","page":"102–109","title":"Accumulation of trace elements in the peripheral and central parts of two species of epiphytic lichens transplanted to a polluted site in Portugal","volume":"157","author":[{"family":"Godinho","given":"R.M."},{"family":"Verburg","given":"T.G."},{"family":"Freitas","given":"M.C."},{"family":"Wolterbeek","given":"H.T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":33368,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZEDJF8RK"],"itemData":{"id":33368,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Markert, A.M.B.B.A., Zechmeister, H.G.(Eds.), Trace Metals and Other Contaminants in the Environment","language":"en","page":"377–419","title":"Biomonitors in use: lichens and metal air pollution","volume":"6. Elsevier","author":[{"family":"Wolterbeek","given":"H.T."},{"family":"Garty","given":"J."},{"family":"Reis","given":"M.A."},{"family":"Freitas","given":"M.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The loss of lichen diversity in response to environmental conditions is widely used as an indicator for several complex phenomena, including air pollution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XIyRgnch","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Giordani, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Giordani, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33235,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RVILK45D"],"itemData":{"id":33235,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environ. Pollut","language":"en","page":"317–323","title":"Is the diversity of epiphytic lichens a reliable indicator of air pollution? A case study from Italy","volume":"146","author":[{"family":"Giordani","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EKsI2IvL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gombert, Asta, and Seaward 2004)","plainCitation":"(Gombert, Asta, and Seaward 2004)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33238,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NPVGVLIY"],"itemData":{"id":33238,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sci. Total Environ","language":"en","page":"324,183–199","title":"Assessment of lichen diversity by index of atmospheric purity (IAP), index of human impact (IHI) and other environmental factors in an urban area (Grenoble, southeast France","author":[{"family":"Gombert","given":"S."},{"family":"Asta","given":"J."},{"family":"Seaward","given":"M.R.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} determined the decreased lichen abundance and spatial trends of lichen diversity around urban and industrial areas, based on the fact that anthropogenic variables are responsible for lichen decline, irrespective of natural succession of epiphytic communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6eeJNL59","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Purvis, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Purvis, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33396,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8XA7YIAX"],"itemData":{"id":33396,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science of the Total Environment","language":"en","page":"179–189","title":"Which factors are responsible for the changing lichen floras of London?","volume":"310","author":[{"family":"Purvis","given":"O.W."},{"family":"Chimonides","given":"J."},{"family":"Din","given":"V."},{"family":"Erotokritou","given":"L."},{"family":"Jeffries","given":"T."},{"family":"Jones","given":"G.C."},{"family":"Louwhoff","given":"S."},{"family":"Read","given":"H."},{"family":"Spiro","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A study conducted by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"B1Ueuihm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Douglas 2017)","plainCitation":"(Douglas 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33266,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QVV2CBPK"],"itemData":{"id":33266,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environments","language":"en","page":"94 2–19","title":"Buellia dispersa (Lichens) Used as Bio-Indicators for Air Pollution Transport: A Case Study within the Las Vegas Valley, Nevada (USA","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Douglas","given":"B.Sims"},{"family":"Amanda","given":"C.Hudson"},{"family":"Park","given":"Ji Hye"},{"family":"Hodge","given":"Vernon"},{"family":"Porter","given":"Heidi"},{"family":"Spaulding","given":"W.Geoffrey"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} indicated that samples from the Las Vegas valley are a good baseline of pollutants in lichens. Furthermore, lichen collected within the valley contained higher concentrations of target pollutants suggesting that the accumulation of pollution is likely anthropogenic in nature (e.g., industries, vehicular traffic). ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WWTDrJ70","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Douglas 2017)","plainCitation":"(Douglas 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33266,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QVV2CBPK"],"itemData":{"id":33266,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environments","language":"en","page":"94 2–19","title":"Buellia dispersa (Lichens) Used as Bio-Indicators for Air Pollution Transport: A Case Study within the Las Vegas Valley, Nevada (USA","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Douglas","given":"B.Sims"},{"family":"Amanda","given":"C.Hudson"},{"family":"Park","given":"Ji Hye"},{"family":"Hodge","given":"Vernon"},{"family":"Porter","given":"Heidi"},{"family":"Spaulding","given":"W.Geoffrey"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} documented that vehicle emissions are a source of nitrate. Findings show NO3-is lower in lichen biomass located in the South and East sectors while higher in the North and West sectors of Las Vegas Valley. It is possible that the South and East sectors with elevated copper, perhaps, are a contributing factor to lower NO3- in lichen biomass. 

4.2.1.6.5. Water pollution
Pollutants are a major driver of species declines in freshwater systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0aIFMPDb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dudgeon, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Dudgeon, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33119,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDGJYFPW"],"itemData":{"id":33119,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Reviews of the Cambridge Philosophical Society","language":"en","page":"163–82","title":"Freshwater biodiversity: importance, threats, status and conservation challenges","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"Dudgeon","given":"D."},{"family":"Arthington","given":"A.H."},{"family":"Gessner","given":"M.O."},{"family":"Kawabata","given":"Z."},{"family":"Knowler","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Lévêque","given":"C."},{"family":"Naiman","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Prieur-Richard","given":"A.H."},{"family":"Soto","given":"D."},{"family":"Stiassny","given":"M.L.J."},{"family":"Sullivan","given":"C.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Water pollution is brought about by various sources such as domestic and industrial sewage, agricultural runoff, waste dumping, oil spills, sediment runoff etc. Water pollutants have been found to be lethal to fish and other aquatic fauna or cause a range of sub-lethal effects, such as physiological stress, dysfunction of iono-regulatory and immune system, histopathological deformities, change in population dynamics and community structure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qriCvwiX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Luebke, 1997; Ozmen, 2008; Sodergren, 1992)","plainCitation":"(Luebke, 1997; Ozmen, 2008; Sodergren, 1992)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33222,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J6U6A8RN"],"itemData":{"id":33222,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fundam. Appl","language":"it","page":"1–15","title":"Aquatic pollution-induced immunotoxicity in Wildlife Species","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Luebke","given":"R.W."},{"family":"Hodson","given":"P.V."},{"family":"Faisal","given":"M."},{"family":"Ross","given":"P.S."},{"family":"Grasman","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Zelikoff","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}},{"id":33327,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZIVD59IG"],"itemData":{"id":33327,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecotoxicology and Environmental Safety","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecoenv.2007.05.011","ISSN":"01476513","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecotoxicology and Environmental Safety","language":"en","page":"163-173","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ecotoxicological assessment of water pollution in Sariyar Dam Lake, Turkey","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S014765130700111X","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Ozmen","given":"Murat"},{"family":"Ayas","given":"Zafer"},{"family":"Güngördü","given":"Abbas"},{"family":"Ekmekci","given":"Guler F."},{"family":"Yerli","given":"Sedat"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",5]]}}},{"id":33378,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6ZHRTAF3"],"itemData":{"id":33378,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Proceedings, International Conference on the Fate and Effects of Pulp Mill Effluents","event-place":"Swedish EPA, Stockholm","language":"en","publisher-place":"Swedish EPA, Stockholm","title":"Environmental effect of bleached pulp mill effluents discharged into the Baltic Sea","editor":[{"family":"Sodergren","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Acidification
Acid precipitation and consequent acidification of waterbodies is a major cause of concern for wild aquatic bioresources and their sustainable use with huge economic impacts (as estimated recently in UK by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8wD6xKlq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mangi, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Mangi, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33326,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TPLKCMLL"],"itemData":{"id":33326,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science & Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.envsci.2018.05.008","ISSN":"14629011","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Science & Policy","language":"en","page":"95-105","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The economic impacts of ocean acidification on shellfish fisheries and aquaculture in the United Kingdom","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1462901117311528","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Mangi","given":"Stephen C."},{"family":"Lee","given":"Jeo"},{"family":"Pinnegar","given":"John K."},{"family":"Law","given":"Robin J."},{"family":"Tyllianakis","given":"Emmanouil"},{"family":"Birchenough","given":"Silvana N.R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, for instance). ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kBhO6bmJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ndubuisi 2015)","plainCitation":"(Ndubuisi 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33408,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UQMCRPDX"],"itemData":{"id":33408,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Research in Biosciences","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"14–20","title":"Effect of pH on the growth performance and survival rate of Clarias gariepinus fry","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Ndubuisi","given":"C.U."},{"family":"Chimezie","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Chinedu","given":"C.U."},{"family":"Chikwem","given":"C.I."},{"family":"Alexander","given":"U."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} reported 100% mortality of fingerlings of Clarais gariepinus at pH 3. Acidification, in conjecture with other drivers such climate change, ultraviolet radiation radiations etc. has serious impacts on phytoplankton ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iHHVe6F8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bach, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Bach, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33279,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SQPNJS2"],"itemData":{"id":33279,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0188198","language":"en","page":"–22","title":"Simulated ocean acidification reveals winners and losers in coastal phytoplankton PLOS","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Bach","given":"L.T."},{"family":"Alvarez-Fernandez","given":"S."},{"family":"Hornick","given":"T."},{"literal":"Stuhr"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, macrophytes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c1o03aK4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. T. Jackson & Charles, 1988; Tucker, 2021)","plainCitation":"(S. T. Jackson & Charles, 1988; Tucker, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33308,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NKK5GPPR"],"itemData":{"id":33308,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Can. J. Bot","language":"en","page":"1449–1460","title":"Aquatic macrophytes in Adirondack (New York) lakes: patterns of species composition in relation to environment","volume":"66","author":[{"family":"Jackson","given":"S.T."},{"family":"Charles","given":"D.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1988"]]}}},{"id":33363,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AHH4QJYR"],"itemData":{"id":33363,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Too Little pH: How Freshwater Acidification Impacts the Abundance of Macrophytes Consumed by Rusty Crayfish","URL":"https://scholarworks.bgsu.edu/honorsprojects/594","author":[{"family":"Tucker","given":"Lauren"},{"family":"Moore","given":"Paul Dr"},{"family":"Jones","given":"Jay Mr"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; and amphibians ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KvICU34D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alton & Franklin, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Alton & Franklin, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33278,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BINKS7MN"],"itemData":{"id":33278,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Clim Chang Responses","DOI":"10.1186/s40665-017-0034-7","language":"en","title":"Drivers of amphibian declines: effects of ultraviolet radiation and interactions with other environmental factors","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s40665-017-0034-7","volume":"4, 6","author":[{"family":"Alton","given":"L.A."},{"family":"Franklin","given":"C.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. According to United States of America Fish and Wildlife Service sources around 3,000 to 4,000 birds such as snow geese perished in December 2016 in the Berkeley Pit’s toxic water due to heavy metals and sulfuric acid. In Hong Kong, the Mai Po and Inner Deep Bay ecosystem is under threat from range of contaminants, especially high levels of chlorinated pesticides in marine sediments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qsOb99V3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. J. Richardson and Zheng 1999; B. J. Richardson, Lam, and R.S.S. 2000)","plainCitation":"(B. J. Richardson and Zheng 1999; B. J. Richardson, Lam, and R.S.S. 2000)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33406,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/38JYS6GT"],"itemData":{"id":33406,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Chemosphere","language":"it","page":"913–23","title":"Chlorinated hydrocarbon contaminants in Hong Kong surficial sediment","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Richardson","given":"B.J."},{"family":"Zheng","given":"G.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":33401,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8L4VT7W6"],"itemData":{"id":33401,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Seas at the Millennium : an Environmental Evaluation","language":"en","page":"535–47","publisher":"Elsevier Science Press","title":"The coast of Hong Kong","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Richardson","given":"B.J."},{"family":"Lam","given":"PKSand Wu"},{"literal":"R.S.S."}],"editor":[{"family":"Sheppard","given":"Charles"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The lower reaches of the Pearl River, which drain into Deep Bay receive 2 million tonnes of various types of wastes and wastewater annually, and are heavily polluted by domestic, industrial and livestock waste, and agrochemicals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Mw4Jn3wu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Neller & Lam, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Neller & Lam, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33332,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DJYI8X8Z"],"itemData":{"id":33332,"type":"chapter","edition":"Y.M. Yeung and D.K.Y. Chu","title":"The Environment. In Guangdong: Survey of a ProvinceUndergoing Rapid Change, pp. 401±28. Hong Kong: TheChinese University Pres","author":[{"family":"Neller","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Lam","given":"K.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. River Nile from Aswan to Cairo involved severe polluted points resulting from sewage drains, industrial and agricultural sources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6KFdghwQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fishar, Kamel, and Wissa 2003; MRA and WP 2006)","plainCitation":"(Fishar, Kamel, and Wissa 2003; MRA and WP 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33298,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GYRBDHXC"],"itemData":{"id":33298,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"J Egypt Acad Environ","language":"en","page":"83–100","title":"Effect of discharged water from Shoubra El-Khima electric power station into the River Nile (Egypt) on the aquatic annelids","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Fishar","given":"M.R.A."},{"family":"Kamel","given":"E.G."},{"family":"Wissa","given":"J.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":33300,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T7LQXD3R"],"itemData":{"id":33300,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Hydrobiologia.;556:137–147","language":"en","title":"A feasibility study to monitor the macroinvertebrate diversity of the River Nile using three sampling methods","author":[{"family":"MRA","given":"Fishar"},{"family":"WP","given":"Williams"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} that resulted in reduced richness of wild taxa. Pollution together with monoculture palm oil plantation in Borneo, Malaysia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bbs7lzPr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zieritz, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Zieritz, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33370,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6J3YXCWP"],"itemData":{"id":33370,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biol. Invasions","DOI":"10.1007/s10530-","language":"en","page":"269–282","title":"Changes in pathways and vectors of biological invasions in Northwest Europe","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Zieritz","given":"A."},{"family":"Gallardo","given":"B."},{"family":"Baker","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Britton","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Valkenburg","given":"J.L.C.H."},{"family":"Verreycken","given":"H."},{"family":"Aldridge","given":"D.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} reduces the diversity of useful wild aquatic species with serious implications for livelihoods.  

Oil spills
Petroleum hydrocarbons are considered as hazardous wastes and the most frequent organic pollutants of aquatic ecosystems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"94CwEKId","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R and Schinnur 1997)","plainCitation":"(R and Schinnur 1997)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33226,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QLNCLEA9"],"itemData":{"id":33226,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Applied and En? vironmental Microbiology","language":"en","page":"2660–2664","title":"Efficiency of Endogenous and Inoculated Cold-adapted Soil Microorganisms for Biodegradation of Diesel Oil in Alpine Soils’","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"R","given":"Margesin"},{"family":"Schinnur","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The ingestion of oil by some wild aquatic species often causes mortality, while surviving organisms often show developmental and reproductive abnormalities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"C1eEFnck","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jiang, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Jiang, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33246,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9ZNW4HRT"],"itemData":{"id":33246,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Acta Ecologica Sinica","language":"en","page":"8–15","title":"Advance in the Toxic Effects of Petroleum Water Accommodated Fraction on Marine Plankton’","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Jiang","given":"Z."},{"family":"Huang","given":"Y."},{"family":"Xu","given":"X."},{"family":"Liao","given":"Y."},{"family":"Shou","given":"L."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Chen","given":"Q."},{"family":"Zeng","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The aquatic organisms that live within and around the coral reefs are at risk of exposure to the toxic substances within oil, as well as smothering thereby suffering significant changes in diversity, species abundance and habitat structure worldwide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nnrF2hNE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. P. Hughes 2007)","plainCitation":"(T. P. Hughes 2007)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33291,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ACBB3IJK"],"itemData":{"id":33291,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Biology","DOI":"10.1016/j.cub.2006.12.049","ISSN":"09609822","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Current Biology","language":"en","page":"360-365","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Phase Shifts, Herbivory, and the Resilience of Coral Reefs to Climate Change","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0960982207008822","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Hughes","given":"Terence P."},{"family":"Rodrigues","given":"Maria J."},{"family":"Bellwood","given":"David R."},{"family":"Ceccarelli","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Hoegh-Guldberg","given":"Ove"},{"family":"McCook","given":"Laurence"},{"family":"Moltschaniwskyj","given":"Natalie"},{"family":"Pratchett","given":"Morgan S."},{"family":"Steneck","given":"Robert S."},{"family":"Willis","given":"Bette"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In a study using coral nubbins in coral reef ecotoxicology testing, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Dg5qsi68","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shafir, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Shafir, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33373,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8UWITGID"],"itemData":{"id":33373,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biomolocule Engineering","language":"en","page":"401–406","title":"The Use of Coral Nubbins in Coral Reef Ecotoxi? cology Testing’","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Shafir","given":"S."},{"family":"Rijn","given":"J.V."},{"family":"Rinkevich","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found that dispersed oil and oil dispersants are harmful to soft and hard coral species at early life stages. They found that the dispersant concentrations recommended by the manufacturer were highly toxic and resulted in mortality of all nubbins. 

Eutrophication and Agricultural Runoff
Nutrient pollution from improper and excessive fertilizer use has several negative consequences for ecosystems. Of the 63 large marine ecosystems evaluated under the Transboundary Waters Assessment Programme, 16% of the ecosystems are in the “high” or “highest” risk categories for coastal eutrophication due to nutrient run-off16 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xOS0oNBe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(ECOSOC, 2017)","plainCitation":"(ECOSOC, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33366,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z77X65WH"],"itemData":{"id":33366,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"UN Economic and Social Council","language":"en","title":"Report of the Secretary-General, \\"Progress towards the Sustainable Development Goals\\" E/2017/66","author":[{"family":"ECOSOC","given":"U.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. African aquatic ecosystems are already suffering the wrath of application of pesticides upstream ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lLylNjt7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hecky, 2006; Odada, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Hecky, 2006; Odada, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33214,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XIP5ZAH7"],"itemData":{"id":33214,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of the human environment","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"13–23","title":"Mitigation of environmental problems in Lake Victoria, East Africa: Causal chain and policy options analyses. A mbio: A","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Odada","given":"E.O."},{"family":"Olago","given":"D.O."},{"family":"Kulindwa","given":"K."},{"family":"Ntiba","given":"M."},{"family":"Wandiga","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":33243,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5V3LI7KZ"],"itemData":{"id":33243,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Lakes and Reservoirs. Research and Management","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"203–213","title":"African lake management initiatives: The global connection","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Hecky","given":"R.E."},{"family":"Bootsma","given":"H.A."},{"family":"Odada","given":"E.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Toxic levels of pesticides capable of altering  health of aquatic organisms have been found in a number of lakes and rivers in Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gG1RAonQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mugachia, Kanja, and Gitau 1992; Kidd 2001; Ezemonye and Ikpesu, n.d.; Okeniyia 2009; Kohler and Triebskorn 2013)","plainCitation":"(Mugachia, Kanja, and Gitau 1992; Kidd 2001; Ezemonye and Ikpesu, n.d.; Okeniyia 2009; Kohler and Triebskorn 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33220,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HE53IWCH"],"itemData":{"id":33220,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bulletin of Environmental Contamination and Toxicology","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"207–210","title":"Organochlorine pesticide-residues in fsh from Lake Naivasha and Tana River, Kenya","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Mugachia","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Kanja","given":"L."},{"literal":"Gitau"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}},{"id":33251,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SYR9LSNH"],"itemData":{"id":33251,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science and Technology","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"14–20","title":"Biomagnifcation of DDT through he benthic and pelagic food webs of Lake Malawi, East Africa: Importance of trophic level and carbon source","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Kidd","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Bootsma","given":"H.A."},{"family":"Hesslein","given":"R.H."},{"family":"Muir","given":"D.C.G."},{"family":"Hecky","given":"R.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":33260,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DYKY9ZEX"],"itemData":{"id":33260,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Archives of Industrial Hygiene and Toxicology","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"261–270","title":"& Tongo, I.(2008). Distribution of Lindane in water, sediment, and fsh from the Warri River of the Niger Delta, Nigeria","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Ezemonye","given":"L."},{"family":"Ikpesu","given":"T."}]}},{"id":33216,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XVLCBVFM"],"itemData":{"id":33216,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Electronic Journal of Environmental, Agricultural and Food Chemistry","issue":"12","language":"en","page":"1269–1274","title":"Distribution of organochlorine and polychlorinated pesticide residue in water bodies of some rivers in Northern Nigeria","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Okeniyia","given":"S.O."},{"family":"Egwaikhideb","given":"P.A."},{"family":"Akporhonorc","given":"E.E."},{"family":"Obazed","given":"I.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":33252,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6BUBFRAC"],"itemData":{"id":33252,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","language":"en","page":"759–765","title":"Wildlife ecotoxicology of pesticides: can we track effects to the population level and beyond?","volume":"341","author":[{"family":"Kohler","given":"H.R."},{"family":"Triebskorn","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Along coastlines, rivers’ low oxygen levels and hypoxic “dead zones” are due to large nitrogen and phosphorus loads draining from fertilized agricultural watersheds, or from sewage and atmospheric nitrogen deposition ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Li5psH2C","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. J. Diaz & Rosenberg, 2008; Rabalais, 2014; Schmidtko, 2017)","plainCitation":"(R. J. Diaz & Rosenberg, 2008; Rabalais, 2014; Schmidtko, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34526,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8J88WYPG"],"itemData":{"id":34526,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Dead zones in the coastal oceans have spread exponentially since the 1960s and have serious consequences for ecosystem functioning. The formation of dead zones has been exacerbated by the increase in primary production and consequent worldwide coastal eutrophication fueled by riverine runoff of fertilizers and the burning of fossil fuels. Enhanced primary production results in an accumulation of particulate organic matter, which encourages microbial activity and the consumption of dissolved oxygen in bottom waters. Dead zones have now been reported from more than 400 systems, affecting a total area of more than 245,000 square kilometers, and are probably a key stressor on marine ecosystems.","container-title":"Science (New York, N.Y.)","DOI":"10.1126/science.1156401","issue":"5891","page":"926-9","title":"Spreading dead zones and consequences for marine ecosystems.","URL":"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/18703733","volume":"321","author":[{"family":"Diaz","given":"Robert J"},{"family":"Rosenberg","given":"Rutger"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]],"season":"août"}}},{"id":33400,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MERKWHS3"],"itemData":{"id":33400,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oceanography","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"172–183","title":"Eutrophication-driven deoxygenation in the coastal ocean","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Rabalais","given":"N.N."},{"family":"Cai","given":"W.J."},{"family":"Carstensen","given":"J."},{"family":"Conley","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Fry","given":"B."},{"family":"Hu","given":"X."},{"family":"Quinones- Rivera","given":"Z."},{"family":"Rosenberg","given":"R."},{"family":"Slomp","given":"C.P."},{"family":"Turner","given":"R.E."},{"family":"Voss","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":33385,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IAGYM4X3"],"itemData":{"id":33385,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7641","language":"en","page":"335–339","title":"Decline in global oceanic oxygen content during the past five decades","volume":"542","author":[{"family":"Schmidtko","given":"S."},{"family":"Stramma","given":"L."},{"family":"Visbeck","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The dead zone has been significantly expanded as a result of the anthropocentric contributions of nutrients coming from mostly agricultural, municipal, and industrial sources. Nutrient-fed hypoxia is ranked as an important threat to the health of aquatic ecosystems, including oceans ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qFjnVgET","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rockstr\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}m, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Rockström, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33379,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K5G6PQKH"],"itemData":{"id":33379,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","language":"en","page":"472–475","title":"A safe operating space for humanity","volume":"461","author":[{"family":"Rockström","given":"J."},{"family":"Steffen","given":"W."},{"family":"Noone","given":"K."},{"family":"Persson","given":"Å."},{"family":"F.S. Chapin","given":"E.F.Lambin","suffix":"III"},{"family":"Lenton","given":"T.M."},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"M."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"Schellnhuber","given":"H.J."},{"family":"Nykvist","given":"B."},{"family":"Wit","given":"C.A."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"T."},{"family":"Leeuw","given":"S."},{"family":"Rodhe","given":"H."},{"family":"Sörlin","given":"S."},{"family":"Snyder","given":"P.K."},{"family":"Costanza","given":"R."},{"family":"Svedin","given":"U."},{"family":"Falkenmark","given":"M."},{"family":"Karlberg","given":"L."},{"family":"Corell","given":"R.W."},{"family":"Fabry","given":"V.J."},{"family":"Hansen","given":"J."},{"family":"Walker","given":"B."},{"family":"Liverman","given":"D."},{"family":"Richardson","given":"K."},{"family":"Crutzen","given":"P."},{"family":"Foley","given":"J.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Challenging threats to the environment exist in Gulf of Mexico region and chief among them is the seasonal hypoxic or “dead” zone that occurs annually off the coast of Louisiana and Texas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bUYldYAO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. J. Diaz & Rosenberg, 2008)","plainCitation":"(R. J. Diaz & Rosenberg, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34526,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8J88WYPG"],"itemData":{"id":34526,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Dead zones in the coastal oceans have spread exponentially since the 1960s and have serious consequences for ecosystem functioning. The formation of dead zones has been exacerbated by the increase in primary production and consequent worldwide coastal eutrophication fueled by riverine runoff of fertilizers and the burning of fossil fuels. Enhanced primary production results in an accumulation of particulate organic matter, which encourages microbial activity and the consumption of dissolved oxygen in bottom waters. Dead zones have now been reported from more than 400 systems, affecting a total area of more than 245,000 square kilometers, and are probably a key stressor on marine ecosystems.","container-title":"Science (New York, N.Y.)","DOI":"10.1126/science.1156401","issue":"5891","page":"926-9","title":"Spreading dead zones and consequences for marine ecosystems.","URL":"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/18703733","volume":"321","author":[{"family":"Diaz","given":"Robert J"},{"family":"Rosenberg","given":"Rutger"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]],"season":"août"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The presence of a large hypoxic water mass off the coast of Louisiana in mid-summer may concentrate brown shrimp into shallower coastal waters ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"H2cydnfn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Craig, Henwood, and T.A. 2005)","plainCitation":"(Craig, Henwood, and T.A. 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33275,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3PW98XXY"],"itemData":{"id":33275,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Canadian Journal of Fisheries and Aquatic Science","language":"en","page":"1295–1308","title":"Spatial distribution of brown shrimp (Farfantepenaeus aztecus) on the northwestern Gulf of Mexico shelf: effects of abundance and hypoxia","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Craig","given":"J.K.Crowder"},{"family":"Henwood","given":"L.B."},{"literal":"T.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} making them more susceptible to predators, including humans with trawls resulting in increased catches, but the overall productivity of the brown shrimp population is diminished by the removal of these smaller shrimp from further increase in size before capture in farther offshore areas later in the season. 
      Coral bleaching and some mortality in reefs within Bahia Almirante, extensive necrosis of sponges, dead bodies of crustaceans, gastropods, and echinoderms suggested that the extreme stress leading to mortality had occurred and that hypoxia likely excluded consumers that otherwise would have targeted dead and moribund prey ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lffJs79B","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Altieri, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Altieri, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33323,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HTWCP43E"],"itemData":{"id":33323,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology","issue":"10","language":"en","page":"2808–2818","title":"Dead zones enhance key fisheries species by providing predation refuge","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Altieri","given":"A.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.   

Plastic pollution
Plastic pollution in aquatic ecosystems is generating huge impacts. Plastic pollution enters the ocean via rivers, sewage, fishing and other sources. About 90 percent of all the plastic that reaches the world's oceans gets flushed through just 10 rivers: Eight of them are in Asia: the Yangtze; Indus; Yellow; Hai He; Ganges; Pearl; Amur; Mekong; and two in Africa – the Nile and the Niger ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6xYTMtbn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Schmidt, 2017)","plainCitation":"(C. Schmidt, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32447,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KHJPTZBD"],"itemData":{"id":32447,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environ. Sci. Technol","language":"en","page":"12246–12253","title":"Export of Plastic Debris by Rivers into the Sea","volume":"51,( 21","author":[{"family":"Schmidt","given":"C."},{"family":"Krauth","given":"T."},{"family":"Wagner","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Plastics kill or harm biodiversity, from zooplankton to fish, shellfish, sea turtles, seabirds and marine mammals. Impacts on marine wild species include entanglement, ingestion, and contamination to a wide variety of species. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jAciaIBS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. 2019)","plainCitation":"(C. 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32509,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/67DHHD7I"],"itemData":{"id":32509,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Pollution Bulletin","language":"en","page":"93–114","title":"Interactions between anthropogenic litter and birds: A global review with a ‘black-list’ of species","volume":"138","author":[{"family":"C.","given":"Battisti"},{"family":"E.","given":"Staffieri"},{"family":"G.","given":"Poeta"},{"family":"A.","given":"Sorace"},{"family":"L.","given":"Luiselli"},{"family":"G","given":"Amori"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} recently prepared a ‘black-list’ of 258 species impacted by anthropogenic litter and found that most of the species (including 79.8% seabirds) are impacted by ingestion rather than by entanglement. The number of marine species affected by contaminants increased from 247 to 680 within a span of few years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5dWon8Rm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gall & Thompson, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Gall & Thompson, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32367,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AIKFFJRT"],"itemData":{"id":32367,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mar. Pollut. Bull","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpolbul.2014.12.041.","language":"en","page":"170–179","title":"The impact of debris on marine life","volume":"92","author":[{"family":"Gall","given":"S.C."},{"family":"Thompson","given":"R.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Marine plastic pollution is generating impacts on marine biota and ecosystems at many different levels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7D0pFAbt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fossi, 2017; Moore, 2020; Ryan, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Fossi, 2017; Moore, 2020; Ryan, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":894,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2MFGACG3"],"itemData":{"id":894,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2017.00167","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","page":"167","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Plastic Debris Occurrence, Convergence Areas and Fin Whales Feeding Ground in the Mediterranean Marine Protected Area Pelagos Sanctuary: A Modeling Approach","title-short":"Plastic Debris Occurrence, Convergence Areas and Fin Whales Feeding Ground in the Mediterranean Marine Protected Area Pelagos Sanctuary","URL":"http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fmars.2017.00167/full","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Fossi","given":"Maria Cristina"},{"family":"Romeo","given":"Teresa"},{"family":"Baini","given":"Matteo"},{"family":"Panti","given":"Cristina"},{"family":"Marsili","given":"Letizia"},{"family":"Campani","given":"Tommaso"},{"family":"Canese","given":"Simonepietro"},{"family":"Galgani","given":"François"},{"family":"Druon","given":"Jean-Noël"},{"family":"Airoldi","given":"Sabina"},{"family":"Taddei","given":"Stefano"},{"family":"Fattorini","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Brandini","given":"Carlo"},{"family":"Lapucci","given":"Chiara"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",5,31]]}}},{"id":32464,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NUH6RUKK"],"itemData":{"id":32464,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Pollution Bulletin","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpolbul.2019.110723","language":"en","page":"1–18","title":"Microplastics in beluga whales (Delphinapterus leucas) from the Eastern Beaufort Sea","volume":"150","author":[{"family":"Moore","given":"R.C."},{"family":"Loseto","given":"L."},{"family":"Noel","given":"M."},{"family":"Etemadifar","given":"A."},{"family":"Brewster","given":"J.D."},{"family":"MacPhee","given":"S."},{"family":"Bendell","given":"L."},{"family":"Ross","given":"P.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32446,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HTBIN2UY"],"itemData":{"id":32446,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Hazardous Chemicals Associated with Plastics in the Marine Environment. Handbook of Environmental Chemistry","event-place":"Berlin","language":"en","page":"1–32","publisher":"Springer","publisher-place":"Berlin","title":"Ingestion of Plastics by Marine Organisms","author":[{"family":"Ryan","given":"P.G."}],"editor":[{"family":"Takada","given":"H."},{"family":"Karapanagioti","given":"H.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eVpDMtA9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wilcox, Van Sebille, and Hardesty 2015)","plainCitation":"(Wilcox, Van Sebille, and Hardesty 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32433,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9GLGGM5T"],"itemData":{"id":32433,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proc. Nat. Acad. Sci","language":"en","page":"11899–11904","title":"Threat of plastic pollution to seabirds is global, pervasive, and increasing","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Wilcox","given":"C."},{"family":"Van Sebille","given":"E."},{"family":"Hardesty","given":"B.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} suggested that nearly all species of seabirds will eventually be found ingesting plastic. 21 per cent of surveyed wedge-tailed shearwater (Ardenna pacifica) chicks on Heron Island in the southern Great Barrier Reef were fed plastic fragments by their parents, ingesting 3.2 fragments on average ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zUXd9kPW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Verlisa, Campbellb, and S.P. 2013)","plainCitation":"(Verlisa, Campbellb, and S.P. 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32371,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AL2AZEFD"],"itemData":{"id":32371,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Pollution Bulletin","language":"en","page":"244–249","title":"Ingestion of marine debris plastic by the wedge-tailed shearwater Ardenna pacifica in the Great Barrier Reef, Australia","volume":"72, (1","author":[{"family":"Verlisa","given":"K.M."},{"family":"Campbellb","given":"MLandWilsona"},{"literal":"S.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Seabird species feeding at the sea surface are more susceptible to plastic ingestion than diving speciesITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"holZ1hUK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ryan & Jackson, 1987)","plainCitation":"(Ryan & Jackson, 1987)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32426,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PDDDUE59"],"itemData":{"id":32426,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mar Pollut Bull","language":"en","page":"217–219","title":"The lifespan of ingested plastic particles in seabirds and their effect on digestive efficiency","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Ryan","given":"P.G."},{"family":"Jackson","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1987"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Sea turtles are exposed to a variety of anthropogenic stressors, including marine plastic pollution, because of their use of diverse habitats, migratory behavior, and complex life histories ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CWEhhX82","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nelms, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Nelms, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32459,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TY4YE7N2"],"itemData":{"id":32459,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES J. Mar. Sci","language":"it","page":"165–181","title":"Plastic and marine turtles: a review and call for research","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Nelms","given":"S.E."},{"family":"Duncan","given":"E.M."},{"family":"Broderick","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Galloway","given":"T.S."},{"family":"Godfrey","given":"M.H."},{"family":"Hamann","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Procellariiform seabirds and marine turtles may be particular risk because chemicals given off by marine plastic debris may imitate natural foraging stimuli ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kUHZa5G7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pfaller, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Pfaller, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32454,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WWZQ4VQL"],"itemData":{"id":32454,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Biology","language":"en","page":"191–214","title":"Current biology Odors from marine plastic debris elicit foraging behavior in sea turtles","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Pfaller","given":"J.B."},{"family":"Goforth","given":"K.M."},{"family":"Gil","given":"M.A."},{"family":"Savoca","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Lohmann","given":"K.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
Litter ingestion and entanglement in plastic debris have been recognized as serious threats to turtle species worldwide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YyRe9SuL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Clukey, 2017; Duncan, 2017; Nelms, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Clukey, 2017; Duncan, 2017; Nelms, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32459,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TY4YE7N2"],"itemData":{"id":32459,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES J. Mar. Sci","language":"it","page":"165–181","title":"Plastic and marine turtles: a review and call for research","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Nelms","given":"S.E."},{"family":"Duncan","given":"E.M."},{"family":"Broderick","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Galloway","given":"T.S."},{"family":"Godfrey","given":"M.H."},{"family":"Hamann","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32504,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UQAAUZDY"],"itemData":{"id":32504,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mar. Pollut. Bull","language":"en","page":"117–125","title":"Investigation of plastic debris ingestion by four species of sea turtles collected as bycatch in pelagic Pacific longline fisheries","volume":"120","author":[{"family":"Clukey","given":"K.E."},{"family":"Lepczyk","given":"C.A."},{"family":"Balazs","given":"G.H."},{"family":"Work","given":"T.M."},{"family":"Lynch","given":"J.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":32488,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GYY9MKSB"],"itemData":{"id":32488,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Endang. Species. Res","DOI":"10.3354/esr00865","language":"en","page":"431–448","title":"A global review of marine turtle entanglement in anthropogenic debris: a baseline for further action","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Duncan","given":"E.M."},{"family":"Boterelli","given":"L.R."},{"family":"Broderick","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Galloway","given":"T."},{"family":"Lindeque","given":"P.K."},{"family":"Nuno","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Five sea turtle species inhabit the SE Pacific (Caretta caretta, Chelonia mydas, Dermochelys coriacea, Eretmochelys imbricata, and Lepidochelys olivacea); all are listed from vulnerable to critically endangered on the International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FOnIPRXZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32423,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WK4RY2FQ"],"itemData":{"id":32423,"type":"webpage","title":"Red List of Threatened Species","URL":"https://www.iucnredlist.org/search/stats?taxonomies=101666&searchType=species","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} with documented interactions with marine litter. The green turtle (C. mydas) is the species most commonly mentioned to have ingested plastic items, with a frequency ranging from 28% in the Ecuadorian part of the northern Humboldt Current upwelling system ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vtIZCwLr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alem\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}n, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Alemán, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32508,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HRDQR3YV"],"itemData":{"id":32508,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Informe Técnico MAE-PN-PNMRA","language":"es","title":"Informe de Actividades realizadas en Conservación de Tortugas Marinas durante el año 2013","volume":"N?010, 9","author":[{"family":"Alemán","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} to 56 and 91% in Peru ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Xsx4wM34","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alfaro-Shigueto, 2005; Jim\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}nez, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Alfaro-Shigueto, 2005; Jiménez, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32507,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AE6VFQ6J"],"itemData":{"id":32507,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the Twenty-first Annual Symposium on Sea Turtle Biology and Conservation","issue":"4","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Philadelphia, PA\\npublisher: NOAA","page":"2009–2041","title":"Diet analysis from green turtle Chelonia mydas agassizii from central Peruvian Coast","volume":"368","author":[{"family":"Alfaro-Shigueto","given":"J."},{"family":"Montes","given":"D."},{"family":"Acleto","given":"C."},{"family":"Zuñiga","given":"R."},{"family":"Huamán","given":"P."},{"family":"Ali","given":"H."},{"family":"Qaiser","given":"M."}],"editor":[{"family":"Coyne","given":"M."},{"family":"Clark","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32482,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3YWCQR4F"],"itemData":{"id":32482,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Lat. Am. J. Aquat. Res","DOI":"10.3856/vol45-issue3-fulltext-8","language":"en","page":"585–596","title":"Feeding ecology of the green turtle Chelonia mydas in northern Peru","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Jiménez","given":"A."},{"family":"Pingo","given":"S."},{"family":"Alfaro-Shigueto","given":"J."},{"family":"Mangel","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Hooker","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The olive ridley turtle (L. olivacea) also has a high incidence of plastic ingestion, reaching up to 43% in Ecuador ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Bw7G06ig","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alem\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}n, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Alemán, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32508,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HRDQR3YV"],"itemData":{"id":32508,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Informe Técnico MAE-PN-PNMRA","language":"es","title":"Informe de Actividades realizadas en Conservación de Tortugas Marinas durante el año 2013","volume":"N?010, 9","author":[{"family":"Alemán","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but this species has a lower incidence in other parts of the northern Humboldt Current upwelling system (8%), both in Peru and southern Chile ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pwRg6ugd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Brito, 2007; Paz, 2005)","plainCitation":"(C. Brito, 2007; Paz, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32496,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F79Z5V79"],"itemData":{"id":32496,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Proceedings of the Twenty-First Annual Symposium on Sea Turtle Biology and Conservation","event-place":"Clark (Philadelphia, PA","language":"en","publisher":"NOAA","publisher-place":"Clark (Philadelphia, PA","title":"Capture and trade of marine turtles at San Andres, Southern Peru","volume":"368","author":[{"family":"Paz","given":"N."},{"family":"Reyes","given":"J."},{"family":"Echegaray","given":"M."}],"editor":[{"family":"Coyne","given":"M."},{"literal":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32314,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PIYS59U5"],"itemData":{"id":32314,"type":"chapter","container-title":"VII Simposio Sobre Medio Ambiente: Estado Actual y Perspectivas de la Investigación y Conservación de las Tortugas Marinas en las Costas del Pacífico Sur Oriental","event-place":"Antofagasta","language":"es","publisher-place":"Antofagasta","title":"Tortugas marinas en el centro de rescate y rehabilitación de fauna silvestre del museo de San Antonio","author":[{"family":"Brito","given":"C."},{"family":"Brito","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Zúñiga","given":"M."},{"family":"Campos","given":"M."},{"family":"Toro","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Furthermore, specific cases of plastic ingestion have been reported for leatherback turtles (D. coriacea) from the northern Humboldt Current upwelling system in southern Peru and central Chile ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"i9mNwFln","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. L. Brito 2001)","plainCitation":"(J. L. Brito 2001)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32512,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PUQCSZGS"],"itemData":{"id":32512,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Taller Nacional de Trabajo Para Definir las Líneas de Acción Prioritarias de un Programa Para la Conservación de las Tortugas Marinas (Valparaíso), 95","language":"es","title":"Informe preliminar de tortugas marinas en Chile: su situación actual","author":[{"family":"Brito","given":"J.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and a hawksbill turtle (E. imbricata) in Rapa Nui ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EoUWrZNf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brain, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Brain, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32510,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B5RBEFVU"],"itemData":{"id":32510,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"First rehabilitation case of hawksbill turtle (Eretmochelys imbricata","author":[{"family":"Brain","given":"M.J."},{"family":"Medrano","given":"C."},{"family":"Brito-Carrasco","given":"B."},{"family":"Del Río","given":"J."},{"family":"Álvarez-Varas","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Items most commonly found in stomachs or intestines of sea turtles are plastic pieces of intermediate size, including plastic bags, monofilament nylon, rope, and fishing nets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Imko8woM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. L. Brito, 2001; Guerra-Correa, 2007; Jim\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}nez, 2017)","plainCitation":"(J. L. Brito, 2001; Guerra-Correa, 2007; Jiménez, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32512,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PUQCSZGS"],"itemData":{"id":32512,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Taller Nacional de Trabajo Para Definir las Líneas de Acción Prioritarias de un Programa Para la Conservación de las Tortugas Marinas (Valparaíso), 95","language":"es","title":"Informe preliminar de tortugas marinas en Chile: su situación actual","author":[{"family":"Brito","given":"J.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":32478,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UVDBBWCV"],"itemData":{"id":32478,"type":"chapter","container-title":"VII Simposio Sobre Medio Ambiente: Estado Actual y Perspectivas de la Investigación y Conservación de las Tortugas Marinas en las Costas del Pacífico","event-place":"Sur Oriental (Antofagasta","language":"es","publisher-place":"Sur Oriental (Antofagasta","title":"de los desechos plásticos en la sobrevivencia de tortugas: el caso de Chelonia mydas en Antofagasta","author":[{"family":"Guerra-Correa","given":"C."},{"family":"Valenzuela","given":"A."},{"family":"Retamal","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Malinarich","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":32482,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3YWCQR4F"],"itemData":{"id":32482,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Lat. Am. J. Aquat. Res","DOI":"10.3856/vol45-issue3-fulltext-8","language":"en","page":"585–596","title":"Feeding ecology of the green turtle Chelonia mydas in northern Peru","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Jiménez","given":"A."},{"family":"Pingo","given":"S."},{"family":"Alfaro-Shigueto","given":"J."},{"family":"Mangel","given":"J.C."},{"family":"Hooker","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Several authors suggested that plastic ingestion has been the cause of death of stranded turtles in Ecuador and Chile ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ytSnI3gy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Brito 2007; Silva, Retamal, and Guerra-Correa 2007; Alem\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}n 2014)","plainCitation":"(C. Brito 2007; Silva, Retamal, and Guerra-Correa 2007; Alemán 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32314,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PIYS59U5"],"itemData":{"id":32314,"type":"chapter","container-title":"VII Simposio Sobre Medio Ambiente: Estado Actual y Perspectivas de la Investigación y Conservación de las Tortugas Marinas en las Costas del Pacífico Sur Oriental","event-place":"Antofagasta","language":"es","publisher-place":"Antofagasta","title":"Tortugas marinas en el centro de rescate y rehabilitación de fauna silvestre del museo de San Antonio","author":[{"family":"Brito","given":"C."},{"family":"Brito","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Zúñiga","given":"M."},{"family":"Campos","given":"M."},{"family":"Toro","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":32443,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KPJZP5F3"],"itemData":{"id":32443,"type":"chapter","container-title":"VII Simposio sobre Medio Ambiente: Estado Actual y Perspectivas de la Investigación y Conservación de las Tortugas Marinas en las Costas del Pacífico","event-place":"Sur Oriental (Antofagasta","language":"es","publisher-place":"Sur Oriental (Antofagasta","title":"Registro de tortugas marinas ingresadas al centro de rescate y rehabilitación de fauna silvestre","author":[{"family":"Silva","given":"A."},{"family":"Retamal","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Guerra-Correa","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":32508,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HRDQR3YV"],"itemData":{"id":32508,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Informe Técnico MAE-PN-PNMRA","language":"es","title":"Informe de Actividades realizadas en Conservación de Tortugas Marinas durante el año 2013","volume":"N?010, 9","author":[{"family":"Alemán","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many different seabird species have been reported to be entangled in marine debris or have ingested plastic ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ESYzPYr2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Luna-Jorquera, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Luna-Jorquera, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32471,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2R55LBLB"],"itemData":{"id":32471,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodivers. Conserv","language":"en","page":"13–32","title":"Determinants of the diversity of plants, birds and mammals of coastal islands of the Humboldt current systems: implications for conservation","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Luna-Jorquera","given":"G."},{"family":"Fernández","given":"C.E."},{"family":"Rivadeneira","given":"M.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Six species were found in which plastic litter has been found in their stomachs, 3 of them (Pelecanoides garnotii, P. urinatrix, Phalacrocorax bougainvillii, and Spheniscus humboldti) being true diving species, and one a plunge diver (Pelecanus thagus). One species with relatively high frequency of plastic ingestion is the kelp gull Larus dominicanus, which is commonly observed feeding in fishing ports, at garbage containers, and on waste disposal facilities. Ingestion of microplastics is being reported in a number of marine invertebrate species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NjpWh6CO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cole 2013; C. J. Foley 2018; Wright, Thompson, and Galloway 2013)","plainCitation":"(Cole 2013; C. J. Foley 2018; Wright, Thompson, and Galloway 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32503,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XWNPTSZ4"],"itemData":{"id":32503,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environ. Sci. Technol","language":"en","page":"6646–6655","title":"Microplastic ingestion by zooplankton","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Cole","given":"M."},{"family":"Lindeque","given":"P."},{"family":"Fileman","given":"E."},{"family":"Halsband","given":"C."},{"family":"Goodhead","given":"R."},{"family":"Moger","given":"J."},{"family":"Galloway","given":"T.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":32362,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JQHBC95C"],"itemData":{"id":32362,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sci. Total Environ","language":"en","page":"550–559","title":"A meta-analysis of the effects of exposure to microplastics on fish and aquatic invertebrates","volume":"631–632","author":[{"family":"Foley","given":"C.J."},{"family":"Feiner","given":"Z.S."},{"family":"Malinich","given":"T.D."},{"family":"Höök","given":"T.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32434,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/82Z4BM78"],"itemData":{"id":32434,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environ. Pollut","language":"en","page":"483–492","title":"The physical impacts of microplastics on marine organisms: A review","volume":"178","author":[{"family":"Wright","given":"S.L."},{"family":"Thompson","given":"R.C."},{"family":"Galloway","given":"T.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Several harmful effects have been reported due to microplastic ingestion, ranging from stomach ulcers, intestinal obstruction, reduced body condition, and increased contaminant load ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4uyZwsmn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Derraik, 2002; Lavers, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Derraik, 2002; Lavers, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SDAGQV6D"],"itemData":{"id":32493,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mar. Pollut. Bull","language":"en","page":"842–852","title":"The pollution of the marine environment by plastic debris: a review","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Derraik","given":"J.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":32486,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IN9DSYY8"],"itemData":{"id":32486,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environ. Pollut","language":"en","page":"124–129","title":"Plastic ingestion by flesh-footed shearwaters (Puffinus carneipes): implications for fledgling body condition and the accumulation of plastic-derived chemicals","volume":"187","author":[{"family":"Lavers","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Bond","given":"A.L."},{"family":"Hutton","given":"I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Notwithstanding some local scale efforts to check the plastic influx into the aquatic ecosystems, the volume of marine plastic debris, for instance, is increasing at an alarming rate of 4.8 to 12.7 million metric tons every year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vBSdsvYZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jambeck, n.d.)","plainCitation":"(Jambeck, n.d.)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32481,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YIHUEZAS"],"itemData":{"id":32481,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","language":"en","page":"768–771","title":"Plastic waste inputs from land into the ocean","volume":"347(6223","author":[{"family":"Jambeck","given":"J."},{"family":"Gever","given":"R."},{"family":"Wilcox","given":"C."},{"family":"Siegler","given":"T."},{"family":"Perryman","given":"M."},{"family":"Andrady","given":"A."},{"family":"Narayan","given":"R."},{"family":"Lavender Law","given":"K."}]}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The polychlorinated biphenyls threaten long-term viability of >50% of the world’s Killer whales’ (Orcinus orca) populations with strong impacts on reproduction and immune function ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6xXFn1bA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Desforges, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Desforges, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32495,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/64HIQDJB"],"itemData":{"id":32495,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","language":"en","page":"1373","title":"Predicting global killer whale population collapse from PCB pollution","volume":"361","author":[{"family":"Desforges","given":"J.P."},{"family":"Hall","given":"A."},{"family":"McConnell","given":"B."},{"family":"Rosing-Asvid","given":"A."},{"family":"Barber","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Brownlow","given":"A."},{"family":"De Guise","given":"S."},{"family":"Eulaers","given":"I."},{"family":"Jepson","given":"P.D."},{"family":"Letcher","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Levin","given":"M."},{"family":"Ross","given":"P.S."},{"family":"Samarra","given":"F."},{"family":"Víkingson","given":"G."},{"family":"Sonne","given":"C."},{"family":"Dietz","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is despite a near global ban of polychlorinated biphenyls over more than past three decades. Recent molecular investigation in mangroves revealed low effective population size in polluted sites than in protected areas, though higher geneflow helps them counteract the effect of pollution on genetic diversity and differentiation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uNTFvTXV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rumisha, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Rumisha, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32445,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PLDFJKDV"],"itemData":{"id":32445,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","language":"en","page":"96–104","title":"Genetic diversity and geneflow among the giant mud crabs (Scylla serrata)in anthropogenic-polluted mangroves of mainland Tanzania: Implications for conservation","volume":"205","author":[{"family":"Rumisha","given":"C."},{"family":"H.M.","given":"Robinson"},{"family":"S.G.","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Marc","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Whether hunting waterfowl is a sustainable use or not is not clear. While in many countries game hunting is considered as sustainable use of waterfowl species, in Argentinian context, for instance, this is not considered so due to many reasons ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"C4MBe95m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Uhart, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Uhart, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32431,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R6KPTX85"],"itemData":{"id":32431,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ambio","language":"en","page":"1015–1022","title":"Lead pollution from hunting ammunition in Argentina and current state of lead shot replacement efforts","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Uhart","given":"M."},{"family":"Ferreyra","given":"Hd"},{"family":"Romano","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The use of lead ammunition, questionable hunting quotas, lack of information on waterfowl population status, breeding sites, etc. are some of the important concerns in this regard. The aforementioned evidence substantiates the significant impact of pollution on survival, dynamics and sustainable use of wild species that merits urgent policy intervention and management action.  

4.2.1.6.6. Soil pollution
Various studies have shown that excessive use of pesticides and insecticides lead to loss of wild species and cause ecosystem degradation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"r6JIrpyj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. E. Green, 2005; Kleijn, 2009; Rundl\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}f, 2015)","plainCitation":"(R. E. Green, 2005; Kleijn, 2009; Rundlöf, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32479,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I46XAHD4"],"itemData":{"id":32479,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"5709","language":"en","title":"Farming and the Fate of Wild Nature","volume":"307","author":[{"family":"Green","given":"Rhys E."},{"family":"Cornell","given":"Stephen J."},{"family":"Scharlemann","given":"Jörn P.W."},{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32483,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BPVIL8NG"],"itemData":{"id":32483,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"Proceedings of the Royal Society of","event-place":"London B","language":"en","page":"276","publisher":"Biological Sciences","publisher-place":"London B","title":"On the relationship between farmland biodiversity and land-use intensity in Europe","URL":"http://rspb.royalsocietypublishing.org/content/276/1658/903","author":[{"family":"Kleijn","given":"D."},{"family":"Kohler","given":"F."},{"family":"Báldi","given":"A."},{"family":"Batáry","given":"P."},{"family":"Concepción","given":"E.D."},{"family":"Clough","given":"Y."},{"family":"Díaz","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":895,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y384KUPW"],"itemData":{"id":895,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/nature14420","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7550","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"77-80","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Seed coating with a neonicotinoid insecticide negatively affects wild bees","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nature14420","volume":"521","author":[{"family":"Rundlöf","given":"Maj"},{"family":"Andersson","given":"Georg K. S."},{"family":"Bommarco","given":"Riccardo"},{"family":"Fries","given":"Ingemar"},{"family":"Hederström","given":"Veronica"},{"family":"Herbertsson","given":"Lina"},{"family":"Jonsson","given":"Ove"},{"family":"Klatt","given":"Björn K."},{"family":"Pedersen","given":"Thorsten R."},{"family":"Yourstone","given":"Johanna"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Henrik G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",5,7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In particular, pesticide use has contributed to reducing populations of birds, insects, amphibians and aquatic and soil communities, either through direct exposure or reduction in food and habitat availability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qJxSzA0p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hallmann, 2014; Kennedy, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Hallmann, 2014; Kennedy, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32484,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QH4ZA3VR"],"itemData":{"id":32484,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology Letters","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"584–599","title":".\\"A global quantitative synthesis of local and landscape effects on wild bee pollinators in agroecosystems","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Kennedy","given":"Christina M."},{"family":"Lonsdorf","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Neel","given":"Maile C."},{"family":"Williams","given":"Neal M."},{"family":"Ricketts","given":"Taylor H."},{"family":"Winfree","given":"Rachael"},{"family":"Bommarco","given":"Riccardo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":32480,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T84X6C27"],"itemData":{"id":32480,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/nature13531.","issue":"7509","language":"en","page":"341–343","title":"Declines in insectivorous birds are associated with high neonicotinoid concentrations","volume":"511","author":[{"family":"Hallmann","given":"Caspar A."},{"family":"Foppen","given":"Ruud P.B."},{"family":"Turnhout","given":"Chris A.M."},{"family":"Kroon","given":"Hans"},{"family":"Jongejans","given":"Eelke"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A Europe-wide study found that insecticide and fungicide use have consistent negative effects on wild species diversity and that insecticides also reduce the potential for biological pest control ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7SSWkVyA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. Geiger, 2010)","plainCitation":"(F. Geiger, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32369,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8HQXD4EQ"],"itemData":{"id":32369,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Basic and Applied Ecology","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"97–105","title":"Persistent negative effects of pesticides on biodiversity and biological control potential on European farmland","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Geiger","given":"F."},{"family":"Bengtsson","given":"J."},{"family":"Berendse","given":"F."},{"family":"Weisser","given":"W.W."},{"family":"Emmerson","given":"M."},{"family":"Morales","given":"M.B."},{"family":"Eggers","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indirect effects of pesticides have been identified as one of the main causes of decline in farmland birds in several European countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nohgoNrj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Donald, 2001; F. Geiger, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Donald, 2001; F. Geiger, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z9UNETPX"],"itemData":{"id":32497,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"page: 25–29\\ncollection-title: Proceedings of the Royal Society of London Series B-Biological Sciences\\nvolume: 268","title":"Agricultural intensification and the collapse of Europe’s farmland bird populations","author":[{"family":"Donald","given":"P.F."},{"family":"Green","given":"R.E."},{"family":"Heath","given":"M.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":32369,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8HQXD4EQ"],"itemData":{"id":32369,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Basic and Applied Ecology","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"97–105","title":"Persistent negative effects of pesticides on biodiversity and biological control potential on European farmland","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Geiger","given":"F."},{"family":"Bengtsson","given":"J."},{"family":"Berendse","given":"F."},{"family":"Weisser","given":"W.W."},{"family":"Emmerson","given":"M."},{"family":"Morales","given":"M.B."},{"family":"Eggers","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This decline is reflected in the falling trends for farmland bird index in several Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development countries. Direct toxicity of nitrogen gases, ozone and aerosols, increased nitrogen availability and soil-dependent acidification in terrestrial systems lead to reduced plant diversity in wild ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eKmNYV4O","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bobbink, 2010; Valliere, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Bobbink, 2010; Valliere, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32370,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A3S7IYDV"],"itemData":{"id":32370,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Global Change Biology","title":"High N, dry: Experimental nitrogen deposition exacerbates native shrub loss and nonnative plant invasion during extreme drought","author":[{"family":"Valliere","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Irvine","given":"I.C."},{"family":"Santiago","given":"L."},{"family":"Allen","given":"E.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":32511,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QVL3TLW3"],"itemData":{"id":32511,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological applications","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"30–59","title":"Global assessment of nitrogen deposition effects on terrestrial plant diversity: a synthesis","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Bobbink","given":"R."},{"family":"Hicks","given":"K."},{"family":"Galloway","given":"J."},{"family":"Spranger","given":"T."},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"R."},{"family":"Ashmore","given":"M."},{"family":"Bustamante","given":"M."},{"family":"Cinderby","given":"S."},{"family":"Davidson","given":"E."},{"family":"Dentener","given":"F."},{"family":"Emmett","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Industrial – based soil contaminants are of growing concern because of the increase in ownership of motor vehicles, mining, and industries in general. Vehicular exhaust pollutants comprising polyaromatic hydrocarbons and tetraethyl lead (which is now in decline due to conversion to unleaded fuel) are deposited on the ground along the motorways and are increasing quantities of toxic metals deposited on the ground ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dEB2jVPx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Davies & Osano, 2005; Olade, 1987)","plainCitation":"(Davies & Osano, 2005; Olade, 1987)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32449,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B3DEDQL7"],"itemData":{"id":32449,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Lead, Mercury, Cadmium and arsenic in the environment","language":"en","page":"335– 341","title":"Heavy metal pollution and the need for monitoring: Illustrated for developing countries in West Africa, Chapter 20","author":[{"family":"Olade","given":"M.A."}],"editor":[{"family":"Hutchinson","given":"T.C."},{"family":"Meema","given":"K.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1987"]]}}},{"id":32492,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GZ7QZ9UK"],"itemData":{"id":32492,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geological Society, London, Special Publications","language":"en","page":"87–93","title":"Sustainable mineral development: Case study from Kenya","volume":"250","author":[{"family":"Davies","given":"T.C."},{"family":"Osano","given":"O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A synthesis of literature from Latin America recently ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3pGSrzAK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A, Lambertucci, and A.J, n.d.)","plainCitation":"(A, Lambertucci, and A.J, n.d.)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32463,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DQLJ2Q5I"],"itemData":{"id":32463,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environ Res","language":"en","title":"Martínez-López E.(2019) From Mexico to the Beagle Channel: A review of metal and metalloid pollution studies on wildlife species in Latin America","volume":"176 108462","author":[{"family":"A","given":"Marzio D."},{"family":"Lambertucci","given":"S.A."},{"family":"A.J","given":"Garcia Fernandez"}]}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found quite high levels of metal contamination, primarily emanating from industrial activity, intensive agriculture, and urban contamination with serious implications for wild species such as sharks. 
      It is worth mentioning that hunting with lead ammunition is now the main source of human-induced lead emissions to soil in the Europe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"R1z7OX7e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tukker, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Tukker, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32432,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F9UF4IA7"],"itemData":{"id":32432,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Resources, Conservation and Recycling","language":"en","page":"89–109","title":"Risks to health and environment of the use of lead in products in the EU","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Tukker","given":"A."},{"family":"Buist","given":"H."},{"family":"Oers","given":"L."},{"family":"Voet","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} with potential implications for soil fauna. For instance, wild growing mushrooms, quite prized in view of myriad of human-health benefits, are significantly affected by heavy metals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2Ebq7wz0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. 2021)","plainCitation":"(M. 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32487,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MIEFCILU"],"itemData":{"id":32487,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Food Science & Technology","language":"en","page":"527–535","title":"Heavy metals content in edible mushrooms: A systematic review, meta-analysis and health risk assessment","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"M.","given":"Dowlati"},{"family":"R.H.","given":"Sobhi"},{"family":"Esrafili","given":"Ali"},{"family":"FarzadKia","given":"Mahdi"},{"family":"Yeganeh","given":"Mojtaba"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is well documented that the fruiting bodies of mushrooms have the ability to bioaccumulate heavy metals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QGfMmpTB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garcia 1998; Barua, Saiful Islam Bhuian, and Hossain 2019)","plainCitation":"(Garcia 1998; Barua, Saiful Islam Bhuian, and Hossain 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32366,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5Z8CP4K6"],"itemData":{"id":32366,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Archives of Environmental Contamination and Toxicology","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"330–335","title":"Lead content in edible wild mushrooms in northwest Spain as indicator of environmental contamination","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Garcia","given":"M.A."},{"family":"Alonso","given":"J."},{"family":"Fernández","given":"M.I."},{"family":"Melgar","given":"M.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":887,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VJU792UJ"],"itemData":{"id":887,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bangladesh. J Radioanal Nucl Chem","language":"en","page":"173–182","title":"The presence of radioactive and metal contaminants in wild mushrooms grown in Chattogram hill tracts","volume":"322","author":[{"family":"Barua","given":"T."},{"family":"Saiful Islam Bhuian","given":"A.K.M."},{"family":"Hossain","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} in concentrations far higher than what is found in agricultural crop plants, vegetables, and fruit ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"R1LHcDFh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. Zhu, 2011)","plainCitation":"(F. Zhu, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32436,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9B53BQIN"],"itemData":{"id":32436,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Monitoring and Assessment","issue":"1–4","language":"en","page":"191–199","title":"Assessment of heavy metals in some wild edible mushrooms collected from Yunnan Province, China","volume":"179","author":[{"family":"Zhu","given":"F."},{"family":"Qu","given":"L."},{"family":"Fan","given":"W."},{"family":"Qiao","given":"M."},{"family":"Hao","given":"H."},{"family":"Wang","given":"X."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example , in Yunnan Province, one of the main production areas of wild edible mushrooms in China, the wild edible mushrooms are endangered with various pollutants, especially heavy metals, due to rapid urbanization and industrialization ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rh9iBHSS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Y. Luo, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Y. Luo, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32470,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E8LYFLHI"],"itemData":{"id":32470,"type":"thesis","genre":"(Master’s thesis).","language":"en","publisher":"Yunnan University of Finance and Economics (in Chinese","title":"Study on EKC curves of Yunnan Province and the prefectures and cities in the province","author":[{"family":"Luo","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The concentration of arsenic, cadmium and lead in mushrooms are potentially hazardous. These elements in edible mushrooms may enter the food chain and potentially harm human health. It can be seen from study conducted by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JkAcPCK4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. Liu 2015)","plainCitation":"(B. Liu 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32467,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6MD3VQ4B"],"itemData":{"id":32467,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Food Chemistry","language":"en","page":"294–300","title":"Study of heavy metal concentrations in wild edible mushrooms in Yunnan Province, China","volume":"188","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"B."},{"family":"Huang","given":"Q."},{"family":"Cai","given":"H."},{"family":"Guo","given":"X."},{"family":"Wang","given":"T."},{"family":"Gui","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} that arsenic and lead concentrations in all of the soil samples were below the safe limits and the cadmium concentrations exceeded the safe limit (Samples M6–M11, and Samples M15–M16), indicating that the soil in the study area where the edible mushrooms grew had been significantly contaminated by cadmium. The cause of the contamination might be the industries in the southern region of China ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IK48MoV5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fang, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Fang, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32489,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4XP32ZIK"],"itemData":{"id":32489,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Food Chemistry","language":"en","page":"147–151","title":"Concentrations and health risks of lead, cadmium, arsenic, and mercury in rice and edible mushrooms in China","volume":"147","author":[{"family":"Fang","given":"Y."},{"family":"Sun","given":"X."},{"family":"Yang","given":"W."},{"family":"Ma","given":"N."},{"family":"Xin","given":"Z."},{"family":"Fu","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There is sufficient evidence for the impact of pollution on wild growing mushrooms from across the world. 
      In addition to soil, water and air pollution, the other types of pollution with profound impact of wild species and wild species include noise pollution, light pollution and radioactive pollution. For instance, chronic and acute marine noise pollution produced by several human activities – such as maritime traffic, pile driving, air guns cause detectable effects on intraspecific communication, vital processes, physiology, behavioural patterns, health status and survival of marine species, including some keystone predators and habitat forming species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xi7rjsyy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Di Franco, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Di Franco, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":897,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GTG2LD49"],"itemData":{"id":897,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Pollution Bulletin","language":"en","page":"111450","title":"Effects of marine noise pollution on Mediterranean fishes and invertebrates: A review","volume":"159","author":[{"family":"Di Franco","given":"E."},{"family":"Pierson","given":"P."},{"family":"Di Iorio","given":"L."},{"family":"Calò","given":"A."},{"family":"Cottalorda","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Derijard","given":"B."},{"family":"Guidetti","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These individual-based effects may cascade to ecosystem-wide effects. Moreover, artificial light at night and noise have been found to interact and produce complex and novel effects on model songbird species, thereby pointing to multisensory pollution being a considerable threat to wild species and stress the importance of including both these anthropogenic stressors in future assessments of the ecological effects of urbanization and human activity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VWfZizxT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dominoni, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dominoni, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32498,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MTJ2LLDE"],"itemData":{"id":32498,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Pollution","issue":"6502","language":"en","page":"379–381","title":"Multisensory pollution: artificial light at night and anthropogenic noise have interactive effects on activity patterns of great tits (Parus major","volume":"369","author":[{"family":"Dominoni","given":"D."},{"family":"Smit","given":"J.A.H."},{"family":"Visser","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Halfwerk","given":"W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There is sufficient evidence to show that the pulsed telephony microwave radiation can produce serious ill effects to wild species by way of affecting nervous, cardiovascular, immune and reproductive systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"592RRz5D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Balmori, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Balmori, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":884,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/657MWX7N"],"itemData":{"id":884,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"type: Pathophysiology 16,191–199","title":"Electromagnetic pollution from phone masts, effects on wildlife","author":[{"family":"Balmori","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Therefore, utmost care needs to be exercised in installing such towers and technology in and around the protected areas that inhabit invaluable threatened wild species.
      
4.2.1.7 Environmental hazards
4.2.1.7.1. Overview 
This section reviews environmental hazards, specifically, geological or geophysical hazards that originate from internal earth processes (earthquakes, volcanic activities, landslides, tsunamis), as well as biological hazards. Hydrometeorological hazards, which are of atmospheric, hydrological or oceanographic origin (tropical cyclones, floods, drought, heatwaves, heavy rainfall, storms, and cold spells) are dealt with in section 4.2.1.2. Environmental hazards have had significant impacts on ecosystems and species.  These hazard events arise from (for the case of zoonotic diseases), or its impacts are exacerbated by (for the case of natural hazards), increased human interactions with their environment. Especially for the case of the coronavirus (COVID-19) pandemic, hazards from zoonotic diseases prompt us to re-examine the relationship between people and wild species.  
      Volcanic activities have had a significant impact on the world’s ecosystem. Although volcanic soil is extremely rich and helps maintain agriculture in many parts of the world, volcanic eruptions can be catastrophic, spewing lava and ashes, posing a serious risk to people and their livelihoods. Thus, volcanic activities can be considered as natural sources of pollution. Ashes ejected from volcanoes can cause much nuisance to farmers, burying agricultural lands, and destroying crops. The ashes can also present harmful impacts on human health and animals, contaminating infrastructures, and disrupting aviation and land transport ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QB2K2PNb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Small & Naumann, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Small & Naumann, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":896,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NQNLL4XB"],"itemData":{"id":896,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environ. Chang. Part B Environ. Hazards","language":"pt","page":"93–109","title":"The global distribution of human population and recent volcanism","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Small","given":"C."},{"literal":"Naumann"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The numerous active volcanic mountains in Africa exemplified by the frequent rage of the Virunga Mountains whose plumes are displaced over a long distance and causes changes to the quality of rainwater including acidity (pH up to 2), increased concentrations of Fluoride (up to 2,400 mg/L), Chloride (up to 1,750 mg/L) and Sulphide (up to 10,000 mg/L). These events have detrimental effects on the equatorial rain forest, and likely impose possible strain on the dwindling populations of gorillas (Gorilla beringei) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"POoeGh8G","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Delfosse, 2005; Plumptre, 2007; Vaselli, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Delfosse, 2005; Plumptre, 2007; Vaselli, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32494,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F5D6SSRI"],"itemData":{"id":32494,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"European Journal of Soil Science","language":"en","page":"127– 133","title":"Acid neutralization and sulphur retention in s-impacted andosols","URL":"https://dial.uclouvain.","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Delfosse","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32455,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ACY9VFZK"],"itemData":{"id":32455,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"279–287","title":"Transboundary conservation in the greater Virunga landscape: Its importance for landscape species","volume":"134","author":[{"family":"Plumptre","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Kujirakwinja","given":"D."},{"family":"Treves","given":"A."},{"family":"Owiunji","given":"I."},{"family":"Rainer","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":32372,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RXYBZ4GA"],"itemData":{"id":32372,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Active Volcanism & Continental Rifting","language":"en","title":"Environmental impact of the Nyiragongo volcanic plume after the January 2002 eruption","author":[{"family":"Vaselli","given":"O."},{"family":"Tassi","given":"F."},{"family":"Tedesco","given":"D."},{"family":"Cuoco","given":"E."},{"family":"Nisi","given":"B."},{"family":"Yalire","given":"M.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Specifically, the gorillas whose censual population stood at a finite 360 in 2003, face dual (anthropogenic and natural) challenges such as fragile and explosive political strife and raging volcanic activities of the Virunga Mountains ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iOzgJ4tO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gray, 2010; Kalpers, 2003; Vaselli, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Gray, 2010; Kalpers, 2003; Vaselli, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":898,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5DERGWI7"],"itemData":{"id":898,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"African Journal of Ecology","language":"en","page":"588–599","title":"Censusing the mountain gorillas in the Virunga volcanoes: Complete sweep method versus monitoring","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Gray","given":"M."},{"family":"McNeilage","given":"A."},{"family":"Fawcett","given":"K."},{"family":"Robbins","given":"M.M."},{"family":"Ssebide","given":"B."},{"family":"Mbula","given":"D."},{"family":"Uwingeli","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":899,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YJJDECB3"],"itemData":{"id":899,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"326–37","title":"Gorillas in the crossfire: Population dynamics of the Virunga Mountain Gorillas over the past three decades","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Kalpers","given":"J."},{"family":"Williamson","given":"E.A."},{"family":"Robbins","given":"M.M."},{"family":"McNeilage","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":32372,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RXYBZ4GA"],"itemData":{"id":32372,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Active Volcanism & Continental Rifting","language":"en","title":"Environmental impact of the Nyiragongo volcanic plume after the January 2002 eruption","author":[{"family":"Vaselli","given":"O."},{"family":"Tassi","given":"F."},{"family":"Tedesco","given":"D."},{"family":"Cuoco","given":"E."},{"family":"Nisi","given":"B."},{"family":"Yalire","given":"M.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
4.2.1.7. 2. Pandemic and sustainable use of wild species 
The Corona disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic has caused millions of deaths and suffering, brought challenges in public health, food systems, education and employment, and disrupted economy and human activities at the unprecedented scale ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Wh0bcnTF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020a; ILO, 2020)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020a; ILO, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32475,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/74MXRBRR"],"itemData":{"id":32475,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Impact of COVID-19 on people's livelihoods, their health and our food systems. A joint statement by ILO, FAO, IFAD and WHO","URL":"https://www.who.int/news/item/13-10-2020-impact-of-covid-19-on-people%27s-livelihoods-their-health-and-our-food-systems","author":[{"family":"ILO","given":"F.A.O."},{"literal":"I.F.A.D."},{"literal":"W.H.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32344,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9TQ8X64G"],"itemData":{"id":32344,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-133587-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.4060/cb1987en","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impacts of COVID-19 on wood value chains and forest sector response","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/cb1987en","author":[{"family":"FAO","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A review of approximately 500 emerging infectious diseases including pandemics found that almost all pandemics and the majority of the emerging infectious diseases are caused by wild species-origin pathogens (60% are dominated by zoonoses of which 71.8% originated in wildlife) showing a linkage between pandemics and biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AXXf4FGh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. E. Jones 2008; IPBES 2020)","plainCitation":"(K. E. Jones 2008; IPBES 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32341,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9I9MNM8H"],"itemData":{"id":32341,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/nature06536","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7181","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"990-993","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Global trends in emerging infectious diseases","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nature06536","volume":"451","author":[{"family":"Jones","given":"Kate E."},{"family":"Patel","given":"Nikkita G."},{"family":"Levy","given":"Marc A."},{"family":"Storeygard","given":"Adam"},{"family":"Balk","given":"Deborah"},{"family":"Gittleman","given":"John L."},{"family":"Daszak","given":"Peter"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",2]]}}},{"id":66,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMSKDRYR"],"itemData":{"id":66,"type":"report","abstract":"The IPBES Bureau and Multidisciplinary Expert Panel (MEP) authorized a workshop on biodiversity and pandemics that was held virtually on 27-31 July 2020 in accordance with the provisions on “Platform workshops” in support of Plenary-approved activities, set out in section 6.1 of the procedures for the preparation of Platform deliverables (IPBES-3/3, annexe I). This workshop report and any recommendations or conclusions contained therein have not been reviewed, endorsed or approved by the IPBES Plenary. The workshop report is considered supporting material available to authors in the preparation of ongoing or future IPBES assessments. While undergoing a scientific peer-review, this material has not been subjected to formal IPBES review processes. <strong>Workshop report:</strong> “<em>IPBES Pandemics Workshop Report 20201029 v1.1.pdf</em>”<br> This workshop report is released in a non-laid out format. It will undergo minor editing before being released in a laid-out format. <strong>Executive summary</strong>: “<em>IPBES Pandemics Workshop Executive Summary 20201028 v1.0.pdf</em>” To get the latest version of the report visit https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4147317 For inquiries, please contact secretariat@ipbes.net https://ipbes.net/events/ipbes-workshop-biodiversity-and-pandemics","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","language":"en","license":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International, Open Access","note":"version: 1.3\\nDOI: 10.5281/ZENODO.4147317","publisher":"IPBES secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"Workshop Report on Biodiversity and Pandemics of the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES)","URL":"https://zenodo.org/record/4147317","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."},{"family":"Neves","given":"C.","non-dropping-particle":"das"},{"family":"Amuasi","given":"J."},{"family":"Hayman","given":"D."},{"family":"Kuiken","given":"T."},{"family":"Roche","given":"B."},{"family":"Zambrana-Torrelio","given":"C."},{"family":"Buss","given":"P."},{"family":"Dundarova","given":"H."},{"family":"Feferholtz","given":"Y."},{"family":"Foldvari","given":"G."},{"family":"Igbinosa","given":"E."},{"family":"Junglen","given":"S."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Q."},{"family":"Suzan","given":"G."},{"family":"Uhart","given":"M."},{"family":"Wannous","given":"C."},{"family":"Woolaston","given":"K."},{"family":"Mosig Reidl","given":"P."},{"family":"O'Brien","given":"K."},{"family":"Pascual","given":"U."},{"family":"Stoett","given":"P."},{"family":"Li","given":"H."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H. T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The emergence of zoonoses is correlated with wild species (mammalian) diversity, human population density, and anthropogenic environmental destruction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"f07Od3DX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Allen 2017; K. E. Jones 2008; Gibb 2020)","plainCitation":"(Allen 2017; K. E. Jones 2008; Gibb 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":888,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NSSDMYAN"],"itemData":{"id":888,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature communications","issue":"1","language":"fr","page":"1–10","title":"Global hotspots and correlates of emerging zoonotic diseases","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Allen","given":"T."},{"family":"Murray","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Zambrana-Torrelio","given":"C."},{"family":"Morse","given":"S.S."},{"family":"Rondinini","given":"C."},{"family":"Di Marco","given":"M."},{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":32341,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9I9MNM8H"],"itemData":{"id":32341,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/nature06536","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7181","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"990-993","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Global trends in emerging infectious diseases","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nature06536","volume":"451","author":[{"family":"Jones","given":"Kate E."},{"family":"Patel","given":"Nikkita G."},{"family":"Levy","given":"Marc A."},{"family":"Storeygard","given":"Adam"},{"family":"Balk","given":"Deborah"},{"family":"Gittleman","given":"John L."},{"family":"Daszak","given":"Peter"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",2]]}}},{"id":32339,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FXSALM28"],"itemData":{"id":32339,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-020-2562-8","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7821","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"398-402","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Zoonotic host diversity increases in human-dominated ecosystems","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-2562-8","volume":"584","author":[{"family":"Gibb","given":"Rory"},{"family":"Redding","given":"David W."},{"family":"Chin","given":"Kai Qing"},{"family":"Donnelly","given":"Christl A."},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim M."},{"family":"Newbold","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Jones","given":"Kate E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Studies have suggested that the emergence of the disease pandemics such as Zika (2015-2016), H1N1 (2009) and SARS (2002-2004) is the results of ecosystem alteration due to land-use change, deforestation, agricultural expansion and intensification, wild species trade, consumption and other drivers that disrupts natural interactions among wild species and their microbes, increases contact among wild species, livestock, people, and their pathogens ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MrFTNAgd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2020; Nu\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}ez, 2020)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2020; Nuñez, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32461,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GUDU5L66"],"itemData":{"id":32461,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"642–645","title":"Invasion science and the global spread of SARS-CoV-2","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Nuñez","given":"M.A."},{"family":"Pauchard","given":"A."},{"family":"Ricciardi","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":66,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMSKDRYR"],"itemData":{"id":66,"type":"report","abstract":"The IPBES Bureau and Multidisciplinary Expert Panel (MEP) authorized a workshop on biodiversity and pandemics that was held virtually on 27-31 July 2020 in accordance with the provisions on “Platform workshops” in support of Plenary-approved activities, set out in section 6.1 of the procedures for the preparation of Platform deliverables (IPBES-3/3, annexe I). This workshop report and any recommendations or conclusions contained therein have not been reviewed, endorsed or approved by the IPBES Plenary. The workshop report is considered supporting material available to authors in the preparation of ongoing or future IPBES assessments. While undergoing a scientific peer-review, this material has not been subjected to formal IPBES review processes. <strong>Workshop report:</strong> “<em>IPBES Pandemics Workshop Report 20201029 v1.1.pdf</em>”<br> This workshop report is released in a non-laid out format. It will undergo minor editing before being released in a laid-out format. <strong>Executive summary</strong>: “<em>IPBES Pandemics Workshop Executive Summary 20201028 v1.0.pdf</em>” To get the latest version of the report visit https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4147317 For inquiries, please contact secretariat@ipbes.net https://ipbes.net/events/ipbes-workshop-biodiversity-and-pandemics","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","language":"en","license":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International, Open Access","note":"version: 1.3\\nDOI: 10.5281/ZENODO.4147317","publisher":"IPBES secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"Workshop Report on Biodiversity and Pandemics of the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES)","URL":"https://zenodo.org/record/4147317","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."},{"family":"Neves","given":"C.","non-dropping-particle":"das"},{"family":"Amuasi","given":"J."},{"family":"Hayman","given":"D."},{"family":"Kuiken","given":"T."},{"family":"Roche","given":"B."},{"family":"Zambrana-Torrelio","given":"C."},{"family":"Buss","given":"P."},{"family":"Dundarova","given":"H."},{"family":"Feferholtz","given":"Y."},{"family":"Foldvari","given":"G."},{"family":"Igbinosa","given":"E."},{"family":"Junglen","given":"S."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Q."},{"family":"Suzan","given":"G."},{"family":"Uhart","given":"M."},{"family":"Wannous","given":"C."},{"family":"Woolaston","given":"K."},{"family":"Mosig Reidl","given":"P."},{"family":"O'Brien","given":"K."},{"family":"Pascual","given":"U."},{"family":"Stoett","given":"P."},{"family":"Li","given":"H."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H. T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Land-use changes deforestation/forest fragmentation/habitat fragmentation, agricultural development/irrigation, and urbanization/suburbanization mainly cause increased pathogen transmission through alteration of the vector, host, and pathogen niche, changes in host and vector community composition, changes in behavior or movement of vectors and/or hosts, altered spatial distribution of hosts and/or vectors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KXiou5GW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gottdenker, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Gottdenker, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32477,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FLL2SEB5"],"itemData":{"id":32477,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EcoHealth","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"619–632","title":"Anthropogenic land use change and infectious diseases: a review of the evidence","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Gottdenker","given":"N.L."},{"family":"Streicker","given":"D.G."},{"family":"Faust","given":"C.L."},{"family":"Carroll","given":"C.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A recent analysis of 6801 ecological assemblages and 376 host species worldwide showed that the richness and abundance of human-shared pathogens are higher in the sites under substantial human use compared with undisturbed habitats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UPPC8U0l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gibb, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Gibb, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32339,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FXSALM28"],"itemData":{"id":32339,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-020-2562-8","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7821","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"398-402","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Zoonotic host diversity increases in human-dominated ecosystems","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-2562-8","volume":"584","author":[{"family":"Gibb","given":"Rory"},{"family":"Redding","given":"David W."},{"family":"Chin","given":"Kai Qing"},{"family":"Donnelly","given":"Christl A."},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim M."},{"family":"Newbold","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Jones","given":"Kate E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      There are several ways that zoonoses spillover—a process that enables a pathogen from a vertebrate animal to establish infection in a human— from wild species to humans ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TDtJaV0v","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Magouras, 2020; Plowright, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Magouras, 2020; Plowright, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35181,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6EBPS3QX"],"itemData":{"id":35181,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Veterinary Science","language":"en","title":"Emerging Zoonotic Diseases: Should We Rethink the Animal–Human Interface?","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Magouras","given":"I."},{"family":"Brookes","given":"V.J."},{"family":"Jori","given":"F."},{"family":"Martin","given":"A."},{"family":"Pfeiffer","given":"D.U."},{"family":"Dürr","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32427,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MLHSGSRQ"],"itemData":{"id":32427,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Reviews Microbiology","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"502–510","title":"Pathways to zoonotic spillover","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Plowright","given":"R.K."},{"family":"Parrish","given":"C.R."},{"family":"McCallum","given":"H."},{"family":"Hudson","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Ko","given":"A.I."},{"family":"Graham","given":"A.L."},{"family":"Lloyd-Smith","given":"J.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. First, direct contact of humans to wild animals. The spillover of pathogens from wild species to humans can occur directly through activities and hobbies of humans such as hunting, farming of exotic animals, companionship and butchering wild species. For example, Bushmeat consumption is linked with the emergence and outbreak of the Ebola virus in the countries of central and west Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lXHr4HbU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Coltart, 2017; Holmes, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Coltart, 2017; Holmes, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32474,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9KFGN9LZ"],"itemData":{"id":32474,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7624","language":"en","page":"193–200","title":"The evolution of Ebola virus: Insights from the 2013–2016 epidemic","volume":"538","author":[{"family":"Holmes","given":"E.C."},{"family":"Dudas","given":"G."},{"family":"Rambaut","given":"A."},{"family":"Andersen","given":"K.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32502,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B4KEWDAU"],"itemData":{"id":32502,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences","issue":"1721","language":"en","page":"20160297","title":"The Ebola outbreak, 2013–2016: old lessons for new epidemics","volume":"372","author":[{"family":"Coltart","given":"C.E."},{"family":"Lindsey","given":"B."},{"family":"Ghinai","given":"I."},{"family":"Johnson","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Heymann","given":"D.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The origin of HID/AIDS caused by HID-1 and HIV-2 viruses is linked with repeated exposure to wildlife including hunting and butchering a chimpanzee or gorilla or sooty mangabey as well as socio-behavioral factors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c2GDFo5o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. M. Sharp & Hahn, 2010)","plainCitation":"(P. M. Sharp & Hahn, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32442,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q6X5X6EH"],"itemData":{"id":32442,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences","issue":"1552","language":"en","page":"2487–2494","title":"The evolution of HIV-1 and the origin of AIDS","volume":"365","author":[{"family":"Sharp","given":"P.M."},{"family":"Hahn","given":"B.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another way of increasing direct contact of wild species with humans is caused by selling and butchering of live animals in wet markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RuxtACYF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Orenstein, 2020)","plainCitation":"(R. Orenstein, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32451,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VDTMFN2B"],"itemData":{"id":32451,"type":"book","event-place":"Washington, DC, USA","language":"en","publisher":"Humane Society International","publisher-place":"Washington, DC, USA","title":"Wildlife markets and COVID-19","URL":"https://www.hsi.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/04/Wildlife-Markets-and-COVID-19-White-Paper.pdf,","author":[{"family":"Orenstein","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, water fowl, especially Anseriformes (ducks, geese and swans) and Charadriiformes (gulls, terns and sandpipers), are thought to be the natural reservoir of Influenza type viruses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"U3IdVDzN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Webster 1992; B. Olsen 2006)","plainCitation":"(Webster 1992; B. Olsen 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32377,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QAZQENUP"],"itemData":{"id":32377,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Microbiological reviews","issue":"1","language":"pt","page":"152–179","title":"Evolution and ecology of influenza A viruses","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Webster","given":"R.G."},{"family":"Bean","given":"W.J."},{"family":"Gorman","given":"O.T."},{"family":"Chambers","given":"T.M."},{"family":"Kawaoka","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}},{"id":32448,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/33KNEJTG"],"itemData":{"id":32448,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"5772","language":"en","page":"384–388","title":"Global patterns of influenza A virus in wild birds","volume":"312","author":[{"family":"Olsen","given":"B."},{"family":"Munster","given":"V.J."},{"family":"Wallensten","given":"A."},{"family":"Waldenström","given":"J."},{"family":"Osterhaus","given":"A.D."},{"family":"Fouchier","given":"R.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and transmission of virus from these avian species to humans might occur in bird markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VnB0yIVA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lycett, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Lycett, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32468,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KG7XIRYJ"],"itemData":{"id":32468,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B","issue":"1775","language":"en","page":"20180257","title":"A brief history of bird flu","volume":"374","author":[{"family":"Lycett","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Duchatel","given":"F."},{"family":"Digard","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A sizeable amount of transactions of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora -listed species could carry potentially zoonotic risk ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BRvtu6dR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNEP-WCMC & JNCC, 2021)","plainCitation":"(UNEP-WCMC & JNCC, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":891,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BUWZWNVN"],"itemData":{"id":891,"type":"document","title":"Zoonotic potential of international trade in CITES listed species. JNCC Report No. 678, JNCC, Peterborough, ISSN 0963-8091.","author":[{"family":"UNEP-WCMC & JNCC","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, the SARS virus outbreak (2002–2003) potentially originated from masked palm civets (Paguma larvata) sold in wild species markets in China ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5I4P4lBq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wang 2006; R. H. Xu 2004)","plainCitation":"(Wang 2006; R. H. Xu 2004)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32378,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DA9HTH5N"],"itemData":{"id":32378,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Emerging infectious diseases","issue":"12","language":"en","page":"1834","title":"Review of bats and SARS","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Wang","given":"L.F."},{"family":"Shi","given":"Z."},{"family":"Zhang","given":"S."},{"family":"Field","given":"H."},{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."},{"family":"Eaton","given":"B.T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":32435,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F9KBKK5I"],"itemData":{"id":32435,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Emerging infectious diseases","issue":"6","language":"pt","title":"Epidemiologic clues to SARS origin in China","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Xu","given":"R.H."},{"family":"He","given":"J.F."},{"family":"Evans","given":"M.R."},{"family":"Peng","given":"G.W."},{"family":"Field","given":"H.E."},{"family":"Yu","given":"D.W."},{"family":"Li","given":"L.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, avian influenza is linked with the increase illegal pet trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1DpDDJYt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kilpatrick, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Kilpatrick, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32485,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NUU2XG7S"],"itemData":{"id":32485,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"51","language":"en","page":"19368–19373","title":"Predicting the global spread of H5N1 avian influenza","volume":"103","author":[{"family":"Kilpatrick","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Chmura","given":"A.A."},{"family":"Gibbons","given":"D.W."},{"family":"Fleischer","given":"R.C."},{"family":"Marra","given":"P.P."},{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The wet markets are symbolized with poor hygiene and inhuman treatment of wild animals that might cause immunosuppression and prolong shedding of pathogens that those animals may carry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FyByKqUo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. P. Fischer & Romero, 2019; Magouras, 2020; L. B. Martin, 2009; Nakajima, 2021)","plainCitation":"(C. P. Fischer & Romero, 2019; Magouras, 2020; L. B. Martin, 2009; Nakajima, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32473,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HB8E8GWP"],"itemData":{"id":32473,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"General and comparative endocrinology","issue":"1-2","language":"en","page":"70–76","title":"Stress and immunity in wild vertebrates: timing is everything","volume":"163","author":[{"family":"Martin","given":"L.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":32363,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C9YZHX6U"],"itemData":{"id":32363,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Physiology","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"093","title":"Chronic captivity stress in wild animals is highly species-specific","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Fischer","given":"C.P."},{"family":"Romero","given":"L.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":35181,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6EBPS3QX"],"itemData":{"id":35181,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Veterinary Science","language":"en","title":"Emerging Zoonotic Diseases: Should We Rethink the Animal–Human Interface?","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Magouras","given":"I."},{"family":"Brookes","given":"V.J."},{"family":"Jori","given":"F."},{"family":"Martin","given":"A."},{"family":"Pfeiffer","given":"D.U."},{"family":"Dürr","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32460,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/244K4ACG"],"itemData":{"id":32460,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Infection and Chemotherapy","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"387–389","title":"Prolonged viral shedding of SARS-CoV-2 in an immunocompromised patient","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Nakajima","given":"Y."},{"family":"Ogai","given":"A."},{"family":"Furukawa","given":"K."},{"family":"Arai","given":"R."},{"family":"Anan","given":"R."},{"family":"Nakano","given":"Y."},{"family":"Okabe","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Intensive wild species farming also causes the spillover of diseases. Avian influenza circulated from Ostrich farms in Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CKUy9UFA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Abolnik, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Abolnik, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32428,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JWESUKGU"],"itemData":{"id":32428,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Avian diseases","issue":"1s","language":"es","page":"286–295","title":"Susceptibility and status of avian influenza in ostriches","volume":"60","author":[{"family":"Abolnik","given":"C."},{"family":"Olivier","given":"A."},{"family":"Reynolds","given":"C."},{"family":"Henry","given":"D."},{"family":"Cumming","given":"G."},{"family":"Rauff","given":"D."},{"family":"Falch","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the recent detection of COVID-19 in mink in the Netherlands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Texudnq9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Oreshkova, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Oreshkova, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32450,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N696EHTV"],"itemData":{"id":32450,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"April and May","issue":"23","language":"en","page":"2001005","title":"SARS-CoV-2 infection in farmed minks, the Netherlands","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Oreshkova","given":"N."},{"family":"Molenaar","given":"R.J."},{"family":"Vreman","given":"S."},{"family":"Harders","given":"F."},{"family":"Munnink","given":"B.B.O."},{"family":"Honing","given":"R.W."},{"family":"Tacken","given":"M.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and an outbreak of rabies in the ranched population of kudu ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tOpFC0Qz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. P. Scott 2013)","plainCitation":"(T. P. Scott 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32441,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YCYPZHMT"],"itemData":{"id":32441,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS One","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"58739","title":"Complete genome and molecular epidemiological data infer the maintenance of rabies among kudu (Tragelaphus strepsiceros) in Namibia","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Scott","given":"T.P."},{"family":"Fischer","given":"M."},{"family":"Khaiseb","given":"S."},{"family":"Freuling","given":"C."},{"family":"Höper","given":"D."},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"B."},{"family":"Nel","given":"L.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, the exact timing, place of origin and source of infection of the COVID-19 is still not fully known ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tgw1vxb7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Frutos, 2021; Pekar, 2021; C. Wang, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Frutos, 2021; Pekar, 2021; C. Wang, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32374,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JP3ZRRM4"],"itemData":{"id":32374,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Lancet","issue":"10223","language":"en","page":"470–473","title":"A novel coronavirus outbreak of global health concern","volume":"395","author":[{"family":"Wang","given":"C."},{"family":"Horby","given":"P.W."},{"family":"Hayden","given":"F.G."},{"family":"Gao","given":"G.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32368,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/26TBVH3E"],"itemData":{"id":32368,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Infection, Genetics and Evolution","language":"en","title":"Understanding the origin of COVID-19 requires to change the paradigm on zoonotic emergence from the spillover model to the viral circulation model","volume":"104812","author":[{"family":"Frutos","given":"R."},{"family":"Gavotte","given":"L."},{"family":"Devaux","given":"C.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":32453,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6HMU3A4F"],"itemData":{"id":32453,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"6540","language":"en","page":"412–417","title":"Timing the SARS-CoV-2 index case in Hubei province","volume":"372","author":[{"family":"Pekar","given":"J."},{"family":"Worobey","given":"M."},{"family":"Moshiri","given":"N."},{"family":"Scheffler","given":"K."},{"family":"Wertheim","given":"J.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Furthermore, livestock and companion animals are also linked with spillover and amplification of emergent infectious disease such as Nipah virus ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8p5n01LQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Daszak, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Daszak, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32491,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PYA9SGL8"],"itemData":{"id":32491,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"Supplement 1","language":"en","page":"3681–3688","title":"Interdisciplinary approaches to understanding disease emergence: the past, present, and future drivers of Nipah virus emergence","volume":"110","author":[{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."},{"family":"Zambrana-Torrelio","given":"C."},{"family":"Bogich","given":"T.L."},{"family":"Fernandez","given":"M."},{"family":"Epstein","given":"J.H."},{"family":"Murray","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Hamilton","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Hendra virus ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"w1tb3Hcn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Plowright, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Plowright, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32427,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MLHSGSRQ"],"itemData":{"id":32427,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Reviews Microbiology","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"502–510","title":"Pathways to zoonotic spillover","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Plowright","given":"R.K."},{"family":"Parrish","given":"C.R."},{"family":"McCallum","given":"H."},{"family":"Hudson","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Ko","given":"A.I."},{"family":"Graham","given":"A.L."},{"family":"Lloyd-Smith","given":"J.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and avian influenzas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"R6tZ871l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fourni\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Fournié, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32365,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XNI4496L"],"itemData":{"id":32365,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"22","language":"en","page":"9177–9182","title":"Interventions for avian influenza A (H5N1) risk management in live bird market networks","volume":"110","author":[{"family":"Fournié","given":"G."},{"family":"Guitian","given":"J."},{"family":"Desvaux","given":"S."},{"family":"Cuong","given":"V.C."},{"family":"Pfeiffer","given":"D.U."},{"family":"Mangtani","given":"P."},{"family":"Ghani","given":"A.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Second, changes in land use such as environmental degradation, deforestation, land conversion for agricultural land change is also associated with the disease emergence ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iPP3D3UX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gibb, 2020; IPBES, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(Gibb, 2020; IPBES, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32339,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FXSALM28"],"itemData":{"id":32339,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-020-2562-8","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7821","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"398-402","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Zoonotic host diversity increases in human-dominated ecosystems","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-2562-8","volume":"584","author":[{"family":"Gibb","given":"Rory"},{"family":"Redding","given":"David W."},{"family":"Chin","given":"Kai Qing"},{"family":"Donnelly","given":"Christl A."},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim M."},{"family":"Newbold","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Jones","given":"Kate E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Land use change is considered as the cause of over 30% of emerging infectious diseases including the emergence of novel zoonoses globally ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pT9l3o4S","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2020; Loh, 2015)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2020; Loh, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32466,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6V8HUE75"],"itemData":{"id":32466,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Vector-Borne and Zoonotic Diseases","issue":"7","language":"en","page":"432–437","title":"Targeting transmission pathways for emerging zoonotic disease surveillance and control","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Loh","given":"E.H."},{"family":"Zambrana-Torrelio","given":"C."},{"family":"Olival","given":"K.J."},{"family":"Bogich","given":"T.L."},{"family":"Johnson","given":"C.K."},{"family":"Mazet","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":66,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMSKDRYR"],"itemData":{"id":66,"type":"report","abstract":"The IPBES Bureau and Multidisciplinary Expert Panel (MEP) authorized a workshop on biodiversity and pandemics that was held virtually on 27-31 July 2020 in accordance with the provisions on “Platform workshops” in support of Plenary-approved activities, set out in section 6.1 of the procedures for the preparation of Platform deliverables (IPBES-3/3, annexe I). This workshop report and any recommendations or conclusions contained therein have not been reviewed, endorsed or approved by the IPBES Plenary. The workshop report is considered supporting material available to authors in the preparation of ongoing or future IPBES assessments. While undergoing a scientific peer-review, this material has not been subjected to formal IPBES review processes. <strong>Workshop report:</strong> “<em>IPBES Pandemics Workshop Report 20201029 v1.1.pdf</em>”<br> This workshop report is released in a non-laid out format. It will undergo minor editing before being released in a laid-out format. <strong>Executive summary</strong>: “<em>IPBES Pandemics Workshop Executive Summary 20201028 v1.0.pdf</em>” To get the latest version of the report visit https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4147317 For inquiries, please contact secretariat@ipbes.net https://ipbes.net/events/ipbes-workshop-biodiversity-and-pandemics","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","language":"en","license":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International, Open Access","note":"version: 1.3\\nDOI: 10.5281/ZENODO.4147317","publisher":"IPBES secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"Workshop Report on Biodiversity and Pandemics of the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES)","URL":"https://zenodo.org/record/4147317","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."},{"family":"Neves","given":"C.","non-dropping-particle":"das"},{"family":"Amuasi","given":"J."},{"family":"Hayman","given":"D."},{"family":"Kuiken","given":"T."},{"family":"Roche","given":"B."},{"family":"Zambrana-Torrelio","given":"C."},{"family":"Buss","given":"P."},{"family":"Dundarova","given":"H."},{"family":"Feferholtz","given":"Y."},{"family":"Foldvari","given":"G."},{"family":"Igbinosa","given":"E."},{"family":"Junglen","given":"S."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Q."},{"family":"Suzan","given":"G."},{"family":"Uhart","given":"M."},{"family":"Wannous","given":"C."},{"family":"Woolaston","given":"K."},{"family":"Mosig Reidl","given":"P."},{"family":"O'Brien","given":"K."},{"family":"Pascual","given":"U."},{"family":"Stoett","given":"P."},{"family":"Li","given":"H."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H. T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Human-dominated landscapes harbor a higher level of species richness and abundance of wild species hosts of human pathogens ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iewJ3YE9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gibb, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Gibb, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32339,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FXSALM28"],"itemData":{"id":32339,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/s41586-020-2562-8","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7821","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"398-402","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Zoonotic host diversity increases in human-dominated ecosystems","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-2562-8","volume":"584","author":[{"family":"Gibb","given":"Rory"},{"family":"Redding","given":"David W."},{"family":"Chin","given":"Kai Qing"},{"family":"Donnelly","given":"Christl A."},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim M."},{"family":"Newbold","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Jones","given":"Kate E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Land use change increases human populations into landscapes where indigenous peoples and local communities have often lived since historical times at relatively low density creating new opportunities for contact between humans and livestock with wild species thus increasing the risk of disease transmission ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KoSLacYm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}hm, 2013; K. A. Murray & Daszak, 2013; Rwego, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Böhm, 2013; K. A. Murray & Daszak, 2013; Rwego, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":885,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4AILJGGY"],"itemData":{"id":885,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Effective and targeted conservation action requires detailed information about species, their distribution, systematics and ecology as well as the distribution of threat processes which affect them. Knowledge of reptilian diversity remains surprisingly disparate, and innovative means of gaining rapid insight into the status of reptiles are needed in order to highlight urgent conservation cases and inform environmental policy with appropriate biodiversity information in a timely manner. We present the first ever global analysis of extinction risk in reptiles, based on a random representative sample of 1500 species (16% of all currently known species). To our knowledge, our results provide the first analysis of the global conservation status and distribution patterns of reptiles and the threats affecting them, highlighting conservation priorities and knowledge gaps which need to be addressed urgently to ensure the continued survival of the world’s reptiles. Nearly one in five reptilian species are threatened with extinction, with another one in five species classed as Data Deficient. The proportion of threatened reptile species is highest in freshwater environments, tropical regions and on oceanic islands, while data deficiency was highest in tropical areas, such as Central Africa and Southeast Asia, and among fossorial reptiles. Our results emphasise the need for research attention to be focussed on tropical areas which are experiencing the most dramatic rates of habitat loss, on fossorial reptiles for which there is a chronic lack of data, and on certain taxa such as snakes for which extinction risk may currently be underestimated due to lack of population information. Conservation actions specifically need to mitigate the effects of human-induced habitat loss and harvesting, which are the predominant threats to reptiles.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.07.015","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"372-385","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The conservation status of the world’s reptiles","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320712003357","volume":"157","author":[{"family":"Böhm","given":"Monika"},{"family":"Collen","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Baillie","given":"Jonathan E. M."},{"family":"Bowles","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Chanson","given":"Janice"},{"family":"Cox","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Hammerson","given":"Geoffrey"},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Livingstone","given":"Suzanne R."},{"family":"Ram","given":"Mala"},{"family":"Rhodin","given":"Anders G. J."},{"family":"Stuart","given":"Simon N."},{"family":"Dijk","given":"Peter Paul","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Young","given":"Bruce E."},{"family":"Afuang","given":"Leticia E."},{"family":"Aghasyan","given":"Aram"},{"family":"García","given":"Andrés"},{"family":"Aguilar","given":"César"},{"family":"Ajtic","given":"Rastko"},{"family":"Akarsu","given":"Ferdi"},{"family":"Alencar","given":"Laura R. V."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Allen"},{"family":"Ananjeva","given":"Natalia"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Steve"},{"family":"Andrén","given":"Claes"},{"family":"Ariano-Sánchez","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Arredondo",