      Fourth, climate change enhanced spillover risk ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1VyvTIhl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2020)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":66,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMSKDRYR"],"itemData":{"id":66,"type":"report","abstract":"The IPBES Bureau and Multidisciplinary Expert Panel (MEP) authorized a workshop on biodiversity and pandemics that was held virtually on 27-31 July 2020 in accordance with the provisions on “Platform workshops” in support of Plenary-approved activities, set out in section 6.1 of the procedures for the preparation of Platform deliverables (IPBES-3/3, annexe I). This workshop report and any recommendations or conclusions contained therein have not been reviewed, endorsed or approved by the IPBES Plenary. The workshop report is considered supporting material available to authors in the preparation of ongoing or future IPBES assessments. While undergoing a scientific peer-review, this material has not been subjected to formal IPBES review processes. <strong>Workshop report:</strong> “<em>IPBES Pandemics Workshop Report 20201029 v1.1.pdf</em>”<br> This workshop report is released in a non-laid out format. It will undergo minor editing before being released in a laid-out format. <strong>Executive summary</strong>: “<em>IPBES Pandemics Workshop Executive Summary 20201028 v1.0.pdf</em>” To get the latest version of the report visit https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4147317 For inquiries, please contact secretariat@ipbes.net https://ipbes.net/events/ipbes-workshop-biodiversity-and-pandemics","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","language":"en","license":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International, Open Access","note":"version: 1.3\\nDOI: 10.5281/ZENODO.4147317","publisher":"IPBES secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","source":"DOI.org (Datacite)","title":"Workshop Report on Biodiversity and Pandemics of the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES)","URL":"https://zenodo.org/record/4147317","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."},{"family":"Neves","given":"C.","non-dropping-particle":"das"},{"family":"Amuasi","given":"J."},{"family":"Hayman","given":"D."},{"family":"Kuiken","given":"T."},{"family":"Roche","given":"B."},{"family":"Zambrana-Torrelio","given":"C."},{"family":"Buss","given":"P."},{"family":"Dundarova","given":"H."},{"family":"Feferholtz","given":"Y."},{"family":"Foldvari","given":"G."},{"family":"Igbinosa","given":"E."},{"family":"Junglen","given":"S."},{"family":"Liu","given":"Q."},{"family":"Suzan","given":"G."},{"family":"Uhart","given":"M."},{"family":"Wannous","given":"C."},{"family":"Woolaston","given":"K."},{"family":"Mosig Reidl","given":"P."},{"family":"O'Brien","given":"K."},{"family":"Pascual","given":"U."},{"family":"Stoett","given":"P."},{"family":"Li","given":"H."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H. T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, therefore, anthropogenic climate change that causes the movements of humans and animals is also considered as a driver of emergent infectious disease. Climate change allows microbes to make contact with new hosts to potentially invade new niches ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TS3ugdxC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pecl, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Pecl, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32452,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TKVFMPBH"],"itemData":{"id":32452,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"6332","language":"en","title":"Biodiversity redistribution under climate change: Impacts on ecosystems and human well-being","volume":"355","author":[{"family":"Pecl","given":"G.T."},{"family":"Araújo","given":"M.B."},{"family":"Bell","given":"J.D."},{"family":"Blanchard","given":"J."},{"family":"Bonebrake","given":"T.C."},{"family":"Chen","given":"I.C."},{"family":"Falconi","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Climate change also facilitates the rapid expansion of the host range and microbial species’ capacity to colonize new hosts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6BKCNaRK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoberg and Brooks 2015; D. R. Brooks, Hoberg, and Boeger 2019)","plainCitation":"(Hoberg and Brooks 2015; D. R. Brooks, Hoberg, and Boeger 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32472,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8R4RUYBS"],"itemData":{"id":32472,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences","issue":"1665","language":"en","page":"20130553","title":"Evolution in action: climate change, biodiversity dynamics and emerging infectious disease","volume":"370","author":[{"family":"Hoberg","given":"E.P."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"D.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":32315,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PAJHG866"],"itemData":{"id":32315,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"University of Chicago Press","title":"The Stockholm paradigm: Climate change and emerging disease","author":[{"family":"Brooks","given":"D.R."},{"family":"Hoberg","given":"E.P."},{"family":"Boeger","given":"W.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The recent spread of bluetongue disease in Europe is caused by the climate-induced migration of biting midge vector ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wxzxHM0Q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Purse, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Purse, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32456,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NT3REG78"],"itemData":{"id":32456,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Rev Sci Tech","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"427–442","title":"Invasion of bluetongue and other orbivirus infections into Europe: the role of biological and climatic processes","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Purse","given":"B.V."},{"family":"Brown","given":"H.E."},{"family":"Harrup","given":"L."},{"family":"Mertens","given":"P.P."},{"family":"Rogers","given":"D.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, the northern migration of vector-borne diseases such as tick-borne encephalitis is also facilitated by climate change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LqMzoKiQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hvidsten, 2020; Semenza, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Hvidsten, 2020; Semenza, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32440,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XEV73DDY"],"itemData":{"id":32440,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"European experts sound alarm as mosquito- and tick-borne diseases set to flourish in warmer climate, <https://www.eurekalert.org/pub_releases/2019-04/esoc-ees041019.php>","author":[{"family":"Semenza","given":"J.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32476,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CP692JZT"],"itemData":{"id":32476,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ticks and tick-borne diseases","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"101388","title":"The distribution limit of the common tick, Ixodes ricinus, and some associated pathogens in north-western Europe","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Hvidsten","given":"D."},{"family":"Frafjord","given":"K."},{"family":"Gray","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Henningsson","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Jenkins","given":"A."},{"family":"Kristiansen","given":"B.E."},{"family":"Stuen","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Overall, the drivers of sustainable use of wild species such as unsustainable and extractive use of wild species including wild species trade, land use change, climate change, invasive species not only have consequences on the sustainable use of wild species but also are connected to the emergence, amplification and spread of disease-causing pathogens. These drivers facilitate the spillover of novel or known pathogens from wild hosts to humans causing severe impacts on human life, economy, and society. Additionally, domestic animals are hosts of several pathogens including Tuberculosis, Brucellosis ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QuhhlH8s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rahman, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Rahman, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32457,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V7D5I5WS"],"itemData":{"id":32457,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Microorganisms","issue":"9","language":"pt","page":"1405","title":"Zoonotic diseases: etiology, impact, and control","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Rahman","given":"M."},{"family":"Sobur","given":"M."},{"family":"Islam","given":"M."},{"family":"Ievy","given":"S."},{"family":"Hossain","given":"M."},{"family":"El Zowalaty","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Ashour","given":"H.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and the expansion of domestic animal trade has led to deforestation and land use conversion. Therefore, curbing those drivers such as preventing deforestation and regulating wild species trade including the sale and consumption of wild animals that can host dangerous pathogen may reduce the risk of future pandemics ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JkwgG34m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dobson, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dobson, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32340,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JEXEHLGC"],"itemData":{"id":32340,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.abc3189","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"6502","note":"publisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science\\n_eprint: https://science.sciencemag.org/content/369/6502/379.full.pdf","page":"379–381","title":"Ecology and economics for pandemic prevention","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/369/6502/379","volume":"369","author":[{"family":"Dobson","given":"Andrew P."},{"family":"Pimm","given":"Stuart L."},{"family":"Hannah","given":"Lee"},{"family":"Kaufman","given":"Les"},{"family":"Ahumada","given":"Jorge A."},{"family":"Ando","given":"Amy W."},{"family":"Bernstein","given":"Aaron"},{"family":"Busch","given":"Jonah"},{"family":"Daszak","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Engelmann","given":"Jens"},{"family":"Kinnaird","given":"Margaret F."},{"family":"Li","given":"Binbin V."},{"family":"Loch-Temzelides","given":"Ted"},{"family":"Lovejoy","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Nowak","given":"Katarzyna"},{"family":"Roehrdanz","given":"Patrick R."},{"family":"Vale","given":"Mariana M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The protected areas with intact natural habitats and limited disturbances may play role in buffering against novel disease outbreaks and spillover of diseases from wild species to people by maintaining ecosystem integrity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wb3wMWzu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Di Marco, 2020; Terraube & Fern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez-Llamazares, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Di Marco, 2020; Terraube & Fernández-Llamazares, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32500,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ITIUZQD8"],"itemData":{"id":32500,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"3888–3892","title":"Opinion: Sustainable development must account for pandemic risk","volume":"117","author":[{"family":"Di Marco","given":"M."},{"family":"Baker","given":"M.L."},{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."},{"family":"De Barro","given":"P."},{"family":"Eskew","given":"E.A."},{"family":"Godde","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Karesh","given":"W.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32430,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DMWTLB2F"],"itemData":{"id":32430,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","language":"en","title":"Strengthening protected areas to halt biodiversity loss and mitigate pandemic risks","author":[{"family":"Terraube","given":"J."},{"family":"Fernández-Llamazares","given":"Á."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Furthermore, restoration of biodiversity is a crucial frontier in the management of zoonotic disease risk ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PoewveNF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Keesing & Ostfeld, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Keesing & Ostfeld, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32356,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5F9V66TG"],"itemData":{"id":32356,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Zoonotic diseases are infectious diseases of humans caused by pathogens that are shared between humans and other vertebrate animals. Previously, pristine natural areas with high biodiversity were seen as likely sources of new zoonotic pathogens, suggesting that biodiversity could have negative impacts on human health. At the same time, biodiversity has been recognized as potentially benefiting human health by reducing the transmission of some pathogens that have already established themselves in human populations. These apparently opposing effects of biodiversity in human health may now be reconcilable. Recent research demonstrates that some taxa are much more likely to be zoonotic hosts than others are, and that these animals often proliferate in human-dominated landscapes, increasing the likelihood of spillover. In less-disturbed areas, however, these zoonotic reservoir hosts are less abundant and nonreservoirs predominate. Thus, biodiversity loss appears to increase the risk of human exposure to both new and established zoonotic pathogens. This new synthesis of the effects of biodiversity on zoonotic diseases presents an opportunity to articulate the next generation of research questions that can inform management and policy. Future studies should focus on collecting and analyzing data on the diversity, abundance, and capacity to transmit of the taxa that actually share zoonotic pathogens with us. To predict and prevent future epidemics, researchers should also focus on how these metrics change in response to human impacts on the environment, and how human behaviors can mitigate these effects. Restoration of biodiversity is an important frontier in the management of zoonotic disease risk.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.2023540118","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"17","journalAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci USA","language":"en","page":"e2023540118","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impacts of biodiversity and biodiversity loss on zoonotic diseases","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/lookup/doi/10.1073/pnas.2023540118","volume":"118","author":[{"family":"Keesing","given":"Felicia"},{"family":"Ostfeld","given":"Richard S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
4.2.1.7. 3. Terrestrial animal harvesting 
There is evidence that intensified contact between people and wild species arising from encroachment of human activities into forest ecosystems, and increased demand for meat and medicine from wild species, lead to transmission of zoonotic diseases, which constitute about 70% of known emerging diseases ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RHkXAsp0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Volpato, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Volpato, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":893,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4KMWGBNG"],"itemData":{"id":893,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              The Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine (JEET), throughout its 15 years of existence, has tried to provide a respected outlet for scientific knowledge concerning the inextricable links between human societies and nature, food, and health. Ethnobiology and ethnomedicine-centred research has moved at the (partially artificial and fictitious) interface between\\n              nature and culture\\n              and has investigated human consumption of wild foods and wild animals, as well as the use of wild animals or their parts for medicinal and other purposes, along with the associated knowledge, skills, practices, and beliefs. Little attention has been paid, however, to the complex interplay of social and cultural reasons behind the increasing pressure on wildlife. The available literature suggest that there are two main drivers that enhance the necessary conditions for infectious diseases to cross the species barrier from wild animals to humans: (1) the encroachment of human activities (e.g., logging, mining, agricultural expansion) into wild areas and forests and consequent ecological disruptions; and, connected to the former, (2) the commodification of wild animals (and natural resources in general) and an expanding demand and market for wild meat and live wild animals, particularly in tropical and sub-tropical areas. In particular, a crucial role may have been played by the\\n              bushmeat-euphoria\\n              and attached elitist gastronomies and conspicuous consumption phenomena. The COVID-19 pandemic will likely require ethnobiologists to reschedule research agendas and to envision new epistemological trajectories aimed at more effectively mitigating the mismanagement of natural resources that ultimately threats our and other beings’ existence.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-020-00366-4","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"19, s13002-020-00366-4","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Baby pangolins on my plate: possible lessons to learn from the COVID-19 pandemic","title-short":"Baby pangolins on my plate","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-020-00366-4","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Volpato","given":"Gabriele"},{"family":"Fontefrancesco","given":"Michele F."},{"family":"Gruppuso","given":"Paolo"},{"family":"Zocchi","given":"Dauro M."},{"family":"Pieroni","given":"Andrea"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Malaysia, a combination of deforestation, drought, and wildfires has led to alterations in the population movements and densities of flying foxes, large fruit bats known to be the reservoir for the zoonosis Nipah virus ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5E5vl7wT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chua, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Chua, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32501,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IBLX6U72"],"itemData":{"id":32501,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Lancet","language":"en","page":"1257–1259","title":"Fatal encephalitis due to Nipah virus among pig-farmers in Malaysia","volume":"354","author":[{"family":"Chua","given":"K.B."},{"family":"Goh","given":"K.J."},{"family":"Wong","given":"K.T."},{"family":"Kamarulzaman","given":"A."},{"family":"Tan","given":"P.S."},{"family":"Ksiazek","given":"T.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although elite gastronomic consumption is said to be behind wild species consumption in Asia, food insecurity and poverty increase bushmeat hunting in Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DuMXn3a4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Volpato, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Volpato, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":893,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4KMWGBNG"],"itemData":{"id":893,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              The Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine (JEET), throughout its 15 years of existence, has tried to provide a respected outlet for scientific knowledge concerning the inextricable links between human societies and nature, food, and health. Ethnobiology and ethnomedicine-centred research has moved at the (partially artificial and fictitious) interface between\\n              nature and culture\\n              and has investigated human consumption of wild foods and wild animals, as well as the use of wild animals or their parts for medicinal and other purposes, along with the associated knowledge, skills, practices, and beliefs. Little attention has been paid, however, to the complex interplay of social and cultural reasons behind the increasing pressure on wildlife. The available literature suggest that there are two main drivers that enhance the necessary conditions for infectious diseases to cross the species barrier from wild animals to humans: (1) the encroachment of human activities (e.g., logging, mining, agricultural expansion) into wild areas and forests and consequent ecological disruptions; and, connected to the former, (2) the commodification of wild animals (and natural resources in general) and an expanding demand and market for wild meat and live wild animals, particularly in tropical and sub-tropical areas. In particular, a crucial role may have been played by the\\n              bushmeat-euphoria\\n              and attached elitist gastronomies and conspicuous consumption phenomena. The COVID-19 pandemic will likely require ethnobiologists to reschedule research agendas and to envision new epistemological trajectories aimed at more effectively mitigating the mismanagement of natural resources that ultimately threats our and other beings’ existence.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-020-00366-4","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"19, s13002-020-00366-4","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Baby pangolins on my plate: possible lessons to learn from the COVID-19 pandemic","title-short":"Baby pangolins on my plate","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-020-00366-4","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Volpato","given":"Gabriele"},{"family":"Fontefrancesco","given":"Michele F."},{"family":"Gruppuso","given":"Paolo"},{"family":"Zocchi","given":"Dauro M."},{"family":"Pieroni","given":"Andrea"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, evidence for clear linkages are lacking. There is a gap in the literature on the link between environmental hazards and hunting. While studies in Sub-Saharian Africa demonstrate such links, for example, poor fish harvest resulted in increased number of bushmeat hunters in Ghana ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6OWCIRSu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brashares, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Brashares, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32355,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6NRGNVHJ"],"itemData":{"id":32355,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The multibillion-dollar trade in bushmeat is among the most immediate threats to the persistence of tropical vertebrates, but our understanding of its underlying drivers and effects on human welfare is limited by a lack of empirical data. We used 30 years of data from Ghana to link mammal declines to the bushmeat trade and to spatial and temporal changes in the availability of fish. We show that years of poor fish supply coincided with increased hunting in nature reserves and sharp declines in biomass of 41 wildlife species. Local market data provide evidence of a direct link between fish supply and subsequent bushmeat demand in villages and show bushmeat's role as a dietary staple in the region. Our results emphasize the urgent need to develop cheap protein alternatives to bushmeat and to improve fisheries management by foreign and domestic fleets to avert extinctions of tropical wildlife.","container-title":"Science (New York, N.Y.)","DOI":"10.1126/science.1102425","ISSN":"1095-9203","issue":"5699","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"eng","note":"PMID: 15539602","page":"1180-1183","source":"PubMed","title":"Bushmeat hunting, wildlife declines, and fish supply in West Africa","volume":"306","author":[{"family":"Brashares","given":"Justin S."},{"family":"Arcese","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Sam","given":"Moses K."},{"family":"Coppolillo","given":"Peter B."},{"family":"Sinclair","given":"A. R. E."},{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",11,12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, agricultural productivity was a driver of incidences of human Ebola virus infections in Sub-Saharan African countries over the 1976 – 2013 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gMUPvlvR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Price, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Price, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":890,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N4GNKTZS"],"itemData":{"id":890,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"SSRN Electronic Journal","DOI":"10.2139/ssrn.2572055","ISSN":"1556-5068","journalAbbreviation":"SSRN Journal","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Does Productivity in the Formal Food Sector Drive Human Ebola Infections in Sub-Saharan Africa?","URL":"http://www.ssrn.com/abstract=2572055","author":[{"family":"Price","given":"Gregory"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and the link between the West African Ebola virus epidemic with decreased consumption of bushmeat ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"geVZ75s9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ordaz-N\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}meth, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Ordaz-Németh, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":892,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SD797LYI"],"itemData":{"id":892,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLOS Neglected Tropical Diseases","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pntd.0005450","ISSN":"1935-2735","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS Negl Trop Dis","language":"en","page":"e0005450","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The socio-economic drivers of bushmeat consumption during the West African Ebola crisis","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pntd.0005450","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Ordaz-Németh","given":"Isabel"},{"family":"Arandjelovic","given":"Mimi"},{"family":"Boesch","given":"Lukas"},{"family":"Gatiso","given":"Tsegaye"},{"family":"Grimes","given":"Trokon"},{"family":"Kuehl","given":"Hjalmar S."},{"family":"Lormie","given":"Menladi"},{"family":"Stephens","given":"Colleen"},{"family":"Tweh","given":"Clement"},{"family":"Junker","given":"Jessica"}],"editor":[{"family":"Akogun","given":"Oladele B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",3,10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which can be inferred as decreased hunting of wild species for bushmeat.  While it can thus be interpreted that increasing number of zoonotic diseases can result in reduction in consumption of bushmeat, which would then lead to increased pressure on other wild species such as through fishing, the evidence is unresolved.

4.2.1.7. 4.  Trends in environmental drivers
Environmental drivers directly and indirectly change the distribution and abundance of species as well as damage service provision of ecosystems and wild resources. Climate change for example causes shifts in the distribution and abundance of species; more than 80% of the species that show changes are shifting in the direction expected based on known physiological constraints of species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wSDAi0kx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Root, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Root, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32342,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HFG3UP3R"],"itemData":{"id":32342,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/nature01333","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"6918","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"57-60","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Fingerprints of global warming on wild animals and plants","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nature01333","volume":"421","author":[{"family":"Root","given":"Terry L."},{"family":"Price","given":"Jeff T."},{"family":"Hall","given":"Kimberly R."},{"family":"Schneider","given":"Stephen H."},{"family":"Rosenzweig","given":"Cynthia"},{"family":"Pounds","given":"J. Alan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Climate change will also lead to an extinction of many species in key regions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CpeOcFyR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. D. Thomas, 2004)","plainCitation":"(C. D. Thomas, 2004)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32429,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z6Z8KFBX"],"itemData":{"id":32429,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"6970","language":"en","page":"145","title":"Extinction risk from climate change","volume":"427","author":[{"family":"Thomas","given":"C.D."},{"family":"Cameron","given":"A."},{"family":"Green","given":"R.E."},{"family":"Bakkenes","given":"M."},{"family":"Beaumont","given":"L.J."},{"family":"Collingham","given":"Y.C."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The frequency and/or intensity of extreme events, such as heatwaves, droughts, heavy rainfall, storms and coastal flooding, marine heatwaves is expected to increase with climate change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GbJFmrwY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. F. Mitchell, 2006)","plainCitation":"(J. F. Mitchell, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32465,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VJF2UIX6"],"itemData":{"id":32465,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London A: Mathematical, Physical and Engineering Sciences","issue":"1845","language":"en","page":"2117–2133","title":"Extreme events due to human-induced climate change","volume":"364","author":[{"family":"Mitchell","given":"J.F."},{"family":"Lowe","given":"J."},{"family":"Wood","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Vellinga","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These extreme events cause damage to the ecosystems and habitats of wild species. Invasive species are also causing changes in ecological systems altering communities and ecosystems however, the evidence supporting a general and primary role for invasive species in extinctions remains limited ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bGDqUv5I","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gurevitch & Padilla, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Gurevitch & Padilla, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32320,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/36QQ637W"],"itemData":{"id":32320,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology and Evolution","page":"470–474","title":"Are invasive species a major cause of extinctions?","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Gurevitch","given":"J."},{"family":"Padilla","given":"D. K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Land degradation particularly land-use related pressure has reduced local species richness by an average of 13.6% and total abundance of plants and animals by 10.7% compared with what they would have been in absence of human effects ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Wd7kpRR9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Newbold, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Newbold, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32462,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7NGLHL22"],"itemData":{"id":32462,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7545","language":"en","page":"45","title":"Global effects of land use on local terrestrial biodiversity","volume":"520","author":[{"family":"Newbold","given":"T."},{"family":"Hudson","given":"L.N."},{"family":"Hill","given":"S.L."},{"family":"Contu","given":"S."},{"family":"Lysenko","given":"I."},{"family":"Senior","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Day","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Pollution of land, air and water has a huge impact on biodiversity. Some notable examples of the pollution causing local extinction is the ‘dead zone’ in the Gulf of Mexico and mass mortality of coral reefs. Dead zones are the areas of water bodies where survival of aquatic live is impossible due to low oxygen levels. Deforestation and forest disturbances, particularly in tropical areas, contribute to biodiversity loss and with the greatest negative effects on species of high conservation and function value ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hgoL492z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barlow, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Barlow, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26035,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZZPBEE3","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D6WRKPHD"],"itemData":{"id":26035,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7610","language":"en","page":"144–147","title":"Anthropogenic disturbance in tropical forests can double biodiversity loss from deforestation","volume":"535","author":[{"family":"Barlow","given":"J."},{"family":"Lennox","given":"G.D."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"J."},{"family":"Berenguer","given":"E."},{"family":"Lees","given":"A.C."},{"family":"Mac Nally","given":"R."},{"family":"Thomson","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Barros Ferraz","given":"S.F."},{"family":"Louzada","given":"J."},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"V.H.F."},{"family":"Parry","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Sub-Saharan Africa has a youth population growth rate that is the highest of any region at nearly 20 per cent ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UBxBOfRL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations, 2019)","plainCitation":"(United Nations, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32422,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CAXKGHAL"],"itemData":{"id":32422,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: New York","title":"World Urbanization Prospects 2018: Highlights. Department of Economic and Social Affairs","author":[{"family":"United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

4.2.2 Political drivers 
4.2.2.1. Overview     
Decisions about the use of wild species, define, and are defined by, the diverse systems of governance that exist at local to global scales. Formal and informal rules vary around the globe and by different species; much more evidence can be found about the structures, and processes of formal governance (e.g., statutory international agreements) and their strengths and weakness in managing wild species use. The customary laws and rules that have been developed by indigenous peoples and local communities are less well documented by comparison. The informal or customary rules that govern use and trade of wild species are also poorly documented.
      Most wild species are defined as public goods or as common property; fewer wild species are defined as private property or found in privately owned spaces. Most of the governance arrangements governing wild species focus on terrestrial megafauna, forests, and freshwater fisheries, with fewer regulations related to marine species. Plants and other non-timber forest products have largely been overlooked and their harvest and use comparatively poorly regulated ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"i49tMw0M","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2010)","plainCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3K7YP5V"],"itemData":{"id":34331,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","number-of-pages":"393","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","title-short":"Wild product governance","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Regulation of non-extractive use is an emergent area of governance but is poorly developed around the globe.
      In regions where wild species have historically been in decline or extirpated, there are generally much stronger formal regulations in place to protect what remains; many of these regulations are highly restrictive of any kinds of use. In other regions, considered to have a larger number of wild species and spaces, there tend to be few rules related to use, or they are weakly enforced. A critical concern are regions where wild species use appears to be unsustainable (by various kinds of indicators) but there is a paucity of formal and informal rules for managing. 
      Informal institutions (e.g., customary laws) are also important in shaping use; they exist even where formal rules do not; indigenous peoples and local communities for example, who have strong relationships to place and histories of use of wild species have well developed rule systems (i.e., rules-in-use). There is growing evidence that these kinds of institutions may be as, or more effective, at mediating or managing for sustainable use. Where governance systems are highly pluralistic (inclusive of different values of stakeholders) and are flexible and adaptive to ecological and social conditions, wild species use is managed in ways that ensure both social and ecological sustainability. There are many international agreements and institutions that influence the rule-systems within and between different nation states, particularly where there are transboundary use issues ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BAhgz6fC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. Liu 2020)","plainCitation":"(F. Liu 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ENNM9VIP"],"itemData":{"id":32359,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Discrepancies in trade statistics can be normal or benign and attributed to a wide variety of unintentional factors, or in some instances within the timber products sector, such discrepancies can be associated with “systemic” factors that distort trade statistics, including (i) measurement and shipment issues, (ii) misreporting of product volumes, (iii) misclassification of timber product types, and (iv) government regulations concerned about trade. This study measured trade discrepancies in logs and lumber trade statistics for China and its trading partner countries from 2002 to 2018 using a time-lagged function, based on the customs data available from Global Trade Information Services (GTIS), with the aim of exploring a more nuanced understanding of trade discrepancies and their “systemic” factors. The results showed that the range of overall discrepancies in logs and lumber trade statistics shrunk over time, from [?0.069, 1.207] in 2002–2007 to [?0.120, 0.408] in 2013–2018. The larger trade flows of logs and lumber from Russia, New Zealand, and the U.S. (each above 10% of total China’s import) showed small trade statistics discrepancy ratios, which were less than ± 0.06. However, trade discrepancies still remained large at the disaggregated level, and significant differences of trade discrepancies between tropical and non-tropical countries. The range of trade discrepancies in hardwood logs increased from 2002 to 2018 and appeared to be attributed to misclassification and misreporting in tropical countries such as Indonesia, the Philippines, Thailand, and Ghana. However, these countries’ trade flows are becoming relatively minor over time. Government policies are suggested to play an important role in influencing both the occurrence and resolution of trade discrepancies.","container-title":"Forests","DOI":"10.3390/f11020205","ISSN":"1999-4907","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Forests","language":"en","page":"205","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Sustainable Timber Trade: A Study on Discrepancies in Chinese Logs and Lumber Trade Statistics","title-short":"Sustainable Timber Trade","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/1999-4907/11/2/205","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Fei"},{"family":"Wheiler","given":"Kent"},{"family":"Ganguly","given":"Indroneil"},{"family":"Hu","given":"Mingxing"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",2,12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These kinds of rule systems are viewed as increasingly important as the world becomes more interdependent due to travel and communication technology and globalized economies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Uu9UeHL1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Marauhn, 2013; Paavola, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Marauhn, 2013; Paavola, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32416,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V9V2D8JF"],"itemData":{"id":32416,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Peacetime Regime for State Activities in Cyberspace: International Law, International Relations, and Diplomacy","journalAbbreviation":"Peacetime Regime for State Activities in Cyberspace: International Law, International Relations, and Diplomacy","note":"publisher: Citeseer","page":"465-84","title":"Customary Rules of International Environmental Law—Can They Provide Guidance for Developing a Peacetime Regime for Cyberspace?","author":[{"family":"Marauhn","given":"Thilo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":32420,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G9WPMJCI"],"itemData":{"id":32420,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental values in a globalising world","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental values in a globalising world","note":"publisher: Routledge","page":"143-58","title":"Interdependence, pluralism and globalisation","author":[{"family":"Paavola","given":"Jouni"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The focus of this section is to assess how different political drivers influence the sustainable use of wild species, their synergies and interactions.  
      
4.2.2.1.1. Methodology
A systematic literature review was carried out in respect of key areas of literature using terms such as political drivers, land tenure, governance, political, rights, gender, indigenous peoples and each of the regions, and practices (e.g., hunting, fishing). Over 5000 sources were found by authors. Based on review of these sources, major themes and interpretation of patterns in political drivers were identified. The sections were developed by experts with 20 + years of experience related to aspects of biodiversity conservation and in the social sciences. Where there were gaps in regions, practices, etc., and case studies were developed to illustrate an important dimension of the political driver and its impacts on practices and uses.

4.2.2.1.2. Gaps
* There is greater published evidence about formal systems of governance when compared to informal systems and customary law including that of indigenous peoples.
* There are gaps in literature related to governance of gathering and non-extractive uses (including viewing) when compared to the practices of hunting, fishing and logging.
* Regional gaps exist in literature published in English with respect to governance for many parts of central Asia, Russia and some parts of Latin America particularly in relation to informal institutions and governance systems of indigenous peoples and local communities. 
* 
4.2.2.1.3. Definitions 
Governance is fundamentally about the distribution of power among different members of society; it encapsulates the interactions among structures, processes, rules and traditions, that determine how people in societies make decisions and share power, exercise responsibility, ensure accountability, and how stakeholders have a say in the management of natural resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pL9Vkbfg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lebel, 2006; Raik & Decker, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Lebel, 2006; Raik & Decker, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32424,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YIUW3ZT7"],"itemData":{"id":32424,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","language":"en","page":"10 5751 –01606–110119","title":"Governance and the Capacity to Manage Resilience in Regional Social-Ecological Systems","volume":"11. 19","editor":[{"family":"Lebel","given":"Louis"},{"family":"Anderies","given":"John"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"Bruce Morgan"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Hatfield-Dodds","given":"Steve"},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Terence"},{"family":"Wilson","given":"James"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":32419,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FFQUAJ56"],"itemData":{"id":32419,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecology and Society","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: JSTOR","title":"A multisector framework for assessing community-based forest management: Lessons from Madagascar","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Raik","given":"Daniela B"},{"family":"Decker","given":"Daniel J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It includes formal kinds of laws and structures but also includes other kinds of collective action, rulemaking, institutions, and general social coordination ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UPiFn1XH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dietz, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Dietz, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32499,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WJQJH47F"],"itemData":{"id":32499,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"5652","language":"en","page":"1907–12","title":"The Struggle to Govern the Commons","volume":"302","author":[{"family":"Dietz","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"},{"family":"Stern","given":"Paul C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Governance research also addresses impacts on sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"k1BUWK16","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kenward, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Kenward, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32418,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VH2M6PQ7"],"itemData":{"id":32418,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","ISSN":"0027-8424","issue":"13","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","note":"number: 13\\npublisher: National Acad Sciences","page":"5308-5312","title":"Identifying governance strategies that effectively support ecosystem services, resource sustainability, and biodiversity","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Kenward","given":"RE"},{"family":"Whittingham","given":"MJ"},{"family":"Arampatzis","given":"S"},{"family":"Manos","given":"BD"},{"family":"Hahn","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Terry","given":"A"},{"family":"Simoncini","given":"R"},{"family":"Alcorn","given":"J"},{"family":"Bastian","given":"O"},{"family":"Donlan","given":"M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In the context of this assessment, the experts are particularly focused on environmental governance or the rules, practices, policies, institutions, and mechanism that shape how humans interact with the environment and influence environmental outcomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a0nuQ7Dh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lemos & Agrawal, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Lemos & Agrawal, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34677,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/298S22RU"],"itemData":{"id":34677,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev.energy.31.042605.135621","ISSN":"1543-5938, 1545-2050","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Environ. Resour.","language":"en","page":"297-325","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Environmental Governance","URL":"http://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev.energy.31.042605.135621","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Lemos","given":"Maria Carmen"},{"family":"Agrawal","given":"Arun"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It includes those mechanisms, and tools that allow actors (public and private sector, non-governmental organizations, local communities) at different scales (local-global) to manage conflicts, seek points of consensus and take accountable actions and decisions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0dU9BUeg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lemos & Agrawal, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Lemos & Agrawal, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34677,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/298S22RU"],"itemData":{"id":34677,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev.energy.31.042605.135621","ISSN":"1543-5938, 1545-2050","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Environ. Resour.","language":"en","page":"297-325","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Environmental Governance","URL":"http://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev.energy.31.042605.135621","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Lemos","given":"Maria Carmen"},{"family":"Agrawal","given":"Arun"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      Although these governance arrangements can be viewed in isolation from one another, a system view of institutions is needed to account for how different actors, laws, regulations, and mechanisms of governance function together. Pluralistic governance arrangements - a mix, hybrid, or bricolage of formal and informal arrangements – tend to coexist at the same time ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bB1CTyVK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jentoft & Bavinck, 2014; North, 1991)","plainCitation":"(Jentoft & Bavinck, 2014; North, 1991)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34408,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8GT4F2B8"],"itemData":{"id":34408,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2014.10.005","ISSN":"18773435","journalAbbreviation":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","language":"en","page":"71-77","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Interactive governance for sustainable fisheries: dealing with legal pluralism","title-short":"Interactive governance for sustainable fisheries","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1877343514000773","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Jentoft","given":"Svein"},{"family":"Bavinck","given":"Maarten"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12]]}}},{"id":32425,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HCAPNWAL"],"itemData":{"id":32425,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Economic Perspectives","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"97–112,","title":"Institutions","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/1942704.","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"North","given":"Douglass C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Different actors (e.g., governments) can be simultaneously involved in multiple, and sometimes conflicting, positions of governance at different times ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"d9mHlzAj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26013,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GJ33X5SZ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HYS9KYFV"],"itemData":{"id":26013,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Indigenous Use and Management of Marine Resources","language":"en","page":"13–31","title":"Commons theory for marine resource management in a complex world","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."}],"editor":[{"family":"Kishigami","given":"N."},{"family":"Savelle","given":"J.M."},{"literal":"eds"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  


Figure 4.5. A practical framework for understanding the objectives, attributes, and elements of environmental governance. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TJWtATnW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bennett and Satterfield 2018)","plainCitation":"(Bennett and Satterfield 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32347,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZXEAZW7"],"itemData":{"id":32347,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12600","ISSN":"1755-263X, 1755-263X","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"CONSERVATION LETTERS","language":"en","page":"e12600","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Environmental governance: A practical framework to guide design, evaluation, and analysis","title-short":"Environmental governance","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/conl.12600","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"Nathan J."},{"family":"Satterfield","given":"Terre"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}) under license CC BY-4.0.
      
      Institutions, structures and processes of environmental governance that are among the most important drivers of the use of wild species and are equally critical mediators of how other drivers (e.g., climate change) influence use.  Institutions are commonly defined as the ‘rules of the game’, norms, values and procedures, which shape human interactions with nature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EiMdY4SJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brechin, 2003; McCay & Jentoft, 1996)","plainCitation":"(Brechin, 2003; McCay & Jentoft, 1996)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32313,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BIYAPV4X"],"itemData":{"id":32313,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Contested Nature. Promoting International Biodiversity with Social Justice in the Twenty-First Century","language":"en","note":"type: and PC West,","page":"159–83","title":"Crafting Conservation Globally and Locally","author":[{"family":"Brechin","given":"Steven R."},{"family":"Wilshusen","given":"Peter R."},{"family":"Benjamin","given":"Charles E."}],"editor":[{"family":"Eds. SR Brechin","given":"CL Fortwangler","suffix":"PR Wilshusen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":34557,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IDJ8JB2F"],"itemData":{"id":34557,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"10.1080/08941929609380969","ISSN":"0894-1920, 1521-0723","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Society & Natural Resources","language":"en","page":"237-250","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"From the bottom up: Participatory issues in fisheries management","title-short":"From the bottom up","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/08941929609380969","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"McCay","given":"Bonnie J."},{"family":"Jentoft","given":"Svein"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. They can give rise to compliance or resistance. An important question guiding the analysis of the evidence presented in this section is what are the characteristics of good governance in relation to the use of wild species? In other words, what kinds of governance arrangements are protective or contribute to sustainable use? Institutions that operate from the local to global level and comprise formal arrangements (e.g., laws, regulation, treaties), are also referred to as statutory or de jure governance arrangements, while informal arrangements (e.g., social norms, taboos, sanctions) with informal institutions are referred to as customary or de facto governance arrangements ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nvvGJJZ9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(North, 1991)","plainCitation":"(North, 1991)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32425,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HCAPNWAL"],"itemData":{"id":32425,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Economic Perspectives","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"97–112,","title":"Institutions","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/1942704.","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"North","given":"Douglass C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Institutions can be seen as different (in)formal regimes and coalitions for collective action and inter-agent coordination, ranging from public-private cooperation and contracting schemes to organizational networking and policy arrangements ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8UnqbkSe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Geels, 2004; Teisman & Klein, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Geels, 2004; Teisman & Klein, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34668,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DJQ52HSQ"],"itemData":{"id":34668,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Research Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.respol.2004.01.015","ISSN":"00487333","issue":"6-7","journalAbbreviation":"Research Policy","language":"en","page":"897-920","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"From sectoral systems of innovation to socio-technical systems","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0048733304000496","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Geels","given":"Frank W."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",9]]}}},{"id":32417,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GD4AZIRR"],"itemData":{"id":32417,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Public private partnerships: theory and practice in international perspective, Routledge","journalAbbreviation":"Public private partnerships: theory and practice in international perspective, Routledge","page":"84-101","title":"Governing public-private partnerships","author":[{"family":"Teisman","given":"GR"},{"family":"Klein","given":"PG"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      Policies are defined as those deliberate and specific principles that guide decisions or express a specific intent; like laws they are implemented through specific policy instruments, procedures and mechanisms, and the outcomes of which are measured against the original intent. Environmental policy specifically focuses on problems arising from human impact on the environment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vQCU8BpM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schelly & Banerjee, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Schelly & Banerjee, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32415,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WW2S5RK9"],"itemData":{"id":32415,"type":"book","ISBN":"1-138-29650-3","publisher":"Routledge","title":"Environmental policy and the pursuit of sustainability","author":[{"family":"Schelly","given":"Chelsea"},{"family":"Banerjee","given":"Aparajita"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Processes, for the purposes of this chapter, refer to the various kinds of negotiated relationships and interactions between actor groups, and the ways in which they lead to resolution of a problem or conflict of sustainable use. A variety of synthesis work related to environmental governance points to various kinds of success factors or design principles Box 4.1. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Z25Eu1oz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Armitage, 2011; N. J. Bennett & Satterfield, 2018; Lemos & Agrawal, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Armitage, 2011; N. J. Bennett & Satterfield, 2018; Lemos & Agrawal, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34677,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/298S22RU"],"itemData":{"id":34677,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev.energy.31.042605.135621","ISSN":"1543-5938, 1545-2050","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Environ. Resour.","language":"en","page":"297-325","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Environmental Governance","URL":"http://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev.energy.31.042605.135621","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Lemos","given":"Maria Carmen"},{"family":"Agrawal","given":"Arun"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",11]]}}},{"id":34067,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L8GRFD3Q"],"itemData":{"id":34067,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2011.02.011","ISSN":"0308597X","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"703-711","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Early-stage transformation of coastal marine governance in Vietnam?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X11000479","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Armitage","given":"Derek"},{"family":"Marschke","given":"Melissa"},{"family":"Tuyen","given":"Truong","non-dropping-particle":"van"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",9]]}}},{"id":32347,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZXEAZW7"],"itemData":{"id":32347,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12600","ISSN":"1755-263X, 1755-263X","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"CONSERVATION LETTERS","language":"en","page":"e12600","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Environmental governance: A practical framework to guide design, evaluation, and analysis","title-short":"Environmental governance","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/conl.12600","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"Nathan J."},{"family":"Satterfield","given":"Terre"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Box 4.1. Success factors for governance systems in managing use of wild species 

* Is there a good fit between the scale of the rule system and the scale of the use issue?  Is there coordination within and between different systems of rules (across geographic scales) and between formal and informal rule systems (e.g., customary law of indigenous peoples and local communities)?
* Can rule systems respond to variability in patterns of wild species use? Are the rule systems adaptive and flexible (not rigid)?
* Do the rule systems include mechanisms of ongoing learning (i.e., monitoring)?
* Are the rule systems based on science and other kinds of knowledges of stakeholders and indigenous peoples and local communities dependent upon wild species? 
* Are the rules seen as legitimate and enforceable? 
* Are there diverse stakeholders engaging in rulemaking? 
* Are the rule systems considered just (able to address inequities in the benefits of use and manage conflicts between different users)?    
* 

   Much about governance arrangements, and their success in addressing questions of sustainable use, hinges on property rights associated with the species and its ecosystem, including its definition as public, private, club, or commons. Most wild species globally are framed as commons, others as public good, with fewer defined as club goods or private property. While a defacto position of governments is to enclose wild species that exist as a commons and govern use like other kinds of public or private goods, this is not feasible. Different kinds of institutions (pluralism) are needed for different kinds of resources and in different kinds of property rights contexts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xpBbyaqN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ostrom 2009a)","plainCitation":"(Ostrom 2009a)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":36,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2NACCRB4"],"itemData":{"id":36,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1172133","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5939","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"419-422","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A General Framework for Analyzing Sustainability of Social-Ecological Systems","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1172133","volume":"325","author":[{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",7,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
   
4.2.2.2. Formal, statutory governance arrangements 
Key messages:
* There is a growing number of formal laws and policies that support sustainable use that have been developed among nation states as well as at regional scales that facilitate sustainable use. The majority of these laws relate to large fauna and timber, with more limited laws and policies related to smaller fauna and flora.  
* Formal systems of governance do not commonly account for indigenous peoples and local communities' values of biodiversity but rather tend to support privatization and commercialization.
* Many of the policies governing wild species use are weak (lacking clarity in institutional responsibility). Many also lack legitimacy and/or are difficult to enforce due to lack of engagement and consultation with stakeholders (particularly rural communities). Lack of coordination among different policies (which can sometimes conflict) compound the challenges of legitimacy and enforcement;
* Pluralistic approaches that account for the diverse values and uses of wild species can be more effective in supporting and nurturing norms and practices of sustainable use. 
* In cases where multiple institutions have been unsystematically developed (in an adhoc fashion), there are ambiguities and conflicts that complicate understanding and compliance with the “rules” of sustainable use.
* Pluralistic approaches that draw upon international and national policy frameworks and laws, and integrate customary law and local practices, are the most effective for supporting and promoting the sustainable use of wild species. This requires ensuring that conflicts and overlapping mandates are avoided, and coordination and complementarity encouraged.  
* Institutions that embrace science and indigenous and local knowledge in the way they are designed and implemented are more effective in that they match ecological and socio-economic conditions; ongoing monitoring and evaluation of the effectiveness of institutions and their sensitivity to variabilities and changes in ecosystems and society also produced more sustainable outcomes. 
* However, laws regulating wild species are often of poor quality, lacking clarity in institutional responsibilities, do not result of participatory processes, engagement and consultation with stakeholders, particularly rural communities, are not coordinated with other measures, and are not implemented. This limits their effectiveness. 

4.2.2.2.1. International agreements and conventions 
Since at least the late 1800s and with increasing regularity in the past half century, countries have negotiated hundreds of international legal agreements to address environmental problems they cannot resolve alone”ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1YNT0mKU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. B. Mitchell, 2003)","plainCitation":"(R. B. Mitchell, 2003)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32414,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W4CBQLU2"],"itemData":{"id":32414,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual review of environment and resources","ISSN":"1543-5938","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annual review of environment and resources","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Annual Reviews 4139 El Camino Way, PO Box 10139, Palo Alto, CA 94303-0139, USA","page":"429-461","title":"International environmental agreements: a survey of their features, formation, and effects","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Mitchell","given":"Ronald B"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some of these agreements pertain to sustainable use of wild species.  International agreements are defined here as legally binding arrangements among two or more states (e.g., treaties, conventions, accords, or modifications of such arrangements) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"G3RKvZeA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aust, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Aust, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32413,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QAWZT9GE"],"itemData":{"id":32413,"type":"book","ISBN":"1-107-02384-X","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Modern treaty law and practice","author":[{"family":"Aust","given":"Anthony"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The Convention on Biological Diversity  is among the most relevant to this assessment, as its’ core objective is to facilitate sustainable use: “… the conservation of biodiversity, the sustainable use of its components and the fair and equitable sharing of benefits arising out of the utilization of genetic resources…” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LfNEkt7t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CBD, 2020)","plainCitation":"(CBD, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32348,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QLIIVMVP"],"itemData":{"id":32348,"type":"report","event-place":"Montreal, Canada","note":"ISBN-9789292256883","page":"211","publisher-place":"Montreal, Canada","title":"Global Biodiversity Outlook 5","URL":"https://www.cbd.int/gbo/gbo5/publication/gbo-5-en.pdf","author":[{"family":"CBD","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. According to the the Convention, member states are required to “as far as possible and as appropriate, adopt economically and socially sound measures that act as incentives for the conservation and sustainable use of components of biological diversity” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yBEsjA2Z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CBD 2020)","plainCitation":"(CBD 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32348,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QLIIVMVP"],"itemData":{"id":32348,"type":"report","event-place":"Montreal, Canada","note":"ISBN-9789292256883","page":"211","publisher-place":"Montreal, Canada","title":"Global Biodiversity Outlook 5","URL":"https://www.cbd.int/gbo/gbo5/publication/gbo-5-en.pdf","author":[{"family":"CBD","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Article 11). 
      The implementation of the Convention on Biological Diversity has been enlivened by the creation of targets, guidelines and principles to conserve biodiversity. The Aichi Targets aim to “reduce the direct pressures on biodiversity and promote sustainable use”; a key factor and mechanism in consideration is poverty reduction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Zpvv4yrI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CBD, 2020)","plainCitation":"(CBD, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32348,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QLIIVMVP"],"itemData":{"id":32348,"type":"report","event-place":"Montreal, Canada","note":"ISBN-9789292256883","page":"211","publisher-place":"Montreal, Canada","title":"Global Biodiversity Outlook 5","URL":"https://www.cbd.int/gbo/gbo5/publication/gbo-5-en.pdf","author":[{"family":"CBD","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These targets, however, have not been easy to reach for many member states as indicated by the IPBES Global Assessment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gQRBdrQU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This may be due to ambiguity in, and excessive complexity of, the targets and a lack of appropriate and quantifiable measures for tracking progress, as well as lack of political will to make the necessary changes in management and policy ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"T1EwyuQM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Butchart, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Butchart, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":900,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V8NSEIZH"],"itemData":{"id":900,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Global assessment report of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","language":"en","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Chapter 3. Assessing progress towards meeting major international objectives related to nature and nature’s contributions to people","author":[{"family":"Butchart","given":"S.H.M."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S.M."},{"family":"Adams","given":"C."},{"family":"Bennett","given":"E."},{"family":"Czúcz","given":"B."},{"family":"Galetto","given":"L."},{"family":"Galvin","given":"K."},{"family":"Reyes-García","given":"V."},{"family":"R.","given":"Gerber L."},{"family":"Bekele","given":"T."},{"family":"Jetz","given":"W."},{"family":"Kosamu","given":"I.B.M."},{"family":"Palomo","given":"M.G."},{"family":"Panahi","given":"M."},{"family":"Selig","given":"E.R."},{"family":"Singh","given":"G.S."},{"family":"Tarkhnishvili","given":"D."},{"family":"Xu","given":"H."},{"family":"Lynch","given":"A.J."},{"family":"H.","given":"Mwampamba T."},{"family":"Samakov","given":"A."}],"editor":[{"family":"Brondízio","given":"E.S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H.T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Strategies to achieve these targets developed by some nation-states, include a range of formal and top-down approaches aimed at planning, education and monitoring. The targets that are anticipated to be met or exceeded include Target 11 (Protected Areas). “Recent analysis shows that if national commitments are implemented as proposed, global protected area coverage will be on track to meet or exceed the 17% and 10% coverage targets for terrestrial and marine protected areas” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wjFJBHao","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bacon, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Bacon, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32438,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/INX2HTRM"],"itemData":{"id":32438,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2019.125723","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"125723","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Aichi Biodiversity Target 11 in the like-minded megadiverse countries","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S161713811830428X","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Bacon","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Gannon","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Stephen","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Seyoum-Edjigu","given":"Edjigayehu"},{"family":"Schmidt","given":"Megan"},{"family":"Lang","given":"Barbara"},{"family":"Sandwith","given":"Trevor"},{"family":"Xin","given":"Jing"},{"family":"Arora","given":"Sujata"},{"family":"Adham","given":"Khairul Naim"},{"family":"Espinoza","given":"Andrew John Rhodes"},{"family":"Qwathekana","given":"Malta"},{"family":"Prates","given":"Ana Paula Leite"},{"family":"Shestakov","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Cooper","given":"David"},{"family":"Ervin","given":"Jamison"},{"family":"Dias","given":"Braulio Ferreira de Souza"},{"family":"Leles","given":"Bruno"},{"family":"Attallah","given":"Marc"},{"family":"Mulongoy","given":"Jo"},{"family":"Gidda","given":"Sarat Babu"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Part of the challenge in meeting Aichi Targets has been the limited availability of meaningful indicators and mechanisms for tracking progress.  To date, none of the Aichi Targets have been met; the greatest progress has been made on Aichi Targets 1, 11, 16, 17, 19 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"B0zRf992","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CBD, 2020)","plainCitation":"(CBD, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32348,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QLIIVMVP"],"itemData":{"id":32348,"type":"report","event-place":"Montreal, Canada","note":"ISBN-9789292256883","page":"211","publisher-place":"Montreal, Canada","title":"Global Biodiversity Outlook 5","URL":"https://www.cbd.int/gbo/gbo5/publication/gbo-5-en.pdf","author":[{"family":"CBD","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Various efforts have been made to assess nation-state progress towards these targets. Although wealth (measured by the gross domestic product) may be a factor, “quality of governance” explains much of variation in public and state investment in biodiversity conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Nnl7E3K1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Baynham-Herd, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Baynham-Herd, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32439,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F2PCX96W"],"itemData":{"id":32439,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"SUMMARY\\n            Growing concern about the biodiversity crisis has led to a proliferation of conservation responses, but with wide variation between countries in the levels of engagement and investment. Much of this variation is inevitably attributed to differences between nations in wealth. However, the relationship between environmentalism and wealth is complex and it is increasingly apparent that other factors are also involved. We review hypotheses that have been developed to explain variation in broad environmentalism and show that many of the factors that explain such variation in individuals, such as wealth, age and experience, also explain differences between nation states. We then assess the extent to which these factors explain variation between nation states in responses to and investment in the more specific area of biodiversity conservation. Unexpectedly, quality of governance explained substantially more variation in public and state investment in biodiversity conservation than did direct measures of wealth. The results inform assessments of where conservation investments might most profitably be directed in the future and suggest that metrics relating to governance might be of considerable use in conservation planning.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S037689291700056X","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Conserv.","language":"en","page":"407-418","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Governance explains variation in national responses to the biodiversity crisis","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S037689291700056X/type/journal_article","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Baynham-Herd","given":"Zachary"},{"family":"Amano","given":"Tatsuya"},{"family":"Sutherland","given":"William J."},{"family":"Donald","given":"Paul F."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other analysis has revealed that greater engagement in developing national plans for achieving biodiversity (Target 17) also does not correlate with greater gross domestic product ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1u4ryqFA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Whitehorn, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Whitehorn, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32421,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G2IBW4SP"],"itemData":{"id":32421,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological conservation","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological conservation","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"157-163","title":"Mainstreaming biodiversity: A review of national strategies","volume":"235","author":[{"family":"Whitehorn","given":"Penelope R"},{"family":"Navarro","given":"Laetitia M"},{"family":"Schröter","given":"Matthias"},{"family":"Fernandez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Rotllan-Puig","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Marques","given":"Alexandra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.



Figure 4.6 - Global distribution of the World Bank’s worldwide governance indicators.
Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Gnu9mxHl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Baynham-Herd, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Baynham-Herd, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32439,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F2PCX96W"],"itemData":{"id":32439,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"SUMMARY\\n            Growing concern about the biodiversity crisis has led to a proliferation of conservation responses, but with wide variation between countries in the levels of engagement and investment. Much of this variation is inevitably attributed to differences between nations in wealth. However, the relationship between environmentalism and wealth is complex and it is increasingly apparent that other factors are also involved. We review hypotheses that have been developed to explain variation in broad environmentalism and show that many of the factors that explain such variation in individuals, such as wealth, age and experience, also explain differences between nation states. We then assess the extent to which these factors explain variation between nation states in responses to and investment in the more specific area of biodiversity conservation. Unexpectedly, quality of governance explained substantially more variation in public and state investment in biodiversity conservation than did direct measures of wealth. The results inform assessments of where conservation investments might most profitably be directed in the future and suggest that metrics relating to governance might be of considerable use in conservation planning.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S037689291700056X","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Envir. Conserv.","language":"en","page":"407-418","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Governance explains variation in national responses to the biodiversity crisis","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S037689291700056X/type/journal_article","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Baynham-Herd","given":"Zachary"},{"family":"Amano","given":"Tatsuya"},{"family":"Sutherland","given":"William J."},{"family":"Donald","given":"Paul F."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. License CC-BY 4.0. The designations employed and the presentation of material on the maps used in the assessment do not imply the expression of any opinion whatsoever on the part of IPBES concerning the legal status of any country, territory, city or area or of its authorities, or concerning the delimitation of its frontiers or boundaries. These maps have been prepared or used for the sole purpose of facilitating the assessment of the broad biogeographical areas represented therein and for purposes of representing scientific data spatially.

      The Addis Ababa Principles and Guidelines for the Sustainable Use of Biodiversity  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uwwthMqy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CBD, 2004)","plainCitation":"(CBD, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32380,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q9FALHCZ"],"itemData":{"id":32380,"type":"book","abstract":"The Addis Ababa principles and guidelines for the sustainable use of biodiversity provide a framework for assisting governments, indigenous and local communities, resource managers, the private sector and other stakeholders, about how to ensure that their uses of biological diversity will not lead to its long-term decline. The practical principles in most instances apply to both consumptive and non-consumptive uses of biodiversity components. They take into account requirements related to policies, laws, and regulations; management of biological diversity; socio-economic conditions; and information, research, and education.","event-place":"Montreal","ISBN":"978-92-9225-003-4","language":"en","note":"OCLC: 57432917","publisher":"Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity","publisher-place":"Montreal","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Addis Ababa principles and guidelines for the sustainable use of biodiversity.","author":[{"family":"CBD","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} challenges states not only to examine formal legal instruments for achieving these targets but also customary law and traditions when drafting new legislation and regulations and creating cooperative and supportive linkages between all levels of governance. Throughout, the principles address drivers and causes of unsustainable use, including deficient policy frameworks, lack of respect for the rights and stewardship of local communities (Principle 2), market distortions (Principle 3), the need for integrated and interdisciplinary research and participatory approaches (Principals 6 and 9), and the need for more effective education and public awareness programs on sustainable use (principle 14). The Convention on Biological Diversity process in turn spurs national governments to draft legislation to implement commitments under the Convention on Biological Diversity, and creates a forum for dialogue and global decision-making relating to biodiversity conservation and the sustainable use of its components. On the whole it is recognized that both top-down  (e.g., statutory laws) and bottom-up approaches (i.e., informal and customary institutions) are needed and should work together to achieve the targets of sustainable use as in the Convention on Biological Diversity. 

Box 4.2. New Zealand National Targets to Enhance Implementation of the Convention on Biological Diversity: “Whanau, Hapu and Iwi are Better Able to Practices their Responsibilities as Kaitiaki” (Related to Aichi Target 1, 18).

M?ori have a strong interconnection with their natural environment governed by the cutural ethic of kaitiakitanga (guardianship). This ethic confers obligations on wh?nau (family), hap? (sub-tribe) and iwi (tribe) (collectively tangata whenua) to steward biodiversity as articulated as: taonga (treasures), including species of indigenous flora and fauna, wai m?ori (freshwater), w?hi tapu or w?hi taonga (treasured or sacred sites), and whenua (land). The Treaty of Waitangi settlement process has been one part of this assessment, with settlements achieved with 86 groups to date. New Zealand Government engagement with M?ori through the relationships supported by these settlements has shown that tangata whenua have worked proactively to lead locally based and culturally monitored conservation projects and indigenous biodiversity protection. For example, Waikato- Tainui fisheries bylaws, effective from 2014, will continue to support sustainable fishing practices and native eel migration while recognising traditional management practices. The Manaaki Tuna Project (supported by the Waikato River Clean-up Trust) is a completed multi-year project to gather and preserve Waikato-Tainui histories associated with the Waikato River. Tracking the implementation is being done through a number of quantative and qualitative indicators (e.g., with intellectual property being held by tangata.

Sources: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"34chYffp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Department of Conservation 2019)","plainCitation":"(Department of Conservation 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32437,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GVKXMIPP"],"itemData":{"id":32437,"type":"document","title":"New Zealand’s Sixth National Report to the United Nations Convention on Biological Diversity. Reporting period: 2014–2018. Department of Conservation, Wellington, New Zealand.","author":[{"literal":"Department of Conservation"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}

      Although highly problematic and efforts to meet associated targets are opaque in many jurisdictions, the Convention on Biodiversity remains a critical tool for signaling the importance of biodiversity conservation and sustainable use of wild species.
      The Convention on the Conservation of Migratory Species of Wild Animals (CMS) provides a global platform for the conservation and sustainable use of migratory animals and their habitats. The Convention brings together the States through which migratory animals pass, the Range States, and lays the legal foundation for internationally coordinated conservation measures throughout a migratory range. The aim of the Convention on the Conservation of Migratory Species of Wild Animals is the long-term conservation of migratory species that cross international jurisdictional boundaries in the course of their migration. It has been negotiated with the primary objective in mind to endure the coordinated management of migratory species that are shared by multiple states. While the convention does not have a focus on harvest per se, it does include provisions that influence the possibility of use of species by Parties, e.g., in relation to taking of species listed on its Appendix I. The Convention also acts as a framework convention, under which tailored multilateral agreement on individual species or groups of related species can be negotiated, at regional or global levels.  The Convention includes animals of the following classes: Mammalia, Aves, Reptilia, Actynoptergii, Chondrichthyes and Insecta. 
   The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, which came into effect in 1975, was developed with the recognition that international co-operation was essential for the protection of certain species of wild fauna and flora against overexploitation through international trade. The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora has 183 contracting Parties (www.cites.org accessed 30 April 2021) and operates primarily through regulating trade in species that are listed on one of three Appendices depending on the level of threat.
* Appendix I includes those species threatened with extinction which are or may be affected by trade. Trade in specimens of these species must be subject to particularly strict regulation in order not to endanger further their survival and must only be authorized in exceptional circumstances.
* Appendix II includes those species, which although not necessarily immediately threatened with extinction, may become so unless trade in specimens of such species is subject to strict regulation in order to avoid utilization incompatible with their survival. It also includes other species which must be subject to regulation in order that trade in specimens of the first set may be brought under effective control, sometimes referred to as ‘look alike’ species.
* Appendix III includes species which any Party identifies as needing regulation within its jurisdiction for the purpose of preventing or restricting exploitation, and which needs the co-operation of other Parties in order to regulate trade.

      The aim of CITES is to protect wild species from over exploitation associated with international trade and avoid utilization that is incompatible with their survival ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cNHZdCvm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hutton & Dickson, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Hutton & Dickson, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32334,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WGV2EI39"],"itemData":{"id":32334,"type":"book","publisher":"Earthscan Publications, London","title":"Endangered Species, Threatened Convention. The Past, Present and Future of CITES","author":[{"family":"Hutton","given":"J. M."},{"family":"Dickson","given":"Barney"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora  is not per se a treaty to promote trade and use of wildlife ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dkFBnoEu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(OECD, 1997)","plainCitation":"(OECD, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32321,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7DJJ4QAW"],"itemData":{"id":32321,"type":"report","number":"COM/TD/ENV(97)10/FINAL","publisher":"Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development, Paris","title":"Experience with the Use of Trade Measures in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species (CITES)","author":[{"family":"OECD","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora vision 2008-2020 aimed to “Conserve biodiversity and contribute to its sustainable use by ensuring that no species of wild fauna or flora becomes or remains subject to unsustainable exploitation through international trade” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lvkkXQ8T","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wijnstekers, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Wijnstekers, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7884,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H82SAK9F"],"itemData":{"id":7884,"type":"book","event-place":"Budakeszi, Hungary","ISBN":"978-963-88778-4-0","language":"en","note":"OCLC: 1056295665","publisher":"International Council for Game and Wildlife Conservation","publisher-place":"Budakeszi, Hungary","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"The evolution of CITES: a reference to the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora","title-short":"The evolution of CITES","URL":"http://www.cites.org/common/resources/Evolution_of_CITES_9.pdf","author":[{"family":"Wijnstekers","given":"Willem"},{"literal":"International Council for Game and Wildlife Conservation"},{"literal":"Secretariat of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, the vision for the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora  post 2020 is that “by 2030, all international trade in wild fauna and flora is legal and sustainable, consistent with the long-term conservation of species, and thereby contributing to halting biodiversity loss, to ensuring its sustainable use, and to achieving the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development”ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4YfqL0zB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CITES CoP 18.3, 2019)","plainCitation":"(CITES CoP 18.3, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32329,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/38S5PK28"],"itemData":{"id":32329,"type":"document","publisher":"CITES Secretariat, Geneva","title":"CITES Strategic Vision: 2021-2030","author":[{"family":"CITES CoP 18.3","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The effective implementation of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora  would therefore be expected to act as a driver for more sustainable levels of use and trade as well as the adoption of practices and processes that ensure greater levels of sustainability. This should be true for all species listed in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Appendices but could also be a more general driver for ensuring that use of wild species is sustainable by raising awareness of the extent and impacts of trade in wild species, the provision of tools and resources, promotion of institutions to ensure sustainable use, and adoption of more sustainable practices by affected industries. The purpose of this section is to assess the evidence for the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora acting as a driver of more sustainable levels of use of wild species that is consistent with the vision for all international trade in wild fauna and flora to be legal and sustainable. Discussion of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora as a policy tool and options for better governance of trade in wild species is provided in Chapter 6.
      The assessment included evidence in the period from 1985-2021. The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora came into effect in 1975 so the period being assessed allows ten years from inception for the resolutions and decisions of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora to take effect. The assessment relied extensively on information curated by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (www.cites.org), which includes all formal documentation as well as information documents, reports and links. To assess independent sources of evidence, an initial search was undertaken using Web of Science in the period 1985-2021 for “Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species” OR “CITES. This yielded 416 publications. For additional analysis on non detrimental findings, the search comprised terms “non detriment* finding” OR “NDF” AND “CITES” and the search was conducted in Google Scholar and Web of Science. Additional sources of evidence were obtained from the reference lists of these publications as well as from the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora information documents and resources accessed from the website (www.cites.org).
	
Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora as a driver for more sustainable practices
The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora adopted a series of indicators to measure progress with the vision 2008-2020 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NqjBcEny","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u8220{}CITES Strategic Vision 2008-2020,\\\\uc0\\\\u8221{} n.d.)","plainCitation":"(“CITES Strategic Vision 2008-2020,” n.d.)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32382,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZD444JDG"],"itemData":{"id":32382,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Notification to the Parties No. 2015/032; Annex 2","language":"en","title":"CITES Strategic Vision 2008-2020"}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} Notification 2015/032 Annex 2). Several of these indicators are appropriate for the assessment of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora as a driver, particularly those that measure progress with laws, institutions and tools to ensure that trade in wild species is sustainable and those that measure outcomes for the species regulated under the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora.
      In terms of improving laws, institutions and tools, the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora requires all contracting Parties to put in place appropriate legislation and institutions to implement the Convention, including separate Management and Scientific Authorities. One of the primary roles of Scientific Authorities is to assess risks associated with trade and determine that trade is not “detrimental to the survival of that species” (Article III, 2 (a), 3 (a), 5(a); Article IV, 2 (a), 6 (a)) and “maintains that species throughout its range at a level consistent with its role in the ecosystems in which it occurs” (Article IV, paragraph 3). These are consistent with the ecological dimensions of sustainable use identified in Chapter 2. As a result, the increase in Parties to the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora from 85 to 183 should have resulted in the establishment of institutions and governance systems to ensure that use of wild species is more sustainable. Parties to the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora vary in the extent to which they have appropriate legislation and institutions, reported in 2019 as 32 (17%) with legislation that does not support implementation of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, 43 (23%) with legislation that only partially supports the implementation and 101 with legislation and institutions required to fully implement the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Zjvyupxt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CITES CoP 18 Doc. 26 (Rev 1) 2019, 18)","plainCitation":"(CITES CoP 18 Doc. 26 (Rev 1) 2019, 18)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32330,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GEZJQGVM"],"itemData":{"id":32330,"type":"document","publisher":"CITES Secretariat, Geneva","title":"National Laws for the Implementation of the Convention","author":[{"family":"CITES CoP 18 Doc. 26 (Rev 1)","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"locator":"18"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This means that the systems to ensure more sustainable use have been strengthened as a result of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, at least in the 55% of Parties who are fully compliant with the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora and to a lesser extent in the 23% that are partially compliant. The remaining levels of non-compliance weaken the role of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora as a driver of more sustainable and legal use of wild species.
      A further question is whether non detrimental findings in all these countries have been implemented in a way that promotes more sustainable practices or levels of harvest and trade. The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Resolution Conf 16.7 rev. CoP 17 emphasizes that non detrimental findings should be based on the best available science and guidelines for non detrimental findings have been periodically refined through workshops and working groups to support this objective ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rz9n1RYN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. J. Smith, 2011)","plainCitation":"(M. J. Smith, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32349,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4DDB928S"],"itemData":{"id":32349,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) came into effect in 1975 to protect certain species of wild fauna and flora against over-exploitation through international trade. Determining which trade is detrimental to the survival of species in the wild can be a major difficulty in the implementation of CITES by national authorities, partly due to limited knowledge and understanding of the species' biology, management, and the impacts of harvesting. Some of this knowledge could be acquired through targeted scientific research. However, to date there exists no general overview of the current use of biological information in determining detriment in CITES to help scientists identify research priorities. For an international meeting in 2008, over 100 scientists and regulators compiled 60 case studies covering a wide range of CITES-listed taxa, outlining how information on the biology, harvesting and management might be used to determine whether international trade is detrimental. We used these case studies, workshop conclusions, and other published literature, to identify 10 potential research directions for the scientific community which, if addressed, could greatly assist in the making of Non-Detriment Findings. We hope that this will encourage more scientists to study CITES-listed species, and foster more collaboration between research scientists, CITES national authorities. CITES technical committees and local communities. The case studies highlight a general need for advice on how to identify and manage levels of risk involved when assessing possible detriment, and for advice on assessing detriment under complex harvesting scenarios such as when multiple species, or parts of individuals, are harvested. Broadly, they highlight an opportunity for scientists to further develop a body of scientific studies that propose, refine and adapt methods for assessing detrimental trade in CITES-listed taxa. Comparisons within life-form groups indicated the potential for the identification of practical advice that could apply to groups of taxa. The case studies highlighted a widespread need for more information gathering studies of CITES-listed taxa such as the broader impacts of harvesting on populations and ecosystems, and the potential long-term evolutionary impacts. The case studies also highlighted the need for practical advice on how to implement adaptive management programmes and for research into enterprises based on the harvesting of CITES-listed species from the wild. (C) 2010 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.","archive_location":"WOS:000287168100008","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2010.10.018","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"1","page":"82-91","title":"Assessing the impacts of international trade on CITES-listed species: Current practices and opportunities for scientific research","URL":"://WOS:000287168100008","volume":"144","author":[{"family":"Smith","given":"M. J."},{"family":"Benitez-Diaz","given":"H."},{"family":"Clemente-Munoz","given":"M. A."},{"family":"Donaldson","given":"J."},{"family":"Hutton","given":"J. M."},{"family":"McGough","given":"H. N."},{"family":"Medellin","given":"R. A."},{"family":"Morgan","given":"D. H. W."},{"family":"O'Criodain","given":"C."},{"family":"Oldfield","given":"T. E. E."},{"family":"Schippmann","given":"U."},{"family":"Williams","given":"R. J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. General guidelines for non detrimental findings  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3qCMoEYb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rosser & Haywood, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Rosser & Haywood, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32335,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8KEVF4ZC"],"itemData":{"id":32335,"type":"book","publisher":"IUCN, Gland Switzerland and Cambridge, UK","title":"Guidance for CITES Scientific Authorities: checklist to assist in making non-detriment findings for Appendix II exports","author":[{"family":"Rosser","given":"Alison"},{"family":"Haywood","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} have included guidance and templates to assess the impacts of harvest and trade based on biological attributes of the species together with information on population status, management and controls, protection, and levels of trade. Further guidance has been developed at least for trees ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Z7heYEzl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wolf, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Wolf, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32333,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K3Z3ZZUV"],"itemData":{"id":32333,"type":"book","publisher":"German Federal Agency for Nature Conservation, Bonn","title":"CITES Non Detriment Findings for timber. A nine step process to support Scientific Authorities make science-based non detriment findings (NDFs) for timber species listed in Appendix II.","author":[{"family":"Wolf","given":"D."},{"family":"Oldfield","given":"T.E.E."},{"family":"McGough","given":"H. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, perennial plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9zGR0F6m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wolf, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Wolf, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32338,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RRJAC3ZI"],"itemData":{"id":32338,"type":"book","publisher":"German Federal Agency for Nature Conservation, Bonn","title":"CITES Non-detriment Findings Guidance for Perennial Plants. A nine step process to support CITES Scientific Authorities making science-based non-detriment findings (NDFs) for species listed on CITES Appendix II.","author":[{"family":"Wolf","given":"D."},{"family":"Oldfield","given":"T. E. E."},{"family":"Schippmann","given":"U."},{"family":"McGough","given":"H. N."},{"family":"Leaman","given":"D.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, sharks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"j2LE5iAR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CITES AC28 Doc. 15 Annex 2), 2015)","plainCitation":"(CITES AC28 Doc. 15 Annex 2), 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32336,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YNPI25DN"],"itemData":{"id":32336,"type":"document","publisher":"IUCN SSC Tortoise and Freshwater Turtle Specialist Group","title":"Non-Detriment Findings and Trade Management for Tortoises and Freshwater Turtles- a guide for CITES Scientific and Management Authorities.","author":[{"family":"CITES AC28 Doc. 15 Annex 2)","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":32343,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VXRJPAI5"],"itemData":{"id":32343,"type":"report","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","publisher":"German Federal Agency for Nature Conservation","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Non Detriment Finding Guidance for Shark Species -2nd Revised Version. A framework to assist Authorities in making Non-Detriment Findings (NDFs) for species listed in Appendix II.","author":[{"family":"Mundy-Taylor","given":"V"},{"family":"Crook","given":"V."},{"family":"Foster","given":"S."},{"family":"Fowler","given":"S."},{"family":"Sant","given":"G"},{"family":"Rice","given":"J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, aquatic invertebrates, and snakes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"A07UE07R","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Natusch, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Natusch, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32337,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ANFT9CTC"],"itemData":{"id":32337,"type":"book","publisher":"IUCN SSC Boa and Python Specialist Group","title":"Developing CITES Non-detriment Findings for Snakes","author":[{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel J. D."},{"family":"Waller","given":"T."},{"family":"Micucci","given":"P."},{"family":"Lichtstein","given":"V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and case studies have been compiled for more than 60 taxa comprising all the major plant and animal groups regulated under the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (www.cites.org/eng/prog/ndf/index.php). The literature review in Google Scholar identified 327 publications referring to non detrimental findings, of which 238 dealt with taxon specific information and a further 99 dealt more generally with non detrimental findings. The templates and tools therefore exist to support the implementation of non detrimental findings for the ~ 37 420 species that are regulated under Appendix II of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. 
      The levels of uptake and application of non detrimental findings standards is harder to assess. Two of the indicators for the implementation of the the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Strategic Vision 2008-2020 were (i) the number of Parties that have adopted standard procedures for non detrimental findings, and (ii) the number of surveys of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listed species undertaken by exporting States to support non detrimental findingsITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0vxagNje","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CITES Notification 2015)","plainCitation":"(CITES Notification 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32384,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6UM9DGR4"],"itemData":{"id":32384,"type":"report","number":"CITES Notification to Parties No. 2015/032","publisher":"CITES Secretariat","title":"Revised set of indicators to measure progress with the CITES Strategic Vision 2008-2020","author":[{"family":"CITES","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These metrics can provide critical insights into the way in which non detrimental findings are being implemented. However, although all Parties have to undertake non detrimental findings and are supposed to report against the agreed indicators, there is no global summary record of progress with these indicators (CITES Secretariat, March 2021). A report compiled for the European Commission ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UxQxx5I1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Musing & Shiraishi, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Musing & Shiraishi, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9340,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D9YUEUDH"],"itemData":{"id":9340,"type":"report","collection-title":"Report prepared for the European Commission","publisher":"TRAFFIC","title":"Analays of EU Member States CITES Implementation Reports 2015-2017","author":[{"family":"Musing","given":"L."},{"family":"Shiraishi","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} noted that 20 member States had undertaken surveys of population status of at least some the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listed taxa, five had undertaken studies of trends and impacts of trade on Appendix II species, and three had published the non detrimental findingsundertaken for the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora taxa. These limited data are not sufficient to allow any assessment of the standards being applied to non detrimental findings nor whether the application of non detrimental findings has had positive outcomes for the affected species.
      One indicator from the the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Vision 2008-2020, which does measure the potential impact of non detrimental findings as a driver of more sustainable levels of use, is “the number of Appendix-II species for which trade is determined to be non-detrimental to the survival of the species as a result of implementing recommendations from the Review of Significant Trade”. The Review of Significant Trade in specimens of Appendix II species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mLFgDzL9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"({\\\\i{}CITES Resolution Conf 12.8 (Rev CoP18) Review of Significant Trade in Specimens of Appendix-II Species}, n.d.)","plainCitation":"(CITES Resolution Conf 12.8 (Rev CoP18) Review of Significant Trade in Specimens of Appendix-II Species, n.d.)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32381,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VCZQEP7F"],"itemData":{"id":32381,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"CITES Resolution Conf 12.8 (Rev CoP18) Review of Significant Trade in specimens of Appendix-II species"}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, was designed to identify Appendix-II listed species that may be subject to unsustainable levels of international trade, i.e., where non detrimental findings may be failing to achieve their objective. The process monitors recorded levels of trade in Appendix II species over a five-year period and identifies species for further analysis based on various risk factors, specifically their threat status, high volumes in trade, a sharp increase in trade or increasing levels of trade. These species are then subjected to additional review and input from Range States to determine whether trade is sustainable and conforms to Article IV of the Convention. If trade in these species, and from specific countries, is deemed to not comply with Article IV, this can result in recommendations to strengthen the capacity of states to ensure sustainable use or to sanctions such as trade suspensions until the trade is compliant. The number of country-species combinations that are subject to recommended actions due to significant trade reviews varies between years. Between 1975 and 2000 there were 138 recommendations arising from the RST process ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VesNOSMm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2000)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32325,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W7WLPMKV"],"itemData":{"id":32325,"type":"report","number":"IUCN Report (09/11/00)","publisher":"The Economics, Trade and Environment Unit United Nations Environment Programme","title":"Trade Measures in Multilateral Environmental Agreements. A report by the IUCN on the Effectiveness of Trade Measures Contained in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES)","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and, in 2020, suspensions were in force for trade in 41 species from 19 countries as a result of the RST. In some cases, species have been subjected to repeat reviews due to concerns about unsustainable trade such as the grey parrot, Psittacus erithacus, which was included in the review of significant trade in 1988, 1992, 2006 and 2014 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"roBkNOXW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(VKM, 2020)","plainCitation":"(VKM, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32327,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YRTKX5AY"],"itemData":{"id":32327,"type":"book","publisher":"Norwegian Scientific Committee for Food and Environment (VKM)","title":"Status and trade assessment of parrots listed in CITES Appendix I. Scientific Opinion of the Panel on alien organisms and trade in endangered species (CITES) of the Norwegian Scientific Committee for Food and Environment","author":[{"family":"VKM","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Given that there are 37.420 species on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Appendix II, which would all be eligible for review if there were concerns about levels of trade, the implication is that trade in the vast majority of taxa is regarded as sustainable, at least within the limits of what is monitored by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, and the implementation of the non detrimental findings process is sufficient to ensure levels of trade that are sustainable. However, a recent analysis of the review of significant trade using seahorses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CYjsDYZq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. J. Foster and Vincent 2021)","plainCitation":"(S. J. Foster and Vincent 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9BY5TPFS"],"itemData":{"id":32331,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2021.e01572","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e01572","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Holding governments accountable for their commitments: CITES Review of Significant Trade for a very high-volume taxon","title-short":"Holding governments accountable for their commitments","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2351989421001220","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Foster","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A.C.J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} noted that implementation of the review of significant trade was failing to prevent trade that was not compliant with Article IV and was therefore failing in its primary mandate to ensure sustainable trade. They concluded that the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora needs to commit to more effective enforcement to improve the effectiveness of the review of significant trade and this highlights the need for independent scientific assessments of the outcomes of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora actions.
      A further self-defining indicator of the success of non detrimental findings as a driver of more sustainable use would be the uplisting of species or populations from Appendix II to Appendix I ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nL0jIbom","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2021)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":883,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CBPTIH6I"],"itemData":{"id":883,"type":"webpage","abstract":"Established in 1964, the IUCN Red List of Threatened Species has evolved to become the world’s most comprehensive information source on the global conservation status of animal, fungi and plant species.","container-title":"IUCN Red List of Threatened Species","title":"The IUCN Red List Data","URL":"https://www.iucnredlist.org/en","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Between 1976 and 2020, approximately 460 species, subspecies or populations/stocks were uplisted from Appendix II to Appendix I (counted here as the taxon specified in the proposal at the time of uplisting) (the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Secretariat based on records compiled by UNEP-WCMC). The uplisting implies that efforts to bring trade in wild specimens to sustainable levels under an Appendix II listing were deemed inadequate. An example is the grey parrot where there is a trend from repeat reviews and trade suspensions (noted above) finally leading to uplisting to Appendix I in 2016. In contrast, species of pangolin (Manis spp.) were uplisted in 2016 in response to rapid increases in trade volumes and therefore not following problems identified with non detrimental findings. Overall, the number of uplistings from Appendix II to Appendix I remains low relative to the large number of species listed on Appendix II. The number of uplistings has also declined over time with 180 taxa uplisted from 1976 to 1994 compared to only 52 from 1997 to 2019. This implies that there might have been uncertainty early on about the appropriate Appendix in which to list species and/or that the processes established to ensure sustainable use of Appendix II species were starting to have an impact. It is also worth noting that taxa uplisted from Appendix II to Appendix I are not a random subset of those listed on Appendix II -cacti make up 52% of all plants uplisted with a further 34% comprising succulent plants; tortoises and turtles make up 61% of all reptiles uplisted and Psittaciformes (including macaws, parrots, cockatoos) comprise 67% of uplisted birds. Uplistings of mammals have been less dominated by particular taxa, with marine mammals, bats, cats (Felidae) and primates all comprising between 13% and 19% of uplisted taxa. The predominance of certain taxa indicates particular challenges with ensuring that trade in these taxa is sustainable.
      Less formalised processes for monitoring the standards of non detrimental findings include interactions between the Scientific Authorities of importing and exporting countries where the Scientific Authority of an importing country can request evidence relating to an non detrimental findings from an exporting country or undertake their own assessment. There is no centralized record of such requests so it is not possible to assess the number of species or trade events where such requests have been made. However, a good example is the European Commission Scientific Review Group (https://ec.europa.eu/environment/cites/srg_en.htm) which reviews evidence of sustainability for imports of wild species and compliance with the conservation requirements of Council Regulation (EC) No 338/97. These determinations may require exporting countries to provide additional evidence to show that use is sustainable. Again, there have been only a relatively small number of negative findings given the volumes of species in trade indicating that non detrimental findings are mostly regarded as being of an adequate standard to ensure sustainable levels of use.
      The search for literature regarding the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora and non detrimental findings yielded only seven publications from Web of Science and 327 from Google Scholar, partly reflecting a greater representation of publications in non-peer reviewed literature. This is partly to be expected, given that non detrimental findings are designed for regulatory purposes and are not necessarily intended for publication in scientific journals. Nevertheless, the literature search yielded only a small number of publications testing the outcomes of non detrimental findings and this represents a data gap given the importance of non detrimental findings for promoting more sustainable use and achieving the objectives of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. 

Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora as a driver for sustainable and legal trade
As noted in previous assessments and reviews of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9nbNS7Ys","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Challender, 2015; IUCN, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Challender, 2015; IUCN, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4793,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LXIIWJME"],"itemData":{"id":4793,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2014.11.010.","issue":"January","language":"en","page":"129– 48","title":"Towards Informed and Multi- Faceted Wildlife Trade Interventions","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gecco.2014.11.010.","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Challender","given":"D.W.S."},{"family":"Harrop","given":"S.R."},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"D.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":32325,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W7WLPMKV"],"itemData":{"id":32325,"type":"report","number":"IUCN Report (09/11/00)","publisher":"The Economics, Trade and Environment Unit United Nations Environment Programme","title":"Trade Measures in Multilateral Environmental Agreements. A report by the IUCN on the Effectiveness of Trade Measures Contained in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES)","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, it is difficult to identify specific indicators for assessing the outcomes of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora as a driver of more sustainable use of wild species. Many indicators identified by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora measure inputs, such as the number of Parties who have received capacity building, or outputs such as the number of Parties that have implemented relevant resolutions and decisions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"S5T9jae9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CITES, 2015)","plainCitation":"(CITES, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32384,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6UM9DGR4"],"itemData":{"id":32384,"type":"report","number":"CITES Notification to Parties No. 2015/032","publisher":"CITES Secretariat","title":"Revised set of indicators to measure progress with the CITES Strategic Vision 2008-2020","author":[{"family":"CITES","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, notification 2015/032). These are appropriate for measuring the operational efficiency of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, consistent with the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora mandate to regulate international trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gbjLSd7O","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2021)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":883,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CBPTIH6I"],"itemData":{"id":883,"type":"webpage","abstract":"Established in 1964, the IUCN Red List of Threatened Species has evolved to become the world’s most comprehensive information source on the global conservation status of animal, fungi and plant species.","container-title":"IUCN Red List of Threatened Species","title":"The IUCN Red List Data","URL":"https://www.iucnredlist.org/en","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",9,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A broader view of the impact of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora as a driver of sustainable and legal trade would need to include indicators for a positive change in the status of the species listed on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EfH9N76v","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Challender, 2015; Felbab-Brown, 2017; S. J. Foster & Vincent, 2021; IUCN, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Challender, 2015; Felbab-Brown, 2017; S. J. Foster & Vincent, 2021; IUCN, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4793,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LXIIWJME"],"itemData":{"id":4793,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2014.11.010.","issue":"January","language":"en","page":"129– 48","title":"Towards Informed and Multi- Faceted Wildlife Trade Interventions","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gecco.2014.11.010.","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Challender","given":"D.W.S."},{"family":"Harrop","given":"S.R."},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"D.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":32328,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NZJZJDC3"],"itemData":{"id":32328,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"The planet is currently experiencing alarming levels of species loss caused in large part by intensified poaching and wildlife trafficking driven by expanding demand, for medicines, for food, and for trophies. Affecting many more species than just the iconic elephants, rhinos, and tigers, the rate of extinction is now as much as 1000 times the historical average and the worst since the dinosaurs died out 65 million years ago. In addition to causing irretrievable biodiversity loss, wildlife trafficking also poses serious threats to public health, potentially triggering a global pandemic. The Extinction Market explores the causes, means, and consequences of poaching and wildlife trafficking, with a view to finding ways of suppressing them. Vanda Felbab-Brown travelled to the markets of Latin America, South and South East Asia, and eastern and southern Africa, to evaluate the effectiveness of various tools, including bans on legal trade, law enforcement, and interdiction; allowing legal supply from hunting or farming; alternative livelihoods; anti- money-laundering efforts; and demand reduction strategies. This is an urgent book offering meaningful solutions to one of the world's most pressing crises.\\"--Provided by publisher","call-number":"HV6410 .F45 2017","event-place":"Oxford ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-19-085511-6","note":"OCLC: ocn986237039","number-of-pages":"406","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"Oxford ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The extinction market: wildlife trafficking and how to counter it","title-short":"The extinction market","author":[{"family":"Felbab-Brown","given":"Vanda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9BY5TPFS"],"itemData":{"id":32331,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2021.e01572","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e01572","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Holding governments accountable for their commitments: CITES Review of Significant Trade for a very high-volume taxon","title-short":"Holding governments accountable for their commitments","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2351989421001220","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Foster","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A.C.J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",6]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32325,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W7WLPMKV"],"itemData":{"id":32325,"type":"report","number":"IUCN Report (09/11/00)","publisher":"The Economics, Trade and Environment Unit United Nations Environment Programme","title":"Trade Measures in Multilateral Environmental Agreements. A report by the IUCN on the Effectiveness of Trade Measures Contained in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES)","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and a decrease in the number of those illegally killed or removed from the wild ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cjaWgsda","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Felbab-Brown, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Felbab-Brown, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32328,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NZJZJDC3"],"itemData":{"id":32328,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"The planet is currently experiencing alarming levels of species loss caused in large part by intensified poaching and wildlife trafficking driven by expanding demand, for medicines, for food, and for trophies. Affecting many more species than just the iconic elephants, rhinos, and tigers, the rate of extinction is now as much as 1000 times the historical average and the worst since the dinosaurs died out 65 million years ago. In addition to causing irretrievable biodiversity loss, wildlife trafficking also poses serious threats to public health, potentially triggering a global pandemic. The Extinction Market explores the causes, means, and consequences of poaching and wildlife trafficking, with a view to finding ways of suppressing them. Vanda Felbab-Brown travelled to the markets of Latin America, South and South East Asia, and eastern and southern Africa, to evaluate the effectiveness of various tools, including bans on legal trade, law enforcement, and interdiction; allowing legal supply from hunting or farming; alternative livelihoods; anti- money-laundering efforts; and demand reduction strategies. This is an urgent book offering meaningful solutions to one of the world's most pressing crises.\\"--Provided by publisher","call-number":"HV6410 .F45 2017","event-place":"Oxford ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-19-085511-6","note":"OCLC: ocn986237039","number-of-pages":"406","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"Oxford ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The extinction market: wildlife trafficking and how to counter it","title-short":"The extinction market","author":[{"family":"Felbab-Brown","given":"Vanda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      One possible outcome from the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listing and processes could be a reduction in the overall trade in wild species. The intention of listing species on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Appendix I is to halt commercial trade in wild-sourced specimens, so reduction in trade in wild-sourced specimens would be the expected outcome for these species. The intention of listing a species on Appendices II and III is to ensure that trade from wild sources does not threaten the survival of the species in the wild. This does not necessarily equate to a reduction in use or trade but the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora could nevertheless act as a driver for reduced trade due to the greater regulation of trade from wild sources to bring it to levels that are sustainable and increased scrutiny of the evidence that trade is sustainable. 
      A comprehensive analysis of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora trade over 40 years showed that overall volumes of international trade in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listed species increased from ca. 9 million ‘whole organism equivalents’ per year between 1985 and 1995 to 100 million per year between 2004 and 2014 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xWVy2oVf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harfoot, 2018a)","plainCitation":"(Harfoot, 2018a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TJH5GCYT"],"itemData":{"id":5225,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2018.04.017.","issue":"July","language":"en","page":"47–57","title":"Unveiling the Patterns and Trends in 40 Years of Global Trade in CITES- Listed Wildlife","volume":"223","author":[{"family":"Harfoot","given":"M."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"S.A.M."},{"family":"Tittensor","given":"D.P."},{"family":"Britten","given":"G.L."},{"family":"McLardy","given":"C."},{"family":"Malsch","given":"K."},{"family":"Burgess","given":"N.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although this suggests an increase in trade, the data refer to the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora records of trade and need to take into account the dynamic nature of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora trade data ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RU6dDdwo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. E. Robinson & Sinovas, 2018)","plainCitation":"(J. E. Robinson & Sinovas, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32357,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RL3X93PT"],"itemData":{"id":32357,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13095","ISSN":"08888892","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"1203-1206","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Challenges of analyzing the global trade in CITES-listed wildlife: CITES data","title-short":"Challenges of analyzing the global trade in CITES-listed wildlife","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/cobi.13095","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"Janine E."},{"family":"Sinovas","given":"Pablo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora records refer to exports and imports from Parties for all the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listed taxa so the addition of new Parties and the listing of new species can result in increased trade records without any actual change in trade volumes (ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uImX09nx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. E. Robinson and Sinovas 2018)","plainCitation":"(J. E. Robinson and Sinovas 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32357,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RL3X93PT"],"itemData":{"id":32357,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13095","ISSN":"08888892","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"1203-1206","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Challenges of analyzing the global trade in CITES-listed wildlife: CITES data","title-short":"Challenges of analyzing the global trade in CITES-listed wildlife","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1111/cobi.13095","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"Janine E."},{"family":"Sinovas","given":"Pablo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, the number of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Parties increased from 85 to 180 between 1985 and 2014 with 107 and 175 Parties at the midpoint of each review period respectively (www.cites.org). This represents an increase of between 63% and 111% so the increase in reported volumes of trade might be explained by an increase in reporting from additional Parties rather than an actual increase in trade. Similarly, changes in the number of taxa listed on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora also affects any assessment of changes in trade volumes but is more difficult to interpret due to listing of higher-level taxa (genera, families) where the number of species is not specified. Even if the overall number of species in trade has not changed by the same order of magnitude as the change in trade volumes, the listing of highly traded taxa, such as sharks, can lead to a substantial increase in the ‘whole organism equivalents’ being reported. As a result, it is not possible to determine whether the overall increase in ‘whole organism equivalents’ from 1985 to 2014 represents an actual increase in the volume of trade for the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listed species.
      Evidence from specific taxa provides a more nuanced perspective on whether the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora acts as a driver for reduced use of wild species. Studies have shown a reduction in recorded trade in specific the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listed taxa for some species, in some regions, and for certain periods. Reduced volumes of trade have been recorded for the following taxa: birds globally since 2005 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"f7L5L60h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harfoot, 2018a)","plainCitation":"(Harfoot, 2018a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TJH5GCYT"],"itemData":{"id":5225,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2018.04.017.","issue":"July","language":"en","page":"47–57","title":"Unveiling the Patterns and Trends in 40 Years of Global Trade in CITES- Listed Wildlife","volume":"223","author":[{"family":"Harfoot","given":"M."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"S.A.M."},{"family":"Tittensor","given":"D.P."},{"family":"Britten","given":"G.L."},{"family":"McLardy","given":"C."},{"family":"Malsch","given":"K."},{"family":"Burgess","given":"N.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; birds in Southeast Asia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QEh48fJn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harfoot, 2018a; Shepherd, Leupen,, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Harfoot, 2018a; Shepherd, Leupen,, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TJH5GCYT"],"itemData":{"id":5225,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2018.04.017.","issue":"July","language":"en","page":"47–57","title":"Unveiling the Patterns and Trends in 40 Years of Global Trade in CITES- Listed Wildlife","volume":"223","author":[{"family":"Harfoot","given":"M."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"S.A.M."},{"family":"Tittensor","given":"D.P."},{"family":"Britten","given":"G.L."},{"family":"McLardy","given":"C."},{"family":"Malsch","given":"K."},{"family":"Burgess","given":"N.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":9373,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3XCT7MMN"],"itemData":{"id":9373,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2020.e01185","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e01185","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"International wildlife trade, avian influenza, organised crime and the effectiveness of CITES: The Chinese hwamei as a case study","title-short":"International wildlife trade, avian influenza, organised crime and the effectiveness of CITES","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2351989420307265","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Shepherd","given":"C.R"},{"family":"Leupen","given":"B.T.C."},{"family":"Siriwat","given":"P."},{"family":"Nijman","given":"V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Australia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ac810nGS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vall-Ilosera & Cassey, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Vall-Ilosera & Cassey, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9384,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/323WGK3Z"],"itemData":{"id":9384,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000395601400005","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2017.01.015","ISSN":"0006-3207","page":"38-46","title":"'Do you come from a land down under?' Characteristics of the international trade in Australian endemic parrots","URL":"://WOS:000395601400005","volume":"207","author":[{"family":"Vall-Ilosera","given":"M."},{"family":"Cassey","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; live reptiles from 2001 to 2012 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ivEGl63r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. E. Robinson, 2015a)","plainCitation":"(J. E. Robinson, 2015a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9367,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YTDW4Q4X"],"itemData":{"id":9367,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2014.12.019","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"42-50","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Dynamics of the global trade in live reptiles: Shifting trends in production and consequences for sustainability","title-short":"Dynamics of the global trade in live reptiles","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320714004984","volume":"184","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"Janine E."},{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Richard A."},{"family":"St. John","given":"Freya A.V."},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; snakes (especially pythons) from 2002-2017 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aBiazFM7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hierink, 2020a)","plainCitation":"(Hierink, 2020a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6044,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/73AUL53I"],"itemData":{"id":6044,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Trade in venomous and non-venomous snakes can negatively impact wild snake populations and may drive snakebite risk for people. However, we often lack sufficient trade data to identify where the potential risks for snake population decline and snakebite are highest. Currently, the legal, international trade of 164 snake species is regulated by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES). We analyzed CITES-listed snake trade from 1975 to 2018 using the recently released shipment-level CITES Trade Database to identify spatiotemporal trends of snake trade and generate insights regarding snake conservation and potential public health risks from snakebite. Commercially purposed pythons dominated the global snake trade, comprising 38.8% of all traded snakes. Live snakes were mainly exported by Ghana, Indonesia, Togo, and Benin, and imported by China and the USA. Venomous snake trade comprised 10.8% of all traded snakes, and over 75% of wild-sourced venomous snakes came from Indonesia. Although traded snakes in recent years are increasingly comprised of captive-bred animals, the majority of snakes are still wild-sourced ( > 60% between 2015 and 2017), including IUCN-listed species, with potentially detrimental impacts on conservation status. Further, the CITES Trade Database reveals geographic regions where venomous snakes are sourced from the wild, posing potential risks to snake catchers, traders, and pet owners. The database also documents the movement of non-native snake species through trade, with implications for conservation of native species. This study represents the first global analysis focused specifically on CITES-listed snake trade using the CITES Trade Database.","archive_location":"WOS:000556843900006","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2020.108601","ISSN":"0006-3207","title":"Forty-four years of global trade in CITES-listed snakes: Trends and implications for conservation and public health","URL":"://WOS:000556843900006","volume":"248","author":[{"family":"Hierink","given":"F."},{"family":"Bolon","given":"I."},{"family":"Durso","given":"A. M."},{"family":"Castaneda","given":"R. R.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Zambrana-Torrelio","given":"C."},{"family":"Eskew","given":"E. A."},{"family":"Ray","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; tortoises and fresh water turtles from Asia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kXwxC0JA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Luiselli, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Luiselli, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7535,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZLLAA8S9"],"itemData":{"id":7535,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Chelonian Conservation and Biology","DOI":"10.2744/CCB-1216.1","ISSN":"1071-8443, 1943-3956","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Chelonian Conservation and Biology","language":"en","page":"167-172","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A Short Review of the International Trade of Wild Tortoises and Freshwater Turtles Across the World and Throughout Two Decades","URL":"http://www.bioone.org/doi/10.2744/CCB-1216.1","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Luiselli","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Starita","given":"Antonio"},{"family":"Carpaneto","given":"Giuseppe Maria"},{"family":"Segniagbeto","given":"Gabriel H."},{"family":"Amori","given":"Giovanni"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; some mammal species, although the trends are less clear ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AHftX7cX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harfoot, 2018a; Nijman, 2010a)","plainCitation":"(Harfoot, 2018a; Nijman, 2010a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TJH5GCYT"],"itemData":{"id":5225,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2018.04.017.","issue":"July","language":"en","page":"47–57","title":"Unveiling the Patterns and Trends in 40 Years of Global Trade in CITES- Listed Wildlife","volume":"223","author":[{"family":"Harfoot","given":"M."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"S.A.M."},{"family":"Tittensor","given":"D.P."},{"family":"Britten","given":"G.L."},{"family":"McLardy","given":"C."},{"family":"Malsch","given":"K."},{"family":"Burgess","given":"N.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32739,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMIG93TC"],"itemData":{"id":32739,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife trade is the very heart of biodiversity conservation and sustainable development providing an income for some of the least economically affluent people and it generates considerable revenue nationally. In Asia the unsustainable trade in wildlife has been identified as one of the main conservation challenges. Internationally, wildlife trade is regulated through the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of wild fauna and flora (CITES) to which all Southeast Asian nations are signatory. I obtained data on international trade in CITES-listed animals in the period 1998-2007. In all \\\\textgreater 35 million animals (0.3 million butterflies; 16.0 million seahorses; 0.1 million other fish; 17.4 million reptiles; 0.4 million mammals; 1.0 million birds) were exported in this period, 30 million (similar to 300 species) of them being wild-caught. In addition 18 million pieces and 2 million kg of live corals were exported. Malaysia, Vietnam, Indonesia and China are the major exporters of wild-caught animals and the European Union and Japan are the most significant importers. Over this period exports in birds significantly decreased, trade in the other taxa either increased or remained stable. For all taxa but butterflies the vast majority of individuals represent wild-caught individuals. Records of illegal or undeclared international trade are scant but can be significantly larger than levels of official exports. It is concluded that there is an urgent need for better assessments of what levels of exploitation are sustainable (including exploring appropriate proxies for Non Detriment Findings), for initiatives to make regulatory mechanisms more effective (including the introduction of minimum mandatory standards and monitoring selected wildlife trade hubs), and for better licensing and registration. Funding for at least some of these initiatives can be obtained by imposing small levies on exports of CITES-listed wildlife.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-009-9758-4","ISSN":"09603115","issue":"4","page":"1101–1114","title":"An overview of international wildlife trade from Southeast Asia","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; bear trade in the Czech Republic ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QNDppt1A","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shepherd, Kufnerova,, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Shepherd, Kufnerova,, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9372,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LNRMXE6C"],"itemData":{"id":9372,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000582324000001","container-title":"European Journal of Wildlife Research","DOI":"10.1007/s10344-020-01425-7","ISSN":"1612-4642","issue":"6","title":"Bear trade in the Czech Republic: an analysis of legal and illegal international trade from 2005 to 2020","URL":"://WOS:000582324000001","volume":"66","author":[{"family":"Shepherd","given":"C. R."},{"family":"Kufnerova","given":"J."},{"family":"Cajthaml","given":"T."},{"family":"Frouzova","given":"J."},{"family":"Gomez","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; and African rosewood (Pterocarpus erinaceus) following listing on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Appendix II ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gENIPrVp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dumenu, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Dumenu, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32351,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8SBIXZ9Z"],"itemData":{"id":32351,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2019.05.044","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"124-133","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Assessing the impact of felling/export ban and CITES designation on exploitation of African rosewood (Pterocarpus erinaceus)","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S000632071930117X","volume":"236","author":[{"family":"Dumenu","given":"William Kwadwo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Trends in trade are not always unidirectional and this often reflects both changes in markets for wild species products as well as the intended and unintended outcomes of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora decisions or national level legislation and enforcement. The causal factors for these shifts can be highly contested and are typically counterfactual, such as whether once off sales of ivory in 2008 triggered an increase in illegal trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oMDhRFNa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. I. Orenstein, 2013; Underwood, 2013)","plainCitation":"(R. I. Orenstein, 2013; Underwood, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32317,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FQFSEMVT"],"itemData":{"id":32317,"type":"book","call-number":"SK36.7 .O74 2013","event-place":"Richmond Hill, Ontario ; Buffalo, New York","ISBN":"978-1-77085-227-3","note":"OCLC: ocn829421978","number-of-pages":"216","publisher":"Firefly Books","publisher-place":"Richmond Hill, Ontario ; Buffalo, New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Ivory, horn and blood: behind the elephant and rhinoceros poaching crisis","title-short":"Ivory, horn and blood","author":[{"family":"Orenstein","given":"Ronald I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":32358,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U6NGPD2G"],"itemData":{"id":32358,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0076539","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e76539","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Dissecting the Illegal Ivory Trade: An Analysis of Ivory Seizures Data","title-short":"Dissecting the Illegal Ivory Trade","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0076539","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Underwood","given":"Fiona M."},{"family":"Burn","given":"Robert W."},{"family":"Milliken","given":"Tom"}],"editor":[{"family":"Montoya","given":"Alejandro Raul Hernandez"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",10,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The recorded reduction in levels of trade across various groups may be attributable to the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora and associated national legislation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DPcACDoL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harfoot, 2018a; Shepherd, Leupen,, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Harfoot, 2018a; Shepherd, Leupen,, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TJH5GCYT"],"itemData":{"id":5225,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2018.04.017.","issue":"July","language":"en","page":"47–57","title":"Unveiling the Patterns and Trends in 40 Years of Global Trade in CITES- Listed Wildlife","volume":"223","author":[{"family":"Harfoot","given":"M."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"S.A.M."},{"family":"Tittensor","given":"D.P."},{"family":"Britten","given":"G.L."},{"family":"McLardy","given":"C."},{"family":"Malsch","given":"K."},{"family":"Burgess","given":"N.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":9373,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3XCT7MMN"],"itemData":{"id":9373,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2020.e01185","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e01185","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"International wildlife trade, avian influenza, organised crime and the effectiveness of CITES: The Chinese hwamei as a case study","title-short":"International wildlife trade, avian influenza, organised crime and the effectiveness of CITES","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2351989420307265","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Shepherd","given":"C.R"},{"family":"Leupen","given":"B.T.C."},{"family":"Siriwat","given":"P."},{"family":"Nijman","given":"V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but can also result from other actions such as bans on the import of birds as a measure to contain the spread of avian influenza  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oEyAjfwx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Challender, 2015; Harfoot, 2018a; Vall-Ilosera & Cassey, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Challender, 2015; Harfoot, 2018a; Vall-Ilosera & Cassey, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4793,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LXIIWJME"],"itemData":{"id":4793,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2014.11.010.","issue":"January","language":"en","page":"129– 48","title":"Towards Informed and Multi- Faceted Wildlife Trade Interventions","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gecco.2014.11.010.","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Challender","given":"D.W.S."},{"family":"Harrop","given":"S.R."},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"D.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":5225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TJH5GCYT"],"itemData":{"id":5225,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2018.04.017.","issue":"July","language":"en","page":"47–57","title":"Unveiling the Patterns and Trends in 40 Years of Global Trade in CITES- Listed Wildlife","volume":"223","author":[{"family":"Harfoot","given":"M."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"S.A.M."},{"family":"Tittensor","given":"D.P."},{"family":"Britten","given":"G.L."},{"family":"McLardy","given":"C."},{"family":"Malsch","given":"K."},{"family":"Burgess","given":"N.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":9384,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/323WGK3Z"],"itemData":{"id":9384,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000395601400005","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2017.01.015","ISSN":"0006-3207","page":"38-46","title":"'Do you come from a land down under?' Characteristics of the international trade in Australian endemic parrots","URL":"://WOS:000395601400005","volume":"207","author":[{"family":"Vall-Ilosera","given":"M."},{"family":"Cassey","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are many factors affecting levels of harvest and trade  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KAr6xWTY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Challender, 2015; Sas-Rolfes, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Challender, 2015; Sas-Rolfes, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4793,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LXIIWJME"],"itemData":{"id":4793,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2014.11.010.","issue":"January","language":"en","page":"129– 48","title":"Towards Informed and Multi- Faceted Wildlife Trade Interventions","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gecco.2014.11.010.","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Challender","given":"D.W.S."},{"family":"Harrop","given":"S.R."},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"D.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":11657,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5GPSNAY9"],"itemData":{"id":11657,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033253.","language":"en","title":"Illegal Wildlife Trade: Patterns, Processes, and Governance","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033253.","author":[{"literal":"Sas-Rolfes"},{"family":"Michael","given":"Daniel W.S.Challender"},{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Diogo"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and, as noted by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PQtMV1Cy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Underwood, 2013a)","plainCitation":"(Underwood, 2013a)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32358,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U6NGPD2G"],"itemData":{"id":32358,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0076539","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e76539","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Dissecting the Illegal Ivory Trade: An Analysis of Ivory Seizures Data","title-short":"Dissecting the Illegal Ivory Trade","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0076539","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Underwood","given":"Fiona M."},{"family":"Burn","given":"Robert W."},{"family":"Milliken","given":"Tom"}],"editor":[{"family":"Montoya","given":"Alejandro Raul Hernandez"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",10,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} “to understand the impact of these and other the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora decisions, it is necessary to identify hypotheses linking them with trade dynamics. Because the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora decisions are implemented in a constantly changing, complex socio-economic environment, a full causal analysis is required that considers all other potential drivers of trade and their interactions along the trade chain. Without this comprehensive analysis the impact of an individual driver may be confounded with the effects of other drivers”. This makes it more difficult to assess the actual impact of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. Nevertheless, the decline in trade across a range of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listed taxa supports the conclusion that these outcomes have been driven at least in part by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora decisions and associated national regulations.
      A second trend that could be strongly influenced by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora is a change away from trade in wild sourced specimens. There has been a significant shift over the past 40 years from wild harvested specimens to animals that are claimed as captive bred and plants that are artificially propagated. This shift is especially evident for mammals, birds, reptiles, invertebrates and plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0ApTIx4k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harfoot, 2018a; Hierink, 2020; Hinsley, 2018; L. L. Li & Jiang, 2014; Nijman, 2010b; J. E. Robinson, 2015; Setlikova & Berec, 2020; Vall-Llosera & Su, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Harfoot, 2018a; Hierink, 2020; Hinsley, 2018; L. L. Li & Jiang, 2014; Nijman, 2010b; J. E. Robinson, 2015; Setlikova & Berec, 2020; Vall-Llosera & Su, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"Uebb2sCb/gAJLoHGJ","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/SB2IFLMT"],"itemData":{"id":5681,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2014.12.019","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"42-50","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Dynamics of the global trade in live reptiles: Shifting trends in production and consequences for sustainability","title-short":"Dynamics of the global trade in live reptiles","volume":"184","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"Janine E."},{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Richard A."},{"family":"St. John","given":"Freya A.V."},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",4]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/Kvj3O6or","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/M32FL3UD"],"itemData":{"id":3849,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000435061700005","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2018.04.017","ISSN":"0006-3207","page":"47-57","title":"Unveiling the patterns and trends in 40 years of global trade in CITES-listed wildlife","volume":"223","author":[{"family":"Harfoot","given":"M."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"S. A. M."},{"family":"Tittensor","given":"D. P."},{"family":"Britten","given":"G. L."},{"family":"McLardy","given":"C."},{"family":"Malsch","given":"K."},{"family":"Burgess","given":"N. D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/7IaqbO50","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/4FDKVAMT"],"itemData":{"id":3923,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000275536700013","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-009-9758-4","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"4","page":"1101-1114","title":"An overview of international wildlife trade from Southeast Asia","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",4]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/YcfTRS0j","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/RIABXEGF"],"itemData":{"id":3984,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000471132000009","container-title":"Ibis","DOI":"10.1111/ibi.12653","ISSN":"0019-1019","issue":"3","page":"590-604","title":"Trends and characteristics of imports of live CITES-listed bird species into Japan","volume":"161","author":[{"family":"Vall-Llosera","given":"M."},{"family":"Su","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",7]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/cFBY6gWN","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/HEFWDDGW"],"itemData":{"id":3885,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000331711900004","container-title":"Plos One","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0085012","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"2","title":"International Trade of CITES Listed Bird Species in China","URL":"://WOS:000331711900004","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Li","given":"L. L."},{"family":"Jiang","given":"Z. G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",2]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/d3JcWR7Y","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/AZEMDB4M"],"itemData":{"id":3955,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000579543500001","container-title":"Australian Journal of Botany","DOI":"10.1071/bt20027","ISSN":"0067-1924","issue":"5","page":"376-383","title":"Diversity and volume of international trade in Old World pitcher plants","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Setlikova","given":"I."},{"family":"Berec","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/e3mtIOaB","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/SW68LRAU"],"itemData":{"id":3856,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Trade in venomous and non-venomous snakes can negatively impact wild snake populations and may drive snakebite risk for people. However, we often lack sufficient trade data to identify where the potential risks for snake population decline and snakebite are highest. Currently, the legal, international trade of 164 snake species is regulated by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES). We analyzed CITES-listed snake trade from 1975 to 2018 using the recently released shipment-level CITES Trade Database to identify spatiotemporal trends of snake trade and generate insights regarding snake conservation and potential public health risks from snakebite. Commercially purposed pythons dominated the global snake trade, comprising 38.8% of all traded snakes. Live snakes were mainly exported by Ghana, Indonesia, Togo, and Benin, and imported by China and the USA. Venomous snake trade comprised 10.8% of all traded snakes, and over 75% of wild-sourced venomous snakes came from Indonesia. Although traded snakes in recent years are increasingly comprised of captive-bred animals, the majority of snakes are still wild-sourced ( > 60% between 2015 and 2017), including IUCN-listed species, with potentially detrimental impacts on conservation status. Further, the CITES Trade Database reveals geographic regions where venomous snakes are sourced from the wild, posing potential risks to snake catchers, traders, and pet owners. The database also documents the movement of non-native snake species through trade, with implications for conservation of native species. This study represents the first global analysis focused specifically on CITES-listed snake trade using the CITES Trade Database.","archive_location":"WOS:000556843900006","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2020.108601","ISSN":"0006-3207","title":"Forty-four years of global trade in CITES-listed snakes: Trends and implications for conservation and public health","URL":"://WOS:000556843900006","volume":"248","author":[{"family":"Hierink","given":"F."},{"family":"Bolon","given":"I."},{"family":"Durso","given":"A. M."},{"family":"Castaneda","given":"R. R.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Zambrana-Torrelio","given":"C."},{"family":"Eskew","given":"E. A."},{"family":"Ray","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8]]}}},{"id":32354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SU6WGFBJ"],"itemData":{"id":32354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society","DOI":"10.1093/botlinnean/box083","ISSN":"0024-4074, 1095-8339","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"435-455","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A review of the trade in orchids and its implications for conservation","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/botlinnean/article/186/4/435/4736317","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Boer","given":"Hugo J","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Fay","given":"Michael F"},{"family":"Gale","given":"Stephan W"},{"family":"Gardiner","given":"Lauren M"},{"family":"Gunasekara","given":"Rajasinghe S"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Pankaj"},{"family":"Masters","given":"Susanne"},{"family":"Metusala","given":"Destario"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L"},{"family":"Veldman","given":"Sarina"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Shan"},{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It should be noted that consistency across these publications is largely because they are based on the same the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Trade Database, which is the official database for the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora records, although some independent data sources also reflect a large proportion of trade from captive sources, e.g. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ijk5HF5w","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Marshall, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Marshall, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32346,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FLRDMTZ6"],"itemData":{"id":32346,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife trade is a key driver of the biodiversity crisis. Unregulated, or under-regulated wildlife trade can lead to unsustainable exploitation of wild populations. International efforts to regulate wildlife mostly miss ‘lower-value’ species, such as those imported as pets, resulting in limited knowledge of trade in groups like reptiles. Here we generate a dataset on web-based private commercial trade of reptiles to highlight the scope of the global reptile trade. We find that over 35% of reptile species are traded online. Three quarters of this trade is in species that are not covered by international trade regulation. These species include numerous endangered or range-restricted species, especially hotspots within Asia. Approximately 90% of traded reptile species and half of traded individuals are captured from the wild. Exploitation can occur immediately after scientific description, leaving new endemic species especially vulnerable. Pronounced gaps in regulation imply trade is having unknown impacts on numerous threatened species. Gaps in monitoring demand a reconsideration of international reptile trade regulations. We suggest reversing the status-quo, requiring proof of sustainability before trade is permitted.","container-title":"Nature Communications","DOI":"10.1038/s41467-020-18523-4","ISSN":"2041-1723","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Commun","language":"en","license":"2020 The Author(s)","note":"Bandiera_abtest: a\\nCc_license_type: cc_by\\nCg_type: Nature Research Journals\\nnumber: 1\\nPrimary_atype: Research\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group\\nSubject_term: Biodiversity;Conservation biology;Herpetology\\nSubject_term_id: biodiversity;conservation;herpetology","page":"4738","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Thousands of reptile species threatened by under-regulated global trade","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-020-18523-4","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Marshall","given":"Benjamin M."},{"family":"Strine","given":"Colin"},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Alice C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is not always possible to attribute these shifts away from wild- sourced specimens to a listing on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Appendices because captive breeding and artificial propagation can also be driven by other factors such as the need for more consistent supply, better quality, or control of the supply chain ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"o8tHduFi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Michael Harfoot 2018; Kasterine and Lichtenstein 2018a)","plainCitation":"(Michael Harfoot 2018; Kasterine and Lichtenstein 2018a)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2Q78L7PC","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RAMZGR28"],"itemData":{"id":11383,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife trade can provide commercial incentives to conserve biodiversity but, if unsustainable, can also pose a threat. CITES (Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora) aims to ensure international trade in CITES-listed species is sustainable, legal and traceable. However, large-scale temporal and spatial patterns in wildlife trade are poorly known. We address this by analysing the CITES Trade Database: > 16 million shipment records for 28,282 species, from 1975 and 2014. Over this period, the volume of reported trade in CITES-listed wildlife quadrupled, from 25 million whole-organism equivalents per year to 100 million, and the ratio of wild- to captive-sourced trade in mammals, birds, reptiles, invertebrates and plants declined by an order of magnitude or more. Our ?ndings start to reveal the scale of the legal wildlife trade, shifting trade routes and sources over time and we describe testable hypotheses for the causes of these changes.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2018.04.017","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"47-57","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Unveiling the patterns and trends in 40 years of global trade in CITES-listed wildlife","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320717312478","volume":"223","author":[{"family":"Harfoot","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Satu A.M."},{"family":"Tittensor","given":"Derek P."},{"family":"Britten","given":"Gregory L."},{"family":"McLardy","given":"Claire"},{"family":"Malsch","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Burgess","given":"Neil D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}},{"id":32352,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XP2T8FRZ"],"itemData":{"id":32352,"type":"book","event-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","publisher":"International Trade Centre","publisher-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","title":"Trade in Vicuña: the Implications for Conservation and Rural Livelihoods.","author":[{"family":"Kasterine","given":"A"},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An upward trend in the number of species being captive bred and the number of breeding facilities was recorded from 1960 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZpPeUx4g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2000)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32325,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W7WLPMKV"],"itemData":{"id":32325,"type":"report","number":"IUCN Report (09/11/00)","publisher":"The Economics, Trade and Environment Unit United Nations Environment Programme","title":"Trade Measures in Multilateral Environmental Agreements. A report by the IUCN on the Effectiveness of Trade Measures Contained in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES)","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and thus precedes the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, but the trend has continued since 1975 with more species affected and a greater number of facilities. The number of facilities registered with the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora for captive breeding of Appendix I animals increased from 59 in 16 countries in 1997 to over 400 in 34 countries in 2021 (www.cites.org). The number of species being registered for captive breeding has also increased from 16 to 34. For these species there is a definite link to the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora because commercial trade from wild sources is prohibited.
      The shift from wild sourced to captive or artificially propagated sources would be expected to reduce unsustainable levels of harvest from wild populations if associated with better harvest practices and/or an actual reduction in harvest pressure to sustainable levels. The counterfactual evidence indicates that current trade volumes for many of the specimens sourced from captive or artificially propagated sources would not be sustainable if sourced from the wild. The data presented by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4hfDDsB8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Michael Harfoot 2018)","plainCitation":"(Michael Harfoot 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2Q78L7PC","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RAMZGR28"],"itemData":{"id":11383,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife trade can provide commercial incentives to conserve biodiversity but, if unsustainable, can also pose a threat. CITES (Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora) aims to ensure international trade in CITES-listed species is sustainable, legal and traceable. However, large-scale temporal and spatial patterns in wildlife trade are poorly known. We address this by analysing the CITES Trade Database: > 16 million shipment records for 28,282 species, from 1975 and 2014. Over this period, the volume of reported trade in CITES-listed wildlife quadrupled, from 25 million whole-organism equivalents per year to 100 million, and the ratio of wild- to captive-sourced trade in mammals, birds, reptiles, invertebrates and plants declined by an order of magnitude or more. Our ?ndings start to reveal the scale of the legal wildlife trade, shifting trade routes and sources over time and we describe testable hypotheses for the causes of these changes.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2018.04.017","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"47-57","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Unveiling the patterns and trends in 40 years of global trade in CITES-listed wildlife","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320717312478","volume":"223","author":[{"family":"Harfoot","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Satu A.M."},{"family":"Tittensor","given":"Derek P."},{"family":"Britten","given":"Gregory L."},{"family":"McLardy","given":"Claire"},{"family":"Malsch","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Burgess","given":"Neil D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} show a consistent decline in trade of wild-sourced specimens especially for reptiles and plants, and to a lesser extent for birds, invertebrates and mammals, which would be expected to reduce pressure on wild populations. More specific evidence for taxa such as crocodiles shows positive outcomes for wild species when trade is directed towards specimens from captive populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AWC65tZ1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. W. G. Jenkins, 2004)","plainCitation":"(R. W. G. Jenkins, 2004)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32318,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9FUC8PYW"],"itemData":{"id":32318,"type":"report","event-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","number":"CITES AC22 Inf.2","publisher-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","title":"Review of Crocodile Ranching Programs","author":[{"family":"Jenkins","given":"R.W.G."},{"family":"Jelden","given":"D."},{"family":"Webb","given":"G.J.W."},{"family":"Manolis","given":"S.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, This is not always the case. The potential benefits for more sustainable use arising from captive sources can be undermined by possible laundering of wild-sourced specimens into trade under the guise of captive breeding ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EdytyhrO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lyons & Natusch, 2011; R. O. Martin, 2018; Nijman, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Lyons & Natusch, 2011; R. O. Martin, 2018; Nijman, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":716,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/28PSAP4U"],"itemData":{"id":716,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife breeding farms have been promoted to aid biodiversity conservation by alleviating the pressure of harvest on wild populations. There is, however, growing concern that many breeding farms are being used to launder illegally caught wildlife. Surveys of wildlife traders in the Indonesian provinces of Maluku, West Papua and Papua were conducted between August 2009 and April 2011 to assess the trade of the green python (Morelia viridis), the species currently exported in the largest numbers from Indonesia declared as captive-bred. In total, 4227 illegally collected wild green pythons were recorded during surveys and high levels of harvest were found to have depleted and skewed the demographics of some island populations. Snakes were traced from their point of capture to breeding farms in Jakarta where they are to be exported for the pet trade, confirming the reports of wildlife laundering. Extrapolation of monthly collection estimates provided by traders revealed that at least 5337 green pythons are collected each year, suggesting that at least 80% of the green pythons exported from Indonesia annually are illegally wild-caught. The results of examination of 139 eggshells from five python species suggest that reptilian eggshells may be used as proof of provenance for each individual reptile exported. This method, in addition to the evidence that breeding farms play a significant role in the illegal exploitation of wildlife, allows conservation managers to begin to adequately monitor, regulate and determine the role of breeding farms in the conservation of wild populations.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2011.10.002","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"3073-3081","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Wildlife laundering through breeding farms: Illegal harvest, population declines and a means of regulating the trade of green pythons (Morelia viridis) from Indonesia","title-short":"Wildlife laundering through breeding farms","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320711003685","volume":"144","author":[{"family":"Lyons","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel J. D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12,1]]}}},{"id":9337,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XQRZ47IY"],"itemData":{"id":9337,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000432248400006","container-title":"Ostrich","DOI":"10.2989/00306525.2017.1397787","ISSN":"0030-6525","issue":"2","page":"139-143","title":"The wild bird trade and African parrots: past, present and future challenges","URL":"://WOS:000432248400006","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Martin","given":"R. O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32332,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D2MEC2W6"],"itemData":{"id":32332,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2018.e00425","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e00425","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wildlife trade, captive breeding and the imminent extinction of a songbird","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2351989418301471","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Langgeng","given":"Abdullah"},{"family":"Birot","given":"Hélène"},{"family":"Imron","given":"Muhammad Ali"},{"family":"Nekaris","given":"K.A.I."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some taxa, there has also been continued unsustainable trade in wild-sourced specimens despite captive bred or artificially propagated alternatives either due to demand for specimens with wild provenance or characteristics, e.g., orchids ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DiyGVcW6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SU6WGFBJ"],"itemData":{"id":32354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society","DOI":"10.1093/botlinnean/box083","ISSN":"0024-4074, 1095-8339","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"435-455","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A review of the trade in orchids and its implications for conservation","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/botlinnean/article/186/4/435/4736317","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Boer","given":"Hugo J","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Fay","given":"Michael F"},{"family":"Gale","given":"Stephan W"},{"family":"Gardiner","given":"Lauren M"},{"family":"Gunasekara","given":"Rajasinghe S"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Pankaj"},{"family":"Masters","given":"Susanne"},{"family":"Metusala","given":"Destario"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L"},{"family":"Veldman","given":"Sarina"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Shan"},{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or because it is still relatively easy to source specimens from the wild, e.g., parrots ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KWWdW2PW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ribeiro, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Ribeiro, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32350,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IAGKC6XD"],"itemData":{"id":32350,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-019-01825-5","ISSN":"0960-3115, 1572-9710","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"3343-3369","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trends in legal and illegal trade of wild birds: a global assessment based on expert knowledge","title-short":"Trends in legal and illegal trade of wild birds","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10531-019-01825-5","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Ribeiro","given":"Joana"},{"family":"Reino","given":"Luís"},{"family":"Schindler","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Strubbe","given":"Diederik"},{"family":"Vall-llosera","given":"Miquel"},{"family":"Araújo","given":"Miguel Bastos"},{"family":"Capinha","given":"César"},{"family":"Carrete","given":"Martina"},{"family":"Mazzoni","given":"Sabrina"},{"family":"Monteiro","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Moreira","given":"Francisco"},{"family":"Rocha","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Tella","given":"José L."},{"family":"Vaz","given":"Ana Sofia"},{"family":"Vicente","given":"Joana"},{"family":"Nuno","given":"Ana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or because of weak enforcement.  There is no synthetic review of evidence available, so it is not possible assess the extent to which this undermines the intended benefits. An unintended outcome of shifts in trade from wild sources to captive breeding and artificial propagation is that it concentrates trade among the few actors who have the technology and capital to engage in captive breeding and this may have negative consequences for social equity, as well as sustainable livelihoods and conservation programs linked to wild populations in developing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"riqLrElr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Coconier & Lichtenstein, 2014; Cooney & Jepson, 2006; Roe, 2006; Roe, 2009)","plainCitation":"(E. Coconier & Lichtenstein, 2014; Cooney & Jepson, 2006; Roe, 2006; Roe, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32316,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N4TD4U4D"],"itemData":{"id":32316,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605306000172","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Oryx","language":"en","page":"27-28","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Blanket bans – conservation or imperialism? A response to Cooney & Jepson","title-short":"Blanket bans – conservation or imperialism?","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0030605306000172/type/journal_article","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",1]]}}},{"id":882,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BLTR5DLJ"],"itemData":{"id":882,"type":"book","abstract":"Provides a pan-African synthesis of community-based natural resource management (CBNRM), drawing on multiple authors and a wide range of documented experiences from Southern, Eastern, Western and Central Africa. This title discusses the degree to which CBNRM has met poverty alleviation, economic development and nature conservation objectives. More than twenty years have passed since community-based natural resource management (CBNRM) rose to prominence in different parts of Africa as a strategy for rural development, local empowerment, and conservation. Led by new ideas about the merits of decentralized, collective resource governance regimes, and creative field experiments such as Zimbabwe's CAMPFIRE, these community-based approaches evolved in a wide range of ecological, political, and social contexts across Africa. This review provides an unprecedented pan-African synthesis of CBNRM, drawing on multiple authors and a wide range of documented experiences from Southern, Eastern, Western and Central Africa. The review discusses the degree to which CBNRM has met poverty alleviation, economic development and nature conservation objectives. In its concluding chapter, the report suggests a way forward for strengthening CBNRM and addressing key challenges in the years ahead.","call-number":"Esub44","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-84369-755-8","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 731367128","publisher":"IIED","publisher-place":"London","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Community management of natural resources in Africa: impacts, experiences and future directions","title-short":"Community management of natural resources in Africa","author":[{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Nelson","given":"Fred"},{"family":"Sandbrook","given":"Chris"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":32506,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VYV2NH39"],"itemData":{"id":32506,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605306000056","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"18–23","title":"The international wild bird trade: What's wrong with blanket bans?","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Cooney","given":"R."},{"family":"Jepson","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":32505,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8ELU9KEY"],"itemData":{"id":32505,"type":"book","event-place":"Posadas","language":"es","number-of-pages":"107–127","publisher":"Programa de Postgrado en Antropología Social- UNaM","publisher-place":"Posadas","title":"Loros, Gripe Aviar y Soja: Los alcances de Políticas Globales sobre Proyectos Locales. Avá, Revista de Antropología N°24 Edición Especial, jun 2014","author":[{"family":"Coconier","given":"E."},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      A compelling indicator for the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora acting as a driver of legal and more sustainable levels of harvest and trade would be an improvement in the conservation status of those species subjected to unsustainable international trade. For birds and mammals assessed for the ternational Union for Conservation of Nature Red List of Threatened Species, where resource use was listed as a threat and they were listed in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Appendices, 84% that were listed on the Appendix I and 76% of those on Appendix II had decreasing population trends (IUCN Red List). The population trend data should be complemented by the Red List Index ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SvWTM1eO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Butchart, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Butchart, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32323,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MNVKP7CR"],"itemData":{"id":32323,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0000140","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e140","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Improvements to the Red List Index","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0000140","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H.M."},{"family":"Resit Akçakaya","given":"H."},{"family":"Chanson","given":"Janice"},{"family":"Baillie","given":"Jonathan E.M."},{"family":"Collen","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Quader","given":"Suhel"},{"family":"Turner","given":"Will R."},{"family":"Amin","given":"Rajan"},{"family":"Stuart","given":"Simon N."},{"family":"Hilton-Taylor","given":"Craig"}],"editor":[{"family":"Lusseau","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",1,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which provides a more composite index for tracking changes in conservation status of groups of species. A Red List Index analysis specifically for species listed on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora is not yet available but other assessments of utilized species provide some insights into the impact of measures to address unsustainable levels of harvest and trade. 
      The Red List Index for all fully assessed species where international trade has been documented (www.iucnredlist.org/api/v4/rlindex/image/988730) shows an ongoing decline in status from 1996 to 2020 without any evidence of an upward inflection that would indicate successful interventions. The rate of decline in the Red List Index for species in international trade is steeper than for all terrestrial species combined but comparable to the rate of decline for marine species. A more detailed assessment of the impact of conservation measures on the status of the world’s vertebrates, using Red List Index for all data-sufficient species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pxlZ2x5H","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hoffmann, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Hoffmann, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34842,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FWN549W6"],"itemData":{"id":34842,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1194442","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6010","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"1503-1509","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Impact of Conservation on the Status of the World’s Vertebrates","URL":"https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.1194442","volume":"330","author":[{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Hilton-Taylor","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Angulo","given":"Ariadne"},{"family":"Böhm","given":"Monika"},{"family":"Brooks","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H. M."},{"family":"Carpenter","given":"Kent E."},{"family":"Chanson","given":"Janice"},{"family":"Collen","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Cox","given":"Neil A."},{"family":"Darwall","given":"William R. T."},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K."},{"family":"Harrison","given":"Lucy R."},{"family":"Katariya","given":"Vineet"},{"family":"Pollock","given":"Caroline M."},{"family":"Quader","given":"Suhel"},{"family":"Richman","given":"Nadia I."},{"family":"Rodrigues","given":"Ana S. L."},{"family":"Tognelli","given":"Marcelo F."},{"family":"Vié","given":"Jean-Christophe"},{"family":"Aguiar","given":"John M."},{"family":"Allen","given":"David J."},{"family":"Allen","given":"Gerald R."},{"family":"Amori","given":"Giovanni"},{"family":"Ananjeva","given":"Natalia B."},{"family":"Andreone","given":"Franco"},{"family":"Andrew","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Ortiz","given":"Aida Luz Aquino"},{"family":"Baillie","given":"Jonathan E. M."},{"family":"Baldi","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Bell","given":"Ben D."},{"family":"Biju","given":"S. D."},{"family":"Bird","given":"Jeremy P."},{"family":"Black-Decima","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Blanc","given":"J. Julian"},{"family":"Bolaños","given":"Federico"},{"family":"Bolivar-G.","given":"Wilmar"},{"family":"Burfield","given":"Ian J."},{"family":"Burton","given":"James A."},{"family":"Capper","given":"David R."},{"family":"Castro","given":"Fernando"},{"family":"Catullo","given":"Gianluca"},{"family":"Cavanagh","given":"Rachel D."},{"family":"Channing","given":"Alan"},{"family":"Chao","given":"Ning Labbish"},{"family":"Chenery","given":"Anna M."},{"family":"Chiozza","given":"Federica"},{"family":"Clausnitzer","given":"Viola"},{"family":"Collar","given":"Nigel J."},{"family":"Collett","given":"Leah C."},{"family":"Collette","given":"Bruce B."},{"family":"Fernandez","given":"Claudia F. Cortez"},{"family":"Craig","given":"Matthew T."},{"family":"Crosby","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Cumberlidge","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Cuttelod","given":"Annabelle"},{"family":"Derocher","given":"Andrew E."},{"family":"Diesmos","given":"Arvin C."},{"family":"Donaldson","given":"John S."},{"family":"Duckworth","given":"J. W."},{"family":"Dutson","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Dutta","given":"S. K."},{"family":"Emslie","given":"Richard H."},{"family":"Farjon","given":"Aljos"},{"family":"Fowler","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Freyhof","given":"Jörg"},{"family":"Garshelis","given":"David L."},{"family":"Gerlach","given":"Justin"},{"famil