."},{"family":"Rodríguez","given":"Lily O."},{"family":"Ron","given":"Santiago R."},{"family":"Rondinini","given":"Carlo"},{"family":"Rylands","given":"Anthony B."},{"family":"Sadovy de Mitcheson","given":"Yvonne"},{"family":"Sanciangco","given":"Jonnell C."},{"family":"Sanders","given":"Kate L."},{"family":"Santos-Barrera","given":"Georgina"},{"family":"Schipper","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Self-Sullivan","given":"Caryn"},{"family":"Shi","given":"Yichuan"},{"family":"Shoemaker","given":"Alan"},{"family":"Short","given":"Frederick T."},{"family":"Sillero-Zubiri","given":"Claudio"},{"family":"Silvano","given":"Débora L."},{"family":"Smith","given":"Kevin G."},{"family":"Smith","given":"Andrew T."},{"family":"Snoeks","given":"Jos"},{"family":"Stattersfield","given":"Alison J."},{"family":"Symes","given":"Andrew J."},{"family":"Taber","given":"Andrew B."},{"family":"Talukdar","given":"Bibhab K."},{"family":"Temple","given":"Helen J."},{"family":"Timmins","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Tobias","given":"Joseph A."},{"family":"Tsytsulina","given":"Katerina"},{"family":"Tweddle","given":"Denis"},{"family":"Ubeda","given":"Carmen"},{"family":"Valenti","given":"Sarah V."},{"family":"Paul van Dijk","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Veiga","given":"Liza M."},{"family":"Veloso","given":"Alberto"},{"family":"Wege","given":"David C."},{"family":"Wilkinson","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Williamson","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Xie","given":"Feng"},{"family":"Young","given":"Bruce E."},{"family":"Akçakaya","given":"H. Resit"},{"family":"Bennun","given":"Leon"},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim M."},{"family":"Boitani","given":"Luigi"},{"family":"Dublin","given":"Holly T."},{"family":"Fonseca","given":"Gustavo A. B.","non-dropping-particle":"da"},{"family":"Gascon","given":"Claude"},{"family":"Lacher","given":"Thomas E."},{"family":"Mace","given":"Georgina M."},{"family":"Mainka","given":"Susan A."},{"family":"McNeely","given":"Jeffery A."},{"family":"Mittermeier","given":"Russell A."},{"family":"Reid","given":"Gordon McGregor"},{"family":"Rodriguez","given":"Jon Paul"},{"family":"Rosenberg","given":"Andrew A."},{"family":"Samways","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Smart","given":"Jane"},{"family":"Stein","given":"Bruce A."},{"family":"Stuart","given":"Simon N."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",12,10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, concluded that measures to address exploitation (hunting) had limited positive impacts on the conservation status of affected mammal species between 1996 and 2008 (62 species deteriorated, 6 improved) but better results for birds from 1988 to 2008 (31 deteriorated, 9 improvements). Separate analyses for birds ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8iL1WTfa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Butchart, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Butchart, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32360,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LFV76C7F"],"itemData":{"id":32360,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            After habitat loss and degradation, the leading threats to biodiversity are over-exploitation and invasive alien species. For birds, newly synthesised data using the standard classification schemes for utilisation and threat types for the IUCN Red List allow novel analyses on the importance of these threats and permit the calculation of Red List Indices (RLIs) to show trends in the status of birds driven by these factors. At least 45.7% of extant bird species (4,561 species) are used by humans, principally for pets (37.0%) and for hunting for food (14.2%), but other uses include sport hunting, ornamentation and traditional medicine. Much of this use drives trade at an international scale, involving at least 3,337 species (33.9%, substantially higher than previous estimates), mostly for the pet trade. RLIs show that although successful control and management of use and trade have led to some species improving in status, this has been outweighed by the number of species deteriorating in status owing to unsustainable exploitation. Overall, the RLI showing trends in extinction risk driven by issues related to use shows a negative slope: human use of birds is currently unsustainable. Similarly, and of relevance to the Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species (CITES), the RLI for internationally traded bird species showing trends in extinction risk driven by issues related to international trade is also declining: international trade remains a threat to the world's birds. Invasive species impact at least one third of the world's threatened bird species (398 species, 32.6%), with mammals being the most important (impacting 81.1%), particularly through predation by carnivores and rodents. The RLI illustrating impacts of invasive species shows that they are driving a deterioration in the status of the world's birds. RLIs for the impacts of use and invasive species will be important indicators to help track progress towards the target of significantly reducing biodiversity loss by 2010.","container-title":"Bird Conservation International","DOI":"10.1017/S095927090800035X","ISSN":"0959-2709, 1474-0001","issue":"S1","journalAbbreviation":"Bird Conservation International","language":"en","page":"S245-S262","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Red List Indices to measure the sustainability of species use and impacts of invasive alien species","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S095927090800035X/type/journal_article","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and parrots ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UvRuDZCg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Olah, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Olah, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32326,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VT473BC4"],"itemData":{"id":32326,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-015-1036-z","ISSN":"0960-3115, 1572-9710","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"205-223","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ecological and socio-economic factors affecting extinction risk in parrots","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10531-015-1036-z","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Olah","given":"George"},{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H. M."},{"family":"Symes","given":"Andy"},{"family":"Guzmán","given":"Iliana Medina"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Ross"},{"family":"Brightsmith","given":"Donald J."},{"family":"Heinsohn","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} also show ongoing declines in the Red List Index for species in trade. 
      There are several caveats to using the available Red List and Red List Index data to assess the impact of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. The first is that Red List Index data refer to all utilized species and not only those listed on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. The second is that few if any species are only traded internationally and for many utilized species (including those listed on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora) there is a significant domestic trade that is not under regulation through the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora- examples include bears (Ursus sp.) in Japan ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oQnPug4x","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mano & Ishii, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Mano & Ishii, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32353,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IF2PENVL"],"itemData":{"id":32353,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"International trade in bears and their parts is regulated under the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) because of the negative effect of gallbladder trade on bear populations. Although a significant number of bear gallbladders seems to come from the roughly 2,000 individuals killed annually as game and nuisance of the 2 species of bears (Ursus thibetanus and U. arctos) in Japan, information about the trade and usage remains obscure due to the lack of a system to regulate trade in bear parts such as gallbladders and meat. Most Japanese bear populations are considered to be at a sufficient level to sustain hunting if well-managed; however, nuisance bear control kills are not properly conducted due to inadequate management systems and regulations. Governmental organizations have not participated directly in nuisance bear control but depend on private hunters in exchange for allowing them to keep bear parts from nuisance kills. However, it will become difficult to continue depending on private hunters due to their aging and the decline in their numbers. This situation will require a new nuisance bear management system. We outline a framework for a management system for the domestic trade in gall derived from wild bears in Japan. Such a system would use the profits from bear gall trade to partially cover the cost of bear management activities, including damage prevention.","container-title":"Ursus","DOI":"10.2192/1537-6176-19.2.122","ISSN":"1537-6176, 1938-5439","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"ursu","note":"publisher: International Association for Bear Research and Management","page":"122-129","source":"bioone.org","title":"Bear Gallbladder Trade Issues and A Framework for Bear Management in Japan","URL":"https://bioone.org/journals/ursus/volume-19/issue-2/1537-6176-19.2.122/Bear-Gallbladder-Trade-Issues-and-A-Framework-for-Bear-Management/10.2192/1537-6176-19.2.122.full","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Mano","given":"Tsutomu"},{"family":"Ishii","given":"Nobuo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, songbirds in Indonesia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QEZZbzJe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nijman, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Nijman, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32332,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D2MEC2W6"],"itemData":{"id":32332,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2018.e00425","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e00425","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wildlife trade, captive breeding and the imminent extinction of a songbird","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2351989418301471","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Langgeng","given":"Abdullah"},{"family":"Birot","given":"Hélène"},{"family":"Imron","given":"Muhammad Ali"},{"family":"Nekaris","given":"K.A.I."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, orchids in Vietnam ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SQZMdogm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bullough, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Bullough, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32319,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9QC6ZGDH"],"itemData":{"id":32319,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable and illegal wildlife trade is a well-known conservation issue, but there are still large gaps in our understanding of how trade chains operate for the majority of over-exploited wildlife products. In particular, the large-scale global plant trade is under-reported and under-researched, and this is even more pronounced when the trade takes place within a country’s borders. A clear example is the trade in orchids, all species of which are listed by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES). Although countries such as Viet Nam are known hotspots for the large-scale collection of wild orchids for the international horticultural trade, little is known about how plants move from the wild to the end-consumer, what role is played by domestic markets and the sustainability of this trade. We use a mixed-methods approach to determine the structure of trade chains for orchids in key trading areas of Northern Viet Nam, and use a thematic framework to identify five groups of actors trading wild-harvested orchids. Trade occurs both domestically and internationally, underpinned by demand for rare, wild plants. An important first step to address the illegal and unsustainable plant trade is to recognise it as a major and growing conservation issue, and develop diverse approaches that consider the complexity of the supply chains involved. It is imperative that the scale and process of domestic trade is understood, and its impact on long term conservation of these species assessed to make more informed decisions about effective interventions that take into account the full supply chain.","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.3389/fevo.2021.631795","ISSN":"2296-701X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Ecol. Evol.","page":"631795","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Orchid Obscurity: Understanding Domestic Trade in Wild-Harvested Orchids in Viet Nam","title-short":"Orchid Obscurity","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fevo.2021.631795/full","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Bullough","given":"Leigh-Anne"},{"family":"Nguy?n","given":"Nga"},{"family":"Drury","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",6,25]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and pangolin in Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1OOYZEhE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mambeya, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Mambeya, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32324,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z4FCUXPC"],"itemData":{"id":32324,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"African Journal of Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/aje.12507","ISSN":"01416707","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Afr J Ecol","language":"en","page":"601-609","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The emergence of a commercial trade in pangolins from Gabon","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/aje.12507","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Mambeya","given":"Meine M."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Francesca"},{"family":"Momboua","given":"Brice R."},{"family":"Koumba Pambo","given":"Aurélie Flore"},{"family":"Hega","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Okouyi Okouyi","given":"Vivien Joseph"},{"family":"Onanga","given":"Martial"},{"family":"Challender","given":"Daniel W. S."},{"family":"Ingram","given":"Daniel J."},{"family":"Wang","given":"Hongyan"},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"Katharine"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Third, in most cases, utilization is not the only driver leading to decline and it is often not possible to separate out the effects of conservation measures aimed at use and trade. Analyses of birds ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RhDCFuLC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Butchart, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Butchart, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32360,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LFV76C7F"],"itemData":{"id":32360,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            After habitat loss and degradation, the leading threats to biodiversity are over-exploitation and invasive alien species. For birds, newly synthesised data using the standard classification schemes for utilisation and threat types for the IUCN Red List allow novel analyses on the importance of these threats and permit the calculation of Red List Indices (RLIs) to show trends in the status of birds driven by these factors. At least 45.7% of extant bird species (4,561 species) are used by humans, principally for pets (37.0%) and for hunting for food (14.2%), but other uses include sport hunting, ornamentation and traditional medicine. Much of this use drives trade at an international scale, involving at least 3,337 species (33.9%, substantially higher than previous estimates), mostly for the pet trade. RLIs show that although successful control and management of use and trade have led to some species improving in status, this has been outweighed by the number of species deteriorating in status owing to unsustainable exploitation. Overall, the RLI showing trends in extinction risk driven by issues related to use shows a negative slope: human use of birds is currently unsustainable. Similarly, and of relevance to the Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species (CITES), the RLI for internationally traded bird species showing trends in extinction risk driven by issues related to international trade is also declining: international trade remains a threat to the world's birds. Invasive species impact at least one third of the world's threatened bird species (398 species, 32.6%), with mammals being the most important (impacting 81.1%), particularly through predation by carnivores and rodents. The RLI illustrating impacts of invasive species shows that they are driving a deterioration in the status of the world's birds. RLIs for the impacts of use and invasive species will be important indicators to help track progress towards the target of significantly reducing biodiversity loss by 2010.","container-title":"Bird Conservation International","DOI":"10.1017/S095927090800035X","ISSN":"0959-2709, 1474-0001","issue":"S1","journalAbbreviation":"Bird Conservation International","language":"en","page":"S245-S262","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Red List Indices to measure the sustainability of species use and impacts of invasive alien species","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S095927090800035X/type/journal_article","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and parrots ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IeVzJ7m9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Olah, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Olah, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32326,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VT473BC4"],"itemData":{"id":32326,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-015-1036-z","ISSN":"0960-3115, 1572-9710","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"205-223","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Ecological and socio-economic factors affecting extinction risk in parrots","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10531-015-1036-z","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Olah","given":"George"},{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H. M."},{"family":"Symes","given":"Andy"},{"family":"Guzmán","given":"Iliana Medina"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Ross"},{"family":"Brightsmith","given":"Donald J."},{"family":"Heinsohn","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} show that species in trade have continued to decline but the larger impacts of habitat loss make it difficult to determine the actual trend linked only to use and trade. A further example is reef-forming corals where substantial decline in the Red List Index due to a coral bleaching event in 1998 obscured most other factors affecting the status of corals, including localized impacts associated with unsustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UYZzATG2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carpenter, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Carpenter, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32322,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SWCEVLK7"],"itemData":{"id":32322,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1159196","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5888","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"560-563","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"One-Third of Reef-Building Corals Face Elevated Extinction Risk from Climate Change and Local Impacts","URL":"https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.1159196","volume":"321","author":[{"family":"Carpenter","given":"Kent E."},{"family":"Abrar","given":"Muhammad"},{"family":"Aeby","given":"Greta"},{"family":"Aronson","given":"Richard B."},{"family":"Banks","given":"Stuart"},{"family":"Bruckner","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Chiriboga","given":"Angel"},{"family":"Cortés","given":"Jorge"},{"family":"Delbeek","given":"J. Charles"},{"family":"DeVantier","given":"Lyndon"},{"family":"Edgar","given":"Graham J."},{"family":"Edwards","given":"Alasdair J."},{"family":"Fenner","given":"Douglas"},{"family":"Guzmán","given":"Héctor M."},{"family":"Hoeksema","given":"Bert W."},{"family":"Hodgson","given":"Gregor"},{"family":"Johan","given":"Ofri"},{"family":"Licuanan","given":"Wilfredo Y."},{"family":"Livingstone","given":"Suzanne R."},{"family":"Lovell","given":"Edward R."},{"family":"Moore","given":"Jennifer A."},{"family":"Obura","given":"David O."},{"family":"Ochavillo","given":"Domingo"},{"family":"Polidoro","given":"Beth A."},{"family":"Precht","given":"William F."},{"family":"Quibilan","given":"Miledel C."},{"family":"Reboton","given":"Clarissa"},{"family":"Richards","given":"Zoe T."},{"family":"Rogers","given":"Alex D."},{"family":"Sanciangco","given":"Jonnell"},{"family":"Sheppard","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Sheppard","given":"Charles"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Stuart","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Turak","given":"Emre"},{"family":"Veron","given":"John E. N."},{"family":"Wallace","given":"Carden"},{"family":"Weil","given":"Ernesto"},{"family":"Wood","given":"Elizabeth"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",7,25]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Even with these caveats, the general conclusion from these higher-level analyses is that interventions to address decline due to utilization have not yet changed the trajectory of decline. 
      Expanding beyond the evidence from Red Lists, the literature reported on various conservation outcomes for species listed on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. A review of the effectiveness of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Tk6muocG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2000)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32325,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W7WLPMKV"],"itemData":{"id":32325,"type":"report","number":"IUCN Report (09/11/00)","publisher":"The Economics, Trade and Environment Unit United Nations Environment Programme","title":"Trade Measures in Multilateral Environmental Agreements. A report by the IUCN on the Effectiveness of Trade Measures Contained in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES)","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} noted the success for reducing unsustainable trade in furs from spotted cats and non-human primates. These were regarded as successful due to them acting in conjunction with other interventions such as media campaigns to end the use of furs. The report noted the far less successful outcomes for rhino and for many plant species. Additional literature on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listed taxa includes camelids ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"w4g8TWI4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Kasterine & Lichtenstein, 2018)","plainCitation":"(A. Kasterine & Lichtenstein, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32352,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XP2T8FRZ"],"itemData":{"id":32352,"type":"book","event-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","publisher":"International Trade Centre","publisher-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","title":"Trade in Vicuña: the Implications for Conservation and Rural Livelihoods.","author":[{"family":"Kasterine","given":"A"},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, eels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"A5DlH8z6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nijman, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Nijman, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9349,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HC4X89LF"],"itemData":{"id":9349,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000356745700005","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2015.04.006","ISSN":"0308-597X","page":"36-41","title":"CITES-listings, EU eel trade bans and the increase of export of tropical eels out of Indonesia","URL":"://WOS:000356745700005","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, manta rays ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wvGp0lO0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Booth, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Booth, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JKGTX2WU"],"itemData":{"id":7545,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2020.e00953","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e00953","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Assessing the impact of regulations on the use and trade of wildlife: An operational framework, with a case study on manta rays","title-short":"Assessing the impact of regulations on the use and trade of wildlife","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2351989419305517","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Booth","given":"Hollie"},{"family":"Pooley","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Clements","given":"Tom"},{"family":"Putra","given":"Mochamad Iqbal Herwata"},{"family":"Lestari","given":"W. Peni"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Warwick","given":"Luke"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, orchids ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BsOsARly","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018; Phelps & Webb, 2015a)","plainCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018; Phelps & Webb, 2015a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SU6WGFBJ"],"itemData":{"id":32354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society","DOI":"10.1093/botlinnean/box083","ISSN":"0024-4074, 1095-8339","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"435-455","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A review of the trade in orchids and its implications for conservation","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/botlinnean/article/186/4/435/4736317","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Boer","given":"Hugo J","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Fay","given":"Michael F"},{"family":"Gale","given":"Stephan W"},{"family":"Gardiner","given":"Lauren M"},{"family":"Gunasekara","given":"Rajasinghe S"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Pankaj"},{"family":"Masters","given":"Susanne"},{"family":"Metusala","given":"Destario"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L"},{"family":"Veldman","given":"Sarina"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Shan"},{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3,27]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":4610,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WYG9W28F"],"itemData":{"id":4610,"type":"document","language":"pt","note":"DOI: 10.1016/j.biocon.2015.03.030.","title":"‘Invisible’ Wildlife Trades: Southeast Asia’s Undocumented Illegal Trade in Wild Ornamental Plants","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biocon.2015.03.030.","author":[{"family":"Phelps","given":"J."},{"family":"Webb","given":"Edward L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, parrots ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Q4Bhw5Fz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. O. Martin, 2018)","plainCitation":"(R. O. Martin, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9337,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XQRZ47IY"],"itemData":{"id":9337,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000432248400006","container-title":"Ostrich","DOI":"10.2989/00306525.2017.1397787","ISSN":"0030-6525","issue":"2","page":"139-143","title":"The wild bird trade and African parrots: past, present and future challenges","URL":"://WOS:000432248400006","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Martin","given":"R. O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"},  reptiles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qNf8pQ82","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. E. Robinson, 2015a)","plainCitation":"(J. E. Robinson, 2015a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9367,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YTDW4Q4X"],"itemData":{"id":9367,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2014.12.019","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"42-50","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Dynamics of the global trade in live reptiles: Shifting trends in production and consequences for sustainability","title-short":"Dynamics of the global trade in live reptiles","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320714004984","volume":"184","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"Janine E."},{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Richard A."},{"family":"St. John","given":"Freya A.V."},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, sea horses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wnVuQDaU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Foster, 2016; T. C. Kuo & Vincent, 2018)","plainCitation":"(S. Foster, 2016; T. C. Kuo & Vincent, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1508,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZJ7Y9AIZ"],"itemData":{"id":1508,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"* In principle, the database generated by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) offers an unparalleled opportunity to analyse trade in species of conservation concern. * The value of the database is assessed in the context of trade in 47 species of seahorse (Hippocampus spp.), all of which are included on CITES Appendix II. This listing requires that all 180 Parties to CITES (member Parties) limit exports to levels that do not damage wild populations, ensure they are obtained legally, and report their trade to CITES. * An evident need for greater universal compliance with CITES reporting requirements was identified. The most glaring problem was a substantial mismatch in species and volumes between export records and import records, indicating that neither dataset is complete nor reliable. * The evaluation also showed that Parties should increase compliance with CITES requirements to record all trade shipments, provide units for exports (e.g. individuals, kilograms) and identify exported taxa to species, perhaps supported by automated checking of entries. * The challenges with the CITES trade database were more evident for the global trade in dried seahorses than the smaller and more easily-tracked trade in live seahorses. Nonetheless, CITES' data from 2004–2011 revealed a seahorse trade involving millions of animals, tens of species, and scores of Parties. * CITES data have also proven invaluable in supporting CITES reviews of how Parties are implementing the Convention for seahorses, and in generating consequent action for their conservation. Copyright © 2014 John Wiley & Sons, Ltd.","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2493","ISSN":"10990755","issue":"1","page":"154–172","title":"Opportunities and challenges for analysis of wildlife trade using CITES data - seahorses as a case study","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Foster","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Wiswedel","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Vincent","given":"Amanda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",2]]}}},{"id":9334,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JJHJNREY"],"itemData":{"id":9334,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000424961300006","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.10.031","ISSN":"0308-597X","page":"48-57","title":"Assessing the changes in international trade of marine fishes under CITES regulations - A case study of seahorses","URL":"://WOS:000424961300006","volume":"88","author":[{"family":"Kuo","given":"T. C."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, snakes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YRyrtllg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hierink, 2020a)","plainCitation":"(Hierink, 2020a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6044,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/73AUL53I"],"itemData":{"id":6044,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Trade in venomous and non-venomous snakes can negatively impact wild snake populations and may drive snakebite risk for people. However, we often lack sufficient trade data to identify where the potential risks for snake population decline and snakebite are highest. Currently, the legal, international trade of 164 snake species is regulated by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES). We analyzed CITES-listed snake trade from 1975 to 2018 using the recently released shipment-level CITES Trade Database to identify spatiotemporal trends of snake trade and generate insights regarding snake conservation and potential public health risks from snakebite. Commercially purposed pythons dominated the global snake trade, comprising 38.8% of all traded snakes. Live snakes were mainly exported by Ghana, Indonesia, Togo, and Benin, and imported by China and the USA. Venomous snake trade comprised 10.8% of all traded snakes, and over 75% of wild-sourced venomous snakes came from Indonesia. Although traded snakes in recent years are increasingly comprised of captive-bred animals, the majority of snakes are still wild-sourced ( > 60% between 2015 and 2017), including IUCN-listed species, with potentially detrimental impacts on conservation status. Further, the CITES Trade Database reveals geographic regions where venomous snakes are sourced from the wild, posing potential risks to snake catchers, traders, and pet owners. The database also documents the movement of non-native snake species through trade, with implications for conservation of native species. This study represents the first global analysis focused specifically on CITES-listed snake trade using the CITES Trade Database.","archive_location":"WOS:000556843900006","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2020.108601","ISSN":"0006-3207","title":"Forty-four years of global trade in CITES-listed snakes: Trends and implications for conservation and public health","URL":"://WOS:000556843900006","volume":"248","author":[{"family":"Hierink","given":"F."},{"family":"Bolon","given":"I."},{"family":"Durso","given":"A. M."},{"family":"Castaneda","given":"R. R.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Zambrana-Torrelio","given":"C."},{"family":"Eskew","given":"E. A."},{"family":"Ray","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, sturgeon (caviar) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"h9pRvfEK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Doukakis, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Doukakis, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":29848,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5I26V56Y"],"itemData":{"id":29848,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0040907","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e40907","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Testing the Effectiveness of an International Conservation Agreement: Marketplace Forensics and CITES Caviar Trade Regulation","title-short":"Testing the Effectiveness of an International Conservation Agreement","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0040907","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Doukakis","given":"Phaedra"},{"family":"Pikitch","given":"Ellen K."},{"family":"Rothschild","given":"Anna"},{"family":"DeSalle","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Amato","given":"George"},{"family":"Kolokotronis","given":"Sergios-Orestis"}],"editor":[{"family":"Steinke","given":"Dirk"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",7,25]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, as well as population studies for species where trade impacts should have declined due to listing on the the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Appendices, e.g. for cycads ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QWddEYDR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Okubamichael, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Okubamichael, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/83MLLFB5"],"itemData":{"id":9359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-016-1183-x","ISSN":"0960-3115, 1572-9710","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"2153-2170","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Repeat photography confirms alarming decline in South African cycads","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10531-016-1183-x","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Okubamichael","given":"Desale Y."},{"family":"Jack","given":"Sam"},{"family":"De Wet Bösenberg","given":"J."},{"family":"Timm Hoffman","given":"M."},{"family":"Donaldson","given":"John S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and elephants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5KRJmLaA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chase, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Chase, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7158,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GBH8YLGB"],"itemData":{"id":7158,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"African elephants (Loxodonta africana) are imperiled by poaching and habitat loss. Despite global attention to the plight of elephants, their population sizes and trends are uncertain or unknown over much of Africa. To conserve this iconic species, conservationists need timely, accurate data on elephant populations. Here, we report the results of the Great Elephant Census (GEC), the first continent-wide, standardized survey of African savannah elephants. We also provide the first quantitative model of elephant population trends across Africa. We estimated a population of 352,271 savannah elephants on study sites in 18 countries, representing approximately 93% of all savannah elephants in those countries. Elephant populations in survey areas with historical data decreased by an estimated 144,000 from 2007 to 2014, and populations are currently shrinking by 8% per year continent-wide, primarily due to poaching. Though 84% of elephants occurred in protected areas, many protected areas had carcass ratios that indicated high levels of elephant mortality. Results of the GEC show the necessity of action to end the African elephants’ downward trajectory by preventing poaching and protecting habitat.","call-number":"Esub44","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.2354","ISSN":"2167-8359","language":"en","page":"e2354","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Continent-wide survey reveals massive decline in African savannah elephants","URL":"https://peerj.com/articles/2354","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Chase","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Schlossberg","given":"Scott"},{"family":"Griffin","given":"Curtice R."},{"family":"Bouché","given":"Philippe J.C."},{"family":"Djene","given":"Sintayehu W."},{"family":"Elkan","given":"Paul W."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"Sam"},{"family":"Grossman","given":"Falk"},{"family":"Kohi","given":"Edward Mtarima"},{"family":"Landen","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Omondi","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Peltier","given":"Alexis"},{"family":"Selier","given":"S.A. Jeanetta"},{"family":"Sutcliffe","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",8,31]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These studies present mixed outcomes associated with the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, with some taxa showing strong recovery associated with the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora decisions, e.g., vicuña ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pj8IHfkO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. Kasterine Alex, Gabriela, 2018)","plainCitation":"(L. Kasterine Alex, Gabriela, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":16662,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YF5HDNR9"],"itemData":{"id":16662,"type":"report","language":"English","number":"SIVC-18.13.E","page":"37","publisher":"International Trade Center","title":"Trade in Vicuña Fibre Implications for Conservation and Rural Livelihoods","URL":"https://heinonline-org.ezproxy.library.wur.nl/HOL/TextGenerator?handle=hein.journals/eclawq41&collection=journals%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20&section=36&id=973&print=section&sectioncount=1&ext=.txt","author":[{"family":"Kasterine","given":"Lichtenstein","suffix":"Alex, Gabriela"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and crocodiles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qMTz3mFb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. W. G. Jenkins, 2004)","plainCitation":"(R. W. G. Jenkins, 2004)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32318,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9FUC8PYW"],"itemData":{"id":32318,"type":"report","event-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","number":"CITES AC22 Inf.2","publisher-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","title":"Review of Crocodile Ranching Programs","author":[{"family":"Jenkins","given":"R.W.G."},{"family":"Jelden","given":"D."},{"family":"Webb","given":"G.J.W."},{"family":"Manolis","given":"S.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, others showing positive trends, such as manta ray and sea horses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CuVmxByO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Booth, 2020; T. C. Kuo & Vincent, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Booth, 2020; T. C. Kuo & Vincent, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JKGTX2WU"],"itemData":{"id":7545,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2020.e00953","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e00953","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Assessing the impact of regulations on the use and trade of wildlife: An operational framework, with a case study on manta rays","title-short":"Assessing the impact of regulations on the use and trade of wildlife","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2351989419305517","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Booth","given":"Hollie"},{"family":"Pooley","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Clements","given":"Tom"},{"family":"Putra","given":"Mochamad Iqbal Herwata"},{"family":"Lestari","given":"W. Peni"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Warwick","given":"Luke"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",6]]}}},{"id":9334,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JJHJNREY"],"itemData":{"id":9334,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000424961300006","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.10.031","ISSN":"0308-597X","page":"48-57","title":"Assessing the changes in international trade of marine fishes under CITES regulations - A case study of seahorses","URL":"://WOS:000424961300006","volume":"88","author":[{"family":"Kuo","given":"T. C."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and yet others showing ongoing declines despite listing on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, such as African elephant ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"768QqJkl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chase, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Chase, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7158,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GBH8YLGB"],"itemData":{"id":7158,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"African elephants (Loxodonta africana) are imperiled by poaching and habitat loss. Despite global attention to the plight of elephants, their population sizes and trends are uncertain or unknown over much of Africa. To conserve this iconic species, conservationists need timely, accurate data on elephant populations. Here, we report the results of the Great Elephant Census (GEC), the first continent-wide, standardized survey of African savannah elephants. We also provide the first quantitative model of elephant population trends across Africa. We estimated a population of 352,271 savannah elephants on study sites in 18 countries, representing approximately 93% of all savannah elephants in those countries. Elephant populations in survey areas with historical data decreased by an estimated 144,000 from 2007 to 2014, and populations are currently shrinking by 8% per year continent-wide, primarily due to poaching. Though 84% of elephants occurred in protected areas, many protected areas had carcass ratios that indicated high levels of elephant mortality. Results of the GEC show the necessity of action to end the African elephants’ downward trajectory by preventing poaching and protecting habitat.","call-number":"Esub44","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.2354","ISSN":"2167-8359","language":"en","page":"e2354","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Continent-wide survey reveals massive decline in African savannah elephants","URL":"https://peerj.com/articles/2354","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Chase","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Schlossberg","given":"Scott"},{"family":"Griffin","given":"Curtice R."},{"family":"Bouché","given":"Philippe J.C."},{"family":"Djene","given":"Sintayehu W."},{"family":"Elkan","given":"Paul W."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"Sam"},{"family":"Grossman","given":"Falk"},{"family":"Kohi","given":"Edward Mtarima"},{"family":"Landen","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Omondi","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Peltier","given":"Alexis"},{"family":"Selier","given":"S.A. Jeanetta"},{"family":"Sutcliffe","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",8,31]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and African cycads ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IV76XVPs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Okubamichael, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Okubamichael, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/83MLLFB5"],"itemData":{"id":9359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-016-1183-x","ISSN":"0960-3115, 1572-9710","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"2153-2170","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Repeat photography confirms alarming decline in South African cycads","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10531-016-1183-x","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Okubamichael","given":"Desale Y."},{"family":"Jack","given":"Sam"},{"family":"De Wet Bösenberg","given":"J."},{"family":"Timm Hoffman","given":"M."},{"family":"Donaldson","given":"John S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      An important question regarding the role of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora as a driver of legal and sustainable use is the extent to which unsustainable legal trade is replaced by unsustainable illegal trade without actually reducing the impact on the target species. Trade is typically regarded as illegal when it violates procedures and laws ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Z9k8FGcH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Felbab-Brown, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Felbab-Brown, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32328,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NZJZJDC3"],"itemData":{"id":32328,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"The planet is currently experiencing alarming levels of species loss caused in large part by intensified poaching and wildlife trafficking driven by expanding demand, for medicines, for food, and for trophies. Affecting many more species than just the iconic elephants, rhinos, and tigers, the rate of extinction is now as much as 1000 times the historical average and the worst since the dinosaurs died out 65 million years ago. In addition to causing irretrievable biodiversity loss, wildlife trafficking also poses serious threats to public health, potentially triggering a global pandemic. The Extinction Market explores the causes, means, and consequences of poaching and wildlife trafficking, with a view to finding ways of suppressing them. Vanda Felbab-Brown travelled to the markets of Latin America, South and South East Asia, and eastern and southern Africa, to evaluate the effectiveness of various tools, including bans on legal trade, law enforcement, and interdiction; allowing legal supply from hunting or farming; alternative livelihoods; anti- money-laundering efforts; and demand reduction strategies. This is an urgent book offering meaningful solutions to one of the world's most pressing crises.\\"--Provided by publisher","call-number":"HV6410 .F45 2017","event-place":"Oxford ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-19-085511-6","note":"OCLC: ocn986237039","number-of-pages":"406","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"Oxford ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The extinction market: wildlife trafficking and how to counter it","title-short":"The extinction market","author":[{"family":"Felbab-Brown","given":"Vanda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and, by design, listing a species on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Appendix I makes commercial trade in wild collected specimens illegal and trade in Appendix II specimens would also be illegal if it does not comply with the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora conditions. There is evidence across many taxa for ongoing and often significant illegal trade in wild collected specimens of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listed taxa and for associated declines in wild populations due, at least in part, to this trade. Illegal trade has been documented generally for the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OTxXNT5e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNODC, 2020a)","plainCitation":"(UNODC, 2020a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9385,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4CEMIA5J"],"itemData":{"id":9385,"type":"book","event-place":"New York","publisher":"United Nations","publisher-place":"New York","title":"World Wildlife Crime Report 2020: Trafficking in Protected Species","author":[{"family":"UNODC","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and specifically for many taxa including rhinoceros TEMPITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KI7UTtwB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Emslie, 2016; le Roex & Ferreira, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Emslie, 2016; le Roex & Ferreira, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6026,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ELHA9LLL"],"itemData":{"id":6026,"type":"document","title":"African and Asian Rhinoceroses- Status, conservation and trade.","URL":"https://cites.org/sites/default/files/eng/cop/17/ WorkingDocs/E-CoP17-68-A5.pdf","author":[{"family":"Emslie","given":"R."},{"family":"Milliken","given":"Tom"},{"family":"Talukdar","given":"B."},{"family":"Ellis","given":"S."},{"family":"Knight","given":"M.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":7537,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9LJ6IR44"],"itemData":{"id":7537,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0236790","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e0236790","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Age structure changes indicate direct and indirect population impacts in illegally harvested black rhino","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0236790","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Roex","given":"Nikki","non-dropping-particle":"le"},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"Sam M."}],"editor":[{"family":"Apollonio","given":"Marco"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,29]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Nj368ZrK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. A. Chapman & White, 2021)","plainCitation":"(L. A. Chapman & White, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6016,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/67QFBQ8M"],"itemData":{"id":6016,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"African Journal of Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/aje.12842","ISSN":"0141-6707, 1365-2028","journalAbbreviation":"Afr J Ecol","language":"en","page":"aje.12842","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Patterns in rhino poaching activity on private land in South Africa","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/aje.12842","author":[{"family":"Chapman","given":"Laura A."},{"family":"White","given":"Piran C. L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, pangolin ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oRW3k74J","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dumenu, 2019; Kukrety, 2013; Nijman & Shepherd, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Dumenu, 2019; Kukrety, 2013; Nijman & Shepherd, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32351,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8SBIXZ9Z"],"itemData":{"id":32351,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2019.05.044","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"124-133","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Assessing the impact of felling/export ban and CITES designation on exploitation of African rosewood (Pterocarpus erinaceus)","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S000632071930117X","volume":"236","author":[{"family":"Dumenu","given":"William Kwadwo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",8]]}}},{"id":32361,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D9T4JJZG"],"itemData":{"id":32361,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Red Sanders (RS, Pterocarpus santalinus L.) is an endangered timber tree species endemic to southern India. Using stakeholders' perceptions, this study evaluates the existing restricted RS wood trade and provides insights for developing a sustainable trade policy by involving private landowners as a strategy for improving its conservation in the wild. Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities and Threats–Analytic Hierarchy Process framework was used to analyze perceptions of four stakeholder groups (Administrators, Landowners, Traders, and Knowledgeable Sources). There was a consensus among the stakeholders that the challenges for sustainable RS wood trade outweigh the positive features. High price of RS wood and availability of niche international market were seen as the most important Strengths. Long gestation crop (+40years), lack of Incentives, small land holdings, administrative complexities, and competition from commercial agriculture crops were identified as the main challenges. Results indicate that before proceeding with an imminent policy change, it is important to address the major challenges. These results provide a basis to prioritize and address the existing challenges and reinforce the positive features of RS wood trade. A sustainable wood trade policy formulated by incorporating stakeholders' perceptions may help in improving the conservation status of this endangered species.","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2012.08.014","ISSN":"1389-9341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"43-53","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Stakeholders' perceptions on developing sustainable Red Sanders (Pterocarpus santalinus L.) wood trade in Andhra Pradesh, India","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1389934112002018","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Kukrety","given":"Sidhanand"},{"family":"Dwivedi","given":"Puneet"},{"family":"Jose","given":"Shibu"},{"family":"Alavalapati","given":"Janaki R. R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",1,1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":9360,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LXAB9K7T"],"itemData":{"id":9360,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2020.108887","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"108887","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Underestimating the illegal wildlife trade: A ton or a tonne of pangolins?","title-short":"Underestimating the illegal wildlife trade","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320720309459","volume":"253","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"Chris R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TJzPk6ny","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Heinrich, 2016)","plainCitation":"(S. Heinrich, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31042,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NMR8WI7Z"],"itemData":{"id":31042,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The pangolin is greatly sought after for its various body parts, largely driven by demand from China. The mammal has been driven to the edge of extinction in Asia, with two Asian species listed as Critically Endangered in the International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List. With declining Asian pangolin populations, a shift in trade from Asian to African pangolin species has been suggested. The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) Trade Database provides a unique opportunity to investigate global trends in pangolin trade at the species level, across a broad temporal scale (1977–2014). We found that CITES trade in Asian pangolin species decreased through time, whilst trade in African species increased post 2000. The total number of incidents involving Asian species declined since 2000, yet they were still being traded in large volumes (more than 17,500 estimated whole Asian pangolins were traded from 2001 to 2014) despite a zero export quota for all wild sourced Asian species, traded for primarily commercial purposes. In 2014 all eight pangolin species were recorded in the CITES trade for the first time. An increasingly complex international network was identified through time, with the United States of America (US) being the dominant player in the global pangolin trade that was reported to CITES. The US was the most frequent trade country throughout the entire period and was the greatest importer of pangolins, and their products; measured both in volume as well as frequency. We hope that identifying these global trade network characteristics, and pangolin trade dynamics will help to inform pangolin conservation efforts, and guide enforcement and legislative changes in the future.","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2016.09.007","ISSN":"2351-9894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"241-253","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Where did all the pangolins go? International CITES trade in pangolin species","title-short":"Where did all the pangolins go?","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2351989416300798","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Heinrich","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Wittmann","given":"Talia A."},{"family":"Prowse","given":"Thomas A. A."},{"family":"Ross","given":"Joshua V."},{"family":"Delean","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"Chris R."},{"family":"Cassey","given":"Phillip"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",10,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, reptiles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BHsmkx9Q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Auliya, Altherr,, 2016; Luiselli, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Auliya, Altherr,, 2016; Luiselli, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32746,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R29PGGD2"],"itemData":{"id":32746,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Of the 10,272 currently recognized reptile species, the trade of fewer than 8% are regulated by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and the European Wildlife Trade Regulations (EWTR). However, the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List has assessed 45% of the world's reptile species and determined that at least 1390 species are threatened by “biological resource use”. Of these, 355 species are intentionally targeted by collectors, including 194 non-CITES-listed species. Herein we review the global reptile pet trade, its impacts, and its contribution to the over-harvesting of species and populations, in light of current international law. Findings are based on an examination of relevant professional observations, online sources, and literature (e.g., applicable policies, taxonomy [reptile database], trade statistics [EUROSTAT], and conservation status [IUCN Red List]). Case studies are presented from the following countries and regions: Australia, Central America, China, Galapagos Islands (Ecuador), Germany, Europe, India, Indonesia (Kalimantan), Islamic Republic of Iran, Japan, Madagascar, Mexico, New Zealand, the Philippines, South Africa, Sri Lanka, Vietnam, Western Africa, and Western Asia. The European Union (EU) plays a major role in reptile trade. Between 2004 and 2014 (the period under study), the EU member states officially reported the import of 20,788,747 live reptiles. This review suggests that illegal trade activities involve species regulated under CITES, as well as species that are not CITES-regulated but nationally protected in their country of origin and often openly offered for sale in the EU. Further, these case studies demonstrate that regulations and enforcement in several countries are inadequate to prevent the overexploitation of species and to halt illegal trade activities.","collection-title":"Advancing reptile conservation: Addressing knowledge gaps and mitigating key drivers of extinction risk","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2016.05.017","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"103-119","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Trade in live reptiles, its impact on wild populations, and the role of the European market","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320716301987","volume":"204","author":[{"family":"Auliya","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Altherr","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Ariano-Sanchez","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Baard","given":"Ernst H."},{"family":"Brown","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Rafe M."},{"family":"Cantu","given":"Juan-Carlos"},{"family":"Gentile","given":"Gabriele"},{"family":"Gildenhuys","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Henningheim","given":"Evert"},{"family":"Hintzmann","given":"Jürgen"},{"family":"Kanari","given":"Kahoru"},{"family":"Krvavac","given":"Milivoje"},{"family":"Lettink","given":"Marieke"},{"family":"Lippert","given":"Jörg"},{"family":"Luiselli","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Nilson","given":"Göran"},{"family":"Nguyen","given":"Truong Quang"},{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Parham","given":"James F."},{"family":"Pasachnik","given":"Stesha A."},{"family":"Pedrono","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Rauhaus","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Córdova","given":"Danny Rueda"},{"family":"Sanchez","given":"Maria-Elena"},{"family":"Schepp","given":"Ulrich"},{"family":"Schingen","given":"Mona","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Schneeweiss","given":"Norbert"},{"family":"Segniagbeto","given":"Gabriel H."},{"family":"Somaweera","given":"Ruchira"},{"family":"Sy","given":"Emerson Y."},{"family":"Türkozan","given":"Oguz"},{"family":"Vinke","given":"Sabine"},{"family":"Vinke","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Vyas","given":"Raju"},{"family":"Williamson","given":"Stuart"},{"family":"Ziegler","given":"Thomas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12,1]]}}},{"id":7535,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZLLAA8S9"],"itemData":{"id":7535,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Chelonian Conservation and Biology","DOI":"10.2744/CCB-1216.1","ISSN":"1071-8443, 1943-3956","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Chelonian Conservation and Biology","language":"en","page":"167-172","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A Short Review of the International Trade of Wild Tortoises and Freshwater Turtles Across the World and Throughout Two Decades","URL":"http://www.bioone.org/doi/10.2744/CCB-1216.1","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Luiselli","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Starita","given":"Antonio"},{"family":"Carpaneto","given":"Giuseppe Maria"},{"family":"Segniagbeto","given":"Gabriel H."},{"family":"Amori","given":"Giovanni"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, big cats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Kttk2DpH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IUCN, 2014; Morcatty, 2020; UNODC, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(IUCN, 2014; Morcatty, 2020; UNODC, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"Uebb2sCb/3eeU3AYg","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/YJKZXQD7"],"itemData":{"id":6907,"type":"book","event-place":"New York","publisher":"United Nations","publisher-place":"New York","title":"World Wildlife Crime Report 2020: Trafficking in Protected Species","author":[{"family":"UNODC","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/uidJfjSb","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/Y7M334UW"],"itemData":{"id":6905,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13498","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"1525-1535","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Illegal trade in wild cats and its link to Chinese?led development in Central and South America","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Morcatty","given":"Thais Q."},{"family":"Bausch Macedo","given":"Jonathan C."},{"family":"Nekaris","given":"K. Anne?Isola"},{"family":"Ni","given":"Qingyong"},{"family":"Durigan","given":"Carlos C."},{"family":"Svensson","given":"Magdalena S."},{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/uB3cIOWu","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/YZFGHESH"],"itemData":{"id":8501,"type":"report","language":"en","note":"type: dataset\\nDOI: 10.2305/IUCN.UK.2015-2.RLTS.T15955A50659951.en","publisher":"International Union for Conservation of Nature","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Panthera tigris: Goodrich, J., Lynam, A., Miquelle, D., Wibisono, H., Kawanishi, K., Pattanavibool, A., Htun, S., Tempa, T., Karki, J., Jhala, Y. & Karanth, U.: The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2015: e.T15955A50659951","title-short":"Panthera tigris","URL":"http://www.iucnredlist.org/details/15955/0","author":[{"literal":"IUCN"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",4,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, cycads ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RyLvOmny","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Okubamichael, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Okubamichael, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9359,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/83MLLFB5"],"itemData":{"id":9359,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-016-1183-x","ISSN":"0960-3115, 1572-9710","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"2153-2170","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Repeat photography confirms alarming decline in South African cycads","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10531-016-1183-x","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Okubamichael","given":"Desale Y."},{"family":"Jack","given":"Sam"},{"family":"De Wet Bösenberg","given":"J."},{"family":"Timm Hoffman","given":"M."},{"family":"Donaldson","given":"John S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, orchids ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Bch7hK0b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Phelps & Webb, 2015b)","plainCitation":"(Phelps & Webb, 2015b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4609,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LL6D7WLH"],"itemData":{"id":4609,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"296–305","title":"‘Invisible’ Wildlife Trades: Southeast Asia’s Undocumented Illegal Trade in Wild Ornamental Plants","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"},{"family":"Webb","given":"Edward L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, birds ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"r1mXsHsl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SU6WGFBJ"],"itemData":{"id":32354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society","DOI":"10.1093/botlinnean/box083","ISSN":"0024-4074, 1095-8339","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"435-455","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A review of the trade in orchids and its implications for conservation","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/botlinnean/article/186/4/435/4736317","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Boer","given":"Hugo J","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Fay","given":"Michael F"},{"family":"Gale","given":"Stephan W"},{"family":"Gardiner","given":"Lauren M"},{"family":"Gunasekara","given":"Rajasinghe S"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Pankaj"},{"family":"Masters","given":"Susanne"},{"family":"Metusala","given":"Destario"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L"},{"family":"Veldman","given":"Sarina"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Shan"},{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, sea horses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MhTz9Npj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Foster, 2016; T. C. Kuo & Vincent, 2018)","plainCitation":"(S. Foster, 2016; T. C. Kuo & Vincent, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1508,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZJ7Y9AIZ"],"itemData":{"id":1508,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"* In principle, the database generated by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) offers an unparalleled opportunity to analyse trade in species of conservation concern. * The value of the database is assessed in the context of trade in 47 species of seahorse (Hippocampus spp.), all of which are included on CITES Appendix II. This listing requires that all 180 Parties to CITES (member Parties) limit exports to levels that do not damage wild populations, ensure they are obtained legally, and report their trade to CITES. * An evident need for greater universal compliance with CITES reporting requirements was identified. The most glaring problem was a substantial mismatch in species and volumes between export records and import records, indicating that neither dataset is complete nor reliable. * The evaluation also showed that Parties should increase compliance with CITES requirements to record all trade shipments, provide units for exports (e.g. individuals, kilograms) and identify exported taxa to species, perhaps supported by automated checking of entries. * The challenges with the CITES trade database were more evident for the global trade in dried seahorses than the smaller and more easily-tracked trade in live seahorses. Nonetheless, CITES' data from 2004–2011 revealed a seahorse trade involving millions of animals, tens of species, and scores of Parties. * CITES data have also proven invaluable in supporting CITES reviews of how Parties are implementing the Convention for seahorses, and in generating consequent action for their conservation. Copyright © 2014 John Wiley & Sons, Ltd.","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2493","ISSN":"10990755","issue":"1","page":"154–172","title":"Opportunities and challenges for analysis of wildlife trade using CITES data - seahorses as a case study","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Foster","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Wiswedel","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Vincent","given":"Amanda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",2]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":9334,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JJHJNREY"],"itemData":{"id":9334,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000424961300006","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.10.031","ISSN":"0308-597X","page":"48-57","title":"Assessing the changes in international trade of marine fishes under CITES regulations - A case study of seahorses","URL":"://WOS:000424961300006","volume":"88","author":[{"family":"Kuo","given":"T. C."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, corals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3TiJzFqS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Petrossian, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Petrossian, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9352,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IE4YGCFF"],"itemData":{"id":9352,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2020.105305","ISSN":"09645691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"105305","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trends and patterns of imports of legal and illegal live corals into the United States","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0964569120302155","volume":"196","author":[{"family":"Petrossian","given":"Gohar A."},{"family":"Sosnowski","given":"Monique C."},{"family":"Weis","given":"Judith S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, sharks and rays ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8eMS7PNe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Friedman, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Friedman, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31575,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LAT6GN7L"],"itemData":{"id":31575,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"CITES (the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora) aims to ensure that international trade in specimens of wild animals and plants does not threaten their survival. However, measuring the effectiveness and impacts of these trade regulations for commercially exploited aquatic species remains challenging. This study highlights observed or documented changes in elasmobranch fisheries in eight Southeast Asian countries before and after the listing of sharks and rays in CITES’ Appendix II, and the influence of CITES across five pillars or sectors of a “fishery assessment framework” developed especially for this purpose. Fisheries experts reported change was most common in the “governance” (e.g., policy, regulation and compliance) and “fisher(y)” sectors (e.g., structure and effort) of the assessment framework. The smallest change was recorded in “markets” (e.g., structures and prices) and “sociocultural” sectors (e.g., consumption, livelihoods and community awareness). Overall, the study demonstrates a measurable, albeit small, mostly positive influence of CITES in five of eight countries, while noting predominantly negative influences across two, and ongoing challenges for all in maintaining legal trade of these CITES-listed species. The study concludes by offering guidance on future needs: most notably, more effort for long-term collection of fundamental fisher-, stock- and market-related data to inform adaptive management and facilitation of legal trade where it is shown to be sustainable. Furthermore, as many of the shark and ray species under CITES provisions are transboundary stocks, increased support for communication and cooperation among regional fishery stakeholders is an ongoing need.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/faf.12281","ISSN":"1467-2979","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"© 2018 John Wiley & Sons Ltd","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/faf.12281","page":"662-676","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Examining the impact of CITES listing of sharks and rays in Southeast Asian fisheries","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/faf.12281","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Friedman","given":"K."},{"family":"Gabriel","given":"S."},{"family":"Abe","given":"O."},{"family":"Nuruddin","given":"A. Adnan"},{"family":"Ali","given":"A."},{"family":"Hassan","given":"R. Bidin Raja"},{"family":"Cadrin","given":"S. X."},{"family":"Cornish","given":"A."},{"family":"Meulenaer","given":"T. De"},{"family":"Dharmadi","given":""},{"family":"Fahmi","given":""},{"family":"Anh","given":"L. Huu Tuan"},{"family":"Kachelriess","given":"D."},{"family":"Kissol","given":"L."},{"family":"Krajangdara","given":"T."},{"family":"Wahab","given":"A. Rahman"},{"family":"Tanoue","given":"W."},{"family":"Tharith","given":"C."},{"family":"Torres","given":"F."},{"family":"Wanchana","given":"W."},{"family":"Win","given":"S."},{"family":"Yokawa","given":"K."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Y."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and elephant ivory ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wgdsCKYi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Burn, 2011; Huang, 2021; Underwood, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Burn, 2011; Huang, 2021; Underwood, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6001,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GXJZKI3K"],"itemData":{"id":6001,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000294686100017","container-title":"Plos One","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0024165","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"9","title":"Global Trends and Factors Associated with the Illegal Killing of Elephants: A Hierarchical Bayesian Analysis of Carcass Encounter Data","URL":"://WOS:000294686100017","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Burn","given":"R. W."},{"family":"Underwood","given":"F. M."},{"family":"Blanc","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",9]]}}},{"id":6048,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LSUDJEF4"],"itemData":{"id":6048,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EcoHealth","DOI":"10.1007/s10393-020-01511-x","ISSN":"1612-9202, 1612-9210","journalAbbreviation":"EcoHealth","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Mapping the Illegal International Ivory Trading Network to Identify Key Hubs and Smuggling Routes: Illegal International Ivory Trading Network from 1975 to 2017","title-short":"Mapping the Illegal International Ivory Trading Network to Identify Key Hubs and Smuggling Routes","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10393-020-01511-x","author":[{"family":"Huang","given":"Wenyang"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Huiwen"},{"family":"Wei","given":"Yigang"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,2]]}}},{"id":32358,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U6NGPD2G"],"itemData":{"id":32358,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0076539","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e76539","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Dissecting the Illegal Ivory Trade: An Analysis of Ivory Seizures Data","title-short":"Dissecting the Illegal Ivory Trade","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0076539","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Underwood","given":"Fiona M."},{"family":"Burn","given":"Robert W."},{"family":"Milliken","given":"Tom"}],"editor":[{"family":"Montoya","given":"Alejandro Raul Hernandez"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",10,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Almost all species that have high commercial value and where there is continuing demand appear to be subject to some level of ongoing illegal trade.
      Illegal trade is sometimes regarded as an issue of national level implementation and should not therefore reflect on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. However, since it can only be implemented through the actions of contracting Parties, the intention to achieve legal and sustainable trade depends on the capacity of Parties to develop and enforce supporting legislation. In order to prevent illegal trade, the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora requires Parties to exercise administrative control over the trade for an increasing number of species and it has been argued that many developing countries are unable to achieve the expected level of control ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zOZxkiw6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chitov, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Chitov, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2561,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3V87Q4U5"],"itemData":{"id":2561,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Asia Pacific Journal of Environmental Law","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"207–227","title":"International law and criminalizing illegal trade in endangered species (from the Far Eastern perspective","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Chitov","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is compounded by lack of appropriate institutions, corrupt officials, and the involvement of transnational crime networks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VhW2J5Jq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dinerstein, 2007; McCusker, 2006; G. E. Rosen & Smith, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Dinerstein, 2007; McCusker, 2006; G. E. Rosen & Smith, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32379,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X6DNWKSI"],"itemData":{"id":32379,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","ISSN":"1525-3244","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"BioScience","note":"number: 6\\npublisher: American Institute of Biological Sciences","page":"508-514","title":"The fate of wild tigers","volume":"57","author":[{"family":"Dinerstein","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Loucks","given":"Colby"},{"family":"Wikramanayake","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Ginsberg","given":"Joshua"},{"family":"Sanderson","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Seidensticker","given":"John"},{"family":"Forrest","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Bryja","given":"Gosia"},{"family":"Heydlauff","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Klenzendorf","given":"Sybille"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":32383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ELQ6WR4K"],"itemData":{"id":32383,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends & Issues in Crime and Criminal Justice","language":"en","page":"1–6","title":"Transnational crime in the Pacific Islands: real or apparent danger","volume":"308","author":[{"family":"McCusker","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":32345,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S6CN6Z8K"],"itemData":{"id":32345,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The global trade in illegal wildlife is a multi-billion dollar industry that threatens biodiversity and acts as a potential avenue for invasive species and disease spread. Despite the broad-sweeping implications of illegal wildlife sales, scientists have yet to describe the scope and scale of the trade. Here, we provide the most thorough and current description of the illegal wildlife trade using 12 years of seizure records compiled by TRAFFIC, the wildlife trade monitoring network. These records comprise 967 seizures including massive quantities of ivory, tiger skins, live reptiles, and other endangered wildlife and wildlife products. Most seizures originate in Southeast Asia, a recently identified hotspot for future emerging infectious diseases. To date, regulation and enforcement have been insufficient to effectively control the global trade in illegal wildlife at national and international scales. Effective control will require a multi-pronged approach including community-scale education and empowering local people to value wildlife, coordinated international regulation, and a greater allocation of national resources to on-the-ground enforcement.","container-title":"EcoHealth","DOI":"10.1007/s10393-010-0317-y","ISSN":"1612-9210","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"EcoHealth","language":"en","page":"24-32","source":"Springer Link","title":"Summarizing the Evidence on the International Trade in Illegal Wildlife","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10393-010-0317-y","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Rosen","given":"Gail Emilia"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Katherine F."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",8,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      It is not possible here to assess the capacity of Parties to implement the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora but the evidence from other forms of illicit trade indicates that it requires considerable capacity and resources to counter illicit activities spanning the supply, transshipment and demand components of trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CMghhxOW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Felbab-Brown, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Felbab-Brown, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32328,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NZJZJDC3"],"itemData":{"id":32328,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"The planet is currently experiencing alarming levels of species loss caused in large part by intensified poaching and wildlife trafficking driven by expanding demand, for medicines, for food, and for trophies. Affecting many more species than just the iconic elephants, rhinos, and tigers, the rate of extinction is now as much as 1000 times the historical average and the worst since the dinosaurs died out 65 million years ago. In addition to causing irretrievable biodiversity loss, wildlife trafficking also poses serious threats to public health, potentially triggering a global pandemic. The Extinction Market explores the causes, means, and consequences of poaching and wildlife trafficking, with a view to finding ways of suppressing them. Vanda Felbab-Brown travelled to the markets of Latin America, South and South East Asia, and eastern and southern Africa, to evaluate the effectiveness of various tools, including bans on legal trade, law enforcement, and interdiction; allowing legal supply from hunting or farming; alternative livelihoods; anti- money-laundering efforts; and demand reduction strategies. This is an urgent book offering meaningful solutions to one of the world's most pressing crises.\\"--Provided by publisher","call-number":"HV6410 .F45 2017","event-place":"Oxford ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-19-085511-6","note":"OCLC: ocn986237039","number-of-pages":"406","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"Oxford ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The extinction market: wildlife trafficking and how to counter it","title-short":"The extinction market","author":[{"family":"Felbab-Brown","given":"Vanda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There is consistent evidence that unsustainable and illegal trade in wild species is a complex socio-ecological problem ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FkOl1Tgt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Challender, 2015; Phelps, 2016; D. L. Roberts & Hinsley, 2020; Symes, 2018; \\\\uc0\\\\u8216{}t Sas-Rolfes, 2019b; TRAFFIC, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Challender, 2015; Phelps, 2016; D. L. Roberts & Hinsley, 2020; Symes, 2018; ‘t Sas-Rolfes, 2019b; TRAFFIC, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4793,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LXIIWJME"],"itemData":{"id":4793,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2014.11.010.","issue":"January","language":"en","page":"129– 48","title":"Towards Informed and Multi- Faceted Wildlife Trade Interventions","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gecco.2014.11.010.","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Challender","given":"D.W.S."},{"family":"Harrop","given":"S.R."},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"D.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":7542,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q2S6CFPQ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F8Y4UHLI"],"itemData":{"id":7542,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","DOI":"10.1002/fee.1325","ISSN":"15409295","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Front Ecol Environ","language":"en","page":"479-489","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Tools and terms for understanding illegal wildlife trade","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/fee.1325","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"},{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Webb","given":"Edward L"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",11]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":7541,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4CLZ6KAW","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XXNF3DXK"],"itemData":{"id":7541,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Initiatives that aim to regulate the international wildlife trade must take into account its multiple and often complex dimensions in order to be effective. To do this, it is essential to understand the interactions between three of the key dimensions of the wildlife trade: (1) taxonomic unit, (2) geographic origin, and (3) product form and transformation. We propose a framework to provide a structured approach to defining the complexities of the wildlife trade, based on Rabinowitz’s seven forms of rarity. We demonstrate the complexities and how they apply to our framework using two contrasting examples: the trade in elephant ivory, and the horticultural orchid trade. Further we use the framework to map different traceability solutions. To be as efficient as possible, efforts to tackle the illegal and unsustainable utilisation of wildlife should take a more structured approach. This framework identifies challenges that current initiatives may face, how they may interact and provides a structure for designing future interventions.","container-title":"Tropical Conservation Science","DOI":"10.1177/1940082920947023","ISSN":"1940-0829, 1940-0829","journalAbbreviation":"Tropical Conservation Science","language":"en","page":"194008292094702","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Seven Forms of Challenges in the Wildlife Trade","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1940082920947023","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L."},{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1]]}}},{"id":32549,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6VE22I2Z"],"itemData":{"id":32549,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable trade in wildlife products both legally and illegally is a leading cause of population declines and increased extinction risk in commercially valuable species. However due to the clandestine nature of illegal trade and paucity of overarching studies of legal trade our understanding on international trade networks is patchy. We develop a gravity–underreporting modelling framework to analyse and compare: (i) data on the legal trade in mammalian, avian and reptilian products from recorded by The Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and (ii) to data on the seizures of illegal products entering the USA between 2004 and 2013. We find substantial differences in the factors driving legal trade for the 3 taxonomic groups considered, indicating different drivers for different product markets. Illegal imports for all groups were associated with increasing exporter GDP. We found higher probabilities of underreporting for avian and reptile products, and in general central Africa, central Asia, Eastern Europe and Pacific Island states showed higher underreporting than other regions, indicating the existence of complex trade networks and the potential for the laundering of illegal products through legal markets. Our results show the important regional and economic trends driving wildlife trade. Our new modelling framework can also help illuminate previously unseen aspects of illegal and legal wildlife trade, which can help with the implementation of interventions to curb the impact of trade on wild populations.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2017.11.007","ISSN":"00063207","page":"268–276","title":"The gravity of wildlife trade","volume":"218","author":[{"family":"Symes","given":"William S."},{"family":"McGrath","given":"Francesca L."},{"family":"Rao","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L. Roman"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":33958,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2XMEHQUI"],"itemData":{"id":33958,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Illegal wildlife trade (IWT) has increased in profile in recent years as a global policy issue, largely because of its association with declines in prominent internationally trafficked species. In this review, we explore the scale of IWT, associated threats to biodiversity, and appropriate responses to these threats. We discuss the historical development of IWT research and highlight the uncertainties that plague the evidence base, emphasizing the need for more systematic approaches to addressing evidence gaps in a way that minimizes the risk of unethical or counterproductive outcomes for wildlife and people. We highlight the need for evaluating interventions in order to learn, and the importance of sharing datasets and lessons learned. A more collaborative approach to linking IWT research, practice, and policy would better align public policy discourse and action with research evidence. This in turn would enable more effective policy making that contributes to reducing the threat to biodiversity that IWT represents.","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033253","ISSN":"1543-5938, 1545-2050","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Environ. Resour.","language":"en","page":"201-228","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Illegal Wildlife Trade: Scale, Processes, and Governance","title-short":"Illegal Wildlife Trade","URL":"https://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033253","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"‘t Sas-Rolfes","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Challender","given":"Daniel W.S."},{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Diogo"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,17]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":857,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TIZMJWC9"],"itemData":{"id":857,"type":"report","abstract":"Worldwide there is a high, and in many cases growing demand for wild plants and animals and products made from them. Wild species are used as the source of a wide variety of goods, including foods, medicines, pets, display, fashion and cultural items, industrial resins and extracts, and household items. Use may be local to the resource itself, e.g. hunting for meat for direct consumption, or take place many thousands of miles away, the wildlife products passing along a complex processing and trade chain from harvester to end-consumer. South-east Asia is both a centre for the consumption of wildlife products, and also a key supplier of wildlife products to the world. Cambodia, Indonesia, Lao PDR and Vietnam are among the south-east Asian countries that act as major sources of wildlife in trade, the trade involving a wide variety of native species, which, in many cases, are declining as a result of unsustainable, and often illegal, harvest. The study aimed to generate findings and recommendations that will be useful to governments, nongovernmental organizations, donors, and others in considering how interventions to reduce illegal and unsustainable wildlife trade might be applied more effectively in future.","language":"en","number":"46791","page":"1-120","publisher":"The World Bank","source":"documents.worldbank.org","title":"What's driving the wildlife trade? A review of expert opinion on economic and social drivers of the wildlife trade and trade control efforts in Cambodia, Indonesia, Lao PDR, and Vietnam","title-short":"What's driving the wildlife trade?","URL":"http://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/608621468139780146/Whats-driving-the-wildlife-trade-A-review-of-expert-opinion-on-economic-and-social-drivers-of-the-wildlife-trade-and-trade-control-efforts-in-Cambodia-Indonesia-Lao-PDR-and-Vietnam","author":[{"family":"TRAFFIC","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",10,1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and that the effectiveness of any measures to address illegal trade is highly contingent on local context ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SEDfkFvL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Felbab-Brown, 2017; Symes, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Felbab-Brown, 2017; Symes, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32328,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NZJZJDC3"],"itemData":{"id":32328,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"The planet is currently experiencing alarming levels of species loss caused in large part by intensified poaching and wildlife trafficking driven by expanding demand, for medicines, for food, and for trophies. Affecting many more species than just the iconic elephants, rhinos, and tigers, the rate of extinction is now as much as 1000 times the historical average and the worst since the dinosaurs died out 65 million years ago. In addition to causing irretrievable biodiversity loss, wildlife trafficking also poses serious threats to public health, potentially triggering a global pandemic. The Extinction Market explores the causes, means, and consequences of poaching and wildlife trafficking, with a view to finding ways of suppressing them. Vanda Felbab-Brown travelled to the markets of Latin America, South and South East Asia, and eastern and southern Africa, to evaluate the effectiveness of various tools, including bans on legal trade, law enforcement, and interdiction; allowing legal supply from hunting or farming; alternative livelihoods; anti- money-laundering efforts; and demand reduction strategies. This is an urgent book offering meaningful solutions to one of the world's most pressing crises.\\"--Provided by publisher","call-number":"HV6410 .F45 2017","event-place":"Oxford ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-19-085511-6","note":"OCLC: ocn986237039","number-of-pages":"406","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"Oxford ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"The extinction market: wildlife trafficking and how to counter it","title-short":"The extinction market","author":[{"family":"Felbab-Brown","given":"Vanda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":32549,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6VE22I2Z"],"itemData":{"id":32549,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable trade in wildlife products both legally and illegally is a leading cause of population declines and increased extinction risk in commercially valuable species. However due to the clandestine nature of illegal trade and paucity of overarching studies of legal trade our understanding on international trade networks is patchy. We develop a gravity–underreporting modelling framework to analyse and compare: (i) data on the legal trade in mammalian, avian and reptilian products from recorded by The Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and (ii) to data on the seizures of illegal products entering the USA between 2004 and 2013. We find substantial differences in the factors driving legal trade for the 3 taxonomic groups considered, indicating different drivers for different product markets. Illegal imports for all groups were associated with increasing exporter GDP. We found higher probabilities of underreporting for avian and reptile products, and in general central Africa, central Asia, Eastern Europe and Pacific Island states showed higher underreporting than other regions, indicating the existence of complex trade networks and the potential for the laundering of illegal products through legal markets. Our results show the important regional and economic trends driving wildlife trade. Our new modelling framework can also help illuminate previously unseen aspects of illegal and legal wildlife trade, which can help with the implementation of interventions to curb the impact of trade on wild populations.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2017.11.007","ISSN":"00063207","page":"268–276","title":"The gravity of wildlife trade","volume":"218","author":[{"family":"Symes","given":"William S."},{"family":"McGrath","given":"Francesca L."},{"family":"Rao","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L. Roman"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The illicit economy for trade in wild species is poorly understood ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rNUbnWBr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Symes, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Symes, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32549,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6VE22I2Z"],"itemData":{"id":32549,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable trade in wildlife products both legally and illegally is a leading cause of population declines and increased extinction risk in commercially valuable species. However due to the clandestine nature of illegal trade and paucity of overarching studies of legal trade our understanding on international trade networks is patchy. We develop a gravity–underreporting modelling framework to analyse and compare: (i) data on the legal trade in mammalian, avian and reptilian products from recorded by The Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and (ii) to data on the seizures of illegal products entering the USA between 2004 and 2013. We find substantial differences in the factors driving legal trade for the 3 taxonomic groups considered, indicating different drivers for different product markets. Illegal imports for all groups were associated with increasing exporter GDP. We found higher probabilities of underreporting for avian and reptile products, and in general central Africa, central Asia, Eastern Europe and Pacific Island states showed higher underreporting than other regions, indicating the existence of complex trade networks and the potential for the laundering of illegal products through legal markets. Our results show the important regional and economic trends driving wildlife trade. Our new modelling framework can also help illuminate previously unseen aspects of illegal and legal wildlife trade, which can help with the implementation of interventions to curb the impact of trade on wild populations.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2017.11.007","ISSN":"00063207","page":"268–276","title":"The gravity of wildlife trade","volume":"218","author":[{"family":"Symes","given":"William S."},{"family":"McGrath","given":"Francesca L."},{"family":"Rao","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L. Roman"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and often poorly policed, except for a few charismatic species such as rhino and elephant ivory. 
      
Unintended outcomes of Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora
In addition to the evidence assessed above, two unintended consequences of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listing are briefly assessed. 
      Leakage and displacement: Listing a species can reduce trade in that species to sustainable levels but displace the trade to other similar species, which may initially be more abundant, or result in leakage from one jurisdiction to another where there are less stringent controls. Displacement and/or leakage has been observed in a number of cases: trade in eels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PU5oVfcK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nijman, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Nijman, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9349,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HC4X89LF"],"itemData":{"id":9349,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000356745700005","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2015.04.006","ISSN":"0308-597X","page":"36-41","title":"CITES-listings, EU eel trade bans and the increase of export of tropical eels out of Indonesia","URL":"://WOS:000356745700005","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, where bans in Europe resulted in increased harvest and trade from Indonesia; large cats, where evidence indicates that restrictions on trade in tiger products has led to increased harvesting and trade in other species such as leopard, jaguar and lion ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"smvdqogg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Morcatty, 2020; UNODC, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(Morcatty, 2020; UNODC, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"Uebb2sCb/3eeU3AYg","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/YJKZXQD7"],"itemData":{"id":6907,"type":"book","event-place":"New York","publisher":"United Nations","publisher-place":"New York","title":"World Wildlife Crime Report 2020: Trafficking in Protected Species","author":[{"family":"UNODC","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/uidJfjSb","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/5876030/items/Y7M334UW"],"itemData":{"id":6905,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13498","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"1525-1535","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Illegal trade in wild cats and its link to Chinese?led development in Central and South America","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Morcatty","given":"Thais Q."},{"family":"Bausch Macedo","given":"Jonathan C."},{"family":"Nekaris","given":"K. Anne?Isola"},{"family":"Ni","given":"Qingyong"},{"family":"Durigan","given":"Carlos C."},{"family":"Svensson","given":"Magdalena S."},{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} but noting that trade in other large cats is also driven by markets not related to substitution for products derived from tigers; trade in manta rays where harvest may be displaced to areas with lower levels of enforcement ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BJwjbQQQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Friedman, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Friedman, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31575,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LAT6GN7L"],"itemData":{"id":31575,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"CITES (the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora) aims to ensure that international trade in specimens of wild animals and plants does not threaten their survival. However, measuring the effectiveness and impacts of these trade regulations for commercially exploited aquatic species remains challenging. This study highlights observed or documented changes in elasmobranch fisheries in eight Southeast Asian countries before and after the listing of sharks and rays in CITES’ Appendix II, and the influence of CITES across five pillars or sectors of a “fishery assessment framework” developed especially for this purpose. Fisheries experts reported change was most common in the “governance” (e.g., policy, regulation and compliance) and “fisher(y)” sectors (e.g., structure and effort) of the assessment framework. The smallest change was recorded in “markets” (e.g., structures and prices) and “sociocultural” sectors (e.g., consumption, livelihoods and community awareness). Overall, the study demonstrates a measurable, albeit small, mostly positive influence of CITES in five of eight countries, while noting predominantly negative influences across two, and ongoing challenges for all in maintaining legal trade of these CITES-listed species. The study concludes by offering guidance on future needs: most notably, more effort for long-term collection of fundamental fisher-, stock- and market-related data to inform adaptive management and facilitation of legal trade where it is shown to be sustainable. Furthermore, as many of the shark and ray species under CITES provisions are transboundary stocks, increased support for communication and cooperation among regional fishery stakeholders is an ongoing need.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/faf.12281","ISSN":"1467-2979","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"© 2018 John Wiley & Sons Ltd","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/faf.12281","page":"662-676","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Examining the impact of CITES listing of sharks and rays in Southeast Asian fisheries","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/faf.12281","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Friedman","given":"K."},{"family":"Gabriel","given":"S."},{"family":"Abe","given":"O."},{"family":"Nuruddin","given":"A. Adnan"},{"family":"Ali","given":"A."},{"family":"Hassan","given":"R. Bidin Raja"},{"family":"Cadrin","given":"S. X."},{"family":"Cornish","given":"A."},{"family":"Meulenaer","given":"T. De"},{"family":"Dharmadi","given":""},{"family":"Fahmi","given":""},{"family":"Anh","given":"L. Huu Tuan"},{"family":"Kachelriess","given":"D."},{"family":"Kissol","given":"L."},{"family":"Krajangdara","given":"T."},{"family":"Wahab","given":"A. Rahman"},{"family":"Tanoue","given":"W."},{"family":"Tharith","given":"C."},{"family":"Torres","given":"F."},{"family":"Wanchana","given":"W."},{"family":"Win","given":"S."},{"family":"Yokawa","given":"K."},{"family":"Ye","given":"Y."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; and a decline in trade in tortoises and freshwater turtles from Asia associated with increased trade from the Nearctic region ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dyrzN88c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Luiselli, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Luiselli, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7535,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZLLAA8S9"],"itemData":{"id":7535,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Chelonian Conservation and Biology","DOI":"10.2744/CCB-1216.1","ISSN":"1071-8443, 1943-3956","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Chelonian Conservation and Biology","language":"en","page":"167-172","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A Short Review of the International Trade of Wild Tortoises and Freshwater Turtles Across the World and Throughout Two Decades","URL":"http://www.bioone.org/doi/10.2744/CCB-1216.1","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Luiselli","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Starita","given":"Antonio"},{"family":"Carpaneto","given":"Giuseppe Maria"},{"family":"Segniagbeto","given":"Gabriel H."},{"family":"Amori","given":"Giovanni"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The shift to other threatened species or less regulated regions requires an adaptive response to changing trade dynamics.
      Increased demand: the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora decisions and the process used to list species have been identified as possible drivers of demand and trade. Proposals to list species on the Appendices are available at least 150 days before any decisions are taken but the listing process can take considerably longer if proposals are not accepted when they are first put forward. There is some evidence for increased trade linked to the listing process, in which harvesters and traders either acquire or offload stocks prior to listing and before restrictions come into place. This has been highlighted for earless monitor lizards, Lanthanotus borneensis  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oaWKVzle","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Janssen & Krishnasamy, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Janssen & Krishnasamy, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2453,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V3P9TCH8"],"itemData":{"id":2453,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2018.e00464","language":"en","title":"Left hung out to dry: How inadequate international protection can fuel trade in endemic species—The case of the earless monitor","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gecco.2018.e00464","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Janssen","given":"J."},{"family":"Krishnasamy","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and lion bone ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"s4M0nahQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. L. Williams, 2017)","plainCitation":"(V. L. Williams, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31106,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P2ZFQ947"],"itemData":{"id":31106,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0185996","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e0185996","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A roaring trade? The legal trade in Panthera leo bones from Africa to East-Southeast Asia","title-short":"A roaring trade?","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0185996","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Williams","given":"V.L."},{"family":"Loveridge","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Newton","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"D.W."}],"editor":[{"family":"Gratwicke","given":"B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",10,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The phenomenon of increased trade linked to impending restrictions has also been recorded for national level regulations that are independent of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, e.g. A partial ban on the harvest of African cherry (Prunus africana), imposed by the government of Cameroon in 1991, resulted in the opportunistic and destructive harvest of twice the annual average amount of bark ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"h9tsghlm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cunningham, Anthony B. & Mbenkum, F. T., 1993)","plainCitation":"(Cunningham, Anthony B. & Mbenkum, F. T., 1993)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":233444,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K9WG64PA"],"itemData":{"id":233444,"type":"report","collection-title":"People and plants working paper series","title":"Sustainability of harvesting Prunus africana bark in Cameroon: a medicinal plant in international trade","URL":"https://unesdoc.unesco.org/ark:/48223/pf0000098761","author":[{"literal":"Cunningham, Anthony B."},{"literal":"Mbenkum, F. T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The limited evidence indicates that these are short term spikes associated with the listing process although they may have negative impacts on affected species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6y6el93c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rivalan, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Rivalan, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34380,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ANJDP2ZQ"],"itemData":{"id":34380,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Proactive management of trade in endangered wildlife makes more sense than last-minute bans that can themselves increase trading activity, argue Philippe Rivalan and his co-authors.","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/447529a","ISSN":"1476-4687","language":"en","license":"2007 Nature Publishing Group","page":"529-530","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Can bans stimulate wildlife trade?","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/447529a","volume":"447","author":[{"family":"Rivalan","given":"Philippe"},{"family":"Delmas","given":"Virginie"},{"family":"Angulo","given":"Elena"},{"family":"Bull","given":"Leigh S."},{"family":"Hall","given":"Richard J."},{"family":"Courchamp","given":"Franck"},{"family":"Rosser","given":"Alison M."},{"family":"Leader-Williams","given":"Nigel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",5,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      A more contentious question is whether the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listing decisions may act as a driver of increased demand and illegal trade. One argument is that when a species is moved from Appendix I to Appendix II allowing legal trade, this stimulates demand (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BGEQSugW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. I. Orenstein, 2013)","plainCitation":"(R. I. Orenstein, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32317,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FQFSEMVT"],"itemData":{"id":32317,"type":"book","call-number":"SK36.7 .O74 2013","event-place":"Richmond Hill, Ontario ; Buffalo, New York","ISBN":"978-1-77085-227-3","note":"OCLC: ocn829421978","number-of-pages":"216","publisher":"Firefly Books","publisher-place":"Richmond Hill, Ontario ; Buffalo, New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Ivory, horn and blood: behind the elephant and rhinoceros poaching crisis","title-short":"Ivory, horn and blood","author":[{"family":"Orenstein","given":"Ronald I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, this demand may not always be met through legal means and this encourages illegal trade and laundering of illegally sourced specimens into legal trade networks. The counter argument is that the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora restrictions on the supply side of trade limit the availability of legally sourced specimens but fail to satisfy an existing demand, resulting in illicit trade. The literature search provided limited evidence relating to this question. The studies focused primarily on issues such as the legalization of trade in rhino horn ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gL2gNeKZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biggs, 2013; Crookes & Blignaut, 2015; Eikelboom, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Biggs, 2013; Crookes & Blignaut, 2015; Eikelboom, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":862,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDB5KSPI"],"itemData":{"id":862,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"A trade ban limits supply, therefore raising prices and driving black market poaching.\\nA trade ban limits supply, therefore raising prices and driving black market poaching.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1229998","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6123","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2013, American Association for the Advancement of Science","note":"PMID: 23449582","page":"1038-1039","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"Legal Trade of Africa's Rhino Horns","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/339/6123/1038","volume":"339","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Courchamp","given":"Franck"},{"family":"Martin","given":"Rowan"},{"family":"Possingham","given":"Hugh P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",3,1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":7552,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8C6DB2KG"],"itemData":{"id":7552,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2015.08.001","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"11-18","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Debunking the myth that a legal trade will solve the rhino horn crisis: A system dynamics model for market demand","title-short":"Debunking the myth that a legal trade will solve the rhino horn crisis","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1617138115300108","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Crookes","given":"Douglas J."},{"family":"Blignaut","given":"James N."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",11]]}}},{"id":7551,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J6UGVZQC"],"itemData":{"id":7551,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2020.e01145","ISSN":"23519894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e01145","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Will legal international rhino horn trade save wild rhino populations?","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2351989420306867","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Eikelboom","given":"Jasper A.J."},{"family":"Nuijten","given":"Rascha J.M."},{"family":"Wang","given":"Yingying X.G."},{"family":"Schroder","given":"Bradley"},{"family":"Heitkönig","given":"Ignas M.A."},{"family":"Mooij","given":"Wolf M."},{"family":"Langevelde","given":"Frank","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Prins","given":"Herbert H.T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and bans on trade in elephant ivory ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CJqYc20X","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Conrad, 2012a; Kurohata, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Conrad, 2012a; Kurohata, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":854,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BBZXGWZB"],"itemData":{"id":854,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tropical Conservation Science","DOI":"10.1177/194008291200500302","ISSN":"1940-0829, 1940-0829","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Tropical Conservation Science","language":"en","page":"245-254","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trade Bans: A Perfect Storm for Poaching?","title-short":"Trade Bans","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/194008291200500302","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Conrad","given":"Kirsten"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",9]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":6053,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZQ4A4CJC"],"itemData":{"id":6053,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Economics and Policy Studies","DOI":"10.1007/s10018-019-00261-7","ISSN":"1432-847X, 1867-383X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Environ Econ Policy Stud","language":"en","page":"383-403","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effect of the CITES trade ban on preferences for ivory in Japan","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10018-019-00261-7","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Kurohata","given":"Mika"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (Orenstein 2013), but also includes analyses of laundering of wild sourced specimens through captive breeding operations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dDLmRhiQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lyons & Natusch, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Lyons & Natusch, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":716,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/28PSAP4U"],"itemData":{"id":716,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife breeding farms have been promoted to aid biodiversity conservation by alleviating the pressure of harvest on wild populations. There is, however, growing concern that many breeding farms are being used to launder illegally caught wildlife. Surveys of wildlife traders in the Indonesian provinces of Maluku, West Papua and Papua were conducted between August 2009 and April 2011 to assess the trade of the green python (Morelia viridis), the species currently exported in the largest numbers from Indonesia declared as captive-bred. In total, 4227 illegally collected wild green pythons were recorded during surveys and high levels of harvest were found to have depleted and skewed the demographics of some island populations. Snakes were traced from their point of capture to breeding farms in Jakarta where they are to be exported for the pet trade, confirming the reports of wildlife laundering. Extrapolation of monthly collection estimates provided by traders revealed that at least 5337 green pythons are collected each year, suggesting that at least 80% of the green pythons exported from Indonesia annually are illegally wild-caught. The results of examination of 139 eggshells from five python species suggest that reptilian eggshells may be used as proof of provenance for each individual reptile exported. This method, in addition to the evidence that breeding farms play a significant role in the illegal exploitation of wildlife, allows conservation managers to begin to adequately monitor, regulate and determine the role of breeding farms in the conservation of wild populations.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2011.10.002","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"3073-3081","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Wildlife laundering through breeding farms: Illegal harvest, population declines and a means of regulating the trade of green pythons (Morelia viridis) from Indonesia","title-short":"Wildlife laundering through breeding farms","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320711003685","volume":"144","author":[{"family":"Lyons","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel J. D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and legal quotas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"J3biglgQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Daut, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Daut, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6020,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ELS6XEHL"],"itemData":{"id":6020,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2015.06.005","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"44-53","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Illegal domestic bird trade and the role of export quotas in Peru","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S161713811530008X","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Daut","given":"Elizabeth F."},{"family":"Brightsmith","given":"Donald J."},{"family":"Mendoza","given":"A. Patricia"},{"family":"Puhakka","given":"Liisa"},{"family":"Peterson","given":"Markus J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The evidence relating to key factors in these counter arguments, such as price elasticity, consumer behavior, and the dynamics of legal and illegal trade networks is limited and often contradictory. For example, studies of ivory trade in Japan showed that initial bans resulted in increased prices but did not reduce demand whereas later interventions to raise awareness of the impact on elephant populations resulted in decreased demand ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a9Y5tfHz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kurohata, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Kurohata, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6053,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZQ4A4CJC"],"itemData":{"id":6053,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Economics and Policy Studies","DOI":"10.1007/s10018-019-00261-7","ISSN":"1432-847X, 1867-383X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Environ Econ Policy Stud","language":"en","page":"383-403","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effect of the CITES trade ban on preferences for ivory in Japan","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10018-019-00261-7","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Kurohata","given":"Mika"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Studies of bushmeat trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"75mV6Tuh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. McNamara, 2016)","plainCitation":"(J. McNamara, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7538,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2PEFTXAY"],"itemData":{"id":7538,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0162972","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e0162972","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Characterising Wildlife Trade Market Supply-Demand Dynamics","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0162972","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"McNamara","given":"J."},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"M."},{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"G."},{"family":"Alexander","given":"J. S."},{"family":"Ntiamoa-Baidu","given":"Y."},{"family":"Brenya","given":"A."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E. J."}],"editor":[{"family":"Romanach","given":"Stephanie S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",9,15]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"},  succulent plants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pP9Hx2ta","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Margulies, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Margulies, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7539,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HI6KVTPB"],"itemData":{"id":7539,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.3389/fevo.2020.604921","ISSN":"2296-701X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Ecol. Evol.","page":"604921","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Korean ‘Housewives’ and ‘Hipsters’ Are Not Driving a New Illicit Plant Trade: Complicating Consumer Motivations Behind an Emergent Wildlife Trade in Dudleya farinosa","title-short":"Korean ‘Housewives’ and ‘Hipsters’ Are Not Driving a New Illicit Plant Trade","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fevo.2020.604921/full","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Margulies","given":"Jared D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,23]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and more general trade in wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"voyg1xqN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Symes, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Symes, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32549,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6VE22I2Z"],"itemData":{"id":32549,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable trade in wildlife products both legally and illegally is a leading cause of population declines and increased extinction risk in commercially valuable species. However due to the clandestine nature of illegal trade and paucity of overarching studies of legal trade our understanding on international trade networks is patchy. We develop a gravity–underreporting modelling framework to analyse and compare: (i) data on the legal trade in mammalian, avian and reptilian products from recorded by The Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and (ii) to data on the seizures of illegal products entering the USA between 2004 and 2013. We find substantial differences in the factors driving legal trade for the 3 taxonomic groups considered, indicating different drivers for different product markets. Illegal imports for all groups were associated with increasing exporter GDP. We found higher probabilities of underreporting for avian and reptile products, and in general central Africa, central Asia, Eastern Europe and Pacific Island states showed higher underreporting than other regions, indicating the existence of complex trade networks and the potential for the laundering of illegal products through legal markets. Our results show the important regional and economic trends driving wildlife trade. Our new modelling framework can also help illuminate previously unseen aspects of illegal and legal wildlife trade, which can help with the implementation of interventions to curb the impact of trade on wild populations.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2017.11.007","ISSN":"00063207","page":"268–276","title":"The gravity of wildlife trade","volume":"218","author":[{"family":"Symes","given":"William S."},{"family":"McGrath","given":"Francesca L."},{"family":"Rao","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L. Roman"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} show how the drivers of trade are not straight forward and differ between species and regions. The evidence is insufficient to provide any clear finding and it remains unresolved. Given the importance of this issue for the governance of use of wild species, this is an area where further research is urgently required.
      In summary, the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora has been an important instrument for driving global action to ensure more sustainable use of species that are threatened by international trade and to strengthen institutions and tools to achieve this. It has been less successful when measured against outcomes for many species affected by unsustainable levels of use. The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora decisions may not always achieve the desired outcomes because significant aspects of trade may occur outside of the control oft, international trade is not the only driver affecting many species, and the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora decisions do not address the underlying drivers of unsustainable and/or illegal trade both from the supply and demand side of trade. Theoretical analyses of use systems have concluded that sustainable outcomes are more likely when the social and biological/ecological components are understood and included in the decision-making process ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fRwFRHPX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ostrom, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Ostrom, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":36,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2NACCRB4"],"itemData":{"id":36,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1172133","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5939","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"419-422","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A General Framework for Analyzing Sustainability of Social-Ecological Systems","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1172133","volume":"325","author":[{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",7,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. One aspect of the social dimension has dominated discussions at the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, namely the impact of decisions on livelihoods and the proposal to strengthen input from communities in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listing process to address the impacts of decisions on livelihoods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xeLUDF3T","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cooney, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Cooney, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32612,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2S72VUJ2"],"itemData":{"id":32612,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.3389/fevo.2021.631556.","language":"en","page":"631556","title":"Think Before You Act: Improving the Conservation Outcomes of CITES Listing Decisions","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"},{"family":"Challender","given":"Dan"},{"family":"Broad","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These proposals have had limited support due to the view that the Convention should focus on the biological status of the affected species as the primary factor in decisions to list or delist taxa in the Appendices. Nevertheless, there is a growing body of evidence relating to social aspects of trade in wild species, comprising economics, behavior change, the structure of legal and illicit markets, the role of communities in promoting sustainable use, and social drivers which could be used to ensure more durable outcomes for the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora decisions and reduce unsustainable levels of trade.
      
4.2.2.2.2. Trans-boundary conventions/agreements 
There are a variety of regional level institutions that facilitate and support sustainable use that predate or are not explicitly tied to the Convention on Biological Diversity. For example, the Agreement on the Conservation of Polar Bears was signed May 26, 1976, in an effort to protect the species through a coordinated approach by the five polar bear range states (Union of Soviet Socialist Republics (now Russia), Norway, Greenland (Denmark), the United States of America, Canada). This agreement coupled with its recognition and fit with the rights, customary laws and knowledge systems of success is most clearly evidenced to scientists by the fact that the majority bear sub-populations are stable and increasing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cWDelaWT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Government of Canada, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Government of Canada, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32573,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G4RGP8F6"],"itemData":{"id":32573,"type":"report","event-place":"Ottawa","genre":"COSEWIC Assessment Report","publisher":"Government of Canada","publisher-place":"Ottawa","title":"Polar Bear (Ursus maritimus): COSEWIC assessment and status report 2018","URL":"https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/species-risk-public-registry/cosewic-assessments-status-reports/polar-bear-2018.html#toc8","author":[{"literal":"Government of Canada"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although there is growing concern that melting sea ice in the north will lead to declining bear numbers, the global population of polar bears, estimated between 22,000 and 31,000 animals with more evidence from science and Inuit knowledge that the population is growing or stable, rather than declining. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"b6iol5LE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Government of Canada, 2019; World Wildlife Fund, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Government of Canada, 2019; World Wildlife Fund, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32573,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G4RGP8F6"],"itemData":{"id":32573,"type":"report","event-place":"Ottawa","genre":"COSEWIC Assessment Report","publisher":"Government of Canada","publisher-place":"Ottawa","title":"Polar Bear (Ursus maritimus): COSEWIC assessment and status report 2018","URL":"https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/species-risk-public-registry/cosewic-assessments-status-reports/polar-bear-2018.html#toc8","author":[{"literal":"Government of Canada"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":881,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8BSQNNN3"],"itemData":{"id":881,"type":"report","event-place":"Solna, Sweden","publisher":"World Wildlife Fund","publisher-place":"Solna, Sweden","title":"Polar Bears and the Numbers Game","URL":"https://arcticwwf.org/newsroom/stories/polar-bears-and-the-numbers-game/","author":[{"literal":"World Wildlife Fund"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Only the Hudson Bay and southern Beaufort subpopulations are listed by the Government of Canada as “likely declined” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"S0wdgqLb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Government of Canada, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Government of Canada, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32573,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G4RGP8F6"],"itemData":{"id":32573,"type":"report","event-place":"Ottawa","genre":"COSEWIC Assessment Report","publisher":"Government of Canada","publisher-place":"Ottawa","title":"Polar Bear (Ursus maritimus): COSEWIC assessment and status report 2018","URL":"https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/species-risk-public-registry/cosewic-assessments-status-reports/polar-bear-2018.html#toc8","author":[{"literal":"Government of Canada"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; however, traditional knowledge of Inuit and Inuvialuit peoples in these regions is not consistent with these conclusions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jHGqkniF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Inuvialuit Joint Secretariat, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Inuvialuit Joint Secretariat, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32563,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YDHNMBY4"],"itemData":{"id":32563,"type":"report","event-place":"Inuvik","publisher":"Inuvialuit Joint Secretariat","publisher-place":"Inuvik","title":"Inuvialuit and Nanuq: A Polar Bear Traditional Knowledge Study","author":[{"literal":"Inuvialuit Joint Secretariat"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      
BOX 4.3 The challenge of contestations and conflicts in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora over value differences

Some iconic taxa that are listed on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora appendices attract a high level of controversial discussion and absorb large amounts of the agenda in its fora. A key challenge is the different values held towards use and management of wildlife. A complicating element is the presence of powerful non-governmental organizations, as representatives of civil society, which have become increasingly numerous and vocal in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora debates in recent decades which hold their own values on the moral acceptability of trade and use of certain species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NNwivxdQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biggs, 2017; Challender & MacMillan, 2019; Duffy, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Biggs, 2017; Challender & MacMillan, 2019; Duffy, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32541,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9D9K9DR4"],"itemData":{"id":32541,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The escalating illegal wildlife trade (IWT) is one of the most high profile conservation challenges today. The crisis has attracted over US$350 million in donor and government funding in recent years, primarily directed at increased enforcement against IWT. There is growing recognition among practitioners and policy makers of the need to engage rural communities that neighbour or live with wildlife as key partners in tackling IWT. However, a framework to guide such community engagement is lacking. Here, we present a Theory of Change (ToC) to guide policy-makers, donors, and practitioners in partnering with communities to combat IWT. Our ToC identifies four pathways for community-level actions: a) strengthening disincentives for illegal behaviour; b) increasing incentives for wildlife stewardship; c) decreasing costs of living with wildlife; and d) supporting non-wildlife related livelihoods. The pathways all require strengthening of enabling conditions, including capacity building, and of governance in order to succeed. Our ToC serves to guide actions to tackle IWT and to inform the evaluation of policies. Moreover, our ToC serves as a tool to foster dialogue among IWT stakeholders from local communities through to governments and international donors to develop a more effective, holistic and sustainable community-based response to the IWT crisis.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12796","ISSN":"15231739","issue":"1","page":"5–12","title":"Developing a theory of change for a community-based response to illegal wildlife trade","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Dublin","given":"Holly T."},{"family":"Allan","given":"James R."},{"family":"Challender","given":"Dan W.S."},{"family":"Skinner","given":"Diane"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}},{"id":32609,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GP9H2QZM"],"itemData":{"id":32609,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of International Wildlife Law & Policy","DOI":"10.1080/13880292.2019.1638549","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"90–114,","title":"Investigating the Influence of Non-state Actors on Amendments to the CITES Appendices","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Challender","given":"D.W.S."},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"D.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32562,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SMI59UDU"],"itemData":{"id":32562,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and Planning C: Government and Policy","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"222–239","title":"Global environmental governance and north—south dynamics: the case of the CITES","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Duffy","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These non-governmental organizations provide a voice for civil society, are a source of scientific and practical information to parties and may assist selected lower income countries at the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora meetings. There is, however, concern among some academics, and regional government bodies s over the extent to which increased non-governmental organisations presence has negatively impacted the extent to which the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora is able to make evidence-based decisions and formulate policies on sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qCTO8w49","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bauer, 2018; Biggs, 2017; Challender & MacMillan, 2019; Cooney, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Bauer, 2018; Biggs, 2017; Challender & MacMillan, 2019; Cooney, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":851,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IJ4DAIBZ"],"itemData":{"id":851,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000446827300002","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12444","ISSN":"1755-263X","issue":"5","title":"Lions in the modern arena of CITES","URL":"://WOS:000446827300002","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Bauer","given":"H."},{"family":"Nowell","given":"K."},{"family":"Sillero-Zubiri","given":"C."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"D. W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",9]]}}},{"id":32541,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9D9K9DR4"],"itemData":{"id":32541,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The escalating illegal wildlife trade (IWT) is one of the most high profile conservation challenges today. The crisis has attracted over US$350 million in donor and government funding in recent years, primarily directed at increased enforcement against IWT. There is growing recognition among practitioners and policy makers of the need to engage rural communities that neighbour or live with wildlife as key partners in tackling IWT. However, a framework to guide such community engagement is lacking. Here, we present a Theory of Change (ToC) to guide policy-makers, donors, and practitioners in partnering with communities to combat IWT. Our ToC identifies four pathways for community-level actions: a) strengthening disincentives for illegal behaviour; b) increasing incentives for wildlife stewardship; c) decreasing costs of living with wildlife; and d) supporting non-wildlife related livelihoods. The pathways all require strengthening of enabling conditions, including capacity building, and of governance in order to succeed. Our ToC serves to guide actions to tackle IWT and to inform the evaluation of policies. Moreover, our ToC serves as a tool to foster dialogue among IWT stakeholders from local communities through to governments and international donors to develop a more effective, holistic and sustainable community-based response to the IWT crisis.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12796","ISSN":"15231739","issue":"1","page":"5–12","title":"Developing a theory of change for a community-based response to illegal wildlife trade","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Dublin","given":"Holly T."},{"family":"Allan","given":"James R."},{"family":"Challender","given":"Dan W.S."},{"family":"Skinner","given":"Diane"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}},{"id":32609,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GP9H2QZM"],"itemData":{"id":32609,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of International Wildlife Law & Policy","DOI":"10.1080/13880292.2019.1638549","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"90–114,","title":"Investigating the Influence of Non-state Actors on Amendments to the CITES Appendices","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Challender","given":"D.W.S."},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"D.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32612,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2S72VUJ2"],"itemData":{"id":32612,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.3389/fevo.2021.631556.","language":"en","page":"631556","title":"Think Before You Act: Improving the Conservation Outcomes of CITES Listing Decisions","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"},{"family":"Challender","given":"Dan"},{"family":"Broad","given":"Steven"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xCxvahte","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CITES 2019)","plainCitation":"(CITES 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":856,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZNMHXRW"],"itemData":{"id":856,"type":"webpage","container-title":"cites.org","title":"How CITES works","URL":"https://cites.org/eng/disc/how.php","author":[{"literal":"CITES"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There is also concern over the extent to which this presence may undermine the ability of smaller countries to effectively represent the interests of their citizens, due to the levels of resources large non-governmental organizations that may hold contrary value-based positions on wildlife trade that could be either pro- or anti-use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NNpC5Pa1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Challender, 2019; Duffy, 2013b)","plainCitation":"(Challender, 2019; Duffy, 2013b)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32543,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D5FRES6R"],"itemData":{"id":32543,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Pangolins are threatened by overexploitation for local and international use. They are subject to an international commercial trade ban, and are also the focus of other interventions, including attempts at commercial captive breeding. The impact that the latter could have on the conservation of wild populations deserves consideration. We critically evaluate the feasibility of commercial captive breeding (or farming) of pangolins to displace wild collection and assess its potential conservation impact on pangolin conservation using a recently published framework developed for this purpose. Of the 17 conditions posited that need to be met for supply-side interventions to displace wild collection, we find that pangolins meet a maximum of only six conditions. This analysis suggests that pangolin farming will not displace wild collection in the near future. Major barriers include an inability to breed pangolins on a commercial scale and available data suggest that it would be unprofitable. The immediate impact of pangolin farming on conservation of the species' is unclear, but it is unlikely to benefit the conservation of wild populations. If commercial captive breeding were possible, it is uncertain how it would affect economic incentives for poaching, interactions between legal and illegal markets, stockpile policies, and how consumers and Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) practitioners would respond. To understand better the potential overall impact of pangolin farming on wild populations there is a need for further research on these uncertainties. The framework used has utility in analysing the potential impact of wildlife farming but there remains a need for a more robust approach to evaluate potential impacts of supply-side interventions.","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gecco.2019.e00714","ISSN":"2351-9894","page":"e00714","title":"Evaluating the feasibility of pangolin farming and its potential conservation impact","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2351989419301544","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Challender","given":"Daniel W S"},{"family":"Sas-Rolfes","given":"Michael","non-dropping-particle":"'t"},{"family":"Ades","given":"Gary W J"},{"family":"Chin","given":"Jason S C"},{"family":"Ching-Min Sun","given":"Nick"},{"family":"Chong","given":"Ju","non-dropping-particle":"lian"},{"family":"Connelly","given":"Ellen"},{"family":"Hywood","given":"Lisa"},{"family":"Luz","given":"Sonja"},{"family":"Mohapatra","given":"Rajesh K"},{"family":"Ornellas","given":"Paul","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Parker","given":"Keri"},{"family":"Pietersen","given":"Darren W"},{"family":"Roberton","given":"Scott I"},{"family":"Semiadi","given":"Gono"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"Debbie"},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"Chris R"},{"family":"Thomson","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Yifu"},{"family":"Wicker","given":"Leanne"},{"family":"Wu","given":"Shi Bao"},{"family":"Nash","given":"Helen C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32562,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SMI59UDU"],"itemData":{"id":32562,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and Planning C: Government and Policy","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"222–239","title":"Global environmental governance and north—south dynamics: the case of the CITES","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Duffy","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"meMxmc4P","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CITES 2019)","plainCitation":"(CITES 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":856,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZNMHXRW"],"itemData":{"id":856,"type":"webpage","container-title":"cites.org","title":"How CITES works","URL":"https://cites.org/eng/disc/how.php","author":[{"literal":"CITES"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition, drawing from successful conflict management processes in other domains, it has been proposed that decision-making processes that combine different cultural value orientations towards plants, animals, and their sustainable use, be incorporated together with scientific evidence ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZUUAkQo0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biggs, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Biggs, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32541,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9D9K9DR4"],"itemData":{"id":32541,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The escalating illegal wildlife trade (IWT) is one of the most high profile conservation challenges today. The crisis has attracted over US$350 million in donor and government funding in recent years, primarily directed at increased enforcement against IWT. There is growing recognition among practitioners and policy makers of the need to engage rural communities that neighbour or live with wildlife as key partners in tackling IWT. However, a framework to guide such community engagement is lacking. Here, we present a Theory of Change (ToC) to guide policy-makers, donors, and practitioners in partnering with communities to combat IWT. Our ToC identifies four pathways for community-level actions: a) strengthening disincentives for illegal behaviour; b) increasing incentives for wildlife stewardship; c) decreasing costs of living with wildlife; and d) supporting non-wildlife related livelihoods. The pathways all require strengthening of enabling conditions, including capacity building, and of governance in order to succeed. Our ToC serves to guide actions to tackle IWT and to inform the evaluation of policies. Moreover, our ToC serves as a tool to foster dialogue among IWT stakeholders from local communities through to governments and international donors to develop a more effective, holistic and sustainable community-based response to the IWT crisis.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12796","ISSN":"15231739","issue":"1","page":"5–12","title":"Developing a theory of change for a community-based response to illegal wildlife trade","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Dublin","given":"Holly T."},{"family":"Allan","given":"James R."},{"family":"Challender","given":"Dan W.S."},{"family":"Skinner","given":"Diane"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.


Box 4.4. Inuit (Inuvialuit) Knowledge (IQ) and the Success of the Agreement on Polar Bear Conservation

The 1973 Agreement on the Conservation of Polar Bears provided a framework for research and sharing of data at national and circumpolar scales (e.g., article vii) while at the same time respecting the authority of each nation to manage its own polar bear resources (Freeman 1996). In Canada, that authority took shape in two committees. The present-day Canadian Polar Bear Administrative Committee and the associated Polar Bear Technical Committee were created in the 1970s to bring together the various voices in polar bear science and management from federal, territorial, and provincial agencies. The Technical Committee serves to facilitate management decisions by reviewing research results, and by making management recommendations directly to the constituent jurisdictions. These committees predate the settlement of the Inuvialuit Final Agreement signed in 1984. That agreement recognized the inherent right and authority of the Inuvialuit to manage polar bears among other lands and resources in their region. Over the years there have been some efforts to ensure that Inuvialuit and Inuit knowledge informs the Polar Bear Technical Committee (and Polar Bear Advisory Committee) however, many scholars suggest ‘science’ mixed with public sentiment have become dominant discourse and basis for decision-making ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9N6wc2OI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. A. Clark, 2009; Tam, 2021; Tyrrell & Clark, 2014)","plainCitation":"(D. A. Clark, 2009; Tam, 2021; Tyrrell & Clark, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":836,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UMBQLUQ2"],"itemData":{"id":836,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ARCTIC","DOI":"10.14430/arctic43","ISSN":"1923-1245, 0004-0843","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"ARCTIC","page":"347-360","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Polar Bear Conservation in Canada: Defining the Policy Problems","title-short":"Polar Bear Conservation in Canada","URL":"https://journalhosting.ucalgary.ca/index.php/arctic/article/view/63111","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Clark","given":"Douglas A."},{"family":"Lee","given":"David S."},{"family":"Freeman","given":"Milton M.R."},{"family":"Clark","given":"Susan G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",8,28]]}}},{"id":353,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L9J5PQHC"],"itemData":{"id":353,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"363–372","source":"Google Scholar","title":"What happened to climate change? CITES and the reconfiguration of polar bear conservation discourse","title-short":"What happened to climate change?","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Tyrrell","given":"Martina"},{"family":"Clark","given":"Douglas A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":837,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YMY3ZHC9"],"itemData":{"id":837,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Arctic and its animals figure prominently as icons of climate change in Western imaginaries. Persuasive storytelling centred on compelling animal icons, like the polar bear, is a powerful strategy to frame environmental challenges, mobilizing collective global efforts to resist environmental degradation and species endangerment. The power of the polar bear in Western climate imagery is in part derived from the perceived “environmental sacredness” of the animal that has gained a totem-like status. In dominant “global” discourses, this connotation often works to the detriment of Indigenous peoples, for whom animals signify complex socio-ecological relations and cultural histories. This Perspective article offers a reflexive analysis on the symbolic power of the polar bear totem and the discursive exclusion of Indigenous peoples, informed by attendance during 2015–2017 at annual global climate change negotiations and research during 2016–2018 in Canada’s Nunavut Territory. The polar bear’s totem-like status in Western imaginaries exposes three discursive tensions that infuse climate change perception, activism, representation and Indigenous citizenship. The first tension concerns the global climate crisis, and its perceived threat to ecologically significant or sacred species, contrasted with locally lived realities. The second tension concerns a perceived sacred Arctic that is global, pristine, fragile and “contemplated,” but simultaneously local, hazardous, sustaining and lived. The third tension concerns Indigenization, distorted under a global climate gaze that reimagines the role of Indigenous peoples. Current discursive hegemony over the Arctic serves to place Indigenous peoples in stasis and restricts the space for Arctic Indigenous engagement and voice.","container-title":"Frontiers in Communication","DOI":"10.3389/fcomm.2021.518759","ISSN":"2297-900X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Commun.","page":"518759","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Climate Change Totems and Discursive Hegemony Over the Arctic","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2021.518759/full","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Tam","given":"Chui-Ling"},{"family":"Chew","given":"Suzanne"},{"family":"Carvalho","given":"Anabela"},{"family":"Doyle","given":"Julie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      While inequities in voice between Inuit Knowledge holders and scientists have led to impacts on Inuit economies, well-being and co-management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"f34qjrGj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Foote & Wenzel, 2009; B. Parlee & Inuvialuit Game Council, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Foote & Wenzel, 2009; B. Parlee & Inuvialuit Game Council, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":864,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IZJPDHAI"],"itemData":{"id":864,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Inuit, Polar Bears and Sustainable Use: Local, National and International Perspectives","page":"13-24","publisher":"University of Alberta Press","source":"ResearchGate","title":"Polar bear conservation hunting in Canada: Economics, culture and unintended consequences","title-short":"Polar bear conservation hunting in Canada","author":[{"family":"Foote","given":"Lee"},{"family":"Wenzel","given":"George"}],"editor":[{"family":"Milton","given":"M. R."},{"family":"Foote","given":"Lee"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",1,1]]}}},{"id":34986,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8QH7TLMK"],"itemData":{"id":34986,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Arctic Crashes: People and Animals in a Changing North","event-place":"Washington D.C.","page":"200-215","publisher":"Smithsonian Institute Scholarly Press","publisher-place":"Washington D.C.","title":"The Politics of a Polar Bear “Crash”","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"B."},{"literal":"Inuvialuit Game Council"}],"editor":[{"family":"Krupnik","given":"I."},{"family":"Crowell","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, this system of governance remained relatively stable between 1970 and the mid-1990s, save for periodic efforts of interest groups to curtail Inuvialuit and Inuit harvesting rights. When the Inuvialuit settled a land claim agreement with the Canadian federal government in 1984, it significantly changed their role in the federal and territorial resource management decision-making processes. The Inuvialuit Final Agreement recognizes and affirms, as other comprehensive land claims in Canada do for other indigenous peoples, the inherent rights of the Inuvialuit for self-government and power in decisions about lands and resources in their homeland. As a result of this agreement, various co-management processes and councils were established that mandated the participation of Inuvialuit in decisions regarding lands and resources in the region, including polar bears. Among these is the Inuvialuit Game Council.
      In 1988, the Council and Inupiat from the Alaskan North Slope Borough signed the Inuvialuit - Inupiat Polar Bear Management Agreement in the Southern Beaufort Sea region, underpinning decision-making about quotes of bear harvest in the region. As in other regions of the north, hunting is recognized as part of the way of life but is not antithetical to conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QrPmZ8Jg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. M. R. Freeman, 2005)","plainCitation":"(M. M. R. Freeman, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32544,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/75UJHHAC"],"itemData":{"id":32544,"type":"book","call-number":"SK471.N59 P46 2004","collection-number":"56","collection-title":"Occasional publication series","event-place":"Edmonton","ISBN":"978-1-896445-35-9","language":"en","number-of-pages":"112","publisher":"CCI Press","publisher-place":"Edmonton","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Conservation hunting: people and wildlife in Canada's north","title-short":"Conservation hunting","editor":[{"family":"Freeman","given":"Milton M. R."},{"family":"Hudson","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Foote","given":"Lee"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Embedded within the oral histories and observational accounts, are insights about polar bear ecology Inuvialuit hunters track bears on the ice by looking for specific details about their size, sex, behavior, direction and condition. Over time and through interpreting and sharing with other elders and land users, Inuvialuit knowledge comprises longitudinal data about body condition, population variability, distribution, reproduction, mating behavior, and hunting practices, as well as broader patterns of ecological change including weather patterns, seal abundance and distribution, sea ice conditions, and human-bear interactions. When interviewed in 2010, most Inuvialuit hunters observed few changes in the abundance of bears, including the number of cubs (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"G7p3KjKy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Inuvialuit Joint Secretariat, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Inuvialuit Joint Secretariat, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32563,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YDHNMBY4"],"itemData":{"id":32563,"type":"report","event-place":"Inuvik","publisher":"Inuvialuit Joint Secretariat","publisher-place":"Inuvik","title":"Inuvialuit and Nanuq: A Polar Bear Traditional Knowledge Study","author":[{"literal":"Inuvialuit Joint Secretariat"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} :182–184). Whereas sea ice conditions were observed to be deteriorating in some areas, making it more difficult and dangerous for hunters to pursue bears, the Inuvialuit from most communities observed that the bears themselves are healthy based on numerous indicators including body fat and reproductive success. The abundance and location of good seal habitat has been changing according to some elders, but this does not seem to be affecting the condition or number of bears. Elders attribute this to hunting becoming easier due to thinning ice in some regions as well as increased abundance of harp seal, a main source of food in the region. This knowledge, combined with the outcomes of scientific research, form the basis of decisions by the regional Inuvialuit government (i.e., Inuvialuit Game Council) to make decisions about the harvest of the populations of the south-Beaufort region. As a result, harvest quotas have tended to vary from year to year as the distribution of bears has varied. "Reported harvest levels for bears in the southern Beaufort area have been below quotas set for “allowable harvest” (modelled by scientists and Inuit/Inuvialuit) or below what is considered sustainable at a population level in an otherwise healthy population (4.5%) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tJAtOVPY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Regehr, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Regehr, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32225,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XNB4ZPRJ"],"itemData":{"id":32225,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"1534–1543","title":"Harvesting wildlife affected by climate change: a modelling and management approach for polar bears","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Regehr","given":"Eric V."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"Ryan R."},{"family":"Rode","given":"Karyn D."},{"family":"Runge","given":"Michael C."},{"family":"Stern","given":"Harry L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}." 
      This harvest has continued in recent years, despite outside pressure, due to the confidence and belief of Inuvialuit peoples in the rigor and validity of their own evidence and knowledge and experience.
      

Box 4.5.  The Convention for the Conservation and Management of Vicuñas -and the lack of a Convention for Guanacos

The Convention for the Conservation and Management of the Vicuña (1979) was signed by Argentina, Bolivia, Chile, Peru and Ecuador and is an example of a regional institutional arrangement that succeeded in the management of a common pool resource over a vast area to protect a wild South American camelid that lives in the High Andes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cCsyRKBU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7370,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7TN9Z64K","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/77KTC5ZS"],"itemData":{"id":7370,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","DOI":"http://doi.org/10.18352/ijc.139","issue":"1","page":"100-121","title":"Vicuña conservation and poverty alleviation? Andean communities and international fibre markets","URL":"https://www.thecommonsjournal.org/articles/10.18352/ijc.139/","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein, Gabriela","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Article I of the Vicuña Convention, and in the signatory states’ subsequent submissions to the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora meetings, Andean people that had been bearing the burden of vicuña conservation were named as the main beneficiaries of future vicuña use. However, translating this article into national legislation and ensuring exclusive benefits to local people has proved to be difficult ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uR2Cgjjk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7370,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7TN9Z64K","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/77KTC5ZS"],"itemData":{"id":7370,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","DOI":"http://doi.org/10.18352/ijc.139","issue":"1","page":"100-121","title":"Vicuña conservation and poverty alleviation? Andean communities and international fibre markets","URL":"https://www.thecommonsjournal.org/articles/10.18352/ijc.139/","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein, Gabriela","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Due to its fleece which has one of the finest fibers in the world, it has long been hunted, ultimately resulting in the species almost being driven to extinction in the middle of the 20th century. The species recovery was a result of concerted international conservation efforts through the Vicuña Convention, the entry into force of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, and the prohibitions imposed by the Fish and Wildlife Service of country ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"S9HmsEL4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010; McNeill, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010; McNeill, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7370,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7TN9Z64K","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/77KTC5ZS"],"itemData":{"id":7370,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","DOI":"http://doi.org/10.18352/ijc.139","issue":"1","page":"100-121","title":"Vicuña conservation and poverty alleviation? Andean communities and international fibre markets","URL":"https://www.thecommonsjournal.org/articles/10.18352/ijc.139/","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein, Gabriela","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32611,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FSNU4ZWX"],"itemData":{"id":32611,"type":"book","event-place":"Nueva York","language":"en","number-of-pages":"63–80","publisher":"Springer","publisher-place":"Nueva York","title":"International policies and national legislation concerning vicuna conservation and exploitation","author":[{"family":"McNeill","given":"D."},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"G."},{"family":"Arc","given":"N.Renaudeau"}],"editor":[{"family":"En","given":"I.Gordon"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In contrast to the approach with Vicuña, another wild South American camelid species that lives in Andean countries - Guanaco - is also listed in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Appendix II, but there is no multilateral regional agreement between the range countries with regards its sustainable use and conservation.  The lack of common goals towards the conservation and sustainable use of the species results in its range countries (Argentina, Chile, Peru, Bolivia, Paraguay) having very different management schemes that range from protection to culling. There is no sharing of information about best practices, no coordinated national measures as in the case of vicuña. Guanaco populations are critically engendered in Bolivia and Paraguay and severely threatened in Peru ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Z6R6ZD1R","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Baldi, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Baldi, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32610,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YWDBIVB3"],"itemData":{"id":32610,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"page: 10 2305 2016–1 11186 18540211","title":"Lama guanicoe. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species","editor":[{"family":"Baldi","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Acebes","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Soto","given":"Cuellar"},{"literal":"Erika"},{"family":"Funes","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Puig","given":"Silvia"},{"family":"Franklin","given":"William"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      

      The Agreement on Conservation of Polar Bears sets out terms for sharing of knowledge and monitoring populations which creates the foundation for local-regional decisions about Inuit harvest quotas.  There have been key examples of such knowledge documented by Inuit that strongly suggest bear populations are healthy, contrary to models and assumptions that sea ice melt is having an adverse impact ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WwSB4PIU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. A. Clark, 2009)","plainCitation":"(D. A. Clark, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":836,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UMBQLUQ2"],"itemData":{"id":836,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ARCTIC","DOI":"10.14430/arctic43","ISSN":"1923-1245, 0004-0843","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"ARCTIC","page":"347-360","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Polar Bear Conservation in Canada: Defining the Policy Problems","title-short":"Polar Bear Conservation in Canada","URL":"https://journalhosting.ucalgary.ca/index.php/arctic/article/view/63111","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Clark","given":"Douglas A."},{"family":"Lee","given":"David S."},{"family":"Freeman","given":"Milton M.R."},{"family":"Clark","given":"Susan G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",8,28]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      There are more examples of regional conventions and agreements related to large fauna and hunting practices; including agreements related to marine and freshwater fisheries. Most notably, regional fisheries management organisations cover almost all oceanic areas and their members (generally national delegations) have established rules regarding the collection and sharing of data, accepted methods for assessing the state of fish stocks, and negotiations to allocate shares of allowed catch among members. Examples include: the Inter-America Tropical Tuna Commission and Northwast Atlantic Fisheries Organisation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eolmfB89","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(DFO-Department of Fisheries and Oceans Canada, 2019)","plainCitation":"(DFO-Department of Fisheries and Oceans Canada, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32559,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QKV4HWFR"],"itemData":{"id":32559,"type":"report","event-place":"Ottawa","publisher":"Government of Canada","publisher-place":"Ottawa","title":"Regional Fisheries Management Organizations","URL":"https://www.dfo-mpo.gc.ca/international/dip-rfmo-eng.htm","author":[{"literal":"DFO-Department of Fisheries and Oceans Canada"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
       Despite their wide spatial coverage and formal cooperation processes, regional fisheries management organisations focus almost exclusively on tuna and other high-value species and have been criticized for overlooking possible impacts on bycatch species, there are also concerns they supersed science-based catch recommendations due to political factors and allowing for unbalanced negotiation power in quota allocation and participation of new or non-members in discussions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sj3wrY5u","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Haas, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Haas, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":868,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8JUGNQFU"],"itemData":{"id":868,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","ISSN":"0308-597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"103787","title":"Factors influencing the performance of regional fisheries management organizations","volume":"113","author":[{"family":"Haas","given":"Bianca"},{"family":"McGee","given":"Jeffrey"},{"family":"Fleming","given":"Aysha"},{"family":"Haward","given":"Marcus"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Another convention is the International Whaling Commission, originally established to support the sustainable harvest of whales by member nations. Over time, open membership and vote-based rules led to the International Whaling Commission functioning as a de facto conservation convention, resulting in the eventual exit of some founding members wishing to continue whaling and ongoing debates regarding impacts on indigenous whaling practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Le6YcEDq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Punt & Donovan, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Punt & Donovan, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":867,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3PJC3XQW"],"itemData":{"id":867,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","ISSN":"1095-9289","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"ICES Journal of Marine Science","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Oxford University Press","page":"603-612","title":"Developing management procedures that are robust to uncertainty: lessons from the International Whaling Commission","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Punt","given":"André E"},{"family":"Donovan","given":"Greg P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Aside from these global or very large-area examples, there are examples of bilateral or multilateral conventions to manage fisheries stocks—including cod and herring in Northern Europe, salmon and halibut in the US and Canada, and even preemptive agreements for Arctic fisheries. There is growing interest in further establishment of such conventions between smaller groups of countries, particularly as climate change increases the number of transboundary fish stocks. One recent example is the Agreement to Prevent Unregulated High Seas Fisheries in the Central Arctic Ocean ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kHWhAEx6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Balton, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Balton, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":873,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AG7B8N3X"],"itemData":{"id":873,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","ISSN":"0308-597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"103745","title":"What will the bbnj agreement mean for the Arctic fisheries agreement?","author":[{"family":"Balton","given":"David"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      A major challenge with regional agreements related to fisheries is the problem of open access in ecosystems for which there are no binding global institutions. Fishing fleets are also increasingly mobile and tend to “operate like roving bandits because global markets often fail to generate the self-interest that arises from attachment to place” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"joi7UuWf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2006a)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2006a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":863,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q57T5MS7"],"itemData":{"id":863,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Marine resource exploitation can deplete stocks faster than regulatory agencies can respond. Institutions with broad authority and a global perspective are needed to create a system with incentives for conservation.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1122804","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5767","language":"en","license":"© 2006 American Association for the Advancement of Science","note":"PMID: 16543444","page":"1557-1558","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"Globalization, Roving Bandits, and Marine Resources","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/311/5767/1557","volume":"311","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"T. P."},{"family":"Steneck","given":"R. S."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"J. A."},{"family":"Bellwood","given":"D. R."},{"family":"Crona","given":"B."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"Gunderson","given":"L. H."},{"family":"Leslie","given":"H. M."},{"family":"Norberg","given":"J."},{"family":"Nyström","given":"M."},{"family":"Olsson","given":"P."},{"family":"Österblom","given":"H."},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"M."},{"family":"Worm","given":"B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",3,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This high degree of mobility coupled with adaptability of fishing fleets to respond to shifts in markets is also part of the governance challenge. “Developing the institutions to deal with commons issues is problematic and slow. new markets can develop so rapidly that the speed of resource exploitation often overwhelms the ability of local institutions to respond” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mPZ6x2qC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2006a)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2006a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":863,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q57T5MS7"],"itemData":{"id":863,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Marine resource exploitation can deplete stocks faster than regulatory agencies can respond. Institutions with broad authority and a global perspective are needed to create a system with incentives for conservation.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1122804","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5767","language":"en","license":"© 2006 American Association for the Advancement of Science","note":"PMID: 16543444","page":"1557-1558","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"Globalization, Roving Bandits, and Marine Resources","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/311/5767/1557","volume":"311","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"T. P."},{"family":"Steneck","given":"R. S."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"J. A."},{"family":"Bellwood","given":"D. R."},{"family":"Crona","given":"B."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"Gunderson","given":"L. H."},{"family":"Leslie","given":"H. M."},{"family":"Norberg","given":"J."},{"family":"Nyström","given":"M."},{"family":"Olsson","given":"P."},{"family":"Österblom","given":"H."},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"M."},{"family":"Worm","given":"B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",3,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The majority or industrial fishing occurs in these areas which lack governance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vS5OpmfG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kroodsma, 2018; Palacios-Abrantes, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Kroodsma, 2018; Palacios-Abrantes, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34094,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q46WLTTS"],"itemData":{"id":34094,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"More than half the fish in the sea\\nAs the human population has grown in recent decades, our dependence on ocean-supplied protein has rapidly increased. Kroodsma took advantage of the automatic identification system installed on all industrial fishing vessels to map and quantify fishing efforts across the world (see the Perspective by Poloczanska). More than half of the world's oceans are subject to industrial-scale harvest, spanning an area four times that covered by terrestrial agriculture. Furthermore, fishing efforts seem not to depend on economic or environmental drivers, but rather social and political schedules. Thus, more active measures will likely be needed to ensure sustainable use of ocean resources.\\nScience, this issue p. 904; see also p. 864\\nAlthough fishing is one of the most widespread activities by which humans harvest natural resources, its global footprint is poorly understood and has never been directly quantified. We processed 22 billion automatic identification system messages and tracked >70,000 industrial fishing vessels from 2012 to 2016, creating a global dynamic footprint of fishing effort with spatial and temporal resolution two to three orders of magnitude higher than for previous data sets. Our data show that industrial fishing occurs in >55% of ocean area and has a spatial extent more than four times that of agriculture. We find that global patterns of fishing have surprisingly low sensitivity to short-term economic and environmental variation and a strong response to cultural and political events such as holidays and closures.\\nMore than half of the ocean is exposed to industrial fishing activities.\\nMore than half of the ocean is exposed to industrial fishing activities.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aao5646","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6378","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2018 The Authors, some rights reserved; exclusive licensee American Association for the Advancement of Science. No claim to original U.S. Government Works. http://www.sciencemag.org/about/science-licenses-journal-article-reuseThis is an article distributed under the terms of the Science Journals Default License.","note":"PMID: 29472481","page":"904-908","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"Tracking the global footprint of fisheries","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/359/6378/904","volume":"359","author":[{"family":"Kroodsma","given":"David A."},{"family":"Mayorga","given":"Juan"},{"family":"Hochberg","given":"Timothy"},{"family":"Miller","given":"Nathan A."},{"family":"Boerder","given":"Kristina"},{"family":"Ferretti","given":"Francesco"},{"family":"Wilson","given":"Alex"},{"family":"Bergman","given":"Bjorn"},{"family":"White","given":"Timothy D."},{"family":"Block","given":"Barbara A."},{"family":"Woods","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Sullivan","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Costello","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Worm","given":"Boris"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2,23]]}}},{"id":32548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N8NFP5ZV"],"itemData":{"id":32548,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0209431","issue":"12","title":"Evaluating the bio-economic performance of a Callo de hacha (Atrina maura, Atrina tuberculosa & Pinna rugosa) fishery restoration plan in La Paz, Mexico","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85058775470&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0209431&partnerID=40&md5=0cc5599bb16631c6354484f1b8836f86","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Palacios-Abrantes","given":"J."},{"family":"Herrera-Correal","given":"J."},{"family":"Rodríguez","given":"S."},{"family":"Brunkow","given":"J."},{"family":"Molina","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is this transboundary nature of fisheries, coupled with a lack of strong institutions in many areas of the globe and for large numbers of fish species, that has led to problems of unsustainable use or overharvesting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Tcr3Qa3I","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2006a; Kroodsma, 2018; Palacios-Abrantes, 2018; Tickler, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2006a; Kroodsma, 2018; Palacios-Abrantes, 2018; Tickler, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34094,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q46WLTTS"],"itemData":{"id":34094,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"More than half the fish in the sea\\nAs the human population has grown in recent decades, our dependence on ocean-supplied protein has rapidly increased. Kroodsma took advantage of the automatic identification system installed on all industrial fishing vessels to map and quantify fishing efforts across the world (see the Perspective by Poloczanska). More than half of the world's oceans are subject to industrial-scale harvest, spanning an area four times that covered by terrestrial agriculture. Furthermore, fishing efforts seem not to depend on economic or environmental drivers, but rather social and political schedules. Thus, more active measures will likely be needed to ensure sustainable use of ocean resources.\\nScience, this issue p. 904; see also p. 864\\nAlthough fishing is one of the most widespread activities by which humans harvest natural resources, its global footprint is poorly understood and has never been directly quantified. We processed 22 billion automatic identification system messages and tracked >70,000 industrial fishing vessels from 2012 to 2016, creating a global dynamic footprint of fishing effort with spatial and temporal resolution two to three orders of magnitude higher than for previous data sets. Our data show that industrial fishing occurs in >55% of ocean area and has a spatial extent more than four times that of agriculture. We find that global patterns of fishing have surprisingly low sensitivity to short-term economic and environmental variation and a strong response to cultural and political events such as holidays and closures.\\nMore than half of the ocean is exposed to industrial fishing activities.\\nMore than half of the ocean is exposed to industrial fishing activities.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aao5646","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6378","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2018 The Authors, some rights reserved; exclusive licensee American Association for the Advancement of Science. No claim to original U.S. Government Works. http://www.sciencemag.org/about/science-licenses-journal-article-reuseThis is an article distributed under the terms of the Science Journals Default License.","note":"PMID: 29472481","page":"904-908","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"Tracking the global footprint of fisheries","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/359/6378/904","volume":"359","author":[{"family":"Kroodsma","given":"David A."},{"family":"Mayorga","given":"Juan"},{"family":"Hochberg","given":"Timothy"},{"family":"Miller","given":"Nathan A."},{"family":"Boerder","given":"Kristina"},{"family":"Ferretti","given":"Francesco"},{"family":"Wilson","given":"Alex"},{"family":"Bergman","given":"Bjorn"},{"family":"White","given":"Timothy D."},{"family":"Block","given":"Barbara A."},{"family":"Woods","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Sullivan","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Costello","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Worm","given":"Boris"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2,23]]}}},{"id":863,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q57T5MS7"],"itemData":{"id":863,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Marine resource exploitation can deplete stocks faster than regulatory agencies can respond. Institutions with broad authority and a global perspective are needed to create a system with incentives for conservation.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1122804","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5767","language":"en","license":"© 2006 American Association for the Advancement of Science","note":"PMID: 16543444","page":"1557-1558","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"Globalization, Roving Bandits, and Marine Resources","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/311/5767/1557","volume":"311","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"T. P."},{"family":"Steneck","given":"R. S."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"J. A."},{"family":"Bellwood","given":"D. R."},{"family":"Crona","given":"B."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"Gunderson","given":"L. H."},{"family":"Leslie","given":"H. M."},{"family":"Norberg","given":"J."},{"family":"Nyström","given":"M."},{"family":"Olsson","given":"P."},{"family":"Österblom","given":"H."},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"M."},{"family":"Worm","given":"B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",3,17]]}}},{"id":832,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MD2QE8V2"],"itemData":{"id":832,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science Advances","language":"en","page":"3279","title":"Far from home: Distance patterns of global fishing fleets","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Tickler","given":"D."},{"family":"Meeuwig","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Palomares","given":"M.L.D."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."},{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N8NFP5ZV"],"itemData":{"id":32548,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0209431","issue":"12","title":"Evaluating the bio-economic performance of a Callo de hacha (Atrina maura, Atrina tuberculosa & Pinna rugosa) fishery restoration plan in La Paz, Mexico","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85058775470&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0209431&partnerID=40&md5=0cc5599bb16631c6354484f1b8836f86","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Palacios-Abrantes","given":"J."},{"family":"Herrera-Correal","given":"J."},{"family":"Rodríguez","given":"S."},{"family":"Brunkow","given":"J."},{"family":"Molina","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (see Figure 4.7). 
      In addition to agreements related specifically to fisheries there are a variety of transboundary arrangements for freshwater ecosystems that have developed to address transboundary issues of water use as well as management of aquatic habitats (e.g., across the Canada-United States of America border, in the Mekong river basin) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yA5Lo6MQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fox & Sneddon, 2007; Hildebrand, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Fox & Sneddon, 2007; Hildebrand, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":871,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5IEE5L8L"],"itemData":{"id":871,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Environmental Agreements: Politics, Law and Economics","ISSN":"1573-1553","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"International Environmental Agreements: Politics, Law and Economics","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Springer","page":"237-261","title":"Transboundary river basin agreements in the Mekong and Zambezi basins: Enhancing environmental security or securitizing the environment?","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Fox","given":"Coleen A"},{"family":"Sneddon","given":"Chris"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":872,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KE5AY8T3"],"itemData":{"id":872,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","ISSN":"0964-5691","issue":"6-7","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","note":"number: 6-7\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"421-457","title":"Cooperative ecosystem management across the Canada–US border: approaches and experiences of transboundary programs in the Gulf of Maine, Great Lakes and Georgia Basin/Puget Sound","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Hildebrand","given":"Lawrence P"},{"family":"Pebbles","given":"Victoria"},{"family":"Fraser","given":"David A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Their role in addressing questions of biodiversity loss and sustainable use is less well-developed.  
      Small scale freshwater fisheries contribute significantly to the food security of those living in transboundary systems including the Amazon, Congo and Mekong. However, their use of species is threatened by a focus on these river systems as a source of development including hydro-electric power ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6yrPwVkx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Winemiller, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Winemiller, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":834,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GALGPIZ8"],"itemData":{"id":834,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"6269","language":"en","page":"128–29","title":"Balancing Hydropower and Biodiversity in the Amazon, Congo, and Mekong","volume":"351","author":[{"family":"Winemiller","given":"Kirk O."},{"family":"Mcintyre","given":"Pb B."},{"family":"Castello","given":"L."},{"family":"Fluet-Chouinard","given":"E."},{"family":"Giarrizzo","given":"T."},{"family":"Nam","given":"S."},{"family":"Baird","given":"I.G."},{"family":"Darwall","given":"W."},{"family":"Lujan","given":"N.K."},{"family":"Harrison","given":"I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The absence of transboundary institutions that deal with questions of sustainable use at basin-wide scales is a major barrier to protecting indigenous peoples and local communities and the freshwater fisheries on which they depend ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HIpIpZj5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Chen 2008; Begossi 2019)","plainCitation":"(P. Chen 2008; Begossi 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32547,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y9SE8XEQ"],"itemData":{"id":32547,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Toxicon","page":"139–145","title":"Purification and characterization of a novel excitatory peptide from Conus distans venom that defines a novel superfamily of conotoxins","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Chen","given":"P."},{"family":"Garrett","given":"J. E."},{"family":"Watkins","given":"M."},{"family":"Olivera","given":"B. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":32545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/87SXB3PK"],"itemData":{"id":32545,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-019-0331-2","issue":"1","title":"Fishers and groupers (Epinephelus marginatus and E. morio) in the coast of Brazil: Integrating information for conservation","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85074625679&doi=10.1186%2fs13002-019-0331-2&partnerID=40&md5=e0aeb9a276a2cfbf05b49ee26cb4ec6f","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Begossi","given":"A."},{"family":"Salyvonchyk","given":"S."},{"family":"Glamuzina","given":"B."},{"family":"De Souza","given":"S.P."},{"family":"Lopes","given":"P.F.M."},{"family":"Priolli","given":"R.H.G."},{"family":"Do Prado","given":"D.O."},{"family":"Ramires","given":"M."},{"family":"Clauzet","given":"M."},{"family":"Zapelini","given":"C."},{"family":"Schneider","given":"D.T."},{"family":"Silva","given":"L.T."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"R.A.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. “Institutions that permit and finance hydropower development should require basin-scale analyses that account for cumulative impacts and climate change. Proposed dam sites should be evaluated within the context of sustaining a portfolio of ecosystem services and biodiversity conservation” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"J6AZlLMu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Winemiller, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Winemiller, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":834,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GALGPIZ8"],"itemData":{"id":834,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"6269","language":"en","page":"128–29","title":"Balancing Hydropower and Biodiversity in the Amazon, Congo, and Mekong","volume":"351","author":[{"family":"Winemiller","given":"Kirk O."},{"family":"Mcintyre","given":"Pb B."},{"family":"Castello","given":"L."},{"family":"Fluet-Chouinard","given":"E."},{"family":"Giarrizzo","given":"T."},{"family":"Nam","given":"S."},{"family":"Baird","given":"I.G."},{"family":"Darwall","given":"W."},{"family":"Lujan","given":"N.K."},{"family":"Harrison","given":"I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  


Figure 4.7. Map presents mean industrial fisheries catch in metric tons per square kilometer by catch location during the (A) 1950s and (B) 2000s. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qS4stJpv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tickler 2018)","plainCitation":"(Tickler 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":832,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MD2QE8V2"],"itemData":{"id":832,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science Advances","language":"en","page":"3279","title":"Far from home: Distance patterns of global fishing fleets","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Tickler","given":"D."},{"family":"Meeuwig","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Palomares","given":"M.L.D."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."},{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} under license 4.0 (CC BY-NC). The designations employed and the presentation of material on the maps used in the assessment do not imply the expression of any opinion whatsoever on the part of IPBES concerning the legal status of any country, territory, city or area or of its authorities, or concerning the delimitation of its frontiers or boundaries. These maps have been prepared or used for the sole purpose of facilitating the assessment of the broad biogeographical areas represented therein and for purposes of representing scientific data spatially.


	
      Addressing broad questions of development in river basins requires institutions to “move beyond the current inward focus on project approvals in projects. toward an outward focus on the cumulative effects of all disturbances in a watershed” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QoRYf9Y7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sheelanere, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Sheelanere, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":870,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/62YXH7P2"],"itemData":{"id":870,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land Use Policy","ISSN":"0264-8377","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Land Use Policy","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"67-75","title":"Institutional requirements for watershed cumulative effects assessment and management: Lessons from a Canadian trans-boundary watershed","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Sheelanere","given":"Poornima"},{"family":"Noble","given":"Bram F"},{"family":"Patrick","given":"Robert J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Balancing such large and basin wide perspectives should not be at the expense of recognition of the complexity and diversity of the values and uses of biodiversity of indigenous peoples and local communities, whose fishing practices have long been sustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2S7Mio0y","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. G. Baird, 2021)","plainCitation":"(I. G. Baird, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":853,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KJBMTFCV"],"itemData":{"id":853,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-020-01418-x","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Downstream Impacts of Hydropower Dams and Indigenous and Local Knowledge: Examples from the Peace–Athabasca, Mekong, and Amazon","title-short":"The Downstream Impacts of Hydropower Dams and Indigenous and Local Knowledge","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-020-01418-x","author":[{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato A. M."},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"},{"family":"Poesch","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Maclean","given":"Bruce"},{"family":"Napoleon","given":"Art"},{"family":"Lepine","given":"Melody"},{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The Mekong River Commission for example, was founded in 1995 as a successor to the Mekong Committee (1957); although historically, there was a stronger focus on economic development, the Mekong River Commission includes a greater scope on sustainability. States involved include Thailand, Laos, Cambodia and Vietnam. The Commission has multiple focal points including sustainable development. However, the continued development of the Lower Mekong for hydropower and the absence of China from the membership of the Commission (where the headwaters of the Mekong Basin are located) have been highlighted weaknesses. As a result, fishing livelihoods of many residents of the basin (particularly those directly affected by upstream/downstream and tributary effects of hydropower) have been compromised ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Z060kxoB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. Baird, 2020; Pearse-Smith, 2012; Soukhaphon, 2021)","plainCitation":"(I. Baird, 2020; Pearse-Smith, 2012; Soukhaphon, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26044,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J7CJXY9B","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LHQS9LKW"],"itemData":{"id":26044,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Water Alternatives","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Water Alternatives","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Water Alternatives Association","page":"141-159","title":"Opening the gates of the Pak Mun Dam: Fish migrations, domestic water supply, irrigation projects and politics","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G"},{"family":"Manorom","given":"Kanokwan"},{"family":"Phenow","given":"Aurore"},{"family":"Gaja-Svasti","given":"Sirasak"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32593,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WUBW36Y7"],"itemData":{"id":32593,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Consilience","ISSN":"1948-3074","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Consilience","note":"number: 7\\npublisher: JSTOR","page":"73-86","title":"The impact of continued Mekong Basin hydropower development on local livelihoods","author":[{"family":"Pearse-Smith","given":"Scott William David"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":838,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WYNHXLIZ"],"itemData":{"id":838,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Mekong River, well known for its aquatic biodiversity, is important to the social, physical, and economic health of millions living in China, Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia, and Vietnam. This paper explores the social and environmental impacts of several Mekong basin hydropower dams and groupings of dams and the geographies of their impacts. Specifically, we examined the 3S (Sesan, Sekong Srepok) river system in northeastern Cambodia, the Central Highlands of Vietnam, and southern Laos; the Khone Falls area in southern Laos; the lower Mun River Basin in northeastern Thailand; and the upper Mekong River in Yunnan Province, China, northeastern Myanmar, northern Laos, and northern Thailand. Evidence shows that these dams and groupings of dams are affecting fish migrations, river hydrology, and sediment transfers. Such changes are negatively impacting riparian communities up to 1000 km away. Because many communities depend on the river and its resources for their food and livelihood, changes to the river have impacted, and will continue to negatively impact, food and economic security. While social and environmental impact assessments have been carried out for these projects, greater consideration of the scale and cumulative impacts of dams is necessary.","container-title":"Water","DOI":"10.3390/w13030265","ISSN":"2073-4441","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Water","language":"en","page":"265","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin: A Review","title-short":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/13/3/265","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Soukhaphon","given":"Akarath"},{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G."},{"family":"Hogan","given":"Zeb S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A more integrated approach to watershed management is needed that deals with the dynamics of transboundary use and cross scale problems such as hydro-electric development ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vRSazwmR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hensengerth, 2009; Hirsch, 2006; Suhardiman, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Hensengerth, 2009; Hirsch, 2006; Suhardiman, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32588,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JZX4WK4L"],"itemData":{"id":32588,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Contemporary Southeast Asia","ISSN":"0129-797X","journalAbbreviation":"Contemporary Southeast Asia","note":"publisher: JSTOR","page":"326-349","title":"Transboundary river cooperation and the regional public good: The case of the Mekong River","author":[{"family":"Hensengerth","given":"Oliver"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":32587,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R2A2VRFB"],"itemData":{"id":32587,"type":"book","publisher":"Australian Mekong Resource Centre, in collaboration with Danish …","title":"National interests and transboundary water governance in the Mekong","author":[{"family":"Hirsch","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Kurt Mørck"},{"family":"Boer","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Carrard","given":"Naomi"},{"family":"FitzGerald","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Lyster","given":"Rosemary"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":32592,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H4LYZRP4"],"itemData":{"id":32592,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","ISSN":"0894-1920","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Society & Natural Resources","note":"number: 6\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"572-586","title":"Scalar disconnect: The logic of transboundary water governance in the Mekong","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Suhardiman","given":"Diana"},{"family":"Giordano","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Molle","given":"François"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

BOX 4.6  Hydro-electric Development in the Lower Mekong and its Impacts on Sustainable Use

The Mekong river is a large transboundary river originating the Tibetan Plateau, spanning 4909 km and eventually ending in the South China Sea ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CGBxfFJz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Q. Chen, 2020; Soukhaphon, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Q. Chen, 2020; Soukhaphon, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":874,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PNMBJ9IP"],"itemData":{"id":874,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"National science review","ISSN":"2095-5138","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"National science review","note":"number: 9\\npublisher: Oxford University Press","page":"1449-1457","title":"Hydropower reservoirs on the upper Mekong River modify nutrient bioavailability downstream","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Chen","given":"Qiuwen"},{"family":"Shi","given":"Wenqing"},{"family":"Huisman","given":"Jef"},{"family":"Maberly","given":"Stephen C"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Jianyun"},{"family":"Yu","given":"Juhua"},{"family":"Chen","given":"Yuchen"},{"family":"Tonina","given":"Daniele"},{"family":"Yi","given":"Qitao"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":838,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WYNHXLIZ"],"itemData":{"id":838,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Mekong River, well known for its aquatic biodiversity, is important to the social, physical, and economic health of millions living in China, Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia, and Vietnam. This paper explores the social and environmental impacts of several Mekong basin hydropower dams and groupings of dams and the geographies of their impacts. Specifically, we examined the 3S (Sesan, Sekong Srepok) river system in northeastern Cambodia, the Central Highlands of Vietnam, and southern Laos; the Khone Falls area in southern Laos; the lower Mun River Basin in northeastern Thailand; and the upper Mekong River in Yunnan Province, China, northeastern Myanmar, northern Laos, and northern Thailand. Evidence shows that these dams and groupings of dams are affecting fish migrations, river hydrology, and sediment transfers. Such changes are negatively impacting riparian communities up to 1000 km away. Because many communities depend on the river and its resources for their food and livelihood, changes to the river have impacted, and will continue to negatively impact, food and economic security. While social and environmental impact assessments have been carried out for these projects, greater consideration of the scale and cumulative impacts of dams is necessary.","container-title":"Water","DOI":"10.3390/w13030265","ISSN":"2073-4441","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Water","language":"en","page":"265","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin: A Review","title-short":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/13/3/265","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Soukhaphon","given":"Akarath"},{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G."},{"family":"Hogan","given":"Zeb S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Its substantial geographic area, vast amount of biodiversity, and connection to local communities makes the Mekong one of the most important freshwater aquatic systems in the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"E0aK6nWk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Souza & Parlee, 2020; Soukhaphon, 2021)","plainCitation":"(D’Souza & Parlee, 2020; Soukhaphon, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E23JKHF9"],"itemData":{"id":811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 18\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"7438","title":"Fishing Livelihoods and Diversifications in the Mekong River Basin in the Context of the Pak Mun Dam, Thailand","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"D’Souza","given":"Amabel"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":838,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WYNHXLIZ"],"itemData":{"id":838,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Mekong River, well known for its aquatic biodiversity, is important to the social, physical, and economic health of millions living in China, Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia, and Vietnam. This paper explores the social and environmental impacts of several Mekong basin hydropower dams and groupings of dams and the geographies of their impacts. Specifically, we examined the 3S (Sesan, Sekong Srepok) river system in northeastern Cambodia, the Central Highlands of Vietnam, and southern Laos; the Khone Falls area in southern Laos; the lower Mun River Basin in northeastern Thailand; and the upper Mekong River in Yunnan Province, China, northeastern Myanmar, northern Laos, and northern Thailand. Evidence shows that these dams and groupings of dams are affecting fish migrations, river hydrology, and sediment transfers. Such changes are negatively impacting riparian communities up to 1000 km away. Because many communities depend on the river and its resources for their food and livelihood, changes to the river have impacted, and will continue to negatively impact, food and economic security. While social and environmental impact assessments have been carried out for these projects, greater consideration of the scale and cumulative impacts of dams is necessary.","container-title":"Water","DOI":"10.3390/w13030265","ISSN":"2073-4441","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Water","language":"en","page":"265","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin: A Review","title-short":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/13/3/265","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Soukhaphon","given":"Akarath"},{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G."},{"family":"Hogan","given":"Zeb S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although purported as a source of clean energy, rapid development of ongoing and proposed hydroelectric dams is adversely impacting local fishing communities and is a major driver of change for fishing practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kLqFPzd6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ziegler, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Ziegler, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":814,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NUP7SVCK"],"itemData":{"id":814,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS Neglected Tropical Diseases","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pntd.0002166","ISSN":"1935-2735","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS Negl Trop Dis","language":"en","page":"e2166","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Dams and Disease Triggers on the Lower Mekong River","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pntd.0002166","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Ziegler","given":"Alan D."},{"family":"Petney","given":"Trevor N."},{"family":"Grundy-Warr","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Andrews","given":"Ross H."},{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G."},{"family":"Wasson","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Sithithaworn","given":"Paiboon"}],"editor":[{"family":"Zhou","given":"Xiao-Nong"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",6,13]]}}},{"id":874,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PNMBJ9IP"],"itemData":{"id":874,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"National science review","ISSN":"2095-5138","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"National science review","note":"number: 9\\npublisher: Oxford University Press","page":"1449-1457","title":"Hydropower reservoirs on the upper Mekong River modify nutrient bioavailability downstream","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Chen","given":"Qiuwen"},{"family":"Shi","given":"Wenqing"},{"family":"Huisman","given":"Jef"},{"family":"Maberly","given":"Stephen C"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Jianyun"},{"family":"Yu","given":"Juhua"},{"family":"Chen","given":"Yuchen"},{"family":"Tonina","given":"Daniele"},{"family":"Yi","given":"Qitao"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E23JKHF9"],"itemData":{"id":811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 18\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"7438","title":"Fishing Livelihoods and Diversifications in the Mekong River Basin in the Context of the Pak Mun Dam, Thailand","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"D’Souza","given":"Amabel"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hydroelectric development complicates traditional practices, and, as a result, local people have had to cope with significant stressed related to loss of livelihood and inability to engage in traditional practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ID8CQ7MN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. Baird, 2020; D\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Souza & Parlee, 2020; Soukhaphon, 2021)","plainCitation":"(I. Baird, 2020; D’Souza & Parlee, 2020; Soukhaphon, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26044,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J7CJXY9B","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LHQS9LKW"],"itemData":{"id":26044,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Water Alternatives","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Water Alternatives","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Water Alternatives Association","page":"141-159","title":"Opening the gates of the Pak Mun Dam: Fish migrations, domestic water supply, irrigation projects and politics","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G"},{"family":"Manorom","given":"Kanokwan"},{"family":"Phenow","given":"Aurore"},{"family":"Gaja-Svasti","given":"Sirasak"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E23JKHF9"],"itemData":{"id":811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 18\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"7438","title":"Fishing Livelihoods and Diversifications in the Mekong River Basin in the Context of the Pak Mun Dam, Thailand","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"D’Souza","given":"Amabel"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":838,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WYNHXLIZ"],"itemData":{"id":838,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Mekong River, well known for its aquatic biodiversity, is important to the social, physical, and economic health of millions living in China, Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia, and Vietnam. This paper explores the social and environmental impacts of several Mekong basin hydropower dams and groupings of dams and the geographies of their impacts. Specifically, we examined the 3S (Sesan, Sekong Srepok) river system in northeastern Cambodia, the Central Highlands of Vietnam, and southern Laos; the Khone Falls area in southern Laos; the lower Mun River Basin in northeastern Thailand; and the upper Mekong River in Yunnan Province, China, northeastern Myanmar, northern Laos, and northern Thailand. Evidence shows that these dams and groupings of dams are affecting fish migrations, river hydrology, and sediment transfers. Such changes are negatively impacting riparian communities up to 1000 km away. Because many communities depend on the river and its resources for their food and livelihood, changes to the river have impacted, and will continue to negatively impact, food and economic security. While social and environmental impact assessments have been carried out for these projects, greater consideration of the scale and cumulative impacts of dams is necessary.","container-title":"Water","DOI":"10.3390/w13030265","ISSN":"2073-4441","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Water","language":"en","page":"265","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin: A Review","title-short":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/13/3/265","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Soukhaphon","given":"Akarath"},{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G."},{"family":"Hogan","given":"Zeb S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Currently, over 65 million people live near or along the Mekong river, and thus, the cumulative impacts of hydropower affect communities for decades after their completion ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7lWPCf7l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Soukhaphon, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Soukhaphon, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":838,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WYNHXLIZ"],"itemData":{"id":838,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Mekong River, well known for its aquatic biodiversity, is important to the social, physical, and economic health of millions living in China, Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia, and Vietnam. This paper explores the social and environmental impacts of several Mekong basin hydropower dams and groupings of dams and the geographies of their impacts. Specifically, we examined the 3S (Sesan, Sekong Srepok) river system in northeastern Cambodia, the Central Highlands of Vietnam, and southern Laos; the Khone Falls area in southern Laos; the lower Mun River Basin in northeastern Thailand; and the upper Mekong River in Yunnan Province, China, northeastern Myanmar, northern Laos, and northern Thailand. Evidence shows that these dams and groupings of dams are affecting fish migrations, river hydrology, and sediment transfers. Such changes are negatively impacting riparian communities up to 1000 km away. Because many communities depend on the river and its resources for their food and livelihood, changes to the river have impacted, and will continue to negatively impact, food and economic security. While social and environmental impact assessments have been carried out for these projects, greater consideration of the scale and cumulative impacts of dams is necessary.","container-title":"Water","DOI":"10.3390/w13030265","ISSN":"2073-4441","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Water","language":"en","page":"265","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin: A Review","title-short":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/13/3/265","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Soukhaphon","given":"Akarath"},{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G."},{"family":"Hogan","given":"Zeb S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      While hydropower dams exist throughout the Mekong basin, there are a significant number in the Lower Mekong Basin. Since the 1990s, 64 dams have been constructed in the Mekong basin, 46 of which are in the Lower Mekong Basin ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EnkgPIuh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(W. N. Green and Baird 2020; Yoshida 2020)","plainCitation":"(W. N. Green and Baird 2020; Yoshida 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":839,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NQGVWZC5"],"itemData":{"id":839,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Political Geography","DOI":"10.1016/j.polgeo.2020.102272","ISSN":"09626298","journalAbbreviation":"Political Geography","language":"en","page":"102272","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The contentious politics of hydropower dam impact assessments in the Mekong River basin","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0962629820303358","volume":"83","author":[{"family":"Green","given":"W. Nathan"},{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",11]]}}},{"id":32546,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZRZF8JX5"],"itemData":{"id":32546,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The riverine ecosystems of the Mekong River Basin possess the world’s most productive inland fishery and provide highly productive food crops for millions of people annually. The development of hydropower potential in the Mekong River has long been of interest to governments in the region. Among the existing 64 dams, 46 dams have been built in the Lower Mekong Basin (LMB) to produce up to 8650 MW of electricity. Additionally, of the 123 proposed built hydropower dams, eleven hydropower plants have been nominated for the river mainstream and are expected to install a total of 13,000 MW in the LMB countries. However, serious concerns have intensified over the potential negative economic consequences, especially on fisheries and agriculture in Cambodia and Vietnam. To date, most of the concerns have concentrated on the impacts on hydrology, environment, livelihood, and diversity in the LMB attributed to hydropower development. This paper, however, discusses the fishery and agricultural sectors of the LMB and focuses on the downstream floodplains of Cambodia and Vietnam. The dam construction has caused greater losses of biodiversity and fisheries than climate change in the LMB. The reduction of 276,847 and 178,169 t of fish, 3.7% and 2.3% of rice, 21.0% and 10.0% of maize will contribute to a decrease of 3.7% and 0.3% of the GDP of Cambodia and Vietnam, respectively. Lao PDR may benefit the most revenue from electricity generation than the other country in the LMB, as most of the proposed dams are projected in the country. Cambodia burdens 3/4 of the reduction of total capture fishery destruction, whilst Lao PDR, Thailand, and Vietnam endure the remaining 1/3 losses. The tradeoff analyses reveal that losses of capture fisheries, sediment or nutrients, and social mitigation costs are greater than the benefits from electricity generation, improved irrigation, and flood control of the LMB region. The socioeconomic and environmental damage caused by hydropower dams in devel","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"6","page":"1-21","title":"Impacts of Mainstream Hydropower Dams on Fisheries and Agriculture in Lower Mekong Basin","URL":"https://ideas.repec.org/a/gam/jsusta/v12y2020i6p2408-d334358.html","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Yoshida","given":"Yuichiro"},{"family":"Lee","given":"Han Soo"},{"family":"Trung","given":"Bui Huy"},{"family":"Tran","given":"Hoang-Dung"},{"family":"Lall","given":"Mahrjan Keshlav"},{"family":"Kakar","given":"Kifayatullah"},{"family":"Xuan","given":"Tran Dang"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Additionally, there are 123 dams that are projected to be built in the Lower Mekong Basin in the future ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GC5rHgeF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yoshida, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Yoshida, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32546,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZRZF8JX5"],"itemData":{"id":32546,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The riverine ecosystems of the Mekong River Basin possess the world’s most productive inland fishery and provide highly productive food crops for millions of people annually. The development of hydropower potential in the Mekong River has long been of interest to governments in the region. Among the existing 64 dams, 46 dams have been built in the Lower Mekong Basin (LMB) to produce up to 8650 MW of electricity. Additionally, of the 123 proposed built hydropower dams, eleven hydropower plants have been nominated for the river mainstream and are expected to install a total of 13,000 MW in the LMB countries. However, serious concerns have intensified over the potential negative economic consequences, especially on fisheries and agriculture in Cambodia and Vietnam. To date, most of the concerns have concentrated on the impacts on hydrology, environment, livelihood, and diversity in the LMB attributed to hydropower development. This paper, however, discusses the fishery and agricultural sectors of the LMB and focuses on the downstream floodplains of Cambodia and Vietnam. The dam construction has caused greater losses of biodiversity and fisheries than climate change in the LMB. The reduction of 276,847 and 178,169 t of fish, 3.7% and 2.3% of rice, 21.0% and 10.0% of maize will contribute to a decrease of 3.7% and 0.3% of the GDP of Cambodia and Vietnam, respectively. Lao PDR may benefit the most revenue from electricity generation than the other country in the LMB, as most of the proposed dams are projected in the country. Cambodia burdens 3/4 of the reduction of total capture fishery destruction, whilst Lao PDR, Thailand, and Vietnam endure the remaining 1/3 losses. The tradeoff analyses reveal that losses of capture fisheries, sediment or nutrients, and social mitigation costs are greater than the benefits from electricity generation, improved irrigation, and flood control of the LMB region. The socioeconomic and environmental damage caused by hydropower dams in devel","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"6","page":"1-21","title":"Impacts of Mainstream Hydropower Dams on Fisheries and Agriculture in Lower Mekong Basin","URL":"https://ideas.repec.org/a/gam/jsusta/v12y2020i6p2408-d334358.html","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Yoshida","given":"Yuichiro"},{"family":"Lee","given":"Han Soo"},{"family":"Trung","given":"Bui Huy"},{"family":"Tran","given":"Hoang-Dung"},{"family":"Lall","given":"Mahrjan Keshlav"},{"family":"Kakar","given":"Kifayatullah"},{"family":"Xuan","given":"Tran Dang"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. One of the most controversial dams in this area is the Pak Mun Dam, a “run-of-the-river” hydroelectric dam that is situated at the confluence of the Mun and Mekong rivers in Northeastern Thailand ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"N3xnycgl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. G. Baird 2021; Amornsakchai, S., Annez, P., Vongvisessomjai, S., Choowaew, S. Thailand Development Research Institute (TDRI), Kunurat, P., Nippanon, J., Schouten, R., Sripapatrprasite, P., Vaddhanaphuti, C., Vidthayanon, C., Wirojanagud, W. & Watana, E. 2000; Soukhaphon, Baird, and Hogan 2021)","plainCitation":"(I. G. Baird 2021; Amornsakchai, S., Annez, P., Vongvisessomjai, S., Choowaew, S. Thailand Development Research Institute (TDRI), Kunurat, P., Nippanon, J., Schouten, R., Sripapatrprasite, P., Vaddhanaphuti, C., Vidthayanon, C., Wirojanagud, W. & Watana, E. 2000; Soukhaphon, Baird, and Hogan 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":853,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KJBMTFCV"],"itemData":{"id":853,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-020-01418-x","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Downstream Impacts of Hydropower Dams and Indigenous and Local Knowledge: Examples from the Peace–Athabasca, Mekong, and Amazon","title-short":"The Downstream Impacts of Hydropower Dams and Indigenous and Local Knowledge","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-020-01418-x","author":[{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato A. M."},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"},{"family":"Poesch","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Maclean","given":"Bruce"},{"family":"Napoleon","given":"Art"},{"family":"Lepine","given":"Melody"},{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,18]]}}},{"id":164,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TDZTJII2"],"itemData":{"id":164,"type":"document","title":"Pak Mun Dam, Mekong River Basin, Thailand. A WCD Case Study prepared as an input to the World Commission on Dams, Cape Town. http://www2.centre-cired.fr/IMG/pdf/F8_PakMunDam.pdf","author":[{"literal":"Amornsakchai, S., Annez, P., Vongvisessomjai, S., Choowaew, S. Thailand Development Research Institute (TDRI), Kunurat, P., Nippanon, J., Schouten, R., Sripapatrprasite, P., Vaddhanaphuti, C., Vidthayanon, C., Wirojanagud, W. & Watana, E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":838,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WYNHXLIZ"],"itemData":{"id":838,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Mekong River, well known for its aquatic biodiversity, is important to the social, physical, and economic health of millions living in China, Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia, and Vietnam. This paper explores the social and environmental impacts of several Mekong basin hydropower dams and groupings of dams and the geographies of their impacts. Specifically, we examined the 3S (Sesan, Sekong Srepok) river system in northeastern Cambodia, the Central Highlands of Vietnam, and southern Laos; the Khone Falls area in southern Laos; the lower Mun River Basin in northeastern Thailand; and the upper Mekong River in Yunnan Province, China, northeastern Myanmar, northern Laos, and northern Thailand. Evidence shows that these dams and groupings of dams are affecting fish migrations, river hydrology, and sediment transfers. Such changes are negatively impacting riparian communities up to 1000 km away. Because many communities depend on the river and its resources for their food and livelihood, changes to the river have impacted, and will continue to negatively impact, food and economic security. While social and environmental impact assessments have been carried out for these projects, greater consideration of the scale and cumulative impacts of dams is necessary.","container-title":"Water","DOI":"10.3390/w13030265","ISSN":"2073-4441","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Water","language":"en","page":"265","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin: A Review","title-short":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/13/3/265","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Soukhaphon","given":"Akarath"},{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G."},{"family":"Hogan","given":"Zeb S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Since 1989, the Pak Mun Dam has caused contention and conflict between local communities and the Electrical Generating Authority of Thailand ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8mEE0wCW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Amornsakchai, S., Annez, P., Vongvisessomjai, S., Choowaew, S. Thailand Development Research Institute (TDRI), Kunurat, P., Nippanon, J., Schouten, R., Sripapatrprasite, P., Vaddhanaphuti, C., Vidthayanon, C., Wirojanagud, W. & Watana, E. 2000)","plainCitation":"(Amornsakchai, S., Annez, P., Vongvisessomjai, S., Choowaew, S. Thailand Development Research Institute (TDRI), Kunurat, P., Nippanon, J., Schouten, R., Sripapatrprasite, P., Vaddhanaphuti, C., Vidthayanon, C., Wirojanagud, W. & Watana, E. 2000)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":164,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TDZTJII2"],"itemData":{"id":164,"type":"document","title":"Pak Mun Dam, Mekong River Basin, Thailand. A WCD Case Study prepared as an input to the World Commission on Dams, Cape Town. http://www2.centre-cired.fr/IMG/pdf/F8_PakMunDam.pdf","author":[{"literal":"Amornsakchai, S., Annez, P., Vongvisessomjai, S., Choowaew, S. Thailand Development Research Institute (TDRI), Kunurat, P., Nippanon, J., Schouten, R., Sripapatrprasite, P., Vaddhanaphuti, C., Vidthayanon, C., Wirojanagud, W. & Watana, E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Its location causes major impacts to upstream and downstream communities, including those beyond Thailand, in Lao, Cambodia, and Vietnam ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wlnSSUz0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Soukhaphon, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Soukhaphon, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":838,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WYNHXLIZ"],"itemData":{"id":838,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Mekong River, well known for its aquatic biodiversity, is important to the social, physical, and economic health of millions living in China, Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia, and Vietnam. This paper explores the social and environmental impacts of several Mekong basin hydropower dams and groupings of dams and the geographies of their impacts. Specifically, we examined the 3S (Sesan, Sekong Srepok) river system in northeastern Cambodia, the Central Highlands of Vietnam, and southern Laos; the Khone Falls area in southern Laos; the lower Mun River Basin in northeastern Thailand; and the upper Mekong River in Yunnan Province, China, northeastern Myanmar, northern Laos, and northern Thailand. Evidence shows that these dams and groupings of dams are affecting fish migrations, river hydrology, and sediment transfers. Such changes are negatively impacting riparian communities up to 1000 km away. Because many communities depend on the river and its resources for their food and livelihood, changes to the river have impacted, and will continue to negatively impact, food and economic security. While social and environmental impact assessments have been carried out for these projects, greater consideration of the scale and cumulative impacts of dams is necessary.","container-title":"Water","DOI":"10.3390/w13030265","ISSN":"2073-4441","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Water","language":"en","page":"265","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin: A Review","title-short":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/13/3/265","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Soukhaphon","given":"Akarath"},{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G."},{"family":"Hogan","given":"Zeb S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For communities that utilize the river, this dam and its resulting embankment has caused both environmental and socioeconomic issues such as decreases in fish migration, flooding, increase in water level, increased reliance on store bought food, loss of traditional community practices, and more ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EuO9hng3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. Baird, 2020; Foran and Manorom, 2009; K. Roberts, 2016; Soukhaphon, 2021)","plainCitation":"(I. Baird, 2020; Foran and Manorom, 2009; K. Roberts, 2016; Soukhaphon, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26044,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J7CJXY9B","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LHQS9LKW"],"itemData":{"id":26044,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Water Alternatives","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Water Alternatives","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Water Alternatives Association","page":"141-159","title":"Opening the gates of the Pak Mun Dam: Fish migrations, domestic water supply, irrigation projects and politics","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G"},{"family":"Manorom","given":"Kanokwan"},{"family":"Phenow","given":"Aurore"},{"family":"Gaja-Svasti","given":"Sirasak"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32561,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DFE84AYB"],"itemData":{"id":32561,"type":"chapter","title":"Contested waterscapes in the Mekong region: Hydropower, livelihoods and governance. London, UK: Earthscan: 55-80","author":[{"literal":"Foran and Manorom"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":840,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YK5VTIKJ"],"itemData":{"id":840,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Austrian Journal of South-East Asian Studies","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"53","title":"It Takes a Rooted Village: Networked Resistance, Connected Communities, and Adaptive Responses to Forest Tenure Reform in Northern Thailand","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Roberts","given":"Kimberly"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":838,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WYNHXLIZ"],"itemData":{"id":838,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Mekong River, well known for its aquatic biodiversity, is important to the social, physical, and economic health of millions living in China, Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia, and Vietnam. This paper explores the social and environmental impacts of several Mekong basin hydropower dams and groupings of dams and the geographies of their impacts. Specifically, we examined the 3S (Sesan, Sekong Srepok) river system in northeastern Cambodia, the Central Highlands of Vietnam, and southern Laos; the Khone Falls area in southern Laos; the lower Mun River Basin in northeastern Thailand; and the upper Mekong River in Yunnan Province, China, northeastern Myanmar, northern Laos, and northern Thailand. Evidence shows that these dams and groupings of dams are affecting fish migrations, river hydrology, and sediment transfers. Such changes are negatively impacting riparian communities up to 1000 km away. Because many communities depend on the river and its resources for their food and livelihood, changes to the river have impacted, and will continue to negatively impact, food and economic security. While social and environmental impact assessments have been carried out for these projects, greater consideration of the scale and cumulative impacts of dams is necessary.","container-title":"Water","DOI":"10.3390/w13030265","ISSN":"2073-4441","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Water","language":"en","page":"265","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin: A Review","title-short":"The Impacts of Hydropower Dams in the Mekong River Basin","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/13/3/265","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Soukhaphon","given":"Akarath"},{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G."},{"family":"Hogan","given":"Zeb S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These negative and serious impacts mean that fishing is no longer a sustainable practice and to cope, communities have had to diversify their livelihoods over the past thirty years. 
      Diversifications of fishing practices include more innovation to fishing gear, including some local community member who use recycled material to create fish and shrimp traps ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ysnZWEPf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Souza & Parlee, 2020)","plainCitation":"(D’Souza & Parlee, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E23JKHF9"],"itemData":{"id":811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 18\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"7438","title":"Fishing Livelihoods and Diversifications in the Mekong River Basin in the Context of the Pak Mun Dam, Thailand","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"D’Souza","given":"Amabel"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Additionally, some community member takes advantage of fibreglass boatmaking programs initiated by their Tessabaan (Municipal Government) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"g0vUfJBv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Souza & Parlee, 2020)","plainCitation":"(D’Souza & Parlee, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E23JKHF9"],"itemData":{"id":811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 18\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"7438","title":"Fishing Livelihoods and Diversifications in the Mekong River Basin in the Context of the Pak Mun Dam, Thailand","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"D’Souza","given":"Amabel"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Tessabaan also offers programs that teach people how to raise Tilapia in personal fishponds ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iY7TmlXV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Souza & Parlee, 2020)","plainCitation":"(D’Souza & Parlee, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E23JKHF9"],"itemData":{"id":811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 18\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"7438","title":"Fishing Livelihoods and Diversifications in the Mekong River Basin in the Context of the Pak Mun Dam, Thailand","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"D’Souza","given":"Amabel"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These personal fishponds offer a way for community members to feed their families while also generating income by selling fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Xr5ImJHy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Souza & Parlee, 2020)","plainCitation":"(D’Souza & Parlee, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E23JKHF9"],"itemData":{"id":811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 18\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"7438","title":"Fishing Livelihoods and Diversifications in the Mekong River Basin in the Context of the Pak Mun Dam, Thailand","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"D’Souza","given":"Amabel"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Selling fish persists and is often preferable, but sharing fish is decreasing, as people cannot afford to share the little catch they have ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UlVggx7l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Souza & Parlee, 2020)","plainCitation":"(D’Souza & Parlee, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E23JKHF9"],"itemData":{"id":811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 18\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"7438","title":"Fishing Livelihoods and Diversifications in the Mekong River Basin in the Context of the Pak Mun Dam, Thailand","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"D’Souza","given":"Amabel"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For upstream communities, such as those along the Sebok River, there may be even less sharing of fishing gear as people wish to preserve their store-bought gear ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1JLHGzOu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Souza & Parlee, 2020)","plainCitation":"(D’Souza & Parlee, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E23JKHF9"],"itemData":{"id":811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 18\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"7438","title":"Fishing Livelihoods and Diversifications in the Mekong River Basin in the Context of the Pak Mun Dam, Thailand","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"D’Souza","given":"Amabel"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KT8aLKTk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Souza & Parlee, 2020)","plainCitation":"(D’Souza & Parlee, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E23JKHF9"],"itemData":{"id":811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 18\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"7438","title":"Fishing Livelihoods and Diversifications in the Mekong River Basin in the Context of the Pak Mun Dam, Thailand","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"D’Souza","given":"Amabel"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} outline common diversification of both upstream and downstream, including preferring to sell their fish over sharing or eating, more reliance on store-bought fish, and wanting more fish stocking from the government. A major shift in perception of fishing livelihood has also occurred, as community members prefer to diversify outside of fishing, including the common practice of farming, mobile markets, and rural-to-urban migration ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"C9qTAZID","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Souza & Parlee, 2020)","plainCitation":"(D’Souza & Parlee, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E23JKHF9"],"itemData":{"id":811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 18\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"7438","title":"Fishing Livelihoods and Diversifications in the Mekong River Basin in the Context of the Pak Mun Dam, Thailand","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"D’Souza","given":"Amabel"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Broader drivers of change have led to more focus on education and children supporting their grandparents by sending money back to their villages ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rg6UqLkB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Souza & Parlee, 2020)","plainCitation":"(D’Souza & Parlee, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E23JKHF9"],"itemData":{"id":811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 18\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"7438","title":"Fishing Livelihoods and Diversifications in the Mekong River Basin in the Context of the Pak Mun Dam, Thailand","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"D’Souza","given":"Amabel"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Local people often must engage in multiple diversifications at once to cope with stressors related to the Pak Mun Dam. 
      However, although community members can and have coped with the longitudinal impacts of the Pak Mun Dam, they wish to return to their previous lives. Since the implementation of the Pak Mun Dam, it’s decommission has been widely recommended by community members, scholars, and non-profits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"86yJqBYT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. Baird 2020)","plainCitation":"(I. Baird 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26044,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J7CJXY9B","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LHQS9LKW"],"itemData":{"id":26044,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Water Alternatives","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Water Alternatives","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Water Alternatives Association","page":"141-159","title":"Opening the gates of the Pak Mun Dam: Fish migrations, domestic water supply, irrigation projects and politics","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G"},{"family":"Manorom","given":"Kanokwan"},{"family":"Phenow","given":"Aurore"},{"family":"Gaja-Svasti","given":"Sirasak"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The opening of the floodgates year-round, or for an extended time period during the rainy season, is mentioned frequently as a way to help restore the environment to its original state, including bringing back the fish populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"M6V2gGuP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. Baird, 2020; D\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Souza & Parlee, 2020; Foran and Manorom, 2009)","plainCitation":"(I. Baird, 2020; D’Souza & Parlee, 2020; Foran and Manorom, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26044,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J7CJXY9B","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LHQS9LKW"],"itemData":{"id":26044,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Water Alternatives","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Water Alternatives","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Water Alternatives Association","page":"141-159","title":"Opening the gates of the Pak Mun Dam: Fish migrations, domestic water supply, irrigation projects and politics","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G"},{"family":"Manorom","given":"Kanokwan"},{"family":"Phenow","given":"Aurore"},{"family":"Gaja-Svasti","given":"Sirasak"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E23JKHF9"],"itemData":{"id":811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 18\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"7438","title":"Fishing Livelihoods and Diversifications in the Mekong River Basin in the Context of the Pak Mun Dam, Thailand","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"D’Souza","given":"Amabel"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32561,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DFE84AYB"],"itemData":{"id":32561,"type":"chapter","title":"Contested waterscapes in the Mekong region: Hydropower, livelihoods and governance. London, UK: Earthscan: 55-80","author":[{"literal":"Foran and Manorom"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is clear that local community members are adversely affected and struggle with the long-term impacts of the Pak Mun Dam on their fishing livelihoods. As more hydroelectric development is projected to take place in the region, including the highly contested, but not yet constructed Sambor dam, more research on hydroelectric development as a driver of change for communities in the region is needed.

      Conventions and treaties related to the management of forest ecosystems and regulation of use are more common in some areas than others. 
      
Box 4.7. Treaty on the Conservation and Sustainable Management of Forest Ecosystems in Central Africa and to establish the Central African Forests Commission (2005) 

The 1999 Yaoundé Declaration recognized the protection of the Congo Basin's ecosystems as an integral component of development processes and reaffirmed commitments to work cooperatively to promote the sustainable use of the Congo ecosystem in accordance with their social, economic, and environmental agendas. The Declaration led to the formalization of commitments to protect the Congo Basin's ecosystems in a 2005 Treaty that legally recognizes the Central African Forests Commission as the decision-making body on forests. Representatives from all the governments have met regularly to discuss an agenda and develop a Convergence Plan (2003-2010) that identifies priorities under the themes of: harmonization of forest policy and taxation, inventory of flora and fauna, ecosystem management, conservation of biodiversity, sustainable use of natural resources, capacity building and community participation, research, innovative financing mechanisms, and the convergence and harmonization of regulations including those concerning wild species use and management. Whilst regional level agreements have been slow to filter down to national level, the alignment focus has been important in raising awareness and simulating data collection (via for example the Central African Forest Observatory and annual State of the Congo Basin Forest reports) at national level, including on un-regulated wild species for which little data exists to inform policymakers. 


