      A critical issue in the usefulness of global agreements is how they are implemented and enforced within national borders. 
      
      4.2.2.2.3. National Laws and Policies 
Although sustainable use of wild species is a global issue, the nation-state has long been considered the answer to most governance problems, including those related to biodiversity conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"w0PaEAQE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sampford, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Sampford, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32599,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RY9Y869Z"],"itemData":{"id":32599,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science & Policy","ISSN":"1462-9011","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Science & Policy","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"79-90","title":"Environmental governance for biodiversity","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Sampford","given":"Charles"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Laws and policies impacting wild species include both those directly regulating species and products, and those indirectly doing so. Direct regulations (e.g., quotas, permitting, quality, safety and efficacy standards, trade restrictions, taxation) tend to exist when species are in commercial trade and governments seek to generate revenue or protect endangered species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZTfz50Xw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2010; Lele, 2010; Pierce & Burgener, 2010)","plainCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2010; Lele, 2010; Pierce & Burgener, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3K7YP5V"],"itemData":{"id":34331,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","number-of-pages":"393","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","title-short":"Wild product governance","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":34183,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BEJ75T5Y"],"itemData":{"id":34183,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Wid product Governance. Finding policies that work for non-timber forest products.","ISBN":"978-1-84407-560-3","page":"85-113","title":"NTFPs in India: Rhetoric and Reality In Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products.","URL":"https://books.google.fr/books?id=n8OUlIlKTq0C&pg=PR4&lpg=PR4&dq=978-1-84407-560-3&source=bl&ots=OHveSTqmZf&sig=ACfU3U3pcS0KW2MrmpqjXIaFgWhwmCconQ&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjA8tK9w832AhVGExoKHd1rDRUQ6AF6BAgCEAM#v=onepage&q=978-1-84407-560-3&f=false","author":[{"family":"Lele","given":"Sharachchandra"},{"family":"Pattanaik","given":"Manoj"},{"family":"Rai","given":"Nitin D."}],"editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"S. A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":34160,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LWRB7V9J"],"itemData":{"id":34160,"type":"chapter","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","container-title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","page":"327-342","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Laws and policies impacting trade in NTFPs","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"author":[{"family":"Pierce","given":"A."},{"family":"Burgener","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Laws and policies that indirectly impact wild species often have equal or greater impact on these species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c22Fuj5Q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cronkleton & Pacheco, 2010; Dewees & Scherr, 1996; Novellino, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Cronkleton & Pacheco, 2010; Dewees & Scherr, 1996; Novellino, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34156,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KGGDBC6R"],"itemData":{"id":34156,"type":"chapter","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","container-title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","page":"15-42","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Changing Policy Trends in the Emergence of Bolivia’s Brazil Nut Sector.","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"author":[{"family":"Cronkleton","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Pacheco","given":"Pablo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32598,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LPJ253DU"],"itemData":{"id":32598,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Available at SSRN 1292507","journalAbbreviation":"Available at SSRN 1292507","title":"Policies and markets for non-timber tree products","author":[{"family":"Dewees","given":"Peter A"},{"family":"Scherr","given":"Sara J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}},{"id":32591,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CTLRWX2T"],"itemData":{"id":32591,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","journalAbbreviation":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","note":"publisher: Earthscan","page":"183-98","title":"From Indigenous Customary Practices to Policy Interventions: The Ecolocal and Sociocultural Underpinnings of the NTFP Trade on Palawan Island, the Philippines","author":[{"family":"Novellino","given":"Dario"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and include agriculture, land tenure, taxation, labor, and broader natural resource laws ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Wqs80Es8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2010)","plainCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3K7YP5V"],"itemData":{"id":34331,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","number-of-pages":"393","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","title-short":"Wild product governance","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In most countries, permitting, harvest quotas, taxation, and restrictions on domestic and international trade are used by national governments to regulate the direct use of wild species. Some of the most common and well-established formal institutions at the national scale are related to the hunting and harvesting of large game.  

Box 4.8. Marine ecosystems and sustainable use in Indonesia – towards an
ecosystem approach to fisheries management 

Indonesia is the world's second largest producer of wild-capture ?sh, 60% is harvested by small- scale ?shers There are more than 2.62 million ?shers in the marine and inland capture sector in Indonesia, and the country is one of the most ?sh-dependent countries in the world.  The Indonesian government has been working with international organizations to establish marine protected areas to protect marine species including coral reefs. Tourism impacts on coral reefs are among those non-extractive uses which is increasing in scale and impact. What kinds of institutions government tourism impacts on coral reefs in Indonesia?  Fisheries management Law No. 31/ 2004 and Law No. 45/2009 regulates all matters concerning fisheries in Indonesia including all aspects of fishing activities, use, management and enforcement. These include: (i) specifying fishing method or gear; (ii) determining the maximum sustainable yield or total allowable catch for domestic and foreign fishing; (iii) specifying fishing and aquaculture activities; (iv) preventing activities such as pollution and destructive fishing of the resource and its ecosystems; and (v) rehabilitation of the resources and its habitat. Article 60 of Law No. 1/2014, amending Law No. 27/2007 also recognize the rights of traditional communities to cultural (traditional) fisheries harvesting practices. So, although there are no specific laws for Ecosystem Approach to Fisheries, this suite of laws together is consistent with the Ecosystem Approach to Fisheries which attempts to balance social and ecological values and needs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nh6WaUkW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Muawanah, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Muawanah, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32597,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IK3SFW3S"],"itemData":{"id":32597,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","ISSN":"0308-597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"150-160","title":"Review of national laws and regulation in Indonesia in relation to an ecosystem approach to fisheries management","volume":"91","author":[{"family":"Muawanah","given":"Umi"},{"family":"Yusuf","given":"Gellwynn"},{"family":"Adrianto","given":"Luky"},{"family":"Kalther","given":"Jimy"},{"family":"Pomeroy","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Abdullah","given":"Habibi"},{"family":"Ruchimat","given":"Toni"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Many national laws for example, deal with limiting the threat of over-harvest of species on public lands; they are premised on protecting the public interest in accessing game as for subsistence, recreational, and/or commercial value. For example, in many Central African countries, current legal and policy frameworks concentrate on a small number of generally traded, high-value wild species such as timber, “bushmeat” (i.e., wild species used for food with a focus on protected animal species), and a generally small proportion of traded, sometimes endangered plant species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EYDEyPzc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2010)","plainCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3K7YP5V"],"itemData":{"id":34331,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","number-of-pages":"393","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","title-short":"Wild product governance","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Lower value, species are generaly not included in these frameworks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"57TXTbTU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sola, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Sola, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":878,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2DX3D8TU"],"itemData":{"id":878,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bois et Forêts des Tropiques","language":"en","title":"Woodfuel policies and practices in selected countries in Sub-Saharan Africa—A critical review","author":[{"family":"Sola","given":"P."},{"family":"Schure","given":"J."},{"family":"Eba’a Atyi","given":"R."},{"family":"Gumbo","given":"D."},{"family":"Okeyo","given":"I."},{"family":"Awono","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. They include species important forfood, medicine and construction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7H6G0I67","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. Ingram & Schure, 2010; Lescuyer, 2016)","plainCitation":"(V. Ingram & Schure, 2010; Lescuyer, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":841,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8NJECQCU"],"itemData":{"id":841,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Atelier Sous-Regional sur«l’Harmonisation des Revues Nationales sur les Produits Forestiers Non Ligneux (PFNL) en Afrique Centrale","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Yaounde\\npublisher: CIFOR","title":"Review of Non Timber Forest Products (NTFPs) in Central Africa: Cameroon","author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Schure","given":"Jolien"}],"editor":[{"literal":"F.O.R.E.N.E.T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":850,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SZTFYKAN"],"itemData":{"id":850,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In Cameroon, sustainable timber management relies on the model of large logging concession. However, over the past fifteen years, small-scale logging has become a common activity, with two different forms. First, the creation of community forests in the late 1990s allowed village associations to legally harvest, process and trade timber, almost always with the support of external actors such as NGOs or private operators. Second, individual chainsaw milling, almost always informal, has grown considerably. The article compares the economic, social and environmental impacts of these two options of small-scale logging. Although much focus has been put on community forestry over the latest two decades, it remains a marginal activity with a turnover of less than € 2 million per year and a small impact on rural economies. Conversely, informal chainsaw milling represents an annual turnover of € 93 million, with a flow of revenues around € 30 million for the benefit of rural population. From an environmental perspective, none of the two options seems to substantially conserve or degrade forest resources, but more research is needed on the issue.","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554816819683744","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"40-51","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Contributions of community and individual small-scale logging to sustainable timber management in Cameroon","URL":"http://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/146554816819683744","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"G."},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"P.O."},{"family":"Tsanga","given":"R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",10,13]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These systems of sustainable harvest contribute greatly to community well-being in most countries and for most species.  Laws within nation states can operate in harmony together to support desired outcomes but are more often  adhoc and contradictory. The latter has been argued to be the case in Brazil, for example, which has been described as a “patchwork” of rules that are often weakly defined and enforced ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"skdjqRY6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Drummond & Barros\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Platiau, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Drummond & Barros?Platiau, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32590,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FTB8LTPE"],"itemData":{"id":32590,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Law & Policy","ISSN":"0265-8240","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Law & Policy","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"83-108","title":"Brazilian environmental laws and policies, 1934–2002: a critical overview","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Drummond","given":"José"},{"family":"Barros?Platiau","given":"Ana Flávia"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      These have been effective due to the flexible way in which these regulations are adapted to local and regional variabilities in ecosystems and species population, distribution, and health. In Arctic ecosystems in Canada, regional co-management boards are responsible for research and monitoring of caribou, muskoxen, bear, and other populations, and regularly update harvest limits to ensure sustainable use. This is also true in respect of Titi or Sooty Shearwater birds (Puffinus griseus) in New Zealand ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"itdg7epc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lyver, 2008; Moller, Charleton,, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Lyver, 2008; Moller, Charleton,, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":807,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EGJRHJY3"],"itemData":{"id":807,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"New Zealand Journal of Ecology","ISSN":"0110-6465","journalAbbreviation":"New Zealand Journal of Ecology","note":"publisher: JSTOR","page":"7-17","title":"T?hoe Tuawhenua m?tauranga of kerer? (Hemiphaga novaseelandiae novaseelandiae) in Te Urewera","author":[{"family":"Lyver","given":"Philip O'B"},{"family":"Taputu","given":"Te Motoi"},{"family":"Kutia","given":"Spady T"},{"family":"Tahi","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":809,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7DCQEM97"],"itemData":{"id":809,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"New Zealand Journal of Zoology","ISSN":"0301-4223","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"New Zealand Journal of Zoology","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"259-274","title":"Traditional ecological knowledge and scientific inference of prey availability: harvests of sooty shearwater (Puffinus griseus) chicks by Rakiura Maori","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Moller","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Charleton","given":"Kristin"},{"family":"Knight","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Lyver","given":"Phil"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and moose harvesting in Sweden and Norway ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"d9RdIKgW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QMVZSCN"],"itemData":{"id":32589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"109-130","title":"Status of moose populations and challenges to moose management in Fennoscandia","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Lavsund","given":"Sten"},{"family":"Nygrén","given":"Tuire"},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A key limitation of the effectiveness of national scale regulation of harvest and use of wild species is the degree to which these regulations are a fit with other kinds of institutional arrangements that are based on data from science and indigenous and local knowledge. Regulations and other kinds of institutional arrangements that are adaptive and flexible are particularly important in the case of wild species that are highly dynamic in distribution, migration, and population, or highly sensitive to environmental conditions (e.g., climate change) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QlRGiSMO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fikret Berkes 2018; Fikret Berkes, Colding, and Folke 2000)","plainCitation":"(Fikret Berkes 2018; Fikret Berkes, Colding, and Folke 2000)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":846,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UPCMNE8P"],"itemData":{"id":846,"type":"book","edition":"Fourth","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-1-138-07149-0","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Sacred Ecology","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":847,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4FYYZ8LU"],"itemData":{"id":847,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","DOI":"10.1890/1051-0761(2000)010[1251:ROTEKA]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"1051-0761","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Applications","language":"en","page":"1251-1262","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Rediscovery of Traditional Ecological Knowledge as Adaptive Management","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1890/1051-0761(2000)010[1251:ROTEKA]2.0.CO;2","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Colding","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, in the case of waterbirds, management (or a lack of appropriate management) in one part of the flyway, be it harvest or site management related, may have consequences for the status of a population throughout its range. An integrated approach and coordinated management is crucial (the waterbird harvest specialist group, Wetlands International, 2015) Weak harvest regulations and/or the absence of customary laws related to sustainable harvesting are particularly concerning where there is a high degree of variability or environmental stochasticity and where there is the absence of ongoing monitoring of population dynamics; in such cases even the most adaptive of regulations may be insufficient to prevent species decline ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FRBobF7q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. Brewin, 2007; Lichtenstein, 2002; Lichtenstein & Vil\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}, 2003a)","plainCitation":"(L. Brewin, 2007; Lichtenstein, 2002; Lichtenstein & Vilá, 2003a)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":877,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y7IGQXNB"],"itemData":{"id":877,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"The vicuña industry in Peru: Has the vicuña lived up to its reputation as the gold of the Andes? MSc Globalisation and Latin American Development, Institute for the Study of the Americas","author":[{"family":"Brewin","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":876,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6RECE82G"],"itemData":{"id":876,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Poverty","language":"pt","note":"ISBN: 1","title":"Manejo comunitario de vicuñas en Perú. Estudio de caso del manejo comunitario de vida silvestre","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"G."},{"family":"Oribe","given":"F."},{"family":"Grieg-Gran","given":"M."},{"family":"Mazzucchelli","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":24,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SYXFDIW","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RE4E67DM"],"itemData":{"id":24,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mountain Research and Development","DOI":"10.1659/0276-4741(2003)023[0197:VUBACA]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0276-4741, 1994-7151","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Mountain Research and Development","language":"en","page":"198-201","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Vicuna Use by Andean Communities: An Overview","title-short":"Vicuna Use by Andean Communities","URL":"http://www.bioone.org/doi/abs/10.1659/0276-4741%282003%29023%5B0197%3AVUBACA%5D2.0.CO%3B2","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Vilá","given":"Bibiana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      

Box 4.9.  Bat conservation in the Philippines and New Zealand 

Bats play an important role in ecosystems around the world. In particular, fruit bats are known pollinators and seed dispersers, meaning that declines in their populations have vast economic consequences in addition to ecological ones. The Philippine bare-backed fruit bat, Dobsonia chapmani, is a species of endangered bat native to the islands of Cebu and Negros and was believed to be extinct in the early 1970’s, only to be rediscovered in 2001 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1GRPw5vT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Raymundo & Caballes, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Raymundo & Caballes, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32596,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3XIIJBBC"],"itemData":{"id":32596,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","ISSN":"0278-0771","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnobiology","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: BioOne","page":"382-394","title":"An insight into bat hunter behavior and perception with implications for the conservation of the critically endangered Philippine bare-backed fruit bat","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Raymundo","given":"ML"},{"family":"Caballes","given":"CF"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The combination of “hunting, deforestation, guano mining, and a general lack of environmental awareness” are believed to have contributed to the steep decline in bare-backed fruit bat populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oLdIKJvE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Raymundo & Caballes, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Raymundo & Caballes, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32596,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3XIIJBBC"],"itemData":{"id":32596,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","ISSN":"0278-0771","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnobiology","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: BioOne","page":"382-394","title":"An insight into bat hunter behavior and perception with implications for the conservation of the critically endangered Philippine bare-backed fruit bat","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Raymundo","given":"ML"},{"family":"Caballes","given":"CF"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Through their research on the bat hunters in the Philippines,ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KcubWjDB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Raymundo & Caballes, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Raymundo & Caballes, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32596,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3XIIJBBC"],"itemData":{"id":32596,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","ISSN":"0278-0771","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnobiology","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: BioOne","page":"382-394","title":"An insight into bat hunter behavior and perception with implications for the conservation of the critically endangered Philippine bare-backed fruit bat","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Raymundo","given":"ML"},{"family":"Caballes","given":"CF"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found that majority of the local bat hunting was for subsistence. The two most common perceived drivers of bat population decline among hunters were large-scale hunting (and increasing numbers of hunters), and habitat destruction (from logging, agriculture, and forest burning for charcoal), with migration to favorable habitats and proximity to a reopened copper mine as less common drivers outlined. The main explanation for reliance on wild food, including bats, among local populations were found to be socioeconomic constraints. According to ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EWJfh2B3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Raymundo & Caballes, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Raymundo & Caballes, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32596,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3XIIJBBC"],"itemData":{"id":32596,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","ISSN":"0278-0771","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnobiology","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: BioOne","page":"382-394","title":"An insight into bat hunter behavior and perception with implications for the conservation of the critically endangered Philippine bare-backed fruit bat","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Raymundo","given":"ML"},{"family":"Caballes","given":"CF"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the Wildlife Resources Conservation and Protection Acts of 2001 in the Philippines has been successful in deterring some wild species hunting among individuals, but it is vague enough that multinational companies are still able to destroy habitats with little consequence, in part due to the shortage of government personnel trained in enforcing conservation laws. In New Zealand, there have been successful management strategies in conserving long-tailed bats, Chalinolobus tuberculatus, which were previously in decline ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0xrPhLwX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. J. Nelson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(N. J. Nelson, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32566,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B36K6SBT"],"itemData":{"id":32566,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of the Royal Society of New Zealand","ISSN":"0303-6758","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of the Royal Society of New Zealand","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"281-300","title":"The winners: species that have benefited from 30 years of conservation action","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Nelson","given":"Nicola J"},{"family":"Briskie","given":"James V"},{"family":"Constantine","given":"Rochelle"},{"family":"Monks","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Wallis","given":"Graham P"},{"family":"Watts","given":"Corinne"},{"family":"Wotton","given":"Debra M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Primary drivers of decline in bat species in New Zealand have been habitat loss and predation by invasive species. Conservation efforts included predator control of mustelids and rodents, which has protected native bats from invasive species. These conservation methods have, however, only been effective in protecting colonies and enabling their recovery where bat populations have not been threatened by habitat modification. In the Ellington Valley, a “strong collaboration between local conservation managers, field staff and scientists (that) involved the local community” has been key in the long-term protection of bat populations, and communities in some regions of New Zealand are being encouraged to engage with bat conservation through different local, government-led projects ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jt9P0IQ5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. J. Nelson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(N. J. Nelson, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32566,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B36K6SBT"],"itemData":{"id":32566,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of the Royal Society of New Zealand","ISSN":"0303-6758","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of the Royal Society of New Zealand","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"281-300","title":"The winners: species that have benefited from 30 years of conservation action","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Nelson","given":"Nicola J"},{"family":"Briskie","given":"James V"},{"family":"Constantine","given":"Rochelle"},{"family":"Monks","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Wallis","given":"Graham P"},{"family":"Watts","given":"Corinne"},{"family":"Wotton","given":"Debra M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9Tn5LgaZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. J. Nelson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(N. J. Nelson, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32566,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B36K6SBT"],"itemData":{"id":32566,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of the Royal Society of New Zealand","ISSN":"0303-6758","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of the Royal Society of New Zealand","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"281-300","title":"The winners: species that have benefited from 30 years of conservation action","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Nelson","given":"Nicola J"},{"family":"Briskie","given":"James V"},{"family":"Constantine","given":"Rochelle"},{"family":"Monks","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Wallis","given":"Graham P"},{"family":"Watts","given":"Corinne"},{"family":"Wotton","given":"Debra M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} highlight, long-tail bat populations require ongoing, active management in order to be sustainable, and active management may not be successful in areas with habitats that have been destructed. Only by addressing the socio-economic pressures that local populations in Cebu and Negros will effective hunting regulation and protection areas be possible ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pMtnf4RP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Raymundo & Caballes, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Raymundo & Caballes, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32596,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3XIIJBBC"],"itemData":{"id":32596,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","ISSN":"0278-0771","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnobiology","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: BioOne","page":"382-394","title":"An insight into bat hunter behavior and perception with implications for the conservation of the critically endangered Philippine bare-backed fruit bat","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Raymundo","given":"ML"},{"family":"Caballes","given":"CF"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Providing support for developing food security may help to reduce local reliance on bat populations for subsistence. Although the drivers of population decline in New Zealand do not include over-hunting, the significance of engagement of actors from multiple levels, including local community members, field scientists, and conservation managers in the effectiveness in conservation strategies of long-tail bats may prove to be applicable to the Philippine bare-back fruit bat. Although relocating bat populations has proven to be an ineffective conservation strategy (because of bats’ honing skills), the protection of further habitat destruction through the development of terrestrial protected areas may also serve to enable further conservation strategies. Although there may be economic pressures to continue to allow multi-national corporations to use land in unsustainable ways, strengthening restrictions on land use may help to limit further habitat destruction. 


Box 4.10. Weaving commercial and subsistence harvest of moose (Alces alces) in Scandinavia

Scandinavian moose are among the most productive and heavily harvested populations in the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yLdYkwIo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003; Wikenros, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003; Wikenros, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QMVZSCN"],"itemData":{"id":32589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"109-130","title":"Status of moose populations and challenges to moose management in Fennoscandia","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Lavsund","given":"Sten"},{"family":"Nygrén","given":"Tuire"},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":32571,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7B4M374X"],"itemData":{"id":32571,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Scientific Reports","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"1-11","title":"Impact of a recolonizing, cross-border carnivore population on ungulate harvest in Scandinavia","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Wikenros","given":"Camilla"},{"family":"Sand","given":"Håkan"},{"family":"Månsson","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Maartmann","given":"Erling"},{"family":"Eriksen","given":"Ane"},{"family":"Wabakken","given":"Petter"},{"family":"Zimmermann","given":"Barbara"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. From a total annual harvest less than 10,000 animals in the early 1900s, harvest levels increased rapidly in the 1970s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jKDiLFRh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cederlund & Bergstr\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}m, 1996; Lavsund, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Cederlund & Bergström, 1996; Lavsund, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32565,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JRFKE56G"],"itemData":{"id":32565,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Conservation of faunal diversity in forested landscapes","page":"265-281","publisher":"Springer","title":"Trends in the moose—forest system in Fennoscandia, with special reference to Sweden","author":[{"family":"Cederlund","given":"Göran"},{"family":"Bergström","given":"Roger"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}},{"id":32589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QMVZSCN"],"itemData":{"id":32589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"109-130","title":"Status of moose populations and challenges to moose management in Fennoscandia","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Lavsund","given":"Sten"},{"family":"Nygrén","given":"Tuire"},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The tremendous population growth of moose in Scandinavia can be explained by several factors including changing forestry practices, abandonment of marginal agricultural land, absence of predators, and changing moose harvesting practicesITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Fj3MmRuy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Browder, 1992; Lavsund, 2003; Ruusila & Kojola, 2010; Thulin, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Browder, 1992; Lavsund, 2003; Ruusila & Kojola, 2010; Thulin, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":849,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TE2VV3CP"],"itemData":{"id":849,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper challenges recent claims that \\"extractive reserves\\" offer a sustainable and economically competitive mode of tropical forest use. In contrast to the popular image of rubber tappers and other \\"extractive\\" populations living in prosperous harmony with their rain forest environment, ample evidence exists that most extractor households in Amazonia are poor, exploited by various forms of debtpeonage, and are fully capable of destroying commercial forest resources when pressed by circumstances threatening their survival. This paper examines several social and economic factors that limit the development and expansion of the \\"extractive reserves model\\" in Amazonia and urges policy makers to face the real challenge ahead, that of developing sustainable modes of production for the Amazon's dominant population group (farmers and ranchers), who are directly responsible for most of the tropical forest clearing in the region.","archive":"JSTOR","container-title":"Advances in Economic Botany","ISSN":"07418280","note":"publisher: New York Botanical Garden Press","page":"33-41","title":"Social and Economic Constraints on the Development of Market-Oriented Extractive Reserves in Amazon Rain Forests","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/43931387","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Browder","given":"John O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}},{"id":32589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QMVZSCN"],"itemData":{"id":32589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"109-130","title":"Status of moose populations and challenges to moose management in Fennoscandia","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Lavsund","given":"Sten"},{"family":"Nygrén","given":"Tuire"},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":32577,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S89STANX"],"itemData":{"id":32577,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"European ungulates and their management in the 21st century/(eds). Apollonio, Marco; Andersen, Reidar; Putman, Rory","ISSN":"0521760615","journalAbbreviation":"European ungulates and their management in the 21st century/(eds). Apollonio, Marco; Andersen, Reidar; Putman, Rory","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","title":"Ungulates and their management in Finland","author":[{"family":"Ruusila","given":"Vesa"},{"family":"Kojola","given":"Ilpo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32570,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YGS45UL6"],"itemData":{"id":32570,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"European journal of wildlife research","ISSN":"1439-0574","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"European journal of wildlife research","note":"number: 5\\npublisher: Springer","page":"649-656","title":"Opportunities and challenges with growing wildlife populations and zoonotic diseases in Sweden","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Thulin","given":"Carl-Gustaf"},{"family":"Malmsten","given":"Jonas"},{"family":"Ericsson","given":"Göran"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Among the most important drivers are state and local management policies and regulations designed to maintain viable populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AwloZYSx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sj\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}lander-Lindqvista & Sandstr\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}mb, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Sjölander-Lindqvista & Sandströmb, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32569,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XJTDAGCA"],"itemData":{"id":32569,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation & Society","ISSN":"0972-4923","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation & Society","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: JSTOR","page":"319-330","title":"Shaking Hands","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Sjölander-Lindqvista","given":"Annelie"},{"family":"Sandströmb","given":"Camilla"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Generally, these policies are rooted in the importance of moose as a game species and source of livelihood ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"L6aXy7O2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bj\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}rstig, 2014; Sandstr\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}m, 2013; Wikenros, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Bjärstig, 2014; Sandström, 2013; Wikenros, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32568,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7Q93GVSQ"],"itemData":{"id":32568,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science, Ecosystem Services & Management","ISSN":"2151-3732","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science, Ecosystem Services & Management","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"228-239","title":"Partnerships implementing ecosystem-based moose management in Sweden","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Bjärstig","given":"Therese"},{"family":"Sandström","given":"Camilla"},{"family":"Lindqvist","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Kvastegård","given":"Emma"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":32567,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SY7Q3BHT"],"itemData":{"id":32567,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","ISSN":"1875-0281","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of the Commons","note":"number: 2","title":"Conflict resolution through ecosystem-based management: the case of Swedish moose management","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Sandström","given":"Camilla"},{"family":"Di Gasper","given":"Sofia Wennberg"},{"family":"Öhman","given":"Karin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":32571,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7B4M374X"],"itemData":{"id":32571,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Scientific Reports","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"1-11","title":"Impact of a recolonizing, cross-border carnivore population on ungulate harvest in Scandinavia","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Wikenros","given":"Camilla"},{"family":"Sand","given":"Håkan"},{"family":"Månsson","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Maartmann","given":"Erling"},{"family":"Eriksen","given":"Ane"},{"family":"Wabakken","given":"Petter"},{"family":"Zimmermann","given":"Barbara"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The introduction of age- and sex-specific harvesting policies in the 1970s is assumed to be the most important factor in the present productivity of moose populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nHOsI2pv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QMVZSCN"],"itemData":{"id":32589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"109-130","title":"Status of moose populations and challenges to moose management in Fennoscandia","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Lavsund","given":"Sten"},{"family":"Nygrén","given":"Tuire"},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As a result of selecting for calves, yearlings and adult males, the proportion of productive females, mean age of females, and recruitment rate increased ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KwksnWen","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QMVZSCN"],"itemData":{"id":32589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"109-130","title":"Status of moose populations and challenges to moose management in Fennoscandia","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Lavsund","given":"Sten"},{"family":"Nygrén","given":"Tuire"},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, as moose population densities increased, so did impacts on commercial forests and transportation (e.g., moose-related traffic accidents) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dH9uUcnQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Selby, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Selby, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32576,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6WGKMV8A"],"itemData":{"id":32576,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"63-74","title":"Threats to the sustainability of moose management in Finland","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Selby","given":"Ashley"},{"family":"Petäjistö","given":"Leena"},{"family":"Koskela","given":"Terhi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Sweden, for example, the economic impact of moose browsing damage on forestry can exceed the economic and recreational values of moose hunting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3WpzLHxh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bj\\\\uc0\\\\u228{}rstig, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Bjärstig, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32568,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7Q93GVSQ"],"itemData":{"id":32568,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science, Ecosystem Services & Management","ISSN":"2151-3732","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science, Ecosystem Services & Management","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"228-239","title":"Partnerships implementing ecosystem-based moose management in Sweden","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Bjärstig","given":"Therese"},{"family":"Sandström","given":"Camilla"},{"family":"Lindqvist","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Kvastegård","given":"Emma"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Consequently, damage to economically important forests as well as moose-vehicle collisions play a central role in moose management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"koMK3qaP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QMVZSCN"],"itemData":{"id":32589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"109-130","title":"Status of moose populations and challenges to moose management in Fennoscandia","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Lavsund","given":"Sten"},{"family":"Nygrén","given":"Tuire"},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SnN0orDb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003; Linnell, 2020; Sandstr\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}m, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003; Linnell, 2020; Sandström, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QMVZSCN"],"itemData":{"id":32589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"109-130","title":"Status of moose populations and challenges to moose management in Fennoscandia","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Lavsund","given":"Sten"},{"family":"Nygrén","given":"Tuire"},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":865,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5NUMDZIY"],"itemData":{"id":865,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The cumulative impact of human activities has driven many species into severe declines across the globe. However, the recent focus on conservation optimism has begun to highlight case studies that go against this trend. Reforestation, agricultural abandonment, reintroduction and legislative change have led to a situation where large mammals have recovered and are now widespread across the European continent. This study summarizes the knowledge about wild ungulate distribution in Europe and review the diversity of ways in which they interact with humans. Drawn from a wide range of sources, we built distribution maps of European wild ungulates. Results show that 90% of Europe is home to at least 1 species of wild native ungulate, with roe deer and wild boar occupying 74% and 64% of Europe respectively. In contrast, wild native mountain ungulates only occupy 5% of Europe, and are often associated with protected areas. The wide distribution of most European ungulates combined with the extensive human activity within Europe result in a wide range of interactions between ungulates and humans. These interactions can be classified as services or disservices depending on the value orientation and economic position of the various stakeholders perceiving this relationship. Overall, our survey highlights the success of wildlife management policies in Europe and the potential for continental scale conservation of large mammals in human-dominated landscapes. However, maintaining the success of wild ungulate conservation requires actions from national and European institutions to improve coordinated management across jurisdictional borders and sectorial coordination for the whole landscape.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2020.108500","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"108500","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The challenges and opportunities of coexisting with wild ungulates in the human-dominated landscapes of Europe's Anthropocene","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320719318312","volume":"244","author":[{"family":"Linnell","given":"J D C"},{"family":"Cretois","given":"Benjamin"},{"family":"Nilsen","given":"Erlend B."},{"family":"Rolandsen","given":"Christer M."},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J."},{"family":"Veiberg","given":"Vebjørn"},{"family":"Kaczensky","given":"Petra"},{"family":"Van Moorter","given":"Bram"},{"family":"Panzacchi","given":"Manuela"},{"family":"Rauset","given":"Geir R."},{"family":"Kaltenborn","given":"Bjørn"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",4,1]]}}},{"id":32567,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SY7Q3BHT"],"itemData":{"id":32567,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","ISSN":"1875-0281","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of the Commons","note":"number: 2","title":"Conflict resolution through ecosystem-based management: the case of Swedish moose management","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Sandström","given":"Camilla"},{"family":"Di Gasper","given":"Sofia Wennberg"},{"family":"Öhman","given":"Karin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. One of the main moose management goals in Scandinavia is finding balance between a sustainable population for hunting and minimizing damage to forestry and public transportation (i.e., vehicle collisions) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fbrqRqlY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QMVZSCN"],"itemData":{"id":32589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"109-130","title":"Status of moose populations and challenges to moose management in Fennoscandia","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Lavsund","given":"Sten"},{"family":"Nygrén","given":"Tuire"},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"95O17Z8a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003; Selby, 2005; Storaas, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003; Selby, 2005; Storaas, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QMVZSCN"],"itemData":{"id":32589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"109-130","title":"Status of moose populations and challenges to moose management in Fennoscandia","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Lavsund","given":"Sten"},{"family":"Nygrén","given":"Tuire"},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":32576,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6WGKMV8A"],"itemData":{"id":32576,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"63-74","title":"Threats to the sustainability of moose management in Finland","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Selby","given":"Ashley"},{"family":"Petäjistö","given":"Leena"},{"family":"Koskela","given":"Terhi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":860,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9JFK5V75"],"itemData":{"id":860,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces","ISSN":"08355851","issue":"1","language":"English","note":"publisher: Alces","page":"97-108","source":"go.gale.com","title":"The economic value of moose in Norway--a review","URL":"https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=AONE&sw=w&issn=08355851&v=2.1&it=r&id=GALE%7CA92803197&sid=googleScholar&linkaccess=abs","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Storaas","given":"Torstein"},{"family":"Gundersen","given":"Hege"},{"family":"Henriksen","given":"Hege"},{"family":"Andreassen","given":"Harry P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Local authorities and stakeholders play a significant role in management efforts. In Sweden landowners and hunters have been delegated management responsibilities since the first Hunting Act in 1938 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UpwLTNeL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bal\\\\uc0\\\\u269{}iauskas, 2020; M. Johansson, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Bal?iauskas, 2020; M. Johansson, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32575,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HJGGYMW2"],"itemData":{"id":32575,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Transportation Research Part D: Transport and Environment","ISSN":"1361-9209","journalAbbreviation":"Transportation Research Part D: Transport and Environment","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"102493","title":"Importance of professional roadkill data in assessing diversity of mammal roadkills","volume":"87","author":[{"family":"Bal?iauskas","given":"Linas"},{"family":"Stratford","given":"Jos"},{"family":"Bal?iauskien?","given":"Laima"},{"family":"Ku?as","given":"Andrius"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32583,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5DLT4EIH"],"itemData":{"id":32583,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","ISSN":"1087-1209","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"154-170","title":"How stakeholder representatives cope with collaboration in the Swedish moose management system","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Johansson","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Dressel","given":"Sabrina"},{"family":"Ericsson","given":"Göran"},{"family":"Sjölander-Lindqvist","given":"Annelie"},{"family":"Sandström","given":"Camilla"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Norway, there is an increased emphasis on local population management plans developed by the landowner. While in Finland, moose hunting is managed by a central organization for hunters that is organized locally into game management districts and local-level associations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zGd00kkr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Selby, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Selby, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32576,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6WGKMV8A"],"itemData":{"id":32576,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"63-74","title":"Threats to the sustainability of moose management in Finland","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Selby","given":"Ashley"},{"family":"Petäjistö","given":"Leena"},{"family":"Koskela","given":"Terhi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Harvesting quotas are set based on data collected by hunters as well as vehicle collisions and damage to forestry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HKfcXFtw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Danielsen, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Danielsen, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32574,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CTH55XW9"],"itemData":{"id":32574,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","note":"number: 1","page":"55-60","title":"LOCAL COMMUNITY BASED MOOSE MANAGEMENT PLANTS IN NORWAY","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Danielsen","given":"Johan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Norway, for example, population indices based on local monitoring efforts are used as a tool in local moose management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RHFQGWcr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QMVZSCN"],"itemData":{"id":32589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"109-130","title":"Status of moose populations and challenges to moose management in Fennoscandia","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Lavsund","given":"Sten"},{"family":"Nygrén","given":"Tuire"},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While local management is a generally acceptable approach, it has not resolved conflict among hunting and forestry stakeholders ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QtNktF2Z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003; Sandstr\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}m, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Lavsund, 2003; Sandström, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QMVZSCN"],"itemData":{"id":32589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","ISSN":"2293-6629","journalAbbreviation":"Alces: A Journal Devoted to the Biology and Management of Moose","page":"109-130","title":"Status of moose populations and challenges to moose management in Fennoscandia","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Lavsund","given":"Sten"},{"family":"Nygrén","given":"Tuire"},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":32567,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SY7Q3BHT"],"itemData":{"id":32567,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","ISSN":"1875-0281","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of the Commons","note":"number: 2","title":"Conflict resolution through ecosystem-based management: the case of Swedish moose management","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Sandström","given":"Camilla"},{"family":"Di Gasper","given":"Sofia Wennberg"},{"family":"Öhman","given":"Karin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Competing interests and values continue to challenge the ability of stakeholders to establish trust and find mutually acceptable management solutions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4ezBhiP9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Johansson, 2020; Linnell, 2020)","plainCitation":"(M. Johansson, 2020; Linnell, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32583,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5DLT4EIH"],"itemData":{"id":32583,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","ISSN":"1087-1209","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"154-170","title":"How stakeholder representatives cope with collaboration in the Swedish moose management system","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Johansson","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Dressel","given":"Sabrina"},{"family":"Ericsson","given":"Göran"},{"family":"Sjölander-Lindqvist","given":"Annelie"},{"family":"Sandström","given":"Camilla"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":865,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5NUMDZIY"],"itemData":{"id":865,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The cumulative impact of human activities has driven many species into severe declines across the globe. However, the recent focus on conservation optimism has begun to highlight case studies that go against this trend. Reforestation, agricultural abandonment, reintroduction and legislative change have led to a situation where large mammals have recovered and are now widespread across the European continent. This study summarizes the knowledge about wild ungulate distribution in Europe and review the diversity of ways in which they interact with humans. Drawn from a wide range of sources, we built distribution maps of European wild ungulates. Results show that 90% of Europe is home to at least 1 species of wild native ungulate, with roe deer and wild boar occupying 74% and 64% of Europe respectively. In contrast, wild native mountain ungulates only occupy 5% of Europe, and are often associated with protected areas. The wide distribution of most European ungulates combined with the extensive human activity within Europe result in a wide range of interactions between ungulates and humans. These interactions can be classified as services or disservices depending on the value orientation and economic position of the various stakeholders perceiving this relationship. Overall, our survey highlights the success of wildlife management policies in Europe and the potential for continental scale conservation of large mammals in human-dominated landscapes. However, maintaining the success of wild ungulate conservation requires actions from national and European institutions to improve coordinated management across jurisdictional borders and sectorial coordination for the whole landscape.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2020.108500","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"108500","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The challenges and opportunities of coexisting with wild ungulates in the human-dominated landscapes of Europe's Anthropocene","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320719318312","volume":"244","author":[{"family":"Linnell","given":"J D C"},{"family":"Cretois","given":"Benjamin"},{"family":"Nilsen","given":"Erlend B."},{"family":"Rolandsen","given":"Christer M."},{"family":"Solberg","given":"Erling J."},{"family":"Veiberg","given":"Vebjørn"},{"family":"Kaczensky","given":"Petra"},{"family":"Van Moorter","given":"Bram"},{"family":"Panzacchi","given":"Manuela"},{"family":"Rauset","given":"Geir R."},{"family":"Kaltenborn","given":"Bjørn"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",4,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, in Sweden, forestry and hunting groups disagree on how to limit browsing damage to commercially important tree species, while at the same time maintaining sustainable harvest levels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4UDALlMb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sandstr\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}m, 2013; Wikenros, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Sandström, 2013; Wikenros, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32567,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SY7Q3BHT"],"itemData":{"id":32567,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","ISSN":"1875-0281","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of the Commons","note":"number: 2","title":"Conflict resolution through ecosystem-based management: the case of Swedish moose management","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Sandström","given":"Camilla"},{"family":"Di Gasper","given":"Sofia Wennberg"},{"family":"Öhman","given":"Karin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":32571,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7B4M374X"],"itemData":{"id":32571,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Scientific Reports","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"1-11","title":"Impact of a recolonizing, cross-border carnivore population on ungulate harvest in Scandinavia","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Wikenros","given":"Camilla"},{"family":"Sand","given":"Håkan"},{"family":"Månsson","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Maartmann","given":"Erling"},{"family":"Eriksen","given":"Ane"},{"family":"Wabakken","given":"Petter"},{"family":"Zimmermann","given":"Barbara"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In an attempt to resolve conflicts, the Swedish government introduced a local ecosystem management system with the aim of coordinating moose harvesting at an ecosystem level ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CB9gfCLC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sandstr\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}m, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Sandström, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32567,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SY7Q3BHT"],"itemData":{"id":32567,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","ISSN":"1875-0281","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of the Commons","note":"number: 2","title":"Conflict resolution through ecosystem-based management: the case of Swedish moose management","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Sandström","given":"Camilla"},{"family":"Di Gasper","given":"Sofia Wennberg"},{"family":"Öhman","given":"Karin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Management efforts, such as the recent ecosystem-based approach in Sweden, have contributed to a dramatic recovery of moose populations. However, ongoing sustainability of moose populations may be challenged by several factors including rapidly expanded carnivore populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"56HEeRKi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wikenros, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Wikenros, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32571,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7B4M374X"],"itemData":{"id":32571,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Scientific Reports","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"1-11","title":"Impact of a recolonizing, cross-border carnivore population on ungulate harvest in Scandinavia","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Wikenros","given":"Camilla"},{"family":"Sand","given":"Håkan"},{"family":"Månsson","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Maartmann","given":"Erling"},{"family":"Eriksen","given":"Ane"},{"family":"Wabakken","given":"Petter"},{"family":"Zimmermann","given":"Barbara"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, climate change effects on distribution ranges of moose ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2YOVkXuX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Johansson, 2020)","plainCitation":"(M. Johansson, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32583,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5DLT4EIH"],"itemData":{"id":32583,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","ISSN":"1087-1209","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"154-170","title":"How stakeholder representatives cope with collaboration in the Swedish moose management system","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Johansson","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Dressel","given":"Sabrina"},{"family":"Ericsson","given":"Göran"},{"family":"Sjölander-Lindqvist","given":"Annelie"},{"family":"Sandström","given":"Camilla"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} as well as the geographic expansion of chronic wasting disease ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"66T9Sclt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sutherland, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Sutherland, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32582,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W7I2EVGI"],"itemData":{"id":32582,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","ISSN":"0169-5347","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"47-58","title":"A 2018 horizon scan of emerging issues for global conservation and biological diversity","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Sutherland","given":"William J"},{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart HM"},{"family":"Connor","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Culshaw","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Dicks","given":"Lynn V"},{"family":"Dinsdale","given":"Jason"},{"family":"Doran","given":"Helen"},{"family":"Entwistle","given":"Abigail C"},{"family":"Fleishman","given":"Erica"},{"family":"Gibbons","given":"David W"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.


Box 4.11. Mobilization of local communities to create Brazilian extractive and sustainable development reserves, conservation units for sustainable use of natural resources 

The movements triggered in the Amazon in the mid-1980s for the creation of protected areas by the Brazilian State should, first, be considered as social movements, and not exclusively as environmental movements. They intended to react to the immediate threats suffered by the populations involved and their territories and social spaces, on which their cultural identity and economic survival depended ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"F0E4iJu8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Browder, 1992)","plainCitation":"(Browder, 1992)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":849,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TE2VV3CP"],"itemData":{"id":849,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper challenges recent claims that \\"extractive reserves\\" offer a sustainable and economically competitive mode of tropical forest use. In contrast to the popular image of rubber tappers and other \\"extractive\\" populations living in prosperous harmony with their rain forest environment, ample evidence exists that most extractor households in Amazonia are poor, exploited by various forms of debtpeonage, and are fully capable of destroying commercial forest resources when pressed by circumstances threatening their survival. This paper examines several social and economic factors that limit the development and expansion of the \\"extractive reserves model\\" in Amazonia and urges policy makers to face the real challenge ahead, that of developing sustainable modes of production for the Amazon's dominant population group (farmers and ranchers), who are directly responsible for most of the tropical forest clearing in the region.","archive":"JSTOR","container-title":"Advances in Economic Botany","ISSN":"07418280","note":"publisher: New York Botanical Garden Press","page":"33-41","title":"Social and Economic Constraints on the Development of Market-Oriented Extractive Reserves in Amazon Rain Forests","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/43931387","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Browder","given":"John O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the first National Meeting of Rubber Tappers, which took place in Brasília in August 1985 and when the National Rubber Tappers Council was created, it was the first time that extractive workers met to discuss land conflicts and forest conservation. The creation of extractive reserves was also a demand made at that meeting. The leaders participating in the meeting demanded that the rights to use these protected areas be transferred to local associations and granted in accordance with traditional land use standards, without the classic occupation models being copied ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sP2Xe9jQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Allegretti, 1990)","plainCitation":"(Allegretti, 1990)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32553,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R6GBWXIX"],"itemData":{"id":32553,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Alternatives to deforestation: steps toward sustainable use of the Amazon rain forest","event-place":"New York","language":"en","page":"252–264","publisher":"Columbia University Press","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Extractive reserves: an alternative for reconciling development and environmental conservation in Amazonia","author":[{"family":"Allegretti","given":"M.H."}],"editor":[{"family":"ANDERSON","given":"A.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1990"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, because contrary to the agrarian reform settlement projects, extractive reserves were already populated by traditional communities, who were familiar with local conditions and ecosystems. Among the main objectives of creating these areas, they claimed the creation of legal guarantees for the extraction of forest products and the promotion of a better quality of life for local populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bghScTpD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Butler, 1992)","plainCitation":"(Butler, 1992)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":848,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LGWG7RTW"],"itemData":{"id":848,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Although extractive reserves today account for a very small percentage of both land area and extracti vist populations, they offer the opportunity to test the viability of extractive reserves. If successful, the \\"lessons\\" of extractive reserves might be replicated in other areas, including Indigenous areas, buffer areas for national parks, and small farmer communities. Generating income for local development while protecting the forest is a complex issue requiring the multidisciplinary efforts of development and environmental organizations alike. Donors should work to develop comprehensive strategies with local community and advocacy groups while bearing in mind lessons from community development experience elsewhere in Latin America. As Reservas Extrativistas hoje representam uma por&#xe7;&#xe3;o relativamente pequena das &#xe1;reas occupadas por extrativistas. Mesmo assim, as atuais Reservas Extrativistas oferecem a opportunidade de demonstrar a viabilidade destas reservas. A implementacao integral de uma reserva pode oferecer li&#xe7;&#xf5;es que podem ser replicadas em outras &#xe1;reas, como por exemplo, &#xc1;reas Indigenas, &#xe1;reas de peque&#xf1;os agricultores, e outras povos da floresta. Gerear fontes de renda para o desenvolvimento local, e ao mesmo tempo proteger a floresta, e um asunto complexo que demanda esforcos multi-disciplinares conjuntos de entidades ambientalistas e de apoio ao desenvolvimento local. Financiadores devem trabalhar junto com grupos communitarios e ONGs para produzir uma estrategia ampia e levando em conta outras experiencias de desvolvimento communitario na America Latina.","archive":"JSTOR","container-title":"Advances in Economic Botany","ISSN":"07418280","note":"publisher: New York Botanical Garden Press","page":"87-99","title":"Non-Timber Forest Product Extraction in Amazonia: Lessons from Development Organizations","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/43931392","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Butler","given":"John R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thus, from the indigenous areas that already existed at the time, the National Rubber Tappers Council borrowed the idea of an area protected by the State, where local populations could practice their traditional culture and economic activities. However, extractivism should first be officially recognized as the main activity of these traditional populations, in order to be differentiated from other rural activities, such as the settlers of the settlements of the National Institute of Colonization and Agrarian Reform, that were predominantly agricultural activities. In extractive reserves, the units of exploitation of extractive families, known as placements (colocações), are not formed according to traditional geographical references. It is the natural resources of the land, and not the land per se, that define the boundaries between placements ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Xa4bmEkP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Allegretti, 1990)","plainCitation":"(Allegretti, 1990)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32553,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R6GBWXIX"],"itemData":{"id":32553,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Alternatives to deforestation: steps toward sustainable use of the Amazon rain forest","event-place":"New York","language":"en","page":"252–264","publisher":"Columbia University Press","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Extractive reserves: an alternative for reconciling development and environmental conservation in Amazonia","author":[{"family":"Allegretti","given":"M.H."}],"editor":[{"family":"ANDERSON","given":"A.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1990"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In the case of extractive reserves, the terms "reserve" and "extractive" carry very particular connotations. The conventional use of the adjective “extractive”, in the context of extractive workers in the Brazilian Amazon, refers to a very specific economic activity that depends on the maintenance of forest areas in the long run. With regard to the term “reserve”, this designates an area that has limits for human use, protected by the State, where the right of use is granted free and collectively to the populations in question ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RkBeTFQr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Allegretti, 1990)","plainCitation":"(Allegretti, 1990)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32553,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R6GBWXIX"],"itemData":{"id":32553,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Alternatives to deforestation: steps toward sustainable use of the Amazon rain forest","event-place":"New York","language":"en","page":"252–264","publisher":"Columbia University Press","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Extractive reserves: an alternative for reconciling development and environmental conservation in Amazonia","author":[{"family":"Allegretti","given":"M.H."}],"editor":[{"family":"ANDERSON","given":"A.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1990"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Since 2002 Brazil has had a National System of Conservation Units, which divides conservation units into two types: Integral Protection and Sustainable Use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CSn4l9ZO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brazilian Ministry Environment, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Brazilian Ministry Environment, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":802,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/92NH6JD3"],"itemData":{"id":802,"type":"book","edition":"5.","event-place":"Brasília","language":"pt","publisher":"SBF","publisher-place":"Brasília","title":"SNUC – Sistema Nacional de Unidades de Conservação da Natureza","author":[{"family":"Brazilian Ministry Environment","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Extractive Reserves and Sustainable Development Reserves fall into this second category. The first Sustainable Development Reserve implemented in Brazil was the Sustainable Development Reserve of Mamirauá, in the state of Amazonas. Its history is relatively different from the creation of the extractive reserves, since it was initially created as an Ecological Station, after demand to allow the protection of some specific natural attributes, especially for the protection of the uacari-white primate (Cacajao calvus calvus), which at that time was already on the list of species officially threatened with extinction in Brazil, as well as the the International Union for Conservation of Nature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gfGOZIyO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Queiroz, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Queiroz, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":858,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NPFLYNV7"],"itemData":{"id":858,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"AS UNIDADES de conservação ainda são um dos instrumentos mais eficientes na promoção da proteção e conservação da biodiversidade na Amazônia. As unidades de uso sustentável têm se mostrado cada vez mais importantes no cenário atual. Dentre os modelos propostos hoje para criação e gestão destas unidades, o Modelo de Reservas de Desenvolvimento Sustentável mostra-se viável e bastante bem-sucedido. Neste trabalho são apresentados os aspectos mais centrais da formulação deste modelo e exemplos oriundos do seu principal representante, a Reserva de Desenvolvimento Sustentável Mamirauá (RDSM), além de considerações acerca do sucesso e validação desse modelo por meio de sua replicação para outras áreas na Amazônia.\\n          , \\n            PROTECTED areas are still one of the most effective tools to promote bio-diversity protection and conservation in the Amazon. The importance of protected areas of sustainable use in the present scenario is increasing. Among the proposed conservation models for protected areas of sustainable use, the Sustainable Development Reserve Model is not only viable, but extremely successful. In the present paper the main aspects of such model are presented, with some illustration from it most well known representative, the Mamirauá Sustainable Development Reserve (MSDR), and with some discussion regarding the success and validation of the model through its replication for other sites in the Amazon.","container-title":"Estudos Avançados","DOI":"10.1590/S0103-40142005000200011","ISSN":"0103-4014","issue":"54","journalAbbreviation":"Estud. av.","language":"pt","page":"183-203","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A reserva de desenvolvimento sustentável Mamirauá","URL":"http://www.scielo.br/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0103-40142005000200011&lng=pt&tlng=pt","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Queiroz","given":"Helder L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      

      The degree to which laws are applied and enforced is also highly complicated and can vary by political priorities and institutional capacity (i.e., resources to hire fish and game officers). Where there is strong synergy between centralized institutions and local and regional level knowledge and management systems (as with co-management), enforcement can be less costly, (i.e., as there is a greater degree of self-regulation), limits social conflict and can lead to improved sustainable use outcomes. In some cases, national, level institutions can favour some kinds of sustainable use and disadvantage others. For example, in North America, a critical concern is how states and provinces privilege recreational, sports and commercial hunting over indigenous and subsistence harvesting of game (See section on indigenous institutions).
      Laws related to sustainable use of wild species, like other kinds of environmental laws, are usefully conceptualized along a sliding scale of ‘hard’ and ‘soft’, referring to the degree of enforceability. “Some hard laws contain soft requirements (e.g., to ‘consider’ or ‘assess’) while some soft law instruments contain hard law norms (e.g., legal principles)” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4pBrLTkC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dernbach & Mintz, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Dernbach & Mintz, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32581,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YCIX6LHK"],"itemData":{"id":32581,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Molecular Diversity Preservation International","page":"531-540","title":"Environmental laws and sustainability: An introduction","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dernbach","given":"John C"},{"family":"Mintz","given":"Joel A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is widely held that a combination of hard and soft laws including customary laws and social norms, when knitted together in a clear system, are the most effective at addressing problems of sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cHWOdWIN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dernbach & Mintz, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Dernbach & Mintz, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32581,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YCIX6LHK"],"itemData":{"id":32581,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Molecular Diversity Preservation International","page":"531-540","title":"Environmental laws and sustainability: An introduction","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Dernbach","given":"John C"},{"family":"Mintz","given":"Joel A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Soft law has the advantage of being flexible in allowing different stakeholders to respond and address issues in their own fashion, creating opportunities for more sustainable outcomes than narrow and rigidly enforced rules. However, too much flexilbility may provids no incentive or assurance of compliance. One example of a strong combination of hard and soft laws related to protecting sustainable use occurs in the North Atlantic; the International North Sea Conferences focused on addressing pollution in the North Sea, which was indirectly impacting the sustainability of fishing and other kinds of harvesting and use of this ecosystem. The North Sea Conferences agreement was attributed with the strengthening and speeding up of the creation of harder laws within the European Union ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0FSL1mA9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Skj\\\\uc0\\\\u230{}rseth, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Skjærseth, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32580,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVEHN4UD"],"itemData":{"id":32580,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Politics","ISSN":"1526-3800","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Politics","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: MIT Press One Rogers Street, Cambridge, MA 02142-1209, USA journals-info …","page":"104-120","title":"Soft law, hard law, and effective implementation of international environmental norms","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Skjærseth","given":"Jon Birger"},{"family":"Stokke","given":"Olav Schram"},{"family":"Wettestad","given":"Jørgen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The continued sustainable use of polar bears in the Northwest Territories, Nunavut, and Greenland, is also grounded in the interconnection between strong regulations and laws protecting rights of Inuit working together with the customary laws of Inuit which are based on generations of Inuit knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lyW2ZNNG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. A. Clark, 2009; J. J. Schmidt & Dowsley, 2010; Tyrrell, 2006)","plainCitation":"(D. A. Clark, 2009; J. J. Schmidt & Dowsley, 2010; Tyrrell, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":836,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UMBQLUQ2"],"itemData":{"id":836,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ARCTIC","DOI":"10.14430/arctic43","ISSN":"1923-1245, 0004-0843","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"ARCTIC","page":"347-360","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Polar Bear Conservation in Canada: Defining the Policy Problems","title-short":"Polar Bear Conservation in Canada","URL":"https://journalhosting.ucalgary.ca/index.php/arctic/article/view/63111","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Clark","given":"Douglas A."},{"family":"Lee","given":"David S."},{"family":"Freeman","given":"Milton M.R."},{"family":"Clark","given":"Susan G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",8,28]]}}},{"id":32586,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PQ7F2U9Q"],"itemData":{"id":32586,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","ISSN":"1572-9915","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Human Ecology","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Springer","page":"377-387","title":"Hunting with polar bears: problems with the passive properties of the commons","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Schmidt","given":"Jeremy J"},{"family":"Dowsley","given":"Martha"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32572,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V4BM5YHJ"],"itemData":{"id":32572,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Études/Inuit/Studies","ISSN":"0701-1008","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Études/Inuit/Studies","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Association Inuksiutiit Katimajiit Inc.","page":"191-208","title":"More bears, less bears: Inuit and scientific perceptions of polar bear populations on the west coast of Hudson Bay","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Tyrrell","given":"Martina"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Soft law includes consideration and implementation of the precautionary principle as set out in the Rio Declaration (1992).
      Statutory laws and policies governing the sustainable use of wild species are highly variable in their quality and comprehensiveness, and their implementation, effectiveness, and enforcement, particularly in the global South and many countries with still high levels of natural biodiversity. There is a lack of clarity in institutional responsibilities and low extent of consultation with and/or engagement of diverse stakeholders, particularly among ethnic or minority groups and the traditional land and resource custodians who have varying levels of recognition, rights and responsibilities. 
      Whilst on paper the quality and comprehensiveness of legal and regulatory frameworks appears strong, there is variation in the strength (i.e., teeth) of various laws and policies as applied and enforced ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GqXemYec","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ingram 2017; Abbott, Tsinda, and Mugisha 2018; Tieguhong 2015)","plainCitation":"(Ingram 2017; Abbott, Tsinda, and Mugisha 2018; Tieguhong 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":861,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4PP7KFH8"],"itemData":{"id":861,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Across the Congo Basin, bush mango (Irvingia spp.) nuts have been harvested from forest landscapes for consumption, sold as a foodstuff and for medicine for centuries. Data on this trade however are sparse. A value chain approach was used to gather information on stakeholders in the chain from the harvesters in three major production areas in Cameroon to traders in Cameroon, Nigeria, and Equatorial Guinea, the socio-economic values, environmental sustainability and governance. Around 5190 people work in the complex chain in Cameroon with an estimated 4109tons harvested on average annually in the period 2007 to 2010. Bush mango incomes contribute on average to 31% of harvester's annual incomes and dependence increases for those further from the forest. Customary rules govern access to resources. Although regulations exist, most trade is illegal, with corruption and collective action governing access to markets. The majority of nuts harvested are sustainably collected. Although 51% of the harvest is sourced from the forest, trees are also managed on cultivated land. Forest degradation and deforestation threaten the species. Policy measures such as linking stakeholders, promoting cultivation, pragmatic regulation, and supporting processer groups may make trade in this forest food more sustainable.","collection-title":"Forest, Food, and Livelihoods","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2016.12.014","ISSN":"1389-9341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"29-37","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Challenges to governing sustainable forest food: Irvingia spp. from southern Cameroon","title-short":"Challenges to governing sustainable forest food","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1389934116304798","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Ewane","given":"Marcus"},{"family":"Ndumbe","given":"Louis Njie"},{"family":"Awono","given":"Abdon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",11,1]]}}},{"id":32552,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S3VFTAZU"],"itemData":{"id":32552,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"issue: 1-2\\npage: 5–13,\\nvolume: 12\\nDOI: 10.1080/21513732.2015.1124453","publisher":"University of Aberdeen","title":"Review of Policies for Biodiversity Informatics in Central Africa: Case Studies of the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC) and Gabon","URL":"https://ssrn.com/abstract=3098716","author":[{"family":"Abbott","given":"P."},{"family":"Tsinda","given":"A."},{"family":"Mugisha","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":831,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3SPABN34"],"itemData":{"id":831,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"1–10","title":"How governance impacts non-timber forest product value chains in Cameroon","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Tieguhong","given":"Julius Chupezi"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Mala","given":"William Armand"},{"family":"Ndoye","given":"Ousseynou"},{"family":"Grouwels","given":"Sophie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A range of soft and harder policy tools can be most effective at encouraging sustainable use, including the development and implementation of international agreements and protocols within national borders ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BxmplI8f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harrop & Pritchard, 2011; Skj\\\\uc0\\\\u230{}rseth, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Harrop & Pritchard, 2011; Skjærseth, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32585,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7ZU3BMJV"],"itemData":{"id":32585,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","ISSN":"0959-3780","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"474-480","title":"A hard instrument goes soft: The implications of the Convention on Biological Diversity's current trajectory","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Harrop","given":"Stuart R"},{"family":"Pritchard","given":"Diana J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":32580,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVEHN4UD"],"itemData":{"id":32580,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Politics","ISSN":"1526-3800","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Politics","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: MIT Press One Rogers Street, Cambridge, MA 02142-1209, USA journals-info …","page":"104-120","title":"Soft law, hard law, and effective implementation of international environmental norms","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Skjærseth","given":"Jon Birger"},{"family":"Stokke","given":"Olav Schram"},{"family":"Wettestad","given":"Jørgen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      
Box 4.12. Precautionary principle – Barren Ground Caribou and mining in Northern Canada

During 1990-2015, barren-ground caribou in northern Canada declined by over 70% as part of a cycle of natural herd population cycles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Qn3pH2BM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vors & Boyce, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Vors & Boyce, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":812,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IBGMCSL3"],"itemData":{"id":812,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2486.2009.01974.x","ISSN":"13541013, 13652486","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"2626-2633","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Global declines of caribou and reindeer: CARIBOU REINDEER DECLINE","title-short":"Global declines of caribou and reindeer","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1365-2486.2009.01974.x","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Vors","given":"Liv Solveig"},{"family":"Boyce","given":"Mark Stephen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the case of the Bathurst herd in the Northwest Territories, Canada. The population declined more than 98% from 475 000 animals to less than 8000; the possible extirpation of the herd was highlighted by the management board. Numerous factors were considered to be driver population decline of this herd; the most controversial being theories have been habitat degradation and loss associated with mining activity and over-harvest by recreational hunters and indigenous peoples. Many indigenous governments interpreted the mining boom in the Bathurst range and disturbance of more than 30 million hectares of summer and fall range to the core driver of the decline, however, these views were little considered by the government which focused instead on n increasing harvest regulations including no-hunting zones and harvest bans.  This privilege of mineral resource development whilst efforts were made to criminalize subsistence hunting was perceived as a major injustice by indigenous peoples. 


      Available harvest data coupled with oral histories and academic research provided ample evidence of sustainable use of Bathurst and other herds by indigenous peoples over the hundreds of years.  In addition to adaptive and flexible harvesting practices (i.e., the decreasing harvest of caribou during periods of decline) and harvest substitution (i.e., harvest for other species such as fish, moose and muskoxen). It is the availability of other wild species, which have provided strong support for continued cultural continuity, economy and food security that has enabled Indigenous populations in the region to ride out periodic declines or variability in barren-ground caribou. The tracking of key indicators of population decline (e.g., body condition, reproductive success, habitat conditions) as well as catch-per-unit effort type information is regularly documented and shared by harvesters with one another. Well-developed sets of rules or norms of how to respect the caribou during times of decline form the foundation of decisions about where, when and how many caribou to harvest during population declines. This case study on the Bathurst caribou is revealing of the value of indigenous knowledge as a foundation of management. Moreover, strong social networks and norms of reciprocity have also offset inequities in those most affected by population declines (i.e., food sharing within and between communities) and also ensured sustainable use. Some of the key principles within Dene knowledge systems that contribute to sustainable use of caribou  include the following: 

