Review","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"78–92","title":"Will the proposed forest policy and regulatory reforms boost the NTFP sector in Cameroon?","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Awono","given":"A."},{"family":"Tchindjang","given":"M."},{"family":"Levang","given":"Patrice"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In some countries there has been consultation with, and engagement of, diverse stakeholders around regulatory changes on wild species trade, for example via community forestry and plants, algae and fungi legal frameworks in Gabon, Cameroon, and Democratic Republic of the Congo ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WmmXi0sC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Awono, 2016; Ingram, 2017; Kimengsi, 2019; S. A. Laird, 2010; Lescuyer, 2019; Yobo & Ito, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Awono, 2016; Ingram, 2017; Kimengsi, 2019; S. A. Laird, 2010; Lescuyer, 2019; Yobo & Ito, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32555,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XSLVGWN3"],"itemData":{"id":32555,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Forestry Review","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"78–92","title":"Will the proposed forest policy and regulatory reforms boost the NTFP sector in Cameroon?","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Awono","given":"A."},{"family":"Tchindjang","given":"M."},{"family":"Levang","given":"Patrice"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":861,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4PP7KFH8"],"itemData":{"id":861,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Across the Congo Basin, bush mango (Irvingia spp.) nuts have been harvested from forest landscapes for consumption, sold as a foodstuff and for medicine for centuries. Data on this trade however are sparse. A value chain approach was used to gather information on stakeholders in the chain from the harvesters in three major production areas in Cameroon to traders in Cameroon, Nigeria, and Equatorial Guinea, the socio-economic values, environmental sustainability and governance. Around 5190 people work in the complex chain in Cameroon with an estimated 4109tons harvested on average annually in the period 2007 to 2010. Bush mango incomes contribute on average to 31% of harvester's annual incomes and dependence increases for those further from the forest. Customary rules govern access to resources. Although regulations exist, most trade is illegal, with corruption and collective action governing access to markets. The majority of nuts harvested are sustainably collected. Although 51% of the harvest is sourced from the forest, trees are also managed on cultivated land. Forest degradation and deforestation threaten the species. Policy measures such as linking stakeholders, promoting cultivation, pragmatic regulation, and supporting processer groups may make trade in this forest food more sustainable.","collection-title":"Forest, Food, and Livelihoods","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2016.12.014","ISSN":"1389-9341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"29-37","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Challenges to governing sustainable forest food: Irvingia spp. from southern Cameroon","title-short":"Challenges to governing sustainable forest food","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1389934116304798","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Ewane","given":"Marcus"},{"family":"Ndumbe","given":"Louis Njie"},{"family":"Awono","given":"Abdon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",11,1]]}}},{"id":843,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CZEGVDXH"],"itemData":{"id":843,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International journal of the commons","issue":"2","language":"en","title":"Constitutionality and the co-management of protected areas: Reflections from Cameroon and Myanmar","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Kimengsi","given":"Jude Ndzifon"},{"family":"Aung","given":"Pyi Soe"},{"family":"Pretzsch","given":"Jürgen"},{"family":"Haller","given":"Tobias"},{"family":"Auch","given":"Eckhard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":34331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3K7YP5V"],"itemData":{"id":34331,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","number-of-pages":"393","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","title-short":"Wild product governance","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32579,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WXZHMU5K"],"itemData":{"id":32579,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecology and Society","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: JSTOR","title":"Are community forests a viable model for the Democratic Republic of Congo?","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Kakundika","given":"Tito Muhindo"},{"family":"Lubala","given":"Ignace Muganguzi"},{"family":"Ekyamba","given":"Isaac Shabani"},{"family":"Tsanga","given":"Raphaël"},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"Paolo Omar"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":835,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IFHFXLEY"],"itemData":{"id":835,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity and Conservation","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"41–54","title":"Evolution of policies and legal frameworks governing the management of forest and National Parks resources in Gabon","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Yobo","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Ito","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Examples of formal, or statutory, institutional arrangements that can drive sustainable use of wild species include: environmental laws on endangered species and designation of protected areas (national parks, provincial/state parks, or international designations such as Biosphere Reserve, RAMSAR site or Geographic Indication); rules and regulations that manage fishing, hunting, logging, or harvesting of plants or other species through licenses, permits, quotas and others means; and tenure or ownership rights of land or seas where wild species lives, and rights to resources contained therein. Internationally, legally binding institutions include conventions or treaties.  
      
      4.2.2.2.4. Co-management and cooperative arrangements
Co-management and cooperative management arrangements are well developed in many areas of the world including Canada, New Zealand, Europe and parts of Asia where they have proven highly effective at engaging local users of resources in the design and implementation of formal governance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"B3hF1AtZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. Berkes 2009)","plainCitation":"(F. Berkes 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32551,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4AV9ELZ2"],"itemData":{"id":32551,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"J. Environ. Manage","language":"en","page":"1692–1702","title":"Evolution of co-management: Role of knowledge generation, bridging organizations and social learning","volume":"90","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These arrangements are particularly well-developed with respect to regulating harvest of fish and wild species as well as forest resources and protected areas. Co-management has been useful in the management of small scale fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OacW6WbB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Borrini-Feyerabend, 2004; C\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}rcamo, 2014, 2014; N. Jones, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Borrini-Feyerabend, 2004; Cárcamo, 2014, 2014; N. Jones, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HFUAIT89"],"itemData":{"id":879,"type":"book","event-place":"Cambridge, UK","language":"en","publisher":"IUCN and Cardiff University","publisher-place":"Cambridge, UK","title":"Indigenous and Local Communities and Protected Areas: Towards Equity and Enhanced Conservation","author":[{"family":"Borrini-Feyerabend","given":"G."},{"family":"Kothari","given":"A."},{"family":"Oviedo","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":32578,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AHVE8DLX"],"itemData":{"id":32578,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science & Policy","ISSN":"1462-9011","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Science & Policy","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"116-131","title":"Using stakeholders’ perspective of ecosystem services and biodiversity features to plan a marine protected area","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Cárcamo","given":"P Francisco"},{"family":"Garay-Flühmann","given":"Rosa"},{"family":"Squeo","given":"Francisco A"},{"family":"Gaymer","given":"Carlos F"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":32578,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AHVE8DLX"],"itemData":{"id":32578,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science & Policy","ISSN":"1462-9011","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Science & Policy","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"116-131","title":"Using stakeholders’ perspective of ecosystem services and biodiversity features to plan a marine protected area","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Cárcamo","given":"P Francisco"},{"family":"Garay-Flühmann","given":"Rosa"},{"family":"Squeo","given":"Francisco A"},{"family":"Gaymer","given":"Carlos F"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":869,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IKN57634"],"itemData":{"id":869,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Impact Assessment Review","DOI":"10.1016/j.eiar.2016.12.007","language":"en","page":"1–7","title":"Improving social impact assessment of protected areas: A review of the literature and directions for future research","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Jones","given":"N."},{"family":"McGinlay","given":"J."},{"family":"Dimitrakopoulos","given":"P.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although top-down fisheries management systems are critical in some cases, fisheries managers in many parts of the world have recognized for decades that most fisheries cannot be managed without the cooperation of local fishers, and in situations of significant resource competition (i.e., many users with diverse interests and values), co-management is critical to trust building and ensuring compliance with laws and regulations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ISRzzJ6c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pomeroy & Williams, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Pomeroy & Williams, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32595,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6MDMCG6V"],"itemData":{"id":32595,"type":"article-journal","note":"publisher: ICLARM","title":"Fisheries co-management and small-scale fisheries: a policy brief","author":[{"family":"Pomeroy","given":"Robert S"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Meryl J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is true in cases where resources are remote or scattered in areas where enforcement is difficult as “under these conditions, delegation of fisheries management and allocation of decisions to local fishers (at the) community level may be more effective than the management efforts that distant, understaffed and underfunded national government agencies can provide” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YpLoNRQV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pomeroy & Williams, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Pomeroy & Williams, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32595,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6MDMCG6V"],"itemData":{"id":32595,"type":"article-journal","note":"publisher: ICLARM","title":"Fisheries co-management and small-scale fisheries: a policy brief","author":[{"family":"Pomeroy","given":"Robert S"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Meryl J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Over the last 30 years the principles or factors that most critically contribute to the effectiveness of co-management of fisheries have been further developed. A key factor in many regions of the world is recognition of the customary laws of local and indigenous peoples who have long-term relationships to fisheries resources and a vested interest in ensuring the sustainability of the resource. These customary laws are based on generations of indigenous and local knowledge and are continuously informed by systems of social learning (e.g., monitoring), are among the strongest kinds of co-management institutions, and tend to be most effective at ensuring sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uAWjGwdU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. R. Armitage, 2009; Berkes, 2009)","plainCitation":"(D. R. Armitage, 2009; Berkes, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32554,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FACHXNVG"],"itemData":{"id":32554,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","DOI":"10.1890/070089","language":"en","page":"95–102","title":"Adaptive co-management for social–ecological complexity","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Armitage","given":"D.R."},{"family":"Plummer","given":"R."},{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Arthur","given":"R.I."},{"family":"Charles","given":"A.T."},{"family":"Davidson-Hunt","given":"I.J."},{"family":"Diduck","given":"A.P."},{"family":"Doubleday","given":"N.C."},{"family":"Johnson","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Marschke","given":"M."},{"family":"McConney","given":"P."},{"family":"Pinkerton","given":"E.W."},{"family":"Wollenberg","given":"E.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":32551,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4AV9ELZ2"],"itemData":{"id":32551,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"J. Environ. Manage","language":"en","page":"1692–1702","title":"Evolution of co-management: Role of knowledge generation, bridging organizations and social learning","volume":"90","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Box 4.13.  Karuak and co-management of the forests in California and Oregon

The Karuk people are the indigenous peoples of the Klamath River valley which is biodiverse region near the border between California and Oregon; their territory spans over 1.38 million acres. Many members of the nation depend on the forest for the subsistence foods from the forest and its waterways. Among the cultural practices and uses of the forest was cultural burning in support of managing forest fire risks and shaping the landscape in ways that nutured the growth of particular kinds of resources and wild species habitats. The Karuk Tribe is now federally recognized by the U.S. government but was not historically recognized with land rights; as a result their capacity to live and harvest resources from their homeland has been tenous. A pilot project of co-management involving the Karuk Tribe and the United States of America Forest Service was established in the Klamath Basin of Northern California, with the intention of building greater equity in decisions being made about the use of resources of the forest. The project was successful due to simultaneous efforts of the United States of America Forest Service to reconsider colonial histories and institutions that excluded the Karuk people and the willingness of Karuk government to engage in institution building that blended Karuk laws and those of the state of Oregon and California in ways that are consistent with cultural values and uses. Challenges continue due to the rigidities of formal government and colonial systems which persist in forest management decision-making ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NWEf0ivf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Diver, 2016; Marks-Block, 2019, p., 2021)","plainCitation":"(Diver, 2016; Marks-Block, 2019, p., 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32594,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z6NZGIHG"],"itemData":{"id":32594,"type":"book","title":"Community Voices: The Making and Meaning of the Xaxli'p Community Forest","author":[{"family":"Diver","given":"Sibyl"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32603,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YEJ799ZI"],"itemData":{"id":32603,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","ISSN":"0378-1127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"117517","title":"Effects of understory fire management treatments on California Hazelnut, an ecocultural resource of the Karuk and Yurok Indians in the Pacific Northwest","volume":"450","author":[{"family":"Marks-Block","given":"Tony"},{"family":"Lake","given":"Frank K"},{"family":"Curran","given":"Lisa M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"locator":"-"},{"id":803,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XYVP8GRD"],"itemData":{"id":803,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fire Ecology","ISSN":"1933-9747","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fire Ecology","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: SpringerOpen","page":"1-20","title":"Revitalized Karuk and Yurok cultural burning to enhance California hazelnut for basketweaving in northwestern California, USA","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Marks-Block","given":"Tony"},{"family":"Lake","given":"Frank K"},{"family":"Bird","given":"Rebecca Bliege"},{"family":"Curran","given":"Lisa M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.


Table 4.2. Co-management Institutions and Transboundary Problems.

Species 


Lesson related to Managing for Sustainable Use
Pacific Salmon 
Salmon populations are under stress in northwestern Canada and the United States of America due to such factors as habitat loss and degradation (due to climate change and resource development) and commercial fishing. “Management is complicated by the geographical scale of salmon production, encompassing terrestrial and aquatic habitats, extending from inland watersheds to ocean basins, and encountering different property and governance regimes” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"10kusIB8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ebbin, S. A. 2002)","plainCitation":"(Ebbin, S. A. 2002)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":827,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TIMRLU32"],"itemData":{"id":827,"type":"article-newspaper","title":"Enhanced fit through institutional interplay in the Pacific Northwest Salmon co-management regime. Marine Policy, 26(4), 253-259.","author":[{"literal":"Ebbin, S. A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Commerical habitat use and fishing interests have long dominated management to the detriment of indigenous peoples and local knowledge and the sustainability of fish stocks. Various kinds of shared decision-making models have emerged that redistribute some power to local fishers through legal agreements (e.g., Nisga’a Final Agreement) requiring the engagement of Indigenous Peoples and knowledge  in the management have emerged in the last two decades and other kinds of Tribal and Indigenous-led conservation efforts (e.g., Columbia River Inter-Tribal Fish Commission, Nuu-chah-nulth Tribal Council) have improved management outcomes. However, many inequities and socio-economic and political barriers to sustainable use continue to exist that are detrimental to salmon and salmon-based economies and cultures ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"soFS9HYA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Atlas, 2021; Ebbin, S. A., 2002; Pinkerton, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Atlas, 2021; Ebbin, S. A., 2002; Pinkerton, 1994)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32608,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6TZWMDVU"],"itemData":{"id":32608,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bioscience","ISSN":"0006-3568","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Bioscience","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Oxford University Press","page":"186-204","title":"Indigenous systems of management for culturally and ecologically resilient Pacific salmon (Oncorhynchus spp.) fisheries","volume":"71","author":[{"family":"Atlas","given":"William I"},{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C"},{"family":"Moore","given":"Jonathan W"},{"family":"Tuohy","given":"Adrian M"},{"family":"Greening","given":"Spencer"},{"family":"Reid","given":"Andrea J"},{"family":"Morven","given":"Nicole"},{"family":"White","given":"Elroy"},{"family":"Housty","given":"William G"},{"family":"Housty","given":"Jess A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":827,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TIMRLU32"],"itemData":{"id":827,"type":"article-newspaper","title":"Enhanced fit through institutional interplay in the Pacific Northwest Salmon co-management regime. Marine Policy, 26(4), 253-259.","author":[{"literal":"Ebbin, S. A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":32602,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BVT5TTWA"],"itemData":{"id":32602,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Canadian journal of fisheries and aquatic sciences","ISSN":"0706-652X","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Canadian journal of fisheries and aquatic sciences","note":"number: 10\\npublisher: NRC Research Press Ottawa, Canada","page":"2363-2378","title":"Local fisheries co-management: a review of international experiences and their implications for salmon management in British Columbia","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Pinkerton","given":"Evelyn W"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
Rangifer (Porcupine Caribou Management Board)
 One of the oldest and most successful transboundary co-management institutions is the Porcupine Caribou Co-Management Board shares power between management authorities of multiple Indigenous governments, Alaska, and the Yukonthe Yukonst Territories and federal governments of Canada. The Porcupine Caribou Co-Management Board is an advisory board established under the Porcupine Caribou Management Agreement (1985) to communicate information about the herd and provide recommendations to agencies responsible for managing the herd. The success of the management can be measured by the sustainability of the population which has remained relatively stable in recent years when compared to other caribou herds in the circumpolar north.  Sustainability is also measurable by the ability of indigenous peoples in the region to continue to sustainably harvest caribou to meet subsistence needs. The strength of land claim institutions that protect Inuvialuit and Gwich’in rights to harvest, respect of indigenous knowledge and customary laws for harvesting, coupled with effective lobbying to protect caribou habitat from resource development are among the factors contributing to this success ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NlcIhHQN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kendrick, 2003, 2003; Kofinas, 2005; Kruse, 1998; Moller, 2004; Nadasdy, 2003; B. L. Parlee, 2018; S. J. Peacock, 2020; S. Peacock L. & Turner, 2000; D. C. Thomas & Schaefer, 1991; Usher, 1993)","plainCitation":"(Kendrick, 2003, 2003; Kofinas, 2005; Kruse, 1998; Moller, 2004; Nadasdy, 2003; B. L. Parlee, 2018; S. J. Peacock, 2020; S. Peacock L. & Turner, 2000; D. C. Thomas & Schaefer, 1991; Usher, 1993)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":817,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V6UFB4NP"],"itemData":{"id":817,"type":"book","language":"en","number-of-pages":"241–267","publisher":"Cambridge Univ. Press","title":"Caribou co-management in northern Canada: Fostering multiple ways of knowing, in Navigating Social-Ecological Systems","author":[{"family":"Kendrick","given":"A."}],"editor":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Colding","given":"J."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":817,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V6UFB4NP"],"itemData":{"id":817,"type":"book","language":"en","number-of-pages":"241–267","publisher":"Cambridge Univ. Press","title":"Caribou co-management in northern Canada: Fostering multiple ways of knowing, in Navigating Social-Ecological Systems","author":[{"family":"Kendrick","given":"A."}],"editor":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Colding","given":"J."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":32557,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PM3QDBP3"],"itemData":{"id":32557,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Anthropologica","page":"179-196","title":"Caribou Hunters and Researchers at the Co-management Interface: Emergent Dilemmas and the Dynamics of Legitimacy in Power Sharing","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Kofinas","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":875,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QFD4M8T8"],"itemData":{"id":875,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human organization","ISSN":"0018-7259","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Human organization","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Society for Applied Anthropology","page":"447-458","title":"Co-management of natural resources: a comparison of two caribou management systems","volume":"57","author":[{"family":"Kruse","given":"Jack"},{"family":"Klein","given":"Dave"},{"family":"Braund","given":"Steve"},{"family":"Moorehead","given":"Lisa"},{"family":"Simeone","given":"Bill"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":32542,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NZCSHQJ6"],"itemData":{"id":32542,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Using a combination of traditional ecological knowledge and science to monitor populations can greatly assist co-management for sustainable customary wildlife harvests by indigenous peoples. Case studies from Canada and New Zealand emphasize that, although traditional monitoring methods may often be imprecise and qualitative, they are nevertheless valuable because they are based on observations over long time periods, incorporate large sample sizes, are inexpensive, invite the participation of harvesters as researchers, and sometimes incorporate subtle multivariate cross checks for environmental change. A few simple rules suggested by traditional knowledge may produce good management outcomes consistent with fuzzy logic thinking. Science can sometimes offer better tests of potential causes of population change by research on larger spatial scales, precise quantification, and evaluation of population change where no harvest occurs. However, science is expensive and may not always be trusted or welcomed by customary users of wildlife. Short scientific studies in which traditional monitoring methods are calibrated against population abundance could make it possible to mesh traditional ecological knowledge with scientific inferences of prey population dynamics. This paper analyzes the traditional monitoring techniques of catch per unit effort and body condition. Combining scientific and traditional monitoring methods can not only build partnership and community consensus, but also, and more importantly, allow indigenous wildlife users to critically evaluate scientific predictions on their own terms and test sustainability using their own forms of adaptive management.","call-number":"Esub43","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-00675-090302","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art2","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Combining Science and Traditional Ecological Knowledge: Monitoring Populations for Co-Management","title-short":"Combining Science and Traditional Ecological Knowledge","URL":"http://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol9/iss3/art2/","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Moller","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Lyver","given":"Philip O'Brian"},{"family":"Kislalioglu","given":"Mina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":801,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TLE6SSIX"],"itemData":{"id":801,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic","issue":"4","note":"number: 4","page":"367-80","title":"Reevaluating the Co-management Success Story","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Nadasdy","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":34961,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JUP575TP"],"itemData":{"id":34961,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science advances","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Science advances","note":"number: 2","page":"e1701611","title":"Undermining subsistence: Barren-ground caribou in a “tragedy of open access”","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda L"},{"family":"Sandlos","given":"John"},{"family":"Natcher","given":"David C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32556,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/52P82NSL"],"itemData":{"id":32556,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic Science","issue":"3","note":"number: 3","page":"247-266","title":"Linking co-monitoring to co-management: bringing together local, traditional, and scientific knowledge in a wildlife status assessment framework","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Peacock","given":"Stephanie J."},{"family":"Mavrot","given":"Fabien"},{"family":"Tomaselli","given":"Matilde"},{"family":"Hanke","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Fenton","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Nathoo","given":"Rosemin"},{"family":"Aleuy","given":"Oscar Alejandro"},{"family":"Di Francesco","given":"Juliette"},{"family":"Aguilar","given":"Xavier Fernandez"},{"family":"Jutha","given":"Naima"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":21,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8TBWUWNT"],"itemData":{"id":21,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Biodiversity and native America","event-place":"Norman (Okla.)","ISBN":"978-0-8061-3345-4","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 470893627","page":"133-79","publisher":"University of Oklahoma Press","publisher-place":"Norman (Okla.)","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"\\"Just Like A Garden\\": Traditional Resource Management and Biodiversity Conservation on the Interior Plateau of British Columbia","editor":[{"family":"Minnis","given":"Paul E"},{"family":"Elisens","given":"Wayne J"}],"author":[{"family":"Peacock","given":"Sandra","suffix":"L."},{"family":"Turner","given":"Nancy J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":32558,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8BJEMTLD"],"itemData":{"id":32558,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Rangifer","page":"73-89","title":"Wildlife co-management defined: the Beverly and Kaminuriak caribou management board","author":[{"family":"Thomas","given":"Donald C."},{"family":"Schaefer","given":"James"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}},{"id":880,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KSW86C44"],"itemData":{"id":880,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Traditional Ecological Knowledge Concepts and Cases","event-place":"Ottawa","publisher":"International Program on Traditional Ecological Knowledge and International Development Research Centre","publisher-place":"Ottawa","title":"The Beverly-Kaminuriak Caribou Management Board: An Experience in Co-Management","author":[{"family":"Usher","given":"Peter J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
Central American Coral Reef and Inshore Fisheries 
The diverse fisheries of Central America and the Caribbean region largely overexploited, particularly those of the nearshore and coral reefs. Development without effective conservation and management measures has led to this problem. Most countries have weak legislation and no active or effective fisheries management plans. Centralized, top-down management has largely been to blame in this region, coupled with limited self-policing of local fishers. The political culture (i.e., weak institutions and poor enforcement of laws) of the region coupled with socio-economic conditions have been factors that have limited power-sharing and effective management towards sustainable use.  In Costa Rica as in many other jurisdictions, “centralized regulation of fishing inputs (e.g., gear and seasonal restrictions) has largely failed to address overcapacity and, in some cases, has intensified competition and uncertainty among fishers” (Garcia and Heninen 2016: 759). The Marine Area of Responsible Fishing (Área Marina de Pesca Responsible, or AMPR), suggests the opportunities that can come for sustainable use through the involvement of local fishers’ organizations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5l3tNWuo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fargier, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Fargier, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":808,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7NU4HS2T"],"itemData":{"id":808,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Fisheries Management of Mexican and Central American Estuaries","page":"155-181","publisher":"Springer","title":"“Marine Areas of Responsible Fishing”: A Path Toward Small-Scale Fisheries Co-Management in Costa Rica? Perspectives from Golfo Dulce","author":[{"family":"Fargier","given":"Luc"},{"family":"Hartmann","given":"Hans J"},{"family":"Molina-Ureña","given":"Helena"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
Forest Ecosystems
(e.g., Mesoamerican Biological Corridor) 

Co-management institutions can be an effective way to mediate 
transboundary resource management problems including competition between users as well as ensuring fit between ecological scales and political scales of decision-making. A bio-regional lens has been used in establishing protected areas and developing forest co-management institutions in some regions such as central America (i.e., Mesoamerican Biological Corridor) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JOq7jq8s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barquet, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Barquet, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":804,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R293ZBP7"],"itemData":{"id":804,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Norsk Geografisk Tidsskrift-Norwegian Journal of Geography","ISSN":"0029-1951","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Norsk Geografisk Tidsskrift-Norwegian Journal of Geography","note":"number: 5\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"265-276","title":"Building a bioregion through transboundary conservation in Central America","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Barquet","given":"Karina"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
Transboundary Protected Areas (e.g., Great Limpopo Transfrontier  Park)
Transboundary of frontier governance arrangement in southern Africa have created opportunities for new kinds of governance of lands, species and protected areas. The South Africa Development Community signed a Treaty in 1992 aimed at managing complex natural resource management problems and creating economic opportunities (e.g., cross-border trade). It is recognized that many peoples in the region are dependent on species that span or are dynamic across borders.  The potential for shared decision-making over these natural resources is seen as an important pathway towards sustainable use of natural resources as well as sustainable economic development more broadly. Despite the Treaty being more than twenty years old, conflicts persist due in large part to economic uncertainties and stresses on local communities and governments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QoqJVT0I","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Katerere, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Katerere, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":806,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QH8MSFGH"],"itemData":{"id":806,"type":"book","publisher":"IUCN, Regional Office for Southern Africa Harare, Zimbabwe","title":"A critique of transboundary natural resource management in Southern Africa","author":[{"family":"Katerere","given":"Yemi"},{"family":"Hill","given":"Ryan"},{"family":"Moyo","given":"Sam"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other kinds of transboundary agreements and initiatives such as the Great Limpopo Transfrontier Park have also had mixed success; rather than decentralizing power to local people, large NGOs and donors may have centralized power elsewhere and limited the rights and interests of local organizations and communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kdBqvsUU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Duffy, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Duffy, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":805,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YY9ZLVG8"],"itemData":{"id":805,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Political geography","ISSN":"0962-6298","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Political geography","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"89-112","title":"The potential and pitfalls of global environmental governance: The politics of transfrontier conservation areas in Southern Africa","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Duffy","given":"Rosaleen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

      Over the last forty-five years, co-management arrangements have been emerging in various regions and for diverse species and habitats around the world. Conservation areas or protected areas have proven to be managed in ways that ensure sustainable use where co-management systems have been designed. Inclusive approaches to governance are evidenced as effective in many nation-states and with respect of numerous wild species including those of wild species, fisheries, timber and other forest resources. The engagement of citizens in governance can take many forms and range in rigor and depth along various kinds of continuums (e.g., Arnstein’s Ladder of Participation) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aRA8s8K0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arnstein, 1969)","plainCitation":"(Arnstein, 1969)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34020,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D5I958L8"],"itemData":{"id":34020,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract The heated controversy over ?citizen participation,? ?citizen control?, and ?maximum feasible involvement of the poor,? has been waged largely in terms of exacerbated rhetoric and misleading euphemisms. To encourage a more enlightened dialogue, a typology of citizen participation is offered using examples from three federal social programs: urban renewal, anti-poverty, and Model Cities. The typology, which is designed to be provocative, is arranged in a ladder pattern with each rung corresponding to the extent of citizens' power in determining the plan and/or program. Abstract The heated controversy over ?citizen participation,? ?citizen control?, and ?maximum feasible involvement of the poor,? has been waged largely in terms of exacerbated rhetoric and misleading euphemisms. To encourage a more enlightened dialogue, a typology of citizen participation is offered using examples from three federal social programs: urban renewal, anti-poverty, and Model Cities. The typology, which is designed to be provocative, is arranged in a ladder pattern with each rung corresponding to the extent of citizens' power in determining the plan and/or program.","container-title":"Journal of the American Planning Association","DOI":"10.1080/01944366908977225","ISSN":"01944363","issue":"4","note":"PMID: 9871","page":"216–224","title":"A Ladder Of Citizen Participation","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Arnstein","given":"Sherry R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1969"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Co-management is considered key in addressing many kinds of sustainable use problems due to the integration of diverse stakeholders into the rule-making process, monitoring, and enforcement ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BYgEXWob","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 1991)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 1991)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32550,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P8KXDIEG"],"itemData":{"id":32550,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Alternatives","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"12–18","title":"Co-management: the evolution of the theory and practice of joint administration of living resources","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"George","given":"P."},{"family":"Preston","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Table 4.3. Success Factors in Co-management. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5QLOTDCV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. R. Armitage, Plummer, Berkes, Arthur, Charles, Davidson-Hunt, Diduck, Doubleday, Johnson, Marschke,, 2009)","plainCitation":"(D. R. Armitage, Plummer, Berkes, Arthur, Charles, Davidson-Hunt, Diduck, Doubleday, Johnson, Marschke,, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32554,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FACHXNVG"],"itemData":{"id":32554,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","DOI":"10.1890/070089","language":"en","page":"95–102","title":"Adaptive co-management for social–ecological complexity","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Armitage","given":"D.R."},{"family":"Plummer","given":"R."},{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Arthur","given":"R.I."},{"family":"Charles","given":"A.T."},{"family":"Davidson-Hunt","given":"I.J."},{"family":"Diduck","given":"A.P."},{"family":"Doubleday","given":"N.C."},{"family":"Johnson","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Marschke","given":"M."},{"family":"McConney","given":"P."},{"family":"Pinkerton","given":"E.W."},{"family":"Wollenberg","given":"E.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} © The Ecological Society of America under license number 5154840749156.


4.2.2.2.5. Indigenous rights
Indigenous peoples and rights are defined and recognized variously in many parts of the globe. A common pattern has been the history of colonization and its impacts on indigenous cultural-spiritual relationships, access and use of natural resources including wild species. A discussion of indigenous rights is a critical question to this assessment because many aspects of unsustainable use of wild species (i.e., compromised health, extirpation, etc.), have direct impacts on the health and well-being of indigenous peoples ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rYEDcphu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Godoy, 2005; Posey, 1996; United Nations, 2019; Westra, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Godoy, 2005; Posey, 1996; United Nations, 2019; Westra, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":842,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YRACPBPJ"],"itemData":{"id":842,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annu. Rev. Anthropol","language":"en","page":"121–38","title":"The Effect of Market Economies on the Well-Being of Indigenous Peoples and on Their Use of Renewable Natural Resources","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Godoy","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Reyes-García","given":"Victoria"},{"family":"Byron","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Leonard","given":"William R."},{"family":"Vadez","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32601,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z475F963"],"itemData":{"id":32601,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment: Science and Policy for Sustainable Development","ISSN":"0013-9157","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Environment: Science and Policy for Sustainable Development","note":"number: 8\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"6-45","title":"Protecting indigenous peoples' rights to biodiversity","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Posey","given":"Darrell A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}},{"id":32422,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CAXKGHAL"],"itemData":{"id":32422,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: New York","title":"World Urbanization Prospects 2018: Highlights. Department of Economic and Social Affairs","author":[{"family":"United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32600,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EYM5LWWW"],"itemData":{"id":32600,"type":"book","ISBN":"1-84977-117-0","publisher":"Routledge","title":"Environmental justice and the rights of Indigenous peoples: International and domestic legal perspectives","author":[{"family":"Westra","given":"Laura"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is because many indigenous peoples rely heavily on hunting, trapping, fishing, gathering of berries and medicinal plants, and visiting of sacred sites are critical to food security ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nkdMs7n8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kuhnlein, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Kuhnlein, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":824,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3HXHIRUC"],"itemData":{"id":824,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Public health nutrition","ISSN":"1368-9800","issue":"13","journalAbbreviation":"Public health nutrition","note":"number: 13\\npublisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"2415-2424","title":"Food system sustainability for health and well-being of Indigenous Peoples","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Kuhnlein","given":"Harriet V"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, cultural continuity, and health and well-being ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xL3wvNpB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biddle & Swee, 2012; C. P. Burgess, 2009; IUCN, 2019b; Stephens, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Biddle & Swee, 2012; C. P. Burgess, 2009; IUCN, 2019b; Stephens, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32606,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CDJDK86K"],"itemData":{"id":32606,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Australian Geographer","ISSN":"0004-9182","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Australian Geographer","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"215-232","title":"The relationship between wellbeing and Indigenous land, language and culture in Australia","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Biddle","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Swee","given":"Hannah"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":32564,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4LVISVLJ"],"itemData":{"id":32564,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Medical journal of Australia","ISSN":"0025-729X","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Medical journal of Australia","note":"number: 10","page":"567-572","title":"Healthy country, healthy people: The relationship between Indigenous health status and “caring for country”","volume":"190","author":[{"family":"Burgess","given":"Christopher P"},{"family":"Johnston","given":"Fay H"},{"family":"Berry","given":"Helen L"},{"family":"McDonnell","given":"Joseph"},{"family":"Yibarbuk","given":"Dean"},{"family":"Gunabarra","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Mileran","given":"Albert"},{"family":"Bailie","given":"Ross S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":32423,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WK4RY2FQ"],"itemData":{"id":32423,"type":"webpage","title":"Red List of Threatened Species","URL":"https://www.iucnredlist.org/search/stats?taxonomies=101666&searchType=species","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32607,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y3PFJCP2"],"itemData":{"id":32607,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The lancet","ISSN":"0140-6736","issue":"9527","journalAbbreviation":"The lancet","note":"number: 9527\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"2019-2028","title":"Disappearing, displaced, and undervalued: a call to action for Indigenous health worldwide","volume":"367","author":[{"family":"Stephens","given":"Carolyn"},{"family":"Porter","given":"John"},{"family":"Nettleton","given":"Clive"},{"family":"Willis","given":"Ruth"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indigenous peoples also have well established knowledge practices and belief systems that have contributed to the sustainable use of wild species for generations; thus, the support and recognition of indigenous rights are a major pathway toward wild species conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Okm5M7Qc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gadgil, F., and D. 1993)","plainCitation":"(Gadgil, F., and D. 1993)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":866,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YAGA3CHZ"],"itemData":{"id":866,"type":"book","event-place":"Biodiversity","language":"en","number-of-pages":"151–156","publisher":"Ecology","publisher-place":"Biodiversity","title":"Indigenous Knowledge for Biodiversity Conservation Ambio: 22: 2/3","author":[{"family":"Gadgil","given":"M.Berkes"},{"family":"F.","given":"Folke"},{"literal":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In 1982, United Nations Special Rapporteur of the Subcommission on the Prevention of Discrimination and Protection of Minorities released a global report on systemic discrimination ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ztYAFCTS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations, 1982)","plainCitation":"(United Nations, 1982)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":845,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q6EDQNRZ"],"itemData":{"id":845,"type":"document","title":"World Charter for Nature","URL":"https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/39295","author":[{"family":"United Nations","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1982"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Following this, over 30 years of consultation and negotiations between nation-states and indigenous peoples led to the creation of the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pGHvW7dY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations, 2007)","plainCitation":"(United Nations, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RUVQJ8RP","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YFGICVWX"],"itemData":{"id":26833,"type":"report","language":"en","title":"United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples—Resolution adopted by the General Assembly on 13 September 2007","URL":"https://www.un.org/development/desa/indigenouspeoples/wp-content/uploads/sites/19/2018/11/UNDRIP_E_web.pdf","author":[{"family":"United Nations","given":"United"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples sets out clear principles in respect of indigenous rights related to land and resource use. These rights are recognized as highly consistent with other principles of the United Nations including the Sustainable Development Goals.
      The extent to which these rights are recognized by nation-states varies significantly.  In some countries, there is little recognition of these rights nor ratification of the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples principles. Canada, although slow to acknowledge the protocol, has made significant efforts to do so in recent years. In many ways, the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peopleis a fortification of other kinds of rights held by indigenous peoples and protected by the Canadian Constitution (1982), through historic and modern treaties, and Supreme Court case law. Many of these legal instruments protect the rights of indigenous peoples to hunt, fish, trap for subsistence, or sustain a reasonable livelihood based on their traditional lands and resources. These rights are often interpreted by the courts as superseding provincial and federal laws and regulations related to management, such as those associated with the east coast lobster fishery as has been the case in recognition of the Marshall Decision. indigenous peoples in Canada bear a significant burden of ill will by non-indigenous peoples who fear these rights will lead to declines or losses in resources. However, there is no evidence that recognition of indigenous rights results in “over-harvesting” and species decline (whether fish, wild species, timber or other resources) but data shows that the recognition of indigenous rights have led to significant conservation outcomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Amt5Bo7a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lynch, 2016; Popp, 2019; Stephenson, 2018a)","plainCitation":"(Lynch, 2016; Popp, 2019; Stephenson, 2018a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":227,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S7J6FZV9"],"itemData":{"id":227,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Though reported capture fisheries are dominated by marine production, inland fish and fisheries make substantial contributions to meeting the challenges faced by individuals, society, and the environment in a changing global landscape. Inland capture fisheries and aquaculture contribute over 40% to the world’s reported finfish production from less than 0.01% of the total volume of water on earth. These fisheries provide food for billions and livelihoods for millions of people worldwide. Herein, using supporting evidence from the literature, we review 10 reasons why inland fish and fisheries are important to the individual (food security, economic security, empowerment), to society (cultural services, recreational services, human health and well-being, knowledge transfer and capacity building), and to the environment (ecosystem function and biodiversity, as aquatic “canaries”, the “green food” movement). However, the current limitations to valuing the services provided by inland fish and fisheries make comparison with other water resource users extremely difficult. This list can serve to demonstrate the importance of inland fish and fisheries, a necessary first step to better incorporating them into agriculture, land-use, and water resource planning, where they are currently often underappreciated or ignored.","container-title":"Environmental Reviews","DOI":"10.1139/er-2015-0064","ISSN":"1181-8700, 1208-6053","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Environ. Rev.","language":"en","page":"115-121","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The social, economic, and environmental importance of inland fish and fisheries","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/er-2015-0064","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Lynch","given":"Abigail J."},{"family":"Cooke","given":"Steven J."},{"family":"Deines","given":"Andrew M."},{"family":"Bower","given":"Shannon D."},{"family":"Bunnell","given":"David B."},{"family":"Cowx","given":"Ian G."},{"family":"Nguyen","given":"Vivian M."},{"family":"Nohner","given":"Joel"},{"family":"Phouthavong","given":"Kaviphone"},{"family":"Riley","given":"Betsy"},{"family":"Rogers","given":"Mark W."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"William W."},{"family":"Woelmer","given":"Whitney"},{"family":"Youn","given":"So-Jung"},{"family":"Beard","given":"T. Douglas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",6]]}}},{"id":825,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L3UMLMSZ"],"itemData":{"id":825,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","ISSN":"1087-1209","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"159-167","title":"The rise of moose co-management and integration of Indigenous knowledge","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Popp","given":"Jesse N"},{"family":"Priadka","given":"Pauline"},{"family":"Kozmik","given":"Cory"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":859,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BJX8CUB4"],"itemData":{"id":859,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fisheries sustainability is recognized to have four pillars: ecological, economic, social (including cultural) and institutional (or governance). Although international agreements, and legislation in many jurisdictions, call for implementation of all four pillars of sustainability, the social, economic and institutional aspects (i.e., the “human dimensions”) have not been comprehensively and collectively addressed to date. This study describes a framework for comprehensive fisheries evaluation developed by the Canadian Fisheries Research Network (CFRN) that articulates the full spectrum of ecological, economic, social and institutional objectives required under international agreements, together with candidate performance indicators for sustainable fisheries. The CFRN framework is aimed at practical fisheries evaluation and management and has a relatively balanced distribution of elements across the four pillars of sustainability relative to 10 alternative management decision support tools and indicator scorecards, which are heavily focused on ecological and economic aspects. The CFRN framework has five immediate uses: (a) It can serve as a logic frame for defining management objectives; (b) it can be used to define alternate management options to achieve given objectives; (c) it can serve as a tool for comparing management scenarios/options in decision support frameworks; (d) it can be employed to create a report card for comprehensive fisheries management evaluation; and (e) it is a tool for practical implementation of an integrated social–ecological system approach.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/faf.12296","ISSN":"1467-2979","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"853-873","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Evaluating and implementing social–ecological systems: A comprehensive approach to sustainable fisheries","title-short":"Evaluating and implementing social–ecological systems","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/faf.12296","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Stephenson","given":"Robert L."},{"family":"Paul","given":"Stacey"},{"family":"Wiber","given":"Melanie"},{"family":"Angel","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Benson","given":"Ashleen J."},{"family":"Charles","given":"Anthony"},{"family":"Chouinard","given":"Omer"},{"family":"Clemens","given":"Marc"},{"family":"Edwards","given":"Dan"},{"family":"Foley","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Jennings","given":"Lindsay"},{"family":"Jones","given":"Owen"},{"family":"Lane","given":"Dan"},{"family":"McIsaac","given":"Jim"},{"family":"Mussells","given":"Claire"},{"family":"Neis","given":"Barbara"},{"family":"Nordstrom","given":"Bethany"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Courtenay"},{"family":"Pinkerton","given":"Evelyn"},{"family":"Saunders","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Squires","given":"Kevin"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U. Rashid"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, in some key cases, the subsistence and rights of indigenous peoples have often been undermined by poor management of resources  For example, contamination of the Athabasca river fishery due to oil sands mining ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ArLS2kdQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Westman and Joly 2019; Kelly, E. N., Schindler, D. W., Hodson, P. V., Short, J. W., Radmanovich, R., & Nielsen, C. C. 2010)","plainCitation":"(Westman and Joly 2019; Kelly, E. N., Schindler, D. W., Hodson, P. V., Short, J. W., Radmanovich, R., & Nielsen, C. C. 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":826,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2S5KQ9MS"],"itemData":{"id":826,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","ISSN":"0300-7839","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Human Ecology","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Springer","page":"233-243","title":"Oil Sands Extraction in Alberta, Canada: a Review of Impacts and Processes Concerning Indigenous Peoples","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Westman","given":"Clinton N"},{"family":"Joly","given":"Tara L"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":810,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W52FS8B8"],"itemData":{"id":810,"type":"article-newspaper","title":"Oil sands development contributes elements toxic at low concentrations to the Athabasca River and its tributaries. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 107(37), 16178-16183.","author":[{"literal":"Kelly, E. N., Schindler, D. W., Hodson, P. V., Short, J. W., Radmanovich, R., & Nielsen, C. C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} collapse of Atlantic and Pacific fish populations  due to industrial fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oRZAmVoU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Atlas, 2021; Lotze & Milewski, 2004; N. Turner, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Atlas, 2021; Lotze & Milewski, 2004; N. Turner, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"cita