      Similar patterns are visible in many other countries with strong evidence of the impact of biodiversity loss on the health and well-being of indigenous peoples ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YGEOsuLY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hunter, 2015; Kuhnlein, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Hunter, 2015; Kuhnlein, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":824,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3HXHIRUC"],"itemData":{"id":824,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Public health nutrition","ISSN":"1368-9800","issue":"13","journalAbbreviation":"Public health nutrition","note":"number: 13\\npublisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"2415-2424","title":"Food system sustainability for health and well-being of Indigenous Peoples","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Kuhnlein","given":"Harriet V"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":33511,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A7KZGC5X"],"itemData":{"id":33511,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Sociology","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-soc-073014-112223","language":"en","page":"377–397","title":"The Environmental Dimensions of Migration","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Hunter","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Luna","given":"J.K."},{"family":"Norton","given":"R.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fishing, forestry, hunting, gathering practices are not only essential component of their traditional diets but of culture and well-being ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ld1tXNmT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(von der Porten, 2019)","plainCitation":"(von der Porten, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33481,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KUZW45PB"],"itemData":{"id":33481,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"AlterNative: An International Journal of Indigenous Peoples","ISSN":"1177-1801","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"AlterNative: An International Journal of Indigenous Peoples","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: SAGE Publications Sage UK: London, England","page":"62-74","title":"Indigenous nationhood and herring governance: strategies for the reassertion of Indigenous authority and inter-Indigenous solidarity regarding marine resources","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Porten","given":"Suzanne","non-dropping-particle":"von der"},{"family":"Corntassel","given":"Jeff"},{"family":"Mucina","given":"Devi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hence rights to terrestrial and marine resources and territory are inseparable from broader indigenous rights to self-determination ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"L1mHBWmU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, 2016a; E. R. Larson, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, 2016a; E. R. Larson, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32305,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N2XQXPUW"],"itemData":{"id":32305,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PeerJ","ISSN":"2167-8359","page":"e1915","title":"Phylogenetic species delimitation for crayfishes of the genus Pacifastacus","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Larson","given":"Eric R"},{"family":"Castelin","given":"Magalie"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Bronwyn W"},{"family":"Olden","given":"Julian D"},{"family":"Abbott","given":"Cathryn L"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":34761,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P2LXMUWA","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZNQMHBSF"],"itemData":{"id":34761,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0166681","issue":"12","title":"A global estimate of seafood consumption by coastal indigenous peoples","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85002898790&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0166681&partnerID=40&md5=597d1afb26d002398d34360f6ed6117c","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"Andrés M."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."},{"family":"Weatherdon","given":"L.V."},{"family":"Ota","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} as well as a broader suite of rights protected by the United Nations Declarations on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples and Human Rights more broadly. Efforts led by indigenous organizations to reduce poverty through sustainable use of biodiversity tend to include key elements of indigenous resurgence strategies, involving the reinvigoration and reestablishment of indigenous land tenure, decision-making, rights, and leadership ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"M9B9Bpdk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(von der Porten, 2019)","plainCitation":"(von der Porten, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33481,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KUZW45PB"],"itemData":{"id":33481,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"AlterNative: An International Journal of Indigenous Peoples","ISSN":"1177-1801","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"AlterNative: An International Journal of Indigenous Peoples","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: SAGE Publications Sage UK: London, England","page":"62-74","title":"Indigenous nationhood and herring governance: strategies for the reassertion of Indigenous authority and inter-Indigenous solidarity regarding marine resources","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Porten","given":"Suzanne","non-dropping-particle":"von der"},{"family":"Corntassel","given":"Jeff"},{"family":"Mucina","given":"Devi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Amazon forests managed by indigenous communities show lower rates of deforestation and carbon emissions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"imvcMFcz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Blackman & Veit, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Blackman & Veit, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33483,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IY9INEF3"],"itemData":{"id":33483,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2018.06.016","language":"fr","page":"56–67","title":"d Amazon indigenous communities cut forest carbon emissions","volume":"153","author":[{"family":"Blackman","given":"A."},{"family":"Veit","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Globally, indigenous territories see lower rates of ecosystem health declines compared to other areas (although trends are nonetheless mostly negative) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HKOLtV4b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Díaz, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33316,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TMY48EZ5"],"itemData":{"id":33316,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The human impact on life on Earth has increased sharply since the 1970s, driven by the demands of a growing population with rising average per capita income. Nature is currently supplying more materials than ever before, but this has come at the high cost of unprecedented global declines in the extent and integrity of ecosystems, distinctness of local ecological communities, abundance and number of wild species, and the number of local domesticated varieties. Such changes reduce vital benefits that people receive from nature and threaten the quality of life of future generations. Both the benefits of an expanding economy and the costs of reducing nature’s benefits are unequally distributed. The fabric of life on which we all depend—nature and its contributions to people—is unravelling rapidly. Despite the severity of the threats and lack of enough progress in tackling them to date, opportunities exist to change future trajectories through transformative action. Such action must begin immediately, however, and address the root economic, social, and technological causes of nature’s deterioration.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aax3100","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6471","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"eaax3100","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Pervasive human-driven decline of life on Earth points to the need for transformative change","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.aax3100","volume":"366","author":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Settele","given":"Josef"},{"family":"Brondízio","given":"Eduardo S."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"Hien T."},{"family":"Agard","given":"John"},{"family":"Arneth","given":"Almut"},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Brauman","given":"Kate A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"Lucas A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"Kazuhito"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Jianguo"},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"Suneetha M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"Guy F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Zsolt"},{"family":"Obura","given":"David"},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Polasky","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Purvis","given":"Andy"},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"Jona"},{"family":"Reyers","given":"Belinda"},{"family":"Chowdhury","given":"Rinku Roy"},{"family":"Shin","given":"Yunne-Jai"},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"Ingrid"},{"family":"Willis","given":"Katherine J."},{"family":"Zayas","given":"Cynthia N."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12,13]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition, it is important to recognize that indigenous worldviews and decisions are unique and should be included and respected in approaches to knowledge co-creation and resource co-management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vuXftBaG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hill, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Hill, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33315,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J2FVG23E"],"itemData":{"id":33315,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2019.12.006","ISSN":"18773435","journalAbbreviation":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","language":"en","page":"8-20","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Working with Indigenous, local and scientific knowledge in assessments of nature and nature’s linkages with people","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1877343519301447","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Hill","given":"Rosemary"},{"family":"Adem","given":"Çi?dem"},{"family":"Alangui","given":"Wilfred V"},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Zsolt"},{"family":"Aumeeruddy-Thomas","given":"Yildiz"},{"family":"Bridgewater","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Tengö","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Thaman","given":"Randy"},{"family":"Adou Yao","given":"Constant Y"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Carino","given":"Joji"},{"family":"Carneiro da Cunha","given":"Manuela"},{"family":"Diaw","given":"Mariteuw C"},{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Viviana E"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Judy"},{"family":"Hardison","given":"Preston"},{"family":"Ichikawa","given":"Kaoru"},{"family":"Kariuki","given":"Peris"},{"family":"Karki","given":"Madhav"},{"family":"Lyver","given":"Phil OB"},{"family":"Malmer","given":"Pernilla"},{"family":"Masardule","given":"Onel"},{"family":"Oteng Yeboah","given":"Alfred A"},{"family":"Pacheco","given":"Diego"},{"family":"Pataridze","given":"Tamar"},{"family":"Perez","given":"Edgar"},{"family":"Roué","given":"Michèle-Marie"},{"family":"Roba","given":"Hassan"},{"family":"Rubis","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Saito","given":"Osamu"},{"family":"Xue","given":"Dayuan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In addition to the adverse impacts of formal institutions on Indigenous rights and sustainable use of wild species, there is clear evidence of how Indigenous knowledges and customary laws have been key to sustainable use of wild species in many places globally.  In addition to ensuring many conservation outcomes, Indigenous systems of stewardship have been protective of food security, health, culture and well-being ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"q0xwDeA2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2000; M. B. Gadgil, 1993)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2000; M. B. Gadgil, 1993)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":847,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4FYYZ8LU"],"itemData":{"id":847,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","DOI":"10.1890/1051-0761(2000)010[1251:ROTEKA]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"1051-0761","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Applications","language":"en","page":"1251-1262","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Rediscovery of Traditional Ecological Knowledge as Adaptive Management","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1890/1051-0761(2000)010[1251:ROTEKA]2.0.CO;2","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Colding","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",10]]}}},{"id":866,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YAGA3CHZ"],"itemData":{"id":866,"type":"book","event-place":"Biodiversity","language":"en","number-of-pages":"151–156","publisher":"Ecology","publisher-place":"Biodiversity","title":"Indigenous Knowledge for Biodiversity Conservation Ambio: 22: 2/3","author":[{"family":"Gadgil","given":"M.Berkes"},{"family":"F.","given":"Folke"},{"literal":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, in the case of forest ecosystems in Nepal, customary laws associated with the conservation of ‘ranivana’ (community forests) were established by shamans and priests; over generations, they have proven successful in ensuring the conservation of valued forest species as well as equitable access for benefits to people (e.g., fuelwood, fodder and medicinal plants) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qE2F5qhK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Khatri, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Khatri, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33498,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PG9G58D2"],"itemData":{"id":33498,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity Conservation in the Kangchenjunga Landscape","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity Conservation in the Kangchenjunga Landscape","page":"151","title":"Traditional practices and customary Laws of the Kirat people of Eastern Nepal and comparison with Nepal’s statutory Laws","author":[{"family":"Khatri","given":"Nrishima Kumar"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many more examples exist (see Table 4.4).  A key challenge for these and other customary law systems is the extent to which they are in conflict with or are not recognized as a system of laws by nation states.  Despite evidence of the social and cultural benefits, many kinds of rules are little recognized. 
 
Table 4.4. Examples of Indigenous Customary Laws and Related Norms that Support Sustainable Use of Wild Species.  (1)ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MkRmBmHP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kosoe, 2020; Ntiamoa-Baidu, 1991)","plainCitation":"(Kosoe, 2020; Ntiamoa-Baidu, 1991)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33496,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JSHGS3S8"],"itemData":{"id":33496,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"GeoJournal","ISSN":"1572-9893","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"GeoJournal","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Springer","page":"1057-1074","title":"From sacrilege to sustainability: the role of indigenous knowledge systems in biodiversity conservation in the Upper West Region of Ghana","volume":"85","author":[{"family":"Kosoe","given":"Enoch Akwasi"},{"family":"Adjei","given":"Prince Osei-Wusu"},{"family":"Diawuo","given":"Francis"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":33497,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P3CVEXV3"],"itemData":{"id":33497,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","ISSN":"0169-2046","issue":"1-3","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape and Urban Planning","note":"number: 1-3\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"41-46","title":"Conservation of coastal lagoons in Ghana: the traditional approach","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Ntiamoa-Baidu","given":"Yaa"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; (2) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AED6oAzB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sudo, 1984)","plainCitation":"(Sudo, 1984)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33495,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8TICFQFD"],"itemData":{"id":33495,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Senri ethnological studies","ISSN":"0387-6004","journalAbbreviation":"Senri ethnological studies","page":"203-230","title":"Social organization and types of sea tenure in Micronesia","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Sudo","given":"Ken'ichi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1984"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (3) (4) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RvalLuR0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Mattos Vieira, 2015)","plainCitation":"(de Mattos Vieira, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33488,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V66QPEJ3"],"itemData":{"id":33488,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation and Society","ISSN":"0972-4923","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation and Society","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: JSTOR","page":"254-264","title":"Participatory monitoring and management of subsistence hunting in the Piagaçu-Purus reserve, Brazil","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Mattos Vieira","given":"Marina Albuquerque Regina","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Muhlen","given":"Eduardo Matheus","non-dropping-particle":"von"},{"family":"Shepard Jr","given":"Glenn H"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (12) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"35Kra8J8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Waller & Reo, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Waller & Reo, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33478,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YWZ6QGJJ"],"itemData":{"id":33478,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Indigenous peoples manage forestlands and wildlife differently than public and private forestland managers. To evaluate ecological outcomes from these differences, we compared the structure, composition, and diversity of Ojibwe and Menominee tribal forests to nearby nontribal forestlands in northern Wisconsin. These indigenous peoples seek to manage forests for mature conditions, accommodate wolves and other predators, and hunt deer to sustain traditional livelihood values. Their forests are often more mature with higher tree volume, higher rates of tree regeneration, more plant diversity, and fewer invasive species than nearby nontribal forestlands. In contrast, nontribal forestlands lost appreciable plant diversity in the 20th century and have failed to regenerate tree species sensitive to deer herbivory. Ensuing shifts in forest composition and wildlife populations have jeopardized the ability of managers to sustain wildlife and meet certification standards on nontribal forestlands. Lessons from tribal forestlands could help improve the sustainable management of nontribal public forestlands.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-09865-230145","ISSN":"17083087","title":"First stewards: Ecological outcomes of forest and wildlife stewardship by indigenous peoples of Wisconsin, USA","author":[{"family":"Waller","given":"Donald M."},{"family":"Reo","given":"Nicholas J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (13) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZsOkAaFR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Deur, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Deur, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33484,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UC2WAMGW"],"itemData":{"id":33484,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","ISSN":"0278-0771","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnobiology","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: BioOne","page":"296-322","title":"“A caretaker responsibility”: revisiting Klamath and Modoc traditions of plant community management","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Deur","given":"Douglas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (14) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jvbQRGK8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. J. Turner & Turner, 2008)","plainCitation":"(N. J. Turner & Turner, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WJ52FNBK"],"itemData":{"id":19,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Knowledge and practices of indigenous peoples relating to local plants used for food, medicine, materials, and other purposes are threatened in many parts of the world. The reasons for declining knowledge and use of traditional resources are complex and multifaceted. We review a series of case examples of culturally valued food plants in British Columbia and identify a suite of interacting social and environmental factors that have resulted in decreased use of and dwindling cultural knowledge about these plants over the past 150 years. Reasons for this loss include compounding influences of changing knowledge systems owing to religious conversion and residential schools, loss of indigenous languages, loss of time and opportunity for traditional practices owing to participation in the wage economy, increasing urbanization of indigenous populations, loss of access to traditional resources, restriction of management practices for sustaining these resources, and most recently, forces of globalization and industrialization. Efforts to renew and restore traditional practices and relationships with plants and environments must recognize the cumulative effects of these factors and find ways to retain and reinforce the knowledge and practices still held by individuals and communities, to reverse some of the negative influences on cultural retention, and to develop new, relevant, and effective ways to revitalize languages, cultures, and ethnobotanical knowledge within contemporary contexts.","container-title":"Botany","DOI":"10.1139/B07-020","ISSN":"1916-2790, 1916-2804","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Botany","language":"en","page":"103-115","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"“Where our women used to get the food”: cumulative effects and loss of ethnobotanical knowledge and practice; case study from coastal British Columbia","title-short":"“Where our women used to get the food”","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/B07-020","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Turner","given":"Nancy J."},{"family":"Turner","given":"Katherine L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (15) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tRqmTPOs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. B. McKemey, 2021)","plainCitation":"(M. B. McKemey, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33492,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5AP37S2Y"],"itemData":{"id":33492,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Austral Ecology","ISSN":"1442-9993","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Austral Ecology","note":"number: 7\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"1011-1029","title":"Co-producing a fire and seasons calendar to support renewed Indigenous cultural fire management","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"McKemey","given":"Michelle B"},{"family":"Ens","given":"Emilie J"},{"family":"Hunter","given":"John T"},{"family":"Ridges","given":"Malcolm"},{"family":"Costello","given":"Oliver"},{"family":"Reid","given":"Nick CH"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (16) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2Za4ZKgn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Samakov & Berkes, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Samakov & Berkes, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33491,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M5XDSNX5"],"itemData":{"id":33491,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","ISSN":"1875-0281","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of the Commons","note":"number: 1","title":"Spiritual commons: sacred sites as core of community-conserved areas in Kyrgyzstan","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Samakov","given":"Aibek"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, (18) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"szQmmwJm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Gadgil, 1987)","plainCitation":"(M. Gadgil, 1987)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32986,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MNEXFEAH"],"itemData":{"id":32986,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Development: seeds of change","journalAbbreviation":"Development: seeds of change","page":"26-30","title":"Social restraints on exploiting nature: the Indian experience","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Gadgil","given":"Madhav"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1987"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (19) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aHDzRAlZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. Parlee, 2006)","plainCitation":"(B. Parlee, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34972,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WM2FB3ZB"],"itemData":{"id":34972,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","language":"en","page":"515–528","title":"Indigenous knowledge of ecological variability and commons management: a case study on berry harvesting from northern Canada","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"B."},{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Council","given":"Teetl’it Gwich’in Renewable Resources"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; (20) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZHodfBWc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gangadhar, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Gangadhar, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33317,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P6FJFZQK"],"itemData":{"id":33317,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"African Journal of Environmental Economics and Management ISSN","ISSN":"2375-0707","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"African Journal of Environmental Economics and Management ISSN","note":"number: 1","page":"374-383","title":"A study of the potentials of traditional natural resources management for biodiversity conservation","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Gangadhar","given":"Jagdish Bihari"},{"family":"Khan","given":"Manmohan"},{"family":"Premji","given":"Akshay Ram"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (21) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"b1J3eEik","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. R. Hickey & Johannes, 2002)","plainCitation":"(F. R. Hickey & Johannes, 2002)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33494,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EAMS2IG5"],"itemData":{"id":33494,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"SPC traditional marine resource management and knowledge information bulletin","journalAbbreviation":"SPC traditional marine resource management and knowledge information bulletin","page":"8-21","title":"Recent evolution of village-based marine resource management in Vanuatu","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Hickey","given":"Francis R"},{"family":"Johannes","given":"Robert E"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (22) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"e4bswiUl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chowdhury, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Chowdhury, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33500,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4Y4PVGVY"],"itemData":{"id":33500,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity","ISSN":"1488-8386","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"23-38","title":"Dietetic use of wild animals and traditional cultural beliefs in the Mro community of Bangladesh: an insight into biodiversity conservation","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Chowdhury","given":"Mohammad Shaheed Hossain"},{"family":"Izumiyama","given":"Shigeyuki"},{"family":"Nazia","given":"Nahid"},{"family":"Muhammed","given":"Nur"},{"family":"Koike","given":"Masao"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rMO1bpWO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Laugrand & Oosten, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Laugrand & Oosten, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7B4YY8IU"],"itemData":{"id":33493,"type":"book","ISBN":"0-7735-7636-3","publisher":"McGill-Queen's Press-MQUP","title":"Inuit shamanism and Christianity: Transitions and transformations in the twentieth century","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Laugrand","given":"Frédéric B"},{"family":"Oosten","given":"Jarich G"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; (23) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"of6o1qaJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Meyer-Rochow, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Meyer-Rochow, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33503,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TH6SULWA"],"itemData":{"id":33503,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Springer","page":"1-10","title":"Food taboos: their origins and purposes","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Meyer-Rochow","given":"Victor Benno"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; (24) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1JqKLbwA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kendrick, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Kendrick, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33499,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P39WGYKL"],"itemData":{"id":33499,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic","ISSN":"0004-0843","journalAbbreviation":"Arctic","note":"publisher: JSTOR","page":"175-191","title":"Denésqliné (Chipewyan) knowledge of barren-ground caribou (Rangifer tarandus groenlandicus) movements","author":[{"family":"Kendrick","given":"A"},{"family":"Lyver","given":"P O'B"},{"literal":"?utsël K'é Dene First Nation"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; (25) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SF2GbpTe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Begossi & de Souza Braga, 1992)","plainCitation":"(A. Begossi & de Souza Braga, 1992)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32987,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SJPF3RBE"],"itemData":{"id":32987,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Amazoniana: Limnologia et Oecologia Regionalis Systematis Fluminis Amazonas","ISSN":"0065-6755","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Amazoniana: Limnologia et Oecologia Regionalis Systematis Fluminis Amazonas","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Kommissions-Verlag WG Mühlau","page":"101-118","title":"Food taboos and folk medicine among fishermen from the Tocantins River (Brazil)","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Begossi","given":"Alpina"},{"family":"Souza Braga","given":"Francisco Manoel","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; (26) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4NZyaHn7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pezzuti, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Pezzuti, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33314,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L9GN4N5Y"],"itemData":{"id":33314,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Uses and Taboos of Turtles and Tortoises Along Rio Negro, Amazon Basin","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","DOI":"10.2993/0278-0771-30.1.153","ISSN":"0278-0771","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnobiology","language":"en","page":"153-168","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Uses and Taboos of Turtles and Tortoises Along Rio Negro, Amazon Basin","URL":"http://www.bioone.org/doi/abs/10.2993/0278-0771-30.1.153","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Pezzuti","given":"Juarez C. B."},{"family":"Lima","given":"Jackson Pantoja"},{"family":"Silva","given":"Daniely Félix","non-dropping-particle":"da"},{"family":"Begossi","given":"Alpina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (27) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"h3JmF8bD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nijhawan & Mihu, 2020; E. B. Ross, 1978)","plainCitation":"(Nijhawan & Mihu, 2020; E. B. Ross, 1978)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33505,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZAY24UDR"],"itemData":{"id":33505,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","ISSN":"0278-0771","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnobiology","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: BioOne","page":"149-166","title":"Relations of blood: hunting taboos and wildlife conservation in the Idu Mishmi of Northeast India","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Nijhawan","given":"Sahil"},{"family":"Mihu","given":"Achili"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":33502,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LQMJW8NI"],"itemData":{"id":33502,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Anthropology","ISSN":"0011-3204","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Current Anthropology","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: University of Chicago Press","page":"1-36","title":"Food Taboos, diet, and hunting strategy: the adaptation to animals in amazon cultural ecology [and Comments and Reply]","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Ross","given":"Eric Barry"},{"family":"Arnott","given":"Margaret L"},{"family":"Basso","given":"Ellen B"},{"family":"Beckerman","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Carneiro","given":"Robert L"},{"family":"Forbis","given":"Richard G"},{"family":"Good","given":"Kenneth R"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Knud-Erik"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Allen"},{"family":"Kaplinski","given":"Jaan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1978"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; (28) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kJSwHjao","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Golden & Comaroff, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Golden & Comaroff, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34256,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7D8QVG2X"],"itemData":{"id":34256,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In Madagascar, the constellation of taboos serves as a form of informal regulatory institution and is foundational to Malagasy culture, regardless of class, ethnic group affiliation, and educational background. Many researchers have credited rapid social change as a crucial mechanism for disturbing taboos. Others suggest that taboos are innately historical. However, very little empirical research has assessed the effects of social change on taboos or quantified the stability of taboo systems over time. Here, we use a case study of the ensemble of taboos in northeastern Madagascar, still a critical aspect of social life there, as a lens through which we investigate its degree of stability over time. Our aim was: (1) to describe the food taboos of local Malagasy living in northeastern Madagascar, and (2) to quantitatively assess the stability of these taboos to address certain claims regarding cultural erosion using an empirical, hypothesis-driven approach with rich ethnographic material to aid in interpretation. We investigated the temporal stability of taboos and local adherence to the moral framework, finding that approximately 3.0% of 4857 taboos were not adhered to at least once during the course of a 7-yr follow-up study. Additionally, we quantitatively explored the mediating effects of migration, modernization, and the spread of Western religion on number of taboos and level of adherence. We found that the presence of extra-local groups and migration did not decrease the number of taboos abided by locally, but did increase rates of nonadherence. Modernization accompanied by generational shifts tended to increase rather than erode the number of taboos, and younger individuals adhered to taboos to the same degree as older individuals. The effect of Western religion depended on the denomination, but generally reduced the number of taboos, although it did not affect adherence. The ways in which social change affects the stability of taboos are complex. Migrants tended to adhere to taboos less than long-standing inhabitants, suggesting that attachment to place is significant in maintaining adherence to taboos.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-07589-200241","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Ecology and Society","note":"publisher: The Resilience Alliance","title":"Effects of social change on wildlife consumption taboos in northeastern Madagascar","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol20/iss2/art41/","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Comaroff","given":"Jean"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; (29) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"n5QrZCJ8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Anbacha & Kjosavik, 2018; Argumedo & Pimbert, 2010a; C. Arnold, 2021; Berkes, 2000; Carothers, 2021; Colding, 2003; DeGeorges & Reilly, 2009; Fowler, 2003; A. T. Francis, 2019; Gonzales, 2013; Kimmerer, 2011; McMillen, 2017; Mulrennan, 2014; Phalan, 2011; Sowerwine, 2019; A. Thomas, 2015; Watkin Lui, 2016; J. R. Welch, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Anbacha & Kjosavik, 2018; Argumedo & Pimbert, 2010a; C. Arnold, 2021; Berkes, 2000; Carothers, 2021; Colding, 2003; DeGeorges & Reilly, 2009; Fowler, 2003; A. T. Francis, 2019; Gonzales, 2013; Kimmerer, 2011; McMillen, 2017; Mulrennan, 2014; Phalan, 2011; Sowerwine, 2019; A. Thomas, 2015; Watkin Lui, 2016; J. R. Welch, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":131,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y55PIHKJ"],"itemData":{"id":131,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pastoralism","ISSN":"2041-7136","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Pastoralism","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Springer","page":"1-12","title":"Borana women’s indigenous social network-marro in building household food security: Case study from Ethiopia","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Anbacha","given":"Abiyot Eliyas"},{"family":"Kjosavik","given":"Darley Jose"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":26311,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NG2JPBZX","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZVKA6YNI"],"itemData":{"id":26311,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Development","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"343–349","title":"Bypassing globalization: Barter markets as a new indigenous economy in Peru","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Argumedo","given":"A."},{"family":"Pimbert","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":33506,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KVH3BM3E"],"itemData":{"id":33506,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Australian Geographer","ISSN":"0004-9182","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Australian Geographer","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"131-147","title":"Reciprocal relationships with trees: rekindling Indigenous wellbeing and identity through the Yuin ontology of oneness","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"Crystal"},{"family":"Atchison","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"McKnight","given":"Anthony"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":847,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4FYYZ8LU"],"itemData":{"id":847,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","DOI":"10.1890/1051-0761(2000)010[1251:ROTEKA]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"1051-0761","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Applications","language":"en","page":"1251-1262","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Rediscovery of Traditional Ecological Knowledge as Adaptive Management","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1890/1051-0761(2000)010[1251:ROTEKA]2.0.CO;2","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Colding","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",10]]}}},{"id":33487,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YXEPG7EY"],"itemData":{"id":33487,"type":"article-journal","title":"Indigenous peoples and salmon stewardship: a critical relationship","author":[{"family":"Carothers","given":"Courtney"},{"family":"Black","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Langdon","given":"Stephen J"},{"family":"Donkersloot","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Ringer","given":"Danielle"},{"family":"Coleman","given":"Jesse"},{"family":"Gavenus","given":"Erika R"},{"family":"Justin","given":"Wilson"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Mike"},{"family":"Christiansen","given":"Freddie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":33485,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7PVKJUMS"],"itemData":{"id":33485,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tropical Ecology","ISSN":"0564-3295","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Tropical Ecology","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Varanasi, India [etc.] International Society for Tropical Ecology, 1961-","page":"25-41","title":"Social institutions in ecosystem management and biodiversity conservation","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Colding","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Elmqvist","given":"Thomas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":34494,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WTKASCUI"],"itemData":{"id":34494,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su1030734","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","language":"en","page":"734-788","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Realities of Community Based Natural Resource Management and Biodiversity Conservation in Sub-Saharan Africa","URL":"http://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/1/3/734","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"DeGeorges","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Reilly","given":"Brian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",4,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",9,25]]}}},{"id":33489,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L4ZSS3RW"],"itemData":{"id":33489,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Worldviews: Global Religions, Culture, and Ecology","ISSN":"1363-5247","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Worldviews: Global Religions, Culture, and Ecology","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Brill","page":"303-329","title":"The ecological implications of ancestral religion and reciprocal exchange in a sacred forest in Karendi (Sumba, Indonesia)","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Fowler","given":"Cynthia"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":33504,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6BIENVH8"],"itemData":{"id":33504,"type":"book","ISBN":"1-08-838404-8","publisher":"Cornell University","title":"Haudenosaunee Forest Stewardship","author":[{"family":"Francis","given":"Abraham Thomas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":33501,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/87PLTIG7"],"itemData":{"id":33501,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Seeds of resistance, seeds of hope: Place and agency in the conservation of biodiversity","ISSN":"0816530149","journalAbbreviation":"Seeds of resistance, seeds of hope: Place and agency in the conservation of biodiversity","note":"publisher: University of Arizona Press Tucson","page":"85","title":"Sense of place and Indigenous people’s biodiversity conservation in the Americas","author":[{"family":"Gonzales","given":"Tirso"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33486,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EKNZ89TZ"],"itemData":{"id":33486,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Human dimensions of ecological restoration","page":"257-276","publisher":"Springer","title":"Restoration and reciprocity: the contributions of traditional ecological knowledge","author":[{"family":"Kimmerer","given":"Robin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33510,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6FFFV8B4"],"itemData":{"id":33510,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","ISSN":"1436-378X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Regional Environmental Change","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Springer","page":"579-592","title":"The future is behind us: traditional ecological knowledge and resilience over time on Hawai ‘i Island","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"McMillen","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Ticktin","given":"Tamara"},{"family":"Springer","given":"Hannah Kihalani"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33508,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U586563I"],"itemData":{"id":33508,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geological Society, London, Special Publications","ISSN":"0305-8719","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Geological Society, London, Special Publications","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Geological Society of London","page":"79-102","title":"On the edge: a consideration of the adaptive capacity of Indigenous Peoples in coastal zones from the Arctic to the Tropics","volume":"388","author":[{"family":"Mulrennan","given":"Monica E"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":33512,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YWEYR898"],"itemData":{"id":33512,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The question of how to meet rising food demand at the least cost to biodiversity requires the evaluation of two contrasting alternatives: land sharing, which integrates both objectives on the same land; and land sparing, in which high-yield farming is combined with protecting natural habitats from conversion to agriculture. To test these alternatives, we compared crop yields and densities of bird and tree species across gradients of agricultural intensity in southwest Ghana and northern India. More species were negatively affected by agriculture than benefited from it, particularly among species with small global ranges. For both taxa in both countries, land sparing is a more promising strategy for minimizing negative impacts of food production, at both current and anticipated future levels of production.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1208742","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6047","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"1289-1291","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Reconciling Food Production and Biodiversity Conservation: Land Sharing and Land Sparing Compared","title-short":"Reconciling Food Production and Biodiversity Conservation","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1208742","volume":"333","author":[{"family":"Phalan","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Onial","given":"Malvika"},{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Green","given":"Rhys E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",9,2]]}}},{"id":33509,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A49CE5FI"],"itemData":{"id":33509,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Food Security","ISSN":"1876-4525","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Food Security","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Springer","page":"579-607","title":"Reframing food security by and for Native American communities: A case study among tribes in the Klamath River basin of Oregon and California","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Sowerwine","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Mucioki","given":"Megan"},{"family":"Sarna-Wojcicki","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Hillman","given":"Lisa"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":33507,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4K3JCQU3"],"itemData":{"id":33507,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Social & Cultural Geography","ISSN":"1464-9365","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Social & Cultural Geography","note":"number: 8\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"974-990","title":"Indigenous more-than-humanisms: Relational ethics with the Hurunui River in Aotearoa New Zealand","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Thomas","given":"Amanda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":33490,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BN5M5INA"],"itemData":{"id":33490,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","ISSN":"1087-1209","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"345-360","title":"Motivations for sharing bushmeat with an urban diaspora in Indigenous Australia","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Watkin Lui","given":"Felecia"},{"family":"Stoeckl","given":"Natalie"},{"family":"Delisle","given":"Aurélie"},{"family":"Kiatkoski Kim","given":"Milena"},{"family":"Marsh","given":"Helene"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33313,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZIP96UVB"],"itemData":{"id":33313,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Conservation attention to indigenous hunting with fire in the cerrado largely focuses on sustainability as construed in scientific terms rather than according to indigenous points of view. Towards the goal of reframing the debate in terms more congruent with indigenous perspectives, I address how the Xavante (A’uw?) view ritualized and collective hunting, including hunting with fire, as indispensable means of acquiring gifts by which to celebrate important events, express feelings of respect and gratitude towards others, promote positive social values among male youth, and maintain the group’s ethnic identity. In particular, ritualized exchanges of game meat are necessary and culturally appropriate means of expressing esteem for others at some of life’s most important moments. For the Xavante, the social imperative to give and receive gifts of meat during weddings and initiation ceremonies motivates efforts to maintain the collective hunting with fire tradition in a manner that ensures its long term environmental viability.","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-013-9637-1","ISSN":"0300-7839, 1572-9915","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Hum Ecol","language":"en","page":"47-59","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Xavante Ritual Hunting: Anthropogenic Fire, Reciprocity, and Collective Landscape Management in the Brazilian Cerrado","title-short":"Xavante Ritual Hunting","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10745-013-9637-1","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Welch","given":"James R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 



Fishing

Hunting

Gathering

Non-Extractive
Landscape and habitat taboos
Taboos limit access to sensitive habitats as in (1) coastal lagoons in Ghana and (2) coral reefs in Micronesia.
Rotating goose hunting to limit hunting disturbance (3) Cree in northern Canada and the Piagaçu-Purus Reserve in Brazil (4). 
(5) Sacred forests that protect valued habitats (e.g., Malawi). (6)
Taboos against travel in areas of glaciers as in northwestern Canada (7) (8)
Rituals and ceremonies that support habitat protection and regeneration
Making offerings or ‘paying’ the water as with the (9) Dene of northern Canada; and “first salmon ceremony” harvest rituals that limited early access to salmon spawning areas. 
Respect of key migration corridors and water crossings and calving grounds of barren-ground caribou as with the Gwich’in of northern Canada and Alaska (11) Culling of deer population (harvest for food to limit over browsing and promote biodiversity richness (12).
Cultural burning practices that support the regeneration of medicinal plants as with the (13) Klamath.  First food ceremonies to recognize spirits of berries and signal access to berry patches as in Northwest Coast Canada (14). Cultural burning in Australia to support social fertility and manage bush fire risk (15) 
(16) Cleaning and care of sacred sites to ensure continued spiritual value and healing properties as in Kirghizstan.
Temporal and dynamic taboos and regulations related to harvesting in breeding / reproductive seasons (i.e., closed seasons)
Taboos instituted when stocks are decreasing - promote stock recruitment as in (17) South-East Asia
Bans hunting certain animals in the four months from July to October exist in many (18) Indian villages.
Care and stewardship of plants such as berries and medicinal plants are visible among the Gwich’in Nation (19)
Taboos against entry of sacred meadows limit impact on sensitive wild species habitats (e.g., high altitude areas of India such as Bhadelguar)(20)
Specific Species-Taboos 
Taboos against harvest of sea turtle in (21) Vanuatu reefs.
(22). There are taboos based on ILK against hunting of rare species or those under stress as with the Mro people of  Bangladesh.
Beliefs and rules about ‘not wasting’ meat are associated with the respect for Sedna or Senna as in Inuit culture in Nunavut and Siberia.

(23) Food and medicinal taboos against gathering of plants are common to protect against adverse health or dietary outcomes and/or ensure availability of species other specific uses.
(24). Access of particular wild species habitats can be seen in many regions and can be gendered (e.g., women should not visit caribou crossings while menstruating)
Taboos for Species Consumption (as food) 
Fish species in the (25) Tocantins River (Amazon) are not eaten due to their value as medicine.  Taboos against turtle harvest in the (26) Amazon as food (due to potential for illness). 
(27) Taboos against the consumption of meat from hunting exist. For example, taboos against consuming primates.
(28) Food taboos are common and indirectly  contribute to conservation of these species (e.g., as in Madagascar)
NA
Rules and incentives related to sharing observations (e.g., reciprocal care)
(29). Food sharing networks and incentives or reciprocity are common in many Indigenous cultures and include care for others (e.g., more vulnerable populations) as well as care for the species and habitats. For example, when animals given themselves as gifts to feed the community, expressions of care and respect are shown for the animal and the ecosystem. 

4.2.2.3. Informal institutions, voluntary measures and collective action
Informal institutions are defined as those sets of rules that are used (i.e., rules in use) that effectively form the basis of decisions about access, use, and sharing of resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HDP05BUV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. Berkes 1998)","plainCitation":"(F. Berkes 1998)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32651,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E33G332J"],"itemData":{"id":32651,"type":"book","event-place":"London","publisher":"Taylor & Francis","publisher-place":"London","title":"Sacred Ecology. Traditional Ecological Knowledge and Resource Management","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. They can, along with international agreements and state-level institutions, be highly effective at ensuring sustainable use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gpm3tEc5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Griffiths, 1986; S. A. Laird, 2010; P. Shanley, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Griffiths, 1986; S. A. Laird, 2010; P. Shanley, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33482,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y3CEAXUF"],"itemData":{"id":33482,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The journal of legal pluralism and unofficial law","ISSN":"0732-9113","issue":"24","journalAbbreviation":"The journal of legal pluralism and unofficial law","note":"number: 24\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"1-55","title":"What is legal pluralism?","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Griffiths","given":"John"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1986"]]}}},{"id":34331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3K7YP5V"],"itemData":{"id":34331,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","number-of-pages":"393","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","title-short":"Wild product governance","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":33480,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WB6ILAFF"],"itemData":{"id":33480,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tropical forestry handbook","journalAbbreviation":"Tropical forestry handbook","note":"publisher: Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg","page":"1-50","title":"From lifelines to livelihoods: Non-timber forest products into the twenty-first century","author":[{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Pierce","given":"Alan R"},{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A"},{"family":"Binnqüist","given":"C López"},{"family":"Guariguata","given":"MR"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Informal institutions may develop as a softer dimension of, or response to, more formal institutions, but may also function as the sole mechanism for regulating sustainable use.
      They are critical in filling gaps in formal, state-level institutions, or where these formal institutions have failed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"x1J0XAWl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ostrom, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Ostrom, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33603,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RZHIZ3TA"],"itemData":{"id":33603,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Social capital: A multifaceted perspective","issue":"173","journalAbbreviation":"Social capital: A multifaceted perspective","note":"number: 173\\npublisher: Washington DC: World Bank","page":"195-198","title":"Social capital: a fad or a fundamental concept","volume":"172","author":[{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Voluntary organizations and efforts at collective action are also very important and can contribute significantly to addressing questions of sustainable use of a variety of species and myriad practices; some of the most effective voluntary organizations are in fisheries management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aGtolroQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Blyth, 2002; Jentoft & Bavinck, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Blyth, 2002; Jentoft & Bavinck, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33602,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HUE5PM5S"],"itemData":{"id":33602,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892902000358","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"493–508","title":"Voluntary management in an inshore fishery has conservation benefits","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Blyth","given":"R.E."},{"family":"Kaiser","given":"M.J."},{"family":"Edwards-Jones","given":"G."},{"family":"Hart","given":"P.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":34408,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8GT4F2B8"],"itemData":{"id":34408,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2014.10.005","ISSN":"18773435","journalAbbreviation":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","language":"en","page":"71-77","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Interactive governance for sustainable fisheries: dealing with legal pluralism","title-short":"Interactive governance for sustainable fisheries","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1877343514000773","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Jentoft","given":"Svein"},{"family":"Bavinck","given":"Maarten"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Collective actions (e.g., information campaign, protest) are important for social groups who are not represented in formal kinds of governance and have been found effective at addressing issues of unsustainable use. These include actions by rural and subsistence communities, indigenous peoples, women and others. Indigenous and local communities are in the frontlines of resisting “development” schemes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FTdakKkx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Díaz, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33316,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TMY48EZ5"],"itemData":{"id":33316,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The human impact on life on Earth has increased sharply since the 1970s, driven by the demands of a growing population with rising average per capita income. Nature is currently supplying more materials than ever before, but this has come at the high cost of unprecedented global declines in the extent and integrity of ecosystems, distinctness of local ecological communities, abundance and number of wild species, and the number of local domesticated varieties. Such changes reduce vital benefits that people receive from nature and threaten the quality of life of future generations. Both the benefits of an expanding economy and the costs of reducing nature’s benefits are unequally distributed. The fabric of life on which we all depend—nature and its contributions to people—is unravelling rapidly. Despite the severity of the threats and lack of enough progress in tackling them to date, opportunities exist to change future trajectories through transformative action. Such action must begin immediately, however, and address the root economic, social, and technological causes of nature’s deterioration.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aax3100","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6471","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"eaax3100","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Pervasive human-driven decline of life on Earth points to the need for transformative change","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.aax3100","volume":"366","author":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Settele","given":"Josef"},{"family":"Brondízio","given":"Eduardo S."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"Hien T."},{"family":"Agard","given":"John"},{"family":"Arneth","given":"Almut"},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Brauman","given":"Kate A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"Lucas A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"Kazuhito"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Jianguo"},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"Suneetha M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"Guy F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Zsolt"},{"family":"Obura","given":"David"},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Polasky","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Purvis","given":"Andy"},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"Jona"},{"family":"Reyers","given":"Belinda"},{"family":"Chowdhury","given":"Rinku Roy"},{"family":"Shin","given":"Yunne-Jai"},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"Ingrid"},{"family":"Willis","given":"Katherine J."},{"family":"Zayas","given":"Cynthia N."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12,13]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As an example, in the Puna area of South America, local communities such as the ones from Salinas Grandes and Laguna Guayatatoyoc engage in collective action against lithium mining in order to protect their pastoralist activities and livelihoods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vJu3ZAu5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pragier, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Pragier, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":505,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JMTQ9RMT"],"itemData":{"id":505,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Polis (online","DOI":"10.32735/s0718-6568/2019-n52-1368.","language":"es","page":"76–91","title":"Comunidades indígenas frente a la explotación de litio en sus territorios: contextos similares, respuestas distintas","URL":"en:","volume":"18, n.52","author":[{"family":"Pragier","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. When compared to more formal institutions, less is known about informal institutions and collective actions; there are gaps in understanding of “the social and political mechanisms involved in large-scale collective action problems and how cooperation in large communities is facilitated or obstructed”ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kpR4wKeH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Duit, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Duit, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33600,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5785XJKI"],"itemData":{"id":33600,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Political Studies","ISSN":"0032-3217","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Political Studies","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: SAGE Publications Sage UK: London, England","page":"900-920","title":"Patterns of environmental collective action: Some cross-national findings","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Duit","given":"Andreas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Forms of collective action aimed at addressing issues of unsustainable use often focus on questions of rights of access, benefit, and use.
      Informal institutions are rooted in the social and cultural norms of communities and societal groups, and customary law can provide effective regulation of wild product harvesting. In the case of Cameroon, it was found that customary laws addressed who owns resources, who can harvest them, where harvesting will take place, in what quantity, and who benefits and in what ways; all with greater specificity and legitimacy than weak government regulations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sXDuHMQt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. J. Ingram, 2014; S. A. Laird, 2010)","plainCitation":"(V. J. Ingram, 2014; S. A. Laird, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33597,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YKTCLBAK"],"itemData":{"id":33597,"type":"book","ISBN":"90-5448-137-4","publisher":"African Studies Centre, Leiden","title":"Win-wins in forest product value chains?: how governance impacts the sustainability of livelihoods based on non-timber forest products from Cameroon","author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina Jane"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":34331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3K7YP5V"],"itemData":{"id":34331,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","number-of-pages":"393","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","title-short":"Wild product governance","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      
Box. 4.1.4 Sooty Shearwater Management based on M?ori Knowledge and Customary Law

The Rakiura M?ori are stewards of more than 30 islands in southern Aotearoa (New Zealand).  Over many generations, they have developed customary laws and monitoring practices that inform their use of wild species including the harvesting of sooty shearwater (Puffinus griseus) which are important to their culture, economy and food security. There are an estimated 20 million sooty shearwaters that migrate between Chile and Australia, however, they are reported to be in gradual decline and a “near-threatened” species by the International Union for Conservation of Nature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"A5VgcCpk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u8220{}IUCN Red List of Threatened Species\\\\uc0\\\\u8221{} 2019)","plainCitation":"(“IUCN Red List of Threatened Species” 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32423,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WK4RY2FQ"],"itemData":{"id":32423,"type":"webpage","title":"Red List of Threatened Species","URL":"https://www.iucnredlist.org/search/stats?taxonomies=101666&searchType=species","author":[{"family":"IUCN","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Pests, habitat disturbance and loss, poaching and impediments to migration are assumed to be factors in this decline ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bSQfNvrl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Department of Conservation, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Department of Conservation, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33608,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6QZPVED3"],"itemData":{"id":33608,"type":"report","event-place":"Wellington: New Zealand","page":"39– 42","publisher":"Government of New Zealand","publisher-place":"Wellington: New Zealand","title":"Stewart Island/Rakiura Conservation Management Strategy and Rakiura National ark Management Plan 2011–2021","URL":"https://hakaimagazine.com/news/maori-management-techniques-might-help-struggling-birds/","author":[{"literal":"Department of Conservation"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A ban on harvest was implemented by the New Zealand government in the 1960s, however, after four decades it proved unsuccessful in addressing the stresses on this population ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JkJj4o7E","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Evans, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Evans, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33607,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QEHNJW3N"],"itemData":{"id":33607,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Hakai Magazine: Coastal Science and Societies","title":"M?ori Management Techniques Might Help Struggling Birds","URL":"https://hakaimagazine.com/news/maori-management-techniques-might-help-struggling-birds/","author":[{"family":"Evans","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In recent years, the limits of top-down management and the value of more participatory approaches have led to a shift in approach to sooty shearwater management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kBaIddQV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Moller, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Moller, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32542,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NZCSHQJ6"],"itemData":{"id":32542,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Using a combination of traditional ecological knowledge and science to monitor populations can greatly assist co-management for sustainable customary wildlife harvests by indigenous peoples. Case studies from Canada and New Zealand emphasize that, although traditional monitoring methods may often be imprecise and qualitative, they are nevertheless valuable because they are based on observations over long time periods, incorporate large sample sizes, are inexpensive, invite the participation of harvesters as researchers, and sometimes incorporate subtle multivariate cross checks for environmental change. A few simple rules suggested by traditional knowledge may produce good management outcomes consistent with fuzzy logic thinking. Science can sometimes offer better tests of potential causes of population change by research on larger spatial scales, precise quantification, and evaluation of population change where no harvest occurs. However, science is expensive and may not always be trusted or welcomed by customary users of wildlife. Short scientific studies in which traditional monitoring methods are calibrated against population abundance could make it possible to mesh traditional ecological knowledge with scientific inferences of prey population dynamics. This paper analyzes the traditional monitoring techniques of catch per unit effort and body condition. Combining scientific and traditional monitoring methods can not only build partnership and community consensus, but also, and more importantly, allow indigenous wildlife users to critically evaluate scientific predictions on their own terms and test sustainability using their own forms of adaptive management.","call-number":"Esub43","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-00675-090302","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art2","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Combining Science and Traditional Ecological Knowledge: Monitoring Populations for Co-Management","title-short":"Combining Science and Traditional Ecological Knowledge","URL":"http://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol9/iss3/art2/","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Moller","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Lyver","given":"Philip O'Brian"},{"family":"Kislalioglu","given":"Mina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. M?ori collaborate with the federal and regional governments to monitor and manage the species based on indigenous knowledge and scientific research.  Specifically, the recognition and support of M?ori harvest rights on Stewart Island has created an incentive and investment into habitat conservation, pest control and sustainable use practices. Informal and formal sanctions are used to enforce the customary rules, Part of the success of these rules is their flexibility and the ability to adapt to changing information about titi bird population dynamics ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"liAXREs8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kitson & Moller, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Kitson & Moller, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33605,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3RT2XW3U"],"itemData":{"id":33605,"type":"paper-conference","event-title":"Papers and Proceedings of the royal Society of tasmania","ISBN":"0080-4703","note":"issue: 1","page":"161-176","title":"Looking after your ground: resource management practice by Rakiura Maori titi harvesters","volume":"142","author":[{"family":"Kitson","given":"Jane Catherine"},{"family":"Moller","given":"Henrik"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      This information is generated through techno-science to understand global pressures (e.g., geolocating technology) as well as through indigenous knowledge studies led by M?ori harvesters ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fQtOcsqb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Geary, 2019; Moller, O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Blyver,, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Geary, 2019; Moller, O’Blyver,, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33609,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UTPQYZRR"],"itemData":{"id":33609,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"New Zealand Journal of Zoology","ISSN":"0301-4223","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"New Zealand Journal of Zoology","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"211-241","title":"Guidelines for cross?cultural participatory action research partnerships: A case study of a customary seabird harvest in New Zealand","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Moller","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"O'Blyver","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Bragg","given":"Corey"},{"family":"Newman","given":"Jamie"},{"family":"Clucas","given":"Rosemary"},{"family":"Fletcher","given":"David"},{"family":"Kitson","given":"Jane"},{"family":"McKechnie","given":"Sam"},{"family":"Scott","given":"Darren"},{"family":"Body","given":"Rakiura Titi Islands Administering"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":33604,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KZ3M7Z7W"],"itemData":{"id":33604,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"New Zealand Journal of Ecology","ISSN":"0110-6465","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"New Zealand Journal of Ecology","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: JSTOR","page":"1-7","title":"M?ori traditional harvest, knowledge and management of sooty shearwaters (Puffinus griseus) in the Marlborough Sounds, New Zealand","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Geary","given":"Amelia F"},{"family":"Nelson","given":"Nicola J"},{"family":"Paine","given":"Glenice"},{"family":"Mason","given":"Waihaere"},{"family":"Dunning","given":"Dawson L"},{"family":"Corin","given":"Steve E"},{"family":"Ramstad","given":"Kristina M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Harvesters primarily monitor the long-term population dynamics of the birds, known commonly as “titi birds” and utilize a catch per unit effort system to guide decisions on harvest levels ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tci1TdD1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kitson, 2004; Moller, O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Blyver,, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Kitson, 2004; Moller, O’Blyver,, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33606,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2RF5A7GA"],"itemData":{"id":33606,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Wildlife Research","ISSN":"1448-5494","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Wildlife Research","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: CSIRO Publishing","page":"319-325","title":"Harvest rate of sooty shearwaters (Puffinus griseus) by Rakiura M?ori: a potential tool to monitor population trends?","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Kitson","given":"Jane Catherine"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":33609,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UTPQYZRR"],"itemData":{"id":33609,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"New Zealand Journal of Zoology","ISSN":"0301-4223","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"New Zealand Journal of Zoology","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"211-241","title":"Guidelines for cross?cultural participatory action research partnerships: A case study of a customary seabird harvest in New Zealand","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Moller","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"O'Blyver","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Bragg","given":"Corey"},{"family":"Newman","given":"Jamie"},{"family":"Clucas","given":"Rosemary"},{"family":"Fletcher","given":"David"},{"family":"Kitson","given":"Jane"},{"family":"McKechnie","given":"Sam"},{"family":"Scott","given":"Darren"},{"family":"Body","given":"Rakiura Titi Islands Administering"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. “Most titi harvesters base their assessment of trends in abundance on whether they can still get their target daily total catch or ‘tally,’ whether that total is achieved in about the same time, or whether it is gathered from about the same area of breeding ground, sometimes measured indirectly as how far birders must go from their base to complete the harvest” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"USJzlB7s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Moller, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Moller, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32542,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NZCSHQJ6"],"itemData":{"id":32542,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Using a combination of traditional ecological knowledge and science to monitor populations can greatly assist co-management for sustainable customary wildlife harvests by indigenous peoples. Case studies from Canada and New Zealand emphasize that, although traditional monitoring methods may often be imprecise and qualitative, they are nevertheless valuable because they are based on observations over long time periods, incorporate large sample sizes, are inexpensive, invite the participation of harvesters as researchers, and sometimes incorporate subtle multivariate cross checks for environmental change. A few simple rules suggested by traditional knowledge may produce good management outcomes consistent with fuzzy logic thinking. Science can sometimes offer better tests of potential causes of population change by research on larger spatial scales, precise quantification, and evaluation of population change where no harvest occurs. However, science is expensive and may not always be trusted or welcomed by customary users of wildlife. Short scientific studies in which traditional monitoring methods are calibrated against population abundance could make it possible to mesh traditional ecological knowledge with scientific inferences of prey population dynamics. This paper analyzes the traditional monitoring techniques of catch per unit effort and body condition. Combining scientific and traditional monitoring methods can not only build partnership and community consensus, but also, and more importantly, allow indigenous wildlife users to critically evaluate scientific predictions on their own terms and test sustainability using their own forms of adaptive management.","call-number":"Esub43","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-00675-090302","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art2","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Combining Science and Traditional Ecological Knowledge: Monitoring Populations for Co-Management","title-short":"Combining Science and Traditional Ecological Knowledge","URL":"http://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol9/iss3/art2/","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Moller","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Lyver","given":"Philip O'Brian"},{"family":"Kislalioglu","given":"Mina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Historically records were passed on through oral traditions. In the last 100 years, harvesters have also kept written qualitative records of weather and other ecological conditions as well as numerical records of catch totals of harvests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lJawMPWu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Moller, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Moller, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32542,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NZCSHQJ6"],"itemData":{"id":32542,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Using a combination of traditional ecological knowledge and science to monitor populations can greatly assist co-management for sustainable customary wildlife harvests by indigenous peoples. Case studies from Canada and New Zealand emphasize that, although traditional monitoring methods may often be imprecise and qualitative, they are nevertheless valuable because they are based on observations over long time periods, incorporate large sample sizes, are inexpensive, invite the participation of harvesters as researchers, and sometimes incorporate subtle multivariate cross checks for environmental change. A few simple rules suggested by traditional knowledge may produce good management outcomes consistent with fuzzy logic thinking. Science can sometimes offer better tests of potential causes of population change by research on larger spatial scales, precise quantification, and evaluation of population change where no harvest occurs. However, science is expensive and may not always be trusted or welcomed by customary users of wildlife. Short scientific studies in which traditional monitoring methods are calibrated against population abundance could make it possible to mesh traditional ecological knowledge with scientific inferences of prey population dynamics. This paper analyzes the traditional monitoring techniques of catch per unit effort and body condition. Combining scientific and traditional monitoring methods can not only build partnership and community consensus, but also, and more importantly, allow indigenous wildlife users to critically evaluate scientific predictions on their own terms and test sustainability using their own forms of adaptive management.","call-number":"Esub43","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-00675-090302","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"E&S","language":"en","page":"art2","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Combining Science and Traditional Ecological Knowledge: Monitoring Populations for Co-Management","title-short":"Combining Science and Traditional Ecological Knowledge","URL":"http://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol9/iss3/art2/","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Moller","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Lyver","given":"Philip O'Brian"},{"family":"Kislalioglu","given":"Mina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  These records guide decisions on harvest location and numbers in the short term but also provide insight into medium and long-term trends. In addition to providing these data, the work also produced valued benefits to community including teaching opportunities for M?ori youth (e.g., skills for harvest management) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"12pY3cdQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lyver, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Lyver, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":807,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EGJRHJY3"],"itemData":{"id":807,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"New Zealand Journal of Ecology","ISSN":"0110-6465","journalAbbreviation":"New Zealand Journal of Ecology","note":"publisher: JSTOR","page":"7-17","title":"T?hoe Tuawhenua m?tauranga of kerer? (Hemiphaga novaseelandiae novaseelandiae) in Te Urewera","author":[{"family":"Lyver","given":"Philip O'B"},{"family":"Taputu","given":"Te Motoi"},{"family":"Kutia","given":"Spady T"},{"family":"Tahi","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This catch per unit effort guide is not unlike other catch per unit effort-type systems developed by Indigenous Peoples in other regions of the world such as those associated with traditional management of fish stocks. (e.g., Cree of northern Quebec, Canada) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"u2b45WBo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Johannes, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Johannes, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32988,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BQDAMIJZ"],"itemData":{"id":32988,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","ISSN":"1467-2960","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Fish and Fisheries","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"257-271","title":"Ignore fishers’ knowledge and miss the boat","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Johannes","given":"Robert E"},{"family":"Freeman","given":"Milton MR"},{"family":"Hamilton","given":"Richard J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This case study also points to the value of customary approaches to monitoring; although low-tech and low-cost, they provide a rapid and easily accessible source of data on which M?ori can use themselves as they hunt. It has also served to improve the sustainability of titi bird harvesting, contributing to conservation outcomes as producing social and cultural benefits to indigenous peoples. 

Box 4.15.  Politics of forest management in the Himalayas: Nepal and Myanmar

The Himalayan temperate forest zone has some of the highest percentage of endemic and threated species in the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9EkakJme","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SMYSM2K"],"itemData":{"id":11879,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12927","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1271–1282","title":"Effects of national forest-management regimes on unprotected forests of the Himalaya","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Brandt","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allendorf","given":"T."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many of the threatened species in this region depend on forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lHTWA6II","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SMYSM2K"],"itemData":{"id":11879,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12927","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1271–1282","title":"Effects of national forest-management regimes on unprotected forests of the Himalaya","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Brandt","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allendorf","given":"T."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Five countries, including Nepal and Myanmar, contain >98% of the remaining forests in this region ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yn8dScOl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SMYSM2K"],"itemData":{"id":11879,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12927","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1271–1282","title":"Effects of national forest-management regimes on unprotected forests of the Himalaya","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Brandt","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allendorf","given":"T."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While deforestation is a concern in the Himalayan temperate forest zone Nepal and Myanmar have different results when it comes to forest management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Cl4x5rIv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SMYSM2K"],"itemData":{"id":11879,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12927","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1271–1282","title":"Effects of national forest-management regimes on unprotected forests of the Himalaya","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Brandt","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allendorf","given":"T."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In general, Nepal is known for having successful forest management through their progressive community forest management that provides benefits to local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"O7m0AbWH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Anup, 2018; Baral, 2018; Brandt, 2017; R. K. Rai, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Anup, 2018; Baral, 2018; Brandt, 2017; R. K. Rai, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26038,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/THZCY72P"],"itemData":{"id":26038,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","DOI":"10.1080/10549811.2018.1490188","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"820–841","title":"Ecological and economical sustainability assessment of community forest management in Nepal: A reality check","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Baral","given":"S."},{"family":"Gautam","given":"A.P."},{"family":"Vacik","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":11879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SMYSM2K"],"itemData":{"id":11879,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12927","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1271–1282","title":"Effects of national forest-management regimes on unprotected forests of the Himalaya","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Brandt","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allendorf","given":"T."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":11646,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QL9RTI9I"],"itemData":{"id":11646,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Forestry Research","DOI":"10.1007/s11676-017-0438-z","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"429–438","title":"Increase of forest carbon biomass due to community forestry management in Nepal","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Anup","given":"K. C."},{"family":"Manandhar","given":"R."},{"family":"Paudel","given":"R."},{"family":"Ghimire","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":12545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BBXCXUAL"],"itemData":{"id":12545,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2017.08.004","language":"en","page":"156–161","title":"Is collaborative forest management in Nepal able to provide benefits to distantly located users?","volume":"83","author":[{"family":"Rai","given":"R.K."},{"family":"Dhakal","given":"A."},{"family":"Khadayat","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Ranabhat","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, studies examining Myanmar show that the country has weak forest policies riddled with corruption ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"clMql5ps","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brandt, 2017; Lim, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brandt, 2017; Lim, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SMYSM2K"],"itemData":{"id":11879,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12927","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1271–1282","title":"Effects of national forest-management regimes on unprotected forests of the Himalaya","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Brandt","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allendorf","given":"T."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":12239,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q29AI84M"],"itemData":{"id":12239,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12984","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1362–1372","title":"Untangling the proximate causes and underlying drivers of deforestation and forest degradation in Myanmar","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Lim","given":"C.L."},{"family":"Prescott","given":"G.W."},{"family":"Alban","given":"J.D.T.D."},{"family":"Ziegler","given":"A.D."},{"family":"Webb","given":"E.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Rates of deforestation in unprotected areas from 2000 to 2014 were very low in Nepal at 0.6% whereas Myanmar had the highest deforestation rates of the region at 1.7% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gXb7NMIx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SMYSM2K"],"itemData":{"id":11879,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12927","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1271–1282","title":"Effects of national forest-management regimes on unprotected forests of the Himalaya","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Brandt","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allendorf","given":"T."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Additionally, Myanmar had the third largest deforestation by area in the world from 2010 to 2015 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ja3bzWAv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Reddy, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Reddy, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12537,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q7BXDRM5"],"itemData":{"id":12537,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-019-01714-xAntoniazzi","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"1129–1149","title":"Quantifying and predicting multi-decadal forest cover changes in Myanmar: A biodiversity hotspot under threat","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10531-019-01714-xAntoniazzi,","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Reddy","given":"C.S."},{"family":"Pasha","given":"S.V."},{"family":"Satish","given":"K.V."},{"family":"Unnikrishnan","given":"A."},{"family":"Chavan","given":"S.B."},{"family":"Jha","given":"C.S."},{"family":"Diwakar","given":"P.G."},{"family":"Dadhwal","given":"V.K."},{"family":"L.","given":"Campos-Filho"},{"family":"E.M.","given":"Vieira"},{"literal":"D.L.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the Himalayan temperate forest zone, Nepal has the best record of successful community management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OQGhhPNV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SMYSM2K"],"itemData":{"id":11879,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12927","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1271–1282","title":"Effects of national forest-management regimes on unprotected forests of the Himalaya","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Brandt","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allendorf","given":"T."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Nepal, 68% of forests are managed by the government and the remaining 32% are under community management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8OyYK1Ob","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SMYSM2K"],"itemData":{"id":11879,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12927","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1271–1282","title":"Effects of national forest-management regimes on unprotected forests of the Himalaya","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Brandt","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allendorf","given":"T."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Community forest management in Nepal has helped to enhance forest cover, conserve biodiversity and support local livelihoods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xYNuzFvx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Anup, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Anup, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11646,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QL9RTI9I"],"itemData":{"id":11646,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Forestry Research","DOI":"10.1007/s11676-017-0438-z","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"429–438","title":"Increase of forest carbon biomass due to community forestry management in Nepal","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Anup","given":"K. C."},{"family":"Manandhar","given":"R."},{"family":"Paudel","given":"R."},{"family":"Ghimire","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. After the civil war in Nepal, the government used conservation, especially forest conservation, as a tool of state building ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"u80XJHDe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dongol & Neumann, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Dongol & Neumann, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12098,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K8R6EH9T"],"itemData":{"id":12098,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Political Geography","DOI":"10.1016/j.polgeo.2020.102327","language":"en","page":"102327","title":"State making through conservation: The case of post-conflict Nepal","volume":"85","author":[{"family":"Dongol","given":"Y."},{"family":"Neumann","given":"R.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A key to Nepal’s success with community forest management is the active involvement of villagers in the protection of their local forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uaUrcGYE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Anup, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Anup, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11646,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QL9RTI9I"],"itemData":{"id":11646,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Forestry Research","DOI":"10.1007/s11676-017-0438-z","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"429–438","title":"Increase of forest carbon biomass due to community forestry management in Nepal","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Anup","given":"K. C."},{"family":"Manandhar","given":"R."},{"family":"Paudel","given":"R."},{"family":"Ghimire","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Community forest management was used as a tool to reduce conflict between the government and rural communities by giving those communities authority over the local forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vIIB3sjz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dongol & Neumann, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Dongol & Neumann, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12098,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K8R6EH9T"],"itemData":{"id":12098,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Political Geography","DOI":"10.1016/j.polgeo.2020.102327","language":"en","page":"102327","title":"State making through conservation: The case of post-conflict Nepal","volume":"85","author":[{"family":"Dongol","given":"Y."},{"family":"Neumann","given":"R.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Not only are the citizens directly proximate to the forests included but distant users are also involved in forest management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a8tfvyz8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. K. Rai, 2017)","plainCitation":"(R. K. Rai, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BBXCXUAL"],"itemData":{"id":12545,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2017.08.004","language":"en","page":"156–161","title":"Is collaborative forest management in Nepal able to provide benefits to distantly located users?","volume":"83","author":[{"family":"Rai","given":"R.K."},{"family":"Dhakal","given":"A."},{"family":"Khadayat","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Ranabhat","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Community forest management in Nepal provides forest products to distantly located citizens who are unable to physically participate in forest management activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3aUIEYfj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. K. Rai, 2017)","plainCitation":"(R. K. Rai, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BBXCXUAL"],"itemData":{"id":12545,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2017.08.004","language":"en","page":"156–161","title":"Is collaborative forest management in Nepal able to provide benefits to distantly located users?","volume":"83","author":[{"family":"Rai","given":"R.K."},{"family":"Dhakal","given":"A."},{"family":"Khadayat","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Ranabhat","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Providing forest products to these citizens has contributed to positive support for forest management in proximal and distant communities alike ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CaoDNvw9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. K. Rai, 2017)","plainCitation":"(R. K. Rai, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BBXCXUAL"],"itemData":{"id":12545,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2017.08.004","language":"en","page":"156–161","title":"Is collaborative forest management in Nepal able to provide benefits to distantly located users?","volume":"83","author":[{"family":"Rai","given":"R.K."},{"family":"Dhakal","given":"A."},{"family":"Khadayat","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Ranabhat","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Overall, Nepal’s community forest management approach to reduce deforestation is a sustainable approach. On the other hand, Myanmar has fallen short with its forest management practices. A huge contributor to the current patterns of deforestation in Myanmar is the long history of military rule and conflict ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gDxccTSe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SMYSM2K"],"itemData":{"id":11879,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12927","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1271–1282","title":"Effects of national forest-management regimes on unprotected forests of the Himalaya","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Brandt","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allendorf","given":"T."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Myanmar’s forests are controlled by the national government through a centralized forest ownership approach ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RX8JP25b","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SMYSM2K"],"itemData":{"id":11879,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12927","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1271–1282","title":"Effects of national forest-management regimes on unprotected forests of the Himalaya","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Brandt","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allendorf","given":"T."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Timber extraction and agricultural advancement are often given priority over forest protection and sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2J0lxir4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brandt, 2017; Lim, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brandt, 2017; Lim, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SMYSM2K"],"itemData":{"id":11879,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12927","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1271–1282","title":"Effects of national forest-management regimes on unprotected forests of the Himalaya","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Brandt","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allendorf","given":"T."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":12239,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q29AI84M"],"itemData":{"id":12239,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12984","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1362–1372","title":"Untangling the proximate causes and underlying drivers of deforestation and forest degradation in Myanmar","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Lim","given":"C.L."},{"family":"Prescott","given":"G.W."},{"family":"Alban","given":"J.D.T.D."},{"family":"Ziegler","given":"A.D."},{"family":"Webb","given":"E.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As a result, government forests are viewed as the equivalent of an open-access area ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OamUkvbb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SMYSM2K"],"itemData":{"id":11879,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12927","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1271–1282","title":"Effects of national forest-management regimes on unprotected forests of the Himalaya","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Brandt","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allendorf","given":"T."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Government forests in Myanmar have insufficient monitoring and enforcement mechanisms which leads to exploitation of forest products ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Jn4f5YhE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Brandt, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SMYSM2K"],"itemData":{"id":11879,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12927","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1271–1282","title":"Effects of national forest-management regimes on unprotected forests of the Himalaya","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Brandt","given":"J.S."},{"family":"Allendorf","given":"T."},{"family":"Radeloff","given":"V."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In conclusion, the community forest management system in Nepal has proven a successful method of forest conservation. Community forest management is a practice of forest management that is sustainable and increases biodiversity. This practice in Nepal has had tremendous results in the Himalayan temperate forest zone that is currently susceptible to deforestation. While Nepal has found success, Myanmar has struggled to maintain integrity in their conservation attempts. The political instability in Myanmar has provided a barrier to achieving effective forest management. The approach that Nepal used of building governance structures around conservation policies is an interesting approach and has been an important development for the region’s conservation of forests. 

      There are similar examples from almost every corner of the globe and with respect to almost every practice and use of wild species. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"i1T8hsd7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ndoye and Awono 2010)","plainCitation":"(Ndoye and Awono 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":516,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FFAUPYMJ"],"itemData":{"id":516,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Wid product Governance. Finding policies that work for non-timber forest products.","ISBN":"978-1-84407-560-3","page":"71-76","title":"Case study B: policies for Gnetum spp. trade in Cameroon: overcoming constraints that reduce benefits and discourage sustainability.","URL":"https://books.google.fr/books?id=n8OUlIlKTq0C&pg=PR4&lpg=PR4&dq=978-1-84407-560-3&source=bl&ots=OHveSTqmZf&sig=ACfU3U3pcS0KW2MrmpqjXIaFgWhwmCconQ&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjA8tK9w832AhVGExoKHd1rDRUQ6AF6BAgCEAM#v=onepage&q=978-1-84407-560-3&f=false","author":[{"family":"Ndoye","given":"O"},{"family":"Awono","given":"A"}],"editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"S. A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pOOXmdPh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ingram, Ndumbe, and Ewane 2012)","plainCitation":"(Ingram, Ndumbe, and Ewane 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":756,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DZHV3FFW"],"itemData":{"id":756,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The leaves of these Gnetum spp. lianas have long been harvested from humid forests for consumption and traded as a popular vegetable across the Congo Basin. Recent data on the current environmental, social and economic aspects of this forest product are however sparse. Value chain analysis was used to gather information on stakeholders involved in the chain from forest to consumer (small scale harvesters, traders, transporters, exporters and consumers), and on the socioeconomic values, volumes, sustainability and governance in major production areas and markets in Cameroon and Nigeria. At least 2,550 people work across the chain, which has seven main routes from forest to consumers. Gnetum contributes on average to 62% of a harvester’s annual income (1,125 US$). Dependence upon Gnetum-based incomes increases for those further from the forest, providing an average of 75% of retailer’s (1,268 US$) and 58% of exporter’s annual incomes (7,000 US$). The better organised Nigerian wholesalers earn almost double their Cameroonian counterparts. Simple processing neither adds much value nor greatly reduces perishability. Whilst a rudimentary regulatory framework exists, trade is mostly illegal. Sporadic customary governance does not fill the void left by unenforced and inappropriate regulation. Over 50% is unsustainably collected from the forest and harvesting over the last 5 years increased as demand rose, particularly from Nigeria. Decreasing availability, increasing prices and low levels of cultivation have led to an unsustainable trade. Policy measures such as linking chain stakeholders, promoting cultivation, pragmatic regulation and enforcing customary control may enhance long-term survival of these IUCN Red List near-threatened species and ensure the continuity of their contribution to livelihood and economic development to continue.","container-title":"Small-scale Forestry","DOI":"10.1007/s11842-012-9200-8","ISSN":"1873-7854","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Small-scale Forestry","language":"en","page":"539-556","source":"Springer Link","title":"Small Scale, High Value: Gnetumafricanum and buchholzianum Value Chains in Cameroon","title-short":"Small Scale, High Value","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11842-012-9200-8","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Ndumbe","given":"Louis Njie"},{"family":"Ewane","given":"Marcus Elah"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} describe for Gnetum africanum how allocation of permits and quotas (which are not based on any inventory of the plants) and common practices of bribes and corruption at official checkpoints, raises costs, increases losses, and leads to increased resource exploitation. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rrq5wOHE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sunderland 2010; Ingram 2017)","plainCitation":"(Sunderland 2010; Ingram 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34194,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WML6NTBH"],"itemData":{"id":34194,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Wid product Governance. Finding policies that work for non-timber forest products.","ISBN":"978-1-84407-560-3","page":"77-84","title":"Case study C: regulatory issues for Bush Mango (Irvingia spp.) trade in southwest Cameroon and southeast Nigeria.","URL":"https://books.google.fr/books?id=n8OUlIlKTq0C&pg=PR4&lpg=PR4&dq=978-1-84407-560-3&source=bl&ots=OHveSTqmZf&sig=ACfU3U3pcS0KW2MrmpqjXIaFgWhwmCconQ&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjA8tK9w832AhVGExoKHd1rDRUQ6AF6BAgCEAM#v=onepage&q=978-1-84407-560-3&f=false","author":[{"family":"Sunderland","given":"T"},{"family":"Asaha","given":"S."},{"family":"Balinga","given":"M."},{"family":"Isoni","given":"O."}],"editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"S. A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":861,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4PP7KFH8"],"itemData":{"id":861,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Across the Congo Basin, bush mango (Irvingia spp.) nuts have been harvested from forest landscapes for consumption, sold as a foodstuff and for medicine for centuries. Data on this trade however are sparse. A value chain approach was used to gather information on stakeholders in the chain from the harvesters in three major production areas in Cameroon to traders in Cameroon, Nigeria, and Equatorial Guinea, the socio-economic values, environmental sustainability and governance. Around 5190 people work in the complex chain in Cameroon with an estimated 4109tons harvested on average annually in the period 2007 to 2010. Bush mango incomes contribute on average to 31% of harvester's annual incomes and dependence increases for those further from the forest. Customary rules govern access to resources. Although regulations exist, most trade is illegal, with corruption and collective action governing access to markets. The majority of nuts harvested are sustainably collected. Although 51% of the harvest is sourced from the forest, trees are also managed on cultivated land. Forest degradation and deforestation threaten the species. Policy measures such as linking stakeholders, promoting cultivation, pragmatic regulation, and supporting processer groups may make trade in this forest food more sustainable.","collection-title":"Forest, Food, and Livelihoods","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2016.12.014","ISSN":"1389-9341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"29-37","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Challenges to governing sustainable forest food: Irvingia spp. from southern Cameroon","title-short":"Challenges to governing sustainable forest food","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1389934116304798","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Ewane","given":"Marcus"},{"family":"Ndumbe","given":"Louis Njie"},{"family":"Awono","given":"Abdon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} report on the trade of bush mango (Irvingia spp.) and njansang (Ricinodendron heudelotii) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QaJflRC8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ndumbe, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Ndumbe, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":506,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EGFZL2EE"],"itemData":{"id":506,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods:1-16","DOI":"10.1080/14728028.2018.1559107","language":"en","title":"From trees to money: the contribution of njansang (Ricinodendron heudelotii) products to value chain stakeholders’ financial assets in the South West Region of Cameroon","author":[{"family":"Ndumbe","given":"Louis Njie"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Tchamba","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Nya","given":"Smith"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} in Central Africa, which is widely traded nationally and regionally and likewise conclude that government institutions are all but absent, with corruption an issue for those that do function. The Sami management of reindeer in Finland and Norway is the result of the interaction between local and national institutions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KjUyPk04","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Marin & Bj\\\\uc0\\\\u248{}rklund, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Marin & Bjørklund, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33599,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FZAG3VQ6"],"itemData":{"id":33599,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","ISSN":"1875-0281","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of the Commons","note":"number: 1","title":"A tragedy of errors? Institutional dynamics and land tenure in Finnmark, Norway","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Marin","given":"Andrei"},{"family":"Bjørklund","given":"Ivar"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Finland, herders work with a range of new formal bureaucratic institutions that have come on top of customary ones. There is a mismatch between the formal and informal institutions and their underlying logics and styles of thinking have resulted in a complex institutional dynamic affecting land and resource tenure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DwQWxCnY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Marin & Bj\\\\uc0\\\\u248{}rklund, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Marin & Bjørklund, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33599,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FZAG3VQ6"],"itemData":{"id":33599,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","ISSN":"1875-0281","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of the Commons","note":"number: 1","title":"A tragedy of errors? Institutional dynamics and land tenure in Finnmark, Norway","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Marin","given":"Andrei"},{"family":"Bjørklund","given":"Ivar"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kqnXuZ1f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Areki & Cunningham, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Areki & Cunningham, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33596,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3FVWTISB"],"itemData":{"id":33596,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Wild Product Governance","ISBN":"1-84977-519-2","page":"257-270","publisher":"Routledge","title":"Fiji: commerce, carving and customary tenure","author":[{"family":"Areki","given":"Francis"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony B"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} report on the sustainability crisis around the wood carving species Intsia bijuga, which requires a complementary approach of strengthening weakened customary law and stronger and better-coordinated national regulation.  
      In some countries, lack of recognition by government of customary systems (including their criminalization) have undermined sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YaEeZTwp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sarah A. Laird, McLain, and Wynberg 2010; Arnold and P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}rez 2001; Wynberg and Laird 2007; Michon 2005; Lele, Pattanaik, and Rai 2010)","plainCitation":"(Sarah A. Laird, McLain, and Wynberg 2010; Arnold and Pérez 2001; Wynberg and Laird 2007; Michon 2005; Lele, Pattanaik, and Rai 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3K7YP5V"],"itemData":{"id":34331,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","number-of-pages":"393","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","title-short":"Wild product governance","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":518,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2I3T33U7"],"itemData":{"id":518,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological economics","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"437–447","title":"Can non-timber forest products match tropical forest conservation and development objectives?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0921-8009(01)00236-1","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Arnold","given":"JE Michael"},{"family":"Pérez","given":"M Ruiz"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":33457,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KVL8UHGG"],"itemData":{"id":33457,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Non-timber forest products (NTFPs) are often seen as tools to promote rural development and biodiversity conservation but little attention has been given to the different policy approaches adopted for their governance, nor to the role played by customary law. Through the lens of one of the most revered and economically important trees in Africa, marula (Sclerocarya birrea), NTFP governance is explored in case studies across South Africa and Namibia. Results are presented from a study that examined the interface between statutory and customary rules and regulations governing marula conservation and use. The major finding is that 'less' is often 'more' when it comes to government regulation of marula, a result that resonates with other NTFP studies from around the world that indicate the need for state-led interventions to be purposely crafted to reflect local circumstances and needs. Such interventions are also most effective when government and traditional authorities cooperate, authorities have legitimacy and sufficient capacity, and there is acceptance of the rules by user groups. It is suggested that state intervention may be least useful where traditional governance is strong, and with the exception of areas and cases in which customary law and traditional authority fall short and commercial pressures on species are significant, governments might do best by leaving well enough alone. Les produits forestiers autres que le bois (NTFPs) sont souvent perc, us comme des outils pour promouvoir le développement rural et la conservation de la biodiversité, mais peu d'attention a ete accordée aux differentes approches de politique adoptées pour leur gestion, et au role joué par la loi coutumiére. En se concentrant sur l'un de arbres les plus révérés et économiquement importants en Afrique, le marula (Sclerocarya birrea), la gestion des NTFPs est explorée en étude-cas du sud de l'Afrique à la Namibie. Les résultats d'une étude qui examine les connections entre les les régies et régulations statutaires et coutumiéres qui gerent la conservation et l' utilisation du marula. La découverte la plus importante est que \\"moins\\" devient souvent \\"plus\\" quand il s'agit de la régulation gouvernementale du marula. Ce résultat résonne avec d'autres études sur les NTFPs autour du monde, qui indiquent la nécessité de former les interventions conduites par l'état en s'efforçant de refléter les besoins et circonstances locaux. La suggestion est que l' intervention de l'etat est peut-être la moins utile dans les endroits ou la gestion traditionnelle est forte, et, les zones et cas où la loi et l'autorite traditionnelle échouent et où la pression commerciale sur les especes est importante mises à part; que les gouvernements feraient peut-être mieux de s'abstenir d'intervenir dans le secteur. Los productos forestales no maderables (PFNMs) son considerados a menudo como instrumentos para la promoción del desarollo rural y de la conservación de la biodiversidad, pero se ha prestado poca atencion a las diferentes politicas adoptadas para su manejo, ni al papel desempeñado por la ley consuetudinaria. Se estudia el caso de uno los árboles mas venerados y económicamente importantes de África, la marula (Sclerocarya birrea), basándose en estudios procedentes de Sudáfrica y Namibia para analizar el manejo de PFNMs. Se presentan los resultados de un estudio que examinó la interrelatión entre normas y reglamentos legales y consuetudinarios relacionados con la conservatión y uso de la marula. El resultado más interesante del estudio fue el descubrimiento de que a menudo \\"menos\\" resulta ser \\"más\\" cuando se trata de la regulatión gubernamental de la marula, hecho que concuerda con otros estudios sobre PFNMs de otras partes del mundo, que demuestran la importancia de adaptar especificamente las intervenciones estatales para reflejar las circunstancias y necesidades locales. Se sugiere que la interventión estatal puede resultar poco útil donde el manejo tradicional sigue en vigor, y que convendría a los gobiernos evitar inmiscuirse, salvo en casos en que la ley consuetudinaria y las autoridades tradicionales se queden cortas y exista una fuerte presión comercial sobre las especies.","container-title":"The International Forestry Review","ISSN":"14655489, 20537778","issue":"1","page":"475–490","title":"Less is often more: governance of a non-timber forest product, marula (Sclerocarya birrea subsp. caffra) in southern Africa","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/43740293","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Wynberg","given":"R."},{"family":"Laird","given":"S.A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":34487,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZF7TFY5G"],"itemData":{"id":34487,"type":"book","publisher":"CIFOR","title":"Domesticating forests: how farmers manage forest resources","author":[{"family":"Michon","given":"Genevieve"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":34183,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BEJ75T5Y"],"itemData":{"id":34183,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Wid product Governance. Finding policies that work for non-timber forest products.","ISBN":"978-1-84407-560-3","page":"85-113","title":"NTFPs in India: Rhetoric and Reality In Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products.","URL":"https://books.google.fr/books?id=n8OUlIlKTq0C&pg=PR4&lpg=PR4&dq=978-1-84407-560-3&source=bl&ots=OHveSTqmZf&sig=ACfU3U3pcS0KW2MrmpqjXIaFgWhwmCconQ&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjA8tK9w832AhVGExoKHd1rDRUQ6AF6BAgCEAM#v=onepage&q=978-1-84407-560-3&f=false","author":[{"family":"Lele","given":"Sharachchandra"},{"family":"Pattanaik","given":"Manoj"},{"family":"Rai","given":"Nitin D."}],"editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"S. A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, in cases where species are under strong commercial pressure, for example Prunus africana across much of Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0ndqRTOA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wiersum, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Wiersum, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8RENKLT3"],"itemData":{"id":833,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","issue":"1-2","language":"en","page":"6–18","title":"Governing access to resources and markets in non-timber forest product chains","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Wiersum","given":"K.F."},{"family":"Ingram","given":"V.J."},{"family":"Ros-Tonen","given":"M.A.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and customary systems of governance have broken down, statutory law is an important and often necessary complement to replace or reinforce customary law ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0cpqoSPw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sarah A. Laird, McLain, and Wynberg 2010)","plainCitation":"(Sarah A. Laird, McLain, and Wynberg 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3K7YP5V"],"itemData":{"id":34331,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","number-of-pages":"393","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","title-short":"Wild product governance","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      
4.2.2.4. Customary laws and common pool resource institutions 
Systems of natural resource management, including those related to wild species use, have changed significantly over the last thirty years in many jurisdictions from centralized, top-down conservation approaches to community-based approaches, which account for social and ecological dimensions of use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c6c6rJsU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Agrawal & Gibson, 2001; Borrini-Feyerabend, 1996; Meinzen-Dick, 2002; Russel & Harshbarger, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Agrawal & Gibson, 2001; Borrini-Feyerabend, 1996; Meinzen-Dick, 2002; Russel & Harshbarger, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33060,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RHBDK75J"],"itemData":{"id":33060,"type":"book","event-place":"New Brunswick, N.J","language":"en","publisher":"Rutgers University Press","publisher-place":"New Brunswick, N.J","title":"Communities and the environment : ethnicity, gender, and the state in community-based conservation","editor":[{"family":"Agrawal","given":"A."},{"family":"Gibson","given":"M.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":33598,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/34CM8PFF"],"itemData":{"id":33598,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Gland (Switzerland) http://www. iucn. org/themes/spg/Files/tailor. html","journalAbbreviation":"Gland (Switzerland) http://www. iucn. org/themes/spg/Files/tailor. html","title":"Collaborative management of protected areas: Tailoring the approach to the context IUCN","author":[{"family":"Borrini-Feyerabend","given":"Grazia"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}},{"id":33595,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5FH9BAA8"],"itemData":{"id":33595,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World development","ISSN":"0305-750X","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"World development","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"649-666","title":"What affects organization and collective action for managing resources? Evidence from canal irrigation systems in India","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Meinzen-Dick","given":"Ruth"},{"family":"Raju","given":"K Vengamma"},{"family":"Gulati","given":"Ashok"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":33594,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/37GTKN2L"],"itemData":{"id":33594,"type":"article-journal","note":"publisher: New York, NY: Alta Mira Press","title":"Groundwork for community-based conservation","author":[{"family":"Russel","given":"D"},{"family":"Harshbarger","given":"C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Part of the success of community-based institutions is attributed to the flexibility and adaptability of rule systems to ups and downs in resource availability and condition. Rules that integrate the well-established cultures and way of life of local peoples are most often successful and may be considered ‘customary’. “Customary rights” refer to community rules and regulations inherited from ancestors that are accepted, interpreted, and enforced by the community, which may or may not be recognized by the State ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"P9BVDCDW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Awono, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Awono, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32555,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XSLVGWN3"],"itemData":{"id":32555,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Forestry Review","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"78–92","title":"Will the proposed forest policy and regulatory reforms boost the NTFP sector in Cameroon?","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Awono","given":"A."},{"family":"Tchindjang","given":"M."},{"family":"Levang","given":"Patrice"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Whether recognized by statutory laws or not, rural communities often consider themselves to be the traditional owners of resources within their respective domains ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ptj3LMcu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wily and Liz 2004; Sarah A. Laird, McLain, and Wynberg 2010)","plainCitation":"(Wily and Liz 2004; Sarah A. Laird, McLain, and Wynberg 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33452,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X3F2AJL9"],"itemData":{"id":33452,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","issue":"2-4","language":"en","page":"217–228","title":"Can we own the forest? Looking at the changing tenure environment for community forestry in Africa","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Wily","given":"Alden"},{"literal":"Liz"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":34331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3K7YP5V"],"itemData":{"id":34331,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","number-of-pages":"393","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","title-short":"Wild product governance","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      A subset of these rules related to “commons” are relevant to this assessment, given that many wild species are defined as common-pool resources. Such institutions are well developed in many countries around the world and in relation to numerous species. These institutions delineate many aspects of management. Rights to use, harvest and associated trade may be governed through systems of short and long-term leases, loans, gifts, payment (including in-kind) and inheritance, which can differ depending on which societal and ethnic groups dominate access to resources and access to markets. These customary norms typically differ within a country and determine who owns resources and may access them; where and in which quantities harvesting may take place; and who benefits and how. 

Box 4.16.  Medicinal and aromatic plants in Asia

Medicinal and aromatic plants have played significant socio-cultural and economic roles in many rural households in the Himalayan region of Asia, for thousands of years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9xNwIsN7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Joshi & Rao, 2011; Karki, 2005; K. Kumar, 2015; Kunwar, 2015; Lal & Samant, 2019; C. S. Olsen & Larsen, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Joshi & Rao, 2011; Karki, 2005; K. Kumar, 2015; Kunwar, 2015; Lal & Samant, 2019; C. S. Olsen & Larsen, 2003)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":507,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YL8XZ7WH"],"itemData":{"id":507,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Earth Sciences","DOI":"10.1007/978-3-540-95991-5-10","language":"fr","note":"publisher: Scopus®","page":"91–101","title":"Role of indigenous people in conservation of biodiversity of medicinal plants: An Indian case study","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-95991-5-10","author":[{"family":"Joshi","given":"P."},{"family":"Rao","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":510,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9DY36MK5"],"itemData":{"id":510,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Acta Horticulturae","DOI":"10.17660/ActaHortic.2005.678.4","language":"en","title":"Creating Livelihoods Enhancing Medicinal and Aromatic Plants based Biodiversity-Rich Production Systems: Preliminary Lessons from South Asia","URL":"https://doi.org/10.17660/ActaHortic.2005.678.4","author":[{"family":"Karki","given":"M."},{"family":"Tiwari","given":"B."},{"family":"Badoni","given":"A."},{"family":"Bhattarai","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":33591,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DQJEFNDW"],"itemData":{"id":33591,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"There is a consensus in the literature and widespread policymaker support on the desirability of democratic decentralisation of natural resources governance. However, few decentralisation initiatives in developing countries have led to more democratic governance of natural resources. India's Recognition of Forest Rights Act (RFRA), 2006, was enacted as a result of democratic processes driven by demand for recognition of forest rights by forest dwellers. RFRA represents a political, demand-based effort to reform forest governance through a provision of rights to forest-dependent people. India also continues to operate the joint forest management (JFM) programme, a more traditional state-initiated decentralisation effort. The two parallel forest reform programmes being carried out in the same landscapes provide a unique opportunity to study democratisation of forest governance in the country with the world's largest number of forest-dependent people. We examine JFM and RFRA on the criteria of delegation of power and authority, downward accountability and impact on forest-dependent poor to understand the substantively different spaces that they open for democratic forest governance. We find that the implementation of the RFRA has been strongly opposed by powerful interests, and its more radical provisions related to community rights over forests have largely remained unimplemented. However, our findings, drawing from both primary and secondary sources, also bring out the potential of the RFRA to hold the forest bureaucracy accountable to forest-dweller communities and its ability to shift tangible legal powers and authority to forest dwellers.","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2014.09.018","ISSN":"1389-9341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","page":"1-8","title":"Decentralisation and democratic forest reforms in India: Moving to a rights-based approach","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1389934114001786","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Kumar","given":"Kundan"},{"family":"Singh","given":"Neera M."},{"family":"Kerr","given":"John M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]],"season":"Fevereiro"}}},{"id":512,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D3XGUUBR"],"itemData":{"id":512,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2015.01.035","language":"en","page":"210–219","title":"Medicinal plant Dynamics in Indigenous Medicines in Farwest Nepal","volume":"163","author":[{"family":"Kunwar","given":"R.M."},{"family":"Acharya","given":"R.P."},{"family":"Chowdhary","given":"C.L."},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"R.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":33053,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FXXEF2TM"],"itemData":{"id":33053,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Commercial alpine medicinal plants are collected from the wild by local$\\\\backslash$nrural households throughout the Himalaya and sold in order to increase$\\\\backslash$nhousehold incomes. Recent studies indicate that this annual trade$\\\\backslash$namounts to thousands of tonnes of roots, rhizomes, tubers, leaves, etc.,$\\\\backslash$nworth millions of US dollars. The main market is in India. Based on a$\\\\backslash$nnational survey, including the most commonly traded species, and a$\\\\backslash$nvillage study this paper investigates the importance of the alpine$\\\\backslash$nmedicinal plant trade at national and local levels in Nepal. The$\\\\backslash$nnational survey included standardized open-ended interviews with 232$\\\\backslash$nharvesters, 64 local traders, 66 central wholesalers, 47 regional$\\\\backslash$nwholesalers, and 16 production companies. The village survey is based on$\\\\backslash$nthe daily records of household activities in 15 households in a one-year$\\\\backslash$nperiod. The annual Nepalese alpine and sub-alpine medicinal plant trade$\\\\backslash$nis conservatively estimated to vary from 480 to 2500 t with a total$\\\\backslash$nharvester value of US$0.8-3.3 million; the average harvester value is\\\\backslashnestimated at US$66.0 +/- 99.0. The trade in 1997/98 amounted to 1600 t$\\\\backslash$nwith a harvester value of US$2.3 million and an export value equivalent\\\\backslashnto 2.50% of total export from Nepal. Medicinal plant harvesting was\\\\backslashnfound to constitute an integrated part of local livelihood strategies,\\\\backslashncontributing from 3 to 44% (average of 12%) of the annual household\\\\backslashnincome. Importance at household level depended on land and animal\\\\backslashnholdings, and the availability of adult male labour. The validity and\\\\backslashnreliability of methods and analyses is evaluated, and issues of\\\\backslashnconservation and management of alpine medicinal plant species under the\\\\backslashncommunity forestry scheme are discussed.","container-title":"Geographical Journal","DOI":"10.1111/1475-4959.00088","ISSN":"00167398","issue":"3","page":"243–254","title":"Alpine medicinal plant trade and Himalayan mountain livelihood strategies","volume":"169","author":[{"family":"Olsen","given":"Carsten Smith"},{"family":"Larsen","given":"Helle Overgaard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":513,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BRAL36GN"],"itemData":{"id":513,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Nutraceutical Plants from the Himalayas. New India Publishing Agency","language":"en","page":"177–203","title":"Ephedra L","author":[{"family":"Lal","given":"M."},{"family":"Samant","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. One such meaningful plant is the deciduous shrub Ephedra, which is traditionally used for primary health care in rural households ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nuFvCxvv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Joshi & Rao, 2011; N. Khan, 2011; Sher, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Joshi & Rao, 2011; N. Khan, 2011; Sher, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":507,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YL8XZ7WH"],"itemData":{"id":507,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Earth Sciences","DOI":"10.1007/978-3-540-95991-5-10","language":"fr","note":"publisher: Scopus®","page":"91–101","title":"Role of indigenous people in conservation of biodiversity of medicinal plants: An Indian case study","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-95991-5-10","author":[{"family":"Joshi","given":"P."},{"family":"Rao","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":511,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HHLXSDQS"],"itemData":{"id":511,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pakistan Journal of Botany","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"797–809","title":"Important medicinal plants of Chitral Gol National park (CGNP) Pakistan","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Khan","given":"N."},{"family":"Ahmed","given":"M."},{"family":"Ahmed","given":"A."},{"family":"Shaukat","given":"S.S."},{"family":"Wahab","given":"M."},{"family":"Ajaib","given":"M."},{"family":"Siddiqui","given":"M."},{"family":"Nasir","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33611,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q687XDBB"],"itemData":{"id":33611,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","language":"en","note":"publisher: ScienceDirect","page":"57–69","title":"Traditional use of medicinal plants among Kalasha, Ismaeli and Sunni groups in Chitral District, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa province, Pakistan","volume":"188","author":[{"family":"Sher","given":"H."},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"R.W."},{"family":"Hart","given":"R."},{"family":"Boer","given":"H.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and has sacred or religious connections ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ngGeFenP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. S. Negi, 2018; Sher, 2016)","plainCitation":"(V. S. Negi, 2018; Sher, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33592,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EXLMFYNK"],"itemData":{"id":33592,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Indian Himalayan Region (IHR) is considered as a rich repository of the Medicinal and Aromatic Plants (MAPs), and has diverse traditional and modern base of therapeutic knowledge. Inhabitants of the region are traditionally well familiar with the healing properties of the available plants and therefore, dependency on medicinal plants for primary healthcare forms integral part of their life. Besides, modern pharmaceutical industries are also relying upon medicinal plants for developing new drugs or searching novel biomolecules. However, over-harvesting and habitat loss of MAPs has reduced its population in the wild, and threatened the sustainability of a number of species in IHR. This calls for an urgent attention and requires selection of suitable criteria and indicators towards encouraging long-term economic and environmental sustainability of the MAP sector. In this context, this study focuses on identifying four major sustainability parameters; i.e. economic, environmental/ecological, socio-cultural and governance, with 11 criteria and 48 indicators. The criteria and indicators approach is expected to be useful in assessing the impacts and monitoring the developmental initiatives in the MAP sector. Further, the study also prioritized potential MAP species for linking cultivation and conservation with livelihood opportunity in Western Himalaya. A total of 152 potential MAP species were identified based on medicinal value, market demand, availability and uses in traditional herbal system. Of these, 43 potential MAPs have been prioritized for cultivation and conservation in Western Himalaya. In addition, distribution and diversity of threatened MAPs of IHR has been documented and presented in a manner to develop strategies for sustainability of the sector at state and national levels.","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2018.03.032","ISSN":"1470-160X","language":"en","page":"434–446","title":"Criteria and indicators for promoting cultivation and conservation of Medicinal and Aromatic Plants in Western Himalaya, India","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1470160X18301882","volume":"93","author":[{"family":"Negi","given":"Vikram S."},{"family":"Kewlani","given":"Pushpa"},{"family":"Pathak","given":"Ravi"},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"Deepika"},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"Indra D."},{"family":"Rawal","given":"Ranbeer S."},{"family":"Sundriyal","given":"R. C."},{"family":"Nandi","given":"S. K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10]]}}},{"id":33611,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q687XDBB"],"itemData":{"id":33611,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","language":"en","note":"publisher: ScienceDirect","page":"57–69","title":"Traditional use of medicinal plants among Kalasha, Ismaeli and Sunni groups in Chitral District, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa province, Pakistan","volume":"188","author":[{"family":"Sher","given":"H."},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"R.W."},{"family":"Hart","given":"R."},{"family":"Boer","given":"H.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} dating back to the beginnings of Zoroastrianism ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LYNvgCdW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Falk, 1989)","plainCitation":"(Falk, 1989)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":514,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FDEVSSAU"],"itemData":{"id":514,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bulletin of the School of Oriental and African Studies, University of London","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"77–90","title":"Soma I and II","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Falk","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1989"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ephedra also provides economically for households through harvest and trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"frFvghpF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Karki, 2005; C. S. Olsen & Larsen, 2003; Sher, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Karki, 2005; C. S. Olsen & Larsen, 2003; Sher, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":510,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9DY36MK5"],"itemData":{"id":510,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Acta Horticulturae","DOI":"10.17660/ActaHortic.2005.678.4","language":"en","title":"Creating Livelihoods Enhancing Medicinal and Aromatic Plants based Biodiversity-Rich Production Systems: Preliminary Lessons from South Asia","URL":"https://doi.org/10.17660/ActaHortic.2005.678.4","author":[{"family":"Karki","given":"M."},{"family":"Tiwari","given":"B."},{"family":"Badoni","given":"A."},{"family":"Bhattarai","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":33053,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FXXEF2TM"],"itemData":{"id":33053,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Commercial alpine medicinal plants are collected from the wild by local$\\\\backslash$nrural households throughout the Himalaya and sold in order to increase$\\\\backslash$nhousehold incomes. Recent studies indicate that this annual trade$\\\\backslash$namounts to thousands of tonnes of roots, rhizomes, tubers, leaves, etc.,$\\\\backslash$nworth millions of US dollars. The main market is in India. Based on a$\\\\backslash$nnational survey, including the most commonly traded species, and a$\\\\backslash$nvillage study this paper investigates the importance of the alpine$\\\\backslash$nmedicinal plant trade at national and local levels in Nepal. The$\\\\backslash$nnational survey included standardized open-ended interviews with 232$\\\\backslash$nharvesters, 64 local traders, 66 central wholesalers, 47 regional$\\\\backslash$nwholesalers, and 16 production companies. The village survey is based on$\\\\backslash$nthe daily records of household activities in 15 households in a one-year$\\\\backslash$nperiod. The annual Nepalese alpine and sub-alpine medicinal plant trade$\\\\backslash$nis conservatively estimated to vary from 480 to 2500 t with a total$\\\\backslash$nharvester value of US$0.8-3.3 million; the average harvester value is\\\\backslashnestimated at US$66.0 +/- 99.0. The trade in 1997/98 amounted to 1600 t$\\\\backslash$nwith a harvester value of US$2.3 million and an export value equivalent\\\\backslashnto 2.50% of total export from Nepal. Medicinal plant harvesting was\\\\backslashnfound to constitute an integrated part of local livelihood strategies,\\\\backslashncontributing from 3 to 44% (average of 12%) of the annual household\\\\backslashnincome. Importance at household level depended on land and animal\\\\backslashnholdings, and the availability of adult male labour. The validity and\\\\backslashnreliability of methods and analyses is evaluated, and issues of\\\\backslashnconservation and management of alpine medicinal plant species under the\\\\backslashncommunity forestry scheme are discussed.","container-title":"Geographical Journal","DOI":"10.1111/1475-4959.00088","ISSN":"00167398","issue":"3","page":"243–254","title":"Alpine medicinal plant trade and Himalayan mountain livelihood strategies","volume":"169","author":[{"family":"Olsen","given":"Carsten Smith"},{"family":"Larsen","given":"Helle Overgaard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":33611,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q687XDBB"],"itemData":{"id":33611,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","language":"en","note":"publisher: ScienceDirect","page":"57–69","title":"Traditional use of medicinal plants among Kalasha, Ismaeli and Sunni groups in Chitral District, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa province, Pakistan","volume":"188","author":[{"family":"Sher","given":"H."},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"R.W."},{"family":"Hart","given":"R."},{"family":"Boer","given":"H.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which is highly driven by interest from pharmaceutical companies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mD6vdpKE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lal & Samant, 2019; V. S. Negi, 2018; Sheng-Ji, 2001; Z. Shinwari & Gilani, 2003; Upadhyay, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Lal & Samant, 2019; V. S. Negi, 2018; Sheng-Ji, 2001; Z. Shinwari & Gilani, 2003; Upadhyay, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":513,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BRAL36GN"],"itemData":{"id":513,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Nutraceutical Plants from the Himalayas. New India Publishing Agency","language":"en","page":"177–203","title":"Ephedra L","author":[{"family":"Lal","given":"M."},{"family":"Samant","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":33592,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EXLMFYNK"],"itemData":{"id":33592,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Indian Himalayan Region (IHR) is considered as a rich repository of the Medicinal and Aromatic Plants (MAPs), and has diverse traditional and modern base of therapeutic knowledge. Inhabitants of the region are traditionally well familiar with the healing properties of the available plants and therefore, dependency on medicinal plants for primary healthcare forms integral part of their life. Besides, modern pharmaceutical industries are also relying upon medicinal plants for developing new drugs or searching novel biomolecules. However, over-harvesting and habitat loss of MAPs has reduced its population in the wild, and threatened the sustainability of a number of species in IHR. This calls for an urgent attention and requires selection of suitable criteria and indicators towards encouraging long-term economic and environmental sustainability of the MAP sector. In this context, this study focuses on identifying four major sustainability parameters; i.e. economic, environmental/ecological, socio-cultural and governance, with 11 criteria and 48 indicators. The criteria and indicators approach is expected to be useful in assessing the impacts and monitoring the developmental initiatives in the MAP sector. Further, the study also prioritized potential MAP species for linking cultivation and conservation with livelihood opportunity in Western Himalaya. A total of 152 potential MAP species were identified based on medicinal value, market demand, availability and uses in traditional herbal system. Of these, 43 potential MAPs have been prioritized for cultivation and conservation in Western Himalaya. In addition, distribution and diversity of threatened MAPs of IHR has been documented and presented in a manner to develop strategies for sustainability of the sector at state and national levels.","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2018.03.032","ISSN":"1470-160X","language":"en","page":"434–446","title":"Criteria and indicators for promoting cultivation and conservation of Medicinal and Aromatic Plants in Western Himalaya, India","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1470160X18301882","volume":"93","author":[{"family":"Negi","given":"Vikram S."},{"family":"Kewlani","given":"Pushpa"},{"family":"Pathak","given":"Ravi"},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"Deepika"},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"Indra D."},{"family":"Rawal","given":"Ranbeer S."},{"family":"Sundriyal","given":"R. C."},{"family":"Nandi","given":"S. K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10]]}}},{"id":33612,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MWEX3XMH"],"itemData":{"id":33612,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pharmaceutical Biology","DOI":"10.1076/phbi.39.s1.74.0005","issue":"sup1","language":"en","page":"74–79","title":"Ethnobotanical Approaches of Traditional Medicine Studies: Some Experiences From Asia","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Sheng-Ji","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":501,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MNWMUIHI"],"itemData":{"id":501,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","issue":"2–3","language":"en","note":"publisher: PASCAL Archive","page":"289–298","title":"Sustainable harvest of medicinal plants at Bulashbar Nullah, Astore (Northern Pakistan","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Shinwari","given":"Zabta"},{"family":"Gilani","given":"Syed"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":32938,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B897QZV7"],"itemData":{"id":32938,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Work Organisation and Emotion","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"339–356","title":"Workplace spirituality and subjective happiness at higher educational institutions: an Indian perspective","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Upadhyay","given":"R.K."},{"family":"Mishra","given":"A.K."},{"family":"Jain","given":"V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Ephedra genus has eleven different species throughout the higher elevations of the Himalayas, one of which is E. gerardiana, found in drier temperate and alpine areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"L5S8wTET","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lal & Samant, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Lal & Samant, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":513,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BRAL36GN"],"itemData":{"id":513,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Nutraceutical Plants from the Himalayas. New India Publishing Agency","language":"en","page":"177–203","title":"Ephedra L","author":[{"family":"Lal","given":"M."},{"family":"Samant","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. E. gerardiana is becoming increasingly vulnerable due to landscape change and over-harvesting in many regions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Qcw71oOi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Roland, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Roland, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":502,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7WVJZWSQ"],"itemData":{"id":502,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.2305/IUCN.UK.2020-3.RLTS.T149444511A150130945.en.","title":"Ephedra Gerardiana. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2020","URL":"https://dx.doi.org/10.2305/IUCN.UK.2020-3.RLTS.T149444511A150130945.en.","author":[{"family":"Roland","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} driven by the need for income in many rural subsistence communities. In the Uttarakhand state of India, a high number of medicinal and aromatic plantsi including E. gerardiana are found to be under pressure, resulting from factors which include: loss of indigenous knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"viPaXasH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. Kumar, 2015)","plainCitation":"(K. Kumar, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33591,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DQJEFNDW"],"itemData":{"id":33591,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"There is a consensus in the literature and widespread policymaker support on the desirability of democratic decentralisation of natural resources governance. However, few decentralisation initiatives in developing countries have led to more democratic governance of natural resources. India's Recognition of Forest Rights Act (RFRA), 2006, was enacted as a result of democratic processes driven by demand for recognition of forest rights by forest dwellers. RFRA represents a political, demand-based effort to reform forest governance through a provision of rights to forest-dependent people. India also continues to operate the joint forest management (JFM) programme, a more traditional state-initiated decentralisation effort. The two parallel forest reform programmes being carried out in the same landscapes provide a unique opportunity to study democratisation of forest governance in the country with the world's largest number of forest-dependent people. We examine JFM and RFRA on the criteria of delegation of power and authority, downward accountability and impact on forest-dependent poor to understand the substantively different spaces that they open for democratic forest governance. We find that the implementation of the RFRA has been strongly opposed by powerful interests, and its more radical provisions related to community rights over forests have largely remained unimplemented. However, our findings, drawing from both primary and secondary sources, also bring out the potential of the RFRA to hold the forest bureaucracy accountable to forest-dweller communities and its ability to shift tangible legal powers and authority to forest dwellers.","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2014.09.018","ISSN":"1389-9341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","page":"1-8","title":"Decentralisation and democratic forest reforms in India: Moving to a rights-based approach","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1389934114001786","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Kumar","given":"Kundan"},{"family":"Singh","given":"Neera M."},{"family":"Kerr","given":"John M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]],"season":"Fevereiro"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, disinterest from younger generations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bgKUrSlP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. Kumar, 2015; V. S. Negi, 2018)","plainCitation":"(K. Kumar, 2015; V. S. Negi, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33591,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DQJEFNDW"],"itemData":{"id":33591,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"There is a consensus in the literature and widespread policymaker support on the desirability of democratic decentralisation of natural resources governance. However, few decentralisation initiatives in developing countries have led to more democratic governance of natural resources. India's Recognition of Forest Rights Act (RFRA), 2006, was enacted as a result of democratic processes driven by demand for recognition of forest rights by forest dwellers. RFRA represents a political, demand-based effort to reform forest governance through a provision of rights to forest-dependent people. India also continues to operate the joint forest management (JFM) programme, a more traditional state-initiated decentralisation effort. The two parallel forest reform programmes being carried out in the same landscapes provide a unique opportunity to study democratisation of forest governance in the country with the world's largest number of forest-dependent people. We examine JFM and RFRA on the criteria of delegation of power and authority, downward accountability and impact on forest-dependent poor to understand the substantively different spaces that they open for democratic forest governance. We find that the implementation of the RFRA has been strongly opposed by powerful interests, and its more radical provisions related to community rights over forests have largely remained unimplemented. However, our findings, drawing from both primary and secondary sources, also bring out the potential of the RFRA to hold the forest bureaucracy accountable to forest-dweller communities and its ability to shift tangible legal powers and authority to forest dwellers.","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2014.09.018","ISSN":"1389-9341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","page":"1-8","title":"Decentralisation and democratic forest reforms in India: Moving to a rights-based approach","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1389934114001786","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Kumar","given":"Kundan"},{"family":"Singh","given":"Neera M."},{"family":"Kerr","given":"John M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]],"season":"Fevereiro"}}},{"id":33592,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EXLMFYNK"],"itemData":{"id":33592,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Indian Himalayan Region (IHR) is considered as a rich repository of the Medicinal and Aromatic Plants (MAPs), and has diverse traditional and modern base of therapeutic knowledge. Inhabitants of the region are traditionally well familiar with the healing properties of the available plants and therefore, dependency on medicinal plants for primary healthcare forms integral part of their life. Besides, modern pharmaceutical industries are also relying upon medicinal plants for developing new drugs or searching novel biomolecules. However, over-harvesting and habitat loss of MAPs has reduced its population in the wild, and threatened the sustainability of a number of species in IHR. This calls for an urgent attention and requires selection of suitable criteria and indicators towards encouraging long-term economic and environmental sustainability of the MAP sector. In this context, this study focuses on identifying four major sustainability parameters; i.e. economic, environmental/ecological, socio-cultural and governance, with 11 criteria and 48 indicators. The criteria and indicators approach is expected to be useful in assessing the impacts and monitoring the developmental initiatives in the MAP sector. Further, the study also prioritized potential MAP species for linking cultivation and conservation with livelihood opportunity in Western Himalaya. A total of 152 potential MAP species were identified based on medicinal value, market demand, availability and uses in traditional herbal system. Of these, 43 potential MAPs have been prioritized for cultivation and conservation in Western Himalaya. In addition, distribution and diversity of threatened MAPs of IHR has been documented and presented in a manner to develop strategies for sustainability of the sector at state and national levels.","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2018.03.032","ISSN":"1470-160X","language":"en","page":"434–446","title":"Criteria and indicators for promoting cultivation and conservation of Medicinal and Aromatic Plants in Western Himalaya, India","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1470160X18301882","volume":"93","author":[{"family":"Negi","given":"Vikram S."},{"family":"Kewlani","given":"Pushpa"},{"family":"Pathak","given":"Ravi"},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"Deepika"},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"Indra D."},{"family":"Rawal","given":"Ranbeer S."},{"family":"Sundriyal","given":"R. C."},{"family":"Nandi","given":"S. K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} over-harvesting and illegal trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Rn7NPGQT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Joshi & Rao, 2011; C. P. Kala, 2005; Phondani, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Joshi & Rao, 2011; C. P. Kala, 2005; Phondani, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":507,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YL8XZ7WH"],"itemData":{"id":507,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Earth Sciences","DOI":"10.1007/978-3-540-95991-5-10","language":"fr","note":"publisher: Scopus®","page":"91–101","title":"Role of indigenous people in conservation of biodiversity of medicinal plants: An Indian case study","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-95991-5-10","author":[{"family":"Joshi","given":"P."},{"family":"Rao","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":509,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/39UY9K5X"],"itemData":{"id":509,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2005.00602.x","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"368–378","title":"Indigenous Uses, Population Density, and Conservation of Threatened Medicinal Plants in Protected Areas of the Indian Himalayas","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Kala","given":"C.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":504,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YKX3MPE2"],"itemData":{"id":504,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Asia-Pacific Biodiversity","DOI":"10.1016/j.japb.2015.12.001","language":"en","title":"Promoting medicinal plants cultivation for livelihood enhancement and biodiversity conservation in Indian Himalayan region","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Phondani","given":"P."},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"I."},{"family":"Negi","given":"V."},{"family":"Kothyari","given":"B."},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, land pressures ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"V41RuKq6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kanwal & Joshi, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Kanwal & Joshi, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":508,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ESACK9A7"],"itemData":{"id":508,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EurAsian Journal of Biosciences","issue":"7/8","language":"en","note":"publisher: Academic Search Complete","page":"61–77","title":"The impact of hydroelectric project development on the ethnobotany of the Alaknanda river basin of Western Himalaya, India","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Kanwal","given":"K.S."},{"family":"Joshi","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, lack of policy and conservation enforcement ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fCISDYw4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Joshi & Rao, 2011; C. P. Kala, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Joshi & Rao, 2011; C. P. Kala, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":507,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YL8XZ7WH"],"itemData":{"id":507,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Earth Sciences","DOI":"10.1007/978-3-540-95991-5-10","language":"fr","note":"publisher: Scopus®","page":"91–101","title":"Role of indigenous people in conservation of biodiversity of medicinal plants: An Indian case study","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-95991-5-10","author":[{"family":"Joshi","given":"P."},{"family":"Rao","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":509,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/39UY9K5X"],"itemData":{"id":509,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2005.00602.x","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"368–378","title":"Indigenous Uses, Population Density, and Conservation of Threatened Medicinal Plants in Protected Areas of the Indian Himalayas","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Kala","given":"C.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and climate change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4Khht0z9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. S. Negi, 2018)","plainCitation":"(V. S. Negi, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33592,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EXLMFYNK"],"itemData":{"id":33592,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Indian Himalayan Region (IHR) is considered as a rich repository of the Medicinal and Aromatic Plants (MAPs), and has diverse traditional and modern base of therapeutic knowledge. Inhabitants of the region are traditionally well familiar with the healing properties of the available plants and therefore, dependency on medicinal plants for primary healthcare forms integral part of their life. Besides, modern pharmaceutical industries are also relying upon medicinal plants for developing new drugs or searching novel biomolecules. However, over-harvesting and habitat loss of MAPs has reduced its population in the wild, and threatened the sustainability of a number of species in IHR. This calls for an urgent attention and requires selection of suitable criteria and indicators towards encouraging long-term economic and environmental sustainability of the MAP sector. In this context, this study focuses on identifying four major sustainability parameters; i.e. economic, environmental/ecological, socio-cultural and governance, with 11 criteria and 48 indicators. The criteria and indicators approach is expected to be useful in assessing the impacts and monitoring the developmental initiatives in the MAP sector. Further, the study also prioritized potential MAP species for linking cultivation and conservation with livelihood opportunity in Western Himalaya. A total of 152 potential MAP species were identified based on medicinal value, market demand, availability and uses in traditional herbal system. Of these, 43 potential MAPs have been prioritized for cultivation and conservation in Western Himalaya. In addition, distribution and diversity of threatened MAPs of IHR has been documented and presented in a manner to develop strategies for sustainability of the sector at state and national levels.","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2018.03.032","ISSN":"1470-160X","language":"en","page":"434–446","title":"Criteria and indicators for promoting cultivation and conservation of Medicinal and Aromatic Plants in Western Himalaya, India","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1470160X18301882","volume":"93","author":[{"family":"Negi","given":"Vikram S."},{"family":"Kewlani","given":"Pushpa"},{"family":"Pathak","given":"Ravi"},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"Deepika"},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"Indra D."},{"family":"Rawal","given":"Ranbeer S."},{"family":"Sundriyal","given":"R. C."},{"family":"Nandi","given":"S. K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Primarily because of the economic demand and overharvesting of medicinal and aromatic plants for harvest and economic trade, the need to turn towards partnerships with local farmers to cultivate certain plants is recognized. Pilot projects are underway in the Champawat district ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1QItix9E","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Phondani, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Phondani, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":504,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YKX3MPE2"],"itemData":{"id":504,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Asia-Pacific Biodiversity","DOI":"10.1016/j.japb.2015.12.001","language":"en","title":"Promoting medicinal plants cultivation for livelihood enhancement and biodiversity conservation in Indian Himalayan region","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Phondani","given":"P."},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"I."},{"family":"Negi","given":"V."},{"family":"Kothyari","given":"B."},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and the Byans valley ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"E7nrLKNc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. S. Negi, 2018)","plainCitation":"(V. S. Negi, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33592,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EXLMFYNK"],"itemData":{"id":33592,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Indian Himalayan Region (IHR) is considered as a rich repository of the Medicinal and Aromatic Plants (MAPs), and has diverse traditional and modern base of therapeutic knowledge. Inhabitants of the region are traditionally well familiar with the healing properties of the available plants and therefore, dependency on medicinal plants for primary healthcare forms integral part of their life. Besides, modern pharmaceutical industries are also relying upon medicinal plants for developing new drugs or searching novel biomolecules. However, over-harvesting and habitat loss of MAPs has reduced its population in the wild, and threatened the sustainability of a number of species in IHR. This calls for an urgent attention and requires selection of suitable criteria and indicators towards encouraging long-term economic and environmental sustainability of the MAP sector. In this context, this study focuses on identifying four major sustainability parameters; i.e. economic, environmental/ecological, socio-cultural and governance, with 11 criteria and 48 indicators. The criteria and indicators approach is expected to be useful in assessing the impacts and monitoring the developmental initiatives in the MAP sector. Further, the study also prioritized potential MAP species for linking cultivation and conservation with livelihood opportunity in Western Himalaya. A total of 152 potential MAP species were identified based on medicinal value, market demand, availability and uses in traditional herbal system. Of these, 43 potential MAPs have been prioritized for cultivation and conservation in Western Himalaya. In addition, distribution and diversity of threatened MAPs of IHR has been documented and presented in a manner to develop strategies for sustainability of the sector at state and national levels.","container-title":"Ecological Indicators","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2018.03.032","ISSN":"1470-160X","language":"en","page":"434–446","title":"Criteria and indicators for promoting cultivation and conservation of Medicinal and Aromatic Plants in Western Himalaya, India","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1470160X18301882","volume":"93","author":[{"family":"Negi","given":"Vikram S."},{"family":"Kewlani","given":"Pushpa"},{"family":"Pathak","given":"Ravi"},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"Deepika"},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"Indra D."},{"family":"Rawal","given":"Ranbeer S."},{"family":"Sundriyal","given":"R. C."},{"family":"Nandi","given":"S. K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",5,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} to create gardens for medicinal and aromatic plants to be sustainably harvested for household income and primary health care. While this is just a beginning, India is showing benefits of local markets and herbal gardens playing a role in sustainable growing and harvesting methods of medicinal and aromatic plantss such as E. gerardiana ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a1dSTAUi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Joshi & Rao, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Joshi & Rao, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":507,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YL8XZ7WH"],"itemData":{"id":507,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Earth Sciences","DOI":"10.1007/978-3-540-95991-5-10","language":"fr","note":"publisher: Scopus®","page":"91–101","title":"Role of indigenous people in conservation of biodiversity of medicinal plants: An Indian case study","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-95991-5-10","author":[{"family":"Joshi","given":"P."},{"family":"Rao","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Pakistan’s upper Chitral Valley and Astore district, medicinal and aromatic plants are vulnerable due to landscape pressures including deforestation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uXko6O28","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. Khan, 2011; Shinwari & Gilani, 2003)","plainCitation":"(N. Khan, 2011; Shinwari & Gilani, 2003)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":511,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HHLXSDQS"],"itemData":{"id":511,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pakistan Journal of Botany","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"797–809","title":"Important medicinal plants of Chitral Gol National park (CGNP) Pakistan","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Khan","given":"N."},{"family":"Ahmed","given":"M."},{"family":"Ahmed","given":"A."},{"family":"Shaukat","given":"S.S."},{"family":"Wahab","given":"M."},{"family":"Ajaib","given":"M."},{"family":"Siddiqui","given":"M."},{"family":"Nasir","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":501,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MNWMUIHI"],"itemData":{"id":501,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","issue":"2–3","language":"en","note":"publisher: PASCAL Archive","page":"289–298","title":"Sustainable harvest of medicinal plants at Bulashbar Nullah, Astore (Northern Pakistan","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Shinwari","given":"Zabta"},{"family":"Gilani","given":"Syed"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and habitat destruction because of over-grazing and soil erosion ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"H0KOa0ze","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alam, 2017; N. Khan, 2011; Ullah & Ur Rehman, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Alam, 2017; N. Khan, 2011; Ullah & Ur Rehman, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33593,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8VLUU37M"],"itemData":{"id":33593,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"volume: 16","title":"The Traditional Knowledge of Some Phenorogames of Molkhow—Valley District Chitral","author":[{"family":"Alam","given":"J."},{"family":"Ali","given":"H."},{"family":"Ahmad","given":"H."},{"family":"Muhammad","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":511,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HHLXSDQS"],"itemData":{"id":511,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pakistan Journal of Botany","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"797–809","title":"Important medicinal plants of Chitral Gol National park (CGNP) Pakistan","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Khan","given":"N."},{"family":"Ahmed","given":"M."},{"family":"Ahmed","given":"A."},{"family":"Shaukat","given":"S.S."},{"family":"Wahab","given":"M."},{"family":"Ajaib","given":"M."},{"family":"Siddiqui","given":"M."},{"family":"Nasir","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33610,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8ME2PIRI"],"itemData":{"id":33610,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pakistan Journal of Weed Science Research","issue":"1","language":"en","title":"Conservation issues of medicinal plants of Zewar Valley Upper Chitral, Hindukush range Pakistan","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Ullah","given":"A."},{"family":"Ur Rehman","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. According to ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7h5o91Qz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sher, 2016; Shinwari & Gilani, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Sher, 2016; Shinwari & Gilani, 2003)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33611,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q687XDBB"],"itemData":{"id":33611,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","language":"en","note":"publisher: ScienceDirect","page":"57–69","title":"Traditional use of medicinal plants among Kalasha, Ismaeli and Sunni groups in Chitral District, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa province, Pakistan","volume":"188","author":[{"family":"Sher","given":"H."},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"R.W."},{"family":"Hart","given":"R."},{"family":"Boer","given":"H.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":501,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MNWMUIHI"],"itemData":{"id":501,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","issue":"2–3","language":"en","note":"publisher: PASCAL Archive","page":"289–298","title":"Sustainable harvest of medicinal plants at Bulashbar Nullah, Astore (Northern Pakistan","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Shin