Ur Rehman, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Alam, 2017; N. Khan, 2011; Ullah & Ur Rehman, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33593,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8VLUU37M"],"itemData":{"id":33593,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"volume: 16","title":"The Traditional Knowledge of Some Phenorogames of Molkhow—Valley District Chitral","author":[{"family":"Alam","given":"J."},{"family":"Ali","given":"H."},{"family":"Ahmad","given":"H."},{"family":"Muhammad","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":511,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HHLXSDQS"],"itemData":{"id":511,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pakistan Journal of Botany","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"797–809","title":"Important medicinal plants of Chitral Gol National park (CGNP) Pakistan","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Khan","given":"N."},{"family":"Ahmed","given":"M."},{"family":"Ahmed","given":"A."},{"family":"Shaukat","given":"S.S."},{"family":"Wahab","given":"M."},{"family":"Ajaib","given":"M."},{"family":"Siddiqui","given":"M."},{"family":"Nasir","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33610,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8ME2PIRI"],"itemData":{"id":33610,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pakistan Journal of Weed Science Research","issue":"1","language":"en","title":"Conservation issues of medicinal plants of Zewar Valley Upper Chitral, Hindukush range Pakistan","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Ullah","given":"A."},{"family":"Ur Rehman","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is largely a result of global demand and dealers without conservation policies, and limited local knowledge of the need for sustainable harvesting practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OjQ98p7j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ali & Qaiser, 2009; Z. Shinwari & Gilani, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Ali & Qaiser, 2009; Z. Shinwari & Gilani, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30059,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8SJYDN8Z","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6BPNAFVF"],"itemData":{"id":30059,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pak. J. Bot","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Pak. J. Bot","note":"number: 4","page":"2009-2041","title":"The ethnobotany of Chitral valley, Pakistan with particular reference to medicinal plants","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Ali","given":"Haidar"},{"family":"Qaiser","given":"M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":501,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MNWMUIHI"],"itemData":{"id":501,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","issue":"2–3","language":"en","note":"publisher: PASCAL Archive","page":"289–298","title":"Sustainable harvest of medicinal plants at Bulashbar Nullah, Astore (Northern Pakistan","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Shinwari","given":"Zabta"},{"family":"Gilani","given":"Syed"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition to landscape pressures, overharvesting of medicinal and aromatic plants for income purposes puts E. gerardiana in a continual vulnerable state. Similar to India, it has been recognized by researchers that in order to decrease pressure the delicate Himalayan ecosystem where medicinal and aromatic plants species have been recorded as extinct ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"B2Oh1pkY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dhiman & Rautela, 2014; Z. Shinwari & Gilani, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Dhiman & Rautela, 2014; Z. Shinwari & Gilani, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":515,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T2MCEHE6"],"itemData":{"id":515,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Biotechnology and Biodiversity","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-09381-9_12","page":"247–270","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","title":"Biotechnological Approaches Towards Micropropagation and Conservation of Cycads and Ephedrales","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-09381-9_12","author":[{"family":"Dhiman","given":"M."},{"family":"Rautela","given":"I."}],"editor":[{"family":"Ahuja","given":"M.R."},{"family":"Ramawat","given":"K.G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":501,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MNWMUIHI"],"itemData":{"id":501,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","issue":"2–3","language":"en","note":"publisher: PASCAL Archive","page":"289–298","title":"Sustainable harvest of medicinal plants at Bulashbar Nullah, Astore (Northern Pakistan","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Shinwari","given":"Zabta"},{"family":"Gilani","given":"Syed"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and over-harvesting for income purposes continues; cultivating E. gerardiana ex-situ by pharmaceutical industry and households will be a way forward to allow for sustainable harvest, sales, and use of this high-demand medicinal and aromatic plants. While E. gerardiana has been shown to be one of a few medicinal and aromatic plants that survive well in cultivation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DK0k0K9D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Z. Shinwari & Gilani, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Z. Shinwari & Gilani, 2003)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":501,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MNWMUIHI"],"itemData":{"id":501,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","issue":"2–3","language":"en","note":"publisher: PASCAL Archive","page":"289–298","title":"Sustainable harvest of medicinal plants at Bulashbar Nullah, Astore (Northern Pakistan","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Shinwari","given":"Zabta"},{"family":"Gilani","given":"Syed"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, this has yet to be seen in Pakistan at the time of this research.

      These common pool resource institutions have proven to be particularly effective at addressing what is defined as the open access problem (i.e., unregulated harvest). Many authors including ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"K7tNL9R2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 1989; McCay & Acheson, 1987; Van Den Berg, 2007; Wakjira & Gole, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 1989; McCay & Acheson, 1987; Van Den Berg, 2007; Wakjira & Gole, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32560,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/64BDD3UB"],"itemData":{"id":32560,"type":"book","language":"en","number-of-pages":"70–88","publisher":"Belhaven Press","title":"Common Property Resources: Ecology and Community-Based Sustainable Development","author":[{"literal":"Berkes"}],"editor":[{"family":"","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1989"]]}}},{"id":33601,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DBN3X246"],"itemData":{"id":33601,"type":"book","ISBN":"0-8165-1205-1","publisher":"University of Arizona Press","title":"The question of the commons: The culture and ecology of communal resources","author":[{"family":"McCay","given":"Bonnie J"},{"family":"Acheson","given":"James M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1987"]]}}},{"id":519,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/27K3EANN"],"itemData":{"id":519,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"183–197","title":"The role and dynamics of community institutions in the management of NTFP resources","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Van Den Berg","given":"J."},{"family":"K.F","given":"Wiersum"},{"family":"Van Dijk","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":520,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6DM94Q46"],"itemData":{"id":520,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"325–338","title":"Customary forest tenure in southwest Ethiopia","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Wakjira","given":"Dereje Tadesse"},{"family":"Gole","given":"Tadesse Woldemariam"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} have evidenced the usefulness of customary and community-based resource management systems in the sustainable management of small-scale fisheries and of plants, algae and fungi. Similarly, there is concrete evidence that participatory, community-based natural resource management approaches are effective in promoting sustainable use of wild species, in Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RuSDVTv4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Abensperg-Traun, 2009; Rowcliff, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Abensperg-Traun, 2009; Rowcliff, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":503,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/88ENLI33"],"itemData":{"id":503,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proc. R. Soc. Lond. B","DOI":"10.1098/rspb.2004.2915","language":"en","page":"2631–2636","title":"Do wildlife laws work? Species protection and the application of a prey choice model to poaching decisions","volume":"271","author":[{"family":"Rowcliff","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Merode","given":"Emmanuel"},{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"Guy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":34640,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WZBYGGSA"],"itemData":{"id":34640,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2008.12.034","ISSN":"00063207","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"948-963","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"CITES, sustainable use of wild species and incentive-driven conservation in developing countries, with an emphasis on southern Africa","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320709000615","volume":"142","author":[{"family":"Abensperg-Traun","given":"Max"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and in Asia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PLg29WjY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nasi, Brown, and Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity 2008)","plainCitation":"(Nasi, Brown, and Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33016,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P5F4WW5N"],"itemData":{"id":33016,"type":"book","event-place":"Montréal, Québec, Canada","language":"en","note":"OCLC: 472721465","publisher":"Secrétariat de la Convention sur la diversité biologique","publisher-place":"Montréal, Québec, Canada","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Conservation et utilisation des ressources fauniques: la crise de la viande de brousse","title-short":"Conservation et utilisation des ressources fauniques","author":[{"family":"Nasi","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Brown","given":"David"},{"literal":"Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, especially with the involvement of local and indigenous peoples and their traditional knowledge of sustainable use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Aec6no1C","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Freeman, 1999; Hakimzumwami, 2000)","plainCitation":"(M. Freeman, 1999; Hakimzumwami, 2000)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32534,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JARHMX4J"],"itemData":{"id":32534,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"International Symposium on Society and Resource Management","event-place":"Brisbane, Australia","language":"en","publisher-place":"Brisbane, Australia","title":"They knew how much to take","URL":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Milton_Freeman/publication/274385326_They_knew_how_much_to_take_Respect_and_reciprocity_in_arctic_sustainable_use_strategies/links/56974a0208ae1c4279041c2e/They-knew-how-much-to-take-Respect-and-reciprocity-in-arctic-sustainable-use-strategies.pdf","author":[{"family":"Freeman","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":33185,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7TGF22ST"],"itemData":{"id":33185,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Biodiversity and Livelihoods Group, International Institute for Environment","title":"Community Wildlife Management in Central Africa: A Regional Review","author":[{"family":"Hakimzumwami","given":"Elie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At the same time, there is also evidence of failure of such approaches ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ByLx43YC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Acheson, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Acheson, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":29434,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SIRU8EIH","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F3WLWALK"],"itemData":{"id":29434,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annu. Rev. Anthropol.","ISSN":"0084-6570","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Anthropol.","note":"publisher: Annual Reviews","page":"117-134","title":"Institutional failure in resource management","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Acheson","given":"James M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	In Cameroon, harvesting wild plants, algae and fungi on land held by a clan or family may take place only with the family’s permission. On communal lands, any member of a community can harvest for subsistence use, but for high value traded products (such as Prunus africana, Gnetum spp and Irvingia spp), approval is generally required from the chief or village council. Outsiders often require permission to harvest and should provide in-kind or cash compensation before or after harvesting. In some communities, conflicts occurred when such proceeds were not used to benefit the wider community ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eilMMbHg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(VJ Ingram 2015)","plainCitation":"(VJ Ingram 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":517,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/99FRNGGG"],"itemData":{"id":517,"type":"report","publisher":"LEI Wageningen UR","title":"Ensuring the future of the pygeum tree (Prunus africana). Factsheet.","URL":"https://www.bioversityinternational.org/fileadmin/user_upload/online_library/publications/pdfs/Ensuring_the_future_of_the_pygeum_tree_Prunus_africana_2073.pdf","author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"VJ"},{"family":"Loo","given":"J","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Vinceti","given":"B"},{"family":"Dawson","given":"I"},{"family":"Muchugi","given":"A"},{"family":"Duminil","given":"J"},{"family":"Awono","given":"A"},{"family":"Asaah","given":"E"},{"family":"Tchoundjeu","given":"Z"},{"family":"Sunderland","given":"T"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
   In general, the principle of customary law and common pool resource institutions involving wild species are focused on safeguarding the health of species while at the same time meeting social and economic needs. Not all common pool resource institutions are effective; those that are successful generally adhere to the following eight design principles as defined byITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hm9u2z0m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Ostrom 1990)","plainCitation":"(E. Ostrom 1990)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33443,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/85RWE99G"],"itemData":{"id":33443,"type":"book","event-place":"Cambridge","language":"en","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge","title":"Governing the Commons : the Evolution of Institutions for Collective Action","author":[{"family":"Ostrom","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1990"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}):
* Boundaries of users and resource are clear.
* Congruence between benefits and costs.
* Users had procedures for making own rules.
* Regular monitoring of users and resource conditions.
* Graduated sanctions.
* Conflict resolution mechanisms.
* Minimal recognition of rights by government.
* Nested enterprises.

   Among the biggest threats to the sustainability of common-pool resource institutions is industrialization and globalization ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"W8jfVxTM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dietz, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Dietz, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32499,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WJQJH47F"],"itemData":{"id":32499,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"5652","language":"en","page":"1907–12","title":"The Struggle to Govern the Commons","volume":"302","author":[{"family":"Dietz","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"},{"family":"Stern","given":"Paul C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The social, cultural, and ecological stresses created through these processes effectively undermine the systems established within local or regional areas, or in respect of particular species, effectively creating a larger scale open-access problem and “tragedy of the commons” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"X5VBCK2U","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hardin, 1968)","plainCitation":"(Hardin, 1968)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33466,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/55A39R5P"],"itemData":{"id":33466,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The population problem has no technical solution; it requires a fundamental extension in morality.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.162.3859.1243","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"3859","language":"en","license":"© 1968","note":"PMID: 5699198","page":"1243-1248","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"The Tragedy of the Commons","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/162/3859/1243","volume":"162","author":[{"family":"Hardin","given":"Garrett"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1968",12,13]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
   Some mixes of governance and institutional arrangements can lead to unsustainable use of wild species when they are confusing and overlapping; developed without scientific evidence; without consultation with local communities and indigenous peoples, and broader stakeholder involvement; where multiple institutions exist to oversee or govern the same activities but their mandates overlap and conflict, and coordination is not encouraged; and when good laws are not implemented. International and national institutional arrangements can fail to achieve intended results when they are not linked with strong institutions at the national levels, and global, national, and local objectives do not align. In many regions, pluralistic approaches that draw upon international and national policy frameworks and laws, and integrate customary law and local practices, are the most effective for supporting and promoting the sustainable use of wild species. Pluralism is most notable between statutory and informal or customary rules, sometimes as a complement to statutory systems of governance in a context of legal pluralism ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"srNtARmT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Griffiths, 1986; S. A. Laird, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Griffiths, 1986; S. A. Laird, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33482,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y3CEAXUF"],"itemData":{"id":33482,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The journal of legal pluralism and unofficial law","ISSN":"0732-9113","issue":"24","journalAbbreviation":"The journal of legal pluralism and unofficial law","note":"number: 24\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"1-55","title":"What is legal pluralism?","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Griffiths","given":"John"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1986"]]}}},{"id":34331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3K7YP5V"],"itemData":{"id":34331,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","number-of-pages":"393","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","title-short":"Wild product governance","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The balance of evidence from the Commons literature for the past few decades is that neither purely local level management nor purely higher-level management work well alone. Rather, there is a need to design and support management institutions both horizontally (across space) and vertically (across levels of organization). Issues need to be considered simultaneously at several scales when there is coupling or interaction between scales ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6KvPOPPU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2008; Ehara, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2008; Ehara, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":572,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VIQ794NZ"],"itemData":{"id":572,"type":"book","ISBN":"1-139-43479-9","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Navigating social-ecological systems: building resilience for complexity and change","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Colding","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":33458,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/29ER4D9C"],"itemData":{"id":33458,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2018.03.008","ISSN":"09218009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"226-238","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Addressing Maladaptive Coping Strategies of Local Communities to Changes in Ecosystem Service Provisions Using the DPSIR Framework","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800916306887","volume":"149","author":[{"family":"Ehara","given":"Makoto"},{"family":"Hyakumura","given":"Kimihiko"},{"family":"Sato","given":"Ren'ya"},{"family":"Kurosawa","given":"Kiyoshi"},{"family":"Araya","given":"Kunio"},{"family":"Sokh","given":"Heng"},{"family":"Kohsaka","given":"Ryo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
   
4.2.2.5. Trends in governance arrangements 
Over time, governance arrangements constantly evolve and adapt ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VN8N7i1J","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Garcia, 2014)","plainCitation":"(S. Garcia, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34130,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QPB9BYSE"],"itemData":{"id":34130,"type":"article-journal","call-number":"QH91","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Governance of marine fisheries and biodiversity conservation: interaction and co-evolution","title-short":"Governance of marine fisheries and biodiversity conservation","editor":[{"family":"Garcia","given":"Serge"},{"family":"Rice","given":"J. C."},{"family":"Charles","given":"Anthony Trevor"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; stakeholders may try to craft their own arrangements, creating hybrid or completely new arrangements ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ucR2hQFQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cleaver, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Cleaver, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33450,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SJRT8WXM"],"itemData":{"id":33450,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Routledge","title":"Development through bricolage: rethinking institutions for natural resource management","author":[{"family":"Cleaver","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Since the 1960s, natural resource management governance arrangements and approaches in many countries have moved from centralized to decentralized management, and from state-controlled to community, private, public and participatory. Participatory management, or co-management, approaches to natural resources grew up at this time in response to greater awareness of social justice issues, and to develop more effective sustainable management approaches that restore resource rights to communities and ensure they benefit from use of their land and resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"g0W7UiMV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Agrawal & Gibson, 2001; Escobar, 1999; Ghermandi, 2013; Mart\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}n-L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez & Montes, 2015; Neumann, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Agrawal & Gibson, 2001; Escobar, 1999; Ghermandi, 2013; Martín-López & Montes, 2015; Neumann, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33060,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RHBDK75J"],"itemData":{"id":33060,"type":"book","event-place":"New Brunswick, N.J","language":"en","publisher":"Rutgers University Press","publisher-place":"New Brunswick, N.J","title":"Communities and the environment : ethnicity, gender, and the state in community-based conservation","editor":[{"family":"Agrawal","given":"A."},{"family":"Gibson","given":"M.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":33433,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CAKZFPI5"],"itemData":{"id":33433,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Political Ecology","language":"en","title":"Whose Knowlege, Whose Nature? Biodiversity Conservation and Social Movements Political Ecology","author":[{"family":"Escobar","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":33474,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WCUW42KS"],"itemData":{"id":33474,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science & Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.envsci.2013.06.004","issue":"0","language":"en","page":"196–208","title":"The social dimension of biodiversity policy in the European Union: Valuing the benefits to vulnerable communities","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Ghermandi","given":"A."},{"family":"Ding","given":"H."},{"family":"Nunes","given":"P.A.L.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":571,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QXQZYSH"],"itemData":{"id":571,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability Science","ISSN":"1862-4057","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability Science","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Springer","page":"699-706","title":"Restoring the human capacity for conserving biodiversity: a social–ecological approach","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Martín-López","given":"Berta"},{"family":"Montes","given":"Carlos"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":32830,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/33HZNVCP"],"itemData":{"id":32830,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The Routledge handbook of political ecology","event-place":"Abingdon, Oxon ; New York","language":"en","page":"391–405","publisher":"NY Routledge","publisher-place":"Abingdon, Oxon ; New York","title":"Nature conservation","author":[{"family":"Neumann","given":"R.P."}],"editor":[{"family":"Perreault","given":"G.B.T."},{"family":"McCarthy","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some cases, participatory projects produced real gains for sustainable use and local communities (e.g., Gelose law in Madagascar, wood energy in Niger), and through projects carried out in various ecological and socio-political contexts (e.g., wild species management in Zambia and Zimbabwe with the Admade and Campfire programs, extractive reserves in the Brazilian Amazon) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9rEmSxfg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Babin, 2002; Rodary, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Babin, 2002; Rodary, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":527,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WIQNFJ9D"],"itemData":{"id":527,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Patrimonialiser la nature tropicale : dynamiques locales, enjeux internationaux","event-place":"Paris","language":"fr","publisher":"IRD","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Gérer à plusieurs des ressources renouvelables: subsidiarité et médiation patrimoniale par récurrence","author":[{"family":"Babin","given":"D."},{"family":"Antona","given":"M."},{"family":"Bertrand","given":"A."},{"family":"Weber","given":"J."}],"editor":[{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."},{"family":"Juhé-Beaulaton","given":"D."},{"family":"Boutrais","given":"J."},{"family":"Roussel","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":32822,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5U9SKTFG"],"itemData":{"id":32822,"type":"book","event-place":"Paris","language":"fr","publisher":"Karthala","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Conservation de la nature et développement : l’intégration impossible ?","editor":[{"family":"Rodary","given":"E."},{"family":"Castellanet","given":"C."},{"family":"Rossi","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In other cases, these projects re-enforced existing inequities and elite capture, and exacerbated tensions between groups. Social contracts can be diverted by a minority of local elites (made up of customary chiefs, elders of lineages, local elected officials, and/or administrative agents), to the detriment of the majority or specific groups (such as women, youths or the landless) in a community, which have no access to decision-making power, nor to the benefits of conservation, nor to natural resources or their markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"omUmi0vZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brechin, 2003; J. C. Ribot, 2010; Sze, 2017; Vandergeest & Peluso, 2006; Wiersum, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Brechin, 2003; J. C. Ribot, 2010; Sze, 2017; Vandergeest & Peluso, 2006; Wiersum, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32313,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BIYAPV4X"],"itemData":{"id":32313,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Contested Nature. Promoting International Biodiversity with Social Justice in the Twenty-First Century","language":"en","note":"type: and PC West,","page":"159–83","title":"Crafting Conservation Globally and Locally","author":[{"family":"Brechin","given":"Steven R."},{"family":"Wilshusen","given":"Peter R."},{"family":"Benjamin","given":"Charles E."}],"editor":[{"family":"Eds. SR Brechin","given":"CL Fortwangler","suffix":"PR Wilshusen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":33444,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V6DSJ6B7"],"itemData":{"id":33444,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","issue":"1","language":"fr","page":"35–44","title":"Democratic Decentralization in Sub-Saharan Africa: Its Contribution to Forest Management, Livelihoods, and Enfranchisement","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Ribot","given":"Jesse C."},{"family":"Lund","given":"Jens Friis"},{"family":"Treue","given":"Thorsten"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":33475,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T2KRK5RN"],"itemData":{"id":33475,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and History","language":"en","title":"Forestry, empire, Southeast Asian history, agrarian change","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Vandergeest","given":"P."},{"family":"Peluso","given":"N.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":33439,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9TL3CAXV"],"itemData":{"id":33439,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Routledge handbook of gender and environment","event-place":"Abingdon, Oxon ; New York","language":"en","page":"159–168","publisher":"NY","publisher-place":"Abingdon, Oxon ; New York","title":"Gender and environmental justice","author":[{"family":"Sze","given":"J."}],"editor":[{"family":"MacGregor","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8RENKLT3"],"itemData":{"id":833,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","issue":"1-2","language":"en","page":"6–18","title":"Governing access to resources and markets in non-timber forest product chains","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Wiersum","given":"K.F."},{"family":"Ingram","given":"V.J."},{"family":"Ros-Tonen","given":"M.A.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
Until the 1970s central governments tended to view natural resource governance as centralized and top-down. Given the perceived failure of top-down policies, decentralization became the new paradigm during the 1990s following a growing consensus that effective management and governance of environmental challenges required policies and actions that were compatible with the realities and perspectives of local communities that have the most direct contact with the environment yet have the most to lose from environmental degradation (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DJ3kYz81","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dressler, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Dressler, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32686,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V3KS53UB"],"itemData":{"id":32686,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"http://www.journals.cambridge.org/abstract_S0376892910000044","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"5–15","title":"From Hope to Crisis and Back Again? A Critical History of the Global CBNRM Narrative","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Dressler","given":"Wolfram"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      This was supported by an earlier shift in the field of conservation away from purely protectionist approaches towards greater integration of conservation and sustainable use. Articulated in the 1987 United Nations publication of the Brundtland Report (Our Common Future: Report of the World Commission on Environment and Development), this new approach linked conservation with sustainable development. The Convention on Biological Diversity and other agreements emerging from the 1992 UN Conference on Environment and Development further linked conservation, sustainable use, and equity, as did national protected area, conservation, Forestry and other laws drafted to implement the Convention on Biological Diversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Y47nXEGB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sarah A. Laird, McLain, and Wynberg 2010)","plainCitation":"(Sarah A. Laird, McLain, and Wynberg 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3K7YP5V"],"itemData":{"id":34331,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","number-of-pages":"393","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","title-short":"Wild product governance","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      
Box 4.17.  Political drivers of sustainable harvest of sturgeon (Acipenseridae) in the Caspian Sea

The sustainable use of sturgeon of the Acipenseridae family, is a challenge for fishery management regimes throughout the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eSfsXRUo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pikitch, 2005; Pollock, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Pikitch, 2005; Pollock, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33461,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZPHNYWK7"],"itemData":{"id":33461,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000232248000005","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-2979.2005.00190.x","ISSN":"1467-2960","issue":"3","page":"233-265","title":"Status, trends and management of sturgeon and paddlefish fisheries","URL":"://WOS:000232248000005","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Pikitch","given":"E. K."},{"family":"Doukakis","given":"P."},{"family":"Lauck","given":"L."},{"family":"Chakrabarty","given":"P."},{"family":"Erickson","given":"D. L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",9]]}}},{"id":577,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JWX5XLM2"],"itemData":{"id":577,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Reviews","ISSN":"1181-8700","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Reviews","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: NRC Research Press","page":"30-43","title":"Review of a species in peril: what we do not know about lake sturgeon may kill them","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Pollock","given":"Michael S"},{"family":"Carr","given":"Meghan"},{"family":"Kreitals","given":"Natasha M"},{"family":"Phillips","given":"Iain D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Sturgeon, or Acipenseridae, a group of semi-armored fish, pose some biological challenges to conservation due to their low reproductive rates, relatively long life spans, and long migrations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DCpozwwV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(W. Scott & Crossman, 1973)","plainCitation":"(W. Scott & Crossman, 1973)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":576,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y4RI4DKJ"],"itemData":{"id":576,"type":"book","title":"Freshwater Fishes of Canada; Bulletin 184","author":[{"family":"Scott","given":"WB"},{"family":"Crossman","given":"EJ"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1973"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Sturgeon are also the source of black caviar, a scarce and highly priced global commodity, meaning their exploitation is tied to boom-bust economic cycles and resulting in the collapse of many sturgeon populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZvpHT8pc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pikitch, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Pikitch, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33461,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZPHNYWK7"],"itemData":{"id":33461,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000232248000005","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-2979.2005.00190.x","ISSN":"1467-2960","issue":"3","page":"233-265","title":"Status, trends and management of sturgeon and paddlefish fisheries","URL":"://WOS:000232248000005","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Pikitch","given":"E. K."},{"family":"Doukakis","given":"P."},{"family":"Lauck","given":"L."},{"family":"Chakrabarty","given":"P."},{"family":"Erickson","given":"D. L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Caspian Sea is the habitat for a variety of sturgeon species, primarily being Beluga (Huso huso), Russian (Acipenser gueldenstaedtii), Persian (Acipenser persicus), and Stellate (Acipenser stellatus) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6MFJqvrR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ruban & Khodorevskaya, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Ruban & Khodorevskaya, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":575,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ILTA6GE8"],"itemData":{"id":575,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ichthyology","ISSN":"0175-8659","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Applied Ichthyology","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"199-208","title":"Caspian Sea sturgeon fishery: a historic overview","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Ruban","given":"GI"},{"family":"Khodorevskaya","given":"RP"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Caspian Sea represents an important example for international species conservation, with the shores of the sea backing onto multiple countries on the south-western border of Russia. Throughout recent history, sturgeon populations and associated products can be partially explained through the area’s political history, specifically the rise and fall of the Soviet Union within the Caspian Sea region ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qpgzKe2a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Akhmadiyeva & Abdullaev, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Akhmadiyeva & Abdullaev, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":574,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BWELBSVR"],"itemData":{"id":574,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of hydrology","ISSN":"0022-1694","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of hydrology","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"997-1006","title":"Water management paradigm shifts in the Caspian Sea region: Review and outlook","volume":"568","author":[{"family":"Akhmadiyeva","given":"Zarema"},{"family":"Abdullaev","given":"Iskandar"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Due to this jurisdictional overlap, and political history, there are multiple socio-political influences that could affect the fishery. In a contemporary frame, a significant driver of unsustainable and declining sturgeon stocks in the Caspian Sea has been the prevalence of illegal fishing activity, with illegal sturgeon harvesting accounting for several times the level of legal harvesting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4aBQ4wxU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aghilinejhad, 2017; Ermolin & Svolkinas, 2016; Ye & Valbo-J\\\\uc0\\\\u248{}rgensen, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Aghilinejhad, 2017; Ermolin & Svolkinas, 2016; Ye & Valbo-Jørgensen, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":570,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HNAH3DHP"],"itemData":{"id":570,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fisheries","ISSN":"2008-5729","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Fisheries","note":"number: 2","page":"161-169","title":"Identifying Factors Involved in Illegal Fishing in the Southwestern Caspian Sea","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Aghilinejhad","given":"Seyed Mostafa"},{"family":"Gorgin","given":"Saeid"},{"family":"Joolaie","given":"Ramtin"},{"family":"Paighambari","given":"Seyed Yousef"},{"family":"Ghorbani","given":"Rasool"},{"family":"Mohammadi","given":"Jahangir"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":569,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PJTRRTS2"],"itemData":{"id":569,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"14","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity and Conservation","note":"number: 14\\npublisher: Springer","page":"2929-2945","title":"Who owns sturgeon in the Caspian? New theoretical model of social responses towards state conservation policy","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Ermolin","given":"Ilya"},{"family":"Svolkinas","given":"Linas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":573,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BTPEMCG5"],"itemData":{"id":573,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","ISSN":"0165-7836","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Research","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"21-29","title":"Effects of IUU fishing and stock enhancement on and restoration strategies for the stellate sturgeon fishery in the Caspian Sea","volume":"131","author":[{"family":"Ye","given":"Yimin"},{"family":"Valbo-Jørgensen","given":"John"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While illegal fishing activity has been closely tied to the price and scarcity of sturgeon products ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ANTTcd0E","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ruban & Khodorevskaya, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Ruban & Khodorevskaya, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":575,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ILTA6GE8"],"itemData":{"id":575,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ichthyology","ISSN":"0175-8659","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Applied Ichthyology","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"199-208","title":"Caspian Sea sturgeon fishery: a historic overview","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Ruban","given":"GI"},{"family":"Khodorevskaya","given":"RP"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, others have shown that social factors also play a significant or parallel role ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xdGboQJp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aghilinejhad, 2017; Akhmadiyeva & Abdullaev, 2019; Ermolin & Svolkinas, 2016; Mirrasooli, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Aghilinejhad, 2017; Akhmadiyeva & Abdullaev, 2019; Ermolin & Svolkinas, 2016; Mirrasooli, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":570,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HNAH3DHP"],"itemData":{"id":570,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fisheries","ISSN":"2008-5729","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Fisheries","note":"number: 2","page":"161-169","title":"Identifying Factors Involved in Illegal Fishing in the Southwestern Caspian Sea","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Aghilinejhad","given":"Seyed Mostafa"},{"family":"Gorgin","given":"Saeid"},{"family":"Joolaie","given":"Ramtin"},{"family":"Paighambari","given":"Seyed Yousef"},{"family":"Ghorbani","given":"Rasool"},{"family":"Mohammadi","given":"Jahangir"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":574,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BWELBSVR"],"itemData":{"id":574,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of hydrology","ISSN":"0022-1694","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of hydrology","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"997-1006","title":"Water management paradigm shifts in the Caspian Sea region: Review and outlook","volume":"568","author":[{"family":"Akhmadiyeva","given":"Zarema"},{"family":"Abdullaev","given":"Iskandar"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":569,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PJTRRTS2"],"itemData":{"id":569,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"14","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity and Conservation","note":"number: 14\\npublisher: Springer","page":"2929-2945","title":"Who owns sturgeon in the Caspian? New theoretical model of social responses towards state conservation policy","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Ermolin","given":"Ilya"},{"family":"Svolkinas","given":"Linas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":579,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4M98N7AD"],"itemData":{"id":579,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Agricultural and Natural Sciences","ISSN":"2651-3617","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Agricultural and Natural Sciences","note":"number: 2","page":"31-37","title":"Factors affecting on illegal fishing of Persian sturgeon (Acipenser persicus) in the southern Caspian Sea","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Mirrasooli","given":"Elham"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The social perceptions of the legality and enforcement of sturgeon fishing regulation contribute to the actions of legal and illegal fishers, with the actions and behavior of enforcement officials discouraging, and in some cases encouraging, illegal fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"f2HmOYRD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ermolin & Svolkinas, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Ermolin & Svolkinas, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":569,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PJTRRTS2"],"itemData":{"id":569,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"14","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity and Conservation","note":"number: 14\\npublisher: Springer","page":"2929-2945","title":"Who owns sturgeon in the Caspian? New theoretical model of social responses towards state conservation policy","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Ermolin","given":"Ilya"},{"family":"Svolkinas","given":"Linas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Underlying all of these social factors in the sustainability of sturgeon is the social context in which illegal fishing activities are occurring. The state of local employment, individual investments in fishing equipment, and general rates of poverty, all contribute to the persistence of illegal fishing activity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DTWDqnbN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aghilinejhad, 2017; Ermolin & Svolkinas, 2016; Mirrasooli, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Aghilinejhad, 2017; Ermolin & Svolkinas, 2016; Mirrasooli, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":570,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HNAH3DHP"],"itemData":{"id":570,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fisheries","ISSN":"2008-5729","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Fisheries","note":"number: 2","page":"161-169","title":"Identifying Factors Involved in Illegal Fishing in the Southwestern Caspian Sea","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Aghilinejhad","given":"Seyed Mostafa"},{"family":"Gorgin","given":"Saeid"},{"family":"Joolaie","given":"Ramtin"},{"family":"Paighambari","given":"Seyed Yousef"},{"family":"Ghorbani","given":"Rasool"},{"family":"Mohammadi","given":"Jahangir"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":569,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PJTRRTS2"],"itemData":{"id":569,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"14","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity and Conservation","note":"number: 14\\npublisher: Springer","page":"2929-2945","title":"Who owns sturgeon in the Caspian? New theoretical model of social responses towards state conservation policy","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Ermolin","given":"Ilya"},{"family":"Svolkinas","given":"Linas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":579,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4M98N7AD"],"itemData":{"id":579,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Agricultural and Natural Sciences","ISSN":"2651-3617","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Agricultural and Natural Sciences","note":"number: 2","page":"31-37","title":"Factors affecting on illegal fishing of Persian sturgeon (Acipenser persicus) in the southern Caspian Sea","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Mirrasooli","given":"Elham"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The role of local knowledge and familiarity with sturgeon stocks generally is a significant influencing factor in the occurrence of illegal fishing of sturgeon in the Caspian Sea ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5H8Eqjh3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aghilinejhad, 2017; Mirrasooli, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Aghilinejhad, 2017; Mirrasooli, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":570,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HNAH3DHP"],"itemData":{"id":570,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fisheries","ISSN":"2008-5729","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Fisheries","note":"number: 2","page":"161-169","title":"Identifying Factors Involved in Illegal Fishing in the Southwestern Caspian Sea","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Aghilinejhad","given":"Seyed Mostafa"},{"family":"Gorgin","given":"Saeid"},{"family":"Joolaie","given":"Ramtin"},{"family":"Paighambari","given":"Seyed Yousef"},{"family":"Ghorbani","given":"Rasool"},{"family":"Mohammadi","given":"Jahangir"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":579,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4M98N7AD"],"itemData":{"id":579,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Agricultural and Natural Sciences","ISSN":"2651-3617","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Agricultural and Natural Sciences","note":"number: 2","page":"31-37","title":"Factors affecting on illegal fishing of Persian sturgeon (Acipenser persicus) in the southern Caspian Sea","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Mirrasooli","given":"Elham"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some have considered this factor to be a “lack of awareness” on behalf of the fisher people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lCYxGgUj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mirrasooli, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Mirrasooli, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":579,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4M98N7AD"],"itemData":{"id":579,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Agricultural and Natural Sciences","ISSN":"2651-3617","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Agricultural and Natural Sciences","note":"number: 2","page":"31-37","title":"Factors affecting on illegal fishing of Persian sturgeon (Acipenser persicus) in the southern Caspian Sea","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Mirrasooli","given":"Elham"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, while others have conceived this variable as “fishers' knowledge” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CDck6wYH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aghilinejhad, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Aghilinejhad, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":570,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HNAH3DHP"],"itemData":{"id":570,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fisheries","ISSN":"2008-5729","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Fisheries","note":"number: 2","page":"161-169","title":"Identifying Factors Involved in Illegal Fishing in the Southwestern Caspian Sea","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Aghilinejhad","given":"Seyed Mostafa"},{"family":"Gorgin","given":"Saeid"},{"family":"Joolaie","given":"Ramtin"},{"family":"Paighambari","given":"Seyed Yousef"},{"family":"Ghorbani","given":"Rasool"},{"family":"Mohammadi","given":"Jahangir"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Studies of indigenous management in North America concerning sturgeon have shown there is a lack of consideration and mobilization of indigenous knowledge (including fisheries knowledge) in fisheries (and specifically sturgeon) management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RxryXqlQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Oloriz & Parlee, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Oloriz & Parlee, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":578,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JFBEKU6M"],"itemData":{"id":578,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"18","journalAbbreviation":"Sustainability","note":"number: 18\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"7320","title":"Towards biocultural conservation: Local and indigenous knowledge, cultural values and governance of the white sturgeon (Canada)","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Oloriz","given":"Carrie"},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Due to the challenges of complex overlapping jurisdiction harvesting regimes and the prevalence of illegal fishing, the state of sturgeon fisheries in the Caspian Sea is concerning.  Studies here have begun to investigate the complex ways in which social and political factors influence this illegal fishing activity and highlight the need to approach the sustainable harvest of wild species from a more holistic perspective ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"i54BJEzC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aghilinejhad, 2017; Akhmadiyeva & Abdullaev, 2019; Ermolin & Svolkinas, 2016; Mirrasooli, 2019; Pikitch, 2005; Pollock, 2015; Ruban & Khodorevskaya, 2011; Ye & Valbo-J\\\\uc0\\\\u248{}rgensen, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Aghilinejhad, 2017; Akhmadiyeva & Abdullaev, 2019; Ermolin & Svolkinas, 2016; Mirrasooli, 2019; Pikitch, 2005; Pollock, 2015; Ruban & Khodorevskaya, 2011; Ye & Valbo-Jørgensen, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":570,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HNAH3DHP"],"itemData":{"id":570,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Fisheries","ISSN":"2008-5729","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Fisheries","note":"number: 2","page":"161-169","title":"Identifying Factors Involved in Illegal Fishing in the Southwestern Caspian Sea","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Aghilinejhad","given":"Seyed Mostafa"},{"family":"Gorgin","given":"Saeid"},{"family":"Joolaie","given":"Ramtin"},{"family":"Paighambari","given":"Seyed Yousef"},{"family":"Ghorbani","given":"Rasool"},{"family":"Mohammadi","given":"Jahangir"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":574,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BWELBSVR"],"itemData":{"id":574,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of hydrology","ISSN":"0022-1694","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of hydrology","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"997-1006","title":"Water management paradigm shifts in the Caspian Sea region: Review and outlook","volume":"568","author":[{"family":"Akhmadiyeva","given":"Zarema"},{"family":"Abdullaev","given":"Iskandar"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":569,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PJTRRTS2"],"itemData":{"id":569,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"14","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity and Conservation","note":"number: 14\\npublisher: Springer","page":"2929-2945","title":"Who owns sturgeon in the Caspian? New theoretical model of social responses towards state conservation policy","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Ermolin","given":"Ilya"},{"family":"Svolkinas","given":"Linas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":579,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4M98N7AD"],"itemData":{"id":579,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Agricultural and Natural Sciences","ISSN":"2651-3617","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Agricultural and Natural Sciences","note":"number: 2","page":"31-37","title":"Factors affecting on illegal fishing of Persian sturgeon (Acipenser persicus) in the southern Caspian Sea","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Mirrasooli","given":"Elham"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":33461,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZPHNYWK7"],"itemData":{"id":33461,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000232248000005","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-2979.2005.00190.x","ISSN":"1467-2960","issue":"3","page":"233-265","title":"Status, trends and management of sturgeon and paddlefish fisheries","URL":"://WOS:000232248000005","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Pikitch","given":"E. K."},{"family":"Doukakis","given":"P."},{"family":"Lauck","given":"L."},{"family":"Chakrabarty","given":"P."},{"family":"Erickson","given":"D. L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",9]]}}},{"id":577,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JWX5XLM2"],"itemData":{"id":577,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Reviews","ISSN":"1181-8700","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Reviews","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: NRC Research Press","page":"30-43","title":"Review of a species in peril: what we do not know about lake sturgeon may kill them","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Pollock","given":"Michael S"},{"family":"Carr","given":"Meghan"},{"family":"Kreitals","given":"Natasha M"},{"family":"Phillips","given":"Iain D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":575,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ILTA6GE8"],"itemData":{"id":575,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ichthyology","ISSN":"0175-8659","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Applied Ichthyology","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"199-208","title":"Caspian Sea sturgeon fishery: a historic overview","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Ruban","given":"GI"},{"family":"Khodorevskaya","given":"RP"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":573,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BTPEMCG5"],"itemData":{"id":573,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fisheries Research","ISSN":"0165-7836","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Research","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"21-29","title":"Effects of IUU fishing and stock enhancement on and restoration strategies for the stellate sturgeon fishery in the Caspian Sea","volume":"131","author":[{"family":"Ye","given":"Yimin"},{"family":"Valbo-Jørgensen","given":"John"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

      While environmental governance views were changing, so too were views about the role of indigenous peoples and local communities in conservation and sustainable use. Movements that pressured governments to recognize the land, resource, human, cultural, intellectual and other rights of indigenous peoples and local communities influenced global processes and laws like the International Labor Organisation’s Convention No 169, the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, and work on traditional knowledge in the World Intellectual Property Organisation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YT2bZVi3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2010; Posey & Dutfield, 1996)","plainCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2010; Posey & Dutfield, 1996)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3K7YP5V"],"itemData":{"id":34331,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","number-of-pages":"393","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","title-short":"Wild product governance","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":586,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TBD8IX43"],"itemData":{"id":586,"type":"book","ISBN":"0-88936-799-X","publisher":"IDRC","title":"Beyond intellectual property: toward traditional resource rights for indigenous peoples and local communities","author":[{"family":"Posey","given":"Darrell Addison"},{"family":"Dutfield","given":"Graham"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many national governments have moved to recognize the rights of indigenous peoples to control the use of their resources and associated knowledge, but in several countries, this continues to be little more than window dressing and centralized systems continue to control benefits from wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IL57OlWI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arquiza, 2010; Castillo & Alvarez-Castillo, 2009; Novellino, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Arquiza, 2010; Castillo & Alvarez-Castillo, 2009; Novellino, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34200,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RN8QHVDQ"],"itemData":{"id":34200,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Wid product Governance. Finding policies that work for non-timber forest products.","ISBN":"978-1-84407-560-3","page":"155-182","title":"From Barter Trade to Brad Pitt’s Bed: NTFPs and Ancestral Domains in the Philippines.","URL":"https://books.google.fr/books?id=n8OUlIlKTq0C&pg=PR4&lpg=PR4&dq=978-1-84407-560-3&source=bl&ots=OHveSTqmZf&sig=ACfU3U3pcS0KW2MrmpqjXIaFgWhwmCconQ&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjA8tK9w832AhVGExoKHd1rDRUQ6AF6BAgCEAM#v=onepage&q=978-1-84407-560-3&f=false","author":[{"family":"Arquiza","given":"Yasmin D."},{"family":"Guerrero","given":"Maria Cristina S."},{"family":"Gatmaytan","given":"Augusto B."},{"family":"Aquino","given":"Arlynn C."}],"editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"S. A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":585,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6PZZL8XF"],"itemData":{"id":585,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Indigenous Peoples, Consent and Benefit Sharing","page":"271-284","publisher":"Springer","title":"The Law is not Enough: Protecting Indigenous Peoples' Rights Against Mining Interests in the Philippines","author":[{"family":"Castillo","given":"Rosa Cordillera A"},{"family":"Alvarez-Castillo","given":"Fatima"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":32591,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CTLRWX2T"],"itemData":{"id":32591,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","journalAbbreviation":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","note":"publisher: Earthscan","page":"183-98","title":"From Indigenous Customary Practices to Policy Interventions: The Ecolocal and Sociocultural Underpinnings of the NTFP Trade on Palawan Island, the Philippines","author":[{"family":"Novellino","given":"Dario"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the recognition of existing customary governance and institutions for natural resource management has become more common place ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"s0P4eNEk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bromley & Cernea, 1989; Wily & Liz, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Bromley & Cernea, 1989; Wily & Liz, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33452,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X3F2AJL9"],"itemData":{"id":33452,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forests, Trees and Livelihoods","issue":"2-4","language":"en","page":"217–228","title":"Can we own the forest? Looking at the changing tenure environment for community forestry in Africa","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Wily","given":"Alden"},{"literal":"Liz"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":33454,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5IN4T866"],"itemData":{"id":33454,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"World Bank Publications","title":"The management of common property natural resources: Some conceptual and operational fallacies","volume":"57","author":[{"family":"Bromley","given":"D.W."},{"family":"Cernea","given":"M.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1989"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The impact of changes in the governance and institutional framework of conservation on the sustainable use of wild species is difficult to quantify, which should not be surprising given the complex and multidimensional nature of wild species use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WHY4wmN3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alexiades & Shanley, 2004; Neumann & Hirsch, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Alexiades & Shanley, 2004; Neumann & Hirsch, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34325,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K7T9MCZS"],"itemData":{"id":34325,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor Barat","ISBN":"978-979-3361-24-6","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 474721369","publisher":"Publ. for Center for International Forestry Research","publisher-place":"Bogor Barat","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Forest products, livelihoods and conservation: case studies of non-timber forest product systems.","author":[{"family":"Alexiades","given":"M. N."},{"family":"Shanley","given":"A. P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":33446,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YP4HH3XY"],"itemData":{"id":33446,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Cifor","title":"Commercialisation of non-timber forest products: review and analysis of research","author":[{"family":"Neumann","given":"R.P."},{"family":"Hirsch","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Individual cases support these approaches, however broader landscape level conservation gains are more difficult to identify. This is not a problem inherent to conservation and sustainable use that involves local groups and indigenous peoples, but rather reflects the outsized impact of global political, cultural and economic factors. These include global and national governance and institutions that do not adequately regulate sustainability or implement environmental laws, and a large and constantly expanding culture of consumerism in wealthy nations that is overwhelming wild species everywhere.
      The growth in protected areas and innovations in protected areas management is another important trend in governance. Historically, protected areas controlled by governments and reinforced through international agreements (e.g., the Aichi Target of “By 2020, at least 17 % of terrestrial and inland water areas, and 10 % of coastal and marine areas, especially areas of particular importance for biodiversity and ecosystem services, are conserved…” ) have been an important mechanism for conserving the world’s biodiversity by increasing protected areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rRIQMUYY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nepstad, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Nepstad, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33445,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CEB4VR9M"],"itemData":{"id":33445,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"65–73","title":"Inhibition of Amazon Deforestation and Fire by Parks and Indigenous Lands","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Nepstad","given":"D.C."},{"family":"Schwartzman","given":"S."},{"family":"Bamberger","given":"B."},{"family":"Santilli","given":"M."},{"family":"Ray","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Over the past two decades protected area governance has diversified, with significant growth in not only government managed areas but also private and community-based management, and a variety of hybrid, partnership-based models that build multi-layered pluralistic approaches into protected area management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Gr0D4avy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Borrini-Feyerabend, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Borrini-Feyerabend, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33426,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4KM475SM"],"itemData":{"id":33426,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Best Practice Protected Area Guidelines Series","issue":"20","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Gland, Switzerland\\npublisher: IUCN","page":"124","title":"Governance of Protected Areas: From understanding to action","author":[{"literal":"Borrini-Feyerabend"},{"family":"Dudley","given":"Nigel"},{"family":"Jaeger","given":"Tilman"},{"family":"Lassen","given":"B."},{"family":"Broome","given":"Pathak"},{"literal":"Neema"},{"family":"Phillips","given":"A."},{"family":"Sandwith","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Collaborative governance of protected areas has the potential to yield multiple biodiversity and socio-economic benefits through the formation of alliances and partnerships between stakeholders (governments, the private sector, local communities, and non-governmental organizations), as a means of developing consensus and efficiently deploying available skills and resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tb9jNA5N","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Munthali, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Munthali, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":233445,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SZVV7GXK"],"itemData":{"id":233445,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              Sub?Saharan Africa continues to face the daunting challenge of alleviating poverty due to slow economic growth. In southern Africa, most countries are adopting policies that promote the integration of biodiversity conservation and rural development to contribute to rural poverty alleviation. Numerous approaches have been undertaken in this endeavour, including Transfrontier Parks (TFPs) and Transfrontier Conservation Areas (TFCAs). This paper discusses some of the limitations of the TFPs. In conclusion I posit that unlike TFPs, which are state controlled and managed, TFCAs, which promote multi?land use and multi?stakeholder participation are attainable and have a higher probability of sustaining biodiversity conservation and contributing to the alleviation of rural poverty, if: (i) areas of high biodiversity conservation within communal areas can be identified, zoned and leveraged to biodiversity conservation and managed in partnership between the communities and the private sector; (ii) local communities can secure legal rights to their customary land being devoted to biodiversity conservation and use such pieces of land as collateral in negotiating partnerships with the private sector in developing conservation?based enterprises; (iii) functional community natural resource governance institutions can be established and empowered to represent their constituencies in securing fair equity from profits made from sustainable use of the conserved biodiversity assets and tourism businesses; (iv) concerted effort can be invested in developing and implementing family planning and fertility reduction strategies that would slow down human population growth to levels that can be sustained by the available natural resources; and (v) if sustainable financing mechanisms can be developed, and the governance of protected areas occurring in the TFCAs can be broadened to include other stakeholders.","container-title":"Natural Resources Forum","DOI":"10.1111/j.1477-8947.2007.00130.x","ISSN":"0165-0203, 1477-8947","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Natural Resources Forum","language":"en","license":"http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/termsAndConditions#vor","page":"51-60","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Transfrontier conservation areas: Integrating biodiversity and poverty alleviation in Southern Africa","title-short":"Transfrontier conservation areas","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1477-8947.2007.00130.x","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Munthali","given":"Simon M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2024",10,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Such arrangements can draw on various knowledge systems to foster trust and develop joint visions, promote experiential and experimental learning, and function as bridging organizations that lower the costs of collaboration and conflict resolution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"R0nwzyAG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. R. Armitage, Plummer, Berkes, Arthur, Charles, Davidson-Hunt, Diduck, Doubleday, Johnson, Marschke,, 2009; Folke, 2005)","plainCitation":"(D. R. Armitage, Plummer, Berkes, Arthur, Charles, Davidson-Hunt, Diduck, Doubleday, Johnson, Marschke,, 2009; Folke, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32554,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FACHXNVG"],"itemData":{"id":32554,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","DOI":"10.1890/070089","language":"en","page":"95–102","title":"Adaptive co-management for social–ecological complexity","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Armitage","given":"D.R."},{"family":"Plummer","given":"R."},{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Arthur","given":"R.I."},{"family":"Charles","given":"A.T."},{"family":"Davidson-Hunt","given":"I.J."},{"family":"Diduck","given":"A.P."},{"family":"Doubleday","given":"N.C."},{"family":"Johnson","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Marschke","given":"M."},{"family":"McConney","given":"P."},{"family":"Pinkerton","given":"E.W."},{"family":"Wollenberg","given":"E.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":33473,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SF899Z74"],"itemData":{"id":33473,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","language":"en","page":"441–473","title":"Adaptive governance of social- ecological systems","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"T.","given":"Hahn"},{"family":"P.","given":"Olsson"},{"family":"J","given":"Norberg"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      Although protected areas historically excluded use by indigenous peoples and local communities, institutional arrangements are changing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"f5hhBDn2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Colchester, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Colchester, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33425,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A2NXZBZA"],"itemData":{"id":33425,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science & Policy","ISSN":"1462-9011","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Science & Policy","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"145-153","title":"Conservation policy and indigenous peoples","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Colchester","given":"Marcus"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; some parts of the world now privilege use for subsistence and cultural continuity. This is true in the laws and arrangements associated with most protected areas in Canada, New Zealand and some areas of Australia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"g71xXJqD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Lee, 2016; Muller, 2003; H. Ross, 2009; Smyth, 1995; United Nations, 2007; Zurba, 2019)","plainCitation":"(E. Lee, 2016; Muller, 2003; H. Ross, 2009; Smyth, 1995; United Nations, 2007; Zurba, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":582,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4IPUHT7T"],"itemData":{"id":582,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Antipode","ISSN":"0066-4812","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Antipode","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"355-374","title":"Protected areas, country and value: The nature–culture tyranny of the IUCN's protected area guidelines for Indigenous Australians","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Lee","given":"Emma"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":581,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GCWZWL8F"],"itemData":{"id":581,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Australian Geographical Studies","ISSN":"0004-9190","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Australian Geographical Studies","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"29-43","title":"Towards decolonisation of Australia's protected area management: the Nantawarrina Indigenous Protected Area experience","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Muller","given":"Samantha"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":583,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EFWMDXBK"],"itemData":{"id":583,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Australasian Journal of Environmental Management","ISSN":"1448-6563","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Australasian Journal of Environmental Management","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"242-252","title":"Co-management and Indigenous protected areas in Australia: achievements and ways forward","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Ross","given":"Helen"},{"family":"Grant","given":"Chrissy"},{"family":"Robinson","given":"Cathy J"},{"family":"Izurieta","given":"Arturo"},{"family":"Smyth","given":"Dermot"},{"family":"Rist","given":"Phil"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":584,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9CFL676I"],"itemData":{"id":584,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Marine Protected Areas","page":"149-173","publisher":"Springer","title":"Caring for sea country—accommodating indigenous peoples’ interests in marine protected areas","author":[{"family":"Smyth","given":"Dermot"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}},{"id":26833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RUVQJ8RP","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YFGICVWX"],"itemData":{"id":26833,"type":"report","language":"en","title":"United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples—Resolution adopted by the General Assembly on 13 September 2007","URL":"https://www.un.org/development/desa/indigenouspeoples/wp-content/uploads/sites/19/2018/11/UNDRIP_E_web.pdf","author":[{"family":"United Nations","given":"United"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":580,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2EDF634E"],"itemData":{"id":580,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Land","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"10","title":"Indigenous protected and conserved areas (IPCAs), Aichi Target 11 and Canada’s Pathway to Target 1: Focusing conservation on reconciliation","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Zurba","given":"Melanie"},{"family":"Beazley","given":"Karen F"},{"family":"English","given":"Emilie"},{"family":"Buchmann-Duck","given":"Johanna"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some protected areas are now defined as indigenous protected and conserved areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nUiPtKEY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Moola & Roth, 2019; Zurba, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Moola & Roth, 2019; Zurba, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":596,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5SHXVM24"],"itemData":{"id":596,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Reviews","ISSN":"1181-8700","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Reviews","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: NRC Research Press","page":"200-201","title":"Moving beyond colonial conservation models: Indigenous protected and conserved areas offer hope for biodiversity and advancing reconciliation in the Canadian boreal forest1","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Moola","given":"Faisal"},{"family":"Roth","given":"Robin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":580,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2EDF634E"],"itemData":{"id":580,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Land","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"10","title":"Indigenous protected and conserved areas (IPCAs), Aichi Target 11 and Canada’s Pathway to Target 1: Focusing conservation on reconciliation","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Zurba","given":"Melanie"},{"family":"Beazley","given":"Karen F"},{"family":"English","given":"Emilie"},{"family":"Buchmann-Duck","given":"Johanna"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This new approach to protected areas management (i.e., that recognizes social, cultural use) has been positive in ensuring sustainable use. Specifically, “protected areas that explicitly integrated local people as stakeholders tended to be more effective at achieving joint biological conservation and socioeconomic development outcomes”ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zg8ToiPC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Oldekop, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Oldekop, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":568,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GZT3IL95"],"itemData":{"id":568,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12568","ISSN":"08888892","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"133-141","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A global assessment of the social and conservation outcomes of protected areas: Social and Conservation Impacts of Protected Areas","title-short":"A global assessment of the social and conservation outcomes of protected areas","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/cobi.12568","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Oldekop","given":"J. A."},{"family":"Holmes","given":"G."},{"family":"Harris","given":"W. E."},{"family":"Evans","given":"K. L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
4.2.2.6. Land tenure and resource rights 
 Key Messages: 
* Land tenure and rights to resources in a given area can involve many separate rights, which taken together are essential components of sustainable use and broader good governance. Tenure arrangements that foster secure rights over land and resource use and trade can incentivize resource conservation, sustainable use, and diverse livelihoods, in part because they allow for longer-term planning. 
* Secure rights are not guaranteed by a formal title or certificate. In fact, in some cases, customary systems are more secure than formal systems, though in general, due to encroachment on the tenure rights of indigenous peoples and local communities, some kind of formal recognition is increasingly needed. Still, many other contextual factors besides formal recognition determine level of security.
* For forests, centralized management that disregards strong traditional systems and cultural relationships with forests has failed historically to support conservation and sustainable management. 
* In fisheries, centralized management has been shown to be difficult and unresponsive to local conditions, resulting in poor implementation of management measures and a subsequent large amount of illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing activity in almost every jurisdiction. 
* Indigenous territories store large quantities of global terrestrial carbon and see lower rates of ecosystem health declines compared to other areas, but governments have only recognized indigenous peoples’ legal rights to a small portion of the lands they occupy. Secure rights and strong customary governance systems are associated with lower deforestation rates.  
* Even if men’s rights are secure, whether in individual or common property systems, women’s rights tend to be less secure. Securing women’s access to assets and participation in decision-making is seen as integrally related to sustainable livelihoods and resilience. 
* Despite challenges associated with recognizing and securing tenure rights, the growing awareness of the role of tenure in achieving development and environmental goals has created a range of new commitments, initiatives, and policy openings at the country level. 

      Land and resource tenure, understood here as the rights of an individual or group over the use of natural resources, is an essential component of sustainable use and broader good governance. Tenure determines who is allowed to use which resources, in what way, for how long and under what conditions, as well as who is entitled to transfer rights to others and how. During colonial and post-colonial regimes, tenure rights, granted by the state in the form of legal titles over land and other resources, often displaced and marginalized indigenous peoples and local communities who held land and resources under customary systems. The ongoing legitimacy of the latter has nonetheless been increasingly affirmed, including by the Inter-American Court of Human Rights and the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5MoaOnSG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations, 2007)","plainCitation":"(United Nations, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RUVQJ8RP","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YFGICVWX"],"itemData":{"id":26833,"type":"report","language":"en","title":"United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples—Resolution adopted by the General Assembly on 13 September 2007","URL":"https://www.un.org/development/desa/indigenouspeoples/wp-content/uploads/sites/19/2018/11/UNDRIP_E_web.pdf","author":[{"family":"United Nations","given":"United"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Yet, in practice the usurpation of customary rights is common, as is the harassment, criminalization, and murder of environmental and resource rights defenders (global witness).
      Tenure arrangements that grant secure rights over land and resource use can incentivize resource conservation, sustainable use, and diverse livelihoods. Secure resource rights allow for longer-term planning and the use of such rights as collateral for investments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ART5dY68","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(German, 2014)","plainCitation":"(German, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":595,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GRA7YRW4"],"itemData":{"id":595,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Peasant Studies","ISSN":"0306-6150","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Peasant Studies","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"51-78","title":"Shifting rights, property and authority in the forest frontier:‘stakes’ for local land users and citizens","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"German","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Mandondo","given":"Alois"},{"family":"Paumgarten","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Mwitwa","given":"Jacob"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and public-private partnerships. This has sometimes been interpreted to mean that rights should be granted to individuals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9P8DVByq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(De Soto, 2011)","plainCitation":"(De Soto, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":590,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FLWF75JU"],"itemData":{"id":590,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Policy Journal","ISSN":"1936-0924","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"World Policy Journal","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Duke University Press","page":"35-40","title":"This land is your land: A conversation with Hernando de Soto","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"De Soto","given":"Hernando"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but decades of research on common property resources has demonstrated both the importance (for livelihoods, resources and human rights) and the effectiveness of common and communal governance systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"E8p5er4i","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ostrom, 1990a; Ostrom & Nagendra, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Ostrom, 1990a; Ostrom & Nagendra, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33443,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/85RWE99G"],"itemData":{"id":33443,"type":"book","event-place":"Cambridge","language":"en","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge","title":"Governing the Commons : the Evolution of Institutions for Collective Action","author":[{"family":"Ostrom","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1990"]]}}},{"id":33470,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X3932MPA"],"itemData":{"id":33470,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.0607962103","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"51","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","language":"en","page":"19224-19231","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Insights on linking forests, trees, and people from the air, on the ground, and in the laboratory","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/doi/10.1073/pnas.0607962103","volume":"103","author":[{"family":"Ostrom","given":"E."},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",12,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      Rights to land and resources in a given area can consist of many separate and overlapping rights, and may differ according to gender, age and ethnic group, as well as within communities and societal groups ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GrxQyNjV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V Ingram 2016; Wiersum 1997, 1; A. M. Larson 2010)","plainCitation":"(V Ingram 2016; Wiersum 1997, 1; A. M. Larson 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33463,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZKYGLITC"],"itemData":{"id":33463,"type":"chapter","call-number":"SD247 .G46 2016","collection-title":"The earthscan forest library","container-title":"Gender and forests: climate change, tenure, value chains and emerging issues","event-place":"London ; New York","ISBN":"978-1-138-95503-5","publisher":"Routledge, Taylor & Francis Group","publisher-place":"London ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Gender and Forest, Tree and Agroforestry Value Chains: Evidence from Literature","editor":[{"family":"Colfer","given":"Carol J. Pierce"},{"family":"Sijapati Basnett","given":"Bimbika"},{"family":"Elias","given":"Marlene"}],"author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"V"},{"family":"Haverhals","given":"M"},{"family":"Petersen","given":"S"},{"family":"Elias","given":"M"},{"family":"Sijapati Basnett","given":"B"},{"family":"Sola","given":"P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33441,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3JA9WZ44"],"itemData":{"id":33441,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agriculture, ecosystems & environment","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–16","title":"Indigenous exploitation and management of tropical forest resources: an evolutionary continuum in forest-people interactions","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Wiersum","given":"K.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}},"locator":"1"},{"id":33449,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5FFR3P4S"],"itemData":{"id":33449,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Earthscan","title":"Forests for people: Community rights and forest tenure reform","author":[{"family":"Larson","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Barry","given":"D."},{"family":"Dahal","given":"G.R."},{"family":"Colfer","given":"C.J.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hence assuring security is not necessarily a simple task, as granting exclusive rights for one group may deny it to another that also manages and depends on resources for local livelihoods. There has been increasing attention to the potential for inequitable distribution of benefits and power imbalances in conferring resource rights ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OrNZJxj9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fitzgerald, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Fitzgerald, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33476,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MNPSAH3S"],"itemData":{"id":33476,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","ISSN":"1540-9295","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"151-158","title":"Catalyzing fisheries conservation investment","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Fitzgerald","given":"Timothy P"},{"family":"Higgins","given":"Phoebe R"},{"family":"Quilligan","given":"Emma"},{"family":"Sethi","given":"Suresh A"},{"family":"Tobin?de la Puente","given":"John"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Conflicts over resource use are prevalent (and in the case of fisheries resources, increasing) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QkRYd5z8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Spijkers, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Spijkers, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33442,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LL5H3LNC"],"itemData":{"id":33442,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","language":"en","page":"798–806","title":"Marine fisheries and future ocean conflict","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Spijkers","given":"J."},{"family":"Morrison","given":"T.H."},{"family":"Blasiak","given":"R."},{"family":"Cumming","given":"G.S."},{"family":"Osborne","given":"M."},{"family":"Watson","given":"J."},{"family":"Österblom","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and lack of transparency and legal clarity can enable inequitable outcomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IXeUbPL5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Fitzpatrick, 2008, p. 200)","plainCitation":"(P. Fitzpatrick, 2008, p. 200)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":528,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5UBUZZYT"],"itemData":{"id":528,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","ISSN":"0364-152X","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Springer","page":"1-18","title":"Environmental impact assessment under the Mackenzie Valley Resource Management Act: deliberative democracy in Canada’s north?","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Fitzpatrick","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Sinclair","given":"A John"},{"family":"Mitchell","given":"Bruce"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}},"locator":"200"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In this context, addressing tenure rights can become a mechanism not only for incentivizing sustainable use but for supporting just transitions to sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"N8W8EzBx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. J. Bennett, 2019)","plainCitation":"(N. J. Bennett, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UZZZ5JYA"],"itemData":{"id":589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Sustainability","ISSN":"2398-9629","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Nature Sustainability","note":"number: 11\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"991-993","title":"Towards a sustainable and equitable blue economy","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"Nathan J"},{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"Andrés M"},{"family":"Blythe","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Silver","given":"Jennifer J"},{"family":"Singh","given":"Gerald"},{"family":"Andrews","given":"Nathan"},{"family":"Calò","given":"Antonio"},{"family":"Christie","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Di Franco","given":"Antonio"},{"family":"Finkbeiner","given":"Elena M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Access and rights to resources, extent of local control and coordination with customary land tenure arrangements are key issues.
      
Box 4.18 Snow leopard in the Himalayas – ecotourism

With a habitat that ranges throughout the Himalayas and Central Asia, the snow leopard (panthera uncia) has a complex relationship to humans. A history of conflict with farmers, as well as decreasing habitat size and climate change increasingly threaten the already-low population of this large felid. With an estimated population between 4,000 and 7,000 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9YvMcKnC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Riordan, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Riordan, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":594,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FD8764T2"],"itemData":{"id":594,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecography","ISSN":"0906-7590","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Ecography","note":"number: 5\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"419-426","title":"Predicting global population connectivity and targeting conservation action for snow leopard across its range","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Riordan","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Cushman","given":"Samuel A"},{"family":"Mallon","given":"David"},{"family":"Shi","given":"Kun"},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Joelene"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} spread across 12 countries, diminishing wild prey has pushed leopards towards more steadily attacking livestock. Consequently, the snow leopard interacts and is mostly threatened by retaliatory herders ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VL9YDxKJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rashid, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Rashid, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33456,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9PB56EAS"],"itemData":{"id":33456,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Simple Summary\\nTrophy hunting and mass tourism were introduced to Khunjerab National Park, northern Pakistan to generate income for the community and help conserve and sustain the ecosystem in the region. These initiatives have provided economic benefits, but only at the cost of other environmental problems, as both trophy hunting and mass tourism have resulted in various ecological issues. Trophy hunting has not been based on scientific population data and has thus not helped increase numbers of wild ungulates or wild carnivores. Although mass tourism has increased enormously in this region, it has damaged the ecosystem through pollution generation and negatively impacted wildlife. We suggest that trophy hunting should be stopped, and mass tourism should be shifted to ecotourism as a sustainable solution to help improve the ecosystem, while generating income for the local community. Further studies are required to investigate ecotourism as a potential mitigation measure for the conservation issues in this region.\\n\\nAbstract\\nTrophy hunting and mass tourism are the two major interventions designed to provide various socioeconomic and ecological benefits at the local and regional levels. However, these interventions have raised some serious concerns that need to be addressed. This study was conducted in Khunjerab National Park (KNP) with an aim to analyze comparatively the socioeconomic and ecological impacts of trophy hunting and mass tourism over the last three decades within the context of sustainability. Focus Group Discussions (FGDs) with key stakeholders and household interviews were conducted to collect data on trophy hunting and mass tourism, and on local attitudes towards these two interventions in and around KNP. The results revealed that 170 Ibex (Capra sibirica) and 12 Blue sheep (Pseudois nayaur) were hunted in the study area over the past three decades, and trophy hunting was not based on a sustainable harvest level. Trophy hunting on average generated USD 16,272 annual revenue, which was invested in community development. However, trophy hunting has greatly changed the attitudes of local residents towards wildlife: a positive attitude towards the wild ungulates and strongly negative attitude towards wild carnivores. In addition, trophy hunting has reduced the availability of ungulate prey species for Snow leopards (Panthera uncia), and consequently, Snow leopards have increased their predation on domestic livestock. This has, in turn, increased human–snow leopard conflict, as negative attitudes towards carnivores result in retaliatory killing of Snow leopards. Furthermore, according to official record data, the number of tourists to KNP has increased tremendously by 10,437.8%, from 1382 in 1999 to 145,633 in 2018. Mass tourism on average generated USD 33,904 annually and provided opportunities for locals to earn high incomes, but it caused damages to the environment and ecosystem in KNP through pollution generation and negative impacts on wildlife. Considering the limited benefits and significant problems created by trophy hunting and mass tourism, we suggest trophy hunting should be stopped and mass tourism should be shifted to ecotourism in and around KNP. Ecotourism could mitigate human–Snow leopard conflicts and help conserve the fragile ecosystem, while generating enough revenue incentives for the community to protect biodiversity and compensate for livestock depredation losses to Snow leopards. Our results may have implications for management of trophy hunting and mass tourism in other similar regions that deserve further investigation.","container-title":"Animals : an Open Access Journal from MDPI","DOI":"10.3390/ani10040597","ISSN":"2076-2615","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Animals (Basel)","note":"PMID: 32244688\\nPMCID: PMC7222849","page":"597","source":"PubMed Central","title":"Issues and Opportunities Associated with Trophy Hunting and Tourism in Khunjerab National Park, Northern Pakistan","URL":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7222849/","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Rashid","given":"Wajid"},{"family":"Shi","given":"Jianbin"},{"family":"Rahim","given":"Inam","dropping-particle":"ur"},{"family":"Dong","given":"Shikui"},{"family":"Sultan","given":"Hameeda"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",4,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Contested living in arid highlands with access to grazing grounds continues to be one of the largest challenges for the conservation of snow leopards ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7poMtzix","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Jackson & Wangchuk, 2001; Vannelli, 2019)","plainCitation":"(R. Jackson & Wangchuk, 2001; Vannelli, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":600,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L8IE29B4"],"itemData":{"id":600,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Endangered Species Update","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Endangered Species Update","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: ??????? ???? ?? ?????? ??????? ?????? ?? ??? ??????????? ?? ??????? ???????? …","page":"138-141","title":"Linking snow leopard conservation and people-wildlife conflict resolution: grassroots measures to protect the endangered snow leopard from herder retribution","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Jackson","given":"Rodney"},{"family":"Wangchuk","given":"Rinchen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":591,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FZ88DY6Z"],"itemData":{"id":591,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","ISSN":"1087-1209","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"180-193","title":"Community participation in ecotourism and its effect on local perceptions of snow leopard (Panthera uncia) conservation","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Vannelli","given":"Kate"},{"family":"Hampton","given":"Mark P"},{"family":"Namgail","given":"Tsewang"},{"family":"Black","given":"Simon A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Throughout the “Roof of the World” region, efforts to maintain population levels have used community-based conservation, and have focused on the education of locals, often in partnership through community-based ecotourism ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mcC2dY0x","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. H. Hanson, 2019; Millican, 2016; Vannelli, 2019)","plainCitation":"(J. H. Hanson, 2019; Millican, 2016; Vannelli, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":588,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T2ULFXRU"],"itemData":{"id":588,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PloS one","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"PloS one","note":"number: 10\\npublisher: Public Library of Science San Francisco, CA USA","page":"e0223565","title":"What factors best explain attitudes to snow leopards in the Nepal Himalayas?","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Hanson","given":"Jonathan H"},{"family":"Schutgens","given":"Maurice"},{"family":"Leader-Williams","given":"Nigel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":592,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V84RC4RP"],"itemData":{"id":592,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"WIT Transactions on Ecology and the Environment","ISSN":"178466085X","journalAbbreviation":"WIT Transactions on Ecology and the Environment","note":"publisher: WIT Press","page":"167-175","title":"Ghosts of the mountains: the role of wildlife conservation in sustainable tourism–a case study of snow leopard conservation and sustainable tourism in Mongolia","volume":"201","author":[{"family":"Millican","given":"A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":591,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FZ88DY6Z"],"itemData":{"id":591,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","ISSN":"1087-1209","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"180-193","title":"Community participation in ecotourism and its effect on local perceptions of snow leopard (Panthera uncia) conservation","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Vannelli","given":"Kate"},{"family":"Hampton","given":"Mark P"},{"family":"Namgail","given":"Tsewang"},{"family":"Black","given":"Simon A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For the impact on leopards to change, a cultural shift is needed in which snow leopards are not seen as another source of conflict, but as an animal to protect who plays a role in the sustained socio-economic presence of communities. Community-based ecotourism with regards to the snow leopard has the opportunity to address various issues. In creating a niche for tourists, community-based ecotourism provides the opportunity to increase revenues into communities and their conservation efforts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YYkT4iNb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. H. Hanson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(J. H. Hanson, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":588,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T2ULFXRU"],"itemData":{"id":588,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PloS one","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"PloS one","note":"number: 10\\npublisher: Public Library of Science San Francisco, CA USA","page":"e0223565","title":"What factors best explain attitudes to snow leopards in the Nepal Himalayas?","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Hanson","given":"Jonathan H"},{"family":"Schutgens","given":"Maurice"},{"family":"Leader-Williams","given":"Nigel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As a result, it creates a more positive image of leopards; rendered sustainable by the heightened education surrounding the carnivore. Even though sightings of leopards are rare, programs package outings with activities such as trekking, trophy hunting of ungulates, and homestays ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uhQeXAQz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. H. Hanson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(J. H. Hanson, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":588,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T2ULFXRU"],"itemData":{"id":588,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PloS one","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"PloS one","note":"number: 10\\npublisher: Public Library of Science San Francisco, CA USA","page":"e0223565","title":"What factors best explain attitudes to snow leopards in the Nepal Himalayas?","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Hanson","given":"Jonathan H"},{"family":"Schutgens","given":"Maurice"},{"family":"Leader-Williams","given":"Nigel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition to assisting in community initiatives, along with funding preservation, the income from nature-based tourism has also been used to compensate for lost livestock ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ec2H0Zsg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rashid, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Rashid, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33456,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9PB56EAS"],"itemData":{"id":33456,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Simple Summary\\nTrophy hunting and mass tourism were introduced to Khunjerab National Park, northern Pakistan to generate income for the community and help conserve and sustain the ecosystem in the region. These initiatives have provided economic benefits, but only at the cost of other environmental problems, as both trophy hunting and mass tourism have resulted in various ecological issues. Trophy hunting has not been based on scientific population data and has thus not helped increase numbers of wild ungulates or wild carnivores. Although mass tourism has increased enormously in this region, it has damaged the ecosystem through pollution generation and negatively impacted wildlife. We suggest that trophy hunting should be stopped, and mass tourism should be shifted to ecotourism as a sustainable solution to help improve the ecosystem, while generating income for the local community. Further studies are required to investigate ecotourism as a potential mitigation measure for the conservation issues in this region.\\n\\nAbstract\\nTrophy hunting and mass tourism are the two major interventions designed to provide various socioeconomic and ecological benefits at the local and regional levels. However, these interventions have raised some serious concerns that need to be addressed. This study was conducted in Khunjerab National Park (KNP) with an aim to analyze comparatively the socioeconomic and ecological impacts of trophy hunting and mass tourism over the last three decades within the context of sustainability. Focus Group Discussions (FGDs) with key stakeholders and household interviews were conducted to collect data on trophy hunting and mass tourism, and on local attitudes towards these two interventions in and around KNP. The results revealed that 170 Ibex (Capra sibirica) and 12 Blue sheep (Pseudois nayaur) were hunted in the study area over the past three decades, and trophy hunting was not based on a sustainable harvest level. Trophy hunting on average generated USD 16,272 annual revenue, which was invested in community development. However, trophy hunting has greatly changed the attitudes of local residents towards wildlife: a positive attitude towards the wild ungulates and strongly negative attitude towards wild carnivores. In addition, trophy hunting has reduced the availability of ungulate prey species for Snow leopards (Panthera uncia), and consequently, Snow leopards have increased their predation on domestic livestock. This has, in turn, increased human–snow leopard conflict, as negative attitudes towards carnivores result in retaliatory killing of Snow leopards. Furthermore, according to official record data, the number of tourists to KNP has increased tremendously by 10,437.8%, from 1382 in 1999 to 145,633 in 2018. Mass tourism on average generated USD 33,904 annually and provided opportunities for locals to earn high incomes, but it caused damages to the environment and ecosystem in KNP through pollution generation and negative impacts on wildlife. Considering the limited benefits and significant problems created by trophy hunting and mass tourism, we suggest trophy hunting should be stopped and mass tourism should be shifted to ecotourism in and around KNP. Ecotourism could mitigate human–Snow leopard conflicts and help conserve the fragile ecosystem, while generating enough revenue incentives for the community to protect biodiversity and compensate for livestock depredation losses to Snow leopards. Our results may have implications for management of trophy hunting and mass tourism in other similar regions that deserve further investigation.","container-title":"Animals : an Open Access Journal from MDPI","DOI":"10.3390/ani10040597","ISSN":"2076-2615","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Animals (Basel)","note":"PMID: 32244688\\nPMCID: PMC7222849","page":"597","source":"PubMed Central","title":"Issues and Opportunities Associated with Trophy Hunting and Tourism in Khunjerab National Park, Northern Pakistan","URL":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7222849/","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Rashid","given":"Wajid"},{"family":"Shi","given":"Jianbin"},{"family":"Rahim","given":"Inam","dropping-particle":"ur"},{"family":"Dong","given":"Shikui"},{"family":"Sultan","given":"Hameeda"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",4,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These initiatives have been taken up in Afghanistan ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qdcvq4fV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Simms, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Simms, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":587,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q5W6EBDM"],"itemData":{"id":587,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Environmental Studies","ISSN":"0020-7233","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Environmental Studies","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"299-312","title":"Saving threatened species in Afghanistan: snow leopards in the Wakhan Corridor","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Simms","given":"Anthony"},{"family":"Moheb","given":"Zalmai"},{"literal":"Salahudin"},{"family":"Ali","given":"Hussain"},{"family":"Ali","given":"Inayat"},{"family":"Wood","given":"Timothy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, India (Kala & Makhuri, 2011;), Jammu and Kashmir ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UCUH6TRO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Jackson & Wangchuk, 2001)","plainCitation":"(R. Jackson & Wangchuk, 2001)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":600,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L8IE29B4"],"itemData":{"id":600,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Endangered Species Update","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Endangered Species Update","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: ??????? ???? ?? ?????? ??????? ?????? ?? ??? ??????????? ?? ??????? ???????? …","page":"138-141","title":"Linking snow leopard conservation and people-wildlife conflict resolution: grassroots measures to protect the endangered snow leopard from herder retribution","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Jackson","given":"Rodney"},{"family":"Wangchuk","given":"Rinchen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Nepal ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9r0WZDUP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. H. Hanson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(J. H. Hanson, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":588,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T2ULFXRU"],"itemData":{"id":588,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PloS one","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"PloS one","note":"number: 10\\npublisher: Public Library of Science San Francisco, CA USA","page":"e0223565","title":"What factors best explain attitudes to snow leopards in the Nepal Himalayas?","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Hanson","given":"Jonathan H"},{"family":"Schutgens","given":"Maurice"},{"family":"Leader-Williams","given":"Nigel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and Mongolia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"N8xd7SUd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Millican, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Millican, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":592,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V84RC4RP"],"itemData":{"id":592,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"WIT Transactions on Ecology and the Environment","ISSN":"178466085X","journalAbbreviation":"WIT Transactions on Ecology and the Environment","note":"publisher: WIT Press","page":"167-175","title":"Ghosts of the mountains: the role of wildlife conservation in sustainable tourism–a case study of snow leopard conservation and sustainable tourism in Mongolia","volume":"201","author":[{"family":"Millican","given":"A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, among others. However, research is most abundant in India and Nepal, with Nepal being the first country to implement community-based conservation of snow leopards through the Annapurna Conservation Area Project ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XhOGygqf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. M. Jackson & Lama, 2016)","plainCitation":"(R. M. Jackson & Lama, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":593,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8Q3RUGPS"],"itemData":{"id":593,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Snow leopards","page":"139-149","publisher":"Elsevier","title":"The role of mountain communities in snow leopard conservation","author":[{"family":"Jackson","given":"Rodney M"},{"family":"Lama","given":"Wendy Brewer"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nature-based tourism has been changing the relationship to leopards, decreasing human-wild species conflicts, but it is neither sufficient nor sustainable as a single approach. Evidently, this strategy should be combined with better anti-predation technologies and training ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NkaGa69G","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Simms, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Simms, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":587,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q5W6EBDM"],"itemData":{"id":587,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Environmental Studies","ISSN":"0020-7233","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Environmental Studies","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"299-312","title":"Saving threatened species in Afghanistan: snow leopards in the Wakhan Corridor","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Simms","given":"Anthony"},{"family":"Moheb","given":"Zalmai"},{"literal":"Salahudin"},{"family":"Ali","given":"Hussain"},{"family":"Ali","given":"Inayat"},{"family":"Wood","given":"Timothy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, tourism that does not rely on wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SE4MJG86","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. M. Jackson & Lama, 2016b; R. Jackson & Wangchuk, 2001; Mishra, 2003)","plainCitation":"(R. M. Jackson & Lama, 2016b; R. Jackson & Wangchuk, 2001; Mishra, 2003)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":599,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y5A2DNQ4"],"itemData":{"id":599,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Snow leopards","page":"139-149","publisher":"Elsevier","title":"The role of mountain communities in snow leopard conservation","author":[{"family":"Jackson","given":"Rodney M"},{"family":"Lama","given":"Wendy Brewer"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":600,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L8IE29B4"],"itemData":{"id":600,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Endangered Species Update","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Endangered Species Update","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: ??????? ???? ?? ?????? ??????? ?????? ?? ??? ??????????? ?? ??????? ???????? …","page":"138-141","title":"Linking snow leopard conservation and people-wildlife conflict resolution: grassroots measures to protect the endangered snow leopard from herder retribution","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Jackson","given":"Rodney"},{"family":"Wangchuk","given":"Rinchen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":33418,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SBTNYAEX"],"itemData":{"id":33418,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","ISSN":"0888-8892","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","note":"number: 6\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"1512-1520","title":"The role of incentive programs in conserving the snow leopard","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Mishra","given":"Charudutt"},{"family":"Allen","given":"Priscilla"},{"family":"McCarthy","given":"TOM"},{"family":"Madhusudan","given":"MD"},{"family":"Bayarjargal","given":"Agvaantserengiin"},{"family":"Prins","given":"Herbert HT"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and compensation unrelated to tourism ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gXVUCdOy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hussain, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Hussain, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":598,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XRYU9I6D"],"itemData":{"id":598,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mountain research and development","ISSN":"0276-4741","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Mountain research and development","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: BioOne","page":"226-231","title":"Protecting the snow leopard and enhancing farmers' livelihoods","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Hussain","given":"Shafqat"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some authors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"h6yJt6E2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mishra, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Mishra, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33418,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SBTNYAEX"],"itemData":{"id":33418,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","ISSN":"0888-8892","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","note":"number: 6\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"1512-1520","title":"The role of incentive programs in conserving the snow leopard","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Mishra","given":"Charudutt"},{"family":"Allen","given":"Priscilla"},{"family":"McCarthy","given":"TOM"},{"family":"Madhusudan","given":"MD"},{"family":"Bayarjargal","given":"Agvaantserengiin"},{"family":"Prins","given":"Herbert HT"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} have addressed the possibility for the relocation of already-small settlements outside of preserve areas, with the need for assistance in establishing sustainable livelihoods outside of herding. Until now, community-based ecotourism has been a relatively successful and sustainable effort in snow leopard preservation, but the ecological impacts of extraction may soon become too large to match in a timely manner. A proposed response to the multinational challenges that snow leopard conservation faces is for initiatives to attain a transboundary scale ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Hv7gtclK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Maheshwari, 2020; Riordan, 2016; T. Rosen & Zahler, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Maheshwari, 2020; Riordan, 2016; T. Rosen & Zahler, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33416,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3JFRMTJ4"],"itemData":{"id":33416,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"6483","journalAbbreviation":"Science","note":"number: 6483\\npublisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science","page":"1203-1203","title":"Ease conflict in Asia with snow leopard peace parks","volume":"367","author":[{"family":"Maheshwari","given":"Aishwarya"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":594,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FD8764T2"],"itemData":{"id":594,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecography","ISSN":"0906-7590","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Ecography","note":"number: 5\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"419-426","title":"Predicting global population connectivity and targeting conservation action for snow leopard across its range","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Riordan","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Cushman","given":"Samuel A"},{"family":"Mallon","given":"David"},{"family":"Shi","given":"Kun"},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Joelene"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33417,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/37QLPLJ9"],"itemData":{"id":33417,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Snow leopards","page":"267-276","publisher":"Elsevier","title":"Transboundary initiatives and snow leopard conservation","author":[{"family":"Rosen","given":"Tatjana"},{"family":"Zahler","given":"Peter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Not only does the leopard’s habitat span a dozen nations, it does so along various borders. Thus, focusing on a country-based response may lead to several oversights or pitfalls in strategy. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vcQVyRRb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Maheshwari, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Maheshwari, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33416,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3JFRMTJ4"],"itemData":{"id":33416,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"6483","journalAbbreviation":"Science","note":"number: 6483\\npublisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science","page":"1203-1203","title":"Ease conflict in Asia with snow leopard peace parks","volume":"367","author":[{"family":"Maheshwari","given":"Aishwarya"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} highlights the possibility for joint governance efforts in preservation, with the possibility of a rippling benefit to the fragile ecosystems inhabited by leopards. In speaking of the political and social drivers of sustainability, cross-boundary models of co-governance are a lever that could bolster conservation while exemplifying a globalized model of community-based conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"B9vXvc9G","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. M. Jackson & Lama, 2016b; T. Rosen & Zahler, 2016; Zahler & Paley, 2016)","plainCitation":"(R. M. Jackson & Lama, 2016b; T. Rosen & Zahler, 2016; Zahler & Paley, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":599,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y5A2DNQ4"],"itemData":{"id":599,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Snow leopards","page":"139-149","publisher":"Elsevier","title":"The role of mountain communities in snow leopard conservation","author":[{"family":"Jackson","given":"Rodney M"},{"family":"Lama","given":"Wendy Brewer"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33417,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/37QLPLJ9"],"itemData":{"id":33417,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Snow leopards","page":"267-276","publisher":"Elsevier","title":"Transboundary initiatives and snow leopard conservation","author":[{"family":"Rosen","given":"Tatjana"},{"family":"Zahler","given":"Peter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33415,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6H224TM2"],"itemData":{"id":33415,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Snow Leopards","page":"151-162","publisher":"Elsevier","title":"Building Community Governance Structures and Institutions for Snow Leopard Conservation","author":[{"family":"Zahler","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Paley","given":"Richard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In doing so, communities across the Roof of the World may become more interconnected and redefine their relationship to large carnivores without sacrificing their local identities.


      As implied above, there is a large body of evidence showing that tenure rights which incentivize sustainable and equitable use of natural resources should be not only secure, but also involve local user communities. From ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"b2n6v5m4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. M. Larson & Springer, 2016)","plainCitation":"(A. M. Larson & Springer, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33430,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SXHA7GW7"],"itemData":{"id":33430,"type":"chapter","container-title":"NRGF Conceptual Paper","event-place":"Gland, Switzerland","language":"en","publisher":"IUCN, CEESP and CIFOR","publisher-place":"Gland, Switzerland","title":"Recognition and Respect for Tenure Rights","author":[{"family":"Larson","given":"Anne M."},{"family":"Springer","given":"Jenny"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, a “systematic review of research on the environmental impacts of different property regimes in forests, fisheries and rangelands found that avoiding open access situations in fisheries and forests and transferring user rights to communities usually led to positive environmental impacts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dNuDtZ0A","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ojanen, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Ojanen, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33414,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZJN7W4JW"],"itemData":{"id":33414,"type":"paper-conference","event-title":"XIV World Forestry Congress","page":"7-11","title":"Linking Forest Tenure Rights To Environmental Impacts In Forests, Fisheries, And Rangelands","author":[{"family":"Ojanen","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Mshale","given":"Baruani"},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Wen"},{"family":"Nieto","given":"Sue Helen"},{"family":"Durey","given":"Louis"},{"family":"Miller","given":"Daniel C"},{"family":"Mwangi","given":"Esther"},{"family":"Petrokofky","given":"Gillian"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.” The benefits of empowering local communities and recognizing local knowledge in management are not limited to local livelihoods and sustainability of resource sectors but have further positive impacts on other ecosystem services (for example, better-managed forests with both improved production and carbon storage) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nppH5e4m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chhatre & Agrawal, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Chhatre & Agrawal, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33468,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AJSUQD6G"],"itemData":{"id":33468,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.0905308106","ISSN":"0027-8424","issue":"42","page":"17667–17670","title":"Trade-offs and synergies between carbon storage and livelihood benefits from forest commons","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/doi/10.1073/pnas.0905308106","volume":"106","author":[{"family":"Chhatre","given":"A."},{"family":"Agrawal","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.   
      Devolving tenure from centralized management to local communities can be difficult to achieve, despite evidence of the benefits. For forests, centralized management that disregards strong traditional management systems and cultural relationships with forests has been shown to lead to poor outcomes, failing to support conservation or sustainable resource use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HGDEUpvH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. M. Larson & Dahal, 2012; J. Ribot & Larson, 2012; Sunderlin, 2011)","plainCitation":"(A. M. Larson & Dahal, 2012; J. Ribot & Larson, 2012; Sunderlin, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33424,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3GG7XYAF"],"itemData":{"id":33424,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation and society","ISSN":"0972-4923","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation and society","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: JSTOR","page":"77-90","title":"Introduction: forest tenure reform: new resource rights for forest-based communities?","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Larson","given":"Anne M"},{"family":"Dahal","given":"Ganga Ram"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":33413,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UNMME45J"],"itemData":{"id":33413,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","ISSN":"1875-0281","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of the Commons","note":"number: 2","title":"Reducing REDD risks: affirmative policy on an uneven playing field","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Ribot","given":"Jesse"},{"family":"Larson","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":597,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IT4WFDCL"],"itemData":{"id":597,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest and People: Property, Governance, and Human Rights; Sikor, T., Stahl, J., Eds","journalAbbreviation":"Forest and People: Property, Governance, and Human Rights; Sikor, T., Stahl, J., Eds","page":"19-32","title":"The global forest tenure transition: background, substance, and prospects","author":[{"family":"Sunderlin","given":"William D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Public trust institutions such as those in the United States of America are among the emerging shifts in land tenure that create incentives for conservation. They involve the spending both public and private funds including donations to ensure lands are set aside for the purposes of conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VD6YykV3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hodge & Adams, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Hodge & Adams, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33434,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EHNXH259"],"itemData":{"id":33434,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geoforum","DOI":"10.1016/j.geoforum.2011.11.007","ISSN":"00167185","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Geoforum","language":"en","page":"472-482","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Neoliberalisation, rural land trusts and institutional blending","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0016718511002090","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Hodge","given":"Ian D."},{"family":"Adams","given":"William M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While there is muc literature on the how-to of creating land trust, evidence about their impact on conservation outcomes as well as sustainable use of wild species is limited ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"l8IK1FAh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Merenlender, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Merenlender, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33435,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6V3L4IG"],"itemData":{"id":33435,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2004.00401.x","ISSN":"08888892, 15231739","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"65-76","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Land Trusts and Conservation Easements: Who Is Conserving What for Whom?","title-short":"Land Trusts and Conservation Easements","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2004.00401.x","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Merenlender","given":"A. M."},{"family":"Huntsinger","given":"L."},{"family":"Guthey","given":"G."},{"family":"Fairfax","given":"S. K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      In fisheries, centralized management has been shown to be difficult and unresponsive to local conditions, resulting in poor implementation of management measures and a subsequent large amount of illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing activity in almost every jurisdiction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bVz36zZ6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zeller & Pauly, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Zeller & Pauly, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33467,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DX8JIP2N"],"itemData":{"id":33467,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            We present a view on global marine fisheries that emphasizes mitigating the conflict between sustainability and the scale of industrial exploitation driven by the demand of continuous economic growth. We then summarize the current state of global fisheries. Finally, we advocate strongly for scaling back industrial fisheries, most of which are non-sustainable. This can be achieved through eliminating the harmful, capacity-enhancing subsidies that prop up industrial fisheries to continue operating despite declining fish stocks. Instead, we propose to support well-managed, locally owned and operated small-scale fisheries, which generally contribute more to local employment and food security. We stress that contrary to deep-seated opinion in the fishing industry and among politicians, reducing overfishing by eliminating overcapacity in fishing fleets will actually lead to greater, not reduced catches. This would address part of the increased global seafood demand over the coming decades, which is driven by population and wealth growth. This seems counterintuitive, but is supported by fisheries science, data and experiences. Thankfully, we are beginning to see that some of these changes are being pursued by a growing number of countries and international institutions.","container-title":"Global Sustainability","DOI":"10.1017/sus.2019.8","ISSN":"2059-4798","journalAbbreviation":"Glob. Sustain.","language":"en","page":"e11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Viewpoint: Back to the future for fisheries, where will we choose to go?","title-short":"Viewpoint","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S2059479819000085/type/journal_article","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Zeller","given":"Dirk"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Importantly, incomplete or passive decentralization, whereby centralized management is formally phased out but without the real transfer of meaningful authority to subnational governments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bIrpmmen","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Ribot, 2006)","plainCitation":"(J. Ribot, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33431,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FWRQ9BAV"],"itemData":{"id":33431,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2006.01.004.","language":"it","page":"115–119","title":"Choose democracy: Environmentalists’ socio-political responsibility","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Ribot","given":"Jesse"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and/or without legal frameworks and institutional support for local tenure rights, can also create power imbalances and vacuums with negative ecological and social impacts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WOb6oTNp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}ndez-Medina, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Méndez-Medina, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33421,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JIII3T9V"],"itemData":{"id":33421,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","ISSN":"0308-597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"103939","title":"Achieving coordination of decentralized fisheries governance through collaborative arrangements: A case study of the Sian Ka'an Biosphere Reserve in Mexico","volume":"117","author":[{"family":"Méndez-Medina","given":"Crisol"},{"family":"Schmook","given":"Birgit"},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Fulton","given":"Stuart"},{"family":"Espinoza-Tenorio","given":"Alejandro"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the case of forest and wild plant species, when commercial pressures increase significantly, local and customary systems of governance are often overwhelmed and require support from statutory systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"r2tFzO4l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wynberg & Laird, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Wynberg & Laird, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33457,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KVL8UHGG"],"itemData":{"id":33457,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Non-timber forest products (NTFPs) are often seen as tools to promote rural development and biodiversity conservation but little attention has been given to the different policy approaches adopted for their governance, nor to the role played by customary law. Through the lens of one of the most revered and economically important trees in Africa, marula (Sclerocarya birrea), NTFP governance is explored in case studies across South Africa and Namibia. Results are presented from a study that examined the interface between statutory and customary rules and regulations governing marula conservation and use. The major finding is that 'less' is often 'more' when it comes to government regulation of marula, a result that resonates with other NTFP studies from around the world that indicate the need for state-led interventions to be purposely crafted to reflect local circumstances and needs. Such interventions are also most effective when government and traditional authorities cooperate, authorities have legitimacy and sufficient capacity, and there is acceptance of the rules by user groups. It is suggested that state intervention may be least useful where traditional governance is strong, and with the exception of areas and cases in which customary law and traditional authority fall short and commercial pressures on species are significant, governments might do best by leaving well enough alone. Les produits forestiers autres que le bois (NTFPs) sont souvent perc, us comme des outils pour promouvoir le développement rural et la conservation de la biodiversité, mais peu d'attention a ete accordée aux differentes approches de politique adoptées pour leur gestion, et au role joué par la loi coutumiére. En se concentrant sur l'un de arbres les plus révérés et économiquement importants en Afrique, le marula (Sclerocarya birrea), la gestion des NTFPs est explorée en étude-cas du sud de l'Afrique à la Namibie. Les résultats d'une étude qui examine les connections entre les les régies et régulations statutaires et coutumiéres qui gerent la conservation et l' utilisation du marula. La découverte la plus importante est que \\"moins\\" devient souvent \\"plus\\" quand il s'agit de la régulation gouvernementale du marula. Ce résultat résonne avec d'autres études sur les NTFPs autour du monde, qui indiquent la nécessité de former les interventions conduites par l'état en s'efforçant de refléter les besoins et circonstances locaux. La suggestion est que l' intervention de l'etat est peut-être la moins utile dans les endroits ou la gestion traditionnelle est forte, et, les zones et cas où la loi et l'autorite traditionnelle échouent et où la pression commerciale sur les especes est importante mises à part; que les gouvernements feraient peut-être mieux de s'abstenir d'intervenir dans le secteur. Los productos forestales no maderables (PFNMs) son considerados a menudo como instrumentos para la promoción del desarollo rural y de la conservación de la biodiversidad, pero se ha prestado poca atencion a las diferentes politicas adoptadas para su manejo, ni al papel desempeñado por la ley consuetudinaria. Se estudia el caso de uno los árboles mas venerados y económicamente importantes de África, la marula (Sclerocarya birrea), basándose en estudios procedentes de Sudáfrica y Namibia para analizar el manejo de PFNMs. Se presentan los resultados de un estudio que examinó la interrelatión entre normas y reglamentos legales y consuetudinarios relacionados con la conservatión y uso de la marula. El resultado más interesante del estudio fue el descubrimiento de que a menudo \\"menos\\" resulta ser \\"más\\" cuando se trata de la regulatión gubernamental de la marula, hecho que concuerda con otros estudios sobre PFNMs de otras partes del mundo, que demuestran la importancia de adaptar especificamente las intervenciones estatales para reflejar las circunstancias y necesidades locales. Se sugiere que la interventión estatal puede resultar poco útil donde el manejo tradicional sigue en vigor, y que convendría a los gobiernos evitar inmiscuirse, salvo en casos en que la ley consuetudinaria y las autoridades tradicionales se queden cortas y exista una fuerte presión comercial sobre las especies.","container-title":"The International Forestry Review","ISSN":"14655489, 20537778","issue":"1","page":"475–490","title":"Less is often more: governance of a non-timber forest product, marula (Sclerocarya birrea subsp. caffra) in southern Africa","URL":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/43740293","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Wynberg","given":"R."},{"family":"Laird","given":"S.A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      
4.2.2.6.1. Gendered land, resource, and trade rights 
Gender differences and inequalities are common in resource access and security, and in the commercialization of wild products. There is limited information available on gender in trade, and a strong bias in the literature towards African countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WaEAbaAF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Haverhals, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Haverhals, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33448,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P6T9VT4I"],"itemData":{"id":33448,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Gender and Forests: Climate Change, Tenure, Value Chains, and Emerging Issues","event-place":"London","language":"en","page":"221–242","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London","title":"Gender and forest, tree and agroforestry value chains - Evidence from literature","author":[{"family":"Haverhals","given":"Merel"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"MarlèneElias","given":"Bimbika Sijapati Basnett"},{"family":"Petersen","given":"Sjoerd"}],"editor":[{"family":"Colfer","given":"C.J.P."},{"family":"B","given":"S.Basnett"},{"family":"Elias","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, though this is changing (see for example, Women’s Studies International Forum issue 65, 2017; or https://gender.cgiar.org/gender-in-value-chains/). Gender differences in participation in trade are mainly the effect of social-cultural factors, including gendered resource access rights. In addition, due to the nature of value chain activities, cultural norms and overlapping customary and formal regulatory arrangements often position men in more favorable positions than women in the value chains of wild sourced products. Although interventions have largely focused on enhancing women’s participation and benefits, they rarely consider the relationships between men and women. Hence, raising awareness of gender biases, relations, and potential trade-offs among those involved in value chains and those supporting inclusive and sustainable trade should accompany technological innovations, and should occur across all stages of the value chain ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EaMWjHpN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V Ingram 2016)","plainCitation":"(V Ingram 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33463,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZKYGLITC"],"itemData":{"id":33463,"type":"chapter","call-number":"SD247 .G46 2016","collection-title":"The earthscan forest library","container-title":"Gender and forests: climate change, tenure, value chains and emerging issues","event-place":"London ; New York","ISBN":"978-1-138-95503-5","publisher":"Routledge, Taylor & Francis Group","publisher-place":"London ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Gender and Forest, Tree and Agroforestry Value Chains: Evidence from Literature","editor":[{"family":"Colfer","given":"Carol J. Pierce"},{"family":"Sijapati Basnett","given":"Bimbika"},{"family":"Elias","given":"Marlene"}],"author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"V"},{"family":"Haverhals","given":"M"},{"family":"Petersen","given":"S"},{"family":"Elias","given":"M"},{"family":"Sijapati Basnett","given":"B"},{"family":"Sola","given":"P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Women make up a substantial amount of labor participation in wild seafood resources (e.g., up to 50% in seafood production chains) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5UE6kmJN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harper, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Harper, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33429,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XYLDS9AL"],"itemData":{"id":33429,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"91–106","title":"Contributions by women to fisheries economies: insights from five maritime countries","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Harper","given":"S."},{"family":"Grubb","given":"C."},{"family":"Stiles","given":"M."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and often fill key roles in value chains including sales, marketing, and business administration. The literature on wild forest and tree product resources generally provides little information on male/female participation in trade. Where quantified, the male to female participation ratio ranged widely from zero to 100%. Information on the rates of participation was specifically lacking for processor (all locations) and trader (in Latin America and Asia) stages in chains. Female dominance is recorded at the harvester, processor, and trader stages, however, there were strong differences depending on geographic region and product ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VT4IOkkC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Haverhals, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Haverhals, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33448,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P6T9VT4I"],"itemData":{"id":33448,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Gender and Forests: Climate Change, Tenure, Value Chains, and Emerging Issues","event-place":"London","language":"en","page":"221–242","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London","title":"Gender and forest, tree and agroforestry value chains - Evidence from literature","author":[{"family":"Haverhals","given":"Merel"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"MarlèneElias","given":"Bimbika Sijapati Basnett"},{"family":"Petersen","given":"Sjoerd"}],"editor":[{"family":"Colfer","given":"C.J.P."},{"family":"B","given":"S.Basnett"},{"family":"Elias","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At the harvester stage, female dominance only holds in Africa. An overall trend is that women are mostly confined to small-scale retail trade, and that men run larger businesses. Generally, men and women gain different levels of revenues and profits from wild product commercializing and spend their related incomes differently, with strong differences occurring across the global. Generally, but not always, men sell a higher proportion of wild forest and tree products than women ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zbeQnm9p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Haverhals, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Haverhals, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33448,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P6T9VT4I"],"itemData":{"id":33448,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Gender and Forests: Climate Change, Tenure, Value Chains, and Emerging Issues","event-place":"London","language":"en","page":"221–242","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London","title":"Gender and forest, tree and agroforestry value chains - Evidence from literature","author":[{"family":"Haverhals","given":"Merel"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"MarlèneElias","given":"Bimbika Sijapati Basnett"},{"family":"Petersen","given":"Sjoerd"}],"editor":[{"family":"Colfer","given":"C.J.P."},{"family":"B","given":"S.Basnett"},{"family":"Elias","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
Box 4.19. Fishing and gender: women marginalization and empowerment 

Millions of women around the world, paid or unpaid, work in the fisheries sector, mainly involved in the tasks that come before and after fish landing. But they also play a significant role in the catch of the small-scale fishery, according to the techniques, and more generally the fishing system (target species, fishing grounds, fishing gear, value-chain, etc.). The contribution of women in the fishery has long been underestimated in the national and international statistics ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8VfareoV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Bank, 2012)","plainCitation":"(World Bank, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34150,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U3RKF2JS"],"itemData":{"id":34150,"type":"document","title":"The Hidden Harvest. The global contribution of capture fisheries.","URL":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277664581_World_Bank_2012_The_Hidden_Harvest_The_global_contribution_of_capture_fisheries","author":[{"family":"World Bank","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. From Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, women represent just 14 percent of the 59.5 million people engaged in the fisheries and aquaculture in 2018. However, their invisible role is more and more highlighted and conceptualized, notably through a feminist approach ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VodUzTQ1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Frangoudes, 2019; Frangoudes & Gerrard, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Frangoudes, 2019; Frangoudes & Gerrard, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32972,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8VWE5I77"],"itemData":{"id":32972,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Maritime Studies","DOI":"10.1007/s40152-018-0113-9","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"117–124","title":"(En)Gendering Change in Small-Scale Fisheries and Fishing Communities in a Globalized World","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Frangoudes","given":"K."},{"family":"Gerrard","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":33472,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NCDUXKHN"],"itemData":{"id":33472,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Maritime Studies","DOI":"10.1007/s40152-019-00159-w","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"241–248","title":"Situated transformations of women and gender relations in small-scale fisheries and communities in a globalized world","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Frangoudes","given":"K."},{"family":"Gerrard","given":"S."},{"family":"Kleiber","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In 2019, the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, with WorldFish and Duke University among others, launched a synthesis « Illuminating Hidden Harvests », for quantifying the contributions of small-scale fisheries to the three dimensions of sustainable development (social, economic and environmental), and better assessing the qualitative and quantitative contribution of the women in this sector ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YrWFem8e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      With a few exceptions - seawomen of Iceland ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7w7eBwhL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Willson, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Willson, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33440,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SC5QN2B6"],"itemData":{"id":33440,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Museum Tusculanum Press","title":"Seawomen of Iceland: survival on the edge","author":[{"family":"Willson","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, freediving to fish sea cucumber or sea urchins, such as Vezo women in Madagascar ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WJKzrWt2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Astuti, 1995)","plainCitation":"(Astuti, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33455,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/86XT2PT5"],"itemData":{"id":33455,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"American Ethnologist","issue":"3","page":"464-482","title":"\\"The Vezo are not a kind of people”: identity, difference, and “ethnicity” among a fishing people of western Madagascar","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/645967","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Astuti","given":"Rita"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or Mentawai women in Siberut ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rEuPTcMF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Burgos & Younger, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Burgos & Younger, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33451,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GJ5DRVZ6"],"itemData":{"id":33451,"type":"article-journal","container-title":". Journal of Ethnobiology","language":"pt","title":"Mollusks","volume":"39(2","author":[{"family":"Burgos","given":"A."},{"family":"Younger","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the sea trips, especially of more than 1 day, whether artisanal or industrial fishing, are carried out by men. Also, in Madagascar ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3zRUH8DH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barnes-Mauthe, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Barnes-Mauthe, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":524,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B794PTQ4"],"itemData":{"id":524,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2013.05.011","page":"175-185","title":"The total economic value of small-scale fisheries with a characterization of post-landing trends: An application in Madagascar with global relevance","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84880582478&doi=10.1016%2fj.fishres.2013.05.011&partnerID=40&md5=086f672e05aeac327b3ae0a2caf2921e","volume":"147","author":[{"family":"Barnes-Mauthe","given":"M."},{"family":"Oleson","given":"K.L.L."},{"family":"Zafindrasilivonona","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, fishers are predominately men (97% of fishers and 95% of fisher–gleaners), while gleaners were predominantly women (98% of all gleaners). Women generally only engage in fishing activities near the shores – lagunas, lakes, rivers or sea. They travel on foot or in non-motorized canoes. They fish with traps and holes or by hand or with the use of spears on reef flats, seagrass beds, mangrove mudflats; they target small species of fish (Tilapiae and Ethmalosis), shrimp, molluscs and crustaceans. On the other hand, for a long time, they have dominated the processing and marketing of catches. In particular, it is the fishermen's wives who have traditionally been entrusted with either all or part of the catch for the purpose of exploitation, and part of the catch is for family consumption and redistribution (ndawal in Wolof) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bnZAIqj1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Thiao, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Thiao, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33438,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XINLNH9K"],"itemData":{"id":33438,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Living Resources","DOI":"10.1051/alr/2018009","language":"en","title":"Need for adaptive solutions to food vulnerability induced by fish scarcity and unaffordability in Senegal","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Thiao","given":"D."},{"family":"Leport","given":"J."},{"family":"Ndiaye","given":"B."},{"family":"Mbaye","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Since the 1950s, with the development of sea fishing, particularly in tropical areas, in connection with multiple innovations - technical (motorization of pirogues, distribution of large nets, cold chain), economic (growth of urban markets), institutional (credit and fishing cooperatives) - women often lost this central role at the wharf and were marginalized. Globalization (long-distance maritime migrations, export-based policy, including removal of trade barriers and low-cost transport) further accentuates this process of marginalization ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Pr6Rhpep","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M.-C. Cormier-Salem, 2017b)","plainCitation":"(M.-C. Cormier-Salem, 2017b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":764,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/99RE3GY4"],"itemData":{"id":764,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"© 2017 by the author. Carbon policy is a fascinating topic in geography and political ecology, because carbon is a new exchangeable good, which links the local to the international arenas through a complex set of instruments, norms, and institutions. In this paper, after explaining my theoretical and conceptual framework rooted in Africanist geography and currents of political ecology, I analyze the responses of local actors (knowledge, practices, logic, and strategies) to environmental policies and consider the effects of new mechanisms, upon local dynamics in Africa. I focus my attention on the impacts of mangrove reforestation policies on women, who harvest shellfish. My research, conducted over 35 years on coastal mangroves in Africa and Madagascar, provides me with examples. Through a long-term approach to the perception of mangroves and the changing modes of policies, I highlight the weight of imperialism and neo-liberalism and analyze types of environmental injustice against mangrove harvesters, particularly the women, who are the most directly concerned by the preservation of their heritage and are rarely recognized as stakeholders in environmental policies that are defined internationally and implemented at national and local levels. They are not informed (or are deliberately kept unware) of new devices such as REDD+. They have lost their rights of access to the reforested mangrove areas. Carbon policy requires comparative and empirical research, giving voice to local actors, especially women, about their perceptions of policies and actions. The approaches in terms of political ecology must be combined with analysis of the bio-ecological and socio-cultural dynamics of the mangrove.","container-title":"Sustainability (Switzerland)","DOI":"10.3390/su9081485","ISSN":"20711050","issue":"8","title":"Let the women harvest the mangrove. carbon policy, and environmental injustice","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, in Mauritania, Imraguen fishermen now sell mullet fish (Mugil cephalus) and in particular the precious eggs to make bottarga to Asian or European export companies; women now have little access to this product and have lost one of the few sources of income in a context of strong constraints in the Banc d'Arguin National Park ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KPGaUiEa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Boulay & Cormier-Salem, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Boulay & Cormier-Salem, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33453,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WIJP6V4D"],"itemData":{"id":33453,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Animal certifié conforme : déchiffrer nos relations avec le vivant","event-place":"Paris (FRA) ; Paris","language":"fr","page":"163–185","publisher":"Dunod ; MNHN","publisher-place":"Paris (FRA) ; Paris","title":"Le mulet jaune, un produit imrâgen requalifié","author":[{"family":"Boulay","given":"S."},{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."}],"editor":[{"family":"Lizet","given":"B."},{"family":"Millet","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The trajectories of women in small-scale fishery are nevertheless varied: in West Africa, it is often women, known as Mama Benz (because they would be used to circulate in shiny Mercedes, external signs of wealth and power) who have the capital to buy the means of production (canoes, engines, large nets, provision for the fishing campaign, etc..) and make fishermen their quasi-employees, not only in their countries of origin, such as Côte d'Ivoire, but also in the countries of migration of these fishermen ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pwJcvAYF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Bennett, 2005; Cormier-Salem, 2017b)","plainCitation":"(E. Bennett, 2005; Cormier-Salem, 2017b)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":525,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EFA5HYUQ"],"itemData":{"id":525,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"451–459","title":"Gender, fisheries and development","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":764,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/99RE3GY4"],"itemData":{"id":764,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"© 2017 by the author. Carbon policy is a fascinating topic in geography and political ecology, because carbon is a new exchangeable good, which links the local to the international arenas through a complex set of instruments, norms, and institutions. In this paper, after explaining my theoretical and conceptual framework rooted in Africanist geography and currents of political ecology, I analyze the responses of local actors (knowledge, practices, logic, and strategies) to environmental policies and consider the effects of new mechanisms, upon local dynamics in Africa. I focus my attention on the impacts of mangrove reforestation policies on women, who harvest shellfish. My research, conducted over 35 years on coastal mangroves in Africa and Madagascar, provides me with examples. Through a long-term approach to the perception of mangroves and the changing modes of policies, I highlight the weight of imperialism and neo-liberalism and analyze types of environmental injustice against mangrove harvesters, particularly the women, who are the most directly concerned by the preservation of their heritage and are rarely recognized as stakeholders in environmental policies that are defined internationally and implemented at national and local levels. They are not informed (or are deliberately kept unware) of new devices such as REDD+. They have lost their rights of access to the reforested mangrove areas. Carbon policy requires comparative and empirical research, giving voice to local actors, especially women, about their perceptions of policies and actions. The approaches in terms of political ecology must be combined with analysis of the bio-ecological and socio-cultural dynamics of the mangrove.","container-title":"Sustainability (Switzerland)","DOI":"10.3390/su9081485","ISSN":"20711050","issue":"8","title":"Let the women harvest the mangrove. carbon policy, and environmental injustice","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Even in India, where fishing is a low-value activity, relegated to the poor and untouchable, women are becoming powerful thanks to globalization ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ecKJoX7z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jalais, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Jalais, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32635,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QTNFXSGV"],"itemData":{"id":32635,"type":"book","event-place":"New Delhi","language":"en","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"New Delhi","title":"Forest of Tigers. People, politics and environment in the Sundarbans","author":[{"family":"Jalais","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, in the Sundarbans, women, who do not have access to the products of the mangrove forest, fish for shrimp fry along the banks of the Ganges and Brahmaputra rivers. With the explosion of shrimp farms since the 1980s, they supply the industrial aquaculture sector and have gained economic and, therefore, social and political power. Besides, women are more and more involved in innovative projects to face poverty and gain social and political recognition, such as marine protected areas co-management, ecocertification schemes and invest new sector such as shell-handicraft ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2LznT5Kj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fr\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}cklin, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Fröcklin, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33460,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RV5VEUA8"],"itemData":{"id":33460,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"We analyzed the potential of small-scale innovations, such as shell-handicraft, as a way to foster transformation toward sustainability, decrease poverty, and increase women’s empowerment in Zanzibar, Tanzania. The shell-handicraft project was founded by USAID in 2006 and was introduced as an alternative livelihood to low-paid seaweed farming and invertebrate harvesting activities. The main objective, however, was to not only alleviate poverty and empower women, but also to improve management of coastal resources, and allegedly by doing so, break poverty traps. To analyze the potential benefits of this enterprise, and more specifically whether or not women involved in this project have been empowered, a framework was used that comprises three inter-related dimensions; agency, access to resources, and outcome. Agency includes the process of decision making, negotiation, etc., in which choices are made and put into effect. Access to resources (financial, physical, human, and social) is the medium through which agency is exercised, and outcome can be defined as the result of agency. Simply put, resources and agency make up people’s potential for living the lives they want. Semistructured interviews were administered to a group of women (n = 36) involved in shell-handicraft and a group of women not involved in shell-handicraft (n = 36) in five villages located in central/south Zanzibar. The results show that over time, the women engaged in shell-handicraft have improved their access to a range of resources, mainly physical (house, cell phone, freezer, and electricity), human (knowledge in marketing, leadership, and entrepreneurship), and social (organization). This further resulted in reported improved self-confidence and decision-making authority within the household. Regarding financial resources, both savings and income improved for the targeted group, but more research is advised. Positively, the environmental impacts of the activity are seemingly low. Old shells are collected for handicraft and a number of no-take zones, as part of the project, have been established to preserve marine resources, which allowed for women’s participation in coastal management. The project has also empowered women and challenged stereotypes, aspects critical for progressive and inclusive management. Although all in all, the women interviewed were satisfied and had increased their standard of living, the discussion problematizes this innovation by addressing scaling up possibilities, market constraints, and the kick-off process having external top-down elements. Even though the recipients of the benefits from the project have been few, this case has valuable elements to learn from and can provide inspiration to drive coastal systems into more sustainable paths.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"2","source":"JSTOR","title":"Small-scale innovations in coastal communities: shell-handicraft as a way to empower women and decrease poverty","title-short":"Small-scale innovations in coastal communities","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/26799097","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Fröcklin","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Jiddawi","given":"Narriman S."},{"family":"Torre-Castro","given":"Maricela","non-dropping-particle":"de la"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Despite recent synthesis ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0sTvWu8x","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO 2020b; Frangoudes and Gerrard 2018; Frangoudes, Gerrard, and Kleiber 2019)","plainCitation":"(FAO 2020b; Frangoudes and Gerrard 2018; Frangoudes, Gerrard, and Kleiber 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32972,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8VWE5I77"],"itemData":{"id":32972,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Maritime Studies","DOI":"10.1007/s40152-018-0113-9","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"117–124","title":"(En)Gendering Change in Small-Scale Fisheries and Fishing Communities in a Globalized World","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Frangoudes","given":"K."},{"family":"Gerrard","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":33472,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NCDUXKHN"],"itemData":{"id":33472,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Maritime Studies","DOI":"10.1007/s40152-019-00159-w","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"241–248","title":"Situated transformations of women and gender relations in small-scale fisheries and communities in a globalized world","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Frangoudes","given":"K."},{"family":"Gerrard","given":"S."},{"family":"Kleiber","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, women in fishing, fisheries labor, and fisheries decision-making still are invisible. The complexity of the intersectional identities and the on-going changes of the women’s situations (gender, gender relations and power relations at diverse scales), need further contextualized studies.

      Despite their contribution and prominence in certain resource sectors, women are often excluded from land and resource tenure rights and wider participation in management discussions; this is true for both legal and customary rights systems.
      Resource access and control is a tenure issue, which is thus also important from a gender perspective. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0GKJpHhK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. M. Larson & Springer, 2016)","plainCitation":"(A. M. Larson & Springer, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33430,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SXHA7GW7"],"itemData":{"id":33430,"type":"chapter","container-title":"NRGF Conceptual Paper","event-place":"Gland, Switzerland","language":"en","publisher":"IUCN, CEESP and CIFOR","publisher-place":"Gland, Switzerland","title":"Recognition and Respect for Tenure Rights","author":[{"family":"Larson","given":"Anne M."},{"family":"Springer","given":"Jenny"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} write that “Women and families depend on tenure security for secure livelihoods and resilience” For example, women’s tenure rights have been found to be associated with their increased participation in household decision-making, increases in household income, and increased expenditure on food and education ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GLS6aeNX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Giovarelli, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Giovarelli, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33420,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TLDRAKSJ"],"itemData":{"id":33420,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"USAID Issue Brief","journalAbbreviation":"USAID Issue Brief","page":"1-15","title":"Land tenure, property rights, and gender: challenges and approaches for strengthening women’s land tenure and property rights","author":[{"family":"Giovarelli","given":"Renee"},{"family":"Wamalwa","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Hannay","given":"Leslie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, women’s security is not the same as men’s, and their tenure rights tend to be weaker than men’s in rural areas of developing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Wy3nZKPB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2011a)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2011a)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34889,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JY5RVSN4"],"itemData":{"id":34889,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Rome","title":"Report of the FAO Workshop on Governance of Tenure for Responsible Capture Fisheries","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Securing women’s participation in decision-making is seen as integrally related to securing women’s resource rights ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2loHUm8K","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations, 2013)","plainCitation":"(United Nations, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33465,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YYQDYYKN"],"itemData":{"id":33465,"type":"report","collection-title":"Sustainable Development Report on Africa","number":"4","publisher":"United Nations Economic Comission for Africa","title":"Managing Africa's Natural Resource Base for Sustainable Growth and Development","URL":"https://www.uneca.org/sites/default/files/PublicationFiles/SDRA4_fin.pdf","author":[{"family":"United Nations","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, as women “have different needs, uses and knowledge in relation to their ecosystems” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8FjTEXAk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. Aguilar, 2016)","plainCitation":"(L. Aguilar, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33477,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9QNCNWQF"],"itemData":{"id":33477,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Gender and Forests: Climate Change, Tenure, Value Chains and Emerging Issues. Earthscan from Routledge","title":"Foreward: Gender and Forests","URL":"https://api.taylorfrancis.com/content/books/mono/download?identifierName=doi&identifierValue=10.4324/978131566624&type=googlepdf","volume":"331","author":[{"family":"Aguilar","given":"Lorena"}],"editor":[{"family":"Colfer","given":"C"},{"family":"Basnett","given":"B"},{"family":"Elias","given":"M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2Qm7Hkxt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Agrawal, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Agrawal, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":526,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F7JXGT6U"],"itemData":{"id":526,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Background Paper","language":"en","title":"Economic contributions of forests","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Agrawal","given":"A."},{"family":"Cashore","given":"B."},{"family":"Hardin","given":"R."},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"G."},{"family":"Benson","given":"C."},{"family":"Miller","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found a significant positive correlation between the number of women on community forestry executive committees and forest conservation outcomes. In another study, women’s participation in forest-related decision making was found to be highly correlated with less disruptive conflict ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Qf4Laijp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. A. Coleman & Mwangi, 2013)","plainCitation":"(E. A. Coleman & Mwangi, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33419,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JJ2TRVSQ"],"itemData":{"id":33419,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","ISSN":"