4.2.2.6.2. Growing awareness and improved policies on community tenure rights
Despite challenges associated with recognizing and securing tenure rights, the growing awareness of the role of tenure in achieving development and environmental goals has created a range of new commitments, initiatives, and policy openings at the country level. Several countries are in the process of reforming their legal frameworks for land tenure, such as Cameroon and the Democratic Republic of the Congo. In other countries, such as Kenya and Liberia, new land laws have recently been enacted. Advocacy regarding the impacts of large-scale land acquisitions have prompted private sector commodity investors to adopt commitments to avoid “land grabbing” in their supply chains (e.g., see Oxfam 2016). Initiatives such as the Global Donor Working Group on Land and the European Union program of support to implement the Voluntary Guidelines on Governance of Tenure indicate that some international donors are providing support to tenure-related activities. These opportunities highlight the importance of learning lessons from previous tenure reforms, including key conditions for reforms and practices that can help advance them.  
      Nepal’s community forest user groups provide an important example of how greater tenure security has enabled community-based institutions to build sustainable livelihoods and improved forest management at scale. While forest devolution started in Nepal since the mid-1970s, major progress in terms of community forestry was observed once the ‘Master Plan for the Forest Sector’ (1988) adopted a ‘user group’ approach, which was based on the existing indigenous forest governance arrangements in various parts of the country ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"I9AnYv31","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gilmour, 1990)","plainCitation":"(Gilmour, 1990)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33423,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GNB8TBW6"],"itemData":{"id":33423,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","ISSN":"0894-1920","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Society & Natural Resources","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"145-158","title":"Resource availability and indigenous forest management systems in Nepal","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Gilmour","given":"DA"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1990"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Traditional users were granted usufruct rights over the forest. The Forest Act (1993) and Forest Regulations (1995) offered strong legal backing for community forestry, which has since contributed to community development, institutionalized inclusive and democratic governance at the local level, and developed leadership of women and other marginalized members ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wq3t2YkA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pokharel, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Pokharel, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33422,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V99AJTML"],"itemData":{"id":33422,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global perspectives on sustainable forest management. InTech, Shanghai, China","journalAbbreviation":"Global perspectives on sustainable forest management. InTech, Shanghai, China","page":"43-58","title":"Nepal’s community forestry: need of better governance","author":[{"family":"Pokharel","given":"Ridish K"},{"family":"Rayamajhi","given":"Santosh"},{"family":"Tiwari","given":"Krishna R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Currently there are over 18,000 forest user groups managing over one third of Nepal’s forest area.  
      Ensuring that reforms create enabling conditions for communities to develop resource-based livelihoods, with strong financial and technical support to meet regulatory requirements, has been another important approach. Guatemala’s community forestry concessions are an exceptional example of community management regimes with positive results for both forests and livelihoods. Between 1994 and 2001, the Guatemalan government, with the backing of important international donors, signed 12 25-year community concessions contracts (for areas ranging from 7,000 ha to 85,000 ha) inside the Mayan Biosphere Reserve with local community groups ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VoQ5gy9m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. Monterroso & Barry, 2012; Radachowsky, 2012; P. L. Taylor, 2010)","plainCitation":"(I. Monterroso & Barry, 2012; Radachowsky, 2012; P. L. Taylor, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33428,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RMPHU7ZM"],"itemData":{"id":33428,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation and Society","DOI":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/26393071.","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"136–50","title":"Legitimacy of Forest Rights: The Underpinnings of the Forest Tenure Reform in the Protected Areas of Petén, Guatemala","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Monterroso","given":"Iliana"},{"family":"Barry","given":"Deborah"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":521,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XEV4KU5H"],"itemData":{"id":521,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In the Multiple-Use Zone of Guatemala’s Maya Biosphere Reserve, the usufruct rights to timber and non-timber forest resources were granted through concession agreements to 12 community organizations and two private timber companies in the late 1990s and early 2000s. After more than a decade, some concessions are successfully managing forests for multiple uses while others have had limited success or failed completely. This paper provides a management unit-based analysis and evaluation of the evolution of these forest concessions. First, we present a critical assessment of the current state of ecological integrity, socio-economic development, governance, and financing within each of the 14 forest concessions, using a series of quantitative and qualitative indicators. Next, we categorize the different trajectories that the concessions have experienced, and describe the key biophysical, socio-economic, and market events and drivers that may have influenced their outcomes. Lastly, we provide suggestions for the continued consolidation of multiple-use forest management practices in the Maya Biosphere Reserve, and draw out lessons for multiple-use forest management elsewhere in the tropics.","container-title":"Multiple Use of Tropical Forests: From Concept to Reality","DOI":"10.1016/j.foreco.2011.08.043","ISSN":"0378-1127","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","page":"18-28","title":"Forest concessions in the Maya Biosphere Reserve, Guatemala: A decade later","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378112711005354","volume":"268","author":[{"family":"Radachowsky","given":"Jeremy"},{"family":"Ramos","given":"Victor H."},{"family":"McNab","given":"Roan"},{"family":"Baur","given":"Erick H."},{"family":"Kazakov","given":"Nikolay"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",3,15]]}}},{"id":530,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MMHT6WT3"],"itemData":{"id":530,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Rural Studies","ISSN":"0743-0167","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Rural Studies","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"173-184","title":"Conservation, community, and culture? New organizational challenges of community forest concessions in the Maya Biosphere Reserve of Guatemala","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Taylor","given":"Peter Leigh"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The previously conflictive forest landscape was transformed, as communities were granted rights to manage and sell both high-value timber and non-timber forest resources in about 400,000 ha. Although the regulations were strict (requiring Forest Stewardship Council certification), it was possible for communities to meet them because of the investment made in the arrangement by multiple actors including key government supporters ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ia1ttYJr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. Monterroso & Larson, 2013)","plainCitation":"(I. Monterroso & Larson, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33427,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2ASC68I5"],"itemData":{"id":33427,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Latin American Geography","DOI":"10.1353/lag.2013.0006.","language":"en","page":"87–110","title":"The Dynamic Forest Commons of Central America: New Directions for Research","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Monterroso","given":"Iliana"},{"family":"Larson","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A comparative study of forests in the region (the Maya Forest of Mexico and Guatemala) found no significant difference in deforestation rates between the community concessions and protected areas  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0yXZMKR5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bray, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Bray, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":529,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T57UG6DI"],"itemData":{"id":529,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and society","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Ecology and society","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: JSTOR","title":"Tropical deforestation, community forests, and protected areas in the Maya Forest","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Bray","given":"David Barton"},{"family":"Duran","given":"Elvira"},{"family":"Ramos","given":"Victor Hugo"},{"family":"Mas","given":"Jean-Francois"},{"family":"Velazquez","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"McNab","given":"Roan Balas"},{"family":"Barry","given":"Deborah"},{"family":"Radachowsky","given":"Jeremy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Income from collective timber and non-timber sales surpasses US$44 million and is distributed to members, invested in social infrastructure, and reinvested in community forest enterprises ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1JD6oCFG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. M. Monterroso, 2015)","plainCitation":"(I. M. Monterroso, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33459,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P33HJ7G5"],"itemData":{"id":33459,"type":"thesis","genre":"Doctoral Dissertation","publisher":"Universitat Autònoma de Barcelona","title":"Forest Tenure Reforms and Socio-Environmental Consequences Case Studies on Guatemala and Nicaragua","URL":"https://ddd.uab.cat/pub/tesis/2016/hdl_10803_385745/immi1de1.pdf","author":[{"family":"Monterroso","given":"Iliana Maria"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The cases from Guatemala and Nepal also demonstrate the ongoing importance of social movements, and specifically the higher-level federations of community organizations, to overcoming implementation challenges ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vVk1A0pE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Paudel, 2012; P. L. Taylor, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Paudel, 2012; P. L. Taylor, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":532,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ANWLA8W8"],"itemData":{"id":532,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Forests for People","ISBN":"1-84977-476-5","page":"132-152","publisher":"Routledge","title":"Community networks, collective action and forest management benefi ts","author":[{"family":"Paudel","given":"Naya S"},{"family":"Monterroso","given":"Iliana"},{"family":"Cronkleton","given":"Peter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":530,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MMHT6WT3"],"itemData":{"id":530,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Rural Studies","ISSN":"0743-0167","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Rural Studies","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"173-184","title":"Conservation, community, and culture? New organizational challenges of community forest concessions in the Maya Biosphere Reserve of Guatemala","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Taylor","given":"Peter Leigh"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      
4.2.2.7. Equity and benefit sharing 
Key messages: 
*  Inequitable distribution of the benefits for the use of wild species undermines sustainability by encouraging over-harvesting, short term gains over long term sustainable management, poaching, and mining of resources by companies. 
* Inequities exist between local communities and companies, governments, and others, but they also exist within communities, where elite capture of benefits is common with wild species use and trade, particularly when sold outside the community.  
* Equitable distribution of benefits from the sustainable use of wild species is a stated goal of many governance and institutional frameworks. However, implementation of these goals is often flawed. This has a direct impact on sustainability, creates incentives to over-harvest species, undermines long term management of species, and can support unsustainable commercial extraction.  
* Marginalization and exclusion stem from a range of political, economic and other factors and leads to inequity in resource allocation, distribution of benefits and participation in decision-making and management; such inequities mean that there are  limits on who has access to and who benefits from (and who does not), from wild species use;
* People’s perception of fairness and justice influences their willingness to comply with regulations that govern sustainable use.   
 
   The sustainability of wild species use is significantly impacted by the broader issues of equity that surrounds this use. Below the experts review aspects of equity in use, including access to and distribution of benefits, extent of engagement and marginalization of stakeholders, and the role of civil society, social movements, and political processes in bringing greater equity to use of wild species. 

4.2.2.7.1.  Impact of marginalization and inequality on sustainable use   
Marginalization in sustainable use is a complex issue, which requires not only consideration for contemporary inequities but also historical, political, and social contextual factors that create an uneven playing field from initial distributions of access, capabilities, and power ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"D0iJFTIH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McDermott, 2013)","plainCitation":"(McDermott, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35150,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RRNJZBPK"],"itemData":{"id":35150,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science & Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.envsci.2012.10.006","language":"fr","page":"416–427","title":"Examining equity: A multidimensional framework for assessing equity in payments for ecosystem services","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"McDermott","given":"M."},{"family":"Mahanty","given":"S."},{"family":"Schreckenberg","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is defined as the significant reduction of capacity and power, and political and economic exclusion, of rural and indigenous people to make or participate in decisions over the sustainable use of plants and animals in areas they depend on, with detrimental impacts on their livelihoods and well-being (adapted from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"p4ydz7GD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Raleigh, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Raleigh, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":542,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JWUWUL5A"],"itemData":{"id":542,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BMJ","ISSN":"0959-8138","journalAbbreviation":"BMJ","note":"publisher: British Medical Journal Publishing Group","title":"Is still inequitable","volume":"341","author":[{"family":"Raleigh","given":"Veena S"},{"family":"Frosini","given":"Francesca"},{"family":"Appleby","given":"John"},{"family":"Gao","given":"Haiyan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Marginalisation and disempowerment can have social equity implications, in that marginalised people may not have access to benefits or rights, they may not have a voice in decision-making process, and their values, knowledge, and culture may not be recognized ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PqHd3dfq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Martin, 2016)","plainCitation":"(A. Martin, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":536,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IGIPBAHI"],"itemData":{"id":536,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"254-261","title":"Justice and conservation: The need to incorporate recognition","volume":"197","author":[{"family":"Martin","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Coolsaet","given":"Brendan"},{"family":"Corbera","given":"Esteve"},{"family":"Dawson","given":"Neil M"},{"family":"Fraser","given":"James A"},{"family":"Lehmann","given":"Ina"},{"family":"Rodriguez","given":"Iokiñe"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.   
      Marginalization can be driven by a variety of mechanisms, including exclusion from decision-making, elite capture and power concentration, management systems not representative of relevant interests or experiences, and a lack of knowledge or recognized rights ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"C4Lxdevt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. J. P. Colfer, 2011)","plainCitation":"(C. J. P. Colfer, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":541,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7K9VZDPN"],"itemData":{"id":541,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","ISSN":"0305-750X","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","note":"number: 12\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"2147-2164","title":"Marginalized forest peoples’ perceptions of the legitimacy of governance: an exploration","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Colfer","given":"Carol J Pierce"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, women make up a substantial amount of labor participation in natural resource sectors (for example, up to 50% in seafood production chains) and often fill key roles in value chains, including sales, marketing, and business administration. However, despite their contribution and prominence in resource sectors, women often are excluded from tenure rights and wider participation in management discussions; this is true for both legal and customary rights systems (ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"E8E1d9Lq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harper, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Harper, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33429,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XYLDS9AL"],"itemData":{"id":33429,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"91–106","title":"Contributions by women to fisheries economies: insights from five maritime countries","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Harper","given":"S."},{"family":"Grubb","given":"C."},{"family":"Stiles","given":"M."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      There has been a (poorly documented) history of evictions from protected areas, where access to and use of natural resources by local people has been restricted ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gzvVhSPW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(West, 2006)","plainCitation":"(West, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":492,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P2NBTA3B"],"itemData":{"id":492,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Anthropology","DOI":"10.1146/annurev.anthro.35.081705.123308","ISSN":"0084-6570, 1545-4290","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Anthropol.","language":"en","page":"251-277","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Parks and Peoples: The Social Impact of Protected Areas","title-short":"Parks and Peoples","URL":"http://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev.anthro.35.081705.123308","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"West","given":"Paige"},{"family":"Igoe","given":"James"},{"family":"Brockington","given":"Dan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"izoLSYmG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. C. Burgess, 2014)","plainCitation":"(S. C. Burgess, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":546,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZLIUWV6H"],"itemData":{"id":546,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","ISSN":"1939-5582","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Applications","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"257-270","title":"Beyond connectivity: how empirical methods can quantify population persistence to improve marine protected?area design","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Burgess","given":"Scott C"},{"family":"Nickols","given":"Kerry J"},{"family":"Griesemer","given":"Chris D"},{"family":"Barnett","given":"Lewis AK"},{"family":"Dedrick","given":"Allison G"},{"family":"Satterthwaite","given":"Erin V"},{"family":"Yamane","given":"Lauren"},{"family":"Morgan","given":"Steven G"},{"family":"White","given":"J Wilson"},{"family":"Botsford","given":"Louis W"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Externally imposed or post-colonial laws often underpin these restrictions (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Zzpg7g8c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McCarthy & Cramb, 2009)","plainCitation":"(McCarthy & Cramb, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KD6RL6GW"],"itemData":{"id":545,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geographical Journal","ISSN":"0016-7398","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Geographical Journal","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"112-123","title":"Policy narratives, landholder engagement, and oil palm expansion on the Malaysian and Indonesian frontiers","volume":"175","author":[{"family":"McCarthy","given":"John F"},{"family":"Cramb","given":"Robert A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, or powerful international organizations (e.g., companies, or even large non-governmental organizations) can impose their vision of what nature should look like ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KeFOcUnu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brockington & Igoe, 2006; Brockington & Scholfield, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Brockington & Igoe, 2006; Brockington & Scholfield, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":544,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/USJWTA8J"],"itemData":{"id":544,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation and society","ISSN":"0972-4923","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation and society","note":"publisher: JSTOR","page":"424-470","title":"Eviction for conservation: a global overview","author":[{"family":"Brockington","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Igoe","given":"James"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":552,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9EBDQRUD"],"itemData":{"id":552,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Antipode","ISSN":"0066-4812","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Antipode","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"551-575","title":"The conservationist mode of production and conservation NGOs in sub?Saharan Africa","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Brockington","given":"Dan"},{"family":"Scholfield","given":"Katherine"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.   
      Elite-capture (or concentration of decision-making power in the hands of a few) and centralization across different levels of government can also entrench inequities and drive marginalization. For instance, studies have documented how local people who engage with government administrations benefit more from forest governance programmes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tt9oES8q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. D. Wright 2016)","plainCitation":"(G. D. Wright 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":567,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RAFHM6MK"],"itemData":{"id":567,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Policy makers around the world tout decentralization as an effective tool in the governance of natural resources. Despite the popularity of these reforms, there is limited scientific evidence on the environmental effects of decentralization, especially in tropical biomes. This study presents evidence on the institutional conditions under which decentralization is likely to be successful in sustaining forests. We draw on common-pool resource theory to argue that the environmental impact of decentralization hinges on the ability of reforms to engage local forest users in the governance of forests. Using matching techniques, we analyze longitudinal field observations on both social and biophysical characteristics in a large number of local government territories in Bolivia (a country with a decentralized forestry policy) and Peru (a country with a much more centralized forestry policy). We find that territories with a decentralized forest governance structure have more stable forest cover, but only when local forest user groups actively engage with the local government officials. We provide evidence in support of a possible causal process behind these results: When user groups engage with the decentralized units, it creates a more enabling environment for effective local governance of forests, including more local government-led forest governance activities, fora for the resolution of forest-related conflicts, intermunicipal cooperation in the forestry sector, and stronger technical capabilities of the local government staff.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Science of the United States of America","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1610650114","ISSN":"0027-8424","issue":"52","page":"14958–14963","title":"Decentralization can help reduce deforestation when user groups engage with local government","volume":"113","author":[{"family":"Wright","given":"Glenn D"},{"family":"Andersson","given":"Krister P"},{"family":"Gibson","given":"Clark C"},{"family":"Evans","given":"Tom P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Involvement of external organizations runs the risk of reinforcing this elite capture and can create dependencies for local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AsBakdPo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Calfucura, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Calfucura, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":523,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R2Y9MPBA"],"itemData":{"id":523,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The effectiveness of Community-Based Conservation (CBC) has been under discussion. Through the review of CBC initiatives in developing countries, we contribute to the debate by analysing how players in?uence different factors that determine the outcomes of CBC. We highlight several points. First, the promise of bene?ts has been overshadowed by constraints on the extraction of natural resources, along with the use of governmental schemes to extract partially the rents from CBC, which has left little to share inside the communities. Second, CBC design and implementation has often overlooked the asymmetries of power within the community and at the individual level, which has produced unequal access to the design, decision-making and outcomes of this activity. Third, while the elites have had a determinant role in shaping the governance of CBC, external agents' promotion of CBC has tended to reproduce the existent negotiating power at the community, local and national levels. Fourth, land contestation plays a key role in the dynamics of relationship and con?ict between the governments, corporate interests and the communities. Finally, CBC adaptation to new scenarios depends critically on the process of community empowerment and the building of networks with external agents.","call-number":"Sub171","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2017.05.012","ISSN":"09218009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"English","page":"18-26","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Governance, Land and Distribution: A Discussion on the Political Economy of Community-Based Conservation","title-short":"Governance, Land and Distribution","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0921800916309399","volume":"145","author":[{"family":"Calfucura","given":"Enrique"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, the promotion of market-based conservation mechanisms can shift power around natural resources use from local people to more powerful organizations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"02GIOthb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Martin, 2013)","plainCitation":"(A. Martin, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":543,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4GQ4AX5F"],"itemData":{"id":543,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Geographical Journal","ISSN":"0016-7398","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"The Geographical Journal","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"122-131","title":"Global environmental justice and biodiversity conservation","volume":"179","author":[{"family":"Martin","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"McGuire","given":"Shawn"},{"family":"Sullivan","given":"Sian"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.   
      People’s perceptions of fairness and justice can influence their willingness to comply with rules and regulations intended to govern the use and management of natural resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4XDQ0SAc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. J. P. Colfer, 2011)","plainCitation":"(C. J. P. Colfer, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":541,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7K9VZDPN"],"itemData":{"id":541,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","ISSN":"0305-750X","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","note":"number: 12\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"2147-2164","title":"Marginalized forest peoples’ perceptions of the legitimacy of governance: an exploration","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Colfer","given":"Carol J Pierce"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There is extensive evidence from the common pool resources literature that empowering people affected by policy changes to participate in discussions, management, and monitoring of natural resources improves outcomes, both for sustainable use and for more effective and lasting implementation of rules to manage and restrict resource harvest and access ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vce03FMS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biggs 2019; Elinor Ostrom 2009a)","plainCitation":"(Biggs 2019; Elinor Ostrom 2009a)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33471,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PSUF3T2J"],"itemData":{"id":33471,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"One strategy to address threats to biodiversity in the face of ongoing budget constraints is to create an enabling environment that facilitates individuals, communities and other groups to self-organise to achieve conservation outcomes. Emergence (new activities and initiatives), and robustness (durability of these activities and initiatives over time), two related concepts from the common pool resources literature, provide guidance on how to support and enable such self-organised action for conservation. To date emergence has received little attention in the literature. Our exploratory synthesis of the conditions for emergence from the literature highlighted four themes: for conservation to emerge, actors need to 1) recognise the need for change, 2) expect positive outcomes, 3) be able to experiment to achieve collective learning, and 4) have legitimate local scale governance authority. Insights from the literature on emergence and robustness suggest that an appropriate balance should be maintained between external guidance of conservation and enabling local actors to find solutions appropriate to their contexts. We illustrate the conditions for emergence, and its interaction with robustness, through discussing the Communal Areas Management Programme for Indigenous Resources (CAMPFIRE) in Zimbabwe and reflect on efforts at strengthening local autonomy and management around the world. We suggest that the delicate balance between external guidance of actions, and supporting local actors to develop their own solutions, should be managed adaptively over time to support the emergence of robust conservation actions.","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2019.e00538","ISSN":"2351-9894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e00538","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Insights on fostering the emergence of robust conservation actions from Zimbabwe's CAMPFIRE program","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2351989419300368","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C."},{"family":"Castilla","given":"Juan Carlos"},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Mills","given":"Morena"},{"family":"Gandiwa","given":"Edson"},{"family":"Etienne","given":"Michel"},{"family":"Knight","given":"Andrew T."},{"family":"Marquet","given":"Pablo A."},{"family":"Possingham","given":"Hugh P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",11,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1,1]]}}},{"id":36,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2NACCRB4"],"itemData":{"id":36,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1172133","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5939","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"419-422","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A General Framework for Analyzing Sustainability of Social-Ecological Systems","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1172133","volume":"325","author":[{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",7,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fairness, and the nature of decision-making processes rather than participation itself, is considered to be central to both quality and justice in natural resources management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6OlhBAj2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Reed, 2008; P. D. Smith & McDonough, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Reed, 2008; P. D. Smith & McDonough, 2001)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":551,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WW3UK7E5"],"itemData":{"id":551,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological conservation","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Biological conservation","note":"number: 10\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"2417-2431","title":"Stakeholder participation for environmental management: a literature review","volume":"141","author":[{"family":"Reed","given":"Mark S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":550,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TKR5PNVE"],"itemData":{"id":550,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & natural resources","ISSN":"0894-1920","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Society & natural resources","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"239-249","title":"Beyond public participation: Fairness in natural resource decision making","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Smith","given":"Patrick D"},{"family":"McDonough","given":"Maureen H"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Stakeholder engagement early on has been linked to more effective and longer-lasting solutions to environmental issues and can contribute to a more comprehensive understanding of the social-ecological context by integrating local and scientific knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9OFRacFr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Reed, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Reed, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":551,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WW3UK7E5"],"itemData":{"id":551,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological conservation","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Biological conservation","note":"number: 10\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"2417-2431","title":"Stakeholder participation for environmental management: a literature review","volume":"141","author":[{"family":"Reed","given":"Mark S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Co-management of protected areas is one governance model employed to recognise rights of indigenous or local people, while building support for sustainable use and natural resource management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5scIfvJI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Timko & Satterfield, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Timko & Satterfield, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":531,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QWFMZDSN"],"itemData":{"id":531,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Natural Areas Journal","ISSN":"0885-8608","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Natural Areas Journal","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: BioOne","page":"307-319","title":"Criteria and indicators for evaluating social equity and ecological integrity in national parks and protected areas","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Timko","given":"Joleen"},{"family":"Satterfield","given":"Terre"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      
4.2.2.8. Impacts of peace and armed conflict on sustainable Use   
 Key messages: 
*  Impacts of conflicts on sustainable use are diverse and varied across contexts and through different pathways and indirect ways.   
*  Post-conflict settings pose significant risks to sustainable use for a variety of reasons  

      4.2.2.8.1. Definitions
Conflicts have a detrimental effect on human well-being and socio-economic development ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7KvIliLr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gates, 2012; Lopez & Wodon, 2005; Machel, 2001; Melander, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Gates, 2012; Lopez & Wodon, 2005; Machel, 2001; Melander, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":535,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YMYN9Y58"],"itemData":{"id":535,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","ISSN":"0305-750X","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","note":"number: 9\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"1713-1722","title":"Development consequences of armed conflict","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Gates","given":"Scott"},{"family":"Hegre","given":"Håvard"},{"family":"Nygård","given":"Håvard Mokleiv"},{"family":"Strand","given":"Håvard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":538,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3VBKW8JU"],"itemData":{"id":538,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of African economies","ISSN":"1464-3723","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of African economies","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Oxford University Press","page":"586-602","title":"The economic impact of armed conflict in Rwanda","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Lopez","given":"Humberto"},{"family":"Wodon","given":"Quentin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":539,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TLB9G4LL"],"itemData":{"id":539,"type":"book","ISBN":"1-85065-485-9","publisher":"ERIC","title":"The Impact of War on Children: A Review of Progress Since the 1996 United Nations Report on the Impact of Armed Conflict on Children.","author":[{"family":"Machel","given":"Graça"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":540,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W3D4A79Q"],"itemData":{"id":540,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Studies Quarterly","ISSN":"1468-2478","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"International Studies Quarterly","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Blackwell Publishing Inc Malden, USA","page":"695-714","title":"Gender equality and intrastate armed conflict","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Melander","given":"Erik"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A variety of definitions of what classifies as armed conflict exist ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ANUpCqhD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gleditsch, 2002; Stewart, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Gleditsch, 2002; Stewart, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":534,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/38CGIAMX"],"itemData":{"id":534,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of peace research","ISSN":"0022-3433","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of peace research","note":"number: 5\\npublisher: Sage Publications London","page":"615-637","title":"Armed conflict 1946-2001: A new dataset","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Gleditsch","given":"Nils Petter"},{"family":"Wallensteen","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Eriksson","given":"Mikael"},{"family":"Sollenberg","given":"Margareta"},{"family":"Strand","given":"Håvard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":33462,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UCD2984J"],"itemData":{"id":33462,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2003.08.002","ISSN":"0378-8741","issue":"1","page":"3-13","title":"The African cherry (Prunus africana): Can lessons be learned from an over-exploited medicinal tree?","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Stewart","given":"K. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For the purposes of this assessment, the experts define conflict as periods of armed violence due to political insecurity, instability, or war. These conflicts often lead to a disruption of economies, government services and the extensive movement of people to flee conflict zones for personal safety and/or better opportunities. These conflicts have a range of impacts, mainly negative, on sustainable use, which are discussed below.   

4.2.2.8.2. Direct pathways of impact
Armed conflict affects sustainable use through multiple pathways including where conflict directly affects wild species and via broad socio-political and economic pathways that arise because of the conflict, such as changing institutional dynamics, migration and displacement of peoples, and transformed economies and livelihoods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4RsDdc0E","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gaynor, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Gaynor, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":533,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UWRIDM5T"],"itemData":{"id":533,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","ISSN":"1540-9295","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","note":"number: 10\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"533-542","title":"War and wildlife: linking armed conflict to conservation","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Gaynor","given":"Kaitlyn M"},{"family":"Fiorella","given":"Kathryn J"},{"family":"Gregory","given":"Gillian H"},{"family":"Kurz","given":"David J"},{"family":"Seto","given":"Katherine L"},{"family":"Withey","given":"Lauren S"},{"family":"Brashares","given":"Justin S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Direct negative effects of conflict on sustainable use include a reduction on economic activity, which can reduce deforestation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xaYpEM1B","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. G. Burgess, 2016a)","plainCitation":"(M. G. Burgess, 2016a)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4339,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CY45NY6K","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JM8RIBAM"],"itemData":{"id":4339,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Balanced harvesting – harvesting all species and sizes in an ecosystem in proportion to their productivity – is a fisheries management strategy that has been suggested recently to increase yields, while reducing overall ecosystem impact. However, some aspects of balanced harvesting are controversial, including its call for extensive harvesting of juveniles and forage fish. Balanced harvesting also calls for targeting species and size-classes that are not currently marketable, possibly at a significant economic cost. Some have argued that this cost is outweighed by the ecological benefits of maintaining the ecosystem size and trophic structures and by the benefits of extra yield for food security. There is broad consensus that balanced harvesting would require major changes to fishery management institutions and consumer behaviour, and it is unclear to what extent it is physically possible with current technologies. For this reason, we argue that steps to implement balanced harvesting are difficult to justify until the case for it is more clearly resolved. We outline some of the pivotal questions that must be answered to make a convincing case for or against balanced harvesting, many of which can be answered empirically. In identifying these questions, we hope to offer a constructive path forward in resolving some of the key issues in the balanced harvesting debate.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12123","ISSN":"1467-2979","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"© 2015 John Wiley & Sons Ltd","page":"1216–1226","title":"Remaining questions in the case for balanced harvesting","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/faf.12123","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Burgess","given":"Matthew G."},{"family":"Diekert","given":"Florian K."},{"family":"Jacobsen","given":"Nis S."},{"family":"Andersen","given":"Ken H."},{"family":"Gaines","given":"Steven D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, unsustainable use of forest resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kKsHv14N","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Butsic, 2015; Machlis & Hanson, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Butsic, 2015; Machlis & Hanson, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":537,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F4MVC7B7"],"itemData":{"id":537,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological conservation","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological conservation","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"266-273","title":"Conservation and conflict in the Democratic Republic of Congo: The impacts of warfare, mining, and protected areas on deforestation","volume":"191","author":[{"family":"Butsic","given":"Van"},{"family":"Baumann","given":"Matthias"},{"family":"Shortland","given":"Anja"},{"family":"Walker","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Kuemmerle","given":"Tobias"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":562,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/39A2K628"],"itemData":{"id":562,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Warfare Ecology","page":"33-40","publisher":"Springer","title":"Warfare ecology","author":[{"family":"Machlis","given":"Gary E"},{"family":"Hanson","given":"Thor"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} including the killing of wild animals and the destruction of habitat ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NGvBtmSK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gaynor, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Gaynor, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":533,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UWRIDM5T"],"itemData":{"id":533,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","ISSN":"1540-9295","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","note":"number: 10\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"533-542","title":"War and wildlife: linking armed conflict to conservation","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Gaynor","given":"Kaitlyn M"},{"family":"Fiorella","given":"Kathryn J"},{"family":"Gregory","given":"Gillian H"},{"family":"Kurz","given":"David J"},{"family":"Seto","given":"Katherine L"},{"family":"Withey","given":"Lauren S"},{"family":"Brashares","given":"Justin S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many forms of weaponry commonly utilised in armed conflict, such as land mines, can inadvertently kill wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mTEzHT1y","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eniang, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Eniang, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":555,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7ZFNBMHX"],"itemData":{"id":555,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Envtl. Pol'y & L.","journalAbbreviation":"Envtl. Pol'y & L.","note":"publisher: HeinOnline","page":"501","title":"Impacts of landmines on the environment and biodiversity","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Eniang","given":"Edem A"},{"family":"Haile","given":"Amleset"},{"family":"Yihdego","given":"Teshale"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Large-scale habitat alteration may occur when armies destroy habitat for tactical advantages (e.g., defoliation used in the Vietnam war ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PA5CznHJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Orians & Pfeiffer, 1970)","plainCitation":"(Orians & Pfeiffer, 1970)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QMRHP665"],"itemData":{"id":548,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","ISSN":"0036-8075","issue":"3931","journalAbbreviation":"Science","note":"number: 3931\\npublisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science","page":"544-554","title":"Ecological Effects of the War in Vietnam: Effects of defoliation, bombing, and other military activities on the ecology of Vietnam are described.","volume":"168","author":[{"family":"Orians","given":"Gordon H"},{"family":"Pfeiffer","given":"EW"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1970"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Armed groups may also use protected areas as staging grounds, due to their remote location and plentiful natural resources, leading to overexploitation of certain species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"f29qFazT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. Hanson, 2009; Machlis & Hanson, 2011)","plainCitation":"(T. Hanson, 2009; Machlis & Hanson, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":549,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DZWR7WS8"],"itemData":{"id":549,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","ISSN":"0888-8892","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"578-587","title":"Warfare in biodiversity hotspots","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Hanson","given":"Thor"},{"family":"Brooks","given":"Thomas M"},{"family":"Da Fonseca","given":"Gustavo AB"},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Lamoreux","given":"John F"},{"family":"Machlis","given":"Gary"},{"family":"Mittermeier","given":"Cristina G"},{"family":"Mittermeier","given":"Russell A"},{"family":"Pilgrim","given":"John D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":562,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/39A2K628"],"itemData":{"id":562,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Warfare Ecology","page":"33-40","publisher":"Springer","title":"Warfare ecology","author":[{"family":"Machlis","given":"Gary E"},{"family":"Hanson","given":"Thor"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An increase in the availability of guns during and following a conflict may increase the prevalence and sophistication of hunting, further undermining sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7h7LgFjy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jacobs & Schloeder, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Jacobs & Schloeder, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":554,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WTWQITTY"],"itemData":{"id":554,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Washington, DC: Biodiversity support program","journalAbbreviation":"Washington, DC: Biodiversity support program","title":"Impacts of conflict on biodiversity and protected areas in Ethiopia","author":[{"family":"Jacobs","given":"Michael J"},{"family":"Schloeder","given":"Catherine A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Soldiers may also kill wild species at high rates for various reasons, including subsistence in the field; and high-value wild species products, such as ivory, are used to finance operations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kWl06cnN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Beyers, 2011; Yamagiwa, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Beyers, 2011; Yamagiwa, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":560,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JU5NJEI6"],"itemData":{"id":560,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PloS one","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"PloS one","note":"number: 11\\npublisher: Public Library of Science San Francisco, USA","page":"e27129","title":"Resource wars and conflict ivory: the impact of civil conflict on elephants in the Democratic Republic of Congo-the case of the Okapi Reserve","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Beyers","given":"Rene L"},{"family":"Hart","given":"John A"},{"family":"Sinclair","given":"Anthony RE"},{"family":"Grossmann","given":"Falk"},{"family":"Klinkenberg","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Dino","given":"Simeon"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":561,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GBH4QZNL"],"itemData":{"id":561,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","ISSN":"1054-9811","issue":"3-4","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","note":"number: 3-4\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"111-130","title":"Bushmeat poaching and the conservation crisis in Kahuzi-Biega National Park, Democratic Republic of the Congo","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Yamagiwa","given":"Juichi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
  
4.2.2.8.3. Indirect pathways of impact   
Indirect effects of conflict on sustainable use include the reduction of international development, investment, and support, which reduces the ability of policymakers, managers and research institutions to fulfil their conservation roles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OFKjsAIa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biggs, 2019a; Conteh, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Biggs, 2019a; Conteh, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33471,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PSUF3T2J"],"itemData":{"id":33471,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"One strategy to address threats to biodiversity in the face of ongoing budget constraints is to create an enabling environment that facilitates individuals, communities and other groups to self-organise to achieve conservation outcomes. Emergence (new activities and initiatives), and robustness (durability of these activities and initiatives over time), two related concepts from the common pool resources literature, provide guidance on how to support and enable such self-organised action for conservation. To date emergence has received little attention in the literature. Our exploratory synthesis of the conditions for emergence from the literature highlighted four themes: for conservation to emerge, actors need to 1) recognise the need for change, 2) expect positive outcomes, 3) be able to experiment to achieve collective learning, and 4) have legitimate local scale governance authority. Insights from the literature on emergence and robustness suggest that an appropriate balance should be maintained between external guidance of conservation and enabling local actors to find solutions appropriate to their contexts. We illustrate the conditions for emergence, and its interaction with robustness, through discussing the Communal Areas Management Programme for Indigenous Resources (CAMPFIRE) in Zimbabwe and reflect on efforts at strengthening local autonomy and management around the world. We suggest that the delicate balance between external guidance of actions, and supporting local actors to develop their own solutions, should be managed adaptively over time to support the emergence of robust conservation actions.","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2019.e00538","ISSN":"2351-9894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e00538","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Insights on fostering the emergence of robust conservation actions from Zimbabwe's CAMPFIRE program","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2351989419300368","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C."},{"family":"Castilla","given":"Juan Carlos"},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Mills","given":"Morena"},{"family":"Gandiwa","given":"Edson"},{"family":"Etienne","given":"Michel"},{"family":"Knight","given":"Andrew T."},{"family":"Marquet","given":"Pablo A."},{"family":"Possingham","given":"Hugh P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",11,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1,1]]}}},{"id":559,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J5R2KI9B"],"itemData":{"id":559,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity and Conservation","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Springer","page":"983-996","title":"Assessing the impacts of war on perceived conservation capacity and threats to biodiversity","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Conteh","given":"Abu"},{"family":"Gavin","given":"Michael C"},{"family":"McCarter","given":"Joe"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} Reductions in funding reduce the ability to enforce laws and regulations within protected areas, including stopping bushmeat hunting and deforestation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"e5Dv27Ky","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Butsic, 2015; Kaimowitz & Faun\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Butsic, 2015; Kaimowitz & Fauné, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":537,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F4MVC7B7"],"itemData":{"id":537,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological conservation","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological conservation","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"266-273","title":"Conservation and conflict in the Democratic Republic of Congo: The impacts of warfare, mining, and protected areas on deforestation","volume":"191","author":[{"family":"Butsic","given":"Van"},{"family":"Baumann","given":"Matthias"},{"family":"Shortland","given":"Anja"},{"family":"Walker","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Kuemmerle","given":"Tobias"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":553,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/586I27T3"],"itemData":{"id":553,"type":"chapter","container-title":"War and tropical forests: conservation in areas of armed conflict","ISBN":"1-00-307521-5","page":"21-47","publisher":"CRC Press","title":"Contras and comandantes: armed movements and forest conservation in Nicaragua’s Bosawas Biosphere Reserve","author":[{"family":"Kaimowitz","given":"David"},{"family":"Fauné","given":"Angelica"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The disruption of traditional institutions in local communities also creates challenges for post-war conservation activities. One of the largest changes arising from armed conflict is the forced movement and migration of people, where large groups of vulnerable people form dense camps and extract natural resources such as wild foods and timber for survival ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZPceEyyZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jambiya, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Jambiya, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33464,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I6V9AHUM"],"itemData":{"id":33464,"type":"report","abstract":"Cambridge, UK , Gland, Switzerland - The lack of meat in refugee rations in East Africa is causing a flourishing illegal trade in wild meat, threatening ...","language":"en","publisher":"TRAFFIC","title":"Night Time Spinach: Conservation and livelihood implications of wild meat use in refugee situations in north western Tanzania - Wildlife Trade Report","title-short":"Night Time Spinach","author":[{"family":"Jambiya","given":"G."},{"family":"Milledge","given":"S."},{"family":"Mtango","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For people remaining in conflict zones, normal livelihood activities are drastically affected, as conflict disrupts industry and trade and creates shortages of goods. Therefore, local people may turn to natural resource use, especially as longer-term investments such as those required by farming and commerce become risky ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JTbibgR2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lanjouw, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Lanjouw, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":558,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4EQ9PDRV"],"itemData":{"id":558,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","ISSN":"1054-9811","issue":"3-4","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Sustainable Forestry","note":"number: 3-4\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"89-110","title":"Building partnerships in the face of political and armed crisis","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Lanjouw","given":"Annette"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The most common positive effect of armed conflict on use of natural resources is the so-called “refuge effect”, where people avoid or move away from an area, creating places where pressue on wild species is lessened ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3qpdpixH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McNeely, 2004)","plainCitation":"(McNeely, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33447,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TGGR94IE"],"itemData":{"id":33447,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agroforestry Systems","language":"en","page":"155–165","title":"Nature vs. nurture: managing relationships between forests, agroforestry and wild biodiversity","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"McNeely","given":"J.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These areas can host flourishing wild species populations (e.g., the Demilitarised zone between North and South Korea ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ngTVwnu2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kim, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Kim, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":557,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HRMFNAKK"],"itemData":{"id":557,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","ISSN":"1095-9203","issue":"5336","journalAbbreviation":"Science","note":"number: 5336\\npublisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science","page":"242-243","title":"Preserving biodiversity in Korea's demilitarized zone","volume":"278","author":[{"family":"Kim","given":"Ke Chung"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} . Similarly, due to the reduction of international trade across borders during times of armed conflict, international trade of wild species products may be reduced ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HkBgm9bu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey 2011)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":522,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YAL627S3"],"itemData":{"id":522,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The prevalence and impacts of the illegal trade in bushmeat are under appreciated in Southern Africa, despite indications that it constitutes a serious conservation threat in parts of the region. Bushmeat trade has emerged as a severe threat to wildlife conservation and the viability of wildlife-based land uses in Zimbabwe during a period of political instability and severe economic decline. We conducted a study around Save´ Valley Conservancy in the South-East Lowveld of Zimbabwe to investigate the dynamics and underlying causes of the bushmeat trade, with the objective of developing solutions. We found that bushmeat hunting is conducted mainly by unemployed young men to generate cash income, used mostly to purchase food. Bushmeat is mainly sold to people with cash incomes in adjacent communal lands and population centres and is popular by virtue of its affordability and availability. Key drivers of the bushmeat trade in the South-East Lowveld include: poverty, unemployment and food shortages, settlement of wildlife areas by impoverished communities that provided open access to wildlife resources, failure to provide stakes for communities in wildlife-based land uses, absence of affordable protein sources other than illegally sourced bushmeat, inadequate investment in anti-poaching in areas remaining under wildlife management, and weak penal systems that do not provide suf?cient deterrents to illegal bushmeat hunters. Each of these underlying causes needs to be addressed for the bushmeat trade to be tackled effectively. However, in the absence of political and economic stability, controlling illegal bushmeat hunting will remain extremely dif?cult and the future of wildlife-based land uses will remain bleak.","call-number":"Esub44","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605310001274","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Oryx","language":"en","page":"84-95","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Dynamics and underlying causes of illegal bushmeat trade in Zimbabwe","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0030605310001274/type/journal_article","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"P. A."},{"family":"Romañach","given":"S.S."},{"family":"Matema","given":"S."},{"family":"Matema","given":"C."},{"family":"Mupamhadzi","given":"I."},{"family":"Muvengwi","given":"J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another positive effect can arise when forced disarmament of local populations by armed groups may disrupt normal hunting efforts. Yet, these refuges may result in displacement of extraction of resources, as people that move away from conflict zones move into other areas where over-extraction and unsustainable use of natural resources occur ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qbMd00F0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alvarez, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Alvarez, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":565,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B7TTZ2JW"],"itemData":{"id":565,"type":"chapter","container-title":"War and tropical forests: conservation in areas of armed conflict","ISBN":"1-00-307521-5","page":"49-70","publisher":"CRC Press","title":"Forests in the time of violence: conservation implications of the Colombian war","author":[{"family":"Alvarez","given":"Maria D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      
4.2.2.8.4. The impacts on sustainable use in post-conflict settings
The end of conflicts can lead to rapid change in the development of agriculture and extractive industries with subsequent impacts on sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OJPDgGgU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Machlis & Hanson, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Machlis & Hanson, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":562,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/39A2K628"],"itemData":{"id":562,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Warfare Ecology","page":"33-40","publisher":"Springer","title":"Warfare ecology","author":[{"family":"Machlis","given":"Gary E"},{"family":"Hanson","given":"Thor"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many post-conflict regions possess valuable oil and mineral reserves as well as timber-rich forests which were off-limits to development because of the conflict. In post-conflict periods, rural return and its associated development can have catastrophic consequences for the natural environment and undermine sustainable use as evidenced in Liberia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Tkv6DO2o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brottem & Unruh, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Brottem & Unruh, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":564,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6H62WCSL"],"itemData":{"id":564,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of the Association of American Geographers","ISSN":"0004-5608","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Annals of the Association of American Geographers","note":"number: 5\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"995-1002","title":"Territorial tensions: rainforest conservation, postconflict recovery, and land tenure in Liberia","volume":"99","author":[{"family":"Brottem","given":"Leif"},{"family":"Unruh","given":"Jon"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Cambodia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Lg5xkXQb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Loucks, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Loucks, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":556,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AWM7RTI9"],"itemData":{"id":556,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","ISSN":"1755-263X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Letters","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"82-92","title":"Wildlife decline in Cambodia, 1953–2005: exploring the legacy of armed conflict","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Loucks","given":"Colby"},{"family":"Mascia","given":"Michael B"},{"family":"Maxwell","given":"Andy"},{"family":"Huy","given":"Keavuth"},{"family":"Duong","given":"Kong"},{"family":"Chea","given":"Nareth"},{"family":"Long","given":"Barney"},{"family":"Cox","given":"Nick"},{"family":"Seng","given":"Teak"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and Colombia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ym0Fwtn3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Negret, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Negret, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":563,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BV8H3KCY"],"itemData":{"id":563,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","ISSN":"0888-8892","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","title":"Need for conservation planning in postconflict Colombia","author":[{"family":"Negret","given":"Pablo Jose"},{"family":"Allan","given":"James"},{"family":"Braczkowski","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Maron","given":"Martine"},{"family":"Watson","given":"James EM"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Post-cold war case studies from Russia and previous republics of the USSR also provide insight into sustainable use in post-conflict settings. After the Soviet period, many basic food resources provided to communities by the state were suddenly reduced and even stopped. “These food shortages incentivized the development of an illegal market, especially for expensive products such as meat. Such incentives, with the help of cars, artificial lights and modern firearms, induced a strong increase of illegal hunting in all these newly independent countries” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Rr7c94af","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Svizzero, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Svizzero, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33437,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YZJ49VY9"],"itemData":{"id":33437,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Zoological Research","language":"en","page":"1–9","title":"Issues and Challenges in the Conservation of the Goitered Gazelle (Gazella subgutturosa; Güldenstädt, 1780","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Svizzero","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This situation was not only the result of the collapse of the centralized government (e.g., formal laws) but was also due to the fact that during the Soviet period, many kinds of community-based systems of natural resource management and customary laws had been severely eroded.  Although in many areas, new informal and formal institutions are now developing to fill the vacuum of central governance, lack of coordination has led to conflicts between resource users. For example, in the Kamchatka region of Russia, there are many obstacles to the sustainable and equitable management of salmon fisheries and reindeer herds which have arisen from tensions between formal regulations and informal entitlements ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cl1MU00Q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gerkey, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Gerkey, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33432,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RFKJTZDX"],"itemData":{"id":33432,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Organization","ISSN":"0018-7259","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Human Organization","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Society for Applied Anthropology","page":"336-345","title":"The emergence of institutions in a post-Soviet commons: salmon fishing and reindeer herding in Kamchatka, Russia","volume":"75","author":[{"family":"Gerkey","given":"Drew"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Post-conflict periods can also have positive outcomes if state control is established rapidly after conflict ceases. New opportunities to plan for, and regulate sustainable use in previously inaccessible areas can be developed. Where there is foreign or outsider control in post-conflict regions, however, these planning efforts and restructuring and creation of new institutions can be unstable, lack relevance to local contexts and have limited longevity (i.e., green grabbing in Sierra Leone) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OerWreON","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cavanagh, 2018; Fairhead, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Cavanagh, 2018; Fairhead, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33436,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NYXGHAMV"],"itemData":{"id":33436,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"African Geographical Review","ISSN":"1937-6812","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"African Geographical Review","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"120-133","title":"Enclosure, dispossession, and the green economy: new contours of internal displacement in Liberia and Sierra Leone?","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Cavanagh","given":"Connor Joseph"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":34661,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/34US2L33"],"itemData":{"id":34661,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Peasant Studies","DOI":"10.1080/03066150.2012.671770","ISSN":"0306-6150, 1743-9361","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Peasant Studies","language":"en","page":"237-261","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Green Grabbing: a new appropriation of nature?","title-short":"Green Grabbing","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/03066150.2012.671770","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Fairhead","given":"James"},{"family":"Leach","given":"Melissa"},{"family":"Scoones","given":"Ian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Understanding the influence of post-conflict on sustainable use is crucial for informing conservation actions in megadiverse countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MEk6bc0e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. Hanson, 2009b)","plainCitation":"(T. Hanson, 2009b)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":547,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/544KEUMF"],"itemData":{"id":547,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","ISSN":"0888-8892","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"578-587","title":"Warfare in biodiversity hotspots","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Hanson","given":"Thor"},{"family":"Brooks","given":"Thomas M"},{"family":"Da Fonseca","given":"Gustavo AB"},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Lamoreux","given":"John F"},{"family":"Machlis","given":"Gary"},{"family":"Mittermeier","given":"Cristina G"},{"family":"Mittermeier","given":"Russell A"},{"family":"Pilgrim","given":"John D"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; however, in many cases the relationship between armed conflict, post-conflict, and unsustainable use of resources is complex with confounding factors that need to be considered. Establishing governmental control where illegal groups are present or where they had influence before the post-conflict periods is essential to avoid unsustainable use of resources. One potential way to improve governmental control of these areas is through working with the local communities to establish development and natural resource needs and objectives ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Hb5Dzja1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Negret, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Negret, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":566,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/89ET2RIQ"],"itemData":{"id":566,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"108176","title":"Emerging evidence that armed conflict and coca cultivation influence deforestation patterns","volume":"239","author":[{"family":"Negret","given":"Pablo Jose"},{"family":"Sonter","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Watson","given":"James EM"},{"family":"Possingham","given":"Hugh P"},{"family":"Jones","given":"Kendall R"},{"family":"Suarez","given":"Cesar"},{"family":"Ochoa-Quintero","given":"Jose Manuel"},{"family":"Maron","given":"Martine"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      
4.2.3 Social drivers 
Key Messages: 
* Social drivers: Various demographic and social factors influence the sustainable (or unsustainable) use of wild species: migration and urbanization, social organization and reproduction, empowerment, effective participation and accountability, poverty and process of marginalization, gender equity and, rural development (roads, infrastructure, access to material assets and immaterial goods-market, credit, internet) (well established) {4.2.2.7}
* Population growth, demographic change and mobility are affecting use patterns of wild species.  Specifically: 
o Population density and growth are leading to increased demand/consumption of wild species in some regions, particularly in urbanized areas of the global south (well established) {4.2.3.2}.
o Increased mobility is leading to unsustainable use of wild species in key areas. Such mobility is associated with displacement (i.e., from conflict, environmental degradation) as well as economic opportunity (e.g., transnational labor movements).  In addition to increasing pressure on species, there is growing displacement of local uses (e.g., of indigenous peoples and local knowledge) (well established) {4.2.3.2}.
o Mobility across political and ecological borders, may be leading to unsustainable use, particularly where such mobility is accompanied by lack of attachment to the place(s) (established but incomplete) {4.2.3.2, 4.2.3.2.2}.
* Urbanization tends to lead to decreased consumption of wild species due to access to the market economy for food (established but incomplete) {4.2.3.2.3}.
o  Mobility of peoples across political and ecological borders, may be leading to unsustainable use, particularly where such mobility is accompanied by lack of attachment to the place(s) (established but incomplete) {4.2.3.2, 4.2.3.2.2}.
* Social organization and networks affect how the benefits and costs of wild species use are distributed. Societies that are more equitable tend to experience less poverty, conflict and social inequality, which are factors correlated with sustainable use patterns (well established) {4.2.3.5}.
o Social inequity and poverty are a growing trend globally, particularly in the global south. In many regions, where alternatives to basic needs (e.g., shelter, food) and economic and social supports (e.g., education) are limited, there is greater dependence on wild species. However, it is an over-simplification at attribute unsustainable use of wild species to those living facing poverty.  (well established) {4.2.3.5}
o Although some evidence points to those living in poverty are culpable for increasing unsustainable use of wild species, the socio-economic and political systems that have created and perpetuate poverty and inequity are the underlying driver (well established) {4.2.3.5}. 
o Given that poverty is multidimensional, eradicating it requires a multidimensional approach. Access to food, shelter, education, employment, and health can lift people out of poverty and make them less dependent on wild species (well established) {4.2.3.5}. 
o Equitable distribution of benefits from the sustainable use of wild species is a stated goal of many governance and institutional frameworks. However, implementation of these goals is often flawed. This has a direct impact on sustainability, creates incentives to over-harvest species, undermines long term management of species, and can support unsustainable commercial extraction (well established) {4.2.3.4, 4.2.3.5}.    
o Use of wild species by women and indigenous peoples are under recognized and poorly protected and consequently create / aggravate problems of food insecurity and poor health for vulnerable populations (e.g., poor nutrition) and increase dependency on commercially produced food resources (well established) {4.2.3.4; 4.2.3.5}.
* Social values and norms influence how wild species are used and many aspects of their sustainability are interpreted:
o Social groups who are most dependent on wild species tend to experience greater concern and anxiety about their health and unsustainable use (i.e., have heightened risk perception (well established) {4.2.3.3.6}. These groups thus tend to be critical stakeholders in identifying sustainable use solutions (well established) {4.2.3.7}, Among the groups with long term dependencies and support sustainable use are indigenous peoples (well-established) {4.2.2.2.5}. 
o Many indigenous peoples and local communities who have long term relationships to wild species have well developed relationships, knowledge systems, practices, and rules (i.e., customary laws) which ensure their sustainable use (well established) {4.2.3.5}.
o Social norms create the social context in which wild species use is structured/organized and also interpreted by users. Where practices of hunting, fishing and gathering are fundamental to food provisioning, and support livelihood and social identity, these practices and uses tend to be more sustainable (established but incomplete) {4.2.3.3}.
o The harvest of wild species is recognized as important to food security, health and well-being in many regions; where there is increasing risk (both reported and perceived) of bioaccumulation of contaminants, presence of disease (including transmissible disease to humans), hunting, fishing and gathering of wild species tend to decrease. However, trust in the actors involved in risk communication is a mediating factor (well established) {4.2.3.7}.
* Gender inequity in how the costs/benefits of wild species use are distributed is visible in key regions of the globe (well established) {4.2.3.6}.

4.2.3.1. Overview 
There are numerous social drivers that (along with other economic, political and environmental drivers have a significant impact on patterns of use of wild species and the associated practices of use. The definition of social drivers as those social structures (class, ethnicity, gender and location), norms (e.g., unwritten but accepted rules of behavior), relationships and broad systems of social interaction that shape individual and collective uses of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NZYqJGbZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dugarova & Utting, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Dugarova & Utting, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33563,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IQMHG59K"],"itemData":{"id":33563,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"UNRISD","title":"The Social Drivers of Sustainable Development (E/CN.5/2014/8, 52nd session of the Commission for Social Development","author":[{"family":"Dugarova","given":"E."},{"family":"Utting","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This section is focused on specific drivers within the context of social organization (demographics, livelihoods and urbanization, migration), social equity, poverty and exclusion, social movements, health and well-being.
4.2.3.1.1 Methodology
A systematic literature review was carried out in respect of key areas of literature using terms such as social organization, norms, population mobility, gender, indigenous peoples and each of the regions, and practices (e.g., hunting, fishing) as well as for well drivers identified in other assessments as begin related to biodiversity including urbanization, rural development, poverty and inequity. Published academic papers in the social sciences (e.g., sociology, geography, economics, interdisciplinary – environmental studies) were reviewed; they were largely from English-speaking journals, were published in the last 20 years. Other kinds of publications (e.g., grey literature, conference proceedings, indigenous and local knowledge reports) were also included in the review, particularly where there was an absence of academic publication. Other kinds of reporting of indigenous and local knowledge were also a consideration (e.g., alternative media). A higher value was placed on papers that offered evidence / data about particular kinds of trends, patterns and dynamics in social system (i.e., less consideration of conceptual / theoretical and editorial articles). Those publications that offered a synthesis of data from multiple locations and over time. Over 2000 sources were found by authors. Based on review of these sources, major themes and interpretation of patterns in political drivers were identified.  The sections were developed by experts with 20 + years of experience related to aspects of biodiversity conservation and in the social sciences. Where there were gaps in regions, practices, etc., and case studies were developed to illustrate an important dimension of the social driver and its impacts on practices and uses.

4.2.3.1.2 Gaps
* Social systems, like many aspects of ecosystems, are highly complex and there are many factors that affect sustainable use that are not well documented.
* There are gaps in literature related to governance of gathering and non-extractive uses (including viewing) when compared to the practices of hunting, fishing and logging. 
* Regional gaps exist with respect to social norms, perceptions, and gendered dimensions of sustainable use in most parts of the globe with particular gaps apparent for Latin, America, Asia, particularly in relation to informal institutions and governance systems of indigenous peoples and local communities. 

4.2.3.2. Demographics and mobility
4.2.3.2.1 Population growth and demographic change
Demographic shifts are occurring with significant population increases occurring in areas of the globe. These demographic increases are creating increased pressures on wild species. Globally the average population density is 25 people per km2 but there are unique patterns in different parts of the world which are factors in the relationship of people to, and use of, nature including fisheries, wild species, timber resources and other resources. For example, in some island natures such as Macao, SAR of China, Singapore, Hong Kong, SAR of China and Gibraltar, population density is well above the average. The population of Singapore for example, has nearly 8,000 people per km2 (2,000 times more densely populated than Australia. Bangladesh is the most densely populated with 1,252 per km2, followed by Lebanon (595 per km2), South Korea (528 per km2), the Netherlands (508 per km2) and Rwanda (495 per km2). Population in and of itself is not a pre-determination of unsustainability; norms of stewardship, for example, meditate patterns such as over-harvest in many regions. This is evident by the examples of Netherlands and Rwanda. Wild species are a reported rarity in the Netherlands (i.e., all land and species are managed), however, some of the most valued and largest megafauna in the world (e.g., mountain gorilla) are being sustainably managed in Rwanda ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"j18HnAeV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Abensperg-Traun, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Abensperg-Traun, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34640,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WZBYGGSA"],"itemData":{"id":34640,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2008.12.034","ISSN":"00063207","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"948-963","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"CITES, sustainable use of wild species and incentive-driven conservation in developing countries, with an emphasis on southern Africa","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320709000615","volume":"142","author":[{"family":"Abensperg-Traun","given":"Max"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition to density, population growth (including rapid population) growth are a consideration in future sustainable use patterns.  Population growth is highest in Sub-Saharan Africa. In other areas of the globe, the rate of population growth is falling ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JacWsOmS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Anr\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}quez & Stloukal, 2008; Saad, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Anríquez & Stloukal, 2008; Saad, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34799,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HP5HZ35L"],"itemData":{"id":34799,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"European View","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"309–317","title":"Rural population change in developing countries: Lessons for policymaking","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Anríquez","given":"G."},{"family":"Stloukal","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":33556,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7FK3SNZR"],"itemData":{"id":33556,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Population Aging: Is Latin America Ready?","event-place":"Washington, DC","language":"en","page":"43–77","publisher":"World Bank","publisher-place":"Washington, DC","title":"Demographic trends in Latin America and the Caribbean","author":[{"family":"Saad","given":"P."}],"editor":[{"family":"Cotlear","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Age differences also affect use patterns. For example, hunters are generally younger than non-hunters ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qB0SQVGo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Loibooki, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Loibooki, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33559,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SQAKIMV4"],"itemData":{"id":33559,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","language":"en","page":"391–398","title":"Bushmeat hunting by communities adjacent to the Serengeti National Park, Tanzania: the importance of livestock ownership and alternative sources of protein and income","volume":"29","author":[{"family":"Loibooki","given":"M."},{"family":"Hofer","given":"H."},{"family":"Campbell","given":"K.L.I."},{"literal":"East"},{"literal":"M.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, mainly due to a lack of income–generating opportunities. In areas with high fertility, children may provide labor to gather natural resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"f6TryxJF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(De Sherbinin, 2008; Gifford & Nilsson, 2014)","plainCitation":"(De Sherbinin, 2008; Gifford & Nilsson, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33545,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YP5ZN2SQ"],"itemData":{"id":33545,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global environmental change","ISSN":"0959-3780","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Global environmental change","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"38-53","title":"Rural household demographics, livelihoods and the environment","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"De Sherbinin","given":"Alex"},{"family":"VanWey","given":"Leah K"},{"family":"McSweeney","given":"Kendra"},{"family":"Aggarwal","given":"Rimjhim"},{"family":"Barbieri","given":"Alisson"},{"family":"Henry","given":"Sabine"},{"family":"Hunter","given":"Lori M"},{"family":"Twine","given":"Wayne"},{"family":"Walker","given":"Robert"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":33575,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XT5KJ7VJ"],"itemData":{"id":33575,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Psychology","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"141–157","title":"Personal and social factors that influence pro?environmental concern and behaviour: A review","volume":"49","author":[{"family":"Gifford","given":"R."},{"family":"Nilsson","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, conservation and norms related to protecting the environment tend to be more prevalent in younger generations.  For example, in Nigeria, Togo and Niger younger people ate less bushmeat than older persons, in part be due to a growing ‘westernization’ of the lifestyles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"n5ZQxcn8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Luiselli, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Luiselli, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33991,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QRUD4XHT"],"itemData":{"id":33991,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Acta Oecologica","DOI":"10.1016/j.actao.2017.10.002","ISSN":"1146609X","journalAbbreviation":"Acta Oecologica","language":"en","page":"47-56","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Understanding the influence of non-wealth factors in determining bushmeat consumption: Results from four West African countries","title-short":"Understanding the influence of non-wealth factors in determining bushmeat consumption","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1146609X17301911","volume":"94","author":[{"family":"Luiselli","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Hema","given":"Emmanuel M."},{"family":"Segniagbeto","given":"Gabriel Hoinsoudé"},{"family":"Ouattara","given":"Valy"},{"family":"Eniang","given":"Edem A."},{"family":"Di Vittorio","given":"Massimiliano"},{"family":"Amadi","given":"Nioking"},{"family":"Parfait","given":"Gnoumou"},{"family":"Pacini","given":"Nic"},{"family":"Akani","given":"Godfrey C."},{"family":"Sirima","given":"Djidama"},{"family":"Guenda","given":"Wendengoudi"},{"family":"Fakae","given":"Barineme B."},{"family":"Dendi","given":"Daniele"},{"family":"Fa","given":"John E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is also true in northern Canada (Kuhnlein).
4.2.3.2.2 Migration and mobility
 Key Messages:
* Many peoples around the world (including indigenous peoples and local communities) have developed livelihoods over many generations, that are interconnected with the migratory wild species; knowledge and practices (e.g., hunting, fishing etc.) developed over many generations have resulted in dynamic but sustainable use patterns.  The loss, fragmentation and/or degradation of migratory species habitats is leading are major challenges to sustaining sustainable use.  
* Forced migration due to war and conflict as well as environmental refugees is leading to changes in the use of wild species in some key areas of the globe.
* Increasing mobility (due to transformation innovations) and the globalization of markets is associated with a break-down of social norms associated with care and stewardship of place (lack of attachment of place).
* There is an unprecedented multi- directional movement of peoples around the world involving new patterns of transnational migration, identities and communities (Robinson 2007). 
* Transcience is associated with a breakdown of social norms and institutions that governed human behavior and increase in individualistic human behavior and choices than are often antithetical to sustainability.

      Livelihoods in many parts of the world are highly interconnected with the dynamics of ecosystems including the variabilities of migratory species. For example, in the circumpolar north, indigenous peoples have well developed systems of travel and tracking of wild species such as barren ground caribou; although there is significant spatial and population variability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1ORUWEdU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vors & Boyce, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Vors & Boyce, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":812,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IBGMCSL3"],"itemData":{"id":812,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2486.2009.01974.x","ISSN":"13541013, 13652486","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"2626-2633","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Global declines of caribou and reindeer: CARIBOU REINDEER DECLINE","title-short":"Global declines of caribou and reindeer","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1365-2486.2009.01974.x","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Vors","given":"Liv Solveig"},{"family":"Boyce","given":"Mark Stephen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, their hunting practices have been sustainable for generations. One of the keys to such success has been the critical importance of learning and adapting to changes in population dynamics and health is the diversity of wild species valued for food security and the flexible livelihood strategies of harvesters who are able to adapt their harvesting practices to other species when caribou populations decline ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Io715Cmj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 1995; Nuttall, 2005; Winterhalder, 1983)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 1995; Nuttall, 2005; Winterhalder, 1983)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33514,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7PGKLP4B"],"itemData":{"id":33514,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic","issue":"1","note":"number: 1","page":"81-93","title":"The persistence of Aboriginal Land Use: Fish and wildlife harvest areas in the Hudson and James Bay Lowland, Ontario","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"A."},{"family":"George","given":"P."},{"family":"Preston","given":"R."},{"family":"Cummins","given":"B."},{"family":"Turner","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}},{"id":32230,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UPCAA7N7"],"itemData":{"id":32230,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Arctic: Arctic Climate Impact Assessment, by Arctic Climate Impact Assessment (ACIA","language":"en","page":"649–690","publisher":"ACIA Overview Report. Cambridge University Press","title":"Hunting, Herding, Fishing, and Gathering: Indigenous Peoples and Renewable Resource Use in the Arctic","URL":"https://www.amap.no/documents/doc/arctic-arctic-climate-impact-assessment/796.","author":[{"family":"Nuttall","given":"Mark"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":828,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FIKWSIJN"],"itemData":{"id":828,"type":"book","language":"en","number-of-pages":"201–241","publisher":"Springer Publishing","title":"Boreal Forest Adaptations","author":[{"family":"Winterhalder","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1983"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similar patterns of sustainable use of migratory species are evident in other regions. However, with increasing pressures of climate change and the loss of wild species habitats due to industrialization and land clearing, migratory species and associated livelihoods are under stress.  Alternatives, however, are not easily constructed. “Given that many hunting and forest peoples are semi-migratory, their lifestyles do not allow for adequate care of livestock, other than easily transported species such as chickens. Dayaks of Long Segar, Kalimantan, for instance, move from villages to their fields at planting and harvest times so that government efforts to introduce cattle into such societies have been a miserable failure” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yUEMiLAK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. L. Bennett & Robinson, 2000)","plainCitation":"(E. L. Bennett & Robinson, 2000)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33572,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B6MQSUCJ"],"itemData":{"id":33572,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Hunting for Sustainability in Tropical Forests","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"0-231-10976-8","page":"1-10","publisher":"Columbia University Press","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Hunting for the Snark","URL":"https://books.google.de/books?hl=en&lr=&id=ZawmOh3IZ_IC&oi=fnd&pg=PR15&dq=hunting+definition&ots=gqfJLS9Ioj&sig=WHmwB9whJFsd4ayG4zziU6vahu8#v=onepage&q=hunting definition&f=false","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"Elizabeth L."},{"family":"Robinson","given":"John G."}],"editor":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"John G."},{"family":"Bennett","given":"Elizabeth L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Protections of migratory species and livelihoods are urgent but also challenging given the rigidity of protected areas and mobility of such wild species and peoples; “traditional conservation strategies involving static tools (e.g., protected areas that have fixed spatial boundaries) may be ineffective for managing species whose ranges are changing” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LtXhSOa2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bull, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Bull, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33546,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UQAPT9YM"],"itemData":{"id":33546,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","ISSN":"1540-9309","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"203-210","title":"Conservation when nothing stands still: moving targets and biodiversity offsets","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Bull","given":"Joseph W"},{"family":"Suttle","given":"Kenwyn B"},{"family":"Singh","given":"Navinder J"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"EJ"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33546,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UQAPT9YM"],"itemData":{"id":33546,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","ISSN":"1540-9309","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"203-210","title":"Conservation when nothing stands still: moving targets and biodiversity offsets","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Bull","given":"Joseph W"},{"family":"Suttle","given":"Kenwyn B"},{"family":"Singh","given":"Navinder J"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"EJ"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Mobile or flexible protected area systems that support migratory species (including a high degree of variability in habitat use), are most successful in ensuring sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6wi84IfG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bull, 2013; Hole, 2011; Maxwell, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Bull, 2013; Hole, 2011; Maxwell, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33546,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UQAPT9YM"],"itemData":{"id":33546,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","ISSN":"1540-9309","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"203-210","title":"Conservation when nothing stands still: moving targets and biodiversity offsets","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Bull","given":"Joseph W"},{"family":"Suttle","given":"Kenwyn B"},{"family":"Singh","given":"Navinder J"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"EJ"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33541,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3HJCYAHS"],"itemData":{"id":33541,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","ISSN":"1476-4687","issue":"7828","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","note":"number: 7828\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"217-227","title":"Area-based conservation in the twenty-first century","volume":"586","author":[{"family":"Maxwell","given":"Sean L"},{"family":"Cazalis","given":"Victor"},{"family":"Dudley","given":"Nigel"},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Rodrigues","given":"Ana SL"},{"family":"Stolton","given":"Sue"},{"family":"Visconti","given":"Piero"},{"family":"Woodley","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Kingston","given":"Naomi"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Edward"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":33708,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UECVECXA"],"itemData":{"id":33708,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","ISSN":"0888-8892","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"305-315","title":"Toward a management framework for networks of protected areas in the face of climate change","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Hole","given":"David G"},{"family":"Huntley","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Arinaitwe","given":"Julius"},{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart HM"},{"family":"Collingham","given":"Yvonne C"},{"family":"Fishpool","given":"Lincoln DC"},{"family":"Pain","given":"Deborah J"},{"family":"Willis","given":"Stephen G"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Perhaps one of the most illustrative examples of livelihoods being interrelated with variable species relates to fishing. The rotation of fishing lakes is a well-established example in the case of indigenous peoples and local communities. It was a historic practice governed by customary law, among indigenous peoples in northern Canada to move camps and fishing from one lake to another at various intervals to allow fish stocks to recover. Indigenous peoples from northern Canada, for example, historically would “rotate their fishing grounds and adjust gillnet mesh size according to what they anticipate to harvest, which results in a diffusion of harvesting pressure over space and time, and by species and size-class” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Cukkj2Oi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 1989, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 1989, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32560,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/64BDD3UB"],"itemData":{"id":32560,"type":"book","language":"en","number-of-pages":"70–88","publisher":"Belhaven Press","title":"Common Property Resources: Ecology and Community-Based Sustainable Development","author":[{"literal":"Berkes"}],"editor":[{"family":"","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1989"]]}}},{"id":32651,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E33G332J"],"itemData":{"id":32651,"type":"book","event-place":"London","publisher":"Taylor & Francis","publisher-place":"London","title":"Sacred Ecology. Traditional Ecological Knowledge and Resource Management","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Fishers in other areas changing their fishing areas in response to local availabilities of the most valued target species, whether demand is for local or international markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3FSM0viU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cripps & Gardner, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Cripps & Gardner, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33547,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FGCGSAZS"],"itemData":{"id":33547,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geoforum","ISSN":"0016-7185","journalAbbreviation":"Geoforum","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"49-62","title":"Human migration and marine protected areas: Insights from Vezo fishers in Madagascar","volume":"74","author":[{"family":"Cripps","given":"Garth"},{"family":"Gardner","given":"Charlie J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Aside from this basic characteristic of fisheries, however, there are also many continuing examples of seasonal migrations as fishers follow moving fish stocks or rotate through various fishing grounds depending on desired species and/or weather conditions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5cvlpTUZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kluger, 2019, 2020; Piezonka, 2020; Wanyonyi, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Kluger, 2019, 2020; Piezonka, 2020; Wanyonyi, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33041,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D9N8V3HR"],"itemData":{"id":33041,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Coastal marine resources provide livelihoods to human communities around the world. The interactions in respective social-ecological systems are usually of complex nature, due to a wide range of different fisheries interacting with the ecosystem. Understanding connectivity within these systems (i.e. among social and ecological actors) helps in establishing meaningful management strategies for sustainable use of marine resources. This work uses the value chain analysis of different fisheries sectors to construct a qualitative social-ecological network (SEN) model of the Sechura Bay in North Peru. Here, a diverse ensemble of small-scale fisheries co-exists with a flourishing mariculture sector, though the respective production chains partially overlap. Directed and unweighted links between actors were defined based on the transfer of biomass (trophic interactions) and money (economic interactions). Several analytical network tools were applied (e.g. degree centrality, dominator tree) to identify the most important nodes of the social-ecological network and to understand interdependencies. Results of the network analysis suggest position of system's components being related to their vulnerability in the face of external disturbances. Fisher groups with a high specialization with respect to fisheries target species were stronger impacted by the last strong El Niño event (the Coastal El Niño 2017), with drastic consequences for actors of the respective production chains. The present approach is envisioned to be applicable beyond this particular case study and to nourish on-going scientific discussions on the use of social-ecological network analysis to describe human-nature interactions. This study allows (i) estimating node (actor) susceptibilities to natural and anthropogenic disturbances, and (ii) reconciling sustainable resource use and nature conservation by enhancing the understanding of the functionality of the respective social-ecological system.","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2019.104861","ISSN":"0964-5691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"104861","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Specialization of fishers leads to greater impact of external disturbance: Evidence from a social-ecological network modelling exercise for Sechura Bay, northern Peru","title-short":"Specialization of fishers leads to greater impact of external disturbance","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0964569119301218","volume":"179","author":[{"family":"Kluger","given":"Lotta C."},{"family":"Scotti","given":"Marco"},{"family":"Vivar","given":"Ivonne"},{"family":"Wolff","given":"Matthias"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",9,1]]}}},{"id":33548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HHFLRZJN"],"itemData":{"id":33548,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","ISSN":"0959-3780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"102095","title":"On the move: The role of mobility and migration as a coping strategy for resource users after abrupt environmental disturbance–the empirical example of the Coastal El Niño 2017","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Kluger","given":"Lotta Clara"},{"family":"Alff","given":"Henryk"},{"family":"Alfaro-Córdova","given":"Eliana"},{"family":"Alfaro-Shigueto","given":"Joanna"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":33543,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FQGP8ERX"],"itemData":{"id":33543,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Quaternary International","ISSN":"1040-6182","journalAbbreviation":"Quaternary International","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"189-203","title":"Migration and its effects on life ways and subsistence strategies of boreal hunter-fishers: Ethnoarchaeological research among the Selkup, Siberia","volume":"541","author":[{"family":"Piezonka","given":"Henny"},{"family":"Poshekhonova","given":"Olga"},{"family":"Adaev","given":"Vladimir"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":33542,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PXQPXTYG"],"itemData":{"id":33542,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","ISSN":"0308-597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"242-255","title":"Migrant fishers of Pemba: Drivers, impacts and mediating factors","volume":"71","author":[{"family":"Wanyonyi","given":"Innocent Ngao"},{"family":"Wamukota","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Tuda","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Mwakha","given":"Victor Alati"},{"family":"Nguti","given":"Lydia Mukii"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Very often these migrations involve movement across more recently established political boundaries, or temporary settlement in communities with which they have historical understandings of shared space and economic linkages. In some cases, including, for example, that of the indigenous Bajau Laut peoples, migrant fishers and their cultures may not be fully recognized as belonging to any nation. For migrant fishers, traditional resource use strategies that involve continuous movement may conflict with modern boundaries and area-based management, which complicates and hinders their inclusion into resource use and management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QIxxnxxG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Clifton & Majors, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Clifton & Majors, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33589,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VAT44XVV"],"itemData":{"id":33589,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","issue":"7","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"716–725","title":"Culture, conservation, and conflict: perspectives on marine protection among the Bajau of Southeast Asia","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Clifton","given":"Julian"},{"family":"Majors","given":"Chris"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, making them highly vulnerable to resource exclusion and unjust prosecution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MdluJYkR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, 2018; Finkbeiner, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, 2018; Finkbeiner, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33586,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X4QPFNA6"],"itemData":{"id":33586,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Natural Resources Forum","DOI":"10.1111/1477-8947.12143","ISSN":"01650203","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Resour Forum","language":"en","page":"83-92","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The market and shadow value of informal fish catch: a framework and application to Panama: Andrés Cisneros-Montemayor, Sarah S. Harper and Travis Tai / Natural Resources Forum","title-short":"The market and shadow value of informal fish catch","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/1477-8947.12143","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"Andrés M."},{"family":"Harper","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Tai","given":"Travis C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",5]]}}},{"id":33587,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8WTIBIPZ"],"itemData":{"id":33587,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2015.03.009","ISSN":"09593780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"139-152","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The role of diversification in dynamic small-scale fisheries: Lessons from Baja California Sur, Mexico","title-short":"The role of diversification in dynamic small-scale fisheries","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0959378015000461","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Finkbeiner","given":"Elena M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is important that current and future management find proactive ways to include such traditional livelihoods, particularly given the rapid impacts of climate change and human use on local resource availabilities. International agreements and the recognition of the value of these customary uses and management strategies are essential in that regard, both for vulnerable indigenous communities and for wider sustainability goals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9m1nb5Bt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vierros, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Vierros, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33540,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WKBPNENJ"],"itemData":{"id":33540,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","ISSN":"0308-597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"104039","title":"Considering Indigenous peoples and local communities in governance of the global ocean commons","volume":"119","author":[{"family":"Vierros","given":"Marjo K"},{"family":"Harrison","given":"Autumn-Lynn"},{"family":"Sloat","given":"Matthew R"},{"family":"Crespo","given":"Guillermo Ortuño"},{"family":"Moore","given":"Jonathan W"},{"family":"Dunn","given":"Daniel C"},{"family":"Ota","given":"Yoshitaka"},{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"Andrés M"},{"family":"Shillinger","given":"George L"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Trisha Kehaulani"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.   

4.2.3.2.3. Forced migration – refugees
In 2000, 175 million people lived outside their country of origin. By 2013 this stood at 232 million. Of these migrants, 35% moved from one developing country to another, while 34% moved from developing to developed countries (Population and Sustainability Network and the Population and Sustainable Development Alliance). In addition to international migration flows, internal (within country) migration is highly significant – especially for movements from rural to urban areas – although data on numbers of internal migrants is much harder to ascertain ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"G22sw7zm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cohen, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Cohen, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33709,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HDVS9KMI"],"itemData":{"id":33709,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Contemporary Chinese Law","ISBN":"0-674-59483-5","page":"84-117","publisher":"Harvard University Press","title":"Interviewing Chinese Refugees: Indispensable Aid to Legal Research on China","author":[{"family":"Cohen","given":"Jerome Alan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. People migrate for multiple reasons – from conflict to environmental degradation, access to better resources such as fertile land ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UAXLnzcP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Crawford & Kujirakwinja, 2016)","plainCitation":"(A. Crawford & Kujirakwinja, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33549,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/66AC9CPR"],"itemData":{"id":33549,"type":"book","publisher":"International Institute for Sustainable Development","title":"Migration and Conservation in the Misotshi, Kabogo Ecosystem","author":[{"family":"Crawford","given":"Alec"},{"family":"Kujirakwinja","given":"Deo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} disparities between countries, and the desire for economic or education or health or social opportunities.  
      Migration from regions of areas with high youth populations into high-income countries with older workforces is a key factor in global migration ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dtKsDMdn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hugo, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Hugo, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33560,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6GI58VWQ"],"itemData":{"id":33560,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"21–33","title":"Future demographic change and its interactions with migration and climate change","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Hugo","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and has attendant effects on natural resources (see migration section). These areas with high youth populations also directly overlap with areas of high risk to climate change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2BzaBlcB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hugo, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Hugo, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33560,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6GI58VWQ"],"itemData":{"id":33560,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"21–33","title":"Future demographic change and its interactions with migration and climate change","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Hugo","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Conflict and migration - Many migration patterns caused by political conflict are leading to unsustainable patterns of wild species use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kobSx0y6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Begemann, 2020; Black, 1994; M. Geiger & P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}coud, 2020; Gushulak, 2021; Hugo, 1996; D. Jacobson, 1998; K. E. McNamara, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Begemann, 2020; Black, 1994; M. Geiger & Pécoud, 2020; Gushulak, 2021; Hugo, 1996; D. Jacobson, 1998; K. E. McNamara, 2007)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33577,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EYY9YRHX"],"itemData":{"id":33577,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EClinicalMedicine","ISSN":"2589-5370","journalAbbreviation":"EClinicalMedicine","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"100345","title":"Accumulated environmental risk in young refugees–A prospective evaluation","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Begemann","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Seidel","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Poustka","given":"Luise"},{"family":"Ehrenreich","given":"Hannelore"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":33582,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G5SX5M5F"],"itemData":{"id":33582,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Management","ISSN":"0301-4797","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Environmental Management","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"261-277","title":"Forced migration and environmental change: the impact of refugees on host environments","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Black","given":"Richard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}},{"id":33585,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2QELLB9D"],"itemData":{"id":33585,"type":"book","ISBN":"3-030-32975-5","publisher":"Springer","title":"The International Organization for Migration: The New'UN Migration Agency'in Critical Perspective","author":[{"family":"Geiger","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Pécoud","given":"Antoine"}],"issued":{"date-par