rran","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":33574,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XNQ9HBZY"],"itemData":{"id":33574,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"182–191","title":"Measuring the environmental dimensions of human migration: The demographer's toolkit","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Fussell","given":"E."},{"family":"Hunter","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Gray","given":"C.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is evident in forest transition trends, especially in the tropics, where deforestation has been partly (but significantly) driven by migration into the forest frontier ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3fKQ8KlY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rudel, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Rudel, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33554,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K8FDPFCC"],"itemData":{"id":33554,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ambio","language":"en","page":"1–11","title":"Whither the forest transition? Climate change, policy responses, and redistributed forests in the twenty-first century","author":[{"family":"Rudel","given":"T.K."},{"family":"Meyfroidt","given":"P."},{"family":"Chazdon","given":"R."},{"family":"Bongers","given":"F."},{"family":"Sloan","given":"S."},{"family":"Grau","given":"H.R."},{"family":"Van Holt","given":"T."},{"family":"Schneider","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, yet forest recovery is seen across global regions as a consequence of people leaving farming and rural areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rnEN3h6H","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aide, 2013; Nanni, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Aide, 2013; Nanni, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33558,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TKYINLIC"],"itemData":{"id":33558,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"148–159","title":"The neotropical reforestation hotspots: A biophysical and socioeconomic typology of contemporary forest expansion","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Nanni","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Sloan","given":"S."},{"family":"Aide","given":"T.M."},{"family":"Graesser","given":"J."},{"family":"Edwards","given":"D."},{"family":"Grau","given":"H.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":33701,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2UULXNXE"],"itemData":{"id":33701,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biotropica","ISSN":"0006-3606","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Biotropica","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"262-271","title":"Deforestation and reforestation of l atin a merica and the c aribbean (2001–2010)","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Aide","given":"T Mitchell"},{"family":"Clark","given":"Matthew L"},{"family":"Grau","given":"H Ricardo"},{"family":"López?Carr","given":"David"},{"family":"Levy","given":"Marc A"},{"family":"Redo","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Bonilla?Moheno","given":"Martha"},{"family":"Riner","given":"George"},{"family":"Andrade?Núñez","given":"María J"},{"family":"Muñiz","given":"María"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In migrant-sending areas, depopulation improves environmental outcomes through reduced resource use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QSo1fnQm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aide & Grau, 2004; Myerson, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Aide & Grau, 2004; Myerson, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33702,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5UAWFZBQ"],"itemData":{"id":33702,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"5692","language":"en","page":"1915–1916","title":"Globalization, migration, and Latin American ecosystems","volume":"305","author":[{"family":"Aide","given":"T.M."},{"family":"Grau","given":"H.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":33533,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NEYEG7GI"],"itemData":{"id":33533,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Available at SSRN 3058134","journalAbbreviation":"Available at SSRN 3058134","title":"Policy analysis with endogenous migration decisions: The case of left-behind children in China","author":[{"family":"Myerson","given":"Rebecca"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but can impact biodiversity in less obvious (and not always positive) ways through changes to landscape use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zfsUFK7T","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Davis & Lopez-Carr, 2014; Lambin & Meyfroidt, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Davis & Lopez-Carr, 2014; Lambin & Meyfroidt, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33562,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J2RLCB2W"],"itemData":{"id":33562,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"9","language":"en","page":"3465–3472","title":"Global land use change, economic globalization, and the looming land scarcity","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Lambin","given":"E.F."},{"family":"Meyfroidt","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33564,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EPEMQ4RB"],"itemData":{"id":33564,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land use policy","language":"en","page":"319–329","title":"Migration, remittances and smallholder decision-making: Implications for land use and livelihood change in Central America","volume":"36","author":[{"family":"Davis","given":"J."},{"family":"Lopez-Carr","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. But out-migration facilitates the aging and shrinking of rural populations, thus potentially weakening the social organization, environmental knowledge, and community institutions that underpin sustainable land management systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5OoUsI8z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Robson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Robson, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33552,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9MAMJDT2"],"itemData":{"id":33552,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Communities Surviving Migration: Village Governance, Environment, and Cultural Survival in Indigenous Mexico","event-place":"Abingdon, UK","language":"en","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"Abingdon, UK","title":"Communities surviving migration: The migration-environment-community nexus","author":[{"family":"Robson","given":"J.P."},{"family":"Klooster","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Hernandez-Diaz","given":"J."}],"editor":[{"family":"Robson","given":"J.P."},{"family":"Klooster","given":"D.J."},{"family":"Hernandez-Diaz","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Depopulated communities may also be more vulnerable to land grabs and the incursion of ecologically damaging resource practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ikk3Qm5g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Padilla 2012)","plainCitation":"(C. Padilla 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33557,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PHSVJGCM"],"itemData":{"id":33557,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Commons Strategy Group","language":"en","title":"Mining as a threat to the commons: The case of South America","URL":"http://wealthofthecommons.org/essay/mining-threat-commons-case-south-america","author":[{"family":"Padilla","given":"C."}],"editor":[{"family":"Bollier","given":"D."},{"family":"Helfrich","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In migrant-receiving areas, the arrival of people can help to alleviate such shortfalls. Yet where in-migration takes place in response to the presence of high-value resources/commodities, it drives environmental degradation in often ecologically sensitive areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LyFNhWUu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pimm, 2014; Wittemyer, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Pimm, 2014; Wittemyer, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33394,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MP9CZWCD"],"itemData":{"id":33394,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"6187","language":"en","page":"1246752","title":"The biodiversity of species and their rates of extinction, distribution, and protection","volume":"344","author":[{"family":"Pimm","given":"S.L."},{"family":"Jenkins","given":"C.N."},{"family":"Abell","given":"R."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"T.M."},{"family":"Gittleman","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Joppa","given":"L.N."},{"family":"Sexton","given":"J.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":33567,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6HH7P5N8"],"itemData":{"id":33567,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000341625600041","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1403984111","ISSN":"0027-8424","issue":"36","page":"13117-13121","title":"Illegal killing for ivory drives global decline in African elephants","URL":"://WOS:000341625600041","volume":"111","author":[{"family":"Wittemyer","given":"G."},{"family":"Northrup","given":"J. M."},{"family":"Blanc","given":"J."},{"family":"Douglas-Hamilton","given":"I."},{"family":"Omondi","given":"P."},{"family":"Burnham","given":"K. P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Other migration patterns- The migration of people underpins, and can exacerbate, demographic/population change and urbanization trends. In 1990, 153 million people lived outside their country of origin ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uHpBqc3c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(International Organization for Migration, 2017)","plainCitation":"(International Organization for Migration, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33590,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H28IWRMP"],"itemData":{"id":33590,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Berlin","publisher":"IOM","title":"MigFacts: International Migration. https://gmdac.iom.int","author":[{"family":"International Organization for Migration","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. By 2017 this stood at approximately 258 million ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LZOUS8G3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(OECD, 2018)","plainCitation":"(OECD, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33571,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GXQC8GQP"],"itemData":{"id":33571,"type":"personal_communication","title":"Green growth and sustainable development - OECD","URL":"http://www.oecd.org/greengrowth/","author":[{"literal":"OECD"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Of these migrants, close to 35% moved from one developing country to another, while 34% moved from developing to developed countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kxknOGrZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(International Organization for Migration, 2017)","plainCitation":"(International Organization for Migration, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33590,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H28IWRMP"],"itemData":{"id":33590,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Berlin","publisher":"IOM","title":"MigFacts: International Migration. https://gmdac.iom.int","author":[{"family":"International Organization for Migration","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition to international migration flows, internal, migration is highly significant – especially for movements from rural to urban areas – although data on numbers of internal migrants is much harder to ascertain ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Y0LTvQWf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cohen, 2013; International Organization for Migration, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Cohen, 2013; International Organization for Migration, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33709,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HDVS9KMI"],"itemData":{"id":33709,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Contemporary Chinese Law","ISBN":"0-674-59483-5","page":"84-117","publisher":"Harvard University Press","title":"Interviewing Chinese Refugees: Indispensable Aid to Legal Research on China","author":[{"family":"Cohen","given":"Jerome Alan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33590,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H28IWRMP"],"itemData":{"id":33590,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Berlin","publisher":"IOM","title":"MigFacts: International Migration. https://gmdac.iom.int","author":[{"family":"International Organization for Migration","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. People migrate for multiple reasons – from conflict to environmental degradation, disparities between countries, and the desire for economic or education or health or social opportunities.
	Rural migration patterns - For rural areas, the impacts of migration and urbanization are also complex and varied. Migration, for example, can have positive or negative environmental implications, with different scenarios expected for sending versus receiving areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c0rz9aPJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Curran, 2002; Fussell, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Curran, 2002; Fussell, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33565,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B3Z8SBAJ"],"itemData":{"id":33565,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Population and Development Review","language":"en","page":"89–125","title":"Migration, social capital, and the environment: Considering migrant selectivity and networks in relation to coastal ecosystems","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Curran","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":33574,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XNQ9HBZY"],"itemData":{"id":33574,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"182–191","title":"Measuring the environmental dimensions of human migration: The demographer's toolkit","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Fussell","given":"E."},{"family":"Hunter","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Gray","given":"C.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, in the tropics (e.g., Indonesia, Amazon) legal and illegal logging, illegal mining and clearly of land for commercial cropping (e.g., soybean farming) has been driven, in part, by migration of marginalized populations (i.e., those living in poverty) to seek out new livelihood and economic opportunities. In some areas, reforestation and other forms of forest recovery are being evidenced as as a consequence of people leaving farming and rural areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sMY7hB3E","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aide, 2013; Grau & Aide, 2008; Nanni, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Aide, 2013; Grau & Aide, 2008; Nanni, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33701,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2UULXNXE"],"itemData":{"id":33701,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biotropica","ISSN":"0006-3606","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Biotropica","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"262-271","title":"Deforestation and reforestation of l atin a merica and the c aribbean (2001–2010)","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Aide","given":"T Mitchell"},{"family":"Clark","given":"Matthew L"},{"family":"Grau","given":"H Ricardo"},{"family":"López?Carr","given":"David"},{"family":"Levy","given":"Marc A"},{"family":"Redo","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Bonilla?Moheno","given":"Martha"},{"family":"Riner","given":"George"},{"family":"Andrade?Núñez","given":"María J"},{"family":"Muñiz","given":"María"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33561,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E3EPYLEA"],"itemData":{"id":33561,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecol Soc","DOI":"10.1057/9780230603554.CrossRefGoogle","issue":"2","language":"en","title":"Globalization and land use transitions in Latin America","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1057/9780230603554.CrossRefGoogle","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Grau","given":"H.R."},{"family":"Aide","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":33558,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TKYINLIC"],"itemData":{"id":33558,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"148–159","title":"The neotropical reforestation hotspots: A biophysical and socioeconomic typology of contemporary forest expansion","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Nanni","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Sloan","given":"S."},{"family":"Aide","given":"T.M."},{"family":"Graesser","given":"J."},{"family":"Edwards","given":"D."},{"family":"Grau","given":"H.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In migrant-sending areas, depopulation improves environmental outcomes because of reduced resource use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9YFICpdS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Myerson, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Myerson, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33533,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NEYEG7GI"],"itemData":{"id":33533,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Available at SSRN 3058134","journalAbbreviation":"Available at SSRN 3058134","title":"Policy analysis with endogenous migration decisions: The case of left-behind children in China","author":[{"family":"Myerson","given":"Rebecca"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but impacts biodiversity in less obvious (and not always positive) ways ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sv9PMNwI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aide, 2013; Robson & Berkes, 2011; Rozendaal, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Aide, 2013; Robson & Berkes, 2011; Rozendaal, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33701,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2UULXNXE"],"itemData":{"id":33701,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biotropica","ISSN":"0006-3606","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Biotropica","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"262-271","title":"Deforestation and reforestation of l atin a merica and the c aribbean (2001–2010)","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Aide","given":"T Mitchell"},{"family":"Clark","given":"Matthew L"},{"family":"Grau","given":"H Ricardo"},{"family":"López?Carr","given":"David"},{"family":"Levy","given":"Marc A"},{"family":"Redo","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Bonilla?Moheno","given":"Martha"},{"family":"Riner","given":"George"},{"family":"Andrade?Núñez","given":"María J"},{"family":"Muñiz","given":"María"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33553,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/63XLX2N2"],"itemData":{"id":33553,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"844–854","title":"Exploring some of the myths of land use change: Can rural to urban migration drive declines in biodiversity?","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Robson","given":"J.P."},{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33555,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6J6CSGIB"],"itemData":{"id":33555,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science advances","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"3114","title":"Biodiversity recovery of Neotropical secondary forests","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Rozendaal","given":"D.M."},{"family":"Bongers","given":"F."},{"family":"Aide","given":"T.M."},{"family":"Alvarez-Dávila","given":"E."},{"family":"Ascarrunz","given":"N."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Becknell","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Bentos","given":"T.V."},{"family":"Brancalion","given":"P.H."},{"family":"Cabral","given":"G.A."},{"family":"Calvo-Rodriguez","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, migration of males to urban areas has been found to result in the increasing feminisation of natural resource collection which may impact the contribution of natural resources to households ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hdABPlkW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Z. Zhu, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Z. Zhu, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33538,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2P324VRY"],"itemData":{"id":33538,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","ISSN":"1389-9341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"102154","title":"How feminization of forest management drives households' adoption of technologies: Evidence from non-timber forest products operations in China","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Zhu","given":"Zhen"},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Jun"},{"family":"Li","given":"Bowei"},{"family":"Shen","given":"Yueqin"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Yaoqi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The drivers of migration into rural areas including poverty, but also landscape and resource and degradation due to over harvesting, industrialization and climate change. Migration can also be caused by land use conflict and political unrest. Land use conflicts can range from legal displacements of people and the erosion of their livelihoods due to industrialization or can be associated with large scale conflicts (e.g., ethnic and political violence).  
      Voluntary or forcible removal of populations due to the imposition of large scale commercial agricultural, hydro-electric dams and mining projects, roads and associated infrastructure is a major concern. Indeed roads, railways and changes in transportation corridors can lead to relocation, new patterns of settlement and different patterns of use of wild species. 
      
4.2.3.3. Social organization 
Key messages:
* Societies that have developed over the long term with a strong dependence upon place and resources, have well organized and developed systems of natural resources management including rules related to the sustainability of species (well established) {4.2.3.3.1}
* Social values expressed at different scales shape perceptions of wild species and the perceived benefits and risks of their use (well established) {4.2.3.3.2}.
* Social inequity and poverty are a major driver of unsustainable use however, it is the structures that have created the inequity that are the greater driver (established but incomplete) {4.2.3.3.3}.
* To face global changes, new individualistic/opportunistic strategies and competition are developing, calling into question traditional social structures (norms, values, institutions) and contributing to the overexploitation of nature (established but incomplete) {4.2.3.3.3}.

      Social structure refers to the architecture and dynamics of society; some key dimensions of structure include institutions and social norms, cooperation, social cohesion, involvement, sense of community. The Organization for Economic Co-operation and Development describes a cohesive society as one which “works towards the well-being of all its members, fights exclusion and marginalization, creates a sense of belonging, promotes trust, and offers its members the opportunity of upward social mobility” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tPIFo5nT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(OECD, 2011)","plainCitation":"(OECD, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33705,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SXQPJLIZ"],"itemData":{"id":33705,"type":"book","collection-title":"Perspectives on Global Development","ISBN":"978-92-64-11314-5","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1787/persp_glob_dev-2012-en","publisher":"OECD","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Perspectives on Global Development 2012: Social Cohesion in a Shifting World","title-short":"Perspectives on Global Development 2012","URL":"https://www.oecd-ilibrary.org/development/perspectives-on-global-development-2012_persp_glob_dev-2012-en","author":[{"literal":"OECD"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",5,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",11,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are different degrees of rigidity (versus flexibility) in social organization (i.e., loosely organized to tightly organized) and different degrees of complexity and specialization.  The units that structure a society (e.g., groups, institutions) are generally studied together with the dynamics of social change including how societies innovate, adapt over time and cope with extremal stresses such as a decline in a valued wild species. Those involved in environmental sociology and other disciplines concern with the environment increasingly consider the function of society in terms of a system and the interconnectedness of ecosystems and social systems (i.e., the social-ecological system) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DqFT0bxR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. Berkes 1998; Elinor Ostrom 2009a)","plainCitation":"(F. Berkes 1998; Elinor Ostrom 2009a)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32651,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E33G332J"],"itemData":{"id":32651,"type":"book","event-place":"London","publisher":"Taylor & Francis","publisher-place":"London","title":"Sacred Ecology. Traditional Ecological Knowledge and Resource Management","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":36,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2NACCRB4"],"itemData":{"id":36,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1172133","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5939","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"419-422","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A General Framework for Analyzing Sustainability of Social-Ecological Systems","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1172133","volume":"325","author":[{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",7,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (see chapter 1).  
      A critical consideration in social systems is how individuals and institutions learn and adapt their practices (e.g., hunting, trapping, fishing) and uses to changing ecological conditions including variation and change in the health, abundance and distribution of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VXgVZnWa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pahl-Wostl, 2010; Reed, 2010; Sigmund, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Pahl-Wostl, 2010; Reed, 2010; Sigmund, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33583,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MNK4WFM2"],"itemData":{"id":33583,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental science & policy","ISSN":"1462-9011","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental science & policy","note":"number: 7\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"571-581","title":"Analyzing complex water governance regimes: the management and transition framework","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Pahl-Wostl","given":"Claudia"},{"family":"Holtz","given":"Georg"},{"family":"Kastens","given":"Britta"},{"family":"Knieper","given":"Christian"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":33710,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5EXDCZXP"],"itemData":{"id":33710,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and society","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Ecology and society","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: JSTOR","title":"What is social learning?","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Reed","given":"Mark S"},{"family":"Evely","given":"Anna C"},{"family":"Cundill","given":"Georgina"},{"family":"Fazey","given":"Ioan"},{"family":"Glass","given":"Jayne"},{"family":"Laing","given":"Adele"},{"family":"Newig","given":"Jens"},{"family":"Parrish","given":"Brad"},{"family":"Prell","given":"Christina"},{"family":"Raymond","given":"Chris"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":33711,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NIMXQ8FT"],"itemData":{"id":33711,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","ISSN":"1476-4687","issue":"7308","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","note":"number: 7308\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"861-863","title":"Social learning promotes institutions for governing the commons","volume":"466","author":[{"family":"Sigmund","given":"Karl"},{"family":"De Silva","given":"Hannelore"},{"family":"Traulsen","given":"Arne"},{"family":"Hauert","given":"Christoph"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Drawing on the large literature on social learning and natural resource management (and social-ecological systems), it is evident that individual and institutional learning is key to sustainable use outcomes in almost every region of the globe and for a large number of wild species. 

4.2.3.3.1. Social organization and place
The practices and uses of wild species by different social groups can be defined as social-ecological systems, many of which are highly complex and characterized by significant uncertainty, non-linearity and self-organization.  In other words, they are messy and difficult to manage.  For example, populations of wild species can vary significantly over time and the behavior of those who harvest the population can also vary depending on many other factors (e.g., the availability of other species to harvest, alternative economic opportunities, values and norms etc. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Vac9Yhr5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2010; Steward, 1968; Zimmerer, 2006)","plainCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2010; Steward, 1968; Zimmerer, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34331,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T3K7YP5V"],"itemData":{"id":34331,"type":"book","abstract":"\\"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, and a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods, generate revenue for companies and governments, and have a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, there is little information available for those seeking to develop effective policy frameworks and regulation. This book addresses that shortage with information and recommendations on the drafting, content and implementation of NTFP policies, and the broader issues of governance associated with these products. It reviews the diverse elements that combine to create laws and policies that promote sustainable and equitable management, trade and use of species. Drawing on a wealth of unique case studies from around the world, this volume examines experiences with NTFP regulation, including its sometimes unintended consequences. It looks at economic factors, the interface between traditional and western knowledge and legal systems, and relationships between NTFP regulation, land tenure and resource rights, as well as power and equity imbalances. The volume includes a review of available literature and resources, plus an annotated bibliography linked to the People and Plants International website.\\"--Publisher's description","call-number":"SD543 .W55 2010","collection-title":"People and Plants International conservation series","event-place":"London ; Washington, DC","ISBN":"978-1-84407-500-3","language":"en","note":"OCLC: ocn499130265","number-of-pages":"393","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London ; Washington, DC","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Wild product governance: finding policies that work for non-timber forest products","title-short":"Wild product governance","editor":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":33539,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GHJ7QFL6"],"itemData":{"id":33539,"type":"article-journal","title":"Cultural ecology","author":[{"family":"Steward","given":"JH"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1968"]]}}},{"id":33523,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PYGPIVQL"],"itemData":{"id":33523,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Progress in Human Geography","ISSN":"0309-1325","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Progress in Human Geography","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Sage Publications Sage CA: Thousand Oaks, CA","page":"63-78","title":"Cultural ecology: at the interface with political ecology-the new geographies of environmental conservation and globalization","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Zimmerer","given":"Karl S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      The extent to which social structures are organized in relation to a particular kind of place, species or ecosystem also affects the extent to which social structures affect sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"e6DKZ6dT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2011)","plainCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32713,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4T7ZS3GU"],"itemData":{"id":32713,"type":"chapter","abstract":"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, ?bers, and fuel and for a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods and generate revenue for companies and governments, and their use has a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, throughout the world, NTFPs have been both overlooked and poorly regulated by governments. Inappropriate policies have not only led to over-exploitation but also generated new forms of inequity. Drawing upon cases from around the world, this chapter reviews these experiences and provides information to support new policy approaches toward NTFP regulation and the broader issues of governance associated with these products.","container-title":"Non-Timber Forest Products in the Global Context","event-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","ISBN":"978-3-642-17982-2","language":"en","note":"collection-title: Tropical Forestry\\nDOI: 10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_11","page":"227-253","publisher":"Springer Berlin Heidelberg","publisher-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Regulating Complexity: Policies for the Governance of Non-timber Forest Products","title-short":"Regulating Complexity","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_11","volume":"7","editor":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Sheona"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"}],"author":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Societies that are more disconnected from place are thought to have less interest in protecting or conserving the species. This is sometimes framed as “sense of place” defined as “the collection of meanings, beliefs, symbols, values, and feelings that individuals and groups associate with a particular locality” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KUf5KwpS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. R. Williams & Stewart, 1998)","plainCitation":"(D. R. Williams & Stewart, 1998)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33524,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3JHF3UZ6"],"itemData":{"id":33524,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of forestry","ISSN":"0022-1201","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of forestry","note":"number: 5\\npublisher: Oxford University Press","page":"18-23","title":"Sense of place: An elusive concept that is finding a home in ecosystem management","volume":"96","author":[{"family":"Williams","given":"Daniel R"},{"family":"Stewart","given":"Susan I"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It “reflects not only experiences with places but also the cultural, religious, historical, and personal meanings of places and the power and economic relationships that shape historical and current interactions with places” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"35lPeboN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chapin III & Knapp, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Chapin III & Knapp, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33704,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PTXFTHVH"],"itemData":{"id":33704,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science & Policy","ISSN":"1462-9011","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Science & Policy","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"38-46","title":"Sense of place: A process for identifying and negotiating potentially contested visions of sustainability","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Chapin","given":"F Stuart"},{"family":"Knapp","given":"Corrine N"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As such sense of place shapes the way wild species are defined (including imagined), understood and valued which in turn shapes use. People who pass through a place as a tourist will have a much different set of norms related to care and use of wild species than a property owner ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SSTNYqVT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cross, 2001; Tonge, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Cross, 2001; Tonge, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33520,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DIYE2Y47"],"itemData":{"id":33520,"type":"paper-conference","event-title":"12th Headwaters Conference, Western State College","title":"Private property rights versus scenic views: A battle over place attachments","author":[{"family":"Cross","given":"Jennifer E"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":33525,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5FKJ96M3"],"itemData":{"id":33525,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Travel Research","ISSN":"0047-2875","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Travel Research","note":"number: 6\\npublisher: Sage Publications Sage CA: Los Angeles, CA","page":"730-743","title":"The effect of place attachment on pro-environment behavioral intentions of visitors to coastal natural area tourist destinations","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Tonge","given":"Joanna"},{"family":"Ryan","given":"Maria M"},{"family":"Moore","given":"Susan A"},{"family":"Beckley","given":"Lynnath E"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, who, if only engaged for a short-term period, may in turn behave differently than a long-term property owner or an indigenous person with a long term and multi-generational relationship to place ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lh7nqOCq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chapin & Knapp, 2015; Vaske & Kobrin, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Chapin & Knapp, 2015; Vaske & Kobrin, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33704,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PTXFTHVH"],"itemData":{"id":33704,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Science & Policy","ISSN":"1462-9011","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Science & Policy","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"38-46","title":"Sense of place: A process for identifying and negotiating potentially contested visions of sustainability","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Chapin","given":"F Stuart"},{"family":"Knapp","given":"Corrine N"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":33516,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T6MX84YN"],"itemData":{"id":33516,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of environmental education","ISSN":"0095-8964","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"The Journal of environmental education","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"16-21","title":"Place attachment and environmentally responsible behavior","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Vaske","given":"Jerry J"},{"family":"Kobrin","given":"Katherine C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      “Sense of place does not always promote stewardship, however, because attitudes may not lead to actions, some actions do not promote sustainability, and different place identities in the same place may lead to different stewardship goals (e.g., conservation vs. development) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rqciiH3x","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chapin III, F. S., & Knapp, C. N. 2015)","plainCitation":"(Chapin III, F. S., & Knapp, C. N. 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33550,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XJY3CIX3"],"itemData":{"id":33550,"type":"article-newspaper","title":"Sense of place: A process for identifying and negotiating potentially contested visions of sustainability. Environmental Science & Policy, 53, 38-46","author":[{"family":"Chapin III, F. S., & Knapp, C. N.","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There is much evidence that the longer one lives within, or frequents a place that is associated with positive personal experiences, the greater the attachment to place and in turn the greater degree of conservation-oriented behavior (e.g., lack of vandalism, less waste, the greater the degree of investment of resources in stewardship). This is strengthened where property rights arrangements create incentives for care and investment (i.e., security of ownership creates greater incentives for conservation). Self-efficacy, or the belief that one has the capacity effect a particular change, can influence whether intentions translate to behaviors consistent with sustainable use.  Sense of place and associated pro-sustainable behavior through addressing issues of open access (i.e., securing property rights), education about the specific behaviors and opportunities to improve sustainable use (i.e., to nurture self-efficacy).  Globalization and the transience and high degree of mobility tend to lead to poorer sense of place but may be positive if it leads to multiple kinds of place attachments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"A77G1XO1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chapin III, F. S., & Knapp, C. N. 2015)","plainCitation":"(Chapin III, F. S., & Knapp, C. N. 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33550,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XJY3CIX3"],"itemData":{"id":33550,"type":"article-newspaper","title":"Sense of place: A process for identifying and negotiating potentially contested visions of sustainability. Environmental Science & Policy, 53, 38-46","author":[{"family":"Chapin III, F. S., & Knapp, C. N.","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      
Box 4.21. Sense of place and sustainability 

The following dimensions of sense of place and its effects on sustainability:
* Sense of place best motivates stewardship in homogeneous communities.
* Multiple senses of the same place can generate conflict.
* Globalization and human mobility may foster commitment to more places.
* These trends provide new stewardship opportunities at local-to-global scales
Reproduced from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"i9sXJpey","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chapin III, F. S., & Knapp, C. N. 2015)","plainCitation":"(Chapin III, F. S., & Knapp, C. N. 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33550,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XJY3CIX3"],"itemData":{"id":33550,"type":"article-newspaper","title":"Sense of place: A process for identifying and negotiating potentially contested visions of sustainability. Environmental Science & Policy, 53, 38-46","author":[{"family":"Chapin III, F. S., & Knapp, C. N.","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}).


4.2.3.3.2. Social norms
Social constructions of wild species
Wild species are variously defined across cultures and societies. In addition to natural science taxonomies and characterizations, wild species are socially constructed through social-ecological interactions, knowledge shared through research and governance, as well as through popular media.  For example, in North American wolf populations have long been considered a threat to people, a perception that was place on experiences of farmers and risks to livestock but has been perpetuated by cultural narratives (e.g., children’s fables of the “big bad wolf”) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DgcRN4WU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lappalainen, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Lappalainen, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33703,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BEQRPA7K"],"itemData":{"id":33703,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ISLE: Interdisciplinary Studies in Literature and Environment","DOI":"10.1093/isle/isz065","ISSN":"1076-0962, 1759-1090","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"744-767","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recall of the Fairy-Tale Wolf: “Little Red Riding Hood” in the Dialogic Tension of Contemporary Wolf Politics in the US West","title-short":"Recall of the Fairy-Tale Wolf","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/isle/article/26/3/744/5548006","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Lappalainen","given":"Kaisa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. One interesting trend of the risk society literature is the extent to which some wild species which might have historically been viewed as a benefit and a contribution to well-being (and cared for accordingly) have been reframed as threats or risks to human health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wCH3auPn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Beck, 1992; Dempsey, 2013; Sidhu, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Beck, 1992; Dempsey, 2013; Sidhu, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33517,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LKGX5I3A"],"itemData":{"id":33517,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Theory, culture & society","ISSN":"0263-2764","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Theory, culture & society","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Sage Publications","page":"97-123","title":"From industrial society to the risk society: Questions of survival, social structure and ecological enlightenment","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Beck","given":"Ulrich"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}},{"id":33518,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W325E8BG"],"itemData":{"id":33518,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geoforum","ISSN":"0016-7185","journalAbbreviation":"Geoforum","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"41-51","title":"Biodiversity loss as material risk: Tracking the changing meanings and materialities of biodiversity conservation","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Dempsey","given":"Jessica"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33519,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VD6VAWSC"],"itemData":{"id":33519,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Regulatory Toxicology and Pharmacology","ISSN":"0273-2300","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Regulatory Toxicology and Pharmacology","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"336-344","title":"Health benefits and potential risks related to consumption of fish or fish oil","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Sidhu","given":"Kirpal S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, in many areas of North America, there are advisories issued by government related to harvesting and consuming many fish species due to the bioaccumulation of contaminants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Uvpe7wBr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Burger, 2000; Chess, 2005; Oken, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Burger, 2000; Chess, 2005; Oken, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33526,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4HNJIN85"],"itemData":{"id":33526,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Planning and Management","ISSN":"0964-0568","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Environmental Planning and Management","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"471-488","title":"Consumption advisories and compliance: the fishing public and the deamplification of risk","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Burger","given":"Joanna"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":33521,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MWW2KNK9"],"itemData":{"id":33521,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society and natural resources","ISSN":"0894-1920","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Society and natural resources","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"267-278","title":"Speaking like a state: environmental justice and fish consumption advisories","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Chess","given":"Caron"},{"family":"Burger","given":"Joanna"},{"family":"McDermott","given":"Melanie Hughes"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32630,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NERC6EY4"],"itemData":{"id":32630,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Health Perspectives","DOI":"10.1289/ehp.1104500","ISSN":"0091-6765, 1552-9924","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Health Perspectives","language":"en","page":"790-798","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Which Fish Should I Eat? Perspectives Influencing Fish Consumption Choices","title-short":"Which Fish Should I Eat?","URL":"https://ehp.niehs.nih.gov/doi/10.1289/ehp.1104500","volume":"120","author":[{"family":"Oken","given":"Emily"},{"family":"Choi","given":"Anna L."},{"family":"Karagas","given":"Margaret R."},{"family":"Mariën","given":"Koenraad"},{"family":"Rheinberger","given":"Christoph M."},{"family":"Schoeny","given":"Rita"},{"family":"Sunderland","given":"Elsie"},{"family":"Korrick","given":"Susan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although there is variation in compliance with advisories, on the whole they lead to decreases in harvesting and other kinds of impacts (i.e., increase of catch and release). Advisories can also lead to an amplification of risk; this not only in the area specifically affected by an advisory but in other regions.  For example, in Nunavut, concerns about contaminants in some species and some areas, led to changes in dietary patterns that had other kinds of consequences including directions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xOFTTnEW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Furgal, 2005; O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Neil, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Furgal, 2005; O’Neil, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33712,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HW2ZL9FL"],"itemData":{"id":33712,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic","ISSN":"0004-0843","journalAbbreviation":"Arctic","page":"103-114","title":"Digesting the message about contaminants and country foods in the Canadian North: a review and recommendations for future research and action","author":[{"family":"Furgal","given":"CM"},{"family":"Powell","given":"S"},{"family":"Myers","given":"H"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":33513,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F3TQDMJA"],"itemData":{"id":33513,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic Anthropology","ISSN":"0066-6939","journalAbbreviation":"Arctic Anthropology","page":"29-40","title":"Poisoned food: Cultural resistance to the contaminants discourse in Nunavik","author":[{"family":"O'Neil","given":"John D"},{"family":"Elias","given":"Brenda"},{"family":"Yassi","given":"Annalee"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Depending on the degree of dependency on a species for subsistence (or other value), biodiversity in the region and mobility of harvesters, reports of a problem in one area and one species, can cause harvests to adapt and harvest other species, change to a market diet, or move to new areas to find healthier species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AtxymIZm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tisdell & Svizzero, 2015; Winterhalder, 1986; Winterhalder & Smith, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Tisdell & Svizzero, 2015; Winterhalder, 1986; Winterhalder & Smith, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33566,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IHGLYXQY"],"itemData":{"id":33566,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Social Evolution & History","issue":"2","note":"publisher: ???????? ? ???????????? ???????????????? «???????????? «???????»","title":"The persistence of hunting and gathering economies","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Tisdell","given":"Clem"},{"family":"Svizzero","given":"Serge"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":33551,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BBS94ZM9"],"itemData":{"id":33551,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"J. Anthropol. Archaeol","language":"en","page":"369–392","title":"Diet choice, risk, and food sharing in a stochastic environment","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Winterhalder","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1986"]]}}},{"id":33527,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DJKA3AHH"],"itemData":{"id":33527,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"evolutionary anthropology","ISSN":"1060-1538","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"evolutionary anthropology","note":"number: 2","page":"51-72","title":"Analyzing adaptive strategies: Human behavioral ecology at twenty-five","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Winterhalder","given":"Bruce"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Eric Alden"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These kinds of adaptive strategies can have significant impact on diet and health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"geKN3C8T","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Badjeck, 2010; Hovelsrud, 2008; Marushka, 2019; E. B. Ross, 1978)","plainCitation":"(Badjeck, 2010; Hovelsrud, 2008; Marushka, 2019; E. B. Ross, 1978)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33522,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T6YHPA6Q"],"itemData":{"id":33522,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine policy","ISSN":"0308-597X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Marine policy","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"375-383","title":"Impacts of climate variability and change on fishery-based livelihoods","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Badjeck","given":"Marie-Caroline"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H"},{"family":"Halls","given":"Ashley S"},{"family":"Dulvy","given":"Nicholas K"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":33528,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YH33QYKF"],"itemData":{"id":33528,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","ISSN":"1939-5582","issue":"sp2","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Applications","note":"number: sp2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"S135-S147","title":"Marine mammal harvests and other interactions with humans","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Hovelsrud","given":"Grete K"},{"family":"McKenna","given":"Meghan"},{"family":"Huntington","given":"Henry P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":33573,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S2LAD49S"],"itemData":{"id":33573,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0211473","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e0211473","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Potential impacts of climate-related decline of seafood harvest on nutritional status of coastal First Nations in British Columbia, Canada","URL":"http://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0211473","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Marushka","given":"Lesya"},{"family":"Kenny","given":"Tiff-Annie"},{"family":"Batal","given":"Malek"},{"family":"Cheung","given":"William W. L."},{"family":"Fediuk","given":"Karen"},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D."},{"family":"Salomon","given":"Anne K."},{"family":"Sadik","given":"Tonio"},{"family":"Weatherdon","given":"Lauren V."},{"family":"Chan","given":"Hing Man"}],"editor":[{"family":"Belgrano","given":"Andrea"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",2,27]]}}},{"id":33502,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LQMJW8NI"],"itemData":{"id":33502,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Anthropology","ISSN":"0011-3204","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Current Anthropology","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: University of Chicago Press","page":"1-36","title":"Food Taboos, diet, and hunting strategy: the adaptation to animals in amazon cultural ecology [and Comments and Reply]","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Ross","given":"Eric Barry"},{"family":"Arnott","given":"Margaret L"},{"family":"Basso","given":"Ellen B"},{"family":"Beckerman","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Carneiro","given":"Robert L"},{"family":"Forbis","given":"Richard G"},{"family":"Good","given":"Kenneth R"},{"family":"Jensen","given":"Knud-Erik"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Allen"},{"family":"Kaplinski","given":"Jaan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1978"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      There are other implications for sustainable use.  For example, shifts away from the harvest of local fish species for food security has indirectly led to an increase in industrial fishing practice that have impacts on species many thousands of miles away. This pattern is perpetuated by the cognitive dissonance that characterized the global food system; lack of awareness of the ecological and social costs of industrial harvests contributes to perceptions that buying food from the store is healthier than can be harvested locally (i.e., wild species) and in addition that resources from elsewhere are limitless.
      Other aspects of globalization shape and, in turn uses, of wild species. For example, public media related to trapping, seal harvesting, whale harvesting and polar bear hunting has had a significant adverse impact on indigenous peoples and local knowledge in northern Canada whose cultures and economies are interrelated with the health of these species. Protests by animal rights activists coupled with popular films and social media sources that are opposed to animal use have tended to artificially construct wild species in more romantic and moral terms rather than those based on science of the knowledge of indigenous peoples and local communities. This moral position of global authorities against ‘hunting' is a driver of declining interest of younger generations in cultural practices of hunting and associated with other adverse impacts on cultural continuity and well-being of indigenous peoples. 
      
4.2.3.3.3. Livelihoods and development 
A livelihoods approach offers an integrated way of thinking about the way in which societies organize themselves in making a living but also in producing other social, cultural and health well-being benefits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dCWnnrhh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. S. Negi, 2011; Rao & McGowan, 2002)","plainCitation":"(V. S. Negi, 2011; Rao & McGowan, 2002)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33568,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YCZMYFMS"],"itemData":{"id":33568,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The present study aims to document detail information of some of the selected wild edible having enormous potential for livelihood enhancement and socio-economic development by making a variety of value added products. To this end, some of the wild edibles of central Himalaya were selected and prioritized for harnessing their economic potential along with their detail information in terms of distribution, ethnobiology, phenophases and appropriate time of harvesting so as to make communities well aware about the resource availability and their harvesting period round the year. The cost-benefit analysis of each value added product prepared from selected wild edibles was worked out in detail and these analyses revealed that total monetary output, as well as the net return, is very high for all value added products prepared. Since wild edible fruits or other edible parts can be collected from wild free of cost except labour is involved in collection of these wild edibles bio-resources. In addition, information on a participatory action research framework & approaches for promoting participatory conservation of these wild edible species were also highlighted for appropriate management of these resources. The present attempt provides a practical example of sustainable utilization of wild edibles, their potential in livelihood improvement of local people, distribution and phenophases and availability in natural conditions, participatory conservation of these wild edibles may help policy planners at the regional and national levels to link livelihood/socio-economic development with conservation.","container-title":"BIODIVERSITY AND CONSERVATION","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-010-9966-y","ISSN":"0960-3115","issue":"3","language":"English","page":"545-559","title":"Non-timber forest products (NTFPs): a viable option for biodiversity conservation and livelihood enhancement in central Himalaya","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Negi","given":"Vikram S."},{"family":"Maikhuri","given":"R. K."},{"family":"Rawat","given":"L. S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",3]]}}},{"id":33569,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QEYR3A4Y"],"itemData":{"id":33569,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1046/j.1523-1739.2002.01634.x","ISSN":"1523-1739","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"580-583","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Wild-Meat Use, Food Security, Livelihoods, and Conservation","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1046/j.1523-1739.2002.01634.x","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Rao","given":""},{"family":"McGowan","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A livelihood comprises: "the capabilities, assets (stores, resources, claims and access) and activities required for a means of living” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JyWTwlXL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chambers & Conway, 1992)","plainCitation":"(Chambers & Conway, 1992)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34577,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZBJHKXP6"],"itemData":{"id":34577,"type":"book","call-number":"HN49.C6 C483 1992","collection-number":"296","collection-title":"DP","event-place":"Brighton, England","ISBN":"978-0-903715-58-4","number-of-pages":"42","publisher":"Institute of Development Studies","publisher-place":"Brighton, England","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Sustainable rural livelihoods: practical concepts for the 21st century","title-short":"Sustainable rural livelihoods","author":[{"family":"Chambers","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Conway","given":"Gordon"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Following the Brundtland Commission on Environment and Development and then expanded by United Nations Conference on Environment and Development (UNCED) in 1992, a large literature related to livelihoods and the ways in which can might be interpreted as sustainable, expanded, among which stressing the sustainable rural livelihoods and targeting the household level.:
“A livelihood is sustainable which can cope with and recover from stress and shocks, maintain or enhance its capabilities and assets, and provide sustainable livelihood opportunities for the next generation; and which contributes net benefits to other livelihoods at the local and global levels and in the short and long term” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HZPuDUsp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chambers & Conway, 1992)","plainCitation":"(Chambers & Conway, 1992)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34577,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZBJHKXP6"],"itemData":{"id":34577,"type":"book","call-number":"HN49.C6 C483 1992","collection-number":"296","collection-title":"DP","event-place":"Brighton, England","ISBN":"978-0-903715-58-4","number-of-pages":"42","publisher":"Institute of Development Studies","publisher-place":"Brighton, England","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Sustainable rural livelihoods: practical concepts for the 21st century","title-short":"Sustainable rural livelihoods","author":[{"family":"Chambers","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Conway","given":"Gordon"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
  Livelihood typologies vary and are also gendered ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UbwUclyh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Oberhauser & Yeboah, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Oberhauser & Yeboah, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33529,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9PCS9BLD"],"itemData":{"id":33529,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Singapore Journal of Tropical Geography","ISSN":"0129-7619","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Singapore Journal of Tropical Geography","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"22-37","title":"Heavy burdens: Gendered livelihood strategies of porters in Accra, Ghana","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Oberhauser","given":"Ann M"},{"family":"Yeboah","given":"Muriel A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Research and evidence is well developed in almost every global region; and much research highlights the ways in which different social groups are able to create, innovate, adapt and contribute to many dimensions of sustainability including sustainable use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EGqhlImY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Koziell, 2001; S. A. Mainka & Trivedi, 2002; Wollenberg, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Koziell, 2001; S. A. Mainka & Trivedi, 2002; Wollenberg, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33570,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MLIIRKXJ"],"itemData":{"id":33570,"type":"book","event-place":"Gland, Switzerland and Cambridge, UK","number-of-pages":"135","publisher":"IUCN","publisher-place":"Gland, Switzerland and Cambridge, UK","title":"Links between Biodiversity Conservation, Livelihoods and Food Security: The Sustainable use od wild species for meat. IUCN","volume":"vi","editor":[{"family":"Mainka","given":"S.A."},{"family":"Trivedi","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":33699,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3URYR49R"],"itemData":{"id":33699,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Current trends to improve the adaptiveness of community forest management focus on monitoring past actions and emphasize internal dynamics. We show how scenario methods can be used to (1) enable managers to better understand landscape and larger scale forces for change and to work with stakeholders at these levels and (2) improve adaptiveness not only by responding to changes, but also by anticipating them. We review methods related to scenario analysis and discuss how they can be adapted to community management settings to improve the responsiveness and the collaboration among stakeholders. The review is used to identify the key elements of scenario methods that CIFOR will test among communities in Bulungan Regency, East Kalimantan, Indonesia and two villages in the buffer zone of Ranomafana National Park, Madagascar. (C) 2000 Published by Elsevier Science B.V.","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","DOI":"10.1016/S0169-2046(99)00071-7","ISSN":"01692046","page":"65–77","title":"Using scenarios to make decisions about the future: Anticipatory learning for the adaptive co-management of community forests","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Wollenberg","given":"Eva"},{"family":"Edmunds","given":"David"},{"family":"Buck","given":"Louise"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":33515,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5ZRM2H8X"],"itemData":{"id":33515,"type":"book","ISBN":"1-899825-68-1","publisher":"IIED","title":"Diversity not adversity: Sustaining livelihoods with biodiversity","author":[{"family":"Koziell","given":"Izabella"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For the purposes of this assessment, the framed livelihoods around practices of wild species use and discuss how the social organization associated with these practices drives different aspects of sustainability.

Artisanal fishing livelihoods
Small-scale fisheries generally present (some of) the following characteristics: (i) low capital investment, (ii) high labor activities often family or community-based, (iii) no vessel or small size vessel (< 12m and < 10 gross tonnage), (iv) relatively low production, which is household consumed or locally and directly sold and (v) operating close to the shoreline on a single day basis. There is a whole gradient, from the individual fisherman on board his dugout pirogue, to crews of more than 20 men on board large motorized pirogues, all of whom, at least in West Africa, identify themselves as small-scale fishermen.  It is therefore not easy to stick to the "official" definition, if not to show the limits of it in terms of the identity of the actors (how they define themselves) and their evolving or adaptive strategies in the face of a changing environment. The small-scale fishermen are organized in fishing units, comprising from 1 to more than 15 men, most often recruited on a family or community basis. Working relations, both on land and at sea, are traditionally tacitly recognized: thus, for small-scale fishing, the means of production (pirogue, motor, catching gear, etc.) are traditionally the property of the eldest of the lineage, who also acts as captain of the unit (or the canoe); if he is too old to go out to sea again, he entrusts the responsibility to his eldest son. The fishing unit thus includes the captain as well as the crew, traditionally recruited from the extended family (sons, nephews, etc.) or allied members. The operating strategies (or fishing system) are defined by the captain.
      Extensive literature on this topic has shown the importance of these strategies (decision-making processes) on resource management and the adaptive capacity of fishermen (or flexibility of fishing systems) for sustainable resource management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BjBrX52V","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chauveau, 2000; S. Garcia, 2014; Gustavsson, 2017; Kalfagianni, 2013; Mar\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}n & Berkes, 2010; S\\\\uc0\\\\u248{}nvisen, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Chauveau, 2000; S. Garcia, 2014; Gustavsson, 2017; Kalfagianni, 2013; Marín & Berkes, 2010; Sønvisen, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33674,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KYDZPBUZ"],"itemData":{"id":33674,"type":"book","event-place":"Paris","language":"fr","publisher":"Karthala","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Les pêches piroguières en Afrique de l'Ouest : dynamiques institutionnelles : pouvoirs, mobilités, marchés","editor":[{"family":"Chauveau","given":"J.-P."},{"family":"Jul-Larson","given":"E."},{"family":"Chaboud","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":34130,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QPB9BYSE"],"itemData":{"id":34130,"type":"article-journal","call-number":"QH91","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Governance of marine fisheries and biodiversity conservation: interaction and co-evolution","title-short":"Governance of marine fisheries and biodiversity conservation","editor":[{"family":"Garcia","given":"Serge"},{"family":"Rice","given":"J. C."},{"family":"Charles","given":"Anthony Trevor"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":33662,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RD7XM9KA"],"itemData":{"id":33662,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Rural Studies","DOI":"10.1016/j.jrurstud.2016.12.012","language":"en","page":"104–116","title":"Exploring the socio-cultural contexts of fishers and fishing: Developing the concept of the ‘good fisher’","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Gustavsson","given":"M."},{"family":"Riley","given":"M."},{"family":"Morrissey","given":"K."},{"family":"Plater","given":"A.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33665,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6PWFXQAD"],"itemData":{"id":33665,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2012.05.028","issue":"0","language":"en","page":"124–132","title":"Fishing in muddy waters: Exploring the conditions for effective governance of fisheries and aquaculture","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Kalfagianni","given":"A."},{"family":"Pattberg","given":"P."},{"family":"Kannan","given":"R."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C.M."},{"family":"Krishnan","given":"S."},{"family":"Shaanker","given":"R.U."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33661,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PXYCWTC3"],"itemData":{"id":33661,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2010.01.007","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"851–858","title":"Network approach for understanding small-scale fisheries governance: The case of the Chilean coastal co-management system","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Marín","given":"A."},{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":33643,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PRZU52FS"],"itemData":{"id":33643,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Rural Studies","DOI":"10.1016/j.jrurstud.2014.01.011","issue":"0","language":"en","page":"193–203","title":"Contemporary fisher images: Ideologies, policies and diversity","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Sønvisen","given":"S.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is critical in many examples of “small scale fishing” as Cambodia and elsewhere ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"N883BEtM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}n\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, 2006; Marschke & Berkes, 2006; Mullon, 2005; Pauly, 2018; Pauly, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Béné, 2006; Marschke & Berkes, 2006; Mullon, 2005; Pauly, 2018; Pauly, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33809,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6LJHJUQH"],"itemData":{"id":33809,"type":"book","event-place":"Rome, Italy","language":"en","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome, Italy","title":"Small-scale fisheries: assessing their contribution to rural livelihoods in developing countries","volume":"1008","author":[{"family":"Béné","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":33634,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/66Z5YIV8"],"itemData":{"id":33634,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecology and Society","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: JSTOR","title":"Exploring strategies that build livelihood resilience: a case from Cambodia","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Marschke","given":"Melissa J"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":33659,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T55JKS2K"],"itemData":{"id":33659,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"11–120","title":"The dynamics of collapse in world fisheries","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Mullon","given":"C."},{"family":"Fréon","given":"P."},{"family":"Cury","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":33647,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NBI6FH9F"],"itemData":{"id":33647,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.11.010","language":"en","page":"371–374","title":"A vision for marine fisheries in a global blue economy","volume":"87","author":[{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":33646,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3JFS6FJ7"],"itemData":{"id":33646,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Towards sustainability in world fisheries Nature","issue":"6898","language":"en","page":"689–695","volume":"418","author":[{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."},{"family":"Christensen","given":"V."},{"family":"Guénette","given":"S."},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"T.J."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U.R."},{"family":"Walters","given":"C.J."},{"family":"Zeller","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      On the one hand, « responsible » and sustainable strategy and flexibility of the system to face the changes: this flexibility is based on the diversity of the elements that make up the fishing system: several fishing gears, various species targeted according to season and location, etc. Fishermen only take the resources they need for their own consumption and selling the surplus to meet their basic needs (or, at least, the reproduction of the system), with well-established and shared rules of use and access to resources, and a system for allocating shares when they return from the tides. 
      In the other hand: with modernization (motorization, mechanized gear, refrigeration on board, etc.) and professionalization (from part-time to full-time), the specialization of new or « young » fishing units, more concerned with profitability, crews recruited on a capital base (employees) targeting a single species with less selective catching techniques (e.g., large purse seines) for a specific export market (e.g., shark fins to supply the South-East Asian market). These opportunistic strategies are unsustainable, leading not only to biodiversity erosion, but also to increased vulnerability of fishermen and conflicts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Pkxa9NCB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mullon, 2005; Pauly, 2018; Pauly, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Mullon, 2005; Pauly, 2018; Pauly, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33659,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T55JKS2K"],"itemData":{"id":33659,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"11–120","title":"The dynamics of collapse in world fisheries","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Mullon","given":"C."},{"family":"Fréon","given":"P."},{"family":"Cury","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":33646,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3JFS6FJ7"],"itemData":{"id":33646,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Towards sustainability in world fisheries Nature","issue":"6898","language":"en","page":"689–695","volume":"418","author":[{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."},{"family":"Christensen","given":"V."},{"family":"Guénette","given":"S."},{"family":"Pitcher","given":"T.J."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U.R."},{"family":"Walters","given":"C.J."},{"family":"Zeller","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":33647,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NBI6FH9F"],"itemData":{"id":33647,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.11.010","language":"en","page":"371–374","title":"A vision for marine fisheries in a global blue economy","volume":"87","author":[{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Faced with the crisis of fishery resources, it is also important to note the capacity of fishermen to innovate from a social and institutional point of view, to set up associations and cooperatives on a regional, national or international scale, to have their rights recognized on their territories (e.g., fishermen's committees which manage Marine Protected Areas, which set up set-aside areas for fishing, see section on "political drivers") ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vIMlPZL2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Likuge & Munas, 2013; Mbaye, 2020, 2020; Sj\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}stedt & Jagers, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Likuge & Munas, 2013; Mbaye, 2020, 2020; Sjöstedt & Jagers, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33669,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HYNLLKUZ"],"itemData":{"id":33669,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Redefining Paradigms of Sustainable Development in South Asia","event-place":"Islamabad, Pakistan","language":"en","page":"109–135","publisher":"SDPI & Sung-e-Meel Publications","publisher-place":"Islamabad, Pakistan","title":"Whose Right to Livelihood Matters? A Case Study of Local and Migratory Fishermen un Tricomalee, Sri Lanka","author":[{"family":"Likuge","given":"G."},{"family":"Munas","given":"M."}],"editor":[{"family":"Ared","given":"U.T.H.Sarah S."},{"family":"Niazi","given":"Ileana"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33656,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8D9AIKQH"],"itemData":{"id":33656,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","title":"Social construction of climate change and adaptation strategies among Senegalese artisanal fishers: between empirical knowledge, magico-religious practices and sciences","volume":"GEC_2019_1116","author":[{"family":"Mbaye","given":"A."},{"family":"Brehmer","given":"P."},{"family":"Schmidt","given":"J."},{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":33656,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8D9AIKQH"],"itemData":{"id":33656,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","title":"Social construction of climate change and adaptation strategies among Senegalese artisanal fishers: between empirical knowledge, magico-religious practices and sciences","volume":"GEC_2019_1116","author":[{"family":"Mbaye","given":"A."},{"family":"Brehmer","given":"P."},{"family":"Schmidt","given":"J."},{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":33642,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UK7H3M3Y"],"itemData":{"id":33642,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy(43","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2013.05.007","language":"en","page":"143–148","title":"Democracy and the environment revisited: The case of African fisheries","author":[{"family":"Sjöstedt","given":"M."},{"family":"Jagers","given":"S.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Gatherer livelihoods
There are many characteristics of gather livelihoods that support sustainable use that are similar to those of hunting livelihoods including flexibility, adaptive capacity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UyxdiGhn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bunce, 2016; D\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}az-Reviriego, 2016; Maclean, 2013; B. Parlee, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Bunce, 2016; Díaz-Reviriego, 2016; Maclean, 2013; B. Parlee, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33636,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JG5C2SIB"],"itemData":{"id":33636,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Natural Hazards","ISSN":"1573-0840","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Natural Hazards","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Springer","page":"1419-1441","title":"Vulnerability and adaptive capacity of Inuit women to climate change: a case study from Iqaluit, Nunavut","volume":"83","author":[{"family":"Bunce","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Ford","given":"James"},{"family":"Harper","given":"Sherilee"},{"family":"Edge","given":"Victoria"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33630,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RAVX8KTF"],"itemData":{"id":33630,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecology and Society","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: JSTOR","title":"Social organization influences the exchange and species richness of medicinal plants in Amazonian homegardens","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Díaz-Reviriego","given":"Isabel"},{"family":"González-Segura","given":"Lara"},{"family":"Fernández-Llamazares","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Howard","given":"Patricia L"},{"family":"Molina","given":"José Luis"},{"family":"Reyes-García","given":"Victoria"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":33635,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8BGW6PB8"],"itemData":{"id":33635,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geoforum","ISSN":"0016-7185","journalAbbreviation":"Geoforum","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"94-105","title":"Healthy country, healthy people: An Australian aboriginal organisation’s adaptive governance to enhance its social–ecological system","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Maclean","given":"Kirsten"},{"family":"Ross","given":"Helen"},{"family":"Cuthill","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Rist","given":"Philip"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":34972,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WM2FB3ZB"],"itemData":{"id":34972,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","language":"en","page":"515–528","title":"Indigenous knowledge of ecological variability and commons management: a case study on berry harvesting from northern Canada","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"B."},{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Council","given":"Teetl’it Gwich’in Renewable Resources"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The management of landscapes for the purposes of gathering of food resources is an important area of research, particularly, in relation of indigenous peoples (e.g., cultural burning practices) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Qnv0xqvt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Christianson, 2014; Marks-Block, 2021; M. McKemey, 2020; McWilliam, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Christianson, 2014; Marks-Block, 2021; M. McKemey, 2020; McWilliam, 1999)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33632,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KW9SQTWW"],"itemData":{"id":33632,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Wildland Fire","ISSN":"1448-5516","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"International Journal of Wildland Fire","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: CSIRO Publishing","page":"190-200","title":"Social science research on Indigenous wildfire management in the 21st century and future research needs","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Christianson","given":"Amy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":803,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XYVP8GRD"],"itemData":{"id":803,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fire Ecology","ISSN":"1933-9747","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fire Ecology","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: SpringerOpen","page":"1-20","title":"Revitalized Karuk and Yurok cultural burning to enhance California hazelnut for basketweaving in northwestern California, USA","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Marks-Block","given":"Tony"},{"family":"Lake","given":"Frank K"},{"family":"Bird","given":"Rebecca Bliege"},{"family":"Curran","given":"Lisa M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":33319,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9Z4MG9XF"],"itemData":{"id":33319,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Indigenous fire management is experiencing a resurgence worldwide. Northern Australia is the world leader in Indigenous savanna burning, delivering social, cultural, environmental and economic benefits. In 2016, a greenhouse gas abatement fire program commenced in the savannas of south-eastern Arnhem Land in the Northern Territory, managed by the Indigenous Yugul Mangi rangers. We undertook participatory action research and semi-structured interviews with rangers and Elders during 2016 and 2019 to investigate Indigenous knowledge and obtain local feedback about fire management. Results indicated that Indigenous rangers effectively use cross-cultural science (including local and Traditional Ecological Knowledge alongside western science) to manage fire. Fire management is a key driver in the production of bush tucker (wild food) resources and impacts other cultural and ecological values. A need for increased education and awareness about Indigenous burning was consistently emphasized. To address this, the project participants developed the Yugul Mangi Faiya En Sisen Kelenda (Yugul Mangi Fire and Seasons Calendar) that drew on Indigenous knowledge of seasonal biocultural indicators to guide the rangers&rsquo; fire management planning. The calendar has potential for application in fire management planning, intergenerational transfer of Indigenous knowledge and locally driven adaptive fire management.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su12030995","issue":"3","language":"en","license":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","page":"995","title":"Indigenous Knowledge and Seasonal Calendar Inform Adaptive Savanna Burning in Northern Australia","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/12/3/995","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"McKemey","given":"Michelle"},{"family":"Ens","given":"Emilie"},{"family":"Rangers","given":"Yugul Mangi"},{"family":"Costello","given":"Oliver"},{"family":"Reid","given":"Nick"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1]]}}},{"id":33633,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DH72SMPL"],"itemData":{"id":33633,"type":"paper-conference","event-title":"ACIAR PROCEEDINGS","ISBN":"1038-6920","page":"80-85","publisher":"ACIAR; 1998","title":"Fire and cultural burning in Nusa tenggara Timur: Some implications of fire management practices for Indonesian Government Policy","author":[{"family":"McWilliam","given":"Andrew"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Concern about the risk of bioaccumulation of contaminant in plants valued for food is leading to deterioration of many gatherer livelihoods.  

Box 4.22. Biodiversity: Drivers of sustainable use of wild species 

Vaccinium vitis-idaea (nat?'at in Teetl’it Gwich'in; puolukka in Finnish) is a common evergreen dwarf shrub in the Ericaceae Family found across the circumpolar north ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"E5xbLyea","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gillespie, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Gillespie, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33797,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5UYAFJDK"],"itemData":{"id":33797,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PhytoKeys","DOI":"10.3897/phytokeys.52.8721","ISSN":"1314-2003, 1314-2011","journalAbbreviation":"PK","page":"23-79","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"New vascular plant records for the Canadian Arctic Archipelago","URL":"http://phytokeys.pensoft.net/articles.php?id=5507","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Gillespie","given":"Lynn J."},{"family":"Saarela","given":"Jeffery M."},{"family":"Sokoloff","given":"Paul C."},{"family":"Bull","given":"Roger D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",6,25]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. V. vitis-idaea produces small, red spherical berries and berry production can differ dramatically year to year depending on climatic variables over that past two growing seasons ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7xU7pvcJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Krebs, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Krebs, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NJK73YK7"],"itemData":{"id":33666,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botany","language":"en","page":"401–408","title":"Climatic determinants of berry crops in the boreal forest of southwestern Yukon","volume":"87","author":[{"family":"Krebs","given":"C.J."},{"family":"Boonstra","given":"R."},{"family":"Cowcill","given":"K."},{"family":"Kenney","given":"A.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The berries are an important food source food many northern inhabitants, including grizzly bears, red-backed voles and indigenous people (ibid.). This case study compares the sustainability of V. vitis-idaea harvest by people in two circumpolar regions: northern Canada and Finland. Wild nat?'at berries have been harvested annually in northern Canada since time immemorial. The Teetl’it Gwich’in identify nat?'at as one of the three most important berries and upwards of 90% of households collect them ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mWjiMkvH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. Murray, Boxall, and Wein 2005)","plainCitation":"(G. Murray, Boxall, and Wein 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33652,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QFZBJQAV"],"itemData":{"id":33652,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","language":"en","page":"174–184","title":"Distribution, abundance, and utilization of wild berries by the Gwich’in people in the Mackenzie River Delta Region","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Murray","given":"G."},{"family":"Boxall","given":"P.C."},{"family":"Wein","given":"R.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Annual harvest estimates range from 6-19 liters per household and for a total of 5,100 liters between the four largest Teetl’it Gwich’in communities and commercial harvesting is limited ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ym5XmZ8l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. Murray, Boxall, and Wein 2005; Parlee, Berkes, and Council 2005)","plainCitation":"(G. Murray, Boxall, and Wein 2005; Parlee, Berkes, and Council 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33652,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QFZBJQAV"],"itemData":{"id":33652,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","language":"en","page":"174–184","title":"Distribution, abundance, and utilization of wild berries by the Gwich’in people in the Mackenzie River Delta Region","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Murray","given":"G."},{"family":"Boxall","given":"P.C."},{"family":"Wein","given":"R.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":34976,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FVINMDIR"],"itemData":{"id":34976,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecohealth","language":"en","page":"127–137","title":"Health of the land, health of the people: a case study on Gwich’in berry harvesting in northern Canada","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"B."},{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Council","given":"Teetl’it Gwich’in Renewable Resources"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Berry picking effectively begins well before fruit production. Harvesters, primarily women, begin “checking in” on their preferred patches and sharing information across their social networks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nVd3uwu3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. E. Murray 2002; Parlee, Berkes, and Council 2006)","plainCitation":"(A. E. Murray 2002; Parlee, Berkes, and Council 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33653,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XKR9WUCY"],"itemData":{"id":33653,"type":"book","event-place":"Edmonton (AB","language":"en","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"University of Alberta","publisher-place":"Edmonton (AB","title":"Learning about the land: Tetlit Gwich’in perspectives on sustainable resource use (Master of Arts Thesis","author":[{"family":"Murray","given":"A.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":34972,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WM2FB3ZB"],"itemData":{"id":34972,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","language":"en","page":"515–528","title":"Indigenous knowledge of ecological variability and commons management: a case study on berry harvesting from northern Canada","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"B."},{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Council","given":"Teetl’it Gwich’in Renewable Resources"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Preferred patches are generally located in easily accessible areas near communities or near family camp or cabin sites ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3IMAJ41D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Parlee, Berkes, and Council 2006)","plainCitation":"(Parlee, Berkes, and Council 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34972,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WM2FB3ZB"],"itemData":{"id":34972,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","language":"en","page":"515–528","title":"Indigenous knowledge of ecological variability and commons management: a case study on berry harvesting from northern Canada","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"B."},{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Council","given":"Teetl’it Gwich’in Renewable Resources"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Berry observations intensify during the picking season and modern technology allows for the information to be shared across a large geographical area (Ibid.). In poor years, pickers may travel large distances to access productive patches. For example, Whitehorse, Yukon, had a particularly bountiful nat?'at crop in 2016 and this enticed four women Caring for particular berry patches is expressed in several ways, including direct management of habitat conditions to promote berry production ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AhSmvXFi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. E. Murray, 2002; Parlee, 2005)","plainCitation":"(A. E. Murray, 2002; Parlee, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33653,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XKR9WUCY"],"itemData":{"id":33653,"type":"book","event-place":"Edmonton (AB","language":"en","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"University of Alberta","publisher-place":"Edmonton (AB","title":"Learning about the land: Tetlit Gwich’in perspectives on sustainable resource use (Master of Arts Thesis","author":[{"family":"Murray","given":"A.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":34976,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FVINMDIR"],"itemData":{"id":34976,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecohealth","language":"en","page":"127–137","title":"Health of the land, health of the people: a case study on Gwich’in berry harvesting in northern Canada","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"B."},{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Council","given":"Teetl’it Gwich’in Renewable Resources"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The pickers are also very responsive to climate-driven variation in berry production and use their social networks to locate alternative, more productive patches ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"T5boXaZS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. E. Murray 2002; Parlee, Berkes, and Council 2006)","plainCitation":"(A. E. Murray 2002; Parlee, Berkes, and Council 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33653,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XKR9WUCY"],"itemData":{"id":33653,"type":"book","event-place":"Edmonton (AB","language":"en","number-of-pages":"118","publisher":"University of Alberta","publisher-place":"Edmonton (AB","title":"Learning about the land: Tetlit Gwich’in perspectives on sustainable resource use (Master of Arts Thesis","author":[{"family":"Murray","given":"A.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":34972,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WM2FB3ZB"],"itemData":{"id":34972,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","language":"en","page":"515–528","title":"Indigenous knowledge of ecological variability and commons management: a case study on berry harvesting from northern Canada","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"B."},{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Council","given":"Teetl’it Gwich’in Renewable Resources"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Across the Arctic Ocean in Finland, harvesting of wild puolakka berries is also a prominent activity with deep historical roots. It is estimated that 60-70% of Finns continue to participate in berry picking and harvest ~12 kg of puolakka berries/household each year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lOpXRSy7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Peltola, 2014; Saastimoinen, 2000; Vaara, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Peltola, 2014; Saastimoinen, 2000; Vaara, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33645,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C7NCRN78"],"itemData":{"id":33645,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","language":"en","page":"645–650","title":"The picking of wild berries in Finland in 1997 and 1998","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Saastimoinen","given":"O."},{"family":"Kangas","given":"K."},{"family":"Aho","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":33695,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/82MJJLUG"],"itemData":{"id":33695,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scandinavian Journal of Forest Research","language":"en","page":"586–595","title":"Changes in wild berry picking in Finland between 1997 and 2011","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Vaara","given":"M."},{"family":"Saastamoinen","given":"O."},{"family":"Turtiainen","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33648,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P8U3KJSD"],"itemData":{"id":33648,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Barents Studies: Peoples, Economies and Politics","language":"en","page":"24–49","title":"Social licence for the utilization of wild berries in the context of local traditional rights and the interests of the berry industry","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Peltola","given":"R."},{"family":"Hallikainen","given":"V."},{"family":"Tuulentie","given":"S."},{"family":"Naskali","given":"A."},{"family":"Manninen","given":"O."},{"family":"Similä","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Unlike northern Canada, commercial harvesting of puolakka has occurred for more than 150 years ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xwTVg3sF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Peltola, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Peltola, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33648,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P8U3KJSD"],"itemData":{"id":33648,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Barents Studies: Peoples, Economies and Politics","language":"en","page":"24–49","title":"Social licence for the utilization of wild berries in the context of local traditional rights and the interests of the berry industry","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Peltola","given":"R."},{"family":"Hallikainen","given":"V."},{"family":"Tuulentie","given":"S."},{"family":"Naskali","given":"A."},{"family":"Manninen","given":"O."},{"family":"Similä","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The majority of forests in Finland are owned privately; however, people are allowed to harvest berries for domestic and commercial use regardless of land ownership under the principle of “Everyman’s right” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5Vj7MqXC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Peltola, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Peltola, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33648,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P8U3KJSD"],"itemData":{"id":33648,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Barents Studies: Peoples, Economies and Politics","language":"en","page":"24–49","title":"Social licence for the utilization of wild berries in the context of local traditional rights and the interests of the berry industry","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Peltola","given":"R."},{"family":"Hallikainen","given":"V."},{"family":"Tuulentie","given":"S."},{"family":"Naskali","given":"A."},{"family":"Manninen","given":"O."},{"family":"Similä","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An estimated 8-10% of the total berry crop are harvested annually ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CMtAYjaA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Turtiainen, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Turtiainen, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33682,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T7TV39LG"],"itemData":{"id":33682,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Silva Fennica","DOI":"10.14214/sf.115","ISSN":"22424075","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Silva Fenn.","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Variations of yield and utilisation of bilberries (Vaccinium myrtillus L.) and cowberries (V. vitis-idaea L.) in Finland","URL":"http://www.silvafennica.fi/article/115","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Turtiainen","given":"Marjut"},{"family":"Salo","given":"Kauko"},{"family":"Saastamoinen","given":"Olli"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Finland, urban as well as rural inhabitants harvest berries; ownership of a summer cottage significantly increases participation in berry picking by urban dwellers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SEekFsRO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kangas & Markkanen, 2001; Pouta, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Kangas & Markkanen, 2001; Pouta, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33664,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IAXMZ9IX"],"itemData":{"id":33664,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Silva Fennica","language":"en","page":"487–495","title":"Factors affecting participation in wild berry picking by rural and urban dwellers","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Kangas","given":"K."},{"family":"Markkanen","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":33683,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DWNRZE39"],"itemData":{"id":33683,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"10.1080/08941920500519156","ISSN":"0894-1920, 1521-0723","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Society & Natural Resources","language":"en","page":"285-304","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recreational Wild Berry Picking in Finland—Reflection of a Rural Lifestyle","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/08941920500519156","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Pouta","given":"Eija"},{"family":"Sievänen","given":"Tuija"},{"family":"Neuvonen","given":"Marjo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Women tend to be more active berry pickers than men ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CwMw048V","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pouta, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Pouta, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33683,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DWNRZE39"],"itemData":{"id":33683,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"10.1080/08941920500519156","ISSN":"0894-1920, 1521-0723","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Society & Natural Resources","language":"en","page":"285-304","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recreational Wild Berry Picking in Finland—Reflection of a Rural Lifestyle","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/08941920500519156","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Pouta","given":"Eija"},{"family":"Sievänen","given":"Tuija"},{"family":"Neuvonen","given":"Marjo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are limited data documenting harvesting practices though the protection of secret berry patches is mentioned ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MISOjHgo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Peltola, 2014; Pouta, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Peltola, 2014; Pouta, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33683,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DWNRZE39"],"itemData":{"id":33683,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"10.1080/08941920500519156","ISSN":"0894-1920, 1521-0723","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Society & Natural Resources","language":"en","page":"285-304","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Recreational Wild Berry Picking in Finland—Reflection of a Rural Lifestyle","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/08941920500519156","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Pouta","given":"Eija"},{"family":"Sievänen","given":"Tuija"},{"family":"Neuvonen","given":"Marjo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",4]]}}},{"id":33648,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P8U3KJSD"],"itemData":{"id":33648,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Barents Studies: Peoples, Economies and Politics","language":"en","page":"24–49","title":"Social licence for the utilization of wild berries in the context of local traditional rights and the interests of the berry industry","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Peltola","given":"R."},{"family":"Hallikainen","given":"V."},{"family":"Tuulentie","given":"S."},{"family":"Naskali","given":"A."},{"family":"Manninen","given":"O."},{"family":"Similä","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The long-term harvesting history and small proportion of berry crop harvested annually indicates picking puolakka berries in Finland remains sustainable. Interestingly, despite the limited documentation of customary practices “regulating” berry picking, there is evidence these practices exist. A common complaint with current commercial harvesting is companies have not provided foreign workers with information on appropriate locations and berry picking practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Y8KLRyqt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Peltola, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Peltola, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33648,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P8U3KJSD"],"itemData":{"id":33648,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Barents Studies: Peoples, Economies and Politics","language":"en","page":"24–49","title":"Social licence for the utilization of wild berries in the context of local traditional rights and the interests of the berry industry","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Peltola","given":"R."},{"family":"Hallikainen","given":"V."},{"family":"Tuulentie","given":"S."},{"family":"Naskali","given":"A."},{"family":"Manninen","given":"O."},{"family":"Similä","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As seen in northern Canada, children are frequently included in berry picking excursions which provides the opportunity for these practices to be transferred between generations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OPjeutgN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kangas & Markkanen, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Kangas & Markkanen, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33664,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IAXMZ9IX"],"itemData":{"id":33664,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Silva Fennica","language":"en","page":"487–495","title":"Factors affecting participation in wild berry picking by rural and urban dwellers","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Kangas","given":"K."},{"family":"Markkanen","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The social and ecological context of the Teet’lit Gwich’in and Finnish peoples provide an interesting comparison of wild berry harvesting. The Gwich’in inhabit an expansive, largely undeveloped subarctic region and Finland is a relatively small country with intensively managed forests. Commonalities between the two groups suggest that sustainable harvesting is achieved, at least in part, by having high rates of participation within the population and intergenerational involvement. This ensures shared appreciation for social and culture aspects of berry picking; the development of customary practices to ensure harvest sustainability; and opportunities occur to transfer this knowledge to the next generation. The influence of women as the primary leaders of berry picking is less clear and requires further investigation. Other similarities include the berries are supplementary rather than a staple component of local diet, the quantity of harvest is directly related to local knowledge and the harvest is very responsive to annual variation, harvesting is labor intensive and mechanization of harvesting is relatively limited. Changes in economic or social pressures that increase barriers to berry picking participation could also threaten populations of Vaccinium vitis-idaea. The fewer people who berry pick could decrease the societal value of berries and reduce incentives to conserve wild berry picking areas.
      

Hunting livelihoods
Hunting livelihoods are characterized by different characteristics of social organization.  In addition to a high degree of mobility (in the case of hunting of migratory species), many hunting societies developed within indigenous cultures share some common characteristics such as strong subsistence versus commercial harvest; social networks that facilitate food sharing; flexibility and adaptive to ecological conditions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mdBDRQBw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Armitage, 2005; Granderson, 2017; Lu, 2010; Mulrennan, 2014; T. Pearce, 2015; Ruiz-Mall\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}n, 2017)","plainCitation":"(D. Armitage, 2005; Granderson, 2017; Lu, 2010; Mulrennan, 2014; T. Pearce, 2015; Ruiz-Mallén, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33793,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VCCIWXMF"],"itemData":{"id":33793,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Why do some community-based natural resource management strategies perform better than others? Commons theorists have approached this question by developing institutional design principles to address collective choice situations, while other analysts have critiqued the underlying assumptions of community-based resource management. However, efforts to enhance communitybased natural resource management performance also require an analysis of exogenous and endogenous variables\\nthat influence how social actors not only act collectively but do so in ways that respond to changing circumstances, foster learning, and build capacity for management adaptation. Drawing on examples from northern Canada and Southeast Asia, this article examines the relationship among adaptive capacity, community-based resource management performance, and the socio-institutional determinants of collective action, such as technical, financial, and legal constraints, and complex issues of politics, scale, knowledge, community and culture. An emphasis on adaptive capacity responds to a conceptual weakness in communitybased natural resource management and highlights an emerging research and policy discourse that builds upon static design principles and the contested concepts in current management practice.","call-number":"Esub44","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-004-0076-z","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"English","page":"703-715","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Adaptive Capacity and Community-Based Natural Resource Management","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-004-0076-z","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Armitage","given":"Derek"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",6]]}}},{"id":33624,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C52EH8NN"],"itemData":{"id":33624,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Weather, Climate, and Society","ISSN":"1948-8327","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Weather, Climate, and Society","note":"number: 3","page":"545-561","title":"The role of traditional knowledge in building adaptive capacity for climate change: Perspectives from Vanuatu","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Granderson","given":"Ainka A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33623,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8XJLCA97"],"itemData":{"id":33623,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ecological Anthropology","ISSN":"1528-6509","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ecological Anthropology","note":"number: 1","page":"5-21","title":"Patterns of indigenous resilience in the Amazon: A case study of Huaorani hunting in Ecuador","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Lu","given":"Flora"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":33508,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U586563I"],"itemData":{"id":33508,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geological Society, London, Special Publications","ISSN":"0305-8719","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Geological Society, London, Special Publications","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Geological Society of London","page":"79-102","title":"On the edge: a consideration of the adaptive capacity of Indigenous Peoples in coastal zones from the Arctic to the Tropics","volume":"388","author":[{"family":"Mulrennan","given":"Monica E"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":33631,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z8JZF5IT"],"itemData":{"id":33631,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic","ISSN":"0004-0843","journalAbbreviation":"Arctic","note":"publisher: JSTOR","page":"233-245","title":"Inuit traditional ecological knowledge (TEK), subsistence hunting and adaptation to climate change in the Canadian Arctic","author":[{"family":"Pearce","given":"Tristan"},{"family":"Ford","given":"James"},{"family":"Willox","given":"Ashlee Cunsolo"},{"family":"Smit","given":"Barry"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":33615,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7KFIDQE6"],"itemData":{"id":33615,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Climatic Change","ISSN":"1573-1480","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Climatic Change","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Springer","page":"227-242","title":"Unravelling local adaptive capacity to climate change in the Bolivian Amazon: the interlinkages between assets, conservation and markets","volume":"140","author":[{"family":"Ruiz-Mallén","given":"Isabel"},{"family":"Fernández-Llamazares","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Reyes-García","given":"Victoria"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and intergenerational knowledge sharing that supports adaptation. Many hunting livelihoods in indigenous cultures are also based around beliefs, values and practices of reciprocity between wild species and dependent communities (i.e., people take care of the animals and the animals take care of the people) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5Y2IrwmI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez-Llamazares, 2020; Kimmerer, 2011; Nadasdy, 2007; Nursey-Bray, 2010; A. Peterson, 2013; J. R. Welch, 2014; Wenzel, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Fernández-Llamazares, 2020; Kimmerer, 2011; Nadasdy, 2007; Nursey-Bray, 2010; A. Peterson, 2013; J. R. Welch, 2014; Wenzel, 2000)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":762,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GPPGE69Y"],"itemData":{"id":762,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Local attitudes towards wildlife encompass environmental, political, sociocultural and psychological dimensions that shape human-wildlife interactions and conservation efforts. Although the political and sociocultural dimensions of these interactions have been extensively examined by political ecologists and cultural anthropologists, psychological aspects have remained largely untapped so far. This article presents an in-depth review of a long historical record of changing attitudes towards wildlife among Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem in southern Kenya, examining their shifts in light of different conservation psychology theories. The historical changes are reviewed in relation to three theories of attitudinal shifts (i.e., cognitive dissonance, reactance, and motivation crowding theory) and discussed in a context of land dispossession, conservation policies and changes in Maasai lifestyles and cultural values. We conclude that conservation psychology adds an important dimension to understanding attitudes towards wildlife and how they bear on conservation policies and practices.","call-number":"Sub 10a","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2019.125763","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Historical shifts in local attitudes towards wildlife by Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem (Kenya): Insights from three conservation psychology theories","title-short":"Historical shifts in local attitudes towards wildlife by Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem (Kenya)","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1617138119302833","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Fernández-Llamazares","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Western","given":"David"},{"family":"Galvin","given":"Kathleen A."},{"family":"McElwee","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"Cabeza","given":"Mar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":33486,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EKNZ89TZ"],"itemData":{"id":33486,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Human dimensions of ecological restoration","page":"257-276","publisher":"Springer","title":"Restoration and reciprocity: the contributions of traditional ecological knowledge","author":[{"family":"Kimmerer","given":"Robin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33681,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VT2R5J5N"],"itemData":{"id":33681,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"American Ethnologist","DOI":"10.1525/ae.2007.34.1.25","ISSN":"00940496","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"25-43","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The gift in the animal: The ontology of hunting and human-animal sociality","title-short":"The gift in the animal","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1525/ae.2007.34.1.25","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Nadasdy","given":"Paul"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",2]]}}},{"id":33676,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M47EBGAD"],"itemData":{"id":33676,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The challenge of developing environmental outcomes acceptable to stakeholders with different values is well documented. Discourse analysis provides insights into how the views of different stakeholders affect decision making. We studied the discourses of key actors associated with the implementation of a Turtle and Dugong Hunting Management Plan developed by Hope Vale Aboriginal community in the Great Barrier Reef World Heritage Area, Australia. The discourses of the environmental managers and community members were very different. Hope Vale people prioritized cultural well-being; the staff of management agencies prioritized biodiversity outcomes. These differences precluded effective outcomes despite considerable investment in hunting management over more than 20 years by both groups. Understanding the discursive terrain within environmental management domains can inform environmental decision making and the implementation of agreed management arrangements, enabling biodiversity objectives and Indigenous cultural aspirations to be met in a socially just, economically viable, and environmentally sustainable way.","DOI":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/08941920903468621","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"366-382","source":"Zotero","title":"Exploring Discourses in Environmental Decision Making: An Indigenous Hunting Case Study","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Nursey-Bray","given":"Melissa"},{"family":"Marsh","given":"Helene"},{"family":"Ross","given":"Helen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":33625,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EJP6IJZP"],"itemData":{"id":33625,"type":"book","ISBN":"0-231-53426-4","publisher":"Columbia University Press","title":"Being animal: Beasts and boundaries in nature ethics","author":[{"family":"Peterson","given":"Anna"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33313,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZIP96UVB"],"itemData":{"id":33313,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Conservation attention to indigenous hunting with fire in the cerrado largely focuses on sustainability as construed in scientific terms rather than according to indigenous points of view. Towards the goal of reframing the debate in terms more congruent with indigenous perspectives, I address how the Xavante (A’uw?) view ritualized and collective hunting, including hunting with fire, as indispensable means of acquiring gifts by which to celebrate important events, express feelings of respect and gratitude towards others, promote positive social values among male youth, and maintain the group’s ethnic identity. In particular, ritualized exchanges of game meat are necessary and culturally appropriate means of expressing esteem for others at some of life’s most important moments. For the Xavante, the social imperative to give and receive gifts of meat during weddings and initiation ceremonies motivates efforts to maintain the collective hunting with fire tradition in a manner that ensures its long term environmental viability.","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-013-9637-1","ISSN":"0300-7839, 1572-9915","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Hum Ecol","language":"en","page":"47-59","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Xavante Ritual Hunting: Anthropogenic Fire, Reciprocity, and Collective Landscape Management in the Brazilian Cerrado","title-short":"Xavante Ritual Hunting","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10745-013-9637-1","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Welch","given":"James R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",2]]}}},{"id":33616,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GMRPUD8C"],"itemData":{"id":33616,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Senri ethnological studies","ISSN":"0387-6004","journalAbbreviation":"Senri ethnological studies","page":"61-85","title":"Sharing, money and modern Inuit subsistence: Obligation and reciprocity at Clyde River, Nunavut","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Wenzel","given":"George W"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Hunting also tends to be a very gendered practice. In many cultures, there are stereotypes of men the hunter, and women as gatherer are well established but have also been critiqued as social constructions of anthropology.  For example, the archetype of “man the hunter” is deeply engrained in the anthropological record of the North ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iEsDw4OB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bodenhorn, 1990; Van Voorst, 2009; Vladimirova & Habeck, 2018; Williamson, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Bodenhorn, 1990; Van Voorst, 2009; Vladimirova & Habeck, 2018; Williamson, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33628,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CZKHP46W"],"itemData":{"id":33628,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Études/Inuit/Studies","ISSN":"0701-1008","journalAbbreviation":"Études/Inuit/Studies","note":"publisher: JSTOR","page":"55-74","title":"\\" I'm Not the Great Hunter, My Wife Is\\": Iñupiat and anthropological models of gender","author":[{"family":"Bodenhorn","given":"Barbara"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1990"]]}}},{"id":33627,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6FV5PTIL"],"itemData":{"id":33627,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Jàmbá: Journal of Disaster Risk Studies","ISSN":"1996-1421","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Jàmbá: Journal of Disaster Risk Studies","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: AOSIS","page":"235-252","title":"\\" I work all the time-he just waits for the animals to come back\\": social impacts of climate changes: a Greenlandic case study","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Van Voorst","given":"Roanne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":33626,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3EXN8QG2"],"itemData":{"id":33626,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Polar Geography","ISSN":"1088-937X","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Polar Geography","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"145-163","title":"Introduction: Feminist approaches and the study of gender in Arctic social sciences","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Vladimirova","given":"Vladislava"},{"family":"Habeck","given":"J Otto"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":33687,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZVHUU8AP"],"itemData":{"id":33687,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Links between Biodiversity, Conserv. Livelihoods Food Secur. Sustain. use wild species meat","event-place":"Gland, Switzerland","page":"19-22","publisher":"IUCN","publisher-place":"Gland, Switzerland","title":"Wild meat, food security and forest conservation","author":[{"family":"Williamson","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This bias has tended to become reproduced in the kinds of research carried out in the North as well as in policy contexts at regional, territorial, and national levels; although some greater attention has been paid to gender biases since the 1970s, there is still relatively little in the academic literature that relates women and hunting. Within this category, the theme of women and hunting is among the most complex. Although not well documented, women in many cultures play pivotal roles in hunting, ranging from spiritual rites to holding spears and other weapons ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jdVAhk0p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Geller & Stockett, 2007; Wadley, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Geller & Stockett, 2007; Wadley, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33617,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/37J5K6BG"],"itemData":{"id":33617,"type":"book","ISBN":"0-8122-2005-6","publisher":"University of Pennsylvania Press","title":"Feminist anthropology: past, present, and future","author":[{"family":"Geller","given":"Pamela L"},{"family":"Stockett","given":"Miranda K"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":33629,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GPXF3UTF"],"itemData":{"id":33629,"type":"book","ISBN":"81-8028-016-0","publisher":"Orient Blackswan","title":"Essays on North Indian folk traditions","author":[{"family":"Wadley","given":"Susan Snow"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The gender bias is not unexpected given that men were the sole leaders of anthropological tradition up until the mid-twentieth century (Parlee, Andre, & Kritsch, 2014). There are more diverse kinds of gender identities than those that follow Judaeo-Christian archetypes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iOLraYgh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kuokkanen, 2019; Subramaniam, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Kuokkanen, 2019; Subramaniam, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33796,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8QG7S3ZX"],"itemData":{"id":33796,"type":"book","publisher":"Oxford University Press","title":"Restructuring relations: Indigenous self-determination, governance, and gender","author":[{"family":"Kuokkanen","given":"Rauna"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":33798,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AFLAE33J"],"itemData":{"id":33798,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The handbook of science and technology studies","ISSN":"0262035685","journalAbbreviation":"The handbook of science and technology studies","note":"publisher: MIT Press","page":"407","title":"Yong","author":[{"family":"Subramaniam","given":"Banu"},{"family":"Foster","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Harding","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Roy","given":"Deboleena"},{"family":"TallBear","given":"Kim"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. But the bias is also one that seems to have been easily and unapologetically replicated in other disciplines and research traditions, including that focused on traditional knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rxTlwB8S","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nadasdy, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Nadasdy, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":801,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TLE6SSIX"],"itemData":{"id":801,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic","issue":"4","note":"number: 4","page":"367-80","title":"Reevaluating the Co-management Success Story","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Nadasdy","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In the case of indigenous peoples and local communities, the practices of hunting are highly intertwined with cultural identities. For example, in the case of the Dene, the practice of hunting is intertwined what it means to be indigenous interconnected with physical-spiritual health and well-being. For example, people refer to themselves as “caribou people”. Even when resources have been lost or eroded, species are still intertwined with sense of self, community and spiritual belief systems as a result the loss is highly impactful on health and well-being. For example, the collapse of the North American plains bison, which was intertwined with the violent colonization of Canada and United States of America has had a devastating effect on the indigenous people. 
      Sports and trophy hunting are also strongly interconnected with the identities and social organization of those engaged ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hPtpQ22K","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Darimont, 2017; Ebeling-Schuld & Darimont, 2017; Mihalik, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Darimont, 2017; Ebeling-Schuld & Darimont, 2017; Mihalik, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33680,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5H9H9E4K"],"itemData":{"id":33680,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biology Letters","DOI":"10.1098/rsbl.2016.0909","ISSN":"1744-9561","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Biol Lett","note":"PMID: 28356410\\nPMCID: PMC5377034","page":"20160909","source":"PubMed Central","title":"Why men trophy hunt","URL":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5377034/","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Darimont","given":"Chris T."},{"family":"Codding","given":"Brian F."},{"family":"Hawkes","given":"Kristen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",3]]}}},{"id":33658,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YLIA5RQS"],"itemData":{"id":33658,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Royal Society Open Science","issue":"9","language":"en","title":"Trophy hunters pay more to target larger-bodied carnivores","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Mihalik","given":"I."},{"family":"Bateman","given":"A.W."},{"family":"Darimont","given":"C.T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":33694,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P729NE2C"],"itemData":{"id":33694,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Big-Game and Trophy Hunting Collection","title":"Online Hunting Forums Identify Achievement as Prominent Among Multiple Satisfactions","URL":"https://www.wellbeingintlstudiesrepository.org/bigthun/2","author":[{"family":"Ebeling-Schuld","given":"Alena"},{"family":"Darimont","given":"Chris"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",9,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, the motivations for recreational and sports hunting can be tied to the desire for self-sufficiency and food security; in other cases, the practise of hunting is strongly interconnected with social relationships and rituals.  Although there is much evidence that a primary motivation in hunting may be the desire to connect with nature, hunting of non-edible species is also associated with desires for control over nature (i.e., need for self-efficacy), and “show-off” (e.g., physical dominance) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"st8QaR4w","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Child and Dairmont, 2015; Darimont, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Child and Dairmont, 2015; Darimont, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33680,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5H9H9E4K"],"itemData":{"id":33680,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biology Letters","DOI":"10.1098/rsbl.2016.0909","ISSN":"1744-9561","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Biol Lett","note":"PMID: 28356410\\nPMCID: PMC5377034","page":"20160909","source":"PubMed Central","title":"Why men trophy hunt","URL":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5377034/","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Darimont","given":"Chris T."},{"family":"Codding","given":"Brian F."},{"family":"Hawkes","given":"Kristen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",3]]}}},{"id":33639,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2L56YGBI"],"itemData":{"id":33639,"type":"article-newspaper","title":"Hunting for trophies: online hunter photographs reveal achievement satisfaction with large and dangerous prey. Hum. Dimens. Wildl. 20, 531–541.","author":[{"family":"Child and Dairmont","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Small-scale agricultural livelihoods
Small scale agriculture has many kinds of organizational structures that including slash-and-burn as well as other kinds of agricultural practices. Agricultural practices can enhance (agro-biodiversity, such as agroecological practices/agroforestry. The rise in agricultural settlement and feudal systems in Europe in the 17th century onwards is largely attributed to land clearing, destruction of habitat for some wild species and persecution of others.  Other forms of small-scale agriculture are notably not commercial or feudal but tied to subsistence and cultural uses. For example, Huron Indians in southern Canada – organized in small scale agricultural communities (i.e., corn production) but with mixed engagement with hunting-gathering. The shift away from small scale agriculture in Europe and the Americas beginning in the 1900s and the mechanization of farming changed social structure in rural areas (i.e., farmers no longer held private property – lost to large scale farms). Changes in social structures and property rights resulted in people no longer being closer to the land on “family farms” but became workers in industrial farms or left farms entirely (i.e., rural-urban migration). The loss of individual property rights to many small areas of land in the United States of America is attributed to the homogenization of land and resource uses, increasing use of pesticides (leading to habitat degradation and losses of species) including unsustainable uses of wild species (i.e., land clearing). There are exceptions to this trend including reclaiming of land and the family farm in some areas.

Urban and peri-urban livelihoods
There are important dimensions of urban and peri-urban livelihoods that intersect with wild species use. While most research has focused on dichotomies of urban versus rural, the longer-term persistence and importance of “fringe or urban transition zones” became more apparent have developed since the 1980s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7pPt74ih","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Simon, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Simon, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33613,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2Q8JGLY5"],"itemData":{"id":33613,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual review of environment and resources","ISSN":"1543-5938","journalAbbreviation":"Annual review of environment and resources","note":"publisher: Annual Reviews","page":"167-185","title":"Urban environments: issues on the peri-urban fringe","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Simon","given":"David"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Population growth and consumer demand e for “green neighborhoods” is a driver behind the phenomenon of expanding urban transition zones and urban spawl and associated wild species habitat degradation. These patterns coincide with increasing concern about the adverse health impacts of living in high density neighborhoods (e.g., air quality) as in major cities around the world. It is also being driven in part by growing evidence of the health benefits, particularly for youth and children of being connected to nature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VKpYRUkR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cheng & Monroe, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Cheng & Monroe, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33614,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L6BG44YP"],"itemData":{"id":33614,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and behavior","ISSN":"0013-9165","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Environment and behavior","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Sage Publications Sage CA: Los Angeles, CA","page":"31-49","title":"Connection to nature: Children’s affective attitude toward nature","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Cheng","given":"Judith Chen-Hsuan"},{"family":"Monroe","given":"Martha C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some areas, city planning is offsetting the problem of urban sprawl in response to green consumers and instead contributing to the maintaining of wild spaces within cities for non-extractive uses.  Such efforts are also associated with the protection and reclamation and re-wilding of some peri-urban environments that might otherwise be taken by shopping outlets etc. Many such examples can be found in western Europe and some parts of North America ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"K0XisX6l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Checker, 2011; Kimari & Parish, 2020; C. Shackleton, 2017; Wolch, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Checker, 2011; Kimari & Parish, 2020; C. Shackleton, 2017; Wolch, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33619,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GSKFQDJJ"],"itemData":{"id":33619,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"City & Society","ISSN":"0893-0465","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"City & Society","note":"number: 2\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"210-229","title":"Wiped out by the “greenwave”: Environmental gentrification and the paradoxical politics of urban sustainability","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Checker","given":"Melissa"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":33620,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XCZLZNTS"],"itemData":{"id":33620,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Geography","ISSN":"0272-3638","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Urban Geography","note":"number: 5\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"643-656","title":"What is a river? A transnational meditation on the colonial city, abolition ecologies and the future of geography","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Kimari","given":"Wangui"},{"family":"Parish","given":"Jessica"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":33678,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ENAQFCLB"],"itemData":{"id":33678,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Although hardly noticed or formally recognised, urban foraging by humans probably occurs in all urban settings around the world. We draw from research in India, South Africa, Sweden, and the United States to demonstrate the ubiquity and varied nature of urban foraging in different contexts. Across these different contexts, we distil seven themes that characterise and thereby advance thinking about research and the understanding of urban foraging. We show that it is widespread and occurs across a variety of urban spaces and places. The species used and the local practices vary between contexts, and are in constant ?ux as urban ecological and social settings change. This requires that urban foragers are knowledgeable about diverse species, harvest locations, and rights of access, and that their practices are adaptable to changing contexts. Despite its ubiquity, most cities have some forms of regulations that prohibit or discourage urban foraging. We highlight a few important exceptions that can provide prototypes and lessons for other cities regarding supportive policy frameworks and initiatives. The formulation of dynamic policy, design, and management strategies in support of urban foraging will bene?t from understanding the common characteristics of foraging in cities worldwide, but also will require comprehension of the speci?c and dynamic contexts in which they would be implemented.","container-title":"Sustainability","DOI":"10.3390/su9101884","ISSN":"2071-1050","issue":"10","language":"en","page":"1884","title":"Urban Foraging: A Ubiquitous Human Practice Overlooked by Urban Planners, Policy, and Research","title-short":"Urban Foraging","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Dahlberg","given":"Annika"},{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",10,20]]}}},{"id":33618,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CNVIC6NP"],"itemData":{"id":33618,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and urban planning","ISSN":"0169-2046","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape and urban planning","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"234-244","title":"Urban green space, public health, and environmental justice: The challenge of making cities ‘just green enough’","volume":"125","author":[{"family":"Wolch","given":"Jennifer R"},{"family":"Byrne","given":"Jason"},{"family":"Newell","given":"Joshua P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Box 4.23. Agroforestry’s ‘roots’ in traditional land management systems in Southeast Asia

Agroforestry aims to intentionally integrate the trees with wild species, crops, and livestock to develop an ecology of symbiosis. A tradition of not separating oneself from these ecological systems is prominent amongst indigenous peoples within the Southeast Asian region, specifically in Malaysia and the Philippines ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NW1GZV52","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Adnan & Othman, 2012; Camacho, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Adnan & Othman, 2012; Camacho, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33638,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QXYA4FZJ"],"itemData":{"id":33638,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"The Relationship between Plants and the Malay Culture","author":[{"family":"Adnan","given":"N."},{"family":"Othman","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":33673,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N4DMJS4X"],"itemData":{"id":33673,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science","language":"en","note":"publisher: Ecosystem Services & Management","title":"Indigenous knowledge and practices for the sustainable management of Ifugao forests in Cordillera, Philippines","author":[{"family":"Camacho","given":"L.D."},{"family":"Gevaña","given":"D.T."},{"family":"Carandang","given":"A.P."},{"family":"S.C","given":"Camacho"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Histories of colonial rule and the erosion of traditional land management practices and rights of the Malay Forest peoples have created unsustainable deforestation in many regions of Malaysia, while the resistance to colonialism and maintenance of indigenous practices within the Cordillera Mountains of the Philippines has helped promote sustainable harvesting of fuelwood and ecological health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jKwyXSDY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Camacho, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Camacho, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33673,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N4DMJS4X"],"itemData":{"id":33673,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science","language":"en","note":"publisher: Ecosystem Services & Management","title":"Indigenous knowledge and practices for the sustainable management of Ifugao forests in Cordillera, Philippines","author":[{"family":"Camacho","given":"L.D."},{"family":"Gevaña","given":"D.T."},{"family":"Carandang","given":"A.P."},{"family":"S.C","given":"Camacho"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In a 2014 report undertaken by Malaysian feminist Carol Yong, Kuching-based, non-profit Sarawakians Access, and Peninsular Malaysia Orang Asli Village Network (Jaringan Kampung Orang Asli Semenanjung Malaysia); deforestation in Malaysia were intimately linked to a value-system based on a source of income. Massive shifts in forest land-use systems – from local customary land use systems to large scale commercial, extractive and developmental uses– have caused “harmful impacts on communities’ access to forest resources for livelihoods and food security, consequently intensifying livelihood hardship and poverty'' within regions of Malaysia. As “pre-existing customary land rights of forest peoples are systematically ignored and overridden”, the loss of natural wealth and biodiversity are affecting the Malay people in both material and non-material ways– livelihoods, cash income, and social-cultural and spiritual needs are arguably the most affected ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aMiyqEBl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Adnan & Othman, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Adnan & Othman, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33638,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QXYA4FZJ"],"itemData":{"id":33638,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"The Relationship between Plants and the Malay Culture","author":[{"family":"Adnan","given":"N."},{"family":"Othman","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, value-systems associating plants ritual and spiritual purposes (i.e., for ceremony) have been abandoned and/or ostracized ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8BIHRHCe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Adnan & Othman, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Adnan & Othman, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33638,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QXYA4FZJ"],"itemData":{"id":33638,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"The Relationship between Plants and the Malay Culture","author":[{"family":"Adnan","given":"N."},{"family":"Othman","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An interrogation of customary agroforestry systems can be a viable alternative to current mass extractive uses for sustainable local livelihood. Evidence of sustainable agroforestry practices may be found amongst the Ifugao peoples of the Philippines. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rNOnaUFk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Camacho, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Camacho, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33673,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N4DMJS4X"],"itemData":{"id":33673,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science","language":"en","note":"publisher: Ecosystem Services & Management","title":"Indigenous knowledge and practices for the sustainable management of Ifugao forests in Cordillera, Philippines","author":[{"family":"Camacho","given":"L.D."},{"family":"Gevaña","given":"D.T."},{"family":"Carandang","given":"A.P."},{"family":"S.C","given":"Camacho"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} explore how the indigenous communities (such as Ifugaos, Isneg, Tingguians and Ikalahans) in the Cordillera region, Philippines have upheld muyongs –key indigenous practices in woodlot or watersheds of privately or clan-owned forests – that has promoted sustainable forest management. Following traditional practices, Ifugao do not ‘own’ land through titles, but rather the rights to land use are community-based. Although muyongs are a “major source of fuel wood for the local people,” the ways of harvesting fuelwood are guided by customary practices. For example, the conservation of many endemic trees (e.g., Ficus spp.) are associated with spirits/anito, and are not being harvested for timber and fuel wood. Furthermore, the presence of endemic trees creates agroforestry environments that help maintain sufficient groundwater supply for muyongs (woodlots) and payoh (rice paddies). 

   
4.2.3.3.4. Urbanization
Key Messages: 
* Urbanization is a world-wide trend which generally decreases household dependence on wild food sources due to access to the market economy.
* Simultaneously, urbanization in some settings can increase the scale, use and harvest of wild species (to fuel urban markets) (established but incomplete) {4.2.3.3.4}.
* Urbanization spatially influences the sustainability of use of wild species; wild species are likely to becoming more depleted in areas closest to cities, whereas in depopulated rural areas, use of wild species is declining (established but incomplete){4.2.3.3.4}.
* Displacement of people due to stressors such as drought, environmental degradation, conflicts and loss of access to land leads to increased pressure on marginal lands and wild species where migrants, refugees and other displaced peoples now move to (established but incomplete) {4.2.3.3.4}.
* Both in-migration and out-migration can destabilize current natural resource use and weaken environmental knowledge systems and existing institutions (established but incomplete) {4.2.3.3.4}.

   The world is also undergoing substantial urbanization. The number of people living in urban centers increased from 2.3 billion in 1990 to 3.9 billion in 2014 and is 1652 expected to reach 5 billion by 2030 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BPhgE41j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations, 2014)","plainCitation":"(United Nations, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":617,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XPKAD6ZX"],"itemData":{"id":617,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"United Nations, Department of Economic and Social Affairs","language":"en","title":"World Urbanization Prospects, the 2014 Revision: Highlights","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The total area of cities doubled from 1992 to 2015, with impacts to tropical and subtropical savannas and grasslands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DR92ZDuJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While the number of megacities (populations of more than 10 million people) will continue to rise, the growth of small to medium-sized cities is now a dominant trend, and where most future urban populations will be found ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gnbkCgJx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations, 2014)","plainCitation":"(United Nations, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":617,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XPKAD6ZX"],"itemData":{"id":617,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"United Nations, Department of Economic and Social Affairs","language":"en","title":"World Urbanization Prospects, the 2014 Revision: Highlights","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Comparing IPBES regions, Africa, and Asia and the Pacific are urbanizing fastest, with future expansions in Asia and the Pacific expected to occur mostly in China and India ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wqUncNry","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. By 2050, it is expected that as many as 3 billion people will be living in informal areas and slums within cities, mostly in developing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gv7sPEtM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nagendra, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Nagendra, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33621,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LKXLL5LA"],"itemData":{"id":33621,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature sustainability","ISSN":"2398-9629","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Nature sustainability","note":"number: 7\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"341-349","title":"The urban south and the predicament of global sustainability","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"},{"family":"Bai","given":"Xuemei"},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"Eduardo S"},{"family":"Lwasa","given":"Shuaib"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
   Urbanization is associated with the nutrition transition in which individuals are less dependent on wild food and more dependent on industrialized food production accessed through the market economy ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"A7tXktFa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Popkin, 2006; van Vliet, Quiceno,, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Popkin, 2006; van Vliet, Quiceno,, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33649,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4L8SB4YA"],"itemData":{"id":33649,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Clinical Nutrition","language":"en","page":"289–98","title":"Global nutrition dynamics: the world is shifting rapidly toward a diet linked with non-communicable diseases.American","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Popkin","given":"B.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":33203,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8A8YLJBY"],"itemData":{"id":33203,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000362913100031","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/es-07782-200321","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","title":"Bushmeat networks link the forest to urban areas in the trifrontier region between Brazil, Colombia, and Peru","URL":"://WOS:000362913100031","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Vliet","given":"N.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Quiceno","given":"M. P."},{"family":"Cruz","given":"D."},{"family":"Aquino","given":"L. J. N.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Yague","given":"B."},{"family":"Schor","given":"T."},{"family":"Hernandez","given":"S."},{"family":"Nasi","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, knowledge of the nutrition transition in tropical and low-income countries is more limited ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BWI9qM9h","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nathalie van Vliet, Fa, and Nasi 2015)","plainCitation":"(Nathalie van Vliet, Fa, and Nasi 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33684,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FHZYRCAB"],"itemData":{"id":33684,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"ABSTRACT. Despite the fact that sustainability of bushmeat hunting in tropical areas is of major concern for conservation and development practitioners, we still know very little about how to measure sustainability and how to put in place sustainable bushmeat hunting systems. We review the current limits of traditional methods used to investigate sustainability of bushmeat hunting, discuss the need to incorporate the characteristics of complex systems into sustainability assessments, and suggest how resilience theories could assist in understanding bushmeat sustainability and more effective conservation of wildlife in tropical areas. Traditional methods used to assess the sustainability of bushmeat hunting include demographic models of population growth, one-off biological indicators, population trend methods, harvest-based indicators, and comparisons of demographic parameters between sites. These traditional biological sustainability indices have proved inadequate for measuring the impact of bushmeat hunting because sustainability is treated as a static, binary (yes or no) question, thus ignoring stochastic processes, the inherent variability of natural systems, and the complexity of hunting systems. We suggest that bushmeat hunting systems in tropical areas should be regarded as social-ecological systems in which the impacts of hunting on prey populations are not the only focus. Instead, the analysis of resilience aims at understanding the complex and dynamic relationships between the hunting ground, its resources, the stakeholders, and the different exogenous drivers of change that affect the components of the system at different scales. The main implication of using the resilience theory in the context of bushmeat hunting is the shift from the need to assess stocks with imprecise measures to the incorporation of the uncertainty and stochasticity inherent to complex systems in participatory and adaptive management processes. As such, the resilience analysis provides an unprecedented opportunity for the sustainable use of bushmeat and allows the identification of strategies to strengthen resilience when the system is found to be close to a given threshold, instead of reinforcing fortress conservation.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","source":"JSTOR","title":"Managing hunting under uncertainty: from one-off ecological indicators to resilience approaches in assessing the sustainability of bushmeat hunting","title-short":"Managing hunting under uncertainty","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/26270261","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Fa","given":"John"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The impacts of urbanization on sustainable use are diverse and varied across different contexts and interacts with other factors such as the extent of infrastructure development, other sources of food, and cultural and socio-economic conditions (Table 4.4) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1Pie2uep","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Poudyal, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Poudyal, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33650,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QTBIDTEP"],"itemData":{"id":33650,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urbanization and the Global Environment. Nova Science Publishers, Inc. Chapter","language":"en","page":"1–18 , 1–18","title":"Modeling the impact of changes in land use and socio-cultural patterns from urbanization of recrecreational fishing","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Poudyal","given":"N.C."},{"family":"Bowker","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Green","given":"G.T."},{"family":"Hodges","given":"D.G."}],"editor":[{"family":"Turunen","given":"Elen"},{"family":"Koskinen","given":"Anton"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, urban consumption of bushmeat in the Congo basin is significantly higher in the Congo basin in Africa, than the Amazon basin in South America ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WZs4uZe8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nasi, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Nasi, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33691,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3UFERZAZ"],"itemData":{"id":33691,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Protein from forest wildlife is crucial to rural food security and livelihoods across the tropics. The harvest of animals such as tapir, duikers, deer, pigs, peccaries, primates and larger rodents, birds and reptiles provides benefits to local people worth millions of US$ annually and represents around 6 million tonnes of animals extracted yearly. Vulnerability to hunting varies, with some species sustaining populations in heavily hunted secondary habitats, while others require intact forests with minimal harvesting to maintain healthy populations. Some species or groups have been characterized as ecosystem engineers and ecological keystone species. They affect plant distribution and structure ecosystems, through seed dispersal and predation, grazing, browsing, rooting and other mechanisms. Global attention has been drawn to their loss through debates regarding bushmeat, the “empty forest” syndrome and their ecological importance. However, information on the harvest remains fragmentary, along with understanding of ecological, socioeconomic and cultural dimensions. Here we assess the consequences, both for ecosystems and local livelihoods, of the loss of these species in the Amazon and Congo basins.","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554811798293872","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"355-368","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Empty forests, empty stomachs? Bushmeat and livelihoods in the Congo and Amazon Basins","title-short":"Empty forests, empty stomachs?","URL":"http://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/146554811798293872","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Nasi","given":"R."},{"family":"Taber","given":"A."},{"family":"Van Vliet","given":"N."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",9,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
   
Box 4.24. Urbanization and re-wilding in European cities

Urbanization and wild species use are a growing issue as in the city of London’s Metropolitan Green Belt in the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland and natural areas the Ruhr metropolitan area in Germany. The Ruhr area is the former center of the German coal and steel industry, comprises 20 cities and is one of the most densely populated conurbations in Europe. In both cases government-regulated green spaces were established in the 1920s-1940s to check urban sprawl and to secure recreational areas for the urban populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"csAqlcHG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Moncl\\\\uc0\\\\u250{}s, 2018; Schepelmann, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Monclús, 2018; Schepelmann, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12511,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4UBKDLUM"],"itemData":{"id":12511,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Urban Visions: From Planning Culture to Landscape Urbanism","event-place":"Cham","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-59047-9_26.","page":"269–78","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","title":"From Park Systems and Green Belts to Green Infrastructures","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-59047-9_26.","author":[{"family":"Monclús","given":"Javier"}],"editor":[{"family":"Medina","given":"Carmen Díez"},{"family":"Monclús","given":"Javier"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":5155,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LH9YZJEL"],"itemData":{"id":5155,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Handbook on Sustainability Transition and Sustainable Peace","event-place":"Cham","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-43884-9_28.","page":"593–612","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","title":"The Eco-Restructuring of the Ruhr District as an Example of a Managed Transition","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-43884-9_28.","author":[{"family":"Schepelmann","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Kemp","given":"René"},{"family":"Schneidewind","given":"Uwe"}],"editor":[{"family":"Hans Günter Brauch","given":"John Grin","non-dropping-particle":"edited by","suffix":"Úrsula Oswald Spring"},{"family":"Scheffran","given":"Jürgen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UJyjPXN9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. T. Han & Go, 2019)","plainCitation":"(A. T. Han & Go, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5231,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TNDKRCW3"],"itemData":{"id":5231,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Land Use Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.landusepol.2018.11.035.","issue":"February","language":"en","page":"644–56","title":"Explaining the National Variation of Land Use: A Cross-National Analysis of Greenbelt Policy in Five Countries","volume":"81","author":[{"family":"Han","given":"Albert T."},{"family":"Go","given":"Min Hee"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Subsequent to their establishment the London’s Metropolitan Green Belt and the green spaces in the Ruhr area went through very different developments. Due to wide public and political support and firm policy implementation, the London’s Metropolitan Green Belt is still largely intact. 24 percent of the area are designated Areas of Outstanding Beauty and 5 percent are Sites of Special Scientific Interest. Thirteen percent of London’s Metropolitan Green Belt land are priority habitat as identified by England’s Biodiversity Action Plan, with 12 out of 20 national priority habitat areas sited in the London’s Metropolitan Green Belt ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gCzTBE8p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CPRE, 2019)","plainCitation":"(CPRE, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":42861,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5XAKMDGG"],"itemData":{"id":42861,"type":"report","title":"A positive vision for London's green belt","URL":"https://www.cpre.org.uk/resources/a-positive-vision-for-londons-green-belt/","author":[{"family":"CPRE","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. But the London’s Metropolitan Green Belt is increasingly coming under social pressure by calls to allow housing development in the belt area to address shortages in London’s housing supply due to its steadily growing population ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UJv7mcG9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Elledge, 2017; Papworth, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Elledge, 2017; Papworth, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12074,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/22LHYJDF"],"itemData":{"id":12074,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Guardian","language":"en","title":"Loosen Britain’s Green Belt. It Is Stunting Our Young People","URL":"http://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2017/sep/22/green-belt-housing-crisis-planning-policy.","author":[{"family":"Elledge","given":"Jonn"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":12610,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4ZDKFL33"],"itemData":{"id":12610,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Adam Smith Institute","title":"A Garden of One’s Own. Suggestions for Development in the Metropolitan Green Belt","URL":"https://www.adamsmith.org/research/a-garden-of-ones-own.","author":[{"family":"Papworth","given":"Tom"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At the same time there are questions about the ecological quality and biodiversity in the London’s Metropolitan Green Belt. In the context of the continuing decrease of biodiversity in the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland, there are calls for the ecological and biodiversity enhancement of the London’s Metropolitan Green Belt through a Nature Recovery Network and the upgrading of existing and creation of new wild species habitats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"i10SpMoK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Assembly, 2020; CPRE, 2019; Lawton, 2010; McNab, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Assembly, 2020; CPRE, 2019; Lawton, 2010; McNab, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12220,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6YX8EQKE"],"itemData":{"id":12220,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"London City Hall","title":"Green Belt Needs Enhanced Use to Tackle the Climate Emergency","URL":"https://www.london.gov.uk//press-releases/assembly/green-belt-needs-enhanced-use.","author":[{"family":"Assembly","given":"London"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":42861,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5XAKMDGG"],"itemData":{"id":42861,"type":"report","title":"A positive vision for London's green belt","URL":"https://www.cpre.org.uk/resources/a-positive-vision-for-londons-green-belt/","author":[{"family":"CPRE","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":25292,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2ZGP42UB"],"itemData":{"id":25292,"type":"report","genre":"[Report to Defra.].","language":"en","title":"Making Space for Nature: A review of England’s wildlife sites and ecological network","URL":"https://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/20130402170324/http://archive.defra.gov.uk/environment/biodiversity/documents/201009space-for-nature.pdf","author":[{"family":"Lawton","given":"J.H."},{"family":"Brotherton","given":"P.N.M."},{"family":"Brown","given":"V.K."},{"family":"Elphick","given":"C."},{"family":"Fitter","given":"A.H."},{"family":"Forshaw","given":"J."},{"family":"Haddow","given":"R.W."},{"family":"Hilborne","given":"S."},{"family":"Leafe","given":"R.N."},{"family":"Mace","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Southgate","given":"M.P."},{"family":"Sutherland","given":"W.J."},{"family":"Tew","given":"T.E."},{"family":"Varley","given":"J."},{"family":"Wynne","given":"G.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":12332,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L772324P"],"itemData":{"id":12332,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Town & Country Planning","language":"en","title":"Time for a London Biodiversity Booster Belt?","URL":"https://greenerplaces.files.wordpress.com/2018/05/london-biodiversity-booster-belt.pdf.","author":[{"family":"McNab","given":"Douglas"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In contrast to the London’s Metropolitan Green Belt, the green spaces in the Ruhr area and its main rivers Emscher and Ruhr continued to be heavily polluted and environmentally degraded until the decline of coal mining in the 1960s-1980s. The Emscher served as an open sewerage for industry and households and its hydromorphology and hydrological cycle have been irreversibly impaired through mine subsidence and canalization. For many decades it was the most polluted river in Germany. Growing public dissatisfaction with the severe environmental degradation in the 1960s turned the Ruhr area into one of the cradles of the German environmental movement. In the ensuing decades, industry decline, job losses and shrinking populations were grasped as an opportunity for the social, ecological and environmental ‘structural change’ of the Ruhr area ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WGjOvxuX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bottmeyer, 2011; Institute, 2013; Schepelmann, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Bottmeyer, 2011; Institute, 2013; Schepelmann, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11891,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XGDDD7K3"],"itemData":{"id":11891,"type":"chapter","container-title":"In","event-place":"Marakech","language":"en","publisher":"International Federation of Surveyors","publisher-place":"Marakech","title":"Land Management of Former Industrial Landscapes in the Economic Metropolis Ruhr","URL":"http://www.fig.net/resources/proceedings/fig_proceedings/fig2011/papers/ts07b/ts07b_bottmeyer_4798.pdf.","author":[{"family":"Bottmeyer","given":"Manfred"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":2666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CY3PLTNK"],"itemData":{"id":2666,"type":"document","language":"de","title":"Emscher 3.0—From Grey to Blue—Or, how the Blue Sky over the Ruhr Region Fell into the Emscher. Verlag Kettler","URL":"https://wupperinst.org/en/a/wi/a/s/ad/6037","author":[{"family":"Institute","given":"Wuppertal"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":5155,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LH9YZJEL"],"itemData":{"id":5155,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Handbook on Sustainability Transition and Sustainable Peace","event-place":"Cham","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-43884-9_28.","page":"593–612","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","title":"The Eco-Restructuring of the Ruhr District as an Example of a Managed Transition","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-43884-9_28.","author":[{"family":"Schepelmann","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Kemp","given":"René"},{"family":"Schneidewind","given":"Uwe"}],"editor":[{"family":"Hans Günter Brauch","given":"John Grin","non-dropping-particle":"edited by","suffix":"Úrsula Oswald Spring"},{"family":"Scheffran","given":"Jürgen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The renaturation of the Emscher river and its tributaries through the ‘Emscher conversion’ and the establishment of a biotope network along the ‘New Emscher Valley,’ serve both as a beacon for the makeover of the Ruhr area and as literal and metaphorical breeding ground for revitalized nature and biodiversity, social, recreational, cultural and economic change. The species diversity in the Emscher catchment has increased from 170 in 1990 to 450 in 2019. Approximately one fifth of the species now found in the catchment are listed as endangered by state and federal governments. But the ecological rejuvenation of the Emscher catchment is only possible due to a large-scale wastewater management engineering project. New treatment plants and over 400 kilometers of underground wastewater canals have been constructed to ensure that no sewerage will be discharged into the Emscher ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"y2N6VxNk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(GR\\\\uc0\\\\u220{}NE, 2019; Institute, 2013)","plainCitation":"(GRÜNE, 2019; Institute, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4125,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HW3Z7MVH"],"itemData":{"id":4125,"type":"chapter","container-title":"GRÜNE LIGA eV","language":"de","title":"Renaturierung des Emscher-Systems","URL":"http://www.wrrl-info.de/docs/wrrl_steckbrief_renaturierungemscher_2019.pdf.","author":[{"family":"GRÜNE","given":"L.I.G.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":2666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CY3PLTNK"],"itemData":{"id":2666,"type":"document","language":"de","title":"Emscher 3.0—From Grey to Blue—Or, how the Blue Sky over the Ruhr Region Fell into the Emscher. Verlag Kettler","URL":"https://wupperinst.org/en/a/wi/a/s/ad/6037","author":[{"family":"Institute","given":"Wuppertal"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Biodiversity in the London’s Metropolitan Green Belt has largely been preserved since its inception but can also been enhanced to reverse overall trends of declining biodiversity in the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland, help mitigate climate change and provide other ecosystems services. After a severe destruction of biodiversity in the Ruhr area, biodiversity is currently being actively recreated in an interplay of deliberate human actions and nature reclaiming its place. The sustainability of biodiversity in the London’s Metropolitan Green Belt in part depends on a willingness of human actors to preserve, increase and enhance habitats and to continue to restrict or limit construction in its area ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WGadhKr4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(CPRE, 2018, 2019)","plainCitation":"(CPRE, 2018, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":42862,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z8JFDI9R"],"itemData":{"id":42862,"type":"report","title":"London's 'protected' land: the extent, location and character of designated Green Belt and Metropolitan Open Land in Greater London","URL":"https://www.london.gov.uk/sites/default/files/ad_83_cpre_gigl_report_final.pdf","author":[{"family":"CPRE","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":42861,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5XAKMDGG"],"itemData":{"id":42861,"type":"report","title":"A positive vision for London's green belt","URL":"https://www.cpre.org.uk/resources/a-positive-vision-for-londons-green-belt/","author":[{"family":"CPRE","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The long-term sustainability of resurging nature and biodiversity in the Ruhr region in part seems to depend on technology: “Technical and natural infrastructure in the Emscher region will always be interconnected and technical ‘support’ for the natural hydrological cycle will be necessary here in the long term because of the subsidence caused by mining” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"q2uIzCei","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Institute, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Institute, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CY3PLTNK"],"itemData":{"id":2666,"type":"document","language":"de","title":"Emscher 3.0—From Grey to Blue—Or, how the Blue Sky over the Ruhr Region Fell into the Emscher. Verlag Kettler","URL":"https://wupperinst.org/en/a/wi/a/s/ad/6037","author":[{"family":"Institute","given":"Wuppertal"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

 
Figure 4.8. Urban Population Density. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YeO7hNJg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations, 2019)","plainCitation":"(United Nations, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33677,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PG44M5YT"],"itemData":{"id":33677,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-92-1-148319-2","language":"English","note":"OCLC: 1120698127","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"World urbanization prospects: the 2018 revision","title-short":"World urbanization prospects","author":[{"literal":"United Nations"},{"literal":"Department of Economic and Social Affairs"},{"literal":"Population Division"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} © 2019 by United Nations, made available under a Creative Commons license CC BY 3.0 IGO: http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/igo/. The designations employed and the presentation of material on the maps used in the assessment do not imply the expression of any opinion whatsoever on the part of IPBES concerning the legal status of any country, territory, city or area or of its authorities, or concerning the delimitation of its frontiers or boundaries. These maps have been prepared or used for the sole purpose of facilitating the assessment of the broad biogeographical areas represented therein and for purposes of representing scientific data spatially.


      
      The interaction between wealth and urbanization also depends on context, for example in sub-Saharan Africa meat from wild species is consumed by wealthier households in urban areas, but the opposite pattern is observed in lower income rural areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EX3YmWG0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. S. Brashares 2011)","plainCitation":"(J. S. Brashares 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32802,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KAYP49FJ"],"itemData":{"id":32802,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1011526108","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"34","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","language":"en","page":"13931-13936","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Economic and geographic drivers of wildlife consumption in rural Africa","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/doi/10.1073/pnas.1011526108","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Brashares","given":"J. S."},{"family":"Golden","given":"C. D."},{"family":"Weinbaum","given":"K. Z."},{"family":"Barrett","given":"C. B."},{"family":"Okello","given":"G. V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8,23]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A further issue is the development of peri-urban areas where densities are urban but economic infrastructure and services are still rural-oriented ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tBKKPOzX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kulabako, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Kulabako, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33622,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QWZQDT5U"],"itemData":{"id":33622,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International journal of environmental health research","ISSN":"0960-3123","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"International journal of environmental health research","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"231-257","title":"Environmental health practices, constraints and possible interventions in peri-urban settlements in developing countries–a review of Kampala, Uganda","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Kulabako","given":"Robinah Nakawunde"},{"family":"Nalubega","given":"Maimuna"},{"family":"Wozei","given":"Eleanor"},{"family":"Thunvik","given":"Roger"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These dense peri-urban contexts can lead to overexploitation and unsustainable use in densely populated areas and also to import of wild species from elsewhere ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"P2SI1zPD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(van Velden, 2018)","plainCitation":"(van Velden, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33689,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4MPLYDH6"],"itemData":{"id":33689,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2018.03.022","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"345-356","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The evidence for the bushmeat crisis in African savannas: A systematic quantitative literature review","title-short":"The evidence for the bushmeat crisis in African savannas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320717321766","volume":"221","author":[{"family":"Velden","given":"Julia","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Wilson","given":"Kerrie"},{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Finally, although urbanization can physically separate people from nature, it may also engender awareness of the importance of nature to human well- being. The gentrification of rural areas also has potential to disrupt culturally and economically important plants, algae and fungi collection ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ly62hysT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grabbatin, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Grabbatin, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33679,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CW8A5AHJ"],"itemData":{"id":33679,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Scholars working around the world have drawn attention to the physical and social changes associated with rural gentri?cation. Case studies from the United States have focused on how these patterns lead to the cultural displacement and replacement of land-based livelihoods, including non-timber forest product (NTFP) practices. Scholars have also documented the persistence of culturally and economically important NTFP practices in urban and suburban areas. We reconcile these disparate outcomes, displacement on the one hand and persistence on the other, by focusing on the social relationships that co-produce land use and livelihood change. Our case investigates how African American sweetgrass basketmakers in Mount Pleasant South Carolina negotiate the complex terrain of a rapidly urbanizing and gentrifying landscape.","container-title":"Geoforum","DOI":"10.1016/j.geoforum.2011.06.007","ISSN":"00167185","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Geoforum","language":"en","page":"638-649","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"“I Still Have the Old Tradition”: The co-production of sweetgrass basketry and coastal development","title-short":"“I Still Have the Old Tradition”","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0016718511001096","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Grabbatin","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Hurley","given":"Patrick T."},{"family":"Halfacre","given":"Angela"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
Table 4.5. Examples of practices in urban areas
Context and Issue 
Impact on Sustainable Use
Geographic region and Reference
Recreational Fishing
Recreational fishing is a popular activity in the United States of America. 
Urbanization measured by the growth in income, employment, structural shifts in demographics, and increases in average commuting time due to sprawling significantly and negatively impact public participation in fishing as measured by fishing license sales determined by regression analysis. 
 
United States of America 
ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aXyLY3Hp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Poudyal, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Poudyal, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33650,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QTBIDTEP"],"itemData":{"id":33650,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urbanization and the Global Environment. Nova Science Publishers, Inc. Chapter","language":"en","page":"1–18 , 1–18","title":"Modeling the impact of changes in land use and socio-cultural patterns from urbanization of recrecreational fishing","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Poudyal","given":"N.C."},{"family":"Bowker","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Green","given":"G.T."},{"family":"Hodges","given":"D.G."}],"editor":[{"family":"Turunen","given":"Elen"},{"family":"Koskinen","given":"Anton"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
Bushmeat hunting for pigeons 
Hunting for pigeons in Samoa is by both rural and lower income individuals as well as wealthier sports hunters from non-rural areas. Wealthier hunters tend to hunt for recreation and personal consumption whereas lower income hunters hunt for sale to restaurants and targeted consumers. Consumers of pigeons tend by the urban wealthy. 
Samoa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tM78AFah","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stirnemann, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Stirnemann, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33644,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ISIATQKJ"],"itemData":{"id":33644,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and conservation","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"931–946","title":"Interactive impacts of by-catch take and elite consumption of illegal wildlife","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Stirnemann","given":"R.L."},{"family":"Stirnemann","given":"I.A."},{"family":"Abbot","given":"D."},{"family":"Biggs","given":"D."},{"family":"Heinsohn","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}
Bushmeat consumption
In the Colombian Amazon rural and urban and residents eat bushmeat, as well as domesticated and industrially processed meat. However, in a rural to peri-urban to urban gradient the frequency of consumption of bushmeat decreases substantially.  
Colombia 
ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BoagX5T7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(van Vliet, Fa,, 2015)","plainCitation":"(van Vliet, Fa,, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33684,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FHZYRCAB"],"itemData":{"id":33684,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"ABSTRACT. Despite the fact that sustainability of bushmeat hunting in tropical areas is of major concern for conservation and development practitioners, we still know very little about how to measure sustainability and how to put in place sustainable bushmeat hunting systems. We review the current limits of traditional methods used to investigate sustainability of bushmeat hunting, discuss the need to incorporate the characteristics of complex systems into sustainability assessments, and suggest how resilience theories could assist in understanding bushmeat sustainability and more effective conservation of wildlife in tropical areas. Traditional methods used to assess the sustainability of bushmeat hunting include demographic models of population growth, one-off biological indicators, population trend methods, harvest-based indicators, and comparisons of demographic parameters between sites. These traditional biological sustainability indices have proved inadequate for measuring the impact of bushmeat hunting because sustainability is treated as a static, binary (yes or no) question, thus ignoring stochastic processes, the inherent variability of natural systems, and the complexity of hunting systems. We suggest that bushmeat hunting systems in tropical areas should be regarded as social-ecological systems in which the impacts of hunting on prey populations are not the only focus. Instead, the analysis of resilience aims at understanding the complex and dynamic relationships between the hunting ground, its resources, the stakeholders, and the different exogenous drivers of change that affect the components of the system at different scales. The main implication of using the resilience theory in the context of bushmeat hunting is the shift from the need to assess stocks with imprecise measures to the incorporation of the uncertainty and stochasticity inherent to complex systems in participatory and adaptive management processes. As such, the resilience analysis provides an unprecedented opportunity for the sustainable use of bushmeat and allows the identification of strategies to strengthen resilience when the system is found to be close to a given threshold, instead of reinforcing fortress conservation.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","source":"JSTOR","title":"Managing hunting under uncertainty: from one-off ecological indicators to resilience approaches in assessing the sustainability of bushmeat hunting","title-short":"Managing hunting under uncertainty","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/26270261","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Fa","given":"John"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} 

4.2.3.3.5. Rural communities and development
Key messages:
* Rural peoples play a vital role in sustainable wild species use – indirectly in supporting and sustaining wild species habitats (established but incomplete) {4.2.3.3.5}.
* Many rural livelihoods include the harvest of wild species as part of mixed and diversified livelihoods (i.e., that include small scale agriculture, hunting, fishing etc.); these kinds of livelihoods are on the decline due to the industrialization of agriculture and other large scale development pressures (e.g., clear cutting of forests) (established by incomplete {4.2.3.3.5}.
* Rural regions and livelihoods including the harvest of wild species play a key role in supporting the health and well-being of peoples, particularly where other livelihoods fail (e.g., in boom-bust economies) (established but incomplete) {4.2.3.3.5}.

Livelihoods – Diversified and Mixed Economies
Many rural livelihoods include the harvest of wild species as part of mixed and diversified livelihoods (i.e., that include small scale agriculture, hunting, fishing, etc.); ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DTwY5uKU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Regmi, 2003; Wunder, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Regmi, 2003; Wunder, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33693,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QIG4P57H"],"itemData":{"id":33693,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2014.03.007","ISSN":"0305750X","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","language":"en","page":"S1-S11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Forests, Livelihoods, and Conservation: Broadening the Empirical Base","title-short":"Forests, Livelihoods, and Conservation","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0305750X14000734","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Wunder","given":"Sven"},{"family":"Angelsen","given":"Arild"},{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12]]}}},{"id":33800,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8LJ7XWKV"],"itemData":{"id":33800,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"International Conference on Rural Livelihoods, Forests and Biodiversity","page":"19–23","publisher":"Citeseer","title":"Contribution of agroforestry for rural livelihoods: A case of Dhading District, Nepal","author":[{"family":"Regmi","given":"Bishwa Nath"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These kinds of livelihoods are on the decline in many regions due to the industrialization of agriculture and other large scale development pressures (e.g., clear cutting of forests) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dVIlRDwQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barrett, 2001; Ellis, 2004; Fabusoro, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Barrett, 2001; Ellis, 2004; Fabusoro, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33810,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/767PDNSN"],"itemData":{"id":33810,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Food policy","ISSN":"0306-9192","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Food policy","note":"number: 4\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"315-331","title":"Nonfarm income diversification and household livelihood strategies in rural Africa: concepts, dynamics, and policy implications","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Barrett","given":"Christopher B"},{"family":"Reardon","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Webb","given":"Patrick"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":33690,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A8YHMKNY"],"itemData":{"id":33690,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations Livelihood Support Programme","title":"Livelihood diversification and natural resource access","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/a-ad689e.pdf","author":[{"family":"Ellis","given":"Frank"},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":33805,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/22Q2G97L"],"itemData":{"id":33805,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Agriculture","issue":"4","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"417–438","title":"Forms and determinants of rural livelihoods diversification in Ogun State, Nigeria","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Fabusoro","given":"E"},{"family":"Omotayo","given":"AM"},{"family":"Apantaku","given":"SO"},{"family":"Okuneye","given":"PA"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Livelihood diversification is particularly important for women ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UmHeEO6V","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lakwo, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Lakwo, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QKFUI7B7"],"itemData":{"id":33811,"type":"book","ISBN":"90-5448-069-6","publisher":"Leiden: African Studies Centre","title":"Microfinance, rural livelihoods, and women's empowerment in Uganda","author":[{"family":"Lakwo","given":"Alfred"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
   The role of wild species within these rural economies can be measures by the proportion of wild meat that contributes to rural diets as in Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YHSZYN2L","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Merode, 2004; Hasselberg, 2020)","plainCitation":"(de Merode, 2004; Hasselberg, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33688,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RHMH8KSL"],"itemData":{"id":33688,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"We examine the relationship between poverty and the use of wild foods, namely bushmeat, fish and wild plants, within a Congolese agricultural community. A sample of 121 households were monitored over a 16-month period, using a combination of participatory and quantitative survey techniques, to assess their wealth and their production, consumption and market sales of wild foods. Households varied in wealth but all could be considered subject to extreme poverty (income below US$1 per capita per day). Our analyses indicate that wild foods play a small role in household consumption but a major role in household income. Hence, over 90% of both bushmeat and fish production is sold at market. In addition, the value of wild foods increases in the “lean season” when agricultural production is low. We also find that the poorest households in this community are unable to capitalise on the most valuable wild foods, bushmeat and fish, as a source of food or cash income. We use an entitlements framework to explain the factors that determine such wealth-related variation between households, indicating that household use of wild foods is determined more by social and economic constraints than by resource abundance in this community. Nevertheless, our findings show that overall the small-scale commercialisation of wild foods provides a vital source of income for rural households living in extreme poverty.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2003.10.005","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","page":"573-581","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The value of bushmeat and other wild foods to rural households living in extreme poverty in Democratic Republic of Congo","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320703004038","volume":"118","author":[{"family":"Merode","given":"Emmanuel","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Homewood","given":"Katherine"},{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"Guy"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",8,1]]}}},{"id":33812,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3IVJ4BHK"],"itemData":{"id":33812,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Food Security","ISSN":"2211-9124","journalAbbreviation":"Global Food Security","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"100380","title":"Fish for food and nutrition security in Ghana: Challenges and opportunities","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Hasselberg","given":"Astrid Elise"},{"family":"Aakre","given":"Inger"},{"family":"Scholtens","given":"Joeri"},{"family":"Overå","given":"Ragnhild"},{"family":"Kolding","given":"Jeppe"},{"family":"Bank","given":"Michael S"},{"family":"Atter","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Kjellevold","given":"Marian"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, Asia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xhuxfbb6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Erni, 2015; Pauly, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Erni, 2015; Pauly, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33647,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NBI6FH9F"],"itemData":{"id":33647,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.11.010","language":"en","page":"371–374","title":"A vision for marine fisheries in a global blue economy","volume":"87","author":[{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":34524,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WXPH6C8F"],"itemData":{"id":34524,"type":"report","title":"Shifting Cultivation, Livelihood and Food Security New and Old Challenges for Indigenous Peoples in Asia","URL":"http://www.fao.org/3/a-i4580e.pdf","author":[{"family":"Erni","given":"Christian"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and in indigenous communities in the Arctic ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"I3pUtUrV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kuhnlein, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Kuhnlein, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33801,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FHUVE238"],"itemData":{"id":33801,"type":"article-newspaper","title":"Indigenous peoples’ food systems: the many dimensions of culture, diversity and environment for nutrition and health. Food and Agriculture Organization, Rome, Italy. [online] URL: http://www.fao.org/3/i0370e/i0370e00.htm","author":[{"family":"Kuhnlein","given":"H. V."},{"family":"Erasmus","given":"B."},{"family":"Spigelski.","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} as examples. Changes in the dynamics and opportunities of sustainable rural livelihoods include the impacts of:
* Structural adjustment and economic policies that displace small land holders in favour of export cropping and industrialization of resourcesITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BcAyGEYc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lipton & Ahmed, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Lipton & Ahmed, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33804,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W7NNQY89"],"itemData":{"id":33804,"type":"article-journal","note":"publisher: IDS","title":"Impact of structural adjustment on sustainable rural livelihoods: a review of the literature","author":[{"family":"Lipton","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Ahmed","given":"Ismail"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
* Mining ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4On8kW5g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. L. Parlee, 2018)","plainCitation":"(B. L. Parlee, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34961,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JUP575TP"],"itemData":{"id":34961,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science advances","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Science advances","note":"number: 2","page":"e1701611","title":"Undermining subsistence: Barren-ground caribou in a “tragedy of open access”","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda L"},{"family":"Sandlos","given":"John"},{"family":"Natcher","given":"David C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
* Exclusionary conservation (i.e., protected areas) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jdzEqLTh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. J. Bennett & Dearden, 2014)","plainCitation":"(N. J. Bennett & Dearden, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26017,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PLID4ZES","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/87D25QK5"],"itemData":{"id":26017,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"107–116","title":"Why local people do not support conservation: Community perceptions of marine protected area livelihood impacts, governance and management in Thailand","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"N.J."},{"family":"Dearden","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
* Climate Change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"w33Gy84g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Agrawal & Perrin, 2009; Gentle & Maraseni, 2012; Ziervogel & Calder, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Agrawal & Perrin, 2009; Gentle & Maraseni, 2012; Ziervogel & Calder, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33808,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M2948MX6"],"itemData":{"id":33808,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Adapting to climate change: thresholds, values, governance","language":"en","page":"350–367","title":"Climate adaptation, local institutions and rural livelihoods","author":[{"family":"Agrawal","given":"A."},{"family":"Perrin","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":33802,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TIIWQCRZ"],"itemData":{"id":33802,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental science & policy","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"24–34","title":"Climate change, poverty and livelihoods: adaptation practices by rural mountain communities in Nepal","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Gentle","given":"Popular"},{"family":"Maraseni","given":"Tek Narayan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":33803,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/66Q3DHQP"],"itemData":{"id":33803,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Area","issue":"4","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"403–417","title":"Climate variability and rural livelihoods: assessing the impact of seasonal climate forecasts in Lesotho","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Ziervogel","given":"Gina"},{"family":"Calder","given":"Rebecca"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"};
* Other factors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HE8A6hIi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Devereux, 2001; Shameem, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Devereux, 2001; Shameem, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33806,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P4DPIWUV"],"itemData":{"id":33806,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Food security in sub-Saharan Africa","note":"publisher: ITDG Publishing","page":"201–230","title":"Food security information systems.","author":[{"family":"Devereux","given":"Stephen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":33799,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K2HBY59A"],"itemData":{"id":33799,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"79–87","title":"Vulnerability of rural livelihoods to multiple stressors: A case study from the southwest coastal region of Bangladesh","volume":"102","author":[{"family":"Shameem","given":"Masud Iqbal Md"},{"family":"Momtaz","given":"Salim"},{"family":"Rauscher","given":"Ray"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Rural regions and wild species use – resilience
Rural regions and livelihoods including the harvest of wild species pay a key role in supporting the health and well-being of peoples, particularly where other livelihoods fail (e.g., in boom-bust economies). Although urban areas are expected to absorb virtually all of the future growth of the world’s population ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cIJpKU9T","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations, 2019)","plainCitation":"(United Nations, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32422,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CAXKGHAL"],"itemData":{"id":32422,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: New York","title":"World Urbanization Prospects 2018: Highlights. Department of Economic and Social Affairs","author":[{"family":"United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the rural population remains representative (45%) and also has major challenges to achieve sustainable development for its dwellers, such as the balance between the environmental, social and economic dimensions. With almost half of the planet's inhabitants still engaged in the appropriation of natural resources, the conservation of biodiversity cannot be separated from the use of natural resources. This appropriation of natural resources is carried out by a myriad of rural or primary producers through the management of terrestrial, marine and freshwater ecosystems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MUzIl8m1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Toledo, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Toledo, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33640,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DSGXDJFI"],"itemData":{"id":33640,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Encyclopedia of biodiversity","language":"en","page":"451–463","title":"Indigenous peoples and biodiversity","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Toledo","given":"V.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many communities living in the rural areas around the world depend on the use of wild species for their livelihoods. This is the case of indigenous peoples and local communities, which possess significant knowledge on the wild species surrounding them and often use it for subsistence and other purposes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DacYJNv5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The use of wild species can have a direct or indirect relation with the development level of their regions. Either by common sense or by some areas of knowledge, rural development is usually associated with physical and services improvement to rural area (e.g., roads, infrastructure, access to material assets and immaterial goods-market, credit, internet), but it goes beyond improvements in infrastructure and services. Rural development can be understood as a driver that facilitates the overexploitation of wild species, but also considering the other direction of this relation, this is, the possibility that the sustainable use of wild species can bring rural sustainable development to a region and its households.
      In the vast majority of times, the improvement of infrastructure in a region aims to increase the exploitation of natural resources in the territory to send the production to different markets (local or for exportation), in some situations, can in fact improve the infrastructure of services and the living conditions of the people who live there. Several cases illustrate the impacts of the transformations in the infrastructure of rural areas in the access and use of natural resources, including those giving origin to emerging diseases by land use changes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nQlKXfHk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Patz, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Patz, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33637,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/REZUWSFM"],"itemData":{"id":33637,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Health Perspectives","issue":"10","language":"en","page":"1092–1098","title":"Working Group on Land Use Change and Disease Emergence","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Patz","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."},{"family":"Tabor","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Aguirre","given":"A.A."},{"family":"Pearl","given":"M."},{"family":"Epstein","given":"J."},{"family":"Wolfe","given":"D.N."},{"family":"Kilpatrick","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Foufopoulos","given":"J."},{"family":"Molyneux","given":"D."},{"family":"Bradley","given":"D."},{"literal":"J."},{"family":"the","given":"Members"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, such as logging roads ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6zhVEGJj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wolfe, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Wolfe, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33641,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QUMPJ9XG"],"itemData":{"id":33641,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Hum Health","language":"en","page":"10–25","title":"Deforestation, hunting and the ecology of microbial emergence","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Wolfe","given":"N.D."},{"family":"Eitel","given":"M.N."},{"family":"Gockowski","given":"J."},{"family":"Muchaal","given":"P.K."},{"family":"Nolte","given":"C."},{"family":"Prosser","given":"A.T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thus, it is essential to contemplate the different perspectives related to the concept of rural development and its interaction with the use of wild species, especially considering indigenous peoples and local communities, for whom the Western concept of development is not part of their worldview, causing a permanent tension between the development objectives of nations, founded upon concepts such as progress, and a broader conception which sees humanity as “essentially integrated within its environment and supported by the notion of the good life” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JffXRY4m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cuadra, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Cuadra, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33672,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E4VKSZ88"],"itemData":{"id":33672,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ambiente & Sociedade","DOI":"10.1590/1809-4422ASOCEx02V1822015en","issue":"2","language":"pt","page":"23–40","title":"Indigenous people, socio-environmental conflict and post-development in Latin America","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Cuadra","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, or the paradigm of Buen Vivir (Good Living), which emerged in Bolivia and Ecuador and raises a relationship of society in harmony with nature from a vision of socio-ecological transition ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"J1k6098K","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Matiolli & Nozica, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Matiolli & Nozica, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33654,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZQXSHKUD"],"itemData":{"id":33654,"type":"document","language":"pt","note":"page: 26–33\\nvolume: 40 -1","title":"Territorial management of good living. Landscape, Heritage and Biodiversity, Divergent or Convergent Concepts? Anuário do Instituto de Geociências – UFRJ","author":[{"family":"Matiolli","given":"L."},{"family":"Nozica","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      Although rural development can be associated with aspects of progress and infrastructure improvements in rural areas, with both positive and negative impacts on local biodiversity, it is important to situate the concept more broadly, considering, for instance, to what extent the existence of highways, power transmission lines, access to internet or other services and infrastructure contribute to the development of a region in an effectively sustainable way. For example, on roads near logging areas or close to forest areas, there is an increased risk of fauna loss, such as gorillas and other monkeys in Congo and neighboring countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"G8X4odFM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wash, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Wash, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33697,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UVP4T4J9"],"itemData":{"id":33697,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","language":"en","page":"462–473","title":"Catastrophic ape decline in western equatorial Africa","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Wash","given":"Peter D."},{"family":"April.xuWatt","given":"W.D."},{"family":"Scott","given":"C.D."},{"family":"White","given":"W.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The impacts can also come from agricultural areas, whether recently implemented or not, as in the case of the changes brought about by the Green Revolution, also called conservative modernization specially in Latin America countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wg2AHrd9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Delgado, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Delgado, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33671,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XGFYNLZT"],"itemData":{"id":33671,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Estudos Avançados","issue":"43","language":"pt","page":"157–172","title":"Expansão e modernização do setor agropecuário no pós-guerra: um estudo de reflexão agrária","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Delgado","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, because, despite being associated with the idea of progress, with agricultural production on an industrial scale, and technological packages involving agricultural implements and modified seeds, it maintained the agrarian structure, not promoting improvements in the socioeconomic conditions of the poorest rural populations, and still causing serious environmental impacts.
      In the same way that the concept of development, since the 1990s, started to be linked to the adjective “sustainable”, the debate on rural development also started to add the discussion around sustainability and environmental issues, the local and territorial approach, non-agricultural rural activities, rural-urban interrelationships, among others. From that period, the resumption of the debate on rural development takes place mainly around four key elements: the eradication of rural poverty, the question of the role of social actors and their political participation, the idea of territory as a reference unit and the central concern with environmental sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ixPXIXlJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. H. Schneider, 2004)","plainCitation":"(S. H. Schneider, 2004)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32219,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N3CGMMC7"],"itemData":{"id":32219,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"245–258","title":"Abrupt non-linear climate change, irreversibility and surprise","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Schneider","given":"S.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Rural development can also be understood as a process of social and economic change that occurs in rural areas, where there is a context of expanding interdependence in social and economic relations on an international scale, as one of the effects and conditions of globalization. This process of global transformation, which is increasingly influenced by modern technologies, was most clearly seen in the industrial or secondary production sector, but it also had an impact on the forms of productive organization and labor relations in the primary sector, in activities related to agriculture, livestock and fishing, as well as in forestry and plant extraction, for example.
      The opening of markets also has caused an intensification of trade and the strengthening of large agri-food chains, changing the configuration of rural areas. In many regions of the world, the rural environment is no longer the specific place for agricultural activities and the varied forms of income complementation allow the income of many families living there to stabilize throughout the year and the children no longer need to leave rural areas to find jobs. In this sense, the notion of pluri-activity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rSS2C2cU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B\\\\uc0\\\\u322{}\\\\uc0\\\\u261{}d, 2015)","plainCitation":"(B??d, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33675,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A2JW3VUV"],"itemData":{"id":33675,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Institute of Rural and Agricultural Development, Polish Academy of Sciences","language":"en","title":"Pluriactivity of farming families – old phenomenon in new times","URL":"http://ageconsearch.umn.edu/bitstream/139799/2/vol.%207_12.pdf.","author":[{"family":"B??d","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} provides a broader meaning than that of diversification of production. Diversification meaning different forms of agricultural production, one oriented at non-food use, with the typical sub-categories of energy crops, fiber crops, herbs for medicinal use, agroforestry (for wood and biomass production), animal breeding, etc. As examples of pluri-activity, there are new on-farm activities, such as farm-based activities (industries, services) not related to food, agricultural production or tourism. Another kind of pluri-activity are new on-farm activities, i.e., farm-based activities (industries, services) that are not related to food, agricultural production or tourism. “Other important forms are sporting activities (not linked to tourism), equestrian activities (e.g., horse breeding), hunting, fishing, bike rental, school farms, offering of workshops/courses, care farms, haulage, etc.” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RYv91s57","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B\\\\uc0\\\\u322{}\\\\uc0\\\\u261{}d, 2015)","plainCitation":"(B??d, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33675,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A2JW3VUV"],"itemData":{"id":33675,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Institute of Rural and Agricultural Development, Polish Academy of Sciences","language":"en","title":"Pluriactivity of farming families – old phenomenon in new times","URL":"http://ageconsearch.umn.edu/bitstream/139799/2/vol.%207_12.pdf.","author":[{"family":"B??d","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      An approach to rural development particularly aimed at developing countries focuses on rural household strategies and livelihood diversification ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"alBG3Z3s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ellis, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Ellis, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34662,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RI7MCNYX"],"itemData":{"id":34662,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Development Studies","DOI":"10.1080/00220389808422553","ISSN":"0022-0388, 1743-9140","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Development Studies","language":"en","page":"1-38","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Household strategies and rural livelihood diversification","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/00220389808422553","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Ellis","given":"Frank"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, showing that the initiatives and actions that generate significant impacts in improving the living conditions of these populations and that expand their perspectives to guarantee social and economic reproduction are, most of the times, in the very localities and territories where they live, i.e., it is important to propose economic activities also related to the rural area vocation, not only to reinforce the natural resources, but also to valorize socio-cultural aspects, protect immaterial heritage and keep the social reproduction. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zKbaE7DS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ellis & Biggs, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Ellis & Biggs, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33670,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LALFJL5G"],"itemData":{"id":33670,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Development Policy Review","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"437–448","title":"Evolving themes in rural development 1950s-2000s","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Ellis","given":"Frank"},{"family":"Biggs","given":"Stephen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} defines rural development as a set of actions and practices that aim to reduce poverty in rural areas, aiming to stimulate a participation process that empowers rural inhabitants, making them capable of defining and controlling their priorities for change.
      In the scientific and policy debates on the future of agriculture and rural development, multifunctional agriculture is another key notion. Apart from public goods (landscape, biodiversity, etc.) this includes goods and services produced for non-food markets (energy, care, tourism, etc.) as well as functions provided by agriculture as distinctive product attributes on niche food markets related to food quality, animal welfare, environment friendliness, etc. Functions that cannot be directly associated with goods, services or product attributes, but rather represent non-marketable public benefits of agriculture, are considered relevant for the analysis of multifunctional agriculture (e.g., quality of life, food security, etc.) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4aJbDChc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Renting, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Renting, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33651,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SWHUVCCK"],"itemData":{"id":33651,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"112– 123","title":"Exploring multifunctional agriculture. A review of conceptual approaches and prospects for an integrative transitional framework","volume":"90","author":[{"family":"Renting","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      
Rural development – wild species 
Among the impacts of economic development in a region and the conservation of local biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lNZqmX6Q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ju, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Ju, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33686,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A7X9NKM7"],"itemData":{"id":33686,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Background: Locally harvested wild edible plants (WEPs) provide food as well as cash income for indigenous people and are of great importance in ensuring global food security. Some also play a significant role in maintaining the productivity and stability of traditional agro-ecosystems. Shangri-la region of Yunnan Province, SW China, is regarded as a biodiversity hotspot. People living there have accumulated traditional knowledge about plants. However, with economic development, WEPs are threatened and the associated traditional knowledge is in danger of being lost. Therefore, ethnobotanical surveys were conducted throughout this area to investigate and document the wild edible plants traditionally used by local Tibetan people. Methods: Twenty-nine villages were selected to carry out the field investigations. Information was collected using direct observation, semi-structured interviews, individual discussions, key informant interviews, focus group discussions, questionnaires and participatory rural appraisal (PRA). Results: Information about 168 wild edible plant species in 116 genera of 62 families was recorded and specimens were collected. Most species were edible greens (80 species) or fruits (78). These WEPs are sources for local people, especially those living in remote rural areas, to obtain mineral elements and vitamins. More than half of the species (70%) have multiple use(s) besides food value. Some are crop wild relatives that could be used for crop improvement. Several also have potential values for further commercial exploitation. However, the utilization of WEPs and related knowledge are eroding rapidly, especially in the areas with convenient transportation and booming tourism. Conclusion: Wild food plants species are abundant and diverse in Shangri-la region. They provide food and nutrients to local people and could also be a source of cash income. However, both WEPs and their associated indigenous knowledge are facing various threats. Thus, conservation and sustainable utilization of these plants in this area are of the utmost importance. Documentation of these species may provide basic information for conservation, possibly further exploitation, and will preserve local traditional knowledge.","container-title":"JOURNAL OF ETHNOBIOLOGY AND ETHNOMEDICINE","DOI":"10.1186/1746-4269-9-28","ISSN":"1746-4269","language":"English","title":"Eating from the wild: diversity of wild edible plants used by Tibetans in Shangri-la region, Yunnan, China","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Ju","given":"Yan"},{"family":"Zhuo","given":"Jingxian"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Bo"},{"family":"Long","given":"Chunlin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4,19]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the risks and benefits of changes in land use to human health should also be considered. Land use changes include “deforestation, road construction, agricultural encroachment, dam building, irrigation, coastal zone degradation, wetland modification, mining, the concentration or expansion of urban environments, and other activities” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SNCADGbV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Patz, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Patz, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33637,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/REZUWSFM"],"itemData":{"id":33637,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Health Perspectives","issue":"10","language":"en","page":"1092–1098","title":"Working Group on Land Use Change and Disease Emergence","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Patz","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."},{"family":"Tabor","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Aguirre","given":"A.A."},{"family":"Pearl","given":"M."},{"family":"Epstein","given":"J."},{"family":"Wolfe","given":"D.N."},{"family":"Kilpatrick","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Foufopoulos","given":"J."},{"family":"Molyneux","given":"D."},{"family":"Bradley","given":"D."},{"literal":"J."},{"family":"the","given":"Members"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. They are the primary drivers of a range of infectious disease outbreaks. Logging and road building in Latin America increased the incidence of cutaneous and visceral leishmaniasis ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KJiWg5Lf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Desjeux, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Desjeux, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33698,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N8KMQHJT"],"itemData":{"id":33698,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Transactions of the Royal Society of Tropical Medicine and Hygiene","DOI":"10.1016/S0035-9203(01)90223-8","issue":"ue 3","language":"en","page":"– , 239–243","title":"The increase in risk factors for leishmaniasis worldwide","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0035-9203(01)90223-8","volume":"95","author":[{"family":"Desjeux","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Road building is also linked to the expansion of wild meat consumption that may have played a key role in the early emergence of human immunodeficiency virus types 1 and 2 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gFCnQ5gh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wolfe, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Wolfe, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33641,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QUMPJ9XG"],"itemData":{"id":33641,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Change Hum Health","language":"en","page":"10–25","title":"Deforestation, hunting and the ecology of microbial emergence","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Wolfe","given":"N.D."},{"family":"Eitel","given":"M.N."},{"family":"Gockowski","given":"J."},{"family":"Muchaal","given":"P.K."},{"family":"Nolte","given":"C."},{"family":"Prosser","given":"A.T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. When tropical forests are cleared for human activities, they are typically converted into agricultural or grazing lands. This process is usually exacerbated by construction of roads, causing erosion and allowing previously inaccessible areas to become colonized by people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"a6MB9XRp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Patz, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Patz, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33637,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/REZUWSFM"],"itemData":{"id":33637,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Health Perspectives","issue":"10","language":"en","page":"1092–1098","title":"Working Group on Land Use Change and Disease Emergence","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Patz","given":"J.A."},{"family":"Daszak","given":"P."},{"family":"Tabor","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Aguirre","given":"A.A."},{"family":"Pearl","given":"M."},{"family":"Epstein","given":"J."},{"family":"Wolfe","given":"D.N."},{"family":"Kilpatrick","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Foufopoulos","given":"J."},{"family":"Molyneux","given":"D."},{"family":"Bradley","given":"D."},{"literal":"J."},{"family":"the","given":"Members"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      If in some regions the infrastructure in rural areas is expanded, in others public services to the rural population are increasingly scarce, as the case of running water, electricity or the closure of rural schools and rural regions in several countries that lost their inhabitants due to the exodus in towards urban centers. Despite the demographic reduction, it is important to maintain a minimum of structure in these regions, even to guarantee alternatives to local families and not to exploit nature unsustainably as the only means of subsistence or source of income, also to meet cooking and heating needs when gas or electricity is not available.
      The improvement in infrastructure in a rural area most of the time benefits the integration of specific supply chains, but impacts considerably the local environment, communities and consequently, biodiversity, especially wild species. At present the lack of internet, electricity, good roads are an obstacle for rural people to market their products, as is the case of the Payun Matru Cooperativa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sJ8Vvhzo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lichtenstein, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Lichtenstein, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33668,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R5269G63"],"itemData":{"id":33668,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Latin America Geography","issue":"ue 1","language":"en","page":"187–213","title":"Guanaco management in Argentina: taking a commons perspective","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} that works on guanacos (Lama guanicoe) in Mendoza/Argentina. In most cases they have to rely on intermediaries or go to the cities, with a great economic cost. In this sense, access to credit, better roads, electricity and internet supply would enable local people to enter the market in a fairer way.
Current changes related to rural development include the processes of socioeconomic and structural changes in rural spaces, comprising rural and urban approaches, as in peri-urban agriculture and in consortium production models involving wild species and agricultural crops ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EM9DvQ2P","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jacobi, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Jacobi, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33663,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/889N2QUK"],"itemData":{"id":33663,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban agriculture magazine","language":"it","page":"45–47","title":"Agricultural biodiversity strengthening livelihoods in Periurban Hyderabad, India","volume":"N. 22","author":[{"literal":"Jacobi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, as in systems agroforestry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IrvBu2f4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McNeely, 2004)","plainCitation":"(McNeely, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33447,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TGGR94IE"],"itemData":{"id":33447,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agroforestry Systems","language":"en","page":"155–165","title":"Nature vs. nurture: managing relationships between forests, agroforestry and wild biodiversity","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"McNeely","given":"J.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and home gardens ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UoxwM6bk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. Cruz-Garcia & Struik, 2015; Kujawska, 2018)","plainCitation":"(G. Cruz-Garcia & Struik, 2015; Kujawska, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33807,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FVQAZ89Y"],"itemData":{"id":33807,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"99–113","title":"Spatial and seasonal diversity of wild food plants in home gardens of Northeast Thailand","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Cruz-Garcia","given":"G."},{"family":"Struik","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":33667,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8C7S9QT8"],"itemData":{"id":33667,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"150–165","title":"Effects of landscape structure on medicinal plant richness in home gardens: evidences for the environmental scarcity compensation hypothesis","volume":"72","author":[{"family":"Kujawska","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In rural regions, agricultural or extractive activities are not exclusively practiced. There are more and more other types of associated activities, such as industrial (agro-industries), tourism and leisure, training, etc. In a more environmental and sustainable rural development perspective, it can also be considered that agroecological conversion initiatives through agroforestry systems can promote not only the recovery of the landscape, but also of biodiversity. In mountainous areas of the Greater Mekong sub region, government programs, scholars, and private sector interests have promoted cash crop cultivation and harvesting of plants, algae and fungi as strategies for rural economic development ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"H4q5ZDBl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McLellan & Brown, 2017)","plainCitation":"(McLellan & Brown, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33655,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F6DFWZU4"],"itemData":{"id":33655,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mountain Research and Development (MRD","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"108–120","title":"Mushrooms and cash crops can coexist in mountain livelihoods: wild mushrooms and economic and recreational resources in the greater Mekong","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"McLellan","given":"Timothy"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Madeline"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, since they are both important parts of rural livelihood portfolios worldwide. However, little is known about how cash crops and plants, algae and fungi interact in the daily lives and economic decisions of rural people in this region, or how they may differentially encourage forest conservation practices and values.
      Improvements in infrastructure, such as rural electrification, also cause changes in the ways of conserving and processing food and medicines, as well as reduction of fuel wood harvest, which is still the dominant source of energy in many rural areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XCZtfzxG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Giannecchini, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Giannecchini, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33696,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8Q6TKU3J"],"itemData":{"id":33696,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Geographical Journal","language":"en","page":"1,","title":"Land-cover change and human-environment interations in a rural cultural landscape in South Africa","volume":"173","author":[{"family":"Giannecchini","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In rural regions where it is possible to use refrigerators to conserve bushmeat or fish, a reduction in collective practices and individualization of marketing processes has been observed. These infrastructure improvements also facilitate the processing of products in rural areas, which generates positive and negative impacts on wild biodiversity. The benefits of rural electrification ripple outward to include increased incomes and economic development, improvements in health and education, protection of water catchment areas and forest environments, and enhancement of gender-balanced development. It also brings the challenge of non-biodegradable wastes. If not properly discarded, broken light bulbs can emit vaporous methylmercury that can easily enter in the blood stream and also persist in soil and groundwater threatening human population and wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yo3bunjY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Allison, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Allison, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/725MDCKB"],"itemData":{"id":33795,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mountain Research and Development","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"205–209","title":"The dark side of light: managing non-biodegradable wastes in Buthan’s rural areas","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Allison","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Technological innovations can also be focused on processes carried out in rural areas, like in agricultural co-operatives in China since the New Rural Reconstruction movement ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kAgElx7M","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Luo, n.d.)","plainCitation":"(J. Luo, n.d.)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33660,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3MCLEEWJ"],"itemData":{"id":33660,"type":"document","language":"pt","title":"Technological innovation in agricultural co-operatives in China: implications for agro-food innovation policies","author":[{"family":"Luo","given":"Jianli"},{"family":"Guo","given":"Hongdong"},{"family":"Jia","given":"Fu"}]}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. More recently initiatives focused on the Bioeconomy and the development of local or territorial resources have been conducted in different regions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"otx847h6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Meis Mason, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Meis Mason, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33657,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V7KG5NCT"],"itemData":{"id":33657,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Enterprising Communities: People and Places in the Global Economy","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"194–212","title":"Inuit culture and opportunity recognition for commercial caribou harvest in the bio economy","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Meis Mason","given":"A."},{"family":"Anderson","given":"R.B."},{"family":"Dana","given":"L.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      
Box 4.25. Changes in gathering practices in Nepal 

In Nepal, large-scale gathering of wild species involving tens of thousands of harvesters began after the ban on collection and trade of yarsagumba (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) was lifted in 2001 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oULTTA00","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shrestha & Bawa, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Shrestha & Bawa, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33692,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RKEILMUR"],"itemData":{"id":33692,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable trade in wildlife is regarded as a major driver of biodiversity loss and ecosystem degradation. Unregulated wildlife trade propels over-exploitation of species, resulting in population declines, and often in combination with other factors may ultimately extirpate species from their natural habitats. Concern about the impacts of trade on biodiversity has largely focused on ?agship animal species. Here, we report on the impact of trade on natural populations of the world’s most expensive biological resource, a unique caterpillar fungus (Ophiocordyceps sinensis). Based on interviews with 203 harvesters and 28 traders, and focus group discussions in Dolpa, Nepal, we quantify the amount of harvest and trade. After legalization of trade in Nepal in 2001, trade volume increased persistently, reaching a peak of 2442.4 kg in 2009 and subsequently declining to 1170.8 kg in 2011. The local market price has increased by up to 2300% over the last 10 years. However, mean annual harvest declined from 260.66 ± 212.21 pieces per person in 2006 to 125.82 ± 96.84 pieces per person in 2010. Our analysis of harvesters’ perceptions of resource abundance and sustainability shows that virtually all harvesters (95.1%) believe the availability of the caterpillar fungus in the pastures to be declining, and 67% consider current harvesting practices to be unsustainable.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.10.032","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"514-520","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trade, harvest, and conservation of caterpillar fungus (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) in the Himalayas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320712004521","volume":"159","author":[{"family":"Shrestha","given":"Uttam Babu"},{"family":"Bawa","given":"Kamaljit S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Yarsagumba, a caterpillar fungus, is used to treat respiratory and heart diseases, and is also known as the Himalayan Viagra. Its harvesting is one of the key income sources for poor mountain communities in Nepal, where the availability of other livelihood opportunities is comparatively low ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Xi1lQnJk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shrestha & Bawa, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Shrestha & Bawa, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33685,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/32BMJDRE"],"itemData":{"id":33685,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Harvesting of Chinese caterpillar fungus, one of the most expensive biological commodities in the world, has become an important livelihood strategy for mountain communities of Nepal. However, very little is known about the role of Chinese caterpillar fungus in household economy. We estimated the economic contribution of Chinese caterpillar fungus to the household income, quanti?ed the extent of ‘‘Chinese caterpillar fungus dependence’’ among households with different economic and social characteristics, and assessed the role of cash income from the Chinese caterpillar fungus harvest in meeting various household needs including education, debt payments, and food security. Results show that Chinese caterpillar fungus income is the second largest contributor to the total household income after farm income with 21.1% contribution to the total household income and 53.3% to the total cash income. The contribution of Chinese caterpillar fungus income to total household income decreases as the household income increases making its contribution highest for the poorest households. There is signi?cant correlation between Chinese caterpillar fungus dependency and percentage of family members involved in harvesting, number of food-suf?cient months, and total income without Chinese caterpillar fungus income. Income from Chinese caterpillar fungus is helping the poorest to educate children, purchase food, and pay debts. However, reported decline of Chinese caterpillar fungus from its natural habitat might threaten local livelihoods that depend on the Chinese caterpillar fungus in future. Therefore, sustainable management of Chinese caterpillar fungus through partnership among local institutions and the state is critical in conserving the species and the sustained ?ow of bene?ts to local communities.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2014.06.019","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"194-202","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Economic contribution of Chinese caterpillar fungus to the livelihoods of mountain communities in Nepal","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320714002468","volume":"177","author":[{"family":"Shrestha","given":"Uttam Babu"},{"family":"Bawa","given":"Kamaljit S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Regarding the expenditure of income from yarsagumba, the largest proportion of the income was used for savings, followed by food and clothes, and children’s education, helping to reduce poverty ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NCIpQYTl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. M. Belcher, 2005)","plainCitation":"(B. M. Belcher, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33794,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XNSTI9CB"],"itemData":{"id":33794,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Forestry Review","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"82–89","title":"Forest product markets, forest poverty reduction","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Belcher","given":"B.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The poorer the family, the more dependent on a key species, such as the case of yarsagumba in Nepal. The higher reliance of the poorest households on the income derived from yarsagumba harvesting indicates that the consequences of resource degradation will be severe for households that are already poor. If the market price drops or remains unchanged and the decline in per capita harvest continues, the consequences will be a reduced income for the poorest people, showing the importance to stimulate maintaining a diversity also among the wild species gathered and marketed, but also seeking an alternative solution, like using a natural or compositive substitute. If the resource becomes scarce, communities will lose income. If there is no management work to maintain sustainable harvesting, it will not be possible to guarantee the livelihood opportunities.


      There are diverse examples of wild species use and practices that are tied to income generation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JLqkjgoF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sher, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Sher, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32632,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EFRST8C4"],"itemData":{"id":32632,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annals of the Missouri Botanical Garden","language":"en","page":"2","title":"Promoting sustainable use of medicinal and aromatic plants for livelihood improvement and biodiversity conservation under Global Climate Change, through Capacity Building in the Himalaya Mountains, Swat District, Pakistan","volume":"102","author":[{"family":"Sher","given":"Hassan"},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"Rainer W."},{"family":"Hart","given":"Robbie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are cases of communities that depend mainly on a wild species, mainly where agricultural productivity is limited and there are few other livelihoods opportunities. The harvest of fungal species (e.g., for pharmaceutical and other value) in the Himalayas (Nepal) is one example.ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7BhI24cX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yadav, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Yadav, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32655,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PN6GCIQ7"],"itemData":{"id":32655,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx. The International Journal of Conservation. Cambridge University Press","issue":"ue 2","language":"en","page":"247–255","title":"Yartsagunbu: transforming people's livelihoods in the Western Himalaya","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Yadav","given":"Pramod Kumar"},{"family":"Saha","given":"Subhajit"},{"family":"Mishra","given":"Ashish Kumar"},{"family":"Kapoor","given":"Mohnish"},{"family":"Kaneria","given":"Manendra"},{"family":"Kaneria","given":"Manish"},{"family":"Dasgupta","given":"Sabyasachi"},{"family":"Shrestha","given":"Uttam Babu"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; agricultural activities being limited, the income from yarsagumba accounts for up to 65% of the total household cash income in some communities.  Other examples are the cases of small-scale river fisheries in the Democratic Republic of Congo where it contributes up to 98% of the fishers’ household income, or yet the case of açaí and other plants, algae and fungi in some regions of the Brazilian Amazon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CYoasFtj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Brond\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}zio, 2002)","plainCitation":"(E. Brondízio, 2002)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32701,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YVJB67VV"],"itemData":{"id":32701,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Urban Ecosystems","issue":"ue 1-2","language":"en","page":"67–97","title":"The urban market of açaí fruit (Euterpe oleracea Mart.) and rural land use change : ethnographic insights into the role of price and land tenure constraining agricultural choices in the Amazon estuary","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Brondízio","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Products obtained from forests or other natural environments play a crucial role in sustaining the livelihoods of poor people in developing countries through income generation and the creation of employment opportunities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AWZyFtRX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shrestha, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Shrestha, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32533,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9PG7GG3W"],"itemData":{"id":32533,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            \\n              Products obtained from forests or other natural environments play a crucial role in sustaining the livelihoods of poor people in developing countries through income generation and the creation of employment opportunities. Although studies have been carried out to evaluate the dependence of local livelihoods on environmental products, quantify the linkage between poverty and biodiversity, and assess the impacts of resource depletion on household economics, most have been focused geographically on the tropics. Our study was conducted in the mountain village of Jumla, Nepal, to quantify the economic contribution of Chinese caterpillar fungus\\n              Ophiocordyceps sinensis\\n              (yarsagumba) to local households. Income from yarsagumba accounted for up to 65% of the total household cash income, on average, and its contribution was highest in the poorest households. It contributed to reducing income inequality by 38%, and the income was utilized to purchase food and clothes, celebrate festivals, pay for medical treatment and children's education, and for savings. There was a mean annual decline of 25 pieces in the per capita harvest of yarsagumba during 2010–2014. However, the decline had no adverse impact on household income, as the price increased.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605317000461","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Oryx","language":"en","page":"256-264","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Economic dependence of mountain communities on Chinese caterpillar fungus <i>Ophiocordyceps sinensis</i> (yarsagumba): a case from western Nepal","title-short":"Economic dependence of mountain communities on Chinese caterpillar fungus <i>Ophiocordyceps sinensis</i> (yarsagumba)","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0030605317000461/type/journal_article","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Shrestha","given":"Uttam Babu"},{"family":"Dhital","given":"Krishna Ram"},{"family":"Gautam","given":"Ambika Prasad"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}  
      Another situation is when the interest of the markets is concentrated in only one species of plants, algae and fungi and the rural dwellers, in order to improve the production, reduce the biodiversity by eliminating other species with minor commercial interest. This is happening in Brazilian Amazon with the acai palm and is known as the phenomenon of “acaization” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4jmfBnvZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hiraoka, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Hiraoka, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32674,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RUL68BDM"],"itemData":{"id":32674,"type":"book","event-place":"Belém","language":"pt","number-of-pages":"133–57","publisher":"Museu Paraense Emílio Goeldi","publisher-place":"Belém","title":"Mudanças nos padrões econômicos de uma população ribeirinha do estuário do Amazonas","author":[{"family":"Hiraoka","given":"M."}],"editor":[{"family":"FURTADO","given":"L."},{"family":"LEITÃO","given":"W."},{"family":"MELLO","given":"A.F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Local and national policies integrating the sustainable use of wild species and rural development should not focus only on key species, favoring the diversity of species. In the international markets and global chains this condition should also be stimulated. Local people with their multiple worldviews should participate in the definition of conservation strategies as well as local development, including other specific aspects associated with politics, such as corruption and access to technologies and others.
      
4.2.3.3.6. Social movements 
Although there is a considerable deficit of research on the direct impact of social movements on sustainable use and biodiversity conservation, it is possible to establish indirect linkages. One of the most obvious indirect linkages is that occurring though community-based natural resource management and conservation. The international recognition of the benefits of community-based natural resource management to achieve resource and biodiversity conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Hd61VO8k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2021; D. Hulme & Murphree, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2021; D. Hulme & Murphree, 2001)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32700,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KS33MP4S"],"itemData":{"id":32700,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Edward Elgar Publishing","title":"Advanced Introduction to Community-Based Conservation","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":32540,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/37I4R74I"],"itemData":{"id":32540,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"James Currey Ltd","title":"African Wildlife and Livelihoods: The Promise and Performance of Community Conservation","author":[{"family":"Hulme","given":"David"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Marshall"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} has come along with concerns about the vulnerability of local communities to globalization, shortsighted government regulations, marginalization, intensified land competition, and other global political economy threats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tHFwGpfE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Villamayor-Tom\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}s & Lopez, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Villamayor-Tomás & Lopez, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32537,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R4DWE4W3"],"itemData":{"id":32537,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Making Commons Dynamic: Understanding Change through Commonization and Decommonization, Routledge","language":"en","title":"Decommonization-Commonization Dynamics and Social Movements: Insights from a Meta-Analysis of Case Studies","author":[{"family":"Villamayor-Tomás","given":"Sergio"},{"family":"Lopez","given":"Gustavo García"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Increasing attention has been paid to the participation of local communities in social movements against those threats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"R3kWO5Y2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Goldman, 1998; Peet & Watts, 2004; Scheidel, 2020; Villamayor-Tomas & Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a-L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Goldman, 1998; Peet & Watts, 2004; Scheidel, 2020; Villamayor-Tomas & García-López, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32675,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y3NW4C38"],"itemData":{"id":32675,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Privatizing Nature: Political Struggles for the Global Commons","author":[{"family":"Goldman","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":32664,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GWH9AD42"],"itemData":{"id":32664,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Psychology Press","title":"Liberation Ecologies: Environment, Development, Social Movements","author":[{"family":"Peet","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Watts","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":32662,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GR658T66"],"itemData":{"id":32662,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"102104","title":"Environmental Conflicts and Defenders: A Global Overview","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Scheidel","given":"Arnim"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32539,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EUVCHKXI"],"itemData":{"id":32539,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959378018302838#ack0005","language":"en","page":"114–26","title":"Social Movements as Key Actors in Governing the Commons: Evidence from Community-Based Resource Management Cases across the World","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Villamayor-Tomas","given":"Sergio"},{"family":"García-López","given":"Gustavo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Social movements have been important promoters of transformative sustainable development agendas internationally, e.g., United Nations's 2030 Agenda ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BMkbvwyW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dressler, 2010; Martinez-Alier, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Dressler, 2010; Martinez-Alier, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32686,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V3KS53UB"],"itemData":{"id":32686,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"http://www.journals.cambridge.org/abstract_S0376892910000044","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"5–15","title":"From Hope to Crisis and Back Again? A Critical History of the Global CBNRM Narrative","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Dressler","given":"Wolfram"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32670,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YIAQVFWS"],"itemData":{"id":32670,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Peasant Studies","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"731–55","title":"Is There a Global Environmental Justice Movement?","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Martinez-Alier","given":"Joan"},{"family":"Temper","given":"Leah"},{"family":"Bene","given":"Daniela Del"},{"family":"Scheidel","given":"Arnim"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; however, they also have strong roots in local environmental conflicts and community-based natural resource management practices. Local environmental conflicts are an endemic phenomenon of societies, with more than 3,000 instances registered ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SzJUX20U","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Scheidel, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Scheidel, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32662,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GR658T66"],"itemData":{"id":32662,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"102104","title":"Environmental Conflicts and Defenders: A Global Overview","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Scheidel","given":"Arnim"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and potentially thousands more unregistered all over the world. Many of them involve local communities in defense of their livelihoods and encompass therefore both environmental and justice grievances. Communities' capacity to mobilize in defense of their livelihoods and the environment can indeed be considered two sides of the same action phenomenon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"00APaWJY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Martinez-Alier, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Martinez-Alier, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32669,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RRFUWH4I"],"itemData":{"id":32669,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Edward Elgar Publishing. `Martinez-Alier","title":"The Environmentalism of the Poor: A Study of Ecological Conflicts and Valuation","author":[{"family":"Martinez-Alier","given":"Joan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the end environmental justice movements and their “governance from below” strategies may be indeed the only recognizable challenge to the control of environmental governance by corporate entities and multilateral organizations and their questionable approach to sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ID0DHclt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lemos & Agrawal, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Lemos & Agrawal, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34677,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/298S22RU"],"itemData":{"id":34677,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev.energy.31.042605.135621","ISSN":"1543-5938, 1545-2050","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Environ. Resour.","language":"en","page":"297-325","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Environmental Governance","URL":"http://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev.energy.31.042605.135621","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Lemos","given":"Maria Carmen"},{"family":"Agrawal","given":"Arun"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Specifically, social movements can contribute to sustainable use via a number of pathways, including the organization of community-based monitoring, the strengthening of boundaries around community and conservation projects, the generation and promotion of local ecological knowledge, and the forging of “peoples and conservation” alliances internationally ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xIb0NK2q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alessa, 2016; Villamayor-Tomas & Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a-L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez, 2018; Villamayor-Tom\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}s & Lopez, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Alessa, 2016; Villamayor-Tomas & García-López, 2018; Villamayor-Tomás & Lopez, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32627,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J6492APS"],"itemData":{"id":32627,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability Science","DOI":"10.1007/s11625-015-0295-7","language":"en","page":"91–102","title":"The Role of Indigenous Science and Local Knowledge in Integrated Observing Systems: Moving Toward Adaptive Capacity Indices and Early Warning Systems","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Alessa","given":"L."},{"family":"Kliskey","given":"A."},{"family":"Gamble","given":"J."},{"family":"Fidel","given":"M."},{"family":"Beaujean","given":"G."},{"family":"Gosz","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32539,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EUVCHKXI"],"itemData":{"id":32539,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959378018302838#ack0005","language":"en","page":"114–26","title":"Social Movements as Key Actors in Governing the Commons: Evidence from Community-Based Resource Management Cases across the World","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Villamayor-Tomas","given":"Sergio"},{"family":"García-López","given":"Gustavo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32537,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R4DWE4W3"],"itemData":{"id":32537,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Making Commons Dynamic: Understanding Change through Commonization and Decommonization, Routledge","language":"en","title":"Decommonization-Commonization Dynamics and Social Movements: Insights from a Meta-Analysis of Case Studies","author":[{"family":"Villamayor-Tomás","given":"Sergio"},{"family":"Lopez","given":"Gustavo García"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Movements can support local monitoring systems through the involvement of communities in data collection and analysis, the elaboration of environmental impact assessments in collaboration with scientists and researchers, and the organization of patrolling. In Indonesia, the Dayak indigenous movement for forest management rights developed a community-based mapping unit which documents Dayak land-use and traditional ecological information (e.g., flora and fauna, waterbodies, sacred areas, topography) to ensure conservation and prosperity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rW1ULJN4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alcorn, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Alcorn, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32696,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HI45NAEG"],"itemData":{"id":32696,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Navigating social-ecological systems: Building resilience for complexity and change","language":"en","page":"299–327","title":"Keeping Ecological Resilience Afloat in Cross-Scale Turbulence: An Indigenous Social Movement Navigates Change in Indonesia","author":[{"family":"Alcorn","given":"Janis B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Mexico and Guatemala, the movements for community forestry concessions were followed by the establishment of local governance systems, which included organizing patrols to monitor the forest’s uses and physical boundaries at local level ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c5vMWS4J","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Klooster, 2000; Paudel, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Klooster, 2000; Paudel, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32682,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7FYPTMFC"],"itemData":{"id":32682,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/B6VC6-3Y80M8C-1/2/852c09bc7c531568db4de7df9db1bf67.","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–20","title":"Institutional Choice, Community, and Struggle: A Case Study of Forest Co-Management in Mexico","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Klooster","given":"Daniel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":32665,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/85SLPXGQ"],"itemData":{"id":32665,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Forests for People: Community Rights and Forest Tenure Reform, Earthscan","event-place":"London, UK","language":"en","publisher-place":"London, UK","title":"Community Networks, Collective Action and Forest Management Benefits","author":[{"family":"Paudel","given":"N.S."},{"family":"Monterroso","given":"I."},{"family":"Cronkleton","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and regional level ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"V9NX5bP4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a-L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez & Antinori, 2018)","plainCitation":"(García-López & Antinori, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32535,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8UMKQJ22"],"itemData":{"id":32535,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation and Society","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"193–204","title":"Between Grassroots Collective Action and State Mandates: The Hybridity of Multi-Level Forest Associations in Mexico","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"García-López","given":"Gustavo A."},{"family":"Antinori","given":"Camille"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Related to monitoring is the creation and defense of conservation boundaries via exclusive-use zones, such as the “extractive forest reserves” promoted by the rubber tappers movement in the Brazilian Amazon, and local forest communities in Petén, Guatemala ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2s6GUdsQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cronkleton, 2008; Paudel, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Cronkleton, 2008; Paudel, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32691,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E5983KZB"],"itemData":{"id":32691,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","language":"en","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","title":"Environmental Governance and the Emergence of Forest-Based Social Movements","author":[{"family":"Cronkleton","given":"Peter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":32665,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/85SLPXGQ"],"itemData":{"id":32665,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Forests for People: Community Rights and Forest Tenure Reform, Earthscan","event-place":"London, UK","language":"en","publisher-place":"London, UK","title":"Community Networks, Collective Action and Forest Management Benefits","author":[{"family":"Paudel","given":"N.S."},{"family":"Monterroso","given":"I."},{"family":"Cronkleton","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; or the “trawler-free coastal fishing zones” reserved for artisanal fishing communities in Kerala and Goa, India ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Buylu9b1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kurien, 1991; Sinha, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Kurien, 1991; Sinha, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32683,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SQWMD8D3"],"itemData":{"id":32683,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"United Nations Research Institute for Social Development","title":"Ruining the Commons and Responses of the Commoners: Coastal Overfishing and Fishermen’s Actions in Kerala State, Indian","author":[{"family":"Kurien","given":"John"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}},{"id":32656,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S33IHHWT"],"itemData":{"id":32656,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Agrarian Change","DOI":"10.1111/j.1471-0366.2011.00349.x.","issue":"2–3","language":"en","page":"364–89","title":"Transnationality and the Indian Fishworkers’ Movement, 1960s–2000","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Sinha","given":"Subir"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Movements can also promote local ecological knowledge. This can occur through different ways, including the actual implementation of said knowledge like in the case of women’s Green Belt Movement in Kenya ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4EmfbzEF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. E. Turner & Brownhill, 2004)","plainCitation":"(T. E. Turner & Brownhill, 2004)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32653,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FBSKTFTA"],"itemData":{"id":32653,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Capitalism Nature Socialism","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"21–40","title":"We Want Our Land Back: Gendered Class Analysis, the Second Contradiction of Capitalism and Social Movement Theory","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Turner","given":"Terisa E."},{"family":"Brownhill","given":"Leigh S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; educational and research campaigns, like in the case of the Dayak of Indonesia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6lxkfRoT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alcorn, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Alcorn, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32696,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HI45NAEG"],"itemData":{"id":32696,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Navigating social-ecological systems: Building resilience for complexity and change","language":"en","page":"299–327","title":"Keeping Ecological Resilience Afloat in Cross-Scale Turbulence: An Indigenous Social Movement Navigates Change in Indonesia","author":[{"family":"Alcorn","given":"Janis B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; the use of frames or narratives that legitimize said knowledge, like in the case of the Process of Black Communities movement in Colombia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"K551uoTi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Escobar, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Escobar, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32690,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZG6QSPIR"],"itemData":{"id":32690,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of political ecology","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"53–82","title":"Whose Knowledge, Whose Nature? Biodiversity, Conservation, and the Political Ecology of Social Movements","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Escobar","given":"Arturo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; or the elaboration of maps and formal encoding of knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VC5U7eip","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alcorn, 2003; K. Roberts, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Alcorn, 2003; K. Roberts, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32696,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HI45NAEG"],"itemData":{"id":32696,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Navigating social-ecological systems: Building resilience for complexity and change","language":"en","page":"299–327","title":"Keeping Ecological Resilience Afloat in Cross-Scale Turbulence: An Indigenous Social Movement Navigates Change in Indonesia","author":[{"family":"Alcorn","given":"Janis B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":840,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YK5VTIKJ"],"itemData":{"id":840,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Austrian Journal of South-East Asian Studies","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"53","title":"It Takes a Rooted Village: Networked Resistance, Connected Communities, and Adaptive Responses to Forest Tenure Reform in Northern Thailand","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Roberts","given":"Kimberly"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The contribution of movements to international “peoples and conservation” alliances has also been well documented. In the forest context, the Mesoamerican Alliance of Peoples and Forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"b8fNGoEJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dupuits & Ongolo, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Dupuits & Ongolo, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32689,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LBY98EBW"],"itemData":{"id":32689,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development Perspectives","language":"en","page":"100169","title":"What Does Autonomy Mean for Forest Communities? The Politics of Transnational Community Forestry Networks in Mesoamerica and the Congo Basin","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Dupuits","given":"Emilie"},{"family":"Ongolo","given":"Symphorien"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the World Rainforest Movement, Friends of the Earth International, and the Global Forest Coalition ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DIhbYewa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u352{}imunovi\\\\uc0\\\\u263{}, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Šimunovi?, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32658,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6ZXKUJJY"],"itemData":{"id":32658,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"9","language":"en","page":"3165","title":"Frame Analysis of ENGO Conceptualization of Sustainable Forest Management: Environmental Justice and Neoliberalism at the Core of Sustainability","URL":"http://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/10/9/3165","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Šimunovi?","given":"Nenad"},{"family":"Hesser","given":"Franziska"},{"family":"Stern","given":"Tobias"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} frame forest conservation as based on small-scale, autonomous and customary practices, traditional knowledge, and the collective land rights of local indigenous and peasant communities, challenging the dominant market-based discourse linked to Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation programs. In the fisheries context, prominent examples are the World Forum of Fish Harvesters and Fish Workers and the World Forum of Fisher People ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cAjzstGL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mills, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Mills, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32672,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7DLFJ2BR"],"itemData":{"id":32672,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Third World Quarterly","DOI":"10.1080/01436597.2017.1416288","issue":"7","language":"en","page":"1270–89","title":"Implicating ‘Fisheries Justice’ Movements in Food and Climate Politics","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Mills","given":"Elyse N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      
4.2.3.4. Social norms, beliefs and risk perceptions
Social norms are strongly interrelated with spiritual belief systems in some cultures. For example, some resources are attributed as having sacred meaning and value; this belief in the sacred provides the architecture of norms around which sustainable use (conservation) is ensured. Some key examples include the beliefs around the radiated tortoise in Madagascar. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TnKIVjfs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. E. Jones, 2008)","plainCitation":"(K. E. Jones, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32341,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9I9MNM8H"],"itemData":{"id":32341,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/nature06536","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7181","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"990-993","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Global trends in emerging infectious diseases","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nature06536","volume":"451","author":[{"family":"Jones","given":"Kate E."},{"family":"Patel","given":"Nikkita G."},{"family":"Levy","given":"Marc A."},{"family":"Storeygard","given":"Adam"},{"family":"Balk","given":"Deborah"},{"family":"Gittleman","given":"John L."},{"family":"Daszak","given":"Peter"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In other areas, forests or mangroves are considered to have sacred value as in India and parts of west Africa with significant literature point to the value of the social norms and beliefs about their sacredness in ensuring their sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qMDbZ3kW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bhagwat & Rutte, 2006; Lebbie & Freudenberger, 1996; Ntiamoa-Baidu, 2008; Sheridan, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Bhagwat & Rutte, 2006; Lebbie & Freudenberger, 1996; Ntiamoa-Baidu, 2008; Sheridan, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34431,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G4HHBP6H"],"itemData":{"id":34431,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","DOI":"10.1890/1540-9295(2006)4[519:SGPFBM]2.0.CO;2","issue":"10","page":"519-524","title":"Sacred groves: potential for biodiversity management Journal Item","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Bhagwat","given":"Shonil A"},{"family":"Rutte","given":"Claudia"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":32625,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JEU77LLS"],"itemData":{"id":32625,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest patches in tropical landscapes","language":"en","page":"324","title":"Sacred groves in Africa: forest patches in transition","volume":"300","author":[{"family":"Lebbie","given":"A.R."},{"family":"Freudenberger","given":"M.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}},{"id":32622,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GWL7ZCG4"],"itemData":{"id":32622,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal for the Study of Religion, Nature & Culture","issue":"3","language":"en","title":"Indigenous Beliefs and Biodiversity Conservation: The Effectiveness of Sacred Groves, Taboos and Totems in Ghana for Habitat and Species Conservation","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Ntiamoa-Baidu","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":32623,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LMM4MUDJ"],"itemData":{"id":32623,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"African Studies Review","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"73–98","title":"The environmental and social history of African sacred groves: a Tanzanian case study","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Sheridan","given":"M.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Social norms and beliefs about the sacredness of the whale, the sentience of the arctic ecosystem and norms (developed over generation) of stewardship also underlie sustainable harvesting (i.e., conservation hunting) of whales in Canada and Greenland ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UumFGROk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Foote & Wenzel, 2008; M. Freeman, 1999; M. M. R. Freeman, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Foote & Wenzel, 2008; M. Freeman, 1999; M. M. R. Freeman, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32528,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6FT95VBM"],"itemData":{"id":32528,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Tourism and the Consumption of Wildlife: Hunting, Shooting and Sport Fishing","ISBN":"978-0-203-93432-6","language":"en","note":"container-title: Tourism and the Consumption of Wildlife\\nDOI: 10.4324/9780203934326-19","page":"137-150","publisher":"Routledge","source":"www.taylorfrancis.com","title":"Conservation hunting concepts, Canada’s Inuit, and polar bear hunting","URL":"https://www.taylorfrancis.com/chapters/conservation-hunting-concepts-canada-inuit-polar-bear-hunting-lee-foote-george-wenzel/e/10.4324/9780203934326-19","author":[{"family":"Foote","given":"Lee"},{"family":"Wenzel","given":"George"}],"editor":[{"family":"Lovelock","given":"Brent"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":32534,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JARHMX4J"],"itemData":{"id":32534,"type":"paper-conference","container-title":"International Symposium on Society and Resource Management","event-place":"Brisbane, Australia","language":"en","publisher-place":"Brisbane, Australia","title":"They knew how much to take","URL":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Milton_Freeman/publication/274385326_They_knew_how_much_to_take_Respect_and_reciprocity_in_arctic_sustainable_use_strategies/links/56974a0208ae1c4279041c2e/They-knew-how-much-to-take-Respect-and-reciprocity-in-arctic-sustainable-use-strategies.pdf","author":[{"family":"Freeman","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":32544,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/75UJHHAC"],"itemData":{"id":32544,"type":"book","call-number":"SK471.N59 P46 2004","collection-number":"56","collection-title":"Occasional publication series","event-place":"Edmonton","ISBN":"978-1-896445-35-9","language":"en","number-of-pages":"112","publisher":"CCI Press","publisher-place":"Edmonton","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Conservation hunting: people and wildlife in Canada's north","title-short":"Conservation hunting","editor":[{"family":"Freeman","given":"Milton M. R."},{"family":"Hudson","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Foote","given":"Lee"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Box 4.26. The fady system in Madagascar 

Social norms are important to ensuring sustainable use of resources in Madagascar. A system of norms, known as fady has been developed by indigenous peoples and local communities over many generations. Fady refers to the agreed upon system of behaviours that lead to the prohibition of use of some species or particularly kind of practices to ensure sustainability.  It goes beyond “good manners” and is enforced through popular disapproval, and a belief that supernatural retribution will affect the transgressor if the fady is not respected or broken (there are numerous examples of fady leading to the long-term protection of species and habitats in Madagascar,for example, the fady against killing of the radiated tortoise (Geochelonia radiata) is highlighted as the main mechanism that has saved this species from extinction. 

Sources: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2AaY2Zhl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. P. G. Jones, 2008)","plainCitation":"(J. P. G. Jones, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32678,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6KSQUEFG"],"itemData":{"id":32678,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"976–986 0888–8892","title":"The importance of taboos and social norms to conservation in Madagascar","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Jones","given":"J.P.G."},{"family":"Andriamarovololona","given":"M.M."},{"family":"Hockley","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gC3Cgtmu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nussbaum & Raxworthy, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Nussbaum & Raxworthy, 2000)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32631,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AZ9RT8U6"],"itemData":{"id":32631,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Amphibian and Reptile Conservation","page":"6-14","title":"Commentary on conservation of \\"Sokatra\\", the radiated tortoise (Geochelone radiata) of Madagascar","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Nussbaum","given":"Ronald"},{"family":"Raxworthy","given":"Christopher"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}


      Social structures also strong affect the way in which individuals, society and institutions perceive risks within their environment including risks related to the use of wild species. There is a large literature on social, cultural and environmental risk perception that details the central role played by formal education, media, science and others. A central issue and insight of much work around wild species and risk perception as well as indigenous knowledge systems is that scientists tend to view risks differently than the public at large differently ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oBP1g5FD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Krewski, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Krewski, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":775,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XYDGIP7J"],"itemData":{"id":775,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Health, Risk & Society","DOI":"10.1080/13698570801919830","ISSN":"1369-8575, 1469-8331","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Health, Risk & Society","language":"en","page":"167-179","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Public perception of population health risks in Canada: Risk perception beliefs","title-short":"Public perception of population health risks in Canada","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/13698570801919830","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Krewski","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Lemyre","given":"Louise"},{"family":"Turner","given":"Michelle C."},{"family":"Lee","given":"Jennifer E.C."},{"family":"Dallaire","given":"Christine"},{"family":"Bouchard","given":"Louise"},{"family":"Brand","given":"Kevin"},{"family":"Mercier","given":"Pierre"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and as a result, there are often challenges in managing perceived risk and what scientists often define as “real risks”. While there are debates as to whether the public misjudges ‘real’ risk when compared to those in the scientific community, accounting for perceived risk in institutions is as critical in addressing questions of sustainability of wild species.
      
Box 4.27. Social risk perception as a driver of species use – what fish should I eat?

The public receives fish consumption advice from a variety of perspectives, including toxi-cant, nutritional, ecological, and economic viewpoints. For example, United States of America federal and state agencies that are concerned about exposure to toxicants in fish, such as methyl-mercury and polychlorinated biphenyls, have issued fish consumption advisories recommending that at-risk groups limit consumption of fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Z4aqFOVN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United States Environmental Protection Agency, 2004)","plainCitation":"(United States Environmental Protection Agency, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":778,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9B7LU2QH"],"itemData":{"id":778,"type":"book","publisher":"DIANE Publishing","title":"Overview of the Ecological Risk Assessment Process in the Office of Pesticide Programs, US Environmental Protection Agency: Endangered and Threatened Species Effects Determinations","author":[{"family":"United States Environmental Protection Agency","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, national organizations of physicians and nutritionists encourage fish consumption for the entire population as a way to increase dietary intake of the n-3 (omega-3) long-chain polyunsaturated fatty acids that may prevent cardio-vascular disease and improve neurological development ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nJcSoooB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kris-Etherton, 2002, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Kris-Etherton, 2002, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":774,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8CVDFXKR"],"itemData":{"id":774,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"circulation","issue":"21","note":"publisher: Am Heart Assoc","page":"2747–2757","title":"Fish consumption, fish oil, omega-3 fatty acids, and cardiovascular disease","volume":"106","author":[{"family":"Kris-Etherton","given":"Penny M"},{"family":"Harris","given":"William S"},{"family":"Appel","given":"Lawrence J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":777,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QIJCF5ZK"],"itemData":{"id":777,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of the American Dietetic Association","issue":"9","page":"1599–1611","title":"Position of the American Dietetic Association and Dietitians of Canada: dietary fatty acids.","volume":"107","author":[{"family":"Kris-Etherton","given":"Penny M"},{"family":"Innis","given":"Sheila"},{"family":"Assocition","given":"Ammerican Dietetic"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, environmental groups have recommended that consumers avoid certain fish on the basis of concerns about species depletion or habitat destruction consequent to farming methods, site of origin, or type of harvesting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1CJP6r5z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Oken, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Oken, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32630,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NERC6EY4"],"itemData":{"id":32630,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Health Perspectives","DOI":"10.1289/ehp.1104500","ISSN":"0091-6765, 1552-9924","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Health Perspectives","language":"en","page":"790-798","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Which Fish Should I Eat? Perspectives Influencing Fish Consumption Choices","title-short":"Which Fish Should I Eat?","URL":"https://ehp.niehs.nih.gov/doi/10.1289/ehp.1104500","volume":"120","author":[{"family":"Oken","given":"Emily"},{"family":"Choi","given":"Anna L."},{"family":"Karagas","given":"Margaret R."},{"family":"Mariën","given":"Koenraad"},{"family":"Rheinberger","given":"Christoph M."},{"family":"Schoeny","given":"Rita"},{"family":"Sunderland","given":"Elsie"},{"family":"Korrick","given":"Susan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Whether how much, and what type of fish a person eats are also influenced by economic and market considerations (e.g., cost and availability) as well as by taste, cultural tradition, recreational habits, and access to alternative foods. Thus, the consumer who wants to know “which fish should I eat?” is likely to encounter contradictory advice, especially because much of the available information considers a single perspective, such as maximizing health or minimizing ecological harms. For example, because farm-raised salmon is high in n-3 fatty acids and very low in mercury, it is promoted for its nutritional benefits. However, environmental groups consider it a “fish to avoid” because salmon aquaculture may adversely affect eco-system integrity and wild fish stocks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qclgEMAG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Oken, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Oken, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32630,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NERC6EY4"],"itemData":{"id":32630,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Health Perspectives","DOI":"10.1289/ehp.1104500","ISSN":"0091-6765, 1552-9924","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Health Perspectives","language":"en","page":"790-798","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Which Fish Should I Eat? Perspectives Influencing Fish Consumption Choices","title-short":"Which Fish Should I Eat?","URL":"https://ehp.niehs.nih.gov/doi/10.1289/ehp.1104500","volume":"120","author":[{"family":"Oken","given":"Emily"},{"family":"Choi","given":"Anna L."},{"family":"Karagas","given":"Margaret R."},{"family":"Mariën","given":"Koenraad"},{"family":"Rheinberger","given":"Christoph M."},{"family":"Schoeny","given":"Rita"},{"family":"Sunderland","given":"Elsie"},{"family":"Korrick","given":"Susan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and relatively high levels of polychlorinated biphenyls have led to concerns about cancer risk ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gvWrBik9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hites, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Hites, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":773,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2FMRDCAF"],"itemData":{"id":773,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"5655","note":"publisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science","page":"226–229","title":"Global assessment of organic contaminants in farmed salmon","volume":"303","author":[{"family":"Hites","given":"Ronald A"},{"family":"Foran","given":"Jeffery A"},{"family":"Carpenter","given":"David O"},{"family":"Hamilton","given":"M Coreen"},{"family":"Knuth","given":"Barbara A"},{"family":"Schwager","given":"Steven J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Furthermore, it may be difficult for consumers to know whether any given fish is “good” to eat because they often do not have access to the facts, they need to make fully informed choices, such as the size of the fish or how or where it was caught.

Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ChAON0RE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Oken, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Oken, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32630,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NERC6EY4"],"itemData":{"id":32630,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Health Perspectives","DOI":"10.1289/ehp.1104500","ISSN":"0091-6765, 1552-9924","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Health Perspectives","language":"en","page":"790-798","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Which Fish Should I Eat? Perspectives Influencing Fish Consumption Choices","title-short":"Which Fish Should I Eat?","URL":"https://ehp.niehs.nih.gov/doi/10.1289/ehp.1104500","volume":"120","author":[{"family":"Oken","given":"Emily"},{"family":"Choi","given":"Anna L."},{"family":"Karagas","given":"Margaret R."},{"family":"Mariën","given":"Koenraad"},{"family":"Rheinberger","given":"Christoph M."},{"family":"Schoeny","given":"Rita"},{"family":"Sunderland","given":"Elsie"},{"family":"Korrick","given":"Susan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.


      One aspects of risk perception directly relevant to wild species use related to prion diseases; public information and social media is known to directly impact decisions to hunt in the case of chronic wasting disease. For example, awareness of this diease in Wisconsin, resulted in an 11% decline in license sales ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rRuogL1l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Heberlein & Stedman, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Heberlein & Stedman, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":772,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KH22F35U"],"itemData":{"id":772,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","issue":"5","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"326–340","title":"Socially amplified risk: Attitude and behavior change in response to CWD in Wisconsin deer","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Heberlein","given":"Thomas A"},{"family":"Stedman","given":"Richard C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Alberta, sports hunters were less interested in visiting a site with higher chronic wasting disease prevalence ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PYQfOhZ3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Zimmer, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Zimmer, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":771,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X6H3NA2F"],"itemData":{"id":771,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Toxicology and Environmental Health, Part A","issue":"22-24","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"1621–1635","title":"The impact of chronic wasting disease and its management on hunter perceptions, opinions, and behaviors in Alberta, Canada","volume":"74","author":[{"family":"Zimmer","given":"Natalie"},{"family":"Boxall","given":"Peter C"},{"family":"Adamowicz","given":"WL"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The e degree of trust of the sources of information is a key dimension of this impact: if people trust the agent providing the information, they will trust the information provided to them by these sources; moreover, they are likely to have lower sense of risk perception as they trust the agents “to take care of any potential problems” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"klVUkSw0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Muringai & Goddard, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Muringai & Goddard, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":770,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QKQELR74"],"itemData":{"id":770,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Agribusiness","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"240–265","title":"Trust and consumer risk perceptions regarding BSE and chronic wasting disease","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Muringai","given":"Violet"},{"family":"Goddard","given":"Ellen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The role of indigenous and local knowledge is also directly relevant to understanding how indigenous peoples deal with risk with some exceptions, scientists have historically disregarded indigenous knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EaR2z5SP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I. G. Baird, 2021; Brook, 2009; Kutz & Tomaselli, 2019)","plainCitation":"(I. G. Baird, 2021; Brook, 2009; Kutz & Tomaselli, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":853,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KJBMTFCV"],"itemData":{"id":853,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-020-01418-x","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Downstream Impacts of Hydropower Dams and Indigenous and Local Knowledge: Examples from the Peace–Athabasca, Mekong, and Amazon","title-short":"The Downstream Impacts of Hydropower Dams and Indigenous and Local Knowledge","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-020-01418-x","author":[{"family":"Baird","given":"Ian G."},{"family":"Silvano","given":"Renato A. M."},{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda"},{"family":"Poesch","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Maclean","given":"Bruce"},{"family":"Napoleon","given":"Art"},{"family":"Lepine","given":"Melody"},{"family":"Hallwass","given":"Gustavo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,18]]}}},{"id":32639,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NL442LDS"],"itemData":{"id":32639,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EcoHealth","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"266–278","title":"Fostering community-based wildlife health monitoring and research in the Canadian North","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Brook","given":"R.K."},{"family":"Kutz","given":"S.J."},{"family":"Veitch","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Popko","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Elkin","given":"B.T."},{"family":"Guthrie","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":776,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7CGPCZFM"],"itemData":{"id":776,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","issue":"6446","note":"publisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science","page":"1135–1137","title":"“Two-eyed seeing” supports wildlife health","volume":"364","author":[{"family":"Kutz","given":"Susan"},{"family":"Tomaselli","given":"Matilde"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At the same time, indigenous peoples themselves have a high level of trust in their own knowledge and capacities to assess wild species health and use this knowledge to make decisions about harvest and consumption of traditional foods as well as in respect of other aspects of conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AxfT2T9l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 1998; Eichler & Baumeister, 2018; Friendship & Furgal, 2012; M. B. Gadgil, 1993; Wray & Parlee, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 1998; Eichler & Baumeister, 2018; Friendship & Furgal, 2012; M. B. Gadgil, 1993; Wray & Parlee, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32651,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E33G332J"],"itemData":{"id":32651,"type":"book","event-place":"London","publisher":"Taylor & Francis","publisher-place":"London","title":"Sacred Ecology. Traditional Ecological Knowledge and Resource Management","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":782,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ECKYSQK9"],"itemData":{"id":782,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and Society","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Berghahn Journals","page":"75–90","title":"Hunting for justice: an indigenous critique of the North American model of wildlife conservation","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Eichler","given":"Lauren"},{"family":"Baumeister","given":"David"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":783,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UEDTQDMX"],"itemData":{"id":783,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International journal of circumpolar health","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"19003","title":"The role of Indigenous knowledge in environmental health risk management in Yukon, Canada","volume":"71","author":[{"family":"Friendship","given":"Katelyn A"},{"family":"Furgal","given":"Chris M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":866,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YAGA3CHZ"],"itemData":{"id":866,"type":"book","event-place":"Biodiversity","language":"en","number-of-pages":"151–156","publisher":"Ecology","publisher-place":"Biodiversity","title":"Indigenous Knowledge for Biodiversity Conservation Ambio: 22: 2/3","author":[{"family":"Gadgil","given":"M.Berkes"},{"family":"F.","given":"Folke"},{"literal":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}},{"id":813,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L8FIL35B"],"itemData":{"id":813,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic","language":"en","page":"68–78","title":"Ways we respect caribou: Teet?’it Gwich’in Rules","volume":"66","author":[{"family":"Wray","given":"K."},{"family":"Parlee","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The tendency to trust their own knowledge is rooted in the multi-generational knowledge, practices and beliefs that have been the foundation of successful environmental stewardship for generations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yzqYy2y0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":846,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UPCMNE8P"],"itemData":{"id":846,"type":"book","edition":"Fourth","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-1-138-07149-0","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Sacred Ecology","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
4.2.3.5. Social inequality and poverty
Key messages: 
* Sustainability of wild species use can be understood by many diverse social values (e.g., sustainable use is not only ecological) (well established) {4.3.3.5}. 
* Poverty and social inequality are drivers of unsustainable use, particularly where there are few livelihood alternatives (well established) {4.3.3.5.2);
* The benefits of wild species use are inequitably distributed in many regions. Inequities are evidenced at local, regional and global scales by numerous kinds of indicators (e.g., income, gross domestic product, access to land) (well established) {4.3.3.5.2);
* Indigenous peoples are among those who experience a high degree of poverty and social marginalization which greater limited their access to wild species valued for food provisioning and well-being (well established) {4.3.3.5.3).

4.2.3.5.1. Overview
Nearly half of the world lives with less than USD $5.5 a day, and almost half the world’s population — 3.4 billion people — still struggles to meet basic needs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"P5dvrX4C","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Bank, 2018)","plainCitation":"(World Bank, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32515,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MFDCY3GJ"],"itemData":{"id":32515,"type":"personal_communication","title":"GINI index","URL":"https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SI.POV.GINI","author":[{"literal":"World Bank"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; (Figure 4.9). Globally, more than 800 million people are still living on less than $1.25 a day; many lack access to adequate food, clean drinking water and sanitation (United Nations Development Program; https://sdgs.un.org). Poor communities, which disproportionately include indigenous peoples, tend to be marginalized from policymaking and government and have little voice in the development of laws that they may be structurally unable to comply with ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"l79lTXCE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wynberg, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Wynberg, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32703,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/55JL3ZHJ"],"itemData":{"id":32703,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Concerns about ecological sustainability and inequality are driving increased formalization of the natural product trade, including both biotrade of bulk, raw materials (or nontimber forest products [NTFPs]) and bioprospecting for genetic resources. However, there has been little interrogation as to whether the policy tools used to achieve sustainability and equity goals are appropriate and effective. This article addresses this gap by examining efforts to formalize biotrade, including the blurred regulatory lines that increasingly exist between biotrade and bioprospecting. Two case studies are explored from southern Africa—baobab and Pelargonium. Findings emphasize the unintended consequences that can arise from overregulation and poorly formulated laws, including the further marginalization of women and=or poor communities; elite capture; weakening small businesses; and leakage across political boundaries. Conclusions underpin the need to pursue solutions that are better informed and more respectful of local knowledge and needs, and that draw upon both customary and statutory laws and institutions.","container-title":"Society & Natural Resources","DOI":"10.1080/08941920.2015.1014604","ISSN":"0894-1920, 1521-0723","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Society & Natural Resources","language":"en","page":"559-574","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Formalization of the Natural Product Trade in Southern Africa: Unintended Consequences and Policy Blurring in Biotrade and Bioprospecting","title-short":"Formalization of the Natural Product Trade in Southern Africa","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/08941920.2015.1014604","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Van Niekerk","given":"Jaci"},{"family":"Kozanayi","given":"Witness"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",5,4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although income was historically the main measure of poverty, even here there has been a shift from absolute poverty (e.g., median income) to relative (e.g., median income relative to the average in a country or region). Income and material wealth indicators continue to be the most common, but well-being, equality, social inclusion, are increasingly recognized as factors contributing to deprivation as an understanding of poverty ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2pNV8q0j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(United Nations, 2009)","plainCitation":"(United Nations, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32633,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HWLSV9BC"],"itemData":{"id":32633,"type":"book","event-place":"New York","language":"en","publisher":"United Nations, Dept. of Economic and Social Affairs","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Rethinking poverty: Report on the world social situation 2010","editor":[{"family":"United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An important challenge arises, however, when attempting to design indicators to be monitored and compared across nations, and even regions within nations; these indicators cannot account for differences in the relative prices of goods, cultural preferences in diets, existing subsidies, social and political marginalization, nutritional requirements depending on types of labor and environments, etc. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pUElKcAP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nations, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Nations, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32628,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U8YPMHLX"],"itemData":{"id":32628,"type":"book","event-place":"New York","language":"en","publisher":"United Nations, Dept. of Economic and Social Affairs","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Rethinking poverty: Report on the world social situation 2010","editor":[{"family":"Nations","given":"United"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Such indicators may thus be useful in a very limited sense due to their comparability across time but can be poor—and potentially entirely inappropriate—reflections of poverty in given nations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gDr7I4wp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hagenaars & Vos, 1988; Kapteyn, 1988; United Nations, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Hagenaars & Vos, 1988; Kapteyn, 1988; United Nations, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32677,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XFAX88CW"],"itemData":{"id":32677,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Human Resources","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"211–221","title":"The Definition and Measurement of Poverty","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Hagenaars","given":"A."},{"family":"Vos","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1988"]]}}},{"id":32679,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FNHJCAAK"],"itemData":{"id":32679,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Human Resources","DOI":"10.2307/145777","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"222","title":"Some Methodological Issues in the Implementation of Subjective Poverty Definitions","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Kapteyn","given":"A."},{"family":"Kooreman","given":"P."},{"family":"Willemse","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1988"]]}}},{"id":32633,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HWLSV9BC"],"itemData":{"id":32633,"type":"book","event-place":"New York","language":"en","publisher":"United Nations, Dept. of Economic and Social Affairs","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Rethinking poverty: Report on the world social situation 2010","editor":[{"family":"United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indeed, the complexity of poverty as understood by different people and in different contexts should be seen as a main reason to consider the term and design its measurement depending on the specific case ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dhunuk9s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Spicker, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Spicker, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32659,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/55IEPLG5"],"itemData":{"id":32659,"type":"book","event-place":"Poverty","language":"en","number-of-pages":"229–243","publisher":"An International Glossary","publisher-place":"Poverty","title":"Definitions of poverty: Twelve clusters of meaning","author":[{"family":"Spicker","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Poverty has been recognized to be multidimensional in nature, and methods were developed to try to measure poverty through alternative instruments that capture deprivation beyond income and consumption. The creation of effective measures that better reflect poor people’s experience enables the design of more effective policies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tHZfpOf5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Alkire, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Alkire, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32697,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZVKK7MVP"],"itemData":{"id":32697,"type":"book","event-place":"Oxford","language":"en","publisher":"Oxford University Press","publisher-place":"Oxford","title":"Multidimensional Poverty Measurement and Analysis","author":[{"family":"Alkire","given":"S."},{"family":"Foster","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Seth","given":"S."},{"family":"Santos","given":"M.E."},{"family":"Roche","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Ballon","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Multidimensional Poverty Index—shown in the world map (Figure 4.9) is the most common international instrument used in this context. Progress to diminish poverty is uneven across the different regions (Appendix I).

Figure 4.9. Map of poverty and potential biodiversity loss, showing the level of poverty (proxied by the log rate of human infant mortality) combined with the log number of threatened species of mammals, birds, and amphibians per one-degree grid square (Behrmann equal-area projection). White areas represent missing data. Source: ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"O5pNkBNr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sachs, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Sachs, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32661,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6RR5952P"],"itemData":{"id":32661,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity Conservation and the Millennium Development Goals. Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1175035.","language":"fr","page":"1502–1503","volume":"325","editor":[{"family":"Sachs","given":"Jeffrey"},{"family":"Baillie","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Sutherland","given":"William"},{"family":"Armsworth","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Ash","given":"Neville"},{"family":"Beddington","given":"John"},{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Collen","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Gardiner","given":"Barry"},{"family":"Gaston","given":"Kevin"},{"family":"Godfray","given":"Charles"},{"family":"Green","given":"Rhys"},{"family":"Harvey","given":"Paul"},{"family":"House","given":"Brett"},{"family":"Knapp","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Kümpel","given":"Noëlle"},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David"},{"family":"Mallet","given":"James"},{"family":"Jones","given":"Kate"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Copyright © 2009, The American Association for the Advancement of Science under license 5154810771990. The designations employed and the presentation of material on the maps used in the assessment do not imply the expression of any opinion whatsoever on the part of IPBES concerning the legal status of any country, territory, city or area or of its authorities, or concerning the delimitation of its frontiers or boundaries. These maps have been prepared or used for the sole purpose of facilitating the assessment of the broad biogeographical areas represented therein and for purposes of representing scientific data spatially.


4.2.3.5.2. Poverty and use of wild species
Poverty and environment are closely interrelated. Extreme poverty and biodiversity hotspots can be geographically coincident ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"U9FzBEyQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barrett, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Barrett, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32620,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XSB2NHSK"],"itemData":{"id":32620,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper introduces a special feature on biodiversity conservation and poverty traps. We define and explain the core concepts and then identify four distinct classes of mechanisms that define important interlinkages between biodiversity and poverty. The multiplicity of candidate mechanisms underscores a major challenge in designing policy appropriate across settings. This framework is then used to introduce the ensuing set of papers, which empirically explore these various mechanisms linking poverty traps and biodiversity conservation.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1011521108","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"34","journalAbbreviation":"Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U.S.A.","language":"en","page":"13907-13912","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"On biodiversity conservation and poverty traps","URL":"https://pnas.org/doi/full/10.1073/pnas.1011521108","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Barrett","given":"Christopher B."},{"family":"Travis","given":"Alexander J."},{"family":"Dasgupta","given":"Partha"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8,23]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} , but the majority of the rural poor in the developing world live in fragile and marginal lands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XJB7Go2s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barbier, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Barbier, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":766,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AILQSB8B"],"itemData":{"id":766,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and Development Economics","DOI":"10.1017/S1355770X1000032X","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"635–660","title":"Poverty, development, and environment","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Barbier","given":"E.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Poverty is indeed prevalent in rural areas where livelihoods depend disproportionately on natural capital embodied in forests, rangelands, soils, water, and wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SoRDeook","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barbier, 2010; Barrett, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Barbier, 2010; Barrett, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":766,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AILQSB8B"],"itemData":{"id":766,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and Development Economics","DOI":"10.1017/S1355770X1000032X","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"635–660","title":"Poverty, development, and environment","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Barbier","given":"E.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32620,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XSB2NHSK"],"itemData":{"id":32620,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper introduces a special feature on biodiversity conservation and poverty traps. We define and explain the core concepts and then identify four distinct classes of mechanisms that define important interlinkages between biodiversity and poverty. The multiplicity of candidate mechanisms underscores a major challenge in designing policy appropriate across settings. This framework is then used to introduce the ensuing set of papers, which empirically explore these various mechanisms linking poverty traps and biodiversity conservation.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1011521108","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"34","journalAbbreviation":"Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U.S.A.","language":"en","page":"13907-13912","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"On biodiversity conservation and poverty traps","URL":"https://pnas.org/doi/full/10.1073/pnas.1011521108","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Barrett","given":"Christopher B."},{"family":"Travis","given":"Alexander J."},{"family":"Dasgupta","given":"Partha"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8,23]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Plants, algae and fungi, fish and shellfish and bushmeat have a well-established role in sustaining and protecting the existing living standards of the poor and ensuring that they do not fall into chronic poverty ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2M4E9Evh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brown, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Brown, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32638,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2DEJ7UU2"],"itemData":{"id":32638,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ODI Wildlife Policy","language":"en","title":"Bushmeat and poverty alleviation, implications for development policy","volume":"Number 2","author":[{"family":"Brown","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A great diversity of wild species is harvested within subsistence (non-commercial) economies in the Americas, Asia and Africa as a cheap and easily accessible resource ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KY7KycZY","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2018a, 2018c)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2018a, 2018c)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":91,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HY7NC7ZH"],"itemData":{"id":91,"type":"book","abstract":"Reproduction This publication may be reproduced in whole or in part and in any form for educational or non-profit services without special permission from the copyright holder, provided acknowledgement of the source is made. The IPBES secretariat would appreciate receiving a copy of any publication that uses this publication as a source. No use of this publication may be made for resale or any other commercial purpose whatsoever without prior permission in writing from the IPBES secretariat. Applications for such permission, with a statement of the purpose and extent of the reproduction, should be addressed to the IPBES secretariat. The use of information from this publication concerning proprietary products for publicity or advertising is not permitted. Traceable accounts The chapter references enclosed in curly brackets (e.g. {2.3.1, 2.3.1.2, 2.3.1.3}) are traceable accounts and refer to sections of the chapters of the IPBES Regional Assessment Report on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services for the Americas. A traceable account is a description within the corresponding texts of these chapters, reflecting the evaluation of the type, amount, quality, and consistency of evidence and the degree of agreement for that particular statement or key finding.","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-01-0","number-of-pages":"41","publisher":"IPBES Secretariat","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the regional assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services for the Americas of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3236252","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"literal":"J. Rice"},{"literal":"C.S. Seixas"},{"literal":"M.E. Zaccagnini"},{"literal":"M. Bedoya-Gaitán"},{"literal":"N. Valderrama"},{"literal":"C.B. Anderson"},{"literal":"M.T.K. Arroyo"},{"literal":"M. Bustamante"},{"literal":"J. Cavender-Bares"},{"literal":"A. Diaz-de-Leon"},{"literal":"S. Fennessy"},{"literal":"J. R. García Márquez"},{"literal":"K. Garcia"},{"literal":"E.H. Helmer"},{"literal":"B. Herrera"},{"literal":"B. Klatt"},{"literal":"J.P. Ometo"},{"literal":"V. Rodríguez Osuna"},{"literal":"F.R. Scarano"},{"literal":"S. Schill"},{"literal":"J. S. Farinaci"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32705,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9P7T8CEM"],"itemData":{"id":32705,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-01-0","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"The IPBES regional assessment report on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Sevices for Africa","URL":"http://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3236178","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"Archer","given":"E"},{"family":"Dziba","given":"L"},{"family":"Mulongoy","given":"K.J"},{"family":"Maoela","given":"M.A"},{"family":"Walters","given":"M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wood-based fuels are a key energy source for the majority of the African population contributing at least 70% of total energy consumption in sub-Saharan Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9G13n8mh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2018c)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2018c)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32705,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9P7T8CEM"],"itemData":{"id":32705,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-01-0","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"The IPBES regional assessment report on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Sevices for Africa","URL":"http://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3236178","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"editor":[{"family":"Archer","given":"E"},{"family":"Dziba","given":"L"},{"family":"Mulongoy","given":"K.J"},{"family":"Maoela","given":"M.A"},{"family":"Walters","given":"M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These examples show that most poor people throughout the world rely on the use of wild species for their living. Environmental degradation and resource depletion threaten their livelihoods. 
      A great bulk of the world's poorest and most vulnerable citizens live in disaster-prone regions and their number keeps increasing. Those groups are disproportionally affected by shocks and stresses. According to dominant narratives, people living in poverty are the principal creators of environmental damage, whilst they often bear the cost of environmental damage. This has been proposed as representing a downward spiral, whereby the poor are forced to deplete resources to survive, and this degradation of the environment further impoverishes people. That neoliberalist and neocapitalism narratives often lead, on the one hand, to coercive environmental policies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QIVMdafq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Blaikie & Brookfield, 1986; N. L. Peluso, 1993)","plainCitation":"(Blaikie & Brookfield, 1986; N. L. Peluso, 1993)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32640,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QH5SM635"],"itemData":{"id":32640,"type":"book","language":"en","number-of-pages":"250","publisher":"Methuen, Routledge","title":"Land Degradation and Society","author":[{"family":"Blaikie","given":"P."},{"family":"Brookfield","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1986"]]}}},{"id":32666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JF6EAXXK"],"itemData":{"id":32666,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","issue":"2","language":"it","page":"199–217","title":"Coercing conservation?: The politics of state resource control","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Peluso","given":"N.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and on the other hand, to the privatization of common pool resources and land grabbing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"llry6o23","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fairhead, 2012; Leach & I., 2015)","plainCitation":"(Fairhead, 2012; Leach & I., 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34661,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/34US2L33"],"itemData":{"id":34661,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Peasant Studies","DOI":"10.1080/03066150.2012.671770","ISSN":"0306-6150, 1743-9361","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Peasant Studies","language":"en","page":"237-261","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Green Grabbing: a new appropriation of nature?","title-short":"Green Grabbing","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/03066150.2012.671770","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Fairhead","given":"James"},{"family":"Leach","given":"Melissa"},{"family":"Scoones","given":"Ian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",4]]}}},{"id":32621,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3PYW9WVB"],"itemData":{"id":32621,"type":"book","event-place":"London and NY","language":"en","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"London and NY","title":"Carbon Conflicts and Forest Landscapes in Africa. Pathways to Sustainability","editor":[{"family":"Leach","given":"M.Scoones"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, that lead to the marginalization of the local people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7TW7bgOK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Benjaminsen, 2015; Braun & Gatzweiler, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Benjaminsen, 2015; Braun & Gatzweiler, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":767,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WK943SVZ"],"itemData":{"id":767,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The Routledge handbook of political ecology","event-place":"Abingdon, Oxon ; New York","language":"en","page":"354–365","publisher":"NY Routledge","publisher-place":"Abingdon, Oxon ; New York","title":"degradation and Marginalization","author":[{"family":"Benjaminsen","given":"T.A."}],"editor":[{"family":"Perreault","given":"T."},{"family":"Bridge","given":"Gavin"},{"family":"McCarthy","given":"James"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":32702,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H5D8JETW"],"itemData":{"id":32702,"type":"book","event-place":"Dordrecht","language":"en","publisher":"Springer","publisher-place":"Dordrecht","title":"Marginality : addressing the nexus of poverty, exclusion and ecology","editor":[{"family":"Braun","given":"J.v"},{"family":"Gatzweiler","given":"F.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. When this self-reinforcing downward spiral becomes extreme, people are forced to move in increasing numbers to marginal and ecologically fragile lands or to cities (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tSrJ5wVh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nayak & Berkes, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Nayak & Berkes, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34656,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DFJ8DJAW"],"itemData":{"id":34656,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Local Environment","DOI":"10.1080/13549839.2010.487527","ISSN":"1354-9839, 1469-6711","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Local Environment","language":"en","page":"553-567","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Whose marginalisation? Politics around environmental injustices in India's Chilika lagoon","title-short":"Whose marginalisation?","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13549839.2010.487527","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Nayak","given":"Prateep Kumar"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other research, however, argues that this approach overly simplifies the poverty-environment relationship, ignores large impacts from non-poor and industrial activity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"z3Qthl6x","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. L. Peluso, 1992)","plainCitation":"(N. L. Peluso, 1992)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32668,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PVFMT3VE"],"itemData":{"id":32668,"type":"book","event-place":"Berkeley","language":"en","publisher":"University of California Press","publisher-place":"Berkeley","title":"Forests, Poor People: Resource Control and Resistance in Java","author":[{"family":"Peluso","given":"N.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and undervalues the capacity of local communities in both perceiving and mitigating degradation when their management systems are in place and recognized ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Repzb5Fq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Angelsen, 1997; Scherr, 2000; Swinton, 2003; Tiffen, 1994)","plainCitation":"(Angelsen, 1997; Scherr, 2000; Swinton, 2003; Tiffen, 1994)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":101,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RW72FXCG","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8YKDQLLI"],"itemData":{"id":101,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forum for Development Studies","DOI":"10.1080/08039410.1997.9666053","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"135–154","title":"The Poverty—Environment Thesis: Was Brundtland Wrong?","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Angelsen","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}},{"id":32660,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4NLD63M3"],"itemData":{"id":32660,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Food Policy","DOI":"10.1016/S0306-9192(00)00022-1","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"479–498","title":"A downward spiral? Research evidence on the relationship between poverty and natural resource degradation","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Scherr","given":"S.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":32652,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8SPMKYRS"],"itemData":{"id":32652,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2003.06.006","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"1865–1872","title":"Poverty and Environment in Latin America: Concepts, Evidence and Policy Implications","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Swinton","given":"S.M."},{"family":"Escobar","given":"G."},{"family":"Reardon","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":32629,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CBKT7NG8"],"itemData":{"id":32629,"type":"book","call-number":"S625.K4 T54 1994","event-place":"Chichester ; New York","ISBN":"978-0-471-94143-9","number-of-pages":"311","publisher":"J. Wiley","publisher-place":"Chichester ; New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"More people, less erosion: environmental recovery in Kenya","title-short":"More people, less erosion","author":[{"family":"Tiffen","given":"Mary"},{"family":"Mortimore","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Gichuki","given":"Francis"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, poverty can be ultimately driven not by the state of surrounding resources, but by a lack of assets and access to other forms of labor, markets, and livelihoods, which subsequently limit peoples’ options to the use of common resources that may have already been substantially depleted by historical industrial processes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cjrHfBxv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barbier, 2010; S. Dasgupta, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Barbier, 2010; S. Dasgupta, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":766,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AILQSB8B"],"itemData":{"id":766,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and Development Economics","DOI":"10.1017/S1355770X1000032X","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"635–660","title":"Poverty, development, and environment","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Barbier","given":"E.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32637,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QVV36X4E"],"itemData":{"id":32637,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2004.10.003","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"617–638","title":"Where is the Poverty–Environment Nexus? Evidence from Cambodia, Lao PDR, and Vietnam","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Dasgupta","given":"S."},{"family":"Deichmann","given":"U."},{"family":"Meisner","given":"C."},{"family":"Wheeler","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other determinants that contribute to poverty include the location of schools and health centers, access to infrastructure, natural resources endowment, access to input and, output markets, and access to jobs.
      The current general relationship between poverty and environmental degradation and biodiversity loss may still be represented by a downward spiral, yet the historical large-scale degradation of the environment may likely not have occurred because of poverty, and the ultimate historical drivers of poverty and marginalization are many-fold and not specifically related to environmental state. There are clear links between poverty/ and unsustainable use of wild species, though, as mentioned above, the initial direction of causality is unclear and very likely context dependent. In short, the question of whether resource users are “poor” because of unsustainable use, or if unsustainable use happens because users are “poor” is much less certain than what was historically assumed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CRPN7H84","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}n\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Béné, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26021,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AY8V9ILG","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/47NCSXJP"],"itemData":{"id":26021,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World development","ISSN":"0305-750X","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"World development","note":"number: 6\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"949-975","title":"When fishery rhymes with poverty: a first step beyond the old paradigm on poverty in small-scale fisheries","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Béné","given":"Christophe"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the case of some primary sectors, such as fisheries, the characterization of poverty and agency of users themselves is increasingly being reassessed as better economic data becomes available ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vKOC9Ts6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Giron-Nava, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Giron-Nava, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32636,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3Y5HBDM6"],"itemData":{"id":32636,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12332","language":"en","title":"Managing at Maximum Sustainable Yield does not ensure economic well-being for artisanal fishers","author":[{"family":"Giron-Nava","given":"A."},{"family":"Johnson","given":"A.F."},{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Aburto-Oropeza","given":"O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, informal markets and incomes are more recognized ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aW9UwBD7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Klein, 1999; Schuhbauer, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Klein, 1999; Schuhbauer, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32681,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7WXL8Z2G"],"itemData":{"id":32681,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Africa: Journal of the International African Institute","DOI":"10.2307/1160875","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"555","title":"The Barracuda’s Tale: Trawlers, the Informal Sector and a State of Classificatory Disorder off the Nigerian Coast","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Klein","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":32663,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EMZFP9SG"],"itemData":{"id":32663,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Ecology Progress Series","DOI":"10.3354/meps12942","language":"en","page":"365–376","title":"Assessing the economic viability of small-scale fisheries: An example from Mexico","volume":"617–618","author":[{"family":"Schuhbauer","given":"A."},{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"A."},{"family":"Chuenpagdee","given":"R."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and more focus is placed on first-hand perspectives ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eZDknExT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Macfadyen & Corcoran, 2002; Narayan, D.,, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Macfadyen & Corcoran, 2002; Narayan, D.,, 2001)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32673,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WVV8WEQ8"],"itemData":{"id":32673,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"FAO Fisheries Circular","language":"en","title":"Literature review of studies on poverty in fishing communities and of lessons learned in using the sustainable livelihoods approach in poverty alleviation strategies and projects","URL":"ftp://193.43.36.93/docrep/fao/005/y3914e/y3914e00.pdf","author":[{"family":"Macfadyen","given":"G."},{"family":"Corcoran","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":32517,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RDNQX4W8"],"itemData":{"id":32517,"type":"book","publisher":"Oxford University Press","title":"Voices of the poor: crying out for change","author":[{"literal":"Narayan, D.,"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another example is that of indigenous communities, including some of the most historically and currently marginalized peoples in the world. Despite high levels of poverty, dispossession, and multiple social stressors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6W6IN3sh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cunneen, 2005; United Nations, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Cunneen, 2005; United Nations, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32692,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5AADJFYB"],"itemData":{"id":32692,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Issues Crim. Just","language":"en","page":"329","title":"Racism, discrimination and the over-representation of Indigenous people in the criminal justice system: Some conceptual and explanatory issues","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Cunneen","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32633,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HWLSV9BC"],"itemData":{"id":32633,"type":"book","event-place":"New York","language":"en","publisher":"United Nations, Dept. of Economic and Social Affairs","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Rethinking poverty: Report on the world social situation 2010","editor":[{"family":"United Nations","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, many such communities have rich and continuing histories of sustainable resource use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0D6i64ed","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, 2016; M.-C. Cormier-Salem, 2017b; Larr\\\\uc0\\\\u232{}re & La Soudi\\\\uc0\\\\u232{}re, 1987; Tsing, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Cisneros-Montemayor, 2016; M.-C. Cormier-Salem, 2017b; Larrère & La Soudière, 1987; Tsing, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34761,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P2LXMUWA","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZNQMHBSF"],"itemData":{"id":34761,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0166681","issue":"12","title":"A global estimate of seafood consumption by coastal indigenous peoples","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85002898790&doi=10.1371%2fjournal.pone.0166681&partnerID=40&md5=597d1afb26d002398d34360f6ed6117c","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"Andrés M."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."},{"family":"Weatherdon","given":"L.V."},{"family":"Ota","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":764,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/99RE3GY4"],"itemData":{"id":764,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"© 2017 by the author. Carbon policy is a fascinating topic in geography and political ecology, because carbon is a new exchangeable good, which links the local to the international arenas through a complex set of instruments, norms, and institutions. In this paper, after explaining my theoretical and conceptual framework rooted in Africanist geography and currents of political ecology, I analyze the responses of local actors (knowledge, practices, logic, and strategies) to environmental policies and consider the effects of new mechanisms, upon local dynamics in Africa. I focus my attention on the impacts of mangrove reforestation policies on women, who harvest shellfish. My research, conducted over 35 years on coastal mangroves in Africa and Madagascar, provides me with examples. Through a long-term approach to the perception of mangroves and the changing modes of policies, I highlight the weight of imperialism and neo-liberalism and analyze types of environmental injustice against mangrove harvesters, particularly the women, who are the most directly concerned by the preservation of their heritage and are rarely recognized as stakeholders in environmental policies that are defined internationally and implemented at national and local levels. They are not informed (or are deliberately kept unware) of new devices such as REDD+. They have lost their rights of access to the reforested mangrove areas. Carbon policy requires comparative and empirical research, giving voice to local actors, especially women, about their perceptions of policies and actions. The approaches in terms of political ecology must be combined with analysis of the bio-ecological and socio-cultural dynamics of the mangrove.","container-title":"Sustainability (Switzerland)","DOI":"10.3390/su9081485","ISSN":"20711050","issue":"8","title":"Let the women harvest the mangrove. carbon policy, and environmental injustice","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":32685,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VGSX3LW4"],"itemData":{"id":32685,"type":"book","event-place":"Lyon","language":"fr","publisher":"La Manufacture","publisher-place":"Lyon","title":"Cueillir la montagne. Plantes, fleurs, champignons en Gévaudan, Auvergne, Cévennes et Limousin","author":[{"family":"Larrère","given":"R."},{"family":"La Soudière","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1987"]]}}},{"id":32654,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EUW5R6F9"],"itemData":{"id":32654,"type":"book","event-place":"Princeton","language":"en","publisher":"Princeton University Press","publisher-place":"Princeton","title":"The Mushroom at the End of the World. On the Possibility of Life in Capitalist Ruins","author":[{"family":"Tsing","given":"A.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In many cases, strategies for sustainability are intricately woven into culture itself, aside from resource use in and of itself, and yet can work in complement with (or sometimes outperform) “western” models of resource management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"V1nZQpO8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. J. Bennett, 2018; Bess, 2001; Capistrano & Charles, 2012; Porten, 2016)","plainCitation":"(N. J. Bennett, 2018; Bess, 2001; Capistrano & Charles, 2012; Porten, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":769,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2A3VGPLG"],"itemData":{"id":769,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2018.06.001","language":"en","page":"139–146","title":"Navigating a just and inclusive path towards sustainable oceans","volume":"97","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"N.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32699,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KRFYBUVZ"],"itemData":{"id":32699,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"23–32","title":"New Zealand’s indigenous people and their claims to fisheries resources","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Bess","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":32693,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R4965YE8"],"itemData":{"id":32693,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2012.08.011","language":"en","page":"200–209","title":"Indigenous rights and coastal fisheries: A framework of livelihoods, rights and equity","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Capistrano","given":"R.C.G."},{"family":"Charles","given":"A.T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":32538,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X8R25H5K"],"itemData":{"id":32538,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Canadian Studies","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"214–243","title":"Incorporating Indigenous Knowledge Systems into Collaborative Governance for Water: Challenges and Opportunities","volume":"50","author":[{"family":"Porten","given":"S."},{"family":"Loë","given":"R.C."},{"family":"McGregor","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
	Under what conditions and to what extent are “people living in a marginal situation able (or not) to sustainably manage socio-ecological systems and/or to sustainably use wild species?
      As discussed in the Political Drivers section, it is increasingly recognized that local access rights can allow communities to sustainably use common resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iJfkHNlE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ostrom, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Ostrom, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":36,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2NACCRB4"],"itemData":{"id":36,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1172133","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5939","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"419-422","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A General Framework for Analyzing Sustainability of Social-Ecological Systems","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.1172133","volume":"325","author":[{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",7,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This does not necessarily require privatization in a market sense; for example, in marine systems, the recognition and support of local and indigenous traditional and customary management strategies and self-identified objectives can allow for sustainable use of biodiversity, both commercially and for subsistence ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pOke9EyY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2000, 2003; Jentoft & Chuenpagdee, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2000, 2003; Jentoft & Chuenpagdee, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":847,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4FYYZ8LU"],"itemData":{"id":847,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","DOI":"10.1890/1051-0761(2000)010[1251:ROTEKA]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"1051-0761","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Applications","language":"en","page":"1251-1262","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Rediscovery of Traditional Ecological Knowledge as Adaptive Management","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1890/1051-0761(2000)010[1251:ROTEKA]2.0.CO;2","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Colding","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",10]]}}},{"id":768,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H6AL3ASM"],"itemData":{"id":768,"type":"book","event-place":"Cambridge, UK","language":"en","publisher":"Cambrige University press","publisher-place":"Cambridge, UK","title":"Navigating social-ecological systems: builiding resilience for complexity and change","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Colding","given":"J."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":32695,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R67GSZ5Y"],"itemData":{"id":32695,"type":"book","collection-title":"MARE Publication Series","event-place":"Cham","ISBN":"978-3-319-17033-6","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-17034-3","publisher":"Springer International Publishing","publisher-place":"Cham","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Interactive Governance for Small-Scale Fisheries","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-319-17034-3","volume":"13","editor":[{"family":"Jentoft","given":"Svein"},{"family":"Chuenpagdee","given":"Ratana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Contrary to the dominant discourses that make shellfish harvesters poor, with divorced, landless marginal women having access only to wastelands, and considering themselves marginal and unworthy, more and more scholars show that shellfish harvesting is a vital activity. It provides a source of nutrition and a very important source of income for women of all ages, who are gaining economic as well as political power through their activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5ol4MQPQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Burgos & Dillais, 2012; Lau & Scales, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Burgos & Dillais, 2012; Lau & Scales, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32529,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HA4927H5"],"itemData":{"id":32529,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Techniques et Culture, 59 (2)?: .","DOI":"10.4000/tc.6748","issue":"2","page":"326-337","title":"Les femmes, les coquillages et la mangrove","URL":"https://journals.openedition.org/tc/6748","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Burgos","given":"Ariadna"},{"family":"Dillais","given":"Peyo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":32684,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LZ2IZR9N"],"itemData":{"id":32684,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geoforum","language":"en","page":"136–146","title":"Identity, subjectivity and natural resource use: How ethnicity, gender and class intersect to influence mangrove oyster harvesting in The Gambia","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Lau","given":"J.D."},{"family":"Scales","given":"I.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Arctic: local communities across the Arctic face exceptional challenges stemming from historical and ongoing social, political, and now environmental change. In the Canadian Arctic, these communities are almost entirely indigenous (Inuit) and have been historically dispossessed of lands, resources, language, and culture. Nevertheless, the Inuit remain largely rooted in traditional territories and rely on traditional diets for both subsistence and cultural identity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"imcrjU5s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kenny, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Kenny, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32680,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6HIYZQEG"],"itemData":{"id":32680,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"EcoHealth","DOI":"10.1007/s10393-018-1348-z","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"590–607","title":"Caribou (Rangifer tarandus) and Inuit Nutrition Security in Canada","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Kenny","given":"T.-A."},{"family":"Fillion","given":"M."},{"family":"Simpkin","given":"S."},{"family":"Wesche","given":"S.D."},{"family":"Chan","given":"H.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. With significant local declines in caribou (Rangifer tarandus, various subspecies), marine resources including fish and marine mammals are particularly important and the traditional ecological knowledge held by these Peoples increasingly recognized. However, movement across traditional fishing and hunting grounds is now restricted due to melting sea ice as a result of global warming. Additionally, methylmercury concentrations in seafood (particularly marine mammals and fatty fishes) pose threats to public health, particularly fetal and cognitive development ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"k0XvENUY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(H. M. Chan & Receveur, 2000)","plainCitation":"(H. M. Chan & Receveur, 2000)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32694,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G59MLJZH"],"itemData":{"id":32694,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Pollution","DOI":"10.1016/S0269-7491(00)00061-0","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–2","title":"Mercury in the traditional diet of indigenous peoples in Canada","volume":"110","author":[{"family":"Chan","given":"H.M."},{"family":"Receveur","given":"O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Even as traditional Inuit ways of life are more respected, much stronger and cross-scale policies will be required to support their continued use of resources. 
	Latin America: The vicuña. Vicugna vicugna has one of the most valuable and highly priced animal fibers on the international market ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"L7uNU6PI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Kasterine & Lichtenstein, 2018)","plainCitation":"(A. Kasterine & Lichtenstein, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32352,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XP2T8FRZ"],"itemData":{"id":32352,"type":"book","event-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","publisher":"International Trade Centre","publisher-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","title":"Trade in Vicuña: the Implications for Conservation and Rural Livelihoods.","author":[{"family":"Kasterine","given":"A"},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The luxury garments made from vicuña are sold in the most exclusive fashion houses around the world. Whereas vicuña products are available only to very affluent consumers, the fiber is produced mainly by extremely low-income communities from the Andes that face high levels of persistent poverty and inequality. Despite the high market value of vicuña products, the generation and distribution of benefits to local people has been limited thus far ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Vb8TImp7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7370,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7TN9Z64K","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/77KTC5ZS"],"itemData":{"id":7370,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","DOI":"http://doi.org/10.18352/ijc.139","issue":"1","page":"100-121","title":"Vicuña conservation and poverty alleviation? Andean communities and international fibre markets","URL":"https://www.thecommonsjournal.org/articles/10.18352/ijc.139/","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein, Gabriela","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Local communities “pay the cost” of vicuña conservation by allowing vicuñas to graze on communal or private land. The production of fiber also relies on a substantial investment that is mostly borne by Governments and local communities. However, most of the benefits are captured by traders and international textile companies. Vicuña fiber prices have historically been related to factors such as: market demand; the bargaining power of the actors involved; actors’ cash flow issues; volume of fiber stocked; and number of channels for commercialization ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AXwcP4iU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. Brewin, 2007; Lichtenstein, 2002; Lichtenstein & Vila, 2003; Sahley, 2004)","plainCitation":"(L. Brewin, 2007; Lichtenstein, 2002; Lichtenstein & Vila, 2003; Sahley, 2004)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":877,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y7IGQXNB"],"itemData":{"id":877,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"The vicuña industry in Peru: Has the vicuña lived up to its reputation as the gold of the Andes? MSc Globalisation and Latin American Development, Institute for the Study of the Americas","author":[{"family":"Brewin","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":876,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6RECE82G"],"itemData":{"id":876,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Poverty","language":"pt","note":"ISBN: 1","title":"Manejo comunitario de vicuñas en Perú. Estudio de caso del manejo comunitario de vida silvestre","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"G."},{"family":"Oribe","given":"F."},{"family":"Grieg-Gran","given":"M."},{"family":"Mazzucchelli","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":24,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SYXFDIW","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RE4E67DM"],"itemData":{"id":24,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mountain Research and Development","DOI":"10.1659/0276-4741(2003)023[0197:VUBACA]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0276-4741, 1994-7151","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Mountain Research and Development","language":"en","page":"198-201","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Vicuna Use by Andean Communities: An Overview","title-short":"Vicuna Use by Andean Communities","URL":"http://www.bioone.org/doi/abs/10.1659/0276-4741%282003%29023%5B0197%3AVUBACA%5D2.0.CO%3B2","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Vilá","given":"Bibiana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",5]]}}},{"id":32521,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2CX3HB6N"],"itemData":{"id":32521,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Culture and Agriculture","issue":"1&2","page":"9–17","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Neoliberalism meets pre-Columbian tradition: Campesino communities and vicuña management in Andean Peru","title-short":"Neoliberalism meets pre-Columbian tradition","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Sahley","given":"Catherine"},{"family":"Torres","given":"Jorge"},{"family":"Sanchez","given":"Jesús"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is almost impossible for a remote Andean community (or producer) to negotiate with a European textile company or large trading company on equal terms. Efforts to increase the benefits accrued to the rural poor should explicitly redress access asymmetries and strengthen producer associations.
	Africa: In Africa, processes of marginalization and mangrove grabbing are largely due to mangrove reforestation campaigns, or carbon policies under the frame of Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZZ0JnSvt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Beymer-Farris & Bassett, 2012; M.-C. Cormier-Salem, 2017a)","plainCitation":"(Beymer-Farris & Bassett, 2012; M.-C. Cormier-Salem, 2017a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32698,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8V2G6RDI"],"itemData":{"id":32698,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"332–341","title":"The REDD menace: Resurgent protectionism in Tanzania’s mangrove forests","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Beymer-Farris","given":"B.A."},{"family":"Bassett","given":"T.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":32530,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AV7M5DNP"],"itemData":{"id":32530,"type":"chapter","container-title":"In H. Artaud & A. Surallés (Eds.), The Sea within. Marine Tenure and Cosmopolitical Debates","event-place":"Copenhagen, Denmark","ISBN":"978-87-92786-87-6","page":"143-162","publisher-place":"Copenhagen, Denmark","title":"Cormier -Salem, M.-C. (2017). Mangrove grabbing. An exploration of changes in mangrove tenure from a political ecology perspective.","author":[{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The women, who are making their household’s livelihood from gathering oysters and cockles, are the most directly concerned with the preservation of the mangrove. For a long time, they conserved certain sites and developed customary rules that have maintained sustainable use of mangroves such as: defining harvesting season, designating zones shared between lineages and neighborhoods, and sacred places that are prohibited for use. In addition, they have reforested certain habitats with Rhizophora plants, and attempted to make better use of their products using appropriate schemes of labelling. 
      However, women are rarely recognized as part-stakeholder of the environmental policies defined internationally and implemented at national and local scales. They are not informed (or deliberately kept ignorant) of new devices such as Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation. This has led in some cases to the loss of their access rights to the reforested mangrove areas.
	Oceania: As expected from ecological theory and models, as oceans warm due to climate change marine species are shifting to cooler areas towards temperate areas or deeper water, with a net loss of marine biodiversity and abundance in tropical regions. This poses many issues for Small Island Developing States that rely on marine ecosystem services. One example is Palau in the South Pacific, which for some time has transitioned into a tourism-based economy focused on coral reef biodiversity. The consumption of reef fishes is still vital in Palauan culture, as is the practice of fishing itself along the island nation; industrial fishing is less developed, but monetary benefits from fishing access agreements for foreign tuna fisheries (that export most catch) do contribute to the national economy. As a response to anticipated effects of climate change, and the growing pressure on local reef fishes from seafood demand by tourists, Palau has initiated a national strategy based on boosting consumption of sustainably caught tuna for the tourist market, thus allowing for the sustainability of local reefs and traditional Palauan subsistence fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"927NE8Wj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wabnitz, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Wabnitz, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32650,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VKKZ2ZCQ"],"itemData":{"id":32650,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.07.022","language":"en","title":"Ecotourism, climate change and reef fish consumption in Palau: Benefits, trade-offs and adaptation strategies","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.marpol.2017.07.022","author":[{"family":"Wabnitz","given":"C."},{"family":"Cisneros-Montemayor","given":"A.M."},{"family":"Hanich","given":"Q."},{"family":"Ota","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The outcomes remain to be seen, but this is a good example of science-based policies that integrate local goals and that could be further supported through international initiatives. 
	Asia: In the Sundarbans, the women, who collect prawn seed along the rivers, are the poorest, and belong to the lowest caste of the society ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aho5Yfsu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jalais, 2010; Lahiri-Dutt & Samanta, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Jalais, 2010; Lahiri-Dutt & Samanta, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32635,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QTNFXSGV"],"itemData":{"id":32635,"type":"book","event-place":"New Delhi","language":"en","publisher":"Routledge","publisher-place":"New Delhi","title":"Forest of Tigers. People, politics and environment in the Sundarbans","author":[{"family":"Jalais","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32634,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QZV64AAF"],"itemData":{"id":32634,"type":"book","event-place":"New Haven, Connecticut, États-Unis","language":"en","publisher":"Yale University press","publisher-place":"New Haven, Connecticut, États-Unis","title":"Dancing with the River. People and life on the Chars of South Asia","author":[{"family":"Lahiri-Dutt","given":"K."},{"family":"Samanta","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. They do not have access to land or the forest. They use a very simple and cheap technique, a mosquito net fixed on pole, for gathering the prawn seed. Thanks to the development of intensive shrimp farms in the North of the Delta, and the huge demand of prawn seed, they are becoming very rich and powerful. They have got a strong identity, not only as "working" women but as "earning" women, daring to reverse the hierarchical order of the village, refusing both tradition and the urban ideals of femininity.

4.2.3.5.3. Poverty and indigenous people
There are about 476,6 million indigenous people in the world today present in over 90 countries. Indigenous communities represent about 6,2% of the world’s population but make up 15% of the world’s extreme poor, and 1/3 of the rural poor ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FHIuo5IN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(ILO, 2020)","plainCitation":"(ILO, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32475,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/74MXRBRR"],"itemData":{"id":32475,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Impact of COVID-19 on people's livelihoods, their health and our food systems. A joint statement by ILO, FAO, IFAD and WHO","URL":"https://www.who.int/news/item/13-10-2020-impact-of-covid-19-on-people%27s-livelihoods-their-health-and-our-food-systems","author":[{"family":"ILO","given":"F.A.O."},{"literal":"I.F.A.D."},{"literal":"W.H.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. According to this recent report, over 73.4 % of the global indigenous population live in rural areas, but there are substantial regional variations. The highest proportion of indigenous peoples residing in rural areas is found in Africa (82.1 per cent), followed by Asia and the Pacific (72.8 per cent) and Europe and Central Asia (66.4 per cent). In Latin America and the Caribbean and in Northern America, a majority of indigenous peoples are urban dwellers (52.2 per cent and 69.0 per cent respectively). They live, own and occupy approximately one quarter of the world’s lands and waters which represents 80% of the world’s biodiversity. Indigenous peoples are nearly three times as likely to be living in extreme poverty as their non-indigenous counterparts represent a sizable share of the global poor ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vCJmITk6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(ILO, 2020)","plainCitation":"(ILO, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32475,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/74MXRBRR"],"itemData":{"id":32475,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Impact of COVID-19 on people's livelihoods, their health and our food systems. A joint statement by ILO, FAO, IFAD and WHO","URL":"https://www.who.int/news/item/13-10-2020-impact-of-covid-19-on-people%27s-livelihoods-their-health-and-our-food-systems","author":[{"family":"ILO","given":"F.A.O."},{"literal":"I.F.A.D."},{"literal":"W.H.O."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indigenous peoples’ life expectancy is up to 20 years lower than the life expectancy of non-indigenous people worldwide. Research conducted byITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hvQbI0y3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. H. Hall, 2014)","plainCitation":"(G. H. Hall, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32649,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ESTR4LVW"],"itemData":{"id":32649,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"DESA Working Paper","issue":"145","language":"en","title":"Indigenous people, poverty and development","author":[{"family":"Hall","given":"G.H."},{"family":"Patrinos","given":"H.A."},{"family":"Cambridge. Islam","given":"S.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} for the World Bank found that poverty among indigenous people manifested in various ways including insecure land and property rights, discrimination, heightened vulnerability to risk and climate change, and a wide range of health, education and other related socio-economic disparities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8Zs6oT5o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. H. Hall, 2014)","plainCitation":"(G. H. Hall, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32649,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ESTR4LVW"],"itemData":{"id":32649,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"DESA Working Paper","issue":"145","language":"en","title":"Indigenous people, poverty and development","author":[{"family":"Hall","given":"G.H."},{"family":"Patrinos","given":"H.A."},{"family":"Cambridge. Islam","given":"S.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indigenous peoples experience a high degree of socio-economic marginalization and are at disproportionate risk in public health emergencies. They often face impediments to their access to natural resources, basic services, the formal economy, and justice, as well as their participation in decision making. Factors such as lack of access to effective monitoring and early-warning systems, and adequate health and social services resulted in indigenous people becoming more vulnerable to the present COVID-19 pandemic ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"twSNYE0o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E.C.L.A.C., 2021)","plainCitation":"(E.C.L.A.C., 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32688,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3CE34IJU"],"itemData":{"id":32688,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Project Documents","language":"en","title":"United Nations.“The impact ofCOVID-19 on indigenous peoples in Latin America (Abya Yala): between invisibility and collective resistance”","author":[{"literal":"E.C.L.A.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Poverty in indigenous groups is probably related to the historical-political conditions that untied indigenous peoples from the control of their territories relegated them to the margins of society or directly excluded them. 

4.2.3.6. Gender equity
Key messages: 	
* The benefits and costs of wild species use are inequitably distributed across genders with women and those of diverse gender identities experiencing the greatest inequity (established but incomplete){4.2.3.6.2}.
* Access to wild species and uses tend to be gendered. This is evident in many indigenous societies.  There are various kinds of roles and responsibilities as well as rules of use that apply to women (established but incomplete){4.2.3.6.2}.	
* Women have particularly key roles in foster sustainable use in many cultures; as they play a primary role in food production and education (established but incomplete){4.2.3.6.2}.	
* Many kinds of resource management institutions are gender blind and do not take into consideration the diversity of roles and responsibilities nor the inequities experience by women and gender diverse peoples (established but incomplete){4.2.3.6.3}.	

4.2.3.6.1. Overview  
Gender shapes the social roles that men and women play and the power relations between them, which can have a profound effect on the use and management of natural resources. Gender is not based on sex or the biological differences between women and men; rather, gender is shaped by culture and social norms. Thus, depending on values, norms, customs and laws, women and men in different parts of the world have adopted different gender roles and relations. Within the same society, gender roles also differ by race/ethnicity, class/caste, religion, ethnicity, age and economic circumstances. Gender and gender roles then affect the economic, political, social, and ecological opportunities and constraints faced by both women and men (Convention on Biological Diversity).
      The case for seeing women as having a special relationship to Nature has been made by ecofeminists for more than three decades. The term "ecofeminism" was coined in the 1970s to raise awareness about interconnections between women's oppression and nature's domination in an attempt to liberate women and nature from subordination ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KsEGdhoz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(d\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Eaubonne, 1978)","plainCitation":"(d’Eaubonne, 1978)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32624,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8YJ3HEDD"],"itemData":{"id":32624,"type":"book","language":"fr","publisher":"Éditions ATP","title":"Écologie, féminisme: révolution ou mutation?","author":[{"family":"Eaubonne","given":"F.","non-dropping-particle":"d'"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1978"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Since then, ecofeminism has attracted scholars and activists from various disciplines, drawing on Marxist critic, animal studies, postcolonialism, and political ecology ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"c7lhS75R","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vakoch & Mickey, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Vakoch & Mickey, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32536,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/URVCM5YQ"],"itemData":{"id":32536,"type":"book","event-place":"Abingdon, Oxon ; New York","language":"en","publisher":"Routledge, Taylor & Francis Group","publisher-place":"Abingdon, Oxon ; New York","title":"Women and nature? : beyond dualism in gender, body, and environment","editor":[{"family":"Vakoch","given":"D.A."},{"family":"Mickey","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Green Belt Movement in Kenya and the Chipko Movement in India are said to epitomize the essence of ecofeminism.
      Conceptualizations of the nature of the link between women and nature vary from asserting a physiological connection (women as birth givers etc.), to a more socially based view linked to women´s social role (as mothers, farmers, water carriers) (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4NO6eCxE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Shiva, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Shiva, 1998)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32657,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SYV6LPDK"],"itemData":{"id":32657,"type":"book","event-place":"London","language":"en","publisher":"Zed Books Ltd","publisher-place":"London","title":"Staying alive. Women, ecology and survival in India","author":[{"family":"Shiva","given":"V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). Other authors consider that reducing women´s decisions to a set of biologically determined characteristics devalues their agency, fails to recognize that they may also align with other identities (e.g., caste, class) and undermines the fact that they are situated in certain localities which impose and offer a specific set of constraints ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kXkNgV1A","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mawdsley, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Mawdsley, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":788,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YTS36G2J"],"itemData":{"id":788,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Development and Change","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"79–103","title":"India's middle classes and the environment","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Mawdsley","given":"Emma"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A common ground between these perspectives is that the roles and responsibilities of men and women in the management of biodiversity, and the ability to participate in decision-making, vary between and within countries and cultures. 
      
4.2.3.6.2. Gender-specific roles, needs, dynamics and the sustainable use of wild species 
The impact of women´s participation and empowerment on sustainable use outcomes will be stressed through a few examples, illustrating what makes some gender-based practices sustainable. For example, across Africa, household chores are often divided according to gender; this in turn shapes the different ways in which women and men relate to trees. For example, in the case of the shea tree, women are the custodians of knowledge concerning the gathering and processing of shea products ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DqmowDaa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Elias, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Elias, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32687,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D7MPVVGQ"],"itemData":{"id":32687,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"CAB Reviews: Perspectives in Agriculture, Veterinary Science, Nutrition and Natural Resources","issue":"040","language":"en","title":"Distinct, shared and complementary: gendered agroecological knowledge in review","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Elias","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The women have ingenious use of the shea nut, bark, roots and leaves: to increase milk production in lactating mothers, to relieve those suffering from malaria and to make the traditional ‘benga’ dish. Since they are not the ones to process shea nuts into butter, men are less concerned about the traits of shea nuts that yield quality butter but "male farmers prize the tree (…) for its shade and its role in improving the fertility of the soil in their fields" ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ntwusa5W","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Elias, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Elias, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32687,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D7MPVVGQ"],"itemData":{"id":32687,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"CAB Reviews: Perspectives in Agriculture, Veterinary Science, Nutrition and Natural Resources","issue":"040","language":"en","title":"Distinct, shared and complementary: gendered agroecological knowledge in review","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Elias","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Women are not the owner of the trees, but they have access thanks to the contributions perceived by men. Nevertheless, with "globalization’ that means trade of forest-based local products on international markets and the increasing demand for quality fair trade products, the women often lost the control of the commercial network, and therefore are "marginalized" by men. From a case?study of fairtrade shea butter produced by women in Burkina Faso and consumed by European and North American women, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Jg6r4ztF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Elias & Saussey, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Elias & Saussey, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32532,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JCP2APJQ"],"itemData":{"id":32532,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sociologia Ruralis","DOI":"10.1111/soru.12007","ISSN":"00380199","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Sociol Ruralis","language":"en","page":"158-179","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"‘The Gift that Keeps on Giving’: Unveiling the Paradoxes of Fair Trade Shea Butter: Paradoxical narratives of fair trade shea butter","title-short":"‘The Gift that Keeps on Giving’","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/soru.12007","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Elias","given":"Marlène"},{"family":"Saussey","given":"Magalie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} showed the low returns for butter producers and doubt the ‘fairness’ and solidarity aspects of the movement. 
      In many parts of the world, women often bear the primary responsibility for feeding their families, collecting, processing, cooking, rationing and storing food, nurturing. In many developing countries, women collect and prepare highly nutritious foods from wild species to complement and add flavor to the staples of family meals. In addition, income generated by women from the harvesting of wild species adds to the purchasing power of households and therefore their food security. Men, on the other hand, are more likely than women to be responsible for gathering wild honey, birds’ eggs and insects, for hunting and fishing, and in many countries, for the commercial exploitation of a forest’s wood resources. 
      
Box 4.28. Women and sustainable use of wild species 

Women play a central role in the conservation and sustainable use of wild species. This can be highlighted in three main aspects: 
	First, they are the most numerous in this activity area, present at all stages of the value chain (extraction, processing, distribution, consumption). 
	Second, they depend closely on these species and uses for their livelihood and the well-being of their family. These activities occupy a major place in their calendar; they spend a lot of time for collecting three basic needs: food, water and fuelwood (e.g., fuelwood; Agarwal, 1986). Wild species are a source of essential income for themselves and their families. It is also a means of economic empowerment, social recognition, and status acquisition within the community. Besides, most of the women who use wild species are elderly, widowed or divorced, and have no land tenure rights (see section on poverty and marginalization). 
	Third, closely dependent on these resources, they are most concerned with their conservation and sustainable use; they have intimate links with these species. Also, they have knowledge, practices, rules of use and access that preserve and value these species.

 
      In a review conducted by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1bkaCfHc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Meinzen-Dick, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Meinzen-Dick, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32671,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AY8G9TEG"],"itemData":{"id":32671,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"29–55","title":"Gender and Sustainability","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Meinzen-Dick","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} suggested that neither men nor women are inherently more resource-conserving; rather, their motives – issues related to closeness to nature, interests and needs for those species- and their material conditions and means – access and use rights to wild species, influences their sustainable practices. Property rights and security of tenure influence the motives and means that men and women have to exploit or conserve natural resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"W8s0BDvw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Meinzen-Dick, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Meinzen-Dick, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32671,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AY8G9TEG"],"itemData":{"id":32671,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"29–55","title":"Gender and Sustainability","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Meinzen-Dick","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thus, adoption of sustainable practices requires attention to control rights. In most circumstances there are gender-based differences and inequalities, which tend to favor males. Gender differences are evident in economic opportunities and access to and control over land, biodiversity resources and other productive assets, in decision-making power, as well as in vulnerability to biodiversity loss, climate change and natural disasters.
      
Box 4.29. Women’s vital role in social movements to conserve biodiversity in the Brazilian Amazon

Deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon has accelerated significantly during the past few years, threatening livelihoods, and becoming a source of carbon emissions rather than a global carbon sink. However, during the period between 2004 and 2012, Brazil decreased deforestation by over 80%. Brazil’s Federal government created 89 extractive and sustainable development reserves in Amazonia, between 1990 and 2009, encompassing 24 million hectares. During a 35-year period in Brazil, nearly half of the Amazon rain forest became protected through a system of extractive and sustainable development reserves. This remarkable accomplishment was the result of rigorous research, advocacy leading to policy change and the collective struggle of a strong social movement. As deforestation accelerates in Brazil today, it is critical to remember the social processes and policies which led to an earlier, highly significant, national, and global conservation gain. As part of this process, women played a pivotal role. Thirty-five years ago, women were not permitted to be members of one of the most prominent organizations of the Amazonian social movement, the National Council of Rubber Tappers. However, the creation of a Secretariat of Women Extractivists within National Rubber Tappers Council was influential in transforming women’s roles within the hierarchy of extractive reserves from invisible to one of consequence ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tbWdfVrY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Shanley, 2011)","plainCitation":"(P. Shanley, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32518,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VPKZWXS3"],"itemData":{"id":32518,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper discusses the evolution of the roles of Brazilian women within one of the most prominent organizations of the Amazonian social movement, the National Council of Extractivist Populations (CNS). Between 1990 and 2009, Brazil’s Federal government created 89 extractive and sustainable development reserves in Amazonia, encompassing 24 million hectares. The conceptual underpinning of these reserves – sustainable and multiple-use forest management – are daily put into practice by thousands of rural Amazonian women. However, rural women’s relative role in forest policy is currently marginal. The Secretariat of Women Extractivists of CNS helped to transform women’s roles within CNS and the political hierarchy of extractive reserves from largely invisible to one of significance. Their work across sectors, cultivation of ties with the State, capacity building and acknowledgement of women’s cultural connections to forests, provide a strong foundation for an increasing role of Amazonian women to promote sustainable forest management and conservation.","container-title":"International Forestry Review","DOI":"10.1505/146554811797406570","ISSN":"1465-5489","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"int. forest. rev.","language":"en","page":"233-244","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Brazil's social movement, women and forests: a case study from the National Council of Rubber Tappers","title-short":"Brazil's social movement, women and forests","URL":"http://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/10.1505/146554811797406570","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Shanley","given":"P."},{"family":"Da Silva","given":"F.C."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",6,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Over the ensuing years, to be more inclusive, the name of National Rubber Tappers Council was changed to the National Council of Extractivist Populations. The work of women in building capacity, cultivating ties with key governmental agencies and recognizing cultural connections to forests, provided a strong foundation for an increasing role of Amazonian women to promote sustainable management and conservation. The conceptual foundation of extractive reserves - multiple-use and sustainable forest management - are practiced by thousands of rural Amazonian women. Women have played a vital role in understanding the direct value of forests to food security and the health and welfare of families. Traditional knowledge of forest resources was central to determine the categories of extractive reserves, and what type of use and management would be permitted including hunting, fishing, logging, gathering etc. Local input from rubber tappers and forest-reliant communities contributed to the policies designating various categories of sustainable use. From not being allowed to be part of Brazil’s largest Amazonian social movement, women now compose 40% of the of the membership of the National Council of Extractivist Populations and are leaders of 25% of conservation units ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sdMgbKQ8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Shanley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(P. Shanley, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32516,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U7ZGAVJY"],"itemData":{"id":32516,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Desenvolvimento e Meio Ambiente","DOI":"10.5380/dma.v48i0.58834","ISSN":"2176-9109, 1518-952X","journalAbbreviation":"Desenvolv. Meio Ambiente","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Women in the wake: expanding the legacy of Chico Mendes in Brazil’s environmental movement","title-short":"Women in the wake","URL":"https://revistas.ufpr.br/made/article/view/58834","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Da Silva","given":"Fatima Cristina"},{"family":"MacDonald","given":"Trilby"},{"family":"Silva","given":"Murilo Da Serra"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Brazilian women currently occupy critical positions from leading grass roots organizations to high level positions in government agencies and worker’s unions. While rubber tapper and conservationist Chico Mendes succeeded in launching a national campaign to create extractive reserves, women played a crucial role in expanding and making tangible a globally significant conservation movement.


      4.2.3.6.3. Gender and public policies
Most programs to promote sustainability have been gender blind and thus ended up working primarily with men, who are more likely to occupy public spaces (including community organizations and government or external programs) and are often more readily recognized by outsiders as the foresters, irrigators, fishers, and even farmers. Justice and equity concerns are now prominent in national and international policies. For instance, Aichi Target 11 calls for protected areas to be equitably managed by 2020, and Gender equality is called for in Sustainable Development Goals 5. However, understanding of how to consider and incorporate equity and the broader concept of justice into conservation and sustainable use of wild species remains nascent.

4.2.3.7. Indigenous peoples and food systems- impacts of pollution 
Indigenous peoples and local communities experience large burdens of environmental pollution linked to the expansion of commodity frontiers and industrial development ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CipQDlzo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Basu, 2018; Fern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez-Llamazares, 2020; Landrigan, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Basu, 2018; Fernández-Llamazares, 2020; Landrigan, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":787,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V67QFNFK"],"itemData":{"id":787,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental health perspectives","issue":"10","page":"106001","title":"A state-of-the-science review of mercury biomarkers in human populations worldwide between 2000 and 2018","volume":"126","author":[{"family":"Basu","given":"Niladri"},{"family":"Horvat","given":"Milena"},{"family":"Evers","given":"David C"},{"family":"Zastenskaya","given":"Irina"},{"family":"Weihe","given":"Pál"},{"family":"Tempowski","given":"Joanna"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":762,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GPPGE69Y"],"itemData":{"id":762,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Local attitudes towards wildlife encompass environmental, political, sociocultural and psychological dimensions that shape human-wildlife interactions and conservation efforts. Although the political and sociocultural dimensions of these interactions have been extensively examined by political ecologists and cultural anthropologists, psychological aspects have remained largely untapped so far. This article presents an in-depth review of a long historical record of changing attitudes towards wildlife among Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem in southern Kenya, examining their shifts in light of different conservation psychology theories. The historical changes are reviewed in relation to three theories of attitudinal shifts (i.e., cognitive dissonance, reactance, and motivation crowding theory) and discussed in a context of land dispossession, conservation policies and changes in Maasai lifestyles and cultural values. We conclude that conservation psychology adds an important dimension to understanding attitudes towards wildlife and how they bear on conservation policies and practices.","call-number":"Sub 10a","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2019.125763","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Historical shifts in local attitudes towards wildlife by Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem (Kenya): Insights from three conservation psychology theories","title-short":"Historical shifts in local attitudes towards wildlife by Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem (Kenya)","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1617138119302833","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Fernández-Llamazares","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Western","given":"David"},{"family":"Galvin","given":"Kathleen A."},{"family":"McElwee","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"Cabeza","given":"Mar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":781,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TZEZ58V9"],"itemData":{"id":781,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental health perspectives","issue":"8","page":"084501","title":"Pollution and global health–an agenda for prevention","volume":"126","author":[{"family":"Landrigan","given":"Philip J"},{"family":"Fuller","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Hu","given":"Howard"},{"family":"Caravanos","given":"Jack"},{"family":"Cropper","given":"Maureen L"},{"family":"Hanrahan","given":"David"},{"family":"Sandilya","given":"Karti"},{"family":"Chiles","given":"Thomas C"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Pushpam"},{"family":"Suk","given":"William A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Increasing demands on indigenous peoples and local communities’ territories from the expansion of industrial resource development and extraction, often result in pollution risks, which endanger the collective continuance of indigenous peoples and local communities, and the foundations of their cultures, subsistence-based livelihoods and ways of life ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vaE3Tqgg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Armstrong & Brown, 2019; B. L. Parlee, 2018; Scheidel, 2020; Spice, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Armstrong & Brown, 2019; B. L. Parlee, 2018; Scheidel, 2020; Spice, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":786,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/38VKX7NN"],"itemData":{"id":786,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","issue":"1","note":"publisher: BioOne","page":"14–31","title":"Frontiers are frontlines: Ethnobiological science against ongoing colonialism","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Armstrong","given":"Chelsey Geralda"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Christie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":34961,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JUP575TP"],"itemData":{"id":34961,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science advances","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Science advances","note":"number: 2","page":"e1701611","title":"Undermining subsistence: Barren-ground caribou in a “tragedy of open access”","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Parlee","given":"Brenda L"},{"family":"Sandlos","given":"John"},{"family":"Natcher","given":"David C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32662,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GR658T66"],"itemData":{"id":32662,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"102104","title":"Environmental Conflicts and Defenders: A Global Overview","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Scheidel","given":"Arnim"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":780,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EDZZVWNY"],"itemData":{"id":780,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and Society","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Berghahn Journals","page":"40–56","title":"Fighting invasive infrastructures: Indigenous relations against pipelines","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Spice","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Environmental pollution has been recorded in numerous indigenous peoples and local communities’ lands worldwide, increasing risks and burdens of disease (e.g.,ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LCUBOIyW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Lewis, 2017)","plainCitation":"(J. Lewis, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":785,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RIJYUN2V"],"itemData":{"id":785,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current environmental health reports","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"130–141","title":"Mining and environmental health disparities in Native American communities","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Lewis","given":"Johnnye"},{"family":"Hoover","given":"Joseph"},{"family":"MacKenzie","given":"Debra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and forcing many communities to shift away from traditional lifestyles (e.g.,ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UQ1iPA56","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Hoover, 2017)","plainCitation":"(E. Hoover, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":779,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JLKP5LJG"],"itemData":{"id":779,"type":"book","publisher":"U of Minnesota Press","title":"The river is in us: Fighting toxics in a Mohawk community","author":[{"family":"Hoover","given":"Elizabeth"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many health impacts documented among indigenous peoples and local communities are mediated through the consumption of wild foods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"npa3AL9r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bordeleau, 2016; Ostertag, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Bordeleau, 2016; Ostertag, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":784,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VULTG2AR"],"itemData":{"id":784,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science of the Total Environment","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"529–538","title":"“Is it still safe to eat traditional food?” Addressing traditional food safety concerns in aboriginal communities","volume":"565","author":[{"family":"Bordeleau","given":"Serge"},{"family":"Asselin","given":"Hugo"},{"family":"Mazerolle","given":"Marc J"},{"family":"Imbeau","given":"Louis"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":798,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LVZKLDV6"],"itemData":{"id":798,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Chemosphere","issue":"9","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"1165–1172","title":"Estimated dietary exposure to fluorinated compounds from traditional foods among Inuit in Nunavut, Canada","volume":"75","author":[{"family":"Ostertag","given":"Sonja K"},{"family":"Tague","given":"Brett A"},{"family":"Humphries","given":"Murray M"},{"family":"Tittlemier","given":"Sheryl A"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Hing Man"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, obtained through hunting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rwzAEq7q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cartr\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}-Sabat\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Cartró-Sabaté, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32707,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6LPHC85K"],"itemData":{"id":32707,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Lead levels and isotopic fingerprints in 315 free-ranging animals belonging to 18 wild game species in four remote areas of the Peruvian Amazon provide a comprehensive picture of anthropogenic lead pollution in tropical rainforests. The high average concentration of lead (0.49?mg?kg?1 wet weight) in livers from Amazonian wild game is comparable to the levels of lead in industrialized countries and mining areas. Although hunting ammunition is probably the main source of lead in wildlife, oil-related pollution is also a major source of contaminant lead in areas in which oil is extracted. Owing to the extended use of lead shot in subsistence hunting worldwide and the ever-encroaching oil-extraction industry in tropical rainforests, these results uncover important health risks to tropical wildlife and local communities that rely on subsistence hunting.","container-title":"Nature Sustainability","DOI":"10.1038/s41893-019-0338-7","ISSN":"2398-9629","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Nature Sustainability","page":"702-709","title":"Anthropogenic lead in Amazonian wildlife","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41893-019-0338-7","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Cartró-Sabaté","given":"Mar"},{"family":"Mayor","given":"Pedro"},{"family":"Orta-Martínez","given":"Martí"},{"family":"Rosell-Melé","given":"Antoni"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]],"season":"1"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, fishing  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"l9pEzEEh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Binnington, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Binnington, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":797,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TWFD8FY8"],"itemData":{"id":797,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment international","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"554–566","title":"Balancing the benefits and costs of traditional food substitution by indigenous Arctic women of childbearing age: impacts on persistent organic pollutant, mercury, and nutrient intakes","volume":"94","author":[{"family":"Binnington","given":"Matthew J"},{"family":"Curren","given":"Meredith S"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Hing Man"},{"family":"Wania","given":"Frank"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and gathering ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iB81Lv5o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Strand, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Strand, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32619,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PUX62BQ3"],"itemData":{"id":32619,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Radioactivity","DOI":"10.1016/S0265-931X(01)00093-5","ISSN":"0265931X","issue":"1-2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Environmental Radioactivity","language":"en","page":"5-21","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Radioactive contamination in the Arctic—sources, dose assessment and potential risks","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0265931X01000935","volume":"60","author":[{"family":"Strand","given":"P"},{"family":"Howard","given":"B.J"},{"family":"Aarkrog","given":"A"},{"family":"Balonov","given":"M"},{"family":"Tsaturov","given":"Y"},{"family":"Bewers","given":"J.M"},{"family":"Salo","given":"A"},{"family":"Sickel","given":"M"},{"family":"Bergman","given":"R"},{"family":"Rissanen","given":"K"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",5,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As a case in point, freshwater crabs and turtles of several rivers in the Amazon Basin, which are both culturally and nutritionally important for indigenous communities, have been impacted by widespread high levels of Hg and Pb pollution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4t0KscLQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. Schneider, 2010)","plainCitation":"(L. Schneider, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":796,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4NFTWFCQ"],"itemData":{"id":796,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Archives of environmental contamination and toxicology","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"444–450","title":"Mercury levels in muscle of six species of turtles eaten by people along the Rio Negro of the Amazon basin","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Schneider","given":"Larissa"},{"family":"Belger","given":"Lauren"},{"family":"Burger","given":"Joanna"},{"family":"Vogt","given":"Richard C"},{"family":"Ferrara","given":"Camila R"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      These pressures can generate legacies of intergenerational trauma and reduced cultural engagement, leading to declines in indigenous peoples and local communities physical and mental well-being, ultimately limiting their ability to engage in the many mutually reinforcing aspects of knowing and being ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"raDOAkQM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez-Llamazares, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Fernández-Llamazares, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":762,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GPPGE69Y"],"itemData":{"id":762,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Local attitudes towards wildlife encompass environmental, political, sociocultural and psychological dimensions that shape human-wildlife interactions and conservation efforts. Although the political and sociocultural dimensions of these interactions have been extensively examined by political ecologists and cultural anthropologists, psychological aspects have remained largely untapped so far. This article presents an in-depth review of a long historical record of changing attitudes towards wildlife among Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem in southern Kenya, examining their shifts in light of different conservation psychology theories. The historical changes are reviewed in relation to three theories of attitudinal shifts (i.e., cognitive dissonance, reactance, and motivation crowding theory) and discussed in a context of land dispossession, conservation policies and changes in Maasai lifestyles and cultural values. We conclude that conservation psychology adds an important dimension to understanding attitudes towards wildlife and how they bear on conservation policies and practices.","call-number":"Sub 10a","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2019.125763","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Historical shifts in local attitudes towards wildlife by Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem (Kenya): Insights from three conservation psychology theories","title-short":"Historical shifts in local attitudes towards wildlife by Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem (Kenya)","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1617138119302833","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Fernández-Llamazares","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Western","given":"David"},{"family":"Galvin","given":"Kathleen A."},{"family":"McElwee","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"Cabeza","given":"Mar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, numerous studies reports impacts of pollution on indigenous peoples and local communities’ mental health, including psychological disorders associated with pollution events ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9j1FQYWj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nriagu, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Nriagu, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":799,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UGSPSXEU"],"itemData":{"id":799,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","DOI":"10.3390/ijerph13030346","ISSN":"1660-4601","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"IJERPH","language":"en","page":"346","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Health Risks Associated with Oil Pollution in the Niger Delta, Nigeria","URL":"http://www.mdpi.com/1660-4601/13/3/346","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Nriagu","given":"Jerome"},{"family":"Udofia","given":"Emilia"},{"family":"Ekong","given":"Ibanga"},{"family":"Ebuk","given":"Godwin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",3,21]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Pollution can also result in fear of consuming traditional wild foods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GXaST0YW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. J. Turner & Turner, 2008)","plainCitation":"(N. J. Turner & Turner, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WJ52FNBK"],"itemData":{"id":19,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Knowledge and practices of indigenous peoples relating to local plants used for food, medicine, materials, and other purposes are threatened in many parts of the world. The reasons for declining knowledge and use of traditional resources are complex and multifaceted. We review a series of case examples of culturally valued food plants in British Columbia and identify a suite of interacting social and environmental factors that have resulted in decreased use of and dwindling cultural knowledge about these plants over the past 150 years. Reasons for this loss include compounding influences of changing knowledge systems owing to religious conversion and residential schools, loss of indigenous languages, loss of time and opportunity for traditional practices owing to participation in the wage economy, increasing urbanization of indigenous populations, loss of access to traditional resources, restriction of management practices for sustaining these resources, and most recently, forces of globalization and industrialization. Efforts to renew and restore traditional practices and relationships with plants and environments must recognize the cumulative effects of these factors and find ways to retain and reinforce the knowledge and practices still held by individuals and communities, to reverse some of the negative influences on cultural retention, and to develop new, relevant, and effective ways to revitalize languages, cultures, and ethnobotanical knowledge within contemporary contexts.","container-title":"Botany","DOI":"10.1139/B07-020","ISSN":"1916-2790, 1916-2804","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Botany","language":"en","page":"103-115","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"“Where our women used to get the food”: cumulative effects and loss of ethnobotanical knowledge and practice; case study from coastal British Columbia","title-short":"“Where our women used to get the food”","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/B07-020","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Turner","given":"Nancy J."},{"family":"Turner","given":"Katherine L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and the decline in wild species availability due to pollution can foster increased reliance on nutrient?poor and expensive market foods, often increasing the risk of malnutrition and chronic diseases ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"R51uOn1a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez-Llamazares, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Fernández-Llamazares, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":762,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GPPGE69Y"],"itemData":{"id":762,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Local attitudes towards wildlife encompass environmental, political, sociocultural and psychological dimensions that shape human-wildlife interactions and conservation efforts. Although the political and sociocultural dimensions of these interactions have been extensively examined by political ecologists and cultural anthropologists, psychological aspects have remained largely untapped so far. This article presents an in-depth review of a long historical record of changing attitudes towards wildlife among Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem in southern Kenya, examining their shifts in light of different conservation psychology theories. The historical changes are reviewed in relation to three theories of attitudinal shifts (i.e., cognitive dissonance, reactance, and motivation crowding theory) and discussed in a context of land dispossession, conservation policies and changes in Maasai lifestyles and cultural values. We conclude that conservation psychology adds an important dimension to understanding attitudes towards wildlife and how they bear on conservation policies and practices.","call-number":"Sub 10a","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2019.125763","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Historical shifts in local attitudes towards wildlife by Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem (Kenya): Insights from three conservation psychology theories","title-short":"Historical shifts in local attitudes towards wildlife by Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem (Kenya)","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1617138119302833","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Fernández-Llamazares","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Western","given":"David"},{"family":"Galvin","given":"Kathleen A."},{"family":"McElwee","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"Cabeza","given":"Mar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, some indigenous communities in British Columbia (Canada) have stopped gathering seaweeds in large amounts due to fears about marine pollution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"10TKhQtb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. J. Turner & Clifton, 2009; N. J. Turner & Turner, 2008)","plainCitation":"(N. J. Turner & Clifton, 2009; N. J. Turner & Turner, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":19,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WJ52FNBK"],"itemData":{"id":19,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Knowledge and practices of indigenous peoples relating to local plants used for food, medicine, materials, and other purposes are threatened in many parts of the world. The reasons for declining knowledge and use of traditional resources are complex and multifaceted. We review a series of case examples of culturally valued food plants in British Columbia and identify a suite of interacting social and environmental factors that have resulted in decreased use of and dwindling cultural knowledge about these plants over the past 150 years. Reasons for this loss include compounding influences of changing knowledge systems owing to religious conversion and residential schools, loss of indigenous languages, loss of time and opportunity for traditional practices owing to participation in the wage economy, increasing urbanization of indigenous populations, loss of access to traditional resources, restriction of management practices for sustaining these resources, and most recently, forces of globalization and industrialization. Efforts to renew and restore traditional practices and relationships with plants and environments must recognize the cumulative effects of these factors and find ways to retain and reinforce the knowledge and practices still held by individuals and communities, to reverse some of the negative influences on cultural retention, and to develop new, relevant, and effective ways to revitalize languages, cultures, and ethnobotanical knowledge within contemporary contexts.","container-title":"Botany","DOI":"10.1139/B07-020","ISSN":"1916-2790, 1916-2804","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Botany","language":"en","page":"103-115","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"“Where our women used to get the food”: cumulative effects and loss of ethnobotanical knowledge and practice; case study from coastal British Columbia","title-short":"“Where our women used to get the food”","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/B07-020","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Turner","given":"Nancy J."},{"family":"Turner","given":"Katherine L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",2]]}}},{"id":792,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MB44FDA4"],"itemData":{"id":792,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"180–190","title":"“It's so different today”: Climate change and indigenous lifeways in British Columbia, Canada","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Turner","given":"Nancy J"},{"family":"Clifton","given":"Helen"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Loss of hunting or fishing activities can also result in reductions in physical activity, with important health implications ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MjPENhdj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. Hoover, 2017)","plainCitation":"(E. Hoover, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":779,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JLKP5LJG"],"itemData":{"id":779,"type":"book","publisher":"U of Minnesota Press","title":"The river is in us: Fighting toxics in a Mohawk community","author":[{"family":"Hoover","given":"Elizabeth"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fears over pollution can also lead to declines in the use of traditional plant-based medicines, as documented among Native American communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7jgOdtsQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arquette, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Arquette, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QK2QF4E6"],"itemData":{"id":795,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental health perspectives","issue":"suppl 2","page":"259–264","title":"Holistic risk-based environmental decision making: a Native perspective.","volume":"110","author":[{"family":"Arquette","given":"Mary"},{"family":"Cole","given":"Maxine"},{"family":"Cook","given":"Katsi"},{"family":"LaFrance","given":"Brenda"},{"family":"Peters","given":"Margaret"},{"family":"Ransom","given":"James"},{"family":"Sargent","given":"Elvera"},{"family":"Smoke","given":"Vivian"},{"family":"Stairs","given":"Arlene"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Environmental pollution impacts both material and nonmaterial cultural dimensions of indigenous peoples and local communities’ ways of life, including their knowledge systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wzByknay","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pufall, 2011; Yakovleva, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Pufall, 2011; Yakovleva, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":791,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CJXILYQU"],"itemData":{"id":791,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Arctic","note":"publisher: JSTOR","page":"242–250","title":"Perception of the importance of traditional country foods to the physical, mental, and spiritual health of Labrador Inuit","author":[{"family":"Pufall","given":"Erica L"},{"family":"Jones","given":"Andria Q"},{"family":"McEwen","given":"Scott A"},{"family":"Lyall","given":"Charlene"},{"family":"Peregrine","given":"Andrew S"},{"family":"Edge","given":"Victoria L"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":790,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/67SB8QYN"],"itemData":{"id":790,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Geoforum","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"708–719","title":"Oil pipeline construction in Eastern Siberia: Implications for indigenous people","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Yakovleva","given":"Natalia"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, herbicide treatments have contaminated plants used by California Native American communities for different cultural uses, such as traditional basket weaving ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VpGBdsQb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Neill, 2003)","plainCitation":"(O’Neill, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":794,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LA3I8H9P"],"itemData":{"id":794,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology LQ","note":"publisher: HeinOnline","page":"1","title":"Risk avoidance, cultural discrimination, and environmental justice for indigenous peoples","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"O'Neill","given":"Catherine A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other traditional practices, such as harvesting local plants for sustenance, ceremonial, or medicinal purposes, can also increase exposure to pollutants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tpV6TJv2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arquette, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Arquette, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QK2QF4E6"],"itemData":{"id":795,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental health perspectives","issue":"suppl 2","page":"259–264","title":"Holistic risk-based environmental decision making: a Native perspective.","volume":"110","author":[{"family":"Arquette","given":"Mary"},{"family":"Cole","given":"Maxine"},{"family":"Cook","given":"Katsi"},{"family":"LaFrance","given":"Brenda"},{"family":"Peters","given":"Margaret"},{"family":"Ransom","given":"James"},{"family":"Sargent","given":"Elvera"},{"family":"Smoke","given":"Vivian"},{"family":"Stairs","given":"Arlene"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thus, recommendations to refrain from fishing or gathering plants can affect indigenous peoples and local communities’ cultural traditions based on these activities. And undermine the knowledge systems that underpin such practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QMJ7SJ6q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez-Llamazares, 2020; Kuhnlein & Chan, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Fernández-Llamazares, 2020; Kuhnlein & Chan, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":762,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GPPGE69Y"],"itemData":{"id":762,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Local attitudes towards wildlife encompass environmental, political, sociocultural and psychological dimensions that shape human-wildlife interactions and conservation efforts. Although the political and sociocultural dimensions of these interactions have been extensively examined by political ecologists and cultural anthropologists, psychological aspects have remained largely untapped so far. This article presents an in-depth review of a long historical record of changing attitudes towards wildlife among Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem in southern Kenya, examining their shifts in light of different conservation psychology theories. The historical changes are reviewed in relation to three theories of attitudinal shifts (i.e., cognitive dissonance, reactance, and motivation crowding theory) and discussed in a context of land dispossession, conservation policies and changes in Maasai lifestyles and cultural values. We conclude that conservation psychology adds an important dimension to understanding attitudes towards wildlife and how they bear on conservation policies and practices.","call-number":"Sub 10a","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2019.125763","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Historical shifts in local attitudes towards wildlife by Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem (Kenya): Insights from three conservation psychology theories","title-short":"Historical shifts in local attitudes towards wildlife by Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem (Kenya)","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1617138119302833","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Fernández-Llamazares","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Western","given":"David"},{"family":"Galvin","given":"Kathleen A."},{"family":"McElwee","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"Cabeza","given":"Mar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":789,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KNTSBGB4"],"itemData":{"id":789,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual review of nutrition","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Annual Reviews 4139 El Camino Way, PO Box 10139, Palo Alto, CA 94303-0139, USA","page":"595–626","title":"Environment and contaminants in traditional food systems of northern indigenous peoples","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Kuhnlein","given":"Harriet V"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Hing Man"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, some studies have documented how pollution risks can affect the spiritual wellbeing of indigenous peoples and local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"e1IwjjXD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(LaDuke, 1999; McCreary & Milligan, 2014; Temper & Martinez-Alier, 2013)","plainCitation":"(LaDuke, 1999; McCreary & Milligan, 2014; Temper & Martinez-Alier, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":793,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KRFIPDAE"],"itemData":{"id":793,"type":"book","publisher":"South End Press","title":"All our relations: Native struggles for land and life","author":[{"family":"LaDuke","given":"Winona"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":32618,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZER7ZAES"],"itemData":{"id":32618,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"cultural geographies","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Sage Publications Sage UK: London, England","page":"115–129","title":"Pipelines, permits, and protests: Carrier Sekani encounters with the Enbridge Northern Gateway project","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"McCreary","given":"Tyler A"},{"family":"Milligan","given":"Richard A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":32615,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AEP6AZCB"],"itemData":{"id":32615,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Economics","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"79–87","title":"The god of the mountain and Godavarman: Net Present Value, indigenous territorial rights and sacredness in a bauxite mining conflict in India","volume":"96","author":[{"family":"Temper","given":"Leah"},{"family":"Martinez-Alier","given":"Joan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Environmental pollution jeopardizes the complex and intimate relations that many indigenous peoples and local communities establish with their lands and waters ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QengpQG5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez-Llamazares, 2020; Hoogeveen, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Fernández-Llamazares, 2020; Hoogeveen, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":762,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GPPGE69Y"],"itemData":{"id":762,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Local attitudes towards wildlife encompass environmental, political, sociocultural and psychological dimensions that shape human-wildlife interactions and conservation efforts. Although the political and sociocultural dimensions of these interactions have been extensively examined by political ecologists and cultural anthropologists, psychological aspects have remained largely untapped so far. This article presents an in-depth review of a long historical record of changing attitudes towards wildlife among Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem in southern Kenya, examining their shifts in light of different conservation psychology theories. The historical changes are reviewed in relation to three theories of attitudinal shifts (i.e., cognitive dissonance, reactance, and motivation crowding theory) and discussed in a context of land dispossession, conservation policies and changes in Maasai lifestyles and cultural values. We conclude that conservation psychology adds an important dimension to understanding attitudes towards wildlife and how they bear on conservation policies and practices.","call-number":"Sub 10a","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2019.125763","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Historical shifts in local attitudes towards wildlife by Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem (Kenya): Insights from three conservation psychology theories","title-short":"Historical shifts in local attitudes towards wildlife by Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem (Kenya)","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1617138119302833","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Fernández-Llamazares","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Western","given":"David"},{"family":"Galvin","given":"Kathleen A."},{"family":"McElwee","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"Cabeza","given":"Mar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":800,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RSLY8RNN"],"itemData":{"id":800,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and Planning D: Society and Space","issue":"2","note":"publisher: SAGE Publications Sage UK: London, England","page":"355–370","title":"Fish-hood: Environmental assessment, critical Indigenous studies, and posthumanism at Fish Lake (Teztan Biny), Tsilhqot’in territory","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Hoogeveen","given":"Dawn"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, thereby affecting prospects for the continuance of indigenous peoples and local communities’ practices of sustainable resource use. Because activities associated with gathering wild foods generally serve important community roles (e.g., intergenerational exchange, maintenance of language), concerns associated with pollution regarding the sustainable use of biodiversity can also impact the continuance of these subsistence-based practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rx3nh8Zk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes & Farkas, 1978; E. Hoover, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Berkes & Farkas, 1978; E. Hoover, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32614,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IMC9GQWC"],"itemData":{"id":32614,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology of Food and Nutrition","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"155–172","title":"Eastern James Bay Cree Indians: changing patterns of wild food use and nutrition","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Farkas","given":"Carol S"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1978"]]}}},{"id":779,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JLKP5LJG"],"itemData":{"id":779,"type":"book","publisher":"U of Minnesota Press","title":"The river is in us: Fighting toxics in a Mohawk community","author":[{"family":"Hoover","given":"Elizabeth"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In response to this, indigenous peoples and local communities are actively contributing to develop innovative strategies to limit the spread of pollution and prevent it from the outset (see ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"h9lnufWH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fern\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}ndez-Llamazares, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Fernández-Llamazares, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":762,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GPPGE69Y"],"itemData":{"id":762,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Local attitudes towards wildlife encompass environmental, political, sociocultural and psychological dimensions that shape human-wildlife interactions and conservation efforts. Although the political and sociocultural dimensions of these interactions have been extensively examined by political ecologists and cultural anthropologists, psychological aspects have remained largely untapped so far. This article presents an in-depth review of a long historical record of changing attitudes towards wildlife among Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem in southern Kenya, examining their shifts in light of different conservation psychology theories. The historical changes are reviewed in relation to three theories of attitudinal shifts (i.e., cognitive dissonance, reactance, and motivation crowding theory) and discussed in a context of land dispossession, conservation policies and changes in Maasai lifestyles and cultural values. We conclude that conservation psychology adds an important dimension to understanding attitudes towards wildlife and how they bear on conservation policies and practices.","call-number":"Sub 10a","container-title":"Journal for Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.jnc.2019.125763","ISSN":"16171381","journalAbbreviation":"Journal for Nature Conservation","language":"en","page":"11","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Historical shifts in local attitudes towards wildlife by Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem (Kenya): Insights from three conservation psychology theories","title-short":"Historical shifts in local attitudes towards wildlife by Maasai pastoralists of the Amboseli Ecosystem (Kenya)","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1617138119302833","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Fernández-Llamazares","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Western","given":"David"},{"family":"Galvin","given":"Kathleen A."},{"family":"McElwee","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"Cabeza","given":"Mar"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} for a review). For example, indigenous peoples and local communities are increasingly leading community-based pollution monitoring programs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MyJtKE46","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Herrmann, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Herrmann, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32617,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NRBJCGBA"],"itemData":{"id":32617,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Polar Journal","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"28–51","title":"Effects of mining on reindeer/caribou populations and indigenous livelihoods: community-based monitoring by Sami reindeer herders in Sweden and First Nations in Canada","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Herrmann","given":"Thora Martina"},{"family":"Sandström","given":"Per"},{"family":"Granqvist","given":"Karin"},{"family":"D’Astous","given":"Natalie"},{"family":"Vannar","given":"Jonas"},{"family":"Asselin","given":"Hugo"},{"family":"Saganash","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Mameamskum","given":"John"},{"family":"Guanish","given":"George"},{"family":"Loon","given":"Jean-Baptiste"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} , engaging in international policy development to reduce pollution burdens ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gDjBvEQL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Basu, 2018; Selin & Selin, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Basu, 2018; Selin & Selin, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":787,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V67QFNFK"],"itemData":{"id":787,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental health perspectives","issue":"10","page":"106001","title":"A state-of-the-science review of mercury biomarkers in human populations worldwide between 2000 and 2018","volume":"126","author":[{"family":"Basu","given":"Niladri"},{"family":"Horvat","given":"Milena"},{"family":"Evers","given":"David C"},{"family":"Zastenskaya","given":"Irina"},{"family":"Weihe","given":"Pál"},{"family":"Tempowski","given":"Joanna"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32613,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7ZSDB6NI"],"itemData":{"id":32613,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Review of European Community & International Environmental Law","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"72–83","title":"Indigenous peoples in international environmental cooperation: Arctic management of hazardous substances","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Selin","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Selin","given":"Noelle Eckley"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and articulating different forms of grassroots resistance to polluting activities on indigenous peoples and local communities’ lands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YG8MLJqj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Armstrong & Brown, 2019; Scheidel, 2020; Spice, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Armstrong & Brown, 2019; Scheidel, 2020; Spice, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":786,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/38VKX7NN"],"itemData":{"id":786,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","issue":"1","note":"publisher: BioOne","page":"14–31","title":"Frontiers are frontlines: Ethnobiological science against ongoing colonialism","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Armstrong","given":"Chelsey Geralda"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Christie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32662,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GR658T66"],"itemData":{"id":32662,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"102104","title":"Environmental Conflicts and Defenders: A Global Overview","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Scheidel","given":"Arnim"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":780,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EDZZVWNY"],"itemData":{"id":780,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environment and Society","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Berghahn Journals","page":"40–56","title":"Fighting invasive infrastructures: Indigenous relations against pipelines","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Spice","given":"Anne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
4.2.4 Economic drivers 
4.2.4.1 Overview 
Economic forces are considered among the most critical in addressing rapid declines in biodiversity including the use of wild species; economic systems directly impact species but also shape perceptions and norms about the importance of particular species and their value within society ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BKbeuPPh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Diaz, 2015)","plainCitation":"(S. Diaz, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35075,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PKF4C362"],"itemData":{"id":35075,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The first public product of the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES) is its Conceptual Framework. This conceptual and analytical tool, presented here in detail, will underpin all IPBES functions and provide structure and comparability to the syntheses that IPBES will produce at different spatial scales, on different themes, and in different regions. Salient innovative aspects of the IPBES Conceptual Framework are its transparent and participatory construction process and its explicit consideration of diverse scientific disciplines, stakeholders, and knowledge systems, including indigenous and local knowledge. Because the focus on co-construction of integrative knowledge is shared by an increasing number of initiatives worldwide, this framework should be useful beyond IPBES, for the wider research and knowledge-policy communities working on the links between nature and people, such as natural, social and engineering scientists, policy-makers at different levels, and decision-makers in different sectors of society.","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2014.11.002","ISSN":"18773435","note":"PMID: 25585296","page":"1–16","title":"The IPBES Conceptual Framework - connecting nature and people","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Demissew","given":"Sebsebe"},{"family":"Carabias","given":"Julia"},{"family":"Joly","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Lonsdale","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Ash","given":"Neville"},{"family":"Larigauderie","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Adhikari","given":"Jay Ram"},{"family":"Arico","given":"Salvatore"},{"family":"Bartuska","given":"Ann"},{"family":"Baste","given":"Ivar Andreas"},{"family":"Bilgin","given":"Adem"},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai M A"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Viviana Elsa"},{"family":"Duraiappah","given":"Anantha"},{"family":"Fischer","given":"Markus"},{"family":"Hill","given":"Rosemary"},{"family":"Koetz","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Leadley","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Lyver","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Mace","given":"Georgina M."},{"family":"Martin-Lopez","given":"Berta"},{"family":"Okumura","given":"Michiko"},{"family":"Pacheco","given":"Diego"},{"family":"Pascual","given":"Unai"},{"family":"Reyers","given":"Belinda"},{"family":"Roth","given":"Eva"},{"family":"Saito","given":"Osamu"},{"family":"Scholes","given":"Robert John"},{"family":"Sharma","given":"Nalini"},{"family":"Tallis","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Thaman","given":"Randolph"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Yahara","given":"Tetsukazu"},{"family":"Hamid","given":"Zakri Abdul"},{"family":"Akosim","given":"Callistus"},{"family":"Al-Hafedh","given":"Yousef"},{"family":"Allahverdiyev","given":"Rashad"},{"family":"Amankwah","given":"Edward"},{"family":"Asah","given":"T. Stanley"},{"family":"Asfaw","given":"Zemede"},{"family":"Bartus","given":"Gabor"},{"family":"Brooks","given":"Anathea L."},{"family":"Caillaux","given":"Jorge"},{"family":"Dalle","given":"Gemedo"},{"family":"Darnaedi","given":"Dedy"},{"family":"Driver","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Erpul","given":"Gunay"},{"family":"Escobar-Eyzaguirre","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Failler","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Fouda","given":"Ali Moustafa Mokhtar"},{"family":"Fu","given":"Bojie"},{"family":"Gundimeda","given":"Haripriya"},{"family":"Hashimoto","given":"Shizuka"},{"family":"Homer","given":"Floyd"},{"family":"Lavorel","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Mala","given":"William Armand"},{"family":"Mandivenyi","given":"Wadzanayi"},{"family":"Matczak","given":"Piotr"},{"family":"Mbizvo","given":"Carmel"},{"family":"Mehrdadi","given":"Mehrasa"},{"family":"Metzger","given":"Jean Paul"},{"family":"Mikissa","given":"Jean Bruno"},{"family":"Moller","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Mooney","given":"Harold A."},{"family":"Mumby","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"Harini"},{"family":"Nesshover","given":"Carsten"},{"family":"Oteng-Yeboah","given":"Alfred Apau"},{"family":"Rubis","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Schultz","given":"Maria"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Peggy"},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"Rashid"},{"family":"Takeuchi","given":"Kazuhiko"},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Spencer"},{"family":"Verma","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Yeo-Chang","given":"Youn"},{"family":"Zlatanova","given":"Diana"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Direct drivers (e.g., a rise in export prices) are of major importance, however, indirect drivers and mediating factors (e.g. access to markets) can also have a great impact on long term sustainability. Typically, economic drivers and mediating factors jointly determine sustainability outcomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1Rit4jtn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mirza, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Mirza, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32520,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G8CBL35N"],"itemData":{"id":32520,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Mirza, M. U., A. Richter, E. H. van Nes, and M. Scheffer. 2020. Institutions and inequality interplay shapes the impact of economic growth on biodiversity loss. Ecology and Society 25(4):39. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-12078-250439","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-12078-250439","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"© 2020 by the author(s)","note":"publisher: The Resilience Alliance","source":"www.ecologyandsociety.org","title":"Institutions and inequality interplay shapes the impact of economic growth on biodiversity loss","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol25/iss4/art39/","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Mirza","given":"M. Usman"},{"family":"Richter","given":"Andries"},{"family":"Nes","given":"Egbert","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"Marten"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,21]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Key element of economic analysis are factors affecting the supply of commodities related to wild species (e.g. lack of alternative employment opportunities) as well as demand (e.g. urbanization). Together, this facilitates trade, i.e., an exchange between buyers and sellers. The interactions between economic incentives, institutions and governance structures, in relation to wider ecological, cultural and social drivers and mediating factors are critical to understand sustainable use of wild species.  

4.2.4.2 Methods, limitations, and gaps of knowledge
The content of this section is based on evidence from research in numerous disciplines including, resource economics, agricultural economics, geography, sociology, ecology and conservation biology. The subsections were developed with a systematic literature review, complemented by grey and peer-reviewed literature based on input of the experts. There are some notable challenges in the availability of data. There is growing availability of economic data on the performance of industries relying on wild species in certain regions of the world (e.g. reports by the ‘Scientific, Technical and Economic Committee for Fisheries’ in the European Union), though many regions of the world lack even basic statistics on the economic situation of harvesters. In such case, official trade statistics may offer insights on scale of exploitation, though trade data insufficiently tracks the status of threatened species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MXNWzcOB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Phelps, 2016; Phelps & Webb, 2015c)","plainCitation":"(Phelps, 2016; Phelps & Webb, 2015c)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7542,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q2S6CFPQ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F8Y4UHLI"],"itemData":{"id":7542,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","DOI":"10.1002/fee.1325","ISSN":"15409295","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Front Ecol Environ","language":"en","page":"479-489","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Tools and terms for understanding illegal wildlife trade","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/fee.1325","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"},{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Webb","given":"Edward L"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",11]]}}},{"id":32526,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5BUYUBQK"],"itemData":{"id":32526,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Conservation and environmental management often rely heavily on official statistics, but there are often concerns over accuracy and reliability, particularly true when dealing with sensitive issues such as illegal harvest and trade. A growing number of cases highlight commercial trades in wild flora and fauna that have been undocumented in official data. Here we present the first in-depth study of the trade of wild-collected ornamental plants in continental Southeast Asia, focusing on the four largest wildlife markets in Thailand. Botanical surveys revealed a massive, previously undocumented commercial trade in wild, protected ornamental plants involving Thailand, Lao PDR and Myanmar, focusing primarily on the Orchidaceae. The results indicate that illegal trade threatens not only charismatic Southeast Asian mammals, reptiles and amphibians, but also hundreds of tropical plant species. Trade surveys identified 347 orchid species in 93 genera, including many listed as threatened. The observed cross-border trade moves plants at a rate orders of magnitude larger than government-reported statistics, and directly conflicts with official policy statement. Harvester interviews strongly indicated that wild collection was negatively affecting the majority of species they traded. Despite three decades of broad restrictions on the international trade of all wild orchids, these results highlight a major conservation challenge that has been almost completely overlooked. There is urgent need to improve botanical trade monitoring, to operationalize existing conservation commitments, and as part of a broader, multifaceted response to illegal trade. We call for increased attention to botanical trade and conservation in Southeast Asia, and argue that efforts to tackle illegal wildlife trade must ensure they include flora.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2015.03.030","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","page":"296-305","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"“Invisible” wildlife trades: Southeast Asia’s undocumented illegal trade in wild ornamental plants","title-short":"“Invisible” wildlife trades","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S000632071500141X","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Phelps","given":"J."},{"family":"Webb","given":"Edward L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",6,1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and there are huge discrepancies in official statistics and trade surveys for other species (e.g. ornamental plants in South East Asia). Also, sustainability outcomes cannot be assessed from static data, but require repeated observations, which are rarely available ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xeVPv5Sg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Allebone\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Webb, 2011; Coad, 2013; G. Taylor, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Allebone?Webb, 2011; Coad, 2013; G. Taylor, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32712,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XF8EQYQB"],"itemData":{"id":32712,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable hunting threatens both biodiversity and local livelihoods. Despite high levels of research effort focused on understanding the dynamics of bushmeat trade and consumption, current research is largely site speci?c. Without synthesis and quantitative analysis of available case studies, the national and regional characteristics of bushmeat trade and consumption remain largely speculative, impeding efforts to inform national and regional policy on bushmeat trade. Here we describe the structure and content of the West and Central African bushmeat database which holds quantitative data on bushmeat sales, consumption and offtake for 177 species from 275 sites across 11 countries in two regions, spanning three decades of research. Despite this wealth of available data, we found important biases in research effort. The majority of studies in West and Central Africa have collected market data, which although providing a useful record of bushmeat sales, are limited in their ability to track changes in hunting offtake. In addition, few data exist for West Africa, and few studies have tracked changes over time, using repeat sampling. With new initiatives in the regions to track bushmeat hunting, this database represents an opportunity to synthesise current and future data on bushmeat hunting, consumption and trade in West and Central Africa, identify gaps in current understanding, and systematically target future monitoring efforts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2014.11.001","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"199-205","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesising bushmeat research effort in West and Central Africa: A new regional database","title-short":"Synthesising bushmeat research effort in West and Central Africa","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320714004182","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Taylor","given":"G."},{"family":"Scharlemann","given":"J.P.W."},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"M."},{"family":"Kümpel","given":"N."},{"family":"Harfoot","given":"M.B.J."},{"family":"Fa","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Melisch","given":"R."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."},{"family":"Bhagwat","given":"S."},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Ajonina","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Albrechtsen","given":"L."},{"family":"Allebone-Webb","given":"S."},{"family":"Brown","given":"E."},{"family":"Brugiere","given":"D."},{"family":"Clark","given":"C."},{"family":"Colell","given":"M."},{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"G."},{"family":"Crookes","given":"D."},{"family":"De Merode","given":"E."},{"family":"Dupain","given":"J."},{"family":"East","given":"T."},{"family":"Edderai","given":"D."},{"family":"Elkan","given":"P."},{"family":"Gill","given":"D."},{"family":"Greengrass","given":"E."},{"family":"Hodgkinson","given":"C."},{"family":"Ilambu","given":"O."},{"family":"Jeanmart","given":"P."},{"family":"Juste","given":"J."},{"family":"Linder","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"D.W."},{"family":"Noss","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Okorie","given":"P.U."},{"family":"Okouyi","given":"V.J.J."},{"family":"Pailler","given":"S."},{"family":"Poulsen","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Riddell","given":"M."},{"family":"Schleicher","given":"J."},{"family":"Schulte-Herbrüggen","given":"B."},{"family":"Starkey","given":"M."},{"family":"Vliet","given":"N.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Whitham","given":"C."},{"family":"Willcox","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Wright","given":"J.H."},{"family":"Coad","given":"L.M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1]]}}},{"id":32513,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5KN3ZRZC"],"itemData":{"id":32513,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Despite widespread recognition of the major threat to tropical forest biological diversity and local food security posed by unsustainable bushmeat hunting, virtually no long-term studies tracking the socioecological dynamics of hunting systems have been conducted. We interviewed local hunters and collected detailed hunting data to investigate changes in offtake and hunter characteristics over 10 years (2001–2010) in Dibouka and Kouagna villages, central Gabon, in the context of hunter recollections of longer term trends since the 1950s. To control for changes in hunter behavior, such as trap location and characteristics, we report hunting offtake data per trap. Our results suggest the hunting area was already highly depleted by 2001; local hunters reported that 16 large-bodied prey species had become rare or locally extirpated over the last 60 years. Overall, we observed no significant declines in hunting offtake or changes in species composition from 2001 to 2010, and offtakes per trap increased slightly between 2004 and 2010. However, trapping distance from the villages increased, and there was a switch in hunting techniques; a larger proportion of the catch was hunted with guns in 2010. The number of hunters declined by 20% from 2004 to 2010, and male livelihood activities shifted away from hunting. Hunters with the lowest hunting incomes in 2004 were more likely than successful hunters to have moved away from the village by 2010 (often in response to alternative employment opportunities). Therefore, changes in trap success (potentially related to biological factors) were interacting with system-level changes in hunter number and composition (related to external socioeconomic factors) to produce a relatively static overall offtake. Our results highlight the importance of understanding the small-scale context of hunting to correctly interpret changes or apparent stasis in hunting effort and offtake over time. Cambio Social y Ecológico a lo Largo de Una Década en un Sistema de Cacería Aldeana, Gabón Central","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12012","ISSN":"1523-1739","issue":"2","language":"en","license":"© 2013 Society for Conservation Biology","page":"270-280","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Social and Ecological Change over a Decade in a Village Hunting System, Central Gabon","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/cobi.12012","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Coad","given":"L."},{"family":"Schleicher","given":"J."},{"family":"Milner?Gulland","given":"E. J."},{"family":"Marthews","given":"T. R."},{"family":"Starkey","given":"M."},{"family":"Manica","given":"A."},{"family":"Balmford","given":"A."},{"family":"Mbombe","given":"W."},{"family":"Bineni","given":"T. R. Diop"},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"K. A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":763,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EU62X7TT"],"itemData":{"id":763,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract: Finding an adequate measure of hunting sustainability for tropical forests has proved difficult. Many researchers have used urban bushmeat market surveys as indicators of hunting volumes and composition, but no analysis has been done of the reliability of market data in reflecting village offtake. We used data from urban markets and the villages that supply these markets to examine changes in the volume and composition of traded bushmeat between the village and the market (trade filters) in Equatorial Guinea. We collected data with market surveys and hunter offtake diaries. The trade filters varied depending on village remoteness and the monopoly power of traders. In a village with limited market access, species that maximized trader profits were most likely to be traded. In a village with greater market access, species for which hunters gained the greatest income per carcass were more likely to be traded. The probability of particular species being sold to market also depended on the capture method and season. Larger, more vulnerable species were more likely to be supplied from less-accessible catchments, whereas there was no effect of forest cover or human population density on probability of being sold. This suggests that the composition of bushmeat offtake in an area may be driven more by urban demand than the geographic characteristics of that area. In one market, traders may have reached the limit of their geographical exploitation range, and hunting pressure within that range may be increasing. Our results demonstrate that it is possible to model the trade filters that bias market data, which opens the way to developing more robust market-based sustainability indices for the bushmeat trade.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2011.01681.x","ISSN":"1523-1739","issue":"3","language":"en","license":"©2011 Society for Conservation Biology","note":"_eprint: https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2011.01681.x","page":"597-606","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Use of Market Data to Assess Bushmeat Hunting Sustainability in Equatorial Guinea","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2011.01681.x","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Allebone?Webb","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Kümpel","given":"N. F."},{"family":"Rist","given":"J."},{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"G."},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"J. M."},{"family":"Milner?Gulland","given":"E. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This limits the quality and quantity of evidence regarding sustainability outcomes.
      Key limitations in the literature are incoherent definitions of what sustainable use entails and also lack of objective measurements against some form of baseline. For example, a literature review on the trade in medicinal plants in Central Himalayan reveals that though there is often a degraded resource base, the empirical basis for inferring sustainability outcomes is rather weak ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ST16iCfT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Larsen & Olsen, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Larsen & Olsen, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32525,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4BELIV9F"],"itemData":{"id":32525,"type":"chapter","abstract":"The trade in medicinal plants for herbal remedies is large and probably increasing. The trade has attracted the attention of scientists and development planners interested in the impact on plant populations and the potential to improve rural livelihoods through community based management and conservation. This has resulted in a large number of publications and development activities, ranging from small NGO projects to new government policies. Through a review of 119 references from Nepal, 4 common assumptions regarding the medicinal plant collection and trade have been identified: I. The commercial medicinal plant resource base is becoming ever more degraded as a consequence of collection; II. The medicinal plants are an open-access resource; III. Cultivation can contribute to conservation of commercially collected medicinal plant species; and IV. Medicinal plant harvesters are cheated by middlemen. The frequency of the assumptions is documented, their empirical support is evaluated, and the consequences of their presence for conservation and rural livelihoods are discussed. It is concluded that the empirical backing for the assumptions is weak, and that some reviewed references use logically flawed argumentation. It is argued that the assumptions are leading to misguided conservation efforts, and an inclusive approach to conservation of commercial central Himalayan medicinal plant species is briefly outlined.","container-title":"Plant Conservation and Biodiversity","event-place":"Dordrecht","ISBN":"978-1-4020-6444-9","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-1-4020-6444-9_8","page":"105-123","publisher":"Springer Netherlands","publisher-place":"Dordrecht","source":"Springer Link","title":"Unsustainable collection and unfair trade? Uncovering and assessing assumptions regarding Central Himalayan medicinal plant conservation","title-short":"Unsustainable collection and unfair trade?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4020-6444-9_8","author":[{"family":"Larsen","given":"Helle Overgaard"},{"family":"Olsen","given":"Carsten Smith"}],"editor":[{"family":"Hawksworth","given":"David L."},{"family":"Bull","given":"Alan T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The same is true for economic sustainability. Often, the role of middlemen is perceived to be important, but there is little quantitative evidence on which economic outcomes would be deemed fair or sustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AK6yce4f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Larsen & Olsen, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Larsen & Olsen, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32525,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4BELIV9F"],"itemData":{"id":32525,"type":"chapter","abstract":"The trade in medicinal plants for herbal remedies is large and probably increasing. The trade has attracted the attention of scientists and development planners interested in the impact on plant populations and the potential to improve rural livelihoods through community based management and conservation. This has resulted in a large number of publications and development activities, ranging from small NGO projects to new government policies. Through a review of 119 references from Nepal, 4 common assumptions regarding the medicinal plant collection and trade have been identified: I. The commercial medicinal plant resource base is becoming ever more degraded as a consequence of collection; II. The medicinal plants are an open-access resource; III. Cultivation can contribute to conservation of commercially collected medicinal plant species; and IV. Medicinal plant harvesters are cheated by middlemen. The frequency of the assumptions is documented, their empirical support is evaluated, and the consequences of their presence for conservation and rural livelihoods are discussed. It is concluded that the empirical backing for the assumptions is weak, and that some reviewed references use logically flawed argumentation. It is argued that the assumptions are leading to misguided conservation efforts, and an inclusive approach to conservation of commercial central Himalayan medicinal plant species is briefly outlined.","container-title":"Plant Conservation and Biodiversity","event-place":"Dordrecht","ISBN":"978-1-4020-6444-9","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-1-4020-6444-9_8","page":"105-123","publisher":"Springer Netherlands","publisher-place":"Dordrecht","source":"Springer Link","title":"Unsustainable collection and unfair trade? Uncovering and assessing assumptions regarding Central Himalayan medicinal plant conservation","title-short":"Unsustainable collection and unfair trade?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4020-6444-9_8","author":[{"family":"Larsen","given":"Helle Overgaard"},{"family":"Olsen","given":"Carsten Smith"}],"editor":[{"family":"Hawksworth","given":"David L."},{"family":"Bull","given":"Alan T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Formulating guidelines for assessing sustainability would be an important step to make cases comparable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"y2zKxHVD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cuesta & Becerra, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Cuesta & Becerra, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32522,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YDQNG3Q7"],"itemData":{"id":32522,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Cuesta, Francisco; Becerra, María Teresa","language":"en","note":"publisher: UN,","source":"digitallibrary.un.org","title":"Guidelines for the sustainable management of BioTrade products :","title-short":"Guidelines for the sustainable management of BioTrade products","URL":"http://digitallibrary.un.org/record/757340","author":[{"family":"Cuesta","given":"Francisco"},{"family":"Becerra","given":"María Teresa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another limitation is a ‘survivorship bias’ meaning that field studies tend to study what is there (and not what is lost and forgotten). Analysis from a bushmeat market in West Africa (Takoradi, Ghana) finds little evidence for overharvesting and unsustainable uses, potentially because the most vulnerable species have already disappeared in the past due to prolonged hunting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OqiKpac7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cowlishaw, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Cowlishaw, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"Uebb2sCb/k2I1mKxq","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P6L39Y2E"],"itemData":{"id":1942,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2664.2005.01046.x","ISSN":"00218901","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"460-468","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Evidence for post-depletion sustainability in a mature bushmeat market: Sustainability of bushmeat markets","title-short":"Evidence for post-depletion sustainability in a mature bushmeat market","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Mendelson","given":"Samantha"},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"J. Marcus"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",6,9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Quantitative data that is comparable across countries is often unavailable for subsistence and indigenous economies, particularly in relation to economic impacts and drivers of cultural, spiritual and social uses of wild species. There are also significant gaps in the availability of documented indigenous and local knowledge related to economic drivers at all scales and in relation to all species. This is partially due to remote government officials being mistrusted by indigeneous communities, and also due to to asymmetric power, diffirent worldview and lack of investment on part of the scientists to have a meaningful collaboration and use of indigenous local ecological knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0b8KlUVG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. S. Brond\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}zio, 2021)","plainCitation":"(E. S. Brondízio, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32706,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KFYELQZ8"],"itemData":{"id":32706,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","note":"publisher: Annual Reviews","page":"481–509","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Locally Based, Regionally Manifested, and Globally Relevant: Indigenous and Local Knowledge, Values, and Practices for Nature","title-short":"Locally Based, Regionally Manifested, and Globally Relevant","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Brondízio","given":"Eduardo S."},{"family":"Aumeeruddy-Thomas","given":"Yildiz"},{"family":"Bates","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Carino","given":"Joji"},{"family":"Fernández-Llamazares","given":"Álvaro"},{"family":"Ferrari","given":"Maurizio Farhan"},{"family":"Galvin","given":"Kathleen"},{"family":"Reyes-García","given":"Victoria"},{"family":"McElwee","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"Molnar","given":"Zsolt"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Despite this gap, indigenous and local knowledge perspectives should be considered critical to our understanding of sustainable use of wild species, particularly as it relates to their social, cultural and spiritual importance to indigenous peoples. Some issues of sustainable use for some countries are also more studied than others. For example, species considered under Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, particularly related to vertebrates have been a greater focus than other species including plants and invertebrates. There are also gaps in our understanding of the economic drivers of sustainable use issues in some regions. As a result, the data available is uneven and recognition should be given to gaps that require further study and assessment. For example, Taylor ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"obDdTX6f","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2015)","plainCitation":"(2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32712,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XF8EQYQB"],"itemData":{"id":32712,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable hunting threatens both biodiversity and local livelihoods. Despite high levels of research effort focused on understanding the dynamics of bushmeat trade and consumption, current research is largely site speci?c. Without synthesis and quantitative analysis of available case studies, the national and regional characteristics of bushmeat trade and consumption remain largely speculative, impeding efforts to inform national and regional policy on bushmeat trade. Here we describe the structure and content of the West and Central African bushmeat database which holds quantitative data on bushmeat sales, consumption and offtake for 177 species from 275 sites across 11 countries in two regions, spanning three decades of research. Despite this wealth of available data, we found important biases in research effort. The majority of studies in West and Central Africa have collected market data, which although providing a useful record of bushmeat sales, are limited in their ability to track changes in hunting offtake. In addition, few data exist for West Africa, and few studies have tracked changes over time, using repeat sampling. With new initiatives in the regions to track bushmeat hunting, this database represents an opportunity to synthesise current and future data on bushmeat hunting, consumption and trade in West and Central Africa, identify gaps in current understanding, and systematically target future monitoring efforts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2014.11.001","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"199-205","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesising bushmeat research effort in West and Central Africa: A new regional database","title-short":"Synthesising bushmeat research effort in West and Central Africa","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320714004182","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Taylor","given":"G."},{"family":"Scharlemann","given":"J.P.W."},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"M."},{"family":"Kümpel","given":"N."},{"family":"Harfoot","given":"M.B.J."},{"family":"Fa","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Melisch","given":"R."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."},{"family":"Bhagwat","given":"S."},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Ajonina","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Albrechtsen","given":"L."},{"family":"Allebone-Webb","given":"S."},{"family":"Brown","given":"E."},{"family":"Brugiere","given":"D."},{"family":"Clark","given":"C."},{"family":"Colell","given":"M."},{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"G."},{"family":"Crookes","given":"D."},{"family":"De Merode","given":"E."},{"family":"Dupain","given":"J."},{"family":"East","given":"T."},{"family":"Edderai","given":"D."},{"family":"Elkan","given":"P."},{"family":"Gill","given":"D."},{"family":"Greengrass","given":"E."},{"family":"Hodgkinson","given":"C."},{"family":"Ilambu","given":"O."},{"family":"Jeanmart","given":"P."},{"family":"Juste","given":"J."},{"family":"Linder","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"D.W."},{"family":"Noss","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Okorie","given":"P.U."},{"family":"Okouyi","given":"V.J.J."},{"family":"Pailler","given":"S."},{"family":"Poulsen","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Riddell","given":"M."},{"family":"Schleicher","given":"J."},{"family":"Schulte-Herbrüggen","given":"B."},{"family":"Starkey","given":"M."},{"family":"Vliet","given":"N.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Whitham","given":"C."},{"family":"Willcox","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Wright","given":"J.H."},{"family":"Coad","given":"L.M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} document quantity of evidence on the impacts of wild species hunting with data on 177 species from 275 sites across 11 African countries collected over 30 years. They find that research efforts and available information are not evenly distributed. There is less evidence from West Africa compared to Central Africa, and also less information about impacts on birds ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QyAeaPxu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. Taylor, 2015)","plainCitation":"(G. Taylor, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32712,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XF8EQYQB"],"itemData":{"id":32712,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable hunting threatens both biodiversity and local livelihoods. Despite high levels of research effort focused on understanding the dynamics of bushmeat trade and consumption, current research is largely site speci?c. Without synthesis and quantitative analysis of available case studies, the national and regional characteristics of bushmeat trade and consumption remain largely speculative, impeding efforts to inform national and regional policy on bushmeat trade. Here we describe the structure and content of the West and Central African bushmeat database which holds quantitative data on bushmeat sales, consumption and offtake for 177 species from 275 sites across 11 countries in two regions, spanning three decades of research. Despite this wealth of available data, we found important biases in research effort. The majority of studies in West and Central Africa have collected market data, which although providing a useful record of bushmeat sales, are limited in their ability to track changes in hunting offtake. In addition, few data exist for West Africa, and few studies have tracked changes over time, using repeat sampling. With new initiatives in the regions to track bushmeat hunting, this database represents an opportunity to synthesise current and future data on bushmeat hunting, consumption and trade in West and Central Africa, identify gaps in current understanding, and systematically target future monitoring efforts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2014.11.001","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"199-205","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesising bushmeat research effort in West and Central Africa: A new regional database","title-short":"Synthesising bushmeat research effort in West and Central Africa","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320714004182","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Taylor","given":"G."},{"family":"Scharlemann","given":"J.P.W."},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"M."},{"family":"Kümpel","given":"N."},{"family":"Harfoot","given":"M.B.J."},{"family":"Fa","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Melisch","given":"R."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."},{"family":"Bhagwat","given":"S."},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Ajonina","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Albrechtsen","given":"L."},{"family":"Allebone-Webb","given":"S."},{"family":"Brown","given":"E."},{"family":"Brugiere","given":"D."},{"family":"Clark","given":"C."},{"family":"Colell","given":"M."},{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"G."},{"family":"Crookes","given":"D."},{"family":"De Merode","given":"E."},{"family":"Dupain","given":"J."},{"family":"East","given":"T."},{"family":"Edderai","given":"D."},{"family":"Elkan","given":"P."},{"family":"Gill","given":"D."},{"family":"Greengrass","given":"E."},{"family":"Hodgkinson","given":"C."},{"family":"Ilambu","given":"O."},{"family":"Jeanmart","given":"P."},{"family":"Juste","given":"J."},{"family":"Linder","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"D.W."},{"family":"Noss","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Okorie","given":"P.U."},{"family":"Okouyi","given":"V.J.J."},{"family":"Pailler","given":"S."},{"family":"Poulsen","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Riddell","given":"M."},{"family":"Schleicher","given":"J."},{"family":"Schulte-Herbrüggen","given":"B."},{"family":"Starkey","given":"M."},{"family":"Vliet","given":"N.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Whitham","given":"C."},{"family":"Willcox","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Wright","given":"J.H."},{"family":"Coad","given":"L.M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      
4.2.4.3 Structure and composition of economies 
4.2.4.3.1 Subsistence economic activity and the use of wild species
Subsistence economies are defined as those that are small in scale and in which the use of resources (including wild species) is  limited and exclusively used to meet local needs rather than accumulated or sold for profit ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9aKzFRXm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Emery & Pierce, 2005; Natcher, 2009; Schumann & Macinko, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Emery & Pierce, 2005; Natcher, 2009; Schumann & Macinko, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34123,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P9FRQ7IL"],"itemData":{"id":34123,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"People continue to hunt, fish, trap, and gather for subsistence purposes in the contemporary United States. This fact has implications for forest policy, as suggested by an international convention on temperate and boreal forests, commonly known as the Montréal Process. Three canons of law provide a legal basis for subsistence activities by designated social groups in Alaska and Hawaii and by American Indians with treaty rights in the coterminous forty-eight states. A literature review also presents evidence of such practices by people from a variety of ethnic backgrounds throughout the nation. Teleological notions of development espoused by both neoliberal and Marxist scholars suggest that subsistence activities should not persist in a First World setting except as failures of the officially sanctioned economic system. However, alternative economic perspectives from peasant studies and economic geography offer a conceptual framework for viewing at least some subsistence activities as having a logic and values outside of, if articulated with, market structures. Meeting the Montréal Process goal of providing for subsistence use of forests will require research focused on local practices and terms of access to resources as well as their relationship to state and capital processes. We outline the basics of a research agenda on subsistence for an emerging First World political ecology.","container-title":"Environment and Planning A: Economy and Space","DOI":"10.1068/a36263","ISSN":"0308-518X, 1472-3409","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Environ Plan A","language":"en","page":"981-993","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Interrupting the Telos: Locating Subsistence in Contemporary US Forests","title-short":"Interrupting the Telos","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1068/a36263","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Emery","given":"Marla R."},{"family":"Pierce","given":"Alan R"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",6]]}}},{"id":34435,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C7ADL6C3"],"itemData":{"id":34435,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper explores the complex social, economic, and political interplay that takes place between subsistence and wage economies, sharing and reciprocity, and regulatory regimes that now mediate Aboriginal community access to wildlife resources. By focusing on subsistence, with its equally important social and economic attributes, this article argues that the harvesting, processing, and distribution of wild foods and resources continues to be a central component of Canada’s northern social economy. This article concludes by arguing that any attempt to develop effective northern policy in the future must account for the complexity and heterogeneity of northern subsistence economies, and remain open to the plurality of forms they may take.","language":"en","page":"16","source":"Zotero","title":"Subsistence and the Social Economy of Canada’s Aboriginal North","author":[{"family":"Natcher","given":"David C"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":2725,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RJ58NFG7"],"itemData":{"id":2725,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2006.12.010","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"706–718","title":"Subsistence in coastal fisheries policy: What’s in a word?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.marpol.2006.12.010","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Schumann","given":"S."},{"family":"Macinko","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Small scale and decentralized economies are generally characterized by short supply and important focal points for discussion on the sustainability of wild species. What is considered local varies (e.g., 30 miles in the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland – 400 miles in the United States of America) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cVW4tx35","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Galli & Brunori, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Galli & Brunori, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5295,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UV7UQSI4"],"itemData":{"id":5295,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Short Food Supply Chains as Drivers of Sustainable Development. Evidence Document","author":[{"family":"Galli","given":"Francesca"},{"family":"Brunori","given":"Gianluca"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The development of small-scale food systems, is considered key to addressing a variety of ecological stresses (e.g., limit carbon foot print as transportation is limited); among these is the impact of food wastage. It is estimated that in North America and Europe, 250-300 kg. of food per annum per person is wasted due to inefficient supply chains and large-scale market systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uiedrmdK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Galli & Brunori, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Galli & Brunori, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5295,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UV7UQSI4"],"itemData":{"id":5295,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Short Food Supply Chains as Drivers of Sustainable Development. Evidence Document","author":[{"family":"Galli","given":"Francesca"},{"family":"Brunori","given":"Gianluca"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.Many small-scale economies are characterized by livelihood diversification considered important in dealing with variability in the availability of species (e.g., due to migration, population cycles etc. periodic drought, fire…etc.). “Livelihood diversification is defined as the process by which rural families construct a diverse portfolio of activities and social support capabilities in their struggle for survival and in order to improve their standards of living” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4VogUycx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ellis, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Ellis, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34662,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RI7MCNYX"],"itemData":{"id":34662,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Development Studies","DOI":"10.1080/00220389808422553","ISSN":"0022-0388, 1743-9140","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Development Studies","language":"en","page":"1-38","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Household strategies and rural livelihood diversification","URL":"http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/00220389808422553","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Ellis","given":"Frank"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A growing body of work indicates how de-centralized and small-scale community and household economies adapt more readily to variability and shocks in the availability of wild species thereby are nimbler in ensuring conservation outcomes. For example, subsistence economies also are an insurance against down swings in the wage economy (e.g., mining sector) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"N0L6tAA0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Usher, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Usher, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12624,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZQFUI3JR"],"itemData":{"id":12624,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Social Indicators Research","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"175–202","title":"The Household as an Economic Unit in Arctic Aboriginal Communities, and Its Measurement by Means of a Comprehensive Survey","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Usher","given":"Peter J."},{"family":"Duhaime","given":"Gérard"},{"family":"Searles","given":"Edmund"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  [In South Africa], “pastoralism still plays an important role for households, it has shifted from being the core economic activity to being an insurance against unemployment and contributing to subsistence” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vz3FdFND","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berzborn, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Berzborn, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":24670,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VHISXG9Z"],"itemData":{"id":24670,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Arid Environments","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"672–685","title":"The household economy of pastoralists and wage-labourers in the Richtersveld, South Africa","volume":"70","author":[{"family":"Berzborn","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Wild species are fundamental to the health and well-being of indigenous communities and many remote and rural communities globally. The degree of dependence of a community on a resource for subsistence depends on the condition of resource, its proximity to the community, access rights, and restrictions as well as local and external demand and income opportunities; for communities with few other economic and food resources, dependence on wild species and other wild resources, is likely to be higher ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"h1WdD1bY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Roe, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Roe, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32648,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YKDHB65U"],"itemData":{"id":32648,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Wildlife trade, trade controls and rural livelihoods. IIED, London and TRAFFIC, Cambridge, UK","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Making a killing or making a living","author":[{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Mulliken","given":"Teresa"},{"family":"Milledge","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Mremi","given":"Josephine"},{"family":"Mosha","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Grieg-Gran","given":"Maryanne"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  The central point is that wild species contribute to the nutrition of many rural and indigenous peoples globally. Although not easily quantifiable, harvest studies and nutrition studies in various parts of the globe suggest the nutritional value and estimated replacement value (dollars) of many species TEMP. “The few studies that have assessed the relative and absolute contribution of wild meat to household economies in the tropics point to a thriving and financially-large informal sector, perhaps of the same order of magnitude, in terms of gross domestic product , as formal sectors like timber exploitation or agriculture” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wZnzXMak","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Coad, 2019; G. M. Hickey, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Coad, 2019; G. M. Hickey, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":327,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/97LTS7HN"],"itemData":{"id":327,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","ISBN":"978-602-387-083-7","note":"DOI: 10.17528/cifor/007046","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","title":"Towards a sustainable, participatory and inclusive wild meat sector","URL":"https://www.cifor.org/online-library/browse/view-publication/publication/7046.html","author":[{"family":"Coad","given":"L"},{"family":"Fa","given":"JE"},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"K"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"N","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Santamaria","given":"C"},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"D"},{"family":"El Bizri","given":"HR"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"DJ"},{"family":"Cawthorn","given":"DM"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":23562,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2IP3KYC7"],"itemData":{"id":23562,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Food Policy","language":"en","page":"122–132","title":"Quantifying the economic contribution of wild food harvests to rural livelihoods: A global-comparative analysis","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Hickey","given":"G.M."},{"family":"Pouliot","given":"M."},{"family":"Smith-Hall","given":"C."},{"family":"Wunder","given":"S."},{"family":"Nielsen","given":"M.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wild meat is critical to the food security of rural and remote communities comprising up to 90% of available protein in the diet. In northern Australia, for example, wild meat comprises up to 81% of protein intake for indigenous communities. As a result, there are complex and deeply embedded informal economic practices that allow for the sharing and trade of wild meat within small and medium sized communities. For example, in Yangambi, Congo, harvesting and trade of wild meat is illegal, but enforcement by the state is limited. This quasi-open acces ssystem may jeopardize sustainability in the long run, though for now “emblematic” species seem to persist while at the same time local communities are able to meet their daily food needs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pJzxiHbS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(van Vliet, 2019)","plainCitation":"(van Vliet, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":324,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UAH8NMGT"],"itemData":{"id":324,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Despite restrictive legal frameworks, hunting for meat is a reality in tropical countries. In this policy paper, we argue that formal regulations are ill adapted to the contexts in which they should be applied and are characterized by gaps and contradictions that maintain the sector in a limbo. We use contemporary examples from Latin America and Africa described in detail in publications ranging from 2015 to 2019, to illustrate the need for legal reforms that clarify the rights to sell surplus of meat and align land tenure rights with wildlife use rights to suggest a new de?nition of subsistence hunting which accounts for the realities of communities from different cultural backgrounds.","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.3389/fevo.2019.00280","ISSN":"2296-701X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Ecol. Evol.","language":"en","note":"-","page":"280","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Frameworks Regulating Hunting for Meat in Tropical Countries Leave the Sector in the Limbo","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fevo.2019.00280/full","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Antunes","given":"André Pinassi"},{"family":"Constantino","given":"Pedro de Araujo Lima"},{"family":"Gómez","given":"Juanita"},{"family":"Santos-Fita","given":"Dídac"},{"family":"Sartoretto","given":"Eugenio"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",3,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Box 4.30. "The fish of the rich devours the fish of the poor"

Key messages:
* Wild fish species cover the provision of micronutrients and proteins that are vital for millions of people, especially in developing countries
* The multiplication of fishmeal factories can lead to unfair competition with the artisanal fishing sector, an acceleration of the overexploitation of wild fish resources and the accentuation of food insecurity for local communities
It is often suggested that aquaculture has potential to eleviate some of the fishing pressure applied to wild stocks. However, this development has been and, still is, strongly dependent on the availability of fishmeal and fish oil obtained from capture fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Z3tqOeGg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(FAO, 2020b; P\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}ron, 2010)","plainCitation":"(FAO, 2020b; Péron, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35100,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LYX5IXNY"],"itemData":{"id":35100,"type":"book","abstract":"The 2020 edition of The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture has a particular focus on sustainability. This reflects a number of specific considerations. First, 2020 marks the twenty-fifth anniversary of the Code of Conduct for Responsible Fisheries (the Code). Second, several Sustainable Development Goal indicators mature in 2020. Third, FAO hosted the International Symposium on Fisheries Sustainability in late 2019, and fourth, 2020 sees the finalization of specific FAO guidelines on sustainable aquaculture growth, and on social sustainability along value chains. \\n\\nWhile Part 1 retains the format of previous editions, the structure of the rest of the publication has been revised. Part 2 opens with a special section marking the twenty fifth anniversary of the Code. It also focuses on issues coming to the fore, in particular, those related to Sustainable Development Goal 14 and its indicators for which FAO is the “custodian” agency. In addition, Part 2 covers various aspects of fisheries and aquaculture sustainability. The topics discussed range widely, from data and information systems to ocean pollution, product legality, user rights and climate change adaptation. Part 3 now forms the final part of the publication, covering projections and emerging issues such as new technologies and aquaculture biosecurity. It concludes by outlining steps towards a new vision for capture fisheries. The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture aims to provide objective, reliable and up-to-date information to a wide audience – policymakers, managers, scientists, stakeholders and indeed everyone interested in the fisheries and aquaculture sector.\\n\\nThe following complementary information is available:\\n\\nRead online the full digital reportSee the interactive storyRead the In BriefRead the Summary of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic on the fisheries and aquaculture sector","collection-number":"2020","collection-title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture (SOFIA)","event-place":"Rome","ISBN":"978-92-5-132692-3","language":"other","note":"DOI: 10.4060/ca9229enAlso Available in:Chinese Spanish Arabic French Russian","number-of-pages":"244","publisher":"Food and Agricultural Organization of the United Nations","publisher-place":"Rome","source":"FAO Publications","title":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020: Sustainability in action","title-short":"The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2020","URL":"http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/ca9229en/","author":[{"literal":"FAO"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32667,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SUEJM6C8"],"itemData":{"id":32667,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2010.01.027","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"815–820","title":"Where do fishmeal and fish oil products come from? An analysis of the conversion ratios in the global fishmeal industry","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Péron","given":"G."},{"family":"François Mittaine","given":"J."},{"family":"Le Gallic","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} besides, industrial fish farming has negative impacts on environment (loss of habitat e.g. destruction of mangrove areas, invasive species, etc.) and local livelihood, depriving people of employments and competing domestic value-chain (Belhabib, Sumaila, & Pauly, 2015; Hoanh, Tuong, Gowing, & Hardy 2006, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yqJTllVP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Konar, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Konar, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32646,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4EAU59V4"],"itemData":{"id":32646,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"People benefit from the existence of forage fish through a wide range of uses, both direct and indirect. However, due to lack of data and gaps in existing research, the commercial importance of these species tends to get prioritized over the wider benefits they provide to society and the environment. This paper aims to identify all the multiple beneficiaries of forage fish and present their global value that encompasses different categories of benefits using both quantitative and qualitative methods. By adopting the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment framework, we estimated the global economic benefit provided by forage fish to be $18.7 billion per annum, over three times of their direct catch value. This is a partial estimate due to data limitation. We demonstrated the importance of forage fish to the livelihoods of coastal communities by providing direct employment to 5.6 million fishermen globally. The analysis also explored the important role forage fish plays by addressing the nutritional needs of indigenous and coastal communities, and their role in shaping the culture and customs - the significance of all of which cannot be captured by money values alone. We concluded that attempts to capture the economic values of forage fish are likely to be underestimates of the true value that forage fish hold for humans and other interlinked ecosystems. Understanding the true value of forage fish is important to avoid inadvertently making undesirable tradeoffs or management decisions that are environmentally and economically unsustainable.","container-title":"Resources, Conservation and Recycling","DOI":"10.1016/j.resconrec.2019.104456","ISSN":"0921-3449","journalAbbreviation":"Resources, Conservation and Recycling","language":"en","page":"104456","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Illustrating the hidden economic, social and ecological values of global forage fish resources","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0921344919303623","volume":"151","author":[{"family":"Konar","given":"Manaswita"},{"family":"Qiu","given":"Siya"},{"family":"Tougher","given":"Brendan"},{"family":"Vause","given":"James"},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Fitzsimmons","given":"Kevin"},{"family":"Barrows","given":"Rick"},{"family":"Cao","given":"Ling"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). This last point is particularly worrying in developing countries, where international agreements are promoting fish exports to match the growing demand for fish in the markets of high-income Western countries and East Asia (FAO 2020). But, for millions of people, fish cover the provision of micronutrients and proteins, that are essential for a balanced diet, particularly for children under five years old (Hicks 2019).
	On the northwestern coast of Africa (Senegal, Mauritania, Gambia, Bissau-Guinea), international agreements in favor of fishing by fleets from the European Union, Russia and East Asia and high fish exports to the European Union, have led to local fish scarcity and price increases, that have made fish increasingly inaccessible to local consumers (Kaczynski & Fluharty 2002; Gagern & van den Bergh 2013; Corten, Braham, & Sadegh 2017; Thiao, Leport, Ndiaye, & Mbaye 2018). The fisheries in the African Large Marine Ecosystems have reached their peak, and in many cases, move beyond peak catches to a declining trend (Zeller 2020). In addition, the development of industrial aquaculture at the international level has led to a new demand for fishmeal and fish oil. This development is questionable from an ecological viewpoint, as aquaculture depends on wild fish and terrestrial crops (which are also consumed by humans) for feeds and freshwater and land for culture sites ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mgTmiTC8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Troell, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Troell, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"Uebb2sCb/dxUvCd1B","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/local/L7afBCgj/items/AN5287XX"],"itemData":{"id":1475,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Aquaculture is the fastest growing food sector and continues to expand alongside terrestrial crop and livestock production. Using portfolio theory as a conceptual framework, we explore how current interconnections between the aquaculture, crop, livestock, and fisheries sectors act as an impediment to, or an opportunity for, enhanced resilience in the global food system given increased resource scarcity and climate change. Aquaculture can potentially enhance resilience through improved resource use efficiencies and increased diversification of farmed species, locales of production, and feeding strategies. However, aquaculture's reliance on terrestrial crops and wild fish for feeds, its dependence on freshwater and land for culture sites, and its broad array of environmental impacts diminishes its ability to add resilience. Feeds for livestock and farmed fish that are fed rely largely on the same crops, although the fraction destined for aquaculture is presently small (?4%). As demand for high-value fed aquaculture products grows, competition for these crops will also rise, as will the demand for wild fish as feed inputs. Many of these crops and forage fish are also consumed directly by humans and provide essential nutrition for low-income households. Their rising use in aquafeeds has the potential to increase price levels and volatility, worsening food insecurity among the most vulnerable populations. Although the diversification of global food production systems that includes aquaculture offers promise for enhanced resilience, such promise will not be realized if government policies fail to provide adequate incentives for resource efficiency, equity, and environmental protection.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1404067111","issue":"37","page":"13257 LP – 13263","title":"Does aquaculture add resilience to the global food system?","volume":"111","author":[{"family":"Troell","given":"Max"},{"family":"Naylor","given":"Rosamond L"},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"},{"family":"Beveridge","given":"Malcolm"},{"family":"Tyedmers","given":"Peter H"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Arrow","given":"Kenneth J"},{"family":"Barrett","given":"Scott"},{"family":"Crépin","given":"Anne-Sophie"},{"family":"Ehrlich","given":"Paul R"},{"family":"Gren","given":"\\\\textbackslashAAsa"},{"family":"Kautsky","given":"Nils"},{"family":"Levin","given":"Simon A"},{"family":"Nyborg","given":"Karine"},{"family":"Österblom","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Polasky","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"Marten"},{"family":"Walker","given":"Brian H"},{"family":"Xepapadeas","given":"Tasos"},{"family":"Zeeuw","given":"Aart","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is also questionable from a social viewpoint, as the multiplication of fishmeal factories leads to unfair competition with the artisanal fishing sector and to an acceleration of the overexploitation of fish resources (such as sardinella/pelagic species), the accentuation of food insecurity, repercussions on employment, environmental nuisances and danger to public health ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"d2HcEypB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aprapam, 2017; Troell, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Aprapam, 2017; Troell, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26063,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9KWFZXUA","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RYRWQFBJ"],"itemData":{"id":26063,"type":"document","language":"fr","title":"La production de farine de poisson ; Enjeux pour les Communautés Côtières Ouest- africaines","author":[{"literal":"Aprapam"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/dxUvCd1B","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/local/L7afBCgj/items/AN5287XX"],"itemData":{"id":1475,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Aquaculture is the fastest growing food sector and continues to expand alongside terrestrial crop and livestock production. Using portfolio theory as a conceptual framework, we explore how current interconnections between the aquaculture, crop, livestock, and fisheries sectors act as an impediment to, or an opportunity for, enhanced resilience in the global food system given increased resource scarcity and climate change. Aquaculture can potentially enhance resilience through improved resource use efficiencies and increased diversification of farmed species, locales of production, and feeding strategies. However, aquaculture's reliance on terrestrial crops and wild fish for feeds, its dependence on freshwater and land for culture sites, and its broad array of environmental impacts diminishes its ability to add resilience. Feeds for livestock and farmed fish that are fed rely largely on the same crops, although the fraction destined for aquaculture is presently small (?4%). As demand for high-value fed aquaculture products grows, competition for these crops will also rise, as will the demand for wild fish as feed inputs. Many of these crops and forage fish are also consumed directly by humans and provide essential nutrition for low-income households. Their rising use in aquafeeds has the potential to increase price levels and volatility, worsening food insecurity among the most vulnerable populations. Although the diversification of global food production systems that includes aquaculture offers promise for enhanced resilience, such promise will not be realized if government policies fail to provide adequate incentives for resource efficiency, equity, and environmental protection.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1404067111","issue":"37","page":"13257 LP – 13263","title":"Does aquaculture add resilience to the global food system?","volume":"111","author":[{"family":"Troell","given":"Max"},{"family":"Naylor","given":"Rosamond L"},{"family":"Metian","given":"Marc"},{"family":"Beveridge","given":"Malcolm"},{"family":"Tyedmers","given":"Peter H"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Arrow","given":"Kenneth J"},{"family":"Barrett","given":"Scott"},{"family":"Crépin","given":"Anne-Sophie"},{"family":"Ehrlich","given":"Paul R"},{"family":"Gren","given":"\\\\textbackslashAAsa"},{"family":"Kautsky","given":"Nils"},{"family":"Levin","given":"Simon A"},{"family":"Nyborg","given":"Karine"},{"family":"Österblom","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Polasky","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"Marten"},{"family":"Walker","given":"Brian H"},{"family":"Xepapadeas","given":"Tasos"},{"family":"Zeeuw","given":"Aart","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	In Senegal, 22 fish processing plants have been set up since 2010s to manufacture fishmeal and fish oil from wild pelagic fish, mainly Sardinella spp and Ethmalosa spp. to supply the Chinese industrial aquaculture and, ultimately, the markets of Europe, America and China ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mRlRWRKE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aprapam, 2017; Greenpeace, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Aprapam, 2017; Greenpeace, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26063,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9KWFZXUA","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RYRWQFBJ"],"itemData":{"id":26063,"type":"document","language":"fr","title":"La production de farine de poisson ; Enjeux pour les Communautés Côtières Ouest- africaines","author":[{"literal":"Aprapam"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":32676,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PRXCIQG4"],"itemData":{"id":32676,"type":"document","language":"fr","title":"Poisson détourné. la sécurité alimentaire menacée par ll’industrie de la farine et de l’huile de poisson en Afrique de l’Ouest","author":[{"literal":"Greenpeace"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Mauritania, 29 factories of fishmeal were in activity in 2015  and two new ones had also been built in Gambia (Thiao 2018). Sardinella (round S. aurita or flat S. maderensis) and Bonga shad (Ethmalosa fimbriata) are popular wild fish in West Africa because of their low price and constitute the main source of protein (up to 80% of animal protein in some areas of Senegal) and nutrients for local human populations. These wild resources are thus fundamental for food security, but also a key driver of social cohesion and a sign of cultural identity (Cormier-Salem & Samba 2010; Thiao 2018). Fish is the staple of the national dish called “cee bu jen” or rice with fish, traditionaly prepared with white grouper (Epinephelus aeneus). Since the 1990s, as demersal species are less abundant, very expensive and export-oriented, this dish is mostly cooked with pelagic species, notably fresh Sardinella or processed Sardinella called kejax (Mbengue, Cormier-Salem, & Gueye 2009). The deterioration of fish affordability for the local consumers due to a substantial increase in the price of the main consumed fish products since 2006 has ultimately resulted in a significant decrease in the quantity of consumed fish (from 36.5 kg/person/year in 1993 to 23.9 kg in 2013), which is further of lower quality (dried Sardinella crumbs replacing more and more fresh fish, (Thiao 2018)). At the same time, fish exports, especially through fishmeal and  oil, have jumped, from 3,000 tonnes to 17,000 tonnes in 2003 and 2014, respectively (Thiao 2018)
	Using pelagic species like Sardinella to make fishmeal for farmed fish does not reduce the pressure on wild fish. Moreover, it deprives vulnerable people of previously affordable, nutritious local fish and have a substantial impact on diet and public health (Pauly 2019). To face the multiplication of fishmeal factories and the unfair international agreements, a few initiatives are conducted, such as "SOS Yaboye" (SOS Sardinella) citizen mobilization in Senegal, that led to the shutdown of a new Chinese factory in the Gambia borders and public inquiries. 


      4.2.4.3.2 Indigenous economies and the sustainable use of wild species
Indigenous economies refer to both traditional and self-governing economies that are grounded in the cultural norms, practices and belief systems of one or more indigenous peoples ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"amQJiXWb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Appiah-Opoku, 1999; Argumedo & Pimbert, 2010; Cullen, 2007; Koptseva, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Appiah-Opoku, 1999; Argumedo & Pimbert, 2010; Cullen, 2007; Koptseva, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":22977,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BL9W2N76"],"itemData":{"id":22977,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmentalist","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"217–227","title":"Indigenous economic institutions and ecological knowledge: A Ghanaian case study","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Appiah-Opoku","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":26311,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NG2JPBZX","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZVKA6YNI"],"itemData":{"id":26311,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Development","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"343–349","title":"Bypassing globalization: Barter markets as a new indigenous economy in Peru","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Argumedo","given":"A."},{"family":"Pimbert","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":5324,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TD9VEIFE"],"itemData":{"id":5324,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The International Journal of Interdisciplinary Social Sciences","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"289–99","title":"Links between Local Ecological Knowledge and Wealth in Indigenous Communities of Indonesia: Implications for Conservation of Marine Resources","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Cullen","given":"Leanne Claire"},{"family":"Pretty","given":"Jules"},{"family":"Smith","given":"David"},{"family":"Pilgrim","given":"Sarah Elizabeth"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":12295,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V2YLHQ5F"],"itemData":{"id":12295,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"??????????? ???????-???","issue":"9–10","language":"en","page":"95–97","title":"The Current Economic Situation in Taymyr (the Siberian Arctic) and the Prospects of Indigenous Peoples’ Traditional Economy","author":[{"family":"Koptseva","given":"Natalia Petrovna"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While the concept of local (or small scale) is often equated with sustainability and conservation, some small-scale economic practices are not sustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jZYwcTK5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Blaikie, 2006; Schumacher, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Blaikie, 2006; Schumacher, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7991,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HKAVQBEJ"],"itemData":{"id":7991,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Community-based natural resource management (CBNRM) remains a popular policy with many international funding institutions, in spite of growing evidence of its disappointing outcomes. It is underpinned by theoretically justified benefits which serve to reproduce and market it. The paper explores approaches to understand and rectify these failures. The conclusion is that explanatory effort should be expanded from the \\"facilitating characteristics\\" of potentially successful CBNRM sites to include two sets of interfaces-those between donors and recipient states, and between the state (especially the local state) and CBNRMs at the local level. Illustrative examples in Botswana and Malawi are given throughout the discussion. © 2006 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2005.11.023","ISSN":"0305750X","issue":"11","page":"1942–1957","title":"Is Small Really Beautiful? Community-based Natural Resource Management in Malawi and Botswana","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Blaikie","given":"Piers"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":5144,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8CJH5ACK"],"itemData":{"id":5144,"type":"book","language":"it","publisher":"Random House","title":"Small Is Beautiful: A Study of Economics as If People Mattered","author":[{"family":"Schumacher","given":"Ernst Friedrich"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Critical reviews of case studies have produced design principles that produce both local economic benefits and sustain ecological values.
	Interrelationships between indigenous economies and the sustainable use of wild species are complex given such economies have strong social, cultural and spiritual dimensions. “The significance of traditional economies in indigenous communities goes beyond the economic realm—they are more than just livelihoods providing subsistence and sustenance to individuals or communities”ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aNGUVQcS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kuokkanen, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Kuokkanen, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32647,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6RZR5FWF"],"itemData":{"id":32647,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"American Indian Quarterly","DOI":"10.5250/amerindiquar.35.2.0215","ISSN":"0095-182X","issue":"2","note":"publisher: University of Nebraska Press","page":"215-240","source":"JSTOR","title":"Indigenous Economies, Theories of Subsistence, and Women: Exploring the Social Economy Model for Indigenous Governance","title-short":"Indigenous Economies, Theories of Subsistence, and Women","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/10.5250/amerindiquar.35.2.0215","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Kuokkanen","given":"Rauna"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Many aspects of indigenous economic practices and associated resource management systems are considered well aligned with the sustainable use and conservation. Indigenous economies are more likely to manage resources including use wild species in ways that are socially, culturally and ecologically sustainability when there is, a) security of tenure, b) a well-developed management system informed by indigenous knowledge, c) clear incentives and d) equitable sharing of benefits from sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UpAD3Lzw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bawa & Gadgil, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Bawa & Gadgil, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26026,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SZRPBTDS"],"itemData":{"id":26026,"type":"book","event-place":"Washington, DC, USA","language":"en","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington, DC, USA","title":"Ecosystem Services in Subsistence Economies and Conservation of Biodiversity","author":[{"family":"Bawa","given":"Kamaljit S."},{"family":"Gadgil","given":"Madhav"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Market economies can also significantly influence the sustainability of indigenous economies and their use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HmfVCg8v","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Godoy, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Godoy, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":842,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YRACPBPJ"],"itemData":{"id":842,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annu. Rev. Anthropol","language":"en","page":"121–38","title":"The Effect of Market Economies on the Well-Being of Indigenous Peoples and on Their Use of Renewable Natural Resources","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Godoy","given":"Ricardo"},{"family":"Reyes-García","given":"Victoria"},{"family":"Byron","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Leonard","given":"William R."},{"family":"Vadez","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, in the Xingu territory of the Amazon Basin in Brazil, 28 villages in the northern Xingu region opposed large scale logging and mining operations and are producing 1-2 tons of certified organic honey annually which is being sold in one of Brazil’s largest supermarket chains ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NUIQEErm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schwartzman & Zimmerman, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Schwartzman & Zimmerman, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5139,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WYM96FY8"],"itemData":{"id":5139,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"721–27","title":"Conservation Alliances with Indigenous Peoples of the Amazon","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Schwartzman","given":"Stephan"},{"family":"Zimmerman","given":"Barbara"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Soligas of the Biligir Rangan Hills of India harvest fruits from local fruit trees which are made into products (e.g., jams) which are exported from the region and provide local incomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"o1N5lWHl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bawa & Gadgil, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Bawa & Gadgil, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26026,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SZRPBTDS"],"itemData":{"id":26026,"type":"book","event-place":"Washington, DC, USA","language":"en","publisher":"Island Press","publisher-place":"Washington, DC, USA","title":"Ecosystem Services in Subsistence Economies and Conservation of Biodiversity","author":[{"family":"Bawa","given":"Kamaljit S."},{"family":"Gadgil","given":"Madhav"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, the Mumeka outstation economy in Australia is “as sustainable in 2003 as it was in 1979; indeed, this economy is structurally the same hybrid economy with customary (hunting), market (arts production and sale) and state (income support transfers) sectors in both periods” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XEGqDJM8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Altman, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Altman, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1473,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EV2BI25V"],"itemData":{"id":1473,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"People on Country, Healthy Landscapes and Sustainable Indigenous Economic Futures: The Arnhem Land Case","author":[{"family":"Altman","given":"Jon"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

4.2.4.4 Globalization and telecouplings 
A major driver of sustainable use is increased mobility and connectivity, which implies that the use of wild species is affected by telecouplings, i.e., processes taking place in regions where the species are not endemic. Broadly, this happens along several dimensions. First, along the flow of goods and commodities, i.e., trade. Secondly, along the flow of financial transactions and money flows. Third, along the flow of people to watch and enjoy wild species, i.e., tourism. These three flows will be discussed in turn. 

4.2.4.4.1 Trade
Key messages: 
* Wild product trade often forms part of an income diversification and risk reduction strategy for rural poor households in developing countries (well established).
* Trade revenues can facilitate and incentivize conservation, but if regulation is absent or not enforced it often encourages overexploitation and unsustainable use, including local extinction. The sustainability outcomes depend on mediating factors such as the total demand and scale of trade, governance arrangements, trade relations and local incentives for conservation, and species characteristics (established, but incomplete).
* Sustainability outcomes depend on enforcement of local management plans, national laws, and international cooperation. Lack of enforcement and monitoring bears the risk of undermining the potential for sustainable use that may provide critically-needed revenue and incentives for conservation, while at the same time fail to discourage illegal harvests and trade (established but incomplete).
* Trade bans have played overall an important role in halting unsustainable use of threatened species, but in some cases they may have negative consequences on sustainability outcomes and local livelihoods (established, but incomplete). 
* Empowering local communities to capture the benefits from wild species conservation with legal user rights and co-designing regulation contributes to sustainable use of wild species (established, but incomplete).

      This section will provide a conceptualization of trade, and analyze its impact at different scales, including the role of trade relations. The role of markets as drivers either for sustainable and unsustainable use will be explored as well as the importance of economic incentives to engage local communities in sustainable practices. The direct and indirect impacts (e.g. through invasive species, teleconnections, shifts of economic activities) of trade on the use of the target species and local communities will be presented. Trade is a basic economic concept involving the buying and selling of goods and services, or the exchange of goods or services between parties. Trade decouples the consumption of a commodity from the place of origin. When it comes to wild species this may involve the wild species directly (when the species is traded or body parts of it) or indirectly, when commodities derived from these species' habitat are traded, potentially leading to land conversion and loss of habitat. Hence, trade allows consumers in importing regions to buy certain goods that are not available domestically or only at higher prices, while sellers in exporting markets can sell volumes and obtain prices that are potentially higher than what could be obtained at local markets. International trade flows imply a diffusion of responsibility between importing and exporting regions in protecting wild species and biodiversity more general ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5Yg4S0c3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lenzen, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Lenzen, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5XUU5TQ5"],"itemData":{"id":6749,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Over the past decade, an increasing number of authors have been examining the nexus of producer versus consumer responsibility, often dealing with the question of how to assign responsibility for internationally traded greenhouse gas emissions. Recently, a similar problem has appeared in drafting the standards for the Ecological Footprint: While the method traditionally assumes a full life-cycle perspective with full consumer responsibility, a large number of producers (businesses and industry sectors) have started to calculate their own footprints (see www.isa.org.usyd.edu.au). Adding any producer's footprint to other producers' footprints, or to population footprints, which all already cover the full upstream supply chain of their operating inputs, leads to double-counting: The sum of footprints of producers and consumers is larger than the total national footprint. The committee in charge of the Footprint standardisation process was hence faced with the decades-old non-additivity problem, posing the following dilemma for the accounting of footprints, or any other production factor: if one disallows double-counting, but wishes to be able to account for producers and consumers, then one cannot impose the requirement of full life-cycle coverage; the supply chains of actors have to be curtailed somehow in order to avoid double-counting. This work demonstrates and discusses a non-arbitrary method of consistently delineating these supply chains, into mutually exclusive and collectively exhaustive portions of responsibility to be shared by all actors in an economy.","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2006.05.018","ISSN":"0921-8009","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","page":"27-42","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Shared producer and consumer responsibility — Theory and practice","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0921800906002953","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Lenzen","given":"Manfred"},{"family":"Murray","given":"Joy"},{"family":"Sack","given":"Fabian"},{"family":"Wiedmann","given":"Thomas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",2,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Trade has particularly negative effects on wild species, if the resource is left to open access, as  potential trade revenues increase the incentive to harvest more or harvest illegally, undermining conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BTamU2oT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. A. Brander & Taylor, 1998)","plainCitation":"(J. A. Brander & Taylor, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6747,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D5HS95L3"],"itemData":{"id":6747,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper develops a two-good, two-country model with national open access renewable resources. We derive an appropriate analog of “factor proportions” for the renewable resource case and link it to trade patterns and to the likelihood of diversified production. The resource importer gains from trade. However, a diversified resource exporting country necessarily suffers a decline in steady state utility resulting from trade, and may lose along the entire transition path. Thus the basic “gains from trade” presumption is substantially undermined by open access resources. Tariffs imposed by the resource importing country always benefit the resource exporter, and may be pareto-improving.","container-title":"Journal of International Economics","DOI":"10.1016/S0022-1996(97)00029-9","ISSN":"0022-1996","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of International Economics","page":"181-209","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Open access renewable resources: Trade and trade policy in a two-country model","title-short":"Open access renewable resources","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022199697000299","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Brander","given":"James A"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Scott M"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998",4,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Trade revenues do not always benefit local communities who may be the ones harvesting, but largely depends on the relations along the trade value chain. For example, fish traders often supply fishers with (loans for) equipment, which could enable sustainable or unsustainable exploitation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"v3YEuuzH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Elsler, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Elsler, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32708,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MM34WMQ5"],"itemData":{"id":32708,"type":"book","event-place":"Stockholm","ISBN":"978-91-7911-192-2","language":"English","number-of-pages":"64","publisher":"Stockholm Resilience Centre, Stockholm University","publisher-place":"Stockholm","title":"The complexity of seafood trade relations across scales","URL":"https://stockholmuniversity.app.box.com/s/pzq9wqk3csawv6bclizcg8dejl327lvz","author":[{"family":"Elsler","given":"Laura G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In absence of functioning regulation, trade tends to put pressure on wild species in exporting regions, while alleviating biodiversity pressures in importing regions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NEoc2jVV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. A. Brander & Taylor, 1998)","plainCitation":"(J. A. Brander & Taylor, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6747,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D5HS95L3"],"itemData":{"id":6747,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper develops a two-good, two-country model with national open access renewable resources. We derive an appropriate analog of “factor proportions” for the renewable resource case and link it to trade patterns and to the likelihood of diversified production. The resource importer gains from trade. However, a diversified resource exporting country necessarily suffers a decline in steady state utility resulting from trade, and may lose along the entire transition path. Thus the basic “gains from trade” presumption is substantially undermined by open access resources. Tariffs imposed by the resource importing country always benefit the resource exporter, and may be pareto-improving.","container-title":"Journal of International Economics","DOI":"10.1016/S0022-1996(97)00029-9","ISSN":"0022-1996","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of International Economics","page":"181-209","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Open access renewable resources: Trade and trade policy in a two-country model","title-short":"Open access renewable resources","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022199697000299","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Brander","given":"James A"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Scott M"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998",4,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The scale of trade may range from the very local scale, where products are brought to the next bigger town, to the global scale. 
      As it was noted in IPBES global assessment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qY4RSwRn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(I.P.B.E.S. 2019)","plainCitation":"(I.P.B.E.S. 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35052,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6ZTTCD2H"],"itemData":{"id":35052,"type":"chapter","container-title":"IPBES Secretariat","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","language":"en","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science- Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","author":[{"literal":"I.P.B.E.S."}],"editor":[{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E.S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H.T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, expanding trade means that consumption affects practices and uses of wild species elsewhere. Essentially, trade established telecouplings, making use of wild species and ultimately biodiversity loss a global (rather than local) systemic phenomenon, at least if trade is a contributing driver ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OxitD0Dk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lenzen, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Lenzen, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32527,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PWL9M7LB"],"itemData":{"id":32527,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/nature11145","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7401","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"109-112","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"International trade drives biodiversity threats in developing nations","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nature11145","volume":"486","author":[{"family":"Lenzen","given":"M."},{"family":"Moran","given":"D."},{"family":"Kanemoto","given":"K."},{"family":"Foran","given":"B."},{"family":"Lobefaro","given":"L."},{"family":"Geschke","given":"A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, international trade and human transport is now recognized as an important and rapidly growing source of introduction of exotic invasions and diseases worldwide ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"B8M3LmcP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. E. Hulme, 2009)","plainCitation":"(P. E. Hulme, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":692,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/427NIZSB"],"itemData":{"id":692,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of applied ecology","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"10–18","title":"Trade, transport and trouble: managing invasive species pathways in an era of globalization","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Hulme","given":"P.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Lenzen ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rIpSoum7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lenzen 2012)","plainCitation":"(Lenzen 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32527,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PWL9M7LB"],"itemData":{"id":32527,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/nature11145","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7401","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"109-112","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"International trade drives biodiversity threats in developing nations","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nature11145","volume":"486","author":[{"family":"Lenzen","given":"M."},{"family":"Moran","given":"D."},{"family":"Kanemoto","given":"K."},{"family":"Foran","given":"B."},{"family":"Lobefaro","given":"L."},{"family":"Geschke","given":"A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found that 30% of threatened global species are due to international trade, excluding considering the role of invasive species. Marques (ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"k1Jaflit","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2019)","plainCitation":"(2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6753,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UBEN8AAW"],"itemData":{"id":6753,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1038/s41559-019-0824-3","ISSN":"2397-334X","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Ecol Evol","language":"en","page":"628-637","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Increasing impacts of land use on biodiversity and carbon sequestration driven by population and economic growth","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41559-019-0824-3","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Marques","given":"Alexandra"},{"family":"Martins","given":"Inês S."},{"family":"Kastner","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Plutzar","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Theurl","given":"Michaela C."},{"family":"Eisenmenger","given":"Nina"},{"family":"Huijbregts","given":"Mark A. J."},{"family":"Wood","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Stadler","given":"Konstantin"},{"family":"Bruckner","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Canelas","given":"Joana"},{"family":"Hilbers","given":"Jelle P."},{"family":"Tukker","given":"Arnold"},{"family":"Erb","given":"Karlheinz"},{"family":"Pereira","given":"Henrique M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4]]}},"label":"page","suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} concluded that trade was driving 25% of the global impacts on biodiversity in 2011, with large regional differences. For example, 33% of Central and Southern America and 26% of Africa’s biodiversity impacts were driven by consumption abroad.

Trade relations
Trade relations encompass bi- and multilateral relations between harvesters, middle(wo)men, traders, countries, and companies involved in the exchange of wild species. Complex harvester-trader relations, producer cooperatives, and exporter-importer relations can be observed for fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"djEmoyUc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Elsler, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Elsler, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32708,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MM34WMQ5"],"itemData":{"id":32708,"type":"book","event-place":"Stockholm","ISBN":"978-91-7911-192-2","language":"English","number-of-pages":"64","publisher":"Stockholm Resilience Centre, Stockholm University","publisher-place":"Stockholm","title":"The complexity of seafood trade relations across scales","URL":"https://stockholmuniversity.app.box.com/s/pzq9wqk3csawv6bclizcg8dejl327lvz","author":[{"family":"Elsler","given":"Laura G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, but also for hunting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MdI6wvRQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Allebone\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Webb, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Allebone?Webb, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":763,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EU62X7TT"],"itemData":{"id":763,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract: Finding an adequate measure of hunting sustainability for tropical forests has proved difficult. Many researchers have used urban bushmeat market surveys as indicators of hunting volumes and composition, but no analysis has been done of the reliability of market data in reflecting village offtake. We used data from urban markets and the villages that supply these markets to examine changes in the volume and composition of traded bushmeat between the village and the market (trade filters) in Equatorial Guinea. We collected data with market surveys and hunter offtake diaries. The trade filters varied depending on village remoteness and the monopoly power of traders. In a village with limited market access, species that maximized trader profits were most likely to be traded. In a village with greater market access, species for which hunters gained the greatest income per carcass were more likely to be traded. The probability of particular species being sold to market also depended on the capture method and season. Larger, more vulnerable species were more likely to be supplied from less-accessible catchments, whereas there was no effect of forest cover or human population density on probability of being sold. This suggests that the composition of bushmeat offtake in an area may be driven more by urban demand than the geographic characteristics of that area. In one market, traders may have reached the limit of their geographical exploitation range, and hunting pressure within that range may be increasing. Our results demonstrate that it is possible to model the trade filters that bias market data, which opens the way to developing more robust market-based sustainability indices for the bushmeat trade.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2011.01681.x","ISSN":"1523-1739","issue":"3","language":"en","license":"©2011 Society for Conservation Biology","note":"_eprint: https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2011.01681.x","page":"597-606","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Use of Market Data to Assess Bushmeat Hunting Sustainability in Equatorial Guinea","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2011.01681.x","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Allebone?Webb","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Kümpel","given":"N. F."},{"family":"Rist","given":"J."},{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"G."},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"J. M."},{"family":"Milner?Gulland","given":"E. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Trade relations operate at and across local and global scales. Local scale trade relations include those between harvesters and traders and harvesters and middle(wo)men, which are often inbalanced and characterized by dependence from harvesters on traders/middle(wo)men. Examples of global scale trade relations include trade relations between countries (through trade agreements), importers and exporters. Local and global scale trade relations are often intertwined through international market value chains. 
      Local scale trade relations, between and amongst harvesters, traders, and middle(wo)men include harvester-trader relations and producer organizations that commercialize harvests such as fishing cooperatives. They are often associated with artisanal harvesting. Harvester-trader relations can provide important social benefits (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xdPrfUNM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Merlijn, 1989)","plainCitation":"(Merlijn, 1989)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8713,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/33QA7QTY"],"itemData":{"id":8713,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Development and Change","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-7660.1989.tb00362.x","ISSN":"1467-7660","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"© 1989 Institute of Social Studies","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1467-7660.1989.tb00362.x","page":"683-700","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The Role of Middlemen in Small-scale Fisheries: A Case Study of Sarawak, Malaysia","title-short":"The Role of Middlemen in Small-scale Fisheries","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1467-7660.1989.tb00362.x","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Merlijn","given":"Alexander G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1989"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, they have also been associated with unsustainable use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QfZnNZwk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. Crona, 2010)","plainCitation":"(B. Crona, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9009,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K3LP2G44"],"itemData":{"id":9009,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper analyzes the middlemen–fishermen link in coastal communities along the coast of southern Kenya and Zanzibar, and explores effects of reciprocal agreements and credit arrangements on social-ecological feedbacks of coastal systems. The existence and generality of such arrangements are mapped and their effect on resource use and ecosystem dynamics is then explored. Data show that credit arrangements are widespread and that fishermen are bound by reciprocal agreements and financial guarantees during periods of lower catches that provide short-term stabilizing social effects. These arrangements create incentives which disconnect resource extraction from ecosystem dynamics and impede development of sustainable use practices. The role of middlemen is seldom accounted for in fisheries governance. Scenarios for the development of small-scale fisheries in the region are outlined and the function of middlemen is discussed considering the influence of external drivers. Policies that incorporate middlemen are recommended to improve the governance of fish stocks and coastal ecosystems in East Africa.","collection-title":"Coping with global change in marine social-ecological systems","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2010.01.023","ISSN":"0308-597X","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","page":"761-771","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Middlemen, a critical social-ecological link in coastal communities of Kenya and Zanzibar","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308597X10000242","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Nyström","given":"Magnus"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Jiddawi","given":"Narriman"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2017",8,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",7,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At the same time, harvesting cooperatives that enable collective action have been associated with sustainable species use (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9jHpTxdH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McCay, 2014; Ostrom, 1990b)","plainCitation":"(McCay, 2014; Ostrom, 1990b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":16869,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BQHP2YHE"],"itemData":{"id":16869,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ten ?shery cooperatives of the Paci?c coast of Mexico were studied to examine reasons for successful community-based management of the ?shery commons. The cooperatives hold exclusive rights to ‘concession’ territories for major ?sheries and are linked by geographic adjacency and through a federation. The case study underscores the role of factors such as smallness of scale; the productivity, visibility and legibility of the resources and ?sheries involved; clarity of social and territorial boundaries; adjacency and linkages among territorial units; a strong sense of community. The cooperatives also made considerable investments in attaining high levels of knowledge, leadership, transparent and democratic decision-making, and “vigilance,” or enforcement of the rules and the running of the organization. The study also shows the workings of windows of opportunity and experience with environmental change in the development of strong and adaptive capacities for co-management between local organizations and government agencies. Although particular histories and larger legal, political, and cultural contexts matter, the Mexican case supports arguments for greater community-level engagement in “catch share” and territorial management throughout the Paci?c.","container-title":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"11","title":"Cooperatives, concessions, and co-management on the Pacific coast of Mexico","author":[{"family":"McCay","given":"Bonnie J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":4646,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PADMQEFE"],"itemData":{"id":4646,"type":"book","event-place":"Cambridge","language":"en","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge","title":"Governing the Commons : the Evolution of Institutions for Collective Action","author":[{"family":"Ostrom","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1990"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Local trade relations are often multi-dimensional including essential functions, such as providing loans for operations and investments ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"k3n7ftRo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Drury O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Neill, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Drury O’Neill, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8771,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SPJBYE6Q"],"itemData":{"id":8771,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Small-scale fishing communities are increasingly connected to international seafood trade via exports to a growing global market. Understanding how this connectedness impacts local fishery systems, both socially and ecologically, has become a necessary challenge for fisheries governance. Market prices are a potential mechanism by which global market demands are transferred to small-scale fishery actors. In most small-scale fisheries this happens through various traders (intermediaries, middlemen/women or patrons). By financing fishing operations, buying and selling products and transferring market information, traders can actively pass international market signals, such as price, to fishers. How these signals influence fishers' decisions and consequent fishing effort is still poorly understood yet significant for future social-ecological sustainability. The paper uses an economic framed field experiment, in combination with interviews, to shed light on this. It does so in the context of the Philippine patron-client 'suki' arrangement. Over 250 fishers in Concepcion, Iloilo were asked in an economic experiment, to make decisions about fuel loans in light of changing market prices. Interviews with participants and their patrons gathered additional information on relevant contextual variables potentially influencing borrowing. They included fisher characteristics and socio-economic conditions. Contrary to our hypotheses, fishers showed no response in their borrowing behaviour to experimental price changes. Instead, gender and the previous experiment round were predictive of their choice of loans in the experiment. We explore possible reasons for this.","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2019.00491","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","language":"English","note":"publisher: Frontiers","source":"Frontiers","title":"An Experimental Approach to Exploring Market Responses in Small-Scale Fishing Communities","URL":"http://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fmars.2019.00491/full","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Drury O’Neill","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Lindahl","given":"Therese"},{"family":"Daw","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Ferrer","given":"Alice Joan G."},{"family":"Pomeroy","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, access, information, and infrastructure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"netStGbv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bailey, 2016; Ferrol-Schulte, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Bailey, 2016; Ferrol-Schulte, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26046,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WZYNH8IH","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6AC2HJ7M"],"itemData":{"id":26046,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability, Sustainability governance and transformation 2016: Informational governance and environmental sustainability","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2015.06.004.","issue":"February","language":"en","page":"25–32","title":"The Role of Traceability in Transforming Seafood Governance in the Global South","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Bailey","given":"Megan"},{"family":"Bush","given":"Simon R."},{"family":"Miller","given":"Alex"},{"family":"Kochen","given":"Momo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":8781,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J5S2K5UV"],"itemData":{"id":8781,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Coastal and marine management cannot ignore the role of community livelihoods and vulnerability if it is to be effective and just, especially when the targeted coastal communities are highly resource-dependent. Previous studies have highlighted the potential role that patrons, or middlemen, can play as knowledge-brokers in marine natural resource management. On Zanzibar, where small-scale fishing is an important livelihood for many coastal communities, patron–client systems operate without regulation. This study investigates the roles patrons play regarding coastal livelihood vulnerability and natural resource management. It asks not only what patron–client relationships as institutions contribute to fishing households within the framework of the Sustainable Livelihoods Approach, but how these contributions differ across coastal villages. Furthermore, what do patron–client relationships mean for coastal livelihood vulnerability as well as marine natural resource management? Data was collected from 3 villages on Zanzibar with questionnaires, semi-structured key informant interviews, focus groups and participant observations. Although patrons have been identified in this and previous studies to be drivers of resource exploitation, they are also potential agents in identifying and activating sustainable solutions to environmental decline and improving fishing household resilience. Our results confirm previous findings that the role of patrons is mainly in providing fishing equipment and short-term relief in times of hardship, either through food or money. Our findings also indicate that there are small but significant differences in the type of contributions patrons make to coastal households.","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2014.07.016","ISSN":"0964-5691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","page":"63-73","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Patron–client relationships, livelihoods and natural resource management in tropical coastal communities","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0964569114002294","volume":"100","author":[{"family":"Ferrol-Schulte","given":"Daniella"},{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2016",12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, or advice, and support next to the exchange of seafood with different consequences for species use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eOM2yelD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Basurto, 2012; Ferse, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Basurto, 2012; Ferse, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8788,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UI334KZC"],"itemData":{"id":8788,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-012-9508-1","ISSN":"0300-7839, 1572-9915","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Hum Ecol","language":"en","page":"597-609","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Emergence of Access Controls in Small-Scale Fishing Commons: A Comparative Analysis of Individual Licenses and Common Property-Rights in Two Mexican Communities","title-short":"The Emergence of Access Controls in Small-Scale Fishing Commons","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10745-012-9508-1","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Cinti","given":"Ana"},{"family":"Bourillón","given":"Luis"},{"family":"Rojo","given":"Mario"},{"family":"Torre","given":"Jorge"},{"family":"Weaver","given":"A. Hudson"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",8]]}}},{"id":8795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6WNBIHG"],"itemData":{"id":8795,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Small-scale fisheries in coral reef areas support the livelihoods of millions of people worldwide. Anthropogenic impacts such as overfishing and climate change increasingly threaten both the reef ecosystem and the livelihood security of the people that depend on the reefs. Adaptive management strategies are needed to adequately deal with these threats, but they require an understanding of the underlying drivers, which often originate and act on multiple levels. Using a social-ecological system approach, the coral reef fishery of the Spermonde Archipelago in South Sulawesi/Indonesia is assessed to identify key drivers and strategic leverage points for management. Under the influence of international markets and technological changes, several export-oriented fisheries have developed in the area that led to distinct subsequent peaks in fishing activity in a pattern of sequential marine resource exploitation. In response to stressors such as seasonality and overfishing of individual locations or species, a number of coping strategies have developed locally. These include extensive borrowing from fishing patrons, diversification of fishing methods, fishing migrations, and the crafting of local institutions to regulate fishing activity. However, the coping strategies hinder, and even decrease, the capacity of the system to adapt to future stressors and undermine the sustainability of the fishery. Potential strategies that target different levels of the fishery system in order to strengthen adaptive management are identified.","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10113-012-0342-1","ISSN":"1436-378X","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Reg Environ Change","language":"en","page":"2053-2065","source":"Springer Link","title":"To cope or to sustain? Eroding long-term sustainability in an Indonesian coral reef fishery","title-short":"To cope or to sustain?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10113-012-0342-1","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Neil","given":"Muhammad"},{"family":"Schwerdtner Máñez","given":"Kathleen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Their different functions and characteristics can determine the outcomes for the use of wild species and the trade partners and affect incentives for the use of wild species. For instance, access to new markets can promote non-selective capture ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"itqUTylf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nascimento, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Nascimento, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8769,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A8F82M9L"],"itemData":{"id":8769,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The large mangrove crab Ucides cordatus (“caranguejo-uçá”) is a key fisheries resource in Brazil, critical for the sustenance of livelihoods of thousands of people in coastal rural areas. Today's crab populations suffer from habitat degradation, disease, and increasing fishing pressure. Crabs are sold alive on local and regional markets, or traded as processed meat and the market chains typically involve intermediaries (i.e. traders). The present study examined the relationship between crab harvesters and the intermediaries, and the socio-ecological implications thereof. The research was performed between September 2013 and October 2014 in the Mamanguape River estuary, northeastern Brazil. Socioeconomic information and data regarding the catch (sex and carapace width of the crabs), the processing of U. cordatus meat and the commercial relationship between harvesters and intermediaries were obtained through structured (questionnaires) and semi-structured interviews and direct observations. The crab harvesters exist under precarious socioeconomic conditions that place them at the edge of society and therefore often seek loans offered by the intermediaries, generating loyalty and dependence that guarantees the intermediaries a stable supply of crabs needed to supply an avid market. Within this relationship, the intermediaries create pressure on natural crab populations by stimulating non-selective captures, as they buy specimens below the legal size limit (6cm wide carapace) for meat processing. During crab meat processing, the intermediaries themselves report that the meat is often mixed with cooked and shredded of other marine vertebrates, such as spotted eagle ray (Aetobatus narinari) and nurse shark (Ginglymostoma cirratum), to increase the weight of the final product. As with the crab harvesters, the women involved in processing the crabmeat often accept loans, resulting in the same type of dependence and loyalty to the intermediaries. The intermediaries, with their strong influence on the crab harvesting, are directly linked to the commercial, social and ecological implications of these harvesting activities, together with the crab harvesters themselves. Hence, to ensure sustainability of the U. cordatus fishery and maintain (better improve) dependant livelihoods, all actors involved in the production chain of U. cordatus must be considered when developing management strategies, rather than the current approach of considering the crab harvesters only. We advise the development and implementation of fisheries associations to give the crab harvesters (and regulating bodies) greater control over and capital gains from their catches.","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2016.08.017","ISSN":"0921-8009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"44-51","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Commercial relationships between intermediaries and harvesters of the mangrove crab Ucides cordatus (Linnaeus, 1763) in the Mamanguape River estuary, Brazil, and their socio-ecological implications","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0921800916302609","volume":"131","author":[{"family":"Nascimento","given":"Douglas Macêdo"},{"family":"Alves","given":"Rômulo Romeu Nóbrega"},{"family":"Barboza","given":"Raynner Rilke Duarte"},{"family":"Schmidt","given":"Anders Jensen"},{"family":"Diele","given":"Karen"},{"family":"Mourão","given":"José Silva"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and credits can decouple species harvest from environmental fluctuations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"twj0XnZC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. Crona, 2010; Kininmonth, 2017)","plainCitation":"(B. Crona, 2010; Kininmonth, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9009,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K3LP2G44"],"itemData":{"id":9009,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper analyzes the middlemen–fishermen link in coastal communities along the coast of southern Kenya and Zanzibar, and explores effects of reciprocal agreements and credit arrangements on social-ecological feedbacks of coastal systems. The existence and generality of such arrangements are mapped and their effect on resource use and ecosystem dynamics is then explored. Data show that credit arrangements are widespread and that fishermen are bound by reciprocal agreements and financial guarantees during periods of lower catches that provide short-term stabilizing social effects. These arrangements create incentives which disconnect resource extraction from ecosystem dynamics and impede development of sustainable use practices. The role of middlemen is seldom accounted for in fisheries governance. Scenarios for the development of small-scale fisheries in the region are outlined and the function of middlemen is discussed considering the influence of external drivers. Policies that incorporate middlemen are recommended to improve the governance of fish stocks and coastal ecosystems in East Africa.","collection-title":"Coping with global change in marine social-ecological systems","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2010.01.023","ISSN":"0308-597X","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","page":"761-771","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Middlemen, a critical social-ecological link in coastal communities of Kenya and Zanzibar","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308597X10000242","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Nyström","given":"Magnus"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Jiddawi","given":"Narriman"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2017",8,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",7,1]]}}},{"id":8778,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RV2MZ393"],"itemData":{"id":8778,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Kininmonth, S., B. Crona, Ö. Bodin, I. Vaccaro, L. J. Chapman, and C. A. Chapman. 2016. Microeconomic relationships between and among fishers and traders influence the ability to respond to social-ecological changes in a small-scale fishery. Ecology and Society 22(2):26. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-08833-220226","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-08833-220226","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"2","language":"en","license":"© 2017 by the author(s)","note":"publisher: The Resilience Alliance","source":"www.ecologyandsociety.org","title":"Microeconomic relationships between and among fishers and traders influence the ability to respond to social-ecological changes in a small-scale fishery","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol22/iss2/art26/","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Kininmonth","given":"Stuart"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Bodin","given":"Örjan"},{"family":"Vaccaro","given":"Ismael"},{"family":"Chapman","given":"Lauren"},{"family":"Chapman","given":"Colin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",5,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In cases, where local trade relations promote sustainable use, trade relations have been found to promote self-governance or commercial interests align with conservation objectives, such as in the case of Indonesia where traders' loans enable fishers to reduce fishing pressure in an overfished marine ecosystem. 
	Some local trade relations promote self-governing strategies and contribute to local level institutional diversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"n6tkIPFV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Basurto, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Basurto, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8788,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UI334KZC"],"itemData":{"id":8788,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-012-9508-1","ISSN":"0300-7839, 1572-9915","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Hum Ecol","language":"en","page":"597-609","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Emergence of Access Controls in Small-Scale Fishing Commons: A Comparative Analysis of Individual Licenses and Common Property-Rights in Two Mexican Communities","title-short":"The Emergence of Access Controls in Small-Scale Fishing Commons","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10745-012-9508-1","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Cinti","given":"Ana"},{"family":"Bourillón","given":"Luis"},{"family":"Rojo","given":"Mario"},{"family":"Torre","given":"Jorge"},{"family":"Weaver","given":"A. Hudson"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This aspect is important because informal governance at the local level can be more effective or contribute to centralized governance. Trade partners can contribute to devising informal social norms and contribute to rule-making. Traders may influence social norms through their key positions of channeling information to local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"otN1EE5J","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. Crona & Bodin, 2010; Glaser, 2010)","plainCitation":"(B. Crona & Bodin, 2010; Glaser, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":13831,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IEART55Z"],"itemData":{"id":13831,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"ABSTRACT. Both global and local environmental problems call for the transformation of many contemporary and unsustainable governance approaches. Therefore, recent interest has sprung up around factors that facilitate and hinder societies from transforming governance of natural resources. Using a social-network approach, we study links between informal power structures and knowledge sharing and consensus building. We examine how this interaction may have affected the (in)ability of a community to move from open-access to some form of collective action for resource management. Individuals occupying central positions in a knowledge network can be instrumental in determining which knowledge and interpretation of ecological signals is most dominant. If the same individuals are also influential in other areas, they are highly likely to become opinion leaders. We use this notion of opinion leaders to frame our study. The study is set in a rural fishing community in East Africa where access to fishing equipment is of utmost importance for generating household income, but such gear ownership is not evenly distributed in the village. Hence, we use gear-exchange networks to explore power. Our results show a clear and strong relationship between centrality in the knowledge network and in-degree centrality (reflecting gear-lending capacity) in the gear-exchange network, supporting the idea that opinion leaders exist. We also indicate that a majority of these potential opinion leaders demonstrate little recognition of declining fisheries. We relate our findings to existing theories of influence and governance transformability at the community level, and explore ideas about how social networks can help identify potential change agents in communities experiencing inertia with respect to collective action for improved resource management.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"4","note":"publisher: Resilience Alliance Inc.","source":"JSTOR","title":"Power Asymmetries in Small-Scale Fisheries: a Barrier to Governance Transformability?","title-short":"Power Asymmetries in Small-Scale Fisheries","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/26268218","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Bodin","given":"Örjan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":9011,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y3PTJYTR"],"itemData":{"id":9011,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"A review of a major community-based marine protected area programme (CB-MPA) in an Indonesian island archipelago is the point of departure for this article. Despite a well-designed institutional structure to facilitate local participation, local knowledge about the CB-MPA is found to be low and resource access and influence on decision-making in the programme is negligible for the majority of islanders. At the same time, most of those who know about the programme consider it as pertaining to the public authority only. These findings stand in contrast to evidence on non-formal ways of protecting and managing marine areas in the same geographical area but outside the formal MPA institutional framework. In particular, the article identifies a number of emergent rules-in-use in marine management, which operate parallel to legally established MPAs. It is argued that emergent forms of marine area protection such as non-formal self-organising island exclusion zones (IEZ) offer as yet mostly unused potentials for formal MPA development, particularly in those coastal and marine areas without traditional forms of marine and coastal management.","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2010.04.006","ISSN":"0308-597X","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","page":"1215-1225","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Whose sustainability? Top–down participation and emergent rules in marine protected area management in Indonesia","title-short":"Whose sustainability?","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308597X10000837","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Baitoningsih","given":"Wasistini"},{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."},{"family":"Neil","given":"Muhammad"},{"family":"Deswandi","given":"Rio"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2018",9,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, traders may devise new rules. For instance, in the case of the Mexican squid fishery, traders have become powerful in the fishery due to collusion and have set quotas to fishers which prevents catch discards ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vxauqx0d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Frawley, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Frawley, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32645,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/24RY2XPK"],"itemData":{"id":32645,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"1","note":"publisher: JSTOR","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Environmental and institutional degradation in the globalized economy","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Frawley","given":"Timothy H."},{"family":"Finkbeiner","given":"Elena M."},{"family":"Crowder","given":"Larry B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. On the downside, the traders’ power allows them to significantly reducfe the beach price fishers fetch for their catch ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"njFZaBEI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Elsler, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Elsler, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32642,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ERI5MPNH"],"itemData":{"id":32642,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Small-scale fisheries are critically important for livelihoods around the world, particularly in tropical regions. However, climate variability and anthropogenic climate change may seriously impact small-scale fisheries by altering the abundance and distribution of target species. Social relationships between fishery users, such as fish traders, can determine how each individual responds and is affected by changes in fisheries. These informal cooperative and competitive relationships provide access, support, and incentives for fishing and affect the distribution of benefits. Yet, individuals’ actions and impacts on individuals are often the primary focus of the economic analyses informing small-scale fisheries’ formal management. This focus dismisses relevant social relationships. We argue that this leads to a disconnect between reality and its model representation used in formal management, which may reduce formal fisheries management’s efficiency and efficacy and potentially trigger adverse consequences. Here, we examine this argument by comparing the predictions of a simple bioeconomic fishery model with those of a social-ecological model that incorporates the dynamics of cooperative relationships between fish traders. We illustrate model outcomes using an empirical case study in the Mexican Humboldt squid fishery. We find that (1) the social-ecological model with relationship dynamics substantially improves accuracy in predicting observed fishery variables to the simple bioeconomic model. (2) Income inequality outcomes are associated with changes in cooperative trade relationships. When environmental temperature is included in the model as a driver of species production dynamics, we find that climate-driven temperature variability drives a decline in catch that, in turn, reduce fishers’ income. We observe an offset of this loss in income by including cooperative relationships between fish traders (oligopoly) in the model. These relationships break down following species distribution changes and result in an increase in prices fishers receive. Finally, (3) our social-ecological model simulations show that the current fishery development program, which seeks to increase fishers’ income through an increase in domestic market demand, is supported by predictions from the simple bioeconomic model, may increase income inequality between fishers and traders. Our findings highlight the real and urgent need to re-think fisheries management models in the context of small-scale fisheries and climate change worldwide to encompass social relationship dynamics.","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10113-021-01747-5","ISSN":"1436-378X","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Reg Environ Change","language":"en","page":"35","source":"Springer Link","title":"Social relationship dynamics mediate climate impacts on income inequality: evidence from the Mexican Humboldt squid fishery","title-short":"Social relationship dynamics mediate climate impacts on income inequality","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10113-021-01747-5","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Elsler","given":"Laura G."},{"family":"Frawley","given":"Timothy Haight"},{"family":"Britten","given":"Gregory L."},{"family":"Crowder","given":"Larry B."},{"family":"DuBois","given":"Timothy C."},{"family":"Radosavljevic","given":"Sonja"},{"family":"Gilly","given":"William F."},{"family":"Crépin","given":"Anne-Sophie"},{"family":"Schlüter","given":"Maja"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,24]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Finally, traders and middle(wo)men can contribute to formal decision-making processes that harvesters, due to their limited financial and administrative capacities, do not have access to ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zcfkvFM7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Basurto, 2012; Frawley, 2019; Maryudi & Myers, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Basurto, 2012; Frawley, 2019; Maryudi & Myers, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8788,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UI334KZC"],"itemData":{"id":8788,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-012-9508-1","ISSN":"0300-7839, 1572-9915","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Hum Ecol","language":"en","page":"597-609","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Emergence of Access Controls in Small-Scale Fishing Commons: A Comparative Analysis of Individual Licenses and Common Property-Rights in Two Mexican Communities","title-short":"The Emergence of Access Controls in Small-Scale Fishing Commons","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10745-012-9508-1","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Cinti","given":"Ana"},{"family":"Bourillón","given":"Luis"},{"family":"Rojo","given":"Mario"},{"family":"Torre","given":"Jorge"},{"family":"Weaver","given":"A. Hudson"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",12,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",8]]}}},{"id":32645,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/24RY2XPK"],"itemData":{"id":32645,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"1","note":"publisher: JSTOR","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Environmental and institutional degradation in the globalized economy","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Frawley","given":"Timothy H."},{"family":"Finkbeiner","given":"Elena M."},{"family":"Crowder","given":"Larry B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32644,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MLJQCETN"],"itemData":{"id":32644,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Over the past few decades, transnational and supranational market-based forest governance systems have been developed to address the complex problems associated with deforestation, by improving the legality and sustainability of timber traded in global markets. This is catalysed by the increasing global production and consumption of timber products and increasing sensitivity of interest groups to how timber products are produced. A broad range of actors is involved in global production networks. This paper discusses how hierarchies and networks of power across the timber production network are encountered and negotiated. More specifically, it investigates the power constellations of wood furniture actors in Indonesia, nested within global production networks: who holds the power, how power is gained and maintained, and who wins and loses over time. Using the case of the timber legality assurance system in the context of the European Union Forest Law Enforcement Governance and Trade (FLEGT) initiative, we demonstrate that legality verification in Indonesia is both entrenching pre-existing inequitable power relations while producing new modes of elite capture. Legality verification requires new knowledge and additional costs that are sometimes beyond the capacity of certain (particularly smaller) furniture manufacturers operators. This has driven a new practice of renting out FLEGT licenses by larger producers/manufacturers to smaller ones in the country. Although the practice implies potential risks (e.g. fines), large companies in Indonesia manage risk by drawing from pre-existing patronage relations. They also appear to find the risk worthwhile, as it produces financial gain but moreover, a new form of control over the market. Meanwhile, small operators and artisanal producers that still aspire to global markets face disproportionate challenges to engage in legality and are becoming more vulnerable as a result of new legality measures.","container-title":"Geoforum","DOI":"10.1016/j.geoforum.2018.10.008","ISSN":"0016-7185","journalAbbreviation":"Geoforum","language":"en","page":"46-53","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Renting legality: How FLEGT is reinforcing power relations in Indonesian furniture production networks","title-short":"Renting legality","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0016718518303038","volume":"97","author":[{"family":"Maryudi","given":"Ahmad"},{"family":"Myers","given":"Rodd"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12,1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	The functions of local trade relations can promote social sustainability. Traders and middle(wo)men may give insurance during hardship, personal support, and creating contingencies between supply volumes and demand ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ulpBs7UJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ferrol-Schulte, 2014; Gonz\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}lez-Mon, 2019; Radjawali, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Ferrol-Schulte, 2014; González-Mon, 2019; Radjawali, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8781,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J5S2K5UV"],"itemData":{"id":8781,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Coastal and marine management cannot ignore the role of community livelihoods and vulnerability if it is to be effective and just, especially when the targeted coastal communities are highly resource-dependent. Previous studies have highlighted the potential role that patrons, or middlemen, can play as knowledge-brokers in marine natural resource management. On Zanzibar, where small-scale fishing is an important livelihood for many coastal communities, patron–client systems operate without regulation. This study investigates the roles patrons play regarding coastal livelihood vulnerability and natural resource management. It asks not only what patron–client relationships as institutions contribute to fishing households within the framework of the Sustainable Livelihoods Approach, but how these contributions differ across coastal villages. Furthermore, what do patron–client relationships mean for coastal livelihood vulnerability as well as marine natural resource management? Data was collected from 3 villages on Zanzibar with questionnaires, semi-structured key informant interviews, focus groups and participant observations. Although patrons have been identified in this and previous studies to be drivers of resource exploitation, they are also potential agents in identifying and activating sustainable solutions to environmental decline and improving fishing household resilience. Our results confirm previous findings that the role of patrons is mainly in providing fishing equipment and short-term relief in times of hardship, either through food or money. Our findings also indicate that there are small but significant differences in the type of contributions patrons make to coastal households.","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2014.07.016","ISSN":"0964-5691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","page":"63-73","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Patron–client relationships, livelihoods and natural resource management in tropical coastal communities","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0964569114002294","volume":"100","author":[{"family":"Ferrol-Schulte","given":"Daniella"},{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2016",12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",11]]}}},{"id":32709,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RPGY6FGY"],"itemData":{"id":32709,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The importance of understanding how social-ecological interdependencies deriving from global trade influence sustainability has been argued for decades. Even if substantial progress has been made, a research gap remains regarding how the adaptability of small-scale fish buyers, whose daily operations have implications for the livelihood of more than 100 million people, are affected by networks of trade relationships. Adaptability is here defined as fish buyers´ abilities to adapt using their relationships with others. We elaborate how these capacities relate to the precise patterns in which a fish buyer is entangled with other fish buyers, with the fishers, and with the targeted fish species, by combining a multilevel social-ecological network model with empirical data from a small-scale fishery in Mexico. Further, we also identify types of fish buyers distinguishable by how they operate, and how they are embedded in the trading network. Our results suggest that adaptability differs substantially amongst these types, thus implying that fish buyers' abilities to respond to changes are unevenly distributed. This study demonstrates the need for a more profound understanding of the consequences of the different ways in which fish buyers operate commercially, and how these operations are affected by patterns of social and social-ecological interdependencies.","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2019.05.018","ISSN":"0921-8009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"106338","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Small-scale fish buyers' trade networks reveal diverse actor types and differential adaptive capacities","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0921800918313247","volume":"164","author":[{"family":"González-Mon","given":"Blanca"},{"family":"Bodin","given":"Örjan"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Nenadovic","given":"Mateja"},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":9007,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GVZ2BHQW"],"itemData":{"id":9007,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reconsidering Development","ISSN":"2474-9117","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"1","language":"en","license":"Copyright (c)","source":"pubs.lib.umn.edu","title":"Coping with Uncertainties: Live Reef Food Fish (LRFF) Trade in Spermonde Archipelago, Indonesia","title-short":"Coping with Uncertainties","URL":"https://pubs.lib.umn.edu/index.php/reconsidering/article/view/901","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Radjawali","given":"Irendra"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",2,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is particularly prominent in committed trade relations based on kinship, friendship, or strong social norms of reciprocity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aMDYw7S6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Drury O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Neill, 2019; Ferse, 2014; Nascimento, 2017; T. L. M. Sharp, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Drury O’Neill, 2019; Ferse, 2014; Nascimento, 2017; T. L. M. Sharp, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8771,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SPJBYE6Q"],"itemData":{"id":8771,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Small-scale fishing communities are increasingly connected to international seafood trade via exports to a growing global market. Understanding how this connectedness impacts local fishery systems, both socially and ecologically, has become a necessary challenge for fisheries governance. Market prices are a potential mechanism by which global market demands are transferred to small-scale fishery actors. In most small-scale fisheries this happens through various traders (intermediaries, middlemen/women or patrons). By financing fishing operations, buying and selling products and transferring market information, traders can actively pass international market signals, such as price, to fishers. How these signals influence fishers' decisions and consequent fishing effort is still poorly understood yet significant for future social-ecological sustainability. The paper uses an economic framed field experiment, in combination with interviews, to shed light on this. It does so in the context of the Philippine patron-client 'suki' arrangement. Over 250 fishers in Concepcion, Iloilo were asked in an economic experiment, to make decisions about fuel loans in light of changing market prices. Interviews with participants and their patrons gathered additional information on relevant contextual variables potentially influencing borrowing. They included fisher characteristics and socio-economic conditions. Contrary to our hypotheses, fishers showed no response in their borrowing behaviour to experimental price changes. Instead, gender and the previous experiment round were predictive of their choice of loans in the experiment. We explore possible reasons for this.","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2019.00491","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","language":"English","note":"publisher: Frontiers","source":"Frontiers","title":"An Experimental Approach to Exploring Market Responses in Small-Scale Fishing Communities","URL":"http://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fmars.2019.00491/full","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Drury O’Neill","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Lindahl","given":"Therese"},{"family":"Daw","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Ferrer","given":"Alice Joan G."},{"family":"Pomeroy","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":8795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6WNBIHG"],"itemData":{"id":8795,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Small-scale fisheries in coral reef areas support the livelihoods of millions of people worldwide. Anthropogenic impacts such as overfishing and climate change increasingly threaten both the reef ecosystem and the livelihood security of the people that depend on the reefs. Adaptive management strategies are needed to adequately deal with these threats, but they require an understanding of the underlying drivers, which often originate and act on multiple levels. Using a social-ecological system approach, the coral reef fishery of the Spermonde Archipelago in South Sulawesi/Indonesia is assessed to identify key drivers and strategic leverage points for management. Under the influence of international markets and technological changes, several export-oriented fisheries have developed in the area that led to distinct subsequent peaks in fishing activity in a pattern of sequential marine resource exploitation. In response to stressors such as seasonality and overfishing of individual locations or species, a number of coping strategies have developed locally. These include extensive borrowing from fishing patrons, diversification of fishing methods, fishing migrations, and the crafting of local institutions to regulate fishing activity. However, the coping strategies hinder, and even decrease, the capacity of the system to adapt to future stressors and undermine the sustainability of the fishery. Potential strategies that target different levels of the fishery system in order to strengthen adaptive management are identified.","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10113-012-0342-1","ISSN":"1436-378X","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Reg Environ Change","language":"en","page":"2053-2065","source":"Springer Link","title":"To cope or to sustain? Eroding long-term sustainability in an Indonesian coral reef fishery","title-short":"To cope or to sustain?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10113-012-0342-1","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Neil","given":"Muhammad"},{"family":"Schwerdtner Máñez","given":"Kathleen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12,1]]}}},{"id":8769,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A8F82M9L"],"itemData":{"id":8769,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The large mangrove crab Ucides cordatus (“caranguejo-uçá”) is a key fisheries resource in Brazil, critical for the sustenance of livelihoods of thousands of people in coastal rural areas. Today's crab populations suffer from habitat degradation, disease, and increasing fishing pressure. Crabs are sold alive on local and regional markets, or traded as processed meat and the market chains typically involve intermediaries (i.e. traders). The present study examined the relationship between crab harvesters and the intermediaries, and the socio-ecological implications thereof. The research was performed between September 2013 and October 2014 in the Mamanguape River estuary, northeastern Brazil. Socioeconomic information and data regarding the catch (sex and carapace width of the crabs), the processing of U. cordatus meat and the commercial relationship between harvesters and intermediaries were obtained through structured (questionnaires) and semi-structured interviews and direct observations. The crab harvesters exist under precarious socioeconomic conditions that place them at the edge of society and therefore often seek loans offered by the intermediaries, generating loyalty and dependence that guarantees the intermediaries a stable supply of crabs needed to supply an avid market. Within this relationship, the intermediaries create pressure on natural crab populations by stimulating non-selective captures, as they buy specimens below the legal size limit (6cm wide carapace) for meat processing. During crab meat processing, the intermediaries themselves report that the meat is often mixed with cooked and shredded of other marine vertebrates, such as spotted eagle ray (Aetobatus narinari) and nurse shark (Ginglymostoma cirratum), to increase the weight of the final product. As with the crab harvesters, the women involved in processing the crabmeat often accept loans, resulting in the same type of dependence and loyalty to the intermediaries. The intermediaries, with their strong influence on the crab harvesting, are directly linked to the commercial, social and ecological implications of these harvesting activities, together with the crab harvesters themselves. Hence, to ensure sustainability of the U. cordatus fishery and maintain (better improve) dependant livelihoods, all actors involved in the production chain of U. cordatus must be considered when developing management strategies, rather than the current approach of considering the crab harvesters only. We advise the development and implementation of fisheries associations to give the crab harvesters (and regulating bodies) greater control over and capital gains from their catches.","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2016.08.017","ISSN":"0921-8009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"44-51","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Commercial relationships between intermediaries and harvesters of the mangrove crab Ucides cordatus (Linnaeus, 1763) in the Mamanguape River estuary, Brazil, and their socio-ecological implications","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0921800916302609","volume":"131","author":[{"family":"Nascimento","given":"Douglas Macêdo"},{"family":"Alves","given":"Rômulo Romeu Nóbrega"},{"family":"Barboza","given":"Raynner Rilke Duarte"},{"family":"Schmidt","given":"Anders Jensen"},{"family":"Diele","given":"Karen"},{"family":"Mourão","given":"José Silva"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,1]]}}},{"id":8794,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XL9HVNQA"],"itemData":{"id":8794,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The betel nut trade in Papua New Guinea is big business. Betel nut, a mild indigenous stimulant, is considered the ‘green gold of the grassroots’. It is the country's most significant domestic cash crop and, in terms of rural incomes, a rival to the dominant export cash crops. Its sale is an important livelihood strategy in both rural and urban areas, the most visible manifestation of a flourishing informal economy. In betel nut marketplaces money ‘flows’ and ‘overflows’, traders wield large wads of cash, and vast sums change hands. Whether seeking their fortunes or only tinned fish, people trade betel nut first and foremost to make money, but such interests in trade do not automatically displace other forms of value. This paper is concerned with marketplaces and trade in contemporary Papua New Guinea and what is conveyed in those transactions between buyer and seller. Against the often impersonal and utilitarian rendering of trade, this paper seeks to foreground the sociability of trade and the multiple forms of value that may be simultaneously attached to monetised market transactions. This is not to conceal the discrete, unenduring, and competitive dynamics of trade, which prominently feature in many betel nut transactions, but instead to examine an important dynamic often overlooked. Market transactions, far from being asocial, or even socially destructive, have the capacity to generate and sustain diverse social relations including those of kinship and friendship.","container-title":"Oceania","DOI":"10.1002/ocea.5116","ISSN":"1834-4461","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"© 2016 Oceania Publications","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1002/ocea.5116","page":"75-91","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Trade's Value: Relational Transactions in the Papua New Guinea Betel Nut Trade","title-short":"Trade's Value","URL":"http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/ocea.5116","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Sharp","given":"Timothy L. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, committed relation do not necessarily promote sustainable species use. In Indonesia, for instance, fisher’s use of blast fishing depends on their trust to a trader who can protect them from prosecution (see Box 4.31). In contrast, in relations in which commitment is lacking and strong power asymmetries due to gender differences or high indebtedness are present, exploitation and misconduct have been observed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sYc8jojG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Drury O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Neill, 2019; Matsue, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Drury O’Neill, 2019; Matsue, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8771,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SPJBYE6Q"],"itemData":{"id":8771,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Small-scale fishing communities are increasingly connected to international seafood trade via exports to a growing global market. Understanding how this connectedness impacts local fishery systems, both socially and ecologically, has become a necessary challenge for fisheries governance. Market prices are a potential mechanism by which global market demands are transferred to small-scale fishery actors. In most small-scale fisheries this happens through various traders (intermediaries, middlemen/women or patrons). By financing fishing operations, buying and selling products and transferring market information, traders can actively pass international market signals, such as price, to fishers. How these signals influence fishers' decisions and consequent fishing effort is still poorly understood yet significant for future social-ecological sustainability. The paper uses an economic framed field experiment, in combination with interviews, to shed light on this. It does so in the context of the Philippine patron-client 'suki' arrangement. Over 250 fishers in Concepcion, Iloilo were asked in an economic experiment, to make decisions about fuel loans in light of changing market prices. Interviews with participants and their patrons gathered additional information on relevant contextual variables potentially influencing borrowing. They included fisher characteristics and socio-economic conditions. Contrary to our hypotheses, fishers showed no response in their borrowing behaviour to experimental price changes. Instead, gender and the previous experiment round were predictive of their choice of loans in the experiment. We explore possible reasons for this.","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2019.00491","ISSN":"2296-7745","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Mar. Sci.","language":"English","note":"publisher: Frontiers","source":"Frontiers","title":"An Experimental Approach to Exploring Market Responses in Small-Scale Fishing Communities","URL":"http://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fmars.2019.00491/full","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Drury O’Neill","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Lindahl","given":"Therese"},{"family":"Daw","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Ferrer","given":"Alice Joan G."},{"family":"Pomeroy","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32641,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WSJKAG2Q"],"itemData":{"id":32641,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Coastal Management","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis","page":"531–554","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Women fish traders on the Kenyan coast: livelihoods, bargaining power, and participation in management","title-short":"Women fish traders on the Kenyan coast","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Matsue","given":"Naomi"},{"family":"Daw","given":"Tim"},{"family":"Garrett","given":"Lucy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Box 4.31. Trade relations in an Indonesian multi-species fishery

In the Spermonde (Indonesia) multi-species fishery, fisher-trader relations are highly influential ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zWBs0tF3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ferse, 2012; Glaser, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Ferse, 2012; Glaser, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1074,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KAG8PLBI"],"itemData":{"id":1074,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Collection of live coral for the marine aquarium trade has increased significantly in recent years, causing concerns over the ecological implications of this activity for the source regions. The Spermonde Archipelago off Southwest Sulawesi is one of the four major ornamental coral collection sites in Indonesia, the country supplying most corals in the trade. Management of the fishery remains ineffective, and overharvesting is widespread. Conservation strategies for marine resources increasingly emphasize the socioeconomic dimensions of resource use, yet little information is available on the livelihoods of fishermen involved in the ornamental fishery. Here, a first analysis of the livelihoods of coral fishermen in the Spermonde Archipelago is presented, along with a discussion of possibilities for future management interventions. The reef fishery of the study area is characterized by a high diversity of target organisms, with none of the fishermen relying exclusively on ornamental corals for income. Moreover, fishermen are organized in a tightly knit web of patron–client relationships, leaving little scope for them to initiate changes in livelihood strategies. The patron–client system emerged as a key institution of the fishery. In order to support sustainable marine resource use, management policies should simultaneously target the fishermen and their patrons as well as the regional coral trade network, broadening the livelihood portfolio of island communities and improving the regulation of the associated trade network.","container-title":"Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1080/08920753.2012.694801","ISSN":"0892-0753","issue":"5","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/08920753.2012.694801","page":"525-555","source":"Taylor and Francis+NEJM","title":"Livelihoods of Ornamental Coral Fishermen in South Sulawesi/Indonesia: Implications for Management","title-short":"Livelihoods of Ornamental Coral Fishermen in South Sulawesi/Indonesia","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/08920753.2012.694801","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."},{"family":"Knittweis","given":"Leyla"},{"family":"Krause","given":"Gesche"},{"family":"Maddusila","given":"Andi"},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",9,1]]}}},{"id":9011,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y3PTJYTR"],"itemData":{"id":9011,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"A review of a major community-based marine protected area programme (CB-MPA) in an Indonesian island archipelago is the point of departure for this article. Despite a well-designed institutional structure to facilitate local participation, local knowledge about the CB-MPA is found to be low and resource access and influence on decision-making in the programme is negligible for the majority of islanders. At the same time, most of those who know about the programme consider it as pertaining to the public authority only. These findings stand in contrast to evidence on non-formal ways of protecting and managing marine areas in the same geographical area but outside the formal MPA institutional framework. In particular, the article identifies a number of emergent rules-in-use in marine management, which operate parallel to legally established MPAs. It is argued that emergent forms of marine area protection such as non-formal self-organising island exclusion zones (IEZ) offer as yet mostly unused potentials for formal MPA development, particularly in those coastal and marine areas without traditional forms of marine and coastal management.","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2010.04.006","ISSN":"0308-597X","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","page":"1215-1225","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Whose sustainability? Top–down participation and emergent rules in marine protected area management in Indonesia","title-short":"Whose sustainability?","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308597X10000837","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Baitoningsih","given":"Wasistini"},{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."},{"family":"Neil","given":"Muhammad"},{"family":"Deswandi","given":"Rio"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2018",9,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, as in many other tropical small-scale fisheries (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SC70WXlm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ferrol-Schulte, 2014; Merlijn, 1989)","plainCitation":"(Ferrol-Schulte, 2014; Merlijn, 1989)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8781,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J5S2K5UV"],"itemData":{"id":8781,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Coastal and marine management cannot ignore the role of community livelihoods and vulnerability if it is to be effective and just, especially when the targeted coastal communities are highly resource-dependent. Previous studies have highlighted the potential role that patrons, or middlemen, can play as knowledge-brokers in marine natural resource management. On Zanzibar, where small-scale fishing is an important livelihood for many coastal communities, patron–client systems operate without regulation. This study investigates the roles patrons play regarding coastal livelihood vulnerability and natural resource management. It asks not only what patron–client relationships as institutions contribute to fishing households within the framework of the Sustainable Livelihoods Approach, but how these contributions differ across coastal villages. Furthermore, what do patron–client relationships mean for coastal livelihood vulnerability as well as marine natural resource management? Data was collected from 3 villages on Zanzibar with questionnaires, semi-structured key informant interviews, focus groups and participant observations. Although patrons have been identified in this and previous studies to be drivers of resource exploitation, they are also potential agents in identifying and activating sustainable solutions to environmental decline and improving fishing household resilience. Our results confirm previous findings that the role of patrons is mainly in providing fishing equipment and short-term relief in times of hardship, either through food or money. Our findings also indicate that there are small but significant differences in the type of contributions patrons make to coastal households.","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2014.07.016","ISSN":"0964-5691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","page":"63-73","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Patron–client relationships, livelihoods and natural resource management in tropical coastal communities","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0964569114002294","volume":"100","author":[{"family":"Ferrol-Schulte","given":"Daniella"},{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2016",12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",11]]}}},{"id":8713,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/33QA7QTY"],"itemData":{"id":8713,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Development and Change","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-7660.1989.tb00362.x","ISSN":"1467-7660","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"© 1989 Institute of Social Studies","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1467-7660.1989.tb00362.x","page":"683-700","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The Role of Middlemen in Small-scale Fisheries: A Case Study of Sarawak, Malaysia","title-short":"The Role of Middlemen in Small-scale Fisheries","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1467-7660.1989.tb00362.x","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Merlijn","given":"Alexander G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1989"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). The sustainability of fishing can be affected by fisher-trader relations through their influence on the reinforcement and emergence of fishing practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OBwvbikl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. Crona, 2010; Ferse, 2014)","plainCitation":"(B. Crona, 2010; Ferse, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9009,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/K3LP2G44"],"itemData":{"id":9009,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper analyzes the middlemen–fishermen link in coastal communities along the coast of southern Kenya and Zanzibar, and explores effects of reciprocal agreements and credit arrangements on social-ecological feedbacks of coastal systems. The existence and generality of such arrangements are mapped and their effect on resource use and ecosystem dynamics is then explored. Data show that credit arrangements are widespread and that fishermen are bound by reciprocal agreements and financial guarantees during periods of lower catches that provide short-term stabilizing social effects. These arrangements create incentives which disconnect resource extraction from ecosystem dynamics and impede development of sustainable use practices. The role of middlemen is seldom accounted for in fisheries governance. Scenarios for the development of small-scale fisheries in the region are outlined and the function of middlemen is discussed considering the influence of external drivers. Policies that incorporate middlemen are recommended to improve the governance of fish stocks and coastal ecosystems in East Africa.","collection-title":"Coping with global change in marine social-ecological systems","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2010.01.023","ISSN":"0308-597X","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","page":"761-771","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Middlemen, a critical social-ecological link in coastal communities of Kenya and Zanzibar","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308597X10000242","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Nyström","given":"Magnus"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Jiddawi","given":"Narriman"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2017",8,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",7,1]]}}},{"id":8795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6WNBIHG"],"itemData":{"id":8795,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Small-scale fisheries in coral reef areas support the livelihoods of millions of people worldwide. Anthropogenic impacts such as overfishing and climate change increasingly threaten both the reef ecosystem and the livelihood security of the people that depend on the reefs. Adaptive management strategies are needed to adequately deal with these threats, but they require an understanding of the underlying drivers, which often originate and act on multiple levels. Using a social-ecological system approach, the coral reef fishery of the Spermonde Archipelago in South Sulawesi/Indonesia is assessed to identify key drivers and strategic leverage points for management. Under the influence of international markets and technological changes, several export-oriented fisheries have developed in the area that led to distinct subsequent peaks in fishing activity in a pattern of sequential marine resource exploitation. In response to stressors such as seasonality and overfishing of individual locations or species, a number of coping strategies have developed locally. These include extensive borrowing from fishing patrons, diversification of fishing methods, fishing migrations, and the crafting of local institutions to regulate fishing activity. However, the coping strategies hinder, and even decrease, the capacity of the system to adapt to future stressors and undermine the sustainability of the fishery. Potential strategies that target different levels of the fishery system in order to strengthen adaptive management are identified.","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10113-012-0342-1","ISSN":"1436-378X","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Reg Environ Change","language":"en","page":"2053-2065","source":"Springer Link","title":"To cope or to sustain? Eroding long-term sustainability in an Indonesian coral reef fishery","title-short":"To cope or to sustain?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10113-012-0342-1","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Neil","given":"Muhammad"},{"family":"Schwerdtner Máñez","given":"Kathleen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Schematically, two mechanisms shape fisher-trader relations' influence on fishing practices: first, interactions within the relation going beyond the exchange of fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"B6H8icAr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pelras, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Pelras, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8715,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8KDKWAKC"],"itemData":{"id":8715,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Bijdragen tot de Taal-, Land-en Volkenkunde","issue":"3","page":"393–432","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Patron-client ties among the Bugis and Makassarese of South Sulawesi","volume":"156","author":[{"family":"Pelras","given":"Christian"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and, second, fishers and traders' relations with other fishery actors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SWVPudRL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Radjawali, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Radjawali, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8714,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W9KLBL9W"],"itemData":{"id":8714,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Indonesian Social Sciences and Humanities","page":"67–102","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Social networks and the live reef food fish trade: examining sustainability","title-short":"Social networks and the live reef food fish trade","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Radjawali","given":"Irendra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  The fishing practices fisher-trader relations enable cannot a priori be classified as sustainable or unsustainable. Fishing migrations can reduce pressure on locally overfished marine populations but also expand depletion of fisheries elsewhere ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZK2LHYcb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2006a; Merino, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2006a; Merino, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":863,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q57T5MS7"],"itemData":{"id":863,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Marine resource exploitation can deplete stocks faster than regulatory agencies can respond. Institutions with broad authority and a global perspective are needed to create a system with incentives for conservation.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1122804","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5767","language":"en","license":"© 2006 American Association for the Advancement of Science","note":"PMID: 16543444","page":"1557-1558","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"Globalization, Roving Bandits, and Marine Resources","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/311/5767/1557","volume":"311","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"T. P."},{"family":"Steneck","given":"R. S."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"J. A."},{"family":"Bellwood","given":"D. R."},{"family":"Crona","given":"B."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"Gunderson","given":"L. H."},{"family":"Leslie","given":"H. M."},{"family":"Norberg","given":"J."},{"family":"Nyström","given":"M."},{"family":"Olsson","given":"P."},{"family":"Österblom","given":"H."},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"M."},{"family":"Worm","given":"B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",3,17]]}}},{"id":9012,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D5WN6DEC"],"itemData":{"id":9012,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Global marine fisheries production has reached a maximum and may even be declining. Underlying this trend is a well-understood sequence of development, overexploitation, depletion and in some instances collapse of individual fish stocks, a pattern that can sequentially link geographically distant populations. Ineffective governance, economic considerations and climate impacts are often responsible for this sequence, although the relative contribution of each factor is contentious. In this paper we use a global bioeconomic model to explore the synergistic effects of climate variability, economic pressures and management measures in causing or avoiding this sequence. The model shows how a combination of climate-induced variability in the underlying fish population production, particular patterns of demand for fish products and inadequate management is capable of driving the world’s fisheries into development, overexploitation, collapse and recovery phases consistent with observations. Furthermore, it demonstrates how a sequential pattern of overexploitation can emerge as an endogenous property of the interaction between regional environmental fluctuations and a globalized trade system. This situation is avoidable through adaptive management measures that ensure the sustainability of regional production systems in the face of increasing global environmental change and markets. It is concluded that global management measures are needed to ensure that global food supply from marine products is optimized while protecting long-term ecosystem services across the world’s oceans.","container-title":"Scientia Marina","DOI":"10.3989/scimar.2011.75n4779","ISSN":"1886-8134","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"Copyright (c) 2011 Consejo Superior de Investigaciones Científicas (CSIC)","note":"number: 4","page":"779-790","source":"scientiamarina.revistas.csic.es.ezp.sub.su.se","title":"Modelling the sequential geographical exploitation and potential collapse of marine fisheries through economic globalization, climate change and management alternatives","URL":"http://scientiamarina.revistas.csic.es/index.php/scientiamarina/article/view/1300","volume":"75","author":[{"family":"Merino","given":"Gorka"},{"family":"Barange","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Rodwell","given":"Lynda"},{"family":"Mullon","given":"Christian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",7,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. To enable fishing migrations, the trader provides large credits to the fisher ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TNisi5yF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ferse, 2014; Navarrete Forero, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Ferse, 2014; Navarrete Forero, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6WNBIHG"],"itemData":{"id":8795,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Small-scale fisheries in coral reef areas support the livelihoods of millions of people worldwide. Anthropogenic impacts such as overfishing and climate change increasingly threaten both the reef ecosystem and the livelihood security of the people that depend on the reefs. Adaptive management strategies are needed to adequately deal with these threats, but they require an understanding of the underlying drivers, which often originate and act on multiple levels. Using a social-ecological system approach, the coral reef fishery of the Spermonde Archipelago in South Sulawesi/Indonesia is assessed to identify key drivers and strategic leverage points for management. Under the influence of international markets and technological changes, several export-oriented fisheries have developed in the area that led to distinct subsequent peaks in fishing activity in a pattern of sequential marine resource exploitation. In response to stressors such as seasonality and overfishing of individual locations or species, a number of coping strategies have developed locally. These include extensive borrowing from fishing patrons, diversification of fishing methods, fishing migrations, and the crafting of local institutions to regulate fishing activity. However, the coping strategies hinder, and even decrease, the capacity of the system to adapt to future stressors and undermine the sustainability of the fishery. Potential strategies that target different levels of the fishery system in order to strengthen adaptive management are identified.","container-title":"Regional Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1007/s10113-012-0342-1","ISSN":"1436-378X","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Reg Environ Change","language":"en","page":"2053-2065","source":"Springer Link","title":"To cope or to sustain? Eroding long-term sustainability in an Indonesian coral reef fishery","title-short":"To cope or to sustain?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10113-012-0342-1","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Neil","given":"Muhammad"},{"family":"Schwerdtner Máñez","given":"Kathleen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12,1]]}}},{"id":9005,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7G29QC7F"],"itemData":{"id":9005,"type":"thesis","event-place":"Bremen","language":"en","number-of-pages":"80","publisher":"University of Bremen","publisher-place":"Bremen","source":"Zotero","title":"Location, use and selection of fishing grounds in Spermonde Archipelago, Indonesia: the case of hook and line fishers from Badi Island","author":[{"family":"Navarrete Forero","given":"Gabriela"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. During the migration fishers would sell fish at sea and return after several months to repay the credit to the trader. Traders need to trust the fishers whom they loan vast credits. Trust either derives from kin relations or a history of working successfully together ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FtEvRXQT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Acciaioli, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Acciaioli, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":29448,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RSCGKDJ7"],"itemData":{"id":29448,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"<section class=\\"abstract\\"></section>","container-title":"Bijdragen tot de taal-, land- en volkenkunde / Journal of the Humanities and Social Sciences of Southeast Asia","DOI":"10.1163/22134379-90003841","ISSN":"0006-2294, 2213-4379","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"publisher: Brill\\nsection: Bijdragen tot de taal-, land- en volkenkunde / Journal of the Humanities and Social Sciences of Southeast Asia","page":"588-617","source":"brill.com","title":"Kinship and debt; The social organization of Bugis migration and fish marketing at Lake Lindu, Central Sulawesi","URL":"https://brill.com/view/journals/bki/156/3/article-p588_10.xml","volume":"156","author":[{"family":"Acciaioli","given":"Greg"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}).  In Spermonde, fisher-trader relations also enable destructive fishing (e.g. blast fishing, cyanide fishing) which negatively impacts marine populations and their reef habitats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"v9sKxbL1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mous, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Mous, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9006,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J62BX4WM"],"itemData":{"id":9006,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Collected essays on the economics of coral reefs. Kalmar, Sweden: CORDIO, Kalmar University","page":"69–76","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Cyanide fishing on Indonesian coral reefs for the live food fish market-what is the problem","author":[{"family":"Mous","given":"P."},{"family":"Pet-Soede","given":"Lida"},{"family":"Erdmann","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Cesar","given":"H."},{"family":"Sadovy","given":"Yvonne"},{"family":"Pet","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. To enable destructive fishing, traders maintain relations with high-level authorities to circumvent enforcement of fishing regulations that ban destructive fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"K89ovhDP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nurdin & Grydeh\\\\uc0\\\\u248{}j, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Nurdin & Grydehøj, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9004,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I554LACQ"],"itemData":{"id":9004,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Efforts to preserve fragile ecosystems that focus on removing human intervention from the environment risk ignoring the political and social systems underlying environmentally destructive economic activities. In contrast, a biocultural diversity perspective allows for environmental protection to be approached with sensitivity to human needs. This paper explores the case of Karanrang Island, Spermonde Archipelago, South Sulawesi, Indonesia, where fishing with toxins and bombs is proving detrimental to fish stocks and the surrounding coral reefs. Interviews with Karanrang fishers reveal that these destructive fishing practices are bound up with the region’s punggawa-sawi political and social system of patron–client relationships. The paper shows how the informal governance operating through these patron–client relationships traps fishers into destructive fishing practices. It is argued that environmental protection efforts should take into account political and social contexts.","container-title":"Journal of Marine and Island Cultures","DOI":"10.1016/j.imic.2014.11.003","ISSN":"2212-6821","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Marine and Island Cultures","page":"54-59","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Informal governance through patron–client relationships and destructive fishing in Spermonde Archipelago, Indonesia","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2212682114000237","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Nurdin","given":"Nurliah"},{"family":"Grydehøj","given":"Adam"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Through this relation, the trader can guarantee to protect the fisher from prosecution ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bILBS0FD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Radjawali, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Radjawali, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9007,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GVZ2BHQW"],"itemData":{"id":9007,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Reconsidering Development","ISSN":"2474-9117","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"1","language":"en","license":"Copyright (c)","source":"pubs.lib.umn.edu","title":"Coping with Uncertainties: Live Reef Food Fish (LRFF) Trade in Spermonde Archipelago, Indonesia","title-short":"Coping with Uncertainties","URL":"https://pubs.lib.umn.edu/index.php/reconsidering/article/view/901","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Radjawali","given":"Irendra"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",2,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In consequence, fishers who work for a trader with such connections could use destructive fishing without risk of punishment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"g4PxaKyC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Glaser, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Glaser, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9008,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/57T5M2FU"],"itemData":{"id":9008,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper arises from a four-year Indonesian-German research cooperation on the governance and management of Indonesian coastal and marine ecosystems. Project objectives were to investigate coastal and marine social-ecological dynamics and feedbacks and to analyse socio-political and institutional structures and processes in order to support adaptive coastal governance. Participating researchers worked in the Spermonde Archipelago, off South Sulawesi, Indonesia, between 2007 and 2010. Methods included ship-based research excursions, several classical surveys, anthropological participant observation, and participatory research methods applied by an interdisciplinary social-natural science team. This paper summarises our findings and draws policy conclusions. First, we discuss Marine Protected Areas and participation focussing on local “rules-in-use”. In addition, reef exploitation and local livelihoods, in particular fisheries and mariculture, and the existing social networks and hierarchies in fisheries are explored to understand social vulnerability, resilience and marine resource governance in the context of the Spermonde Archipelago. An outline of major policy recommendations concludes this article.","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2015.07.022","ISSN":"0964-5691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","page":"193-213","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Of exploited reefs and fishers – A holistic view on participatory coastal and marine management in an Indonesian archipelago","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0964569115002124","volume":"116","author":[{"family":"Glaser","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Breckwoldt","given":"Annette"},{"family":"Deswandi","given":"Rio"},{"family":"Radjawali","given":"Irendra"},{"family":"Baitoningsih","given":"Wasistini"},{"family":"Ferse","given":"Sebastian C. A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2018",9,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.


	A nascent literature has started drawing first links between global trade relations and sustainable use of wild species. This literature highlights that the structure and dynamics of trade relations and trade networks matter, that multiple trade relations allow to divert trade routes from source to destination ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rEYCnZVb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stoll, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Stoll, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32643,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UQWG6VR4"],"itemData":{"id":32643,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Reliance on international seafood markets leaves small-scale fishers and fishing economies vulnerable to distant disturbances that can negatively affect market prices and trigger social, economic, and environmental crises at local levels. This paper examines the role of seafood trade routes and re-exports in masking such market linkages. We employ a network approach to map the global trade routes of lobster (Homarus spp.) from small-scale producers in North America to terminal markets and evaluate the extent to which intermediary nations act to obscure producer-market relationships. In taking this approach, we provide a method for systematically measuring “teleconnectivity” created through seafood trade routes, and thus making explicit vulnerabilities to small-scale fisheries from this teleconnectivity. Our empirical analysis shows that the perceived trade diversification of lobster producers is masking increased dependencies on a reduced number of end-markets, particularly in Asia. These results suggest, paradoxically, that the apparent diversification of trade partnerships may actually amplify, rather than reduce, the vulnerabilities of small-scale fishers associated with international trade by making risk harder to identify and anticipate. We discuss our results in the context of local fisheries and global seafood trade and describe key impediments to being able to monitor market dependencies and exposure to potential vulnerabilities.","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","ISSN":"2296-7745","source":"Frontiers","title":"Seafood Trade Routes for Lobster Obscure Teleconnected Vulnerabilities","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fmars.2018.00239","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Stoll","given":"Joshua S."},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice I."},{"family":"Fabinyi","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Farr","given":"Emily R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and that links to global traders changes local trade relations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zetfeV1q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wamukota, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Wamukota, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9010,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CBT2J5PY"],"itemData":{"id":9010,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This study, carried out in five fishing communities along the Kenyan coast, examined fisheries-derived income of fishers and traders in two different invertebrate fisheries (octopus and sea cucumber) and tested if differences in global market integration of these two products could explain differences in income inequalities among actors involved in the two fisheries. The structure of the value chains was mapped, differences in income between fishers and traders tested, and income inequalities among actors in each fishery examined. Although the octopus fishery included a greater diversity of actors and thereby provides income to a larger group of people, income inequality in this fishery was higher among fishers and traders than in the sea cucumber fishery. Thus, the often cited relationship between increasing market integration and income inequality may require a re-evaluation and a more nuanced treatment.","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2014.03.013","ISSN":"0308-597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"93-101","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Market integration and its relation to income distribution and inequality among fishers and traders: The case of two small-scale Kenyan reef fisheries","title-short":"Market integration and its relation to income distribution and inequality among fishers and traders","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308597X14000852","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Wamukota","given":"A."},{"family":"Brewer","given":"T. D."},{"family":"Crona","given":"B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",9,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Along global value chains there are different compositions of trade relations, for instance, there might be few exporters interacting with many local middle(wo)men ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"o9hDtid3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Purcell, 2017; zu Ermgassen, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Purcell, 2017; zu Ermgassen, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1560,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LLM7VMX2"],"itemData":{"id":1560,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Small-scale fishers are often believed to receive marginal earnings for seafood relative to other value- chain actors but proportionate incomes across different traded species are rarely compared. This study compares value chains for 15 species of sea cucumbers between Fiji and Kiribati using data collected on sale prices of dried products (bêche-de-mer) from fishers to middlemen and exporters, export prices and market retail prices in China. Pacific islanders comprised almost all fishers, but represented only some middlemen and few exporters. Proportional increases in prices along the value chains differed greatly among sea cucumber species and between countries. Fishers’ earnings varied greatly among species. The relative share of the end market value they received was negatively related to product end-market value; on average 50% of the end retail value for the lowest-value species but <10% for the highest-value species. Most fishers lacked information about market prices. The gross markup of exporters differed greatly between the two countries. Downstream actors reaped increasingly higher proportions of the product value for higher value species. Variation in sale prices between countries and fishers for the same product indicates a potential for higher earnings to fishers. Improved transparency of prices to fishers could empower them to negotiate higher prices, especially for more valuable species. Upgrading of value-chain governance, e.g. through fisher cooperatives or auction systems, could improve efficiency and fisher incomes, potentially reducing the need for high fishing rates. Such interventions will benefit from understanding the value-chain patterns among different species harvested in multispecies fisheries.","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2017.09.001","ISSN":"0308-597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"9-16","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Distribution of economic returns in small-scale fisheries for international markets: A value-chain analysis","title-short":"Distribution of economic returns in small-scale fisheries for international markets","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308597X17304244","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Purcell","given":"Steven W."},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice I."},{"family":"Lalavanua","given":"Watisoni"},{"family":"Eriksson","given":"Hampus"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12,1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32616,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YQZPF7UU"],"itemData":{"id":32616,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Research Letters","issue":"3","note":"publisher: IOP Publishing","page":"035003","title":"Using supply chain data to monitor zero deforestation commitments: an assessment of progress in the Brazilian soy sector","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Ermgassen","given":"Erasmus KHJ","non-dropping-particle":"zu"},{"family":"Ayre","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Godar","given":"Javier"},{"family":"Lima","given":"Mairon G Bastos"},{"family":"Bauch","given":"Simone"},{"family":"Garrett","given":"Rachael"},{"family":"Green","given":"Jonathan"},{"family":"Lathuillière","given":"Michael J"},{"family":"Löfgren","given":"Pernilla"},{"family":"MacFarquhar","given":"Christina"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The resulting global trade network structure and its dynamics can be shaped by the expansion of exploitation of a particular species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fFEh5jql","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Anderson, 2011; Berkes, 2006b; Eriksson, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Anderson, 2011; Berkes, 2006b; Eriksson, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"Uebb2sCb/Jz0FkYEt","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/local/L7afBCgj/items/CRUSZF5M"],"itemData":{"id":2006,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract In recent decades, invertebrate fisheries have expanded in catch and value worldwide. One increasingly harvested group is sea cucumbers (class Holothuroidea), which are highly valued in Asia and sold as trepang or bêche-de-mer. We compiled global landings, economic data, and country-specific assessment and management reports to synthesize global trends in sea cucumber fisheries, evaluate potential drivers, and test for local and global serial exploitation patterns. Although some sea cucumber fisheries have existed for centuries, catch trends of most individual fisheries followed boom-and-bust patterns since the 1950s, declining nearly as quickly as they expanded. New fisheries expanded five to six times faster in 1990 compared to 1960 and at an increasing distance from Asia, encompassing a global fishery by the 1990s. Global sea cucumber production was correlated to the Japanese yen at a leading lag. Regional assessments revealed that population declines from overfishing occurred in 81% of sea cucumber fisheries, average harvested body size declined in 35%, harvesters moved from near- to off-shore regions in 51% and from high- to low-value species in 76%. Thirty-eight per cent of sea cucumber fisheries remained unregulated, and illegal catches were of concern in half. Our results suggest that development patterns of sea cucumber fisheries are largely predictable, often unsustainable and frequently too rapid for effective management responses. We discuss potential ecosystem and human community consequences and urge for better monitoring and reporting of catch and abundance, proper scientific stock assessment and consideration of international trade regulations to ensure long-term and sustainable harvesting of sea cucumbers worldwide.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/j.1467-2979.2010.00397.x","ISSN":"1467-2960","issue":"3","page":"317–339","title":"Serial exploitation of global sea cucumber fisheries","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Anderson","given":"Sean C"},{"family":"Flemming","given":"Joanna Mills"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Reg"},{"family":"Lotze","given":"Heike K"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",9]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/iKYBj8UU","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/local/L7afBCgj/items/FWI6YZB2"],"itemData":{"id":745,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Marine resource exploitation can deplete stocks faster than regulatory agencies can respond. Institutions with broad authority and a global perspective are needed to create a system with incentives for conservation.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1122804","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"5767","language":"en","note":"PMID: 16543444","page":"1557–1558","title":"Globalization, Roving Bandits, and Marine Resources","volume":"311","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Hughes","given":"T. P."},{"family":"Steneck","given":"R. S."},{"family":"Wilson","given":"J. A."},{"family":"Bellwood","given":"D. R."},{"family":"Crona","given":"B."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."},{"family":"Gunderson","given":"L. H."},{"family":"Leslie","given":"H. M."},{"family":"Norberg","given":"J."},{"family":"Nyström","given":"M."},{"family":"Olsson","given":"P."},{"family":"Österblom","given":"H."},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"M."},{"family":"Worm","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006",3]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/K2r3MGlN","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/local/L7afBCgj/items/WAIZQKQ4"],"itemData":{"id":8541,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Global seafood sourcing networks are expanding to meet demand. To describe contemporary fishery expansion patterns, we analyzed the worldwide exploitation of sea cucumber (Echinodermata: Holothuroidea) traded via Hong Kong for consumers in China. In just 15 years (1996–2011), the sea cucumber sourcing network expanded from 35 to 83 countries; sea cucumber fisheries serving the Chinese market now operate within countries cumulatively spanning over 90% of the world's tropical coastlines. The emergence of such fisheries in nations where they were previously absent could not be explained either by their national governance capacity or by their distance from Hong Kong. Surging imports from these new fisheries have compensated for declines in long-standing fisheries elsewhere. The case of commercial sea cucumber trade for the Chinese market exemplifies a new global extraction phenomenon that we call contagious resource exploitation – a fast-moving system resembling a disease epidemic, where long-distance transport expedites large-scale expansion followed by diffusive local spread into neighboring areas. Multi-level and multi-scale decision making is urgently needed to control and mitigate the effects of contagious exploitation.","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","DOI":"10.1890/140312","ISSN":"1540-9309","issue":"8","language":"en","page":"435–440","title":"Contagious exploitation of marine resources","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Eriksson","given":"Hampus"},{"family":"Österblom","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Troell","given":"Max"},{"family":"Andrew","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Wilen","given":"James"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Expansion can help cater increasing demand but also mask declines of wild populations in one area ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZvkRwWMy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. I. Crona, 2016)","plainCitation":"(B. I. Crona, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":1269,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GLDRX6CK"],"itemData":{"id":1269,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Fish and fish-related products are among the most highly traded commodities globally and the proportion of globally harvested fish that is internationally traded has steadily risen over time. Views on the benefits of international seafood trade diverge, partly as a result from adopting either an aggregate national focus or a focus on local market actors. However, both views generally assume that the trade in question is characterized by export of fisheries resources to international markets. This is potentially misleading as empirical evidence suggests that import of seafood can also have impacts on local SSF dynamics. A systematic analysis of the different ways in which local production systems connect to international seafood markets can therefore help shed more light on why small-scale fisheries exhibit such differences in outcomes as they engage in an increasingly global seafood trade. This paper conducts a synthesis across 24 cases from around the world and develops a typology of small-scale fisheries and how they connect to and interact with international seafood trade. The analysis is based on key features drawn from trade theory regarding how trade interacts with local production. The implications of the findings for social and ecological sustainability of small-scale fisheries are discussed with the aim of identifying further research topics which deserve attention to better inform trade policy for more sustainable fisheries and more just wealth distribution from their trade.","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2015.11.016","ISSN":"0308-597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"1-10","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Towards a typology of interactions between small-scale fisheries and global seafood trade","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308597X15003607","volume":"65","author":[{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice I."},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Squires","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Daw","given":"Tim M."},{"family":"Khan","given":"Ahmed"},{"family":"Havice","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Chomo","given":"Victoria"},{"family":"Troell","given":"Max"},{"family":"Buchary","given":"Eny A."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The emerging global network structure has consequences for future propagation. For instance, the presence of multiple trade relations enables diverting trade routes from source to destination to avoid trade barriers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pxGACVuP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Stoll, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Stoll, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32643,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UQWG6VR4"],"itemData":{"id":32643,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Reliance on international seafood markets leaves small-scale fishers and fishing economies vulnerable to distant disturbances that can negatively affect market prices and trigger social, economic, and environmental crises at local levels. This paper examines the role of seafood trade routes and re-exports in masking such market linkages. We employ a network approach to map the global trade routes of lobster (Homarus spp.) from small-scale producers in North America to terminal markets and evaluate the extent to which intermediary nations act to obscure producer-market relationships. In taking this approach, we provide a method for systematically measuring “teleconnectivity” created through seafood trade routes, and thus making explicit vulnerabilities to small-scale fisheries from this teleconnectivity. Our empirical analysis shows that the perceived trade diversification of lobster producers is masking increased dependencies on a reduced number of end-markets, particularly in Asia. These results suggest, paradoxically, that the apparent diversification of trade partnerships may actually amplify, rather than reduce, the vulnerabilities of small-scale fishers associated with international trade by making risk harder to identify and anticipate. We discuss our results in the context of local fisheries and global seafood trade and describe key impediments to being able to monitor market dependencies and exposure to potential vulnerabilities.","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","ISSN":"2296-7745","source":"Frontiers","title":"Seafood Trade Routes for Lobster Obscure Teleconnected Vulnerabilities","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fmars.2018.00239","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Stoll","given":"Joshua S."},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice I."},{"family":"Fabinyi","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Farr","given":"Emily R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. High connectivity in trade networks can enable supply shocks to propagate through redistribution of sourcing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"P8MkmkHx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gephart, 2016; Tu, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Gephart, 2016; Tu, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8792,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ETTWJ5PX"],"itemData":{"id":8792,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Trade can allow countries to overcome local or regional losses (shocks) to their food supply, but reliance on international food trade also exposes countries to risks from external perturbations. Countries that are nutritionally or economically dependent on international trade of a commodity may be adversely affected by such shocks. While exposure to shocks has been studied in financial markets, communication networks, and some infrastructure systems, it has received less attention in food-trade networks. Here, we develop a forward shock-propagation model to quantify how trade flows are redistributed under a range of shock scenarios and assess the food-security outcomes by comparing changes in national fish supplies to indices of each country’s nutritional fish dependency. Shock propagation and distribution among regions are modeled on a network of historical bilateral seafood trade data from UN Comtrade using 205 reporting territories grouped into 18 regions. In our model exposure to shocks increases with total imports and the number of import partners. We find that Central and West Africa are the most vulnerable to shocks, with their vulnerability increasing when a willingness-to-pay proxy is included. These findings suggest that countries can reduce their overall vulnerability to shocks by reducing reliance on imports and diversifying food sources. As international seafood trade grows, identifying these types of potential risks and vulnerabilities is important to build a more resilient food system.","container-title":"Environmental Research Letters","DOI":"10.1088/1748-9326/11/3/035008","ISSN":"1748-9326","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Environ. Res. Lett.","language":"en","page":"035008","source":"Institute of Physics","title":"Vulnerability to shocks in the global seafood trade network","URL":"http://stacks.iop.org/1748-9326/11/i=3/a=035008","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Gephart","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Rovenskaya","given":"Elena"},{"family":"Dieckmann","given":"Ulf"},{"family":"Pace","given":"Michael L."},{"family":"Brännström","given":"Åke"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2017",4,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32710,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4CK3AHN9"],"itemData":{"id":32710,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The movement of goods links consumers and producers of natural resources in a web of interactions. This study finds that the resilience of a food trade network depends on interconnectedness and that the increasing connectivity of global food trade is making it less resilient, including to supply shocks.","container-title":"Nature Sustainability","DOI":"10.1038/s41893-019-0260-z","ISSN":"2398-9629","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Sustain","language":"en","license":"2019 The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Limited","page":"283-289","source":"www-nature-com.ezp.sub.su.se","title":"Impact of globalization on the resilience and sustainability of natural resources","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41893-019-0260-z","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Tu","given":"Chengyi"},{"family":"Suweis","given":"Samir"},{"family":"D’Odorico","given":"Paolo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",2,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and have been associated with unsustainable biomass levels of fish populations. Access to international market value chains can affect local trade relations. For example, they allow diversification of harvested species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dmzQw124","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. G. Abbott, 2015)","plainCitation":"(J. G. Abbott, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":29481,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WI2DVI98"],"itemData":{"id":29481,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This case study of a fish market near the border of Namibia and Zambia examines how economic and environmental changes have affected the marketing channels associated with fishery in southern Africa. We monitored the volume and origin of fish entering the market in Katima Mulilo, Namibia, between 2007 and 2012, and conducted semi-structured interviews with fish committee members and market vendors. Prior to 2010, the market for fish had a simple commodity chain, involving local producers and consumers. Since then, a nearby lake has refilled, providing a new source of fish. The market has expanded and diversified, with large volumes of fish now being sent to consumers in urban centres in Zambia and even to the Democratic Republic of Congo. We consider the sustainability of the fishery in relation to expansion and re-alignment of the marketing channel and the degree to which intra-regional trade in fish promotes or hampers food security and sustainable use of the resource.","container-title":"Journal of Southern African Studies","DOI":"10.1080/03057070.2015.991619","ISSN":"0305-7070","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Routledge\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/03057070.2015.991619","page":"29-45","source":"Taylor and Francis+NEJM","title":"Rain and Copper: The Evolution of a Fish Marketing Channel in a Rapidly Changing Region of Southern Africa","title-short":"Rain and Copper","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/03057070.2015.991619","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Abbott","given":"James G."},{"family":"Hay","given":"Clinton J."},{"family":"Næsje","given":"Tor F."},{"family":"Tweddle","given":"Denis"},{"family":"Waal","given":"Ben C. W.","dropping-particle":"van der"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, can increase income for harvesters ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"i0dDIaXX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Elsler, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Elsler, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8780,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NBJF7MG4"],"itemData":{"id":8780,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Price and market structures in fisheries change rapidly, now 40% of seafood is traded internationally and are associated with overharvesting of marine species. We have developed a bio-economic fishery model to address the pressing need of managing the interplay of different markets. We first regard local, multi-level and global markets individually and then analyze the effect of transitioning between markets on the exploitation of species and the stability of income. We find that in gradually globalizing markets, transition management needs to account for non-linear price changes since earlier policies may not be suitable after globalization. We hypothesize that short-term policies to ban harvest in the interest of species recovery benefit a local market in which incentives prevent overharvesting. In global markets we expect that sustained initiatives are needed to prevent overharvesting. Individual fisheries using contextualized models representing local ecological and trade structures may benefit from assessing the price dynamics presented in this analysis.","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2018.09.008","ISSN":"0921-8009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","page":"185-195","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Local, Global, Multi-Level: Market Structure and Multi-Species Fishery Dynamics","title-short":"Local, Global, Multi-Level","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0921800918303963","volume":"156","author":[{"family":"Elsler","given":"Laura G."},{"family":"Drohan","given":"Sarah E."},{"family":"Schlüter","given":"Maja"},{"family":"Watson","given":"James R."},{"family":"Levin","given":"Simon A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",2,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and reduce incentives for collective action ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"McHzcF1V","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Bennett & Basurto, 2018a)","plainCitation":"(A. Bennett & Basurto, 2018a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2936,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4P9ES7U6"],"itemData":{"id":2936,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","language":"en","page":"57–70","title":"Local institutional responses to global market pressures: The sea cucumber trade in Yucatán, Mexico","volume":"102","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"A."},{"family":"Basurto","given":"X."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This last point is particularly important because self-governing strategies may shift from collective towards more individual based harvest and selling ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kaDvAzGu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Bennett & Basurto, 2018b; Frawley, 2019; Lindkvist, 2017)","plainCitation":"(A. Bennett & Basurto, 2018b; Frawley, 2019; Lindkvist, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"Uebb2sCb/FP2ZT9zz","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/local/L7afBCgj/items/VCGBUTG3"],"itemData":{"id":11236,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","note":"ISBN: 0305-750X\\npublisher: Elsevier","page":"57-70","title":"Local institutional responses to global market pressures: the sea cucumber trade in Yucatán, Mexico","volume":"102","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"Abigail"},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32645,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/24RY2XPK"],"itemData":{"id":32645,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"1","note":"publisher: JSTOR","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Environmental and institutional degradation in the globalized economy","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Frawley","given":"Timothy H."},{"family":"Finkbeiner","given":"Elena M."},{"family":"Crowder","given":"Larry B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":8779,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8VVJLN79"],"itemData":{"id":8779,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Small-scale fisheries (SSFs) in developing countries are expected to play a significant role in poverty alleviation and enhancing food security in the decades to come. To realize this expectation, a better understanding of their informal self-governance arrangements is critical for developing policies that can improve fishers’ livelihoods and lead to sustainable ecosystem stewardship. The goal of this paper is to develop a more nuanced understanding of micro-level factors—such as fishers’ characteristics and behavior—to explain observed differences in self-governance arrangements in Northwest Mexico. We focus on two ubiquitous forms of self-governance: hierarchical non-cooperative arrangements between fishers and fishbuyers, such as patron-client relationships (PCs), versus more cooperative arrangements amongst fishers, such as fishing cooperatives (co-ops). We developed an agent-based model of an archetypical SSF that captures key hypotheses from in-depth fieldwork in Northwest Mexico of fishers’ day-to-day fishing and trading. Results from our model indicate that high diversity in fishers’ reliability, and low initial trust between co-op members, makes co-ops’ establishment difficult. PCs cope better with this kind of diversity because, in contrast to co-ops, they have more flexibility in choosing whom to work with. However, once co-ops establish, they cope better with seasonal variability in fish abundance and provide long-term security for the fishers. We argue that existing levels of trust and diversity among fishers matter for different self-governance arrangements to establish and persist, and should therefore be taken into account when developing better, targeted policies for improved SSFs governance.","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0175532","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"PLOS ONE","language":"en","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e0175532","source":"PLoS Journals","title":"Micro-level explanations for emergent patterns of self-governance arrangements in small-scale fisheries—A modeling approach","URL":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0175532","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Lindkvist","given":"Emilie"},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Schlüter","given":"Maja"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",4,13]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	
Wild species trade 
Wild species trade is any commercial exchange (involving money or barter) by people of wild animals, fungi, and plant (including algae) resources, both at local levels and across legal jurisdictions and international borders. Wild species trade can be legal or illegal, formal or informal; domestic or international, and can result in a sustainable or unsustainable use of animal and plant species. Wild resources are traded in many forms to produce a wide variety of products such as homeware, healthcare (including traditional medicines), food, cosmetics, ornaments, furniture, pets, fiber, and building supplies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Zrc71xt0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. M. Lee, 2020; \\\\uc0\\\\u8216{}t Sas-Rolfes, 2019a)","plainCitation":"(T. M. Lee, 2020; ‘t Sas-Rolfes, 2019a)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32519,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XL3BAZS3"],"itemData":{"id":32519,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Bushmeat is not only an important source of fat, micronutrients, and macronutrients, but it also has medicinal uses. Extensive human–wildlife interactions may lead to pathogen exchange and trigger zoonotic infectious disease outbreaks such as severe acute respiratory syndrome, Ebola, and coronavirus disease 2019. In the tropics, bushmeat has become one of the most threatened resources due to widespread habitat loss and overexploitation, largely driven by increased global demand, weak governance, and lack of enforcement. Unsustainable harvesting, consumption, and production practices are common, although drivers are complex and intertwined and vary regionally, pointing to a looming rural nutrition security and wildlife conservation issue. Growing demand in fast urbanizing markets coupled with easy access fuels the illegal trade of bushmeat, medicinal products, and wildlife-based luxury goods. Although bushmeat contributes significantly to rural people's income and poverty alleviation, overharvesting impacts those who are most dependent on the forest. To balance the rural and culturalimportance of bushmeat with conservation and public health priorities, strategies to safeguard tropical biodiversity, sustainable harvest of wildlife with reduced health risk for nutrition and medicine are urgently needed.","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-102016-060827","issue":"1","note":"_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-102016-060827","page":"145-170","source":"Annual Reviews","title":"The Harvest of Tropical Wildlife for Bushmeat and Traditional Medicine","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-102016-060827","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Lee","given":"Tien Ming"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/LB4zoP0C","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L75NL24S"],"itemData":{"id":1771,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Illegal wildlife trade (IWT) has increased in profile in recent years as a global policy issue, largely because of its association with declines in prominent internationally trafficked species. In this review, we explore the scale of IWT, associated threats to biodiversity, and appropriate responses to these threats. We discuss the historical development of IWT research and highlight the uncertainties that plague the evidence base, emphasizing the need for more systematic approaches to addressing evidence gaps in a way that minimizes the risk of unethical or counterproductive outcomes for wildlife and people. We highlight the need for evaluating interventions in order to learn, and the importance of sharing datasets and lessons learned. A more collaborative approach to linking IWT research, practice, and policy would better align public policy discourse and action with research evidence. This in turn would enable more effective policy making that contributes to reducing the threat to biodiversity that IWT represents. Expected final online publication date for the Annual Review of Environment and Resources, Volume 44 is October 17, 2019. Please see http://www.annualreviews.org/page/journal/pubdates for revised estimates.","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033253","ISSN":"1543-5938","title":"Illegal Wildlife Trade: Patterns, Processes, and Governance","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033253","author":[{"family":"‘t Sas-Rolfes","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Challender","given":"Daniel W S"},{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Diogo"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]],"season":"août"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Several wild species are hunted around the wild for their perceived potency of certain body parts in traditional and religious practices, as well as for trophy collections ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zsIU8KjM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Atuo & O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Connell Timothy, 2015; Sinovas, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Atuo & O’Connell Timothy, 2015; Sinovas, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26054,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GLKU7W9C","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CQGPCPHL"],"itemData":{"id":26054,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"614–622","source":"Google Scholar","title":"An assessment of socio-economic drivers of avian body parts trade in West African rainforests","volume":"191","author":[{"family":"Atuo","given":"Fidelis A."},{"family":"O'Connell Timothy","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":32704,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5JPFZ6TB"],"itemData":{"id":32704,"type":"report","publisher":"Technical report prepared for the South African National Biodiversity …","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Southern Africa’s wildlife trade: an analysis of CITES trade in SADC countries","title-short":"Southern Africa’s wildlife trade","author":[{"family":"Sinovas","given":"P."},{"family":"Price","given":"B."},{"family":"King","given":"E."},{"family":"Davis","given":"F."},{"family":"Hinsley","given":"A."},{"family":"Pavitt","given":"A."},{"family":"Pfab","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Use for those purposes occurs regardless of the rarity or conservation status of those species. If anything, the perceived value of a species often increases with rarity (though this may not be true for some species; ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6n52gSVG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sumaila, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Sumaila, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34366,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JYVRWI55"],"itemData":{"id":34366,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The period from 2019 to 2020 is critical in determining whether the World Trade Organization (WTO), tasked with eliminating capacity-enhancing fisheries subsidies, can deliver to the world an agreement that will disci­ pline subsidies that lead to overfishing. Here, following extensive data collection efforts, we present an update of the current scope, amount and analysis of the level of subsidisation of the fisheries sector worldwide. We estimate global fisheries subsidies at USD 35.4 billion in 2018, of which capacity-enhancing subsidies are USD 22.2 billion. The top five subsidising political entities (China, European Union, USA, Republic of Korea and Japan) contribute 58% (USD 20.5 billion) of the total estimated subsidy. The updated global figure has decreased since the most recent previous estimate from 2009, of USD 41.4 billion in 2018 constant dollars. The difference be­ tween these two estimates can be largely explained by improvements in methodology and the difference in the actual amount of subsidies provided. Thus, we consider direct statistical comparison of these numbers to be inappropriate. Having said that, the difference between the estimates suggest that the increase in fisheries subsidies provided in the preceding decades may have halted. Still, the bulk of harmful ‘capacity-enhancing’ subsidies, particularly those for fossil fuels have actually increased as a proportion of total subsidies. As such, for the benefit of marine ecosystems, and current and future generations of people, all hands must be on deck in helping the WTO reach a meaningful agreement to discipline subsidies that lead to overcapacity and overfishing.","container-title":"Marine Policy","DOI":"10.1016/j.marpol.2019.103695","ISSN":"0308597X","journalAbbreviation":"Marine Policy","language":"en","page":"103695","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Updated estimates and analysis of global fisheries subsidies","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0308597X19303677","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Sumaila","given":"U. Rashid"},{"family":"Ebrahim","given":"Naazia"},{"family":"Schuhbauer","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Skerritt","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Li","given":"Yang"},{"family":"Kim","given":"Hong Sik"},{"family":"Mallory","given":"Tabitha Grace"},{"family":"Lam","given":"Vicky W.L."},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}), leading to even more aggressive harvesting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iT6Fhfyx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Atuo & O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Connell Timothy, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Atuo & O’Connell Timothy, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26054,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GLKU7W9C","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CQGPCPHL"],"itemData":{"id":26054,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"614–622","source":"Google Scholar","title":"An assessment of socio-economic drivers of avian body parts trade in West African rainforests","volume":"191","author":[{"family":"Atuo","given":"Fidelis A."},{"family":"O'Connell Timothy","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} which may result in commercial or local extinctions  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"A9ihWuEw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ulman, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Ulman, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12650,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UJD5WQ9G"],"itemData":{"id":12650,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Front. Mar. Sci","issue":"650","language":"en","title":"The Lost Fish of Turkey: A Recent History of Disappeared Species and Commercial Fishery Extinctions for the Turkish Marmara and Black Seas","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Ulman","given":"A."},{"family":"Zengin","given":"M."},{"family":"N","given":"Demirel"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.   Globally, the predominant direction of the trade of products derived from wild species is South-to-North, mainly driven by consumer demand from affluent developed countries and their profitable fashion, medical, and food industries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sYBsB4Bd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ripple, Abernethy,, 2016; Sand, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Ripple, Abernethy,, 2016; Sand, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32514,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/99SRFGYK"],"itemData":{"id":32514,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Terrestrial mammals are experiencing a massive collapse in their population sizes and geographical ranges around the world, but many of the drivers, patterns and consequences of this decline remain poorly understood. Here we provide an analysis showing that bushmeat hunting for mostly food and medicinal products is driving a global crisis whereby 301 terrestrial mammal species are threatened with extinction. Nearly all of these threatened species occur in developing countries where major coexisting threats include deforestation, agricultural expansion, human encroachment and competition with livestock. The unrelenting decline of mammals suggests many vital ecological and socio-economic services that these species provide will be lost, potentially changing ecosystems irrevocably. We discuss options and current obstacles to achieving effective conservation, alongside consequences of failure to stem such anthropogenic mammalian extirpation. We propose a multi-pronged conservation strategy to help save threatened mammals from immediate extinction and avoid a collapse of food security for hundreds of millions of people.","container-title":"Royal Society Open Science","DOI":"10.1098/rsos.160498","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Royal Society Open Science","note":"publisher: Royal Society","page":"160498","source":"royalsocietypublishing.org (Atypon)","title":"Bushmeat hunting and extinction risk to the world's mammals","URL":"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/full/10.1098/rsos.160498","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Ripple","given":"William J."},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"Katharine"},{"family":"Betts","given":"Matthew G."},{"family":"Chapron","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Dirzo","given":"Rodolfo"},{"family":"Galetti","given":"Mauro"},{"family":"Levi","given":"Taal"},{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter A."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."},{"family":"Machovina","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Newsome","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Peres","given":"Carlos A."},{"family":"Wallach","given":"Arian D."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Young","given":"Hillary"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32626,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LA44VMRJ"],"itemData":{"id":32626,"type":"document","abstract":"The world-wide commercial exchange of wildlife (live animals and plants) and wildlife products (hides and furskins, ivory, timber, and other derivatives) is big business—valued at between $US5–50 billion annually.1 The predominant direction of the trade is South-to-North, mainly driven by consumer demand from affluent developed countries and their profitable fashion and food industries but also from other users of rare animals and plants for medical and pharmaceutical research, exhibition, or collection purposes.2 A characteristic feature of the trade is its luxury orientation, reflecting consumption patterns often ranging from the non-essential to the perverse.3 While exports of wildlife and wildlife products are thus a significant source of foreign-currency revenue for a number of countries, especially in the Third World, unsustainable","publisher":"GREEN GLOBE YEARBOOK","source":"CiteSeer","title":"Commodity or Taboo? International Regulation of Trade in Endangered Species","title-short":"GREEN GLOBE YEARBOOK 1997 19 Commodity or Taboo?","author":[{"family":"Sand","given":"Peter H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The value of legal wildlife trade from 1997 to 2016 totaled between US$2,9 and US$ 4,4 trillion. The top commercial categories were seafood (82%), furniture (7%) and fashion (furs and hides) (6%) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gTVZUvfu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. A. Andersson, 2021)","plainCitation":"(A. A. Andersson, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H92SKYTQ"],"itemData":{"id":765,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Nature has the potential to provide wide-ranging economic contributions to society – from ecosystem services to providing income to communities via fair trade of resources. Unsustainable trade in wildlife, however, threatens biodiversity and its ability to support communities and a functioning planet. It is therefore important to have clear systems in place for tracing traded wildlife. Monitoring legal wildlife trade in all species is as important as it is for trade in protected species, since flows of the legal trade correlate with, and provide cover for, illegally traded wildlife. The majority of wildlife trade research is focused on species listed in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES). The concurrent, considerably larger legal wildlife trade in both CITES and non-CITES-listed species, however, remains unexamined – despite the fact that if mismanaged, it can lead to over-exploitation. Here we analyzed 20 years of data from the UN Comtrade database, aiming to detail the scale, composition, and trends across all taxa of wildlife traded legally, and to indicate opportunities for improvement. From 1997 to 2016 the value of this trade totaled between US$2.9 and 4.4 trillion. Of this, $2.9 trillion wildlife was traded under “specific” codes that specify taxonomic Order and below, while around $1.4 trillion was traded under “broad” codes that declare wildlife to taxonomic Class and above. The top 10 trading nations/territories accounted for 51.4% of the total value of wildlife traded. The top commercial categories for wildlife trade were seafood (82%), furniture (7%), and fashion (furs and hides) (6%). In these three major categories, vague commodity codes such as “Fish”, “Tropical wood”, and “Other furs” were used to declare 23%, 24% and 26% of items traded, respectively – despite encompassing thousands of species. This lack of granularity imperils biodiversity as trade cannot be comprehensively monitored. We recommend a review of what species are traded under these broad code descriptions, and a distillation of codes to taxonomic Family or Genus level in the next HS Code review period, particularly in the pet, traditional Chinese medicine and furniture categories. In addition, interdisciplinary research into legal wildlife trade should be increased to provide forensic, policy, economic and social solutions to improve trade management.","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2021.e01455","ISSN":"2351-9894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e01455","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"CITES and beyond: Illuminating 20 years of global, legal wildlife trade","title-short":"CITES and beyond","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2351989421000056","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Andersson","given":"Astrid Alexandra"},{"family":"Tilley","given":"Hannah B."},{"family":"Lau","given":"Wilson"},{"family":"Dudgeon","given":"David"},{"family":"Bonebrake","given":"Timothy C."},{"family":"Dingle","given":"Caroline"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	According to the latest update of the International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List of Threatened Species (July, 2019), improperly managed national and international trade is driving the decline of species in the land, oceans and freshwater. Trade can affect sustainable use of wild species directly through harvests and indirectly, for example by shifting towards practices that affect wild species through use and transformation of habitat (e.g. unsustainable logging / land use change). Naturally, those effects will be very different for terrestrial than for marine species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"r2bGvuvG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bulte & Barbier, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Bulte & Barbier, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32711,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HHMARNWZ"],"itemData":{"id":32711,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental and Resource Economics","issue":"4","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"423–463","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Trade and renewable resources in a second best world: an overview","title-short":"Trade and renewable resources in a second best world","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Bulte","given":"Erwin H."},{"family":"Barbier","given":"Edward B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 72% of the species listed as threatened or near-threatened, (6,241) are being overexploited for commerce, recreation or subsistence ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Evtkm3Ky","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Maxwell, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Maxwell, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7825,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WGCUXY2H"],"itemData":{"id":7825,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/536143a","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7615","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"143-145","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Biodiversity: The ravages of guns, nets and bulldozers","title-short":"Biodiversity","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/536143a","volume":"536","author":[{"family":"Maxwell","given":"Sean L."},{"family":"Fuller","given":"Richard A."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Watson","given":"James E. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The same study revealed that unsustainable harvesting is now the most prevalent threat affecting threatened marine species and is the second most prevalent (after agriculture/aquaculture) for terrestrial and freshwater species.  Overfishing has pushed two families of rays to the brink of extinction. Bushmeat hunting for mostly food and medicinal products is driving a global crisis whereby 301 terrestrial mammal species are threatened with extinction in developing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CC5PPwGU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ripple, Abernethy,, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Ripple, Abernethy,, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32514,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/99SRFGYK"],"itemData":{"id":32514,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Terrestrial mammals are experiencing a massive collapse in their population sizes and geographical ranges around the world, but many of the drivers, patterns and consequences of this decline remain poorly understood. Here we provide an analysis showing that bushmeat hunting for mostly food and medicinal products is driving a global crisis whereby 301 terrestrial mammal species are threatened with extinction. Nearly all of these threatened species occur in developing countries where major coexisting threats include deforestation, agricultural expansion, human encroachment and competition with livestock. The unrelenting decline of mammals suggests many vital ecological and socio-economic services that these species provide will be lost, potentially changing ecosystems irrevocably. We discuss options and current obstacles to achieving effective conservation, alongside consequences of failure to stem such anthropogenic mammalian extirpation. We propose a multi-pronged conservation strategy to help save threatened mammals from immediate extinction and avoid a collapse of food security for hundreds of millions of people.","container-title":"Royal Society Open Science","DOI":"10.1098/rsos.160498","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Royal Society Open Science","note":"publisher: Royal Society","page":"160498","source":"royalsocietypublishing.org (Atypon)","title":"Bushmeat hunting and extinction risk to the world's mammals","URL":"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/full/10.1098/rsos.160498","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Ripple","given":"William J."},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"Katharine"},{"family":"Betts","given":"Matthew G."},{"family":"Chapron","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Dirzo","given":"Rodolfo"},{"family":"Galetti","given":"Mauro"},{"family":"Levi","given":"Taal"},{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter A."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."},{"family":"Machovina","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Newsome","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Peres","given":"Carlos A."},{"family":"Wallach","given":"Arian D."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Young","given":"Hillary"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A recent quantitative meta-analysis of wild species trade revealed that overall wild species trade caused a 61.6% decline in species abundance ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6DyOJ1HJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Morton, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Morton, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30323,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J842VNRS"],"itemData":{"id":30323,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1038/s41559-021-01399-y","ISSN":"2397-334X","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Ecol Evol","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impacts of wildlife trade on terrestrial biodiversity","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41559-021-01399-y","author":[{"family":"Morton","given":"Oscar"},{"family":"Scheffers","given":"Brett R."},{"family":"Haugaasen","given":"Torbjørn"},{"family":"Edwards","given":"David P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Extraction for bushmeat trade caused declines of 59.7% (excluding subsistence studies), while pet trade precipitated extreme declines of 73.0% ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dPkVHfXu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Morton, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Morton, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30323,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J842VNRS"],"itemData":{"id":30323,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1038/s41559-021-01399-y","ISSN":"2397-334X","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Ecol Evol","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impacts of wildlife trade on terrestrial biodiversity","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41559-021-01399-y","author":[{"family":"Morton","given":"Oscar"},{"family":"Scheffers","given":"Brett R."},{"family":"Haugaasen","given":"Torbjørn"},{"family":"Edwards","given":"David P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. National and international trade significantly reduced species abundance by 76.3% and 65.8%, respectively, whereas local trade had limited impacts. Though impacts of trade on wild species are widespread globally, one can identify certain hotspots of human impact ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SeuOf0UN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Possingham & Atkinson, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Possingham & Atkinson, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30037,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8TWZM32B","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LKSPQT8J"],"itemData":{"id":30037,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Conserving threatened species requires identifying where across their range they are being impacted by threats, yet this remains unresolved across most of Earth. Here, we present a global analysis of cumulative human impacts on threatened species by using a spatial framework that jointly considers the co-occurrence of eight threatening processes and the distribution of 5,457 terrestrial vertebrates. We show that impacts to species are widespread , occurring across 84% of Earth's surface, and identify hotspots of impacted species richness and coolspots of unimpacted species richness. Almost one-quarter of assessed species are impacted across \\\\textgreater90% of their distribution, and approximately 7% are impacted across their entire range. These results foreshadow localised extirpations and potential extinctions without conservation action. The spatial framework developed here offers a tool for defining strategies to directly mitigate the threats driving species' declines, providing essential information for future national and global conservation agendas. Author summary The biggest drivers of global biodiversity loss are hunting, harvesting, and the conversion of natural habitats for agriculture, urbanisation, and other industrial activity. However, our understanding of where these 'threats' actually impact sensitive species is extremely limited across Earth. Here, we map the distribution of threats within the known ranges of 5,457 terrestrial birds, mammals, and amphibians globally. We map only those threats within a species range that are known to specifically endanger that species. We show that threats are extensive across the majority of species' ranges, severely limiting the area within which species can survive. Concerningly, 1,237 species (almost one-quarter of those assessed) are impacted by threats across \\\\textgreater90% of their distribution, and 395 species are impacted by threats across their entire range. These species will almost certainly face extinction without conservation intervention to remove threats. We identify global PLOS Biology | https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.","container-title":"PLoS Biol","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pbio.3000158","issue":"3","title":"Hotspots of human impact on threatened terrestrial vertebrates","URL":"http://datazone.birdlife.org/home http://dx.doi.org/10.5061/dryad.052q5","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Possingham","given":"C J"},{"family":"Atkinson","given":"H P"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Di Minin ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YwcVmQ7C","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2019)","plainCitation":"(2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31368,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L3XGLTJ8"],"itemData":{"id":31368,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"\\\\textlessp\\\\textgreaterOverexploitation is one of the main threats to biodiversity, but the intensity of this threat varies geographically. We identified global concentrations, on land and at sea, of 4543 species threatened by unsustainable commercial harvesting. Regions under high-intensity threat (based on accessibility on land and on fishing catch at sea) cover 4.3% of the land and 6.1% of the seas and contain 82% of all species threatened by unsustainable harvesting and \\\\textgreater80% of the ranges of Critically Endangered species threatened by unsustainable harvesting. Currently, only 16% of these regions are covered by protected areas on land and just 6% at sea. Urgent actions are needed in these centers of unsustainable harvesting to ensure that use of species is sustainable and to prevent further species' extinctions.\\\\textless/p\\\\textgreater","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.aau2879","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"4","page":"eaau2879","title":"Identifying global centers of unsustainable commercial harvesting of species","URL":"http://advances.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/sciadv.aau2879","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Di Minin","given":"Enrico"},{"family":"Brooks","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Toivonen","given":"Tuuli"},{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H. M."},{"family":"Heikinheimo","given":"Vuokko"},{"family":"Watson","given":"James E. M."},{"family":"Burgess","given":"Neil D."},{"family":"Challender","given":"Daniel W. S."},{"family":"Goettsch","given":"Bárbara"},{"family":"Jenkins","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Moilanen","given":"Atte"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4]]}},"label":"page","suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} identified global centers for unsustainable commercial harvesting of species. 4.3% of the land and 6.1% of the seas contain 82% of all species threatened by unsustainable harvesting and more than 80% of critically endangered species. Those centers of unsustainable commodity are found globally, but are especially concentrated in Asia and North and South America, in areas where harvesting intensity was the highest and governance and political stability the lowest. The regions with the most mammal species threatened by unsustainable levels of hunting and trade of bushmeat were found to be in Asia (especially South-East Asia) and Africa, whereas the countries with the most mammal endemic species threatened include Madagascar, Indonesia, Philippines, Brazil, Papua New Guinea, India and China  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lKd3uybr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. C. Hughes, 2017; Ripple, 2015; Ripple, Abernethy,, 2016; Ripple, Chapron,, 2016; Symes, 2018a)","plainCitation":"(A. C. Hughes, 2017; Ripple, 2015; Ripple, Abernethy,, 2016; Ripple, Chapron,, 2016; Symes, 2018a)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32514,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/99SRFGYK"],"itemData":{"id":32514,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Terrestrial mammals are experiencing a massive collapse in their population sizes and geographical ranges around the world, but many of the drivers, patterns and consequences of this decline remain poorly understood. Here we provide an analysis showing that bushmeat hunting for mostly food and medicinal products is driving a global crisis whereby 301 terrestrial mammal species are threatened with extinction. Nearly all of these threatened species occur in developing countries where major coexisting threats include deforestation, agricultural expansion, human encroachment and competition with livestock. The unrelenting decline of mammals suggests many vital ecological and socio-economic services that these species provide will be lost, potentially changing ecosystems irrevocably. We discuss options and current obstacles to achieving effective conservation, alongside consequences of failure to stem such anthropogenic mammalian extirpation. We propose a multi-pronged conservation strategy to help save threatened mammals from immediate extinction and avoid a collapse of food security for hundreds of millions of people.","container-title":"Royal Society Open Science","DOI":"10.1098/rsos.160498","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Royal Society Open Science","note":"publisher: Royal Society","page":"160498","source":"royalsocietypublishing.org (Atypon)","title":"Bushmeat hunting and extinction risk to the world's mammals","URL":"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/full/10.1098/rsos.160498","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Ripple","given":"William J."},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"Katharine"},{"family":"Betts","given":"Matthew G."},{"family":"Chapron","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Dirzo","given":"Rodolfo"},{"family":"Galetti","given":"Mauro"},{"family":"Levi","given":"Taal"},{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter A."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."},{"family":"Machovina","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Newsome","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Peres","given":"Carlos A."},{"family":"Wallach","given":"Arian D."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Young","given":"Hillary"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32531,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QAA3YPVC"],"itemData":{"id":32531,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Large wild herbivores are crucial to ecosystems and human societies. We highlight the 74 largest terrestrial herbivore species on Earth (body mass ?100 kg), the threats they face, their important and often overlooked ecosystem effects, and the conservation efforts needed to save them and their predators from extinction. Large herbivores are generally facing dramatic population declines and range contractions, such that ~60% are threatened with extinction. Nearly all threatened species are in developing countries, where major threats include hunting, land-use change, and resource depression by livestock. Loss of large herbivores can have cascading effects on other species including large carnivores, scavengers, mesoherbivores, small mammals, and ecological processes involving vegetation, hydrology, nutrient cycling, and fire regimes. The rate of large herbivore decline suggests that ever-larger swaths of the world will soon lack many of the vital ecological services these animals provide, resulting in enormous ecological and social costs.\\nThe collapsing populations of large herbivores will have extensive ecological and social consequences.\\nThe collapsing populations of large herbivores will have extensive ecological and social consequences.","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.1400103","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2015, The Authors. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.","note":"publisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science\\nsection: Review","page":"e1400103","source":"advances.sciencemag.org","title":"Collapse of the world’s largest herbivores","URL":"https://advances.sciencemag.org/content/1/4/e1400103","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Ripple","given":"William J."},{"family":"Newsome","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Dirzo","given":"Rodolfo"},{"family":"Everatt","given":"Kristoffer T."},{"family":"Galetti","given":"Mauro"},{"family":"Hayward","given":"Matt W."},{"family":"Kerley","given":"Graham I. H."},{"family":"Levi","given":"Taal"},{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter A."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."},{"family":"Malhi","given":"Yadvinder"},{"family":"Painter","given":"Luke E."},{"family":"Sandom","given":"Christopher J."},{"family":"Terborgh","given":"John"},{"family":"Valkenburgh","given":"Blaire Van"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",5,1]]}}},{"id":32523,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ETTD9UU9"],"itemData":{"id":32523,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","DOI":"10.1093/biosci/biw092","ISSN":"0006-3568","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"BioScience","page":"807-812","source":"Silverchair","title":"Saving the World's Terrestrial Megafauna","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1093/biosci/biw092","volume":"66","author":[{"family":"Ripple","given":"William J."},{"family":"Chapron","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"López-Bao","given":"José Vicente"},{"family":"Durant","given":"Sarah M."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."},{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter A."},{"family":"Bennett","given":"Elizabeth L."},{"family":"Beschta","given":"Robert L."},{"family":"Bruskotter","given":"Jeremy T."},{"family":"Campos-Arceiz","given":"Ahimsa"},{"family":"Corlett","given":"Richard T."},{"family":"Darimont","given":"Chris T."},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy J."},{"family":"Dirzo","given":"Rodolfo"},{"family":"Dublin","given":"Holly T."},{"family":"Estes","given":"James A."},{"family":"Everatt","given":"Kristoffer T."},{"family":"Galetti","given":"Mauro"},{"family":"Goswami","given":"Varun R."},{"family":"Hayward","given":"Matt W."},{"family":"Hedges","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Hunter","given":"Luke T. B."},{"family":"Kerley","given":"Graham I. H."},{"family":"Letnic","given":"Mike"},{"family":"Levi","given":"Taal"},{"family":"Maisels","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Morrison","given":"John C."},{"family":"Nelson","given":"Michael Paul"},{"family":"Newsome","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Painter","given":"Luke"},{"family":"Pringle","given":"Robert M."},{"family":"Sandom","given":"Christopher J."},{"family":"Terborgh","given":"John"},{"family":"Treves","given":"Adrian"},{"family":"Van Valkenburgh","given":"Blaire"},{"family":"Vucetich","given":"John A."},{"family":"Wirsing","given":"Aaron J."},{"family":"Wallach","given":"Arian D."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Woodroffe","given":"Rosie"},{"family":"Young","given":"Hillary"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Li"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",10,1]]}}},{"id":32524,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L4CR4X9G"],"itemData":{"id":32524,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Southeast Asia (SE Asia) is a known global hotspot of biodiversity and endemism, yet the region is also one of the most biotically threatened. Ecosystems across the region are threatened by an array of drivers, each of which increases the probability of extinction of species in a variety of ecosystems. These issues are symptomatic of the issues that face the global tropics; however, with around 4 billion people in the wider region and the associated pressures on biodiversity, this region may be under some of the greatest levels of biotic threat. Deforestation rates in SE Asia are some of the highest globally, additionally it has the highest rate of mining in the tropics, around the greatest number of hydropower dams under construction, and a consumption of species for traditional medicines which is a threat to biodiversity globally. In this review, the greatest threats to regional biodiversity in the SE Asian region are discussed. Tree-plantations and deforestation represent one of the most imminent threats, and some countries have already lost over half their original forest cover (i.e., the Philippines, parts of Indonesia), with projections of as much as 98% loss for some regions in the coming decade. Hunting and trade represent a significant threat as demand stems not only for food, but also for medicine, for ornamentation, and as a status symbol. Mining represents a frequently overlooked threat, as the Asian region is one of the greatest exporters of limestone and various minerals globally, and the cost of this to biodiversity is not only through the direct loss of areas for mines, but also through the development of roads that further fragment the landscape, the leakage of heavy metals, and the destruction of limestone karsts, which represent global endemicity hotspots. Reservoir construction, wetland drainage, fires, pollution, invasive species, disease, and finally climate change are also considered. Once each issue has been discussed, the overall prognosis of regional biodiversity and priority actions to protect SE Asian biodiversity in the future is discussed.","container-title":"Ecosphere","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ecs2.1624","ISSN":"2150-8925","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"© 2017 Hughes.","note":"_eprint: https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1002/ecs2.1624","page":"e01624","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Understanding the drivers of Southeast Asian biodiversity loss","URL":"https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/ecs2.1624","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Hughes","given":"Alice C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":32549,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6VE22I2Z"],"itemData":{"id":32549,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable trade in wildlife products both legally and illegally is a leading cause of population declines and increased extinction risk in commercially valuable species. However due to the clandestine nature of illegal trade and paucity of overarching studies of legal trade our understanding on international trade networks is patchy. We develop a gravity–underreporting modelling framework to analyse and compare: (i) data on the legal trade in mammalian, avian and reptilian products from recorded by The Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and (ii) to data on the seizures of illegal products entering the USA between 2004 and 2013. We find substantial differences in the factors driving legal trade for the 3 taxonomic groups considered, indicating different drivers for different product markets. Illegal imports for all groups were associated with increasing exporter GDP. We found higher probabilities of underreporting for avian and reptile products, and in general central Africa, central Asia, Eastern Europe and Pacific Island states showed higher underreporting than other regions, indicating the existence of complex trade networks and the potential for the laundering of illegal products through legal markets. Our results show the important regional and economic trends driving wildlife trade. Our new modelling framework can also help illuminate previously unseen aspects of illegal and legal wildlife trade, which can help with the implementation of interventions to curb the impact of trade on wild populations.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2017.11.007","ISSN":"00063207","page":"268–276","title":"The gravity of wildlife trade","volume":"218","author":[{"family":"Symes","given":"William S."},{"family":"McGrath","given":"Francesca L."},{"family":"Rao","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L. Roman"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although overhunting of wild meat is primarily a problem in developing countries, wealthier nations can exacerbate or possibly even drive the problem by inflating demand and prices for meat, trophy, medicinal and ornamental product ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IVVJeoWc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. M. Lee, 2020; Ripple, Abernethy,, 2016)","plainCitation":"(T. M. Lee, 2020; Ripple, Abernethy,, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32519,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XL3BAZS3"],"itemData":{"id":32519,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Bushmeat is not only an important source of fat, micronutrients, and macronutrients, but it also has medicinal uses. Extensive human–wildlife interactions may lead to pathogen exchange and trigger zoonotic infectious disease outbreaks such as severe acute respiratory syndrome, Ebola, and coronavirus disease 2019. In the tropics, bushmeat has become one of the most threatened resources due to widespread habitat loss and overexploitation, largely driven by increased global demand, weak governance, and lack of enforcement. Unsustainable harvesting, consumption, and production practices are common, although drivers are complex and intertwined and vary regionally, pointing to a looming rural nutrition security and wildlife conservation issue. Growing demand in fast urbanizing markets coupled with easy access fuels the illegal trade of bushmeat, medicinal products, and wildlife-based luxury goods. Although bushmeat contributes significantly to rural people's income and poverty alleviation, overharvesting impacts those who are most dependent on the forest. To balance the rural and culturalimportance of bushmeat with conservation and public health priorities, strategies to safeguard tropical biodiversity, sustainable harvest of wildlife with reduced health risk for nutrition and medicine are urgently needed.","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-102016-060827","issue":"1","note":"_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-102016-060827","page":"145-170","source":"Annual Reviews","title":"The Harvest of Tropical Wildlife for Bushmeat and Traditional Medicine","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-102016-060827","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Lee","given":"Tien Ming"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32514,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/99SRFGYK"],"itemData":{"id":32514,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Terrestrial mammals are experiencing a massive collapse in their population sizes and geographical ranges around the world, but many of the drivers, patterns and consequences of this decline remain poorly understood. Here we provide an analysis showing that bushmeat hunting for mostly food and medicinal products is driving a global crisis whereby 301 terrestrial mammal species are threatened with extinction. Nearly all of these threatened species occur in developing countries where major coexisting threats include deforestation, agricultural expansion, human encroachment and competition with livestock. The unrelenting decline of mammals suggests many vital ecological and socio-economic services that these species provide will be lost, potentially changing ecosystems irrevocably. We discuss options and current obstacles to achieving effective conservation, alongside consequences of failure to stem such anthropogenic mammalian extirpation. We propose a multi-pronged conservation strategy to help save threatened mammals from immediate extinction and avoid a collapse of food security for hundreds of millions of people.","container-title":"Royal Society Open Science","DOI":"10.1098/rsos.160498","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Royal Society Open Science","note":"publisher: Royal Society","page":"160498","source":"royalsocietypublishing.org (Atypon)","title":"Bushmeat hunting and extinction risk to the world's mammals","URL":"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/full/10.1098/rsos.160498","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Ripple","given":"William J."},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"Katharine"},{"family":"Betts","given":"Matthew G."},{"family":"Chapron","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Dirzo","given":"Rodolfo"},{"family":"Galetti","given":"Mauro"},{"family":"Levi","given":"Taal"},{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter A."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."},{"family":"Machovina","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Newsome","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Peres","given":"Carlos A."},{"family":"Wallach","given":"Arian D."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Young","given":"Hillary"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, whether or not this is the case depends on a number of other factors (e.g. see Figure 4.10).

Figure 4.10. Wild species trade and sustainability of wild species. Several drivers can have an impact on wild species trade. The drivers are affected by mediating factors and result either in legal or illegal trade. Unselective harvesting methods, incoherent regulatory frameworks, and lack of ability to recognize protected species imply that the line between legal and illegal trade is often fuzzy. Either legal or illegal trade can happen at local through global scale, and be sustainable or unsustainable. For example, when there is market demand (a driver) for certain species as pets or for use in traditional medicine, poor enforcement (a mediating factor) may affect the sustainability outcome of such species that are traded legally and illegally in local and global context. License CC-BY.

      An analysis of Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora trade data from 1975 to 2014 revealed that on average over 100 million whole organism equivalents were reported in trade per year between 2005 and 2014 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Xi2KO5pz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harfoot, 2018c)","plainCitation":"(Harfoot, 2018c)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2Q78L7PC","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RAMZGR28"],"itemData":{"id":11383,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife trade can provide commercial incentives to conserve biodiversity but, if unsustainable, can also pose a threat. CITES (Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora) aims to ensure international trade in CITES-listed species is sustainable, legal and traceable. However, large-scale temporal and spatial patterns in wildlife trade are poorly known. We address this by analysing the CITES Trade Database: > 16 million shipment records for 28,282 species, from 1975 and 2014. Over this period, the volume of reported trade in CITES-listed wildlife quadrupled, from 25 million whole-organism equivalents per year to 100 million, and the ratio of wild- to captive-sourced trade in mammals, birds, reptiles, invertebrates and plants declined by an order of magnitude or more. Our ?ndings start to reveal the scale of the legal wildlife trade, shifting trade routes and sources over time and we describe testable hypotheses for the causes of these changes.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2018.04.017","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"47-57","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Unveiling the patterns and trends in 40 years of global trade in CITES-listed wildlife","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320717312478","volume":"223","author":[{"family":"Harfoot","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Satu A.M."},{"family":"Tittensor","given":"Derek P."},{"family":"Britten","given":"Gregory L."},{"family":"McLardy","given":"Claire"},{"family":"Malsch","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Burgess","given":"Neil D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In total, between 1975 and 2014, plant whole organism equivalents were traded at the highest volume (1.80 billion reported by exporters), followed by reptiles (152 million), invertebrates (79.8 million), birds (24.1 million), mammals (13 million), fish (12.8 million) and amphibians (1.07 million). There was a substantial shift from wild to captive sourced over time. Both the volume and value of international wild species trade are expanding ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JJREpB3G","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Roe, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Roe, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6JA35DPU"],"itemData":{"id":32749,"type":"book","call-number":"SK592.D44 R64 2008","event-place":"Cambridge, UK : Gland, Switzerland","ISBN":"978-1-85850-237-3","language":"en","number-of-pages":"84","publisher":"TRAFFIC International ; WWF-International","publisher-place":"Cambridge, UK : Gland, Switzerland","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Trading nature: a report, with case studies, on the contribution of wildlife trade management to sustainable livelihoods and the Millennium Development Goals","title-short":"Trading nature","author":[{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"}],"contributor":[{"family":"International Traffic Network","given":""},{"family":"WWF (Organization)","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. According to the IPBES global assessment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"H64BJEgz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(IPBES, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":70,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W8MUUZXN"],"itemData":{"id":70,"type":"book","event-place":"Bonn, Germany","ISBN":"978-3-947851-13-3","publisher":"Secretariat of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","publisher-place":"Bonn, Germany","title":"Summary for policymakers of the global assessment report on biodiversity and ecosystem services of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services","URL":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3831673","author":[{"family":"IPBES","given":""}],"contributor":[{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Settele","given":"J."},{"family":"Brondizio","given":"E."},{"family":"Ngo","given":"H."},{"family":"Guèze","given":"M."},{"family":"Agard","given":"J."},{"family":"Arneth","given":"A."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Brauman","given":"K. A."},{"family":"Butchart","given":"S. H. M."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K. M. A."},{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Ichii","given":"K."},{"family":"Liu","given":"J."},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Midgley","given":"G. F."},{"family":"Miloslavich","given":"P."},{"family":"Molnár","given":"Z"},{"family":"Obura","given":"D."},{"family":"Pfaff","given":"A."},{"family":"Polasky","given":"F."},{"family":"Purvis","given":"A."},{"family":"Razzaque","given":"J."},{"family":"Reyers","given":"B."},{"family":"Roy Chowdhury","given":"R."},{"family":"Shin","given":"Y. J."},{"family":"Visseren-Hamakers","given":"I. J."},{"family":"Willis","given":"K. J."},{"family":"Nayas","given":"C. N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the international legal wild species trade has increased by 500% in value since 2005, and 2,000% since the 1980s, albeit a proportion of this increase may reflect enhanced captive breeding or ranching. TRAFFIC has estimated legal international trade, including timber and fisheries products, at US$ 323 billion in 2009 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WJdS52Ut","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cooney, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Cooney, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32784,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8B4IJ2XW"],"itemData":{"id":32784,"type":"book","publisher":"International Trade Centre, Geneva, Switzerland","title":"The trade in wildlife: a framework to improve biodiversity and livelihood outcomes","author":[{"family":"Cooney","given":"R."},{"family":"Kasterine","given":"A."},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"D."},{"family":"Milledge","given":"S."},{"family":"Nossal","given":"K."},{"family":"Roe","given":"D."},{"family":"S.,’t Sas-Rolfes","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      
Wild species trade: Fishing 
Of all food items, fish and fish products are amongst those that are traded most widely in the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NOaYwbH0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pavitt, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Pavitt, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32769,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BYM9EJ88"],"itemData":{"id":32769,"type":"book","ISBN":"92-5-133877-9","publisher":"Food & Agriculture Org.","title":"CITES and the sea: Trade in commercially exploited CITES-listed marine species","volume":"666","author":[{"family":"Pavitt","given":"Alyson"},{"family":"Malsch","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"King","given":"Emily"},{"family":"Chevalier","given":"Audrey"},{"family":"Kachelriess","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Vannuccini","given":"Stefania"},{"family":"Friedman","given":"Kim"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Fisheries exports have been identified as a contributing factor to unsustainable exploitation, contributing to overfishing, and also fish stock collapses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eflgGZNo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Lenzen 2012a; Gars and Spiro 2017; Crona 2015)","plainCitation":"(M. Lenzen 2012a; Gars and Spiro 2017; Crona 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32527,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PWL9M7LB"],"itemData":{"id":32527,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/nature11145","ISSN":"0028-0836, 1476-4687","issue":"7401","journalAbbreviation":"Nature","language":"en","page":"109-112","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"International trade drives biodiversity threats in developing nations","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nature11145","volume":"486","author":[{"family":"Lenzen","given":"M."},{"family":"Moran","given":"D."},{"family":"Kanemoto","given":"K."},{"family":"Foran","given":"B."},{"family":"Lobefaro","given":"L."},{"family":"Geschke","given":"A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",6]]}}},{"id":729,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BUWYKGK2"],"itemData":{"id":729,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Does international trade increase the risk of renewable-resource collapse? We show that the answer is affirmative if trade increases the variety of goods and the resource is harvested under open access. Then there is increased likelihood of collapse both as a steady-state outcome and following shocks to the stock. Two separate mechanisms underlie the results. (1) Trade gives resource harvesters access to new varieties of goods. To enable buying these goods, they exert more harvesting effort. (2) The resource constitutes a new variety for other countries, implying that the resource price increases with trade openness, especially when the stock is small, which makes the resource “exclusive.” Unlike in the existing literature, which predicts that trade cannot lead to collapse, each of our two mechanisms can cause collapse and they both increase the vulnerability to shocks. We present several examples of collapses that can plausibly be attributed to each of the mechanisms.","container-title":"Journal of the Association of Environmental and Resource Economists","DOI":"10.1086/694035","ISSN":"2333-5955","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of the Association of Environmental and Resource Economists","note":"publisher: The University of Chicago Press","page":"155-206","source":"journals.uchicago.edu (Atypon)","title":"Trade and the Risk of Renewable-Resource Collapse","URL":"https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/full/10.1086/694035","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Gars","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Spiro","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",8,4]]}}},{"id":32787,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SMAFQGJK"],"itemData":{"id":32787,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Globalization has increased the speed and flow of people, information, and commodities across space, integrating markets and increasing interdependence of geographically dispersed places worldwide. Places historically driven by largely local forces and market demands are now increasingly affected by drivers at multiple scales. Trade is particularly important in driving these changes and more fish is now exported to international markets than ever before. When small-scale fisheries are integrated into global markets, local social–ecological systems change with potentially both positive and negative impacts on livelihoods, economics and ecology, but few studies systematically investigate how and why the outcomes of market integration vary from case to case. This paper systematically assesses multiple (social, ecological, economic and institutional) local effects of market integration in cases around the world by drawing on the global environmental change syndromes approach. Furthermore, we examine the factors contributing to the syndromes observed. Our analysis identifies three distinct social–ecological syndromes associated with international seafood trade. Results suggest that the presence of strong and well-enforced institutions is the principal factor behind the syndrome characterized by sustained fish stocks, while a combination of weak institutions, patron–client relationships, high demand from China and highly vulnerable target species explain the other two syndromes distinguished by declining stocks, conflict and debt among fishers. A key finding is that the factors emerging as important for explaining the different syndromes derive from different scales (e.g. local market structures vs distant market characteristics), indicating a need for multi-level governance approaches to deal with the effects of market integration. Furthermore, the meta-analysis shows that each syndrome encompasses fisheries from multiple continents. This suggests that the increasingly global nature of the seafood trade appears to be driving local dynamics by creating similar conditions for vulnerabilities in localities around the world, lending support to the notion of tele-connectivity across geographic space.","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2015.07.006","ISSN":"0959-3780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"162-175","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Using social–ecological syndromes to understand impacts of international seafood trade on small-scale fisheries","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959378015300133","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Crona","given":"B. I."},{"family":"Van Holt","given":"T."},{"family":"Petersson","given":"M."},{"family":"Daw","given":"T. M."},{"family":"Buchary","given":"E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Using global export data from 1950-2006, Eisenbarth ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cgt7wTzc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2022)","plainCitation":"(2022)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32748,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J8V769PN"],"itemData":{"id":32748,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper shows that exports are an important cause of resource depletion. The paper uses detailed country–species–level fisheries data to estimate the causal effect of a fishery’s exports on the collapse of the fishery. Identification is based on an export demand shock originating from Japan. The results reveal that an increase in logged exports by one standard deviation raises the probability of a fishery’s collapse in the following year by 31 percentage points. Particularly fisheries without catch share programs collapse when exports surge.","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Economics and Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.jeem.2021.102603","ISSN":"0095-0696","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Environmental Economics and Management","language":"en","page":"102603","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Do exports of renewable resources lead to resource depletion? Evidence from fisheries","title-short":"Do exports of renewable resources lead to resource depletion?","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0095069621001418","volume":"112","author":[{"family":"Eisenbarth","given":"Sabrina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,1]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} finds that exports have a significant effect on the probability of stock collapse. At the same time, the impacts from trade are largely mediated by governance arrangements that organize access and regulation of fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2nYGOnZF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Copeland & Taylor, 2009; Erhardt, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Copeland & Taylor, 2009; Erhardt, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":759,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H774QQIU"],"itemData":{"id":759,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Trade, Tragedy, and the Commons by Brian R. Copeland and M. Scott Taylor. Published in volume 99, issue 3, pages 725-49 of American Economic Review, June 2009, Abstract: We develop a theory of resource management where the degree to which countries escape the tragedy of the commons, and hence the d...","container-title":"American Economic Review","DOI":"10.1257/aer.99.3.725","ISSN":"0002-8282","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"725-49","source":"www.aeaweb.org","title":"Trade, Tragedy, and the Commons","URL":"https://www.aeaweb.org/articles/pdf/doi/10.1257/aer.99.3.725","volume":"99","author":[{"family":"Copeland","given":"Brian R."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"M. Scott"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",6]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":12067,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NJL5DCSV"],"itemData":{"id":12067,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of the Association of Environmental and Resource Economists","DOI":"10.1086/698362.","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"695–711","title":"Does International Trade Cause Overfishing?","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Erhardt","given":"Tobias"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This implies that trade will be a driver of unsustainable use in absence of functioning rules and regulations that safeguard sustainability. 
	Trade, and especially demand of fast-growing Asian economies is the most important driver of the depletion of shark stocks on a global scale ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Q7vJtCFs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Erhardt & Weder, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Erhardt & Weder, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":728,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TW3YHF72"],"itemData":{"id":728,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper examines the unprecedented decimation of sharks. In accordance with the biological characteristics and the economic environment of sharks, we extend the Brander-Taylor model of renewable resources and trade to a resource with a continuum of species which are heterogeneous with respect to their growth rate. The model implies that heterogeneity increases the vulnerability of a resource as relatively abundant species make harvesting economically viable even though slow-growing species are driven to extinction. The empirical analysis assesses the fate of sharks in the light of the model and finds that the likelihood of extinction is, indeed, significantly greater for shark species which exhibit low intrinsic growth.","container-title":"Resource and Energy Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.reseneeco.2020.101181","ISSN":"0928-7655","journalAbbreviation":"Resource and Energy Economics","language":"en","page":"101181","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Shark hunting: On the vulnerability of resources with heterogeneous species","title-short":"Shark hunting","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0928765519303719","volume":"61","author":[{"family":"Erhardt","given":"Tobias"},{"family":"Weder","given":"Rolf"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The diversity of traded shark species makes it more difficult to ban and discourage unsustainable practices, as species substitution could mask depletion of vulnerable species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zRwBJQNy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fields, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Fields, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":734,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YQTP4BTA"],"itemData":{"id":734,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The shark fin trade is a major driver of shark exploitation in fisheries all over the world, most of which are not managed on a species-specific basis. Species-specific trade information highlights taxa of particular concern and can be used to assess the efficacy of management measures and anticipate emerging threats. The species composition of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region of China, one of the world's largest fin trading hubs, was partially assessed in 1999–2001. We randomly selected and genetically identified fin trimmings (n = 4800), produced during fin processing, from the retail market of Hong Kong in 2014–2015 to assess contemporary species composition of the fin trade. We used nonparametric species estimators to determine that at least 76 species of sharks, batoids, and chimaeras supplied the fin trade and a Bayesian model to determine their relative proportion in the market. The diversity of traded species suggests species substitution could mask depletion of vulnerable species; one-third of identified species are threatened with extinction. The Bayesian model suggested that 8 species each comprised >1% of the fin trimmings (34.1–64.2% for blue [Prionace glauca], 0.2–1.2% for bull [Carcharhinus leucas] and shortfin mako [Isurus oxyrinchus]); thus, trade was skewed to a few globally distributed species. Several other coastal sharks, batoids, and chimaeras are in the trade but poorly managed. Fewer than 10 of the species we modeled have sustainably managed fisheries anywhere in their range, and the most common species in trade, the blue shark, was not among them. Our study and approach serve as a baseline to track changes in composition of species in the fin trade over time to better understand patterns of exploitation and assess the effects of emerging management actions for these animals.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/cobi.13043","ISSN":"1523-1739","issue":"2","language":"en","license":"© 2017 The Authors. Conservation Biology published by Wiley Periodicals, Inc. on behalf of Society for Conservation Biology.","note":"_eprint: https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/cobi.13043","page":"376-389","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Species composition of the international shark fin trade assessed through a retail-market survey in Hong Kong","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/cobi.13043","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Fields","given":"Andrew T."},{"family":"Fischer","given":"Gunter A."},{"family":"Shea","given":"Stanley K. H."},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Huarong"},{"family":"Abercrombie","given":"Debra L."},{"family":"Feldheim","given":"Kevin A."},{"family":"Babcock","given":"Elizabeth A."},{"family":"Chapman","given":"Demian D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Basking shark Cetorhinus maximus, is especially sensitive to exploitation and is listed on Appendix II of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xgMaORTW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Magnussen, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Magnussen, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35186,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q5M33URW"],"itemData":{"id":35186,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Mounting evidence that sharks are being over-fished to supply shark fin markets is causing widespread concern about the sustainability of these practices. The basking shark Cetorhinus maximus, whose fins command high market prices, has proven especially sensitive to exploitation. To prevent further population declines, this species is now protected in the territorial waters of several countries, and is listed on Appendix II of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species (CITES) requiring monitoring of trade in its products by all parties to CITES. Tracking trade in basking shark products, however, is often hampered by difficulties in identifying shark products to species of origin. Here, we present the development and application of a streamlined genetic forensics assay that does not require DNA sequencing to identify basking shark products. The dual-primer, species-specific polymerase chain reaction strategy provides diagnostic redundancy for robustness in legal venues. It is also effective for identifying basking shark products regardless of geographic origin, an important consideration, given the global distribution of the species and international sourcing of fins to the trade. Application of the assay confirmed the presence of basking shark fins in the Hong Kong and Japan markets, and indicated an apparent relationship between the Chinese fin trader category ‘Nuo Wei Tian Jiu’ and fins from basking sharks. The assay was also used in a law enforcement investigation to document illegal trade in basking shark fins in the United States where this species is prohibited from harvest and trade. These trade detections suggest that the high market value of basking shark fins is continuing to drive the exploitation, surreptitious and otherwise, of this highly threatened species, underscoring the need for improved trade monitoring. The streamlined assay developed here can assist in monitoring and conservation on a worldwide scale.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00088.x","ISSN":"1469-1795","issue":"2","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00088.x","page":"199-207","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Genetic tracking of basking shark products in international trade","URL":"https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00088.x","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Magnussen","given":"J. E."},{"family":"Pikitch","given":"E. K."},{"family":"Clarke","given":"S. C."},{"family":"Nicholson","given":"C."},{"family":"Hoelzel","given":"A. R."},{"family":"Shivji","given":"M. S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Yet, tracking trade in basking shark products is difficult as shark products are often visually indistinguishable and therefore one cannot easily identify single species and origin ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sxVKfRn4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carde\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}osa, 2018; Magnussen, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Cardeñosa, 2018; Magnussen, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35186,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q5M33URW"],"itemData":{"id":35186,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Mounting evidence that sharks are being over-fished to supply shark fin markets is causing widespread concern about the sustainability of these practices. The basking shark Cetorhinus maximus, whose fins command high market prices, has proven especially sensitive to exploitation. To prevent further population declines, this species is now protected in the territorial waters of several countries, and is listed on Appendix II of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species (CITES) requiring monitoring of trade in its products by all parties to CITES. Tracking trade in basking shark products, however, is often hampered by difficulties in identifying shark products to species of origin. Here, we present the development and application of a streamlined genetic forensics assay that does not require DNA sequencing to identify basking shark products. The dual-primer, species-specific polymerase chain reaction strategy provides diagnostic redundancy for robustness in legal venues. It is also effective for identifying basking shark products regardless of geographic origin, an important consideration, given the global distribution of the species and international sourcing of fins to the trade. Application of the assay confirmed the presence of basking shark fins in the Hong Kong and Japan markets, and indicated an apparent relationship between the Chinese fin trader category ‘Nuo Wei Tian Jiu’ and fins from basking sharks. The assay was also used in a law enforcement investigation to document illegal trade in basking shark fins in the United States where this species is prohibited from harvest and trade. These trade detections suggest that the high market value of basking shark fins is continuing to drive the exploitation, surreptitious and otherwise, of this highly threatened species, underscoring the need for improved trade monitoring. The streamlined assay developed here can assist in monitoring and conservation on a worldwide scale.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00088.x","ISSN":"1469-1795","issue":"2","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00088.x","page":"199-207","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Genetic tracking of basking shark products in international trade","URL":"https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1469-1795.2006.00088.x","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Magnussen","given":"J. E."},{"family":"Pikitch","given":"E. K."},{"family":"Clarke","given":"S. C."},{"family":"Nicholson","given":"C."},{"family":"Hoelzel","given":"A. R."},{"family":"Shivji","given":"M. S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GUN89YGK"],"itemData":{"id":733,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) is a multilateral environmental agreement to ensure that the international trade of threatened species is either prohibited (Appendix I listed species) or being conducted legally, sustainably, and transparently (Appendix II listed species). Twelve threatened shark species exploited for their fins, meat, and other products have been listed under CITES Appendix II. Sharks are often traded in high volumes, some of their products are visually indistinguishable, and most importing/exporting nations have limited capacity to detect illicit trade and enforce the regulations. High volume shipments often must be screened after only a short period of detainment (e.g., a maximum of 24?hours), which together with costs and capacity issues have limited the use of DNA approaches to identify illicit trade. Here, we present a reliable, field-based, fast (<4?hours), and cost effective ($0.94 USD per sample) multiplex real-time PCR protocol capable of detecting nine of the twelve sharks listed under CITES in a single reaction. This approach facilitates detection of illicit trade, with positive results providing probable cause to detain shipments for more robust forensic analysis. We also provide evidence of its application in real law enforcement scenarios in Hong Kong. Adoption of this approach can help parties meet their CITES requirements, avoiding potential international trade sanctions in the future.","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/s41598-018-34663-6","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"2018 The Author(s)","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"16313","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Multiplex real-time PCR assay to detect illegal trade of CITES-listed shark species","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-018-34663-6","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Cardeñosa","given":"Diego"},{"family":"Quinlan","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Shea","given":"Kwok Ho"},{"family":"Chapman","given":"Demian D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Full traceability, and strict control and enforcement of fishing regulations would contribute towards sustainable fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8gDjkXSY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bailey, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Bailey, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26046,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WZYNH8IH","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6AC2HJ7M"],"itemData":{"id":26046,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability, Sustainability governance and transformation 2016: Informational governance and environmental sustainability","DOI":"10.1016/j.cosust.2015.06.004.","issue":"February","language":"en","page":"25–32","title":"The Role of Traceability in Transforming Seafood Governance in the Global South","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Bailey","given":"Megan"},{"family":"Bush","given":"Simon R."},{"family":"Miller","given":"Alex"},{"family":"Kochen","given":"Momo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Tanjung Luar in East Lombok (Indonesia), almost half of the shark catch comprises Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora -listed species, and insufficient management regulations are in place to incentivize or enforce avoidance of threatened stocks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NWHKbJuD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Yulianto, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Yulianto, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":730,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TCNLR2QC"],"itemData":{"id":730,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Overfishing is a major threat to the survival of shark species, primarily driven by international trade in high-value fins, as well as meat, liver oil, skin and cartilage. The Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) aims to ensure that commercial trade does not threaten wild species, and several shark species have recently been listed on CITES as part of international efforts to ensure that trade does not threaten their survival. However, as international trade regulations alone will be insufficient to reduce overexploitation of sharks, they must be accompanied by practical fisheries management measures to reduce fishing mortality. To examine which management measures might be practical in the context of a targeted shark fishery, we collected data from 52 vessels across 595 fishing trips from January 2014 to December 2015 at Tanjung Luar fishing port in East Lombok, Indonesia. We recorded 11,920 landed individuals across 42 species, a high proportion of which were threatened and regulated species. Catch per unit effort depended primarily on the number of hooks and type of fishing gear used, and to a lesser degree on month, boat engine power, number of sets and fishing ground. The most significant factors influencing the likelihood of catching threatened and regulated species were month, fishing ground, engine power and hook number. We observed significant negative relationships between standardised catch per unit effort and several indicators of fishing effort, suggesting diminishing returns above relatively low levels of fishing effort. Our results suggest that management measures focusing on fishing effort controls, gear restrictions and modifications and spatiotemporal closures could have significant benefits for the conservation of shark species, and may help to improve the overall sustainability of the Tanjung Luar shark fishery. These management measures may also be applicable to shark fisheries in other parts of Indonesia and beyond, as sharks increasingly become the focus of global conservation efforts.","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0206437","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"PLOS ONE","language":"en","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e0206437","source":"PLoS Journals","title":"Practical measures for sustainable shark fisheries: Lessons learned from an Indonesian targeted shark fishery","title-short":"Practical measures for sustainable shark fisheries","URL":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0206437","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Yulianto","given":"Irfan"},{"family":"Booth","given":"Hollie"},{"family":"Ningtias","given":"Prayekti"},{"family":"Kartawijaya","given":"Tasrif"},{"family":"Santos","given":"Juan"},{"family":"Sarmintohadi","given":""},{"family":"Kleinertz","given":"Sonja"},{"family":"Campbell","given":"Stuart J."},{"family":"Palm","given":"Harry W."},{"family":"Hammer","given":"Cornelius"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Arabian Seas Region plays an important role in the global trade of sharks and rays, where the United Arab Emirates and Yemen can be characterized as major regional trade hubs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"x8dVJba1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jabado & Spaet, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Jabado & Spaet, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":731,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/79U488RN"],"itemData":{"id":731,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Arabian Seas Region plays an important role in the global landings and trade of sharks and rays. The United Arab Emirates (UAE) and Yemen, two countries with stark socio-economic differences, serve as major regional trade hubs for shark and ray products and four countries (Oman, Pakistan, UAE and Yemen) supply nearly 11% of dried fin exports to Hong Kong. Yet, little information is available on the characteristics of this trade and the fisheries contributing to it. Here, we review the fisheries characteristics, trade, utilization and distribution chain of sharks and rays in 15 countries of the Arabian Seas Region based on published and grey literature, landing surveys, field observations and interviews with fishermen and traders. Although regional shark fisheries remain mostly artisanal, reported shark and ray landings represent 28% of the regional total fish production, reaching 56,074 mt in 2012 (7.3% of total world catches), with Iran, Oman, Pakistan and Yemen ranking as the primary catchers. Utilization and distribution patterns are complex, vary between landing sites and countries, and remain unmonitored. Based on widespread over-exploitation of most teleost fisheries, current exploitation levels for most sharks and rays are potentially unsustainable. The situation is exacerbated by limited research and political will to support policy development, the incomplete nature of fisheries data, as well as insufficient regulations and enforcement. A better understanding of shark and ray fisheries will be key for regulating trade, promoting conservation and developing management initiatives to secure food security, livelihoods and biodiversity conservation in the region.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/faf.12227","ISSN":"1467-2979","issue":"6","language":"en","license":"© 2017 John Wiley & Sons Ltd","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/faf.12227","page":"1096-1118","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Elasmobranch fisheries in the Arabian Seas Region: Characteristics, trade and management","title-short":"Elasmobranch fisheries in the Arabian Seas Region","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/faf.12227","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Jabado","given":"Rima W."},{"family":"Spaet","given":"Julia L. Y."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Reported shark and ray landings represent 28% of the regional total fish catches, amounting to 56,074 tonnes in 2012, with most catches taking place in Iran, Oman, Pakistan and Yemen  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FIZwlDnZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jabado & Spaet, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Jabado & Spaet, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":731,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/79U488RN"],"itemData":{"id":731,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Arabian Seas Region plays an important role in the global landings and trade of sharks and rays. The United Arab Emirates (UAE) and Yemen, two countries with stark socio-economic differences, serve as major regional trade hubs for shark and ray products and four countries (Oman, Pakistan, UAE and Yemen) supply nearly 11% of dried fin exports to Hong Kong. Yet, little information is available on the characteristics of this trade and the fisheries contributing to it. Here, we review the fisheries characteristics, trade, utilization and distribution chain of sharks and rays in 15 countries of the Arabian Seas Region based on published and grey literature, landing surveys, field observations and interviews with fishermen and traders. Although regional shark fisheries remain mostly artisanal, reported shark and ray landings represent 28% of the regional total fish production, reaching 56,074 mt in 2012 (7.3% of total world catches), with Iran, Oman, Pakistan and Yemen ranking as the primary catchers. Utilization and distribution patterns are complex, vary between landing sites and countries, and remain unmonitored. Based on widespread over-exploitation of most teleost fisheries, current exploitation levels for most sharks and rays are potentially unsustainable. The situation is exacerbated by limited research and political will to support policy development, the incomplete nature of fisheries data, as well as insufficient regulations and enforcement. A better understanding of shark and ray fisheries will be key for regulating trade, promoting conservation and developing management initiatives to secure food security, livelihoods and biodiversity conservation in the region.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/faf.12227","ISSN":"1467-2979","issue":"6","language":"en","license":"© 2017 John Wiley & Sons Ltd","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/faf.12227","page":"1096-1118","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Elasmobranch fisheries in the Arabian Seas Region: Characteristics, trade and management","title-short":"Elasmobranch fisheries in the Arabian Seas Region","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/faf.12227","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Jabado","given":"Rima W."},{"family":"Spaet","given":"Julia L. Y."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While the fishery is mostly artisanal, some gear types (e.g. dynamite fishing) and exploitation levels have potentially unsustainable impacts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uvZEz2Ry","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jabado & Spaet, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Jabado & Spaet, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":731,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/79U488RN"],"itemData":{"id":731,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Arabian Seas Region plays an important role in the global landings and trade of sharks and rays. The United Arab Emirates (UAE) and Yemen, two countries with stark socio-economic differences, serve as major regional trade hubs for shark and ray products and four countries (Oman, Pakistan, UAE and Yemen) supply nearly 11% of dried fin exports to Hong Kong. Yet, little information is available on the characteristics of this trade and the fisheries contributing to it. Here, we review the fisheries characteristics, trade, utilization and distribution chain of sharks and rays in 15 countries of the Arabian Seas Region based on published and grey literature, landing surveys, field observations and interviews with fishermen and traders. Although regional shark fisheries remain mostly artisanal, reported shark and ray landings represent 28% of the regional total fish production, reaching 56,074 mt in 2012 (7.3% of total world catches), with Iran, Oman, Pakistan and Yemen ranking as the primary catchers. Utilization and distribution patterns are complex, vary between landing sites and countries, and remain unmonitored. Based on widespread over-exploitation of most teleost fisheries, current exploitation levels for most sharks and rays are potentially unsustainable. The situation is exacerbated by limited research and political will to support policy development, the incomplete nature of fisheries data, as well as insufficient regulations and enforcement. A better understanding of shark and ray fisheries will be key for regulating trade, promoting conservation and developing management initiatives to secure food security, livelihoods and biodiversity conservation in the region.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/faf.12227","ISSN":"1467-2979","issue":"6","language":"en","license":"© 2017 John Wiley & Sons Ltd","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/faf.12227","page":"1096-1118","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Elasmobranch fisheries in the Arabian Seas Region: Characteristics, trade and management","title-short":"Elasmobranch fisheries in the Arabian Seas Region","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/faf.12227","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Jabado","given":"Rima W."},{"family":"Spaet","given":"Julia L. Y."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The large geographic area, complicate trade dynamics, and ongoing political instability and convoluted governance and administrative make a centralized control approach inherently difficult ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7rZAjtO3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jabado & Spaet, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Jabado & Spaet, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":731,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/79U488RN"],"itemData":{"id":731,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Arabian Seas Region plays an important role in the global landings and trade of sharks and rays. The United Arab Emirates (UAE) and Yemen, two countries with stark socio-economic differences, serve as major regional trade hubs for shark and ray products and four countries (Oman, Pakistan, UAE and Yemen) supply nearly 11% of dried fin exports to Hong Kong. Yet, little information is available on the characteristics of this trade and the fisheries contributing to it. Here, we review the fisheries characteristics, trade, utilization and distribution chain of sharks and rays in 15 countries of the Arabian Seas Region based on published and grey literature, landing surveys, field observations and interviews with fishermen and traders. Although regional shark fisheries remain mostly artisanal, reported shark and ray landings represent 28% of the regional total fish production, reaching 56,074 mt in 2012 (7.3% of total world catches), with Iran, Oman, Pakistan and Yemen ranking as the primary catchers. Utilization and distribution patterns are complex, vary between landing sites and countries, and remain unmonitored. Based on widespread over-exploitation of most teleost fisheries, current exploitation levels for most sharks and rays are potentially unsustainable. The situation is exacerbated by limited research and political will to support policy development, the incomplete nature of fisheries data, as well as insufficient regulations and enforcement. A better understanding of shark and ray fisheries will be key for regulating trade, promoting conservation and developing management initiatives to secure food security, livelihoods and biodiversity conservation in the region.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/faf.12227","ISSN":"1467-2979","issue":"6","language":"en","license":"© 2017 John Wiley & Sons Ltd","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/faf.12227","page":"1096-1118","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Elasmobranch fisheries in the Arabian Seas Region: Characteristics, trade and management","title-short":"Elasmobranch fisheries in the Arabian Seas Region","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/faf.12227","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Jabado","given":"Rima W."},{"family":"Spaet","given":"Julia L. Y."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The whole genus of seahorses was listed in Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Appendix II in 2002 because of the adverse effects of harvests and trade on sustainability of wild populations. Evidence from Thailand suggested that seahorses were often caught as bycatch in trawl fisheries, allowing fishers to continue fishing if harvesting the target species was not so profitable, for example due to overexploitation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FcaG0SGA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T.-C. Kuo, 2018)","plainCitation":"(T.-C. Kuo, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":744,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y8AJXBFU"],"itemData":{"id":744,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Exploitation for trade is one of the biggest threats to many species, especially for marine fishes. Trade regulations should, therefore, be effective in helping conserve marine fish populations. The Convention of International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES), one of the few multilateral environmental agreements with enforcement capacity, has embraced a number of marine fishes in recent years. However, the impacts of such measures on wildlife trade have rarely been assessed. We conducted a case study of the dried seahorse (Hippocampus spp.) trade in Thailand to understand the trade of these species under CITES regulations. We carried out 203 semi-structured interviews with traders to estimate the economic scale of Thai seahorse trade, and compared perceived changes with official trade datasets. Even though most seahorses were incidentally caught, we estimated that dried seahorses could be worth US$26.5 million per year for Thai fishers. However, the total declared annual export value was only around US$5.5 million, and had decreased to US$1 million in 2013. Considering the economic value of seahorses, the large discrepancy between declared export volumes and catch estimates suggested that trade may be underreported. While official data shows the export volume decreased after the implementation of CITES listing in 2005, our respondents did not report a similar trend. In contrast, the prices of seahorses were reported to be increasing. Our study highlights the economic importance of marine fishes captured as bycatch and the importance of international and domestic management measures for the trade of bycatch species.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-018-1610-2","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"13","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"3447-3468","source":"Springer Link","title":"Changes in the trade of bycatch species corresponding to CITES regulations: the case of dried seahorse trade in Thailand","title-short":"Changes in the trade of bycatch species corresponding to CITES regulations","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10531-018-1610-2","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Kuo","given":"T.-C."},{"family":"Laksanawimol","given":"P."},{"family":"Aylesworth","given":"L."},{"family":"Foster","given":"S. J."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A. C. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Declining abundances, as reported in Malaysia and Thailand suggests that current harvest rates are unsustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HyEk76yK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T.-C. Kuo, 2018; A. L. Perry, 2010)","plainCitation":"(T.-C. Kuo, 2018; A. L. Perry, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32113,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PTUVYK4I"],"itemData":{"id":32113,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","issue":"4","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"464–475","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Fisheries, large-scale trade, and conservation of seahorses in Malaysia and Thailand","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Perry","given":"Allison L."},{"family":"Lunn","given":"Kristin E."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"Amanda CJ"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":744,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y8AJXBFU"],"itemData":{"id":744,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Exploitation for trade is one of the biggest threats to many species, especially for marine fishes. Trade regulations should, therefore, be effective in helping conserve marine fish populations. The Convention of International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES), one of the few multilateral environmental agreements with enforcement capacity, has embraced a number of marine fishes in recent years. However, the impacts of such measures on wildlife trade have rarely been assessed. We conducted a case study of the dried seahorse (Hippocampus spp.) trade in Thailand to understand the trade of these species under CITES regulations. We carried out 203 semi-structured interviews with traders to estimate the economic scale of Thai seahorse trade, and compared perceived changes with official trade datasets. Even though most seahorses were incidentally caught, we estimated that dried seahorses could be worth US$26.5 million per year for Thai fishers. However, the total declared annual export value was only around US$5.5 million, and had decreased to US$1 million in 2013. Considering the economic value of seahorses, the large discrepancy between declared export volumes and catch estimates suggested that trade may be underreported. While official data shows the export volume decreased after the implementation of CITES listing in 2005, our respondents did not report a similar trend. In contrast, the prices of seahorses were reported to be increasing. Our study highlights the economic importance of marine fishes captured as bycatch and the importance of international and domestic management measures for the trade of bycatch species.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-018-1610-2","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"13","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"3447-3468","source":"Springer Link","title":"Changes in the trade of bycatch species corresponding to CITES regulations: the case of dried seahorse trade in Thailand","title-short":"Changes in the trade of bycatch species corresponding to CITES regulations","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10531-018-1610-2","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Kuo","given":"T.-C."},{"family":"Laksanawimol","given":"P."},{"family":"Aylesworth","given":"L."},{"family":"Foster","given":"S. J."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A. C. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Wild species trade: Non -lethal fishing 
From 1990 to 2016, the number of direct export transactions of Appendix II Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora -listed marine species increased sevenfold (from around 14 000 in 1990 to around 98 000 in 2016) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BZOQVZSS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pavitt, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Pavitt, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32769,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BYM9EJ88"],"itemData":{"id":32769,"type":"book","ISBN":"92-5-133877-9","publisher":"Food & Agriculture Org.","title":"CITES and the sea: Trade in commercially exploited CITES-listed marine species","volume":"666","author":[{"family":"Pavitt","given":"Alyson"},{"family":"Malsch","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"King","given":"Emily"},{"family":"Chevalier","given":"Audrey"},{"family":"Kachelriess","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Vannuccini","given":"Stefania"},{"family":"Friedman","given":"Kim"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Approximately 97 percent of those exports fall into the group of corals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nY2wVBrF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pavitt, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Pavitt, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32769,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BYM9EJ88"],"itemData":{"id":32769,"type":"book","ISBN":"92-5-133877-9","publisher":"Food & Agriculture Org.","title":"CITES and the sea: Trade in commercially exploited CITES-listed marine species","volume":"666","author":[{"family":"Pavitt","given":"Alyson"},{"family":"Malsch","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"King","given":"Emily"},{"family":"Chevalier","given":"Audrey"},{"family":"Kachelriess","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Vannuccini","given":"Stefania"},{"family":"Friedman","given":"Kim"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is only a small fraction of the total trade, as many fish species can be can be traded with few regulatory and monitoring systems in place ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NZieQ9jA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biondo & Burki, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Biondo & Burki, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34338,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FTSYRERF"],"itemData":{"id":34338,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The multi-billion dollar trade in ornamental fishes has rarely been reliably monitored. Almost all coral reef fishes are wild-caught, and few scientific analyses have attempted to elicit exact quantities and identify species involved. The consequences of the removal of millions of these fishes are poorly understood. This article collates and examines available information, including scientific studies and formal publications, in order to create a more accurate picture of this commerce. We demonstrate that it is almost impossible to analyse the trade in marine ornamental fishes due to a lack of data, and that available data for marine species is frequently combined with that for freshwater species. Figures range from 15 to 30 million coral reef fishes being traded annually, but could be as high as 150 million specimens. The global value of this trade was only estimated for 1976 and 1999 between USD 28&ndash;40 million. This review highlights the urgent need to introduce a specific harmonised system tariff code and for a global monitoring system, such as the Trade Control and Expert System already in use in Europe, in order to gather accurate and timely information on the number and species of marine ornamental fishes in commerce, where specimens originated, and whether they were wild-caught or captive-bred.","container-title":"Animals","DOI":"10.3390/ani10112014","issue":"11","language":"en","license":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","note":"number: 11\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"2014","source":"www.mdpi.com","title":"A Systematic Review of the Ornamental Fish Trade with Emphasis on Coral Reef Fishes—An Impossible Task","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-2615/10/11/2014","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."},{"family":"Burki","given":"Rainer P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, it has been estimated that trade in coral reef fishes alone range from 13 to 35 million fishes being traded annually ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZvzXpots","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biondo & Burki, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Biondo & Burki, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34338,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FTSYRERF"],"itemData":{"id":34338,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The multi-billion dollar trade in ornamental fishes has rarely been reliably monitored. Almost all coral reef fishes are wild-caught, and few scientific analyses have attempted to elicit exact quantities and identify species involved. The consequences of the removal of millions of these fishes are poorly understood. This article collates and examines available information, including scientific studies and formal publications, in order to create a more accurate picture of this commerce. We demonstrate that it is almost impossible to analyse the trade in marine ornamental fishes due to a lack of data, and that available data for marine species is frequently combined with that for freshwater species. Figures range from 15 to 30 million coral reef fishes being traded annually, but could be as high as 150 million specimens. The global value of this trade was only estimated for 1976 and 1999 between USD 28&ndash;40 million. This review highlights the urgent need to introduce a specific harmonised system tariff code and for a global monitoring system, such as the Trade Control and Expert System already in use in Europe, in order to gather accurate and timely information on the number and species of marine ornamental fishes in commerce, where specimens originated, and whether they were wild-caught or captive-bred.","container-title":"Animals","DOI":"10.3390/ani10112014","issue":"11","language":"en","license":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","note":"number: 11\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"2014","source":"www.mdpi.com","title":"A Systematic Review of the Ornamental Fish Trade with Emphasis on Coral Reef Fishes—An Impossible Task","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-2615/10/11/2014","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."},{"family":"Burki","given":"Rainer P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. More generally, pet trade can be an important driver of biodiversity loss and overharvesting of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"METIQWwh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. E. Baker, 2013; Bush, 2014; Ng, 2016)","plainCitation":"(S. E. Baker, 2013; Bush, 2014; Ng, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":741,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5CEA5DC8"],"itemData":{"id":741,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The ornamental pet trade is often considered a key culprit for conservation problems such as the introduction of invasive species (including infectious diseases) and overharvesting of rare species. Here, we present the first assessment of the biodiversity of freshwater molluscs in the ornamental pet trade in Singapore, one of the most important global hubs of the ornamental aquarium trade, and discuss associated conservation concerns. We recorded freshwater molluscs from ornamental pet shops and major exporters including non-ornamental species (e.g., hitchhikers, molluscs sold as fish feed). We recorded an unexpectedly high diversity—59 species—of freshwater bivalves and gastropods, with the majority (38 species or 64%) being from the Oriental region. In addition to morphological examination, we sequenced the DNA barcode region of mitochondrial CO1 and 16S genes to provide molecular data for the confirmation of the identification and for future re-identification. DNA barcodes were obtained for 50 species, and all but four were separated by > 3% uncorrected pairwise distances. The trade has been considered a main introduction pathway for non-native species to Singapore, and we found that out of 15 species in the trade as well as in the wild in Singapore, 12 are either introduced or of unknown origin, representing almost half of the known non-native freshwater molluscs in Singapore. Particularly prevalent are non-ornamental species: six hitchhikers on aquarium plants and six species sold as fish feed. We found that a quarter of the trade species have a history of introduction, which includes 11 known or potentially invasive species. We conclude that potential overharvesting is difficult to assess because only half of the trade species have been treated by IUCN. Of these, 21 species are of Least Concern and three are Data Deficient. Our checklist, with accompanying DNA barcodes, images, and museum vouchers, provides an important reference library for future monitoring, and constitutes a step toward creating a more sustainable ornamental pet trade.","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0161130","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"PLOS ONE","language":"en","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e0161130","source":"PLoS Journals","title":"Molluscs for Sale: Assessment of Freshwater Gastropods and Bivalves in the Ornamental Pet Trade","title-short":"Molluscs for Sale","URL":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0161130","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Ng","given":"Ting Hui"},{"family":"Tan","given":"Siong Kiat"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Wing Hing"},{"family":"Meier","given":"Rudolf"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Sow-Yan"},{"family":"Tan","given":"Heok Hui"},{"family":"Yeo","given":"Darren C. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",8,15]]}}},{"id":32742,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SJ82QLSW"],"itemData":{"id":32742,"type":"book","abstract":"International trade in exotic pets is an important and increasing driver of biodiversity loss and often compromises the standards required for good animal welfare. We systematically reviewed the scientific and gray literature and used the United Nations Environment Programme - World Conservation Monitoring Centre (UNEP-WCMC) Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) trade database to establish temporal and geographical trade patterns of live exotic birds, mammals, and reptiles and to describe trends in research, taxonomic representation, and level of threat and legal protection of species traded. Birds were the most species-rich and abundant class reported in trade; reptiles were second most abundant but unusually the most studied in this context; and mammals were least abundant in trade. Mammalian and reptilian species traded as pets were more likely to be threatened than expected by random. There have been a substantial number of Appendix I listed captive-bred mammals and birds and wild-caught birds and reptiles reported in trade to CITES. We identified the Middle East's emerging role as a driver of demand for exotic pets of all taxa alongside the well-established and increasing role of South America and Southeast Asia in the market. Europe, North America, and the Middle East featured most heavily in trade reports to CITES, whereas trade involving South America and Southeast Asia were given most emphasis in the literature. For effective monitoring of and appropriate response to the international exotic pet trade, it is imperative that the reliability and detail of CITES trade reports improve and that scientific research be directed toward those taxa and locations that are most vulnerable. El Mercado Global de Mascotas Exóticas 2006-2012 El mercado internacional de mascotas exóticas es un factor importante y creciente de pérdida de la biodiversidad y continuamente compromete los estándares requeridos para un buen bienestar animal. Sistemáticamente revisamos la literatura científica y gris y usamos la base de datos de mercados de UNEP-WCMC CITES para establecer patrones temporales y geográficos del mercado de aves, mamíferos y reptiles exóticos vivos y describir las tendencias en la investigación, la representación taxonómica y el nivel de amenaza y la protección legal de las especies comercializadas. Las aves fueron la clase con mayor riqueza de especies y la más abundante; los reptiles fueron la segunda más abundante pero inusualmente la más estudiada en este contexto; los mamíferos fueron los menos abundantes en el mercado. Las especies de mamíferos y reptiles comercializadas como mascotas tuvieron mayor probabilidad de estar amenazadas que lo esperado por el azar. Ha habido un número sustancial de especies de mamíferos y aves criados en cautiverio y listados en el Apéndice-I y de aves y reptiles capturados en vida silvestre reportados en el mercado a CITES. Identificamos el papel emergente del Oriente Medio como conductor de la demanda de mascotas exóticas de todos los taxones junto con el bien establecido y creciente papel de América del Sur y el Sureste Asiático en el mercado. Europa, América del Norte y el Oriente Medio figuraron más en los reportes de mercado hechos a CITES, mientras que al mercado en América del Sur y el Sureste Asiático se le dio más énfasis en la literatura. Para un monitoreo efectivo de y una respuesta apropiada al mercado internacional de mascotas exóticas, es imperativo que la confiabilidad y el detalle de los reportes de mercado de CITES mejoren y que la investigación científica sea dirigida hacia esos taxones y las localidades que sean más vulnerables.","collection-number":"3","note":"DOI: 10.1111/cobi.12240","number-of-pages":"663–676","publisher":"Blackwell Publishing Inc.","title":"Global trade in exotic pets 2006-2012","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Bush","given":"Emma R."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Sandra E."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":721,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H8KV6TSV"],"itemData":{"id":721,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","issue":"12","note":"publisher: American Institute of Biological Sciences Circulation, AIBS, 1313 Dolley …","page":"928–938","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Rough trade: animal welfare in the global wildlife trade","title-short":"Rough trade","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Baker","given":"Sandra E."},{"family":"Cain","given":"Russ"},{"family":"Van Kesteren","given":"Freya"},{"family":"Zommers","given":"Zinta A."},{"family":"D'cruze","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It can also be an an indirect driver such as a vector for invasive species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ALOY9N0a","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fitzpatrick, 2018; L\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}tters, 2018; Martel, 2014; D. A. Travis, 2011; Woeltjes, 2011; Yuan, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Fitzpatrick, 2018; Lötters, 2018; Martel, 2014; D. A. Travis, 2011; Woeltjes, 2011; Yuan, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":703,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R45YVVYM"],"itemData":{"id":703,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Revue Scientifique et Technique-OIE","issue":"1","page":"219","source":"Google Scholar","title":"The spread of pathogens through trade in wildlife","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Travis","given":"D. A."},{"family":"Watson","given":"R. P."},{"family":"Tauer","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":702,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3C9HFC7M"],"itemData":{"id":702,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"<section class=\\"abstract\\"><div id=\\"\\" class=\\"section\\"><h3 class=\\"abstractTitle text-title my-1\\" id=\\"d169396e3\\">Abstract</h3><p>\\n               <em>Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis</em> is the cause of the fungal disease chytridiomycosis, a potentially lethal skin disease of amphibians. Asymptomatically infected amphibians may pose a risk for environmental pathogen pollution. This study therefore assessed the role of healthy, captive amphibians as a reservoir of <em>Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis</em>. Samples were collected from captive amphibians in Belgium, the Netherlands, Germany and France (559 from anurans, 330 from urodelans and 4 from gymnophians) from private owners, zoos, and laboratories. In addition to which, 78 anurans from 19 living collections were sampled during a pet fair in the Netherlands. Nearly 3% of the captive amphibians were infected by <em>B. dendrobatidis</em>, and 13.6% of the collections yielded at least one positive result. At the fair, 7 out of 78 anurans, representing 2 collections were positive. None of the animals that tested positive showed any obvious health problems at the time of sampling. Our results demonstrate the potential of the amphibian pet trade as a vehicle for the spread of <em>B. dendrobatidis.</em>\\n            </p></div></section>","container-title":"Amphibia-Reptilia","DOI":"10.1163/017353711X579830","ISSN":"1568-5381, 0173-5373","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"publisher: Brill\\nsection: Amphibia-Reptilia","page":"419-423","source":"brill.com","title":"Clinically healthy amphibians in captive collections and at pet fairs: A reservoir of Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis","title-short":"Clinically healthy amphibians in captive collections and at pet fairs","URL":"https://brill.com/view/journals/amre/32/3/article-p419_13.xml","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Woeltjes","given":"Tonnie"},{"family":"Rendle","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Sluijs","given":"Annemarieke Spitzen-van","dropping-particle":"der"},{"family":"Haesebrouck","given":"Freddy"},{"family":"Martel","given":"An"},{"family":"Pasmans","given":"Frank"},{"family":"Wombwell","given":"Emma"},{"family":"Rooij","given":"Pascale Van"},{"family":"Zollinger","given":"Ronald"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",1,1]]}}},{"id":712,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ASRQ5YI7"],"itemData":{"id":712,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Understanding introduction routes for wildlife pathogens is vital for the development of threat abatement plans. The chytrid fungus Batrachochytrium salamandrivorans (Bsal) has recently emerged in Europe, where it is considered to be a serious threat for urodelan conservation. If the highly diverse Chinese urodelans were to constitute a Bsal reservoir, then the significant international trade in these species may vector Bsal into naïve urodelan communities. Here, we analyzed a total of 1,143 samples, representing 36 Chinese salamander species from 51 localities across southern China for the presence of Bsal. We found Bsal was present across a wide taxonomic, geographical, and environmental range. In particular, Bsal DNA was detected in 33 samples from the genera Cynops, Pachytriton, Paramesotriton, Tylototriton, and Andrias, including the heavily traded species Paramesotriton hongkongensis and Cynops orientalis. The true Bsal prevalence across our data set was estimated between 2% and 4%, with a maximum of 50% in a population of P. hongkongensis. Even at this overall relatively low Bsal prevalence, the exportation of millions of animals renders Bsal introduction in naïve, importing countries a near certainty, which calls for the urgent implementation of proper biosecurity in the international wildlife trade.","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/conl.12436","ISSN":"1755-263X","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"© 2018 The Authors. Conservation Letters published by Wiley Periodicals, Inc.","note":"_eprint: https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/conl.12436","page":"e12436","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Widespread occurrence of an emerging fungal pathogen in heavily traded Chinese urodelan species","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/conl.12436","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Yuan","given":"Zhiyong"},{"family":"Martel","given":"An"},{"family":"Wu","given":"Jun"},{"family":"Praet","given":"Sarah Van"},{"family":"Canessa","given":"Stefano"},{"family":"Pasmans","given":"Frank"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":706,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FRLEUE52"],"itemData":{"id":706,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The amphibian chytrid fungus Batrachochytrium salamandrivorans (Bsal) infects newts and salamanders (urodele amphibians), in which it can cause fatal disease. This pathogen has caused dramatic fire salamander population declines in Belgium, the Netherlands and Germany since its discovery in 2010. Thought to be native to Asia, it has been hypothesised that Bsal was introduced to Europe with the importation of infected amphibians for the commercial pet trade. Following the discovery of Bsal in captive amphibians in the United Kingdom in 2015, we used contact-tracing to identify epidemiologically-linked private amphibian collections in Western Europe. Of 16 linked collections identified, animals were tested from 11 and urodeles tested positive for Bsal in seven, including the identification of the pathogen in Spain for the first time. Mortality of Bsal-positive individuals was observed in five collections. Our results indicate that Bsal is likely widespread within the private amphibian trade, at least in Europe. These findings are important for informing policy regarding Bsal control strategies.","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/s41598-018-31800-z","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"2018 The Author(s)","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"13845","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Epidemiological tracing of Batrachochytrium salamandrivorans identifies widespread infection and associated mortalities in private amphibian collections","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-018-31800-z","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Fitzpatrick","given":"Liam D."},{"family":"Pasmans","given":"Frank"},{"family":"Martel","given":"An"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Andrew A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",9,14]]}}},{"id":713,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S568KE7W"],"itemData":{"id":713,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Salamandra","issue":"4","page":"287–290","source":"Google Scholar","title":"First report of host co-infection of parasitic amphibian chytrid fungi","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Lötters","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Wagner","given":"Norman"},{"family":"Kerres","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Vences","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Steinfartz","given":"Sebastian"},{"family":"Sabino-Pinto","given":"Joana"},{"family":"Seufer","given":"Larissa"},{"family":"Preissler","given":"Kathleen"},{"family":"Schulz","given":"Vanessa"},{"family":"Veith","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":705,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BLXF54G5"],"itemData":{"id":705,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"A new, yet old, threat to amphibians\\nGlobally, populations of amphibians have been severely affected by a disease caused by the fungus Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis. Recently, some European salamander populations were decimated by the emergence of a new, related chytrid fungus, B. salamandrivorans. Martel screened amphibians across continents. This newly emerging threat seems to have originated in Asia and traveled to Europe with salamanders transported as part of the pet trade. Asian salamanders have evolved resistance to the pathogen, but salamanders from other parts of the world are highly susceptible.\\nScience, this issue p. 630\\nEmerging infectious diseases are reducing biodiversity on a global scale. Recently, the emergence of the chytrid fungus Batrachochytrium salamandrivorans resulted in rapid declines in populations of European fire salamanders. Here, we screened more than 5000 amphibians from across four continents and combined experimental assessment of pathogenicity with phylogenetic methods to estimate the threat that this infection poses to amphibian diversity. Results show that B. salamandrivorans is restricted to, but highly pathogenic for, salamanders and newts (Urodela). The pathogen likely originated and remained in coexistence with a clade of salamander hosts for millions of years in Asia. As a result of globalization and lack of biosecurity, it has recently been introduced into naïve European amphibian populations, where it is currently causing biodiversity loss.\\nA new fungal disease from Asia threatens salamanders in Europe, the Middle East, and North Africa.\\nA new fungal disease from Asia threatens salamanders in Europe, the Middle East, and North Africa.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1258268","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6209","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2014, American Association for the Advancement of Science","note":"publisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science\\nsection: Report\\nPMID: 25359973","page":"630-631","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"Recent introduction of a chytrid fungus endangers Western Palearctic salamanders","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/346/6209/630","volume":"346","author":[{"family":"Martel","given":"A."},{"family":"Blooi","given":"M."},{"family":"Adriaensen","given":"C."},{"family":"Rooij","given":"P. Van"},{"family":"Beukema","given":"W."},{"family":"Fisher","given":"M. C."},{"family":"Farrer","given":"R. A."},{"family":"Schmidt","given":"B. R."},{"family":"Tobler","given":"U."},{"family":"Goka","given":"K."},{"family":"Lips","given":"K. R."},{"family":"Muletz","given":"C."},{"family":"Zamudio","given":"K. R."},{"family":"Bosch","given":"J."},{"family":"Lötters","given":"S."},{"family":"Wombwell","given":"E."},{"family":"Garner","given":"T. W. J."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"A. A."},{"family":"Sluijs","given":"A. Spitzen-van","dropping-particle":"der"},{"family":"Salvidio","given":"S."},{"family":"Ducatelle","given":"R."},{"family":"Nishikawa","given":"K."},{"family":"Nguyen","given":"T. T."},{"family":"Kolby","given":"J. E."},{"family":"Bocxlaer","given":"I. Van"},{"family":"Bossuyt","given":"F."},{"family":"Pasmans","given":"F."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",10,31]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The trade of living marine species for aquaria has become a major business ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5vBeIn8W","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rhyne, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Rhyne, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GEBU3NUX"],"itemData":{"id":740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The trade of live marine animals for home and public aquaria has grown into a major global industry. Millions of marine fishes and invertebrates are removed from coral reefs and associated habitats each year. The majority are imported into the United States, with the remainder sent to Europe, Japan, and a handful of other countries. Despite the recent growth and diversification of the aquarium trade, to date, data collection is not mandatory, and hence comprehensive information on species volume and diversity is lacking. This lack of information makes it impossible to study trade pathways. Without species-specific volume and diversity data, it is unclear how importing and exporting governments can oversee this industry effectively or how sustainability should be encouraged. To expand our knowledge and understanding of the trade, and to effectively communicate this new understanding, we introduce the publically-available Marine Aquarium Biodiversity and Trade Flow online database (https://www.aquariumtradedata.org/). This tool was created to communicate the volume and diversity of marine fishes and/or invertebrates imported into the US over three complete years (2008, 2009, and 2011) and three partial years (2000, 2004, 2005). To create this tool, invoices pertaining to shipments of live marine fishes and invertebrates were scanned and analyzed for species name, species quantities, country of origin, port of entry, and city of import destination. Here we focus on the analysis of the later three years of data and also produce an estimate for the entirety of 2000, 2004, and 2005. The three-year aggregate totals (2008, 2009, 2011) indicate that just under 2,300 fish and 725 invertebrate species were imported into the US cumulatively, although just under 1,800 fish and 550 invertebrate species were traded annually. Overall, the total number of live marine animals decreased between 2008 and 2011. In 2008, 2009, and 2011, the total number of individual fish (8.2, 7.3, and 6.9 million individuals) and invertebrates (4.2, 3.7, and 3.6 million individuals) assessed by analyzing the invoice data are roughly 60% of the total volumes recorded through the Law Enforcement Management Information System (LEMIS) dataset. Using these complete years, we back-calculated the number of individuals of both fishes and invertebrates imported in 2000, 2004, and 2005. These estimates (9.3, 10.8, and 11.2 million individual fish per year) were consistent with the three years of complete data. We also use these data to understand the global trade in two species (Banggai cardinalfish, Pterapogon kauderni, and orange clownfish, Amphiprion ocellaris / percula) recently considered for Endangered Species Act listing. Aquariumtradedata.org can help create more effective management plans for the traded species, and ideally could be implemented at key trade ports to better assess the global trade of aquatic wildlife.","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.2949","ISSN":"2167-8359","journalAbbreviation":"PeerJ","language":"en","note":"publisher: PeerJ Inc.","page":"e2949","source":"peerj.com","title":"Expanding our understanding of the trade in marine aquarium animals","URL":"https://peerj.com/articles/2949","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Szczebak","given":"Joseph T."},{"family":"Holmberg","given":"Robert J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,26]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 20 percent of recent wild species trade reports are due to demand for pets or animals for use in entertainment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UDcVLvNB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. E. Baker, 2013; Bush, 2014)","plainCitation":"(S. E. Baker, 2013; Bush, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32742,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SJ82QLSW"],"itemData":{"id":32742,"type":"book","abstract":"International trade in exotic pets is an important and increasing driver of biodiversity loss and often compromises the standards required for good animal welfare. We systematically reviewed the scientific and gray literature and used the United Nations Environment Programme - World Conservation Monitoring Centre (UNEP-WCMC) Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) trade database to establish temporal and geographical trade patterns of live exotic birds, mammals, and reptiles and to describe trends in research, taxonomic representation, and level of threat and legal protection of species traded. Birds were the most species-rich and abundant class reported in trade; reptiles were second most abundant but unusually the most studied in this context; and mammals were least abundant in trade. Mammalian and reptilian species traded as pets were more likely to be threatened than expected by random. There have been a substantial number of Appendix I listed captive-bred mammals and birds and wild-caught birds and reptiles reported in trade to CITES. We identified the Middle East's emerging role as a driver of demand for exotic pets of all taxa alongside the well-established and increasing role of South America and Southeast Asia in the market. Europe, North America, and the Middle East featured most heavily in trade reports to CITES, whereas trade involving South America and Southeast Asia were given most emphasis in the literature. For effective monitoring of and appropriate response to the international exotic pet trade, it is imperative that the reliability and detail of CITES trade reports improve and that scientific research be directed toward those taxa and locations that are most vulnerable. El Mercado Global de Mascotas Exóticas 2006-2012 El mercado internacional de mascotas exóticas es un factor importante y creciente de pérdida de la biodiversidad y continuamente compromete los estándares requeridos para un buen bienestar animal. Sistemáticamente revisamos la literatura científica y gris y usamos la base de datos de mercados de UNEP-WCMC CITES para establecer patrones temporales y geográficos del mercado de aves, mamíferos y reptiles exóticos vivos y describir las tendencias en la investigación, la representación taxonómica y el nivel de amenaza y la protección legal de las especies comercializadas. Las aves fueron la clase con mayor riqueza de especies y la más abundante; los reptiles fueron la segunda más abundante pero inusualmente la más estudiada en este contexto; los mamíferos fueron los menos abundantes en el mercado. Las especies de mamíferos y reptiles comercializadas como mascotas tuvieron mayor probabilidad de estar amenazadas que lo esperado por el azar. Ha habido un número sustancial de especies de mamíferos y aves criados en cautiverio y listados en el Apéndice-I y de aves y reptiles capturados en vida silvestre reportados en el mercado a CITES. Identificamos el papel emergente del Oriente Medio como conductor de la demanda de mascotas exóticas de todos los taxones junto con el bien establecido y creciente papel de América del Sur y el Sureste Asiático en el mercado. Europa, América del Norte y el Oriente Medio figuraron más en los reportes de mercado hechos a CITES, mientras que al mercado en América del Sur y el Sureste Asiático se le dio más énfasis en la literatura. Para un monitoreo efectivo de y una respuesta apropiada al mercado internacional de mascotas exóticas, es imperativo que la confiabilidad y el detalle de los reportes de mercado de CITES mejoren y que la investigación científica sea dirigida hacia esos taxones y las localidades que sean más vulnerables.","collection-number":"3","note":"DOI: 10.1111/cobi.12240","number-of-pages":"663–676","publisher":"Blackwell Publishing Inc.","title":"Global trade in exotic pets 2006-2012","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Bush","given":"Emma R."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Sandra E."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":721,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H8KV6TSV"],"itemData":{"id":721,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","issue":"12","note":"publisher: American Institute of Biological Sciences Circulation, AIBS, 1313 Dolley …","page":"928–938","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Rough trade: animal welfare in the global wildlife trade","title-short":"Rough trade","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Baker","given":"Sandra E."},{"family":"Cain","given":"Russ"},{"family":"Van Kesteren","given":"Freya"},{"family":"Zommers","given":"Zinta A."},{"family":"D'cruze","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	In 2011, 6.9 million individual fish and 3.6 million individual invertebrates have been imported into the United States of America ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"i1y3vwQZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rhyne, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Rhyne, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GEBU3NUX"],"itemData":{"id":740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The trade of live marine animals for home and public aquaria has grown into a major global industry. Millions of marine fishes and invertebrates are removed from coral reefs and associated habitats each year. The majority are imported into the United States, with the remainder sent to Europe, Japan, and a handful of other countries. Despite the recent growth and diversification of the aquarium trade, to date, data collection is not mandatory, and hence comprehensive information on species volume and diversity is lacking. This lack of information makes it impossible to study trade pathways. Without species-specific volume and diversity data, it is unclear how importing and exporting governments can oversee this industry effectively or how sustainability should be encouraged. To expand our knowledge and understanding of the trade, and to effectively communicate this new understanding, we introduce the publically-available Marine Aquarium Biodiversity and Trade Flow online database (https://www.aquariumtradedata.org/). This tool was created to communicate the volume and diversity of marine fishes and/or invertebrates imported into the US over three complete years (2008, 2009, and 2011) and three partial years (2000, 2004, 2005). To create this tool, invoices pertaining to shipments of live marine fishes and invertebrates were scanned and analyzed for species name, species quantities, country of origin, port of entry, and city of import destination. Here we focus on the analysis of the later three years of data and also produce an estimate for the entirety of 2000, 2004, and 2005. The three-year aggregate totals (2008, 2009, 2011) indicate that just under 2,300 fish and 725 invertebrate species were imported into the US cumulatively, although just under 1,800 fish and 550 invertebrate species were traded annually. Overall, the total number of live marine animals decreased between 2008 and 2011. In 2008, 2009, and 2011, the total number of individual fish (8.2, 7.3, and 6.9 million individuals) and invertebrates (4.2, 3.7, and 3.6 million individuals) assessed by analyzing the invoice data are roughly 60% of the total volumes recorded through the Law Enforcement Management Information System (LEMIS) dataset. Using these complete years, we back-calculated the number of individuals of both fishes and invertebrates imported in 2000, 2004, and 2005. These estimates (9.3, 10.8, and 11.2 million individual fish per year) were consistent with the three years of complete data. We also use these data to understand the global trade in two species (Banggai cardinalfish, Pterapogon kauderni, and orange clownfish, Amphiprion ocellaris / percula) recently considered for Endangered Species Act listing. Aquariumtradedata.org can help create more effective management plans for the traded species, and ideally could be implemented at key trade ports to better assess the global trade of aquatic wildlife.","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.2949","ISSN":"2167-8359","journalAbbreviation":"PeerJ","language":"en","note":"publisher: PeerJ Inc.","page":"e2949","source":"peerj.com","title":"Expanding our understanding of the trade in marine aquarium animals","URL":"https://peerj.com/articles/2949","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Rhyne","given":"Andrew L."},{"family":"Tlusty","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Szczebak","given":"Joseph T."},{"family":"Holmberg","given":"Robert J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",1,26]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Singapore is an important global hub of the ornamental aquarium trade in general, and freshwater mollusks in particular  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XQpN7vbI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ng, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Ng, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":741,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5CEA5DC8"],"itemData":{"id":741,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The ornamental pet trade is often considered a key culprit for conservation problems such as the introduction of invasive species (including infectious diseases) and overharvesting of rare species. Here, we present the first assessment of the biodiversity of freshwater molluscs in the ornamental pet trade in Singapore, one of the most important global hubs of the ornamental aquarium trade, and discuss associated conservation concerns. We recorded freshwater molluscs from ornamental pet shops and major exporters including non-ornamental species (e.g., hitchhikers, molluscs sold as fish feed). We recorded an unexpectedly high diversity—59 species—of freshwater bivalves and gastropods, with the majority (38 species or 64%) being from the Oriental region. In addition to morphological examination, we sequenced the DNA barcode region of mitochondrial CO1 and 16S genes to provide molecular data for the confirmation of the identification and for future re-identification. DNA barcodes were obtained for 50 species, and all but four were separated by > 3% uncorrected pairwise distances. The trade has been considered a main introduction pathway for non-native species to Singapore, and we found that out of 15 species in the trade as well as in the wild in Singapore, 12 are either introduced or of unknown origin, representing almost half of the known non-native freshwater molluscs in Singapore. Particularly prevalent are non-ornamental species: six hitchhikers on aquarium plants and six species sold as fish feed. We found that a quarter of the trade species have a history of introduction, which includes 11 known or potentially invasive species. We conclude that potential overharvesting is difficult to assess because only half of the trade species have been treated by IUCN. Of these, 21 species are of Least Concern and three are Data Deficient. Our checklist, with accompanying DNA barcodes, images, and museum vouchers, provides an important reference library for future monitoring, and constitutes a step toward creating a more sustainable ornamental pet trade.","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0161130","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"PLOS ONE","language":"en","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e0161130","source":"PLoS Journals","title":"Molluscs for Sale: Assessment of Freshwater Gastropods and Bivalves in the Ornamental Pet Trade","title-short":"Molluscs for Sale","URL":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0161130","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Ng","given":"Ting Hui"},{"family":"Tan","given":"Siong Kiat"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Wing Hing"},{"family":"Meier","given":"Rudolf"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Sow-Yan"},{"family":"Tan","given":"Heok Hui"},{"family":"Yeo","given":"Darren C. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",8,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  A quarter of the sampled traded species have a history of introduction, which includes 19% that are either certainly or potentially causing negative impacts in their invaded habitats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"T5hmjIPs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ng, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Ng, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":741,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5CEA5DC8"],"itemData":{"id":741,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The ornamental pet trade is often considered a key culprit for conservation problems such as the introduction of invasive species (including infectious diseases) and overharvesting of rare species. Here, we present the first assessment of the biodiversity of freshwater molluscs in the ornamental pet trade in Singapore, one of the most important global hubs of the ornamental aquarium trade, and discuss associated conservation concerns. We recorded freshwater molluscs from ornamental pet shops and major exporters including non-ornamental species (e.g., hitchhikers, molluscs sold as fish feed). We recorded an unexpectedly high diversity—59 species—of freshwater bivalves and gastropods, with the majority (38 species or 64%) being from the Oriental region. In addition to morphological examination, we sequenced the DNA barcode region of mitochondrial CO1 and 16S genes to provide molecular data for the confirmation of the identification and for future re-identification. DNA barcodes were obtained for 50 species, and all but four were separated by > 3% uncorrected pairwise distances. The trade has been considered a main introduction pathway for non-native species to Singapore, and we found that out of 15 species in the trade as well as in the wild in Singapore, 12 are either introduced or of unknown origin, representing almost half of the known non-native freshwater molluscs in Singapore. Particularly prevalent are non-ornamental species: six hitchhikers on aquarium plants and six species sold as fish feed. We found that a quarter of the trade species have a history of introduction, which includes 11 known or potentially invasive species. We conclude that potential overharvesting is difficult to assess because only half of the trade species have been treated by IUCN. Of these, 21 species are of Least Concern and three are Data Deficient. Our checklist, with accompanying DNA barcodes, images, and museum vouchers, provides an important reference library for future monitoring, and constitutes a step toward creating a more sustainable ornamental pet trade.","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0161130","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"PLOS ONE","language":"en","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e0161130","source":"PLoS Journals","title":"Molluscs for Sale: Assessment of Freshwater Gastropods and Bivalves in the Ornamental Pet Trade","title-short":"Molluscs for Sale","URL":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0161130","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Ng","given":"Ting Hui"},{"family":"Tan","given":"Siong Kiat"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Wing Hing"},{"family":"Meier","given":"Rudolf"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Sow-Yan"},{"family":"Tan","given":"Heok Hui"},{"family":"Yeo","given":"Darren C. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",8,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	The endangered redline torpedo barb (Sahyadria denisonii) from the Western Ghats region (Sri Lanka) are caught for the aquarium trade. While this activity provides income to the local communities, the unmanaged fishery has led to unsustainable levels of exploitation, and some populations of torpedo barb have declined sharply, making the danger of a collapse immanent ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YJyWcmW7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Raghavan, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Raghavan, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34378,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XKPR73D8"],"itemData":{"id":34378,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The freshwater aquarium trade provides economic and livelihood opportunities to the rural poor, but has been implicated in biodiversity loss through population declines and local extirpation of high-value endemic species. Previous observations on population declines are largely based on anecdotal data, however, and there are no studies on the population dynamics or stock assessment of freshwater fish species collected for the aquarium pet trade. To understand how wild collection of endemic and threatened species for the aquarium trade could affect their populations, the sustainability of collecting the endangered redline torpedo barb (Sahyadria denisonii), and its evolutionarily distinct lineages (henceforth RLTBs), from the Western Ghats region (part of the Western Ghats–Sri Lanka biodiversity hot spot) for the aquarium trade was studied to provide baseline data to inform conservation action. Local populations of RLTBs at the five commercial collection sites showed high mortality rates, and fish were exploited before they attained sexual maturity. The unmanaged fishery has led to unsustainable levels of exploitation, and populations of RLTBs at three sites are vulnerable to an impending collapse. Endemic and threatened freshwater fish species used in the aquarium pet trade are exposed to local extirpations and global extinctions unless management plans and legislative controls are implemented and strictly enforced. Management plans and conservation actions to help guide the responsible fisheries of RLTBs are suggested, and the wider implications of unmanaged exploitation of endemic aquarium fisheries are discussed.","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1002/aqc.2895","ISSN":"1099-0755","issue":"3","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2018 John Wiley & Sons, Ltd.","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1002/aqc.2895","page":"567-574","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Effect of unmanaged harvests for the aquarium trade on the population status and dynamics of redline torpedo barb: A threatened aquatic flagship","title-short":"Effect of unmanaged harvests for the aquarium trade on the population status and dynamics of redline torpedo barb","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/aqc.2895","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Raghavan","given":"Rajeev"},{"family":"Ali","given":"Anvar"},{"family":"Philip","given":"Siby"},{"family":"Dahanukar","given":"Neelesh"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. International trade also threatens the sustainability of the Banggai cardinalfish (Pterapogon kauderni) that is a popular aquarium fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pYEeixqw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vagelli, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Vagelli, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":711,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/52GUUCC7"],"itemData":{"id":711,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"SPC Live Reef Fish Information Bulletin","note":"publisher: Citeseer","page":"17–28","source":"Google Scholar","title":"The unfortunate journey of Pterapogon kauderni: A remarkable apogonid endangered by the international ornamental fish trade, and its case in CITES","title-short":"The unfortunate journey of Pterapogon kauderni","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Vagelli","given":"Alejandro A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Overall, important knowedge gaps pertain to the scale and scope of trade of ornamental fish and corresponding sustainability outcomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UGM83ESr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biondo & Burki, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Biondo & Burki, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34338,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FTSYRERF"],"itemData":{"id":34338,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The multi-billion dollar trade in ornamental fishes has rarely been reliably monitored. Almost all coral reef fishes are wild-caught, and few scientific analyses have attempted to elicit exact quantities and identify species involved. The consequences of the removal of millions of these fishes are poorly understood. This article collates and examines available information, including scientific studies and formal publications, in order to create a more accurate picture of this commerce. We demonstrate that it is almost impossible to analyse the trade in marine ornamental fishes due to a lack of data, and that available data for marine species is frequently combined with that for freshwater species. Figures range from 15 to 30 million coral reef fishes being traded annually, but could be as high as 150 million specimens. The global value of this trade was only estimated for 1976 and 1999 between USD 28&ndash;40 million. This review highlights the urgent need to introduce a specific harmonised system tariff code and for a global monitoring system, such as the Trade Control and Expert System already in use in Europe, in order to gather accurate and timely information on the number and species of marine ornamental fishes in commerce, where specimens originated, and whether they were wild-caught or captive-bred.","container-title":"Animals","DOI":"10.3390/ani10112014","issue":"11","language":"en","license":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/","note":"number: 11\\npublisher: Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute","page":"2014","source":"www.mdpi.com","title":"A Systematic Review of the Ornamental Fish Trade with Emphasis on Coral Reef Fishes—An Impossible Task","URL":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-2615/10/11/2014","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Biondo","given":"Monica V."},{"family":"Burki","given":"Rainer P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  

Wild species trade: Gathering
While roughly a third of all terrestrial plant species are at risk of extinction, the scale of exploitation is often inconclusive ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"39QXAjpi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Corlett, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Corlett, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":737,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BNKRRWD2"],"itemData":{"id":737,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The conservation of plants has not generated the sense of urgency—or the funding—that drives the conservation of animals, although plants are far more important for us. There are an estimated 500,000 species of land plants (angiosperms, gymnosperms, ferns, lycophytes, and bryophytes), with diversity strongly concentrated in the humid tropics. Many species are still unknown to science. Perhaps a third of all land plants are at risk of extinction, including many that are undescribed, or are described but otherwise data deficient. There have been few known global extinctions so far, but many additional species have not been recorded recently and may be extinct. Although only a minority of plant species have a specific human use, many more play important roles in natural ecosystems and the services they provide, and rare species are more likely to have unusual traits that could be useful in the future. The major threats to plant diversity include habitat loss, fragmentation, and degradation, overexploitation, invasive species, pollution, and anthropogenic climate change. Conservation of plant diversity is a massive task if viewed globally, but the combination of a well-designed and well-managed protected area system and ex situ gap-filling and back-up should work anywhere. The most urgent needs are for the completion of the global botanical inventory and an assessment of the conservation status of the 94% of plant species not yet evaluated, so that both in and ex situ conservation can be targeted efficiently. Globally, the biggest conservation gap is in the hyperdiverse lowland tropics and this is where attention needs to be focused.","container-title":"Plant Diversity","DOI":"10.1016/j.pld.2016.01.001","ISSN":"2468-2659","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Plant Diversity","language":"en","page":"10-16","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Plant diversity in a changing world: Status, trends, and conservation needs","title-short":"Plant diversity in a changing world","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2468265916300300","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Corlett","given":"Richard T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",2,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, the contributing role of trade in enabling unsustainable practices in absence of well-functioning regulations is established, but the exact scale of trade, as well as the sustainability impacts are often incomplete. Especially Southeast-Asia is a region where a massive commercial and often illegal trade of wild collected ornamental plants occur ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"avzzZW7Y","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. C. Hughes, 2017; Phelps & Webb, 2015b)","plainCitation":"(A. C. Hughes, 2017; Phelps & Webb, 2015b)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32524,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L4CR4X9G"],"itemData":{"id":32524,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Southeast Asia (SE Asia) is a known global hotspot of biodiversity and endemism, yet the region is also one of the most biotically threatened. Ecosystems across the region are threatened by an array of drivers, each of which increases the probability of extinction of species in a variety of ecosystems. These issues are symptomatic of the issues that face the global tropics; however, with around 4 billion people in the wider region and the associated pressures on biodiversity, this region may be under some of the greatest levels of biotic threat. Deforestation rates in SE Asia are some of the highest globally, additionally it has the highest rate of mining in the tropics, around the greatest number of hydropower dams under construction, and a consumption of species for traditional medicines which is a threat to biodiversity globally. In this review, the greatest threats to regional biodiversity in the SE Asian region are discussed. Tree-plantations and deforestation represent one of the most imminent threats, and some countries have already lost over half their original forest cover (i.e., the Philippines, parts of Indonesia), with projections of as much as 98% loss for some regions in the coming decade. Hunting and trade represent a significant threat as demand stems not only for food, but also for medicine, for ornamentation, and as a status symbol. Mining represents a frequently overlooked threat, as the Asian region is one of the greatest exporters of limestone and various minerals globally, and the cost of this to biodiversity is not only through the direct loss of areas for mines, but also through the development of roads that further fragment the landscape, the leakage of heavy metals, and the destruction of limestone karsts, which represent global endemicity hotspots. Reservoir construction, wetland drainage, fires, pollution, invasive species, disease, and finally climate change are also considered. Once each issue has been discussed, the overall prognosis of regional biodiversity and priority actions to protect SE Asian biodiversity in the future is discussed.","container-title":"Ecosphere","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ecs2.1624","ISSN":"2150-8925","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"© 2017 Hughes.","note":"_eprint: https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1002/ecs2.1624","page":"e01624","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Understanding the drivers of Southeast Asian biodiversity loss","URL":"https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/ecs2.1624","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Hughes","given":"Alice C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":32526,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5BUYUBQK"],"itemData":{"id":32526,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Conservation and environmental management often rely heavily on official statistics, but there are often concerns over accuracy and reliability, particularly true when dealing with sensitive issues such as illegal harvest and trade. A growing number of cases highlight commercial trades in wild flora and fauna that have been undocumented in official data. Here we present the first in-depth study of the trade of wild-collected ornamental plants in continental Southeast Asia, focusing on the four largest wildlife markets in Thailand. Botanical surveys revealed a massive, previously undocumented commercial trade in wild, protected ornamental plants involving Thailand, Lao PDR and Myanmar, focusing primarily on the Orchidaceae. The results indicate that illegal trade threatens not only charismatic Southeast Asian mammals, reptiles and amphibians, but also hundreds of tropical plant species. Trade surveys identified 347 orchid species in 93 genera, including many listed as threatened. The observed cross-border trade moves plants at a rate orders of magnitude larger than government-reported statistics, and directly conflicts with official policy statement. Harvester interviews strongly indicated that wild collection was negatively affecting the majority of species they traded. Despite three decades of broad restrictions on the international trade of all wild orchids, these results highlight a major conservation challenge that has been almost completely overlooked. There is urgent need to improve botanical trade monitoring, to operationalize existing conservation commitments, and as part of a broader, multifaceted response to illegal trade. We call for increased attention to botanical trade and conservation in Southeast Asia, and argue that efforts to tackle illegal wildlife trade must ensure they include flora.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2015.03.030","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","page":"296-305","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"“Invisible” wildlife trades: Southeast Asia’s undocumented illegal trade in wild ornamental plants","title-short":"“Invisible” wildlife trades","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S000632071500141X","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Phelps","given":"J."},{"family":"Webb","given":"Edward L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",6,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Observed cross-border trade tends to be orders of magnitude larger than government-reported and the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora statistics ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mj9ly6ev","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Phelps and Webb 2015)","plainCitation":"(J. Phelps and Webb 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32526,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5BUYUBQK"],"itemData":{"id":32526,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Conservation and environmental management often rely heavily on official statistics, but there are often concerns over accuracy and reliability, particularly true when dealing with sensitive issues such as illegal harvest and trade. A growing number of cases highlight commercial trades in wild flora and fauna that have been undocumented in official data. Here we present the first in-depth study of the trade of wild-collected ornamental plants in continental Southeast Asia, focusing on the four largest wildlife markets in Thailand. Botanical surveys revealed a massive, previously undocumented commercial trade in wild, protected ornamental plants involving Thailand, Lao PDR and Myanmar, focusing primarily on the Orchidaceae. The results indicate that illegal trade threatens not only charismatic Southeast Asian mammals, reptiles and amphibians, but also hundreds of tropical plant species. Trade surveys identified 347 orchid species in 93 genera, including many listed as threatened. The observed cross-border trade moves plants at a rate orders of magnitude larger than government-reported statistics, and directly conflicts with official policy statement. Harvester interviews strongly indicated that wild collection was negatively affecting the majority of species they traded. Despite three decades of broad restrictions on the international trade of all wild orchids, these results highlight a major conservation challenge that has been almost completely overlooked. There is urgent need to improve botanical trade monitoring, to operationalize existing conservation commitments, and as part of a broader, multifaceted response to illegal trade. We call for increased attention to botanical trade and conservation in Southeast Asia, and argue that efforts to tackle illegal wildlife trade must ensure they include flora.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2015.03.030","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","page":"296-305","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"“Invisible” wildlife trades: Southeast Asia’s undocumented illegal trade in wild ornamental plants","title-short":"“Invisible” wildlife trades","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S000632071500141X","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Phelps","given":"J."},{"family":"Webb","given":"Edward L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",6,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Plant populations are declining across South-East Asia because of overharvesting to meet high demand from Chinese traditional medicine and herbal products industry. There are also many documented cases of trade in plants, algae and fungi leading to resource depletion (e.g. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Up9Ta3PY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Neumann & Hirsch, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Neumann & Hirsch, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33446,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YP4HH3XY"],"itemData":{"id":33446,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Cifor","title":"Commercialisation of non-timber forest products: review and analysis of research","author":[{"family":"Neumann","given":"R.P."},{"family":"Hirsch","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1cPgqTvk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(B. Belcher, 2005)","plainCitation":"(B. Belcher, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7922,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/65VMMKJU"],"itemData":{"id":7922,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"There is a range of forest management systems between pure extraction and plantation systems. Such \\"intermediate systems\\" range from wild forests modified for increased production of selected products to anthropogenic forests with a high-density of valuable species growing within a relatively diverse and complex structure. These systems, classed here as \\"Forest Garden Systems\\" (FGS), have important socioeconomic and ecological benefits, and yet they have been largely overlooked by researchers, development practitioners, and policy makers. Based on case examples and the authors' experience, this paper analyzes the socioeconomic and institutional factors that explain the development, persistence, and decline of FGS. These systems combine productivity and biodiversity values and are important components in the diverse economic systems of their managers. As such, the model warrants increased attention to protect existing values, to support the adaptation of existing systems to changing circumstances , and to inform the development of new models of integrated forest management.","container-title":"Economic Botany","issue":"3","page":"245-253","title":"THE SOCIOECONOMIC CONDITIONS DETERMINING THE DEVELOPMENT, PERSISTENCE, AND DECLINE OF FOREST GARDEN SYSTEMS","URL":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1663/0013-0001(2005)059%5B0245:TSCDTD%5D2.0.CO%3B2.pdf","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Michon","given":"Genevieve"},{"family":"Angelsen","given":"Arild"},{"family":"Ruiz Perez","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Asbjornsen","given":"Heidi"},{"family":"Ruiz P~rez","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Ashjornsen","given":"Heidi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In a comparative study on plants, algae, and fungi trade in Asia, Africa and Latin America, Kusters ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CUFDpG5M","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2006)","plainCitation":"(2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32783,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DZJ7RMR6"],"itemData":{"id":32783,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"2","note":"publisher: JSTOR","title":"Balancing development and conservation? An assessment of livelihood and environmental outcomes of nontimber forest product trade in Asia, Africa, and Latin America","URL":"http://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol11/iss2/art20/","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Kusters","given":"Koen"},{"family":"Achdiawan","given":"Ramadhani"},{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Pérez","given":"Manuel Ruiz"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} conclude that trade of plants, algae, and fungi products tends to lead to positive livelihood outcomes, though perhaps also higher inequality between households. At the same time trade leads to resource depletion if left unmanaged. 
	Trade is an important driver that threatens orchids globally. All 29,000 orchid species are listed by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, which comprise 70% of all species listed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vJPmsXWs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gale, 2019; Hinsley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Gale, 2019; Hinsley, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":745,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4UIEEK6T"],"itemData":{"id":745,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Despite the grave threat illegal wildlife trade poses to species survival, few studies have attempted to link supply and demand data for the same wildlife product. All ca. 29,000 orchid species are listed by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and many are protected under domestic legislation too, but a growing body of evidence suggests that orchids continue to be subject to unsustainable harvesting and undocumented trade. South China is a known black spot for trade in wild-collected ornamental orchids but understanding of the drivers determining the flow of species diversity, volume and value remains wanting. We conducted systematic monthly surveys at five markets along a West-East transect from Yunnan to Hong Kong for one year, recording variables including species, numbers of individuals, weight and price. Although wild orchid diversity is highest in Yunnan, the diversity of orchids in trade increased eastwards and mean price per stem rose more than four-fold, albeit always significantly cheaper than that for artificially produced hybrids. Part of this trade appears to be in breach of CITES. Few orchids in trade conformed to six criteria highlighted in prior demand-side studies as being of higher utility value, but most conformed to a combination of four or more, suggesting that vendors can readily offer products that meet a majority of consumer preferences. Effective supply-side regulation, through government intervention and social media campaigns, is needed to facilitate behavioural change and allow artificially propagated plants to compete in the market-place.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2019.108204","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"108204","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Quantifying the trade in wild-collected ornamental orchids in South China: Diversity, volume and value gradients underscore the primacy of supply","title-short":"Quantifying the trade in wild-collected ornamental orchids in South China","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320719307979","volume":"238","author":[{"family":"Gale","given":"Stephan W."},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Pankaj"},{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Cheuk","given":"Mang Lung"},{"family":"Gao","given":"Jiangyun"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Hong"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Zhi-Long"},{"family":"Williams","given":"Sophie J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,1]]}}},{"id":32354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SU6WGFBJ"],"itemData":{"id":32354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society","DOI":"10.1093/botlinnean/box083","ISSN":"0024-4074, 1095-8339","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"435-455","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A review of the trade in orchids and its implications for conservation","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/botlinnean/article/186/4/435/4736317","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Boer","given":"Hugo J","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Fay","given":"Michael F"},{"family":"Gale","given":"Stephan W"},{"family":"Gardiner","given":"Lauren M"},{"family":"Gunasekara","given":"Rajasinghe S"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Pankaj"},{"family":"Masters","given":"Susanne"},{"family":"Metusala","given":"Destario"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L"},{"family":"Veldman","given":"Sarina"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Shan"},{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In spite of being officially protected, many orchid species around the world are under threat from illegal and unsustainable trade for horticulture, food and medicine ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DdHr5j8V","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SU6WGFBJ"],"itemData":{"id":32354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society","DOI":"10.1093/botlinnean/box083","ISSN":"0024-4074, 1095-8339","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"435-455","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A review of the trade in orchids and its implications for conservation","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/botlinnean/article/186/4/435/4736317","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Boer","given":"Hugo J","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Fay","given":"Michael F"},{"family":"Gale","given":"Stephan W"},{"family":"Gardiner","given":"Lauren M"},{"family":"Gunasekara","given":"Rajasinghe S"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Pankaj"},{"family":"Masters","given":"Susanne"},{"family":"Metusala","given":"Destario"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L"},{"family":"Veldman","given":"Sarina"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Shan"},{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While orchid trade is concentrated in Asian countries, such as China and Nepal ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Vu58Zy5m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018; Subedi, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018; Subedi, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SU6WGFBJ"],"itemData":{"id":32354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society","DOI":"10.1093/botlinnean/box083","ISSN":"0024-4074, 1095-8339","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"435-455","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A review of the trade in orchids and its implications for conservation","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/botlinnean/article/186/4/435/4736317","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Boer","given":"Hugo J","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Fay","given":"Michael F"},{"family":"Gale","given":"Stephan W"},{"family":"Gardiner","given":"Lauren M"},{"family":"Gunasekara","given":"Rajasinghe S"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Pankaj"},{"family":"Masters","given":"Susanne"},{"family":"Metusala","given":"Destario"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L"},{"family":"Veldman","given":"Sarina"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Shan"},{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3,27]]}}},{"id":32801,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FRT4JZE5"],"itemData":{"id":32801,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wild orchids are illegally harvested and traded in Nepal for use in local traditional medicine, horticulture, and international trade. This study aims to: 1) identify the diversity of species of wild orchids in trade in Nepal; 2) study the chain of commercialization from collector to client and/or export; 3) map traditional knowledge and medicinal use of orchids; and 4) integrate the collected data to propose a more sustainable approach to orchid conservation in Nepal. Trade, species diversity, and traditional use of wild-harvested orchids were documented during field surveys of markets and through interviews. Trade volumes and approximate income were estimated based on surveys and current market prices. Orchid material samples were identified to species level using a combination of morphology and DNA barcoding. Orchid trade is a long tradition, and illegal export to China, India and Hong Kong is rife. Estimates show that 9.4 tons of wild orchids were illegally traded from the study sites during 2008/2009. A total of 60 species of wild orchids were reported to be used in traditional medicinal practices to cure at least 38 different ailments, including energizers, aphrodisiacs and treatments of burnt skin, fractured or dislocated bones, headaches, fever and wounds. DNA barcoding successfully identified orchid material to species level that remained sterile after culturing. Collection of wild orchids was found to be widespread in Nepal, but illegal trade is threatening many species in the wild. Establishment of small-scale sustainable orchid breeding enterprises could be a valuable alternative for the production of medicinal orchids for local communities. Critically endangered species should be placed on CITES Appendix I to provide extra protection to those species. DNA barcoding is an effective method for species identification and monitoring of illegal cross-border trade.","archive":"Readcube","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/1746-4269-9-64","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","page":"64","title":"Collection and trade of wild-harvested orchids in Nepal","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1186/1746-4269-9-64","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Subedi","given":"Abishkar"},{"family":"Kunwar","given":"Bimal"},{"family":"Choi","given":"Young"},{"family":"Dai","given":"Yuntao"},{"family":"Andel","given":"Tinde","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"Ram P."},{"family":"Boer","given":"Hugo J.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Gravendeel","given":"Barbara"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, it takes place at a global scale. In Mexico, wild orchids are frequently traded on local markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QfDMAZec","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. Cruz-Garcia, 2015)","plainCitation":"(G. Cruz-Garcia, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":746,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8MHGM2SF"],"itemData":{"id":746,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-015-9321-z","ISSN":"0013-0001","language":"Inglés estadounidense","note":"publisher: Springer New York","page":"291-305","source":"ipn.elsevierpure.com","title":"The Wild Orchid Trade in a Mexican Local Market: Diversity and Economics","title-short":"The Wild Orchid Trade in a Mexican Local Market","URL":"https://ipn.elsevierpure.com/es/publications/the-wild-orchid-trade-in-a-mexican-local-market-diversity-and-eco","author":[{"family":"Cruz-Garcia","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Lagunez-Rivera","given":"Lucita"},{"family":"Chavez-Angeles","given":"Manuel Gerardo"},{"family":"Solano-Gomez","given":"Rodolfo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The harvesters and sellers are mostly women, with little or no formal schooling and come from indigenous communities. Often, the orchid trade is not the sellers’ only economic activity, but an important part of a poverty alleviating strategy ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ihx1Kwmx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. Cruz-Garcia, 2015)","plainCitation":"(G. Cruz-Garcia, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":746,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8MHGM2SF"],"itemData":{"id":746,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-015-9321-z","ISSN":"0013-0001","language":"Inglés estadounidense","note":"publisher: Springer New York","page":"291-305","source":"ipn.elsevierpure.com","title":"The Wild Orchid Trade in a Mexican Local Market: Diversity and Economics","title-short":"The Wild Orchid Trade in a Mexican Local Market","URL":"https://ipn.elsevierpure.com/es/publications/the-wild-orchid-trade-in-a-mexican-local-market-diversity-and-eco","author":[{"family":"Cruz-Garcia","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Lagunez-Rivera","given":"Lucita"},{"family":"Chavez-Angeles","given":"Manuel Gerardo"},{"family":"Solano-Gomez","given":"Rodolfo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Different harvesting practices persist, in particular in situ techniques (removing flowers and leaving roots and renewal buds) and ex situ techniques (extracting entire wild plants), where the latter is thought to be less sustainable.  Though trading of wild species is regulated by Mexican law, these laws are often not known to harvesters, and also not applicable as trade of wild orchids is considered a traditional practice and therefore locally allowed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qpJXzsm7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. Cruz-Garcia, 2015)","plainCitation":"(G. Cruz-Garcia, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":746,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8MHGM2SF"],"itemData":{"id":746,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-015-9321-z","ISSN":"0013-0001","language":"Inglés estadounidense","note":"publisher: Springer New York","page":"291-305","source":"ipn.elsevierpure.com","title":"The Wild Orchid Trade in a Mexican Local Market: Diversity and Economics","title-short":"The Wild Orchid Trade in a Mexican Local Market","URL":"https://ipn.elsevierpure.com/es/publications/the-wild-orchid-trade-in-a-mexican-local-market-diversity-and-eco","author":[{"family":"Cruz-Garcia","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Lagunez-Rivera","given":"Lucita"},{"family":"Chavez-Angeles","given":"Manuel Gerardo"},{"family":"Solano-Gomez","given":"Rodolfo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Cacti are among the most threatened taxonomic groups assessed to date  under the International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List Categories and Criteria, with 31% of the 1,478 evaluated species threatened, demonstrating the high anthropogenic pressures on biodiversity in arid lands. The dominant drivers of extinction risk are the unscrupulous collection of live plants and seeds for horticultural trade and private ornamental collections, smallholder livestock ranching and smallholder annual agriculture ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YrLKEmHQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Goettsch, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Goettsch, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32062,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G9MWISD6"],"itemData":{"id":32062,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Plants","DOI":"10.1038/nplants.2015.142","ISSN":"2055-0278","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Nature Plants","language":"en","page":"15142","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"High proportion of cactus species threatened with extinction","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nplants2015142","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Goettsch","given":"Bárbara"},{"family":"Hilton-Taylor","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Cruz-Piñón","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Duffy","given":"James P."},{"family":"Frances","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Hernández","given":"Héctor M."},{"family":"Inger","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Pollock","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Schipper","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Superina","given":"Mariella"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Nigel P."},{"family":"Tognelli","given":"Marcelo"},{"family":"Abba","given":"Agustín M."},{"family":"Arias","given":"Salvador"},{"family":"Arreola-Nava","given":"Hilda J."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Marc A."},{"family":"Bárcenas","given":"Rolando T."},{"family":"Barrios","given":"Duniel"},{"family":"Braun","given":"Pierre"},{"family":"Butterworth","given":"Charles A."},{"family":"Búrquez","given":"Alberto"},{"family":"Caceres","given":"Fátima"},{"family":"Chazaro-Basañez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Corral-Díaz","given":"Rafael"},{"family":"Valle Perea","given":"Mario","non-dropping-particle":"del"},{"family":"Demaio","given":"Pablo H."},{"family":"Duarte de Barros","given":"Williams A."},{"family":"Durán","given":"Rafael"},{"family":"Yancas","given":"Luis Faúndez"},{"family":"Felger","given":"Richard S."},{"family":"Fitz-Maurice","given":"Betty"},{"family":"Fitz-Maurice","given":"Walter A."},{"family":"Gann","given":"George"},{"family":"Gómez-Hinostrosa","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Gonzales-Torres","given":"Luis R."},{"family":"Patrick Griffith","given":"M."},{"family":"Guerrero","given":"Pablo C."},{"family":"Hammel","given":"Barry"},{"family":"Heil","given":"Kenneth D."},{"family":"Hernández-Oria","given":"José Guadalupe"},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Ishihara","given":"Mario Ishiki"},{"family":"Kiesling","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Larocca","given":"João"},{"family":"León-de la Luz","given":"José Luis"},{"family":"Loaiza S.","given":"Christian R."},{"family":"Lowry","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Machado","given":"Marlon C."},{"family":"Majure","given":"Lucas C."},{"family":"Ávalos","given":"José Guadalupe Martínez"},{"family":"Martorell","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Maschinski","given":"Joyce"},{"family":"Méndez","given":"Eduardo"},{"family":"Mittermeier","given":"Russell A."},{"family":"Nassar","given":"Jafet M."},{"family":"Negrón-Ortiz","given":"Vivian"},{"family":"Oakley","given":"Luis J."},{"family":"Ortega-Baes","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"Ana Beatriz Pin"},{"family":"Pinkava","given":"Donald J."},{"family":"Porter","given":"J. Mark"},{"family":"Puente-Martinez","given":"Raul"},{"family":"Gamarra","given":"José Roque"},{"family":"Pérez","given":"Patricio Saldivia"},{"family":"Martínez","given":"Emiliano Sánchez"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Manuel Sotomayor M. del C.","given":"J."},{"family":"Stuart","given":"Simon N."},{"family":"Muñoz","given":"José Luis Tapia"},{"family":"Terrazas","given":"Teresa"},{"family":"Terry","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Trevisson","given":"Marcelo"},{"family":"Valverde","given":"Teresa"},{"family":"Van Devender","given":"Thomas R."},{"family":"Véliz-Pérez","given":"Mario Esteban"},{"family":"Walter","given":"Helmut E."},{"family":"Wyatt","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"Zappi","given":"Daniela"},{"family":"Alejandro Zavala-Hurtado","given":"J."},{"family":"Gaston","given":"Kevin J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Across the literature, there was inconclusive evidence regarding whether trade could be contributing towards incentivizing sustainable use via higher prices and stability of income provided to livelihoods. Also, the social and economic impacts were often inconclusive. Gathering is a practice often conducted by women of all ages with little or no formal education, who do often not have access to alternative economic activities. In Sierra Leone, more than 30 plant species are traded for medicinal purposes, three of which are considered vulnerable under the the International Union for Conservation of Nature Red list: Garcinia kola, Fleroya stipulosa, and Nauclea diderrichii ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LQpnZUHy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jusu & Sanchez, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Jusu & Sanchez, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":747,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IASFFKXA"],"itemData":{"id":747,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This study used a quantitative market survey to examine the ecological importance of the medicinal plant trade in Sierra Leone and the existing trading systems, so that it could be determined (1) if trade has a negative impact on the species traded, and/or (2) if trade could be used to support conservation projects. We interviewed vendors in three major cities and two towns and carried out focus–group discussions among collectors at forest edge communities. In the markets, specimen samples were collected and identified. In several forests, observations were made on harvesting techniques and relative abundance. More than 40 species are traded in urban markets, nine of which are the most frequently traded. Some plants are transported great distances to reach the urban markets, especially Xylopia aethiopica (Dunal) A.Rich. and Garcinia kola Heckel. Certain species might not be sustainably harvested depending on the collector practices (e.g., ring debarking, tree felling), and this might threaten these species, especially Piper guineense Schumach. & Thonn. However, results also suggest that the trade of some species could be promoted as an alternative livelihood strategy for edge communities of forest reserves. In this latter case, special attention should be paid to sustainable harvesting techniques.","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-013-9255-2","ISSN":"1874-9364","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"16-29","source":"Springer Link","title":"Medicinal Plant Trade in Sierra Leone: Threats and Opportunities for Conservation","title-short":"Medicinal Plant Trade in Sierra Leone","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s12231-013-9255-2","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Jusu","given":"Amadu"},{"family":"Sanchez","given":"Aida Cuni"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Whether or not harvesting practices are sustainable largely depends on the actions of the collector. However, in a few cases (e.g. P. guineense) the species are never sustainably harvested ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vD05Gs9i","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jusu & Sanchez, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Jusu & Sanchez, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":747,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IASFFKXA"],"itemData":{"id":747,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This study used a quantitative market survey to examine the ecological importance of the medicinal plant trade in Sierra Leone and the existing trading systems, so that it could be determined (1) if trade has a negative impact on the species traded, and/or (2) if trade could be used to support conservation projects. We interviewed vendors in three major cities and two towns and carried out focus–group discussions among collectors at forest edge communities. In the markets, specimen samples were collected and identified. In several forests, observations were made on harvesting techniques and relative abundance. More than 40 species are traded in urban markets, nine of which are the most frequently traded. Some plants are transported great distances to reach the urban markets, especially Xylopia aethiopica (Dunal) A.Rich. and Garcinia kola Heckel. Certain species might not be sustainably harvested depending on the collector practices (e.g., ring debarking, tree felling), and this might threaten these species, especially Piper guineense Schumach. & Thonn. However, results also suggest that the trade of some species could be promoted as an alternative livelihood strategy for edge communities of forest reserves. In this latter case, special attention should be paid to sustainable harvesting techniques.","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-013-9255-2","ISSN":"1874-9364","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"16-29","source":"Springer Link","title":"Medicinal Plant Trade in Sierra Leone: Threats and Opportunities for Conservation","title-short":"Medicinal Plant Trade in Sierra Leone","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s12231-013-9255-2","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Jusu","given":"Amadu"},{"family":"Sanchez","given":"Aida Cuni"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Medicinal species that are traded in the largest volumes, sell at the highest prices, and travel the greatest distances, are most likely to be unsustainably harvested. A key issue in unsustainable use is the harvesting technique, as a number of species are harvested unsustainably (e.g. removing whole plants; ring debarking) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LWFb9TdN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jusu & Sanchez, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Jusu & Sanchez, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":747,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IASFFKXA"],"itemData":{"id":747,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This study used a quantitative market survey to examine the ecological importance of the medicinal plant trade in Sierra Leone and the existing trading systems, so that it could be determined (1) if trade has a negative impact on the species traded, and/or (2) if trade could be used to support conservation projects. We interviewed vendors in three major cities and two towns and carried out focus–group discussions among collectors at forest edge communities. In the markets, specimen samples were collected and identified. In several forests, observations were made on harvesting techniques and relative abundance. More than 40 species are traded in urban markets, nine of which are the most frequently traded. Some plants are transported great distances to reach the urban markets, especially Xylopia aethiopica (Dunal) A.Rich. and Garcinia kola Heckel. Certain species might not be sustainably harvested depending on the collector practices (e.g., ring debarking, tree felling), and this might threaten these species, especially Piper guineense Schumach. & Thonn. However, results also suggest that the trade of some species could be promoted as an alternative livelihood strategy for edge communities of forest reserves. In this latter case, special attention should be paid to sustainable harvesting techniques.","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-013-9255-2","ISSN":"1874-9364","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"16-29","source":"Springer Link","title":"Medicinal Plant Trade in Sierra Leone: Threats and Opportunities for Conservation","title-short":"Medicinal Plant Trade in Sierra Leone","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s12231-013-9255-2","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Jusu","given":"Amadu"},{"family":"Sanchez","given":"Aida Cuni"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}  Caterpillar mushroom (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) is a medicinal fungus found in alpine grasslands in the Himalayan mountain regions and the Tibetan Plateau ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2O8vgsCs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(He, 2018)","plainCitation":"(He, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":748,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F7R3X8Y2"],"itemData":{"id":748,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ethnopharmacological relevance\\nCaterpillar mushroom (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) is a unique medicinal fungi which is only found in alpine grasslands in Himalayan mountain regions and the Tibetan Plateau. Known locally as Yartsa Gunbu, it has been widely used in Tibetan and Chinese Medicine for centuries. It is crucial to understand local commercial harvest and trade practices of caterpillar mushroom to support the sustainable management of this valuable resource. However, data derived from empirically grounded research is currently limited, particularly in China.\\nAim of this study\\nThe research aims to provide the most up-to-date insights into caterpillar mushroom harvest and trade in the main production area of the Tibet Region in Southwest China and to generate policy recommendations for sustainable use.\\nMaterials and methods\\nThe research was conducted in 2015–2016 in six Tibetan communities located in two counties in Diqing Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture, Southwest China. Quantitative and qualitative data were collected from in-depth interviews with local households engaged in caterpillar mushroom harvesting (n?=?157), local caterpillar mushroom traders (n?=?14), and from focus groups discussions (n?=?5) with regional caterpillar mushroom industry stakeholders.\\nResults\\nThe research found large regional- and community-level differences in caterpillar mushroom harvest practices. The harvest practices of communities involved in the co-management of a Nature Reserve were more sustainable than those communities not involved in such a scheme, and this was due to the external support and training provided via the co-management scheme. Moreover, a customary tenure system was proving effective for avoiding competition over caterpillar mushroom collection. However, in both counties, narrow marketing channel and non-grading system in trade limits the possibility of improving the local benefits generated from the commercial harvest of caterpillar mushroom. Meanwhile, the local traders play an important bridging role in the value chain and generate greater benefits from product grading.\\nConclusion\\nTo support the sustainable management of the caterpillar mushroom industry in Southwest China, the prefectural governments should invest in training on appropriate harvesting techniques and the dissemination of market information. It is also critical that prefectural governments recognize and support the customary tenure system of mushroom collection to avoid competition between collectors.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2018.04.022","ISSN":"0378-8741","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","language":"en","page":"86-90","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Harvest and trade of caterpillar mushroom (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) and the implications for sustainable use in the Tibet Region of Southwest China","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378874118302459","volume":"221","author":[{"family":"He","given":"Jun"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The harvest practices of communities involved in a co-management scheme of the nature reserve were more sustainable than those communities not involved in such a scheme. This difference was largely due to clarity and security or tenure and resource access, and also because of external support and training in sustainable practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4Jut3WMh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(He, 2018)","plainCitation":"(He, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":748,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F7R3X8Y2"],"itemData":{"id":748,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ethnopharmacological relevance\\nCaterpillar mushroom (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) is a unique medicinal fungi which is only found in alpine grasslands in Himalayan mountain regions and the Tibetan Plateau. Known locally as Yartsa Gunbu, it has been widely used in Tibetan and Chinese Medicine for centuries. It is crucial to understand local commercial harvest and trade practices of caterpillar mushroom to support the sustainable management of this valuable resource. However, data derived from empirically grounded research is currently limited, particularly in China.\\nAim of this study\\nThe research aims to provide the most up-to-date insights into caterpillar mushroom harvest and trade in the main production area of the Tibet Region in Southwest China and to generate policy recommendations for sustainable use.\\nMaterials and methods\\nThe research was conducted in 2015–2016 in six Tibetan communities located in two counties in Diqing Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture, Southwest China. Quantitative and qualitative data were collected from in-depth interviews with local households engaged in caterpillar mushroom harvesting (n?=?157), local caterpillar mushroom traders (n?=?14), and from focus groups discussions (n?=?5) with regional caterpillar mushroom industry stakeholders.\\nResults\\nThe research found large regional- and community-level differences in caterpillar mushroom harvest practices. The harvest practices of communities involved in the co-management of a Nature Reserve were more sustainable than those communities not involved in such a scheme, and this was due to the external support and training provided via the co-management scheme. Moreover, a customary tenure system was proving effective for avoiding competition over caterpillar mushroom collection. However, in both counties, narrow marketing channel and non-grading system in trade limits the possibility of improving the local benefits generated from the commercial harvest of caterpillar mushroom. Meanwhile, the local traders play an important bridging role in the value chain and generate greater benefits from product grading.\\nConclusion\\nTo support the sustainable management of the caterpillar mushroom industry in Southwest China, the prefectural governments should invest in training on appropriate harvesting techniques and the dissemination of market information. It is also critical that prefectural governments recognize and support the customary tenure system of mushroom collection to avoid competition between collectors.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2018.04.022","ISSN":"0378-8741","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","language":"en","page":"86-90","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Harvest and trade of caterpillar mushroom (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) and the implications for sustainable use in the Tibet Region of Southwest China","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378874118302459","volume":"221","author":[{"family":"He","given":"Jun"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An obstacle towards ecological and economic sustainability is the difficulty to generate greater local benefits along the value chain, also when it comes to product grading which could incentivize more sustainable practices (e.g. not harvesting pre-mature mushrooms) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"V2W8uI2W","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(He, 2018)","plainCitation":"(He, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":748,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F7R3X8Y2"],"itemData":{"id":748,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ethnopharmacological relevance\\nCaterpillar mushroom (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) is a unique medicinal fungi which is only found in alpine grasslands in Himalayan mountain regions and the Tibetan Plateau. Known locally as Yartsa Gunbu, it has been widely used in Tibetan and Chinese Medicine for centuries. It is crucial to understand local commercial harvest and trade practices of caterpillar mushroom to support the sustainable management of this valuable resource. However, data derived from empirically grounded research is currently limited, particularly in China.\\nAim of this study\\nThe research aims to provide the most up-to-date insights into caterpillar mushroom harvest and trade in the main production area of the Tibet Region in Southwest China and to generate policy recommendations for sustainable use.\\nMaterials and methods\\nThe research was conducted in 2015–2016 in six Tibetan communities located in two counties in Diqing Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture, Southwest China. Quantitative and qualitative data were collected from in-depth interviews with local households engaged in caterpillar mushroom harvesting (n?=?157), local caterpillar mushroom traders (n?=?14), and from focus groups discussions (n?=?5) with regional caterpillar mushroom industry stakeholders.\\nResults\\nThe research found large regional- and community-level differences in caterpillar mushroom harvest practices. The harvest practices of communities involved in the co-management of a Nature Reserve were more sustainable than those communities not involved in such a scheme, and this was due to the external support and training provided via the co-management scheme. Moreover, a customary tenure system was proving effective for avoiding competition over caterpillar mushroom collection. However, in both counties, narrow marketing channel and non-grading system in trade limits the possibility of improving the local benefits generated from the commercial harvest of caterpillar mushroom. Meanwhile, the local traders play an important bridging role in the value chain and generate greater benefits from product grading.\\nConclusion\\nTo support the sustainable management of the caterpillar mushroom industry in Southwest China, the prefectural governments should invest in training on appropriate harvesting techniques and the dissemination of market information. It is also critical that prefectural governments recognize and support the customary tenure system of mushroom collection to avoid competition between collectors.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"10.1016/j.jep.2018.04.022","ISSN":"0378-8741","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","language":"en","page":"86-90","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Harvest and trade of caterpillar mushroom (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) and the implications for sustainable use in the Tibet Region of Southwest China","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378874118302459","volume":"221","author":[{"family":"He","given":"Jun"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the Palas valley (Pakistan), several species of morels (Morchella spp.) are collected by local families and traded all over Pakistan. A continuous decline in supply was observed, potentially indicating unsustainable use in the past ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MwffX7Oz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sher, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Sher, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32796,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/STJDGBRD"],"itemData":{"id":32796,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Morels of Palas Valley, Pakistan: A Potential Source for Generating Income and Improving Livelihoods of Mountain Communities. The local economy in the Palas Valley of Pakistan is agro-pastoral, and people rely on income and subsistence from livestock production followed by wild-collection of morels. The purpose of this study was to investigate the market potential of morels, their folk uses, traditions, and habitat conditions. Market and field surveys were conducted in various regions of the country. The information regarding ethnic uses of morels and the traditional beliefs of people in the Palas Valley and its surroundings were recorded. The data we collected included supply and demand, pricing, and market fluctuations and trends. A specially designed questionnaire was used to gather details from focus groups, and individual discussions and personal interviews during field visits were conducted. The study areas included: Palas Valley, Peshawar, Lahore, and Karachi, which are the major herbal market places in Pakistan. The Palas Valley was found to have the potential and natural resources to become an excellent market for morels. The results of the present study indicate that five species of morels (Morchella esculenta, M. conica, M. elata, M. deliciosa, and M. gigas) growing in Palas Valley are traded all over Pakistan. We found that a total of 15,000 kg morels from the Palas Valley were exported annually to national and international markets. This generated significant revenue (USD 343,000 per year) at the valley level. Local people, especially herdsmen, together with their wives and children, were mainly collecting this resource. Local knowledge about the methods of morel collection, drying, storage, and market price was limited. A continuous decline was observed in morel market supply in Pakistan. The main reasons identified included quality variation due to poor harvest treatments, trade practices of middlemen, availability of materials, and poor marketing strategies. The study concludes that morels are a potential source of income generation and improving livelihoods for underprivileged mountain communities. However, morel populations seem to be gradually declining due to possibly unsustainable collection techniques. There is tremendous potential to boost the income of ethnic communities by promoting training and enhancing the trade of morels based on modern scientific studies. This would not only bring considerable income opportunities for the population of Palas Valley, but would also decrease their dependence on timber sales and thus decrease the pressure on local forests.","ISSN":"0013-0001","language":"en","source":"pubag.nal.usda.gov","title":"Morels of Palas Valley, Pakistan: A Potential Source for Generating Income and Improving Livelihoods of Mountain Communities","title-short":"Morels of Palas Valley, Pakistan","URL":"https://pubag.nal.usda.gov/catalog/4776293","author":[{"family":"Sher","given":"Hassan"},{"family":"Aldosari","given":"Ali"},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"Rainer W."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Though morels could play a role in supporting livelihoods, unsustainable collection techniques, unfavorable trade practices and limited income generation along the value chains pose obstacles  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"F4MEJMz6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sher, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Sher, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32796,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/STJDGBRD"],"itemData":{"id":32796,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Morels of Palas Valley, Pakistan: A Potential Source for Generating Income and Improving Livelihoods of Mountain Communities. The local economy in the Palas Valley of Pakistan is agro-pastoral, and people rely on income and subsistence from livestock production followed by wild-collection of morels. The purpose of this study was to investigate the market potential of morels, their folk uses, traditions, and habitat conditions. Market and field surveys were conducted in various regions of the country. The information regarding ethnic uses of morels and the traditional beliefs of people in the Palas Valley and its surroundings were recorded. The data we collected included supply and demand, pricing, and market fluctuations and trends. A specially designed questionnaire was used to gather details from focus groups, and individual discussions and personal interviews during field visits were conducted. The study areas included: Palas Valley, Peshawar, Lahore, and Karachi, which are the major herbal market places in Pakistan. The Palas Valley was found to have the potential and natural resources to become an excellent market for morels. The results of the present study indicate that five species of morels (Morchella esculenta, M. conica, M. elata, M. deliciosa, and M. gigas) growing in Palas Valley are traded all over Pakistan. We found that a total of 15,000 kg morels from the Palas Valley were exported annually to national and international markets. This generated significant revenue (USD 343,000 per year) at the valley level. Local people, especially herdsmen, together with their wives and children, were mainly collecting this resource. Local knowledge about the methods of morel collection, drying, storage, and market price was limited. A continuous decline was observed in morel market supply in Pakistan. The main reasons identified included quality variation due to poor harvest treatments, trade practices of middlemen, availability of materials, and poor marketing strategies. The study concludes that morels are a potential source of income generation and improving livelihoods for underprivileged mountain communities. However, morel populations seem to be gradually declining due to possibly unsustainable collection techniques. There is tremendous potential to boost the income of ethnic communities by promoting training and enhancing the trade of morels based on modern scientific studies. This would not only bring considerable income opportunities for the population of Palas Valley, but would also decrease their dependence on timber sales and thus decrease the pressure on local forests.","ISSN":"0013-0001","language":"en","source":"pubag.nal.usda.gov","title":"Morels of Palas Valley, Pakistan: A Potential Source for Generating Income and Improving Livelihoods of Mountain Communities","title-short":"Morels of Palas Valley, Pakistan","URL":"https://pubag.nal.usda.gov/catalog/4776293","author":[{"family":"Sher","given":"Hassan"},{"family":"Aldosari","given":"Ali"},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"Rainer W."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Stimulating and incentivizing the use of best practices of sustainable harvesting and collection methods can be an important step towards sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mouvJkR7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Becerra, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Becerra, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":719,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6I5W5QXQ"],"itemData":{"id":719,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Becerra, María Teresa","language":"en","note":"publisher: UN,","source":"digitallibrary.un.org","title":"Guidelines for the development and implementation of management plans for wild-collected plant species used by organizations working with natural ingredients","URL":"http://digitallibrary.un.org/record/658602","author":[{"family":"Becerra","given":"María Teresa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	In central Australian rangelands several native plant products (including Solanum centrale J.M. Black, Acacia Mill. spp.) are commercially harvested and traded at a small scale ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PIxoquvY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. Walsh & Douglas, 2011)","plainCitation":"(F. Walsh & Douglas, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":751,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RKTZKMM4"],"itemData":{"id":751,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Improvement in Aboriginal people’s livelihoods and economic opportunities has been a major aim of increased research and development on bush foods over the past decade. But worldwide the development of trade in non-timber forest products from natural populations has raised questions about the ecological sustainability of harvest. Trade-offs and tensions between commercialisation and cultural values have also been found. We investigated the sustainability of the small-scale commercial harvest and trade in native plant products sourced from central Australian rangelands (including Solanum centrale J.M. Black, Acacia Mill. spp.). We used semi-structured interviews with traders and Aboriginal harvesters, participant observation of trading and harvesting trips, and analysis of species and trader records. An expert Aboriginal reference group guided the project. We found no evidence of either taxa being vulnerable to over-harvest. S. centrale production is enhanced by harvesting when it co-occurs with patch-burning. Extreme fluctuations in productivity of both taxa, due to inter-annual rainfall variability, have a much greater impact on supply than harvest effects. Landscape-scale degradation (including cattle grazing and wildfire) affected ecological sustainability according to participants. By contrast, we found that sustainability of bush food trade is more strongly impacted by social and economic factors. The relationship-based links between harvesters and traders are critical to monetary trade. Harvesters and traders identified access to productive lands and narrow economic margins between costs and returns as issues for the future sustainability of harvest and trade. Harvesters and the reference group emphasised that sustaining bush harvest relies on future generations having necessary knowledge and skills; these are extremely vulnerable to loss. Aboriginal people derive multiple livelihood benefits from harvest and trade. Aboriginal custodians and harvester groups involved in recent trade are more likely to benefit from research and development investment to inter-generational knowledge and skill transfer than from investments in plant breeding and commercial horticultural development. In an inductive comparison, our study found there to be strong alignment between key findings about the strategies used by harvesters and traders in bush produce and the ‘desert system’..","container-title":"The Rangeland Journal","DOI":"10.1071/RJ11028","ISSN":"1834-7541","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Rangel. J.","language":"en","note":"publisher: CSIRO PUBLISHING","page":"395-416","source":"www.publish.csiro.au","title":"No bush foods without people: the essential human dimension to the sustainability of trade in native plant products from desert Australia","title-short":"No bush foods without people","URL":"https://www.publish.csiro.au/rj/RJ11028","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Walsh","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Douglas","given":"Josie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While there is no evidence of overharvesting yet, narrow economic margins may increase future pressure on the resource. Also, sustainable practices rely heavily on future generations having necessary knowledge and skills ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ezFVuYVS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. Walsh & Douglas, 2011)","plainCitation":"(F. Walsh & Douglas, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":751,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RKTZKMM4"],"itemData":{"id":751,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Improvement in Aboriginal people’s livelihoods and economic opportunities has been a major aim of increased research and development on bush foods over the past decade. But worldwide the development of trade in non-timber forest products from natural populations has raised questions about the ecological sustainability of harvest. Trade-offs and tensions between commercialisation and cultural values have also been found. We investigated the sustainability of the small-scale commercial harvest and trade in native plant products sourced from central Australian rangelands (including Solanum centrale J.M. Black, Acacia Mill. spp.). We used semi-structured interviews with traders and Aboriginal harvesters, participant observation of trading and harvesting trips, and analysis of species and trader records. An expert Aboriginal reference group guided the project. We found no evidence of either taxa being vulnerable to over-harvest. S. centrale production is enhanced by harvesting when it co-occurs with patch-burning. Extreme fluctuations in productivity of both taxa, due to inter-annual rainfall variability, have a much greater impact on supply than harvest effects. Landscape-scale degradation (including cattle grazing and wildfire) affected ecological sustainability according to participants. By contrast, we found that sustainability of bush food trade is more strongly impacted by social and economic factors. The relationship-based links between harvesters and traders are critical to monetary trade. Harvesters and traders identified access to productive lands and narrow economic margins between costs and returns as issues for the future sustainability of harvest and trade. Harvesters and the reference group emphasised that sustaining bush harvest relies on future generations having necessary knowledge and skills; these are extremely vulnerable to loss. Aboriginal people derive multiple livelihood benefits from harvest and trade. Aboriginal custodians and harvester groups involved in recent trade are more likely to benefit from research and development investment to inter-generational knowledge and skill transfer than from investments in plant breeding and commercial horticultural development. In an inductive comparison, our study found there to be strong alignment between key findings about the strategies used by harvesters and traders in bush produce and the ‘desert system’..","container-title":"The Rangeland Journal","DOI":"10.1071/RJ11028","ISSN":"1834-7541","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Rangel. J.","language":"en","note":"publisher: CSIRO PUBLISHING","page":"395-416","source":"www.publish.csiro.au","title":"No bush foods without people: the essential human dimension to the sustainability of trade in native plant products from desert Australia","title-short":"No bush foods without people","URL":"https://www.publish.csiro.au/rj/RJ11028","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Walsh","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Douglas","given":"Josie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Transkei, located in the Eastern Cape province of South Africa, hand brushes made from fronds of the wild date palm (Phoenix reclinata) are locally made and traded to nearby urban areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5PY4IyFK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mjoli & Shackleton, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Mjoli & Shackleton, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":750,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NX49FN6M"],"itemData":{"id":750,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Trade in and Household Use of Phoenix reclinata Palm Frond Hand Brushes on the Wild Coast, South Africa. This paper reports on an investigation of the harvesting, trade, and use of hand brushes made from fronds of the wild palm, Phoenix reclinata. We considered both the abundance of the resource as well as the demand. Within the harvesting areas, there were approximately 141 palm plants per hectare, of which almost two-thirds showed no signs of frond harvesting. During harvesting, most fronds (82%) were left on the plant, 16% were removed to make brushes, and 2% were cut and discarded. Although the number of harvesters had increased during the last decade, most felt that the number of palm plants had remained stable or even increased over the same period. There was strong consensus that cut fronds were replaced within two months, after which a particular stem could be harvested again. Harvesting and trade were practiced largely by middle-aged to elderly women, who had limited formal education, skills, and employment prospects. Most had entered the trade because of cash income poverty. The main markets for selling the palm brushes were in nearby urban areas. The income earned from the trade was modest, but still rated highly by the traders, for most of whom it was the second most-important source of cash income. For many users, the palm brushes was found to be the only type of brush suitable for cleaning mud and cow-dung flooring and, most importantly for many, their use forms part of a long household use history and culture.","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-015-9316-9","ISSN":"1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"218-229","source":"Springer Link","title":"The Trade in and Household Use of Phoenix reclinata Palm Frond Hand Brushes on the Wild Coast, South Africa","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s12231-015-9316-9","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Mjoli","given":"Nwabisa"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",9,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The key actors involved in harvesting and trading are middle-aged to elderly women with little formal education and opportunities to earn cash income elsewhere. The trade of palm brushed played a significant role in supporting livelihoods of local traders. Demand is stable, if not increasing, in spite of increasing availability of synthetic substitutes because of cultural and practical value attached to palm brushes. As of now, there is no evidence that current practice is unsustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Wv30iC5d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mjoli & Shackleton, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Mjoli & Shackleton, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":750,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NX49FN6M"],"itemData":{"id":750,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Trade in and Household Use of Phoenix reclinata Palm Frond Hand Brushes on the Wild Coast, South Africa. This paper reports on an investigation of the harvesting, trade, and use of hand brushes made from fronds of the wild palm, Phoenix reclinata. We considered both the abundance of the resource as well as the demand. Within the harvesting areas, there were approximately 141 palm plants per hectare, of which almost two-thirds showed no signs of frond harvesting. During harvesting, most fronds (82%) were left on the plant, 16% were removed to make brushes, and 2% were cut and discarded. Although the number of harvesters had increased during the last decade, most felt that the number of palm plants had remained stable or even increased over the same period. There was strong consensus that cut fronds were replaced within two months, after which a particular stem could be harvested again. Harvesting and trade were practiced largely by middle-aged to elderly women, who had limited formal education, skills, and employment prospects. Most had entered the trade because of cash income poverty. The main markets for selling the palm brushes were in nearby urban areas. The income earned from the trade was modest, but still rated highly by the traders, for most of whom it was the second most-important source of cash income. For many users, the palm brushes was found to be the only type of brush suitable for cleaning mud and cow-dung flooring and, most importantly for many, their use forms part of a long household use history and culture.","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1007/s12231-015-9316-9","ISSN":"1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"218-229","source":"Springer Link","title":"The Trade in and Household Use of Phoenix reclinata Palm Frond Hand Brushes on the Wild Coast, South Africa","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s12231-015-9316-9","volume":"69","author":[{"family":"Mjoli","given":"Nwabisa"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",9,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the Congo Basin, bush mango (Irvingia spp.) nuts are harvested from forest landscapes for own consumption and trade, contributing on average to 31% of harvester's annual incomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Xi390aMP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. Ingram, 2017)","plainCitation":"(V. Ingram, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":861,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4PP7KFH8"],"itemData":{"id":861,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Across the Congo Basin, bush mango (Irvingia spp.) nuts have been harvested from forest landscapes for consumption, sold as a foodstuff and for medicine for centuries. Data on this trade however are sparse. A value chain approach was used to gather information on stakeholders in the chain from the harvesters in three major production areas in Cameroon to traders in Cameroon, Nigeria, and Equatorial Guinea, the socio-economic values, environmental sustainability and governance. Around 5190 people work in the complex chain in Cameroon with an estimated 4109tons harvested on average annually in the period 2007 to 2010. Bush mango incomes contribute on average to 31% of harvester's annual incomes and dependence increases for those further from the forest. Customary rules govern access to resources. Although regulations exist, most trade is illegal, with corruption and collective action governing access to markets. The majority of nuts harvested are sustainably collected. Although 51% of the harvest is sourced from the forest, trees are also managed on cultivated land. Forest degradation and deforestation threaten the species. Policy measures such as linking stakeholders, promoting cultivation, pragmatic regulation, and supporting processer groups may make trade in this forest food more sustainable.","collection-title":"Forest, Food, and Livelihoods","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2016.12.014","ISSN":"1389-9341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"29-37","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Challenges to governing sustainable forest food: Irvingia spp. from southern Cameroon","title-short":"Challenges to governing sustainable forest food","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1389934116304798","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Ewane","given":"Marcus"},{"family":"Ndumbe","given":"Louis Njie"},{"family":"Awono","given":"Abdon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Evidence regarding sustainability of harvesting is mixed. On the one hand, harvesters tend to gather fallen fruits, and trees are left or actively managed on farmland, suggesting sustainable use. On the other hand, reports of declining wild resources, the need for harvesters to travel longer distances, together with clearance of the species' natural habitat, low levels of cultivation, continuing high demand, and a lack of consistent regulation and enforcement may threaten sustainability  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FMh4iIdy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. Ingram, 2017)","plainCitation":"(V. Ingram, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":861,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4PP7KFH8"],"itemData":{"id":861,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Across the Congo Basin, bush mango (Irvingia spp.) nuts have been harvested from forest landscapes for consumption, sold as a foodstuff and for medicine for centuries. Data on this trade however are sparse. A value chain approach was used to gather information on stakeholders in the chain from the harvesters in three major production areas in Cameroon to traders in Cameroon, Nigeria, and Equatorial Guinea, the socio-economic values, environmental sustainability and governance. Around 5190 people work in the complex chain in Cameroon with an estimated 4109tons harvested on average annually in the period 2007 to 2010. Bush mango incomes contribute on average to 31% of harvester's annual incomes and dependence increases for those further from the forest. Customary rules govern access to resources. Although regulations exist, most trade is illegal, with corruption and collective action governing access to markets. The majority of nuts harvested are sustainably collected. Although 51% of the harvest is sourced from the forest, trees are also managed on cultivated land. Forest degradation and deforestation threaten the species. Policy measures such as linking stakeholders, promoting cultivation, pragmatic regulation, and supporting processer groups may make trade in this forest food more sustainable.","collection-title":"Forest, Food, and Livelihoods","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.forpol.2016.12.014","ISSN":"1389-9341","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Policy and Economics","language":"en","page":"29-37","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Challenges to governing sustainable forest food: Irvingia spp. from southern Cameroon","title-short":"Challenges to governing sustainable forest food","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1389934116304798","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Ewane","given":"Marcus"},{"family":"Ndumbe","given":"Louis Njie"},{"family":"Awono","given":"Abdon"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Across the Congo basin, the leaves of the Gnetum spp. forest lianas have long been harvested from humid forests for consumption and traded as a popular vegetable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"40lUbckK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. Ingram, 2012)","plainCitation":"(V. Ingram, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":756,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DZHV3FFW"],"itemData":{"id":756,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The leaves of these Gnetum spp. lianas have long been harvested from humid forests for consumption and traded as a popular vegetable across the Congo Basin. Recent data on the current environmental, social and economic aspects of this forest product are however sparse. Value chain analysis was used to gather information on stakeholders involved in the chain from forest to consumer (small scale harvesters, traders, transporters, exporters and consumers), and on the socioeconomic values, volumes, sustainability and governance in major production areas and markets in Cameroon and Nigeria. At least 2,550 people work across the chain, which has seven main routes from forest to consumers. Gnetum contributes on average to 62% of a harvester’s annual income (1,125 US$). Dependence upon Gnetum-based incomes increases for those further from the forest, providing an average of 75% of retailer’s (1,268 US$) and 58% of exporter’s annual incomes (7,000 US$). The better organised Nigerian wholesalers earn almost double their Cameroonian counterparts. Simple processing neither adds much value nor greatly reduces perishability. Whilst a rudimentary regulatory framework exists, trade is mostly illegal. Sporadic customary governance does not fill the void left by unenforced and inappropriate regulation. Over 50% is unsustainably collected from the forest and harvesting over the last 5 years increased as demand rose, particularly from Nigeria. Decreasing availability, increasing prices and low levels of cultivation have led to an unsustainable trade. Policy measures such as linking chain stakeholders, promoting cultivation, pragmatic regulation and enforcing customary control may enhance long-term survival of these IUCN Red List near-threatened species and ensure the continuity of their contribution to livelihood and economic development to continue.","container-title":"Small-scale Forestry","DOI":"10.1007/s11842-012-9200-8","ISSN":"1873-7854","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Small-scale Forestry","language":"en","page":"539-556","source":"Springer Link","title":"Small Scale, High Value: Gnetumafricanum and buchholzianum Value Chains in Cameroon","title-short":"Small Scale, High Value","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11842-012-9200-8","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Ndumbe","given":"Louis Njie"},{"family":"Ewane","given":"Marcus Elah"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At least 2,550 people work across the value chain, Gnetum contributing on average to 62% of a harvester’s annual income. Over 50% is unsustainably collected from the forest and rising demand, increasing prices and low levels of cultivation put further pressure on the resource ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"g0KPmcJA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. Ingram, 2012)","plainCitation":"(V. Ingram, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":756,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DZHV3FFW"],"itemData":{"id":756,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The leaves of these Gnetum spp. lianas have long been harvested from humid forests for consumption and traded as a popular vegetable across the Congo Basin. Recent data on the current environmental, social and economic aspects of this forest product are however sparse. Value chain analysis was used to gather information on stakeholders involved in the chain from forest to consumer (small scale harvesters, traders, transporters, exporters and consumers), and on the socioeconomic values, volumes, sustainability and governance in major production areas and markets in Cameroon and Nigeria. At least 2,550 people work across the chain, which has seven main routes from forest to consumers. Gnetum contributes on average to 62% of a harvester’s annual income (1,125 US$). Dependence upon Gnetum-based incomes increases for those further from the forest, providing an average of 75% of retailer’s (1,268 US$) and 58% of exporter’s annual incomes (7,000 US$). The better organised Nigerian wholesalers earn almost double their Cameroonian counterparts. Simple processing neither adds much value nor greatly reduces perishability. Whilst a rudimentary regulatory framework exists, trade is mostly illegal. Sporadic customary governance does not fill the void left by unenforced and inappropriate regulation. Over 50% is unsustainably collected from the forest and harvesting over the last 5 years increased as demand rose, particularly from Nigeria. Decreasing availability, increasing prices and low levels of cultivation have led to an unsustainable trade. Policy measures such as linking chain stakeholders, promoting cultivation, pragmatic regulation and enforcing customary control may enhance long-term survival of these IUCN Red List near-threatened species and ensure the continuity of their contribution to livelihood and economic development to continue.","container-title":"Small-scale Forestry","DOI":"10.1007/s11842-012-9200-8","ISSN":"1873-7854","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Small-scale Forestry","language":"en","page":"539-556","source":"Springer Link","title":"Small Scale, High Value: Gnetumafricanum and buchholzianum Value Chains in Cameroon","title-short":"Small Scale, High Value","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11842-012-9200-8","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Ndumbe","given":"Louis Njie"},{"family":"Ewane","given":"Marcus Elah"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Trade is mostly illegal, and sporadic customary governance and enforcement, and an rudimentary framework cannot ensure trade to be sustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RNZTZZ2v","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. Ingram, 2012)","plainCitation":"(V. Ingram, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":756,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DZHV3FFW"],"itemData":{"id":756,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The leaves of these Gnetum spp. lianas have long been harvested from humid forests for consumption and traded as a popular vegetable across the Congo Basin. Recent data on the current environmental, social and economic aspects of this forest product are however sparse. Value chain analysis was used to gather information on stakeholders involved in the chain from forest to consumer (small scale harvesters, traders, transporters, exporters and consumers), and on the socioeconomic values, volumes, sustainability and governance in major production areas and markets in Cameroon and Nigeria. At least 2,550 people work across the chain, which has seven main routes from forest to consumers. Gnetum contributes on average to 62% of a harvester’s annual income (1,125 US$). Dependence upon Gnetum-based incomes increases for those further from the forest, providing an average of 75% of retailer’s (1,268 US$) and 58% of exporter’s annual incomes (7,000 US$). The better organised Nigerian wholesalers earn almost double their Cameroonian counterparts. Simple processing neither adds much value nor greatly reduces perishability. Whilst a rudimentary regulatory framework exists, trade is mostly illegal. Sporadic customary governance does not fill the void left by unenforced and inappropriate regulation. Over 50% is unsustainably collected from the forest and harvesting over the last 5 years increased as demand rose, particularly from Nigeria. Decreasing availability, increasing prices and low levels of cultivation have led to an unsustainable trade. Policy measures such as linking chain stakeholders, promoting cultivation, pragmatic regulation and enforcing customary control may enhance long-term survival of these IUCN Red List near-threatened species and ensure the continuity of their contribution to livelihood and economic development to continue.","container-title":"Small-scale Forestry","DOI":"10.1007/s11842-012-9200-8","ISSN":"1873-7854","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Small-scale Forestry","language":"en","page":"539-556","source":"Springer Link","title":"Small Scale, High Value: Gnetumafricanum and buchholzianum Value Chains in Cameroon","title-short":"Small Scale, High Value","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11842-012-9200-8","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Ingram","given":"Verina"},{"family":"Ndumbe","given":"Louis Njie"},{"family":"Ewane","given":"Marcus Elah"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Gaharu (agarwood) is a highly valuable fragrant derived from Aquilaria spp. (Thymelaeaceae) that is traded internationally. In Indonesia, traditional harvesting practices are declining, as more nonlocal collectors become involved, leading to more intensive harvesting practices. More intensified and less careful collection suggest that the current Indonesian trade in gaharu is not sustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GclZKBjq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Soehartono & Newton, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Soehartono & Newton, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32797,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X99HHXXS"],"itemData":{"id":32797,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"When subjected to fungal attack, Aquilaria spp. (Thymelaeaceae) produce a fragrant resin that is traded internationally as gaharu. Socioeconomic aspects of the gaharu trade were investigated via interviews with collectors and local and international traders. In addition, the extent of local and international trade was evaluated by reference to official government statistics. Evidence that gaharu resources are declining was obtained from the personal experience of gaharu collectors, and official statistics relating to the declining number of gaharu export companies in operation. Traders also reported that the main source of gaharu has recently switched from Sumatra and Kalimantan to sources in eastern Indonesia (Maluku and Irian Jaya), a finding supported by official statistics. Disparities recorded between official figures for the price and volume of gaharu in local and international trade, supported by comments made by export traders, indicate that a high proportion of the more valuable, high-grade gaharu is traded illegally by personal transaction. Interviews with gaharu collectors indicated that traditional approaches to harvesting are declining, as more nonlocal people become involved in collection, leading to more intensive harvesting practices. Together, these findings suggest that the current Indonesian trade in gaharu is not sustainable.","container-title":"Economic Botany","DOI":"10.1663/0013-0001(2002)056[0271:TGTIII]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"1874-9364","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Econ Bot","language":"en","page":"271-284","source":"Springer Link","title":"The gaharu trade in Indonesia: Is it sustainable?","title-short":"The gaharu trade in Indonesia","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1663/0013-0001(2002)056[0271:TGTIII]2.0.CO;2","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Soehartono","given":"Tonny"},{"family":"Newton","given":"Adrian C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002",9,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the case of argan oil, the boom has enabled some rural households in Morocco to send their girls to secondary school, increase consumption, but also increase their goat herds which impacts negatively on the argan forest as well as privatization pressures ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Cv4Snifc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lybbert, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Lybbert, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30582,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S2NUHSXZ"],"itemData":{"id":30582,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Morocco's argan oil is now the most expensive edible oil in the world. High-value argan markets have sparked a bonanza of argan activity. Nongovernmental organizations, international and domestic development agencies, and argan oil cooperatives aggressively promote the win–win aim of simultaneously benefiting local people and the health of the argan forest. This paper tests some of these win–win claims. Analysis of a panel of detailed household data suggests that the boom has enabled some rural households to increase consumption, increase their goat herds (which bodes poorly for the argan forest), and send their girls to secondary school. The boom has predictably made households vigilant guardians of fruit on the tree, but it has not incited investments in longer term tree and forest health. We evaluate landscape-level impacts of these changes using commune-level data on educational enrollment and normalized difference vegetation index data over the period from 1981 to 2009. The results of the mesoanalysis of enrollment are consistent with the microanalysis: the argan boom seems to have improved educational outcomes, especially for girls. Our normalized difference vegetation index analysis, however, suggests that booming argan prices have not improved the forest and may have even induced degradation. We conclude by exploring the dynamic interactions between argan markets, local institutions, rural household welfare, and forest conservation and sustainability.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1106382108","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"34","journalAbbreviation":"PNAS","language":"en","note":"PMID: 21873185","page":"13963-13968","source":"www.pnas.org","title":"Booming markets for Moroccan argan oil appear to benefit some rural households while threatening the endemic argan forest","URL":"https://www.pnas.org/content/108/34/13963","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Lybbert","given":"Travis J."},{"family":"Aboudrare","given":"Abdellah"},{"family":"Chaloud","given":"Deborah"},{"family":"Magnan","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Nash","given":"Maliha"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8,23]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. To use wild medicinal plant resources sustainably, both conservation strategies (e.g. in situ and ex situ conservation) and resource management (e.g. good agricultural practices) should be considered ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XRJSlu9j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S.-L. Chen, 2016; Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","plainCitation":"(S.-L. Chen, 2016; Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32800,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XRRJTBFC"],"itemData":{"id":32800,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Medicinal plants are globally valuable sources of herbal products, and they are disappearing at a high speed. This article reviews global trends, developments and prospects for the strategies and methodologies concerning the conservation and sustainable use of medicinal plant resources to provide a reliable reference for the conservation and sustainable use of medicinal plants. We emphasized that both conservation strategies (e.g. in situ and ex situ conservation and cultivation practices) and resource management (e.g. good agricultural practices and sustainable use solutions) should be adequately taken into account for the sustainable use of medicinal plant resources. We recommend that biotechnical approaches (e.g. tissue culture, micropropagation, synthetic seed technology, and molecular marker-based approaches) should be applied to improve yield and modify the potency of medicinal plants.","container-title":"Chinese Medicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13020-016-0108-7","ISSN":"1749-8546","issue":"1","page":"37","title":"Conservation and sustainable use of medicinal plants: problems, progress, and prospects","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13020-016-0108-7","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Chen","given":"Shi-Lin"},{"family":"Yu","given":"Hua"},{"family":"Luo","given":"Hong-Mei"},{"family":"Wu","given":"Qiong"},{"family":"Li","given":"Chun-Fang"},{"family":"Steinmetz","given":"André"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":7370,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7TN9Z64K","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/77KTC5ZS"],"itemData":{"id":7370,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","DOI":"http://doi.org/10.18352/ijc.139","issue":"1","page":"100-121","title":"Vicuña conservation and poverty alleviation? Andean communities and international fibre markets","URL":"https://www.thecommonsjournal.org/articles/10.18352/ijc.139/","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein, Gabriela","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Wild species trade: Terrestrial animal harvesting
Excessive hunting pressure, due in large part to commercialization and trade, is unsustainable and has reduced the populations of many tropical large mammal species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VQ3vTjFl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ben\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}tez-L\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}pez, 2017; Brashares & Gaynor, 2017; R. J. Lee, 2005; Milner-Gulland & Bennett, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Benítez-López, 2017; Brashares & Gaynor, 2017; R. J. Lee, 2005; Milner-Gulland & Bennett, 2003)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CHE4Y68S"],"itemData":{"id":32795,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Excessive hunting pressure, due in large part to commercialization, has reduced the populations of many tropical large mammal species. Wildlife over-exploitation is severe in Indonesia, especially on Sulawesi, where human resources and funding are inadequate to monitor the wildlife trade and enforce existing protection laws. In response, the Wildlife Crimes Unit program was established in December 2001 to: (i) monitor wildlife transportation into North Sulawesi and market sales; (ii) provide legal and technical support to law enforcement agencies; and (iii) promote public awareness of wildlife and protection laws. Over a two-year period, 6963 wild mammals en route to markets were encountered (?8 individuals h?1) and 96,586 wild mammals were documented during market surveys. The trade of some protected mammals declined significantly over this period, but overall trade in wild mammals increased by 30%. High volume of trade in non-protected animals such as the Sulawesi pig Sus celebensis and large flying foxes (Pteropodidae), raise concerns about the sustainability of current harvesting. To combat this problem, we recommend that: (1) efforts are continued to reduce trade in protected species; (2) protected status is extended to heavily traded but non-protected taxa, such as flying foxes; (3) the effects of hunting on rat and bat populations, as well as its impact on forest dynamics, are quantified; and (4) work is carried out with local communities to strengthen awareness, set sustainable limits on wild mammal harvesting, and establish practical mechanisms for enforcing these limits.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2005.01.009","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"477-488","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Wildlife trade and implications for law enforcement in Indonesia: a case study from North Sulawesi","title-short":"Wildlife trade and implications for law enforcement in Indonesia","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320705000078","volume":"123","author":[{"family":"Lee","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Gorog","given":"Antonia J."},{"family":"Dwiyahreni","given":"Asri"},{"family":"Siwu","given":"Stephan"},{"family":"Riley","given":"Jon"},{"family":"Alexander","given":"Harry"},{"family":"Paoli","given":"Gary D."},{"family":"Ramono","given":"Widodo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",6,1]]}}},{"id":32799,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4S2SAEPX"],"itemData":{"id":32799,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aan0499","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6334","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"136-137","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Eating ecosystems","URL":"https://www.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/science.aan0499","volume":"356","author":[{"family":"Brashares","given":"Justin S."},{"family":"Gaynor","given":"Kaitlyn M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",4,14]]}}},{"id":32806,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/47YTIETH"],"itemData":{"id":32806,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/S0169-5347(03)00123-X","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"351-357","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Wild meat: the bigger picture","title-short":"Wild meat","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S016953470300123X","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."},{"family":"Bennett","given":"Elizabeth L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",7]]}}},{"id":32743,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N2HX86CJ"],"itemData":{"id":32743,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Quantifying hunting-induced defaunation\\nAs the human population grows and increasingly encroaches on remaining wildlife habitat, hunting threatens many species. Benítez-López conducted a large-scale meta-analysis of hunting trends and impacts across the tropics (see the Perspective by Brashares and Gaynor). Bird and mammal populations were considerably lower in areas where hunting occurred. Although commercial hunting and proximity to roads and urban centers were the most damaging factors, all hunting had worrying impacts, even in protected areas. Protection and alternative approaches for sustainable subsistence hunting must be implemented soon if we are to prevent further, rapid defaunation.\\nScience, this issue p. 180; see also p. 136\\nHunting is a major driver of biodiversity loss, but a systematic large-scale estimate of hunting-induced defaunation is lacking. We synthesized 176 studies to quantify hunting-induced declines of mammal and bird populations across the tropics. Bird and mammal abundances declined by 58% (25 to 76%) and by 83% (72 to 90%) in hunted compared with unhunted areas. Bird and mammal populations were depleted within 7 and 40 kilometers from hunters’ access points (roads and settlements). Additionally, hunting pressure was higher in areas with better accessibility to major towns where wild meat could be traded. Mammal population densities were lower outside protected areas, particularly because of commercial hunting. Strategies to sustainably manage wild meat hunting in both protected and unprotected tropical ecosystems are urgently needed to avoid further defaunation.\\nHunting of wildlife significantly affects mammal and bird populations across the tropics.\\nHunting of wildlife significantly affects mammal and bird populations across the tropics.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aaj1891","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6334","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2017, American Association for the Advancement of Science","note":"publisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science\\nsection: Reports\\nPMID: 28408600","page":"180-183","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"The impact of hunting on tropical mammal and bird populations","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/356/6334/180","volume":"356","author":[{"family":"Benítez-López","given":"A."},{"family":"Alkemade","given":"R."},{"family":"Schipper","given":"A. M."},{"family":"Ingram","given":"D. J."},{"family":"Verweij","given":"P. A."},{"family":"Eikelboom","given":"J. a. J."},{"family":"Huijbregts","given":"M. a. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",4,14]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Regarding socio-economic impacts, hunting provides income and protein to local hunters, but if it does not go hand in hand with ecological sustainability, it will also negatively affect livelihoods in the long run ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0RFHjO93","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bowen\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Jones & Pendry, 1999; Cowlishaw, 2005; G. Taylor, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Bowen?Jones & Pendry, 1999; Cowlishaw, 2005; G. Taylor, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4Q9SXMG5"],"itemData":{"id":749,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The threat that the bushmeat trade presents to primates and other taxa was assessed from the literature, including data from markets, village hunting studies and logging concessions in Central and West Africa. In many cases the numbers of both common and protected species of primate being killed throughout the region are thought to be unsustainable. This is also the case for other taxa involved in the bushmeat trade, which crosses geographic, cultural and economic boundaries. A suite of measures must be considered to mitigate the effects of this trade, and these measures will have to recognize the local, regional and national socio-economic importance of the trade if they are to result in long-term conservation success.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","ISSN":"1365-3008","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","page":"233-246","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The threat to primates and other mammals from the bushmeat trade in Africa, and how this threat could be diminished 1","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Bowen?Jones","given":"Evan"},{"family":"Pendry","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/k2I1mKxq","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P6L39Y2E"],"itemData":{"id":1942,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2664.2005.01046.x","ISSN":"00218901","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"460-468","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Evidence for post-depletion sustainability in a mature bushmeat market: Sustainability of bushmeat markets","title-short":"Evidence for post-depletion sustainability in a mature bushmeat market","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Mendelson","given":"Samantha"},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"J. Marcus"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",6,9]]}}},{"id":32712,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XF8EQYQB"],"itemData":{"id":32712,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable hunting threatens both biodiversity and local livelihoods. Despite high levels of research effort focused on understanding the dynamics of bushmeat trade and consumption, current research is largely site speci?c. Without synthesis and quantitative analysis of available case studies, the national and regional characteristics of bushmeat trade and consumption remain largely speculative, impeding efforts to inform national and regional policy on bushmeat trade. Here we describe the structure and content of the West and Central African bushmeat database which holds quantitative data on bushmeat sales, consumption and offtake for 177 species from 275 sites across 11 countries in two regions, spanning three decades of research. Despite this wealth of available data, we found important biases in research effort. The majority of studies in West and Central Africa have collected market data, which although providing a useful record of bushmeat sales, are limited in their ability to track changes in hunting offtake. In addition, few data exist for West Africa, and few studies have tracked changes over time, using repeat sampling. With new initiatives in the regions to track bushmeat hunting, this database represents an opportunity to synthesise current and future data on bushmeat hunting, consumption and trade in West and Central Africa, identify gaps in current understanding, and systematically target future monitoring efforts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2014.11.001","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"199-205","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesising bushmeat research effort in West and Central Africa: A new regional database","title-short":"Synthesising bushmeat research effort in West and Central Africa","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320714004182","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Taylor","given":"G."},{"family":"Scharlemann","given":"J.P.W."},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"M."},{"family":"Kümpel","given":"N."},{"family":"Harfoot","given":"M.B.J."},{"family":"Fa","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Melisch","given":"R."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."},{"family":"Bhagwat","given":"S."},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Ajonina","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Albrechtsen","given":"L."},{"family":"Allebone-Webb","given":"S."},{"family":"Brown","given":"E."},{"family":"Brugiere","given":"D."},{"family":"Clark","given":"C."},{"family":"Colell","given":"M."},{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"G."},{"family":"Crookes","given":"D."},{"family":"De Merode","given":"E."},{"family":"Dupain","given":"J."},{"family":"East","given":"T."},{"family":"Edderai","given":"D."},{"family":"Elkan","given":"P."},{"family":"Gill","given":"D."},{"family":"Greengrass","given":"E."},{"family":"Hodgkinson","given":"C."},{"family":"Ilambu","given":"O."},{"family":"Jeanmart","given":"P."},{"family":"Juste","given":"J."},{"family":"Linder","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"D.W."},{"family":"Noss","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Okorie","given":"P.U."},{"family":"Okouyi","given":"V.J.J."},{"family":"Pailler","given":"S."},{"family":"Poulsen","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Riddell","given":"M."},{"family":"Schleicher","given":"J."},{"family":"Schulte-Herbrüggen","given":"B."},{"family":"Starkey","given":"M."},{"family":"Vliet","given":"N.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Whitham","given":"C."},{"family":"Willcox","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Wright","given":"J.H."},{"family":"Coad","given":"L.M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Trade mediates demand from one region to another, potentially giving incentives to hunt in large scales, which together with uncontrolled access and poorly enforced regulation negatively affects the sustainability of protected and unprotected species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IiO2m3dt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bowen\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Jones & Pendry, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Bowen?Jones & Pendry, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4Q9SXMG5"],"itemData":{"id":749,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The threat that the bushmeat trade presents to primates and other taxa was assessed from the literature, including data from markets, village hunting studies and logging concessions in Central and West Africa. In many cases the numbers of both common and protected species of primate being killed throughout the region are thought to be unsustainable. This is also the case for other taxa involved in the bushmeat trade, which crosses geographic, cultural and economic boundaries. A suite of measures must be considered to mitigate the effects of this trade, and these measures will have to recognize the local, regional and national socio-economic importance of the trade if they are to result in long-term conservation success.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","ISSN":"1365-3008","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","page":"233-246","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The threat to primates and other mammals from the bushmeat trade in Africa, and how this threat could be diminished 1","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Bowen?Jones","given":"Evan"},{"family":"Pendry","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wild meat (also known as bushmeat) hunting is usually not only practiced for subsistence, but is usually traded and serves local markets, as well as urban or even international markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RG38JSEE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bowen\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Jones & Pendry, 1999; Brashares, 2011; P. A. Lindsey, 2013; Nielsen & Meilby, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Bowen?Jones & Pendry, 1999; Brashares, 2011; P. A. Lindsey, 2013; Nielsen & Meilby, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32802,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KAYP49FJ"],"itemData":{"id":32802,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1011526108","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"34","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","language":"en","page":"13931-13936","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Economic and geographic drivers of wildlife consumption in rural Africa","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/doi/10.1073/pnas.1011526108","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Brashares","given":"J. S."},{"family":"Golden","given":"C. D."},{"family":"Weinbaum","given":"K. Z."},{"family":"Barrett","given":"C. B."},{"family":"Okello","given":"G. V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8,23]]}}},{"id":32740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W43CISEZ"],"itemData":{"id":32740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The bushmeat trade, or the illegal acquisition and exchange of wild meat, has long been recognised as a severe problem in forest biomes, but receives little attention in savannas, perhaps due to a misconception that bushmeat hunting is a low-impact subsistence activity. Though data on impacts are scarce, indications are that bushmeat hunting is a widespread problem in savannas, with severe impacts on wildlife populations and wildlife-based land uses. The impacts of the bushmeat trade in savannas vary from edge-effects around protected areas, to disproportionate declines of some species, to severe wildlife declines in areas with inadequate anti-poaching. In some areas, bushmeat contributes signi?cantly to food security, but these bene?ts are unsustainable, and hunting is wasteful, utilising a fraction of the wildlife killed or of its ?nancial value obtainable through tourism, trophy hunting and/or legal game meat production. The bushmeat trade appears to be becoming increasingly commercialised due to elevated demand in rural areas, urban centres and even overseas cities. Other drivers for the trade include human encroachment of wildlife areas; poverty and food insecurity; and inadequate legal frameworks to enable communities to bene?t legally from wildlife, and to create incentives for people to desist from illegal bushmeat hunting. These drivers are exacerbated by inadequate wildlife laws and enforcement and in some areas, political instability. Urgent efforts are needed to address these drivers and raise awareness among local and international governments of the seriousness of the threat. Failure to address this will result in severe wildlife declines widely in African savannas, with signi?cant ecological, economic and social impacts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.12.020","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"80-96","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas: Impacts, drivers, and possible solutions","title-short":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320712005186","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter Andrew"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen"},{"family":"Bento","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Bocchino","given":"Clara"},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Diggle","given":"Richard W."},{"family":"Eves","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Marnewick","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Mattheus","given":"Jaco"},{"family":"Weldon McNutt","given":"J."},{"family":"McRobb","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Milanzi","given":"James"},{"family":"Morley","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Opyene","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Phadima","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Rentsch","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Roche","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Westhuizen","given":"Hugo","dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie Van"},{"family":"Zisadza-Gandiwa","given":"Patience"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}},{"id":749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4Q9SXMG5"],"itemData":{"id":749,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The threat that the bushmeat trade presents to primates and other taxa was assessed from the literature, including data from markets, village hunting studies and logging concessions in Central and West Africa. In many cases the numbers of both common and protected species of primate being killed throughout the region are thought to be unsustainable. This is also the case for other taxa involved in the bushmeat trade, which crosses geographic, cultural and economic boundaries. A suite of measures must be considered to mitigate the effects of this trade, and these measures will have to recognize the local, regional and national socio-economic importance of the trade if they are to result in long-term conservation success.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","ISSN":"1365-3008","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","page":"233-246","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The threat to primates and other mammals from the bushmeat trade in Africa, and how this threat could be diminished 1","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Bowen?Jones","given":"Evan"},{"family":"Pendry","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":32804,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MRPJCYI7"],"itemData":{"id":32804,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Bushmeat hunting in the savannah biomes of East Africa is often considered to be subsistence oriented and undertaken as a gap-filler in the lean agricultural season. The price of bushmeat is furthermore often thought uniform regardless of species, but if hunting is commercially oriented and price premiums are paid for particular species this needs to be considered. This paper investigates these issues in the Kilombero Valley of Tanzania, based on one year of market data and interviews with 80 hunters, 169 traders and 67 retailers. Motivations were overwhelmingly commercial and the bushmeat trade constituted a year-round income generating activity. Monte Carlo simulations based on the deterrence model revealed average cost-benefit ratios of 0.15–0.43 for hunters, 0.56–0.62 for traders and 0.88 for retailers, and a 12–401 fold increase in likelihood of apprehension may be required to render the trade unprofitable. Willingness-to-pay data showed that elephant, buffalo, hippopotamus, puku, bushpig and warthog meat were preferred. Enhanced enforcement may thus drive prices for these species higher, encouraging hunters to seek ways around constraints. Community-based wildlife management and improved firearms control may be the most pragmatic ways to regulate the trade.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892914000198","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"61-72","source":"Cambridge Core","title":"Hunting and trading bushmeat in the Kilombero Valley, Tanzania: motivations, cost-benefit ratios and meat prices","title-short":"Hunting and trading bushmeat in the Kilombero Valley, Tanzania","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/environmental-conservation/article/hunting-and-trading-bushmeat-in-the-kilombero-valley-tanzania-motivations-costbenefit-ratios-and-meat-prices/DE54BF642E4FC9CFA58F2E8F79525BD1","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Nielsen","given":"Martin Reinhardt"},{"family":"Meilby","given":"Henrik"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Typically, domestic trade is substantially larger than international trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"swYYTz46","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brashares, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Brashares, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32802,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KAYP49FJ"],"itemData":{"id":32802,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1011526108","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"34","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","language":"en","page":"13931-13936","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Economic and geographic drivers of wildlife consumption in rural Africa","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/doi/10.1073/pnas.1011526108","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Brashares","given":"J. S."},{"family":"Golden","given":"C. D."},{"family":"Weinbaum","given":"K. Z."},{"family":"Barrett","given":"C. B."},{"family":"Okello","given":"G. V."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8,23]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There is established evidence that trade in wild animals for meat is one of the most important threats to wild species in Central and West Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LAl32agj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bowen\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Jones & Pendry, 1999; Cowlishaw, 2005; P. A. Lindsey, 2013; Thibault & Blaney, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Bowen?Jones & Pendry, 1999; Cowlishaw, 2005; P. A. Lindsey, 2013; Thibault & Blaney, 2003)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4Q9SXMG5"],"itemData":{"id":749,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The threat that the bushmeat trade presents to primates and other taxa was assessed from the literature, including data from markets, village hunting studies and logging concessions in Central and West Africa. In many cases the numbers of both common and protected species of primate being killed throughout the region are thought to be unsustainable. This is also the case for other taxa involved in the bushmeat trade, which crosses geographic, cultural and economic boundaries. A suite of measures must be considered to mitigate the effects of this trade, and these measures will have to recognize the local, regional and national socio-economic importance of the trade if they are to result in long-term conservation success.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","ISSN":"1365-3008","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","page":"233-246","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The threat to primates and other mammals from the bushmeat trade in Africa, and how this threat could be diminished 1","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Bowen?Jones","given":"Evan"},{"family":"Pendry","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/k2I1mKxq","uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P6L39Y2E"],"itemData":{"id":1942,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2664.2005.01046.x","ISSN":"00218901","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"460-468","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Evidence for post-depletion sustainability in a mature bushmeat market: Sustainability of bushmeat markets","title-short":"Evidence for post-depletion sustainability in a mature bushmeat market","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Mendelson","given":"Samantha"},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"J. Marcus"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",6,9]]}}},{"id":32740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W43CISEZ"],"itemData":{"id":32740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The bushmeat trade, or the illegal acquisition and exchange of wild meat, has long been recognised as a severe problem in forest biomes, but receives little attention in savannas, perhaps due to a misconception that bushmeat hunting is a low-impact subsistence activity. Though data on impacts are scarce, indications are that bushmeat hunting is a widespread problem in savannas, with severe impacts on wildlife populations and wildlife-based land uses. The impacts of the bushmeat trade in savannas vary from edge-effects around protected areas, to disproportionate declines of some species, to severe wildlife declines in areas with inadequate anti-poaching. In some areas, bushmeat contributes signi?cantly to food security, but these bene?ts are unsustainable, and hunting is wasteful, utilising a fraction of the wildlife killed or of its ?nancial value obtainable through tourism, trophy hunting and/or legal game meat production. The bushmeat trade appears to be becoming increasingly commercialised due to elevated demand in rural areas, urban centres and even overseas cities. Other drivers for the trade include human encroachment of wildlife areas; poverty and food insecurity; and inadequate legal frameworks to enable communities to bene?t legally from wildlife, and to create incentives for people to desist from illegal bushmeat hunting. These drivers are exacerbated by inadequate wildlife laws and enforcement and in some areas, political instability. Urgent efforts are needed to address these drivers and raise awareness among local and international governments of the seriousness of the threat. Failure to address this will result in severe wildlife declines widely in African savannas, with signi?cant ecological, economic and social impacts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.12.020","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"80-96","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas: Impacts, drivers, and possible solutions","title-short":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320712005186","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter Andrew"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen"},{"family":"Bento","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Bocchino","given":"Clara"},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Diggle","given":"Richard W."},{"family":"Eves","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Marnewick","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Mattheus","given":"Jaco"},{"family":"Weldon McNutt","given":"J."},{"family":"McRobb","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Milanzi","given":"James"},{"family":"Morley","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Opyene","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Phadima","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Rentsch","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Roche","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Westhuizen","given":"Hugo","dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie Van"},{"family":"Zisadza-Gandiwa","given":"Patience"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}},{"id":32793,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2N92WTYA"],"itemData":{"id":32793,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract: Although deforestation and habitat loss are considered the main causes of wildlife depletion, the bushmeat trade is one of the foremost threats to biodiversity conservation in central Africa. In 1997 and 1998, we set up a bushmeat trade monitoring program in the town of Gamba to assess the pressure exerted on wildlife by the human population attracted by the oil industry in the Gamba Protected Areas Complex. During 279 days of observations, we recorded 19 mammal species, 4 bird species, and 7 reptile taxa, for a total of 2845 animal carcasses with an estimated weight of 189,728 kg. Even though the bushmeat trade is illegal in protected areas, calculation of annual production levels and hunting yields showed that this trade is not sustainable for some species. We calculated the ratio of bushmeat found in the markets of Gamba to the human population. Compared with other Gabonese towns, Gamba had the highest ratio in the country. We suggest that the oil industry is another contributor to the bushmeat crisis in central Africa. It is important to recognize the role of oil companies in the current crisis in order to heighten the awareness of the public and of oil companies and to seek ways of lessening the impact of the oil industry on the bushmeat trade in central Africa.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2003.00159.x","ISSN":"1523-1739","issue":"6","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2003.00159.x","page":"1807-1813","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The Oil Industry as an Underlying Factor in the Bushmeat Crisis in Central Africa","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2003.00159.x","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Thibault","given":"Marc"},{"family":"Blaney","given":"Sonia"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At the same time, there is incomplete information about the scale of wild species trade and also how this maps to sustainability outcomes. Evidence is largely anecdotal, for example it has been documented that around five tonnes of wild meat  is smuggled in personal baggage through Paris Roissy?Charles de Gaulle airport per week ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qB8d7Oyj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chaber, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Chaber, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32812,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XTK9ZW5D"],"itemData":{"id":32812,"type":"paper-conference","abstract":"Concerns have been raised about the illegal import of bushmeat from Africa into Europe, particularly regarding the health risks posed to people and livestock. The role of international trade in driving unsustainable hunting in source countries is unknown, but generally assumed to be limited. Here, we present the first systematic study of the scale and nature of this international trade. We estimate that around five tonnes of bushmeat per week is smuggled in personal baggage through Paris Roissy-Charles de Gaulle airport. Bushmeat is not only imported for personal consumption but is part of a lucrative organized trade, with high prices indicating luxury status. A wide range of species is carried, many of which are CITES-listed. Based on these findings, we suggest ways in which customs, airlines, and airport authorities could reduce imports, focussing on raising awareness of regulations, and improving surveillance and deterrence, particularly where CITES-listed species are concerned.","DOI":"10.1111/j.1755-263X.2010.00121.x","source":"Semantic Scholar","title":"The scale of illegal meat importation from Africa to Europe via Paris.","author":[{"family":"Chaber","given":"A. L."},{"family":"Allebone-Webb","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Lignereux","given":"Yves"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Andrew A."},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"Justin Marcus"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Taylor ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Mwvm8w6r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2015)","plainCitation":"(2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32712,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XF8EQYQB"],"itemData":{"id":32712,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable hunting threatens both biodiversity and local livelihoods. Despite high levels of research effort focused on understanding the dynamics of bushmeat trade and consumption, current research is largely site speci?c. Without synthesis and quantitative analysis of available case studies, the national and regional characteristics of bushmeat trade and consumption remain largely speculative, impeding efforts to inform national and regional policy on bushmeat trade. Here we describe the structure and content of the West and Central African bushmeat database which holds quantitative data on bushmeat sales, consumption and offtake for 177 species from 275 sites across 11 countries in two regions, spanning three decades of research. Despite this wealth of available data, we found important biases in research effort. The majority of studies in West and Central Africa have collected market data, which although providing a useful record of bushmeat sales, are limited in their ability to track changes in hunting offtake. In addition, few data exist for West Africa, and few studies have tracked changes over time, using repeat sampling. With new initiatives in the regions to track bushmeat hunting, this database represents an opportunity to synthesise current and future data on bushmeat hunting, consumption and trade in West and Central Africa, identify gaps in current understanding, and systematically target future monitoring efforts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2014.11.001","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"199-205","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesising bushmeat research effort in West and Central Africa: A new regional database","title-short":"Synthesising bushmeat research effort in West and Central Africa","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320714004182","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Taylor","given":"G."},{"family":"Scharlemann","given":"J.P.W."},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"M."},{"family":"Kümpel","given":"N."},{"family":"Harfoot","given":"M.B.J."},{"family":"Fa","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Melisch","given":"R."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."},{"family":"Bhagwat","given":"S."},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Ajonina","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Albrechtsen","given":"L."},{"family":"Allebone-Webb","given":"S."},{"family":"Brown","given":"E."},{"family":"Brugiere","given":"D."},{"family":"Clark","given":"C."},{"family":"Colell","given":"M."},{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"G."},{"family":"Crookes","given":"D."},{"family":"De Merode","given":"E."},{"family":"Dupain","given":"J."},{"family":"East","given":"T."},{"family":"Edderai","given":"D."},{"family":"Elkan","given":"P."},{"family":"Gill","given":"D."},{"family":"Greengrass","given":"E."},{"family":"Hodgkinson","given":"C."},{"family":"Ilambu","given":"O."},{"family":"Jeanmart","given":"P."},{"family":"Juste","given":"J."},{"family":"Linder","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"D.W."},{"family":"Noss","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Okorie","given":"P.U."},{"family":"Okouyi","given":"V.J.J."},{"family":"Pailler","given":"S."},{"family":"Poulsen","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Riddell","given":"M."},{"family":"Schleicher","given":"J."},{"family":"Schulte-Herbrüggen","given":"B."},{"family":"Starkey","given":"M."},{"family":"Vliet","given":"N.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Whitham","given":"C."},{"family":"Willcox","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Wright","given":"J.H."},{"family":"Coad","given":"L.M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} document evidence on the impacts of wild species hunting with data on 177 species from 275 sites across 11 African countries collected over 30 years. They find that research efforts and available information are not evenly distributed. There is less evidence from West Africa compared to Central Africa, and also less information about impacts on birds ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eqde0EBC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G. Taylor, 2015)","plainCitation":"(G. Taylor, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32712,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XF8EQYQB"],"itemData":{"id":32712,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Unsustainable hunting threatens both biodiversity and local livelihoods. Despite high levels of research effort focused on understanding the dynamics of bushmeat trade and consumption, current research is largely site speci?c. Without synthesis and quantitative analysis of available case studies, the national and regional characteristics of bushmeat trade and consumption remain largely speculative, impeding efforts to inform national and regional policy on bushmeat trade. Here we describe the structure and content of the West and Central African bushmeat database which holds quantitative data on bushmeat sales, consumption and offtake for 177 species from 275 sites across 11 countries in two regions, spanning three decades of research. Despite this wealth of available data, we found important biases in research effort. The majority of studies in West and Central Africa have collected market data, which although providing a useful record of bushmeat sales, are limited in their ability to track changes in hunting offtake. In addition, few data exist for West Africa, and few studies have tracked changes over time, using repeat sampling. With new initiatives in the regions to track bushmeat hunting, this database represents an opportunity to synthesise current and future data on bushmeat hunting, consumption and trade in West and Central Africa, identify gaps in current understanding, and systematically target future monitoring efforts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2014.11.001","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"199-205","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Synthesising bushmeat research effort in West and Central Africa: A new regional database","title-short":"Synthesising bushmeat research effort in West and Central Africa","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320714004182","volume":"181","author":[{"family":"Taylor","given":"G."},{"family":"Scharlemann","given":"J.P.W."},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"M."},{"family":"Kümpel","given":"N."},{"family":"Harfoot","given":"M.B.J."},{"family":"Fa","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Melisch","given":"R."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."},{"family":"Bhagwat","given":"S."},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"K.A."},{"family":"Ajonina","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Albrechtsen","given":"L."},{"family":"Allebone-Webb","given":"S."},{"family":"Brown","given":"E."},{"family":"Brugiere","given":"D."},{"family":"Clark","given":"C."},{"family":"Colell","given":"M."},{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"G."},{"family":"Crookes","given":"D."},{"family":"De Merode","given":"E."},{"family":"Dupain","given":"J."},{"family":"East","given":"T."},{"family":"Edderai","given":"D."},{"family":"Elkan","given":"P."},{"family":"Gill","given":"D."},{"family":"Greengrass","given":"E."},{"family":"Hodgkinson","given":"C."},{"family":"Ilambu","given":"O."},{"family":"Jeanmart","given":"P."},{"family":"Juste","given":"J."},{"family":"Linder","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"D.W."},{"family":"Noss","given":"A.J."},{"family":"Okorie","given":"P.U."},{"family":"Okouyi","given":"V.J.J."},{"family":"Pailler","given":"S."},{"family":"Poulsen","given":"J.R."},{"family":"Riddell","given":"M."},{"family":"Schleicher","given":"J."},{"family":"Schulte-Herbrüggen","given":"B."},{"family":"Starkey","given":"M."},{"family":"Vliet","given":"N.","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Whitham","given":"C."},{"family":"Willcox","given":"A.S."},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Wright","given":"J.H."},{"family":"Coad","given":"L.M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The International Council for the Exploration of the Sea data reveals that about 18 000 individuals of wild species mostly traded as hunting trophies were exported annually from South Africa between 2005 and 2014 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"91fpBbml","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sinovas, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Sinovas, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32704,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5JPFZ6TB"],"itemData":{"id":32704,"type":"report","publisher":"Technical report prepared for the South African National Biodiversity …","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Southern Africa’s wildlife trade: an analysis of CITES trade in SADC countries","title-short":"Southern Africa’s wildlife trade","author":[{"family":"Sinovas","given":"P."},{"family":"Price","given":"B."},{"family":"King","given":"E."},{"family":"Davis","given":"F."},{"family":"Hinsley","given":"A."},{"family":"Pavitt","given":"A."},{"family":"Pfab","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Wild meat  trade is as a severe problem in forest biomes and savannas, though it is extremely difficult to accurately quantify the number of wild species hunted or quantity of wild meat  traded ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"S3eceotC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W43CISEZ"],"itemData":{"id":32740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The bushmeat trade, or the illegal acquisition and exchange of wild meat, has long been recognised as a severe problem in forest biomes, but receives little attention in savannas, perhaps due to a misconception that bushmeat hunting is a low-impact subsistence activity. Though data on impacts are scarce, indications are that bushmeat hunting is a widespread problem in savannas, with severe impacts on wildlife populations and wildlife-based land uses. The impacts of the bushmeat trade in savannas vary from edge-effects around protected areas, to disproportionate declines of some species, to severe wildlife declines in areas with inadequate anti-poaching. In some areas, bushmeat contributes signi?cantly to food security, but these bene?ts are unsustainable, and hunting is wasteful, utilising a fraction of the wildlife killed or of its ?nancial value obtainable through tourism, trophy hunting and/or legal game meat production. The bushmeat trade appears to be becoming increasingly commercialised due to elevated demand in rural areas, urban centres and even overseas cities. Other drivers for the trade include human encroachment of wildlife areas; poverty and food insecurity; and inadequate legal frameworks to enable communities to bene?t legally from wildlife, and to create incentives for people to desist from illegal bushmeat hunting. These drivers are exacerbated by inadequate wildlife laws and enforcement and in some areas, political instability. Urgent efforts are needed to address these drivers and raise awareness among local and international governments of the seriousness of the threat. Failure to address this will result in severe wildlife declines widely in African savannas, with signi?cant ecological, economic and social impacts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.12.020","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"80-96","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas: Impacts, drivers, and possible solutions","title-short":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320712005186","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter Andrew"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen"},{"family":"Bento","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Bocchino","given":"Clara"},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Diggle","given":"Richard W."},{"family":"Eves","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Marnewick","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Mattheus","given":"Jaco"},{"family":"Weldon McNutt","given":"J."},{"family":"McRobb","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Milanzi","given":"James"},{"family":"Morley","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Opyene","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Phadima","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Rentsch","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Roche","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Westhuizen","given":"Hugo","dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie Van"},{"family":"Zisadza-Gandiwa","given":"Patience"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, case studies from 15 African countries suggest that given the scale and ubiquity of wild meat  hunting, current uses are ecologically unsustainable, at least without immediate interventions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9N2Wjnli","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W43CISEZ"],"itemData":{"id":32740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The bushmeat trade, or the illegal acquisition and exchange of wild meat, has long been recognised as a severe problem in forest biomes, but receives little attention in savannas, perhaps due to a misconception that bushmeat hunting is a low-impact subsistence activity. Though data on impacts are scarce, indications are that bushmeat hunting is a widespread problem in savannas, with severe impacts on wildlife populations and wildlife-based land uses. The impacts of the bushmeat trade in savannas vary from edge-effects around protected areas, to disproportionate declines of some species, to severe wildlife declines in areas with inadequate anti-poaching. In some areas, bushmeat contributes signi?cantly to food security, but these bene?ts are unsustainable, and hunting is wasteful, utilising a fraction of the wildlife killed or of its ?nancial value obtainable through tourism, trophy hunting and/or legal game meat production. The bushmeat trade appears to be becoming increasingly commercialised due to elevated demand in rural areas, urban centres and even overseas cities. Other drivers for the trade include human encroachment of wildlife areas; poverty and food insecurity; and inadequate legal frameworks to enable communities to bene?t legally from wildlife, and to create incentives for people to desist from illegal bushmeat hunting. These drivers are exacerbated by inadequate wildlife laws and enforcement and in some areas, political instability. Urgent efforts are needed to address these drivers and raise awareness among local and international governments of the seriousness of the threat. Failure to address this will result in severe wildlife declines widely in African savannas, with signi?cant ecological, economic and social impacts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.12.020","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"80-96","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas: Impacts, drivers, and possible solutions","title-short":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320712005186","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter Andrew"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen"},{"family":"Bento","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Bocchino","given":"Clara"},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Diggle","given":"Richard W."},{"family":"Eves","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Marnewick","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Mattheus","given":"Jaco"},{"family":"Weldon McNutt","given":"J."},{"family":"McRobb","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Milanzi","given":"James"},{"family":"Morley","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Opyene","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Phadima","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Rentsch","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Roche","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Westhuizen","given":"Hugo","dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie Van"},{"family":"Zisadza-Gandiwa","given":"Patience"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ultimately, the expected loss of wild species will lead to severe economic and social impacts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fbw5WlDJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W43CISEZ"],"itemData":{"id":32740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The bushmeat trade, or the illegal acquisition and exchange of wild meat, has long been recognised as a severe problem in forest biomes, but receives little attention in savannas, perhaps due to a misconception that bushmeat hunting is a low-impact subsistence activity. Though data on impacts are scarce, indications are that bushmeat hunting is a widespread problem in savannas, with severe impacts on wildlife populations and wildlife-based land uses. The impacts of the bushmeat trade in savannas vary from edge-effects around protected areas, to disproportionate declines of some species, to severe wildlife declines in areas with inadequate anti-poaching. In some areas, bushmeat contributes signi?cantly to food security, but these bene?ts are unsustainable, and hunting is wasteful, utilising a fraction of the wildlife killed or of its ?nancial value obtainable through tourism, trophy hunting and/or legal game meat production. The bushmeat trade appears to be becoming increasingly commercialised due to elevated demand in rural areas, urban centres and even overseas cities. Other drivers for the trade include human encroachment of wildlife areas; poverty and food insecurity; and inadequate legal frameworks to enable communities to bene?t legally from wildlife, and to create incentives for people to desist from illegal bushmeat hunting. These drivers are exacerbated by inadequate wildlife laws and enforcement and in some areas, political instability. Urgent efforts are needed to address these drivers and raise awareness among local and international governments of the seriousness of the threat. Failure to address this will result in severe wildlife declines widely in African savannas, with signi?cant ecological, economic and social impacts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.12.020","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"80-96","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas: Impacts, drivers, and possible solutions","title-short":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320712005186","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter Andrew"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen"},{"family":"Bento","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Bocchino","given":"Clara"},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Diggle","given":"Richard W."},{"family":"Eves","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Marnewick","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Mattheus","given":"Jaco"},{"family":"Weldon McNutt","given":"J."},{"family":"McRobb","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Milanzi","given":"James"},{"family":"Morley","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Opyene","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Phadima","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Rentsch","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Roche","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Westhuizen","given":"Hugo","dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie Van"},{"family":"Zisadza-Gandiwa","given":"Patience"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In many cases, wild meat  hunters are male, poor, without formal employment, and with little education and few livestock, though wild meat  hunting can be quite lucrative  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SQxKuVlZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W43CISEZ"],"itemData":{"id":32740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The bushmeat trade, or the illegal acquisition and exchange of wild meat, has long been recognised as a severe problem in forest biomes, but receives little attention in savannas, perhaps due to a misconception that bushmeat hunting is a low-impact subsistence activity. Though data on impacts are scarce, indications are that bushmeat hunting is a widespread problem in savannas, with severe impacts on wildlife populations and wildlife-based land uses. The impacts of the bushmeat trade in savannas vary from edge-effects around protected areas, to disproportionate declines of some species, to severe wildlife declines in areas with inadequate anti-poaching. In some areas, bushmeat contributes signi?cantly to food security, but these bene?ts are unsustainable, and hunting is wasteful, utilising a fraction of the wildlife killed or of its ?nancial value obtainable through tourism, trophy hunting and/or legal game meat production. The bushmeat trade appears to be becoming increasingly commercialised due to elevated demand in rural areas, urban centres and even overseas cities. Other drivers for the trade include human encroachment of wildlife areas; poverty and food insecurity; and inadequate legal frameworks to enable communities to bene?t legally from wildlife, and to create incentives for people to desist from illegal bushmeat hunting. These drivers are exacerbated by inadequate wildlife laws and enforcement and in some areas, political instability. Urgent efforts are needed to address these drivers and raise awareness among local and international governments of the seriousness of the threat. Failure to address this will result in severe wildlife declines widely in African savannas, with signi?cant ecological, economic and social impacts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.12.020","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"80-96","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas: Impacts, drivers, and possible solutions","title-short":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320712005186","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter Andrew"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen"},{"family":"Bento","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Bocchino","given":"Clara"},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Diggle","given":"Richard W."},{"family":"Eves","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Marnewick","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Mattheus","given":"Jaco"},{"family":"Weldon McNutt","given":"J."},{"family":"McRobb","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Milanzi","given":"James"},{"family":"Morley","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Opyene","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Phadima","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Rentsch","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Roche","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Westhuizen","given":"Hugo","dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie Van"},{"family":"Zisadza-Gandiwa","given":"Patience"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Social impacts of illegal wild meat  trade include negative effects on food security in the long term through unsustainable harvesting, loss of potential tourism-based revenues and employment, and also loss of wild species heritage  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HdzX7euD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W43CISEZ"],"itemData":{"id":32740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The bushmeat trade, or the illegal acquisition and exchange of wild meat, has long been recognised as a severe problem in forest biomes, but receives little attention in savannas, perhaps due to a misconception that bushmeat hunting is a low-impact subsistence activity. Though data on impacts are scarce, indications are that bushmeat hunting is a widespread problem in savannas, with severe impacts on wildlife populations and wildlife-based land uses. The impacts of the bushmeat trade in savannas vary from edge-effects around protected areas, to disproportionate declines of some species, to severe wildlife declines in areas with inadequate anti-poaching. In some areas, bushmeat contributes signi?cantly to food security, but these bene?ts are unsustainable, and hunting is wasteful, utilising a fraction of the wildlife killed or of its ?nancial value obtainable through tourism, trophy hunting and/or legal game meat production. The bushmeat trade appears to be becoming increasingly commercialised due to elevated demand in rural areas, urban centres and even overseas cities. Other drivers for the trade include human encroachment of wildlife areas; poverty and food insecurity; and inadequate legal frameworks to enable communities to bene?t legally from wildlife, and to create incentives for people to desist from illegal bushmeat hunting. These drivers are exacerbated by inadequate wildlife laws and enforcement and in some areas, political instability. Urgent efforts are needed to address these drivers and raise awareness among local and international governments of the seriousness of the threat. Failure to address this will result in severe wildlife declines widely in African savannas, with signi?cant ecological, economic and social impacts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.12.020","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"80-96","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas: Impacts, drivers, and possible solutions","title-short":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320712005186","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter Andrew"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen"},{"family":"Bento","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Bocchino","given":"Clara"},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Diggle","given":"Richard W."},{"family":"Eves","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Marnewick","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Mattheus","given":"Jaco"},{"family":"Weldon McNutt","given":"J."},{"family":"McRobb","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Milanzi","given":"James"},{"family":"Morley","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Opyene","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Phadima","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Rentsch","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Roche","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Westhuizen","given":"Hugo","dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie Van"},{"family":"Zisadza-Gandiwa","given":"Patience"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A key element of unsustainable use tends to be high demand, much more than technology, in combination with absence of effective regulation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"opy7wEOS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bowen\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Jones & Pendry, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Bowen?Jones & Pendry, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4Q9SXMG5"],"itemData":{"id":749,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The threat that the bushmeat trade presents to primates and other taxa was assessed from the literature, including data from markets, village hunting studies and logging concessions in Central and West Africa. In many cases the numbers of both common and protected species of primate being killed throughout the region are thought to be unsustainable. This is also the case for other taxa involved in the bushmeat trade, which crosses geographic, cultural and economic boundaries. A suite of measures must be considered to mitigate the effects of this trade, and these measures will have to recognize the local, regional and national socio-economic importance of the trade if they are to result in long-term conservation success.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","ISSN":"1365-3008","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","page":"233-246","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The threat to primates and other mammals from the bushmeat trade in Africa, and how this threat could be diminished 1","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Bowen?Jones","given":"Evan"},{"family":"Pendry","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Increased urbanization and access to formerly remote areas are mediating factors that increase hunting pressure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cphIMfav","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Allebone\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Webb, 2011; Bowen\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Jones & Pendry, 1999; Brugiere & Magassouba, 2009; P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Allebone?Webb, 2011; Bowen?Jones & Pendry, 1999; Brugiere & Magassouba, 2009; P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4Q9SXMG5"],"itemData":{"id":749,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The threat that the bushmeat trade presents to primates and other taxa was assessed from the literature, including data from markets, village hunting studies and logging concessions in Central and West Africa. In many cases the numbers of both common and protected species of primate being killed throughout the region are thought to be unsustainable. This is also the case for other taxa involved in the bushmeat trade, which crosses geographic, cultural and economic boundaries. A suite of measures must be considered to mitigate the effects of this trade, and these measures will have to recognize the local, regional and national socio-economic importance of the trade if they are to result in long-term conservation success.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","ISSN":"1365-3008","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","page":"233-246","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The threat to primates and other mammals from the bushmeat trade in Africa, and how this threat could be diminished 1","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Bowen?Jones","given":"Evan"},{"family":"Pendry","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":32740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W43CISEZ"],"itemData":{"id":32740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The bushmeat trade, or the illegal acquisition and exchange of wild meat, has long been recognised as a severe problem in forest biomes, but receives little attention in savannas, perhaps due to a misconception that bushmeat hunting is a low-impact subsistence activity. Though data on impacts are scarce, indications are that bushmeat hunting is a widespread problem in savannas, with severe impacts on wildlife populations and wildlife-based land uses. The impacts of the bushmeat trade in savannas vary from edge-effects around protected areas, to disproportionate declines of some species, to severe wildlife declines in areas with inadequate anti-poaching. In some areas, bushmeat contributes signi?cantly to food security, but these bene?ts are unsustainable, and hunting is wasteful, utilising a fraction of the wildlife killed or of its ?nancial value obtainable through tourism, trophy hunting and/or legal game meat production. The bushmeat trade appears to be becoming increasingly commercialised due to elevated demand in rural areas, urban centres and even overseas cities. Other drivers for the trade include human encroachment of wildlife areas; poverty and food insecurity; and inadequate legal frameworks to enable communities to bene?t legally from wildlife, and to create incentives for people to desist from illegal bushmeat hunting. These drivers are exacerbated by inadequate wildlife laws and enforcement and in some areas, political instability. Urgent efforts are needed to address these drivers and raise awareness among local and international governments of the seriousness of the threat. Failure to address this will result in severe wildlife declines widely in African savannas, with signi?cant ecological, economic and social impacts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.12.020","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"80-96","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas: Impacts, drivers, and possible solutions","title-short":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320712005186","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter Andrew"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen"},{"family":"Bento","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Bocchino","given":"Clara"},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Diggle","given":"Richard W."},{"family":"Eves","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Marnewick","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Mattheus","given":"Jaco"},{"family":"Weldon McNutt","given":"J."},{"family":"McRobb","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Milanzi","given":"James"},{"family":"Morley","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Opyene","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Phadima","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Rentsch","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Roche","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Westhuizen","given":"Hugo","dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie Van"},{"family":"Zisadza-Gandiwa","given":"Patience"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}},{"id":763,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EU62X7TT"],"itemData":{"id":763,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract: Finding an adequate measure of hunting sustainability for tropical forests has proved difficult. Many researchers have used urban bushmeat market surveys as indicators of hunting volumes and composition, but no analysis has been done of the reliability of market data in reflecting village offtake. We used data from urban markets and the villages that supply these markets to examine changes in the volume and composition of traded bushmeat between the village and the market (trade filters) in Equatorial Guinea. We collected data with market surveys and hunter offtake diaries. The trade filters varied depending on village remoteness and the monopoly power of traders. In a village with limited market access, species that maximized trader profits were most likely to be traded. In a village with greater market access, species for which hunters gained the greatest income per carcass were more likely to be traded. The probability of particular species being sold to market also depended on the capture method and season. Larger, more vulnerable species were more likely to be supplied from less-accessible catchments, whereas there was no effect of forest cover or human population density on probability of being sold. This suggests that the composition of bushmeat offtake in an area may be driven more by urban demand than the geographic characteristics of that area. In one market, traders may have reached the limit of their geographical exploitation range, and hunting pressure within that range may be increasing. Our results demonstrate that it is possible to model the trade filters that bias market data, which opens the way to developing more robust market-based sustainability indices for the bushmeat trade.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2011.01681.x","ISSN":"1523-1739","issue":"3","language":"en","license":"©2011 Society for Conservation Biology","note":"_eprint: https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2011.01681.x","page":"597-606","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Use of Market Data to Assess Bushmeat Hunting Sustainability in Equatorial Guinea","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2011.01681.x","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Allebone?Webb","given":"S. M."},{"family":"Kümpel","given":"N. F."},{"family":"Rist","given":"J."},{"family":"Cowlishaw","given":"G."},{"family":"Rowcliffe","given":"J. M."},{"family":"Milner?Gulland","given":"E. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":753,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D3SJBAF8"],"itemData":{"id":753,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"To document the bushmeat trade in the buffer zone of the Haut Niger National Park (HNNP), Republic of Guinea, we monitored the number of carcasses offered for sale in 16 villages over a one year period in 2001. Overall, we counted a total of 9134 carcasses from 27 species. This represents a biomass harvest of about 170,000 kg, a figure that could reach 335,100 kg when game consumed by hunters (i.e. not sold) and markets not sampled are taken into account. Ungulates (eleven species) and rodents (four species) accounted for 53.5% and 24.8% of the carcasses counted respectively (80.8% and 11.4% of the total biomass, respectively). The number of carcasses counted in villages bordering a heavily trafficked road was significantly higher than in more rural villages, thus highlighting the stimulating effect of roads on the bushmeat trade. Compared with existing data collected in 1995, the proportion of ungulates counted in the Mansira Moribaya village in 2001 increased significantly while that of rodents decreased significantly. The harvest of duiker species at Mansira Moribaya was in excess of the maximum sustainable harvest. These results of this study were used to establish a sustainable wildlife management scheme in the HNNP.","container-title":"African Journal of Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1365-2028.2008.01013.x","ISSN":"1365-2028","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"© 2009 The Authors. Journal compilation © 2009 Blackwell Publishing Ltd","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1365-2028.2008.01013.x","page":"630-639","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Pattern and sustainability of the bushmeat trade in the Haut Niger National Park, Republic of Guinea","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1365-2028.2008.01013.x","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Brugiere","given":"David"},{"family":"Magassouba","given":"Bakary"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Economic activities that rely on a large number of personnel, such as the oil industry may drive up demand, increasing incentives to hunt, which can put additional pressure on wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9ugrQAAa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Thibault & Blaney, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Thibault & Blaney, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32793,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2N92WTYA"],"itemData":{"id":32793,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract: Although deforestation and habitat loss are considered the main causes of wildlife depletion, the bushmeat trade is one of the foremost threats to biodiversity conservation in central Africa. In 1997 and 1998, we set up a bushmeat trade monitoring program in the town of Gamba to assess the pressure exerted on wildlife by the human population attracted by the oil industry in the Gamba Protected Areas Complex. During 279 days of observations, we recorded 19 mammal species, 4 bird species, and 7 reptile taxa, for a total of 2845 animal carcasses with an estimated weight of 189,728 kg. Even though the bushmeat trade is illegal in protected areas, calculation of annual production levels and hunting yields showed that this trade is not sustainable for some species. We calculated the ratio of bushmeat found in the markets of Gamba to the human population. Compared with other Gabonese towns, Gamba had the highest ratio in the country. We suggest that the oil industry is another contributor to the bushmeat crisis in central Africa. It is important to recognize the role of oil companies in the current crisis in order to heighten the awareness of the public and of oil companies and to seek ways of lessening the impact of the oil industry on the bushmeat trade in central Africa.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2003.00159.x","ISSN":"1523-1739","issue":"6","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2003.00159.x","page":"1807-1813","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The Oil Industry as an Underlying Factor in the Bushmeat Crisis in Central Africa","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1523-1739.2003.00159.x","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Thibault","given":"Marc"},{"family":"Blaney","given":"Sonia"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	According to data using the standard classification schemes for utilization and threat types for the International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List, at least 45.7% of extant bird species (4,561 species) are used by humans, principally for pets (37.0%) and for hunting for food (14.2%), but other uses include sport hunting, ornamentation and traditional medicine ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JPpyfsWK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Butchart, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Butchart, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32360,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LFV76C7F"],"itemData":{"id":32360,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            After habitat loss and degradation, the leading threats to biodiversity are over-exploitation and invasive alien species. For birds, newly synthesised data using the standard classification schemes for utilisation and threat types for the IUCN Red List allow novel analyses on the importance of these threats and permit the calculation of Red List Indices (RLIs) to show trends in the status of birds driven by these factors. At least 45.7% of extant bird species (4,561 species) are used by humans, principally for pets (37.0%) and for hunting for food (14.2%), but other uses include sport hunting, ornamentation and traditional medicine. Much of this use drives trade at an international scale, involving at least 3,337 species (33.9%, substantially higher than previous estimates), mostly for the pet trade. RLIs show that although successful control and management of use and trade have led to some species improving in status, this has been outweighed by the number of species deteriorating in status owing to unsustainable exploitation. Overall, the RLI showing trends in extinction risk driven by issues related to use shows a negative slope: human use of birds is currently unsustainable. Similarly, and of relevance to the Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species (CITES), the RLI for internationally traded bird species showing trends in extinction risk driven by issues related to international trade is also declining: international trade remains a threat to the world's birds. Invasive species impact at least one third of the world's threatened bird species (398 species, 32.6%), with mammals being the most important (impacting 81.1%), particularly through predation by carnivores and rodents. The RLI illustrating impacts of invasive species shows that they are driving a deterioration in the status of the world's birds. RLIs for the impacts of use and invasive species will be important indicators to help track progress towards the target of significantly reducing biodiversity loss by 2010.","container-title":"Bird Conservation International","DOI":"10.1017/S095927090800035X","ISSN":"0959-2709, 1474-0001","issue":"S1","journalAbbreviation":"Bird Conservation International","language":"en","page":"S245-S262","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Red List Indices to measure the sustainability of species use and impacts of invasive alien species","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S095927090800035X/type/journal_article","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. International trade is a key driver, involving at least 3,337 species (33.9%, substantially higher than previous estimates), mostly for pet trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"53cKzBOM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Butchart, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Butchart, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32360,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LFV76C7F"],"itemData":{"id":32360,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            After habitat loss and degradation, the leading threats to biodiversity are over-exploitation and invasive alien species. For birds, newly synthesised data using the standard classification schemes for utilisation and threat types for the IUCN Red List allow novel analyses on the importance of these threats and permit the calculation of Red List Indices (RLIs) to show trends in the status of birds driven by these factors. At least 45.7% of extant bird species (4,561 species) are used by humans, principally for pets (37.0%) and for hunting for food (14.2%), but other uses include sport hunting, ornamentation and traditional medicine. Much of this use drives trade at an international scale, involving at least 3,337 species (33.9%, substantially higher than previous estimates), mostly for the pet trade. RLIs show that although successful control and management of use and trade have led to some species improving in status, this has been outweighed by the number of species deteriorating in status owing to unsustainable exploitation. Overall, the RLI showing trends in extinction risk driven by issues related to use shows a negative slope: human use of birds is currently unsustainable. Similarly, and of relevance to the Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species (CITES), the RLI for internationally traded bird species showing trends in extinction risk driven by issues related to international trade is also declining: international trade remains a threat to the world's birds. Invasive species impact at least one third of the world's threatened bird species (398 species, 32.6%), with mammals being the most important (impacting 81.1%), particularly through predation by carnivores and rodents. The RLI illustrating impacts of invasive species shows that they are driving a deterioration in the status of the world's birds. RLIs for the impacts of use and invasive species will be important indicators to help track progress towards the target of significantly reducing biodiversity loss by 2010.","container-title":"Bird Conservation International","DOI":"10.1017/S095927090800035X","ISSN":"0959-2709, 1474-0001","issue":"S1","journalAbbreviation":"Bird Conservation International","language":"en","page":"S245-S262","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Red List Indices to measure the sustainability of species use and impacts of invasive alien species","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S095927090800035X/type/journal_article","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Trade generally correlates with declining abundances, and also increased risk of extinction, though other drivers are even more important  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zDL0AD6H","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Butchart, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Butchart, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32360,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LFV76C7F"],"itemData":{"id":32360,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            After habitat loss and degradation, the leading threats to biodiversity are over-exploitation and invasive alien species. For birds, newly synthesised data using the standard classification schemes for utilisation and threat types for the IUCN Red List allow novel analyses on the importance of these threats and permit the calculation of Red List Indices (RLIs) to show trends in the status of birds driven by these factors. At least 45.7% of extant bird species (4,561 species) are used by humans, principally for pets (37.0%) and for hunting for food (14.2%), but other uses include sport hunting, ornamentation and traditional medicine. Much of this use drives trade at an international scale, involving at least 3,337 species (33.9%, substantially higher than previous estimates), mostly for the pet trade. RLIs show that although successful control and management of use and trade have led to some species improving in status, this has been outweighed by the number of species deteriorating in status owing to unsustainable exploitation. Overall, the RLI showing trends in extinction risk driven by issues related to use shows a negative slope: human use of birds is currently unsustainable. Similarly, and of relevance to the Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species (CITES), the RLI for internationally traded bird species showing trends in extinction risk driven by issues related to international trade is also declining: international trade remains a threat to the world's birds. Invasive species impact at least one third of the world's threatened bird species (398 species, 32.6%), with mammals being the most important (impacting 81.1%), particularly through predation by carnivores and rodents. The RLI illustrating impacts of invasive species shows that they are driving a deterioration in the status of the world's birds. RLIs for the impacts of use and invasive species will be important indicators to help track progress towards the target of significantly reducing biodiversity loss by 2010.","container-title":"Bird Conservation International","DOI":"10.1017/S095927090800035X","ISSN":"0959-2709, 1474-0001","issue":"S1","journalAbbreviation":"Bird Conservation International","language":"en","page":"S245-S262","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Red List Indices to measure the sustainability of species use and impacts of invasive alien species","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S095927090800035X/type/journal_article","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Marsh ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kOwO0aev","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Marsh 2020)","plainCitation":"(Marsh 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32760,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AZ5LXZSH"],"itemData":{"id":32760,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13844","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"cobi.13844","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Prevalence of sustainable and unsustainable use of wild species inferred from the IUCN Red List of Threatened Species","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/cobi.13844","author":[{"family":"Marsh","given":"Sophie M. E."},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Burgess","given":"Neil D."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Challender","given":"Daniel W. S."},{"family":"Cremona","given":"Patricia J."},{"family":"Hilton?Taylor","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Micheaux","given":"Flore Lafaye"},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Böhm","given":"Monika"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} expand those results, also using International Union for Conservation of Nature data  show that across the 25,009 species in 10 taxonomic groups, 10,098 (40%) had some purpose of use documented. The proportion of species documented as having at least one purpose of use coded ranged from 15% (crustaceans) to nearly 100% of cone snails (544 of 545 species) among aquatic groups, and 11% (amphibians) to 76% (conifers) among terrestrial groups  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WjcObdMe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Marsh, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Marsh, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32760,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AZ5LXZSH"],"itemData":{"id":32760,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13844","ISSN":"0888-8892, 1523-1739","journalAbbreviation":"Conservation Biology","language":"en","page":"cobi.13844","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Prevalence of sustainable and unsustainable use of wild species inferred from the IUCN Red List of Threatened Species","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/cobi.13844","author":[{"family":"Marsh","given":"Sophie M. E."},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Burgess","given":"Neil D."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Challender","given":"Daniel W. S."},{"family":"Cremona","given":"Patricia J."},{"family":"Hilton?Taylor","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Micheaux","given":"Flore Lafaye"},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Böhm","given":"Monika"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Africa, more than 354 bird species are hunted for that purpose in 25 countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Fk84uMrc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(V. L. Williams, 2014)","plainCitation":"(V. L. Williams, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":739,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P4LJB9NZ"],"itemData":{"id":739,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Few regional or continent-wide assessments of bird use for traditional medicine have been attempted anywhere in the world. Africa has the highest known diversity of bird species used for this purpose. This study assesses the vulnerability of 354 bird species used for traditional medicine in 25 African countries, from 205 genera, 70 families, and 25 orders. The orders most represented were Passeriformes (107 species), Falconiformes (45 species), and Coraciiformes (24 species), and the families Accipitridae (37 species), Ardeidae (15 species), and Bucerotidae (12 species). The Barn owl (Tyto alba) was the most widely sold species (seven countries). The similarity of avifaunal orders traded is high (analogous to “morphospecies”, and using Sørensen's index), which suggests opportunities for a common understanding of cultural factors driving demand. The highest similarity was between bird orders sold in markets of Benin vs. Burkina Faso (90%), but even bird orders sold in two geographically separated countries (Benin vs. South Africa and Nigeria vs. South Africa) were 87% and 81% similar, respectively. Rabinowitz's “7 forms of rarity” model, used to group species according to commonness or rarity, indicated that 24% of traded bird species are very common, locally abundant in several habitats, and occur over a large geographical area, but 10% are rare, occur in low numbers in specific habitats, and over a small geographical area. The order with the highest proportion of rare species was the Musophagiformes. An analysis of species mass (as a proxy for size) indicated that large and/or conspicuous species tend to be targeted by harvesters for the traditional medicine trade. Furthermore, based on cluster analyses for species groups of similar risk, vultures, hornbills, and other large avifauna, such as bustards, are most threatened by selective harvesting and should be prioritised for conservation action.","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0105397","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"PLOS ONE","language":"en","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e105397","source":"PLoS Journals","title":"Risks to Birds Traded for African Traditional Medicine: A Quantitative Assessment","title-short":"Risks to Birds Traded for African Traditional Medicine","URL":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0105397","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Williams","given":"Vivienne L."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Anthony B."},{"family":"Kemp","given":"Alan C."},{"family":"Bruyns","given":"Robin K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",8,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Atuo ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wgimWIBI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2015)","plainCitation":"(2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26054,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GLKU7W9C","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CQGPCPHL"],"itemData":{"id":26054,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"614–622","source":"Google Scholar","title":"An assessment of socio-economic drivers of avian body parts trade in West African rainforests","volume":"191","author":[{"family":"Atuo","given":"Fidelis A."},{"family":"O'Connell Timothy","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} analyze the trade in avian body parts around major protected areas in the Cross River region of south-eastern Nigeria, which is an economic activity pursued primarily by younger people and villagers with low monthly income. In spite of three of the top 5 most reported species being globally threatened, knowledge of the threat status of species was not common among hunters and traders ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WwPvQJK6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Atuo & O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Connell Timothy, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Atuo & O’Connell Timothy, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26054,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GLKU7W9C","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CQGPCPHL"],"itemData":{"id":26054,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"614–622","source":"Google Scholar","title":"An assessment of socio-economic drivers of avian body parts trade in West African rainforests","volume":"191","author":[{"family":"Atuo","given":"Fidelis A."},{"family":"O'Connell Timothy","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.Twelve (42%) species were known to be declining and 5 (18%) are already listed as globally threatened under the International Union for Conservation of Nature /BirdLife threat criteria ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2IA8BBcG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Atuo & O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Connell Timothy, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Atuo & O’Connell Timothy, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":26054,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GLKU7W9C","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CQGPCPHL"],"itemData":{"id":26054,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"614–622","source":"Google Scholar","title":"An assessment of socio-economic drivers of avian body parts trade in West African rainforests","volume":"191","author":[{"family":"Atuo","given":"Fidelis A."},{"family":"O'Connell Timothy","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      A case study from the Hkakaborazi National Park reports that commercially valuable species that had been previously targeted by hunters (tiger, otter, pangolin) appear to be completely absent from current harvest records, which may suggest population decline and very low abundances ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iRbsGcm4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rao, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Rao, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7XIM9IFG"],"itemData":{"id":32811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-010-9519-x","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"143-153","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Hunting, Livelihoods and Declining Wildlife in the Hponkanrazi Wildlife Sanctuary, North Myanmar","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-010-9519-x","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Rao","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Htun","given":"Saw"},{"family":"Zaw","given":"Than"},{"family":"Myint","given":"Than"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Though farming is the predominant occupation (70% of surveyed population) hunting was conducted by a quarter of the surveyed people, and hunting was reported to be a significantly higher source of income than any other livelihood activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rW38BG46","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rao, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Rao, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7XIM9IFG"],"itemData":{"id":32811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-010-9519-x","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"143-153","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Hunting, Livelihoods and Declining Wildlife in the Hponkanrazi Wildlife Sanctuary, North Myanmar","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-010-9519-x","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Rao","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Htun","given":"Saw"},{"family":"Zaw","given":"Than"},{"family":"Myint","given":"Than"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Myanmar, a key mediating factor that facilitates the ongoing illegal hunting and trade is weak enforcement of laws and regulations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nGFkjTZI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rao, 2010; Shepherd & Nijman, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Rao, 2010; Shepherd & Nijman, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32788,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HVI4DCB2"],"itemData":{"id":32788,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"35–42","source":"Google Scholar","title":"The trade in bear parts from Myanmar: an illustration of the ineffectiveness of enforcement of international wildlife trade regulations","title-short":"The trade in bear parts from Myanmar","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Shepherd","given":"Chris R."},{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":32811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7XIM9IFG"],"itemData":{"id":32811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-010-9519-x","ISSN":"0364-152X, 1432-1009","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"143-153","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Hunting, Livelihoods and Declining Wildlife in the Hponkanrazi Wildlife Sanctuary, North Myanmar","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00267-010-9519-x","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Rao","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Htun","given":"Saw"},{"family":"Zaw","given":"Than"},{"family":"Myint","given":"Than"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	In Japan two species of bears (Ursus thibetanus and U. arctos) are traded for their gallbladder and meat. Yet, information about the scale of trade is poorly documented and also obscured by the fact that hunting bears is allowed in Japan, as long as it meant to control nuisance caused by bears ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SAKscRKv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mano & Ishii, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Mano & Ishii, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32353,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IF2PENVL"],"itemData":{"id":32353,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"International trade in bears and their parts is regulated under the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) because of the negative effect of gallbladder trade on bear populations. Although a significant number of bear gallbladders seems to come from the roughly 2,000 individuals killed annually as game and nuisance of the 2 species of bears (Ursus thibetanus and U. arctos) in Japan, information about the trade and usage remains obscure due to the lack of a system to regulate trade in bear parts such as gallbladders and meat. Most Japanese bear populations are considered to be at a sufficient level to sustain hunting if well-managed; however, nuisance bear control kills are not properly conducted due to inadequate management systems and regulations. Governmental organizations have not participated directly in nuisance bear control but depend on private hunters in exchange for allowing them to keep bear parts from nuisance kills. However, it will become difficult to continue depending on private hunters due to their aging and the decline in their numbers. This situation will require a new nuisance bear management system. We outline a framework for a management system for the domestic trade in gall derived from wild bears in Japan. Such a system would use the profits from bear gall trade to partially cover the cost of bear management activities, including damage prevention.","container-title":"Ursus","DOI":"10.2192/1537-6176-19.2.122","ISSN":"1537-6176, 1938-5439","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"ursu","note":"publisher: International Association for Bear Research and Management","page":"122-129","source":"bioone.org","title":"Bear Gallbladder Trade Issues and A Framework for Bear Management in Japan","URL":"https://bioone.org/journals/ursus/volume-19/issue-2/1537-6176-19.2.122/Bear-Gallbladder-Trade-Issues-and-A-Framework-for-Bear-Management/10.2192/1537-6176-19.2.122.full","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Mano","given":"Tsutomu"},{"family":"Ishii","given":"Nobuo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Though most Japanese bear populations are considered to be at a sufficient level to sustain hunting if well-managed, reconciling (perceived) threats of bears to the public and sustainability goals poses challenges to the bear nuisance management system ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7EOnyW0j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mano & Ishii, 2008; Sakurai, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Mano & Ishii, 2008; Sakurai, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32353,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IF2PENVL"],"itemData":{"id":32353,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"International trade in bears and their parts is regulated under the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) because of the negative effect of gallbladder trade on bear populations. Although a significant number of bear gallbladders seems to come from the roughly 2,000 individuals killed annually as game and nuisance of the 2 species of bears (Ursus thibetanus and U. arctos) in Japan, information about the trade and usage remains obscure due to the lack of a system to regulate trade in bear parts such as gallbladders and meat. Most Japanese bear populations are considered to be at a sufficient level to sustain hunting if well-managed; however, nuisance bear control kills are not properly conducted due to inadequate management systems and regulations. Governmental organizations have not participated directly in nuisance bear control but depend on private hunters in exchange for allowing them to keep bear parts from nuisance kills. However, it will become difficult to continue depending on private hunters due to their aging and the decline in their numbers. This situation will require a new nuisance bear management system. We outline a framework for a management system for the domestic trade in gall derived from wild bears in Japan. Such a system would use the profits from bear gall trade to partially cover the cost of bear management activities, including damage prevention.","container-title":"Ursus","DOI":"10.2192/1537-6176-19.2.122","ISSN":"1537-6176, 1938-5439","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"ursu","note":"publisher: International Association for Bear Research and Management","page":"122-129","source":"bioone.org","title":"Bear Gallbladder Trade Issues and A Framework for Bear Management in Japan","URL":"https://bioone.org/journals/ursus/volume-19/issue-2/1537-6176-19.2.122/Bear-Gallbladder-Trade-Issues-and-A-Framework-for-Bear-Management/10.2192/1537-6176-19.2.122.full","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Mano","given":"Tsutomu"},{"family":"Ishii","given":"Nobuo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",11]]}}},{"id":32778,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4VSJ9U2F"],"itemData":{"id":32778,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Human–wildlife conflict challenges wildlife managers globally. In Japan, the Asiatic black bear (Ursus thibetanus) is listed as a vulnerable species under IUCN's Red List of Threatened Species. In Hyogo Prefecture it is listed as an endangered local population, but bears are considered a nuisance because of agricultural damage and occasional human casualties. The bear population in the prefecture is increasing, and human–bear conflicts are also increasing. We conducted a mail survey in July 2010 of residents in 58 villages (n = 2,315) to examine their perceptions of risks, government performance, and acceptance of bears. Survey response was 52% (n = 1,210). Eighty-one percent of respondents reported that risks from bears had increased and 83% believed there were too many bears around their village. Risk perception was negatively correlated with acceptance capacity of bears. In contrast to our prediction, increased agreement that government provided necessary information was associated with greater perceived risk from bears. However, agreement that the government listened to people's concerns was a significant factor predicting respondents' behavior in taking preventive actions such as reporting bear sightings. This study suggests that, by providing problem-prevention information and adequate opportunities for residents to voice their concerns, government officials may be able to increase residents' confidence in their ability to prevent bear-related problems and their tolerance of interactions with bears. If widespread, such outcomes would improve conservation of bears of Japan.","container-title":"Ursus","DOI":"10.2192/URSUS-D-12-00011.1","ISSN":"1537-6176, 1938-5439","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"ursu","note":"publisher: International Association for Bear Research and Management","page":"70-82","source":"bioone.org","title":"Public perceptions of risk and government performance regarding bear management in Japan","URL":"https://bioone.org/journals/ursus/volume-24/issue-1/URSUS-D-12-00011.1/Public-perceptions-of-risk-and-government-performance-regarding-bear-management/10.2192/URSUS-D-12-00011.1.full","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Sakurai","given":"Ryo"},{"family":"Jacobson","given":"Susan K."},{"family":"Ueda","given":"Gouhei"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	In North Sumatra, hunting and trade of blood pythons (Python brongersmai) is an important activity, with around 50,000 individual snakes hunted each year since 1997  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gFHNEeaa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Natusch, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Natusch, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32792,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DHWS45FJ"],"itemData":{"id":32792,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife populations exposed to intense harvesting often exhibit shifts in attributes of the commercial offtake (e.g., numbers, body sizes, body condition, sex ratios, size at maturation, reproductive frequency). To look for such changes, we examined >2,500 specimens of field-collected blood pythons (Python brongersmai) brought to 2 processing facilities in North Sumatra over 2 survey periods (1996–1997 and 2014–2015). Over the 18 years between our surveys, approximately 900,000 blood pythons were taken from Indonesia for the commercial trade; North Sumatra accounted for about 35% of that trade volume. Between survey periods, numbers of snakes brought to 1 processing facility decreased and mean body size increased, whereas at the second facility numbers and sizes of snakes remained the same. Overall, the pythons collected in 2015–2016 were thinner-bodied than in 1996–1997, with fewer immature animals and a reduced size at maturation and reproductive frequency among females. Clutch size relative to maternal size decreased also. These temporal shifts in python demographics likely reflect a response to harvesting, although ecosystem changes may have played a role. Compared to other squamate reptiles, blood pythons may be more vulnerable to over-harvesting because most snakes are intensively collected from discrete habitat patches (oil palm plantations), and the species can be targeted by specific hunting methods. To ensure sustainability of the harvest, we recommend minimum and maximum size limits for skins used in the commercial trade, and more intensive monitoring at processing facilities. © 2019 The Wildlife Society.","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","DOI":"10.1002/jwmg.21790","ISSN":"1937-2817","issue":"2","license":"© 2019 The Wildlife Society","note":"_eprint: https://wildlife.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1002/jwmg.21790","page":"249-255","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Harvest Effects on Blood Pythons in North Sumatra","URL":"https://wildlife.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/jwmg.21790","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel J. D."},{"family":"Lyons","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Mumpuni","given":""},{"family":"Riyanto","given":"Awal"},{"family":"Shine","given":"Richard"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Comparing changes in numbers, demography (e.g. sex ratio, proportion of adults vs. juveniles), and life?history traits (e.g body size at maturation) of snakes brought to processing facilities in 1997 versus 2015 suggest that harvesting rates are unsustainable. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TgiYGFy5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Natusch, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Natusch, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32792,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DHWS45FJ"],"itemData":{"id":32792,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife populations exposed to intense harvesting often exhibit shifts in attributes of the commercial offtake (e.g., numbers, body sizes, body condition, sex ratios, size at maturation, reproductive frequency). To look for such changes, we examined >2,500 specimens of field-collected blood pythons (Python brongersmai) brought to 2 processing facilities in North Sumatra over 2 survey periods (1996–1997 and 2014–2015). Over the 18 years between our surveys, approximately 900,000 blood pythons were taken from Indonesia for the commercial trade; North Sumatra accounted for about 35% of that trade volume. Between survey periods, numbers of snakes brought to 1 processing facility decreased and mean body size increased, whereas at the second facility numbers and sizes of snakes remained the same. Overall, the pythons collected in 2015–2016 were thinner-bodied than in 1996–1997, with fewer immature animals and a reduced size at maturation and reproductive frequency among females. Clutch size relative to maternal size decreased also. These temporal shifts in python demographics likely reflect a response to harvesting, although ecosystem changes may have played a role. Compared to other squamate reptiles, blood pythons may be more vulnerable to over-harvesting because most snakes are intensively collected from discrete habitat patches (oil palm plantations), and the species can be targeted by specific hunting methods. To ensure sustainability of the harvest, we recommend minimum and maximum size limits for skins used in the commercial trade, and more intensive monitoring at processing facilities. © 2019 The Wildlife Society.","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","DOI":"10.1002/jwmg.21790","ISSN":"1937-2817","issue":"2","license":"© 2019 The Wildlife Society","note":"_eprint: https://wildlife.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1002/jwmg.21790","page":"249-255","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Harvest Effects on Blood Pythons in North Sumatra","URL":"https://wildlife.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/jwmg.21790","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel J. D."},{"family":"Lyons","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Mumpuni","given":""},{"family":"Riyanto","given":"Awal"},{"family":"Shine","given":"Richard"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wild species breeding farms can help to enable sustainable use, though there is a danger that illegally caught wild animals are ‘laundered’ and traded through the legal channel ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ukNIHoDi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lyons & Natusch, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Lyons & Natusch, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":716,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/28PSAP4U"],"itemData":{"id":716,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife breeding farms have been promoted to aid biodiversity conservation by alleviating the pressure of harvest on wild populations. There is, however, growing concern that many breeding farms are being used to launder illegally caught wildlife. Surveys of wildlife traders in the Indonesian provinces of Maluku, West Papua and Papua were conducted between August 2009 and April 2011 to assess the trade of the green python (Morelia viridis), the species currently exported in the largest numbers from Indonesia declared as captive-bred. In total, 4227 illegally collected wild green pythons were recorded during surveys and high levels of harvest were found to have depleted and skewed the demographics of some island populations. Snakes were traced from their point of capture to breeding farms in Jakarta where they are to be exported for the pet trade, confirming the reports of wildlife laundering. Extrapolation of monthly collection estimates provided by traders revealed that at least 5337 green pythons are collected each year, suggesting that at least 80% of the green pythons exported from Indonesia annually are illegally wild-caught. The results of examination of 139 eggshells from five python species suggest that reptilian eggshells may be used as proof of provenance for each individual reptile exported. This method, in addition to the evidence that breeding farms play a significant role in the illegal exploitation of wildlife, allows conservation managers to begin to adequately monitor, regulate and determine the role of breeding farms in the conservation of wild populations.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2011.10.002","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"3073-3081","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Wildlife laundering through breeding farms: Illegal harvest, population declines and a means of regulating the trade of green pythons (Morelia viridis) from Indonesia","title-short":"Wildlife laundering through breeding farms","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320711003685","volume":"144","author":[{"family":"Lyons","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel J. D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hunting of Scorpion Mud Turtle (K. scorpioides) is an activity practiced by artisanal fishermen (only men) on Marajó Island, Brazil ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gmtRxMWf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Cristo, 2017)","plainCitation":"(de Cristo, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32791,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FNR7JF47"],"itemData":{"id":32791,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Herpetological Journal","issue":"4","source":"Google Scholar","title":"The trade of Kinosternon scorpioides on Marajó island, Brazilian Amazon: from hunting to consumption.","title-short":"The trade of Kinosternon scorpioides on Marajó island, Brazilian Amazon","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Cristo","given":"Samuel Silva","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Baía Júnior","given":"Pedro Chaves"},{"family":"Silva","given":"Joilson Silva","non-dropping-particle":"da"},{"family":"Marques","given":"José Ribamar Felipe"},{"family":"Araújo Guimarães","given":"Diva Anelie","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While the scorpions are hunted for own consumption, a sizable number is traded and sold at urban centres. Current uses are often unsustainable, for example by setting fires in the grasslands, which causes scorpions to move into open areas where large quantities can be caught regardless of sex and size ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"l1L1An6j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de Cristo, 2017)","plainCitation":"(de Cristo, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32791,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FNR7JF47"],"itemData":{"id":32791,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Herpetological Journal","issue":"4","source":"Google Scholar","title":"The trade of Kinosternon scorpioides on Marajó island, Brazilian Amazon: from hunting to consumption.","title-short":"The trade of Kinosternon scorpioides on Marajó island, Brazilian Amazon","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Cristo","given":"Samuel Silva","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Baía Júnior","given":"Pedro Chaves"},{"family":"Silva","given":"Joilson Silva","non-dropping-particle":"da"},{"family":"Marques","given":"José Ribamar Felipe"},{"family":"Araújo Guimarães","given":"Diva Anelie","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	In Cambodia, an estimated 6.9 million snakes of seven species are estimated to be harvested from Tonle Sap Lake annually ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"x9PZUqZ9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. E. Brooks, 2007, 2010)","plainCitation":"(S. E. Brooks, 2007, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":738,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IVKSKT7X"],"itemData":{"id":738,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper documents the emergent snake ‘fishery’ occurring on Tonle Sap Lake where an estimated 6.9 million snakes (mostly homalopsids) are removed annually, representing the world’s largest exploitation of a single snake assemblage. Based on interviews with hunters, we found that snake catches declined by 74–84% between 2000 and 2005, raising strong concerns about the sustainability of this hunting operation. A combination of experimental trials to estimate population sizes and extensive catch and trade monitoring programs indicated that population density varies both spatially and temporally, largely due to the seasonally fluctuating environment of Tonle Sap Lake. The quantity of snakes captured mirrors the lake’s seasonal fluctuations, due to temporal changes in both catch per unit effort and the number of people hunting. Through interviews with hunters we scored the seven exploited species for perceived changes in catch size. All species were reported as declining and their scores match their predicted vulnerability based on a combination of timing of exploitation relative to breeding, proportion of catch consisting of mature females and large fecund females, fecundity, body size, size at maturity, and vulnerability to capture by gill nets. This information can inform conservation decisions for the long-term preservation of this snake assemblage. We propose emphasis should be placed on the snake skin trade that is targeting the largest, highly fecund females, and that any efforts to reduce hunting should focus on the peak in trade that occurs during the main breeding season.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2007.07.009","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"401-414","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Vulnerability of Cambodian water snakes: Initial assessment of the impact of hunting at Tonle Sap Lake","title-short":"Vulnerability of Cambodian water snakes","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320707002819","volume":"139","author":[{"family":"Brooks","given":"Sharon E."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Reynolds","given":"John D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",10,1]]}}},{"id":701,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C5FKF9B5"],"itemData":{"id":701,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Commercial trade is a major driver of over-exploitation of wild species, but the pattern of demand and how it responds to changes in supply is poorly understood. Here we explore the markets for snakes from Tonle Sap Lake in Cambodia to evaluate future exploitation scenarios, identify entry points for conservation and, more generally, to illustrate the value of multi-scale analysis of markets to traded wildlife conservation. In Cambodia, the largest driver of snake exploitation is the domestic trade in snakes as crocodile food. We estimate that farmed crocodiles consume between 2.7 and 12.2 million snakes per year. The market price for crocodiles has been in decline since 2003, which, combined with rising prices for their food, has led to a reduced frequency of feeding and closure of small farms. The large farms that generate a disproportionate amount of the demand for snakes continue to operate in anticipation of future market opportunities, and preferences for snakes could help maintain demand if market prices for crocodiles rise to pre 2003 levels. In the absence of a sustained demand from crocodile farms, it is also possible that alternative markets will develop, such as one for human snack food. The demand for snakes, however, also depends on the availability of substitute resources, principally ?sh. The substitutability and low price elasticity of demand offers a relatively sustainable form of consumerism. Given the nature of these market drivers, addressing consumer preferences and limiting the protection of snakes to their breeding season are likely to be the most effective tools for conservation. This study highlights the importance of understanding the structure of markets and the behaviour of consumer demand prior to implementing regulations on wildlife hunting and trade.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2010.05.023","ISSN":"00063207","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"2127-2135","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Snake prices and crocodile appetites: Aquatic wildlife supply and demand on Tonle Sap Lake, Cambodia","title-short":"Snake prices and crocodile appetites","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320710002600","volume":"143","author":[{"family":"Brooks","given":"Sharon E."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Gill","given":"Jennifer A."},{"family":"Reynolds","given":"John D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The largest driver of snake exploitation is the domestic trade in snakes as crocodile food, and to a smaller extent demand from international markets for exotic leather, luxury food and traditional medicine ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JH3gSO7V","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. E. Brooks, 2010)","plainCitation":"(S. E. Brooks, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":701,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C5FKF9B5"],"itemData":{"id":701,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Commercial trade is a major driver of over-exploitation of wild species, but the pattern of demand and how it responds to changes in supply is poorly understood. Here we explore the markets for snakes from Tonle Sap Lake in Cambodia to evaluate future exploitation scenarios, identify entry points for conservation and, more generally, to illustrate the value of multi-scale analysis of markets to traded wildlife conservation. In Cambodia, the largest driver of snake exploitation is the domestic trade in snakes as crocodile food. We estimate that farmed crocodiles consume between 2.7 and 12.2 million snakes per year. The market price for crocodiles has been in decline since 2003, which, combined with rising prices for their food, has led to a reduced frequency of feeding and closure of small farms. The large farms that generate a disproportionate amount of the demand for snakes continue to operate in anticipation of future market opportunities, and preferences for snakes could help maintain demand if market prices for crocodiles rise to pre 2003 levels. In the absence of a sustained demand from crocodile farms, it is also possible that alternative markets will develop, such as one for human snack food. The demand for snakes, however, also depends on the availability of substitute resources, principally ?sh. The substitutability and low price elasticity of demand offers a relatively sustainable form of consumerism. Given the nature of these market drivers, addressing consumer preferences and limiting the protection of snakes to their breeding season are likely to be the most effective tools for conservation. This study highlights the importance of understanding the structure of markets and the behaviour of consumer demand prior to implementing regulations on wildlife hunting and trade.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2010.05.023","ISSN":"00063207","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"2127-2135","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Snake prices and crocodile appetites: Aquatic wildlife supply and demand on Tonle Sap Lake, Cambodia","title-short":"Snake prices and crocodile appetites","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320710002600","volume":"143","author":[{"family":"Brooks","given":"Sharon E."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Gill","given":"Jennifer A."},{"family":"Reynolds","given":"John D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The key driver, demand for snakes as crocodile food is strongly influenced by the price of alternatives, such as fish ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"54eRLN5y","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. E. Brooks, 2010)","plainCitation":"(S. E. Brooks, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":701,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C5FKF9B5"],"itemData":{"id":701,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Commercial trade is a major driver of over-exploitation of wild species, but the pattern of demand and how it responds to changes in supply is poorly understood. Here we explore the markets for snakes from Tonle Sap Lake in Cambodia to evaluate future exploitation scenarios, identify entry points for conservation and, more generally, to illustrate the value of multi-scale analysis of markets to traded wildlife conservation. In Cambodia, the largest driver of snake exploitation is the domestic trade in snakes as crocodile food. We estimate that farmed crocodiles consume between 2.7 and 12.2 million snakes per year. The market price for crocodiles has been in decline since 2003, which, combined with rising prices for their food, has led to a reduced frequency of feeding and closure of small farms. The large farms that generate a disproportionate amount of the demand for snakes continue to operate in anticipation of future market opportunities, and preferences for snakes could help maintain demand if market prices for crocodiles rise to pre 2003 levels. In the absence of a sustained demand from crocodile farms, it is also possible that alternative markets will develop, such as one for human snack food. The demand for snakes, however, also depends on the availability of substitute resources, principally ?sh. The substitutability and low price elasticity of demand offers a relatively sustainable form of consumerism. Given the nature of these market drivers, addressing consumer preferences and limiting the protection of snakes to their breeding season are likely to be the most effective tools for conservation. This study highlights the importance of understanding the structure of markets and the behaviour of consumer demand prior to implementing regulations on wildlife hunting and trade.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2010.05.023","ISSN":"00063207","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"2127-2135","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Snake prices and crocodile appetites: Aquatic wildlife supply and demand on Tonle Sap Lake, Cambodia","title-short":"Snake prices and crocodile appetites","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320710002600","volume":"143","author":[{"family":"Brooks","given":"Sharon E."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Edward H."},{"family":"Gill","given":"Jennifer A."},{"family":"Reynolds","given":"John D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Non-lethal terrestrial animal harvesting
Pet trade can be an important driver of biodiversity loss and harvesting of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Rr8zgVwr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. E. Baker, 2013; Bush, 2014; Ng, 2016)","plainCitation":"(S. E. Baker, 2013; Bush, 2014; Ng, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":741,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5CEA5DC8"],"itemData":{"id":741,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The ornamental pet trade is often considered a key culprit for conservation problems such as the introduction of invasive species (including infectious diseases) and overharvesting of rare species. Here, we present the first assessment of the biodiversity of freshwater molluscs in the ornamental pet trade in Singapore, one of the most important global hubs of the ornamental aquarium trade, and discuss associated conservation concerns. We recorded freshwater molluscs from ornamental pet shops and major exporters including non-ornamental species (e.g., hitchhikers, molluscs sold as fish feed). We recorded an unexpectedly high diversity—59 species—of freshwater bivalves and gastropods, with the majority (38 species or 64%) being from the Oriental region. In addition to morphological examination, we sequenced the DNA barcode region of mitochondrial CO1 and 16S genes to provide molecular data for the confirmation of the identification and for future re-identification. DNA barcodes were obtained for 50 species, and all but four were separated by > 3% uncorrected pairwise distances. The trade has been considered a main introduction pathway for non-native species to Singapore, and we found that out of 15 species in the trade as well as in the wild in Singapore, 12 are either introduced or of unknown origin, representing almost half of the known non-native freshwater molluscs in Singapore. Particularly prevalent are non-ornamental species: six hitchhikers on aquarium plants and six species sold as fish feed. We found that a quarter of the trade species have a history of introduction, which includes 11 known or potentially invasive species. We conclude that potential overharvesting is difficult to assess because only half of the trade species have been treated by IUCN. Of these, 21 species are of Least Concern and three are Data Deficient. Our checklist, with accompanying DNA barcodes, images, and museum vouchers, provides an important reference library for future monitoring, and constitutes a step toward creating a more sustainable ornamental pet trade.","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0161130","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"PLOS ONE","language":"en","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e0161130","source":"PLoS Journals","title":"Molluscs for Sale: Assessment of Freshwater Gastropods and Bivalves in the Ornamental Pet Trade","title-short":"Molluscs for Sale","URL":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0161130","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Ng","given":"Ting Hui"},{"family":"Tan","given":"Siong Kiat"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Wing Hing"},{"family":"Meier","given":"Rudolf"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Sow-Yan"},{"family":"Tan","given":"Heok Hui"},{"family":"Yeo","given":"Darren C. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",8,15]]}}},{"id":32742,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SJ82QLSW"],"itemData":{"id":32742,"type":"book","abstract":"International trade in exotic pets is an important and increasing driver of biodiversity loss and often compromises the standards required for good animal welfare. We systematically reviewed the scientific and gray literature and used the United Nations Environment Programme - World Conservation Monitoring Centre (UNEP-WCMC) Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) trade database to establish temporal and geographical trade patterns of live exotic birds, mammals, and reptiles and to describe trends in research, taxonomic representation, and level of threat and legal protection of species traded. Birds were the most species-rich and abundant class reported in trade; reptiles were second most abundant but unusually the most studied in this context; and mammals were least abundant in trade. Mammalian and reptilian species traded as pets were more likely to be threatened than expected by random. There have been a substantial number of Appendix I listed captive-bred mammals and birds and wild-caught birds and reptiles reported in trade to CITES. We identified the Middle East's emerging role as a driver of demand for exotic pets of all taxa alongside the well-established and increasing role of South America and Southeast Asia in the market. Europe, North America, and the Middle East featured most heavily in trade reports to CITES, whereas trade involving South America and Southeast Asia were given most emphasis in the literature. For effective monitoring of and appropriate response to the international exotic pet trade, it is imperative that the reliability and detail of CITES trade reports improve and that scientific research be directed toward those taxa and locations that are most vulnerable. El Mercado Global de Mascotas Exóticas 2006-2012 El mercado internacional de mascotas exóticas es un factor importante y creciente de pérdida de la biodiversidad y continuamente compromete los estándares requeridos para un buen bienestar animal. Sistemáticamente revisamos la literatura científica y gris y usamos la base de datos de mercados de UNEP-WCMC CITES para establecer patrones temporales y geográficos del mercado de aves, mamíferos y reptiles exóticos vivos y describir las tendencias en la investigación, la representación taxonómica y el nivel de amenaza y la protección legal de las especies comercializadas. Las aves fueron la clase con mayor riqueza de especies y la más abundante; los reptiles fueron la segunda más abundante pero inusualmente la más estudiada en este contexto; los mamíferos fueron los menos abundantes en el mercado. Las especies de mamíferos y reptiles comercializadas como mascotas tuvieron mayor probabilidad de estar amenazadas que lo esperado por el azar. Ha habido un número sustancial de especies de mamíferos y aves criados en cautiverio y listados en el Apéndice-I y de aves y reptiles capturados en vida silvestre reportados en el mercado a CITES. Identificamos el papel emergente del Oriente Medio como conductor de la demanda de mascotas exóticas de todos los taxones junto con el bien establecido y creciente papel de América del Sur y el Sureste Asiático en el mercado. Europa, América del Norte y el Oriente Medio figuraron más en los reportes de mercado hechos a CITES, mientras que al mercado en América del Sur y el Sureste Asiático se le dio más énfasis en la literatura. Para un monitoreo efectivo de y una respuesta apropiada al mercado internacional de mascotas exóticas, es imperativo que la confiabilidad y el detalle de los reportes de mercado de CITES mejoren y que la investigación científica sea dirigida hacia esos taxones y las localidades que sean más vulnerables.","collection-number":"3","note":"DOI: 10.1111/cobi.12240","number-of-pages":"663–676","publisher":"Blackwell Publishing Inc.","title":"Global trade in exotic pets 2006-2012","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Bush","given":"Emma R."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Sandra E."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":721,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H8KV6TSV"],"itemData":{"id":721,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","issue":"12","note":"publisher: American Institute of Biological Sciences Circulation, AIBS, 1313 Dolley …","page":"928–938","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Rough trade: animal welfare in the global wildlife trade","title-short":"Rough trade","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Baker","given":"Sandra E."},{"family":"Cain","given":"Russ"},{"family":"Van Kesteren","given":"Freya"},{"family":"Zommers","given":"Zinta A."},{"family":"D'cruze","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It can also be an an indirect driver as a vector for zoonotic diseases and invasive species more generally ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"99tPsbVb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Borsky, 2020; Fitzpatrick, 2018; JNCC, 2021; L\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}tters, 2018; Martel, 2014; D. A. Travis, 2011; Woeltjes, 2011; Yuan, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Borsky, 2020; Fitzpatrick, 2018; JNCC, 2021; Lötters, 2018; Martel, 2014; D. A. Travis, 2011; Woeltjes, 2011; Yuan, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":703,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R45YVVYM"],"itemData":{"id":703,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Revue Scientifique et Technique-OIE","issue":"1","page":"219","source":"Google Scholar","title":"The spread of pathogens through trade in wildlife","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Travis","given":"D. A."},{"family":"Watson","given":"R. P."},{"family":"Tauer","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":702,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3C9HFC7M"],"itemData":{"id":702,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"<section class=\\"abstract\\"><div id=\\"\\" class=\\"section\\"><h3 class=\\"abstractTitle text-title my-1\\" id=\\"d169396e3\\">Abstract</h3><p>\\n               <em>Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis</em> is the cause of the fungal disease chytridiomycosis, a potentially lethal skin disease of amphibians. Asymptomatically infected amphibians may pose a risk for environmental pathogen pollution. This study therefore assessed the role of healthy, captive amphibians as a reservoir of <em>Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis</em>. Samples were collected from captive amphibians in Belgium, the Netherlands, Germany and France (559 from anurans, 330 from urodelans and 4 from gymnophians) from private owners, zoos, and laboratories. In addition to which, 78 anurans from 19 living collections were sampled during a pet fair in the Netherlands. Nearly 3% of the captive amphibians were infected by <em>B. dendrobatidis</em>, and 13.6% of the collections yielded at least one positive result. At the fair, 7 out of 78 anurans, representing 2 collections were positive. None of the animals that tested positive showed any obvious health problems at the time of sampling. Our results demonstrate the potential of the amphibian pet trade as a vehicle for the spread of <em>B. dendrobatidis.</em>\\n            </p></div></section>","container-title":"Amphibia-Reptilia","DOI":"10.1163/017353711X579830","ISSN":"1568-5381, 0173-5373","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"publisher: Brill\\nsection: Amphibia-Reptilia","page":"419-423","source":"brill.com","title":"Clinically healthy amphibians in captive collections and at pet fairs: A reservoir of Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis","title-short":"Clinically healthy amphibians in captive collections and at pet fairs","URL":"https://brill.com/view/journals/amre/32/3/article-p419_13.xml","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Woeltjes","given":"Tonnie"},{"family":"Rendle","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Sluijs","given":"Annemarieke Spitzen-van","dropping-particle":"der"},{"family":"Haesebrouck","given":"Freddy"},{"family":"Martel","given":"An"},{"family":"Pasmans","given":"Frank"},{"family":"Wombwell","given":"Emma"},{"family":"Rooij","given":"Pascale Van"},{"family":"Zollinger","given":"Ronald"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",1,1]]}}},{"id":712,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ASRQ5YI7"],"itemData":{"id":712,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Understanding introduction routes for wildlife pathogens is vital for the development of threat abatement plans. The chytrid fungus Batrachochytrium salamandrivorans (Bsal) has recently emerged in Europe, where it is considered to be a serious threat for urodelan conservation. If the highly diverse Chinese urodelans were to constitute a Bsal reservoir, then the significant international trade in these species may vector Bsal into naïve urodelan communities. Here, we analyzed a total of 1,143 samples, representing 36 Chinese salamander species from 51 localities across southern China for the presence of Bsal. We found Bsal was present across a wide taxonomic, geographical, and environmental range. In particular, Bsal DNA was detected in 33 samples from the genera Cynops, Pachytriton, Paramesotriton, Tylototriton, and Andrias, including the heavily traded species Paramesotriton hongkongensis and Cynops orientalis. The true Bsal prevalence across our data set was estimated between 2% and 4%, with a maximum of 50% in a population of P. hongkongensis. Even at this overall relatively low Bsal prevalence, the exportation of millions of animals renders Bsal introduction in naïve, importing countries a near certainty, which calls for the urgent implementation of proper biosecurity in the international wildlife trade.","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/conl.12436","ISSN":"1755-263X","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"© 2018 The Authors. Conservation Letters published by Wiley Periodicals, Inc.","note":"_eprint: https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/conl.12436","page":"e12436","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Widespread occurrence of an emerging fungal pathogen in heavily traded Chinese urodelan species","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/conl.12436","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Yuan","given":"Zhiyong"},{"family":"Martel","given":"An"},{"family":"Wu","given":"Jun"},{"family":"Praet","given":"Sarah Van"},{"family":"Canessa","given":"Stefano"},{"family":"Pasmans","given":"Frank"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32785,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9HLU43VA"],"itemData":{"id":32785,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000555732200006","container-title":"Environmental & Resource Economics","DOI":"10.1007/s10640-020-00456-7","ISSN":"0924-6460","issue":"4","page":"1001-1017","title":"CITES and the Zoonotic Disease Content in International Wildlife Trade","URL":"://WOS:000555732200006","volume":"76","author":[{"family":"Borsky","given":"S."},{"family":"Hennighausen","given":"H."},{"family":"Leiter","given":"A."},{"family":"Williges","given":"K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",8]]}}},{"id":706,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FRLEUE52"],"itemData":{"id":706,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The amphibian chytrid fungus Batrachochytrium salamandrivorans (Bsal) infects newts and salamanders (urodele amphibians), in which it can cause fatal disease. This pathogen has caused dramatic fire salamander population declines in Belgium, the Netherlands and Germany since its discovery in 2010. Thought to be native to Asia, it has been hypothesised that Bsal was introduced to Europe with the importation of infected amphibians for the commercial pet trade. Following the discovery of Bsal in captive amphibians in the United Kingdom in 2015, we used contact-tracing to identify epidemiologically-linked private amphibian collections in Western Europe. Of 16 linked collections identified, animals were tested from 11 and urodeles tested positive for Bsal in seven, including the identification of the pathogen in Spain for the first time. Mortality of Bsal-positive individuals was observed in five collections. Our results indicate that Bsal is likely widespread within the private amphibian trade, at least in Europe. These findings are important for informing policy regarding Bsal control strategies.","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/s41598-018-31800-z","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"2018 The Author(s)","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"13845","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Epidemiological tracing of Batrachochytrium salamandrivorans identifies widespread infection and associated mortalities in private amphibian collections","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-018-31800-z","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Fitzpatrick","given":"Liam D."},{"family":"Pasmans","given":"Frank"},{"family":"Martel","given":"An"},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"Andrew A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",9,14]]}}},{"id":713,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S568KE7W"],"itemData":{"id":713,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Salamandra","issue":"4","page":"287–290","source":"Google Scholar","title":"First report of host co-infection of parasitic amphibian chytrid fungi","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Lötters","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Wagner","given":"Norman"},{"family":"Kerres","given":"Alexander"},{"family":"Vences","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Steinfartz","given":"Sebastian"},{"family":"Sabino-Pinto","given":"Joana"},{"family":"Seufer","given":"Larissa"},{"family":"Preissler","given":"Kathleen"},{"family":"Schulz","given":"Vanessa"},{"family":"Veith","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":705,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BLXF54G5"],"itemData":{"id":705,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"A new, yet old, threat to amphibians\\nGlobally, populations of amphibians have been severely affected by a disease caused by the fungus Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis. Recently, some European salamander populations were decimated by the emergence of a new, related chytrid fungus, B. salamandrivorans. Martel screened amphibians across continents. This newly emerging threat seems to have originated in Asia and traveled to Europe with salamanders transported as part of the pet trade. Asian salamanders have evolved resistance to the pathogen, but salamanders from other parts of the world are highly susceptible.\\nScience, this issue p. 630\\nEmerging infectious diseases are reducing biodiversity on a global scale. Recently, the emergence of the chytrid fungus Batrachochytrium salamandrivorans resulted in rapid declines in populations of European fire salamanders. Here, we screened more than 5000 amphibians from across four continents and combined experimental assessment of pathogenicity with phylogenetic methods to estimate the threat that this infection poses to amphibian diversity. Results show that B. salamandrivorans is restricted to, but highly pathogenic for, salamanders and newts (Urodela). The pathogen likely originated and remained in coexistence with a clade of salamander hosts for millions of years in Asia. As a result of globalization and lack of biosecurity, it has recently been introduced into naïve European amphibian populations, where it is currently causing biodiversity loss.\\nA new fungal disease from Asia threatens salamanders in Europe, the Middle East, and North Africa.\\nA new fungal disease from Asia threatens salamanders in Europe, the Middle East, and North Africa.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1258268","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6209","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2014, American Association for the Advancement of Science","note":"publisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science\\nsection: Report\\nPMID: 25359973","page":"630-631","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"Recent introduction of a chytrid fungus endangers Western Palearctic salamanders","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/346/6209/630","volume":"346","author":[{"family":"Martel","given":"A."},{"family":"Blooi","given":"M."},{"family":"Adriaensen","given":"C."},{"family":"Rooij","given":"P. Van"},{"family":"Beukema","given":"W."},{"family":"Fisher","given":"M. C."},{"family":"Farrer","given":"R. A."},{"family":"Schmidt","given":"B. R."},{"family":"Tobler","given":"U."},{"family":"Goka","given":"K."},{"family":"Lips","given":"K. R."},{"family":"Muletz","given":"C."},{"family":"Zamudio","given":"K. R."},{"family":"Bosch","given":"J."},{"family":"Lötters","given":"S."},{"family":"Wombwell","given":"E."},{"family":"Garner","given":"T. W. J."},{"family":"Cunningham","given":"A. A."},{"family":"Sluijs","given":"A. Spitzen-van","dropping-particle":"der"},{"family":"Salvidio","given":"S."},{"family":"Ducatelle","given":"R."},{"family":"Nishikawa","given":"K."},{"family":"Nguyen","given":"T. T."},{"family":"Kolby","given":"J. E."},{"family":"Bocxlaer","given":"I. Van"},{"family":"Bossuyt","given":"F."},{"family":"Pasmans","given":"F."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",10,31]]}}},{"id":32777,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KL9G3865"],"itemData":{"id":32777,"type":"report","language":"en","page":"JNCC Report No. 678","source":"Zotero","title":"Zoonotic potential of international trade in CITES-listed species","author":[{"literal":"JNCC"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another indirect effect of pet trade is the habitat destruction caused by non-lethal harvesting, for example the collection of reptiles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iBQ3z6hC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Auliya, Altherr,, 2016; Goode, 2004, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Auliya, Altherr,, 2016; Goode, 2004, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32746,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R29PGGD2"],"itemData":{"id":32746,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Of the 10,272 currently recognized reptile species, the trade of fewer than 8% are regulated by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and the European Wildlife Trade Regulations (EWTR). However, the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List has assessed 45% of the world's reptile species and determined that at least 1390 species are threatened by “biological resource use”. Of these, 355 species are intentionally targeted by collectors, including 194 non-CITES-listed species. Herein we review the global reptile pet trade, its impacts, and its contribution to the over-harvesting of species and populations, in light of current international law. Findings are based on an examination of relevant professional observations, online sources, and literature (e.g., applicable policies, taxonomy [reptile database], trade statistics [EUROSTAT], and conservation status [IUCN Red List]). Case studies are presented from the following countries and regions: Australia, Central America, China, Galapagos Islands (Ecuador), Germany, Europe, India, Indonesia (Kalimantan), Islamic Republic of Iran, Japan, Madagascar, Mexico, New Zealand, the Philippines, South Africa, Sri Lanka, Vietnam, Western Africa, and Western Asia. The European Union (EU) plays a major role in reptile trade. Between 2004 and 2014 (the period under study), the EU member states officially reported the import of 20,788,747 live reptiles. This review suggests that illegal trade activities involve species regulated under CITES, as well as species that are not CITES-regulated but nationally protected in their country of origin and often openly offered for sale in the EU. Further, these case studies demonstrate that regulations and enforcement in several countries are inadequate to prevent the overexploitation of species and to halt illegal trade activities.","collection-title":"Advancing reptile conservation: Addressing knowledge gaps and mitigating key drivers of extinction risk","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2016.05.017","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"103-119","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Trade in live reptiles, its impact on wild populations, and the role of the European market","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320716301987","volume":"204","author":[{"family":"Auliya","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Altherr","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Ariano-Sanchez","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Baard","given":"Ernst H."},{"family":"Brown","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Rafe M."},{"family":"Cantu","given":"Juan-Carlos"},{"family":"Gentile","given":"Gabriele"},{"family":"Gildenhuys","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Henningheim","given":"Evert"},{"family":"Hintzmann","given":"Jürgen"},{"family":"Kanari","given":"Kahoru"},{"family":"Krvavac","given":"Milivoje"},{"family":"Lettink","given":"Marieke"},{"family":"Lippert","given":"Jörg"},{"family":"Luiselli","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Nilson","given":"Göran"},{"family":"Nguyen","given":"Truong Quang"},{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Parham","given":"James F."},{"family":"Pasachnik","given":"Stesha A."},{"family":"Pedrono","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Rauhaus","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Córdova","given":"Danny Rueda"},{"family":"Sanchez","given":"Maria-Elena"},{"family":"Schepp","given":"Ulrich"},{"family":"Schingen","given":"Mona","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Schneeweiss","given":"Norbert"},{"family":"Segniagbeto","given":"Gabriel H."},{"family":"Somaweera","given":"Ruchira"},{"family":"Sy","given":"Emerson Y."},{"family":"Türkozan","given":"Oguz"},{"family":"Vinke","given":"Sabine"},{"family":"Vinke","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Vyas","given":"Raju"},{"family":"Williamson","given":"Stuart"},{"family":"Ziegler","given":"Thomas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12,1]]}}},{"id":32781,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JQ6WWPBQ"],"itemData":{"id":32781,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of wildlife management","issue":"2","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"429–434","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Effects of destructive collecting practices on reptiles: a field experiment","title-short":"Effects of destructive collecting practices on reptiles","volume":"68","author":[{"family":"Goode","given":"Matthew J."},{"family":"Swann","given":"Don E."},{"family":"Schwalbe","given":"Cecil R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":709,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VD5UV3H6"],"itemData":{"id":709,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Declines in biodiversity caused by habitat loss have been well documented on large spatial scales, however, effects of habitat loss on small scales have received little attention. Some common methods of reptile collection, primarily for commercial harvest, result in destruction of cracks, crevices, and other cool, moist microhabitats in desert rock outcrops. We developed a method for identifying habitat destruction associated with reptile collecting. We surveyed lightly and heavily disturbed areas near Phoenix, Arizona to determine if microhabitat loss caused by collectors was associated with decreased relative abundance of reptiles. Of four diurnal lizard species studied, relative abundance of two rock-dwelling species was negatively correlated with level of microhabitat destruction, whereas relative abundance of one ground-dwelling species and one habitat generalist species was not. Habitat destruction caused by collectors may have negative effects, not only at the individual level, but at the population and community levels as well. We recommend regulation of commercial trade in reptiles; disallowing collecting activities that cause habitat damage; increased law enforcement; and educational programs directed primarily at novice collectors.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2005.03.010","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"47-54","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Habitat destruction by collectors associated with decreased abundance of rock-dwelling lizards","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320705001333","volume":"125","author":[{"family":"Goode","given":"Matthew J."},{"family":"Horrace","given":"William C."},{"family":"Sredl","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Howland","given":"Jeffrey M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",9,1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	20 percent of recent wild species trade reports are related to pets or animals for use in entertainment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZCZxGOhj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. E. Baker, 2013; Bush, 2014)","plainCitation":"(S. E. Baker, 2013; Bush, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32742,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SJ82QLSW"],"itemData":{"id":32742,"type":"book","abstract":"International trade in exotic pets is an important and increasing driver of biodiversity loss and often compromises the standards required for good animal welfare. We systematically reviewed the scientific and gray literature and used the United Nations Environment Programme - World Conservation Monitoring Centre (UNEP-WCMC) Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) trade database to establish temporal and geographical trade patterns of live exotic birds, mammals, and reptiles and to describe trends in research, taxonomic representation, and level of threat and legal protection of species traded. Birds were the most species-rich and abundant class reported in trade; reptiles were second most abundant but unusually the most studied in this context; and mammals were least abundant in trade. Mammalian and reptilian species traded as pets were more likely to be threatened than expected by random. There have been a substantial number of Appendix I listed captive-bred mammals and birds and wild-caught birds and reptiles reported in trade to CITES. We identified the Middle East's emerging role as a driver of demand for exotic pets of all taxa alongside the well-established and increasing role of South America and Southeast Asia in the market. Europe, North America, and the Middle East featured most heavily in trade reports to CITES, whereas trade involving South America and Southeast Asia were given most emphasis in the literature. For effective monitoring of and appropriate response to the international exotic pet trade, it is imperative that the reliability and detail of CITES trade reports improve and that scientific research be directed toward those taxa and locations that are most vulnerable. El Mercado Global de Mascotas Exóticas 2006-2012 El mercado internacional de mascotas exóticas es un factor importante y creciente de pérdida de la biodiversidad y continuamente compromete los estándares requeridos para un buen bienestar animal. Sistemáticamente revisamos la literatura científica y gris y usamos la base de datos de mercados de UNEP-WCMC CITES para establecer patrones temporales y geográficos del mercado de aves, mamíferos y reptiles exóticos vivos y describir las tendencias en la investigación, la representación taxonómica y el nivel de amenaza y la protección legal de las especies comercializadas. Las aves fueron la clase con mayor riqueza de especies y la más abundante; los reptiles fueron la segunda más abundante pero inusualmente la más estudiada en este contexto; los mamíferos fueron los menos abundantes en el mercado. Las especies de mamíferos y reptiles comercializadas como mascotas tuvieron mayor probabilidad de estar amenazadas que lo esperado por el azar. Ha habido un número sustancial de especies de mamíferos y aves criados en cautiverio y listados en el Apéndice-I y de aves y reptiles capturados en vida silvestre reportados en el mercado a CITES. Identificamos el papel emergente del Oriente Medio como conductor de la demanda de mascotas exóticas de todos los taxones junto con el bien establecido y creciente papel de América del Sur y el Sureste Asiático en el mercado. Europa, América del Norte y el Oriente Medio figuraron más en los reportes de mercado hechos a CITES, mientras que al mercado en América del Sur y el Sureste Asiático se le dio más énfasis en la literatura. Para un monitoreo efectivo de y una respuesta apropiada al mercado internacional de mascotas exóticas, es imperativo que la confiabilidad y el detalle de los reportes de mercado de CITES mejoren y que la investigación científica sea dirigida hacia esos taxones y las localidades que sean más vulnerables.","collection-number":"3","note":"DOI: 10.1111/cobi.12240","number-of-pages":"663–676","publisher":"Blackwell Publishing Inc.","title":"Global trade in exotic pets 2006-2012","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Bush","given":"Emma R."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Sandra E."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":721,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H8KV6TSV"],"itemData":{"id":721,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","issue":"12","note":"publisher: American Institute of Biological Sciences Circulation, AIBS, 1313 Dolley …","page":"928–938","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Rough trade: animal welfare in the global wildlife trade","title-short":"Rough trade","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Baker","given":"Sandra E."},{"family":"Cain","given":"Russ"},{"family":"Van Kesteren","given":"Freya"},{"family":"Zommers","given":"Zinta A."},{"family":"D'cruze","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At least 45.7% of extant bird species (4,561 species) are used by humans, from which 37% as pets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QTrdNBqS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Butchart, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Butchart, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32360,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LFV76C7F"],"itemData":{"id":32360,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract\\n            After habitat loss and degradation, the leading threats to biodiversity are over-exploitation and invasive alien species. For birds, newly synthesised data using the standard classification schemes for utilisation and threat types for the IUCN Red List allow novel analyses on the importance of these threats and permit the calculation of Red List Indices (RLIs) to show trends in the status of birds driven by these factors. At least 45.7% of extant bird species (4,561 species) are used by humans, principally for pets (37.0%) and for hunting for food (14.2%), but other uses include sport hunting, ornamentation and traditional medicine. Much of this use drives trade at an international scale, involving at least 3,337 species (33.9%, substantially higher than previous estimates), mostly for the pet trade. RLIs show that although successful control and management of use and trade have led to some species improving in status, this has been outweighed by the number of species deteriorating in status owing to unsustainable exploitation. Overall, the RLI showing trends in extinction risk driven by issues related to use shows a negative slope: human use of birds is currently unsustainable. Similarly, and of relevance to the Convention on the International Trade in Endangered Species (CITES), the RLI for internationally traded bird species showing trends in extinction risk driven by issues related to international trade is also declining: international trade remains a threat to the world's birds. Invasive species impact at least one third of the world's threatened bird species (398 species, 32.6%), with mammals being the most important (impacting 81.1%), particularly through predation by carnivores and rodents. The RLI illustrating impacts of invasive species shows that they are driving a deterioration in the status of the world's birds. RLIs for the impacts of use and invasive species will be important indicators to help track progress towards the target of significantly reducing biodiversity loss by 2010.","container-title":"Bird Conservation International","DOI":"10.1017/S095927090800035X","ISSN":"0959-2709, 1474-0001","issue":"S1","journalAbbreviation":"Bird Conservation International","language":"en","page":"S245-S262","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Red List Indices to measure the sustainability of species use and impacts of invasive alien species","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S095927090800035X/type/journal_article","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Butchart","given":"Stuart H. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In a systematic literature review, birds were the most species-rich class reported (585 species) in trade, followed by reptiles (485 species) and mammals (113 species) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hF7OwuvO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bush, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Bush, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32742,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SJ82QLSW"],"itemData":{"id":32742,"type":"book","abstract":"International trade in exotic pets is an important and increasing driver of biodiversity loss and often compromises the standards required for good animal welfare. We systematically reviewed the scientific and gray literature and used the United Nations Environment Programme - World Conservation Monitoring Centre (UNEP-WCMC) Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) trade database to establish temporal and geographical trade patterns of live exotic birds, mammals, and reptiles and to describe trends in research, taxonomic representation, and level of threat and legal protection of species traded. Birds were the most species-rich and abundant class reported in trade; reptiles were second most abundant but unusually the most studied in this context; and mammals were least abundant in trade. Mammalian and reptilian species traded as pets were more likely to be threatened than expected by random. There have been a substantial number of Appendix I listed captive-bred mammals and birds and wild-caught birds and reptiles reported in trade to CITES. We identified the Middle East's emerging role as a driver of demand for exotic pets of all taxa alongside the well-established and increasing role of South America and Southeast Asia in the market. Europe, North America, and the Middle East featured most heavily in trade reports to CITES, whereas trade involving South America and Southeast Asia were given most emphasis in the literature. For effective monitoring of and appropriate response to the international exotic pet trade, it is imperative that the reliability and detail of CITES trade reports improve and that scientific research be directed toward those taxa and locations that are most vulnerable. El Mercado Global de Mascotas Exóticas 2006-2012 El mercado internacional de mascotas exóticas es un factor importante y creciente de pérdida de la biodiversidad y continuamente compromete los estándares requeridos para un buen bienestar animal. Sistemáticamente revisamos la literatura científica y gris y usamos la base de datos de mercados de UNEP-WCMC CITES para establecer patrones temporales y geográficos del mercado de aves, mamíferos y reptiles exóticos vivos y describir las tendencias en la investigación, la representación taxonómica y el nivel de amenaza y la protección legal de las especies comercializadas. Las aves fueron la clase con mayor riqueza de especies y la más abundante; los reptiles fueron la segunda más abundante pero inusualmente la más estudiada en este contexto; los mamíferos fueron los menos abundantes en el mercado. Las especies de mamíferos y reptiles comercializadas como mascotas tuvieron mayor probabilidad de estar amenazadas que lo esperado por el azar. Ha habido un número sustancial de especies de mamíferos y aves criados en cautiverio y listados en el Apéndice-I y de aves y reptiles capturados en vida silvestre reportados en el mercado a CITES. Identificamos el papel emergente del Oriente Medio como conductor de la demanda de mascotas exóticas de todos los taxones junto con el bien establecido y creciente papel de América del Sur y el Sureste Asiático en el mercado. Europa, América del Norte y el Oriente Medio figuraron más en los reportes de mercado hechos a CITES, mientras que al mercado en América del Sur y el Sureste Asiático se le dio más énfasis en la literatura. Para un monitoreo efectivo de y una respuesta apropiada al mercado internacional de mascotas exóticas, es imperativo que la confiabilidad y el detalle de los reportes de mercado de CITES mejoren y que la investigación científica sea dirigida hacia esos taxones y las localidades que sean más vulnerables.","collection-number":"3","note":"DOI: 10.1111/cobi.12240","number-of-pages":"663–676","publisher":"Blackwell Publishing Inc.","title":"Global trade in exotic pets 2006-2012","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Bush","given":"Emma R."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Sandra E."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The most common avian orders in reported trade were parrots (Psittaciformes), song birds (Passeriformes), and falcons (Falconiformes) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lo7OyKbN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bush, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Bush, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32742,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SJ82QLSW"],"itemData":{"id":32742,"type":"book","abstract":"International trade in exotic pets is an important and increasing driver of biodiversity loss and often compromises the standards required for good animal welfare. We systematically reviewed the scientific and gray literature and used the United Nations Environment Programme - World Conservation Monitoring Centre (UNEP-WCMC) Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) trade database to establish temporal and geographical trade patterns of live exotic birds, mammals, and reptiles and to describe trends in research, taxonomic representation, and level of threat and legal protection of species traded. Birds were the most species-rich and abundant class reported in trade; reptiles were second most abundant but unusually the most studied in this context; and mammals were least abundant in trade. Mammalian and reptilian species traded as pets were more likely to be threatened than expected by random. There have been a substantial number of Appendix I listed captive-bred mammals and birds and wild-caught birds and reptiles reported in trade to CITES. We identified the Middle East's emerging role as a driver of demand for exotic pets of all taxa alongside the well-established and increasing role of South America and Southeast Asia in the market. Europe, North America, and the Middle East featured most heavily in trade reports to CITES, whereas trade involving South America and Southeast Asia were given most emphasis in the literature. For effective monitoring of and appropriate response to the international exotic pet trade, it is imperative that the reliability and detail of CITES trade reports improve and that scientific research be directed toward those taxa and locations that are most vulnerable. El Mercado Global de Mascotas Exóticas 2006-2012 El mercado internacional de mascotas exóticas es un factor importante y creciente de pérdida de la biodiversidad y continuamente compromete los estándares requeridos para un buen bienestar animal. Sistemáticamente revisamos la literatura científica y gris y usamos la base de datos de mercados de UNEP-WCMC CITES para establecer patrones temporales y geográficos del mercado de aves, mamíferos y reptiles exóticos vivos y describir las tendencias en la investigación, la representación taxonómica y el nivel de amenaza y la protección legal de las especies comercializadas. Las aves fueron la clase con mayor riqueza de especies y la más abundante; los reptiles fueron la segunda más abundante pero inusualmente la más estudiada en este contexto; los mamíferos fueron los menos abundantes en el mercado. Las especies de mamíferos y reptiles comercializadas como mascotas tuvieron mayor probabilidad de estar amenazadas que lo esperado por el azar. Ha habido un número sustancial de especies de mamíferos y aves criados en cautiverio y listados en el Apéndice-I y de aves y reptiles capturados en vida silvestre reportados en el mercado a CITES. Identificamos el papel emergente del Oriente Medio como conductor de la demanda de mascotas exóticas de todos los taxones junto con el bien establecido y creciente papel de América del Sur y el Sureste Asiático en el mercado. Europa, América del Norte y el Oriente Medio figuraron más en los reportes de mercado hechos a CITES, mientras que al mercado en América del Sur y el Sureste Asiático se le dio más énfasis en la literatura. Para un monitoreo efectivo de y una respuesta apropiada al mercado internacional de mascotas exóticas, es imperativo que la confiabilidad y el detalle de los reportes de mercado de CITES mejoren y que la investigación científica sea dirigida hacia esos taxones y las localidades que sean más vulnerables.","collection-number":"3","note":"DOI: 10.1111/cobi.12240","number-of-pages":"663–676","publisher":"Blackwell Publishing Inc.","title":"Global trade in exotic pets 2006-2012","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Bush","given":"Emma R."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Sandra E."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The capture of wild birds is a major source of population decline and wider environmental problems in Brazil, with about 23% of all bird species, (i.e., 295 out of 400) being hunted for pet trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jRlEsk4C","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. R. N. Alves, 2013; Fernandes-Ferreira, 2012)","plainCitation":"(R. R. N. Alves, 2013; Fernandes-Ferreira, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":704,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UCTNTDWJ"],"itemData":{"id":704,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Brazil has one of the highest avifauna diversities in the world, and many species are used by human populations in many different ways. Capturing wild birds for food, or to raise as pets, or for commercial purposes, together with the loss of habitat, have been the principal causes of population reductions among many species. The present work provides new information about the diversity of wild bird species used in Ceará State (Northeast Brazil) and the techniques used for capturing them, as well as the forces motivating this hunting, the commercial relationships involved, the maintenance of captive species, and the legal and conservation implications of the use of the regional avifauna. A total of 92 wild bird species belonging to 26 families were found to be used as food (39 spp.), for medicinal (3 spp.) and magic-religious (1 spp.) purposes, for commercial ends (46 spp.), or raised as pets (44 spp.), or killed to control their predation on domestic animals (5 spp.). Raising or selling birds is motivated by their singing and/or their natural beauty, with Thraupidae, Icteridae and Emberizidae being the principal families sought after for those purposes. The Family Columbidae has the greatest importance as a food source. Eleven traditional capture techniques (both active and passive) were identified. The commercial value of the birds varied depending on both intra- and inter-specific factors, and the trade in these animals involved both dealers who sold large numbers of individuals at low prices and specialists (“passarinheiros”) who sold just a few domesticated individuals of greater value. Our evaluations of transactions taking place in public markets indicated the probable over-exploitation of a number of species, and these were often imported from other states and countries. In addition to being illegal, the capture of wild birds can cause serious environmental problems that can only be addressed through public policies directed towards education, monitoring and control, alternative sources of income, wildlife management, and ecosystem conservation.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-011-0179-9","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"221-244","source":"Springer Link","title":"Hunting, use and conservation of birds in Northeast Brazil","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10531-011-0179-9","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Fernandes-Ferreira","given":"Hugo"},{"family":"Mendonça","given":"Sanjay Veiga"},{"family":"Albano","given":"Ciro"},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"Felipe Silva"},{"family":"Alves","given":"Rômulo Romeu Nóbrega"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",1,1]]}}},{"id":32744,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YN22VWUH"],"itemData":{"id":32744,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Brazil’s rich biological and cultural diversity makes it an exceptional location for examining the commerce in live wild birds and its implications for conservation. This paper catalogues the live bird species being traded in Brazil, characterises the trade in these animals, and discusses the implications for avian conservation. In spite of being illegal, capturing and selling birds is still a very common practice in Brazil and involves many actors who make up part of a large commercial network that distributes wild animals to every corner of the country. Our survey revealed that at least 295 bird species are illegally sold as pets in Brazil, with estimates derived from this data pointing to a total of more than 400 species - about 23% of the number of extant bird species in the country. Of the bird species recorded, two were classified as “Critically Endangered”, nine as “Endangered”, six as “Vulnerable”, and 19 as “Near Threatened” according to the most recent IUCN Red List. Most of the species recorded in this study as being widely bought and sold (including on the international market) are not listed by CITES even though many of them are in fact threatened. In light of the widespread illegal trade in wild birds in Brazil and the conservation implications for the species involved, there is an urgent need for actions that can control these activities. Steps should be taken to address the illegal traffic directly and these must include monitoring, law enforcement, effective sentencing (including deterrent sentences), targeting end-users, captive breeding, and education at all levels, taking into account the cultural, economic, social, and ecological aspects of the human populations involved.\\n         , \\n            \\n            A rica diversidade biológica e cultural brasileira faz do país um lugar excepcional para examinar o comércio de aves silvestres e suas implicações para a conservação. O presente trabalho registra as espécies sendo comercializadas no Brasil, caracteriza o comercio destes animais, e discute suas implicações para a conservação de aveifauna. Apesar de ser ilegal, a captura e o comércio de pássaros é uma prática muito comum no país e envolve muitos atores que compoem uma grande rede comercial que distribui animais silvestres para diferentes regiões. Nossa pesquisa revelou que pelo menos 295 espécies diferentes de pássaros são comercializadas ilegalmente no Brasil como animais de estimação, com estimativas apontando para um total de mais que 400 espécies – aproximadamente 23% do número total de espécies de aves nativas do país. Das espécies registradas, duas estão classificadas como “criticamente ameaçada”, nove como “ameaçada”, seis “vulnerável” e 19 como “quase ameaçada”, segundo a edição mais recente da lista vermelha da IUCN. A maioria das espécies registradas, embora amplamente comercializadas (mesmo no mercado internacional) não estão listadas pela CITES, apesar do fato que muitas consideradas ameaçadas. Considerando o disseminado comércio ilegal de aves silvestres no Brasil e as suas implicações para as espécies envolvidas, existe a necessidade urgente de controlar esta atividade. Medidas devem ser tomadas para combater o trafico ilegal de animais silvestres, incluindo fiscalização rigorosa, medidas legais, ações judiciais (incluindo condenações exemplares), medidas legais contra os compradores finais, criação em cativeiro, e medidas educativas em todos os níveis - levando em conta os aspectos culturais, econômicos, sociais e ecológicos das populações envolvidos.","container-title":"Bird Conservation International","DOI":"10.1017/S095927091200010X","ISSN":"0959-2709, 1474-0001","issue":"1","language":"en","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"53-65","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"The live bird trade in Brazil and its conservation implications: an overview","title-short":"The live bird trade in Brazil and its conservation implications","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/bird-conservation-international/article/live-bird-trade-in-brazil-and-its-conservation-implications-an-overview/31F71BEEEF88DCD9C938B333C4200DC1","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Alves","given":"Rômulo Romeu Nóbrega"},{"family":"Lima","given":"José Ribamar De Farias"},{"family":"Araujo","given":"Helder Farias P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Trade as pets also threatens sustainability of parrots in Africa and Madagascar ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wKS3MQhx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. O. Martin, 2014)","plainCitation":"(R. O. Martin, 2014)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":710,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3WPRVESF"],"itemData":{"id":710,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Parrot populations in Africa and Madagascar are declining and the need for conservation actions to address threats is increasingly recognised. Effective conservation requires a robust knowledge base on which decisions over appropriate actions can be made, yet at present there is no current and readily accessible synthesis of the status of populations, the threats they face and knowledge gaps. Here we begin to address this shortfall for the larger species in the region belonging to the genera Coracopsis, Poicephalus, Psittacus and Psittacula. We review developments since the production of the IUCN Parrot Action Plan published in 2000, identify areas where critical knowledge is lacking and highlight opportunities to address them. While advances have been made over the last decade, progress has not been evenly spread, with a strong bias towards populations in southern Africa. To date much research has focused on describing aspects of ecology and behaviour and there remains a need for studies determining the current status of populations and the factors limiting distributions and abundance. This review aims to provide a basis upon which progress towards an improved understanding of the conservation needs of the larger parrots of Africa and Madagascar can be made.","container-title":"Ostrich","DOI":"10.2989/00306525.2014.948943","ISSN":"0030-6525","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.2989/00306525.2014.948943","page":"205-233","source":"Taylor and Francis+NEJM","title":"Research and conservation of the larger parrots of Africa and Madagascar: a review of knowledge gaps and opportunities","title-short":"Research and conservation of the larger parrots of Africa and Madagascar","URL":"https://doi.org/10.2989/00306525.2014.948943","volume":"85","author":[{"family":"Martin","given":"Rowan O."},{"family":"Perrin","given":"Michael R."},{"family":"Boyes","given":"Rutledge S."},{"family":"Abebe","given":"Yilma D."},{"family":"Annorbah","given":"Nathaniel D."},{"family":"Asamoah","given":"Augustus"},{"family":"Bizimana","given":"Dieudonné"},{"family":"Bobo","given":"Kadiri S."},{"family":"Bunbury","given":"Nancy"},{"family":"Brouwer","given":"Joost"},{"family":"Diop","given":"Moussa S."},{"family":"Ewnetu","given":"Mihret"},{"family":"Fotso","given":"Roger C."},{"family":"Garteh","given":"Jerry"},{"family":"Hall","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Holbech","given":"Lars H."},{"family":"Madindou","given":"Ireene R."},{"family":"Maisels","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"Mokoko","given":"Jérôme"},{"family":"Mulwa","given":"Ronald"},{"family":"Reuleaux","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Symes","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Tamungang","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Stuart"},{"family":"Valle","given":"Simon"},{"family":"Waltert","given":"Matthias"},{"family":"Wondafrash","given":"Mengistu"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",9,2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Repeated bird surveys in Sumatra (Indonesia) have documented that trapping for pet trade depleted bird populations in the wild ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5yIiEXRA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. B. C. Harris, 2017)","plainCitation":"(J. B. C. Harris, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":720,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S3AJPA7Q"],"itemData":{"id":720,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The trade in wild animals involves one-third of the world's bird species and thousands of other vertebrate species. Although a few species are imperiled as a result of the wildlife trade, the lack of field studies makes it difficult to gauge how serious a threat it is to biodiversity. We used data on changes in bird abundances across space and time and information from trapper interviews to evaluate the effects of trapping wild birds for the pet trade in Sumatra, Indonesia. To analyze changes in bird abundance over time, we used data gathered over 14 years of repeated bird surveys in a 900-ha forest in southern Sumatra. In northern Sumatra, we surveyed birds along a gradient of trapping accessibility, from the edge of roads to 5 km into the forest interior. We interviewed 49 bird trappers in northern Sumatra to learn which species they targeted and how far they went into the forest to trap. We used prices from Sumatran bird markets as a proxy for demand and, therefore, trapping pressure. Market price was a significant predictor of species declines over time in southern Sumatra (e.g., given a market price increase of approximately $50, the log change in abundance per year decreased by 0.06 on average). This result indicates a link between the market-based pet trade and community-wide species declines. In northern Sumatra, price and change in abundance were not related to remoteness (distance from the nearest road). However, based on our field surveys, high-value species were rare or absent across this region. The median maximum distance trappers went into the forest each day was 5.0 km. This suggests that trapping has depleted bird populations across our remoteness gradient. We found that less than half of Sumatra's remaining forests are >5 km from a major road. Our results suggest that trapping for the pet trade threatens birds in Sumatra. Given the popularity of pet birds across Southeast Asia, additional studies are urgently needed to determine the extent and magnitude of the threat posed by the pet trade.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/cobi.12729","ISSN":"1523-1739","issue":"2","language":"es","license":"© 2016 Society for Conservation Biology","note":"_eprint: https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/cobi.12729","page":"394-405","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Measuring the impact of the pet trade on Indonesian birds","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/cobi.12729","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Harris","given":"J. Berton C."},{"family":"Tingley","given":"Morgan W."},{"family":"Hua","given":"Fangyuan"},{"family":"Yong","given":"Ding Li"},{"family":"Adeney","given":"J. Marion"},{"family":"Lee","given":"Tien Ming"},{"family":"Marthy","given":"William"},{"family":"Prawiradilaga","given":"Dewi M."},{"family":"Sekercioglu","given":"Cagan H."},{"family":"Suyadi","given":""},{"family":"Winarni","given":"Nurul"},{"family":"Wilcove","given":"David S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Pet trade is also an important driver for the decline of many reptile species globally, and particularly in Southeast Asia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WvaghkQe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Auliya, Altherr,, 2016; B\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}hm, 2013; Natusch & Lyons, 2012; Nijman, 2009; Nijman, Shepherd,, 2012; Nijman, Todd,, 2012; Shaney, 2017; Wakao, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Auliya, Altherr,, 2016; Böhm, 2013; Natusch & Lyons, 2012; Nijman, 2009; Nijman, Shepherd,, 2012; Nijman, Todd,, 2012; Shaney, 2017; Wakao, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":708,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9UKNGZI7"],"itemData":{"id":708,"type":"article-journal","page":"64-71","source":"ResearchGate","title":"Scaling up: the contemporary reptile pet market in Japan","title-short":"Scaling up","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Wakao","given":"Kay"},{"family":"Janssen","given":"Jordi"},{"family":"Chng","given":"Serene"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,1]]}}},{"id":32746,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R29PGGD2"],"itemData":{"id":32746,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Of the 10,272 currently recognized reptile species, the trade of fewer than 8% are regulated by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and the European Wildlife Trade Regulations (EWTR). However, the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List has assessed 45% of the world's reptile species and determined that at least 1390 species are threatened by “biological resource use”. Of these, 355 species are intentionally targeted by collectors, including 194 non-CITES-listed species. Herein we review the global reptile pet trade, its impacts, and its contribution to the over-harvesting of species and populations, in light of current international law. Findings are based on an examination of relevant professional observations, online sources, and literature (e.g., applicable policies, taxonomy [reptile database], trade statistics [EUROSTAT], and conservation status [IUCN Red List]). Case studies are presented from the following countries and regions: Australia, Central America, China, Galapagos Islands (Ecuador), Germany, Europe, India, Indonesia (Kalimantan), Islamic Republic of Iran, Japan, Madagascar, Mexico, New Zealand, the Philippines, South Africa, Sri Lanka, Vietnam, Western Africa, and Western Asia. The European Union (EU) plays a major role in reptile trade. Between 2004 and 2014 (the period under study), the EU member states officially reported the import of 20,788,747 live reptiles. This review suggests that illegal trade activities involve species regulated under CITES, as well as species that are not CITES-regulated but nationally protected in their country of origin and often openly offered for sale in the EU. Further, these case studies demonstrate that regulations and enforcement in several countries are inadequate to prevent the overexploitation of species and to halt illegal trade activities.","collection-title":"Advancing reptile conservation: Addressing knowledge gaps and mitigating key drivers of extinction risk","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2016.05.017","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"103-119","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Trade in live reptiles, its impact on wild populations, and the role of the European market","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320716301987","volume":"204","author":[{"family":"Auliya","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Altherr","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Ariano-Sanchez","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Baard","given":"Ernst H."},{"family":"Brown","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Rafe M."},{"family":"Cantu","given":"Juan-Carlos"},{"family":"Gentile","given":"Gabriele"},{"family":"Gildenhuys","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Henningheim","given":"Evert"},{"family":"Hintzmann","given":"Jürgen"},{"family":"Kanari","given":"Kahoru"},{"family":"Krvavac","given":"Milivoje"},{"family":"Lettink","given":"Marieke"},{"family":"Lippert","given":"Jörg"},{"family":"Luiselli","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Nilson","given":"Göran"},{"family":"Nguyen","given":"Truong Quang"},{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Parham","given":"James F."},{"family":"Pasachnik","given":"Stesha A."},{"family":"Pedrono","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Rauhaus","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Córdova","given":"Danny Rueda"},{"family":"Sanchez","given":"Maria-Elena"},{"family":"Schepp","given":"Ulrich"},{"family":"Schingen","given":"Mona","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Schneeweiss","given":"Norbert"},{"family":"Segniagbeto","given":"Gabriel H."},{"family":"Somaweera","given":"Ruchira"},{"family":"Sy","given":"Emerson Y."},{"family":"Türkozan","given":"Oguz"},{"family":"Vinke","given":"Sabine"},{"family":"Vinke","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Vyas","given":"Raju"},{"family":"Williamson","given":"Stuart"},{"family":"Ziegler","given":"Thomas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12,1]]}}},{"id":32808,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/235U79VG"],"itemData":{"id":32808,"type":"book","event-place":"Place of publication not identified","ISBN":"978-983-3393-29-9","language":"en","note":"OCLC: 946063173","publisher":"Traffic Southeast Asia","publisher-place":"Place of publication not identified","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Wildlife trade from asean to the eu.","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":32789,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6H7VJ46A"],"itemData":{"id":32789,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"We report on the commercial trade in three reptile species harvested for different purposes in western Indonesia (Sumatra, Java, Kalimantan) for international markets: (1) Tokay geckos, Gekko gecko, traded for medicinal uses, (2) Javan filesnakes, Acrochordus javanicus,\\nharvested for skins, and (3) Asiatic softshell turtles, Amyda cartilaginea, harvested for meat; each species is also exploited for the pet trade, but to a lesser extent. All three species are harvested from wild populations. None of these species are protected by Indonesian law, but\\nthere is a national harvest and export quota system in place to prevent overexploitation. For each species, we collected data from catchers, middlemen and exporters on harvest volumes, catching locales, turn-over and prices, and compared these figures with the quota allocated by the Indonesian\\nauthorities. The trade in G. gecko from Central and East Java (3 traders, 2006) amounts to around 1.2 million individuals annually, greatly exceeding the national quota of 50,000 G. gecko for the entire year and representing a monetary value for exporters of around one million\\nUSD / year. The annual trade in A. javanicus (in five cities in East and South Kalimantan, and North Sumatra, Riau (central Sumatra) and South Sumatra, 2005–6) was estimated at around 300,000 individuals from Kalimantan and 30,000 from Sumatra, exceeding the national quota of\\n200,000 individuals / year and representing a monetary value for exporters of at least three million USD / year. The trade in A. cartilaginea was monitored in three cities in North Sumatra and Riau in 1999: 200– 450,000 individuals were traded in 1998 and 1999, greatly exceeding\\nthe national quota of 10,000, with a monetary value for exporters in excess of ten million USD / year. We conclude that implementation of wildlife trade regulations by and large are not abided by many reptile traders and are not sufficiently enforced by the Indonesian authorities. We further\\nnote that the quota-setting process rarely involves non-detriment findings based on reliable biological information. In order for reptile trade to be sustainable in Indonesia, it is paramount that non-detriment findings are undertaken and existing regulations are sufficiently enforced.","container-title":"The Herpetological Journal","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"The Herpetological Journal","page":"83-89","source":"IngentaConnect","title":"Over-exploitation and illegal trade of reptiles in Indonesia","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"Chris R."},{"literal":"Mumpuni"},{"family":"Sanders","given":"Kate L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",4,1]]}}},{"id":707,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WCLU9SUV"],"itemData":{"id":707,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biotic evolution and environmental change in southeast Asia","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"390","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Wildlife trade as an impediment to conservation as exemplified by the trade in reptiles in Southeast Asia","volume":"82","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Todd","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"Chris R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":32810,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XIMLBAZS"],"itemData":{"id":32810,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Over-exploitation of wildlife is a signi?cant threat to global biodiversity, but addressing the sustainability of harvests can be dif?cult when trade is conducted illegally. The wildlife trade is driven chie?y by consumer demand, largely in developed nations (but increasingly in Asia), and more species are traded to meet international demand for pets than for any other purpose. We surveyed traders of amphibians and reptiles in the Indonesian provinces of Maluku, West Papua and Papua between September 2010 and April 2011. We recorded 5,370 individuals representing 52 species collected solely for the pet trade. At least 44 % were either fully protected or had not been allocated a harvest quota, making their harvest and trade illegal. Approximately half were listed within the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. Trade operates via a complex chain, with hunters receiving little income compared to middlemen and exporters. Examination of Indonesian harvest quotas for amphibians and reptiles suggests limited knowledge of species distributions, with quotas often set for species in provinces where they do not occur. Illegal trade is due, partly, to an inadequate understanding of the species being traded and is facilitated by poor monitoring and enforcement at key trade hubs. As a ?rst step to combatting illegal trade, and to better understand the effects of harvest on wild populations, we recommend the need for increased monitoring and enforcement, improving the knowledge base of species traded and educating consumers about the effects their demand for pets has on these species.","container-title":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"15","source":"Zotero","title":"Exploited for pets: the harvest and trade of amphibians and reptiles from Indonesian New Guinea","author":[{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel J D"},{"family":"Lyons","given":"Jessica A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":32786,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XCT4SDA4"],"itemData":{"id":32786,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"IUCN Red List assessments are important for conservation and management initiatives. However, status assessments are often challenging because of poor sampling between biogeographical regions. Researchers sometimes assess poorly known species, which can have unforeseen ramifications, including the trade of rare and cryptic species under common species names. Here, we address this issue in relation to economically important reptile species in Indonesia. We reviewed examples of single species categorized as Least Concern for which the assessments probably encompassed multiple closely related species. We also examined Red List assessments that utilized species distribution modelling techniques, and identified biogeography as a major barrier to using such methods. To test how biogeography may affect status assessments we used our own model lizard system from Indonesia, taking an integrative phylogeographical approach to quantify status assessments under contrasting scenarios. We show that failure to account for biogeographical breaks leads to significant variation in Red List status. Our model system fluctuates from Least Concern to Endangered, depending upon whether biogeographical boundaries are considered in taxonomic evaluations. We identify Sauria (lizards) and Serpentes (snakes) as major lineages requiring taxonomic and conservation attention in Indonesia. We also make the following recommendations: (1) Indonesia's trade quotas should further subdivide management zones to account for gaps in taxonomic evaluations; (2) genetic sampling should be considered a high priority during wildlife exportation processes from poorly studied geographical areas; and (3) continuation of thorough biological inventory is critical for conservation initiatives across heterogeneous mountain and island landscapes.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605316000351","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"4","language":"en","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"627-638","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Conservation challenges regarding species status assessments in biogeographically complex regions: examples from overexploited reptiles of Indonesia","title-short":"Conservation challenges regarding species status assessments in biogeographically complex regions","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/conservation-challenges-regarding-species-status-assessments-in-biogeographically-complex-regions-examples-from-overexploited-reptiles-of-indonesia/092CD2B5C4134C892358BD5DB9278314","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Shaney","given":"Kyle J."},{"family":"Wostl","given":"Elijah"},{"family":"Hamidy","given":"Amir"},{"family":"Kurniawan","given":"Nia"},{"family":"Harvey","given":"Michael B."},{"family":"Smith","given":"Eric N."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",10]]}}},{"id":885,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4AILJGGY"],"itemData":{"id":885,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Effective and targeted conservation action requires detailed information about species, their distribution, systematics and ecology as well as the distribution of threat processes which affect them. Knowledge of reptilian diversity remains surprisingly disparate, and innovative means of gaining rapid insight into the status of reptiles are needed in order to highlight urgent conservation cases and inform environmental policy with appropriate biodiversity information in a timely manner. We present the first ever global analysis of extinction risk in reptiles, based on a random representative sample of 1500 species (16% of all currently known species). To our knowledge, our results provide the first analysis of the global conservation status and distribution patterns of reptiles and the threats affecting them, highlighting conservation priorities and knowledge gaps which need to be addressed urgently to ensure the continued survival of the world’s reptiles. Nearly one in five reptilian species are threatened with extinction, with another one in five species classed as Data Deficient. The proportion of threatened reptile species is highest in freshwater environments, tropical regions and on oceanic islands, while data deficiency was highest in tropical areas, such as Central Africa and Southeast Asia, and among fossorial reptiles. Our results emphasise the need for research attention to be focussed on tropical areas which are experiencing the most dramatic rates of habitat loss, on fossorial reptiles for which there is a chronic lack of data, and on certain taxa such as snakes for which extinction risk may currently be underestimated due to lack of population information. Conservation actions specifically need to mitigate the effects of human-induced habitat loss and harvesting, which are the predominant threats to reptiles.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.07.015","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"372-385","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The conservation status of the world’s reptiles","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320712003357","volume":"157","author":[{"family":"Böhm","given":"Monika"},{"family":"Collen","given":"Ben"},{"family":"Baillie","given":"Jonathan E. M."},{"family":"Bowles","given":"Philip"},{"family":"Chanson","given":"Janice"},{"family":"Cox","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Hammerson","given":"Geoffrey"},{"family":"Hoffmann","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Livingstone","given":"Suzanne R."},{"family":"Ram","given":"Mala"},{"family":"Rhodin","given":"Anders G. J."},{"family":"Stuart","given":"Simon N."},{"family":"Dijk","given":"Peter Paul","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Young","given":"Bruce E."},{"family":"Afuang","given":"Leticia E."},{"family":"Aghasyan","given":"Aram"},{"family":"García","given":"Andrés"},{"family":"Aguilar","given":"César"},{"family":"Ajtic","given":"Rastko"},{"family":"Akarsu","given":"Ferdi"},{"family":"Alencar","given":"Laura R. V."},{"family":"Allison","given":"Allen"},{"family":"Ananjeva","given":"Natalia"},{"family":"Anderson","given":"Steve"},{"family":"Andrén","given":"Claes"},{"family":"Ariano-Sánchez","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Arredondo","given":"Juan Camilo"},{"family":"Auliya","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Austin","given":"Christopher C."},{"family":"Avci","given":"Aziz"},{"family":"Baker","given":"Patrick J."},{"family":"Barreto-Lima","given":"André F."},{"family":"Barrio-Amorós","given":"César L."},{"family":"Basu","given":"Dhruvayothi"},{"family":"Bates","given":"Michael F."},{"family":"Batistella","given":"Alexandre"},{"family":"Bauer","given":"Aaron"},{"family":"Bennett","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Böhme","given":"Wolfgang"},{"family":"Broadley","given":"Don"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Rafe"},{"family":"Burgess","given":"Joseph"},{"family":"Captain","given":"Ashok"},{"family":"Carreira","given":"Santiago"},{"family":"Castañeda","given":"Maria del Rosario"},{"family":"Castro","given":"Fernando"},{"family":"Catenazzi","given":"Alessandro"},{"family":"Cedeño-Vázquez","given":"José R."},{"family":"Chapple","given":"David G."},{"family":"Cheylan","given":"Marc"},{"family":"Cisneros-Heredia","given":"Diego F."},{"family":"Cogalniceanu","given":"Dan"},{"family":"Cogger","given":"Hal"},{"family":"Corti","given":"Claudia"},{"family":"Costa","given":"Gabriel C."},{"family":"Couper","given":"Patrick J."},{"family":"Courtney","given":"Tony"},{"family":"Crnobrnja-Isailovic","given":"Jelka"},{"family":"Crochet","given":"Pierre-André"},{"family":"Crother","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Cruz","given":"Felix"},{"family":"Daltry","given":"Jennifer C."},{"family":"Daniels","given":"R. J. Ranjit"},{"family":"Das","given":"Indraneil"},{"family":"Silva","given":"Anslem","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Diesmos","given":"Arvin C."},{"family":"Dirksen","given":"Lutz"},{"family":"Doan","given":"Tiffany M."},{"family":"Dodd","given":"C. Kenneth"},{"family":"Doody","given":"J. Sean"},{"family":"Dorcas","given":"Michael E."},{"family":"Duarte de Barros Filho","given":"Jose"},{"family":"Egan","given":"Vincent T."},{"family":"El Mouden","given":"El Hassan"},{"family":"Embert","given":"Dirk"},{"family":"Espinoza","given":"Robert E."},{"family":"Fallabrino","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Feng","given":"Xie"},{"family":"Feng","given":"Zhao-Jun"},{"family":"Fitzgerald","given":"Lee"},{"family":"Flores-Villela","given":"Oscar"},{"family":"França","given":"Frederico G. R."},{"family":"Frost","given":"Darrell"},{"family":"Gadsden","given":"Hector"},{"family":"Gamble","given":"Tony"},{"family":"Ganesh","given":"S. R."},{"family":"Garcia","given":"Miguel A."},{"family":"García-Pérez","given":"Juan E."},{"family":"Gatus","given":"Joey"},{"family":"Gaulke","given":"Maren"},{"family":"Geniez","given":"Philippe"},{"family":"Georges","given":"Arthur"},{"family":"Gerlach","given":"Justin"},{"family":"Goldberg","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Gonzalez","given":"Juan-Carlos T."},{"family":"Gower","given":"David J."},{"family":"Grant","given":"Tandora"},{"family":"Greenbaum","given":"Eli"},{"family":"Grieco","given":"Cristina"},{"family":"Guo","given":"Peng"},{"family":"Hamilton","given":"Alison M."},{"family":"Hare","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Hedges","given":"S. Blair"},{"family":"Heideman","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Hilton-Taylor","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Hitchmough","given":"Rod"},{"family":"Hollingsworth","given":"Bradford"},{"family":"Hutchinson","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Ineich","given":"Ivan"},{"family":"Iverson","given":"John"},{"family":"Jaksic","given":"Fabian M."},{"family":"Jenkins","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Joger","given":"Ulrich"},{"family":"Jose","given":"Reizl"},{"family":"Kaska","given":"Yakup"},{"family":"Kaya","given":"U?ur"},{"family":"Keogh","given":"J. Scott"},{"family":"Köhler","given":"Gunther"},{"family":"Kuchling","given":"Gerald"},{"family":"Kumluta?","given":"Yusuf"},{"family":"Kwet","given":"Axel"},{"family":"La Marca","given":"Enrique"},{"family":"Lamar","given":"William"},{"family":"Lane","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Lardner","given":"Bjorn"},{"family":"Latta","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Latta","given":"Gabrielle"},{"family":"Lau","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Lavin","given":"Pablo"},{"family":"Lawson","given":"Dwight"},{"family":"LeBreton","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Lehr","given":"Edgar"},{"family":"Limpus","given":"Duncan"},{"family":"Lipczynski","given":"Nicola"},{"family":"Lobo","given":"Aaron S."},{"family":"López-Luna","given":"Marco A."},{"family":"Luiselli","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Lukoschek","given":"Vimoksalehi"},{"family":"Lundberg","given":"Mikael"},{"family":"Lymberakis","given":"Petros"},{"family":"Macey","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Magnusson","given":"William E."},{"family":"Mahler","given":"D. Luke"},{"family":"Malhotra","given":"Anita"},{"family":"Mariaux","given":"Jean"},{"family":"Maritz","given":"Bryan"},{"family":"Marques","given":"Otavio A. V."},{"family":"Márquez","given":"Rafael"},{"family":"Martins","given":"Marcio"},{"family":"Masterson","given":"Gavin"},{"family":"Mateo","given":"José A."},{"family":"Mathew","given":"Rosamma"},{"family":"Mathews","given":"Nixon"},{"family":"Mayer","given":"Gregory"},{"family":"McCranie","given":"James R."},{"family":"Measey","given":"G. John"},{"family":"Mendoza-Quijano","given":"Fernando"},{"family":"Menegon","given":"Michele"},{"family":"Métrailler","given":"Sébastien"},{"family":"Milton","given":"David A."},{"family":"Montgomery","given":"Chad"},{"family":"Morato","given":"Sérgio A. A."},{"family":"Mott","given":"Tami"},{"family":"Muñoz-Alonso","given":"Antonio"},{"family":"Murphy","given":"John"},{"family":"Nguyen","given":"Truong Q."},{"family":"Nilson","given":"Göran"},{"family":"Nogueira","given":"Cristiano"},{"family":"Núñez","given":"Herman"},{"family":"Orlov","given":"Nikolai"},{"family":"Ota","given":"Hidetoshi"},{"family":"Ottenwalder","given":"José"},{"family":"Papenfuss","given":"Theodore"},{"family":"Pasachnik","given":"Stesha"},{"family":"Passos","given":"Paulo"},{"family":"Pauwels","given":"Olivier S. G."},{"family":"Pérez-Buitrago","given":"Néstor"},{"family":"Pérez-Mellado","given":"Valentín"},{"family":"Pianka","given":"Eric R."},{"family":"Pleguezuelos","given":"Juan"},{"family":"Pollock","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Ponce-Campos","given":"Paulino"},{"family":"Powell","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Pupin","given":"Fabio"},{"family":"Quintero Díaz","given":"Gustavo E."},{"family":"Radder","given":"Raju"},{"family":"Ramer","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Rasmussen","given":"Arne R."},{"family":"Raxworthy","given":"Chris"},{"family":"Reynolds","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Richman","given":"Nadia"},{"family":"Rico","given":"Edmund L."},{"family":"Riservato","given":"Elisa"},{"family":"Rivas","given":"Gilson"},{"family":"Rocha","given":"Pedro L. B.","non-dropping-particle":"da"},{"family":"Rödel","given":"Mark-Oliver"},{"family":"Rodríguez Schettino","given":"Lourdes"},{"family":"Roosenburg","given":"Willem M."},{"family":"Ross","given":"James P."},{"family":"Sadek","given":"Riyad"},{"family":"Sanders","given":"Kate"},{"family":"Santos-Barrera","given":"Georgina"},{"family":"Schleich","given":"Hermann H."},{"family":"Schmidt","given":"Benedikt R."},{"family":"Schmitz","given":"Andreas"},{"family":"Sharifi","given":"Mozafar"},{"family":"Shea","given":"Glenn"},{"family":"Shi","given":"Hai-Tao"},{"family":"Shine","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Sindaco","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Slimani","given":"Tahar"},{"family":"Somaweera","given":"Ruchira"},{"family":"Spawls","given":"Steve"},{"family":"Stafford","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Stuebing","given":"Rob"},{"family":"Sweet","given":"Sam"},{"family":"Sy","given":"Emerson"},{"family":"Temple","given":"Helen J."},{"family":"Tognelli","given":"Marcelo F."},{"family":"Tolley","given":"Krystal"},{"family":"Tolson","given":"Peter J."},{"family":"Tuniyev","given":"Boris"},{"family":"Tuniyev","given":"Sako"},{"family":"Üzüm","given":"Nazan"},{"family":"Buurt","given":"Gerard","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Van Sluys","given":"Monique"},{"family":"Velasco","given":"Alvaro"},{"family":"Vences","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Veselý","given":"Milan"},{"family":"Vinke","given":"Sabine"},{"family":"Vinke","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Vogel","given":"Gernot"},{"family":"Vogrin","given":"Milan"},{"family":"Vogt","given":"Richard C."},{"family":"Wearn","given":"Oliver R."},{"family":"Werner","given":"Yehudah L."},{"family":"Whiting","given":"Martin J."},{"family":"Wiewandt","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Wilkinson","given":"John"},{"family":"Wilson","given":"Byron"},{"family":"Wren","given":"Sally"},{"family":"Zamin","given":"Tara"},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Kaiya"},{"family":"Zug","given":"George"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 35 % of all reptiles are traded online, which is very hard to regulate  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gwD7bS7t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Marshall, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Marshall, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32346,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FLRDMTZ6"],"itemData":{"id":32346,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife trade is a key driver of the biodiversity crisis. Unregulated, or under-regulated wildlife trade can lead to unsustainable exploitation of wild populations. International efforts to regulate wildlife mostly miss ‘lower-value’ species, such as those imported as pets, resulting in limited knowledge of trade in groups like reptiles. Here we generate a dataset on web-based private commercial trade of reptiles to highlight the scope of the global reptile trade. We find that over 35% of reptile species are traded online. Three quarters of this trade is in species that are not covered by international trade regulation. These species include numerous endangered or range-restricted species, especially hotspots within Asia. Approximately 90% of traded reptile species and half of traded individuals are captured from the wild. Exploitation can occur immediately after scientific description, leaving new endemic species especially vulnerable. Pronounced gaps in regulation imply trade is having unknown impacts on numerous threatened species. Gaps in monitoring demand a reconsideration of international reptile trade regulations. We suggest reversing the status-quo, requiring proof of sustainability before trade is permitted.","container-title":"Nature Communications","DOI":"10.1038/s41467-020-18523-4","ISSN":"2041-1723","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Commun","language":"en","license":"2020 The Author(s)","note":"Bandiera_abtest: a\\nCc_license_type: cc_by\\nCg_type: Nature Research Journals\\nnumber: 1\\nPrimary_atype: Research\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group\\nSubject_term: Biodiversity;Conservation biology;Herpetology\\nSubject_term_id: biodiversity;conservation;herpetology","page":"4738","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Thousands of reptile species threatened by under-regulated global trade","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-020-18523-4","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Marshall","given":"Benjamin M."},{"family":"Strine","given":"Colin"},{"family":"Hughes","given":"Alice C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This includes many endangered or endemic species with over 90% species and half of traded individuals taken from the wild. Especially the European Union plays a major role in reptile trade, having imported officially more than 20 million live reptiles between 2004 and 2014 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"m5jFJuIN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Auliya, Altherr,, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Auliya, Altherr,, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32746,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R29PGGD2"],"itemData":{"id":32746,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Of the 10,272 currently recognized reptile species, the trade of fewer than 8% are regulated by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and the European Wildlife Trade Regulations (EWTR). However, the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List has assessed 45% of the world's reptile species and determined that at least 1390 species are threatened by “biological resource use”. Of these, 355 species are intentionally targeted by collectors, including 194 non-CITES-listed species. Herein we review the global reptile pet trade, its impacts, and its contribution to the over-harvesting of species and populations, in light of current international law. Findings are based on an examination of relevant professional observations, online sources, and literature (e.g., applicable policies, taxonomy [reptile database], trade statistics [EUROSTAT], and conservation status [IUCN Red List]). Case studies are presented from the following countries and regions: Australia, Central America, China, Galapagos Islands (Ecuador), Germany, Europe, India, Indonesia (Kalimantan), Islamic Republic of Iran, Japan, Madagascar, Mexico, New Zealand, the Philippines, South Africa, Sri Lanka, Vietnam, Western Africa, and Western Asia. The European Union (EU) plays a major role in reptile trade. Between 2004 and 2014 (the period under study), the EU member states officially reported the import of 20,788,747 live reptiles. This review suggests that illegal trade activities involve species regulated under CITES, as well as species that are not CITES-regulated but nationally protected in their country of origin and often openly offered for sale in the EU. Further, these case studies demonstrate that regulations and enforcement in several countries are inadequate to prevent the overexploitation of species and to halt illegal trade activities.","collection-title":"Advancing reptile conservation: Addressing knowledge gaps and mitigating key drivers of extinction risk","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2016.05.017","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"103-119","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Trade in live reptiles, its impact on wild populations, and the role of the European market","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320716301987","volume":"204","author":[{"family":"Auliya","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Altherr","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Ariano-Sanchez","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Baard","given":"Ernst H."},{"family":"Brown","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Rafe M."},{"family":"Cantu","given":"Juan-Carlos"},{"family":"Gentile","given":"Gabriele"},{"family":"Gildenhuys","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Henningheim","given":"Evert"},{"family":"Hintzmann","given":"Jürgen"},{"family":"Kanari","given":"Kahoru"},{"family":"Krvavac","given":"Milivoje"},{"family":"Lettink","given":"Marieke"},{"family":"Lippert","given":"Jörg"},{"family":"Luiselli","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Nilson","given":"Göran"},{"family":"Nguyen","given":"Truong Quang"},{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Parham","given":"James F."},{"family":"Pasachnik","given":"Stesha A."},{"family":"Pedrono","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Rauhaus","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Córdova","given":"Danny Rueda"},{"family":"Sanchez","given":"Maria-Elena"},{"family":"Schepp","given":"Ulrich"},{"family":"Schingen","given":"Mona","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Schneeweiss","given":"Norbert"},{"family":"Segniagbeto","given":"Gabriel H."},{"family":"Somaweera","given":"Ruchira"},{"family":"Sy","given":"Emerson Y."},{"family":"Türkozan","given":"Oguz"},{"family":"Vinke","given":"Sabine"},{"family":"Vinke","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Vyas","given":"Raju"},{"family":"Williamson","given":"Stuart"},{"family":"Ziegler","given":"Thomas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Reptile trade threatens wild populations and effective control is hampered by ineffective regulation and monitoring ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qXeohWLo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Auliya, Altherr,, 2016; Auliya, Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a-Moreno,, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Auliya, Altherr,, 2016; Auliya, García-Moreno,, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32746,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R29PGGD2"],"itemData":{"id":32746,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Of the 10,272 currently recognized reptile species, the trade of fewer than 8% are regulated by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) and the European Wildlife Trade Regulations (EWTR). However, the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List has assessed 45% of the world's reptile species and determined that at least 1390 species are threatened by “biological resource use”. Of these, 355 species are intentionally targeted by collectors, including 194 non-CITES-listed species. Herein we review the global reptile pet trade, its impacts, and its contribution to the over-harvesting of species and populations, in light of current international law. Findings are based on an examination of relevant professional observations, online sources, and literature (e.g., applicable policies, taxonomy [reptile database], trade statistics [EUROSTAT], and conservation status [IUCN Red List]). Case studies are presented from the following countries and regions: Australia, Central America, China, Galapagos Islands (Ecuador), Germany, Europe, India, Indonesia (Kalimantan), Islamic Republic of Iran, Japan, Madagascar, Mexico, New Zealand, the Philippines, South Africa, Sri Lanka, Vietnam, Western Africa, and Western Asia. The European Union (EU) plays a major role in reptile trade. Between 2004 and 2014 (the period under study), the EU member states officially reported the import of 20,788,747 live reptiles. This review suggests that illegal trade activities involve species regulated under CITES, as well as species that are not CITES-regulated but nationally protected in their country of origin and often openly offered for sale in the EU. Further, these case studies demonstrate that regulations and enforcement in several countries are inadequate to prevent the overexploitation of species and to halt illegal trade activities.","collection-title":"Advancing reptile conservation: Addressing knowledge gaps and mitigating key drivers of extinction risk","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2016.05.017","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"103-119","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Trade in live reptiles, its impact on wild populations, and the role of the European market","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320716301987","volume":"204","author":[{"family":"Auliya","given":"Mark"},{"family":"Altherr","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Ariano-Sanchez","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Baard","given":"Ernst H."},{"family":"Brown","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Brown","given":"Rafe M."},{"family":"Cantu","given":"Juan-Carlos"},{"family":"Gentile","given":"Gabriele"},{"family":"Gildenhuys","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Henningheim","given":"Evert"},{"family":"Hintzmann","given":"Jürgen"},{"family":"Kanari","given":"Kahoru"},{"family":"Krvavac","given":"Milivoje"},{"family":"Lettink","given":"Marieke"},{"family":"Lippert","given":"Jörg"},{"family":"Luiselli","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Nilson","given":"Göran"},{"family":"Nguyen","given":"Truong Quang"},{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Parham","given":"James F."},{"family":"Pasachnik","given":"Stesha A."},{"family":"Pedrono","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Rauhaus","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Córdova","given":"Danny Rueda"},{"family":"Sanchez","given":"Maria-Elena"},{"family":"Schepp","given":"Ulrich"},{"family":"Schingen","given":"Mona","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Schneeweiss","given":"Norbert"},{"family":"Segniagbeto","given":"Gabriel H."},{"family":"Somaweera","given":"Ruchira"},{"family":"Sy","given":"Emerson Y."},{"family":"Türkozan","given":"Oguz"},{"family":"Vinke","given":"Sabine"},{"family":"Vinke","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Vyas","given":"Raju"},{"family":"Williamson","given":"Stuart"},{"family":"Ziegler","given":"Thomas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12,1]]}}},{"id":32745,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GXLR29RC"],"itemData":{"id":32745,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and conservation","issue":"13","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"2581–2595","source":"Google Scholar","title":"The global amphibian trade flows through Europe: the need for enforcing and improving legislation","title-short":"The global amphibian trade flows through Europe","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Auliya","given":"Mark"},{"family":"García-Moreno","given":"Jaime"},{"family":"Schmidt","given":"Benedikt R."},{"family":"Schmeller","given":"Dirk S."},{"family":"Hoogmoed","given":"Marinus S."},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Matthew C."},{"family":"Pasmans","given":"Frank"},{"family":"Henle","given":"Klaus"},{"family":"Bickford","given":"David"},{"family":"Martel","given":"An"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A survey in the Indonesian provinces of Maluku, West Papua and Papua, documented that at least 44 % of amphibians and reptiles were traded illegaly ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5rGHeRQG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Natusch & Lyons, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Natusch & Lyons, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32810,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XIMLBAZS"],"itemData":{"id":32810,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Over-exploitation of wildlife is a signi?cant threat to global biodiversity, but addressing the sustainability of harvests can be dif?cult when trade is conducted illegally. The wildlife trade is driven chie?y by consumer demand, largely in developed nations (but increasingly in Asia), and more species are traded to meet international demand for pets than for any other purpose. We surveyed traders of amphibians and reptiles in the Indonesian provinces of Maluku, West Papua and Papua between September 2010 and April 2011. We recorded 5,370 individuals representing 52 species collected solely for the pet trade. At least 44 % were either fully protected or had not been allocated a harvest quota, making their harvest and trade illegal. Approximately half were listed within the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. Trade operates via a complex chain, with hunters receiving little income compared to middlemen and exporters. Examination of Indonesian harvest quotas for amphibians and reptiles suggests limited knowledge of species distributions, with quotas often set for species in provinces where they do not occur. Illegal trade is due, partly, to an inadequate understanding of the species being traded and is facilitated by poor monitoring and enforcement at key trade hubs. As a ?rst step to combatting illegal trade, and to better understand the effects of harvest on wild populations, we recommend the need for increased monitoring and enforcement, improving the knowledge base of species traded and educating consumers about the effects their demand for pets has on these species.","container-title":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"15","source":"Zotero","title":"Exploited for pets: the harvest and trade of amphibians and reptiles from Indonesian New Guinea","author":[{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel J D"},{"family":"Lyons","given":"Jessica A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Inability to identify species correctly, weak governance and harvesters being economically vulnerable (receiving little income compared to middlemen and exporters) are key obstacles towards sustainable use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bSyfevkf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Natusch & Lyons, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Natusch & Lyons, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32810,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XIMLBAZS"],"itemData":{"id":32810,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Over-exploitation of wildlife is a signi?cant threat to global biodiversity, but addressing the sustainability of harvests can be dif?cult when trade is conducted illegally. The wildlife trade is driven chie?y by consumer demand, largely in developed nations (but increasingly in Asia), and more species are traded to meet international demand for pets than for any other purpose. We surveyed traders of amphibians and reptiles in the Indonesian provinces of Maluku, West Papua and Papua between September 2010 and April 2011. We recorded 5,370 individuals representing 52 species collected solely for the pet trade. At least 44 % were either fully protected or had not been allocated a harvest quota, making their harvest and trade illegal. Approximately half were listed within the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. Trade operates via a complex chain, with hunters receiving little income compared to middlemen and exporters. Examination of Indonesian harvest quotas for amphibians and reptiles suggests limited knowledge of species distributions, with quotas often set for species in provinces where they do not occur. Illegal trade is due, partly, to an inadequate understanding of the species being traded and is facilitated by poor monitoring and enforcement at key trade hubs. As a ?rst step to combatting illegal trade, and to better understand the effects of harvest on wild populations, we recommend the need for increased monitoring and enforcement, improving the knowledge base of species traded and educating consumers about the effects their demand for pets has on these species.","container-title":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"15","source":"Zotero","title":"Exploited for pets: the harvest and trade of amphibians and reptiles from Indonesian New Guinea","author":[{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel J D"},{"family":"Lyons","given":"Jessica A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Pet trade has, next to habitat destruction, been a major driver threatening the turquoise dwarf gecko (Lygodactylus williamsi) that is endemic to two small forests in eastern Tanzania ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"L8K93SUt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Flecks, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Flecks, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":715,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KDI9VGXU"],"itemData":{"id":715,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Salamandra","issue":"1","page":"12–20","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Watching extinction happen: the dramatic population decline of the critically endangered Tanzanian Turquoise Dwarf Gecko, Lygodactylus williamsi","title-short":"Watching extinction happen","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Flecks","given":"Morris"},{"family":"Weinsheimer","given":"Frank"},{"family":"Böhme","given":"Wolfgang"},{"family":"Chenga","given":"Jumapili"},{"family":"Lötters","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Rödder","given":"Dennis"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, many turtles are traded as pets, potentially causing population declines in their natural habitat ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MqNAeezc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bush, 2014; C. P. Ceballos & Fitzgerald, 2004; Lyons, 2013; Nijman & Shepherd, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Bush, 2014; C. P. Ceballos & Fitzgerald, 2004; Lyons, 2013; Nijman & Shepherd, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":742,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HB4S8BP4"],"itemData":{"id":742,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"There are growing concerns among conservationists that commercial trade in many species of wild-caught turtles may not be sustainable. Little information on the turtle trade and its impact on populations is available at the national level, and there are no analyses of the commercial trade in turtles in Texas. To quantitatively describe the turtle trade in Texas, we surveyed government organizations, pet and meat establishments, reptile expositions, zoos, aquariums, and Texas herpetological societies by mail, phone calls, or visits. We used the United States Fish and Wildlife Service's (USFWS) Law Enforcement Management Information System (LEMIS) database to quantify the turtle trade in Texas from 1995–2000. Five turtle species accounted for 99% of collections from the wild in 1999, but at least 69 native and exotic species were available in the pet and meat markets. Nonnative species were exported from Texas, implying that they were either imported from other states and re-exported from Texas or were bred in captivity. Unfortunately, the current national wildlife trade monitoring system does not distinguish between export and re-export, making it difficult to fully understand wildlife trade patterns. Turtle populations in general were susceptible to overexploitation. We recommend that populations of commercially exploited turtles be monitored to build databases required for establishing harvest quotas and size limits. Methods used in this study could be implemented by other states to monitor their turtle trade.","container-title":"Wildlife Society Bulletin","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.2193/0091-7648(2004)032[0881:TTINAE]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"1938-5463","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://wildlife.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.2193/0091-7648%282004%29032%5B0881%3ATTINAE%5D2.0.CO%3B2","page":"881-892","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The trade in native and exotic turtles in Texas","URL":"https://wildlife.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.2193/0091-7648%282004%29032%5B0881%3ATTINAE%5D2.0.CO%3B2","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Ceballos","given":"Claudia P."},{"family":"Fitzgerald","given":"Lee A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":32742,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SJ82QLSW"],"itemData":{"id":32742,"type":"book","abstract":"International trade in exotic pets is an important and increasing driver of biodiversity loss and often compromises the standards required for good animal welfare. We systematically reviewed the scientific and gray literature and used the United Nations Environment Programme - World Conservation Monitoring Centre (UNEP-WCMC) Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) trade database to establish temporal and geographical trade patterns of live exotic birds, mammals, and reptiles and to describe trends in research, taxonomic representation, and level of threat and legal protection of species traded. Birds were the most species-rich and abundant class reported in trade; reptiles were second most abundant but unusually the most studied in this context; and mammals were least abundant in trade. Mammalian and reptilian species traded as pets were more likely to be threatened than expected by random. There have been a substantial number of Appendix I listed captive-bred mammals and birds and wild-caught birds and reptiles reported in trade to CITES. We identified the Middle East's emerging role as a driver of demand for exotic pets of all taxa alongside the well-established and increasing role of South America and Southeast Asia in the market. Europe, North America, and the Middle East featured most heavily in trade reports to CITES, whereas trade involving South America and Southeast Asia were given most emphasis in the literature. For effective monitoring of and appropriate response to the international exotic pet trade, it is imperative that the reliability and detail of CITES trade reports improve and that scientific research be directed toward those taxa and locations that are most vulnerable. El Mercado Global de Mascotas Exóticas 2006-2012 El mercado internacional de mascotas exóticas es un factor importante y creciente de pérdida de la biodiversidad y continuamente compromete los estándares requeridos para un buen bienestar animal. Sistemáticamente revisamos la literatura científica y gris y usamos la base de datos de mercados de UNEP-WCMC CITES para establecer patrones temporales y geográficos del mercado de aves, mamíferos y reptiles exóticos vivos y describir las tendencias en la investigación, la representación taxonómica y el nivel de amenaza y la protección legal de las especies comercializadas. Las aves fueron la clase con mayor riqueza de especies y la más abundante; los reptiles fueron la segunda más abundante pero inusualmente la más estudiada en este contexto; los mamíferos fueron los menos abundantes en el mercado. Las especies de mamíferos y reptiles comercializadas como mascotas tuvieron mayor probabilidad de estar amenazadas que lo esperado por el azar. Ha habido un número sustancial de especies de mamíferos y aves criados en cautiverio y listados en el Apéndice-I y de aves y reptiles capturados en vida silvestre reportados en el mercado a CITES. Identificamos el papel emergente del Oriente Medio como conductor de la demanda de mascotas exóticas de todos los taxones junto con el bien establecido y creciente papel de América del Sur y el Sureste Asiático en el mercado. Europa, América del Norte y el Oriente Medio figuraron más en los reportes de mercado hechos a CITES, mientras que al mercado en América del Sur y el Sureste Asiático se le dio más énfasis en la literatura. Para un monitoreo efectivo de y una respuesta apropiada al mercado internacional de mascotas exóticas, es imperativo que la confiabilidad y el detalle de los reportes de mercado de CITES mejoren y que la investigación científica sea dirigida hacia esos taxones y las localidades que sean más vulnerables.","collection-number":"3","note":"DOI: 10.1111/cobi.12240","number-of-pages":"663–676","publisher":"Blackwell Publishing Inc.","title":"Global trade in exotic pets 2006-2012","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Bush","given":"Emma R."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Sandra E."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":32807,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4TSZFH6I"],"itemData":{"id":32807,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-014-0809-0","ISSN":"0960-3115, 1572-9710","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"309-318","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Analysis of a decade of trade of tortoises and freshwater turtles in Bangkok, Thailand","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10531-014-0809-0","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"Chris R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",2]]}}},{"id":717,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JWV84LRR"],"itemData":{"id":717,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The international trade in wildlife is currently one of the greatest threats to biodiversity. One group, the turtles, has experienced far-reaching population declines and extinctions because the majority of animals are sourced from the wild. The island of New Guinea has the highest diversity of freshwater turtles (Chelidae) in the Australasian region and large numbers are harvested from the Indonesian province of Papua to supply the international pet trade. A total of 264 of these turtles representing six species were recorded between December 2010 and March 2011 while gathering information about wildlife trade in the Indonesian province of Papua. Most were juveniles, although a substantial number of large adults were also harvested. None of the species recorded are CITES-listed. Despite one species not being allocated an annual harvest quota, it was observed being traded. Illegal and unregulated trade coupled with a lack of basic ecological data for these species can have a severe impact on wild populations. We present recommendations for law enforcement and conservation of these species.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605312000932","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"2","language":"en","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"298-302","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"The harvest of freshwater turtles (Chelidae) from Papua, Indonesia, for the international pet trade","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/harvest-of-freshwater-turtles-chelidae-from-papua-indonesia-for-the-international-pet-trade/7E5E46F638EAD4B2BF7315A7B6E1C320","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Lyons","given":"Jessica Ann"},{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel James Deans"},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"Chris R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In particular, Asian turtles are also being kept as pets, in addition to being collected or farmed for food and traditional Chinese medicine and hence have been reported to be heavily exploited and threatened ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Opr3zPea","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. M. Cheung & Dudgeon, 2006; Nijman & Shepherd, 2015)","plainCitation":"(S. M. Cheung & Dudgeon, 2006; Nijman & Shepherd, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32747,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XXNII25G"],"itemData":{"id":32747,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"1. A total of 950 251 individuals of 157 turtle species were recorded during a 35-month survey of the turtle trade in Hong Kong, Shenzhen and Guangzhou, southern China. All but two of the 157 species were encountered in Hong Kong; Guangzhou ranked second in diversity (113 species) and Shenzhen third (89 species). Together, these turtles made up around 60% of the global chelonian fauna; 124 (?80%) of them were freshwater turtles. 2. Seventy-two globally threatened species were traded in southern China during the survey: 13 classified by the IUCN as critically endangered (CE), 29 as endangered (EN), and 30 as vulnerable (VU). Thirteen species listed on CITES Appendix I and 64 species on Appendix II, as well as eight species nationally protected in China, were traded. 3. The majority of species traded had natural ranges that included China and neighbouring Southeast Asian countries, or Southeast Asian countries other than China. These non-Chinese Asian turtles (primarily Bataguridae) constituted around two-thirds of the 77 species in the food trade, and turtles sold as food accounted for 73% of individuals encountered during the survey. Most species sold as food were also traded as traditional Chinese medicine, and nearly all turtles (155 of 157 species) were sold as pets. Eighty-one species were traded only as pets. 4. Large numbers of Cuora galbinifrons (CE; CITES-II) were traded (>15 000 individuals) and even greater quantities (>210 000 individuals) of C. amboinensis (VU; CITES-II), as were significant numbers of other CR, EN and VU batagurids. Observed levels of exploitation of wild populations appeared unsustainable. 5. Enforcement of relevant CITES regulations during the survey seemed limited and globally threatened Asian species remained in trade in Hong Kong without the relevant licences. Trade within China is not subject to CITES, but could be regulated by enforcement of existing national laws and expansion of protected-species lists. Copyright © 2006 John Wiley & Sons, Ltd.","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.803","ISSN":"1099-0755","issue":"7","language":"fr","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1002/aqc.803","page":"751-770","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Quantifying the Asian turtle crisis: market surveys in southern China, 2000–2003","title-short":"Quantifying the Asian turtle crisis","URL":"http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/aqc.803","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Cheung","given":"Sze Man"},{"family":"Dudgeon","given":"David"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":32807,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4TSZFH6I"],"itemData":{"id":32807,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-014-0809-0","ISSN":"0960-3115, 1572-9710","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"309-318","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Analysis of a decade of trade of tortoises and freshwater turtles in Bangkok, Thailand","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10531-014-0809-0","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Shepherd","given":"Chris R."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Primate species have also been threatened by the pet trade around the world  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NYrN0GqH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ni, 2018; Norconk, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Ni, 2018; Norconk, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32776,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/97FCEZJI"],"itemData":{"id":32776,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This commentary emerged from a panel presentation at the International Primatological Society Congress in Nairobi, Kenya, 2018. The goal was to provide regional updates on the status of primate removal from habitat countries, especially for the pet trade, and develop guidelines that could help primatologists address this critical problem. The trade in live primates includes those used as pets, in entertainment, and as subjects of biomedical experimentation, but here we focus on those primates destined for the pet trade. Such transactions are a hugely lucrative business, impacting hundreds of thousands of individuals annually and affecting the survival of wild populations. Being intimately familiar with primate social behavior, life history and biology, primatologists, whether they work with captive or wild primates, are in a unique position to understand the nature of the trade and attempt to counter its effects. In addition to updating the status of the primate pet trade, we provide recommendations that may help primatologists formulate a plan to deal, locally and regionally, with illegal trafficking in live primates. General guidelines include increasing awareness of local customs, policies and laws; developing collaborative research opportunities for local people; engaging in training/informational opportunities; and instructing on how to take action when encountering illegally-trafficked primates.","container-title":"American Journal of Primatology","DOI":"10.1002/ajp.23079","ISSN":"1098-2345","issue":"1","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1002/ajp.23079","page":"e23079","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Reducing the primate pet trade: Actions for primatologists","title-short":"Reducing the primate pet trade","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/ajp.23079","volume":"82","author":[{"family":"Norconk","given":"Marilyn A."},{"family":"Atsalis","given":"Sylvia"},{"family":"Tully","given":"Gregg"},{"family":"Santillán","given":"Ana Maria"},{"family":"Waters","given":"Siân"},{"family":"Knott","given":"Cheryl D."},{"family":"Ross","given":"Stephen R."},{"family":"Shanee","given":"Sam"},{"family":"Stiles","given":"Daniel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32775,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3WQUH46N"],"itemData":{"id":32775,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Primate species have been increasingly threatened by legal and illegal trade in China, mainly for biomedical research or as pets and traditional medicine, yet most reports on trade from China regard international trade. To assess a proxy for amount of national primate trades, we quantified the number of reports of native primate species featuring in unique web news reports from 2000 to 2017, including accuracy of their identification, location where they were confiscated or rescued, and their condition upon rescue. To measure temporal trends across these categories, the time span was divided into three sections: 2000–2005, 2006–2011 and 2012–2017. A total of 735 individuals of 14 species were reported in 372 news reports, mostly rhesus macaques (n = 165, 22.5%, Macaca mulatta) and two species of slow lorises (n = 487, 66.3%, Nycticebus spp.). During the same period, live individuals of rhesus macaques were recorded 206 times (70,949 individuals) in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Trade Database, whereas slow lorises were only recorded four times (nine individuals), indicating that the species originated illegally from China or were illegally imported into China. Due to their rescued locations in residential areas (n = 211, 56.7%), most primates appeared to be housed privately as pets. A higher proportion of ‘market’ rescues during 2006–2011 (?2 = 8.485, df = 2, p = 0.014), could be partly attributed to an intensive management on wildlife markets since the outbreak of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) in 2003. More than half (68.3%, 502 individuals) of the primate individuals were unhealthy, injured or dead when rescued. Thus, identification and welfare training and capacity-building should be provided to husbandry and veterinary professionals, as well as education to the public through awareness initiatives. The increase in presence of some species, especially slow lorises, with a declining population in restricted areas, also suggests the urgent need for public awareness about the illegal nature of keeping these taxa as pets.","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.6069","ISSN":"2167-8359","journalAbbreviation":"PeerJ","language":"en","note":"publisher: PeerJ Inc.","page":"e6069","source":"peerj.com","title":"Conservation implications of primate trade in China over 18 years based on web news reports of confiscations","URL":"https://peerj.com/articles/6069","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Ni","given":"Qingyong"},{"family":"Wang","given":"Yu"},{"family":"Weldon","given":"Ariana"},{"family":"Xie","given":"Meng"},{"family":"Xu","given":"Huailiang"},{"family":"Yao","given":"Yongfang"},{"family":"Zhang","given":"Mingwang"},{"family":"Li","given":"Ying"},{"family":"Li","given":"Yan"},{"family":"Zeng","given":"Bo"},{"family":"Nekaris","given":"K. a. I."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12,6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Over two thousand individuals from seventeen Indonesian primate species continued to be sold in numerous open wild species markets as recorded intemittenly from 1990 through 2014 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aCXwal33","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nijman, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Nijman, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32756,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KV583849"],"itemData":{"id":32756,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"American Journal of Primatology","issue":"11","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"e22517","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Changes in the primate trade in Indonesian wildlife markets over a 25-year period: Fewer apes and langurs, more macaques, and slow lorises","title-short":"Changes in the primate trade in Indonesian wildlife markets over a 25-year period","volume":"79","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Spaan","given":"Denise"},{"family":"Rode-Margono","given":"Eva Johanna"},{"family":"Nekaris","given":"K. A. I."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the early 2000s while orangutans, gibbons, langurs, macaques and slow lorises were all commonly traded, only the latter two groups made up the bulk of the trade in the last decade.       
	Except pet trade, fiber trade is an important driver of sustainable use of wild species. Vicuñas (Vicugna vicugna) produce one of the finest natural fibers in the world. Due to its fineness, vicuña occupies a position in the luxury fashion market. It is used to produce garments, shawls and stoles for retail mainly in high end shops in Italy, Japan and Dubai. Before 1980, vicuñas were almost extinct due to overhunting. By 1960, it was estimated that the vicuña population had dropped from its pre-colonial population of 2 million to an estimated 10,000 individuals. International, regional and national conservation efforts were successful in halting further population decline. Strict conservation regulation, through the Vicuña Convention, and the entry into force of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora in 1975, helped to rebuild populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qEeGAwSe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7370,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7TN9Z64K","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/77KTC5ZS"],"itemData":{"id":7370,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","DOI":"http://doi.org/10.18352/ijc.139","issue":"1","page":"100-121","title":"Vicuña conservation and poverty alleviation? Andean communities and international fibre markets","URL":"https://www.thecommonsjournal.org/articles/10.18352/ijc.139/","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein, Gabriela","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. After a successful first stage of absolute protection, a second stage started with the involvement of local communities in the national programmes for conservation and management of the species. In 1979 the Convention for the Conservation and Management of Vicuña, was signed, which promoted the economic exploitation of the species “for the benefit of the Andean people” (Article 1, Convention for the Conservation and Management of the Vicuña, 1979). The involvement of local Andean communities in vicuña management, and fibre trade was key to the recovery of the species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"stixmiGU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7370,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7TN9Z64K","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/77KTC5ZS"],"itemData":{"id":7370,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","DOI":"http://doi.org/10.18352/ijc.139","issue":"1","page":"100-121","title":"Vicuña conservation and poverty alleviation? Andean communities and international fibre markets","URL":"https://www.thecommonsjournal.org/articles/10.18352/ijc.139/","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein, Gabriela","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. On top of the historical strong cultural link between Vicuñas and Andean communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TzMc1k0e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vil\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Vilá, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":24374,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IAPAVBRY"],"itemData":{"id":24374,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Case Studies in the Environment","issue":"1","language":"en","title":"Vicuñas (Vicugna vicugna), Wild Andean Altiplano CamelidsMultiple Valuation for Their Sustainable Use and Biocultural Role in Local Communities","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Vilá","given":"B."},{"family":"Arzamendia","given":"Y."},{"family":"Rojo","given":"V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} (see chapter 1), vicuña have become an economic asset to communities, reducing poaching and motivating communities to carry out anti-poaching and protection. From the community perspective, vicuña management also fulfills non-economic objectives. In the cases of Bolivia and Peru, these entail enhancing community identity, social cohesion, revitalizing communal work, reaffirming community boundaries and a means to solidify land claims ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jKopitok","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lichtenstein & Ros, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Lichtenstein & Ros, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":35208,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KSFG3KQC"],"itemData":{"id":35208,"type":"chapter","abstract":"Vicuña management by Andean communities is one of the few success stories about international conservation. The species has not only recovered from the brink of extinction, but also ancient traditions were restored along with it. Vicuñas, Vicugna vicugna\\n                        , are wild South American camelids that roam in the Puna and Altiplano of the high Andean ecoregions in Argentina, Bolivia, Chile, Ecuador and Peru which is situated between 3,500 and 5,000 metres above sea level. The vicuña’s highly prized fleece has been both its greatest asset and its biggest downfall. In this chapter, first, we provide a historical background of the decommonisation process of vicuñas and their near extinction that occurred following the Spanish Conquest in the Andean Region. Second, we analyse their recent commonisation led by the Indigenous communities living in the province of Jujuy, Argentina, including the enabling conditions, and the resulting socio-political, cultural, economic and ecological benefits that are derived by the communities involved.","container-title":"Making Commons Dynamic","ISBN":"978-0-429-02863-2","note":"DOI: 10.4324/9780429028632-9","publisher":"Routledge","title":"Vicuña conservation and the reinvigoration of Indigenous communities in the Andes","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Ros","given":"Carlos Cowan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Participating in vicuña use probably also helps remote (and usually neglected) communities to become visible to local and central governments and be in a better position to ask for credit, schools, health service, better roads, infrastructure and support for economic activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wf52UTpi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7370,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7TN9Z64K","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/77KTC5ZS"],"itemData":{"id":7370,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","DOI":"http://doi.org/10.18352/ijc.139","issue":"1","page":"100-121","title":"Vicuña conservation and poverty alleviation? Andean communities and international fibre markets","URL":"https://www.thecommonsjournal.org/articles/10.18352/ijc.139/","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein, Gabriela","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At present vicuñas are categorized as Least Concern (International Union for Conservation of Nature SSC Red List), and the current population trend is increasing  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"p8XEt9QJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Acebes, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Acebes, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":29443,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FHQYA5XJ"],"itemData":{"id":29443,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species","DOI":"10.2305/IUCN.UK.2018-2.RLTS.T22956A18540534.en.","language":"en","page":"22956 18540534","title":"Vicugna vicugna","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.2305/IUCN.UK.2018-2.RLTS.T22956A18540534.en.","author":[{"family":"Acebes","given":"P."},{"family":"Wheeler","given":"J."},{"family":"Baldo","given":"J."},{"family":"Tuppia","given":"P."},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"G."},{"family":"D","given":"Hoces"},{"family":"Franklin","given":"W.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Sustainable use through legal trade was key to success ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JbGeCEUR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cooney, Rosie, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Cooney, Rosie, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7366,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FB6R6IJ6"],"itemData":{"id":7366,"type":"report","collection-title":"CITES & Livelihoods","publisher":"CITES","title":"Vicuña fibre harvesting and trade in Bolivia","author":[{"family":"Cooney, Rosie","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Without trade in vicuña fibre it is likely that communities will lose motivation and capacity to conserve vicuña and this could spark poaching and conflicts with domestic livestock ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zzPOcs1k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vil\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Vilá, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":24374,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IAPAVBRY"],"itemData":{"id":24374,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Case Studies in the Environment","issue":"1","language":"en","title":"Vicuñas (Vicugna vicugna), Wild Andean Altiplano CamelidsMultiple Valuation for Their Sustainable Use and Biocultural Role in Local Communities","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Vilá","given":"B."},{"family":"Arzamendia","given":"Y."},{"family":"Rojo","given":"V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Overall  exports of vicuña fibre from  range countries  were approximately 60,000 kg of  fibre over the period 2007-2016. Peru is the main exporter of vicuña fibre, accounting for 80% of exports ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JAPkPDpK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Kasterine & Lichtenstein, 2018)","plainCitation":"(A. Kasterine & Lichtenstein, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32352,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XP2T8FRZ"],"itemData":{"id":32352,"type":"book","event-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","publisher":"International Trade Centre","publisher-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","title":"Trade in Vicuña: the Implications for Conservation and Rural Livelihoods.","author":[{"family":"Kasterine","given":"A"},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Peru produced 58,8  tons of fibre from 2012 to 2018 as a result of shearing 34200 vicuñas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"x4BWY9wt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ministerio de Agricultura y Riego, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Ministerio de Agricultura y Riego, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7367,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3GE8KZRK"],"itemData":{"id":7367,"type":"report","event-place":"Lima, Peru","language":"Spanish","publisher":"Viceministerio de Agricultura","publisher-place":"Lima, Peru","title":"Perfil de Mercado de la fibra de vicuña","author":[{"family":"Ministerio de Agricultura y Riego","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 


Figure 4.11. Change in vicuña numbers in the Andean countries 1969-2012. Source: Kasterine and Lichtenstein, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2LGSMp2z","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2018)","plainCitation":"(2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32352,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XP2T8FRZ"],"itemData":{"id":32352,"type":"book","event-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","publisher":"International Trade Centre","publisher-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","title":"Trade in Vicuña: the Implications for Conservation and Rural Livelihoods.","author":[{"family":"Kasterine","given":"A"},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page","suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. © International Trade Centre (ITC). License CC-BY.

Wild species trade: Logging
There is established evidence that trade is an important driver affecting sustainable use of forests. However, there is incomplete information regarding under which conditions trade alleviates and when it aggravates pressures. Chaudhary ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"N6sYJ3Cu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2017)","plainCitation":"(2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":752,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UAH4DPBU"],"itemData":{"id":752,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Identifying the global hotspots of forestry driven species, ecosystem services losses and informing the consuming nations of their environmental footprint domestically and abroad is essential to design demand side interventions and induce sustainable production methods. Here we first use countryside species area relationship model to project species extinctions of four vertebrate taxa (mammals, birds, amphibians and reptiles) due to forest land use in 174 countries. We combine the projected extinctions with a global database on the monetary value of ecosystem services provided by different biomes and with bilateral trade data of wood products to calculate species extinctions and ecosystem services losses inflicted by national wood consumption and international wood trade. Results show that globally a total of 485 species are projected to go extinct due to current forest land use. About 32% of this projected loss can be attributed to land use devoted for export production. However, under the counterfactual scenario with the same consumption levels but no international trade of wood products, an additional 334 species are projected to go extinct. Globally, we find that losses of ecosystem services worth $1.5trillion/year are embodied in the timber trade. Compared to high-income nations, tropical countries such as Philippines, Nicaragua, Sri Lanka, Gambia and Bolivia presented the highest net ecosystem services losses (>3000US$/ha/year) that could not be compensated through current land rents, indicating underpriced exports. Small tropical countries also gained much lower rents per species extinction suffered. These results can help internalize these costs into the global trade through financial compensation mechanisms such as REDD+ or through price premiums on wood sourced from these countries. Overall the results can provide valuable insights for devising national strategies to meet several of the global Aichi 2020 biodiversity targets and can also be useful for life cycle assessment and product labelling schemes.","container-title":"Science of The Total Environment","DOI":"10.1016/j.scitotenv.2017.02.078","ISSN":"0048-9697","journalAbbreviation":"Science of The Total Environment","language":"en","page":"985-994","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Linking national wood consumption with global biodiversity and ecosystem service losses","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0048969717303194","volume":"586","author":[{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"Abhishek"},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L. Roman"},{"family":"Kastner","given":"Thomas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",5,15]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} use a model to project species extinctions of four vertebrate taxa (mammals, birds, amphibians and reptiles) due to wood extraction in 174 countries. Globally, 485 species are projected to go extinct due to current forest land use, where 32% of this projected loss can be attributed to exports. At the same time, trade reduces pressure on forests in importing countries. If the same consumption level would have to be met by only domestic sources, an additional 334 species are projected to go extinct ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cXTccXqx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chaudhary, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Chaudhary, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":752,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UAH4DPBU"],"itemData":{"id":752,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Identifying the global hotspots of forestry driven species, ecosystem services losses and informing the consuming nations of their environmental footprint domestically and abroad is essential to design demand side interventions and induce sustainable production methods. Here we first use countryside species area relationship model to project species extinctions of four vertebrate taxa (mammals, birds, amphibians and reptiles) due to forest land use in 174 countries. We combine the projected extinctions with a global database on the monetary value of ecosystem services provided by different biomes and with bilateral trade data of wood products to calculate species extinctions and ecosystem services losses inflicted by national wood consumption and international wood trade. Results show that globally a total of 485 species are projected to go extinct due to current forest land use. About 32% of this projected loss can be attributed to land use devoted for export production. However, under the counterfactual scenario with the same consumption levels but no international trade of wood products, an additional 334 species are projected to go extinct. Globally, we find that losses of ecosystem services worth $1.5trillion/year are embodied in the timber trade. Compared to high-income nations, tropical countries such as Philippines, Nicaragua, Sri Lanka, Gambia and Bolivia presented the highest net ecosystem services losses (>3000US$/ha/year) that could not be compensated through current land rents, indicating underpriced exports. Small tropical countries also gained much lower rents per species extinction suffered. These results can help internalize these costs into the global trade through financial compensation mechanisms such as REDD+ or through price premiums on wood sourced from these countries. Overall the results can provide valuable insights for devising national strategies to meet several of the global Aichi 2020 biodiversity targets and can also be useful for life cycle assessment and product labelling schemes.","container-title":"Science of The Total Environment","DOI":"10.1016/j.scitotenv.2017.02.078","ISSN":"0048-9697","journalAbbreviation":"Science of The Total Environment","language":"en","page":"985-994","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Linking national wood consumption with global biodiversity and ecosystem service losses","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0048969717303194","volume":"586","author":[{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"Abhishek"},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L. Roman"},{"family":"Kastner","given":"Thomas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",5,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hence, trade may encourage sustainable ways of use in some areas, and lead to unsustainable uses in others. 
      There may also be important socio-economic dynamics as wild species are exploited over time ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Fkl7sPZ6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Marchak, 1995)","plainCitation":"(Marchak, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":724,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CRRHX5J2"],"itemData":{"id":724,"type":"book","abstract":"Patricia Marchak examines issues particular to the northern and southern regions and the global effects of trends in each region, using British Columbia, Brazil, Chile, Indonesia, and Thailand as full case studies and Malaysia, Myanmar, and other south-east Asian regions as shorter case studies. She also examines Japanese forestry and the Japanese paper industry. Logging the Globe provides in-depth analyses of the restructuring of the global division of labour; the effect of Japanese demand for pulp; changes in employment, production, land policies, and markets in northern countries; deforestation; plantation forestry; and the influence of European, North American, and Japanese companies on tropical forests and peoples. Marchak considers whether industrial forestry is sustainable and suggests ways in which global demand for forest products can be met in more efficient and more nearly sustainable ways. Logging the Globe presents a global picture of a critically important environmental and social issue. It will be of great interest to professionals in the industry, policy makers and environmental activists, and those concerned with environmental and social issues.","ISBN":"978-0-7735-1345-7","language":"en","note":"Google-Books-ID: WcfW3Nvsve0C","number-of-pages":"426","publisher":"McGill-Queen's Press - MQUP","source":"Google Books","title":"Logging the Globe","author":[{"family":"Marchak","given":"M. Patricia"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In some cases, when commercially valuable tree species are gone, highly capitalized loggers may be replaced by lower budget loggers. Also, logging and land conversion for agricultural purposes often goes hand in hand. In some cases, landholders may convert the stocking forests even without using one single log because agriculture is so much more attractive, yet in other may even use the cash generated from timber to convert forests to plantations. Some smallholders making a living in forest landscapes supplement their incomes from logging ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9DxdNEkN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Angelsen, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Angelsen, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CQD2A92G"],"itemData":{"id":34811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","page":"S12–S28","title":"Environmental income and rural livelihoods: a global-comparative analysis","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305750X14000722","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Angelsen","given":"Arild"},{"family":"Jagger","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"Babigumira","given":"Ronnie"},{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Hogarth","given":"Nicholas J"},{"family":"Bauch","given":"Simone"},{"family":"Börner","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Smith-Hall","given":"Carsten"},{"family":"Wunder","given":"Sven"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} but as far as the rapidly depleting forests still allows it. Another important group depend on chainsaw milling with intensities that vary, leading to incremental forest degradation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aHFJZnrQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Eba\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}a Atyi, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Eba’a Atyi, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2070,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N4MYJ5DJ"],"itemData":{"id":2070,"type":"book","event-place":"Yaoundé, Cameroon","language":"en","note":"issue: 4)","publisher":"CIFOR report for ITTO","publisher-place":"Yaoundé, Cameroon","title":"Domestic markets, cross-border trade and the role of the informal sector in Cote d’Ivoire, Cameroon and the Democratic Republic of Congo","author":[{"family":"Eba’a Atyi","given":"R."},{"family":"Lescuyer","given":"G."},{"family":"Cerutti","given":"P.O."},{"family":"Tsanga","given":"R."},{"family":"Essiane Mendoula","given":"E."},{"family":"Collins","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Only limited number of communities have proven successful to sustain their commercial timber operations over time and remain competitive in timber market ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ejNb5QjW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bray, 2020; G. Medina & Pokorny, 2011; Pokorny, 2013; Stoian, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Bray, 2020; G. Medina & Pokorny, 2011; Pokorny, 2013; Stoian, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":24623,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NRR7CVZ6"],"itemData":{"id":24623,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"The University of Arizona Press","title":"Mexico’s community forest enterprises: Success on the commons and the seeds of a good anthropocene","author":[{"family":"Bray","given":"D.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":9473,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SMUUNZIC"],"itemData":{"id":9473,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Amazonian communities have the potential for improving their livelihoods by ef?ciently managing their forest resources. Aiming to support communities, there are different externally-driven initiatives promoting community forest management (CFM) for timber extraction. However, there is limited understanding of the ?nancial viability of those initiatives. This study revealed that families received limited ?nancial bene?ts from externally-driven CFM initiatives. A critical debate on the real potential of these approaches needs to take place in order to adjust the concepts to the interests and capacities of local communities.","container-title":"Novos Cadernos NAEA","DOI":"10.5801/ncn.v14i2.627","ISSN":"21797536, 15166481","issue":"2","language":"pt","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Avaliação Financeira do Manejo Florestal Comunitário","URL":"http://www.periodicos.ufpa.br/index.php/ncn/article/view/627","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Medina","given":"Gabriel"},{"family":"Pokorny","given":"Benno"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12,1]]}}},{"id":23726,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PTJEX58E"],"itemData":{"id":23726,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"ROUTLEDGE","title":"Smallholders, forest management and rural development in the amazon","author":[{"family":"Pokorny","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":24132,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FMNW358T"],"itemData":{"id":24132,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"CIFOR","language":"fr","title":"Forest concessions in Petén, Guatemala: A Systematic Analysis of the Socioeconomic Performance of Community Enterprises in the Maya Biosphere Reserve","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Stoian","given":"D."},{"family":"Rodas","given":"A."},{"family":"Butler","given":"M."},{"family":"Monterroso","given":"I."},{"family":"Hodgdon","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} yet that results from a combination of different factors, mainly long-term external support, willingness to maintain relatively lower extraction rates, access to high value timber species, and strong market engagement ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nDVohmbh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pacheco, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Pacheco, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33829,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I549BUNA"],"itemData":{"id":33829,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation and Society","DOI":"10.4103/0972-4923.97484","ISSN":"0972-4923","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Conservat Soc","language":"en","page":"114","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Smallholders and communities in timber markets: Conditions shaping diverse forms of engagement in tropical Latin America","title-short":"Smallholders and communities in timber markets","URL":"http://www.conservationandsociety.org/text.asp?2012/10/2/114/97484","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Pacheco","given":"Pablo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.	
      In tropical countries, trade liberalization has contributed considerably to deforestation in the past ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HKoMBZhd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Abman & Lundberg, 2019; Kaimowitz & Angelsen, 1998; Marchak, 1995; D. Pearce, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Abman & Lundberg, 2019; Kaimowitz & Angelsen, 1998; Marchak, 1995; D. Pearce, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":724,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CRRHX5J2"],"itemData":{"id":724,"type":"book","abstract":"Patricia Marchak examines issues particular to the northern and southern regions and the global effects of trends in each region, using British Columbia, Brazil, Chile, Indonesia, and Thailand as full case studies and Malaysia, Myanmar, and other south-east Asian regions as shorter case studies. She also examines Japanese forestry and the Japanese paper industry. Logging the Globe provides in-depth analyses of the restructuring of the global division of labour; the effect of Japanese demand for pulp; changes in employment, production, land policies, and markets in northern countries; deforestation; plantation forestry; and the influence of European, North American, and Japanese companies on tropical forests and peoples. Marchak considers whether industrial forestry is sustainable and suggests ways in which global demand for forest products can be met in more efficient and more nearly sustainable ways. Logging the Globe presents a global picture of a critically important environmental and social issue. It will be of great interest to professionals in the industry, policy makers and environmental activists, and those concerned with environmental and social issues.","ISBN":"978-0-7735-1345-7","language":"en","note":"Google-Books-ID: WcfW3Nvsve0C","number-of-pages":"426","publisher":"McGill-Queen's Press - MQUP","source":"Google Books","title":"Logging the Globe","author":[{"family":"Marchak","given":"M. Patricia"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}},{"id":727,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J55V76QR"],"itemData":{"id":727,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper provides new, global evidence on the relationship between trade liberalization and deforestation. Using event studies around the enactment of regional trade agreements (RTAs) on a panel of 189 countries from 2001 to 2012, we find a large and statistically significant increase in deforestation over the 3 years following the enactment of an RTA, which coincides with an increase in agricultural land conversion. The findings are robust to a variety of controls and fixed effects. The results on deforestation and agricultural land expansion are driven by developing countries in the tropics, suggesting that trade liberalization not only increases net deforestation but may also shift deforestation into ecologically sensitive locations. We find no evidence that these effects are driven by increases in timber harvest. Our results suggest that trade liberalization efforts should be accompanied by policies to direct agricultural land expansion away from forests and sensitive habitat regions.","container-title":"Journal of the Association of Environmental and Resource Economists","DOI":"10.1086/705787","ISSN":"2333-5955","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of the Association of Environmental and Resource Economists","note":"publisher: The University of Chicago Press","page":"35-72","source":"journals.uchicago.edu (Atypon)","title":"Does Free Trade Increase Deforestation? The Effects of Regional Trade Agreements","title-short":"Does Free Trade Increase Deforestation?","URL":"https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/full/10.1086/705787","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Abman","given":"Ryan"},{"family":"Lundberg","given":"Clark"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,13]]}}},{"id":735,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V7RP9YUK"],"itemData":{"id":735,"type":"article-journal","note":"publisher: Cifor","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Economic models of tropical deforestation: a review","title-short":"Economic models of tropical deforestation","author":[{"family":"Kaimowitz","given":"David"},{"family":"Angelsen","given":"Arild"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":736,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GY7TELUU"],"itemData":{"id":736,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The profitability of uncontrolled logging can be a significant obstacle to sustainable forest management, especially in the tropics. Rice [Scientific American 273 (1997) 34] have argued that not only does traditional selective logging provide higher returns but also incurs less damage to forests than sustainable forest management systems that involve harvesting of many species and the creation of large gaps in the forest canopy to foster regeneration of light-demanding species. They claimed that protected areas were the only viable way to conserve forest ecosystems and proposed that loggers be allowed to log forests selectively once, after which the forests should become parks. Here, we respond to the challenge posed by Rice by exhaustively reviewing the evidence regarding the viability and desirability of sustainable forest management in the tropics. Following Rice, we use the term conventional timber harvesting to refer to existing practice, which typically pays little attention to maintaining long-term timber supply. Sustainable timber management implies taking steps to ensure forests continue to produce timber in the longer-term, while maintaining the full complement of environmental services and non-timber products of the forest. Empirical studies tend to confirm the conclusion of (Rice [Scientific American 273 (1997) 34]) that although sustainable timber management sometimes provides reasonable rates of return, conventional timber harvesting is generally more profitable. This implies that without additional incentives, one cannot expect companies to adopt sustainable management. The shortsightedness of many loggers, the slow rise in international timber prices, political uncertainty, and tenure insecurity simply reinforce this tendency. However, we reject the claim that sustainable timber management generally damages forests more than conventional logging. Rice base their conclusion largely on the particular case of mahogany extraction in Bolivia, and even there it may not hold. In many cases, sustainable timber management performs better in terms of carbon storage and bio-diversity conservation than conventional logging approaches, as well as producing more timber. If new carbon markets emerge, sustainable forest management might compete effectively with conventional timber harvesting. Timber certification systems may also provide a sufficient incentive for sustainable forest management in certain circumstances.","container-title":"Forest Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1016/S0378-1127(01)00798-8","ISSN":"0378-1127","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Forest Ecology and Management","language":"en","page":"229-247","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Sustainable forestry in the tropics: panacea or folly?","title-short":"Sustainable forestry in the tropics","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378112701007988","volume":"172","author":[{"family":"Pearce","given":"David"},{"family":"Putz","given":"Francis E"},{"family":"Vanclay","given":"Jerome K"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",1,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the period from 2001 to 2012, enacting regional trade agreements has led to significant increases in deforestation, with cumulative effects of 19%–26% above the annual average three years after removing the trade barriers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pGqer7jl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Abman & Lundberg, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Abman & Lundberg, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":727,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J55V76QR"],"itemData":{"id":727,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper provides new, global evidence on the relationship between trade liberalization and deforestation. Using event studies around the enactment of regional trade agreements (RTAs) on a panel of 189 countries from 2001 to 2012, we find a large and statistically significant increase in deforestation over the 3 years following the enactment of an RTA, which coincides with an increase in agricultural land conversion. The findings are robust to a variety of controls and fixed effects. The results on deforestation and agricultural land expansion are driven by developing countries in the tropics, suggesting that trade liberalization not only increases net deforestation but may also shift deforestation into ecologically sensitive locations. We find no evidence that these effects are driven by increases in timber harvest. Our results suggest that trade liberalization efforts should be accompanied by policies to direct agricultural land expansion away from forests and sensitive habitat regions.","container-title":"Journal of the Association of Environmental and Resource Economists","DOI":"10.1086/705787","ISSN":"2333-5955","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of the Association of Environmental and Resource Economists","note":"publisher: The University of Chicago Press","page":"35-72","source":"journals.uchicago.edu (Atypon)","title":"Does Free Trade Increase Deforestation? The Effects of Regional Trade Agreements","title-short":"Does Free Trade Increase Deforestation?","URL":"https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/full/10.1086/705787","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Abman","given":"Ryan"},{"family":"Lundberg","given":"Clark"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,13]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Often, deforestation is mediated by unclear property rights, corruption and overall insufficient incentives to conserve tropical forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rFXOBXGV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bulte & Barbier, 2005; M. L. Ross, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Bulte & Barbier, 2005; M. L. Ross, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32711,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HHMARNWZ"],"itemData":{"id":32711,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental and Resource Economics","issue":"4","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"423–463","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Trade and renewable resources in a second best world: an overview","title-short":"Trade and renewable resources in a second best world","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Bulte","given":"Erwin H."},{"family":"Barbier","given":"Edward B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":723,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4EVGVFJQ"],"itemData":{"id":723,"type":"book","abstract":"Scholars have long studied how institutions emerge and become stable. But why do institutions sometimes break down? In this book, Michael L. Ross explores the breakdown of the institutions that govern natural resource exports in developing states. He shows that these institutions often break down when states receive positive trade shocks - unanticipated windfalls. Drawing on the theory of rent-seeking, he suggests that these institutions succumb to a problem he calls 'rent-seizing' - the predatory behavior of politicians who seek to supply rent to others, and who purposefully dismantle institutions that restrain them. Using case studies of timber booms in Indonesia, Malaysia and the Philippines, he shows how windfalls tend to trigger rent-seizing activities that may have disastrous consequences for state institutions, and for the government of natural resources. More generally, he shows how institutions can collapse when they have become endogenous to any rent-seeking process.","ISBN":"978-1-139-43211-5","language":"en","note":"Google-Books-ID: rdWxlCRVB8YC","number-of-pages":"262","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","source":"Google Books","title":"Timber Booms and Institutional Breakdown in Southeast Asia","author":[{"family":"Ross","given":"Michael L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001",1,8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Though logging contributes to deforestation, conversion towards agricultural land is an important mechanism explaining deforestation as well ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oz5MyXJe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Abman & Lundberg, 2019; Faria & Almeida, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Abman & Lundberg, 2019; Faria & Almeida, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":727,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J55V76QR"],"itemData":{"id":727,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This paper provides new, global evidence on the relationship between trade liberalization and deforestation. Using event studies around the enactment of regional trade agreements (RTAs) on a panel of 189 countries from 2001 to 2012, we find a large and statistically significant increase in deforestation over the 3 years following the enactment of an RTA, which coincides with an increase in agricultural land conversion. The findings are robust to a variety of controls and fixed effects. The results on deforestation and agricultural land expansion are driven by developing countries in the tropics, suggesting that trade liberalization not only increases net deforestation but may also shift deforestation into ecologically sensitive locations. We find no evidence that these effects are driven by increases in timber harvest. Our results suggest that trade liberalization efforts should be accompanied by policies to direct agricultural land expansion away from forests and sensitive habitat regions.","container-title":"Journal of the Association of Environmental and Resource Economists","DOI":"10.1086/705787","ISSN":"2333-5955","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of the Association of Environmental and Resource Economists","note":"publisher: The University of Chicago Press","page":"35-72","source":"journals.uchicago.edu (Atypon)","title":"Does Free Trade Increase Deforestation? The Effects of Regional Trade Agreements","title-short":"Does Free Trade Increase Deforestation?","URL":"https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/full/10.1086/705787","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Abman","given":"Ryan"},{"family":"Lundberg","given":"Clark"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,13]]}}},{"id":32798,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y3L5F2LH"],"itemData":{"id":32798,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"One of the objectives of this paper is to investigate how international trade has affected the dynamics of deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon at the level of the municipality. This analysis focuses on the expansion of crop and cattle activities, and other determinants of deforestation such as GDP per capita, conservation areas and property rights. We combine standard econometrics with spatial econometrics to capture the socioeconomic interactions among the agents in their interrelated economic system. The data used in this study correspond to a balanced panel of 732 municipalities from 2000 to 2010. The main findings suggest that as openness to trade in the Amazon increases, deforestation also increases. We also find that it is the production of soybeans and beef cattle that drives deforestation in the region. The property rights indicator also has a significant impact in deforestation. Moreover, as the GDP per capita goes up, deforestation increases. The conservation areas have a negative impact on deforestation.","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2015.11.014","ISSN":"0921-8009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"en","page":"85-97","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Relationship between openness to trade and deforestation: Empirical evidence from the Brazilian Amazon","title-short":"Relationship between openness to trade and deforestation","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0921800915004462","volume":"121","author":[{"family":"Faria","given":"Weslem Rodrigues"},{"family":"Almeida","given":"Alexandre Nunes"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Big-leaf mahogany Swietenia macrophylla King (Meliaceae) is an important timber species in Latin America that is globally in high demand. Over the past decades, lacking enforcement and unsustainable harvest have depleted local stocks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mVD0vvPz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Blundell, 2004; Kometter, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Blundell, 2004; Kometter, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":722,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JTGB3L9B"],"itemData":{"id":722,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Big-leaf mahogany Swietenia macrophylla King (Meliaceae) is the premier timber species of Latin America. A lack of law enforcement allows widespread illegal logging, and its unsustainable harvest depletes local stocks, necessitating a shift in sources of supply. To better control this exploitation, parties to the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) recently elected to list mahogany on Appendix II. This listing requires exporting countries to verify that each shipment was legally acquired and its harvest was non-detrimental to the survival of mahogany. The CITES listing poses considerable challenges: 1) there is no precedent, as mahogany is the first commonly traded timber species listed on Appendix II, and 2) given current, unsustainable logging practices, CITES regulations will be difficult to implement. This paper synthesizes information on the conservation and management of mahogany that informed the listing decision, and provides recommendations for implementation, including timber tracking to ensure legality.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605304000134","ISSN":"1365-3008, 0030-6053","issue":"1","language":"en","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"84-90","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"A review of the CITES listing of big-leaf mahogany","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/review-of-the-cites-listing-of-bigleaf-mahogany/3004ABB2A6057B19265253AD49C67D85","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Blundell","given":"Arthur G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",1]]}}},{"id":732,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6GJRVML8"],"itemData":{"id":732,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"ABSTRACT. Although bigleaf mahogany [Swietenia macrophylla King (Meliaceae)] is the premier timber species of Latin America, its exploitation is unsustainable because of a pattern of local depletion and shifting supply. We surveyed experts on the status of mahogany in Bolivia and Peru, the world’s past and present largest exporters. Bolivia no longer has commercially viable mahogany (trees > 60 cm diameter at breast height) across 79% of its range. In Peru, mahogany’s range has shrunk by 50%, and, within a decade, a further 28% will be logged out. Approximately 15% of the mahogany range in these two countries is protected, but low densities and illegal logging mean that this overestimates the extent of mahogany under protection. The international community can support mahogany conservation by funding park management and by encouraging independent verification of the legality of mahogany in trade. Our findings demonstrate that a systematic expert survey can generate reliable and cost-effective information on the status of widespread species of concern and help to inform appropriate management policy.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Resilience Alliance Inc.","source":"JSTOR","title":"Impacts of Unsustainable Mahogany Logging in Bolivia and Peru","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/26267650","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Kometter","given":"Roberto F."},{"family":"Martinez","given":"Martha"},{"family":"Blundell","given":"Arthur G."},{"family":"Gullison","given":"Raymond E."},{"family":"Steininger","given":"Marc K."},{"family":"Rice","given":"Richard E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As a result, it has been the first commercial timber species that has been listed in Appendix II of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, which implies that trade is restricted and controlled ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aj3fe7AW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Blundell, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Blundell, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":722,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JTGB3L9B"],"itemData":{"id":722,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Big-leaf mahogany Swietenia macrophylla King (Meliaceae) is the premier timber species of Latin America. A lack of law enforcement allows widespread illegal logging, and its unsustainable harvest depletes local stocks, necessitating a shift in sources of supply. To better control this exploitation, parties to the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) recently elected to list mahogany on Appendix II. This listing requires exporting countries to verify that each shipment was legally acquired and its harvest was non-detrimental to the survival of mahogany. The CITES listing poses considerable challenges: 1) there is no precedent, as mahogany is the first commonly traded timber species listed on Appendix II, and 2) given current, unsustainable logging practices, CITES regulations will be difficult to implement. This paper synthesizes information on the conservation and management of mahogany that informed the listing decision, and provides recommendations for implementation, including timber tracking to ensure legality.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605304000134","ISSN":"1365-3008, 0030-6053","issue":"1","language":"en","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"84-90","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"A review of the CITES listing of big-leaf mahogany","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/review-of-the-cites-listing-of-bigleaf-mahogany/3004ABB2A6057B19265253AD49C67D85","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Blundell","given":"Arthur G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As a result, mahagony harvests have slowed down considerably in most regions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PSuVYgMc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grogan, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Grogan, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":725,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F7VF3HB2"],"itemData":{"id":725,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Consumer demand for the premier neotropical luxury timber, big-leaf mahogany (Swietenia macrophylla), has driven boom-and-bust logging cycles for centuries, depleting local and regional supplies from Mexico to Bolivia. We revise the standard historic range map for mahogany in South America and estimate the extent to which commercial stocks have been depleted using satellite data, expert surveys, and sawmill processing center data from Brazil. We estimate an historic range of 278 million hectares spanning Venezuela to Bolivia, 57% of this in Brazil. Approximately 58 million hectares (21%) of mahogany's historic range had been lost to forest conversion by 2001. Commercial populations had been logged from at least 125 million more hectares, reducing the commercial range to 94 million hectares (34% of historic). Surviving stocks are extremely low-density populations in remote regions representing a smaller fraction of historic stocks than expected based on estimated current commercial range. Our method could advance international policy debates such as listing proposals for CITES Appendices by clarifying the commercial and conservation status of high-value timber species similar to mahogany about which little information is available. The fate of remaining mahogany stocks in South America will depend on transforming current forest management practices into sustainable production systems.","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1755-263X.2009.00082.x","ISSN":"1755-263X","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"©2009 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.","note":"_eprint: https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1755-263X.2009.00082.x","page":"12-20","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Over-harvesting driven by consumer demand leads to population decline: big-leaf mahogany in South America","title-short":"Over-harvesting driven by consumer demand leads to population decline","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1755-263X.2009.00082.x","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Grogan","given":"James"},{"family":"Blundell","given":"Arthur G."},{"family":"Landis","given":"R. Matthew"},{"family":"Youatt","given":"Ani"},{"family":"Gullison","given":"Raymond E."},{"family":"Martinez","given":"Martha"},{"family":"Kómetter","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Lentini","given":"Marco"},{"family":"Rice","given":"Richard E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, in Peru export volumes reached 52,138 m3 in 2002, while they gradually reduced to 3,071 m3 in 2007 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lQlGaiX6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grogan, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Grogan, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":725,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F7VF3HB2"],"itemData":{"id":725,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Consumer demand for the premier neotropical luxury timber, big-leaf mahogany (Swietenia macrophylla), has driven boom-and-bust logging cycles for centuries, depleting local and regional supplies from Mexico to Bolivia. We revise the standard historic range map for mahogany in South America and estimate the extent to which commercial stocks have been depleted using satellite data, expert surveys, and sawmill processing center data from Brazil. We estimate an historic range of 278 million hectares spanning Venezuela to Bolivia, 57% of this in Brazil. Approximately 58 million hectares (21%) of mahogany's historic range had been lost to forest conversion by 2001. Commercial populations had been logged from at least 125 million more hectares, reducing the commercial range to 94 million hectares (34% of historic). Surviving stocks are extremely low-density populations in remote regions representing a smaller fraction of historic stocks than expected based on estimated current commercial range. Our method could advance international policy debates such as listing proposals for CITES Appendices by clarifying the commercial and conservation status of high-value timber species similar to mahogany about which little information is available. The fate of remaining mahogany stocks in South America will depend on transforming current forest management practices into sustainable production systems.","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1755-263X.2009.00082.x","ISSN":"1755-263X","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"©2009 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.","note":"_eprint: https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/j.1755-263X.2009.00082.x","page":"12-20","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Over-harvesting driven by consumer demand leads to population decline: big-leaf mahogany in South America","title-short":"Over-harvesting driven by consumer demand leads to population decline","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1755-263X.2009.00082.x","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Grogan","given":"James"},{"family":"Blundell","given":"Arthur G."},{"family":"Landis","given":"R. Matthew"},{"family":"Youatt","given":"Ani"},{"family":"Gullison","given":"Raymond E."},{"family":"Martinez","given":"Martha"},{"family":"Kómetter","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Lentini","given":"Marco"},{"family":"Rice","given":"Richard E."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At the same time, transforming an unsustainable timber sector towards sustainability, bears the risk that illegal extracted timber enters the legal channel. In the Peruvian Amazon, efforts are made to promote sustainable logging in the tropics. Peru introduced a legal concession timber harvesting system in 2000, which was later also part of the US–Peru Trade Promotion Agreement, to curb illegal and unsustainable practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"An56sraw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Finer, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Finer, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":761,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I2L68RSD"],"itemData":{"id":761,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Peruvian Amazon is an important arena in global efforts to promote sustainable logging in the tropics. Despite recent efforts to achieve sustainability, such as provisions in the US-Peru Trade Promotion Agreement, illegal logging continues to plague the region. We present evidence that Peru's legal logging concession system is enabling the widespread illegal logging via the regulatory documents designed to ensure sustainable logging. Analyzing official government data, we found that 68.3% of all concessions supervised by authorities were suspected of major violations. Of the 609 total concessions, nearly 30% have been cancelled for violations and we expect this percentage to increase as investigations continue. Moreover, the nature of the violations indicate that the permits associated with legal concessions are used to harvest trees in unauthorized areas, thus threatening all forested areas. Many of the violations pertain to the illegal extraction of CITES-listed timber species outside authorized areas. These findings highlight the need for additional reforms.","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep04719","ISSN":"20452322","title":"Logging concessions enable illegal logging crisis in the peruvian amazon","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Finer","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Jenkins","given":"Clinton N."},{"family":"Sky","given":"Melissa A.Blue"},{"family":"Pine","given":"Justin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Yet, there is evidence that legal concessions are used to also harvest trees in unauthorized areas, thus undermining  conservation efforts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BFKwhTLb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Finer, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Finer, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":761,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I2L68RSD"],"itemData":{"id":761,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Peruvian Amazon is an important arena in global efforts to promote sustainable logging in the tropics. Despite recent efforts to achieve sustainability, such as provisions in the US-Peru Trade Promotion Agreement, illegal logging continues to plague the region. We present evidence that Peru's legal logging concession system is enabling the widespread illegal logging via the regulatory documents designed to ensure sustainable logging. Analyzing official government data, we found that 68.3% of all concessions supervised by authorities were suspected of major violations. Of the 609 total concessions, nearly 30% have been cancelled for violations and we expect this percentage to increase as investigations continue. Moreover, the nature of the violations indicate that the permits associated with legal concessions are used to harvest trees in unauthorized areas, thus threatening all forested areas. Many of the violations pertain to the illegal extraction of CITES-listed timber species outside authorized areas. These findings highlight the need for additional reforms.","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep04719","ISSN":"20452322","title":"Logging concessions enable illegal logging crisis in the peruvian amazon","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Finer","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Jenkins","given":"Clinton N."},{"family":"Sky","given":"Melissa A.Blue"},{"family":"Pine","given":"Justin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Illegal extraction is hard to detect if illegal harvests outside the concession are declared as authorized timber harvesting. Unless someone inspects the exact location where the logging should have taken place (and notices that the tree is still there or was never there in the first place) the violation will not be detected further down the value chain ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9lthcoQ8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Finer, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Finer, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":761,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I2L68RSD"],"itemData":{"id":761,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Peruvian Amazon is an important arena in global efforts to promote sustainable logging in the tropics. Despite recent efforts to achieve sustainability, such as provisions in the US-Peru Trade Promotion Agreement, illegal logging continues to plague the region. We present evidence that Peru's legal logging concession system is enabling the widespread illegal logging via the regulatory documents designed to ensure sustainable logging. Analyzing official government data, we found that 68.3% of all concessions supervised by authorities were suspected of major violations. Of the 609 total concessions, nearly 30% have been cancelled for violations and we expect this percentage to increase as investigations continue. Moreover, the nature of the violations indicate that the permits associated with legal concessions are used to harvest trees in unauthorized areas, thus threatening all forested areas. Many of the violations pertain to the illegal extraction of CITES-listed timber species outside authorized areas. These findings highlight the need for additional reforms.","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep04719","ISSN":"20452322","title":"Logging concessions enable illegal logging crisis in the peruvian amazon","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Finer","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Jenkins","given":"Clinton N."},{"family":"Sky","given":"Melissa A.Blue"},{"family":"Pine","given":"Justin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Since most controls take place outside the forests, violations will not be detected easily. 
	Rosewood, mostly originating from Africa, is a highly priced commodity and tropical forests are threatened by increasing global demand since the early 2000s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UXw76cga","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Waeber, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Waeber, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":757,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6X4YK99"],"itemData":{"id":757,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Illegal timber trade is a global issue; highly prized rosewoods are mainly sourced from Africa and Madagascar. In Madagascar, where corruption and political instability are rampant, forest regulations have been issued during the last 15?years to facilitate illegal rosewood exploitation. The current situation precludes non-detriment findings (under which the exporting State ensures that a proposed action will not be detrimental to the survival of a species) intended to enable sustainable use of standing populations, but the Malagasy government, backed by the World Bank, is promoting the sale of massive stocks of confiscated precious wood. We argue that allowing the sale of these stocks would encourage further illegal harvest. No suitable tools are available to identify, control or monitor standing trees or cut timber, and there are substantial knowledge gaps regarding species limits, population sizes, distribution and abundance. When combined with taxonomic confusion and weak governance, these factors necessitate uplifting all of Madagascar's precious woods to Appendix I of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES).","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2019.04.007","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"89-92","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Uplisting of Malagasy precious woods critical for their survival","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320719304276","volume":"235","author":[{"family":"Waeber","given":"Patrick O."},{"family":"Schuurman","given":"Derek"},{"family":"Ramamonjisoa","given":"Bruno"},{"family":"Langrand","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Barber","given":"Charles V."},{"family":"Innes","given":"John L."},{"family":"Lowry","given":"Porter P."},{"family":"Wilmé","given":"Lucienne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In 1992, Brazilian rosewood (Dalbergia nigra) was placed on the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Appendix I in 1992. In 2013, Siamese rosewood (Dalbergia cochinchinensis) and all Malagasy species of Dalbergia and Diospyros (ebony) were added to Appendix II  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MNZTZ1lo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Waeber, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Waeber, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":757,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6X4YK99"],"itemData":{"id":757,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Illegal timber trade is a global issue; highly prized rosewoods are mainly sourced from Africa and Madagascar. In Madagascar, where corruption and political instability are rampant, forest regulations have been issued during the last 15?years to facilitate illegal rosewood exploitation. The current situation precludes non-detriment findings (under which the exporting State ensures that a proposed action will not be detrimental to the survival of a species) intended to enable sustainable use of standing populations, but the Malagasy government, backed by the World Bank, is promoting the sale of massive stocks of confiscated precious wood. We argue that allowing the sale of these stocks would encourage further illegal harvest. No suitable tools are available to identify, control or monitor standing trees or cut timber, and there are substantial knowledge gaps regarding species limits, population sizes, distribution and abundance. When combined with taxonomic confusion and weak governance, these factors necessitate uplifting all of Madagascar's precious woods to Appendix I of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES).","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2019.04.007","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"89-92","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Uplisting of Malagasy precious woods critical for their survival","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320719304276","volume":"235","author":[{"family":"Waeber","given":"Patrick O."},{"family":"Schuurman","given":"Derek"},{"family":"Ramamonjisoa","given":"Bruno"},{"family":"Langrand","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Barber","given":"Charles V."},{"family":"Innes","given":"John L."},{"family":"Lowry","given":"Porter P."},{"family":"Wilmé","given":"Lucienne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Yet, lack of clarifity about which rosewood species are exploited and how to identify those makes it notoriously difficult to separate legal harvests from illegal ones. Rosewood comprised 35% of the value of all global wild species and forest product-related seizures from 2005 to 2014 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ULOxio3T","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Waeber, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Waeber, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":757,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6X4YK99"],"itemData":{"id":757,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Illegal timber trade is a global issue; highly prized rosewoods are mainly sourced from Africa and Madagascar. In Madagascar, where corruption and political instability are rampant, forest regulations have been issued during the last 15?years to facilitate illegal rosewood exploitation. The current situation precludes non-detriment findings (under which the exporting State ensures that a proposed action will not be detrimental to the survival of a species) intended to enable sustainable use of standing populations, but the Malagasy government, backed by the World Bank, is promoting the sale of massive stocks of confiscated precious wood. We argue that allowing the sale of these stocks would encourage further illegal harvest. No suitable tools are available to identify, control or monitor standing trees or cut timber, and there are substantial knowledge gaps regarding species limits, population sizes, distribution and abundance. When combined with taxonomic confusion and weak governance, these factors necessitate uplifting all of Madagascar's precious woods to Appendix I of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES).","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2019.04.007","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"89-92","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Uplisting of Malagasy precious woods critical for their survival","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320719304276","volume":"235","author":[{"family":"Waeber","given":"Patrick O."},{"family":"Schuurman","given":"Derek"},{"family":"Ramamonjisoa","given":"Bruno"},{"family":"Langrand","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Barber","given":"Charles V."},{"family":"Innes","given":"John L."},{"family":"Lowry","given":"Porter P."},{"family":"Wilmé","given":"Lucienne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A key challenge remains that the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora regulations are based on individual species, while even experts are often unable to identify and distinguish different species. Also, harvesters do not consider individual species (or genera), but rather consider the quality of the wood ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KLzjYckq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Waeber, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Waeber, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":757,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6X4YK99"],"itemData":{"id":757,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Illegal timber trade is a global issue; highly prized rosewoods are mainly sourced from Africa and Madagascar. In Madagascar, where corruption and political instability are rampant, forest regulations have been issued during the last 15?years to facilitate illegal rosewood exploitation. The current situation precludes non-detriment findings (under which the exporting State ensures that a proposed action will not be detrimental to the survival of a species) intended to enable sustainable use of standing populations, but the Malagasy government, backed by the World Bank, is promoting the sale of massive stocks of confiscated precious wood. We argue that allowing the sale of these stocks would encourage further illegal harvest. No suitable tools are available to identify, control or monitor standing trees or cut timber, and there are substantial knowledge gaps regarding species limits, population sizes, distribution and abundance. When combined with taxonomic confusion and weak governance, these factors necessitate uplifting all of Madagascar's precious woods to Appendix I of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES).","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2019.04.007","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"89-92","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Uplisting of Malagasy precious woods critical for their survival","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320719304276","volume":"235","author":[{"family":"Waeber","given":"Patrick O."},{"family":"Schuurman","given":"Derek"},{"family":"Ramamonjisoa","given":"Bruno"},{"family":"Langrand","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Barber","given":"Charles V."},{"family":"Innes","given":"John L."},{"family":"Lowry","given":"Porter P."},{"family":"Wilmé","given":"Lucienne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As a result, harvesters often end up in illegal practices, intentionally or unintentionally. If taxonomic confusion and weak governance render sustainable use impossible, uplifting to Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Appendix I may be the only way to prevent overexploitation, as has been suggested for Malagasy rosewood  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1o5pyMpV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Waeber, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Waeber, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":757,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W6X4YK99"],"itemData":{"id":757,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Illegal timber trade is a global issue; highly prized rosewoods are mainly sourced from Africa and Madagascar. In Madagascar, where corruption and political instability are rampant, forest regulations have been issued during the last 15?years to facilitate illegal rosewood exploitation. The current situation precludes non-detriment findings (under which the exporting State ensures that a proposed action will not be detrimental to the survival of a species) intended to enable sustainable use of standing populations, but the Malagasy government, backed by the World Bank, is promoting the sale of massive stocks of confiscated precious wood. We argue that allowing the sale of these stocks would encourage further illegal harvest. No suitable tools are available to identify, control or monitor standing trees or cut timber, and there are substantial knowledge gaps regarding species limits, population sizes, distribution and abundance. When combined with taxonomic confusion and weak governance, these factors necessitate uplifting all of Madagascar's precious woods to Appendix I of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES).","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2019.04.007","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"89-92","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Uplisting of Malagasy precious woods critical for their survival","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320719304276","volume":"235","author":[{"family":"Waeber","given":"Patrick O."},{"family":"Schuurman","given":"Derek"},{"family":"Ramamonjisoa","given":"Bruno"},{"family":"Langrand","given":"Marion"},{"family":"Barber","given":"Charles V."},{"family":"Innes","given":"John L."},{"family":"Lowry","given":"Porter P."},{"family":"Wilmé","given":"Lucienne"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora regulations are often very specific, information further down the value chain is much more crude, which makes enforcement and detection very difficult. For example, United Nations Comtrade Harmonized System (HS) Codes  are often fairly general with broad descriptions, opening a channel for illegal harvests. (e.g. 26% of seafood trade is declared as “Fish” and 22% of furniture trade is declared as “Tropical wood” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8fxaJGKU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. A. Andersson, 2021)","plainCitation":"(A. A. Andersson, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H92SKYTQ"],"itemData":{"id":765,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Nature has the potential to provide wide-ranging economic contributions to society – from ecosystem services to providing income to communities via fair trade of resources. Unsustainable trade in wildlife, however, threatens biodiversity and its ability to support communities and a functioning planet. It is therefore important to have clear systems in place for tracing traded wildlife. Monitoring legal wildlife trade in all species is as important as it is for trade in protected species, since flows of the legal trade correlate with, and provide cover for, illegally traded wildlife. The majority of wildlife trade research is focused on species listed in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES). The concurrent, considerably larger legal wildlife trade in both CITES and non-CITES-listed species, however, remains unexamined – despite the fact that if mismanaged, it can lead to over-exploitation. Here we analyzed 20 years of data from the UN Comtrade database, aiming to detail the scale, composition, and trends across all taxa of wildlife traded legally, and to indicate opportunities for improvement. From 1997 to 2016 the value of this trade totaled between US$2.9 and 4.4 trillion. Of this, $2.9 trillion wildlife was traded under “specific” codes that specify taxonomic Order and below, while around $1.4 trillion was traded under “broad” codes that declare wildlife to taxonomic Class and above. The top 10 trading nations/territories accounted for 51.4% of the total value of wildlife traded. The top commercial categories for wildlife trade were seafood (82%), furniture (7%), and fashion (furs and hides) (6%). In these three major categories, vague commodity codes such as “Fish”, “Tropical wood”, and “Other furs” were used to declare 23%, 24% and 26% of items traded, respectively – despite encompassing thousands of species. This lack of granularity imperils biodiversity as trade cannot be comprehensively monitored. We recommend a review of what species are traded under these broad code descriptions, and a distillation of codes to taxonomic Family or Genus level in the next HS Code review period, particularly in the pet, traditional Chinese medicine and furniture categories. In addition, interdisciplinary research into legal wildlife trade should be increased to provide forensic, policy, economic and social solutions to improve trade management.","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2021.e01455","ISSN":"2351-9894","journalAbbreviation":"Global Ecology and Conservation","language":"en","page":"e01455","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"CITES and beyond: Illuminating 20 years of global, legal wildlife trade","title-short":"CITES and beyond","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2351989421000056","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Andersson","given":"Astrid Alexandra"},{"family":"Tilley","given":"Hannah B."},{"family":"Lau","given":"Wilson"},{"family":"Dudgeon","given":"David"},{"family":"Bonebrake","given":"Timothy C."},{"family":"Dingle","given":"Caroline"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Mediating factors of trade
Formal international wild species trade can link the high market with local indigenous/rural communities (e.g., python breeding ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ULuGF7Ih","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lyons & Natusch, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Lyons & Natusch, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":716,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/28PSAP4U"],"itemData":{"id":716,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife breeding farms have been promoted to aid biodiversity conservation by alleviating the pressure of harvest on wild populations. There is, however, growing concern that many breeding farms are being used to launder illegally caught wildlife. Surveys of wildlife traders in the Indonesian provinces of Maluku, West Papua and Papua were conducted between August 2009 and April 2011 to assess the trade of the green python (Morelia viridis), the species currently exported in the largest numbers from Indonesia declared as captive-bred. In total, 4227 illegally collected wild green pythons were recorded during surveys and high levels of harvest were found to have depleted and skewed the demographics of some island populations. Snakes were traced from their point of capture to breeding farms in Jakarta where they are to be exported for the pet trade, confirming the reports of wildlife laundering. Extrapolation of monthly collection estimates provided by traders revealed that at least 5337 green pythons are collected each year, suggesting that at least 80% of the green pythons exported from Indonesia annually are illegally wild-caught. The results of examination of 139 eggshells from five python species suggest that reptilian eggshells may be used as proof of provenance for each individual reptile exported. This method, in addition to the evidence that breeding farms play a significant role in the illegal exploitation of wildlife, allows conservation managers to begin to adequately monitor, regulate and determine the role of breeding farms in the conservation of wild populations.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2011.10.002","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"3073-3081","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Wildlife laundering through breeding farms: Illegal harvest, population declines and a means of regulating the trade of green pythons (Morelia viridis) from Indonesia","title-short":"Wildlife laundering through breeding farms","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320711003685","volume":"144","author":[{"family":"Lyons","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel J. D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; vicuña fibre trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EzCkJRI7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Lichtenstein, Gabriela, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7370,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7TN9Z64K","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/77KTC5ZS"],"itemData":{"id":7370,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of the Commons","DOI":"http://doi.org/10.18352/ijc.139","issue":"1","page":"100-121","title":"Vicuña conservation and poverty alleviation? Andean communities and international fibre markets","URL":"https://www.thecommonsjournal.org/articles/10.18352/ijc.139/","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein, Gabriela","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). Where local stakeholders benefit directly from a resource (with cash and also non cash benefits), they may have an incentive to protect it ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YDJC4cnV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Salafsky & Wollenberg, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Salafsky & Wollenberg, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32755,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N37I7L73"],"itemData":{"id":32755,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Although there has been increasing interest in trying to link the livelihoods of people living near natural resources to the conservation of those resources, there has been little attempt to systematically assess or measure this linkage. We develop a conceptual framework for defining the linkage between livelihood activities and conservation. We then develop a scale to assess the strength of linkage across five dimensions: species, habitat, spatial, temporal and conservation association. We test the framework and scale by evaluating 39 project sites in the Biodiversity Conservation Network. Finally, we discuss the relevance of linkage to designing appropriate conservation strategies.","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/S0305-750X(00)00031-0","ISSN":"0305-750X","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"World Development","language":"en","page":"1421-1438","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Linking Livelihoods and Conservation: A Conceptual Framework and Scale for Assessing the Integration of Human Needs and Biodiversity","title-short":"Linking Livelihoods and Conservation","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305750X00000310","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Salafsky","given":"Nick"},{"family":"Wollenberg","given":"Eva"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",8,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is of course only possible if the different practices and wild species trade can be regulated, monitored, controlled, and enforced. In practice, enforcing regulation turns often to be unfeasible and difficult ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yQlpd6Ny","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nielsen & Meilby, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Nielsen & Meilby, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32804,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MRPJCYI7"],"itemData":{"id":32804,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Bushmeat hunting in the savannah biomes of East Africa is often considered to be subsistence oriented and undertaken as a gap-filler in the lean agricultural season. The price of bushmeat is furthermore often thought uniform regardless of species, but if hunting is commercially oriented and price premiums are paid for particular species this needs to be considered. This paper investigates these issues in the Kilombero Valley of Tanzania, based on one year of market data and interviews with 80 hunters, 169 traders and 67 retailers. Motivations were overwhelmingly commercial and the bushmeat trade constituted a year-round income generating activity. Monte Carlo simulations based on the deterrence model revealed average cost-benefit ratios of 0.15–0.43 for hunters, 0.56–0.62 for traders and 0.88 for retailers, and a 12–401 fold increase in likelihood of apprehension may be required to render the trade unprofitable. Willingness-to-pay data showed that elephant, buffalo, hippopotamus, puku, bushpig and warthog meat were preferred. Enhanced enforcement may thus drive prices for these species higher, encouraging hunters to seek ways around constraints. Community-based wildlife management and improved firearms control may be the most pragmatic ways to regulate the trade.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892914000198","ISSN":"0376-8929, 1469-4387","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"61-72","source":"Cambridge Core","title":"Hunting and trading bushmeat in the Kilombero Valley, Tanzania: motivations, cost-benefit ratios and meat prices","title-short":"Hunting and trading bushmeat in the Kilombero Valley, Tanzania","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/environmental-conservation/article/hunting-and-trading-bushmeat-in-the-kilombero-valley-tanzania-motivations-costbenefit-ratios-and-meat-prices/DE54BF642E4FC9CFA58F2E8F79525BD1","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Nielsen","given":"Martin Reinhardt"},{"family":"Meilby","given":"Henrik"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In such case, trade bans can played overall an important role in halting unsustainable use of threatened species. One example here is the International Whaling Comission that have contributed to the recovery of many whale species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MQD3B2Q3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hurd, 2012; Roman, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Hurd, 2012; Roman, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32764,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E25PFBKB"],"itemData":{"id":32764,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2015.04.003","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"299-302","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Lifting baselines to address the consequences of conservation success","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534715000932","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Roman","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Dunphy-Daly","given":"Meagan M."},{"family":"Johnston","given":"David W."},{"family":"Read","given":"Andrew J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",6]]}}},{"id":32770,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BUNE35TZ"],"itemData":{"id":32770,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The international regulation of whaling has been a tremendous success. It has reduced whale hunting dramatically from its peak in the 1960s and brought almost all species of whales out of danger of extinction. Today, whaling conservation stands as a—or perhaps the—paradigm of a successful international regime. Yet the international organization responsible for this success is itself in such crisis that it may not survive.","container-title":"Ethics & International Affairs","DOI":"10.1017/S0892679412000081","ISSN":"1747-7093, 0892-6794","issue":"1","language":"en","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"103-112","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Almost Saving Whales: The Ambiguity of Success at the International Whaling Commission","title-short":"Almost Saving Whales","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/ethics-and-international-affairs/article/almost-saving-whales-the-ambiguity-of-success-at-the-international-whaling-commission/9A111A42AD62EC7F058BD441C02F18DC","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Hurd","given":"Ian"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]],"season":"ed"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, trade relations are salient for sustainability outcomes. if most of the revenues from trade go to outsiders (e.g. middlemen), there may be little incentive to conserve for local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"R5mEqn19","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Elsler, 2020; Natusch & Lyons, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Elsler, 2020; Natusch & Lyons, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32810,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XIMLBAZS"],"itemData":{"id":32810,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Over-exploitation of wildlife is a signi?cant threat to global biodiversity, but addressing the sustainability of harvests can be dif?cult when trade is conducted illegally. The wildlife trade is driven chie?y by consumer demand, largely in developed nations (but increasingly in Asia), and more species are traded to meet international demand for pets than for any other purpose. We surveyed traders of amphibians and reptiles in the Indonesian provinces of Maluku, West Papua and Papua between September 2010 and April 2011. We recorded 5,370 individuals representing 52 species collected solely for the pet trade. At least 44 % were either fully protected or had not been allocated a harvest quota, making their harvest and trade illegal. Approximately half were listed within the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. Trade operates via a complex chain, with hunters receiving little income compared to middlemen and exporters. Examination of Indonesian harvest quotas for amphibians and reptiles suggests limited knowledge of species distributions, with quotas often set for species in provinces where they do not occur. Illegal trade is due, partly, to an inadequate understanding of the species being traded and is facilitated by poor monitoring and enforcement at key trade hubs. As a ?rst step to combatting illegal trade, and to better understand the effects of harvest on wild populations, we recommend the need for increased monitoring and enforcement, improving the knowledge base of species traded and educating consumers about the effects their demand for pets has on these species.","container-title":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"15","source":"Zotero","title":"Exploited for pets: the harvest and trade of amphibians and reptiles from Indonesian New Guinea","author":[{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel J D"},{"family":"Lyons","given":"Jessica A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":32708,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MM34WMQ5"],"itemData":{"id":32708,"type":"book","event-place":"Stockholm","ISBN":"978-91-7911-192-2","language":"English","number-of-pages":"64","publisher":"Stockholm Resilience Centre, Stockholm University","publisher-place":"Stockholm","title":"The complexity of seafood trade relations across scales","URL":"https://stockholmuniversity.app.box.com/s/pzq9wqk3csawv6bclizcg8dejl327lvz","author":[{"family":"Elsler","given":"Laura G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9,9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Appropriately governed, trade may provide incentives to relevant local stakeholders to conserve and generate economic support to area-based conservation initiatives ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jmS7x9Uw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u8216{}t Sas-Rolfes, 2019b)","plainCitation":"(‘t Sas-Rolfes, 2019b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33958,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2XMEHQUI"],"itemData":{"id":33958,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Illegal wildlife trade (IWT) has increased in profile in recent years as a global policy issue, largely because of its association with declines in prominent internationally trafficked species. In this review, we explore the scale of IWT, associated threats to biodiversity, and appropriate responses to these threats. We discuss the historical development of IWT research and highlight the uncertainties that plague the evidence base, emphasizing the need for more systematic approaches to addressing evidence gaps in a way that minimizes the risk of unethical or counterproductive outcomes for wildlife and people. We highlight the need for evaluating interventions in order to learn, and the importance of sharing datasets and lessons learned. A more collaborative approach to linking IWT research, practice, and policy would better align public policy discourse and action with research evidence. This in turn would enable more effective policy making that contributes to reducing the threat to biodiversity that IWT represents.","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033253","ISSN":"1543-5938, 1545-2050","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Environ. Resour.","language":"en","page":"201-228","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Illegal Wildlife Trade: Scale, Processes, and Governance","title-short":"Illegal Wildlife Trade","URL":"https://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033253","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"‘t Sas-Rolfes","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Challender","given":"Daniel W.S."},{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Diogo"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the mere fact that conservation would be beneficial to local communities, does not imply that strong incentives to conserve exist and conservation materializes. A study on the wildlife trade in Madagascar revealed that the households who are most dependent on the resource (and were expected to have the strongest interest to conserve) did not have different perception on conservation or were more inclined to take conservation efforts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Yvn92ewA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Robinson 2018)","plainCitation":"(J. Robinson 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32794,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NBSPDPPR"],"itemData":{"id":32794,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Robinson, J. E., R. A. Griffiths, I. M. Fraser, J. Raharimalala, D. L. Roberts, and F. A. V. St. John. 2018. Supplying the wildlife trade as a livelihood strategy in a biodiversity hotspot. Ecology and Society 23(1):13. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-09821-230113","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-09821-230113","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"© 2018 by the author(s)","source":"www.ecologyandsociety.org","title":"Supplying the wildlife trade as a livelihood strategy in a biodiversity hotspot","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol23/iss1/art13/","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"Janine"},{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Fraser","given":"Iain"},{"family":"Raharimalala","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David"},{"family":"St. John","given":"Freya"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",1,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the Columbian Amazon, most hunters now primarily hunt for subsistence, with only little pressure on wild species, but also little incentives to conserve ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JwgwSyvQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ponta, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Ponta, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":754,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DWNBG6CH"],"itemData":{"id":754,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Despite growing industrialization, the shift to cash economy and natural resources overexploitation, indigenous people of the Amazon region hunt and trade wildlife in order to meet their livelihood requirements. Individual strategies, shaped by the hunters’ values and expectations, are changing in response to the region’s economic development but still face the contrasting challenges of poverty and overhunting. For conservation initiatives to be implemented effectively, it is crucial to take into account people’s strategies with their underlying drivers and their adaptive capabilities within a transforming socio-economic environment. To uncover hunting strategies in the Colombian Amazon and their evolution under the current transition, we co-designed a role-playing game together with the local stakeholders. The game revolves around the tension between ecological sustainability and food security—hunters’ current main concern. It simulates the mosaic of activities that indigenous people perform in the wet and dry season, while also allowing for specific hunting strategies. Socio-economic conditions change while the game unfolds, opening up to emerging alternative potential scenarios suggested by the stakeholders themselves. Do hunters give up hunting when given the opportunity of an alternative income and protein source? Do institutional changes affect their livelihoods? We played the game between October and December 2016 with 39 players—all of them hunters—from 9 different communities within the Ticoya reserve. Our results show that providing alternatives would decrease overall hunting effort but impacts are not spatially homogenous. Legalizing trade could lead to overhunting except when market rules and competition come into place. When it comes to coupled human-nature systems, the best way forward to produce socially just and resilient conservation strategies might be to trigger an adaptive process of experiential learning and scenario exploration. The use of games as “boundary objects” can guide stakeholders through the process, eliciting the plurality of their strategies, their drivers and how outside change affect them.","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.3389/fevo.2019.00034","ISSN":"2296-701X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Ecol. Evol.","language":"English","note":"publisher: Frontiers","source":"Frontiers","title":"Hunting in Times of Change: Uncovering Indigenous Strategies in the Colombian Amazon Using a Role-Playing Game","title-short":"Hunting in Times of Change","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fevo.2019.00034/full","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Ponta","given":"Nicole"},{"family":"Cornioley","given":"Tina"},{"family":"Dray","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Waeber","given":"Patrick O."},{"family":"Garcia","given":"Claude A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While trade could be part of a strategy to incentivize conservation and also sustainable harvesting, there is also the risk that hunting rates outpace any efforts to implement conservation programs and rules  regulating access and sharing of benefits. Hence, to ensure ecological and economic sustainability trade should go hand in hand with clear rules and regulations, ideally co-designed and co-enforced by local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1ijTvaFJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ponta, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Ponta, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":754,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DWNBG6CH"],"itemData":{"id":754,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Despite growing industrialization, the shift to cash economy and natural resources overexploitation, indigenous people of the Amazon region hunt and trade wildlife in order to meet their livelihood requirements. Individual strategies, shaped by the hunters’ values and expectations, are changing in response to the region’s economic development but still face the contrasting challenges of poverty and overhunting. For conservation initiatives to be implemented effectively, it is crucial to take into account people’s strategies with their underlying drivers and their adaptive capabilities within a transforming socio-economic environment. To uncover hunting strategies in the Colombian Amazon and their evolution under the current transition, we co-designed a role-playing game together with the local stakeholders. The game revolves around the tension between ecological sustainability and food security—hunters’ current main concern. It simulates the mosaic of activities that indigenous people perform in the wet and dry season, while also allowing for specific hunting strategies. Socio-economic conditions change while the game unfolds, opening up to emerging alternative potential scenarios suggested by the stakeholders themselves. Do hunters give up hunting when given the opportunity of an alternative income and protein source? Do institutional changes affect their livelihoods? We played the game between October and December 2016 with 39 players—all of them hunters—from 9 different communities within the Ticoya reserve. Our results show that providing alternatives would decrease overall hunting effort but impacts are not spatially homogenous. Legalizing trade could lead to overhunting except when market rules and competition come into place. When it comes to coupled human-nature systems, the best way forward to produce socially just and resilient conservation strategies might be to trigger an adaptive process of experiential learning and scenario exploration. The use of games as “boundary objects” can guide stakeholders through the process, eliciting the plurality of their strategies, their drivers and how outside change affect them.","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.3389/fevo.2019.00034","ISSN":"2296-701X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Ecol. Evol.","language":"English","note":"publisher: Frontiers","source":"Frontiers","title":"Hunting in Times of Change: Uncovering Indigenous Strategies in the Colombian Amazon Using a Role-Playing Game","title-short":"Hunting in Times of Change","URL":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fevo.2019.00034/full","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Ponta","given":"Nicole"},{"family":"Cornioley","given":"Tina"},{"family":"Dray","given":"Anne"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Waeber","given":"Patrick O."},{"family":"Garcia","given":"Claude A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	If – and only if – trade generates benefits to local communities, it may promote rural development, contribute to avoid rural migration, return equitable profits from nature conservation to local communities, catalyze community investments in nature conservation, law enforcement and stewardship of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ycciwn7v","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cooney, 2015; Jaramillo Castro, Lorena, 2012; Roe, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Cooney, 2015; Jaramillo Castro, Lorena, 2012; Roe, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":718,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8ICTGYPC"],"itemData":{"id":718,"type":"book","event-place":"New York","publisher":"UN","publisher-place":"New York","title":"Trade and biodiversity : the BioTrade experiences in Latin America","URL":"https://www.google.com/search?client=firefox-b-d&q=Trade+and+biodiversity+%3A+the+BioTrade+experiences+in+Latin+America+","author":[{"literal":"Jaramillo Castro, Lorena"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":32784,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8B4IJ2XW"],"itemData":{"id":32784,"type":"book","publisher":"International Trade Centre, Geneva, Switzerland","title":"The trade in wildlife: a framework to improve biodiversity and livelihood outcomes","author":[{"family":"Cooney","given":"R."},{"family":"Kasterine","given":"A."},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"D."},{"family":"Milledge","given":"S."},{"family":"Nossal","given":"K."},{"family":"Roe","given":"D."},{"family":"S.,’t Sas-Rolfes","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":32737,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FHIKSHXS"],"itemData":{"id":32737,"type":"book","abstract":"Provides a pan-African synthesis of community-based natural resource management (CBNRM), drawing on multiple authors and a wide range of documented experiences from Southern, Eastern, Western and Central Africa. This title discusses the degree to which CBNRM has met poverty alleviation, economic development and nature conservation objectives. More than twenty years have passed since community-based natural resource management (CBNRM) rose to prominence in different parts of Africa as a strategy for rural development, local empowerment, and conservation. Led by new ideas about the merits of decentralized, collective resource governance regimes, and creative field experiments such as Zimbabwe's CAMPFIRE, these community-based approaches evolved in a wide range of ecological, political, and social contexts across Africa. This review provides an unprecedented pan-African synthesis of CBNRM, drawing on multiple authors and a wide range of documented experiences from Southern, Eastern, Western and Central Africa. The review discusses the degree to which CBNRM has met poverty alleviation, economic development and nature conservation objectives. In its concluding chapter, the report suggests a way forward for strengthening CBNRM and addressing key challenges in the years ahead.","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-84369-755-8","language":"en","note":"OCLC: 731367128","publisher":"IIED","publisher-place":"London","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Community management of natural resources in Africa: impacts, experiences and future directions","title-short":"Community management of natural resources in Africa","author":[{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; but see ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bu6cyslP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2018)","plainCitation":"(2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32774,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9KGDPIL5"],"itemData":{"id":32774,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Advances in Social Sciences Research Journal","issue":"8","page":"522–533","source":"Google Scholar","title":"The dilemma on reconceptualising natural resources in CAMPFIRE Areas in Zimbabwe","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Dzvimbo","given":"Munyaradzi A."},{"family":"Monga","given":"Monica"},{"family":"Magijani","given":"Freddy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Therefore, wild species trade can give incentives to conserve habitat and species, potentially leading to species recovery (e.g. crocodiles in Australia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"U445cDAw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fukuda, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Fukuda, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31123,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/49SMVY53"],"itemData":{"id":31123,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Saltwater crocodiles (Crocodylus porosus) in the Northern Territory of Australia were protected in 1971, after a severe population decline resulting from 26 yr of intense commercial hunting. By that time wild saltwater crocodiles were rarely sighted anywhere and they were commercially extinct in areas where they had once been abundant. Standardized monitoring by spotlight surveys started in 1975 and provided relative density indices over time (1975-2009) as a unique record of the post-protection recovery of a wild crocodilian population. We examined the survey data for populations at 12 major tidal rivers, individually and as a single subpopulation. The pattern of recovery in the subpopulation in both abundance and biomass was approximated by logistic curves, predicting 5.26 non-hatchling crocodiles weighing 387.64 kg sighted per kilometer of river in 2010. We predicted potential carrying capacity as 5.58 non-hatchling crocodiles (5.73% higher than 2010) weighing 519.0 kg (25.31% higher than 2010). Individual rivers showed largely different abundance and biomass among rivers. The statistical model that best described the recovery in individual rivers was not always logistic. However, where it was logistic, expected carrying capacity of different rivers showed considerable variation in abundance and biomass. The variation indicates different habitat quality among the rivers. Recovery occurred despite various consumptive uses, particularly a widespread egg-harvest program, which has been an integral part of the incentive-driven conservation program for saltwater crocodiles in the Northern Territory since 1983. We suggest that the saltwater crocodile population of the Northern Territory is achieving full recovery from uncontrolled hunting in 1945-1971. Although saltwater crocodiles are considered an important natural resource, their increase in number, size, and distribution is posing management issues for public safety. Continuation of human-crocodile conflict management through public education and strategic removal of problem crocodiles will be essential.","container-title":"Journal of Wildlife Management","DOI":"10.1002/jwmg.191","ISSN":"0022-541X","issue":"6","page":"1253-1266","source":"Charles Darwin University's Research Webportal","title":"Recovery of saltwater crocodiles following unregulated hunting in tidal rivers of the Northern Territory, Australia","volume":"75","author":[{"family":"Fukuda","given":"Yusuke"},{"family":"Webb","given":"Grahame"},{"family":"Manolis","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Delaney","given":"Robyn"},{"family":"Letnic","given":"Mike"},{"family":"Lindner","given":"Garry"},{"family":"Whitehead","given":"Peter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the Amazonian pirarucu ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mvhS7gWT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Campos-Silva & Peres, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Campos-Silva & Peres, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":11559,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GH5F3F3L","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PZ8SCRFZ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CVPJDIXP"],"itemData":{"id":11559,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep34745","ISSN":"2045-2322","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","language":"en","page":"34745","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Community-based management induces rapid recovery of a high-value tropical freshwater fishery","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/srep34745","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Campos-Silva","given":"João Vitor"},{"family":"Peres","given":"Carlos A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}). Empowering local people to capture legal benefits from wild species trade can be an important step in reducing excessive illegal harvests, when efforts to provide alternative livelihoods are unsuccessful ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ie1Qoprm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ripple, Abernethy,, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Ripple, Abernethy,, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32514,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/99SRFGYK"],"itemData":{"id":32514,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Terrestrial mammals are experiencing a massive collapse in their population sizes and geographical ranges around the world, but many of the drivers, patterns and consequences of this decline remain poorly understood. Here we provide an analysis showing that bushmeat hunting for mostly food and medicinal products is driving a global crisis whereby 301 terrestrial mammal species are threatened with extinction. Nearly all of these threatened species occur in developing countries where major coexisting threats include deforestation, agricultural expansion, human encroachment and competition with livestock. The unrelenting decline of mammals suggests many vital ecological and socio-economic services that these species provide will be lost, potentially changing ecosystems irrevocably. We discuss options and current obstacles to achieving effective conservation, alongside consequences of failure to stem such anthropogenic mammalian extirpation. We propose a multi-pronged conservation strategy to help save threatened mammals from immediate extinction and avoid a collapse of food security for hundreds of millions of people.","container-title":"Royal Society Open Science","DOI":"10.1098/rsos.160498","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Royal Society Open Science","note":"publisher: Royal Society","page":"160498","source":"royalsocietypublishing.org (Atypon)","title":"Bushmeat hunting and extinction risk to the world's mammals","URL":"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/full/10.1098/rsos.160498","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Ripple","given":"William J."},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"Katharine"},{"family":"Betts","given":"Matthew G."},{"family":"Chapron","given":"Guillaume"},{"family":"Dirzo","given":"Rodolfo"},{"family":"Galetti","given":"Mauro"},{"family":"Levi","given":"Taal"},{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter A."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."},{"family":"Machovina","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Newsome","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Peres","given":"Carlos A."},{"family":"Wallach","given":"Arian D."},{"family":"Wolf","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Young","given":"Hillary"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      Local livelihood outcomes of wild species trade may provide incentives for conservation but also for  overexploitation and extirpation (e.g. endangered frog M cowani in Madagascar ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8IirUWAr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Andreone, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Andreone, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":121,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3EGVYL4D"],"itemData":{"id":121,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Natura","issue":"2","page":"81–96","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Between environmental degradation and international pet trade: conservation strategies for the threatened amphibians of Madagascar","title-short":"Between environmental degradation and international pet trade","volume":"95","author":[{"family":"Andreone","given":"Franco"},{"family":"Mercurio","given":"Vincenzo"},{"family":"Mattioli","given":"Fabio"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, parrot harvest (Ara ararauna, Ara macao and Amazona amazonica) in Peru ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jgNaWdce","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gonz\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}lez, 2003)","plainCitation":"(González, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32754,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/73A2EPXF"],"itemData":{"id":32754,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In some places of the northeastern Peruvian Amazon the harvesting and local trade of parrot nestlings is still a common practice (loreada) that takes place every year between February and April, despite being banned by the national laws. Between 1996 and 1999, I monitored the use of these birds in 3890-ha of Mauritia flexuosa-palm swamps, located close to the village of Victoria (Loreto, Peru). Seven species of parrots were collected by local poachers in the study area, with Amazona amazonica (61.1% of the captures) and Ara ararauna (25.9%) the most commonly harvested. The total number of nestlings taken during the 4-year study period was 1718, ranging from 680 birds harvested in 1996 to 166 in 1998. The two methods used to collect nestlings, cutting down the nesting tree or opening a hole in the trunk to reach the nest cavity, are not sustainable because nest-trees become useless and the next generation is completely removed. The analyses of demographic data and annual harvest rates suggest that at least three species (Ara ararauna, Ara macao and Amazona amazonica) are being over-harvested and may be seriously threatened in the long term.\\nResumen\\nEn algunos lugares de la Amazon??a Peruana la cosecha y comercialización de pichones de loros y guacamayos constituye una importante actividad económica (loreada) que tiene lugar entre los meses de febrero y abril de cada año, a pesar de las prohibiciones legales existentes. Entre los años 1996 y 1999, se ha estudiado el uso de estas aves en un área de aguajales (pantanos de Mauritia flexuosa; 3890 ha) cercana a la comunidad de Victoria (Loreto, Perú). Siete especies de loros y guacamayos fueron extra??das en esta zona destacando, por su cantidad e importancia económica, Amazona amazonica (61.1% de las capturas) y Ara ararauna (25.9%). El número total de pichones cosechados durante los cuatro años de estudio fue de 1718, oscilando entre las 680 aves colectadas en 1996 y las 166 colectadas en 1998. Los dos métodos de extracción utilizados, la tala rasa del tronco-nido o la apertura de un hueco en el mismo, no resultan sostenibles en el tiempo ya que agotan progresivamente los sustratos de anidación y eliminan todos los pichones de cada nido. Los análisis de datos demográficos y tasas de captura anual sugieren que al menos tres especies (Ara ararauna, Ara macao y Amazona amazonica) están siendo cosechadas por encima de los niveles máximos de sostenibilidad, poniendo en peligro su conservación a largo plazo.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/S0006-3207(03)00071-5","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"437-446","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Harvesting, local trade, and conservation of parrots in the Northeastern Peruvian Amazon","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320703000715","volume":"114","author":[{"family":"González","given":"José A"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, plants, algae and fungi gathering ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"f0Her1MK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kusters, 2006; Lybbert, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Kusters, 2006; Lybbert, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32783,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DZJ7RMR6"],"itemData":{"id":32783,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"2","note":"publisher: JSTOR","title":"Balancing development and conservation? An assessment of livelihood and environmental outcomes of nontimber forest product trade in Asia, Africa, and Latin America","URL":"http://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol11/iss2/art20/","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Kusters","given":"Koen"},{"family":"Achdiawan","given":"Ramadhani"},{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Pérez","given":"Manuel Ruiz"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}},{"id":30582,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S2NUHSXZ"],"itemData":{"id":30582,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Morocco's argan oil is now the most expensive edible oil in the world. High-value argan markets have sparked a bonanza of argan activity. Nongovernmental organizations, international and domestic development agencies, and argan oil cooperatives aggressively promote the win–win aim of simultaneously benefiting local people and the health of the argan forest. This paper tests some of these win–win claims. Analysis of a panel of detailed household data suggests that the boom has enabled some rural households to increase consumption, increase their goat herds (which bodes poorly for the argan forest), and send their girls to secondary school. The boom has predictably made households vigilant guardians of fruit on the tree, but it has not incited investments in longer term tree and forest health. We evaluate landscape-level impacts of these changes using commune-level data on educational enrollment and normalized difference vegetation index data over the period from 1981 to 2009. The results of the mesoanalysis of enrollment are consistent with the microanalysis: the argan boom seems to have improved educational outcomes, especially for girls. Our normalized difference vegetation index analysis, however, suggests that booming argan prices have not improved the forest and may have even induced degradation. We conclude by exploring the dynamic interactions between argan markets, local institutions, rural household welfare, and forest conservation and sustainability.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1106382108","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"34","journalAbbreviation":"PNAS","language":"en","note":"PMID: 21873185","page":"13963-13968","source":"www.pnas.org","title":"Booming markets for Moroccan argan oil appear to benefit some rural households while threatening the endemic argan forest","URL":"https://www.pnas.org/content/108/34/13963","volume":"108","author":[{"family":"Lybbert","given":"Travis J."},{"family":"Aboudrare","given":"Abdellah"},{"family":"Chaloud","given":"Deborah"},{"family":"Magnan","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"Nash","given":"Maliha"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",8,23]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Balancing livelihood outcomes and conservation goals results in trade-offs, and sometimes it is challenging to reconcile both objectives (e.g. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DihkRANM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Robinson 2018)","plainCitation":"(Robinson 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32794,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NBSPDPPR"],"itemData":{"id":32794,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Robinson, J. E., R. A. Griffiths, I. M. Fraser, J. Raharimalala, D. L. Roberts, and F. A. V. St. John. 2018. Supplying the wildlife trade as a livelihood strategy in a biodiversity hotspot. Ecology and Society 23(1):13. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-09821-230113","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-09821-230113","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"© 2018 by the author(s)","source":"www.ecologyandsociety.org","title":"Supplying the wildlife trade as a livelihood strategy in a biodiversity hotspot","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol23/iss1/art13/","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"Janine"},{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Fraser","given":"Iain"},{"family":"Raharimalala","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David"},{"family":"St. John","given":"Freya"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",1,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}).         
 International demands, and high market value of traded species may encourage intensified exploitation, intensification or captive breeding, causing in some situations resource stocks to decline ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AI9vNxxz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Fischer, 2010)","plainCitation":"(C. Fischer, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":15793,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/75SERA2T","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E7CZQAYK"],"itemData":{"id":15793,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Trade exerts important influences on the exploitation and protection of natural resources. Indeed, recognition of this influence is codified in the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade, which allows exceptions to treaty obligations for measures “relating to the conservation of exhaustible natural resources,” motivates the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species, and underlies the Convention on Biological Diversity. Trade impacts operate through several channels. Trade liberalization changes relative prices, which affects exploitation incentives. Trade can also have broader effects, such as impacts on labor markets and incomes, which may affect demand for resource-intensive products—or for ecosystem services. Trade interacts with, and can influence, the institutions governing the management of natural resources. Finally, trade can also introduce threats to ecosystems, in the form of invasive species. All of these potential impacts pose special challenges for the conservation of renewable resources, which inherently involves dynamic economic and ecological processes. This article reviews and takes stock of the lessons from the recent economics literature on the links between trade and the conservation of natural resources.","container-title":"Review of Environmental Economics and Policy","DOI":"10.1093/reep/rep023","ISSN":"1750-6816","issue":"1","note":"publisher: The University of Chicago Press","page":"103-121","source":"journals.uchicago.edu (Atypon)","title":"Does Trade Help or Hinder the Conservation of Natural                         Resources?","URL":"https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/full/10.1093/reep/rep023","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Fischer","given":"Carolyn"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Moving from wild harvest to intensive management systems, including captive breeding for animals and cultivation, plantations and/or artificial propagation for plants, fungi and algae, can create benefits for or risks to conservation and livelihoods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FFhiUyaM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cooney, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Cooney, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32784,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8B4IJ2XW"],"itemData":{"id":32784,"type":"book","publisher":"International Trade Centre, Geneva, Switzerland","title":"The trade in wildlife: a framework to improve biodiversity and livelihood outcomes","author":[{"family":"Cooney","given":"R."},{"family":"Kasterine","given":"A."},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"D."},{"family":"Milledge","given":"S."},{"family":"Nossal","given":"K."},{"family":"Roe","given":"D."},{"family":"S.,’t Sas-Rolfes","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Concerns over the conservation, animal welfare, and local livelihoods impact of captive breeding were raised for several species. Captive breeding provides little or no incentive for in situ management and conservation (e.g. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"F6R4tG4T","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lichtenstein & Vil\\\\uc0\\\\u225{}, 2003; Lyons & Natusch, 2011; Natusch & Lyons, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Lichtenstein & Vilá, 2003; Lyons & Natusch, 2011; Natusch & Lyons, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":24,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SYXFDIW","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RE4E67DM"],"itemData":{"id":24,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mountain Research and Development","DOI":"10.1659/0276-4741(2003)023[0197:VUBACA]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"0276-4741, 1994-7151","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Mountain Research and Development","language":"en","page":"198-201","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Vicuna Use by Andean Communities: An Overview","title-short":"Vicuna Use by Andean Communities","URL":"http://www.bioone.org/doi/abs/10.1659/0276-4741%282003%29023%5B0197%3AVUBACA%5D2.0.CO%3B2","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"},{"family":"Vilá","given":"Bibiana"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",5]]}}},{"id":716,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/28PSAP4U"],"itemData":{"id":716,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife breeding farms have been promoted to aid biodiversity conservation by alleviating the pressure of harvest on wild populations. There is, however, growing concern that many breeding farms are being used to launder illegally caught wildlife. Surveys of wildlife traders in the Indonesian provinces of Maluku, West Papua and Papua were conducted between August 2009 and April 2011 to assess the trade of the green python (Morelia viridis), the species currently exported in the largest numbers from Indonesia declared as captive-bred. In total, 4227 illegally collected wild green pythons were recorded during surveys and high levels of harvest were found to have depleted and skewed the demographics of some island populations. Snakes were traced from their point of capture to breeding farms in Jakarta where they are to be exported for the pet trade, confirming the reports of wildlife laundering. Extrapolation of monthly collection estimates provided by traders revealed that at least 5337 green pythons are collected each year, suggesting that at least 80% of the green pythons exported from Indonesia annually are illegally wild-caught. The results of examination of 139 eggshells from five python species suggest that reptilian eggshells may be used as proof of provenance for each individual reptile exported. This method, in addition to the evidence that breeding farms play a significant role in the illegal exploitation of wildlife, allows conservation managers to begin to adequately monitor, regulate and determine the role of breeding farms in the conservation of wild populations.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2011.10.002","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"3073-3081","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Wildlife laundering through breeding farms: Illegal harvest, population declines and a means of regulating the trade of green pythons (Morelia viridis) from Indonesia","title-short":"Wildlife laundering through breeding farms","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320711003685","volume":"144","author":[{"family":"Lyons","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel J. D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12,1]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32810,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XIMLBAZS"],"itemData":{"id":32810,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Over-exploitation of wildlife is a signi?cant threat to global biodiversity, but addressing the sustainability of harvests can be dif?cult when trade is conducted illegally. The wildlife trade is driven chie?y by consumer demand, largely in developed nations (but increasingly in Asia), and more species are traded to meet international demand for pets than for any other purpose. We surveyed traders of amphibians and reptiles in the Indonesian provinces of Maluku, West Papua and Papua between September 2010 and April 2011. We recorded 5,370 individuals representing 52 species collected solely for the pet trade. At least 44 % were either fully protected or had not been allocated a harvest quota, making their harvest and trade illegal. Approximately half were listed within the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. Trade operates via a complex chain, with hunters receiving little income compared to middlemen and exporters. Examination of Indonesian harvest quotas for amphibians and reptiles suggests limited knowledge of species distributions, with quotas often set for species in provinces where they do not occur. Illegal trade is due, partly, to an inadequate understanding of the species being traded and is facilitated by poor monitoring and enforcement at key trade hubs. As a ?rst step to combatting illegal trade, and to better understand the effects of harvest on wild populations, we recommend the need for increased monitoring and enforcement, improving the knowledge base of species traded and educating consumers about the effects their demand for pets has on these species.","container-title":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"15","source":"Zotero","title":"Exploited for pets: the harvest and trade of amphibians and reptiles from Indonesian New Guinea","author":[{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel J D"},{"family":"Lyons","given":"Jessica A"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}; it may create incentives for converting natural habitats ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"LQ3Sxpys","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Weinstein & Moegenburg, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Weinstein & Moegenburg, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32753,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3NW457PR"],"itemData":{"id":32753,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In the late 1980s, the açaí (Euterpe oleracea) fruit and palmito extraction system of eastern Amazonia was heralded as a promising alternative to deforestation that could simultaneously provide income to rural producers and protect forest integrity. We tested these claims in five communities located along a distance gradient from the largest regional market in Belém, Brazil. We evaluated the market accessibility and management strategies of açaí producers, and assessed the impacts of management on forest characteristics. In contrast to other NTFP systems, we found that distance to the major market is not a limiting factor for açaí sales because throughout the region intermediaries are readily available to transport açaí from producer to market. Demand for açaí fruit is increasing, leading to intensification of palm management, which results in the conversion of native floodplain forests into açaí-dominated forests that closely resemble plantations. We conclude that the açaí system is not typical of other NTFP and should not be regarded as a model for merging forest conservation with rural development. However, the increased demand for açaí, especially from educated consumers, together with the ease of production and marketing, present an opportunity to develop the açaí system into one in which both rural livelihoods and forest integrity are supported.","container-title":"Conservation and Society","ISSN":"0972-4923","issue":"2","note":"publisher: [Ashoka Trust for Research in Ecology and the Environment, Wolters Kluwer India Pvt. Ltd.]","page":"315-346","source":"JSTOR","title":"Açaí Palm Management in the Amazon Estuary: Course for Conservation or Passage to Plantations?","title-short":"Açaí Palm Management in the Amazon Estuary","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/26396631","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Weinstein","given":"Stephanie"},{"family":"Moegenburg","given":"Susan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, depleting wild populations to secure breeding stock and reduces incentives for in situ management and conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Vxcaerad","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cooney, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Cooney, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32784,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8B4IJ2XW"],"itemData":{"id":32784,"type":"book","publisher":"International Trade Centre, Geneva, Switzerland","title":"The trade in wildlife: a framework to improve biodiversity and livelihood outcomes","author":[{"family":"Cooney","given":"R."},{"family":"Kasterine","given":"A."},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"D."},{"family":"Milledge","given":"S."},{"family":"Nossal","given":"K."},{"family":"Roe","given":"D."},{"family":"S.,’t Sas-Rolfes","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Although international wild species trade is a big business, the distribution of benefits along the commodity chain remains usually uneven, with resource owners and users receiving only a fraction compared to intermediaries and retailers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nEbtLSTx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Jenkins, 2002; A. Kasterine & Lichtenstein, 2018)","plainCitation":"(M. Jenkins, 2002; A. Kasterine & Lichtenstein, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32752,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XMLD3IMB"],"itemData":{"id":32752,"type":"book","publisher":"Fauna & Flora International Cambridge","source":"Google Scholar","title":"International trade in African blackwood","author":[{"family":"Jenkins","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Oldfield","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Aylett","given":"Tiffany"},{"family":"Jenkins","given":"M."},{"family":"Oldfield","given":"S."},{"family":"Aylett","given":"T."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":32352,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XP2T8FRZ"],"itemData":{"id":32352,"type":"book","event-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","publisher":"International Trade Centre","publisher-place":"Geneva, Switzerland","title":"Trade in Vicuña: the Implications for Conservation and Rural Livelihoods.","author":[{"family":"Kasterine","given":"A"},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"Gabriela"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Interventions aimed at enhancing benefits to local communities, and minimizing impacts on collected species, could be considered to promote opportunities from the trade ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"H79UtB7d","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Robinson, 2018)","plainCitation":"(J. Robinson, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32794,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NBSPDPPR"],"itemData":{"id":32794,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Robinson, J. E., R. A. Griffiths, I. M. Fraser, J. Raharimalala, D. L. Roberts, and F. A. V. St. John. 2018. Supplying the wildlife trade as a livelihood strategy in a biodiversity hotspot. Ecology and Society 23(1):13. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-09821-230113","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-09821-230113","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"© 2018 by the author(s)","source":"www.ecologyandsociety.org","title":"Supplying the wildlife trade as a livelihood strategy in a biodiversity hotspot","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol23/iss1/art13/","volume":"23","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"Janine"},{"family":"Griffiths","given":"Richard"},{"family":"Fraser","given":"Iain"},{"family":"Raharimalala","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David"},{"family":"St. John","given":"Freya"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",1,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Informal wild species trade within countries, i.e. domestic, contributes to food security for millions of people, particularly in developing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"r8oFN88D","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cawthorn & Hoffman, 2015; Coad, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Cawthorn & Hoffman, 2015; Coad, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33014,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/99IMT4WE"],"itemData":{"id":33014,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wild meat or ‘bushmeat’ has long served as a principal source of protein and a key contributor to the food security of millions of people across the developing world, most notably in Africa, Latin America and Asia. More recently, however, growing human populations, technological elaborations and the emergence of a booming commercial bushmeat trade have culminated in unprecedented harvest rates and the consequent decline of numerous wildlife populations. Most research efforts aimed at tackling this problem to date have been rooted in the biological disciplines, focused on quantifying the trade and measuring its level of destruction on wildlife and ecosystems. Comparatively little effort, on the other hand, has been expended on illuminating the role of bushmeat in human livelihoods and in providing alternative sources of food and income, as well as the infrastructure to make these feasible. This paper aims to shift the focus to the human dimension, emphasising the true contributions of bushmeat to food security, nutrition and well-being, while balancing this perspective by considering the far-reaching impacts of overexploitation. What emerges from this synthesis is that bushmeat management will ultimately depend on understanding and working with people, with any approaches focused too narrowly on biodiversity preservation running the risk of failure in the long term. If wildlife is to survive and be utilised in the future, there is undoubtedly a need to relax adherence to unswerving biocentric or anthropocentric convictions, to appreciate the necessity for certain trade-offs and to develop integrated and ?exible approaches that reconcile the requirements of both the animals and the people.","container-title":"Food Research International","DOI":"10.1016/j.foodres.2015.03.025","ISSN":"09639969","journalAbbreviation":"Food Research International","language":"en","page":"906-925","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The bushmeat and food security nexus: A global account of the contributions, conundrums and ethical collisions","title-short":"The bushmeat and food security nexus","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0963996915001301","volume":"76","author":[{"family":"Cawthorn","given":"Donna-Mareè"},{"family":"Hoffman","given":"Louwrens C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",10]]}}},{"id":327,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/97LTS7HN"],"itemData":{"id":327,"type":"book","event-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","ISBN":"978-602-387-083-7","note":"DOI: 10.17528/cifor/007046","publisher":"CIFOR","publisher-place":"Bogor, Indonesia","title":"Towards a sustainable, participatory and inclusive wild meat sector","URL":"https://www.cifor.org/online-library/browse/view-publication/publication/7046.html","author":[{"family":"Coad","given":"L"},{"family":"Fa","given":"JE"},{"family":"Abernethy","given":"K"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"N","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Santamaria","given":"C"},{"family":"Wilkie","given":"D"},{"family":"El Bizri","given":"HR"},{"family":"Ingram","given":"DJ"},{"family":"Cawthorn","given":"DM"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Despite its lack of recognition in national level accounting, rural people, including indigenous peoples and local communities , rely on trading wild resources, by selling and consuming wild meat, fish, insects and plants, extracting timber and forest products, and many other activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Zyl6eAeB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Roe, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Roe, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32736,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AZ7QRL4N"],"itemData":{"id":32736,"type":"document","abstract":"For years now, trophy hunting and tourism have been the two main ways for many communities across Africa to generate revenue from wildlife. The benefi ts generated by these activities have provided signifi cant conservation incentives for people to share land with sometimes dangerous wildlife. The result has been large-scale conservation and often dramatic restoration of wildlife populations and habitat[1] in many areas.[2]\\nToday, both these ways of generating revenue from wildlife on communal land face a challenging future, with potentially substantial consequences for the conservation of iconic species and entire ecosystems.\\nTrophy hunting is under increased pressure from animal protection activists and organisations, \\nand is being increasingly challenged on ethical and welfare grounds in developed countries – where most buyers of hunting permits live. Even science-based conservation organisations, which point to the evidence of signifi cant revenue and conservation benefi ts in specifi c cases, fi nd their arguments sidelined in the face of concerted, and often very emotional campaigns[3]. In a world increasingly dominated by the simplicity and reach of social media, technocratic responses are ineffectual and can put the membership and revenue of conservation organisations at risk. As such campaigns gain ground, critical fi nancial incentives for conserving wildlife across communal and private lands are being lost. \\nPhotographic and ‘eco-’tourism are often cited as possible ways to fi ll this revenue gap. There are some notable examples of community-private sector partnerships that generate signifi cant benefi ts for communities from this kind of tourism. Yet that too faces challenges. \\nFirst, photo-tourism is not viable in many areas due to their remoteness and relative paucity of scenery and wildlife. Second, poorly managed tourism can itself have signifi cant negative impacts on wildlife populations, such as disrupting breeding and feeding patterns[4]. Badly managed tourism has also had wider environmental, social and cultural effects, including pressure on scarce water resources, littering, and cultural exploitation. Third, tourism is a notoriously unreliable industry, and vulnerable to perceptions of risk linked to disease outbreaks (sometimes far away on the same continent), economic and political instability, as well as the potential for local saturation of the market. The industry is also largely reliant on tourists fl ying in from long distance and producing large amounts of greenhouse gas emissions, challenging  ‘eco’ credentials. As a result, while tourism is often presented as a straightforward replacement for trophy hunting and a form of nonconsumptive use of wildlife, it is not a panacea either in terms of its revenues or its impacts.","call-number":"Esub44","language":"English","title":"Diversifying local livelihoods while sustaining wildlife. Exploring incentives for community-based conservation.","author":[{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Booker","given":"Francesca"},{"family":"Wilson-Holt","given":"Olivia"},{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Domestic trade can support the survival of traditional knowledge and culture by linking local communities in local/regional markets ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tAFj4LqC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Tinitana, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Tinitana, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32751,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C3TM8EIU"],"itemData":{"id":32751,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The traditional markets in southern Ecuador and within the Andean region are especially important for plant resource trading among local people, even since before Spanish colonization; therefore, ethnobotanical studies are currently necessary and important. These strategic spaces persist for the traditional medicine cultural value reflected in the higher consumption of medicinal plants, which span all socioeconomic levels of rural and urban people. The purpose of this study includes the following: 1) to create a novel list of medicinal plants sold at 33 traditional markets; 2) to establish medicinal plant use agreement amongst vendors with the Factor of Informant Consensus (FIC); and 3) to determine the most sold medicinal plant species using the Fidelity Level (FL).","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-016-0100-4","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","page":"29","source":"BioMed Central","title":"Medicinal plants sold at traditional markets in southern Ecuador","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13002-016-0100-4","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Tinitana","given":"Fani"},{"family":"Rios","given":"Montserrat"},{"family":"Romero-Benavides","given":"Juan Carlos"},{"family":"Cruz Rot","given":"Marcelino","non-dropping-particle":"de la"},{"family":"Pardo-de-Santayana","given":"Manuel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",7,5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.The literature identifies three primary roles for wild species trade in supporting rural livelihoods: (i) supporting current consumption, (ii) providing safety-nets in response to shocks and gap-filling of seasonal shortfalls, and (iii) providing means to accumulate assets and providing a pathway out of poverty ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ax2OLCz4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Angelsen, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Angelsen, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34811,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CQD2A92G"],"itemData":{"id":34811,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","page":"S12–S28","title":"Environmental income and rural livelihoods: a global-comparative analysis","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305750X14000722","volume":"64","author":[{"family":"Angelsen","given":"Arild"},{"family":"Jagger","given":"Pamela"},{"family":"Babigumira","given":"Ronnie"},{"family":"Belcher","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Hogarth","given":"Nicholas J"},{"family":"Bauch","given":"Simone"},{"family":"Börner","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Smith-Hall","given":"Carsten"},{"family":"Wunder","given":"Sven"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Illegal wild species trade and the role of regulation
The illicit trade in animal products for consumer markets is global and putting many species at risk of extinction  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8fYDKoME","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Challender & MacMillan, 2014; Duffy, 2016; MacMillan, 2017; Phelps, 2016; G. E. Rosen & Smith, 2010; Sas-Rolfes, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Challender & MacMillan, 2014; Duffy, 2016; MacMillan, 2017; Phelps, 2016; G. E. Rosen & Smith, 2010; Sas-Rolfes, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32741,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/688TECQL"],"itemData":{"id":32741,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Today record levels of funding are being invested in enforcement and an- tipoaching measures to tackle the “war on poaching,” but many species are on the path to extinction. In our view, intensifying enforcement effort is cru- cial, but will ultimately prove an inadequate long-term strategy with which to conserve high-value species. This is because: regulatory approaches are be- ing overwhelmed by the drivers of poaching and trade, financial incentives for poaching are increasing due to rising prices and growing relative poverty be- tween areas of supply and centers of demand, and aggressive enforcement of trade controls, in particular bans, can increase profits and lead to the involve- ment of organized criminals with the capacity to operate even under increased enforcement effort. With prices for high-value wildlife rising, we argue that interventions need to go beyond regulation and that new and bold strategies are needed urgently. In the immediate future, we should incentivize and build capacity within local communities to conserve wildlife. In the medium term, we should drive prices down by reexamining sustainable off-take mechanisms such as regulated trade, ranching and wildlife farming, using economic levers such as taxation to fund conservation efforts, and in the long-term reduce demand through social marketing programs.","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12082","ISSN":"1755263X","issue":"5","page":"484–494","title":"Poaching is more than an enforcement problem","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Challender","given":"Daniel W.S."},{"family":"MacMillan","given":"Douglas C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":32772,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6RSSXIE5"],"itemData":{"id":32772,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"\\"The illegal wildlife trade in global perspective\\" published on 29 Jul 2016 by Edward Elgar Publishing.","container-title":"Handbook of Transnational Environmental Crime","language":"en_US","note":"ISBN: 9781783476237\\npublisher: Edward Elgar Publishing\\nsection: Handbook of Transnational Environmental Crime","source":"www.elgaronline.com","title":"The illegal wildlife trade in global perspective","URL":"https://www.elgaronline.com/view/edcoll/9781783476220/9781783476220.00015.xml","author":[{"family":"Duffy","given":"Rosaleen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",7,29]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32771,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YE36E6FZ"],"itemData":{"id":32771,"type":"book","abstract":"This study aims to understand better the demand for traditional medicine and more contemporary health-related reasons, collectively referred to as ATM in this report, in Viet Nam. The country is strategically important for illegally traded wildlife products, such as pangolin scales and rhino horn. Using an in-person survey of over 1,000 respondents on wildlife consumed as medicine, the study had four main objectives: identify consumer profile of ATM users (age, income, gender, etc.), identify recent trends in ATM consumption among users, focusing on two high conservation-value species (pangolin and rhino), identify the main attributes of demand (quality, price, harvesting method, etc.), evaluate the policy options to reduce demand for high-conservation species. To provide insights and broader overview of market trends, the authors also interviewed experts in ATM in Viet Nam.","collection-title":"ITC publications","publisher":"International Trade Centre (UN/WTO)","source":"Queen's University Belfast","title":"Demand  in  Viet  Nam  for  rhino  horn  used  in  traditional  medicine","author":[{"family":"MacMillan","given":"Douglas"},{"family":"Bozzola","given":"Martina"},{"family":"Hanley","given":"Nick"},{"family":"Sheremet","given":"Oleg"},{"family":"Kasterine","given":"Alexander"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":7542,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q2S6CFPQ","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F8Y4UHLI"],"itemData":{"id":7542,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","DOI":"10.1002/fee.1325","ISSN":"15409295","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Front Ecol Environ","language":"en","page":"479-489","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Tools and terms for understanding illegal wildlife trade","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/fee.1325","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"},{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Webb","given":"Edward L"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",11]]}},"label":"page"},{"id":32345,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S6CN6Z8K"],"itemData":{"id":32345,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The global trade in illegal wildlife is a multi-billion dollar industry that threatens biodiversity and acts as a potential avenue for invasive species and disease spread. Despite the broad-sweeping implications of illegal wildlife sales, scientists have yet to describe the scope and scale of the trade. Here, we provide the most thorough and current description of the illegal wildlife trade using 12 years of seizure records compiled by TRAFFIC, the wildlife trade monitoring network. These records comprise 967 seizures including massive quantities of ivory, tiger skins, live reptiles, and other endangered wildlife and wildlife products. Most seizures originate in Southeast Asia, a recently identified hotspot for future emerging infectious diseases. To date, regulation and enforcement have been insufficient to effectively control the global trade in illegal wildlife at national and international scales. Effective control will require a multi-pronged approach including community-scale education and empowering local people to value wildlife, coordinated international regulation, and a greater allocation of national resources to on-the-ground enforcement.","container-title":"EcoHealth","DOI":"10.1007/s10393-010-0317-y","ISSN":"1612-9210","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"EcoHealth","language":"en","page":"24-32","source":"Springer Link","title":"Summarizing the Evidence on the International Trade in Illegal Wildlife","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10393-010-0317-y","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Rosen","given":"Gail Emilia"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Katherine F."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",8,1]]}}},{"id":11657,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5GPSNAY9"],"itemData":{"id":11657,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033253.","language":"en","title":"Illegal Wildlife Trade: Patterns, Processes, and Governance","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-101718-033253.","author":[{"literal":"Sas-Rolfes"},{"family":"Michael","given":"Daniel W.S.Challender"},{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Diogo"},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Recent estimates of illegal logging, illegal fishing, and illegal wildlife trade in 2016 are between US$ 69-199 billion a year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CBPlumhw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Bank, 2019)","plainCitation":"(World Bank, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32768,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8656XJ88"],"itemData":{"id":32768,"type":"document","publisher":"World Bank","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Illegal logging, fishing, and wildlife trade: The costs and how to combat it","title-short":"Illegal logging, fishing, and wildlife trade","author":[{"family":"World Bank","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, the full impacts, including impacts on ecosystem services, are estimated to be between US$1 trillion and US$2 trillion per year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UE5QP2on","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Bank, 2019)","plainCitation":"(World Bank, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32768,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8656XJ88"],"itemData":{"id":32768,"type":"document","publisher":"World Bank","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Illegal logging, fishing, and wildlife trade: The costs and how to combat it","title-short":"Illegal logging, fishing, and wildlife trade","author":[{"family":"World Bank","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the case of elephants,  an estimated 100,000 elephants of both savanna elephants (Loxodonta africana) and forest elephants (L. cyclotis) were poached between 2010 and 2013. In some countries, elephant populations declined by over 50% in under 10 years.  Chase ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"f4TcIzu6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chase, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Chase, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7158,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GBH8YLGB"],"itemData":{"id":7158,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"African elephants (Loxodonta africana) are imperiled by poaching and habitat loss. Despite global attention to the plight of elephants, their population sizes and trends are uncertain or unknown over much of Africa. To conserve this iconic species, conservationists need timely, accurate data on elephant populations. Here, we report the results of the Great Elephant Census (GEC), the first continent-wide, standardized survey of African savannah elephants. We also provide the first quantitative model of elephant population trends across Africa. We estimated a population of 352,271 savannah elephants on study sites in 18 countries, representing approximately 93% of all savannah elephants in those countries. Elephant populations in survey areas with historical data decreased by an estimated 144,000 from 2007 to 2014, and populations are currently shrinking by 8% per year continent-wide, primarily due to poaching. Though 84% of elephants occurred in protected areas, many protected areas had carcass ratios that indicated high levels of elephant mortality. Results of the GEC show the necessity of action to end the African elephants’ downward trajectory by preventing poaching and protecting habitat.","call-number":"Esub44","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.2354","ISSN":"2167-8359","language":"en","page":"e2354","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Continent-wide survey reveals massive decline in African savannah elephants","URL":"https://peerj.com/articles/2354","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Chase","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Schlossberg","given":"Scott"},{"family":"Griffin","given":"Curtice R."},{"family":"Bouché","given":"Philippe J.C."},{"family":"Djene","given":"Sintayehu W."},{"family":"Elkan","given":"Paul W."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"Sam"},{"family":"Grossman","given":"Falk"},{"family":"Kohi","given":"Edward Mtarima"},{"family":"Landen","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Omondi","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Peltier","given":"Alexis"},{"family":"Selier","given":"S.A. Jeanetta"},{"family":"Sutcliffe","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",8,31]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} estimated a population of 352,271 savanna elephants on study sites in 18 countries, representing approximately 93% of all savanna elephants in those countries. Elephant populations in survey areas with historical data decreased by an estimated 144,000 from 2007 to 2014, and populations were currently shrinking by 8% per year continent-wide, primarily due to poaching (i.e. illegal hunting) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IVlmpG9p","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chase, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Chase, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7158,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GBH8YLGB"],"itemData":{"id":7158,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"African elephants (Loxodonta africana) are imperiled by poaching and habitat loss. Despite global attention to the plight of elephants, their population sizes and trends are uncertain or unknown over much of Africa. To conserve this iconic species, conservationists need timely, accurate data on elephant populations. Here, we report the results of the Great Elephant Census (GEC), the first continent-wide, standardized survey of African savannah elephants. We also provide the first quantitative model of elephant population trends across Africa. We estimated a population of 352,271 savannah elephants on study sites in 18 countries, representing approximately 93% of all savannah elephants in those countries. Elephant populations in survey areas with historical data decreased by an estimated 144,000 from 2007 to 2014, and populations are currently shrinking by 8% per year continent-wide, primarily due to poaching. Though 84% of elephants occurred in protected areas, many protected areas had carcass ratios that indicated high levels of elephant mortality. Results of the GEC show the necessity of action to end the African elephants’ downward trajectory by preventing poaching and protecting habitat.","call-number":"Esub44","container-title":"PeerJ","DOI":"10.7717/peerj.2354","ISSN":"2167-8359","language":"en","page":"e2354","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Continent-wide survey reveals massive decline in African savannah elephants","URL":"https://peerj.com/articles/2354","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Chase","given":"Michael J."},{"family":"Schlossberg","given":"Scott"},{"family":"Griffin","given":"Curtice R."},{"family":"Bouché","given":"Philippe J.C."},{"family":"Djene","given":"Sintayehu W."},{"family":"Elkan","given":"Paul W."},{"family":"Ferreira","given":"Sam"},{"family":"Grossman","given":"Falk"},{"family":"Kohi","given":"Edward Mtarima"},{"family":"Landen","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Omondi","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Peltier","given":"Alexis"},{"family":"Selier","given":"S.A. Jeanetta"},{"family":"Sutcliffe","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",8,31]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Concerns are growing that illegal hunting to procure ingredients for traditional medicine, is becoming the major threat to the survival of high-value conservation species including tiger (Panthera tigris), pangolins (e.g. Chinese pangolin (Manis pentadactyla)) and rhinoceros (e.g. Rhinoceros spp). Increased urbanization and a growing middle class in Asia have increased demand for wild meat  and are fueling the lucrative illegal wild species trade, and potentially undermining rural livelihoods and food security ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"shsBKnmb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lee 2020)","plainCitation":"(Lee 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32519,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XL3BAZS3"],"itemData":{"id":32519,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Bushmeat is not only an important source of fat, micronutrients, and macronutrients, but it also has medicinal uses. Extensive human–wildlife interactions may lead to pathogen exchange and trigger zoonotic infectious disease outbreaks such as severe acute respiratory syndrome, Ebola, and coronavirus disease 2019. In the tropics, bushmeat has become one of the most threatened resources due to widespread habitat loss and overexploitation, largely driven by increased global demand, weak governance, and lack of enforcement. Unsustainable harvesting, consumption, and production practices are common, although drivers are complex and intertwined and vary regionally, pointing to a looming rural nutrition security and wildlife conservation issue. Growing demand in fast urbanizing markets coupled with easy access fuels the illegal trade of bushmeat, medicinal products, and wildlife-based luxury goods. Although bushmeat contributes significantly to rural people's income and poverty alleviation, overharvesting impacts those who are most dependent on the forest. To balance the rural and culturalimportance of bushmeat with conservation and public health priorities, strategies to safeguard tropical biodiversity, sustainable harvest of wildlife with reduced health risk for nutrition and medicine are urgently needed.","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-102016-060827","issue":"1","note":"_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-102016-060827","page":"145-170","source":"Annual Reviews","title":"The Harvest of Tropical Wildlife for Bushmeat and Traditional Medicine","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-102016-060827","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Lee","given":"Tien Ming"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zXFWlRGX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. M. Lee, 2014, 2020)","plainCitation":"(T. M. Lee, 2014, 2020)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32750,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YBKCL83H"],"itemData":{"id":32750,"type":"article-journal","note":"publisher: CIFOR","source":"Google Scholar","title":"The harvest of wildlife for bushmeat and traditional medicine in East, South and Southeast Asia: Current knowledge base, challenges, opportunities and areas for future research","title-short":"The harvest of wildlife for bushmeat and traditional medicine in East, South and Southeast Asia","author":[{"family":"Lee","given":"Tien Ming"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"Robert"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":32519,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XL3BAZS3"],"itemData":{"id":32519,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Bushmeat is not only an important source of fat, micronutrients, and macronutrients, but it also has medicinal uses. Extensive human–wildlife interactions may lead to pathogen exchange and trigger zoonotic infectious disease outbreaks such as severe acute respiratory syndrome, Ebola, and coronavirus disease 2019. In the tropics, bushmeat has become one of the most threatened resources due to widespread habitat loss and overexploitation, largely driven by increased global demand, weak governance, and lack of enforcement. Unsustainable harvesting, consumption, and production practices are common, although drivers are complex and intertwined and vary regionally, pointing to a looming rural nutrition security and wildlife conservation issue. Growing demand in fast urbanizing markets coupled with easy access fuels the illegal trade of bushmeat, medicinal products, and wildlife-based luxury goods. Although bushmeat contributes significantly to rural people's income and poverty alleviation, overharvesting impacts those who are most dependent on the forest. To balance the rural and culturalimportance of bushmeat with conservation and public health priorities, strategies to safeguard tropical biodiversity, sustainable harvest of wildlife with reduced health risk for nutrition and medicine are urgently needed.","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-102016-060827","issue":"1","note":"_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-102016-060827","page":"145-170","source":"Annual Reviews","title":"The Harvest of Tropical Wildlife for Bushmeat and Traditional Medicine","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-environ-102016-060827","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Lee","given":"Tien Ming"},{"family":"Sigouin","given":"Amanda"},{"family":"Pinedo-Vasquez","given":"Miguel"},{"family":"Nasi","given":"Robert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"})
	In the South Fly region of Papua New Guinea, illegal trading of Bêche-de-mer (dried sea cucumbers), shark fins, and fish maw (dried swim bladders) is a serious threat to sustainability of marine species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XEnJfZ3q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Busilacchi, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Busilacchi, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":760,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7NK4STKW"],"itemData":{"id":760,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Both the characteristics of the value chains and the social-ecological context within which they exist are important to uncover motivations for engagement in legal and illegal value chains. Bêche-de-mer (dried sea cucumbers), shark fins, and fish maw (dried swim bladders) are valued products in the South Fly region of Papua New Guinea (PNG). We applied value chain analysis (VCA) to map the ‘vertical’ characteristics of trade and ‘horizontal’ contextual issues driving the trade. Vertical VCA showed that both legal and illegal value chains end in the same Asian cities, but using different routes. Prices offered by illegal middlemen to PNG fishers were significantly lower than those offered by legal buyers. Horizontal analysis showed five drivers (lack of information, obligatory dependencies to middlemen, kinship ties, need for cash, inaccessibility of legal markets) for accepting lower prices. We recommend interventions necessary to tackle these systemic drivers of illegal trade.","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","DOI":"10.1080/10871209.2021.1876963","ISSN":"1087-1209","issue":"0","note":"publisher: Routledge\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/10871209.2021.1876963","page":"1-18","source":"Taylor and Francis+NEJM","title":"Why does illegal wildlife trade persist in spite of legal alternatives in transboundary regions?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/10871209.2021.1876963","volume":"0","author":[{"family":"Busilacchi","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Butler","given":"James R. A."},{"family":"Putten","given":"Ingrid","dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Cosijn","given":"Michaela"},{"family":"Posu","given":"Joseph"},{"family":"Fitriana","given":"Ria"},{"family":"Slamet","given":"Archie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While legal and illegal commodities typically served the same Asian cities, the channels travelled different routes. In spite of prices offered by illegal middlemen being significantly lower than those offered by legal buyers, many fishers engage in illegal market ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"H9PxEbl8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Busilacchi, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Busilacchi, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":760,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7NK4STKW"],"itemData":{"id":760,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Both the characteristics of the value chains and the social-ecological context within which they exist are important to uncover motivations for engagement in legal and illegal value chains. Bêche-de-mer (dried sea cucumbers), shark fins, and fish maw (dried swim bladders) are valued products in the South Fly region of Papua New Guinea (PNG). We applied value chain analysis (VCA) to map the ‘vertical’ characteristics of trade and ‘horizontal’ contextual issues driving the trade. Vertical VCA showed that both legal and illegal value chains end in the same Asian cities, but using different routes. Prices offered by illegal middlemen to PNG fishers were significantly lower than those offered by legal buyers. Horizontal analysis showed five drivers (lack of information, obligatory dependencies to middlemen, kinship ties, need for cash, inaccessibility of legal markets) for accepting lower prices. We recommend interventions necessary to tackle these systemic drivers of illegal trade.","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","DOI":"10.1080/10871209.2021.1876963","ISSN":"1087-1209","issue":"0","note":"publisher: Routledge\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/10871209.2021.1876963","page":"1-18","source":"Taylor and Francis+NEJM","title":"Why does illegal wildlife trade persist in spite of legal alternatives in transboundary regions?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/10871209.2021.1876963","volume":"0","author":[{"family":"Busilacchi","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Butler","given":"James R. A."},{"family":"Putten","given":"Ingrid","dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Cosijn","given":"Michaela"},{"family":"Posu","given":"Joseph"},{"family":"Fitriana","given":"Ria"},{"family":"Slamet","given":"Archie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The underlying reasons were dependencies to middlemen or kinship ties, urgent need for cash or inaccessibility of legal markets, or simply lacking information about legal alternatives ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"plzGubaK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Busilacchi, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Busilacchi, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":760,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7NK4STKW"],"itemData":{"id":760,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Both the characteristics of the value chains and the social-ecological context within which they exist are important to uncover motivations for engagement in legal and illegal value chains. Bêche-de-mer (dried sea cucumbers), shark fins, and fish maw (dried swim bladders) are valued products in the South Fly region of Papua New Guinea (PNG). We applied value chain analysis (VCA) to map the ‘vertical’ characteristics of trade and ‘horizontal’ contextual issues driving the trade. Vertical VCA showed that both legal and illegal value chains end in the same Asian cities, but using different routes. Prices offered by illegal middlemen to PNG fishers were significantly lower than those offered by legal buyers. Horizontal analysis showed five drivers (lack of information, obligatory dependencies to middlemen, kinship ties, need for cash, inaccessibility of legal markets) for accepting lower prices. We recommend interventions necessary to tackle these systemic drivers of illegal trade.","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","DOI":"10.1080/10871209.2021.1876963","ISSN":"1087-1209","issue":"0","note":"publisher: Routledge\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/10871209.2021.1876963","page":"1-18","source":"Taylor and Francis+NEJM","title":"Why does illegal wildlife trade persist in spite of legal alternatives in transboundary regions?","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/10871209.2021.1876963","volume":"0","author":[{"family":"Busilacchi","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Butler","given":"James R. A."},{"family":"Putten","given":"Ingrid","dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Cosijn","given":"Michaela"},{"family":"Posu","given":"Joseph"},{"family":"Fitriana","given":"Ria"},{"family":"Slamet","given":"Archie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Illegal trade is often also supplied by species that were caught or hunted unintentionally. The inability to select or choose target species may imply that protected species may be caught. For example, snare traps capture most forest mammals, birds and reptiles regardless of protection status ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Bqd3xRvp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bowen\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Jones & Pendry, 1999; Mbete, 2011; Noss, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Bowen?Jones & Pendry, 1999; Mbete, 2011; Noss, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32803,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I7WJ92E3"],"itemData":{"id":32803,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Cable snares are probably the most widespread hunting method used in central African forests today. I evaluated the impacts of cable snare hunting on wildlife in the Central African Republic. I accompanied 17 snare hunters to hunting camps to check snare lines. I identified and weighed all animals captured, recorded losses to decomposition and scavengers, and identified escaped animals when possible. Data from observed hunting activities were extrapolated to estimate the animal rate of hunting offtake from snares within the Bayanga snare hunting range of 1000 km<sup>2</sup>. All mammals, as well as several birds and reptiles, were captured in cable snares. But 75% of snare captures were of four species: the three duikers (Cephalophus callipygus, C. dorsalis, and C. monticola) and the brush-tailed porcupine (Atherurus africanus). Losses to scavengers and decomposition amounted to 27% of total captures. Over one-third of animals caught by cable snares broke the cable and escaped with an injury. Protected species represent 2% of animals caught by snares. Population densities for the three duiker species and the brush-tailed porcupine were estimated from line-transect surveys and net-hunt encounters. Additional density estimates from research sites elsewhere in central Africa were taken from the literature, as were figures on reproductive productivity. The wide range of estimates for population densities, reproductive productivity, and sustainable offtake rates make the sustainability of snare hunting difficult to assess; however, snare hunting is probably unsustainable for the three duiker species.","archive":"JSTOR","container-title":"Conservation Biology","ISSN":"0888-8892","issue":"2","page":"390-398","source":"JSTOR","title":"The Impacts of Cable Snare Hunting on Wildlife Populations in the Forests of the Central African Republic","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/2387509","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Noss","given":"Andrew J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4Q9SXMG5"],"itemData":{"id":749,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The threat that the bushmeat trade presents to primates and other taxa was assessed from the literature, including data from markets, village hunting studies and logging concessions in Central and West Africa. In many cases the numbers of both common and protected species of primate being killed throughout the region are thought to be unsustainable. This is also the case for other taxa involved in the bushmeat trade, which crosses geographic, cultural and economic boundaries. A suite of measures must be considered to mitigate the effects of this trade, and these measures will have to recognize the local, regional and national socio-economic importance of the trade if they are to result in long-term conservation success.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","ISSN":"1365-3008","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","page":"233-246","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The threat to primates and other mammals from the bushmeat trade in Africa, and how this threat could be diminished 1","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Bowen?Jones","given":"Evan"},{"family":"Pendry","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":755,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HA4J4N47"],"itemData":{"id":755,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The consumption of wildlife meat is an important source of animal protein for rural and urban population in Congo. A survey on bushmeat trade was undertaken in 21 municipal markets during four weeks, in Brazzaville. The objective of this study was to define the traders' profile and to evaluate the quantities of games and meat merchandised on the municipal markets. The study methodology combined two approaches: a quantitative one using a questionnaire as principal tool for collecting data, and a qualitative one associating informal survey methods via individual or group interviews. The results showed that women were the most involved in this trade (52%). Markets trader's monthly income was estimated at 210,428 (US$420) ± 49,128 (US$98,256) FCFA. On average, the bushmeat traders' were 39 ± 10 years old and 69% of them attended the two levels of secondary education. During four weeks, 3,711 animal carcasses were recorded on the 21 surveyed markets, representing almost 35,790 kg of biomass. Overall, 35 animal species were identified, including 9 the hunting of which was prohibited. The mammals constituted 93.8% of total number of hunted animals, with three dominant orders as the artiodactyls (49.2%), the rodents (22.6%) and the primates (17.7%), of which the Cephalophus, the Potamochoerus, the Atherurus and the Cercopithecus were the most represented. It was also noticed that trapping, an activity which leads to great losses, becomes very important, followed by be helpful alternatives to meet Congolese demand for bushmeat. For a sustainable management of the Congo's faunic resources, this study suggests the introduction gun-hunting. The Congo-Ocean Railway and the National Road n°2 were used regularly in the transportation of game, to a 72% level. The district of Niari, Lekoumou, Sangha and Pool were the main sources of supply. At the present time, the meat quantities offered in Brazzaville markets don't seem to be reduced because hunting areas extend always further, often to the detriment of protected areas. Despite the fact that such business squarely growths and shows evidence of overexploitation, activities promoting game farming and breeding of domestic species, in Brazzaville suburbs, are needed to mitigate the impact of bushmeat trade. © Roger Albert Mbete, Henri Banga-Mboko, Christophe Ngokaka, Quevin Farège Bouckacka III, Innocent Nganga, Jean-Luc Hornick, Pascal Leroy, and Cédric Vermeulen.","container-title":"Tropical Conservation Science","journalAbbreviation":"Tropical Conservation Science","page":"207-213","source":"ResearchGate","title":"Profile sellers of bushmeat and evaluation of biomass sold in municipal markets of Brazzaville, Congo","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Mbete","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Banga-Mboko","given":"Henri"},{"family":"Ngokaka","given":"C."},{"family":"III","given":"Q.F."},{"family":"Nganga","given":"I."},{"family":"Hornick","given":"Jean-Luc"},{"family":"Leroy","given":"Pascal"},{"family":"Vermeulen","given":"Cédric"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, many in fisheries many protected  species are caught as by-take or by-catch ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TisW4OAf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lawson, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Lawson, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":743,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DPC83XYP"],"itemData":{"id":743,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Nonselective fishing gears extract a great many small marine species, with limited documentation or assessment of their impacts. Among those species, seahorses (genus Hippocampus) are unusual because this genus has been the focus of scientific surveys and international trade regulation. Our review of published and unpublished data sources analyzed data on seahorse bycatch for five gear-type categories and 22 countries. The median catch per unit effort of seahorse bycatch across all five gear types was 0.96 seahorses per vessel?1 day?1. Nonetheless, fleet sizes were so large that annual catches were estimated at approximately 37 million seahorses across our sampled countries. Fisher interviews suggested that seahorse catches were declining (although information on changes in effort over time were not available). Furthermore, international export data did not capture the magnitude of seahorses in bycatch. Our work emphasizes the importance of evaluating bycatch, even for taxa where reported daily catch rates are low.","container-title":"Fisheries","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1080/03632415.2017.1259944","ISSN":"1548-8446","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"© 2017 American Fisheries Society","note":"_eprint: https://afspubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1080/03632415.2017.1259944","page":"19-33","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Low Bycatch Rates Add Up to Big Numbers for a Genus of Small Fishes","URL":"https://afspubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1080/03632415.2017.1259944","volume":"42","author":[{"family":"Lawson","given":"Julia M."},{"family":"Foster","given":"Sarah J."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"Amanda C. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, seahorses are often caught as bycatch in trawl fisheries  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Bv5iNezj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T.-C. Kuo, 2018)","plainCitation":"(T.-C. Kuo, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":744,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y8AJXBFU"],"itemData":{"id":744,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Exploitation for trade is one of the biggest threats to many species, especially for marine fishes. Trade regulations should, therefore, be effective in helping conserve marine fish populations. The Convention of International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES), one of the few multilateral environmental agreements with enforcement capacity, has embraced a number of marine fishes in recent years. However, the impacts of such measures on wildlife trade have rarely been assessed. We conducted a case study of the dried seahorse (Hippocampus spp.) trade in Thailand to understand the trade of these species under CITES regulations. We carried out 203 semi-structured interviews with traders to estimate the economic scale of Thai seahorse trade, and compared perceived changes with official trade datasets. Even though most seahorses were incidentally caught, we estimated that dried seahorses could be worth US$26.5 million per year for Thai fishers. However, the total declared annual export value was only around US$5.5 million, and had decreased to US$1 million in 2013. Considering the economic value of seahorses, the large discrepancy between declared export volumes and catch estimates suggested that trade may be underreported. While official data shows the export volume decreased after the implementation of CITES listing in 2005, our respondents did not report a similar trend. In contrast, the prices of seahorses were reported to be increasing. Our study highlights the economic importance of marine fishes captured as bycatch and the importance of international and domestic management measures for the trade of bycatch species.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-018-1610-2","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"13","journalAbbreviation":"Biodivers Conserv","language":"en","page":"3447-3468","source":"Springer Link","title":"Changes in the trade of bycatch species corresponding to CITES regulations: the case of dried seahorse trade in Thailand","title-short":"Changes in the trade of bycatch species corresponding to CITES regulations","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10531-018-1610-2","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Kuo","given":"T.-C."},{"family":"Laksanawimol","given":"P."},{"family":"Aylesworth","given":"L."},{"family":"Foster","given":"S. J."},{"family":"Vincent","given":"A. C. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Many orchid species around the world are under threat from illegal and unsustainable trade for horticulture, food and medicine ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"49sESj0R","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SU6WGFBJ"],"itemData":{"id":32354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society","DOI":"10.1093/botlinnean/box083","ISSN":"0024-4074, 1095-8339","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"435-455","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A review of the trade in orchids and its implications for conservation","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/botlinnean/article/186/4/435/4736317","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Boer","given":"Hugo J","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Fay","given":"Michael F"},{"family":"Gale","given":"Stephan W"},{"family":"Gardiner","given":"Lauren M"},{"family":"Gunasekara","given":"Rajasinghe S"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Pankaj"},{"family":"Masters","given":"Susanne"},{"family":"Metusala","given":"Destario"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L"},{"family":"Veldman","given":"Sarina"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Shan"},{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A key challenge is that insights on the legal and illegal trade dynamics, and how those channels interact are largely incomplete ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KnF8I99U","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SU6WGFBJ"],"itemData":{"id":32354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society","DOI":"10.1093/botlinnean/box083","ISSN":"0024-4074, 1095-8339","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"435-455","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A review of the trade in orchids and its implications for conservation","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/botlinnean/article/186/4/435/4736317","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Boer","given":"Hugo J","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Fay","given":"Michael F"},{"family":"Gale","given":"Stephan W"},{"family":"Gardiner","given":"Lauren M"},{"family":"Gunasekara","given":"Rajasinghe S"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Pankaj"},{"family":"Masters","given":"Susanne"},{"family":"Metusala","given":"Destario"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L"},{"family":"Veldman","given":"Sarina"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Shan"},{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. To transform the orchid trade towards sustainability, it is vital to track, trace, and sanction illegal trade and harvesting and also incentivize sustainable methods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bv6KusIB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Hinsley, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32354,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SU6WGFBJ"],"itemData":{"id":32354,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society","DOI":"10.1093/botlinnean/box083","ISSN":"0024-4074, 1095-8339","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"435-455","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"A review of the trade in orchids and its implications for conservation","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/botlinnean/article/186/4/435/4736317","volume":"186","author":[{"family":"Hinsley","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Boer","given":"Hugo J","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Fay","given":"Michael F"},{"family":"Gale","given":"Stephan W"},{"family":"Gardiner","given":"Lauren M"},{"family":"Gunasekara","given":"Rajasinghe S"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Pankaj"},{"family":"Masters","given":"Susanne"},{"family":"Metusala","given":"Destario"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David L"},{"family":"Veldman","given":"Sarina"},{"family":"Wong","given":"Shan"},{"family":"Phelps","given":"Jacob"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",3,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This could involve uplisting orchid species from Appendix II to Appendix I of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, use DNA barcoding to identify individual species and also establish small scale sustainable orchid breeding businesses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2WRHleRm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Subedi, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Subedi, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32801,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FRT4JZE5"],"itemData":{"id":32801,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wild orchids are illegally harvested and traded in Nepal for use in local traditional medicine, horticulture, and international trade. This study aims to: 1) identify the diversity of species of wild orchids in trade in Nepal; 2) study the chain of commercialization from collector to client and/or export; 3) map traditional knowledge and medicinal use of orchids; and 4) integrate the collected data to propose a more sustainable approach to orchid conservation in Nepal. Trade, species diversity, and traditional use of wild-harvested orchids were documented during field surveys of markets and through interviews. Trade volumes and approximate income were estimated based on surveys and current market prices. Orchid material samples were identified to species level using a combination of morphology and DNA barcoding. Orchid trade is a long tradition, and illegal export to China, India and Hong Kong is rife. Estimates show that 9.4 tons of wild orchids were illegally traded from the study sites during 2008/2009. A total of 60 species of wild orchids were reported to be used in traditional medicinal practices to cure at least 38 different ailments, including energizers, aphrodisiacs and treatments of burnt skin, fractured or dislocated bones, headaches, fever and wounds. DNA barcoding successfully identified orchid material to species level that remained sterile after culturing. Collection of wild orchids was found to be widespread in Nepal, but illegal trade is threatening many species in the wild. Establishment of small-scale sustainable orchid breeding enterprises could be a valuable alternative for the production of medicinal orchids for local communities. Critically endangered species should be placed on CITES Appendix I to provide extra protection to those species. DNA barcoding is an effective method for species identification and monitoring of illegal cross-border trade.","archive":"Readcube","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/1746-4269-9-64","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","page":"64","title":"Collection and trade of wild-harvested orchids in Nepal","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1186/1746-4269-9-64","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Subedi","given":"Abishkar"},{"family":"Kunwar","given":"Bimal"},{"family":"Choi","given":"Young"},{"family":"Dai","given":"Yuntao"},{"family":"Andel","given":"Tinde","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"Ram P."},{"family":"Boer","given":"Hugo J.","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Gravendeel","given":"Barbara"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Illegal wild species trade has been consistently tied to unsustainable levels of exploitation. Trade that is illegal and unsustainable, but preserves some level of social legitimacy among harvesters, and consumers, can undermine policies. Even though very large overall volumes of illegal wild species trade are observed, trade structure is very complex (e.g. role of corruption) and highly heterogeneous, and extracting information from the trade is difficult because it is illicit, posing challenges to enforcement actitivies and policy solutions. Moreover, the illegal trade of wild species is often interconnected with the legal trade. There is therefore a strong need for legal trade to be heavily monitored and regulated ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Hs0ZyzEI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(van Uhm & Moreto, 2018)","plainCitation":"(van Uhm & Moreto, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32773,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L6ZEZBSP"],"itemData":{"id":32773,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This study focuses on the role of corruption in facilitating the illegal wildlife trade. This research attempts to contribute to the literature by disentangling the existence, influence and nested nature of corruption within the illegal wildlife trade based on ethnographic fieldwork conducted in China, Morocco, Russia and Uganda. By utilizing Passas’ concepts of symbiotic and antithetical relationships as theoretical framework, we examine the presence of corruption within illegal wildlife trafficking. Our findings lend support for, and extend the framework with the concept of legal exploitation, while highlighting the unique nature of corrupt practices influenced by different socio-political and cultural settings. Symbiotic and antithetical relationships were revealed through qualitative fieldwork and provided in-depth knowledge behind the social world of wildlife trafficking.","container-title":"The British Journal of Criminology","DOI":"10.1093/bjc/azx032","ISSN":"0007-0955","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"The British Journal of Criminology","page":"864-885","source":"Silverchair","title":"Corruption Within the Illegal Wildlife Trade: A Symbiotic and Antithetical Enterprise","title-short":"Corruption Within the Illegal Wildlife Trade","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1093/bjc/azx032","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Uhm","given":"Daan P","non-dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Moreto","given":"William D"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",6,13]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Harvesting and trade of wild species is often regulated through a large number of laws and regulations –sometimes internally contradicting, undermining sustainability goals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Uek9LxmE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de la Torre, 2011)","plainCitation":"(de la Torre, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32805,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GAPNZB7Q"],"itemData":{"id":32805,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Non-timber forest products (NTFPs) derived from palms and other plants are economically and culturally important to a large part of the more than 240 million people who live in the forest areas of developing countries. The sustainable extraction of NTFPs is increasingly regarded as an important part for forest conservation strategies. This paper provides an overview and comparison of existing statutory legislation with respect to the extraction and trade of NTFPs in four Andean countries and discusses its adequacy with respect to ensuring legal and sustainable extraction and trade of NTFPs. The related legal framework surrounding indigenous peoples’ rights, traditional knowledge and access to genetic resources is also reviewed. Forest laws are primarily concerned with the regulation of timber. Hence, legal and administrative frameworks to regulate the extraction and trade of NTFPs are fragmented and ambiguous. By providing an overview over the existing legal situation, this paper seeks to inform and open debates about ways to improve the regulation of the extraction and trade of NTFPs in the region.","container-title":"The Botanical Review","DOI":"10.1007/s12229-011-9066-z","ISSN":"0006-8101, 1874-9372","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Bot. Rev.","language":"en","page":"327-369","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Legal and Administrative Regulation of Palms and Other NTFPs in Colombia, Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12229-011-9066-z","volume":"77","author":[{"family":"Torre","given":"Lucía","non-dropping-particle":"de la"},{"family":"Valencia","given":"Renato"},{"family":"Altamirano","given":"Carolina"},{"family":"Ravnborg","given":"Helle Munk"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A comparative analysis in Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, and Bolivia on plants, algae and fungi identifies the inconsistency of legal norms, such as internal contradictions between national legislation and indigenous rights, and lack of clarity regarding legal requirements or e responsibilities of individual authorities as the main obstacles  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TxcuTLtK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(de la Torre, 2011)","plainCitation":"(de la Torre, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32805,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GAPNZB7Q"],"itemData":{"id":32805,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Non-timber forest products (NTFPs) derived from palms and other plants are economically and culturally important to a large part of the more than 240 million people who live in the forest areas of developing countries. The sustainable extraction of NTFPs is increasingly regarded as an important part for forest conservation strategies. This paper provides an overview and comparison of existing statutory legislation with respect to the extraction and trade of NTFPs in four Andean countries and discusses its adequacy with respect to ensuring legal and sustainable extraction and trade of NTFPs. The related legal framework surrounding indigenous peoples’ rights, traditional knowledge and access to genetic resources is also reviewed. Forest laws are primarily concerned with the regulation of timber. Hence, legal and administrative frameworks to regulate the extraction and trade of NTFPs are fragmented and ambiguous. By providing an overview over the existing legal situation, this paper seeks to inform and open debates about ways to improve the regulation of the extraction and trade of NTFPs in the region.","container-title":"The Botanical Review","DOI":"10.1007/s12229-011-9066-z","ISSN":"0006-8101, 1874-9372","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Bot. Rev.","language":"en","page":"327-369","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Legal and Administrative Regulation of Palms and Other NTFPs in Colombia, Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s12229-011-9066-z","volume":"77","author":[{"family":"Torre","given":"Lucía","non-dropping-particle":"de la"},{"family":"Valencia","given":"Renato"},{"family":"Altamirano","given":"Carolina"},{"family":"Ravnborg","given":"Helle Munk"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	In most African countries, hunting is regulated by legal instruments, usually  controlled through systems of licensing and quotas. Wild species are generally either considered to be without ownership (‘res nullius) or owned by the state or president ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"U0z2RINp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W43CISEZ"],"itemData":{"id":32740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The bushmeat trade, or the illegal acquisition and exchange of wild meat, has long been recognised as a severe problem in forest biomes, but receives little attention in savannas, perhaps due to a misconception that bushmeat hunting is a low-impact subsistence activity. Though data on impacts are scarce, indications are that bushmeat hunting is a widespread problem in savannas, with severe impacts on wildlife populations and wildlife-based land uses. The impacts of the bushmeat trade in savannas vary from edge-effects around protected areas, to disproportionate declines of some species, to severe wildlife declines in areas with inadequate anti-poaching. In some areas, bushmeat contributes signi?cantly to food security, but these bene?ts are unsustainable, and hunting is wasteful, utilising a fraction of the wildlife killed or of its ?nancial value obtainable through tourism, trophy hunting and/or legal game meat production. The bushmeat trade appears to be becoming increasingly commercialised due to elevated demand in rural areas, urban centres and even overseas cities. Other drivers for the trade include human encroachment of wildlife areas; poverty and food insecurity; and inadequate legal frameworks to enable communities to bene?t legally from wildlife, and to create incentives for people to desist from illegal bushmeat hunting. These drivers are exacerbated by inadequate wildlife laws and enforcement and in some areas, political instability. Urgent efforts are needed to address these drivers and raise awareness among local and international governments of the seriousness of the threat. Failure to address this will result in severe wildlife declines widely in African savannas, with signi?cant ecological, economic and social impacts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.12.020","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"80-96","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas: Impacts, drivers, and possible solutions","title-short":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320712005186","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter Andrew"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen"},{"family":"Bento","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Bocchino","given":"Clara"},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Diggle","given":"Richard W."},{"family":"Eves","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Marnewick","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Mattheus","given":"Jaco"},{"family":"Weldon McNutt","given":"J."},{"family":"McRobb","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Milanzi","given":"James"},{"family":"Morley","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Opyene","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Phadima","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Rentsch","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Roche","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Westhuizen","given":"Hugo","dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie Van"},{"family":"Zisadza-Gandiwa","given":"Patience"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  What make sustainable uses more difficult is the use of unselective capture techniques. For example, the use of snare traps are not species-specific, but capture most forest mammals, birds and reptiles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YB1NuFfs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bowen\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Jones & Pendry, 1999; Mbete, 2011; Noss, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Bowen?Jones & Pendry, 1999; Mbete, 2011; Noss, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32803,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I7WJ92E3"],"itemData":{"id":32803,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Cable snares are probably the most widespread hunting method used in central African forests today. I evaluated the impacts of cable snare hunting on wildlife in the Central African Republic. I accompanied 17 snare hunters to hunting camps to check snare lines. I identified and weighed all animals captured, recorded losses to decomposition and scavengers, and identified escaped animals when possible. Data from observed hunting activities were extrapolated to estimate the animal rate of hunting offtake from snares within the Bayanga snare hunting range of 1000 km<sup>2</sup>. All mammals, as well as several birds and reptiles, were captured in cable snares. But 75% of snare captures were of four species: the three duikers (Cephalophus callipygus, C. dorsalis, and C. monticola) and the brush-tailed porcupine (Atherurus africanus). Losses to scavengers and decomposition amounted to 27% of total captures. Over one-third of animals caught by cable snares broke the cable and escaped with an injury. Protected species represent 2% of animals caught by snares. Population densities for the three duiker species and the brush-tailed porcupine were estimated from line-transect surveys and net-hunt encounters. Additional density estimates from research sites elsewhere in central Africa were taken from the literature, as were figures on reproductive productivity. The wide range of estimates for population densities, reproductive productivity, and sustainable offtake rates make the sustainability of snare hunting difficult to assess; however, snare hunting is probably unsustainable for the three duiker species.","archive":"JSTOR","container-title":"Conservation Biology","ISSN":"0888-8892","issue":"2","page":"390-398","source":"JSTOR","title":"The Impacts of Cable Snare Hunting on Wildlife Populations in the Forests of the Central African Republic","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/2387509","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Noss","given":"Andrew J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":749,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4Q9SXMG5"],"itemData":{"id":749,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The threat that the bushmeat trade presents to primates and other taxa was assessed from the literature, including data from markets, village hunting studies and logging concessions in Central and West Africa. In many cases the numbers of both common and protected species of primate being killed throughout the region are thought to be unsustainable. This is also the case for other taxa involved in the bushmeat trade, which crosses geographic, cultural and economic boundaries. A suite of measures must be considered to mitigate the effects of this trade, and these measures will have to recognize the local, regional and national socio-economic importance of the trade if they are to result in long-term conservation success.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","ISSN":"1365-3008","issue":"3","language":"en","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","page":"233-246","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The threat to primates and other mammals from the bushmeat trade in Africa, and how this threat could be diminished 1","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1046/j.1365-3008.1999.00066.x","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Bowen?Jones","given":"Evan"},{"family":"Pendry","given":"Stephanie"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":755,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HA4J4N47"],"itemData":{"id":755,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The consumption of wildlife meat is an important source of animal protein for rural and urban population in Congo. A survey on bushmeat trade was undertaken in 21 municipal markets during four weeks, in Brazzaville. The objective of this study was to define the traders' profile and to evaluate the quantities of games and meat merchandised on the municipal markets. The study methodology combined two approaches: a quantitative one using a questionnaire as principal tool for collecting data, and a qualitative one associating informal survey methods via individual or group interviews. The results showed that women were the most involved in this trade (52%). Markets trader's monthly income was estimated at 210,428 (US$420) ± 49,128 (US$98,256) FCFA. On average, the bushmeat traders' were 39 ± 10 years old and 69% of them attended the two levels of secondary education. During four weeks, 3,711 animal carcasses were recorded on the 21 surveyed markets, representing almost 35,790 kg of biomass. Overall, 35 animal species were identified, including 9 the hunting of which was prohibited. The mammals constituted 93.8% of total number of hunted animals, with three dominant orders as the artiodactyls (49.2%), the rodents (22.6%) and the primates (17.7%), of which the Cephalophus, the Potamochoerus, the Atherurus and the Cercopithecus were the most represented. It was also noticed that trapping, an activity which leads to great losses, becomes very important, followed by be helpful alternatives to meet Congolese demand for bushmeat. For a sustainable management of the Congo's faunic resources, this study suggests the introduction gun-hunting. The Congo-Ocean Railway and the National Road n°2 were used regularly in the transportation of game, to a 72% level. The district of Niari, Lekoumou, Sangha and Pool were the main sources of supply. At the present time, the meat quantities offered in Brazzaville markets don't seem to be reduced because hunting areas extend always further, often to the detriment of protected areas. Despite the fact that such business squarely growths and shows evidence of overexploitation, activities promoting game farming and breeding of domestic species, in Brazzaville suburbs, are needed to mitigate the impact of bushmeat trade. © Roger Albert Mbete, Henri Banga-Mboko, Christophe Ngokaka, Quevin Farège Bouckacka III, Innocent Nganga, Jean-Luc Hornick, Pascal Leroy, and Cédric Vermeulen.","container-title":"Tropical Conservation Science","journalAbbreviation":"Tropical Conservation Science","page":"207-213","source":"ResearchGate","title":"Profile sellers of bushmeat and evaluation of biomass sold in municipal markets of Brazzaville, Congo","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Mbete","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Banga-Mboko","given":"Henri"},{"family":"Ngokaka","given":"C."},{"family":"III","given":"Q.F."},{"family":"Nganga","given":"I."},{"family":"Hornick","given":"Jean-Luc"},{"family":"Leroy","given":"Pascal"},{"family":"Vermeulen","given":"Cédric"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In 2001 the Indonesian Department of Forestry and the Wild species Conservation Society established the Wild species Crimes Unit in North Sulawesi, to curb over-exploitation of wild species. Over a two-year period, 6963 wild mammals on their way  to markets were encountered, which is about 8 individuals per hectare and 96,586 wild mammals were documented during market surveys ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yMVzXvwr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. J. Lee, 2005)","plainCitation":"(R. J. Lee, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CHE4Y68S"],"itemData":{"id":32795,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Excessive hunting pressure, due in large part to commercialization, has reduced the populations of many tropical large mammal species. Wildlife over-exploitation is severe in Indonesia, especially on Sulawesi, where human resources and funding are inadequate to monitor the wildlife trade and enforce existing protection laws. In response, the Wildlife Crimes Unit program was established in December 2001 to: (i) monitor wildlife transportation into North Sulawesi and market sales; (ii) provide legal and technical support to law enforcement agencies; and (iii) promote public awareness of wildlife and protection laws. Over a two-year period, 6963 wild mammals en route to markets were encountered (?8 individuals h?1) and 96,586 wild mammals were documented during market surveys. The trade of some protected mammals declined significantly over this period, but overall trade in wild mammals increased by 30%. High volume of trade in non-protected animals such as the Sulawesi pig Sus celebensis and large flying foxes (Pteropodidae), raise concerns about the sustainability of current harvesting. To combat this problem, we recommend that: (1) efforts are continued to reduce trade in protected species; (2) protected status is extended to heavily traded but non-protected taxa, such as flying foxes; (3) the effects of hunting on rat and bat populations, as well as its impact on forest dynamics, are quantified; and (4) work is carried out with local communities to strengthen awareness, set sustainable limits on wild mammal harvesting, and establish practical mechanisms for enforcing these limits.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2005.01.009","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"477-488","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Wildlife trade and implications for law enforcement in Indonesia: a case study from North Sulawesi","title-short":"Wildlife trade and implications for law enforcement in Indonesia","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320705000078","volume":"123","author":[{"family":"Lee","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Gorog","given":"Antonia J."},{"family":"Dwiyahreni","given":"Asri"},{"family":"Siwu","given":"Stephan"},{"family":"Riley","given":"Jon"},{"family":"Alexander","given":"Harry"},{"family":"Paoli","given":"Gary D."},{"family":"Ramono","given":"Widodo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",6,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While the trade of some protected mammals declined significantly over this period, overall trade in wild mammals increased by 30%, indicating that traders switch from controlled to uncontrolled species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jrLaMcAB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. J. Lee, 2005)","plainCitation":"(R. J. Lee, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CHE4Y68S"],"itemData":{"id":32795,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Excessive hunting pressure, due in large part to commercialization, has reduced the populations of many tropical large mammal species. Wildlife over-exploitation is severe in Indonesia, especially on Sulawesi, where human resources and funding are inadequate to monitor the wildlife trade and enforce existing protection laws. In response, the Wildlife Crimes Unit program was established in December 2001 to: (i) monitor wildlife transportation into North Sulawesi and market sales; (ii) provide legal and technical support to law enforcement agencies; and (iii) promote public awareness of wildlife and protection laws. Over a two-year period, 6963 wild mammals en route to markets were encountered (?8 individuals h?1) and 96,586 wild mammals were documented during market surveys. The trade of some protected mammals declined significantly over this period, but overall trade in wild mammals increased by 30%. High volume of trade in non-protected animals such as the Sulawesi pig Sus celebensis and large flying foxes (Pteropodidae), raise concerns about the sustainability of current harvesting. To combat this problem, we recommend that: (1) efforts are continued to reduce trade in protected species; (2) protected status is extended to heavily traded but non-protected taxa, such as flying foxes; (3) the effects of hunting on rat and bat populations, as well as its impact on forest dynamics, are quantified; and (4) work is carried out with local communities to strengthen awareness, set sustainable limits on wild mammal harvesting, and establish practical mechanisms for enforcing these limits.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2005.01.009","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"477-488","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Wildlife trade and implications for law enforcement in Indonesia: a case study from North Sulawesi","title-short":"Wildlife trade and implications for law enforcement in Indonesia","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320705000078","volume":"123","author":[{"family":"Lee","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Gorog","given":"Antonia J."},{"family":"Dwiyahreni","given":"Asri"},{"family":"Siwu","given":"Stephan"},{"family":"Riley","given":"Jon"},{"family":"Alexander","given":"Harry"},{"family":"Paoli","given":"Gary D."},{"family":"Ramono","given":"Widodo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",6,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, high volume of trade in non-protected species such as the Sulawesi pig Sus celebensis and large flying foxes (Pteropodidae), imply a greater risk of unsustainable harvesting for unprotected species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"U6P4LijN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. J. Lee, 2005)","plainCitation":"(R. J. Lee, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32795,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CHE4Y68S"],"itemData":{"id":32795,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Excessive hunting pressure, due in large part to commercialization, has reduced the populations of many tropical large mammal species. Wildlife over-exploitation is severe in Indonesia, especially on Sulawesi, where human resources and funding are inadequate to monitor the wildlife trade and enforce existing protection laws. In response, the Wildlife Crimes Unit program was established in December 2001 to: (i) monitor wildlife transportation into North Sulawesi and market sales; (ii) provide legal and technical support to law enforcement agencies; and (iii) promote public awareness of wildlife and protection laws. Over a two-year period, 6963 wild mammals en route to markets were encountered (?8 individuals h?1) and 96,586 wild mammals were documented during market surveys. The trade of some protected mammals declined significantly over this period, but overall trade in wild mammals increased by 30%. High volume of trade in non-protected animals such as the Sulawesi pig Sus celebensis and large flying foxes (Pteropodidae), raise concerns about the sustainability of current harvesting. To combat this problem, we recommend that: (1) efforts are continued to reduce trade in protected species; (2) protected status is extended to heavily traded but non-protected taxa, such as flying foxes; (3) the effects of hunting on rat and bat populations, as well as its impact on forest dynamics, are quantified; and (4) work is carried out with local communities to strengthen awareness, set sustainable limits on wild mammal harvesting, and establish practical mechanisms for enforcing these limits.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2005.01.009","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"477-488","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Wildlife trade and implications for law enforcement in Indonesia: a case study from North Sulawesi","title-short":"Wildlife trade and implications for law enforcement in Indonesia","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320705000078","volume":"123","author":[{"family":"Lee","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Gorog","given":"Antonia J."},{"family":"Dwiyahreni","given":"Asri"},{"family":"Siwu","given":"Stephan"},{"family":"Riley","given":"Jon"},{"family":"Alexander","given":"Harry"},{"family":"Paoli","given":"Gary D."},{"family":"Ramono","given":"Widodo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",6,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Trade creates higher revenues in exporting countries, which may imply a shift from open access towards stricter enforcement ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PSqEofjz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Copeland & Taylor, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Copeland & Taylor, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":759,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H774QQIU"],"itemData":{"id":759,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Trade, Tragedy, and the Commons by Brian R. Copeland and M. Scott Taylor. Published in volume 99, issue 3, pages 725-49 of American Economic Review, June 2009, Abstract: We develop a theory of resource management where the degree to which countries escape the tragedy of the commons, and hence the d...","container-title":"American Economic Review","DOI":"10.1257/aer.99.3.725","ISSN":"0002-8282","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"725-49","source":"www.aeaweb.org","title":"Trade, Tragedy, and the Commons","URL":"https://www.aeaweb.org/articles/pdf/doi/10.1257/aer.99.3.725","volume":"99","author":[{"family":"Copeland","given":"Brian R."},{"family":"Taylor","given":"M. Scott"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There may have higher revenues, mediated through trade may initiate a transition towards more private property, but also common property, depending on what makes monitoring easier, as shown for the case of palm trees in Nigeria ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"iFmw8lzX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fenske, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Fenske, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6752,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BP44FVLP"],"itemData":{"id":6752,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The conventional view is that an increase in the value of a natural resource can lead to private property over it. Many Igbo groups in Nigeria, however, curtailed private rights over palm trees in response to the palm produce trade of the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. I use the Ostrom (2007, 2009) framework for analyzing social-ecological systems to guide the construction of a model of this transition. An increase in the resource price leads the owner to prefer communal harvesting, which simplifies monitoring against theft. I support this framework with evidence from colonial court records. “Palm cutting always cause palaver.”\\n\\n\\nObuba of Ububa, Nkwo Udara Civil Suit 111/37","container-title":"The Journal of Economic History","DOI":"10.1017/S0022050714000023","ISSN":"0022-0507, 1471-6372","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"39-68","source":"Cambridge Core","title":"Imachi Nkwu: Trade and the Commons","title-short":"Imachi Nkwu","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-economic-history/article/imachi-nkwu-trade-and-the-commons/0CE821136F415B670F994F570DB83A58","volume":"74","author":[{"family":"Fenske","given":"James"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      The International Tropical Timber Agreements, for the first time signed in 1983,  encourage trade from sustainably managed forests. Global data reveals that total timber exports from member countries have not decreased as a result, but rather exports have shifted from member to non-member  countries. Also exports have shifted across timber categories ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"w7qfLYHJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Houghton & Naughton, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Houghton & Naughton, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":726,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6SD77HYP"],"itemData":{"id":726,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Environmental sustainability standards are often portrayed as a hindrance to trade and growth. A set of novel international environmental agreements (IEAs), the International Tropical Timber Agreements (ITTAs), seeks to promote both. The ITTAs encourage international trade for member nations while requiring sustainable timber practices. This paper uses the ITTAs as a case study to examine whether IEAs can lead to environmental cooperation at the same time as increasing trade. Membership in both the 1983 and 1994 ITTAs is examined for an effect on timber exports. The analysis is conducted using panel data for 165 countries between 1970 and 2011 while controlling for year fixed effects, country fixed effects and country-specific trend terms. Estimated ITTA effects vary by ITTA year, timber category and country type. Logs exports fell for both tropical and non-tropical country members, but these decreases were offset by increases in other timber category exports. Tropical country members increased plywood exports, while non-tropical country ITTA members increased exports of sawn wood and veneer sheets. Total exports of targeted timber were unaffected in non-tropical member countries, while the 1983 ITTA increased total exports for tropical countries. These results together suggest that the sustainability clauses entailed in ITTAs have not decreased total timber exports from member countries, but have shifted exports across timber categories.","container-title":"International Environmental Agreements: Politics, Law and Economics","DOI":"10.1007/s10784-017-9373-x","ISSN":"1573-1553","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Int Environ Agreements","language":"en","page":"755-778","source":"Springer Link","title":"Trade and sustainability: the impact of the International Tropical Timber Agreements on exports","title-short":"Trade and sustainability","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10784-017-9373-x","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Houghton","given":"Kendall"},{"family":"Naughton","given":"Helen"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In particular, tropical country members increased plywood exports. 
      
The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES)
The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) is an international treaty to prevent species from becoming endangered or extinct because of international trade. Under this treaty, countries work together to regulate the international trade of animal and plant species and ensure that this trade is not detrimental to the survival of wild populations. Any trade in protected plant and animal species should be sustainable, based on sound biological understanding and principles. Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora currently has 183 Parties, and it regulates international trade in approximately 35,800 species, 84% of which are plants. It includes species in its three appendices with corresponding trade controls implemented through national legislation and enforcement measures. Endangered species are listed in several Appendices that have different legal implications and give different protection status. 3% of species (?1,000) are included in Appendix I, prohibiting any commercial international trade in wild-harvested specimens. Ninety-seven percent of species (?35,000) are included in Appendix II, requiring trade to be closely regulated and subject to a nondetrimental finding by the exporting country’s Sscientific Aauthority. This may also involve a process of setting and controlling trade quotas.
      Appendix III is a list of species included at the request of a Party that already regulates trade in the species and that needs the cooperation of other countries to prevent unsustainable or illegal exploitation, and it encompasses 220 species. 
      Overall, Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora listings have a positive impact on sustainability by regulating trade, though it does not close all loopholes and may give adverse incentives when it comes to conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SH5aIdbU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Conrad, 2012b; Harfoot, 2018c; Phelps, 2010; Rivalan, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Conrad, 2012b; Harfoot, 2018c; Phelps, 2010; Rivalan, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34380,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ANJDP2ZQ"],"itemData":{"id":34380,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Proactive management of trade in endangered wildlife makes more sense than last-minute bans that can themselves increase trading activity, argue Philippe Rivalan and his co-authors.","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/447529a","ISSN":"1476-4687","language":"en","license":"2007 Nature Publishing Group","page":"529-530","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Can bans stimulate wildlife trade?","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/447529a","volume":"447","author":[{"family":"Rivalan","given":"Philippe"},{"family":"Delmas","given":"Virginie"},{"family":"Angulo","given":"Elena"},{"family":"Bull","given":"Leigh S."},{"family":"Hall","given":"Richard J."},{"family":"Courchamp","given":"Franck"},{"family":"Rosser","given":"Alison M."},{"family":"Leader-Williams","given":"Nigel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",5,30]]}}},{"id":32809,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MK9KD8TM"],"itemData":{"id":32809,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1195558","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6012","journalAbbreviation":"Science","language":"en","page":"1752-1753","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Boosting CITES","URL":"http://www.sciencemag.org/cgi/doi/10.1126/science.1195558","volume":"330","author":[{"family":"Phelps","given":"J."},{"family":"Webb","given":"E. L."},{"family":"Bickford","given":"D."},{"family":"Nijman","given":"V."},{"family":"Sodhi","given":"N. S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",8,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",12,24]]}}},{"id":32790,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2BQRMSEW"],"itemData":{"id":32790,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tropical Conservation Science","DOI":"10.1177/194008291200500302","ISSN":"1940-0829, 1940-0829","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Tropical Conservation Science","language":"en","page":"245-254","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trade Bans: A Perfect Storm for Poaching?","title-short":"Trade Bans","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/194008291200500302","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Conrad","given":"Kirsten"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",9]]}}},{"id":11383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2Q78L7PC","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RAMZGR28"],"itemData":{"id":11383,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife trade can provide commercial incentives to conserve biodiversity but, if unsustainable, can also pose a threat. CITES (Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora) aims to ensure international trade in CITES-listed species is sustainable, legal and traceable. However, large-scale temporal and spatial patterns in wildlife trade are poorly known. We address this by analysing the CITES Trade Database: > 16 million shipment records for 28,282 species, from 1975 and 2014. Over this period, the volume of reported trade in CITES-listed wildlife quadrupled, from 25 million whole-organism equivalents per year to 100 million, and the ratio of wild- to captive-sourced trade in mammals, birds, reptiles, invertebrates and plants declined by an order of magnitude or more. Our ?ndings start to reveal the scale of the legal wildlife trade, shifting trade routes and sources over time and we describe testable hypotheses for the causes of these changes.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2018.04.017","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"47-57","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Unveiling the patterns and trends in 40 years of global trade in CITES-listed wildlife","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320717312478","volume":"223","author":[{"family":"Harfoot","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Glaser","given":"Satu A.M."},{"family":"Tittensor","given":"Derek P."},{"family":"Britten","given":"Gregory L."},{"family":"McLardy","given":"Claire"},{"family":"Malsch","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Burgess","given":"Neil D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",1,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Since the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora has been implemented, trade has shifted towards captive-sourced trade, alleviating pressure on wild species. At the same time, there is a risk that what is documented on paper does not match harvesting from the field and illegal harvests are declared and traded as legal  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hI884WZD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Finer, 2014; Lyons & Natusch, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Finer, 2014; Lyons & Natusch, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":716,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/28PSAP4U"],"itemData":{"id":716,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife breeding farms have been promoted to aid biodiversity conservation by alleviating the pressure of harvest on wild populations. There is, however, growing concern that many breeding farms are being used to launder illegally caught wildlife. Surveys of wildlife traders in the Indonesian provinces of Maluku, West Papua and Papua were conducted between August 2009 and April 2011 to assess the trade of the green python (Morelia viridis), the species currently exported in the largest numbers from Indonesia declared as captive-bred. In total, 4227 illegally collected wild green pythons were recorded during surveys and high levels of harvest were found to have depleted and skewed the demographics of some island populations. Snakes were traced from their point of capture to breeding farms in Jakarta where they are to be exported for the pet trade, confirming the reports of wildlife laundering. Extrapolation of monthly collection estimates provided by traders revealed that at least 5337 green pythons are collected each year, suggesting that at least 80% of the green pythons exported from Indonesia annually are illegally wild-caught. The results of examination of 139 eggshells from five python species suggest that reptilian eggshells may be used as proof of provenance for each individual reptile exported. This method, in addition to the evidence that breeding farms play a significant role in the illegal exploitation of wildlife, allows conservation managers to begin to adequately monitor, regulate and determine the role of breeding farms in the conservation of wild populations.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2011.10.002","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"12","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"3073-3081","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Wildlife laundering through breeding farms: Illegal harvest, population declines and a means of regulating the trade of green pythons (Morelia viridis) from Indonesia","title-short":"Wildlife laundering through breeding farms","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320711003685","volume":"144","author":[{"family":"Lyons","given":"Jessica A."},{"family":"Natusch","given":"Daniel J. D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",12,1]]}}},{"id":761,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I2L68RSD"],"itemData":{"id":761,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The Peruvian Amazon is an important arena in global efforts to promote sustainable logging in the tropics. Despite recent efforts to achieve sustainability, such as provisions in the US-Peru Trade Promotion Agreement, illegal logging continues to plague the region. We present evidence that Peru's legal logging concession system is enabling the widespread illegal logging via the regulatory documents designed to ensure sustainable logging. Analyzing official government data, we found that 68.3% of all concessions supervised by authorities were suspected of major violations. Of the 609 total concessions, nearly 30% have been cancelled for violations and we expect this percentage to increase as investigations continue. Moreover, the nature of the violations indicate that the permits associated with legal concessions are used to harvest trees in unauthorized areas, thus threatening all forested areas. Many of the violations pertain to the illegal extraction of CITES-listed timber species outside authorized areas. These findings highlight the need for additional reforms.","container-title":"Scientific Reports","DOI":"10.1038/srep04719","ISSN":"20452322","title":"Logging concessions enable illegal logging crisis in the peruvian amazon","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Finer","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Jenkins","given":"Clinton N."},{"family":"Sky","given":"Melissa A.Blue"},{"family":"Pine","given":"Justin"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Southeast-Asia, at least 35 million animals (0.3 million butterflies; 16.0 million seahorses; 0.1 million other fish; 17.4 million reptiles; 0.4 million mammals; 1.0 million birds) that were listed by Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora were exported in the period 1998–2007 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jrCdkhLU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nijman, 2010a)","plainCitation":"(Nijman, 2010a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32739,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMIG93TC"],"itemData":{"id":32739,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife trade is the very heart of biodiversity conservation and sustainable development providing an income for some of the least economically affluent people and it generates considerable revenue nationally. In Asia the unsustainable trade in wildlife has been identified as one of the main conservation challenges. Internationally, wildlife trade is regulated through the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of wild fauna and flora (CITES) to which all Southeast Asian nations are signatory. I obtained data on international trade in CITES-listed animals in the period 1998-2007. In all \\\\textgreater 35 million animals (0.3 million butterflies; 16.0 million seahorses; 0.1 million other fish; 17.4 million reptiles; 0.4 million mammals; 1.0 million birds) were exported in this period, 30 million (similar to 300 species) of them being wild-caught. In addition 18 million pieces and 2 million kg of live corals were exported. Malaysia, Vietnam, Indonesia and China are the major exporters of wild-caught animals and the European Union and Japan are the most significant importers. Over this period exports in birds significantly decreased, trade in the other taxa either increased or remained stable. For all taxa but butterflies the vast majority of individuals represent wild-caught individuals. Records of illegal or undeclared international trade are scant but can be significantly larger than levels of official exports. It is concluded that there is an urgent need for better assessments of what levels of exploitation are sustainable (including exploring appropriate proxies for Non Detriment Findings), for initiatives to make regulatory mechanisms more effective (including the introduction of minimum mandatory standards and monitoring selected wildlife trade hubs), and for better licensing and registration. Funding for at least some of these initiatives can be obtained by imposing small levies on exports of CITES-listed wildlife.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-009-9758-4","ISSN":"09603115","issue":"4","page":"1101–1114","title":"An overview of international wildlife trade from Southeast Asia","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Of those, at least 30 million animals, which comprises about 300 species had been caught in the wild ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OgOYwkTH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nijman, 2010a)","plainCitation":"(Nijman, 2010a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32739,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMIG93TC"],"itemData":{"id":32739,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife trade is the very heart of biodiversity conservation and sustainable development providing an income for some of the least economically affluent people and it generates considerable revenue nationally. In Asia the unsustainable trade in wildlife has been identified as one of the main conservation challenges. Internationally, wildlife trade is regulated through the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of wild fauna and flora (CITES) to which all Southeast Asian nations are signatory. I obtained data on international trade in CITES-listed animals in the period 1998-2007. In all \\\\textgreater 35 million animals (0.3 million butterflies; 16.0 million seahorses; 0.1 million other fish; 17.4 million reptiles; 0.4 million mammals; 1.0 million birds) were exported in this period, 30 million (similar to 300 species) of them being wild-caught. In addition 18 million pieces and 2 million kg of live corals were exported. Malaysia, Vietnam, Indonesia and China are the major exporters of wild-caught animals and the European Union and Japan are the most significant importers. Over this period exports in birds significantly decreased, trade in the other taxa either increased or remained stable. For all taxa but butterflies the vast majority of individuals represent wild-caught individuals. Records of illegal or undeclared international trade are scant but can be significantly larger than levels of official exports. It is concluded that there is an urgent need for better assessments of what levels of exploitation are sustainable (including exploring appropriate proxies for Non Detriment Findings), for initiatives to make regulatory mechanisms more effective (including the introduction of minimum mandatory standards and monitoring selected wildlife trade hubs), and for better licensing and registration. Funding for at least some of these initiatives can be obtained by imposing small levies on exports of CITES-listed wildlife.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-009-9758-4","ISSN":"09603115","issue":"4","page":"1101–1114","title":"An overview of international wildlife trade from Southeast Asia","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, 18 million pieces and 2 million kg of live corals were exported ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UhQUDKpD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nijman, 2010a)","plainCitation":"(Nijman, 2010a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32739,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMIG93TC"],"itemData":{"id":32739,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife trade is the very heart of biodiversity conservation and sustainable development providing an income for some of the least economically affluent people and it generates considerable revenue nationally. In Asia the unsustainable trade in wildlife has been identified as one of the main conservation challenges. Internationally, wildlife trade is regulated through the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of wild fauna and flora (CITES) to which all Southeast Asian nations are signatory. I obtained data on international trade in CITES-listed animals in the period 1998-2007. In all \\\\textgreater 35 million animals (0.3 million butterflies; 16.0 million seahorses; 0.1 million other fish; 17.4 million reptiles; 0.4 million mammals; 1.0 million birds) were exported in this period, 30 million (similar to 300 species) of them being wild-caught. In addition 18 million pieces and 2 million kg of live corals were exported. Malaysia, Vietnam, Indonesia and China are the major exporters of wild-caught animals and the European Union and Japan are the most significant importers. Over this period exports in birds significantly decreased, trade in the other taxa either increased or remained stable. For all taxa but butterflies the vast majority of individuals represent wild-caught individuals. Records of illegal or undeclared international trade are scant but can be significantly larger than levels of official exports. It is concluded that there is an urgent need for better assessments of what levels of exploitation are sustainable (including exploring appropriate proxies for Non Detriment Findings), for initiatives to make regulatory mechanisms more effective (including the introduction of minimum mandatory standards and monitoring selected wildlife trade hubs), and for better licensing and registration. Funding for at least some of these initiatives can be obtained by imposing small levies on exports of CITES-listed wildlife.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-009-9758-4","ISSN":"09603115","issue":"4","page":"1101–1114","title":"An overview of international wildlife trade from Southeast Asia","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The main importers are European Union and Japan, while main exporting nations are Malaysia, Vietnam, Indonesia and China ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KUVrfdlG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nijman, 2010a)","plainCitation":"(Nijman, 2010a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32739,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VMIG93TC"],"itemData":{"id":32739,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife trade is the very heart of biodiversity conservation and sustainable development providing an income for some of the least economically affluent people and it generates considerable revenue nationally. In Asia the unsustainable trade in wildlife has been identified as one of the main conservation challenges. Internationally, wildlife trade is regulated through the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of wild fauna and flora (CITES) to which all Southeast Asian nations are signatory. I obtained data on international trade in CITES-listed animals in the period 1998-2007. In all \\\\textgreater 35 million animals (0.3 million butterflies; 16.0 million seahorses; 0.1 million other fish; 17.4 million reptiles; 0.4 million mammals; 1.0 million birds) were exported in this period, 30 million (similar to 300 species) of them being wild-caught. In addition 18 million pieces and 2 million kg of live corals were exported. Malaysia, Vietnam, Indonesia and China are the major exporters of wild-caught animals and the European Union and Japan are the most significant importers. Over this period exports in birds significantly decreased, trade in the other taxa either increased or remained stable. For all taxa but butterflies the vast majority of individuals represent wild-caught individuals. Records of illegal or undeclared international trade are scant but can be significantly larger than levels of official exports. It is concluded that there is an urgent need for better assessments of what levels of exploitation are sustainable (including exploring appropriate proxies for Non Detriment Findings), for initiatives to make regulatory mechanisms more effective (including the introduction of minimum mandatory standards and monitoring selected wildlife trade hubs), and for better licensing and registration. Funding for at least some of these initiatives can be obtained by imposing small levies on exports of CITES-listed wildlife.","container-title":"Biodiversity and Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-009-9758-4","ISSN":"09603115","issue":"4","page":"1101–1114","title":"An overview of international wildlife trade from Southeast Asia","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Nijman","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Trade bans may reduce the risk of biological invasions in destination countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oJyWeLYZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cardador, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Cardador, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32759,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZGM3SHFF"],"itemData":{"id":32759,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"e12631","source":"Google Scholar","title":"The European trade ban on wild birds reduced invasion risks","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Cardador","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Tella","given":"José L."},{"family":"Anadón","given":"José D."},{"family":"Abellán","given":"Pedro"},{"family":"Carrete","given":"Martina"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A limitation of the effectiveness of Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora lies in its very nature that it regulates international trade, while trade often is domestic. For example, in Japan two species of bears (Ursus thibetanus and U. arctos) are hunted and traded for their gallbladder and meat mostly domestically .  By removing legal trade, incentives to preserve wild species may diminish; this can push trade ‘underground’ where it is unmonitored, uncontrolled, and ultimately the preservation of a species can be ineffective and lost ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PYXLDbNq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biggs, 2013; Conrad, 2012b; Hutton & Dickson, 2000; Hutton & Leader-Williams, 2003; Rivalan, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Biggs, 2013; Conrad, 2012b; Hutton & Dickson, 2000; Hutton & Leader-Williams, 2003; Rivalan, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":862,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDB5KSPI"],"itemData":{"id":862,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"A trade ban limits supply, therefore raising prices and driving black market poaching.\\nA trade ban limits supply, therefore raising prices and driving black market poaching.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.1229998","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6123","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2013, American Association for the Advancement of Science","note":"PMID: 23449582","page":"1038-1039","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"Legal Trade of Africa's Rhino Horns","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/339/6123/1038","volume":"339","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Courchamp","given":"Franck"},{"family":"Martin","given":"Rowan"},{"family":"Possingham","given":"Hugh P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",3,1]]}}},{"id":32790,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2BQRMSEW"],"itemData":{"id":32790,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Tropical Conservation Science","DOI":"10.1177/194008291200500302","ISSN":"1940-0829, 1940-0829","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Tropical Conservation Science","language":"en","page":"245-254","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trade Bans: A Perfect Storm for Poaching?","title-short":"Trade Bans","URL":"http://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/194008291200500302","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Conrad","given":"Kirsten"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",9]]}}},{"id":32334,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WGV2EI39"],"itemData":{"id":32334,"type":"book","publisher":"Earthscan Publications, London","title":"Endangered Species, Threatened Convention. The Past, Present and Future of CITES","author":[{"family":"Hutton","given":"J. M."},{"family":"Dickson","given":"Barney"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":32738,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RQIIYJ5P"],"itemData":{"id":32738,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Discussions of sustainable use have become polarized. Welfarists oppose all use that involves killing animals. Among conservationists polarization arises in part from failure to distinguish between different ideas nestled under the umbrella term of ‘sustainable use’. These include direct use as an imperative or choice, the ideal of keeping any use within biologically sustainable limits, and use as a possible conservation strategy that can create positive incentives, which are key where land could otherwise be converted to biodiversity-unfriendly practices. People will continue to use wild living resources, which increasing human populations could further deplete. In response the conservation community can follow one of two approaches. On the one hand, it can try to stop use through the establishment of strictly protected areas and by enforcing legislation, although many would question the ethical position of imposing such an approach. On the other hand, it can work to introduce the wider management systems needed to deliver sustainable use and, if possible, incentive-driven conservation. Because most rural populations will continue using wild living resources in human-dominated landscapes, sustainable use and incentive-driven conservation should both be at the centre of the conservation agenda this century. Both species- and ecosystem-based management are likely to have a role in sustainable use. However, current enthusiasm for the ecosystem approach may throw up unexpected consequences, making the search for sustainability even more polarized. Nevertheless, direct use of species cannot provide sufficient incentives to ensure the continued delivery of ecosystem services, which need to be fully incorporated in the global accounting system.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605303000395","ISSN":"1365-3008, 0030-6053","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"215-226","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Sustainable use and incentive-driven conservation: realigning human and conservation interests","title-short":"Sustainable use and incentive-driven conservation","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/sustainable-use-and-incentivedriven-conservation-realigning-human-and-conservation-interests/5C8B0923D18E8FFBB39FA7DFA70F0B72","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Hutton","given":"Jon M."},{"family":"Leader-Williams","given":"Nigel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":34380,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ANJDP2ZQ"],"itemData":{"id":34380,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Proactive management of trade in endangered wildlife makes more sense than last-minute bans that can themselves increase trading activity, argue Philippe Rivalan and his co-authors.","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"10.1038/447529a","ISSN":"1476-4687","language":"en","license":"2007 Nature Publishing Group","page":"529-530","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Can bans stimulate wildlife trade?","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/447529a","volume":"447","author":[{"family":"Rivalan","given":"Philippe"},{"family":"Delmas","given":"Virginie"},{"family":"Angulo","given":"Elena"},{"family":"Bull","given":"Leigh S."},{"family":"Hall","given":"Richard J."},{"family":"Courchamp","given":"Franck"},{"family":"Rosser","given":"Alison M."},{"family":"Leader-Williams","given":"Nigel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",5,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Prohibition on trading wild species products such as tusks and timber reduce supply (which is the whole point), but this also tends to drive up illegal prices. Often, markets are supplied illicitly despite the trade’s prohibition under the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora as poaching gangs are incentivized largely due to very high prices for these illicit goods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rbw1e1nj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(MacMillan, 2017)","plainCitation":"(MacMillan, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32771,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YE36E6FZ"],"itemData":{"id":32771,"type":"book","abstract":"This study aims to understand better the demand for traditional medicine and more contemporary health-related reasons, collectively referred to as ATM in this report, in Viet Nam. The country is strategically important for illegally traded wildlife products, such as pangolin scales and rhino horn. Using an in-person survey of over 1,000 respondents on wildlife consumed as medicine, the study had four main objectives: identify consumer profile of ATM users (age, income, gender, etc.), identify recent trends in ATM consumption among users, focusing on two high conservation-value species (pangolin and rhino), identify the main attributes of demand (quality, price, harvesting method, etc.), evaluate the policy options to reduce demand for high-conservation species. To provide insights and broader overview of market trends, the authors also interviewed experts in ATM in Viet Nam.","collection-title":"ITC publications","publisher":"International Trade Centre (UN/WTO)","source":"Queen's University Belfast","title":"Demand  in  Viet  Nam  for  rhino  horn  used  in  traditional  medicine","author":[{"family":"MacMillan","given":"Douglas"},{"family":"Bozzola","given":"Martina"},{"family":"Hanley","given":"Nick"},{"family":"Sheremet","given":"Oleg"},{"family":"Kasterine","given":"Alexander"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Also, prohibiting trade does not necessarily create incentives for conservation, which could be especially relevant if the habitat of a species interacts with alternative land uses . Red Sanders (RS, Pterocarpus santalinus L.) is an endangered timber tree species in Andhra Prades in Southern India . The species was put on the list of endangered species by the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, restricting international trade substantially. Only a small quantity auctioned by governmental agencies enter the international market. High demand and limited supply from private landowners, creates strong incentives for illegal removals from public forests . Local communities do not tend to benefit from timber harvesting (as it is illegal) and does not create any incentives to conserve the forest. Providing incentives to private landowners to grow red Sanders on private land and ensuring that local communities benefit are key elements to encourage practices that are economically and ecologically sustainable. Yet, facilitating sustainable trade, without also creating opportunities to intensify illegal harvests is perceived as very challenging by various stakeholder groups .  
      A further challenge in implementing the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Appendix II listing of species arises if species are caught as by-take or by-catch . The whole genus of seahorses was listed in the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora Appendix II in 2002 because of the adverse effects of harvests and trade on sustainability of wild populations. Evidence from Thailand suggested that seahorses where often caught as bycatch in trawl fisheries, allowing fishers to continue fishing if harvesting the target species was not so profitable, for example due to overexploitation . Globally, most of these by-catches were sold and traded without entering official statistics. As a result, official Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora data is often substantially lower than actual catches . Declining abundances, as reported in Malaysia and Thailand suggests that current harvest rates are unsustainable . What hampers sustainable use is the fact that the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora process of listing or uplisting can be slow and respond with a delay to evidence on how threatened a species is . Also, sustainable use would be further facilitated if trade data collected by public authorities mostly for tax reasons were closer aligned with individual species to provide insights on scale of trade .  
      
4.2.4.4.2 Global financial flows 
Key messages: 
* “Tax havens” and global crime facilitate unsustainable use of wild species (established, but incomplete)
* Micro-credits and foreign investments can play a positive role in enabling sustainable uses if properly combined with wider enabling factors such as investments in human and social capital, but investments may also enable destructive, and unsustainable use (inconclusive). 
* In some cases, remittances play a role in supporting livelihoods and may reduce pressure on resources, but may also provide the capital to enable unsustainable uses and practices (inconclusive)

   Financial actors, such as international development and commercial banks and institutional investors are increasingly interested in sustainability, and play a role in ecosystem change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9457Yy0v","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Galaz, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Galaz, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B2P6L6N2"],"itemData":{"id":6833,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Financial actors and capital play a key role in extractive economic activities around the world, as well as in current efforts to avoid dangerous climate change. Here, in contrast to standard approaches in finance, sustainability and climate change, we elaborate in what ways financial actors affect key biomes around the world, and through this known “tipping elements” in the Earth system. We combine Earth system and sustainability sciences with corporate finance to develop a methodology that allows us to link financial actors to economic activities modifying biomes of key importance for stabilizing Earth’s climate system. Our analysis of key owners of companies operating in the Amazon rainforest (Brazil) and boreal forests (Russia and Canada) identifies a small set of international financial actors with considerable, but as of yet unrealized, globally spanning influence. We denote these “Financial Giants”, and elaborate how incentives and disincentives currently influence their potential to bolster or undermine the stability of the Earth’s climate system.","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2018.09.008","ISSN":"0959-3780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"296-302","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Finance and the Earth system – Exploring the links between financial actors and non-linear changes in the climate system","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959378018300360","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Galaz","given":"Victor"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Dauriach","given":"Alice"},{"family":"Scholtens","given":"Bert"},{"family":"Steffen","given":"Will"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. ‘Green’ finance, with the explicit goal to have positive ecological and social impacts holds potential to contribute to sustainable use of wild species, though it is currently a niche market, as green bonds account for less than 0.6% of the total market ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rI6qvrUW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Scholtens, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Scholtens, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6832,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6PAAEBBI"],"itemData":{"id":6832,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Finance ignores ecosystems, which has resulted in a growing list of environmental and social problems. In this article, the importance of ecology for finance is assessed. We suggest The piece also suggests that the financial intermediation perspective can align finance and ecology for the benefit of society. This requires that financial institutions account for information about the impact of finance on the environment and vice versa, and that they are held accountable by their supervisors in this domain.","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2017.03.013","ISSN":"0169-5347","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"500-505","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Why Finance Should Care about Ecology","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0169534717300915","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Scholtens","given":"Bert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",7,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, most ‘green’ initiatives tend to focus on reducing emissions, rather than ecosystem change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tNLnGNR6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Galaz, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Galaz, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B2P6L6N2"],"itemData":{"id":6833,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Financial actors and capital play a key role in extractive economic activities around the world, as well as in current efforts to avoid dangerous climate change. Here, in contrast to standard approaches in finance, sustainability and climate change, we elaborate in what ways financial actors affect key biomes around the world, and through this known “tipping elements” in the Earth system. We combine Earth system and sustainability sciences with corporate finance to develop a methodology that allows us to link financial actors to economic activities modifying biomes of key importance for stabilizing Earth’s climate system. Our analysis of key owners of companies operating in the Amazon rainforest (Brazil) and boreal forests (Russia and Canada) identifies a small set of international financial actors with considerable, but as of yet unrealized, globally spanning influence. We denote these “Financial Giants”, and elaborate how incentives and disincentives currently influence their potential to bolster or undermine the stability of the Earth’s climate system.","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2018.09.008","ISSN":"0959-3780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"296-302","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Finance and the Earth system – Exploring the links between financial actors and non-linear changes in the climate system","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959378018300360","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Galaz","given":"Victor"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Dauriach","given":"Alice"},{"family":"Scholtens","given":"Bert"},{"family":"Steffen","given":"Will"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Conservation finance is a promising instrument to facilitate and incentivize the sustainable use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JFkcKGEb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Huwyler, 2014; Meyers, 2020; UNEP, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Huwyler, 2014; Meyers, 2020; UNEP, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32766,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BDQLX569"],"itemData":{"id":32766,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Credit Suisse, WWF, McKinsey&Company","title":"Conservation Finance: Moving beyond donor funding toward an investor-driven approach","author":[{"family":"Huwyler","given":"Fabian"},{"family":"Käppeli","given":"Jürg"},{"family":"Serafimova","given":"Katharina"},{"family":"Swanson","given":"Eric"},{"family":"Tobin","given":"John"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":32765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YTVFNMXH"],"itemData":{"id":32765,"type":"article-journal","title":"Conservation finance: a framework","author":[{"family":"Meyers","given":"David"},{"family":"Bohorquez","given":"John"},{"family":"Cumming","given":"Tracey"},{"family":"Emerton","given":"Lucy"},{"literal":"Heuvel, Onno van den"},{"family":"Riva","given":"Massimiliano"},{"family":"Victurine","given":"Ray"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32763,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HTAS86WS"],"itemData":{"id":32763,"type":"article-journal","note":"publisher: United Nations Environment Programme","title":"State of finance for nature. Tripling investments in nature?based solutions by 2030","author":[{"family":"UNEP","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Conservation finance can be defined as ‘mechanisms and strategies that generate, manage, and deploy financial resources and align incentives to achieve nature conservation outcomes” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YbvZEbbl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Meyers, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Meyers, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YTVFNMXH"],"itemData":{"id":32765,"type":"article-journal","title":"Conservation finance: a framework","author":[{"family":"Meyers","given":"David"},{"family":"Bohorquez","given":"John"},{"family":"Cumming","given":"Tracey"},{"family":"Emerton","given":"Lucy"},{"literal":"Heuvel, Onno van den"},{"family":"Riva","given":"Massimiliano"},{"family":"Victurine","given":"Ray"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Obstacles around conservation finance are related to underfunding, and also inefficient use of available funds, i.e. funds not ending up where they would make most sustainability impact  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"F7xsLlKg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Anyango-van Zwieten, 2020, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Anyango-van Zwieten, 2020, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32762,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DNU48T84"],"itemData":{"id":32762,"type":"thesis","event-place":"Wageningen","genre":"phd","language":"eng","note":"ISBN: 9789463953405\\npage: -","publisher":"Wageningen University","publisher-place":"Wageningen","source":"library.wur.nl","title":"Networks and flows of conservation finance: the case of World Wide Fund for Nature (WWF)","title-short":"Networks and flows of conservation finance","URL":"https://library.wur.nl/WebQuery/wurpubs/565520","author":[{"family":"Anyango-van Zwieten","given":"Nowella"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32779,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HGVYMAV5"],"itemData":{"id":32779,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"One of the often-cited challenges in biodiversity conservation is financing. But publications on financing are mostly confined to specific financial mechanisms, regions and actors. Fewer attempts have been made to find overall trends. Through a thematic review of 64 peer-reviewed articles, covering different disciplines and published since 2010, this study found three dominant themes recurring around the topic of biodiversity financing: underfunding, inefficient funding distribution and the pursuit for innovative financial mechanisms. Estimates of the funding gap are much higher than previously calculated and continuously increasing given the acceleration of new threats. Proposals for better targeting of available finances advocate for the use of priority protocols focussed on objectivity and efficiency. However, in practice funding allocation does not seem to follow objective indicators and remains quite inconsistent with conservation needs. This analysis shows that at the core of innovative financial mechanisms are new strategic networks between governments, civil society and businesses. Understanding these new networks is crucial for better capturing the patterns of pooling, mixing and directing financial flows, and the subsequent implications for policy and prioritization criteria. This paper argues that theories focusing on networks and flows could present a useful perspective for future studies on biodiversity financing.","container-title":"Journal of Integrative Environmental Sciences","DOI":"10.1080/1943815X.2020.1866616","ISSN":"1943-815X","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Taylor & Francis\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/1943815X.2020.1866616","page":"19-35","source":"Taylor and Francis+NEJM","title":"Topical themes in biodiversity financing","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/1943815X.2020.1866616","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Anyango-van Zwieten","given":"Nowella"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Financial standards may play a positive role in promoting sustainable practices. 	The recent Principles for Investment in Sustainable Wild-Caught Fisheries (www.fisheriesprinciples.org), launched in 2018 represent a voluntary framework to provide guidance to financial institutions in ensuring that  investments in wild-caught fisheries are in line with environmental sustainability and social responsibility ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"68U5o1cF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jouffray, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Jouffray, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6831,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/G2QNDXXS"],"itemData":{"id":6831,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Can finance contribute to seafood sustainability? This is an increasingly relevant question given the projected growth of seafood markets and the magnitude of social and environmental challenges associated with seafood production. As more capital enters the seafood industry, it becomes crucial that investments steer the sector toward improved sustainability, as opposed to fueling unsustainable working conditions and overexploitation of resources. Using a mixed-methods approach, we map where different financial mechanisms are most salient along a seafood firm’s development trajectory and identify three leverage points that can redirect capital toward more sustainable practices: loan covenants, stock exchange listing rules, and shareholder activism. We argue that seafood sustainability requirements need to be integrated into traditional financial services and propose key research avenues for academic, policy, and practice communities. While our study focuses on the role of finance in seafood sustainability, the insights developed are also of high relevance to other extractive industries.","container-title":"Science Advances","DOI":"10.1126/sciadv.aax3324","ISSN":"2375-2548","issue":"10","journalAbbreviation":"Sci. Adv.","language":"en","page":"eaax3324","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Leverage points in the financial sector for seafood sustainability","URL":"https://advances.sciencemag.org/lookup/doi/10.1126/sciadv.aax3324","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Jouffray","given":"Jean-Baptiste"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Wassénius","given":"Emmy"},{"family":"Bebbington","given":"Jan"},{"family":"Scholtens","given":"Bert"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While there is established, but incomplete evidence of large flows of finance being channeled through tax havens enabling unsustainable practices, there is much fewer, and generally inconclusive evidence of global financial flows being used to enable sustainable practices regarding the use of wild species.  Literature on foreign investments targeting the sustainable use of wild species is limited to examples of micro-credit schemes and remittances, with a noteworthy absence of  literature on larger foreign financial flows. Also, while large (foreign) corporations tend to have a key impact on sustainability, evidence on the question under which conditions those key actors can contribute to sustainable use of wild species is inconclusive. 
   
The role of “tax havens” and global crime
An increasingly globalized financial system may create opportunities and loopholes to engage in unsustainable and illegal practices. While it is well established that tax havens can have a decisive role in enabling the unsustainable use of wild species, evidence on the size of the actual impact is incomplete. Recent estimates show that between 10 and 30% of all foreign direct investments are channeled through tax haven jurisdictions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VyuVjYc4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Galaz, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Galaz, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B2P6L6N2"],"itemData":{"id":6833,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Financial actors and capital play a key role in extractive economic activities around the world, as well as in current efforts to avoid dangerous climate change. Here, in contrast to standard approaches in finance, sustainability and climate change, we elaborate in what ways financial actors affect key biomes around the world, and through this known “tipping elements” in the Earth system. We combine Earth system and sustainability sciences with corporate finance to develop a methodology that allows us to link financial actors to economic activities modifying biomes of key importance for stabilizing Earth’s climate system. Our analysis of key owners of companies operating in the Amazon rainforest (Brazil) and boreal forests (Russia and Canada) identifies a small set of international financial actors with considerable, but as of yet unrealized, globally spanning influence. We denote these “Financial Giants”, and elaborate how incentives and disincentives currently influence their potential to bolster or undermine the stability of the Earth’s climate system.","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2018.09.008","ISSN":"0959-3780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"296-302","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Finance and the Earth system – Exploring the links between financial actors and non-linear changes in the climate system","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959378018300360","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Galaz","given":"Victor"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Dauriach","given":"Alice"},{"family":"Scholtens","given":"Bert"},{"family":"Steffen","given":"Will"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Galaz ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cHccdm4w","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2018)","plainCitation":"(2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B2P6L6N2"],"itemData":{"id":6833,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Financial actors and capital play a key role in extractive economic activities around the world, as well as in current efforts to avoid dangerous climate change. Here, in contrast to standard approaches in finance, sustainability and climate change, we elaborate in what ways financial actors affect key biomes around the world, and through this known “tipping elements” in the Earth system. We combine Earth system and sustainability sciences with corporate finance to develop a methodology that allows us to link financial actors to economic activities modifying biomes of key importance for stabilizing Earth’s climate system. Our analysis of key owners of companies operating in the Amazon rainforest (Brazil) and boreal forests (Russia and Canada) identifies a small set of international financial actors with considerable, but as of yet unrealized, globally spanning influence. We denote these “Financial Giants”, and elaborate how incentives and disincentives currently influence their potential to bolster or undermine the stability of the Earth’s climate system.","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2018.09.008","ISSN":"0959-3780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"296-302","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Finance and the Earth system – Exploring the links between financial actors and non-linear changes in the climate system","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959378018300360","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Galaz","given":"Victor"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Dauriach","given":"Alice"},{"family":"Scholtens","given":"Bert"},{"family":"Steffen","given":"Will"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,1]]}},"label":"page","suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} investigated flows of foreign capital from financial actors based outside Brazil, to the nine largest companies operating in the soy and beef sectors of the Brazilian Amazon — two sectors representing key drivers of deforestation. Between October 2000 and August 2011, 68% of all investigated foreign capital into this region was transferred through tax havens, representing 90–100% of foreign capital for some companies investigated ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XbVbjOzn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Galaz, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Galaz, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B2P6L6N2"],"itemData":{"id":6833,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Financial actors and capital play a key role in extractive economic activities around the world, as well as in current efforts to avoid dangerous climate change. Here, in contrast to standard approaches in finance, sustainability and climate change, we elaborate in what ways financial actors affect key biomes around the world, and through this known “tipping elements” in the Earth system. We combine Earth system and sustainability sciences with corporate finance to develop a methodology that allows us to link financial actors to economic activities modifying biomes of key importance for stabilizing Earth’s climate system. Our analysis of key owners of companies operating in the Amazon rainforest (Brazil) and boreal forests (Russia and Canada) identifies a small set of international financial actors with considerable, but as of yet unrealized, globally spanning influence. We denote these “Financial Giants”, and elaborate how incentives and disincentives currently influence their potential to bolster or undermine the stability of the Earth’s climate system.","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2018.09.008","ISSN":"0959-3780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"296-302","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Finance and the Earth system – Exploring the links between financial actors and non-linear changes in the climate system","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959378018300360","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Galaz","given":"Victor"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Dauriach","given":"Alice"},{"family":"Scholtens","given":"Bert"},{"family":"Steffen","given":"Will"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}
	In the fishing industry, 4% of all registered vessels worldwide are currently flagged in a tax haven ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6nPOoMup","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Galaz, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Galaz, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B2P6L6N2"],"itemData":{"id":6833,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Financial actors and capital play a key role in extractive economic activities around the world, as well as in current efforts to avoid dangerous climate change. Here, in contrast to standard approaches in finance, sustainability and climate change, we elaborate in what ways financial actors affect key biomes around the world, and through this known “tipping elements” in the Earth system. We combine Earth system and sustainability sciences with corporate finance to develop a methodology that allows us to link financial actors to economic activities modifying biomes of key importance for stabilizing Earth’s climate system. Our analysis of key owners of companies operating in the Amazon rainforest (Brazil) and boreal forests (Russia and Canada) identifies a small set of international financial actors with considerable, but as of yet unrealized, globally spanning influence. We denote these “Financial Giants”, and elaborate how incentives and disincentives currently influence their potential to bolster or undermine the stability of the Earth’s climate system.","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2018.09.008","ISSN":"0959-3780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"296-302","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Finance and the Earth system – Exploring the links between financial actors and non-linear changes in the climate system","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959378018300360","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Galaz","given":"Victor"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Dauriach","given":"Alice"},{"family":"Scholtens","given":"Bert"},{"family":"Steffen","given":"Will"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Belhabib ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tGjfYUlv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2018)","plainCitation":"(2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":9797,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NIYKV52V"],"itemData":{"id":9797,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12360","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"e12360","title":"Trends in industrial and artisanal catch per effort in West African fisheries","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Belhabib","given":"Dyhia"},{"family":"Greer","given":"Krista"},{"family":"Pauly","given":"Daniel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}},"label":"page","suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} used the database of the Criminal Record of Fishing Vessels, that includes all vessels that were caught or identified as involved in illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing within national, regional and high seas jurisdictions, to investigate whether or not the vessels (2,800) and their associated companies or owners (900) were blacklisted. Using such list, less than 2% of fishing vessels caught or observed specifically engaging in unreported and unregulated activities were flagged in tax haven jurisdictions at the time of the offence, and only 11% of the total number of offending vessels belonged to companies whose address was listed within a tax haven jurisdiction ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9TFllYRi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Belhabib & Le Billon, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Belhabib & Le Billon, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6830,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YVKNI3KH"],"itemData":{"id":6830,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1038/s41559-018-0704-2","ISSN":"2397-334X","issue":"11","language":"en","license":"2018 Springer Nature Limited","note":"number: 11\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group","page":"1679-1679","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Tax havens are the tip of the iceberg","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41559-018-0704-2","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Belhabib","given":"Dyhia"},{"family":"Le Billon","given":"Philippe"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Still, tax havens play a larger role when looking at the vessels that have been caught violating more often and are consequently blacklisted. Using a list of 209 blacklisted vessels by regional fisheries management organizations and the vessels for which a purple notice was issued by the International Criminal Police Organization, Galaz ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JeQTy8uh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2018)","plainCitation":"(2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B2P6L6N2"],"itemData":{"id":6833,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Financial actors and capital play a key role in extractive economic activities around the world, as well as in current efforts to avoid dangerous climate change. Here, in contrast to standard approaches in finance, sustainability and climate change, we elaborate in what ways financial actors affect key biomes around the world, and through this known “tipping elements” in the Earth system. We combine Earth system and sustainability sciences with corporate finance to develop a methodology that allows us to link financial actors to economic activities modifying biomes of key importance for stabilizing Earth’s climate system. Our analysis of key owners of companies operating in the Amazon rainforest (Brazil) and boreal forests (Russia and Canada) identifies a small set of international financial actors with considerable, but as of yet unrealized, globally spanning influence. We denote these “Financial Giants”, and elaborate how incentives and disincentives currently influence their potential to bolster or undermine the stability of the Earth’s climate system.","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2018.09.008","ISSN":"0959-3780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"296-302","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Finance and the Earth system – Exploring the links between financial actors and non-linear changes in the climate system","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959378018300360","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Galaz","given":"Victor"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Dauriach","given":"Alice"},{"family":"Scholtens","given":"Bert"},{"family":"Steffen","given":"Will"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,1]]}},"label":"page","suppress-author":true}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found that 70% of the known vessels engaging  in illegal, unreported and unregulated fishing are, or have been, flagged under a tax haven jurisdiction, in particular Belize or Panama. 
	The use of tax havens is problematic firstly because of substantive losses in tax revenues, undermining public investments in accordance with, among others, the ambitions of the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals. Secondly, the use of these jurisdictions reduces financial transparency, making it difficult to track the contribution of distant financial flows to sustainable use of wild species in land- and seascapes globally ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZG5QHrWV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Galaz, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Galaz, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6833,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B2P6L6N2"],"itemData":{"id":6833,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Financial actors and capital play a key role in extractive economic activities around the world, as well as in current efforts to avoid dangerous climate change. Here, in contrast to standard approaches in finance, sustainability and climate change, we elaborate in what ways financial actors affect key biomes around the world, and through this known “tipping elements” in the Earth system. We combine Earth system and sustainability sciences with corporate finance to develop a methodology that allows us to link financial actors to economic activities modifying biomes of key importance for stabilizing Earth’s climate system. Our analysis of key owners of companies operating in the Amazon rainforest (Brazil) and boreal forests (Russia and Canada) identifies a small set of international financial actors with considerable, but as of yet unrealized, globally spanning influence. We denote these “Financial Giants”, and elaborate how incentives and disincentives currently influence their potential to bolster or undermine the stability of the Earth’s climate system.","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2018.09.008","ISSN":"0959-3780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"296-302","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Finance and the Earth system – Exploring the links between financial actors and non-linear changes in the climate system","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959378018300360","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Galaz","given":"Victor"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Dauriach","given":"Alice"},{"family":"Scholtens","given":"Bert"},{"family":"Steffen","given":"Will"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	While vessels have a salient role, ports are important, too. There are 4764 ports across the globe where fish might be offloaded, and approximately 2395 of these ports are under no legal obligation to implement the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations Agreement on Port State Measures requiring inspections ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qrfCSYKa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Telesetsky, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Telesetsky, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8559,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6A5Z2PQF"],"itemData":{"id":8559,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"With illegal unreported, and unregulated fishing accounting for the removal of nearly one out of every eight fish from the oceans, illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing is a problem of global proportion. A growing amount of illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing is the result of expansion into new \\"business ventures\\" by transnational organized criminal groups that are easily facilitated within the margins of the law by unregulated access to flags of convenience, little regulation of transshipments, the existence of ports of convenience, and an active business in offshore shell companies and tax havens. This Article argues that part of the failure of states to respond to the growing illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing crisis is a lack of effective governance by both vertical and horizontal government networks. In contrast, transnational criminal networks have functional and flexible governance networks that permit them to respond nimbly to changes in government enforcement. To address global illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing, horizontal government networks should focus on addressing large-scale illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing as a transnational crime problem and not as a fishery management challenge. Given the irreparable damage to both marine living resources and to fishery-related livelihood, illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing should be regarded as a transnational \\"serious crime.\\" Under the United Nations Convention against Transnational Organized Crime, states should create sanctions that explicitly identify illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing by transnational syndicates as \\"serious crime\\" (requiring at least four years of imprisonment) and invest additional state funding in monitoring and enforcement of illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing. This Article proposes a number of additional suggestions to strengthen the capacity of domestic government networks, including characterizing illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing as a \\"serious crime\\" in national fisheries codes, harmonizing criminal laws on illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing across jurisdictions, creating concurrent extraterritorial jurisdiction over large-scale illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing crimes, and authorizing states to acquire information about \\"beneficial owners\\" of illegal, unreported, and unregulated vessels.","container-title":"Ecology Law Quarterly","ISSN":"0046-1121","issue":"4","note":"publisher: [University of California, Berkeley, University of California, Berkeley, School of Law]","page":"939-997","source":"JSTOR","title":"Laundering Fish in the Global Undercurrents: Illegal, Unreported, and Unregulated Fishing and Transnational Organized Crime","title-short":"Laundering Fish in the Global Undercurrents","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/44320331","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Telesetsky","given":"Anastasia"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, complex financial transactions that may involve third-party state tax havens, and digital currencies play a role in facilitating the sale and trade of illegal catches ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jTztEkxq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Telesetsky, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Telesetsky, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8559,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6A5Z2PQF"],"itemData":{"id":8559,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"With illegal unreported, and unregulated fishing accounting for the removal of nearly one out of every eight fish from the oceans, illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing is a problem of global proportion. A growing amount of illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing is the result of expansion into new \\"business ventures\\" by transnational organized criminal groups that are easily facilitated within the margins of the law by unregulated access to flags of convenience, little regulation of transshipments, the existence of ports of convenience, and an active business in offshore shell companies and tax havens. This Article argues that part of the failure of states to respond to the growing illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing crisis is a lack of effective governance by both vertical and horizontal government networks. In contrast, transnational criminal networks have functional and flexible governance networks that permit them to respond nimbly to changes in government enforcement. To address global illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing, horizontal government networks should focus on addressing large-scale illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing as a transnational crime problem and not as a fishery management challenge. Given the irreparable damage to both marine living resources and to fishery-related livelihood, illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing should be regarded as a transnational \\"serious crime.\\" Under the United Nations Convention against Transnational Organized Crime, states should create sanctions that explicitly identify illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing by transnational syndicates as \\"serious crime\\" (requiring at least four years of imprisonment) and invest additional state funding in monitoring and enforcement of illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing. This Article proposes a number of additional suggestions to strengthen the capacity of domestic government networks, including characterizing illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing as a \\"serious crime\\" in national fisheries codes, harmonizing criminal laws on illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing across jurisdictions, creating concurrent extraterritorial jurisdiction over large-scale illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing crimes, and authorizing states to acquire information about \\"beneficial owners\\" of illegal, unreported, and unregulated vessels.","container-title":"Ecology Law Quarterly","ISSN":"0046-1121","issue":"4","note":"publisher: [University of California, Berkeley, University of California, Berkeley, School of Law]","page":"939-997","source":"JSTOR","title":"Laundering Fish in the Global Undercurrents: Illegal, Unreported, and Unregulated Fishing and Transnational Organized Crime","title-short":"Laundering Fish in the Global Undercurrents","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/44320331","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Telesetsky","given":"Anastasia"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Enforcing sustainable fishing practices through monitoring, control and surveillance projects requires substantial investments and maintenance costs. In West-African coastal countries, this implies reliance on foreign funding, where the longevity and stability can be uncertain ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vttPteFD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Neill, 2018)","plainCitation":"(O’Neill, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4372,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5PZD6TNN"],"itemData":{"id":4372,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This study investigated how an industrial tuna fishery functions in terms of procedures, practices, governance and finance in the context of Ghana, West Africa. Tuna is Ghana’s biggest seafood export, contributing significantly to the domestic fisheries sector. A case-study approach was used to analyse relevant social and economic factors at the local scale to better understand how the global seafood industry operates in a low-income country. A value-chain framework was adopted to assess market structures, sales pathways and revenue distribution. We also investigated the role of actors engaged in the industry using secondary data, interviews, questionnaires and participant observations. The results revealed a changing organisational structure in tuna production, moving from bait-boat fishing with smaller companies to large-scale purse-seine fishing backed by consolidated Asian seafood companies. Production was found to depend significantly on local female intermediaries for access to funds through prefinancing arrangements. Considerable illegalities were identified within the value chain, highlighting the need for improved partnership and licensing negotiations, and for low-cost marine control and surveillance tools. As vertical integration increases within the industry, the influence of corporations as keystone actors becomes evident for the future social and ecological sustainability of the industry.","container-title":"African Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.2989/1814232X.2018.1513866","ISSN":"1814-232X","issue":"3","page":"303–313","title":"Socioeconomic dynamics of the Ghanaian tuna industry: a value-chain approach to understanding aspects of global fisheries","title-short":"Socioeconomic dynamics of the Ghanaian tuna industry","URL":"https://doi.org/10.2989/1814232X.2018.1513866","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"O’Neill","given":"E. Drury"},{"family":"Asare","given":"N. K."},{"family":"Aheto","given":"D. W."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Global drug-trafficking has an increasing impact on the sustainable use of wild species. It has been estimated that the contribution of fishing vessels to drug trafficking has tripled between 2010 and 2017 and may account for 15% of the global retail value of illicit drugs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"keGMPeCf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Belhabib, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Belhabib, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6829,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FLE2ZQ7M"],"itemData":{"id":6829,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This study analyses drug trafficking associated with fisheries around the globe. Records of vessel interdiction carried out between 2010 and 2017 suggest that the global trade of illicit drugs relies increasingly on fishing vessels. Fishery-based trafficking is growing. A key obstacle to understanding the scope of this problem is the limited data on activities that are intentionally obscured, such as drug trafficking. Using a Fermi estimation technique for determining unknown values from limited data, we analyse 292 known cases of fishing boats engaged in drug shipment between 2010 and 2017. Results suggest that drug shipment sizes per vessel are becoming smaller over time, even as the total flow of drugs is increasing. Counter-drug enforcement intensifies this effect, suggesting that drug trafficking networks adapt to interdiction efforts making use of smaller vessels to lower the risk of seizure. The use of fishing vessels in drug trans-shipment has tripled over the past 8 years to about 15% of the global retail value of illicit drugs. Small-scale fishers are at risk of turning to drug trade as an economic buffer against poverty, especially in contexts of mounting competition over declining fish stocks or strict marine conservation. At the same time, illicit capital flowing from the narcotics trade into fisheries may be driving over-capitalization of fisheries and unsustainable resource use, ultimately to the detriment of resource-dependent coastal communities and marine ecosystems. Future research is needed to better understand whether and how small-scale fishermen turn to drug trade to counter livelihood risks of various kinds.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1111/faf.12483","ISSN":"1467-2979","issue":"5","language":"en","license":"© 2020 John Wiley & Sons Ltd","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/faf.12483","page":"992-1007","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Narco-Fish: Global fisheries and drug trafficking","title-short":"Narco-Fish","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/faf.12483","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Belhabib","given":"Dyhia"},{"family":"Billon","given":"Philippe Le"},{"family":"Wrathall","given":"David J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This may fuel overcapitalization of fishing fleets and intensify fishing efforts and ultimately undermine conservation goals. In a similar vein, cocaine trade has been shown to lead to deforestation in Central America ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kg5WYUac","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wrathall, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Wrathall, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6828,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I2XZEZW6"],"itemData":{"id":6828,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This research is motivated by the compelling finding that the illicit cocaine trade is responsible for extensive patterns of deforestation in Central America. This pattern is most pronounced in the region's large protected areas. We wanted to know how cocaine trafficking affects conservation governance in Central America's protected areas, and whether deforestation is a result of impacts on governance. To answer this question, we interviewed conservation stakeholders from key institutions at various levels in three drug-trafficking hotspots: Peten, Guatemala, Northeastern Honduras, and the Osa Peninsula in Costa Rica. We found that, in order to establish and maintain drug transit operations, drug-trafficking organizations compete with and undermine conservation governance actors and institutions. Drug trafficking impacts conservation governance in three ways: 1) it undermines long standing conservation coalitions; 2) it fuels booms in extractive activities inside protected lands; and 3) it erodes the territorial control that conservation institutions exert, exploiting strict “fortress” conservation governance models. Participatory governance models that provide locals with strong expectations of land tenure and/or institutional support for local decision-making may offer resistance to the impacts on governance institutions that we documented.","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2020.102098","ISSN":"0959-3780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"102098","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The impacts of cocaine-trafficking on conservation governance in Central America","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959378019310076","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Wrathall","given":"David J."},{"family":"Devine","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Aguilar-González","given":"Bernardo"},{"family":"Benessaiah","given":"Karina"},{"family":"Tellman","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Sesnie","given":"Steve"},{"family":"Nielsen","given":"Erik"},{"family":"Magliocca","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"McSweeney","given":"Kendra"},{"family":"Pearson","given":"Zoe"},{"family":"Ponstingel","given":"John"},{"family":"Sosa","given":"Andrea Rivera"},{"family":"Dávila","given":"Anayansi"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Drug trafficking has negative impacts on conservation, as it (i) undermines social relations and participation that are needed for conservation, (ii) fuels a highly extractive and unsustainable activity taking place in remote, and often conserved areas, and (iii) erode conservation institutions and replace them with new rules around resource use and access ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ACNkdLRh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wrathall, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Wrathall, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6828,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I2XZEZW6"],"itemData":{"id":6828,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This research is motivated by the compelling finding that the illicit cocaine trade is responsible for extensive patterns of deforestation in Central America. This pattern is most pronounced in the region's large protected areas. We wanted to know how cocaine trafficking affects conservation governance in Central America's protected areas, and whether deforestation is a result of impacts on governance. To answer this question, we interviewed conservation stakeholders from key institutions at various levels in three drug-trafficking hotspots: Peten, Guatemala, Northeastern Honduras, and the Osa Peninsula in Costa Rica. We found that, in order to establish and maintain drug transit operations, drug-trafficking organizations compete with and undermine conservation governance actors and institutions. Drug trafficking impacts conservation governance in three ways: 1) it undermines long standing conservation coalitions; 2) it fuels booms in extractive activities inside protected lands; and 3) it erodes the territorial control that conservation institutions exert, exploiting strict “fortress” conservation governance models. Participatory governance models that provide locals with strong expectations of land tenure and/or institutional support for local decision-making may offer resistance to the impacts on governance institutions that we documented.","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2020.102098","ISSN":"0959-3780","journalAbbreviation":"Global Environmental Change","language":"en","page":"102098","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The impacts of cocaine-trafficking on conservation governance in Central America","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959378019310076","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Wrathall","given":"David J."},{"family":"Devine","given":"Jennifer"},{"family":"Aguilar-González","given":"Bernardo"},{"family":"Benessaiah","given":"Karina"},{"family":"Tellman","given":"Elizabeth"},{"family":"Sesnie","given":"Steve"},{"family":"Nielsen","given":"Erik"},{"family":"Magliocca","given":"Nicholas"},{"family":"McSweeney","given":"Kendra"},{"family":"Pearson","given":"Zoe"},{"family":"Ponstingel","given":"John"},{"family":"Sosa","given":"Andrea Rivera"},{"family":"Dávila","given":"Anayansi"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",11,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Foreign investments and role of large corporations
If extractive resource use is capital intensive, large corporations tend to play a large role in sustainability outcomes, though the exact impacts are inconclusive. Foreign investment in forest products, such as timber, pulp, and paper can be a factor in driving forest conversion ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yNblqPGv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Seymour & Forwand, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Seymour & Forwand, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8551,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DVHDY45U"],"itemData":{"id":8551,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Wiley Interdisciplinary Reviews: Climate Change","issue":"6","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"803–810","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Governing sustainable forest management in the new climate regime","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Seymour","given":"Frances"},{"family":"Forwand","given":"Elizabeth"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Large capital requirements and the ability to cope with price fluctuations in certain industries tend to attract larger and vertically integrated companies, the Ghanaian tuna fishery being one example ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NLfzQjQm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Neill, 2018)","plainCitation":"(O’Neill, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4372,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5PZD6TNN"],"itemData":{"id":4372,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This study investigated how an industrial tuna fishery functions in terms of procedures, practices, governance and finance in the context of Ghana, West Africa. Tuna is Ghana’s biggest seafood export, contributing significantly to the domestic fisheries sector. A case-study approach was used to analyse relevant social and economic factors at the local scale to better understand how the global seafood industry operates in a low-income country. A value-chain framework was adopted to assess market structures, sales pathways and revenue distribution. We also investigated the role of actors engaged in the industry using secondary data, interviews, questionnaires and participant observations. The results revealed a changing organisational structure in tuna production, moving from bait-boat fishing with smaller companies to large-scale purse-seine fishing backed by consolidated Asian seafood companies. Production was found to depend significantly on local female intermediaries for access to funds through prefinancing arrangements. Considerable illegalities were identified within the value chain, highlighting the need for improved partnership and licensing negotiations, and for low-cost marine control and surveillance tools. As vertical integration increases within the industry, the influence of corporations as keystone actors becomes evident for the future social and ecological sustainability of the industry.","container-title":"African Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.2989/1814232X.2018.1513866","ISSN":"1814-232X","issue":"3","page":"303–313","title":"Socioeconomic dynamics of the Ghanaian tuna industry: a value-chain approach to understanding aspects of global fisheries","title-short":"Socioeconomic dynamics of the Ghanaian tuna industry","URL":"https://doi.org/10.2989/1814232X.2018.1513866","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"O’Neill","given":"E. Drury"},{"family":"Asare","given":"N. K."},{"family":"Aheto","given":"D. W."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Here, smaller companies showed to be weakly resilient to the constant fluctuations in prices, landings and wider financial-market dynamics typical for the global fishing industry. Bigger companies were more resilient, being protected by larger parent corporations with access to substantial capital, often less reliant on and less beneficial to local economies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2GLrSYPr","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Neill, 2018)","plainCitation":"(O’Neill, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4372,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5PZD6TNN"],"itemData":{"id":4372,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This study investigated how an industrial tuna fishery functions in terms of procedures, practices, governance and finance in the context of Ghana, West Africa. Tuna is Ghana’s biggest seafood export, contributing significantly to the domestic fisheries sector. A case-study approach was used to analyse relevant social and economic factors at the local scale to better understand how the global seafood industry operates in a low-income country. A value-chain framework was adopted to assess market structures, sales pathways and revenue distribution. We also investigated the role of actors engaged in the industry using secondary data, interviews, questionnaires and participant observations. The results revealed a changing organisational structure in tuna production, moving from bait-boat fishing with smaller companies to large-scale purse-seine fishing backed by consolidated Asian seafood companies. Production was found to depend significantly on local female intermediaries for access to funds through prefinancing arrangements. Considerable illegalities were identified within the value chain, highlighting the need for improved partnership and licensing negotiations, and for low-cost marine control and surveillance tools. As vertical integration increases within the industry, the influence of corporations as keystone actors becomes evident for the future social and ecological sustainability of the industry.","container-title":"African Journal of Marine Science","DOI":"10.2989/1814232X.2018.1513866","ISSN":"1814-232X","issue":"3","page":"303–313","title":"Socioeconomic dynamics of the Ghanaian tuna industry: a value-chain approach to understanding aspects of global fisheries","title-short":"Socioeconomic dynamics of the Ghanaian tuna industry","URL":"https://doi.org/10.2989/1814232X.2018.1513866","volume":"40","author":[{"family":"O’Neill","given":"E. Drury"},{"family":"Asare","given":"N. K."},{"family":"Aheto","given":"D. W."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In fishing, large corporations have a sizable impact on global marine stocks and can play influential roles in political decision-making. These corporations can thus be seen as keystone actors in the global seafood industry, with the ability to play a key role for positive and negative outcomes sustainability in this industry ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gJslrGOZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(\\\\uc0\\\\u214{}sterblom, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Österblom, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6812,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4VYAPIIR"],"itemData":{"id":6812,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Keystone species have a disproportionate influence on the structure and function of ecosystems. Here we analyze whether a keystone-like pattern can be observed in the relationship between transnational corporations and marine ecosystems globally. We show how thirteen corporations control 11-16% of the global marine catch (9-13 million tons) and 19-40% of the largest and most valuable stocks, including species that play important roles in their respective ecosystem. They dominate all segments of seafood production, operate through an extensive global network of subsidiaries and are profoundly involved in fisheries and aquaculture decision-making. Based on our findings, we define these companies as keystone actors of the Anthropocene. The phenomenon of keystone actors represents an increasingly important feature of the human-dominated world. Sustainable leadership by keystone actors could result in cascading effects throughout the entire seafood industry and enable a critical transition towards improved management of marine living resources and ecosystems.","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0127533","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLOS ONE","language":"en","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e0127533","source":"PLoS Journals","title":"Transnational Corporations as ‘Keystone Actors’ in Marine Ecosystems","URL":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0127533","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Österblom","given":"Henrik"},{"family":"Jouffray","given":"Jean-Baptiste"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Crona","given":"Beatrice"},{"family":"Troell","given":"Max"},{"family":"Merrie","given":"Andrew"},{"family":"Rockström","given":"Johan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",5,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	On the Solomon Islands, unregulated investment in tropical timber harvesting, has led to ecologically unsustainable outcomes and created economic and political vulnerabilities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"x4C8sqp3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Firth, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Firth, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4376,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3ZZQ72S5"],"itemData":{"id":4376,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Contemporary Pacific","page":"111–135","title":"Pacific islands trade, labor, and security in an era of globalization","author":[{"family":"Firth","given":"Stewart"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A study from the Fujian Province in China, where deforestation has been a major problem shows that more sustainable practices using a mix of longer standing species offer a potentially better return ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gj03HF5c","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ying, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Ying, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6809,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SBE6PD96"],"itemData":{"id":6809,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"223–230","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Plantation development: Economic analysis of forest management in Fujian Province, China","title-short":"Plantation development","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Ying","given":"Zhang"},{"family":"Irland","given":"LIoyd"},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Xiaohong"},{"family":"Song","given":"Yajie"},{"family":"Wen","given":"Yali"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Junchang"},{"family":"Song","given":"Weimin"},{"family":"Qiu","given":"Yang"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Property rights reforms that stimulate private, and also potentially foreign investments, have played a role in a transition towards more sustainable practices in that case ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Xj79Ofif","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ying, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Ying, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6809,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SBE6PD96"],"itemData":{"id":6809,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forest Policy and Economics","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"223–230","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Plantation development: Economic analysis of forest management in Fujian Province, China","title-short":"Plantation development","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Ying","given":"Zhang"},{"family":"Irland","given":"LIoyd"},{"family":"Zhou","given":"Xiaohong"},{"family":"Song","given":"Yajie"},{"family":"Wen","given":"Yali"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Junchang"},{"family":"Song","given":"Weimin"},{"family":"Qiu","given":"Yang"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Micro-credits 
Loans in the form of micro-credits combined with trainings or briefings by the loan provider on the environment have shown to be effective in positively changing attitudes towards ecosystems in coastal communities. In a case study on coastal community livelihoods in Aceh, Indonesia, a community-based micro credit program resulted in 35% of respondents reporting a positive change in behavior towards the marine environment, among which a large group of fishermen ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bcnAEFS4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Novriyanto, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Novriyanto, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6819,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WKRP6JHW"],"itemData":{"id":6819,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Many tropical biodiversity projects seek to combine development and conservation goals. Here, we assess the performance of a revolving fund, established by Fauna & Flora International (FFI), in delivering sustainable and equitable benefits to coastal community livelihood groups and individuals while ensuring that it did no apparent harm to the environment. Semi-structured interviews were conducted with 103 loan recipients to determine changes in attitude, perception and behaviour. After a 9 month preparation phase the project succeeded in changing a community view towards the fund from being overwhelmingly pessimistic to overwhelmingly optimistic and then loaned IDR 602,977,400 (USD 66,261), with near perfect repayment rates thereafter (indicating financial sustainability). Most (92%) respondents thought that the fund had been a success, mainly (56%) because it had increased their perceived daily income. Even though most (59%) of the loan recipients met with FFI staff at least once per month on average during the project, the linkages between conservation and development were only understood by some (40%). Nevertheless, understanding was highest amongst the project's main target group, fishermen. Furthermore, nearly half of all respondents said they now acted if they found people fishing with spear guns or throwing litter into the sea (a violation of customary law), indicating a positive change in behaviour. Although this study cannot unambiguously demonstrate that this change was purely due to the fund, as FFI was conducting other conservation activities, it helped to develop a strong relationship with the community and create an enabling environment for implementing this broader set of activities.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605312000622","ISSN":"1365-3008, 0030-6053","issue":"4","language":"en","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"508-515","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Linking coastal community livelihoods to marine conservation in Aceh, Indonesia","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/linking-coastal-community-livelihoods-to-marine-conservation-in-aceh-indonesia/57A42AE5B56A15E6F5B0AF06565062C8","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Novriyanto","given":""},{"family":"Wibowo","given":"Joni T."},{"family":"Iskandar","given":"Wiwin"},{"family":"Campbell-Smith","given":"Gail"},{"family":"Linkie","given":"Matthew"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These behaviors included acting if they found someone littering the ocean or removing coral. Reasons for this changed behavior included understanding the importance of protecting the marine environment but also general abiding by the micro-credit provider’s conservation principles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"65JLUUhc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Novriyanto, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Novriyanto, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6819,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WKRP6JHW"],"itemData":{"id":6819,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Many tropical biodiversity projects seek to combine development and conservation goals. Here, we assess the performance of a revolving fund, established by Fauna & Flora International (FFI), in delivering sustainable and equitable benefits to coastal community livelihood groups and individuals while ensuring that it did no apparent harm to the environment. Semi-structured interviews were conducted with 103 loan recipients to determine changes in attitude, perception and behaviour. After a 9 month preparation phase the project succeeded in changing a community view towards the fund from being overwhelmingly pessimistic to overwhelmingly optimistic and then loaned IDR 602,977,400 (USD 66,261), with near perfect repayment rates thereafter (indicating financial sustainability). Most (92%) respondents thought that the fund had been a success, mainly (56%) because it had increased their perceived daily income. Even though most (59%) of the loan recipients met with FFI staff at least once per month on average during the project, the linkages between conservation and development were only understood by some (40%). Nevertheless, understanding was highest amongst the project's main target group, fishermen. Furthermore, nearly half of all respondents said they now acted if they found people fishing with spear guns or throwing litter into the sea (a violation of customary law), indicating a positive change in behaviour. Although this study cannot unambiguously demonstrate that this change was purely due to the fund, as FFI was conducting other conservation activities, it helped to develop a strong relationship with the community and create an enabling environment for implementing this broader set of activities.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605312000622","ISSN":"1365-3008, 0030-6053","issue":"4","language":"en","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"508-515","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Linking coastal community livelihoods to marine conservation in Aceh, Indonesia","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/linking-coastal-community-livelihoods-to-marine-conservation-in-aceh-indonesia/57A42AE5B56A15E6F5B0AF06565062C8","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Novriyanto","given":""},{"family":"Wibowo","given":"Joni T."},{"family":"Iskandar","given":"Wiwin"},{"family":"Campbell-Smith","given":"Gail"},{"family":"Linkie","given":"Matthew"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A micro-credit plan that was combined with training on, e.g. health and hygiene, coastal ecology and home industries in coastal villages in India, has had a positive impact on the economic status of women, as well as increased awareness of the importance of conserving the coastal ecology ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NjEFhsDG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lakshmi & Rajagopalan, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Lakshmi & Rajagopalan, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6818,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IA3DH26F"],"itemData":{"id":6818,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The coastal zones are home to almost 60% of the people worldwide. The land–water interface at the coast results in high fish productivity. Industrialization has resulted in the creation of urban agglomerations on the coast primarily because of facilitation of trade by way of sea. Environmental problems resulting from industrialization and urbanization have had disastrous effect on the coastal ecology and on the socio-economic status of a number of coastal communities. The concept of “integrated coastal management” is central to the management of coastal zones and ocean areas under national jurisdiction in Chapter 17 of the Agenda 21, adopted at the UNCED in 1992. For this to be effective, coastal zone communities have to be involved, and this will be sustainable only if their socio-economic conditions are improved. This paper reports on one such attempt made in India where a global NGO is working along with a local NGO to improve the socio-economic as well as environmental conditions of a part of the east coast of India. The focus has been on achieving economic security by creating new avenues for employment and using micro-credit schemes as a forerunner for environmental protection activities.","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/S0964-5691(00)00057-0","ISSN":"0964-5691","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"749-762","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Socio-economic implications of coastal zone degradation and their mitigation: a case study from coastal villages in India","title-short":"Socio-economic implications of coastal zone degradation and their mitigation","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0964569100000570","volume":"43","author":[{"family":"Lakshmi","given":"Ahana"},{"family":"Rajagopalan","given":"R"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",1,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	However, loan programs can be ineffective when improperly targeted. For instance, a micro-credit loan scheme of the Indonesian government to promote smallholder timber production gained insufficient interest among smallholder farmers due to mismatches between the loan scheme and the characteristics of borrowers. These mismatches include: a minimum loan size that is too large for small farmers to manage, an overly burdensome application and reporting process, a lack of loan management at the local level, and improper geographic targeting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7L8LDVEM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Nugroho, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Nugroho, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6816,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9I34SIUE"],"itemData":{"id":6816,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Forestry Review","issue":"4","note":"publisher: Commonwealth Forestry Association","page":"499–508","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Financing smallholder timber planting in Indonesia: mismatches between loan scheme attributes and smallholder borrowing characteristics","title-short":"Financing smallholder timber planting in Indonesia","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Nugroho","given":"Bramasto"},{"family":"Dermawan","given":"A."},{"family":"Putzel","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Also, a study in coastal villages in the Iloilo Province, Philippines, shows that external financial support from non-governmental organizations can be an important mechanism to diversify economic activities and curb overfishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"X8w8jYni","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Andriesse, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Andriesse, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":111,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/342TBR75"],"itemData":{"id":111,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The European Journal of Development Research","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1–20","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Local differentiation in diversification challenges in eleven coastal villages in Iloilo Province, Philippines","author":[{"family":"Andriesse","given":"Edo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, financial capital will always be a complement to social and human capital, and the combination of those will affect sustainability outcomes. Hence, programs aimed at encouraging fishers’ livelihood diversification to reduce overfishing need to take all those forms of capital into consideration ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wjsAOZdH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Andriesse, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Andriesse, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":111,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/342TBR75"],"itemData":{"id":111,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The European Journal of Development Research","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1–20","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Local differentiation in diversification challenges in eleven coastal villages in Iloilo Province, Philippines","author":[{"family":"Andriesse","given":"Edo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While various independent studies have shown the potential for combinations of loans with conservation requirements for the sustainable use of wild species,  the overall evidence how this maps to sustainability outcomes is incomplete.

Remittances
Overseas remittances constitute a substantial foreign financial flow in a number of developing countries, and an important source of income for many rural and coastal households. For such households, remittances can be a poverty alleviation strategy, especially in the short term. The evidence of remittances leading to sustainable or unsustainable use of wild species is inconclusive. 
	In the coastal community of Lofanga, Tonga, overseas remittances were identified as a supporting factor for local fishing practices not yet turning into overexploitation of the open access fishery system. Remittances in this community are a sustaining factor for current, traditional ways of living and fishing, without perceptible detrimental impact on the local fish stocks ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"T1brovg2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kronen & Bender, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Kronen & Bender, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":16456,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RI6K5ZRB"],"itemData":{"id":16456,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","issue":"2","page":"195–207","title":"Assessing marine resource exploitation in Lofanga, Tonga: One case study—Two approaches","title-short":"Assessing marine resource exploitation in Lofanga, Tonga","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"Kronen","given":"Mecki"},{"family":"Bender","given":"Andrea"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Java, Indonesia, remittances sent home by mobile (mostly female) family members are used to invest in rural resources, such as dairy cows or planting of elephant grass as a fodder crop ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"M02avD41","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N. L. Peluso & Purwanto, 2018)","plainCitation":"(N. L. Peluso & Purwanto, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6808,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9FT3TNWT"],"itemData":{"id":6808,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Singapore Journal of Tropical Geography","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"6–36","source":"Google Scholar","title":"The remittance forest: Turning mobile labor into agrarian capital","title-short":"The remittance forest","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Peluso","given":"Nancy Lee"},{"family":"Purwanto","given":"Agus Budi"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} TEMPwith positive impacts on local livelihoods. 
	Yet, remittances can also provide capital for changing to capital-intensive land uses such as timber production, potentially undermining sustainable use of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hpBTW85A","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mayer, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Mayer, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6807,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BKJIKBSI"],"itemData":{"id":6807,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Landscape and Urban Planning","note":"publisher: Elsevier","page":"4–18","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Family forest owners and landscape-scale interactions: A review","title-short":"Family forest owners and landscape-scale interactions","volume":"188","author":[{"family":"Mayer","given":"Audrey L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Still, the use of remittances for changing practices remains limited. Often, only a small fraction of remittances is used in capital formation, the largest share being used for daily consumptive purposes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Dxo5lq2A","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Andriesse, 2019; Cedamon, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Andriesse, 2019; Cedamon, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":111,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/342TBR75"],"itemData":{"id":111,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The European Journal of Development Research","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1–20","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Local differentiation in diversification challenges in eleven coastal villages in Iloilo Province, Philippines","author":[{"family":"Andriesse","given":"Edo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32757,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/J5TVEQCQ"],"itemData":{"id":32757,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Farmers in Nepal mid-hills have practiced agroforestry for generations as main source or supplement of timber, firewood and fodder from government forests. The nature and extent of agroforestry practice is being challenged by rapid social and economic change particularly in the recent rise of labour out-migration and remittance income. Understanding is required of the critical factors that influence farmers in the way they adapt agroforestry to their circumstances. This paper analyses the relationship of households’ livelihood resources and agroforestry practice to identify trajectories of agroforestry adaptation to improve livelihood outcomes. Using data from a survey of 668 households, it was found that landholding, livestock holding and geographic location of farmers are key drivers for agroforestry adaptation. A multinomial logistic regression model showed that in addition to these variables, household income, household-remittance situation (whether the household is receiving remittance or not) and caste influence adaptation of agroforestry practice. The analysis indicates that resource-poor households are more likely to adapt to terraced-based agroforestry while resource-rich households adapt to woodlot agroforestry. Appropriate agroforestry interventions are: (1) develop simple silvicultural regimes to improve the quality and productivity of naturally-regenerating timber on under-utilised land; (2) develop a suite of tree and groundcover species that can be readily integrated within existing terrace-riser agroforestry practices; (3) acknowledge the different livelihood capitals of resource-poor and resource-rich groups and promote terrace-riser and woodlot agroforestry systems respectively to these groups; and (4) develop high-value fodder production systems on terrace-riser agroforestry, and also for non-arable land. The analysis generates important insights for improving agroforestry policies and practices in Nepal and in many developing countries.","container-title":"Agroforestry Systems","DOI":"10.1007/s10457-017-0090-9","ISSN":"1572-9680","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Agroforest Syst","language":"en","page":"1437-1453","source":"Springer Link","title":"Adaptation factors and futures of agroforestry systems in Nepal","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10457-017-0090-9","volume":"92","author":[{"family":"Cedamon","given":"Edwin"},{"family":"Nuberg","given":"Ian"},{"family":"Pandit","given":"Bishnu H."},{"family":"Shrestha","given":"Krishna K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10,1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the Sierra Gorda Biosphere Reserve in Mexico, most people rely to a large degree on remittances from emigrants. These remittances reduce financial pressure, but do not necessarily reduce incentives to hunt illegally ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UigSVHit","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Arroyo-Quiroz, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Arroyo-Quiroz, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32780,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KSCPHV35"],"itemData":{"id":32780,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Human-wildlife conflict (HWC) is a widespread issue of increasing concern to conservationists, as it impacts people's lives and livelihoods and reduces their tolerance to the species concerned. HWC is often interpreted as a result of people encroaching upon and destroying natural habitats, but some incidents could be linked to economically driven emigration that results in depopulation and institutional and cultural disruption. Here we use an ethnobiological approach to gain insights on HWC dynamics from a case study in Mexico, where emigration is common in rural areas. We carried out a five-year study of HWC in the Sierra Gorda Biosphere Reserve, a biodiversity-rich protected area in Mexico that also supports a human population of nearly 100,000 mostly poor and marginalized people. We found that villagers reported conflict incidents involving 25 terrestrial vertebrate species, contradicting the original self-perception that HWC mostly involved cattle ranchers and a few large carnivore species. As a response, we develop a multi-layered assessment of villagers' perspectives, emotions, and attitudes towards wildlife based on the local roles of gender, probability of encountering wildlife, and the conflicting moral beliefs and switching ethical responses of people with different cultural and economic backgrounds. Our assessment identifies the need for pluralistic approaches to enhance the sustainable use and management of wildlife in the Sierra Gorda Biosphere Reserve.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology","DOI":"10.2993/0278-0771-37.4.719","ISSN":"0278-0771, 2162-4496","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"etbi","note":"publisher: Society of Ethnobiology","page":"719-742","source":"bioone.org","title":"Local Perspectives on Conflicts with Wildlife and Their Management in the Sierra Gorda Biosphere Reserve, Mexico","URL":"https://bioone.org/journals/journal-of-ethnobiology/volume-37/issue-4/0278-0771-37.4.719/Local-Perspectives-on-Conflicts-with-Wildlife-and-Their-Management-in/10.2993/0278-0771-37.4.719.full","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Arroyo-Quiroz","given":"Inés"},{"family":"García-Barrios","given":"Raúl"},{"family":"Argueta-Villamar","given":"Arturo"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Salcido","given":"Ramón Pérez Gil"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
 
In coastal communities reliant on fishing as primary livelihood, using remittances for investments can be especially difficult due to characteristic limiting factors, such as poor infrastructure, weak human, financial and social capital and attachment to traditional ways of living ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aDMUVSx7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Andriesse, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Andriesse, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":111,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/342TBR75"],"itemData":{"id":111,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The European Journal of Development Research","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"1–20","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Local differentiation in diversification challenges in eleven coastal villages in Iloilo Province, Philippines","author":[{"family":"Andriesse","given":"Edo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The impacts of investments – and the lack thereof – on sustainability outcome is inconclusive.  

4.2.4.4.3 Tourism
Key messages:
* Activities related to tourism and supporting infrastructure may disturb wild species and undermine sustainability outcomes. At the same time, revenues from tourism can be used for conservation projects which have positive impacts on sustainable use of wild species (unresolved). 
* Traditional practices that are ecologically more sustainable, but economically less profitable may be supported when linked to tourism activities that generate additional revenues. At the same time, certain tourism related activities, such as the sale of wildlife parts and the use of live animals in entertainment, incentivizes unsustainable and sometimes illegal practices.  (unresolved). 
* In some cases, extractive forms of tourism (i.e. terrestrial animal harvesting and fishing) has a positive impact on ecological, social, and economic sustainability by generating revenues for conservation and livelihoods. However, in many cases the revenues do not reach local communities, do not contribute to conservation, and extractive tourism is unsustainable (unresolved).
Mostly, tourism can be categorized as a non-extractive practice, though tourism based on trophy hunting and fishing can be described as extractive. Tourism itself is not a driver, but a practice, where the underlying driver is increased mobility. More specifically, the opportunity to travel recreationally has increased on a global scale, and also possibilities to reach places that were inaccessible in the past for tourists.

Non-extractive forms of tourism 
Non-extractive forms of tourisms, i.e., observing wild species affects sustainability in various ways. First, as a direct driver, tourist activities and infrastructure can disturb wild species, acting as a stressor. Second, tourism generates revenues which may allow for conservation investments. Third, revenues from tourists may give incentives to users to adapt practices that may be more or less sustainable. Hence, whether tourism has a positive or negative effect on ecological sustainability is highly context-dependent. Pegas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gVIMtYGL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pegas, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Pegas, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6871,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/C5GA8DGV"],"itemData":{"id":6871,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Local communities are often encouraged to adopt alternative land-use practices to minimize their impacts on biodiversity. Tourism is one of these practices but it can also impact conservation and traditional resource use activities. Using a review of the International Union for Conservation of Nature Red List database as well as nature-based tourism, ethnozoology, ethnobiology, and wildlife conservation and trade literature, we examined the influence of tourism on the traditional resource use–conservation balance and the potential outcomes for species conservation. We identified 547 local culturally important species (LCIS): 369 were threatened by traditional practices (e.g., hunting) and 161 were protected (e.g., totemic species). Most LCIS were found in the Indomalayan, Neotropical, and Afrotropical regions and roughly 30% were involved in nature-based tourism, particularly mammals. Very few LCIS (<5) were threatened by tourism. Results suggested that tourism can support species conservation and protect traditional practices by providing alternative local sustainable development options.","container-title":"Human Dimensions of Wildlife","DOI":"10.1080/10871209.2015.1037939","ISSN":"1087-1209","issue":"5","note":"publisher: Routledge\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/10871209.2015.1037939","page":"454-469","source":"Taylor and Francis+NEJM","title":"Interdependencies Among Traditional Resource Use Practices, Sustainable Tourism, and Biodiversity Conservation: A Global Assessment","title-short":"Interdependencies Among Traditional Resource Use Practices, Sustainable Tourism, and Biodiversity Conservation","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/10871209.2015.1037939","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Pegas","given":"Fernanda de Vasconcellos"},{"family":"Grignon","given":"Julien"},{"family":"Morrison","given":"Clare"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",9,3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} assess how tourism affects the sustainability of 547 locally culturally important species. While a third of those were part of some form of nature-based tourism only three percent were actually threatened by tourism. This suggests that threats from tourism are mostly indirect, while it can play a big role in supporting species conservation and protect traditional practices.
	Investment in conservation areas is an important dimension of tourism that contributes to the sustainable use of wild species. Tourism to protected areas generates an estimated US$ 600 billion annually ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1lges7J7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(The World Bank, 2018)","plainCitation":"(The World Bank, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":495,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X63KD9FV"],"itemData":{"id":495,"type":"document","title":"Growing Wildlife-Based Tourism Sustainably: A New Report and Q&A","URL":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2018/03/01/growing-wildlife-based-tourism-sustainably-a-new-report-and-qahttps://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2018/03/01/growing-wildlife-based-tourism-sustainably-a-new-report-and-qa","author":[{"family":"The World Bank","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the USA alone, park fees contributed US$ 21 billion in 2019 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HQtgnGBQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(National Park Service, 2019)","plainCitation":"(National Park Service, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32758,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F2X2GM6U"],"itemData":{"id":32758,"type":"document","title":"2019 National Park Visitor Spending Effects","author":[{"literal":"National Park Service"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Africa, 14 countries generate an estimated US$ 142 million in park entrance fees ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9HMVsCfo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Tourism Organization, 2014)","plainCitation":"(World Tourism Organization, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31342,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V2YAVGY3"],"itemData":{"id":31342,"type":"book","event-place":"Madrid","ISBN":"978-92-844-1675-2","publisher":"UNWTO","publisher-place":"Madrid","title":"Towards measuring the economic value of wildlife watching tourism in Africa - briefing paper","author":[{"family":"World Tourism Organization","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Despite the growth in tourism investment over the past decade, it is estimated that African protected areas are currently experiencing at least a USD$ 1.25 billion funding shortfall for effective management and conservation of threatened species like lions Panthera leo ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KlitjMAq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2016)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4318,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9ES6P65Z","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RB5A7V9A"],"itemData":{"id":4318,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Trophy hunting is widely used in Africa to generate funding for wildlife areas. In 2015, a global media frenzy resulted from the illegal killing of a radio-collared lion, “Cecil,” by a trophy hunter in Zimbabwe. Trophy hunting is contentious and much of the media discourse is emotional and polarized, focusing on animal welfare and debating the value of hunting as a conservation tool. We use the Cecil incident to urge a change in the focus of discussion and make a call for global action. We highlight the dual challenge to African governments posed by the need to fund vast wildlife estates and provide incentives for conservation by communities in the context of growing human populations and competing priorities. With or without trophy hunting, Africa's wildlife areas require much more funding to prevent serious biodiversity loss. In light of this, we urge a shift away from perpetual debates over trophy hunting to the more pressing question of “How do we fund Africa's wildlife areas adequately?” We urge the international community to greatly increase funding and technical support for Africa's wildlife estate. Concurrently, we encourage African governments and hunters to take decisive steps to reform hunting industries and address challenges associated with that revenue generating option.","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12224","ISSN":"1755-263X","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"Copyright and Photocopying: © 2015 The Authors. Conservation Letters published by Wiley Periodicals, Inc.","page":"296–301","title":"Life after Cecil: channelling global outrage into funding for conservation in Africa","title-short":"Life after Cecil","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/conl.12224","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter A."},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy A."},{"family":"Funston","given":"Paul J."},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp H."},{"family":"Hunter","given":"Luke T. B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Marine reserves cover 6.97% (25.3 million km2) of sea area protected globally ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Vm86PsyF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jantke, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Jantke, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"Uebb2sCb/hORxFYiJ","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/local/L7afBCgj/items/KHTNWGML"],"itemData":{"id":2141,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Global areal protection targets have driven a dramatic expansion of the marine protected area (MPA) estate. We analyzed how cost?effective global MPA expansion has been since the inception of the first global target (set in 1982) in achieving ecoregional representation. By comparing spatial patterns of MPA expansion against optimal MPA estates using the same expansion rates, we show the current MPA estate is both expensive and ineffective. Although the number of ecoregions represented tripled and 12.7% of national waters was protected, 61% of ecoregions and 81% of countries are not 10% protected. Only 10.3% of the national waters of the world would be sufficient to protect 10% of each ecoregion if MPA growth since 1982 strategically targeted underrepresented ecoregions. Unfortunately 16.3% of national waters are required for the same representative target if systematic protection started in 2016 (an extra 3.6% on top of 12.7%). To avoid the high costs of adjusting increasingly biased MPA systems, future efforts should embrace target?driven systematic conservation planning.","ISSN":"1755-263X","language":"en","title":"Poor ecological representation by an expensive reserve system: Evaluating 35 years of marine protected area expansion","title-short":"Poor ecological representation by an expensive reserve system","URL":"https://pubag.nal.usda.gov/catalog/6257012","author":[{"family":"Jantke","given":"Kerstin"},{"family":"Jones","given":"Kendall R."},{"family":"Allan","given":"James R."},{"family":"Chauvenet","given":"Alienor L. M."},{"family":"Watson","given":"James E. M."},{"family":"Possingham","given":"Hugh P."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} compared to 14.7% of terrestrial systems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zUgyIQue","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. R. Jones, 2018)","plainCitation":"(K. R. Jones, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33138,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PKDHVNTN"],"itemData":{"id":33138,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Protected yet pressured\\nProtected areas are increasingly recognized as an essential way to safeguard biodiversity. Although the percentage of land included in the global protected area network has increased from 9 to 15%, Jones found that a third of this area is influenced by intensive human activity. Thus, even landscapes that are protected are experiencing some human pressure, with only the most remote northern regions remaining almost untouched.\\nScience, this issue p. 788\\nIn an era of massive biodiversity loss, the greatest conservation success story has been the growth of protected land globally. Protected areas are the primary defense against biodiversity loss, but extensive human activity within their boundaries can undermine this. Using the most comprehensive global map of human pressure, we show that 6 million square kilometers (32.8%) of protected land is under intense human pressure. For protected areas designated before the Convention on Biological Diversity was ratified in 1992, 55% have since experienced human pressure increases. These increases were lowest in large, strict protected areas, showing that they are potentially effective, at least in some nations. Transparent reporting on human pressure within protected areas is now critical, as are global targets aimed at efforts required to halt biodiversity loss.\\nHuman pressure is present in a third of the land designated as protected, globally.\\nHuman pressure is present in a third of the land designated as protected, globally.","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aap9565","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6390","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2018 The Authors, some rights reserved; exclusive licensee American Association for the Advancement of Science. No claim to original U.S. Government Works. http://www.sciencemag.org/about/science-licenses-journal-article-reuseThis is an article distributed under the terms of the Science Journals Default License.","note":"publisher: American Association for the Advancement of Science\\nsection: Report\\nPMID: 29773750","page":"788-791","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"One-third of global protected land is under intense human pressure","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/360/6390/788","volume":"360","author":[{"family":"Jones","given":"Kendall R."},{"family":"Venter","given":"Oscar"},{"family":"Fuller","given":"Richard A."},{"family":"Allan","given":"James R."},{"family":"Maxwell","given":"Sean L."},{"family":"Negret","given":"Pablo Jose"},{"family":"Watson","given":"James E. M."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",5,18]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Responsible tourism, i.e., nature-based tourism, can play a role in conservation of wild species, provided that tourism flows and protected areas are carefully managed, and benefits are fairly shared ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IEEGL1Aa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Das & Chatterjee, 2015; Fennell, 2020; INTOSAI WGEA, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Das & Chatterjee, 2015; Fennell, 2020; INTOSAI WGEA, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32782,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VQDLDNH8"],"itemData":{"id":32782,"type":"report","page":"44","title":"Impact of Tourism on Wildlife Conservation","URL":"http://iced.cag.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2014/02/2013_wgea_Wild-Life_view.pdf","author":[{"family":"INTOSAI WGEA","given":""}],"collection-editor":[{"family":"Karis","given":"Alar"},{"family":"Utouh","given":"Ludovick"},{"family":"Liphafa","given":"Lucy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":33831,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H77TECEM"],"itemData":{"id":33831,"type":"book","abstract":"The new fifth edition of Ecotourism focuses on an array of economic, social and ecological inconsistencies that continue to plague ecotourism in theory and practice, and examines the sector in reference to other related forms of tourism, impacts, conservation, sustainability, education and interpretation, policy and governance, and the ethical imperative of ecotourism as these apply to the world’s greenest form of tourism. Building on the success of prior editions, the text has been revised throughout to incorporate recent research, including ecotourism taking place in under-represented world regions. It includes new case studies on important themes in research and practice as well as learning objectives in each chapter. David Fennell provides an authoritative and comprehensive review of the most important issues, including climate change and UN Sustainable Development Goals. Ecotourism continues to be embraced as the antithesis of mass tourism because of its promise of achieving sustainability through conservation mindedness, community development, education and learning, and the promotion of nature-based activities that are sensitive to both ecological and social systems. The book debates to what extent this promise has been realised. An essential reference for those interested in ecotourism, the book is accessible to students, but retains the depth required for use by researchers and practitioners in the field. This book will be of interest to students across a range of disciplines including geography, economics, business, ethics, biology, and environmental studies.","ISBN":"978-1-00-004928-2","language":"en","number-of-pages":"445","publisher":"Routledge","source":"Google Books","title":"Ecotourism","author":[{"family":"Fennell","given":"David A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",4,19]]}}},{"id":758,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7QIUF8ZN"],"itemData":{"id":758,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The paper aims to provide a description of the vision and present practices of ecotourism. Promotion of local livelihoods through ecotourism has been widely considered as an important policy instrument for biodiversity conservation. But ecotourism has become a hotly debated topic since its implementation across countries because of the mismatch in vision and practice. The paper uses content analysis method for reviewing published literature. Published peer-reviewed journal articles on ecotourism during 2000–2013 were collected and reviewed. The overall evidence on the outcomes of ecotourism in the world shows mixed results. Though there are many success stories, the list of failures is very high. Owing to the structural, operational and cultural problems, ecotourism in many places has become a predicament. Thus, ecotourism should be introduced with proper monitoring, evaluation and management of ecotourism sites for reinforcing long term conservation.","container-title":"Tourism Management Perspectives","DOI":"10.1016/j.tmp.2015.01.002","ISSN":"2211-9736","journalAbbreviation":"Tourism Management Perspectives","language":"en","page":"3-16","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Ecotourism: A panacea or a predicament?","title-short":"Ecotourism","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2211973615000185","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Das","given":"Madhumita"},{"family":"Chatterjee","given":"Bani"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",4,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. There are multiple examples where nature-based tourism as an economic development strategy has significant social and conservation benefits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cUp5PrtM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Coria & Calfucura, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Coria & Calfucura, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7191,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SC8IQDGH"],"itemData":{"id":7191,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"A large part of the literature analyzing the links between biodiversity conservation and community development assumes that nature-based tourism managed by indigenous communities will result not only in conservation of natural resources but also in increased development. In practice, ecotourism has often failed to deliver the expected bene?ts to indigenous communities due to a combination of factors, including shortages in the endowments of human, ?nancial and social capital within the community, lack of mechanisms for a fair distribution of the economic bene?ts of ecotourism, and land insecurity. Based on a review of experiences, we analyze the complex interaction among the factors shaping the success and failure of ecotourism experiences in indigenous communities, and we stress the need for a better approach to enhance the indigenous communities' livelihood possibilities coming from ecotourism, as well as to promote land tenure and communities' empowerment.","call-number":"Sub171","container-title":"Ecological Economics","DOI":"10.1016/j.ecolecon.2011.10.024","ISSN":"09218009","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Economics","language":"English","page":"47–55","title":"Ecotourism and the development of indigenous communities: The good, the bad, and the ugly","title-short":"Ecotourism and the development of indigenous communities","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolecon.2011.10.024","volume":"73","author":[{"family":"Coria","given":"Jessica"},{"family":"Calfucura","given":"Enrique"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, the Potato Park in the district of Cuzco, Peru is implementing an agro-nature-based tourism program, highlighting the diversity of native potato varieties, and other Andean grains such as quinoa and kiwicha ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ntyTMQDJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Argumedo & Stenner, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Argumedo & Stenner, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32761,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DY5P4BNF"],"itemData":{"id":32761,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"International Institute for Environment and Development","title":"Association ANDES: Conserving Indigenous Biocultural Heritage in Peru","author":[{"family":"Argumedo","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Stenner","given":"Tammy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is managed by local economic collectives, where income is generated through walking tours, a restaurant using local ingredients, and through the sale of crafts and medicinal plant products ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wE3hM3Cg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Argumedo & Stenner, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Argumedo & Stenner, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32761,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DY5P4BNF"],"itemData":{"id":32761,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"International Institute for Environment and Development","title":"Association ANDES: Conserving Indigenous Biocultural Heritage in Peru","author":[{"family":"Argumedo","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Stenner","given":"Tammy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Tourism is also seen as a crucial tool, and perhaps the only viable tool, to conserve gorillas in Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"g7Vo4Vn1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Litchfield, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Litchfield, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4336,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q3AM7T7Z"],"itemData":{"id":4336,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Developments in Primatology: Progress and Prospects","container-title":"Conservation in the 21st Century: Gorillas as a Case Study","event-place":"Boston, MA","ISBN":"978-0-387-70721-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-0-387-70721-1_4","page":"107–127","publisher":"Springer US","publisher-place":"Boston, MA","title":"Responsible Tourism: A Conservation Tool or Conservation Threat?","title-short":"Responsible Tourism","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-70721-1_4","author":[{"family":"Litchfield","given":"Carla A."}],"editor":[{"family":"Stoinski","given":"T. S."},{"family":"Steklis","given":"H. D."},{"family":"Mehlman","given":"P. T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, without mountain gorilla tourism in Uganda, it is unlikely that the small Mgahinga Gorilla National Park (34 km2) would even exist ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cg87HnKN","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Litchfield, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Litchfield, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4336,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q3AM7T7Z"],"itemData":{"id":4336,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Developments in Primatology: Progress and Prospects","container-title":"Conservation in the 21st Century: Gorillas as a Case Study","event-place":"Boston, MA","ISBN":"978-0-387-70721-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-0-387-70721-1_4","page":"107–127","publisher":"Springer US","publisher-place":"Boston, MA","title":"Responsible Tourism: A Conservation Tool or Conservation Threat?","title-short":"Responsible Tourism","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-70721-1_4","author":[{"family":"Litchfield","given":"Carla A."}],"editor":[{"family":"Stoinski","given":"T. S."},{"family":"Steklis","given":"H. D."},{"family":"Mehlman","given":"P. T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Yet, it remains unclear to what extent gorilla tourism is sustainable. Most importantly, economic and ecological sustainability ask for an optimal number of visitors to visit gorilla tourism sites, which requires appropriate governance arrangements ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AQEWABiI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Litchfield, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Litchfield, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4336,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q3AM7T7Z"],"itemData":{"id":4336,"type":"chapter","collection-title":"Developments in Primatology: Progress and Prospects","container-title":"Conservation in the 21st Century: Gorillas as a Case Study","event-place":"Boston, MA","ISBN":"978-0-387-70721-1","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-0-387-70721-1_4","page":"107–127","publisher":"Springer US","publisher-place":"Boston, MA","title":"Responsible Tourism: A Conservation Tool or Conservation Threat?","title-short":"Responsible Tourism","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-70721-1_4","author":[{"family":"Litchfield","given":"Carla A."}],"editor":[{"family":"Stoinski","given":"T. S."},{"family":"Steklis","given":"H. D."},{"family":"Mehlman","given":"P. T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Economic revenues from tourism in Africa amount to US$ 34.2 billion in 2013 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8UQ64zxX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(World Tourism Organization, 2014)","plainCitation":"(World Tourism Organization, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31342,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V2YAVGY3"],"itemData":{"id":31342,"type":"book","event-place":"Madrid","ISBN":"978-92-844-1675-2","publisher":"UNWTO","publisher-place":"Madrid","title":"Towards measuring the economic value of wildlife watching tourism in Africa - briefing paper","author":[{"family":"World Tourism Organization","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Potential revenues through tourism can also play a role in stimulating more sustainable use, by incentivizing non-extractive practices, such as tourism tours over fishing or hunting. For example, Manta rays (Manta alfredi and Manta birostris) are charismatic fish species that are vulnerable to extinction. Globally, direct revenue to dive operators related to Mata rays are US$73 million annually and direct economic impact, including associated tourism expenditures, are US$140 million annually, making tourism substantially more profitable than Manta ray fishing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"V8tFI1DO","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(O\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Malley, 2013)","plainCitation":"(O’Malley, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4309,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X53AWQAG"],"itemData":{"id":4309,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As manta rays face increased threats from targeted and bycatch fisheries, manta ray watching tourism, if managed properly, may present an attractive economic alternative to consumptive use of these species. Both species in the genus Manta (Manta alfredi and Manta birostris) are classified by the International Union for the Conservation of Nature Red List as species Vulnerable to extinction in the wild, and are considered unsustainable as fisheries resources due to their conservative life history characteristics, which considerably reduce their ability to recover population numbers when depleted. Utilising dive operator surveys, Internet research, and a literature review, this study provides the first global estimate of the direct economic impact of manta ray watching tourism and examines the potential socio-economic benefits of non-consumptive manta ray watching operations relative to consumptive use of manta rays as a fishery resource. In the 23 countries in which manta ray watching operations meeting our criteria were identified, we estimated direct revenue to dive operators from manta ray dives and snorkels at over US$73 million annually and direct economic impact, including associated tourism expenditures, of US$140 million annually. Ten countries account for almost 93% of the global revenue estimate, specifically Japan, Indonesia, the Maldives, Mozambique, Thailand, Australia, Mexico, United States, Federated States of Micronesia and Palau. In many of the areas where directed fisheries for manta rays are known to occur, these activities overlap with manta ray tourism sites or the migratory range of the mantas on which these sites depend, and are likely to be unsustainable and detrimental to manta ray watching tourism.","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0065051","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"e65051","title":"The Global Economic Impact of Manta Ray Watching Tourism","URL":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0065051","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"O’Malley","given":"Mary P."},{"family":"Lee-Brooks","given":"Katie"},{"family":"Medd","given":"Hannah B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",5]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the Ningaloo Marine Park, Western Australia, manta ray interaction tourism is suggested as a non-extractive alternative towards the hunting of reef manta ray, Manta alfredi ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"v9LEYYQA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Venables, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Venables, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6870,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ITLDGTYK"],"itemData":{"id":6870,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Localised population declines and increased pressure from fisheries have prompted the promotion of manta ray interaction tourism as a non-consumptive, yet economically attractive, alternative to the unsustainable harvesting of these animals. Unfortunately, however, wildlife tourism activities have the potential to adversely impact focal species. In order to be sustainable, operations must be managed to mitigate negative impacts. A preliminary assessment of reef manta ray, Manta alfredi, behaviour identified short-term behavioural responses during a third of tourism interactions in the Ningaloo Marine Park, Western Australia. Although it remains unknown whether these responses translate to biologically significant impacts on the population as a whole, it is proposed that the precautionary principle be used to guide management intervention in the absence of conclusive evidence of the magnitude of tourism impacts. The principle supports the implementation of precautionary strategies to protect species and their environment from harm, even when the extent of the harm is yet to be confirmed. An increase in the level of industry management is recommended, including the implementation of a licensing system and adherence of all operators to a mandatory code of conduct during manta ray interactions. Considering the well designed and precautionary-driven management program of the Ningaloo whale shark tourism industry operating within the same marine park, a management program with the same underlying principles and objectives is deemed to be an ideal framework to build a comprehensive management plan for the manta ray interaction industry.","container-title":"Pacific Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1071/PC16003","ISSN":"2204-4604","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Pac. Conserv. Biol.","language":"en","note":"publisher: CSIRO PUBLISHING","page":"295-300","source":"www.publish.csiro.au","title":"Manta ray tourism management, precautionary strategies for a growing industry: a case study from the Ningaloo Marine Park, Western Australia","title-short":"Manta ray tourism management, precautionary strategies for a growing industry","URL":"https://www.publish.csiro.au/pc/PC16003","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Venables","given":"Stephanie"},{"family":"McGregor","given":"Frazer"},{"family":"Brain","given":"Lesley"},{"family":"Keulen","given":"Mike","dropping-particle":"van"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The shark-diving industry generates US$18 million to the economy of Palau annually, benefiting several sectors of the economy, while ensuring the ecological sustainability of shark populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"29gucSn4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Vianna, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Vianna, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6861,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LB4YUYH4"],"itemData":{"id":6861,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Arguments for conservation of sharks based on their role in the maintenance of healthy marine ecosystems have failed to halt the worldwide decline in populations. Instead, the value of sharks as a fishery commodity has severely reduced the abundance of these animals. Conservation may be assisted by the development of an alternative approach that emphasizes the economic value of sharks as a non-harvested resource. Our study quantifies the value of a tourism industry based on shark diving. Using data collected from surveys, as well as government statistics, we show that shark diving is a major contributor to the economy of Palau, generating US$18 million per year and accounting for approximately 8% of the gross domestic product of the country. Annually, shark diving was responsible for the disbursement of US$1.2 million in salaries to the local community, and generated US$1.5 million in taxes to the government. If the population of approximately 100 sharks that interact with tourists at popular dive sites was harvested by fishers, their economic value would be at most US$10 800, a fraction of the worth of these animals as a non-consumptive resource. Fishers earn more selling fish for consumption by shark divers than they would gain by catching sharks. Shark diving provides an attractive economic alternative to shark fishing, with distribution of revenues benefiting several sectors of the economy, stimulating the development and generating high revenues to the government, while ensuring the ecological sustainability of shark populations.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2011.11.022","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"267-277","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Socio-economic value and community benefits from shark-diving tourism in Palau: A sustainable use of reef shark populations","title-short":"Socio-economic value and community benefits from shark-diving tourism in Palau","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S000632071100440X","volume":"145","author":[{"family":"Vianna","given":"G. M. S."},{"family":"Meekan","given":"M. G."},{"family":"Pannell","given":"D. J."},{"family":"Marsh","given":"S. P."},{"family":"Meeuwig","given":"J. J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Nature-based tourism can also help to preserve traditional practices that are ecologically more sustainable, but economically less profitable. This is in part because of the location of indigenous communities in remote locations and ecosystems characterized by ecological diversity including wild flora and fauna, and often indigenous economic practices are less consumptive and ecologically more sustainable. For example, mycological tourism can play an important role in preserving or achieving sustainable use of wild fungi, though it is important that such activities are carefully tailored to local context to ensure that local communities are benefiting and use is indeed sustainable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"k5pyySKk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jim\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}nez-Ruiz, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Jiménez-Ruiz, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6851,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3K2H2KJN","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T7KMQ2E2"],"itemData":{"id":6851,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In several countries around the world, including Mexico, there is a trend towards sustainable forest management, which includes non-timber forest resources and new non-extractive activities such as tourism. One of the resources that has arisen high interest in this regard are wild edible mushrooms, whose economic and cultural importance gives them the ability to be diversified in a wide range of...","container-title":"Bosque","ISSN":"0304-8799","issue":"3","language":"Spanish","note":"publisher: Facultad de Ciencias Forestales y Recursos Naturales, Universidad Austral de Chile","page":"447-456","source":"www.cabdirect.org","title":"Recreational use of wild edible mushrooms: mycological tourism in the world with an emphasis on Mexico.","title-short":"Recreational use of wild edible mushrooms","URL":"https://www.cabdirect.org/cabdirect/abstract/20193083850","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Jiménez-Ruiz","given":"A."},{"family":"Thomé-Ortiz","given":"H."},{"family":"Espinoza-Ortega","given":"A."},{"family":"Vizcarra Bordi","given":"I."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Yunnan is one of the hotspots of edible fungi in China, which has more than 600 species of edible fungi, and 30% of the edible fungi species in the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DkP5jUBG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Liu, 2018)","plainCitation":"(D. Liu, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6B4AVFTW"],"itemData":{"id":8765,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"42","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An ethnobotanical survey of edible fungi in Chuxiong City, Yunnan, China","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Dongyang"},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Hong"},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"Rainer W."},{"family":"Guo","given":"Zhiyong"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Bo"},{"family":"Long","given":"Chunlin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Increasing demand triggers overexploitation and threatens ecological sustainability and income of farmers. Traditionally, local people harvest wild fungi without destroying their hyphae, while today young harvesters often simply uproot the fungi, which is much less sustainable. The traditional culture and rich fungi resources are becoming popular destinations for eco-tourism, which may help preserving sustainable practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9GBbiU8m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Liu, 2018)","plainCitation":"(D. Liu, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8765,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6B4AVFTW"],"itemData":{"id":8765,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","ISSN":"1746-4269","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"J Ethnobiology Ethnomedicine","language":"en","page":"42","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An ethnobotanical survey of edible fungi in Chuxiong City, Yunnan, China","URL":"https://ethnobiomed.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13002-018-0239-2","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Liu","given":"Dongyang"},{"family":"Cheng","given":"Hong"},{"family":"Bussmann","given":"Rainer W."},{"family":"Guo","given":"Zhiyong"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Bo"},{"family":"Long","given":"Chunlin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Tourism can also play a role to maintain fishing activities using traditional gear that is less disruptive, but also gives lower yield than modern gear. One example comes from the Streat of Sicily in the Mediterranean Sea where bottom longline is a traditional fishing gear that is not used anymore, but the practice could be revived when combined with tourism activity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VEcSpH7C","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cillari, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Cillari, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":383,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5CSYQBEV"],"itemData":{"id":383,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The crisis of small-scale fisheries is causing the progressive abandonment of ancient fishing traditions and customs along a coastal area of the Strait of Sicily. For instance, worthwhile mentioning is the rapid decline in the use of bottom longline, which used to be one of the most popular gears in this area in past times. This study aims at investigating the potential of bottom longline for tourism purposes, in the perspective of recovering ancient fishing crafts. Experimental surveys were carried out to explore the effectiveness of this gear on soft bottoms, which are not usually exploited by local fishermen. An analysis of catches was carried out to assess their suitability within fishing tourism actions. The results gathered during the period of investigation demonstrate a reasonable variety of catches, with large-size specimens representing the bulk of captures. The highest values in terms of abundance and biomass were recorded for bogue Boops boops (L.) and axillary seabream Pagellus acarne (Risso). The possible enhancement of these two low commercial value species is discussed. Results also highlight the suitability of the use of bottom longline during tourism low seasons. The use of longline would produce several positive impacts in the framework of fishing tourism in the study area, such as for instance the creation of alternative sources of income for artisanal fishers, the enhancement of underexploited species in the perspective of environmental sustainability, the recovery and re-launch of a disappearing fishing gear, thus contributing to safeguard cultural diversity.","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2011.10.007","ISSN":"0964-5691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"20-26","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"The use of bottom longline on soft bottoms: An opportunity of development for fishing tourism along a coastal area of the Strait of Sicily (Mediterranean Sea)","title-short":"The use of bottom longline on soft bottoms","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0964569111001608","volume":"55","author":[{"family":"Cillari","given":"Tiziana"},{"family":"Falautano","given":"Manuela"},{"family":"Castriota","given":"Luca"},{"family":"Marino","given":"Vanessa"},{"family":"Vivona","given":"Pietro"},{"family":"Andaloro","given":"Franco"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As a cautionary note, whether or not traditional indigenous practices are (more) sustainable, depends on the specific context. Traditional fishing gear is often unselective, which may lead to a more balanced catch profile, which gives ecological and economic benefits, as well as costs ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JaXdTVfo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. G. Burgess, 2016; Kolding & van Zwieten, 2014)","plainCitation":"(M. G. Burgess, 2016; Kolding & van Zwieten, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4339,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CY45NY6K","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JM8RIBAM"],"itemData":{"id":4339,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Balanced harvesting – harvesting all species and sizes in an ecosystem in proportion to their productivity – is a fisheries management strategy that has been suggested recently to increase yields, while reducing overall ecosystem impact. However, some aspects of balanced harvesting are controversial, including its call for extensive harvesting of juveniles and forage fish. Balanced harvesting also calls for targeting species and size-classes that are not currently marketable, possibly at a significant economic cost. Some have argued that this cost is outweighed by the ecological benefits of maintaining the ecosystem size and trophic structures and by the benefits of extra yield for food security. There is broad consensus that balanced harvesting would require major changes to fishery management institutions and consumer behaviour, and it is unclear to what extent it is physically possible with current technologies. For this reason, we argue that steps to implement balanced harvesting are difficult to justify until the case for it is more clearly resolved. We outline some of the pivotal questions that must be answered to make a convincing case for or against balanced harvesting, many of which can be answered empirically. In identifying these questions, we hope to offer a constructive path forward in resolving some of the key issues in the balanced harvesting debate.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12123","ISSN":"1467-2979","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"© 2015 John Wiley & Sons Ltd","page":"1216–1226","title":"Remaining questions in the case for balanced harvesting","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/faf.12123","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Burgess","given":"Matthew G."},{"family":"Diekert","given":"Florian K."},{"family":"Jacobsen","given":"Nis S."},{"family":"Andersen","given":"Ken H."},{"family":"Gaines","given":"Steven D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":4338,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GYV4SJ56"],"itemData":{"id":4338,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Sustainability in fisheries has over the past decades evolved from a single species maximization concept to covering ecosystem and biodiversity considerations. This expansion of the notion, together with increased evidence that the targeted removal of selected components of the fish community may have adverse ecological consequences, poses a serious dilemma to the conventional fisheries management approach of protecting juveniles and targeting adults. Recently, the idea of balanced harvest, i.e., harvesting all components in the ecosystem in proportion to their productivity, has been promoted as a unifying solution in accordance with the ecosystem approach to fisheries, but this will require a fundamental change to management. In this paper, we review the objectives, theoretical background, and practicalities of securing high yielding fisheries in inland waters, with empirical examples from tropical freshwater fisheries which satisfy the extended objectives of minimal impact on community and ecosystem structure. We propose a framework of ecological indicators to assess these objectives.","container-title":"1","DOI":"10.4081/jlimnol.2014.818","ISSN":"1129-5767","language":"eng","license":"Attribution CC BY-NC","title":"Sustainable fishing of inland waters","URL":"https://bora.uib.no/handle/1956/10492","author":[{"family":"Kolding","given":"Jeppe"},{"family":"Zwieten","given":"Paul A. M.","non-dropping-particle":"van"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, on the Galapagos islands a traditional unselective longline fishery has caused substantial undesired bycatch of protected megafauna species, such as turtles ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"idSvKTGg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cerutti-Pereyra, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Cerutti-Pereyra, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6865,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BMVKW3LW"],"itemData":{"id":6865,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Marine megafauna (elasmobranchs, marine mammals, turtles, and seabirds) are important ecologically and economically because many species often occupy upper trophic levels as adults and are essential for marine-based tourism in many areas of the world. This group of species is also heavily impacted by fishing because most have late sexual maturity, longevity, and low reproductive output, which affects their ability to recover from depletion. In Galapagos, marine megafauna species are protected from fishing throughout the marine reserve and are the main attraction for marine-based tourism, helping generate millions of dollars in revenue annually. Despite their importance in the archipelago, these species are being caught as bycatch in the multiple artisanal longlining projects that have been carried out since the implementation of the reserve in 1998. Longlining was originally proposed as a way of redirecting fishing effort from the severely depleted coastal-demersal species to pelagic fish such as yellowfin tuna and swordfish. Although all these projects have resulted in high bycatch of megafauna, longline fishing projects have continued without independent scientific studies to evaluate their impact, largely due to poor objective definition, data collection, and enforcement. To fill in this knowledge gap, we analyzed data of the fifth experimental longline fishing project undertaken in 2012–2013 to describe the fishery, identify variables affecting the composition and quantity of bycatch, and suggest mitigation strategies. This experimental project had twelve vessels, which deployed 42,007 hooks catching 4893 individuals of 33 species, mostly yellowfin tuna and swordfish. Of those, 16 species were protected megafauna, particularly blacktip sharks (Carcharhinus limbatus) and oceanic manta (Mobula birostris). These species were regularly captured during the two seasons and in the three bioregions that occur in the archipelago, suggesting little potential to mitigate their catch. As an alternative, we identified 14 hotspots where yellowfin tuna and swordfish could be harvested in large numbers sustainably through more selective fishing techniques such as pole fishing, a method that is also more economical for artisanal fishers. In an archipelago where the main economic activity is marine wildlife tourism, the implementation of an extractive and unselective activity such as pelagic longing fishing should be avoided to ensure the sustainability of the Galapagos marine ecosystem and its booming tourism industry.","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2019.104995","ISSN":"0964-5691","journalAbbreviation":"Ocean & Coastal Management","language":"en","page":"104995","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Artisanal longline fishing the Galapagos Islands –effects on vulnerable megafauna in a UNESCO World Heritage site","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0964569119301243","volume":"183","author":[{"family":"Cerutti-Pereyra","given":"Florencia"},{"family":"Moity","given":"Nicolás"},{"family":"Dureuil","given":"Manuel"},{"family":"Ramírez-González","given":"Jorge"},{"family":"Reyes","given":"Harry"},{"family":"Budd","given":"Kayla"},{"family":"Marín Jarrín","given":"José"},{"family":"Salinas-de-León","given":"Pelayo"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Tourism can also act as a stressor, though the negative impacts of non-extractive wild species tourism on population health of wild species is poorly understood, as many studies are site-specific and lack long time series ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JQBLt1jT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Burgin & Hardiman, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Burgin & Hardiman, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30148,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KQWL6XVN"],"itemData":{"id":30148,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.jenvman.2014.12.018","ISSN":"03014797","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"210-220","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Effects of non-consumptive wildlife-oriented tourism on marine species and prospects for their sustainable management","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0301479714005970","volume":"151","author":[{"family":"Burgin","given":"Shelley"},{"family":"Hardiman","given":"Nigel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",8,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",3]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Unregulated tourism centered around wild species attractions tends to have adverse effects on wild species populations. Negative impacts stem from touristic activities, the intensification of tourism infrastructure, destruction of habitat  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SnXw6Gap","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mbaiwa, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Mbaiwa, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6502,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TVA2D4FG"],"itemData":{"id":6502,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Socio economic, tourism, community based, impacts, environmental, ecotourism, sustainability, sustainable development, Botswana, Africa","container-title":"Journal of Arid Environments","DOI":"10.1006/jare.2002.1101","ISSN":"01401963","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Arid Environments","language":"en","page":"447-467","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The socio-economic and environmental impacts of tourism development on the Okavango Delta, north-western Botswana","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0140196302911018","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Mbaiwa","given":"Joseph E"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003",6]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} that can even result in reduced recruitment and juvenile survival in species like the cheetah Acinonyx jubatus ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rEtz75Ls","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Broekhuis, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Broekhuis, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31670,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9DF9SNUC"],"itemData":{"id":31670,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Evolution","DOI":"10.1002/ece3.4180","ISSN":"20457758","issue":"13","journalAbbreviation":"Ecol Evol","language":"en","page":"6748-6755","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Natural and anthropogenic drivers of cub recruitment in a large carnivore","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1002/ece3.4180","volume":"8","author":[{"family":"Broekhuis","given":"Femke"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, marine provisioning-tourism in the Cayman Islands has had an impact on the physiology of southern stingrays (Dasyatis americana), through non-natural food, higher injury rates from boats, and higher parasite loads from crowding conditions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8XMjaRU5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Semeniuk, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Semeniuk, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8637,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FY6LA6AJ"],"itemData":{"id":8637,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Wildlife tourism alters the environmental conditions in which the focal animal lives, and it is therefore necessary to assess the ability of the animal to adjust to and persist in these novel conditions if the industry is to be sustainable. Here, we report on the physiological responses of southern stingrays (Dasyatis americana) which are the focus of intense marine provisioning-tourism in the Cayman Islands. Using stingrays from non-tourist sites about Grand Cayman as a basis for comparison, we show in this natural experiment that tourist-exposed stingrays exhibit hematological changes indicative of physiological costs of wildlife tourism. The novel conditions with which the stingrays must interact include non-natural food, higher injury rates (from boats, conspecifics and predators), and higher parasite loads (from crowding conditions). As a result of this year-round environment, stingrays display sub-optimal health: lower hematocrit, total serum protein concentrations, and oxidative stress (i.e., lower total antioxidant capacity combined with higher total oxidative status). Moreover, they show evidence of attenuation of the defense system: for tourist stingrays only, animals possessing both injuries and high parasite loads also exhibit lowest leukocrit, serum proteins and antioxidant potential, as well as differing proportions of differential leukocytes indicative of suppression (lymphocytes and heterophils) and down-regulation (eosinophils), thus suggesting that the physiological changes of tourist stingrays are in partial response to these stressors. While survival- and reproduction- quantification was not possible in this long-lived marine species, the physiological measures -situated within ecological context, indicate that the long-term health and survival of tourist stingrays have a significant probability of being affected. Consequently, management of the tourism attraction is essential. The indicators chosen in this study reflect general health indices and defense capabilities used across taxa, and represent a tradeoff between ease of collection/analysis and interpretation so that managers can continue the research for monitoring purposes.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2009.03.022","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"8","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"1818-1829","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Hematological differences between stingrays at tourist and non-visited sites suggest physiological costs of wildlife tourism","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320709001578","volume":"142","author":[{"family":"Semeniuk","given":"Christina A. D."},{"family":"Bourgeon","given":"Sophie"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Sylvia L."},{"family":"Rothley","given":"Kristina D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",8,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Increased boat traffic has also had an adverse impact on behavior and stress levels in the Scandola marine protected area (UNESCO World Heritage Site, Corsica island), on the population of the Osprey Pandion haliaetus ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eRdeqA8N","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Monti, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Monti, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6862,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4U2Y699B"],"itemData":{"id":6862,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Disturbance of wildlife by ecotourism has become a major concern in the last decades. In the Mediterranean, sea-based tourism and related recreational activities are increasing rapidly, especially within marine protected areas (MPAs) hosting emblematic biodiversity. We investigated the impact of ecotourism in the Scandola MPA (UNESCO World Heritage Site, Corsica island), on the population of a conservation flagship, the Osprey Pandion haliaetus. Over the 37-year study period, tourists flow increased sharply. Osprey breeding performance initially increased, but then dropped for pairs nesting within the MPA compared to those breeding elsewhere in Corsica. We examined several hypotheses that could explain such reduction in breeding performance. Recent osprey breeding failures in the MPA are not caused by food scarcity. Using underwater fish surveys, we showed that fish consumed by ospreys were more numerous within the MPA. Focal observation at nests revealed that the overall number of boat passages within 250 m of osprey nests were three times higher inside the MPA compared to a control area. Elevated boat traffic significantly modified osprey time-budgets, by decreasing prey provisioning rate by males, and increasing time spent alarming and flying off the nest in females. This caused stress, and corticosterone levels in chick feathers were three times higher in high-traffic areas compared to places with lower touristic flow in Corsica, the Balearic Islands and Italy. Overall, our integrative, long-term study demonstrates the negative impact of sea-based ecotourism on the Corsican osprey population. This stresses the worldwide importance of rigorously implementing sustainable ecotourism, within well-enforced MPAs.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/acv.12407","ISSN":"1469-1795","issue":"6","language":"en","license":"© 2018 The Zoological Society of London","note":"_eprint: https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/acv.12407","page":"448-458","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The price of success: integrative long-term study reveals ecotourism impacts on a flagship species at a UNESCO site","title-short":"The price of success","URL":"https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/acv.12407","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Monti","given":"F."},{"family":"Duriez","given":"O."},{"family":"Dominici","given":"J.-M."},{"family":"Sforzi","given":"A."},{"family":"Robert","given":"A."},{"family":"Fusani","given":"L."},{"family":"Grémillet","given":"D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Birds use less time to search for prey, and are more stressed, indicated by corticosterone levels in chick feathers being three times higher in high?traffic areas compared to places with lower touristic flow ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vmHlQagy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Monti, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Monti, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6862,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4U2Y699B"],"itemData":{"id":6862,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Disturbance of wildlife by ecotourism has become a major concern in the last decades. In the Mediterranean, sea-based tourism and related recreational activities are increasing rapidly, especially within marine protected areas (MPAs) hosting emblematic biodiversity. We investigated the impact of ecotourism in the Scandola MPA (UNESCO World Heritage Site, Corsica island), on the population of a conservation flagship, the Osprey Pandion haliaetus. Over the 37-year study period, tourists flow increased sharply. Osprey breeding performance initially increased, but then dropped for pairs nesting within the MPA compared to those breeding elsewhere in Corsica. We examined several hypotheses that could explain such reduction in breeding performance. Recent osprey breeding failures in the MPA are not caused by food scarcity. Using underwater fish surveys, we showed that fish consumed by ospreys were more numerous within the MPA. Focal observation at nests revealed that the overall number of boat passages within 250 m of osprey nests were three times higher inside the MPA compared to a control area. Elevated boat traffic significantly modified osprey time-budgets, by decreasing prey provisioning rate by males, and increasing time spent alarming and flying off the nest in females. This caused stress, and corticosterone levels in chick feathers were three times higher in high-traffic areas compared to places with lower touristic flow in Corsica, the Balearic Islands and Italy. Overall, our integrative, long-term study demonstrates the negative impact of sea-based ecotourism on the Corsican osprey population. This stresses the worldwide importance of rigorously implementing sustainable ecotourism, within well-enforced MPAs.","container-title":"Animal Conservation","DOI":"10.1111/acv.12407","ISSN":"1469-1795","issue":"6","language":"en","license":"© 2018 The Zoological Society of London","note":"_eprint: https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/acv.12407","page":"448-458","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"The price of success: integrative long-term study reveals ecotourism impacts on a flagship species at a UNESCO site","title-short":"The price of success","URL":"https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/acv.12407","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Monti","given":"F."},{"family":"Duriez","given":"O."},{"family":"Dominici","given":"J.-M."},{"family":"Sforzi","given":"A."},{"family":"Robert","given":"A."},{"family":"Fusani","given":"L."},{"family":"Grémillet","given":"D."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. On Panaon Island in the Visayas region of the Philippines whale shark (Rhincodon typus) tourism is a growing industry, where the impacts on the shark population remains unclear ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vPnS2D96","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Araujo, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Araujo, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2609,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U3Q2KNLL"],"itemData":{"id":2609,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2762","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"986–994","title":"Assessing the impacts of tourism on the world’s largest fish Rhincodon typus at Panaon Island, Southern Leyte, Philippines","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1002/aqc.2762","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Araujo","given":"G."},{"family":"Vivier","given":"F."},{"family":"Labaja","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Hartley","given":"D."},{"family":"Ponzo","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 56% had anthropogenic scars from boat propellers, fishing gear or vessel collision, though it is not clear to what extent these are caused by tourist activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0dzokzeg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Araujo, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Araujo, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":2609,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U3Q2KNLL"],"itemData":{"id":2609,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Aquatic Conservation: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems","DOI":"10.1002/aqc.2762","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"986–994","title":"Assessing the impacts of tourism on the world’s largest fish Rhincodon typus at Panaon Island, Southern Leyte, Philippines","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1002/aqc.2762","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Araujo","given":"G."},{"family":"Vivier","given":"F."},{"family":"Labaja","given":"J.J."},{"family":"Hartley","given":"D."},{"family":"Ponzo","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This shows that motorboats can be a threat to marine species, and rules around speed limits, and also distance regulations can mitigate some of those threats. 
	Tourism can degrade coral reefs through coastal development, as well as through unsustainable tourist activities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0xaXztDL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gil, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Gil, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6872,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HLUAI2JD"],"itemData":{"id":6872,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ecosystem-based management efforts in coral reefs typically focus on reducing fishing pressure. However, independent of overfishing, tourism can degrade coral reefs through coastal development, as well as the physical presence of tourists within the ecosystem, the effects of which remain poorly understood. We combined a 3-year dataset on coral and algal cover with a more extensive survey of the benthic community to examine the effect of intensive tourism on a coral reef in Akumal Bay, Mexico. Results from our 3-year dataset indicated that near the peak snorkeling area in the bay, coral cover decreased by 79 % from summer 2011 through summer 2014, a period in which the number of monthly snorkelers increased by more than 400 %. Our summer 2013 survey of the benthic community between sites within a zone of dense snorkeler traffic versus site at a nearby control location revealed negative effects of intensive tourism on particular coral morphologies and on the abundance of herbivorous reef fishes. Our results suggest that uncontrolled tourism, including accelerating growth in the number of snorkelers, is likely contributing to the decline of the coral reef in Akumal Bay, where further expansions in tourism are planned. Indeed, the ecosystems threatened by overexploitation via tourism in the Mayan Riviera also form the basis for the regional tourism industry. Thus, long-term ecological monitoring coupled with the establishment and enforcement of regulations on tourism may be essential for the sustainability of coral reefs, as well as the socioeconomic benefits they provide in Mexico.","container-title":"Marine Biology","DOI":"10.1007/s00227-015-2748-z","ISSN":"1432-1793","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Mar Biol","language":"en","page":"2225-2233","source":"Springer Link","title":"Rapid tourism growth and declining coral reefs in Akumal, Mexico","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00227-015-2748-z","volume":"162","author":[{"family":"Gil","given":"Michael A."},{"family":"Renfro","given":"Bobbie"},{"family":"Figueroa-Zavala","given":"Baruch"},{"family":"Penié","given":"Iván"},{"family":"Dunton","given":"Kenneth H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the touristic region of the Mayan Riviera, Mexico coral cover decreased by 79 % from 2011 to 2014 near the peak snorkeling area in the bay ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XHyRTaCA","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gil, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Gil, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6872,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HLUAI2JD"],"itemData":{"id":6872,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ecosystem-based management efforts in coral reefs typically focus on reducing fishing pressure. However, independent of overfishing, tourism can degrade coral reefs through coastal development, as well as the physical presence of tourists within the ecosystem, the effects of which remain poorly understood. We combined a 3-year dataset on coral and algal cover with a more extensive survey of the benthic community to examine the effect of intensive tourism on a coral reef in Akumal Bay, Mexico. Results from our 3-year dataset indicated that near the peak snorkeling area in the bay, coral cover decreased by 79 % from summer 2011 through summer 2014, a period in which the number of monthly snorkelers increased by more than 400 %. Our summer 2013 survey of the benthic community between sites within a zone of dense snorkeler traffic versus site at a nearby control location revealed negative effects of intensive tourism on particular coral morphologies and on the abundance of herbivorous reef fishes. Our results suggest that uncontrolled tourism, including accelerating growth in the number of snorkelers, is likely contributing to the decline of the coral reef in Akumal Bay, where further expansions in tourism are planned. Indeed, the ecosystems threatened by overexploitation via tourism in the Mayan Riviera also form the basis for the regional tourism industry. Thus, long-term ecological monitoring coupled with the establishment and enforcement of regulations on tourism may be essential for the sustainability of coral reefs, as well as the socioeconomic benefits they provide in Mexico.","container-title":"Marine Biology","DOI":"10.1007/s00227-015-2748-z","ISSN":"1432-1793","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Mar Biol","language":"en","page":"2225-2233","source":"Springer Link","title":"Rapid tourism growth and declining coral reefs in Akumal, Mexico","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00227-015-2748-z","volume":"162","author":[{"family":"Gil","given":"Michael A."},{"family":"Renfro","given":"Bobbie"},{"family":"Figueroa-Zavala","given":"Baruch"},{"family":"Penié","given":"Iván"},{"family":"Dunton","given":"Kenneth H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In a control region that was less exposed to tourists, a similar decline was not observed, suggesting that uncontrolled tourist activities were the main cause of the decline ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Efs1uqhb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gil, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Gil, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6872,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HLUAI2JD"],"itemData":{"id":6872,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ecosystem-based management efforts in coral reefs typically focus on reducing fishing pressure. However, independent of overfishing, tourism can degrade coral reefs through coastal development, as well as the physical presence of tourists within the ecosystem, the effects of which remain poorly understood. We combined a 3-year dataset on coral and algal cover with a more extensive survey of the benthic community to examine the effect of intensive tourism on a coral reef in Akumal Bay, Mexico. Results from our 3-year dataset indicated that near the peak snorkeling area in the bay, coral cover decreased by 79 % from summer 2011 through summer 2014, a period in which the number of monthly snorkelers increased by more than 400 %. Our summer 2013 survey of the benthic community between sites within a zone of dense snorkeler traffic versus site at a nearby control location revealed negative effects of intensive tourism on particular coral morphologies and on the abundance of herbivorous reef fishes. Our results suggest that uncontrolled tourism, including accelerating growth in the number of snorkelers, is likely contributing to the decline of the coral reef in Akumal Bay, where further expansions in tourism are planned. Indeed, the ecosystems threatened by overexploitation via tourism in the Mayan Riviera also form the basis for the regional tourism industry. Thus, long-term ecological monitoring coupled with the establishment and enforcement of regulations on tourism may be essential for the sustainability of coral reefs, as well as the socioeconomic benefits they provide in Mexico.","container-title":"Marine Biology","DOI":"10.1007/s00227-015-2748-z","ISSN":"1432-1793","issue":"11","journalAbbreviation":"Mar Biol","language":"en","page":"2225-2233","source":"Springer Link","title":"Rapid tourism growth and declining coral reefs in Akumal, Mexico","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00227-015-2748-z","volume":"162","author":[{"family":"Gil","given":"Michael A."},{"family":"Renfro","given":"Bobbie"},{"family":"Figueroa-Zavala","given":"Baruch"},{"family":"Penié","given":"Iván"},{"family":"Dunton","given":"Kenneth H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Improvements can be made by targeting tourists directly and educating them about how to sustainably dive or snorkel. Showing tourists a video message about sustainable use prior to a trip has led to a five-fold reduction in the rate of contact compared to a control group in an experiment in Puerto Rico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XPYOMwSX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Webler & Jakubowski, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Webler & Jakubowski, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6860,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RYZCH6IK"],"itemData":{"id":6860,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"There is widespread consensus that recreational snorkelers are damaging coral reefs, but the magnitude of the issue is unknown. Loss of reefs jeopardizes tourism, which is a significant economic driver. Recreational snorkeling is a popular activity and yet there has been little research about the behavior of snorkelers at reefs. The authors carried out observations at several reef locations in Puerto Rico to determine the baseline level of snorkeler behavior that threatens coral reefs. From August of 2010 until June 2012, they observed 328 different recreational snorkelers in-water at various reef locations and recorded number and types of potentially damaging behaviors. Snorkelers exhibited 0.26 potentially damaging behaviors per minute. Most were fin kicks (39%) and the next most frequent behavior was sitting, standing or kneeling on the reef (22%). The authors asked a subset of the people who were observed to make self-reports of their behavior, evaluated the accuracy of self-reports, and found that people underreported their potentially harmful behaviors. The authors experimented with a video message and signed pledge to promote proper snorkeling etiquette. From March 2012 until June 2012, snorkelers watched the video and signed the pledge before they boarded a tour operator led excursion. The pledge expressed commitment to specific pro-reef behaviors. Post-treatment in-water observations of 79 different snorkelers found a five-fold reduction in the rate of potentially damaging behaviors. Furthermore, the percentage of snorkelers who never harmed the reef shot up from 65% to 89%. The research suggests the pre-trip messaging together with a written pledge can change behaviors, thus improving the ability of ecotourism operators to help sustain reefs as well as the economic livelihoods of their employees.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2016.03.012","ISSN":"0006-3207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"223-228","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Mitigating damaging behaviors of snorkelers to coral reefs in Puerto Rico through a pre-trip media-based intervention","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320716300891","volume":"197","author":[{"family":"Webler","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Jakubowski","given":"Karin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",5,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
Tourism can also encourage unsustainable uses of wild species, snake charming being one example. While snake charming in Marocco is practiced for at least 500 years, it now mostly used to attract tourists ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3n7aflFM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pleguezuelos, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Pleguezuelos, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6850,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WVSNFAT9"],"itemData":{"id":6850,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Traditional activities that potentially threaten biodiversity represent a challenge to conservationists as they try to reconcile the cultural dimensions of such activities. Quantifying the impact of traditional activities on biodiversity is always helpful for decision making in conservation. In the case of snake charming in Morocco, the practice was introduced there 500 years ago by the religious order the Aissawas, and is now an attraction in the country's growing tourism industry. As a consequence wild snake populations may be threatened by overexploitation. The focal species for snake charming, the Egyptian cobra Naja haje, is undergoing both range and population declines. We estimated the level of exploitation of snakes based on field surveys and questionnaires administered to Aissawas during 2003–2014, and compared our results with those of a study conducted 25 years previously. Aissawas use four venomous and four non-venomous species for snake charming and we estimate they harvest a minimum of 4,500 individuals annually, mostly venomous snakes. For exhibition purposes they selectively remove the largest specimens from the wild (i.e. those that could have the highest reproductive output). Compared to the previous data, we detected (1) a reduction in the number of species collected, (2) an increased distance to collecting fields, and (3) an increase in the market price for snakes, after correction for accumulated inflation, signifying a higher demand for these animals.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605316000910","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"2","language":"en","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"374-381","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Snake charming and the exploitation of snakes in Morocco","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/snake-charming-and-the-exploitation-of-snakes-in-morocco/7B77F8BFD9122A1F61FD9A9830FD946C/core-reader","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Pleguezuelos","given":"Juan M."},{"family":"Feriche","given":"Mónica"},{"family":"Brito","given":"José C."},{"family":"Fahd","given":"Soumía"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Mortality during transport and captivity is high and hunters select predominantly snakes that have certain traits that appeal to tourists (e.g. large body size), undermining population sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jf14O80l","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pleguezuelos, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Pleguezuelos, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6850,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WVSNFAT9"],"itemData":{"id":6850,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Traditional activities that potentially threaten biodiversity represent a challenge to conservationists as they try to reconcile the cultural dimensions of such activities. Quantifying the impact of traditional activities on biodiversity is always helpful for decision making in conservation. In the case of snake charming in Morocco, the practice was introduced there 500 years ago by the religious order the Aissawas, and is now an attraction in the country's growing tourism industry. As a consequence wild snake populations may be threatened by overexploitation. The focal species for snake charming, the Egyptian cobra Naja haje, is undergoing both range and population declines. We estimated the level of exploitation of snakes based on field surveys and questionnaires administered to Aissawas during 2003–2014, and compared our results with those of a study conducted 25 years previously. Aissawas use four venomous and four non-venomous species for snake charming and we estimate they harvest a minimum of 4,500 individuals annually, mostly venomous snakes. For exhibition purposes they selectively remove the largest specimens from the wild (i.e. those that could have the highest reproductive output). Compared to the previous data, we detected (1) a reduction in the number of species collected, (2) an increased distance to collecting fields, and (3) an increase in the market price for snakes, after correction for accumulated inflation, signifying a higher demand for these animals.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605316000910","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"2","language":"en","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"374-381","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Snake charming and the exploitation of snakes in Morocco","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/snake-charming-and-the-exploitation-of-snakes-in-morocco/7B77F8BFD9122A1F61FD9A9830FD946C/core-reader","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Pleguezuelos","given":"Juan M."},{"family":"Feriche","given":"Mónica"},{"family":"Brito","given":"José C."},{"family":"Fahd","given":"Soumía"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Consequently, a population decline of Egyptina Cobra N. haje has been observed, though it not established to what extent this is due to hunting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"F1CR7ekq","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pleguezuelos, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Pleguezuelos, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6850,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WVSNFAT9"],"itemData":{"id":6850,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Traditional activities that potentially threaten biodiversity represent a challenge to conservationists as they try to reconcile the cultural dimensions of such activities. Quantifying the impact of traditional activities on biodiversity is always helpful for decision making in conservation. In the case of snake charming in Morocco, the practice was introduced there 500 years ago by the religious order the Aissawas, and is now an attraction in the country's growing tourism industry. As a consequence wild snake populations may be threatened by overexploitation. The focal species for snake charming, the Egyptian cobra Naja haje, is undergoing both range and population declines. We estimated the level of exploitation of snakes based on field surveys and questionnaires administered to Aissawas during 2003–2014, and compared our results with those of a study conducted 25 years previously. Aissawas use four venomous and four non-venomous species for snake charming and we estimate they harvest a minimum of 4,500 individuals annually, mostly venomous snakes. For exhibition purposes they selectively remove the largest specimens from the wild (i.e. those that could have the highest reproductive output). Compared to the previous data, we detected (1) a reduction in the number of species collected, (2) an increased distance to collecting fields, and (3) an increase in the market price for snakes, after correction for accumulated inflation, signifying a higher demand for these animals.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605316000910","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"2","language":"en","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"374-381","source":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Snake charming and the exploitation of snakes in Morocco","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/snake-charming-and-the-exploitation-of-snakes-in-morocco/7B77F8BFD9122A1F61FD9A9830FD946C/core-reader","volume":"52","author":[{"family":"Pleguezuelos","given":"Juan M."},{"family":"Feriche","given":"Mónica"},{"family":"Brito","given":"José C."},{"family":"Fahd","given":"Soumía"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, gathering shells and selling those to tourists has adverse effects on marine ecosystems, as shown in Zanzibar, Tanzania ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KBxtSw5j","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G\\\\uc0\\\\u246{}ssling, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Gössling, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6849,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LTFISQL5"],"itemData":{"id":6849,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The collection of shells is a popular tourist activity in coastal areas of tropical countries. Tourists also buy shells, particularly large gastropods, which may be important species in marine ecosystems. While some, though outdated, information exists on the magnitude of the international trade with ornamental shells, virtually nothing is known about the extent of the regional tourist-related curio trade. A survey in Zanzibar, Tanzania, showed that 39% of the tourists had collected shells and 7% had bought shells, contributing US$ 136,000 to the local economy. The weight of shells exported from Zanzibar by tourists may have been in the order of 13?t. Regarding other marine species, tourists consume only 1% of the fish catches, but they have substantially increased the consumption of crustaceans and the trade in shark teeth and jaws.","container-title":"Biodiversity & Conservation","DOI":"10.1007/s10531-004-2139-0","ISSN":"1572-9710","issue":"14","journalAbbreviation":"Biodiversity and Conservation","language":"en","page":"2623-2639","source":"Springer Link","title":"Use of molluscs, fish, and other marine taxa by tourism in Zanzibar, Tanzania","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10531-004-2139-0","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Gössling","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Kunkel","given":"Timo"},{"family":"Schumacher","given":"Kim"},{"family":"Zilger","given":"Maiken"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	In some cases, nature-based tourism has resulted in unintended consequences for biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"D6q81v6w","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hinch, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Hinch, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6844,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VGV5MAFN"],"itemData":{"id":6844,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Despite the initial impression that ecotourists are an ideal market for indigenous tourism developers, a closer examination suggests that these groups do not necessarily share similar views of the relationship between humans and nature. Conflict is likely to arise between these groups unless a greater understanding of these differences is achieved and successfully used in the planning and management of indigenous tourism developments.","container-title":"Current Issues in Tourism","DOI":"10.1080/13683509808667834","ISSN":"1368-3500","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Routledge\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/13683509808667834","page":"120-124","source":"Taylor and Francis+NEJM","title":"Ecotourists and Indigenous Hosts: Diverging Views on Their Relationship With Nature","title-short":"Ecotourists and Indigenous Hosts","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/13683509808667834","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Hinch","given":"Thomas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, tourists may increase demand for locally caught seafood. A case study from Fernando de Noronha, located about 345 km offshore the Brazilian coast illustrates that tourists consume 70% of the caught fish, posing further sustainability challenges ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AHMR1ZPp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lopes, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Lopes, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6856,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4XSQST4T"],"itemData":{"id":6856,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Although critical tools for protecting ocean habitats, Marine Protected Areas (MPAs) are sometimes challenged for social impacts and conflicts they may generate. Some conflicts have an economic base, which, once understood, can be used to resolve associated socioenvironmental problems. We addressed how the fish trade in an MPA that combines no-take zones and tourist or resident zones creates incentives for increased fisheries. We performed a value chain analysis following the fish supply and trade through interviews that assessed consumer demand and preference. The results showed a simple and closed value chain driven by tourism (70% of the consumption). Both tourists and local consumers preferred high trophic level species (predators), but the former preferred large pelagics (tuna and dolphinfish) and the latter preferred reef species (barracuda and snapper). Pelagic predators are caught with fresh sardines, which are sometimes located only in the no-take zone. Pelagic species are mainly served as fillet, and the leftover fish parts end up as waste, an issue that, if properly addressed, can help reduce fishing pressure. Whereas some of the target species may be sustainable (e.g., dolphinfish), others are more vulnerable (e.g., wahoo) and should not be intensively fished. We advise setting stricter limits to the number of tourists visiting MPAs, according to their own capacity and peculiarities, in order to avoid conflicts with conservations goals through incentives for increased resource use.","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.jenvman.2017.05.080","ISSN":"0301-4797","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"123-134","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Tourism as a driver of conflicts and changes in fisheries value chains in Marine Protected Areas","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301479717305650","volume":"200","author":[{"family":"Lopes","given":"P. F. M."},{"family":"Mendes","given":"L."},{"family":"Fonseca","given":"V."},{"family":"Villasante","given":"S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",9,15]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, ecotourists can have very different views and relationships to nature than indigenous communities. Therefore, careful consideration of the alignment between the conservation values of the nature-based tourism enterprise and the social and cultural values of communities is critical for sustainability outcomes ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7TUW921S","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hinch, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Hinch, 1998)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6844,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VGV5MAFN"],"itemData":{"id":6844,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Despite the initial impression that ecotourists are an ideal market for indigenous tourism developers, a closer examination suggests that these groups do not necessarily share similar views of the relationship between humans and nature. Conflict is likely to arise between these groups unless a greater understanding of these differences is achieved and successfully used in the planning and management of indigenous tourism developments.","container-title":"Current Issues in Tourism","DOI":"10.1080/13683509808667834","ISSN":"1368-3500","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Routledge\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/13683509808667834","page":"120-124","source":"Taylor and Francis+NEJM","title":"Ecotourists and Indigenous Hosts: Diverging Views on Their Relationship With Nature","title-short":"Ecotourists and Indigenous Hosts","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/13683509808667834","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Hinch","given":"Thomas"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998",1,1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	To mitigate negative effects of tourism on wild species, it is important that management schemes are in place that make sure that tourism is taking place sustainably. This implies steering the number of tourists, which is often managed through licenses, as well as the practices, which often implies enforcing rules of conduct, and giving out licenses.  For example, in the Ningaloo Marine Park, Western Australia, a management plan is implemented to make sure that whale watching takes place sustainably ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dTbKaccv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. D. Andersson, 2014)","plainCitation":"(T. D. Andersson, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31683,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VFN8U8SB"],"itemData":{"id":31683,"type":"chapter","call-number":"QL737.C4 W435 2014","container-title":"Whale-watching: sustainable tourism and ecological management","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-0-521-19597-3","page":"95-109","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Iceland and the resumption of whaling: An empirical study of the attitudes of international tourists and whale-watch tour operators","editor":[{"family":"Higham","given":"James E. S."},{"family":"Williams","given":"Rob"}],"author":[{"family":"Andersson","given":"Tommy D."},{"family":"Gothall","given":"Susanna E."},{"family":"Wende","given":"Beatrice D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The number of whale shark tours at Ningaloo increased by approx. 70% and the number of interactions with whale sharks by 370% between 2006 and ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"y3NXQMZB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(T. D. Andersson, 2014)","plainCitation":"(T. D. Andersson, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31683,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VFN8U8SB"],"itemData":{"id":31683,"type":"chapter","call-number":"QL737.C4 W435 2014","container-title":"Whale-watching: sustainable tourism and ecological management","event-place":"New York","ISBN":"978-0-521-19597-3","page":"95-109","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"New York","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Iceland and the resumption of whaling: An empirical study of the attitudes of international tourists and whale-watch tour operators","editor":[{"family":"Higham","given":"James E. S."},{"family":"Williams","given":"Rob"}],"author":[{"family":"Andersson","given":"Tommy D."},{"family":"Gothall","given":"Susanna E."},{"family":"Wende","given":"Beatrice D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	While sustainable tourism needs to carefully regulate the number of tourists and type of tourist activities, this may create tensions.  An unfair distribution of benefits within communities and inability to coordinate on a larger scale may undermine sustainable use, as was illustrated in the case of whale watching in Baja California, Mexico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CMEbvW3G","properties":{"formattedCitation":"({\\\\i{}Balancing Conservation with Development in Small-Scale Fisheries: Is Ecotourism an Empty Promise? | SpringerLink}, n.d.)","plainCitation":"(Balancing Conservation with Development in Small-Scale Fisheries: Is Ecotourism an Empty Promise? | SpringerLink, n.d.)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6858,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LQ22VANR"],"itemData":{"id":6858,"type":"article-journal","title":"Balancing Conservation with Development in Small-Scale Fisheries: Is Ecotourism an Empty Promise? | SpringerLink","URL":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/A:1018744011286","accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,28]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.The poaching of African elephants is estimated to represent US$ 25 million in lost revenue annually ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"61j8ecjK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Naidoo, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Naidoo, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32767,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PYI924BR"],"itemData":{"id":32767,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Recent surveys suggest tens of thousands of elephants are being poached annually across Africa, putting the two species at risk across much of their range. Although the financial motivations for ivory poaching are clear, the economic benefits of elephant conservation are poorly understood. We use Bayesian statistical modelling of tourist visits to protected areas, to quantify the lost economic benefits that poached elephants would have delivered to African countries via tourism. Our results show these figures are substantial (?USD $25 million annually), and that the lost benefits exceed the anti-poaching costs necessary to stop elephant declines across the continent’s savannah areas, although not currently in the forests of central Africa. Furthermore, elephant conservation in savannah protected areas has net positive economic returns comparable to investments in sectors such as education and infrastructure. Even from a tourism perspective alone, increased elephant conservation is therefore a wise investment by governments in these regions.","container-title":"Nature Communications","DOI":"10.1038/ncomms13379","ISSN":"2041-1723","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Commun","language":"en","license":"2016 The Author(s)","note":"Bandiera_abtest: a\\nCc_license_type: cc_by\\nCg_type: Nature Research Journals\\nnumber: 1\\nPrimary_atype: Research\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group\\nSubject_term: Conservation biology;Ecosystem services;Environmental economics\\nSubject_term_id: conservation;ecosystem-services;environmental-economics","page":"13379","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Estimating economic losses to tourism in Africa from the illegal killing of elephants","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/ncomms13379","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Brendan"},{"family":"Manica","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, those annual losses to tourism are small compared to the estimated $597 million that ivory from Africa’s poached elephants was worth annually on Chinese black markets from 2010–2012 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"R4Tuk7pl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Naidoo, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Naidoo, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32767,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PYI924BR"],"itemData":{"id":32767,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Recent surveys suggest tens of thousands of elephants are being poached annually across Africa, putting the two species at risk across much of their range. Although the financial motivations for ivory poaching are clear, the economic benefits of elephant conservation are poorly understood. We use Bayesian statistical modelling of tourist visits to protected areas, to quantify the lost economic benefits that poached elephants would have delivered to African countries via tourism. Our results show these figures are substantial (?USD $25 million annually), and that the lost benefits exceed the anti-poaching costs necessary to stop elephant declines across the continent’s savannah areas, although not currently in the forests of central Africa. Furthermore, elephant conservation in savannah protected areas has net positive economic returns comparable to investments in sectors such as education and infrastructure. Even from a tourism perspective alone, increased elephant conservation is therefore a wise investment by governments in these regions.","container-title":"Nature Communications","DOI":"10.1038/ncomms13379","ISSN":"2041-1723","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Commun","language":"en","license":"2016 The Author(s)","note":"Bandiera_abtest: a\\nCc_license_type: cc_by\\nCg_type: Nature Research Journals\\nnumber: 1\\nPrimary_atype: Research\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group\\nSubject_term: Conservation biology;Ecosystem services;Environmental economics\\nSubject_term_id: conservation;ecosystem-services;environmental-economics","page":"13379","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Estimating economic losses to tourism in Africa from the illegal killing of elephants","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/ncomms13379","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Brendan"},{"family":"Manica","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",11,1]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, the potential benefits from conservation do not necessarily trickle down to local communities, who may still experience crop losses and damages from elephants ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"T3oRffoh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Blignaut & de Wit, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Blignaut & de Wit, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6847,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EWWN3Z5P"],"itemData":{"id":6847,"type":"chapter","abstract":"ELEPHANTS PLAY a huge role within any landscape where they occur. They are habitat engineers. As charismatic species they awaken emotions among people like few others. As keystone species, they contribute significantly to the integrity of ecosystems and must be very carefully managed. From an economic perspective, they are also value generators. In this broad context, we first consider the range of relevant economic values, using the Total Economic Value approach in a generic sense, and then apply this framework to identify the specific factors that determine the economic value of elephants in South Africa. Thereafter we summarise both regional (southern African) and international studies that consider the economic value of elephants. We conclude with an assessment of the state of knowledge on elephants' contribution to the economic value of elephant-containing ecosystems and the economy as a whole. This assessment borrows heavily from studies concerning the economic value of elephants carried out in Botswana, Namibia, and Zimbabwe, since similar studies in South Africa could not be located. To date, published studies in South Africa focused either on the cost of the individual elephant management options – which is a subject treated in the relevant management chapters of this book – or else investigations of the value of tourism. The specific contribution of elephants to the value of tourism was not isolated in these studies.","ISBN":"978-1-86814-479-2","page":"446-476","source":"ResearchGate","title":"The Economic Value of Elephants","author":[{"family":"Blignaut","given":"James"},{"family":"Wit","given":"Martin","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",1,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hence, the investment in conservation areas for elephants for tourism may not be as successful as it might be if communities were benefiting directly ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"F1RFqKiK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Naidoo, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Naidoo, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32767,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PYI924BR"],"itemData":{"id":32767,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Recent surveys suggest tens of thousands of elephants are being poached annually across Africa, putting the two species at risk across much of their range. Although the financial motivations for ivory poaching are clear, the economic benefits of elephant conservation are poorly understood. We use Bayesian statistical modelling of tourist visits to protected areas, to quantify the lost economic benefits that poached elephants would have delivered to African countries via tourism. Our results show these figures are substantial (?USD $25 million annually), and that the lost benefits exceed the anti-poaching costs necessary to stop elephant declines across the continent’s savannah areas, although not currently in the forests of central Africa. Furthermore, elephant conservation in savannah protected areas has net positive economic returns comparable to investments in sectors such as education and infrastructure. Even from a tourism perspective alone, increased elephant conservation is therefore a wise investment by governments in these regions.","container-title":"Nature Communications","DOI":"10.1038/ncomms13379","ISSN":"2041-1723","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Commun","language":"en","license":"2016 The Author(s)","note":"Bandiera_abtest: a\\nCc_license_type: cc_by\\nCg_type: Nature Research Journals\\nnumber: 1\\nPrimary_atype: Research\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group\\nSubject_term: Conservation biology;Ecosystem services;Environmental economics\\nSubject_term_id: conservation;ecosystem-services;environmental-economics","page":"13379","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Estimating economic losses to tourism in Africa from the illegal killing of elephants","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/ncomms13379","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Brendan"},{"family":"Manica","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A way to counter this is to involve local communities directly into the benefit sharing of wild species populations. This can be done through either providing communities a portion of entrance fees, as done in Uganda  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mmDEJQPV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ahebwa, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Ahebwa, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30493,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RXELQPLF"],"itemData":{"id":30493,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Debates on how to deliver conservation benefits to communities living close to protected high-biodiversity areas have preoccupied conservationists for over 20 years. Tourism revenue sharing (TRS) has become a widespread policy intervention in Africa and elsewhere where charismatic populations of wildlife remain. This paper analyzes TRS policy at Bwindi Impenetrable National Park (BINP), Uganda, from a policy arrangements perspective. It is based on data collected at BINP and three surrounding parishes, using qualitative methods. It concludes that the governance capacity of the TRS policy arrangement at BINP is low due to the structural incongruence of the dimensions of the policy arrangement (analyzed in terms of actors, resources, rules of the game and discourses). Despite the participatory rhetoric of policy reforms, the Uganda Wildlife Authority remains the most powerful actor: it has control over resources and consequently determines the rules of the game. Local communities do not feel adequately compensated for conservation costs. This issue is exacerbated by weak communications with local people, problems of fair distribution locally and nationally, corruption claims and powerful local elites. To maximize TRS’ ability to contribute to conservation through development, inequities in the design of the TRS and dispersion of benefits need to be addressed.","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Tourism","DOI":"10.1080/09669582.2011.622768","ISSN":"0966-9582","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Routledge\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/09669582.2011.622768","page":"377-394","source":"Taylor and Francis+NEJM","title":"Tourism revenue sharing policy at Bwindi Impenetrable National Park, Uganda: a policy arrangements approach","title-short":"Tourism revenue sharing policy at Bwindi Impenetrable National Park, Uganda","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/09669582.2011.622768","volume":"20","author":[{"family":"Ahebwa","given":"Wilber Manyisa"},{"family":"Duim","given":"Rene","dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Sandbrook","given":"Chris"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",4,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or including them into private land conservation schemes, as done in Kenya ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"YNGt5JVv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. Blackburn, 2016)","plainCitation":"(S. Blackburn, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6846,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7G8R3WMP"],"itemData":{"id":6846,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Like many wildlife populations across Africa, recent analyses indicate that African lions are declining rapidly outside of small fenced areas. Community conservancies – privately protected areas that engage community members in conservation – may potentially maintain wildlife populations in unfenced pastoralist regions, but their effectiveness in conserving large carnivores has been largely unknown until now. We identify drivers of lion survival in community conservancies within the Masai Mara ecosystem, Kenya, applying mark–recapture analyses to continuous sight–resight surveys. We use the number of livestock and human settlements as proxies for potential human–lion conflict whilst controlling for environmental variables and lion socioecology. Average lion densities within the Mara conservancies between 2008 and 2013 (11·87 lions 100 km?2) were 2·6 times higher than those previously reported in 2003. Survival rates varied amongst prides and were highest for lions utilizing central regions of conservancies. The number of livestock settlements (bomas [corrals] and manyattas) that were not members of a conservancy, and that fell within a pride home-range, had a large negative effect on female survival and was the most influential external predictor in models. These non-conservancy settlements accounted for 37·2% of total observed variation in survival, whereas conservancy settlements, which benefit financially from wildlife through their membership, had no effect on lion survival. Internal drivers of survival agreed with known ecology and social behaviour including age plus a negative effect of male takeovers on cub survival <6 months. Vegetation cover, prey availability and the density of grazing livestock within a pride's range did not explain patterns in lion survival. Synthesis and applications. We show that lion densities have increased substantially within the Mara conservancies over the last decade and suggest that the creation of community conservancies has benefitted their survival. This suggests that lions can survive outside of fenced areas within pastoral regions if communities gain benefits from wildlife. We highlight the importance of expanding existing conservancies beyond their current geographical and political scope and forming buffer zones if wildlife ranges outside them. We suggest that changing attitudes to predators should be a key goal of community-based conservancies. Further work is recommended to identify what specific aspects of conservancy membership promote lion survival.","container-title":"Journal of Applied Ecology","DOI":"10.1111/1365-2664.12632","ISSN":"1365-2664","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"© 2016 The Authors. Journal of Applied Ecology published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd on behalf of British Ecological Society.","note":"_eprint: https://besjournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/1365-2664.12632","page":"1195-1205","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Human–wildlife conflict, benefit sharing and the survival of lions in pastoralist community-based conservancies","URL":"https://besjournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/1365-2664.12632","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Blackburn","given":"Sara"},{"family":"Hopcraft","given":"J. Grant C."},{"family":"Ogutu","given":"Joseph O."},{"family":"Matthiopoulos","given":"Jason"},{"family":"Frank","given":"Laurence"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Marine protected areas are important governance arrangements to make sure that marine resources are sustainably used. Support for such measures depend on how the costs and benefits are distributed. For example, on Mafia Island (Tanzania) an marine protected area faced local opposition because fisheries closures were affecting mostly those who live near those closed areas or have been fishing there historically. Gear and size regulations that affect all fishers similarly did not meet similar opposition ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JwzRFNJ4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McCLANAHAN 2008)","plainCitation":"(McCLANAHAN 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32722,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RUK5FUEW"],"itemData":{"id":32722,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Conflicts between resource users and managers are common and well documented on Mafia Island (Tanzania), where there has been a history of unresolved conflict over marine conservation initiatives. The perceptions of fisheries and park restrictions among resource users and managers were evaluated to try to understand the underlying causes of these conflicts. Responses concerning management preferences of government officials employed by the Mafia Island Marine Park (MIMP), personnel of the fisheries department, and heads of households in three villages in and out of the Park were compared. The largest differences in perceptions were found between villagers and managers, but all respondents agreed that minimum fish lengths and gear restrictions were beneficial and that benefits increased along the scale of the individual — community — national government. Villagers and government officials differed most in their perceptions towards areabased management, spatial and temporal closures, and species restrictions. Perceptions of management restrictions and benefits were only weakly correlated with the socioeconomic status of the villagers, but more strongly correlated with their living in or out of the Park and their family's economic options. The most negative perceptions towards restrictions were found in villages near fisheries closures, where there was a heavy reliance on marine resources and a higher numbers of jobs per household, but less reliance on cash crops, animal husbandry and tourism. The lack of these three options appears to have produced lower levels of support for MIMP and associated restrictions, and might be overcome by (1) using gear and minimum size restrictions more than fisheries closures and (2) increasing access to tourism, cash crops, animal husbandry and salaried employment, rather than simply increasing livelihood diversity.","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","ISSN":"0376-8929","issue":"4","note":"publisher: Cambridge University Press","page":"340-350","source":"JSTOR","title":"Management preferences, perceived benefits and conflicts among resource users and managers in the Mafia Island Marine Park, Tanzania","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/44520364","volume":"35","author":[{"family":"McCLANAHAN","given":"T. R."},{"family":"CINNER","given":"J."},{"family":"KAMUKURU","given":"A. T."},{"family":"ABUNGE","given":"C."},{"family":"NDAGALA","given":"J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In a survey among locals at the Pacific Coast of Costa Rica within a 30 km radius surrounding the Manuel Antonio National Park, perceived tourism to have positive effects on biodiversity through increased values of flora and fauna and decreased hunting and deforestation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sFLreqeB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Broadbent, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Broadbent, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6854,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/V6KFJI3G"],"itemData":{"id":6854,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Development in biodiversity rich areas is of global concern. While development may lead to socioeconomic benefits, this often comes concomitant with biodiversity loss and deforestation. Biodiversity rich areas present the opportunity for both improvements in socioeconomic conditions and conservation; however numerous challenges exist. Costa Rica’s Manuel Antonio National Park presents an ideal case study to investigate the balance between alternative forms of development which have contrasting environmental impacts. The Manuel Antonio region is a highly dynamic landscape experiencing deforestation, from agriculture, cattle ranching and oil palm plantations; and also reforestation from abandonment of land holdings and nature oriented tourism. Landscape dynamics are closely intertwined with the livelihoods and perspectives on biodiversity conservation of local communities, determining ecological sustainability. We use an analysis combining multi-temporal remote sensing of land cover dynamics from 1985 to 2008 with questionnaire data from local families on their socioeconomic status, perspectives on conservation, and perceived changes in local wildlife populations. Our results show that, while regeneration occurred and forest fragmentation in the area decreased from 1985 to 2008, Manuel Antonio National Park is rapidly becoming isolated. Decreasing ecological connectivity is related to the rapid expansion of oil palm plantations adjacent to the park and throughout the lowland areas. Perceived decreases in wildlife abundance and compositional change are evident throughout the area, with local communities attributing this primarily to illegal hunting activities. Nature based tourism in the area presents an effective strategy for conservation, including reductions in hunting, through increased valuation of biodiversity and protected areas, and socioeconomic advantages. However, without urgent efforts to limit deforestation and preserve the remaining forested corridor connecting the park to core primary forest, the ability to maintain biodiversity in the park will be reduced.","container-title":"Landscape Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10980-012-9722-7","ISSN":"1572-9761","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Landscape Ecol","language":"en","page":"731-744","source":"Springer Link","title":"The effect of land use change and ecotourism on biodiversity: a case study of Manuel Antonio, Costa Rica, from 1985 to 2008","title-short":"The effect of land use change and ecotourism on biodiversity","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10980-012-9722-7","volume":"27","author":[{"family":"Broadbent","given":"Eben N."},{"family":"Zambrano","given":"Angélica M. Almeyda"},{"family":"Dirzo","given":"Rodolfo"},{"family":"Durham","given":"William H."},{"family":"Driscoll","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Gallagher","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Salters","given":"Rosalyn"},{"family":"Schultz","given":"Jared"},{"family":"Colmenares","given":"Angélica"},{"family":"Randolph","given":"Shannon G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",5,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	Common property rights arrangements can be an important tool to control unsustainable tourism growth and make sure that the benefits are distributed in a fair way. Such management approach was also ranked as the most favorable one among stakeholders in long-term whale shark (Rhincodon typus) tourism in Bahia de Angeles, located in the oriental coast of Baja California, Mexico ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4SqpmjOX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rodr\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}guez-Dowdell, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Rodríguez-Dowdell, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6859,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RLRTPETT"],"itemData":{"id":6859,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Predictable and long-term whale shark (Rhincodon typus) aggregations can be observed in few locations around the world. In some places where this occurs the use of the species through ecotourism has become an important economic activity. Bahia de los Angeles, Mexico is an important habitat for whale sharks for up to 7 months per year. Based on their presence, ecotourism activities with the species have become more popular among the local community in recent years. Whale sharks and their habitat represent an important form of natural capital with high potential to produce economic value; however this has not translated in an improvement of the local communities’ quality of life due to several limitations that the activity, resource and users confront. The most evident threat is free access, carrying with it a potential loss of economic benefits due to resource saturation and when external groups use the resource or tourists observe the species without having to hire local tour operators. It is recognized that property right regimes are fundamental because they define the rights and obligations for the use of natural resources and the rules by which these rights and obligations are implemented. The present study recommends the implementation of a strategy for the sustainable management of whale sharks based on property rights; taking into account both the characteristics of the resource and the social context where it is used. Through the opinion of a consultant panel comprised of representatives from federal, state and municipal governments, as well as academics, non-governmental organizations and local users of the resource, three different options are analysed – free access; a limited number of permits for local users; a concession of the area in favour of the group of local users – by means of four qualitative criteria (efficiency, equity, transaction costs and acceptance) and a quantitative criterion (duration), using multicriteria analysis. The evaluation concludes that the scenario which is the most efficient, equitable, with lower transaction costs and more acceptable is a concession of the area in favour of the group of local users.","collection-title":"Whale Sharks: Science, Conservation and Management","container-title":"Fisheries Research","DOI":"10.1016/j.fishres.2006.11.020","ISSN":"0165-7836","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Fisheries Research","language":"en","page":"119-127","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Property rights-based management: Whale shark ecotourism in Bahia de los Angeles, Mexico","title-short":"Property rights-based management","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0165783606004097","volume":"84","author":[{"family":"Rodríguez-Dowdell","given":"Natalie"},{"family":"Enríquez-Andrade","given":"Roberto"},{"family":"Cárdenas-Torres","given":"Nirari"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",3,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Further evidence on potential success factor of community-based management comes from Bigodi Village, located in the Kamwenge district of western Uganda. The is managed by a community-based organisation that tries to reconcile ecological, economic, and social sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kahO9hfv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gosling, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Gosling, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34379,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CJCALMSU"],"itemData":{"id":34379,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Conservation and sustainable management of wetlands requires participation of local stakeholders, including communities. The Bigodi Wetland is unusual because it is situated in a common property landscape but the local community has been running a successful community-based natural resource management programme (CBNRM) for the wetland for over a decade. Whilst external visitors to the wetland provide ecotourism revenues we sought to quantify community benefits through the use of wetland goods such as firewood, plant fibres, and the like, and costs associated with wild animals damaging farming activities. We interviewed 68 households living close to the wetland and valued their cash and non-cash incomes from farming and collection of non-timber forest products (NTFPs) and water. The majority of households collected a wide variety of plant and fish resources and water from the wetland for household use and livestock. Overall, 53% of total household cash and non-cash income was from collected products, mostly the wetland, 28% from arable agriculture, 12% from livestock and 7% from employment and cash transfers. Female-headed households had lower incomes than male-headed ones, and with a greater reliance on NTFPs. Annual losses due to wildlife damage were estimated at 4.2% of total gross income. Most respondents felt that the wetland was important for their livelihoods, with more than 80% identifying health, education, craft materials and firewood as key benefits. Ninety-five percent felt that the wetland was in a good condition and that most residents observed the agreed CBNRM rules regarding use of the wetland. This study confirms the success of the locally run CBNRM processes underlying the significant role that the wetland plays in local livelihoods.","container-title":"Wetlands Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1007/s11273-017-9546-y","ISSN":"1572-9834","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Wetlands Ecol Manage","language":"en","page":"717-730","source":"Springer Link","title":"Community-based natural resource use and management of Bigodi Wetland Sanctuary, Uganda, for livelihood benefits","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11273-017-9546-y","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Gosling","given":"A."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."},{"family":"Gambiza","given":"J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Tourism initiatives include guided walks, homestays with local households and visits to the houses of crafters, healers, and elders, where the Bigodi community benefits from the wetland sanctuary as 75–80% of the tourism generated funds are invested into village infrastructure, including a school, library, a clinic, roads, pathways, sanitation and training courses ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NPi4vZqI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gosling, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Gosling, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34379,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CJCALMSU"],"itemData":{"id":34379,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Conservation and sustainable management of wetlands requires participation of local stakeholders, including communities. The Bigodi Wetland is unusual because it is situated in a common property landscape but the local community has been running a successful community-based natural resource management programme (CBNRM) for the wetland for over a decade. Whilst external visitors to the wetland provide ecotourism revenues we sought to quantify community benefits through the use of wetland goods such as firewood, plant fibres, and the like, and costs associated with wild animals damaging farming activities. We interviewed 68 households living close to the wetland and valued their cash and non-cash incomes from farming and collection of non-timber forest products (NTFPs) and water. The majority of households collected a wide variety of plant and fish resources and water from the wetland for household use and livestock. Overall, 53% of total household cash and non-cash income was from collected products, mostly the wetland, 28% from arable agriculture, 12% from livestock and 7% from employment and cash transfers. Female-headed households had lower incomes than male-headed ones, and with a greater reliance on NTFPs. Annual losses due to wildlife damage were estimated at 4.2% of total gross income. Most respondents felt that the wetland was important for their livelihoods, with more than 80% identifying health, education, craft materials and firewood as key benefits. Ninety-five percent felt that the wetland was in a good condition and that most residents observed the agreed CBNRM rules regarding use of the wetland. This study confirms the success of the locally run CBNRM processes underlying the significant role that the wetland plays in local livelihoods.","container-title":"Wetlands Ecology and Management","DOI":"10.1007/s11273-017-9546-y","ISSN":"1572-9834","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"Wetlands Ecol Manage","language":"en","page":"717-730","source":"Springer Link","title":"Community-based natural resource use and management of Bigodi Wetland Sanctuary, Uganda, for livelihood benefits","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11273-017-9546-y","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Gosling","given":"A."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie M."},{"family":"Gambiza","given":"J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Territorial user rights have shown to be a promising governance arrangements, though it requires trust and careful consideration of local contexts to foster sustainable use and reconcile conflicting objectives and practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"A34GWT51","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biggs, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Biggs, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33044,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/42D9VW69"],"itemData":{"id":33044,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Novel solutions to conserve biodiversity whilst allowing for resource harvesting are urgently needed. In marine systems, Territorial User Rights for Fisheries (TURFs) are promoted to enable sustainable use of resources. We investigate the potential for synergies between nature-based tourism and TURFs on Chile’s central coast. Of 135 recreational divers surveyed, 77% indicated that the fish species they preferred sighting were declining and 80% indicated that they would dive more often in TURFs, which have higher abundance of favoured species. Regression analysis shows that respondents that perceive that TURFs fulfil a conservation function are more willing to pay to dive in a TURF. However, respondents who understand the bureaucratic functioning of a TURF are less willing to pay, and there is diversity in how divers feel payments should be made. A participatory approach is required to navigate these complexities to achieve synergies between nature-based tourism and resource harvesting in TURFs.","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0148862","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"PLOS ONE","language":"en","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e0148862","source":"PLoS Journals","title":"Potential Synergies between Nature-Based Tourism and Sustainable Use of Marine Resources: Insights from Dive Tourism in Territorial User Rights for Fisheries in Chile","title-short":"Potential Synergies between Nature-Based Tourism and Sustainable Use of Marine Resources","URL":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0148862","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Amar","given":"Francisca"},{"family":"Valdebenito","given":"Abel"},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"Stefan"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",3,29]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  

Extractive forms of tourism – terrestrial animal harvesting and fishing
So-called ‘trophy hunting’ is one dimension of tourism that is fiercely controversial ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JTTn7D1m","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(F. Nelson, 2013)","plainCitation":"(F. Nelson, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33012,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7ZJDXWGW"],"itemData":{"id":33012,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Lion Panthera leo populations and distributions in Africa have contracted considerably in the past 30 years. Recent policy debates focus on restricting trophy hunting as a measure to address concerns about excessive o?takes of lions. We review the impact of trophy hunting in relation to lion conservation goals, using comparative case studies from Southern and East Africa, which together contain most of Africa’s remaining lion populations. The comparison demonstrates that the impact of trophy hunting on lion populations is variable and shaped by the way trophy hunting is managed and wildlife is governed in di?erent range states. In Tanzania, the most important lion range state, hunting produces signi?cant revenues but weaknesses in how hunting is managed and revenues are distributed undermine the potential of hunting and encourage overharvesting. In Southern Africa linkages are stronger between revenue generated by trophy hunting and lion conservation outcomes on private and communal lands. Trophy hunting is most bene?cial to lion conservation where revenues and user rights over wildlife are devolved, ensuring bene?ts from lion hunting compensate for their costs to local people, and where hunting is managed through long-term and competitively allocated concession systems. Policy interventions should focus on supporting trophy hunting as a conservation tool where it is e?ective and well-managed, and work to promote reform of hunting and wildlife governance elsewhere.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S003060531200035X","ISSN":"0030-6053, 1365-3008","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Oryx","language":"en","page":"501-509","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Trophy hunting and lion conservation: a question of governance?","title-short":"Trophy hunting and lion conservation","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S003060531200035X/type/journal_article","volume":"47","author":[{"family":"Nelson","given":"Fred"},{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",10]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The idea – and images – of the hunting of iconic species for recreational value is unappealing to a growing urban population ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TG4eQJeo","properties":{"unsorted":true,"formattedCitation":"(Biggs, 2019b; Manfredo, 2017; Parker, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Biggs, 2019b; Manfredo, 2017; Parker, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"Uebb2sCb/wseVfO8O","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/local/L7afBCgj/items/Q7QUXHXD"],"itemData":{"id":1813,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"One strategy to address threats to biodiversity in the face of ongoing budget constraints is to create an enabling environment that facilitates individuals, communities and other groups to self-organise to achieve conservation outcomes. Emergence (new activities and initiatives), and robustness (durability of these activities and initiatives over time), two related concepts from the common pool resources literature, provide guidance on how to support and enable such self-organised action for conservation. To date emergence has received little attention in the literature. Our exploratory synthesis of the conditions for emergence from the literature highlighted four themes: for conservation to emerge, actors need to 1) recognise the need for change, 2) expect positive outcomes, 3) be able to experiment to achieve collective learning, and 4) have legitimate local scale governance authority. Insights from the literature on emergence and robustness suggest that an appropriate balance should be maintained between external guidance of conservation and enabling local actors to find solutions appropriate to their contexts. We illustrate the conditions for emergence, and its interaction with robustness, through discussing the Communal Areas Management Programme for Indigenous Resources (CAMPFIRE) in Zimbabwe and reflect on efforts at strengthening local autonomy and management around the world. We suggest that the delicate balance between external guidance of actions, and supporting local actors to develop their own solutions, should be managed adaptively over time to support the emergence of robust conservation actions.","container-title":"Global Ecology and Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.gecco.2019.e00538","ISSN":"2351-9894","language":"en","page":"e00538","title":"Insights on fostering the emergence of robust conservation actions from Zimbabwe's CAMPFIRE program","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Ban","given":"Natalie C."},{"family":"Castilla","given":"Juan Carlos"},{"family":"Gelcich","given":"Stefan"},{"family":"Mills","given":"Morena"},{"family":"Gandiwa","given":"Edson"},{"family":"Etienne","given":"Michel"},{"family":"Knight","given":"Andrew T."},{"family":"Marquet","given":"Pablo A."},{"family":"Possingham","given":"Hugh P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",1]]}}},{"id":33001,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GA7BZKBP"],"itemData":{"id":33001,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"303–311","title":"Values, trust, and cultural backlash in conservation governance: The case of wildlife management in the United States","volume":"214","author":[{"family":"Manfredo","given":"M.J."},{"family":"Teel","given":"T.L."},{"family":"Sullivan","given":"L."},{"family":"Dietsch","given":"A.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33874,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LXW45EBY"],"itemData":{"id":33874,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Science and Practice","DOI":"10.1111/csp2.214","ISSN":"2578-4854, 2578-4854","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Conservat Sci and Prac","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Impacts of a trophy hunting ban on private land conservation in South African biodiversity hotspots","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/csp2.214","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Parker","given":"Kim"},{"family":"De Vos","given":"Alta"},{"family":"Clements","given":"Hayley S."},{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Biggs","given":"Reinette"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hunting can at times have adverse population consequences (e.g. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mz6mYSd2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Packer 2011)","plainCitation":"(Packer 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32727,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/53HGBIYN"],"itemData":{"id":32727,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract:Tanzania holds most of the remaining large populations of African lions (Panthera leo) and has extensive areas of leopard habitat (Panthera pardus), and both species are subjected to sizable harvests by sport hunters. As a first step toward establishing sustainable management strategies, we","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2010.01576.x","ISSN":"0888-8892","issue":"1","language":"ENGLISH","note":"PMID: 20825444","page":"142-153","source":"insights.ovid.com","title":"Effects of Trophy Hunting on Lion and Leopard Populations in Tanzania","URL":"insights.ovid.com","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Packer","given":"C."},{"family":"Brink","given":"H."},{"family":"Kissui","given":"B."},{"family":"Maliti","given":"H."},{"family":"Kushnir","given":"H."},{"family":"Caro","given":"T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, especially in combination with environmental factors ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rLWmsgO6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wilfred & MacColl, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Wilfred & MacColl, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":16370,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TU6I7EV4"],"itemData":{"id":16370,"type":"article-journal","title":"Status of wildlife at trophy hunting sites in the Ugalla Game Reserve of Western Tanzania","URL":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/1940082916667336","author":[{"family":"Wilfred","given":"Paulo"},{"family":"MacColl","given":"Andrew D.C."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The revenues by trophy hunting are sizable, but small compared to total tourism expenditures and often not benefiting local communities, leaving conservation benefits unclear in many cases  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zzlWdx8G","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Campbell, R, 2013; Chardonnet, 2019; Grijalva, 2016; Naidoo, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Campbell, R, 2013; Chardonnet, 2019; Grijalva, 2016; Naidoo, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32721,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/F48CTSLX"],"itemData":{"id":32721,"type":"book","publisher":"A report for the African Lion Coalition, prepared by Economists at Large …","source":"Google Scholar","title":"The 200 Million Question: How Much Does Trophy Hunting Really Contribute to African Communities","title-short":"The $??? Million Question","author":[{"family":"Campbell, R","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":32720,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/78GZF9AD"],"itemData":{"id":32720,"type":"book","publisher":"A report by the Democratic staff of the House Committee on Natural Resources …","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Missing the Mark: African trophy hunting fails to show consistent conservation benefits","title-short":"Missing the Mark","author":[{"family":"Grijalva","given":"R. M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32718,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QW5JAW72"],"itemData":{"id":32718,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"IUCN PAPACO. p","title":"Africa is changing: Should its protected areas evolve reconfiguring the protected areas in Africa","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Chardonnet","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32767,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PYI924BR"],"itemData":{"id":32767,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Recent surveys suggest tens of thousands of elephants are being poached annually across Africa, putting the two species at risk across much of their range. Although the financial motivations for ivory poaching are clear, the economic benefits of elephant conservation are poorly understood. We use Bayesian statistical modelling of tourist visits to protected areas, to quantify the lost economic benefits that poached elephants would have delivered to African countries via tourism. Our results show these figures are substantial (?USD $25 million annually), and that the lost benefits exceed the anti-poaching costs necessary to stop elephant declines across the continent’s savannah areas, although not currently in the forests of central Africa. Furthermore, elephant conservation in savannah protected areas has net positive economic returns comparable to investments in sectors such as education and infrastructure. Even from a tourism perspective alone, increased elephant conservation is therefore a wise investment by governments in these regions.","container-title":"Nature Communications","DOI":"10.1038/ncomms13379","ISSN":"2041-1723","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Commun","language":"en","license":"2016 The Author(s)","note":"Bandiera_abtest: a\\nCc_license_type: cc_by\\nCg_type: Nature Research Journals\\nnumber: 1\\nPrimary_atype: Research\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group\\nSubject_term: Conservation biology;Ecosystem services;Environmental economics\\nSubject_term_id: conservation;ecosystem-services;environmental-economics","page":"13379","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Estimating economic losses to tourism in Africa from the illegal killing of elephants","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/ncomms13379","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Brendan"},{"family":"Manica","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",11,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Tanzania, for instance, hunting operators distributed to the communities an average annual sum of 1.04 million US%, i.e. 0.08 US% per hectare per year ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WYWb26E8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chardonnet, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Chardonnet, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32718,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QW5JAW72"],"itemData":{"id":32718,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"IUCN PAPACO. p","title":"Africa is changing: Should its protected areas evolve reconfiguring the protected areas in Africa","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Chardonnet","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For comparison, the Maasai Mara conservancies in Kenya – where trophy hunting is forbidden – distribute around 40 US$ per hectare per year, while also contributing to local employment ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"trmBGCJi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chardonnet, 2019; Oduor, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Chardonnet, 2019; Oduor, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32717,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/94LNIAJ3"],"itemData":{"id":32717,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Community-based wildlife conservation (CBWC) programmes have been a pervasive paradigm in the conservation circles since the 1970s. The key elements of such programmes are that local communities are given ownership rights or custodianship and management responsibilities over wildlife, and that they gain social and economic benefits from conservation of the resources. However, to date, there have been only a few studies that offer in-depth analyses of the interplay between governance processes and livelihood impacts of CBWC programmes. Here, I conducted key informant interviews and focus group discussions in five wildlife conservancies in the Maasai Mara ecosystem in Kenya to address the following questions: i) What are the perceived impacts of the wildlife conservancies on livelihoods of the local people? ii) To what extent are the wildlife conservancies governed in relation to the principles of environmental governance? I assessed impacts on livelihoods by applying the Sustainable Livelihoods Framework to explore perceived conservancy-related benefits and costs (i.e. perceived changes in social, financial, human, physical, and natural capitals). I assessed governance by asking the respondents whether the following eight principles of environmental governance were applied: legitimacy, transparency, accountability, inclusiveness, fairness, integration, capability, and adaptability. Perceived benefits of participating in wildlife co-management were identified as: enhanced income from gainful employment and new business opportunities, membership to cooperative societies and participation in community work (e.g. school bursary and feeding programmes), enhanced social relations, improved access to credit and health facilities, enhanced physical infrastructure (schools, roads and bridges), improved physical security and coordinated sharing of provisioning ecosystem services like pasture and water. The principles of legitimacy, inclusiveness, and integration had reportedly been well implemented in wildlife co-management. However, the institutional mechanisms for sharing resources within the conservancies lacked transparency, accountability, and fairness, and tended to favour those who were politically connected to the leadership of the conservancies. Moreover, most of the conservancies had weak systems and few resources to facilitate delivery on responsibilities (i.e. had low capability), and had some costs associated with human-wildlife conflicts. For an improved co-management of wildlife to be achieved in these conservancies, local institutions should be reconfigured to allow active participation by conservancy landowners in decision-making, information sharing, and equitable access to conservancy-related benefits.","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.jenvman.2020.110133","ISSN":"0301-4797","journalAbbreviation":"Journal of Environmental Management","language":"en","page":"110133","source":"ScienceDirect","title":"Livelihood impacts and governance processes of community-based wildlife conservation in Maasai Mara ecosystem, Kenya","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301479720300712","volume":"260","author":[{"family":"Oduor","given":"Ayub M. O."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",4,15]]}}},{"id":32718,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QW5JAW72"],"itemData":{"id":32718,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"IUCN PAPACO. p","title":"Africa is changing: Should its protected areas evolve reconfiguring the protected areas in Africa","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Chardonnet","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	However, systems of trophy hunting have been shown to have potential to ensure the sustainable use of wild species if managed appropriately ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9NRBoljK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. E. Baker, 1997; Begg, 2018; Dickman, 2019; IUCN, 2016)","plainCitation":"(J. E. Baker, 1997; Begg, 2018; Dickman, 2019; IUCN, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"Uebb2sCb/L6DAyML2","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/local/L7afBCgj/items/BTED8Y9V"],"itemData":{"id":2188,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Preserving wildlife in a pristine state on a large scale is no longer feasible in view of continued human population increases, economic development, habitat fragmentation and degradation, the introduction of nonnative species, and commercialisation of wildlife products. The wise use of the planet's remaining wildlife resources will depend on management practices which recognise that indigenous people are integral parts of ecosystems. Community-based conservation, which attempts to devolve responsibility for the sustainable use of wildlife resources to the local level, can include consumptive activities, such as trophy hunting, as well as nonconsumptive forms of tourism. The trophy hunting management systems of six countries of eastern and southern Africa are profiled and critiqued, demonstrating a number of essential conditions for obtaining optimal wildlife conservation and community benefits.","container-title":"Journal of Sustainable Tourism","DOI":"10.1080/09669589708667294","ISSN":"0966-9582","issue":"4","page":"306–321","title":"Trophy Hunting as a Sustainable Use of Wildlife Resources in Southern and Eastern Africa","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Baker","given":"Joni E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997",12]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/SYRTzEVp","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/local/L7afBCgj/items/V6GSQKHX"],"itemData":{"id":2047,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Sport hunting of wildlife can play a role in conservation but can also drive population declines if not managed sustainably. Previous simulation modelling found that large felid species could theoretically be hunted sustainably by restricting harvests to older individuals that have likely reproduced. Several African countries currently use age?based hunting for lions although the outcomes have yet to be evaluated in a wild population. Here we provide the first empirical evidence that a system of incentives sufficiently encouraged age?based hunting and reduced offtake of a wild felid, thereby reducing the potential risk of unsustainable hunting on a threatened species. We examined long?term hunting data and the lion population trend in Niassa National Reserve, Mozambique. To incentivise hunter compliance, a “points” system was developed, which rewards operators that harvest lions older than the 6?year minimum trophy age recommended for sustainable hunting and penalises operators that hunt “underage” lions (\\\\textless4 years). A key component of this system is the ecological application of key physical traits that predictably change with age in order to estimate (by hunters) and validate (by authorities) trophy individuals’ ages pre? and post?mortem, respectively. Analysis of 138 lion hunts and 87 lion trophies from 2003 to 2015 revealed that after enforcement of age restrictions in 2006, hunters shifted harvests to suitably aged lions (\\\\textgreater6 years), from 25% of offtakes in 2004 to 100% by 2014. Simultaneously, the number of lions and percentage of quota harvested decreased, resulting in lower lion offtakes. Following an initial decrease after enforcement of the ageing system, the percentage of hunts harvesting lions stabilised, demonstrating that hunters successfully located and aged older lions. Synthesis and applications. Evidence suggests that age restrictions combined with an incentive?based points system regulated sport hunting and reduced pressure on the lion population. We attribute the successful implementation of this management system to: (1) committed, consistent enforcement by management authorities, (2) genuine involvement of all stakeholders from the start, (3) annual auditing by an independent third party, (4) the reliable, transparent, straightforward ageing process and (5) the simple, pragmatic points system for incentivising hunter compliance. Our study demonstrates that the use of age restrictions can increase the selectivity of sport hunting and lower trophy offtakes to reduce the possibility of unsustainable sport hunting negatively impacting species populations in the absence of reliable estimates of population size. It must be noted, however, that there was no measurable change in the lion numbers over the past decade that could be attributed to the implementation of this policy alone.","ISSN":"0021-8901","language":"en","title":"Effective implementation of age restrictions increases selectivity of sport hunting of the African lion","URL":"https://pubag.nal.usda.gov/catalog/5871979","author":[{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen M."},{"family":"Miller","given":"Jennifer R. B."},{"family":"Begg","given":"Keith S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/ZQzSjkyT","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/local/L7afBCgj/items/W4NL2G7N"],"itemData":{"id":1814,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aaz0735","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6456","language":"en","page":"874–874","title":"Trophy hunting bans imperil biodiversity","volume":"365","author":[{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"},{"family":"Johnson","given":"Paul J."},{"family":"Louis","given":"Maxi Pia"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"literal":"128 signatories"}],"editor":[{"family":"Sills","given":"Jennifer"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",8]]}}},{"id":"Uebb2sCb/FUDzA25v","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/local/L7afBCgj/items/JWPIX9WY"],"itemData":{"id":2190,"type":"report","abstract":"Trophy hunting is hunting of animals with specific desired characteristics (such as large antlers), and overlaps with widely practiced hunting for meat. ...","publisher":"IUCN","title":"Informing decisions on trophy hunting: A briefing paper for European Union decision-makers regarding potential plans for restriction of imports of hunting trophies","title-short":"Informing decisions on trophy hunting","URL":"https://www.iucn.org/sites/dev/files/iucn_sept_briefing_paper_-_informingdecisionstrophyhunting.pdf","author":[{"literal":"IUCN"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, in Namibia, wild species have multiple economic uses and values including that of wild species tourism and trophy hunting, often on the same property ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zKIbDzx9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Naidoo, 2016; J. A. Richardson, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Naidoo, 2016; J. A. Richardson, 1998)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32767,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PYI924BR"],"itemData":{"id":32767,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Recent surveys suggest tens of thousands of elephants are being poached annually across Africa, putting the two species at risk across much of their range. Although the financial motivations for ivory poaching are clear, the economic benefits of elephant conservation are poorly understood. We use Bayesian statistical modelling of tourist visits to protected areas, to quantify the lost economic benefits that poached elephants would have delivered to African countries via tourism. Our results show these figures are substantial (?USD $25 million annually), and that the lost benefits exceed the anti-poaching costs necessary to stop elephant declines across the continent’s savannah areas, although not currently in the forests of central Africa. Furthermore, elephant conservation in savannah protected areas has net positive economic returns comparable to investments in sectors such as education and infrastructure. Even from a tourism perspective alone, increased elephant conservation is therefore a wise investment by governments in these regions.","container-title":"Nature Communications","DOI":"10.1038/ncomms13379","ISSN":"2041-1723","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Commun","language":"en","license":"2016 The Author(s)","note":"Bandiera_abtest: a\\nCc_license_type: cc_by\\nCg_type: Nature Research Journals\\nnumber: 1\\nPrimary_atype: Research\\npublisher: Nature Publishing Group\\nSubject_term: Conservation biology;Ecosystem services;Environmental economics\\nSubject_term_id: conservation;ecosystem-services;environmental-economics","page":"13379","source":"www.nature.com","title":"Estimating economic losses to tourism in Africa from the illegal killing of elephants","URL":"https://www.nature.com/articles/ncomms13379","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Naidoo","given":"Robin"},{"family":"Fisher","given":"Brendan"},{"family":"Manica","given":"Andrea"},{"family":"Balmford","given":"Andrew"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",11,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",11,1]]}}},{"id":32998,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KSKIIRSC"],"itemData":{"id":32998,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity & Conservation","language":"en","page":"549–59","title":"Wildlife Utilization and Biodiversity Conservation in Namibia: Conflicting or Complementaryobjectives?","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Richardson","given":"Julie A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Moreover, they are often a primary source of revenue in regions that are either non-conducive for commercial photo tourism safaris or politically unstable ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tPmuzK5s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2007)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31039,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ERD2GERZ"],"itemData":{"id":31039,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2006.09.005","ISSN":"00063207","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"455-469","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Economic and conservation significance of the trophy hunting industry in sub-Saharan Africa","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320706003831","volume":"134","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"P.A."},{"family":"Roulet","given":"P.A."},{"family":"Romañach","given":"S.S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",2]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Trophy hunting often provides vital financial resources needed for conservation, though it also puts pressure on a population, which – unless well-regulated and managed in line with scientific principles – undermines sustainability goals. Sustainability may be especially threatened in the presence of illegal harvesting and interaction with other drivers ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kofHvZ57","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Muposhi, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Muposhi, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34000,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EKWWSBBV"],"itemData":{"id":34000,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Not Available","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0164429","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","page":"e0164429","source":"NASA ADS","title":"Trophy Hunting and Sustainability: Temporal Dynamics in Trophy Quality and Harvesting Patterns of Wild Herbivores in a Tropical Semi-Arid Savanna Ecosystem","title-short":"Trophy Hunting and Sustainability","URL":"http://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2016PLoSO..1164429M","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Muposhi","given":"Victor K."},{"family":"Gandiwa","given":"Edson"},{"family":"Bartels","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Makuza","given":"Stanley M."},{"family":"Madiri","given":"Tinaapi H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",10,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Community-based hunting of the snow leopard, Panthere uncia, in Tajikistan offers an example of the benefits of well-managed trophy hunting. There, adequate revenue-sharing and transparency community- based trophy hunting programs have improved the availability of food resources for the snow leopard by incentivizing communities to protect and manage wild ungulate populations at levels that can sustain trophy hunting as well as predation by snow leopards ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ke4wghv8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kachel, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Kachel, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12120,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KIJP2RYD"],"itemData":{"id":12120,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"597–604","title":"Investigating the Potential Impact of Trophy Hunting of Wild Ungulates on Snow Leopard Panthera Uncia Conservation in Tajikistan","volume":"51","author":[{"family":"Kachel","given":"Shannon M."},{"family":"McCarthy","given":"Kyle P."},{"family":"McCarthy","given":"Thomas M."},{"family":"Oshurmamadov","given":"Nuzar"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	However, while trophy hunting creates revenues, conflicts arise if benefits of various conservation activities are distributed unequally. In the Tarangire National Park in the Maasai Steppe (Tanzania), widespread conflicts between centrally-issued trophy hunting concessions and village–private tourism ventures have been observed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xb2fnZvC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sachedina & Nelson, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Sachedina & Nelson, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4325,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B6YBTBF6"],"itemData":{"id":4325,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Two issues of central importance to conservation are developing an improved understanding of the relative roles of state protected areas and local institutions and developing effective strategies for creating community-based incentives for conservation. We provide a case study of northern Tanzania’s Maasai Steppe to explore these issues in the context of a savannah ecosystem where wildlife is mobile and depends extensively on community lands for seasonal habitats. We compare the impacts and outcomes of four approaches to developing local incentives for wildlife conservation on community lands: protected area benefit-sharing, trophy hunting donations, village–private tourism concession contracts, and a direct payment scheme for habitat conservation. Tourism and direct payment concession areas have resulted in large areas of community land being protected for wildlife by villages as a result of the conditional and contractual nature of these ventures. By contrast, other approaches that provide economic benefits to communities but are not conditional on defined conservation actions at the local level demonstrate little impact on wildlife conservation on community lands. In spatially extensive ecosystems where protected areas cover limited areas and wildlife relies heavily on community and private lands, strategies based on maximizing the direct income of communities from wildlife are fundamental to the sustainability of such systems.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605310000499","ISSN":"1365-3008, 0030-6053","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"390–398","title":"Protected areas and community incentives in savannah ecosystems: a case study of Tanzania’s Maasai Steppe","title-short":"Protected areas and community incentives in savannah ecosystems","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/protected-areas-and-community-incentives-in-savannah-ecosystems-a-case-study-of-tanzanias-maasai-steppe/6F15A4ECC78995622DDA39F557016206/core-reader","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Sachedina","given":"Hassan"},{"family":"Nelson","given":"Fred"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These conflicts arise because local communities capture revenues directly from tourism whereas hunting revenues go to the state, putting a constraint on the viability of community-level tourism ventures ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4uLOXF8q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sachedina & Nelson, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Sachedina & Nelson, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4325,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B6YBTBF6"],"itemData":{"id":4325,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Two issues of central importance to conservation are developing an improved understanding of the relative roles of state protected areas and local institutions and developing effective strategies for creating community-based incentives for conservation. We provide a case study of northern Tanzania’s Maasai Steppe to explore these issues in the context of a savannah ecosystem where wildlife is mobile and depends extensively on community lands for seasonal habitats. We compare the impacts and outcomes of four approaches to developing local incentives for wildlife conservation on community lands: protected area benefit-sharing, trophy hunting donations, village–private tourism concession contracts, and a direct payment scheme for habitat conservation. Tourism and direct payment concession areas have resulted in large areas of community land being protected for wildlife by villages as a result of the conditional and contractual nature of these ventures. By contrast, other approaches that provide economic benefits to communities but are not conditional on defined conservation actions at the local level demonstrate little impact on wildlife conservation on community lands. In spatially extensive ecosystems where protected areas cover limited areas and wildlife relies heavily on community and private lands, strategies based on maximizing the direct income of communities from wildlife are fundamental to the sustainability of such systems.","container-title":"Oryx","DOI":"10.1017/S0030605310000499","ISSN":"1365-3008, 0030-6053","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"390–398","title":"Protected areas and community incentives in savannah ecosystems: a case study of Tanzania’s Maasai Steppe","title-short":"Protected areas and community incentives in savannah ecosystems","URL":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/oryx/article/protected-areas-and-community-incentives-in-savannah-ecosystems-a-case-study-of-tanzanias-maasai-steppe/6F15A4ECC78995622DDA39F557016206/core-reader","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Sachedina","given":"Hassan"},{"family":"Nelson","given":"Fred"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010",7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A key challenge regarding sustainable use remains setting appropriate quota in light of insufficient scientific evidence ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Zj2LQdqI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W43CISEZ"],"itemData":{"id":32740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The bushmeat trade, or the illegal acquisition and exchange of wild meat, has long been recognised as a severe problem in forest biomes, but receives little attention in savannas, perhaps due to a misconception that bushmeat hunting is a low-impact subsistence activity. Though data on impacts are scarce, indications are that bushmeat hunting is a widespread problem in savannas, with severe impacts on wildlife populations and wildlife-based land uses. The impacts of the bushmeat trade in savannas vary from edge-effects around protected areas, to disproportionate declines of some species, to severe wildlife declines in areas with inadequate anti-poaching. In some areas, bushmeat contributes signi?cantly to food security, but these bene?ts are unsustainable, and hunting is wasteful, utilising a fraction of the wildlife killed or of its ?nancial value obtainable through tourism, trophy hunting and/or legal game meat production. The bushmeat trade appears to be becoming increasingly commercialised due to elevated demand in rural areas, urban centres and even overseas cities. Other drivers for the trade include human encroachment of wildlife areas; poverty and food insecurity; and inadequate legal frameworks to enable communities to bene?t legally from wildlife, and to create incentives for people to desist from illegal bushmeat hunting. These drivers are exacerbated by inadequate wildlife laws and enforcement and in some areas, political instability. Urgent efforts are needed to address these drivers and raise awareness among local and international governments of the seriousness of the threat. Failure to address this will result in severe wildlife declines widely in African savannas, with signi?cant ecological, economic and social impacts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.12.020","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"80-96","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas: Impacts, drivers, and possible solutions","title-short":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320712005186","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter Andrew"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen"},{"family":"Bento","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Bocchino","given":"Clara"},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Diggle","given":"Richard W."},{"family":"Eves","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Marnewick","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Mattheus","given":"Jaco"},{"family":"Weldon McNutt","given":"J."},{"family":"McRobb","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Milanzi","given":"James"},{"family":"Morley","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Opyene","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Phadima","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Rentsch","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Roche","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Westhuizen","given":"Hugo","dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie Van"},{"family":"Zisadza-Gandiwa","given":"Patience"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Hunting quota are often too high or insufficiently taking into account population dynamics, especially if populations are declining and abundances are low ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uDRHvhZh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Packer, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Packer, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31114,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/42KWXJKI"],"itemData":{"id":31114,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Sport hunting has provided important economic incentives for conserving large predators since the early 1970's, but wildlife managers also face substantial pressure to reduce depredation. Sport hunting is an inherently risky strategy for controlling predators as carnivore populations are difficult to monitor and some species show a propensity for infanticide that is exacerbated by removing adult males. Simulation models predict population declines from even moderate levels of hunting in infanticidal species, and harvest data suggest that African countries and U.S. states with the highest intensity of sport hunting have shown the steepest population declines in African lions and cougars over the past 25 yrs. Similar effects in African leopards may have been masked by mesopredator release owing to declines in sympatric lion populations, whereas there is no evidence of overhunting in non-infanticidal populations of American black bears. Effective conservation of these animals will require new harvest strategies and improved monitoring to counter demands for predator control by livestock producers and local communities.","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0005941","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"PLOS ONE","language":"en","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e5941","source":"PLoS Journals","title":"Sport Hunting, Predator Control and Conservation of Large Carnivores","URL":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0005941","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Packer","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Kosmala","given":"Margaret"},{"family":"Cooley","given":"Hilary S."},{"family":"Brink","given":"Henry"},{"family":"Pintea","given":"Lilian"},{"family":"Garshelis","given":"David"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Strauss","given":"Megan"},{"family":"Swanson","given":"Alexandra"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Hunter","given":"Luke"},{"family":"Nowell","given":"Kristin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",6,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Examples include African lions and leopards in Tanzania ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1Mif6K4X","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Packer, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Packer, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31114,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/42KWXJKI"],"itemData":{"id":31114,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Sport hunting has provided important economic incentives for conserving large predators since the early 1970's, but wildlife managers also face substantial pressure to reduce depredation. Sport hunting is an inherently risky strategy for controlling predators as carnivore populations are difficult to monitor and some species show a propensity for infanticide that is exacerbated by removing adult males. Simulation models predict population declines from even moderate levels of hunting in infanticidal species, and harvest data suggest that African countries and U.S. states with the highest intensity of sport hunting have shown the steepest population declines in African lions and cougars over the past 25 yrs. Similar effects in African leopards may have been masked by mesopredator release owing to declines in sympatric lion populations, whereas there is no evidence of overhunting in non-infanticidal populations of American black bears. Effective conservation of these animals will require new harvest strategies and improved monitoring to counter demands for predator control by livestock producers and local communities.","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0005941","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"6","journalAbbreviation":"PLOS ONE","language":"en","note":"publisher: Public Library of Science","page":"e5941","source":"PLoS Journals","title":"Sport Hunting, Predator Control and Conservation of Large Carnivores","URL":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0005941","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Packer","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Kosmala","given":"Margaret"},{"family":"Cooley","given":"Hilary S."},{"family":"Brink","given":"Henry"},{"family":"Pintea","given":"Lilian"},{"family":"Garshelis","given":"David"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Strauss","given":"Megan"},{"family":"Swanson","given":"Alexandra"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Hunter","given":"Luke"},{"family":"Nowell","given":"Kristin"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009",6,17]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, lions in Zimbabwe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"spcKSTyS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Loveridge, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Loveridge, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4345,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7SGHGI5M"],"itemData":{"id":4345,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Between 1999 and 2004 we undertook an ecological study of African lions (Panthera leo) in Hwange National Park, western Zimbabwe to measure the impact of sport-hunting beyond the park on the lion population within the park, using radio-telemetry and direct observation. 34 of 62 tagged lions died during the study (of which 24 were shot by sport hunters: 13 adult males, 5 adult females, 6 sub-adult males). Sport hunters in the safari areas surrounding the park killed 72% of tagged adult males from the study area. Over 30% of all males shot were sub-adult (\\\\textless4 years). Hunting off-take of male lions doubled during 2001–2003 compared to levels in the three preceding years, which caused a decline in numbers of adult males in the population (from an adult sex ratio of 1:3 to 1:6 in favour of adult females). Home ranges made vacant by removal of adult males were filled by immigration of males from the park core. Infanticide was observed when new males entered prides. The proportion of male cubs increased between 1999 and 2004, which may have occurred to compensate for high adult male mortality.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2006.09.010","ISSN":"0006-3207","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"548–558","title":"The impact of sport-hunting on the population dynamics of an African lion population in a protected area","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006320706003843","volume":"134","author":[{"family":"Loveridge","given":"A. J."},{"family":"Searle","given":"A. W."},{"family":"Murindagomo","given":"F."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"D. W."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, leopards in several African countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XPpGUrCh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Trouwborst, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Trouwborst, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6840,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3PUZ7M2Q"],"itemData":{"id":6840,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract.  Leopard (Panthera pardus) conservation has a strong international dimension. Hunting trophy export quotas established for African range states under","container-title":"Journal of Environmental Law","DOI":"10.1093/jel/eqz032","ISSN":"0952-8873","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"J Environmental Law","language":"en","note":"publisher: Oxford Academic","page":"253-278","source":"academic.oup.com","title":"Spotty Data: Managing International Leopard (Panthera pardus) Trophy Hunting Quotas Amidst Uncertainty","title-short":"Spotty Data","URL":"https://academic.oup.com/jel/article/32/2/253/5673585","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Trouwborst","given":"Arie"},{"family":"Loveridge","given":"Andrew J."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and elephants across parts of Southern Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KB4o1wKd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Selier, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Selier, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":15986,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A8E7DP9J"],"itemData":{"id":15986,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","DOI":"10.1002/jwmg.641","ISSN":"0022-541X","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Wiley\\ntype: journal-article","page":"122–132","title":"Sustainability of elephant hunting across international borders in southern Africa: A case study of the greater Mapungubwe Transfrontier Conservation Area","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1002/jwmg.641","volume":"78","author":[{"family":"Selier","given":"Sarah-Anne Jeanetta"},{"family":"Page","given":"Bruce R."},{"family":"Vanak","given":"Abi Tamim"},{"family":"Slotow","given":"Rob"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Differences in ecosystem productivity may call for a variable and more selective hunting pattern, while quotas are often constant over time and unselective ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3nHgXGc8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Muposhi, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Muposhi, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34000,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EKWWSBBV"],"itemData":{"id":34000,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Not Available","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0164429","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","page":"e0164429","source":"NASA ADS","title":"Trophy Hunting and Sustainability: Temporal Dynamics in Trophy Quality and Harvesting Patterns of Wild Herbivores in a Tropical Semi-Arid Savanna Ecosystem","title-short":"Trophy Hunting and Sustainability","URL":"http://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2016PLoSO..1164429M","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Muposhi","given":"Victor K."},{"family":"Gandiwa","given":"Edson"},{"family":"Bartels","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Makuza","given":"Stanley M."},{"family":"Madiri","given":"Tinaapi H."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",10,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Also, several countries have a system of ‘fixed’ quota, where the operators are charged for a quota, regardless of whether animals are actually hunted. Such a system is likely to encourage less selective hunting and a higher probability of killing an animal upon sighting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"0vNLbP7J","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Diekert, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Diekert, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":29979,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NYCL5742"],"itemData":{"id":29979,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Hunting is the predominant way of controlling many wildlife populations devoid of large carnivores. It subjects animals to mortality rates that far exceed natural rates and that differ markedly in which age, sex, or size classes are removed relative to those of natural predators. To explain the emerging selection pattern we develop behavioral microfoundations for a hunting model, emphasizing in particular the constraints given by the formal and informal norms, rules, and regulations that govern the hunter’s choice. We show how a shorter remaining season, competition among hunters, lower sighting probabilities, and higher costs all lead to lower reservation values, i.e., an increased likelihood of shooting a particular animal. Using a unique dataset on seen and shot deer from Norway, we test and confirm the theoretical predictions in a recreational and meat-motivated hunting system. To achieve sustainability, future wildlife management should account for this predictable selection pressure.","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","ISSN":"0027-8424","issue":"50","note":"publisher: National Academy of Sciences","page":"14450-14455","source":"JSTOR","title":"How constraints affect the hunter’s decision to shoot a deer","URL":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/26472858","volume":"113","author":[{"family":"Diekert","given":"Florian K."},{"family":"Richter","given":"Andries"},{"family":"Rivrud","given":"Inger Maren"},{"family":"Mysterud","given":"Atle"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
	African lions, Panthera leo attract the highest mean price for all tropic species and are estimated to generate 5-17% of gross trophy hunting income ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7hrYW8a9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Lindsey, 2012)","plainCitation":"(P. Lindsey, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":207,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E2ANYP6S"],"itemData":{"id":207,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Recent studies indicate that trophy hunting is impacting negatively on some lion populations, notably in Tanzania. In 2004 there was a proposal to list lions on CITES Appendix I and in 2011 animal-welfare groups petitioned the United States government to list lions as endangered under their Endangered Species Act. Such listings would likely curtail the trophy hunting of lions by limiting the import of lion trophies. Concurrent efforts are underway to encourage the European Union to ban lion trophy imports. We assessed the significance of lions to the financial viability of trophy hunting across five countries to help determine the financial impact and advisability of the proposed trade restrictions. Lion hunts attract the highest mean prices (US$24,000–US$71,000) of all trophy species. Lions generate 5–17% of gross trophy hunting income on national levels, the proportional significance highest in Mozambique, Tanzania, and Zambia. If lion hunting was effectively precluded, trophy hunting could potentially become financially unviable across at least 59,538 km2 that could result in a concomitant loss of habitat. However, the loss of lion hunting could have other potentially broader negative impacts including reduction of competitiveness of wildlife-based land uses relative to ecologically unfavourable alternatives. Restrictions on lion hunting may also reduce tolerance for the species among communities where local people benefit from trophy hunting, and may reduce funds available for anti-poaching. If lion off-takes were reduced to recommended maximums (0.5/1000 km2), the loss of viability and reduction in profitability would be much lower than if lion hunting was stopped altogether (7,005 km2). We recommend that interventions focus on reducing off-takes to sustainable levels, implementing age-based regulations and improving governance of trophy hunting. Such measures could ensure sustainability, while retaining incentives for the conservation of lions and their habitat from hunting.","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0029332","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e29332","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Significance of African Lions for the Financial Viability of Trophy Hunting and the Maintenance of Wild Land","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0029332","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"P."},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy Andrew"},{"family":"Booth","given":"Vernon Richard"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"}],"editor":[{"family":"Fenton","given":"Brock"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",1,11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At the same time, hunting pressure has declined the abundance of lion populations with the most severe contraction occurring in West Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bHNSztlX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013; Packer, 2011)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013; Packer, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32727,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/53HGBIYN"],"itemData":{"id":32727,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Abstract:Tanzania holds most of the remaining large populations of African lions (Panthera leo) and has extensive areas of leopard habitat (Panthera pardus), and both species are subjected to sizable harvests by sport hunters. As a first step toward establishing sustainable management strategies, we","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2010.01576.x","ISSN":"0888-8892","issue":"1","language":"ENGLISH","note":"PMID: 20825444","page":"142-153","source":"insights.ovid.com","title":"Effects of Trophy Hunting on Lion and Leopard Populations in Tanzania","URL":"insights.ovid.com","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Packer","given":"C."},{"family":"Brink","given":"H."},{"family":"Kissui","given":"B."},{"family":"Maliti","given":"H."},{"family":"Kushnir","given":"H."},{"family":"Caro","given":"T."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011",2]]}}},{"id":32740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W43CISEZ"],"itemData":{"id":32740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The bushmeat trade, or the illegal acquisition and exchange of wild meat, has long been recognised as a severe problem in forest biomes, but receives little attention in savannas, perhaps due to a misconception that bushmeat hunting is a low-impact subsistence activity. Though data on impacts are scarce, indications are that bushmeat hunting is a widespread problem in savannas, with severe impacts on wildlife populations and wildlife-based land uses. The impacts of the bushmeat trade in savannas vary from edge-effects around protected areas, to disproportionate declines of some species, to severe wildlife declines in areas with inadequate anti-poaching. In some areas, bushmeat contributes signi?cantly to food security, but these bene?ts are unsustainable, and hunting is wasteful, utilising a fraction of the wildlife killed or of its ?nancial value obtainable through tourism, trophy hunting and/or legal game meat production. The bushmeat trade appears to be becoming increasingly commercialised due to elevated demand in rural areas, urban centres and even overseas cities. Other drivers for the trade include human encroachment of wildlife areas; poverty and food insecurity; and inadequate legal frameworks to enable communities to bene?t legally from wildlife, and to create incentives for people to desist from illegal bushmeat hunting. These drivers are exacerbated by inadequate wildlife laws and enforcement and in some areas, political instability. Urgent efforts are needed to address these drivers and raise awareness among local and international governments of the seriousness of the threat. Failure to address this will result in severe wildlife declines widely in African savannas, with signi?cant ecological, economic and social impacts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.12.020","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"80-96","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas: Impacts, drivers, and possible solutions","title-short":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320712005186","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter Andrew"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen"},{"family":"Bento","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Bocchino","given":"Clara"},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Diggle","given":"Richard W."},{"family":"Eves","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Marnewick","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Mattheus","given":"Jaco"},{"family":"Weldon McNutt","given":"J."},{"family":"McRobb","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Milanzi","given":"James"},{"family":"Morley","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Opyene","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Phadima","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Rentsch","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Roche","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Westhuizen","given":"Hugo","dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie Van"},{"family":"Zisadza-Gandiwa","given":"Patience"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A key element of sustainable use is to incentivize users to hunt more selectively, e.g. by sparing younger animals. In Niassa National Reserve, Mozambique, a system was introduced to incentivize hunters to select older individuals, reducing overall harvesting pressure to sustainable limits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UT0d0i4Y","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Begg, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Begg, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"Uebb2sCb/SYRTzEVp","uris":["http://zotero.org/users/local/L7afBCgj/items/V6GSQKHX"],"itemData":{"id":2047,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Sport hunting of wildlife can play a role in conservation but can also drive population declines if not managed sustainably. Previous simulation modelling found that large felid species could theoretically be hunted sustainably by restricting harvests to older individuals that have likely reproduced. Several African countries currently use age?based hunting for lions although the outcomes have yet to be evaluated in a wild population. Here we provide the first empirical evidence that a system of incentives sufficiently encouraged age?based hunting and reduced offtake of a wild felid, thereby reducing the potential risk of unsustainable hunting on a threatened species. We examined long?term hunting data and the lion population trend in Niassa National Reserve, Mozambique. To incentivise hunter compliance, a “points” system was developed, which rewards operators that harvest lions older than the 6?year minimum trophy age recommended for sustainable hunting and penalises operators that hunt “underage” lions (\\\\textless4 years). A key component of this system is the ecological application of key physical traits that predictably change with age in order to estimate (by hunters) and validate (by authorities) trophy individuals’ ages pre? and post?mortem, respectively. Analysis of 138 lion hunts and 87 lion trophies from 2003 to 2015 revealed that after enforcement of age restrictions in 2006, hunters shifted harvests to suitably aged lions (\\\\textgreater6 years), from 25% of offtakes in 2004 to 100% by 2014. Simultaneously, the number of lions and percentage of quota harvested decreased, resulting in lower lion offtakes. Following an initial decrease after enforcement of the ageing system, the percentage of hunts harvesting lions stabilised, demonstrating that hunters successfully located and aged older lions. Synthesis and applications. Evidence suggests that age restrictions combined with an incentive?based points system regulated sport hunting and reduced pressure on the lion population. We attribute the successful implementation of this management system to: (1) committed, consistent enforcement by management authorities, (2) genuine involvement of all stakeholders from the start, (3) annual auditing by an independent third party, (4) the reliable, transparent, straightforward ageing process and (5) the simple, pragmatic points system for incentivising hunter compliance. Our study demonstrates that the use of age restrictions can increase the selectivity of sport hunting and lower trophy offtakes to reduce the possibility of unsustainable sport hunting negatively impacting species populations in the absence of reliable estimates of population size. It must be noted, however, that there was no measurable change in the lion numbers over the past decade that could be attributed to the implementation of this policy alone.","ISSN":"0021-8901","language":"en","title":"Effective implementation of age restrictions increases selectivity of sport hunting of the African lion","URL":"https://pubag.nal.usda.gov/catalog/5871979","author":[{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen M."},{"family":"Miller","given":"Jennifer R. B."},{"family":"Begg","given":"Keith S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, the way hunting concessions are handed out affects sustainable use. Usually, hunting blocks are allocated with a tender process with limited benefits for local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2VJXRS7t","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W43CISEZ"],"itemData":{"id":32740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The bushmeat trade, or the illegal acquisition and exchange of wild meat, has long been recognised as a severe problem in forest biomes, but receives little attention in savannas, perhaps due to a misconception that bushmeat hunting is a low-impact subsistence activity. Though data on impacts are scarce, indications are that bushmeat hunting is a widespread problem in savannas, with severe impacts on wildlife populations and wildlife-based land uses. The impacts of the bushmeat trade in savannas vary from edge-effects around protected areas, to disproportionate declines of some species, to severe wildlife declines in areas with inadequate anti-poaching. In some areas, bushmeat contributes signi?cantly to food security, but these bene?ts are unsustainable, and hunting is wasteful, utilising a fraction of the wildlife killed or of its ?nancial value obtainable through tourism, trophy hunting and/or legal game meat production. The bushmeat trade appears to be becoming increasingly commercialised due to elevated demand in rural areas, urban centres and even overseas cities. Other drivers for the trade include human encroachment of wildlife areas; poverty and food insecurity; and inadequate legal frameworks to enable communities to bene?t legally from wildlife, and to create incentives for people to desist from illegal bushmeat hunting. These drivers are exacerbated by inadequate wildlife laws and enforcement and in some areas, political instability. Urgent efforts are needed to address these drivers and raise awareness among local and international governments of the seriousness of the threat. Failure to address this will result in severe wildlife declines widely in African savannas, with signi?cant ecological, economic and social impacts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.12.020","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"80-96","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas: Impacts, drivers, and possible solutions","title-short":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320712005186","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter Andrew"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen"},{"family":"Bento","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Bocchino","given":"Clara"},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Diggle","given":"Richard W."},{"family":"Eves","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Marnewick","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Mattheus","given":"Jaco"},{"family":"Weldon McNutt","given":"J."},{"family":"McRobb","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Milanzi","given":"James"},{"family":"Morley","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Opyene","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Phadima","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Rentsch","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Roche","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Westhuizen","given":"Hugo","dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie Van"},{"family":"Zisadza-Gandiwa","given":"Patience"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An exception is Namibia, where user rights were given to communities, resulting in larger local benefits and also increases in wild species populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gRwE2GsQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32740,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W43CISEZ"],"itemData":{"id":32740,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The bushmeat trade, or the illegal acquisition and exchange of wild meat, has long been recognised as a severe problem in forest biomes, but receives little attention in savannas, perhaps due to a misconception that bushmeat hunting is a low-impact subsistence activity. Though data on impacts are scarce, indications are that bushmeat hunting is a widespread problem in savannas, with severe impacts on wildlife populations and wildlife-based land uses. The impacts of the bushmeat trade in savannas vary from edge-effects around protected areas, to disproportionate declines of some species, to severe wildlife declines in areas with inadequate anti-poaching. In some areas, bushmeat contributes signi?cantly to food security, but these bene?ts are unsustainable, and hunting is wasteful, utilising a fraction of the wildlife killed or of its ?nancial value obtainable through tourism, trophy hunting and/or legal game meat production. The bushmeat trade appears to be becoming increasingly commercialised due to elevated demand in rural areas, urban centres and even overseas cities. Other drivers for the trade include human encroachment of wildlife areas; poverty and food insecurity; and inadequate legal frameworks to enable communities to bene?t legally from wildlife, and to create incentives for people to desist from illegal bushmeat hunting. These drivers are exacerbated by inadequate wildlife laws and enforcement and in some areas, political instability. Urgent efforts are needed to address these drivers and raise awareness among local and international governments of the seriousness of the threat. Failure to address this will result in severe wildlife declines widely in African savannas, with signi?cant ecological, economic and social impacts.","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2012.12.020","ISSN":"00063207","journalAbbreviation":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"80-96","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas: Impacts, drivers, and possible solutions","title-short":"The bushmeat trade in African savannas","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0006320712005186","volume":"160","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter Andrew"},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Matthew"},{"family":"Begg","given":"Colleen"},{"family":"Bento","given":"Carlos"},{"family":"Bocchino","given":"Clara"},{"family":"Dickman","given":"Amy"},{"family":"Diggle","given":"Richard W."},{"family":"Eves","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp"},{"family":"Lewis","given":"Dale"},{"family":"Marnewick","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Mattheus","given":"Jaco"},{"family":"Weldon McNutt","given":"J."},{"family":"McRobb","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Milanzi","given":"James"},{"family":"Morley","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Michael"},{"family":"Opyene","given":"Vincent"},{"family":"Phadima","given":"Joe"},{"family":"Purchase","given":"Gianetta"},{"family":"Rentsch","given":"Dennis"},{"family":"Roche","given":"Christopher"},{"family":"Shaw","given":"Joanne"},{"family":"Westhuizen","given":"Hugo","dropping-particle":"van der"},{"family":"Vliet","given":"Nathalie Van"},{"family":"Zisadza-Gandiwa","given":"Patience"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",4]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Tanzania, hunting concessions are leased in block to companies. Shorter leased blocks tend to generate higher revenues for the government ($133 per km2)  compared to $62 per km2 from long-term tenure blocks. However, long-term blocks had a significantly lower hunting offtake, which were also closer to the sustainable limits ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VsMm828k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brink, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Brink, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":31429,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/D9CNF7J3"],"itemData":{"id":31429,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"It is argued that trophy hunting of large, charismatic mammal species can have considerable conservation benefits but only if undertaken sustainably. Social-ecological theory suggests such sustainability only results from developing governance systems that balance financial and biological requirements. Here we use lion (Panthera leo) trophy hunting data from Tanzania to investigate how resource ownership patterns influence hunting revenue and offtake levels. Tanzania contains up to half of the global population of free-ranging lions and is also the main location for lion trophy hunting in Africa. However, there are concerns that current hunting levels are unsustainable. The lion hunting industry in Tanzania is run by the private sector, although the government leases each hunting block to companies, enforces hunting regulation, and allocates them a species-specific annual quota per block. The length of these leases varies and theories surrounding property rights and tenure suggest hunting levels would be less sustainable in blocks experiencing a high turnover of short-term leases. We explored this issue using lion data collected from 1996 to 2008 in the Selous Game Reserve (SGR), the most important trophy hunting destination in Tanzania. We found that blocks in SGR with the highest lion hunting offtake were also those that experienced the steepest declines in trophy offtake. In addition, we found this high hunting offtake and the resultant offtake decline tended to be in blocks under short-term tenure. In contrast, lion hunting levels in blocks under long-term tenure matched more closely the recommended sustainable offtake of 0.92 lions per 1000 km2. However, annual financial returns were higher from blocks under short-term tenure, providing $133 per km2 of government revenue as compared to $62 per km2 from long-term tenure blocks. Our results provide evidence for the importance of property rights in conservation, and support calls for an overhaul of the system in Tanzania by developing competitive market-based approaches for block allocation based on long-term tenure of ten years.","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0162610","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS One","note":"PMID: 27648566\\nPMCID: PMC5029936","source":"PubMed Central","title":"Sustainability and Long Term-Tenure: Lion Trophy Hunting in Tanzania","title-short":"Sustainability and Long Term-Tenure","URL":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5029936/","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Brink","given":"Henry"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Robert J."},{"family":"Skinner","given":"Kirsten"},{"family":"Leader-Williams","given":"Nigel"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",9,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
	Also, if monitoring, quotas, and age-based harvesting are difficult to enforce, a full moratorium or a complete ban may be a good option to ensure sustainability ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WrIxCRdm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mweetwa, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Mweetwa, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":13820,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KWXNUJ6G"],"itemData":{"id":13820,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PLOS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0197030","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e0197030","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Quantifying lion (Panthera leo) demographic response following a three-year moratorium on trophy hunting","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0197030","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Mweetwa","given":"Thandiwe"},{"family":"Christianson","given":"David"},{"family":"Becker","given":"Matt"},{"family":"Creel","given":"Scott"},{"family":"Rosenblatt","given":"Elias"},{"family":"Merkle","given":"Johnathan"},{"family":"Dröge","given":"Egil"},{"family":"Mwape","given":"Henry"},{"family":"Masonde","given":"Jones"},{"family":"Simpamba","given":"Twakundine"}],"editor":[{"family":"Festa-Bianchet","given":"Marco"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",5,21]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Finally, while conservation and management are often done on a single species level, there may be important interactions when it comes to the financial viability of trophy hunting  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SFywrFPG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Lindsey, 2012)","plainCitation":"(P. Lindsey, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":207,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E2ANYP6S"],"itemData":{"id":207,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Recent studies indicate that trophy hunting is impacting negatively on some lion populations, notably in Tanzania. In 2004 there was a proposal to list lions on CITES Appendix I and in 2011 animal-welfare groups petitioned the United States government to list lions as endangered under their Endangered Species Act. Such listings would likely curtail the trophy hunting of lions by limiting the import of lion trophies. Concurrent efforts are underway to encourage the European Union to ban lion trophy imports. We assessed the significance of lions to the financial viability of trophy hunting across five countries to help determine the financial impact and advisability of the proposed trade restrictions. Lion hunts attract the highest mean prices (US$24,000–US$71,000) of all trophy species. Lions generate 5–17% of gross trophy hunting income on national levels, the proportional significance highest in Mozambique, Tanzania, and Zambia. If lion hunting was effectively precluded, trophy hunting could potentially become financially unviable across at least 59,538 km2 that could result in a concomitant loss of habitat. However, the loss of lion hunting could have other potentially broader negative impacts including reduction of competitiveness of wildlife-based land uses relative to ecologically unfavourable alternatives. Restrictions on lion hunting may also reduce tolerance for the species among communities where local people benefit from trophy hunting, and may reduce funds available for anti-poaching. If lion off-takes were reduced to recommended maximums (0.5/1000 km2), the loss of viability and reduction in profitability would be much lower than if lion hunting was stopped altogether (7,005 km2). We recommend that interventions focus on reducing off-takes to sustainable levels, implementing age-based regulations and improving governance of trophy hunting. Such measures could ensure sustainability, while retaining incentives for the conservation of lions and their habitat from hunting.","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0029332","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e29332","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Significance of African Lions for the Financial Viability of Trophy Hunting and the Maintenance of Wild Land","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0029332","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"P."},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy Andrew"},{"family":"Booth","given":"Vernon Richard"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"}],"editor":[{"family":"Fenton","given":"Brock"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",1,11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Banning hunting of single species, for example lions, may imply that trophy hunting as a whole may become unviable. As a result, local communities that depend on trophy hunting for income, as well as funding for anti-poaching activities, may be adversely affected, having repercussions on sustainable use of wild species  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jV4pPSJj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Lindsey, 2012)","plainCitation":"(P. Lindsey, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":207,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E2ANYP6S"],"itemData":{"id":207,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Recent studies indicate that trophy hunting is impacting negatively on some lion populations, notably in Tanzania. In 2004 there was a proposal to list lions on CITES Appendix I and in 2011 animal-welfare groups petitioned the United States government to list lions as endangered under their Endangered Species Act. Such listings would likely curtail the trophy hunting of lions by limiting the import of lion trophies. Concurrent efforts are underway to encourage the European Union to ban lion trophy imports. We assessed the significance of lions to the financial viability of trophy hunting across five countries to help determine the financial impact and advisability of the proposed trade restrictions. Lion hunts attract the highest mean prices (US$24,000–US$71,000) of all trophy species. Lions generate 5–17% of gross trophy hunting income on national levels, the proportional significance highest in Mozambique, Tanzania, and Zambia. If lion hunting was effectively precluded, trophy hunting could potentially become financially unviable across at least 59,538 km2 that could result in a concomitant loss of habitat. However, the loss of lion hunting could have other potentially broader negative impacts including reduction of competitiveness of wildlife-based land uses relative to ecologically unfavourable alternatives. Restrictions on lion hunting may also reduce tolerance for the species among communities where local people benefit from trophy hunting, and may reduce funds available for anti-poaching. If lion off-takes were reduced to recommended maximums (0.5/1000 km2), the loss of viability and reduction in profitability would be much lower than if lion hunting was stopped altogether (7,005 km2). We recommend that interventions focus on reducing off-takes to sustainable levels, implementing age-based regulations and improving governance of trophy hunting. Such measures could ensure sustainability, while retaining incentives for the conservation of lions and their habitat from hunting.","container-title":"PLoS ONE","DOI":"10.1371/journal.pone.0029332","ISSN":"1932-6203","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"PLoS ONE","language":"en","page":"e29332","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Significance of African Lions for the Financial Viability of Trophy Hunting and the Maintenance of Wild Land","URL":"https://dx.plos.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0029332","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"P."},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy Andrew"},{"family":"Booth","given":"Vernon Richard"},{"family":"Midlane","given":"Neil"}],"editor":[{"family":"Fenton","given":"Brock"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",1,11]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
	Sportfisheries create income and hold the potential to provide alternative and diversified livelihoods for coastal villages ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3qmxjNPD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barnett, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Barnett, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6863,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8MHYJNIR"],"itemData":{"id":6863,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ecotourism ventures in developing countries are often among the few alternatives for enhancing sustainable livelihoods without altering traditional ways of life. The best way forward is to continually develop and implement best practice guidelines and, in particular, to flexibly develop them to suit individual cases. We conduct a multidisciplinary assessment of best practice guidelines required to develop and sustain sportfishing tourism in developing countries, while enhancing local livelihoods and promoting environmental stewardship. In general, best practice guidelines should be developed around a sustainable livelihood framework that includes short-term coping mechanisms and longer-term capacity building. Sportfishing development that conforms to ecological and socially orientated criteria, founded on site-specific research that captures local environmental and social complexities, has the potential to provide mutual benefits to tourists and local people, fuelling community development and enhancing the cultural experience of tourists. Best practice guidelines for sportfishing that do not address these dimensions are unlikely to result in a viable industry. Given the current interest and growth of sportfishing in developing countries, the proposed guidelines can help a range of end users manage, conserve and maximize livelihood benefits from their fishery.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12140","ISSN":"1467-2979","issue":"3","language":"en","license":"© 2015 John Wiley & Sons Ltd","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/faf.12140","page":"696-713","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Sportfisheries, conservation and sustainable livelihoods: a multidisciplinary guide to developing best practice","title-short":"Sportfisheries, conservation and sustainable livelihoods","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/faf.12140","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Barnett","given":"Adam"},{"family":"Abrantes","given":"Kátya G."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Ronald"},{"family":"Diedrich","given":"Amy S."},{"family":"Farr","given":"Marina"},{"family":"Kuilboer","given":"Alf"},{"family":"Mahony","given":"Tracey"},{"family":"McLeod","given":"Ian"},{"family":"Moscardo","given":"Gianna"},{"family":"Prideaux","given":"Murray"},{"family":"Stoeckl","given":"Natalie"},{"family":"Luyn","given":"Ariella","dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Sheaves","given":"Marcus"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At the same time, they can generate significant environmental benefits by creating incentives to conserve targeted species and their key habitats. However, a truly sustainable sportfishery in the developing world should produce benefits for, and be supported by, local people ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Bf2N23i0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barnett, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Barnett, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6863,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8MHYJNIR"],"itemData":{"id":6863,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ecotourism ventures in developing countries are often among the few alternatives for enhancing sustainable livelihoods without altering traditional ways of life. The best way forward is to continually develop and implement best practice guidelines and, in particular, to flexibly develop them to suit individual cases. We conduct a multidisciplinary assessment of best practice guidelines required to develop and sustain sportfishing tourism in developing countries, while enhancing local livelihoods and promoting environmental stewardship. In general, best practice guidelines should be developed around a sustainable livelihood framework that includes short-term coping mechanisms and longer-term capacity building. Sportfishing development that conforms to ecological and socially orientated criteria, founded on site-specific research that captures local environmental and social complexities, has the potential to provide mutual benefits to tourists and local people, fuelling community development and enhancing the cultural experience of tourists. Best practice guidelines for sportfishing that do not address these dimensions are unlikely to result in a viable industry. Given the current interest and growth of sportfishing in developing countries, the proposed guidelines can help a range of end users manage, conserve and maximize livelihood benefits from their fishery.","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1111/faf.12140","ISSN":"1467-2979","issue":"3","language":"en","license":"© 2015 John Wiley & Sons Ltd","note":"_eprint: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/faf.12140","page":"696-713","source":"Wiley Online Library","title":"Sportfisheries, conservation and sustainable livelihoods: a multidisciplinary guide to developing best practice","title-short":"Sportfisheries, conservation and sustainable livelihoods","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/faf.12140","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Barnett","given":"Adam"},{"family":"Abrantes","given":"Kátya G."},{"family":"Baker","given":"Ronald"},{"family":"Diedrich","given":"Amy S."},{"family":"Farr","given":"Marina"},{"family":"Kuilboer","given":"Alf"},{"family":"Mahony","given":"Tracey"},{"family":"McLeod","given":"Ian"},{"family":"Moscardo","given":"Gianna"},{"family":"Prideaux","given":"Murray"},{"family":"Stoeckl","given":"Natalie"},{"family":"Luyn","given":"Ariella","dropping-particle":"van"},{"family":"Sheaves","given":"Marcus"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Potential conflicts may arise if historically important fishing spots for locals are used as tourism sites, creating a conflict over fishing space. One example comes from the Alligator Rivers Region of Kakadu National Park in Northern Territory of Australia where Aboriginal people indicated that tourist activity limited access to their favorite hunting and fishing sites and aggravated fishing pressure, leading to population decline ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Lv4OsKHf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ligtermoet, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Ligtermoet, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6855,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E6Z8ADRN"],"itemData":{"id":6855,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Freshwater resources underpin multiple livelihood systems around the world, particularly in highly productive tropical floodplain regions. Sustaining Indigenous people’s access to freshwater resources for customary harvesting, while developing alternative livelihood strategies can be challenging. The sustainable livelihoods approach was applied to examine the ways in which multiple livelihoods in the East Alligator River floodplain region in northern Australia influence Aboriginal people’s access to freshwater resources for customary harvesting. Interviews with Aboriginal residents were conducted to understand changes to freshwater customary harvesting practices. The dominant floodplain-based livelihoods analysed were pastoralism, biodiversity conservation and tourism and they were found to generate both opportunities and constraints for sustaining freshwater customary harvesting. Opportunities were provided through facilitating regular access to floodplain country and opportunistic access for harvesting, which assists in sustaining bio-cultural knowledge. Partnerships developed through these mainstream livelihoods built human capacity that enhanced all livelihood resource capitals (natural, human, social, financial and physical). Three key ways the dominant livelihoods constrained access to key freshwater resources were identified. Tourism required sacrificing certain hunting places and had to accommodate recreational fishing pressure. The successful recovery of the saltwater crocodile population through biodiversity conservation policy has inadvertently reduced people’s customary access to in-stream resources. Pastoralism on the floodplain had restricted traditional floodplain burning practices associated with accessing aestivating long-necked turtles, affecting access and abundance. These findings highlight the need for the development of remote Indigenous livelihood strategies to make explicit their influences on freshwater customary harvesting practices, to better support their maintenance amongst multiple, non-customary floodplain livelihoods.","container-title":"Reviews in Fish Biology and Fisheries","DOI":"10.1007/s11160-016-9429-y","ISSN":"1573-5184","issue":"4","journalAbbreviation":"Rev Fish Biol Fisheries","language":"en","page":"649-678","source":"Springer Link","title":"Maintaining customary harvesting of freshwater resources: sustainable Indigenous livelihoods in the floodplains of northern Australia","title-short":"Maintaining customary harvesting of freshwater resources","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11160-016-9429-y","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Ligtermoet","given":"Emma"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",12,1]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
4.2.4.5 Consumer values, behaviours, choices  
Sustainable use of wild species implies that consumption takes place within sustainable limits. Therefore, overconsumption can be considered one of the key factors undermining or preventing a regime of sustainability. Consumption of wild species varies by country, socio-economic and demographic factors as well as by species. To understand the trends in the use of wild species, thus requires consideration of other economic and sociocultural trends. By virtually any measure—household expenditures, number of consumers, extraction of raw materials—consumption of goods and services have risen steadily in industrial nations for decades, and it is growing rapidly in many developing countries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KhpnUb43","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gardner, 2004; UNCTAD, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Gardner, 2004; UNCTAD, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5291,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YUL2WEKK"],"itemData":{"id":5291,"type":"chapter","container-title":"State of the World","language":"en","page":"28","title":"The State of Consumption Today","author":[{"family":"Gardner","given":"G."},{"family":"Assadourian","given":"E."},{"family":"Sarin","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":32971,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XH7878X2"],"itemData":{"id":32971,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Geneva, Switzerland","title":"Towards more balanced growth strategies in developing countries: issues related to market size, trade balances and purchasing power","author":[{"family":"UNCTAD","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",12]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Consumption is one of the determining factors of global impacts, surpassing other socio-economic-demographic factors, such as age, household size, qualification or dwelling structure ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"O6iDFLcj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wiedmann, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Wiedmann, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6896,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XDU3DQ89"],"itemData":{"id":6896,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature Communications","DOI":"10.1038/s41467-020-16941-y","ISSN":"2041-1723","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Nat Commun","language":"en","page":"3107","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Scientists’ warning on affluence","URL":"http://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-020-16941-y","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Wiedmann","given":"Thomas"},{"family":"Lenzen","given":"Manfred"},{"family":"Keyßer","given":"Lorenz T."},{"family":"Steinberger","given":"Julia K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",7,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",12]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. A lifestyle and culture that became common in Europe, North America, Japan, and a few other pockets of the world in the twentieth century is going global in the twenty-first century. While the consumer class thrives, great disparities remain. In the beginning of the twenty-first century the 12 % of the world’s population that lives in North America and Western Europe accounted for 60 % of private consumption spending, while the one-third living in South Asia and sub-Saharan Africa accounted for only 3.2 % ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"n3Rfknn5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gardner, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Gardner, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5291,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YUL2WEKK"],"itemData":{"id":5291,"type":"chapter","container-title":"State of the World","language":"en","page":"28","title":"The State of Consumption Today","author":[{"family":"Gardner","given":"G."},{"family":"Assadourian","given":"E."},{"family":"Sarin","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, rapid growth in many of the developing countries over the past decades may well have changed the situation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZstanHEF","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(2019; UNCTAD 2013)","plainCitation":"(2019; UNCTAD 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32980,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/99PAKS6U"],"itemData":{"id":32980,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: New York","title":"World Urbanization Prospects 2018: Highlights. Department of Economic and Social Affairs","author":[{"family":"Nations","given":"United"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"suppress-author":true},{"id":32971,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XH7878X2"],"itemData":{"id":32971,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Geneva, Switzerland","title":"Towards more balanced growth strategies in developing countries: issues related to market size, trade balances and purchasing power","author":[{"family":"UNCTAD","given":""}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013",12]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	In a globalized world and with increasing demand for food and changing consumption patterns the strain on ecosystem and biodiversity is growing substantially. The consumption of goods and services has become a defining characteristic of modern-day (industrial) societies and is accompanied by an enormous and continuously increasing use of natural resources. Consumption also has a significant impact on the provision of ecosystem services worldwide. This section analyzes and illustrates the impacts of consumption patterns on sustainable use of biodiversity. 
      Despite the high level of concern and awareness among the general public about the need for conservation, there have not been considerable changes in personal actions or widespread patterns of behaviour and so individuals in developed nations continue to consume high levels of resources in unsustainable way ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FpERvQtP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schultz, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Schultz, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":8009,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HVRMNE5F"],"itemData":{"id":8009,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Most instances of deteriorating environmental conditions are caused by human behavior. Although there are cer- tainly instances of such environmental conditions devel- oping from natural processes, most are largely the result of human activity. Drivers of phenomena such as climate change, loss of species' habitats, and ocean acidification rarely are the result of malicious intent, but rather the con- sequence of the lifestyles of billions of humans. Accord- ingly, efforts to promote conservation must change be- havior (Ehrlich & Kennedy 2005; Schultz & Kaiser 2012). This fundamental link between conservation and be- havior has been noted in a number of recent publications. Mascia (2003) state that “Biodiversity conservation is a human endeavor: initiated by humans, designed by hu- mans, and intended to modify human behavior....” Cowl- ing (2005) calls this realization “an epiphany for...natural scientists.” And Balmford and Cowling (2006) note that “conservation is primarily not about biology but about people and the choices theymake.” Here I would go one step further and propose that conservation is a goal that can only be achieved by changing behavior.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/j.1523-1739.2011.01766.x","ISSN":"08888892","issue":"6","page":"1080–1083","title":"Conservation Means Behavior","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Schultz","given":"P. Wesley"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The conditions for conservation can only be achieved by changing behaviour. Nevertheless, the driver for this behaviour change is not proportional to increasing knowledge through education, but rather motivation, or a reason for action, such as self-interest, social responsibility, and self-transcendent values.
	Consumer behaviour is related to how individual customers, groups or organizations select, buy, use, and dispose of ideas, goods, and services to satisfy their needs and wants. It refers to the actions of the consumers and their main motivations. Individual, social and situational factors influence this decision process. Concerning social factors, societal norms, cultural context and mass media can influence consumer behavior and choices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tEJQyyYP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Terlau & Hirsch, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Terlau & Hirsch, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":3642,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YUS7WKPJ"],"itemData":{"id":3642,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings in Food System Dynamics","DOI":"10.18461/pfsd.2015.1516.","issue":"0","language":"en","page":"199–214","title":"Sustainable Consumption and the Attitude-Behaviour-Gap Phenomenon - Causes and Measurements towards a Sustainable Development","volume":"0","author":[{"family":"Terlau","given":"Wiltrud"},{"family":"Hirsch","given":"Darya"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the case of experiences of sustainable use of wild fauna that, as a necessary condition for profitability of local communities, require their conservation in order to be successful, it is essential that consumers be able to identify, distinguish and give preference to products that meet the condition of sustainability at the expense of those who do not, thus reducing the development of other productive  activities that generate negative impacts on wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CVKSbOqJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Banchs & Moschione, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Banchs & Moschione, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4244,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/88RBHXD4"],"itemData":{"id":4244,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Manejo de fauna silvestre en la Argentina. Programas de uso sustentable","language":"es","publisher":"Dirección de Fauna Silvestre, Buenos Aires","title":"Proyecto Elé. Para la conservación y el aprovechamiento sustentable del loro hablador (Amazona aestiva) en la Argentina","author":[{"family":"Banchs","given":"R."},{"literal":"Moschione"}],"editor":[{"family":"Bolkovic","given":"M.L."},{"family":"Ramadori","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. An emblematic example is the case of talking parrot (Amazona estiva) in Argentina, where the ban on exporting the animal to the European Union, its main buyer, increased illegal trade and did not have the resources to carry out controls on the management of the parrot, resulting in a genuine loss of income for the local population, a decline in the population of the parrot and the loss of forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"M7B2DLfU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Coconier & Lichtenstein, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Coconier & Lichtenstein, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32505,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8ELU9KEY"],"itemData":{"id":32505,"type":"book","event-place":"Posadas","language":"es","number-of-pages":"107–127","publisher":"Programa de Postgrado en Antropología Social- UNaM","publisher-place":"Posadas","title":"Loros, Gripe Aviar y Soja: Los alcances de Políticas Globales sobre Proyectos Locales. Avá, Revista de Antropología N°24 Edición Especial, jun 2014","author":[{"family":"Coconier","given":"E."},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

4.2.4.5.1 Media and consumer behavior
Media vehicles (press, television, cinema, radio and internet) may have a positive or negative influence on consumer behavior. Considering the influence of the media on consumer behaviour with regard to wild species, there are more cases related to animal species, such as advertising campaigns to reduce consumer demand on wild species in general, or for exotic pet more specifically ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"GQ8noaU8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wallen & Daut, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Wallen & Daut, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6732,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9JXEY7C8"],"itemData":{"id":6732,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Biodiversity conservation is contingent upon managing human behaviour and, at times, changing behaviour. This is particularly relevant to the illegal trade in wildlife and wildlife products, both flora and fauna. Driven by dynamics of consumer demand and illicit supply, mitigation of illegal trade requires a fuller appreciation of human behaviour and methods to change it. In various sectors, social influence, behavioural insights, social marketing and human-centred approaches trend towards mainstream practice and policy application. However, in the context of conservation and wildlife trafficking, these approaches and their usefulness are not well-articulated nor application widespread. Here, we provide a practical overview of relevant behaviour change methods and frameworks. We discuss their usefulness and potential application to mitigating the illegal wildlife trade, in general and consumer demand, in particular.","container-title":"Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.3897/natureconservation.26.22725","ISSN":"1314-6947","page":"55–75","title":"The challenge and opportunity of behaviour change methods and frameworks to reduce demand for illegal wildlife","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Wallen","given":"Kenneth E."},{"family":"Daut","given":"Elizabeth"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. On the other hand, media may also stimulate demand for wild species, by creating awareness about its existence and highlighting positive health benefits of consumption, as in the case of products known as superfoods ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"9bbfqV31","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sikka, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Sikka, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32975,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2SNPFCJM"],"itemData":{"id":32975,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"This article examines the rise in the consumption of superfoods as a normative food trend among affluent groups in the global North that has embedded itself in Western food culture. It is argued that superfoods are a marker of idealized identity that is mobilized using neoliberal, postfeminist, and food justice discourses. The article examines the visual and textual framings of these products as they are implicitly and explicitly taken up on social media. In particular, it examines the material and ideological outcomes of tensions between the binaries of plenty and constraint, “clean” and “dirty” foods, and individual identity and conformity as they are expressed in the visual and textual discourse surrounding foods like goji berries, chia seeds, maca powder, and hemp. Also examined are the effects of a kind of body entrepreneurism that is encouraged by these discourses, which further pathologizes non-conforming bodies and produces, on the part of the consumer, corporal anxiety and a pained relationship with food.","container-title":"Food, Culture & Society","DOI":"10.1080/15528014.2019.1580534","ISSN":"1552-8014","issue":"3","note":"publisher: Routledge\\n_eprint: https://doi.org/10.1080/15528014.2019.1580534","page":"354-375","source":"Taylor and Francis+NEJM","title":"The contradictions of a superfood consumerism in a postfeminist, neoliberal world","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1080/15528014.2019.1580534","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Sikka","given":"Tina"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",5,27]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}
or with wild mushrooms ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"b90UPI8r","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barroetave\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}a, 2020; Peris, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Barroetaveña, 2020; Peris, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33009,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2B36MBBY"],"itemData":{"id":33009,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Manual N°20 CIEFAP, Esquel","language":"es","page":"88 1514–2256","title":"Cocinar con hongos silvestres. Descripción nutricional, propiedades, modos de consumo y preservación de los hongos silvestres de Patagonia","author":[{"family":"Barroetaveña","given":"C."},{"family":"López","given":"S.N."},{"family":"Pildain","given":"M.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32995,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/37PNVI9T"],"itemData":{"id":32995,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-987-28380-2-7","language":"es","number-of-pages":"173","publisher":"En Prensa). CIEFAP, Esquel","title":"Micogastronomía Patagónica. Nuevos recursos productivos para la región","author":[{"family":"Peris","given":"P."},{"family":"Pildain","given":"M.B."},{"family":"Barroetaveña","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is especially relevant for plant species that have been traditionally used as medicinal herbs by rural communities who gather wild native species. Those plants may now also be demanded by urban consumers that rarely are informed about the risks of extinction or biodiversity losses due to high consumption and demand of those products.
	Consumer demand is a significant and inherent driver of illegal wild species trade and biodiversity conservation depends on the management of consumer behaviour as well as changing behaviour as well as changing behavior of other actors, such as in the case of marine recreational fishery, where efforts should be also oriented towards the preferences, motivations and demands of fishers regarding fisheries ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1PWCw5T8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Guidi, 2021; Llompart, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Guidi, 2021; Llompart, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":25101,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PV8ZF5HU"],"itemData":{"id":25101,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Regional Studies in Marine Science","language":"en","page":"101828","title":"Characteristics, preferences and perceptions of recreational fishers in northern Patagonia, Argentina","volume":"45","author":[{"family":"Guidi","given":"C."},{"family":"Baigún","given":"C.R.M."},{"family":"Ginter","given":"L.G."},{"family":"Soricetti","given":"M."},{"family":"Rivas","given":"F.G."},{"family":"Morawicki","given":"S."},{"family":"Quezada","given":"F."},{"family":"Bazzani","given":"J.L."},{"family":"Solimano","given":"P.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":12221,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N3AS7M2F"],"itemData":{"id":12221,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"New Zealand Journal of Marine and Freshwater Research","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"57–70","title":"Assessment of a major shore-based marine recreational fishery in the southwest Atlantic, Argentina","volume":"46","author":[{"family":"Llompart","given":"F.M."},{"family":"Colautti","given":"D.C."},{"family":"Baigún","given":"C.R.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      In the context of illegal trade, behaviour change methods are rarely applied and usually not well-documented. However, some organisations are emphasising application of behaviour change methods, as the example of TRAFFIC, a wild species trade-monitoring network of researchers and practitioners hosted by World Wide Fund (WWF) and the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN). They developed a Wild species Consumer Behaviour Change Toolkit, which has been made widely available ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oTAqv6XE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Wallen & Daut, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Wallen & Daut, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6732,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9JXEY7C8"],"itemData":{"id":6732,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Biodiversity conservation is contingent upon managing human behaviour and, at times, changing behaviour. This is particularly relevant to the illegal trade in wildlife and wildlife products, both flora and fauna. Driven by dynamics of consumer demand and illicit supply, mitigation of illegal trade requires a fuller appreciation of human behaviour and methods to change it. In various sectors, social influence, behavioural insights, social marketing and human-centred approaches trend towards mainstream practice and policy application. However, in the context of conservation and wildlife trafficking, these approaches and their usefulness are not well-articulated nor application widespread. Here, we provide a practical overview of relevant behaviour change methods and frameworks. We discuss their usefulness and potential application to mitigating the illegal wildlife trade, in general and consumer demand, in particular.","container-title":"Nature Conservation","DOI":"10.3897/natureconservation.26.22725","ISSN":"1314-6947","page":"55–75","title":"The challenge and opportunity of behaviour change methods and frameworks to reduce demand for illegal wildlife","volume":"26","author":[{"family":"Wallen","given":"Kenneth E."},{"family":"Daut","given":"Elizabeth"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Another example of media as a communication tool to address awareness regarding wild species conservation concerns consumption of palm oil and orang-utan threatening, where an education video presentation played at the Melbourne Zoo, Australia, as well as on the YouTube platform, mobilized celebrity ambassadors and social media ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zGN7xQDi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pearson, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Pearson, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4623,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HQBYH4T7"],"itemData":{"id":4623,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Zoo Biology","DOI":"10.1002/zoo.21120.","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"184–96","title":"Evaluating the Conservation Impact of an Innovative Zoo-Based Educational Campaign: ‘Don’t Palm Us Off’ for Orang-Utan Conservation","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1002/zoo.21120.","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Pearson","given":"Elissa L."},{"family":"Lowry","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"Dorrian","given":"Jillian"},{"family":"Litchfield","given":"Carla A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
	Although there is no regulation about selling and buying some wild species, such as marine ornamental fishes online ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HkJMVPuX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Borges, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Borges, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NX85E8LY"],"itemData":{"id":4548,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","DOI":"10.1016/j.biocon.2021.109344.","language":"en","page":"0006–3207,","title":"Caught in the (inter)net: Online trade of ornamental fish in Brazil","volume":"263,2021, 109344","author":[{"family":"Borges","given":"A.K.M."},{"family":"Oliveira","given":"T.P.R."},{"family":"Rosa","given":"I.L."},{"family":"Braga-Pereira","given":"F."},{"family":"Ramos","given":"H.A.C."},{"family":"Rocha","given":"L.A."},{"family":"Alves","given":"R.R.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, social media and social networks are gaining more and more importance and are being used to influence consumer behaviour in many situations. On the one hand, this may promote demand of products related to wild species, as the internet increases exposure to those commodities and facilitate its consumption by suggesting suppliers, including related products or recommending on modes of consumption, such as with wild mushroom species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vznVO39e","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barroetave\\\\uc0\\\\u241{}a, 2020; Peris, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Barroetaveña, 2020; Peris, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33009,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2B36MBBY"],"itemData":{"id":33009,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Manual N°20 CIEFAP, Esquel","language":"es","page":"88 1514–2256","title":"Cocinar con hongos silvestres. Descripción nutricional, propiedades, modos de consumo y preservación de los hongos silvestres de Patagonia","author":[{"family":"Barroetaveña","given":"C."},{"family":"López","given":"S.N."},{"family":"Pildain","given":"M.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":32995,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/37PNVI9T"],"itemData":{"id":32995,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-987-28380-2-7","language":"es","number-of-pages":"173","publisher":"En Prensa). CIEFAP, Esquel","title":"Micogastronomía Patagónica. Nuevos recursos productivos para la región","author":[{"family":"Peris","given":"P."},{"family":"Pildain","given":"M.B."},{"family":"Barroetaveña","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. On the other hand, social media helps to inform consumers about potential negative effects of certain commodities, such as palm oil ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KiWrFhlg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Carrasco, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Carrasco, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6754,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A5DUVA3H"],"itemData":{"id":6754,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Carrasco, L. R., J. Chan, F. L. McGrath, and L. T. P. Nghiem. 2017. Biodiversity conservation in a telecoupled world. Ecology and Society 22(3):24. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-09448-220324","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-09448-220324","ISSN":"1708-3087","issue":"3","language":"en","license":"© 2017 by the author(s)","source":"www.ecologyandsociety.org","title":"Biodiversity conservation in a telecoupled world","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol22/iss3/art24/","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L. Roman"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Joleen"},{"family":"McGrath","given":"Francesca"},{"family":"Nghiem","given":"Le"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",9,14]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. If there is sufficient critical mass among consumers, a societal tipping point may be passed, changing social norms and affecting business practices ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MOxsXWcm","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gladwell, 2000; Nyborg, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Gladwell, 2000; Nyborg, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5270,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XZ4C9G34"],"itemData":{"id":5270,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Little, Brown","title":"The Tipping Point: How Little Things Can Make a Big Difference","author":[{"family":"Gladwell","given":"Malcolm"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":6751,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4VDRU8SW"],"itemData":{"id":6751,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Climate change, biodiversity loss, antibiotic resistance, and other global challenges pose major collective action problems: A group benefits from a certain action, but no individual has sufficient incentive to act alone. Formal institutions, e.g., laws and treaties, have helped address issues like ozone depletion, lead pollution, and acid rain. However, formal institutions are not always able to enforce collectively desirable outcomes. In such cases, informal institutions, such as social norms, can be important. If conditions are right, policy can support social norm changes, helping address even global problems. To judge when this is realistic, and what role policy can play, we discuss three crucial questions: Is a tipping point likely to exist, such that vicious cycles of socially damaging behavior can potentially be turned into virtuous ones? Can policy create tipping points where none exist? Can policy push the system past the tipping point?\\nPolicies may influence large-scale behavioral tipping\\nPolicies may influence large-scale behavioral tipping","container-title":"Science","DOI":"10.1126/science.aaf8317","ISSN":"0036-8075, 1095-9203","issue":"6308","language":"en","license":"Copyright © 2016, American Association for the Advancement of Science","note":"PMID: 27846488","page":"42-43","source":"science.sciencemag.org","title":"Social norms as solutions","URL":"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/354/6308/42","volume":"354","author":[{"family":"Nyborg","given":"Karine"},{"family":"Anderies","given":"John M."},{"family":"Dannenberg","given":"Astrid"},{"family":"Lindahl","given":"Therese"},{"family":"Schill","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Schlüter","given":"Maja"},{"family":"Adger","given":"W. Neil"},{"family":"Arrow","given":"Kenneth J."},{"family":"Barrett","given":"Scott"},{"family":"Carpenter","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Chapin","given":"F. Stuart"},{"family":"Crépin","given":"Anne-Sophie"},{"family":"Daily","given":"Gretchen"},{"family":"Ehrlich","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"},{"family":"Jager","given":"Wander"},{"family":"Kautsky","given":"Nils"},{"family":"Levin","given":"Simon A."},{"family":"Madsen","given":"Ole Jacob"},{"family":"Polasky","given":"Stephen"},{"family":"Scheffer","given":"Marten"},{"family":"Walker","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Weber","given":"Elke U."},{"family":"Wilen","given":"James"},{"family":"Xepapadeas","given":"Anastasios"},{"family":"Zeeuw","given":"Aart","dropping-particle":"de"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2019",7,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",10,7]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
	Although not typically considered to be part of the conservation science toolbox, but also related to media vehicles, are marketing techniques to influence human preferences and behaviour. Marketing professionals use techniques to influence the public to buy particular products by developing relationships or creating positive associations with that particular item or service. While marketing is ubiquitous in commerce, the same techniques can be used to positively influence public behaviour regarding conservation matters ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4gxICrmp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. J. Wright, 2015)","plainCitation":"(A. J. Wright, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":7716,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H8BTXMB2"],"itemData":{"id":7716,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Addressing impacts from human activities requires the change of current practices. However, reaching a target audience about conservation issues and influencing their behaviour is not easy in a world where people are continually bombarded with information, and distractions are permanently available. Although not typically considered to be part of the conservation science toolbox, marketing techniques were designed in the commercial sector to identify and influence human preferences and behaviour by placing target audiences at the core of the marketing process. It thus seems reasonable that the same marketing principles and tools could and should be used to address pressing conservation issues. In this manuscript, we provide an introduction to the main objectives of marketing and illustrate how these can be applied to conservation and animal welfare issues. To that end we offer two examples: Project Ocean, where a major UK retailer joined forces with the Zoological Society of London to influence consumer behaviour around seafood; and Blackfish, which coupled social media with an award-winning documentary to create a discussion around the welfare of large cetaceans in captivity. Without the ability to influence human behaviour, a conservationists' role will likely be limited to that of describing the loss of biodiversity and the decline of the environment. We thus hope that conservation practitioners can embrace marketing as a fundamental component of the conservation toolbox.","container-title":"Ocean and Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2015.06.029","ISSN":"09645691","page":"41–48","title":"Competitive outreach in the 21st century: Why we need conservation marketing","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2015.06.029","volume":"115","author":[{"family":"Wright","given":"Andrew J."},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Diogo"},{"family":"Pilfold","given":"Kathleen"},{"family":"Parsons","given":"E. C.M."},{"family":"Ventre","given":"Kimberly"},{"family":"Cousins","given":"Jenny"},{"family":"Jefferson","given":"Rebecca"},{"family":"Koldewey","given":"Heather"},{"family":"Llewellyn","given":"Fiona"},{"family":"McKinley","given":"Emma"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
The purchase decisions of green consumers, for example, can be influenced by factors that are intrinsic and extrinsic to the consumers. The actual behaviour is a result of consumers’ regular habits, their product knowledge and the situational factors such as promotional campaigns ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"STq5Sv2y","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Kumar & Ghodeswar, 2015)","plainCitation":"(P. Kumar & Ghodeswar, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12285,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QTJ82L9U"],"itemData":{"id":12285,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marketing Intelligence and Planning","DOI":"10.1108/MIP-03-2014-0068.","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"330–47","title":"Factors Affecting Consumers’ Green Product Purchase Decisions","volume":"33","author":[{"family":"Kumar","given":"Prashant"},{"family":"Ghodeswar","given":"Bhimrao M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nevertheless, it is observed that attitudes towards sustainable consumption deviate from the actual consumption behaviour. Closing this attitude-behaviour-gap (or attitude-intention behaviour gap) remains a challenge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KAL3gUDb","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Terlau & Hirsch, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Terlau & Hirsch, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":3642,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YUS7WKPJ"],"itemData":{"id":3642,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings in Food System Dynamics","DOI":"10.18461/pfsd.2015.1516.","issue":"0","language":"en","page":"199–214","title":"Sustainable Consumption and the Attitude-Behaviour-Gap Phenomenon - Causes and Measurements towards a Sustainable Development","volume":"0","author":[{"family":"Terlau","given":"Wiltrud"},{"family":"Hirsch","given":"Darya"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Also, 
gaps between best practice in social marketing and current practices in the design of demand reduction campaigns are frequent ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MLLEawOa","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Greenfield & Ver\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}ssimo, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Greenfield & Veríssimo, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":5248,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5KM8DAT2"],"itemData":{"id":5248,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Social Marketing Quarterly","DOI":"10.1177/1524500418813543","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"40–54","title":"To What Extent Is Social Marketing Used in Demand Reduction Campaigns for Illegal Wildlife Products? Insights From Elephant Ivory and Rhino Horn","volume":"25","author":[{"family":"Greenfield","given":"S."},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Understanding the impacts of different outreach efforts remains limited and it is a challenge for conservation scientists and practitioners to apply the same  scientific rigour as in other parts of conservation practice ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ajRTv6Og","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ver\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}ssimo & Wan, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Veríssimo & Wan, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":13007,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AQ9DSFB8"],"itemData":{"id":13007,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conserv Biol","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.13227.","language":"pt","note":"PMID: 30259569","title":"Characterizing efforts to reduce consumer demand for wildlife products","volume":"Jun;33(3):623-633","author":[{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"D."},{"family":"Wan","given":"A.K.Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. MacFarlane ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZHzUyPMu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(MacFarlane, 2020)","plainCitation":"(MacFarlane, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":12396,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5Z7R3PZT"],"itemData":{"id":12396,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Lessons from Systematic Reviews from Outside Conservation Science. OSF Preprints","language":"en","title":"Reducing Demand for Overexploited Wildlife Products","URL":"https://osf.io/8935b/.","author":[{"family":"MacFarlane","given":"D."},{"family":"Hurlstone","given":"M.J."},{"family":"Ecker","given":"U.K.H."},{"family":"Ferraro","given":"P.J."},{"family":"Linden","given":"S.van","dropping-particle":"der"},{"family":"Wan","given":"A.K.Y."},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"D."},{"family":"Burgess","given":"G."},{"family":"Chen","given":"F."},{"family":"Hall","given":"W."},{"family":"Hollands","given":"G.J."},{"family":"Sutherland","given":"W.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} found that  mass-media campaigns and incentive programs were ineffective or short-lived, while advertising bans, social marketing, and locations bans seemed to be more promising approaches, but need more high-quality evidence to draw firm conclusions. Finally, demand reduction can play a role in reducing unsustainable use, but to be impactful it requires thinking about wider systemic effects ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NPcel3SC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Thomas\\\\uc0\\\\u8208{}Walters, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Thomas?Walters, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32726,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3TVIF5NA"],"itemData":{"id":32726,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Science and Practice","DOI":"10.1111/csp2.248","ISSN":"2578-4854, 2578-4854","issue":"9","journalAbbreviation":"Conservat Sci and Prac","language":"en","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Taking a more nuanced look at behavior change for demand reduction in the illegal wildlife trade","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/csp2.248","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Thomas?Walters","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"Diogo"},{"family":"Gadsby","given":"Erica"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"David"},{"family":"Smith","given":"Robert J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

4.2.5 ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BbFh27Df","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Xu 2012)","plainCitation":"(Xu 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":"Uebb2sCb/N8BlHQfr","uris":["http://www.mendeley.com/documents/?uuid=8aa1aee9-5390-472d-9bca-3dc26acfacd6"],"itemData":{"DOI":"10.1016/j.foodcont.2012.04.008","ISSN":"09567135","abstract":"As the world's largest seafood consumer and exporter, China is challenged by frequent seafood contamination incidents. To restore consumer confidence in seafood safety, China's Ministry of Agriculture (MOA) mandated a nation wide quality standard that awards a green label to qualified safer seafood. MOA is also planning for an environmental friendly label to address consumers' concerns about wild sea species sustainability. This study developed a three-stage purchase framework model and applied a multivariate Probit regression to analyze questionnaire information collected from 14 supermarkets in Beijing, China. The results show that Chinese consumers consider the seafood label a more important information source than previous consumption experience. They are willing to pay more for green-labeled seafood for the protection of individual benefits. Moreover, consumers are willing to pay more for the eco-labeled seafood for the protection of societal benefits. Gender, shopping venues, education, seafood expenditure and knowledge of the labeled products affected purchase intention and willingness to pay. Price was not a statistically significant factor affecting purchase decisions. © 2012 Elsevier Ltd.","author":[{"dropping-particle":"","family":"Xu","given":"Pei","non-dropping-particle":"","parse-names":false,"suffix":""},{"dropping-particle":"","family":"Zeng","given":"Yinchu","non-dropping-particle":"","parse-names":false,"suffix":""},{"dropping-particle":"","family":"Fong","given":"Quentin","non-dropping-particle":"","parse-names":false,"suffix":""},{"dropping-particle":"","family":"Lone","given":"Todd","non-dropping-particle":"","parse-names":false,"suffix":""},{"dropping-particle":"","family":"Liu","given":"Yuanyuan","non-dropping-particle":"","parse-names":false,"suffix":""}],"container-title":"Food Control","id":"ITEM-1","issue":"1","issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]},"page":"74-82","publisher":"Elsevier Ltd","title":"Chinese consumers' willingness to pay for green- and eco-labeled seafood","type":"article-journal","volume":"28"}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}Cultural drivers, value systems, customs and beliefs 
Key Messages :
* World views, religions, customs and belief systems have direct and indirect influence over the practices and uses of wild flora and fauna (established but incomplete) {4.2.5}.
* Indigenous and local knowledge includes cultural norms and ethics  support sustainable use (established but incomplete) {4.2.5}.
o Observation is central to sustainable use, allowing indigenous peoples and local knowledge to closely monitor and assess resources over time and providing a strong foundation on which to build sustainable management plans (well established) {4.2.5.2.5}.
o Indigenous and local knowledge is poorly documented when compared to other knowledges; where it has been documented and embraced there are greater sustainable use outcomes. It also offers a crucial foundation for sustainable use in and beyond indigenous peoples and local communities. Realizing its full benefits will require enhanced documentation as well as greater recognition of Indigenous rights (well established) {4.2.5}.
* Cultural norms often mediate practices and uses of wild species; where there are long term relationships between people-nature, norms around stewardship and care of wild species are more common (well established) {4.2.5.2}. Cultural taboos against harvest, consumption and other uses of wild species, play an important role in the conservation of some key species (e.g., sacred groves) (well established), {4.2.5.2.2}.
* Beliefs about the perceived medicinal value of wild species (coupled with clinical evidence about improved health outcomes) are a driver of the harvest and use of some flora and fauna (well established) {4.2.5.7}.
* Spiritual beliefs that wild species have equal value to humans (e.g, are relatives, or are gifts from the spirit world), are common in some cultures, particularly those of indigenous peoples. These beliefs often include recognitions or demonstrations of respect (e.g., ceremonies) when flora-fauna are harvested or used (well established) {4.2.5.2.5}.  
* In many indigenous cultures, practices that facilitate good relationships with wild species (e.g., take only what you need) are interconnected with cultural values norms of community well-being of communities (established but incomplete) {4.2.5.2}. Take only what you need, is not a common principle or value in cultures tied to globalization and industrialization tend to focus more on accumulation of wild species for profit. 
* Many indigenous peoples and local knowledge have traditional norms and practices to ensure appropriate, or sustainable, relationships with wild species. These norms and practices are based in indigenous and local knowledge and frequently central to spiritual practices. Often, they include significant sanctions or punishments when violated (established but incomplete) {4.2.5.2.7}.
* Human treatment of wild in a humane way is also highlighted in the Convention on Biological Diversity’s Addis Ababa Principles and Guidelines for the sustainable use of components of biodiversity (established but incomplete) {4.2.5.2.4}.

4.2.5.1. Overview 
Human societies are distinguished by their culture, which affects their behavior, consumption and attitudes towards nature and its constituents. Though the definition of culture has been long debated by anthropologists, ‘culture’ or “cultural diversity” is generally defined by the variety of religion, language, knowledge, food habits, values and philosophies in human societies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pWlzWsMt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Maiero & Shen, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Maiero & Shen, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32952,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6HFVT6U9"],"itemData":{"id":32952,"type":"manuscript","language":"en","note":"publisher: University of Bonn","title":"Commonalities between cultural and biodiversity","author":[{"family":"Maiero","given":"M."},{"family":"Shen","given":"X."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Since time immemorial, culture has played a pivotal role behind nature conservation and sustainable use of wild species; including both extractive and non- extractive uses, and in general, shaped a symbiotic and harmonious relationship with nature. The concept of sacred groves, where a small patch of forest is worshipped, exists worldwide and possibly dates back to millennia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Jg1LLrDI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. R. N. Alves, 2014; C. S. Negi, 2005)","plainCitation":"(R. R. N. Alves, 2014; C. S. Negi, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32950,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8YNBG3AD"],"itemData":{"id":32950,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Systems Research","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"751–80","title":"Conservation and Management of Sacred Groves, Myths and Beliefs of Tribal Communities: A Case Study from North-India","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Alves","given":"Rômulo R.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":32955,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZDVJAN4E"],"itemData":{"id":32955,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science & Management","DOI":"10.1080/17451590509618083","issue":"2","language":"fr","page":"85–96","title":"Religion and Biodiversity Conservation: Not a Mere Analogy","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Negi","given":"Chandra Singh"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Traditional, resource-reliant communities engaged in an intimate relationship with nature, which is reflected in the multitude of myths, legends and lore linked to the flora and fauna surrounding them. In fact, indigenous knowledge and practices are considered to represent ‘the oldest form of conservation known to mankind’ ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"1avQcWZ0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Pretty, 2009; Wild, 2008)","plainCitation":"(J. Pretty, 2009; Wild, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":17,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/56MJNR5B"],"itemData":{"id":17,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation and Society","DOI":"10.4103/0972-4923.58642","ISSN":"0972-4923","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Conservat Soc","language":"en","page":"100","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Intersections of Biological Diversity and Cultural Diversity: Towards Integration","title-short":"The Intersections of Biological Diversity and Cultural Diversity","URL":"http://www.conservationandsociety.org/text.asp?2009/7/2/100/58642","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Pretty","given":"Jules"},{"family":"Adams","given":"Bill"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Athayde","given":"Simone Ferreira","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Dudley","given":"Nigel"},{"family":"Hunn","given":"Eugene"},{"family":"Maffi","given":"Luisa"},{"family":"Milton","given":"Kay"},{"family":"Rapport","given":"David"},{"family":"Robbins","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor"},{"family":"Stolton","given":"Sue"},{"family":"Tsing","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Vintinnerk","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Pilgrim","given":"Sarah"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":32969,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SM67P4AW"],"itemData":{"id":32969,"type":"book","language":"en","note":"issue: 16)","publisher":"IUCN","title":"Sacred natural sites: guidelines for protected area managers","author":[{"family":"Wild","given":"R."},{"family":"McLeod","given":"C."},{"family":"Valentine","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, places that are regarded as sacred by local indigenous people are protected as sanctified locations and species are protected through beliefs in totem or taboo species (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"kGnQMabx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Posey, 1999; Sheridan & Nyamweru, 2008; Verschuuren, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Posey, 1999; Sheridan & Nyamweru, 2008; Verschuuren, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7GUX7585"],"itemData":{"id":34548,"type":"book","call-number":"GF80 .C85 1999","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-85339-397-6","publisher":"Intermediate Technology","publisher-place":"London","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Cultural and spiritual values of biodiversity","URL":"file:///C:/Users/MARIE-~1/AppData/Local/Temp/Cultural_Spiritual_thebible.pdf","author":[{"family":"Posey","given":"D. A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":32940,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KE8GE6RS"],"itemData":{"id":32940,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"James Currey","title":"African Sacred Groves: Ecological Dynamics and Social Change","author":[{"family":"Sheridan","given":"M.J."},{"family":"Nyamweru","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}},{"id":34421,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3PCJESYI"],"itemData":{"id":34421,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-1-84971-166-1","title":"Sacred Natural Sites Conserving Nature and Culture publishing for a sustainable future","URL":"www.earthscan.co.uk.","author":[{"family":"Verschuuren","given":"Bas"},{"family":"Wild","given":"Robert"},{"family":"Mcneely","given":"Jeffrey"},{"family":"Oviedo","given":"Gonzalo"},{"family":"Washington","given":"London •"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, human use of wild animals (e.g., totem species or tabooed species) for a variety of ethnic and religious purposes has been an integral part of many cultures and traditional societies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KjhGyOX0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(R. Alves, 2012)","plainCitation":"(R. Alves, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32719,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/56855GSE"],"itemData":{"id":32719,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Faunal resources have played a wide range of roles in human life from the earliest days of recorded history. In addition to their utilitarian importance, animals have been recognized in religion, art, music and literature and other different cultural manifestations of mankind. Animals uses and the means by which they are exploited by humans, as well as the cultural aspects which conduct interactions between people and animals, are implicated in a lower or higher pressure on populations of exploited species, reflected in either their sustainable use or even lead to their extinction. The variety of interactions between humans and animals is the subject matter of ethnozoology - the branch of ethnobiology that investigates the knowledge human societies have accumulated concerning animals, as well as their significance to those people and their uses. Ethnozoological studies can be a valuable asset to increase our understanding of the cultural, economic, social, and traditional roles of played by animals. In this context, they have a central role in conservation and management. This work provides a review on the main forms of interactions between humans and the fauna along history, and their ecological implications, and discusses the role of the ethnozoology in animal conservation.","container-title":"Ethnobiology and Conservation","DOI":"10.15451/ec2012-8-1.2-1-69","journalAbbreviation":"Ethnobiology and Conservation","page":"1-69","source":"ResearchGate","title":"Relationship between fauna and people and the role of ethnozoology in animal conservation","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Alves","given":"Rômulo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",8,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. At the same time, pervasive animism is also practiced in many traditional societies, which, at times, resulted in unsustainable use of wild species. 
      The concept of biocultural diversity was originally proposed to denote this intimate link between nature and culture in relation to indigenous communities, especially those living in areas of high biodiversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Fkg1yKXc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Maffi, 2005; Maffi & Woodley, 2012; Posey, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Maffi, 2005; Maffi & Woodley, 2012; Posey, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":23,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N2TM889P"],"itemData":{"id":23,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Anthropology","DOI":"10.1146/annurev.anthro.34.081804.120437","ISSN":"0084-6570, 1545-4290","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Annu. Rev. Anthropol.","language":"en","page":"599-617","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Linguistic, Cultural and Biological Diversity","URL":"http://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev.anthro.34.081804.120437","volume":"34","author":[{"family":"Maffi","given":"Luisa"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005",10]]}}},{"id":35196,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NIJTU7LQ"],"itemData":{"id":35196,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Routledge","title":"Biocultural diversity conservation: a global sourcebook","author":[{"family":"Maffi","given":"L."},{"family":"Woodley","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":34548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7GUX7585"],"itemData":{"id":34548,"type":"book","call-number":"GF80 .C85 1999","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-85339-397-6","publisher":"Intermediate Technology","publisher-place":"London","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Cultural and spiritual values of biodiversity","URL":"file:///C:/Users/MARIE-~1/AppData/Local/Temp/Cultural_Spiritual_thebible.pdf","author":[{"family":"Posey","given":"D. A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Biocultural diversity recognizes “the interweave of biological and cultural diversity, people and places, and the continuing adaptation and co-evolution between landscapes and ways of life” ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3BfFkcj1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2011)","plainCitation":"(S. A. Laird, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32713,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4T7ZS3GU"],"itemData":{"id":32713,"type":"chapter","abstract":"Products from the wild, also known as non-timber forest products (NTFPs), are used as medicines, foods, spices, ?bers, and fuel and for a multitude of other purposes. They contribute substantially to rural livelihoods and generate revenue for companies and governments, and their use has a range of impacts on biodiversity conservation. However, throughout the world, NTFPs have been both overlooked and poorly regulated by governments. Inappropriate policies have not only led to over-exploitation but also generated new forms of inequity. Drawing upon cases from around the world, this chapter reviews these experiences and provides information to support new policy approaches toward NTFP regulation and the broader issues of governance associated with these products.","container-title":"Non-Timber Forest Products in the Global Context","event-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","ISBN":"978-3-642-17982-2","language":"en","note":"collection-title: Tropical Forestry\\nDOI: 10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_11","page":"227-253","publisher":"Springer Berlin Heidelberg","publisher-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Regulating Complexity: Policies for the Governance of Non-timber Forest Products","title-short":"Regulating Complexity","URL":"http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-642-17983-9_11","volume":"7","editor":[{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Sheona"},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"Charlie"},{"family":"Shanley","given":"Patricia"}],"author":[{"family":"Laird","given":"Sarah A."},{"family":"Wynberg","given":"Rachel"},{"family":"McLain","given":"Rebecca J."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",3,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the broader sense, it encompasses coding for knowledge, values, norms and protocols. The concept has been used to successfully promote the value of local indigenous knowledge and to curb the loss of biodiversity and the decline of intergenerational transmission of traditional knowledge, practices, and languages ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OiQwGqNW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rapport & Maffi, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Rapport & Maffi, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32965,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S7YEIDER"],"itemData":{"id":32965,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature and culture: Rebuilding lost connections","language":"en","page":"22","title":"The dual erosion of biological and cultural diversity: Implications for the health of ecocultural systems","volume":"103","author":[{"family":"Rapport","given":"D."},{"family":"Maffi","given":"L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It further increased the understanding of how relationships with nature are informed by different worldviews ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6vzwGkB1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Descola, 1994, 2005; Mathez-Stiefel, 2007; Posey, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Descola, 1994, 2005; Mathez-Stiefel, 2007; Posey, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32923,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S585RBHG"],"itemData":{"id":32923,"type":"chapter","container-title":"De la préhistoire aux missiles balistiques","language":"fr","page":"329–344","publisher":"La Découverte","title":"Pourquoi les Indiens d'Amazonie n'ont-ils pas domestiqué le pécari?","author":[{"family":"Descola","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1994"]]}}},{"id":33005,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/T9IT6MAT"],"itemData":{"id":33005,"type":"book","event-place":"Paris","language":"fr","publisher":"Gallimard","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Par-delà nature et culture","author":[{"family":"Descola","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32979,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S84V5RTS"],"itemData":{"id":32979,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Endogenous Development and Biocultural Diversity: The Interplay of Worldviews, Globalization and Locality","event-place":"Leusden","language":"en","page":"67–81","publisher":"COMPAS and CDE","publisher-place":"Leusden","title":"Promoting the Diversity of Worldviews: An Ontological Approach to Biocultural Diversity","author":[{"family":"Mathez-Stiefel","given":"Sarah-Lan"},{"family":"Boillat","given":"Sébastien"},{"family":"Rist","given":"Stephan"}],"editor":[{"family":"Haverkort","given":"Bertus"},{"family":"Rist","given":"Stephan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":34548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7GUX7585"],"itemData":{"id":34548,"type":"book","call-number":"GF80 .C85 1999","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-85339-397-6","publisher":"Intermediate Technology","publisher-place":"London","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Cultural and spiritual values of biodiversity","URL":"file:///C:/Users/MARIE-~1/AppData/Local/Temp/Cultural_Spiritual_thebible.pdf","author":[{"family":"Posey","given":"D. A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and how they contribute to a sense of personal and collective identity and heightened states of mental, physical and spiritual wellbeing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8kC7j9dz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Pretty, 2009; Russell, 2013)","plainCitation":"(J. Pretty, 2009; Russell, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":17,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/56MJNR5B"],"itemData":{"id":17,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation and Society","DOI":"10.4103/0972-4923.58642","ISSN":"0972-4923","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Conservat Soc","language":"en","page":"100","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Intersections of Biological Diversity and Cultural Diversity: Towards Integration","title-short":"The Intersections of Biological Diversity and Cultural Diversity","URL":"http://www.conservationandsociety.org/text.asp?2009/7/2/100/58642","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Pretty","given":"Jules"},{"family":"Adams","given":"Bill"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Athayde","given":"Simone Ferreira","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Dudley","given":"Nigel"},{"family":"Hunn","given":"Eugene"},{"family":"Maffi","given":"Luisa"},{"family":"Milton","given":"Kay"},{"family":"Rapport","given":"David"},{"family":"Robbins","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor"},{"family":"Stolton","given":"Sue"},{"family":"Tsing","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Vintinnerk","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Pilgrim","given":"Sarah"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":32723,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EF73QVSJ"],"itemData":{"id":32723,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Environment and Resources","DOI":"10.1146/annurev-environ-012312-110838","note":"publisher: Annual Reviews","page":"473–502","title":"Humans and nature: how knowing and experiencing nature affect well-being","volume":"38","author":[{"family":"Russell","given":"Roly"},{"family":"Guerry","given":"Anne D"},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Gould","given":"Rachelle K"},{"family":"Basurto","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Chan","given":"Kai MA"},{"family":"Klain","given":"Sarah"},{"family":"Levine","given":"Jordan"},{"family":"Tam","given":"Jordan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and promote sustainable use and harmonious existence. Engagement in biocultural relations also elicit emotions of happiness, enjoyment, inspiration, love, belonging and connectedness, among others. Conversely, feelings of sadness, pain and loss are experienced when culturally relevant nature is no longer accessible ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FRG5l4v5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cocks & Shackleton, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Cocks & Shackleton, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32974,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YSHUSS8C"],"itemData":{"id":32974,"type":"book","event-place":"London","language":"en","publisher":"Earthscan","publisher-place":"London","title":"Situating biocultural relations in city and townscapes: conclusion and recommendations","author":[{"family":"Cocks","given":"M."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C.In","dropping-particle":"press"}],"editor":[{"family":"Cocks","given":"M."},{"family":"Shackleton","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Many traditional practices, nonetheless, have faded in time and continue to erode under multiple pressures including globalization, human migration, urbanization, scholarization, changes in religion, and state control of land and resources. Still different elements of nature, including both biotic and abiotic components, continue to inspire and enrich people's cultural and ceremonial lives. They provide rich symbolic values and compassion for nature, and in a way, generate a positive affinity for nature and encourage sustainable use of wild species. 
      This section looks into the role of cultural drivers, including cultural diversity, religion and other belief systems, indigenous local knowledge and different customary values which contributed to the sustainable use of wild species, and how changes in these drivers have resulted in unsustainable and/or uncontrolled exploitation of wild species. The following four sub-sections are developed following systematic review methodology with specific search terms and engines specified in each sub-section. 
      
Methodology
The following four sub-sections were developed following different types of literature review methodology. The sub-section on cultural diversity, religion and belief system is derived from the three specific Scopus database searches, i.e., understanding of the relationship with language diversity with sustainable use of wild species, the experts administered the search terms ( TITLE-ABS-KEY ( cultural  AND diversity )  AND  TITLE-ABS-KEY ( language  AND diversity )  AND  TITLE-ABS-KEY ( wild  AND food )  OR  TITLE-ABS-KEY ( hunting )  OR  TITLE-ABS-KEY ( fishing )  OR  TITLE-ABS-KEY ( logging ) ) in Scopus database. The search produced 22 articles, therefore, additional search for grey literature, including important reports and books were performed.  For the section 4.2.5.1.2 on religious belief and sustainable use of wild species, the experts made a search using TITLE-ABS-KEY (religio) OR TITLE-ABS-KEY(belief) AND TITLE-ABS-KEY(species) AND TITLE-ABS-KEY(wild)). The search yielded a list of 195 articles, of which 55 articles were found relevant. The section on sacred groves was further developed based on an additional  Scopus database search using the following search terms ( TITLE-ABS-KEY ( sacred AND groves )  AND  TITLE-ABS-KEY ( hunting )  OR  TITLE-ABS-KEY ( fishing )  OR  TITLE-ABS KEY ( meditation )  OR  TITLE-ABS-KEY ( gathering )  OR  TITLE-ABS-KEY ( medicine )  OR  TITLE-ABS-KEY ( healing )  OR  TITLE-ABS-KEY ( tourism ) ). This search yielded 56 articles, out of which, 49 papers were considered appropriate.  For the section 4.2.5.1.3. on the role of taboos and traditional belief systems on sustainable use of species, the experts used the publish and perish search engine.  A total of 37 papers were considered for writing this sub-section.
      Bibliographic search engines, cross-referencing keywords, are useful but the results are not perfectly satisfactory always, because they privilege 1) scientific articles from English-speaking journals, 2) recent publications, often pointed or targeted, to the detriment of reference and/or more synthetic publications, as the concepts and search terms change over time, and 3) do not sufficiently consider non-academic productions (grey literature, government reports, conference proceedings, indigenous and local knowledge productions or reports, indigenous and local knowledge dialogue workshops, media, etc.), which are nonetheless essential for taking into account local knowledge and cultural drivers.
      To counterbalance these biases, the experts used other research methods for reviewing literature. The experts have mobilized partners and networks (students, colleagues, field workers), and have relied on diversified bibliographical resources, searching for relevant, synthetic or major references to the subject; some old, some not yet published (and graciously made available by the authors). The 54 cited references for the customary values section have been analysed and integrated in databases.
      
Gaps and limitations
Although biocultural diversity, indigenous and local knowledge, and customary values have been identified as a key component for the sustainable use of wild species, methods to document these and complement scientific studies, are not yet widely used and need to be further explored and implemented. There is still a clear dearth of quality documentation on the diverse indigenous and cultural use of wild species. For example, academic research has not understood well how languages changed certain practices and how they influenced their relationship with the sustainable use of wild species. Likewise, while the proportionate relationship between language and biodiversity is well-documented, underlying mechanisms remain unexplored.  Similarly, academic efforts have focused on documenting the loss of systems knowledge indigenous systems and not on understanding the processes that drive those changes and their effects on society’s capacity to generate, apply, and transmit knowledge to maintain living systems knowledge indigenous systems. There is a requirement to connect these dots via. qualitative analysis to understand the impact of cultural factors on the sustainable use of wild species. Besides, it will be crucial to understand the enabling conditions that contribute to the effective transmission of indigenous and local knowledge. This will entail a critical analysis of current educational curricula that minimize contact with local flora, fauna, and land, contributing to the erosion of local knowledge. By contrast, it will be essential to analyze the factors that support local cultural, political, educational, and economic institutions which lead to enduring connections to the land among youth, collectors, and citizens, even amid modernity. For use of wild species to truly be sustainable over time, analysis is required to understand how to stem the tide of rural abandonment, to raise the status of careers linked with wild harvest and natural resource management, and to attract youth to environmentally oriented vocations. while recognising/ valuing the existing knowledge of indigenous people and local communities.

4.2.5.2. Cultural diversity, religion and belief systems
4.2.5.2.1. Cultural diversity 
The variety in religion, language, food habits and philosophies principally distinguish human societies from one another. Although biological and cultural dimensions of diversity have long been dealt with separately, there is an increasing recognition that cultural diversity plays a pivotal role in sustainable use of wild species and their conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JKPwz5Da","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Anthony, 2011; Dunn, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Anthony, 2011; Dunn, 2008)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33008,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3AUBMT2K"],"itemData":{"id":33008,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Researches in Biodiversity—Models and Applications","language":"en","page":"3–24","title":"Towards bridging worldviews in biodiversity conservation: exploring the Tsonga concept of ntumbuloko in South Africa","author":[{"family":"Anthony","given":"B.P."},{"family":"Abonyi","given":"S."},{"family":"Terblanche","given":"P."},{"family":"Watt","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":30244,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I274CBST"],"itemData":{"id":30244,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","issue":"7220","language":"en","title":"Biocultural Diversity Should Be a Priority for Conservation","volume":"456","author":[{"family":"Dunn","given":"Christopher P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, research conducted across countries and continents identified patterns of co-occurrence of linguistic and biological diversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"b2Ym0j1A","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gorenflo, 2012; Harmon, 1996; Oviedo, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Gorenflo, 2012; Harmon, 1996; Oviedo, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":28,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CF3NQF95"],"itemData":{"id":28,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1117511109","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"21","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","language":"en","page":"8032-8037","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Co-occurrence of linguistic and biological diversity in biodiversity hotspots and high biodiversity wilderness areas","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/doi/10.1073/pnas.1117511109","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Gorenflo","given":"L. J."},{"family":"Romaine","given":"S."},{"family":"Mittermeier","given":"R. A."},{"family":"Walker-Painemilla","given":"K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",5,22]]}}},{"id":32947,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P5E68K4N"],"itemData":{"id":32947,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Southwest journal of Linguistics","issue":"1&2","language":"en","page":"89–108","title":"Losing species, losing languages: connections between biological and linguistic diversity","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Harmon","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}},{"id":32970,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JUNVNIRK"],"itemData":{"id":32970,"type":"book","event-place":"Gland, Switzerland","language":"en","publisher":"World Wildlife Foundation International and Terralingua","publisher-place":"Gland, Switzerland","title":"Indigenous and Traditional Peoples of the World and Ecoregion Conservation: An Integrated Approach to Conserving the World’s Biological and Cultural Diversity","author":[{"family":"Oviedo","given":"G."},{"family":"Maffi","given":"L."},{"family":"Larsen","given":"P.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Evidence indicates that areas with high language diversity are strongly correlated with biodiversity, in particular high bird and mammal diversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"rZOLKdgw","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Frainer, 2020; Gorenflo, 2012; Sutherland, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Frainer, 2020; Gorenflo, 2012; Sutherland, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32948,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6GY4KZT5"],"itemData":{"id":32948,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"43","language":"en","page":"26539–26543","title":"Opinion: Cultural and linguistic diversities are underappreciated pillars of biodiversity","volume":"117","author":[{"family":"Frainer","given":"A."},{"family":"Mustonen","given":"T."},{"family":"Hugu","given":"S."},{"family":"Andreeva","given":"T."},{"family":"Arttijeff","given":"E.M."},{"family":"Arttijeff","given":"I.S."},{"family":"Pecl","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}},{"id":28,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CF3NQF95"],"itemData":{"id":28,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1117511109","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"21","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","language":"en","page":"8032-8037","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Co-occurrence of linguistic and biological diversity in biodiversity hotspots and high biodiversity wilderness areas","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/doi/10.1073/pnas.1117511109","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Gorenflo","given":"L. J."},{"family":"Romaine","given":"S."},{"family":"Mittermeier","given":"R. A."},{"family":"Walker-Painemilla","given":"K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",5,22]]}}},{"id":32962,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WGCBXBKZ"],"itemData":{"id":32962,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Nature","DOI":"http://www.nature.com/articles/nature01607","issue":"6937","language":"en","page":"276–79","title":"Parallel Extinction Risk and Global Distribution of Languages and Species","volume":"423","author":[{"family":"Sutherland","given":"William J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. According to ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"chuTaAVo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gorenflo, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Gorenflo, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":28,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CF3NQF95"],"itemData":{"id":28,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1117511109","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"21","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","language":"en","page":"8032-8037","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Co-occurrence of linguistic and biological diversity in biodiversity hotspots and high biodiversity wilderness areas","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/doi/10.1073/pnas.1117511109","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Gorenflo","given":"L. J."},{"family":"Romaine","given":"S."},{"family":"Mittermeier","given":"R. A."},{"family":"Walker-Painemilla","given":"K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",5,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, a total of 3,202 languages, which accounts for nearly half of the existing 7000 languages, are found in the 35 global biodiversity hotspots. Languages encode non-transferable knowledge bases which serve as rich repositories of diverse uses of plant and animal species. Within these languages exist several thousand years of undocumented indigenous scientific knowledge of biodiversity inventory (e.g., folk taxonomy) and instructions (folklores, stories, proverbs). Languages often underpin the preference for species which are subjected to hunting or fishing (Established but incomplete). For example, in Senegal, “Essegnaille” refers to three commonly consumed species from the Carangidae taxonomic family, namely, Caranx hippos, Caranx senegallus, and Hemicaranx bicolor. The local fishers, however, do not have a name for Chloroscombrus chrysurus despite belonging to the same family. This is because it is usually rejected when caught ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"g1F0922K","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Frainer, 2020)","plainCitation":"(Frainer, 2020)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32948,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6GY4KZT5"],"itemData":{"id":32948,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","issue":"43","language":"en","page":"26539–26543","title":"Opinion: Cultural and linguistic diversities are underappreciated pillars of biodiversity","volume":"117","author":[{"family":"Frainer","given":"A."},{"family":"Mustonen","given":"T."},{"family":"Hugu","given":"S."},{"family":"Andreeva","given":"T."},{"family":"Arttijeff","given":"E.M."},{"family":"Arttijeff","given":"I.S."},{"family":"Pecl","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2020"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Folklores often prevent harming animals, foster positive environmental attitudes and teach harmonious coexistence. It symbolizes myths, tales, riddles, proverbs which are generally unwritten but passes through oral traditions. Historically, most indigenous folklores influenced the sustainable use of species, although with a few exceptions. Evidence indicates that folkloric or religious associations have particularly protected primates, predominantly in Asia and Africa, due to the physical and behavioural resemblances with human ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"F8SEjSi9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. E. Baker, 2013; E. P. Riley, 2010; Waters, 2018)","plainCitation":"(S. E. Baker, 2013; E. P. Riley, 2010; Waters, 2018)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":721,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H8KV6TSV"],"itemData":{"id":721,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"BioScience","issue":"12","note":"publisher: American Institute of Biological Sciences Circulation, AIBS, 1313 Dolley …","page":"928–938","source":"Google Scholar","title":"Rough trade: animal welfare in the global wildlife trade","title-short":"Rough trade","volume":"63","author":[{"family":"Baker","given":"Sandra E."},{"family":"Cain","given":"Russ"},{"family":"Van Kesteren","given":"Freya"},{"family":"Zommers","given":"Zinta A."},{"family":"D'cruze","given":"Neil"},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"David W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":32964,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/REZDTMQ8"],"itemData":{"id":32964,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Indonesia. Oryx","issue":"2","language":"fr","page":"235–240","title":"The importance of human–macaque folklore for conservation in Lore Lindu National Park, Sulawesi","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Riley","given":"E.P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":32961,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SFXLNSL4"],"itemData":{"id":32961,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Folia Primatologica","issue":"1","language":"it","page":"13–29","title":"Understanding human-animal relations in the context of primate conservation: a multispecies ethnographic approach in North Morocco","volume":"89","author":[{"family":"Waters","given":"S."},{"family":"Bell","given":"S."},{"family":"Setchell","given":"J.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In central Sulawesi, for instance, indigenous folklores prevented harming the macaques (Macaca tonkeana) despite the species’ frequent crop-raiding behavior ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4BlRyWfW","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(E. P. Riley & Priston, 2010)","plainCitation":"(E. P. Riley & Priston, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32997,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7FAY6IGJ"],"itemData":{"id":32997,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"American Journal of Primatology","issue":"10","language":"it","page":"848–854","title":"Macaques in farms and folklore: exploring the human–nonhuman primate interface in Sulawesi, Indonesia","volume":"72","author":[{"family":"Riley","given":"E.P."},{"family":"Priston","given":"N.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, folklores also contributed to unsustainable use and indiscriminate killing. In Portugal, indigenous folklores negatively portrayed the wolf's image as demon which resulted in strong apathy for the species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"loOJPRCH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cer\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}aco, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Ceríaco, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32985,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XLD2WXWC"],"itemData":{"id":32985,"type":"chapter","abstract":"Portugal has a very rich ethnozoological heritage due to its rich biodiversity, history, and culture. However, there are very few works dedicated to this, and little is known about the zootherapeutic remedies used in Portugal. Due to its location in a biodiversity hotspot, its agro-pastoralist traditions and also its role during the maritime discoveries of the Renaissance, Portugal has a long list of animals and animal parts that have been used in its folk medicine. These uses can still be found in many historical documents and pharmacopeias. Although these uses started to decline in the nineteenth century, many of them survived, being passed from generation to generation by oral tradition, and are still used by local populations. We can still find 225 remedies, using 54 animal species, mainly domestic animals and common species, in use in modern day Portugal. The use of some animals in traditional medicine, such as Lataste’s viper, the Iberian wolf, and the European pond turtle, can be an additional pressure on these already endangered species. Studies are much needed to better understand zootherapeutic uses in Portuguese folk medicine and their implications for conservation. This chapter intends to be a starting point to these future investigations.","container-title":"Animals in Traditional Folk Medicine: Implications for Conservation","event-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","ISBN":"978-3-642-29026-8","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1007/978-3-642-29026-8_15","page":"317-345","publisher":"Springer","publisher-place":"Berlin, Heidelberg","source":"Springer Link","title":"A Review of Fauna Used in Zootherapeutic Remedies in Portugal: Historical Origins, Current Uses, and Implications for Conservation","title-short":"A Review of Fauna Used in Zootherapeutic Remedies in Portugal","URL":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-29026-8_15","author":[{"family":"Ceríaco","given":"Luis Miguel Pires"}],"editor":[{"family":"Alves","given":"Rômulo Romeu Nóbrega"},{"family":"Rosa","given":"Ierecê Lucena"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As such, reptiles and amphibians are generally least appreciated in the indigenous folklores despite their high ecological and economic importance. Reptiles, in particular, epitomises many negative values within traditional folklores. Nonetheless, as cultures and languages are lost, precious information about species, their uses, as well as the philosophies’ meanings are also lost. Of the estimated 7,000 languages, nearly half is already lost, while some studies predict up to 90% of the existing language may well disappear by the end of this century ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PeBsxxVS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gorenflo, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Gorenflo, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":28,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CF3NQF95"],"itemData":{"id":28,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","DOI":"10.1073/pnas.1117511109","ISSN":"0027-8424, 1091-6490","issue":"21","journalAbbreviation":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","language":"en","page":"8032-8037","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Co-occurrence of linguistic and biological diversity in biodiversity hotspots and high biodiversity wilderness areas","URL":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/doi/10.1073/pnas.1117511109","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Gorenflo","given":"L. J."},{"family":"Romaine","given":"S."},{"family":"Mittermeier","given":"R. A."},{"family":"Walker-Painemilla","given":"K."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012",5,22]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Africa, in particular, is losing a great amount of linguistic diversity. It is estimated that one language gets extinct every 3.5 months ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WfwVWrpx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. Rogers & Campbell, 2015)","plainCitation":"(C. Rogers & Campbell, 2015)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32992,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/64YYHDCF"],"itemData":{"id":32992,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Oxford Research Encyclopedia of Linguistics","language":"en","note":"DOI: 10.1093/acrefore/9780199384655.013.21","publisher":"Oxford University Press","title":"Endangered languages","author":[{"family":"Rogers","given":"C."},{"family":"Campbell","given":"L."}],"editor":[{"family":"Aronoff","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This poses an imminent threat to biocultural diversity, in particular, loss of traditional knowledge, including folklore, traditional music, literature and songs etc. (see section on local practices and indigenous knowledge). There is, however, a dearth of quality documentation on how the annihilation of languages changed certain practices and their relationship with sustainable use of wild species. More so, while the proportionate relationship between language and biodiversity is well-documented, the underlying mechanism of sustainable use remain fairly unexplored.    

4.2.5.2.2. Religion and belief systems  
Human societies are governed by both formal and informal set of belief systems, including religious beliefs, principles and moral values such as kindness and compassion.  Of these, religious belief is defined as a unified system of practices relative to sacred things, aiming to answer the quest and purpose of human life and beyond. Throughout history, every human society has followed certain religious narratives, symbols, and commitments in their public and personal life. Religious practices also include a significant part of ceremonial life, including different festivals, marriage and funeral. All major world religions, irrespective of their geographic occurrence, envision a symbiotic relationship between humans and nature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZCHBKUoz","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. S. Negi, 2005a)","plainCitation":"(C. S. Negi, 2005a)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32955,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZDVJAN4E"],"itemData":{"id":32955,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science & Management","DOI":"10.1080/17451590509618083","issue":"2","language":"fr","page":"85–96","title":"Religion and Biodiversity Conservation: Not a Mere Analogy","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Negi","given":"Chandra Singh"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. While some religions symbolize nature as ‘God’ or ‘Godly’, some believe nature as the creation of God, which deserves sympathy and respect. Today, nearly 80 percent of the world population follows a specific religion, which includes 2 billion Christians, about 1.34 billion Muslims, 950 million Hindus, and over 200 million Buddhists. In Africa, and Latin America, frequent syncretism occurs between ancient religion (animism) and revealed religion (Islam, Christianity), with ongoing engagement of ancient rites to preserve the heritage of the ancestors. In addition, there are more than 4000 religions, including animism, totemism with many believers belong to traditional indigenous communities.  
      According to ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yH8h1827","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32976,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YY6ACT9M"],"itemData":{"id":32976,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"IDRC","language":"en","title":"Managing small-scale fisheries: alternative directions and methods","URL":"http://hdl.handle.net/10625/31968","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Mahon","given":"R."},{"family":"McConney","given":"P."},{"family":"Pollnac","given":"R."},{"family":"Pomeroy","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, religious traditions, as such, have a fairly limited role in biodiversity. However, they provide critical values, worldviews, and beliefs, which determine how a society perceives it should interact with biodiversityITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"alFMKi9g","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. S. Negi, 2005b)","plainCitation":"(C. S. Negi, 2005b)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32956,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L78LT9ME"],"itemData":{"id":32956,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science & Management","DOI":"10.1080/17451590509618083","issue":"2","language":"fr","page":"85–96","title":"Religion and Biodiversity Conservation: Not a Mere Analogy","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Negi","given":"Chandra Singh"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Religious beliefs can directly influence or mediate sustainable use in mainly two ways, firstly by providing effective protection to wild species in sacred natural sites, and secondly, by altering the attitude towards wild species by declaring it potentially sacred or important for ceremonial life ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"xJedlyxC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(J. Pretty, 2009)","plainCitation":"(J. Pretty, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":17,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/56MJNR5B"],"itemData":{"id":17,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation and Society","DOI":"10.4103/0972-4923.58642","ISSN":"0972-4923","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Conservat Soc","language":"en","page":"100","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Intersections of Biological Diversity and Cultural Diversity: Towards Integration","title-short":"The Intersections of Biological Diversity and Cultural Diversity","URL":"http://www.conservationandsociety.org/text.asp?2009/7/2/100/58642","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Pretty","given":"Jules"},{"family":"Adams","given":"Bill"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Athayde","given":"Simone Ferreira","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Dudley","given":"Nigel"},{"family":"Hunn","given":"Eugene"},{"family":"Maffi","given":"Luisa"},{"family":"Milton","given":"Kay"},{"family":"Rapport","given":"David"},{"family":"Robbins","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor"},{"family":"Stolton","given":"Sue"},{"family":"Tsing","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Vintinnerk","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Pilgrim","given":"Sarah"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In fact, all traditional societies, to a meaningful extent, were able to conserve the functions of productive ecosystems, which were supported by various kinds of animism, rituals, magico-religious practices and taboos ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dSEycvb6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. S. Negi, 2005b; J. Pretty, 2009)","plainCitation":"(C. S. Negi, 2005b; J. Pretty, 2009)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":17,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/56MJNR5B"],"itemData":{"id":17,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation and Society","DOI":"10.4103/0972-4923.58642","ISSN":"0972-4923","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Conservat Soc","language":"en","page":"100","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"The Intersections of Biological Diversity and Cultural Diversity: Towards Integration","title-short":"The Intersections of Biological Diversity and Cultural Diversity","URL":"http://www.conservationandsociety.org/text.asp?2009/7/2/100/58642","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Pretty","given":"Jules"},{"family":"Adams","given":"Bill"},{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Athayde","given":"Simone Ferreira","non-dropping-particle":"de"},{"family":"Dudley","given":"Nigel"},{"family":"Hunn","given":"Eugene"},{"family":"Maffi","given":"Luisa"},{"family":"Milton","given":"Kay"},{"family":"Rapport","given":"David"},{"family":"Robbins","given":"Paul"},{"family":"Sterling","given":"Eleanor"},{"family":"Stolton","given":"Sue"},{"family":"Tsing","given":"Anna"},{"family":"Vintinnerk","given":"Erin"},{"family":"Pilgrim","given":"Sarah"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":32956,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L78LT9ME"],"itemData":{"id":32956,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science & Management","DOI":"10.1080/17451590509618083","issue":"2","language":"fr","page":"85–96","title":"Religion and Biodiversity Conservation: Not a Mere Analogy","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Negi","given":"Chandra Singh"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Religious sanctions, in particular, played a significant role in conserving threatened species, while also reducing animosity towards wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"siuJ79JC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McKay, 2018)","plainCitation":"(McKay, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32957,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5NRL9FVA"],"itemData":{"id":32957,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PloS one","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"0201447","title":"Tolerating tigers: Gaining local and spiritual perspectives on human-tiger interactions in Sumatra through rural community interviews","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"McKay","given":"J.E."},{"family":"St. John","given":"F.A."},{"family":"Harihar","given":"A."},{"family":"Martyr","given":"D."},{"family":"Leader-Williams","given":"N."},{"family":"Milliyanawati","given":"B."},{"family":"Linkie","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This is particularly prominent in the Asian sub-continent and in parts of Africa. Religious sanctions facilitated conservation in multiple ways, e.g., through the prohibition of entering certain areas or killing certain species and through instigating a fear of repercussions from doing dreadful things ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Azw3dS0y","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. S. Negi, 2005b)","plainCitation":"(C. S. Negi, 2005b)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32956,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L78LT9ME"],"itemData":{"id":32956,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science & Management","DOI":"10.1080/17451590509618083","issue":"2","language":"fr","page":"85–96","title":"Religion and Biodiversity Conservation: Not a Mere Analogy","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Negi","given":"Chandra Singh"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, Islam prohibits the hunting and killing of wild animals for sports or for sale but permits hunting when meat is consumed as food. In this way, Islam prohibits unnecessary killings of animals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MyYVRhrC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. S. Negi, 2005b)","plainCitation":"(C. S. Negi, 2005b)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32956,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L78LT9ME"],"itemData":{"id":32956,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science & Management","DOI":"10.1080/17451590509618083","issue":"2","language":"fr","page":"85–96","title":"Religion and Biodiversity Conservation: Not a Mere Analogy","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Negi","given":"Chandra Singh"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Hinduism, several wild animals are considered sacred and the followers are generally not allowed to kill or harm those animals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sttfiWic","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kandari, 2014)","plainCitation":"(Kandari, 2014)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32954,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EU5N9N7V"],"itemData":{"id":32954,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Systems Research","issue":"1","language":"fr","page":"1–10","title":"Conservation and management of sacred groves, myths and beliefs of tribal communities: a case study from north-India","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Kandari","given":"L.S."},{"family":"Bisht","given":"V.K."},{"family":"Bhardwaj","given":"M."},{"family":"Thakur","given":"A.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Hindu theology, God exists in every living being and every animal has the equal right to live. Likewise, in Buddhism, certain wild species, like elephants, enjoy special privilege and recognition. Other religions too, such as Jainism, promote the idea of ahimsa (nonviolence) toward human beings and all creations, and harming or hunting wild species is strictly prohibited.
      There is strong evidence that religious beliefs and animism promoted sustainable use of wild resources by mainly controlling human practices of extractive use of wild species, especially hunting of animals even in the most unfavorable situations. For instance, according to the local belief, a giant crocodile formed the island of Timor in Timor-Leste, which restricts the killing of saltwater crocodile in the islands despite recurring attacks and crocodile hunting in near-by islands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"CzExzOyJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Brackhane, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Brackhane, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32715,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MRU5AWN5"],"itemData":{"id":32715,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"In northern Australia and nearby Timor-Leste, saltwater crocodile (Crocodylus porosus) populations were seriously depleted historically but recovered rapidly after protection: 1969–1974 in northern Australia, and 2000–2005 in Timor-Leste. In both places, recovery caused increased rates of humancrocodile con?ict (HCC). Within northern Australia, the crocodile recovery and HCC have been documented over time. In contrast, this has not been the situation in Timor-Leste, where we investigated HCC based on 130 attack records (1996–2014; 52% fatal). In 1996–2006, 0.55 attacks/year were reported in Timor-Leste. By 2007–2014, 9 years later, a 23-fold increase had occurred (13 attacks/year). Traditional subsistence ?shing (82.5% of all attack records) is the highest risk activity, followed by bathing (7.5%) and water collecting (4.2%). Although the human population was correlated with crocodile attacks in TimorLeste, it likely does not explain the dramatic increase in crocodile attacks. Alternatively, crocodile numbers may have increased, either in the remnant resident crocodile population, or via migrants from elsewhere. Permanent crocodile habitat is limited, and limited breeding does not explain the high number of large crocodiles, and consequent increase in attacks in such a short time. A plausible explanation, consistent with traditional knowledge in Timor-Leste, is that the in?ux of large crocodiles attacking people are migrants from Australia. We examined this possibility from available sources. Within Australia crocodiles have recovered since protection and they regularly invade adjacent habitats, such as Darwin harbor, where they are removed to prevent attacks on people. Saltwater crocodiles have been sighted at oil rigs, in the open ocean, moving between northern Australia and the south coast of Timor-Leste. The likelihood of crocodiles migrating from Australia to Timor-Leste raises obvious conservation, moral, and ethical dilemmas when conserving a large dangerous predator in one country to increase abundance results in dispersal to another country, where the predator attacks and kills people. Ó 2018 The Wildlife Society.","container-title":"The Journal of Wildlife Management","DOI":"10.1002/jwmg.21497","ISSN":"0022541X","issue":"7","journalAbbreviation":"Jour. Wild. Mgmt.","language":"en","page":"1332-1344","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"When conservation becomes dangerous: Human-Crocodile conflict in Timor-Leste: Crocodile Dispersal","title-short":"When conservation becomes dangerous","URL":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jwmg.21497","volume":"82","author":[{"family":"Brackhane","given":"Sebastian"},{"family":"Webb","given":"Grahame"},{"family":"Xavier","given":"Flaminio M.E."},{"family":"Gusmao","given":"Marcal"},{"family":"Pechacek","given":"Peter"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",10,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",9]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Sumatra, despite hostile human-wild species conflicts, local communities do not kill or harm tigers as the communities consider tigers as spirit tigers or as an enforcer of moral rule ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"PRk3sQVV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(McKay, 2018)","plainCitation":"(McKay, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32957,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5NRL9FVA"],"itemData":{"id":32957,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PloS one","issue":"11","language":"en","page":"0201447","title":"Tolerating tigers: Gaining local and spiritual perspectives on human-tiger interactions in Sumatra through rural community interviews","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"McKay","given":"J.E."},{"family":"St. John","given":"F.A."},{"family":"Harihar","given":"A."},{"family":"Martyr","given":"D."},{"family":"Leader-Williams","given":"N."},{"family":"Milliyanawati","given":"B."},{"family":"Linkie","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In coastal Bangladesh, local religious beliefs have protected the Black Soft-shell Turtle, Mugger Crocodile, Rock Pigeon and Rhesus Macaque ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zL1zzsZY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mukul, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Mukul, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32968,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VU67IIN5"],"itemData":{"id":32968,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"issue: 2\\npage: 108–14\\nvolume: 13\\nDOI: 10.1080/14888386.2012.694596","title":"The Role of Spiritual Beliefs in Conserving Wildlife Species in Religious Shrines of Bangladesh","author":[{"family":"Mukul","given":"Ahmed"},{"family":"Manzoor","given":"A.Z.M."},{"family":"Cd","given":"Rashid"},{"family":"Uddin","given":"Mohammad Belal"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Ghana, local religious beliefs consider monkeys as ‘untouchable children of God’ and communities refrained from the killing of monkeys, while other species significantly declined ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oPvcpRMH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Attuquayefio & Gyampoh, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Attuquayefio & Gyampoh, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32910,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N5A338LY"],"itemData":{"id":32910,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"West African Journal of Applied Ecology","language":"en","page":"1–10","title":"The Boabeng-Fiema Monkey Sanctuary, Ghana: A Case for Blending Traditional and Introduced Wildlife Conservation Systems","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Attuquayefio","given":"D.K."},{"family":"Gyampoh","given":"Sandra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Golestan National Park, Iran, a sharp decline of mammals was reported due to illegal hunting. Only wild boar (Sus scrofa) showed a population increase because of religious sanctions not to hunt or eat those ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8bKcCcCl","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ghoddousi, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Ghoddousi, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32943,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9V9KZXML"],"itemData":{"id":32943,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"ORYX","DOI":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85017472421&doi=10.1017%2FS003060531600154X&partnerID=40&md5=75fb4cfe4cc6b5f86394c266a0f3a0a","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"151–58","title":"The Decline of Ungulate Populations in Iranian Protected Areas Calls for Urgent Action against Poaching","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Ghoddousi","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Strong evidence is available that religious sanctions can prevent poaching and uncontrolled exploitation of wild species, and thus promote sustainable use. For instance, in 2014, Indonesian Islamic clerics issued a fatwa against the illegal hunting or harvesting of wild species, which immensely helped in the conservation of Asian Elephant. Broader religious efforts such as, Sacred Earth: Faiths for Conservation, engage religious leaders to collaborate in the protection of biodiversity. However, some religious beliefs can also drive unsustainable use of wild species. For example, a study in Singapore indicated that certain beliefs attached to traditional Chinese medicines facilitated unsustainable wild species trade for Saiga – a critically endangered antelope from Central Asia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DWOMAPfd","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Doughty, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Doughty, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32944,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9WW87Q3M"],"itemData":{"id":32944,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"PloS one","issue":"9","language":"en","page":"0222038","title":"Saiga horn user characteristics, motivations, and purchasing behaviour in Singapore","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Doughty","given":"H."},{"family":"Veríssimo","given":"D."},{"family":"Tan","given":"R.C.Q."},{"family":"Lee","given":"J.S.H."},{"family":"Carrasco","given":"L.R."},{"family":"Oliver","given":"K."},{"family":"Milner-Gulland","given":"E.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Most of the Saiga users were characterized as middle-aged Buddhists and Taoists, who believed in miracle cures. Nonetheless, literature indicate a generally positive role of religion in promoting sustainable use of wild species. 
      The importance of religious beliefs in promoting sustainable use of wild species is incomplete without the mention of sacred groves. Sacred natural sites are identified as sites that protect nature and wild species by restricting anthropogenic disturbances due to religious sanctions. Sacred sites are religiously protected territories, the oldest form of conservation, which also find its mention in all the major religious scriptures, including the Holy Vedas and the Holy Quran. Consequently, a large number of self-imposed bio-divinity directives work in these sites. There is substantial evidence that religious beliefs associated with sacred sites halted deforestation of primary forests and in general, promoted non-extractive use of both flora and faunal species. Communities have traditionally attached great importance to sacred forests, and principally used these forests for religious tourism, watching, praying, meditation and healing purposes . Examples of sustainable use of sacred groves and its floral and faunal species can be found throughout the world, starting from Sub-Saharan Africa, parts of Europe, South America, South and Southeast Asia ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RCznK5pJ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kandari, 2014; C. S. Negi, 2005b; Pungetti, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Kandari, 2014; C. S. Negi, 2005b; Pungetti, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32954,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EU5N9N7V"],"itemData":{"id":32954,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Systems Research","issue":"1","language":"fr","page":"1–10","title":"Conservation and management of sacred groves, myths and beliefs of tribal communities: a case study from north-India","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Kandari","given":"L.S."},{"family":"Bisht","given":"V.K."},{"family":"Bhardwaj","given":"M."},{"family":"Thakur","given":"A.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":32956,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L78LT9ME"],"itemData":{"id":32956,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Biodiversity Science & Management","DOI":"10.1080/17451590509618083","issue":"2","language":"fr","page":"85–96","title":"Religion and Biodiversity Conservation: Not a Mere Analogy","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Negi","given":"Chandra Singh"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32966,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/L2FRHSQ3"],"itemData":{"id":32966,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Sacred Species and Sites : Advances in Biocultural Conservation","author":[{"family":"Pungetti","given":"Gloria"},{"family":"Oviedo","given":"Gonzalo"},{"family":"Hooke","given":"Della"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. However, sacred groves are predominant in some countries, for example, in India, Ghana and Nigeria. Over 13,270 sacred groves have been reported from India alone, representing different faiths, including both traditional and temple forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pgEHqCRt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ormsby & Bhagwat, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Ormsby & Bhagwat, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32978,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MVTTLAXJ"],"itemData":{"id":32978,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892910000561","issue":"3","language":"fr","page":"320–326","title":"Sacred forests of India: A strong tradition of community-based natural resource management","volume":"37","author":[{"family":"Ormsby","given":"A."},{"family":"Bhagwat","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ghana, on the other hand, has more than 1900 sacred groves, along with a long history of cultural forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"63tlLZYp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ormsby, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Ormsby, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32967,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VIG4L7KB"],"itemData":{"id":32967,"type":"book","event-place":"Cambridge, UK","language":"en","number-of-pages":"335–350","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge, UK","title":"Cultural and conservation values of sacred forests in Ghana. Sacred species and sites: advances in biocultural conservation","URL":"http://dx.","volume":"32","author":[{"family":"Ormsby","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Commensurate with these numbers, the number of peer-reviewed articles reporting multiple use of sacred groves from these countries are considerably high. As such, religious sanctions, beliefs, myths and taboos pertaining to the use of certain plants and hunting ban of animals, are the primary determinant behind sustainable use within the sacred groves.  For instance, in Ethiopia, Orthodox Tewahido Church communities have ensured the protection of church forests over centuries despite widespread deforestation in adjacent forests ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"KRRJbBMp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aerts, 2016; Klepeis, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Aerts, 2016; Klepeis, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32946,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XPTTGU7X"],"itemData":{"id":32946,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science of The Total Environment","DOI":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0048969716302467","language":"en","page":"404–14","title":"Conservation of the Ethiopian Church Forests: Threats, Opportunities and Implications for Their Management","volume":"551–552","author":[{"family":"Aerts","given":"Raf"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32953,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7E449EF5"],"itemData":{"id":32953,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","DOI":"10.1007/s10745-016-9868-z","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"715–30","title":"Ethiopian Church Forests: A Hybrid Model of Protection","volume":"44","author":[{"family":"Klepeis","given":"Peter"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Similarly, Bishnoi cult of western India protect sacred groves and its species, locally known as ‘Orans’, which are dedicated to the local deity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4RUFG2KI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Kala & Sharma, 2010)","plainCitation":"(M. Kala & Sharma, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32958,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A98NXX7A"],"itemData":{"id":32958,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Environmentalist","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"85–89","title":"Traditional Indian beliefs: a key toward sustainable living","volume":"30","author":[{"family":"Kala","given":"M."},{"family":"Sharma","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Ghana, sacred groves were used as the refuge for wild species breeding ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"K8FBOb5k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L. W. Robinson & Sasu, 2013)","plainCitation":"(L. W. Robinson & Sasu, 2013)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32963,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ADDWQGRC"],"itemData":{"id":32963,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental Values","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"647–6664","title":"The role of values in a community-based conservation initiative in northern Ghana","volume":"22","author":[{"family":"Robinson","given":"L.W."},{"family":"Sasu","given":"K.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. It is evident that religious protection significantly enhanced biodiversity, in particular plants diversity within sacred groves as compared to adjacent forests and protected areas. Informal networks of sacred groves also played a critical role in maintaining plant diversity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cvSilzyV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bhagwat, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Bhagwat, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32949,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q7HNPIGZ"],"itemData":{"id":32949,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1853–62","title":"A Landscape Approach to Biodiversity Conservation of Sacred Groves in the Western Ghats of India","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Bhagwat","given":"Shonil A."},{"family":"Kushalappa","given":"Cheppudira G."},{"family":"Williams","given":"Paul H."},{"family":"Brown","given":"Nick D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For instance, the sacred natural site in Jaintia Hills in India gained widespread attention when just 0.5 ha of sacred grove was reported to have 82 tree species, out of which 54 are endemic species and 31 are rare species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"oshXOIFI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Jamir & Pandey, 2003; Upadhyay, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Jamir & Pandey, 2003; Upadhyay, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32959,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/622MF7MS"],"itemData":{"id":32959,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biodiversity & Conservation","issue":"7","language":"en","page":"1497–1510","title":"Vascular plant diversity in the sacred groves of Jaintia Hills in northeast India","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Jamir","given":"S.A."},{"family":"Pandey","given":"H.N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":32938,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B897QZV7"],"itemData":{"id":32938,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"International Journal of Work Organisation and Emotion","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"339–356","title":"Workplace spirituality and subjective happiness at higher educational institutions: an Indian perspective","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Upadhyay","given":"R.K."},{"family":"Mishra","given":"A.K."},{"family":"Jain","given":"V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Some floral species, however, are typically favoured for sacred groves. For instance, in India, banyan tree (Ficus bengalensis), Peepul (Ficus religiosa), wood apple (Aegale marmelos) and the neem tree (Azardirachta indica) are typically considered highly sacred and often used for ceremonial purposes. Felling of these trees are often considered amongst heinous religious crimes. In particular, large old trees are often considered keystone ecological entities within the sacred groves. Sacred groves have contributed to improve the quality of life for people not only in the traditional communities, but also many people used these areas as refuge from stressful urban life. Within the extractive use, sacred groves worldwide continue to serve as rich repositories for traditional medicines (well-established). Medicinal and therapeutic uses of sacred groves are particularly common in Asia and Africa, where several trees and their parts are used as traditional medicine. For the traditional societies, the need for traditional medicine is one of the primary causes of maintaining sacred groves ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MbKBj9Yi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Adeniyi, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Adeniyi, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32926,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3AEY3J5T"],"itemData":{"id":32926,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"page: 76–87","title":"Ethnobotanical study of medicinal plants from Ghana; confirmation of ethnobotanical uses, and review of biological and toxicological studies on medicinal plants used in Apra Hills Sacred Grove. Journal of herbal medicine, 14","author":[{"family":"Adeniyi","given":"A."},{"family":"Asase","given":"A."},{"family":"Ekpe","given":"P.K."},{"family":"Asitoakor","given":"B.K."},{"family":"Adu-Gyamfi","given":"A."},{"family":"Avekor","given":"P.Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Apart from medicinal plants, these also serve as natural pesticides and sacred ingredients widely used in cultural and ceremonial activities. Yet, despite their bio-cultural significance, the spatial extent of sacred groves is rapidly shrinking. A number of factors, including rapid urbanization, globalization and transition to a market-based economy (e.g., rising land prices, land demand) have changed the core religious values and perceptions of local communities, eroding the emotional affinity and appreciation for wild species. Furthermore, current priorities supporting rapid economic development have conferred lesser importance to sacred sites ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JUmYvJAg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. Xu, 2012b)","plainCitation":"(P. Xu, 2012b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":6731,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZSBVF4W3"],"itemData":{"id":6731,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"As the world's largest seafood consumer and exporter, China is challenged by frequent seafood contamination incidents. To restore consumer confidence in seafood safety, China's Ministry of Agriculture (MOA) mandated a nation wide quality standard that awards a green label to qualified safer seafood. MOA is also planning for an environmental friendly label to address consumers' concerns about wild sea species sustainability. This study developed a three-stage purchase framework model and applied a multivariate Probit regression to analyze questionnaire information collected from 14 supermarkets in Beijing, China. The results show that Chinese consumers consider the seafood label a more important information source than previous consumption experience. They are willing to pay more for green-labeled seafood for the protection of individual benefits. Moreover, consumers are willing to pay more for the eco-labeled seafood for the protection of societal benefits. Gender, shopping venues, education, seafood expenditure and knowledge of the labeled products affected purchase intention and willingness to pay. Price was not a statistically significant factor affecting purchase decisions. © 2012 Elsevier Ltd.","container-title":"Food Control","DOI":"10.1016/j.foodcont.2012.04.008","ISSN":"09567135","issue":"1","page":"74–82","title":"Chinese consumers' willingness to pay for green- and eco-labeled seafood","URL":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.foodcont.2012.04.008","volume":"28","author":[{"family":"Xu","given":"Pei"},{"family":"Zeng","given":"Yinchu"},{"family":"Fong","given":"Quentin"},{"family":"Lone","given":"Todd"},{"family":"Liu","given":"Yuanyuan"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}},"label":"page"}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition, many of the traditional religious practices are rapidly diminishing, due to conversion and changes in religion, particularly religion with smaller number of followers are vanishing quickly. In fact, formal world religions are also experiencing losses of population share and this might have caused annihilation of several ethno-religious practices. There is also a frequent syncretism, for instance in Africa, between “ancient” religion (animism, totemism, etc.) and revealed religion (Islam, Christianity). If the maintenance of customary rites and preservation of the ancestors’ heritage are guaranteed, the links to wild species could be reinforcing ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"C874Q3NR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bassett & Cormier-Salem, 2007; Dugast, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Bassett & Cormier-Salem, 2007; Dugast, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32907,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5JETNMH9"],"itemData":{"id":32907,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Africa","issue":"1","language":"it","page":"150","title":"Nature as Local Heritage in Africa","volume":"77","author":[{"family":"Bassett","given":"T.J."},{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":32983,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NCVWYXZ2"],"itemData":{"id":32983,"type":"document","language":"fr","note":"publisher-place: Paris","publisher":"IRD","title":"Modes d'appréhension de la nature et gestion patrimoniale du miieu\\".In M.C","author":[{"family":"Dugast","given":"S."}],"editor":[{"literal":"Cormier-Salem"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Information pertaining to these are not rigorously documented, however, significant evidence suggests that religious beliefs are vanishing due to the materialistic transitions of human societies, while global mobility and technology has led to cultural homogenization of human societies. 
      
Figure 4.12. The figure summarizes different feedback loops that emerge from the specific literature review conducted for above sub-sections. It outlines the three pillars of bio-cultural diversity and how it promoted sustainable/unsustainable use of wild species. The darker lines signify strength of the relationship. Confidence levels are mentioned wherever possible. License CC-BY. 


4.2.5.2.3. Taboos and traditional belief systems
Taboos are usually social rules that are not written but regulate human behavior. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"G8ImW8Ir","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Adler, 1998; L\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}vi-Strauss, 1962a)","plainCitation":"(Adler, 1998; Lévi-Strauss, 1962a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32984,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5RXD7XUT"],"itemData":{"id":32984,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Systèmes de pensée en Afrique noire","language":"fr","page":"13–107","title":"Le totémisme en Afrique noire","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Adler","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":32982,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y9GFPVVY"],"itemData":{"id":32982,"type":"document","language":"fr","note":"publisher-place: Paris","title":"Le totémisme aujourd’hui","author":[{"family":"Lévi-Strauss","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1962"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} Such limitations not only govern human social life, but also may affect, and sometimes even directly manage, many constituents of the local natural environment. Whatever the reason for such constraints, taboos, at least locally, play a major role in the protection of natural resources, species, and ecosystems ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hh4z7MHy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Begossi, 2004; M. Gadgil, 1987; M. B. Gadgil, 1993; Johannes, 1978, 1981, 1982, 1984)","plainCitation":"(A. Begossi, 2004; M. Gadgil, 1987; M. B. Gadgil, 1993; Johannes, 1978, 1981, 1982, 1984)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32908,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XKIN8UUU"],"itemData":{"id":32908,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"1334–1343","title":"Food Chain and the Reasons for Fish Food Taboos among Amazonian and Atlantic Forest Fishers (Brazil","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Begossi","given":"A."},{"family":"Hanazaki","given":"N."},{"family":"Ramos","given":"R.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":32986,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MNEXFEAH"],"itemData":{"id":32986,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Development: seeds of change","journalAbbreviation":"Development: seeds of change","page":"26-30","title":"Social restraints on exploiting nature: the Indian experience","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Gadgil","given":"Madhav"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1987"]]}}},{"id":866,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YAGA3CHZ"],"itemData":{"id":866,"type":"book","event-place":"Biodiversity","language":"en","number-of-pages":"151–156","publisher":"Ecology","publisher-place":"Biodiversity","title":"Indigenous Knowledge for Biodiversity Conservation Ambio: 22: 2/3","author":[{"family":"Gadgil","given":"M.Berkes"},{"family":"F.","given":"Folke"},{"literal":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}},{"id":32925,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HKWD637A"],"itemData":{"id":32925,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Ecology and","language":"en","page":"349–364","title":"Traditional marine conservation methods in Oceania and their demise","volume":"Systematics 9","author":[{"family":"Johannes","given":"R.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1978"]]}}},{"id":32714,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H9EVID4H"],"itemData":{"id":32714,"type":"book","event-place":"Berkeley/Los Angeles/London","ISBN":"0-520-03929-7","language":"English","number-of-pages":"245","publisher":"University of California Press","publisher-place":"Berkeley/Los Angeles/London","title":"Words of the lagoon: fishing and marine lore in the Palau district of Micronesia","URL":"https://books.google.fr/books?id=TloVDfV7QLoC&pg=PR3&hl=fr&source=gbs_selected_pages&cad=3#v=onepage&q&f=false","author":[{"family":"Johannes","given":"Robert Earle"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1981"]]}}},{"id":32941,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CQ2H8CCQ"],"itemData":{"id":32941,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"Ambio","title":"Traditional conservation methods and protected marine areas in Oceania","author":[{"family":"Johannes","given":"R.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1982"]]}}},{"id":32939,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/M9GTK6VN"],"itemData":{"id":32939,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Environmentalist","language":"en","page":"30–35","title":"7. Marine conservation in relation to traditional life-styles of tropical artisanal fishermen","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Johannes","given":"R.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1984"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Taboos resemble mechanisms for the protection of species and habitats in present-day society, but they have other social rules and sanctions, rooted deep in traditional belief systems. In marine ecosystems, taboos are imposed on specific marine areas to prevent overexploitation of aquatic resources ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8vUGVyWG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Chapman, 1985; Johannes, 1978)","plainCitation":"(M. Chapman, 1985; Johannes, 1978)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32932,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/87N56843"],"itemData":{"id":32932,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Environmental","language":"en","page":"217–230","title":"Environmental influences on the development of traditional conservation in the South Pacific region","volume":"Conservation 12","author":[{"family":"Chapman","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1985"]]}}},{"id":32925,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HKWD637A"],"itemData":{"id":32925,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Annual Review of Ecology and","language":"en","page":"349–364","title":"Traditional marine conservation methods in Oceania and their demise","volume":"Systematics 9","author":[{"family":"Johannes","given":"R.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1978"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Taboos are not always permanent in time and space but can be removed when food resources are plentiful. Taboos that directly result in management of natural resources are reportedly found among traditional groups from several parts of the world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VFTgP3zv","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(A. Begossi & de Souza Braga, 1992; A. Begossi & Souza Braga, 1992; Kwapena, 1984; Reichel-Dolmatoff, 1971; Sankhala, 1993; Sarkar, 1984)","plainCitation":"(A. Begossi & de Souza Braga, 1992; A. Begossi & Souza Braga, 1992; Kwapena, 1984; Reichel-Dolmatoff, 1971; Sankhala, 1993; Sarkar, 1984)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32987,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SJPF3RBE"],"itemData":{"id":32987,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Amazoniana: Limnologia et Oecologia Regionalis Systematis Fluminis Amazonas","ISSN":"0065-6755","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Amazoniana: Limnologia et Oecologia Regionalis Systematis Fluminis Amazonas","note":"number: 1\\npublisher: Kommissions-Verlag WG Mühlau","page":"101-118","title":"Food taboos and folk medicine among fishermen from the Tocantins River (Brazil)","volume":"12","author":[{"family":"Begossi","given":"Alpina"},{"family":"Souza Braga","given":"Francisco Manoel","non-dropping-particle":"de"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}},{"id":32931,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/97ZZ9JV9"],"itemData":{"id":32931,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"page: 101–118\\npublisher-place: Amazoniana XII","title":"Food taboos and folk medicine among fishermen from the Tocantins River (Brazil","author":[{"family":"Begossi","given":"A."},{"family":"Souza Braga","given":"F.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}},{"id":32936,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XGJGYR6P"],"itemData":{"id":32936,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The","issue":"supplement 7","language":"en","page":"22–26","title":"Traditional conservation and utilization of wildlife in Papua New Guinea","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Kwapena","given":"N."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1984"]]}}},{"id":32937,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DYVJG568"],"itemData":{"id":32937,"type":"book","event-place":"Chicago, Illinois, USA","language":"en","publisher":"Indians. University of Chicago Press","publisher-place":"Chicago, Illinois, USA","title":"Amazonian cosmos: the sexual and religious symbolism of the Tukano","author":[{"family":"Reichel-Dolmatoff","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1971"]]}}},{"id":32930,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HAKR6EJH"],"itemData":{"id":32930,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Indigenous peoples and protected areas. Earthscan","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: London, UK","page":"18–23","title":"Prospering from the desert","author":[{"family":"Sankhala","given":"K."}],"editor":[{"family":"Kemf","given":"E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}},{"id":32935,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7NYCS62R"],"itemData":{"id":32935,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The fishing culture of the world. Akadéiai Kiadó","event-place":"Budapest, Hungary","language":"en","publisher-place":"Budapest, Hungary","title":"Significance of fish in Bengalee Hindu folk culture. Pages 705-725 in B","author":[{"family":"Sarkar","given":"S.R."}],"editor":[{"literal":"Gunda"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1984"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These regulations may have been the outcome of a trial?and?error process of resource management strategies like those of the contemporary practice of adaptive management ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"SQ9ixyX5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Holling, 1978; C. J. Walters, 1986)","plainCitation":"(Holling, 1978; C. J. Walters, 1986)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32934,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I2CUFQEC"],"itemData":{"id":32934,"type":"document","language":"fr","note":"publisher-place: London, UK","title":"Adaptive environmental assessment and mangement. Wiley","author":[{"family":"Holling","given":"C.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1978"]]}}},{"id":32933,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/A9BFL8HW"],"itemData":{"id":32933,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: New York, New York, USA","title":"Adaptive management of renewable resources. McGraw-Hill","author":[{"family":"Walters","given":"C.J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1986"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example, ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XQLToqPp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 1997)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32929,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JKPK29ST"],"itemData":{"id":32929,"type":"book","event-place":"Cambridge, UK","language":"en","publisher":"resilience. Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambridge, UK","title":"Indigenous knowledge and resource management systems in the Canadian subarctic. Pages xxx-xxx in F","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."}],"editor":[{"literal":"Berkes"},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} argues that periods of mismanagement of North American caribou among the Cree in the 1900s resulted, in part, from a neglect of traditional hunting rules. After a change in Cree hunting behavior, the caribou population returned to previous levels. In the same manner, taboos may be employed as a social mechanism for the enforcement of ecologically adaptive behavior, even though different cultural contexts are attached to them. Taboos protect not only threatened and endemic species, but also keystone species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"86uPsdLe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Colding & Folke, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Colding & Folke, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34269,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XAJW4AG7"],"itemData":{"id":34269,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Colding, J. and C. Folke 1997. The Relations Among Threatened Species, Their Protection, and Taboos. Conservation Ecology 1(1):6. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-00018-010106","container-title":"Conservation Ecology","DOI":"10.5751/ES-00018-010106","ISSN":"1195-5449","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"© 1997 by the author(s)","title":"The Relations Among Threatened Species, Their Protection, and Taboos","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol1/iss1/art6/","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Colding","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]],"season":"juin"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      A study conducted by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UAc3AR9Y","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Colding & Folke, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Colding & Folke, 1997)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34269,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XAJW4AG7"],"itemData":{"id":34269,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Colding, J. and C. Folke 1997. The Relations Among Threatened Species, Their Protection, and Taboos. Conservation Ecology 1(1):6. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-00018-010106","container-title":"Conservation Ecology","DOI":"10.5751/ES-00018-010106","ISSN":"1195-5449","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"© 1997 by the author(s)","title":"The Relations Among Threatened Species, Their Protection, and Taboos","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol1/iss1/art6/","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Colding","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]],"season":"juin"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} identified 70 species-specific taboos which included both flora and fauna throughout the world. They found that nearly 30% of the identified taboos ban any use of species listed as threatened by the International Union for Conservation of Nature. Of the species-specific taboos, 60% are directed at reptiles and mammals. In these two classes, nearly 50% of the species are threatened, representing all the threatened species in analysis, except for one bird species. Both endemic and keystone species that are important for ecosystem function are avoided by species-specific taboos (Figure 4.13). These reviews indicate that species specific taboos have significant ecological ramifications for the protection of threatened and ecologically important populations of species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"EZXxl8HD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Colding & Folke, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Colding & Folke, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34269,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XAJW4AG7"],"itemData":{"id":34269,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Colding, J. and C. Folke 1997. The Relations Among Threatened Species, Their Protection, and Taboos. Conservation Ecology 1(1):6. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-00018-010106","container-title":"Conservation Ecology","DOI":"10.5751/ES-00018-010106","ISSN":"1195-5449","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"© 1997 by the author(s)","title":"The Relations Among Threatened Species, Their Protection, and Taboos","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol1/iss1/art6/","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Colding","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]],"season":"juin"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      

Figure 4.13. Class distribution of avoided and threatened species. Source: adapted from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vyEqShWY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Colding & Folke, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Colding & Folke, 1997)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34269,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XAJW4AG7"],"itemData":{"id":34269,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Colding, J. and C. Folke 1997. The Relations Among Threatened Species, Their Protection, and Taboos. Conservation Ecology 1(1):6. https://doi.org/10.5751/ES-00018-010106","container-title":"Conservation Ecology","DOI":"10.5751/ES-00018-010106","ISSN":"1195-5449","issue":"1","language":"en","license":"© 1997 by the author(s)","title":"The Relations Among Threatened Species, Their Protection, and Taboos","URL":"https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol1/iss1/art6/","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Colding","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]],"season":"juin"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. © 1997 by The Resilience Alliance. License: CC BY-NC 4.0.

Box 4.32. The fading taboo 

Bonobo (Pan paniscus), endemic to the Democratic Republic of Congo (Zaire) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IulU3JdG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Badrian & Malenky, 1984; J. M. Thompson, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Badrian & Malenky, 1984; J. M. Thompson, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32912,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ALQHRQR5"],"itemData":{"id":32912,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The Pygmy Chimpanzee","event-place":"New York, London","language":"en","page":"275–299","publisher":"Plenum Press","publisher-place":"New York, London","title":"Feeding ecology of Pan paniscus in the Lomako Forest, Zaire","author":[{"family":"Badrian","given":"N."},{"family":"Malenky","given":"R.K."}],"editor":[{"family":"Sussman","given":"R.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1984"]]}}},{"id":32928,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z8ISUT7C"],"itemData":{"id":32928,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The Bonobos","event-place":"New York, NY","language":"en","page":"227–244","publisher":"Springer","publisher-place":"New York, NY","title":"Traditional land-use practices for bonobo conservation","author":[{"family":"Thompson","given":"J.M."},{"family":"Nestor","given":"L.M."},{"family":"Kabanda","given":"R.B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} is an endangered species, included under Appendix I of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora, and is protected by the Democratic Republic of Cong government. Not hunting or and eating bonobo has been a traditional taboo held by the ethnic group (Bongando) that live in Wamba region of Democratic Republic of Cong. In Bongando folk taxonomy, bonobos are considered not as wild species, but as human beings. The resemblance of bonobo bodily characteristics and behaviours to those of humans is the key reason for this categorization and this similarity has helped to conserve this endangered species. Social and cultural interchanges with other ethnic groups are altering he tradition of “folk conservation.” Research indicates that although this taboo is persisting in older generations, some youth have begun to eat bonobo meat ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Oz3MDpLZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lingomo & Kimura, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Lingomo & Kimura, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32905,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TB8BZ92Y"],"itemData":{"id":32905,"type":"report","abstract":"Among the Bongando, an ethnic group living in and around the village of Wamba, a well-known base for research on the bonobo (Pan paniscus (() in the Democratic Republic of Congo, it has traditionally been taboo to eat bonobo. In Bongando folk taxonomy, bonobos are categorized not as animals, but as human beings. The resemblance of bonobo bodily characteristics and behaviors to those of humans is the main reason for this categoriza-tion. While this recognition has helped in the conservation of this endangered species, social and cultural interchanges with other ethnic groups are changing the tradition of \\"folk conservation .\\" Through interviews with villagers, we investigated changes in the eating of bonobo meat. This taboo persists in older generations, whereas a certain number of younger people have begun to eat bonobo meat. With regards to this change, bonobo conservation and the meaning of food taboo as a \\"cultural resource\\" is discussed.","number":"4","page":"209–225","title":"Taboo of eating bobobo among the bongando people in the wamba region, Democratic Repblic of Congo.","author":[{"family":"Lingomo & Kimura","given":"Lingomo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The taboo is weakening due to numerous influences including, economic incentives, foreigners who choose to eat bonobo meat, decrease in the availability of other game, and the influence of, and hardships caused by, the civil war. To avoid incurring the wrath of God and the ancestral spirits, people make food offerings in the hopes of obtaining game and other wild meat ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8CF8psBi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lingomo & Kimura, 2009; Tashiro, 1995)","plainCitation":"(Lingomo & Kimura, 2009; Tashiro, 1995)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32905,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TB8BZ92Y"],"itemData":{"id":32905,"type":"report","abstract":"Among the Bongando, an ethnic group living in and around the village of Wamba, a well-known base for research on the bonobo (Pan paniscus (() in the Democratic Republic of Congo, it has traditionally been taboo to eat bonobo. In Bongando folk taxonomy, bonobos are categorized not as animals, but as human beings. The resemblance of bonobo bodily characteristics and behaviors to those of humans is the main reason for this categoriza-tion. While this recognition has helped in the conservation of this endangered species, social and cultural interchanges with other ethnic groups are changing the tradition of \\"folk conservation .\\" Through interviews with villagers, we investigated changes in the eating of bonobo meat. This taboo persists in older generations, whereas a certain number of younger people have begun to eat bonobo meat. With regards to this change, bonobo conservation and the meaning of food taboo as a \\"cultural resource\\" is discussed.","number":"4","page":"209–225","title":"Taboo of eating bobobo among the bongando people in the wamba region, Democratic Repblic of Congo.","author":[{"family":"Lingomo & Kimura","given":"Lingomo"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":32922,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FKNNN2MD"],"itemData":{"id":32922,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pan Africa News","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"8–9","title":"Reporls from the Field: Wamba, Zaire: Economic Difficulties in Zaire and the Disappearing Taboo against Hunting Bonobos in the Wamba Area","volume":"2","author":[{"family":"Tashiro","given":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1995"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.


4.2.5.2.4. Local practices and indigenous and local knowledge  
Worldwide, indigenous people number over 370-500 million ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"qydCurnx","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(UNESCO, 2021)","plainCitation":"(UNESCO, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32973,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3GPF67FJ"],"itemData":{"id":32973,"type":"webpage","title":"World Heritage Centre _ Interactive Map","URL":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/interactive-map/","author":[{"family":"UNESCO","given":""}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",8,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. They live in about 75 of the world's 184 countries and are inhabitants of practically each main biome of the earth, with a remarkable overlap between indigenous territories and world’s remaining areas of high biodiversity. According to the United Nations, “indigenous peoples are inheritors and practitioners of unique cultures and ways of relating to people and the environment. They retain social, cultural, economic and political characteristics that are distinct from those of the dominant societies in which they live.” Despite their cultural differences, indigenous peoples of the world share common challenges and are among the most vulnerable people in the world. 
      The term indigenous and local knowledge is broadly defined as the local knowledge held by indigenous peoples ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"AHYMGavk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Posey, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Posey, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7GUX7585"],"itemData":{"id":34548,"type":"book","call-number":"GF80 .C85 1999","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-85339-397-6","publisher":"Intermediate Technology","publisher-place":"London","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Cultural and spiritual values of biodiversity","URL":"file:///C:/Users/MARIE-~1/AppData/Local/Temp/Cultural_Spiritual_thebible.pdf","author":[{"family":"Posey","given":"D. A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indigenous peoples with a historical continuity of resource-use practices often possess a broad information base of the workings of complex ecosystems in their own localities. This accumulated knowledge is passed from generation to generation and generally distilled through their world views, religious affiliations, and customary experiences with nature. Such observations can be of great value and complement the observations on which science is based. The discoveries and inventions of traditional science may not be fully understood by science and, as ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ME3js9xD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}vi-Strauss, 1962b)","plainCitation":"(Lévi-Strauss, 1962b)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33004,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4947HFP8"],"itemData":{"id":33004,"type":"document","language":"fr","note":"publisher-place: Paris","title":"Le totémisme aujourd’hui","author":[{"family":"Lévi-Strauss","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1962"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} suggests, traditional science may be able to perceive and anticipate the discoveries of science. As indigenous peoples have depended on the local environments for a long expanse of time for a variety of resources, they have developed a stake in conserving, and in many cases, enhancing biodiversity. Their practices for the preservation of biodiversity were grounded in a progression of general guidelines, which emerged through experimentation over a lengthy timespan ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IJqpokR2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}vi-Strauss, 1962b)","plainCitation":"(Lévi-Strauss, 1962b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33004,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4947HFP8"],"itemData":{"id":33004,"type":"document","language":"fr","note":"publisher-place: Paris","title":"Le totémisme aujourd’hui","author":[{"family":"Lévi-Strauss","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1962"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This implies that their knowledge base is specific, and their implementation involves an intimate relationship with their belief system. 
      Due to the influence of different world views and a belief system of cosmovision, such knowledge is often difficult to fit into the parameters of science. While modern hegemonic science is based in concepts, traditional science is based on perception.  To realize sustainable use of wild species, it is critical however, that the value of the knowledge-practice-belief systems of indigenous people are recognized, respected and utilized in the design and implementation of land use and resource management systems. Some societies, such as the Peruvian Yagua, are considered cultural conservationists. Their ideology involves limited exploitation of natural resources, as human beings are perceived as sustainers of the equilibrium of the universe, including the natural and supernatural. Their values, interdictions on food and hunting, and institutional or supernatural sanctions, guide them to act according to their ideology ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"M5OZk6rt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cunha & Almeida, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Cunha & Almeida, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32942,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VUXVWH36"],"itemData":{"id":32942,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Daedalus","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"315–338","title":"Indigenous people, traditional people, and conservation in the Amazon","volume":"129","author":[{"family":"Cunha","given":"Carneiro","dropping-particle":"da"},{"family":"Almeida","given":"M.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Rituals and offerings are a way to establish communication and a relation of reciprocity with nature. When community members in the Yucatan region of Mexico prepare the land for cultivation, for example, they touch the soil and ask for permission to plant. They engage in rituals as a demonstration of respect for the earth. Their actions and prayers ensure that soil quality is maintained and that good relations are kept with the deities associated with the soil and land. Such rituals of reciprocity echo those of cultures worldwide and reflect a world view of being one with the earth, rather than separate from it.  This is not a demonstration of dominion over the earth, as is common in western societies and agribusiness, but a sense of belonging to, and kinship with, the natural world. In this way, reciprocity and sustainable management practices are ensured. According to ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZsBvcVnP","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barabas, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Barabas, 2003)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32921,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/57VYH9TE"],"itemData":{"id":32921,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"La comunidad sin límites. Estructura social y organización comunitaria en las regiones indígenas de México","title":"La ética del don en Oaxaca: los sistemas indígenas de reciprocidad","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Barabas","given":"Alicia"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} reciprocity occupies a range of behaviors from altruism to deferred exchange. A force in all aspects of indigenous life, reciprocity is central to a value system that envisions humans in relationship with both the tangible and intangible worlds. 

      4.2.5.2.5. Management and indigenous and local knowledge 
Effective and sustainable management of populations of flora and fauna needs quantifiable information on rates of harvest, survival, and reproduction, as well as data on population size, habitat, and demographics to ensure that harvest does not endanger the survival of populations ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"8uiIKdvo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lebreton, 1992; Ludwig, 1993)","plainCitation":"(Lebreton, 1992; Ludwig, 1993)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32732,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VTLB7HWU"],"itemData":{"id":32732,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Monographs","DOI":"10.2307/2937171","language":"en","page":"67–118","title":"Modeling Survival and Testing Biological Hypotheses Using Marked Animals: A Unified Approach with Case Studies","volume":"62","author":[{"family":"Lebreton","given":"J."},{"family":"Burnham","given":"K.P."},{"family":"Clobert","given":"J."},{"family":"Anderson","given":"D.R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}},{"id":32735,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B23CMLVL"],"itemData":{"id":32735,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","DOI":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/1942074","issue":"4","language":"fr","page":"548–549","title":"Uncertainty, Resource Exploitation, and Conservation: Lessons from History","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Ludwig","given":"D."},{"family":"Hilborn","given":"R."},{"family":"Walters","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For many populations of wild species, however, scientific information required to gauge sustainable management practices is lacking. Over the last few decades, there has been growing recognition by the research community, that traditional and local ecological knowledge (see Chapter 1) offers a valuable source of information to complement “western scientific approaches” to resource management (e.g., ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OFlnX5yt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2000; Chemilinsky, 1991)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2000; Chemilinsky, 1991)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":847,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4FYYZ8LU"],"itemData":{"id":847,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","DOI":"10.1890/1051-0761(2000)010[1251:ROTEKA]2.0.CO;2","ISSN":"1051-0761","issue":"5","journalAbbreviation":"Ecological Applications","language":"en","page":"1251-1262","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Rediscovery of Traditional Ecological Knowledge as Adaptive Management","URL":"http://doi.wiley.com/10.1890/1051-0761(2000)010[1251:ROTEKA]2.0.CO;2","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"Fikret"},{"family":"Colding","given":"Johan"},{"family":"Folke","given":"Carl"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",10]]}}},{"id":32914,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UKY35V7E"],"itemData":{"id":32914,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Science","page":"226–231","title":"On social science’s contribution to government decision making","volume":"254","author":[{"family":"Chemilinsky","given":"E"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1991"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Because local ecological knowledge is typically derived from people who have lived with, hunted, and gathered wild species over decades, their role in resource management is analogous to “expert opinion” used in population modeling (e.g.,ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yR5rOMNZ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(C. J. Walters & Holling, 1990; Zabel, 2002)","plainCitation":"(C. J. Walters & Holling, 1990; Zabel, 2002)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32734,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/CPH2TJ6S"],"itemData":{"id":32734,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology","DOI":"10.2307/1938620","issue":"6","language":"cs","page":"2060–2068","title":"Large-Scale Management Experiments and Learning by Doing","URL":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1938620","volume":"71","author":[{"family":"Walters","given":"C.J."},{"family":"Holling","given":"C.S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1990"]]}}},{"id":32920,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y4ZJRTJJ"],"itemData":{"id":32920,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Pages 241-253 in J. Michael Scott, Patricia J. Heglund, Michael L. Morrison, Jonathan B. Haufler, Martin G. Raphael, William A. Wall, and Fred B. Samson, editors. Prediction species occurrences: issues of scale and accuracy. Island Press, Covello, CA","page":"241–253","title":"A collaborative approach in adaptive management at a large-landscape scale","author":[{"family":"Zabel","given":"Cynthia J"},{"family":"Roberts","given":"Lynn M"},{"family":"Mulder","given":"Barry S"},{"family":"Stauffer","given":"Howard B"},{"family":"Dunk","given":"Jeffrey R"},{"family":"Wolcott","given":"Kelly"},{"family":"Solis","given":"David"},{"family":"Gertsch","given":"Mike"},{"family":"Woodbridge","given":"Brian"},{"family":"Wright","given":"Adrienne"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ecological knowledge has been applied to various scientific disciplinesITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"eMsgiIeU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. B. Gadgil, 1993; Johannes, 2000; Krupnik & Jolly, 2002)","plainCitation":"(M. B. Gadgil, 1993; Johannes, 2000; Krupnik & Jolly, 2002)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":866,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YAGA3CHZ"],"itemData":{"id":866,"type":"book","event-place":"Biodiversity","language":"en","number-of-pages":"151–156","publisher":"Ecology","publisher-place":"Biodiversity","title":"Indigenous Knowledge for Biodiversity Conservation Ambio: 22: 2/3","author":[{"family":"Gadgil","given":"M.Berkes"},{"family":"F.","given":"Folke"},{"literal":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}},{"id":32988,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BQDAMIJZ"],"itemData":{"id":32988,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","ISSN":"1467-2960","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Fish and Fisheries","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"257-271","title":"Ignore fishers’ knowledge and miss the boat","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Johannes","given":"Robert E"},{"family":"Freeman","given":"Milton MR"},{"family":"Hamilton","given":"Richard J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":32913,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LJTLLHJH"],"itemData":{"id":32913,"type":"book","publisher":"ERIC","title":"The Earth Is Faster Now: Indigenous Observations of Arctic Environmental Change. Frontiers in Polar Social Science.","author":[{"family":"Krupnik","given":"Igor"},{"family":"Jolly","given":"Dyanna"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and, in managing wild species, it may be primarily useful when managing populations that occur in isolated locations where widespread scientific studies may be impractical ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hjJJx1OU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Barsh, 1997)","plainCitation":"(Barsh, 1997)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32916,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/95W6QDZP"],"itemData":{"id":32916,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Organization","issue":"1","note":"publisher: Society for Applied Anthropology","page":"28–37","title":"The epistemology of traditional healing systems","volume":"56","author":[{"family":"Barsh","given":"Russel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1997"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Despite the profound utility of local ecological knowledge, it has been received skeptically by some wild species managers and conservation biologists. 
      The coexistence of different forms of plant management and manipulation is particularly important in the case of edible plants. Multiple forms of management result in the persistence of diverse cultivars including those that combine superior flavor and better medicinal properties, with resistance to pests, floods, droughts, and the impacts of climate change ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bSbWtmv4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Mastretta-Yanes, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Mastretta-Yanes, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32716,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2887G72Q"],"itemData":{"id":32716,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Domestication has been in?uenced by formal plant breeding since the onset of intensive agriculture and the Green Revolution. Despite providing food security for some regions, intensive agriculture has had substantial detrimental consequences for the environment and does not ful?ll smallholder’s needs under most developing countries conditions. Therefore, it is necessary to look for alternative plant production techniques, effective for each environmental, socio-cultural, and economic conditions. This is particularly relevant for countries that are megadiverse and major centers of plant domestication and diversi?cation. In this white paper, a Mexico-centered initiative is proposed, with two main objectives: (1) to study, understand, conserve, and sustainably use the genetic diversity of domesticated plants and their wild relatives, as well as the ongoing evolutionary processes that generate and maintain it; and (2) to strengthen food and forestry production in a socially fair and environmentally friendly way. To ful?ll these objectives, the initiative focuses on the source of variability available for domestication (genetic diversity and functional genomics), the context in which domestication acts (breeding and production) and one of its main challenges (environmental change). Research on these components can be framed to target and connect both the theoretical understanding of the evolutionary processes, the practical aspects of conservation, and food and forestry production. The target, main challenges, problems to be faced and key research questions are presented for each component, followed by a roadmap for the consolidation of this proposal as a national initiative.","container-title":"Frontiers in Plant Science","DOI":"10.3389/fpls.2018.00209","ISSN":"1664-462X","journalAbbreviation":"Front. Plant Sci.","language":"en","page":"209","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"An Initiative for the Study and Use of Genetic Diversity of Domesticated Plants and Their Wild Relatives","URL":"http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fpls.2018.00209/full","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Mastretta-Yanes","given":"Alicia"},{"family":"Acevedo Gasman","given":"Francisca"},{"family":"Burgeff","given":"Caroline"},{"family":"Cano Ramírez","given":"Margarita"},{"family":"Piñero","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Sarukhán","given":"José"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",2,20]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. As ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"wdczmVV7","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Maxted, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Maxted, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32915,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PXA93QXJ"],"itemData":{"id":32915,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Plant genetic resources and climate change","note":"publisher: CABI Wallingford, UK","title":"Crop wild relatives and climate change","volume":"291","author":[{"family":"Maxted","given":"Nigel"},{"family":"Kell","given":"Shelagh"},{"family":"Brehm","given":"J Magos"},{"family":"Jackson","given":"M"},{"family":"Ford-Lloyd","given":"B"},{"family":"Parry","given":"M"},{"literal":"others"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} establishes that wild relatives have been found to have higher genetic diversity in terms of drought, pest, and disease resistance than their cultivated counterparts. 
      
Box 4.33. Cosmovision, transformation and fallow - Milpa management in the Yucatan

Among the Mayans in the state of Yucatan in Mexico, specialized milpa production systems termed, the ‘monte’, are fallow areas where secondary vegetation grows. According to Maya cosmovision the vegetative growth undergoing regeneration is on its way to resurrection. Therefore, Maya campesinos do not abandon the milpa but instead return it to the owners. the Yumilo'ob K'axo'ob, the Lords of the Mountains. These powerful inhabitants ensure the continuity of life and supernatural force circulates through them, giving permanence to the milpa and the milperos. In the cosmovision of the native peoples, the phases which are not cultivated or intensively managed at the species and landscape level, are part of the transformation of the elements of nature and their symbolic forces ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WCDglwst","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Quintanilla, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Quintanilla, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32728,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9RWFLJBY"],"itemData":{"id":32728,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Mesoamerica","page":"255-285","title":"El dilema de Ah Kimsah K'ax, \\"el que mata al monte\\": significados del monte entre los Mayas Milperos de Yucatan","URL":"https://dialnet.unirioja.es/descarga/articulo/2446548.pdf","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Quintanilla","given":"A. G."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",4,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      In the milpa, a wide variety of wild species are collected for food use, among them 20 species of wild sweet potato. These wild sweet potatoes have been crucial for survival during times of famine, even within the living memory of the elderly. Archeological evidence, and historical records show that since pre-Hispanic times, locusts (Schistocerca piceifrons), have periodically caused plagues with widespread destruction and fatality. Having an underground source of food such as sweet potatoes, has been especially important during such disasters. The cultivated and wild sweet potatoes are key, as the tortilla mixed with ground sweet potatoes was the main food source.  
      The sweet potato is collected in the young or mature growth, which is left fallow after its use for the production of the milpa. The cultivated and wild sweet potatoes are key, the tortilla mixed with ground sweet potatoes was then the main food.  		
      

4.2.5.2.6. Indigenous and local knowledge and wild food plants
Food is central to the culture, health, and well-being of society. Maintaining traditional food systems is important to revitalization of indigenous peoples and conservation of local knowledge. Traditional use of wild plants, utilized as food or medicines is common to both rural and urban societies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"cImsCS6s","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aziz, 2018; Meragiaw, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Aziz, 2018; Meragiaw, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32945,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EDS42KVR"],"itemData":{"id":32945,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine","DOI":"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/29378636","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"11","title":"Traditional Uses of Medicinal Plants Used by Indigenous Communities for Veterinary Practices at Bajaur Agency, Pakistan","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Aziz","given":"Muhammad Abdul"},{"family":"Khan","given":"Amir Hasan"},{"family":"Adnan","given":"Muhammad"},{"family":"Ullah","given":"Habib"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32951,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5DVCU6AX"],"itemData":{"id":32951,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Jmphtr","issue":"February","language":"en","page":"1–16","title":"Wild Useful Plants with Emphasis on Traditional Use of Medicinal and Edible Plants by the People of Aba’ala, North-Eastern Ethiopia","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Meragiaw","given":"Misganaw"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Ethnobotanical studies have documented medicinal and other traditional uses of wild plants, employed for human health, nutrition and veterinary use ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"V3bwtxC9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Aziz, 2018; Halmy, 2017; Heneidy, 2017; Meragiaw, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Aziz, 2018; Halmy, 2017; Heneidy, 2017; Meragiaw, 2016)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32945,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/EDS42KVR"],"itemData":{"id":32945,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of ethnobiology and ethnomedicine","DOI":"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/29378636","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"11","title":"Traditional Uses of Medicinal Plants Used by Indigenous Communities for Veterinary Practices at Bajaur Agency, Pakistan","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Aziz","given":"Muhammad Abdul"},{"family":"Khan","given":"Amir Hasan"},{"family":"Adnan","given":"Muhammad"},{"family":"Ullah","given":"Habib"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":33002,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UG6S5CU2"],"itemData":{"id":33002,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Knowing our lands and resources: Indigenous and local knowledge of biodiversity and ecosystem services in Africa","language":"en","page":"108–144 ,","publisher":"UNESCO: Paris","title":"Traditional knowledge associated with desert ecosystems in Egypt","author":[{"family":"Halmy","given":"M.W.A."}],"editor":[{"family":"Roué","given":"M."},{"family":"Césard","given":"N."},{"family":"Yao","given":"Y.C.Adou"},{"family":"Oteng-Yeboah","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33003,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2ZICPHBY"],"itemData":{"id":33003,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Feddes Repertorium","DOI":"10.1002/fedr.201600024.","language":"en","page":"105–128","title":"The ethnobotanical importance and conservation value of native plants in eastern Arabian Peninsula","volume":"128","author":[{"family":"Heneidy","given":"S."},{"family":"Halmy","given":"M.W.A."},{"family":"Bidak","given":"L.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":32951,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5DVCU6AX"],"itemData":{"id":32951,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Jmphtr","issue":"February","language":"en","page":"1–16","title":"Wild Useful Plants with Emphasis on Traditional Use of Medicinal and Edible Plants by the People of Aba’ala, North-Eastern Ethiopia","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Meragiaw","given":"Misganaw"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Nutritional values of wild foods and their key place in sustaining indigenous peoples and local communities' culture is important to be considered when designing sustainable use systems. Traditional practices of collection, preparation and consumption of wild plants are widely practiced in both rural and urban settings. Wild plants contribute to the nutritional wellbeing of families as well as offering a source of income generation. However, in situations of increased demand for a particular species, some traditional practices may become threatened by local populations, as well as by third parties (see Box 4.34), interested in generating income from increased exploitation of the natural resource ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"dYUic2UD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chauhan, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Chauhan, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32725,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/MB93YCGG"],"itemData":{"id":32725,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ethnobotanical relevance Trillium govanianum Wall. ex D. Don (Melanthiaceae) is valued as a traditional medicinal herb in the Himalayan region. Applications include treatment of cancer, hypertension, neurasthenia, giddiness, arthritis, dysentery, inflammation, sepsis and reproductive disorders. Its range is highly specific, and limited to cold, shaded and moist habitats at 2400-3500 m a.s.l. Rhizomes are gathered from wild populations for trade, and this has recently emerged as a significant source of income among indigenous people of the Indian Himalayan region. Aim To assess the impact of changing rhizome prices on T. govanianum in the Indian Himalayan region by observing the status of existing populations and assessing the typical methods used for gathering and trade of rhizomes. Material and methods Ecological attributes were assessed in 17 sites from Tirthan Valley (Himachal Pradesh), Munsiyari and Tunghnath (Uttarakhand). Socio-economic attributes assessed through communal focus groups and interviews with 579 medicinal plants gatherers and 19 traders, using semi-structured open ended questionnaires. Results Population decline was highest in Munsiyari, followed by Tirthan Valley and Tunghnath, indicating that the species may become locally extinct in some areas. Methods used for gathering did not consider regeneration. Conclusion Gathering of T. govanianum from wild populations is unselective and unmanaged, raising the potential threat of local extinctions. Sustainable utilization and effective conservation is needed to protect the species and maintain community incomes. A status of ‘threatened’ is justified for the species in the Indian Himalayan region, due to its slow life cycle, specific habitat requirement, low population density, and commercial value.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jep.2017.11.003","ISSN":"0378-8741","page":"302 - 310","title":"Population change of Trillium govanianum (Melanthiaceae) amid altered indigenous harvesting practices in the Indian Himalayas","URL":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378874117325758","volume":"213","author":[{"family":"Chauhan","given":"Harsh K."},{"family":"Bisht","given":"Anil K."},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"Indra D."},{"family":"Bhatt","given":"Arvind"},{"family":"Gallacher","given":"David"},{"family":"Santo","given":"Andrea"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 

Box 4.34. Native maize: A protected cultural hertage

In the new Mexican political context, a set of conflicting public policies are emerging that may impact wild plant management and consumption. On the one hand, there is the April 13 agreement arrived at after a long negotiation between decision makers and collectives and rural communities with the goal of protecting local varieties of maize in Mexico. This Federal Law for the Development and Protection of the Native recognizes that the production, marketing, consumption, and diversity of native maize are cultural manifestations of Mexico. The preservation of native maize in all its variety places an implicit obligation on the State to guarantee a nutritious, sufficient and high quality food free of genetically modified organisms. It is important to emphasize that even though maize has been domesticated since pre-Hispanic times, populations of the closest wild relatives of maize (Zea mays cultivated), known as teocintles (Zea parviglumis, Zea diploperennis, Zea luxurians, etc.) and Tripsacum species, continue to live together in the environments of the Mexican maize fields and contribute to enriching the genetic variability of native or creole maize cultivated via free pollination ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3S85zVdf","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kato, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Kato, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32919,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KQ58Q9Y2"],"itemData":{"id":32919,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Universidad Nacional Autónoma de México, Comisión Nacional para el Conocimiento y Uso de la Biodiversidad. México, DF","title":"Origen y diversificación del maíz: una revisión analítica","volume":"116","author":[{"family":"Kato","given":"Takeo Ángel"},{"family":"Mapes","given":"Cristina"},{"family":"Mera","given":"Luz María"},{"family":"Serratos","given":"José Antonio"},{"family":"Bye","given":"Robert Arthur"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This law will favor the permanence of traditional milpa management practices, which include maintaining wild relatives of maize and other species with local uses. 
      At the same time, the national Plant Variety Law is under discussion. This law would prohibit the free exchange of seeds, which has been essential for the protection of biocultural wealth and food sovereignty in Mexico. In addition, it would allow companies and external agents to obtain the intellectual property rights for seeds from the rural towns and communities that have maintained the great diversity of native species of multiple use in the country.

      Great emphasis has been recently placed on the role of traditional food in human health and nutritional status. Many traditional foods, particularly wild variants, are nutrient dense, making a substantial contribution to daily nutritional intake, especially for marginalized social classes. Additionally, traditional food as well as condiments, constitute an essential aspect of cultural heritage. Since foodways are highly ingrained and part of the evolution of human behavior they are developed by the interaction between the ecological environment, cultural institutions, and family dietary patterns ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ofee0QAY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Contreras & Gracia, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Contreras & Gracia, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32729,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WAZMC85R"],"itemData":{"id":32729,"type":"document","language":"pt","title":"Alimentacion y cultura: perspectivas antropológicas","author":[{"family":"Contreras","given":"J."},{"family":"Gracia","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  Studies in Mexico have found that an especially important set of condiment species with unique flavors and properties are found in wild populations and missing in cultivated populations. Wild collection of these species is the most common way of managing them. Notably, in recent years, as migrant populations from Mexico move to the United States of America, demand for these plants have increased in North America. Worldwide, the large number of immigrants with a significant amount of traditional communities’ influences consumption in the countries where they settle. Demand for wild plant species by immigrants has strongly influenced local and national cuisines, increasing the marketing of imported wild products which are often transported by immigrants themselves. 
      The Ethnobotanical Database of Mexican Plants of the Botanical Garden of the National Autonomous University of Mexico, records 16,000 uses for a total of 4,000 plant species which corresponds to more than 50% of the total estimated useful plant species in Mexico.  According to ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hWXPlRAg","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Caballero, 1998)","plainCitation":"(Caballero, 1998)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32918,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/43XCV9US"],"itemData":{"id":32918,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Estudios Atacameños","note":"publisher: JSTOR","page":"181–195","title":"Patrones en el conocimiento, uso y manejo de plantas en pueblos indígenas de México","author":[{"family":"Caballero","given":"Javier"},{"family":"Casas","given":"Alejandro"},{"family":"Cortés","given":"Laura"},{"family":"Mapes","given":"Cristina"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} two patterns of usage emerge. First, the majority of utilized plant species are wild, and second, the main uses are for food and medicine. Although the basic diet in rural populations consists of a set of cultivated and domesticated species, the diet is significantly supplemented by a large number of other plant resources, most of them wild or under “incipient management”. These supplemental species provide essential vitamins, minerals, and other important nutrients. Although traditional food is being studied for nutritional composition and cultural food use, there is still a significant research gap ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BRDL3I5I","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Heinrich, 2006)","plainCitation":"(M. Heinrich, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32960,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7INSINDE"],"itemData":{"id":32960,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forum of Nutrition","language":"en","page":"1–17","title":"‘Local Food-Nutraceuticals’: Bridging the Gap between Local Knowledge and Global Needs","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Heinrich","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, which could endanger food and health security as traditional knowledge erodes. 
      Local traditions rely on information being passed on from one generation to the next in one community or in a small region. Traditional food knowledge and traditional ecological knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"O6q7hybS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Heinrich, 2006)","plainCitation":"(M. Heinrich, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32960,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7INSINDE"],"itemData":{"id":32960,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forum of Nutrition","language":"en","page":"1–17","title":"‘Local Food-Nutraceuticals’: Bridging the Gap between Local Knowledge and Global Needs","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Heinrich","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} are strongly influenced by socioeconomic and cultural determinants, religion and history and their dissemination differs in local or national languages. However, transmission of indigenous and local knowledge is being interrupted in many communities. This trend represents a challenge to indigenous peoples and local communities and their traditional ways of living, including spiritual practices and relationships to land, waters, and other beings. Among the adverse consequences are a shift away from nutritious wild foods. The loss of a traditional languages is closely tied to the erosion of practices and uses related to wild species. 
      Traditional ecological knowledge is both cumulative and dynamic, building upon the experience of early generations and adapting to new technological and socioeconomic changes of the present. Although less attention has been paid to the situation of traditional ecological knowledge within Europe and highly industrialized countries, there are many cases of traditional use and consumption of species in specific and isolated regions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Y7CTMIM9","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M. Heinrich, 2006)","plainCitation":"(M. Heinrich, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32960,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7INSINDE"],"itemData":{"id":32960,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Forum of Nutrition","language":"en","page":"1–17","title":"‘Local Food-Nutraceuticals’: Bridging the Gap between Local Knowledge and Global Needs","volume":"59","author":[{"family":"Heinrich","given":"Michael"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, as well as in urban areas, which are important for biodiversity conservation, local cultures, and the maintenance of a healthy life for coming generations.
      
Box 4.35 Apatani’s and their indigenous knowledge – A classic tale from Eastern Himalayas

The Apatani eco-cultural landscape in Ziro Valley of Eastern Himalyas represents as an excellent example of a uniquely distinct natural resource management practice which involves local and traditional knowledge systems. Apatani (earliest ancestors are termed as Abotani), is a small hill tribe that resides in the Eastern Himalayan region rich traditional knowledge. They have rich knowledge of medicinal plant use (45 species are listed) and some of these are nutrient-rich plant species supporting food security ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"7nw4JoJH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. C. Rai, 2005; Srivastava, 2010)","plainCitation":"(S. C. Rai, 2005; Srivastava, 2010)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32996,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DNKCZPS6"],"itemData":{"id":32996,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Indian Journal of Traditional Knowledge","language":"en","page":"65–71","title":"Apatani paddy-cum fish cultivation: an indigenous hill farming system of North East India","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Rai","given":"S.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":32990,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8DYSKFS9"],"itemData":{"id":32990,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The Indian Journal of Traditional Knowledge","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"432–442","title":"Indigenous biodiversity of Apatani plateau: Learning on biocultural knowledge of Apatani tribe of Arunachal Pradesh for sustainable livelihoods","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Srivastava","given":"R.C."},{"family":"Singh","given":"R.K."},{"family":"Community","given":"A."},{"family":"Mukherjee","given":"T.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Apatani practice a unique, advanced agricultural practice called, the paddy-cum-fish cultivation. The main advantage from the practice is that the land gives sustained yield year after year, unlike the Jhum system, that is under cropping only once in a few years of fallow interval, depending upon the Jhum cycle. The economic and energy efficiency of this agro-ecosystem is exceptionally high, and, after meeting local needs, rice is exported. Rain fed cultivation of millet and mixed cropping contributes toward meeting the diverse needs of the people. Mithun (Bos frontalis Lam.), Swine and poultry husbandry are an important link with agro-ecosystems (see ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DjyB1HyE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(S. C. Rai, 2005)","plainCitation":"(S. C. Rai, 2005)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32996,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DNKCZPS6"],"itemData":{"id":32996,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Indian Journal of Traditional Knowledge","language":"en","page":"65–71","title":"Apatani paddy-cum fish cultivation: an indigenous hill farming system of North East India","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Rai","given":"S.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} for further reading). This indigenous knowledge system helps reduce the pressure and dependency on the surrounding biodiversity.


      There are cases where despite difficult conditions, indigenous peoples and local communities are drawing on indigenous and local knowledge and customary rules and norms to revitalize traditional practices, restore disturbed environments, recover wild species populations, and reinvigorate more traditional ways of living, as in the case of the Xingu Seeds Network in Brazil ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fsrfOejc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Antoniazzi,, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Antoniazzi,, 2011)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32730,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/HPQP5XUC"],"itemData":{"id":32730,"type":"article-newspaper","title":"Seed-based restoration: how experiences in Brazil are increasing in boths scale and benefits. International Network for Seed-based Restoration. Available at: https://ser-insr.org/news/2021/2/10/seed-based-restoration-how-experiences-in-brazil-are-increasing-in-both-scale-and-co-benefits.","author":[{"family":"Antoniazzi,","given":"L."},{"family":"Campos-Filho","given":"E.M."},{"family":"Vieira,","given":"D.L.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Ethno-veterinary use of plants also remains very important, because many indigenous peoples depend on their livestock on a daily basis. Several animal diseases are treated through the use of indigenous knowledge on the use of parts of fruit trees and other herbaceous plants and trees ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UyygZ9yD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cheikhyoussef & Embashu, 2013; K. Khan, 2019; Maroyi, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Cheikhyoussef & Embashu, 2013; K. Khan, 2019; Maroyi, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32906,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RPS7FBBQ"],"itemData":{"id":32906,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"BACKGROUND: Indigenous communities in Namibia possess a rich indigenous knowledge expressed within many practices of these communities. Indigenous wild edible fruits are available along the Namibian 13 regions of which it forms a rich source of vitamins, fibres, minerals and a heterogeneous collection of bioactive compounds referred to as phytochemicals for indigenous people's diet. The aim of this study was to record the different IKS practices on the indigenous fruit trees in Ohangwena and Oshikoto regions of Namibia.$\\\\backslash$n$\\\\backslash$nMETHODS: An ethnobotanical survey was undertaken to collect information from local communities from 23-29 October 2011. Data was collected through the use of questionnaires and personal interviews during field trips in the Ohangwena and Oshikoto regions. A total of 65 respondents were interviewed; 54%; women, 38%; men and 8%; both in group interviews.$\\\\backslash$n$\\\\backslash$nRESULTS: The majority of the people interviewed were in their thirty's, with the youngest being 18 years old and the oldest being 98 years old. Forty three plant specimens were collected from the two regions; these specimens belong to 20 genera and 25 species. Regarding to the indigenous knowledge; 87%; of the respondents indicated that their knowledge on indigenous fruits was learnt mainly through their parent. Indigenous people's perception on declining indigenous fruits revealed that 56.3%; of the respondents reported that indigenous fruits were declining. Only a 42.2%; indicated that the indigenous fruits populations are increasing. Regarding to the management practices to improve the production of these indigenous fruit trees; 38.6%; reported that there are some efforts on management practices; on the other hand 61.4%; reported there are no management practices on the indigenous fruit trees in their areas. Four species were found to be the most frequently used and mentioned fruits which need to be given high preference in terms of conservation are: Berchemia discolor, Hyphaene petersiana, Sclerocarya birrea and Diospyros mespiliformis. The following diseases and ailments have been reported to be treated by the indigenous fruit trees which include: toothache, diarrhoea, cough, tonsillitis, burns, skin allergy, stomach ache, snake bit, constipation, etc. 28%; of the respondents mentioned an ethno-veterinary use(s) of these fruits, e.g. the use of the Ziziphus mucronata roots to treat diarrhoea in cattle, the bark of eembe (Berchemia discolor) to treat calf weakness.$\\\\backslash$n$\\\\backslash$nCONCLUSIONS: The local communities in Oshikoto and Ohangwena regions have relatively good knowledge and practices regarding the indigenous fruit. This study enhances our understanding on the indigenous fruit in Namibia and their uses by local communities.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/1746-4269-9-34","ISSN":"17464269","issue":"1","title":"Ethnobotanical knowledge on indigenous fruits in Ohangwena and Oshikoto regions in Northern Namibia","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Cheikhyoussef","given":"Ahmad"},{"family":"Embashu","given":"Werner"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":32902,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/USNKYCJV"],"itemData":{"id":32902,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Domestic animals play a very important role in the human civilization. Besides human being, plants are used as medicines for many domestic animals. The therapeutic practices are very common among the tribes of Chamla, rich in ethnoveterinary medicinal plants. Due to poor availability of modern healthcare facilities and poverty of indigenous people, they depend on local medicinal plants for the healthcare of their domestic animals. This study is the first attempt to document the indigenous knowledge and evaluate the conservation status of medicinal plants and practices of herbal remedies by the local people of Chamla Valley in the treatment of their livestock. Semi-structured questionnaire was used and 120 local inhabitants were interviewed to note the traditional practices regarding plant species uses. Well-known statistical indices, Use Value formula and Relative Frequency Citations were used for quantification of the recorded data. It was observed that 50 medicinal plants belonging to 38 families were reported, where Poaceae was the most cited. The common livestock are goats, sheep, buffalos, cows, bulls and donkeys. Most of the herbs, which are used in livestock treatment, are wild and few plants are cultivated. The common livestock diseases are red water, three days sickness, diarrhea, tympany and indigestion among others. Most of the plants are used in fresh condition. According to the results, Brassica nigra was used for placenta retention, Butea monosperma for constipation, Calotropis procera for indigestion and three days sickness. Canabis sativa, Cedrella serrata, Allium sativum and Origanum vulgare were used for fever. The traditional plant collection techniques have resulted in huge losses of these valuable plant resources. The ethnobotanical conservation assessment revealed that due to increased exploitation and un-sustainable harvesting, 49% of these economically valued medicinal plant species are decreasing in last 30 years. Some of the plants are only present on high altitudes while they had been finished in the foothills like Paeonia emodi and Berberis lycium. Lack of scientific knowledge, ignorance, poverty and joblessness, as well as land development, construction and fires, add more pressure on flora and fauna of the area and various species are under the threat of extinction.","container-title":"Frontiers in Veterinary Science","DOI":"10.3389/fvets.2019.00122","issue":"May","title":"Ethnoveterinary Therapeutic Practices and Conservation Status of the Medicinal Flora of Chamla Valley, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa, Pakistan","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Khan","given":"Khalid"},{"family":"Rahman","given":"Inayat Ur"},{"family":"Calixto","given":"Eduardo Soares"},{"family":"Ali","given":"Niaz"},{"family":"Ijaz","given":"Farhana"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32904,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S6GSKNSJ"],"itemData":{"id":32904,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Background: Traditional ecological knowledge among indigenous communities plays an important role in retaining cultural identity and achieving sustainable natural resource management. Hundreds of millions of people mostly in developing countries derive a substantial part of their subsistence and income from plant resources. The aim of this study was to assess useful plant species diversity, plant use categories and local knowledge of both wild and cultivated useful species in the Eastern Cape province, South Africa. Methods: The study was conducted in six villages in the Eastern Cape province, South Africa between June 2014 and March 2017. Data on socio-economic characteristics of the participants, useful plants harvested from the wild, managed in home gardens were documented by means of questionnaires, observation and guided field walks with 138 participants. Results: A total of 125 plant species belonging to 54 genera were recorded from the study area. More than half of the species (59.2%) are from 13 families, Apiaceae, Apocynaceae, Araliaceae, Asparagaceae, Asphodelaceae, Asteraceae, Fabaceae, Lamiaceae, Malvaceae, Myrtaceae, Poaceae, Rosaceae and Solanaceae. More than a third of the useful plants (37.6%) documented in this study are exotic to South Africa. About three quarters of the documented species (74.4%) were collected from the wild, while 20.8% were cultivated and 4.8% were spontaneous. Majority of the species (62.4%) were used as herbal medicines, followed by food plants (30.4%), ethnoveterinary medicine (18.4%), construction timber and thatching (11.2%). Other minor plant use categories (1–5%) included firewood, browse, live fence, ornamentals, brooms and crafts. Conclusion: This study demonstrated that local people in the Eastern Cape province harbour important information on local vegetation that provides people with food, fuel and medicines, as well as materials for construction and the manufacturing of crafts and many other products. This study also demonstrated the dynamism of traditional ecological knowledge, practices and beliefs of local people demonstrated by the incorporation of exotic plants in their diets and indigenous pharmacopoeia.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-017-0173-8","ISSN":"17464269","issue":"1","page":"1–16","title":"Diversity of use and local knowledge of wild and cultivated plants in the Eastern Cape province, South Africa","volume":"13","author":[{"family":"Maroyi","given":"Alfred"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Conventional remedies for animal health care are inaccessible or unaffordable to indigenous people, incentivizing local communities to maintain traditional treatment practices.
There is much evidence that traditional knowledge systems contribute significantly to wild species conservation globally (i.e., see Table 4.4 on practices and customary laws)  Traditional knowledge systems are under stress in many parts of the world due to many factors associated with colonization, globalization (i.e., economic stress and socio-political marginalization ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"s06buiIn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}mez-Baggethun, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Gómez-Baggethun, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":30429,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3SZTW4DS","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TN2LFZMS"],"itemData":{"id":30429,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and society: a journal of integrative science for resilience and sustainability","issue":"4","language":"en","title":"Traditional ecological knowledge and global environmental change: research findings and policy implications","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Gómez-Baggethun","given":"E."},{"family":"Corbera","given":"E."},{"family":"Reyes-García","given":"V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. These broad patterns are compounded by land use change associated with industrialization and globalization forces and westernization ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"nJsd32uV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Harmon & Loh, 2010; N. J. Turner & Turner, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Harmon & Loh, 2010; N. J. Turner & Turner, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32733,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6FGAYJ9C"],"itemData":{"id":32733,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Language Documentation and Conservation","language":"en","page":"97–151","title":"The Index of Linguistic Diversity: A New Quantitative Measure of Trends in the Status of the World’s Languages","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Harmon","given":"D."},{"family":"Loh","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":19,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WJ52FNBK"],"itemData":{"id":19,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Knowledge and practices of indigenous peoples relating to local plants used for food, medicine, materials, and other purposes are threatened in many parts of the world. The reasons for declining knowledge and use of traditional resources are complex and multifaceted. We review a series of case examples of culturally valued food plants in British Columbia and identify a suite of interacting social and environmental factors that have resulted in decreased use of and dwindling cultural knowledge about these plants over the past 150 years. Reasons for this loss include compounding influences of changing knowledge systems owing to religious conversion and residential schools, loss of indigenous languages, loss of time and opportunity for traditional practices owing to participation in the wage economy, increasing urbanization of indigenous populations, loss of access to traditional resources, restriction of management practices for sustaining these resources, and most recently, forces of globalization and industrialization. Efforts to renew and restore traditional practices and relationships with plants and environments must recognize the cumulative effects of these factors and find ways to retain and reinforce the knowledge and practices still held by individuals and communities, to reverse some of the negative influences on cultural retention, and to develop new, relevant, and effective ways to revitalize languages, cultures, and ethnobotanical knowledge within contemporary contexts.","container-title":"Botany","DOI":"10.1139/B07-020","ISSN":"1916-2790, 1916-2804","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Botany","language":"en","page":"103-115","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"“Where our women used to get the food”: cumulative effects and loss of ethnobotanical knowledge and practice; case study from coastal British Columbia","title-short":"“Where our women used to get the food”","URL":"http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.1139/B07-020","volume":"86","author":[{"family":"Turner","given":"Nancy J."},{"family":"Turner","given":"Katherine L."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",6,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Historically, academic efforts have focused on documenting the loss of traditional knowledge systems (e.g., salvage paradigm) and not in understanding the processes that drive those changes. Moreover, there is less focus on how to support and nurture traditional knowledge in wasy that ensure its continued use and value in wild species stewardship ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"osI5ESZk","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(G\\\\uc0\\\\u243{}mez-Baggethun & Reyes-Garc\\\\uc0\\\\u237{}a, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Gómez-Baggethun & Reyes-García, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32977,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4MTNIXUY"],"itemData":{"id":32977,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Human Ecology","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"643–647","title":"Reinterpreting change in traditional ecological knowledge","volume":"41","author":[{"family":"Gómez-Baggethun","given":"E."},{"family":"Reyes-García","given":"V."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
		However, during changing, challenging conditions, indigenous peoples and local communities can draw on indigenous and local knowledge and customary rules and norms to revitalize traditional practices, restore disturbed environments, recover populations of wild species, and reinvigorate more traditional ways of living, as in the Mapuche communities in northwest Patagonia of Argentina, where regional knowledge about fungi is an important aspect of their tradition but process of changes also responds to complex and dynamic socioeconomic and ecological contexts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"lWkQS5CT","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Morales, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Morales, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32999,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KQJN2NBB"],"itemData":{"id":32999,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Argentina\\nDOI: 10.1080/00275514.2019.1680219.","title":"Traditional mycological knowledge and processes of change in Mapuche communities from Patagonia","author":[{"family":"Morales","given":"S."},{"family":"Toledo","given":"C.V."},{"family":"Stecher","given":"G."},{"family":"Barroetaveña","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Other activities, such as gathering gum brea, an exudate of the Cercidium praecox tree in the Province of Salta, Argentina, despite not being traditional, can contribute to the generation of moments of cultural transmission, intergenerational learning and the strengthening of local identities and links between communities and territories ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TbCqOHTV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Olivera, 2018)","plainCitation":"(Olivera, 2018)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32994,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JMR66V3L"],"itemData":{"id":32994,"type":"book","language":"es","number-of-pages":"104","publisher":"Facultad de Agronomía","title":"Análisis de la cadena de valor de la goma brea para la valorización del producto con identidad territorial e indígena. Trabajo de Intensificación para obtener el grado de Licenciado en Economía y Administración Agrarias otorgado por Universidad de Buenos Aires","URL":"http://ri.agro.uba.ar","author":[{"family":"Olivera","given":"Serena"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.	

4.2.5.2.7. Customary values of wild species and policy-making challenge for sustainable use
The complex links, that bind indigenous peoples and local communities to wild species, are based on multiple values, uses and institutions, that change regarding the actors and the historical and geographical context. In this section, the purposes are to highlight first, in what way the customary values attached to wild species are (or not) a driver of sustainable use; second, to what extent changes in customs, positively or negatively, impact the use of wild species; third, to what extent policies take into account customs and associated values for sustainable wild species governance.

Customary values of wild species as driver of sustainable use
The customary values of wild species encompass a set of anthropocentric values ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"IBkMV0yo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(K. M. Chan, 2016; Maitre d\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}H\\\\uc0\\\\u244{}tel & Pelegrin, 2012; Skubel, 2019; St\\\\uc0\\\\u229{}lhammar & Thor\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}n, 2019)","plainCitation":"(K. M. Chan, 2016; Maitre d’Hôtel & Pelegrin, 2012; Skubel, 2019; Stålhammar & Thorén, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32927,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P5YZFUVF"],"itemData":{"id":32927,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Proceedings of the national academy of sciences","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1525002113","issue":"6","language":"en","page":"1462–1465","title":"Why protect nature? Rethinking values and the environment","volume":"113","author":[{"family":"Chan","given":"K.M."},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"P."},{"family":"Benessaiah","given":"K."},{"family":"Chapman","given":"M."},{"family":"Díaz","given":"S."},{"family":"Gómez-Baggethun","given":"E."},{"family":"Gould","given":"R."},{"family":"Hannahs","given":"N."},{"family":"Jax","given":"K."},{"family":"Klain","given":"S."},{"family":"Luck","given":"G.W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32924,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2D4UPB2E"],"itemData":{"id":32924,"type":"document","language":"fr","note":"publisher-place: Paris","title":"Les valeurs de la biodiversité : un état des lieux de la recherche française","author":[{"family":"Maitre d'Hôtel","given":"E."},{"family":"Pelegrin","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":32989,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5PHY98YM"],"itemData":{"id":32989,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2019.00053","language":"fr","title":"Introducing Relational Values as a Tool for Shark Conservation, Science, and Management","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Skubel","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Shriver-Rice","given":"M."},{"family":"Maranto","given":"G.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32991,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7SGGNERI"],"itemData":{"id":32991,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Sustainability Science","DOI":"10.1007/s11625-019-00718-4","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"1201–1212","title":"Three perspectives on relational values of nature","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Stålhammar","given":"S."},{"family":"Thorén","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} , either instrumental, either relational, either intrinsic, defined by a group. This group, at multiple scales (clans, lineages, villages, collectivity, community, associations), is the main player for the management and the governance of the species, which implies the set-up of local institutions or customary rules. 
      The close and deep relationships between a group and a species (or a space/ a site) are anchored in the local culture and the feeling of identity and/or dependence for the subsistence and the well-being ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"A50rOBBC","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Borrini-Feyerabend, 2010; Garibaldi & Turner, 2004)","plainCitation":"(Borrini-Feyerabend, 2010; Garibaldi & Turner, 2004)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33011,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/97CUQLGB"],"itemData":{"id":33011,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Bio-cultural diversity conserved by indigenous peoples and local communities : examples and analysis","author":[{"family":"Borrini-Feyerabend","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}},{"id":34590,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Z9L4ZZEF"],"itemData":{"id":34590,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Ecologists have long recognized that some species, by virtue of the key roles they play in the overall structure and functioning of an ecosystem, are essential to its integrity; these are known as keystone species. Similarly, in human cultures everywhere, there are plants and animals that form the contextual underpinnings of a culture, as reflected in their fundamental roles in diet, as materials, or in medicine. In addition, these species often feature prominently in the language, ceremonies, and narratives of native peoples and can be considered cultural icons. Without these \\" cultural keystone species, \\" the societies they support would be completely different. An obvious example is western red-cedar (Thuja plicata) for Northwest Coast cultures of North America. Often prominent elements of local ecosystems, cultural keystone species may be used and harvested in large quantities and intensively managed for quality and productivity. Given that biological conservation and ecological restoration embody human cultures as crucial components, one approach that may improve success in overall conservation or restoration efforts is to recognize and focus on cultural keystone species. In this paper, we explore the concept of cultural keystone species, describe similarities to and differences from ecological keystone species, present examples from First Nations cultures of British Columbia, and discuss the application of this concept in ecological restoration and conservation initiatives.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"3","title":"Cultural Keystone Species: Implications for Ecological Conservation and Restoration","URL":"http://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol9/iss3/art1","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Garibaldi","given":"Ann"},{"family":"Turner","given":"Nancy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Those relations are mediated by customary institutions, among others: totemic species, sacred sites, ancestral sites and meeting places, ancestral shrines (pioneer settlers/ land use in and around the shrine sites), animal taboos, spiritual practices and ceremonies (songs, dances, initiation), traditional calendar, village lands (“terroirs”) and territories, etc. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"FYN0lahy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Adler, 1998; Artaud, 2021; A. Begossi, 2004; L\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}vi-Strauss, 1962b)","plainCitation":"(Adler, 1998; Artaud, 2021; A. Begossi, 2004; Lévi-Strauss, 1962b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32984,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5RXD7XUT"],"itemData":{"id":32984,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Systèmes de pensée en Afrique noire","language":"fr","page":"13–107","title":"Le totémisme en Afrique noire","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Adler","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":32911,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TNCPQF3W"],"itemData":{"id":32911,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Marine species and biodiversity : new anthropological perspectives","event-place":"Paris","language":"en","publisher":"MNHN","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Species “good for thinking”: species “good to preserve”? The paradox of marine species co-management","author":[{"family":"Artaud","given":"H."}],"editor":[{"family":"Artaud","given":"H."},{"family":"Chlous","given":"F."},{"family":"Levain","given":"A."},{"family":"M. Mariat-Roy","given":"E.P."},{"literal":"Paris"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":32908,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XKIN8UUU"],"itemData":{"id":32908,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecological Applications","issue":"5","language":"en","page":"1334–1343","title":"Food Chain and the Reasons for Fish Food Taboos among Amazonian and Atlantic Forest Fishers (Brazil","volume":"14","author":[{"family":"Begossi","given":"A."},{"family":"Hanazaki","given":"N."},{"family":"Ramos","given":"R.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2004"]]}}},{"id":33004,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4947HFP8"],"itemData":{"id":33004,"type":"document","language":"fr","note":"publisher-place: Paris","title":"Le totémisme aujourd’hui","author":[{"family":"Lévi-Strauss","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1962"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Indigenous peoples and local communities depend on those institutions for their livelihoods, with which they identify and which they control through ritual offices. These customary institutions are more or less formalized and recognized by official institutions (cf. chap 6) or combined with official systems of governance (positive law) in a context of legal and institutional pluralism. Therefore, custom systems (values, norms, institutions and rules) are recognized to play a major role in the development, management and control of wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4IqdHupo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(D. Armitage, 2017; Artaud & Surrall\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}s, 2017; N. J. Bennett, 2017; Cinner, 2009; W. A. Clark, 2011; Frangoudes & Gerrard, 2018; D. Hulme & Murphree, 2001; Johannes, 2000; Pascual, 2017)","plainCitation":"(D. Armitage, 2017; Artaud & Surrallés, 2017; N. J. Bennett, 2017; Cinner, 2009; W. A. Clark, 2011; Frangoudes & Gerrard, 2018; D. Hulme & Murphree, 2001; Johannes, 2000; Pascual, 2017)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33010,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E5ZJ962H"],"itemData":{"id":33010,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Communities, Resilience and Transformation","language":"en","note":"publisher: Earthscan Routledge","title":"Governing the Coastal Commons","editor":[{"family":"Armitage","given":"D."},{"family":"Charles","given":"A."},{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":34660,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/FZQE5ARR"],"itemData":{"id":34660,"type":"book","abstract":"Recent texts on the rights of indigenous peoples clearly show that indigenous ownership of marine and coastal spaces must be respected. In addition, their rights to their sea spaces must be guaranteed according to local perceptions, and this is masterly described by some articles in this book. This is expressed in the 2007 United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples (UNDRIP) in the articles dealing with territoriality (from 25 to 27). The UNDRIP emphasis on coastal and maritime spaces exists to support the deep tradition of international law considering the sea to be an area of free circulation. Indigenous minorities are staking claims as they seek to preserve or restore their rights on maritime environments to which they have close ties. State recognition of these claims entails political and legal translations. Inter-state disputes over maritime territorial expansion have proliferated. And finally, environmental crisis raises new issues, and calls for new types of practical response and negotiation. As an unprecedented climate emerges around maritime questions, the sea is, more than ever before, permeated by human affairs. Social anthropologists, whose attention to the sea was previously quite timorous, are becoming more keenly interested in the socialization of maritime space.","edition":"IWGIA","event-place":"Copenhagen, Denmark","ISBN":"978-87-92786-87-6","language":"English","number-of-pages":"228","publisher-place":"Copenhagen, Denmark","title":"The Sea Within: Marine Tenure and Cosmopolitical Debates","author":[{"family":"Artaud","given":"Hélène"},{"family":"Surrallés","given":"Alexandre"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":32909,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YTMAZP3I"],"itemData":{"id":32909,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"93–108","title":"Conservation social science: Understanding and integrating human dimensions to improve conservation","volume":"205","author":[{"family":"Bennett","given":"N.J."},{"family":"Roth","given":"R."},{"family":"Klain","given":"S.C."},{"family":"Chan","given":"K."},{"family":"Christie","given":"P."},{"family":"Clark","given":"D.A."},{"family":"Epstein","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}},{"id":33007,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BNH76WKY"],"itemData":{"id":33007,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Curr. Biol","language":"en","page":"206–212","title":"Linking social and ecological systems to sustain coral reef fisheries","volume":"19","author":[{"family":"Cinner","given":"J.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}},{"id":33006,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/79ZLNRDQ"],"itemData":{"id":33006,"type":"article-journal","container-title":". Journal for the Study of Religion, Nature & Culture","issue":"1","language":"en","title":"Clarifying the Spiritual Value of Forests and their Role in Sustainable Forest Management","volume":"5","author":[{"family":"Clark","given":"W.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}},{"id":32972,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8VWE5I77"],"itemData":{"id":32972,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Maritime Studies","DOI":"10.1007/s40152-018-0113-9","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"117–124","title":"(En)Gendering Change in Small-Scale Fisheries and Fishing Communities in a Globalized World","volume":"17","author":[{"family":"Frangoudes","given":"K."},{"family":"Gerrard","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32540,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/37I4R74I"],"itemData":{"id":32540,"type":"book","language":"en","publisher":"James Currey Ltd","title":"African Wildlife and Livelihoods: The Promise and Performance of Community Conservation","author":[{"family":"Hulme","given":"David"},{"family":"Murphree","given":"Marshall"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":32988,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BQDAMIJZ"],"itemData":{"id":32988,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Fish and Fisheries","ISSN":"1467-2960","issue":"3","journalAbbreviation":"Fish and Fisheries","note":"number: 3\\npublisher: Wiley Online Library","page":"257-271","title":"Ignore fishers’ knowledge and miss the boat","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Johannes","given":"Robert E"},{"family":"Freeman","given":"Milton MR"},{"family":"Hamilton","given":"Richard J"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000"]]}}},{"id":32724,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XNGH4NKP"],"itemData":{"id":32724,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Nature is perceived and valued in starkly different and often conflicting ways. This paper presents the rationale for the inclusive valuation of nature’s contributions to people (NCP) in decision making, as well as broad methodological steps for doing so. While developed within the context of the Intergovernmental Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES), this approach is more widely applicable to initiatives at the knowledge–policy interface, which require a pluralistic approach to recognizing the diversity of values. We argue that transformative practices aiming at sustainable futures would benefit from embracing such diversity, which require recognizing and addressing power relationships across stakeholder groups that hold different values on human nature-relations and NCP.","container-title":"Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability","DOI":"10.1016/J.COSUST.2016.12.006","page":"7-16","title":"Valuing nature’s contributions to people: the IPBES approach","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1877343517300040","volume":"26-27","author":[{"family":"Pascual","given":"Unai"},{"family":"Balvanera","given":"Patricia"},{"family":"Díaz","given":"Sandra"},{"family":"Pataki","given":"György"},{"family":"Roth","given":"Eva"},{"family":"Stenseke","given":"Marie"},{"family":"Watson","given":"Robert T"},{"family":"Ba?ak Dessane","given":"Esra"},{"family":"Islar","given":"Mine"},{"family":"Kelemen","given":"Eszter"},{"family":"Maris","given":"Virginie"},{"family":"Quaas","given":"Martin"},{"family":"Subramanian","given":"Suneetha M"},{"family":"Wittmer","given":"Heidi"},{"family":"Adlan","given":"Asia"},{"family":"Ahn","given":"SoEun"},{"family":"Al-Hafedh","given":"Yousef S"},{"family":"Amankwah","given":"Edward"},{"family":"Asah","given":"Stanley T"},{"family":"Berry","given":"Pam"},{"family":"Bilgin","given":"Adem"},{"family":"Breslow","given":"Sara J"},{"family":"Bullock","given":"Craig"},{"family":"Cáceres","given":"Daniel"},{"family":"Daly-Hassen","given":"Hamed"},{"family":"Figueroa","given":"Eugenio"},{"family":"Golden","given":"Christopher D"},{"family":"Gómez-Baggethun","given":"Erik"},{"family":"González-Jiménez","given":"David"},{"family":"Houdet","given":"Joël"},{"family":"Keune","given":"Hans"},{"family":"Kumar","given":"Ritesh"},{"family":"Ma","given":"Keping"},{"family":"May","given":"Peter H"},{"family":"Mead","given":"Aroha"},{"family":"O’Farrell","given":"Patrick"},{"family":"Pandit","given":"Ram"},{"family":"Pengue","given":"Walter"},{"family":"Pichis-Madruga","given":"Ramón"},{"family":"Popa","given":"Florin"},{"family":"Preston","given":"Susan"},{"family":"Pacheco-Balanza","given":"Diego"},{"family":"Saarikoski","given":"Heli"},{"family":"Strassburg","given":"Bernardo B"},{"family":"Belt","given":"Marjan","non-dropping-particle":"van den"},{"family":"Verma","given":"Madhu"},{"family":"Wickson","given":"Fern"},{"family":"Yagi","given":"Noboyuki"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]],"season":"juin"}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.

Box 4.3.6 : Use of “chaguar” by Wichi women  

In Argentina's dry Chaco eco-region, local communities depend on the integral use of the forest for hunting, and obtaining food, fiber, shelter, and medicine from timber and non-timber forest products. In the context of increasing deforestation, expanding agribusiness and the loss of communal lands, traditional territories and resources are rapidly diminishing with negative repercussions for rural families ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Uf5gSjQS","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Paolasso, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Paolasso, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32731,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/YTXBUTT4"],"itemData":{"id":32731,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Kieler Geographische Schriften","language":"en","page":"12–2012 51–76","title":"Agriculture and Cattle Frontier Advance and Variation of Poverty in the North of the \\"Gran Chaco Argentino\\" during the 1990s; Selbstverlag des Geographisches Instituts der Universität Kiel","volume":"123","author":[{"family":"Paolasso","given":"Pablo Cristian"},{"family":"Krapovickas","given":"Julieta"},{"family":"Longhi","given":"Hugo Fernando"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The Wichi people give special importance to the collection, processing, spinning, dyeing, and weaving of chaguar, (Bromelia hieronymi), an herbaceous plant with succulent leaves. Both the fiber of “chaguar” and the products derived from it are not considered as mere objects of use to be eventually discarded by the communities, but hold a strong symbolic meaning giving identity and character to local communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sQ9S6QfR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sastre-Merino, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Sastre-Merino, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32917,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JYHNJTGM"],"itemData":{"id":32917,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Cuadernos de Desarrollo Rural","issue":"SPE70","note":"publisher: Instituto de Estudios Rurales de la Facultad de Estudios Ambientales y …","page":"219–243","title":"Sustainability of rural development projects within the working with people model: Application to Aymara Women Communities in the Puno Region, Peru","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Sastre-Merino","given":"Susana"},{"family":"Negrillo","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Hernández-Castellano","given":"Daniel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The myth "The Advent of Women" conveys that the first women who descended from heaven into the world of men, did so using braided ropes of chaguar. The plant has a prominent symbolic role in the female rite of initiation, in which women begin to spin and weave from adolescence. Beginning in childhood, girls accompany their mothers and female elders to the forest to collect chaguar, and learn the processes of spinning, coloring, and weaving that becomes central to their lives as adults. In the courtyards of their homes, grandmothers, mothers, and daughters come together to make textiles. These shared activities serve to transmit knowledge to new generations, and to integrate young women and girls into the community ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"uplYiKZt","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Sastre-Merino, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Sastre-Merino, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32917,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JYHNJTGM"],"itemData":{"id":32917,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Cuadernos de Desarrollo Rural","issue":"SPE70","note":"publisher: Instituto de Estudios Rurales de la Facultad de Estudios Ambientales y …","page":"219–243","title":"Sustainability of rural development projects within the working with people model: Application to Aymara Women Communities in the Puno Region, Peru","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Sastre-Merino","given":"Susana"},{"family":"Negrillo","given":"Xavier"},{"family":"Hernández-Castellano","given":"Daniel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Appropriation of land by the agribusiness sector and forest clearing and degradation have resulted in diminished access for women to the chaguarales, as well as other resources necessary for food, shelter, and medicine.  In this way women lose not only the material resources needed to sustain daily life, but their socio-cultural and economic role of contributing to the community economy. The disappearance of these activities also contributes to the loss of knowledge and symbologies that the Wichi peoples have developed for centuries. Therefore, it is important to encourage more sustainable forms of resource use, the addition of value to forest products through processing, and access to fair trade markets. In this context, the USUBI (Sustainable Use of Biodiversity) project, carried out by the Ministry of Environment and Sustainable Development of Argentina, works with Wichi women from the communities of Los Baldes, La Cortada and Pozo El Chañar surveying chaguarales, promoting enrichment practices with chaguar in experimental plots, and hosting training workshops to improve artisanal products. These community-driven actions are aiming to generate locally derived, socially and environmentally sustainable solutions to prevent the loss of biodiversity and shore up centuries-old traditional knowledge.
      There are a number of scientific-based evidences (or an extensive literature) on the subject. Customary institutions, social practices and cultural values participate, intentionally or not, in natural resource conservation ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mzq37K2o","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dounias, 2007; IPBES, 2019a; Lizet & Millet, 2012; Ruddle, 1992)","plainCitation":"(Dounias, 2007; IPBES, 2019a; Lizet & Millet, 2012; Ruddle, 1992)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":34583,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/XRHV3HPL"],"itemData":{"id":34583,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-2-7099-1616-5","language":"French","note":"OCLC: 1039777257","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Le symbolisme des animaux: l'animal, clef de voûte de la relation entre l'homme et la nature = Animal symbolism : animals, keystone in the relationship between man and nature?","title-short":"Le symbolisme des animaux","URL":"https://www.documentation.ird.fr/hor/fdi:010041913","editor":[{"family":"Dounias","given":"Edmond"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":68,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KHBWAVKY"],"itemData":{"id":68,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Montreal, Canada","title":"Report from the second ILK Dialogue Workshop for the IPBES assessment of the sustainable use of wild species","author":[{"literal":"IPBES"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",10]]}}},{"id":33000,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BKAF6483"],"itemData":{"id":33000,"type":"book","event-place":"Paris (FRA","language":"fr","publisher":"Dunod/MNHN","publisher-place":"Paris (FRA","title":"Animal certifié conforme : déchiffrer nos relations avec le vivant","editor":[{"family":"Lizet","given":"B."},{"family":"Millet","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":32993,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P87B9GDM"],"itemData":{"id":32993,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Marine Resource Economics","language":"en","page":"249–273","title":"Marine resources management in the context of customary tenure","volume":"7","author":[{"family":"Ruddle","given":"K."},{"family":"Hviding","given":"E."},{"family":"Johannes","given":"R.E."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} indigenous peoples and local communities, by virtue of their proximity to natural resources, are the most capable of preserving them ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"twUlTXk0","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Agrawal & Gibson, 2001; Bouamrane, 2016; Dugast, 2002; Posey, 1999)","plainCitation":"(Agrawal & Gibson, 2001; Bouamrane, 2016; Dugast, 2002; Posey, 1999)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33060,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RHBDK75J"],"itemData":{"id":33060,"type":"book","event-place":"New Brunswick, N.J","language":"en","publisher":"Rutgers University Press","publisher-place":"New Brunswick, N.J","title":"Communities and the environment : ethnicity, gender, and the state in community-based conservation","editor":[{"family":"Agrawal","given":"A."},{"family":"Gibson","given":"M.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":32903,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5YM4R75C"],"itemData":{"id":32903,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"© 2016 by the author(s).Biosphere reserves are an example of social-ecological systems that combine biodiversity conservation and socioeconomic development with knowledge generation and dissemination (both scientific and local). We review lessons learned from case studies biosphere reserves in western African and France, highlighting the importance of early stakeholder engagement to build knowledge for achieving sustainable development. We discuss the evolution of the concept of biosphere reserves and its application over time in different socioeconomic and cultural settings. The diversity of stakeholders and their different needs and perceptions about nature conservation complicate implementation processes, sometimes resulting in conflicts about the objectives and zonation of biosphere reserves. Dialogue among the different stakeholders must start at an early planning phase and be based on the principle of social and ecological solidarity. Dialogue must then be pursued, formalized, ritualized, and translated both in terms of biosphere reserve management and in terms of political support. Tools and methods exist that can facilitate such dialogue and colearning.","container-title":"Ecology and Society","DOI":"10.5751/ES-08812-210425","ISSN":"17083087","issue":"4","title":"Stakeholder engagement and biodiversity conservation challenges in socialecological systems: Some insights from biosphere reserves in Western Africa and France","volume":"21","author":[{"family":"Bouamrane","given":"M."},{"family":"Spierenburg","given":"M."},{"family":"Agrawal","given":"A."},{"family":"Boureima","given":"A."},{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."},{"family":"Etienne","given":"M."},{"family":"Le Page","given":"C."},{"family":"Levrel","given":"H."},{"family":"Mathevet","given":"R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}},{"id":32983,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NCVWYXZ2"],"itemData":{"id":32983,"type":"document","language":"fr","note":"publisher-place: Paris","publisher":"IRD","title":"Modes d'appréhension de la nature et gestion patrimoniale du miieu\\".In M.C","author":[{"family":"Dugast","given":"S."}],"editor":[{"literal":"Cormier-Salem"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":34548,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/7GUX7585"],"itemData":{"id":34548,"type":"book","call-number":"GF80 .C85 1999","event-place":"London","ISBN":"978-1-85339-397-6","publisher":"Intermediate Technology","publisher-place":"London","source":"Library of Congress ISBN","title":"Cultural and spiritual values of biodiversity","URL":"file:///C:/Users/MARIE-~1/AppData/Local/Temp/Cultural_Spiritual_thebible.pdf","author":[{"family":"Posey","given":"D. A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 


Figure 4.14. Set of customary values of wild species. Source: adapted from ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"flMxIteB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Skubel, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Skubel, 2019)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32989,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5PHY98YM"],"itemData":{"id":32989,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Frontiers in Marine Science","DOI":"10.3389/fmars.2019.00053","language":"fr","title":"Introducing Relational Values as a Tool for Shark Conservation, Science, and Management","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Skubel","given":"R.A."},{"family":"Shriver-Rice","given":"M."},{"family":"Maranto","given":"G.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} © 2019 under license CC BY.
      From a vast literature review and critical analysis, three categories currently are identified, namely instrumental, relational and intrinsic. These norms and values exclude or combine, succeed one another or are superimposed on one another, mediated by institutions and customary rules, which regulate practices (hunting, fishing, gathering, etc.). As a matter of fact, the convergence of a species as “good for using (eating, clothing, healing...)”, “good for preserving” and “good for thinking” most often assure a more efficient conservation of wild species.
      Thus, the capture of an animal, its killing and the sharing of its body are subject to customary rules of respect, reciprocity and sobriety. The indigenous and local knowledge report cites many examples of such customary rules including the relationship of Maasai with wild species.
      Many indigenous peoples and local communities have rules for sharing the catch, with a special sin granted to the genitals of animals, endowed with aphrodisiac virtues and most often intended for "big chief" or lineage elder ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TDZj868Q","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Agrawal & Gibson, 2001; Artaud, 2014; Benitez-Capistros & Couenberg, 2019; Johannes, 1981)","plainCitation":"(Agrawal & Gibson, 2001; Artaud, 2014; Benitez-Capistros & Couenberg, 2019; Johannes, 1981)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33060,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RHBDK75J"],"itemData":{"id":33060,"type":"book","event-place":"New Brunswick, N.J","language":"en","publisher":"Rutgers University Press","publisher-place":"New Brunswick, N.J","title":"Communities and the environment : ethnicity, gender, and the state in community-based conservation","editor":[{"family":"Agrawal","given":"A."},{"family":"Gibson","given":"M.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":33065,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4TCXV69N"],"itemData":{"id":33065,"type":"article-journal","container-title":". Revue d’ethnoécologie (in line","DOI":"10.4000/ethnoecologie.2055","language":"fr","title":"De l’«efficacité» symbolique des interdits à leur fonctionnalité écologique. Variations sur le «tabou» en milieux maritimes","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Artaud","given":"H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":33059,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BUBZECSG"],"itemData":{"id":33059,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Technique Application. Sustainability","issue":"10","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Santa Cruz, Galapagos\\npublisher: A Nominal Group","page":"2937","title":"Identifying Shared Strategies and Solutions to the Human–Giant Tortoise Interactions in","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Benitez-Capistros","given":"F."},{"family":"Couenberg","given":"P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32714,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H9EVID4H"],"itemData":{"id":32714,"type":"book","event-place":"Berkeley/Los Angeles/London","ISBN":"0-520-03929-7","language":"English","number-of-pages":"245","publisher":"University of California Press","publisher-place":"Berkeley/Los Angeles/London","title":"Words of the lagoon: fishing and marine lore in the Palau district of Micronesia","URL":"https://books.google.fr/books?id=TloVDfV7QLoC&pg=PR3&hl=fr&source=gbs_selected_pages&cad=3#v=onepage&q&f=false","author":[{"family":"Johannes","given":"Robert Earle"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1981"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      Moreover, the construction of wild species as iconic, and its transmission from generation to generation – is often considered a tool for sustainable natural resource management and are susceptible to gaining heritage status ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"NP8WogbU","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bassett & Cormier-Salem, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Bassett & Cormier-Salem, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32907,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5JETNMH9"],"itemData":{"id":32907,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Africa","issue":"1","language":"it","page":"150","title":"Nature as Local Heritage in Africa","volume":"77","author":[{"family":"Bassett","given":"T.J."},{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The relations between people and wild species are complex and dynamic: they are complex, in so far as there are differential relations between species and what they reveal about the differential relations between social groups; one species can be considered by a group or certain members of that group (female or male initiated, priests, intercessors, etc.), at the same time and in the same place or according to the period and to the place, good for eating, preserving and/or thinking. The relations are dynamic in the same way that the social structures should adapt to the change. Human societies look to animal and plant species in their environment as a means to structure their social world ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"axX6M0tY","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(L\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}vi-Strauss, 1962b)","plainCitation":"(Lévi-Strauss, 1962b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33004,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4947HFP8"],"itemData":{"id":33004,"type":"document","language":"fr","note":"publisher-place: Paris","title":"Le totémisme aujourd’hui","author":[{"family":"Lévi-Strauss","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1962"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The reproduction of the social system (the establishment and maintenance of human interactions) requires a permanent reorganisation of these relations between humans among themselves and between humans and nature (regarding inks between tradition and innovation and resilience ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6xNfZeow","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berkes, 2003; Cinner & Barnes, 2019; Cosens, 2013; Matin, 2018; Prado, 2015; Yadav, 2019; O. R. Young, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Berkes, 2003; Cinner & Barnes, 2019; Cosens, 2013; Matin, 2018; Prado, 2015; Yadav, 2019; O. R. Young, 2006)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":768,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H6AL3ASM"],"itemData":{"id":768,"type":"book","event-place":"Cambridge, UK","language":"en","publisher":"Cambrige University press","publisher-place":"Cambridge, UK","title":"Navigating social-ecological systems: builiding resilience for complexity and change","author":[{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."},{"family":"Colding","given":"J."},{"family":"Folke","given":"C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":34779,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/4YYNBCDJ"],"itemData":{"id":34779,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"One Earth","DOI":"10.1016/j.oneear.2019.08.003","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"51–56","title":"Social Dimensions of Resilience in Social-Ecological Systems","volume":"1","author":[{"family":"Cinner","given":"J.E."},{"family":"Barnes","given":"M.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}},{"id":32869,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/N35H5BNS"],"itemData":{"id":32869,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ecology and Society","issue":"1","language":"en","title":"Legitimacy, adaptation, and resilience in ecosystem management","volume":"18","author":[{"family":"Cosens","given":"B.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}},{"id":32831,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BYVBJKYR"],"itemData":{"id":32831,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"World Development","DOI":"10.1016/j.worlddev.2018.04.020","language":"en","page":"197–205","title":"What is equitable resilience?","volume":"109","author":[{"family":"Matin","given":"N."},{"family":"Forrester","given":"J."},{"family":"Ensor","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018"]]}}},{"id":32827,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/B72S9R8F"],"itemData":{"id":32827,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Ocean & Coastal Management","DOI":"10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2015.05.018","issue":"0","language":"en","page":"29–37","title":"Looking back and looking forward: Exploring livelihood change and resilience building in a Brazilian coastal community","volume":"113","author":[{"family":"Prado","given":"D.S."},{"family":"Seixas","given":"C.S."},{"family":"Berkes","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":32655,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PN6GCIQ7"],"itemData":{"id":32655,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Oryx. The International Journal of Conservation. Cambridge University Press","issue":"ue 2","language":"en","page":"247–255","title":"Yartsagunbu: transforming people's livelihoods in the Western Himalaya","volume":"53","author":[{"family":"Yadav","given":"Pramod Kumar"},{"family":"Saha","given":"Subhajit"},{"family":"Mishra","given":"Ashish Kumar"},{"family":"Kapoor","given":"Mohnish"},{"family":"Kaneria","given":"Manendra"},{"family":"Kaneria","given":"Manish"},{"family":"Dasgupta","given":"Sabyasachi"},{"family":"Shrestha","given":"Uttam Babu"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019",4]]}}},{"id":323,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/PNZ5ITAB"],"itemData":{"id":323,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","DOI":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gloenvcha.2006.03.004","ISSN":"0959-3780","issue":"3","note":"type: Journal Article","page":"304-316","title":"The globalization of socio-ecological systems: An agenda for scientific research","URL":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959378006000276","volume":"16","author":[{"family":"Young","given":"Oran R."},{"family":"Berkhout","given":"Frans"},{"family":"Gallopin","given":"Gilberto C."},{"family":"Janssen","given":"Marco A."},{"family":"Ostrom","given":"Elinor"},{"family":"Leeuw","given":"Sander","non-dropping-particle":"van der"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      The following sub-section will have two lenses: first on wild plants and medicinal values; second on wild fauna and animal-symbolism.
      
Customary (traditional) medicinal values of wild plants
Medicinal values of wild species, especially wild plants have been extensively studied in recent years. For example, a study by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"vTbR6fjp","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Z. K. Shinwari & Qaiser, 2011)","plainCitation":"(Z. K. Shinwari & Qaiser, 2011)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32888,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/UPQRN23Z"],"itemData":{"id":32888,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Since the beginning of civilization, and perhaps as early as Neanderthal man, people have used plants as medicine. Evidence indicates that people used plants to cure themselves, e.g. Chinese Emperor (2800 BC); Babylon (1770 BC); Ancient Egypt (1550 BC). Islamic and Indian physicians also wrote many works prior to 1100 AD and the Seals from the Harappan site in Pakistan (2000 BC) also indicate use of plants. New aspects of medicinal plants need to be studied. For example, we should address the question \\"why plant diversity declines, when plants with weedy traits become more abundant\\". This is consequently followed by, species that have traits permitting their persistence in degraded and speciespoor ecosystems which are likely to carry high pathogen and vector burdens. Indigenous communities of Pakistan play a vital role in conservation of medicinal plants. Intentionally or unintentionally, people have evolved strategies for doing so in the form of rituals, beliefs and taboos. Various traditional harvesting methods described in one of the study suggest that they were efficient to utilize the natural resources. Our efforts are towards not only providing food security, nutrition and health care to the tribal people, but also to recover, record and diffuse local botanical knowledge and wisdom.","container-title":"Pakistan Journal of Botany","DOI":"10.14355/ijape.2014.0301.08","ISSN":"05563321","title":"Efforts on conservation and sustainable use of medicinal plants of Pakistan","author":[{"family":"Shinwari","given":"Zabta Khan"},{"family":"Qaiser","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2011"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} in Margalla hills National Park in Islamabad, Pakistan provides a list of wild plant species that are used by local people and Hakims (local practitioners) to treat several ailments. For instance, Achyranthus aspera is used for seasonal cough leaves of Calendula arvensis to heal wounds, leaves of Achyranthus aspera with fruit of Rubus fruiticosus for eye diseases, Chenopodium ambrosoides for piles, etc. However, wide spread recognition of such values, as well as population growth, promote over exploitation. The regeneration and propagation of the above-mentioned species is of concern in the region due to their unsustainable use, which includes untimely plucking to leaves and flowers or uprooting the whole plant without considering its further propagation. Similarly, local communities living around Queen Elizabeth National Park, Maramagambo Central Forest Reserve and Ihimbo Central Forest Reserve in Southern Uganda identified nearly 302 medicinal plants, many which are used to treat several ailments including digestive disorders and allergy ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"njGe3Xoo","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Gumisiriza, 2019)","plainCitation":"(Gumisiriza, 2019)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33086,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/322WCTR9"],"itemData":{"id":33086,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of ethnopharmacology","language":"en","page":"111926","title":"Medicinal plant species used by local communities around queen elizabeth national park, maramagambo central forest reserve and ihimbo central forest reserve, south western Uganda","volume":"239","author":[{"family":"Gumisiriza","given":"H."},{"family":"Birungi","given":"G."},{"family":"Olet","given":"E.A."},{"family":"Sesaazi","given":"C.D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2019"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In addition to medicinal values, 47 wild plants species are also consumed as food, highlighting the nutraceutical value of the species. Out of 302 plant species identified, 91 were reported to be rare. In some cases, the use of roots, or several parts of the same plant for medicinal use results in killing the plant, thus destroying the chances of survival and propagation. In another study, 79 wild medicinal plant species were listed from Jordon ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"XuK9JrZK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Al-Qura\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}n, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Al-Qura’n, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33061,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/U5RZ45VX"],"itemData":{"id":33061,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnopharmacology","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"45–50","title":"Ethnopharmacological survey of wild medicinal plants in Showbak, Jordan","volume":"123","author":[{"family":"Al-Qura’n","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Though the medicinal values of wild species are generally well-acknowledged, lack of ethnobotanical, ecological, as well as ethno pharmacological information on wild species often leave them vulnerable to overexploitation. In addition, indiscriminate and commercial use is also resulting in loss of various wild plant species making them rarely available.
      Communities value wild species not only for their medicinal or market values, but also for a combination of criteria, including socio-cultural and environmental values. An ethnic group in Bénin, for instance, used a range of criteria including the plant’s nutritive value, popularity, absence of taboos, its availability, and the energy input necessary for processing  ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bl1WRtfu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(N\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Danikou, 2015)","plainCitation":"(N’Danikou, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32880,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WALRCBHV"],"itemData":{"id":32880,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Background: Cultivation is the most appropriate management option when both demand and harvesting of wild plant species increase beyond natural production levels. In the current study we made the assumption that, besides the intrinsic biological and ecological characteristics of the species, the decision to cultivate and/or to conserve an overharvested wild plant species is triggered by the socioeconomic factors such as land tenure and size, origin of respondents, gender, and users' knowledge of the plant phenology. Methods: We carried out semi-structured interviews with 178 informants involved in V. doniana exploitation. The data collected were related to socio-demographic characteristics of informants' household situation, knowledge of the biology and propagation of the species, willingness to cultivate the species, in-situ maintenance of populations, and costs associated with management of the species. According to data types we used Student's t, Spearman correlation, Kruskal-Wallis, Fisher's exact and X-2 tests to test the effects of land tenure, origin of respondents, gender and users' knowledge of plant phenology on the decision making process. Conditional inference tree models and generalized additive models were also used to identify variables which were significantly determinant in the decision to cultivate and/or to conserve the species in-situ. Results: We found that men were more willing to cultivate the species than women and this is conditioned by land area available. The willingness to conserve the species in-situ depends mainly on the total land area available, the number of trees within the landscape, accessibility of the trees, land tenure, gender, location, seedling cost, and trade-off cost for conservation. People who offered more than one US dollar to acquire a seedling of V. doniana, landowners, and those who own a total land area in excess of 6.5 ha were most willing to conserve the species in-situ. Conclusions: From our findings we conclude that future management and conservation initiatives for V. doniana should first target specific user groups for sustainable exploitation of the species. Also, the Cultivation Opportunity Ratio is an important indicator for quick determination of the likelihood of farmers to engage into cultivation and conservation of the species.","container-title":"JOURNAL OF ETHNOBIOLOGY AND ETHNOMEDICINE","DOI":"10.1186/s13002-015-0017-3","ISSN":"1746-4269","language":"English","title":"Modelling socioeconomic determinants for cultivation and in-situ conservation of Vitex doniana Sweet (Black plum), a wild harvested economic plant in Benin","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"N'Danikou","given":"Sognigbe"},{"family":"Achigan-Dako","given":"Enoch G."},{"family":"Tchokponhoue","given":"Dedeou A."},{"family":"Agossou","given":"Chaldia O. A."},{"family":"Houdegbe","given":"Carlos A."},{"family":"Vodouhe","given":"Raymond S."},{"family":"Ahanchede","given":"Adam"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015",4,30]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The community also considered the perceived value of the species over their rarity for conservation. Wild species, especially wild edible plants, are considered important to maintain cultural identity ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"agqe5JCQ","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Schunko & Vogl, 2018; Seeland & Staniszewski, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Schunko & Vogl, 2018; Seeland & Staniszewski, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33054,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/H56TNN47"],"itemData":{"id":33054,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"European countries are split over the appreciation of wild berries, fruits, mushrooms, and herbs. While some countries provide public statistics on wild plants, others seem to neglect wild plant gathering and commercialization. In this study, we aimed to understand if wild plant commercialization is neglected or irrelevant in Austria, a country that does not provide statistics. We focus on organic producers, because organic certification of wild plant gathering might have potential for countering frequent concerns about commercial gathering, including destructive gathering and overharvesting. Using a mixed-methods approach with a concurrent triangulation design, databases of six organic certification bodies were analysed concurrently with semi-structured expert interviews of their representatives. We found that organic certification for gathering was issued to 1.5% of organic producers in the year 2016 in Austria and is relevant for three distinct gatherer types: regular, diversified, and single-plant gatherers. Organic gathering is most frequently part of agricultural or horticultural farms and rarely an isolated commercial activity. It is related to mixed farming, deepening on-farm diversification, and contributes to maintaining traditions, as well as the local socio-ecological memory of wild plant products. Organic wild plants are directly marketed to consumers as traditional and innovative products, but also supplied to mass markets. We conclude that from a socio-cultural perspective and a focus on regional economies, organic gathering is neglected in Austria, whereas from an income perspective, wild plant gathering seems to be indeed relevant for few organic producers, although exhibiting potential.","container-title":"Sustainability","issue":"11","page":"1-14","title":"Is the Commercialization of Wild Plants by Organic Producers in Austria Neglected or Irrelevant?","URL":"https://ideas.repec.org/a/gam/jsusta/v10y2018i11p3989-d179612.html","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Schunko","given":"Christoph"},{"family":"Vogl","given":"Christian R."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",10]]}}},{"id":33067,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LAFJPW2F"],"itemData":{"id":33067,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Small-scale forestry","issue":"4","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"411–422","title":"Indicators for a European cross-country state-of-the-art assessment of non-timber forest products and services","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Seeland","given":"Klaus"},{"family":"Staniszewski","given":"Pawel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and spirituality ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"5ibq4rJe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Hummer, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Hummer, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33068,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Q3GN2YYJ"],"itemData":{"id":33068,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"HortScience","issue":"4","note":"publisher: American Society for Horticultural Science","page":"413–417","title":"Manna in winter: indigenous Americans, huckleberries, and blueberries","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Hummer","given":"Kim E"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Wild food is considered as a mark of local and regional traditions and is an irreplaceable expression of natural and cultural heritage ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"euAKT9w4","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Pardo de Santayana, 2012; Seeland & Staniszewski, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Pardo de Santayana, 2012; Seeland & Staniszewski, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33098,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LWTU33RW"],"itemData":{"id":33098,"type":"book","collection-number":"99","collection-title":"Ambienta: La revista del Ministerio de Medio Ambiente","language":"es","publisher":"Ministerio de Agricultura, Pesca y Alimentación (España)","title":"Etnología y biodiversidad: El inventario español de los conocimientos tradicionales","author":[{"family":"Pardo de Santayana","given":"M."},{"family":"Aceituno-Mata","given":"L."},{"family":"Morales Valverde","given":"R."},{"family":"Molina","given":"M."},{"family":"Tardío","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}},{"id":33067,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LAFJPW2F"],"itemData":{"id":33067,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Small-scale forestry","issue":"4","note":"publisher: Springer","page":"411–422","title":"Indicators for a European cross-country state-of-the-art assessment of non-timber forest products and services","volume":"6","author":[{"family":"Seeland","given":"Klaus"},{"family":"Staniszewski","given":"Pawel"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For instance, wild foods have been part of traditional cuisines across the world, thus imparting cultural identities to various communities. 
      
Box 4.37. Generational transmission of ancient healing knowledge – Tibetan Amchis

Traditional Tibetan medical doctors and the traditional practitioners called Amchis, who use herbal remedies and analyze pulse, urine, and tongue to diagnose disease and are one of the oldest surviving medical traditions of the world. It has been popular throughout the central Asian regions of Mongolia, Bhutan, some parts of China (Tibet Autonomous Region), Nepal, the Himalayan regions of India, and a few areas of the former Soviet Union. In the trans-Himalayan district Dolpa of Nepal, Amchi knowledge is passed down through dedicated apprenticeships under the tutelage of senior Amchi ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"v1FohTtL","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bhattarai, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Bhattarai, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32879,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/SWRYG29H"],"itemData":{"id":32879,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Plant diversity plays an important role in maintaining the world's foods demands. Even today in Manang, a remote, mountainous district within the Annapurna Conservation Area, Nepal, local people gather substantial amounts of wild plants to meet their daily nutritional needs, with several species also used for trade. There has been little documented regarding the use of these wild foods, which play a part in both daily nutrition, and survival during times of famine. Moreover, planning for development, land use, economic growth and biodiversity conservation has not taken them into account. Information about edible wild plant use was gathered by interviewing knowledgeable villagers over a period of five years (2002-2006). In total, 41 plant species were used as sources of fruits, juice, vegetables, and achar (local relish or pickle). Several wild food plants that need intensive processing before consumption were documented, including the calcium oxalate containing Arisaema species. The traditional knowledge of use of wild food plants is decreasing in many parts of the world with the introduction of modern packed food items and Manang is not the exception. The results of this research will help to play a catalytic role to encourage dialogue among the people of Manang, and national and international scientific communities regarding long term bioprospecting research, and shape the creation of a rural livelihood strategy. Analysis of the nutritive and health values of some of these edible plant species may encourage the scientific community to build partnerships with local community to work towards long term sustainable utilization and conservation.","container-title":"ECOLOGY OF FOOD AND NUTRITION","DOI":"10.1080/03670240802034996","ISSN":"0367-0244","issue":"1","language":"English","page":"1-20","title":"Wild Edible Plants Used by the People of Manang District, Central Nepal","volume":"48","author":[{"family":"Bhattarai","given":"Shandesh"},{"family":"Chaudhary","given":"Ram Prasad"},{"family":"Taylor","given":"Robin S. L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Dolpa – a northern and remote district of Nepal, more than 400 plant species were documented, and the area was found to be exceptionally rich in ethno-medicine and indigenous knowledge ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"WN9nXU8k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ghimire, 1999, 2001a)","plainCitation":"(Ghimire, 1999, 2001a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33090,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S4BVFN6T"],"itemData":{"id":33090,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"issue: 38\\npublisher-place: WWF Nepal Program, Kathmandu, Nepal\\ncollection-title: WWF Nepal Program Report Series","title":"Conservation of the Plant Resources, Community Development and Training in Applied Ethnobotany at Shey Phoksundo National Park and its Buffer-zone, Dolpa","author":[{"family":"Ghimire","given":"S.K."},{"family":"Parajuli","given":"D.B."},{"family":"Gurung","given":"T.N."},{"family":"Lama","given":"Y.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":33085,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/DP6DKEMB"],"itemData":{"id":33085,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"issue: 41\\npublisher-place: WWF Nepal Program, Kathmandu, Nepal\\ncollection-title: Fourth Year. WWF Nepal Program Report Series","title":"Conservation of the Plant Resources, Community Development and Training in Applied Ethnobotany at Shey Phoksundo National Park and its Buffer-zone, Dolpa","author":[{"family":"Ghimire","given":"S.K."},{"family":"Lama","given":"Y.C."},{"family":"Tripathi","given":"G.R."},{"family":"Schmit","given":"S."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Y.Aumeeruddy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, including use of caterpillar fungus (Ophiocordyceps sinensis) for the treatment of various diseases ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Ny8HTK82","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Devkota, 2006)","plainCitation":"(Devkota, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32889,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/BT48RECB"],"itemData":{"id":32889,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Cordyceps sinensis (Berk.) Sacc., a well-known and valued traditional medicine, is also called winter worm summer grass. The product deserves high potential to generate income opportunities, enhance rural income and raise the national revenue. The study of C. sinensis was carried out in Dolpa district, Western Nepal to document ethnomycological uses and local practices. Data were collected using open ended questionnaires administered to collectors/users (n = 74), traders (n = 25) and local healers or amchis (n = 3). Indigenous peoples are utilizing this Himalayan treasure for the treatment of different diseases like diarrhea, headache, cough, rheumatism, liver disease, and also as an aphrodisiac and tonic. Internationally it is regarded as Himalayan Viagra. The present study therefore aims at highlighting the ethnomycological uses of C. sinensis resource of the Trans-Himalayan region in their niche.","container-title":"Our Nature","DOI":"10.3126/on.v4i1.502","ISSN":"2091-2781, 1991-2951","issue":"1","journalAbbreviation":"Our Nature","language":"en","page":"48-52","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Yarsagumba [<i>Cordyceps sinensis</i> (Berk.) Sacc.]; Traditional Utilization in Dolpa District, Western Nepal","URL":"https://www.nepjol.info/index.php/ON/article/view/502","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Devkota","given":"S."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. According to Tibetan concept of illness, disease arises when the dynamic equilibrium between the three psycho-physiological conditions or ‘humors’ (nyepa-sum) translated as wing (lüng), bile (tripa) and phlem (beken) are disturbed ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"3Ohvnyfu","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Khang, 1981; Lama, 2001; McGehee, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Khang, 1981; Lama, 2001; McGehee, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33077,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5FPRTHD"],"itemData":{"id":33077,"type":"chapter","container-title":"People and Plants Initiatives, WWF Nepal Program","event-place":"Kathmandu, Nepal","language":"en","publisher-place":"Kathmandu, Nepal","title":"Aumeeruddy Thomas. in collaboration with the amchis of Dolpo","author":[{"family":"Lama","given":"C.Y."},{"family":"Ghimire","given":"S.K."},{"literal":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":33095,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VWVAZZCS"],"itemData":{"id":33095,"type":"book","event-place":"New Delhi","language":"pt","publisher":"Indraprashta Press","publisher-place":"New Delhi","title":"Fundamentals of Tibetan medicines","author":[{"family":"Khang","given":"Men Tsee"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1981"]]}}},{"id":33076,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/WULK294C"],"itemData":{"id":33076,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Independent Study Project (ISP) Collection","language":"en","title":"Lost in translation teaching, translation, and transliteration of Amchi medicine in Nepal","URL":"https://digitalcollections.sit.edu/isp_collection/1450","author":[{"literal":"McGehee"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Amchis diagnose the disease without any sophisticated instruments by determining whether the nature of the disease as ‘hot’ (tsa) or ‘cold’ (dang) ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"z3auARD5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Lama, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Lama, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33077,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5FPRTHD"],"itemData":{"id":33077,"type":"chapter","container-title":"People and Plants Initiatives, WWF Nepal Program","event-place":"Kathmandu, Nepal","language":"en","publisher-place":"Kathmandu, Nepal","title":"Aumeeruddy Thomas. in collaboration with the amchis of Dolpo","author":[{"family":"Lama","given":"C.Y."},{"family":"Ghimire","given":"S.K."},{"literal":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The basis of their diagnosis is analysis of pulse, urine and tongue. The treatment of disease involves the use of herbal medicines as well as moxibustion and bloodletting ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"U8s0xtvI","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ghimire, 1999, 2001b; Lama, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Ghimire, 1999, 2001b; Lama, 2001)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33090,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S4BVFN6T"],"itemData":{"id":33090,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"issue: 38\\npublisher-place: WWF Nepal Program, Kathmandu, Nepal\\ncollection-title: WWF Nepal Program Report Series","title":"Conservation of the Plant Resources, Community Development and Training in Applied Ethnobotany at Shey Phoksundo National Park and its Buffer-zone, Dolpa","author":[{"family":"Ghimire","given":"S.K."},{"family":"Parajuli","given":"D.B."},{"family":"Gurung","given":"T.N."},{"family":"Lama","given":"Y.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1999"]]}}},{"id":33096,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/3RY5FHBY"],"itemData":{"id":33096,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"issue: 41\\npublisher-place: WWF Nepal Program, Kathmandu, Nepal\\ncollection-title: Fourth Year. WWF Nepal Program Report Series","title":"Conservation of the Plant Resources, Community Development and Training in Applied Ethnobotany at Shey Phoksundo National Park and its Buffer-zone, Dolpa","author":[{"family":"Ghimire","given":"S.K."},{"family":"Lama","given":"Y.C."},{"family":"Tripathi","given":"G.R."},{"family":"Schmit","given":"S."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"Y.Aumeeruddy"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":33077,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/I5FPRTHD"],"itemData":{"id":33077,"type":"chapter","container-title":"People and Plants Initiatives, WWF Nepal Program","event-place":"Kathmandu, Nepal","language":"en","publisher-place":"Kathmandu, Nepal","title":"Aumeeruddy Thomas. in collaboration with the amchis of Dolpo","author":[{"family":"Lama","given":"C.Y."},{"family":"Ghimire","given":"S.K."},{"literal":"Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Amchi medicine animal parts, and various minerals, stones, such as alum, calcium, camphor, copper, emerald, gold, granite, iron, lead, limonite, magnesium, mercury, sapphire, quartz, talcum, zinc, etc.ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2bCv7zIj","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Khang, 1981)","plainCitation":"(Khang, 1981)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33095,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VWVAZZCS"],"itemData":{"id":33095,"type":"book","event-place":"New Delhi","language":"pt","publisher":"Indraprashta Press","publisher-place":"New Delhi","title":"Fundamentals of Tibetan medicines","author":[{"family":"Khang","given":"Men Tsee"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1981"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, are also used for the preparation of medicine. However, Amchis face great difficulties in getting some of these products because of legal restrictions ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"yid9Pfx8","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rokaya, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Rokaya, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33089,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X6W5JVCD"],"itemData":{"id":33089,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Banko Janakari","language":"it","page":"13–18","title":"Ethnoecology of natural environment in trans-himalayan region of west Nepal","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Rokaya","given":"M.B."},{"family":"Shrestha","given":"M.R."},{"family":"Ghimire","given":"S.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Training and less restrictive guidelines for collection of medicinal resources, would assist local people in scaling up production, generating income and preserving an age-old healing practice ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"zbNulqjh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rokaya, 2005)","plainCitation":"(Rokaya, 2005)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33089,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/X6W5JVCD"],"itemData":{"id":33089,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Banko Janakari","language":"it","page":"13–18","title":"Ethnoecology of natural environment in trans-himalayan region of west Nepal","volume":"15","author":[{"family":"Rokaya","given":"M.B."},{"family":"Shrestha","given":"M.R."},{"family":"Ghimire","given":"S.K."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  


      A study by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"UmZDpaf5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Ali-Shtayeh, 2008)","plainCitation":"(Ali-Shtayeh, 2008)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33087,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/P3PWAVDA"],"itemData":{"id":33087,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","issue":"1","language":"en","page":"1–13","title":"Traditional knowledge of wild edible plants used in Palestine (Northern West Bank): a comparative study","volume":"4","author":[{"family":"Ali-Shtayeh","given":"M.S."},{"family":"Jamous","given":"R.M."},{"family":"Al-Shafie","given":"J.H."},{"family":"Elgharabah","given":"W.A."},{"family":"Kherfan","given":"F.A."},{"family":"Qarariah","given":"K.H."},{"family":"Nasrallah","given":"H.A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2008"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} of 15 communities living under Palestinian Authority, communities were found to gather nearly 100 wild edible plant species and to consume many of them cooked. For example, the leaves of Rumex acetosa are used as filling for a traditional pie called 'sambosek' or Majorana syriaca is used for preparing a traditional recipe that is popular in Palestinian communities called 'za'tar'. A few of these wild plants are mentioned in local folk songs and proverbs (Malva sylvestris, Gundelia tournefortii, Salvia fruticosa). The study also highlighted how some wild plants are considered sacred as they are mentioned in holy books (e.g., Majorana syriaca in the bible), or blessed for being mentioned in legends linked with holy people (e.g., Salvia fruticosa and Virgin Mary). In Northern India, similar findings are reported ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"p6oqZTse","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chandra, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Chandra, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33078,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LMIRQHCY"],"itemData":{"id":33078,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Where wild species of temperate Himalaya, like Dioscorea belophylla (locally known","author":[{"literal":"Chandra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} where wild species of temperate Himalaya, like Dioscorea belophylla (locally known as Tairu) are used in traditional recipes to be consumed during winter, especially at festivals ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aheQduTD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Chandra, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Chandra, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33078,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/LMIRQHCY"],"itemData":{"id":33078,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Where wild species of temperate Himalaya, like Dioscorea belophylla (locally known","author":[{"literal":"Chandra"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      Indigenous peoples and local communities in many regions depend on flora and fauna for food provisioning and cultural uses. In many cases, these uses are non-commercial (i.e., subsistence); in other cases, flora and fauna are shared through commercial markets (i.e., products sold commercially); in both cases, flora and fauna make important contributions to health and welfare of communities ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Jdu1FJup","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Cheikhyoussef & Embashu, 2013)","plainCitation":"(Cheikhyoussef & Embashu, 2013)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32906,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RPS7FBBQ"],"itemData":{"id":32906,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"BACKGROUND: Indigenous communities in Namibia possess a rich indigenous knowledge expressed within many practices of these communities. Indigenous wild edible fruits are available along the Namibian 13 regions of which it forms a rich source of vitamins, fibres, minerals and a heterogeneous collection of bioactive compounds referred to as phytochemicals for indigenous people's diet. The aim of this study was to record the different IKS practices on the indigenous fruit trees in Ohangwena and Oshikoto regions of Namibia.$\\\\backslash$n$\\\\backslash$nMETHODS: An ethnobotanical survey was undertaken to collect information from local communities from 23-29 October 2011. Data was collected through the use of questionnaires and personal interviews during field trips in the Ohangwena and Oshikoto regions. A total of 65 respondents were interviewed; 54%; women, 38%; men and 8%; both in group interviews.$\\\\backslash$n$\\\\backslash$nRESULTS: The majority of the people interviewed were in their thirty's, with the youngest being 18 years old and the oldest being 98 years old. Forty three plant specimens were collected from the two regions; these specimens belong to 20 genera and 25 species. Regarding to the indigenous knowledge; 87%; of the respondents indicated that their knowledge on indigenous fruits was learnt mainly through their parent. Indigenous people's perception on declining indigenous fruits revealed that 56.3%; of the respondents reported that indigenous fruits were declining. Only a 42.2%; indicated that the indigenous fruits populations are increasing. Regarding to the management practices to improve the production of these indigenous fruit trees; 38.6%; reported that there are some efforts on management practices; on the other hand 61.4%; reported there are no management practices on the indigenous fruit trees in their areas. Four species were found to be the most frequently used and mentioned fruits which need to be given high preference in terms of conservation are: Berchemia discolor, Hyphaene petersiana, Sclerocarya birrea and Diospyros mespiliformis. The following diseases and ailments have been reported to be treated by the indigenous fruit trees which include: toothache, diarrhoea, cough, tonsillitis, burns, skin allergy, stomach ache, snake bit, constipation, etc. 28%; of the respondents mentioned an ethno-veterinary use(s) of these fruits, e.g. the use of the Ziziphus mucronata roots to treat diarrhoea in cattle, the bark of eembe (Berchemia discolor) to treat calf weakness.$\\\\backslash$n$\\\\backslash$nCONCLUSIONS: The local communities in Oshikoto and Ohangwena regions have relatively good knowledge and practices regarding the indigenous fruit. This study enhances our understanding on the indigenous fruit in Namibia and their uses by local communities.","container-title":"Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine","DOI":"10.1186/1746-4269-9-34","ISSN":"17464269","issue":"1","title":"Ethnobotanical knowledge on indigenous fruits in Ohangwena and Oshikoto regions in Northern Namibia","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Cheikhyoussef","given":"Ahmad"},{"family":"Embashu","given":"Werner"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2013"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
      The contributions of flora and fauna to improved health outcomes are key for many Indigenous Peoples and local communities. Clinically proven and perceived health benefits are also driving broader use of flora and fauna. In many countries common ailments, are treated at the household level, since diseases are considered to be connected with natural causes and hence their symptoms. This, in turn, can trigger unsustainable use. Moreover, large number of wild animals are consumed as ornaments, medicines, cosmetics, weapons, leather, and luxury lifestyle products (e.g., fur, wool…etc.). From large mammals to amphibians, unsustainable exploitation of wild species is often driven by social status and lifestyle, leading to uncontrolled exploitation of wild species. For instance, luxury seafood such as sharkfin soup, live reef food fish…etc. are often associated with traditional Chinese culture and medicine ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"4V9gwUcV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dell\\\\uc0\\\\u8217{}Apa, 2014; Fabinyi, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Dell’Apa, 2014; Fabinyi, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32876,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2KL4P6W3"],"itemData":{"id":32876,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environmental Management","DOI":"10.1007/s00267-014-0291-1","issue":"2","page":"151-161","title":"The influence of culture on the international management of shark finning","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84905569622&doi=10.1007%2fs00267-014-0291-1&partnerID=40&md5=ae6a5e865c4c6a6d6f828e19a05cc48e","volume":"54","author":[{"family":"Dell'Apa","given":"A."},{"family":"Chad Smith","given":"M."},{"family":"Kaneshiro-Pineiro","given":"M.Y."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2014"]]}}},{"id":32875,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2LYKP5L9"],"itemData":{"id":32875,"type":"article-journal","archive":"Scopus","container-title":"Environmental Conservation","DOI":"10.1017/S0376892911000609","issue":"1","page":"83-92","title":"Historical, cultural and social perspectives on luxury seafood consumption in China","URL":"https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84856899980&doi=10.1017%2fS0376892911000609&partnerID=40&md5=07163ed95e0a878c6115def83a92b7bd","volume":"39","author":[{"family":"Fabinyi","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Although in most cases such perceptions are misguided, the perceived benefits trigger high demand and consequently unsustainable exploitation of wild species.
      
4.2.5.2.8. Relational values of wild fauna 
Nature occupies a privileged place in the symbolism produced by cultures around the world. Among all the elements of nature, animals are considered as privileged communication partners in the processes of knowledge of humans. As expressed by ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pBtYJPyi","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dounias, 2007a)","plainCitation":"(Dounias, 2007a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2HCBSE4R"],"itemData":{"id":33107,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Animal symbolism. Animals, keystone in the relationship between Man and Nature?","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Paris\\npublisher: IRD","title":"Le symbolisme des animaux. L'animal, clef de voûte de la relation entre l'homme et la nature ?","author":[{"family":"Dounias","given":"E."},{"family":"Motte-Florac","given":"É."},{"literal":"Dunham"}],"editor":[{"literal":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, the animal-symbol is the means by which man constantly strives to distinguish himself from or, on the contrary, to resemble, hoping to acquire its ability to liberate himself from certain human constraints. This animal is usually a real, tangible animal, and often a wild one. The species chosen differ from culture to culture and according to environmental, geographical, social, and historical contexts, as well as the psychological context, because all relations with animals call upon people’s sensitivities and shared emotions. The formation (birth, with its reasons, choice modes, etc.), form (universe of signs, dialogue between concrete inventions, oral traditions and writing), meaning (cosmogonies, myths, taxonomies), function (of mediator, unifier, pedagogue, socializer...) of animal-symbol ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"i8b6H2sM","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dounias, 2007a)","plainCitation":"(Dounias, 2007a)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33107,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/2HCBSE4R"],"itemData":{"id":33107,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Animal symbolism. Animals, keystone in the relationship between Man and Nature?","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Paris\\npublisher: IRD","title":"Le symbolisme des animaux. L'animal, clef de voûte de la relation entre l'homme et la nature ?","author":[{"family":"Dounias","given":"E."},{"family":"Motte-Florac","given":"É."},{"literal":"Dunham"}],"editor":[{"literal":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} illustrate the complex nature of the relationships between humans and wild species, the  variability (sometimes of contradictions), multiplicity (of the layered levels of meaning), ambivalence, and also their constant reactivation or recreation.
      The analysis of the local taxonomies is recognized as particularly efficient in documenting the place or the customary value that different species occupied for the indigenous peoples and local communities. Process of categorization is also considered as one of the most powerful solutions used by human to break up the world’s complexity and there are a number of examples to illustrate this. A key finding from the indigenous and local knowledge workshop is that there can be sanctions or punishments for violating customary rules and norms designed to assure good relations with species and with nature.
      
4.2.5.2.9. Iconic species for indigenous peoples and local communities 
Two major types of iconic species designation processes can be identified: 
* exogenous ones that originate at the international scale: foreign actors, environmental lobbies, large non-governmental conservation organizations, scientific experts. One only has to cite it in the International Union for Conservation of Nature red list or at the Appendices of the Convention on International Trade of Endangered Species.
   The history of these exogenous designations is well documented ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"TqtdnpQX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(W. M. Adams, 2006; Grove, 1996; Mackenzie, 1998; Neumann, 1998)","plainCitation":"(W. M. Adams, 2006; Grove, 1996; Mackenzie, 1998; Neumann, 1998)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33104,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y22JPAYK"],"itemData":{"id":33104,"type":"book","event-place":"Cambrige","language":"en","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambrige","title":"Green Imperialism. Colonial Expansion, Tropical Island Edens and the Origins of Environmentalism, 1600-1860","author":[{"family":"Grove","given":"R.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}},{"id":33106,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NAGRD2FL"],"itemData":{"id":33106,"type":"document","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Manchester","publisher":"Manchester Univ","title":"The empire of nature. Hunting, conservation and British imperialism","author":[{"family":"Mackenzie","given":"J.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":32877,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6Z8NQE3E"],"itemData":{"id":32877,"type":"book","event-place":"Berkeley","ISBN":"0-520-21178-2","publisher":"University of California Press","publisher-place":"Berkeley","title":"Imposing Wilderness: Struggles Over Livelihood and Nature Preservation in Africa","author":[{"family":"Neumann","given":"Roderick P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":33081,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KIR8L7KB"],"itemData":{"id":33081,"type":"article-journal","language":"en","page":"19","source":"Zotero","title":"The Future of Sustainability: Re-thinking Environment and Development in the Twenty-first Century","author":[{"family":"Adams","given":"W M"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. They date from the late nineteenth century with the establishment during the colonial period of game reserves for hunting by foreigners and continue today in the creation of national parks by modern states ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"fdCrqQQs","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Rodary, 2003)","plainCitation":"(Rodary, 2003)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32822,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5U9SKTFG"],"itemData":{"id":32822,"type":"book","event-place":"Paris","language":"fr","publisher":"Karthala","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Conservation de la nature et développement : l’intégration impossible ?","editor":[{"family":"Rodary","given":"E."},{"family":"Castellanet","given":"C."},{"family":"Rossi","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. This history is replete with exactions made on the peoples living in or adjacent to the protected areas who have frequently been expelled, displaced and deprived of access to and use of their customary resources. It also provides many examples of imbalanced land-use patterns between protected areas that are little more than private reserves of a foreign elite engaged in trophy hunting or nature-based tourism, and non-protected areas that are subject to intense demographic pressure and overuse of resources. 
*  endogenous ones (indigenous peoples and local communities and their organizations) that are more locally derived. In many instances, the two merge and thus become indistinguishable in origin ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"s0Thz9ES","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fairhead & Leach, 2003; Luning, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Fairhead & Leach, 2003; Luning, 2012)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33071,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/8V27V8CB"],"itemData":{"id":33071,"type":"book","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","title":"Science, society and power: environmental knowledge and policy in West Africa and the Caribbean","author":[{"family":"Fairhead","given":"James"},{"family":"Leach","given":"Melissa"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2003"]]}}},{"id":33072,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AWHFRESG"],"itemData":{"id":33072,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Africa Today","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"23–39","title":"Processing promises of gold: A minefield of company–community relations in Burkina Faso","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Luning","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
   The Paris Convention, for example, takes into consideration the aesthetic, symbolic and historical value of retained sites.
   
4.2.5.2.10. Changes in customary values and status and effect on the use of wild species
Certain elements can lose their customary (or privileged) status while others might acquire it.  The content of a custom is thus susceptible to alterations in the context of religious and political change, environmental transformations or diffusion of new ideas, products and peoples (through migration, for example). In Africa the erosion or transformation of ancestral cults by the spread of Islam and Christianity has turned sacred groves into profane places that are no longer protected from the axe ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"2H4FfAYR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dugast, 2002; Juh\\\\uc0\\\\u233{}-Beaulaton & Roussel, 2002)","plainCitation":"(Dugast, 2002; Juhé-Beaulaton & Roussel, 2002)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32983,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NCVWYXZ2"],"itemData":{"id":32983,"type":"document","language":"fr","note":"publisher-place: Paris","publisher":"IRD","title":"Modes d'appréhension de la nature et gestion patrimoniale du miieu\\".In M.C","author":[{"family":"Dugast","given":"S."}],"editor":[{"literal":"Cormier-Salem"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}},{"id":32846,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/69B228PQ"],"itemData":{"id":32846,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Patrimonialiser la nature tropicale. Dynamiques locales, enjeux internationaux","event-place":"Paris","language":"fr","page":"415–438","publisher":"IRD","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Les sites religieux vodun: des patrimoines en permanente évolution","author":[{"family":"Juhé-Beaulaton","given":"D."},{"family":"Roussel","given":"B."}],"editor":[{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"J.-B.M.-C."},{"family":"D.","given":"Boutrais"},{"literal":"J."},{"family":"Roussel","given":"B."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2002"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Thus, certain elements that were previously the object of implicit or explicit conservation measures have now lost their meaning and value and are no longer preserved. Such is the case with formerly taboo animals. Conversely, new natural elements can be invested with a patrimonial or identity dimension. For example, in Sub-saharan Africa, indigenous peoples and local communities link land degradation to the erosion of customary institutions and knowledge, to ritual neglect, and to the diminished authority of land priests. While the Maane (Burkina Faso) recognize that demographic pressure has produced demand for arable land on the Moose (Mossi) plateau, they ultimately interpret the disappearance of the bush as a consequence of a breakdown in their socio-cultural system ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OayGy0SG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Luning, 2012)","plainCitation":"(Luning, 2012)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33072,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/AWHFRESG"],"itemData":{"id":33072,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Africa Today","issue":"3","language":"en","page":"23–39","title":"Processing promises of gold: A minefield of company–community relations in Burkina Faso","volume":"58","author":[{"family":"Luning","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2012"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In Mauritania, Imraguen fisherfolk ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"pmuTUXQn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Artaud, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Artaud, 2021)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32911,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TNCPQF3W"],"itemData":{"id":32911,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Marine species and biodiversity : new anthropological perspectives","event-place":"Paris","language":"en","publisher":"MNHN","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Species “good for thinking”: species “good to preserve”? The paradox of marine species co-management","author":[{"family":"Artaud","given":"H."}],"editor":[{"family":"Artaud","given":"H."},{"family":"Chlous","given":"F."},{"family":"Levain","given":"A."},{"family":"M. Mariat-Roy","given":"E.P."},{"literal":"Paris"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}: cultural values attached to 2 species (turtle and manatee): it's to-day loss of strength or its rebirth. While the manatee has lost its cultural relevance with the end of relations between herders and coastal communities, the importance of the turtle has doubled since the development of the National Park of Banc d’Arguin.

Box 4.38. The changing status of shark 

From the (artisanal) fisherfolk knowledge, the shark is neither a predator nor a prey, but rather a social partner, with various protective, vigilante or even totemic status for certain lineages in Pacific islands ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"RPFQvUxH","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bataille-Benguigui, 1988)","plainCitation":"(Bataille-Benguigui, 1988)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33056,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6DVAC7DW"],"itemData":{"id":33056,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"The journal of the Polynesian Society","issue":"2","language":"en","page":"185– 198","title":"The Fish of Tonga: Prey or Social Partners?","volume":"97","author":[{"family":"Bataille-Benguigui","given":"M.-C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1988"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} or in Africa ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Yeg1wZQ5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M.-C. Cormier-Salem, 2006)","plainCitation":"(M.-C. Cormier-Salem, 2006)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32870,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/KXNULAHE"],"itemData":{"id":32870,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Pêche et aquaculture : pour une exploitation durable des ressources vivantes de la mer et du littoral","event-place":"Rennes","language":"fr","page":"141–160","publisher":"Presses Universitaires de Rennes","publisher-place":"Rennes","title":"Requins, raies et autres chimères en marche vers le patrimoine","author":[{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."}],"editor":[{"family":"Chaussade","given":"J."},{"family":"Guillaume","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2006"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The shark only became a target species with the removal of fins, which has resulted in many social and spatial changes on tropical coasts (Africa, Madagascar, Asia) 
Regarding the customary values of the shark within West African communities, three main relations can be schematically identified:  
	1) in some communities, estuarine, lagoon and island, the shark is considered to be game, the capture of which gives rise to collective hunting and meat is a delicacy. Smoked or salted-dried shark flesh constitutes an essential source of protein for certain forest and Sahelian populations; in certain coastal communities, it is a sought-after dish, and even locally, with a strong identity burden as among the Aïzi Ivorian lagoons ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"MY0DmPMy","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Verdeaux, 1981)","plainCitation":"(Verdeaux, 1981)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33074,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/E5FY2LHS"],"itemData":{"id":33074,"type":"document","language":"fr","title":"L'Aïzi pluriel: chronique d'une ethnie lagunaire de Côte d'Ivoire","author":[{"family":"Verdeaux","given":"F."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1981"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. 
	2) In others, on the contrary, among peasant-fisherfolk, it is an iconic animal, which gives rise to many myths and rites (see below initiation rituals of Bijjogo people, Bissau Guinea). Among the Bijagos, in Guinea Bissau, sharks are an emblematic species, as evidenced by the sumptuous prows of the Bijagos canoes bearing the image of this animal, ritual dances with masks dominated by shark (or rather fish) ostres saws) or initiation rites: young boys, to reach adulthood, should capture a shark and present its liver to the elders. This rite is very similar to that practiced among the Maasai populations of Kenya, which the writer J. Kessel helped to magnify: the morane does not become a man and can only take a woman if he has killed a lion with a knife and a javelin. For this hunt, he is adorned with hair similar to the lion's mane. This parallelism between the lion and the shark is also noted in certain coastal communities: in Wolof (Senegal), the generic name for the shark is gainde gej, that is to say literally "lion of the sea. Like the lion, who owes his power, his strength and his majesty, to be the king of the jungle, the shark, sea monster, is the king of fish, or more precisely the king of marine fauna. We don't fish it, but we hunt it like all great predators. The liver sample is not surprising because, with the heart, it is the most valued part of the body.  
      In the animistic pantheon of Diola de Casamance, marine animals are widely represented. Sharks, alongside manatees, dolphins and caimans, often have the status of totem animals. The origin myths of certain clans, for example in Kabrousse, clearly refer to the sea and a sea monster, cousin of the shark. In addition to its symbolic and cultural value, it should also be emphasized its nutritional and utilitarian importance. Investigations carried out in Casamance in the 1980s ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"Qo9EQOFh","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(M.-C. Cormier-Salem, 1992)","plainCitation":"(M.-C. Cormier-Salem, 1992)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33073,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZM4U3BQJ"],"itemData":{"id":33073,"type":"document","language":"fr","note":"publisher-place: THE","publisher":"Thèses). ORSTOM","title":"Gestion et évolution des espaces aquatiques: la Casamance","author":[{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1992"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} showed that incidental catches of sharks were "traditionally" remarkably valued: both the flesh (consumed smoked, in sauce, with rice) and the fat (processed in oil, soap, cosmetics and pharmaceuticals 15, etc.), the skin (flaking and tanned like leather), teeth and bones (various tools, such as hooks, jewelry, weapons) were used. 
	3) In most of the artisanal fisherfolk, the professional of the sea, who have knowledge of the sea, the shark has a privileged status, like all sea monsters, the consumption of which is prohibited. Shark is considered as an avenger, protector and repairer. In Senegal, among the migratory fisherfolk (Guet-Ndariens, Wolof, Lebu, Serer), the tradition recognizes various categories within sea creatures, distinguishing in particular good-luck or bad-luck fish. The shark capture is avoided.  Its flesh, red and bloody, is poorly valued (this food ban also affects tuna). If a fisherman is wounded by a shark, it is considered as the fair punishment for his fault. The "victim" has transgressed a prohibition, justifying the manifestation of the sea monster and the necessary intercession of the priest, whose incantatory formulas will make it possible to be reconciled with the sea and its creatures. 
	The shark's status has changed from that of a respected, feared, and avoided social partner to that of a target species with the globalization of the market and the arrival of new actors in the fisheries sector. The fining is a fishing system that targets the capture of selacians for their fins and has developed considerably since the 1980s in all coastal countries of the world to meet the demand of the Southeast Asian market. Shark fins are the basis of a Chinese soup appreciated for its gastronomic and healing properties. In West Africa, the specialization of certain fisherfolk in the “fining” is first of all due to the “old” shark fishermen, such as the Ghanaians (Fanti and Ahena) who, from the 1970s, controlled the salt-dried shark meat sector. Nowadays, they constitute important communities of migrants in Ivory Coast, but also in Senegal, Gambia, Guinea-Bissau, Republic of Guinea, playing a major role at all stages of the sector (fisherman, processor, trader). On their model, other actors, originating from the circles of fisherfolk – guet-ndarien, lebu, serer, niominka - specialize in fining. Finally, this sector is attracting a growing number of actors from the interior regions, foreign to the sector, farmers, breeders, who convert to fishing but are above all very present in the marketing and processing of sharks. With the development of fining, from the 1980s, the shark became a target species with high market value, and consequently some species of shark would have disappeared, others would have become rare. This is the case with sawfish (Pristis pristis), represented on all the coins. The main socio-cultural drivers of the unsustainable uses are the attractivity of this sector for new actors, coming from the hinterland with their uses and knowledge, the loss of traditions, a dysfunction of the customary institutions and of the old systems of control over the coast and its resources, a questioning of the knowledge and powers of the elders. Islamisation, the marginalisation of former customary chiefs, the loss of control of the elders over fishing grounds, non-compliance with prohibitions lead also to unsustainable practices.


4.2.5.2.11. Iconic species and the policy-making challenge for sustainable use 
The evolution of representations and their impact on the responsibilities and duties that indigenous peoples and local communities impose upon themselves as regards certain species, as well as their consequences, particularly in the domain of sustainable use and conservation are pretty well documented ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"HXX6fKKn","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Artaud, 2021; Dounias, 2007b)","plainCitation":"(Artaud, 2021; Dounias, 2007b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32911,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TNCPQF3W"],"itemData":{"id":32911,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Marine species and biodiversity : new anthropological perspectives","event-place":"Paris","language":"en","publisher":"MNHN","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Species “good for thinking”: species “good to preserve”? The paradox of marine species co-management","author":[{"family":"Artaud","given":"H."}],"editor":[{"family":"Artaud","given":"H."},{"family":"Chlous","given":"F."},{"family":"Levain","given":"A."},{"family":"M. Mariat-Roy","given":"E.P."},{"literal":"Paris"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}},{"id":32856,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W7B745UD"],"itemData":{"id":32856,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Animals, keystone in the relationship between Man and Nature?","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Paris\\npublisher: IRD","title":"Le symbolisme des animaux. L'animal, clef de voûte de la relation entre l'homme et la nature ? Animal symbolism","editor":[{"family":"Dounias","given":"E."},{"family":"Motte-Florac","given":"É."},{"family":"Dunham","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Besides, differences in wild species norms and values (cf fig below with 3 categories of values): ecological, economical, anthropocentric or customary, between stakeholders (indigenous peoples and local communities, non-governmental organizations, managers, decision-makers, etc.), locally, nationally and internationally, and even within local populations (between farmers and fisherfolk, herders and collectors, but also according to gender, origin, status, etc.;) are at the root of environmental violence around the world, which have also given rise to many accounts ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"DfZDbkiB","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Neumann, 1998, 2015; N. L. Peluso, 1993; N. Peluso & Watts, 2001)","plainCitation":"(Neumann, 1998, 2015; N. L. Peluso, 1993; N. Peluso & Watts, 2001)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32877,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/6Z8NQE3E"],"itemData":{"id":32877,"type":"book","event-place":"Berkeley","ISBN":"0-520-21178-2","publisher":"University of California Press","publisher-place":"Berkeley","title":"Imposing Wilderness: Struggles Over Livelihood and Nature Preservation in Africa","author":[{"family":"Neumann","given":"Roderick P."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1998"]]}}},{"id":32830,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/33HZNVCP"],"itemData":{"id":32830,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The Routledge handbook of political ecology","event-place":"Abingdon, Oxon ; New York","language":"en","page":"391–405","publisher":"NY Routledge","publisher-place":"Abingdon, Oxon ; New York","title":"Nature conservation","author":[{"family":"Neumann","given":"R.P."}],"editor":[{"family":"Perreault","given":"G.B.T."},{"family":"McCarthy","given":"J."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}},{"id":32666,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/JF6EAXXK"],"itemData":{"id":32666,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Global Environmental Change","issue":"2","language":"it","page":"199–217","title":"Coercing conservation?: The politics of state resource control","volume":"3","author":[{"family":"Peluso","given":"N.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}},{"id":33103,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/72VIRSY9"],"itemData":{"id":33103,"type":"book","event-place":"Ithaca","language":"en","publisher":"Cornell University press","publisher-place":"Ithaca","title":"Violent Environments","editor":[{"family":"Peluso","given":"N."},{"family":"Watts","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The divergence of values regarding wild species (ecological versus anthropocentric, economical versus socio-cultural) most often are at the root of inefficient policies, environmental injustice and unsustainable use.


Figure 4.15 Values of wild species. License CC-BY.

      These conflicts accompanied the colonisation of the Southern part of the globe (colonial imperialism), then from the end of the 19th century onwards, what some call « green imperialism » with the rise of environmental lobbies ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"6VcMjalD","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Grove, 1996)","plainCitation":"(Grove, 1996)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33104,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/Y22JPAYK"],"itemData":{"id":33104,"type":"book","event-place":"Cambrige","language":"en","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","publisher-place":"Cambrige","title":"Green Imperialism. Colonial Expansion, Tropical Island Edens and the Origins of Environmentalism, 1600-1860","author":[{"family":"Grove","given":"R.H."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1996"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}, and finally the neoliberalism and the commodification of nature ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"hfZqTmif","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Fletcher, 2015)","plainCitation":"(Fletcher, 2015)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32897,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/S75STPJT"],"itemData":{"id":32897,"type":"chapter","container-title":"The International Handbook of Political Ecology","event-place":"Cheltenham, UK","language":"en","page":"359–372","publisher":"Edward Elgar","publisher-place":"Cheltenham, UK","title":"NatureTM Inc. : nature as neoliberal capitalist imaginery","author":[{"family":"Fletcher","given":"R."},{"family":"Dressler","given":"W."},{"family":"Büscher","given":"B."}],"editor":[{"family":"Bryant","given":"R.L."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The debates are always vehement (cf. in France regarding the reintroduction of large predators, wolves, lynx, bears, etc.). 
      There are examples and counter-examples of sustainable and unsustainable use of iconic species (or customary lands), notably due to the changes of policymaking regarding the land tenure or the enclosure of the commons during the colonial period. Customary claims on land within national park boundaries are pointed out by Neumann and others. Heritage claims in Africa often serve to mediate territorial and identity claims ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"JzPmuSBV","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Berry, 1993; M.-C. Cormier-Salem, 2005; Zerner, 2000)","plainCitation":"(Berry, 1993; M.-C. Cormier-Salem, 2005; Zerner, 2000)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32871,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9TNTBZ9F"],"itemData":{"id":32871,"type":"book","event-place":"Madison","language":"en","publisher":"the University of Wisconsin Press","publisher-place":"Madison","title":"No Condition is permanent. The social Dynamics of Agrarian Change in Sub-Saharan Africa","author":[{"family":"Berry","given":"S."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["1993"]]}}},{"id":34549,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/NRKMS6VC"],"itemData":{"id":34549,"type":"book","event-place":"Paris","ISBN":"978-2-7099-1560-1","language":"French and English.","note":"OCLC: 60740498","publisher":"IRD","publisher-place":"Paris","source":"Open WorldCat","title":"Patrimoines naturels au sud: territoires, identités et stratégies locales : ouvrage issu du séminaire organisé conjointement par le Département \\"hommes, natures, sociétés\\" du Muséum national d'histoire naturelle, le MALD, Mutation africaine sur la longue durée (Paris-I) et l'UR 026 de l'IRD \\"patrimoines et territoires\\"","title-short":"Patrimoines naturels au sud","author":[{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."},{"family":"Juhé-Beaulaton","given":"Dominique"},{"family":"Roussel","given":"Bernard"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2005"]]}}},{"id":33069,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/77X7MUPH"],"itemData":{"id":33069,"type":"book","ISBN":"978-0-231-10811-9","note":"DOI: 10.7312/zern10810","publisher":"Columbia University Press","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"People, Plants, and Justice: The Politics of Nature Conservation","title-short":"People, Plants, and Justice","URL":"https://www.degruyter.com/document/doi/10.7312/zern10810/html","editor":[{"family":"Zerner","given":"Charles"}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2022",5,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2000",12,31]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. The changes of governance from customary laws to positive law contributed to the decline of the traditional chief, and the arrival of new elites ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"mFnGkSF2","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Agrawal & Gibson, 2001; Bassett & Cormier-Salem, 2007)","plainCitation":"(Agrawal & Gibson, 2001; Bassett & Cormier-Salem, 2007)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33060,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RHBDK75J"],"itemData":{"id":33060,"type":"book","event-place":"New Brunswick, N.J","language":"en","publisher":"Rutgers University Press","publisher-place":"New Brunswick, N.J","title":"Communities and the environment : ethnicity, gender, and the state in community-based conservation","editor":[{"family":"Agrawal","given":"A."},{"family":"Gibson","given":"M.C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2001"]]}}},{"id":32907,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/5JETNMH9"],"itemData":{"id":32907,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Africa","issue":"1","language":"it","page":"150","title":"Nature as Local Heritage in Africa","volume":"77","author":[{"family":"Bassett","given":"T.J."},{"family":"Cormier-Salem","given":"M.-C."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} 
      As highlighted above, the convergence of a species as «good for eating », « good for thinking » and «good for preserving” most often assure a more efficient conservation of wild species. Nevertheless, there are counter-examples of « false convergence » with a risk of freezing the tradition, which can actually lead to weakening conservation attempts. ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"tbJvI8A5","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Artaud, 2021)","plainCitation":"(Artaud, 2021)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32911,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/TNCPQF3W"],"itemData":{"id":32911,"type":"chapter","container-title":"Marine species and biodiversity : new anthropological perspectives","event-place":"Paris","language":"en","publisher":"MNHN","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Species “good for thinking”: species “good to preserve”? The paradox of marine species co-management","author":[{"family":"Artaud","given":"H."}],"editor":[{"family":"Artaud","given":"H."},{"family":"Chlous","given":"F."},{"family":"Levain","given":"A."},{"family":"M. Mariat-Roy","given":"E.P."},{"literal":"Paris"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2021"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} highlights the limits of these “hybrid” models, which wish to integrate the cultural values attributed to a species, but which do so only partially, without considering the complexities and the dynamics (studied case: Fish co-management in two marine protected areas in Guadeloupe and in Mauritania).
      Although the existence of indigenous conservation territories and areas conserved by indigenous peoples and local communities is as old and widespread as human civilization itself, they have emerged only recently as a major phenomenon in formal conservation circles. International policies and programmes, notably those of the International Union for the Conservation of Nature and the Convention on Biological Diversity, encourage today all countries to recognise and support indigenous peoples and local communities as examples of « effective governance of bio-cultural diversity ». It is clear, however, that such recognition and support need to be carefully tailored, and cannot be improvised ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"gM2e8Go1","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Borrini-Feyerabend, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Borrini-Feyerabend, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33011,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/97CUQLGB"],"itemData":{"id":33011,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Bio-cultural diversity conserved by indigenous peoples and local communities : examples and analysis","author":[{"family":"Borrini-Feyerabend","given":"G."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.
      
Changes: shifting or porous frontiers between human and animal
Besides, since a couple of years, the divide between humanity and animality is becoming less radical, new horizons open to philosophical and anthropological thought ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"aOR0DnPc","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dounias, 2007b)","plainCitation":"(Dounias, 2007b)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32856,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W7B745UD"],"itemData":{"id":32856,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Animals, keystone in the relationship between Man and Nature?","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Paris\\npublisher: IRD","title":"Le symbolisme des animaux. L'animal, clef de voûte de la relation entre l'homme et la nature ? Animal symbolism","editor":[{"family":"Dounias","given":"E."},{"family":"Motte-Florac","given":"É."},{"family":"Dunham","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. For example: whale ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"OjhB1mAe","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Kalland, 2009)","plainCitation":"(Kalland, 2009)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32844,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/ZRGXLXCF"],"itemData":{"id":32844,"type":"document","language":"en","title":"Unveiling the whale: discourses on whales and whaling: Berghahn Books","author":[{"family":"Kalland","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2009"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}: metonymic relationship to nature (i.e., whales represent nature at large) and, at the same time, a metaphoric relationship to society (i.e., whales symbolise human relations.). In the case of the chimpanzee, the biological relationship with humans is closer. Scientific research and the observation of language, but also aesthetic sensitivity and cognitive anthropology lead to a blurring of the frontiers, making them more labile ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ckoXQwhX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Dounias, 2007b; Leblan, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Dounias, 2007b; Leblan, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32856,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/W7B745UD"],"itemData":{"id":32856,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Animals, keystone in the relationship between Man and Nature?","language":"en","note":"publisher-place: Paris\\npublisher: IRD","title":"Le symbolisme des animaux. L'animal, clef de voûte de la relation entre l'homme et la nature ? Animal symbolism","editor":[{"family":"Dounias","given":"E."},{"family":"Motte-Florac","given":"É."},{"family":"Dunham","given":"M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2007"]]}}},{"id":32874,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R2JPWGMS"],"itemData":{"id":32874,"type":"book","event-place":"Paris","ISBN":"978-2-7132-2681-6","number-of-pages":"287","publisher":"Editions de l’EHESS","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Aux frontières du singe. Relations entre hommes et chimpanzés au Kakandé, (XIXe-XXIe siècle)","author":[{"family":"Leblan","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In the case of the Landouma people in Rep. of Guinea, they can hunt chimpanzees, they don't consume them. There is no competition between them; they can coexist and therefore there is no reason to separate humans and animals by creating Protected Areas ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"BxKqVasK","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Leblan, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Leblan, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32874,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/R2JPWGMS"],"itemData":{"id":32874,"type":"book","event-place":"Paris","ISBN":"978-2-7132-2681-6","number-of-pages":"287","publisher":"Editions de l’EHESS","publisher-place":"Paris","title":"Aux frontières du singe. Relations entre hommes et chimpanzés au Kakandé, (XIXe-XXIe siècle)","author":[{"family":"Leblan","given":"Vincent"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}.  
      A growing challenge facing science-based policymaking on sustainable use is the growth in dominance of intrinsic (and ecological) values and morals opposing the sustainable use, particularly of iconic species such as elephants, lions and rhino ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"QMVkR7cX","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Bauer, 2018; Biggs, 2017; Di Minin, 2016; P. A. Lindsey, 2016)","plainCitation":"(Bauer, 2018; Biggs, 2017; Di Minin, 2016; P. A. Lindsey, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":851,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/IJ4DAIBZ"],"itemData":{"id":851,"type":"article-journal","archive_location":"WOS:000446827300002","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12444","ISSN":"1755-263X","issue":"5","title":"Lions in the modern arena of CITES","URL":"://WOS:000446827300002","volume":"11","author":[{"family":"Bauer","given":"H."},{"family":"Nowell","given":"K."},{"family":"Sillero-Zubiri","given":"C."},{"family":"Macdonald","given":"D. W."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2018",9]]}}},{"id":32541,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9D9K9DR4"],"itemData":{"id":32541,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The escalating illegal wildlife trade (IWT) is one of the most high profile conservation challenges today. The crisis has attracted over US$350 million in donor and government funding in recent years, primarily directed at increased enforcement against IWT. There is growing recognition among practitioners and policy makers of the need to engage rural communities that neighbour or live with wildlife as key partners in tackling IWT. However, a framework to guide such community engagement is lacking. Here, we present a Theory of Change (ToC) to guide policy-makers, donors, and practitioners in partnering with communities to combat IWT. Our ToC identifies four pathways for community-level actions: a) strengthening disincentives for illegal behaviour; b) increasing incentives for wildlife stewardship; c) decreasing costs of living with wildlife; and d) supporting non-wildlife related livelihoods. The pathways all require strengthening of enabling conditions, including capacity building, and of governance in order to succeed. Our ToC serves to guide actions to tackle IWT and to inform the evaluation of policies. Moreover, our ToC serves as a tool to foster dialogue among IWT stakeholders from local communities through to governments and international donors to develop a more effective, holistic and sustainable community-based response to the IWT crisis.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12796","ISSN":"15231739","issue":"1","page":"5–12","title":"Developing a theory of change for a community-based response to illegal wildlife trade","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Dublin","given":"Holly T."},{"family":"Allan","given":"James R."},{"family":"Challender","given":"Dan W.S."},{"family":"Skinner","given":"Diane"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}},{"id":31079,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/63APAYQR","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/VJBVT2C3"],"itemData":{"id":31079,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","DOI":"10.1016/j.tree.2015.12.006","ISSN":"01695347","issue":"2","journalAbbreviation":"Trends in Ecology & Evolution","language":"en","page":"99-102","source":"DOI.org (Crossref)","title":"Banning Trophy Hunting Will Exacerbate Biodiversity Loss","URL":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0169534715003031","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Di Minin","given":"Enrico"},{"family":"Leader-Williams","given":"Nigel"},{"family":"Bradshaw","given":"Corey J.A."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2021",2,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016",2]]}}},{"id":4318,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9ES6P65Z","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RB5A7V9A"],"itemData":{"id":4318,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Trophy hunting is widely used in Africa to generate funding for wildlife areas. In 2015, a global media frenzy resulted from the illegal killing of a radio-collared lion, “Cecil,” by a trophy hunter in Zimbabwe. Trophy hunting is contentious and much of the media discourse is emotional and polarized, focusing on animal welfare and debating the value of hunting as a conservation tool. We use the Cecil incident to urge a change in the focus of discussion and make a call for global action. We highlight the dual challenge to African governments posed by the need to fund vast wildlife estates and provide incentives for conservation by communities in the context of growing human populations and competing priorities. With or without trophy hunting, Africa's wildlife areas require much more funding to prevent serious biodiversity loss. In light of this, we urge a shift away from perpetual debates over trophy hunting to the more pressing question of “How do we fund Africa's wildlife areas adequately?” We urge the international community to greatly increase funding and technical support for Africa's wildlife estate. Concurrently, we encourage African governments and hunters to take decisive steps to reform hunting industries and address challenges associated with that revenue generating option.","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12224","ISSN":"1755-263X","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"Copyright and Photocopying: © 2015 The Authors. Conservation Letters published by Wiley Periodicals, Inc.","page":"296–301","title":"Life after Cecil: channelling global outrage into funding for conservation in Africa","title-short":"Life after Cecil","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/conl.12224","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter A."},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy A."},{"family":"Funston","given":"Paul J."},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp H."},{"family":"Hunter","given":"Luke T. B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. In particular there has been a growth in what is mutualist orientations towards wild species ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"bluvPDwE","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Manfredo, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Manfredo, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":33001,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/GA7BZKBP"],"itemData":{"id":33001,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Biological Conservation","language":"en","page":"303–311","title":"Values, trust, and cultural backlash in conservation governance: The case of wildlife management in the United States","volume":"214","author":[{"family":"Manfredo","given":"M.J."},{"family":"Teel","given":"T.L."},{"family":"Sullivan","given":"L."},{"family":"Dietsch","given":"A.M."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} which dictate that wild species should be treated in the same way as people and should have the same right ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"ZqpO9mI6","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Maris & Bechet, 2010)","plainCitation":"(Maris & Bechet, 2010)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32837,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/QFXJLU3K"],"itemData":{"id":32837,"type":"article-journal","container-title":"Conservation Biology","issue":"4","language":"en","page":"966–973","title":"From adaptive management to adjustive management: a pragmatic account of biodiversity values","volume":"24","author":[{"family":"Maris","given":"V."},{"family":"Bechet","given":"A."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2010"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Sustainable use of wild species is therefore perceived to be immoral ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"REfF9jzG","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biggs, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Biggs, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32541,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9D9K9DR4"],"itemData":{"id":32541,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The escalating illegal wildlife trade (IWT) is one of the most high profile conservation challenges today. The crisis has attracted over US$350 million in donor and government funding in recent years, primarily directed at increased enforcement against IWT. There is growing recognition among practitioners and policy makers of the need to engage rural communities that neighbour or live with wildlife as key partners in tackling IWT. However, a framework to guide such community engagement is lacking. Here, we present a Theory of Change (ToC) to guide policy-makers, donors, and practitioners in partnering with communities to combat IWT. Our ToC identifies four pathways for community-level actions: a) strengthening disincentives for illegal behaviour; b) increasing incentives for wildlife stewardship; c) decreasing costs of living with wildlife; and d) supporting non-wildlife related livelihoods. The pathways all require strengthening of enabling conditions, including capacity building, and of governance in order to succeed. Our ToC serves to guide actions to tackle IWT and to inform the evaluation of policies. Moreover, our ToC serves as a tool to foster dialogue among IWT stakeholders from local communities through to governments and international donors to develop a more effective, holistic and sustainable community-based response to the IWT crisis.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12796","ISSN":"15231739","issue":"1","page":"5–12","title":"Developing a theory of change for a community-based response to illegal wildlife trade","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Dublin","given":"Holly T."},{"family":"Allan","given":"James R."},{"family":"Challender","given":"Dan W.S."},{"family":"Skinner","given":"Diane"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Examples of policies on sustainable use and real impacts on human livelihoods and wild species can be seen in the outbursts in response to the hunting of Cecil the Lion ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"VAp8sOMR","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2016)","plainCitation":"(P. A. Lindsey, 2016)","dontUpdate":true,"noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":4318,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9ES6P65Z","http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/RB5A7V9A"],"itemData":{"id":4318,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"Trophy hunting is widely used in Africa to generate funding for wildlife areas. In 2015, a global media frenzy resulted from the illegal killing of a radio-collared lion, “Cecil,” by a trophy hunter in Zimbabwe. Trophy hunting is contentious and much of the media discourse is emotional and polarized, focusing on animal welfare and debating the value of hunting as a conservation tool. We use the Cecil incident to urge a change in the focus of discussion and make a call for global action. We highlight the dual challenge to African governments posed by the need to fund vast wildlife estates and provide incentives for conservation by communities in the context of growing human populations and competing priorities. With or without trophy hunting, Africa's wildlife areas require much more funding to prevent serious biodiversity loss. In light of this, we urge a shift away from perpetual debates over trophy hunting to the more pressing question of “How do we fund Africa's wildlife areas adequately?” We urge the international community to greatly increase funding and technical support for Africa's wildlife estate. Concurrently, we encourage African governments and hunters to take decisive steps to reform hunting industries and address challenges associated with that revenue generating option.","container-title":"Conservation Letters","DOI":"10.1111/conl.12224","ISSN":"1755-263X","issue":"4","language":"en","license":"Copyright and Photocopying: © 2015 The Authors. Conservation Letters published by Wiley Periodicals, Inc.","page":"296–301","title":"Life after Cecil: channelling global outrage into funding for conservation in Africa","title-short":"Life after Cecil","URL":"https://conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/conl.12224","volume":"9","author":[{"family":"Lindsey","given":"Peter A."},{"family":"Balme","given":"Guy A."},{"family":"Funston","given":"Paul J."},{"family":"Henschel","given":"Philipp H."},{"family":"Hunter","given":"Luke T. B."}],"accessed":{"date-parts":[["2020",10,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[["2016"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and the restrictions on transporting wild species trophies by numerous airlines. These developments point to a central challenge for science-based decision-making on Sustainable Use. Underlying this challenge are the different values that individuals and groups hold over the moral acceptability of sustainable use of iconic species as a source of conservation revenue ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"sqSHpaL3","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biggs, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Biggs, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32541,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9D9K9DR4"],"itemData":{"id":32541,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The escalating illegal wildlife trade (IWT) is one of the most high profile conservation challenges today. The crisis has attracted over US$350 million in donor and government funding in recent years, primarily directed at increased enforcement against IWT. There is growing recognition among practitioners and policy makers of the need to engage rural communities that neighbour or live with wildlife as key partners in tackling IWT. However, a framework to guide such community engagement is lacking. Here, we present a Theory of Change (ToC) to guide policy-makers, donors, and practitioners in partnering with communities to combat IWT. Our ToC identifies four pathways for community-level actions: a) strengthening disincentives for illegal behaviour; b) increasing incentives for wildlife stewardship; c) decreasing costs of living with wildlife; and d) supporting non-wildlife related livelihoods. The pathways all require strengthening of enabling conditions, including capacity building, and of governance in order to succeed. Our ToC serves to guide actions to tackle IWT and to inform the evaluation of policies. Moreover, our ToC serves as a tool to foster dialogue among IWT stakeholders from local communities through to governments and international donors to develop a more effective, holistic and sustainable community-based response to the IWT crisis.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12796","ISSN":"15231739","issue":"1","page":"5–12","title":"Developing a theory of change for a community-based response to illegal wildlife trade","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Dublin","given":"Holly T."},{"family":"Allan","given":"James R."},{"family":"Challender","given":"Dan W.S."},{"family":"Skinner","given":"Diane"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"}. Drawing on experience and evidence from other areas of conflict resolution, Biggs and others propose a structured iterative process that incorporates differences in values together with scientific evidence ITEM CSL_CITATION {"citationID":"jpooAa5k","properties":{"formattedCitation":"(Biggs, 2017)","plainCitation":"(Biggs, 2017)","noteIndex":0},"citationItems":[{"id":32541,"uris":["http://zotero.org/groups/2344805/items/9D9K9DR4"],"itemData":{"id":32541,"type":"article-journal","abstract":"The escalating illegal wildlife trade (IWT) is one of the most high profile conservation challenges today. The crisis has attracted over US$350 million in donor and government funding in recent years, primarily directed at increased enforcement against IWT. There is growing recognition among practitioners and policy makers of the need to engage rural communities that neighbour or live with wildlife as key partners in tackling IWT. However, a framework to guide such community engagement is lacking. Here, we present a Theory of Change (ToC) to guide policy-makers, donors, and practitioners in partnering with communities to combat IWT. Our ToC identifies four pathways for community-level actions: a) strengthening disincentives for illegal behaviour; b) increasing incentives for wildlife stewardship; c) decreasing costs of living with wildlife; and d) supporting non-wildlife related livelihoods. The pathways all require strengthening of enabling conditions, including capacity building, and of governance in order to succeed. Our ToC serves to guide actions to tackle IWT and to inform the evaluation of policies. Moreover, our ToC serves as a tool to foster dialogue among IWT stakeholders from local communities through to governments and international donors to develop a more effective, holistic and sustainable community-based response to the IWT crisis.","container-title":"Conservation Biology","DOI":"10.1111/cobi.12796","ISSN":"15231739","issue":"1","page":"5–12","title":"Developing a theory of change for a community-based response to illegal wildlife trade","volume":"31","author":[{"family":"Biggs","given":"Duan"},{"family":"Cooney","given":"Rosie"},{"family":"Roe","given":"Dilys"},{"family":"Dublin","given":"Holly T."},{"family":"Allan","given":"James R."},{"family":"Challender","given":"Dan W.S."},{"family":"Skinner","given":"Diane"}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2017",2]]}}}],"schema":